ssertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Sodium wire efficiently dries ethers by reacting with water impurities to form sodium hydroxide, eliminating moisture and ensuring a dry solvent. So the option (1) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(28984,'chemistry',67,1074,2562,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Aryl sulphonic acid gives phenol on reacting with $\\mathrm{NaOH}$ at high temperature.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> This reaction is electrophilic substitution reaction.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'<img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/67_28984_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />  This is nucleophilic aromatic substitution reaction and occurs via the addition- elimination mechanism with $\\mathrm{SO}_{3}^{2-}$ as the leaving group. So the option (3) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(28985,'chemistry',68,1111,2647,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Formaldehyde cannot be prepared by Rosenmund\'s reduction.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Acid chlorides can be reduced into aldehydes with hydrogen in boiling xylene using palladium or platinum as a catalyst supported on barium sulphate. This is known as<br>Rosenmund\'s reaction','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Acyl chloride (acid chloride) is hydrogenated over the catalyst palladium on barium sulphate. This reaction is called Rosenmund reduction.<br>Formaldehyde $(\\mathrm{HCHO})$ cannot be prepared by Rosenmund\'s reduction because formyl chloride $(\\mathrm{HCOCl})$ is unstable at room temperature. So the option (2) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(28986,'chemistry',69,1174,2809,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Ammonolysis of alkyl halides involves the reaction between alkyl halides and alcoholic ammonia.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Reaction can be used to prepare$1^{\\circ}, 2^{\\circ}, 3^{\\circ}$ amines and finally quaternary ammonium salts.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'<img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/69_28986_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />  Ammonolysis of alkyl or benzyl halides in ethanolic ammonia results in nucleophilic substitution, replacing the halogen with an amino group, leading to the formation of primary amines that can further react to produce secondary, tertiary amines, and quaternary ammonium salt. So the option (2) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(28987,'chemistry',70,1261,3076,'Choose the correct statement','[{"text": "Proteins are the natural polymers of L-amino acids.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Insulin is a hormone having proteinous nature.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Denatured proteins lose its biological activity.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "All of these", "isCorrect": true}]',3,NULL,NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(29040,'chemistry',67,1083,2599,'A compound  \' X \'  when treated with phthalic anhydride in presence of concentrated   $\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{SO}_{4}$  yields \' Y \'. \' Y \' is used as an acid/base indicator. \' X \'  and  \'Y\' respectively are :','[{"text": "carbolic acid, phenolphthalein", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "toluidine, phenolphthalein.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "anisole, methyl orange", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "salicylaldehyde, phenolphthalein", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Carbolic acid (commonly known as phenol) reacts with phthalic anhydride in the presence of conc. $\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{SO}_{4}$ to form phenolphthalein. Phenolphthalein is an acid/base indicator. Thus, X is carbolic acid and Y is phenolphthalein.<br><img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/67_29040_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',2023,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(28989,'chemistry',67,1083,2597,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Phenol undergoes Kolbe reaction whereas ethanol does not.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Phenoxide ion is more basic than ethoxide ion.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Phenol is a stronger acid than ethanol due to the resonance stabilization of the phenoxide ion, resulting in increased stability compared to the ethoxide ion formed from ethanol. When acid is strong it\'s conjugate base is weak. So ethoxide is more basic than phenoxide. So the option (3) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(28990,'chemistry',68,1111,2651,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Oxo process is also called hydroformylation reaction.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Palladium chloride and cupric chloride are used as catalysts in oxo process.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Oxo process is used for preparing aldehydes. At high temperature and pressure, an alkene reacts with $\\mathrm{CO}$ and $\\mathrm{H}_{2}$ (water gas) in presence of cobalt carbonyl catalyst. So the option (3) is correct.',NULL,'Hard','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(28991,'chemistry',70,1254,3065,'The proteins with a prosthetic group are called','[{"text": "Pseudo proteins", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Complex proteins", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Polypeptides", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Conjugated proteins", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Simple protein and Prosthetic group (nonprotein material) gives conjugated proteins.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(28992,'chemistry',68,1113,2662,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Aldehydes and ketones show condensation reactions.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> The hydrogen atoms attached to $\\alpha$ carbon of aldehydes and ketones are reactive.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Aldehydes and ketones undergo condensation reactions, due to acidic nature of the alpha hydrogen. It involves nucleophilic attack of the oxygen atom on the carbonyl group\'s electrophilic carbon. So the option (1) is correct',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(28994,'chemistry',67,1089,2613,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Phenol is more acidic than ethanol.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Phenoxide ion is resonance stabilised.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Phenol\'s higher acidity than ethanol is attributed to the electronegative oxygen in the phenolic - $\\mathrm{OH}$ group, which stabilizes the resulting negative charge through resonance over the aromatic ring. This facilitates proton donation. Ethanol lacks such resonance stabilization. So the option (1) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(28995,'chemistry',70,1254,3061,'Which of the following statement is incorrect about the secondary structure of protein?','[{"text": "It refers to the shape in which long polypeptide chain can exist.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "It exists in two different type of structures, i.e., $\\\\mathrm{\\\\alpha}$-helix and $\\\\mathrm{\\\\beta}$-pleated sheet structure.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Its structure arises due to regular folding of the backbone of the polypeptide chain.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "There is the covalent bonding between $\\\\mathrm{>\\\\mathrm{C}}$ $\\\\mathrm{=\\\\mathrm{O}}$ and $\\\\mathrm{\\\\mathrm{NH}}$ groups of the peptide bond.", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'The secondary structure of proteins refers to the shape in which a long polypeptide chain can exist. They are found to exist in two different types of structures, i.e., $\\mathrm{\\alpha}$ - helix and $\\mathrm{\\beta}$-pleated sheet structure. These structures arise due to the regular folding of the backbone of the polypeptide chain due to hydrogen bonding between  <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/70_28995_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />    and $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{NH}}$ group of the peptide bond.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(28996,'chemistry',68,1113,2656,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Chlorine atom exhibits -I effect in trichloroacetaldehyde.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Acetaldehyde is more reactive than trichloroacetaldehyde.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Trichloroacetaldehyde, with three chlorine atoms inducing a greater -I effect, enhances the electropositivity of the carbonyl carbon. This makes it more reactive in nucleophilic addition reactions compared to acetaldehyde, which lacks such electron-withdrawing chlorine atoms. So the option (3) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(28997,'chemistry',67,1071,2554,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/67_28997_qtext_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />  bond angle is less than normal tetrahedral bond angle.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Lone pair-lone pair repulsion decreases bond angle.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'The bond angle in a molecule or ion can be less than the normal tetrahedral bond angle (approximately $109.5^{\\circ}$ ) due to a phenomenon known as distortion or bending. In the given structure, which is of alcohols, there are two lone pairs on central oxygen that repel each other. This reduces the bond angle to $108.9^{\\circ}$. So the option (1) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(28999,'chemistry',67,1083,2596,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Phenol forms $2,4,6$-tribromophenol on treatment with $\\mathrm{Br}_{2}$ - water at $273 \\mathrm{~K.}$<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Phenol is o / p- directing group.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Phenol forms 2,4,6-Tribromophenol (white $\\mathrm{ppt})$ when treated with $\\mathrm{Br}_{2}$ in water. In phenols, the polarization of the bromine molecule occurs even in the absence of a Lewis acid. This is because phenols are more reactive than benzene due to the presence of the hydroxyl ($\\mathrm{-OH}$ ) group, which activates the ring and makes it more susceptible to electrophilic aromatic substitution reactions. <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/67_28999_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />  So the option (2) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(29000,'chemistry',69,1174,2810,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> In Hoffmann bromamide reaction. the amine formed has one carbon atom less than the parent $1^{\\circ}$ amide<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> N-methyl acetamide undergoes<br>Hoffmann bromamide reaction.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'<img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/69_29000_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />  It is a method for preparation of primary amines only. In this degradation reaction, migration of an alkyl or aryl group takes place from carbonyl carbon of the amide to the nitrogen atom. The amine so formed contains one carbon less than that present in the amide. Only primary amides will give this reaction.<br>$\\mathrm{N}$-methyl acetamide $\\left(\\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\mathrm{CONHCH}_{3}\\right)$ is a secondary amide and cannot give Hoffmann bromamide reaction, as it cannot form the reaction intermediate. So the option (3) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(29001,'chemistry',68,1113,2657,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Urotropine is used as medicine in case of urinary trobules.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> $\\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\mathrm{CHO}$ reacts with $\\mathrm{NH}_{3}$ to form urotropine.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Formaldehyde $(\\mathrm{HCHO})$ can undergo a reaction with ammonia $\\left(\\mathrm{NH}_{3}\\right)$ to produce urotropine (hexamethylenetetramine), a cyclic compound with the formula $\\left(\\mathrm{CH}_{2}\\right)_{6} \\mathrm{~N}_{4}$.<br>$6 \\mathrm{HCHO}+4 \\mathrm{NH}_{3} \\rightarrow\\left(\\mathrm{CH}_{2}\\right)_{6} \\mathrm{~N}_{4}+6 \\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O}$<br>So the option (3) is correct.',NULL,'Hard','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(29002,'chemistry',70,1254,3062,'The main force(s) which stabilise the ${\\text{2}}^\\circ $ and  ${\\text{3}}^\\circ $ structures of proteins is/are','[{"text": "Hydrogen bonds", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Disulphide linkages", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Vander Waals and electrostatic forces of attraction", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "All of the above", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'The main forces which stabilise the $\\mathrm{2^{\\circ}}$ and $\\mathrm{3^{\\circ}}$ structures of proteins are hydrogen bonds, i.e., further folding of the secondary structure. It gives rise to two major molecular shape, i.e., fibrous and globular.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(29003,'chemistry',67,1083,2591,'The ascending order of acidity of - $\\mathrm{OH}$ group in the following compounds is : Choose the correct answer from the options given below :','[{"text": "(C) $ < $ (A) $ < $ (D) $ < $ (B) $ < $ (E)", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "(A) $ < $ (C) $ < $ (D) $ < $ (B) $ < $ (E)", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "(A) $ < $ (D) $ < $ (C) $ < $ (B) $ < $ (E)", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "(C) $ < $ (D) $ < $ (B) $ < $ (A) $ < $ (E)", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Phenols are more acidic than alcohols. EWG increases acidic nature and EDG decreases acidic nature, $-\\mathrm{NO}_{2}$ is EWG and $-\\mathrm{OCH}_{3}$ is<br>EDG. <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/67_29003_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2024,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','chemistry/67_29003_question_1.png'),
(29004,'chemistry',69,1176,2816,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Aniline is a weaker base than ethylamine.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Lower the value of $\\mathrm{pK}_{\\mathrm{b}}$ stronger is the base.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'The lone pair of electrons on the nitrogen $(\\mathrm{N})$ atom in aniline is delocalized over the benzene ring, and this delocalization reduces its availability for protonation. <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/69_29004_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />  This makes aniline a weaker base than ethylamine, which does not have such extensive electron delocalization. Reason is also true. So the option (2) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(29005,'chemistry',68,1114,2671,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Formaldehyde contains no alkyl group in its molecule.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Formaldehyde condenses with phenol to give resin.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Formaldehyde is,  <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/68_29005_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />  Formaldehyde undergoes condensation with phenol, catalyzed by dilute $\\mathrm{NaOH}$ or ammonia at elevated temperatures, forming phenolformaldehyde resin (bakelite) through crosslinking, resulting in a strong and resilient threedimensional network structure. So the option (2) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(29006,'chemistry',70,1257,3069,'Vitamin $\\mathrm{B_{2}}$, a water soluble vitamin is also known as','[{"text": "ascorbic acid", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "riboflavin", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "thiamine", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "pyridoxine", "isCorrect": false}]',1,NULL,NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(29007,'chemistry',67,1083,2591,'Given below are two statements :<br>Statement (A) : p-nitrophenol is more acidic than m-nitrophenol and o-nitrophenol.<br>Statement (B) : Ethanol will give immediate turbidity with Lucas reagent.','[{"text": "Statement A is correct but statement B is incorrect.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Statement A is incorrect but statement $\\\\mathrm{B}$ is correct.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Statements A and B are correct.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Statements A and B are incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'$\\mathrm{NO}_{2}$ is an EWG and increases acidic strength of phenols. This effect is pronounced at para and ortho positions than at meta position. Thus, statement A is correct. Ethanol is a primary alcohol and gives turbidity after heating.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ type-2',2024,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(29008,'chemistry',69,1178,2830,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Dipole moment of p- chloronitrobenzene is less than p-nitrotoluene.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> In p-chloronitrobenzene, both vectors of dipole moment act in opposite direction.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'<img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/69_29008_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />  In p-chloronitrobenzene, the individual dipole moments due to the chlorine atom and nitro group are in opposite directions and hence partially get cancelled. This reduces the overall dipole moment. Whereas, in p-nitrotoluene both the bond dipoles due to the methyl group and the nitro group are in the same direction and get added. So the option (1) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(29009,'chemistry',68,1111,2650,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Aldehydes and ketones are functional isomers of each other.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> A better yield of aldehydes can be prepared by the action of Grignard reagents on ethyl formate instead of ethyl orthoformate.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Grignard reagents react with ethyl orthoformate more efficiently than with ethyl formate, giving higher aldehyde yields due to the enhanced reactivity of the aldehydes generated from ethyl orthoformate in forming tertiary alcohols. So the option (3) is correct.',NULL,'Hard','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(29010,'chemistry',70,1258,3072,'The number of Nucleotide pairs present in one turn of DNA helix is','[{"text": "10", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "9", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "8", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "4", "isCorrect": false}]',0,NULL,NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(29012,'chemistry',67,1084,2604,'products are','[{"text": "", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Reduction of ethers with $\\mathrm{HI}$ gives a tertiary halide, when one of the alkyl group is a tertiary group, because the intermediate formed will be a more stable tertiary carbocation.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2024,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','chemistry/67_29012_question_1.png'),
(29013,'chemistry',68,1118,2677,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Carboxylic acids are stabilised by resonance.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Chloroacetic acid is weaker than acetic acid.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'$\\mathop {{\\text{Cl}} - {\\text{C}}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{2}}}{\\text{CHO}}}\\limits_{{\\text{Chloroacetic}}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\text{acid}}} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} \\mathop {{\\text{C}}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{3}}}{\\text{CHO}}}\\limits_{{\\text{acetic}}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\text{acid}}} $<br>Chloroacetic acid\'s enhanced dissociation compared to acetic acid is attributed to the -I effect of the chlorine atom. This withdraws electron density and promotes proton release. Whereas in acetic acid, the methyl group induces $\\mathrm{a}+\\mathrm{I}$ effect, and destabilizes the acetate ion, reducing its dissociation tendency. So the option (3) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(29015,'chemistry',67,1083,2600,'Phenolic group can be identified by a positive','[{"text": "Lucas test", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Carbylamine test", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Phthalein test", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Tollen\'s test", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'In Phthalein test, phenol is heated with phthalic anhydride in the presence of concentrated sulphuric acid to form a colourless condensation compound called phenolphthalein.  <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/67_29015_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2024,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(29016,'chemistry',68,1113,2670,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Hydroxyketones are not directly used in Grignard reaction.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Grignard reagents react with hydroxyl group.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Hydroxyketones are avoided in Grignard reactions due to the reactivity of Grignard reagents with the electrophilic hydroxyl group, forming unstable intermediates that can undergo rearrangements or decomposition. This gives undesired products like alcohols instead of the intended ketones. So the option (1) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(29018,'chemistry',67,1077,2578,'Find out product $(\\mathrm{X})$<br>${\\text{Ph   -   CH   =   C}}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{2}}}\\xrightarrow[\\substack{ {\\text{(2)THBr}} \\\\ {\\text{ }}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\text{(3) Mg,dry}}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\text{ether }} \\\\ {\\text{ (4) HCHO}} } ]{{{\\text{ (1) }}{{\\text{B}}_{\\text{2}}}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{6}}}{\\text{/THF/}}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{2}}}{{\\text{O}}_{\\text{2}}}{\\text{/O}}{{\\text{H}}^\\Theta }}}{\\text{X (major)}}$','[{"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{Ph}-\\\\mathrm{CH}_{2}-\\\\mathrm{CH}_{2}-\\\\mathrm{CH}_{2}-\\\\mathrm{OH}$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'${\\text{Ph}} - {\\text{CH}} = {\\text{C}}{{\\text{H}}_2}\\xrightarrow{{(1){{\\text{B}}_2}{{\\text{H}}_6}/{\\text{THF}}/{{\\text{H}}_2}{{\\text{O}}_2}/{\\text{O}}{{\\text{H}}^ - }}}$<br>$\\mathrm{Ph}-\\mathrm{CH}_{2}-\\mathrm{CH}_{2}-\\mathrm{OH}$<br>$\\xrightarrow{\\mathrm{HBr}} \\mathrm{Ph}-\\mathrm{CH}_{2}-\\mathrm{CH}_{2}-\\mathrm{Br}$<br>$\\xrightarrow[\\text { dry ether }]{\\mathrm{Mg}} \\mathrm{Ph}-\\mathrm{CH}_{2}-\\mathrm{CH}_{2}-\\mathrm{MgBr}$<br>$\\xrightarrow{\\mathrm{HCHO}} \\mathrm{Ph}-\\mathrm{CH}_{2}-\\mathrm{CH}_{2}-\\mathrm{CH}_{2}-\\mathrm{OH}$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',2024,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(29069,'chemistry',70,1247,3032,'Which of the following pairs represents anomers?','[{"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Anomers have different configuration at $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{C}-1}$. If $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{OH}}$ is present at right side anomeric carbon is known as $\\mathrm{\\alpha}$ - form and if $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{OH}}$ is present at left side of anomeric carbon is known as $\\mathrm{\\beta}$-form.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,'NCERT Exemplar','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(29020,'chemistry',68,1109,2643,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> The carbon atom in carbonyl group is $\\mathrm{sp}^{2}$ hybridised.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/68_29020_qtext_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'The bond energy of a carbonyl group is typically around $179 \\mathrm{kcal} / \\mathrm{mol}$, whereas in the absence of resonance, it is approximately 145.8 $\\mathrm{kcal} / \\mathrm{mol}$. The carbonyl group exhibits resonance, which contributes to its higher bond energy. So the option (3) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(29022,'chemistry',69,1176,2816,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Aniline is a weaker base than cyclohexyl amine.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Aniline undergoes halogenation even in the absence of a catalyst.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'<img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/69_29022_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />  Aniline\'s resonance hybridization diminishes the availability of nitrogen\'s lone pair for protonation, stemming from conjugation with the aromatic benzene ring.<br>Unlike aniline, cyclohexylamine lacks resonance, rendering its nitrogen lone pair more accessible for protonation, making it a stronger base. Given  reason is also a correct statement. So the option (2) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(29023,'chemistry',67,1077,2578,'‘ A ’   and   ‘ B ’  formed in the following set of reactions are','[{"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'<img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/67_29023_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',2023,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','chemistry/67_29023_question_1.png'),
(29024,'chemistry',68,1113,2657,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Addition of $\\mathrm{HCN}$ to carbonyl compounds gives cyanohydrins.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Pure HCN reacts with aldehydes and ketones.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'$\\mathrm{HCN}$ is generally considered a weak acid with a low degree of dissociation. However, in the presence of a base, even something as simple as water $\\left(\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O}\\right)$, the dissociation of $\\mathrm{HCN}$ increases significantly. This enhanced dissociation yields a notable amount of $\\mathrm{CN}^{-}$ ions, which are then available to participate in nucleophilic attacks on chemical bonds. So the option (3) is correct .',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(29025,'chemistry',67,1077,2576,'Decreasing order of dehydration of the following alcohols is','[{"text": "D $>$ B $>$ C $>$ A", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "A $>$ D $>$ B $>$ C", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "B $>$ A $>$ D $>$ C", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "B $>$ D $>$ C $>$ A", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Order of stability : <br>(B) $>$ (D) $>$ (C) $>(\\mathrm{A})$<br>Ease of dehydration is directly proportional to the stability of carbocations formed.<br><img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/67_29025_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',2023,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','chemistry/67_29025_question_1.png'),
(29026,'chemistry',69,1175,2814,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Boiling point of amines are lower than those of alcohols and carboxylic acids. <br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Amines are much more soluble in water than less polar solvents like alcohol. ether, etc.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'The higher electronegativity of oxygen in alcohols and carboxylic acids, coupled with the structural characteristics, results in stronger hydrogen bonding compared to amines. Thus, they have higher boiling points than amines. The reason is also true as amines can form hydrogen bonds with water. So the option (2) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(29027,'chemistry',68,1113,2657,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Carbonyl compounds undergo nucleophilic addition reactions<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Addition of $\\mathrm{KCN}$ solution to carbonyl compounds decreases the $\\mathrm{pH}$ of solution.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'The addition of a $\\mathrm{KCN}$ solution to carbonyl compounds results in an increase in the $\\mathrm{pH}$ of the solution. Hydrolysis of KCN produces $\\mathrm{HCN}$ and $\\mathrm{KOH}$ according to the equation:<br>$2 \\mathrm{KCN}+\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O} \\rightarrow \\mathrm{KOH}+\\mathrm{HCN}$<br>The HCN generated reacts with the carbonyl group in the carbonyl compounds to form cyanohydrins. This process helps to maintain the hydrolysis equilibrium. As the hydrolysis of $\\mathrm{KCN}$ continues, more $\\mathrm{KOH}$ is produced, contributing to the overall alkalinity of the solution. So the option (3) is correct.',NULL,'Hard','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(29029,'chemistry',67,1077,2572,'The strongest acid from the following is','[{"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Electron withdrawing groups increase acidic nature. Nitro group is the most electron withdrawing among the given groups. Thus, compound given in option (4) is the most acidic.',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',2023,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(29031,'chemistry',68,1113,2656,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Presence of acids and bases activates carbonyl compounds for reaction.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Carbonyl compounds possess positive and negative centres and provide seat for electrophilic and nucleophilic attack.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Presence of acid intensifies the partial positive charge on carbonyl carbon and hence, activates the group. <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/68_29031_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />  Presence of base activates $\\alpha$-methylene component of the carbonyl compounds by converting them in carbanions.<br>$\\mathrm{RCH}_{2} \\mathrm{CHO}+: \\mathrm{B}^{-} \\rightarrow \\mathrm{R} \\overline{\\mathrm{C}} \\mathrm{HCHO}+\\mathrm{BH}$.<br>So the option (2) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(29033,'chemistry',69,1176,2816,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Electron releasing $-\\mathrm{CH}_{3}$ group increases the electron density on nitrogen atom in alkyl amines.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Trimethylamine is more basic than methyl and dimethylamine in aq. solution.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Steric hindrance from three methyl groups reduces trimethylamine\'s basicity despite increased electron density on nitrogen. Thus, option (3) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(29034,'chemistry',68,1113,2657,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> $\\mathrm{RCHO}, \\mathrm{RCOCH}_{3}$, and cyclic<br>ketones react with sodium bisulphite.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Crystalline solid derivatives are formed when aldehydes or ketones are shaken with saturated aqueous solution of sodium bisulphite.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'The addition of the nucleophile, $\\mathrm{HSO}_{3}^{-}$, is feasible only when the carbonyl group is not sterically hindered. This condition is met in compounds such as aliphatic aldehydes <br>( $\\mathrm{RCHO}$ ), ketones $\\left(\\mathrm{RCOCH}_{3}\\right)$, and cyclic ketones, where the structure does not exhibit significant steric hindrance around the carbonyl group, allowing the $\\mathrm{HSO}_{3}^{-}$ion to effectively attack the electrophilic carbonyl carbon. So the option (2) is correct.',NULL,'Hard','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(29035,'chemistry',67,1077,2576,'In the following reaction,  \' B \'  is','[{"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'<img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/67_29035_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',2023,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','chemistry/67_29035_question_1.png'),
(29036,'chemistry',67,1076,2569,'Read Assertion and Reason carefully and mark correct option.<br><br><strong>Assertion:</strong> Butan –1– ol has higher boiling point than ethoxyethane.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Extensive hydrogen bonding leads to stronger association of molecules.','[{"text": "Assertion is false but Reason is true", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is true but Reason is false", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Alcohols have higher boiling point than ethers due to the hydrogen bonding. These hydrogen bonding help in intermolecular association.',NULL,'Hard','Assertion and Reason',2023,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(29037,'chemistry',68,1112,2652,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Chloral is a geminal diol.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Chloral hydrate is an unstable compound.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'<img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/68_29037_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />  In chloral hydrate, the presence of three $\\mathrm{Cl}$ atoms with -I effect increases the magnitude of the positive charge on the carbonyl carbon atom. As a result, even a weak nuleophile like water attacks to form a hydrate. Moreover, there is weak intermolecular hydrogen bonding in the chloral hydrate which tends to increase it\'s stability. So the option (3) is correct.',NULL,'Hard','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(29038,'chemistry',69,1177,2828,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> The reaction between a diazo salt and an aromatic amine or a phenol, giving an aminoazo or hydroxyazo compounds is called coupling reaction.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Condensation of diazonium salt with phenol is carried out in weakly acidic medium.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Two aromatic rings joined through the $\\mathrm{-N}=\\mathrm{N}-$ bond is called as coupling reaction. Phenol molecule at its para position is coupled with the diazonium salt to form $\\mathrm{p}$ -hydroxyazobenzene. It takes place in basic medium. <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/69_29038_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />  Hence, option (3) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(29126,'chemistry',67,1070,2553,'The IUPAC name of<br>{$\\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\mathrm{OCH}_{2} \\mathrm{CH}_{2} \\mathrm{OCH}_{2} \\mathrm{CH}_{2} \\mathrm{CH}_{2} \\mathrm{CH}_{3}$}','[{"text": "Ethoxyethyl methoxy ethyl ether", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Ethoxy methoxy diethyl ether", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "1-Butoxy-2-methoxyethane", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "3,6,9-Trioxadecane", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'<img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/67_29126_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(29041,'chemistry',68,1118,2682,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Benzoic acid does not give Friedel Craft\'s reaction.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Benzoic acid is obtained by catalytic oxidation of toluene with air in presence of $\\mathrm{Co}-$ $\\mathrm{Mn}$ as catalyst.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'The electron-withdrawing nature of the carboxyl group $(-\\mathrm{COOH})$ deactivates the benzene ring, preventing compounds like benzoic acid from readily undergoing Friedel-Craft\'s reactions. So the option (2) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(29044,'chemistry',69,1176,2818,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Carbylamine reaction involves the reaction between $1^{\\circ}$ amine and chloroform in basic medium.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> In carbylamine reaction, $-\\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ group is converted into $-\\mathrm{NC}$ group.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Aliphatic and aromatic primary amines on heating with chloroform and ethanolic potassium hydroxide form isocyanides or carbylamines which are foul smelling substances. Secondary and tertiary amines do not show this reaction.<br>${\\text{RN}}{{\\text{H}}_2} + {\\text{CHC}}{{\\text{l}}_3} + 3{\\text{KOH}}({\\text{ alc }}) \\to $<br>${\\text{R}} - {\\text{N }}\\mathop {\\text{ = }}\\limits^ \\to  {\\text{ C}} + 3{\\text{KCl}} + 3{{\\text{H}}_2}{\\text{O}}$<br>Both statements are correct, but it is not correct explanation. Therefore, option (2) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(29045,'chemistry',67,1075,2565,'Suitable reaction condition for preparation of methyl phenyl ether is','[{"text": "$\\\\mathrm{PhO}^{-} \\\\mathrm{Na}^{+}, \\\\mathrm{MeOH}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{Ph}-\\\\mathrm{Br}, \\\\mathrm{MeO}^{-} \\\\mathrm{Na}^{+}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{PhO}^{-} \\\\mathrm{Na}^{+}, \\\\mathrm{MeBr}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Benzene, MeBr", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Methyl phenyl ether or anisole  $\\left(\\mathrm{C}_{6} \\mathrm{H}_{5}-\\mathrm{O}-\\mathrm{CH}_{3}\\right)$ can be obtained by reacting phenolate ions and methyl bromide.<br>$\\mathop {{{\\rm{C}}_6}{{\\rm{H}}_5}}\\limits_{{\\rm{Phenol}}}  - {\\rm{OH}} + {\\rm{NaOH}} \\to \\mathop {{{\\rm{C}}_6}{{\\rm{H}}_5} - \\mathop {\\rm{O}}\\limits^ -  {\\rm{N}}{{\\rm{a}}^ + }}\\limits_{{\\rm{Sodium}}\\,\\,{\\rm{phenoxide}}} $<br>$\\xrightarrow{{{\\text{C}}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{3}}}{\\text{Br}}}}\\mathop {{{\\text{C}}_6}{{\\text{H}}_5} - {\\text{O}} - {\\text{C}}{{\\text{H}}_3}}\\limits_{{\\text{ Methyl phenylether }}} $<br>So, the correct option is (3).',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2023,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(29046,'chemistry',68,1117,2676,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> First four aliphatic monocarboxylic acids are colourless.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Carboxylic acids with more than five carbon atoms are insoluble in water.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Carboxylic acids are soluble in water due to hydrogen bonding between the $-\\mathrm{COOH}$ group and water molecules. But increased hydrophobicity from larger alkyl (-R) groups, especially beyond five carbon atoms, reduces solubility. So the option (2) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(29048,'chemistry',69,1174,2810,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Acetamide reacts with $\\mathrm{Br}_{2}$ in presence of methanolic $\\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\mathrm{ONa}$ to form methyl $\\mathrm{N}$-methylcarbamate.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Methyl isocyanate is formed as an intermediate which reacts with methanol to form methyl N-methylcarbamate.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Hofmann bromamide reaction is a reaction in which amides are converted to amines with $\\mathrm{Br}_{2}$ in the presence of a base. The reaction proceeds through the formation of isocyanate as an intermediate. It then forms amine upon hydrolysis. Here, the base is methanolic $\\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\mathrm{ONa}$. Therefore, option (1) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(29049,'chemistry',67,1077,2572,'The correct order for acidity of the following hydroxyl compound is<br> <br>Choose the correct answer from the options given below:','[{"text": "E $>$ C $>$ D $>$ A $>$ B", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "E $>$ D $>$ C $>$ B $>$ A", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "D $>$ E $>$ C $>$ A $>$ B", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "C $>$ E $>$ D $>$ B $>$ A", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Among the given compounds, p – nitrophenol is the most acidic as it consists of   $-\\mathrm{NO}_{2}$   group which is an EWG.<br>Thus, phenol is less acidic than p – nitrophenol. The $\\left(-\\mathrm{OCH}_{3}\\right)$   groups exhibits $ + \\,{\\rm{I}}$ effect and is an electron donating group.Thereby, it decreases the acidity of phenol. 2 methylpropan –2 –ol   $\\left(\\left(\\mathrm{CH}_{3}\\right)_{3} \\mathrm{COH}\\right)$  is the least acidic due to +I effect of three alkyl groups. Whereas, methanol   $\\left(\\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\mathrm{OH}\\right)$  is more acidic than  $\\left(\\mathrm{CH}_{3}\\right)_{3} \\mathrm{COH}$  as only one alkyl group is present in it.Thus, option (1) shows the correct order. Alcohols are less acidic than phenols.',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',2023,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','chemistry/67_29049_question_1.png'),
(29050,'chemistry',68,1117,2676,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Acetic acid in vapour state shows a molecular mass of 120 .<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> It undergoes intermolecular hydrogen bonding.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'<img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/68_29050_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />  Acetic acid has actual molecular mass of $60 \\mathrm{~g} /$ mol. But due to intermolecular hydrogen bonding and dimerization, its apparent molecular weight is $120 \\mathrm{~g} / \\mathrm{mol}$. So the option (1) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(29051,'chemistry',67,1073,2559,'Incorrect method of preparation of alcohols from the following is','[{"text": "reaction of ketone with RMgBr followed by hydrolysis", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "reaction of alkyl halide with aqueous NaOH .", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "ozonolysis of alkene", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "hydroboration-oxidation of alkene", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Ozonolysis of alkenes does not produce alcohols, instead it gives aldehyde, ketone & carboxylic acid. Rest of the given reactions produce alcohols.',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',2023,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(29052,'chemistry',68,1117,2676,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Hydrogen bonding in carboxylic acid is stronger than alcohols.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Highly branched carboxylic acids are more acidic than unbranched acids.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Highly branched carboxylic acids exhibit reduced acidity compared to unbranched counterparts due to the electron donating $+\\mathrm{I}$ effect of alkyl groups. This diminishes the negative charge on the carboxylate ion and hinders effective solvation, resulting in weaker proton release and decreased acidity. So the option (3) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(29056,'chemistry',68,1118,2677,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> More electropositive element has greater electron donating effect.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> $\\mathrm{Me}_{3} \\mathrm{SiCH}_{2} \\mathrm{COOH}$ is more acidic than $\\mathrm{Me}_{3} \\mathrm{CCH}_{2} \\mathrm{COOH}$.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Silicon is electropositive as compared to carbon and therefore, has a larger electrondonating $(+\\mathrm{I})$ effect. <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/68_29056_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />   This increases the electron density on oxygen atoms of carboxylate group. This means that<br>$\\mathrm{Me}_{3} \\mathrm{CCH}_{2} \\mathrm{COOH}$ is more acidic. So the option<br>(3) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(29057,'chemistry',69,1176,2821,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Aniline hydrogen sulphate, on heating, forms p-aminosulphonic acid.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> The sulphonic acid group is electronwithdrawing.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'<img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/69_29057_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />  In aniline hydrogen sulfate, the amino group\'s directing effect favors para substitution, yielding p-aminobenzenesulfonic acid (sulphanilic acid) as the major product upon heating with sulfuric acid. Therefore, the option (2) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(29059,'chemistry',67,1077,2578,'Compound  \' B \'  is','[{"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'<img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/67_29059_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',2023,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','chemistry/67_29059_question_1.png'),
(29060,'chemistry',68,1118,2685,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Carboxylic acids do not give characteristic reactions of carbonyl group.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Carboxylic acids exist as cyclic dimers in solid, liquid and even in vapour state.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Carboxylic acids, being resonance stabilized compounds, exhibit a conjugated structure with the carbonyl group $(-\\mathrm{C}=\\mathrm{O})$ and hydroxyl group $(-\\mathrm{OH})$. They have a resonance hybrid with distributed double bond character, distinguishing them from simple carbonyl compounds. Carboxylic acids exists as dimer even in vapour state upto some extent. So the option (2) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(29061,'chemistry',69,1176,2816,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> $\\mathrm{Me}_{3} \\mathrm{~N}$ reacts with $\\mathrm{BF}_{3}$ whereas $\\mathrm{Ph}_{3} \\mathrm{~N}$ does not.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> The electron pair on nitrogen atom in ${\\text{N}}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{3}}}$  is delocalised in the benzene ring and is not available to boron in $\\mathrm{BF}_{3}$.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Trimethylamine, with three methyl groups, is a strong Lewis base, readily forming a complex with $\\mathrm{BF}_{3}$. In contrast, triphenylamine, containing phenyl groups, is less basic due to resonance and less reactive towards Lewis acids like $\\mathrm{BF}_{3}$ Hence, option (1) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(29062,'chemistry',67,1077,2573,'<br>Product [X] formed in the above reaction is','[{"text": "", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{H}_{3} \\\\mathrm{C}-\\\\mathrm{CH}=\\\\mathrm{CH}-\\\\mathrm{CH}_{3}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{H}_{3} \\\\mathrm{C}-\\\\mathrm{CH}_{2}-\\\\mathrm{CH}=\\\\mathrm{CH}_{2}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'<img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/67_29062_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',2023,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','chemistry/67_29062_question_1.png'),
(29063,'chemistry',68,1118,2680,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> $\\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\mathrm{COOH}$ but not $\\mathrm{HCOOH}$ can be halogenated in presence of red $\\mathrm{P}$ and $\\mathrm{Cl}_{2}$.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Both formic acid and $\\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\mathrm{COOH}$ are highly soluble in water.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'It is HVZ reaction, in which carboxylic acids are halogenated using red $\\mathrm{P}$ and halogen, to replace $\\alpha$ - hydrogen. Formic acid lacks an $\\alpha$ - carbon and cannot be halogenated this way. Aliphatic carboxylic acids up to four carbon atoms are water miscible due to hydrogen bonding with water.<br>${\\text{C}}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{3}}}{\\text{COOH}}\\xrightarrow[{{\\text{ (ii) }}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{2}}}{\\text{O}}}]{{{\\text{ (i) C}}{{\\text{l}}_{\\text{2}}}{\\text{/}}\\,\\,{\\text{Red}}\\,\\,{\\text{P}}}}{\\text{ClC}}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{2}}}{\\text{COOH}}$<br>So the option (2) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(29065,'chemistry',69,1176,2821,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Controlled nitration of aniline at low temperature mainly gives m-nitroaniline.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> In acidic medium, $-\\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ group gets converted into $\\mathrm{m}$-directing group.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'<img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/69_29065_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />    Protonation of aniline under acidic conditions forms the anilinium ion, a deactivating group that directs electrophilic substitutions to the meta position, leading to the major product $\\mathrm{m-}$ nitroaniline during nitration.Thus, option (1) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(29066,'chemistry',67,1077,2578,'<br>In the above reaction, left hand side and right hand side rings are named as \' A \' and \' B \', respectively. They undergo ring expansion. The correct statement for this process is','[{"text": "Finally both rings will become six membered each.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Ring expansion can go upto seven membered rings.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Only ring   \' A \'   will become 6 membered.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Finally both rings will become five membered each.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'<img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/67_29066_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',2023,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','chemistry/67_29066_question_1.png'),
(29067,'chemistry',68,1118,2677,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Pure acetic acid is converted into ice like solid called glacial acetic acid.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Acetic acid is stronger than $\\mathrm{HCOOH}$.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'The melting point of pure anhydrous acetic acid is $17^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C}$. Crystal structure below this temperature looks like ice ( glacial) . So, pure acetic acid is known as glacial acetic acid.<br>Formic acid is stronger than acetic acid  <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/68_29067_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />  Presence of $\\mathrm{CH}_{3}$ group in acetate ion shows $+\\mathrm{I}$ effect and destabilizes acetate ion. Thus, formate ion is more stable than acetate ion or $\\mathrm{HCOOH}$ loses proton more easily than $\\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\mathrm{COOH}$. So the option (3) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(29068,'chemistry',69,1177,2827,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Benzene diazonium salt on boiling with water forms phenol.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> $\\mathrm{C}-\\mathrm{N}$ bond is polar.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'This is the substitution or replacement reaction of benzene diazonium salt, where nitrogen is lost as $\\mathrm{N}_{2}$ and different groups are introduced in its place. <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/69_29068_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />  Therefore, option (2) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(29136,'chemistry',69,1176,2823,'In the reaction given<br> <br>‘ B ’ is','[{"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'<img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/69_29136_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',2023,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','chemistry/69_29136_question_1.png'),
(29071,'chemistry',68,1118,2677,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Fluoroacetic acid is stronger than chloroacetic acid.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Carboxylic acids are weak acids and turn blue litmus red.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'The stronger -I effect of fluorine increases the stability of the fluoroacetate ion, enhancing its acidic nature compared to the chlorinated counterpart. So the option (2) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(29073,'chemistry',70,1258,3073,'DNA multiplication is called :','[{"text": "transcription", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "transduction", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "replication", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "translation", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'The genetic information of the cell is contained in the sequence of the bases, A T, G and $\\mathrm{C}$ in the DNA molecule. When a cell divides, DNA molecules replicate and make exact copies of themselves so that each daughter cell will have DNA identical to that of the parent cell.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,'NCERT Exemplar','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(29074,'chemistry',67,1077,2573,'The major product for the following reaction is','[{"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Both $ - $ OH and  $ - $ SH are nucleophilic, but  <br>$ - $ SH is a better nucleophile than $ - $ OH in polar protic solvent.<br><img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/67_29074_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',2023,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','chemistry/67_29074_question_1.png'),
(29075,'chemistry',68,1115,2672,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Carbon-oxygen bond length in<br>formic acid are  1.23 $ \\mathop {\\rm{A}}^{\\circ}  $ and   1.36 $ \\mathop {\\rm{A}}^{\\circ}  $<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Formic acid gives rise to nucleophilic addition reaction.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Resonance in the carboxylate ion limits nucleophilic additions, impeding reactions with $\\mathrm{HCN}$, sodium bisulfite and hydroxylamine that aim to replace the carboxylic oxygen. So the option (3) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(29076,'chemistry',69,1174,2810,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> When acetamide reacts with $\\mathrm{NaOH}$ and $\\mathrm{Br}_{2}$ methyl amine is formed. <br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> The reaction occurs through intermediate formation of isocyanate.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'<img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/69_29076_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />  overall reaction is<br>$\\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\mathrm{CONH}_{2}+\\mathrm{Br}_{2}+4 \\mathrm{NaOH} \\rightarrow$<br>$\\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\mathrm{NH}_{2}+\\mathrm{Na}_{2} \\mathrm{CO}_{3}+2 \\mathrm{NaBr}+2 \\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O}$<br>The Hoffmann bromamide degradation of acetamide involves the formation of methyl isocyanate as an intermediate, which, upon hydrolysis, yields methylamine and sodium carbonate.. Thus, option (2) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(29077,'chemistry',67,1083,2591,'Read the Assertion and Reason carefully and mark correct option.<br><br><strong>Assertion:</strong> Order of acidic nature of the following compounds is $\\mathrm{A}>\\mathrm{B}>\\mathrm{C}$.<br>https://res.cloudinary.com/doe6oiv2h/image/upload/v1764949323/preptracker/questions/q_29077_col1_1764949322834.png<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Fluoro is a stronger electron withdrawing group than chloro group.','[{"text": "Assertion is false but Reason is true.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is true but Reason is false.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Electron withdrawing groups increase acidic nature. Electron withdrawing nature of   $\\mathrm{F}>\\mathrm{Cl}$. But in compound  B, F  is at para position and cannot increase the acidic nature as much as chlorine can do at the ortho position. Thus, assertion is true, but reason is not the correct explanation.<br>So, the correct option is (2).',NULL,'Hard','Assertion and Reason',2023,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(29137,'chemistry',67,1073,2559,'<br>B and C are, respectively,<br> <br>','[{"text": "B is I and C is II", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "B is II and C is I", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "I in both cases", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "II in both cases", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{LiAlH}_{4}}}$ reduces both ester and ketone groups whereas ${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{NaBH}_{4}}}$ reduces only ketone but not ester.<br><img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/67_29137_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','chemistry/67_29137_question_1.png'),
(29078,'chemistry',68,1118,2678,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Carboxylic acids on heating with alcohols in presence of dehydrating agent gives esters.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Ethanol reacts with excess of sulphuric acid at $170^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C}$ and forms esters.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Esterification does not take place between ethanol and excess of $\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{SO}_{4}$ at $170^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C}$. On heating, ethanol with $\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{SO}_{4}$ at $170^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C}$, the ethyl hydrogen sulphate formed readily loses<br>$\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{SO}_{4}$ to give ethylene.<br>${{\\text{C}}_{\\text{2}}}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{5}}}{\\text{OH  +  }}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{2}}}{\\text{S}}{{\\text{O}}_{\\text{4}}} \\to \\mathop {{{\\text{C}}_{\\text{2}}}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{5}}}{\\text{HS}}{{\\text{O}}_{\\text{4}}}}\\limits_{{\\text{ (Un}}\\,{\\text{stable}}\\,\\,{\\text{at}}\\,\\,{\\text{170}}^\\circ {\\text{C ) }}}  \\to {{\\text{C}}_{\\text{2}}}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{4}}}$<br>So the option (3) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL);
