(29096,'chemistry',67,1078,2580,'Solution of   $\\mathrm{CrO}_{5}$  in amyl alcohol has a colour.','[{"text": "orange-red", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "green", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "blue", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "yellow", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'A solution of   $\\mathrm{CrO}_{5}$  in amyl alcohol has a blue colour.',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',2023,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(29097,'chemistry',68,1116,2673,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Ketones can be converted into acids by haloform reaction.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Addition of Grignard reagents to dry ice followed by hydrolysis gives ketones.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Grignard reagents reacts with dry ice followed by hydrolysis to form carboxylic acids instead of ketones, as the intermediate carboxylate is further hydrolyzed into the carboxylic acid <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/68_29097_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />   So the option (3) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(29460,'chemistry',69,1176,2823,'<br> The final product \'C\' in the above reactions is','[{"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'<img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/69_29460_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','chemistry/69_29460_question_1.png'),
(29098,'chemistry',69,1176,2821,'Read the Statement - A and Statement - B carefully and mark the correct option.<div class="matching-question"><div class="column-left">Sulphanilic acid gives esterification test for carboxyl group.</div><div class="column-right">Sulphanilic acid gives red colour in Lassaigne\'s test for extra element detection.</div></div>','[{"text": "Both Statement A and Statement B are correct.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Statement A and Statement B are incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Statement A is incorrect but Statement B is correct.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Statement A is correct but Statement B is  incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'<img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/69_29098_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' /> <br>Sulphanilic acid does not contain any - COOH and cannot give a estirification test for carboxylic acid. Thus, statement A is incorrect. Sulphanilic acid gives a blood red colour in Lassaigne\'s test due to the formation of the complex  $\\left[\\mathrm{Fe}(\\mathrm{SCN})_{3}\\right]$. This is because it contains N, S and C. Thus, statement B is correct.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ type-2',2023,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(29099,'chemistry',70,1258,3072,'The reason for double helical structure of DNA is due to the operation of:','[{"text": "Electrostatic attractions", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Hydrogen bonding", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Dipole-Dipole interactions", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Vander Waal\'s forces", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Hydrogen bonding.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2014,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(29101,'chemistry',68,1118,2679,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Carboxylic acids are reduced to alkanes on reaction with $\\mathrm{HI}$ in presence of red phosphorus.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Melting point of carboxylic acids shows a regular pattern.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Carboxylic acids can be reduced to alkanes by reacting with $\\mathrm{HI}$ in the presence of red phosphorus. Initially this forms an acyl iodide $(\\mathrm{RCOCl})$ and subsequently undergoes further reduction to yield an alkane with the removal of carbonyl oxygen and the generation of iodide ion $\\left(\\mathrm{I}^{-}\\right)$.<br>The overall reaction can be represented as:<br>$\\mathrm{RCOCl}+\\mathrm{HI} \\rightarrow \\mathrm{RH}+\\mathrm{ICl}$<br>Melting point of carboxylic acids are not in a regular order. So the option (3) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(29102,'chemistry',69,1174,2810,'The major product formed in the following reaction is','[{"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'<img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/69_29102_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',2023,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','chemistry/69_29102_question_1.png'),
(29103,'chemistry',67,1083,2591,'The correct order of $\\mathrm{pK}_{\\mathrm{a}}$ values for the following compound is <br>','[{"text": "A $>$ B $>$ C $>$ D", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "B $>$ A $>$ D $>$ C", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "B $>$ D $>$ A $>$ C", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "C $>$ A $>$ D $>$ B", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Higher the value of  ${{\\text{K}}_{\\text{a}}}$  or lower the value of  $\\mathrm{pK}_{\\mathrm{a}}$, stronger will be the acid.<br>Presence of electron donating groups reduces acidic nature of phenol. Presence of electron withdrawing group increases the acidic strength of phenol. Thus, order of acidic strength<br><img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/67_29103_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' /> <br>  Hence, $\\mathrm{pK}_{\\mathrm{a}}$ order: $\\mathrm{B}>\\mathrm{D}>\\mathrm{A}>\\mathrm{C}$.',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',2023,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','chemistry/67_29103_question_1.png'),
(29104,'chemistry',70,1247,3036,'Accumulation of which of the following molecules in the muscles occurs as a result of vigorous exercise?','[{"text": "Pyruvic acid", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Glucose", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Glycogen", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "L - lactic acid", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'<img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/70_29104_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />   During vigorous exercise sufficient oxygen is not available to meet the energy demand so, energy is derived through conversion of pyruvic acid to lactic acid.',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',2015,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(29105,'chemistry',68,1118,2677,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Ortho substituted benzoic acids are generally stronger acids than benzoic acids.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Increased strength is due to ortho effect.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Ortho substituted benzoic acids exhibit enhanced acidity compared to benzoic acid, independent of the substituent\'s electronic nature, due to the ortho effect. So the option (1) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(29106,'chemistry',69,1174,2810,'The major products A and B from the following reactions are','[{"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'<img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/69_29106_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',2023,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','chemistry/69_29106_question_1.png'),
(29107,'chemistry',67,1083,2601,'An organic compound  \' A \'  with empirical formula  $\\mathrm{C}_{6} \\mathrm{H}_{6} \\mathrm{O}$  gives sooty flame on burning. Its reaction with bromine solution in low polarity solvent results in high yield of B. B is','[{"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Aromatic compound burns with sooty flame.<br>So, $\\mathrm{C}_{6} \\mathrm{H}_{6} \\mathrm{O}$ is phenol.<br><img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/67_29107_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2023,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(29164,'chemistry',70,1245,3026,'Consider the following statements.<br>I. Carbohydrates, proteins, nucleic acids and lipids all are biomolecules.<br>II. Biomolecules are the organic compounds.<br>III. Plants and animals are made up of biomolecules.<br>Select the correct statements and mark the correct option','[{"text": "Only I", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Only II", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Only III", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "I, II and III", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'All statements are correct.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ type-2',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(29108,'chemistry',68,1118,2685,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Methanoic acid reduces Tollens\' reagent and Fehling solution.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Methanoic acid occurs in a variety of biting and stinging plants and insects.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Methanoic acid is a potent reducing agent because of the presence of the aldehyde group. It has the capability to reduce compounds like Tollen\'s reagent and Fehling\'s solution.<br>$\\begin{aligned}\\mathrm{HCOOH} & +\\underset{\\substack{\\text { Tollen\'s } \\\\\\text { reagent }}}{\\mathrm{Ag}_{2} \\mathrm{O}} \\xrightarrow{\\text { warm }} \\\\\\mathrm{CO}_{2}+\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O} & +\\underset{\\substack{\\text { silver } \\\\\\text { mirror }}}{2 \\mathrm{Ag}} \\downarrow\\end{aligned}$<br>$\\begin{aligned}\\mathrm{HCOOH} & +\\underset{\\substack{\\text { Fehling } \\\\\\text { solution }}}{2 \\mathrm{CuO}} \\xrightarrow{\\Delta} \\\\& \\mathrm{CO}_{2}+\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O}+\\underset{\\text { Red ppt }}{\\mathrm{Cu}_{2} \\mathrm{O} \\downarrow}\\end{aligned}$<br>So the option (2) is correct',NULL,'Hard','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(29109,'chemistry',70,1253,3054,'Consider the following sequence for aspartic acid: The (pI isoelectric point) of aspartic acid is:','[{"text": "5.74", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "3.65", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "2.77", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "1.88", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'In given reaction sequence<br>$\\mathrm{\\mathrm{pI}=\\dfrac{\\mathrm{pK}_{1}+\\mathrm{pK}_{\\mathrm{R}}}{2}=\\dfrac{1.88+3.65}{2}=2.77}$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',2016,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','chemistry/70_29109_question_1.png'),
(29111,'chemistry',67,1077,2576,'When a tertiary alcohol \' A \' ${\\mathrm{\\left(\\mathrm{C}_{4} \\mathrm{H}_{10} \\mathrm{O}\\right)}}$ reacts with ${\\mathrm{20 \\%\\, \\mathrm{H}_{3} \\mathrm{PO}_{4}}}$ at 358 K , it gives a compound \'B\' ${\\mathrm{\\left(\\mathrm{C}_{4} \\mathrm{H}_{8}\\right)}}$ as a major product. The IUPAC name of the compound \' ${\\mathrm{B}}$ \' is','[{"text": "But-1-ene", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "But-2-ene", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Cyclobutane", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "2-Methylpropene", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'2-Methyl-2-propanol is the ${\\mathrm{3^{\\circ}}}$ alcohol of formula ${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{C}_{4} \\mathrm{H}_{10} \\mathrm{O}}}$.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2024,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(29112,'chemistry',68,1117,2676,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Ethanoic acid is called glacial acetic acid.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Ethanoic acid is a main constituent of vinegar.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Ethanoic acid freezes at unusually high temperature (melting point $289 \\mathrm{~K}$ ) forming ice like crystals. For this reason, water free ethanoic acid obtained by melting of the crystals is called glacial acetic acid. So the option (2) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(29113,'chemistry',69,1177,2829,'Match Column I with Column II<br> is reacted with reagents in Column I to form products in Column II.<br> <br>Choose the correct answer from the options given below:<div class="matching-question"></div>','[{"text": "A - R,\\tB - P,\\tC - S,\\t\\tD - Q", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "A - S,\\tB - R,\\tC - Q ,\\tD - P", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "A - R,\\tB - P,\\tC - Q ,\\tD - S", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "A - P,\\tB - R,\\tC - S,\\t\\tD - Q", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'<img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/69_29113_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />  ${\\text{A}} - {\\text{R}},{\\text{B}} - {\\text{P}},{\\text{C}} - {\\text{S}},{\\text{D}} - {\\text{Q}}$<br>So, the correct option is (1).',NULL,'Hard','Match the following',2023,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','chemistry/69_29113_question_1.png'),
(29114,'chemistry',70,1254,3064,'Observation of "Ruhemann\'s purple" is a confirmatory test for the presence of :','[{"text": "Reducing sugar", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Starch", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Protein", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Cupricion", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Ninhydrin is often used to detect $\\mathrm{\\alpha}$-Amino acids and also free amino and carboxylic acid groups on proteins and peptides. Ninhydrin produce blue or purple pigment, sometimes called Ruhemann\'s purple.',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',2016,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(29115,'chemistry',67,1077,2577,'PCC is','[{"text": "${\\\\mathrm{\\\\mathrm{K}_{2} \\\\mathrm{Cr}_{2} \\\\mathrm{O}_{7}+}}$ Pyridine", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\mathrm{\\\\mathrm{CrO}_{3}+\\\\mathrm{CHCl}_{3}}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\mathrm{\\\\mathrm{CrO}_{3}+\\\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\\\mathrm{SO}_{4}}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "A complex of chromium trioxide with pyridine +HCl", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'PCC is pyridinium chlorochromate<br>${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{C}_{5} \\mathrm{H}_{5} \\stackrel{\\oplus}{\\mathrm{N}} \\mathrm{HCrO}_{3} \\mathrm{Cl}^{-}}}$(a complex of pyridine, ${\\mathrm{\\left.\\mathrm{CrO}_{3}, \\mathrm{HCl}\\right)}}$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2024,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(29116,'chemistry',68,1118,2677,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Substituents affect the stability of the conjugate base and acidity of carboxylic acids.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Electron withdrawing groups decrease the acidity of carboxylic acids.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Electron withdrawing groups enhance carboxylic acid acidity by stabilizing the conjugate base through inductive and resonance effects, facilitating charge delocalization and strengthening the acid\'s proton donating ability. So the option (3) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(29118,'chemistry',67,1078,2579,'Which one of the following alcohols reacts instantaneously with Lucas reagent?','[{"text": "${\\\\mathrm{\\\\mathrm{CH}_{3}-\\\\mathrm{CH}_{2}-\\\\mathrm{CH}_{2}-\\\\mathrm{CH}_{2} \\\\mathrm{OH}}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Tertiary alcohols <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/67_29118_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />  react instantaneously with Lucas reagent and give immediate turbidity. Reactivity towards Lucas reagent:<br>$\\begin{array}{*{20}{l}}\n{{\\rm{3}}^\\circ \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu}  > {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{\\rm{2}}^\\circ {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} \\,\\,\\,{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu}  > {\\mkern 1mu} \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{\\rm{1}}^\\circ {\\mkern 1mu} {\\rm{alcohols}}}\\\\\n{{\\rm{Immediately}}\\,\\,\\,\\,{\\rm{after}}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\rm{after}}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} }\\\\\n\\begin{array}{l}\n{\\mkern 1mu} \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{\\rm{5}}\\,{\\rm{min}}{\\mkern 1mu} \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{\\rm{turbidity}}\\\\\n\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{\\rm{heating}}\n\\end{array}\n\\end{array}$<br>So, the correct option is 4.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2024,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(29119,'chemistry',70,1253,3053,'Among the following, the essential amino acid is :','[{"text": "Valine", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Alanine", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Serine", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Aspartic acid", "isCorrect": false}]',0,NULL,NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2017,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(29120,'chemistry',68,1117,2676,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Formic acid usually exists as a dimer but hydrogen fluoride exists as a polymer.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> In case of $\\mathrm{HCOOH}$, intramolecular hydrogen bond is present, but in HF, intermolecular hydrogen bond is present.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'<img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/68_29120_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />  Formic acid\'s bent structure promotes cyclic dimer formation via intermolecular hydrogen bonding. On the other hand, the linear arrangement of $\\mathrm{HF}$ molecules facilitates extensive intermolecular hydrogen bonding, resulting in a polymer-like structure. So the option (3) iscorrect.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(29121,'chemistry',69,1176,2819,'Isomeric amines with molecular formula  $\\mathrm{C}_{8} \\mathrm{H}_{11} \\mathrm{~N}$  give the following tests<br>Isomer $(\\mathrm{P}) \\Rightarrow$ Can be prepared by Gabriel phthalimide synthesis<br>Isomer $(\\mathrm{Q}) \\Rightarrow$ Reacts with Hinsberg\'s reagent to give solid insoluble in NaOH<br>Isomer $(\\mathrm{R}) \\Rightarrow$ Reacts with HONO followed by  β – naphthol in NaOH to give red dye. Isomers P, Q and R, respectively are','[{"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'P  can be prepared by Gabriel pthalimide synthesis so, it is aliphatic primary amine.<br>Q reacts with Hinsberg\'s reagent to give solid insoluble in NaOH so it must be $2^{\\circ}$ amine.  <br>R  should be sterically free at ortho position.',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',2023,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(29122,'chemistry',67,1073,2559,'Identify the correct reagents that would bring about the following transformation.','[{"text": "(I) ${\\\\mathrm{\\\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\\\mathrm{O} / \\\\mathrm{H}^{+}}}$<br>(II) ${\\\\mathrm{\\\\mathrm{CrO}_{3}}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "(I) ${\\\\mathrm{\\\\mathrm{BH}_{3}}}$<br>(II) ${\\\\mathrm{\\\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\\\mathrm{O}_{2} / \\\\stackrel{\\\\ominus}{\\\\mathrm{O}} \\\\mathrm{H}}}$<br>(III) PCC", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "(I) ${\\\\mathrm{\\\\mathrm{BH}_{3}}}$<br>(II) ${\\\\mathrm{\\\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\\\mathrm{O}_{2} / \\\\stackrel{\\\\ominus}{\\\\mathrm{O}} \\\\mathrm{H}}}$<br>(III) alk. ${\\\\mathrm{\\\\mathrm{KMnO}_{4}}}$<br>(IV) ${\\\\mathrm{\\\\mathrm{H}_{3} \\\\mathrm{O}^{\\\\oplus}}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "(I) ${\\\\mathrm{\\\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\\\mathrm{O} / \\\\mathrm{H}^{+}}}$<br>(II) PCC", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'<img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/67_29122_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2024,'NEET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','chemistry/67_29122_question_1.png'),
(29123,'chemistry',68,1118,2682,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Nitration of benzoic acid gives $m-$ nitrobenzoic acid.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Carboxyl group increases the electron density at the meta-position.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'The carboxyulic acid group is an electron withdrawing group. It decreases the electron density from ortho and para position and does not increase the electron density from meta position. The decreasing effect is less at meta position. This directs the incoming group to the meta position. So the option (3) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(29124,'chemistry',69,1174,2809,'In the reaction given below :<br> <br>The product \' X \' is','[{"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'<img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/69_29124_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',2023,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','chemistry/69_29124_question_1.png'),
(29125,'chemistry',70,1246,3028,'Which of the following compounds will behave as a reducing sugar in an aqueous $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{KOH}}$ solution?','[{"text": "", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'(i) Ester in presence of aqueous $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{KOH}}$ solution give $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{S}_{\\mathrm{N}}{ }^{\\mathrm{AE}}}$ reaction so following reaction takes place <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/70_29125_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />   (ii) In above compound in presence of Aq. $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{KOH}}$ $\\mathrm{\\left(\\mathrm{S}_{\\mathrm{N}}{ }^{\\mathrm{AE}}\\right.}$ ) reaction takes place $\\mathrm{\\& \\propto-}$ Hydroxy carbonyl compound is formed which gives positive Tollen\'s test.<br>So this compound behaves as reducing sugar in an aqueous $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{KOH}}$ solution.',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',2017,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(29127,'chemistry',68,1118,2677,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> ${\\text{m}}$ -Chlorobenzoic acid is a stronger acid than p-chlorobenzoic acid.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> In $\\mathrm{m}$-chlorobenzoic acid both -I effect and $+\\mathrm{R}$ effect of $\\mathrm{Cl}$ operate but in $\\mathrm{p-}$ chlorobenzoic acid only $+\\mathrm{R}$ effect of $\\mathrm{Cl}$ operates.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'In p-chlorobenzoic acid, both the electronreleasing $+\\mathrm{R}$ and electron withdrawing $-\\mathrm{I}$ effects are in operation, but in m-chlorobenzoic acid, only the -I effect operates. Consequently, $\\mathrm{m}$-chlorobenzoic acid is a stronger acid compared to p-chlorobenzoic acid. The -I effect predominates in $\\mathrm{m}$-chlorobenzoic acid, enhancing its acidity. So the option (3) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(29128,'chemistry',69,1176,2822,'o – Phenylenediamine $\\begin{array}{*{20}{c}}\n  {\\xrightarrow{{{\\text{HN}}{{\\text{O}}_{\\text{2}}}}}}&{\'{\\text{X}}\'} \\\\ \n  {}&{{\\text{Major}}\\,\\,{\\text{Product}}} \n\\end{array}$<br> \' X \'  is','[{"text": "", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'<img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/69_29128_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />  So, the correct option is (1).',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',2023,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(29129,'chemistry',70,1258,3071,'Which of the following is the correct structure of Adenosine?','[{"text": "", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}]',0,NULL,NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2018,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(29130,'chemistry',67,1073,2559,'The acid-catalyzed hydration of alkenes involves the following three steps:<br>I. Nucleophilic attack of water on carbocation.<br>II. Protonation of alkene to form carbocation by the electrophilic attack of $\\mathrm{H}_{3} \\mathrm{O}^{+}$.<br>III. Deprotonation to form an alcohol.<br> Identify the sequence for the mechanism of reaction in the acid-catalyzed hydration of alkenes.','[{"text": "I, II, and III", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "II, I, and III", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "III, I, and II", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "III, II, and I", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'The reaction of an alkene with water in the presence of acid gives alcohol. This reaction involves three steps in the following sequence:<br>(I) Protonation of alkene to form carbocation by the electrophilic attack of ${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{H}_{3} \\mathrm{O}^{+}}}$.<br>(II) Nucleophilic attack of water on carbocation.<br>(III) Deprotonation to form an alcohol.<br><img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/67_29130_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(29131,'chemistry',68,1118,2677,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Fluoroacetic acid is stronger acid than bromoacetic acid.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Acidity depends upon the electron withdrawing effects of the fluorine and bromine.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Fluorine is more electronegative than bromine. Also fluorine is smaller than bromine. These two factors make fluorine highly electron withdrawing (-I effect), which increases the acidic nature of fluoroacetic acid. So the option (1) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(29132,'chemistry',69,1176,2821,'The incorrect statement regarding the reaction given below is','[{"text": "The reaction occurs at low temperature.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "The electrophile involved in the reaction is  $\\\\mathrm{NO}^{+}$.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "The product \' B \' formed in the above reaction is p -nitroso compound at low temperature.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "B \' is  N  – Nitroso ammonium compound.", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'<img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/69_29132_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',2023,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','chemistry/69_29132_question_1.png'),
(29133,'chemistry',67,1073,2559,'Which of the following statement(s) is/are correct about hydroboration oxidation?','[{"text": "Diborane reacts with alkenes to give trialkyl boranes as an addition product which is oxidized to alcohol by $\\\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\\\mathrm{O}_{2}$ in the presence of aqueous NaOH .", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "The alcohol so formed can also be obtained by the addition of water to the alkene by Markovnikov\'s rule.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Alcohol is obtained in poor yield.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "All of the above", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Hydroboration of alkene is a two-step process in which the first step is addition of ${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{BH}_{3}}}$ on alkene to give trialkyl borane which on further oxidation with ${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O}_{2} / \\mathrm{NaOH}}}$ gives three molecules of alcohols. This reaction follows anti Markovnikov\'s addition.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(29134,'chemistry',70,1250,3047,'Among the following, the incorrect statement is:','[{"text": "Cellulose and amylose has, 1,4-glycosidic linkage", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Lactose contains $\\\\mathrm{\\\\beta-D}$-galactose and $\\\\mathrm{\\\\beta-D-}$ glucose", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Maltose and lactose has, 1,4-glycosidic linkage", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Sucrose and amylose has 1,2-glycosidic linkage", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'In amylose 1,4-glycosidic linkage is present.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2018,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(29135,'chemistry',68,1117,2676,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Carboxylic acids have higher boiling points than alkanes.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Carboxylic acids are resonance hybrids.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Carboxylic acids exhibit higher boiling points due to enhanced hydrogen bonding, leading to increased intermolecular forces compared to hydrocarbons of similar size. So the option (2) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(29138,'chemistry',68,1118,2677,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Formic acid is a stronger acid than benzoic acid.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> $\\mathrm{pK}_{\\mathrm{a}}$ of formic acid is lower than that of benzoic acid.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'<img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/68_29138_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />  The acidity of carboxylic acids is influenced by substituent groups. Electron withdrawing groups increase acidity, whereas electron donating groups decreases acidic nature. Benzoic acid, with a phenyl group as an electron donating group, is less acidic. Formic acid, lacking such groups, is a stronger acid and has a lower $\\mathrm{pKa}$ value. So the option (2) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(29139,'chemistry',69,1176,2823,'In the reaction given below, <br> <br>‘ A ’ is','[{"text": "", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'<img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/69_29139_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',2023,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','chemistry/69_29139_question_1.png'),
(29140,'chemistry',70,1253,3054,'The increasing order of $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{p} K a}$ of the following amino acids in aqueous solution is Gly, Asp, Lys, Arg','[{"text": "Arg $\\\\mathrm{ < }$ Lys $\\\\mathrm{ < }$ Gly $\\\\mathrm{ < }$ Asp", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Asp $\\\\mathrm{ < }$ Gly $\\\\mathrm{ < }$ Lys $\\\\mathrm{ < }$ Arg", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Asp $\\\\mathrm{ < }$ Gly $\\\\mathrm{ < }$ Arg $\\\\mathrm{ < }$ Lys", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Gly $\\\\mathrm{ < }$ Asp $\\\\mathrm{ < }$ Arg $\\\\mathrm{ < }$ Lys", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Order of acidic strength   <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/70_29140_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />  So , ${\\text{p}}{{\\text{K}}_{\\text{a}}}$ value increases as Asp < Gly < Lys < Arg.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2019,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(29141,'chemistry',67,1073,2559,'What are A and B in the following reaction?','[{"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{H}_{2} / \\mathrm{Ni}}}$ reduces both alkene and ketone whereas ${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{LiAlH}_{4}}}$ reduces ketone group but not alkene.<br><img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/67_29141_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','chemistry/67_29141_question_1.png'),
(29142,'chemistry',68,1118,2683,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Both formic acid and oxalic acid decolourize $\\mathrm{KMnO}_{4}$ solution<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Both are easily oxidised to<br>$\\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ and $\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O}$.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Formic acid $(\\mathrm{HCOOH})$ and oxalic acid $\\left(\\mathrm{C}_{2} \\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O}_{4}\\right)$ decolorize potassium permanganate by acting as reducing agents in an acidic medium, causing the reduction of permanganate ions $\\left(\\mathrm{MnO}_{4}^{-}\\right)$to manganese ions $\\left(\\mathrm{Mn}^{2+}\\right)$ and resulting in decolorization. So the option (1) is correct.',NULL,'Hard','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(29143,'chemistry',69,1177,2829,'Compound A from the following reaction sequence is','[{"text": "Benzoic acid", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Phenol", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Aniline", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Salicylic acid", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'<img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/69_29143_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',2023,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','chemistry/69_29143_question_1.png'),
(29144,'chemistry',70,1246,3028,'Fructose is an example of','[{"text": "Pyranose", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Aldohexose", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Ketohexose", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Pentose", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Fructose, $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{C}_{6} \\mathrm{H}_{12} \\mathrm{O}_{6}}$ contains 6 carbon atoms and keto functional group.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2021,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(29145,'chemistry',67,1073,2559,'cannot be prepared by','[{"text": "$\\\\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\\\mathrm{CH}_{2} \\\\mathrm{COCH}_{3}+\\\\mathrm{PhMgX}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{PhCOCH}_{3}+\\\\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\\\mathrm{CH}_{2} \\\\mathrm{MgX}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{PhCOCH}_{2} \\\\mathrm{CH}_{3}+\\\\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\\\mathrm{MgX}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{HCHO}+\\\\mathrm{PhCH}\\\\left(\\\\mathrm{CH}_{3}\\\\right) \\\\mathrm{CH}_{2} \\\\mathrm{MgX}$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'<img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/67_29145_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' /> <br>${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{HCHO} \\xrightarrow{\\text { RMgX }} 1^{\\circ}}}$ alcohol but given question is about ${\\mathrm{3^{\\circ}}}$ alcohol.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','chemistry/67_29145_question_1.png'),
(29146,'chemistry',68,1111,2650,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Dimethylsulphide is commonly used for the reduction of an ozonide of an alkene to get the carbonyl compounds.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> It reduces the ozonide giving water soluble dimethyl sulphoxide and excess of it evaporates.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'We can reduce ozonides, by $\\left(\\mathrm{CH}_{3}\\right)_{2} \\mathrm{~S}$ to yield carbonyl compounds and dimethyl sulphoxide. <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/68_29146_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />  Ozonide<br>So the option (1) is correct.',NULL,'Hard','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(29165,'chemistry',69,1174,2810,'Reaction of propanamide with $\\mathrm{Br}_{2} / \\mathrm{KOH}_{\\text {(aq) }}$ produces:','[{"text": "propylamine", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "ethylnitrile", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "propanenitrile", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "ethylamine.", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'$\\mathop {{\\text{C}}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{3}}}{\\text{C}}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{2}}}{\\text{CON}}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{2}}}}\\limits_{{\\text{Propanamide}}} \\xrightarrow{{{\\text{B}}{{\\text{r}}_2}/{\\text{KO}}{{\\text{H}}_{({\\text{aq}})}}}}\\mathop {{\\text{C}}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{3}}}{\\text{C}}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{2}}}{\\text{N}}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{2}}}}\\limits_{{\\text{Ethylamine}}} $',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',2023,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(29148,'chemistry',67,1074,2563,'Which of the following reactions will yield phenol?<br> <br>','[{"text": "(a), (b), and (c)", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "(a) and (c)", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "(a), (c), and (d)", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "(b), (c), and (d)", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'<img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/67_29148_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' /> <br>This reaction is not possible at 298 K as given in option (4).<br><img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/67_29148_explanation_2.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','chemistry/67_29148_question_1.png'),
(29149,'chemistry',70,1257,3068,'Which vitamins are stored in body for longer time?','[{"text": "Thiamine and Vitamin A", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Vitamin D & Vitamin A", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Ascorbic acid and thiamine", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Ascorbic acid and Vitamin D", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Fat soluble vitamins (A, D, E, K) are stored in body.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2021,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(29151,'chemistry',69,1177,2828,'Consider the following sequence of reactions :','[{"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'<img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/69_29151_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',2023,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','chemistry/69_29151_question_1.png'),
(29152,'chemistry',67,1076,2569,'Select the incorrect option from the following regarding the physical properties of alcohols and phenols.','[{"text": "The boiling points of alcohols and phenols are higher in comparison to hydrocarbons, ethers, haloalkanes and haloarenes of comparable molecular masses.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Ethanol and propane have comparable boiling points but differ only in the molecular masses.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "The - OH group in alcohols and phenols is involved in the intermolecular hydrogen bonding.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "The trend of boiling points is ethanol $>$ methoxymethane > propane.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Ethanol and propane have different boiling points but comparable molecular masses. Ethanol has higher boiling point than propane because of H - bonding.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(29153,'chemistry',68,1113,2657,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> $\\left(\\mathrm{CH}_{3}\\right)_{3} \\mathrm{CCOC}\\left(\\mathrm{CH}_{3}\\right)_{3}$ and acetone can be distinguished by the reaction with $\\mathrm{NaHSO}_{3}$.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> $\\mathrm{HSO}_{3}^{-}$is the nucleophile in bisulphite addition.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'The bulky bisulfite ion is hindered in nucleophilic addition reactions with sterically hindered ketones due to its size and steric hindrance, limiting its accessibility to the carbonyl carbon and impacting reaction efficiency and selectivity. So the option (2) is correct.',NULL,'Hard','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(29154,'chemistry',69,1176,2816,'The correct order in aqueous medium of basic strength in case of methyl substituted amines is','[{"text": "$\\\\mathrm{NH}_{3}>\\\\mathrm{Me}_{3} \\\\mathrm{~N}>\\\\mathrm{MeNH}_{2}>\\\\mathrm{Me}_{2} \\\\mathrm{NH}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{Me}_{2} \\\\mathrm{NH}>\\\\mathrm{Me}_{3} \\\\mathrm{~N}>\\\\mathrm{MeNH}_{2}>\\\\mathrm{NH}_{3}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{Me}_{2} \\\\mathrm{NH}>\\\\mathrm{MeNH}_{2}>\\\\mathrm{Me}_{3} \\\\mathrm{~N}>\\\\mathrm{NH}_{3}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{Me}_{3} \\\\mathrm{~N}>\\\\mathrm{Me}_{2} \\\\mathrm{NH}>\\\\mathrm{MeNH}_{2}>\\\\mathrm{NH}_{3}$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'In aqueous medium basic strength is dependent on electron density on nitrogen as well as solvation of cation formed after accepting $\\mathrm{H}^{+}$  ion. Considering the combined effects of solvation and inductive effect, the order of basic strength of methyl amines in the aqueous state is<br>$\\left(\\mathrm{CH}_{3}\\right)_{2} \\mathrm{NH}>\\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\mathrm{NH}_{2}>\\left(\\mathrm{CH}_{3}\\right)_{3} \\mathrm{~N}>\\mathrm{NH}_{3}$<br>So, the correct option is (3).',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',2023,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(29155,'chemistry',70,1251,3049,'A polysaccharide \' $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{X}}$ \' on boiling with dil $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{SO}_{4}}$ at $\\mathrm{393 \\mathrm{~K}}$ under 2-3 atm pressure yields \' $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{Y}}$ \'. \' $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{Y}}$ \' on treatment with bromine water gives gluconic acid. \' $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{X}}$ \' contains $\\mathrm{\\beta}$-glycosidic linkages only. Compound \' $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{X}}$ \' is :','[{"text": "starch", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "cellulose", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "amylose", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "amylopectin", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Cellulose contains $\\mathrm{\\beta}$-glycosidic linkages only',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',2022,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(29156,'chemistry',67,1077,2578,'Consider the following statements:<br>I. Solubility decreases with an increase in the size of alkyl/aryl groups.<br>II. Alcohols and phenols react with metals like Na, K, etc., to produce corresponding alkoxides or phenoxides.<br>III. Alcohols act as Bronsted acids in the presence of strong acid.<br>IV. Alcohols react both as nucleophiles and electrophiles.<br>The correct statements are','[{"text": "Only II", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "II and III", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "I, II, and IV", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "All of these", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'(i) As the size of alkyl or aryl group increases, solubility of alcohols decreases.<br>(ii) Since alcohol and phenol are acidic in nature, they react with active metal to produce corresponding alkoxide and phenoxide.<br>(iii) Alcohol is weak acid, so in the presence of strong Bronsted acid, it acts as base (not as an acid).<br>(iv) Alcohol react both as ${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{Nu}^{\\oplus}}}$ and ${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{E}^{+}}}$based on the cleavage of bond in their reactions.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ type-2',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(29157,'chemistry',68,1110,2644,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Cyclohexanone exhibits keto-enol tautomerism.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> In cyclohexanone, one form contains the keto group  https://image.cleverb.in/upload/2024/2/1/1706781723930-128.png, while the other contains enolic group $(-\\mathrm{C}=\\mathrm{C}-\\mathrm{OH})$, due to the presence of $\\alpha$-hydrogen in cyclohexanone.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'<img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/68_29157_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />  Cyclohexanone exhibits keto-enol tautomerism because of the $\\alpha$-hydrogens which can shift between the $\\alpha$-carbon and the carbonyl oxygen. The keto form is dominant and more stable tautomer. Therefore the option (1) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(29160,'chemistry',67,1077,2572,'Which of the following statements are correct regarding esterification of alcohols?<br>I. Alcohols react with carboxylic acids, acid chlorides, and acid anhydrides to form esters. <br>II. The reaction with carboxylic acid and acid anhydride is carried out in presence of a small amount of conc. $\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{SO}_{4}$. <br>III. The reaction with acid chloride is carried out in presence of a base (pyridine) to neutralize the side product HCl .<br>IV. The reaction with carboxylic acid and acid anhydride is reversible while with acid chloride, it is irreversible.','[{"text": "I, II and III", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "II and III", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "I, II and IV", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "I, II, III and IV", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{RO}-\\mathrm{H}+\\mathrm{R}-\\mathrm{COOH} \\xrightarrow{\\mathrm{H}^{+}} \\mathrm{RCOOR}+\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O}}}$<br>${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{RO}-\\mathrm{H}+\\mathrm{RCOCl} \\xrightarrow{\\text { Pyridine }} \\mathrm{RCOOR}+\\mathrm{HCl}}}$<br>${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{RO}-\\mathrm{H}+\\left(\\mathrm{RCO}_{2}\\right) \\mathrm{O} \\stackrel{\\mathrm{H}^{+}}{\\rightleftharpoons} \\mathrm{RCOOR}+\\mathrm{RCOOH}}}$<br>The reaction of ${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{R}-\\mathrm{OH}}}$ with acid chloride is irreversible, so to shift the equilibrium to the right hand side, pyridine is used which removes HCl from the reaction mixture.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ type-2',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(29161,'chemistry',68,1113,2656,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Benzaldehyde is less reactive than ethanal towards nucleophilic attack.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> The $+\\mathrm{R}$ effect of phenyl group decreases the electron density on the carbon atom of group in benzaldehyde.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Benzaldehyde\'s reduced reactivity compared to ethanal in nucleophilic addition reactions is due to the electron donating ${\\text{ + R}}$ effect of the phenyl group, which decreases the electrophilicity of the carbonyl carbon in benzaldehyde. So the option (3) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(29162,'chemistry',69,1176,2816,'Match Column I with Column II.<br> <br>Choose the correct answer from the options given below:<div class="matching-question"></div>','[{"text": "A - R,\\tB - S,\\tC - Q ,\\tD - P", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "A - R,\\tB - Q ,\\tC - S,\\t\\tD - P", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "A - P,\\tB - S,\\tC - Q ,\\tD - R", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "A - R,\\tB - Q ,\\tC - P,\\t\\tD - S", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Stronger the base, higher the value of  $K_{b}$  and lower will be the value of $\\mathrm{pK}_{\\mathrm{b}}$.<br>The decreasing order of basic strength of given amines is<br>$\\left(\\mathrm{C}_{2} \\mathrm{H}_{5}\\right)_{2} \\mathrm{NH}>\\left(\\mathrm{C}_{2} \\mathrm{H}_{5}\\right)_{3} \\mathrm{~N}$<br>$>\\mathrm{C}_{2} \\mathrm{H}_{5} \\mathrm{NH}_{2}>\\mathrm{C}_{6} \\mathrm{H}_{5} \\mathrm{NH}_{2}$<br>Hence, the order of  $\\mathrm{pK}_{\\mathrm{b}}$ will be<br><img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/69_29162_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />',NULL,'Hard','Match the following',2023,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','chemistry/69_29162_question_1.png'),
(29163,'chemistry',67,1077,2578,'Which of the following statements is correct?<br>(1)  Alcohols react as nucleophiles in the reactions involving cleavage of the $\\mathrm{O}-\\mathrm{H}$ bond.<br>(2)  Alcohols react as electrophiles in the reactions involving cleavage of the $\\mathrm{O}-\\mathrm{H}$ bond.<br>(3)  Alcohol react as a nucleophile in the reaction involving cleavage of the $\\mathrm{C}-\\mathrm{O}$ bond.<br>(4)  Alcohols react as electrophiles in the reactions involving $\\mathrm{C}-\\mathrm{O}$ bond.','[{"text": "(1) only", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "(1) and (4)", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "(2) and (3)", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "(2) only", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'(I) When the bond between ${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{O}-\\mathrm{H}}}$ is broken, alcohol reacts as nucleophile.<br><img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/67_29163_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' /> <br>(ii) When bond between ${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{C}-\\mathrm{O}}}$ is broken, the alcohol reacts as electrophile.<br><img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/67_29163_explanation_2.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />',NULL,'Medium','MCQ type-2',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(29166,'chemistry',68,1113,2665,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> p-N,N-dimethylaminobenzaldehyde undergoes benzoin condensation.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> The aldehydic $(-\\mathrm{CHO})$ group is meta directing.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'p-N,N-dimethylaminobenzaldehyde is a benzaldehyde with a tertiary amine group at the para position. Thus, it undergoes the benzoin condensation reaction. <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/68_29166_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />    Aldehydic group is an electron withdrawing group<br>(-M effect). Thus, it deactivates the ortho and para positions and is meta directing. So the option (2) is correct.',NULL,'Hard','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(29167,'chemistry',67,1077,2576,'Which of the following statements are correct regarding dehydration of alcohols?<br>I. Conc. $\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{SO}_{4}$ or conc. $\\mathrm{H}_{3} \\mathrm{PO}_{4}$ or anhyd. $\\mathrm{ZnCl}_{2}$ or $\\mathrm{Al}_{2} \\mathrm{O}_{3}$ can be used as dehydrating agent for alcohol.<br>II. The mechanism involves: Protonation $\\rightarrow$ Formation of carbocation $\\rightarrow$ Deprotonation <br>III. Primary alcohols are the easiest to dehydrate.<br>IV. Formation of carbocation is the rate determining step.','[{"text": "I, II, and III", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "II and IV", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "I, II, and IV", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "I, II, III and IV", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Primary alcohol gives primary carbocation which is least stable, so primary alcohols are most difficult to dehydrate. Ease of dehydration ${\\mathrm{3^{\\circ}>2^{\\circ}>1^{\\circ}}}$ alcohols.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ type-2',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(29169,'chemistry',68,1109,2643,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> In sodium formate, both the C-bonds have same value 1.27 $ \\mathop {\\rm{A}}^{\\circ}  $<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Equal bond length is due to the phenomenon of resonance.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Sodium formate, with a linear geometry, displays equal C-O bond lengths  1.27 $ \\mathop {\\rm{A}}^{\\circ}  $ in the formate ion due to its symmetric structure and even electron density distribution between the identical oxygen atoms. So the option (1) is correct.',NULL,'Hard','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(29170,'chemistry',70,1247,3034,'Consider the following statements.<br>I. Pyran is a cyclic organic compound with one oxygen atom and five carbon atom.<br>II. The cyclic structure of glucose is correctly represented by Haworth structure.<br>III. Five membered cyclic structure of glucose is called pyranose structure.<br>Which of the following statement (s) is/are true?','[{"text": "I and III", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "I and II", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Only III", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "I,II and III", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'The six membered cyclic structure of glucose is called pyranose structure $\\mathrm{(\\alpha}$ or $\\mathrm{\\beta)}$, in analogy with pyran. Pyran is a cyclic organic compound with one oxygen atom and five carbon atoms in the ring. The cyclic structure of glucose is correctly represented by Haworth structure.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ type-2',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(29171,'chemistry',67,1077,2577,'PCC (Pyridinium chlorochromate) is a good reagent for which of the following transformations?','[{"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Pyridinium chlorochromate (PCC) is a mild oxidizing agent which oxidizes ${\\mathrm{1^{\\circ}}}$ alcohol and ${\\mathrm{2^{\\circ}}}$ alcohol to aldehyde and ketone, respectively.<br> <br><img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/67_29171_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(29172,'chemistry',68,1113,2665,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Esters which contain $\\alpha$-hydrogens undergo Claisen condensation.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> $\\mathrm{LiAlH}_{4}$ reduction of esters gives acids.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Esters with $\\alpha$-hydrogens exhibit versatility, undergoing nucleophilic substitution to form keto esters and reduction to form alcohols using $\\mathrm{LiAlH}_{4}$. So the option (3) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(29174,'chemistry',70,1254,3062,'Few proteins are given below:<br>$\\begin{gathered}\n  {\\text{I}}{\\text{. Insulin}}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{\\text{II}}{\\text{. Keratin}} \\hfill \\\\\n  {\\text{III}}{\\text{. Albumin}}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{\\text{IV}}{\\text{. Myosin}} \\hfill \\\\ \n\\end{gathered} $<br>The fibrous proteins are','[{"text": "I and II", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "II and IV", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "III and IV", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "All of these", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Some common examples of fibrous proteins are keratin (present in hair, wool, silk), myosin (present in muscles).',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(29175,'chemistry',67,1077,2578,'Regarding the given sequence of reactions:<br> <br>Choose the correct option from the following.<br>(a) Product Z is 2-Methylpropan-2-ol<br>(b) Product Y is acetone<br>(c) Step II is nucleophilic addition followed by hydrolysis<br>(d) Step I is the oxidation of alcohol into corresponding carboxylic acid.','[{"text": "a and b", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "a, b, and c", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "a, b,c, and d", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "b, c, and d", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'<img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/67_29175_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' /> <br>Step - I is oxidation of ${\\mathrm{2^{\\circ}}}$ alcohol, into ketone, not carboxylic acid.<br>So, the correct option is (2).',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','chemistry/67_29175_question_1.png'),
(29177,'chemistry',68,1113,2657,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Aldol condensation can be catalysed both by acids and bases.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> $\\beta  - $  Hydroxy aldehydes or ketones readily undergo acid catalysed dehydration.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Both acids and bases can catalyze aldol condensation by abstracting the $\\alpha$-hydrogen, but bases are generally preferred due to faster reaction rates. Acid catalyzed dehydration of  $\\beta- $ hydroxy aldehydes/ketones yields a stable $\\alpha $  $\\beta $ - unsaturated carbonyl compound. But the reason doesn\'t explain the assertion. So the option (2) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(29178,'chemistry',67,1083,2601,'Product \'P\' is','[{"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'<img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/67_29178_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','chemistry/67_29178_question_1.png'),
(29179,'chemistry',70,1254,3065,'Consider the following statements.<br>I. Tripeptide contains three amino acids linked by three peptide linkage.<br>II. In $\\mathrm{\\beta}$-pleated sheet structure all peptide chain after maximum extension laid side by side and are held together by intramolecular H-bonds.<br>III. The tertiary structure of proteins arises due to further folding of the secondary structure.<br>Select the correct statements and mark the correct option.','[{"text": "Only I", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Only III", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "II and III", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "None of these", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'I. Tripeptide contains three amino acids linked by two peptide linkages.<br>II. In $\\mathrm{\\beta}$-pleated sheet structure all peptide chains after maximum extension laid side by side and are held together by intermolecular H-bonds.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ type-2',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(29181,'chemistry',68,1113,2665,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Mixture of benzaldehyde and acetaldehyde in hot alkaline medium gives cinnamaldehyde.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Benzaldehyde is strong electrophile than acetaldehyde.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'<img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/68_29181_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />  Claisen-Schmidt condensation reaction.<br>So the option (1) is correct.',NULL,'Hard','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(29182,'chemistry',67,1083,2597,'Find out the major product of the given reaction.<br> <br>','[{"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Reimer-Tiemann reaction occurs at ortho position (-CHO group at o-position). But if o - position is occupied by some other group, in that case para product will be the major product.<br><img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/67_29182_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','chemistry/67_29182_question_1.png'),
(29183,'chemistry',69,1176,2820,'Cyclohexylamine when treated with nitrous acid yields (P). On treating (P) with PCC resultsi n $(\\mathrm{Q})$. When (Q) is heated with dil. NaOH we get (R). The final product (R) is','[{"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'<img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/69_29183_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',2023,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(29184,'chemistry',68,1117,2676,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> cis-3-Chloroprop-2-enoic acid is less stable than its trans-form.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Dipole moment of cis-form is greater than trans-form.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'In acrylic systems, trans isomers prevail due to decreased steric hindrance and minimal dipole moments, rendering them more stable and less polar compared to cis isomers. So the option (2) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(29185,'chemistry',70,1258,3071,'The two main differences between RNA and DNA are<br>I. ribose sugar and thymine in RNA<br>II. deoxyribose sugar and uracil in DNA<br>III. ribose sugar and uracil in RNA.<br>IV. deoxyribose sugar and guanine in DNA.','[{"text": "Only I", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Only II", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Only III", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Only IV", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'RNA contains ribose sugar and uracil while DNA contains deoxyribose sugar and thymine.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(29186,'chemistry',67,1077,2576,'The relative rate of acid catalyzed dehydration of following alcohols would be<br>','[{"text": "${\\\\rm{III}}\\\\,{\\\\rm{ > }}\\\\,{\\\\rm{II}}\\\\,{\\\\rm{ > }}\\\\,{\\\\rm{IV}}\\\\,{\\\\rm{ > }}\\\\,{\\\\rm{I}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{III}}\\\\,{\\\\rm{ > }}\\\\,{\\\\rm{IV}}\\\\,{\\\\rm{ > }}\\\\,{\\\\rm{I}}\\\\,{\\\\rm{ > }}\\\\,{\\\\rm{II}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${{\\\\rm{III  >  I  >  IV  > II}}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{III}}\\\\,{\\\\rm{ > }}\\\\,{\\\\rm{II}}\\\\,{\\\\rm{ > }}\\\\,{\\\\rm{I}}\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\rm{ > }}\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\rm{IV}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Rate of dehydration $ \\propto $ stability of first formed carbocation.<br>Carbocation formed from (III)….stabilizes through resonance and hyper conjugation.<br>Carbocation formed from (I)….stabilizes through hyper conjugation and inductive effect.<br>Carbocations (II) and (IV) are primary but stability of (IV) is more eventhough it has less number of H.C hydrogen because it has strong +1 group.<br>So, the correct option is (3).',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','chemistry/67_29186_question_1.png'),
(29187,'chemistry',69,1176,2819,'An organic compound  ${\\rm{[A]}}\\left( {{{\\rm{C}}_{\\rm{4}}}{{\\rm{H}}_{{\\rm{11}}}}{\\rm{N}}} \\right)$, shows optical activity and gives $\\mathrm{N}_{2}$ gas on treatment with $\\mathrm{HNO}_{2}$. The compound [A] reacts with $\\mathrm{PhSO}_{2} \\mathrm{Cl}$  producing a compound which is soluble in KOH . The structure of A is','[{"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Compounds given in option (3) and (4) show optical activity, $\\left(\\mathrm{C}_{4} \\mathrm{H}_{11} \\mathrm{~N}\\right)$. Out of (3) and (4), compound given in option (4), being a primary amine, reacts with $\\mathrm{PhSO}_{2} \\mathrm{Cl}$ and is soluble in$\\quad $KOH .',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',2023,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(29188,'chemistry',68,1118,2680,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> $\\left(\\mathrm{CH}_{3}\\right)_{3} \\mathrm{CCOOH}$ does not give $\\mathrm{HVZ}$ reaction.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> $\\left(\\mathrm{CH}_{3}\\right)_{3} \\mathrm{CCOOH}$ does not have $\\alpha$ hydrogen atom.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'The HVZ reaction is a method used to introduce a bromine atom at the $\\alpha$-carbon position (the carbon adjacent to the carboxyl group) of carboxylic acids.<br>But $\\left(\\mathrm{CH}_{3}\\right)_{3} \\mathrm{CCOOH}$ cannot undergo this reaction because the $\\alpha$-carbon in this compound does not contain a hydrogen atom. So the option (1) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(29189,'chemistry',70,1253,3054,'<div class="matching-question"><div class="column-left">Amino acids react with acid and base both.<br></div><div class="column-right">In Zwitter ionic form, amino acids show the amphoteric behaviour.</div></div>','[{"text": "Statement A is correct but Statement B is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Statement A is incorrect but Statement B is correct.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Statements are correct.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Statements are incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'In Zwitter ionic form, amino acids show amphoteric behaviour as they react with both acids and bases.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ type-2',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(29190,'chemistry',67,1077,2578,'0.092 g of a compound with the molecular formula $\\mathrm{C}_{3} \\mathrm{H}_{8} \\mathrm{O}_{3}$ on reaction with an excess $\\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\mathrm{MgI}$ gives 67.00 mL of methane at STP. The number of active hydrogen atoms present in a molecule of the compound is','[{"text": "Four", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Three", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Two", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "One", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{ROH}+\\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\mathrm{MgI} \\rightarrow \\mathrm{CH}_{4}+\\mathrm{Mg}(\\mathrm{OR}) \\mathrm{I}}}$ <br>According to the above reaction, every one mole of a compound containing one active H atom per molecule releases one mole of ${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{CH}_{4}}}$ gas at STP. Thus, number of moles of ${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{CH}_{4}}}$ gas released per mole of compound should be an indicator of the number of active hydrogen atoms per molecule of compound.<br>$\\mathrm{n}_{\\mathrm{CH}_{4}}=\\dfrac{\\text { Vol. of } \\mathrm{CH}_{4} \\text { at STP }}{22,400 \\mathrm{~mL} \\mathrm{~mol}^{-1}}=0.003 \\mathrm{~mol}$<br>${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{n}_{\\text {compound }}=\\dfrac{\\text { Given mass }}{\\text { Molar mass }}}}$<br>${\\mathrm{=\\dfrac{0.092 \\mathrm{~g}}{92 \\mathrm{~g} \\mathrm{~mol}^{-1}}=0.001 \\mathrm{~mol}}}$<br>This implies that 1 mol of compound releases 3 moles of ${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{CH}_{4}}}$ gas i.e., there are three active hydrogen atoms present in a molecule of the compound.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(29191,'chemistry',69,1176,2823,'<br>Compound P is neutral, Q gives effervescence with $\\mathrm{NaHCO}_{3}$ while R reacts with Hinsberg\'s reagent to give solid soluble in NaOH.<br>Compound P is','[{"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'<img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/69_29191_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',2023,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','chemistry/69_29191_question_1.png'),
(29192,'chemistry',68,1118,2685,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Formic acid reduces mercuric chloride to mercurous chloride on heating, while acetic acid does not.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Formic acid is stronger acid than acetic acid.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Formic acid behaves as reducing agent as it is oxidised to an unstable acid i.e., carbonic acid, which decomposes into $\\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ and $\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O}$.<br>$\\mathrm{HCOOH}+2 \\mathrm{HgCl}_{2} \\xrightarrow{\\Delta} \\mathrm{Hg}_{2} \\mathrm{Cl}_{2}+\\mathrm{CO}_{2}+2 \\mathrm{HCl}$<br>$\\mathrm{HCOOH}+\\mathrm{HgCl}_{2} \\rightarrow \\mathrm{CO}_{2}+2 \\mathrm{HCl}+2 \\mathrm{Hg}($ Black $)$<br>Acetic acid remains unaffected by mercuric chloride, as it does not show reducing properties. So the option (2) is correct.',NULL,'Hard','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(29218,'chemistry',68,1113,2656,'R\'   formed in the following sequence of reactions is','[{"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'<img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/68_29218_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />  So, the correct option is (3).',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',2023,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','chemistry/68_29218_question_1.png'),
(29193,'chemistry',67,1089,2613,'Identify ( Z ) in the following series<br>Ethanol $\\xrightarrow{\\mathrm{PBr}_{3}}(\\mathrm{~W}) \\xrightarrow{\\mathrm{Alc} . \\mathrm{KOH}}$<br>$({\\text{X}})\\xrightarrow[{{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{2}}}{\\text{O}}\\,;\\,\\,{\\text{heat}}}]{{({\\text{i}})\\,\\,{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{2}}}{\\text{S}}{{\\text{O}}_{\\text{4}}}/{\\text{Room}}\\,{\\text{temp}}}}({\\text{Y}})\\xrightarrow[{({\\text{ii}})\\,\\,{{\\text{C}}_{\\text{2}}}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{5}}}{\\text{Br}}}]{{({\\text{i}})\\,\\,{\\text{Na}}}}({\\text{Z}})$','[{"text": "$\\\\mathrm{CH}_{2}=\\\\mathrm{CH}_{2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{CH}_{3}-\\\\mathrm{CH}_{2}-\\\\mathrm{OH}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{CH}_{3}-\\\\mathrm{CH}_{2}-\\\\mathrm{O}-\\\\mathrm{CH}_{2}-\\\\mathrm{CH}_{3}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{CH}_{3}-\\\\mathrm{CH}_{2}-\\\\mathrm{SO}_{3} \\\\mathrm{H}$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'<img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/67_29193_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(29194,'chemistry',70,1257,3069,'<div class="matching-question"><div class="column-left">Fresh tomatoes are better source of vitamic $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{C}}$ than those which have been stored for sometime.<br></div><div class="column-right">On prolonged exposure to air, vitamin $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{C}}$ is destroyed due to aerial oxidation.</div></div>','[{"text": "Statement A is correct but Statement B is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Statement A is incorrect but Statement B is correct.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Statements are correct.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Statements are incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Vitamin C present in tomatoes, is destroyed due to aerial oxidation.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ type-2',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(29195,'chemistry',69,1177,2828,'In the reaction<br>Aniline ${\\mathrm{\\xrightarrow[\\text { dil. } \\mathrm{HCl}]{\\mathrm{NaNO}_{2}} \\mathrm{P} \\xrightarrow[\\text { NaOH }]{\\text { Phenol }} \\mathrm{Q}}}$','[{"text": "${\\\\mathrm{\\\\mathrm{C}_{6} \\\\mathrm{H}_{5} \\\\mathrm{~N}_{2} \\\\mathrm{Cl}}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "ortho-hydroxyazobenzene", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "para-hydroxyazobenzene", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "meta-hydroxyazobenzene", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'P is Benzene diazonium chloride.<br>${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{P} \\rightarrow \\mathrm{Q}}}$ is coupling reaction.  <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/69_29195_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2024,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(29196,'chemistry',68,1113,2664,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Aromatic aldehydes and formaldehyde undergo Cannizzaro reaction.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Aromatic aldehydes are almost as reactive as formaldehyde.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Aromatic aldehydes and formaldehyde, lacking $\\alpha$-hydrogen atoms, undergo Cannizzaro reaction. Formaldehyde exhibits heightened reactivity, being the simplest aldehyde, due to its smaller size and absence of steric hindrance, leading to efficient formation of carboxylic acid and alcohol. So the option (3) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(29197,'chemistry',67,1077,2576,'At 413 K , ethanol is dehydrated to produce ethoxyethane.<br>$\\mathrm{CH}_{3}-\\mathrm{CH}_{2}-\\mathrm{OH} \\xrightarrow[413 \\mathrm{~K}]{\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{SO}_{4}} \\mathrm{C}_{2} \\mathrm{H}_{5} \\mathrm{OC}_{2} \\mathrm{H}_{5}$<br>Read the statements given below, and choose the option containing the correct statements.<br>I. Such a reaction is suitable for the preparation of ethers having primary alkyl groups only.<br>II. The mechanism involves the following sequence Protonation of alcohol $\\rightarrow$ Attack of another alcohol molecule on protonated alcohol $\\rightarrow$ Deprotonation.<br>III. At 443 K , the same reaction would produce ethene as the major product.','[{"text": "I, II", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "II, III", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "I, III", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "I, II, and III", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Alkene and ether can be formed by dehydration of alcohol, that depends on reaction condition. At 443 K , dehydration of alcohol will give alkene whereas at 413 K  the same reaction will give ether.<br><img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/67_29197_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' /> <br>This reaction follows ${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{S}_{\\mathrm{N}} 2}}$ reaction pathway so primary alkyl halide is suitable for preparation of ether.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(29198,'chemistry',69,1176,2823,'Read Statement -A and Statement -B carefully to mark the correct options given below.<div class="matching-question"><div class="column-left">Aniline does not undergo Friedel Crafts alkylation reaction.</div><div class="column-right">Aniline cannot be prepared through Gabriel synthesis.\n<br>In the light of the above statements, choose the correct answer from the options given below:</div></div>','[{"text": "Statement A is correct but Statement B is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Statement A is incorrect but Statement B is correct.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both statements are correct.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Statements are incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Aniline does not undergo Friedel-Crafts alkylation reaction due to salt formation with aluminium chloride, the Lewis acid, which is used as the catalyst.<br> Aniline (aromatic primary amine) cannot be prepared by Gabriel phthalimide synthesis because aryl halides do not undergo nucleophilic substitution with anion formed by phthalimide.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ type-2',2024,'NEET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(29199,'chemistry',68,1117,2676,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Esters are hydrolysed with dilute mineral acids to give carboxylic acid.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Boiling points of acids are lower than the corresponding alcohols.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'<img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/68_29199_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />  Carboxylic acids have elevated boiling points due to strong intermolecular forces, primarily hydrogen bonding, resulting from association between molecules in the liquid state, forming dimeric structures with two hydrogen bonds per molecule. So the option (3) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(29470,'chemistry',67,1089,2613,'Glycerol on treatment with oxalic acid at $110^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C}$ forms','[{"text": "formic acid", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "allyl alcohol", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ and $\\\\mathrm{CO}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "acrolein.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'<img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/67_29470_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(29200,'chemistry',70,1258,3073,'<div class="matching-question"><div class="column-left">DNA is responsible for maintaining the identity of different species of organisms over millions of years.<br></div><div class="column-right">DNA molecule is capable of self duplication during cell division and identical DNA strands are transferred to daughter cell.</div></div>','[{"text": "Statement A is correct but Statement B is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Statement A is incorrect but Statement B is correct.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Statements are correct.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Statements are incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'DNA is the chemical basis of heredity and may be regarded as the reserve of genetic information. DNA is exclusively responsible for maintaining the identity of different species of organisms over millions of years. A DNA molecule is capable of self duplication during cell division and identical DNA strands are transferred to the daughter cells.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ type-2',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(29201,'chemistry',67,1084,2608,'Identify the minor products formed in the given reaction and choose the correct option from the codes given below.','[{"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Para is the major product and ortho will be the minor product in electrophilic substitution reactions of anisole.<br>So, the correct option is (2).',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','chemistry/67_29201_question_1.png'),
(29202,'chemistry',69,1174,2810,'Identify the major product ${\\mathrm{C}}$ formed in the following reaction sequence:<br>${\\text{C}}{{\\text{H}}_3} - {\\text{C}}{{\\text{H}}_2} - {\\text{C}}{{\\text{H}}_2} - {\\text{I}}\\xrightarrow{{{\\text{NaCN}}}}{\\text{A}}\\xrightarrow[{{\\text{ Partial hydrolysis }}}]{{{\\text{O}}{{\\text{H}}^ - }}}{\\text{B}}\\xrightarrow[{{\\text{B}}{{\\text{r}}_2}}]{{{\\text{NaOH}}}}\\mathop {\\text{C}}\\limits_{{\\text{ (major) }}} $','[{"text": "Propylamine", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Butylamine", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Butanamide", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\mathrm{\\\\alpha}}$-Bromobutanoic acid<br>", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'<img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/69_29202_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />   Step-I is ${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{S}_{\\mathrm{N}}}}$ reaction with ${\\mathrm{\\stackrel{\\ominus}{\\mathrm{C}} \\mathrm{N}}}$ nucleophile.<br> Step-II is partial hydrolysis of cyanide to amide.<br>Step-III is Hoffmann bromamide degradation reaction of amides to amines.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2024,'NEET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(29203,'chemistry',68,1113,2663,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Benzaldehyde undergoes aldol condensation.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Aldehydes that do not have $\\alpha$ hydrogen undergo Cannizzaro reaction.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Aldehydes with a hydrogen in the $\\alpha $  -position undergo aldol condensation, forming  $\\beta $-hydroxy aldehydes, known as aldols, through dimerization in the presence of a base at low temperatures.<br>Benzaldehyde,  <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/68_29203_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />  $\\alpha $ - hydrogen. Thus it cannot undergo aldol condensation. Instead, it undergoes Cannizzaro reaction. So the option (4) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(29204,'chemistry',70,1247,3032,'<div class="matching-question"><div class="column-left">${\\text{D - ( + )}}$ - Glucose is dextrorotatory in nature.<br></div><div class="column-right">\'D\' represents its dextrorotatory nature.</div></div>','[{"text": "Statement A is correct but Statement B is incorrect.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Statement A is incorrect but Statement B is correct.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Statements are correct.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Statements are incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,NULL,NULL,'Medium','MCQ type-2',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(29205,'chemistry',67,1073,2559,'Read Assertion and Reason carefully to mark the correct option given below.<br><br><strong>Assertion:</strong> Commercially, acids are reduced to alcohols by converting them to esters followed by their reduction using catalytic hydrogenation.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> They can also reduce carboxylic acids to primary alcohols but the reagent used is expensive and is only used for preparing special chemicals.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is true but Reason is false.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is false but reason is true.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Direct reduction of carboxylic acid to alcohol is expensive so, commercially, acid is reduced to alcohol by converting it into ester followed by their reduction (weekly hydrogenation or catalytic hydrogenation.)',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(29206,'chemistry',69,1173,2806,'The IUPAC name of<br>','[{"text": "4-Amino-2-ethyl pent-1-ene", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "2-Ethylpentan-4-amine", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Amino pent-4-ene", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "4-Ethylpent-4-en-2-amine", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Amine is more prior than double bond.<br><img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/69_29206_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','chemistry/69_29206_question_1.png'),
(29207,'chemistry',68,1118,2677,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> The $\\mathrm{pK}_{\\mathrm{a}}$ of acetic acid is lower than that of phenol<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Phenoxide ion is more resonance stabilised.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Acetic acid $(\\mathrm{pKa}=4.76)$ is more acidic than phenol $(\\mathrm{pKa}=10)$. This is due to the greater resonance stabilization in the acetate ion compared to the phenoxide ion. So the option (3) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(29220,'chemistry',67,1083,2596,'Match Column I with Column II and choose the correct combination from the options given.<div class="matching-question"></div>','[{"text": "A - S,\\tB - R,\\tC - S,\\t\\tD - P", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "A - Q, \\tB - R,\\tC - P, \\t\\tD - S", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "A - S,\\tB - P,\\tC - R, \\tD - Q", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "A - P,\\tB - R,\\tC - S,\\t\\tD - Q", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'<img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/67_29220_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />',NULL,'Medium','Match the following',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','chemistry/67_29220_question_1.png'),
(29208,'chemistry',67,1077,2576,'Read Assertion and Reason carefully to mark the correct option given below.<br><br><strong>Assertion:</strong> Alcohols do not react with NaBr but when $\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{SO}_{4}$ is added they form alkyl bromides.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Protonation of the – OH group by  $\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{SO}_{4}$ convert it into the $-\\stackrel{\\oplus}{\\mathrm{O}} \\mathrm{H}_{2}$ group which has a better-leaving tendency.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is true but Reason is false.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is false but reason is true.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Alcohols react with NaBr in the presence of ${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{SO}_{4}}}$ to form alkyl halides because in the acidic medium the ${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{O}-\\mathrm{H}}}$ group protonates and  form ${\\mathrm{{ }^{+} \\mathrm{OH}_{2}}}$ which is a good leaving group.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(29209,'chemistry',70,1250,3044,'<div class="matching-question"><div class="column-left">Sucrose is called an invert sugar.</div><div class="column-right">On hydrolysis, sucrose bring the change in the sign of rotation from dextro $\\mathrm{(+)}$ to laevo (-).</div></div>','[{"text": "Statement A is correct but Statement B is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Statement A is incorrect but Statement B is correct.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Statements are correct.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Statements are incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'The hydrolysis of sucrose brings about a change in the sign of rotation from dextro $\\mathrm{(+)}$ to laevo (-) and the product is name as invert sugar.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ type-2',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(29210,'chemistry',68,1113,2662,'Which of the following has highly acidic hydrogen?','[{"text": "", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Active methylene group has more acidic hydrogen atom. The third carbon in compound  ${\\text{(1)}}$ is highly acidic as it is attached to two EWG <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/68_29210_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',2024,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(29211,'chemistry',69,1173,2808,'<br>The relation between (A) and (B) is','[{"text": "Chain isomers", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Homologes", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Functional isomers", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Identical compounds", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'It is Hoffmann Bromamide reaction.<br><img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/69_29211_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' /> <br>so, the compounds formed are homologes.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','chemistry/69_29211_question_1.png'),
(29212,'chemistry',67,1080,2586,'Read the Statement - A and Statement - B carefully to mark the correct options given below:<div class="matching-question"><div class="column-left">A methanol-poisoned patient is treated by giving an intravenous infusion of diluted ethanol.</div><div class="column-right">Ethanol reduces the action of enzymes which converts the methanol into toxic products, by competitive inhibition, thus allowing time for the kidneys to excrete methanol.</div></div>','[{"text": "Statement A is correct but Statement B is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Statement A is incorrect but Statement B is correct.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both statements are correct.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Statements are incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'A methanol poisoned patient is treated by giving intravenous infusion of diluted ethanol. The enzyme responsible for oxidation of aldehyde ${\\mathrm{(\\mathrm{HCHO})}}$ to acid is swamped, allowing time for kidneys to excrete methanol.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ type-2',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
