(29315,'chemistry',67,1077,2574,'The alcohol which easily reacts with conc. $\\mathrm{HCl}$ is','[{"text": "$\\\\mathrm{CH}_{3}-\\\\mathrm{CHOH}-\\\\mathrm{CH}_{2}-\\\\mathrm{CH}_{3}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\left(\\\\mathrm{CH}_{3}\\\\right)_{3}-\\\\mathrm{C}-\\\\mathrm{OH}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{CH}_{3}-\\\\mathrm{CH}_{2}-\\\\mathrm{CH}_{2}-\\\\mathrm{CH}_{2}-\\\\mathrm{OH}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\left(\\\\mathrm{CH}_{3}\\\\right)_{2}-\\\\mathrm{CH}-\\\\mathrm{CH}_{2} \\\\mathrm{OH}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Tertiary alcohol readily reacts with halogen acid. <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/67_29315_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />  Presence of three alkyl groups increases electron density on $3^{\\circ}$ carbon atom. Hence, $-\\mathrm{OH}$ group is easily removed. After removal of - $\\mathrm{OH}$ group, $3^{\\circ}$ carbonium ion is formed which is most stable.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(29316,'chemistry',70,1247,3032,'Which among the following compounds is obtained when glucose react with hydrogen cyanide?','[{"text": "Gluconic acid", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Glucose cyanohydrin", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Saccharic acid", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "n-hexane", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Glucose react with hydrogen cyanide to form glucose cyanohydrin.<br>${\\text{HOC}}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{2}}}{\\text{ - }}\\mathop {{{({\\text{CHOH}})}_{\\text{4}}}}\\limits_{{\\text{ Glucose }}} {\\text{ -  CHO  +  HCN}} \\to $         <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/70_29316_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />  This reaction confirm the presence of a carboxyl group  <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/70_29316_explanation_2.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />    in glucose.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2020,'MHTCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(29317,'chemistry',69,1176,2821,'Given below are two statements:<div class="matching-question"><div class="column-left">Controlled nitration of aniline can be done conveniently by protecting $-\\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ group through acetylation.</div><div class="column-right">Direct nitration of aniline yields ortho, meta and para derivatives.</div></div>','[{"text": "Statement A is correct but Statement B is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Statement A is incorrect but Statement B is correct.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both statements are correct.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Statements are incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Aniline is base, so reacts with nitricacid to form anilinium which is a meta director. So, on nitration along with ortho, para nitro anilines, meta nitro aniline is also formed. To avoid meta nitro product, ${\\mathrm{-\\mathrm{NH}_{2}}}$ group is protected by acetylation.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ type-2',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(29319,'chemistry',67,1083,2597,'When phenol is treated with $\\mathrm{CHCl}_{3}$ and $\\mathrm{NaOH}$ followed by acidification, salicylaldehyde is obtained. Which of the following species is involved in the above mentioned reaction as intermediates?','[{"text": "", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'On refluxing alkaline solution of phenol with $\\mathrm{CHCl}_{3}$ at $330 \\mathrm{~K}$ and on subsequent acidification, <br>o-hydroxy benzaldehyde (salicylaldehyde) <br>is obtained. This reaction is known as Riemer-Tiemann reaction.<br> Mechanism <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/67_29319_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />   <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/67_29319_explanation_2.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />  $\\therefore $The correct option is (1).',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(29320,'chemistry',70,1249,3042,'Fructose reduces Tollen\'s reagent due to:','[{"text": "Secondary alcoholic group", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Enolisation of fructose followed by conversion to aldehyde by base", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Primary alcoholic group", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Asymmetric carbon atoms", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'In presence of alkali, fructose is converted into mixture of mannose and glucose showing enolisation. Glucose then reduces Tollen\'s reagent.',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(29321,'chemistry',69,1177,2827,'In Gattermann reaction, a diazonium group is replaced by X using ${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{Y} \\cdot \\mathrm{X}}}$ and Y are','[{"text": "${\\\\rm{X}}\\\\, - \\\\,{\\\\rm{C}}{{\\\\rm{l}}^\\\\Theta }\\\\,,\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\mkern 1mu} {\\\\kern 1pt} {\\\\mkern 1mu} {\\\\mkern 1mu} {\\\\rm{Y}}\\\\, - \\\\,{\\\\rm{Cu}}/{\\\\rm{HCl}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{X}}\\\\, - \\\\,{\\\\rm{C}}{{\\\\rm{l}}^ \\\\oplus }\\\\,,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\mkern 1mu} {\\\\kern 1pt} {\\\\mkern 1mu} {\\\\mkern 1mu} {\\\\rm{Y}}\\\\, - \\\\,{\\\\rm{CuC}}{{\\\\rm{l}}_{\\\\rm{2}}}/{\\\\rm{HCl}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{X}}\\\\, - \\\\,{\\\\rm{C}}{{\\\\rm{l}}^\\\\Theta },\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\mkern 1mu} {\\\\kern 1pt} {\\\\mkern 1mu} {\\\\mkern 1mu} {\\\\rm{Y}}\\\\, - \\\\,{\\\\rm{CuC}}{{\\\\rm{l}}_{\\\\rm{2}}}/{\\\\rm{HCl}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{X}}\\\\, - \\\\,{\\\\rm{C}}{{\\\\rm{l}}_2}\\\\,,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\mkern 1mu} {\\\\kern 1pt} {\\\\mkern 1mu} {\\\\mkern 1mu} {\\\\mkern 1mu} {\\\\rm{Y}}\\\\, - \\\\,{\\\\rm{C}}{{\\\\rm{u}}_{\\\\rm{2}}}{\\\\rm{O}}/{\\\\rm{HCl}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Gattermann\'s reaction is<br><img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/69_29321_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(29322,'chemistry',67,1083,2597,'In the above reaction, end product \' ${\\rm{C}}$ \' is','[{"text": "salicylaldehyde", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "salicylic acid", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "phenyl acetate", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "aspirin", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'<img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/67_29322_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','chemistry/67_29322_question_1.png'),
(29323,'chemistry',68,1113,2659,'The major product \' P \' for the following sequence of reactions is','[{"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'${\\rm{Zn}} - {\\rm{Hg}}/{\\rm{HCl}}$  reduces aldehyde and keto group to $-\\mathrm{CH}_{2}-$ group and $\\mathrm{LiAH}_{4}$ reduces amide to $-\\mathrm{CH}_{2} \\mathrm{NH}_{2}$. <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/68_29323_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',2023,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','chemistry/68_29323_question_1.png'),
(29324,'chemistry',69,1176,2817,'The main product formed in the following reaction is','[{"text": "", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'As, $-\\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ group is more reactive than $-\\mathrm{OH}$ group, $\\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\mathrm{COCl}$ will attack on $-\\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ group and gives the following product,  <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/69_29324_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' /> Reaction is given as follows <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/69_29324_explanation_2.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','chemistry/69_29324_question_1.png'),
(29325,'chemistry',70,1250,3045,'Find the hydrolysis product of maltose','[{"text": "$\\\\mathrm{\\\\alpha}$-D-fructose $\\\\mathrm{+\\\\alpha}$-D-galactose", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{\\\\alpha}$-D-mannose $\\\\mathrm{+\\\\alpha}$-D-galactose", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{\\\\alpha}$-D-glucose $\\\\mathrm{+\\\\alpha}$-D-glucose", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{\\\\alpha}$-D-glucose $\\\\mathrm{+\\\\alpha}$-D-fructose", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'$\\mathop {{{\\text{C}}_{{\\text{12}}}}{{\\text{H}}_{{\\text{22}}}}{{\\text{O}}_{{\\text{11}}}}}\\limits_{{\\text{ Maltose }}} {\\text{  +  }}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{2}}}{\\text{O}}\\xrightarrow{{{\\text{ maltase}}}}\\mathop {{{\\text{C}}_{\\text{6}}}{{\\text{H}}_{{\\text{12}}}}{{\\text{O}}_{\\text{6}}}}\\limits_{{\\text{ }}\\alpha {\\text{ - D  -  Glucose }}} {\\text{  +  }}\\mathop {{{\\text{C}}_{\\text{6}}}{{\\text{H}}_{{\\text{12}}}}{{\\text{O}}_{\\text{6}}}}\\limits_{{\\text{ }}\\alpha {\\text{ - D  -  Glucose }}} $',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(29326,'chemistry',67,1083,2601,'When phenol is treated with $\\mathrm{D}_{2} \\mathrm{SO}_{4} / \\mathrm{D}_{2} \\mathrm{O}$, some of the hydrogens get exchanged. The final product in this exchange reaction is','[{"text": "", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'In acidic medium, phenol exists in the following resonating structures. <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/67_29326_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' /> Since, o/p positions are electron rich sites, so electrophile will attack on these sites, i.e. hydrogen of these sites get exchanged by $\\mathrm{D}$ (deuterium). Hence, the final product of the reaction is <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/67_29326_explanation_2.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' /> which is formed as <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/67_29326_explanation_3.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(29327,'chemistry',68,1113,2670,'Match Column I with Column II.<br> <br>Choose the correct answer from the options given below:<div class="matching-question"></div>','[{"text": "A - R,\\tB - S,\\tC - Q ,\\tD - P", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "A - R,\\tB - S,\\tC - P,\\t\\tD - Q", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "A - Q ,\\tB - S,\\tC - P,\\t\\tD - R", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "A - Q ,\\tB - P,\\tC - R,\\tD - S", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Hoffmann degradation $-\\mathrm{Br}_{2}, \\mathrm{NaOH}$<br>Clemmensen reduction $-\\mathrm{Zn}-\\mathrm{Hg} / \\mathrm{HCl}$<br>Cannizzaro reaction - conc. $\\mathrm{KOH}, \\Delta$<br>Reimer-Tiemann reaction - $\\mathrm{CHCl}_{3}, \\mathrm{NaOH} / \\mathrm{H}_{3} \\mathrm{O}^{+}$',NULL,'Hard','Match the following',2023,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','chemistry/68_29327_question_1.png'),
(29328,'chemistry',69,1176,2816,'The correct order of basic strength of the following are','[{"text": "I $>$ II $>$ III $>$ IV", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "IV $>$ II $>$ III $>$ I", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "III $>$ IV $>$ II $>$ I", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "III $>$ II $>$ IV $>$ I", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Electron releasing group $-\\mathrm{OH}$ in compound (I) increases the basic strength while electron withdrawing group ( $-\\mathrm{COCH}_{3}$ in compound $\\mathrm{I}$ and IV) decreases the basic strength of amines. In compound IV, $-\\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ is directly attached to benzene ring, it results in conjugation of unshared pair of electrons with benzene ring and thus making it less available for protonation. Therefore, correct order is III $>$ II $>$ IV $>$ I.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','chemistry/69_29328_question_1.png'),
(29329,'chemistry',67,1083,2595,'The main product obtained from phenol with $\\mathrm{PCl}_{5}$ is','[{"text": "$\\\\mathrm{BHC}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "hexachlorobenzene", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "chlorobenzene", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "triphenyl phosphate", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'<img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/67_29329_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(29330,'chemistry',68,1113,2668,'Which of the following compounds would give the following set of qualitative analysis?<br>(i) Fehling\'s Test: Positive<br>(ii) Na fusion extract upon treatment with sodium nitroprusside gives a blood red colour but not prussian blue.','[{"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Aromatic aldehydes do not give Fehling\'s test. Both nitrogen and sulphur must be present to obtain blood red colour with sodium nitroprusside.All the conditions satisfied by option (2).<br>So, the correct option is (2).',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',2023,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(29331,'chemistry',70,1247,3036,'A diabetic person carries a packet of glucose with him always, because','[{"text": "glucose increase the blood sugar level slowly", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "glucose reduce the blood sugar level", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "glucose increases the blood sugar level almost instantaneously", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "glucose reduce the blood sugar level slowly.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'The sugar level in the blood of a diabetic person may suddenly fall, immediate intake of glucose increase the blood sugar level almost instantaneously.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2009,'2009','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(29332,'chemistry',69,1176,2821,'Consider the following reaction, $X$ is','[{"text": "benzoic acid", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "salicylic acid", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "phenol", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "aniline", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'<img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/69_29332_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','chemistry/69_29332_question_1.png'),
(29354,'chemistry',69,1176,2816,'Amines are basic in character because they have','[{"text": "a lone pair of electrons on the nitrogen atom", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "a hydroxyl group in the molecule", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "replaceable hydrogen atom", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "tetrahedral structure", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'According to Lewis, species having a tendency of donating an electron pair are called bases. In amines, a lone pair of electrons is present over nitrogen. Since, it is available for donation, thus, it provides basic character to the amines.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(29333,'chemistry',67,1083,2591,'Which one of the following properties is exhibited by phenol?','[{"text": "It is soluble in ${\\\\rm{aq}}\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\rm{NaOH}}$ and evolves ${\\\\rm{C}}{{\\\\rm{O}}_{\\\\rm{2}}}\\\\,{\\\\rm{with}}\\\\,{\\\\rm{aq}}\\\\,{\\\\rm{NaHC}}{{\\\\rm{O}}_{\\\\rm{3}}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "It is soluble in ${\\\\rm{aq}}{\\\\rm{.NaOH}}$ and does not evolve ${\\\\rm{C}}{{\\\\rm{O}}_{\\\\rm{2}}}{\\\\rm{with}}\\\\,{\\\\rm{aq}}\\\\,{\\\\rm{.NaHC}}{{\\\\rm{O}}_{\\\\rm{3}}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "It is not soluble in ${\\\\rm{aq\\\\,NaOH}}$ but evolves ${\\\\rm{C}}{{\\\\rm{O}}_{\\\\rm{2}}}\\\\,{\\\\rm{with}}{\\\\mkern 1mu} \\\\,{\\\\rm{aq}}{\\\\mkern 1mu} \\\\,{\\\\rm{NaHC}}{{\\\\rm{O}}_{\\\\rm{3}}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "It is insoluble in ${\\\\text{aq}}\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\text{NaOH}}\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\text{but}}\\\\,{\\\\text{does}}\\\\,{\\\\text{not}}\\\\,{\\\\text{evolve C}}{{\\\\text{O}}_{\\\\text{2}}}\\\\,{\\\\text{with}}{\\\\mkern 1mu} \\\\,{\\\\text{aq}}{\\\\text{.NaHC}}{{\\\\text{O}}_{\\\\text{3}}}{\\\\text{.}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Phenol is a weak acid. It reacts with $\\mathrm{NaOH}$ to produce salt. <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/67_29333_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' /> But it is not sufficiently acidic to evolve $\\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ from $\\mathrm{NaHCO}_{3}$ solution.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(29334,'chemistry',68,1113,2659,'Read the Assertion and Reason carefully and mark the correct option.<br><br><strong>Assertion:</strong> <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/68_29334_qtext_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />  can be easily  reduced using $\\mathrm{Zn}-\\mathrm{Hg} / \\mathrm{HCl}$ to  https://res.cloudinary.com/doe6oiv2h/image/upload/v1764949429/preptracker/questions/q_29334_col1_1764949428699.png<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> $\\mathrm{Zn}-\\mathrm{Hg} / \\mathrm{HCl}$ is used to reduce carbonyl group to $-\\mathrm{CH}_{2}-$ group.','[{"text": "Assertion is false but Reason is true.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is true but Reason is false.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'The carbonyl group of aldehyde or ketone gets reduced to alkane on treatment with $\\mathrm{Zn}(\\mathrm{Hg})$ with HCl and the reaction is named as Clemmensen reduction<br>But for hydroxyaldehyde or hydroxyketone, the alcoholic group gets converted to alkyl halide while carbonyl group gets reduced to alkane.The product of the given assertion reaction will be<br><img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/68_29334_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />',NULL,'Hard','Assertion and Reason',2023,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(29335,'chemistry',70,1246,3028,'Match the sugars in column I with their types given in column II and mark the appropriate choice.<div class="matching-question"><div class="column-left">A Glucose <br> B Fructose <br> C Ribose <br> D Erythrose</div><div class="column-right">P Ketohexose  <br> Q Aldohexose  <br> R Aldotetrose  <br> S Aldopentose</div></div>','[{"text": "${\\\\text{A  -  S,  B  -  P,  C  -  R, D  -  Q }}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\text{A  -  R,  B  -  S,  C  -  P, D  -  Q }}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\text{A  -  P,  B  -  Q,  C  -  R, D  -  S }}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\text{A  -  Q,  B  -  P,  C  -  S, D  -  R }}$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,NULL,NULL,'Medium','Match the following',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(29336,'chemistry',69,1177,2827,'Z is defined as','[{"text": "$\\\\mathrm{C}_{6} \\\\mathrm{H}_{5}-\\\\mathrm{NH}-\\\\mathrm{CH}_{3}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{C}_{6} \\\\mathrm{H}_{5}-\\\\mathrm{COOH}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{C}_{6} \\\\mathrm{H}_{5}-\\\\mathrm{CH}_{2} \\\\mathrm{NH}_{2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{C}_{6} \\\\mathrm{H}_{5}-\\\\mathrm{CH}_{2}-\\\\mathrm{COOH}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'<img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/69_29336_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />   Thus, product Z  is identified as $\\mathrm{C}_{6} \\mathrm{H}_{5}-\\mathrm{COOH}$.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','chemistry/69_29336_question_1.png'),
(29337,'chemistry',67,1083,2600,'Phenol is heated with phthalic anhydride in the presence of conc. $\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{SO}_{4}$. The product gives pink colour with alkali. The product is','[{"text": "phenolphthalein", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "bakelite", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "salicylic acid", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "fluorescein", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'<img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/67_29337_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
