(29340,'chemistry',67,1083,2599,'The major product in the above reaction is','[{"text": "p-benzoquinone", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "p-benzoic acid", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "aniline", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "None of the above", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'<img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/67_29340_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','chemistry/67_29340_question_1.png'),
(29341,'chemistry',68,1113,2662,'Consider the following reaction :<br> <br>The correct statement for product B is ______ .<br>It is','[{"text": "optically active and adds one mole of bromine.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "optically active alcohol and is neutral.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "racemic mixture and gives a gas with saturated $\\\\mathrm{NaHCO}_{3}$ solution.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "racemic mixture and is neutral.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'First step is aldol condensation reaction.Then nucleophilic addition of $\\mathrm{CN}^{\\ominus}$, followed by hydrolysis gives a racemic mixture of unsaturated hydroxy acid. <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/68_29341_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',2023,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','chemistry/68_29341_question_1.png'),
(29342,'chemistry',70,1253,3054,'In an alkaline medium, glycine predominantly exists as/in a/an','[{"text": "cation", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "anion", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "zwitter ion", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "covalent form.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'In alkaline medium (high $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{pH}}$ ) amino acids exists as negatively charged particles (carboxyl group donates protons).<br>${{\\text{H}}_{\\text{2}}}{\\text{N  -  C}}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{2}}}{\\text{  -  COOH}}\\xrightarrow{{{\\text{ high pH}}}}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{2}}}{\\text{N }}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\text{ -  C}}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{2}}}{\\text{  -  CO}}{{\\text{O}}^{\\Theta {\\text{ }}}}$. i.e., anion form.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2013,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(29343,'chemistry',69,1176,2816,'Arrange the following in the increasing order of their basic strength.$\\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ (I);$\\left(\\mathrm{CH}_{3}\\right)_{2} \\mathrm{NH}$ (II);$\\left(\\mathrm{CH}_{3}\\right)_{3} \\mathrm{~N}(\\mathrm{III})$;$\\mathrm{C}_{6} \\mathrm{H}_{5} \\mathrm{CH}_{2} \\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ (IV)','[{"text": "IV $ < $ III $ < $ II $ < $ I", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "IV $ < $ III $ < $ I $ < $ II", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "I $ < $ II $ < $ III $ < $ IV", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "IV $ < $ III $ < $ I $=$ II", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Basic character depends upon the availability of lone pair of electrons over nitrogen for donation. Presence of electron releasing group like $-\\mathrm{CH}_{3}$ increases the electron density over nitrogen, thus, lone pair of nitrogen are more available for donation. Hence, basic character increases.But $3^{\\circ}$ methyl amines are less basic as compared to $2^{\\circ}$ and $1^{\\circ}$ amines due to steric hindrance. Thus, the order of basic strength of the given compounds is  <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/69_29343_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(29344,'chemistry',67,1084,2606,'Phenol reacts with  ${\\rm{N}}{{\\rm{H}}_{\\rm{3}}}$ to give','[{"text": "benzamide", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "benzoic acid", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "aniline", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "None of the above", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Phenol reacts with ammonia in the presence of anhydrous $\\mathrm{ZnCl}_{2}$ to give aniline. <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/67_29344_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(29345,'chemistry',68,1113,2670,'Compound $\\mathrm{A}_{,} \\mathrm{C}_{5} \\mathrm{H}_{10} \\mathrm{O}_{5}$ gives a tetracetate with $\\mathrm{Ac}_{2} \\mathrm{O}$ and oxidation of A with $\\mathrm{Br}_{2}-\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O}$ gives an acid, $\\mathrm{C}_{5} \\mathrm{H}_{10} \\mathrm{O}_{6}$. Reduction of A with HI gives isopentane. The possible structure of A is','[{"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'$\\mathrm{Br}_{2} / \\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O}$ oxidises aldehyde to acid. Formation of iso pentane indicates four \' C \' atoms in parent chain. Formation of tetracetate indicates presence of four -OH groups.  <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/68_29345_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',2023,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(29346,'chemistry',70,1253,3053,'Which among the following amino acids has lowest molar mass?','[{"text": "Arginine", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Aspartic acid", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Asparagine", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Alanine", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Alanine has lowest molecular mass with chemical formula $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{C}_{3} \\mathrm{H}_{7} \\mathrm{NO}_{2}}$. Alanine is the simplest $\\mathrm{\\alpha}$-amino acid after glycine. The methyl side chain of alanine is non-reactive and is therefore hardly ever directly involved in protein function.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(29347,'chemistry',69,1176,2819,'${\\rm{\\,}}{{\\rm{N}}_{\\rm{2}}}$ gas will not be evolved upon reaction of ${\\rm{HN}}{{\\rm{O}}_{\\rm{2}}}$ with which of the following amines?','[{"text": "$1^{\\\\circ}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$2^{\\\\circ}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$3^{\\\\circ}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both (b) and (c)", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Only $1^{\\circ}$ amines evolve nitrogen when reacts with nitrous acid  ${\\text{HN}}{{\\text{O}}_{\\text{2}}}$  <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/69_29347_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(29348,'chemistry',67,1083,2597,'Phenol is heated with $\\mathrm{CHCl}_{3}$, and alcoholic $\\mathrm{KOH}$, salicylaldehyde is produced. This reaction is known as','[{"text": "Rosenmund\'s reaction", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Reimer-Tiemann reaction", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Friedel-Crafts reaction", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Sommelet reaction", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Phenol reacts with chloroform and alcoholic $\\mathrm{KOH}$ to give salicylaldehyde. This reaction is called as Reimer-Tiemann reaction. <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/67_29348_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(29350,'chemistry',70,1253,3053,'Which of the following amino acid is basic in nature?','[{"text": "Valine", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Tyrosine", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Arginine", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Leucine", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Arginine amino acid is basic in nature. (Basic due to the presence of \'NH\' groups)    <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/70_29350_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2016,'MHTCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(29351,'chemistry',69,1176,2819,'Primary, secondary and tertiary amines can be distinguished by','[{"text": "Schiff\'s test", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Fehling\'s test", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Tollen\'s test", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Hinsberg\'s test", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Hinsberg\'s reagent (benzene sulphonyl chloride) is used to distinguish $1^{\\circ}, 2^{\\circ}$ and $3^{\\circ}$ amines. With Hinsberg\'s reagent, $1^{\\circ}$ amines give a sulphonamide, soluble in alkali; $2^{\\circ}$ amines form a sulphonamide, insoluble in alkali while, $3^{\\circ}$ amines do not react at all.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(29352,'chemistry',67,1083,2596,'Sulphonation of phenol with conc. $\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{SO}_{4}$ at 288 - $293 \\mathrm{~K}$ gives','[{"text": "0-phenolsulphonic acid", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "sulphanilic acid", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "p-phenolsulphonic acid", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "sulphone", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Sulphonation of phenol with conc. $\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{SO}_{4}$ at $288-293 \\mathrm{~K}$ gives $o$-phenolsulphonic acid. <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/67_29352_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(29353,'chemistry',68,1111,2649,'In a reaction<br> <br>reagents \' X \' and \' Y \' respectively are :','[{"text": "$\\\\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\\\mathrm{OH} / \\\\mathrm{H}^{+}, \\\\Delta$  and   $\\\\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\\\mathrm{OH} / \\\\mathrm{H}^{+}, \\\\Delta$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\left(\\\\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\\\mathrm{CO}\\\\right)_{2} \\\\mathrm{O} / \\\\mathrm{H}^{+}$   and   $\\\\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\\\mathrm{OH} / \\\\mathrm{H}^{+}, \\\\Delta$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\\\mathrm{OH} / \\\\mathrm{H}^{+}, \\\\Delta$   and   $\\\\left(\\\\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\\\mathrm{CO}\\\\right)_{2} \\\\mathrm{O} / \\\\mathrm{H}^{+}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\left(\\\\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\\\mathrm{CO}\\\\right)_{2} \\\\mathrm{O} / \\\\mathrm{H}^{+}$  and   $\\\\left(\\\\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\\\mathrm{CO}\\\\right)_{2} \\\\mathrm{O} / \\\\mathrm{H}^{+}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'<img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/68_29353_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',2023,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','chemistry/68_29353_question_1.png'),
(29355,'chemistry',67,1083,2591,'Which of the following is most acidic?','[{"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'The phenoxide ion left from<br> o-hydroxybenzaldehyde and $p$-hydroxybenzaldehyde are stabilised by -I and -R effect of the - $\\mathrm{CHO}$ group. But in $o$-isomer due to chelation, it is difficult to remove the $\\mathrm{H}$-atom. Hence, <br>p-hydroxybenzaldehyde is the strongest acid.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
