(29358,'chemistry',69,1176,2822,'Acetamide and ethylamine can be distinguished by reacting with','[{"text": "aqueous $\\\\mathrm{HCl}$ and heat", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "aqueous $\\\\mathrm{NaOH}$ and heat", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "acidified $\\\\mathrm{KMnO}_{4}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "bromine water", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Acetamide and ethylamine can be distinguished by heating with $\\mathrm{NaOH}$ solution when acetamide liberates $\\mathrm{NH}_{3}$ but ethylamine does not. <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/69_29358_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(29359,'chemistry',67,1089,2613,'Which of the following reactions involves carbon- carbon bond formation?','[{"text": "Reimer-Tiemann reaction", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Hydroboration-oxidation", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Cannizzaro reaction", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Reaction of primary alcohol with PCC", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'(a) Reimer-Tiemann reaction <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/67_29359_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />  $\\therefore \\mathrm{C}-\\mathrm{C}$ bond is formed.<br>(b) Hydroboration-oxidation  <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/67_29359_explanation_2.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />   $\\therefore \\mathrm{C}-\\mathrm{O}$ bond is formed.<br>(c) Cannizzaro reaction <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/67_29359_explanation_3.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />   $\\therefore \\mathrm{C}-\\mathrm{OH}$ and $\\mathrm{C}=\\mathrm{O}$ bonds are formed.(d) Reaction of $1^{\\circ}$ alcohol by PCC <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/67_29359_explanation_4.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' /> $\\therefore \\mathrm{C}=\\mathrm{O}$ bond is formed.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(29360,'chemistry',70,1254,3061,'$\\mathrm{\\beta}$ - pleated sheets of polypeptide chains are present in','[{"text": "secondary structure", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "primary structure", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "tertiary structure", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "quaternary structure", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'$\\mathrm{\\beta}$-pleated sheets of polypeptide chains are present in the secondary structure of proteins. All peptide chains are strecthed out to nearly maximum extension and then laid ide by side which are held together by intermolecular hydrogen bonds.     <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/70_29360_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2019,'MHTCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(29361,'chemistry',68,1113,2670,'8 g of monohydric alcohol added to ethyl magnesium iodide in ether liberates ${\\mathrm{2240 \\mathrm{~cm}^{3}}}$ of ethane at STP. This monohydric alcohol when oxidised using pyridinium chloro chromate, forms a carbonyl compound that answers silver mirror test (Tollen\'s test). The monohydric alcohol is','[{"text": "Butan-2-ol", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "2, 2-Dimethyl propan-1-ol", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Pentan-2-ol", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "2, 2-Dimethyl ethan-1-ol", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'${\\mathrm{2240 \\mathrm{~cm}^{3}}}$ ethane is given by 8.8 g alcohol ${\\mathrm{\\therefore 22400 \\mathrm{~cm}^{3}}}$ (1mole) ethane is given by<br>${\\mathrm{=\\dfrac{8.8 \\times 22400}{2240}=88 \\mathrm{~g}}}$<br>The carbonyl compound that can oxidise Tollen\'s reagent is aldehyde and it is obtained by the mild oxidation of primary alcohol.So, 88 gram is the molar mass of alcohol. Of the given options, 2,2-Dimethylpropan-1-ol is ${\\mathrm{1^{\\circ}}}$ alcohol with molar mass of ${\\mathrm{88 \\mathrm{~g} \\mathrm{~mol}^{-}}}$. so, the correct option is (2).',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2024,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(29362,'chemistry',69,1176,2816,'The strongest base among the following is','[{"text": "$\\\\mathrm{C}_{6} \\\\mathrm{H}_{5} \\\\mathrm{NH}_{2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\left(\\\\mathrm{C}_{6} \\\\mathrm{H}_{5}\\\\right)_{2} \\\\mathrm{NH}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{NH}_{3}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\left(\\\\mathrm{C}_{2} \\\\mathrm{H}_{5}\\\\right)_{2} \\\\mathrm{NH}$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Basic nature of amines is due to the presence of lone pair of electrons on nitrogen atom which is available for the bond formation with Lewis acid. Due to the +I-effect, $2^{\\circ}$ amine is more basic than $1^{\\circ}$ amine and $\\mathrm{NH}_{3}$. In case of aromatic amines, the lone pair on nitrogen atom are involved in resonance, therefore not available for bond formation, so aromatic amines are less basic.Hence, $\\left(\\mathrm{C}_{2} \\mathrm{H}_{5}\\right)_{2} \\mathrm{NH}$ is the strongest base among the given options.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(29363,'chemistry',67,1083,2601,'In which of the following compounds $-\\mathrm{OH}$ group is least reactive?','[{"text": "", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "All are equally reactive", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'In salicylaldehyde, the $-\\mathrm{OH}$ group is less reactive than in m-and p-isomers due to chelation.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(29364,'chemistry',68,1113,2668,'Fehling\'s solution \' ${\\mathrm{A}}$ \' is','[{"text": "aqueous copper sulphate", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "alkaline copper sulphate", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "alkaline solution of sodium potassium tartrate(Rochelle\'s salt)", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "aqueous sodium citrate", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Fehling solution \' A \' = Aqueous copper sulphate<br> Fehling solution \' B \' = Alkaline sodium potassium tartrate (Rochelle\'s salt)',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2024,'NEET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(29365,'chemistry',70,1256,3067,'The enzyme which converts maltose to glucose is','[{"text": "maltase", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "insulin", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "lysine", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "zymase", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'The enzyme which converts maltose to glucose is maltase. During digestion, starch is partially transformed into maltose by pancreatic or salivary enzymes called amylases; maltase secreated by the intestine then converts maltose into glucose. The glucose so produced is either utilised by the body or stored in the liver as glycogen.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2019,'MHTCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(29366,'chemistry',69,1176,2816,'Which of the following statements are correct?','[{"text": "Aniline is a stronger base than ethyl amine", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Aniline is a stronger base than p-methoxyaniline", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Aniline must be acetylated before nitration with an acid derivative", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Aniline is soluble in an ammonium hydroxide solution", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Only statement (3) is correct, whereas statement ( 1 , 2 and 4 ) are incorrect. Basic character depends upon the availability of lone pair of electrons for donation. Aliphatic amines are generally more basic than aromatic amines because lone pair takes part in delocalisation in case of aromatic amines. Presence of electron releasing group at benzene nucleus increases the basic character of amine. Amino group strongly activates the ring through delocalisation of lone pair of electrons of $\\mathrm{N}$-atom over the aromatic ring. Thus, electron density increases more at $o$ - and $p$ - positions as compared to $m$-position.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(29367,'chemistry',67,1083,2601,'When o-or p-phenolsulphonic acid is treated with bromine water, the product formed is','[{"text": "2,4-dibromophenol", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "2,4,6-tribromophenol", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "3-bromophenol boric acid", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "3,5-dibromophenol", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Bromination of o-or p-phenolsulphonic acid occurs with simultaneous replacement of $\\mathrm{SO}_{3} \\mathrm{H}$-group by $\\mathrm{Br}$-atom to ultimately give 2,4,6-tribromophenol.',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(29368,'chemistry',68,1120,2687,'Match Column I with Column II.<div class="matching-question"></div>','[{"text": "${\\\\rm{A}} - {\\\\rm{S,B}} - {\\\\rm{P,C}} - {\\\\rm{R,D}} - {\\\\rm{Q}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{A}} - {\\\\rm{R,B}} - {\\\\rm{P,C}} - {\\\\rm{Q,D}} - {\\\\rm{S}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\,{\\\\rm{A}} - {\\\\rm{S,B}} - {\\\\rm{P,C}} - {\\\\rm{Q,D}} - {\\\\rm{R}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\,{\\\\rm{A}} - {\\\\rm{P,B}} - {\\\\rm{S,C}} - {\\\\rm{Q,D}} - {\\\\rm{R}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'<img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/68_29368_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />  (reductive ozonolysis)  <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/68_29368_explanation_2.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />  It is Friedel-Crafts acylation reaction.  <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/68_29368_explanation_3.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' /> Secondary alcohols are oxidised to ketones by ${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{CrO}_{3}}}$  <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/68_29368_explanation_4.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />  Irrespective of length of side chain, alkyl benzenes with one benzylic hydrogen are oxidised to benzoic acids.',NULL,'Medium','Match the following',2024,'NEET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','chemistry/68_29368_question_1.png'),
(29369,'chemistry',69,1176,2816,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Basicity of $\\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\mathrm{CH}_{2} \\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ (I), $\\mathrm{NH}_{3}$ (II) and $\\mathrm{C}_{6} \\mathrm{H}_{5} \\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ (III) is in the order I $>$ II $>$ III.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Electron-donating groups (such as alkyl group) increase the basicity of amines, and electron-withdrawing groups (such as aryl group) decrease the basicity of amines.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.Presence of electron releasing group at benzene nucleus increases the basic character whereas electron withdrawing group decrease the basic character. Hence, correct order of basicity is<br>${\\rm{C}}{{\\rm{H}}_3}{\\rm{C}}{{\\rm{H}}_2}{\\rm{N}}{{\\rm{H}}_2} > {\\rm{N}}{{\\rm{H}}_3} > {{\\rm{C}}_6}{{\\rm{H}}_5}{\\rm{N}}{{\\rm{H}}_2}$<br>${\\rm{(I)}}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{\\rm{(II)}}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{\\rm{(III)}}$',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(29370,'chemistry',70,1261,3076,'Dialysis can be used to separate','[{"text": "glucose and fructose", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "protein and starch", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "glucose and protein", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "glucose and $\\\\mathrm{\\\\mathrm{NaCl}}$.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,NULL,NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2012,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(29371,'chemistry',67,1083,2596,'At low temperature, phenol reacts with $\\mathrm{Br}_{2}$ in $\\mathrm{CS}_{2}$ to form','[{"text": "m-bromophenol", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "o and p-bromophenol", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "p-bromophenol", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "2,4,6-tribromophenol", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'<img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/67_29371_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' /> In the presence of non-polar solvent $\\left(\\mathrm{CS}_{2}\\right)$, the ionisation of phenol is suppressed. The ring is slightly activated and hence, mono substitution occurs. Low temperature favours the formation of p-product.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(29372,'chemistry',68,1111,2648,'Identify the product ${\\mathrm{X}}$ in the given reaction.<br>${{\\text{H}}_{\\text{2}}}{\\text{C}} = {\\text{CHC}}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{2}}}{\\text{CN}}\\xrightarrow[{2.\\,\\,{{\\text{H}}^ + }}]{{1.\\,\\,{\\text{PhMgBr}}}}{\\text{X}}\\,\\,{\\text{is}}$','[{"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'<img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/68_29372_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(29373,'chemistry',69,1176,2816,'The incorrect order with respect to the acidic strength is','[{"text": "formic acid $>$ acetic acid $>$ propionic acid", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "cyclohexanol $ < $ phenol $ < $ benzoic acid", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "benzamide $ < $ aniline $ < $ cyclohexylamine", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{FCH}_{2} \\\\mathrm{COOH}>\\\\mathrm{ClCH}_{2} \\\\mathrm{COOH}>\\\\mathrm{BrCH}_{2} \\\\mathrm{COOH}$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Usually presence of electron withdrawing groups such as F, $\\mathrm{Cl}, \\mathrm{CHO}$ etc., increase the acidic strength and presence of electron releasing groups such as $ - {\\rm{R}}, - {\\rm{OC}}{{\\rm{H}}_3}$ etc., decrease the acidic strength. Aromatic compounds are more acidic than their corresponding aliphatic compounds due to resonance stabilisation. Hence, the correct order of acidic strength is <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/69_29373_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(29384,'chemistry',69,1176,2819,'Reaction of nitrous acid with aliphatic primary amine in cold gives','[{"text": "a diazonium salt", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "an alcohol", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "a nitrite", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "a dye", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Aliphatic primary amine reacts with nitrous acid in cold to give alcohol. <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/69_29384_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(29374,'chemistry',67,1075,2566,'<br>$\\begin{gathered}\n  {\\text{Iso}}\\,{\\text{ - }}\\,{\\text{propyl}}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\text{chloride + A}}\\xrightarrow{\\Delta } \\hfill \\\\\n  \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{\\text{2  -  ethoxy}}{\\mkern 1mu} \\,{\\text{propane}}{\\mkern 1mu} \\,{\\text{ + }}\\,{\\text{NaCl}} \\hfill \\\\ \n\\end{gathered} $.<br>The compound A  is','[{"text": "$\\\\mathrm{C}_{2} \\\\mathrm{H}_{5} \\\\mathrm{Cl}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{C}_{2} \\\\mathrm{H}_{5} \\\\mathrm{ONa}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{CH}_{2} \\\\mathrm{~N}_{2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{C}}{{\\\\rm{H}}_{\\\\rm{3}}}{\\\\rm{ONa}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'<img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/67_29374_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(29375,'chemistry',70,1257,3068,'Which one of the following vitamins is not stored in adipose tissue?','[{"text": "$\\\\mathrm{\\\\mathrm{B}_{6}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{\\\\mathrm{D}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{\\\\mathrm{E}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{\\\\mathrm{A}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Vitamins $\\mathrm{A, D}$ and $\\mathrm{E}$ are fat soluble, hence they can be stored in adipose tissue. Vitamin<br>$\\mathrm{\\mathrm{B}_{6}}$ is water soluble hence, it cannot be stored in adipose tissue.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2020,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(29376,'chemistry',68,1111,2647,'Which of the following substrates give the same product on the reduction with DIBAL-H?','[{"text": "${\\\\mathrm{\\\\mathrm{CH}_{3}\\\\left(\\\\mathrm{CH}_{2}\\\\right)_{9} \\\\mathrm{CN}}}$ and  ${\\\\mathrm{\\\\mathrm{CH}_{3}\\\\left(\\\\mathrm{CH}_{2}\\\\right)_{9} \\\\mathrm{COOH}}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\mathrm{\\\\mathrm{CH}_{3}\\\\left(\\\\mathrm{CH}_{2}\\\\right)_{9} \\\\mathrm{CN}}}$ and ${\\\\mathrm{\\\\mathrm{CH}_{3}\\\\left(\\\\mathrm{CH}_{2}\\\\right)_{9} \\\\mathrm{COOC}_{2} \\\\mathrm{H}_{5}}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${\\\\mathrm{\\\\mathrm{CH}_{3}\\\\left(\\\\mathrm{CH}_{2}\\\\right)_{9} \\\\mathrm{COOH}}}$ and  $\\\\mathrm{CH}_{3}\\\\left(\\\\mathrm{CH}_{2}\\\\right)_{9} \\\\mathrm{CHO}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\mathrm{\\\\mathrm{CH}_{3}\\\\left(\\\\mathrm{CH}_{2}\\\\right)_{9} \\\\mathrm{COOH}}}$ and  $\\\\mathrm{CH}_{3}\\\\left(\\\\mathrm{CH}_{2}\\\\right)_{9} \\\\mathrm{COOC}_{2} \\\\mathrm{H}_{5}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Esters and cyanides on reaction with DIBAL-H give aldehydes.<br><img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/68_29376_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(29377,'chemistry',69,1176,2819,'Which of the following is soluble in sodium hydroxide?','[{"text": "", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Only $\\mathrm{N}$-alkylbenzene sulphonamide is soluble in $\\mathrm{NaOH}$ due to the presence of acidic hydrogen on $\\mathrm{N}$-atom. These $\\mathrm{N}$-alkyl benzene sulphonamides are formed by primary amines on reaction with benzene sulphonyl chloride. <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/69_29377_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(29378,'chemistry',67,1075,2566,'The product (P) is','[{"text": "", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'<img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/67_29378_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' /> this is an example of Williamson\'s synthesis.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','chemistry/67_29378_question_1.png'),
(29379,'chemistry',68,1111,2648,'Which of the following reactions is/are correct regarding the preparation of ketone?','[{"text": "Treatment of acyl chlorides with dialkyl cadmium, prepared by the reaction of cadmium chloride with Grignard reagent, gives ketones.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "When benzene or substituted benzene is treated with acid chloride in the presence of anhydrous aluminium chloride, it gives the corresponding ketone.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Treating an alkyl nitrile with Grignard reagent followed by hydrolysis yields a ketone.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "All of the above", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'All the given methods are correct for the preparation of ketones.<br><img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/68_29379_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
