(29382,'chemistry',67,1075,2566,'Which of the following is an approximate set of reactants for the preparation of 1-methoxy- 4-nitrobenzene?','[{"text": "A", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "B", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both A and B", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "None of these", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'When the alkyl halide is primary, alkoxide (or aryloxide) attacks on it and ether is obtained as a major product. <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/67_29382_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />  Aryl halides generally do not react with sodium alkoxide but presence of strong electron with drawing group like $ - {\\rm{N}}{{\\rm{O}}_{\\rm{2}}}$ at ortho/para position facilitates the reaction.Thus,reaction given option (a) also gives<br>1-methoxy-4-nitrobenzene',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','chemistry/67_29382_question_1.png'),
(29383,'chemistry',68,1113,2656,'Study the mechanism given below carefully:<br> <br>Select the correct statement for the above diagram from the options given below.','[{"text": "A nucleophile attacks the electrophilic carbon of the polar carbonyl group from direction parallel to the plane of ${\\\\mathrm{\\\\mathrm{sp}^{2}}}$ hybridized orbitals of carbonyl carbon.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "The hybridization of carbon changes from sp to ${\\\\mathrm{\\\\mathrm{sp}^{2}}}$ in this process.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "The net result is the addition of nucleophile and ${\\\\mathrm{\\\\mathrm{H}^{+}}}$across the carbon-oxygen double bond.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Step (2) is rate determination step.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Nucleophile attacks the electrophilic carbon group in direction perpendicular to the carbonyl group and its hybridization changes from ${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{sp}^{2}}}$ to ${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{sp}^{3}}}$ and slow step is the rate determining step.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','chemistry/68_29383_question_1.png'),
(29386,'chemistry',67,1084,2604,'Tert-butyl methyl ether on heating with anhydrous $\\mathrm{HI}$ in ether gives','[{"text": "$\\\\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\\\mathrm{OH}+\\\\left(\\\\mathrm{CH}_{3}\\\\right)_{3} \\\\mathrm{CI}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\\\mathrm{I}+\\\\left(\\\\mathrm{CH}_{3}\\\\right)_{3} \\\\mathrm{COH}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\\\mathrm{I}+\\\\left(\\\\mathrm{CH}_{3}\\\\right)_{3} \\\\mathrm{CI}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "None of these", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Here, ether is the solvent. Being less polar, it favours $\\mathrm{S}_{\\mathrm{N}} 2$ reaction and the nucleophile $\\mathrm{I}^{-}$attacks $1^{\\circ}$ carbon of $\\mathrm{CH}_{3}$. <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/67_29386_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
