(29411,'chemistry',69,1176,2822,'Which of the following chemicals are used to manufacture methyl isocyanate that caused "Bhopal gas tragedy"?<br>I. Methylamine<br>II. Phosgene<br>III. Phosphine<br>IV. Dimethyl amine','[{"text": "I and III", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "III and IV", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "I and II", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "II and IV", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Methyl isocyanate, MIC $\\left(\\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\mathrm{NCO}\\right)$ is industrially prepared by the reaction of methyl amine and phosgene (carbonyl chloride). <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/69_29411_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' /> On the night of December 2,1984 a major gas tragedy struck Bhopal due to leakage of MIC from storage tank of Union Carbide Ltd. Plant. MIC was used in the manufacture of insecticide.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(29412,'chemistry',67,1076,2569,'An ether is more volatile than an alcohol having the same molecular formula. This is due to','[{"text": "dipolar character of ethers", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "alcohols having resonance structures", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "intermolecular hydrogen bonding in ethers", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "intermolecular hydrogen bonding in alcohols.", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'The reason for the lesser volatility of alcohols than ethers is the intermolecular association of a large number of molecules due to hydrogen bonding as $-\\mathrm{OH}$ group is highly polarised. <br> <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/67_29412_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' /> No such hydrogen bonding is present in ethers.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(29415,'chemistry',67,1076,2569,'The decreasing order of boiling points of the following alcohols is. <br> I. 1-pentanol. <br> II. 2-pentanol <br> III. 3-methyl-2-butanol <br> IV. 2-methyl-2-butanol.','[{"text": "I $>$ II $>$ III $>$ IV", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "I $>$ III $>$ II $>$ IV", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "II $>$ I $>$ III $>$ IV", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "IV $>$ III $>$ II $>$ I", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Higher the branching, lower is the boiling point.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(29416,'chemistry',70,1247,3032,'Which reagent among the following is used to confirm presence of aldehydic carbonyl group in glucose?','[{"text": "Dilute nitric acid", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Bromine water", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Acetic anhydride", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Hydroxylamine", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Bromine water is used to confirm presence of aldehydic carbonyl group in glucose.<br><img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/70_29416_explanation_1.jpg\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' /> <br>Glucose gets oxidised to six carbon carboxylic acid (gluconic acid) on reaction with a mild oxidising agent ( $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{Br}_{2}}$ water).',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(29417,'chemistry',68,1113,2657,'Read the Statement - A and Statement - B carefully to mark the correct options given below:<div class="matching-question"><div class="column-left">Sodium hydrogen sulphite adds to aldehydes and ketones is a useful method for separation and the purification of aldehydes.</div><div class="column-right">The hydrogen sulphite addition compound is water soluble and can be converted back to the original carbonyl compound by treating it with dilute mineral acid or alkali.</div></div>','[{"text": "Statement A is correct but Statement B is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Statement A is incorrect but Statement B is correct.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both statements are correct.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Statements are incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Both statements are correct.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ type-2',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
