(29496,'chemistry',67,1084,2607,'Which reaction is used in Zeisel\'s method of detection and estimation of alkoxy group?','[{"text": "Alkyl halide + sodium alkoxide", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Natural products + excess of HI", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Ether $+\\\\mathrm{O}_{2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Alcohol + acid", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'In Zeisel\'s method the compound is heated with excess HI, forming an alcohol and iodomethane. The iodomethane is distilled off and added into an acidic solution of silver nitrate where it precipitates silver iodide which is then filtered, weighed and the number of iodine atoms as well as the number of methoxy groups can be calculated.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(29497,'chemistry',69,1174,2809,'Which of the following converts $\\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\mathrm{CONH}_{2}$ to $\\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ ?','[{"text": "$\\\\mathrm{NaBr}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{NaOBr}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{Br}_{2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "None of the above.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'$\\mathrm{NaOBr}$ converts amide group $\\left(-\\mathrm{CONH}_{2}\\right)$ to amine group $\\left(-\\mathrm{NH}_{2}\\right)$.<br>$(\\mathop {{\\text{C}}{{\\text{H}}_3}{\\text{CON}}{{\\text{H}}_2}}\\limits_{{\\text{Acetamide}}} \\xrightarrow{{{\\text{NaOBr}}}}\\mathop {{\\text{C}}{{\\text{H}}_3}{\\text{N}}{{\\text{H}}_2}}\\limits_{{\\text{Methyl}}\\,\\,{\\text{amine}}} )$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(29498,'chemistry',70,1258,3071,'Adenosine is an example of','[{"text": "purine base", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "nucleoside", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "nucleotide", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "pyrimidine base.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'A nucleoside contains only two components of nucleic acids- a pentose sugar and a nitrogenous base.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2015,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(29499,'chemistry',68,1113,2661,'Benzophenone can be converted into benzene using','[{"text": "fused alkali", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "anhydrous $\\\\mathrm{AlCl}_{3}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "sodium amalgam in water", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "acidified dichromate", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'<img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/68_29499_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(29500,'chemistry',67,1083,2601,'2, 4, 6-Tribromophenol is formed when the organic compound \' X \' reacts with \' Y \' in the presence of \' Z \'. What are X ,Y and Z respectively?','[{"text": "$\\\\mathrm{C}_{6} \\\\mathrm{H}_{5} \\\\mathrm{OH} ; \\\\mathrm{Br}_{2} ; \\\\mathrm{CS}_{2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{C}_{6} \\\\mathrm{H}_{5} \\\\mathrm{OH} ; \\\\mathrm{Br}_{2} ; \\\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\\\mathrm{O}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{C}_{6} \\\\mathrm{H}_{5} \\\\mathrm{CHO} ; \\\\mathrm{Br}_{2} ; \\\\mathrm{FeBr}_{3}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{C}_{6} \\\\mathrm{H}_{6} ; \\\\mathrm{Br}_{2} ; \\\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\\\mathrm{O}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'<img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/67_29500_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(29501,'chemistry',69,1176,2823,'$\\mathrm{C}_{6} \\mathrm{H}_{5} \\mathrm{~N}_{2} \\mathrm{Cl}$ in presence of KCN and CuCN gives an organic compound, which on acid hydrolysis gives \' X \'. The compound \' X \' may be','[{"text": "benzonitrile", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "benzoic acid", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "benzonitrite", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "phenylacetic acid.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'<img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/69_29501_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(29502,'chemistry',70,1258,3071,'What type of sugar molecule is present in DNA?','[{"text": "D-3-deoxyribose", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "D-ribose", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "D-2-deoxyribose", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "D-glucopyranose", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'The sugar component in RNA is ribose and in DNA is $\\mathrm{2^{\\prime}}$-deoxyribose.   <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/70_29502_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2017,'MHTCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(29503,'chemistry',68,1111,2646,'Name the reagent used to bring about the following transformation: But-2-ene to ethanal','[{"text": "$\\\\mathrm{K}_{2} \\\\mathrm{Cr}_{2} \\\\mathrm{O}_{7}$ in acidic medium", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{CrO}_{2} \\\\mathrm{Cl}_{2} / \\\\mathrm{H}_{3} \\\\mathrm{O}^{+}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{PCC}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{O}_{3} / \\\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\\\mathrm{O}-\\\\mathrm{Zn}$ dust", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'<img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/68_29503_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(29504,'chemistry',67,1075,2567,'Which of the following is treated to get anisole?','[{"text": "$\\\\mathrm{C}_{6} \\\\mathrm{H}_{5} \\\\mathrm{ONa}+\\\\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\\\mathrm{OH}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{C}_{6} \\\\mathrm{H}_{5} \\\\mathrm{OH}+\\\\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\\\mathrm{Cl}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{C}_{6} \\\\mathrm{H}_{5} \\\\mathrm{ONa}+\\\\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\\\mathrm{I}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{C}_{6} \\\\mathrm{H}_{5} \\\\mathrm{OH}+\\\\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\\\mathrm{OH}$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'<img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/67_29504_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />  So, the correct option is (3).',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(29505,'chemistry',69,1176,2823,'$\\mathrm{C}_{6} \\mathrm{H}_{5} \\mathrm{NH}_{2}+\\mathrm{C}_{6} \\mathrm{H}_{5} \\mathrm{CHO} \\rightarrow \\mathrm{C}_{6} \\mathrm{H}_{5} \\mathrm{~N}=\\mathrm{CHC}_{6} \\mathrm{H}_{5}$ involves','[{"text": "addition/elimination", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "substitution", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "addition", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "elimination.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'<img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/69_29505_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(29514,'chemistry',68,1113,2670,'The reaction by which benzaldehyde is converted in benzyl alcohol, is','[{"text": "Fittig reaction", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Cannizzaro reaction", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Wurtz reaction", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "aldol condensation", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'The reaction is called Cannizzaro reaction and involves oxidation and reduction of same compound.',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(29545,'chemistry',67,1078,2583,'How many of the following compounds will give positive iodoform test?<br>Butan-2-ol, 1-cyclobutylethanol, benzyl alcohol, ethanol, propan-1-ol, phenol, propan-2-ol, pentan-3-ol, methanol, 2-methylpropan-1-ol, butan-1-ol, 1-phenylethanol','[{"text": "6", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "3", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "1", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "5", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Iodoform test is given by alcohols containing ${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{CH}_{3}-\\mathrm{CH}(\\mathrm{OH})}}$ - group linked to carbon or hydrogen atom. Thus, butan-2-ol, 1-cyclobutylethanol, ethanol, propan-2-ol, 1-phenylethanol will give positive iodoform test.',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(29506,'chemistry',68,1113,2656,'Arrange the following compounds in increasing order of their reactivity in nucleophilic addition reactions. Ethanal (I), propanal (II), propanone (III), butanone (IV)','[{"text": "III $ < $ II $ < $ I $ < $ IV", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "II $ < $ I $ < $ III $ < $ IV", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "IV $ < $ III $ < $ II $ < $ I", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "I $ < $ II $ < $ III $ < $ IV", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Carbonyl group is polar $(\\stackrel{\\delta+}{\\mathrm{C}}=\\stackrel{\\delta-}{\\mathrm{O}})$ because of the presence of partial positive charge at carbon and partial negative charge at oxygen. Presence of electron releasing substituent increases the electron density at carbon, thus, reduces the magnitude of positive charge and hence, the polar nature. Thus, it can be said that as the number of alkyl group or size of alkyl group increases, reactivity decreases. Thus, the order of reactivity is <br>${{\\text{Butanone}}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu}  < {\\mkern 1mu} {\\text{Propanone}}{\\mkern 1mu}  < {\\mkern 1mu} {\\text{Propanal}}{\\mkern 1mu}  < {\\mkern 1mu} {\\text{Ethanal}}}$<br>${{\\text{(IV)}}\\,{\\text{ < }}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\text{(III)}}{\\mkern 1mu} \\,{\\text{ < }}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\text{(II)}}\\,{\\text{ < }}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\text{(I)}}}$<br>(aldehydes are more reactive as compared to ketones.)',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(29507,'chemistry',67,1076,2570,'Which of the following is soluble in $\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O}$ ?','[{"text": "Phenol", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Alkenes", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Alcohols", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Alkanes", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Alcohols are soluble in water due to formation of intermolecular H -bonding.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(29508,'chemistry',70,1258,3071,'Which carbon atom of deoxyribose sugar in DNA does not contain','[{"text": "$\\\\mathrm{\\\\mathrm{C}_{5}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{\\\\mathrm{C}_{3}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{\\\\mathrm{C}_{2}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{\\\\mathrm{C}_{1}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Deoxyribose is a pentose sugar with five carbon atoms. The four carbon atoms of the ring are numbered $\\mathrm{1^{\\prime}, 2^{\\prime}, 3^{\\prime}}$ and $\\mathrm{4^{\\prime}}$. The carbon atom of $\\mathrm{-\\mathrm{CH}_{2} \\mathrm{OH}}$ is numbered $\\mathrm{5^{\\prime}}$. There are three -OH groups at position $\\mathrm{1^{\\prime}, 3^{\\prime}}$ and $\\mathrm{5^{\\prime}}$. Hydrogen atoms are attached to carbon atoms $\\mathrm{1^{\\prime}, 2^{\\prime}, 3^{\\prime}, 4^{\\prime}}$ and $\\mathrm{5^{\\prime}}$. Only carbon atom at $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{C}_{2}}$ of deoxyribose sugar in DNA does not contain -C OH bond.    <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/70_29508_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />    Note The prefix $\\mathrm{2^{\\prime}}$ - deoxy means that oxygen is missing from the $\\mathrm{2^{\\prime}}$ position of ribose.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2018,'MHTCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','chemistry/70_29508_question_1.png'),
(29509,'chemistry',69,1177,2825,'Action of $\\mathrm{NaNO}_{2}$ with dilute HCl on $\\mathrm{ArNH}_{2}$ yields $\\mathrm{ArN}_{2}^{+} \\mathrm{Cl}^{-}$. A similar reaction with cyclohexylamine will yield','[{"text": "benzenediazonium chloride", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "nitrosocyclohexane", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "nitrocyclohexane", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "cyclohexanol.", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Aromatic primary amines react with nitrous acid at 273 - 278 K to form are diazonium salts. On the other hand, aliphatic primary amines react with cold nitrous acid to give alcohols with evolution of $\\mathrm{N}_{2}$ gas. <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/69_29509_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(29510,'chemistry',68,1113,2670,'The compound, with which ethanal does not react, is','[{"text": "$\\\\mathrm{HCl}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{Cl}_{2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{PCl}_{5}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "aq. $\\\\mathrm{NaHSO}_{3}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'$\\mathrm{HCl}$ is a strong inorganic acid, hence it does not react with ethanal whereas, with other given reagents, it reacts in the following manner. <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/68_29510_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(29511,'chemistry',67,1077,2578,'The treatment of $\\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\mathrm{OH}$ with ${\\text{C}}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{3}}}{\\text{MgI}}$ releases 1.04 mL of a gas at STP. The mass of $\\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\mathrm{OH}$ added is','[{"text": "1.485 mg", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "2.98 mg", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "3.71 mg", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "4.47 mg", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'${\\text{C}}{{\\text{H}}_3}{\\text{OH}} + {\\text{C}}{{\\text{H}}_3}{\\text{MgI}} \\to {\\text{C}}{{\\text{H}}_4} + {\\text{C}}{{\\text{H}}_3}{\\text{OMgI}}$<br>${\\text{1mole}}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{\\text{1mole}}$ <br>1 mole of any gas at STP occupies $=22400 \\mathrm{~mL}$<br>$\\therefore$ No. of moles of $\\mathrm{CH}_{4}=\\dfrac{1.04}{22400}$<br>$=$ No. of moles of $\\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\mathrm{OH}=4.64 \\times 10^{-5}$<br>${{\\rm{No}}{\\rm{.}}\\,{\\rm{of}}\\,{\\rm{moles}}\\,\\,{\\rm{of}}\\,{\\rm{C}}{{\\rm{H}}_{\\rm{3}}}{\\rm{OH}}}$<br>${{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu}  = \\frac{{{\\rm{Mass}}\\,\\,{\\rm{of}}\\,{\\rm{C}}{{\\rm{H}}_{\\rm{3}}}{\\rm{OH}}}}{{{\\rm{Molar}}\\,\\,{\\rm{mass}}\\,{\\rm{of}}\\,{\\rm{C}}{{\\rm{H}}_{\\rm{3}}}{\\rm{OH}}}}}$<br>Mass of $\\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\mathrm{OH}=4.64 \\times 10^{-5} \\times 32$<br>${ = 1.485 \\times {{10}^3}{\\rm{\\,g}} = 1.485\\,{\\rm{mg}}}$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(29512,'chemistry',69,1176,2816,'Arrange the above compounds in the decreasing order of their basic strength.','[{"text": "$2>1>3$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1>2>3$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$3>1>2$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$3>2>1$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'In p-nitroaniline (2) due to -I effect of $-\\mathrm{NO}_{2}$ group the lone pair of electrons on nitrogen is not available for donation and hence, it will be least basic while in o -methylaniline (3) due to +I effect of $-\\mathrm{CH}_{3}$ group there is high electron density on nitrogen hence, will be most basic among the given options.<br>$\\therefore$ Order of basicity will be <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/69_29512_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','chemistry/69_29512_question_1.png'),
(29513,'chemistry',70,1258,3071,'Match the column I with column II and mark the appropriate choice.<div class="matching-question"><div class="column-left">A Nucleoside <br> B Nucleotide <br> C DNA <br> D RNA</div><div class="column-right">P  Sugar+base+phosph- oric acid group<br>Q Cytosine + uracil  <br>R Sugar + base<br>S Cytosine + thymine</div></div>','[{"text": "${\\\\text{A  -  R,  B  -  P,  C  -  S, D  -  Q }}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${\\\\text{A  -  P,  B  -  S,  C  -  R, D  -  Q }}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\text{A  -  Q,  B  -  R,  C  -  P, D  -  S }}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\text{A  -  S,  B  -  Q,  C  -  P, D  -  R }}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,NULL,NULL,'Medium','Match the following',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(29515,'chemistry',67,1078,2583,'A compound corresponding to molecular formula $\\mathrm{C}_{4} \\mathrm{H}_{10} \\mathrm{O}$ does not give reaction with cold aqueous $\\mathrm{KMnO}_{4}$ but reacts with $\\mathrm{PCl}_{5}$ evolving HCl . The most likely structural formula of the compound is','[{"text": "$\\\\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\\\mathrm{OCH}_{2} \\\\mathrm{CH}_{2} \\\\mathrm{CH}_{3}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\left(\\\\mathrm{CH}_{3}\\\\right)_{2} \\\\mathrm{CHCH}_{2} \\\\mathrm{OH}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\\\mathrm{CH}(\\\\mathrm{OH}) \\\\mathrm{CH}_{2} \\\\mathrm{CH}_{2} \\\\mathrm{CH}_{3}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\left(\\\\mathrm{CH}_{3}\\\\right)_{3} \\\\mathrm{COH}$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'$\\begin{gathered}\n  {{\\text{C}}_{\\text{4}}}{{\\text{H}}_{{\\text{10}}}}{\\text{O}}\\xrightarrow{{{\\text{ Cold aq}}{\\text{. KMn}}{{\\text{O}}_{\\text{4}}}}}{\\text{No}}\\,{\\text{reaction}} \\hfill \\\\\n   \\downarrow {\\text{PC}}{{\\text{l}}_{\\text{5}}} \\hfill \\\\\n  {\\text{HCl}} \\uparrow  \\hfill \\\\ \n\\end{gathered} $<br>Since the compound does not undergo oxidation, it is a tertiary alcohol.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL);
INSERT INTO `qb_questions` (`id`, `subject_id`, `chapter_id`, `topic_id`, `subtopic_id`, `question_html`, `options`, `correct_index`, `explanation`, `explanation_image_url`, `difficulty`, `qtype`, `year`, `tag`, `created_at`, `question_image_url`) VALUES
(29516,'chemistry',69,1177,2825,'Which of the following reactants combine to produce $\\mathrm{C}_{6} \\mathrm{H}_{6}, \\mathrm{~N}_{2}$ and HCl ?','[{"text": "$\\\\mathrm{C}_{6} \\\\mathrm{H}_{5} \\\\mathrm{~N}_{2} \\\\mathrm{Cl}$, Hot $\\\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\\\mathrm{O}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{C}_{6} \\\\mathrm{H}_{3} \\\\mathrm{~N}_{2} \\\\mathrm{Cl}, \\\\mathrm{C}_{6} \\\\mathrm{H}_{5} \\\\mathrm{OH}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{C}_{6} \\\\mathrm{H}_{5} \\\\mathrm{~N}_{2} \\\\mathrm{Cl}, \\\\mathrm{HCl}, \\\\mathrm{Cu}_{2} \\\\mathrm{Cl}_{2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{C}_{6} \\\\mathrm{H}_{5} \\\\mathrm{~N}_{2} \\\\mathrm{Cl}, \\\\mathrm{H}_{3} \\\\mathrm{PO}_{2}, \\\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\\\mathrm{O}$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'The replacement of the diazo group by hydrogen or the reduction of diazonium salts to arenes (hydrocarbons) can be carried out by using $\\mathrm{H}_{3} \\mathrm{PO}_{2}$ or phosphinic acid at room temperature preferably in presence of  $\\mathrm{Cu}(\\mathrm{I})$ salts as a catalyst. <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/69_29516_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(29517,'chemistry',70,1258,3072,'Which of the following is correct about $\\mathrm{\\mathrm{H}}$ bonding in DNA?','[{"text": "A-T, G-C", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "A-G, T-G", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "G-T, A-C", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "A-A, T-T", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'In DNA, adenine (A) forms two hydrogen bonds with thymine $\\mathrm{(\\mathrm{T})}$ and guanine $\\mathrm{(\\mathrm{G})}$ forms three hydogen bonds with cytosine(C).',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2016,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(29518,'chemistry',68,1113,2663,'Which of the following will respond to Cannizzaro\'s reaction?','[{"text": "2,2-dimethylpropanal", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Acetaldehyde", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Propionaldehyde", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Cinnamaldehyde", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'The aldehydes which do not contain $\\alpha$-hydrogen atom, give Cannizzaro\'s reaction. 2,2-dimethylpropanal does not contain any $\\alpha$-hydrogen atom and hence, undergoes Cannizzaro\'s reaction.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(29519,'chemistry',67,1078,2583,'Oxidation of an alcohol $\\left(\\mathrm{C}_{5} \\mathrm{H}_{12} \\mathrm{O}\\right)$ with acid dichromate gives a mixture of dimethyl ketone and acetic acid. The alcohol could be','[{"text": "2 -methyl-3-butanol", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "2-methylbutanol", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "2-methylbutan-2-ol", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "3-methylbutan-2-ol.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Tertiary alcohols are oxidised in acidic  $\\mathrm{K}_{2} \\mathrm{Cr}_{2} \\mathrm{O}_{7}$  to give a mixture of ketone and an acid. <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/67_29519_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(29520,'chemistry',69,1174,2809,'By heating alkyl halide with alcoholic ammonia ____ are obtained.','[{"text": "primary amines", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "secondary amines", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Tertiary amines", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "all of these", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Alkyl halides usually gives a mixture of primary, secondary and tertiary amines along with some quaternary ammonium halides.  <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/69_29520_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(29521,'chemistry',68,1112,2653,'Arrange the following compounds in decreasing order of their boiling points.<br>${\\text{C}}{{\\text{H}}_3}{\\text{CHO}},{\\text{C}}{{\\text{H}}_3}{\\text{C}}{{\\text{H}}_2}{\\text{OH}},{\\text{C}}{{\\text{H}}_3}{\\text{OC}}{{\\text{H}}_3},$<br>${\\text{C}}{{\\text{H}}_3}{\\text{C}}{{\\text{H}}_2}{\\text{C}}{{\\text{H}}_3}$','[{"text": "$\\\\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\\\mathrm{CH}_{2} \\\\mathrm{CH}_{3} < \\\\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\\\mathrm{OCH}_{3} < \\\\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\\\mathrm{CHO}$$ < \\\\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\\\mathrm{CH}_{2} \\\\mathrm{OH}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\\\mathrm{CH}_{2} \\\\mathrm{CH}_{3} < \\\\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\\\mathrm{CHO} < \\\\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\\\mathrm{OCH}_{3}$$ < \\\\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\\\mathrm{CH}_{2} \\\\mathrm{OH}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\\\mathrm{CH}_{2} \\\\mathrm{CH}_{3}>\\\\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\\\mathrm{CHO}>\\\\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\\\mathrm{OCH}_{3}$$>\\\\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\\\mathrm{CH}_{2} \\\\mathrm{OH}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\\\mathrm{CH}_{2} \\\\mathrm{OH}>\\\\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\\\mathrm{CHO}>\\\\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\\\mathrm{OCH}_{3}$$>\\\\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\\\mathrm{CH}_{2} \\\\mathrm{CH}_{3}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Among these only $\\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\mathrm{CH}_{2} \\mathrm{OH}$ undergoes H-bonding.<br> So, it will have the highest boiling point.<br> In the case of remaining compounds, <br>$\\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\mathrm{COCH}_{3}$ has slightly<br> higher boiling point, than $\\mathrm{H}_{3} \\mathrm{CCHO}$ and <br>$\\mathrm{H}_{3} \\mathrm{CCH}_{2} \\mathrm{CH}_{3}$.<br>This is due to higher molecular weight <br>of $\\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\mathrm{OCH}_{3}$ than $\\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\mathrm{CH}_{2} \\mathrm{CH}_{3}$ and $\\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\mathrm{CHO} .<br> \\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\mathrm{CHO}$ <br>has higher boiling point than alkane due to appreciable intermolecular attraction <br>(dipole-dipole interaction).<br>Thus, the correct order is<br>$\\begin{array}{l}\n{\\rm{C}}{{\\rm{H}}_{\\rm{3}}}{\\rm{C}}{{\\rm{H}}_{\\rm{2}}}{\\rm{C}}{{\\rm{H}}_{\\rm{3}}}{\\rm{ < }}\\,{\\rm{C}}{{\\rm{H}}_{\\rm{3}}}{\\rm{CHO}}\\\\\n{\\rm{ <  C}}{{\\rm{H}}_{\\rm{3}}}{\\rm{OC}}{{\\rm{H}}_{\\rm{3}}}{\\rm{ < }}\\,{\\rm{C}}{{\\rm{H}}_{\\rm{3}}}{\\rm{C}}{{\\rm{H}}_{\\rm{2}}}{\\rm{OH}}\n\\end{array}$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(29522,'chemistry',67,1089,2613,'A crystalline solid, $\\mathrm{C}_{7} \\mathrm{H}_{8} \\mathrm{O}$ reacts with sodium to liberate hydrogen. It is insoluble in water but soluble in dilute aqueous NaOH . Upon treatment with $\\mathrm{Br}_{2}$, only one monobrominated compound with the formula $\\mathrm{C}_{7} \\mathrm{H}_{7} \\mathrm{OBr}$ is formed. The original compound has the structure','[{"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'<img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/67_29522_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />  Since, the compound is soluble in dilute aqueous NaOH , it is phenol. Since, only one monobrominated product is formed on bromination, the compound is <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/67_29522_explanation_2.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(29523,'chemistry',70,1259,3074,'Hormones are secreted by ductless glands of human body. Iodine containing hormone is','[{"text": "insulin", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "adrenaline", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "testosterone", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "thyroxine.", "isCorrect": true}]',3,NULL,NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2017,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(29525,'chemistry',68,1111,2651,'Which of the following is the industrial method of preparation of acetaldehyde?','[{"text": "$\\\\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\\\mathrm{CN} \\\\xrightarrow[\\\\mathrm{HCl}]{\\\\mathrm{SnCl}_{2}} \\\\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\\\mathrm{CH}=\\\\mathrm{NH} \\\\xrightarrow{\\\\mathrm{H}_{3} \\\\mathrm{O}^{+}} \\\\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\\\mathrm{CHO}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\text{C}}{{\\\\text{H}}_{\\\\text{3}}}{\\\\text{COCl}} + {{\\\\text{H}}_{\\\\text{2}}}\\\\xrightarrow[{{\\\\text{BaS}}{{\\\\text{O}}_{\\\\text{4}}}}]{{{\\\\text{Pd}}}}{\\\\text{C}}{{\\\\text{H}}_{\\\\text{3}}}{\\\\text{CHO}} + {\\\\text{HCl}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{CH}_{2}=\\\\mathrm{CH}_{2}+\\\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\\\mathrm{O} \\\\xrightarrow{\\\\mathrm{Pd}^{2+}} \\\\mathrm{H}_{3} \\\\mathrm{CHO}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "None of the above", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'The industrial method of preparation of acetaldehyde is wacker process.<br>Reaction involved is as follows: <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/68_29525_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />  So, the correct option is (3).',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(29527,'chemistry',69,1174,2811,'Predict the final product (C) in the following sequence of reactions :<br>$\\begin{gathered}\n  {\\text{C}}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{3}}}{\\text{C}}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{2}}}{\\text{COOH}}\\xrightarrow{{{\\text{SOC}}{{\\text{l}}_{\\text{2}}}}}({\\text{A}})\\xrightarrow{{{{\\left( {{\\text{C}}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{3}}}{\\text{C}}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{2}}}{\\text{C}}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{2}}}} \\right)}_{\\text{2}}}{\\text{NH}}}} \\hfill \\\\\n  \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,({\\text{B)}}\\xrightarrow{{{\\text{LiAl}}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{4}}}}}({\\text{C)}} \\hfill \\\\ \n\\end{gathered} $','[{"text": "", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\\\mathrm{CH}_{2} \\\\mathrm{~N}\\\\left(\\\\mathrm{CH}_{2} \\\\mathrm{CH}_{2} \\\\mathrm{CH}_{3}\\\\right)_{2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\left(\\\\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\\\mathrm{CH}_{2} \\\\mathrm{CH}_{2}\\\\right)_{3} \\\\mathrm{~N}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'<img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/69_29527_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(29529,'chemistry',68,1113,2662,'Aldol condensation does not occur between','[{"text": "two different aldehydes", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "two different ketone", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "an aldehyde and ketone", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "an aldehyde and an ester", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'In aldol condensation, two or more molecules of same or different carbonyl compounds containing $\\alpha$-H-atom unite together in presence of dil. $\\mathrm{NaOH}, \\mathrm{Ba}(\\mathrm{OH})_{2}$, etc., to form a condensation product called aldol. This reaction may occur between (i) same or different aldehydes (ii) an aldehyde and ketone and (iii) same or different ketones.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(29531,'chemistry',69,1176,2821,'The final product is','[{"text": "", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both (2) and (3)", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'<img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/69_29531_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','chemistry/69_29531_question_1.png'),
(29532,'chemistry',68,1113,2670,'Compound $A$ undergoes Cannizzaro reaction and $B$ undergoes positive iodoform test. Therefore','[{"text": "$A=$ Acetaldehyde; B=1-pentanal", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{A = }}{{\\\\rm{C}}_{\\\\rm{6}}}{{\\\\rm{H}}_{\\\\rm{5}}}{\\\\rm{C}}{{\\\\rm{H}}_{\\\\rm{2}}}{\\\\rm{CHO;}}\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\kern 1pt} {\\\\rm{B = 3}} - {\\\\rm{pentanone}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "A Formaldehyde; B = 2-pentanone", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "A Propionaldehyde; B=1-pentanol", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Aldehydes having no $\\alpha-\\mathrm{H}$ atom, on heating with conc. alkali undergo disproportionation, known as Cannizzaro reaction. <br>Ketones do not give this reaction.<br>Compounds containing $\\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\mathrm{CO}$ - group or<br>$\\mathrm{CH}_{3}-\\mathrm{CH}-\\mathrm{OH}$ group give positive iodoform test. Among the given options, only option (3) i.e. $\\mathrm{HCHO}$ and<br>$\\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\mathrm{CH}_{2} \\mathrm{CH}_{2} \\mathrm{COCH}_{3}$ respond to the respective reactions.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(29533,'chemistry',67,1076,2569,'How many of the following compounds will have higher boiling point than butan-2-ol?','[{"text": "4", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "8", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "3", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "2", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Factors affecting boiling point of alcohols:<br>i. The boiling points of alcohols are higher than the corresponding alkanes, aldehydes, ketones, ethers, etc., of nearly same molecular masses due to the presence of intermolecular hydrogen bonding.<br>ii. The boiling point of alcohol increases with increase in molecular mass.<br>iii. The boiling point of alcohol decreases with increase in branching.<br>Thus pentan-1-ol (II), butan-1-ol (VI) and hexan-1-ol (VII) will have higher boiling point as compared to butan-2-ol.<br>So, the correct option is (3).',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','chemistry/67_29533_question_1.png'),
