(29566,'chemistry',68,1118,2677,'Which is correct statement?','[{"text": "o-nitrobenzoic acid is stronger acid than<br> 3,5 - dinitrobenzoic acid in $\\\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\\\mathrm{O}$.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Branched carboxylic acids are more acidic <br>than unbranched acids.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Butanoic acid is stronger acid than succinic acid.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Due to ortho effect, o-nitrobenzoic acid is stronger acid than 3,5-dinitrobenzoic acid in $\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O}$.<br>So, the correct option is (1).',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(29580,'chemistry',70,1250,3044,'Sucrose is dextrorotatory but after hydrolysis the mixture show laevorotation, this is because of','[{"text": "Racemic mixture is formed", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Laevorotation of fructose is more than dextrorotation of glucose", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Laevorotation of glucose is more than dextrorotation of fructose.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Sucrose is a non-reducing sugar", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Sucrose is dextrorotatory but after hydrolysis the mixture show laevorotation this is because of laevorotation of fructose is more than dextrorotation of glucose.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2023,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(29567,'chemistry',67,1075,2566,'How many of the following ethers CANNOT be prepared by Williamson\'s synthesis?<br>${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\mathrm{OCH}_{2} \\mathrm{CH}_{3}, \\mathrm{C}_{6} \\mathrm{H}_{5} \\mathrm{OCH}_{3},\\left(\\mathrm{C}_{6} \\mathrm{H}_{5}\\right)_{2} \\mathrm{O}}}$<br>${\\left( {{\\text{C}}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{3}}}} \\right)_{\\text{3}}}{\\text{COC}}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{3}}}{\\text{,}}{\\left( {{{\\text{C}}_{\\text{2}}}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{5}}}} \\right)_{\\text{2}}}{\\text{O,}}$<br>${\\left( {{\\text{C}}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{3}}}} \\right)_{\\text{3}}}{\\text{C - O - C}}{\\left( {{\\text{C}}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{3}}}} \\right)_{\\text{3}}}$','[{"text": "4", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "2", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "1", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "5", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'In Williamson\'s synthesis,<br>i. ${\\mathrm{ 1^{\\circ}}}$ alkyl halide and ${\\mathrm{3^{\\circ}}}$ alkoxide give best yield of corresponding ethers.<br>ii. In case of, ${\\mathrm{3^{\\circ}}}$ alkyl halide, elimination competes over substitution and alkene is obtained as a product.<br>iii. Aryl halides do not undergo nucleophilic substitution unless it is activated by the presence of electron withdrawing group.<br>Thus, ${\\mathrm{\\left(\\mathrm{C}_{6} \\mathrm{H}_{5}\\right)_{2} \\mathrm{O}}}$ and ${\\mathrm{\\left(\\mathrm{CH}_{3}\\right)_{3} \\mathrm{C}-\\mathrm{O}-\\mathrm{C}\\left(\\mathrm{CH}_{3}\\right)_{3}}}$ cannot be prepared by Williamson\'s synthesis.',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(29568,'chemistry',69,1176,2816,'Total number of compounds which are less basic than aniline is ____ .<br>p -Toluidine, p-chloroaniline, p-methoxyaniline,<br>m -nitroaniline, o -bromoaniline','[{"text": "1", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "2", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "3", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "4", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Substituted aniline containing electron withdrawing groups are less basic than aniline. Thus, p-chloroaniline, m-nitroaniline, o-bromoaniline are less basic than aniline.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(29569,'chemistry',70,1258,3072,'<div class="matching-question"><div class="column-left">A unit formed by the attachment of a base to 1 position of sugar is known as nucleoside.</div><div class="column-right">When nucleoside is linked to phosphorous acid at 5’-position of sugar moiety, we get nucleotide.</div></div>','[{"text": "Both statement A and B are incorrect", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Statement A is correct but Statement B is  incorrect.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Statement A is incorrect but Statement B is  correct.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both statement A and statement B are correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'<img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/70_29569_explanation_1.jpg\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />  Base link with 1\' position of sugar nucleoside so statement A is correct <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/70_29569_explanation_2.jpg\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />  $\\rightarrow \\quad$ When nucleoside is linked to phosphoric acid at $5^{\\prime}$ position of sugar moiety we get a nucleotide<br>$\\Downarrow$<br>Statement B is incorrect because it should be linked to phosphoric acid not phosphorous acid.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ type-2',2023,'NEET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(29570,'chemistry',68,1118,2685,'In the conversion, The sequence of the reagent used are','[{"text": "(i) $\\\\mathrm{SOCl}_{2}$(ii) $\\\\mathrm{N}_{3}^{-}$(iii) $\\\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\\\mathrm{O}$, heat", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "(i) $\\\\mathrm{SOCl}_{2}$(ii) $\\\\mathrm{NH}_{3}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "(i) $\\\\mathrm{SOCl}_{2}$(ii) $\\\\mathrm{NH}_{3}$<br>(iii) Heat", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "(i) $\\\\mathrm{SOCl}_{2}$(ii) $\\\\mathrm{KCN}$<br>(iii) $\\\\mathrm{LiAlH}_{4}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'For the conversion of carboxylic acids into primary amines, Curtius degradation method is employed.  <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/68_29570_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','chemistry/68_29570_question_1.png'),
(29573,'chemistry',68,1118,2683,'Oxalic acid on heating with conc. $\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{SO}_{4}$ gives','[{"text": "Only CO", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Only $\\\\mathrm{CO}_{2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{CO}_{2}+\\\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\\\mathrm{O}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{CO}} + {\\\\rm{C}}{{\\\\rm{O}}_{\\\\rm{2}}} + {{\\\\rm{H}}_{\\\\rm{2}}}{\\\\rm{O}}$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'<img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/68_29573_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' /> ( $\\because$ Conc. $\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{SO}_{4}$ is a strong dehydrating agent, i.e. removes water readily)',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(29575,'chemistry',70,1259,3074,'Thyroxine produced in the thyroid gland is an iodinated derivative of','[{"text": "tyrosine", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "tryptophan", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "threonine", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "lysine", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Thyroxine produced in the  thyroid gland is an iodinated derivative of tyrosine',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2023,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(29577,'chemistry',68,1118,2679,'Which of the following carboxylic acid undergoes decarboxylation easily?','[{"text": "$\\\\mathrm{C}_{6} \\\\mathrm{H}_{5}-\\\\mathrm{CO}-\\\\mathrm{CH}_{2}-\\\\mathrm{COOH}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{C}_{6} \\\\mathrm{H}_{5}-\\\\mathrm{CO}-\\\\mathrm{COOH}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'$\\beta$-keto carboxylic acids readily undergo decarboxylation. They get decarboxylated just by heating as, <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/68_29577_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(29578,'chemistry',67,1083,2591,'How many of the following compounds are less acidic as compared to phenol?<br>m-Nitrophenol, benzyl alcohol, o-cresol,<br>o-chlorophenol, ethanol, picric acid, cyclohexanol','[{"text": "1", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "2", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "3", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "4", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Phenols are more acidic in nature than alcohols. Also, in phenols the presence of electron releasing groups like ${\\mathrm{-\\mathrm{CH}_{3}}}$ in o-cresol decreases the acidic strength. Thus benzyl alcohol, o-cresol, ethanol and cyclohexanol are less acidic than phenol.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(29579,'chemistry',69,1176,2819,'Organic compound containing nitrogen is treated with Hinsberg\'s reagent followed by addition of excess alkali, which on acidification form insoluble compound.<br>Identify total number of amines from the following which will give above reaction.Propan-2-amine, triphenylamine, ethylmethylamine, propan-l-amine, N-phenylaniline, trimethylamine, benzylamine.','[{"text": "4", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "6", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "3", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "2", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Reaction of ${\\mathrm{1^{\\circ}}}$ amines with Hinsberg\'s reagent: <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/69_29579_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' /> Therefore, ${\\mathrm{1^{\\circ}}}$ amines like propan-2-amine, propan-1-amine and benzylamine will give above reaction.',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(29582,'chemistry',68,1118,2683,'Phenol and benzoic acid may be distinguished by','[{"text": "$\\\\mathrm{NaHCO}_{3}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{NaOH}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{Na}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both (1) and (3)", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Benzoic acid reacts with $\\mathrm{NaHCO}_{3}$ to give effervescence due to the evolution of $\\mathrm{CO}_{2}$. <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/68_29582_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' /> Phenol does not give effervescence. Phenol and benzoic acid cannot be distinguished by $\\mathrm{NaOH}$ and $\\mathrm{Na}$ because both can react with $\\mathrm{NaOH}$ and $\\mathrm{Na}$.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(29584,'chemistry',70,1257,3069,'Match column \\[{\\text{I}}\\] (vitamin) with column \\[{\\text{II}}\\] (deficiency disease) and identify the correct option.<div class="matching-question"><div class="column-left">A. Vitamin A<br>B. Vitamin D<br>C. Vitamin E<br>D. Vitamin K</div><div class="column-right">P. Muscular weakness<br>Q. Increased blood clotting time<br>R. Night blindness<br>S. Osteomalacia</div></div>','[{"text": "${\\\\text{A  -  R,   B  -  Q,   C  -  S,   D  -  P }}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\text{A  -  R,   B  -  S,   C  -  P,   D  -  Q}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${\\\\text{A  -  S,   B  -  R,   C  -  Q,   D  -  P}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\text{A  -  Q,   B  -  P,   C  -  R,   D  -  S}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Vitamin A-Night blindness<br>Vitamin D - Osteomalacia<br>Vitamin E - Muscular weakness<br>Vitamin K - Blood clotting time',NULL,'Medium','Match the following',2023,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(29586,'chemistry',68,1118,2684,'Here, the compound $X$ can be','[{"text": "acetic acid", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "formaldehyde", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "formic acid", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "propionic acid", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Only carboxylic acids give acyl chloride with $\\mathrm{SOCl}_{2} /$ pyridine. Among acids, only $\\mathrm{HCOOH}$ (formic acid) due to the presence of aldehyde group, reduces Fehling solution. <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/68_29586_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','chemistry/68_29586_question_1.png'),
(29588,'chemistry',70,1250,3045,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Maltose is a reducing sugar which gives two moles of D - glucose on hydrolysis<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Maltose has a $1,4-\\beta-$ glycosidic linkage.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both  Assertion and  Reason are correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Maltose, a disaccharide $\\left(\\mathrm{C}_{12} \\mathrm{H}_{22} \\mathrm{O}_{11}\\right)$ is a reducing sugar, which upon hydrolysis yields two moles of D- $(+)$ - glucose.<br>In maltose, the two D - glucose units are linked through $\\alpha$-glycosidic linkage between $\\mathrm{C}-1$ of a glucose unit and $\\mathrm{C}-4$ of the other. So, Assertion is true and reason is false.<br>',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',2005,'AIIMS','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(29589,'chemistry',67,1072,2555,'The possible number of structural isomers which contain chiral carbon atom(s) for alcohol with molecular formula of ${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{C}_{6} \\mathrm{H}_{14} \\mathrm{O}}}$ is ____ .','[{"text": "6", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "5", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "4", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "3", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'<img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/67_29589_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(29590,'chemistry',68,1118,2677,'The acidic nature of carboxylic acids ${\\rm{RCOOH}} \\to {\\rm{RCO}}{{\\rm{O}}^{\\rm{ - }}} + {{\\rm{H}}^{\\rm{ + }}}$ is due to','[{"text": "high degree of ionisation of carboxylic acids", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "greater resonance stabilisation of the carboxylic acid over the carboxylate ion", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "greater resonance stabilisation of the carboxylate ion over carboxylic acid", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "solubility of carboxylic acids in water", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'The acidic nature of carboxylic acids is due to greater resonance stabilisation of the carboxylate ion over carboxylic acid. The high degree of ionisation or the solubility of carboxylic acids in water is not the correct Reason for the acidic nature of carboxylic acids.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(29591,'chemistry',69,1174,2810,'The number of mole(s) of NaOH consumed per mole of amine produced is ____ .','[{"text": "1", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "2", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "3", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "4", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Hoffmann bromamide degradation: <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/69_29591_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' /> Thus, 4 moles of NaOH are consumed per mole of amine produced.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','chemistry/69_29591_question_1.png'),
(29593,'chemistry',68,1118,2677,'When propionic acid is treated with aqueous sodium bicarbonate, $\\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ is liberated. The \' $\\mathrm{C}$ \' of $\\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ comes from','[{"text": "methyl group", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "carboxylic acid group", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "methylene group", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "bicarbonate", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'<img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/68_29593_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />  Hence, carbon of $\\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ comes from bicarbonate.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(29595,'chemistry',70,1247,3033,'The beta and alpha glucose have different specific rotations. When either is dissolved in water, their rotation changes until the same fixed value results. This is called','[{"text": "Epimerisation", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Racemisation", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Anomerisation", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Mutarotation", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'<img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/70_29595_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2008,'AIIMS','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
