(29627,'chemistry',68,1118,2677,'In the following reaction, ${{\\rm{C}}_{\\rm{2}}}{{\\rm{H}}_{\\rm{5}}}{\\rm{OH}}$ acts as','[{"text": "electrophile", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "nucleophile", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "dehydrating agent", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "All of these", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Since, in the given reaction, $\\mathrm{RO}-\\mathrm{H}$ bond breaks, $\\mathrm{C}_{2} \\mathrm{H}_{5} \\mathrm{OH}$ acts as nucleophile.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','chemistry/68_29627_question_1.png'),
(29629,'chemistry',70,1253,3054,'Which of the following does not exist as a Zwitter ion?','[{"text": "Glycine", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Glutamic acid", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Sulphanilic acid", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "p - amino benzoic acid<br>", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'o and p-aminobenzoic acids do not exist as Zwitter ions. The lone pair of electrons on the $-\\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ group is delocalised over the benzene ring due to resonance effect.<br>As a result, acidic character of $-\\mathrm{COOH}$ group and basic character of $-\\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ group decreases. Therefore, the weakly acidic $-\\mathrm{COOH}$ group cannot transfer a proton to the weakly basic $-\\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ group. Thus, o or $\\mathrm{p}$-aminobenzoic acids do not exist as Zwitter ions. <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/70_29629_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2007,'AIIMS','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(29633,'chemistry',67,1073,2559,'Number of set of carbonyl compound and $\\mathrm{RMgX}$ that can produce $3^{\\circ}$ alcohol are ____.<br>','[{"text": "2", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "1", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "3", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "4", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'<img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/67_29633_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','chemistry/67_29633_question_1.png'),
(29634,'chemistry',70,1255,3066,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Disruption of the natural structure of a protein is called denaturation.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> The change in colour and appearance of precipitates during cooking an egg is due to denaturation.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both  Assertion and  Reason are correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Due to denaturation, a protein loses its biological activity. In this process, the protein molecule uncoils and forms a more random conformation and precipitates from the solution. Egg is a protein. On heating, it changes colour and precipitates. Therefore, both assertion and reason are correct but reason is not the correct explanation of assertion.<br>',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',2008,'AIIMS','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(29636,'chemistry',69,1176,2819,'An amine on reaction with $\\mathrm{HNO}_{2}$ evolved $\\mathrm{N}_{2}$. The amine on exhaustive methylation with $\\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\mathrm{I}$ formed a quaternary salt containing $59.07 \\%$ iodine. The molecular mass of amine is ____.','[{"text": "127", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "135", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "215", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "230", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Evolution of $\\mathrm{N}_{2}$ with $\\mathrm{HNO}_{2}$, suggests that $1^{\\circ}$ amine $59.07 \\mathrm{gm}$ of (I) is present in $100\\,\\,{\\rm{gm}}$ of compound<br>$127\\,\\,{\\rm{gm}}$  of $\\mathrm{I}(1 \\mathrm{~mol})$ is present in $\\dfrac{100 \\times 127}{59.07}=215$<br>Molecular mass $=215$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(29640,'chemistry',70,1254,3063,'The number of sub units present in haemoglobin are','[{"text": "2", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "3", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "4", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "5", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'One molecule of haemoglobin has four heme groups and each of them can take up one molecule of oxygen. Thus, one molecule of haemoglobin can combine with four molecules of $\\mathrm{O}_{2}$ to form oxyhaemoglobin<br>',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2013,'AIIMS','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(29643,'chemistry',68,1116,2673,'In the reaction, ${\\text{C}}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{3}}}{\\text{C}}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{2}}}{\\text{COON}}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{4}}}\\xrightarrow{{{{\\text{P}}_{\\text{2}}}{{\\text{O}}_{\\text{5}}}}}{\\text{A}}\\xrightarrow{{{{\\text{H}}^{\\text{ + }}}{\\text{/}}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{2}}}{\\text{O}}}}{\\text{B}}$  A and B are','[{"text": "$\\\\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\\\mathrm{CH}_{2} \\\\mathrm{CONH}_{2}, \\\\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\\\mathrm{CH}_{2} \\\\mathrm{COO}^{-}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\\\mathrm{CH}_{2} \\\\mathrm{COONH}_{2}, \\\\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\\\mathrm{CH}_{2} \\\\mathrm{COOH}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\\\mathrm{CH}_{2} \\\\mathrm{CN}, \\\\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\\\mathrm{CH}_{2} \\\\mathrm{COO}^{-}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\\\mathrm{CH}_{2} \\\\mathrm{CN}, \\\\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\\\mathrm{CH}_{2} \\\\mathrm{COOH}$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'<img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/68_29643_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(29644,'chemistry',70,1254,3065,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Haemoglobin is an oxygen carrier<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Oxygen binds as $\\mathrm{O}_{2}^{-}$to $\\mathrm{Fe}$ of haemoglobin.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both  Assertion and  Reason are correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Haemoglobin is an $\\mathrm{O}_{2}$ - carrier, so assertion is right, but the reason that oxygen binds as $\\mathrm{O}_{2}^{-}$ to $\\mathrm{Fe}$ is wrong, because $\\mathrm{O}_{2}$ binds to $\\mathrm{Fe}$ of heam part to form oxyhaemoglobin.<br>',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',2013,'AIIMS','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(29645,'chemistry',67,1072,2556,'Number of aromatic alcoholic isomers of following compound possible are ____.<br> <br>','[{"text": "1", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "2", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "3", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "4", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'<img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/67_29645_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','chemistry/67_29645_question_1.png'),
(29646,'chemistry',68,1118,2684,'Sodium formate on heating gives','[{"text": "oxalic acid and $\\\\mathrm{H}_{2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "sodium oxalate and $\\\\mathrm{H}_{2}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "sodium oxalate", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ and caustic soda", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'<img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/68_29646_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(29647,'chemistry',69,1176,2816,'The most basic nitrogen in the following compound is ____.<br>','[{"text": "1", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "2", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "3", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "4", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'The lone pair of electrons in (4) position is not involved in delocalization with the ring. Hence, because of availability, it is most basic.',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','chemistry/69_29647_question_1.png'),
(29648,'chemistry',70,1247,3036,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Glycosides are hydrolysed in acidic conditions.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Glycosides are acetals.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both  Assertion and  Reason are correct.", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Glycosides are formed by treating glucose with methanol in the presence of dry $\\mathrm{HCl}$ gas. They cannot be hydrolysed in acidic medium. Also, they are hemiacetals, not acetals.<br>Hence, both, Assertion and Reason are false.<br>',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',2013,'AIIMS','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(29651,'chemistry',68,1118,2684,'Which of the following represents soap?','[{"text": "$\\\\mathrm{C}_{17} \\\\mathrm{H}_{35} \\\\mathrm{COOK}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{C}_{17} \\\\mathrm{H}_{35} \\\\mathrm{COOH}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{C}_{15} \\\\mathrm{H}_{31} \\\\mathrm{COOH}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\left(\\\\mathrm{C}_{17} \\\\mathrm{H}_{35} \\\\mathrm{COO}\\\\right)_{2} \\\\mathrm{Ca}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Soaps are the sodium or potassium salts of higher fatty acids,<br>e.g., ${{\\text{C}}_{17}}{{\\text{H}}_{35}}{\\text{CO}}\\mathop {\\text{O}}\\limits^ -  \\mathop {\\text{K}}\\limits^ +  \\,({\\text{potassium}}\\,{\\text{stearate}}).$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(29652,'chemistry',67,1077,2572,'$0.336\\,{\\rm{g}}\\,\\,{\\rm{KOH}}$ hydrolyses with $0.436 \\mathrm{~g}$ of acetyl derivative of polyhydric alcohol. (molecular mass $=92$ ). Then the number of hydroxyl groups in the alcohol are ____.','[{"text": "5", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "2", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "3", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "4", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'We know that, $\\frac{{\\rm{w}}}{{\\rm{W}}}({\\rm{M}} + 42\\,{\\rm{n}}) = 56\\,{\\rm{n}}$<br>Where $\\mathrm{w}$ is the amount of alkali, $\\mathrm{W}$ is amount of acetyl derivative, M  is the molecular mass of alcohol and n is the number of hydroxyl groups.<br>$\\frac{{0.336}}{{0.436}}(92 + 42\\,{\\rm{n}}) = 56\\,{\\rm{n}}$<br>$92 + 42{\\rm{n}} = \\frac{{0.436}}{{0.336}} \\times 56{\\rm{n}} = \\frac{{218}}{3}{\\rm{n}}$<br>or $276+126 \\mathrm{n}=218 \\mathrm{n}$<br>or $\\mathrm{n}=3$ (the alcohol is trihydric.)',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(29655,'chemistry',70,1253,3053,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Tryptophan is an example of non - essential amino acid<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> The amino acids that are not synthesised in human body are non - essential amino acids.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both  Assertion and  Reason are correct.", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'The amino acids that are not synthesised in human body and must be obtained from our diet are essential amino acids. Tryptophan is an example of essential amino acids.<br>Therefore, both Assertion and Reason are incorrect.<br>',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',2019,'AIIMS','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
