(29682,'chemistry',68,1109,2643,'The correct statement regarding a carbonyl<br> compound with a hydrogen atom on its alpha<br> carbon, is?','[{"text": "A carbonyl compound with a hydrogen atom on its alpha-carbon never equilibrates with its corresponding enol.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "A carbonyl compound with a hydrogen atom on its alpha-carbon rapidly equilibrates with its corresponding enol and this process is known as aldehyde-ketone equilibration.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "A carbonyl compound with a hydrogen atom on its alpha-carbon rapidly equilibrates with its corresponding enol and this process is known as carbonylation.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "A carbonyl compound with a hydrogen atom on its alpha-carbon rapidly equilibrates with its corresponding enol and this process is known as keto-enol tautomerism.", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Keto-enol tautomerism is possible only in those aldehydes and ketones which have at least one ${\\rm{\\alpha }}$-hydrogen atom, which can <br> convert the ketonic group to the enolic group. e.g.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
