(29731,'chemistry',69,1176,2816,'Correct order of basic strength of Pyrrole','[{"text": "Pyrrole ${\\\\mathrm{>}}$ pyridine ${\\\\mathrm{>}}$ piperidine.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Pyridine ${\\\\mathrm{>}}$ piperidine ${\\\\mathrm{>}}$ pyrrole.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Pyrrole ${\\\\mathrm{>}}$ piperidine ${\\\\mathrm{>}}$ pyridine.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Piperidine ${\\\\mathrm{>}}$ pyridine ${\\\\mathrm{>}}$ pyrrole.", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Piperidine is most basic because the lone pair is present in ${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{sp}^{3}}}$ hybrid orbital of N -atom while in pyridine the lone pair is present in ${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{sp}^{2}}}$ hybrid orbital of N - atom. Greater the s character of orbital, more strongly the electrons will be bonded with the atom and less will be the basicity. Pyrrole is least basic among the given compounds due to involvement of its lone pair of electrons towards aromatic sextet formation. Hence, the basicity order is piperidine ${\\mathrm{>}}$ pyridine ${\\mathrm{>}}$ pyrrole. So, the correct option is (4).',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2024,'JEE Main','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','chemistry/69_29731_question_1.png'),
(29733,'chemistry',68,1113,2656,'The order of reactivity of phenyl magnesium bromide ( $\\mathrm{PhMgBr}$ ) with the following compounds :','[{"text": "III $>$ II $>$ I", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "II $>$ I $>$ III", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "I $>$ III $>$ II", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "I $>$ II $>$ III", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Since, alkyl group has $ + {\\rm{I}}$ effect and aryl group has $ + {\\rm{R}}$ effect, thus greater the number of alkyl and aryl groups attached to the carbonyl group, its reactivity towards nucleophilic addition reaction decreases. Secondly, as the steric crowding on carbonyl group increases, the reactivity decreases accordingly.<br>$\\therefore \\quad$ Correct reactivity order for reaction with $\\mathrm{PhMgBr}$ is <br> <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/68_29733_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','chemistry/68_29733_question_1.png'),
(29734,'chemistry',70,1247,3032,'Identify the reagent $R$ used in following conversion.<br>${\\text{Glucose}}\\xrightarrow{{\\text{R}}}{\\text{n}} - {\\text{hexane}}$','[{"text": "$\\\\mathrm{HI}, \\\\Delta$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{NH}_{2} \\\\mathrm{OH}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{HCN}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{HNO}_{3}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'${\\text{CHO  - }}\\mathop {{{{\\text{(CHOH)}}}_{\\text{4}}}}\\limits_{{\\text{ Glucose }}} {\\text{ -  C}}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{2}}}{\\text{OH}}\\xrightarrow{{{\\text{HI, }}\\Delta }}$<br>$\\mathop {{\\text{C}}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{3}}}}\\limits_{{\\text{ n  -  Hexane }}} {\\text{ - }}{\\left( {{\\text{C}}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{2}}}} \\right)_{\\text{4}}}{\\text{ -  C}}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{3}}}$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2021,'MHTCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(29735,'chemistry',69,1173,2806,'Read the Statement - A and Statement - B carefully to mark the correct options given below<div class="matching-question"><div class="column-left">Aminobenzene and aniline are same organic compounds.</div><div class="column-right">Aminobenzene and aniline are different organic compounds.</div></div>','[{"text": "Statement A is correct but Statement B is incorrect.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Statement A is incorrect but Statement B is correct.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both statements are correct.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Statements are incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Aniline has an amino group substituted to a benzene ring and is also called amino benzene. <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/69_29735_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />  So, the correct option is (1)',NULL,'Medium','MCQ type-2',2024,'JEE Main','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(29736,'chemistry',67,1077,2572,'If (dl) or ( ${\\mathrm{\\pm}}$ ) 2-methyl butanoic acid were esterified by reaction with (dl) or ${\\mathrm{( \\pm)}}$ 2-butanol, how many optically active compounds would be present in the final equilibrium reaction mixture?','[{"text": "1", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "2", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "3", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "4", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'<img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/67_29736_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' /> Ester consists of two stereoisomers.Chiral centre during whole reaction are not effected,that\'s why all esters are optical active.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(29737,'chemistry',68,1113,2663,'Which of the following will give Cannizzaro reaction?','[{"text": "$\\\\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\\\mathrm{CHO}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\\\mathrm{COCH}_{3}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\left(\\\\mathrm{CH}_{3}\\\\right)_{3} \\\\mathrm{CCHO}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\\\mathrm{CH}_{2} \\\\mathrm{CHO}$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'$\\left(\\mathrm{CH}_{3}\\right)_{3} \\mathrm{CCHO}$ does not contain ${\\rm{\\alpha }} - {\\rm{H}}$ atom, hence it will undergo Cannizzaro reaction. <br> <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/68_29737_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(29738,'chemistry',69,1176,2821,'Read the Statement - A and Statement - B carefully to mark the correct options given below<div class="matching-question"><div class="column-left">The ${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{NH}_{2}}}$ group in aniline is ortho and para directing and a powerful activating group.</div><div class="column-right">Aniline does not undergo Friedel Crafts reaction (alkylation and acylation).</div></div>','[{"text": "Statement A is correct but Statement B is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Statement A is incorrect but Statement B is correct.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both statements are correct.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Statements are incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Due to lone pair of electrons on nitrogen atom of aniline, it is ring activating and ortho/ para directing.<br>As aniline is basic, anhy. ${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{AlCl}_{3}}}$ (Lewis acid) reacts with ${\\mathrm{-\\mathrm{NH}_{2}}}$ group and can\'t form electrophile ( ${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{R}^{+}}}$or ${\\rm{R}}\\mathop {\\rm{C}}\\limits^{\\rm{ + }} {\\rm{O)}}$. So, Friedel-Crafts reaction is not possible on aniline. So, the correct option is (3)',NULL,'Medium','MCQ type-2',2024,'JEE Main','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(29739,'chemistry',70,1250,3047,'Identify the sugar containing  $\\alpha {\\text{ }},{\\text{ }}\\beta  - 1,2$  - glycosidic linkage','[{"text": "Sucrose", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Maltose", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Lactose", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Raffinose", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Structure of sucrose  <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/70_29739_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2021,'MHTCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(29741,'chemistry',68,1113,2660,'X is','[{"text": "", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "none of the above.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'$\\mathrm{SeO}_{2}$ oxidises the reactive methylene $\\left(\\mathrm{CH}_{2}\\right)$ group adjacent to carbonyl group to another $\\mathrm{CO}$ group. <br> <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/68_29741_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','chemistry/68_29741_question_1.png'),
(29742,'chemistry',69,1176,2816,'Which of the following is strongest Bronsted base?','[{"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'In  <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/69_29742_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />  nitrogen atom can donate lone pair of electrons easily as they are not in conjugation. Thus it behaves as the strongest Bronsted base. In all the other options, lone pairs of nitrogen are either involved in aromaticity such as in (1) or they are involved in resonance with rings such as in (2) and (3). So, the correct option is (4).',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2024,'JEE Main','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(29744,'chemistry',70,1247,3032,'Which of the following compound is obtained when glucose is treated with dilute nitric acid?','[{"text": "Glucose oxime", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Gluconic acid", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Saccharic acid", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Glucose cyanohydrin", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'<img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/70_29744_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2021,'MHTCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(29745,'chemistry',68,1111,2651,'Which of the following is a Gattermann aldehyde synthesis?','[{"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Gattermann aldehyde synthesis reaction is <br> <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/68_29745_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(29746,'chemistry',69,1174,2809,'Identify B formed in the reaction.${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{Cl}-\\left(\\mathrm{CH}_{2}\\right)_{4}-\\mathrm{Cl} \\xrightarrow{\\text { excess } \\mathrm{NH}_{3}} \\mathrm{~A} \\xrightarrow{\\mathrm{NaOH}} \\mathrm{B}+}}$${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O}+\\mathrm{NaCl}}}$','[{"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{Cl}}\\\\mathop {\\\\rm{N}}\\\\limits^{\\\\rm{ + }} {{\\\\rm{H}}_{\\\\rm{3}}} - {({\\\\rm{C}}{{\\\\rm{H}}_{\\\\rm{2}}}{\\\\rm{)}}_4} - \\\\mathop {\\\\rm{N}}\\\\limits^{\\\\rm{ + }} {{\\\\rm{H}}_{\\\\rm{3}}}{\\\\rm{C}}{{\\\\rm{l}}^ - }$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${{\\\\rm{H}}_{\\\\rm{2}}}{\\\\rm{N}} - {{\\\\rm{(C}}{{\\\\rm{H}}_{\\\\rm{2}}}{\\\\rm{)}}_4} - {\\\\rm{N}}{{\\\\rm{H}}_{\\\\rm{2}}}$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'In the first step $-$Cl is replaced by ${\\mathrm{-\\mathrm{NH}_{2}}}$ group and immediately forms salt with HCl formed in the reaction.  <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/69_29746_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />  <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/69_29746_explanation_2.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />  So, the correct option is (4).',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2024,'JEE Main','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(29747,'chemistry',67,1073,2559,'${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{CH}_{3}-\\mathrm{CH}_{2}-\\mathrm{CH}_{2}-\\mathrm{Br}+\\mathrm{NaOH}}}$${\\mathrm{\\xrightarrow{\\mathrm{C}_{2} \\mathrm{H}_{5} \\mathrm{OH}}}}$ Product A Consider the above reactions, identify product B and product C .','[{"text": "${\\\\mathrm{\\\\mathrm{B}=\\\\mathrm{C}=1}}$ - Propanol", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\mathrm{\\\\mathrm{B}=2}}$-Propanol, ${\\\\mathrm{\\\\mathrm{C}=1}}$-Propanol", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${\\\\mathrm{\\\\mathrm{B}=}}$ 1-Propanol, ${\\\\mathrm{\\\\mathrm{C}=2}}$-Propanol", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\mathrm{\\\\mathrm{B}=\\\\mathrm{C}=2}}$-Propanol", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Formation of \' ${\\mathrm{A}}$ \' is elimination.<br>${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{A} \\rightarrow \\mathrm{B}}}$ is addition of water according to Markovnikov\'s rule.<br>${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{A} \\rightarrow \\mathrm{C}}}$ is HBO (antimarkovnikov\'s addition of water). <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/67_29747_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />   So, correct option is (2).',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',2024,'JEE Main','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','chemistry/67_29747_question_1.png'),
(29748,'chemistry',68,1113,2662,'Aldol is','[{"text": "${\\\\rm{\\\\beta }} - {\\\\rm{hydroxybutyraldehyde}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{\\\\alpha }} - {\\\\rm{hydroxybutanal}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{\\\\beta }} - {\\\\rm{hydroxypropanal}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "none of the above.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Aldol is ${\\rm{\\beta }} - $ hydroxybutyraldehyde or 3-hydroxybutanal. <br> <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/68_29748_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
