(29802,'chemistry',67,1089,2613,'Read the Statement - A and Statement - B carefully to mark the correct options given below<div class="matching-question"><div class="column-left">p-Nitrophenol is more acidic than m-nitrophenol and o-nitrophenol.</div><div class="column-right">Ethanol will give immediate turbidity with Lucas reagent.</div></div>','[{"text": "Statement A is correct but Statement B is incorrect.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Statement A is incorrect but Statement B is correct.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both statements are correct.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Statements are incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'${\\mathrm{p}}$-Nitrophenol is more acidic than meta and ortho-isomers. The electron withdrawing group, ${\\mathrm{-\\mathrm{NO}_{2}}}$ stabilises the phenoxide ion through $-$R effect at the ortho and para position. The effective delocalisation of negative charge in phenoxide ion enhance the acidic strength of phenols. The ${\\mathrm{-\\mathrm{NO}_{2}}}$ group at meta position will show $-$I effect only. The ${\\mathrm{-\\mathrm{NO}_{2}}}$ group at ortho position forms hydrogen bond with $-$OH group. Ethanol being primary alcohol does not react with Lucas reagent at room temperature. Tertiary alcohol will give turbidity immediately with Lucas reagent. So, correct option is (1).',NULL,'Medium','MCQ type-2',2024,'JEE Main','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(29804,'chemistry',70,1252,3052,'Which of the following sugars occur in the urine of \'diabetics\'?','[{"text": "Dextrose", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Galactose", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Fructose", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Ribose", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Dextrose is present in the urine of diabetics.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(29805,'chemistry',68,1116,2673,'${\\text{C}}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{3}}}{\\text{MgI}} + {\\text{C}}{{\\text{O}}_{\\text{2}}} \\to {\\text{A}} \\to {\\text{B}},\\,\\,{\\text{B}}\\,{\\text{is}}$','[{"text": "HCOOH", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\\\mathrm{COCOOH}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\\\mathrm{COOH}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\\\mathrm{CHO}$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'<img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/68_29805_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(29806,'chemistry',67,1083,2591,'The ascending order of acidity of $-$OH group in the following compounds is<br>(I) ${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{Bu}-\\mathrm{OH}}}$<br>(II) (III) (IV) (V) Choose the correct answer from the options given below :','[{"text": "(III) ${\\\\mathrm{ < }}$ (I) ${\\\\mathrm{ < }}$ (IV) ${\\\\mathrm{ < }}$ (II) ${\\\\mathrm{ < }}$ (V)", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "(III) ${\\\\mathrm{ < }}$ (IV) ${\\\\mathrm{ < }}$ (II) ${\\\\mathrm{ < }}$ (I) ${\\\\mathrm{ < }}$ (V)", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "(I) ${\\\\mathrm{ < }}$ (IV) ${\\\\mathrm{ < }}$ (III) ${\\\\mathrm{ < }}$ (II) ${\\\\mathrm{ < }}$ (V)", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "(I) ${\\\\mathrm{ < }}$ (III) ${\\\\mathrm{ < }}$ (IV) ${\\\\mathrm{ < }}$ (II) ${\\\\mathrm{ < }}$ (V)", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Presence of electron withdrawing groups enhances the acidity of phenols, while the presence of electron donating groups decreases the acidity of phenol. Alcohols are less acidic than phenols. So, the correct order is<br> I ${\\mathrm{ < }}$ III ${\\mathrm{ < }}$ IV ${\\mathrm{ < }}$ II ${\\mathrm{ < }}$ V.<br> So, correct option is (4).',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2024,'JEE Main','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','chemistry/67_29806_question_1.png'),
(29807,'chemistry',69,1177,2827,'Identify the product (A)  in the following reaction','[{"text": "", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Phenol is formed in the given sequence of reaction. <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/69_29807_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />  So, the correct option is (1).',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2024,'JEE Main','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','chemistry/69_29807_question_1.png'),
