(29843,'chemistry',67,1076,2569,'Which among the following has highest boiling point?','[{"text": "${\\\\mathrm{\\\\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\\\mathrm{CH}_{2} \\\\mathrm{CH}_{2} \\\\mathrm{CH}_{2}-\\\\mathrm{OH}}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${\\\\mathrm{\\\\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\\\mathrm{CH}_{2} \\\\mathrm{CH}_{2} \\\\mathrm{CH}_{3}}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\mathrm{\\\\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\\\mathrm{CH}_{2} \\\\mathrm{CH}_{2} \\\\mathrm{CHO}}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\mathrm{\\\\mathrm{H}_{5} \\\\mathrm{C}_{2}-\\\\mathrm{O}-\\\\mathrm{C}_{2} \\\\mathrm{H}_{5}}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Among the given compounds, butan-1-ol has highest boiling point. The high boiling points of alcohols are mainly due to the presence of intermolecular hydrogen bonding in them which is lacking in ethers, aldehydes and hydrocarbons. Boiling point order is 1 - Butanol ${\\mathrm{>}}$ Butanal ${\\mathrm{>}}$ diethylether ${\\mathrm{>}}$ butane. So, the correct option is (1).',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2024,'JEE Main','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(29844,'chemistry',70,1253,3053,'Which one of the following  is $\\beta$ amino acid?','[{"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{NH}_{2}-\\\\mathrm{CH}_{2}-\\\\mathrm{CH}_{2}-\\\\mathrm{COOH}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{NH}_{2}-\\\\left(\\\\mathrm{CH}_{2}\\\\right)_{3} \\\\mathrm{COOH}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'$\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{~N}-\\underset{\\beta}{\\mathrm{CH}_{2}}-\\underset{\\alpha}{\\mathrm{CH}_{2}}-\\mathrm{COOH}$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(29846,'chemistry',67,1076,2569,'For the compounds,<br>(I) ${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{H}_{3} \\mathrm{C}-\\mathrm{CH}_{2}-\\mathrm{O}-\\mathrm{CH}_{2}-\\mathrm{CH}_{2}-\\mathrm{CH}_{3}}}$<br>(II) ${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{H}_{3} \\mathrm{C}-\\mathrm{CH}_{2}-\\mathrm{CH}_{2}-\\mathrm{CH}_{2}-\\mathrm{CH}_{3}}}$<br> (III) (IV) The increasing order of boiling point is Choose the correct answer from the options given below.','[{"text": "(IV) ${\\\\mathrm{ < }}$ (III) ${\\\\mathrm{ < }}$ (I) ${\\\\mathrm{ < }}$ (II)", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "(II) ${\\\\mathrm{ < }}$ (I) ${\\\\mathrm{ < }}$ (III) ${\\\\mathrm{ < }}$ (IV)", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "(II) ${\\\\mathrm{ < }}$ (I) ${\\\\mathrm{ < }}$ (IV) ${\\\\mathrm{ < }}$ (III)", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "(I) ${\\\\mathrm{ < }}$ (II) ${\\\\mathrm{ < }}$ (III) ${\\\\mathrm{ < }}$ (IV)<br>", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'B.P ${\\mathrm{\\propto}}$ molar mass, <br>B.P ${\\mathrm{\\propto}}$ Hydrogen bonding, <br>B.P ${\\mathrm{\\propto}}$ polarity.<br>So, alcohols (IV) ${\\mathrm{>}}$ carbonyl compounds (III) ${\\mathrm{>}}$ ethers (II) ${\\mathrm{>}}$ alkanes (I), (in general) of comparable molar masses.So, the correct option is (2).',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2024,'JEE Main','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','chemistry/67_29846_question_1.png'),
(29848,'chemistry',70,1254,3059,'In the following polypeptide sequence Arg- Gly - Cys - Leu, C terminal is:','[{"text": "$\\\\operatorname{Arg}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Cys", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Leu", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Gly", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'In polypeptides , starting aminoacid is named as N-terminal and  end amino acid is C-terminal<br>$\\mathop {{\\text{Arg}}}\\limits_{{\\text{N - terminal}}}  - {\\text{Gly}} - {\\text{Cys}} - \\mathop {{\\text{Leu}}}\\limits_{{\\text{C - terminal}}} $',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(29849,'chemistry',68,1108,2640,'According to IUPAC nomenclature, the parent chain in the following compound will constitute how many C -atoms?','[{"text": "6", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "5", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "7", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "8", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'According to IUPAC nomenclature, the given compound is named as, 2-ethyl-3-oxohexanal. <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/68_29849_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />  The parent chain is \'hexane\' =6 C -atoms',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','chemistry/68_29849_question_1.png'),
(29850,'chemistry',70,1250,3047,'In disaccharides, if the reducing groups of monosaccharides, i.e, aldehydic or ketonic groups are bonded, these are non-reducing sugars. Which of the following disaccharide is a non-reucing suagr?','[{"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'This structure represents sucrose in which $\\alpha$ - $\\mathrm{D}$ glucose and $\\beta$-D -fructose is attached to each other by $\\mathrm{C}_{1}-\\mathrm{C}_{2}$ glycosidic linkage.<br>Since, reducing groups of glucose and fructose are involved in glycosidic bond formation, this is consdered as non-reducing sugar.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(29852,'chemistry',70,1247,3036,'Assuming sweetness of cane sugar to be 10 ,the sweetness of glucose is (sweetness of glucose is $74 \\%$ of cane sugar).','[{"text": "15", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "1.5", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "12.5", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "7.4", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'If sweetness of cane sugar is 10,<br>sweetness of glucose $=\\dfrac{74}{100} \\times 10=7.4$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(29854,'chemistry',68,1116,2675,'Identify the total number of compounds from the following which will yield monocarboxylic acid on oxidation with ${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{KMnO}_{4}}}$ in acidic medium.','[{"text": "6", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "4", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "3", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "2", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'In case of aromatic compounds, the complete side chain (consisting of an alkyl group) is oxidised to $ - {\\text{COOH}}$ group irrespective of the length of the chain except in case of tert-butylbenzene. In case of cyclohexene, it gives dicarboxylic acid. <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/68_29854_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' /> $\\therefore $ Compounds (i), (ii), and (v) will yield monocarboxylic acids on oxidation with ${\\text{KMn}}{{\\text{O}}_{\\text{4}}}$ in acidic medium.',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','chemistry/68_29854_question_1.png'),
(29855,'chemistry',70,1253,3057,'','[{"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "No reaction", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'<img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/70_29855_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','chemistry/70_29855_question_1.png'),
(29857,'chemistry',70,1253,3058,'Which of the following statements are correct?<br>1. $\\alpha$-amino acid present in proteins is L-amino acid<br>2. Amino acids contain $\\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ as well as $\\mathrm{COOH}$ group<br>3. No. of amino groups & carboxylic groups are same in amino acids always<br>4.Tyrosine was first obtained from cheese','[{"text": "A,B", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "A,B,C", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "B,C.D", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "A,B.D", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'No. of amino & $\\mathrm{COOH}$ groups may be same or different in amino acids.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(29859,'chemistry',70,1256,3067,'Protein $\\xrightarrow{\\text { Enzyme }(\\mathrm{A})}$ Polypeptide<br>$\\xrightarrow[\\mathrm{B}]{\\text { Enzyme }}$ Amino acids. \'A\' and \'B\' are:','[{"text": "Diastase & lipase", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Pepsin & trypsin", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Invertase & zymase", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Amylase & maltase", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Protein $\\xrightarrow{\\text { Pepsin }}$ polypeptide $\\xrightarrow{\\text { trypsin }}$ Amino acids.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(29873,'chemistry',70,1246,3028,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Glucose and fructose are reducing sugars.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Glucose and fructose contain a free aldehydic and ketonic group adjacent to > CHOH  group respectively.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Reducing sugars possess a free aldehydic or ketonic group adjacent to a hydroxyl ($\\mathrm{-CHOH})$ group. They can reduce reagents like Tollen\'s reagent, Schiff\'s reagent, or Benedict\'s solution. So, option (1) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(29860,'chemistry',68,1113,2668,'${\\text{C}}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{3}}} - {\\text{CHO + 2C}}{{\\text{u}}^{{\\text{2 + }}}}{\\text{ + 5O}}{{\\text{H}}^{{ - }}}$<br>$\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\xrightarrow{\\Delta }$ Red brown ppt<br>The change that occurs in the reaction is<br>${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{Cu}^{2+} \\longrightarrow \\mathrm{Cu}^{x+}}}$.<br>The value of ${\\mathrm{x}}$ is ____ .','[{"text": "1", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "2", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "3", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "4", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Acetaldehyde reacts with Fehling\'s solution to give red brown precipitate of copper(I) oxide or cuprous oxide.<br>$\\mathop {{\\text{C}}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{3}}}{\\text{ - CHO}}}\\limits_{{\\text{ Acetaldehyde }}} {\\text{ + 2C}}{{\\text{u}}^{{\\text{2 + }}}}{\\text{ + 5O}}{{\\text{H}}^{{ - }}}\\xrightarrow{\\Delta }$<br>${\\text{C}}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{3}}}{\\text{CO}}{{\\text{O}}^ - }{\\text{  +  }}\\mathop {\\mathop {{\\text{Cu}}}\\limits^{{\\text{  + 1 }}} {\\text{O}} \\downarrow }\\limits_{{\\text{ (Red brown ppt) }}} {\\text{  +  3}}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{2}}}{\\text{O}}$<br>So, ${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}}}$ ion ${\\mathrm{\\longrightarrow \\mathrm{Cu}^{1+}}}$ ion<br>$\\therefore $ ${\\mathrm{x=1}}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(29862,'chemistry',70,1250,3047,'Which of the following statements is not true regarding $(+)$ lactose?','[{"text": "$(+)$ lactose, $\\\\mathrm{C}_{12} \\\\mathrm{H}_{22} \\\\mathrm{O}_{11}$ contains - $\\\\mathrm{OH}$ groups", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "On hydrolysis, $(+)$ lactose gives equal amount of $\\\\mathrm{D}(+)$ glucose & $\\\\mathrm{D}(+)$ galactose", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "(+) lactose is a $\\\\beta$ glycoside ${\\\\rm{\\\\& }}$ a molecule of $\\\\mathrm{D}(+)$ galactose", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "(+) lactose is reducing sugar & does not exhibit mutarotation.", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'$\\mathrm{D}(+)$ lactose in reducing sugar and exhibit mutarotaion.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(29864,'chemistry',70,1257,3069,'Match the vitamins with their name<div class="matching-question"><div class="column-left">A. Vit A<br>B. Vit ${{\\text{B}}_{{\\text{1}}}}$<br>C.  Vit ${{\\text{B}}_{{\\text{2}}}}$<br>D.  Vit ${{\\text{B}}_{{\\text{12}}}}$</div><div class="column-right">P. Thiamine<br>Q. Cyanocobalamine<br>R. Riboflavin<br>S. Retinol</div></div>','[{"text": "$\\\\mathrm{A}-\\\\mathrm{S}, \\\\quad \\\\mathrm{B}-\\\\mathrm{Q}, \\\\quad \\\\mathrm{C}-\\\\mathrm{P}, \\\\quad \\\\mathrm{D}-\\\\mathrm{R}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{A}-\\\\mathrm{S}, \\\\quad \\\\mathrm{B}-\\\\mathrm{P}, \\\\quad \\\\mathrm{C}-\\\\mathrm{R}, \\\\quad \\\\mathrm{D}-\\\\mathrm{Q}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{A}-\\\\mathrm{R}, \\\\quad \\\\mathrm{B}-\\\\mathrm{S}, \\\\quad \\\\mathrm{C}-\\\\mathrm{P}, \\\\quad \\\\mathrm{D}-\\\\mathrm{Q}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{A}-\\\\mathrm{P}, \\\\quad \\\\mathrm{B}-\\\\mathrm{S}, \\\\quad \\\\mathrm{C}-\\\\mathrm{R}, \\\\quad \\\\mathrm{D}-\\\\mathrm{Q}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Vitamin A-Retinol<br>Vitamin $\\mathrm{B}_{1}=$ Thiamine<br>Vitamin $\\mathrm{B}_{2}=$ Riboflavin<br>Vitamin $\\mathrm{B}_{12}=$ Cyano cobalamine',NULL,'Medium','Match the following',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(29865,'chemistry',68,1113,2668,'How many of the following compounds will NOT reduce Fehling\'s solution?<br>${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{HCHO}, \\quad \\mathrm{C}_{6} \\mathrm{H}_{5} \\mathrm{COCH}_{3}, \\quad \\mathrm{C}_{6} \\mathrm{H}_{5} \\mathrm{COC}_{6} \\mathrm{H}_{5}}}$, ${\\mathrm{\\left(\\mathrm{CH}_{3}\\right)_{3} \\mathrm{C}-\\mathrm{CHO}, \\quad \\mathrm{C}_{6} \\mathrm{H}_{5} \\mathrm{CHO}, \\quad \\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\mathrm{CHO}}}$, ${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{m}-\\mathrm{HOC}_{6} \\mathrm{H}_{4} \\mathrm{CHO}, \\mathrm{p}-\\mathrm{O}_{2} \\mathrm{NC}_{6} \\mathrm{H}_{4} \\mathrm{CHO}}}$.','[{"text": "2", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "5", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "6", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "7", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Aromatic aldehydes and ketones do not reduce Fehling\'s solution. Thus, ${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{C}_{6} \\mathrm{H}_{5} \\mathrm{COCH}_{3}, \\mathrm{C}_{6} \\mathrm{H}_{5} \\mathrm{COC}_{6} \\mathrm{H}_{5}}}$, ${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{C}_{6} \\mathrm{H}_{5} \\mathrm{CHO}, \\mathrm{m}-\\mathrm{HOC}_{6} \\mathrm{H}_{4} \\mathrm{CHO}}}$ and ${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{p}-\\mathrm{O}_{2} \\mathrm{NC}_{6} \\mathrm{H}_{4} \\mathrm{CHO}}}$ will not reduce Fehling\'s solution.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(29866,'chemistry',70,1254,3059,'A decapeptide (mol wt - 796) on complete hydrolysis gives glycine ( mol wt -75), alanine & phenylalanine. Glycine contributes $47.0 \\%$ of total weight of hydrolysed products. The number of glycine units present in the decapeptide is','[{"text": "6", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "8", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "2", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "5", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'The mol, wt,decapeptide $=796$<br>Glycine $($ Mol wt $=75)$ contribute $=47 \\%$<br>Let $\\mathrm{n}$ be number of glycine units. Since its a decapeptide, on hydrolysis water molecules gets added.<br>$\\Rightarrow$ so mass of product $=796+(9 \\times 18)=958$<br>Glycine contribution $47 \\%$ of 958<br>$=\\dfrac{47}{100} \\times 958=450$<br>$\\Rightarrow$ Number of glycine units<br>$=\\dfrac{450}{75}=6$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(29869,'chemistry',70,1253,3055,'Product is:','[{"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'<img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/70_29869_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','chemistry/70_29869_question_1.png'),
(29870,'chemistry',68,1118,2680,'How many isomeric carboxylic acids having ${{\\text{C}}_{\\text{5}}}{{\\text{H}}_{{\\text{10}}}}{{\\text{O}}_{\\text{2}}}$ formula will give Hell-Volhard-Zeilinsky reaction? (Excluding stereoisomers)','[{"text": "1", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "2", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "3", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "4", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Hell-Volhard-Zeilinsky reaction (or ${\\mathrm{\\alpha}}$-halogenation) is given by carboxylic acids having ${\\mathrm{\\alpha-\\mathrm{H}}}$ atom. Carboxylic acids having ${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{C}_{5} \\mathrm{H}_{10} \\mathrm{O}_{2}}}$ formula are: <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/68_29870_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' /> Except structure (IV), all (i.e., structure I, II, III) contain ${\\mathrm{\\alpha}}$-H atom',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(29871,'chemistry',70,1253,3055,'Product $\\mathrm{X}$ is:','[{"text": "", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\stackrel{\\\\ominus}{\\\\mathrm{C}} \\\\mathrm{H}_{3} \\\\stackrel{\\\\oplus}{\\\\mathrm{N}}-\\\\mathrm{CH}_{2}-\\\\mathrm{COOH}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'<img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/70_29871_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','chemistry/70_29871_question_1.png'),
(29875,'chemistry',68,1113,2658,'Amongst the following the total number of compounds that gives positive iodoform test are ____ .<br>${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\mathrm{CH}_{2} \\mathrm{OH}, \\quad \\mathrm{PhCHO}, \\quad \\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\mathrm{COCH}_{3}}}$, ${\\mathrm{\\left(\\mathrm{CH}_{3}\\right)_{2} \\mathrm{CHOH}, \\quad \\mathrm{HCHO}, \\quad \\mathrm{CH}_{3}\\left(\\mathrm{CH}_{2}\\right)_{2} \\mathrm{COCH}_{3}}}$, ${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{PhCH}_{2} \\mathrm{OH}, \\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\mathrm{CH}_{2} \\mathrm{COPh}}}$','[{"text": "6", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "3", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "5", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "4", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Positive iodoform test is given by,<br>i. alcohols containing <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/68_29875_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' /> ii. acetaldehyde (the only aldehyde) and ketones<br>containing ${\\mathrm{\\left(\\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\mathrm{CO}-\\right)}}$ group.<br>Thus ${\\text{C}}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{3}}}{\\text{C}}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{2}}}{\\text{OH}},{\\text{C}}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{3}}}{\\text{COC}}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{3}}},$<br>${\\left( {{\\text{C}}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{3}}}} \\right)_{\\text{2}}}{\\text{CHOH}},{\\text{C}}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{3}}}{\\left( {{\\text{C}}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{2}}}} \\right)_{\\text{2}}}{\\text{COC}}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{3}}}$<br>will give positive iodoform test.',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(29876,'chemistry',70,1246,3028,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Sucrose is a disaccharide.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Sucrose is dextro rotatory.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Carbohydrates that, upon hydrolysis, yield two molecules of the same or different monosaccharides are categorized as disaccharides. Sucrose is a disaccharide. Upon hydrolysis it gives equimolar mixture of D -(+)-glucose and D-(-) fructose. Sucrose is dextrorotatory. So, option (2) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(29878,'chemistry',70,1248,3040,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Fructose reduces Fehling\'s solution and Tollens\' reagent.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Fructose does not contain any aldehyde group.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'When fructose is heated with dilute alkali, it undergoes a reaction that leads to the formation of an equilibrium mixture containing glucose, fructose, and mannose. There is isomerization of fructose into glucose and mannose during this process. This transformation is responsible for the reducing properties exhibited by fructose. Fructose has a keto group. So, option (2) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(29881,'chemistry',70,1251,3051,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Starch and cellulose do not reduce Tollen\'s reagent<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Starch and cellulose on hydrolysis give hundreds to thousands of monosaccharides molecules.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Despite the presence of an aldehydic group at the end of their respective chains, starch and cellulose do not exhibit the ability to reduce Tollen\'s reagent. This lack of reactivity is primarily due to their high molecular weights as polysaccharides. The extensive size of these molecules effectively conceals their reducing properties, making it difficult to detect any observable reduction when they come into contact with Tollen\'s reagent. So, option (2) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(29882,'chemistry',68,1118,2685,'One mole each of calcium acetate and calcium propionate undergoes dry distillation to form \' ${\\mathrm{x}}$ \' mole(s) of ethyl methyl ketone. Find the value of ${\\mathrm{x}}$.','[{"text": "1", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "2", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "3", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "4", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'<img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/68_29882_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(29885,'chemistry',70,1250,3044,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Acid or enzymatic hydrolysis of sucrose to give an equimolar mixture of glucose and fructose is called inversion.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Sucrose is the only naturally occurring disaccharide which is reducing sugar.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Sucrose is dextrorotatory but after hydrolysis gives dextrorotatory glucose and laevorotatory fructose. Since the laevorotation of fructose $\\left(92.4^{\\circ}\\right)$ is more than dextrorotation of glucose $\\left(+52.5^{\\circ}\\right)$, the mixture is laevorotatory. Thus, hydrolysis of sucrose brings about a change in the sign of rotation, from dextro (+) to laevo<br>$(-)$ and the product is named as invert sugar.<br>Since the reducing groups of glucose and fructose are involved in glycosidic bond formation, sucrose is a non reducing sugar. So, option (3) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(29888,'chemistry',70,1250,3044,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> The specific rotation of a freshly prepared solution of $\\alpha$-glucose decreases from $+112^{\\circ}$ to $52.7^{\\circ}$ while that of $\\beta$-glucose increases from $+19^{\\circ}$ to $52.7^{\\circ}$.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> The change in specific rotation of an optically active compound with time to an equilibrium value is called mutarotation.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Glucose exists in two forms, i.e. $\\alpha  - {\\text{D-}}$  glucose with a specific rotation of $+112^{\\circ}$ and $\\beta$-D-glucose with a specific rotation of $+19^{\\circ}$. However, when either of these two forms is dissolved in water and allowed to stand, it gets converted into equilibrium mixture of both the $\\alpha$ - and $\\beta$-forms with a small amount of open chain form. As a result of this equilibrium, the specific rotation of a freshly prepared solution of $\\alpha$-glucose decreases from $+112^{\\circ}$ to $52.7^{\\circ}$ while that of $\\beta$-glucose increases from $+19^{\\circ}$ to $52.7^{\\circ}$. So, option (2) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(29890,'chemistry',68,1113,2670,'The number of H -atoms in the product formed is ____.','[{"text": "8", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "7", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "6", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "5", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Unsaturated aldehydes and ketones can be reduced to unsaturated alcohols without affecting carbon-carbon double bond in presence of ${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{NaBH}_{4}}}$ in alcohol. <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/68_29890_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' /> The molecular formula of product is ${{\\text{C}}_{\\text{5}}}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{8}}}{\\text{O}}$.',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','chemistry/68_29890_question_1.png'),
(29892,'chemistry',70,1251,3049,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Cellulose can be easily converted into fibres.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Cotton contains about $90 \\%$ cellulose.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Cellulose is a linear condensation polymer of $\\beta$-glucose. Because of linear structure, cellulose can be easily converted into fibres. So, option (2) is correct',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(29896,'chemistry',70,1247,3035,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Glucose is used in silvering of mirrors.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Glucose is less acidic than a monohydric alcohol.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Glucose being the reducing sugar undergoes Tollen\'s test giving silver mirror (known as silvering). $\\mathrm{K}_{\\mathrm{a}}$ for glucose is $6.6 \\times 10^{-12}$ and $\\mathrm{K}_{\\mathrm{a}}$ for $\\mathrm{C}_{2} \\mathrm{H}_{5} \\mathrm{OH}$ is $7.4 \\times 10^{-20}$. Thus, glucose is more acidic than a monohydric alcohol. So, option (3) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(29899,'chemistry',68,1118,2684,'2-Chlorobutane $\\xrightarrow[\\Delta ]{{{\\text{Alc}}{\\text{.KOH}}}}\\mathop {\\text{X}}\\limits_{\\left( {{\\text{Major}}} \\right)} \\xrightarrow[{{\\text{ii}}{\\text{.}}\\,{\\text{Zn/}}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{2}}}{\\text{O}}}]{{{\\text{i}}{\\text{.}}\\,{{\\text{O}}_{\\text{3}}}}}{\\text{Y}}$<br>Methyl formate + Ethyl magnesium bromide<br>$\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\xrightarrow[{{\\text{ii}}{\\text{.}}\\,{{\\text{H}}^{\\text{ + }}}{\\text{,}}\\,{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{2}}}{\\text{O}}}]{{{\\text{i}}{\\text{.}}\\,{\\text{Dry}}\\,{\\text{ether}}}}{\\text{P}}$<br>Product \'Y\' and \'P\' differ in molecular mass by ______ u.','[{"text": "12", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "14", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "16", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "18", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'i.  <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/68_29899_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />  ii. <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/68_29899_explanation_2.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' /> Acetaldehyde ( Y ) and propionaldehyde ( P ) are homologues of each other. They differ by one ${\\mathrm{-\\mathrm{CH}_{2}}}$ unit. Hence, they differ in molecular mass by 14 u .',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(29901,'chemistry',70,1251,3049,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Cellulose cannot be directly utilized by the human body as a source of energy.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Cellulose forms the chief structural material of cell walls of plants.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'The human digestive system does not contain enzymes which can hydrolyse cellulose into glucose and hence are unable to digest cellulose.<br>Reason is also correct statement, but not the appropriate explanation for the assertion. So, option (2) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(29905,'chemistry',70,1253,3054,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> $\\alpha$-Amino acids exist as dipolar ions or zwitter ions.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> $\\alpha$-Amino acids are the building blocks of proteins.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Amino acids exist as zwitter ions, possessing both acidic (-COOH) and basic (- $\\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ ) groups, resulting in internal ionization and unique amphoteric properties. So, option (2) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(29910,'chemistry',68,1109,2643,'The number of ${\\mathrm{\\alpha-\\mathrm{H}}}$ atoms in 2-methylcyclohexanone is ____ .','[{"text": "2", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "4", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "3", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "1", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'<img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/68_29910_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />  There are $3\\alpha  - {\\text{H}}$-atoms.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(29911,'chemistry',70,1253,3053,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Valine is an essential amino acid.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> The lack of essential amino acids in the diet causes Kwashiorkor.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Valine is an essential amino acid. The amino acids which the body cannot synthesize are called essential amino acid. Reason is also correct but not the correct explanation of assertion. So option (2) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(29915,'chemistry',70,1253,3056,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Simple proteins on hydrolysis gives $\\alpha$-amino acids.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Prosthetic group is present in simple proteins.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Conjugated proteins contain a prosthetic group. These proteins on hydrolysis give a non-protein part in addition to $\\alpha$-amino acids which is called the prosthetic group. So, option (3) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(29919,'chemistry',70,1247,3035,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Glucose when treated with $\\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\mathrm{OH}$ in presence of dry $\\mathrm{HCl}$ gas gives $\\alpha$ and $\\beta$-methyl glucosides.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Glucose reacts with phenyl hydrazine to form crystalline osazone.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Due to the ring structure formed by glucose, the carbon atom labeled as $\\left(\\mathrm{C}_{1}\\right)$ becomes chiral. As a result, glucose exists in two stereoisomeric forms: $\\alpha$-glucose and $\\beta$-glucose, corresponding to each stereoisomeric configuration.<br>Furthermore, glucose can form two distinct methyl glucosides, known as $\\alpha$-methyl glucoside and $\\beta$-methyl glucoside. These different isomeric forms and derivatives are a result of the variations in the spatial arrangement of atoms around carbon atom $\\left(\\mathrm{C}_{1}\\right)$ in the glucose molecule. So, option (2) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(29921,'chemistry',68,1113,2659,'<br>Sum of ${\\rm{x}} + {\\rm{y}}$ is ____.','[{"text": "1", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "2", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "3", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "4", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'<img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/68_29921_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />  So, the correct option is (3).',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','chemistry/68_29921_question_1.png'),
(29926,'chemistry',70,1258,3072,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> DNA has a double helix structure.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> The two strands in a DNA molecule are exactly similar.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'DNA has a double helix structure.The two strands in a DNA molecule are not exactly similar but are complimentary. So, option (3) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(29930,'chemistry',70,1258,3073,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> DNA undergoes replication.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> DNA contains cytosine and thymine as pyrimidine bases.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'The genetic information of the cell is contained in the sequence of base $\\mathrm{A}, \\mathrm{T}, \\mathrm{G}$ and $\\mathrm{C}$ in DNA molecule. When a cell divides, DNA molecules replicate and make exact copies of themselves so that each daughter cell will have DNA identical to that of the parent cell. Reason is also correct but not the correct explanation of assertion. So, option (2) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(29931,'chemistry',68,1113,2662,'The value of $({\\rm{m}} + {\\rm{n}})$ in the following reaction is ____.<br>','[{"text": "3", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "5", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "7", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "9", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'<img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/68_29931_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' /> <br>One mole of HCHO gives one additional group $ - {\\rm{C}}{{\\rm{H}}_{\\rm{2}}} - {\\rm{OH}}.$ In the given product, there are $6,\\,\\, - {\\rm{C}}{{\\rm{H}}_{\\rm{2}}} - {\\rm{OH}}$ group added to one mole of ${({\\rm{Me}})_2}{\\rm{CO}}$<br>Hence, $({\\rm{m}} + {\\rm{n}} = 7)$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','chemistry/68_29931_question_1.png'),
(29934,'chemistry',70,1251,3048,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Starch contains $20 \\%$ amylose and $80 \\%$ amylopectin.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> In starch, the glucose units are linked by $\\beta$-linkages.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'In starch, the glucose units are linked to one another by $\\alpha$-glycosidic linkages. So, option (3) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(29939,'chemistry',70,1256,3067,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Enzymes increases the rate of a reaction.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Enzyme action is inhibited by certain organic molecules called inhibitors.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Enzymes are very efficient catalysts. They speed up the rate of a reaction by factors up to $10^{20}$ by lowering the energy of activation of the reaction. Its activity is inhibited by inhibitors. So, option (2) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(29942,'chemistry',68,1113,2670,'<br>____ reagents are required for above conversion.','[{"text": "6", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "5", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "3", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "10", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'<img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/68_29942_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','chemistry/68_29942_question_1.png'),
(29945,'chemistry',70,1256,3067,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> All enzymes are proteins but all proteins are not enzymes.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Keratin is an enzyme.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Enzymes are a category of proteins known for their ability to catalyze biochemical reactions, and they are typically globular in structure. On the other hand, keratin is classified as a fibrous protein. So, option (3) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(29949,'chemistry',70,1253,3054,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Glycine exists as zwitter ion but o-and $\\mathrm{p}$-aminobenzoic acids do not.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Due to the presence of both acid and base group within the same molecule, they neutralise each other and hence, $\\alpha$-amino acids exist as dipolar ions or zwitter ions.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'In o-or p-aminobenzoic acids, the lone pair of electrons on the $-\\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ group is donated towards the benzene ring. As a result, the basic character of $-\\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ group and acidic character of $-\\mathrm{COOH}$ group decreases. Therefore the weakly acidic $-\\mathrm{COOH}$ group cannot transfer $\\mathrm{H}^{+}$ion to the weakly basic $-\\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ group. Therefore, o- and p-amino benzoic acid does not exist as zwitter ion. So, option (2) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(29954,'chemistry',70,1253,3056,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Amino acids are insoluble in benzene and ether.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Amino acids exist as zwitter ions.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Amino acids exhibit solubility in polar solvents such as water $\\left(\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O}\\right)$,sodium hydroxide $(\\mathrm{NaOH})$, and hydrochloric acid $(\\mathrm{HCl})$. In contrast, they are generally insoluble in non-polar solvents like benzene and ether. This solubility behavior is primarily due to the polar nature of amino acid molecules. Aminoacids exists as zwitter ions due to $-\\mathrm{COOH}$ and $-\\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ groups. But, it is not the correct reason for the assertion. So option (2) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(29959,'chemistry',70,1254,3062,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Haemoglobin is a globular protein.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Globular proteins are insoluble in water.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Haemoglobin is a globular protein. Globular proteins possess relatively weak intermolecular forces of attraction, making them soluble in water. The weak forces between protein molecules allow them to disperse and interact effectively with water molecules, leading to their solubility in aqueous solutions. So option (3) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(29964,'chemistry',70,1249,3042,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Glucose and fructose give the same osazone.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> During osazone formation stereochemistry at $\\mathrm{C}_{1}$ and $\\mathrm{C}_{2}$ is destroyed.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Glucose and fructose differ from each other only at $\\mathrm{C}_{1}$ and $\\mathrm{C}_{2}$ positions. So option (1) is correct.',NULL,'Hard','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(29965,'chemistry',68,1113,2659,'Examine the structural formulas given below and identify number of compounds which are reduced by $\\mathrm{NaBH}_{4}$.','[{"text": "4", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "5", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "9", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "6", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'<img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/68_29965_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','chemistry/68_29965_question_1.png'),
(29969,'chemistry',70,1251,3048,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> In the iodometric titration, starch is used as an indicator.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Starch is a polysaccharide.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Starch $\\left(\\mathrm{C}_{6} \\mathrm{H}_{10} \\mathrm{O}_{5}\\right)_{\\mathrm{n}}$ is a polysaccharide. It consists two fractions, one is known as $\\alpha$- amylose and the other is $\\beta$-amylose or amylopectin. In iodometric titrations, strach solution is used as an indicator. It gives blue or violet colour with free iodine. At the end point, the blue or violet colour disappears when iodine is completely changed to iodide. So, option (2) is correct.',NULL,'Hard','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(29973,'chemistry',70,1253,3056,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Alpha ( $\\alpha$ )-amino acids exist as internal salt in solution as they have amino and carboxylic acid groups in near vicinity.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> $\\mathrm{H}^{+}$ion given by carboxylic group $(-\\mathrm{COOH})$ is captured by amino group $\\left(-\\mathrm{NH}_{2}\\right)$ having lone pair of electrons.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'$\\mathrm{NH}_{2}-\\mathrm{CH}_{2}-\\mathrm{COOH}$ is a typical $\\alpha-$ amino acid. In solution it exists as, internal salt or<br>Zwitter ion, $\\left( {\\mathop {\\text{N}}\\limits^{\\text{ + }} {{\\text{H}}_{\\text{3}}}{\\text{ -  C}}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{2}}}{\\text{ -  CO}}\\mathop {\\text{O}}\\limits^{\\text{ - }} } \\right)$  because the proton $\\left(\\mathrm{H}^{+}\\right)$of $-\\mathrm{COOH}$ group is captured by $-\\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ group as $\\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ has a lone pair of electrons on $\\mathrm{N}$ atom. So, option (1) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(29976,'chemistry',68,1116,2674,'An aromatic compound ${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{C}_{9} \\mathrm{H}_{12}}}$ on oxidation with acidic ${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{KMnO}_{4}}}$ gives tricarboxylic acid ( ${\\mathrm{\\left.\\mathrm{C}_{9} \\mathrm{O}_{6} \\mathrm{H}_{6}\\right)}}$. Compound (A) forms only one isomeric chloro derivative on reaction with ${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{Fe}+\\mathrm{Cl}_{2}}}$ (in dark). The ratio of ${\\mathrm{3^{\\circ}}}$ carbon atoms present in (A) to the ${\\mathrm{3^{\\circ}}}$ carbon atoms present in chlorinated product is ____ .','[{"text": "0.75", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "1.33", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "1.9", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "2.9", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Degree of unsaturation in A is ${\\mathrm{4^{\\circ}}}$, shows benzene ring tricarboxylic show 3 methyl groups at ${\\mathrm{o, m}}$ or p-position. But 1-chloroderivative on chlorination shows it is 1, 3, 5-trimethylbenzene reaction is: <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/68_29976_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />  ${\\mathrm{3^{\\circ}}}$ carbon atoms in ${\\mathrm{(\\mathrm{A})=3}}$<br>${\\mathrm{3^{\\circ}}}$ carbon atoms in product ${\\mathrm{=4}}$<br>Ratio ${\\mathrm{=\\dfrac{3}{4}=0.75}}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(29978,'chemistry',70,1254,3061,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> $\\beta$-pleated sheet structure of protein shows maximum extension.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Intramolecular hydrogen bonding is present in them.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'In $\\beta$-pleated sheet structure all peptide chains are stretched out to nearly maximum extension and then laid side by side which are held together by intermolecular hydrogen bonds. So, option (3) is correct',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(29985,'chemistry',70,1246,3028,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Fructose is a reducing sugar. <br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> It has a ketonic group.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Fructose is classified as a reducing sugar due to its possession of a keto group that can undergo keto-enol tautomerism, resulting in the formation of an aldehyde group. This aldehyde group enables fructose to reduce specific chemical reagents, confirming its status as a reducing sugar. So option (1) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(29986,'chemistry',68,1118,2684,'In case of stephen\'s reduction of benzoic acid in which liquid ammonia is in the presence of ethanol converts reactant to product, the ratio of number of ${\\mathrm{\\pi}}$-bonds present in reactant to that in product formed after reaction is ____ .','[{"text": "0.75", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "5.2", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "1.33", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "2.8", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Stephen\'s reaction is: <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/68_29986_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' /> $\\begin{aligned}& \\text { Ratio }=\\dfrac{4}{3}=1.33\\end{aligned}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(29991,'chemistry',70,1254,3062,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Insulin is water soluble.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Insulin is a globular protein.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Insulin, a globular protein, exhibits water solubility attributed to its three-dimensional folded structure, stabilized by internal hydrogen bonds, facilitating interaction with aqueous environments for biological functionality. So, option (2) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(30140,'chemistry',70,1247,3036,'${\\mathrm{\\quad \\alpha-\\mathrm{D}-(+)-}}$ glucose and ${\\mathrm{\\beta-\\mathrm{D}-(+)-}}$ glucose are','[{"text": "Enantiomers", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Conformers", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Epimers", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Anomers", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'${\\mathrm{\\alpha-\\mathrm{D}-(+)-}}$ glucose and ${\\mathrm{\\beta-\\mathrm{D}-(+)-}}$ glucose differ in configuration at first carbon so called anomers.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2024,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(29995,'chemistry',70,1253,3056,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Solubility of proteins is minimum at the isoelectric point.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> At isoelectric point, protein molecule behaves as a zwitter ion.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'At the isoelectric point, proteins become zwitter ionic, with both positive and negative charges, leading to reduced solubility, aggregation, and precipitation, facilitating protein separation and purification. So, option (1) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(29997,'chemistry',68,1120,2687,'(a) and (b) are number of moles of ${\\rm{NaB}}{{\\rm{H}}_{\\rm{4}}}$ and LAH<br>$\\therefore $ a + b=','[{"text": "3", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "1", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "6", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "5", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'<img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/68_29997_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />  a+b=2+3=5',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','chemistry/68_29997_question_1.png'),
(30000,'chemistry',70,1250,3044,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> A solution of sucrose in water is dextrorotatory. But on hydrolysis in the presence of a little hydrochloric acid, it becomes laevorotatory.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Sucrose on hydrolysis gives unequal amounts of glucose and fructose. As a result of this, change in sign of rotation is observed.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Sucrose is dextrorotatory but after hydrolysis gives dextrorotatory glucose and laevorotatory fructose. Since the laevorotation of fructose $\\left(92.4^{\\circ}\\right)$ is more than dextrorotation of glucose $\\left(+52.5^{\\circ}\\right)$, the mixture is laevorotatory. Thus, hydrolysis of sucrose brings about a change in the sign of rotation, from dextro (+) to laevo (-). So, option (3) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(30005,'chemistry',70,1258,3071,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Nucleotides are phosphate esters of nucleosides.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> The various nucleotides in nucleic acids are linked either through purine or pyrimidine bases.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'In nucleic acids, the different nucleotides are connected through phosphate ester groups. These phosphate ester bonds link the sugar molecules of adjacent nucleotides, forming the sugar-phosphate backbone of the nucleic acid. So, option (3) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(30008,'chemistry',68,1113,2657,'In the above reaction product formed is X. The ratio of possible number of stereoisomers of the product to the sterocenters in X is ____ .','[{"text": "2.67", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "0.81", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "3.9", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "6.8", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'<img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/68_30008_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />   Stereocentres ${\\mathrm{=3}}$<br>Possible stereoisomers ${\\mathrm{=2^{3}=8}}$<br>$\\text { Ratio }=\\dfrac{8}{3}=2.67$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','chemistry/68_30008_question_1.png'),
