(30168,'chemistry',70,1249,3042,'Which of the following statements are correct?<br>(I) The natural glucose and fructose are D – forms.<br>(II) Glucose and fructose both are monosaccharides.<br>(III) The solution having equal molecules of D- glucose and D-fructose is termed as invert sugar.<br>(IV)  Aldohexoses exist in ${\\mathrm{2^{6}}}$ optical forms.','[{"text": "(I) and (II)", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "(I), (II), (III)", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Only (I)", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Only (IV)", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Aldohexoses have 4 chiral carbon atoms, so they exist in ${\\mathrm{2^{4}(16)}}$ optical forms. So, statement (IV) is not correct.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ type-2',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(30169,'chemistry',68,1118,2681,'Identify the product $(\\mathrm{P})$ in the following reaction.','[{"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'It is HVZ reaction and ${\\rm{\\alpha }}$ -halogenation takes place in this reaction. <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/68_30169_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />   So, the correct option is (3).',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2024,'JEE Main','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','chemistry/68_30169_question_1.png'),
(30172,'chemistry',70,1251,3049,'Pick up the correct statements from the following.<br>(I) Glucose exists in two different crystalline forms, ${\\mathrm{\\alpha-\\mathrm{D}-}}$ glucose and ${\\mathrm{\\beta-\\mathrm{D}-}}$ glucose.<br>(II) ${\\mathrm{\\alpha-\\mathrm{D}-}}$ glucose and ${\\mathrm{\\beta-\\mathrm{D}-}}$ glucose are enantiomers.<br>(III) Cellulose is a straight chain polysaccharide made up of only ${\\mathrm{\\beta}}$ - glucose units.<br>(IV)Cyclic structure of ${\\mathrm{\\alpha-\\mathrm{D}-}}$ glucose or ${\\mathrm{\\beta-\\mathrm{D}}}$ - glucose is called pyranose structure.','[{"text": "(I) and (II)", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "(I), (III), (IV)", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Only (I)", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Only (IV)", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'${\\mathrm{\\alpha-\\mathrm{D}}}$ glucose and ${\\mathrm{\\beta-\\mathrm{D}}}$ glucose are two cyclic hemiacetal forms of glucose which differ only in configuration of hydroxyl group at anomeric carbon. Thus, ${\\mathrm{\\alpha-\\mathrm{D}-}}$ glucose and  ${\\mathrm{\\beta-\\mathrm{D}-}}$ glucose are anomers but not enantiomers.These are six membered ring structures also known as pyranose rings.<br>Cellulose consists of ${\\mathrm{1,4-\\beta}}$-glycosidic linkages of glucose. So, (1), (3), and (4) are correct statements.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ type-2',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(30173,'chemistry',68,1118,2677,'The correct sequence of acidic strength of the following aliphatic acids in their decreasing order is $\\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\mathrm{CH}_{2} \\mathrm{COOH}, \\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\mathrm{COOH}$, <br>$\\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\mathrm{CH}_{2} \\mathrm{CH}_{2} \\mathrm{COOH}, \\mathrm{HCOOH}$','[{"text": "$\\\\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\\\mathrm{COOH}>\\\\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\\\mathrm{CH}_{2} \\\\mathrm{COOH}>$$\\\\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\\\mathrm{CH}_{2} \\\\mathrm{CH}_{2} \\\\mathrm{COOH}>\\\\mathrm{HCOOH}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{HCOOH}>\\\\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\\\mathrm{CH}_{2} \\\\mathrm{CH}_{2} \\\\mathrm{COOH}>$ $\\\\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\\\mathrm{CH}_{2} \\\\mathrm{COOH}>\\\\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\\\mathrm{COOH}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{HCOOH}>\\\\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\\\mathrm{COOH}>$ $\\\\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\\\mathrm{CH}_{2} \\\\mathrm{COOH}>\\\\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\\\mathrm{CH}_{2} \\\\mathrm{CH}_{2} \\\\mathrm{COOH}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\\\mathrm{CH}_{2} \\\\mathrm{CH}_{2} \\\\mathrm{COOH}>\\\\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\\\mathrm{CH}_{2} \\\\mathrm{COOH}>$ $\\\\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\\\mathrm{COOH}>\\\\mathrm{HCOOH}$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Acidic strength of carboxylic acids $\\propto-\\mathrm{I}$ groups $\\propto \\dfrac{1}{+ \\text { I groups }}$. Hence, the correct order is<br>$\\mathrm{HCOOH}>\\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\mathrm{COOH}>\\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\mathrm{CH}_{2} \\mathrm{COOH}>$ $\\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\mathrm{CH}_{2} \\mathrm{CH}_{2} \\mathrm{COOH}$.<br>So, the correct option is (3).',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2024,'JEE Main','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(30176,'chemistry',68,1113,2668,'Which of the following compounds will give silver mirror with ammonical silver nitrate?<br>(I) Formic acid<br>(II) Formaldehyde<br>(III) Benzaldehyde<br> (IV) Acetone<br>Choose the correct answer from the options given below','[{"text": "(II) and (III) only", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "(III) and (IV) only", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "(I), (II) and (III) only", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "(I) only", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Silver mirror test is given by aliphatic & aromatic aldehydes. But formic acid also gives positive silver mirror test due to the presence of   <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/68_30176_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />  group.<br> So, the correct option is (3).',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2024,'JEE Main','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(30179,'chemistry',70,1248,3041,'Which of the following monosaccharides are present as five membered cyclic structure (furanose structure)?<br>(I) Ribose<br>(II) Glucose<br>(III) Fructose<br>(IV) Galactose','[{"text": "(I) and (II)", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "(I) and (III)", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "(III) and (IV)", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "(II) and (III)", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Glucose and galactose are present as six membered rings. Ribose and fructose are present as five membered rings(furanose)<br><img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/70_30179_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(30180,'chemistry',68,1113,2670,'Match Column I with Column II<div class="matching-question"></div>','[{"text": "${\\\\text{A}} - {\\\\text{R,B}} - {\\\\text{S,C}} - {\\\\text{Q,D}} - {\\\\text{P}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\text{A}} - {\\\\text{S,B}} - {\\\\text{Q,C}} - {\\\\text{P,D}} - {\\\\text{R}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${\\\\text{A}} - {\\\\text{R,B}} - {\\\\text{S,C}} - {\\\\text{P,D}} - {\\\\text{Q}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\text{A}} - {\\\\text{S,B}} - {\\\\text{Q,C}} - {\\\\text{R,D}} - {\\\\text{P}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'DIBAL-H reduces ester into aldehyde <br> (A $-$ S)  <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/68_30180_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />   <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/68_30180_explanation_2.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />  (B-Q ) $\\mathrm{C}_{6} \\mathrm{H}_{5} \\mathrm{COC}_{6} \\mathrm{H}_{5} \\xrightarrow[\\text { conc. } \\mathrm{HCl}]{\\mathrm{Zn}(\\mathrm{Hg})} \\mathrm{C}_{6} \\mathrm{H}_{5} \\mathrm{CH}_{2} \\mathrm{C}_{6} \\mathrm{H}_{5}$ <br>This reaction is known as Clemmensen reduction.<br>(C-P) Aldehyde $+\\mathrm{RMgX} \\xrightarrow{\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O}} 2^{\\circ}$ alcohol <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/68_30180_explanation_3.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />  <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/68_30180_explanation_4.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />   (D-R) Esters are not reduced by $\\mathrm{NaBH}_{4}$. It reduces ketones into $2^{\\circ}$ alcohols .  <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/68_30180_explanation_5.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />   So, the correct option is (2).',NULL,'Medium','Match the following',2024,'JEE Main','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','chemistry/68_30180_question_1.png'),
(30183,'chemistry',70,1247,3036,'Read the following statements.<br>(I) Pyranose structure refers to a cyclic organic compound with one oxygen atom and five carbon atoms.<br>(II) The cyclic structure of glucose is correctly represented by Haworth structure.<br>(III) Five membered cyclic structure of glucose is called pyranose structure.<br>Which of the above statement(s) is/are true?','[{"text": "(I) and (III)", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "(I) and (II)", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Only (III)", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "(I), (II) and (III)", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Glucose exists as a six membered cyclic structure which is called pyranose structure.<br><img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/70_30183_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' /> <br>fructose exists as a five membered cyclic structure which is known as furanose structure.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ type-2',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(30184,'chemistry',68,1116,2675,'${{\\text{C}}_{\\text{2}}}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{5}}}{\\text{Br}}\\xrightarrow{{{\\text{alc}}{\\text{.KOH}}}}{\\text{A}}\\xrightarrow[{{\\text{CC}}{{\\text{l}}_{\\text{4}}}}]{{{\\text{B}}{{\\text{r}}_{\\text{2}}}}}{\\text{B}}\\xrightarrow[{{\\text{Excess}}}]{{{\\text{KCN}}}}$<br>${\\text{C}}\\xrightarrow[{{\\text{Excess}}}]{{{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{3}}}{{\\text{O}}^{\\text{ + }}}}}{\\text{D}}$ <br>Acid D formed in above reaction is','[{"text": "Malonic acid", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Oxalic acid", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Succinic acid", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Gluconic acid.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'<img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/68_30184_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />  So, the correct option is (3).',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2024,'JEE Main','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(30187,'chemistry',70,1247,3036,'Consider the following statements regarding glucose.<br>I. Glucose occurs freely in nature.<br>II. Glucose occurs in the combined form.<br>III. Glucose is present in sweet fruits and honey.<br>IV. Ripe grapes contain glucose in large amounts.<br>Select the correct statement(s) and choose the most appropriate option.','[{"text": "I and III", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "II and III", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "IV and I", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "I, II, III and IV", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Glucose occurs in both combined and in the free state in nature. Glucose is present in sweet fruits, honey. It is present in ripe grapes in a large amount.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ type-2',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(30188,'chemistry',68,1113,2670,'Identify the reagents used for the following conversion','[{"text": "<br>$\\\\begin{array}{l}\\n{\\\\rm{A = LiAl}}{{\\\\rm{H}}_{\\\\rm{4}}}{\\\\rm{,\\\\,B = NaO}}{{\\\\rm{H}}_{{\\\\rm{(aq )}}}}{\\\\rm{,}}\\\\\\\\\\n{\\\\rm{C = N}}{{\\\\rm{H}}_{\\\\rm{2}}} - {\\\\rm{N}}{{\\\\rm{H}}_{\\\\rm{2}}}{\\\\rm{/KOH,}}\\\\,{\\\\rm{ethylene}}\\\\,{\\\\rm{glycol}}\\n\\\\end{array}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "<br>${\\\\rm{A = LiAl}}{{\\\\rm{H}}_{\\\\rm{4}}}{\\\\rm{,\\\\,B = NaO}}{{\\\\rm{H}}_{{\\\\rm{(alc) }}}}{\\\\rm{,C = Zn/HCl}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "<br>$\\\\begin{array}{l}\\n{\\\\rm{A = DIBAL}} - {\\\\rm{H,B = NaO}}{{\\\\rm{H}}_{{\\\\rm{(aq) }}}}{\\\\rm{,}}\\\\\\\\\\n{\\\\rm{C = N}}{{\\\\rm{H}}_{\\\\rm{2}}} - {\\\\rm{N}}{{\\\\rm{H}}_{\\\\rm{2}}}{\\\\rm{/KOH,ethylene}}\\\\,{\\\\rm{glycol}}\\n\\\\end{array}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "<br>$\\\\begin{array}{l}\\n{\\\\rm{A = DIBAL}} - {\\\\rm{H,B = NaO}}{{\\\\rm{H}}_{{\\\\rm{(alc) }}}}{\\\\rm{,}}\\\\\\\\\\n{\\\\rm{C = Zn/HCl}}\\n\\\\end{array}$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'<img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/68_30188_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />  So, the correct option is (4).',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',2024,'JEE Main','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','chemistry/68_30188_question_1.png'),
(30192,'chemistry',70,1250,3047,'Read the following statements and choose the correct answer<br>(I) All monosaccharides are reducing sugars.<br>(II) All monosaccharides are non-reducing sugars.<br>(III) In disaccharides if aldehydic or ketonic groups are bonded, these are non-reducing sugars.<br>(IV)In disaccharides if aldehydic or ketonic groups are free, these are reducing sugars. The correct statements are','[{"text": "(I), (III) and (IV)", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "(II), (III) and (IV)", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "(I) and (IV)", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "(II) and (IV)", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Carbonyl group is free in all the monosaccharides. Hence, they are all reducing sugars.<br>The disaccharides in which carbonyl group is involved in bonding cannot react with Tollens\' reagent or Fehling\'s solution. Hence, they are nonreducing.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ type-2',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(30198,'chemistry',70,1250,3047,'Which of the following statements regarding carbohydrates are correct?<br>(I) Lactose is the carbohydrate found in milk.<br>(II) More than 25 monosaccharides occur naturally.<br>(III) Sucrose on hydrolysis gives one molecule each of glucose and fructose.<br>(IV)Maltose is a non-reducing sugar whereas sucrose is a reducing disaccharide sugar.','[{"text": "(I), (II) and (III)", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "(I) and (III)", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "(II), (III) and (IV)", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "(III) and (IV)", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'(I) Lactose is found in milk (true)<br>(II) About 20 monosaccharides are known to occur in nature. So, statement -II is incorrect.<br>(III) Sucrose is made up of glucose and fructose, So on hydrolysis of sucrose glucose and fructose are formed. So,statement -III is correct.<br>(IV) In maltose, ${\\mathrm{1-4}}$ glycosidic linkage exists between two ${\\mathrm{\\alpha-\\mathrm{D}}}$-glucose molecules. Hence, free aldehyde group can be produced on C-1 of one of glucose molecules. Thus, it is a reducing sugar and statement IV is incorrect.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ type-2',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(30202,'chemistry',70,1251,3051,'Read the following statements and choose the correct option?<br>(I) Starch is a polymer of ${\\mathrm{\\alpha}}$-glucose.<br>(II) Starch consists of amylose and amylopectin.<br>(III) Amylose is insoluble in water.<br>(IV) Amylopectin is soluble in water.<br>The correct statements are','[{"text": "(I) (III) and (IV)", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "(I), (II) and (III)", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "(I) and (II)", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "(III) and (IV)", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Amylopectin is insoluble in water and amylose is soluble in water. So statements III, IV are incorrect.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ type-2',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(30203,'chemistry',68,1111,2648,'Major product of the following reaction is','[{"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'RMgX converts ester into $3^{\\circ}$ alcohol and cyanide into ketone. <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/68_30203_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />  (or)  <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/68_30203_explanation_2.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />  So, the correct option is (4).',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',2024,'JEE Main','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','chemistry/68_30203_question_1.png'),
(30206,'chemistry',70,1250,3047,'Consider the following statements:<br>I. Disaccharides on hydrolysis with dilute acid give three molecules of same or different monosaccharides.<br>II. Disaccharides are formed by the loss of water and have an oxide linkage.<br>Choose the incorrect statement about disaccharide and select the most appropriate option.','[{"text": "Only I", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both I and II", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Only II", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "None of these", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Disaccharides on hydrolysis with dilute acids give two molecules of same or different monosaccharides. These monosaccharides bonded to each other through an oxide linkage (also called glycosidic linkage) by losing water molecule.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ type-2',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(30211,'chemistry',70,1253,3058,'Which of the following statements are correct?<br>(I) Proteins on hydrolysis give only ${\\mathrm{\\alpha}}$-amino acids.<br>(II)  Gln stands for glutamic acid.<br>(III) Amino acids with equal number of amino and carboxyl groups are neutral.<br>(IV) All naturally occurring ${\\mathrm{\\alpha}}$-amino acids are optically active.','[{"text": "(I) and (III)", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "(I), (II) and (IV)", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "(III) and (IV)", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "(II), (III) and (IV)", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'"Glu" stands for glutamic acid.<br>Glycine is a naturally occurring amino acid which is optically inactive.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ type-2',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(30214,'chemistry',70,1255,3066,'Which of the statements about "Denaturation" given below are correct?<br>(I) Denaturation of proteins causes loss of secondary and tertiary structures of the protein.<br>(II) Denaturation leads to the conversion of a double strand of DNA into single strand.<br>(III) Denaturation affects primary structure which gets distorted.','[{"text": "(II) and (III)", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "(I) and (III)", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "(I) and (II)", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "(I), (II) and (III)", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Denaturation of protein does not affect primary structure of protein. It causes loss of tertiary and secondary structures of protein and converts double stranded DNA into single strand.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ type-2',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(30217,'chemistry',70,1255,3066,'Mark the correct statements about denaturation of proteins.<br>(I) After denaturation, the biological activity of the protein is destroyed.<br>(II) The primary structure of the protein does not change.<br>(III) Fibrous proteins are converted into globular proteins.<br>(IV)Curdling of milk is an example of denaturation of protein.','[{"text": "I, II, and IV", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "I, II, and III", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Only I", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Only IV", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Denaturation causes loss of biological activity, loss of ${\\mathrm{2^{\\circ}}}$ and ${\\mathrm{3^{\\circ}}}$ structures. But, it does not cause any change in ${\\mathrm{1^{\\circ}}}$ structure of protein. Curdling of milk is an example of denaturation of protein.<br>In denaturation, globular proteins are converted into fibrous proteins.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ type-2',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(30221,'chemistry',70,1256,3067,'Name the enzyme that catalyzes the reaction given below.','[{"text": "Talose", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Lactose", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Pepsin", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Maltase", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Given molecule is maltose and maltase catalyses hydrolysis of maltose to glucose.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','chemistry/70_30221_question_1.png'),
(30225,'chemistry',70,1256,3067,'Which of the following statements is incorrect?','[{"text": "Enzymes are organic catalysts.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Enzymes have a very large turnover number.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Enzymes action is specific.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Enzymes always require a coenzyme in their catalytic action.", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Enzymes do not require coenzymes always, in their catalytic action.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(30238,'chemistry',70,1258,3071,'The base sequence for nucleic acid segment Data insufficient question it is needed can be deleted is given as CAG, AGG, CGA, and CCA. from this, it can definitely be concluded that it is a segment of','[{"text": "t-RNA", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "m-RNA", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "DNA", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Data not sufficient", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Data is not sufficient, because the distinction is done on the basis of presence of uracil in RNA and thymine in DNA, which are unique nitrogenous bases for those nucleic acids.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(30283,'chemistry',70,1254,3059,'The end product of protein digestion is','[{"text": "Peptides", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Peptones", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Protones", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\mathrm{\\\\alpha}}$-Amino acids", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'All proteins are made up of polypeptide chains between ${\\mathrm{\\alpha}}$-amino acids. So, on digestion of proteins end products will be ${\\mathrm{\\alpha}}$-amino acids.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(30228,'chemistry',70,1256,3067,'Of the following statements about enzymes, which ones are true?<br>(I) Enzymes lack in nucleophilic groups.<br>(II) Enzymes are highly specific both in binding chiral substrates and in catalyzing their reactions.<br>(III) Enzymes catalyze chemical reactions by lowering the energy of activation.<br>(IV)Pepsin is a proteolytic enzyme.','[{"text": "(I) and (IV)", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "(I) and (III)", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "(II), (III), and (IV)", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Only (I)", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Enzymes are protein molecules which have groups like ${\\mathrm{-\\mathrm{OH},-\\mathrm{NH}_{2}}}$, so they can act as nucleophiles.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ type-2',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(30231,'chemistry',70,1257,3068,'Which of the following statements are correct?<br>(I) Vitamins A, D, E, and K are insoluble in water.<br>(II) Vitamins A, D, E, and K are stored in liver and adipose tissues.<br>(III) Vitamin B and vitamin C are water soluble.<br>(IV) Water soluble vitamins should not be supplied regularly in diet.','[{"text": "(I), (II) and (IV)", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "(I), (II) and (III)", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "(I) and (IV)", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "(II) and (IV)", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Vitamins A,D,E, and ${\\mathrm{K}}$ are fat soluble vitamins and are stored in fat storing tissues like liver and adipose tissues. Vitamins B and C are water soluble and are removed from the body in the form of excreta, urine,sweat, etc. So, vitamins which are water soluble should be supplied regularly in diet.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ type-2',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(30235,'chemistry',70,1257,3068,'Consider the following statements regarding vitamins: <br>I. Vitamins are the organic compounds required in small amounts in the diet, but their deficiency causes specific disease.<br>II. Vitamins cannot be synthesized by plants, but our body can synthesize them.<br>III. Vitamins are necessary to perform the specific biological functions for the normal maintenance of optimum growth and health of the organism.<br>Select the false statements about the vitamins and choose the correct option.','[{"text": "Only I", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Only II", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "I and II", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "II and III", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Most of the vitamins cannot be synthesized by human body, but plants can synthesize almost all of them.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ type-2',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(30253,'chemistry',70,1259,3074,'Which of the following is responsible for preparing the uterus for implantation of fertilized egg?','[{"text": "Glucocorticoids", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Estradiol", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Testosterone", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Progesterone", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Progesterone is responsible for preparing the uterus for implantation of fertilized egg.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(30242,'chemistry',70,1258,3073,'Characters are transferred from parents to offspring through','[{"text": "RNA", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "DNA", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Lipid", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Protein", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'DNA, also known as the genetic material is responsible for the transfer of trails from parents to their offsprings.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(30245,'chemistry',70,1258,3073,'Among the two strands of DNA, the one that contains gene is called the coding strand. The other one, which is complementary to the coding strand, is called the template strand. The template strand directs the synthesis of complementary strand of RNA, called the messenger RNA. This process is called','[{"text": "Mutation", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Replication", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Transcription", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Translation", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Transcription is the process by which the information in a strand of DNA is copied into a new molecule of m-RNA.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(30248,'chemistry',70,1259,3074,'Which of the following hormones are amino acid derivatives?','[{"text": "Androgens", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Epinephrine", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Norepinephrine", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both (2) and (3)", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Epinephrine and norepinephrine are very similar neuro transmitters and hormones.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(30256,'chemistry',70,1253,3054,'Read Assertion and Reason carefully to mark the correct option given below.<br><br><strong>Assertion:</strong> Amino acids, exist as internal salts are called zwitter ions.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> The ${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{H}^{+}}}$given by ${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{CO}_{2} \\mathrm{H}}}$ is captured\nby ${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{NH}_{2}}}$ group having lone pair of electrons.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is true but Reason is false.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is false but reason is true.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Amino acids contain $ - \\,{\\rm{COOH}}$ & $ - {\\rm{N}}{{\\rm{H}}_{\\rm{2}}}$ groups. – COOH group is acidic so releases ${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{H}^{+}}}$while ${\\mathrm{-\\mathrm{NH}_{2}}}$ group is basic and accepts ${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{H}^{\\oplus}}}$ ions.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(30259,'chemistry',70,1256,3067,'Read Assertion and Reason carefully to mark the correct option given below.<br><br><strong>Assertion:</strong> All enzymes are proteins, but all proteins are not enzymes.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Enzymes are biocatalysts and possess a stable configuration having an active site pocket.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is true but Reason is false.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is false but reason is true.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Some proteins that are capable of catalyzing biochemical reactions are called enzymes, but all proteins can\'t function like enzymes.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
