(30301,'chemistry',70,1251,3051,'Consider the following statements.<br>(I) Linkage between two monosaccharide units through oxygen atom is called glycosidic linkage.<br>(II) Sucrose on hydrolysis gives an equimolar mixture of fructose and glucose which is dextrorotatory.<br>(III) Lactose consists of linkage between ${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{C}_{1}}}$ of galactose and ${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{C}_{4}}}$ of glucose.<br>(IV)Out of two components of starch, the component present in greater proportion is insoluble in water.<br>(V) Glycogen is also known as animal starch because it is structurally similar to amylose, a component of starch.<br>which of the following is the correct option regarding above statements (F- false, T-true).','[{"text": "FFFTT", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "FTTTF", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "TFTFT", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "TFTTF", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Sucrose on hydrolysis gives an equimolar mixture of glucose and fructose which is laevo rotatory.<br>Glycogen is known as animal starch because it is structurally similar to amylopectin, a component of starch.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ type-2',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(30302,'physics',12,72,279,'If an object of mass 1 $kg$ contains $4 \\times {10^{20}}$ atoms. If one electron is removed from every  atom of the solid, the charge gained by the solid in 1 $g$ is','[{"text": "$6.4 \\\\times {10^{ - 2}}C$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$2.8\\\\,C$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$9.2 \\\\times {10^{ - 4}}C$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$3.6 \\\\times {10^{ - 3}}C$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Here, number of electrons removed ${\\rm{ = }}$ number of atoms in 1$g$<br>$ \\Rightarrow n = \\frac{{4 \\times {{10}^{20}}}}{{{\\rm{1}}{{\\rm{0}}^3}}} = 4 \\times {10^{17}}$<br>$q = ne = 4 \\times {10^{17}} \\times 1.6 \\times {\\rm{1}}{{\\rm{0}}^{ - 19}}C$<br>$ = {\\rm{ }}6.4 \\times {10^{ - 2}}C$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(30305,'physics',12,71,278,'An uncharged metal object $M$ is insulated from its surroundings. A positively charged metal  sphere $S$ is then brought near to $M$. Which diagram best illustrates the resultant distributions of charge on $S$ and $M$ ?','[{"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Negative charges are attracted to surface of $M$ near $S$ by electrostatic induction and  positive charges are repelled to surface of  $M$ away from  $S$such that entire  still remains  $M$ neutral (uncharged). Charge on $S$  is also  redistributed to have more charge on surface  near to  $M$ due to the field set up by negative  charges on $M$.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(30308,'chemistry',70,1251,3051,'Match the following.<div class="matching-question"></div>','[{"text": "${\\\\mathrm{\\\\mathrm{A}-\\\\mathrm{T}, \\\\mathrm{B}-\\\\mathrm{P}, \\\\mathrm{C}-\\\\mathrm{S}, \\\\mathrm{D}-\\\\mathrm{Q}, \\\\mathrm{E}-\\\\mathrm{R}}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${\\\\text{A}} - {\\\\text{Q}},\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\text{B}} - {\\\\text{T}},\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\text{C}} - {\\\\text{P}},\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\text{D}} - {\\\\text{S}},\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\text{E}} - {\\\\text{R}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\mathrm{\\\\mathrm{A}-\\\\mathrm{R}, \\\\mathrm{B}-\\\\mathrm{S}, \\\\mathrm{C}-\\\\mathrm{Q}, \\\\mathrm{D}-\\\\mathrm{T}, \\\\mathrm{E}-\\\\mathrm{P}}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\text{A}} - {\\\\text{P}},\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\text{B}} - {\\\\text{R}},\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\text{C}} - {\\\\text{P}},{\\\\text{S}},\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\text{D}} - {\\\\text{Q}},\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\text{E}} - {\\\\text{T}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Sucrose ${\\mathrm{\\rightarrow \\alpha-1, \\beta-2}}$ glycosidic linkage of glucose and fructose molecules.<br>maltose ${\\mathrm{\\rightarrow \\alpha-1,4-}}$ glycosidic linkage of two glucose molecules.<br>Lactose ${\\mathrm{\\rightarrow \\beta-1,4-}}$ glycosidic linkage of galactose and glucose molecules.<br>Starch ${\\mathrm{\\rightarrow \\alpha-1,4}}$ glycosidic linkage for linear polymerisation and ${\\mathrm{\\alpha-1,6}}$ glycosidic linkage for branching (glucose molecules)<br>Callulose ${\\mathrm{\\rightarrow \\beta-1,4}}$ glycosidic linkage (glucose molecules)',NULL,'Medium','Match the following',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','chemistry/70_30308_question_1.png'),
(30309,'physics',12,74,286,'A charged particle is free to move in an electric field. It will travel','[{"text": "Always along a line of force", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Along a line of force, if its initial velocity is zero", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Along a line of force, if it has some initial velocity in the direction of an acute angle with the line of force", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "None of these", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Because $E$ points along the tangent to the lines of force. If initial velocity is zero, then due to the force, it always moves in the direction of $E$.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(30312,'chemistry',70,1250,3044,'Invert sugar is mixture of','[{"text": "D-glucose + D-fructose", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "L-glucose $+\\\\mathrm{D}$-fructose", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "L-glucose + D-glucose", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "L-glucose + L-glucose", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Invert sugar is a mixture of D-glucose and D-fructose. Solution of sucrose is dextrorotatory. When sucrose is hydrolysed it forms equimolar quantity of D-glucose and D-fructose. The solution of formed products is found to be laevorotatory. This change in optical properties of sucrose is called inversion of cane sugar. The equimolar product formed, i.e D-glucose and D-fructose is called invert sugar.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(30313,'physics',12,73,280,'Two charges are placed at a distance apart. If a glass slab is placed between them, the force between them will','[{"text": "Be zero", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Increase", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Decrease", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Remains same", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Force reduces by $K$ times its intial value.<br>Where $K$ is the dielectric constant of the slab.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(30316,'chemistry',70,1251,3049,'Which one of the following represents the correct structure of cellulose?','[{"text": "${\\\\rm{Branched}},{\\\\rm{\\\\alpha }} \\\\,{\\\\mkern 1mu} ({\\\\rm{1}},{\\\\rm{4}})\\\\,{\\\\rm{and}}{\\\\mkern 1mu} {\\\\mkern 1mu} {\\\\rm{\\\\beta }}\\\\,{\\\\mkern 1mu} ({\\\\rm{1}},{\\\\rm{6}})\\\\,{\\\\rm{ - glucose}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{Unbranched,\\\\alpha (1,6) - glucose}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{Branched,}}\\\\,{\\\\rm{\\\\alpha }}\\\\,{\\\\rm{(1,4) - glucose}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{Unbranched}}{\\\\mkern 1mu} {\\\\rm{,}}{\\\\mkern 1mu} \\\\,{\\\\mkern 1mu} {\\\\rm{\\\\beta }}{\\\\mkern 1mu} \\\\,{\\\\rm{(1,4) }} - {\\\\rm{ glucose}}$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Cellulose has a straight chain polysaccharide composed only of ${\\rm{\\beta }}-{\\rm{D}}$ glucose units. These ${\\rm{\\beta }}-{\\rm{D}}$ glucose units are linked together by $\\mathrm{C}_{1}-\\mathrm{C}_{4}$ glycosidic linkage. The structure is shown below:  <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/70_30316_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(30317,'physics',12,73,282,'Two positively charged spheres of masses, ${m_1}\\,{\\rm{and}}\\,{m_2}$ are suspended from a common point at the ceiling by identical insulating massless strings of length $l$. Charges on the two spheres are ${q_1}\\,{\\rm{and}}\\,{q_2}$,respectively. At equilibrium both strings make the same angle $\\theta $ with the vertical.Then','[{"text": "${q_1}{m_1} = {q_2}{m_2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${m_1} = {m_2}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${m_1} = {m_2}\\\\sin \\\\theta $", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${q_2}{m_1} = {q_1}{m_2}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'The forces on the two masses are shown in the figure. Let $\\theta $ is the angle made by the strings with vertical <img src=\'/img/data/physics/12_30317_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' /> For equilibrium of ${m_1}$<br>${T_1}\\cos \\theta = {m_1}g$<br>${T_1}\\sin \\theta = F$<br>$\\tan \\theta = \\frac{F}{{{m_1}g}}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,(1)$<br>For equilibrium of ${m_2}$<br>${T_2}\\cos \\theta = {m_2}g$<br>${T_2}\\sin \\theta = F$<br>$\\tan \\theta = \\frac{F}{{{m_1}g}}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,(2)$<br>From (1) &amp; (2)<br>${m_1} = {m_2}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(30320,'physics',12,73,280,'An electron moving around the nucleus of a hydrogen atom in a circular orbit of radius $r$.  The coulomb force  $\\overrightarrow F $ between the two is (where $K = \\frac{1}{{4\\pi {\\varepsilon _0}}}$)','[{"text": "$ - K\\\\frac{{{e^2}}}{{{r^2}}}\\\\hat r$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$ - K\\\\frac{{{e^2}}}{{{r^3}}}\\\\overrightarrow r $", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$ - K\\\\frac{{{e^2}}}{{{r^3}}}\\\\hat r$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$K\\\\frac{{{e^2}}}{{{r^4}}}\\\\overrightarrow r $", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'The radial force present towards the centre is taken as negative. <br>$\\vec F =  - k\\frac{{{e^2}}}{{{r^2}}}\\hat r =  - k \\cdot \\frac{{{e^2}}}{{{r^3}}}\\vec r\\left[ {\\because \\hat r = \\frac{{\\vec r}}{r}} \\right]$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(30321,'chemistry',70,1251,3048,'On hydrolysis of starch, we finally get','[{"text": "glucose", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "fructose", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both (1) and (2)", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "sucrose", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Starch is a polymer of D-glucose units, thus on hydrolysis it gives glucose only.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(30324,'physics',12,73,280,'Force of attraction between two point electric charges placed at a distance $d$ in a medium is $F$.   What distance apart should these be kept in the  same medium, so that force between them  becomes $F/3$?','[{"text": "$\\\\sqrt 3 \\\\,d$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$2\\\\sqrt 3 \\\\,d$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$3{\\\\kern 1pt} d$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$9{\\\\kern 1pt} d$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Let ${q_1}$ and ${q_2}$ be the two point charges. The force between the charges, at a separation of $d$.<br>$F = \\frac{1}{{4\\pi {\\varepsilon _0}}} \\cdot \\frac{{{q_1}\\,{q_2}}}{{{d^2}}}$<br>Suppose that force between the two charges become $F/3$, when the charges are kept at a  distance  apart. Then <br>$\\frac{1}{{4\\pi {\\varepsilon _0}}} \\cdot \\frac{{{q_1}\\,{q_2}}}{{{x^2}}} = \\frac{F}{3}$<br>or    $\\frac{1}{{4\\pi {\\varepsilon _0}}} \\cdot \\frac{{{q_1}\\,{q_2}}}{{{x^2}}} = \\frac{1}{3}\\left( {\\frac{1}{{4\\pi {\\varepsilon _0}}} \\cdot \\frac{{{q_1}\\,{q_2}}}{{{d^2}}}} \\right)$<br>or   $x = \\sqrt 3 \\,d$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(30327,'chemistry',70,1251,3048,'What is the nature of glucose-glucose linkage in starch that makes it so susceptible to acid hydrolysis?','[{"text": "Starch is a hemiacetal", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Starch is an acetal", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Starch is a polymer", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Starch contains only few molecules of glucose", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Starch is an acetal.<br>Monosaccharides are hemiacetals.<br>Acetals and hemiacetals get readily<br> hydrolysed by acids to give alcohols.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(30328,'physics',12,73,280,'The nucleus of helium atom contains two protons that are separated by distance $3.0 \\times {10^{ - 15}}m$. The &nbsp;magnitude of the electrostatic force that each proton exerts on the other is','[{"text": "$25.6\\\\,N$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$20.6\\\\,N$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$12.6\\\\,N$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$15.6\\\\,N$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Charge of proton, ${q_p} = 1.6 \\times {10^{ - 19}}\\,C$<br>Distance between the protons, $r = 3 \\times {10^{ - 15}}\\,m$<br>The magnitude of electrostatic force between then is <br>${{\\rm{F}}_e} = \\frac{{{q_p}{q_p}}}{{4\\pi {\\varepsilon _0}{r^2}}} = \\frac{{9 \\times {{10}^9} \\times 1.6 \\times {{10}^{ - 19}} \\times 1.6 \\times {{10}^{ - 19}}}}{{{{\\left( {3 \\times {{10}^{ - 15}}} \\right)}^2}}}$<br>$ = 25.6\\,N$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(30331,'chemistry',70,1250,3044,'Hydrolysis of sucrose is called','[{"text": "inversion", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "esterification", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "hydration", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "saponification", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'On hydrolysis, sucrose gives an equimolar mixutre of<br>D-(+)-glucose and D-(-)- fructose  <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/70_30331_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />  Here, the configuration of sucrose changes (gets inverted) after hydrolysis, thus its hydrolysis is called inversion.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(30332,'physics',12,73,280,'A certain charge 2$Q$ is divided at first into two parts ${q_1}$ and ${q_2}$. Later the charges are placed at a certain distance. If the force of interaction between two charges is maximum then $\\frac{Q}{{{q_1}}} = $ ……','[{"text": "$1$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$4$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$0.5$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Let $r$ be the distance between ${q_1}$ and ${q_2}$. According to Coulomb\'s law, the force  between them is <br>$F = \\frac{1}{{4\\pi {\\varepsilon _0}}}\\frac{{{q_1}{q_2}}}{{{r^2}}}$$ = \\frac{1}{{4\\pi {\\varepsilon _0}}}\\frac{{{q_1}(2Q - {q_1})}}{{{r^2}}}$<br>$(\\because \\,\\,{q_1} + {q_2} = 2Q)$<br>For $F$ to be maximum $\\frac{{dF}}{{d{q_1}}} = 0$  as $Q$ and $r$ are constant,<br>$\\frac{d}{{d{q_1}}}\\left[ {{q_1}(2Q - {q_1})} \\right] = 0$<br>$2Q - 2{q_1} = 0;$ Hence,$\\frac{Q}{{{q_1}}} = 1$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(30335,'physics',12,73,280,'Two equal point charges each of $3\\mu C$ are separated by a certain distance in metres. If they are located at $(\\hat i + \\hat j + \\hat k)$ and $(2\\hat i + 3\\hat j + 3\\hat k),$ the electrostatic force between them is','[{"text": "$9 \\\\times {10^{ - 2}}N$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$9 \\\\times {10^{ - 3}}N$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${10^{ - 3}}N$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$9 \\\\times {10^3}N$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Here, ${q_1} = 3 \\times {10^{ - 6}}C,\\,{q_2} = 3 \\times {10^{ - 6}}C$<br>${r_1} = \\hat i + \\hat j + \\hat k,{r_2} = 2\\hat i + 3\\hat j + 3\\hat k$<br>$\\therefore \\overrightarrow r = \\overrightarrow {{r_2}} - \\overrightarrow {{r_1}} $<br>$ = (2\\hat i + 3\\hat j + 3\\hat k) - (\\hat i + \\hat j + \\hat k) = \\hat i + 2\\hat j + 2\\hat k$<br>The distance between the two charges is<br>$\\left| {\\overrightarrow {{r_1}} } \\right| = \\sqrt {{{(1)}^2} + {{(2)}^2} + {{(2)}^2}} = 3$<br>According to <span style="text-decoration: underline;">coulomb&rsquo;s</span> law,<br>$F = \\frac{1}{{4\\pi {\\varepsilon _0}}} \\cdot \\frac{{{q_1}{q_2}}}{{{r^2}}}$<br>$ = \\frac{{9 \\times {{10}^9} \\times 3 \\times {{10}^{ - 6}} \\times 3 \\times {{10}^{ - 6}}}}{{{{(3)}^2}}} = 9 \\times {10^{ - 3}}N$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(30336,'chemistry',70,1250,3044,'The change in optical rotation of freshly prepared solution of cane sugar with time is known as','[{"text": "mutarotation", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "inversion", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "specific rotation", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "rotatory motion", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Inversion is the change in specific rotation of an optically active compound in solution with time to an equilibrium value.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(30339,'physics',12,74,285,'Two charges $ + 6\\mu C$ and $ + 15\\mu C$ are placed  along the $x$-axis at $x = 0$ and $x = 2m$ respectively.  A negative charge is placed between them such that the resultant force on it is zero.The negative charge is placed at','[{"text": "$x = 0.775m$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$x = 0.5m$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$x = 1.2m$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Position depends on the amount of charge", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Let the charge $( - q)$ be placed at a  distance $x$ from $ + 6\\mu C$. As the resultant force on $( - q)$ is zero.<br>$\\therefore \\frac{{\\left( q \\right) \\times 6 \\times {{10}^{ - 6}}}}{{4\\pi {\\varepsilon _0} \\times {x^2}}} = \\frac{{\\left( q \\right) \\times 15 \\times {{10}^{ - 6}}}}{{4\\pi {\\varepsilon _0} \\times \\left( {2 - {x^2}} \\right)}}$<br>$\\frac{6}{{{x^2}}} = \\frac{{15}}{{{{(2 - x)}^2}}} \\Rightarrow \\frac{1}{x} =  \\pm \\frac{{\\sqrt 5 }}{{\\sqrt 2 (2 - x)}}$<br>On solving, we get $x = 0.775m$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(30340,'chemistry',70,1247,3035,'Glucose + Tollen\'s reagent $\\longrightarrow$ Silver mirror, <br>the above process shows','[{"text": "presence of - $\\\\mathrm{COOH}$ group", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "presence of keto group", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "presence of - CHO group", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "presence of - $\\\\mathrm{CONH}_{2}$ group", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'<img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/70_30340_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />  This reaction indicates the presence of - $\\mathrm{CHO}$ group in glucose.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(30343,'physics',12,73,281,'A charge $ + q$ is placed $(a,0,0)$ and another $ + q$ charge is placed at $( - a,0,0)$.A charged $ - {q_1}$  is placed at the origin. If it is slightly displaced along $y$-axis then','[{"text": "It will stand at the displaced position", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "It wil move away", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "It will execute SHM", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "It will oscillate but not SHM", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'When the charge $ - {q_1}$  is displaced slightly along $y$ - axis by $y$ then a restoring force will be developed which is proportion to small  displacement $y$<br>$F \\propto  - y$<br>The charge executes SHM',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(30345,'chemistry',70,1250,3047,'When sucrose is heated with conc. $\\mathrm{HNO}_{3}$, the product formed is','[{"text": "sucrose nitrate", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "adipic acid", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "oxalic acid", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "citric acid", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Sucrose reacts with conc. $\\mathrm{HNO}_{3}$ to give oxalic acid. <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/70_30345_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />  In this reaction, sucrose oxidises into oxalic acid.',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(30347,'physics',12,78,295,'A thin semi-circular ring of radius $r$ has a positive charge $q$ distributed uniformly over it. The net  electric field $\\overrightarrow E $ at the centre $O$ is','[{"text": "$\\\\frac{q}{{2{\\\\pi ^2}{\\\\varepsilon _0}{r^2}}}\\\\hat j$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{q}{{2{\\\\pi ^2}{\\\\varepsilon _0}{r^2}}}\\\\hat j$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$ - \\\\frac{q}{{2{\\\\pi ^2}{\\\\varepsilon _0}{r^2}}}\\\\hat j$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$ - \\\\frac{q}{{4{\\\\pi ^2}{\\\\varepsilon _0}{r^2}}}\\\\hat j$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Consider a small charge $dq$ that subtends an angle $d\\theta $ at the centre.<br>From symmetry the net field is along $y$-direction.<br>$d{E_y} = dE\\sin \\theta $<br>${E_y} = \\int\\limits_0^\\pi  {dE\\sin \\theta } $<br>$ \\Rightarrow {E_y} = \\int\\limits_0^\\pi  {k\\frac{{dq}}{{{r^2}}}\\sin \\theta } $<br>$ = \\frac{k}{{{r^2}}}\\int\\limits_0^\\pi  {\\left( {\\frac{{qd\\theta }}{\\pi }} \\right)\\sin \\theta } \\quad \\left( {{\\rm{ }}dq = \\frac{{qrd\\theta }}{{\\pi r}}} \\right)$<br>$ = \\frac{{kq}}{{\\pi {r^2}}}\\int\\limits_0^\\pi  {\\sin \\theta } d\\theta  = \\frac{q}{{2{\\pi ^2}{\\varepsilon _0}{r^2}}}\\quad \\left( {\\int\\limits_0^\\pi  {\\sin \\theta } d\\theta  = 2} \\right)$<br>${\\overrightarrow E _y} =  - \\frac{q}{{2{\\pi ^2}{\\varepsilon _0}{r^2}}}\\hat j$<br>(as $\\vec E$ is directed along -$Y$ axis )',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/12_30347_question_1.png'),
(30350,'chemistry',70,1247,3035,'Glucose forms many derivatives. The derivative which will help to prove the furanose structure is','[{"text": "osazone", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "benzoyl", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "acetyl", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "isopropylidene", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Formation of isopropylidene derivative requires the presence of two adjacent free - $\\mathrm{OH}$ groups in cis-orientation which is present only in the furanose form and hence, isopropylidene formation proves the presence of furanose form.',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(30351,'physics',12,78,297,'In a region with uniform electric field, the number of lines of force per unit area is $E$. If a spherical metallic conductor is placed in this region, the number of lines of force per unit area inside the conductor will be','[{"text": "More than $E$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Zero", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$E$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Less than $E$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Electric field inside the metallic conductor is zero.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(30354,'physics',12,78,297,'The electric field at a distance $\\frac{{3R}}{2}$ from the  centre of a charged conducting spherical shell of radius $R$ is $E$. The electric field at distance $\\frac{R}{2}$ from the centre of the sphere is','[{"text": "$E/2$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{Zero}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$E/3$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$E$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Electric field inside a conducting shell is zero.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(30355,'chemistry',70,1247,3034,'In the following structure, anomeric carbon is','[{"text": "1", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "2", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "3", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "4", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'$\\mathrm{C}-1$ is an anomeric carbon and due to this, the given structure exists in two forms, $\\alpha$ and $\\beta$.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','chemistry/70_30355_question_1.png'),
(30358,'physics',12,74,286,'A proton and an electron are placed in a uniform electric field. Which of the following is correct?','[{"text": "Their accelerations will be equal", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "The magnitude of their accelerations will be equal", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "The electric forces acting on them will be equal", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "The magnitude of the forces will be equal", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Both the proton and the electron carry  equal charges. As the field is same then the  magnitude of forces are equal.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(30359,'chemistry',70,1261,3076,'Which of the following does not form an oxime?','[{"text": "Glucose", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Glucose pentaacetate", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Arabinose", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Galactose", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Glucose pentaacetate does not form an oxime because the glycosidic - $\\mathrm{OH}$ group is not free because it is involved in ring formation. As a result, it cannot get converted into the open chain form required for the formation of oxime.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(30362,'physics',12,74,286,'A positively charged particle moving along  horizontal-axis with a certain velocity enters a  uniform electric field directed along positive  vertical-axis. Its','[{"text": "Horizontal velocity changes but vertical velocity remains constant", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Vertical velocity changes but horizontal velocity remains constant", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Neither vertical nor horizontal velocity changes", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both vertical and horizontal velocities change", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Vertical velocity changes due to electric field, but no change in horizontal velocity.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(30364,'chemistry',70,1247,3033,'Number of chiral carbon atoms in ${\\beta  - {\\rm{D}}{\\kern 1pt} {\\rm{(}} + )}$-glucose is','[{"text": "five", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "six", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "three", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "four", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'The number of chiral carbon atoms in $\\beta$-D (+)- glucose is five. <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/70_30364_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(30366,'physics',12,74,286,'A pendulum bob of mass $m$ carrying a charge $q$  is at rest in a uniform horizontal electric field of  intensity $E$. The tension in the thread is','[{"text": "$T = \\\\sqrt {{{\\\\left( {Eq} \\\\right)}^2} + {{\\\\left( {mg} \\\\right)}^2}} $", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$T = \\\\sqrt {{{\\\\left( {\\\\frac{E}{q}} \\\\right)}^2} + {{\\\\left( {mg} \\\\right)}^2}} $", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$T = \\\\sqrt {{{\\\\left( {\\\\frac{E}{q}} \\\\right)}^2} + {{\\\\left( {\\\\frac{m}{g}} \\\\right)}^2}} $", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$T = mg + Eq$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'$T = \\sqrt {{F_g}^2 + {F_e}^2}  = \\sqrt {{{\\left( {mg} \\right)}^2} + {{\\left( {Eq} \\right)}^2}} $',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(30369,'physics',12,74,286,'A charged particle of mass $m$ and charge $q$ is released from rest in an electric field of constant magnitude $E$. The kinetic energy of the particle after time $t$ is','[{"text": "$\\\\frac{{2{E^2}{t^2}}}{{2q}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{{{E^2}{q^2}{t^2}}}{{2m}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{{E{q^2}m}}{{2{t^2}}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{{Eqm}}{{2t}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'$F = qE \\Rightarrow a = \\frac{F}{m} = \\frac{{qE}}{m}$<br>$v = at = \\frac{{qEt}}{m}$<br>Kinetic energy $ = \\frac{1}{2}m{\\left[ {\\frac{{qEt}}{m}} \\right]^2} = \\frac{{{E^2}{q^2}{t^2}}}{{2m}}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL);
INSERT INTO `qb_questions` (`id`, `subject_id`, `chapter_id`, `topic_id`, `subtopic_id`, `question_html`, `options`, `correct_index`, `explanation`, `explanation_image_url`, `difficulty`, `qtype`, `year`, `tag`, `created_at`, `question_image_url`) VALUES
(30370,'chemistry',70,1246,3028,'Hexoses and pentoses are','[{"text": "disaccharides", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "monosaccharides", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "polysaccharides", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "oligosaccharides", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Those sugars which contain $2,3,5$,6 carbon atoms and which cannot be hydrolysed further into simpler units are known as monosaccharides.Hence, hexoses and pentoses are monosaccharides.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(30373,'physics',12,75,287,'A cube of a metal is given a positive charge  $Q$. For the given system, which of the following statements is true?','[{"text": "Electric field is normal to the surface of the cube.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Electric field varies within the cube.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Electric potential at the surface of the cube is zero.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Electric potential within the cube is zero.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Electric lines of force are always normal to metallic body.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(30374,'chemistry',70,1256,3067,'The enzymes which have control site in addition to active site are called','[{"text": "holozymes", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "coenzymes", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "apoenzymes", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "allosteric enzymes", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'A coenzyme is a substance that works with an enzyme to initiate or aid the function of enzyme. They act as control site and active sites.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(30377,'physics',12,75,287,'Which of the following lines of force is uniform field?','[{"text": "", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'When the lines of force are equidistant straight lines, the field is uniform.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(30378,'chemistry',70,1254,3065,'Sanger\'s reagent is used for the identification of','[{"text": "C-terminal amino acid of peptide chain", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "N-terminal amino acid of peptide chain", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "molecular mass of protein", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "secondary structure of protein", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'The Sanger\'s reagent is FDNB. <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/70_30378_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />  It reacts with $\\mathrm{N}$-terminal amino acid of peptide chain.',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(30380,'physics',12,75,287,'The given figure gives electric lines of force due to two charges ${q_1}\\,{\\rm{and}}\\,{q_2}$.  What are the signs of the two charges?','[{"text": "Both are positive", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both are negative", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${q_1}$ is negative but ${q_2}$ is positive", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${q_1}$ is positive ${q_2}$ is negative", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Since the lines of forces are terminating on the charges both have to be negative.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/12_30380_question_1.png'),
(30383,'chemistry',70,1254,3064,'Proteins when heated with conc. $\\mathrm{HNO}_{3}$ give a yellow colour. This is','[{"text": "oxidising test", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "xanthoprotic test", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Hoppe\'s test", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "acid-base test", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Xanthoprotic test is given by a protein which consists of $\\alpha$-amino acids containing a benzene ring such as tyrosine, phenyl alanine etc. This yellow colour is due to nitration of benzene ring and this colour is same as that which forms on skin when conc. $\\mathrm{HNO}_{3}$ comes in contact.<br>$\\begin{array}{*{20}{l}}\n  {{\\text{Protein}} + {\\text{Conc}}{\\text{.HN}}{{\\text{O}}_3} \\to } \\\\ \n  {{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\text{Yellow}}\\,{\\mkern 1mu} {\\text{colour}}} \n\\end{array}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(30384,'physics',12,75,287,'If a point charge $q$ is placed at a point inside a  hollow conducting sphere, then which of the  following electric lines of force pattern is  correct?','[{"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "None of these", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'The charge $q$ induces $ - q$ on the inner  surface of the shell and $ + q$ is induced on the outer surface of the shell.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(30387,'physics',12,75,287,'A point positive charge brought near an isolated conducting sphere in the figure. The electric field lines are best given by','[{"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'The surface of a conducting body is an equipotential surface. Field lines are always perpendicular to the conducting surface. $( - )ve$ charge is induced on the leftside and $( + )ve$ charge is induced on the rightside.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(30390,'physics',12,75,287,'A long cylindrical shell carries positive surface charge $\\sigma $ in the upper half and negative surface  $ - \\sigma $ in the lower half. The electric field lines around the cylinder will look like (figures are not drawn to scale)','[{"text": "", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Field lines travel from $( + )ve$ charge to the $( - )ve$ charge and they are perpendicular to the  surface.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(30393,'chemistry',70,1254,3059,'Which one of the following structures represents the peptide chain?','[{"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'The peptide linkage ( $-\\mathrm{NH}-\\mathrm{CO}-)$ is formed by the condensation of amino acid molecules. <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/70_30393_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' /> Hence, following structure represents the peptide chain. <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/70_30393_explanation_2.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(30394,'physics',12,77,289,'The formation of a dipole is due to two equal  and dissimilar point charges placed at a','[{"text": "Short distance", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Long distance", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Above each other", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "None of these", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Dipole is formed when two equal and unlike  charges are placed at a short distance.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(30397,'chemistry',70,1254,3064,'Which of the following test is not used for testing of proteins?','[{"text": "Millon\'s test", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Molisch\'s test", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Biuret test", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Ninhydrin test", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Molisch\'s test is used for testing the presence of carbohydrate and is not used in testing of protein.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(30398,'physics',12,77,292,'If field is non-uniform and a dipole is placed in it. Then,','[{"text": "Dipole must be in rotation", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Dipole can be in rotation and translation", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Dipole must be in translation", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Dipole does not show any rotation or translation", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'If ${\\vec E}$ is not parallel or antiparallel to ${\\vec p}$ then  force and torque will be present on the dipole.  So option (2) is correct.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(30401,'physics',12,77,292,'An electric dipole is kept in non-uniform electric field. Predict the wrong option','[{"text": "It can experience a force but not a torque.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "It can experience a torque but not a force.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "It can experience a force and a torque.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "It can experience neither a force nor a torque.", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'As the dipole will feel two forces which are  opposite but not equal.<br>$\\therefore $ A net force will be there and as these forces act at different points of a body. A torque can also be there.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(30403,'chemistry',70,1254,3065,'The correct statement in respect of protein haemoglobin is that it','[{"text": "functions as a catalyst for biological reactions", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "maintains blood sugar level", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "acts as an oxygen carrier in the blood", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "forms antibodies and offers resistance to diseases", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Haemoglobin acts as an oxygen carrier in the blood because four $\\mathrm{Fe}^{2+}$ ions of each haemoglobin can bind with four molecules of $\\mathrm{O}_{2}$ and form oxyhaemoglobin.<br>$4{\\rm{Hb}} + 4{{\\rm{O}}_2} \\to \\mathop {{\\rm{H}}{{\\rm{b}}_4}{{\\rm{O}}_8}}\\limits_{{\\rm{ Oxyhaemoglobin }}} $',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(30405,'physics',12,77,291,'An electric dipole is placed at an angle of $30^\\circ $  with an electric field of intensity  $4 \\times {10^5}\\,N{C^{ - 1}}$ , It experiences a torque of $8\\,N - m$.  The charge on the dipole, if the dipole  length is 4 $cm$ will be','[{"text": "$8\\\\,mC$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$4\\\\,mC$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2\\\\,\\\\mu C$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1\\\\,mC$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Torque,$\\vec \\tau  = \\vec p \\times \\vec E = pE\\,\\sin \\,\\theta $<br>$8 = p \\times 4 \\times {10^5} \\times \\sin \\,\\,30^\\circ $<br>$ \\Rightarrow p = 4 \\times {10^{ - 5}}\\,\\,C{\\rm{ - }}m$<br>Dipole moment, $p = q \\times l$<br>$q = \\frac{p}{l} = \\frac{{4 \\times {{10}^{ - 5}}}}{{0.04}} = 1 \\times {10^{ - 3}}C = 1\\,mC$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(30408,'chemistry',70,1258,3072,'The pair of nitrogenous of bases in DNA are held together by','[{"text": "ionic bonds", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "H-bond", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "phosphate group", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "deoxyribose group", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'The pair of nitrogenous bases in DNA are held with each other by $\\mathrm{H}$-bond.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(30409,'physics',12,77,291,'An electric dipole is formed by two particles  fixed at the ends of a light rigid rod of length $l$.   The mass of each particle is $m$ and charges are  $ - q\\,{\\rm{and}}\\, + q$. The system is suspended by a  torsionless thread in an electric field of intensity  $E$ such that the dipole axis is parallel to the field.  If it is slightly displaced,  the period of angular  motion is','[{"text": "$\\\\infty $", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2\\\\pi \\\\sqrt {\\\\frac{{ml}}{{qE}}} $", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2\\\\pi \\\\sqrt {\\\\frac{{ml}}{{2qE}}} $", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{1}{{2\\\\pi }}\\\\sqrt {\\\\frac{{ml}}{{4qE}}} $", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'The torque on the dipole is <br>$\\tau  = pE\\,\\,\\sin \\theta $<br>$\\tau  = I\\alpha $<br>$I\\alpha  = pE\\,\\theta ({\\rm{For}}\\,{\\rm{small}}\\,\\theta )$<br>The moment of inertia is<br>$I = 2\\left( {m{{\\left( {\\frac{l}{2}} \\right)}^2}} \\right) = \\frac{{m{l^2}}}{2}$<br>$\\therefore $ Time period $ = 2\\pi \\sqrt {\\frac{I}{{pE}}}  = 2\\pi \\sqrt {\\frac{{ml}}{{2qE}}} $',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(30412,'chemistry',70,1258,3073,'A codon has a sequence of A and specifies a particular B that is to be incorporated into C. What are A ,B ,C ?','[{"text": "A-3 bases    B-Amino acid     C-Carbohydrate", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "A- 3 acids   B-Carbohydrate   C-Protein", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "A-3 bases   B-Protein  Aminoacid  C-acid", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "A-3 bases   B-Amino acid   C-Protein", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'A codon is a specific sequence of three adjacent bases on a strand of DNA or RNA that provides genetic code information for a particular amino acid, that is to be incorporated into protein.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(30413,'physics',12,132,301,'Gauss’s law is valid for','[{"text": "Only irregular open surfaces", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Any open surfaces", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Any closed surfaces", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Only irregular close surfaces", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Gauss’s law is valid for any closed  surface, no matter what its shape or size is.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(30416,'chemistry',70,1258,3073,'Which is not the correct statement about RNA and DNA?','[{"text": "DNA is active in virus whereas RNA never appears in virus", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "DNA exists as dimer while RNA is usually single-stranded", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "DNA contains deoxyribose as its sugar and RNA contains ribose", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "RNA contains uracil in place of thymine (found in DNA) as a base", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'A retrovirus is a RNA virus that is duplicated in a host cell.$\\therefore$ Statement (a) is incorrect and rest all statement are correct.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(30417,'physics',12,132,301,'In which of the following cases, the flux crossing through the surface is zero?','[{"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'In option (1) the net force is non zero as  the surface is enclosed by a charge. In options (2) and (3) the surfaces are open and hence flux through them is non zero as number of field lines entering and leaving are not equal. In option (4) the net flux through the closed surface is zero as there is no charge inside it.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(30420,'physics',12,76,288,'A uniform electrical field $a\\widehat i + b\\widehat j$ intersects a surface of area $A$. What is the flux through this area if the surface lies in $XZ$ plane?','[{"text": "$Ab$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$Aa$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{{A\\\\left( {ab} \\\\right)}}{{a + b}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$A\\\\sqrt {{a^2} + {b^2}} $", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'The direction of the field is<br>Given that  $\\overline {\\rm{E}}  = a\\hat i + b\\hat j\\,\\& \\overline A  = A\\hat j$<br>$\\phi  = \\overline E .\\overline A  = Ab$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(30424,'physics',12,132,301,'${{\\rm{q}}_1},{q_2},{q_3}\\,{\\rm{and}}\\,{q_4}$are point charges located at points as shown in the figure and $S$ is a spherical Gaussian surface of radius $R$. Which of the following is true according to Gauss’s law?','[{"text": "$\\\\oint {\\\\left( {{{\\\\vec E}_1} + {{\\\\vec E}_2} + {{\\\\vec E}_3}} \\\\right).d\\\\vec A = \\\\frac{{{q_1} + {q_2} + {q_3}}}{{2{\\\\varepsilon _0}}}} $", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\oint {\\\\left( {{{\\\\vec E}_1} + {{\\\\vec E}_2} + {{\\\\vec E}_3}} \\\\right).d\\\\vec A = \\\\frac{{\\\\left( {{q_1} + {q_2} + {q_3}} \\\\right)}}{{{\\\\varepsilon _0}}}} $", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\oint {\\\\left( {{{\\\\vec E}_1} + {{\\\\vec E}_2} + {{\\\\vec E}_3}} \\\\right).d\\\\vec A = \\\\frac{{\\\\left( {{q_1} + {q_2} + {q_3} + {q_4}} \\\\right)}}{{{\\\\varepsilon _0}}}} $", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\oint {\\\\left( {{{\\\\vec E}_1} + {{\\\\vec E}_2} + {{\\\\vec E}_3} + {{\\\\overrightarrow E }_4}} \\\\right).d\\\\vec A = \\\\frac{{\\\\left( {{q_1} + {q_2} + {q_3}} \\\\right)}}{{{\\\\varepsilon _0}}}} $", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'From Gauss’s law <br>$\\oint {\\overline E .d\\overline A }  = \\frac{{{q_{total}}}}{{{\\varepsilon _o}}}$<br>Where $\\overline {\\rm{E}} $  is the resultant electric field at a point by both inside and outside charges. ${q_{total}}$  is the net charge enclosed inside the gaussian surface.<br>$\\overline E  = {\\overline E _1} + {\\overline E _2} + {\\overline E _3} + {\\overline E _4}$<br>$q = {q_1} + {q_2} + {q_3}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/12_30424_question_1.png'),
(30428,'physics',12,76,288,'The number of electric field lines crossing an area $\\Delta S$ is ${n_1}$ when $\\Delta \\overrightarrow S \\parallel \\overrightarrow E $, while number of field lines crossing same area is  ${n_2}$ when $\\Delta S$  makes an angle of $30^\\circ $  with ${\\vec E}$ , then:','[{"text": "${n_1} < {n_2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${n_1} > {n_2}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${n_1} = {n_2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Cannot say anything", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'The no.of field lines crossing an area is directly related to electric flux passing through surface. In first case the flux crossing the surface is more then the flux crossing through surface in ${2^{nd}}$ case, so ${n_1} > {n_2}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(30431,'chemistry',70,1258,3073,'The transfer RNA anticodon for the messenger RNA codon $\\mathrm{G}-\\mathrm{C}-\\mathrm{A}$ is','[{"text": "${\\\\rm{C}} - {\\\\rm{G}} - {\\\\rm{U}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{G}} - {\\\\rm{C}} - {\\\\rm{U}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{U}} - {\\\\rm{G}} - {\\\\rm{C}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{G}} - {\\\\rm{U}} - {\\\\rm{C}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'The transfer RNA anticodon for the messenger RNA codon $\\mathrm{G}-\\mathrm{C}-\\mathrm{A}$ is $\\mathrm{C}-\\mathrm{G}-\\mathrm{U}$.<br>$\\because $C (cytosine) forms triple bond with G (guanine) and A (adenine) forms double bond with U (uracil)',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(30432,'physics',12,80,302,'An early model for an atom considered it to have a positively charged point nucleus of charge $Ze$, surrounded by a uniform density of negative charge upto a radius $R$. The atom  as a whole is neutral. The electric field at a distance $r$ from  the nucleus is $(r < R)$','[{"text": "$\\\\frac{{Ze}}{{4\\\\pi {\\\\varepsilon _0}}}\\\\left[ {\\\\frac{1}{{{r^2}}} - \\\\frac{r}{{{R^2}}}} \\\\right]$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{{Ze}}{{4\\\\pi {\\\\varepsilon _0}}}\\\\left[ {\\\\frac{1}{{{r^2}}} - \\\\frac{r}{{{R^3}}}} \\\\right]$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{{Ze}}{{4\\\\pi {\\\\varepsilon _0}}}\\\\left[ {\\\\frac{r}{{{R^3}}} + \\\\frac{1}{{{r^2}}}} \\\\right]$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{{Ze}}{{4\\\\pi {\\\\varepsilon _0}}}\\\\left[ {\\\\frac{r}{{{R^3}}} - \\\\frac{1}{{{r^2}}}} \\\\right]$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Charge on nucleus $ =  + Ze$<br>Total negative charge $ =  - Ze$<br>Negative charge density,<br>$\\rho  = \\frac{{{\\rm{Charge}}}}{{{\\rm{Volume}}}} = \\frac{{ - Ze}}{{\\frac{4}{3}\\pi {R^3}}}$<br>i.e., $\\rho  =  - \\frac{3}{4}\\frac{{Ze}}{{\\pi {R^3}}}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,(1)$<br>Consider a Gaussian surface with radius $r$.<br>By Gauss’s theorem <br>$\\phi  = E\\left( r \\right) \\times 4\\pi {r^2} = \\frac{q}{{{\\varepsilon _0}}}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,(2)$<br>Charge enclosed by Gaussian surface<br>$q = Ze + \\frac{{4\\pi {r^3}}}{3}\\rho  = Ze - Ze\\frac{{{r^3}}}{{{R^3}}}$<br>$E\\left( r \\right) = \\frac{q}{{4\\pi {\\varepsilon _0}{r^2}}} = \\frac{{Ze - Ze\\frac{{{r^3}}}{{{R^3}}}}}{{4\\pi {\\varepsilon _0}{r^2}}} = \\frac{{Ze}}{{4\\pi {\\varepsilon _0}}}\\left[ {\\frac{1}{{{r^2}}} - \\frac{r}{{{R^3}}}} \\right]$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/12_30432_question_1.png'),
(30447,'chemistry',70,1257,3069,'Vitamin C is<br>1247','[{"text": "Citric acid", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "lactic acid", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "paracetamol", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "ascorbic acid", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Vitamin C is chemically ascorbic acid.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(30435,'chemistry',70,1258,3071,'The base found only in the nucleotides of RNA,is','[{"text": "adenine", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "uracil", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "guanine", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "cytosine", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'nucleotide contains sugar +base+ phosphate.The bases present in RNA are adenine,guanine,uracil and cytosine.While the bases present in DNA are adenine,guanine,thymine and cytosine.Hence,uracil is the base which found only in the nucleotide of RNA.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(30436,'physics',12,80,304,'Three infinitely long charged sheets are placed as shown in figure. The electric field at point $P$ is','[{"text": "$ - \\\\frac{{2\\\\sigma }}{{{\\\\varepsilon _0}}}\\\\hat k$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{{2\\\\sigma }}{{{\\\\varepsilon _0}}}\\\\hat k$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$ - \\\\frac{{4\\\\sigma }}{{{\\\\varepsilon _0}}}\\\\hat k$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{{4\\\\sigma }}{{{\\\\varepsilon _0}}}\\\\hat k$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'The electric field due to an infinite plane sheet of charge is independent of distance of point from the sheet. Applying the principle of superposition of electric field, the total electric field at $p$ due to various plane sheets of charge will be<br>${\\overrightarrow E _P} = \\frac{\\sigma }{{2{\\varepsilon _0}}}( - \\hat k) + \\frac{{2\\sigma }}{{2{\\varepsilon _0}}}( - \\hat k) + \\frac{\\sigma }{{2{\\varepsilon _0}}}( - \\hat k)$<br>$ =  - \\frac{{2\\sigma }}{{{\\varepsilon _0}}}(\\hat k)$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/12_30436_question_1.png'),
(30439,'physics',12,80,305,'An unchanged conducting large plate is placed as shown. Now an electric field $E$ toward right is applied. Find the induced charge density on the right surface of the plate.','[{"text": "$ - {\\\\varepsilon _0}E$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\varepsilon _0}E$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$ - 2{\\\\varepsilon _0}E$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2{\\\\varepsilon _0}E$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Let $\\sigma $ and $ - \\sigma $ are the charge densities on the two surfaces. Net electric field at point $P$ should be zero. For this electric field due to induced charges is equal to the applied electric field. So<br>$\\frac{\\sigma }{{2{\\varepsilon _0}}} + \\frac{\\sigma }{{2{\\varepsilon _0}}} = E\\,or\\,\\sigma  = {\\varepsilon _0}E$<br>',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/12_30439_question_1.png'),
(30442,'chemistry',70,1258,3071,'Which of the following is not present in a nucleotide?','[{"text": "Cytosine", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Guanine", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Adenine", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Tyrosine", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Nucleotide contains nitrogenous bases like adenine, guanine, thymine, cytosine and uracil, so tyrosine is not present in nucleotide.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(30443,'physics',12,69,276,'A comb run through one’s dry hair attracts small bits of paper. This is due to','[{"text": "Comb is a good conductor", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Paper is a good conductor", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "The atoms in the paper get polarised by the charged comb", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "The comb possesses magnetic properties.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'A comb run through dry hair gets charged. The field due to the charges in the  comb, polarises the atoms in the paper and  induces opposite charges in paper. Finally the  opposite charges  attract each other and the bits of paper are attracted towards the comb.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(30446,'physics',12,70,277,'When a charge is given to a conductor','[{"text": "More charge accumulates at regions of small curvature", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "More charge accumulates at regions of large curvature", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Charge is distributed uniformly irrespective of curvature", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "None of the above is true", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Charge accumulation is more at the small regions of a conductor.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(30450,'physics',12,70,277,'A body can be negatively charged by :','[{"text": "By connecting it with negatively charged conductor", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Rubbing", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "By connecting it with earth under the influence of positively charged conductor", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "All of the above", "isCorrect": true}]',3,NULL,NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(30451,'chemistry',70,1259,3074,'Insulin, a hormone, chemically is','[{"text": "fat", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "steroid", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "protein", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "carbohydrate", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Insulin is a hormone, secreted by pancreas. It is chemically protein as it contains polypeptide bonds.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(30454,'physics',12,71,278,'A body is charged by rubbing it. Its weight','[{"text": "Always increases slightly", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "May increase slightly or may decrease slightly", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Remains precisely the same", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Always decreases slightly", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Weight may increase or decrease depending upon whether the body is charged negatively or positively.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(30456,'chemistry',70,1252,3052,'The substance with the highest calorific value is','[{"text": "milk", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "rice", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "ghee", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "egg", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Milk and egg mainly contain protein, rice is rich in carbohydrate while ghee is a fat. The order of calorific value for carbohydrates, proteins and fats is fats $>$ carbohydrates $>$ proteins<br>$\\therefore $  Ghee has the highest calorific value among the given options.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(30458,'physics',12,71,278,'A girl brings a positively charged rod near a thin neutral stream of water from a tap. She observes that the water stream bends towards her. Instead, if she brings a negatively charged rod near to the stream, it will','[{"text": "Bend in the same direction", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Bend in the opposite direction", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Not bend at all", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Bend in the opposite direction above and below  and rod", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'When a charged rod is brought nearer to stream of water, dipoles will be induced in the water (polar molecules). The dipoles will be always attracted by the charged rod. So stream bend in the same direction.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(30460,'chemistry',70,1261,3076,'Which of the following statements is not true?','[{"text": "Pheromones are secreted outside the body by the insects", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Aspirin is analgesic and antipyretic", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Sucrose is a dipeptide commonly known as aspartame", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "The DNA assists in the synthesis of RNA molecules", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Sucrose is a disaccharide having ${\\rm{\\alpha }} - {\\rm{D}}$-glucopyranose and ${\\rm{\\beta }} - {\\rm{D}}$-fructofuranose units.<br>Rest all other given statements are correct.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(30462,'physics',12,73,280,'Two identical size insulating balls $A$ and $B$ have positive charges ${q_1}$ and ${q_2}$ respectively but  ${q_1} \\ne {q_2}$.The balls are brought together so they  touch each other and then kept in their original  positions. The force between them:','[{"text": "Zero", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Remains same", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Increases", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Decreases", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Charged transfer does not take place between insulated spheres. So force remains same.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(30464,'physics',12,72,279,'If a charge on the body is 1 $nC$, then how many electrons are present on the body ?','[{"text": "${\\\\rm{6}}{\\\\rm{.25}} \\\\times {\\\\rm{1}}{{\\\\rm{0}}^{27}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1.6 \\\\times {\\\\rm{1}}{{\\\\rm{0}}^{19}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$6.25 \\\\times {\\\\rm{1}}{{\\\\rm{0}}^{28}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$6.25 \\\\times {\\\\rm{1}}{{\\\\rm{0}}^9}$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Charge on the body is $q = ne$<br>$\\therefore $ No of electrons present on the body is<br>$n = \\frac{q}{e} = \\frac{{1 \\times {{10}^{ - 9}}C}}{{1.6 \\times {{10}^{ - 19}}C}} = 6.25 \\times {10^9}$<br>',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(30466,'chemistry',70,1259,3074,'Which of the following statements is not correct?','[{"text": "Allergic conditions are cured by anti-histamines", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Hormones are continuously produced but not stored in the body", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "The function of the white blood cells is to protect the body against infections", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Catabolism involves degradation of molecules", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Hormones are not continuously produced, rather they are produced in a controlled manner according to the requirement of the body.<br>Other given statements are correct.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(30468,'physics',12,72,279,'What is the amount of charge possessed by 1 $kg$ of electrons? $({m_e} = 9.1 \\times {10^{ - 31}}kg)$','[{"text": "$1.76 \\\\times {10^{11}}C$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$6.25 \\\\times {10^{10}}C$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1.25 \\\\times {10^{10}}C$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1.76 \\\\times {10^{10}}C$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'The number of electrons $n$ is<br>$n = \\frac{M}{{{m_e}}} = \\frac{{1kg}}{{9.1 \\times {{10}^{ - 31}}kg}} = 1.1 \\times {10^{30}}$<br>Therefore, charge on 1 kg of electrons,<br>$q = ne = 1.1 \\times {10^{30}} \\times 1.6 \\times {10^{ - 19}}$<br>$ = 1.76 \\times {10^{11}}C$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(30471,'chemistry',70,1259,3074,'The hormone that helps in the conversion of glucose to glycogen is','[{"text": "cortisone", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "bile acids", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "adrenaline", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "insulin", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Insulin hormone helps in the conversion of glucose into glycogen in the liver and skeletal muscle. Insulin is secreted by pancreas that lower blood glucose level.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(30472,'physics',12,72,279,'How many electrons should be removed from a coin of mass $1.6g,$ so that it may float in an electric field of intensity ${10^9}N{C^{ - 1}}$ directed upward? (Take $g = 10m/{s^2}$)','[{"text": "${10^7}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${10^6}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${10^8}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${10^9}$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Let $n$ be the number of electrons  removed from the coin.<br>Then, charge on coin, $q =  + ne$<br>Now,  $qE = mg$<br>or $\\left( {ne} \\right)E = mg$<br>or $n = \\frac{{mg}}{{eE}} = \\frac{{1.6 \\times {{10}^{ - 3}} \\times 10}}{{1.6 \\times {{10}^{ - 19}} \\times {{10}^9}}} = {10^8}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(30475,'chemistry',70,1257,3069,'Scurvy is caused by the deficiency of which vitamin?','[{"text": "${\\\\rm{Vitamin}}\\\\,{\\\\rm{K}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{Vitamin}}\\\\,{{\\\\rm{B}}_{\\\\rm{1}}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Vitamin C", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{Vitamin }}{{\\\\rm{B}}_{\\\\rm{6}}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Scurvy is caused by the deficiency of vitamin C.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(30476,'physics',12,72,279,'Assume that each of copper atoms has one free electron. Estimate the number of free electrons in 1 $mg$ of copper. Given that atomic weight of  copper $ = 63.5g$ and Avogadro number is  $6.023 \\times {10^{23}}$.What is the charge possessed by these free electrons?','[{"text": "$1.76\\\\,C$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1.52\\\\,C$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$1.25\\\\,C$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$4.76\\\\,C$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Number of atoms in 1 $g$ of copper,<br>$N = \\frac{{{\\rm{Avogadro number}}}}{{{\\rm{atomic weight}}}} = \\frac{{6.02 \\times {{10}^{23}}}}{{63.5}}$<br>Therefore, number of atoms i.e., number of free electrons in 1 mg of copper,<br>$N\' = 9.48 \\times {10^{21}} \\times {10^{ - 3}} = 9.48 \\times {10^{18}}$<br>Charge possessed by these free electrons,<br>$q = N\'e = 9.48 \\times {10^{18}} \\times 1.6 \\times {10^{ - 19}} = 1.517\\,{\\rm{C}}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(30479,'physics',12,74,286,'If an electron has an initial velocity in a direction different from that of an electric field, the path of the electron is','[{"text": "A circle", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "A straight line", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "A parabola", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "An ellipse", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'The path is a parabola, because initial velocity can be resolved into two rectangular components, one along $\\overrightarrow E $ and other $ \\bot $ to $\\overrightarrow E $',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(31324,'physics',12,69,276,'One million electrons are added to a glass rod. The total charge on the rod is','[{"text": "${10^{ - 13}}\\\\,C$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$ - 1.6 \\\\times {10^{ - 13}}\\\\,C$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$ + 1.6 \\\\times {10^{ - 12}}\\\\,C$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${10^{ - 12}}\\\\,C$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'$Q=-n e, n=10^{6}$<br>$e=1.6 \\times 10^{-13}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(30480,'chemistry',70,1257,3069,'Which of the following statements about vitamin<br> B-12 is incorrect?','[{"text": "It has a cobalt atom.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "It also occurs in plants.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "It is also present in rain water.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "It is needed for human body in very small amounts.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Vitamin ${{\\rm{B}}_{{\\rm{12}}}}$ does not occur in plants.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
