(30485,'chemistry',70,1259,3074,'The hormone which controls the processes of  burning of fats, proteins and carbohydrates to liberate energy in the body is :','[{"text": "Cortisone", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Adrenaline", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Thyroxine", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Insulin", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Insulin',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(30487,'physics',12,73,282,'Two identical simple pendulums, $A$ and $B$, are suspended from the same point. The bobs are given positive charges, with $A$ having more charge than $B$. they diverge and reach  equilibrium, with $A$ and $B$ making angles ${\\theta _1}$ and ${\\theta _2}$  with the vertical respectively. Which of the following is correct?','[{"text": "${\\\\theta _1} < {\\\\theta _2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\theta _1} = {\\\\theta _2}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${\\\\theta _1} > {\\\\theta _2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "The tension in $A$ is greater than that in $B$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Coulomb forces between unequal  charges are equal. As both have same masses then ${\\theta _1} = {\\theta _2}$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(30489,'chemistry',70,1257,3068,'The water soluble vitamin is','[{"text": "$\\\\mathrm{B}_{1}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{K}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{E}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{D}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'${{\\rm{B}}_{\\rm{1}}}$ is water soluble vitamin.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(30491,'physics',12,73,280,'Two spheres carrying charges $ + 6\\,\\mu C$ and $ + 9\\,\\mu C$, separated by a distance $d$, experience a force of repulsion $F$. When a charge of $ - 3\\,\\mu C$ is given to both the spheres and kept at the same distance as before, the new force of repulsion is','[{"text": "$3F$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$F/9$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$F$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$F/3$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'<img src=\'/img/data/physics/12_30491_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' /> Let us call spheres as $A$ and $B$.<br>Accroding to Coulomb\'s law, the force of repulsion between $A$ and $B$ separated by a distance $d$ is<br>$F = \\frac{1}{{4\\pi {\\varepsilon _0}}}\\frac{{( + 6\\mu C)( + 9\\mu C)}}{{{d^2}}}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{\\rm{(1)}}$<br>When a charge of $ - 3\\,\\mu C$ is given to both the spheres, then charge on $A = &nbsp;+ 6\\,\\mu C - 3\\,\\mu C = &nbsp;+ 3\\,\\mu C$ and on $B = &nbsp;+ 9\\,\\mu C - 3\\,\\mu C = 6\\,\\mu C$<br>Again by Columbo\'s law, then new force of repulsion between $A$ and $B$ separated by the same distance is<br>$F\' = \\frac{1}{{4\\pi {\\varepsilon _0}}}\\frac{{{\\rm{(}} + 3\\mu C{\\rm{)(}} + 6\\mu C{\\rm{)}}}}{{{d^2}}}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{\\rm{(2)}}$<br>Dividing equ (2) eqn (1), we get<br>$\\frac{{F\'}}{F} = \\frac{{\\frac{1}{{4\\pi {\\varepsilon _0}}}\\frac{{{\\rm{(}} + 3\\mu C{\\rm{)(}} + 6\\mu C{\\rm{)}}}}{{{d^2}}}}}{{\\frac{1}{{4\\pi {\\varepsilon _0}}}\\frac{{{\\rm{(}} + 6\\mu C{\\rm{)(}} + 9\\mu C{\\rm{)}}}}{{{d^2}}}}}$<br>$ = \\frac{{{\\rm{(}} + 3\\mu C{\\rm{)(}} + 6\\mu C{\\rm{)}}}}{{{\\rm{(}} + 6\\mu C{\\rm{)(}} + 9\\mu C{\\rm{)}}}} = \\frac{1}{3}$ or $F\' = \\frac{F}{3}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(30564,'physics',12,74,286,'The magnitude of electric field force experienced by a charged particle in an electric field depends on','[{"text": "Charge of the particle", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Mass of the particle", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Velocity of the particle", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Direction of the electric field", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Force on a charge is $F = Eq$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(30494,'chemistry',70,1259,3074,'Which of the following hormones contain iodine?','[{"text": "Thyroxine", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Progesterone", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Adrenaline", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Estron", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Thyroxine contains iodine.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(30495,'physics',12,73,280,'The force of repulsion between two identical positive charges when kept with a separation $r$ in air is $F$. Half the gap between the two charges is filled by a dielectic slab of dielectric constant $ = 4$.Then the new force of repulsion between those two charges becomes','[{"text": "$\\\\frac{F}{3}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{F}{2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{F}{4}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{{4F}}{9}$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Let $q$ be each charge,<br>According to Coloumb\'s law, the force of  repulsion between the two charges when kept  in air is<br>$F = \\frac{1}{{4\\pi {\\varepsilon _0}}}\\frac{{{q^2}}}{{{r^2}}}$<br>When half of the gap between the two charges is filled with the dielectric slab of dielectric  constant $(K = 4)$,the new force of repulsion between them becomes<br>$F\' = \\frac{1}{{4\\pi {\\varepsilon _0}}}\\frac{{{q^2}}}{{((r - t) + t\\sqrt {K{)^2}} }}$<br>(where $t$ is the thickness of the slab)<br>$ = \\frac{1}{{4\\pi {\\varepsilon _0}}}\\frac{{{q^2}}}{{{{\\left[ {\\left( {r - \\frac{r}{2}} \\right) + \\frac{r}{2}\\sqrt 4 } \\right]}^2}}}$<br>$\\left( {as\\,t = \\frac{r}{2},K = 4} \\right)$<br>$ = \\frac{1}{{4\\pi {\\varepsilon _0}}}\\frac{{{q^2}}}{{{{\\left( {\\frac{r}{2} + r} \\right)}^2}}} = \\frac{1}{{4\\pi {\\varepsilon _0}}}\\frac{{{q^2}}}{{{{\\left( {\\frac{3}{2}r} \\right)}^2}}}$<br>$ = \\frac{4}{9}\\left( {\\frac{1}{{4\\pi {\\varepsilon _0}}}\\frac{{{q^2}}}{{{r^2}}}} \\right) = \\frac{{4F}}{9}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2018,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(30498,'physics',12,73,280,'Two point charges placed at a distance $r$ in air experience a certain force. Then the distance at which they will experience the same force in a medium of dielectric constant $K$ is','[{"text": "$Kr$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$r/K$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$r + \\\\sqrt K $", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{r}{{\\\\sqrt K }}$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'As the forces are equal<br>$\\frac{{{q_1}{q_2}}}{{{r^2}}} = \\frac{{{q_1}{q_2}}}{{K{d^2}}}$ or $K\\,{d^2} = {r^2}$ or $d = \\frac{r}{{\\sqrt K }}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(30499,'chemistry',70,1257,3069,'Deficiency of vitamin E causes :','[{"text": "Loss of fertility", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Impaired clotting", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Scurvy", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Night blindness", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Loss of fertility is caused by vitamin E.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(30502,'physics',12,73,280,'Two identical charges repel each other with a force equal to $10\\,g\\,wt$ when they are $0.9\\,m$ apart in air.$(g = 10m{s^2})$.The value of each  charge is','[{"text": "$2\\\\,nC$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2 \\\\times {10^{ - 7}}C$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2\\\\,mC$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$3\\\\mu C$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'$F = \\frac{1}{{4\\pi {\\varepsilon _0}}}\\frac{{{q^2}}}{{{d^2}}}$<br>$\\therefore (10 \\times {10^{ - 3}}) \\times 10 = \\frac{{(9 \\times {{10}^9}) \\times {q^2}}}{{{{(0.9)}^2}}}$<br>$ \\Rightarrow {q^2} = 9 \\times {10^{ - 12}}$<br>$ \\Rightarrow q = 3 \\times {10^{ - 6}}C = 3\\mu C$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(30565,'chemistry',70,1250,3047,'Raffinose on hydrolysis gives','[{"text": "glucose", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "galactose", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "fructose", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "all of these.", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Raffinose is a trisaccharide which is composed of galactose, glucose and fructose and on hydrolysis it gives all these molecules.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(30504,'chemistry',70,1254,3059,'In peptide formation, $-\\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ group of one molecule condenses with','[{"text": "$-\\\\mathrm{COOH}$ group of same molecule", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$-\\\\mathrm{COOH}$ group of another molecule", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$-\\\\mathrm{COCl}$ group of another molecule", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$-\\\\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ group of another molecule.", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'${\\rm{\\alpha  - }}$amino acids combines as  <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/70_30504_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />  Hence, $-\\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ group of one molecule condenses with $-\\mathrm{COOH}$ group of another molecule.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(30506,'physics',12,73,280,'Two copper balls, each weighing 10 $g$ are kept in air 10 $cm$ apart. If one electron from every ${10^6}$ atoms is transferred from one ball to the  other, the coulomb force between them is  (atomic weight of copper is $63.5g$)','[{"text": "$2.0 \\\\times {10^6}N$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2.0 \\\\times {10^4}N$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2.0 \\\\times {10^{10}}N$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2.0 \\\\times {10^8}N$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Number of atoms in given mass <br>$ = \\frac{{10}}{{63.5}} \\times 6.02 \\times {10^{23}} = 9.48 \\times {10^{22}}$<br>Transfer of electrons between balls <br>$ = \\frac{{9.48 \\times {{10}^{22}}}}{{{{10}^6}}}$<br>$ = 9.48 \\times {10^{16}}$<br>Hence magnitude of charge gained by each ball.<br>$q = 9.48 \\times {10^{16}} \\times 1.6 \\times {10^{ - 19}} = 0.015\\,C$<br>Force of attraction between the balls<br>$F = 9 \\times {10^9} \\times \\frac{{{{\\left( {0.015} \\right)}^2}}}{{{{\\left( {0.1} \\right)}^2}}} = 2 \\times {10^8}N.$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(30509,'chemistry',70,1248,3039,'When a monosaccharide forms a cyclic hemiacetal, the carbon atom that contained the carbonyl group is identified as the ____ carbon atom, because','[{"text": "D, the carbonyl group is drawn to the right", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "L,  the carbonyl group is drawn to the left", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "acetal, it forms bond to an $-\\\\mathrm{OR}$ and an $-\\\\mathrm{OR}$ \'", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "anomeric, its substituents can assume an ${\\\\rm{\\\\alpha }}$ or ${\\\\rm{\\\\beta }} - $ position.", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'A pair of stereoisomers which differ in configuration only around ${\\text{C}} - 1$ are called anomers and the $\\mathrm{C}-1$ carbon is called the anomeric carbon.<br> <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/70_30509_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(30510,'physics',12,73,282,'Two particles of equal mass $m$ and charge $q$ are placed at a distance of 16 $cm$. They do not experience any force. The value of $\\frac{q}{m}$ is','[{"text": "$\\\\sqrt {\\\\frac{{\\\\pi {\\\\varepsilon _0}}}{G}} $", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\sqrt {\\\\frac{G}{{4\\\\pi {\\\\varepsilon _0}}}} $", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\sqrt {\\\\pi {\\\\varepsilon _0}G} $", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\sqrt {4\\\\pi {\\\\varepsilon _0}G} $", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'They will not experience any force if gravitational and electrostatic force are  equal. $\\left| {\\overrightarrow {{F_g}} } \\right| = \\left| {\\overrightarrow {{F_e}} } \\right|$ <br>$ \\Rightarrow G\\frac{{{m^2}}}{{{{(16 \\times {{10}^{ - 2}})}^2}}} = \\frac{1}{{4\\pi {\\varepsilon _0}}} \\cdot \\frac{{{q^2}}}{{{{(16 \\times {{10}^{ - 2}})}^2}}}$<br>$ \\Rightarrow \\frac{q}{m} = \\sqrt {4\\pi {\\varepsilon _0}G} $',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(31207,'physics',18,124,446,'While viewing a distant object with telescope a housefly sits on objective lens. Then','[{"text": "Housefly will be seen enlarged in image.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Housefly will be seen reduced in image.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Intensity of image will be decreased.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Intensity of image will be increased.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,NULL,NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(30513,'physics',12,73,280,'Two spheres of electric charges $ + 2\\,nC$ and $ - 8\\,nC$ are placed at a distance $d$ apart. If they are  allowed to touch each other, what is the new  distance between them to get a repulsive force  of same magnitude as before?','[{"text": "$\\\\frac{d}{2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$d$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{{3d}}{4}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{{4d}}{3}$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Force on each sphere<br>$ = \\frac{{k{q_1}{q_1}}}{2} = \\frac{{ - 16k}}{{{d^2}}} \\times {10^{ - 18}}N$<br>When both the spheres are allowed to touch each other, the charges get redistributed.<br>Charge on each sphere $ = \\frac{{(2 - 8)}}{2}nC =  - 3nC$<br>Force on each sphere<br> $ = \\frac{{k{{( - 3nC)}^2}}}{{{r^2}}} = \\frac{{9k}}{{{r^2}}} \\times {10^{ - 18}}N$<br>According to question,<br>$\\frac{{16k}}{{{d^2}}} \\times {10^{ - 18}} = \\frac{{9k}}{{{r^2}}} \\times {10^{ - 18}};r = \\frac{3}{4}d$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(30514,'chemistry',70,1253,3053,'Which of the following is/are ${\\rm{\\alpha }} - $ amino acid?','[{"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both (1) and (2)", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "None of these.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'${\\rm{\\alpha  - }}$amino acids a basic amino group $\\left( { - {\\text{N}}{{\\text{H}}_2}} \\right)$ on the ${\\rm{\\alpha  - }}$carbon to the carboxyl group.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(30517,'physics',12,73,280,'Two point charges are 3 $m$ apart and their combined charge is $8\\,\\mu C$.The force of repulsion between them is $0.012\\,N$, Charges  are','[{"text": "$6\\\\,\\\\mu C,{\\\\rm{ }}2\\\\,\\\\mu C$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$4\\\\,\\\\mu C,{\\\\rm{ }}4\\\\,\\\\mu C$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$7\\\\,\\\\mu C,{\\\\rm{ }}1\\\\,\\\\mu C$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$5\\\\,\\\\mu C,{\\\\rm{ }}3\\\\,\\\\mu C$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Let ${q_1} = q\\,\\mu {\\rm{C}}$ and ${q_2} = \\left( {8 - q} \\right)\\mu C$<br>$r = 3\\,m,{\\rm{ }}F{\\rm{ }} = {\\rm{ }}0.012{\\rm{ }}N$<br>According to Coulomb’s law<br>$F = \\frac{{{q_1}{{\\rm{q}}_2}}}{{4\\pi {\\varepsilon _0}{r^2}}}$<br>$0.012 = \\frac{{9 \\times {{10}^9}q\\left( {8 - q} \\right) \\times {{10}^{ - 12}}}}{{{3^2}}}$<br>$0.012 = q\\left( {8 - q} \\right){10^{ - 3}}$<br>or  ${q^2} - 8q + 12 = 0$<br>Solving the quadratic equation, we get <br>$q = 6$ or 2<br>Hence, the two charges are $6\\,\\mu C,{\\rm{ }}2\\,\\mu C$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(30518,'chemistry',70,1254,3061,'The secondary structure of a protein refers to','[{"text": "${\\\\rm{\\\\alpha }} - $ helicalbackbone", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "hydrophobic interactions", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "sequence of ${\\\\rm{\\\\alpha }} - $ amino acids", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "fixed configuration of the polypeptide backbone.<br>", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Primary structure involves sequence of ${\\rm{\\alpha  - }}$amino acids in a polypeptide chain. Secondary structure involves ${\\rm{\\alpha  - }}$helical and $\\beta  - $pleated sheet like structure.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(30521,'physics',12,73,282,'A charge $q$ is placed at the centre of the line  joining two equal charges $Q$. The system of three charges will be in equilibrium if $q$ is equal to','[{"text": "$ - \\\\frac{Q}{2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$ - \\\\frac{Q}{4}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$ + \\\\frac{Q}{4}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{Q}{2}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'The central charge is in the equilibrium. If the system has to be in equilibrium then the net force on any of the charge $Q$ should be zero.<br>$\\frac{1}{{4\\pi {\\varepsilon _0}}}\\frac{{QQ}}{{{l^2}}} + \\frac{1}{{4\\pi {\\varepsilon _0}}}\\frac{{qQ}}{{{{\\left( {\\frac{l}{2}} \\right)}^2}}} = 0$<br>$ \\Rightarrow \\,q = \\frac{{ - Q}}{4}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(30522,'chemistry',70,1255,3066,'Denaturation does not involve','[{"text": "breaking up of H -bonding in proteins", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "the loss of biological action of enzyme", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "the loss of secondary structure", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "the loss of primary structure.", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'When a protein in its native form, is subjected to a physical change like change in temperature, or a chemical change like change in pH , the native conformation of the molecule is disrupted. This is called as denaturation which leads to breaking up of hydrogen bonding, loss in biological action of enzyme and loss of secondary and tertiary structures but the primary structure remains intact.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(30525,'physics',12,73,282,'Two charges $ + 4e$ and $ + e$ are at a distance  $x$ apart. At what distance, a charge  $q$ must be placed from charge $ + e$ so that it is in equilibrium','[{"text": "$x/2$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2x/3$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$x/6$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$x/3$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'For equilibrium of the charge $q$<br>$\\frac{{q\\left( e \\right)}}{{4\\pi {\\varepsilon _0}{d^2}}} = \\frac{{q\\left( {4e} \\right)}}{{4\\pi {\\varepsilon _0}{{\\left( {x - d} \\right)}^2}}}$<br>where $d$ is the distance of $q$ from the charge $e$.<br>$ \\Rightarrow d = \\frac{x}{3}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(30527,'chemistry',70,1258,3072,'X-ray studies show that there are approximately ____ amino acids for each turn in the helix.','[{"text": "1.6", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "4.6", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "3.6", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "2.6", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'In ${\\rm{\\alpha }}$-helix, each turn has approximately 3.6 amino acids.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(30529,'physics',12,74,284,'Two particles $A$ and $B$ having equal charges are placed at a distance $d$ apart. A third charged particle placed on the perpendicular bisect or of $AB$ at distance $x$. The third particle experiences maximum force when','[{"text": "$x = \\\\frac{d}{2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$x = \\\\frac{d}{{\\\\sqrt 2 }}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$x = \\\\frac{d}{{3\\\\sqrt 2 }}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$x = \\\\frac{d}{{2\\\\sqrt 2 }}$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Let $q$ is the charge of both charges<br>The resultant force on the charge ${q_0}$ is<br>${F_y} = 2F\\cos \\theta $<br>$ = \\frac{{2q{q_0}}}{{4\\pi {\\varepsilon _0}\\left( {{x^2} + \\frac{{{d^2}}}{4}} \\right)}}\\left( {\\frac{x}{{\\sqrt {{x^2} + \\frac{{{d^2}}}{4}} }}} \\right)$<br>$ = \\frac{{2q{q_0}}}{{4\\pi {\\varepsilon _0}}}\\frac{x}{{{{\\left( {{x^2} + \\frac{{{d^2}}}{4}} \\right)}^{3/2}}}}$<br>When ${F_y}$ is maximum<br>$\\frac{{d{F_y}}}{{dx}} = 0$<br>$\\frac{{d{F_y}}}{{dx}} = \\frac{{2q{q_0}}}{{4\\pi {\\varepsilon _0}}}\\left[ {\\frac{{{{\\left( {{x^2} + \\frac{{{d^2}}}{4}} \\right)}^{3/2}}\\frac{{d(x)}}{{dx}} - x\\frac{d}{{dx}}{{\\left( {{x^2} + \\frac{{{d^2}}}{4}} \\right)}^{3/2}}}}{{{{\\left( {{x^2} + \\frac{{{d^2}}}{4}} \\right)}^3}}}} \\right]$<br>As  $\\frac{{d{F_y}}}{{dx}} = 0$<br>${\\left( {{x^2} + \\frac{{{d^2}}}{4}} \\right)^{3/2}} - x\\left( {\\frac{3}{2}} \\right){\\left( {{x^2} + \\frac{{{d^2}}}{4}} \\right)^{\\frac{1}{2}}}(2x) = 0$<br>$ \\Rightarrow x = \\frac{d}{{2\\sqrt 2 }}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(30532,'physics',12,78,297,'$4 \\times {10^{10}}$ electrons are removed from a neutral metal sphere of diameter $20\\,cm$ placed in air. The magnitude of the electric field (in $N{C^{ - 1}}$) at a distance of 20 $cm$ from its centre is','[{"text": "${\\\\rm{zero}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{5760}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{640}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{1440}}$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Number of electrons removed $ = 4 \\times {10^{10}}$<br>Charge on this sphere $ = 4 \\times {10^{10}} \\times 1.6 \\times {10^{ - 19}}C$<br>$ = 6.4 \\times {10^{ - 19}}C = 6.4\\,nC$<br>Diameter of Sphere,$D = 20\\,cm = 0.2\\,m$<br>Distance from the centre,$d = 20\\,cm = 0.2\\,m$<br>Electric field at point p, $E = \\frac{q}{{4\\pi {\\varepsilon _0}{d^2}}}$<br>$\\left( {\\frac{{9 \\times {{10}^9} \\times 6.4 \\times {{10}^{ - 9}}}}{{{{(0.2)}^2}}}} \\right)N{C^{ - 1}} = 1440\\,N{C^{ - 1}}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(30533,'chemistry',70,1258,3072,'To which of the following does thymine form hydrogen bonds in DNA?','[{"text": "Adenine", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Thymine", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Cytosine", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Guanine", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'In double $\\alpha$-helix structure of DNA,<br> G (guanine) pairs with C (cytosine) through three hydrogen bonds $(i.e.,{\\text{ }}({\\text{C}} \\equiv {\\text{G)}}$  and A (adenine) pairs with T (thymine) through two hydrogen bonds (i.e., A = T).',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(30536,'physics',12,74,283,'The magnitude of point charge due to which the electric field $30\\,cm$ away has the magnitude $2\\,N/C$ will be','[{"text": "$2 \\\\times {10^{ - 11}}C$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$3 \\\\times {10^{ - 11}}C$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$5 \\\\times {10^{ - 11}}C$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$9 \\\\times {10^{ - 11}}C$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'The magnitude of electric field due to a point &nbsp;charge q at a distance r from the charge is<br>$E = \\frac{1}{{4\\pi {\\varepsilon _0}}}\\frac{q}{{{r^2}}}$<br>Here $E = 2\\,N{C^{ - 1}},r = 30\\,cm = 0.3\\,m,$<br>$\\frac{1}{{4\\pi {\\varepsilon _0}}} = 9 \\times {10^9}N{m^2}{C^{ - 2}},q = ?$<br>$\\therefore 2 = \\frac{{(9 \\times {{10}^9})q}}{{{{(0.3{\\mkern 1mu} )}^2}}}$<br>$ \\Rightarrow {\\mkern 1mu} q = \\frac{{(2)(0.09)}}{{(9 \\times {{10}^9})}}$<br>$ \\Rightarrow {\\mkern 1mu} q = \\frac{{2 \\times 9}}{{9 \\times {{10}^9} \\times 100}} = 2 \\times {10^{ - 11}}C$<br>Thus, the magnitude of point charge will be<br>$2 \\times {10^{ - 11}}C$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(30537,'chemistry',70,1246,3029,'All monosaccharides ____ Tollens\' reagent.','[{"text": "oxidise", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "condense with", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "reduce", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "add to", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Due to presence of  $ - {\\rm{CHO}}$  group all monosaccharides and disaccharides except sucrose reduce Tollens\' reagent to shining silver mirror. <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/70_30537_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(30539,'physics',12,78,295,'A thin conducting ring of radius $R$ is given a charge + $Q$. The electric field at the centre $O$ of the ring due to the charge on the part $AKB$ of the ring is $E$. The electric field at the centre due to the charge on the part $ACD$ of the ring is','[{"text": "$\\\\sqrt 2 E\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\rm{along}}\\\\,\\\\,OB$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\sqrt 2 E\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\rm{along}}\\\\,\\\\,KO$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2E\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\rm{along}}\\\\,\\\\,OK$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\sqrt 3 E\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\rm{along}}\\\\,\\\\,KO$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'According to symmetry<br>${E_{AKB}} = {E_{ALC}} + {E_{CMD}} = E$<br>$ \\Rightarrow {E_{ACD}} = \\sqrt 2 E$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/12_30539_question_1.png'),
(30541,'chemistry',70,1247,3033,'The number of chiral centers in ( $ \\pm $ )${\\rm{  }} - {\\rm{glucose}}$ is','[{"text": "4", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "3", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "2", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "1", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'There are four chiral centres present in ($\\pm$)$ - $glucose. <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/70_30541_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(30542,'physics',12,78,297,'A spherical conductor of radius 2 $cm$ is uniformly charged with 3 $nC$. What is the electric field at a distance of 3 $cm$ from the centre of the sphere?','[{"text": "$3 \\\\times {10^4}V{m^{ - 1}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$3 \\\\times {10^6}V{m^{ - 1}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$3 \\\\times {10^{ - 4}}V{m^{ - 1}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$3\\\\,V{m^{ - 1}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Here,$Q = 3\\,n\\,C = 3 \\times {10^{ - 9}}C$<br>$R = 2\\,cm = 2 \\times {10^{ - 2}}m$<br>At a  point 3 $cm$ from the centre,<br>i.e.,$r = 3\\,cm = 3 \\times {10^{ - 2}}m$<br>$\\therefore $ Electric field,<br>$E = \\frac{1}{{4\\pi {\\varepsilon _0}}}\\frac{Q}{{{r^2}}} = \\frac{{9 \\times {{10}^9} \\times 3 \\times {{10}^{ - 9}}}}{{{{(3 \\times {{10}^{ - 2}})}^2}}}$<br>$ = 3 \\times {10^4}V{m^{ - 1}}$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(30546,'physics',12,74,284,'Two charges $Q$ and $ - 3Q$ are placed at some distance apart. If the electric field at the location of $Q$ is $E$ then at the locality of $ - 3Q$, it  is','[{"text": "$E/3$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$2E$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$4E/3$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$3E$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'The field produced by charge $ - 3Q\\,{\\rm{at}}\\,Q$ is $E$<br>$\\therefore E = \\frac{{3Q}}{{{x^2}}}$<br>Now field at location of  $ - 3Q$ due to charge $Q$  will be $\\therefore E\' = \\frac{Q}{{{x^2}}} = \\frac{E}{3}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(30549,'physics',12,77,289,'Two charges $ \\pm 20\\,\\mu C$ are placed 10 $mm$ apart. The electric field at point $P$, On the axis of the dipole 10 $cm$ away from its centre $O$ on the side of the positive charge is','[{"text": "$4.1 \\\\times {10^6}\\\\,N{C^{ - 1}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$8.6 \\\\times {10^9}\\\\,N{C^{ - 1}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$4.6 \\\\times {10^5}\\\\,N{C^{ - 1}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$3.6 \\\\times {10^6}\\\\,N{C^{ - 1}}$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Here,$q = \\pm 20\\mu C = \\pm 20 \\times {10^{ - 6}}\\,C$<br>$2a = 10\\,mm = 10 \\times {10^{ - 3}}\\,m$<br>$r = OP = 10\\,cm = 10 \\times {10^{ - 2}}\\,m$<br>$\\left| {\\overrightarrow p } \\right| = q \\times 2a = 20 \\times {10^{ - 6}} \\times 10 \\times {10^{ - 3}} = 2 \\times {10^{ - 7}}\\,m$<br>The electric field along $BP$, <br>$E = \\frac{{2pr}}{{4\\pi {\\varepsilon _0}{{\\left( {{r^2} - {a^2}} \\right)}^2}}}$<br>As $a &lt; &lt; r,$<br>$ = 3.6 \\times {10^6}\\,N\\,{C^{ - 1}}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/12_30549_question_1.png'),
(30552,'chemistry',70,1250,3047,'Among the following statements :<br>(i) Sucrose is a non-reducing sugar.<br>(ii) Glucose is oxidised by bromine water.<br>(iii) Glucose is dextrorotatory.<br>(iv) Fructose is an aldohexose.<br>True statements are','[{"text": "(i) and (ii)", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "(i), (ii) and (iii)", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "(ii) and (iii)", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "(ii), (iii) and (iv)", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Glucose is known as dextrose because it occurs in nature as the optically active dextrorotatory isomer. Glucose is oxidised to gluconic acid with bromine water.<br>$\\begin{gathered}\n  {\\text{C}}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{2}}}{\\text{OH(CHOH}}{{\\text{)}}_{\\text{4}}}{\\text{CHO}} + {\\text{[O}}]\\xrightarrow[{{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{2}}}{\\text{O}}}]{{{\\text{B}}{{\\text{r}}_{\\text{2}}}}} \\hfill \\\\\n  \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{\\text{Glucose}} \\hfill \\\\\n  \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{\\text{C}}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{2}}}{\\text{OH(CHOH}}{{\\text{)}}_{\\text{4}}}{\\text{COOH}}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu}  \\hfill \\\\\n  \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\text{Gluconic}}\\,{\\mkern 1mu} {\\text{acid}} \\hfill \\\\ \n\\end{gathered} $<br>Sucrose is a non reducing sugar (does not reduce Fehling\'s solution and Tollens\' reagent) because in this disaccharides, two monosaccharide units are linked through their reducing centres i.e., aldehydic or ketonic group.<br>Fructose is a ketohexose.  <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/70_30552_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />',NULL,'Medium','MCQ type-2',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(30553,'physics',12,74,284,'In the following four situations charged particles are at equal distance from the origin. Arrange them the magnitude of the net electric field at origin greatest first &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;','[{"text": "(ii) &gt; (i) &gt; (iii) &gt; (iv)", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "(i) &gt; (ii) &gt; (iii) &gt; (iv)", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "(iv) &gt; (iii) &gt; (ii) &gt; (i)", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "(i) &gt; (iii) &gt; (ii) &gt; (iv)", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'If electric field due to charge $\\left| q \\right|$ at origin is $E$ then electric field due to charges $\\left| {2q} \\right|,\\left| {3q} \\right|,\\left| {4q} \\right|{\\rm{and}}\\left| {5q} \\right|$ are respectively $2E,3E,4E\\,{\\rm{and}}\\,5E$<br>${E_{\\left( i \\right)}} = \\sqrt {{{\\left( {5E} \\right)}^2} + {{\\left( {5E} \\right)}^2}} = 5\\sqrt 2 E\\,,$<br>${E_{\\left( {ii} \\right)}} = \\sqrt {{{\\left( {3E} \\right)}^2} + {{\\left( {3E} \\right)}^2}} = 3\\sqrt 2 E\\,$,<br>${E_{\\left( {iii} \\right)}} = 4E + 2E = 6E$ and <br>${E_{\\left( {iv} \\right)}} = 3E + E = 4E$<br>$ \\Rightarrow {E_{\\left( i \\right)}} &gt; {E_{\\left( {iii} \\right)}} &gt; {E_{\\left( {ii} \\right)}} &gt; {E_{\\left( {iv} \\right)}}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/12_30553_question_1.png'),
(30556,'chemistry',70,1248,3040,'Find the correct option listed below:<br><br><strong>Assertion:</strong> Fructose reacts with excess of HI in the presence of red phosphorus to give n-Hexane.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> A molecule of fructose has six carbon atoms in the form of unbranched chain.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Fructose reacts with excess of HI in the presence of red phosphorus at 373 K to give n-hexane. HI and red phosphorus is a strong reducing agent which reduces ketonic and alcoholic groups of fructose to $-\\mathrm{CH}_{2}$ group.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(30557,'physics',12,78,295,'A ring of radius $r$ carries a charge $Q$ uniformly distributed over its length. A charge $q$ is placed at its centre will experience a force equal to','[{"text": "$\\\\frac{{qQ}}{{8\\\\pi {\\\\varepsilon _0}{r^3}}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{{qQ}}{{4\\\\pi {\\\\varepsilon _0}{r^2}}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Zero", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "None of these", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'The electric field ($E$) at the centre of  circular charged ring of radius $R$ is zero.<br>$\\therefore $ $Force = qE = Zero$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(30560,'chemistry',70,1252,3052,'Sugars are','[{"text": "sweet", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "crystalline solids", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "soluble in water", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "all of these.", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Sugars are crystalline solids which are sweet in taste and are soluble in water.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(30561,'physics',12,74,286,'An electron enters in an electric field with its  velocity in the direction of the electric lines of force. Then','[{"text": "The velocity of the electron will increase", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "The velocity of the electron will decrease", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "The path of the electron will be a parabola", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "The path of the electron will be a circle", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Electric field applies the force on electron in the direction opposite to its motion.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(30568,'physics',12,74,286,'A pendulum bob of mass $m$ carrying a charge $q$ is at rest with its string making an angle $\\theta $ with the vertical in a uniform horizontal  electric field $E$. The tension in the string is :','[{"text": "$\\\\frac{{mg}}{{\\\\cos \\\\theta }}\\\\,{\\\\rm{and}}\\\\,\\\\frac{{qE}}{{\\\\sin \\\\theta }}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{{mg}}{{\\\\sin \\\\theta }}{\\\\rm{and}}\\\\,\\\\frac{{qE}}{{\\\\cos \\\\theta }}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{{mg}}{{qE}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{{qE}}{{mg}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'For the equilibrium of the bob,<br>$T\\,\\sin \\theta  = qE\\,{\\rm{and}}\\,T\\,\\cos \\theta  = mg$<br>$T = \\frac{{mg}}{{\\cos \\theta }}\\,{\\rm{and}}\\,T = \\frac{{qE}}{{\\sin \\theta }}$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(30570,'chemistry',70,1247,3032,'The IUPAC name of glucose is','[{"text": "1, 2, 3, 4, 5-pentahydroxy hexanal", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "1, 3, 4, 5, 6-pentahydroxy - 2 - hexanal", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "2, 3, 4, 5, 6-pentahydroxyhexanal", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "1, 2, 3, 4, 5-pentahydroxy hexanone", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'<img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/70_30570_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />  IUPAC name:2,3,4,5,6-Pentahydroxyhexanal',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(30572,'physics',12,74,286,'An electron initially at rest falls a distance of   1.5 $cm$ in a uniform electric field of  magnitude $2 \\times {10^4}N\\,{C^{ - 1}}$. The time taken by the  electron to fall this distance is','[{"text": "$2.1 \\\\times {10^{ - 12}}\\\\,s$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1.3 \\\\times {10^2}\\\\,s$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2.9 \\\\times {10^{ - 9}}\\\\,s$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$1.6 \\\\times {10^{ - 10}}\\\\,s$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'In figure the field is upward. So the  negatively charged electron experiences a  downward force.<br>$\\therefore $ The acceleration of electron is ${a_e} = \\frac{{eE}}{m}$ (1)<br>The time required by the electron to fall through a distance $h$ is<br>${t_e} = \\sqrt {\\left( {\\frac{{2h}}{{{a_e}}}} \\right)}  = \\sqrt {\\frac{{2h{m_e}}}{{eE}}} $     (using (1))<br>$ = {\\left[ {\\frac{{2 \\times 1.5 \\times {{10}^{ - 2}} \\times 9.11 \\times {{10}^{ - 31}}}}{{1.6 \\times {{10}^{ - 19}} \\times 2 \\times {{10}^4}}}} \\right]^{1/2}} = 2.9 \\times {10^{ - 9}}s$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(30576,'physics',12,74,286,'An electron is moving with the speed  $5 \\times {10^6}\\,m{\\rm{ }}{s^{ - 1}}$ is shooted parallel to the electric  field of intensity $1 \\times {10^3}\\,N\\,{C^{ - 1}}$. Field is responsible for the retardation of motion of electron. Now evalute the distance travelled by the electron before coming to rest<br>$\\left( {{m_e} = 9.1 \\times {{10}^{ - 31}}kg;{\\rm{ }}e = 1.6 \\times {{10}^{ - 19}}\\,C} \\right)$','[{"text": "$7\\\\,m$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$0.7\\\\,mm$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$7\\\\,cm$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$0.7\\\\,cm$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'The distance travelled by the electron before coming to rest is<br>$d = \\frac{{{u^2}}}{{2a}}$<br>$u = 5 \\times {10^6}m/s$<br>$a = \\frac{{Eq}}{m} = \\frac{{{{10}^3} \\times 1.6 \\times {{10}^{ - 19}}}}{{9.1 \\times {{10}^{ - 31}}}}$<br>$ \\Rightarrow d = 7.1cm$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(30580,'physics',12,74,286,'An $\\alpha $ particle of mass $6.4 \\times {10^{ - 27}}kg$ and charge $3.2 \\times {10^{ - 19}}C$ is situated in a uniform electric  field of $1.6 \\times {10^5}V{m^{ - 1}}$. The velocity of the   particle at the end of $2 \\times {10^{ - 2}}m$ path when it  starts from rest is','[{"text": "$2\\\\sqrt 3  \\\\times {10^5}m{s^{ - 1}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$8 \\\\times {10^5}m{s^{ - 1}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$16 \\\\times {10^5}m{s^{ - 1}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$4\\\\sqrt 2  \\\\times {10^5}m{s^{ - 1}}$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Here, mass of  a particle ${m_a} = 6.4 \\times {10^{ - 27}}kg$<br>Charge of a particle ${q_a} = 3.2 \\times {10^{ - 19}}C$<br>Electric field $E = 1.6 \\times {10^5}V{m^{ - 1}}$<br>Force on a particle in a uniform electric field $E$:<br>$F = {q_a}\\,E = 3.2 \\times {10^{ - 19}} \\times 1.6 \\times {10^5}N$<br>$ = 3.2 \\times 1.6 \\times {10^{ - 14}}N$<br>Acceleration of the a particle is<br>$a = \\frac{F}{{{m_\\alpha }}} = \\frac{{3.2 \\times 1.6 \\times {{10}^{ - 14}}}}{{6.4 \\times {{10}^{ - 27}}}}m{s^{ - 2}}$<br>Given :$u = 0$<br>${v^2} = 2as\\frac{{2 \\times 3.2 \\times 1.6 \\times {{10}^{ - 14}} \\times 2 \\times {{10}^{ - 2}}}}{{6.4 \\times {{10}^{ - 27}}}}$<br>(As $s = 2 \\times {10^{ - 2}}m$)<br>$ = 3.2 \\times {10^{11}} = 32 \\times {10^{10}}{m^2}{s^2}$<br>${\\rm{or}}\\,v = 4\\sqrt 2  \\times {10^5}m{s^{ - 1}}$<br>Alternate solution :<br>According to work energy theorem,<br>$\\frac{1}{2}{m_\\alpha }{v^2} - \\frac{1}{2}{m_\\alpha }{(0)^2} = {q_\\alpha }ES$<br>or $\\frac{1}{2}{m_\\alpha }{v^2} = {q_\\alpha }ES$ or $v = \\sqrt {\\frac{{2{q_\\alpha }ES}}{{{m_\\alpha }}}} $<br>$v = \\frac{{2 \\times 3.2 \\times {{10}^{ - 19}} \\times 1.6 \\times {{10}^5} \\times 2 \\times {{10}^{ - 2}}}}{{6.4 \\times {{10}^{ - 27}}}}$<br>$v = \\sqrt {3.2 \\times {{10}^{11}}}  = \\sqrt {32 \\times {{10}^{10}}} $<br>$ = 4\\sqrt 2  \\times {10^5}\\,m/s$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(30583,'physics',12,74,286,'A particle of mass $m$ and charge $q$ is placed at  rest in a uniform electric field $E$ and then  released. The velocity attained by the particle after moving a distance $y$ is','[{"text": "$\\\\sqrt {\\\\frac{{qEy}}{{4m}}} $", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\sqrt {q{E^2}y} $", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\sqrt {\\\\frac{{2qEy}}{m}} $", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\sqrt {\\\\frac{{qEy}}{{2m}}} $", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'From work energy theorem<br>$K.E = F.S \\Rightarrow K.E = qEy = \\frac{1}{2}m{v^2}$<br>$v = \\sqrt {\\frac{{2qEy}}{m}} $',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(30584,'chemistry',70,1253,3054,'How many of the following amino acids have a $ - {\\text{C}}{{\\text{H}}_3}$ group in its side chain?','[{"text": "4", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "2", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "5", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "6", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'<img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/70_30584_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />  Only 2 amino acids i.e. valine, theroline have a $ - \\,{\\text{C}}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{3}}}$group in its side chain.<br>So, the correct option is (2).',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','chemistry/70_30584_question_1.png'),
(30587,'physics',12,75,287,'The electric field ${\\vec E}$ at a point just outside a  perfect conductor is','[{"text": "Present in any direction", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Zero", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Parallel to the surface", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Perpendicular to the surface", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Electric lines of force are always normal to conducting surface.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(30604,'physics',12,75,287,'An uncharged sphere of metal is placed in between two charged plates as shown. The lines of force look like','[{"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Electric lines of force never intersect the conductor. They are perpendicular and curved near the surface of conductor.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(30591,'physics',12,75,287,'The points resembling equal potentials are','[{"text": "P and Q", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "S and Q", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "S and R", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "P and R", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'For a uniform electric field, say, along the $x$-axis, the equipotential surfaces are planes normal to the $x$-axis, i.e.,<br>Plane parallel to the $y$-$z$ plane is an equipotential surface. The points $S$ and $R$ have equal  potentials.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/12_30591_question_1.png'),
(30595,'physics',12,75,287,'Figure shows the electric lines of force from a charged body. If the electric field at $A$ and $B$ are ${E_A}\\,{\\rm{and}}\\,{E_B}$  respectively then','[{"text": "${E_A} < {E_B}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${E_A} > {E_B}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${E_A} = {E_B}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${E_A} = 2{E_B}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'The density of lines of force $ \\propto E$<br>Here, the density of lines of force at $A$ is greater than at $B$.<br>${E_A} > {E_B}$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/12_30595_question_1.png'),
(30598,'physics',12,75,287,'Which of the following figures correctly shows the top view sketch of the electric field lines for a uniformly positive charged hollow cylinder as shown in figure?','[{"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'For a uniformly charged hollow cylinder, the electric field lines are in radial direction.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(30599,'chemistry',70,1250,3047,'How many among the following is/are reducing sugar?<br>Sucrose, glucose, maltose and lactose','[{"text": "1", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "2", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "3", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "4", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Among the given, only sucrose is a non-reducing sugar.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(30602,'physics',12,75,287,'If a positively charged pendulum is oscillating in  a uniform electric field as shown in figure its  time period as compared to that when it was  unchanged will','[{"text": "Decrease", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "First increase and then decrease", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Not change", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Increase", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Bob will experience an additional force,$F = qE$ in vertically upward direction and hence, effective acceleration due to gravity is reduced from $g$ to$(g - a)$. Time period of oscillation will become,<br>$T = 2\\pi \\sqrt {\\frac{{\\rm{l}}}{{(g - {\\rm{a}})}}} $<br>i.e., time period will increase.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/12_30602_question_1.png'),
(30608,'physics',12,75,287,'A charge $q$ is fixed at a distance $d$ infront of an inifinite metal plate. The lines of force are represented by','[{"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Metal plate acts as an equipotential surface, therefore the field lines should enter normal to the surface of the metal plate.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/12_30608_question_1.png'),
(30611,'physics',12,75,287,'Which of the following curves shown below cannot possibly represent electrostatic field lines?','[{"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Electrostatic field lines start or end only at $90^\\circ $ to the surface of the conductor. So option (2) cannot represent electrostatic field lines.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(30612,'chemistry',70,1253,3054,'How many of the following amino acids are neutral in nature?<br>Alanine, lysine, histidine, asparagine, tryptophan, isoleucine, methionine','[{"text": "5", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "4", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "3", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "2", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Among the given options, alanine, asparagine, tryptophan, isoleucine and methionine are neutral amino acids.<br>Lysine and histidine are basic amino acids.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(30615,'physics',12,75,287,'Figure shows the electric field lines around three points charges $A$, $B$ and $C$. Which charge has  the largest magnitude?','[{"text": "Charge B", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Charge A and B", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Charge A", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Charge C", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Charge $C$ has the largest magnitude since maximum number of field lines are associated with it.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/12_30615_question_1.png'),
(30619,'physics',12,75,287,'A test charge ${q_o}$ is moved without acceleration from $A$ to $C$ along the path $ABC$ as shown in figure. The potential difference between $A$ and $C$ is','[{"text": "$Ed$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$E/d$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2Ed$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$Ed/2$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'As it is clear from figure<br>$\\overline {AB}  + \\overline {BC}  = \\overline {AC} $<br>As $E =  - \\frac{{dV}}{{dr}} =  - \\frac{{({V_C} - {V_A})}}{d} = \\frac{{{V_A} - {V_C}}}{d}$<br>$\\therefore \\quad \\quad {V_A} - {V_C} = Ed$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/12_30619_question_1.png'),
(50378,'biology',96,1164,2779,'Which one of the following helps in absorption of phosphorus from soil by plants?','[{"text": "<i>Glomus</i>", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "<i>Rhizobium</i>", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "<i>Frankia</i>", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "<i>Anabaena</i>", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Many members of the genus Glomus form mycorrhiza.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(30623,'physics',12,77,289,'Which of the following statements about dipole moment is not true?','[{"text": "The unit of dipole moment is $C$ $m$.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "The dimensions of dipole moment is [LTA].", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Dipole moment is a vector quantity and directed from negative to positive charge.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Dipole moment is a scalar quantity", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Dipole moment is a vector quantity and  it is in the direction from $ - q\\,to\\,q$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(30626,'chemistry',70,1253,3054,'Some amino acids are listed below.<br>Tyr, Cys, Glu, Leu, Phe<br>From the above, the number of amino acids having an aromatic ring in its structure is ____ .','[{"text": "1", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "2", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "3", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "4", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Tyrosine (Tyr) and Phenylalanine (Phe) have an aromatic ring in its structure',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(30627,'physics',12,77,291,'An electric dipole in a uniform electric field  experiences (When it is placed at an angle   with the field)','[{"text": "Force but no torque", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Torque but no force", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "No force and no torque", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Force and torque both", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'In uniform electric field dipole experience only torque, but no force.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(30630,'physics',12,77,289,'A region surrounding a stationary electric dipoles has','[{"text": "Both electric and magnetic fields", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Electric field only", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Magnetic field only", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "No electric and magnetic fields", "isCorrect": false}]',1,NULL,NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(30634,'physics',12,77,291,'An electric dipole is placed in an electric field  generated by a point charge','[{"text": "The net electric force on the dipole may be zero", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "The net electric force on the dipole must be zero", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "The torque on the dipole due to the field may be zero", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "The torque on the dipole due to the field must be zero", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Point charge produces non-uniform electric  field. If the dipole moment vector is parallel or  antiparallel with the external field then the torque  on a dipole in non-uniform field can be zero.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(30638,'physics',12,77,293,'If the centre of mass of positive charge does  not coincide with that of the molecule, then','[{"text": "Molecule is called polar but it does not have any dipole moment", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Molecule is called polar and it has an intrinsic dipole moment", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Molecule is called non-polar and it has zero dipole moment", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "None of the given options", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'If the centre of mass of the positive charge  does not conincide with that of the negative  charge, the molecule has intrinsic (or permanent)  dipole moment. Such molecules are called polar  molecules.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(30641,'chemistry',70,1247,3035,'With how many molecules of acetic anhydride does one molecule of glucose react?','[{"text": "5", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "4", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "3", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "2", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'There are five $ - $OH groups in glucose, hence 5 molecules of acetic anhydride are needed.',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(30642,'physics',12,77,289,'For the dipole shown,<br>Dipole moment is given by','[{"text": "$\\\\vec p = q\\\\left( d \\\\right)\\\\hat p$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\vec p = \\\\frac{1}{2}q\\\\left( d \\\\right)\\\\hat p$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\vec p =  - q\\\\left( d \\\\right)\\\\hat p$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\vec p = 3q\\\\left( d \\\\right)\\\\hat p$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,NULL,NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/12_30642_question_1.png'),
(30645,'physics',12,77,291,'An electric dipole has the magnitude of its charge  as $q$ and its dipole moment is $p$. It is placed in  uniform electric field $E$. If its dipole moment is  along the direction of the field, the force on it  is','[{"text": "$qE$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2qE$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$3qE$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{zero}}$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'When the dipole is in the direction of a uniform  field then net force is $qE + ( - qE) = 0$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(30649,'physics',12,77,289,'What is the angle between the electric dipole moment and the electric field strength due to it on the equatorial line?','[{"text": "$0^\\\\circ $", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$90^\\\\circ $", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$180^\\\\circ $", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "None of these", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Observing ${E_{net}}$ and $p$ are in opposite directions,  so angle between them is $180^\\circ $',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(31654,'physics',19,139,317,'The dimension of quantity  ($L$/$RCV$)  is','[{"text": "$[A]$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${A^2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$[{A^{ - 1}}]$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{None}}\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\rm{of}}\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\rm{these}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'$\\frac{L}{{RCV}} = \\left[ {\\frac{L}{R}} \\right]\\frac{1}{{CV}} = \\frac{T}{Q} = \\left[ {{A^{ - 1}}} \\right]$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(30652,'physics',12,77,292,'There exists a non-uniform electric field along  $x$-axis as shown in the figure below. The field  increases at a uniform rate along +$ve$ $x$-axis. A  dipole is placed inside the field as shown. Which  one of the following is correct for the dipole?','[{"text": "Dipole moves along positive $x$-axis and undergoes a clockwise rotation", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Dipole moves along negative $x$-axis and undergoes a clockwise rotation", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Dipole moves along postive $x$-axis and undergoes a anticlockwise rotation", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Dipole moves along negative $x$-axis and undergoes a anticlockwise rotation", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'The dipole is placed in a non-uniform field,  therefore a force as well as a torque acts on it.<br>Let ${E_1}\\,{\\rm{and}}\\,{E_2}$ be the electric fields at the charges  $ - q$ and q respectively.<br>The force on the negative charge is more $\\left( {F \\propto E} \\right)$ and is directed along negative $x$-axis.  Thus the dipole moves along negative $x$-axis and  rotates in an anticlockwise direction.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/12_30652_question_1.png'),
(30655,'chemistry',70,1246,3029,'How many asymmetric carbon atoms are present in glyceraldehyde?','[{"text": "1", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "2", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "3", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "4", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Glyceraldehyde contains one asymmetric carbon atom. <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/70_30655_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(30658,'physics',12,77,291,'When an electric dipole $p$ is placed in a uniform electric field $E$, then at what angle between $p$ and $E$ the value of torque will be minimum?','[{"text": "$90^\\\\circ $", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$135^\\\\circ $", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$0^\\\\circ $", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$45^\\\\circ $", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Torque,$\\tau  = pE\\,\\,\\sin \\,\\,\\theta $<br>$\\left| \\tau  \\right| = pE\\,\\,\\sin \\,\\,\\theta  = 0$<br>$\\therefore $ Torque is minimum, when<br>$\\theta  = 0^\\circ \\,\\,or\\,\\,180^\\circ $',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(30662,'physics',12,77,289,'Two point charges of  $5\\mu C\\,\\,\\,{\\mathop{\\rm and}\\nolimits} \\,\\,\\, - 5\\mu C$ are separated by a distance of $10\\,\\mathop A\\limits^ \\circ  $, $A$ point $B$ is  at a distance of 10 $cm$ from the midpoint and on  the perpendicular bisector of the line joining the  two charges. The electric field at $B $ will be','[{"text": "$5\\\\,\\\\,N\\\\,{C^{ - 1}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$0.9\\\\,\\\\,N\\\\,{C^{ - 1}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$45\\\\,N\\\\,{C^{ - 1}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$0.045\\\\,N{C^{ - 1}}$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'The point lies on equatorial line of a short  dipole.<br>$\\therefore E = \\frac{{qd}}{{4\\pi {\\varepsilon _0}r{}^3}} = \\frac{{9 \\times {{10}^9} \\times 5 \\times {{10}^{ - 6}} \\times {{10}^{ - 9}}}}{{{{\\left( {{{10}^{ - 1}}} \\right)}^3}}}$<br>$ = 0.045\\,\\,N{C^{ - 1}}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(30664,'physics',12,77,291,'An electric dipole is situated in an electric field  of uniform intensity $E$ whose dipole moment is  $p$ and moment of inertia is $I$. If the dipole is  displaced slightly from the equilibrium position,  then the time period of its oscillation is','[{"text": "$2\\\\pi \\\\sqrt {\\\\frac{I}{{pE}}} $", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\pi \\\\sqrt {\\\\frac{I}{{pE}}} $", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\sqrt {\\\\frac{I}{{pE}}} $", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\sqrt {\\\\frac{{pE}}{I}} $", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'As, torque i.e., $\\tau  = pE\\,\\,\\sin \\,\\theta  = I\\alpha $<br>For small $\\theta ,\\alpha  = \\frac{{ - pE}}{I}\\theta $(As $\\alpha $ and $\\theta $are  opposite)<br>$\\omega  = \\sqrt {\\frac{{pE}}{I}} $<br>$T = \\frac{{2\\pi }}{\\omega } = 2\\pi \\sqrt {\\frac{I}{{pE}}} $',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(30667,'physics',12,77,291,'An electric dipole is placed at an angle of $60^\\circ $  with an electric field of intensity $\\sqrt 3  \\times {10^5}\\,N{C^{ - 1}}$.  It experiences a torque equal to 6 $N m$. The  charge on the dipole if the dipole length is 4 $cm$  is','[{"text": "$5\\\\,mC$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$3\\\\,mC$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$6\\\\,mC$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1\\\\,mC$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Here,$E = \\sqrt 3  \\times {10^5}N{C^{ - 1}},\\,l = 4\\,cm,\\,\\tau  = 6\\,Nm$<br>Torque, $\\vec \\tau  = \\vec p \\times \\vec E;\\tau  = pE\\,\\,\\sin \\theta $<br>$\\therefore 6 = p \\times \\sqrt 3  \\times {10^5} \\times \\sin 60^\\circ $ or $p = 4 \\times {10^{ - 5}}Cm$<br>$\\therefore \\,\\,\\,{\\rm{Charge}},\\,\\,q = \\frac{p}{l} = \\frac{{4 \\times {{10}^{ - 5}}Cm}}{{0.04\\,\\,m}}$<br>$ = 1 \\times {10^{ - 3}}C = 1\\,\\,mC$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(30670,'chemistry',70,1254,3059,'How many carboxylic acid functional groups is present in one molecule of dipeptide glycylalanine?','[{"text": "1", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "2", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "3", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "4", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'A dipeptide glycylalanine is formed when $ - {\\text{COOH}}$ group of glycine combines with $ - {\\text{N}}{{\\text{H}}_{\\text{2}}}$ group of alanine. <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/70_30670_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' /> Dipeptide glycylalanine has 1 carboxylic acid functional group.',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(30671,'physics',12,132,301,'Gauss’s law should be invalid if','[{"text": "The velocity of light were not a universal constant", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "The inverse square law were not exactly true", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "There were magnetic monopoles", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "None of these", "isCorrect": false}]',1,NULL,NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(30675,'physics',12,132,301,'What is the nature of Gaussian surface involved in Gauss law of electrostatics ?','[{"text": "Magnetic", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Scalar", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Vector", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Electrical", "isCorrect": false}]',2,NULL,NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(30679,'physics',12,132,301,'Shown below is a distribution of charges. The flux of electric field due to these charges through the surface is','[{"text": "$2q/{\\\\varepsilon _0}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$q/{\\\\varepsilon _0}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$3q/{\\\\varepsilon _0}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Zero", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'The total flux is $\\phi  = \\frac{{{q_{enc}}}}{{{\\varepsilon _0}}} = \\frac{1}{{{\\varepsilon _0}}}(2q)$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/12_30679_question_1.png'),
(30682,'physics',12,132,301,'Eight dipoles of charges of magnitude $e$ are placed inside a cube. The total electric flux coming out of the cube will be','[{"text": "$\\\\frac{{16e}}{{{\\\\varepsilon _0}}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{e}{{{\\\\varepsilon _0}}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{{8e}}{{{\\\\varepsilon _0}}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Zero", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'The total flux is<br>$\\phi = \\frac{{{q_{total}}}}{{{\\varepsilon _0}}} = 0$ (Net charge on dipole is zero)',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(30684,'chemistry',70,1251,3051,'2 moles of stachyose on complete hydrolysis yields ____ moles of galactose.','[{"text": "5", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "2", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "3", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "4", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'1 mole of stachyose on complete hydrolysis yields 1 mole of glucose, 1 mole of fructose and 2 moles of galactose.<br>Therefore, 2 moles of stachyose on complete hydrolysis yields 4 moles of galactose.',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(30686,'physics',12,132,301,'If there were only one type of charge in the  universe, then','[{"text": "$\\\\oint_S {\\\\overrightarrow E  \\\\cdot d\\\\overrightarrow s }  \\\\ne 0$ on any surface", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\oint_S {\\\\overrightarrow E  \\\\cdot d\\\\overrightarrow s }  = 0$ if the charge is outside the surface", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\oint_S {\\\\overrightarrow E  \\\\cdot d\\\\overrightarrow s }  = \\\\frac{q}{{{\\\\varepsilon _0}}}$ if charges of magnitude $q$ were inside the surface", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both (2) and (3) are correct", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'According to gauss’s theorem in  electrostatics<br>$\\oint_S {\\overrightarrow E  \\cdot \\overrightarrow {ds} }  = \\frac{q}{{{\\varepsilon _0}}}$<br>Here $q$ is charge enclosed by the surface.<br>if the charge is outside the surface, then<br>${q_{inside}} = 0$<br>Also,$\\oint_S {\\overrightarrow E  \\cdot d\\overrightarrow s }  = 0$<br>So, both (2) and (3) are correct.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(30690,'physics',12,132,301,'A hollow cylinder has a charge $q$ coulomb within it. If $\\phi $ is the electric flux associated with the curved surface $B$, the flux linked with the plane surface $A$ in units of volt-meter will be','[{"text": "$\\\\frac{1}{2}\\\\left( {\\\\frac{q}{{{\\\\varepsilon _0}}} - \\\\phi } \\\\right)$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{\\\\phi }{3}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{q}{{2{\\\\varepsilon _0}}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{q}{{{\\\\varepsilon _0}}} - \\\\phi $", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'The total flux through the cylinder is<br>${\\phi _{Total}} = {\\phi _A} + {\\phi _B} + {\\phi _C} = \\frac{q}{{{\\varepsilon _0}}};$<br>$\\because {\\phi _B} = \\phi \\,and\\,{\\phi _A} = {\\phi _C} = \\phi \'$<br>$\\therefore 2\\phi \' + \\phi  = \\frac{q}{{{\\varepsilon _0}}} \\Rightarrow \\phi \' = \\frac{1}{2}\\left( {\\frac{q}{{{\\varepsilon _0}}} - \\phi } \\right)$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/12_30690_question_1.png'),
(30694,'physics',12,132,301,'A point charge $ + Q$ is positioned at the centre of the base of a square pyramid as shown. The flux through one of the four identical upper faces  of the pyramid is','[{"text": "$\\\\frac{Q}{{16{\\\\varepsilon _0}}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{Q}{{4{\\\\varepsilon _0}}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{Q}{{8{\\\\varepsilon _0}}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "None of these", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Flux going in pyramid $ = \\frac{Q}{{2{\\varepsilon _0}}}$<br>Which is divided equally among all 4 faces.<br>$\\therefore $ Flux through one face $ = \\frac{Q}{{8{\\varepsilon _0}}}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/12_30694_question_1.png'),
(30697,'physics',12,132,301,'What is the electric flux linked with closed surface?','[{"text": "${10^{12}}N{m^2}{C^{ - 1}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${10^{11}}N{m^2}{C^{ - 1}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$8.86 \\\\times {10^{13}}N{m^2}{C^{ - 1}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${10^{10}}N{m^2}{C^{ - 1}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'According to Gauss&rsquo;s law<br>Electric flux,$\\phi = \\frac{q}{{{\\varepsilon _0}}}$<br>Where,$q = $ total charge enclosed by closed surface<br>$\\therefore \\,\\phi = \\frac{{1.25 + 7 + 1 - 0.4}}{{{\\varepsilon _0}}}$<br>$ = \\frac{{8.85C}}{{8.85 \\times {{10}^{ - 12}}{C^2}{N^{ - 1}}{m^{ - 2}}}} = {10^{12}}N{m^2}{C^{ - 1}}$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/12_30697_question_1.png'),
(30698,'chemistry',70,1251,3051,'How many among the following are oligosaccharides?<br>$\\begin{array}{*{20}{c}}\n{{\\rm{i}}{\\rm{.}}\\,{\\rm{Cellulose}}\\,\\,\\,}&{{\\rm{ii}}{\\rm{.}}\\,{\\rm{Lactose}}\\,\\,\\,}\\\\\n{{\\rm{iii}}{\\rm{.}}\\,{\\rm{Sucrose}}\\,\\,\\,}&{{\\rm{iv}}{\\rm{.}}\\,{\\rm{Raffinose}}}\\\\\n{{\\rm{v}}{\\rm{.}}\\,{\\rm{Ribose}}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,}&{{\\rm{vi}}{\\rm{.}}\\,{\\rm{Galactose}}}\\\\\n{{\\rm{vii}}{\\rm{.}}\\,{\\rm{Glycogen}}}&{{\\rm{viii}}{\\rm{.}}\\,{\\rm{Starch}}\\,\\,\\,}\n\\end{array}$','[{"text": "8", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "6", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "3", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "4", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Carbohydrates that yield two to ten monosaccharide units, on hydrolysis, are called oligosaccharides. Lactose (disaccharide), sucrose (disaccharide) and raffinose (trisaccharide) are examples of oligosaccharides.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(30701,'physics',12,76,288,'A uniform electric field $E = 2 \\times {10^3}N{C^{ - 1}}$ is acting along the positive $X$-axis. The flux of  this field  through a square of 10 $cm$ side whose plane is  parallel to the $YZ$ plane is','[{"text": "$30N{C^{ - 1}}{m^2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$20N{C^{ - 1}}{m^2}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$40N{C^{ - 1}}{m^2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$10N{C^{ - 1}}{m^2}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Here,$E = 2 \\times {10^3}N{C^{ - 1}}$ along $ + x$ axis<br>$S = {\\left( {10cm} \\right)^2} = {10^2} \\times {10^{ - 4}}{m^2} = {10^{ - 2}}{m^2}$<br>When plane is parallel to $yz$ plane, ${\\rm{\\theta }} = {\\rm{0}}^\\circ $<br>So $\\phi  = ES\\cos \\theta  = 2 \\times {10^3} \\times {10^{ - 2}}\\cos {\\rm{0}}^\\circ $<br>$ = 20N{C^{ - 1}}{m^2}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(30705,'physics',12,132,301,'A charge $q$ is distributed uniformly on a ring of radius $R$. A sphere of equal radius $R$ is constructed with its centre at the periphery of the ring. Find the flux of the electric field through the surface of the sphere.','[{"text": "$\\\\frac{q}{{{\\\\varepsilon _0}}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{q}{{2{\\\\varepsilon _0}}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{q}{{3{\\\\varepsilon _0}}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${\\\\mathop{\\\\rm Zero}\\\\nolimits} $", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'A part of the ring present inside the sphere is shown in the figure.<br>The length of the ring present in the sphere is<br>$l = \\left( {\\frac{{2\\pi }}{3}} \\right)R$<br>The charge of the ring present inside the sphere is<br>$\\frac{q}{{2\\pi }} \\times \\frac{{2\\pi }}{3} = \\frac{q}{3}$<br>$\\Phi = \\frac{q}{{3{\\varepsilon _o}}}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/12_30705_question_1.png'),
(30709,'physics',12,132,301,'An infinitely long line of charge having a uniform charge per unit length $\\lambda $ lies at a distance $x$ from a point $O$ as shown in the figure. Determine the total electric flux through the surface of a sphere of radius $R$. $(x < R)$','[{"text": "$\\\\frac{{2\\\\lambda R}}{{{\\\\varepsilon _o}}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{{\\\\lambda \\\\sqrt {{R^2} - {x^2}} }}{{{\\\\varepsilon _o}}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{{2\\\\lambda \\\\sqrt {{R^2} - {x^2}} }}{{{\\\\varepsilon _o}}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{{\\\\lambda R}}{{{\\\\varepsilon _o}}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'The length of the rod present inside the sphere is $2\\sqrt {{R^2} - {x^2}} $<br>Flux through the sphere is <br>$\\Phi  = \\frac{{2\\lambda \\sqrt {{R^2} - {x^2}} }}{{{\\varepsilon _o}}}$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/12_30709_question_1.png'),
(36569,'physics',27,1382,3321,'There is a black spot on a body. If the body is heated and carried in a dark room then it glows more. This can be explained on the basis of','[{"text": "Newton\'s law of cooling", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Wein\'s law", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Kirchhoff\'s law", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Stefan\'s law", "isCorrect": false}]',2,NULL,NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(30713,'physics',12,78,299,'Two conducting plates $A$ and $B$ are parellel to each other. $A$ is given a charge ${Q_1}\\,$ and $B$ is given a charge ${Q_2}\\,$ . The charge on inner side of $B$ is','[{"text": "$\\\\frac{{{Q_1} - {Q_2}}}{2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{{\\\\left( {{Q_2} - {Q_1}} \\\\right)}}{2}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{{\\\\left( {{Q_1} + {Q_2}} \\\\right)}}{2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{{ - \\\\left( {{Q_1} + {Q_2}} \\\\right)}}{2}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Electric field inside the conductor at point $P = 0$<br>From Gauss law the charges on the inside faces of $A$ and $B$ are equal in magnitude and have opposite signs. From conservation of charges on $A$ and $B$ <br>${q_1} + {q_2} = {Q_1}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,(1)$<br>$ - {q_2} + {q_3} = {Q_2}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,(2)$<br>As net electric field inside plate $A$ is zero.<br>$\\frac{{{q_1}}}{{2A{\\varepsilon _0}}} = \\frac{{{q_2}}}{{2A{\\varepsilon _0}}}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,(3)$<br>From 1,2, & 3<br>We get $ - {q_2} = \\frac{{{Q_2} - {Q_1}}}{2}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/12_30713_question_1.png'),
(30716,'physics',12,78,299,'The parallel plane sheets 1 and 2 carry uniform charge densities ${\\sigma _{1\\,}}{\\rm{and}}\\,{\\sigma _2}$ as shown in the figure. The magnitude of the resultant electric field in the region marked III is $({\\sigma _{1\\,}} > \\,{\\sigma _2})$','[{"text": "$\\\\frac{{{\\\\sigma _1}\\\\, + {\\\\sigma _2}}}{{2{\\\\varepsilon _0}}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{{{\\\\sigma _2}}}{{2{\\\\varepsilon _0}}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{{{\\\\sigma _1}{\\\\sigma _2}}}{{{\\\\varepsilon _0}}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{{{\\\\sigma _1}}}{{2{\\\\sigma _2}{\\\\varepsilon _0}}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'The field produced by an infinite sheet of charge density $\\sigma $ is $\\frac{\\sigma }{{2{\\varepsilon _0}}}$. The resultant electric field is given by $E = {E_1} + {E_2} \\Rightarrow $<br>$E = \\frac{{{\\sigma _1}}}{{2{\\varepsilon _0}}} + \\frac{{{\\sigma _2}}}{{2{\\varepsilon _0}}} = \\frac{{{\\sigma _1} + {\\sigma _2}}}{{2{\\varepsilon _0}}}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/12_30716_question_1.png'),
(30720,'physics',12,78,300,'Electric field due to<div class="matching-question"><div class="column-left">A. Infinite plane sheet of charge<br>B. Infinite plane sheet of uniform thickness<br>C. Non -conducting charged solid sphere at its surface<br>D. Conducting charged solid sphere at its centre</div><div class="column-right">P.$0$<br>Q. $\\frac{\\sigma }{{2{\\varepsilon _0}}}$<br>R. $\\frac{{R\\rho }}{{3{\\varepsilon _0}}}$<br>S. $\\frac{\\sigma }{{{\\varepsilon _0}}}$</div></div>','[{"text": "A-Q,  B-S,   C-R,  D-P", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "A-R,  B-Q,   C-P,  D-S", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "A-P,   B-S,   C-Q,  D-R", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "A-Q,  B-P,   C-S,  D-R", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'A - Q, B - S, C - R, D - P<br>A. Infinite plane sheet of charge $ = \\frac{{\\rm{\\sigma }}}{{2{{\\rm{\\varepsilon }}_0}}}$<br>B.  Infinite plane sheet of uniform thickness ${\\rm{ = }}\\frac{{\\rm{\\sigma }}}{{2{{\\rm{\\varepsilon }}_{\\rm{0}}}}}{\\rm{ + }}\\frac{{\\rm{\\sigma }}}{{2{{\\rm{\\varepsilon }}_{\\rm{0}}}}}{\\rm{ = }}\\frac{{\\rm{\\sigma }}}{{{{\\rm{\\varepsilon }}_{\\rm{0}}}}}$<br>C.  Non-conducting charged solid  sphere at its surface  $ = \\frac{Q}{{4\\pi {\\varepsilon _0}{R^2}}} = \\frac{{QR}}{{3\\left( {\\frac{4}{3}\\pi {R^3}} \\right){\\varepsilon _0}}} = \\frac{{\\rho R}}{{3{\\varepsilon _0}}}$<br>D. At the centre of a solid sphere the field is  zero. <br>So option (1) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Match the following',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(30722,'chemistry',70,1253,3054,'The isoelectric point of glutamic acid if the $\\mathrm{Pk}_{\\mathrm{a}_{1}}$ values of three ionisable groups $\\mathrm{X}, \\mathrm{Y}$ and $\\mathrm{Z}$ are 4.9, 9.7 and 3.0 respectively is ____.<br> <br>','[{"text": "1", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "2", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "3", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "4", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'In acidic medium  $ = \\frac{{{\\rm{X}} + {\\rm{Z}}}}{{\\rm{Z}}} = \\frac{{4.9 + 3.0}}{2} = 3.95$<br>$ \\approx 4$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','chemistry/70_30722_question_1.png'),
(36587,'physics',26,1138,2729,'Mercury does not wet glass, wood or iron because','[{"text": "Cohesive force is less than adhesive force", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Cohesive force is greater than adhesive froce", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Angle of contact is less than $$90^{\\\\circ}$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Cohesive force is equal to adhesive force", "isCorrect": false}]',1,NULL,NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(30724,'physics',12,72,279,'A solid sphere of radius ${R_1}$ and volume charge density $\\rho  = \\frac{{{\\rho _0}}}{r}$  is enclosed by a hollow sphere of radius ${R_2}$ with negative surface charge density $\\sigma ,$ such that the total charge in the  system is zero. ${\\rho _0}$ is a positive constant and $r$ is  the distance from the centre of the sphere. The ratio ${R_2}/{R_1}$ is','[{"text": "$\\\\sigma /{\\\\rho _0}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rho _0}/\\\\sigma $", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\sqrt {{\\\\rho _0}/2\\\\sigma } $", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\sqrt {2\\\\sigma /{\\\\rho _0}} $", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'The total positive charge in the inner solid sphere is $\\int\\limits_0^{{R_1}} {4\\pi {r^2}\\,dr.\\frac{{{\\rho _0}}}{r} =  + \\,4\\pi \\frac{{R_1^2}}{2}} {\\rho _0}$<br>The total negative charge on the outer shell is $ - 4\\pi R_2^2\\sigma $.But both are equal in magnitude as the sum is zero.<br>$\\frac{{R_1^2}}{{R_2^2}} = \\frac{{{\\rho _0}}}{{2\\sigma }}\\quad \\therefore \\frac{{{R_2}}}{{{R_1}}} = \\sqrt {\\frac{{{\\rho _0}}}{{2\\sigma }}} $',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(30726,'physics',12,73,281,'Four point $ + ve$ charges of same magnitude (Q)are placed at four corners of a rigid square frame as shown in figure. The plane of the frame is perpendicular to $Z$-axis. If a $ - ve$ point charge is placed at a distance $z$ away from the above frame  $(Z <  < L)$ then','[{"text": "$ - ve$ charge oscillates along the Z-axis", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "It passes through the frame only once", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "It moves away from the frame", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "It moves slowely towards the frame and stays in the plane of the frame", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'The resultant force acts as a restoring force and proportional to displacement. When it reaches the plane $XY$, the resultant force is zero and the mass moves down due to inertia. Thus oscillation is set.',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/12_30726_question_1.png'),
(30730,'physics',12,73,280,'Two charges ${Q_1}$ and ${Q_2}$ are present as shown.Two dielectrics of thickness ${t_1}$ and ${t_2}$ and dielectric constants ${k_1}$ and ${k_2}$ are introduced as shown. Find the force between the charges','[{"text": "$\\\\frac{{{Q_1}{Q_2}}}{{4\\\\pi {\\\\varepsilon _0}{{\\\\left[ {d + \\\\sqrt {{k_1}} {t_1} + \\\\sqrt {{k_2}} {t_2}} \\\\right]}^2}}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{{{Q_1}{Q_2}}}{{4\\\\pi {\\\\varepsilon _0}{{\\\\left[ {d - ({t_1} + {t_2}) + {k_1}{t_1} + {k_2}{t_2}} \\\\right]}^2}}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{{{Q_1}{Q_2}}}{{4\\\\pi {\\\\varepsilon _0}{{\\\\left[ {d - ({t_1} + {t_2}) + \\\\sqrt {{k_1}} {t_1} + \\\\sqrt {{k_2}} {t_2}} \\\\right]}^2}}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Zero", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Effective distance in vacuum<br>$ = \\sqrt {{k_1}} {t_1} + \\sqrt {{k_2}} {t_2} + d - ({t_1} + {t_2})$<br>$F = \\frac{{{Q_1}{Q_2}}}{{4\\pi {\\varepsilon _0}{{\\left[ {\\sqrt {{k_1}} {t_1} + \\sqrt {{k_2}} {t_2} + d - ({t_1} + {t_2})} \\right]}^2}}}$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/12_30730_question_1.png'),
(30734,'physics',12,74,283,'A positive point charge $50\\,\\mu C$ is located in the plane $XY$ at a point with radius vector $\\overrightarrow {{r_0}}  = 2\\widehat i + 3\\widehat j$ . The electric field vector at $\\overrightarrow E $ a  point with radius vector $\\overrightarrow r  = 8\\widehat i - 5\\widehat j$ ,  where ${r_0}$ and $r$ are expressed in meter, is','[{"text": "$(1.4\\\\widehat i - 2.6\\\\widehat j)kN{C^{ - 1}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$(1.4\\\\widehat i + 2.6\\\\widehat j)kN{C^{ - 1}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$(2.7\\\\widehat i - 3.6\\\\widehat j)kN{C^{ - 1}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$(2.7\\\\widehat i + 3.6\\\\widehat j)kN{C^{ - 1}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'${\\rm{As}}\\,\\,\\,\\overrightarrow E  = \\frac{1}{{4\\pi {\\varepsilon _0}}}\\frac{q}{{{{\\left| {\\vec r - \\overrightarrow {{r_0}} } \\right|}^3}}}(\\overrightarrow r  - \\overrightarrow {{r_0}} )$<br>$\\overrightarrow r  - \\overrightarrow {{r_0}}  = (8\\widehat i - 5\\widehat j) - (2\\widehat i + 3\\widehat j)$<br>$ = (6\\widehat i - 8\\widehat j)$<br>$\\left| {\\overrightarrow r  - \\overrightarrow {{r_0}} } \\right| = \\sqrt {{6^2} + {8^2}}  = 10\\,m$<br>$\\overrightarrow E  = 9 \\times {10^9} \\times \\frac{{50 \\times {{10}^{ - 6}}}}{{{{(10)}^3}}}(6\\widehat i - 8\\widehat j)$<br>$\\overrightarrow E  = (2.7\\widehat i - 3.6\\widehat j)kN{C^{ - 1}}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(30737,'chemistry',70,1253,3054,'The isoelectric point of Alanine if $\\mathrm{Pk}_{\\mathrm{a}_{1}}$ and $\\mathrm{Pk}_{\\mathrm{a}_{2}}$ is 2.3 and 9.7 respectively ____.','[{"text": "5", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "6", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "4", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "3", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Isoelectric point $ = \\frac{1}{2}\\left[ {{\\rm{P}}{{\\rm{k}}_{{{\\rm{a}}_{\\rm{1}}}}} + {\\rm{P}}{{\\rm{k}}_{{{\\rm{a}}_{\\rm{2}}}}}} \\right]$<br>$\\frac{1}{2}\\left[ {2.3 + 9.7} \\right] = 6$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(30738,'physics',12,133,307,'Two infinitely long parallel wires having linear charge densities ${\\lambda _1}\\,{\\rm{and}}\\,{\\lambda _2}$ respectively are placed at a distance of $R$ metres. The force per unit length on either wire will be $\\left( {k = \\frac{1}{{4\\pi {\\varepsilon _0}}}} \\right)$','[{"text": "$\\\\frac{{{\\\\lambda _1}{\\\\lambda _2}\\\\,}}{R}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$k\\\\frac{{2{\\\\lambda _1}{\\\\lambda _2}\\\\,}}{R}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$k\\\\frac{{2{\\\\lambda _1}{\\\\lambda _2}}}{R}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$k\\\\frac{{{\\\\lambda _1}{\\\\lambda _2}\\\\,}}{{{R^2}}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'The force on $l$ length  of the wire 2 is<br>${F_2} = Q{E_1} = ({\\lambda _2}{\\rm{l}})\\frac{{2k{\\lambda _1}}}{R} \\Rightarrow \\frac{{{F_2}}}{{\\rm{l}}} = \\frac{{2k{\\lambda _1}{\\lambda _2}\\,}}{R}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(30741,'physics',12,78,297,'A positively charged sphere of radius ${r_0}$ carries a volume charge density $\\rho $. A spherical cavity of radius ${r_0}/2$ is then scooped out and left empty.${C_1}$ is the centre of the sphere and ${C_2}$ that of the cavity. What is the direction and magnitude of the electric field at point $B$?','[{"text": "$\\\\frac{{17\\\\rho {r_0}}}{{54{\\\\varepsilon _0}}}left$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{{\\\\rho {r_0}}}{{6{\\\\varepsilon _0}}}left$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{{17\\\\rho {r_0}}}{{54{\\\\varepsilon _0}}}right$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{{\\\\rho {r_0}}}{{6{\\\\varepsilon _0}}}right$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'The electric field at point $B$ is given by<br>${{\\vec E}_{original}} = {{\\vec E}_{removed}} + {{\\vec E}_{remaining}}$<br>${{\\vec E}_{original}}$ &ndash; Electric field by the original sphere without cavity<br>${{\\vec E}_{removed}}$ &ndash; Electric field by the removed part<br>${{\\vec E}_{remaining}}$ &ndash; Electric field by the remaining part.<br>${{\\vec E}_{original}} = \\frac{{ - \\rho \\left( {\\frac{4}{3}\\pi r_0^3} \\right)}}{{4\\pi {\\varepsilon _0}r_0^2}}\\hat i = \\frac{{ - \\rho {r_0}}}{{3{\\varepsilon _0}}}\\hat i$<br>${{\\vec E}_{removed}} = \\frac{{ - \\rho \\frac{4}{3}\\pi {{\\left( {\\frac{{{r_0}}}{2}} \\right)}^3}}}{{4\\pi {\\varepsilon _0}{{\\left( {\\frac{{3{r_0}}}{2}} \\right)}^2}}} = \\frac{{ - \\rho {r_0}}}{{54{\\varepsilon _0}}}\\hat i$<br>${{\\vec E}_{remaining}} = \\frac{{ - 17\\rho {r_0}}}{{54{\\varepsilon _0}}}\\hat i$ (Left to the point $B$)',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/12_30741_question_1.png'),
(30745,'physics',12,74,286,'A block of mass $m$ and charge $q$ is placed on a smooth horizontal table which terminates in a vertical wall at a distance $d$ from the block. A horizontal electric field $E$ towards right is switched on. Assume elastic collision, find time period of resulting oscillatory motion.','[{"text": "$\\\\sqrt {\\\\frac{{8{\\\\rm{dm}}}}{{{\\\\rm{qE}}}}} $", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\sqrt {\\\\frac{{2{\\\\rm{dm}}}}{{{\\\\rm{qE}}}}} $", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\sqrt {\\\\frac{{4{\\\\rm{dm}}}}{{{\\\\rm{qE}}}}} $", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "None of these", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'The acceleration of the block is<br>$a = \\frac{{qE}}{m}\\,and\\,\\,d = \\frac{1}{2}a{t^2}$ or $t = \\sqrt {\\frac{{2d}}{a}}  = \\sqrt {\\frac{{2dm}}{{qE}}} $<br>Time period<br>$T = 2t = 2\\sqrt {\\frac{{2dm}}{{qE}}}  = \\sqrt {\\frac{{8dm}}{{qE}}} $',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/12_30745_question_1.png'),
(30749,'physics',12,75,287,'A metallic shell has a point charge $\'q\'$ kept inside its cavity. Which one of the following diagrams correctly represents the electric lines of forces?','[{"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Electric field is perpendicular to the  equipotential surface and is zero every where inside the metal.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(30751,'chemistry',70,1261,3076,'What is the sum of locations of axial hydroxyl group in β -D-iodopyranose?','[{"text": "6", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "7", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "8", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "9", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'<img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/70_30751_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />  $\\therefore \\quad$ The sum of locations of axial $-\\mathrm{OH}$ groups<br>$=2+3+4=9$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(30753,'physics',12,75,287,'In the electric field shown in figure, the electric  lines in the left have twice the separation as that  between those on right. If the magnitude of the  field at point $A$ is $40\\,N{C^{ - 1}}$ then the force  experienced by a proton placed at point $B$ is','[{"text": "$6.4 \\\\times {10^{ - 18}}N$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$3.2 \\\\times {10^{ - 18}}N$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$5.0 \\\\times {10^{ - 12}}N$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1.2 \\\\times {10^{ - 18}}N$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'As the field lines do not cross each other the electric flux at $A$ and $B$ are same.<br>$\\left( {{E_A}} \\right)A = \\left( {{E_B}} \\right)2A$<br>${E_A} = 2{E_B} \\Rightarrow {E_B} = 20N{C^{ - 1}}$<br>${F_B} = q{E_B} = 20 \\times 1.6 \\times {10^{ - 19}}N$<br>$ = 3.2 \\times {10^{ - 18}}N$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/12_30753_question_1.png'),
(30757,'physics',12,75,287,'Some equipotential spherical surfaces are  shown. The electric field at a distance  $x$ from  the centre is','[{"text": "$\\\\frac{6}{{{x^2}}}$ perpendicular to the plane of paper", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{{600}}{{{x^2}}}$ perpendicular to the plane of paper", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{6}{{{x^2}}}$ radially", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{{600}}{{{x^2}}}$ radially", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'As the equipotential surfaces are spherical a point charge should be present at the centre. Let $q$ is the point charge<br>The potential at a distance 10 $cm$ is<br>$60V = \\frac{q}{{4\\pi {\\varepsilon _o}(10cm)}}$<br>The field at a distance $x$ from the centre is <br>$E = \\frac{q}{{4\\pi {\\varepsilon _o}{x^2}}} = \\frac{6}{{{x^2}}}(SI\\,units)$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/12_30757_question_1.png'),
(30759,'physics',12,77,290,'Two electric dipoles of moment $p$ and 27$p$ are  placed in opposite direction on a line at a distance  of 10 $cm$. The electric field will be zero at point  between the dipoles whose distance from the  dipole of moment $p$ is','[{"text": "$2.5\\\\,cm$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$5\\\\,cm$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$10\\\\,cm$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$4cm$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Suppose neutral point $N$ lies at a distance $x$  from dipole of moment $p$ or at a distance<br> $(10 - x)$ from dipole of 27 $p$<br>At $N,{E_1} = {E_2}$<br>$ \\Rightarrow \\frac{1}{{4\\pi {\\varepsilon _0}}} \\cdot \\frac{{2p}}{{{x^3}}} = \\frac{1}{{4\\pi {\\varepsilon _0}}} \\cdot \\frac{{2\\left( {27\\,p} \\right)}}{{{{\\left( {10 - x} \\right)}^3}}}$<br>$ \\Rightarrow \\frac{1}{{{x^3}}} = \\frac{{27}}{{{{\\left( {10 - x} \\right)}^3}}} \\Rightarrow x = 2.5\\,\\,cm$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(30761,'chemistry',70,1251,3049,'Consider an amylase chain of 4000 glucose units. How many cleavage are required to lower the average length to 400 units?','[{"text": "9", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "7", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "5", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "3", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Number of cleavage required<br>$=\\dfrac{4000}{400}-1=10-1=9$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(30763,'physics',12,77,290,'Electric charges $2q,2q, - 4q$ are placed at the corners of an equilateral $\\Delta \\,ABC$ of side $l$. The magnitude of electric dipole moment of the system is','[{"text": "$\\\\,ql$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\sqrt 3 \\\\,ql$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2\\\\sqrt 3 ql$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\sqrt 2 \\\\,ql$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'The dipole moments of the combination is shown in the following figure<br>The charge $ - 4q$ can be considered as a combination of two charges each of charge $ - 2q$. So $ + 2q$ forms dipole with $ - 2q$ . So we have two dipoles.<br>Net dipole moment, i.e.,<br>${p_{net}} = \\sqrt {{p^2} + {p^2} + 2pp\\,\\,\\cos \\,\\,60^\\circ } = \\sqrt 3 p$<br>$ = \\sqrt 3 \\left( {2ql} \\right) = 2\\sqrt 3 ql\\left( {p = ql} \\right)$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(30767,'physics',12,77,293,'Three small dipoles are arranged as shown below. What will be the net electric field at  $O\\left( {k = \\frac{1}{{4\\pi {\\varepsilon _0}}}} \\right)$','[{"text": "$\\\\frac{{8kp}}{{{x^3}}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{{kp}}{{{x^3}}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{{\\\\sqrt 2 kp}}{{{x^3}}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Zero", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Point $O$ lies at axial positions of dipole 1 and 2 and equatorial position of dipole 3.<br>Hence field at $O$ is<br>${E_1} = \\frac{{k2p}}{{{x^3}}}$ (towards right) due to dipole 1<br>${E_2} = \\frac{{k2p}}{{{x^3}}}$ (towards right) due to dipole 2<br>${E_3} = \\frac{{k\\left( {4p} \\right)}}{{{x^3}}}$ (towards left) due to dipole 3<br>The net field at $P$ is zero.',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/12_30767_question_1.png'),
(30770,'physics',12,77,293,'A short electric dipole of dipole moment ${\\vec p}$ is placed at a distance $r$ from the centre of a solid metallic sphere of radius $a\\left( { &lt; &lt; r} \\right)$ as shown in the figure. The electric field intensity at the centre of sphere $C$ due to induced charge on the sphere is','[{"text": "${\\\\rm{Zero}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{{2kp}}{{{r^3}}}{\\\\rm{along}}\\\\,\\\\,CO$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{{2kp}}{{{r^3}}}{\\\\rm{along}}\\\\,\\\\,OC$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{{kp}}{{{r^3}}}{\\\\rm{along}}\\\\,\\\\,CO$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Electric field at $C$ due to electric dipole<br>$ = \\frac{1}{{4\\pi {\\varepsilon _0}}}\\frac{{2p}}{{{r^3}}}\\,\\,{\\rm{along}}\\,\\,OC$ $\\left( {{\\rm{since}}\\,\\,r &gt; &gt; a} \\right)$<br>We know that net electric field at $C$ is zero. So electric field at $C$ due to induced charges must be equal and opposite to electric field due to dipole.<br>i.e., ${{\\vec E}_{Dipole}} + {{\\vec E}_{induced\\,\\,ch\\arg e}} = 0\\left( {{\\rm{at}}\\,\\,{\\rm{point}}\\,\\,C} \\right)$<br>$ \\Rightarrow {{\\vec E}_{induced\\,\\,ch\\arg e}} = \\frac{1}{{4\\pi {\\varepsilon _0}}}\\frac{{2p}}{{{r^3}}}\\left( {{\\rm{along}}\\,\\,CO} \\right)$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/12_30770_question_1.png'),
(30774,'physics',12,132,301,'A point charge $Q$ is located just above the centre of the flat face of hemisphere as shown in figure. The electric flux through the flat face and curved face of hemisphere are respectively','[{"text": "$ - \\\\frac{Q}{{{\\\\varepsilon _o}}},\\\\frac{Q}{{{\\\\varepsilon _o}}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$ - \\\\frac{Q}{{2{\\\\varepsilon _o}}},\\\\frac{Q}{{2{\\\\varepsilon _o}}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{Q}{{{\\\\varepsilon _o}}},\\\\frac{Q}{{{\\\\varepsilon _o}}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{Q}{{2{\\\\varepsilon _o}}},\\\\frac{Q}{{2{\\\\varepsilon _o}}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Place another identical hemisphere on given one so that flat faces of two coincide. Then total flux linked with cross-section of bottom half (lower hemisphere) is  $\\frac{Q}{{2{\\varepsilon _o}}}$,as  charge enclosed by given hemisphere is zero,  so flux linked with flat face of hemisphere is $\\frac{{ - Q}}{{2{\\varepsilon _o}}}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/12_30774_question_1.png'),
(30776,'chemistry',70,1247,3035,'Glucose undergoes oxidation reaction in the presence of ${\\mathrm{n}}$-moles of periodic acid forming mixture of formic acid and formaldehyde. The ratio of number of moles of periodic acid required to the number of chiral carbons present in reactant molecule is ____  .','[{"text": "0.5", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "1.25", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "2.5", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "5", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'<img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/70_30776_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' /> Chiral carbon atoms in glucose ${\\mathrm{=4}}$<br>$\\begin{array}{l}\n{\\rm{Ratio}}\\,{\\rm{ = }}\\,\\,\\frac{{\\rm{5}}}{{\\rm{4}}}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{\\rm{(n = 5)}}\\\\\n\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{\\rm{ = 1}}{\\rm{.25}}\n\\end{array}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(30778,'physics',12,132,301,'The electric field in a region is <br>$E = \\frac{{5 \\times {{10}^3}(N{C^{ - 1}}c{m^{ - 1}})x}}{2}\\widehat i$<br>The charge contained inside a cubical volume  bounded by the surfaces <br>$x = 0,x = 2,y = 0,y = 2,z = 0,z = 2$ is (where  $x$,$y$,$z$ are in $cm$)<br>','[{"text": "$1.76 \\\\times {10^{ - 11}}C$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$3.52 \\\\times {10^{ - 11}}C$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2.21 \\\\times {10^{ - 8}}C$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2 \\\\times {10^{ - 10}}C$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'The charge present inside the cube is $q = \\left( {\\phi {\\varepsilon _0}} \\right)$<br>where $\\phi  = \\frac{{5 \\times {{10}^3} \\times 2}}{2} \\times 4 \\times {10^{ - 4}} = 2\\,N.{m^2}{C^{ - 1}}$<br>$q = 2 \\times 8.8 \\times {10^{ - 12}} = 1.76 \\times {10^{ - 11}}C$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(30782,'physics',12,132,301,'Consider an infinite line of charge having uniform linear charge density and passing through the axis of a cylinder. What will be the effect on the flux passing through the curved surface if the portions of the line charge outside the cylinder is removed.','[{"text": "Decreases", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Increases", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Remains same", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Cannot say", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'The net electric field at a point on the  curved surface of the cylinder is the resultant of fields produced by the charges present inside and outside cylinders. If portion outside the cylinder is removed, electric field at points on curved surface will decrease.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/12_30782_question_1.png'),
(30786,'physics',12,80,302,'The adjacent diagram shows a charge +$Q$ held on an insulating support $S$ and enclosed by a hollow spherical conductor. $O$ represents the centre of the spherical conductor and $P$ is a point such that $OP = x\\,{\\rm{and}}\\,SP = r$. The electric field at a point $P$ will be','[{"text": "$\\\\frac{Q}{{4\\\\pi {\\\\varepsilon _0}{r^2}}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{Q}{{4\\\\pi {\\\\varepsilon _0}{x^2}}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Zero", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "None of these", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'The charge present at $S$ induces charges -Q and +Q on the inner and outer surfaces of the spherical shell. The charge on the inner  surface will be distributed unsymmetrically and  on the outer surface symmetrically (due to  shielding effect)<br>Due to the shielding effect by the conducting medium the effect of point charge $Q$ and induced charge -Q is zero outside the shell and also at point $P$. The field at point $P$ is only because of +Q on the outer surface<br>$E = \\frac{Q}{{4\\pi {\\varepsilon _0}{x^2}}}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/12_30786_question_1.png'),
(30788,'physics',12,78,299,'The parallel plane sheets 1 and 2 carry uniform charge densities ${\\sigma _{1\\,}}{\\rm{and}}\\,{\\sigma _2}$ as shown in the figure. The magnitude of the resultant electric field in the region marked II is $({\\sigma _{1\\,}} > \\,{\\sigma _2})$','[{"text": "$\\\\frac{{({\\\\sigma _1}\\\\, + {\\\\sigma _2})\\\\,}}{{2{\\\\varepsilon _0}}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{{ - ({\\\\sigma _1}\\\\,{\\\\sigma _2})\\\\,}}{{2{\\\\varepsilon _0}}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$ - \\\\frac{{({\\\\sigma _1}\\\\, + {\\\\sigma _2})\\\\,}}{{2{\\\\varepsilon _0}}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{{({\\\\sigma _1}\\\\, - {\\\\sigma _2})\\\\,}}{{2{\\\\varepsilon _0}}}$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'${E_{net}} = {E_1} - {E_2}$<br>$ = \\frac{{{\\sigma _1}}}{{2{\\varepsilon _0}}} - \\frac{{{\\sigma _2}}}{{2{\\varepsilon _0}}}$<br>${E_{net}} = \\frac{{({\\sigma _1}\\, - {\\sigma _2})\\,}}{{2{\\varepsilon _0}}}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/12_30788_question_1.png'),
(30790,'chemistry',70,1246,3029,'Aldohexose and aldopentose are optically active carbohydrates. The ratio of number of unsymmetrical carbon atoms present in aldohexose to aldepentose is ____ .','[{"text": "0.75", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "1.5", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "3", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "4.5", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'The open chain structure of Aldopentose and aldohexose  is:  <img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/70_30790_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />  Ratio ${\\mathrm{=\\dfrac{3}{4}=0.75}}$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(30792,'physics',12,70,277,'A non-conducting wall is given a positive net charge. Next, a sheet of flexible rubber with zero net charge is suspended from the ceiling near the charged wall as shown below. The rubber sheet will','[{"text": "Bend away from the wall due to the electrial repulsion between the electrons in the rubber and the charges on the wall", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Bend towards the wall due to the polarisation of the rubber molecules by charged wall", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Not be affected by the charged wall because the rubber sheet has zero net charge", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Not be effected by the charge on the wall since rubber is an insulator", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'The rubber gets polarised and it will get attracted towards the wall.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/12_30792_question_1.png'),
(30796,'physics',12,74,286,'An oil drop, carrying six electronic charges and having a mass of $1.6 \\times {10^{ - 12}}g$ , falls with some terminal velocity in a medium. What magnitude of vertical electric field is required to make the drop move upward with the same speed as it was formerly moving downward with? Ignore buoyancy.','[{"text": "${10^5}N{C^{ - 1}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${10^4}N{C^{ - 1}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$3.3 \\\\times {10^4}N{C^{ - 1}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$3.3 \\\\times {10^5}N{C^{ - 1}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Viscous force $f = 6\\pi nrv$ acts on the oil drop<br>Case (i) $f = mg$       (1)<br>Case (ii) $6eE = f + mg$     (2)<br>From the above two equations<br>$E = \\frac{{mg}}{{3e}} = \\frac{{1.6 \\times {{10}^{ - 15}} \\times 10}}{{3 \\times 1.6 \\times {{10}^{ - 19}}}} = 3.3 \\times {10^4}N{C^{ - 1}}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(30800,'physics',12,70,277,'Four metal conductors having different shapes<br>${\\rm{A}}{\\rm{.}}\\,{\\rm{Sphere}}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{\\rm{B}}{\\rm{.}}\\,{\\rm{Cylinder}}$<br>${\\rm{C}}{\\rm{.}}\\,{\\rm{Pear}}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{\\rm{D}}{\\rm{.}}\\,{\\rm{Lighting}}\\,{\\rm{conductor}}$<br>are mounted on insulting stands and charged.The one which is best suited to retain the charges\nfor a longer time is','[{"text": "${\\\\rm{A}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{D}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{C}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{B}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'In case of spherical metal conductor the charge quickly spreads uniformly over the entire surface because of which charges stay for longer time on the spherical surface. While in case of non-spherical surface, the charge concentration is different at different points due  to which electric sparks gets produced.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(30803,'physics',12,73,282,'Two small spheres each of mass $m$ and with a charge $q$ lie inside a nonconducting smooth hemispherical bowl of radius $R$. Find $q$ if the equilibrium separation between the two charges is $d$.','[{"text": "$\\\\sqrt {\\\\frac{{2mg\\\\pi {\\\\varepsilon _0}{d^3}}}{{\\\\sqrt {{R^2} - \\\\frac{{{d^2}}}{4}} }}} $", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\sqrt {\\\\frac{{mg\\\\pi {\\\\varepsilon _0}{d^3}}}{{\\\\sqrt {{R^2} - {d^2}} }}} $", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\sqrt {\\\\frac{{mg{\\\\varepsilon _0}{d^3}}}{{\\\\sqrt {{R^2} + {d^2}} }}} $", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\sqrt {mg{\\\\varepsilon _0}{d^3}} $", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'The two charges are shown in the figure.<br>By considering the equilibrium of any charge <br>$N\\cos \\theta = \\frac{{{q^2}}}{{4\\pi {\\varepsilon _0}{d^2}}}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,(1)$<br>$N\\sin \\theta = mg\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,(2)$<br>From both the equations<br>${\\mathop{\\rm Tan}\\nolimits} \\theta = \\frac{{mg4\\pi {\\varepsilon _0}{d^2}}}{{{q^2}}} = \\frac{{\\sqrt {{R^2} - \\frac{{{d^2}}}{4}} }}{{d/2}}$&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; $q = \\sqrt {\\frac{{2mg\\pi {\\varepsilon _0}{d^3}}}{{\\sqrt {{R^2} - \\frac{{{d^2}}}{4}} }}} $',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(30804,'chemistry',70,1246,3029,'How many compound which is given below is isomer of D-Glucose?<br>a. D-Mannose, b. D-Fructose, c. D-Glucose,<br> d. D-Indoes, e. D-Galactose, f. D-Arabinose, g. D-Ribose.','[{"text": "4", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "5", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "6", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "8", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'a, b, c, d, e',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(30807,'physics',12,74,283,'A point charge of $100\\,\\mu C$ is placed at $3\\widehat i + 4\\widehat j\\,m$. Find the electric field intensity due to this charge at a point located at $9\\widehat i + 12\\widehat j\\,m$.','[{"text": "$8000\\\\,V{m^{ - 1}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$9000\\\\,V{m^{ - 1}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$2250\\\\,V{m^{ - 1}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$4500\\\\,V{m^{ - 1}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'$E = \\frac{1}{{4\\pi {\\varepsilon _0}}}\\frac{q}{{{{\\left| r \\right|}^2}}}$<br>$\\overrightarrow r  = (9 - 3)\\widehat i + (12 - 4)\\widehat j = 6\\widehat i + 8\\widehat j$<br>$ \\Rightarrow r = \\sqrt {{6^2} + {8^2}}  = 10m$<br>$E = \\frac{{9 \\times {{10}^9} \\times 100 \\times {{10}^{ - 6}}}}{{{{10}^2}}} = 9000\\,V{m^{ - 1}}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(30811,'physics',12,73,280,'Two point charges exert on each other a force $F$ when they are placed $r$ distnce apart in air. If they are placed $R$ distance apart in a medium of dielectric constant $K$, they exert double the  force. The distance $R$ equals','[{"text": "$\\\\frac{r}{{\\\\sqrt {2k} }}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$rk$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{r}{k}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$r\\\\sqrt k $", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'In air medium the force is $F = \\frac{{{q_1}{q_2}}}{{4\\pi {\\varepsilon _0}{r^2}}}$<br>In a medium of dielectric constant $k$, the force is<br>$F\' = \\frac{{{q_1}{q_2}}}{{4\\pi k\\varepsilon {R^2}}}$<br>As,$F\' = 2F \\Rightarrow \\frac{{{q_1}{q_2}}}{{4\\pi k{\\varepsilon _0}{R^2}}} = \\frac{{{q_1}{q_2}}}{{4\\pi {\\varepsilon _0}{r^2}}}$<br>$\\therefore R = \\frac{r}{{\\sqrt {2k} }}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(30815,'physics',12,74,284,'Two charges each of magnitude $Q$ are fixed at  each of two opposite corners of a square. Two  other charges each of magnitude $q$ are placed  at each of the other two corners. If the resultant  electric force on $Q$ is zero, then $Q$ and $q$ are  related as','[{"text": "$Q = 2\\\\sqrt 2 q$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$Q =  - 2\\\\sqrt 2 q$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$Q = \\\\sqrt 2 q$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$Q =  - \\\\sqrt 2 q$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'The charges are placed as shown in the figure.<br>The net force on Q is zero<br>${F_1} = \\left( {{F_2}\\cos 45^\\circ  + {F_3}\\cos 45^\\circ } \\right)$<br>$\\frac{{{Q^2}}}{{4\\pi {\\varepsilon _0}{{\\left( {\\sqrt 2 l} \\right)}^2}}} = 2\\left[ {\\frac{1}{{\\sqrt 2 }}\\frac{{Qq}}{{4\\pi {\\varepsilon _0}{l^2}}}} \\right]$<br>$\\left| Q \\right| = 2\\sqrt 2 q \\Rightarrow Q =  - 2\\sqrt 2 q$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(30818,'physics',12,74,284,'Four point charges are placed at the corners of a square with diagonal 2$a$ as shown. What is the total electric field at the center of the square?','[{"text": "$kq/{a^2}$ at an angle ${45^0}$ above the $ + x$-axis", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$kq/{a^2}$ at an angle ${45^0}$ below the $ - x$-axis", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$4kq/{a^2}$  at an angle ${45^0}$ above the $ - x$-axis", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$4kq/{a^2}$  at an angle ${45^0}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'At center electric field due to both 4$q$ will be canceled. Now net field at center is<br>$E = {E_1} - {E_2}$<br>$ = \\frac{{k2q}}{{{{(a)}^2}}} - \\frac{{kq}}{{{{(a)}^2}}} = \\frac{{kq}}{{{{(a)}^2}}}$<br>This is ${45^0}$ below negative $x$-axis',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/12_30818_question_1.png'),
(30819,'chemistry',70,1246,3029,'A triglyceride can have how many different acyl groups?','[{"text": "1", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "2", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "3", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "4", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'<img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/70_30819_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(30822,'physics',12,78,297,'A charge $Q$ is uniformly distributed in a hollow sphere of radii ${r_1}$ and ${r_2}({r_2} > {r_1})$. The electric  field at a point $P$ distance $x$ from the centre for ${r_1} < x < {r_2}$ is','[{"text": "$\\\\frac{{Q(x)}}{{4\\\\pi {\\\\varepsilon _0}(r_2^3 - r_1^3)}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{{Q({x^3} - r_1^3)}}{{4\\\\pi {\\\\varepsilon _0}(r_2^3 - r_1^3)}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{{Q({x^3} - r_1^3)}}{{4\\\\pi {\\\\varepsilon _0}{x^2}(r_2^3 - r_1^3)}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{{Qr_1^3}}{{4\\\\pi {\\\\varepsilon _3}{x^2}(r_3^3 - r_1^3)}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Consider a gaussian spherical surface of radius $x$.<br>$\\oint {E \\cdot d\\,{\\rm{\\vec A}}}  = \\frac{q}{{{\\varepsilon _0}}}$<br>where $q = \\frac{Q}{{\\frac{4}{3}\\pi \\left( {r_2^3 - r_1^2} \\right)}} \\times \\frac{4}{3}\\pi \\left( {{x^3} - r_1^3} \\right)$<br>$ = \\frac{{Q\\left( {{x^3} - r_1^3} \\right)}}{{\\left( {r_2^3 - r_1^3} \\right)}}$<br>$\\oint {\\overrightarrow E }  \\cdot d{\\rm{\\vec A}} = E4\\pi {x^2} = \\frac{{Q\\left( {{x^3} - r_1^3} \\right)}}{{{\\varepsilon _0}\\left( {r_2^3 - r_1^3} \\right)}}$<br>$ \\Rightarrow \\,E = \\frac{{Q\\left( {{x^3} - r_1^3} \\right)}}{{4\\pi {\\varepsilon _0}{x^2}\\left( {r_2^3 - r_1^3} \\right)}}$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(30826,'physics',12,78,295,'A ring of charge with radius 0.5 $m$ has $0.002\\,\\pi m$ gap. If the ring carries a charge of $ + 1C$, the electric field at the center is','[{"text": "$7.5 \\\\times {10^7}N{C^{ - 1}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$7.2 \\\\times {10^7}N{C^{ - 1}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$6.2 \\\\times {10^7}N{C^{ - 1}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$6.5 \\\\times {10^7}N{C^{ - 1}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'As the filed at the centre is zero for a  uniformly closed ring, so the field at the centre will be equal and opposite to the field produced by the small element.<br>$dq = \\frac{Q}{{2\\pi r}}(0.002\\pi )$<br>$ = \\frac{1}{{2\\pi (0.5)}} \\times \\frac{{2\\pi }}{{1000}} = 2 \\times {10^{ - 3}}{\\rm{C}}$<br>$E = \\frac{{9 \\times {{10}^9} \\times 2 \\times {{10}^{ - 3}}}}{{{{(0.5)}^2}}} = 7.2 \\times {10^7}{\\rm{N}}{{\\rm{C}}^{{\\rm{ - 1}}}}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(30830,'physics',12,78,294,'An infinitely long thin straight wire has uniform  charge density of $\\frac{1}{4} \\times {10^{ - 2}}C{m^{ - 1}}$. What is the  magnitude of electric field at a distance 20 $cm$ from the axis of the wire ?','[{"text": "$2.25 \\\\times {10^8}N{C^{ - 1}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$9 \\\\times {10^8}N{C^{ - 1}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1.12 \\\\times {10^8}N{C^{ - 1}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$4.5 \\\\times {10^8}N{C^{ - 1}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Given $\\lambda  = \\frac{1}{4} \\times {10^{ - 2}}C{m^{ - 1}}$<br>$x = 20\\,cm = 20 \\times {10^{ - 2}}m$<br>From Gauss law, electric field due to an infinitely long wire,<br>$E = \\frac{1}{{2\\pi {\\varepsilon _0}}}\\frac{\\lambda }{x} = \\frac{2}{{4\\pi {\\varepsilon _0}}} \\cdot \\frac{{\\rm{\\lambda }}}{{\\rm{x}}}$<br>$or\\,E = 2 \\times 9 \\times {10^9} \\times \\frac{1}{4} \\times {10^{ - 2}} \\times \\frac{1}{{20 \\times {{10}^{ - 2}}}}$<br>$or\\, E = 0.225 \\times {10^9}$<br>$\\,E = 2.25 \\times {10^8}N{C^{ - 1}}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(30833,'physics',12,78,297,'A spherical portion has been removed from a solid sphere having a charge distributed uniformly in its volume in the figure. The electric field inside the emptied space is','[{"text": "Zero everywhere", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Uniform", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Non-uniform", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Zero only at its center", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Let ${O_1}\\,and\\,{O_2}$ are centres of the sphere and the cavity. Let $P$ is an arbitary point in the spherical cavity.<br>The electric field inside a solid sphere of total charge $Q$ and radius $R$ at a radial $r$ from the centre is<br>$E = \\frac{{Qr}}{{4\\pi {\\varepsilon _0}{R^3}}} = \\frac{{\\rho r}}{{3{\\varepsilon _0}}}\\left( {\\rho = \\frac{Q}{{\\frac{4}{3}\\pi {R^3}}}} \\right)$<br>In vector form $\\overline E = \\frac{{\\rho \\,\\overline r }}{{3{\\varepsilon _0}}}$<br>The electric field at $P$ is given as <br>${\\overline E _{original}} = {\\overline E _{removed}} + {\\overline E _{remaining}} = {\\overline E _{removed}} + {\\overline E _p}$<br>Where ${\\overline E _P}$ - Electric field at point $P$ because of the remaining part.<br>${\\overline E _{original}} = \\frac{{\\rho \\overline {{O_1}P} }}{{3{\\varepsilon _0}}},\\,{\\overline E _{removed}} = \\frac{{\\rho \\overline {{O_2}P} }}{{3{\\varepsilon _0}}}\\,$<br>$ = \\frac{\\rho }{{3{\\varepsilon _0}}}\\left( {\\overline {{O_1}P} - \\overline {{O_2}P} } \\right) = \\frac{{\\rho \\overline {{O_2}{O_2}} }}{{3{\\varepsilon _0}}}$<br>The electric field inside the cavity is uniform and its direction is present along ${O_1}{O_2}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/12_30833_question_1.png'),
(30834,'chemistry',70,1247,3033,'The number of Stereogenic centers in ${\\mathrm{\\alpha}}$-D-Glucose are:','[{"text": "5", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "3", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "4", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "1", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'${\\mathrm{\\alpha}}$-D-glucose have 5 chiral atoms',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(30837,'physics',12,78,295,'A thin conducting ring of radius $R$ is given a charge + $Q$. The electric field at the centre $O$ of the ring due to the charge on the part $AKB$ of the ring is $E$. The electric field at the centre due to the charge on the part $ACDB$ of the ring is','[{"text": "E along KO", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "E along OK", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "3E along  KO", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "3E along OK", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Net elecrtric field at the centre of thin  conducting ring is zero.<br>${E_{total}} = 0$<br>${E_{AKB}} + {E_{ACDB}} = 0$<br>${E_{ACDB}} =  - {E_{AKB}}$<br>${E_{ACDB}} = E\\,along\\,OK$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/12_30837_question_1.png'),
(30841,'physics',12,74,286,'The figure shows the path of a positively charged particle 1 through a rectangular region of uniform electric field as shown in the figure. What is the direction of electric field and the direction of particles 2, 3 and 4?','[{"text": "Top; down, top, down", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Down; top, down, down", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Top; down, down, top", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Down; top, top, down", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Since,$ + ve$ charged particle moves  upward in electric field, it means the direction electric field is upward. The direction of  particle(2) which is $-ve$ downward.<br>The direction of deflection of particle (3) which is $ + ve$  is upward and direction of particles (4) is downward.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/12_30841_question_1.png'),
(30844,'physics',12,74,286,'There is a uniform electric field of strength ${10^3}V/m$ along $y$-axis. A body of mass $1g$ and ${10^{ - 6}}C$ charge  is projected into the field from origin along the positive $x$-axis with a velocity $10\\,m/s$. Its speed in $m/s$ after 10 $s$ is (Neglect gravitation)','[{"text": "$10\\\\,$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$5\\\\sqrt 2 $", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$10\\\\sqrt 2 $", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$20$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Body moves along the parabolic path. For motion along $y$ - axis: By using $v = u + at$<br>$ \\Rightarrow {v_y} = 0 + \\frac{{QE}}{m}.\\,\\,\\,t = \\frac{{{{10}^{ - 6}} \\times {{10}^3}}}{{{{10}^{ - 3}}}} \\times 10 = 10m/\\sec $<br>For motion along $x$ - axis. Its horizontal velocity remains the same i.e.after 10 sec, horizontal velocity of body ${v_x} = 10\\,m/\\sec $<br>Velocity after 10 sec<br>$v = \\sqrt {v_x^2 + v_y^2}  = 10\\sqrt 2 \\,{\\rm{m}}/\\sec $',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
