(31605,'physics',19,138,316,'The number of significant figures in 0.06900 is','[{"text": "$3$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$5$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$4$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'If the number is less than 1, the zero ($s$) on the right of decimal point and before the first non - zero digits are not significant. In 0.06900, the underlined zeros are not significant. Hence, number of significant figures are four.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(31607,'physics',13,803,2188,'The graph showing the dependence of intensity\nof transmitted light on the angle \\[\\theta \\] between\npolariser and analyser, is<div class="matching-question"></div>','[{"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'According to the law of Malus, when there is a beam of completely plane polarised light is incident on an analyser, the resultant intensity of light (I) transmitted from the analyser varies directly as the square of the cosine of the angle $\\left( \\theta  \\right)$  between planes of transmission of analyser and polariser.<br>i.e, $I \\propto {\\cos ^2}\\theta \\,{\\rm{and}}\\,I = {I_0}{\\cos ^2}\\theta \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,(1)$ <br>  <img src=\'/img/data/physics/13_31607_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' /> <br> Where ${I_0}$ = intensity of the light from polariser<br>From eq. (i), we note that if the transmission axes of polariser and analyser are parallel (i,e., $q = 0^\\circ $or $180^\\circ \\,$) then $I = {I_0}$ . It means that the intensity of transmitted light is maximum. When the axes of polariser and analyser are perpendicular $(ie,\\,\\theta  = 90^\\circ ),$ then $I = 0$.',NULL,'Medium','Graph/Figure',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(31608,'physics',18,122,437,'In the achromatic prism, we have','[{"text": "Deviation without dispersion", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Dispersion without deviation", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Refraction without deviation", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Deviation and dispersion", "isCorrect": false}]',0,NULL,NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(31609,'physics',19,138,316,'Which of the following numerical values have three significant figures?','[{"text": "$0.030$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$3.033$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$30.30$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$0.300$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Options (2) and (3) have four significant figures; (1) has two and (4) has three significant figures.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(31610,'physics',12,74,285,'A point charge of $10\\,\\mu C$ is placed at the origin. At what location on the $X$-axis should a point charge of $40\\,\\mu C$ be placed so that the net electric field is zero at $x = 2\\,cm$ on the $X$-axis?','[{"text": "$x =  - 4\\\\,cm$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$x =  4\\\\,cm$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$x =  8\\\\,cm$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$x = 6\\\\,cm$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Given, $E_{1}=E_{2}$ at $x = 2\\,cm$ from zero on <br>$x$-axis. <img src=\'/img/data/physics/12_31610_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' /> $E=\\dfrac{K q}{r^{2}}$<br>$\\dfrac{K \\cdot q_{1}}{(2)^{2}}=\\dfrac{K \\cdot q_{2}}{(x-2)^{2}} ; \\dfrac{10}{4}=\\dfrac{40}{(x-2)^{2}}$<br>${(x - 2)^2} = 16 \\Rightarrow x - 2 = 4$<br>$\\therefore x = 6\\,cm$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2023,'Jee','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(31611,'physics',13,803,2188,'Which of the following diagrams represent the variation of electric field vector with time for a circularly polarised light?<div class="matching-question"></div>','[{"text": "", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'A circularly polarised light consists of two sinusoidal plane waves of equal amplitudes and differ by a phase $90^\\circ $.<br>${E_x} = A\\sin \\omega t,\\,\\,{E_y} = A\\cos \\omega t$<br>Resultant amplitude<br>${\\left| {\\overrightarrow E } \\right|^2} = {A^2} + {A^2} + 2A.A\\cos \\frac{\\pi }{2}$<br>$ \\Rightarrow {\\left| {\\overrightarrow E } \\right|^2} = A\\sqrt 2 $<br>= constant.',NULL,'Hard','Graph/Figure',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(31612,'physics',18,122,435,'If a small angled prism, made of glass is immersed in a liquid of refractive index 1 and a ray of light is made incident on it, then','[{"text": "Its deviation will be zero", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "It will suffer total reflection", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "The emergent  ray is bent towards the edge of the prism", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "The emergent ray is bent towards the base of prism", "isCorrect": true}]',3,NULL,NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(31613,'physics',19,138,316,'More the number of significant figures shows more the','[{"text": "Accuracy", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Error", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Number of figures", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Value", "isCorrect": false}]',0,NULL,NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(31614,'physics',12,132,301,'In a cuboid of dimension $2 L \\times 2 L \\times L$, a charge $q$ is placed at the center of the surface \'$S$\' having area of $4 L^{2}$. The flux through the opposite surface to \'$S$\' is given by','[{"text": "$\\\\dfrac{q}{12 \\\\varepsilon_{0}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{q}{2 \\\\varepsilon_{0}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{q}{3 \\\\varepsilon_{0}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{q}{6 \\\\varepsilon_{0}}$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'<img src=\'/img/data/physics/12_31614_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' /> $P$ is the centre of the larger cuboid of size $2 L \\times 2 L \\times 2 L\\, P$ is also the centre of the surface $S$.<br>By Gauss\'s law in electrostatics<br>$\\phi=\\dfrac{q}{\\varepsilon_{0}}$ (for larger cuboid)<br>For opposite surface of $S$<br>$\\phi=\\dfrac{q}{6 \\varepsilon_{0}}$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',2023,'Jee','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(31615,'physics',13,800,2180,'Consider sunlight incident on a slit of width ${10^4}\\,\\mathop A\\limits^o $. The image seen through the slit shall','[{"text": "A bright slit white at the centre diffusing to zero intensities at the edges", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Be a fine sharp slit white in colour at the centre", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Only be a diffused slit white in colour", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "A bright slit white at the centre diffusing to regions of different colours", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Here, the width of the slit is $10000{\\rm{ }}\\mathop A\\limits^ \\circ  $. The wavelength of (visible) sunlight varies from$4000\\mathop A\\limits^0 \\,{\\rm{to}}\\,8000\\mathop A\\limits^o $. As width of slit $a > \\lambda $.<br>(wavelength of light), diffraction takes place and maxima will be formed at the centre. At the centre all the colours mixing take place and a white patch will be present at the centre. As the width of the slit is close to the wavelength the secondary bright colour’s will not be observed, therefore no diffraction occurs. The image seen through the slit shall be a fine sharp slit white in colour at the centre.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,'NCERT Exemplar','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(31616,'physics',18,122,437,'Which of the following diagrams shows correctly the dispersion of white light by a prism?','[{"text": "", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Because in dispersion of white light, the rays of different colours are not parallel to each other. Also deviation takes place in same direction.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(31617,'physics',19,138,316,'If  a measured quantity has $n$ significant figures, the reliable digits in it are','[{"text": "$n$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$n - 1$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$2n$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$n/2$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Reliable digits $ = $ No. of significant figure $ - 1$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(31619,'physics',13,804,2190,'Monochromatic light of wavelength $589\\,nm$ is incident from air on a water surface. What are the wavelength and speed of refracted light? Refractive index of water is 1.33.','[{"text": "$4.42 \\\\times {10^{ - 7}}\\\\,m\\\\,{\\\\rm{and}}\\\\,2.25 \\\\times {10^8}\\\\,m/s$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$1.9 \\\\times {10^{ - 10}}\\\\,m\\\\,{\\\\rm{and}}\\\\,3.2 \\\\times {10^8}\\\\,m/s$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$4.20 \\\\times {10^{ - 8}}\\\\,m\\\\,{\\\\rm{and}}\\\\,4.0 \\\\times {10^7}\\\\,m{\\\\rm{/}}s$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$3.68 \\\\times {10^{ - 9}}\\\\,m\\\\,{\\\\rm{and}}\\\\,3.02 \\\\times {10^8}\\\\,m/s$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'In this process wavelength and speed changes but frequency remains the same.<br>Wavelength of refracted light<br>$\\lambda \' = \\frac{\\lambda }{\\mu } = \\frac{{589 \\times {{10}^{ - 9}}}}{{1.33}} = 4.42 \\times {10^{ - 7}}m$<br>Velocity of refracted light<br>$v = \\frac{c}{\\mu } = \\frac{{3 \\times {{10}^8}}}{{1.33}} = 2.25 \\times {10^8}m/s$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,'NCERT Exemplar','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(31620,'physics',18,122,435,'A ray of light is incident on an equilateral glass prism placed on a horizontal table. For minimum deviation, which of  the following is true?','[{"text": "${\\\\rm{QR }}$ is horizontal", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{PQ }}$ is horizontal", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Either ${\\\\rm{PQ }}$ or ${\\\\rm{RS}}$ is horizontal", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{RS}}$ is horizontal", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'In minimum deviation position refracted ray inside the prism is parallel to the base of the prism.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/18_31620_question_1.png'),
(31621,'physics',19,138,316,'Two numbers $a = 0.92$ and $b = 0.08$ are given. The number of significant figures present in the result after the following operation $a + b,\\,\\,a - b,\\,\\,a \\times b$ and $a/b$ respectively are','[{"text": "$3,2,2,2{\\\\rm{ }}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$3,3,2,2{\\\\rm{ }}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$3,2,1,1{\\\\rm{ }}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$2,2,2,2{\\\\rm{ }}$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'$a + b = 1.00,\\,a - b = 0.84.$ In case of 	multiplication and division result will contain least number of significant figure. In 0.08 there is only one significant figure.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(31622,'physics',12,133,307,'Electric field in a certain region is given by $\\vec{E}=\\left(\\dfrac{A}{x^{2}} \\hat{i}+\\dfrac{B}{y^{3}} \\hat{j}\\right)$. The SI unit of $A$ and $B$ are','[{"text": "$N{m^2}{C^{ - 1}};N{m^3}{C^{ - 1}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$N{m^3}C;N{m^2}C$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$N{m^2}C;N{m^3}C$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$N{m^3}{C^{ - 1}};N{m^2}{C^{ - 1}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Since, $\\vec{E}=\\dfrac{\\vec{F}}{q},\\left(\\dfrac{N}{C}\\right)$ units<br>Now $\\left[\\dfrac{A}{x^{2}}\\right]=\\left[\\dfrac{B}{y^{3}}\\right]=[E]$<br>Therefore, SI unit of $A$ is $N m^{2} C^{-1}$ and $S I$ unit of $B$ is $N{m^3}{C^{ - 1}}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2023,'Jee','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(31623,'physics',13,799,2176,'In a Young’s double slit experiment, the source is white light. One of the holes is covered by a red filter and another by a blue filter. In this case','[{"text": "There shall be an interference pattern for red distinct from that for blue", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "There shall be alternative interference patterns of red and blue", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "There shall be an interference pattern for red mixing with one for blue", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "There shall be no interference fringes", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'As light from two slits of $YDSE$ is of different colours/wavelength/frequencies, therefore, there shall be no interference fringes.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,'NCERT Exemplar','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(31624,'physics',18,122,434,'A triangular prism of glass is shown in the figure. A ray incident normally to one face is totally reflected, if ${\\rm{\\theta }} = {45^0}.$ The index of refraction of glass is','[{"text": "Greater than 1.41", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Less than 1.41", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Equal to 1.41", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "None of these", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'For total internal reflection $\\theta &gt; C$<br>$ \\Rightarrow \\sin \\theta &gt; \\sin C \\Rightarrow \\sin \\theta &gt; \\frac{1}{\\mu }$<br>or<br>$\\mu &gt; \\frac{1}{{\\sin \\theta }} \\Rightarrow \\mu &gt; \\frac{1}{{\\sin {{45}^0}}} \\Rightarrow \\mu &gt; \\sqrt 2 \\Rightarrow \\mu &gt; 1.41$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/18_31624_question_1.png'),
(31625,'physics',19,138,316,'If $L = 2.331\\,cm$, $B = 2.1\\,cm$, then $L + B = $','[{"text": "$4.4\\\\,\\\\,cm$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$4.43\\\\,\\\\,cm$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$4\\\\,\\\\,cm$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$4.431\\\\,\\\\,cm$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Given, $L = 2.331\\,cm$<br>$ = 2.33$ (correct up to two decimal places) and $B = 2.1\\,cm = 2.10\\,cm$<br>$\\therefore \\,L + B = 2.33 + 2.10 = 4.43\\,cm = 4.4\\,cm$<br>Since minimum significant figure is 2.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(31626,'physics',12,78,297,'As shown in the figure, a point charge $Q$ is placed at the centre of conducting spherical shell of inner radius $a$ and outer radius $b$. The electric field due to charge $Q$ in three different regions  $I,$  $II$  and  $III$  is given by<br>  ${\\rm{ }}(I:{\\rm{ }}r{\\rm{ }} < {\\rm{ }}a,{\\rm{ }}II:{\\rm{ }}a{\\rm{ }} < {\\rm{ }}r{\\rm{ }} < {\\rm{ }}b,{\\rm{ }}III{\\rm{ }}:{\\rm{ }}r > b\\,)$','[{"text": "$E_{I}=0, E_{I I}=0, E_{I I I}=0$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$E_{I} \\\\neq 0, E_{I I}=0, E_{I I I}=0$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$E_{I}=0, E_{I I}=0, E_{I I I} \\\\neq 0$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$E_{I} \\\\neq 0, E_{I I}=0, E_{I I I} \\\\neq 0$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'In regron -$  I$., electric field is produced by the point charge $Q$ at the center.<br>Regron - ${\\text{ }}II$ is metal medium where electric field is zero. In region - $III$  electric field is produced by the induced charge on the outer surface of the shell.',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',2023,'Jee','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/12_31626_question_1.png'),
(31627,'physics',13,796,2166,'For light diverging from a point source','[{"text": "The intensity at the wave front does not depend on the distance", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "The wavefront is parabolic", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "The wavefront is plane", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "The intensity dereasing in proportion to the distance squared", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'For light diverging from a point source, the wavefront is sphericaal in shape. The intensity varies inversely $\\left( {I\\alpha \\frac{1}{{4\\pi {r^2}}}} \\right)$ i.e., intensity decreases in proportion to the distance squared.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,'NCERT Exemplar','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(31628,'physics',18,122,435,'Three prisms 1,2 and 3 have the prism angle $A = 60^\\circ ,$  but their refractive indices are respectively 1.4, 1.5  and 1.6. If ${\\delta _1},{\\delta _2},{\\delta _3}$  be their respective angles of deviation then','[{"text": "${\\\\delta _1} > {\\\\delta _2} > {\\\\delta _3}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\delta _3} > {\\\\delta _2} > {\\\\delta _1}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${\\\\delta _2} > {\\\\delta _1} > {\\\\delta _3}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\delta _1} = {\\\\delta _2} = {\\\\delta _3}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'$\\delta  \\propto \\left( {\\mu  - 1} \\right)$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(31632,'physics',18,122,435,'Angle of minimum deviation for a prism of refractive index 1.5  is equal to the angle of prism. Then the angle of prism is $\\left( {\\sin {{48}^0}36\' = 0.75} \\right)$','[{"text": "$80^\\\\circ $", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$41^\\\\circ 24\'$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$82^\\\\circ 48\'$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$A = 60^\\\\circ $", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'$\\mu  = \\frac{{\\sin \\frac{{(A + \\delta )}}{2}}}{{\\sin \\frac{A}{2}}}$<br>Given that $\\delta  = A$<br>$\\frac{3}{2} = \\frac{{2\\sin \\frac{A}{2}\\cos \\frac{A}{2}}}{{\\sin \\frac{A}{2}}} \\Rightarrow \\cos \\frac{A}{2} = \\frac{3}{4}$<br>$A = 2{\\cos ^{ - 1}}\\left( {\\frac{3}{4}} \\right) = 82^\\circ \\,42\'$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(31633,'physics',12,78,298,'Graphical variation of electric field due to a uniformly charged insulating solid sphere of radius \\(R\\), with distance \\(r\\) from the centre \\(O\\) is represented by<div class="matching-question"></div>','[{"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'As inside the sphere, $E \\propto r$ and outside the sphere, $E \\propto \\dfrac{1}{r^{2}}$<br>The correct depiction of graph of $E$ versus $r$ is shown below. <img src=\'/img/data/physics/12_31633_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />',NULL,'Medium','Graph/Figure',2023,'Jee','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/12_31633_question_1.png'),
(31634,'physics',19,138,316,'The radius of a sphere is 1.41 $cm$. Its volume to an appropriate number of significant figures is','[{"text": "$11.73\\\\,c{m^3}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$117\\\\,c{m^3}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$11.736\\\\,c{m^3}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$11.7\\\\,c{m^3}$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Radius of the sphere, $r = 1.41\\,cm$ (3 significant figures) Volume of the sphere, <br>$V = \\frac{4}{3}\\pi {r^3} = \\frac{4}{3} \\times 3.14 \\times {\\left( {1.41} \\right)^3}$<br>$ = 11.736\\,c{m^3}$<br>Round off up to 3 significant figure $ = 11.7\\,c{m^3}$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(31635,'physics',13,799,2175,'In a double-slit experiment the angular width of a fringe is found to be $0.2^\\circ $ on a screen placed ${\\rm{ }}1{\\rm{ }}m$  away. The wavelength of light used is $600\\,nm$.What will be the angular width of the fringe if the entire experimental apparatus is immersed in water? Take refractive index of water to be $4/3$.','[{"text": "$0.30^\\\\circ $", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$0.15^\\\\circ $", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$0.45^\\\\circ $", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$0.27^\\\\circ $", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Using the formula of angular width<br>$\\theta  = \\frac{\\lambda }{d}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,(1)$<br> and $\\theta \' = \\frac{{\\lambda \'}}{d}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,(2)$<br>where,  $\\lambda \' = \\frac{\\lambda }{\\mu }$<br>Dividing Eq.(2) from Eq.(1), we get<br>$\\frac{{\\theta \'}}{\\theta } = \\frac{{\\lambda \'}}{\\lambda } = \\frac{\\lambda }{{\\mu \\lambda }}$<br>$ \\Rightarrow \\theta \' = \\frac{\\theta }{\\mu } = \\frac{{0.2 \\times 3}}{4} = 0.15^\\circ {\\text{ }}\\,\\,\\,{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} \\,\\,\\,\\,\\left( {\\because \\mu  = \\frac{4}{3}} \\right)$ <br> Thus, the angular fringe width is $0.15^\\circ $ as the apparatus is immersed in water.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,'NCERT Exemplar','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(31636,'physics',18,122,435,'When a ray of light is incident normally on one refracting surface of an equilateral prism (Refractive index of the material of the prism $ = {\\rm{ }}1.5)$','[{"text": "Emerging ray is deviated by $45^\\\\circ $", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "The ray undergoes total internal reflection at the second refracting surface", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Emerging ray is deviated by $30^\\\\circ $", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Emerging ray just grazes the second refracting surface", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Critical angle for the material of prism $C = {\\sin ^{ - 1}}\\left( {\\frac{1}{\\mu }} \\right) = {\\sin ^{ - 1}}\\left( {\\frac{1}{{1.5}}} \\right) = 42^\\circ $ since angle of incidence at surface $AB\\left( {60^\\circ } \\right)$ is more than the critical angle $\\left( {42^\\circ } \\right)$ so total internal reflection takes place.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(31638,'physics',19,138,316,'What is the number of significant figures in $9.062 \\times {10^{20}}$ ?','[{"text": "$2$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$4$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$3$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,NULL,NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(31639,'physics',13,799,2172,'In double-slit experiment using light of wavelength $600\\,nm$  the angular width of a fringe formed on a distant screen is $0.1^\\circ $. What is the spacing between the two slits?','[{"text": "$3.03 \\\\times {10^{ - 4}}{\\\\rm{ }}m$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$3.44 \\\\times {10^{ - 4}}{\\\\rm{ }}m$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$2.68 \\\\times {10^{ - 4}}{\\\\rm{ }}m$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$4.03 \\\\times {10^{ - 4}}{\\\\rm{ }}m$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Given, $\\lambda  = 600\\,nm = 600 \\times {10^{ - 9}}m$<br>Angular width of fringe $\\theta  = 0.1^\\circ  = \\frac{{0.1\\pi }}{{180}}\\,rad$<br>Using the formula $\\theta  = \\frac{\\lambda }{d}$<br>Spacing between the slits<br>$d = \\frac{\\lambda }{\\theta } = \\frac{{600 \\times {{10}^{ - 9}} \\times 180}}{{0.1 \\times \\pi }}$<br>$d = 3.44 \\times {10^{ - 4}}\\,m$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,'NCERT Exemplar','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(31640,'physics',18,121,430,'Two lens of power $15\\,D$ and $ - 3\\,D$ are placed in contact. The focal length of the combination  is','[{"text": "$12\\\\,cm$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$10\\\\,cm$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$18\\\\,cm$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$8.33\\\\,cm$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Given,${p_1} = 15D$ and ${p_2} =  - 3D$<br>$p = {p_1} + {p_2} = 15D - 3D = 12D$<br>$p = 12 = \\frac{1}{f}$<br>${f = \\frac{1}{{12}}m = 8.33\\,cm}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(31652,'physics',18,121,430,'How many images are formed by the lens shown, if an object is kept on its axis?','[{"text": "2", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "1", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "4", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "3", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Only one converging point is found by this lens. Therefore, only one image is formed.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/18_31652_question_1.png'),
(31642,'physics',19,139,317,'Which of the following is a dimensional constant?','[{"text": "Refractive index", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Dielectric constant", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Relative density", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Gravitational constant", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Gravitational constant $G$ has a constant value and dimensions. In $SI$ system value of gravitational constant $G$ is  $6.6 \\times {10^{ - 11}}N{m^2}k{g^{ - 2}}.$ Dimensional formula of $G$ is  $\\left[ {{M^{ - 1}}{L^3}{T^{ - 2}}} \\right]$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(31643,'physics',13,804,2190,'According to electromagnetic wave theory of light following phenomenon can be explained.<br>I. propagation of light in vaccum<br>II. interference of light<br>III. polarisation of light<br>IV. photoelectric effect','[{"text": "I, II and III", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "I, II, and IV", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "I, III and IV", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "II, III and IV", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Except photoelectric effect, all others phenomenon such as propagation of light in vacuum, interference and polarisation of light can be explained by wave theory of light. In photoelectric effect light behaves as it is made up of particles.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(31644,'physics',18,121,432,'A convex lens of focal length $12\\,cm$ is made of glass of $\\mu  = \\frac{3}{2}.$  What will be its focal length when immersed in liquid of $\\mu  = \\frac{5}{4}?$','[{"text": "$12\\\\,cm$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$6\\\\,cm$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$30\\\\,cm$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$24\\\\,cm$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'$\\frac{{{f_2}}}{{{f_1}}} = \\frac{{\\left( {{\\mu _g} - 1} \\right)}}{{\\left( {\\frac{{{\\mu _g}}}{{{\\mu _l}}} - 1} \\right)}} = \\frac{{\\left( {\\frac{3}{2} - 1} \\right)}}{{\\left( {\\frac{{3/2}}{{5/4}} - 1} \\right)}} = \\frac{5}{2}$<br>$\\therefore {f_2} = {f_1}\\left( {\\frac{5}{2}} \\right) = \\frac{{12 \\times 5}}{2} = 30cm$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(31645,'physics',12,80,304,'Let $\\sigma$ be the uniform surface charge density of two infinite thin plane sheets shown in figure. Then the electric fields in three different region $E_{I}, E_{I I}$ and $E_{I I I}$ are','[{"text": "$\\\\vec{E}_{I}=0, \\\\vec{E}_{I I}=\\\\dfrac{\\\\sigma}{\\\\epsilon_{0}} \\\\hat{n}, \\\\vec{E}_{I I I}=0$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\vec{E}_{I}=\\\\dfrac{2 \\\\sigma}{\\\\epsilon_{0}} \\\\hat{n}, \\\\vec{E}_{I I}=0, \\\\vec{E}_{I I I}=\\\\dfrac{2 \\\\sigma}{\\\\epsilon_{0}} \\\\hat{n}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\vec{E}_{I}=-\\\\dfrac{\\\\sigma}{\\\\epsilon_{0}} \\\\hat{n}, \\\\vec{E}_{I I}=0, \\\\vec{E}_{I I I}=\\\\dfrac{\\\\sigma}{\\\\epsilon_{0}} \\\\hat{n}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\vec{E}_{I}=\\\\dfrac{\\\\sigma}{2 \\\\epsilon_{0}} \\\\hat{n}, \\\\vec{E}_{I I}=0, \\\\vec{E}_{I I I}=\\\\dfrac{\\\\sigma}{2 \\\\epsilon_{0}} \\\\hat{n}$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'$\\vec{E}_{I}=\\left(\\dfrac{-\\sigma}{2 \\epsilon_{0}}-\\dfrac{\\sigma}{2 \\epsilon_{0}}\\right) \\hat{n}=-\\dfrac{\\sigma}{\\epsilon_{0}} \\hat{n}$<br>$\\begin{aligned}& \\vec{E}_{I I}=\\dfrac{\\sigma}{2 \\epsilon_{0}} \\hat{n}+\\dfrac{\\sigma}{2 \\epsilon_{0}}(-\\hat{n})=0 \\\\& \\vec{E}_{I I I}=\\left(\\dfrac{\\sigma}{2 \\epsilon_{0}}+\\dfrac{\\sigma}{2 \\epsilon_{0}}\\right) \\hat{n}=\\dfrac{\\sigma}{\\epsilon_{0}} \\hat{n}\\end{aligned}$ <img src=\'/img/data/physics/12_31645_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',2023,'Jee','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/12_31645_question_1.png'),
(31646,'physics',19,139,318,'If $x$ times momentum is work, then the dimensional formula of $x$ is','[{"text": "$[{L^{ - 1}}T]$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$[L{T^{ - 1}}]$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$[M{L^{ - 1}}{T^{ - 1}}]$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$[M{L^1}{T^1}]$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'$[x] = \\frac{{[W]}}{{[P]}} = \\left[ {\\frac{{M{L^2}{T^{ - 2}}}}{{ML{T^{ - 1}}}}} \\right] = L{T^{ - 1}}$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(31647,'physics',13,796,2166,'Which of the following statement (s) is/are correct?<br>I. A point source emits waves uniformly in all directions.<br>II. The wave fronts emitted by a point source are spherical in shape.<br>III. At a large distance from a point source, a small portion of sphere can be considered as plane wave.','[{"text": "Only I", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both I and II", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Only III", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "I, II and III", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'For a point emitting waves uniformly in all directions, the locus of points which have the same amplitude and vibrate in the same phase are spheres. But at a large distance from the source, some portion of the sphere can be considered as plane wave. A point source emmits spherical wave fronts. So option (4) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ type-2',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(31648,'physics',18,121,428,'Match the following<div class="matching-question"><div class="column-left">A. Lens of power${+ 1.0}\\,D$<br>B. Lens of combination of power $ + 0.5\\,D$ and $ + 0.5\\,D$ <br>C. Lens of power $ - \\,2.5\\,D$ <br>D. Lenses combination of power $ - 4\\,D$ and $ + 2\\,D$</div><div class="column-right">P. Convex lens of focal length $400\\,cm$<br>Q . Convex lens of focal length $25\\,cm$ <br>R. Convex lens of focal length $100\\,cm$<br>S. Concave lens of focal length $40\\,cm$</div></div>','[{"text": "A - P,B - Q,C - R,D - S", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "A - R,B - P,C - S,\\tD - Q", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "A - S,  B - R,   C - P,D - Q", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "A - R, B - S,  C - Q,D - P", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'$\\left( {A - R} \\right)F = \\frac{1}{P} = \\frac{1}{1} =  + 100\\,cm$<br>$\\left( {B - P} \\right)\\frac{1}{F} = \\frac{1}{{0.5}} + \\frac{1}{{0.5}} =  + 400\\,cm$<br>${\\left( {C - S} \\right)F =  - \\frac{1}{{2.5}} =  - 0.4 =  - 40\\,cm}$<br>$\\left( {D - Q} \\right)\\frac{1}{F} = \\frac{1}{2} - \\frac{1}{4} = 0.25 = 25\\,cm$<br>Option (2) is correct',NULL,'Medium','Match the following',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(31650,'physics',19,139,317,'Dimensional formula for angular momentum is','[{"text": "${M^0}{L^2}{T^{ - 2}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$M{L^2}{T^{ - 2}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$ML{T^{ - 1}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$M{L^2}{T^{ - 1}}$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Angular momentum $ = mvr$<br>$ = [ML{T^{ - 1}}][L] = [M{L^2}{T^{ - 1}}].$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(31651,'physics',13,798,2169,'Let  ${S_1}\\,{\\rm{and}}\\,{S_2}$ be two sources as shown in the figure.<br>Which of the following statement  $(s)$  is/are correct?<br>I. ${S_2}P - {S_1}P = 3\\lambda $<br>II. Waves from  ${S_1}$  arrives exactly three cycles earlier than waves from ${S_2}$<br>III. At $P$ waves from ${S_1}{\\rm{ and }}{S_2}$ are in phase.','[{"text": "I, II and III", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "I and III", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "II and III", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "I and II", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'It is given that ${S_1}P = 7\\lambda \\,{\\rm{and}}\\,{S_2}P = 10\\lambda $ <br>We have, ${S_2}P - {S_1}P = 10\\lambda  - 7\\lambda $<br>$ \\Rightarrow {S_2}P - {S_1}P = 3\\lambda $ <br> The waves emanating from ${S_1}$ will arrive exactly three cycles earlier than the waves from ${S_2}$ and will again be in phase. So options (1) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ type-2',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/13_31651_question_1.png'),
(31655,'physics',13,798,2168,'The conditions for producing sustained interference are<br>I. Phase difference between interferring waves remains constant with time.<br>II. interferring waves are of same frequency.<br>III. interferring waves are moving in opposite directions.','[{"text": "I and II", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "II and III", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "I and III", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "I, II and III", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'When interferring sources have same frequency and their phase difference remains constant with time, interference is sustained (stayed for a finite time interval). So Option (1) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(31656,'physics',18,121,427,'Optic axis of a thin equiconvex lens is the${\\rm{ x}}$-axis. The co-ordinates of a point object and its image are $( - 40cm,{\\rm{ }}1cm)$ and $(50cm,{\\rm{ }} - 2cm)$ respectively. Lens is located at :-','[{"text": "$x =  - 30\\\\,cm$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$x =  + 20\\\\,cm$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$x = 0$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$x =  - 10\\\\,cm$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'The object and image are shown in the figure <img src=\'/img/data/physics/18_31656_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' /> From the figure $m = \\frac{{h\'}}{h} =  - 2 = \\frac{{\\left| v \\right|}}{{ - \\left| u \\right|}}$<br>$ \\Rightarrow \\left| v \\right| = 2\\left| u \\right|$<br>${\\left| v \\right| + \\left| u \\right| = 90cm \\Rightarrow \\left| u \\right| = 30\\,cm}$<br>So the lens should be at $ - 10\\,cm$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(31658,'physics',19,139,317,'The dimensional formula for pressure gradient is','[{"text": "$[M{L^{ - 1}}{T^{ - 2}}]$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$[{M^1}{L^{ - 2}}{T^{ - 2}}]$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$[{M^1}{L^2}{T^{ - 2}}]$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$[{M^1}{L^{ - 1}}{T^{ - 3}}]$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'$\\left[ {\\frac{{dp}}{{dx}}} \\right] = \\frac{{[ML{T^{ - 2}}.{L^{ - 2}}]}}{{[L]}} = [M{L^{ - 2}}{T^{ - 2}}].$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(31659,'physics',13,800,2180,'Which of the given statements is/are correct for phenomenon of diffraction?<br>I. For diffraction through a single-slit, the wavelength of wave must be comparable to the size of slit.<br> II. The diffraction is possible  in sound waves .<br>III. Diffraction is only observed in electromagnetic waves.','[{"text": "Only I", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "II and III", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "I and II", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "I, II and III", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'I. For diffraction pattern, the size of slit should be comparable, to the wavelength of wave used.<br>II. Diffraction phenomenon is commonly observed in longitudinal wave because wavelength of sound waves is large  $(0.1 - 1m).$<br>III. Diffraction is observed in electromagnetic and sound  waves as well. So option (3) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ type-2',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(31660,'physics',18,121,430,'A layered lens as shown in figure is made of two types of transparent materials indicated by different shades. A point object is placed on its axis. The object will form','[{"text": "3 image", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "9 image", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "1 image", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "2 image", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Two converging points will be there for the lens',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/18_31660_question_1.png'),
(31662,'physics',19,139,317,'The dimensions of electromotive force in terms of current $A$ are','[{"text": "$[M{T^{ - 2}}{A^{ - 2}}]$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$[M{L^2}{T^{ - 2}}{A^2}]$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$[M{L^2}{T^{ - 2}}{A^{ - 2}}]$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$[M{L^2}{T^{ - 3}}{A^{ - 1}}]$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Electromotive force $=$ Potential difference<br>$V = \\frac{W}{q} = \\frac{{\\left[ {M{L^2}{T^{ - 2}}} \\right]}}{{\\left[ {AT} \\right]}} = [M{L^2}{T^{ - 3}}{A^{ - 1}}]$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(31663,'physics',13,799,2177,'<div class="matching-question"><div class="column-left">In $YDSE$ number of bright fringe or dark fringe can  be limited.</div><div class="column-right">In $YDSE$ path difference between the superposing waves can not be more than the distance between the slits.</div></div>','[{"text": "Statement A is correct but Statement B is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Statement A is incorrect but Statement B is correct.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Statements are correct.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Statements are incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'If $d\\sin \\theta  > \\lambda  \\Rightarrow \\sin \\theta  > 1$ which is not possible. Number of fringes in $YDSE$ are limited and the number depends on the value of $d$.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ type-2',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(31664,'physics',18,121,432,'If a convex lens is dipped in a liquid whose refractive index is equal to the refractive index of the lens, then lens act like a','[{"text": "Concave lens", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Plane parallel glass plate", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Plano convex lens", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Plano concave lens", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'In liquid medium <br>$\\frac{1}{f} = \\left( {\\frac{{{\\mu _g}}}{{{\\mu _l}}} - 1} \\right)\\left( {\\frac{1}{{{R_1}}} - \\frac{1}{{{R_2}}}} \\right)$<br> As ${\\mu _g} = {\\mu _l},\\,\\,\\frac{1}{f} = 0 \\Rightarrow f = \\infty $<br>For a glass plate $f = \\infty $',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(31666,'physics',19,139,318,'Suppose $A = {B^n}{C^m}$ , where $A$ has dimensions $LT$, $B$ has dimensions ${L^2}{T^{ - 1}}$, and $C$ has dimensions $L{T^2}$. Then find the values of  exponents $n$ and $m$ :','[{"text": "$\\\\frac{2}{5},\\\\frac{4}{5}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{2}{5},\\\\frac{3}{5}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{3}{5},\\\\frac{1}{5}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{1}{5},\\\\frac{3}{5}$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'$LT = {\\left[ {{L^2}{T^{ - 1}}} \\right]^n}{\\left[ {L{T^2}} \\right]^m}$<br>$LT = {L^{2n + m}}{T^{2m - n}}$<br>$2n + m = 1\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,(1)$<br>$ - n + 2m = 1\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,(2)$<br>On solving  $n = \\frac{1}{5},m = \\frac{3}{5}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(31667,'physics',13,799,2172,'<div class="matching-question"><div class="column-left">Interference pattern is made by using yellow light instead of red light, the fringes becomes narrower.</div><div class="column-right">In $YDSE$, fringe width is given by $\\beta  = \\frac{{D\\lambda }}{d}$.</div></div>','[{"text": "Statement A is correct but Statement B is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Statement A is incorrect but Statement B is correct.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Statements are correct.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Statements are incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'As  $\\beta  = \\frac{{D\\lambda }}{d}$ and wavelength of yellow light is shorter than red, so fringe width is narrower for yellow light. So option (3) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ type-2',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(33846,'physics',21,231,458,'Which of the following groups of forces could be in equilibrium?','[{"text": "$3N, 4N, 5N$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$4N, 5N, 10N$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$30N, 40N, 80N$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1N, 3N, 5N$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'For equilibrium of forces, the resultant of two (smaller) forces should be equal and opposite to third one. This is only possible with option (1)',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(31668,'physics',18,121,427,'A converging lens is used to form an image on a screen. When the upper half of the lens is covered by an opaque screen<br>1.  Half of the image will disappear<br>2.  No part of image will disappear<br> 3.  Intensity of the image will increase<br >4.  Intensity of the image will decrease','[{"text": "1,3 are true", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "1,4 are true", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "2,3 are true", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "2,4 are true", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Some of the incident rays will be cut-off. So intensity of image will decrease. No part of the image will decrease. <br> So correct option is (4).',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(31669,'physics',12,77,291,'A dipole comprises of two charged particles of identical magnitude $q$ and opposite in nature. The mass \' $m$ \' of the positive charged particle is half of the mass of the negative charged particle. The two charges are separated by a distance \' $l$ \' the dipole is pivoted at the mid point of dipole axis. If the dipole is placed in a uniform electric field $\\vec{E}$; in such a way that dipole axis makes a very small angle with the electric field $\\vec{E}$. The angular frequency of the oscillations of the dipole when released is given by','[{"text": "$\\\\sqrt{\\\\dfrac{8 q E}{m l}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\sqrt{\\\\dfrac{4 q E}{m l}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\sqrt{\\\\dfrac{4 q E}{3 m l}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\sqrt{\\\\dfrac{8 q E}{3 m l}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'As, $\\tau  = pE\\sin \\theta  \\Rightarrow \\tau  \\approx pE\\theta $ (for small angle  $\\sin \\theta  \\approx \\theta $) $I\\alpha  = pE\\theta \\,\\,\\,{\\mkern 1mu} {\\kern 1pt} (\\because \\tau  = I\\alpha )$<br> So, $\\alpha  = \\frac{{pE}}{I}\\theta $<br>$ \\Rightarrow {\\omega ^2} = \\frac{{pE}}{I} \\Rightarrow \\omega  = \\sqrt {\\frac{{pE}}{I}} {\\text{ }}\\left( {\\because \\alpha  = {\\omega ^2}\\theta } \\right)\\quad \\quad (1)$<br>$\\therefore \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,M.I.,\\,\\,\\,I = \\frac{{2m{l^2}}}{4} + \\frac{{m{l^2}}}{4} = \\frac{{3m{l^2}}}{4}\\quad $<br> (put in (1))<br>$\\omega  = \\sqrt {\\frac{{q(l)E}}{{3m\\left( {\\frac{{{l^2}}}{4}} \\right)}}}  = \\sqrt {\\frac{{4qE}}{{3ml}}} $ <img src=\'/img/data/physics/12_31669_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',2023,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(31670,'physics',19,139,318,'Find the dimensions of  $\\frac{a}{b}$ in the equation 	 $P = \\frac{{a - {t^2}}}{{bx}}$ where $P$ is pressure, $x$ is distance and $t$ is time.','[{"text": "$M{T^{ - 2}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$MT$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${M^{ - 1}}{T^{ - 1}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$M{T^2}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'$P = \\frac{{a - {t^2}}}{{bx}}$<br>$ \\Rightarrow \\,\\,Pbx = a - {t^2}$<br>$ \\Rightarrow \\,\\,[Pbx] = [a] - [{T^2}]$<br>$[a]$ and $[{T^2}]$ have same dimensions<br>$ \\Rightarrow \\,\\,\\,[b] = \\frac{{[{T^2}]}}{{[P][x]}} = \\frac{{[{T^2}]}}{{[M{L^{ - 1}}{T^{ - 2}}][L]}} = [{M^{ - 1}}{T^4}]$<br>$\\therefore \\,\\,\\left[ {\\frac{a}{b}} \\right] = \\frac{{\\left[ {{T^2}} \\right]}}{{\\left[ {{M^{ - 1}}{T^4}} \\right]}} = \\left[ {M{T^{ - 2}}} \\right]$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(31671,'physics',13,800,2180,'<div class="matching-question"><div class="column-left">Coloured spectrum is seen when we look through a muslin cloth.</div><div class="column-right">It is due the diffraction of white light on passing through fine slits.</div></div>','[{"text": "Statement A is correct but Statement B is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Statement A is incorrect but Statement B is correct.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Statements are correct.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Statements are incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'When we look through a muslin cloth colored spectrum can be seen. Because muslin cloth has fine slits in it. When white light passes through this fine  slits,  diffraction occurs. So option (3) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ type-2',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(31672,'physics',18,121,426,'One end of a cylindrical glass rod $\\left( {\\mu = 1.5} \\right)$ is given the shape of a concave refracing surface of radius $10\\,cm$ . An air bubble is situated in the glass rod at a point on its axis such that it appears to be at distance $10\\,cm$ from the surface and inside glass when seen from the other medium. Find the actual location of air bubble, from the surface.','[{"text": "20 $cm$ outside the glass", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "10 $cm$ inside the glass", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "40 $cm$ inside the glass", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "30 $cm$ inside the glass", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Using ${\\frac{{{\\mu _2}}}{v} - \\frac{{{\\mu _1}}}{u} = \\frac{{{\\mu _2} - {\\mu _1}}}{R}}$<br>Here, ${\\mu _1} = 1.5,{\\mu _2} = 1$ <img src=\'/img/data/physics/18_31672_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' /> ${v = - 10\\,cm,R = + 10\\,cm}$ (given)<br>$\\therefore \\quad \\quad - \\frac{1}{{10}} - \\frac{{1.5}}{u} = \\frac{{1 - 1.5}}{{10}} = - \\frac{1}{{20}}$<br>${\\frac{{1.5}}{u} = - \\frac{1}{{10}} + \\frac{1}{{20}} = - \\frac{1}{{20}}\\quad \\therefore \\quad u = - 30\\,cm}$<br>Hence, the air bubble is actually located at a distance $30\\,cm$ from the surface and inside glass.',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(31673,'physics',12,72,279,'A body has a charge of ${-3.2 \\mu C}$. The number of excess electrons it has is','[{"text": "${5.12 \\\\times 10^{25}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${5 \\\\times 10^{12}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${2 \\\\times 10^{13}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${5.12 \\\\times 10^{13}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Charge on any body is quantized.<br>${\\therefore Q=n e}$<br>${n=\\dfrac{Q}{e}=\\dfrac{3.2 \\times 10^{-6}}{1.6 \\times 10^{-19}}}$<br>${\\Rightarrow n=2 \\times 10^{13}}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2024,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(31674,'physics',13,799,2172,'<div class="matching-question"><div class="column-left">In Young’s double slit experiment interference pattern disappear when one of the slits is closed.</div><div class="column-right">Interference occurs due to superimposition of light waves from two coherent sources.</div></div>','[{"text": "Statement A is correct but Statement B is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Statement A is incorrect but Statement B is correct.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Statements are correct.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Statements are incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Option (3) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ type-2',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(31675,'physics',19,139,318,'Find the dimensional formula of  surface tension $S$,equation  involving the surface tension $S = \\frac{{\\rho grh}}{2}$','[{"text": "${M^{ - 1}}{T^{ - 1}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$M{T^2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${M^{ - 1}}{T^2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$M{T^{ - 2}}$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'$S = \\frac{{\\rho grh}}{2}$<br>or $[S] = [\\rho ][g]{L^2} = \\frac{M}{{{L^3}}}.\\frac{L}{{{T^2}}}.{L^2} = M{T^{ - 2}}$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(31816,'physics',12,77,289,'The electric dipole moment of an electron and a proton $4.3\\,nm$ apart is','[{"text": "$6.88 \\\\times {10^{ - 28}}C - m$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$2.56 \\\\times {10^{ - 29}}{C^2} - {m^{ - 1}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$3.72 \\\\times {10^{ - 14}}C - {m^{ - 1}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1.1 \\\\times {10^{ - 46}}{C^2} - m$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'The electric dipole moment is equal<br> to $\\quad p=1.6 \\times 10^{-19} \\times 4.3 \\times 10^{-9}$<br>$[\\because p = q \\times 2l]$<br>$ = 6.88 \\times {10^{ - 28}}C - m$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(31676,'physics',18,121,426,'A point source ${\\rm{O}}$ kept at distance of $2R{\\rm{ }}$ from pole ${\\rm{ P}}$ of a concave interface having radius of curvature $R$ emits a beam at an angle of $30^\\circ $ to the principle axis ${\\rm{ PO}}$. The distance of the image of point source ${\\rm{O}}$ from pole ${\\rm{ P }}$is:','[{"text": "$R$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$2R{\\\\rm{/}}3$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2R/\\\\sqrt 3 $", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "None of these", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'As the distance $O$ &amp; $P$ is $2R{\\rm{ }}$ so the point $O$ is diametrically on the opposite side of the spherical surface. <img src=\'/img/data/physics/18_31676_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' /> From Snell&rsquo;s law<br>$2\\sqrt 3 \\sin 30^\\circ = \\sqrt 3 \\sin C$<br>$ \\Rightarrow r = 90^\\circ $<br>From the right angle triangle $CPQ$<br>$\\cos 60^\\circ = \\frac{R}{{CQ}} \\Rightarrow CQ = 2R$<br>$ \\Rightarrow PQ = R$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/18_31676_question_1.png'),
(31677,'physics',12,73,280,'A point charge ${A}$ of ${+10 \\mu C}$ and another point charge ${B}$ of $ + 20\\,\\mu C$ are kept ${1 m}$ apart in free space. The electrostatic force on ${A}$ due to ${B}$ is ${\\vec{F}_{1}}$ and the electrostatic force on ${B}$ due to ${A}$ is ${\\vec{F}_{2}}$. Then','[{"text": "${\\\\vec{F}_{1}=-2 \\\\vec{F}_{2}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\vec{F}_{1}=-\\\\vec{F}_{2}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${2 \\\\vec{F}_{1}=-\\\\vec{F}_{2}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\vec{F}_{1}=\\\\vec{F}_{2}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Forces between 2 point charges form action reaction pair. Force on ${1^{\\text {st }}}$ charge due to ${2^{\\text {nd }}}$ charge is equal to force on ${2^{\\text {nd }}}$ charge due to first charge. ${\\vec{F}_{1}=-\\vec{F}_{2}}$.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2024,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(31678,'physics',13,799,2174,'<div class="matching-question"><div class="column-left">When thin transparent sheets are placed in front of both the slits of Young’s experiment, the fringe width will change.</div><div class="column-right">In Young’s experiment, the fringe width is inversely  proportional to wavelength of the source used.</div></div>','[{"text": "Statement A is correct but Statement B is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Statement A is incorrect but Statement B is correct.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Statements are correct.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Statements are incorrect.", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Fringe width $\\beta  = \\frac{{\\lambda D}}{d} \\Rightarrow \\beta  \\propto \\lambda {\\mkern 1mu} \\,{\\rm{and}}{\\mkern 1mu} \\,\\beta $ does not change after placing the transparent sheets. Both statements are wrong. So option (4) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ type-2',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(31679,'physics',19,139,317,'The physical quantity denoted by $\\frac{{{\\rm{mass}}\\, \\times \\,{\\rm{pressure}}}}{{{\\rm{density}}}}$ is','[{"text": "Force", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Angular momentum", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Work", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Momentum", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Given, $\\frac{{mass \\times pressure}}{{density}}$ ${\\rm{ = }}\\frac{{\\left[ M \\right] \\times \\left[ {M{L^{ - 1}}{T^{ - 2}}} \\right]}}{{\\left[ {M{L^{ - 3}}} \\right]}}$<br>$ = \\left[ {M{L^2}{T^{ - 2}}} \\right]$<br>Which represent work.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(31680,'physics',18,120,423,'A$4\\,cm$ thick layer of water covers a $6\\,cm$ thick glass slab. A coin is placed at the bottom of the slab and is being observed from the air side along the normal to the surface. Find the apparent depth of the coin from surface is<br>','[{"text": "$10\\\\,cm$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$5\\\\,cm$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$8.0\\\\,cm$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$7.0\\\\,cm$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'The total apparent shift is<br> $s = {h_1}\\left( {1 - \\frac{1}{{{\\mu _1}}}} \\right) + {h_2}\\left( {1 - \\frac{1}{{{\\mu _2}}}} \\right)$<br>$s = 4\\left( {1 - \\frac{1}{{4/3}}} \\right) + 6\\left( {1 - \\frac{1}{{3/2}}} \\right)$<br>$ = 3.0\\,cm$<br>Thus the apparent depth $h = {h_1} + {h_2} - s = 4 + 6 - 3$<br>$ = 7.0\\,cm$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/18_31680_question_1.png'),
(31681,'physics',12,76,288,'A uniform electric field ${E=3 \\times 10^{5} N C^{-1}}$ is acting along the positive Y -axis. The electric flux through a rectangle of area ${10 {~cm} \\times 30 {~cm}}$ whose plane is parallel to the ${{Z}-{X}}$ plane is','[{"text": "${12 \\\\times 10^{3} {Vm}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${9 \\\\times 10^{3} {Vm}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${15 \\\\times 10^{3} {Vm}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${18 \\\\times 10^{3} {Vm}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Electric flux is given by ${\\phi=\\vec{E} \\cdot \\vec{A}}$<br>${\\therefore \\phi=3 \\times 10^{5} \\times 300 \\times 10^{-4}}$<br>$ = 9 \\times {10^3}\\,\\,Vm$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2024,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(31682,'physics',13,799,2176,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> In Young’s experiment the fringe width for dark fringe is same from that for white fringes.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> In Young’s double slit experiment, when a source of white light is used, then only black and bright fringes are observed.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reasons are true and the Reason is a correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not a correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is true but the Reason is false.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is false but Reason is true.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'In Young’s experiment fringe width for dark and bright fringes are same while in the same experiment, when a white light is used, the central fringe is white around which few coloured fringes are observed on either side. So option 	(3) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(31683,'physics',19,139,318,'The dimensional formula of product and quotient of two physical quantities $A$ and $B$ are given by $[AB] = [M{L^2}{T^{ - 2}}]$ and $\\left[ {\\frac{A}{B}} \\right] = M{T^{ - 2}}$. Find the 	quantities $A$ and $B$','[{"text": "Force, displacement", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Force, momentum", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Energy, acceleration", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Force, velocity", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Given that $\\left[ {AB} \\right] \\cdot \\left[ {\\frac{A}{B}} \\right]{\\left[ A \\right]^2} = \\left[ {{M^2}{L^2}{T^{ - 4}}} \\right]$<br>$ \\Rightarrow \\,[A] = [ML{T^{ - 2}}] = {\\mathop{\\rm Force}\\nolimits} $<br>$[B] = \\frac{{[AB]}}{{[A]}} = [L]$<br>$ \\Rightarrow [B] = [L] \\equiv $ displacement',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(31684,'physics',18,120,423,'Each quarter of a vessel of depth $H$ is filled with liquids of the refractive indices ${n_1},{n_2},{n_3}\\,and\\,{n_4}$ from the bottom respectively. The apparent depth of the vessel when looked normally is','[{"text": "$\\\\frac{{H\\\\left( {\\\\frac{1}{{{n_1}}} + \\\\frac{1}{{{n_2}}} + \\\\frac{1}{{{n_3}}} + \\\\frac{1}{{{n_4}}}} \\\\right)}}{4}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{{H\\\\left( {{n_1} + {n_2} + {n_3} + {n_4}} \\\\right)}}{4}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{{H\\\\left( {\\\\frac{1}{{{n_1}}} + \\\\frac{1}{{{n_2}}} + \\\\frac{1}{{{n_3}}} + \\\\frac{1}{{{n_4}}}} \\\\right)}}{2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{{\\\\left( {{n_1} + {n_2} + {n_3} + {n_4}} \\\\right)}}{{4H}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Apparent depth of bottom<br>$ = \\frac{{H/4}}{{{n_1}}} + \\frac{{H/4}}{{{n_2}}} + \\frac{{H/4}}{{{n_3}}} + \\frac{{H/4}}{{{n_4}}}$<br>$ = \\frac{H}{4}\\left( {\\frac{1}{{{n_1}}} + \\frac{1}{{{n_2}}} + \\frac{1}{{{n_3}}} + \\frac{1}{{{n_4}}}} \\right)$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(31685,'physics',12,132,301,'The total electric flux through a closed spherical surface of radius \' ${r}$ \' enclosing an electric dipole of dipole moment ${2 a q}$ is (Give ${\\varepsilon_{0}=}$ permittivity of free space)','[{"text": "Zero", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${\\\\dfrac{q}{\\\\varepsilon_{0}}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\dfrac{2 q}{\\\\varepsilon_{0}}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\dfrac{8 \\\\pi r^{2} q}{\\\\varepsilon_{0}}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Using Gauss\'s law ${\\phi=\\oint \\vec{E} \\cdot d \\vec{A}=\\dfrac{q_{\\text {in }}}{\\varepsilon_{0}}}$<br>As net charge is zero, so ${\\phi=}$ zero .',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2024,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(31686,'physics',13,804,2190,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Corpuscular theory fails in explaining the velocities of light in air and water.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> According to corpuscular theory, light should travel faster in denser media than in rarer media.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reasons are true and the Reason is a correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not a correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is true but the Reason is false.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is false but Reason is true.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'According to Newton’s corpuscular theory of light, the light should travel faster in denser than in rarer media. It is contrary to present theory of light which explains the light travels faster in air (rarer) and slow in water (denser). So option (1) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(31687,'physics',19,136,310,'An example to define length in the form of time at a place is','[{"text": "Wrist watch", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Linear expansion of iron rod", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Frequency of ripples on the surface of \\twater", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Seconds pendulum", "isCorrect": true}]',3,NULL,NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(31688,'physics',18,120,423,'A glass slab of thickness $3\\,cm$ and refractive index $3{\\rm{/}}2{\\rm{ }}$  is placed on ink mark on a piece of paper. For a person looking at the mark at a distance $5.0\\,cm$ above it, the distance of the mark will appear to be','[{"text": "$5.0\\\\,cm$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$3.0\\\\,cm$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$4.5\\\\,cm$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$4.0\\\\,cm$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'The apparent depth of ink mark<br>$ = \\frac{{real\\,depth}}{\\mu } = \\frac{3}{{3/2}} = 2\\,cm$<br>Thus person views mark at distance <br>$ = 2 + 2 = 4\\,cm$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(31689,'physics',12,72,279,'A body has ${-40 \\mu C}$ micro coulomb of charge. Number of additional (excess) electrons in it will be','[{"text": "${4 \\\\times 10^{-5}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${8 \\\\times 10^{-17}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${2 \\\\cdot 5 \\\\times 10^{14}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${4 \\\\cdot 5 \\\\times 10^{-17}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Total Charge ${Q=n e}$<br>${\\Rightarrow n=\\dfrac{Q}{e}=\\dfrac{40 \\times 10^{-6}}{1.6 \\times 10^{-19}}=2.5 \\times 10^{14}}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(31690,'physics',13,799,2175,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> If the whole apparatus of Young’s experiment is immersed in water, the fringe width will decrease.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> The wavelength of light in water is more than that of air.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reasons are true and the Reason is a correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not a correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is true but the Reason is false.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is false but Reason is true.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'When whole apparatus is immersed in water then wavelength of light decreases from the relation $\\lambda \' = \\lambda /\\mu ,\\mu $ being refractive index of water .<br>Therefore, fringe width $\\left( {\\beta  = \\frac{{D\\lambda }}{d}\\,{\\rm{or}}\\,\\beta  \\propto \\lambda } \\right)$ decrease when immersed in water. Also we conclude that wavelength of light in water is less than that in air.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(31691,'physics',19,139,319,'A scientist obtains an answer that has units equivalent to those from computing the square root of the ratio of the Universal Gravitational constant to Coulomb’s constant. Which of the following choices has these same units?','[{"text": "Mass divided by energy", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Length divided by time", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Charge divided by mass", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Energy divided by time", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'The units for these quantities are given by $ = \\sqrt {\\frac{{\\frac{{N{m^2}}}{{k{g^2}}}}}{{\\frac{{N{m^2}}}{{{c^2}}}}}}  = \\sqrt {\\frac{{{c^2}}}{{k{g^2}}}}  = \\frac{c}{{kg}}.$<br>In other words, this has units of charges divide by mass.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(31692,'physics',18,120,423,'A hunter desires to shoot a fish whose image could be seen through clear water.  His aim should be','[{"text": "Above the apparent image of fish", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Below the apparent image of fish", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "In the line of sight of fish", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Parallel to the surface of water", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'With respect to the observer the apparent position will be shifted up. So he should aim below the fish.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(31693,'physics',12,72,279,'${10^{5}}$ electrons are removed per second from a large metallic sphere. How long will it take to gain ${+4 C}$ on the sphere?','[{"text": "${10^{2} y r}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${10^{11} {yr}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${10^{6} y r}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${10^{3} y r}$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Total electrons to be removed are<br>${Q=n e \\Rightarrow n=\\dfrac{4}{1 \\cdot 6 \\times 10^{-19}}=2.5 \\times 10^{19}}$<br>time ${=\\dfrac{n}{(\\text { rate } / {sec})}=\\dfrac{2 \\cdot 5 \\times 10^{19}}{10^{5}}=2 \\cdot 5 \\times 10^{14}}$<br>${=\\dfrac{2 \\cdot 5 \\times 10^{14}}{365 \\times 24 \\times 60 \\times 60}=7 \\cdot 9 \\times 10^{6}}$ years',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(31695,'physics',18,120,422,'Glass has refractive index $\\mu $ with respect to air and the critical angle for a ray of light going from glass to air is $\\theta $.  If  a ray of light is incident from air on the glass with angle of incidence $\\theta $, the corresponding angle of refraction is','[{"text": "$90^\\\\circ $", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\sin ^{ - 1}}\\\\left( {\\\\frac{1}{{\\\\sqrt \\\\mu  }}} \\\\right)$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\sin ^{ - 1}}\\\\left( {\\\\frac{1}{\\\\mu }} \\\\right)$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\sin ^{ - 1}}\\\\left( {\\\\frac{1}{{{\\\\mu ^2}}}} \\\\right)$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'$_a{\\mu _g} = \\frac{1}{{\\sin \\theta }} \\Rightarrow \\mu  = \\frac{1}{{\\sin \\theta }}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,(1)$ <br>Now from Snell’s law $\\mu  = \\frac{{\\sin i}}{{\\sin r}} = \\frac{{\\sin \\theta }}{{\\sin r}}$<br>$ \\Rightarrow \\sin r = \\frac{{\\sin \\theta }}{\\mu }\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,(2)$ <br>From equations (1) and (2) <br>$\\sin r = \\frac{1}{{{\\mu ^2}}} \\Rightarrow r = {\\sin ^{ - 1}}\\left( {\\frac{1}{{{\\mu ^2}}}} \\right)$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(31696,'physics',19,135,309,'1 Kilo watt hour is equal to ___ $eV$','[{"text": "$2.25 \\\\times {10^{25}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$3.6 \\\\times {10^{18}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1.6 \\\\times {10^{10}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2.25 \\\\times {10^{20}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'$1kWhr = {10^3} \\times \\frac{J}{s} \\times 3600s$<br>$ = \\frac{{3.6 \\times {{10}^6}}}{{1.6 \\times {{10}^{ - 19}}}}eV(eV = 1.6 \\times {10^{ - 19}}J)$<br>$ = 2.25 \\times {10^{25}}eV$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(31697,'physics',12,73,280,'Force between ${A}$ and ${B}$ is ${F}$ as shown in the figure. If ${50 \\%}$ charge ${A}$ of is transferred to ${B}$, then force between ${A}$ and ${B}$ is','[{"text": "${\\\\dfrac{F}{3}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${4 F}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\dfrac{5 F}{2}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${2 F}$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'For the given combination of charges <img src=\'/img/data/physics/12_31697_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' /> ${\\Rightarrow F=\\dfrac{k(8 Q)(Q)}{r^{2}}=\\dfrac{8 k Q^{2}}{r^{2}}}$ <img src=\'/img/data/physics/12_31697_explanation_2.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' /> ${\\Rightarrow F^{\\prime}=\\dfrac{k(4 Q)(5 Q)}{r^{2}}=\\dfrac{5 F}{2}}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/12_31697_question_1.png'),
(31698,'physics',13,803,2188,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> The unpolarised light and polarised\nlight can be distinguished from each other by\nusing polaroid<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> A polaroid is capable of producing\nplane polarised beams of light.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reasons are true and the Reason is a correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not a correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is true but the Reason is false.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is false but Reason is true.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'When a polaroid is rotated in the path of unpolarised light, the intensity of light transmitted from polaroid remains undiminished. However, when the polaroid is rotated in path of plane polarised light, its intensity will vary (when the direction of the vibrations becomes perpendicular to the axis of the crystal). Thus using polaroid we can easily verify whether the light is polarised or not. So option (1) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(31699,'physics',18,120,422,'For total internal reflection to take place, the angle of incidence $i$ and the refractive index $\\mu $ of the medium must satisfy the inequality','[{"text": "$\\\\sin i < \\\\mu $", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{1}{{\\\\sin i}} < \\\\mu $", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\sin i < \\\\mu \\\\sin i < \\\\mu $", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{1}{{\\\\sin i}} > \\\\mu $", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'For total internal reflection $i > C$<br>$ \\Rightarrow \\sin i > \\sin C \\Rightarrow \\sin i > \\frac{1}{\\mu } \\Rightarrow \\frac{1}{{\\sin i}} < \\mu .$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(31700,'physics',19,136,310,'The Sun’s angular diameter is measured to be ${1920^{\'\'}}$. The distance D of the Sun from the Earth is $1.496 \\times {10^{11}}m$. What is the diameter of the Sun?','[{"text": "$1.5 \\\\times {10^6}m$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1.39 \\\\times {10^9}m$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$1.12 \\\\times {10^7}m$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1.2 \\\\times {10^6}m$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Sun’s angular diameter $\\alpha  = {1920^{\'\'}}$<br>$ = 1920\'\' \\times \\frac{{1\'}}{{60}} \\times \\frac{{{1^0}}}{{60\'}} \\times \\frac{\\pi }{{180^\\circ }}rad$<br>$ = 9.31 \\times {10^{ - 3}}\\,rad$<br>Sun’s diameter<br>$d = \\alpha D$<br>$ = (9.31 \\times {10^{ - 3}}) \\times (1.496 \\times {10^{11}})\\,m$<br>$ = 1.39 \\times {10^9}\\,m$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(31701,'physics',12,74,283,'A point charge ${64 \\mu C}$ is placed at ${(3 \\hat{i}+4 \\hat{j}) m}$. Find ${|\\vec{E}|}$ at point ${(5 \\hat{i}+12 \\hat{j}) m}$.','[{"text": "${6000 {~N} / {C}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${8000 {~N} / {C}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${2500 {~N} / {C}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${9000 {~N} / {C}}$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Here, ${r=\\sqrt{(5)^{2}+(12)^{2}}-\\sqrt{3^{2}+4^{2}}=8}$<br>${E=\\dfrac{k Q}{r^{2}}=\\dfrac{9 \\times 10^{9} \\times 64 \\times 10^{-6}}{(8)^{2}}=9 \\times 10^{3} {~N} / {C}}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(31702,'physics',13,799,2172,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> In Young’s experiment, for two coherent sources, each of intensity ${I_0}$, the resultant intensity is given by $I = 4{I_0}{\\cos ^2}\\frac{\\phi }{2}$<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Ratio of maximum to minimum intensity is $\\frac{{{I_{\\max }}}}{{{I_{\\min }}}} = \\frac{{{{\\left( {\\sqrt {{I_1}}  + \\sqrt {{I_2}} } \\right)}^2}}}{{{{\\left( {\\sqrt {{I_1}}  - \\sqrt {{I_2}} } \\right)}^2}}}.$','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reasons are true and the Reason is a correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not a correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is true but the Reason is false.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is false but Reason is true.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'For two coherent sources,<br>$I = {I_1} + {I_2} + \\sqrt {{I_1}{I_2}} \\cos \\phi $<br>Putting, ${I_1} = {I_2} = {I_0}$<br>We have $I = {I_0} + {I_0} + 2\\sqrt {{I_0} \\times {I_0}} \\cos \\phi $<br>Simplifying the above expression<br>$I = 2{I_0}\\left( {1 + \\cos \\phi } \\right) = 4{I_0}{\\cos ^2}\\frac{\\phi }{2}$<br>Also, $\\frac{{{I_{\\max }}}}{{{I_{\\min }}}} = \\frac{{{{\\left( {\\sqrt {{I_1}}  + \\sqrt {{I_2}} } \\right)}^2}}}{{{{\\left( {\\sqrt {{I_1}}  - \\sqrt {{I_2}} } \\right)}^2}}}$<br>So option (2) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(31721,'physics',12,76,288,'If ${E=\\hat{i}+2 \\hat{j}+3 \\hat{k}}$, then electric flux (in ${V-m}$ ) through a surface area of ${100 m^{2}}$, lying in the ${X Y}$ plane is','[{"text": "300", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "100", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "400", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "200", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Flux through the surface present in ${X Y}$ plane is ${\\phi=\\vec{E} \\cdot \\vec{A}}$, where<br>${\\vec{E}=\\hat{i}+2 \\hat{j}+3 \\hat{k}}$<br>${\\vec{A}=100 \\hat{k} \\Rightarrow \\phi=300 {~V} \\cdot {m}}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(31703,'physics',18,120,422,'White light is incident on the interface of glass and air as shown in the figure. If green light is just totally internally reflected then the emerging ray in air contains','[{"text": "Yellow, orange, red", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Violet, indigo, blue", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "All colours", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "All colours except green", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'As  ${\\lambda _R} > {\\lambda _V} \\Rightarrow {\\mu _V} > {\\mu _R}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,(1)$ <br>For TIR $\\sin \\theta  = \\frac{1}{\\mu } \\Rightarrow \\theta {\\mkern 1mu} \\alpha \\frac{1}{\\mu }\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,(2)$ <br>From (1) & (2) ${\\theta _R} > {\\theta _V}$ <br>Where ${\\theta _r}\\& {\\theta _R}$ are critical angle for violet and red. If green undergoes TIR then Y, O & R pass through air.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/18_31703_question_1.png'),
(31704,'physics',19,136,310,'The parallax of a far off planet as measured from the two diametric extremes on the equator of the earth is 3.0 minute. If the radius of the earth is 6400 $km$, find the distance of the planet from the earth.','[{"text": "$1.5 \\\\times {10^{10}}m$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$1.5 \\\\times {10^8}m$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1.5 \\\\times {10^6}m$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1.5 \\\\times {10^{12}}m$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Angle of parallax $\\theta  = 3$ minute<br>$ = 3 \\times {\\left( {\\frac{1}{{60}}} \\right)^0}$<br>$ \\Rightarrow \\theta  = 3 \\times \\frac{1}{{60}} \\times \\frac{\\pi }{{180}}rad$ $\\left[ {\\because 1^\\circ  = \\frac{\\pi }{{180}}rad} \\right]$<br>$\\theta  = \\frac{\\pi }{{3600}}rad$<br>From parallax method<br>$BP = \\frac{{AB}}{\\theta }$<br>$ \\Rightarrow \\,BP = $ $\\frac{{{\\rm{Diameter}}\\,\\,{\\rm{of}}\\,\\,{\\rm{earth}}}}{{\\rm{\\theta }}}$<br>$ \\Rightarrow BP = \\frac{{2 \\times 6400}}{{\\left( {\\frac{\\pi }{{3600}}} \\right)}}km$<br>$ \\Rightarrow BP = 1.5 \\times {10^7}\\,km\\, = 1.5 \\times {10^{10\\,}}m$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/19_31704_question_1.png'),
(31705,'physics',12,74,284,'Calculate ${E}$ at centre $(O)$  for the following figure.','[{"text": "${\\\\dfrac{k Q}{25 a^{2}}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\dfrac{4 k Q}{25 a^{2}}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${\\\\dfrac{7 k Q}{5 a^{2}}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\dfrac{3 k Q}{25 a^{2}}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'The Fields produced by charges present in the corners ${A}$ and ${C}$ get cancelled. Fields produced by other two opposite charges get added. <img src=\'/img/data/physics/12_31705_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' /> The field at ${O}$ is ${E_{\\text {centre }}=E_{-2 Q}+E_{+2 Q}}$<br>${=\\dfrac{k(2 Q)}{(5 a)^{2}}=\\dfrac{k Q}{(5 a)^{2}}=\\dfrac{4 k Q}{25 a^{2}}}$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/12_31705_question_1.png'),
(31706,'physics',18,120,425,'If the speed of light in vacuum is $c\\,m{s^{ - 1}}$, then the velocity of light in a refractive index 1.5 is :','[{"text": "$c$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1.5\\\\,c$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{c}{{1.5}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Can have any velocity", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'$\\mu  = \\frac{C}{{{C_m}}} \\Rightarrow {C_m} = \\frac{C}{{1.5}}$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(31707,'physics',19,137,312,'A physicist performs an experiment and takes 200 readings. He repeats the same experiment and now takes 800 readings. By doing so','[{"text": "The probable error remains same", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "The probable error is four times", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "The probable error is halved", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "The probable error is reduced by a factor", "isCorrect": true}]',3,NULL,NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(31708,'physics',13,799,2172,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> No interference pattern is detected when two coherent sources are very close to each other.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> In $YDSE$ the fringe width is inversely proportional to the distance between the slits.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reasons are true and the Reason is a correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not a correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is true but the Reason is false.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is false but Reason is true.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'When $d$ is negligibily small, fringe width $\\beta $ which is proportional to $1{\\rm{/}}d$ may become too large. Even a single fringe may occupy the whole\nscreen. Hence the pattern cannot be detected.<br>So option (1) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(31709,'physics',12,74,283,'A point charge ${q=36 \\mu C}$ is located in the ${X-Y}$ plane at the point with position vector ${\\vec{r}_{0}=2 \\hat{i}+3 \\hat{j}+\\hat{k}}$. What is the electric field vector (in ${{kV} / {m}}$ ) at the point with position vector ${\\vec{r}=8 \\hat{i}-3 \\hat{j}+\\hat{k}}$','[{"text": "8", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "10", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "7", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "9", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Separation of two points is<br>${r=\\sqrt{\\left(x_{1}-x_{2}\\right)^{2}+\\left(y_{1}-y_{2}\\right)^{2}+\\left(z_{1}-z_{2}\\right)^{2}}}$<br>${r=\\sqrt{(2-8)^{2}+(3-3)^{2}+(1-1)^{2}}=6 m}$<br>${|\\vec{E}|=\\dfrac{1}{4 \\pi \\epsilon_{0}} \\dfrac{q}{r^{2}}=\\dfrac{9 \\times 10^{9} \\times 36 \\times 10^{-6}}{(6)^{2}}=9 \\times 10^{3}}$<br>${=9 {kV} / {m}}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(31710,'physics',18,120,422,'A cut diamond sparkles because of its','[{"text": "High  refractive index", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Absortion of  light  by  the diamond", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Hardness", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Emission of light by the diamond", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Due to high refractive index its critical angle is very  small so that most of the light incident on the diamond  is  total internally reflected repeatedly and diamond sparkles.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(31711,'physics',13,800,2180,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> No diffraction is produced in sound waves near a very small opening.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> For diffraction to take place the 	aperture of opening should be the same order as wavelength of the waves.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reasons are true and the Reason is a correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not a correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is true but the Reason is false.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is false but Reason is true.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'The condition for first minima is $\\sin \\theta  = \\frac{\\lambda }{a}{\\rm{ }}$. The diffraction of sound is only possible when the size of opening should be of the same order as its wavelength and the wavelength of sound is of the order of $1.0\\,m,$ hence, for a very small opening no diffraction is produced in sound since $\\sin \\theta  > 1{\\rm{ }}$ . So option (1) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(31722,'physics',18,120,425,'The main cause of decrease in intensity of light on refraction is','[{"text": "Scattering of light", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Passing of light from one medium to another", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Total internal reflection", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Partial reflection and partial absorption", "isCorrect": true}]',3,NULL,NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(31712,'physics',19,137,312,'In a circuit, potential difference across 	resistance, is $V = \\left( {4 \\pm 0.25} \\right)V$ and current in 	resistance, $I = \\left( {1 \\pm 0.1} \\right)A.$ What is the value of resistance with its percentage error?','[{"text": "$4\\\\Omega  \\\\pm 16.25\\\\% $", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$4\\\\Omega  \\\\pm 18.25\\\\% $", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$4\\\\Omega  \\\\pm 22.25\\\\% $", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$4\\\\Omega  \\\\pm 0.4\\\\% $", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'${R_0} = \\frac{{{V_0}}}{{{I_0}}} = \\frac{4}{1} = 4\\Omega $<br>$\\frac{{\\Delta R}}{R} = \\frac{{\\Delta V}}{V} + \\frac{{\\Delta I}}{I}$<br>$ = \\frac{{0.25}}{4} + \\frac{{0.1}}{{1.0}} = 0.1625$<br>$R = 4\\Omega  + 16.25\\% $',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(31713,'physics',12,75,287,'Which one of the following pattern of electrostatic line of force cannot be possible','[{"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Electrostatic lines do not form closed loops. So the answer is (3).',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(31714,'physics',18,120,420,'A short pulse of white light is incident from air to glass slab at normal incidence. After travelling through the slab, the first colour to emerge is :-','[{"text": "Green", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Blue", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Red", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Violet", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'We know that <br>${\\mu _{Violet}} > {\\mu _{{\\mathop{\\rm Re}\\nolimits} d}} \\Rightarrow {v_{{\\mathop{\\rm Re}\\nolimits} d}} > {v_{Violet}}$<br>$\\sin 60^\\circ  = \\mu \\sin {r_1}$ <br>So red colour comes first',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(31715,'physics',13,800,2181,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> If the slit width is equal to wavelength in diffraction then the entire screen is bright.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Angular position of ${1^{st}}$ minima is $\\sin \\theta  = \\frac{{2\\lambda }}{a}$','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reasons are true and the Reason is a correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not a correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is true but the Reason is false.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is false but Reason is true.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Angular position of 1st minima is $\\sin \\theta  = \\frac{\\lambda }{a}\\,{\\rm{if}}\\,\\lambda  = a$<br>$ \\Rightarrow \\theta  = {90^o}$  i.e., 1st minima will not appear on the screen. So the entire screen is bright.<br>So option (3) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(31716,'physics',19,137,312,'A person measures two quantities as $A = 1.0m \\pm 0.2m,$<br>$B = 2.0m \\pm 0.2m.$ We should report correct value for  $\\sqrt {AB} $ as','[{"text": "$1.4m + 0.3m$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1.4m \\\\pm 0.2m$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$1.4m \\\\pm 0.4m$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1.41m \\\\pm 0.15m$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Given, $A = 1.0m \\pm 0.2m,B = 2.0 \\pm 0.2m$<br>Let, $Y = \\sqrt {AB}  = \\sqrt {\\left( {1.0} \\right)\\left( {2.0} \\right)}  = 1.414\\,m$<br>Rounding off to two significant digit $Y = 1.4\\,m$<br>$\\frac{{\\Delta Y}}{Y} = \\frac{1}{2}\\left[ {\\frac{{\\Delta A}}{A} + \\frac{{\\Delta B}}{B}} \\right]$<br>$ = \\frac{1}{2}\\left[ {\\frac{{0.2}}{{1.0}} + \\frac{{0.2}}{{2.0}}} \\right] = \\frac{{0.6}}{{2 \\times 2.0}}$<br>$ \\Rightarrow \\Delta Y = \\frac{{0.6Y}}{{2 \\times 2.0}} = \\frac{{0.6 \\times 1.4}}{{2 \\times 20}} = 0.212$<br>Rounding off to one significant digit<br>$\\Delta Y = 0.2m$<br>Thus, correct value for<br>$\\sqrt {AB}  = Y + \\Delta Y = 1.4 \\pm 0.2m$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(31717,'physics',12,75,287,'From the field pattern shown in the figure, identify the correct statement related to the charges ${Q_{1}}$ and ${Q_{2}}$','[{"text": "${\\\\dfrac{Q_{1}}{Q_{2}}=+5 \\\\cdot 5}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\dfrac{Q_{1}}{Q_{2}}=+6 \\\\cdot 5}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\dfrac{Q_{1}}{Q_{2}}=\\\\dfrac{-13}{7}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${\\\\dfrac{Q_{1}}{Q_{2}}=+4 \\\\cdot 5}$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'As the field lines are coming out from ${Q_{1}}$ so it is ${(+) v e}$ and ${Q_{2}}$ is ${(-) v e}$ as field line entering into it.<br>${\\dfrac{Q_{1}}{Q_{2}}=\\dfrac{\\text { No. of lines originating from } Q_{1}}{\\text { No. of lines entering into } Q_{2}}}$<br>${=\\dfrac{+13}{-7}=\\dfrac{-13}{7}}$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/12_31717_question_1.png'),
(31718,'physics',18,120,421,'The refractive index of water, glass and diamond are  1.33, 1.50, 2.40   respectively. The refractive index of diamond relative to water and refractive index of glass relative to diamond, respectively are nearly','[{"text": "0.554, 0.625", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "1.80,0.625", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "0.554, 1.6", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "1.80, 1.6", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Given that ${\\mu _w} = 1.33,\\,\\,{\\mu _g} = 1.5\\,\\& \\,\\,{\\mu _d} = 2.4$<br>$\\mathop \\mu \\limits_{w\\,d}  = \\frac{{{\\mu _d}}}{{{\\mu _w}}} = 1.8\\,\\,\\& \\,\\,\\mathop \\mu \\limits_{d\\,g}  = \\frac{{{\\mu _g}}}{{{\\mu _d}}} = \\frac{{1.5}}{{2.4}} = 0.625$<br>',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(31719,'physics',13,800,2181,'In a diffraction pattern due to a single slit of width $\'a\'$, the first minimum is observed at an angle $30^\\circ $ when light of wavelength $5000\\mathop A\\limits^ \\circ  $ is incident on the slit. The first secondary maximum is observed at an angle of','[{"text": "${\\\\sin ^{ - 1}}\\\\left( {\\\\frac{1}{4}} \\\\right)$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\sin ^{ - 1}}\\\\left( {\\\\frac{2}{3}} \\\\right)$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\sin ^{ - 1}}\\\\left( {\\\\frac{1}{2}} \\\\right)$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\sin ^{ - 1}}\\\\left( {\\\\frac{3}{4}} \\\\right)$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'For first minima, $\\sin {30^ \\circ } = \\frac{\\lambda }{a} = \\frac{1}{2}$  <br>First secondary maxima will be at <br>$\\sin \\theta  = \\frac{{3\\lambda }}{{2a}} = \\frac{3}{2}\\left( {\\frac{1}{2}} \\right) \\Rightarrow \\theta  = {\\sin ^{ - 1}}\\left( {\\frac{3}{4}} \\right)$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2016,'NEET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(31720,'physics',19,137,312,'The period of oscillation of a simple pendulum is $T = 2\\pi \\sqrt {\\frac{L}{g}} .$ Measured value of $L$ is 20.0 $cm$ known to 4 $mm$ accuracy and time Period &nbsp;of the pendulum is found to be 1 $s$ using a wrist watch of accuracy $0.005$. The accuracy in the determination of $g$ is:&nbsp;','[{"text": "$2\\\\,\\\\% $", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1\\\\,\\\\% $", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$5\\\\,\\\\% $", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$3\\\\,\\\\% $", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'$g = 4{\\pi ^2} \\cdot \\frac{1}{{{T^2}}}$<br>$ \\Rightarrow \\frac{{\\Delta g}}{g} \\times 100 = \\frac{{\\Delta l}}{l} \\times 100 + 2\\frac{{\\Delta T}}{T} \\times 100$<br>$ = \\frac{{\\Delta l}}{l} \\times 100 + 2 \\cdot \\frac{{\\Delta T}}{T} \\times 100$<br>Given that $\\Delta l = 0.4\\,cm,\\,\\,l = 20\\,cm$<br>$\\Delta T = 0.005,\\,\\,T = 1\\,s$<br>$ \\Rightarrow \\frac{{0.4}}{{20}} \\times 100 + \\frac{{2 \\times 0.005}}{1} \\times 100 = 3\\% $',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(35505,'physics',25,892,2055,'The length of a wire increases by $2 \\%$ by a load of $5\\,kg - wt$. The linear strain produced in the wire will be','[{"text": "0.02", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "0.001", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "0.01", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "0.002", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Strain $=\\dfrac{\\text { Change in length }}{\\text { Original length }}$<br>$=\\dfrac{2 \\% \\text { of } L}{L}=\\dfrac{0.02 L}{L}=0.02$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(31723,'physics',13,798,2170,'The interference pattern is obtained with two coherent light sources of intensity ratio $n$. In the interference pattern, the ratio $\\frac{{{I_{\\max }} - {I_{\\min }}}}{{{I_{\\max }} + {I_{\\min }}}}$ will be','[{"text": "$\\\\frac{{\\\\sqrt n }}{{n + 1}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{{2\\\\sqrt n }}{{n + 1}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{{\\\\sqrt n }}{{{{\\\\left( {n + 1} \\\\right)}^2}}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{{2\\\\sqrt n }}{{{{\\\\left( {n + 1} \\\\right)}^2}}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Given that $\\frac{{{I_1}}}{{{I_2}}} = n$<br>Let ${I_2} = {I_0} \\Rightarrow {I_1} = n{I_0}$<br>${I_{\\min }} = {\\left( {\\sqrt {{I_1}}  + \\sqrt {{I_2}} } \\right)^2} = {\\left( {\\sqrt {{I_0}}  + \\sqrt n \\sqrt {{I_0}} } \\right)^2}$<br>${I_{\\max }} = {\\left( {\\sqrt {{I_1}}  - \\sqrt {{I_2}} } \\right)^2} = {\\left( {\\sqrt {{I_0}}  - \\sqrt n \\sqrt {{I_0}} } \\right)^2}$<br>$\\frac{{{I_{\\max }} - {I_{\\min }}}}{{{I_{\\max }} + {I_{\\min }}}} = \\frac{{{{\\left( {\\sqrt {{I_0}}  + \\sqrt n \\sqrt {{I_0}} } \\right)}^2} - {{\\left( {\\sqrt {{I_0}}  - \\sqrt n \\sqrt {{I_0}} } \\right)}^2}}}{{{{\\left( {\\sqrt {{I_0}}  + \\sqrt n \\sqrt {{I_0}} } \\right)}^2} + {{\\left( {\\sqrt {{I_0}}  - \\sqrt n \\sqrt {{I_0}} } \\right)}^2}}}$<br>$ = \\frac{{2\\sqrt n }}{{1 + n}}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2016,'NEET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(31724,'physics',12,132,301,'In the figure, a cone of radius ${R}$ is shown. Electric field of intensity ${E_{0}}$ is present perpendicular to the circular cross-section of the cone. The electric flux through the carved surface of the hemisphere is','[{"text": "0", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${E_{0} \\\\times \\\\pi R^{2}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${E_{0} \\\\times 2 \\\\pi R^{2}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${E_{0} \\\\times 3 \\\\pi R^{2}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'The flux through both flat and carved surface are same. ${\\phi_{\\text {Flat }}=\\phi_{\\text {carved }}=E_{0} \\pi R^{2}}$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/12_31724_question_1.png'),
(31725,'physics',19,138,316,'What is the number of significant figures in $0.310 \\times {10^3}?$','[{"text": "$3$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$2$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$6$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$4$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Number of significant figures &nbsp;are 3, because ${10^3}$ is decimal multiplier.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(31726,'physics',18,120,421,'If $_j{{\\bf{\\mu }}_i}$ represents refractive index  medium $i$ w.r.to medium $j$.  Then the product $_2{\\mu _1} \\times {\\,_3}{\\mu _2} \\times {\\,_4}{\\mu _3}$  is equal to','[{"text": "$_3{\\\\mu _1}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$_3{\\\\mu _2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{1}{{_1{\\\\mu _4}}}\\\\,$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$_4{\\\\mu _2}$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'$\\frac{{{\\mu _1}}}{{{\\mu _2}}} \\times \\frac{{{\\mu _2}}}{{{\\mu _3}}} \\times \\frac{{{\\mu _3}}}{{{\\mu _4}}} = \\frac{{{\\mu _1}}}{{{\\mu _4}}} = {}_4{\\mu _1} = \\frac{1}{{{}_1{\\mu _4}}}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(31727,'physics',13,799,2172,'The intensity at the maximum in a Young’s double slit experiment is ${I_0}$ . Distance between two slits is $d = 5\\lambda ,$ where $\\lambda $ is the wavelength of light used in the experiment. What will be the intensity in front of one of the slits on the screen placed at a distance $D = 10d?$','[{"text": "${I_0}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{{{I_0}}}{4}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{3}{4}{I_0}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{{{I_0}}}{2}$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'The $YDSE$ is shown in the figure<br>Path difference $\\Delta x = {S_2}P - {S_1}P = \\sqrt {{D^2} + {d^2}}  - D$<br>$ = D\\left( {1 + \\frac{1}{2}\\frac{{{d^2}}}{{{D^2}}}} \\right) - D = D\\left[ {1 + \\frac{{{d^2}}}{{2{D^2}}} - 1} \\right] = \\frac{{{d^2}}}{{2D}}$<br>$\\Delta x = \\frac{{{d^2}}}{{2 \\times 10d}} = \\frac{d}{{20}} = \\frac{{5\\lambda }}{{20}} = \\frac{\\lambda }{4}$<br>$\\Delta \\phi  = \\frac{{2\\pi }}{\\lambda }.\\frac{\\lambda }{4} = \\frac{\\pi }{2}$<br>So, intensity at the point $P$ is<br>$I = {I_0}{\\cos ^2}\\frac{\\phi }{2} = {I_0}{\\cos ^2}\\frac{\\pi }{4} = \\frac{{{I_0}}}{2}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2016,'NEET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(31728,'physics',12,132,301,'Electric flux through the Gaussian surface ${S}$, as shown in the figure, is ${\\dfrac{q_{1}+q_{2}}{\\varepsilon_{0}}}$. Electric field E on the Gaussian surface is due to','[{"text": "${q_{1}}$ and ${q_{2}}$ only", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${q_{3}, q_{4}, q_{5}}$ and ${q_{6}}$ only", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${q_{1}, q_{3}}$ and ${q_{5}}$ only", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "all charges", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'The Field at any point on the surface of the conductor is the resultant of fields produced by all the charges. Correct option is (4).',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/12_31728_question_1.png'),
(31729,'physics',19,138,316,'What is the number of significant figure in $\\left( {3.20 + 4.80} \\right) \\times {10^5}?$','[{"text": "$4$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$5$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$3$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'$\\left( {3.20 + 4.80} \\right) \\times {10^5} = 8.00 \\times {10^5}$<br>Number of significant figures are &nbsp;3.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(31730,'physics',18,119,418,'A spherical mirror forms an image of magnification $m =  \\pm 3.$ The object distance, if focal length of mirror is $24{\\rm{ }}cm,{\\rm{ }}$ may be','[{"text": "$32{\\\\rm{ }}cm,{\\\\rm{ }}16{\\\\rm{ }}cm$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$32{\\\\rm{ }}cm,{\\\\rm{ }}24{\\\\rm{ }}cm{\\\\rm{   }}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$16{\\\\rm{ }}cm$ only", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$32{\\\\rm{ }}cm$ only", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'As, magnification, $m = \\frac{f}{{f - u}}$<br>If, $f =  - 24$ and $m =  + 3,$ then<br>$3 = \\frac{{ - 24}}{{ - 24 - {\\rm{u}}}}$<br>$ \\Rightarrow u =  - 16\\,cm$<br>Now, if, $m =  - 3,$ then <br>$ - 3 = \\frac{{ - 24}}{{ - 24 - {\\rm{u}}}} \\Rightarrow {\\rm{u}} =  - 32\\,cm$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(31731,'physics',13,800,2181,'A linear aperture whose width is $0.02\\,cm$ is placed immediately infront of a lens of focal length  $60\\,cm$.  The aperture is illuminated normally by a parallel beam of wavelength $5 \\times {10^{ - 5}}cm.$  The distance of the first dark band of the diffraction pattern from the centre of the screen is','[{"text": "$0.10\\\\,cm$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$0.25\\\\,cm$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$0.20\\\\,cm$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$0.15\\\\,cm$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'The distance of first minima from the centre of the screen is <br>$y = \\frac{{\\lambda D}}{a} = \\frac{{5 \\times {{10}^{ - 5}} \\times {{10}^{ - 2}} \\times 0.6}}{{0.02 \\times {{10}^{ - 2}}}} = 0.15cm$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2016,'NEET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(31732,'physics',12,132,301,'Two charges ${Q,-3 Q}$ are is kept in the cavity inside an uncharged metal, as shown in the figure. Electric flux through the Gaussian surface ${S}$ inside the metal shown in figure is','[{"text": "${\\\\dfrac{2 Q}{\\\\varepsilon_{0}}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "zero", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${\\\\dfrac{-2 Q}{\\\\varepsilon_{0}}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\dfrac{3 Q}{2 \\\\varepsilon_{0}}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Electric field inside the conducting medium is always zero under electrostatic condition. ${E=0 \\Rightarrow}$ Flux ${=0}$. Option (2) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/12_31732_question_1.png'),
(31733,'physics',19,138,316,'The result after adding  $3.8 \\times {10^{ - 6}}$ to $4.2 \\times {10^{ - 5}}$ with due regard to significant figure is','[{"text": "$45.8 \\\\times {10^{ - 6}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$4.58 \\\\times {10^{ - 5}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$0.458 \\\\times {10^{ - 4}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$4.6 \\\\times {10^{ - 5}}$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'$3.8 \\times {10^{ - 6}} + 4.2 \\times {10^{ - 5}}$<br>$ = \\left( {3.8 \\times {{10}^{ - 1}} + 4.2} \\right) \\times {10^{ - 5}}$<br>$ = \\left( {0.38 + 4.2} \\right) \\times {10^{ - 5}}$<br>$ = \\left( {4.58} \\right){10^{ - 5}}$<br>Rounding off to one place of decimal.<br>The sum $ = 4.6 \\times {10^{ - 5}}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(31734,'physics',18,119,418,'With a concave mirror, an object is placed at a distance ${x_1}$ from the principle focus, on the principle axis. The image is formed at a distance ${x_2}$ from the principle focus. The focal length of the mirror is','[{"text": "$\\\\sqrt {\\\\frac{{{x_1}}}{{{x_2}}}} $", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\sqrt {{x_1}{x_2}} $", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{{{x_1} + {x_2}}}{2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${x_1}{x_2}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'$\\frac{1}{u} + \\frac{1}{v} = \\frac{1}{f}$<br>$\\frac{1}{{f + {x_1}}} + \\frac{1}{{f + {x_2}}} = \\frac{1}{f}\\,$<br>$ \\Rightarrow \\frac{{f + {x_2} + f + {x_1}}}{{\\left( {f + {x_1}} \\right)\\left( {f + {x_2}} \\right)}} = \\frac{1}{f}$<br>$f = \\sqrt {{x_1}{x_2}} $<br>This is Newton’s mirror formula.<br>',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(31735,'physics',13,799,2175,'Young’s double slit experiment is first performed in air and then in a medium other than air. It is found that ${8^{th}}$ bright fringe in the medium lies where ${5^{th}}$ dark fringe lies in air. The refractive index of the medium is nearly','[{"text": "1.59", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "1.69", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "1.78", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "1.25", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'${\\left( {{y_8}} \\right)_{{\\rm{Bright,}}\\,{\\rm{medium}}}} = {\\left( {{y_5}} \\right)_{{\\rm{Dark,}}\\,{\\rm{air}}}}$<br>$\\frac{{8{\\lambda _m}D}}{d} = \\left( {\\frac{{{\\rm{2(5)}} - {\\rm{1}}}}{{\\rm{2}}}} \\right)\\frac{{\\lambda D}}{d}$<br>$\\frac{{8\\lambda }}{\\mu }\\frac{D}{d} = \\frac{9}{2}\\frac{{\\lambda D}}{d} \\Rightarrow \\mu  = \\frac{{16}}{9} = 1.78$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2017,'NEET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(31736,'physics',12,80,304,'The variation of electric field ${(E)}$ with distance ${d}$ from an infinite thin sheet of charge can be represented as<div class="matching-question"></div>','[{"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'The electric field produced by a large thin sheet of charge having charge density ${\\sigma}$ is ${\\dfrac{\\sigma}{2 \\varepsilon_{0}}}$.<br>It is independent of the distance of the point from the sheet. So correct option is (3).',NULL,'Medium','Graph/Figure',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(31737,'physics',19,137,314,'What is the relation between pitch and least count of a screw gauge?','[{"text": "${\\\\mathop{\\\\rm Pitch}\\\\nolimits}  = \\\\frac{{L.C}}{{100}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$L.C = \\\\frac{{Pitch}}{{100}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\mathop{\\\\rm Pitch}\\\\nolimits}  = L.C \\\\times 200$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$L.C$ ${\\\\rm{ = }}\\\\frac{{{\\\\rm{Pitch}}}}{{{\\\\rm{number}}\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\rm{of}}\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\rm{divisions}}\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\rm{on}}\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\rm{circular}}\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\rm{scale}}}}$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,NULL,NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(31738,'physics',18,119,418,'The relation between the linear magnification $m$, the object distance $u$ and the focal length $f$  is','[{"text": "$m = \\\\frac{f}{{f + u}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$m = \\\\frac{{f - u}}{f}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$m = \\\\frac{{f + u}}{f}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$m = \\\\frac{f}{{f - u}}$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'$m =  - \\frac{v}{u}\\& \\frac{1}{f} = \\frac{1}{v} + \\frac{1}{u} \\Rightarrow \\frac{u}{f} = \\frac{u}{v} + 1$<br>$ \\Rightarrow  - \\frac{u}{v} = 1 - \\frac{u}{f} \\Rightarrow \\frac{{ - v}}{u} = \\frac{f}{{f - u}} \\Rightarrow {\\mkern 1mu} m = \\frac{f}{{f - u}}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(31739,'physics',13,803,2188,'Two Polaroids ${P_1}$ and ${P_2}$ are placed with their axis perpendicular to each other. Unpolarised light ${I_0}$ is incident on ${P_1}$. A third polaroid ${P_3}$ is kept in between${P_1}$ and ${P_2}$ such that its axis makes an angle $45^\\circ $ with that of ${P_1}$ .The intensity of transmitted light through ${P_2}$ is','[{"text": "$\\\\frac{{{I_0}}}{4}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{{{I_0}}}{8}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{{{I_0}}}{{16}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{{{I_0}}}{2}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'The polaroids are arranged as shown in\nthe figure.<br>${I_1} = \\frac{{{I_0}}}{2},{I_2} = \\frac{{{I_0}}}{2}{\\cos ^2}45^\\circ  = \\frac{{{I_0}}}{4}$<br>${I_3} = \\frac{{{I_0}}}{4}{\\cos ^2}45^\\circ  = \\frac{{{I_0}}}{8}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2017,'NEET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(31740,'physics',12,74,284,'Figures below shows a regular hexagon, in which the charges are placed at the vertices. In which of the following cases the electric field at the centre is zero?','[{"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'In Figure (3) the fields produced by the charges at the opposite vertex cancel each other. So net field at the centre is zero for option (3).',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(31741,'physics',19,137,312,'The decrease in percentage error','[{"text": "Increases the accuracy", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Does not effect the accuracy", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Decreases the accuracy", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both 1 and 3", "isCorrect": false}]',0,NULL,NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(31742,'physics',18,119,418,'An object $2.5\\,cm$  high placed at a distance of $10\\,cm$ from a concave mirror of radius of curvature $30\\,cm$ . The size of the image is','[{"text": "$10.5\\\\,cm$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$9.2\\\\,cm$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$7.5\\\\,cm$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$5.6\\\\,cm$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'$R =  - 30cm \\Rightarrow f =  - 15cm,$<br>$h =  + 2.5cm,\\,u =  - 10cm.$<br>By mirror formula $\\frac{1}{{ - 15}} = \\frac{1}{v} + \\frac{1}{{\\left( { - 10} \\right)}} \\Rightarrow v = 30cm$<br>Also $\\frac{{h\'}}{h} =  - \\frac{v}{u} \\Rightarrow \\frac{1}{{\\left( { + 2.5} \\right)}} = \\frac{{30}}{{\\left( { - 10} \\right)}} \\Rightarrow I =  + 7.5cm$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(31761,'physics',19,137,311,'The mass of a block is 26 $mg$. A student measures the volume and finds it be 25.1 $mg$, 25.2 $mg$, and 25.3 $mg$ in the first, second, third, and fourth trial, respectively. Which of the following statements is true for his measurements?','[{"text": "They are neither precise nor accurate.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "They have good accuracy.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "They have good precision.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "They have poor precision.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'They have good precision',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(31743,'physics',13,799,2172,'In Young’s double slit experiment the separation $d$ between the slits is $2{\\rm{ }}mm$, the wavelength $\\lambda $ of the light used is $5896\\mathop A\\limits^ \\circ  $ and distance $D$ between the screen and slits is $100\\,cm$. It is found that the angular width of the fringes is $0.20^\\circ $ . To increase the fringe angular width to $0.21^\\circ $   (with same $\\lambda \\,{\\rm{and}}\\,D$) the separation between the slits needs to be changed to','[{"text": "$2.1\\\\,mm$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1.7\\\\,mm$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1.9\\\\,mm$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$1.8\\\\,mm$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Given that <br>$d = 2mm = 2 \\times {10^{ - 3}}m,{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} \\lambda  = 5896\\mathop A\\limits^ \\circ  \\,\\, = 5896 \\times {10^{ - 10}}m$<br> $D = 100cm = 100 \\times {10^{ - 2}}m$<br>Angular width of a fringe is $\\alpha  = \\frac{\\lambda }{d}$<br>$\\therefore \\quad \\frac{{{\\alpha _1}}}{{{\\alpha _2}}} = \\frac{{{d_2}}}{{{d_1}}} = \\frac{{20}}{{21}}$<br>${d_2} = \\frac{{20}}{{21}}{d_1} = \\frac{{20}}{{21}} \\times 2\\,mm = \\frac{{40}}{{21}} = 1.9\\,mm$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2018,'NEET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(31744,'physics',12,73,280,'If point charges ${Q_1} = 2 \\times {10^{ - 7}}C\\,\\,{\\text{and }}{Q_2} = 3 \\times {10^{ - 7}}C$ are at $30 \\mathrm{~cm}$ separation, then find electrostatic force between them.','[{"text": "$2 \\\\times {10^{ - 3}}\\\\,N$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$6 \\\\times {10^{ - 3}}\\\\,N$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$5 \\\\times {10^{ - 3}}\\\\,N$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1 \\\\times {10^{ - 3}}\\\\,N$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'$\\because$ Electrostatic force, $F=k \\dfrac{Q_{1} Q_{2}}{r^{2}}$ <br> $ = 9 \\times {10^9} \\times \\frac{{2 \\times {{10}^{ - 7}} \\times 3 \\times {{10}^{ - 7}}}}{{{{\\left( {30 \\times {{10}^{ - 2}}} \\right)}^2}}}$<br> $ = 6 \\times {10^{ - 3}}\\,{\\rm{N}}$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(31745,'physics',19,137,312,'The mean absolute error in the time period of 	second’s pendulum is 0.05 $s$. To express maximum estimate of error, the time period should be written as','[{"text": "$\\\\left( {2.00 \\\\pm 0.05} \\\\right)s$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\left( {2.00 \\\\pm 0.10} \\\\right)s$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\left( {2.00 \\\\pm 0.01} \\\\right)s$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\left( {2.00 + 0.025} \\\\right)s$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Mean time period $T = 2.00\\,\\sec $<br>Mean absolute error $\\Delta T = 0.05\\,\\sec .$<br>To express maximum estimate of error, the time period should be written as $\\left( {2.00 \\pm 0.05} \\right)\\sec .$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(31746,'physics',18,119,418,'If a spherical mirror is immersed in a liquid it’s focal length will','[{"text": "Increase", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Decrease", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Remain unchanged", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Depend on the nature of liquid", "isCorrect": false}]',2,NULL,NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(31747,'physics',13,803,2187,'Unpolarised light is incident from air on a plane surface of a material of refractive index $\'\\mu \'$. At a particular angle of incidence ‘i’, it is found that the reflected and refracted rays are perpendicular to each other. Which of the following options is correct for this situation?','[{"text": "$i = {\\\\sin ^{ - 1}}\\\\left( {\\\\frac{1}{\\\\mu }} \\\\right)$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$i = {\\\\tan ^{ - 1}}\\\\left( {\\\\frac{1}{\\\\mu }} \\\\right)$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Reflected light is polarised with its electric\\nvector perpendicular to the plane of\\nincidence", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Reflected light is polarised with its electric\\nvector parallel to the plane of incidence", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Brewster\'s angle. $i = {\\tan ^{ - 1}}\\,\\mu $<br>In polarised light the electric vectors are perpendicular to the plane of incidence. So option (3) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2018,'NEET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(31748,'physics',12,73,280,'Two identical conducting balls $A$ and $B$ have positive charges $q_{1}$ and $q_{2}$ respectively but $q_{1} \\neq q_{2}$. The balls are brought together so that they touch each other and then kept in their original positions. The force between them is','[{"text": "less than that before the balls touched", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "greater than that before the balls touched", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "same as that before the balls touched", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "zero", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'According to Coulomb\'s law, the force of repulsion between them is given by  $(F = \\frac{{{q_1}{q_2}}}{{4\\pi {\\varepsilon _0}{r^2}}})$  <img src=\'/img/data/physics/12_31748_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />   When the charged spheres $A$ and $B$ are brought in contact, each sphere will attain equal charge ${q\'}$<br> $q\' = \\frac{{{q_1} + {q_2}}}{2}$<br>Now, the force of repulsion between them at the same distance $r$ is <br> ${F^\\prime } = \\frac{{{q^1} \\times {q^1}}}{{4\\pi {\\varepsilon _0}{r^2}}} = \\frac{1}{{4\\pi {\\varepsilon _0}}}$ <br> $\\frac{{\\left( {\\frac{{{q_1} + {q_2}}}{2}} \\right)\\left( {\\frac{{{q_1} + {q_2}}}{2}} \\right)}}{{{r^2}}} = \\frac{{{{\\left( {\\frac{{{q_1} + {q_2}}}{2}} \\right)}^2}}}{{4\\pi {\\varepsilon _0}{r^2}}}{\\rm{ }}$<br> As, ${\\left( {\\frac{{{q_1} + {q_2}}}{2}} \\right)^2} > {q_1}{q_2}$<br> $\\therefore \\quad \\quad {F^\\prime } > F$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(31749,'physics',19,137,312,'If $X = \\frac{A}{B}$ and $\\Delta X,\\Delta A$ and $\\,\\Delta B$ are maximum absolute errors in $X$, $A$ and $B$ respectively, then the maximum fractional error in $X$ is given by','[{"text": "$\\\\Delta X = \\\\Delta A\\\\, - \\\\Delta B$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{{\\\\Delta X}}{X} = \\\\frac{{\\\\Delta A}}{A}\\\\, + \\\\frac{{\\\\Delta B}}{B}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{{\\\\Delta X}}{X} = \\\\frac{{\\\\Delta A}}{A}\\\\, - \\\\frac{{\\\\Delta B}}{B}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\Delta X = \\\\Delta A\\\\, + \\\\Delta B$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'When two quantities are divided, their maximum fractional or relative errors are added up hence: $\\frac{{\\Delta X}}{X} = \\frac{{\\Delta A}}{A}\\, + \\frac{{\\Delta B}}{B}$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
