(36749,'physics',25,897,2064,'The elastic limit of brass is $3.5 \\times {10^{10}}\\,N{\\rm{/}}{m^2}$. Find the maximum load that can be applied to a brass wire of $0.75\\,mm$ diameter without exceeding the elastic limit.','[{"text": "$4.12 \\\\times {10^4}\\\\,N$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$5.15 \\\\times {10^4}\\\\,N$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$0.55 \\\\times {10^4}\\\\,N$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1.55 \\\\times {10^4}\\\\,N$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Stress $ = \\frac{F}{A}$; For elastic limit,${\\rm{stress }} = 3.5 \\times {10^{10}}\\,N{\\rm{/}}{m^2}{\\rm{ (given)}}.$<br>${\\rm{Thus}},{\\rm{ }}F = \\left( {\\pi {r^2}} \\right) \\times {\\rm{ stress}}$<br>$ = 3.14 \\times {\\left( {\\frac{{0.75}}{2} \\times {{10}^{ - 3}}} \\right)^2} \\times 3.5 \\times {10^{10}}$<br>$ = 3.14 \\times \\frac{{{{(0.75)}^2}}}{4} \\times 3.5 \\times {10^4}$<br>$ = 1.55 \\times {10^4}\\,N$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(36750,'physics',26,1133,2710,'A cork is submerged in water by a spring attached to the bottom of a bowl. When the bowl is kept in an elevator moving upward with an acceleration, the length of spring','[{"text": "Increases", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Remains unchanged", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Decreases", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "None of these", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Pseudo force acts on the cork in the downward direction. So spring undergoes compression.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(36752,'physics',27,1380,3312,'The coefficient of apparent expansion of a liquid depends upon','[{"text": "The nature of the liquid", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "The scale of temperature", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Mass of the liquid", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Pressure of the liquid", "isCorrect": false}]',0,NULL,NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(36753,'physics',25,896,2063,'The relationship between Young\'s modulus $Y$, bulk modulus $B$ and modulus of rigidity $\\eta$ is','[{"text": "$Y=\\\\dfrac{9 \\\\eta B}{\\\\eta+3 B}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\eta=\\\\dfrac{9 Y B}{Y+3 B}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$Y=\\\\dfrac{9 \\\\eta B}{3 \\\\eta+B}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$Y=\\\\dfrac{3 \\\\eta B}{9 \\\\eta+B}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,': As,$\\frac{9}{Y} = \\frac{3}{\\eta } + \\frac{1}{B} = \\frac{{3B + \\eta }}{{\\eta B}}$<br>or $Y = \\frac{{9\\eta B}}{{3B + \\eta }}$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(36754,'physics',26,1133,2710,'A boat having a length of 3 meters and breadth 2 meters is floating on a lake. The boat sinks by one $cm$ when a man gets on it. The mass of the man is','[{"text": "$62\\\\,\\\\,kg$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$60\\\\,\\\\,kg$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$128\\\\,\\\\,kg$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$72\\\\,\\\\,kg$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Weight of $$\\operatorname{man}=$$ Change in buyoancy force =volume $$\\times$$ density $ = 3 \\times 2 \\times \\frac{1}{{100}} \\times {10^3} = 60\\,kg$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(36755,'physics',23,869,1995,'A stick of length $\\dfrac{10}{3}$ metre is held vertically with one end on a horizontal floor. It is then allowed to fall on the floor. Assuming that the end of the stick in contact with the floor and it does not slip, the speed of the other end of the rod when it hits the floor is<br>(Take $g = 10\\,m{\\rm{/}}{s^2}$)','[{"text": "$5\\\\,m/s$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$10\\\\,m/s$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$9\\\\,m/s$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$3\\\\,m/s$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Moment of inertia of the rod about the lower end,<br>$I{\\text{ }} = {I_c} + M{h^2}$<br>$ = \\frac{{M{L^2}}}{{12}} + M{\\left( {\\frac{L}{2}} \\right)^2} = \\frac{{M{L^2}}}{3}$<br>From conservation of energy,<br>$\\frac{1}{2}I{\\omega ^2} = Mg\\frac{L}{2}$<br>$ \\Rightarrow \\omega {\\text{ }} = \\sqrt {\\frac{{MgL}}{I}} $<br>$ = \\sqrt {\\frac{{3\\,g}}{{\\,L}}} \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\left( {I = \\frac{{M{L^2}}}{3}} \\right)$<br>$\\therefore \\quad v = \\omega L = \\sqrt {3gL} $<br>$ = \\sqrt {3 \\times 10 \\times \\frac{{10}}{3}} $<br>$ = 10\\,m/s$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(36756,'physics',27,1380,3311,'A metal ball suspended from the hook of a spring balance is kept immersed in a liquid other than water. On increasing the temperature of this liquid, the reading in the spring balance.','[{"text": "Increases", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Decreases", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Remains same", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "May increases or decreases", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'On heating density of liquid decreases and hence buoyancy force decreases and the tension in the spring increases.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(36793,'physics',27,1381,3314,'Water equivalent of a body is measured in','[{"text": "$m^{3}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "kelvin", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$kg$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "calorie", "isCorrect": false}]',2,NULL,NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(36757,'physics',25,896,2059,'A metal rod is fixed rigidly at two ends so as to prevent its thermal expansion. If $L, \\alpha$ and $Y$ respectively denote the length of the rod, coefficient of linear thermal expansion and Young\'s modulus of its material, then for an increase in temperature of the rod by $\\Delta T$, the longitudinal stress developed in the rod is','[{"text": "inversely proportional to $\\\\alpha$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "inversely proportional to $Y$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "directly proportional to $\\\\dfrac{\\\\Delta T}{Y}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "independent of $L$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Strain $=\\dfrac{\\Delta L}{L}$<br>As $\\Delta L=\\alpha L \\Delta T \\quad \\therefore \\dfrac{\\Delta L}{L}=\\alpha \\Delta T$<br>As $Y=\\dfrac{\\text { Stress }}{\\text { Strain }}$<br>$\\therefore \\quad$ Stress $=Y \\times$ Strain $=Y \\alpha \\Delta T$<br>So correct option is (4).',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(36758,'physics',26,1133,2710,'A cubical block of density ${10^2}Kg/{m^3}$ is floating in water. Find the fraction of the volume of the cube present outside water.','[{"text": "0.2", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "0.1", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "0.5", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "0.9", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'As the cube is in equilibrium<br>${F_b} = {F_g}$<br>${\\rho _l}{V^\\prime }g = {\\rho _s}Vg$<br>${\\rho _l} = {10^3}Kg/{m^3},\\quad {\\rho _s} = {10^2}Kg/{m^3}$<br>$$V^{\\prime}$$ - volume of the cube present inside water<br>$$V$$ - volume of the cube<br>$$\\begin{aligned}& 10^{3} V^{\\prime}=10^{2} V \\\\& \\dfrac{V^{\\prime}}{V}=\\dfrac{1}{10}=0.1\\end{aligned}$$<br>The fractional volume present outside is 0.9',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(36760,'physics',27,1380,3312,'A vessel is partly filled with a liquid. Coefficients of cubical expansion of material of vessel and liquid are $\\gamma_{V}$ and $\\gamma_{L}$ respectively. If the system is heated, then volume unoccupied by the liquid will necessarily','[{"text": "Remain unchanged if $\\\\gamma_{V}=\\\\gamma_{L}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Increase if $\\\\gamma_{V}=\\\\gamma_{L}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Decrease if $\\\\gamma_{V}=\\\\gamma_{L}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "None of the above", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Since the vessel is partly filled, volume of the vessel is greater than that of the liquid. Hence on heating expansion of vessel will be greater than that of liquid, since $\\gamma_{V}=\\gamma_{L}$. It means unoccupied volume will necessarily increase. So, option (2) is correct.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(36761,'physics',25,900,2067,'Proof resilience is related to','[{"text": "potential energy stored in the elastic body", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "stiffness of beam", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "elastic fatigue", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "elastic relaxation", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Proof resilience is the maximum energy that can be absorbed upto the elastic limit, without creating permanent distortion.<br>So correct option is (1)',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(36762,'physics',26,1133,2710,'A block of aluminium of mass $1\\,kg$ and volume $3.6 \\times {10^{ - 4}}\\,{m^3}$ is suspended from a string and then completely immersed in a container of water. The decrease in tension in the string after immersion is','[{"text": "$1.0\\\\,\\\\,N$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$3.6\\\\,\\\\,N$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$6.2\\\\,\\\\,N$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$9.8\\\\,\\\\,N$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Here, mass of block, $m = 1\\,kg$<br>Volume of a block, $V = 3.6 \\times {10^{ - 4}}\\,{m^3}$<br>Tension in the string, $$T=m g-V \\rho_{\\text {water }} g$$<br>$$\\therefore$$ Decrease in the tension of string<br>$ = mg - \\left[ {mg - V{\\rho _{water{\\text{ }}}}g} \\right] = V{\\rho _{water{\\text{ }}}}g$<br>$ = \\left( {3.6 \\times {{10}^{ - 4}}\\,{m^3}} \\right) \\times \\left( {{{10}^3}kg{m^{ - 3}}} \\right) \\times \\left( {10\\,m{s^{ - 2}}} \\right) = 3.6\\,N.$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(36763,'physics',27,1380,3311,'The coefficient of volume expansion of a liquid is $49 \\times {10^{ - 5}}\\,{K^{ - 1}}$. Calculate the fractional change in its density when the temperature is raised by $30^\\circ C.$','[{"text": "$3.0 \\\\times 10^{-2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$7.5 \\\\times 10^{-2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1.1 \\\\times 10^{-2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1.5 \\\\times 10^{-2}$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'When the temperature of a liquid is increased by $\\Delta T^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C}$ the mass will remain unchanged while due to thermal expansion volume will increase and becomes $V^{\\prime}$.<br>$V^{\\prime}=V(1+\\gamma \\Delta T)$<br>where $\\gamma$ is the coefficient of volume expansion of liquid<br>$\\therefore \\quad \\rho^{\\prime}=\\dfrac{m}{V^{\\prime}}=\\dfrac{m}{V(1+\\gamma \\Delta T)}=\\dfrac{\\rho}{1+\\gamma \\Delta T}$<br>Fractional change in density $=\\dfrac{\\rho-\\rho^{\\prime}}{\\rho}$<br>$\\begin{aligned}& =\\left(1-\\dfrac{\\rho^{\\prime}}{\\rho}\\right)=\\left(1-\\dfrac{1}{1+\\gamma \\Delta T}\\right) \\\\& =\\dfrac{\\gamma \\Delta T}{1+\\gamma \\Delta T}=\\dfrac{49 \\times 10^{-5} \\times 30}{1+49 \\times 10^{-5} \\times 30} \\\\& =\\dfrac{0.0147}{1.0147}=0.0145 \\approx 1.5 \\times 10^{-2}\\end{aligned}$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(36765,'physics',25,896,2059,'The ratio of the length of two wires ${a}$ and ${b}$ of same materials is ${1: 3}$ and the ratio of their diameters is ${3: 1}$. They are stretched by the same force then the ratio of increase in lengths  will be','[{"text": "${1: 9}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${27: 1}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${1: 27}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${9: 1}$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Given that ${: l_{a}: l_{b}=1: 3, d_{a}: d_{b}=3: 1}$<br>${F_{a}: F_{b}=1: 1}$<br>Since, wires ${a}$ and ${b}$ are made of same material.<br>${\\therefore \\quad Y_{a}=Y_{b}}$<br>${\\dfrac{F_{a} l_{a}}{A_{a} \\Delta l_{a}}=\\dfrac{F_{b} l_{b}}{A_{b} \\Delta l_{b}}}$<br>$\\frac{{\\Delta {l_a}}}{{\\Delta {l_b}}} = \\left( {\\frac{{{F_a}{l_a}}}{{{A_a}}}} \\right)\\left( {\\frac{{{A_b}}}{{{F_b}{l_b}}}} \\right)$<br>$ = \\left( {\\frac{{{F_a}}}{{{F_b}}}} \\right)\\left( {\\frac{{{l_a}}}{{{l_b}}}} \\right)\\left( {\\frac{{\\frac{{\\pi d_b^2}}{4}}}{{\\frac{{\\pi d_a^2}}{4}}}} \\right)$<br>$ = \\left( {\\frac{{{F_a}}}{{{F_b}}}} \\right)\\left( {\\frac{{{l_a}}}{{{l_b}}}} \\right){\\left( {\\frac{{{d_b}}}{{{d_a}}}} \\right)^2} = \\left( {\\frac{1}{1}} \\right)\\left( {\\frac{1}{3}} \\right){\\left( {\\frac{1}{3}} \\right)^2}$<br>$ = 1:27$.<br>So correct option is (3)',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(36766,'physics',26,1133,2710,'The volume of the hollow portion of a sphere is $$\\dfrac{3}{4}$$ of the external volume of the sphere. If it floats in a liquid of relative density $$\\dfrac{3}{2}$$, half of its external volume immersed, the relative density of the material of the solid is:','[{"text": "3", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "2", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "2.4", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "1.8", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Let $$R$$ be external radius of sphere and $$r$$ be radius of cavity.<br>Given, $$\\dfrac{4}{3} \\pi r^{3}=\\dfrac{3}{4}\\left(\\dfrac{4}{3} \\pi R^{3}\\right) ; \\quad r^{3}=\\dfrac{3}{4} R^{3}$$<br>Further, $\\frac{4}{3}\\pi \\left( {{R^3} - {r^3}} \\right)\\rho g = \\frac{1}{2} \\times \\frac{4}{3}\\pi {R^3} \\times \\frac{3}{2}{\\rho _w} \\times g\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\left( 1 \\right)$<br>Using (1), $$\\dfrac{\\rho}{\\rho_{w}}=3$$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(36767,'physics',27,1380,3312,'The absolute coefficient of expansion of a liquid is 7 times that the volume coefficient of expansion of the vessel. Then the ratio of absolute and apparent expansion of the liquid is','[{"text": "$\\\\dfrac{1}{7}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{7}{6}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{6}{7}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "None of these", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Apparent coefficient of volume expansion $\\gamma_{a p p .}=\\gamma_{L}-\\gamma_{s}=7 \\gamma_{s}-\\gamma_{s}=6 \\gamma_{s}\\left(\\right.$ given $\\left.\\gamma_{L}=7 \\gamma_{s}\\right)$<br>ratio of absolute and apparent expansion of liquid<br>$\\dfrac{\\gamma_{L}}{\\gamma_{\\text {app. }}}=\\dfrac{7 \\gamma_{s}}{6 \\gamma_{s}}=\\dfrac{7}{6}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(36769,'physics',25,896,2059,'How much force is required to produce an increase of ${0.2 \\%}$ in the length of a brass wire of diameter $0.6\\,mm$ ? Given Young\'s modulus for brass ${=0.9 \\times 10^{11} {~N} / {m}^{2}}$','[{"text": "nearly 17 $N$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "nearly 51 $N$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "nearly 34 $N$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "nearly 68 $N$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Given : $\\frac{{\\Delta \\,l}}{l} = 0.2\\,\\%  = \\frac{{0.2}}{{100}} = 0.002,d = 0.6\\,\\,mm,$<br>$Y = 0.9 \\times {10^{11}}\\,N/{m^2}\\,r = \\frac{d}{2} = \\frac{{0.6}}{2} = 0.3\\,\\,mm$<br>$ \\Rightarrow A = \\pi {r^2} = 0.283\\,\\,m{m^2}$<br>$Y = \\frac{{Fl}}{{A\\Delta l}} \\Rightarrow F = \\frac{{YA\\,\\Delta l}}{l}$<br>$ = \\left( {0.9 \\times {{10}^{11}}\\,N/{m^2}} \\right) \\times $<br>$\\left( {0.283 \\times {{10}^{ - 6}}\\,{m^2}} \\right) \\times (0.002)$<br>$ = 50.94\\,N = 51\\,N.$<br>So correct option is (2)',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(36770,'physics',26,1133,2710,'A sphere of solid material of specific gravity 8 has a concentric spherical cavity and just sinks in water. Then the ratio of radius of cavity to that of outer radius of the sphere must be:','[{"text": "$$\\\\dfrac{(3)^{1 / 3}}{2}$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$\\\\dfrac{(5)^{1 / 3}}{2}$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$\\\\dfrac{(7)^{1 / 3}}{2}$$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$$\\\\dfrac{(9)^{1 / 3}}{2}$$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'As the object just sinks $$m g=F_{B}$$<br>$$\\Rightarrow 8 \\times\\left[\\dfrac{4}{3} \\pi R^{3}-\\dfrac{4}{3} \\pi r^{3}\\right] g=1 \\times \\dfrac{4}{3} \\pi R^{3} \\times g$$<br>$$\\Rightarrow 8\\left[1-\\dfrac{r^{3}}{R^{3}}\\right]=1 \\quad \\Rightarrow\\left(\\dfrac{r}{R}\\right)=\\left(\\dfrac{7}{8}\\right)^{1 / 3}=\\left(\\dfrac{7^{1 / 3}}{2}\\right)$$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(36771,'physics',23,871,2006,'A small block of mass $3\\,kg$ is attached to a cord passing through a hole in a horizontal frictionless surface. The block is originally revolving in a circle of radius $0.5\\,m$ about the hole with a tangential velocity of $6\\,m{\\rm{/}}s.$ The cord is then pulled slowly from below, shortening the radius of the circle in which the block revolves. The breaking strength of the cord is $1000\\,N.$ What will be the radius  of the circle when the cord breaks?','[{"text": "$0.1\\\\,m$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$0.9\\\\,m$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$0.3\\\\,m$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$0.7\\\\,m$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'The tension of the rope is the only net force on the block and it does not exert any torque about the axis of rotation. Hence, the angular momentum of the block about the axis should remain conserved.<br>$ \\Rightarrow mvr = \\,$ constant<br>Let $r_{2}, \\mathrm{v}_{2}, \\mathrm{~T}_{2}$ be the radius, velocity and tension respectively when the string breaks.According to law of conservation of momentum,<br> $\\mathrm{mv}_{1} \\mathrm{r}_{1}=\\mathrm{mv}_{2} \\mathrm{r}_{2}$<br>$\\therefore \\quad m{v_1}{r_1} = m\\sqrt {\\frac{{{r_2}{T_2}}}{m}{r_2}} $<br>$\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\left( {\\because {T_2} = \\frac{{mv_2^2}}{{{r_2}}}} \\right)$<br>${m^2}v_1^2r_1^2 = {m^2}\\frac{{{r_2}{T_2}}}{m}r_2^2$<br>${r_2} = {\\left( {\\frac{{mv_1^2r_1^2}}{{\\,{T_2}}}} \\right)^{\\frac{1}{3}}} = {\\left( {\\frac{{3 \\times 36 \\times 0.25}}{{1000}}} \\right)^{\\frac{1}{3}}}$<br>$ = {\\left( {\\frac{{27}}{{1000}}} \\right)^{\\frac{1}{3}}}$<br>$\\therefore {r_2} = 0.3\\,m$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(36772,'physics',27,1380,3312,'A 2 litres glass flask contains some mercury. It is found that at all temperatures the volume of the air inside the flask remains the same. The volume of the mercury inside the flask is $\\left( {\\alpha  = 9 \\times {{10}^{ - 6}}\\,^\\circ {C^{ - 1}},{\\gamma _m} = 1.8 \\times {{10}^{ - 4}}\\,^\\circ {C^{ - 1}}} \\right)$','[{"text": "$150\\\\,cc$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1500\\\\,cc$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$300\\\\,cc$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$3000$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'It is given that the volume of air in the flask remains the same. This means that the expansion in volume of the container is exactly equal to the volume expansion of mercury,<br>$\\begin{aligned}& \\text { i.e. } \\Delta V_{v}=\\Delta V_{m} \\\\& c \\Rightarrow V_{c} \\gamma_{c} \\Delta T=V_{m} \\gamma_{m} \\Delta T \\\\& \\therefore V_{c}=\\dfrac{V_{c} \\gamma_{c}}{\\gamma_{m}}=\\dfrac{V_{c} \\times 3 \\alpha}{\\gamma_{m}} \\quad\\left(\\because \\gamma_{c}=3 \\alpha\\right) \\\\& \\Rightarrow V_{m}=\\dfrac{2000 \\times 3 \\times 9 \\times 10^{-6}}{1.8 \\times 10^{-4}} \\\\& V_{m}=300 c c\\end{aligned}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(36774,'physics',26,1133,2710,'An ice cube of edge $1\\,cm$ melts in a gravityfree container. The approximate surface area of water formed is:','[{"text": "${[36\\\\pi ]^{1/3}}\\\\,c{m^2}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${[36\\\\pi ]^{1/2}}\\\\,c{m^2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$36 \\\\pi m^{2}$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${[36\\\\pi ]^2}\\\\,c{m^2}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Water will take spherical shape.<br>$V = \\frac{4}{3}\\pi {R^3} \\Rightarrow 1\\,c{m^3} = \\frac{4}{3}\\pi {R^3} \\Rightarrow R = {\\left[ {\\frac{3}{{4\\pi }}} \\right]^{1/3}}\\,cm.$<br>Surface $ = 4\\pi {R^2} = 4\\pi {\\left[ {\\frac{3}{{4\\pi }}} \\right]^{2/3}}\\,c{m^2}$<br>$ = {(4\\pi )^{1/3}} \\times {(9)^{1/3}}\\,c{m^2} = {(36\\pi )^{1/3}}\\,c{m^2}$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(36777,'physics',27,1381,3314,'The heat capacity of a material depends upon','[{"text": "The change in temperature", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Mass", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Nature of substance", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "All of the above", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'The heat capacity of a material depends on all of the above factors: the change in temperature, mass and the nature of the substance.Heat capacity is the amount of heat energy required to raise the temeprature of a saubstance, and it is influenced by these three factors in combination.Correct option is (4).',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(36778,'physics',26,1133,2710,'A metallic block weighs $15\\,N$ in air. It weighs $12\\,N$ when immersed in water and $13\\,N$ when immersed in another liquid. What is the specific gravity of the liquid?','[{"text": "$1 / 3$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2 / 3$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$12 / 13$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$13 / 15$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Given that $m g=15$<br>$\\begin{aligned}& m g-\\rho_{w} V g=12 \\Rightarrow \\rho_{w} V g=3 \\\\& m g-\\rho_{l} V g=13 \\Rightarrow \\rho_{l} V g=2 \\\\& \\dfrac{\\rho_{l}}{\\rho_{w}}=\\dfrac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(36780,'physics',25,896,2059,'A $45 - kg$ traffic light is suspended with two steel wires of equal lengths and radii of $0.5\\,cm$. If the wires make an angle of $30^{\\circ}$ with the horizontal, the fractional increase in their length due to the weight of the traffic light is $C \\times {10^{ - 5}}.$ Find $C$. (Young\'s modulus of steel $ = 2 \\times {10^{11}}N{m^{ - 2}}.$<br> $\\pi=3.14$ and $g = 9.8\\,m{\\rm{/}}{s^2}$)','[{"text": "5.81", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "2.81", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "7.61", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "3.81", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Area of cross-section of the wire,<br>$a = \\pi {r^2} = \\pi  \\times {0.005^2}\\,{m^2}$<br> The traffic light of weight $Mg$ is suspended with two steel wires each of length $L$ (say) as shown below.  <img src=\'/img/data/physics/25_36780_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />  Let $T$ be the tension in each of the two wires and $l$ be extension produced in their lengths. <br>From the equilibrium of the system, we have <br>$T\\cos 60^\\circ  + T\\cos 60^\\circ  = Mg$<br>$i.e.,{\\rm{ }}T = \\frac{{Mg}}{{2\\cos 60^\\circ }} = \\frac{{45 \\times 9:8}}{{2 \\times 0.5}} = 441\\,N$<br>Then tension in a wire acts as the stretching force for it.<br>$\\therefore \\quad Y = \\frac{{TL}}{{al}}$<br>$\\therefore \\frac{l}{{\\,L}} = \\frac{T}{{aY}}$<br>$ = \\frac{{441}}{{\\pi  \\times {{0.005}^2} \\times 20 \\times {{10}^{10}}}}$<br>$ = \\frac{{441}}{{3.14 \\times 5 \\times {{10}^6}}}$<br>$ = \\frac{{8.82}}{{3.14}} \\times {10^{ - 5}} \\approx 2.81 \\times {10^{ - 5}}$<br>$ \\Rightarrow {\\rm{ }}C = 2.81$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(36781,'physics',27,1381,3314,'Heat capacity of a substance is infinite. It means that','[{"text": "Heat is given out", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Heat is taken in", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "No change in temperature whether heat is taken in (or) given out", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "All of the above", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Heat capacity $c=\\dfrac{1}{m} \\dfrac{\\Delta Q}{\\Delta T}$<br>when $\\Delta T = 0\\,\\,\\,{\\mkern 1mu} {\\kern 1pt} c \\to \\infty \\,(\\Delta Q$ can be $(+) v e$ or $(-) v e)$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(36782,'physics',26,1133,2710,'A body floats in water with one third of its volume above the surface of water. If it is placed in some oil, it floats with half of its volume above the oil surface. then, specific gravity of the oil is','[{"text": "$$\\\\dfrac{3}{2}$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$\\\\dfrac{3}{4}$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$\\\\dfrac{4}{3}$$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "1", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'In first case $$\\dfrac{2 V}{3} \\rho_{w} g=W$$<br>second case $$\\dfrac{V}{2} \\rho_{l} g=W$$<br>$$\\dfrac{\\rho_{l}}{\\rho_{w}}=\\dfrac{4}{3}$$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(36784,'physics',25,896,2059,'A stone of $0.5\\,kg$ mass is attached to one end of a metre long aluminium wire of $1\\,mm$ diameter and is suspended vertically. The stone is now rotated in a horizontal plane at a rate, such that the wire makes an angle of $75^{\\circ}$ with the vertical. Find the increase in length of the wire. (Take Young\'s modulus of aluminium $ = 7 \\times {10^{10}}N{m^{ - 2}},\\sin 75^\\circ $$ = 0.97,\\cos 75^\\circ  = 0.26,$$\\pi  = 3.14,g = 10\\,m{\\rm{/}}{s^2}$','[{"text": "$0.35\\\\,m$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$0.73\\\\,m$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$0.92\\\\,m$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$0.15\\\\,m$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'When the stone is rotated so that it makes an angle $75^\\circ $ with the vertical as shown in the figure below, it moves along a circle of radius $OA = r$ (say).  <img src=\'/img/data/physics/25_36784_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' /> $T\\cos 75^\\circ $ is along the vertical and balances the weight of the stone i.e.,<br>$T\\cos 75^\\circ  = Mg$<br>$T = \\frac{{Mg}}{{\\cos 75^\\circ }} = \\frac{{0.5 \\times 10}}{{0.26}} = 19.23\\,N$<br>Now, $Y = \\frac{{F/a}}{{l/L}} = \\frac{{4TL}}{{\\pi {D^2}l}}$<br>$\\therefore l = \\frac{{4TL}}{{\\pi {D^2}Y}}$<br>$ = \\frac{{4 \\times 19.23 \\times 1}}{{\\pi  \\times {{\\left( {{{10}^{ - 3}}} \\right)}^2} \\times 7 \\times {{10}^{10}}}} = \\frac{{76.92}}{{21.98}} \\times {10^{ - 4}}$<br>$ \\approx 3.50 \\times {10^{ - 4}}\\,m$<br>$\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,({\\rm{Rounding}}\\,{\\rm{off}}\\,{\\rm{to}}\\,{\\rm{2}}\\,{\\rm{decimal}}\\,{\\rm{places}})$<br>$\\therefore \\quad l = 0.35\\,mm$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(36785,'physics',27,1381,3314,'Two bodies at different temperatures are mixed in a calorimeter. Which of the following quantities remain conserved?','[{"text": "Total heat of the two bodies", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Internal energy of each body", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Sum of the temperatures of the two bodies", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Total internal energy of the two bodies", "isCorrect": true}]',3,NULL,NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(36786,'physics',26,1133,2710,'An air balloon is floating in water above which a layer of a lighter oil is poured. As the balloon is ruptured, the level of','[{"text": "Only Interface will fall", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Interface will rise", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Free surface will rise", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both interface and free surface will fall.", "isCorrect": true}]',3,NULL,NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/26_36786_question_1.jpg'),
(36789,'physics',27,1381,3314,'Water is used to cool radiators of engines, because','[{"text": "It is easily available", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Of its lower density", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "It has high specific heat", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "It is cheap", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Due to large specific heat of water, it releases or absorb large heat with very small temperature change.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(36790,'physics',26,1133,2710,'The weight indicated on a balance is $$X$$ when a beaker of water is placed on it. A solid object has weight $Y$ in air and displaces weight $Z$ of water when completely immersed.The given diagram shows the object suspended from a light string and completely immersed in the beaker of water. what is the balance reading in the given arrangement ?','[{"text": "$X$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$X+Z$$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$X + Y$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$X + Y - Z$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'New reading $$=$$ weight of water $$(X)+$$ weight of water displaced by the object $$(Z)$$<br>$$\\Rightarrow$$ New reading $$=X+Z(Z$$ - buoyancy force $$)$$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/26_36790_question_1.png'),
(36791,'physics',23,863,1977,'If ${c=\\hat{i} \\times(a \\times \\hat{i})+\\hat{j} \\times(a \\times \\hat{j})+\\hat{k} \\times(a \\times \\hat{k})}$ and ${c=n a}$.  Find  the value of $n$','[{"text": "2", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "5", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "9", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "1", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Let ${a=a_{1} \\hat{i}+a_{2} \\hat{j}+a_{3} \\hat{k}}$<br>$\\therefore c = \\hat i \\times (a \\times \\hat i) + \\hat j \\times (a \\times \\hat j) + \\hat k(a \\times \\hat k)$<br>${ = \\hat i \\times \\left( { - {a_2}\\hat k + {a_3}\\hat j} \\right) + \\hat j \\times }$<br>${\\left( {{a_1}\\hat k - {a_3}\\hat i} \\right) + \\hat k \\times \\left( { - {a_1}\\hat j + {a_2}\\hat i} \\right)}$<br>$ = {a_2}\\hat j + {a_3}\\hat k + {a_1}\\hat i + {a_3}\\hat k + {a_1}\\hat i + {a_2}\\hat j$<br>$ = 2\\left( {{a_1}\\hat i + {a_2}\\hat j + {a_3}\\hat k} \\right) = 2a$<br>$\\therefore n = 2$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(36799,'physics',23,860,1973,'Both the blocks shown in the given arrangement are given together a horizontal velocity towards right. If ${x {~ms}^{-2}}$ be the subsequent acceleration of centre of mass of the system of blocks, find the minimum value of ${x}$ <br>(Given, ${m_{A}=2 m_{B}}$ and ${g=10 {~ms}^{-2}}$, all in SI units)','[{"text": "5", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "2", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "7", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "8", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'${F_{\\text {ext }} \\geq(0.2)(2 m+m)(10)=6 m}$<br>$\\begin{aligned}& \\bar{a}_{{COM}}=\\dfrac{F_{{exI}}}{2 m+m}=\\dfrac{6 m}{2 m+m}=2 {~ms}^{-2} \\\\& \\therefore \\quad x=2\\end{aligned}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/23_36799_question_1.png'),
(36801,'physics',27,1381,3314,'The ratio of specific heat capacity to molar heat capacity of a body','[{"text": "Is dimensionless", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Depends on the mass of the body", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Depends on the molecular weight of the body", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Is a universal constant", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Specific heat capacity $c=\\dfrac{1}{m}\\left(\\dfrac{\\Delta Q}{\\Delta t}\\right)_{1}$<br>Molar heat capacity $C=\\dfrac{1}{n}\\left(\\dfrac{\\Delta Q}{\\Delta t}\\right)_{2}$<br>$\\dfrac{c}{C}=\\dfrac{\\dfrac{n}{n M}\\left(\\dfrac{\\Delta Q}{\\Delta T}\\right)_{1}}{\\left(\\dfrac{\\Delta Q}{\\Delta T}\\right)_{2}} \\quad(m=n M)$<br>so option (3) is correct',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(36802,'physics',26,1134,2713,'When the value of Reynold\'s number is less, the predominant forces are','[{"text": "Viscous forces", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Inertial forces", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Surface tension forces", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Gravitational forces", "isCorrect": false}]',0,NULL,NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(36804,'physics',25,896,2061,'A rubber sphere is surrounded by a liquid in a cylindrical container. A massless piston of area $0.05\\,{m^2}$ floats on the surface of liquid covering cross-section of container completely. When a mass of $10\\,kg$ is placed on the surface of the piston to compress liquid, the fractional decrease in radius of the sphere is $C \\times {10^{ - 6}}.$ Find $C$.<br>(Bulk modulus of rubber $ = {10^8}\\,N/{m^2},$ $g = 10\\,m/{s^2}$)','[{"text": "3.27", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "6.67", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "9.62", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "5.67", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'$K = \\frac{{dP}}{{dV/V}}$<br>$\\therefore $ Fractional decrease in volume,<br>$\\frac{{dV}}{V} = \\frac{{dP}}{K}$ <br>In this case, $dP = \\frac{{mg}}{a}$<br>where, $a$ is area of cross-section of cylinder.<br>$\\therefore \\frac{{dV}}{V} = \\frac{{mg}}{{Ka}}$<br>But $V = \\frac{4}{3}\\pi {r^3} \\Rightarrow \\frac{{dV}}{V} = 3\\frac{{dr}}{r}$<br>$\\therefore \\quad \\frac{{dr}}{r} = \\frac{{dV}}{{3\\,V}} = \\frac{{mg}}{{3Ka}}$<br>$ = \\frac{{10 \\times 10}}{{3 \\times {{10}^8} \\times 0.05}} = 6.67 \\times {10^{ - 6}}$<br>$ \\Rightarrow C = 6.67$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(36805,'physics',27,1381,3315,'A metallic ball and a spring are made of the same material and have the same mass. They are heated so that they melt, the latent heat required','[{"text": "For the two may or may not be the same depending upon the metal", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Is greater for the spring", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Are the same for both", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Is greater for the ball", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Latent heat is independent of its physical structure. So option (3) is correct',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(36806,'physics',26,1134,2713,'In turbulent flow, the velocity of the liquid molecules in contact with the walls of the tube.','[{"text": "Is zero", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Is maximum", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Is equal to critical velocity", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "May have any value", "isCorrect": true}]',3,NULL,NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(36807,'physics',23,870,1996,'A disc of radius $\\frac{1}{2}m$ has $\\omega  = 2\\,rad/s$ and speed ${v_C} = 2m/s.$ The speed of the plank for pure rolling  is','[{"text": "$1\\\\,m/s$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$5\\\\,m/s$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$7\\\\,m/s$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$3\\\\,m/s$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'As disc if rolling the speed of point of contact of disc with plank should be equal to velocity of the plank<br>$V_{\\text {plank }}=v_{\\text {disc }}+\\omega R$<br>$ = 2 + \\left( {2 \\times \\frac{1}{2}} \\right) = 3\\,m/s$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/23_36807_question_1.png'),
(36809,'physics',27,1381,3315,'In a pressure cooker, the cooking is fast, because','[{"text": "The boiling point of water is lowered by pressure", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "The boiling point of water is raised by the increased pressure inside the cooker", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "More pressure is available to cook the food at $100^\\\\circ C$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "More steam is available to cook the food at $100^\\\\circ C$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,NULL,NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(36810,'physics',26,1134,2713,'If the flow is stream lined in a pipe then Reynold number is less than','[{"text": "2000", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "3000", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "1000", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "4000", "isCorrect": false}]',0,NULL,NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(36813,'physics',27,1381,3314,'Which of the following states of matter have two specific heats?','[{"text": "Solid", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Gas", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Liquid", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "All three", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'For solids and liquids $C_{P} \\approx C_{V}$. Whereas for gases $C_{P}-C_{V}=R$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(36814,'physics',26,1135,2714,'The rate of flow of the liquid is the product of','[{"text": "Area of cross section of the liquid and velocity of the liquid.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Length of the tube of the flow and velocity of the liquid.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Volume of the tube of the flow and velocity of the liquid.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Viscous force acting on the liquid layer and velocity of the liquid", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'$$\\dfrac{d V}{d t}=A \\dfrac{d y}{d t}=A v$$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(36815,'physics',23,870,2001,'A small solid ball (mass ${=0.1 {~kg}}$) rolls without slipping along the track shown in the figure. The radius of the circular track is ${R}$. If the ball starts from rest at a height ${8 R}$ above the bottom, the horizontal force acting on it at point ${P}$ is ${5 x}$ newton.  Find  the value of ${x}$   (Given, ${g=10 {~ms}^{-2}}$)','[{"text": "1", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "7", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "2", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "5", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'At point ${P}$,<br>Decrease in GPH ${=}$  Increase in ${(K)_{T}+}$ Increase in ${(K)_{R}}$<br>$mg \\cdot 7R = \\frac{1}{2}m{v^2} + \\frac{1}{2}I{\\omega ^2}$<br>$ = \\frac{1}{2}m{v^2} + \\frac{1}{2}\\left( {\\frac{2}{5}m{r^2}} \\right) \\cdot \\frac{{{v^2}}}{{{r^2}}}$<br>${=\\dfrac{7}{10} m v^{2}(r}$ is radius of ball ${)}$<br>${\\therefore \\quad v^{2}=10 g R}$<br>Centripetal force is<br>$\\frac{{m{v^2}}}{R} = \\frac{{m(10gR)}}{R} = 10mg$<br>$ = 10(0.1)(10) = 10\\,N \\Rightarrow x = 2$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/23_36815_question_1.png'),
(36817,'physics',27,1381,3315,'If water at $0^\\circ C$, kept in a container with an open top, is placed in a large evacuated chamber,','[{"text": "Part of the water will vaporise and the rest will freeze", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "All the water will vaporize", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Ice, water and water vapour will be formed and reach equilibrium at the triple point", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "All the water will freeze", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'At low pressure melting point increases and at $0^\\circ C$ it can\'t exist like water so it freezes.<br>$Q=m c=\\text { Thermal capacity. }$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(36818,'physics',26,1135,2714,'Water is flowing through a tube of non-uniform cross-section. Ratio of the radius at entry and exit end of the pipe is $$3: 2$$. Then the ratio of velocities at entry and exit of liquid is','[{"text": "$$1: 1$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$8: 27$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$9: 4$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$4: 9$$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'If velocities of water at entry and exit points are $$v_{1}$$ and $$v_{2}$$, then from equation of continuity,<br>$$A_{1} v_{1}=A_{2} v_{2} \\Rightarrow \\dfrac{v_{1}}{v_{2}}=\\dfrac{A_{2}}{A_{1}}=\\left(\\dfrac{r_{2}}{r_{1}}\\right)^{2}=\\left(\\dfrac{2}{3}\\right)^{2}=\\dfrac{4}{9} \\text {. }$$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(36821,'physics',27,1381,3314,'Which of the following is the unit of specific heat','[{"text": "$J/kg^\\\\circ C$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$Jkg^\\\\circ {C^{ - 1}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$J/kg^\\\\circ {C^{ - 2}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$kg^\\\\circ C/J$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'$c=\\dfrac{Q}{m \\cdot \\Delta \\theta} \\rightarrow \\dfrac{J}{k g \\times{ }^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C}}$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(36822,'physics',26,1135,2714,'Water is flowing in a pipe of diameter $4\\,\\,cm$ with a velocity $3\\,m/s$. The water then enters into a tube of diameter $2\\,\\,cm$. The velocity of water in the other pipe is','[{"text": "$6\\\\,m/s$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$3\\\\,m/s$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$8\\\\,m/s$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$12\\\\,m/s$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Equation of continuity $$a v=$$ constant<br>${a_1}{v_1} = {a_2}{v_2}$<br>$ \\Rightarrow \\frac{{{v_2}}}{{{v_1}}} = \\frac{{{a_1}}}{{{a_2}}} = {\\left( {\\frac{{{r_1}}}{{{r_2}}}} \\right)^2}$<br>$ \\Rightarrow {v_2} = 3 \\times {(2)^2} = 12\\,m/s.$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(36825,'physics',27,1381,3314,'Two liquids $A$ and $B$ are at $32^\\circ C$ and $24^\\circ C$. When mixed in equal masses the temperature of the mixture is found to be $28^\\circ C$. Their specific heats are in the ratio of','[{"text": "$4: 3$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1: 1$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$3: 2$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2: 3$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Heat lost by $A=$ Heat gained by $B$<br>$\\begin{aligned}& \\Rightarrow m_{A} \\times c_{A} \\times\\left(T_{A}-T\\right)=m_{B} \\times c_{B} \\times\\left(T-T_{B}\\right) \\\\& m_{A}=m_{B} \\\\& T_{A}-T=4^{\\circ}, \\quad T-T_{B}=4^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} \\\\& \\Rightarrow \\dfrac{c_{A}}{c_{B}}=1\\end{aligned}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(36826,'physics',26,1136,2715,'Bernoulli\'s theorem is applicable in the case of','[{"text": "Compressible liquid in stream line flow", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Compressible liquid in turbulent flow", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Incompressible liquid in stream line flow", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Incompressible liquid in turbulent flow.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,NULL,NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(36827,'physics',25,896,2059,'A uniform rod of length $L$ and density $\\rho$ is rotated in a horizontal plane about a vertical axis through one of its ends. The angular speed of rotation is $\\omega$. If increase in the length is found to be $\\dfrac{\\rho \\omega^{2} L^{n}}{n Y}$ where, $Y$ is Young\'s modulus of rod, determine value of $n$.','[{"text": "2", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "5", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "1", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "3", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'<img src=\'/img/data/physics/25_36827_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' /> $Y = \\frac{{FL}}{{Al}}$<br>Here, $F = $ centrifugal force due to rotation.<br>$\\therefore \\quad l = \\frac{{FL}}{{AY}}$<br>From the figure, considering small elongation $dx$ of length segment $x$<br>$dl = \\frac{{dF \\times x}}{{AY}}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,(1)$<br>where, $dF = $ centrifugal force of differential mass $dM$ <br>but $dF = dM{\\omega ^2}x = (\\rho Adx){\\omega ^2}x$<br>$ = \\rho A{\\omega ^2}x\\,dx$<br>Substituting in equation (1),<br>$\\therefore dl = \\frac{{\\left( {\\rho A{\\omega ^2}x\\,dx} \\right) \\times x}}{{AY}}$<br>Integrating over limits,<br>$\\therefore \\quad \\int_0^1 {\\,d} l = \\frac{{\\rho {\\omega ^2}}}{Y}\\int_0^L {{x^2}} \\,dx$<br>$\\therefore l = \\frac{{\\rho {\\omega ^2}}}{Y}\\left[ {\\frac{{{x^3}}}{3}} \\right]_0^L = \\frac{{\\rho {\\omega ^2}}}{{3Y}}\\left[ {{L^3} - 0} \\right]$<br>$\\therefore l = \\frac{{\\rho {\\omega ^2}\\,{L^3}}}{{3Y}}$<br>$ \\Rightarrow n = 3$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(36829,'physics',27,1381,3314,'A copper calorimeter of mass $1.5\\,kg$ has $200\\,g$ of water at $25^\\circ C$. How much water (approx) at $50^\\circ C$ is required to be poured in the calorimeter, so that the equilibrium temperature attained is $40^\\circ C$? (Specific heat of copper $ = 390\\,J/kg^\\circ C$ )','[{"text": "$450\\\\,g$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$370\\\\,g$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$610\\\\,g$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$508\\\\,g$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Heat lost by hot water $=$ Heat gained by (water + copper calorimeter)<br>$m{s_w}(50 - 40) = \\left( {1.5 \\times {s_c} + 0.2{s_w}} \\right)(40 - 25)$<br>${s_w} = 4200\\,J/kgk,$<br>${s_c} = 390\\,J/kg.k$<br>solving $m=508 g$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(36830,'physics',26,1136,2716,'The dynamic lift of an aeroplane is based on','[{"text": "Torricellii\'s theorem", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Bernoulli\'s theorem", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Conservation of angular Momentum", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Principle of continuity", "isCorrect": false}]',1,NULL,NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(36833,'physics',27,1381,3315,'Compared to a burn due to water at $100^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C}$, a burn due to steam at $100^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C}$ is:','[{"text": "More dangerous", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Less dangerous", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Equally dangerous", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "None of these", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Steam at $100^\\circ C$ contains extra $2260\\,J/gm$ energy as compared to water at $100^\\circ C$. So it\'s more dangerous to burn with steam than water.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(36834,'physics',26,1136,2720,'Select the incorrect statement from the following.','[{"text": "Bunsen burner and sprayers work on Bernoulli\'s principle", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Blood flow in arteries is explained by Bernoulli\'s principle", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "A siphon works on account of atmospheric pressure.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "All are correct", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Siphon works without atmospheric pressure. Siphon works because of gravity.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(36844,'physics',27,1381,3315,'Work done in converting $1\\,g$ of ice at $-10^\\circ C$ into steam at $100^\\circ C$ is','[{"text": "$6\\\\,J$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$3045\\\\,J$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$6056\\\\,J$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$721\\\\,J$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Work done in converting  $1\\,g$ of ice at $ - 10^\\circ C$ to steam at $100^\\circ C = $ Heat supplied to raise temperature of $1\\,g$ of<br>ice from $ - 10^\\circ C$ to $0^\\circ C\\left( {m \\times {c_{ice}} \\times \\Delta T} \\right)$. + Heat supplied to melt<br>$1\\,g$ of ice at $0^\\circ Cm{L_{fus{\\text{ }}}} + $ Heat supplied to raise temperature<br>$1\\,g$ of water $0^\\circ C$ to $100^\\circ C\\left( {m \\times {c_{water{\\text{ }}}} \\times \\Delta T} \\right) + $ Heat supplied to convert<br>$1\\,g$ water into steam at $100^{\\circ} C\\left(m \\times L_{\\text {vapour }}\\right)$<br>$ = \\left[ {m \\times {c_{{\\text{ice }}}} \\times \\Delta T} \\right] + \\left[ {m \\times {L_{{\\text{fus }}}}} \\right] + \\left[ {m \\times {c_{{\\text{water }}}} \\times \\Delta T} \\right]$<br>$ + \\left[ {m \\times {L_{{\\text{vapour }}}}} \\right]$<br>$ = [1 \\times 0.5 \\times 10] + [1 \\times 80] + [1 \\times 1 \\times 100] + [1 \\times 540]$<br>$ = 725\\,cal = 725 \\times 4.2 = 3045\\,J$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(36845,'physics',23,864,1979,'In the given figure, a uniform plank of length $6\\,m$ and weight $200\\,N$ rests horizontally on two supports, with $1\\,m$ of the plank hanging over the right support. To what distance ${x}$ (in ${m}$) can a person who weighs $800\\,N$ walk on the overhanging part of the plank before it just begins to tip?','[{"text": "1", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "5", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$0.50$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "7", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'At every instant before the plank begins to tip, it is in equilibrium, and the net torque on it is zero: ${\\Sigma \\tau=0}$. When the person reaches the maximum distance ${x}$ along the overhanging part, the plank is just about to rotate about the right support. At that instant, the plank loses contact with the left support, the normal reaction at this point will become zero. This leaves only three vertical forces acting on the plank: the weight ${W_{\\text {plank }}}$ of the plank, the normal reaction ${N_{{R}}}$ due to the right support, and the weight of the man ${W_{\\operatorname{man}}}$. <img src=\'/img/data/physics/23_36845_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' /> Let us taking torque about point ${B}$<br>${\\Sigma \\tau=W_{\\text {plank }} l_{\\text {plank }}-W_{\\text {man }} l_{\\text {man }}=0}$ or ${l_{\\text {man }}=x=\\dfrac{W_{\\text {plank }} l_{\\text {plank }}}{W_{\\text {man }}}}$<br>Here, ${l_{\\text {plank }}+1=\\dfrac{6}{2}}$ or ${l_{\\text {plank }}=2 {~m}}$<br>Hence from (1), ${x=\\dfrac{(200 {~N})(2 {~m})}{800 {~N}}=0.50 {~m}}$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/23_36845_question_1.png'),
(36846,'physics',26,1136,2717,'A cup filled with water has a hole in the side, through which the liquid is following out. If the cup is dropped from a height, what will happen to the water flowing from the cup?','[{"text": "Flow at the same rate as before", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "It will stop flowing", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Flow upwards relative to the cup", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Flow horizontally with respect to the cup", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Velocity of the water that comes out w.r. to the cup is $$v=\\sqrt{2 a_{r} h}$$<br>$$\\begin{aligned}& a_{r}=0(\\text { For free }- \\text { fall }) \\\\& v=0\\end{aligned}$$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(36848,'physics',27,1381,3315,'$1\\,g$ of a steam at $100^\\circ C$ melts how much ice at $0^\\circ C$ (Latent heat of ice $ = 80\\,cal/gm$ and latent heat of steam $ = 540\\,cal/gm)$','[{"text": "$2\\\\,gm$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1\\\\,gm$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$8\\\\,gm$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$4\\\\,gm$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Suppose $m$ $gm$ ice melted, then heat required for its melting $ = mL = m \\times 80\\,cal$<br>Heat available with steam for being condensed and then brought to $0^\\circ C$<br>$ = 1 \\times 540 + 1 \\times 1 \\times (100 - 0) = 640\\,cal$<br>$ \\Rightarrow {\\text{ Heat lost }} = {\\text{ Heat taken }}$<br>$ \\Rightarrow 640 = m \\times 80$<br>$ \\Rightarrow m = 8gm$<br>Short cut : You can remember that amount of steam $\\left(m^{\\prime}\\right)$ at $100^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C}$ required to melt $m g m$ ice at $0^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C}$ is<br>$m^{\\prime}=\\dfrac{m}{8} .$<br>Here, $m=8 \\times m^{\\prime}=8 \\times 1=8 g m$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(36850,'physics',26,1136,2717,'Water is filled in a cylindrical container to a height of $3\\,m$. The ratio of the cross-sectional area of the orifice and the beaker is 0.1 . The square of the speed of the liquid coming out from the orifice is $\\left( {g = 10\\,m/{s^2}} \\right)$','[{"text": "$40\\\\,\\\\,{m^2}/{s^2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$60\\\\,\\\\,{m^2}/{s^2}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$30\\\\,\\\\,{m^2}/{s^2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$20\\\\,\\\\,{m^2}/{s^2}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Let $$A_{1}=$$ Cross-section of the tank<br>$$\\begin{aligned}& A_{2}=\\text { Cross-sectional hole } \\\\& v_{1}=\\text { Velocity with which level decreases } \\\\& v_{2}=\\text { Velocity of efflux }\\end{aligned}$$<br>From equation of continuity, $$A_{1} v_{1}=A_{2} v_{2}$$<br>By using Bernoullis theorem<br>$$p+\\rho g h+\\dfrac{1}{2} \\rho v_{1}^{2}=p+0+\\dfrac{1}{2} \\rho v_{2}^{2}$$<br>$ \\Rightarrow v_2^2 = {\\rm{ }}\\frac{{2gh}}{{1 - {{\\left( {\\frac{{{A_2}}}{{{A_1}}}} \\right)}^2}}} = \\frac{{2 \\times 10 \\times (3)}}{{1 - {{(0.1)}^2}}} \\approx 60\\,{m^2}/{s^2}.$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(36851,'physics',25,896,2059,'A $15\\,kg$ mass attached to the end of a steel wire of unstretched length $1\\,m$ is whirled in a vertical circle with an angular frequency of $2\\,\\,rev{s^{ - 1}}$ at the bottom of the circle. The cross-section of the wire is $0.05\\,c{m^2}.$ The elongation of the wire in $mm$ when the mass is at the lowest point of its path is<br>(Take, $g = 10\\,m{s^2},{Y_{steel{\\rm{ }}}} = 2 \\times {10^{11}}N{m^{ - 2}}$ and ${\\pi ^2} = 10$)','[{"text": "2.55", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "5.65", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "9.7", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "3.5", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'When the mass is at the lowest point of the vertical circle, the stretching force is $F = mg + mr{\\omega ^2} = mg + mr{\\left( {2\\pi n} \\right)^2}$<br>$ = 15 \\times 10 + 15 \\times (1) \\times {(2\\pi  \\times 2)^2} = 2550\\,N$<br>Elongation $l = \\frac{{FL}}{{AY}}$<br>$l = \\frac{{2550 \\times 1}}{{0.05 \\times {{10}^{ - 4}} \\times 2 \\times {{10}^{11}}}}$<br>$ = 2.55 \\times {10^{ - 3}}\\,m$<br>$\\therefore \\quad l = 2.55\\,mm$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(36852,'physics',27,1382,3317,'In order that the heat flows from one part of a solid to another part, what is required','[{"text": "Density gradient", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Uniform density", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Uniform temperature", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Temperature gradient", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Heat energy always flow from higher temperature to lower temperature. Hence, temperature difference w.r.to length (temperature gradient) is required to flow heat from one part of a solid to another part',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(36853,'physics',23,864,1979,'Two rods each of mass ${m=4 {~kg}}$ and length ${l=1 {~m}}$ are in equilibrium being smoothly hinged at ${P}$. The tension in the connecting string as shown in figure is','[{"text": "$10\\\\,N$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$30\\\\,N$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$25\\\\,N$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$40\\\\,N$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Drawing $FBD$ <img src=\'/img/data/physics/23_36853_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' /> ${N_{{x}} l \\cos \\phi-m g \\dfrac{l}{2} \\sin \\phi-T \\dfrac{l}{3} \\cos \\phi=0}$<br>${N_x} - \\frac{T}{3} = \\frac{{mg}}{2}\\tan \\phi \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\left( 1 \\right)$<br>${N_x} = T{\\rm{ }}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\left( 2 \\right)$<br>Then ${T=N_{x}=\\dfrac{3 m g}{4}}$<br>On substituting ${m=4 {~kg}}$, we get ${T=30 {~N}}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/23_36853_question_1.png'),
(36854,'physics',26,1136,2717,'A cylindrical vessel of $90\\,\\,cm$ height is kept filled upto the brim. It has four holes 1,2,3,4 which are respectively at heights of $20\\,cm,30\\,cm,45$ $cm$ and $50\\,\\,cm$ from the base. The water falling at the maximum horizontal distance from the vessel comes from','[{"text": "Hole number 1", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Hole number 3", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Hole number 4", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Hole number 2", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Horizontal range will be maximum when $h = \\frac{H}{2} = \\frac{{90}}{2} = 45\\,cm$, i.e. hole 3<br><img src=\'/img/data/physics/26_36854_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(36856,'physics',27,1382,3317,'Mud houses are cooler in summer and warmer in winter because:','[{"text": "Mud is a superconductor of heat", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Mud is a good conductor of heat", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Mud is a bad conductor of heat", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "None of the above", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Mud is a bad conductor of heat. So it prevents the flow of heat between surroundings and inside.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(36858,'physics',26,1136,2716,'Air flows horizontally with a speed $v = 108\\,km/hr$. A house has a plane roof of area $A = 20\\,{m^2}$. Find the magnitude of aerodynamic lift on the roof $\\left( {{\\rho _{air{\\text{ }}}} = 1.26Kg/{m^3}} \\right)$','[{"text": "$1.13\\\\,kN$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$11.3\\\\,kN$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$0.13\\\\,kN$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$113\\\\,kN$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Here,<br>$$\\dfrac{1}{2} \\rho v_{1}^{2}+\\rho g h_{1}+p_{1}=\\dfrac{1}{2} \\rho v_{2}^{2}+\\rho g h_{2}+p_{2}$$<br><img src=\'/img/data/physics/26_36858_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' /> <br>${v_2} = \\frac{{108 \\times {{10}^3}}}{{3600}}\\,m/\\sec  = 30\\,m/\\sec $<br>${v_1} = 0;{h_2} = {h_1}$<br>$$p_{1}=$$ Atmosphere pressure $ = {10^5}\\,N/{m^2}$<br>$$\\rho=$$ density of air $ = 1.26\\,kg/{m^3}$<br>$\\therefore {p_1} - {p_2} = \\frac{1}{2}\\rho v_2^2{\\rm{ }} = \\frac{1}{2} \\times 1.26 \\times {30^2}\\,N/{m^2} = 567\\,N/{m^2}$<br>$$\\therefore$$ Aerodynamic lift on the roof<br>$$=A\\left(p_{1}-p_{2}\\right)=20 \\times 567 N=11.34 k N$$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(36860,'physics',27,1382,3317,'If the temperature difference on the two sides of a wall increase from $100^\\circ C$ to $200^\\circ C,$ its thermal conductivity','[{"text": "Is doubled", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Remains unchanged", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Becomes four times", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Is halved", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Thermal conductivity is independent of temperature of the wall, it is a constant for the material, so it will remain unchanged.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(36862,'physics',26,1136,2716,'Air is streaming past a horizontal aeroplane wing such that its speed is $120\\,\\,m/s$ over the upper surface and $90\\,\\,m/s$ at the lower surface. If the density of air is $1.3\\,\\,kg/{m^3}$, then the difference of the pressure on the two sides of the wing is :-','[{"text": "$409.5\\\\,\\\\,N/{m^2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$40950\\\\,\\\\,N/{m^2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$4095\\\\,\\\\,N/{m^2}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$40.95\\\\,\\\\,N/{m^2}$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'$$p_{1}-p_{2}=\\dfrac{1}{2} \\rho\\left(v_{2}^{2}-v_{1}^{2}\\right)$$ <br>$ = \\frac{1}{2} \\times 1.3 \\times \\left( {{{120}^2} - {{90}^2}} \\right) = 4095\\,N/{m^2}$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(36863,'physics',25,898,2065,'A catapult has rubber cord of length $10\\,cm$ and cross-sectional area $20\\,m{m^2}.$ It is stretched by $4\\,cm$ to project a pebble of $10\\,g.$ Find velocity (in $m{\\rm{/}}s$) of the pebble just after its launch  is <br>(${Y_{rubber{\\rm{ }}}} = {10^8}\\,N{\\rm{/}}{m^2}$ and $\\sqrt 2  = 1.41$)','[{"text": "$75.2\\\\,m/s$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$56.4\\\\,m/s$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$33.6\\\\,m/s$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$22.6\\\\,m/s$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Elastic potential energy $ = $ kinetic energy<br>$\\therefore \\quad \\frac{1}{2}\\frac{{YA{l^2}}}{{\\,L}} = \\frac{1}{2}m{v^2}$<br>$\\therefore \\quad v = \\sqrt {\\frac{{YA{l^2}}}{{\\,mL}}} $<br>$\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, = \\sqrt {\\frac{{{{10}^8} \\times 20 \\times {{10}^{ - 6}} \\times {{\\left( {4 \\times {{10}^{ - 2}}} \\right)}^2}}}{{10 \\times {{10}^{ - 3}} \\times 10 \\times {{10}^{ - 2}}}}} $<br>$\\therefore \\quad v = 40\\sqrt 2  = 56.4\\,m/s$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(36864,'physics',27,1382,3319,'The ratio of the coefficient of thermal conductivity of two different materials is $5: 3$. If the thermal resistance of the two rods of these materials of same length is same, then the ratio of the area of these rods will be','[{"text": "$5: 3$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$3: 5$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$9: 25$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$25: 9$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'The thermal resistance of a rod of length $l$ and area of cross section $A$ is<br>$R=\\dfrac{l}{k A}$<br>$K$ - Thermal conductivity of the rod.<br>$\\Rightarrow \\dfrac{A_{1}}{A_{2}}=\\dfrac{k_{2}}{k_{1}}=\\dfrac{3}{5}$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(36865,'physics',23,864,1982,'Figure shows a uniform block of mass ${m=\\sqrt{\\dfrac{3}{13}} {~kg}}$ having square base and equilateral triangular cross section placed at the edge of the floor of a truck which is under constant acceleration ${a=\\dfrac{g \\sqrt{3}}{4} {~m} / {s}^{2}}$ to the right. A horizontal force on the apex of the triangle ${F}$ is just enough to just tilt the block. Find the force  on the block at point ${A}$ where the block is in contact with the step edge when ${F}$ is applied. (Take ${g=10 {~m} / {s}^{2}}$)','[{"text": "$5\\\\,N$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$2\\\\,N$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$8\\\\,N$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$3\\\\,N$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Torque about ${A}$<br>$\\begin{aligned}& F \\dfrac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} d=m g \\dfrac{d}{2}+m a \\dfrac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} d \\dfrac{1}{3} \\\\& \\therefore \\quad F=\\dfrac{m g}{\\sqrt{3}}+\\dfrac{m a}{3} \\\\& \\therefore \\quad F_{H}=\\dfrac{m g}{\\sqrt{3}}-\\dfrac{2 m a}{3} \\\\& \\quad F_{v}=m g \\\\& \\text { So, } \\quad F_{A}=5 {~N}\\end{aligned}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/23_36865_question_1.png'),
(36866,'physics',26,1136,2717,'A large open tank has two holes in the wall. One is a square hole of side $$L$$ at a depth $$y$$ from the top and the other is a circular hole of radius $r$ at a depth $$4 y$$ from the top. When the tank is completely filled with water the quantities of water flowing out per second from both the holes are the same. Then $r$ is equal to','[{"text": "$$\\\\dfrac{L}{\\\\sqrt{2 \\\\pi}}$$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$$2 \\\\pi L$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$\\\\dfrac{L}{2 \\\\pi}$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$L$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Velocity of efflux when the hole is at depth<br>$$h, v=\\sqrt{2 g h}$$<br>Rate of flow of water from square hole<br>$$Q_{1}=a_{1} v_{1}=L^{2} \\sqrt{2 g y}$$<br>Rate flow of water from circular hole<br>$$Q_{2}=a_{2} v_{2}=\\pi R^{2} \\sqrt{2 g(4 y)}$$<br>Given that $$Q_{1}=Q_{2}$$<br>$$\\Rightarrow L^{2} \\sqrt{2 g y}=\\pi R^{2} \\sqrt{2 g(4 y)} \\Rightarrow R=\\dfrac{L}{\\sqrt{2 \\pi}}$$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(36868,'physics',27,1382,3317,'Two rods $A$ and $B$ are of equal lengths. Their ends are kept between the same temperature and their area of cross section are ${A_1}$ and ${A_2}$ and thermal conductivities $K_{1}$ and ${K_2}$. The rate of heat transmission in the two rods will be equal, if','[{"text": "${K_1} = {K_2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${K_1}\\\\,{A_2} = {K_2}\\\\,{A_1}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${K_1}\\\\,A_1^2 = {K_2}\\\\,A_2^2$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${K_1}\\\\,{A_1} = {K_2}\\\\,{A_2}$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'$i_{1}=\\dfrac{K_{1} A_{1}\\left(\\theta_{1}-\\theta_{2}\\right)}{l}$ and<br>$i_{2}=\\dfrac{K_{2} A_{2}\\left(\\theta_{1}-\\theta_{2}\\right)}{l}$<br>given $i_{1}=i_{2} \\Rightarrow K_{1} A_{1}=K_{2} A_{2}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(36870,'physics',26,1137,2722,'Which of the following substances has the greatest viscosity?','[{"text": "Mercury", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Water", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Kerosene", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Glycerine", "isCorrect": true}]',3,NULL,NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(36872,'physics',27,1382,3317,'A cylindrical rod having temperature $T_{1}$ and $T_{2}$ at its ends. The rate of flow of heat is ${Q_1}\\,cal/\\sec $. If all the linear dimensions are doubled keeping temperature constant then rate of flow of heat $Q_{2}$ will be:','[{"text": "$2 Q_{1}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$4 Q_{1}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{Q_{1}}{2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{Q_{1}}{4}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Rate of heat flow<br>$\\begin{aligned}& \\left(\\dfrac{Q}{t}\\right)=\\dfrac{k \\pi r^{2}\\left(\\theta_{1}-\\theta_{2}\\right)}{L} \\alpha \\dfrac{r^{2}}{L} \\\\& \\therefore \\dfrac{Q_{1}}{Q_{2}}=\\left(\\dfrac{r_{1}}{r_{2}}\\right)^{2}\\left(\\dfrac{L_{2}}{L_{1}}\\right)=\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right)^{2} \\times\\left(\\dfrac{2}{1}\\right)=\\dfrac{1}{2} \\\\& \\Rightarrow Q_{2}=2 Q_{1}\\end{aligned}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(36874,'physics',26,1137,2724,'As the depth of the river increases, the velocity of flow','[{"text": "Increases", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Decreases", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Remains unchanged", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "May increase or decrease", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'When water flows in a river the velocity is maximum at the surface and it decreases as we go down.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(36876,'physics',27,1382,3318,'A metal rod of length $2\\,m$ has crosssectional area $3\\,A$ and $2\\,A$ as shown in the figure. $100^{\\circ}$ and $120^{\\circ}$ temperature are maintained at the ends respectively. The temperature at middle point $C$ is','[{"text": "$120^\\\\circ C$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$108^\\\\circ C$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$180^\\\\circ C$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$360^\\\\circ C$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Let $\\theta$ is the temperature of the mid point<br>$\\begin{aligned}& \\dfrac{\\left(\\theta_{1}-\\theta\\right) A_{1}}{l_{1}}=\\dfrac{\\left(\\theta-\\theta_{2}\\right) A_{2}}{l_{2}} \\\\& \\dfrac{(100-\\theta) 3 A}{1}=\\dfrac{\\left(\\theta-120^{\\circ}\\right) 2 A}{1}\\end{aligned}$<br>$300 - 3\\theta  = 2\\theta  - 240^\\circ $<br>$300 + 240 = 2\\theta  + 3\\theta  \\Rightarrow 540 = 5\\theta  \\Rightarrow \\theta  = 108^\\circ C$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/27_36876_question_1.png'),
(36878,'physics',26,1137,2722,'A good lubricant must have','[{"text": "High viscosity", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Low viscosity", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "High density", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Low surface tension", "isCorrect": false}]',0,NULL,NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(36880,'physics',27,1382,3318,'Two bars of thermal conductivities $K$ and $3 K$ and length $1\\,cm$ and $2\\,cm$ respectively have equal cross-sectional area, they are joined lengths wise as shown in figure. If the temperature at the ends of this composite bar are $0^\\circ C$ and $100^\\circ C$ respectively (see figure), then the temperature of the interface is','[{"text": "$\\\\frac{{100^\\\\circ }}{3}C$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$50^\\\\circ C$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{{200^\\\\circ }}{3}C$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$60^\\\\circ C$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Temperature of interface<br>$\\theta  = \\frac{{\\frac{{{\\theta _1}}}{{{R_1}}} + \\frac{{{\\theta _2}}}{{{R_2}}}}}{{\\frac{1}{{{R_1}}} + \\frac{1}{{{R_2}}}}}$<br>${\\theta _1} = 0^\\circ C,{\\theta _2} = 100^\\circ C$<br>${R_1} = \\frac{1}{{kA}},{R_2} = \\frac{2}{{3kA}}$<br>$ \\Rightarrow \\theta  = 60^\\circ C$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/27_36880_question_1.png'),
(36882,'physics',26,1137,2722,'Viscosity is the property by virtue of which a liquid','[{"text": "Occupies minimum surface area", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Offers resistance for the relative motion between its layers.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Becomes spherical in shape", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Tends to gain its deformed position.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,NULL,NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(36883,'physics',25,896,2059,'The bar shown in the figure is made of a single piece of material. It is fixed at one end. It consists of two segments of equal length ${L / 2}$ each but different cross-sectional area ${A}$ and ${2 A}$. Find the ratio of total elongation in the bar to the elongation produced in thicker segment under the action of an axial force ${F}$. Consider the shape of joint to remain circular. (Given: ${Y}$ is Young\'s modulus).','[{"text": "2", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "5", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "7", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "3", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'${\\Delta L_{1}=\\dfrac{F(L / 2)}{A Y}=\\dfrac{F L}{2 A Y}}$<br>${\\Delta L_{2}=\\dfrac{F(L / 2)}{2 A Y}=\\dfrac{F L}{4 A Y}}$<br>Required ratio ${=\\dfrac{\\Delta L_{1}+\\Delta L_{2}}{\\Delta L_{2}}=\\dfrac{\\Delta L_{1}}{\\Delta L_{2}}+1=2+1=3}$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/25_36883_question_1.png'),
(36900,'physics',27,1382,3321,'Which of the follwoing is the example of ideal black body?','[{"text": "Kajal", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Black board", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "A pin hole in a box", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "None of these", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'When light incident on pin hole enters into the box and suffers successive reflection at the inner wall. At each reflection some energy is absorbed. Hence the ray once it enters the box can never come out and pin hole acts like a perfect black body.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(36884,'physics',27,1382,3318,'One end of a thermally insulated rod is kept at a temperature $T_{1}$ and the other at $T_{2}$. The rod is composed of two sections of length $l_{1}$ and $l_{2}$ and thermal conductivities $K_{1}$ and $K_{2}$ respectively. The temperature at the interface of the two sections is','[{"text": "$\\\\dfrac{\\\\left(K_{1} l_{1} T_{1}+K_{2} l_{2} T_{2}\\\\right)}{\\\\left(K_{1} l_{1}+K_{2} l_{2}\\\\right)}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{\\\\left(K_{2} l_{2} T_{1}+K_{1} l_{1} T_{2}\\\\right)}{\\\\left(K_{1} l_{1}+K_{2} l_{2}\\\\right)}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{\\\\left(K_{2} l_{1} T_{1}+K_{1} l_{2} T_{2}\\\\right)}{\\\\left(K_{2} l_{1}+K_{1} l_{2}\\\\right)}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{\\\\left(K_{1} l_{2} T_{1}+K_{2} l_{1} T_{2}\\\\right)}{\\\\left(K_{1} l_{2}+K_{2} l_{1}\\\\right)}$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Let $T$ be the temperature of the interface. As the two sections are in series, the rate of flow of heat in them will be equal.<br><img src=\'/img/data/physics/27_36884_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' /> <br>where $A$ is the area of cross-section.<br>$\\begin{aligned}& K_{1} A\\left(T_{1}-T\\right) l_{2}=K_{2} A\\left(T-T_{2}\\right) l_{1} \\\\& K_{1} T_{1} l_{2}-K_{1} T l_{2}=K_{2} T l_{1}-K_{2} T_{2} l_{1} \\\\& \\left(K_{2} l_{1}+K_{1} l_{2}\\right) T=K_{1} T_{1} l_{2}+K_{2} T_{2} l_{1} \\\\& T=\\dfrac{K_{1} T_{1} l_{2}+K_{2} T_{2} l_{1}}{K_{2} l_{1}+K_{1} l_{2}}\\end{aligned}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/27_36884_question_1.png'),
(36885,'physics',23,871,2004,'An ice skater starts a spin with her arms stretched out to the sides. She balances on the tip of one skate to turn without friction. She then pulls her arms in so that her moment of inertia decreases by a factor of 2 . In the process of her doing so, her kinetic energy becomes ${n}$ times the initial kinetic energy.  Find  the value of ${n}$ is','[{"text": "2", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "4", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "8", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "10", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Her angular momentum stays constant as ${I}$ is cut in half and ${\\omega}$ doubles. Then ${\\dfrac{1}{2} I \\omega^{2}}$ doubles. So $n=2$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(36886,'physics',26,1137,2722,'The main cause of viscosity is','[{"text": "Force of repulsion between molecules", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Cohesive forces", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Adhesive forces", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both cohesive and adhesive forces.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Cohesive forces are the intermolecular forces like hydrogen bonding, Vander waals forces etc. Cohesive forces present between liquid layers resist the motion thus are responsible for viscosity.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(36888,'physics',27,1382,3319,'Two identical slabs are connected side by side having thickness $l$ each and area of cross section $A$. Thermal conductivities are $K$ and $2K$. The equivalent thermal resistance is','[{"text": "$\\\\frac{{2l}}{{KA}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{l}{{2KA}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{{3l}}{{2KA}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{l}{{KA}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Thermal resistance<br>$R = \\frac{l}{{KA}} + \\frac{l}{{2KA}} = \\frac{{3l}}{{2KA}}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(36890,'physics',26,1137,2722,'From kinetic theory point of view viscosity represents transport of','[{"text": "Momentum", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Mass", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Energy", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "None of these", "isCorrect": false}]',0,NULL,NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(36892,'physics',27,1382,3320,'In an open room, a hot body is placed then heat transfer takes place by','[{"text": "Convection", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Conduction", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Radiation", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both (1) & (3)", "isCorrect": true}]',3,NULL,NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(36894,'physics',26,1137,2722,'Machine parts are jammed in winter due to','[{"text": "Increase in viscosity of lubricant", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Decrease in viscosity of lubricant", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Increase in surface tension of lubricant", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Decrease in surface tension of lubricant", "isCorrect": false}]',0,NULL,NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(36895,'physics',25,896,2060,'Two parallel and opposite forces, each of magnitude $4000\\,N$, are applied tangentially to the upper and lower faces of a cubical metal block $25\\,cm$ on a side. Find the displacement of the upper surface relative to the lower surface . The shear modulus for the metal is $80\\,Gpa$.','[{"text": "$2.0 \\\\times {10^{ - 5}}cm$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$5.0 \\\\times {10^{ - 5}}cm$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$9.0 \\\\times {10^{ - 5}}cm$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$8.0 \\\\times {10^{ - 5}}cm$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'We use the approximate from ${S=F /(A \\phi)}$.<br>Putting ${S=8 \\times 10^{10} {~N} / {m}^{2}, F=4000 {~N}}$ and ${A=(0.25 {~m})^{2}}$<br>${=6.25 \\times 10^{-2} {~m}^{2}}$ in the above equation and solving for ${\\phi}$, we get<br>$\\phi  = \\frac{{(4000\\,N)}}{{\\left( {6.25 \\times {{10}^{ - 2}}\\,{m^2}} \\right)\\left( {8 \\times {{10}^{10}}\\,N/{m^2}} \\right)}}$<br>$ = 8.0 \\times {10^{ - 7}}rad$<br>The displacement of the upper surface is given by ${d=L \\phi}$, where ${L}$ is an edge of the cube.<br>$\\therefore \\quad d=\\left(8.0 \\times 10^{-7}\\right)(25 {~cm})=2.0 \\times 10^{-5} {~cm}$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(36896,'physics',27,1382,3320,'The freezer in a refrigerator is located at the top section so that','[{"text": "The motor is not heated", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "The entire parts of the refrigerator is cooled quickly due to convection", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "The heat gained from the environment is low", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "The heat gained from the environment is high", "isCorrect": false}]',1,NULL,NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(36897,'physics',23,870,1996,'A solid sphere of mass $10\\,kg$ is placed on a rough surface having coefficient of friction $\\mu  = 0.1.$ A constant force $F=7\\,N$ is applied along a line passing through the centre of the sphere as shown in the figure. What is the value of frictional force  on the sphere?','[{"text": "$5\\\\,N$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$9\\\\,N$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2\\\\,N$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$1\\\\,N$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Let ${f}$ be frictional force towards left for pure rolling.<br>$a=\\alpha R$<br>where, ${a=\\dfrac{F-f}{m}=\\dfrac{7-f}{10}}$<br>${\\alpha=\\dfrac{\\tau}{I}=\\dfrac{f R}{I}=\\dfrac{f R}{\\dfrac{2}{5} m R^{2}}=\\dfrac{5 f}{2 m R}}$<br>Hence, ${\\dfrac{7-f}{10}=\\dfrac{5 f}{20} \\Rightarrow f=2 {~N}}$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/23_36897_question_1.png'),
(36898,'physics',26,1137,2722,'If the shearing stress between the horizontal layers of water in a river is $1.5\\,milli$ newton $/{m^2}$ and $$\\eta_{\\text {water }}=1 \\times 10^{-3}$$ $pa.s$, The velocity gradient is ${s^{ - 1}}$','[{"text": "1.5", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "3", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "0.7", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "1", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'The velocity gradient is<br>$$\\dfrac{d v}{d x}=\\dfrac{F}{A \\eta}=1.5 s^{-1}$$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(36902,'physics',26,1137,2722,'A square plate of $0.1\\,\\,m$ side moves parallel to a second plate with a velocity of $0.1\\,m/s$, both plates being immersed in water. If the viscous force is $0.002\\,\\,N$ and the coefficient of viscocity is 0.01 poise, distance between the plates in $$m$$ is','[{"text": "0.0005", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "0.05", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "0.005", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "0.1", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'$A = {(0.1)^2} = 0.01\\,{m^2}$,<br>$$\\eta=0.01$$ Poise $$=0.001$$ decapoise (M.K.S.unit)<br>$dv = 0.1\\,m/s$ and $F = 0.002\\,N$<br>$F = \\eta A\\frac{{dv}}{{dx}}$<br>$\\therefore dx = \\frac{{\\eta Adv}}{F} = \\frac{{0.001 \\times 0.01 \\times 0.1}}{{0.002}} = 0.0005\\,m$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(36904,'physics',27,1382,3321,'For a perfectly black body, its absorptive power is','[{"text": "0.5", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "1", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Infinity", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "0", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'A perfectly black body is a good absorbers of radiations falls on it. So it\'s absorptive power is 1 .',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(36905,'physics',26,1137,2723,'The viscous force on a sphere moving in a liquid depends on','[{"text": "Coefficient of viscosity", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Volume of the sphere", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Velocity of the sphere", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "All of the above", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Viscosity force on a moving sphere in a liquid is $$f=6 \\pi \\eta r v$$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(36906,'physics',23,871,2004,'A man sits on a freely rotating stool holding two dumbbells, each of mass $2.0\\,kg$. When his arms are extended horizontally [Fig. (a)], the dumbbells are $1.0\\,m$ from the axis of rotation and the man rotates with an angular speed of $0.50\\,rad/s$. The moment of inertia of the man plus stool is ${5.0 {~kg} \\cdot {m}^{2}}$ and is assumed to be constant. The man pulls the dumbbells inward horizontally to a position $0.50\\,m$ from the rotation axis [Fig. (b)]. Find the new angular speed of the man','[{"text": "$0.90\\\\,rad/s$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$0.67\\\\,rad/s$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$0.45\\\\,rad/s$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$0.15\\\\,rad/s$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'A man is seated on a stool that can rotate freely about a vertical axis with his arms stretched horizontally with equal weights in each hand. As no net external torque acts on the system that consists of the \'student, stool and dumbbells\'. It means, the angular momentum of that system about the rotation axis must remain constant. The total angular momentum of the system of the student, the stool is conserved then,<br>$\\vec{L}_{i}=\\vec{L}_{f} \\text { or } I_{i} \\omega_{i}=I_{f} \\omega_{f}$<br>Moment of inertia of the system,<br>$I_{\\text {total }}=I_{\\text {student }}+I_{\\text {weights }}=5.0+2\\left({mr}^{2}\\right)$<br>Initial moment of inertia, ${r_{\\text {initial }}=1.0 {~m}}$<br>Thus, ${I_{i}=5.0+2 \\times(2.0)(1.0)^{2}=9.0 {~kg} \\cdot {m}^{2}}$<br>Final moment of inertia, ${r_{\\text {final }}=0.50 {~m}}$<br>Thus, ${I_{f}=5.0+2(2.0)(0.50)^{2}=5.0 {~kg} \\cdot {m}^{2}}$<br>We now using conservation of angular momentum ${I_{f} \\omega_{f}=I_{i} \\omega_{i}}$<br>or ${\\omega _f} = \\left( {\\frac{{{I_i}}}{{{I_f}}}} \\right){\\omega _i} = \\left( {\\frac{{9.0}}{{5.0}}} \\right)(0.50) = 0.90\\,rad/s$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/23_36906_question_1.png'),
(36908,'physics',27,1382,3321,'Which of the following law states that "good absorbers of heat are good emitters"','[{"text": "Kirchhoff\'s law", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Stefan\'s law", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Wien\'s law", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Planck\'s law", "isCorrect": false}]',0,NULL,NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(36909,'physics',26,1137,2723,'A rain drop of radius $0.3\\,\\,mm$ falling vertically downwards in air has a terminal velocity of 1 $m/s$. The viscosity of air is $$18 \\times 10^{-5}$$ poise. The viscous force on the drop is','[{"text": "$$101.73 \\\\times 10^{-4}$$ dyne", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$$101.73 \\\\times 10^{-5}$$ dyne", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$16.95 \\\\times 10^{-5}$$ dyne", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$16.95 \\\\times 10^{-4}$$ dyne", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Viscous force<br>$ = 6\\pi \\eta rv = 6\\pi  \\times 18 \\times {10^{ - 5}} \\times 0.03 \\times 100$<br>$ = 101.73 \\times {10^{ - 4}}dyn\\quad \\left( {dyn = 1g\\,cm/{s^2}} \\right)$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(36912,'physics',27,1382,3323,'The rate of dissipation of heat by a black body at temperature $T$ is $Q$. What will be the rate of dissipation of heat by another body at temperature $2T$ and emissivity 0.25 ?','[{"text": "$4Q$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$16Q$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$4.5Q$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$8Q$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'By Stefan\'s law<br>$Q=\\sigma A T^{4}$<br>The rate of dissipation of heat by a body of emissivity e is given by<br>$Q^{\\prime}=e \\sigma A T^{\\prime 4}$<br>Here, $T^{\\prime}=2 T, e=0.25$<br>$\\therefore Q^{\\prime}=0.25 \\times \\sigma(2 T)^{4}=4 \\sigma T^{4}=4 Q$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(36913,'physics',26,1137,2723,'A sphere of mass $$M$$ and radius $$R$$ is falling in a viscous fluid. The terminal velocity attained by the object will be proportional to:','[{"text": "$$R$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$R^{2}$$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$$1 / R^{2}$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$1 / R$$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'The terminal velocity of a sphere of radius $$R$$ moving in a liquid of coefficient of viscosity<br>$$\\eta \\text { is } \\quad \\Rightarrow v=\\dfrac{2}{9} \\dfrac{R^{2}(\\rho-\\sigma) g}{\\eta}$$<br>Where $$\\rho$$ - density of the sphere<br>$$\\sigma$$-density of the liquid<br>$$\\Rightarrow v \\propto R^{2}$$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(36914,'physics',23,871,2006,'A small block of mass $4\\,kg$ is attached to a cord passing through a hole in a horizontal frictionless surface. The block is originally revolving in a circle of radius $0.5\\,m$ about the hole, with a tangential velocity of ${4 {~m} / {s}}$. The cord is then pulled slowly from below, shortening the radius of the circle in which the block revolves. The breaking strength of the cord is $600\\,N$. What will be the radius  of the circle when the cord breaks?','[{"text": "$1.0\\\\,m$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$3.0\\\\,m$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$7.0\\\\,m$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$10.0\\\\,m$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'The tension of the rope is the only net force on the block and it does not exert any torque about the axis of rotation. Hence, the angular momentum of the block about the axis should remain conserved. <img src=\'/img/data/physics/23_36914_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' /> $ \\Rightarrow \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,mvr = $constant<br>Let ${r_1} = 0.5\\,m$ and ${v_1} = 4\\,m/s$<br>Let ${r_{2}, v_{2}, T_{2}}$ be the radius, velocity and tension, respectively, when the string breaks.<br>$ \\Rightarrow \\,\\,{T_2} = 600\\,N$<br>$m{v_1}{r_1} = m{v_2}{r_2}{\\text{ and }}{T_2} = mv_2^2/r$<br>$ \\Rightarrow {\\text{ }}m{v_1}{r_1} = m\\sqrt {\\frac{{{r_2}{T_2}}}{m}{r_2}} $<br>${r_2} = {\\left( {\\frac{{mv_1^2r_1^2}}{{{T_2}}}} \\right)^{\\frac{1}{3}}} = {\\left( {\\frac{{4 \\times 16 \\times 0.25}}{{600}}} \\right)^{\\frac{1}{3}}}$<br>$ = {\\left( {\\frac{{16}}{{600}}} \\right)^{\\frac{1}{3}}} = 3.0\\,m$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/23_36914_question_1.png'),
(36915,'physics',25,896,2060,'A die is designed to punch holes with a radius of $R=$ $1.00 \\times 10^{-2} {~m}$ in a metal sheet of thickness $t=3.0 \\times 10^{-3} {~m}$ thick, as the drawing illustrates. To punch through the sheet, the die must exert a shearing stress of $\\sigma=3.5 \\times 10^{8} {~Pa}$. What  force $\\vec{F}$   must be applied to the die?','[{"text": "$3.5 \\\\times {10^4}\\\\,N$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$6.6 \\\\times {10^4}\\\\,N$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$7.4 \\\\times {10^4}\\\\,N$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$10.2 \\\\times {10^4}\\\\,N$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'From the drawing we have $t=3.0 \\times 10^{-3} {~m}$ and <br>Area of surface of the hole $A=2 \\pi r t$<br>$\\Rightarrow A=2 \\pi\\left(1.00 \\times 10^{-2} {~m}\\right)\\left(3.0 \\times 10^{-3} {~m}\\right)$<br>We now have Stress $\\sigma=F / A$. Therefore, <br>$F=($ Stress $) A$<br>$ = \\left( {3.5 \\times {{10}^8}\\,Pa} \\right)\\left[ {\\begin{array}{*{20}{l}}\n{2\\pi \\left( {1.00 \\times {{10}^{ - 2}}\\,m} \\right)}\\\\\n{\\left( {3.0 \\times {{10}^{ - 3}}} \\right)m}\n\\end{array}} \\right]$<br>$ = 6.6 \\times {10^4}\\,N$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/25_36915_question_1.png'),
(36916,'physics',27,1382,3322,'According to Wien\'s law','[{"text": "$\\\\dfrac{\\\\lambda_{m}}{T}=$ constant", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\lambda_{m} T=$ constant", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$T+\\\\lambda_{m}=$ constant", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{T}{\\\\lambda_{m}}=$ constant", "isCorrect": false}]',1,NULL,NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(36917,'physics',26,1137,2723,'A small steel ball of mass $$m$$ and radius $$r$$ is falling under gravity through a viscous liquid of coefficient of viscocity $$\\eta$$. If $$g$$ is the value of acceleration due to gravity, then the terminal velocity of the ball is proportional to (ignore buoyancy)','[{"text": "$$\\\\dfrac{m g}{r \\\\eta}$$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$$\\\\dfrac{m g \\\\eta}{r}$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$\\\\dfrac{m g r}{\\\\eta}$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$m g \\\\eta r$$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'As the steel ball is small therefore upthrust can be neglected.<br>Now, $$6 \\pi \\eta r v_{0}=m g$$ or $$v_{0} \\propto \\dfrac{m g}{\\eta r}$$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(36919,'physics',25,896,2063,'A light rod of length 2 $m$ is suspended from the ceiling horizontally by means of two vertical wires of equal length tied to its ends. One of the wires is made of steel and is of cross-section $10^{-3} {~m}^{2}$ and the other is of brass of cross-section $2 \\times 10^{-3} {~m}^{2}$. Find out the distance  along the rod from steel wire, at which a weight may be hung to produce equal strains in both wires. Young\'s modulus for brass  $ = {10^{11}}N{m^{ - 2}}$  and Young\'s modulus for steel $=2 \\times 10^{11} {~N} {~m}^{-2}$.','[{"text": "$1\\\\,m$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$3\\\\,m$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$7\\\\,m$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$9\\\\,m$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Figure shows a light rod $A B$ suspended by means of the two wires $S_{1} A$ (steel) and $S_{2} B$ (brass).  <img src=\'/img/data/physics/25_36919_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />  Let $a_{1}$, and $a_{2}$ be the areas of cross - section of the two wires,Suppose that a weight $W$ is suspended from a point $C$ at a distance $x$ from the end $A$ of the rod.As a result,let ${T_1}$ and  ${T_2}$ be the tensions in the wires $S_{1} A$ and $S_{2} B$ respectively.<br> Now, $Y=\\dfrac{\\text { stress }}{\\text { strain }}$ or strain $=\\dfrac{\\text { stress }}{Y}$<br>Therefore, strain in the steel wire  $ = \\frac{{{T_1}\\,{a_1}}}{{{Y_1}}} = \\frac{{{T_1}}}{{{a_1}{Y_1}}}$<br>and strain in the brass wire  $ = \\frac{{{T_2}/{a_2}}}{{{Y_2}}} = \\frac{{{T_2}}}{{{a_2}{Y_2}}}$<br>Since strains in the two wires are equal, $\\dfrac{T_{1}}{a_{1} Y_{1}}=\\dfrac{T_{2}}{a_{2} Y_{2}}$<br>Or $\\dfrac{T_{1}}{10^{-3} \\times 2 \\times 10^{11}}=\\dfrac{T_{2}}{2 \\times 10^{-13} \\times 10^{11}}$<br>Or $\\quad T_{1}=T_{2}$<br>Again for the equilibrium of the system,<br>$T_{1} \\times A C=T_{2} \\times B C$<br>Or $T_{1} \\times x=T_{2} \\times(2-x)$<br>Or $x=2-x$  $\\left(\\because T_{1}=T_{2}\\right)$<br>Or $x=1 {~m}$ (from steel wire)',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/25_36919_question_1.png'),
(36920,'physics',27,1382,3322,'The maximum wavelength of radiation emitted at $2000\\,K$  is $4\\mu m$. What will be the maximum wavelength of radiation emitted at $2400\\,K$','[{"text": "$0.66\\\\mu m$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$3.33\\\\mu m$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$1m$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1 \\\\mu m$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'$\\therefore \\dfrac{\\lambda_{m_{2}}}{\\lambda_{m_{1}}}=\\dfrac{T_{1}}{T_{2}} \\Rightarrow \\lambda_{m_{2}}=\\dfrac{2000}{2400} \\times 4=3.33 \\mu m$.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(36921,'physics',26,1137,2723,'Two rain drops reach the earth with different terminal velocities having ratio $$9: 4$$. Then the ratio of their volume is','[{"text": "$$3: 2$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$4: 9$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$9: 4$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$27: 8$$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'<br>$$\\begin{gathered}v_{t}=\\dfrac{2}{9} \\dfrac{g r^{2}[\\rho-\\sigma]}{\\eta} \\Rightarrow v_{t} \\propto r^{2} \\propto\\left(\\mathrm{V}^{1 / 3}\\right)^{2} \\propto \\mathrm{V}^{2 / 3} \\\\\\dfrac{v_{t_{1}}}{v_{t_{2}}}=\\left(\\dfrac{v_{1}}{v_{2}}\\right)^{2 / 3} \\\\\\dfrac{9}{4}=\\left(\\dfrac{v_{1}}{v_{2}}\\right)^{2 / 3} \\Rightarrow \\dfrac{v_{1}}{v_{2}}=\\dfrac{27}{8}\\end{gathered}$$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(36922,'physics',23,874,2009,'A disc of radius ${r=0.1 {~m}}$ is rolled from a point ${A}$ on a track as shown in the figure. The part ${A B}$ of the track is a semi-circle of radius ${R}$ in a vertical plane. The disc rolls without sliding and leaves contact with the track at its highest point ${B}$. Flying through the air it strikes the ground at point ${C}$. The velocity of the centre of mass of the disc makes an angle of ${30^{\\circ}}$ below the horizontal at the time of striking the ground. At the same instant, velocity of the topmost point ${P}$ of the disc is found to be ${6 {~m} / {s}}$. The value of ${R}$ (in $m$) (Take ${g=10 {~m} / {s}^{2}}$) is','[{"text": "$1\\\\,m$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$5\\\\,m$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$7\\\\,m$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$3\\\\,m$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Since the disc was rolling, the velocity of its top point at the instant of leaving the track was zero. It means ${v_{H}=\\omega r}$. When the disc is in air ${v_{H}}$ and ${\\omega}$ both do not change. Hence the horizontal component of the velocity of the top point ${P}$ of the disc at every instant is zero and the vertical component of the velocity of the point ${P}$ is equal to the vertical component of velocity of the ${C M}$ of disc.<br>${\\Rightarrow \\sqrt{2 g h}=\\sqrt{2 \\cdot g \\cdot 2(R-0.1)}=6}$<br>${\\Rightarrow \\quad 4 \\times 10(R-0.1)=36}$<br>$R=1 {~m}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/23_36922_question_1.png'),
(36952,'physics',27,1382,3323,'A black body radiates energy at the rate of $E W / m^{2}$ at a high temperature $T$. When the temperature is reduced to $\\dfrac{T}{2}$, the radiant energy will be','[{"text": "$E / 4$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$E / 16$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$16\\\\,E$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$4\\\\,E$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'$E \\propto T^{4} \\Rightarrow \\dfrac{E_{1}}{E_{2}}=\\dfrac{T_{1}^{4}}{T_{2}^{4}}=2^{4} \\Rightarrow E_{2}=\\dfrac{E}{16}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37577,'physics',27,1382,3317,'The unit of thermal conductivity is :','[{"text": "$J\\\\,m\\\\,{K^{ - 1}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$J\\\\,{m^{ - 1}}\\\\,{K^{ - 1}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$W\\\\,m\\\\,{K^{ - 1}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$W\\\\,{m^{ - 1}}\\\\,{K^{ - 1}}$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'$\\dfrac{d H}{d t}=\\dfrac{K A}{L} \\Delta T$ ( $\\mathrm{K}=$ coefficient of thermal conductivity)<br>$\\therefore \\quad K=\\dfrac{L d H}{A d t \\Delta T}$<br>Unit of $K=W m^{-1} K^{-1}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2019,'NEET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(36923,'physics',25,896,2059,'A steel rod of cross-sectional area $1 {~m}^{2}$ is acted upon $y$ forces shown in figure. Determine the total elongation of the bar. Take $Y=2.0 \\times 10^{11} {~N} / {m}^{2}$.','[{"text": "$5.1\\\\,\\\\mu m$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2.7\\\\,\\\\mu m$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1.3\\\\,\\\\mu m$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$6.7\\\\,\\\\mu m$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'The action of forces on each part of rod is shown in figure   <img src=\'/img/data/physics/25_36923_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />  We know that the extension due to external force $F$ is given by<br>$\\Delta=\\dfrac{F l}{A Y}$<br>$\\Delta_{A B}=\\dfrac{\\left(60 \\times 10^{3}\\right) \\times 1.5}{1 \\times 2 \\times 10^{11}}=4.5 \\times 10^{-7} {~m}$<br>$\\Delta_{B C}=\\dfrac{\\left(70 \\times 10^{3}\\right) \\times 1}{1 \\times 2 \\times 10^{11}}=3.5 \\times 10^{-7} {~m}$<br>and $\\Delta_{C D}=\\dfrac{\\left(50 \\times 10^{3}\\right) \\times 2}{1 \\times 2 \\times 10^{11}}=5.0 \\times 10^{-7} {~m}$<br>The total extension, <br>$\\Delta=\\Delta_{A B}+\\Delta_{B C}+\\Delta_{C D}$<br>$ = 4.5 \\times {10^{ - 7}} + 3.5 \\times {10^{ - 7}} + 5.0 \\times {10^{ - 7}}$<br>$ = 13 \\times {10^{ - 7}}\\,m = 1.3\\,\\,\\mu m$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/25_36923_question_1.png'),
(36924,'physics',27,1382,3323,'A black body radiated maximum energy around a particular wavelength at a given temperature. If the temperature of the black body is now changed so that it radiates maximum energy around a wavelength $n$ time the original wavelength $(n < 1)$ then power radiated by it will increase by a factor of:','[{"text": "$n^{2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{1}{n^{2}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$n^{4}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{1}{n^{4}}$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'From Wien\'s law<br>$\\begin{aligned}& \\lambda_{1} T_{1}=b \\\\& n \\lambda_{1} \\cdot T_{2}=b \\Rightarrow T_{2}=T_{1} / n \\\\& P_{1}=C T_{1}^{4} \\\\& P_{2}=C T_{2}^{4}=C\\left(T_{1} / n\\right)^{4}=1 / n^{4}\\end{aligned}$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(36926,'physics',26,1137,2723,'A rain drop of radius $1.5\\,mm$, experiences a drag force $$F=\\left(2 \\times 10^{-5} v\\right) N$$, while falling through air from a height $2\\,km$, with a velocity $$v$$. The terminal velocity of the rain drop will be nearly ( use $g = 10\\,m{s^{ - 2}}$ )','[{"text": "$80\\\\,\\\\,m{s^{ - 1}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$200\\\\,\\\\,m{s^{ - 1}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$3\\\\,\\\\,m{s^{ - 1}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$7\\\\,\\\\,m{s^{ - 1}}$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'When the rain drop reaches terminal velocity then<br>$$\\begin{gathered}F=2 \\times 10^{-5} v=\\dfrac{4}{3} \\pi r^{3} \\rho g \\\\\\Rightarrow 2 \\times 10^{-5} v=\\dfrac{4}{3} \\times \\dfrac{22}{7} \\times\\left(1.5 \\times 10^{-3}\\right)^{3} \\times 10^{3} \\times 10\\end{gathered}$$<br>On solving, $$v=7.07 \\mathrm{~ms}^{-1} \\approx 7 \\mathrm{~ms}^{-1}$$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(36928,'physics',27,1382,3322,'Experimental investigations show that the intensity of solar radiation is maximum for a wavelength $480\\,nm$ in the visible region.<br>Estimate the surface temperature of the sun. (Given, Wien\'s constant $( = 2.88 \\times {10^{ - 3}}mK.)$','[{"text": "$6000\\\\,K$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$4000\\\\,K$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${10^6}\\\\,K$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$8000\\\\,K$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'The Wien\'s radiation law is given by<br>$\\lambda_{m} T=b$<br>For the given problem,  $b = 2.88 \\times {10^{ - 3}}mK$.<br>Hence, $\\lambda_{m} T=2.88 \\times 10^{-3}$<br>$ \\Rightarrow T = \\frac{{2.88 \\times {{10}^{ - 3}}}}{{{\\lambda _m}}}$<br>$ = \\frac{{2.88 \\times {{10}^{ - 3}}}}{{480 \\times {{10}^{ - 9}}}} = 6000\\,K$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(36929,'physics',26,1137,2723,'Two identical spherical drops of water are falling (vertically downwards) through air with a steady velocity of $5\\,\\,cm/\\sec $. If both the drops coalesce (combine) to form a new spherical drop, the terminal velocity of the new drop will be (neglect buoyant force on the drops).','[{"text": "$5 \\\\times 2\\\\,cm/\\\\sec $", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$5 \\\\times \\\\sqrt 2 \\\\,cm/\\\\sec $", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$5 \\\\times {(4)^{1/3}}\\\\,cm/\\\\sec $", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{5}{{\\\\sqrt 2 }}\\\\,cm/\\\\sec $", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'When two drops of radius $r$ each combine to form a big drop, the radius of big drop will be given by<br>$$\\dfrac{4}{3} \\pi R^{3}=\\dfrac{4 \\pi}{3} r^{2}+\\dfrac{4 \\pi}{3} r^{3}$$<br><br>$$\\text { or } R^{3}=2 r^{3} \\text { or } R=2^{1 / 3} r$$<br>Now $$\\quad \\dfrac{v_{R}}{v_{r}}=\\left(\\dfrac{R}{r}\\right)^{2}=2^{\\frac{2}{3}}=4^{\\frac{1}{3}}$$<br>$\\therefore \\quad {v_R} = 5 \\times {4^{1/3}}\\,cm/s$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(36932,'physics',27,1382,3323,'Heat travels through vacuum by:','[{"text": "Conduction", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Convection", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Radiation", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both (1) and (2)", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Heat radiations are electromagnetic waves of high wavelength. They can travel in vacuum',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(36933,'physics',26,1137,2724,'Poiseuille\'s equation holds good when','[{"text": "The flow is steady and stream line", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "The pressure is constant at every cross section", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "The liquid in contact with the walls is stationary", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "All of the above", "isCorrect": true}]',3,NULL,NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(36935,'physics',25,896,2061,'The pressure increases by $1.0 \\times {10^4}N/{m^2}$ for every meter of depth beneath the surface of the ocean. At what depth (in $m$) does the volume surface of a Pyrex glass cube, $1.0 \\times {10^{ - 2}}m$ on an edge at the ocean\'s surface, decrease by $1.0 \\times {10^{ - 10}}\\,{m^3}$','[{"text": "$155\\\\,m$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$260\\\\,m$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$310\\\\,m$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$300\\\\,m$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'We know change in pressure,<br>$\\Delta P =  - K\\frac{{\\Delta V}}{{{V_0}}}$<br>$ =  - \\left( {2.6 \\times {{10}^{10}}\\,N/{m^2}} \\right)\\frac{{ - 1.0 \\times {{10}^{ - 10}}\\,{m^3}}}{{1.0 \\times {{10}^{ - 6}}\\,{m^3}}}$<br>$ = 2.6 \\times {10^6}\\,{\\rm{N}}/{{\\rm{m}}^2}$<br>where we have expressed the volume $V_{0}$ <br>of the cube at the ocean\' surface as <br>$V_{0}=\\left(1.0 \\times 10^{-2} {~m}\\right)^{3}=1.0 \\times 10^{-6} {~m}^{3}$.<br>Since the pressure increases by $1.0 \\times 10^{4} {~N} / {m}^{2}$ per meter of depth,<br> the depth is $\\frac{{2.6 \\times {{10}^6}\\,N/{m^2}}}{{1.0 \\times {{10}^4}\\frac{{N/{m^2}}}{{\\,m}}}} = 260\\,m$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(36936,'physics',27,1382,3322,'The surface temperature of the stars is determined using:','[{"text": "Wien\'s displacement law", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Planck\'s law", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Kirchhoff\'s law", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Rayleigh-Jeans law", "isCorrect": false}]',0,NULL,NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(36937,'physics',26,1137,2724,'In Poiseuille\'s method of determination of coefficient of viscosity, the physical quantity that requires greater accuracy in measurement is','[{"text": "Pressure difference", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Volume of the liquid collected", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Length of the capillary tube", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Inner radius of the capillary tube", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'As the poiseulle\'s equation has $$r^{4}$$ so the radius is to be measured with more accuracy.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(36938,'physics',23,860,1973,'Two blocks $A$ and $B$ each of equal masses $m$ are released from the top of a smooth fixed wedge as shown in the figure. Find the magnitude of the acceleration of the centre of mass of the two blocks.  Take $g=10 {~m} / {s}^{2}$','[{"text": "$5.0\\\\,m/{s^2}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$1.0\\\\,m/{s^2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$8.0\\\\,m/{s^2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$3.0\\\\,m/{s^2}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Let us consider incline surfaces of wedge as<br> $x$-axis and $y$-axis respectively. We can write accelerations of blocks $A$ and $B$. <img src=\'/img/data/physics/23_36938_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' /> ${{\\vec a}_A} = g\\sin 30\\hat i$ and ${{\\vec a}_B} = g\\sin 60^\\circ \\hat j$<br> The acceleration of centre of mass of two blocks can be written as<br>${{\\vec a}_{cm}} = \\frac{{{m_A}{{\\vec a}_A} + {m_B}{{\\vec a}_B}}}{{{m_A} + {m_B}}}$<br>$ = \\frac{{mg\\sin {\\text{ }}30^\\circ {\\text{ }}\\hat i + mg\\sin {\\text{ }}60^\\circ \\hat j{\\text{ }}}}{{m + m}}$<br>$ = \\frac{{g\\sin 30^\\circ \\hat i + g\\sin 60^\\circ \\hat j}}{2}$<br>$ = \\frac{g}{4}\\hat i + \\frac{{\\sqrt 3 g}}{4}\\hat j$<br>$ \\Rightarrow \\left| {{{\\vec a}_{cm}}} \\right| = \\frac{g}{2} = 5.0\\,m/{s^2}$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/23_36938_question_1.png'),
(36940,'physics',27,1382,3323,'A hot and a cold body are kept in vacuum separated from each other. Even though the bodies are not in contact temperature changes for both. The correct explanation for the above observations is possible by using:','[{"text": "Wien\'s displacement", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Stefan\'s law", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Newton\'s law of cooling", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Kirchhoff\'s law", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Convection and conduction mode of heat transfer require physical contact between the bodies to transfer heat but radiation does not require any medium. As they transfer heat as electromagnetic waves/radiation. This is explained by stefan\'s law',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(36941,'physics',26,1137,2724,'Which factor controls the better flow rate of a liquid through the syringe?','[{"text": "The pressure exerted by the thumb", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "The length of the needle", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "The nature of the liquid", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "The radius of the syringe", "isCorrect": true}]',3,NULL,NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(36942,'physics',23,860,1972,'Two balls $A$ and $B$ of equal masses are projected upward simultaneously, one from the ground with speed $50\\,\\,m{s^{ - 1}}$ and other from height  $40\\,m$ above the first ball high tower, with initial speed $30 {~ms}^{-1}$. Find the maximum height  attained by their centre of mass. Take $g=10 {~m} / {s}^{2}$','[{"text": "$150\\\\,m$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$200\\\\,m$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$400\\\\,m$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$100\\\\,m$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Let us take balls \'$A \'$ and \'$B$ \' as system the initial height of centre of mass from ground,<br>$y_{ic}=\\dfrac{m \\times 0+m \\times 40}{2 m}=20 {~m}$<br>Initial velocity of centre of mass<br>${u_{cm}} = \\frac{{m \\times 50 + m \\times 30}}{{2\\,m}} = 40\\,m{s^{ - 1}}$<br>Acceleration of centre of mass, $a_{{cm}}=-g$<br>Using kinematics equation: $v_{{cm}}^{2}=u_{{cm}}^{2}+2 a_{{cm}} H$ <br>Here $H$ is the maximum height reach by centre of mass of two balls from initial level.<br>$\\therefore 0^{2}=40^{2}-2 \\times 10 \\times H \\Rightarrow H=\\dfrac{1600}{20}=80 {~m}$<br>Hence, maximum height reach by centre of mass from ground level will be<br>$h_{\\max }=\\left(h_{{cm}}\\right)_{\\text {initial }}+H=20+80=100 {~m}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/23_36942_question_1.png'),
(36944,'physics',27,1382,3323,'From stefan\'s law the radiation emitted by a body depends on','[{"text": "Area", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Square of temperature", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Cube of temperature", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Density of the body", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'$\\dfrac{d Q}{d t}=\\sigma A T^{4} \\Rightarrow \\dfrac{d Q}{d t} \\alpha A$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(36945,'physics',26,1137,2724,'Water flows in a streamlined manner through a capillary tube of radius $$a$$, the pressure difference being $$P$$ and the rate of flow $$Q$$. If the radius is reduced to $$a / 2$$ and the pressure increased to $$2 P$$, the rate of flow becomes','[{"text": "$$4 Q$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$Q$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$\\\\dfrac{Q}{8}$$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$$\\\\dfrac{Q}{4}$$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'$$V=\\dfrac{\\pi \\Delta P r^{4}}{8 \\eta l} \\quad \\therefore V \\propto \\Delta \\operatorname{Pr}^{4}$$<br>[ $$\\eta$$ and $$l$$ are constants]<br>$$\\begin{aligned}& \\therefore \\quad \\dfrac{V_{2}}{V_{1}}=\\left(\\dfrac{P_{2}}{P_{1}}\\right)\\left(\\dfrac{r_{2}}{r_{1}}\\right)^{4}=2 \\times\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right)^{4}=\\dfrac{1}{8} \\\\& \\therefore V_{2}=\\dfrac{Q}{8}\\end{aligned}$$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(36947,'physics',25,898,2065,'A catapult consists of two parallel rubber strings, each of lengths 10 $cm$ and cross-sectional area $10 {~mm}^{2}$. When stretched by 5 $cm$ , it can throw a stone of mass 100 $g$ to a vertical height of 25 $m$ . Determine Young\'s modulus of elasticity of rubber','[{"text": "$10 \\\\times {10^8}\\\\,N/{m^2}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$8 \\\\times {10^8}\\\\,N/{m^2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$15 \\\\times {10^8}\\\\,N/{m^2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$20 \\\\times {10^8}\\\\,N/{m^2}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'A stretched catapult has elastic potential energy stored in it(elastic potential energy stored in both rubber strings) <br>$U=\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2} \\dfrac{Y A l^{2}}{L}\\right) \\times 2$<br>This energy, when imparted to the stone, it flies off a height 20 m .<br>Energy possessed by the stone $=m g h$.<br> Now,<br>$\\begin{gathered}U=m g h \\Rightarrow \\dfrac{Y A l^{2}}{L}=m g h \\\\Y=\\dfrac{m g h L}{A l^{2}}=\\dfrac{1 \\times 10^{-1} \\times 10 \\times 25 \\times 10^{-1}}{10 \\times 10^{-6} \\times\\left(5 \\times 10^{-2}\\right)^{2}} \\\\\\therefore Y=10 \\times 10^{8} {~N} / {m}^{2}\\end{gathered}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(36948,'physics',27,1382,3323,'At temperature $T$, the power radiated by a body is $Q$ watts. At the temperature $3T$ the power radiated by it will be','[{"text": "$9Q$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$3Q$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$81Q$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$27Q$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Power radiation is<br>$Q\\,\\alpha \\,{T^4}$<br>$\\frac{{{Q_1}}}{{{Q_2}}} = {\\left( {\\frac{{{T_1}}}{{\\,{T_2}}}} \\right)^4} = {\\left( {\\frac{T}{{3\\,T}}} \\right)^4}$<br>$ \\Rightarrow {Q_2} = 81{Q_1} = 81Q$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(36949,'physics',26,1137,2724,'The formula for the resistance of a fluid is','[{"text": "$$R=\\\\dfrac{8 \\\\eta l}{\\\\pi r^{2}}$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$R=\\\\dfrac{\\\\pi r^{4}}{8 \\\\eta l}$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$R=\\\\dfrac{8 \\\\eta l}{\\\\pi r^{4}}$$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$$R=\\\\dfrac{8 \\\\eta l}{\\\\pi r^{3}}$$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'The rate of liquid flow is<br>$Q = \\frac{{\\pi \\Delta {{\\Pr }^4}}}{{8\\eta l}} = \\frac{{\\Delta P}}{{\\left( {\\frac{{8\\eta l}}{{\\pi {r^4}}}} \\right)}}$<br>By comparing the above equation with ohm\'s law $$i=\\dfrac{\\Delta V}{R}$$ then the resistance offered to the fluid is $$\\quad R=\\dfrac{8 \\eta \\ell}{\\pi r^{4}}$$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(36953,'physics',26,1137,2725,'A volume $V$ of viscous liquid flows per unit time due to a pressure head $$\\Delta P$$ along a pipe of diameter $D$ and length $L$. Instead of this pipe a set of 4 pipes each of diameter $$\\dfrac{D}{2}$$ and length $$2 L$$ is connected to the same pressure head $$\\Delta P$$. Now, the volume of the liquid flowing per unit time is','[{"text": "$$V$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$\\\\dfrac{V}{4}$$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$$\\\\dfrac{V}{8}$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$\\\\dfrac{V}{16}$$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Given that<br>$$\\begin{aligned}& V=\\dfrac{\\pi \\Delta P D^{4}}{64 \\eta L} \\quad\\left(r=\\dfrac{D}{2}\\right) \\\\& V^{\\prime}=4\\left[\\dfrac{\\pi \\Delta P D^{4}}{8 \\times 64 \\eta 2 L}\\right]=\\dfrac{V}{4}\\end{aligned}$$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(36954,'physics',25,896,2063,'Match the Column I and Column II<br> <br>Choose the correct answer from the options given below<div class="matching-question"></div>','[{"text": "A - R,      B - S,\\tC - P,  \\tD - Q", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "A - Q,      B - S, \\tC - P, \\tD - R", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "A - S,      B - Q, \\tC - R, \\tD - P", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "A - R,      B - P, \\tC - Q, \\tD - S", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'A - R, B - S, C - P, D - Q <br>(1) Stress is the restoring force per unit area set up inside a body, when an external force is applied on it.<br><img src=\'/img/data/physics/25_36954_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />  <br>Shear modulus $=\\dfrac{\\text { Shear stress }}{\\text { Shear strain }}$<br><img src=\'/img/data/physics/25_36954_explanation_2.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />  <br>Bulk modulus $=\\dfrac{\\text { Volumetric stress }}{\\text { Volumetric strain }}$<br><img src=\'/img/data/physics/25_36954_explanation_3.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />  <br>Young\'s modulus $=\\dfrac{\\text { Longitudinal stress }}{\\text { Longitudinal strain }}$',NULL,'Hard','Match the following',2024,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/25_36954_question_1.png'),
(36956,'physics',27,1382,3323,'The ratio of radiant energies radiated per unit surface area by the two bodies is $16: 1$, the temperature of hotter body is $1000\\,K$, then the temperature of colder body will be','[{"text": "$500\\\\,K$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$250\\\\,K$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$62.5\\\\,K$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1000\\\\,K$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'$Q \\propto T^{4} \\Rightarrow \\dfrac{Q_{1}}{Q_{2}}=\\dfrac{T_{1}^{4}}{T_{2}^{4}} \\Rightarrow T_{2}^{4}=\\left(\\dfrac{E_{2}}{E_{1}}\\right) T_{1}^{4}$<br>$ \\Rightarrow T_2^4 = \\frac{1}{{16}} \\times {(1000)^4} = {\\left( {\\frac{{1000}}{2}} \\right)^4}$<br>$ \\Rightarrow {T_2} = 500\\,K$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(36957,'physics',26,1138,2730,'The rain drops are in spherical shape due to','[{"text": "Viscosity", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Surface tension", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Thrust on drop", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both (1) and (2)", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Surface tension tends to make the area of rain drop minimum.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(36958,'physics',25,896,2059,'A wire of length $L$ and radius $r$ is clamped at one end. If its other end is pulled by a force $F$, its length increases by $l$. If the radius of the wire and the applied force both are reduced to half of their original values keeping original length constant, the increase in length will become','[{"text": "3 times", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "2 times", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{3}{2}$ times", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "4 times", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Young\'s Modulus is given by<br>$Y=\\dfrac{F L}{A \\Delta l}$<br>$F=\\dfrac{Y\\left(\\pi r^{2}\\right) \\Delta l}{L}$   $\\quad \\quad (1)$<br>Now,<br>$Y = \\frac{{(F/2)L}}{{\\left( {\\pi {r^2}/4} \\right)\\Delta \\,l\'}} \\Rightarrow F = Y \\cdot \\frac{{\\Delta l\'}}{L} \\cdot 2 \\times \\frac{{\\pi {r^2}}}{4}\\quad \\quad (2)$<br>From (1) and (2), we get<br>$\\Rightarrow \\Delta l^{\\prime}=2 \\Delta l$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',2024,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(36959,'physics',23,860,1972,'A small ball of mass $m$ is projected with a minimum horizontal velocity $v_{0}$ on a smooth wedge of mass $M$ so that it will reach the highest point $P$ of the wedge. If $\\dfrac{m}{M}=\\dfrac{1}{4}$ and $R=0.60 {~m}$, then find the value of $v_{0}$. Take $g=10 {~m} / {s}^{2}$.','[{"text": "$6\\\\,m/s$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$2\\\\,m/s$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$10\\\\,m/s$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$4\\\\,m/s$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'<img src=\'/img/data/physics/23_36959_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />    If the ball reaches at point P, <br>the velocity of the ball with respect to wedge should be $\\sqrt{g R}$.<br>Using work-energy theorem from centre of mass frame  at $A$ and the highest point $P$<br>${W_{{\\rm{ext}}}} + {W_{{\\rm{int }}}} = {W_{{\\rm{ext}}}} + 0 = {(\\Delta K)_{{\\rm{cm}}}}$<br>${W_{{\\rm{gravity}}}} = {(\\Delta K)_{{\\rm{CM}}}}$<br>$ - mg(2R) = {\\left[ {\\frac{1}{2}\\mu v_{{\\rm{rel}}}^2} \\right]_{{\\rm{final}}}} - {\\left[ {\\frac{1}{2}\\mu v_{{\\rm{rel}}}^2} \\right]_{{\\rm{initial}}}}$<br>$ - mg2R = \\frac{1}{2}\\mu {(V + v)^2} - \\frac{1}{2}\\mu v_0^2\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,(1)$<br>$\\mu=\\left(\\dfrac{m M}{m+M}\\right)$ and $v=\\sqrt{g R}$<br>As there is no external force acting on the system<br> in horizontal direction. <br>The linear momentum of the  system should be conserved.<br>$i.\\,e.$  $m v_{0}-m(V-v)+M V$<br>$ \\Rightarrow V = \\frac{{m\\left( {{v_0} + v} \\right)}}{{(m + M)}}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,(2)$<br>From Eqs. (1) and (2), we get<br>$ \\Rightarrow \\quad {v_0} = \\sqrt {\\left( {5 + \\frac{{4m}}{M}} \\right)gR} $<br>$ = \\sqrt {\\left( {5 + \\frac{4}{4}} \\right) \\times 10 \\times 0.6}  = 6\\,m/s$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/23_36959_question_1.png'),
(36960,'physics',27,1382,3323,'If the temperature of a hot body is increased by $50 \\%$ then the increase in the quantity of emitted heat radiation will be','[{"text": "$200 \\\\%$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$400 \\\\%$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$125 \\\\%$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$300 \\\\%$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'$Q \\propto T^{4} \\Rightarrow \\dfrac{Q_{2}}{Q_{1}}=\\left(\\dfrac{T_{2}}{T_{1}}\\right)^{4}$<br>$\\Rightarrow \\dfrac{Q_{1}}{Q_{2}}=\\left(\\dfrac{T}{T+T / 2}\\right)^{4}=\\dfrac{16}{81} \\Rightarrow Q_{2}=\\dfrac{81}{16} Q_{1}$<br>$\\%$ increase in energy $=\\dfrac{Q_{2}-Q_{1}}{Q_{1}} \\times 100=400 \\%$.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(36961,'physics',26,1138,2729,'Soap helps in cleaning clothes, because','[{"text": "Chemicals of soap change", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "It increases the surface tension of the solution", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "It absorbs the dirt", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "It lowers the surface tension of the solution", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Soap helps to lower the surface tension of solution, thus soap get stick to the dust particles and grease and these are removed by action of water.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38148,'physics',28,1377,3303,'Microwave oven acts on the principle of :','[{"text": "Giving rotational energy to water molecules", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Giving vibrational energy to water molecules", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Giving translational energy to water molecules", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Transferring electrons from lower to higher<br>energy levels in water molecule", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Energy of microwaves lie in range of vibration energy of water molecules.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2016,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(36962,'physics',25,896,2059,'Young\'s modules of material of a wire of length \' $L$ \' and cross-sectional area $A$ is $Y$. If the length of the wire is doubled and cross-sectional area is halved then Young\'s modules will be','[{"text": "$2 Y$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$Y$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$4 Y$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{Y}{4}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Young\'s modulus is the property of the material and it does not depend upon the physical dimensions of the wire. Therefore, Young\'s modulus will be $Y$. So, correct option is $(2)$.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2024,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(36964,'physics',27,1382,3323,'A man has total surface area of $1.5\\,{m^2}$. If the temperature of his body is $310\\,K$ then find the total rate of radiation of energy from the body.','[{"text": "$785\\\\,J$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$566\\\\,J$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$682\\\\,J$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$732\\\\,J$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'$P_{R}=\\sigma A T^{2}$<br>$ = 5.67 \\times {10^{ - 8}} \\times 1.5{(310)^4} = 785\\,J.$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(36965,'physics',26,1138,2727,'A pin or a needle floats on the surface of water, the reason for this is','[{"text": "Surface tension", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Less weight", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Upthrust of liquid", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "None of the above", "isCorrect": false}]',0,NULL,NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(36967,'physics',23,860,1972,'A child of mass 4 $kg$ jumps from cart $B$ to cart $A$ and then immediately back to cart $B$. The mass of each cart is 20 $kg$ and they are initially at rest. In both the cases the child jumps at $6 {~m} / {s}$ relative to the cart. If the cart moves along the same line with negligible friction with the final velocities of $v_{B}$ and $v_{A}$, respectively, find the ratio of $6 v_{B}$ and $5 v_{A}$.','[{"text": "3", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "2", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "1", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "5", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'All the velocities shown in diagrams are w.r.t. ground. <br>After first jump:  <img src=\'/img/data/physics/23_36967_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />  $20 v_{1}=4 v_{2} \\text { and } v_{1}+v_{2}=6 \\text { (given) }$<br>Solve to get $v_{1}=1 {~m} / {s}, v_{2}=5 {~m} / {s}$<br>When child arrives on $A$ :    <img src=\'/img/data/physics/23_36967_explanation_2.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />    $(20+4) v_{3}=4 v_{2} \\Rightarrow v_{3}=5 / 6 {~m} / {s}$<br>After the second jump:<img src=\'/img/data/physics/23_36967_explanation_3.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' /> $v_{4}+v_{A}=6,24 v_{3}=20 v_{A}-4 v_{4}$<br>Solve to get $v_{A}=\\dfrac{11}{6} {~m} / {s}, v_{4}=\\dfrac{25}{6} {~m} / {s}$<br>when child arrives on $B$ :<img src=\'/img/data/physics/23_36967_explanation_4.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />  $24{v_B} = 4{v_4} + 20{v_1}$<br>$ \\Rightarrow 24{v_B} = 4\\left( {\\frac{{25}}{6}} \\right) + 20 \\times 1 \\Rightarrow {v_B} = \\frac{{55}}{{36}}m/s$<br>Now $\\dfrac{6 v_{B}}{5 v_{A}}=\\dfrac{6 \\times 55 \\times 6}{36 \\times 5 \\times 11}=1$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/23_36967_question_1.png'),
(36968,'physics',27,1382,3323,'Two black metallic spheres of radius $4 m$, at $2000\\,k$ and $1\\,m$ at $4000\\,k$ will have ratio of energy radiation as','[{"text": "$4: 1$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1: 1$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$1: 2$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2: 1$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'$\\dfrac{Q_{1}}{Q_{2}}=\\dfrac{r_{1}^{2} T_{1}^{4}}{r_{2}^{2} T_{2}^{4}}=\\dfrac{4^{2}}{1^{2}} \\times\\left(\\dfrac{2000}{4000}\\right)^{4}=1$.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(36969,'physics',26,1138,2727,'Surface tension is an atomic/molecular property of','[{"text": "Atoms in the interior", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Atoms at the center", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Atoms which are exterior", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Atoms at surface bounded entirely by different atoms", "isCorrect": true}]',3,NULL,NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(36972,'physics',27,1382,3323,'Suppose the sun expands so that its radius becomes 100 times its present radius and its surface temperature becomes half of its present value. The total energy emitted by it then will increase by a factor of:','[{"text": "$625$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$10^{4}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$16$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$256$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'From Stefan\'s law, the emissive power of a body at absolute temperature $T$ is<br>$E=e \\sigma T^{4} A$<br>When $R^{\\prime}=100 R$ and $T^{\\prime}=\\dfrac{T}{2}$ then energy emitted is<br>$\\begin{aligned}& E^{\\prime}=\\sigma 4 \\pi(100 R)^{2}\\left(\\dfrac{T}{2}\\right)^{4} \\\\& \\therefore E^{\\prime}=\\left(\\dfrac{100}{4}\\right)^{2} \\times E \\\\& \\therefore \\dfrac{E^{\\prime}}{E}=625\\end{aligned}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(36973,'physics',26,1138,2727,'A square frame of side $$L$$ is dipped in a liquid. On taking out, a membrane is formed. If the surface tension of the liquid is $$T$$, the force acting on the frame will be','[{"text": "$$2 T L$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$4 T L$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$8 T L$$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$$10 T L$$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Force on each side $$=2 T L$$ [due to two surfaces]<br>$$\\therefore$$ Force on the frame $$=4(2 T L)=8 T L$$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(36975,'physics',23,860,1972,'A massless spring of force constant $1000 {Nm}^{-1}$ is compressed a distance of 20 $cm$ between discs of 8 $kg$ and 2 $kg$ , spring is not attached to discs. The system is given an initial velocity $3 {~ms}^{-1}$ perpendicular to length of spring as shown in the figure. What is ground frame velocity of 2 $kg$ block  when spring regains its natural length.','[{"text": "$1\\\\,m/{s^{ - 1}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$8\\\\,m/{s^{ - 1}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$6\\\\,m/{s^{ - 1}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$5\\\\,m/{s^{ - 1}}$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Let $v_{1}$ and $v_{2}$ are the velocities along the spring of $A$ and $B$ when spring regains it natural length then<br>$2{v_1} = 8{v_2}\\quad \\quad \\quad \\quad \\quad \\quad \\quad \\quad \\quad \\quad \\quad \\quad \\quad (1)$<br>$\\frac{1}{2}2v_1^2 + \\frac{1}{2}8v_2^2 = \\frac{1}{2}1000{\\left( {\\frac{{20}}{{100}}} \\right)^2}\\quad \\quad \\quad \\quad \\,\\,\\,(2)$<br>From (1) and (2),  ${v_1} = 4\\,m{s^{ - 1}},{v_2} = 1\\,m{s^{ - 1}}$<br>Ground frame velocity of $A$$=\\sqrt{v^{2}+v_{1}^{2}}=\\sqrt{3^{2}+4^{2}}=5 {~ms}^{-1}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/23_36975_question_1.png'),
(36976,'physics',27,1382,3323,'If the temperature of the sun were to be increased from $T$ to $2\\,T$ and its radius from $ R$ to $2 R$, then the ratio of the radiant energy received on the earth to what it was previously will be','[{"text": "4", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "32", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "64", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "8", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'$Q \\propto r^{2} T^{4} \\Rightarrow \\dfrac{Q_{2}}{Q_{1}}=\\left(\\dfrac{r_{2}}{r_{1}}\\right)^{2}\\left(\\dfrac{T_{2}}{T_{1}}\\right)^{4}$<br>$=(2)^{2} \\times(2)^{4}=64 .$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(36977,'physics',26,1138,2727,'What is the radius of the biggest aluminium coin of thickness, $$t$$ and density $$\\rho$$, which will still be able to float on the water surface of surface tension $S$ ?','[{"text": "$$\\\\dfrac{3 S}{4 \\\\rho g t}$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$\\\\dfrac{4 S}{3 \\\\rho g t}$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$\\\\dfrac{S}{\\\\rho g t}$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$\\\\dfrac{2 S}{\\\\rho g t}$$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Let $R$ be the radius of the biggest aluminium coin which will be supported on the surface of water due to surface tension.<br>Then, $$m g=S \\times 2 \\pi R$$<br>$$\\begin{aligned}& \\Rightarrow \\quad \\pi R^{2} t \\rho g=S \\times 2 \\pi R \\\\& \\Rightarrow \\quad R=2 S / \\rho g t\\end{aligned}$$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(36979,'physics',27,1382,3323,'A sphere of $3\\,cm$ radius acts like a black body. If it is in equilibrium with its surroundings and absorb $30k$watt of energy from the surrounding. Then the temperature of the sphere will be:','[{"text": "$916{\\\\mkern 1mu} K$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$816{\\\\mkern 1mu} K$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2616{\\\\mkern 1mu} K$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$1616{\\\\mkern 1mu} K$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Radius of sphere $r = 3\\,cm = 0.03\\,m$<br>The energy absorbed<br>$E = 30\\,kW = 30 \\times {10^3}watt$<br>Energy absorbed by black body when the temperature of surroundings is $T$, is given by<br>$E = \\sigma A{T^4}{\\text{ }}$<br>${\\text{or }}\\,\\,\\,{\\mkern 1mu} {\\kern 1pt} 30 \\times {10^3} = 5.672 \\times {10^{ - 8}} \\times 4\\pi {(0.03)^2} \\times {T^4}$<br>$30 \\times {10^3} = 0.064 \\times {10^{ - 8}} \\times {T^4}{T^4} = \\frac{{30 \\times {{10}^3}}}{{0.064 \\times {{10}^{ - 8}}}}$<br>$ = 46.875 \\times {10^{12}}$<br>$ = 2.616 \\times {10^3} = 2616\\,K$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(36981,'physics',26,1138,2729,'Angle of contact of a liquid with a solid depends on','[{"text": "Solid only", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Liquid only", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both solid and liquid only", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Orientation of the solid surface in liquid", "isCorrect": false}]',2,NULL,NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(36983,'physics',27,1382,3323,'A black body is heated from $27^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C}$ to $927^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C}$. The ratio of radiations emitted will be:-','[{"text": "$1: 64$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1: 256$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$1: 4$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1: 16$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Here, initial temperature,<br>${T_1} = 27^\\circ C = 300\\,K$<br>Final temperature, ${T_2} = 927^\\circ C = 1200\\,K$<br>According to Stefan\'s law, the radiant energy is $E \\alpha T^{4}$<br>Here, $\\dfrac{E_{1}}{E_{2}}=\\dfrac{T_{1}^{4}}{T_{2}^{4}}$<br>$=\\left(\\dfrac{300}{1200}\\right)^{4}=\\left(\\dfrac{1}{4}\\right)^{4}=\\dfrac{1}{256}$<br>Hence, $E_{1}: E_{2}=1: 256$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(36985,'physics',26,1138,2729,'If the surface of a liquid is plane, then the angle of contact of the liquid with the walls of container is','[{"text": "Acute angle", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Obtuse angle", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$90^{\\\\circ}$$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$$0^{\\\\circ}$$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Tangent drawn at point of contact makes $$90^{\\circ}$$ with wall of container.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(36990,'physics',26,1138,2729,'Nature of meniscus for liquid of $$0^{\\circ}$$ angle of contact','[{"text": "Plane", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Parabolic", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Hemi-spherical", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Cylindrical", "isCorrect": false}]',2,NULL,NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(36993,'physics',25,896,2059,'With rise in temperature, the Young\'s modulus of elasticity','[{"text": "remains unchanged", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "changes erratically", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "decreases", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "increases", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Young\'s Modulus, $Y=\\dfrac{F L}{A \\Delta L}$<br>$Y \\propto \\dfrac{1}{\\Delta L}$<br>$\\Delta L=L(1+\\alpha \\Delta T)$<br>As $\\Delta T$ increases, $\\Delta L$ increases and thus Young\'s modulus, $Y$, decreases.<br>So, correct option is (3).',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2024,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(36994,'physics',26,1138,2729,'A liquid does not wet the sides of a solid, if the angle of contact is','[{"text": "Zero", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Obtuse (More than $$90^{\\\\circ}$$ )", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Acute (Less than $$90^{\\\\circ}$$ )", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$90^{\\\\circ}$$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,NULL,NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37008,'physics',25,896,2063,'Two wires of equal lengths and cross-section areas are suspended, as shown in the figure. If their respective Young\'s moduli are ${Y_{1}}$ and ${Y_{2}}$, then the equivalent Young\'s modulus will be','[{"text": "${Y_{1}+Y_{2}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\dfrac{Y_{1}+Y_{2}}{2}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${\\\\dfrac{Y_{1} Y_{2}}{Y_{1}+Y_{2}}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\sqrt{Y_{1} Y_{2}}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'<img src=\'/img/data/physics/25_37008_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' /> ${K_{e q}=K_{1}+K_{2}}$<br>${\\dfrac{Y 2 A}{l}=\\dfrac{Y_{1} A}{l}+\\dfrac{Y_{2} A}{l}}$<br>${Y=\\dfrac{Y_{1}+Y_{2}}{2}}$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/25_37008_question_1.png'),
(36996,'physics',23,870,1996,'A bobbin has inner radius $r$ and outer radius $R$ is placed on a rough horizontal surface. A light is string wrapped over inner core, connects a block with bobbin as shown in the figure. Now system is released from rest and bobbin moves on the horizontal surface without sliding, and the string does not slide from bobbin, If $\\dfrac{r}{R}=0.25$, find the ratio of the acceleration of the block and bobbin $\\left(\\dfrac{a}{A}\\right)$.','[{"text": "0.15", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "0.3", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "0.75", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "0.97", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'As the motion of the bobbin is pure rolling, point $P$ should be instantaneous centre of rotation $(ICR)$ of the bobbin. Hence we can consider pure rotation of the bobbin about $P$. The acceleration of point $C ; a_{C}=\\alpha R$.  <img src=\'/img/data/physics/23_36996_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />  But the bobbin is moving with acceleration $A$,<br>Hence $A = \\alpha R\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,(1)$<br>The acceleration of point $Q ; a_{Q}=\\alpha(R-r)$.<br>But point $O$ is directly connected with block, it means the magnitude of acceleration of the point $Q$ should be equal to the acceleration of the block<br>${a_Q} = a = \\alpha (R - r)\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,(2)$<br>From (1) and (2), we get<br>$\\dfrac{a}{A}=\\dfrac{R-r}{R}=\\left(1-\\dfrac{r}{R}\\right)=1-0.25=0.75$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/23_36996_question_1.png'),
(36997,'physics',25,894,2057,'The stress-strain graph for materials ${A}$ and ${B}$ are shown in the figure. Graphs are drawn to the same scale. Which of the two is the stronger material?<div class="matching-question"></div>','[{"text": "${A}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${B}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both are equally strong", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "None of these", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Strength of material is determined by amount of stress required to  fracture the material.<br>So correct option is (1).',NULL,'Medium','Graph/Figure',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/25_36997_question_1.png'),
(36998,'physics',26,1138,2729,'Which of the following statements are true w.r.to contact angle','[{"text": "It\'s value lies between $$0^{\\\\circ}$$ and $$180^{\\\\circ}$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "It\'s value changes with the pair of solid and liquid", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "It does not depend upon the inclination of the solid in the liquid", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "All the above", "isCorrect": true}]',3,NULL,NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL);
INSERT INTO `qb_questions` (`id`, `subject_id`, `chapter_id`, `topic_id`, `subtopic_id`, `question_html`, `options`, `correct_index`, `explanation`, `explanation_image_url`, `difficulty`, `qtype`, `year`, `tag`, `created_at`, `question_image_url`) VALUES
(37001,'physics',25,896,2063,'The bar shown in the figure is made of a single piece of material. It is fixed at one end and consists of two segments of equal length ${\\dfrac{L}{2}}$ but different cross-sectional area ${A}$ and ${2 A}$. What is the change in length of the entire system under the action of an axial force ${F}$ ?','[{"text": "${\\\\dfrac{3 F L}{4 A Y}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${\\\\dfrac{3 F L}{8 A Y}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\dfrac{3 F L}{2 A Y}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\dfrac{2 F L}{3 A Y}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Elongation in first part with area ${A=\\dfrac{F L}{2 A Y}}$<br>Elongation in second part with area ${2 A=\\dfrac{F L}{4 A Y}}$<br>Total elongation ${=\\dfrac{3}{4} \\dfrac{F L}{A Y}}$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/25_37001_question_1.png'),
(37002,'physics',26,1138,2728,'If the surface tension of a liquid is $$T$$, the gain in surface energy for an increase in liquid surface by $$A$$ is','[{"text": "$$A T^{-1}$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$A T$$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$$A^{2} T$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$A^{2} T^{2}$$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Surface energy $$=$$ surface tension $$\\times$$ increment in area $$=T \\times A$$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37005,'physics',25,896,2059,'The graph shows the extension of a wire of length \\[1\\,m\\] suspended from the top of a roof at one end and with a load ${W}$ connected to the other end. If the cross sectional area of the wire is ${1 {~mm}^{2}}$, then the Young\'s modulus of the material of the wire is<div class="matching-question"></div>','[{"text": "${2 \\\\times 10^{11} {~N} {~m}^{-2}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${2 \\\\times 10^{10} {~N} {~m}^{-2}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${\\\\dfrac{1}{2} \\\\times 10^{11} {Nm}^{-2}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${4 \\\\times 10^{10} {~N} {~m}^{-2}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'${Y=\\dfrac{F / A}{\\Delta l / l}=\\dfrac{W l}{A \\Delta l} \\Rightarrow \\dfrac{W}{\\Delta l}=\\dfrac{Y A}{l}=}$ slope<br>${\\Rightarrow Y=\\dfrac{l}{A} \\times(}$ slope ${)=\\dfrac{1}{10^{-6}}\\left(\\dfrac{40-20}{(2-1) \\times 10^{-3}}\\right)}$<br>$=2 \\times 10^{10} {Nm}^{-2}$',NULL,'Medium','Graph/Figure',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/25_37005_question_1.png'),
(37006,'physics',26,1138,2728,'The surface tension of a liquid is $5\\,N{m^{ - 1}}$. If a thin film formed on a loop of area $0.02\\,\\,{m^{ - 2}}$ then its surface energy will be','[{"text": "$5 \\\\times {10^{ - 2}}\\\\,J$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2.5 \\\\times {10^{ - 2}}\\\\,J$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$3 \\\\times {10^{ - 1}}\\\\,J$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2 \\\\times {10^{ - 1}}\\\\,J$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Effective area $ = 2 \\times 0.02\\,{m^2} = 0.04\\,{m^2}$<br>Surface energy $ = 5N{m^{ - 1}} \\times 0.04\\,{m^2} = 2 \\times {10^{ - 1}}\\,J$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37012,'physics',25,896,2063,'If the ratio of lengths, radii and Young\'s modulii of steel and brass wires in the figure are ${a, b, c}$, respectively, then the corresponding ratio of increase in their lengths would be','[{"text": "${\\\\dfrac{2 a c}{b^{2}}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\dfrac{3 a}{2 b^{2} c}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${\\\\dfrac{3 c}{2 a b^{2}}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\dfrac{2 a^{2} c}{b}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'<img src=\'/img/data/physics/25_37012_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />  <br>Given, ${\\dfrac{r_{1}}{r_{2}}=b ; \\dfrac{l_{1}}{l_{2}}=a ; \\dfrac{Y_{1}}{Y_{2}}=c}$<br>${\\therefore \\Delta l_{1}=\\dfrac{(3 m g) l_{1}}{A_{1} Y_{1}}}$<br>and ${\\Delta l_{2}=\\dfrac{(2 m g) l_{2}}{A_{2} Y_{2}}}$<br>${\\therefore \\dfrac{\\Delta l_{1}}{\\Delta l_{2}}=\\dfrac{3 l_{1}}{2 l_{2} A_{1} Y_{1}} \\times A_{2} Y_{2}=\\dfrac{3}{2} \\dfrac{a}{b^{2} c}=\\dfrac{3 a}{2 b^{2} c}}$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/25_37012_question_1.png'),
(37013,'physics',26,1138,2730,'When $$10^{6}$$ small drops coalesce to make a new larger drop','[{"text": "Density decreases", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Density increases", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Temperature increases", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Temperature decreases", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Because energy is liberated.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37015,'physics',27,1378,3304,'A copper rod and a steel rod are heated. At $0^\\circ C$, the copper rod has a length $L_{C}$ and the steel one has a length $L_{S}$. When the rods are being heated or cooled, a difference of $5.00\\,cm$ is maintained between their lengths. Determine the value of $L_{C}$.<br>${\\alpha _{Cu}} = 5 \\times {10^{ - 5}}/\\,^\\circ C,{\\alpha _S} = 2 \\times {10^{ - 5}}/\\,^\\circ C.$','[{"text": "$3.33\\\\,cm$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$5\\\\,cm$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$8.33\\\\,cm$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$15\\\\,cm$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'As the different in their lengths is same<br>$\\Delta {L_{cu}} = \\Delta {L_s}$<br>${L_{Cu}}{\\alpha _{Cu}} = {L_S} \\cdot {\\alpha _S} \\Rightarrow 5{L_C} = 2{L_S}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\left( 1 \\right)$<br>${L_S} = \\frac{5}{2}{L_C}$<br>${L_S} - {L_C} = 5\\,cm\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\left( 2 \\right)$<br>$2.5{L_C} - {L_C} = 5$<br>${L_C} = \\frac{{5 \\times 2}}{3} = \\frac{{10}}{3} = 3.33\\,cm$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37016,'physics',26,1138,2730,'If a section of a soap bubble of radius $$r$$ by a plane through its centre is considered, the force on the half due to surface tension is:','[{"text": "$$2 \\\\pi r T$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$4 \\\\pi r T$$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$$\\\\pi r T$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$2 r T$$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Soap bubble has two surfaces<br>Total length $$2(2 \\pi r)$$<br>$$F=(4 \\pi r) \\times T$$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37018,'physics',27,1380,3306,'The coefficient of linear expansion of an in homogeneous rod changes linearly from $\\alpha_{1}$ to $\\alpha_{2}$ from one end to the other end of the rod. The effective coefficient of linear expansion of rod is','[{"text": "$\\\\dfrac{\\\\alpha_{1}+\\\\alpha_{2}}{2}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\alpha_{1}+\\\\alpha_{2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\sqrt{\\\\alpha_{1} \\\\alpha_{2}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\alpha_{1}-\\\\alpha_{2}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'The coefficient of linear expansion of the rod as a function of $x$ is<br>$\\alpha=\\alpha_{1}+\\left(\\dfrac{\\alpha_{2}-\\alpha_{1}}{L}\\right) x$<br>Let $d L$ is the small change in the length of the small part of length $d x$, for a rise in temperature of $\\Delta T$<br>$\\begin{aligned}d L & =\\alpha d x T \\\\\\Delta L & =\\int_{0}^{L} \\alpha d x \\Delta T \\\\L & =\\left(\\dfrac{\\alpha_{1}+\\alpha_{2}}{2}\\right) L \\Delta T ; \\quad \\alpha_{e f f}=\\left(\\dfrac{\\alpha_{1}+\\alpha_{2}}{2}\\right)\\end{aligned}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37019,'physics',26,1138,2730,'A spherical drop of water has $1\\,\\,mm$ radius. If the surface tension of water is $70 \\times {10^{ - 3}}\\,N/m$, then the difference of pressure between inside and outside of the spherical drop is:','[{"text": "$14\\\\,\\\\,N/{m^2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$140\\\\,\\\\,N/{m^2}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$35\\\\,\\\\,N/{m^2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "none of these", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Difference of pressure between inside and outside of the spherical drop is given by<br>$$\\Delta P=\\dfrac{2 T}{R}$$<br>Given, $R = 1\\,mm = 1 \\times {10^{ - 3}}\\,m$<br>$T = 70 \\times {10^{ - 3}}\\,N/m$<br>$\\therefore \\Delta P = \\frac{{2 \\times 70 \\times {{10}^{ - 3}}}}{{1 \\times {{10}^{ - 3}}}} = 140\\,N/{m^2}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37021,'physics',27,1380,3313,'A wire of length $L_{0}$ is supplied heat to raise its temperature by $T$. If $\\gamma$ is the coefficient of volume expansion of the wire and $Y$ is young\'s modulus of the wire then the energy density stored in the wire is','[{"text": "$\\\\dfrac{1}{2} \\\\gamma^{2} T^{2} Y$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{1}{3} \\\\gamma^{2} T^{2} Y^{3}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{1}{18} \\\\dfrac{\\\\gamma^{2} T^{2}}{Y}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{1}{18} \\\\gamma^{2} T^{2} Y$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Due to heating the length of the wire increases.<br>$\\therefore \\quad$ Longitudinal strain is produced<br>$\\begin{aligned}\\Rightarrow & \\dfrac{\\Delta L}{L}=\\alpha \\times \\Delta T \\\\E & =\\dfrac{1}{2} \\times \\text { Stress } \\times \\text { Strain }=\\dfrac{1}{2} \\times Y \\times(\\text { Strain })^{2}\\end{aligned}$$\\Rightarrow E=\\dfrac{1}{2} \\times Y \\times\\left(\\dfrac{\\Delta L}{L}\\right)^{2}=\\dfrac{1}{2} \\times Y \\times \\alpha^{2} \\times \\Delta T^{2}$<br>[As $\\gamma=3 \\alpha$ and $\\Delta T=T$ (given)]<br>$\\Rightarrow E=\\dfrac{1}{2} \\times Y \\times\\left(\\dfrac{\\gamma}{3}\\right)^{2} \\times T^{2}=\\dfrac{1}{18} \\gamma^{2} Y T^{2}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37388,'physics',29,1302,3150,'Choose the correct statement. When the temperature of an ideal gas is increased','[{"text": "The kinetic energy of its moelcules increases", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "The potential energy of its molecules increases", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "The potential energy decreases and the kinetic energy increases; the total energy<br>remaining unchanged", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "The potential energy increases and the kinetic<br>decreases; the total energy<br>remaining unchanged", "isCorrect": false}]',0,NULL,NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37022,'physics',26,1138,2730,'The pressures inside two soap bubbles are 1.01 and 1.02 atmosphere respectively. The ratio of their volumes is','[{"text": "$$102: 101$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$(102)^{3}:(101)^{3}$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$8: 1$$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$$2: 1$$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Given that $$P_{1}=0.01 P_{o} ; P_{2}=1.02 P_{o}$$ <br>${P_1} - {P_o} = 0.01{P_o} = \\frac{{4T}}{{{r_1}}}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,(1)$<br>${P_2} - {P_o} = 0.02{P_o} = \\frac{{4T}}{{{r_2}}}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,(2)$<br>From (1) & (2) $$\\dfrac{r_{2}}{r_{1}}=\\dfrac{1}{2}$$<br>The ratio of volumes is $$\\dfrac{V_{1}}{V_{2}}=\\left(\\dfrac{r_{1}}{r_{2}}\\right)^{3}=8$$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37024,'physics',27,1380,3309,'A steel wire of uniform area $2\\,\\,m{m^2}$ is heated upto $50^\\circ C$ and is stretched by tying its ends rigidly. The change in tension when the temperature falls from $50^\\circ C$ to $30^\\circ C$ is (Take $Y = 2 \\times {10^{11}}N{m^{ - 2}},\\alpha  = 1.1 \\times {10^{ - 5^\\circ }}{C^{ - 1}}$ )','[{"text": "$5\\\\,N$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1.5 \\\\times {10^{10}}\\\\,N$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2.5 \\\\times {10^{10}}\\\\,N$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$88\\\\,N$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'As, $F=\\alpha(\\Delta T) A Y$<br>$ = 1.1 \\times {10^{ - 5}} \\times (50 - 30) \\times 2 \\times {10^{ - 6}} \\times 2 \\times {10^{11}}$<br>$ = 88\\,N$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37025,'physics',26,1138,2730,'Two soap bubbles of radii $$R_{1}$$ and $$R_{2}$$ are kept in vacuum at constant temperature, the ratio of masses of air inside them, is','[{"text": "$$\\\\dfrac{R_{1}}{R_{2}}$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$\\\\left(\\\\dfrac{R_{1}}{R_{2}}\\\\right)^{3}$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$\\\\dfrac{R_{2}}{R_{1}}$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$\\\\left(\\\\dfrac{R_{1}}{R_{2}}\\\\right)^{2}$$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'From, $$P V=\\dfrac{m}{M} R T$$ i.e., $$m \\propto P V$$<br>$$\\begin{aligned}& P_{1}=P_{o}+\\dfrac{4 T}{R_{1}}=0+\\dfrac{4 T}{R_{1}} ; \\\\& P_{2}=P_{o}+\\dfrac{4 T}{R_{2}}=0+\\dfrac{4 T}{R_{2}} \\\\& \\dfrac{m_{1}}{m_{2}}=\\dfrac{P_{1} V_{1}}{P_{2} V_{2}}=\\dfrac{\\left(\\dfrac{4 T}{R_{1}}\\right) \\dfrac{4}{3} \\pi R_{1}^{3}}{\\left(\\dfrac{4 T}{R_{2}}\\right) \\dfrac{4}{3} \\pi R_{2}^{3}} \\\\& \\therefore \\dfrac{m_{1}}{m_{2}}=\\dfrac{R_{1}^{2}}{R_{2}^{2}}\\end{aligned}$$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37027,'physics',27,1380,3306,'A wheel is $80.3\\,cm$ in circumference. An iron tyre measures $80.0\\,cm$ around its inner face. If the coefficient of linear expansion for iron is $1.2 \\times {10^{ - 5}}\\,^\\circ {C^{ - 1}}$, the temperature of the tyre must be raised by','[{"text": "$417^\\\\circ C$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$105^\\\\circ C$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$223^\\\\circ C$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$312^\\\\circ C$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Here, $\\Delta l = 80.3 - 80.0 = 0.3\\,cm$<br>$l = 80\\,cm,\\alpha  = 1.2 \\times {10^{ - 6}}\\,^\\circ {C^{ - 1}}$<br>Rise in temperature $\\Delta T=\\dfrac{\\Delta l}{l \\alpha}$<br>$\\Delta T = \\frac{{0.3}}{{80 \\times 1.2 \\times {{10}^{ - 5}}}} = 312.5^\\circ C$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37028,'physics',26,1138,2731,'A liquid rises in a capillary tube if the angle of contact is','[{"text": "Acute", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Obtuse", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$\\\\pi / 2$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$\\\\pi$$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,NULL,NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37030,'physics',27,1380,3306,'A pendulum clock (fitted with a small heavy bob that is connected with a metal rod ) is 5 seconds fast each day at a temperature of  $15^\\circ C$ and 10 seconds slow at a temperature of $30^\\circ C$. The temperature at which it is designed to give correct time, is','[{"text": "$18^\\\\circ C$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$20^\\\\circ C$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$24^\\\\circ C$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$25^\\\\circ C$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Fractional loss of time per second $=\\dfrac{1}{2} \\alpha \\Delta T$<br>Therefore <br>$\\frac{1}{2}\\alpha \\left( {{T_0} - 15} \\right) \\times (24hrs) = 5\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\left( 1 \\right)$<br>and<br>$\\frac{1}{2}\\alpha \\left( {30 - {T_0}} \\right) \\times (24hrs) = 10\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\left( 2 \\right)$<br>from eqn (1) and (2)<br>on solving ${T_0} = 20^\\circ C$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37031,'physics',26,1138,2731,'If capillary experiment is performed in vacuum then the liquid','[{"text": "Will remain same", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Will rise", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Rise to the top", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Will fall", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'When no gravity or insufficient length the liquid level rise upto top. When capillary tube kept in a place no gravity or artificial satellite, or freely falling lift or insufficient length the liquid rises upto top.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37033,'physics',27,1380,3306,'An isosceles triangle is formed with in a rod of length $l_{1}$ and coefficient of linear expansion $\\alpha_{1}$ as the base and two thin rods each of length $l_{2}$ and coefficient of linear expansions $\\alpha_{2}$ as the two sides. If the distance between the apex and the midpoint of the base remain unchanged as the temperature is varied, then','[{"text": "$\\\\dfrac{l_{1}}{l_{2}}=2 \\\\sqrt{\\\\dfrac{\\\\alpha_{2}}{\\\\alpha_{1}}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{l_{1}}{l_{2}}=\\\\sqrt{\\\\dfrac{\\\\alpha_{2}}{\\\\alpha_{1}}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{l_{1}}{l_{2}}=\\\\dfrac{\\\\alpha_{2}}{\\\\alpha_{1}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{l_{1}}{l_{2}}=\\\\dfrac{2 \\\\alpha_{2}}{\\\\alpha_{1}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Let $l_1^1\\,\\& \\,\\,l_2^1$ are new lengths of the rods after increasing temperature $\\Delta T$ as shown in the figure.<br><img src=\'/img/data/physics/27_37033_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' /> <br>As $h$ remains constant<br>$\\sqrt{l_{2}^{2}-\\dfrac{l_{1}^{2}}{4}}=\\sqrt{l_{2}^{l^{2}}-\\dfrac{l_{1}^{l^{2}}}{4}}$<br>square on both sides.<br>$\\begin{aligned}& l_{2}^{2}-\\dfrac{l_{1}^{2}}{4}=l_{2}^{1^{2}}-\\dfrac{l_{1}^{1^{2}}}{4} \\\\& l_{2}^{2}-\\dfrac{l_{1}^{2}}{4}=l_{2}^{2}\\left(1+\\alpha_{2} \\Delta T\\right)^{2}-\\dfrac{l_{1}^{2}\\left(1+\\alpha_{1} \\Delta T\\right)^{2}}{4} \\\\& \\Rightarrow \\dfrac{l_{1}}{l_{2}}=2 \\sqrt{\\dfrac{\\alpha_{2}}{\\alpha_{1}}}\\end{aligned}$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/27_37033_question_1.png'),
(37034,'physics',26,1138,2731,'The quantity on which the rise of liquid in a capillary tube does not depend is','[{"text": "Density of liquid", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Radius of capillary tube", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Angle of contact", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Atmospheric pressure", "isCorrect": true}]',3,NULL,NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37036,'physics',26,1138,2731,'A capillary is dipped in water vessel kept on a freely falling lift, then','[{"text": "Water will not rise in the tube", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Water will rise to the maximum available height of the tube", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Water will rise to the height observed under normal condition", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Water will rise to the height below that observed under normal condition.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'When no gravity or freely falling lift then liquid rises to top. When capillary tube kept in a place no gravity or artificial satellite, or freely falling lift or insufficient length the liquid rises upto top.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37037,'physics',27,1380,3306,'The figure shows a rectangular brass plate at $0^\\circ C$ in which there is a rectangular hole of dimensions indicated. If the temperature of the plate is raised to $150^\\circ C$.','[{"text": "$x$ will increase and $y$ will decrease", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$x$ will decrease and $y$ will increase", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both $x$ and $y$ will increase", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "All the dimensions$x,y$and $z$ increase", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'For isotropic material separation between two points varies as<br>$l=l_{0}(1+\\alpha \\Delta T)$<br>After heating, all the dimensions $x,y\\,\\& \\,z$ increase.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/27_37037_question_1.png'),
(37038,'physics',23,867,1993,'A uniform rod $A B$ of mass 2 $kg$ is hinged at one end $A$. The rod is kept in the horizontal position by a massless string tied to point $B$. Find the reaction of the hinge  on end$A$ of the rod at the instant when string is cut. $\\left(g=10 {~m} / {s}^{2}\\right)$','[{"text": "$1\\\\,N$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$4\\\\,N$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$9\\\\,N$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$5\\\\,N$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'When the string is cut, the weight of the rod constitutes torque about the hinge, so<br>${\\tau _A} = mg\\frac{l}{2}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,(1)$<br>According to Newton\'s second law,<br>${\\tau _A} = I\\alpha \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,(2)$<br>where $\\alpha$ is the angular acceleration of the rod about the end $A$<br> From Eqs. (1) and (2), we get<br>$I \\alpha=m g \\dfrac{l}{2}$<br>or $\\alpha=\\dfrac{m g \\dfrac{l}{2}}{I}$<br>Here $I=m l^{2} / 3$, therefore<br>$\\therefore \\quad \\alpha=\\dfrac{m g l / 2}{m l^{2} / 3}=\\dfrac{3}{2} \\dfrac{g}{l}$<br>Acceleration of the CM of the rod is$a_{{CM}}=\\alpha r=\\dfrac{3}{2} \\dfrac{g}{l} \\times \\dfrac{l}{2}=\\dfrac{3 g}{4}$<br>Again by Newton\'s second law,<br>$\\begin{array}{ll} & m g-R_{A}=m a_{{CM}} \\\\\\text { or } & m g-R_{A}=m \\times \\dfrac{3 g}{4} \\\\\\therefore & R_{A}=\\dfrac{M g}{4}=\\dfrac{2 \\times 10}{4}=5 {~N}\\end{array}$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/23_37038_question_1.png'),
(37039,'physics',26,1138,2731,'If a liquid rises to the same height in two capillaries of the same material at the same temperature, then','[{"text": "The weight of liquid in both capillaries are not equal", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "The radius of meniscus must be equal", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "The capillaries must be cylindrical and vertical", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Given conditions are not possible", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'$h = \\frac{{2T\\cos \\theta }}{{r\\rho g}}$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37040,'physics',27,1380,3310,'A uniform pressure $P$ is exerted by an external agent on all sides of a solid cube at temperature $t^\\circ C$. By what amount should the temperature of the cube be raised in order to bring its volume back to its original volume before the pressure was applied if the bulk modulus is $B$ and co-efficient of volumetric expansion is $\\gamma$ ?','[{"text": "$\\\\dfrac{P \\\\gamma}{B}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{P}{B \\\\gamma}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{B \\\\gamma}{P}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{1}{B P \\\\gamma}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'$d V=-\\dfrac{p V}{B} \\Rightarrow V \\gamma \\Delta T=\\dfrac{p V}{B} \\Rightarrow \\Delta T=\\dfrac{p}{\\gamma B}$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37042,'physics',27,1380,3312,'A glass flask is filled up to a mark with $50\\,cc$ of mercury at $18^\\circ C$. If the flask and contents are heated to $38^\\circ C$, how much mercury will be above the mark ( $\\alpha$ for glass is $9 \\times {10^{ - 6}}/^\\circ C$ and coefficient of real expansion of mercury is $180 \\times {10^{ - 6}}/^\\circ C$ )','[{"text": "$0.46\\\\,cc$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$0.85\\\\,cc$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$0.05\\\\,cc$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$0.153\\\\,cc$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Due to volume expansion of both mercury and flask, the change in volume of mercury relative to flask is given by<br>$\\Delta V = {V_0}\\left[ {{\\gamma _L} - {\\gamma _g}} \\right]\\Delta \\theta  = {V_0}\\left[ {{\\gamma _m} - 3{\\alpha _g}} \\right]\\Delta \\theta $<br>$ = 50\\left[ {180 \\times {{10}^{ - 6}} - 3 \\times 9 \\times {{10}^{ - 6}}} \\right](38 - 18) = 0.153\\,cc$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37043,'physics',26,1138,2731,'The rise of liquid into capillary tube is $$h_{1}$$. If the apparatus is taken in a lift moving up with acceleration, the height is $$h_{2}$$, then','[{"text": "${h_1} = {h_2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$h_{1}>h_{2}$$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$$h_{2}>h_{1}$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$h_{2}=0$$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,NULL,NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37045,'physics',27,1380,3311,'A solid whose volume does not change with temperature floats in a liquid. For two different temperatures $t_{1}$ and $t_{2}$ of the liquid, dfractions $f_{1}$ and $f_{2}$ of the volume of the solid remain submerged in the liquid. The coefficient of volume expansion of the liquid is equal to','[{"text": "$\\\\dfrac{f_{1}-f_{2}}{f_{2} t_{1}-f_{1} t_{2}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{f_{1}-f_{2}}{f_{1} t_{1}-f_{2} t_{2}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{f_{1}+f_{2}}{f_{2} t_{1}+f_{1} t_{2}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{f_{1}+f_{2}}{f_{1} t_{1}+f_{2} t_{2}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'As with the rise in temperature, the liquid undergoes volume expansion therefore the fraction of solid submerged in liquid increases. Initially when the block is floating at temperature $t_{1}$<br>${F_b} = {F_g} \\Rightarrow {V_1}{\\rho _l}g = {V_0}{\\rho _s}g$<br>$ \\Rightarrow \\,\\,\\,{\\mkern 1mu} {\\kern 1pt} {f_1} = \\frac{{{V_1}}}{{{V_0}}} = \\frac{{{\\rho _s}}}{{{\\rho _l}}} = \\frac{{{\\rho _{{s_0}}}\\left( {1 + {\\gamma _l}{t_1}} \\right)}}{{{\\rho _{{l_0}}}}}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\left( 1 \\right)$<br>Here $V_{0}$ is total volume of block which is constant and $\\rho_{s}$ is also constant that is $\\rho_{s}=\\rho_{s_{0}} \\cdot V_{0}$ is the volume of the solid present inside the liquid.<br>Similarly at temperature $t_{2}$<br>${f_2} = \\frac{{{\\rho _{{s_0}}}}}{{{\\rho _{{l_0}}}}}\\left( {1 + {\\gamma _l}{t_2}} \\right)\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\left( 2 \\right)$<br>From Eqs. (1) and (2), $\\dfrac{f_{1}}{f_{2}}=\\dfrac{1+\\gamma t_{1}}{1+\\gamma t_{2}}$<br>$\\Rightarrow \\quad \\gamma=\\dfrac{f_{1}-f_{2}}{f_{2} t_{1}-f_{1} t_{2}}$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37046,'physics',26,1138,2731,'In a surface tension experiment with a capillary tube water rises upto $0.1\\,\\,m$. If the same experiment is repeated in an artificial satellite, which is revolving around the earth; water will rise in the capillary tube upto a height of :','[{"text": "$0.1\\\\,\\\\,m$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$0.2\\\\,\\\\,m$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$0.98\\\\,\\\\,m$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Full length of tube", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Because of large force of cohesion or When capillary tube kept in a place no gravity or artificial satellite, or freely falling lift or insufficient length of  the liquid rises upto top.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37048,'physics',27,1380,3312,'The coefficient of apparent expansion of a liquid when determined using two different vessels $A$ and $B$ are $\\gamma_{1}$ and $\\gamma_{2}$ respectively. If the coefficient of linear expansion of the vessel $A$ is $\\alpha$, the coefficient of linear expansion of the vessel $B$ is','[{"text": "$\\\\dfrac{\\\\gamma_{1}-\\\\gamma_{2}}{2 \\\\alpha}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{\\\\alpha \\\\gamma_{1} \\\\gamma_{2}}{\\\\gamma_{1}+\\\\gamma_{2}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{\\\\gamma_{1}-\\\\gamma_{2}}{3}+\\\\alpha$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{\\\\gamma_{1}-\\\\gamma_{2}+\\\\alpha}{3}$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'The relation between apparent and real expansion of a liquid is<br>$\\gamma_{\\text {real }}=\\gamma_{\\text {app }}+\\gamma_{\\text {vessel }} ; \\gamma_{\\text {vessel }}=3 \\alpha$<br>For vessel \'$A$\' $\\Rightarrow \\gamma_{\\text {real }}=\\gamma_{1}+3 \\alpha_{A}$<br>For vessel \'$B$\' $\\Rightarrow \\gamma_{\\text {real }}=\\gamma_{2}+3 \\alpha_{B}$<br>Hence, $\\gamma_{1}+3 \\alpha_{A}=\\gamma_{2}+3 \\alpha_{B} \\Rightarrow \\alpha_{B}=\\dfrac{\\gamma_{1}-\\gamma_{2}}{3}+\\alpha_{A}$<br>$\\alpha_{B}=\\dfrac{\\gamma_{1}-\\gamma_{2}}{3}+\\alpha$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37049,'physics',26,1138,2731,'A \\[20\\;\\,cm\\] long capillary tube is dipped in water. The water rises upto \\[8\\;cm\\]. If the entire arrangement is put in a freely falling elevator, the length of water column in the capillary tube will be<div class="matching-question"></div>','[{"text": "$20\\\\,\\\\,cm$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$4\\\\,\\\\,cm$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$10\\\\,\\\\,cm$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$8\\\\,\\\\,cm$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'In the condition of weightlessness, water rises to the whole of the available length.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37050,'physics',23,871,2004,'A cube of mass $m$ and edge length $l=1 {~m}$ is sliding without friction on a smooth horizontal floor with velocity $v_{0}=8 {~m} / {s}$ it strikes a long obstruction $P P$ of small height The obstruction is parallel to the leading bottom edge of the cube. The leading bottom edge of the cube gets pivoted with the obstruction and the cube starts rotating about the edge. Determine angular velocity of the cube immediately after the impact.','[{"text": "$2\\\\,rad/s$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$4\\\\,rad/s$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$8\\\\,rad/s$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$10\\\\,rad/s$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Before the impact, there is no external force in the horizontal direction and the cube slides with uniform velocity $v_{0}$, and during the impact reaction forces of the obstruction stops its leading bottom edge and cause it to rotate about<br> its leading bottom.Let the velocity of the mass center and angular velocity of the cube immediately after the impact are $v_{C_{0}}$ and $\\omega_{0}$.<br> These velocities are shown in the adjacent figure.  <img src=\'/img/data/physics/23_37050_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />  We denote the angular momentum of the cube about axis coincident with the obstruction edge before and after the impact by  $L_{P_{1}}$ and $L_{P_{2}}$.<br>Applying principle of conservation of angular momentum about an axis coincident with the obstruction, we have<br>${\\vec L_{{P_1}}} = {\\vec L_{{P_2}}} \\Rightarrow {\\vec r_{C/P}} \\times m{\\vec v_o} = {I_P}\\vec \\omega $<br>Using theorem of parallel axes for moment of inertia $I_{P}$  about the leading bottom edge, we get  ${I_P} = {I_C} + mr_{C/P}^2 = \\frac{2}{3}m{l^2}.$<br>Substituting this in the above equation, we have Angular velocity immediately after the impact<br>${\\omega _o} = \\frac{{3{v_o}}}{{4l}} = \\frac{{3 \\times 8}}{{4 \\times 1}} = 4\\,\\,rad/s$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/23_37050_question_1.png'),
(37051,'physics',27,1381,3314,'Equal masses of three liquids $A,B$ and $C$ have temperatures $10^\\circ C,25^\\circ C$ and $40^\\circ C$ respectively. If $A$ and $B$ are mixed, the mixture has a temperature of $15^\\circ C$. If $B$ and $C$ are mixed, the mixture has a temperature of $30^\\circ C$. If $A$ and $C$ are mixed, the mixture will have a temperature of','[{"text": "$29^\\\\circ C$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$16^\\\\circ C$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$20^\\\\circ C$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$25^\\\\circ C$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'When $A, B$ are mixed:<br>$\\begin{aligned}& m s_{1}(15-10)=m(25-15) s_{2} \\\\& 5 s_{1}=5 s_{2} \\Rightarrow s_{1}=s_{2}\\end{aligned}$<br>When $A\\,\\& \\,B$ are mixed:<br>$\\begin{aligned}& m s_{2}(30-25)=m s_{3}(40-30) \\\\& 5 s_{2}=10 s_{3} \\\\& s_{2}=2 s_{3}=s_{1}\\end{aligned}$<br>When A & C are mixed:<br>$m{s_1}(T - 10) = m{s_3}(40 - T)$<br>$2{s_3}(T - 10) = {s_3}(40 - T)$<br>$2T - 20 = 40 - T \\Rightarrow T = 20^\\circ C$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37052,'physics',26,1138,2731,'Find the pressure at point $$A$$ just below the meniscus is... $T$ is surface tension and $r$ is radius of the cavity. Assume that contact angle is $$180^{\\circ}$$','[{"text": "$$P_{\\\\text {atm }}$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$P_{a t m}+\\\\dfrac{2 T}{r}$$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$$P_{a t m}-\\\\dfrac{2 T}{r}$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$P_{a t m}+\\\\dfrac{4 T}{r}$$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'The pressure difference across the surface is $$P_{A}-P_{a t m}=\\dfrac{2 T}{R}$$<br>$$\\mathrm{R}$$ - radius of the meniscus<br>$$\\begin{aligned}& R=r\\left(\\text { For } \\theta=0^{\\circ}\\right) \\\\& P_{A}=P_{a t m}+\\dfrac{2 T}{r}\\end{aligned}$$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/26_37052_question_1.png'),
(37054,'physics',26,1138,2731,'Find the pressure at $A$ just below the meniscus.','[{"text": "$$P_{a t m}+\\\\rho g h$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$P_{a t m}-\\\\rho g h$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$P_{a t m}+\\\\rho g h+\\\\dfrac{2 T}{r}$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$P_{a t m}+\\\\dfrac{2 T}{r}$$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'The pressure difference across the meniscus is<br>${P_A} - {P_{atm}} = \\frac{{2T}}{r}$<br>${P_A} = {P_{atm}} + \\frac{{2T}}{r}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/26_37054_question_1.png'),
(37055,'physics',27,1381,3314,'Water of volume 2 litre in a container is heated with a coil of $1\\,kW$ at $27^\\circ C$. The lid of the container is open and energy dissipates at rate of $160\\,J/s$. In how much time temperature will rise from $27^\\circ C$ to $77^\\circ C$ [Given specific heat of water is $4.2\\,kJ/kg$ ]','[{"text": "$6\\\\min 2\\\\,s$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$8\\\\min 20\\\\,s$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$14\\\\,\\\\min $", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$7\\\\,\\\\min $", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Heat gained by the water $=($ Heat supplied by the coil) - (Heat dissipapted to environment)<br>$\\begin{array}{*{20}{l}}\n  { \\Rightarrow \\,\\,\\,{\\mkern 1mu} {\\kern 1pt} mc\\Delta \\theta  = {P_{Coil{\\text{ }}}}t - {P_{Loss{\\text{ }}}}t} \\\\ \n  { \\Rightarrow \\,\\,\\,{\\mkern 1mu} {\\kern 1pt} 2 \\times 4.2 \\times {{10}^3} \\times (77 - 27) = 1000t - 160t} \\\\ \n  { \\Rightarrow \\,\\,\\,{\\mkern 1mu} {\\kern 1pt} t = \\frac{{4.2 \\times {{10}^5}}}{{840}} = 500\\,s = 8\\,\\min 20\\,s} \n\\end{array}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37066,'physics',26,1133,2708,'The vessel shown in figure has two sections of area of cross section $A_{1}$ and $A_{2}$. A liquid of density $\\rho$ fills both the sections, up to height $h$ in each neglecting atmospheric pressure.','[{"text": "The weight of the liquid in vessel in equal to $2\\\\,h\\\\,\\\\rho g{A_2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "The pressure at the base of the vessel is $2 h \\\\rho g$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "The walls of the vessel at the level $X$ exert a force $2 h \\\\rho g\\\\left(A_{2}-A_{1}\\\\right)$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "The force exerted by the liquid on the base of vessel is $2 h g A_{1}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Weight of liquid $=A_{1} h \\rho g+A_{2} h \\rho g$<br>Walls of the vessel at level $x$ exert a force<br>$=\\rho g h\\left(A_{2}-A_{1}\\right) .$<br>The pressure at the base of the vessel is<br>$P = \\rho g{h_1} + \\rho g{h_2} = 2\\rho gh({h_1} = {h_2}).$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/26_37066_question_1.png'),
(37057,'physics',27,1381,3314,'A $10\\,kW$ drilling machine is used to drill a bore in a small aluminium block of mass $8.0\\,kg$. How much is the rise in temperature of the block in 2.5 minutes, assuming $50 \\%$ of power is used up in heating the machine itself or lost to the surroundings? Specific heat of aluminium$ = 0.91\\,J/g^\\circ C$.','[{"text": "$123^\\\\circ C$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$83^\\\\circ C$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$103^\\\\circ C$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$150^\\\\circ C$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'We are given that power of the machine,<br>$P = 10\\,kW = {10^4}\\,W$<br>Time for which the machine is used,<br>$t = 2.5\\,\\min  = 150\\,s$<br>Mass of the aluminium block,<br>$m = 8\\,kg = 8 \\times {10^3}\\,g$<br>Specific heat of aluminium, $c = 0.91\\,J/g^\\circ C$<br>Since $50 \\%$ of the energy is used up in heating<br>Energy absorbed by the block, $Q = 0.5Pt$<br>$ = 0.5 \\times {10^4} \\times 150\\,J = 7.5 \\times {10^5}\\,J$<br>Let $\\Delta T$ be the rise in temperature of the block.<br>As $Q=m c \\Delta T$<br>$\\Delta T = \\frac{Q}{{mc}} = \\frac{{7.5 \\times {{10}^5}\\left( J \\right)}}{{8 \\times {{10}^3}\\left( g \\right) \\times 0.91\\left( {J/g^\\circ C} \\right)}} = 103^\\circ C$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37058,'physics',26,1137,2722,'Match the Column I with Column II<div class="matching-question"><div class="column-left">A Terminal velocity<br>B. Objects of high density can also float<br>c. A stone present inside water beaker under free fall<br>D. Viscous drag</div><div class="column-right">P. Average densiy becomes less than that of liquid<br>Q. Upthrust is zero<br>R. Varies with velocity<br>S. Upthrust and viscous force</div></div>','[{"text": "A - S,B - Q,C - R,D - P", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "A - P,$$\\\\mathrm{B}-\\\\mathrm{Q}$$,$$\\\\mathrm{C}-\\\\mathrm{R}$$,D - S", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "A - S,$$\\\\mathrm{B}-\\\\mathrm{P}$$,C - Q,D - R", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "A - R,B - S,C - Q,D - P", "isCorrect": false}]',2,NULL,NULL,'Medium','Match the following',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37060,'physics',26,1136,2717,'Match the Column I with Column II<div class="matching-question"><div class="column-left">A.  Floating body<br>B. Hydraulic lift<br>C. Energy conservation<br>D. Speed of efflux</div><div class="column-right">P. Torricelli\'s law<br>Q. Bernoulli\'s principle<br>R. Archimedes principle<br>S. Pascal\'s law</div></div>','[{"text": "A - S, B - Q,  C - R, D - P", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "A - R, B - S,  C - Q, D - P", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "A - Q, B - P,  C - S, D - R", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "A - R, B - P,  C - Q, D - S", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Option (2) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Match the following',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37061,'physics',27,1381,3315,'An iron rocket fragment initially at $ - 100^\\circ C$ enters the earth\'s atmosphere almost horizontally and quickly fuses completely in atmospheric friction. Specific heat of iron is$0.11\\,kcal/kg^\\circ C$ its melting point is $1535^\\circ C$ and the latent heat of fusion is $3\\,kcal/kg$. The minimum velocity with which the fragment must have entered the atmosphere is','[{"text": "$0.45\\\\,km/s$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1.32\\\\,km/s$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$2.32\\\\,km/s$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{Zero}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'The entire kinetic energy of the fragment is changed to heat. Expressing mass $m$ in kilograms everywhere, we have<br>$\\frac{1}{2}m{v^2} = \\left[ {m(30) + m(0.11)\\left( {1535^\\circ  + 100^\\circ } \\right)} \\right]4184$<br>${v^2} = (8368)[30 + 180] \\simeq 1.76 \\times {10^6}$<br>$ \\Rightarrow v = (\\sqrt {1.76} ) \\times {10^3} = 1.32\\,km/s$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37062,'physics',23,870,1997,'A uniform cylinder (mass $M$ ) of radius $R$ is kept on an accelerating platform (mass $M$ ) as shown in figure. If the cylinder rolls without slipping on the platform, determine the magnitude of acceleration of the centre of mass of cylinder. Assuming the coefficient of friction $\\mu=0.4$. determine  the maximum acceleration of the platform   may have without slip between the cylinder and the platform. (Take $g=10 {~m} / {s}^{2}$ )','[{"text": "$12\\\\,m/{s^2}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$19\\\\,m/{s^2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$15\\\\,m/{s^2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$10\\\\,m/{s^2}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'As there is no sliding between the cylinder and plank, the friction will be static in nature. For finding the direction of friction assume, there is no friction anywhere, it suggest sliding tendency of the cylinder w.r.t plank will be in backward direction. It means friction force on the cylinder will act in forward direction. The friction acting on the cylinder will be responsible for angular acceleration of the cylinder.  <img src=\'/img/data/physics/23_37062_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />  Let angular acceleration of the cylinder is $\\alpha$ and the acceleration be $a_{1}$.<br>From F.B.D of cylinder<br> Force equation of cylinder; $f = M{a_1}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,(1)$<br>As axes of rotation is moving so we can apply torque equation about centripetal axis<br>$\\begin{align*}& \\tau_{0}=I_{0} \\alpha \\Rightarrow f \\cdot R=\\dfrac{M R^{2}}{2} \\alpha \\\\& \\Rightarrow \\quad R \\alpha=\\dfrac{2 f}{M} \\tag{2}\\end{align*}$<br>As the cylinder is rolling, it means point of contact of cylinder with plank will not slide.<br>It means acceleration of the plank $=$ acceleration of point $P$<br>$\\begin{equation*}a=a_{0}+\\alpha R \\tag{3}\\end{equation*}$<br>From (1),(2) and (3) we get<br>$f = \\frac{{Ma}}{3}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,(4)$<br>As there is no sliding, it means<br>$\\frac{{Ma}}{3} \\le \\mu Mg \\Rightarrow a = 3\\mu g$<br>$ = 3 \\times 0.4 \\times 10 = 12\\,m/{s^2}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/23_37062_question_1.png'),
(37063,'physics',26,1133,2708,'A smooth gate is kept in equilibrium by applying a horizontal force. What is the value of $y$ so that no horizontal reaction force acts at the pivot?','[{"text": "$$\\\\dfrac{H}{6}$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$\\\\dfrac{H}{3}$$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$$\\\\dfrac{2 H}{3}$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Zero", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'The torque and force due to liquid force and torque and force due to $F$ must be balanced then $N = 0$<br><img src=\'/img/data/physics/26_37063_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' /> <br>$$\\tau_{\\text {due to liquid }}=\\int_{0}^{H} y \\rho g \\cdot d y b(H-y)=F . h$$<br>where $$b$$ is the breadth of the plate<br>$\\rho gb\\left( {\\frac{{H{y^2}}}{2} - \\frac{{{y^3}}}{3}} \\right)_0^H = \\frac{{\\rho gb{H^3}}}{6} = Fh\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,(1)$<br>$\\int d F = F \\Rightarrow \\int_0^H y \\rho g\\,dyb = F{\\text{ }}$<br>$\\frac{{{H^2}}}{2}\\rho gb = F\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\left( 2 \\right)$<br>From (1) & (2)<br>$$\\dfrac{2 F}{H^{2}} \\cdot \\dfrac{H^{3}}{6}=F h \\Rightarrow h=\\dfrac{H}{3}$$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/26_37063_question_1.png'),
(37064,'physics',27,1381,3314,'Three liquids with masses $m_{1}, m_{2}, m_{3}$ are thoroughly mixed. If their specific heats are $c_{1}, c_{2}, c_{3}$ and their temperatures $T_{1}, T_{2}, T_{3}$ respectively, then the temperature of the mixture is','[{"text": "$\\\\dfrac{m_{1} c_{1} T_{1}+m_{2} c_{2} T_{2}+m_{3} c_{3} T_{3}}{m_{1} c_{1}+m_{2} c_{2}+m_{3} c_{3}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{c_{1} T_{1}+c_{2} T_{2}+c_{3} T_{3}}{m_{1} c_{1}+m_{2} c_{2}+m_{3} c_{3}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{m_{1} T_{1}+m_{2} T_{2}+m_{3} T_{3}}{c_{1} T_{1}+c_{2} T_{2}+c_{3} T_{3}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{m_{1} c_{1} T_{1}+m_{2} c_{2} T_{2}+m_{3} c_{3} T_{3}}{m_{1} T_{1}+m_{2} T_{2}+m_{3} T_{3}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Let the final temperature be $T^{\\circ} C$.<br>The net heat change of the system is zero.<br>$\\begin{gathered}\\Delta q_{1}+\\Delta q_{2}+\\Delta q_{3}=0 \\\\m_{1} c_{1}\\left(T-T_{1}\\right)+m_{2} c_{2}\\left(T-T_{2}\\right)+m_{3} c_{3}\\left(T-T_{3}\\right)=0 \\\\\\Rightarrow T=\\dfrac{m_{1} c_{1} T_{1}+m_{2} c_{2} T_{2}+m_{3} c_{3} T_{3}}{m_{1} c_{1}+m_{2} c_{2}+m_{3} c_{3}}\\end{gathered}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37067,'physics',27,1381,3314,'In similar calorimeters, equal volumes of water and alcohol, when poured, take $100\\,\\sec $ and 74 sec respectively, to cool from $50^{\\circ} C$ to $40^{\\circ} C$. If the thermal capacity of each calorimeter is numerically equal to volume of either liquid, then calculate the specific heat capacity of alcohol (given: the relative density of alcohol as 0.8 and specific heat capacity of water as<br>1 cal g $\\left.{ }^{-10} C^{-1}\\right)$.','[{"text": "$0.8\\\\,cal/gm\\\\,^\\\\circ C$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$0.6\\\\,cal/gm\\\\,^\\\\circ C$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$0.9\\\\,cal/gm\\\\,^\\\\circ C$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1\\\\,cal/gm\\\\,^\\\\circ C$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Let $V$ be volume of either liquid<br>Mass of water $ = V \\times 1gm$<br>Mass of alcohol $ = V \\times 0.8 = 0.8\\,Vgm$<br>Rate of cooling of the water calorimeter<br>$ = \\frac{1}{{100}}\\left[ {\\,V \\times \\left( {50^\\circ  - 40^\\circ } \\right) + V \\times \\left( {50^\\circ  - 40^\\circ } \\right)} \\right]$<br>$ = (1/5)V{\\mkern 1mu} cal/\\sec $<br>Rate of cooling of alcohol calorimeter $\\left. { = \\frac{1}{{74}}\\left[ {\\,V \\times \\left[ {50^\\circ  - 40^\\circ } \\right] + 0.8\\,V \\times c \\times \\left( {50^\\circ  - 40^\\circ } \\right)} \\right]} \\right]$<br>$ = (1/74)(10\\,V + 8Vc)\\,{\\mkern 1mu} cal/\\sec $<br>It is given that calorimetrers are similar to as rate of cooling of both is same<br>$\\dfrac{1}{5} V=(1 / 74) 10 V+8 V c$<br>$c = 0.6cal/gm\\,^\\circ C$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37068,'physics',23,870,2001,'A solid sphere of mass $m$ and radius $r$ is released from rest from the given position. The sphere rolls without sliding on the circular track of radius $R=8 r$. If $r=1 {~m}$ then find its speed  when it reaches its lowest position. (Take $g=10 {~m} / {s}^{2}$ )','[{"text": "$5\\\\,m/s$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$10\\\\,m/s$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$8\\\\,m/s$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2\\\\,m/s$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'As the sphere is rolling, the static friction does not perform any work. Hence mechanical energy is conserved<br>According to conservation of energy, $\\Delta K+\\Delta U=0$<br>or $ \\Rightarrow \\left( {{K_f} - {K_i}} \\right) + \\Delta U = 0\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,(1)$<br>When the sphere reaches its lowest position it loses gravitational potential energy by $\\Delta U$ and gains a kinetic energy by $\\Delta K$, where$\\Delta U =  - mg(R - r)$  <img src=\'/img/data/physics/23_37068_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />  The sphere is released from rest, its initial kinetic energy $K_{i}=0$<br> And kinetic energy when the sphere reaches at its lowest position<br> ${K_f} = (1 + c)\\frac{1}{2}m{v^2}$<br>For sphere,<br>$c=\\dfrac{k^{2}}{R^{2}}=\\dfrac{\\dfrac{2}{5} R^{2}}{R^{2}}=\\dfrac{2}{5}$<br>$K_{f}=\\dfrac{1}{2} m v^{2}\\left(1+\\dfrac{2}{5}\\right)=\\dfrac{7}{10} m v^{2}$<br>Hence from (1), $\\left(\\dfrac{7}{10} m v^{2}-0\\right)+(-m g(R-r))=0$<br>We get,<br> $v = \\sqrt {\\frac{{10}}{7}g(R - r)} $<br>$ = \\sqrt {\\frac{{10}}{7} \\times 10 \\times 7 \\times 1}  = 10\\,m/s$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/23_37068_question_1.png'),
(37069,'physics',26,1133,2709,'A tube of lenght $$L$$ is filled completely with an incompressible liquid of mass $$M$$ and closed at both the ends. The tube is then rotated in a horizontal plane about one of its end with a uniform angular velocity $$\\omega$$. The force exerted by the liquid at the other end is -','[{"text": "$$M \\\\omega^{2} L$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$\\\\dfrac{M \\\\omega^{2} L}{2}$$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$$\\\\dfrac{M \\\\omega^{2} L^{2}}{2}$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$\\\\dfrac{M \\\\omega^{2} L}{4}$$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'The total centrifugal force on the liquid is balanced by the normal force exterted by the right end of the tube.<br>$$F_{c}=M r_{C M} \\omega^{2}=\\dfrac{M L \\omega^{2}}{2}$$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37070,'physics',27,1381,3315,'A piece of ice falls from a height $h$ so that it melts completely. Only half of the heat produced is absorbed by the ice. The value of $h$ is (Latent heat of ice is $3.4 \\times {10^5}\\,J/kg$ and $g = 10\\,N/kg)$','[{"text": "$544\\\\,km$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$34\\\\,km$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$74\\\\,km$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$68\\\\,km$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'<br>$\\frac{{mgh}}{2} = mL \\Rightarrow h = \\frac{{2L}}{g} = \\frac{{2 \\times 3.4 \\times {{10}^5}}}{{10}} = 68\\,km.$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37072,'physics',26,1133,2708,'The density $$\\rho$$ of water of bulk modulus $$B$$ at a depth $$y$$ in the ocean is related to the density at surface $$\\rho_{0}$$ by the relation','[{"text": "$$\\\\rho=\\\\rho_{0}\\\\left[1-\\\\dfrac{\\\\rho_{0} g y}{B}\\\\right]$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$\\\\rho=\\\\rho_{0}\\\\left[1-\\\\dfrac{B}{\\\\rho_{0} g y}\\\\right]$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$\\\\rho=\\\\rho_{0}\\\\left[1+\\\\dfrac{B}{\\\\rho_{0} h g y}\\\\right]$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$\\\\rho=\\\\rho_{0}\\\\left[1+\\\\dfrac{\\\\rho_{0} g y}{B}\\\\right]$$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Bulk modulus,<br>$$\\begin{gathered}B=-V_{0} \\dfrac{\\Delta p}{\\Delta V} \\Rightarrow \\Delta V=-V_{0} \\dfrac{\\Delta p}{B} \\\\\\Rightarrow V=V_{0}\\left[1-\\dfrac{\\Delta p}{B}\\right]\\end{gathered}$$<br>$$\\therefore$$ Density, $$\\rho=\\rho_{0}\\left[1-\\dfrac{\\Delta p}{B}\\right]^{-1}=\\rho_{0}\\left[1+\\dfrac{\\Delta p}{B}\\right]$$<br>where, $$\\Delta p=p-p_{0}=h \\rho_{0} g=$$ pressure difference between depth and surface of ocean<br>$$\\therefore \\quad \\rho=\\rho_{0}\\left[1+\\dfrac{\\rho_{0} g y}{B}\\right]$$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37073,'physics',27,1381,3315,'$2\\,kg$ of ice at $ - 20^\\circ C$ is mixed with $5\\,kg$ of water at $20^\\circ C$ in an insulating vessel having negligible heat capacity. Calculate the final mass of water remaining in the container. It is given that the specific heats of water and ice are 1<br>$kcal/kg\\,per\\,^\\circ C$ and $0.5kcal/kg/\\,^\\circ C$ while the latent heat of fusion of ice is $80\\,kcal/kg$','[{"text": "$6\\\\,kg$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$7\\\\,kg$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2\\\\,kg$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$4\\\\,kg$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Initially ice will absorb heat to raise it\'s temperature to $0^\\circ C$ then it\'s melting takes place. If $m_{i}=$ Initial mass of ice, $m_{i}^{\\prime}=$ Mass of ice that melts and $m_{w}=$ Initial mass of water<br>By law of mixture, Heat gained by ice $=$ Heat lost by water<br>$\\Rightarrow m_{i} \\times c \\times(20)+m_{i}^{\\prime} \\times L=m_{w} c_{w}[20]$<br>$ \\Rightarrow 2 \\times 0.5(20) + m_i^\\prime  \\times 80 = 5 \\times 1 \\times 20 \\Rightarrow m_i^\\prime  = 1\\,kg$<br>So final mass of water $=$ Initial mass of water + Mass of ice that melts $ = 5 + 1 = 6\\,kg$.',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37099,'physics',28,1371,3281,'Among the following processes which one is irreversible process?','[{"text": "Free expansion of gas", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Extension or compression of spring very slowly", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Motion of an object on a perfectly frictionless surface", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "All of them", "isCorrect": false}]',0,NULL,NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(42456,'physics',36,908,2097,'A coil of $N = 100$ turns carries a current $I = 5\\,A$ and creates a magnetic flux $\\phi  = {10^{ - 5}}T{m^{ - 2}}$per turn. The value of its inductance $L$ will be','[{"text": "$0.15\\\\,mH$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$0.05\\\\,mH$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$0.10\\\\,mH$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$0.20\\\\,mH$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'$N\\phi  = Li \\Rightarrow 100 \\times {10^{ - 5}} = L \\times 5 \\Rightarrow L = 0.2mH$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37075,'physics',26,1133,2705,'There is a circular tube in a vertical plane. Two liquids which do not mix and of densities $$d_{1}$$ and $$d_{2}$$ are filled in the tube. Each liquid subtends $$90^{\\circ}$$ angle at centre. Radius joining their interface makes an angle $$\\alpha$$ with vertical. Ratio $$d_{1} / d_{2}$$ is','[{"text": "$$\\\\dfrac{1+\\\\sin \\\\alpha}{1-\\\\sin \\\\alpha}$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$\\\\dfrac{1+\\\\sin \\\\alpha}{1-\\\\cos \\\\alpha}$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$\\\\dfrac{1+\\\\cos \\\\alpha}{1-\\\\cos \\\\alpha}$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$\\\\dfrac{1+\\\\tan \\\\alpha}{1-\\\\tan \\\\alpha}$$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'<br><img src=\'/img/data/physics/26_37075_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' /> <br>Let $$R$$ be the radius of the tube<br>$$\\begin{aligned}& E F=R \\sin \\alpha ; C I=R \\cos \\alpha ; B G=R-R \\cos \\alpha \\\\& B H=R-R \\sin \\alpha\\end{aligned}$$<br>Pressure at point $$B$$ must be same on both sides<br>$$\\begin{gathered}d_{1} g(B H)=d_{1} g(B G)+d_{2} g(C I)+d_{2} g(E F) \\\\d_{1} R(1-\\sin \\alpha)=d_{1} R(1-\\cos \\alpha)+d_{2}(R \\cos \\alpha) \\\\+d_{2}(R \\sin \\alpha) \\\\\\dfrac{d_{1}}{d_{2}}=\\dfrac{\\sin \\alpha+\\cos \\alpha}{\\cos \\alpha-\\sin \\alpha}=\\dfrac{1+\\tan \\alpha}{1-\\tan \\alpha}\\end{gathered}$$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/26_37075_question_1.png'),
(37076,'physics',27,1381,3315,'$10\\,g$ of ice at $ - 20^\\circ C$ is dropped into a calorimeter containing $10\\,g$ of water at $10^\\circ C$; the specific heat of water is twice that the ice. When equilibrium is reached, the calorimeter will contain','[{"text": "$10\\\\,g$ ice and $10\\\\,g$ of water", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$20\\\\,g$ of water", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$20\\\\,g$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$5\\\\,g$ of ice and $15\\\\,g$ of water", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Heat lost by water is $Q_{1}$<br>${Q_1} = 10 \\times 1 \\times 10 = 100\\,cal$<br>Heat gained by ice is $Q_{2}$<br>${Q_2} = 10 \\times 0.50[0 - ( - 20)] + 10 \\times 80$<br>$ = (100 + 800)\\,cal = 900\\,cal$<br>As $Q_{1} < Q_{2}$, so ice will not completely melt and final temperature $ = 0^\\circ C$.<br>As heat given by water in cooling up to $ = 0^\\circ C$ is only just sufficient to increase the temperature of the ice from $ - 20^\\circ C$ to $0^\\circ C$, hence mixture in equilibrium will consist of $10\\,g$ of ice and $10\\,g$ of water, both at $0^\\circ C$.',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37077,'physics',23,872,2007,'$A B$ is a horizontal diameter of a ball of mass $m=0.4 {~kg}$ and radius $R=0.10 {~m}$. At time $t=0$, a sharp impulse is applied at $B$ at angle of $45^{\\circ}$ with the horizontal, as shown in figure so that the ball immediately starts to move with velocity ${V_0} = 10\\,m{s^{ - 1}}$. Calculate the angular velocity of ball   just after impulse provided.','[{"text": "$250\\\\,rad/s$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$100\\\\,rad/s$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$330\\\\,rad/s$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$500\\\\,rad/s$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Since, line of action of impulse does not pass through centre of mass of the sphere, therefore, just after application of impulse. the sphere starts to move, both translationally and rotationally. Translation motion is produced by horizontal component of the impulse, while rotational motion is produced by moment of the impulse. Let the impulse applied be $J$.<img src=\'/img/data/physics/23_37077_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' /> Then its horizontal component provides horizontal motion.<br>$J \\cdot \\cos 45^\\circ $  where gives $J = 4\\sqrt 2 \\,kg\\,m{s^{ - 1}}$ <br>Moment of inertial of ball about centroidal axis is<br> $l=\\dfrac{2}{4} m R^{2}=1.6 \\times 10^{-3} {~kg} {~m}^{2}$<br>Initial angular momentum of ball (about centre), $ = J\\left. {\\left( {R\\sin 45^\\circ } \\right.} \\right)$<br> Angular impulse will change angular momentum of the ball <br> $J \\cdot R \\cdot \\sin 45^\\circ  = \\Delta L = \\left( {I{\\omega _0} - 0} \\right)$<br>which gives $\\omega_{0}=250\\,\\,{rad} {s}^{-1}$ (clockwise)',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/23_37077_question_1.png'),
(37078,'physics',26,1133,2705,'By sucking through a straw a student can reduce the pressure in his lungs to $750\\,\\,mm$ of $Hg$ (density $\\left. { = 13.6\\,gm/c{m^3}} \\right)$. Using the straw, he can drink water from a glass upto a maximum depth of:','[{"text": "$75\\\\,\\\\,cm$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$10\\\\,\\\\,cm$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1.36\\\\,\\\\,cm$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$13.6\\\\,\\\\,cm$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Pressure difference created $ = 10\\,mm$ of $Hg$. This must be equal to the pressure of water column being created in the straw. If height of water column be $$h$$.<br>$h\\rho g = 1\\,cm \\times 13.6 \\times g$<br>$h \\times 1 = 13.6 \\Rightarrow h = 13.6\\,cm.$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37079,'physics',27,1381,3315,'Assume that a drop of liquid evaporates by decrease in its surface energy, so that its temperature remains unchanged. What should be the minimum radius of the drop for this to be possible? The surface tension is $T$, density of liquid is $\\rho$ and $L$ is its latent heat of vaporisation.','[{"text": "$\\\\rho L / T$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\sqrt{T / \\\\rho L}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$T / \\\\rho L$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2 T / \\\\rho L$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Assume that radius is decreased by $\\Delta R$<br>$\\Delta m L=\\Delta A T$<br>where $\\Delta m$ is the decrease in mass of the drop. $\\Delta A$ is decrease in surface area. $T$ is the coefficient of surface tension.<br>$4\\pi {R^2}\\Delta R\\rho L = 4\\pi T\\left[ {{R^2} - {{(R - \\Delta R)}^2}} \\right]$<br>$ \\Rightarrow \\rho {R^2}\\Delta RL = T\\left[ {{R^2} - {R^2} + 2R\\Delta R - \\Delta {R^2}} \\right]$<br>$ \\Rightarrow \\rho {R^2}\\Delta RL = T2R\\Delta R[\\Delta {\\text{ R}}\\,{\\text{is very}}\\,{\\text{small }}]$<br>$ \\Rightarrow R = \\frac{{2T}}{{\\rho L}}$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37081,'physics',26,1133,2709,'The cubical container $ABCDEFGH$ which is completely filled with an ideal (nonviscous and incompressible) fluid, moves in a gravity free space with acceleration of $$a=a_{0}(\\hat{i}+\\hat{j}+\\hat{k})$$ where $$a_{0}$$ is a positive constant. then the only point in the container shown in the figure where pressure is maximum, is','[{"text": "$C$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$B$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$F$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$E$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'As $$a=a_{0}(\\hat{i}+\\hat{j}+\\hat{k})$$ the pseudo force components act along $ - x, - y\\,\\& \\, - z.$ directions. So pressure increases along<br>$$D \\rightarrow A, D \\rightarrow C \\& D \\rightarrow H$$ so maximum pressure is possible at point $$F$$.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/26_37081_question_1.png'),
(37101,'physics',26,1133,2710,'A jar is filled with two non-mixing liquids 1 and 2 having densities $\\rho_{1}$ and $\\rho_{2}$, respectively. A solid ball made of material of density $\\rho_{3}$, is dropped in the jar. It comes to equilibrium in the position shown in the figure. Which of the following is true for $\\rho_{1}, \\rho_{2}$ and $\\rho_{3}$ ?','[{"text": "$\\\\rho_{3} &lt; \\\\rho_{1} &lt; \\\\rho_{2}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\rho_{1}&gt;\\\\rho_{3}&gt;\\\\rho_{2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\rho_{1} &lt; \\\\rho_{2} &lt; \\\\rho_{3}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\rho_{1} &lt; \\\\rho_{3} &lt; \\\\rho_{2}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'As liquid 2 is present below the liquid 1<br>then<br>${\\rho _2} &gt; {\\rho _1}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} (1)$<br>As ball of density ${\\rho _3}$ is floating in liquid of <br>density ${\\rho _1}$]<br>${\\rho _1} &gt; {\\rho _3}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} (2)$<br>From eqn. (1) and (2)<br>${\\rho _3} &lt; {\\rho _1} &lt; {\\rho _2}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/26_37101_question_1.png'),
(37082,'physics',27,1381,3315,'In an industrial process $10\\,kg$ of water per hour is to be heated from $20^\\circ C$ to $80^\\circ C$. To do this steam at $150^\\circ C$ is passed from a boiler into copper coil immersed in water. The steam condenses in the coil and is returned to the boiler as water at $90^\\circ C$. How many kilogram of steam is required per hour (specific heat of steam $ = 1\\,cal/gm\\,^\\circ C$, latent heat of vaporization $ = 540\\,cal/gm)$ ?','[{"text": "$1\\\\,gm$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1\\\\,kg$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$10\\\\,gm$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$10\\\\,kg$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Heat required by $10\\,kg$ water to change its temperature from $20^\\circ C$ to $80^\\circ C$  in one hour is<br>${Q_1} = {(mc\\Delta T)_{{\\text{water }}}}$<br>$ = \\left( {10 \\times {{10}^3}} \\right) \\times 1 \\times (80 - 20) = 600 \\times {10^3}cal$<br>In condensation<br>(i) Steam releases heat when it looses its temperature from $150^\\circ C$ to $100^\\circ C\\left[ {m{c_{{\\text{steam }}}}\\Delta T} \\right]$.<br>(ii) At $100^\\circ C$ it converts into water and gives the latent heat $[mL]$.<br>(iii) Water releases heat when it looses its temperature from $100^\\circ C$ to $90^\\circ C\\left[ {m{c_{{\\text{water }}}}\\Delta T} \\right]$.<br>If $m$ grams of steam is condensed per hour, then heat released by steam in converting water of $90^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C}$<br>$Q_{2}=(m c \\Delta T)_{\\text {steam }}+m L_{\\text {steam }}+(m s \\Delta T)_{\\text {water }}$<br>$=m[1 \\times(150-100)+540+1 \\times(100-90)]$<br>$ = 600\\,m\\,cal$<br>According to problem, ${Q_1} = {Q_2} \\Rightarrow 600 \\times {10^3}cal{\\text{ }} = 600\\,m\\,cal$<br>$ \\Rightarrow \\,\\,\\,{\\mkern 1mu} {\\kern 1pt} m = {10^3}gm = 1\\,kg$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37083,'physics',28,1367,3277,'Which of the following does not characterize the thermodynamic state of matter?','[{"text": "Volume", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Temperature", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Pressure", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Work", "isCorrect": true}]',3,NULL,NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37085,'physics',26,1133,2707,'A U- tube of base length \' $$l$$ \' filled with the same volume of two liquids of densities $$\\rho$$ and $$2 \\rho$$ is moving with an acceleration \' $$a$$ \' on the horizontal plane. If the height difference between the two liquid surfaces (open to atmosphere) becomes zero, then the height $$h$$ is given by','[{"text": "$$\\\\dfrac{a}{2 g} l$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$\\\\dfrac{3 a}{2 g} l$$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$$\\\\dfrac{a}{g} l$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$\\\\dfrac{2 a}{3 g} l$$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'<br><img src=\'/img/data/physics/26_37085_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' /> <br>For the given situation, liquid of density $$2 \\rho$$ should be behind that of $$\\rho$$. Let $$A$$ and $$C$$ are present very closely on both sides of $B$.<br>From the right limb, $$P_{A}=P_{a t m}+\\rho g h+\\rho a \\dfrac{l}{2}$$<br>$$P_{C}=P_{a t m}+2 \\rho g h-2 \\rho a \\dfrac{l}{2}$$<br>From above two equations<br>As the liquid section at $$\\mathrm{B}$$ is in equilibrium $$P_{A}=P_{C}$$<br>$$\\Rightarrow h=\\dfrac{3 a l}{2 g}$$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/26_37085_question_1.png'),
(37086,'physics',27,1382,3317,'There are two identical vessels filled with equal amounts of ice. The vessels are of different metals. If the ice melts in the two vessels in 20 and 35 minutes respectively, the ratio of the coefficients of thermal conductivity of the two metals is','[{"text": "$49: 16$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$16: 49$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$7: 4$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$4: 7$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'The heat required to melt ice is<br>$\\begin{aligned}& Q=\\dfrac{K A\\left(\\theta_{1}-\\theta_{2}\\right)}{l} t \\Rightarrow K_{1} t_{1}=K_{2} t_{2} \\\\& \\Rightarrow \\dfrac{K_{1}}{K_{2}}=\\dfrac{t_{2}}{t_{1}}=\\dfrac{35}{20}=\\dfrac{7}{4}\\end{aligned}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37087,'physics',28,1369,3279,'For an ideal gas its internal energy is increased, then','[{"text": "Its temperature will decrease", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Its temperature will remain constant", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Its temperature will increase", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "None of these", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'With rise in temperature, internal energy also increases.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37089,'physics',26,1133,2710,'You are holding a bottle of sparkling water inside a car moving forward. When the driver applies the brakes:','[{"text": "Depending on the speed of the car, bubbles might move forward or backward.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Bubbles will start to move backward with respect to the bottle.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Bubbles in the middle of the liquid will start to move forward with respect to the bottle.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Bubbles will stay at the same horizontal location in the water.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'When brakes are applied then pseudo force acts forward and pressure increases in forward direction and hence the bubbles move back ward (towards the less pressure side)',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37090,'physics',27,1382,3317,'A metal plate $4\\,mm$ thick has a temperature difference of $32^\\circ C$ between its faces. It transmits $200\\,kcal/h$ through an area of $5\\,c{m^2}$. Thermal conductivity of the material is','[{"text": "$5 \\\\times {10^{ - 4}}\\\\,W/m - \\\\,^\\\\circ C$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$58.33\\\\,W/m - \\\\,^\\\\circ C$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$33.58\\\\,W/m - \\\\,^\\\\circ C$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "None of these", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Here, $\\Delta x = 4mm = 4 \\times {10^{ - 3}}\\,m,\\Delta T = 32^\\circ C$<br>$\\frac{{dQ}}{{dT}} = 200kcal/h = \\frac{{200 \\times 1000 \\times 4.2}}{{60 \\times 60}}\\,J/s$<br>$ = 233.33\\,J/s$<br>$A = 5\\,c{m^2} = 5 \\times {10^{ - 4}}\\,{m^2}$<br>We know that, $\\dfrac{d Q}{d t}=K A\\left(\\dfrac{\\Delta T}{\\Delta x}\\right)$<br>$\\therefore$ Thermal conductivity of material,<br>$K = \\frac{{\\Delta Q/\\Delta T}}{{A(\\Delta T/\\Delta x)}}$<br>$K = \\frac{{233.33 \\times 4 \\times {{10}^{ - 3}}}}{{5 \\times {{10}^{ - 4}} \\times 32}} = 58.33\\,W/m - \\,^\\circ C.$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37091,'physics',28,1369,3279,'Which of the following is incorrect regarding the first law of thermodynamics?','[{"text": "It introduces the concept of change in internal energy", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "It introduces the concept of entropy", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "It is applicable to any process", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "It is a restatement of principle of conservation of energy.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,NULL,NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37114,'physics',27,1382,3322,'A particular star (assuming it as a black body) has a surface temperature of about $5 \\times {10^4}\\,K$. The wavelength ( in nanometers ) at which its radiation becomes maximum is $(b = 0.0029\\,m - K)$','[{"text": "70", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "48", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "58", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "60", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'According to Wien\'s displacement law<br>${\\lambda _m} = \\frac{b}{T} = \\frac{{0.0029}}{{5 \\times {{10}^4}}} = 58 \\times {10^{ - 9}} = 58\\,nm$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37092,'physics',23,873,2008,'A ball of radius $R=20 {~cm}$ has mass $m=0.75 {~kg}$ and moment of inertia (about its diameter) $I=0.0125 {~kg} {~m}^{2}$. The ball rolls without sliding over a rough horizontal floor with velocity ${v_0} = 10\\,\\,m{s^{ - 1}}$ towards a smooth vertical wall.If coefficient of restitution between the wall and the ball is $e=0.7$, calculate velocity $v$ of the ball long after the collision (in ${m} / {s}$ ). (Take $g=10 {~m} / {s}^{2}$ )','[{"text": "$2\\\\,m{s^{ - 1}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$5\\\\,m{s^{ - 1}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$7\\\\,m{s^{ - 1}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1\\\\,m{s^{ - 1}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'As the ball is rolling on ground, the lowest point of the ball should be at rest for this, $v_{0}=\\omega_{0} R$  hence the angular velocity $\\left(\\omega_{0}\\right)$ of the ball just before collision with wall is,<br>${\\omega _0} = \\frac{{{v_0}}}{R} = \\frac{{10}}{{0.2}} = 50\\,\\,rad{s^{ - 1}}$(in counterclockwise sense)<br> Now the ball collides with wall.<br> The velocity of centre of ball, just after collision should be<br>$u=e v_{0}=0.7 \\times 10=7 {~ms}^{-1} \\text { (Rightwards) }$<br>Since the wall is smooth, therefore no tangential force is applied by the wall. It means, angular velocity $\\omega_{0}$ of ball remains unchanged during collision. Now surface of ball slides on floor to the right as shown in the figure.  Hence the friction should act towards left  <img src=\'/img/data/physics/23_37092_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />  From free-body diagram of ball while sliding:  <img src=\'/img/data/physics/23_37092_explanation_2.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />   For vertical forces,$N=m g$<br> Let coefficient of friction between ball and the floor be $\\mu$.<br>For horizontal forces, $\\mu N=m a \\Rightarrow a=\\mu g$<br>Applying torque equation about centre of ball (about $O$ ) of force acting on the ball,<br>$\\mu NR = I\\alpha $<br>$ \\Rightarrow \\alpha  = \\frac{{\\mu (mg)R}}{I}$<br>$ = \\frac{{\\mu  \\times (0.75 \\times 10) \\times 0.20}}{{0.0125}}$<br>$\\Rightarrow \\alpha=120 \\mu$ (in clockwise sense)<br>Long after the collision, there will be no sliding or it will be pure rolling. Let sliding stop after a time $t$ after collision, then final translational velocity,<br>$v=(7-a t)$ or $v=7-10 \\mu t$<br>and final angular velocity, $\\omega=\\left(-\\omega_{0}\\right)+\\alpha t$<br>$\\omega  = (120\\mu t - 50)rad\\,\\,{s^{ - 1}}$  (clockwise)<br>But at that instant, $v_{c m}=\\omega R \\Rightarrow(7-10 \\mu t)=0.2(120 \\mu t-50)$<br>$\\mu t=0.5 \\Rightarrow v=2 {~ms}^{-1}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/23_37092_question_1.png'),
(37093,'physics',26,1133,2710,'A body is floating in liquid with $$50 \\%$$ of its volume outside the liquid. When the entire system accelerates upwards with an acceleration $$g / 3$$. The percentage of its volume outside the liquid is','[{"text": "$$33 \\\\%$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$50 \\\\%$$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$25.5\\\\% $", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$66 \\\\%$$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Let $$V$$ be volume of a body.<br>$$\\rho_{b}$$ and $$\\rho_{l}$$ be the density of the body and liquid respectively. When the body is floating in the liquid, then<br>Weight of the body $$=$$ Weight of the liquid displaced<br>$V{\\rho _b}g = {V_{in{\\text{ }}}}{\\rho _l}g$<br>$\\frac{{{V_{in{\\text{ }}}}}}{V} = \\frac{{{\\rho _b}}}{{{\\rho _l}}}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,(1)$<br>$$\\therefore \\quad$$ Volume of the body outside the liquid is<br>${V_{out}} = V - {V_{in}} = V - V\\frac{{{\\rho _b}}}{{{\\rho _l}}}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\left[ {{\\rm{Using}}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\rm{(1)}}} \\right]$<br>$ = V\\left( {1 - \\frac{{{\\rho _b}}}{{{\\rho _l}}}} \\right)$<br>$\\therefore \\quad \\frac{{{\\text{ Volume}}\\,{\\text{of}}\\,{\\text{the}}\\,{\\text{body}}\\,{\\text{out}}\\,{\\text{side}}\\,{\\text{the}}\\,{\\text{liquid }}}}{{{\\text{ Volume}}\\,{\\text{of}}\\,{\\text{the}}\\,{\\text{body }}}}$<br>$ = \\left( {1 - \\frac{{{\\rho _b}}}{{{\\rho _1}}}} \\right)$<br>As this ratio is independent of the effective value of acceleration due to gravity, so the percentage of the volume of the body outside the liquid will remain unchanged.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37094,'physics',27,1382,3317,'In winter atmospheric temperature falls below $0^\\circ C$ and water in the lake start freezing. Let at time $t$ thickness of ice on the surface of the lake is $y$ and air temperature $-\\theta$. The temperature of water in contact with the lower surface of ice is $0^\\circ C$. The area of the lake is $A$ and latent heat of fusion of ice is $L$. The thickness of ice $y$ as a function of $t$ is','[{"text": "$t=\\\\dfrac{1}{2} \\\\dfrac{\\\\rho L}{K \\\\theta} y^{2}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$t=\\\\dfrac{\\\\rho L y^{2}}{4 K \\\\theta}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$t=\\\\dfrac{K \\\\rho L y^{2}}{2 \\\\theta}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$t=\\\\dfrac{1}{4} \\\\dfrac{\\\\rho L \\\\theta}{K} y^{2}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'When water gets converted into ice heat liberates and this heat flows through ice slab.<br>The heat flow through the ice slab of thickness<br>$y$ is $\\dfrac{d Q}{d t}=K A \\dfrac{[\\theta-(-\\theta)]}{y}$<br>The small heat $dQ$ comes when water converts into ice<br>$\\begin{aligned}& d Q=d m L=(\\rho A d y) L \\\\& \\dfrac{\\rho L}{K \\theta} \\int_{0}^{y} y d y=\\int_{0}^{t} d t \\Rightarrow t=\\dfrac{1}{2} \\dfrac{\\rho L}{K \\theta} y^{2}\\end{aligned}$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/27_37094_question_1.png'),
(37095,'physics',28,1371,3281,'In thermodynamic reversible process predict the wrong option.','[{"text": "The process can be reversed by inducing infinitesimal changes to some property of the system", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Throughout the process, the system is in thermodynamic equilibrium with surroundings.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "All natural processes are reversible", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "None of these", "isCorrect": false}]',1,NULL,NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37097,'physics',26,1133,2708,'Find the angle $$\\theta$$ to stay the rod at equilibrium as shown in fig. If the density of the material of the rod is half of the density of water.','[{"text": "$$60^{\\\\circ}$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$30^{\\\\circ}$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$45^{\\\\circ}$$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$$37^{\\\\circ}$$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'The weight of the rod is<br>$$W=L A \\rho g$$<br>where $$L$$ is length of the rod and $$\\rho$$ is its density<br>$$\\begin{aligned}& O T=\\dfrac{L}{2 \\sin \\theta} \\\\& F_{b}=\\left(\\dfrac{L}{2 \\sin \\theta}\\right) A \\rho_{W} g\\end{aligned}$$<br><img src=\'/img/data/physics/26_37097_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' /> <br>$$O P=\\dfrac{O T}{2}=\\dfrac{l}{4 \\sin \\theta}$$<br>At equilibrium about $$O$$<br>$$\\begin{aligned}& \\tau_{m g}=\\tau_{F_{b}} \\\\& L A \\rho g \\dfrac{L}{2} \\cos \\theta=\\dfrac{b}{4 \\sin \\theta}\\left(\\dfrac{L}{2 \\sin \\theta} A \\rho_{W} g\\right) \\\\& \\sin ^{2} \\theta=\\dfrac{\\rho_{W}}{4 \\rho} \\Rightarrow \\sin ^{2} \\theta=\\dfrac{1}{2} \\\\& \\Rightarrow \\theta=45^{\\circ}\\end{aligned}$$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/26_37097_question_1.png'),
(37098,'physics',27,1382,3317,'The figure shows a system of two concentric spheres of radii $r_{1}$ and $r_{2}$ and kept at temperatures $T_{1}$ and $T_{2}$ respectively. The radial rate of flow of heat in a substance between the two concentric spheres is proportional to','[{"text": "$\\\\operatorname{In}\\\\left(\\\\dfrac{r_{2}}{r_{1}}\\\\right)$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\left(r_{2}-r_{1}\\\\right)$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{r_{1} r_{2}}{r_{2}-r_{1}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\left(r_{2}-r_{1}\\\\right)\\\\left(r_{1} r_{2}\\\\right)$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Consider a concentric spherical shell of radius $r$ and thickness $d r$ as shown in the figure. The radial rate of flow of heat through this shell in steady state will be<br>$\\begin{gathered}H=\\dfrac{d Q}{d t}=-K A \\dfrac{d T}{d r} \\\\=-K\\left(4 \\pi r^{2}\\right) \\dfrac{d T}{d r} \\\\\\Rightarrow \\int_{r_{1}}^{r_{2}} \\dfrac{d r}{r^{2}}=-\\dfrac{4 \\pi K}{H} \\int_{T_{1}}^{T_{2}} d T \\\\\\ddots\\end{gathered}$<br><img src=\'/img/data/physics/27_37098_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' /> <br>Which on integration and simplification gives<br>$H=\\dfrac{d Q}{d t}=\\dfrac{4 \\pi K r_{1} r_{2}\\left(T_{1}-T_{2}\\right)}{r_{2}-r_{1}} \\Rightarrow \\dfrac{d Q}{d t} \\propto \\dfrac{r_{1} r_{2}}{\\left(r_{2}-r_{1}\\right)}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/27_37098_question_1.png'),
(37102,'physics',27,1382,3318,'Six identical metallic rods are joined together in a pattern as shown in the figure. Points $A$ and $D$ are maintained at temperature $60^\\circ C$ and $240^\\circ C$. The temperature of the junction $B$ will be','[{"text": "$80^\\\\circ C$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$120^\\\\circ C$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$150^\\\\circ C$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$60^\\\\circ C$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Thermal resistance of a rod is <br><img src=\'/img/data/physics/27_37102_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' /> <br>$R = \\frac{l}{{KA}}$<br>The equivalent resistance between $B$ and $C$ is As rate of heat flow is same<br>$\\frac{{{T_A} - {T_B}}}{R} = \\frac{{{T_B} - {T_C}}}{R} = \\frac{{{T_C} - {T_D}}}{R}$<br>$60 - {T_B} = {T_B} - {T_C}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\left( 1 \\right)$<br>${T_C} - 240 = {T_B} - {T_C}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\left( 2 \\right)$<br>Solving (1) and (2)<br>${T_B} = 120^\\circ C$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/27_37102_question_1.png'),
(37103,'physics',28,1371,3289,'Which of the two will require more work to be done?<br>(i) compressing a gas isothermally until its volume is reduced by half<br>(ii) compressing the same gas adiabatically until its volume is reduced by half','[{"text": "(ii)", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "It will depend upon the nature of the gas", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "(i)", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both will require the same amount of work", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'The area under $P-V$ curve for isothermal curve is less than for adiabatic curve.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37105,'physics',26,1136,2717,'For the area $$a$$ of the hole is much lesser than the area of the base of a vessel of liquid, velocity of efflux $$v$$ of the liquid in an upward accelerating vessel is','[{"text": "$$\\\\sqrt{2 g h}$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$\\\\sqrt{2\\\\left|g-a_{0}\\\\right| h}$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$\\\\sqrt{2\\\\left(g+a_{0}\\\\right) h}$$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "None of these", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Effective value of acceleration due to gravity becomes $$\\left(g+a_{0}\\right)$$.<br>The velocity $$v=\\sqrt{2\\left(g+a_{0}\\right) h}$$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/26_37105_question_1.png'),
(37106,'physics',27,1382,3319,'A cylinder $A$ of a material of thermal conductivity $K_{1}$ is surrounded by a cylindrical shell of inner radius equal to the cylinder. The shell is made of a material of thermal conductivity $K_{2}$. The two ends of the combined system are maintained at two different temperatures. If the effective thermal conductivity of the system is $\\dfrac{4 K_{1}+5 K_{2}}{9}$, find the ratio of outer radius of the shell to the radius of the cylinder.','[{"text": "$5 / 2$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$5 / 3$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$3 / 2$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$2 / 3$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Radius of cylinder $=R$<br>Outer radius of shell $ = nR$<br>$R_{e q}=\\dfrac{R_{1} R_{2}}{R_{1}+R_{2}}=\\dfrac{\\dfrac{l}{K_{1} \\pi R^{2}} \\times \\dfrac{l}{K_{2}\\left(n^{2}-1\\right) \\pi R^{2}}}{\\dfrac{l}{K_{1} \\pi R^{2}}+\\dfrac{l}{K_{2}\\left(n^{2}-1\\right) \\pi R^{2}}}$<br>Where $l$ is the length of the cylinders.<br>But $R_{e q}=\\dfrac{l}{K \\pi n^{2} R^{2}}$<br>On solving, $K=\\dfrac{\\left[K_{1}+K_{2}\\left(n^{2}-1\\right)\\right]}{n^{2}}$<br>$\\begin{aligned}& =\\dfrac{4 K_{1}+5 K_{2}}{9}=\\dfrac{K_{1}+5 K_{2} / 4}{9 / 4} \\\\& n=3 / 2\\end{aligned}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/27_37106_question_1.png'),
(37107,'physics',28,1371,3285,'In a given process $dW = 0,\\,\\,dQ < 0$ then for a gas','[{"text": "Temperature increases", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Volume decreases", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Pressure decreases", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Pressure increases", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'As $d W=0$ so it is an isochoric process $\\dfrac{P}{T}=$ constant<br>Given $d q=d U < 0 \\Rightarrow T$ decreases and hence $P$ also decreases.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37108,'physics',26,1133,2710,'A block is submerged in vessel filled with water by a spring attached to the bottom of the vessel. In equilibrium, the spring is compressed. The vessel now moves downwards with an acceleration $$a( < g)$$. The spring length','[{"text": "Will decreases but not zero", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Will become zero", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "May increase or decrease or remain constant", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Will increase", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'As the vessel is accelerating down then pseudo force acts on the block in the upward direction and hence the spring length increases.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/26_37108_question_1.png'),
(37110,'physics',27,1382,3322,'We consider the radiation emitted by the human body. Which of the following statements is true','[{"text": "The radiation emitted is in the infra-red region", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "The radiation is emitted only during the day", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "The radiation emitted lies in the ultraviolet region and hence is not visible", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "The radiation is emitted during the summers and absorbed during the winters.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Every body at all time, at all temperature emits radiation (except at $T = 0$ ). The radiation emitted by the human body is in the infra-red region.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37111,'physics',28,1371,3287,'If $Q,\\,\\,E$ and $W$ denote respectively the heat added, change in internal energy and the work done in a closed cyclic process, then','[{"text": "$Q = 0$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$W = 0$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$E = 0$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$Q = W = 0$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'In a cyclic process, a system starts from one point and ends at the same point. In that case, the change in the internal energy must be zero and therefore, the thermal energy added to the system must be equal to the work done during the cycle, i.e., in a cyclic process,<br>$\\Rightarrow \\quad E=0$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37112,'physics',26,1136,2717,'A cylindrical drum, open at the top, contains 15 $L$ of water. It drains out through a small opening at the bottom.$5\\,L$ water comes out in time $$t_{1}$$, the next $5\\,L$ in further time $$t_{2}$$ and the last $5\\,L$ in further time $$t_{3}$$. Then','[{"text": "$$t_{1}>t_{2}>t_{3}$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$t_{1} < t_{2} < t_{3}$$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$$t_{2}>t_{1}=t_{3}$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$t_{1}=t_{2}=t_{3}$$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'If $$h$$ is the initial height of liquid in drum above the small opening, then velocity of efflux, $$v=\\sqrt{2 g h}$$. As the water drains out, $h$ decreases, hence $$v$$ decreases. This reduces the rate of discharge, a longer time is required to drain out the same volume of water. So, clearly $$t_{1} < t_{2} < t_{3}$$.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37115,'physics',28,1369,3279,'The internal energy change in a system that has absorbed $2\\,K\\,cals$ of heat and done $500\\,J$ of work is','[{"text": "$8900\\\\,J$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$6400\\\\,J$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$5400\\\\,J$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$7900\\\\,J$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'$\\Delta Q=\\Delta U+W \\Rightarrow 2 \\times 10^{3} \\times 4.2=\\Delta U+500$<br>$\\Rightarrow \\Delta U=7900 J$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37116,'physics',26,1136,2718,'A liquid flows through a horizontal tube as shown in figure. The velocities of the liquid in the two sections, which have areas of cross-section $$A_{1}$$ and $$A_{2}$$, are $$v_{1}$$ and $$v_{2}$$, respectively. The difference in the levels of the liquid in the two vertical tubes is $$h$$. Then','[{"text": "$$v_{2}^{2}-v_{1}^{2}=2 g h$$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$$v_{2}^{2}+v_{1}^{2}=2 g h$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$v_{2}^{2}-v_{1}^{2}=g h$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$v_{2}^{2}+v_{1}^{2}=g h$$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Let $$\\rho$$ be density of liquid flowing in the tube.<br>$$\\begin{aligned}A_{1} v_{1}= & A_{2} v_{2} \\\\& \\dfrac{v_{1}}{v_{2}}=\\dfrac{A_{2}}{A_{1}}\\end{aligned}$$<br>According to Bernoulli\'s equation for horizontal flow of liquid,<br>$$\\begin{aligned}& P_{1}+\\dfrac{1}{2} \\rho v_{1}^{2}=P_{2}+\\dfrac{1}{2} \\rho v_{2}^{2} \\\\& P_{1}-P_{2}=\\dfrac{1}{2}\\left(\\rho v_{2}^{2}-\\rho v_{1}^{2}\\right) \\\\& h \\rho g=\\dfrac{1}{2} \\rho\\left(v_{2}^{2}-v_{1}^{2}\\right) \\quad\\left(\\because P_{1}-P_{2}=h \\rho g\\right) \\\\& v_{2}^{2}-v_{1}^{2}=2 h g\\end{aligned}$$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/26_37116_question_1.png'),
(37117,'physics',23,870,1996,'A boy is pushing a ring of mass 2 $kg$ and radius 0.5 $m$ with a stick as shown in the figure. The stick applies a force of 2 $N$ on the ring and rolls it without slipping with an acceleration of $0.3\\,m/{s^2}.$ The coefficient of friction between the ground and the ring is large enough that rolling always occurs and the coefficient of friction between the stick and the ring is $(P / 10)$.  Find the value of $P$','[{"text": "2", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "4", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "9", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "6", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Note: If net force applied by the rod is considered to be 2 $N$ .  <img src=\'/img/data/physics/23_37117_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' /> $\\sqrt {{f^{\\prime 2}} + {F^2}}  = 2\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,(1)$<br>$FR - {f^\\prime }R = 2m{R^2}\\frac{a}{R}$<br>$F - f\' = 2\\,ma = 1.2{\\mkern 1mu} \\quad \\quad \\quad (2)$<br>From Eqs. (1) and (2), $\\left(1.2+f^{\\prime}\\right)^{2}+f^{\\prime 2}=2^{2}$<br>$2{f^{\\prime 2}} + 2.4{f^\\prime } + 1.44 = 4$<br>${f^{\\prime 2}} + 1.2{f^\\prime } + 0.72 - 2 = 0$<br>${f^{\\prime 2}} + 1.2{f^\\prime } - 1.28 = 0$<br>${f^\\prime } = \\frac{{ - 1.2 \\pm \\sqrt {1.44 + 4 \\times (1.28)} }}{2}$<br>$ = 0.6 \\pm \\sqrt {0.36 + 1.28} $<br>$ =  - 0.6 \\pm \\sqrt {0.64}  = 0.68$<br>From Eq. (2), $F=1.88$.<br>$\\mu  = \\frac{{0.68}}{{1.88}} = \\frac{P}{{10}} \\Rightarrow P = 3.16 \\approx 4$<br> But if only normal reaction applied by the rod is considered to be 2 $N$ .<br>IInd Law $\\Rightarrow 2-f=2[0.3] \\Rightarrow f=2-0.6$<br>$f = 1.4\\,Nx\\quad \\quad \\quad (1)$<br>$A = R\\,\\alpha $<br>$ \\Rightarrow \\quad 0.3 = \\alpha [0.5]$<br>$ \\Rightarrow \\quad \\alpha  = \\frac{3}{5}rad/s\\quad \\quad \\quad (2)$<br>${\\tau _c} = {I_c}\\alpha  \\Rightarrow fR - 2\\,\\,\\mu R = m{R^2}\\alpha $<br>$f - 2\\,\\,\\mu  = mR\\alpha $<br>$1.4 - 2\\,\\,\\mu  = \\frac{2}{2}\\left( {\\frac{3}{2}} \\right)$<br>$1.4 - 0.6 = 2\\mu $<br>$0.8 = 2\\,\\,\\mu  \\Rightarrow \\mu  = 0.4 = \\frac{P}{{10}}$<br>$\\therefore \\quad P = 4$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/23_37117_question_1.png'),
(37118,'physics',27,1382,3323,'The tungsten filament of an electric lamp has a surface area $A$ and a power rating $P$. If the emissivity of the filament is $\\varepsilon$ and $\\sigma$ is Stefan\'s constant, the steady temperature of the filament will be','[{"text": "$T=\\\\left(\\\\dfrac{P}{A \\\\varepsilon \\\\sigma}\\\\right)^{\\\\frac{1}{4}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$T=\\\\left(\\\\dfrac{P}{A \\\\varepsilon \\\\sigma}\\\\right)^{4}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$T=\\\\left(\\\\dfrac{P}{A \\\\varepsilon \\\\sigma}\\\\right)$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$T=\\\\left(\\\\dfrac{A \\\\varepsilon \\\\sigma}{P}\\\\right)^{\\\\frac{1}{4}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'At steady state condition<br>$\\begin{aligned}P & =\\varepsilon \\sigma A T^{4} \\\\T^{4} & =\\dfrac{P}{\\varepsilon \\sigma A} \\Rightarrow T=\\left(\\dfrac{P}{A \\sigma \\varepsilon}\\right)^{\\frac{1}{4}}\\end{aligned}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37119,'physics',28,1371,3282,'Heat is absorbed by a body. But its temperature does not rise. Which of the following statement explains the phenomena?','[{"text": "Only K.E. of vibration increases", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Only P.E. of inter molecular force changes", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "No increase in internal energy takes place", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Increase in K.E. is balanced by decrease in P.E.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,NULL,NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37120,'physics',26,1136,2719,'A horizontal tube of uniform cross-sectional area $A$ is bent in the form of $U$-tube as shown. A liquid of density $$\\rho$$ enters and leaves the tube with velocity $$v$$. External force $F$ required to hold the tube stationary is<br>$$F=(x) \\times\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2} \\rho a v^{2}\\right)$$. Find $$x$$','[{"text": "2", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "3", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "6", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "4", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'As the momentum of the liquid gets reversed the net force on the tube is<br>$$\\begin{aligned}& F=2 \\rho A v^{2}=x\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2} \\rho a v^{2}\\right) \\\\& \\Rightarrow \\quad x=4\\end{aligned}$$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/26_37120_question_1.png'),
(37122,'physics',27,1382,3323,'A wire of length $1.0\\,m$ and radius ${10^{ - 3}}\\,m$ is carrying a heavy current and it is assumed to radiate as a black body. At equilibrium its temperature is $900\\,K$ while that of the surroundings is $300\\,K$. The resistivity of the material of the wire at $300\\,K$ is ${\\pi ^2} \\times {10^{ - 8}}ohm - m$ and its temperature coefficient of resistance is $7.8 \\times 10^{-3} C^{-1}$. Find the current in the wire. Given $\\sigma  = 5.68 \\times {10^{ - 8}}W{m^{ - 2}}\\,{K^{ - 4}}$.','[{"text": "$36\\\\,A$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$26\\\\,A$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$16\\\\,A$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$6\\\\,A$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'$P=\\sigma A\\left(T^{4}-T_{0}^{4}\\right)$<br>$ = \\sigma 2\\pi rl\\left( {{T^4} - T_0^4} \\right)$<br>$ = 5.68 \\times {10^{ - 8}}\\left( {2\\pi  \\times {{10}^{ - 3}} \\times 1} \\right)\\left[ {{{(900)}^4} - {{(300)}^4}} \\right]$<br>$ = 73.6\\pi W$<br>$P = {I^2}R = {I^2}\\rho \\frac{L}{A}$<br>$ = {I^2}\\frac{L}{A}{\\rho _0}[1 + \\alpha \\Delta \\theta ]$<br>$73.6\\pi  = \\frac{{{I^2} \\times {\\pi ^2} \\times {{10}^{ - 8}}\\left[ {1 + 7.8 \\times {{10}^{ - 3}}(900 - 300)} \\right]}}{{\\pi {{\\left( {{{10}^{ - 3}}} \\right)}^2}}}{\\text{ }}$<br>${\\text{or }}\\,\\,\\,{\\mkern 1mu} {\\kern 1pt} I = 36\\,A.$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37123,'physics',28,1371,3284,'In which process the $PV$ indicator diagram is straight line parallel to volume axis','[{"text": "Isobaric", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Isothermal", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Adiabatic", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Irreversible", "isCorrect": false}]',0,NULL,NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37124,'physics',26,1136,2717,'There are two identical small holes on the opposite sides of a tank containing a liquid. The tank is open at the top. The difference in height between the two holes is $$h$$. As the liquid comes out of the two holes, the tank will experience a net horizontal force proportional to','[{"text": "$$h^{1 / 2}$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$h^{3 / 2}$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$h^{2}$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$h$$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Here, $$v_{1}=\\sqrt{2 g(h+x)} ; v_{2}=\\sqrt{2 g x}$$<br>Let, $$a=$$ area of cross-section of each hole<br>$$\\rho=$$ density of the liquid<br><img src=\'/img/data/physics/26_37124_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' /> <br>The force exerted on the lower hole towards left<br>$$=a \\rho v_{1}^{2}$$<br>Similarly, the force exerted on the upper hole towards right<br>$$=a \\rho v_{2}^{2}$$<br>Net force on the tank, $$F=a \\rho\\left(v_{1}^{2}-v_{2}^{2}\\right)$$<br>$$\\begin{aligned}& F=a \\rho[2 g(h+x)-2 g x]=2 a \\rho g h \\\\& \\Rightarrow \\quad F \\propto h\\end{aligned}$$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/26_37124_question_1.png'),
(37126,'physics',27,1382,3323,'The surface temperature of the sun is $T{\\mkern 1mu} K$ and the solar constant for a planet is $S$. The sun subtends an angle $2 \\theta$ at the planet. Then:','[{"text": "$S\\\\,\\\\alpha \\\\,{\\\\theta ^{1/2}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$S\\\\,\\\\alpha \\\\,{\\\\theta ^3}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$S\\\\,\\\\alpha \\\\,{\\\\theta ^2}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$S\\\\,\\\\alpha \\\\,\\\\theta $", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Let radius of sun $ = R$<br>Distance of the earth from the sun $ = d$ Power radiated from the sun<br>$=\\left(4 \\pi R^{2}\\right) \\sigma T^{4}=P$<br>Energy received per unit area is $S=\\dfrac{P}{4 \\pi d^{2}}$<br>$\\begin{aligned}& =4 \\pi R^{2} \\sigma \\dfrac{T^{4}}{4 \\pi d^{2}}=\\sigma T^{4} \\dfrac{R^{2}}{d^{2}} \\\\& =\\dfrac{1}{4} \\sigma T^{4}\\left(\\dfrac{2 R}{d}\\right)^{2}\\end{aligned}$<br>Angle subtend by sun at the earth, <br>$\\begin{gathered}\\quad \\theta=\\dfrac{2 R}{d} \\\\S=\\text { constant } \\times T^{4} \\times \\theta^{2} \\\\S \\alpha \\theta^{2}\\end{gathered}$<br>$S\\,\\alpha \\,{\\theta ^2}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37127,'physics',28,1371,3284,'A gas expands under constant pressure $P$ from volume $V_{1}$ to $V_{2}$. The work done by the gas is','[{"text": "$P=\\\\dfrac{V_{1} V_{2}}{V_{2}-V_{1}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$P\\\\left(V_{1}^{\\\\gamma}-V_{2}^{\\\\gamma}\\\\right)$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$P\\\\left(V_{1}-V_{2}\\\\right)$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$P\\\\left(V_{2}-V_{1}\\\\right)$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Work done $=P \\Delta V=P\\left(V_{2}-V_{1}\\right)$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37128,'physics',26,1137,2722,'A cubical block of side \' $$a$$ \' and density \' $$\\rho$$ \' slides over a fixed inclined plane with constant velocity \' $$v$$ \'. There is a thin film of viscous fluid of thickness \' $$t$$ \' between the plane and the block. Then the coefficient of viscosity of the thin film will be: (Acceleration due to gravity is $$g$$ )','[{"text": "$$\\\\dfrac{v}{\\\\rho a g t \\\\sin \\\\theta}$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$\\\\dfrac{\\\\rho a g t^{2} \\\\sin \\\\theta}{v}$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$\\\\dfrac{\\\\rho a g t \\\\sin \\\\theta}{v}$$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "None of these", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'As the block moves with constant velocity<br>Viscous force $$=m g \\sin \\theta$$<br>$$\\begin{gathered}\\therefore \\quad \\eta A \\dfrac{v}{t}=m g \\sin \\theta \\\\\\eta a^{2} \\dfrac{v}{t}=a^{3} \\rho g \\sin \\theta \\\\\\eta=\\dfrac{t \\rho g \\sin \\theta a}{v}\\end{gathered}$$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/26_37128_question_1.png'),
(37130,'physics',27,1382,3323,'The temperature of an isolated black body falls from $T_{1}$ and $T_{2}$ in time $t$. Let $c$ be a constant.','[{"text": "$t=c\\\\left[\\\\dfrac{1}{T_{2}^{3}}-\\\\dfrac{1}{T_{1}^{3}}\\\\right]$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$t=c\\\\left[\\\\dfrac{1}{T_{2}^{4}}-\\\\dfrac{1}{T_{1}^{4}}\\\\right]$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$t=c\\\\left[\\\\dfrac{1}{T_{2}}-\\\\dfrac{1}{T_{1}}\\\\right]$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$t=c\\\\left[\\\\dfrac{1}{T_{2}^{2}}-\\\\dfrac{1}{T_{1}^{2}}\\\\right]$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'$P=\\left(4 \\pi r^{2}\\right)\\left(\\sigma T^{4}\\right)=m s\\left(-\\dfrac{d T}{d t}\\right)$<br>Where $m$ is the mass and $s$ is the specific heat of the body<br>$\\begin{gathered}4 \\pi r^{2} \\sigma T^{4}=-\\dfrac{4}{3} \\pi r^{3} \\rho s \\cdot \\dfrac{d T}{d t} \\\\\\dfrac{d T}{d t}=\\dfrac{-3 \\sigma T^{4}}{\\rho s r} \\\\\\int_{T_{1}}^{T_{2}}-\\dfrac{d T}{T^{4}}=(\\text { constant }) \\int_{0}^{t} d t \\\\\\text { or } \\quad c\\left[\\dfrac{1}{T_{2}^{3}}-\\dfrac{1}{T_{1}^{3}}\\right]=t\\end{gathered}$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37131,'physics',28,1371,3284,'Heat is supplied to a di-atomic gas at constant pressure. The ratio of $\\Delta Q: \\Delta U: \\Delta W$ is :-','[{"text": "$5: 3: 2$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$5: 2: 3$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$7: 5: 2$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$7: 2: 5$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'$\\dfrac{\\Delta U}{\\Delta Q}=\\dfrac{n C_{V} \\Delta T}{n C_{P} \\Delta T}=\\dfrac{1}{\\gamma}=\\dfrac{5}{7}$<br>$\\dfrac{\\Delta W}{\\Delta Q}=\\dfrac{\\Delta Q-\\Delta U}{\\Delta Q}=1-\\dfrac{1}{\\gamma}=\\dfrac{2}{7}$<br>$\\Rightarrow \\Delta Q: \\Delta U: \\Delta W=7: 5: 2$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37132,'physics',26,1137,2722,'A thin plate of area $2\\,\\,{m^2}$ is moving between two fixed parallel surfaces as shown in the figure. The liquid present on both sides of the plate is same and has coefficient of viscosity $$\\eta=0.01$$ poise. Find the external force require to make the plate to move with constant velocity $2\\,m/s$.','[{"text": "$12 \\\\times {10^{ - 3}}\\\\,\\\\,N$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$8 \\\\times {10^{ - 3}}\\\\,\\\\,N$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$4 \\\\times {10^{ - 3}}\\\\,\\\\,N$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$12 \\\\times {10^{ - 2}}\\\\,\\\\,N$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'As the liquid is present on both sides of the plate the viscous force on the plate by both the liquids is<br>$$f=\\dfrac{\\eta A v}{d_{1}}+\\dfrac{\\eta A v}{d_{2}}$$<br>Given that $$\\eta=0.01$$ Poise $ = {10^{ - 3}}\\frac{{Ns}}{{{m^2}}}$<br>$A = 2\\,{m^2},v = 2\\,m/s$<br>${d_1} = 0.5\\,m,{d_2} = 0.1\\,m$<br>$f = 12 \\times {10^{ - 3}}\\,N$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/26_37132_question_1.png'),
(37135,'physics',28,1371,3282,'A gas is compressed isothermally. The rms velocity of its molecules','[{"text": "First increases and then decreases", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Increases", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Decreases", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Remains the same", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'The root mean square velocity of a gas molecule is given by<br>$v_{\\mathrm{rms}}=\\sqrt{\\dfrac{3 R T}{M}}$<br>Gas is compressed isothermally, so $T$ remains constant and hence, root mean square velocity will remain same',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37147,'physics',28,1371,3284,'Air in a cylinder is suddenly compressed by a piston, which is then maintained at the same position. With the passage of time','[{"text": "The pressure increases", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "The pressure decreases", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "The pressure may increase or decrease depending upon the nature of the gas", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "The pressure remains the same", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Due to compression the temperature of the system increases to a very high value. This causes the flow of heat from system to the surroundings, thus decreasing the temperature. This decrease in temperature results in decrease in pressure.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37136,'physics',26,1137,2723,'The terminal speed attained by an aluminium sphere of radius $1\\,mm$ falling through water at $20^\\circ {\\text{ }}C$ will be close to (Assume laminar flow, specific gravity of $$A l=2.7$$ and $$\\eta_{\\text {water }}=8 \\times 10^{-4}$$ )','[{"text": "$4.6\\\\,\\\\,m/s$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$6.9\\\\,\\\\,m/s$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$9.2\\\\,\\\\,m/s$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2.3\\\\,\\\\,m/s$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Terminal speed, $$v=\\dfrac{2}{9} \\dfrac{r^{2}(\\rho-\\sigma) g}{\\eta}$$<br>Where $$\\rho$$ and $$\\sigma$$ are the densities of spherical body and medium respectively, $$r$$ is the radius of the spherical body and $$\\eta$$ is the coefficient of viscosity of the medium.<br>Here, $r = 1\\,mm = {10^{ - 3}}$<br>Specific gravity of $$A l=2.7$$<br>Density of $Al,\\rho  = 2.7 \\times {10^3}\\,kg/{m^3}$<br>Density of water, $\\sigma  = {10^3}\\,kg/{m^3}$<br>$\\eta  = 8 \\times {10^{ - 4}},g = 9.8\\,m/{s^2}$<br>$\\therefore \\quad v = \\frac{{2{{\\left( {{{10}^{ - 3}}} \\right)}^2} \\times \\left( {2.7 \\times {{10}^3} - {{10}^3}} \\right) \\times 9.8}}{{9 \\times 8 \\times {{10}^{ - 4}}}} = 4.6\\,m/s$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37137,'physics',23,870,2001,'A loop and a disc roll without slipping with same linear velocity $v$. The mass of the loop and the disc is same. If the total kinetic energy of the loop is 8 $J$ , find the kinetic energy of the disc','[{"text": "$3\\\\,J$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$6\\\\,J$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$9\\\\,J$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1\\\\,J$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Total kinetic energy of loop <br>$=\\dfrac{1}{2} I \\omega+\\dfrac{1}{2} M v^{2}$$=\\dfrac{1}{2}\\left(M R^{2} \\omega^{2}\\right)+\\dfrac{1}{2} M v^{2}=M v^{2}=8 {~J}(\\text { given })$<br>Total kinetic energy of disc<br>$ = \\frac{1}{2}I{\\omega ^2} + \\frac{1}{2}M{v^2}$<br>$ = \\frac{1}{2}\\left( {\\frac{1}{2}M{R^2}} \\right)\\left( {\\frac{{{v^2}}}{{{R^2}}}} \\right) + \\frac{1}{2}M{v^2}$<br>$ = \\frac{3}{4}M{v^2} = \\frac{3}{4}(8) = 6\\,J$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37139,'physics',28,1371,3282,'Work done in an isothermal gas depends','[{"text": "Only on volume expansion ratio", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Only on temperature", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Only on initial and final pressure", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "On both the temperature and volume expansion ratio", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'$W=n R T \\ln \\left(\\dfrac{V_{2}}{V_{1}}\\right)$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37140,'physics',26,1137,2722,'When a body falls in air, the resistance of air depends to a great extent on the shape of the body, 3 different shapes are given. Identify the combination of air resistances which truly represents the physical situation. (The cross sectional areas are the same).','[{"text": "$3 < 2 < 1$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$2 < 3 < 1$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1 < 2 < 3$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$3 < 1 < 2$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'A stream lined body has less resistance due to air. Here cigar shaped body will have least resistance.',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/26_37140_question_1.png'),
(37143,'physics',28,1371,3284,'Which of the following is a slow process?','[{"text": "Adiabatic", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Isothermal", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Isobaric", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "None of these", "isCorrect": false}]',1,NULL,NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37144,'physics',26,1133,2710,'A stream lined body falls through air from a height on the surface of a liquid. Let $$d$$ and $D$ denote the densities of the materials of the body and the liquid respectively. If $$D>d$$, then the time after which the body will be instantaneously at rest is','[{"text": "$$\\\\sqrt{\\\\dfrac{2 h}{g}}$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$\\\\sqrt{\\\\dfrac{2 h}{g} \\\\dfrac{D}{g}}$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$\\\\sqrt{\\\\dfrac{2 h}{g} \\\\dfrac{d}{D}}$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$\\\\dfrac{d}{(D-d)} \\\\sqrt{\\\\dfrac{2 h}{g}}$$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Retardation<br>$$\\begin{gathered}=\\dfrac{F_{b}-m g}{m}=\\dfrac{V D g-V d g}{V d}=\\dfrac{D g}{d}-g \\\\a=g\\left(\\dfrac{D}{d}-1\\right)=\\dfrac{g(D-d)}{d}\\end{gathered}$$<br>The time at which the body comes to rest is<br>$$\\begin{gathered}t=\\dfrac{u}{a} \\$u-\\text { initial value }) \\\\t=\\dfrac{\\sqrt{2 g h} d}{g(D-d)}=\\sqrt{\\dfrac{2 h}{g}}\\left(\\dfrac{d}{D-d}\\right)\\end{gathered}$$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37146,'physics',27,1380,3306,'A $Cu$ rod and a steel rod maintain a difference in their lengths constant at all temperatures. If their coefficients of expansion are $1.6 \\times {10^{ - 5}}\\,{K^{ - 1}}$ and  $1.2 \\times {10^{ - 5}}\\,{K^{ - 1}}.$ If the length of steel $\\operatorname{rod}$ is $10\\,cm$ then the length of the $Cu$ rod is','[{"text": "$10\\\\,cm$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$7.5\\\\,cm$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$22\\\\,cm$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$14\\\\,cm$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Let $l_{1}$ be the length of $Cu$ and $l_{2}$ that of steel<br>$\\left(l_{2}^{1}-l_{1}^{1}\\right)=\\left(l_{2}-l_{1}\\right)+\\left(l_{2} \\alpha_{2}-l_{1} \\alpha_{1}\\right) \\Delta T$<br>$\\Rightarrow l_{2} \\alpha_{2}-l_{1} \\alpha_{1}=0 \\quad\\left(l_{2}^{1}-l_{1}^{1}=l_{2}-l_{1}\\right)$<br>that is, $\\dfrac{l_{2}}{l_{1}}=\\dfrac{\\alpha_{1}}{\\alpha_{2}}$<br>$\\dfrac{10}{l_{1}}=\\dfrac{1.6}{1.2}$<br>${\\rm{or}}$$\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{l_1} = 7.5\\,cm$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37148,'physics',26,1137,2723,'What is the velocity $$v$$ of a metallic ball of radius $$r$$ falling in a tank of liquid at the instant when its acceleration is one-half that of a freely falling body? (The densities of metal and of liquid are $$\\rho$$ and $$\\sigma$$ respectively, and the viscosity of the liquid is $$\\eta$$ )','[{"text": "$$\\\\dfrac{r^{2} g}{9 \\\\eta}(\\\\rho-2 \\\\sigma)$$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$$\\\\dfrac{r^{2} g}{9 \\\\eta}(2 \\\\rho-\\\\sigma)$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$\\\\dfrac{r^{2} g}{9 \\\\eta}(\\\\rho-\\\\sigma)$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$\\\\dfrac{2 r^{2} g}{9 \\\\eta}(\\\\rho-\\\\sigma)$$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Given that $$a=\\dfrac{g}{2}$$<br>$$\\begin{aligned}& m a=m g-F_{b}-f \\\\& m \\dfrac{g}{2}=m g-F_{b}-f \\\\& m \\dfrac{g}{2}=F_{b}+f\\end{aligned}$$<br>$$\\begin{aligned}& \\rho V \\dfrac{g}{2}=\\sigma V g+6 \\pi \\eta r v \\\\& v=\\dfrac{V g\\left(\\dfrac{\\rho}{2}-\\sigma\\right)}{6 \\pi \\eta r}=\\dfrac{r^{2} g(\\rho-2 \\sigma)}{9 \\eta}\\end{aligned}$$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37150,'physics',27,1380,3306,'What should be the lengths of a steel and copper rod at $0^\\circ C$ so that the length of the steel rod is $5\\,cm$ longer than the copper rod at any temperature<br>$\\alpha \\,\\left( {Steel} \\right){\\rm{ }} = 1.1 \\times {10^{ - 5}}\\,^\\circ {C^{ - 1}}$ Copper $ = 1.7 \\times {10^{ - 5}}\\,^\\circ C$','[{"text": "$9.17\\\\,cm;14.17\\\\,cm$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$14.17\\\\,cm;9.17\\\\,cm$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$14.17\\\\,cm;18.34\\\\,cm$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$28.34\\\\,cm;18.34\\\\,cm$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Here, $\\alpha \\,\\left( {steel} \\right){\\rm{ }} = 1.1 \\times {10^{ - 5}}\\,^\\circ {C^{ - 1}}$<br>$\\alpha \\left( {copper} \\right) = 1.7 \\times {10^{ - 5}}\\,^\\circ {C^{ - 1}}$<br>As the difference in the lengths remain constant<br>$l_{0}(s) \\alpha(s)=l_{0}(c) \\alpha(c)$<br>As, $\\quad \\dfrac{l_{0}(s)}{l_{0}(c)}=\\dfrac{\\alpha(c)}{\\alpha(s)}=\\dfrac{1.7 \\times 10^{-5}}{1.1 \\times 10^{-5}}=1.545$<br>$\\therefore l_{0}(s)=1.545 l_{0}(c)$<br>Also $l_{0}(s)-l_{0}(c)=5$<br>$0.545{l_0}(c) = 5$<br>$ \\Rightarrow {l_0}(c) = \\frac{5}{{0.545}} = 9.17\\,cm$<br>and ${l_0}(s) = 1.545 \\times 9.17\\,cm = 14.17\\,cm$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37151,'physics',28,1371,3282,'For an isothermal expansion of a perfect gas, the value of $\\dfrac{\\Delta P}{P}$ is equal to','[{"text": "$\\\\dfrac{\\\\Delta V}{V}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$-\\\\gamma^{1 / 2} \\\\dfrac{\\\\Delta V}{V}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$-\\\\gamma^{2} \\\\dfrac{\\\\Delta V}{V}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$-\\\\gamma \\\\dfrac{\\\\Delta V}{V}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Differentiating $P V=$ constant w.r.t $V$<br>$\\Rightarrow P \\Delta V+V \\Delta P=0 \\Rightarrow \\dfrac{\\Delta P}{P}=-\\dfrac{\\Delta V}{V}$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37153,'physics',26,1137,2725,'A tube of radius $$r$$ and length $$l$$ is connected in series with another tube of radius $$\\dfrac{r}{2}$$ and length $$\\dfrac{l}{8}$$. If the pressure difference across the tubes taken together is $P$, the pressure difference across the two tubes separately','[{"text": "$$\\\\dfrac{P}{2}$$ and $$\\\\dfrac{P}{2}$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$\\\\dfrac{P}{3}$$ and $$\\\\dfrac{3 P}{2}$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$\\\\dfrac{P}{4}$$ and $$\\\\dfrac{3 P}{2}$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$\\\\dfrac{P}{3}$$ and $$\\\\dfrac{2 P}{3}$$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'The rate of liquid flow is<br>$$Q=\\dfrac{\\pi \\operatorname{Pr}^{4}}{8 \\eta l}$$<br>Let $$P_{1} \\& P_{2}$$ are the pressure difference across the tubes.<br>$P = {P_1} + {P_2}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,(1)$<br>As the flow of liquid is same in both tubes<br>${Q_1} = {Q_2}$<br>$\\frac{{\\pi {P_1}{r^4}}}{{8\\eta l}} = \\frac{{\\pi {P_2}{{\\left( {\\frac{r}{2}} \\right)}^4}}}{{8\\eta \\frac{l}{8}}} \\Rightarrow {P_1} = \\frac{{{P_2}}}{2}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,(2)$<br>From eq\'s (1) & (2)<br>$$P_{1}=\\dfrac{P}{3} \\& P_{2}=\\dfrac{2 P}{3}$$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37154,'physics',27,1380,3306,'The design of some physical instrument requires that there be a constant difference in length of $10\\,cm$ between an iron rod and a copper cylinder laid side by side at all temperatures. The lengths are $\\left(\\alpha_{F e}=11 \\times 10^{-6 \\circ} c^{-1}, \\alpha_{c u}=17 \\times 10^{-60} c^{-1}\\right)$','[{"text": "$18.3\\\\,cm,28.3\\\\,cm$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$28.3\\\\,cm,38.3\\\\,cm$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$8.3\\\\,cm,18.3\\\\,cm$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "None of these", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Let $l_{0}$ be the length of the copper at $0^\\circ C$. The length of iron at $0^\\circ C$ is ${l_0} + 10^\\circ cm$. After a change of temperature $\\Delta T$ the difference in their length must remain same<br>$\\left( {{l_0} + 10\\,cm} \\right)\\left( {1 + {\\alpha _{Fe}}\\Delta T} \\right) - {l_0}\\left( {1 + {\\alpha _{cu}}\\Delta T} \\right) = 10\\,cm$<br>Given that $\\alpha_{c u}=17 \\times 10^{-6 \\circ} c^{-1}$<br>${\\alpha _{Fe}} = 11 \\times {10^{ - 6}}\\,^\\circ {C^{ - 1}} \\Rightarrow \\,\\,\\,{\\mkern 1mu} {\\kern 1pt} {l_0} = 18.3\\,cm$<br>so option (1) is correct.',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37155,'physics',28,1371,3283,'During an adiabatic expansion, the increase in volume is associated with which of the following possibilities regarding pressure and temperature ?<br>$\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{\\rm{Pressure}}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} \\,\\,{\\mkern 1mu} \\,\\,{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\rm{Temperature}}$','[{"text": "$\\\\begin{array}{*{20}{c}}\\n{{\\\\rm{Increase}}}&{\\\\,{\\\\rm{Increase}}}\\n\\\\end{array}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\begin{array}{*{20}{c}}\\n{{\\\\rm{Decrease}}}&{{\\\\rm{Decrease}}}\\n\\\\end{array}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\begin{array}{*{20}{c}}\\n{{\\\\rm{Increase}}}&{{\\\\rm{Decrease}}}\\n\\\\end{array}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\begin{array}{*{20}{c}}\\n{{\\\\rm{Decrease}}}&{\\\\,{\\\\rm{Increase}}}\\n\\\\end{array}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'$P{V^\\gamma } = $ const $\\Rightarrow V^{\\gamma} \\propto \\dfrac{1}{P}$<br>$T V^{\\gamma-1}=$ const $\\Rightarrow V^{\\gamma-1} \\propto \\dfrac{1}{T}$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37156,'physics',23,864,1979,'A heavy iron bar, of weight $W$ is having its one end on the ground and the other on the shoulder of a person. The bar makes an angle $\\theta$ with the horizontal. The weight experienced by the person is ____ .','[{"text": "$\\\\dfrac{W}{2}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$W \\\\sin \\\\theta$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$W$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$W \\\\cos \\\\theta$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'<img src=\'/img/data/physics/23_37156_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />  <br>Let $R$ be the reaction force on shoulder.<br>Let $L$ be the length of bar.<br>For the equilibrium, $\\tau_{\\text {net }}=0$ (or) $C W T=A C W T$<br>Take torque about $O$<br>$\\therefore W\\left(\\dfrac{L}{2} \\cos \\theta\\right)=R \\times L \\cos \\theta$<br>$R=\\dfrac{W}{2}$<br>Thus, weight experienced by the person is due to the reaction force.',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',2024,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37185,'physics',26,1133,2708,'A uniformly tapering vessel of height $$h$$ whose lower and upper radii are $r$ and $R$ is completely filled with a liquid of density $$\\rho$$. The force that acts on the base of the vessel due to pressure of the liquid is','[{"text": "$$\\\\pi R^{2} h \\\\rho g$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$\\\\pi r^{2} h \\\\rho g$$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$$\\\\pi\\\\left(\\\\dfrac{R+r}{2}\\\\right)^{2} h \\\\rho g$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$\\\\dfrac{1}{3} \\\\pi\\\\left(R^{2}-r^{2}\\\\right) h \\\\rho g$$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Force $$=$$ area of base $$\\times$$ pressure due to liquid column of height $$h=\\pi r^{2} \\times h \\rho g$$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37157,'physics',26,1138,2727,'A ring is cut from a platinum tube of $8.5\\,\\,cm$ internal and $8.7\\,cm$ external diameter. It is supported horizontally from a balance so that it comes in contact with the water in a glass vessel. What is the surface tension of water if an extra $3.97\\,\\,g$ weight is required to pull it away from water ? $\\left( {g = 980\\,cm/{s^2}} \\right)$','[{"text": "42.06 dyne/cm", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "36.02 dyne/cm", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "30.84 dyne/cm", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "47.32 dyne/cm", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'The ring is in contact with water along its inner and outer circumference. Total force on it due to surface tension will be<br>$F = T\\left( {2\\pi {r_1} + 2\\pi {r_2}} \\right)$<br>$ \\Rightarrow \\quad T = \\frac{{mg}}{{2\\pi \\left( {{r_1} + {r_2}} \\right)}}$<br>$ = \\frac{{3.97 \\times 980}}{{2 \\times 3.14 \\times (8.5 + 8.7)}}$<br>$ = 36.02\\,dyne/cm$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/26_37157_question_1.png'),
(37158,'physics',27,1380,3306,'The co-efficient of thermal expansion of a rod is temperature dependent and is given by the formula $\\alpha=a T$, where $a$ is a positive constant and $T$ in $^\\circ C$. If the length of the rod is $l$ at temperature $0^\\circ C$, then the temperature at which the length will be $2 l$ is:','[{"text": "$\\\\sqrt{\\\\dfrac{\\\\ln 2}{a}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\sqrt{\\\\dfrac{\\\\ln 4}{a}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{1}{a}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{2}{a}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'As $d l=\\alpha l d T$<br>$\\therefore \\quad \\int_{l}^{2 l} \\dfrac{d l}{l}=a \\int_{0}^{T} T d T(\\alpha=a T)$<br>$\\ln 2=a \\dfrac{T^{2}}{2} \\quad \\therefore \\quad T=\\left[\\dfrac{\\ln 4}{a}\\right]^{1 / 2}$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37159,'physics',28,1371,3283,'During an adiabatic process, the pressure of a gas is found to be proportional to the cube of its absolute temperature. The $\\gamma$ of the gas is','[{"text": "1.5", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "1.3", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "2", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "1.7", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Given that<br>$P T^{-3}=$ constant$\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\left( 1 \\right)$<br>For an adiabatic process,<br>$P T^{\\frac{\\gamma}{1-\\gamma}}=$ constant<br>Comparing (1) and (2), we get<br>$\\dfrac{\\gamma}{1-\\gamma}=-3$ or $-3+3 \\gamma=\\gamma$<br>$2 \\gamma=3$ or $\\gamma=\\dfrac{3}{2}=1.5$.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37160,'physics',23,866,1987,'Ratio of radius of gyration of a hollow sphere to that of a solid cylinder of equal mass, for moment of inertia about their diameter axis $A B$ as shown in figure is $\\sqrt{\\dfrac{8}{x}}$. The value of $x$ is','[{"text": "17", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "51", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "34", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "67", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Moment of inertia of hollow sphere,<br>$I_{S}=\\dfrac{2}{3} M R^{2}$<br>Radius of gyration of hollow sphere,<br>$\\dfrac{1}{2} m K_{S}^{2}=\\dfrac{2}{3} M R^{2}$<br>$K_{s}=\\sqrt{\\dfrac{I_{S}}{M}}=\\sqrt{\\dfrac{2}{3}} R$<br>For solid cylinder:<br>Applying theorem of parallel axes,<br>$I_{C}=I_{c m}+M d^{2}$<br><img src=\'/img/data/physics/23_37160_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' /> <br>or, $\\quad I_{C}=\\dfrac{M R^{2}}{4}+\\dfrac{M L^{2}}{12}+M\\left(\\dfrac{L}{2}\\right)^{2}$<br>or, $I_{C}=\\dfrac{M R^{2}}{4}+\\dfrac{M L^{2}}{3}$<br>or, $I_{C}=\\dfrac{M R^{2}}{4}+\\dfrac{M}{3}(4 R)^{2}$<br>or, $I_{C}=\\dfrac{67}{12} M R^{2}$<br>$\\therefore \\quad$ Radius of gyration,<br>$\\dfrac{1}{2} m K_{C}^{2}=\\dfrac{67}{12} M R^{2}$<br>$K_{C}=\\sqrt{\\dfrac{I_{C}}{M}}=\\sqrt{\\dfrac{67}{12}} R$<br>Now, $\\dfrac{K_{s}}{K_{C}}=\\sqrt{\\dfrac{2}{3} \\times \\dfrac{12}{67}}=\\sqrt{\\dfrac{8}{67}} \\quad \\therefore \\quad x=67$<br>So, option (4) is correct.',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',2024,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/23_37160_question_1.png'),
(37161,'physics',26,1138,2727,'A film of the liquid is supported in a vertical rectangular area. The top border of which is a sliding wire. An external force of $5\\,mN$ acts on the sliding wire of length $50\\,mm$ and linear mass density of $1.75 \\times {10^{ - 3}}\\,kg/m$. Find the surface tension of the liquid.','[{"text": "$0.05\\\\,\\\\,N/m$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$0.041\\\\,\\\\,N/m$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$0.083\\\\,N/m$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$0.1\\\\,\\\\,N/m$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'$$F=m g+2 T l$$<br>$F = \\lambda l\\,g + 2Tl$<br>$T = \\frac{{F - \\lambda gl}}{{2l}} = 0.041\\,N/m$<br>$F = 5mN,\\lambda  = 1.75 \\times {10^{ - 3}}\\,kg/m,l = 50\\,mm$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/26_37161_question_1.png'),
(37162,'physics',27,1380,3306,'Two rods, one of aluminium and the other made of steel, having initial length $l_{1}$ and $l_{2}$ are connected together to form a single rod of length $l_{1}+l_{2}$. The coefficients of linear expansion for aluminium and steel are $\\alpha_{a}$ and $\\alpha_{s}$ respectively. If the length of each rod increase by the same amount when their temperature is raised by $t^\\circ C$, then find the ratio $\\dfrac{l_{1}}{\\left(l_{1}+l_{2}\\right)}$','[{"text": "$\\\\dfrac{\\\\alpha_{s}}{\\\\alpha_{a}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{\\\\alpha_{a}}{\\\\alpha_{s}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{\\\\alpha_{s}}{\\\\left(\\\\alpha_{a}+\\\\alpha_{s}\\\\right)}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{\\\\alpha_{a}}{\\\\left(\\\\alpha_{a}+\\\\alpha_{s}\\\\right)}$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Given $\\Delta l_{1}=\\Delta l_{2}$ or $l_{1} \\alpha_{a} t=l_{2} \\alpha_{s} t$<br>$\\therefore \\dfrac{l_{1}}{l_{2}}=\\dfrac{\\alpha_{s}}{\\alpha_{a}} \\Rightarrow \\dfrac{l_{1}}{l_{1}+l_{2}}=\\dfrac{\\alpha_{s}}{\\alpha_{a}+\\alpha_{s}}$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37163,'physics',28,1371,3283,'For adiabatic processes $\\left(\\gamma=\\dfrac{C_{P}}{C_{V}}\\right)$','[{"text": "$T^{\\\\gamma} V=$ constant", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$P^{\\\\gamma} V=$ constant", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$T V^{\\\\gamma}=$ constant", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$T V^{\\\\gamma-1}=$ constant", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'In adiabatic process $P V^{\\gamma}=$ constant $ \\Rightarrow \\left( {\\frac{{RT}}{V}} \\right) \\cdot {V^\\gamma } \\Rightarrow T{V^{\\gamma  - 1}} = $ constant.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37165,'physics',26,1138,2728,'A frame made of a metallic wire enclosing a surface area $A$ is covered with a soap film. If the area of the frame of metallic wire is reduced by $$50 \\%$$, the energy of the soap film will be changed by','[{"text": "$$75 \\\\%$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$100 \\\\%$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$25 \\\\%$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$50 \\\\%$$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'As surface energy $$=$$ Surface tension x surface area<br>i.e., $$\\quad E=S \\times 2 A$$<br>New surface energy, $$E_{1}=S \\times 2(A / 2)=S \\times A$$<br>$$\\%$$ decreases in surface energy $$\\dfrac{E-E_{1}}{E} \\times 100$$<br>$$=\\dfrac{2 S A-S A}{2 S A} \\times 100=50 \\%$$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37253,'physics',27,1381,3314,'If earth suddenly stops rotating about its own axis, the increase in it\'s temperature will be:','[{"text": "$\\\\dfrac{R^{2} \\\\omega^{2}}{5 J_{S}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{R^{2} \\\\omega^{2}}{J_{S}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{R m \\\\omega^{2}}{5 J_{S}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "None of these", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'$W=J Q \\Rightarrow \\dfrac{1}{2} I \\omega^{2}=J(M s \\Delta \\theta)$<br>$\\Rightarrow \\dfrac{1}{2}\\left(\\dfrac{2}{5} M R^{2}\\right) \\omega^{2}=J(M s \\Delta \\theta) \\Rightarrow \\Delta \\theta=\\dfrac{1}{5} \\dfrac{R^{2} \\omega^{2}}{J S}$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37166,'physics',27,1380,3307,'A clock with an iron pendulum keeps correct time at $15^\\circ C$. What will be the error in second per day, if the room temperature is $20^\\circ C$? (The coefficient of linear expansion of iron is $0.000012^{\\circ} C^{-1}$.)','[{"text": "$6.2\\\\,s$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2.6\\\\,s$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$3.1\\\\,s$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1.3\\\\,s$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Here, $\\Delta T = 20 - 15 = 5^\\circ C$<br>$\\alpha  = 0.000012^\\circ {C^{ - 1}} = 12 \\times {10^{ - 6}}\\,^\\circ {C^{ - 1}}$<br>Time lost per day $ = \\frac{1}{2}\\alpha (\\Delta T) \\times 86400\\,s$<br>$ = \\frac{1}{2} \\times 12 \\times {10^{ - 6}} \\times 5 \\times 86400\\,s = 2.560\\,s$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37167,'physics',28,1371,3283,'In an adiabatic compression the volume of an ideal gas decreases to ${\\frac{1}{8}^{th}}$ of its initial value. Its final pressure becomes $\\left(\\gamma=\\dfrac{5}{3}\\right)$','[{"text": "8 times the initial pressure", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "16 times the initial pressure", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "32 times the initial pressure", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "None of the above", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'$P_{1} V_{1}^{\\gamma}=P_{2} V_{2}^{\\gamma}$<br>$P_{2}=P_{1}\\left(\\dfrac{V_{1}}{V_{2}}\\right)^{\\gamma}=P_{1}\\left(\\dfrac{V}{V / 8}\\right)^{\\gamma}=P_{1}\\left(2^{3}\\right)^{5 / 3}=32 P_{1}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37169,'physics',26,1138,2730,'Two soap bubbles $A$ and $B$ are kept in a closed chamber where the air is maintained at pressure $8\\,N{m^{ - 2}}$. The radii of bubbles $A$ and $B$ are $2\\,\\,cm$ and $3\\,\\,cm$ respectively. Surface tension of the soap-water used to make bubbles is $0.004\\,N{m^{ - 1}}$.<br>Find the ratio $$\\dfrac{n_{B}}{n_{A}}$$, where $$n_{A}$$ and $$n_{B}$$ are the number of moles of air bubbles $A$ and $B$, respectively. [Neglect the effect of gravity]','[{"text": "4.6", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "3.2", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "10", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "2.4", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Although not given in the equation, but we have to assume that temperatures of $A$ and $B$ are same.<br> <img src=\'/img/data/physics/26_37169_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' /> <br>$\\frac{{{n_B}}}{{{n_A}}} = \\frac{{{p_B}{V_B}/RT}}{{{p_A}{V_A}/RT}} = \\frac{{{p_B}{V_B}}}{{{p_A}{V_A}}}$<br>$ = \\frac{{\\left( {p + 4T/{r_B}} \\right) \\times 4/3\\pi {{\\left( {{r_B}} \\right)}^3}}}{{\\left( {p + 4T/{r_A}} \\right) \\times 4/3\\pi {{\\left( {{r_A}} \\right)}^3}}}\\,\\,\\,{\\mkern 1mu} {\\kern 1pt} (\\,T = {\\rm{surfacetension}})$<br>Substituting the values, we get<br>$$\\dfrac{n_{B}}{n_{A}}=3.2$$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37170,'physics',27,1380,3307,'A clock with a metal pendulum beating seconds keeps correct time at $0^\\circ C$. If it loses $12.5\\,s$ a day at $25^\\circ C$, the coefficient of linear expansion of metal of pendulum is','[{"text": "$\\\\frac{1}{{86400}}{\\\\rm{per}}\\\\,^\\\\circ C$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{1}{{43200}}{\\\\mathop{\\\\rm per}\\\\nolimits} \\\\,^\\\\circ C$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{1}{{14400}}{\\\\mathop{\\\\rm per}\\\\nolimits} \\\\,^\\\\circ C$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{{\\\\rm{1}}}{{{\\\\rm{28800}}}}{\\\\rm{per}}\\\\,\\\\,^\\\\circ C$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Loss of time due to heating a pendulum is given as<br>$\\Delta T = \\frac{1}{2}\\alpha \\Delta \\theta T$<br>$ \\Rightarrow \\,\\,\\,{\\mkern 1mu} {\\kern 1pt} 12.5 = \\frac{1}{2} \\times \\alpha  \\times (25 - 0)\\,^\\circ C \\times 86400$<br>$ \\Rightarrow \\,\\,\\,{\\mkern 1mu} {\\kern 1pt} \\alpha  = \\frac{1}{{86400}}^\\circ C$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37171,'physics',28,1371,3283,'The gas law $\\dfrac{P V}{T}=$ constant for a given gas is true for','[{"text": "Isothermal change only", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Adiabatic change only", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both isothermal & adiabatic", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Neither isothermal nor adiabatic", "isCorrect": false}]',2,NULL,NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37173,'physics',26,1138,2731,'A vertical glass capillary tube open at both ends contains some ${H_2}O$. Which of the following shapes for water is correct?','[{"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Resultant of weight of liquid and surface tension makes the orientation of liquid in the pipe as in option (3).',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37174,'physics',27,1380,3306,'A uniform metal rod is used as a bar pendulum. If the room temperature rises by $10^\\circ C,$ and the coefficient of linear expansion of the metal of the rod is $2 \\times 10^{-6}$ per $^\\circ C$, the period of the pendulum will have percentage increase of','[{"text": "$-2 \\\\times 10^{-3}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$-1 \\\\times 10^{-3}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2 \\\\times 10^{-3}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1 \\\\times 10^{-3}$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Fractional change in period<br>$\\begin{aligned}\\dfrac{\\Delta T}{T} & =\\dfrac{1}{2} \\dfrac{\\Delta l}{l} \\\\\\Rightarrow & \\dfrac{\\Delta T}{T}=\\dfrac{1}{2} \\alpha \\Delta \\theta=\\dfrac{1}{2} \\times 2 \\times 10^{-6} \\times 10=10^{-5}\\end{aligned}$<br>$\\%$ change $=\\dfrac{\\Delta T}{T} \\times 100=10^{-5} \\times 100=10^{-3} \\%$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37175,'physics',28,1371,3283,'A monoatomic gas is compressed adiabatically till its temperature is doubled. The ratio of its final volume to initial volume will be:','[{"text": "More than $1 / 2$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1 / 2$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Between 1 and 2", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Less than $1 / 2$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'For adiabatic process<br>$\\begin{aligned}& T_{1} V_{1}^{\\gamma-1}=T_{2} V_{2}^{\\gamma-1} \\\\& \\dfrac{T_{2}}{T_{1}}=\\left(\\dfrac{V_{1}}{V_{2}}\\right)^{\\gamma-1}=2 \\Rightarrow\\left(\\dfrac{V_{2}}{V_{1}}\\right)^{\\gamma-1}=\\dfrac{1}{2} \\\\& \\Rightarrow \\dfrac{V_{2}}{V_{1}}=\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right)^{\\frac{1}{\\gamma-1}} < \\dfrac{1}{2}\\left(\\gamma=\\dfrac{5}{3}\\right)\\end{aligned}$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37186,'physics',27,1380,3306,'Three rods of equal length $l$ are joined to form an equilateral triangle $P Q R$. $O$  is the mid point of $P Q$. Distance $O R$ remains same for small change in temperature. Coefficient of linear expansion for $P R$ ad $R Q$ is same, i.e., $\\alpha_{2}$ but that for $P Q$ is $\\alpha_{1}$. Then','[{"text": "$\\\\alpha_{2}=4 \\\\alpha_{1}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\alpha_{2}=3 \\\\alpha_{1}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\alpha_{1}=4 \\\\alpha_{2}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\alpha_{1}=3 \\\\alpha_{2}$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'<br>From the right angle triangle $PRO$<br>$(O R)^{2}=(P R)^{2}-(P O)^{2}=l^{2}-\\left(\\dfrac{l}{2}\\right)^{2}$<br>After expansion<br>$\\begin{aligned}& (\\text { OR })^{2}=\\left[l\\left(1+\\alpha_{2} t\\right)\\right]^{2}-\\left[\\dfrac{1}{2} l\\left(1+\\alpha_{1} t\\right)\\right]^{2} \\\\& l^{2}-\\dfrac{l^{2}}{4}=l^{2}\\left(1+\\alpha_{2}^{2} t^{2}+2 \\alpha_{2} t\\right)-\\dfrac{l^{2}}{4}\\left(1+\\alpha_{1}^{2} t^{2}+2 \\alpha_{1} t\\right)\\end{aligned}$<br>Neglecting $\\alpha_{2}^{2} t^{2}$ and $\\alpha_{1}^{2} t^{2}$<br>$0=l^{2}\\left(2 \\alpha_{2} t\\right)-\\dfrac{l^{2}}{4}\\left(2 \\alpha_{1} t\\right) \\Rightarrow 2 \\alpha_{2}=\\dfrac{2 \\alpha_{1}}{4} \\Rightarrow \\alpha_{1}=4 \\alpha_{2}$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/27_37186_question_1.png'),
(37177,'physics',26,1138,2731,'A glass capillary sealed at the upper end is of length $0.11\\,\\,m$ and internal diameter $2 \\times {10^{ - 5}}\\,m.$ The tube is immersed vertically into a liquid of surface tension $5.06 \\times {10^{ - 2}}\\,N/m$. To what length the capillary has to be immeresed so that the liquid level inside and outside the capillary becomes the same.','[{"text": "$2\\\\,\\\\,cm$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$5\\\\,\\\\,cm$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1\\\\,\\\\,cm$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$3\\\\,\\\\,cm$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Let $$P_{0}$$ be atmospheric pressure and $$P$$ the pressure of air within the sealed tube. Then for equilibrium pressure just below the meniscus should be equal to atmospheric pressure because levels of water inside and outside the tube is same.<br><img src=\'/img/data/physics/26_37177_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' /> <br>$$\\text { i.e., }\\left(P-\\dfrac{2 T}{r}\\right)=P_{0} \\text { or } P=P_{0}+\\dfrac{2 T}{r}$$<br>If $L = 0.11\\,m$ is the length of tube and $x$ is the length of immersed part, then from Boyle\'s law<br>$$\\begin{aligned}& P_{1} V_{1}=P_{2} V_{2} \\\\& P_{0} L A=\\left(P_{0}+\\dfrac{2 T}{r}\\right)(L-x) A\\end{aligned}$$<br>where $A$ is the cross-sectional area of tube,<br>i.e., $$\\quad P_{0} \\times L=\\left(P_{0}+\\dfrac{2 T}{r}\\right)(L-x)$$<br>$$P_{0} \\times L=P_{0}(L-x)+\\dfrac{2 T}{r}(L-x)$$<br>i.e., $$\\quad P_{0} x=\\dfrac{2 T}{r}(L-x)$$<br>$$1 \\times 10^{5} \\times x=\\dfrac{2 \\times 5.06 \\times 10^{-2}}{1 \\times 10^{-5}}(0.11-x)$$<br>Solving for $$x$$, we get $x = 0.01\\,m$<br>Length of capillary to be immersed $ = 0.01\\,m$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/26_37177_question_1.png'),
(37178,'physics',27,1380,3306,'If two rods of length $L$ and $2\\,L$ having coefficient of linear expansion $\\alpha$ and $2 \\alpha$ respectively are connected so that total length becomes $3\\,L$, the average coefficient of linear expansion of the composition rod equals:','[{"text": "$\\\\dfrac{3}{2} \\\\alpha$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{5}{2} \\\\alpha$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{5}{3} \\\\alpha$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "None of these", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Effective linear coefficient<br>$\\begin{gathered}\\Delta L=\\Delta L_{1}+\\Delta L_{2} \\\\L \\alpha \\Delta T=\\alpha_{1} L_{1} \\Delta T+\\alpha_{2} L_{2} \\Delta T \\\\\\alpha=\\dfrac{L_{1} \\alpha_{1}+L_{2} \\alpha_{2}}{L_{1}+L_{2}}=\\dfrac{L(\\alpha)+2 L(2 \\alpha)}{3 L}\\end{gathered}$<br>$ = \\frac{{5\\alpha }}{3}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37181,'physics',26,1138,2732,'Two vertical parallel plates are partially submerged in water. The distance between the plates is $d$ and their length is $$l$$. Assuming that the wetting is complete, find the force of their mutual attraction','[{"text": "$$\\\\dfrac{T^{2} l}{4 \\\\rho g d^{2}}$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$\\\\dfrac{T^{2} l^{2}}{\\\\rho g d^{3}}$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$\\\\dfrac{T^{2} l}{g d^{2}}$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$\\\\dfrac{2 T^{2} l}{\\\\rho g d^{2}}$$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'The plates attract due to pressure difference between water lying in between the plates and outside the plates. The force on a plate is $$F=\\left(P_{0}-P_{a v g}\\right) l h$$<br>$$P_{0}-$$ atmospheric pressure<br>$$P_{\\text {avg }}$$ - average pressure that acts on the plate by the liquid<br>$$P_{\\text {avg }}=\\dfrac{P_{1}+P_{2}}{2}$$<br>$$P_{2}-$$ Pressure on the surface of the water<br>$$P_{1}-$$ Pressure at the highest point of the liquid<br>${P_0} - {P_1} = \\frac{T}{r} = {\\text{ }}({\\text{cylindrical}}\\,{\\text{surface}}){\\text{ }}$<br>$ \\Rightarrow {P_1} = {P_0} - \\frac{{2T}}{d}{P_2} = {P_0}$<br>${P_{avg{\\text{ }}}} = \\frac{{\\left( {{P_0}} \\right) + \\left( {{P_0} - 2T/d} \\right)}}{2} = {P_0} - \\frac{T}{d}$<br>$F = \\left( {{P_0} - \\left( {{P_0} - \\frac{T}{d}} \\right)} \\right)lh = \\frac{{Tlh}}{d}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,(1)$<br>The height of the liquid is given by<br><img src=\'/img/data/physics/26_37181_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' /> <br>${F_{ST}} = {F_g}$<br>$T(2l) = \\rho \\left( {dlh} \\right)g$<br>$h = \\frac{{2T}}{{\\rho gd}}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,(2)$<br>From (1) & (2) $$\\quad F=\\dfrac{2 T^{2} l}{\\rho g d^{2}}$$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/26_37181_question_1.png'),
(37182,'physics',27,1380,3306,'A rail track made of steel having length $10\\,cm$ is clamped on a railway line at its two ends (figure). On a summer day due to rise in temperature by $20^\\circ C$. It is deformed as shown in figure. Find $x$ (displacement of the centre) if ${\\alpha _{{\\rm{steel }}}} = 1.2 \\times {10^{ - 5}}/^\\circ C$','[{"text": "$15\\\\sqrt {30}  \\\\times {10^{ - 3}}\\\\,cm$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$20\\\\sqrt {30}  \\\\times {10^{ - 3}}\\\\,cm$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$25\\\\sqrt {30}  \\\\times {10^{ - 3}}\\\\,cm$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$10\\\\sqrt {30}  \\\\times {10^{ - 3}}\\\\,cm$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Consider the diagram<br><img src=\'/img/data/physics/27_37182_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' /> <br>Applying Pythagorean theorem in right angle triangle in figure<br>${\\left( {\\frac{{L + \\Delta L}}{2}} \\right)^2} = {\\left( {\\frac{L}{2}} \\right)^2} + {x^2}$<br>$ \\Rightarrow \\,\\,\\,\\,x = \\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt {\\left( {{L^2} + \\Delta {L^2} + 2L\\Delta L} \\right) - {L^2}} $<br>$ = \\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt {\\left( {\\Delta {L^2} + 2L\\Delta L} \\right)} \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\left( 1 \\right)$<br>If $\\Delta L$ is very small, then, neglecting $\\left(\\Delta L^{2}\\right)$, we get<br>$x = \\frac{1}{2} \\times \\sqrt {2L\\Delta L} \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\left( 2 \\right)$<br>But $\\quad \\Delta L=L \\alpha \\Delta t$<br>Substituting value of $\\Delta L$ in Eq. (1) from Eq. (2)<br>$x = \\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt {2L \\times L\\alpha \\Delta t}  = \\frac{1}{2}L\\sqrt {2\\alpha \\Delta t} $<br>$ = \\frac{{10}}{2} \\times \\sqrt {2 \\times 1.2 \\times {{10}^{ - 5}} \\times 20} $<br>$ = 5 \\times \\sqrt {4 \\times 1.2 \\times {{10}^{ - 4}}}  = 20\\sqrt {30}  \\times {10^{ - 3}}\\,cm$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/27_37182_question_1.png'),
(37189,'physics',26,1133,2708,'A smooth gate is kept in equilibrium by applying a horizontal force. What is the torque exerted by the liquid pressure force about $O$.','[{"text": "$$\\\\dfrac{\\\\rho g b H^{3}}{2}$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$\\\\dfrac{\\\\rho g b H^{3}}{6}$$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Zero", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$\\\\dfrac{\\\\rho g b H^{3}}{4}$$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'The small force exerted on a strip of width dy and length $$b$$ is<br>$dF = \\rho ygdyb$<br><img src=\'/img/data/physics/26_37189_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' /> <br>The torque about $O$ is<br>${\\tau _{{\\text{due to liquid }}}} = \\int_0^H y \\rho g \\cdot dy\\,b(H - y)$<br>Where $$b$$ is the breadth of the plate<br>$$\\tau=\\rho g b\\left(\\dfrac{H y^{2}}{2}-\\dfrac{y^{3}}{3}\\right)_{0}^{H}=\\dfrac{\\rho g b H^{3}}{6}$$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/26_37189_question_1.png'),
(37190,'physics',27,1380,3306,'A steel meter scale is to be ruled so that millimeter intervals are accurate within about $5 \\times {10^{ - 5}}\\,mm$ at a certain temperature. The maximum temperature variation allowable during the ruling is (Coefficient of linear expansion of steel $ = 10 \\times {10^{ - 6}}\\,{K^{ - 1}})$','[{"text": "$5^\\\\circ C$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$2^\\\\circ C$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$10^\\\\circ C$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$7^\\\\circ C$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'As we know that coefficient of linear expansion is<br>$\\alpha  = \\frac{{\\Delta L}}{{{L_0}\\Delta \\theta }} \\Rightarrow \\Delta \\theta  = \\frac{{\\Delta L}}{{\\alpha {L_0}}} = \\frac{{5 \\times {{10}^{ - 5}}}}{{10 \\times {{10}^{ - 6}} \\times 1}} = 5^\\circ C.$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37193,'physics',26,1133,2708,'A square of size $$1 m \\times 1 m$$ is hinged at its midpoint. A fluid of density $$\\rho$$ fills the space to the left of the gate. The minimum force $$F$$ required to hold the gate stationary is','[{"text": "$$\\\\dfrac{\\\\rho g}{3}$$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$$\\\\dfrac{\\\\rho g}{2}$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$\\\\dfrac{\\\\rho g}{6}$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$\\\\dfrac{\\\\rho g}{8}$$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'At $a$ depth $$y$$ from the surface of the fluid, the net force acting on the gate element of width $$d y$$ is<br>$$d F=\\left(P_{0}+\\rho g y-P_{0}\\right) \\times 1 d y=\\rho g y d y$$<br><img src=\'/img/data/physics/26_37193_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' /> <br>Torque of this force about the hinge is<br>$d\\tau  = \\rho gydy\\,y$<br>Torque experienced by the gate due to liquid is<br>$$\\begin{aligned}& \\tau=\\int_{0}^{1} \\rho g y^{2} d y \\\\& =\\left[\\dfrac{\\rho g y^{3}}{3}\\right]_{0}^{1}=\\dfrac{\\rho g}{3}\\end{aligned}$$<br>As the torque is balanced by the external force $F$<br>$$\\rho \\dfrac{g}{3}=F(1) \\Rightarrow F=\\dfrac{\\rho g}{3}$$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/26_37193_question_1.png'),
(37194,'physics',27,1380,3307,'A pendulum clock loses $12\\,s$ a day if the temperature is $40^\\circ C$ and gains $4\\,s$ a day if the temperature is $20^\\circ C$. The temperature at which the clock will show correct time, and the coefficient of linear expansion $(\\alpha)$ of the metal of the pendulum shaft are respectively :','[{"text": "$60^\\\\circ C;\\\\alpha  = 1.85 \\\\times {10^{ - 4}}/\\\\,^\\\\circ C$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$30^\\\\circ C;\\\\alpha  = 1.85 \\\\times {10^{ - 3}}/\\\\,^\\\\circ C$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$55^\\\\circ C;\\\\alpha  = 1.85 \\\\times {10^{ - 2}}/\\\\,^\\\\circ C$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$25^\\\\circ C;\\\\alpha  = 1.85 \\\\times {10^{ - 5}}/\\\\,^\\\\circ C$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'According to given conditions<br>$86412 = 2\\pi \\sqrt {\\frac{{{\\ell _{40}}}}{g}} \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\left( 1 \\right)$<br>$86396 = 2\\pi \\sqrt {\\frac{{{\\ell _{20}}}}{g}} \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\left( 2 \\right)$<br>$86400 = 2\\pi \\sqrt {\\frac{\\ell }{g}} \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\left( 3 \\right)$<br>From equations (1) and (3)<br>$12 = \\frac{{2\\pi }}{{\\sqrt g }}\\left[ {\\sqrt {{\\ell _{40}}}  - \\sqrt \\ell  } \\right]\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\left( 4 \\right)$<br>From equations (2) & (3)<br>and $\\,{\\mkern 1mu} {\\kern 1pt} 4 = \\frac{{2\\pi }}{{\\sqrt g }}\\left[ {\\sqrt \\ell   - \\sqrt {{\\ell _{20}}} } \\right]\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\left( 5 \\right)$<br>On dividing (4) and (5)<br>$\\begin{aligned}& 3=\\dfrac{\\left[\\sqrt{l_{40}}-\\sqrt{l}\\right]}{\\sqrt{l}-\\sqrt{l_{20}}} \\\\& =\\dfrac{[\\sqrt{l(1+\\alpha \\Delta \\theta)}-\\sqrt{l}]}{\\sqrt{l}-\\sqrt{l+\\alpha \\Delta \\theta}} \\\\& =\\dfrac{\\sqrt{1+\\alpha(40-\\theta)}-1}{1-\\sqrt{1+\\alpha(\\theta-20)}} \\\\& \\Rightarrow 3=\\dfrac{40-\\theta}{\\theta-20} \\text { (by Binomial theorem) } \\\\& \\Rightarrow \\theta=25^{\\circ}\\end{aligned}$<br>From eq\'s (1) and (3)<br>$\\frac{{86412}}{{86400}} = \\sqrt {1 + \\alpha 15}  \\Rightarrow \\alpha  = 1.85 \\times {10^{ - 5}}/^\\circ C$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37196,'physics',23,870,2001,'A disc of radius $R$ and mass $M$ is rolling horizontally without slipping with speed $v$. It then moves up an inclined smooth surface as shown in figure. The maximum height that the disc can go up the incline is','[{"text": "$\\\\dfrac{3}{4} \\\\dfrac{v^{2}}{g}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{v^{2}}{g}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{2}{3} \\\\dfrac{v^{2}}{g}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{1}{2} \\\\dfrac{v^{2}}{g}$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Applying the conservation of energy at point $(A)$ and $(B)$, (friction is zero on the inclined surface)<br>$\\begin{aligned}& E_{A}=E_{B} \\\\& \\dfrac{1}{2} M v^{2}+\\dfrac{1}{2} I \\omega^{2}=M g h+\\dfrac{1}{2} I \\omega^{2} \\\\& \\dfrac{1}{2} M v^{2}=M g h\\end{aligned}$<br><img src=\'/img/data/physics/23_37196_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' /> <br>$\\Rightarrow \\dfrac{3}{2} M v^{2}=M g h$<br>So, maximum height, $h=\\dfrac{1 v^{2}}{2 g}$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',2024,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/23_37196_question_1.png'),
(37197,'physics',26,1133,2706,'A container shown in figure contains a liquid to a depth $H$, and of density $$\\rho$$. The gauge pressure at point $P$ is:','[{"text": "$$\\\\dfrac{\\\\rho g H}{2+P_{0}}$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$\\\\dfrac{\\\\rho g H}{2}$$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$$\\\\dfrac{\\\\rho g H}{2 \\\\cos \\\\theta}$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$\\\\dfrac{\\\\rho g H \\\\cos \\\\theta}{2}$$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Gauge pressure $$=h \\rho g$$<br>$$h=\\dfrac{H}{2}$$<br>$$\\therefore$$ Gauge pressure is $$\\dfrac{H \\rho g}{2}$$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/26_37197_question_1.png'),
(37198,'physics',27,1380,3310,'The coefficient of linear expansion of crystal in one direction is $\\alpha_{1}$ and that in every direction perpendicular to it is $\\alpha_{2}$. The coefficient of cubical expansion is','[{"text": "$\\\\alpha_{1}+\\\\alpha_{2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2 \\\\alpha_{1}+\\\\alpha_{2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\alpha_{1}+2 \\\\alpha_{2}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "None of these", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'$V=V_{0}(1+\\gamma \\Delta \\theta)$<br>$\\begin{aligned}L^{3} & =L_{0}\\left(1+\\alpha_{1} \\Delta \\theta\\right) L_{0}^{2}\\left(1+\\alpha_{2} \\Delta \\theta\\right)^{2} \\\\& =L_{0}^{3}\\left(1+\\alpha_{1} \\Delta \\theta\\right)\\left(1+\\alpha_{2} \\Delta \\theta\\right)^{2}\\end{aligned}$<br>Since $L_{0}^{3}=V_{0}$ and $L^{3}=V$<br>$\\begin{aligned}\\text { Hence } & 1+\\gamma \\Delta \\theta=\\left(1+\\alpha_{1} \\Delta \\theta\\right)\\left(1+\\alpha_{2} \\Delta \\theta\\right)^{2} \\\\\\cong & \\left(1+\\alpha_{1} \\Delta \\theta\\right)\\left(1+2 \\alpha_{2} \\Delta \\theta\\right) \\\\\\cong & \\left(1+\\alpha_{1} \\Delta \\theta+2 \\alpha_{2} \\Delta \\theta\\right)\\end{aligned}$<br>$\\Rightarrow \\gamma=\\alpha_{1}+2 \\alpha_{2}$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37201,'physics',26,1133,2708,'In the figure shown water is filled in a symmetrical container. Four pistons of equal area $A$ are used at the four opening to keep the water in equilibrium. Now an additional force $$F$$ is applied at each piston. The increase in the pressure at the centre of the container due to this addition is','[{"text": "$$\\\\dfrac{2 F}{A}$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$\\\\dfrac{F}{A}$$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "0", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$\\\\dfrac{4 F}{A}$$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'If $$F$$ is additional force applied then pressure increases at every piston by $$\\dfrac{F}{A}$$ which will be carried out through out the liquid since liquid is in static state.',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/26_37201_question_1.png'),
(37202,'physics',27,1380,3306,'A circular disc made of iron is rotated about its axis at a constant velocity $\\omega$. Calculate the percentage change in the linear speed of a particle of the rim as the disc is slowly heated from $20^\\circ C$ to $50^\\circ C$ keeping the angular velocity constant. (Coefficient of linear expansion of iron $\\left. { = 1.2 \\times {{10}^{ - 5}}/^\\circ C} \\right)$.','[{"text": "$3.6 \\\\times 10^{-2}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$3.2 \\\\times 10^{-1}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1.6 \\\\times 10^{-2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2.4 \\\\times 10^{-2}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'The linear velocity<br>$v=r \\omega$<br>$\\dfrac{d v}{v}=\\dfrac{d r}{r} \\quad(\\omega=$ constant $)$<br>$\\dfrac{d v}{v}=\\dfrac{r \\alpha d T}{r}=\\alpha d T$<br>$\\dfrac{d v}{v} \\times 100=\\alpha d T \\times 100=3.6 \\times 10^{-2} \\%$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37205,'physics',27,1380,3310,'An external pressure $P$ is applied on a cube at $0^\\circ C$ so that it is equally compressed from all sides. $K$ is the bulk modulus of the material of the cube and $\\alpha$ is its coefficient of linear expansion. Suppose we want to bring the cube to its original size of heating. The temperature should be raised by:','[{"text": "$\\\\dfrac{3 \\\\alpha}{P K}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$3 P K \\\\alpha$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{P}{3 \\\\alpha K}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{P}{\\\\alpha K}$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'As we know, Bulk modulus<br>$K = \\frac{{\\Delta P}}{{\\left( {\\frac{{ - \\Delta V}}{V}} \\right)}} \\Rightarrow \\frac{{\\Delta V}}{V} = \\frac{P}{K}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\left( 1 \\right)$<br>$\\frac{{\\Delta V}}{V} = \\gamma \\Delta t\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\left( 2 \\right)$<br>$\\therefore \\frac{P}{K} = \\gamma \\Delta t$<br>The temperature that should be rised to make same strain is $\\Delta t=\\dfrac{P}{\\gamma K}=\\dfrac{P}{3 \\alpha K}$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37206,'physics',26,1133,2707,'A given shaped glass tube having uniform cross section is filled with water and is mounted on a rotatable shaft as shown in figure. If the tube is rotated with a constant angular velocity $$\\omega$$ then','[{"text": "Water levels in both sections $$A$$ and $$B$$ go up", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Water level in Section $$A$$ goes up and that in $$B$$ comes down", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Water level in Section $$A$$ comes down and that in $$B$$ it goes up", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Water levels remains same in both sections", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Pressure due to pseudo force causes the rise in level in both the limbs.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/26_37206_question_1.png'),
(37209,'physics',27,1380,3312,'A glass flask of volume one litre at $0^\\circ C$ is filled, level full of mercury at this temperature. The flask and mercury are now heated to $100^\\circ C$. How much mercury will spill out, if coefficient of volume expansion of mercury is $1.82 \\times {10^{ - 4}}/^\\circ C$ and linear expansion of glass is $0.1 \\times {10^{ - 4}}/^\\circ C$ respectively?','[{"text": "$15.2\\\\,cc$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$21.2\\\\,cc$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1.52\\\\,cc$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2.21\\\\,cc$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Due to volume expansion of both liquid and vessel, the change in volume of liquid relative to container is given by<br>$\\Delta V=V_{0}\\left[\\gamma_{L}-\\gamma_{g}\\right] \\Delta T$<br>Given ${V_0} = 1000\\,cc,{\\alpha _g} = 0.1 \\times {10^{ - 4}}/^\\circ C$<br>$\\therefore {\\gamma _g} = 3{\\alpha _g} = 3 \\times 0.1 \\times {10^{ - 4}}/^\\circ C = 0.3 \\times {10^{ - 4}}/^\\circ C$<br>$\\therefore \\quad \\Delta V=1000\\left[1.82 \\times 10^{-4}-0.3 \\times 10^{-4}\\right] \\times 100$<br>$=15.2 c c$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37210,'physics',26,1133,2707,'A tank with base area $A$ is filled with a liquid to height $H$. The tank is accelerated horizontally with acceleration $$a$$ as shown in the figure. If a small hole is made at point $A$, then it is observed that the liquid does not come out of the tank. the magnitude of acceleration should be:','[{"text": "$$\\\\dfrac{H}{L} g$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$\\\\dfrac{L}{H} g$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$\\\\dfrac{2 H}{L} g$$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "None of these", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'The liquid will not come out of the tank at the point $$A$$ if the free surface passes through the point itself.<br><img src=\'/img/data/physics/26_37210_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' /> <br>Note that volume of water in the tank remains constant.<br>$$\\begin{gathered}V_{i}=V_{f} \\\\A H=\\dfrac{1}{2}(A h) \\Rightarrow h=2 H \\\\\\therefore \\tan \\theta=\\dfrac{h}{L}=\\dfrac{a}{g} \\\\\\Rightarrow a=\\left(\\dfrac{2 H}{L}\\right) g\\end{gathered}$$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/26_37210_question_1.png'),
(37213,'physics',27,1380,3312,'The figure shows a glass cylinder (linear coefficient of expansion is \\(\\alpha\\) ) completely filled with a liquid of volume expansion co-efficient \\(\\gamma\\). On heating length of the liquid column does not change. Choose the correct relation between \\(\\gamma\\) and \\(\\alpha\\)<div class="matching-question"></div>','[{"text": "$\\\\gamma=2 \\\\alpha$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\gamma=\\\\alpha$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\gamma=\\\\dfrac{\\\\alpha}{3}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\gamma=3 \\\\alpha$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Let $A_{0}$ is the base area of the container initially. After increasing the temperature its base area is becomes $A$.<br><img src=\'/img/data/physics/27_37213_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' /> <br>The area of the base after increasing temperature is<br>$A = {A_0}(1 + 2\\alpha \\Delta T)\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\left( 1 \\right)$<br>The volume of the liquid after increasing temperature is<br>$V = {V_0}(1 + \\gamma \\Delta T)$<br>$A{h_0} = {A_0}{h_0}(1 + \\gamma \\Delta T)$<br>$A = {A_0}(1 + \\gamma \\Delta T)\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\left( 2 \\right)$<br>From eq\'s (1) & (2)<br>$\\gamma=2 \\alpha$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/27_37213_question_1.png'),
(37214,'physics',26,1133,2710,'A candle of diameter $$d$$ is floating on a liquid in a cylindrical container of diameter $$D(D>>d)$$ as shown in figure. If it is burning at the rate of $2\\,cm/$ hour then the top of the candle will','[{"text": "Remain at the same height", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Fall at the rate of $1\\\\,cm/hour$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Fall at the rate of $2\\\\,cm/hour$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Go up the rate of $1\\\\,cm/hour$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Let $$x$$ is the length of the candle above the water surface at equilibrium.<br>Given that $$h=2 L, x=L$$<br>$ \\Rightarrow \\quad h = 2x$<br>$\\frac{{dh}}{{dt}} = 2\\frac{{dx}}{{dt}}$<br>$\\frac{{dh}}{{dt}} = 2\\frac{{cm}}{{hr}}$<br>$ \\Rightarrow \\frac{{dx}}{{dt}} = 1\\frac{{cm}}{{hr}}$<br>So the top of the candle falls at $1\\,cm/hr.$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/26_37214_question_1.png'),
(37217,'physics',27,1380,3312,'The coefficient of apparent expansion of mercury in a glass vessel is $153 \\times {10^{ - 6}}/^\\circ C$  and in a steel vessel is $144 \\times {10^{ - 6}}/^\\circ C$. If $\\alpha$ for steel is $12 \\times {10^{ - 6}}/^\\circ C$, then $\\alpha$ that of glass is :','[{"text": "$9 \\\\times {10^{ - 6}}/\\\\,^\\\\circ C$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$6 \\\\times {10^{ - 6}}/\\\\,^\\\\circ C$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$36 \\\\times {10^{ - 6}}/^\\\\circ C$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$27 \\\\times {10^{ - 6}}/^\\\\circ C$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'$\\gamma_{a g}=\\gamma_{r}-3 \\alpha_{g}\\left(\\gamma_{a g}-\\right.$ apparent expansion of glass)<br>$\\gamma_{a s}=\\gamma_{r}-3 \\alpha_{s} \\quad\\left(\\gamma_{a s}-\\right.$ apparent expansion of steel)<br>From the above two eq\'s<br>${\\gamma _{ag}} - {\\gamma _{as}} = 3\\left( {{\\alpha _s} - {\\alpha _g}} \\right) \\Rightarrow {\\alpha _g} = 9 \\times {10^{ - 6}}/^\\circ C$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37218,'physics',26,1133,2710,'A wooden ball of density $$\\rho$$ is immersed in water of density $$\\rho_{0}$$ to depth $h$ and then released. The height $H$ above the surface of water upto which the ball jump out of water is','[{"text": "$$h$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Zero", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$\\\\left(\\\\dfrac{\\\\rho_{0}}{\\\\rho}-1\\\\right) h$$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$$\\\\dfrac{\\\\rho_{0} h}{\\\\rho}$$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Let $$V$$ be the volume of wooden ball. The mass of ball is $$m=V \\rho$$.<br>Upward acceleration,<br>$$\\begin{aligned}a & =\\dfrac{\\text { upward thrust }- \\text { weight of ball }}{\\text { mass of ball }} \\\\& =\\dfrac{V \\rho_{0} g-V \\rho g}{V \\rho}=\\dfrac{\\left(\\rho_{0}-\\rho\\right) g}{\\rho}\\end{aligned}$$<br>If $$v$$ is the velocity of ball on reaching the surface after being released at depth $$h$$ is <br>$$v=\\sqrt{2 a s}=\\left[2\\left(\\dfrac{\\rho_{0}-\\rho}{\\rho}\\right) g h\\right]^{1 / 2}$$<br>If $$h^{\\prime}$$ is the vertical distance reached by ball above the surface of water, then<br>$$\\begin{aligned}& h^{\\prime}=\\dfrac{v^{2}}{2 g}=\\dfrac{2\\left(\\rho_{0}-\\rho\\right)}{\\rho} g h \\times \\dfrac{1}{2 g} \\\\& =\\left(\\dfrac{\\rho_{0}-\\rho}{\\rho}\\right) h=\\left(\\dfrac{\\rho_{0}}{\\rho}-1\\right) h\\end{aligned}$$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37243,'physics',28,1369,3279,'The first law of thermodynamics is based on the law of conservation of','[{"text": "Energy", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Mass", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Momentum", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Pressure", "isCorrect": false}]',0,NULL,NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37221,'physics',27,1380,3311,'If $d_{1}$ and $d_{2}$ are the densities of a liquid at ${t_1}^\\circ C$ and ${t_2}^\\circ C,$ then $\\dfrac{d_{1}}{d_{2}}$ is $(\\gamma=$ coefficient of real expansion)','[{"text": "$\\\\dfrac{1+\\\\gamma t_{1}}{1+\\\\gamma t_{2}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{1-\\\\gamma t_{1}}{1-\\\\gamma t_{2}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{1+\\\\gamma t_{2}}{1+\\\\gamma t_{1}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{1-\\\\gamma t_{2}}{1-\\\\gamma t_{1}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Let $d$ is the density of the liquid at $0^\\circ C$ then<br>${d_1} = \\frac{d}{{1 + \\gamma {t_1}}}\\,\\& \\,{d_2} = \\frac{d}{{1 + \\gamma {t_2}}}$<br>$ \\Rightarrow \\frac{{{d_1}}}{{{d_2}}} = \\frac{{1 + \\gamma {t_2}}}{{1 + \\gamma {t_1}}}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37222,'physics',26,1133,2710,'The thickness of the ice layer on the surface of lake is $20\\,m$. A hole is made in the ice layer. What is the minimum length of the rope required to take a bucket full of water out?<br>(Take density of ice $ = 0.9 \\times {10^3}\\,kg/{m^3})$','[{"text": "$2\\\\,m$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$5\\\\,m$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$9\\\\,m$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$18\\\\,m$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Given that density of ice $$=\\dfrac{9}{10} \\times$$ density of water, so only $$\\dfrac{1}{10}$$ th of ice will be outside the water. This means that $20 \\times \\frac{1}{{10}} = 2\\,m$ of ice layer will be above the water surface.<br><img src=\'/img/data/physics/26_37222_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' /> <br>In other words, in the hole, the water level will rise up to $18\\,m$ of the ice layer. So minimum length of rope required $ = 2\\,m$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37225,'physics',27,1381,3315,'A substance of mass $M\\,kg$ requires a power input of $P$ watts to remain in the molten state at its melting point. When the power source is turned off, the sample completely solidifies in time $t$ seconds. The specific latent heat of fusion of the substance is','[{"text": "$\\\\dfrac{P M}{t}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$Pt$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{P t}{M}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$PtM$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'The heat input in time $t$ is $P t$<br>$P T=M L \\Rightarrow L=\\dfrac{P t}{M}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37226,'physics',26,1133,2710,'A ball is made of a material of density $$\\rho$$ where $$\\rho_{\\text {oil }} < \\rho < \\rho_{\\text {water }}$$ with $$\\rho_{\\text {oil }}$$ and $$\\rho_{\\text {water }}$$ representing the densities of oil and water, respectively. The oil and water are immiscible. If the above ball is in equilibrium in a maximum in a mixture of this oil and water, which of the following pictures represents its equilibrium position?','[{"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Oil will float on water so, (2) or (4) is the correct option. But density of ball is more than that of oil, hence it will sink in oil.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37227,'physics',28,1371,3289,'Match the Column I (type of processes) with Column II (feature).<div class="matching-question"><div class="column-left">A Isothermal <br> B Isobaric <br>C Isochoric <br>D Adiabatic <br></div><div class="column-right">P  $\\Delta Q = 0$ <br> Q Volume constant  <br> R Pressure constant <br> S Temperature constant</div></div>','[{"text": "A-S,B-R,C-Q,D-P", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "A-P,B-S,C-R,D-Q", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "A-Q,B-R,C-P,D-S", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "A-R,B-P, C-Q,D-S", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'<br>$\\begin{array}{*{20}{c}}\n{{\\rm{Type}}\\,{\\rm{of}}\\,{\\rm{processes}}}&{{\\rm{Feature}}}\\\\\n{{\\rm{Isothermal}}}&{{\\rm{Temperature}}\\,\\,{\\rm{constant}}}\\\\\n{{\\rm{Isobaric}}}&{{\\rm{Pressure}}\\,{\\rm{constant}}}\\\\\n{{\\rm{Isochoric}}}&{{\\rm{Volume}}\\,\\,{\\rm{constant}}}\\\\\n{{\\rm{Adiabatic}}}&{{\\rm{\\Delta Q = 0}}}\n\\end{array}$<br>So options (1) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Match the following',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37229,'physics',26,1135,2714,'Water flowing out of the mouth of a tap and falling vertically in streamline flow forms a tapering column, i.e., the area of cross-section of the liquid column decreases as it moves down. Which of the following is the most accurate explanation for this?<div class="matching-question"></div>','[{"text": "As the water moves down, its speed increases and hence, its pressure decreases. It is then compressed by atmosphere", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Falling water tries to reach a terminal velocity and hence, reduces the area of cross-section to balance upward and downward forces", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "The mass of water flowing out per second through any cross-section must remain constant. As the water is almost incompressible, so the volume of water flowing out per second must remain constant. As this is equal to velocity $$\\\\times$$ area, the area decreases as velocity increases", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "The surface tension causes the exposed surface area of the liquid to decrease continuously", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'According to equation of continuity, $$a v=$$ constant. As $$v$$ increases, $$a$$ decreases.',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/26_37229_question_1.png'),
(37230,'physics',27,1381,3315,'A bullet of mass $10 \\times {10^{ - 3}}\\,kg$ moving with a speed of $40\\,m{s^{ - 1}}$  hits an ice block $\\left( {0^\\circ C} \\right)$ of $990\\,g$ kept at rest on a frictionless floor and gets embedded in it. If ice takes $50 \\%$ of $K.E$  lost by the system, the amount of ice melted (in gram) approximately is: $(1\\,cal = 4.2\\,J)$ Latent heat of ice $ = 80\\,cal/g)$','[{"text": "$6$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$3$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$6 \\\\times 10^{-3}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$12 \\\\times 10^{-3}$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Kinetic energy of bullet $ = \\frac{1}{2}m{v^2}$<br>$ = \\frac{1}{2} \\times 10 \\times {10^{ - 3}} \\times 40 \\times 40 = 2\\,J = \\frac{8}{{4.2}}cal$<br>Heat gained by ice to melt<br>$=\\dfrac{8}{4.2} \\times \\dfrac{50}{100} \\Rightarrow m L=\\dfrac{4}{4.2}$<br>$\\Rightarrow \\quad m=\\dfrac{4}{4.2 \\times 80} g=12 \\times 10^{-3} g$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37231,'physics',28,1367,3277,'Zeroth law of thermodynamics gives the concept of','[{"text": "Pressure", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Volume", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Temperature", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Heat", "isCorrect": false}]',2,NULL,NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37232,'physics',23,863,1977,'Scalar product of two vectors is $2 \\sqrt{3}$ and the magnitude of their vector product is equal to $2,$ then the angle (in degrees) between them will be','[{"text": "$50^\\\\circ $", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$30^\\\\circ $", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$25^\\\\circ $", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$35^\\\\circ $", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'$\\vec A \\cdot \\overrightarrow B  = \\left| {\\overrightarrow A } \\right|\\left| {\\overrightarrow B } \\right|\\cos \\theta  = 2\\sqrt 3 \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,(1)$<br>$\\left| {\\overrightarrow A } \\right| \\times \\left| {\\overrightarrow B } \\right| = \\left| {\\overrightarrow A } \\right|\\left| {\\overrightarrow B } \\right|\\sin \\theta  = 2\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,(2)$<br>Dividing equation (2) by equation (1) we get,<br>$\\tan \\theta  = \\frac{2}{{2\\sqrt 3 }} = \\frac{1}{{\\sqrt 3 }}$<br>$\\therefore \\theta  = {\\tan ^{ - 1}}\\left( {\\frac{1}{{\\sqrt 3 }}} \\right) = 30^\\circ $',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37233,'physics',26,1135,2714,'In the figure, the velocity $$v_{3}$$ will be','[{"text": "$1\\\\,\\\\,m{s^{ - 1}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$$4 m s^{-1}$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Zero", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$3\\\\,\\\\,m{s^{ - 1}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'According to equation of continuity<br>${A_1}{v_1} = {A_2}{v_2} + {A_3}{v_3}$<br>$ \\Rightarrow 4 \\times 0.2 = 2 \\times 0.2 + 0.4 \\times {v_3} \\Rightarrow {v_3} = 1\\,m/s$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/26_37233_question_1.png'),
(37234,'physics',27,1381,3314,'A vessel contains $110\\,g$ of water. The heat capacity of the vessel is equal to $10\\,g$ of water. The initial temperature of water in vessel is $10^\\circ C$. If $220\\,g$ of hot water at $70^\\circ C$ poured in the vessel, the final temperature neglecting radiation loss, will be approximately','[{"text": "$80^\\\\circ C$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$70^\\\\circ C$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$50^\\\\circ C$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$60^\\\\circ C$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Let final temperature of water be $\\theta$<br>Heat taken $=$ Heat given<br>$110 \\times 1(\\theta  - 10) + 10(\\theta  - 10) = 220 \\times 1(70 - \\theta )$<br>$ \\Rightarrow {\\kern 1pt} \\theta  = 48.8^\\circ C \\approx 50^\\circ C.$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37235,'physics',28,1369,3279,'The internal energy of a perfect monoatomic gas is','[{"text": "Complete kinetic", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Complete potential", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Sum of potential and kinetic energy of the molecules", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Difference of kinetic and potential energies of the molecules", "isCorrect": false}]',0,NULL,NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37236,'physics',23,860,1972,'A spring is compressed between two toy carts of mass $m_{1}$ and $m_{2}$. When the toy carts are released, the springs exert equal and opposite average forces for the same time on each toy cart. If $v_{1}$ and $v_{2}$ are the velocities of the toy carts and there is no friction between the toy carts and the ground, then:','[{"text": "$v_{1} / v_{2}=m_{1} / m_{2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$v_{1} / v_{2}=m_{2} / m_{1}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$v_{1} / v_{2}=-m_{2} / m_{1}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$v_{1} / v_{2}=-m_{1} / m_{2}$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Applying law of conservation of linear momentum<br>${m_1}{v_1} + {m_2}{v_2} = 0$<br>$\\frac{{{m_1}}}{{\\,{m_2}}} =  - \\frac{{{v_2}}}{{{v_1}}}{\\rm{ or }}\\frac{{{v_1}}}{{{v_2}}} =  - \\frac{{{m_2}}}{{\\,{m_1}}}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37237,'physics',27,1381,3314,'A liquid of mass $m$ and specific heat $c$ is heated to a temperature $2\\,T$. Another liquid of mass $m/2$ and specific heat $2c$ is heated to temperature $T$. If these two liquids are mixed, the resulting temperature of the mixture is:','[{"text": "$(2/3)T$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$(8/5)T$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$(3/5)T$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$(3/2)T$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Temperature of mixture is given by $T=\\dfrac{m_{1} c_{1} T_{1}+m_{2} c_{2} T_{2}}{m_{1} c_{1}+m_{2} c_{2}}=\\dfrac{m \\cdot c \\cdot 2 T+\\dfrac{m}{2} \\cdot 2 c \\cdot T}{m \\cdot c \\cdot+\\dfrac{m}{2} \\cdot 2 c}=\\dfrac{3}{2} T$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37238,'physics',26,1135,2714,'A lawn sprinkler has 20 holes, each of cross -<br>sectional area $2.0 \\times {10^{ - 2}}\\,c{m^2}$, and is connected to a hose pipe of cross sectional area $2.4\\,\\,c{m^2}$. If the speed of water in the hose pipe is $1.5\\,\\,m{s^{ - 1}}$, the speed of water as it emerges from the holes in $m/s$ is','[{"text": "5", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "10", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "9", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "7", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'From equation of continuity<br>${A_1}{v_1} = N{A_2}{v_2}$<br>$2.4\\,c{m^2} \\times 1.5\\frac{m}{s} = 20 \\times 2 \\times {10^{ - 2}}\\,c{m^2} \\times {v_2}$<br>${v_2} = 9\\,m/s$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37239,'physics',28,1369,3279,'The internal energy of an ideal gas depends upon','[{"text": "Only its pressure", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Only its volume", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Only its temperature", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Its pressure and volume", "isCorrect": false}]',2,NULL,NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37240,'physics',23,867,2244,'A ceiling fan is rotating around a fixed axle as shown. The direction of angular velocity is along','[{"text": "${+\\\\hat{j}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${-\\\\hat{j}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${+\\\\hat{k}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${-\\\\hat{k}}$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Using right hand thumb rule, direction of angular velocity is along ${-{ve} z}$ axis. Correct option is (4).',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2024,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/23_37240_question_1.png'),
(37241,'physics',27,1381,3314,'During illness an $80\\,kg$ man ran a fever of  $102.2^\\circ F$ instead of normal body temperature of $98.6^\\circ F$. Assuming that human body is mostly water, how much heat is required to raise his temperature by that amount?','[{"text": "$100k\\\\,cal$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$160k\\\\,cal$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$50k\\\\,cal$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$92k\\\\,cal$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'<br>Since $102.2^\\circ F \\to 39^\\circ C\\,\\,{\\rm{and}}\\,\\,98.6^\\circ F \\to 37^\\circ C$<br>Hence, $\\Delta Q=m s \\Delta T=80 \\times 1000 \\times(39-37)$<br>$16 \\times {10^4}cal = 160{\\mkern 1mu} k\\,cal$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37242,'physics',26,1135,2714,'Figure shows a liquid being pushed out of a tube by pressing a piston. The cross-sectional area of the piston is $1\\,c{m^2}$ and that of the tube at the outlet is $20\\,m{m^2}$. If the piston is pushed at a speed of $1\\,cm{s^{ - 1}}$, then the velocity of the outgoing liquid is :','[{"text": "$5\\\\,cm{s^{ - 1}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$2.5\\\\,cm{s^{ - 1}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$10\\\\,cm{s^{ - 1}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$7.5\\\\,cm{s^{ - 1}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'From equation of continuity<br>$20 \\times {10^{ - 2}} \\times v = 1 \\times 1$<br>$v = \\frac{1}{{20 \\times {{10}^{ - 2}}}}cm{s^{ - 1}} = 5\\,cm{s^{ - 1}}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/26_37242_question_1.png'),
(37244,'physics',27,1381,3314,'Two spheres $A$ and $B$ have diameters in the ratio $1: 2$, densities in the ratio $2: 1$ and specific heats in the ratio $1: 3$. Find the ratio of their thermal capacities','[{"text": "$1: 4$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1: 6$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1: 12$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$1: 3$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'<br>$\\begin{aligned}\\dfrac{m_{A} c_{A}}{m_{B} c_{B}} & =\\dfrac{(4 / 3) \\pi r_{A}^{3} \\rho_{A} c_{A}}{(4 / 3) \\pi r_{B}^{3} \\rho_{B} c_{B}}=\\left(\\dfrac{r_{A}}{r_{B}}\\right)^{3} \\times \\dfrac{\\rho_{A} c_{A}}{\\rho_{B} c_{B}} \\\\& =\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right)^{3} \\times\\left(\\dfrac{2}{1}\\right) \\times\\left(\\dfrac{1}{3}\\right)=\\dfrac{1}{12}\\end{aligned}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37245,'physics',26,1136,2717,'A large tank filled with water to a height \' $$h$$ \' is to be emptied through a small hole at the bottom. The ratio of time taken for the level of water to fall from $$h$$ to $$\\dfrac{h}{2}$$ and from $$\\dfrac{h}{2}$$ to zero is','[{"text": "$$\\\\sqrt{2}$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$\\\\dfrac{1}{\\\\sqrt{2}}$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$\\\\sqrt{2}-1$$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$$\\\\dfrac{1}{\\\\sqrt{2}-1}$$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Time taken for the level to fall from $$H$$ to $$H^{\\prime}$$<br>$$t=\\dfrac{A}{A_{0}} \\sqrt{\\dfrac{2}{g}}\\left[\\sqrt{H}-\\sqrt{H^{\\prime}}\\right]$$<br>According to problem - the time taken for the level to fall from $$h$$ to $$\\dfrac{h}{2} \\quad t_{1}=\\dfrac{A}{A_{0}} \\sqrt{\\dfrac{2}{g}}\\left[\\sqrt{h}-\\sqrt{\\dfrac{h}{2}}\\right]$$ and similarly time taken for the level to fall from<br>$$\\begin{aligned}\\dfrac{h}{2} \\text { to zero } t_{2} & =\\dfrac{A}{A_{0}} \\sqrt{\\dfrac{2}{g}}\\left[\\sqrt{\\dfrac{h}{2}}-0\\right] \\\\\\therefore \\quad \\dfrac{t_{1}}{t_{2}} & =\\dfrac{1-\\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}}{\\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}-0}=\\sqrt{2}-1\\end{aligned}$$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37246,'physics',28,1371,3287,'Thermodynamic system returns to its original state, which of the following is NOT possible?','[{"text": "The work done is zero", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "The work done is positive", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "The work done is negative", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "The work done is independent of the path followed", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Work is a path function so it depends on path.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37247,'physics',27,1381,3314,'A steel ball of mass $0.1\\,kg$ falls freely from a height of $10\\,m$ and bounces to a height of $5.4\\,m$ from the ground. If the dissipated energy in this process is absorbed by the ball, the rise in its temperature is (specific heat of steel $ = 460\\,J/kg\\,^\\circ C,g = 10\\,m/{s^2})$','[{"text": "$0.01^\\\\circ C$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$0.1^\\\\circ C$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$1^\\\\circ C$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1.1^\\\\circ C$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Loss in energy $=m g\\left(h-h^{\\prime}\\right)$<br>$ = 0.1 \\times 10 \\times (10 - 5.4) = 4.6\\,J$<br>Now, $4.6\\,J = ms\\Delta \\theta $<br>$=0.1 \\times 460 \\times \\Delta \\theta$<br>$\\therefore \\Delta \\theta  = 0.1^\\circ C$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37248,'physics',26,1136,2717,'There is a hole of area $\\frac{1}{{25}}\\,c{m^2}$ in the bottom of a cylindrical vessel containing fluid up to height $h$. The liquid flows out in time $$t$$. If the liquid were filled in the vessel up to height $$4 h$$, then it would flow out in time','[{"text": "$$t$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$2 t$$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$$4 t$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$\\\\dfrac{t}{2}$$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Let $A$ and $$a$$ be the cross-sectional areas of the vessel and hole respectively. Let $$h$$ be the height of water in the vessel at time $$t$$.<br>Let $$\\left(-\\dfrac{d h}{d t}\\right)$$ represent the rate of fall of level.<br>Then, $$A\\left(-\\dfrac{d h}{d t}\\right)=a v=a \\sqrt{2 g h}$$<br>or $$\\quad-\\dfrac{d h}{\\sqrt{h}}=\\dfrac{a \\sqrt{2 g}}{A} d t$$<br>$$\\begin{aligned}& -\\int_{h}^{0} \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{h}} d h=\\dfrac{a \\sqrt{2 g}}{A} \\int_{0}^{t} d t \\\\& -(-2 \\sqrt{h})=\\dfrac{a \\sqrt{2 g}}{A} t \\\\& t=\\dfrac{A}{a} \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{2 g}} \\times 2 \\sqrt{h} \\Rightarrow t=\\dfrac{A}{a} \\sqrt{\\dfrac{2 h}{g}}\\end{aligned}$$<br>Now, $$t \\propto \\sqrt{h}$$<br>When $$h$$ is quadrupled, $$t$$ is doubled.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37249,'physics',28,1371,3282,'In a reversible isothermal process undergone by an ideal gas','[{"text": "Heat transfer is zero.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Work done is zero.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Internal energy increases.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Heat transfer is equal to work done.", "isCorrect": true}]',3,NULL,NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37250,'physics',26,1136,2717,'A wide vessel with a small hole in the bottom is filled with water and kerosene. Neglecting viscosity, the velocity of water flow $$v$$ if the thickness of water layer is $$h_{1}$$ and that of kerosene layer is $$h_{2}$$ is (density of water is ${\\rho _1}\\,g$ / cc and that of kerosene is $\\left. {{\\rho _2}\\,g/cc} \\right)\\left( {{\\rho _1} > {\\rho _2}} \\right)$','[{"text": "$$v=\\\\sqrt{2 g\\\\left(h_{1}+h_{2}\\\\right)}$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$v=\\\\sqrt{2 g\\\\left(h_{1}+h_{2} \\\\dfrac{\\\\rho_{2}}{\\\\rho_{1}}\\\\right)}$$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$$v=\\\\sqrt{2 g\\\\left(h_{1} \\\\rho_{1}+h_{2} \\\\rho_{2}\\\\right)}$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$v=\\\\sqrt{2 g\\\\left(h_{1} \\\\dfrac{\\\\rho_{1}}{\\\\rho_{2}}+h_{2}\\\\right)}$$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'<br><img src=\'/img/data/physics/26_37250_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' /> <br>From Bernoulli\'s equation<br>$$\\begin{gathered}\\rho_{2} g h_{2}+\\rho_{1} g h_{1}=\\dfrac{1}{2} \\rho_{1} v^{2} \\\\\\Rightarrow v^{2}=\\dfrac{2 g\\left(\\rho_{1} h_{1}+\\rho_{2} h_{2}\\right)}{\\rho_{1}} \\Rightarrow v=\\sqrt{2 g\\left(h_{1}+\\dfrac{\\rho_{2}}{\\rho_{1}} h_{2}\\right)}\\end{gathered}$$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37251,'physics',27,1381,3314,'The specific heat of a substance varies with temperature $T\\left( {^\\circ C} \\right)$ as $c = 0.20 + 0.14T + 0.023{T^2}\\left( {cal/g/\\,^\\circ C} \\right)$. The heat required to raise the temperature of $2\\,g$ of substance from $5^\\circ C$  to $15^\\circ C$ will be','[{"text": "$82\\\\,cal$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$56\\\\,cal$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$100\\\\,cal$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$24\\\\,cal$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Heat required to raise the temperature of $m$ grams of substance by $dT$ is given as<br>$d Q=m c d T \\Rightarrow Q=\\int m c d T$<br>Therefore, to raise the temperature of $2\\,g$of substance from $5^\\circ C$ to $15^\\circ C$<br>$Q = \\int_5^{15} 2  \\times \\left( {0.2 + 0.14T + 0.023{T^2}} \\right)dT$<br>$ = 2 \\times \\left[ {0.2T + \\frac{{0.14{T^2}}}{2} + \\frac{{0.023{T^3}}}{3}} \\right]_5^{15} = 81.83\\,cal$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37252,'physics',28,1371,3281,'Which of the following is true in the case of a reversible process?','[{"text": "There will be energy loss due to friction", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "System and surroundings will not be in thermodynamic equilibrium", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both system and surroundings retain their initial states", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "(1) and (3)", "isCorrect": false}]',2,NULL,NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37254,'physics',26,1136,2717,'A cylindrical tank has cross sectional area $A$. There is an orifice at the bottom of area \' $$a$$ \'. There is a constant in flow of water into the tank at a rate \' $$Q$$ \' through another pipe. Then the equilibrium height of water in the tank is','[{"text": "$$Q^{2} / g a^{2}$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$3 Q^{2} / 4 g a^{2}$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$Q^{2} / 2 g a^{2}$$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$$Q^{2} / 4 g a^{2}$$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Rate of inflow $$=$$ rate of out flow<br>$$\\begin{aligned}& Q=a v=a \\sqrt{2 g h} \\\\\\therefore \\quad & h=\\dfrac{Q^{2}}{a^{2} \\cdot 2 g}\\end{aligned}$$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37255,'physics',28,1371,3282,'Heat is added to an ideal gas and the gas expands. In such a process the temperature','[{"text": "Must always increase", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Will remain the same if the work done equals the heat added", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Must always decrease", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Will remain the same if change in internal energy equals the heat added", "isCorrect": false}]',1,NULL,NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37256,'physics',27,1381,3314,'A chef, on finding his stove out of order, decides to boil coffee by shaking it in a thermosflask. Suppose that he uses water at $15^\\circ C$ and that the water falls $30\\,cm$ each shake, the chef making 30 shakes each minute. Neglecting any loss of thermal energy by the flask, how long must he shake the flask until the water reaches $100^\\circ C\\left( {{c_{{\\rm{coffee }}}} = 4190\\,J} \\right)$ ?','[{"text": "$2.25 \\\\times {10^3}\\\\,\\\\min $", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$3.97 \\\\times {10^3}\\\\,\\\\min $", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$4.03 \\\\times {10^3}\\\\,\\\\min $", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$5.25 \\\\times {10^3}\\\\,\\\\min $", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'The time taken to boil the coffee is<br>Hence, $t=\\dfrac{Q}{N m g h}=\\dfrac{c m\\left(T_{f}-T_{i}\\right)}{N m g h}=\\dfrac{c\\left(T_{f}-T_{i}\\right)}{N g h}$<br>$=\\dfrac{(4190)(100-15)}{(30)(9.8)(0.30)}=4.03 \\times 10^{3} \\mathrm{~min}$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37257,'physics',26,1136,2717,'A tank of height $$H$$ is fully filled with water. If the water rushing from a hole made in the tank below free surface, strikes the floor at maximum horizontal distance, then the depth of the hole from the free surface must be','[{"text": "$$(3 / 4) H$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$(1 / 4) H$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$(2 / 3) H$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$(1 / 2) H$$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Water rushing from a hole made in the tank below the free surface follows a parabolic path.<br>Let $$h$$ is is the depth of the hole from the free surface<br>The velocity of efflux of liquid, $$v=\\sqrt{2 g h}$$<br>Time, $$t=\\sqrt{\\dfrac{2(H-h)}{g}}$$, Horizontal range, $$R=v t$$<br>$$R=\\left[2 g h \\times \\dfrac{2(H-h)}{g}\\right]^{1 / 2}$$<br>The range is maximum if $$d R / d h=0$$<br>$$\\therefore \\quad R^{2}=4 h(H-h)=4\\left(H h-h^{2}\\right)$$<br>or $$\\quad 2 R \\dfrac{d R}{d h}=4(H-2 h)$$ or $$0=(H-2 h)$$ or $$h=H / 2$$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37258,'physics',28,1371,3281,'Which of the following processes is reversible?','[{"text": "Transfer of heat by radiation", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Transfer of heat by conduction", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Electrical heating of a nichrome wire", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Isothermal compression", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'For process to be reversible it must be quasi-static. For quasi static process all changes take place very slowly. Isothermal process occur very slowly so it is quasi-static and hence it is reversible.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37259,'physics',26,1136,2716,'An aircraft has a mass $4 \\times {10^5}\\,kg$ with total wing area $500\\,{m^2}$ flying at a speed of $720\\,\\,km/h$. The density of air at its height is $1.2\\,\\,kg/{m^3}$. Estimate the dfractional increase in air speed on the upper surface of its wings relative to lower surface','[{"text": "0.04", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "0.32", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "0.17", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "0.08", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'The weight of the aircraft is balanced by the upward force due to the pressure difference.<br>i.e., $$\\Delta P \\times A=m g$$<br>$\\Delta P = \\frac{{mg}}{A} = \\frac{{\\left( {4 \\times {{10}^5}\\,kg} \\right)\\left( {10\\,m/{s^2}} \\right)}}{{500\\,{m^2}}} = \\frac{4}{5} \\times {10^4}\\,N/{m^2}$<br>Let $$v_{1}, v_{2}$$ are the speeds of air on the lower and upper surface of the wings of the aircraft and $$P_{1}, P_{2}$$ are the pressures there.<br>Using Bernoulli\'s theorem, we get<br>$$\\begin{aligned}& P_{1}+\\dfrac{1}{2} \\rho v_{1}^{2}=P_{2}+\\dfrac{1}{2} \\rho v_{2}^{2} \\\\& P_{1}-P_{2}=\\dfrac{1}{2} \\rho\\left(v_{2}^{2}-v_{1}^{2}\\right) \\\\& \\Delta P=\\dfrac{\\rho}{2}\\left(v_{2}+v_{1}\\right)\\left(v_{2}-v_{1}\\right)=\\rho v_{a v}\\left(v_{2}-v_{1}\\right)\\end{aligned}$$<br>or<br>$$v_{2}-v_{1}=\\dfrac{\\Delta P}{\\rho v_{a v}}$$<br>Here, $\\quad {v_{av}} = \\frac{{{v_1} + {v_2}}}{2} = 720\\,km/h$<br>$ = 720 \\times \\frac{5}{{18}}\\,m/s = 200\\,m/s$<br>$\\therefore \\quad \\frac{{{v_2} - {v_1}}}{{{v_{av}}}} = \\frac{{\\Delta P}}{{\\rho v_{av}^2}} = \\frac{{\\frac{4}{5} \\times {{10}^4}}}{{1.2 \\times {{(200)}^2}}}$<br>$ = \\frac{{4 \\times {{10}^4}}}{{5 \\times 1.2 \\times 4 \\times {{10}^4}}} = 0.17$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37260,'physics',27,1381,3314,'Calculate the amount of heat (in calories) required to convert $5\\,g$ of ice at $0^\\circ C$ to steam at $100^\\circ C$','[{"text": "$3200\\\\,cal$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$3100\\\\,cal$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$4200\\\\,cal$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$3600\\\\,cal$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Ice $\\left( {0^\\circ C} \\right)$ converts into steam $\\left( {100^\\circ C} \\right)$ in three steps.<br>The heat required $Q=Q_{1}+Q_{2}+Q_{3}$<br>$ = 5 \\times 80 + 5 \\times 1 \\times (100 - 0) + 5 \\times 540 = 3600\\,cal$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37261,'physics',28,1371,3287,'An ideal gas is taken through a cyclic process completely once. Change in the internal energy of the gas is','[{"text": "Infinity", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Zero", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Small", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Large", "isCorrect": false}]',1,NULL,NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37262,'physics',26,1136,2716,'To get the maximum flight a ball must be thrown as:','[{"text": "", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Any of (1), (2) and (3)", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'If a ball is moving from left to right and also spinning about a horizontal axis in clockwise direction about a horizontal axis perpendicular to the direction of motion then relative to the ball air will be moving from right to left.<br><img src=\'/img/data/physics/26_37262_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' /> <br>The resultant velocity of air above the ball will be $$(v+r \\omega)$$ while below it $$(v-r \\omega)$$. So in accordance with Bernoulli\'s principle, pressure above the ball will be less than below it. Due to this difference of pressure an upward force will act on the ball as shown in the figure.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37265,'physics',26,1136,2719,'A cylinder containing water up to a height of $25\\,\\,cm$ has a hole of cross-section $\\frac{1}{4}\\,c{m^2}$ in its bottom. It is counterpoised in a balance. What is the initial change in the balancing weight when water begins to flow out?','[{"text": "$6.25\\\\,gwt$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$12.5\\\\,gwt$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$25\\\\,gwt$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$16\\\\,gwt$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Let $A = {\\text{The}}$ area of cross section of the hole<br>$$\\begin{aligned}& v=\\text { Initial velocity of efflux } \\\\& d=\\text { Density of water, }\\end{aligned}$$<br>Initial mass of water flowing out per second<br>$$=A v d$$<br>Rate of change of momentum $$=A d v^{2}$$<br>$$\\therefore$$ Initial upward reaction $$=A d v^{2}$$<br>$$\\therefore$$ Initial decrease in weight $$=A d(2 g h)$$<br>$ = 2Adgh = 2 \\times \\left( {\\frac{1}{4}} \\right) \\times 1 \\times 980 \\times 25 = 12.5gwt.$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/26_37265_question_1.png'),
(37266,'physics',27,1381,3315,'A small quantity, mass $m$, of water at a temperature $\\theta \\left( {in\\,^\\circ C} \\right)$ is poured on to a larger mass $M$ of ice which is at its melting point. If $c$ is the specific heat capacity of water and $L$ the latent heat of fusion of ice, then the mass of ice melted is given by','[{"text": "$\\\\dfrac{m c \\\\theta}{L}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{m c \\\\theta}{M L}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{M c \\\\theta}{L}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{M L}{m c \\\\theta}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Heat given out by water $=$ heat absorbed by the melted ice<br>$\\Rightarrow m c(\\theta-0)=M L \\Rightarrow M=\\dfrac{m c \\theta}{L}$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37267,'physics',28,1371,3285,'$d U=d Q$ is valid for','[{"text": "Adiabatic process", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Isothermal process", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Isobaric process", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Isochoric process", "isCorrect": true}]',3,NULL,NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37268,'physics',26,1136,2719,'A light cylindrical vessel is kept on a horizontal surface. Its base area is $$A$$. A hole of cross sectional area $$a$$ is made just at its bottom side. The minimum coefficient of friction necessary for sliding of the vessel due to the the impact force of the emerging liquid is : $(a <  < A)$','[{"text": "$a/A$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$\\\\dfrac{4 a}{A}$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2a/A$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "None of these", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Velocity of efflux $$=\\sqrt{2 g h}$$<br>Thrust force $$=v \\dfrac{d m}{d t}=\\rho a v^{2}$$<br>Frictional force $$\\leq \\mu m g$$<br>$$\\begin{aligned}& \\rho a v^{2} \\leq \\mu \\rho A h g \\\\& \\rho a(\\sqrt{2 g h})^{2} \\leq \\mu(\\rho A h) g \\\\& \\Rightarrow \\mu \\geq \\dfrac{2 a}{A} \\\\& \\Rightarrow \\quad \\mu_{\\min }=\\dfrac{2 a}{A}\\end{aligned}$$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37269,'physics',27,1381,3315,'Equal masses of ice and water with temperature equally below and above the melting point of ice respectively are mixed with each other. At equilibrium $40 \\%$ of the ice melted. The initial temperature of water was','[{"text": "$64^\\\\circ C$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$96^\\\\circ C$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$32^\\\\circ C$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$16^\\\\circ C$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Heat gained by ice + heat for melting ice $=$ heat lost by water<br>$(m)\\left( {\\frac{1}{2}\\frac{{cal}}{{g - \\,^\\circ C}}} \\right)\\left( {T\\,^\\circ C} \\right) + (m)\\left( {80\\frac{{cal}}{g}} \\right) \\times \\frac{{40}}{{100}}$<br>$ = (m)\\left( {1\\frac{{cal}}{{g - \\,^\\circ C}}} \\right)\\left( {T\\,^\\circ C} \\right)$<br>$(T/2) + 32 = T \\Rightarrow T = 64^\\circ C$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37270,'physics',28,1369,3279,'A thermodynamical system is changed from state $\\left(p_{1} V_{1}\\right)$ to $\\left(p_{2} V_{2}\\right)$ by two different process. The quantity which will remain same is','[{"text": "$\\\\Delta W$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\Delta Q$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\Delta Q-\\\\Delta W$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\Delta Q+\\\\Delta W$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Change in internal energy does not depend upon path so $\\Delta U=\\Delta Q-\\Delta W$ remains constant.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37271,'physics',26,1136,2719,'There are two identical small holes of area of cross-section $$a$$ on the opposite sides of a tank containing a liquid of density $$\\rho$$. The difference in height between the holes is $$h$$. Tank is resting on a smooth horizontal surface. Horizontal force which will have to be applied on the tank to keep it in equilibrium is','[{"text": "$$g h \\\\rho a$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$\\\\dfrac{2 g h}{\\\\rho a}$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$2 g h \\\\rho a$$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$$\\\\dfrac{2 \\\\rho g h}{a}$$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Velocity of a liquid that comes out from a hole which is present at a depth $$H$$ is $$v=\\sqrt{2 g H}$$<br>Let ${H_1}{\\text{ }}\\,\\& \\,{H_2}$are depths of holes from the free surface. The thrust forces exerted by the liquid on the container are<br>$$\\begin{aligned}& F_{1}=\\rho a v_{1}^{2}=a \\rho\\left(2 g H_{1}\\right), \\quad F_{2}=\\rho a v_{2}^{2}=a \\rho\\left(2 g H_{2}\\right) \\\\& F_{n e t}=a \\rho 2 g\\left(H_{1} \\sim H_{2}\\right)=a \\rho 2 g h\\end{aligned}$$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/26_37271_question_1.png');
INSERT INTO `qb_questions` (`id`, `subject_id`, `chapter_id`, `topic_id`, `subtopic_id`, `question_html`, `options`, `correct_index`, `explanation`, `explanation_image_url`, `difficulty`, `qtype`, `year`, `tag`, `created_at`, `question_image_url`) VALUES
(37272,'physics',27,1381,3315,'An indian pitcher has $10\\,kg$ of water. Water cools by means of evaporation through the pores. Find the time in which the temperature of water falls by $5^\\circ C.$ The rate of vaporisation is $5\\,g\\,{\\min ^{ - 1}}$','[{"text": "$20\\\\min 10\\\\,s$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$14\\\\min 12\\\\,s$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$18\\\\min 26\\\\,s$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "None of these", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Let $\\dfrac{d m}{d t}$ be the rate of evaporation and $M=M_{0}-t \\dfrac{d m}{d t}$ is the amount of liquid left in the pitcher at any instant.<br>The heat reqiured for evaporation is equal to heat gained<br>$\\begin{gathered}M c d \\theta=\\left(\\dfrac{d m}{d t} t\\right) L \\\\\\left(M_{0}-\\dfrac{d m}{d t} \\cdot t\\right) c d \\theta=L t \\dfrac{d m}{d t} \\\\\\left(10000-\\dfrac{5}{60} \\cdot t\\right) 1 \\times 5=(540) t \\dfrac{5}{60} \\\\\\Rightarrow 50000=45 t+\\dfrac{5}{12} t \\Rightarrow t=\\dfrac{50000 \\times 12}{545}=18 \\min 26 s\\end{gathered}$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37273,'physics',28,1369,3279,'If a amount of heat given to a system be $50\\,J$ and work done on the system be $15\\,J$, then change in internal energy of the system is','[{"text": "$50\\\\,J$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$35\\\\,J$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$15\\\\,J$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$65\\\\,J$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Here $\\Delta Q = 50\\,J,W =  - 15\\,J$<br>$\\therefore \\Delta U = \\Delta Q - W = 50 - ( - 15) = 65\\,J$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37433,'physics',26,1134,2713,'I. Turbulence dissipates kinetic energy in the form of heat energy.<br>II. Turbulence like friction is sometimes desirable.<br>III. Turbulence promotes mixing and increases the rate of transfer of mass, momentum and energy.<br>Which of the following statements(s) is/are correct?','[{"text": "Only I", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both II and III", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Only III", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "I, II and III", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Option (4) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ type-2',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37274,'physics',26,1137,2722,'The space between two large horizontal metal plates, $6\\,\\,cm$ apart, is filled with a liquid of viscosity $0.8\\,N/{m^2}$. A thin plate of surface area $0.01\\,\\,{m^2}$ is moved parallel to the length of the plate such that the plate is at a distance of $2\\,m$ from one of the plates and $4\\,cm$ from the other. If the plate moves with a constant speed of $1\\,m{s^{ - 1}}$, then','[{"text": "The layers of the fluid, which is in contact with the moving plate, is having the maximum velocity", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "The layer of the fluid, which is having the maximum velocity, is lying mid-way between the plates", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "The layer of the fluid, which is in contact with the two large plates, moving with the maximum velocity.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "The layer of the fluid, which is in contact with the moving plate and is on the side of nearer plate, is moving with the maximum velocity", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'The profile of the velocity of fluid is as shown in the figure.<br><img src=\'/img/data/physics/26_37274_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' /> <br>The velocity of the layer of fluid, which is in contact with metal plates (fixed), is zero. As we move towards the centre from either plate, the velocity of the layer of fluid increases and it becomes maximum at the location of moving plate.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37275,'physics',27,1381,3315,'If $10\\,g$ of water is evaporated from $1\\,{m^2}$ field in one second then heat radiation incident is','[{"text": "$280\\\\frac{{cal}}{s}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$5400\\\\frac{{cal}}{s}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$54\\\\frac{{cal}}{s}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$540\\\\frac{{cal}}{s}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'The total heat absorbed by water.<br>$H=m L$<br>The radiation power is $P = \\frac{{10}}{1} \\times 540 = 5400\\frac{{cal}}{s}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37276,'physics',28,1369,3279,'$2\\,g$ of ${H_2}O$ changes from liquid to vapour phase at constant pressure at $2\\,atm$. The volume increases from $1 \\mathrm{cc}$ to $3342\\,cc$. The heat of vaporisation at this pressure is $540\\,cal/g$. The increase in internal energy of water is','[{"text": "$3000\\\\,J$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$4202\\\\,J$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$2122\\\\,J$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$992\\\\,J$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'<br>$W = P(dV) = 2.02 \\times {10^5}(3342 - 1) \\times {10^{ - 6}} = 334.2\\,J$<br>$\\Delta Q = \\Delta U + W$<br>$ \\Rightarrow \\Delta U = \\Delta Q - W = mL - 167 = 540 \\times 4.2 \\times 2 - 334.2$<br>$\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, = 4202{\\mkern 1mu} J$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37277,'physics',27,1381,3315,'To raise the temperature of $200\\,g$ of ice at $0^\\circ C$ to $10^\\circ C$ by a heater of $420\\,W$, the time required is $(1Cal = 4.2\\,J)$','[{"text": "${\\\\rm{90}}\\\\,{\\\\rm{min}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{180}}\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\rm{seconds}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$21.2 \\\\mathrm{~min}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{21}}{\\\\rm{.2 seconds}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Total heat required is $Q=m L+m c \\Delta T$<br>$ = 200 \\times 80 + 200 \\times 1 \\times 10$<br>$Q = 18000\\,cal = 75,600\\,J$<br>Time required is<br>$\\Delta t = \\frac{Q}{p} = \\frac{{75,600}}{{420}} = 180\\,s$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37278,'physics',26,1137,2722,'A thin plate of large area $$A$$ is placed midway in a gap of height $$h$$ filled with oil of viscosity $$\\eta_{0}$$ and the plate is pulled at constant velocity $$v$$ by applying drag force on the plate. If a lighter oil of viscosity $$\\eta$$ is then substituted in the gap, it is found that for the velocity $$v$$, and the same drag force as previous case the plate is located unsymmetrically in the gap but parallel to the walls. Find $$\\eta$$ in terms of distance from nearer wall to the plane $$y$$.','[{"text": "$$\\\\dfrac{4 \\\\eta_{0} y}{h}$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$\\\\dfrac{4 \\\\eta_{0} y}{h}\\\\left[1-\\\\dfrac{y}{h}\\\\right]$$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$$4 \\\\eta_{0}\\\\left[1-\\\\dfrac{y}{h}\\\\right]$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$\\\\dfrac{2 \\\\eta_{0} y}{h}$$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Case I:<br><img src=\'/img/data/physics/26_37278_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' /> <br>Here viscous force acts on the plate on both sides<br>Drag force $$=F_{1}+F_{2}$$<br>$$=\\left[\\eta_{0} \\dfrac{v}{h / 2}+\\eta_{0} \\dfrac{v}{h / 2}\\right] A=\\dfrac{4 \\eta_{0} v A}{h}$$<br>Case II:<br><img src=\'/img/data/physics/26_37278_explanation_2.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' /> <br>Drag force on the plate<br>$$=\\left[\\eta \\dfrac{v}{h-y}+\\eta \\dfrac{v}{y}\\right] A=\\dfrac{\\eta v h A}{y(h-y)}$$<br>As drag forces are equal<br>$$\\begin{aligned}& \\Rightarrow \\dfrac{4 \\eta_{0} v A}{h}=\\dfrac{\\eta v h A}{y(h-y)} \\\\& \\Rightarrow \\eta=\\dfrac{4 \\eta_{0} y}{h}\\left[1-\\dfrac{y}{h}\\right]\\end{aligned}$$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/26_37278_question_1.png'),
(37279,'physics',28,1371,3284,'When heat is given to a gas in an isobaric process, then','[{"text": "Internal energy of the gas increases", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "The work is done by the gas", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Internal energy of the gas decreases", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both (1) and (2)", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'When heat is supplied at constant pressure, a part of it goes in the expansion of gas and remaining part is used to increase the temperature of the gas which in turn increases the internal energy.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37280,'physics',26,1137,2723,'Spherical balls of radius \' $$R$$ \' are falling in a viscous fluid of viscosity \' $$\\eta$$ \' with a velocity \' $$v$$ \'. The retarding viscous force acting on the spherical ball is','[{"text": "Inversely proportional to both radius \' $$R$$ \' and velocity \' $$v$$ \'", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Directly proportional to both radius \' $$R$$ \' and velocity \' $$v$$ \'", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Directly proportional to \' $$R$$ \' but inversely proportional to \' $$v$$ \'", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Inversely proportional to \' $$R$$ \' but Directly proportional to velocity \' $$v$$ \'", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'From Stoke\'s law<br>Viscous force $$F=6 \\pi \\eta R v$$<br>hence $F$ is directly proportional to radius & velocity.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37281,'physics',27,1381,3315,'$50\\,g$ of ice at $ - 5^\\circ C$ is added to $200\\,g$ of water at $40^\\circ C$ in a calorimeter. The water equivalent of calorimeter is $50\\,g$ of water. The temperature of the mixture is','[{"text": "$9.58^\\\\circ C$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$12.58^\\\\circ C$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$19.58^\\\\circ C$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$29.58^\\\\circ C$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'As water equivalent of calorimeter is $50\\,g$ of water the total water present in the system is $250\\,g$. Heat absorbed by ice to become water at $0^\\circ C$ is<br>$Q{\\rm{ }} = 50 \\times 0.5 \\times 5 + 50 \\times 80 = 4125\\,cal$<br>The decrease in temperature of water is<br>$4125 = 250 \\times 1 \\times \\Delta T$<br>$ \\Rightarrow \\Delta T = 165^\\circ C$<br>Now we have $50\\,g$ of water at $0^\\circ C$  and $250\\,g$ of water at $23.5^\\circ C$. The temperature of the mixture is<br>$50 \\times 1(T - 0) + 250 \\times 1 \\times (T - 235) = 0$<br>$ \\Rightarrow T = 19.58^\\circ C$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37282,'physics',28,1371,3284,'One mole of an ideal monoatomic gas is heated at a constant pressure of one atmosphere from $0^\\circ C$ to $100^\\circ C$. Then the change in the internal energy is','[{"text": "$20.80\\\\,joules$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$12.48 \\\\times {10^2}\\\\,joules$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$8.32 \\\\times {10^2}\\\\,joules$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$6.56{\\\\rm{ }}\\\\,joules$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Change in internal energy is $\\Delta U=n C_{V} \\Delta T$<br>For monoatomic gas $C_{V}=\\dfrac{3}{2} R$<br>$\\begin{aligned}& \\Delta U=n \\dfrac{3}{2} R \\Delta T=1 \\times \\dfrac{3}{2} \\times 8.31 \\times(100-0) \\\\& =12.48 \\times 10^{2} J .\\end{aligned}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37283,'physics',26,1137,2723,'Eight drops of water, each of radius $2\\,mm$ are falling through air at a terminal velocity of $8\\,cm\\,{s^{ - 1}}$. If they coalesce to form a single drop, then the terminal velocity of combined drop will be','[{"text": "$30\\\\,cm\\\\,{s^{ - 1}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$28\\\\,cm\\\\,{s^{ - 1}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$24\\\\,\\\\,cm\\\\,{s^{ - 1}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$32\\\\,\\\\,cm\\\\,{s^{ - 1}}$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Let the radius of bigger drop is $R$ and that of smaller drop is $$r$$ then<br>$\\frac{4}{3}\\pi {R^3} = 8 \\times \\frac{4}{3} \\times \\pi {r^3}$<br>${\\text{ or }}\\quad R = 2r\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,(1)$<br>Terminal velocity, $$v \\propto r^{2}$$<br>$\\therefore \\quad \\frac{{{v^\\prime }}}{v} = \\frac{{{R^2}}}{{{r^2}}} = {\\left( {\\frac{{2r}}{r}} \\right)^2} = 4$<br>$ \\Rightarrow \\quad {v^\\prime } = 4v = 4 \\times 8 = 32\\,cm\\,{s^{ - 1}}$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37284,'physics',27,1382,3319,'The ratio of thermal conductivity of two rods of different material is $5: 4$. The two rods of same area of cross-section and same thermal resistance will have the lengths in the ratio.','[{"text": "$5: 4$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$4: 5$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$9: 1$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1: 9$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Given $A_{1}=A_{2}$ and $\\dfrac{K_{1}}{K_{2}}=\\dfrac{5}{4}$<br>$\\because R_{1}=R_{2} \\Rightarrow \\dfrac{l_{1}}{K_{1} A}=\\dfrac{l_{2}}{K_{2} A} \\Rightarrow \\dfrac{l_{1}}{l_{2}}=\\dfrac{K_{1}}{K_{2}}=\\dfrac{5}{4}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37285,'physics',28,1371,3284,'The ratio of work done by an ideal diatomic gas to the heat supplied by the gas in an isobaric process is','[{"text": "$\\\\dfrac{3}{5}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{5}{7}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{5}{3}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{2}{7}$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'$\\Delta U=n C_{V} \\Delta T=n(5 / 2) R \\Delta T$<br>$\\begin{aligned}& \\Delta Q=n C_{P} \\Delta T=n(7 / 2) R \\Delta T \\\\& W=\\Delta Q-\\Delta U=\\dfrac{n 7}{2} R \\Delta T-\\dfrac{n 5}{2} R \\Delta T=n R \\Delta T \\\\& \\dfrac{W}{\\Delta U}=\\dfrac{2}{7}\\end{aligned}$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37286,'physics',27,1382,3317,'Which of the following circular rods, (given radius $r$ and lenght $l$) each made of the same material and whose ends are maintained at the same temperature will conduct more heat?','[{"text": "$r=2 r_{0}, l=2 l_{0}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$r=2 r_{0}, l=l_{0}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$r=r_{0}, l=l_{0}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$r=r_{0}, l=2 l_{0}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Heat conduction through a rod is<br>$H = \\frac{{\\Delta Q}}{{\\Delta t}} = KA\\left( {\\frac{{{T_1} - {T_2}}}{l}} \\right) \\Rightarrow H \\propto \\frac{{{r^2}}}{l}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\left( 1 \\right)$<br>(1) When $r=2 r_{0}, l=2 l_{0}$<br>$H \\propto \\dfrac{\\left(2 r_{0}\\right)^{2}}{2 l_{0}} \\Rightarrow H \\propto \\dfrac{2 r_{0}^{2}}{l_{0}}$<br>(2) When $r=2 r_{0}, l=l_{0}$<br>$H \\propto \\dfrac{\\left(2 r_{0}\\right)^{1}}{l_{0}} \\Rightarrow H \\propto \\dfrac{4 r_{0}^{2}}{l_{0}}$<br>(3) When $r=r_{0}, l=l_{0}$<br>$H \\propto \\dfrac{r_{0}^{2}}{l_{0}}$<br>(4) When $r=r_{0}, l=2 l_{0}$<br>$H \\propto \\dfrac{r_{0}^{2}}{2 l_{0}}$<br>So heat conduction will be more in case (2)',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37287,'physics',26,1137,2723,'A marble of mass $$x$$ and diameter $$2 r$$ is gently released in a tall cylinder containing honey. If the marble displaces mass $$y( < x)$$ of the liquid, then the terminal velocity is proportional to','[{"text": "$$(x-y)$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$(x+y)$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$\\\\dfrac{(x-y)}{r}$$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$$\\\\dfrac{x+y}{r}$$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'If $$v$$ is the terminal velocity, then the force,<br>$$\\begin{aligned}& x g-y g=6 \\pi \\eta r v \\\\& \\Rightarrow v=\\dfrac{(x-y)}{r} \\dfrac{g}{6 \\pi \\eta} \\\\& \\Rightarrow v \\propto \\dfrac{(x-y)}{r}\\end{aligned}$$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37288,'physics',28,1371,3282,'Equation of state in an isothermal process is','[{"text": "$P = $ Constant", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$V = $ Constant", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$PT = $ Constant", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$PV = $ Constant", "isCorrect": true}]',3,NULL,NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37289,'physics',26,1137,2723,'A uniform solid sphere of relative density 5 is released in water filled in a long vertical tube. Its terminal velocity achieved is $$V$$. If another uniform solid sphere of same material but double the radius is released in the same water then the terminal velocity achieved will be.','[{"text": "$4\\\\,V$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$$V$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$V / 4$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2\\\\,\\\\,V$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Initially the terminal velocity $$V$$ of sphere of radius $$a$$ is<br>${W_{eff{\\text{ }}}} = 6\\pi \\eta aV\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,(1)$<br>$\\left( {{W_{eff{\\text{ }}}} = {\\text{ weight  -  buoyant force }}} \\right)$<br>As the radius is doubled, mass is increased to 8 times and new terminal velocity will be<br>$8{W_{eff{\\text{ }}}} = 6\\pi \\eta 2a{V^\\prime }\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,(2)$<br>From (1) and (2) $$V^{\\prime}=4 V$$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37290,'physics',27,1382,3317,'Ice starts forming in lake with water at $0^\\circ C$ and when the atmospheric temperature is $ - 10^\\circ C$. If the time taken for $1\\,cm$ of ice be 7 hours. Find the time taken for the thickness of ice to change from $1\\,cm\\,{\\rm{to}}\\,2\\,cm.$','[{"text": "6 hours", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "11 hours", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "16 hours", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "21 hours", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'The time required to increase the thickness of ice from $x_{1}$ to $x_{2}$<br>$t=\\dfrac{\\rho L}{2 K \\theta}\\left(x_{2}^{2}-x_{1}^{2}\\right) \\Rightarrow t \\propto\\left(x_{2}^{2}-x_{1}^{2}\\right)$<br>Where $\\theta$ is the temperature of atmosphere<br>$\\Rightarrow \\dfrac{t}{t^{\\prime}}=\\dfrac{\\left(x_{2}^{2}-x_{1}^{2}\\right)}{\\left(x_{1}^{\\prime 2}-x_{2}^{\\prime 2}\\right)} \\Rightarrow \\dfrac{7}{t^{\\prime}}=\\dfrac{\\left(1^{2}-0^{2}\\right)}{\\left(2^{2}-1^{2}\\right)} \\Rightarrow t^{\\prime}=21$ hours.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37291,'physics',28,1371,3282,'Certain amount of heat supplied to an ideal gas under isothermal conditions will result in','[{"text": "A rise in temperature", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Doing external work and a change in temperature", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Doing external work", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "An increase in the internal energy of the gas", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'At constant temp $d U=0$<br>$\\therefore \\quad d Q=d W$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37293,'physics',26,1137,2723,'When a ball is released from rest in a very long column of viscous liquid, its downward acceleration is \' $$a$$ \' (just after release). Then its acceleration when it has acquired two third of the maximum velocity:<div class="matching-question"></div>','[{"text": "$$\\\\dfrac{a}{3}$$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$$\\\\dfrac{2 a}{3}$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$\\\\dfrac{a}{6}$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "None of these", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'When the ball is just released, the net force on ball is $$m a=m g-F_{b}$$<br>The terminal velocity \' $$v_{t}$$ \' of the ball is attained when net force on the ball is zero.<br>$$m g=F_{b}+6 \\pi \\eta r v_{t}$$<br>When the ball acquires $$\\dfrac{2}{3} \\mathrm{rd}$$ of its maximum velocity $$v_{f}$$<br>$$\\begin{aligned}& m a^{\\prime}=m g-F_{b}-6 \\pi \\eta r\\left(\\dfrac{2 v_{t}}{3}\\right) \\\\& m a^{\\prime}=m a-\\dfrac{2}{3}(m a) \\\\& a^{\\prime}=\\dfrac{a}{3}\\end{aligned}$$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37294,'physics',27,1382,3317,'A hollow spherical ball of inner radius $a$ and outer radius $2 a$ is made of a uniform material of constant thermal conductivity $K$. The temperature within the ball is maintained at $2 T_{0}$ and outside the ball is $T_{0}$. Find the temperature at $r = \\frac{{3a}}{2}$','[{"text": "$\\\\dfrac{7 T_{0}}{5}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{4 T_{0}}{3}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{5 T_{0}}{3}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{3 T_{0}}{2}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'In steady state, the net outward heat flow is constant, and does not depend on radial position.<br>Let $c_{1}=i=-K\\left(4 \\pi r^{2}\\right) \\dfrac{d T}{d r}$<br>$ \\Rightarrow \\frac{{dT}}{{dr}} =  - \\frac{{{c_1}}}{{4\\pi K{r^2}}}$<br>$\\int_{2{T_0}}^T d T =  - \\frac{{{c_1}}}{{4\\pi K}}\\int_a^r {\\frac{1}{{{r^2}}}} dr$<br>$T - 2{T_0} = \\frac{{{c_1}}}{{4\\pi K}}\\left[ {\\frac{1}{r} - \\frac{1}{a}} \\right]\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\left( 1 \\right)$<br>When $r=2 a \\Rightarrow T=T_{0}$<br>${T_0} - 2{T_0} = \\frac{{{c_1}}}{{4\\pi K}}\\left[ {\\frac{1}{{2a}} - \\frac{1}{a}} \\right]\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\left( 2 \\right)$<br>From eq\'s (1) & (2) we get<br>$T=\\dfrac{4 T_{0}}{3} \\text { at } r=\\dfrac{3 a}{2}$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/27_37294_question_1.png'),
(37295,'physics',28,1371,3282,'The specific heat of a gas in an isothermal process is','[{"text": "Zero", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Infinite", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Remains constant", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Negative", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'In isothermal process temperature remains constant. i.e., $\\Delta T=0$. Hence according to $C=\\dfrac{Q}{m \\Delta T} \\Rightarrow C_{i s o}=\\infty$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37296,'physics',29,1300,3146,'A gas deviates maximum from the ideal gas laws at','[{"text": "High temperature and high pressure", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Low temperature and low pressure", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "High temperature and low pressure", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "low temperature and high pressure", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'At low temperature the molecules K.E is low and they come closer at high pressure. So intermolecular forces start acting among the particles and hence the gas deviates at low temperature and high pressure.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37297,'physics',26,1137,2723,'Two metal spheres are falling through a liquid of density $2 \\times {10^3}\\,kg/{m^3}$ with the same uniform speed. The material density of sphere 1 and sphere 2 are $8 \\times {10^3}\\,kg/{m^3}$ and $11 \\times {10^3}\\,kg/{m^3}$ respectively. The ratio of their radii is','[{"text": "$$\\\\dfrac{11}{8}$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$\\\\sqrt{\\\\dfrac{3}{2}}$$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$$\\\\dfrac{3}{2}$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$\\\\sqrt{\\\\dfrac{11}{8}}$$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'The terminal velocity of the spherical body of radius $$R$$, density $$\\rho$$ falling through a liquid of density $$\\sigma$$ is given by<br>$$v_{t}=\\dfrac{2}{9} \\dfrac{R^{2}(\\rho-\\sigma) g}{\\eta}$$<br>Where $$\\eta$$ is the coefficient of viscosity of the liquid<br>$$\\therefore v_{t_{1}}=\\dfrac{2 R_{1}^{2}\\left(\\rho_{1}-\\sigma\\right) g}{9 \\eta} \\text { and } v_{t_{2}}=\\dfrac{2 R_{2}^{2}\\left(\\rho_{2}-\\sigma\\right) g}{9 \\eta}$$<br>According to the given problem, $$v_{t_{1}}=v_{t_{2}}$$<br>$$R_{1}^{2}\\left(\\rho_{1}-\\sigma\\right)=R_{2}^{2}\\left(\\rho_{2}-\\sigma\\right) \\text { or } \\dfrac{R_{1}^{2}}{R_{2}^{2}}=\\dfrac{\\rho_{2}-\\sigma}{\\rho_{1}-\\sigma}$$<br>Substituting the given values, we get<br>$$\\begin{aligned}& \\dfrac{R_{1}^{2}}{R_{2}^{2}}=\\dfrac{\\left(11 \\times 10^{3}-2 \\times 10^{3}\\right)}{\\left(8 \\times 10^{3}-2 \\times 10^{3}\\right)}=\\dfrac{3}{2} \\\\& \\dfrac{R_{1}}{R_{2}}=\\sqrt{\\dfrac{3}{2}}\\end{aligned}$$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37298,'physics',27,1382,3319,'Two rectangular blocks, having identical dimensions, can be arranged either in configuration $I$ or in configuration II as shown in the figure, one of the blocks has thermal conductivity $K$ and the other $2K$. The temperature difference between the two ends along the $x$ axis is the same in both the configurations. It takes 9 min to transport a certain amount of heat from the hot end to the cold end in the configuration I. The time to transport the same amount of heat in the configuration II is','[{"text": "$2\\\\,\\\\min $", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$4\\\\,\\\\min $", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$9\\\\,\\\\min $", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$3\\\\,\\\\min $", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'In configuration I<br>total thermal resistance<br>$R_{1}=\\dfrac{\\ell}{K A}+\\dfrac{\\ell}{2 K A}=\\dfrac{3}{2} \\dfrac{\\ell}{K A}$<br>In configuration II<br>$R_{2}=\\left(\\dfrac{1 \\times \\dfrac{1}{2}}{1+\\dfrac{1}{2}}\\right) \\dfrac{\\ell}{K A}=\\dfrac{1}{3} \\dfrac{\\ell}{K A}$<br>We have $Q=\\dfrac{(\\Delta T)}{R} t$<br>As $Q$ is constant<br>$\\begin{aligned}& t \\propto R \\\\& \\dfrac{t_{2}}{t_{1}}=\\dfrac{R_{2}}{R_{1}} \\Rightarrow t_{2}=\\dfrac{2}{9} \\times 9=2 \\mathrm{~min} .\\end{aligned}$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/27_37298_question_1.png'),
(37299,'physics',28,1371,3282,'An ideal gas volume is increased from $V$ to $2\\,V$ under isothermal conditions. The change in internal energy','[{"text": "Zero", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Positive", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Negative", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Can\'t be predicted", "isCorrect": false}]',0,NULL,NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37300,'physics',29,1301,3147,'When a gas is forced in a smaller volume without change in temperature, its pressure increase because its molecules','[{"text": "Strike unit area of the container walls more number of times", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Strike the unit area of the container walls at higher speed", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Strike the unit area of the container wall with greater area.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Have more energy", "isCorrect": false}]',0,NULL,NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37468,'physics',29,1300,3145,'Which of the following methods will enable the volume of an ideal gas to be made 4 times','[{"text": "Absolute temperature and pressure must be doubled", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "At constant pressure the absolute temperature must be increased by 4 times", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "At constant temperature the pressure must be increased by 4 times", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "quarter the absolute temperature at constant pressure.<br>", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'When $P$ is constant<br>$\\dfrac{V}{T}=$ constant<br>If $T_{2}=4 T_{1} \\Rightarrow V_{2}=4 V_{1}$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37301,'physics',27,1382,3318,'Three identical rods $A, B$ and $C$ of equal lengths and equal diameter are joined in series as shown in the figure. Their thermal conductivites are $2\\,K,{\\rm{ }}K$ and $K/2$ respectively. Calculate the temperature at two junction points','[{"text": "$85.7,57.1^\\\\circ C$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$75.8,49.3^\\\\circ C$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$80.85,50.3^\\\\circ C$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$77.3,48.3^\\\\circ C$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'The rate of heat flow is same in all the three rods.<br>$\\frac{{\\left( {100 - {T_1}} \\right)2KA}}{l} = \\frac{{\\left( {{T_1} - {T_2}} \\right)KA}}{l} = \\frac{{\\left( {{T_2} - 0} \\right)KA}}{{2l}}$<br>$2\\left( {100 - {T_1}} \\right) = \\left( {{T_1} - {T_2}} \\right) = \\frac{{{T_2}}}{2}$<br>${T_1} = 85.7^\\circ C\\,{\\rm{and}}\\,\\, = \\frac{{2{T_1}}}{3} = 57.1^\\circ C$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/27_37301_question_1.png'),
(37302,'physics',26,1137,2723,'A small sphere falls from rest in a viscous liquid. Due to friction, heat is produced. The rate of production of heat depends on the radius as (At terminal velocity).','[{"text": "$$r^{5}$$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$$r^{2}$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$r^{4}$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$r^{3}$$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Viscous force on a falling sphere in a liquid $$F=6 \\pi \\eta r v_{t}$$ where $$v_{t}=\\dfrac{(2) r^{2}(\\rho-\\sigma) g}{9 \\eta}$$ is terminal velocity, $$\\rho=$$ density of sphere, $$\\sigma=$$ density of liquid.<br>Rate of production of heat $$=F v_{t}$$<br>$$\\begin{gathered}=6 \\pi \\eta r\\left[\\dfrac{2}{9} \\dfrac{r^{2}(\\rho-\\sigma) g}{\\eta}\\right]^{2}=\\dfrac{8}{27} \\dfrac{\\pi g^{2}(\\rho-\\sigma)^{2}}{\\eta} r^{5} \\\\\\Rightarrow \\dfrac{d Q}{d t} \\propto r^{5}\\end{gathered}$$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37303,'physics',28,1371,3289,'In which of the process the internal kinetic energy of the system varies ?','[{"text": "Adiabatic", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Isochoric", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Isobaric", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1,\\\\,2\\\\,\\\\& \\\\,3$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,NULL,NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37304,'physics',29,1300,3145,'When the temperature of a gas is increased at constant pressure, the number of molecules per $c{m^3}$','[{"text": "Decreases", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Increases", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Remains same", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Zero", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'At constant pressure $\\dfrac{V}{T}=$ constant<br>If $T$  increases $V$ also increases.<br>As $n$ remains same so number of molecules per $c{m^3}$ decreases.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37305,'physics',27,1382,3318,'Consider two insulating sheets with thermal resistance $R_{1}$ and $R_{2}$ as shown in the figure. The temperature $\\theta$ is','[{"text": "$\\\\dfrac{\\\\theta_{1} \\\\theta_{2} R_{1} R_{2}}{\\\\left(\\\\theta_{1}+\\\\theta_{2}\\\\right)\\\\left(R_{1}+R_{2}\\\\right)}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{\\\\theta_{1} R_{2}+\\\\theta_{2} R_{1}}{R_{1}+R_{2}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{\\\\left(\\\\theta_{1}+\\\\theta_{2}\\\\right) R_{1} R_{2}}{R_{1}^{2}+R_{2}^{2}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{\\\\theta_{1} R_{1}+\\\\theta_{2} R_{2}}{R_{1}+R_{2}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'For the two sheets, shown in figure, rate of heat transfer is same, i,e.,<br>$\\begin{aligned}& \\dfrac{d Q_{1}}{d t}=\\dfrac{d Q_{2}}{d t} \\Rightarrow \\dfrac{\\theta_{1}-\\theta}{R_{1}}=\\dfrac{\\theta-\\theta_{2}}{R_{2}} \\\\& \\Rightarrow \\theta_{1} R_{2}-\\theta R_{2}=\\theta R_{1}-\\theta_{2} R_{1} \\\\& \\theta=\\dfrac{\\theta_{1} R_{2}+\\theta_{2} R_{1}}{R_{1}+R_{2}}\\end{aligned}$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/27_37305_question_1.png'),
(37306,'physics',26,1137,2725,'Two capillaries of same length and radii in the ratio 1: 2 are connected in series. A liquid flows through them in streamlined condition. If the pressure across the two extreme ends of the combination is $2\\,m$ of water, the pressure difference across first capillary is','[{"text": "$9.4\\\\,m$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1.88\\\\,m$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$4.9\\\\,m$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$0.49\\\\,m$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Given, $$l_{1}=l_{2}=l$$ and $$\\dfrac{r_{1}}{r_{2}}=\\dfrac{1}{2}$$<br>As the tubes are connected in series.<br>$$\\begin{aligned}& Q=\\dfrac{\\pi P_{1} r_{1}^{4}}{8 \\eta l}=\\dfrac{\\pi P_{2} r_{2}^{4}}{8 \\eta l} \\Rightarrow \\dfrac{P_{1}}{P_{2}}=\\left(\\dfrac{r_{2}}{r_{1}}\\right)^{4}=16 \\\\& \\Rightarrow P_{1}=16 P_{2}\\end{aligned}$$<br>Since both tubes are connected in series, hence pressure difference across combination,<br>$$P=P_{1}+P_{2} \\Rightarrow 2=P_{1}+\\dfrac{P_{1}}{16} \\Rightarrow P_{1}=\\dfrac{32}{17}=1.88 m$$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37307,'physics',28,1371,3283,'In adiabatic expansion of a gas','[{"text": "Its pressure increases", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Its temperature falls", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Its density increases", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Its thermal energy increases", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'In adiabatic expansion as volume increases pressure and temperature decreases. In adiabatic expansion work done by the gas is at the expense of its own internal energy so temperature decreases.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37308,'physics',29,1300,3146,'Under which of the following conditions  $PV = nRT$  is obeyed most closely by a real gas ?','[{"text": "High pressure and high temperature", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Low pressure and low temperature", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Low pressure and high temperature", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "High pressure and low temperature", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'At low pressure and high temperature a real gas behaves like an ideal gas and hence it obeys ideal gas equation $P V=n R T$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37309,'physics',27,1382,3319,'Two plates of same thickness, of coefficients of thermal conductivity $K_{1}$ and $K_{2}$ and areas of cross section $A_{1}$ and $A_{2}$ are connected as shown in the figure. The common coefficient of thermal conductivity $K$ wil be','[{"text": "$\\\\dfrac{K_{1} A_{1}+K_{2} A_{2}}{A_{1}+A_{2}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{K_{1} A_{2}+K_{2} A_{1}}{K_{2}+K_{2}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{K_{1} A_{1}}{K_{2} A_{2}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$K_{1} A_{1}+K_{2} A_{2}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'As it is clear from figure.<br>$\\begin{aligned}& \\dfrac{d Q}{d t}=\\dfrac{d Q_{1}}{d t}+\\dfrac{d Q_{2}}{d t} \\\\& \\dfrac{K\\left(A_{1}+A_{2}\\right) d T}{d x}=K_{1} A_{1} \\dfrac{d T}{d x}+K_{2} A_{2} \\dfrac{d Q}{d x} \\\\& K=\\dfrac{K_{1} A_{1}+K_{2} A_{2}}{A_{1}+A_{2}}\\end{aligned}$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/27_37309_question_1.png'),
(37331,'physics',28,1371,3283,'The volume of gas is reduced adiabatically to (1/4) of its volume at $27^\\circ C$. If $\\gamma=1.4$ the new temperature will be:','[{"text": "$150 \\\\times {(4)^{0.4}}\\\\,K$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$300 \\\\times {(4)^{0.4}}\\\\,K$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$250 \\\\times {(4)^{0.4}}K$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "None of these", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'For adiabatic change the relation between temperature and volume is $T V^{\\gamma-1}=$ constant where $\\gamma$ is ratio of specific heats of the gas.<br>Given, $T_{1}=27+273=300 K, V_{1}=V, V_{2}=\\dfrac{V}{4}$<br>$T_{1} V_{1}^{\\gamma-1}=T_{2} V_{2}^{\\gamma-1} \\Rightarrow T_{2}=\\left(\\dfrac{V_{1}}{V_{2}}\\right)^{\\gamma-1} \\times T_{1}$<br>$T_{2}=\\left(\\dfrac{V}{V / 4}\\right)^{1.4-1} \\times 300$<br>${T_2} = {(4)^{0.4}} \\times 300 = 300 \\times {(4)^{0.4}}\\,K$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37310,'physics',26,1137,2725,'Three horizontal capillary tubes of same radii and lengths ${L_1},\\,{L_2}$ and ${L_3}$ are fitted side by side to the wall of a tank that is filled with water. The length of a single capillary tube of same radius that can replace the three tubes such that the rate of flow of water through the single tube equals the combined rate of flow through the three tubes is','[{"text": "$$\\\\dfrac{L_{1} L_{2} L_{3}}{L_{1}+L_{2}+L_{3}}$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$\\\\dfrac{L_{1} L_{2} L_{3}}{L_{1} L_{2}+L_{2} L_{3}+L_{3} L_{1}}$$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$$\\\\dfrac{L_{1}+L_{2}+L_{3}}{L_{1} L_{2} L_{3}}$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$\\\\dfrac{L_{1} L_{2}+L_{2} L_{3}+L_{3} L_{1}}{L_{1} L_{2} L_{3}}$$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'The rate of flow of liquid is<br>$$\\begin{aligned}& Q=Q_{1}+Q_{2}+Q_{3} \\\\& \\dfrac{\\pi P r^{4}}{8 \\eta L}=\\dfrac{\\pi P r^{4}}{8 \\eta L_{1}}+\\dfrac{\\pi P r^{4}}{8 \\eta L_{2}}+\\dfrac{\\pi P r^{4}}{8 \\eta L_{3}} \\\\& \\dfrac{1}{L}=\\dfrac{1}{L_{1}}+\\dfrac{1}{L_{2}}+\\dfrac{1}{L_{3}} \\\\& L=\\dfrac{L_{1} L_{2} L_{3}}{L_{1} L_{2}+L_{2} L_{3}+L_{3} L_{1}}\\end{aligned}$$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37311,'physics',28,1371,3283,'In case of an adiabatic process the correct relation in terms of pressure $p$ and density $\\rho$ of a gas is:-','[{"text": "$p \\\\rho^{\\\\gamma}=$ constant", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$p^{\\\\gamma} \\\\rho^{\\\\gamma-1}$ constant", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$p \\\\rho^{\\\\gamma-1}=$ constant", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$p \\\\rho^{-\\\\gamma}=$ constant", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'For adiabatic process $p V^{\\gamma}=$ constant<br>$\\begin{aligned}& p\\left(\\dfrac{M}{\\rho}\\right)^{\\gamma}=\\text { constant } \\\\& p \\cdot M^{\\gamma} \\cdot \\rho^{-\\gamma}=\\text { constant } \\\\& \\text { p. } \\rho^{-\\gamma}=\\text { constant }\\end{aligned}$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37312,'physics',29,1300,3145,'Two balloons are filled, one with pure He gas and other by air, respectively. If the pressure and temperature of these balloons are same, then the number of molecules per unit volume is:','[{"text": "More in the He filled balloon", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Same in both balloons", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "More in air filled balloon", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "In the ratio of $1: 4$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'$P V=n R T$<br>$n=\\dfrac{P V}{R T}$<br>As $P, V$ and $T$ are same then $n$ is also same in both balloons',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37313,'physics',26,1138,2727,'A thin metal disc of radius $$r$$ floats on water surface and bends the surface downwards along the perimeter making an angle $$\\theta$$ with vertical edge of the disc. If the disc displaces a weight of water $$W$$ and surface tension of water is $$T$$, then the weight of metal disc is','[{"text": "$$2 \\\\pi r T+W$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$2 \\\\pi r T \\\\cos \\\\theta-W$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$2 \\\\pi r T \\\\cos \\\\theta+W$$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$$W-2 \\\\pi r T \\\\cos \\\\theta$$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'<br><img src=\'/img/data/physics/26_37313_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' /> <br>Weight of metal disc $$=$$ total upward force<br>$$=$$ upthrust force + force due to surface tension<br>$$=$$ weight of displaced water $$+T \\cos \\theta[2 \\pi r]$$<br>$$=W+2 \\pi r T \\cos \\theta$$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37314,'physics',27,1382,3319,'The temperature of the two outer surface of a composite slab, consisting of two meterials having coefficient of thermal conductivity $K$ and $2 K$ and thickness $x$ and $4 x$, respectively are $T_{2}$ and $T_{1}\\left(T_{2}>T_{1}\\right)$. The rate of heat transfer through the slab, in a steady state $i$ $s\\left( {\\frac{{A\\left( {{T_2} - {T_1}} \\right)K}}{x}} \\right)f$, the value of $f$ is','[{"text": "$\\\\dfrac{1}{3}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "1", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{2}{3}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{1}{2}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'The equivalent thermal conductivity is<br>$R = {R_1} + {R_2}$<br>$\\frac{{{l_1} + {l_2}}}{{{K_{eq}}A}} = \\frac{{{l_1}}}{{{K_1}A}} + \\frac{{{l_2}}}{{{K_2}A}}$<br>${K_{eq}} = \\frac{{{l_1} + {l_2}}}{{\\frac{{{l_1}}}{{{K_1}}} + \\frac{{{l_2}}}{{{K_2}}}}} = \\frac{{x + 4x}}{{\\frac{x}{K} + \\frac{{4x}}{{2K}}}} = \\frac{5}{3}K.$<br>Hence rate of flow of heat through the given combination is<br>$\\begin{aligned}\\dfrac{Q}{t} & =\\dfrac{K_{e q} \\cdot A\\left(T_{2}-T_{1}\\right)}{(x+4 x)}=\\dfrac{\\dfrac{5}{3} K A\\left(T_{2}-T_{1}\\right)}{5 x} \\\\& =\\dfrac{\\dfrac{1}{3} K A\\left(T_{2}-T\\right)_{1}}{x}\\end{aligned}$<br>On comparing it with given equation we get $f=\\dfrac{1}{3}$.',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/27_37314_question_1.png'),
(37315,'physics',28,1371,3283,'$\\Delta U+\\Delta W=0$ is valid for','[{"text": "Isothermal process", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Adiabatic process", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Isochoric process", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Isobaric process", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'In adiabatic process<br>$\\begin{aligned}& \\Delta Q=0 \\Rightarrow \\Delta U+\\Delta W=0 \\\\& (\\because \\Delta Q=\\Delta U+\\Delta W)\\end{aligned}$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37316,'physics',29,1306,3156,'The upper atmosphere, an astronaut feels','[{"text": "Extremely hot", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Slightly hotter", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Extremely cool", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Slightly cooler", "isCorrect": false}]',2,NULL,NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37317,'physics',27,1382,3321,'Which of the following statements is wrong','[{"text": "Black surfaces are better absorbers than white ones", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Rough surfaces are better radiators than smooth surface", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Highly polished mirror like surfaces are very good radiators", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Black surfaces are better radiators than white", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Highly polished mirror like surfaces are good reflectors, but not good radiators.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37318,'physics',26,1138,2727,'A disc of paper of radius $$R$$ has a hole of radius $$r$$. It is floating on a liquid of surface tension $$T$$. The force of surface tension on the disc is','[{"text": "$$T .3 \\\\pi R$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$T .4 \\\\pi(R+r)$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$T .2 \\\\pi(R+r)$$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$$T .2 \\\\pi(R-r)$$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Effective length $$=2 \\pi r+2 \\pi R$$.<br>The surface tension force is $$F=T 2 \\pi(r+R)$$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37319,'physics',28,1371,3283,'Temperature of the system decreases in the process of','[{"text": "Free expansion", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Isothermal expansion", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Adiabatic expansion", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Isothermal compression", "isCorrect": false}]',2,NULL,NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37332,'physics',29,1300,3145,'If $P = {10^6}KT$, Then the number of molecules per unit volume of the gas is [The letters have usual meaning.]','[{"text": "$10^{9}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$10^{5}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$10^{6}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$10^{8}$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'The ideal gas equation is<br>$P V=n R T=\\dfrac{N}{N_{A}} R T=N K T$<br>Where $K$ is the Boltzmann\'s constant<br>Given that $P = {10^6}\\,\\,KT$<br>$\\Rightarrow \\dfrac{N}{V}=10^{6}$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37320,'physics',29,1300,3145,'Match the Column I with Column II<div class="matching-question"><div class="column-left">A  $P V=k_{B} N T$ <br> B $\\begin{array}{l}P \\propto 1 / V \\\\T=\\text { constant }\\end{array}$ <br> C $\\begin{array}{l}V \\propto T \\\\P=\\text { constant }\\end{array}$ <br> D $P=P_{1}+P_{2}+\\ldots .$.</div><div class="column-right">P  Dalton\'s law <br>Q  Boyle\'s law <br> R   Ideal gas equation <br>S  Charle\'s law</div></div>','[{"text": "${\\\\text{A  -  R,  B  -  Q,  C  -  P,   D  -  S}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\text{A  -  R,  B  -  R,  C  -  P,   D  -  S}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\text{A  -  R,  B  -  Q,  C  -  S,   D  -  P}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${\\\\text{A  -  R,  B  -  P,  C  -  Q,   D  -  S}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'$P V=n R T=\\left(\\dfrac{N}{N_{A}}\\right) R T$<br>$=N k_{B} T\\left(k_{B}=\\dfrac{N}{N_{A}}\\right)$<br>$P V=$ constant (Boyle\'s law)<br>$V \\propto T$ (charle\'s law)<br>$P=P_{1}+P_{2}+\\ldots$. (Dalton\'s law).',NULL,'Easy','Match the following',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37321,'physics',26,1138,2727,'A thin liquid film formed between a $$U$$-shaped wire and a light slider supports a weight of $1.5 \\times {10^{ - 2}}\\,N$ (see figure). The length of the slider is $30\\,\\,cm$ and its weight is negligible. The surface tension of the liquid film is','[{"text": "$0.0125\\\\,N{m^{ - 1}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$0.1\\\\,N{m^{ - 1}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$0.05\\\\,N{m^{ - 1}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$0.025\\\\,N{m^{ - 1}}$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'$$2 T L=m g$$ (Since film has two surfaces)<br>$T = \\frac{{mg}}{{2L}} = \\frac{{1.5 \\times {{10}^{ - 2}}}}{{2 \\times 30 \\times {{10}^{ - 2}}}} = \\frac{{1.5}}{{60}} = 0.025\\,N/m$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/26_37321_question_1.png'),
(37322,'physics',27,1382,3322,'Colour of shining bright star is an indication of its','[{"text": "Temperature", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Mass", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Size", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Distance", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'According to Wien\'s displacement law a hot body emits different wavelengths at different temperatures.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37323,'physics',28,1371,3283,'The adiabatic Bulk modulus of a perfect gas at a pressure is given by','[{"text": "$2 P$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$P$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\gamma P$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$P / 2$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'For adiabatic process $P V^{\\gamma}=$ constant<br>$\\begin{aligned}& P \\gamma V^{\\gamma-1} d V+V^{\\gamma} d P=0 \\\\& \\dfrac{P \\gamma}{V} d V+d P=0\\end{aligned}$<br>Bulk modulus $=\\dfrac{-d P}{d V / V}=\\gamma P$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37324,'physics',29,1300,3145,'The equation of state for $5 g$ of oxygen at a pressure $P$ and temperature $T$. when occupying a volume $V$, will be','[{"text": "$P V=(5 / 2) R T$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$P V=5 R T$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$P V=(5 / 32) R T$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$P V=(5 / 16) R T$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'$P V=n R T=\\dfrac{m}{M} R T=\\dfrac{5}{32} R T$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37325,'physics',26,1138,2727,'There is a horizontal film of soap solution. On it a thread is placed in the form of a loop. The film is pierced inside the loop and the thread becomes a circular loop of radius $$R$$. If the surface tension of the loop be $$T$$, then what will be the tension in the thread?','[{"text": "$$\\\\pi R^{2} / T$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$\\\\pi R^{2} T$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$2 \\\\pi R T$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$2 R T$$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Suppose tension in thread is $F$, then for small part $$\\Delta l$$ of thread<br><img src=\'/img/data/physics/26_37325_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' /> <br>$$\\Delta l=R \\theta$$ and $$2 F \\sin \\theta / 2=2 T \\Delta l=2 T R \\theta$$<br>$$\\begin{gathered}\\left(\\because \\sin \\dfrac{\\theta}{2} \\approx \\dfrac{\\theta}{2}\\right) \\\\\\Rightarrow \\quad F=\\dfrac{T R \\theta}{\\sin \\theta / 2}=\\dfrac{T R \\theta}{\\theta / 2}=2 T R\\end{gathered}$$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37326,'physics',27,1382,3322,'The wavelength of maximum energy released during an atomic explosion was $2.93 \\times {10^{ - 10}}\\,m$. Given that Wien\'s constant is $2.93 \\times {10^{ - 3}}\\,m - K$, the maximum temperature attained must be of the order of','[{"text": "${10^{ - 13}}\\\\,K$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${10^{ - 7}}\\\\,K$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${10^7}\\\\,K$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${10^{13}}\\\\,K$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'$T = \\frac{b}{{{\\lambda _m}}} = \\frac{{2.93 \\times {{10}^{ - 3}}}}{{2.93 \\times {{10}^{ - 10}}}} = {10^7}\\,K.$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37327,'physics',28,1371,3283,'In adiabatic process, $R=\\dfrac{2}{3} C_{V}$. The pressure of the gas will be proportional to:','[{"text": "$T^{5 / 2}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$T^{5 / 3}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$T^{5 / 6}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$T^{5 / 4}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'$R=\\dfrac{2}{3} C_{V}$<br>We know, $C_{p}-C_{V}=R$<br>$\\gamma-1=\\dfrac{R}{C_{V}} \\Rightarrow R=C_{V}(\\gamma-1)$<br>Comparing $\\gamma=\\dfrac{5}{3}$<br>$P^{1-\\gamma} T^{\\gamma}=$ constant<br>$P \\propto T^{(\\gamma / \\gamma-1)}$, Given $\\gamma=\\dfrac{5}{3}$<br>$\\dfrac{\\gamma}{(\\gamma-1)}=\\dfrac{5}{2}$<br>So, $P \\propto T^{5 / 2}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37328,'physics',29,1300,3145,'Volume pressure and temperature of an ideal gas are $V, P$ and $T$ respectively. If mass of its molecule is $m_{0}$, then its density is $(k=$ boltzmann\'s constant)','[{"text": "$\\\\dfrac{P m_{0}}{k T}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{P}{k T V}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{P}{k T}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$m_{0} k T$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'$P V=n R T$<br>$\\Rightarrow P V=\\dfrac{m}{M} R T$<br>Where $m=$ mass of the gass<br>$\\dfrac{P M}{R T}=\\dfrac{m}{V}=\\rho$<br>Where $\\rho=$ density<br>$\\Rightarrow \\rho=\\dfrac{P M}{R T}=\\dfrac{P \\times m_{0} \\times N_{A}}{R T}=\\dfrac{P m_{0}}{\\left(\\dfrac{R}{N_{A}}\\right) T}=\\dfrac{P m_{0}}{k T}$<br>',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37329,'physics',27,1382,3323,'The surface temperature of the sun is $T_{0}$ and it is at average distance $d$ from a planet. The radius of the sun is $R$. The temperature of the planet at which the energy is radiated','[{"text": "$T_{0}\\\\left(\\\\dfrac{R}{d}\\\\right)^{1 / 4}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$T_{0} \\\\sqrt{\\\\dfrac{2 R}{d}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$T_{0} \\\\sqrt{\\\\dfrac{R}{2 d}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$T_{0} \\\\sqrt{\\\\dfrac{R}{d}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Let $r$ is radius of planet<br>$T_{1}=$ Temperature of planet at which energy is radiated<br>Power radiated by Sun, $P=4 \\pi R^{2} \\sigma T_{0}^{4}$<br>Power received by planet<br>$=\\dfrac{P}{4 \\pi d^{2}} \\times \\pi r^{2}=\\dfrac{R^{2} T_{0}^{4} \\pi r^{2} \\sigma}{d^{2}}$<br>Power radiated by planet $=4 \\pi r^{2} \\sigma T_{1}^{4}$<br>For thermal equilibrium,<br>$\\begin{aligned}& \\dfrac{R^{2} T_{0}^{4} \\pi r^{2} \\sigma}{d^{2}}=4 \\pi r^{2} \\sigma T_{1}^{4} \\\\& R^{2} T_{0}^{4}=4 d^{2} T_{1}^{4} \\\\& T_{1}=T_{0} \\sqrt{\\dfrac{R}{2 d}}\\end{aligned}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37333,'physics',27,1382,3323,'Two identical metal balls at temperature $200^\\circ C$ and $400^\\circ C$ kept in air at $27^\\circ C$. The ratio of net heat loss by these bodies is:','[{"text": "$1 / 4$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1 / 2$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1 / 8$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{473^{4}-300^{4}}{673^{4}-300^{4}}$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'The net loss of energy of a body by radiation<br>$Q=A e \\sigma\\left(T^{4}-T_{0}^{4}\\right) t \\Rightarrow Q \\propto\\left(T^{4}-T_{0}^{4}\\right)$<br><br>$\\begin{aligned}& =\\dfrac{Q_{1}}{Q_{2}}=\\dfrac{T_{1}^{4}-T_{0}^{4}}{T_{2}^{4}-T_{0}^{4}} \\\\& =\\dfrac{(273+200)^{4}-(273+27)^{4}}{(273+400)^{4}-(273+27)^{4}} \\\\& =\\dfrac{(473)^{4}-(300)^{4}}{(673)^{4}-(300)^{4}}\\end{aligned}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37334,'physics',26,1138,2730,'A soap bubble has radius $$r$$. The work done in increasing its radius to three times its original radius, without any rise of temperature, is (Given: surface tension of soap solution is $$T$$)','[{"text": "$$12 \\\\pi r^{2} T$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$64 \\\\pi r^{2} T$$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$$16 \\\\pi r^{2} T$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$48 \\\\pi r^{2} T$$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'$$W=\\left[2 \\times 4 \\pi(3 r)^{2}-2 \\times 4 \\pi r^{2}\\right] T=64 \\pi r^{2} T$$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37336,'physics',29,1300,3145,'When the pressure of $1200\\,\\,ml$ of a gas is increased from $70 cm$ to$120 cm$ of mercury at constant temperature, the new volume of the gas will be','[{"text": "$500\\\\,\\\\,ml$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$600\\\\,\\\\,ml$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$700\\\\,\\\\,ml$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$400\\\\,\\\\,ml$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'At constant temperature<br>$PV = constant$<br>$ \\Rightarrow \\frac{{{P_1}}}{{{P_2}}} = \\frac{{{V_2}}}{{{V_1}}} \\Rightarrow \\frac{{70}}{{120}} = \\frac{{{V_2}}}{{1200}} \\Rightarrow {V_2} = 700{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} ml$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37337,'physics',27,1382,3323,'A sphere and a cube of same material and same total surface area are placed in the same evacuated space turn by turn after they are heated to the same temperature. Find the ratio of their initial rates of cooling in the enclosure.','[{"text": "$\\\\sqrt{\\\\dfrac{\\\\pi}{6}}: 1$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\sqrt{\\\\dfrac{\\\\pi}{3}}: 1$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{\\\\pi}{\\\\sqrt{6}}: 1$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{\\\\pi}{\\\\sqrt{3}}: 1$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Rate of emission of energy $=\\sigma T^{4} S$<br>Let $m_{1}$ be the mass of sphere, $C$  is the specific heat and $(d \\theta / d t)$, the rate of cooling.<br>For sphere<br>$\\sigma {T^4}A = {m_1}C{\\left( {\\frac{{d\\theta }}{{dt}}} \\right)_s}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\left( 1 \\right)$<br>Let $m_{2}$ be the mass of cube, $C$ is its specific heat and $(d \\theta / d t)$, the rate of cooling.<br>For cube<br>$\\sigma {T^4}A = {m_2}C{\\left( {\\frac{{d\\theta }}{{dt}}} \\right)_c}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\left( 2 \\right)$<br>From Eqs. (1) and (2)<br>$\\dfrac{(d \\theta / d t)_{s}}{(d \\theta / d t)_{c}}=\\dfrac{m_{2}}{m_{1}}$<br>We know that<br>$\\dfrac{m_{2}}{m_{1}}=\\dfrac{a^{3} \\rho}{(4 / 3) \\pi r^{3} \\rho}=\\dfrac{R_{s}}{R_{c}}$<br>Where $a$ is the side of cube and $r$  is the radius of sphere, $\\rho$ is the density.<br>$\\therefore \\dfrac{R_{s}}{R_{c}}=\\dfrac{3 a^{3}}{4 \\pi r^{3}}$<br>Since Area ($A$) is same,<br>$\\begin{aligned}& 6 a^{2}=4 \\pi r^{2} \\Rightarrow a^{2}=(2 / 3) \\pi r^{2} \\\\& \\therefore \\dfrac{R_{s}}{R_{c}}=\\dfrac{3\\left(2 \\pi r^{2} / 3\\right)^{3 / 2}}{4 \\pi r^{3}}=\\dfrac{2 \\pi \\sqrt{2 \\pi}}{\\sqrt{3}(4 \\pi)}\\end{aligned}$<br>$=\\sqrt{\\dfrac{2 \\pi}{12}}=\\sqrt{\\dfrac{\\pi}{6}}$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37338,'physics',26,1138,2728,'The surface energy of a liquid drop is $U$. It is sprayed into 1000 equal droplets. Then its surface energy becomes','[{"text": "10 $U$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$U$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "1000 $U$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "100 $U$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Given, $$U=S \\times 4 \\pi R^{2}$$; when droplet is splitted into 1000 droplets each of radius $$r$$, then<br>$$\\dfrac{4}{3} \\pi R^{3}=1000 \\times \\dfrac{4}{3} \\pi r^{3} \\text { or } r=R / 10$$<br>$$\\therefore$$ Surface energy of all droplets<br>$$\\begin{aligned}=S & \\times 1000 \\times 4 \\pi r^{2}=S \\times 1000 \\times 4 \\pi(R / 10)^{2} \\\\= & 10\\left(S \\times 4 \\pi R^{2}\\right)=10 U\\end{aligned}$$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37340,'physics',29,1300,3145,'Two vessels separately contain two ideal gases $A$ and $B$ at the same temperature, the pressure of $A$ being twice that of $B$. Under such condition, the density of $A$ is found to be 1.5 times the density of B. The ratio of molecular weights of $A$ and $B$ is:','[{"text": "$\\\\dfrac{2}{3}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{1}{2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{4}{3}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{3}{4}$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'According to ideal gas equation<br>$\\begin{gathered}P=\\dfrac{\\rho R T}{M} \\Rightarrow M=\\dfrac{\\rho R T}{P} \\\\\\dfrac{M_{A}}{M_{B}}=\\dfrac{\\rho_{A}}{\\rho_{B}} \\cdot \\dfrac{T_{A}}{T_{B}} \\cdot \\dfrac{P_{B}}{P_{A}}=(1.5)(1)\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right) \\Rightarrow \\dfrac{M_{A}}{M_{B}}=\\dfrac{3}{4}\\end{gathered}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37341,'physics',26,1138,2728,'Work done in increasing the size of a soap bubble from a radius of $3\\,\\,cm$ to $5\\,\\,cm$ is nearly (Surface tension of soap solution $ = 0.03\\,N{m^{ - 1}}$ )','[{"text": "$0.2\\\\,\\\\pi mJ$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$2 \\\\pi m J$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$0.4 \\\\pi m J$$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$$4 \\\\pi m J$$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'$$W=2 T \\Delta A$$<br>$$W=2 T 4 \\pi\\left[(5)^{2}-(3)^{2}\\right] \\times 10^{-4}$$<br><br>$ = 2 \\times 0.03 \\times 4\\pi [25 - 9] \\times {10^{ - 4}}\\,J$<br>$ = 0.4\\pi  \\times {10^{ - 3}}\\,J = 0.4\\,\\pi mJ$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37344,'physics',29,1300,3145,'At what temperature volume of an ideal gas at $0^\\circ C$  becomes triple (Pressure is constant)?','[{"text": "$182^\\\\circ C$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$546^\\\\circ C$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$646^\\\\circ C$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$819^\\\\circ C$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'At constant pressure<br>$V \\propto T \\Rightarrow \\frac{{{V_2}}}{{{V_1}}} = \\frac{{{T_2}}}{{{T_1}}} \\Rightarrow {T_2} = \\left( {\\frac{{{V_2}}}{{{V_1}}}} \\right){T_1}$<br>$ \\Rightarrow {T_2} = \\left( {\\frac{{3V}}{V}} \\right) \\times 273 = 819K = 546^\\circ C$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37345,'physics',27,1382,3323,'A solid copper cube of edges $1\\,cm$each is suspended in an evacuated enclosure. Its temperature is found to fall from<br>$100^\\circ C$ to $99^\\circ C$ in 100 s. Another solid copper cube of edges $2\\,cm$, with similar surface nature, is suspended in a similar manner. The timer required for this cube to cool from $100^\\circ C$ to $99^\\circ C$ will be approximately','[{"text": "$50\\\\,s$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$25\\\\,s$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$400\\\\,s$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$200\\\\,s$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Rate of<br>${\\rm{cooling }} = \\frac{{\\Delta \\theta }}{t} = \\frac{{A\\varepsilon \\sigma \\left( {{T^4} - T_0^4} \\right)}}{{mc}}$<br>$ \\Rightarrow t\\alpha \\frac{m}{A}\\left[ {\\Delta \\theta ,t,\\sigma ,\\left( {{T^4} - T_0^4} \\right){\\text{are}}\\,{\\text{constant}}} \\right]$<br>$ \\Rightarrow t\\alpha \\frac{m}{A}\\alpha \\frac{{{\\rm{ density }} \\times {\\rm{ Volume }}}}{{{\\rm{ Area }}}}\\alpha \\frac{{{\\ell ^3}}}{{{\\ell ^2}}} \\Rightarrow t\\alpha \\ell $<br>$ \\Rightarrow \\frac{{{t_1}}}{{{t_2}}} = \\frac{{{\\ell _1}}}{{{\\ell _2}}}$<br>$\\frac{{100}}{{{t_2}}} = \\frac{{1\\,cm}}{{2\\,cm}} \\Rightarrow {t_2} = 200\\sec $',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37346,'physics',26,1138,2730,'A soap bubble with a radius \' $$r$$ \' is placed on another bubble with a radius $R$ (figure). Angles between the films at the points of contact will be','[{"text": "$$120^{\\\\circ}$$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$$30^{\\\\circ}$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$45^{\\\\circ}$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$90^{\\\\circ}$$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'The angles between any two tangents which are drawn to the interfaces are equal<br>$$\\alpha=\\beta=\\gamma=120^{\\circ}$$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/26_37346_question_1.png'),
(37347,'physics',28,1371,3287,'In a cyclic process, work done by the system is','[{"text": "Equal to the heat change across the system", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Zero", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Independent of heat given to the system", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "More than the heat given to system", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'For cyclic forces $\\Delta U=0$ So, $\\Delta Q=\\Delta W$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37348,'physics',29,1300,3145,'At constant temperature on increasing the pressure of a gas by $5 \\%$ its volume will decrease by','[{"text": "$5 \\\\%$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$4.76 \\\\%$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$4.26 \\\\%$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$5.26 \\\\%$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'$P \\propto \\dfrac{1}{V} \\Rightarrow \\dfrac{V_{2}}{V_{1}}=\\dfrac{P_{1}}{P_{2}}=\\dfrac{100}{105} \\Rightarrow V_{2}=\\dfrac{100}{105} V_{1}=0.953 V_{1}$<br>$\\%$ change in volume $=\\dfrac{V_{1}-V_{2}}{V_{1}} \\times 100$<br>$=\\dfrac{V-0.953 V_{1}}{V_{1}} \\times 100=4.76 \\%$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37349,'physics',26,1138,2730,'The adjoining diagram shows three soap bubbles $A$, $B$ and $C$ prepared by blowing the capillary tube fitted with stop clock $$S, S_{1}, S_{2}$$ and $$S_{3}$$. With stop clock $S$ closed and stop clock $$S_{1}, S_{2}$$ and $$S_{3}$$ opened:','[{"text": "$B$ will be start collapsing with volumes of $A$ and $C$ increasing", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$C$ will start collapsing with volumes of $$A$$ and $B$ increasing", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$C$ and $A$ will both start collapsing with the volume of $B$ increasing", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Volumes of $A$, $B$ and $C$ will become equal at equilibrium", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Bubble $C$ size increase for any value of $A$ and $B$.',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/26_37349_question_1.png'),
(37352,'physics',29,1301,3149,'The molecules in an ideal gas at $27^\\circ C$ have a certain mean velocity. At what approximate temperature, will the mean velocity be doubled','[{"text": "$54^\\\\circ C$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$927^\\\\circ C$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$1200^\\\\circ C$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$327^\\\\circ C$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Mean velocity of gas molecules is, $v=\\sqrt{\\dfrac{8 R T}{\\pi M}}$ where, $T = $ Temperature of the gas in kelvin $M = $ Molar mass,<br>$R = $ Universal gas constant<br>For the same gas, $v \\propto \\sqrt{T}$<br>$\\therefore \\dfrac{v_{1}}{v_{2}}=\\sqrt{\\dfrac{T_{1}}{T_{2}}}=\\sqrt{\\dfrac{300}{T_{2}}} \\Rightarrow \\dfrac{v_{1}}{2 v_{1}}=\\sqrt{\\dfrac{300}{T_{2}}}$<br>Squaring both sides, we get, $\\dfrac{1}{4}=\\dfrac{300}{T_{2}}$<br>${T_2} = 1200\\,\\,K = 1200 - 273 = 927^\\circ C$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37354,'physics',26,1138,2730,'Assume that a drop of liquid evaporates by decrease in its surface energy, so that its temperature remains unchanged. What should be the minimum radius of the drop for this to be possible ? The surface tension is $T$, density of liquid is $\\rho \\,{\\text{and}}\\,L$ is its latent heat of vaporization.','[{"text": "$\\\\rho L/T$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$\\\\sqrt{T / \\\\rho L}$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$T / \\\\rho L$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$2 T / \\\\rho L$$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'When radius is decreased by $$d r$$<br>Decrease in surface energy $$=$$ Heat required for vaporisation<br>$$\\begin{aligned}& 2 d A T=d V \\rho L \\\\& (4 \\pi r d r) \\times T \\times 2=4 \\pi r^{2} d r \\rho L \\Rightarrow r=\\dfrac{2 T}{\\rho L}\\end{aligned}$$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37356,'physics',29,1301,3148,'If the molecular weight of two gases are $M_{1}$ and $M_{2}$, then at a temperature the ratio of rms velocity $c_{1}$ and $c_{2}$ will be','[{"text": "$\\\\left(\\\\dfrac{M_{1}}{M_{2}}\\\\right)^{1 / 2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\left(\\\\dfrac{M_{2}}{M_{1}}\\\\right)^{1 / 2}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\left(\\\\dfrac{M_{1}-M_{2}}{M_{1}+M_{2}}\\\\right)^{1 / 2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\left(\\\\dfrac{M_{1}+M_{2}}{M_{1}-M_{2}}\\\\right)^{1 / 2}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'As, $\\dfrac{1}{2} M c^{2}=\\dfrac{3}{2} R T$ or $c=\\left(\\dfrac{3 R T}{M}\\right)^{1 / 2}$<br>$\\Rightarrow c \\propto \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{M}}$<br>So, $\\quad \\dfrac{c_{1}}{c_{2}}=\\left(\\dfrac{M_{2}}{M_{1}}\\right)^{1 / 2}$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37358,'physics',26,1138,2730,'A spherical soap bubble of radius $1\\,cm$ is formed inside another of radius $3\\,\\,cm$ the radius of single soap bubble which maintains the same pressure difference as inside the smaller and outside the larger soap bubble is _ $cm$','[{"text": "$$4 / 3$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$3 / 4$$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$$1 / 2$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "2", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'The given soap bubble is<br><img src=\'/img/data/physics/26_37358_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' /> <br>Let ${P_1},{P_2}{\\text{ }}\\,\\& \\,{P_0}$ are the pressures shown in the figure<br>${P_2} - {P_0} = \\frac{{4T}}{{3\\,cm}}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,(1)$<br>${P_1} - {P_2} = \\frac{{4T}}{{1\\,cm}}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,(2)$<br>From Eq\'s (1) & (2) we get<br>${P_1} - {P_0} = 4T\\left[ {\\frac{1}{{3\\,cm}} + \\frac{1}{{1\\,cm}}} \\right] = \\frac{{16T}}{{3\\,cm}}$<br>The radius of the single soap bubble that maintains the same pressure difference is<br>$\\frac{{16T}}{{3\\,cm}} = \\frac{{4T}}{r} \\Rightarrow r = \\frac{3}{4}\\,cm$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/26_37358_question_1.png'),
(37359,'physics',28,1377,3303,'Which of the following statements is correct for any thermodynamic system?','[{"text": "Internal energy and entropy are state functions", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "The change in entropy can never be zero", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "The work done in an adiabatic process is always zero.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "The internal energy changes in all processes", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Internal energy and entropy are state function, they do not depend upon path but on the state.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37360,'physics',29,1301,3148,'Four molecules of a gas have speeds $1,2,3$ and $4 \\mathrm{kms}^{-1}$ respectively. The value of rms speed of the molecules is (in $\\mathrm{kms}^{-1}$ )','[{"text": "$2 \\\\sqrt{15}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{\\\\sqrt{15}}{2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\sqrt{\\\\dfrac{15}{2}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "None of the above", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'$c=\\sqrt{\\dfrac{1^{2}+2^{2}+3^{2}+4^{2}}{4}}$<br>$=\\sqrt{\\dfrac{1+4+9+16}{4}}=\\sqrt{\\dfrac{15}{2}} k m s^{-1}$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37361,'physics',26,1138,2731,'A glass rod of radius $$r_{1}$$ is inserted symmetrically into a vertical capillary tube of radius $$r_{2}$$ such that their lower ends are at the same level. The arrangement is now dipped in water. The height to which water will rise into the tube will be ( $$s=$$ surface tension of water, $$\\rho=$$ density of water)','[{"text": "$$\\\\dfrac{2 S}{\\\\left(r_{2}-r_{1}\\\\right) \\\\rho g}$$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$$\\\\dfrac{S}{\\\\left(r_{2}-r_{1}\\\\right) \\\\rho g}$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$\\\\dfrac{2 S}{\\\\left(r_{2}+r_{1}\\\\right) \\\\rho g}$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$\\\\dfrac{2 S}{\\\\left(r_{2}^{2}+r_{1}^{2}\\\\right) \\\\rho g}$$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'The free body diagram of the water that raises is given as<br><img src=\'/img/data/physics/26_37361_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' /> <br>$$\\begin{aligned}& F_{1}+F_{2}=F g \\\\& F_{1}=2 \\pi r_{1} S \\quad \\& \\quad F_{2}=2 \\pi r_{2} S \\\\& F_{g}=\\rho \\pi\\left(r_{2}^{2}-r_{1}^{2}\\right) h g\\end{aligned}$$<br>From the above equations<br>$$2 \\pi\\left(r_{1}+r_{2}\\right) s=\\rho \\pi\\left(r_{2}^{2}-r_{1}^{2}\\right) h g$$<br>we get $$h=\\dfrac{2 S}{\\rho g\\left(r_{2}-r_{1}\\right)}$$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/26_37361_question_1.png'),
(37364,'physics',29,1301,3149,'For a gas at a temperature $\\mathrm{T}$ the root-meansquare velocity $v_{r m s}$, the most probable speed $v_{m p}$, and the average speed $v_{a v}$ obey the relationship','[{"text": "$v_{m p}>v_{a v}>v_{r m s}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$v_{r m s}>v_{a v}>v_{m p}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$v_{m p}>v_{r m s}>v_{a v}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$v_{a v}>v_{r m s}>v_{m p}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'$v_{r m s}>v_{a v}>v_{m p}$,<br>$v_{r m s}=\\sqrt{\\dfrac{3 R T}{M}}, v_{m p}=\\sqrt{\\dfrac{2 R T}{M}}$<br>and $v_{a v}=\\sqrt{\\dfrac{8 R T}{\\pi M}}$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37365,'physics',27,1382,3321,'The following figure shows two air-filled bulbs connected by a $U$-tube partly filled with water. What happens to the levels of alcohol in the limbs $X$ and $Y$. When an electric bulb placed midway between the bulbs is lighted?','[{"text": "The level of alcohol in limb $A$ rises while that in limb $Y$ falls.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "The level of alcohol in limb $X$ falls while that in limb $Y$ rises.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "There is no change in the levels of alcohol in the two limbs.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "The level of alcohol falls in both limbs.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Black bulb absorbs more heat in comparison with painted bulb. So air in black bulb expands more. Hence, the level of alcohol in limb $X$ falls while that in limb $Y$ rises.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/27_37365_question_1.png'),
(37366,'physics',26,1138,2731,'A capillary tube (\\[A\\]) is dipped in water. Another identical tube (\\[B\\]) is dipped in a soap-water solution. Which of the following shows the relative nature of the liquid columns in the two tubes<div class="matching-question"></div>','[{"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'$$h=\\dfrac{2 T \\cos \\theta}{r d g}$$, Height in capillary depends upon the surface tension of the liquid.<br>Surface tension of soap water solution is less than water. So, height will be less in second case.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37368,'physics',29,1305,3155,'The diameter of a gas molecule is $2.4 \\times 10^{-10} \\mathrm{~m}$. The mean free path of gas molecule at NTP is','[{"text": "$2.46 \\\\times {10^{ - 6}} m$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1.46 \\\\times {10^{ - 7}} m$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$2.46 \\\\times {10^{ - 7}} m$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1.46 \\\\times {10^{ - 6}} m$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'At NTP, $T = 273 K,\\quad p = 1.01 \\times {10^5}N{m^{ - 2}}$ Here, $d = 2.4 \\times {10^{ - 10}} m$<br>$\\lambda  = \\frac{{kT}}{{\\sqrt 2 \\pi {d^2}p}}$<br>$ = \\frac{{\\left( {1.38 \\times {{10}^{ - 23}}} \\right) \\times 273}}{{1.414 \\times 3.14 \\times {{\\left( {2.4 \\times {{10}^{ - 10}}} \\right)}^2} \\times 1.01 \\times {{10}^5}}}$<br>$ = 1.46 \\times {10^{ - 7}}m$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37369,'physics',26,1138,2732,'Two very wide parallel glass plates are held vertically at a small separation $d$, and dipped in water. Some water climbs up in the gap between the plates. Let $T$ be the surface tension of water, $$p_{o}=$$ atmospheric pressure, $$p=$$ pressure of water just below the water surface in the region between the plates. Then predict the correct option','[{"text": "$$p=p_{o}-\\\\dfrac{2 T}{d}$$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$$p=p_{o}+\\\\dfrac{2 T}{d}$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$p=p_{o}-\\\\dfrac{4 T}{d}$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$p=p_{o}+\\\\dfrac{4 T}{d}$$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'The top surface of the liquid is curved and the pressure difference across the surface is<br>$$p_{0}-p=\\dfrac{T}{r}$$<br>where $$\\quad r=d / 2$$<br>$$\\begin{aligned}& \\Rightarrow p_{0}=p+\\dfrac{2 T}{d} \\\\& \\Rightarrow p=p_{0}-\\dfrac{2 T}{d}\\end{aligned}$$<br>Note : Pressure difference $$(\\Delta p)$$<br>$$\\begin{aligned}& \\Delta p=\\dfrac{2 T}{r}(\\text { spherical surface }) \\\\& \\Delta p=\\dfrac{T}{r}(\\text { Cylindrical surface })\\end{aligned}$$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/26_37369_question_1.png'),
(42457,'physics',35,710,1493,'Earth\'s magnetic field always has a horizontal component except at','[{"text": "Equator", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Magnetic pole", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "A latitude of $60^{\\\\circ}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "An inclination of $60^{\\\\circ}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'At poles field directs vertically. So the component of earth\'s field along horizontal is zero at poles.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37372,'physics',29,1303,3152,'The molar specific heat at constant pressure for a monoatomic gas is','[{"text": "$\\\\dfrac{3}{2} R$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{5}{2} R$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{7}{2} R$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$4 R$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'${C_P} - {C_V} = R \\Rightarrow {C_P} = R + {C_V} = R + \\frac{f}{2}R\\,\\,$<br>$ = R + \\frac{3}{2}R = \\frac{5}{2}R$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37373,'physics',27,1378,3304,'The graph \\[AB\\] shown in figure is a plot of temperature of a body in degree celsius and degree Fahrenheit. Then<div class="matching-question"></div>','[{"text": "Slope of line $A B$ is $5 / 9$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Slope of line $A B$ is $9 / 5$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Slope of line $AB$ is $3 / 9$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Slope of line is $1 / 9$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Standard equation of line $y=m x+c$ we get slope of the line $AB$ is $m=\\dfrac{100}{212-32}=\\dfrac{5}{9}$',NULL,'Easy','Graph/Figure',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/27_37373_question_1.png'),
(37374,'physics',26,1138,2731,'A long capillary tube of radius $0.2\\,\\,mm$ is placed vertically inside a beaker of water. The surface tension of water is $7 \\times {10^{ - 2}}\\,N/m$ and the angle of contact between glass and water is zero, if the tube is pushed into water so that only 5.0 $cm$ of its length is above the surface, then determine the angle of contact between the liquid and glass surface.','[{"text": "$$\\\\cos ^{-1}\\\\left(\\\\dfrac{4}{5}\\\\right)$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$\\\\cos ^{-1}\\\\left(\\\\dfrac{5}{7}\\\\right)$$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$$\\\\cos ^{-1}\\\\left(\\\\dfrac{3}{5}\\\\right)$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$\\\\cos ^{-1}\\\\left(\\\\dfrac{5}{4}\\\\right)$$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Here $S = 7.0 \\times {10^{ - 2}}\\,N/m$;<br>$r = 0.2 \\times {10^{ - 3}}\\,m,\\quad \\rho  = {10^3}\\,kg/{m^3}$<br>We know that the height of liquid rise in the tube is $$\\quad h=2 S \\cos \\theta / \\rho r g$$<br>where $$r$$ is the radius of the tube and $$\\theta$$ is the angle of contact.<br>$\\therefore h = \\frac{{2\\left( {7.0 \\times {{10}^{ - 2}}} \\right)}}{{\\left( {{{10}^3}} \\right)\\left( {0.2 \\times {{10}^{ - 3}}} \\right)(10)}} = 7\\,cm$<br>When the length of the capillary tube above the free surface of the liquid is less than the height of liquid that rise in the tube, radius of the free surface is not equal to the radius of the tube.<br>It is greater than $$r$$ as the surface tends to be flatter.<br><img src=\'/img/data/physics/26_37374_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' /> <br>From the figure $$r=R \\cos \\theta$$<br>$$R=\\dfrac{r}{\\cos \\theta}$$<br>We know that $$P_{0}-P_{1}=\\dfrac{2 S}{R}=\\dfrac{2 S \\cos \\theta}{r}$$<br>$$\\begin{aligned}& P_{2}=P_{0} \\Rightarrow P_{2}-P_{1}=\\rho g h \\\\& \\dfrac{2 S \\cos \\theta}{r}=\\rho g h\\end{aligned}$$<br>When the tube is long enough<br>$\\frac{{2S\\cos {\\theta _1}}}{{{r_1}}} = \\rho g{h_1}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,(1)$<br>When the tube is having smaller length<br>$\\frac{{2S\\cos {\\theta _2}}}{{{r_2}}} = \\rho g{h_2}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,(2)$<br>$\\frac{{\\cos {\\theta _1}}}{{\\cos {\\theta _2}}} = \\frac{{{h_1}}}{{{h_2}}}$<br>${\\theta _1} = {\\rm{ }}0^\\circ {\\rm{ }}\\,\\,\\,{\\mkern 1mu} {\\kern 1pt} {h_1} = 7\\,cm\\,\\,\\,{\\mkern 1mu} {\\kern 1pt} \\& \\,\\,\\,{\\mkern 1mu} {\\kern 1pt} {h_2} = 5\\,cm$<br>$\\cos {\\theta _2} = \\frac{{{h_2}}}{{{h_1}}} \\Rightarrow {\\theta _2} = {\\cos ^{ - 1}}\\left( {\\frac{5}{7}} \\right)$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37376,'physics',29,1303,3152,'For a gas molecule with 6 degrees of freedom the law of equipartition of energy gives the following relation between the molar specific heat $\\left(C_{V}\\right)$ and gas constant $(R)$','[{"text": "$C_{V}=R$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$C_{V}=\\\\dfrac{R}{2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$C_{V}=2 R$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$C_{V}=3 R$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'$\\frac{{{C_P}}}{{{C_V}}} = \\gamma  = 1 + \\frac{2}{f} \\Rightarrow \\gamma  = 1 + \\frac{2}{6} = \\frac{4}{3}$<br>${C_P} - {C_V} = R \\Rightarrow \\frac{4}{3}{C_V} - {C_V} = R$<br>$ \\Rightarrow \\frac{1}{3}{C_V} = R \\Rightarrow {C_V} = 3\\,\\,R$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37377,'physics',27,1381,3315,'Heat is supplied to a certain homogeneous sample of matter, at a uniform rate. Its temperature is plotted aganist time, as shown. Which of the following conclusions can be drawn<div class="matching-question"></div>','[{"text": "Its specific heat capacity is greater in the liquid state than in the solid state", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Its specific heat capacity is greater in the solid state than in the liquid state", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Its latent heat of vaporisation is smaller than its latent of fusion", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both (1) and (3) are correct", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'The horizontal parts of the curve, where the system absorbs heat at constant temperature must represent changes of state. Latent heats are proportional to lengths of the horizontal parts. In the sloping parts, specific heat capacity is inversely proportional to the slopes.',NULL,'Medium','Graph/Figure',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/27_37377_question_1.png'),
(42458,'physics',33,536,1080,'In the following circuit, the reading of the voltmeter will be: (in volt)','[{"text": "7.2", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "4.8", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "6", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "4", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Voltage on $40 \\mathrm{~cm}=4.8 \\mathrm{~V}$<br>Voltage on $60 \\mathrm{~cm}$ so $=\\dfrac{4.5}{40} \\times 60=7.2$ volt .',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/33_42458_question_1.png'),
(37378,'physics',26,1136,2715,'A non-viscous liquid is flowing through a frictionless duct, with cross-section varying as shown in figure. Which of the following graph represents the variation of pressure $$p$$ along the axis of the tube?<div class="matching-question"></div>','[{"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'According to the equation of continuity, when cross-section of duct decreases, the velocity of flow of liquid increases $$A v=$$ constant.<br>From Bernoulli\'s theorem,<br>$$\\mathrm{p}+\\dfrac{1}{2} \\rho v^{2}+\\rho \\mathrm{gh}=\\mathrm{constant}$$<br>When velocity increases pressure decreases.',NULL,'Hard','Graph/Figure',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/26_37378_question_1.png'),
(37380,'physics',29,1303,3152,'$4.0 \\,g$ of gas occupies 22.4 litres at NTP. The specific heat capacity of the gas at constant volume is $5.0J{\\text{ }}{K^{ - 1}}\\,mo{l^{ - 1}}$. If the speed of sound in this gas at NTP is $999 m{s^{ - 1}}$ then the specific heat capacity at constant pressure in $J{k^{ - 1}} mo{l^{ - 1}}$ is (Take gas $R = 8.3\\,J{\\text{ }}{K^{ - 1}} mo{l^{ - 1}})$','[{"text": "8", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "7", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "6.5", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "6", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'As the given conditions are at NTP $m = M = 4.0 \\,\\,g$<br>(As it occupies 22.4 liters)<br>${v_{rms}} = 999\\,\\,m/s\\quad R = 8.3J \\cdot {k^{ - 1}}mo{l^{ - 1}}$<br>${v_{rms}} = \\sqrt {\\frac{{\\gamma RT}}{M}}  \\Rightarrow \\gamma  = \\frac{{M{v^2}}}{{RT}} = 1.6$<br>$\\gamma  = \\frac{{{C_P}}}{{{C_V}}} \\Rightarrow {C_P} = \\gamma {C_V} = 1.6 \\times 5 = 8J.{k^{ - 1}} mo{l^{ - 1}}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37381,'physics',27,1381,3315,'A block of ice at \\[ - 10^\\circ C\\] is slowly heated and converted to steam at \\[100^\\circ C\\]. Which of the following curves represents the phenomenon qualitatively<div class="matching-question"></div>','[{"text": "", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Initially, on heating temperature rises from $ - 10^\\circ C$  to $0^\\circ C$. Then ice melts and temperature does not rise After the whole ice has melted, temperature begins to rise until it reaches $100^\\circ C$. Then it becomes constant, during vaporisation',NULL,'Medium','Graph/Figure',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37382,'physics',26,1136,2715,'The cross-sectional area of a horizontal tube increases along its length linearly, as we move in the direction of flow. The variation of pressure, as we move along its length in the direction of flow ( $$x$$-direction), is best depicted by which of the following graphs?<div class="matching-question"></div>','[{"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'From the equation of continuity and Bernoulli\'s theorem $$A v=$$ constant<br>$p + \\frac{1}{2}\\rho {v^2} + \\rho gh = {\\text{constant}}$<br>When $A$ increases velocity decreases and the pressure increases non linearly.',NULL,'Medium','Graph/Figure',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37384,'physics',29,1303,3152,'A gaseous mixture enclosed in a vessel consists of one mole of a gas $A$ with $(\\gamma=5 / 3)$ and another $B$ with $(\\gamma=7 / 5)$ at a temperature $T$. The gases $A$ and $B$do not react with each other and assumed to be ideal. Find the number of moles of the gas $B$, if $\\gamma$ for the gaseous mixture is $(19 / 13)$.','[{"text": "3", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "2", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "6", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "4", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'We define specific heat capacity at constant volume<br>$C_{V}=\\dfrac{R}{(\\gamma-1)}$<br>For gas $A,$ $\\left(C_{V}\\right)_{A}=\\dfrac{R}{(5 / 3)-1}=\\dfrac{3}{2} R$<br>For gas $B,{\\left( {{C_V}} \\right)_B} = \\frac{R}{{(7/5) - 1}} = \\frac{5}{2}R$<br>For gaseous mixture,<br>$\\left(C_{V}\\right)_{\\text {mix }}=\\dfrac{R}{(19 / 13)-1}=\\dfrac{13}{6} R$<br>We define specific heat capacity of mixture<br>$\\begin{aligned}& \\left(C_{V}\\right)_{\\text {mix }}=\\dfrac{n_{1}\\left(C_{V}\\right)_{1}+n_{2}\\left(C_{V}\\right)_{2}}{n_{1}+n_{2}} \\\\& \\dfrac{13}{6} R=\\dfrac{1 \\times \\dfrac{3}{2} R+n \\times \\dfrac{5}{2} R}{1+n}=\\dfrac{(3+5 n)}{2(1+n)}\\end{aligned}$<br>or $13+13 n=9+15 n$, i.e., $n=2$ mole.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37385,'physics',26,1133,2710,'A large metal cylindrical cup floats in a rectangular tub half-filled with water. The tap is placed over the cup and turned on, releasing water at a constant rate. Eventually the cup sinks to the bottom and is completely submerged. Which of the following four graphs could represent the water level in the sink as a function of time?<div class="matching-question"></div>','[{"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'From Archimedes Principle, the amount of water displaced is equal to the amount of water added, so the rate the water level increases is the same before and after the cup is submerged. When the water level is at the edge of the cup, and as water is added, the water fills the cup, displacing air in the cup which results in a small decrease in the water level of the tub.',NULL,'Medium','Graph/Figure',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37386,'physics',27,1382,3323,'A ball of surface temperature \\(T\\) is in thermal equilibrium with its environment. Which of the curve gives the energy \\(E\\) radiated by the sphere as a function of time \' \\[t\\] \'?<div class="matching-question"></div>','[{"text": "1", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "4", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "2", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "3", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Temperature does not change when a body is in thermal equilibrium. Hence energy radiated remains same.<br>so option (1) is correct.',NULL,'Easy','Graph/Figure',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/27_37386_question_1.png'),
(42590,'physics',34,637,1310,'A magnetic field can be produced by','[{"text": "A changing electric field", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "A moving charge", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both of these", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "None of these", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'A moving charge and changing electric field both produce magnetic field.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37389,'physics',27,1382,3322,'The plots of intensity versus wavelength for three black bodies at temperatures \\(T_{1}, T_{2}\\), and \\(T_{3}\\) respectively are as shown. Their temperature are such that<div class="matching-question"></div>','[{"text": "$T_{1}>T_{3}>T_{2}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$T_{1}>T_{2}>T_{3}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$T_{3}>T_{2}>T_{1}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$T_{2}>T_{3}>T_{1}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'According to Wien\'s law $\\lambda_{m} \\propto \\dfrac{1}{T}$ and from the figure<br>$\\left(\\lambda_{m}\\right)_{1} < \\left(\\lambda_{m}\\right)_{3} < \\left(\\lambda_{m}\\right)_{2}$ therefore $T_{1}>T_{3}>T_{2}$.',NULL,'Medium','Graph/Figure',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/27_37389_question_1.png'),
(37390,'physics',26,1137,2723,'A body is dropped in a viscous fluid. Predict the correct graph between acceleration \\[vs\\] time.<div class="matching-question"></div>','[{"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'The acceleration of the body is maximum initially and decreases to zero when the body reaches terminal velocity.',NULL,'Medium','Graph/Figure',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37392,'physics',29,1302,3150,'Choose the only corret statement from the following:','[{"text": "The pressure of a gas is equal to the total kinetic energy of the molecules in a unit volume of the gas.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "The product of pressure and volume of a gas is always constant.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "The average kinetic energy of molecules of a gas is proportional to its absolute temperature.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "The average kinetic energy of molecules of a gas is proportional to the square root of its absolute temperature.<br>", "isCorrect": false}]',2,NULL,NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37393,'physics',27,1380,3311,'The variation of density of water with temperature is represented by the<div class="matching-question"></div>','[{"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Density of water is maximum at $4^\\circ C$ and is less on either side of this temperature.',NULL,'Easy','Graph/Figure',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37394,'physics',26,1138,2727,'Which graph represents the variation of surface tension with temperature over small temperature ranges for water<div class="matching-question"></div>','[{"text": "", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Surface tension decreases with increase in temperature. The approximate relation with temperature is $$T=C_{1}-C_{2} t$$<br>Where $$T$$ - Coefficient of surface tension $$t$$ - temperature.',NULL,'Medium','Graph/Figure',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37395,'physics',28,1377,3303,'Match the Column I (quantity) with Column II (value) and select the correct answer from the codes given below.<div class="matching-question"><div class="column-left">A $1\\,\\,cal = $ <br> B  $p{\\left( {\\frac{{\\Delta V}}{{\\Delta T}}} \\right)_p} = $<br> C  Specific heat capacity of solids<br> D For isothermal process, $W$</div><div class="column-right">P  $4.186\\,J/gK$ <br> Q  $nR$<br>R  $\\Delta Q$<br> S $3R$</div></div>','[{"text": "A-S,B-P,C-Q,D-R", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "A-P,B-Q,C-S,D-R", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "A-P,B-S,C-Q,D-R", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "A-Q,B-P,C-Q,D-R", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'$A.1\\,cal = 4.186\\,J/g\\,K$ which is the heat required to raise temperature of $1\\,g$ water from $14.5^\\circ C$ to $15.5^\\circ C$,<br>B. $P V=n R T$ when $P$ is contsant $P \\Delta V=n R \\Delta T \\Rightarrow p\\left(\\dfrac{\\Delta V}{\\Delta T}\\right)_{p}=n R$<br>C. Specific heat capacity of solids $=3 R$.<br>D. For isothermal process, $\\Delta T=0$<br>$\\Rightarrow \\Delta U=0$<br>From first law of thermodynamics<br>$\\Rightarrow W+\\Delta U=\\Delta Q$<br>$\\Rightarrow W+0=\\Delta Q \\Rightarrow W=\\Delta Q$<br>Hence, option (2) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Match the following',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37396,'physics',29,1302,3150,'Which of the following statements about kinetic theory of gases is wrong ?','[{"text": "The molecules of a gas are in continuous random motion", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "The molecules continuously undergo inelastic collisions", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "The molecules do not interact with each other except during collisions", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "The collisions amongst the molecules are of short duration", "isCorrect": false}]',1,NULL,NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37397,'physics',26,1137,2722,'A container is filled with non-viscous fluid of density \' $$\\rho$$ \'. Initially, the entire system is at rest. Now, the container starts rotating with a constant angular velocity $$\\omega$$ about its vertical axis. Choose the appropriate plot from below which depicts the shape of free surface of the fluid after a long time.<div class="matching-question"></div>','[{"text": "", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'As the liquid is non-viscous the rotation of container will not have any effect on the liquid.',NULL,'Medium','Graph/Figure',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/26_37397_question_1.png'),
(37398,'physics',27,1381,3315,'A solid material is supplied with heat at constant rate and the temperature of the material changes as shown. From the graph, the false conclusion drawn is<div class="matching-question"></div>','[{"text": "$AB$ represents the change of state from solid to liquid", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$AB$ and $CD$ of the graph represent phase changes", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$CD$ represents change of state from liquid to vapour", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Latent heat of fusion is twice the latent heat of vaporisation", "isCorrect": true}]',3,NULL,NULL,'Medium','Graph/Figure',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/27_37398_question_1.png'),
(37399,'physics',28,1371,3284,'A diatomic gas is heated at constant pressure. What fraction of the heat energy is used to increase the internal energy?','[{"text": "$3 / 7$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$3 / 5$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$5 / 9$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$5 / 7$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'$\\Delta Q=n C_{P} \\Delta T$<br>$\\Delta U=n C_{V} \\Delta T$<br>$\\dfrac{\\Delta U}{\\Delta Q}=\\dfrac{C_{V}}{C_{P}}=\\dfrac{1}{\\gamma}=\\dfrac{5}{7}$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37400,'physics',29,1302,3150,'Mean kinetic energy per degree of freedom of gas molecules is','[{"text": "$\\\\dfrac{3}{2} k T$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{3}{2} R T$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{1}{2} k T$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$k T$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'According to law of equipartition of energy, kinetic energy per degree of freedom of a gas molecule is $\\dfrac{1}{2} k T$.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37401,'physics',26,1133,2710,'A metallic cylinder of radius $$r$$ and height $$h$$ is totally submerged in a liquid contained in a beaker as shown in the figure. If force \\[F\\] is pulling the string with a constant velocity $$v$$, the graph showing the variation of force $$F$$ against displacement of cylinder is best represented by:<div class="matching-question"></div>','[{"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Consider the cylinder when it is inside the liquid. Both weight and buoyancy forces are constant.<br>$$F=m g-F_{b}$$<br>when the cylinder is coming out from the liquid then $$F_{b}$$ decreases. When the cylinder comes out finally $$F_{b}=0$$ and $F = mg.$',NULL,'Hard','Graph/Figure',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/26_37401_question_1.png'),
(37402,'physics',27,1382,3317,'A long metallic bar is carrying heat from one of its ends to the other end under steady-state. The variation of temperature \\(\\theta\\) along the length \\(x\\) of the bar from its hot end is best described by which of the following figures?<div class="matching-question"></div>','[{"text": "", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Let $\\theta_{0}$ is the temperature of the hot end and $\\theta$ is the temperature at $a$ distance $x$ from hot end<br>$\\begin{aligned}& \\dfrac{d Q}{d t}=\\dfrac{k A\\left(\\theta_{0}-\\theta\\right)}{x} \\\\& \\Rightarrow \\theta_{0}-\\theta=\\dfrac{x}{k A} \\dfrac{d Q}{d t} \\Rightarrow \\theta=\\theta_{0}-\\dfrac{x}{k A} \\dfrac{d Q}{d t}\\end{aligned}$<br>Hence $\\dfrac{d Q}{d t}$ is constant throughout the rod. The graph between $\\theta$ and $x$ is a straight line.',NULL,'Medium','Graph/Figure',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37403,'physics',28,1369,3279,'$1\\,c{m^3}$ of water at its boiling point absorbs $540\\,cal$ of heat to become steam with a volume of  $1671\\,c{m^3}$ and pressure of  $1.013 \\times {10^5}N{m^{ - 2}}$ and the mechanical equivalent of heat $ = 4.19J\\,ca{l^{ - 1}}$. The energy spent in this process in overcoming intermolecular forces is','[{"text": "$40\\\\,cal$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$540\\\\,cal$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{Zero}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$500\\\\,cal$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'$\\Delta Q=\\Delta U+W$<br>$\\Delta U = \\Delta Q - W = mL - P\\left( {{V_2} - {V_1}} \\right)$<br>$ = 1 \\times 540 - \\frac{{1.013 \\times {{10}^5}(1671 - 1){{10}^{ - 6}}}}{{4.2}}$<br>$ = 540 - 40 = 500\\,cal$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37404,'physics',29,1300,3145,'A gas at absolute temperature $300 K$ has pressure $ = 4 \\times {10^{ - 10}} N/{m^2}$. Boltzmann constant $ = k = 1.38 \\times {10^{ - 23}} J / K$. The number of molecules per $c{m^3}$ is of the order','[{"text": "$10^{5}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "100", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$10^{11}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$10^{8}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Pressure $P V=\\dfrac{N}{N_{A}} R T=N k T$<br>$ \\Rightarrow \\frac{N}{V} = \\frac{P}{{kT}}$<br>$\\frac{N}{V} = \\frac{P}{{kT}} = \\frac{{4 \\times {{10}^{ - 10}}}}{{1.38 \\times {{10}^{ - 23}} \\times 300}} \\cong {10^{11}}{\\rm{ }}per{\\rm{ }}{m^3}$<br>$ = {10^5}/c{m^3}.$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37405,'physics',26,1138,2730,'A soap bubble is blown with the help of mechanical pump at the mouth of a tube. The pump produces a certain increase in the volume of the bubble per minute, irrespective of its internal pressure. The graph between the pressure inside the soap bubble and time $$t$$ will be<div class="matching-question"></div>','[{"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'$$\\Delta P=\\dfrac{4 T}{r} \\therefore \\Delta P \\propto \\dfrac{1}{r}$$<br>Where $$r$$ is the radius of the bubble.<br>$$\\Delta P$$ is the pressure difference between inside and outside pressures.<br>As radius of soap bubble increases with time<br>$$\\therefore \\Delta P \\propto \\dfrac{1}{r} \\text {. }$$',NULL,'Medium','Graph/Figure',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37406,'physics',27,1381,3314,'The temperature across two different slabs \\[A\\] and \\[B\\] connected in series are shown in the steady state ( as shown in fig ). The two slabs have same cross-sectional area. The ratio of thermal conductivities of \\[A\\] and \\[B\\] is<div class="matching-question"></div>','[{"text": "$1: 3$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$2: 3$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$5: 3$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1: 1$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'As the slabs are connected in series the rate of heat flow across them must be same<br>$\\begin{aligned}& \\dfrac{K_{1} A \\Delta T_{1}}{\\Delta l_{1}}=\\dfrac{K_{2} A \\Delta T_{2}}{\\Delta l_{2}} \\\\& \\dfrac{K_{1} \\times 10}{3}=\\dfrac{K_{2} \\times 30}{3} \\Rightarrow \\dfrac{K_{1}}{K_{2}}=\\dfrac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}$',NULL,'Hard','Graph/Figure',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/27_37406_question_1.png'),
(37407,'physics',28,1371,3284,'In an isobaric process, the correct ratio is','[{"text": "$\\\\Delta Q: \\\\Delta U=1: 1$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\Delta Q: \\\\Delta U=1: \\\\gamma-1$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\Delta Q: \\\\Delta U=\\\\gamma-1: 1$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\Delta Q: \\\\Delta U=\\\\gamma: 1$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'$\\Delta Q: \\Delta U=n C_{P} \\Delta T: n C_{V} \\Delta T=\\dfrac{C_{P}}{C_{V}}=\\gamma: 1$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37408,'physics',29,1300,3145,'A cylinder of 5 litre capacity, filled with air at NTP is connected with another evacuated cylinder of 30 litres of capacity. The resultant air pressure in both the cylinders will be (Assume isothermal condition)','[{"text": "$14.85 cm{\\\\text{  }}of{\\\\text{ }}Hg$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$38.85 \\\\,cm{\\\\text{  }}of{\\\\text{ }}Hg$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$21.85\\\\, cm{\\\\text{  }}of{\\\\text{ }}Hg$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$10.85 \\\\,cm{\\\\text{  }}of{\\\\text{ }}Hg$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Here temperature remains constant so<br>${P_1}{V_1} = {P_2}{V_2} \\Rightarrow 76 \\times 5 = {P_2} \\times 35$<br>$ \\Rightarrow {P_2} = \\frac{{76 \\times 5}}{{35}} = 10.85\\,\\,cm\\,\\,of\\,Hg.$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37409,'physics',26,1138,2731,'Which of the following graphs may represent the relation between the capillary rise $$h$$ and the radius $$r$$ of the capillary?<div class="matching-question"></div>','[{"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'The capillary rise $$h$$ is given by,<br>$$h=\\dfrac{2 S \\cos \\theta}{\\rho g r}$$<br>$$h r=$$ constant for a given glass and liquid.<br>The correct graph is hyperbolic in shape.',NULL,'Medium','Graph/Figure',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37410,'physics',27,1382,3322,'Shown below are the black body radiation curves at temperatures \\(T_{1}\\) and \\(T_{2}\\left(T_{2}>T_{1}\\right)\\). Which of the following plots is correct<div class="matching-question"></div>','[{"text": "", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'According to Wien\'s displacement law<br>$\\lambda_{m} \\propto \\dfrac{1}{T} \\Rightarrow \\lambda_{m_{2}} < \\lambda_{m_{1}}\\left[\\because T_{1} < T_{2}\\right]$<br>There fore $I-\\lambda$ graph for $T_{2}$ has lesser wavelength $\\left(\\lambda_{m}\\right)$ and so curve for $T_{2}$ will shift towards left side.',NULL,'Medium','Graph/Figure',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37411,'physics',28,1371,3292,'When 2 moles of air is given 70 calorie of heat, its temperature changes from $20^\\circ C$ to $25^\\circ C$ at constant pressure. The amount of heat required to rise the temperature of air through same range $\\left( {20^\\circ C} \\right.$ to $\\left. {25^\\circ C} \\right)$ at constant volume is $\\left(\\gamma=\\dfrac{7}{5}\\right)$','[{"text": "50 calorie", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "45 calorie", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "40 calorie", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "70 calorie", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'$\\Delta Q_{P}=n C_{P} \\Delta T$<br>$\\Delta Q_{V}=n C_{V} \\Delta T$<br>$ \\Rightarrow \\frac{{\\Delta {Q_P}}}{{\\Delta {Q_V}}} = \\gamma  \\Rightarrow \\Delta {Q_V} = 50\\,cal$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37412,'physics',29,1301,3147,'Relationship between $P, V$ and $E$ for a gas is ( $E$ is the kinetic energy of the gas)','[{"text": "$P V=\\\\dfrac{3}{2} E$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$V=\\\\dfrac{2}{3} E P$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$P=\\\\dfrac{3}{2} E V$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$P V=\\\\dfrac{2}{3} E$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'$P V=\\dfrac{1}{3} m N c^{2}=\\dfrac{2}{3} N\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2} m c^{2}\\right)$<br>$P V=\\dfrac{2}{3} E$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37413,'physics',26,1134,2712,'Which of the following diagrams (figure) does not represent a streamline flow?','[{"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'In a streamline flow the two streams cannot cross each other.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,'NCERT Exempler','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37414,'physics',27,1382,3322,'Which one of the following is \\(v_{m}-T\\) graph for perfectly black body? \\[{\\upsilon _m}\\] is the frequency of radiation with maximum intensity. \\(T\\) is the absolute temperature.<div class="matching-question"></div>','[{"text": "$A$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$B$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$C$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$D$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'From Wien\'s displacement law<br>$\\therefore T \\lambda_{m}=b \\Rightarrow T \\propto v_{m}$<br>The graph between $T\\,\\& \\,{\\upsilon _m}$ is straight line passing through origin. So option (3) is correct.',NULL,'Hard','Graph/Figure',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/27_37414_question_1.png'),
(37415,'physics',28,1371,3284,'A container having 1 mole of a gas at a temperature $27^\\circ C$ has a movable piston which maintains at constant pressure in container of 1 atm. The gas is compressed until temperature becomes $127^\\circ C$. The work done is $\\left( {{C_p}} \\right.$ for gas is $\\left. {7.03{\\mkern 1mu} \\,cal/mol{\\mkern 1mu} K} \\right)$','[{"text": "$831\\\\,J$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$703\\\\,J$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2035\\\\,J$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$121\\\\,J$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'At constant pressure<br>$\\Delta W = P\\Delta V = nR\\Delta T = 1 \\times 8.31 \\times 100 = 831\\,J$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37416,'physics',29,1301,3147,'Which of the following is a property of ideal gas','[{"text": "Forces exist between gas particles", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Particles have small voulme", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Collisions among particles are inelastic", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Particles have no volume", "isCorrect": true}]',3,NULL,NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37417,'physics',26,1134,2712,'Along a streamline','[{"text": "The velocity of fluid particle remains constant", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "The velocity of all fluid particle crossing a given position is constant", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "The velocity of all fluid particle at a given instant is constant", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "The speed of a fluid particle remains constant", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Along a streamline, the velocity of every fluid particle while crossing a given position is the same.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,'NCERT Exempler','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37418,'physics',27,1378,3304,'Two absolute scales $A$ and $B$ have triple points of water defined to be $200\\,A$ and $350\\,B$. What is the relation between $T_{A}$ and $T_{B}$ ?','[{"text": "$\\\\dfrac{T_{A}}{T_{B}}=\\\\dfrac{4}{7}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{T_{A}}{T_{B}}=\\\\dfrac{3}{7}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{T_{A}}{T_{B}}=\\\\dfrac{7}{3}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{T_{A}}{T_{B}}=\\\\dfrac{7}{4}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Given triple point of water on scale $A=200 A$<br>Triple point of water on scale $B=350 B$<br>We know that triple point of water on absolute scale<br>$\\begin{aligned}& =273.16 K \\\\& 200 A=350 B=273.16 K \\\\& 1 A=\\dfrac{273.16}{200} K \\text { and } 1 B=\\dfrac{273.16}{350} K\\end{aligned}$<br>If $T_{A}$ and $T_{B}$ are the triple point of water on two scales $A$ and $B$ then<br>$\\begin{aligned}& \\dfrac{273.16}{200} T_{A}=\\dfrac{273.16}{350} T_{B} \\\\& \\dfrac{T_{A}}{T_{B}}=\\dfrac{200}{350}=\\dfrac{4}{7} \\text { or } T_{A}=\\dfrac{4}{7} T_{B} .\\end{aligned}$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,'NCERT Exempler','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37419,'physics',28,1371,3282,'When an ideal gas in a cylinder was compressed isothermally by a piston, the work done on the gas was found to be $1.5 \\times 10^{4}$ joules. During this process','[{"text": "$3.6 \\\\times {10^3}cal$ of heat flowed into the gas", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$3.6 \\\\times {10^3}cal$ of heat flowed goes out from the gas", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$1.5 \\\\times {10^4}cal$ of heat flowed goes out from the gas", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1.5 \\\\times {10^4}cal$ of heat flowed into the gas", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'In isothermal compression, there is always an increase of heat which must flow out of the gas.<br>$$\\Delta Q = \\Delta U + \\Delta W \\Rightarrow \\Delta Q = \\Delta W\\quad [\\because \\Delta U = 0]$$<br>$ \\Rightarrow \\Delta Q =  - 1.5 \\times {10^4}\\,J = \\frac{{1.5 \\times {{10}^4}}}{{4.18}}cal =  - 3.6 \\times {10^3}cal.$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37420,'physics',29,1300,3145,'It is decided to verify Boyle\'s law over a wide range of temperature and pressures. The most suitable gas to be selected for this purpose is','[{"text": "Carbon dioxide", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Methane", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Oxygen", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Hydrogen", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Hydrogen gas can be liquified at $ - 240^\\circ C$. So this gas is more preferable among the others as it liquifies at very low temperature.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37421,'physics',26,1135,2714,'An ideal fluid flows through a pipe of circular cross-section made of two sections with diameters $2.5\\,cm$ and $3.75\\,cm$. The ratio of the velocities in the two pipes is','[{"text": "$$9: 4$$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$$3: 2$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$\\\\sqrt{3}: \\\\sqrt{2}$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$\\\\sqrt{2}: \\\\sqrt{3}$$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'According to equation of continuity<br>$$\\begin{gathered}a_{1} v_{1}=a_{2} v_{2} \\\\\\dfrac{v_{1}}{v_{2}}=\\dfrac{a_{2}}{a_{1}}=\\dfrac{\\pi d_{2}^{2} / 4}{\\pi d_{1}^{2} / 4}=\\left(\\dfrac{d_{2}}{d_{1}}\\right)^{2}=\\left(\\dfrac{3.75}{2.50}\\right)^{2}=\\dfrac{9}{4}\\end{gathered}$$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,'NCERT Exempler','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(42591,'physics',35,707,1481,'The ultimate individual unit of magnetism in any magnet is called','[{"text": "South pole", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "North pole", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Quadrapole", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Dipole", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Monopole does not exist.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37422,'physics',27,1380,3311,'An aluminium sphere is dipped into water. Which of the following is true?','[{"text": "Buoyancy will be less in water at $0^\\\\circ C$ than that is water at $4^\\\\circ C$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Buoyancy will be more in water at $0^\\\\circ C$ than that is water at $4^\\\\circ C$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Buoyancy in water at $0^\\\\circ C$will be same as that in water at $4^\\\\circ C$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Buoyancy may be more or less in water at $4^\\\\circ C$ depending on the radius of the sphere", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Let $V$ be the volume of the sphere and $\\rho$ be the density of water. Buoyancy ($F$) on the sphere due to water is<br>$F=V \\rho g$<br>Since, $\\rho_{0{ }^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C}} < \\rho_{4{ }^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C}}$, so, $F_{0^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C}} < F_{4{ }^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C}}$.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,'NCERT Exempler','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37423,'physics',28,1371,3289,'If $\\gamma$ denotes the ratio of two specific heats of gas, the ratio of slope of adiabatic and isothermal $\\mathrm{PV}$ curves at their point of intersection is','[{"text": "$\\\\gamma$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\gamma / 1$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\gamma+1$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\gamma-1$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Slope of adiabatic curve $=\\gamma \\times$ (Slope of isothermal curve)',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37424,'physics',29,1300,3145,'An ideal gas is that which','[{"text": "Cannot be liquified", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Can be easily liquified", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Has strong intermolecular forces", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Has a larger size of molecules", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Intermolecular forces should not be present among ideal gas particle. So it can\'t be liquified.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37425,'physics',27,1380,3306,'As the temperature is increased, the time period of a pendulum.','[{"text": "Increase as the effective length increases even though its centre of mass still remains at the centre of the bob.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Decreases as its effective length increases even through its centre of mass still remains at the centre of the bob.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Increases as its effective length increases due to shifting of centre of mass below below centre of the bob", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Decreases as its effective length increases remains same but centre of the mass shifts above the centre of the bob.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'With increase in temperature, the effective length $(l)$ of simple pendulum increases even through its centre of mass still remains at the centre of the bob. Time period, $T=2 \\pi \\sqrt{l / g}$ or $T \\propto \\sqrt{l}$. $T$ increases as temperature increases',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,'NCERT Exempler','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37426,'physics',26,1137,2723,'A tall cylinder is filled with viscous oil. A round pebble is dropped from the top with zero initial velocity. From the plot shown in figure, indicate the one that represents the velocity $$(v)$$ of the pebble as function of time $$(t)$$.<div class="matching-question"></div>','[{"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'When the pebble is dropped from the top of a tall cylinder, filled with viscous oil the pebble acquire terminal velocity (i.e., constant velocity) after some time.',NULL,'Medium','Graph/Figure',NULL,'NCERT Exempler','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37427,'physics',28,1371,3283,'A sound wave passing through air at NTP produces a pressure of  $0.001\\,dyne/c{m^2}$ during a compression. The corresponding change in temperature (given $\\gamma=1.5$ and assume gas to be ideal) is','[{"text": "$8.97 \\\\times {10^{ - 8}}\\\\,K$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$8.97 \\\\times {10^{ - 4}}\\\\,K$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$8.97 \\\\times {10^{ - 6}}\\\\,K$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "None of these", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'We generally assume that a sound wave propagates under adiabatic condition.<br>$T^{\\gamma} \\cdot P^{(1-\\gamma)}=$ const.<br>Differentiating<br>$\\gamma \\cdot T^{\\gamma-1} \\cdot d T \\cdot P^{(1-\\gamma)}+T^{\\gamma} \\cdot(1-\\gamma) P^{-\\gamma} \\cdot d P=0$<br>$\\Rightarrow d T=\\dfrac{(\\gamma-1) T}{\\gamma P} d P$<br>$ \\Rightarrow dT = \\left( {\\frac{{1.5 - 1}}{{1.5}}} \\right)\\left( {\\frac{{273}}{{{{10}^5}\\left( {\\,N/{m^2}} \\right)}} \\times 0.001 \\times \\frac{{{{10}^{ - 5}}}}{{{{10}^{ - 4}}}}\\frac{N}{{{m^2}}}} \\right)$<br>$ = 8.97 \\times {10^{ - 8}}\\,K$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37428,'physics',29,1300,3146,'A real gas can be approximated to an ideal gas at','[{"text": "Low density", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "High pressure", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "High density", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Low temperature", "isCorrect": false}]',0,NULL,NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37429,'physics',26,1134,2712,'In streamline flow, which of the following statement (s) is /are correct?<br>I. Every fluid particle follows the path of its preceding particle with same velocity.<br>II. Eddies and whirls are formed.<br>III. Speed of particle is less than the critical speed.','[{"text": "Only I", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both I and III", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Only III", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both II and III", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Statements I and III are correct but II is incorrect since eddies and whirls are formed in turbulent flow.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ type-2',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37430,'physics',27,1380,3306,'A bimetallic strip is made of aluminium and steel $\\left(\\alpha_{A l}>\\alpha_{\\text {steel }}\\right)$. On heating, the strip will','[{"text": "Remain straight", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Get twisted", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Will bend with aluminium on concave side", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Will bend with steel on concave side", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Since, $\\alpha_{A l}>\\alpha_{\\text {steel }}$, so in bimetallic strip on heating, aluminium strip will expand more than steel strip. Due to it, aluminium strip will bend more on convex side and steel strip on concave side.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,'NCERT Exempler','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37431,'physics',28,1371,3283,'During an adiabatic process, the density of a gas is found to be proportional to the cube of temperature. The degree of freedom of gas molecule is','[{"text": "6", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "5", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "4", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "3", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'For adiabatic process, $T{V^{\\gamma  - 1}} = $ constant<br>$T{\\left( {\\frac{m}{\\rho }} \\right)^{\\gamma  - 1}} = $ constant<br>$\\dfrac{T}{\\rho^{\\gamma-1}}=$ constant<br>$\\rho \\propto T^{1 / \\gamma-1} \\Rightarrow \\dfrac{1}{\\gamma-1}=3 \\Rightarrow \\gamma=4 / 3$<br>$f=\\dfrac{2}{\\gamma-1}=\\dfrac{2}{\\left(\\dfrac{4}{3}-1\\right)}=6$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37432,'physics',29,1300,3146,'Ideal gas and real gas have major difference of','[{"text": "Temperature", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Phase transition", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Pressure", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "None of these", "isCorrect": false}]',1,NULL,NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37434,'physics',27,1380,3310,'The radius of a metal sphere at room temperature $T$ is $R$, and the coefficient of linear expansion of the metal is $\\alpha$. The sphere is heated a little by a temperature $\\Delta T$ so that its new temperature is $T+\\Delta T$. The increase in the volume of the sphere is approximiately','[{"text": "$2 \\\\pi R \\\\alpha \\\\Delta T$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\pi R^{2} \\\\alpha \\\\Delta T$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$4 \\\\pi R^{2} \\\\alpha \\\\Delta T / 3$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$4 \\\\pi R^{3} \\\\alpha \\\\Delta T$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Let $V$ be the volume sphere of radius $R$ at temperature $T$ then $V=\\dfrac{4}{3} \\pi R^{3}$.<br>Increase in volume of sphere with rise in temperature $\\Delta T$ is<br>$\\begin{aligned}& \\Delta V=\\gamma V \\Delta T=3 \\alpha \\times \\dfrac{4}{3} \\pi R^{3} \\Delta T \\\\& \\Delta V=4 \\pi R^{3} \\alpha \\Delta T\\end{aligned}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,'NCERT Exempler','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37435,'physics',28,1371,3283,'An ideal gas enclosed in a vertical cylindrical container supports a freely moving piston of mass $M$. The piston and the cylinder have equal cross-sectional area $A$. When the piston is in equilibrium the volume of the gas is $V_{0}$ and its pressure is $P_{0}$. The piston is slightly displaced from the equilibrium position and released. Assuming that the system is completely isolated from its surrounding, the piston executes a simple harmonic motion with frequency.','[{"text": "$\\\\frac{1}{{2\\\\pi }}\\\\frac{{A\\\\gamma {P_0}}}{{{V_0}M}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{1}{{2\\\\pi }}\\\\frac{{{V_0}M{P_0}}}{{{A^2}\\\\gamma }}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{1}{{2\\\\pi }}\\\\sqrt {\\\\frac{{{A^2}\\\\gamma {P_0}}}{{M{V_0}}}} $", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{1}{{2\\\\pi }}\\\\sqrt {\\\\frac{{M{V_0}}}{{A\\\\gamma {P_0}}}} $", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'When piston is slightly displaced through equilibrium position, it will oscillate under a net restoring force. The piston will perform SHM.<br>Pressure applied by piston<br>$\\dfrac{M g}{A}=P_{0}$ (At equilibrium)<br>$M g=P_{0} A$<br><img src=\'/img/data/physics/28_37435_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' /> <br>Let piston be moved by a distance $x$<br>Here, the system is completely isolated so it is adiabatic.<br>$\\begin{aligned}& p_{0} V_{0}^{\\gamma}=p V^{\\gamma} \\\\& p_{0} A x_{0}^{\\gamma}=p A^{\\gamma}\\left(x_{0}-x\\right)^{\\gamma} \\\\& p=\\dfrac{p_{0} x_{0}^{\\gamma}}{\\left(x_{0}-x\\right)^{\\gamma}}\\end{aligned}$<br>The equation of motion for the piston<br>$Mg - \\left( {\\frac{{{p_0}x_0^\\gamma }}{{{{\\left( {{x_0} - x} \\right)}^\\gamma }}}} \\right)A = {F_{restoring{\\rm{ }}}}$<br>${p_0}A\\left( {1 - \\frac{{x_0^\\gamma }}{{{{\\left( {{x_0} - x} \\right)}^\\gamma }}}} \\right) = {F_{restoring{\\rm{ }}}}$<br>${p_0}A\\left[ {1 - \\frac{1}{{\\left( {1 - \\frac{x}{{{x_0}}}} \\right)}}\\gamma } \\right] = {F_{restoring{\\rm{ }}}}$<br>${p_0}A\\left( {1 - {{\\left( {1 - \\frac{x}{{{x_0}}}} \\right)}^{ - \\gamma }}} \\right) = {F_{restoring{\\rm{ }}}}$<br>$F = Ma =  - \\frac{{\\gamma {p_0}Ax}}{{{x_0}}}\\left( {{{\\left( {1 - \\frac{x}{{{x_0}}}} \\right)}^{ - \\gamma }} \\approx 1 + \\frac{{\\gamma x}}{{{x_0}}}} \\right)$<br>$a =  - \\left( {\\frac{{\\gamma {p_0}A}}{{M{x_0}}}} \\right)x =  - {\\omega ^2}x =  - 4{\\pi ^2}{v^2}x$<br>$ \\Rightarrow v = \\frac{1}{{2\\pi }}\\sqrt {\\frac{{\\gamma {p_0}A}}{{{x_0}M}}}  = \\frac{1}{{2\\pi }}\\sqrt {\\frac{{\\gamma {p_0}{A^2}}}{{M{V_0}}}} $<br>$$\\left[ {\\because {x_0} = \\frac{{{V_0}}}{A}} \\right]$$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37436,'physics',29,1301,3147,'The temperature of a gas is raised while its volume remains constant, the pressure exerted by the gas on the walls of the container increases because its molecules.','[{"text": "Lose more kinetic energy to the wall", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Are in contact with thewall for a shorter time", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Strike the wall more often with higher velocities", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Collide with each other with less frequency.<br>", "isCorrect": false}]',2,NULL,NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37437,'physics',26,1137,2723,'I. Viscous force is proportional to the velocity of the object.<br>II. Viscous force is a dragging force which acts opposite to the direction of motion.<br>III. Viscous force depends on viscosity $$\\eta$$ of the fluids and radius $$r$$ of the sphere.<br>Which of the following statements(s) is/are correct?','[{"text": "Only I", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both I and II", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Only III", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "I, II and III", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'$$f=6 \\pi \\eta r v$$. So option (4) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ type-2',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37438,'physics',27,1378,3304,'The triple point of neon and carbon dioxide are 24.57$K$ and $216.55\\,K$  respectively. These temperatures on the celsius and fahrenheit scales are respectively.','[{"text": "$ - 415.44^\\\\circ F$ and $ - 69.88\\\\,^\\\\circ F$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$415.44\\\\,^\\\\circ F$ and $69.88\\\\,^\\\\circ F$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$ - 315.44\\\\,^\\\\circ F$ and $69.88\\\\,^\\\\circ F$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$ - 69.88\\\\,^\\\\circ F$ and $415.44\\\\,^\\\\circ F$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Triple point of neon, ${T_1} = 24.57\\,K$<br>Triple point of $C{O_2},\\,{T_2} = 216.55\\,K$<br>On celsius of neon,<br>$t_1^oC = 24.57 - 273.15 =  - {\\text{ }}248.58^\\circ C$<br>Triple point of $C{O_2},t_2^{\\text{o}}C = 216.55 - 273.15$<br>$ =  - 56.60^\\circ C$<br>The temperatures in Fahrenheit scale are<br>${F_1} = 32 + \\frac{9}{5}( - 248.58) =  - 415.44^\\circ F$<br>${F_2} = 32 + \\frac{9}{5}( - 56.6) =  - 69.88^\\circ F$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,'NCERT Exempler','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37439,'physics',28,1371,3289,'A monoatomic gas at a pressure $P$, having a volume $V$ expands isothermally to a volume $2\\,V$ and then adiabatically to a volume $16\\,V$. The final pressure of the gas is : ( take $\\left.\\gamma=\\dfrac{5}{3}\\right)$','[{"text": "$\\\\dfrac{P}{4}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{P}{32}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{P}{64}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{P}{128}$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'For isothermal process<br>$\\dfrac{P_{1}}{P_{2}}=\\dfrac{V_{2}}{V_{1}}=2$<br>For adiabatic process<br>$\\begin{aligned}& \\dfrac{P_{2}}{P_{3}}=\\left(\\dfrac{V_{3}}{V_{2}}\\right)^{5 / 3} \\Rightarrow \\dfrac{P_{2}}{P_{3}}=\\left(\\dfrac{16 V}{2 V}\\right)^{5 / 3}=2^{5}=32 \\\\& \\Rightarrow P_{3}=\\dfrac{P_{2}}{32} \\Rightarrow P_{2}=\\dfrac{P}{64}\\end{aligned}$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37440,'physics',29,1299,3144,'At absolute zero temperature, the kinetic energy of the molecules:','[{"text": "Becomes zero", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Becomes maximum", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Becomes minimum", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Remains constant", "isCorrect": false}]',0,NULL,NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37441,'physics',27,1380,3306,'A hole is drilled in a copper sheet. The diameter of the hole is $4.24\\,cm$ at $27^\\circ C$. What is the change in the diameter of the hole when the sheet is heated to $227^\\circ C$ ? Coefficient of linear expansion of copper is $1.70 \\times {10^{ - 5}}/\\,^\\circ C$.','[{"text": "$1.44 \\\\times {10^{ - 2}}\\\\,cm$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$2.44 \\\\times {10^{ - 3}}\\\\,cm$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1.44 \\\\times {10^{ - 2}}\\\\,mm$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2.44 \\\\times {10^{ - 2}}\\\\,mm$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Given, diameter of the hole<br>$\\left( {{d_1}} \\right) = 4.24\\,cm$<br>Initial temperature<br>$T_{1}=27+273=300 K$<br>Final temperature ${T_2} = 227 + 273 = 500\\,K$<br>Coefficient of linear expansion<br>$(\\alpha ) = 1.70 \\times {10^{ - 5}}/^\\circ C$<br>Coefficient of areal expansion $(\\beta)=2 \\alpha$<br>$ = 3.40 \\times {10^{ - 5}}/^\\circ C$<br>Initial area of hole at $27^{\\circ} C\\left(A_{1}\\right)=\\pi r^{2}=\\dfrac{\\pi d_{1}^{2}}{4}$<br>$\\frac{\\pi }{4}{(4.24)^2} = 4.494\\pi c{m^2}$<br>Area of hole at $227^{\\circ} C\\left(A_{2}\\right)=A_{1}(1+\\beta . \\Delta t)$<br>$\\begin{aligned}& =4.494 \\pi\\left[1+3.40 \\times 10^{-5} \\times(227-27)\\right] \\\\& =4.495 \\pi \\times 1.0068\\end{aligned}$<br>If diameter of hole becomes ${d_2}\\,{\\rm{at}}\\,227^\\circ C$, then<br>${A_2} = \\frac{{\\pi d_2^2}}{4}4.525\\pi  = \\frac{{\\pi d_2^2}}{4}{\\rm{ }}$<br>${\\rm{or }}{d_2} = 4.2544\\,cm$<br>Change in diameter $(\\Delta d) = {d_2} - {d_1}$<br>$ = 1.44 \\times {10^{ - 2}}\\,cm$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,'NCERT Exempler','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37442,'physics',26,1138,2729,'<div class="matching-question"><div class="column-left">The angle of contact of a liquid with a solid increases with increase in temperature of liquid.<br></div><div class="column-right">With increase in temperature, the surface tension of the liquid decreases.</div></div>','[{"text": "Statement A is correct but Statement B is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Statement A is incorrect but Statement B is correct.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both statements are correct.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Statements are incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Angle of contact increases with increase in temperature. The surface tension of a liquid decreases with increase in temperature. So option (3) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ type-2',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37443,'physics',28,1371,3286,'Pressure $P$, volume $V$ and temperature $T$ of a certain material are related by $P = \\alpha {T^2}/V$, where $\\alpha$ is constant. Work done by the material when temperature changes from $T_{0}$ and $2 T_{0}$ and pressure remains constant is','[{"text": "$5\\\\,\\\\,\\\\alpha \\\\,T_0^2$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$3\\\\,\\\\alpha \\\\,T_0^2$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$7\\\\,\\\\alpha \\\\,T_0^2$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{3}{2}\\\\,\\\\,\\\\alpha \\\\,T_0^2$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'The given relation is $P = \\frac{{\\alpha {T^2}}}{V}$<br>$\\therefore \\quad V = \\frac{{\\alpha {T^2}}}{P}$<br>As pressure is kept constant,<br>$\\therefore \\quad dV = \\left( {\\frac{{2\\alpha T}}{P}} \\right)dT$<br>Now, $W = \\int P dV = \\int_{{T_0}}^{2{T_0}} P \\left( {\\frac{{2\\alpha T}}{P}} \\right)dT$<br>$\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, = 2\\alpha \\left[ {\\frac{{{T^2}}}{2}} \\right]_{{T_0}}^{2{T_0}} = 3\\alpha T_0^2$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37444,'physics',29,1300,3146,'A real gas deviates from perfect gas behaviour as its molecules','[{"text": "Have definite size", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Attract each other", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Are not spherical", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both (1) and (2)", "isCorrect": true}]',3,NULL,NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37445,'physics',27,1380,3311,'The coefficient of volume expansion of glycerine is $49 \\times 10^{-5} / K$. What is the fractional change in its density for a $30^\\circ C$ rise in temperature?','[{"text": "$1.45 \\\\times 10^{-3}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2.45 \\\\times 10^{-3}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2.45 \\\\times 10^{-2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1.45 \\\\times 10^{-2}$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Given, coefficient of volume expension<br>$(\\gamma)=49 \\times 10^{-5} / K$<br>Rise in temperature $(\\Delta T) = 30^\\circ C$<br>Let initial volume of glycerine be $V_{0}$.<br>Volume of glycerine when temperature is increased by $30^\\circ C$<br>$V = {V_0}[1 + \\gamma \\Delta T]$<br>$ = {V_0}\\left[ {1 + 49 \\times {{10}^{ - 5}} \\times 30} \\right]$<br>$ = {V_0}[1 + 0.01470] = 1.0147{V_0}$<br>$\\frac{{{V_0}}}{V} = \\frac{1}{{1.0147}}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\left( 1 \\right)$<br>$\\frac{\\rho }{{{\\rho _0}}} = \\frac{{m/V}}{{m/{V_0}}} = \\frac{{{V_0}}}{V} = \\frac{1}{{1.0147}}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\left( 2 \\right)$<br>Fractional change in density<br>$=\\dfrac{\\Delta \\rho}{\\rho_{0}}=\\dfrac{\\rho-\\rho_{0}}{\\rho_{0}}=\\dfrac{\\rho}{\\rho_{0}}-1$<br>Substituting value from Eq. (2), we get<br>Fractional change in density $=\\dfrac{1}{1.0147}-1$<br>$=-1.45 \\times 10^{-2}$<br>Negative sign shows that the density of glycerine decrease with rise in temperature',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,'NCERT Exempler','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37446,'physics',26,1138,2731,'<div class="matching-question"><div class="column-left">When height of a tube is less than liquid rise in the capillary tube, the liquid does not overflow.<br></div><div class="column-right">Product of radius of meniscus and height of liquid in the capillary tube always remain constant.</div></div>','[{"text": "Statement A is correct but Statement B is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Statement A is incorrect but Statement B is correct.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both statements are correct.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Statements are incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'$$\\dfrac{r h}{\\cos \\theta}=\\dfrac{2 T}{\\rho g}=$$ constant<br>Hence when the tube is of insufficient length, radius of curvature of the liquid meniscus increases, so as to maintain the $$\\dfrac{h r}{\\cos \\theta}$$ as constant, i.e., as $$h$$ decreases, $$r$$ increases and $$\\cos \\theta$$ decreases ( $$\\theta$$ increases) the liquid meniscus becomes more and more flat, but the liquid does not overflow. So option (3) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ type-2',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37447,'physics',28,1371,3286,'An ideal gas mixture filled inside a balloon expands according to the relation $P V^{2 / 3}=$ constant the temperature inside the balloon is','[{"text": "Decreasing", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Increasing", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "ss", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Constant", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Given that<br>$P V^{2 / 3}=\\text { constant }$<br>From ideal gas equation<br>$PV = nRT$<br>$ \\Rightarrow T{V^{ - 1/3}} = {\\rm{ }}const{\\rm{ }}$<br>$T\\alpha {V^{1/3}}$<br>As $V$ increases $T$ also increases.',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37448,'physics',29,1300,3145,'Boyle\'s law is applicable in','[{"text": "Isobaric process", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Isochoric process", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Isothermal process", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Adiabatic process", "isCorrect": false}]',2,NULL,NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37449,'physics',26,1133,2710,'<div class="matching-question"><div class="column-left">If an object is submerged in a fluid at rest, the fluid exerts a force on its surface.<br></div><div class="column-right">The force exerted by the fluid at rest has to be parallel to the surface in contact with it.</div></div>','[{"text": "Statement A is correct but Statement B is incorrect.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Statement A is incorrect but Statement B is correct.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both statements are correct.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Statements are incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'When an object is placed in fluid at rest, the fluid always exertes a force normal to the object\'s surface. So option (1) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ type-2',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37469,'physics',26,1133,2710,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> For a floating body to be in stable equilibrium, its centre of buoyancy must be located above the centre of gravity.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> The torque produced by the weight of the body and the upthrust will restore body back to its normal position, after the body is disturbed.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'The stability of a floating body depends on the relative position of center of gravity of a body, through which its weight acts and center of buoyancy through which the upthrust act. So option (1) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37450,'physics',27,1381,3315,'A child running at a temperature of  $101^\\circ F$ is given an antipyrin (i.e., medicine that lower fever)which causes an increase in the rate of evaporation of sweet from his body. If the fever is brought down to $97^\\circ F$ in $20\\,\\min $, what is the average rate of extra evaporation caused by the drug. Assume the evaporation mechanism to be the only way by which heat is lost. The mass of child is $30\\,kg$. The specific heat of the human body is approximately the same as that of water and latent heat of evaporation of water at that temperature is about $580\\,cal/g$.','[{"text": "$4.31\\\\,g/\\\\min $", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$4.31\\\\,g/s$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2.31\\\\,g/\\\\min $", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2.31\\\\,g/s$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Given, mass of the child $(m) = 30\\,kg$<br>Time taken $(t) = 20\\,\\min $<br>Fall in temperature $ = (101 - 98)^\\circ F$<br>$\\Delta T = 3\\,^\\circ F = 3 \\times \\frac{5}{9}{\\mkern 1mu} \\,^\\circ C = \\frac{5}{3}{\\mkern 1mu} \\,^\\circ C$<br>Specific heat of human body<br>$(s) = 4.2 \\times {10^3}\\,J/kg - \\,^\\circ C$<br>Latent heat of evaporation<br>$(L) = 580\\,cal/g = \\left( {580 \\times {{10}^3} \\times 4.2} \\right)J/kg$<br>Heat given by body during fall in temperature<br>$Q_{1}=m s \\Delta T$<br>Let $m^{\\prime}$ be the mass of sweat evaporates from the human body.<br>Heat required for evaporation<br>$Q_{2}=m^{\\prime} L$<br>But<br>${Q_1} = {Q_2} \\Rightarrow ms\\Delta T = {m^\\prime }L$<br>${m^\\prime } = \\frac{{ms\\Delta T}}{L} = \\frac{{30 \\times 4.2 \\times {{10}^3} \\times 5/3}}{{580 \\times 4.2 \\times {{10}^3}}}$<br>$ = \\frac{{10}}{{116}} = 0.0862\\,kg$<br>Rate of evaporation of sweat $=\\dfrac{0.0862}{20}$<br>$ = 0.00431\\,kg/\\min $<br>$ = 4.31\\,g/\\min $',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,'NCERT Exempler','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37451,'physics',28,1371,3288,'In the free expansion of a gas in an adiabatic vessel<br>(i) $W=0$  (ii) $\\Delta Q=0$  (iii) $\\Delta U=0$','[{"text": "(i)", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "(i) (ii) & (iii)", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "(iii)", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "(ii) & (iii)", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'In the free expansion $W=0, \\Delta Q=0$ (adiabatic vessel) then $\\Delta U=0$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37452,'physics',29,1303,3152,'If $\\gamma$ is the ratio of specific heats and $\\mathrm{R}$ is the universal gas constant, then the molar specific heat at constant volume $\\mathrm{C}_{\\mathrm{v}}$ is given by','[{"text": "$\\\\dfrac{R}{\\\\gamma-1}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{\\\\gamma R}{\\\\gamma-1}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\gamma \\\\mathrm{R}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{(\\\\gamma-1) R}{\\\\gamma}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'We know that<br>$\\frac{{{C_p}}}{{{C_v}}} = \\gamma \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,(1)$<br>But<br>${C_p} - {C_v} = R\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,(2)$<br>(2)<br>From (1) & (2)<br>$\\gamma C_{v}-C_{v}=R \\Rightarrow C_{v}=\\dfrac{R}{\\gamma-1}$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37453,'physics',26,1136,2716,'Match the following:<div class="matching-question"><div class="column-left">Aeroplanes are made to run on the runway before taking off, so that they achieve the necessary lift.<br></div><div class="column-right">This is based on Pascal\'s law.</div></div>','[{"text": "Statement A is correct but Statement B is incorrect.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Statement A is incorrect but Statement B is correct.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both statements are correct.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Statements are incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Usually the air does not strike the wings of the aeroplane with large velocity. To get the lift, the aeroplane runs for some distance on the runway before taking off. Due to the special shape of wings, the velocity of the layers of air above the wings increases and hence pressure decreases. Due to this aeroplane get uplift. This is based on Bernoulli\'s principle. So option (1) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ type-2',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37454,'physics',27,1381,3315,'A copper block of mass $2.5\\,kg$ is heated in furnance to a temperature of  $500^\\circ C$ and then placed on a large ice that can melt? (Specific heat of copper $ = 0.39\\,J/g - K$; heat of fusion of water $ = 335\\,J/g)$','[{"text": "$25\\\\,kg$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1.5\\\\,kg$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$9\\\\,kg$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$13\\\\,kg$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'<br>Given, mass of copper block $(m) = 2.5\\,kg$<br>Change in temperature $(\\Delta T) = 500^\\circ C$<br>Specific heat $(s) = 390\\,J/g - K$<br>Latent heat of fusion of water<br>$(L) = 335 \\times {10^3}\\,J/kg$<br>Heat energy absorbed by copper block<br>$Q_{1}=m s \\Delta T=2.5 \\times 390 \\times 500 J$<br>Let ${m^\\prime }kg$ of ice be melted.<br>Heat energy required to melt ice, $Q_{2}=m^{\\prime} L$<br>${Q_1} = {Q_2}$<br>$ms\\Delta T = {m^\\prime }L$<br>${m^\\prime } = \\frac{{ms\\Delta T}}{L}$<br>$ = \\frac{{2.5 \\times 390 \\times 500}}{{335 \\times {{10}^3}}} = 1.5\\,kg$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,'NCERT Exempler','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37456,'physics',29,1300,3145,'When the pressure of a gas is doubled under constant temperature the density becomes','[{"text": "Double", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Halved", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Does not change", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Decrease to $1 / 4$.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'The ideal gas equation is<br>$\\begin{aligned}& P V=n R T=\\dfrac{m}{M} R T=\\dfrac{\\rho V}{M} R T \\\\& P=\\dfrac{\\rho R T}{M}\\end{aligned}$<br>When temperature is constant<br>$\\dfrac{P_{1}}{\\rho_{1}}=\\dfrac{P_{2}}{\\rho_{2}} \\Rightarrow \\rho_{2}=\\dfrac{P_{2} \\rho_{1}}{P_{1}}=2 \\rho_{1}$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37457,'physics',26,1137,2722,'<div class="matching-question"><div class="column-left">When non ideal fluids flow, there is no loss of energy due to friction.<br></div><div class="column-right">Different layers of the fluid do not exert forces on each other.</div></div>','[{"text": "Statement A is correct but Statement B is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Statement A is incorrect but Statement B is correct.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both statements are correct.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Statements are incorrect.", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'In the flow of fluids, some energy does get lost due to internal friction. This arises due to the fact that in a fluid flow, the different layers of the fluid flow with different velocities. These layers exert frictional forces on each other resulting in a loss of energy. So option (4) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ type-2',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37458,'physics',27,1381,3314,'Three copper blocks of masses $M_{1}, M_{2}$ and $M_{3}$ $kg$ respectively are brought into thermal contact till they reach equilibrium. Before contact, they were at $T_{1}, T_{2}, T_{3}\\left(T_{1}>T_{2}>T_{3}\\right)$. Assuming there is no heat loss to the surroundings, the equilibrium temperature $T$ is ($s$ is specific heat of copper)','[{"text": "$T=\\\\dfrac{T_{1}+T_{2}+T_{3}}{3}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$T=\\\\dfrac{M_{1} T_{1}+M_{2} T_{2}+M_{3} T_{3}}{M_{1}+M_{2}+M_{3}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$T=\\\\dfrac{M_{1} T_{1}+M_{2} T_{2}+M_{3} T_{3}}{3\\\\left(M_{1}+M_{2}+M_{3}\\\\right)}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$T=\\\\dfrac{M_{1} T_{1} s+M_{2} T_{2} s+M_{3} T_{3} s}{M_{1}+M_{2}+M_{3}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Let $T$ is the equilibrium temperature of the system. The total change across the system is zero<br>$\\begin{gathered}\\Delta Q_{1}+\\Delta Q_{2}+\\Delta Q_{3}=0 \\\\M_{1} s\\left(T-T_{1}\\right)+M_{2} s\\left(T-T_{2}\\right)+M_{3} s\\left(T-T_{3}\\right)=0 \\\\M_{1} T_{1}+M_{2} T_{2}+M_{3} T_{3}=\\left(M_{1}+M_{2}+M_{3}\\right) T \\\\\\Rightarrow \\quad T=\\dfrac{M_{1} T_{1}+M_{2} T_{2}+M_{3} T_{3}}{M_{1}+M_{2}+M_{3}}\\end{gathered}$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,'NCERT Exempler','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37460,'physics',29,1300,3145,'Sometimes a cycle with well inflated tyres left in the sun has its tube burst often because','[{"text": "The volume of air inside the tube increases", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "The pressure of air inside the tube increases", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "The tube containing air melts", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "The volume and pressure both increase.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,NULL,NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37462,'physics',26,1136,2715,'<div class="matching-question"><div class="column-left">Bernoulli\'s equation hold for non - steady or turbulent flows.<br></div><div class="column-right">In these situations, velocity and pressure are constant with time.</div></div>','[{"text": "Statement A is correct but Statement B is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Statement A is incorrect but Statement B is correct.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both statements are correct.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Statements are incorrect.", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Bernoulli\'s equation does not hold for non steady or turbulent flow if liquid, it is because velocity and pressure are constantly fluctutating with time. So option (4) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ type-2',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37464,'physics',29,1306,3156,'The air of the atmosphere becomes cold at higher altitudes due to','[{"text": "Decrease in density", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Variation in pressure", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Expansion of the air", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Height above the surface of the earth", "isCorrect": false}]',2,NULL,NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37465,'physics',27,1380,3308,'Two identical similar rods of metal are welded end to end as shown in figure. 20 $cal$ of heat flows through it in 4 minutes. If the rods are welded as shown in the figure the same amount of heat will flow through the rods in','[{"text": "$1 \\\\mathrm{~min}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$2 \\\\mathrm{~min}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$4 \\\\mathrm{~min}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$16 \\\\mathrm{~min}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'$\\mathrm{As}, \\dfrac{Q}{t}=\\dfrac{k A \\Delta \\theta}{l}=\\dfrac{\\Delta \\theta}{(l / k A)}=\\dfrac{\\Delta \\theta}{R}$<br>($R$- Thermal resistance)<br>Given that $Q$ and $\\Delta \\theta$ are same for two combinations.<br>$\\begin{aligned}& t \\propto R \\\\& \\dfrac{t_{p}}{t_{s}}=\\dfrac{R_{p}}{R_{s}}=\\dfrac{R / 2}{2 R}=\\dfrac{1}{4} \\\\& t_{p}=\\dfrac{t_{s}}{4}=\\dfrac{4}{4}=1 \\mathrm{~min} .\\end{aligned}$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,'NCERT Exempler','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/27_37465_question_1.png'),
(37466,'physics',26,1138,2727,'<div class="matching-question"><div class="column-left">The surface tension force does not depend on the shape of the object.<br></div><div class="column-right">A needle placed carefully on the surface of water may float while a ball of the same material will always sink.</div></div>','[{"text": "Statement A is correct but Statement B is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Statement A is incorrect but Statement B is correct.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both statements are correct.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Statements are incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Surface tension force depends on the shape of the object also. So option (2) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ type-2',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37472,'physics',29,1300,3145,'A closed vessel is maintained at a constant temperature. It is first evacuated and then vapour is injected into it continuously. The pressure of the vapour in the vessel','[{"text": "First increases and then remains constant", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Increases continuously", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "First increases and then decreases", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "None of the above<br>", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'If pressure of water vapour increases at constant temperature then condensation begins and pressure remains constant.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37473,'physics',26,1133,2710,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> To float, a body must displace liquid whose weight is greater than actual weight of the body.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> The body will experience no net downward force in that case.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'A body must displaces as much amount of liquid greater than the actual weight of the body to float in it. In case of floating, no net downward force acts on the body. So option (2) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37474,'physics',27,1382,3317,'A brass boiler has a base area $0.15\\,{m^2}$ and thickness $1.0\\,cm$. It boils water at the rate of $6.0\\,kg/\\min $ when placed on a gas stove. The temperature of the part of the flame in contact with the boiler will be. (Thermal conductivity of brass $ = 109\\,J/s - m - K,$ heat of vapourisation of water $ = 2256 \\times {10^3}\\,J/kg$)','[{"text": "$158^\\\\circ C$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$208^\\\\circ C$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$238^\\\\circ C$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$264^\\\\circ C$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Given, base area of boiler $(A) = 0.15\\,{m^2}$<br>Thickness $(d) = 1\\,cm = 1 \\times {10^{ - 2}}\\,m$<br>Rate of water boils<br>$ = 6.0\\,kg/\\min  = \\frac{{6.0}}{{60}}\\,kg/s = 0.1\\,kg/s.$<br>Thermal conductivity of brass<br>$K=109 J / s-m-K$<br>Latent heat of vapourization of water<br>$L = 2256 \\times {10^3}\\,J/Kg$<br>Let $\\theta_{1}$ be the temperature of the part of the boiler in contact with the stove.<br>Rate of heat energy supplied<br>$\\begin{aligned}& \\dfrac{K A \\Delta \\theta}{d}=m L \\\\& \\dfrac{K A\\left(\\theta_{1}-\\theta_{2}\\right)}{d}=m L\\end{aligned}$<br>As the water boils at $100^\\circ C$<br>${\\theta _2} = 100\\,^\\circ C\\,{\\text{and}}\\,{\\theta _1} = 238\\,^\\circ C$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,'NCERT Exempler','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37476,'physics',29,1304,3154,'A gas mixture contains monoatomic and diatomic molecules of 2 moles each. The mixture has a total internal energy of (symbols have usual meanings)','[{"text": "$3\\\\,RT$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$5\\\\,RT$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$8\\\\,RT$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$9\\\\,RT$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'$U=n_{1} C_{v_{1}} T+n_{2} C_{v_{2}} T$<br>$\\begin{aligned}& U=(2) \\dfrac{3}{2} R T+(2) \\dfrac{5}{2} R T \\\\& U=8 R T\\end{aligned}$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37477,'physics',27,1381,3315,'During vaporisation,<br>I. The change of state from liquid to vapour state occurs.<br>II. The temperature remains constant.<br>III. Both liquid and vapour states co-exist in equilibrium.<br>Which of the following statement(s) is/are correct?','[{"text": "I", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "I and II", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "I and III", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "I, II and III", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'The change of state from liquid to vapour (for gas) is called vaporisation. It is observed that when liquid is heated, the temperature remains constant until the entire amount of the liquid is converted into vapour. i.e. both the liquid and vapour states of the substance co-exist in thermal equilibrium, during the change of state from liquid to vapour.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ type-2',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37498,'physics',27,1381,3315,'<div class="matching-question"><div class="column-left">In change of state from solid to liquid the temperature decreases until the entire amount of the solid substance melts.<br></div><div class="column-right">The phenomenon of refreezing is called melting.</div></div>','[{"text": "Statement A is correct but Statement B is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Statement A is incorrect but Statement B is correct.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both statements are correct.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Statements are incorrect.", "isCorrect": true}]',3,NULL,NULL,'Medium','MCQ type-2',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37478,'physics',26,1133,2710,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> A hydrogen filled balloon stops rising after it has attained a certain height in the sky.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> The atmospheric pressure decreases with height.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'As we go up the atmospheric pressure decreases and baloon stops raising when weight of the balloon is equal to buyoancy force. So option (1) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37480,'physics',29,1300,3145,'During an experiment an ideal gas is found to obey an additional law $V^{2} P=$ constant. The gas is initially at temperature $\\mathrm{T}$ and volume $\\mathrm{V}$. When it expands to a volume $2 \\mathrm{~V}$, the temperature becomes','[{"text": "$2 T$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2 T$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$T / 2$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$T \\\\sqrt{2}$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'$P V=R T$ (ideal gas equation)<br>$V^{2} P=C$ (additional law)<br>Dividing both the equations<br>$\\begin{aligned}& V=\\dfrac{C}{R T} \\Rightarrow V \\propto \\dfrac{1}{T} \\\\& \\Rightarrow \\quad T \\propto \\dfrac{1}{V}\\end{aligned}$<br>When volume $(V)$  is doubled, temperature is halved i.e., $T^{\\prime}=\\dfrac{T}{2}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37481,'physics',27,1382,3320,'I. Convection is a mode of heat transfer by actual motion of matter.<br>II. Convection is possible only in gases.<br>III. Convection can be natural or forced.<br>Which of the following statement(s) is/are correct?','[{"text": "Only I", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both I and III", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Only II", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "I, II, and III", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Statement I and III are correct but II is incorrect since convection is possible in both liquids and gases.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ type-2',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37482,'physics',26,1136,2715,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> The velocity of horizontal flow of a liquid is smaller when pressure is larger and vice versa.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> According to Bernoulli\'s theorem, for the stream line flow of an ideal liquid, the total energy per unit mass remains constant.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'According to Bernoulli\'s theorem,<br>$$P+\\dfrac{1}{2} \\rho v^{2}=$$ constant i.e., when velocity is large, the pressure is less in a stream line flow of an ideal liquid through a horizontal tube. So option (1) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37483,'physics',28,1369,3279,'When small amount of common salt is dissolved in water. In this process,','[{"text": "Entropy decreases", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Entropy increases", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Entropy becomes zero", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Entropy remains constant", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'No heat is added or removed from the system.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37484,'physics',27,1382,3317,'I. Gases are poor thermal conductors.<br>II. Liquids have conductivities intermediate between solids and gases.<br>III. Heat conduction can take place from cold body to hotter body.<br>Which of the following statement(s) is/are correct?','[{"text": "Only I", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Only II", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Only III", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both I and II", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Statement I and II are correct but III is incorrect since heat conduction takes place from hot body to cold body.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ type-2',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37485,'physics',29,1300,3145,'Equation of gas in terms of pressure $(P)$, absolute temperature $(T)$ and denity (4) is','[{"text": "$\\\\dfrac{P_{1}}{T_{1} d_{1}}=\\\\dfrac{P_{2}}{T_{2} d_{2}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{P_{1} d_{1}}{T_{1}}=\\\\dfrac{P_{2} d_{2}}{T_{2}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{P_{1} d_{2}}{T_{1}}=\\\\dfrac{P_{2} d_{1}}{T_{1}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{P_{1} T_{1}}{d_{1}}=\\\\dfrac{P_{2} T_{2}}{d_{2}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'$P V=n R T=\\dfrac{m}{M} R T \\Rightarrow P=\\dfrac{d}{M} R T$<br>[Density $d=\\dfrac{m}{V}$ ]<br>$\\Rightarrow \\dfrac{P}{d T}=\\text { constant or } \\dfrac{P_{1}}{d_{1} T_{1}}=\\dfrac{P_{2}}{d_{2} T_{2}}$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37486,'physics',26,1137,2723,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Terminal velocity is same as the critical velocity<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> The constant velocity of fall of a body through a viscous fluid is called terminal velocity.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but reason is correct.", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Terminal velocity and critical velocity are not same. Critical velocity is the velocity below which the flow of liquid is streamline. So option (4) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37487,'physics',28,1371,3283,'A thermally insulated vessel contains an ideal gas of molecular mass $\\mathrm{M}$ and ratio of specific heat $\\gamma$. It is moving with speed $v$ and it suddenly brought to rest. Assuming no heat is lost to the surroundings, its temperature increases by','[{"text": "$\\\\dfrac{(\\\\gamma-1)}{2 \\\\gamma R} M v^{2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{\\\\gamma M v^{2}}{2 R}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{(\\\\gamma-1)}{2 R} M v^{2}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{(\\\\gamma-1)}{2(\\\\gamma+1) R} M v^{2}$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'As on heat is lost, therefore<br>Loss of kinetic energy $=$ Gain of internal energy of gas<br>i.e., $\\dfrac{1}{2} m v^{2}=n C_{V} \\Delta T \\Rightarrow \\dfrac{1}{2} m v^{2}=\\dfrac{m}{M} \\cdot \\dfrac{R}{\\gamma-1} \\Delta T$<br>$\\Rightarrow \\Delta T=\\dfrac{M v^{2}(\\gamma-1)}{2 R}$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37488,'physics',27,1380,3306,'<div class="matching-question"><div class="column-left">Copper expands more than glass for same rise in temperature.<br></div><div class="column-right">Copper is far better conductor of heat than glass.</div></div>','[{"text": "Statement A is correct but Statement B is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Statement A is incorrect but Statement B is correct.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both statements are correct.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Statements are incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Metals generally have higher coefficient of linear expansion. So, copper expands five times more than glass.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ type-2',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37489,'physics',26,1137,2722,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> The viscosity of liquid increases with rise of tempature.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Viscosity of a liquid is the property of the liquid by virtue of which it opposes the relative motion among its different layers.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but reason is correct.", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'When the temperature of the liquid increases distance between molecules increases then cohesive force between liquid molecules decreases hence viscosity decreases. It is the property that opposes relative motion between adjacent layers. So option (4) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37490,'physics',29,1300,3145,'A perfect gas at $27^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C}$ is heated at constant pressure so as to double its volume. The increase in temperature of the gas will be','[{"text": "$600^\\\\circ \\\\,C$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$327^\\\\circ \\\\,C$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$54^\\\\circ \\\\,C$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$300^\\\\circ \\\\,C$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Here${V_1} = V,{V_2} = 2 V$<br>${T_1} = 300 \\,\\,K$<br>At constant pressure<br>$\\frac{{{V_1}}}{{{T_1}}}{\\rm{ }} = \\frac{{{V_2}}}{{{T_2}}}$<br>$ \\Rightarrow {T_2}{\\rm{ }} = {T_1}\\left( {\\frac{{{V_2}}}{{{V_1}}}} \\right) = 300\\,\\,K\\left( {\\frac{{2V}}{V}} \\right) = 600 \\,\\,K$<br>${T_2}{\\rm{ }} = 327^\\circ C$<br>Increase in temperature, $DT = {T_2} - {T_1}$<br>$ = 300^\\circ C$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37491,'physics',28,1371,3285,'If $R = $ universal gas constant, the amount of heat needed to rise the temperature of $2\\,\\,mol$ of an ideal monoatomic gas from $273\\,K$ to $373\\,K$ when no work is done is','[{"text": "$100R$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$150R$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$300R$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$500R$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'$\\Delta Q=\\Delta U=n C_{V} \\Delta T=2 \\times \\dfrac{3 R}{2} \\times 100=300 R$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37492,'physics',29,1300,3145,'If $300\\,\\,ml$  of a gas at $27^\\circ \\,C$  is cooled to $7^\\circ \\,C$ at constant pressure, then its final volume will be','[{"text": "$350\\\\,\\\\,ml$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$540\\\\,\\\\,ml$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$135\\\\,\\\\,ml$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$280\\\\,\\\\,ml$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'At constant pressure $V\\alpha \\,\\,T$<br>$ \\Rightarrow \\frac{{{V_1}}}{{{V_2}}} = \\frac{{{T_1}}}{{{T_2}}} \\Rightarrow {V_2} = \\frac{{{V_1}{T_2}}}{{{T_1}}} = \\frac{{300 \\times 280}}{{300}} = 280ml.$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37493,'physics',26,1137,2722,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Machine parts are jammed in winter<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> The viscosity of lubricant used in machine parts increase at low temperature.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Viscosities of fluids depend on temperature, increasing for gases and decreasing for liquids as the temperature is increased. So option (1) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37494,'physics',27,1381,3315,'<div class="matching-question"><div class="column-left">Melting of solid causes no change in internal energy.</div><div class="column-right">Latent heat is the heat required to melt a unit mass of solid.</div></div>','[{"text": "Statement A is correct but Statement B is incorrect.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Statement A is incorrect but Statement B is correct.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both statements are correct.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Statements are incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'During melting heat absorbed will be converted into increase in internal energy. So (1) is the correct option.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ type-2',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37495,'physics',28,1371,3285,'In an isochoric process, the correct ratio is','[{"text": "$\\\\Delta Q: W=1: 1$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\Delta Q: W=\\\\gamma: \\\\gamma-1$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\Delta Q: W=\\\\gamma-1: \\\\gamma$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\Delta Q: W=\\\\gamma: 1$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'$\\dfrac{\\Delta Q}{W}=\\dfrac{\\Delta Q}{\\Delta Q-\\Delta U}=\\dfrac{C_{P}}{C_{P}-C_{V}}=\\dfrac{\\gamma}{(\\gamma-1)}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37496,'physics',26,1138,2727,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> More is the cohesive force, more is the surface tension.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> More cohesive force leads to more shrinking of liquid surface.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Surface tension can be understood as a property of a surface due to which it tries to acquire the minimum possible area. If cohesive force is more, the liquid is having the capability to shrink its surface more, i.e., surface area of the liquid is less and hence more is the surface tension.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37497,'physics',29,1300,3145,'If ${N_A}$  is avogadro\'s number, then the number of molecules in $6 g$ of hydrogen at NTP is','[{"text": "${3N_A}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$2{N_A}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${N_A}{\\\\text{ }}/{\\\\text{ }}6$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${N_A}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'1 mole of $H_{2}$ mass is $2\\,\\,g$. So 3 moles of $H_{2}$ are present.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL);
INSERT INTO `qb_questions` (`id`, `subject_id`, `chapter_id`, `topic_id`, `subtopic_id`, `question_html`, `options`, `correct_index`, `explanation`, `explanation_image_url`, `difficulty`, `qtype`, `year`, `tag`, `created_at`, `question_image_url`) VALUES
(42603,'physics',35,707,1481,'A bar magnet of moment $M$ is bent into arc, its moment now','[{"text": "Decreases", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Increases", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Does not change", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "May change", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'When it is bent in the form of an arc then poles become close therefore magnetic moment decreases.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37500,'physics',26,1138,2729,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> It is easier to spray water in which some soap is dissolved.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Surface tension of soap solution is greater than surface tension of water.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'When a liquid is sprayed, the surface area of the liquid increases. Therefore, work has to be done in spraying the liquid, which is directly proportional to the surface tension. Because on adding soap, surface tension of water decreases, the spraying of water becomes easy. So option (3) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37501,'physics',29,1300,3145,'At constant pressure if temperature of a gas is increased by $4 \\%$ its density will decrease by','[{"text": "$2.18 \\\\%$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$3.84 \\\\%$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$4.68 \\\\%$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$6.72 \\\\%$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'The ideal gas equation is<br>$\\begin{aligned}& P V=n R T=\\dfrac{V \\rho R T}{M} \\\\& \\rho T=c \\quad(P=\\text { constant })\\end{aligned}$<br>where $c$ is a constant<br>Let $T_{1}=T_{0} \\& T_{2}=1.04 T_{0}$<br>$\\dfrac{\\rho_{2}-\\rho_{1}}{\\rho_{1}} \\times 100=\\dfrac{\\dfrac{c}{T_{2}}-\\dfrac{c}{T_{1}}}{\\dfrac{c}{T_{1}}} \\times 100=3.84 \\%$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37502,'physics',27,1382,3322,'<div class="matching-question"><div class="column-left">All bodies emit radiant energy whether they are solid, liquid or gases.<br></div><div class="column-right">Black bodies absorb and emit radiant energy better than bodies of lighter colours.</div></div>','[{"text": "Statement A is correct but Statement B is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Statement A is incorrect but Statement B is correct.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both statements are correct.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Statements are incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,NULL,NULL,'Medium','MCQ type-2',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37503,'physics',28,1371,3284,'Two $kg$ of water is converted into steam by boiling at atmospheric pressure. The volume changes from $2 \\times {10^{ - 3}}\\,{m^3}$ to $3.34\\,{m^3}$. The work done by the system is about','[{"text": "$ - 170\\\\,kJ$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$ - 340\\\\,kJ$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$340\\\\,kJ$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$170\\\\,kJ$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'$W=P \\Delta V=1.01 \\times 10^{5}\\left(3.34-2 \\times 10^{-3}\\right)$<br>$ = 337 \\times {10^3}\\,J \\approx 340\\,kJ$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37504,'physics',29,1300,3145,'The temperature of a gas contained in a closed vessel of constant volume increases by  $1^\\circ {\\rm{ }}C$  when the pressure of the gas is increased by $1 \\%$. The initial temperature of the gas is','[{"text": "$100 K$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$273^\\\\circ {\\\\rm{ }}C$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$300^\\\\circ {\\\\rm{ }}C$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$200 K$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Let the initial temperature of the gas be $T$. At constant volume  $P \\propto T$<br>$\\therefore \\dfrac{P}{P+0.01 P}=\\dfrac{T}{T+1}$<br>On solving, we get<br>$T = 100 \\,\\,K$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37505,'physics',26,1138,2730,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Shape of a liquid drop is governed by force of gravity and surface tension.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Excess pressure inside a drop is directly proportional to surface tension.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'When a drop of liquid is poured on a glass plate, the shape of the drop is governed by the force of gravity and surface tension. Excess pressure inside a drop is directly proportional to surface tension. So option (2) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37506,'physics',27,1380,3310,'<div class="matching-question"><div class="column-left">A change in the temperature of a body causes change in its dimensions<br></div><div class="column-right">The dimensions of a body decreases due to the increase in its temperature.</div></div>','[{"text": "Statement A is correct but Statement B is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Statement A is incorrect but Statement B is correct.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both statements are correct.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Statements are incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,NULL,NULL,'Medium','MCQ type-2',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37507,'physics',28,1371,3284,'A frictionless piston-cylinder based enclosure contains some amount of gas at a pressure of $400\\,k\\,Pa$. Then heat is transferred to the gas at constant pressure in a quasi-static process. The piston moves up slowly through a height of $10\\,cm$. If the piston has a cross-section area of $0.3\\,{m^2}$, the work done by the gas in this process is','[{"text": "$12\\\\,kJ$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$6\\\\,kJ$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$24\\\\,kJ$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$7.5\\\\,kJ$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'$W=P \\times \\Delta V=400 \\times 10^{3} \\times 0.3 \\times 10 \\times 10^{-2}$<br>$ = 400 \\times 10 \\times 3 = 12\\,kJ.$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37508,'physics',26,1133,2710,'Two non-mixing liquid of densities $$\\rho$$ and $$n \\rho(\\mathrm{n}>1)$$ are put in a container. The height of each liquid is $$h$$. A solid cylinder of length $$L$$ and density $$d$$ is put in this container. The cylinder floats with its axis vertical and length $$\\mathrm{pL}(\\mathrm{p} < 1)$$ in the denser liquid. The density $d$ is equal to.','[{"text": "$$\\\\{1+(\\\\mathrm{n}+1) \\\\mathrm{p}\\\\} \\\\rho$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$\\\\{2+(n+1) p\\\\} \\\\rho$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$\\\\{2+(n-1) p\\\\} \\\\rho$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$\\\\{1+(n-1) p\\\\} \\\\rho$$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'The cylinder present in two liquids are shown in the figure.<br><img src=\'/img/data/physics/26_37508_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' /> <br>As the cylinder is in equilibrium $$F_{g}=F_{b_{1}}+F_{b_{2}}$$<br>$LAdg = (pL)A(n\\rho )g + (1 - p)LA\\rho g$<br>$ \\Rightarrow d = (1 - p)\\rho  + pn\\rho  = [1 + (n - 1)p]\\rho $',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2016,'NEET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37509,'physics',29,1301,3148,'The root mean square speed of gas molecules','[{"text": "Is the same for all gases at the same temperature", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Depends on the mass of the gas molecule and its temperature", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Is independent of the density and presure of the gas", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Depends only on the temperature and volume of the gas", "isCorrect": false}]',1,NULL,NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37510,'physics',27,1380,3311,'<div class="matching-question"><div class="column-left">The density of water remains constant as it is cooled from room temperature until its temperature reaches $4^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C}$<br></div><div class="column-right">Below $4^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C}$, the density increases.</div></div>','[{"text": "Statement A is correct but Statement B is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Statement A is incorrect but Statement B is correct.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both statements are correct.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Statements are incorrect.", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Density of water increases from $0^\\circ C$ and it reaches maximum at $4^\\circ C$and then it decreases. Option (4) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ type-2',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37511,'physics',28,1371,3282,'A vessel containing 5 liters of a gas at $0.8\\,m$ pressure is connected to an evacuated vessel of volume 3 litres. The resultant pressure inside will be (assuming temperature is constant)','[{"text": "$0.5\\\\,m$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$4/3\\\\,m$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$3/4\\\\,m$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2.0\\\\,m$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Temperature remains constant <br>$P_{1} V_{1}=P_{2} V_{2}$<br>$0.8 \\times 5 = {P_2} \\times (3 + 5) \\Rightarrow {P_2} = 0.5\\,m$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37512,'physics',29,1301,3149,'Match the Column I with Column II<div class="matching-question"><div class="column-left">A Average speed $v_{a v}$ <br>B   Root mean square ${v_{rms{\\text{ }}}}$<br>C  Most probable speed ${v_{{\\text{mp}}}}$</div><div class="column-right">P  $\\sqrt{\\dfrac{3 R T}{M}}$  <br> Q  $\\sqrt{\\dfrac{8 R T}{\\pi M}}$ <br>R  $\\sqrt{\\dfrac{2 R T}{M}}$</div></div>','[{"text": "${\\\\text{A}} - {\\\\text{R}},\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\text{B}} - {\\\\text{Q}},\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\text{C}} - {\\\\text{P}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\text{A}} - {\\\\text{P}},\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\text{B}} - {\\\\text{Q}},\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\text{C}} - {\\\\text{R}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\text{A}} - {\\\\text{Q}},\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\text{B}} - {\\\\text{P}},\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\text{C}} - {\\\\text{R}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${\\\\text{A}} - {\\\\text{R}},\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\text{B}} - {\\\\text{P}},\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\text{C}} - {\\\\text{Q}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Option (3) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Match the following',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37513,'physics',26,1136,2717,'A small hole of area of cross-section $2\\,m{m^2}$ is present near the bottom of a fully filled open tank of height $2\\,\\,m$. Taking $g = 10\\,m/{s^2}$, the rate of flow of water through the open hole would be nearly','[{"text": "$12.6 \\\\times {10^{ - 6}}\\\\,{m^3}/s$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$8.9 \\\\times {10^{ - 6}}\\\\,{m^3}/s$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2.23 \\\\times {10^{ - 6}}\\\\,{m^3}/s$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$6.4 \\\\times {10^{ - 6}}\\\\,{m^3}/s$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Rate of flow of liquid is<br>$Q = au = a\\sqrt {2gh} $<br>$ = 2 \\times {10^{ - 6}}\\,{m^2} \\times \\sqrt {2 \\times 10 \\times 2} \\,m/s$<br>$ = 12.6 \\times {10^{ - 6}}\\,{m^3}/s$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2019,'NEET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37514,'physics',27,1382,3320,'<div class="matching-question"><div class="column-left">In the human body the heart act as the pump that circulates blood through different parts of the body, transferring heat by forced convection.<br></div><div class="column-right">In forced convection, material is forced to move by a pump or by some other physical means.</div></div>','[{"text": "Statement A is correct but Statement B is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Statement A is incorrect but Statement B is correct.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both statements are correct.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Statements are incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,NULL,NULL,'Medium','MCQ type-2',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37515,'physics',28,1371,3282,'One mole of ${O_2}$ gas having a volume equal to $22.4\\,liters$ at $0^\\circ C$ and 1 atomspheric pressure is compressed isothermally so that its volume reduces to $11.2\\,liters$. The work done in this process is','[{"text": "$1728\\\\,J$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1672.5\\\\,J$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$ - 1572.5\\\\,J$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$ - 1728\\\\,J$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'$W=-n R T \\log _{e} \\dfrac{V_{2}}{V_{1}}$<br>$=-1 \\times 8.31 \\times(273+0) \\log _{e}\\left(\\dfrac{22.4}{11.2}\\right)$<br>$ =  - 831 \\times 273 \\times {\\log _e}2 =  - 1572.5\\,\\,J$<br>$\\left[\\because \\log _{e} 2=0.693\\right]$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37516,'physics',29,1301,3148,'According to the kinetic theory of gases the r.m.s. velocity of gas molecules is directly proportional to','[{"text": "$1 / \\\\sqrt{T}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\sqrt{T}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$T^{2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$T$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'$v_{r m s} \\propto \\sqrt{T}$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37517,'physics',27,1381,3314,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Molar heat capacity is the amount of heat required to raise the temperature of a unit mole by one degree.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Heat capacity is the amount of heat needed to raise a system temperature by one degree.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Option (2) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37518,'physics',26,1138,2730,'A soap bubble, having radius of $1\\,mm$, is blown from a detergent solution having a surface tension of $2.5 \\times {10^{ - 2}}\\,N/m$. The pressure inside the bubble equals at a point $$Z_{0}$$ below the free surface of water in a container. Taking $g = 10\\,m/{s^2}$, density of water $ = {10^3}\\,kg/{m^3}$, the value of ${Z_0}$ is :','[{"text": "$100\\\\,\\\\,cm$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$10\\\\,cm$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1\\\\,cm$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$0.5\\\\,\\\\,cm$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Excess pressure $$=\\dfrac{4 T}{R}$$,<br>Gauge pressure $$=\\rho g Z_{0}$$<br>${P_0} + \\frac{{4T}}{R} = {P_0} + \\rho g{Z_0}$<br>${Z_0} = \\frac{{4T}}{{R \\times \\rho g}}$<br>${Z_0} = \\frac{{4 \\times 2.5 \\times {{10}^{ - 2}}}}{{{{10}^{ - 3}} \\times 1000 \\times 10}}\\,m$<br>${Z_0} = 1\\,cm$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2019,'NEET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37519,'physics',28,1371,3283,'A tyre pumped to a pressure $3.375 \\mathrm{~atm}$ at $27^\\circ C$ suddenly bursts. What is the final temperature $(\\gamma=1.5)$ ?','[{"text": "$ - 27^\\\\circ C$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$27^\\\\circ C$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$ - 73^\\\\circ C$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$0^\\\\circ C$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Sudden bursting of a tyre can be treated as adiabatic expansion<br>$T_{1}^{\\gamma} P_{1}^{1-\\gamma}=T_{2}^{\\gamma} P_{2}^{1-\\gamma}$<br>$\\left(\\dfrac{T_{1}}{T_{2}}\\right)^{\\gamma}=\\left(\\dfrac{P_{1}}{P_{2}}\\right)^{\\gamma-1} \\Rightarrow\\left(\\dfrac{300}{T_{2}}\\right)^{3 / 2}=\\left(\\dfrac{3.375}{1}\\right)^{3 / 2-1}$<br>${T_2} = \\frac{{300}}{{{{(3.375)}^{1/3}}}} = 200\\,K =  - 73^\\circ C$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37520,'physics',29,1301,3148,'The temperature of an ideal gas is increased from $27^\\circ {\\rm{ }}C$ to $127^\\circ {\\rm{ }}C$, then percentage increase in $v_{r m s}$ is','[{"text": "$11 \\\\%$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$33 \\\\%$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$37 \\\\%$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$15.5 \\\\%$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'${v_{rms}} = \\sqrt {\\frac{{3RT}}{M}} $<br>$\\% {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\text{increases}}\\,{\\text{in}}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {v_{rms}} = \\frac{{\\sqrt {\\frac{{3R{T_2}}}{M}}  - \\sqrt {\\frac{{3R{T_1}}}{M}} }}{{\\sqrt {\\frac{{3R{T_1}}}{M}} }} \\times 100$<br>$ = \\frac{{20 - 17.32}}{{17.32}} \\times 100 = 15.5% $',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37521,'physics',27,1378,3304,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> The temperature at which Centigrade and Fahrenheit thermometers read the same is $-40^{\\circ}$.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> There is no relation between Fahrenheit and Centigrade temperature.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'The relation between $F$ and $C$ scale is, $\\dfrac{C}{5}=\\dfrac{F-32}{9}$. If $F=C \\Rightarrow C=-40^{\\circ} C$, i.e., at $-40^{\\circ}$ the Centrigrade and Fahrenheit thermometers reads the same. So option (3) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37522,'physics',26,1138,2731,'A capacillary tube of radius $$r$$ is immersed in water and water rises in it to a height $$h$$. The mass of the water in the capillary is $5\\,g$. Another capillary tube of radius $2\\,r$ is immersed in water. The mass of water that will rise in this tube is :','[{"text": "$5.0\\\\,g$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$10.0\\\\,g$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$20.0\\\\,g$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2.5\\\\,g$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'$$m \\propto r$$<br>$$\\begin{aligned}\\dfrac{m_{2}}{m_{1}} & =\\dfrac{r_{2}}{r_{1}} \\\\\\dfrac{m_{2}}{5} & =\\dfrac{2 r}{r} \\\\m_{2} & =10 g\\end{aligned}$$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2020,'NEET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37523,'physics',28,1371,3283,'$1\\,g$ mole of an ideal gas at STP is subjected to a reversible adiabatic expansion to double its volume. Find the change in internal energy $(\\gamma=1.4)$.','[{"text": "$769.5\\\\,J$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1169.5\\\\,J$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$969.5\\\\,J$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1369.5\\\\,J$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Use $T_{1} V_{1}^{\\gamma-1}=T_{2} V_{2}^{\\gamma-1}$<br>${T_2} = \\frac{{{T_1}V_1^{\\gamma  - 1}}}{{V_2^{\\gamma  - 1}}} = \\frac{{273}}{{{{(2)}^{0.4}}}} = 207\\,K$<br>Change in internal energy<br>$\\Delta u = \\frac{R}{{(1 - \\gamma )}}\\left( {{T_1} - {T_2}} \\right) = \\frac{{8.31(273 - 207)}}{{1.4 - 1}} = 1369.5\\,J$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37524,'physics',26,1133,2710,'The velocity of a small ball of mass $M$ and density $d$, when dropped in a container filled with glycerine becomes constant after some time. If the density of glycerine is $\\dfrac{d}{2}$, then the viscous force acting on the ball will be','[{"text": "$Mg$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{3}{2} M g$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2Mg$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{M g}{2}$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'When the velocity of the ball is constant<br>$\\begin{aligned}&amp; F_{n e t}=0 \\\\&amp; \\begin{aligned}F_{v} &amp; +F_{b}=M g \\\\F_{v} &amp; =M g-F_{b} \\\\&amp; =\\dfrac{4}{3} \\pi R^{3} d g-\\dfrac{4}{3} \\pi R^{3} \\dfrac{d}{2} g \\\\&amp; =\\dfrac{4}{3} \\pi R^{3} d g\\left(1-\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right)\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}$<br>$=\\dfrac{4}{3} \\pi R^{3} d g\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right)=\\dfrac{M g}{2}$<br><br>',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2021,'NEET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37525,'physics',29,1301,3148,'The r.m.s velocity of a gas at a certain temperature is $\\sqrt{2}$ times than that of the oxygen molecules at that temperature. The gas can be:','[{"text": "${H_2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$He$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$C{H_4}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$S{O_2}$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'$\\quad v_{r m s} \\propto \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{M}} \\Rightarrow \\dfrac{v_{1}}{v_{2}}=\\sqrt{\\dfrac{M_{2}}{M_{1}}}$<br>$\\Rightarrow \\dfrac{v}{\\sqrt{2} v}=\\sqrt{\\dfrac{M_{2}}{32}} \\Rightarrow M_{2}=16$<br>So the given gas is $C{H_4}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37526,'physics',27,1380,3311,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Water expands both when heated or cooled from $4{ }^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C}$.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Density of water is minimum at $4{ }^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C}$.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'At $4^\\circ C$ the volume of water is minimum. When it is cooled below $4^\\circ C$ or heated above $4^\\circ C$, then its volume increases. As volume at $4^\\circ C$ is minimum, thus its density will be maximum. So option (3) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37527,'physics',28,1371,3283,'A monoatomic ideal gas, initially at temperature \\(T_{1}\\), is enclosed in a cylinder fitted with a frictionless piston. The gas is allowed to expand adiabatically to a temperature \\(T_{2}\\) by releasing the piston suddenly. If \\(L_{1}\\) and \\(L_{2}\\) are the lengths of the gas column before and after expansion respectively, then \\(T_{1} / T_{2}\\) is given by<div class="matching-question"></div>','[{"text": "$\\\\dfrac{L_{1}}{L_{2}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\left(\\\\dfrac{L_{1}}{L_{2}}\\\\right)^{2 / 3}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\left(\\\\dfrac{L_{2}}{L_{1}}\\\\right)^{2 / 3}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{L_{2}}{L_{1}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'$T_{1} V_{1}^{\\gamma-1}=T_{2} V_{2}^{\\gamma-1}$<br>$\\Rightarrow \\dfrac{T_{1}}{T_{2}}=\\left(\\dfrac{V_{2}}{V_{1}}\\right)^{\\gamma-1}=\\left(\\dfrac{L_{2} A}{L_{1} A}\\right)^{\\frac{5}{3}-1}=\\left(\\dfrac{L_{2}}{L_{1}}\\right)^{\\frac{2}{3}}$.',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37528,'physics',26,1137,2723,'A spherical ball is dropped in a long column of a highly viscous liquid. The curve in the graph shown, which represents the speed of the ball (\\[v\\]) as a function of time ( \\[t\\]) is:<div class="matching-question"></div>','[{"text": "$B$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$C$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$D$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$A$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Initially speed is zero, then increases & after some time it becomes constant.<br>Acceleration (slope of $v/t$ curve) of ball first decreases and after some time it becomes zero.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2022,'NEET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/26_37528_question_1.png'),
(48814,'biology',80,477,964,'The light reactions are temperature sensitive they are affected to __ extent.','[{"text": "Much higher", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Much lesser", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Unaffected by temperature", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Unlimited", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'The light reactions are also temperature sensitive but they are affected to a much lesser extent.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37529,'physics',27,1380,3310,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> A solid and hollow sphere of same diameter and same material when heated through the same temperature will expand by the same amount<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> The change in volume is independent of the original mass but depends on original volume.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'The change in volume depends on original volume so, when heated through the same temperature, both will expand by the same amount. So option (1) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37530,'physics',29,1301,3148,'The ratio of the densities of two gases at the same temperature is $8: 9$. The ratio of the rms velocities of their molecules is','[{"text": "$1: 1$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$8: 9$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\sqrt{8}: \\\\sqrt{9}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\sqrt{9}: \\\\sqrt{8}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'RMS velocity depends only on temperature. So the ratio of rms velocities is $1: 1$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37531,'physics',28,1371,3283,'In an adiabatic change, the pressure and temperature of a monoatomic gas are related with relation as $P \\propto T^{C}$, where $C$ is equal to:','[{"text": "$\\\\dfrac{5}{3}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{5}{4}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{3}{5}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{5}{2}$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'For adiabatic process,<br>$P V^{\\gamma}=$ constant$\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\left( 1 \\right)$<br>Ideal gas relation is<br>$ \\Rightarrow V = \\frac{{nRT}}{P}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\left( 2 \\right)$<br>From Eqs. (1) and (2), we get<br>$P\\left(\\dfrac{R T}{P}\\right)^{\\gamma}=$ constant $\\Rightarrow \\dfrac{T^{\\gamma}}{P^{\\gamma-1}}=$ constant<br>Given, $P \\propto T^{C}$<br>$\\Rightarrow P \\alpha T^{\\frac{\\gamma}{\\gamma-1}} \\Rightarrow C=\\dfrac{\\gamma}{\\gamma-1}$<br>For a monoatomic gas $\\gamma=\\dfrac{5}{3} \\Rightarrow C=\\dfrac{5}{2}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37532,'physics',27,1381,3315,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Cooking food is difficult on hills.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> The boiling point decrease with increase in pressure.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'At hills, the atmospheric pressure is lower, reducing the boiling point of water as compared to that at sea level. So at low temperature only water boils and food can\'t be cooked properly. So option (1) is correct',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37533,'physics',29,1305,3155,'If the temperature of a gas is doubled at constant pressure then mean free path becomes','[{"text": "Same", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Half", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Double", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "One fourth", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'$\\lambda=\\dfrac{R T}{\\sqrt{2} \\pi d^{2} N_{A} P}$<br>$\\frac{{{\\lambda _1}}}{{{\\lambda _2}}} = \\frac{{{T_1}}}{{{T_2}}} = \\frac{T}{{2T}} = \\frac{1}{2}$<br>$ \\Rightarrow {\\lambda _2} = 2{\\lambda _1}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37534,'physics',26,1138,2730,'If a soap bubble expands, the pressure inside the bubble','[{"text": "increases", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "remains the same", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "is equal to the atmospheric pressure", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "decreases", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'$P = {P_0} + \\frac{{4\\,T}}{R}$ (Where $$P_{0}$$ is atmospheric pressure and $T$ is surface tension)<br>$ \\Rightarrow R$ increases and $P$ decreases',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2022,'NEET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37535,'physics',28,1371,3283,'An ideal gas at $27^\\circ C$ is compressed adiabatically to $\\dfrac{8}{27}$ of its original volume. If $\\gamma=\\dfrac{5}{3}$, then rise in temperature is','[{"text": "$450\\\\,K$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$375\\\\,K$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$675\\\\,K$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$405\\\\,K$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'$T V^{\\gamma-1}=$ constant<br>$(300){V^{5/3 - 1}} = {T_2}{\\left( {\\frac{{8V}}{{27}}} \\right)^{5/3 - 1}}$<br>$(300){V^{\\frac{2}{3}}} = {T_2}{\\left( {\\frac{{8V}}{{27}}} \\right)^{2/3}}$<br>$(300) = {T_2}\\left( {\\frac{4}{9}} \\right)$<br>$ \\Rightarrow {T_2} = 675\\,K$<br>Rise in temperature $ = 675 - 300 = 375\\,K$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37536,'physics',29,1305,3155,'If the mean free path of atoms is doubled keeping temperature constant then the pressure of gas will become','[{"text": "$P / 4$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$P / 2$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$P / 8$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$P$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Mean free path $\\lambda=\\dfrac{R T}{\\sqrt{2} \\pi d^{2} N_{A} P}$ $\\lambda \\propto \\dfrac{1}{P}$; If $\\lambda$ is doubled then $P$ becomes half.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37537,'physics',27,1381,3314,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Water is used in automobile radiatiors.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Water has a high heat capacity.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'So option (1) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37538,'physics',26,1133,2708,'Three liquids of equal masses are taken in three identical cubical vessels $A$, $B$ and $C$. Their densities are ${\\rho _A},{\\rho _B}$ and ${\\rho _C}$ respectively. But ${\\rho _A} < {\\rho _B} < {\\rho _C}$. The force exerted by the liquid on the base on the cubical vessel is','[{"text": "maximum in vessel $C$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "minimum in vessel $C$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "the same in all the vessels", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "maximum in vessel $A$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Pressure exerted by the liquid on the base of the vessels is<br>$$P=\\rho \\times g \\times h$$<br>where,<br>$$\\rho=\\dfrac{m}{A \\times h} \\Rightarrow h=\\dfrac{m}{\\rho \\times A}$$<br>Substituting the value of $$h$$ we get<br>$$P=\\rho \\times g \\times \\dfrac{m}{\\rho \\times A}=\\dfrac{g m}{A}$$<br>Force exerted by the liquid on the base of the vessel is<br>$F = $ Pressure $$\\times$$ Area $ = P \\times A = \\frac{{gm}}{A} \\times A = gm$<br>Given that<br>${m_A} = {m_B} = {m_C} \\Rightarrow \\quad \\because {F_A} = {F_B} = {F_C}$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2010,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37539,'physics',28,1371,3283,'A monoatomic gas is enclosed in a cylinder with a movable frictionless piston. Its initial thermodynamic state at pressure ${P_i} = {10^5}\\,Pa$ and volume ${V_i} = {10^{ - 3}}\\,{m^3}$ changes to a final state at ${P_f} = (1/32) \\times {10^5}\\,Pa$ and ${V_f} = 8 \\times {10^{ - 3}}\\,{m^3}$ in an adiabatic quasi-static process, The amount of heat supplied to the system in the process is','[{"text": "$294\\\\,J$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$112\\\\,J$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$813\\\\,J$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$588\\\\,J$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'$\\gamma=\\dfrac{5}{3} \\Rightarrow$ monoatomic gas.<br>From first law of thermodynamics<br>$\\Delta Q = W + \\Delta U$<br>$W = {P_i}\\Delta V = {10^5} \\times 7 \\times {10^{ - 3}} = 700\\,\\,J$<br>$\\Delta U = n{C_V}\\Delta T = \\frac{3}{2}\\left[ {{P_f}{V_f} - {P_i}{V_i}} \\right] =  - \\frac{{900}}{8}\\,\\,J.$<br>So, $\\Delta Q = W + \\Delta U = 588\\,\\,J$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37540,'physics',29,1303,3152,'The molar specific heat relation for ideal gas is','[{"text": "$C_{P}+C_{V}=R$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$C_{P}-C_{V}=R$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$C_{P} / C_{V}=R$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$C_{V} / C_{P}=R$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'$C_{P}-C_{V}=R=$ Universal gas constant',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37541,'physics',27,1381,3314,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Two bodies at different temperature, if brought in contact do not necessary settle to a common temperature<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> The two bodies must have same thermal capacities.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'When two bodies at different temperatures $T_{1}$ and $T_{2}$ are brought in contact, they do settle to common temperature. They will do so even if the bodies may be having different thermal capacities. So option (3) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37542,'physics',26,1133,2705,'The volume of an air bubble becomes three times as it rises from the bottom of a lake to its surface. Assuming temperature to be constant and atmospheric pressure to be $75\\,cm$ of $Hg$ and the density of water to be $$1 / 10$$ of the density of then mercury, the depth of the lake is','[{"text": "$5\\\\,m$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$10\\\\,m$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$15\\\\,m$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$20\\\\,m$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'${p_1}\\,{V_1} = {p_2}\\,{V_2}$<br>$(75 + h)V = (75)(3\\,V)$<br>$\\therefore h = 150\\,cm$ of $Hg$<br>$ = 1.5\\,m$ of $Hg$ or $15\\,m$ of water',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37543,'physics',28,1371,3286,'An ideal gas is taken through a quasi-static process described by $P=\\alpha V^{2}$, with $\\alpha  = 5.00\\,atm/{m^6}$. The gas is expanded to twice its original volume of  $1.00\\,{m^3}$. How much work is done by the gas in expanding gas in this process?','[{"text": "$2.18\\\\,MJ$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1.18\\\\,MJ$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$3.18\\\\,MJ$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1.28\\\\,MJ$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'The work done by the gas  $W=\\int_{i}^{f} P d V$<br>$W=\\int_{i}^{f} \\alpha V^{2} d V=\\dfrac{1}{3} \\alpha\\left(V_{f}^{3}-V_{i}^{3}\\right)$<br>${V_f} = 2{V_i} = 2\\left( {1.00\\,{m^3}} \\right) = 2.00\\,{m^3}$<br>$\\left. {W = \\frac{1}{3}\\left[ {5.00\\,atm/{m^6}} \\right)\\left( {1.013 \\times {{10}^5}\\,Pa/atm} \\right)} \\right]$<br>$ \\times \\left[ {{{\\left( {2.00{m^3}} \\right)}^3} - {{\\left( {1.00{m^3}} \\right)}^3}} \\right] = 1.18\\,MJ$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37544,'physics',29,1303,3152,'If the degree of freedom of a gas are $f$, then the ratio of two specific heats $C_{P} / C_{V}$ is given by','[{"text": "$\\\\dfrac{2}{f}+1$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$1+\\\\dfrac{1}{f}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1-\\\\dfrac{1}{f}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1-\\\\dfrac{2}{f}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'$\\dfrac{C_{P}}{C_{V}}=\\gamma=1+\\dfrac{2}{f}$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37545,'physics',26,1133,2711,'Two substances of relative densities $$\\rho_{1}$$ and $$\\rho_{2}$$ are mixed in equal volume and the relative density of mixture is 4 . When they are mixed in equal masses, the relative density of the mixture is 3 . The values $$\\rho_{1}$$ and $$\\rho_{2}$$ are','[{"text": "$$\\\\rho_{1}=6, \\\\rho_{2}=2$$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$$\\\\rho_{1}=3, \\\\rho_{2}=5$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$\\\\rho_{1}=12, \\\\rho_{2}=4$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "None of these", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'<br>$4 = \\frac{{{\\rho _1}\\,V + {\\rho _2}\\,V}}{{2\\,V}}{\\rm{ }}or{\\rm{ }}{\\rho _1} + {\\rho _2} = 8\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,(1)$<br>$$3=\\dfrac{m+m}{\\left(m / \\rho_{1}\\right)+\\left(m / \\rho_{2}\\right)}$$<br>${\\rm{ or }}\\frac{{{\\rho _1}{\\rho _2}}}{{{\\rho _1} + {\\rho _2}}} = \\frac{3}{2}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,(2)$<br>Solving these two equations, we get<br>$$\\rho_{1}=6, \\rho_{2}=2$$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37546,'physics',27,1382,3317,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> If thermal conductivity of a rod is 5 , then its thermal resistivity is 0.2 .<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Thermal conductivity<br>$=\\dfrac{1}{\\text { thermal resistivity }}$','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'<br>$\\begin{aligned}\\text { Thermal Conductivity } & =\\dfrac{1}{\\text { themal resistivity }} \\\\& =\\dfrac{1}{0.2}=5\\end{aligned}$<br>So option (1) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37547,'physics',28,1371,3286,'A gas expand with temperature according to the relation $V=K T^{2 / 3}$. What is the work done when the temperature changes by $30^\\circ C$ ?','[{"text": "$20\\\\,R$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$10\\\\,R$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$40\\\\,R$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$30\\\\,R$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'$W=\\int P d V=\\int \\dfrac{R T}{V} d V$<br>Since $V=K T^{2 / 3}$<br>Hence, $d V=\\dfrac{2}{3} K T^{-1 / 3} d T \\quad \\therefore \\dfrac{d V}{V}=\\dfrac{2}{3} \\cdot \\dfrac{d T}{T}$<br>Hence<br>$W=\\int_{T_{1}}^{T_{2}} \\dfrac{2}{3} \\dfrac{R T}{T} d T=\\dfrac{2}{3} R\\left(T_{2}-T_{1}\\right)=\\dfrac{2}{3} R(30)=20 R$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37548,'physics',29,1303,3151,'A diatomic molecule has how many degrees of freedom','[{"text": "6", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "4", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "5", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "3", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'A diatomic molecule has three translational and two rotational degrees of freedom.<br>Hence total degrees of freedom $f=3+2=5$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37549,'physics',27,1382,3319,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Greater the coefficient of thermal conductivity of a material, smaller is the thermal resistance of a rod of that material.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Thermal resistance is the ratio of temperature difference between the ends of the conductor and rate of flow of heat.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but reason is correct.", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Resistance $\\propto \\dfrac{1}{\\text { conductivity }}$<br>Resistance $=\\dfrac{\\text { heat flow }}{\\Delta T}$<br>So option (4) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37550,'physics',26,1133,2710,'Iceberg floats in water with part of it submerged. What is the dfraction of the volume of iceberg submerged if the density of ice is $\\rho  = 0.917\\,g$ $c{m^{ - 3}}$ ?','[{"text": "0.458", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "0", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "0.917", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "1", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Given that ${\\rho _{ice{\\rm{ }}}} = 0.917\\,g\\,c{m^{ - 3}}$ and<br>${\\rho _w} = 1\\,g\\,c{m^{ - 3}}$<br>Let $V$ be the total volume of the iceberg and $\\therefore V{\\rho _{ice{\\rm{ }}}}g = {V_1}{\\rho _w}g$ be the volume of iceberg submerged in water. Since, iceberg is floating.<br>Weight of iceberg = Weight of water displaced by the immersed part of the icebreg<br>$\\therefore V{\\rho _{ice{\\rm{ }}}}g = {V_1}{\\rho _w}g$<br>$ \\Rightarrow \\frac{{{V_1}}}{{\\,V}} = \\frac{{{\\rho _{ice{\\rm{ }}}}}}{{{\\rho _w}}} = 0.917$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2020,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37551,'physics',28,1371,3286,'For a process of monoatomic gas pressure $(P)$ and volume $(V)$ related as $P V^{-3}=$ constant. What will be the molar heat capacity of gas ?','[{"text": "$\\\\dfrac{5 R}{3}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{3}{5} R$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{7}{4} R$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{R}{4}$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'The polytropic equation is $P V^{x}=$ constant<br> $x=-3$<br>$P V^{-3}=$ constant<br>$C=\\dfrac{R}{\\gamma-1}-\\dfrac{R}{x-1} \\gamma=\\dfrac{5}{3}$<br>$C=\\dfrac{R}{\\dfrac{5}{3}-1}-\\dfrac{R}{-3-1}=\\dfrac{3 R}{2}+\\dfrac{R}{4}=\\dfrac{7}{4} R$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37552,'physics',29,1303,3152,'Specific heats of monoatomic and diatomic gases are same and satisfy the relation which is','[{"text": "$C_{p}$ (mono) $=C_{v}$ (dia)", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$C_{p}$ (mono) $=C_{p}($ dia $)$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$C_{v}($ mono $)=C_{v}$ (dia)", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$C_{v}$ (mono) $=C_{p}$ (dia)", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'For monoatomic gas<br>$C_{P}=\\dfrac{5}{2} R$<br>For diatomic gas<br>$C_{V}=\\dfrac{5}{2} R$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37554,'physics',26,1133,2710,'Sphere of iron and lead having same mass are completely immersed in water. Density of lead is more than that of iron. Apparent loss of weight is ${W_1}$ for iron sphere and  ${W_2}$ for lead sphere. Then $\\frac{{{W_1}}}{{\\,{W_2}}}$ is','[{"text": "$$=1$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "between 0 and 1", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$=0$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$>1$$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'<br>${\\rm{Density(\\rho ) = }}\\frac{{{\\rm{Mass(}}m{\\rm{)}}}}{{{\\rm{Volume(}}V{\\rm{)}}}}$<br>$\\rho  \\propto \\frac{1}{{\\,V}}{\\rm{for}}\\,{\\rm{the}}\\,{\\rm{same}}\\,{\\rm{mass}}.$<br>$\\frac{{{\\rho _{{\\rm{lead }}}}}}{{{\\rho _{{\\rm{iron }}}}}} = \\frac{{{V_{i{\\rm{ron }}}}}}{{{V_{{\\rm{lead }}}}}}$<br>Given that $$\\rho_{\\text {lead }}>\\rho_{\\text {iron }}$$<br>$\\therefore \\frac{{{V_{{\\rm{iron }}}}}}{{{V_{{\\rm{lead }}}}}} > 1\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,(1)$<br>The buyoancy forces on the spheres are<br>For iron sphere<br>${W_1} = {V_{{\\rm{iron}}}}{\\rho _{{\\rm{water}}}}g\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,(2)$<br>For lead sphere <br>${W_2} = {V_{{\\rm{lead }}}}{\\rho _{{\\rm{water }}}}g\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,(3)$<br>From the above three eq\'s<br>$\\frac{{{W_1}}}{{\\,{W_2}}} = \\frac{{{V_{{\\rm{iron }}}}}}{{{V_{{\\rm{lead }}}}}} > 1$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2012,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37556,'physics',29,1304,3154,'One gram of ${H_2}$ at $27^\\circ {\\rm{ }}C$ is mixed with $16\\,g$ of ${O_2}$  at $37^\\circ {\\rm{ }}C$. The temperature of the mixture is about:-','[{"text": "$32^\\\\circ {\\\\rm{ }}C$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$37^\\\\circ {\\\\rm{ }}C$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$45^\\\\circ {\\\\rm{ }}C$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$27^\\\\circ {\\\\rm{ }}C$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'When two gases are mixed then their total internal energy remains constant.<br>$\\begin{aligned}& n_{1} C_{V_{1}} T_{1}+n_{2} C_{V_{2}} T_{2}=\\left(n_{1}+n_{2}\\right) C_{V} T \\\\& \\left(C_{V_{1}}=C_{V_{2}}=C_{V}, \\text { As both are diatomic }\\right)\\end{aligned}$<br>${n_1} = \\frac{{{m_1}}}{{{M_1}}},{n_2} = \\frac{{{m_2}}}{{{M_2}}}$<br>$T = \\frac{{{n_1}{T_1} + {n_2}{T_2}}}{{{n_1} + {n_2}}} = 305\\,\\,k = 32^\\circ C$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37557,'physics',26,1133,2710,'Two solid pieces, one of steel and the other of aluminium when immersed completely in water have equal weights. When the solid pieces are weighed in air','[{"text": "The weight of aluminium is half the weight of steel", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Steel piece will weigh more", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "They have the same weight", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Aluminium piece will weigh more", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'As weight in water is same,<br>Let ${m_s}{\\rm{ }}\\,\\& \\,{m_{Al}}$ be the mass of steel and aluminium<br>$$\\rho_{w}$$ - density of water<br>$$\\rho_{s}$$ - density of steel<br>$$\\rho_{A l}$$ - density of aluminium<br>$$m_{s} g-\\rho_{w}\\left(\\dfrac{m_{s}}{\\rho_{s}}\\right) g=m_{A l}(g)-\\rho_{w}\\left(\\dfrac{m_{A l}}{\\rho_{A l}}\\right) g$$<br>$$\\Rightarrow \\dfrac{m_{A l}}{m_{s}}=\\dfrac{1-\\dfrac{\\rho_{w}}{\\rho_{s}}}{1-\\dfrac{\\rho_{w}}{\\rho_{A l}}}$$<br>Since $$\\rho_{A l} < \\rho_{s} \\Rightarrow m_{A l}>m_{s}$$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2009,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37558,'physics',27,1380,3306,'Coefficient of linear expansion of brass and steel rods are $\\alpha_{1}$ and $\\alpha_{2}$. Lengths of brass and steel rods are $\\ell_{1}$ and $\\ell_{2}$ respectively. If $\\left(\\ell_{2}-\\ell_{1}\\right)$ is maintained same at all temperatures, which one of the following relations holds good?','[{"text": "$\\\\alpha_{1} \\\\ell_{2}=\\\\alpha_{2} \\\\ell_{1}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\alpha_{1} \\\\ell_{2}^{2}=\\\\alpha_{2} \\\\ell_{1}^{2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\alpha_{1}^{2} \\\\ell_{2}=\\\\alpha_{2} \\\\ell_{1}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\alpha_{1} \\\\ell_{1}=\\\\alpha_{2} \\\\ell_{2}$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Given that<br>$\\begin{aligned}& \\ell_{2}-\\ell_{1}=\\text { constant } \\\\& \\Rightarrow \\quad \\ell_{2}-\\ell_{1}=\\ell_{2}^{1}-\\ell_{1}^{1} \\\\& \\ell_{2}^{1}-\\ell_{2}=\\ell_{1}^{1}-\\ell_{1}\\end{aligned}$<br>Change in length for both rods should be same<br>$\\begin{aligned}& \\Delta \\ell_{1}=\\Delta \\ell_{2} \\\\& \\ell_{1} \\alpha_{1} \\Delta T=\\ell_{2} \\alpha_{2} \\Delta T \\\\& \\ell_{1} \\alpha_{1}=\\ell_{2} \\alpha_{2}\\end{aligned}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2016,'NEET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37560,'physics',29,1302,3150,'If the volume of a gas is doubled at constant pressure, the average translational kinetic energy of its molecules will','[{"text": "Remains the same", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Be doubled", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Become four times", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Increase by a factor", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'If volume is doubled at constant pressure, then absolute temperature of the gas is doubled. So, average translational kinetic energy is also doubled.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37561,'physics',26,1133,2710,'A block of wood is floating on the surface of water in a beaker. The beaker is covered with a bell jar and the air is evacuated. What will happen to the block?','[{"text": "Sink a little", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Rise a liitle", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Remain unchanged", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Sink completely", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'<img src=\'/img/data/physics/26_37561_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' /> <br>In the first case<br>Initially $$(p-p) A=$$ weight of block<br>or $\\left[ {\\left( {{p_0} + \\rho g\\,{h_1}} \\right) - {p_0}} \\right]A = W\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,(1)$<br>or ${h_1} = \\frac{w}{{A\\rho g}}$<br>In the second case<br>${P_2}\\,A = w$<br>or $$\\left(\\rho g h_{2}\\right) A=w$$<br>${\\rm{ or }}{h_2} = \\frac{w}{{\\rho Ag}}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,(2)$<br>From eq.(1) and (2) we see that $$h_{1}=h_{2}$$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37562,'physics',27,1381,3315,'A piece of ice falls from a height $h$ so that it melts completely. Only one-quater of the heat produced is absorbed by the ice and all energy of ice gets converted into heat during its fall. The value of $h$ is (Latent heat of ice is $3.4 \\times {10^5}\\,J/kg$ and $g = 10\\,N/kg)$','[{"text": "$34\\\\,km$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$544\\\\,km$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$136\\\\,km$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$68\\\\,km$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'$\\dfrac{m g h}{4}=m L$<br>where $\\mathrm{L}$ is is the latent heat of fusion<br>$ \\Rightarrow \\,\\,h = \\frac{{4L}}{g} = \\frac{{4 \\times 3.4 \\times {{10}^5}}}{{10}} = 136\\,km$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2016,'NEET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37564,'physics',29,1302,3150,'Choose the correct statement from the following:','[{"text": "The average kinetic energy of a molecule of any gas is same at the same temperature.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "The average kinetic energy of a molecule of a gas is independent of its temperature.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "The average kinetic energy of $1 g$ of any gas is the same at the same temperature.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "The average kinetic energy of $1 g$  of a gas is independent of its termperature.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'The average kinetic energy of a gas particle is<br>$K . E=\\dfrac{3}{2} K T$<br>K.E is independent of nature of gas',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37565,'physics',26,1133,2710,'A small block of wood of relative density 0.5 is submerged in water. When the block is released, it starts moving upwards, the acceleration of the block is $\\left( {g = 10\\,m{s^{ - 2}}} \\right)$','[{"text": "$5\\\\,m{s^{ - 2}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$10\\\\,m{s^{ - 2}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$7.5\\\\,m{s^{ - 2}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$15\\\\,m{s^{ - 2}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'<br>$a = \\frac{{{\\rm{ Upthrust  -  weight }}}}{{{\\rm{ mass }}}} = \\frac{{V{\\rho _w}g - V\\rho }}{{V\\rho }}$<br>$ = \\left( {\\frac{{{\\rho _w} - \\rho }}{\\rho }} \\right)g$<br>$ = \\left( {\\frac{{1 - 0.5}}{{0.5}}} \\right)(10)$<br>$ = 10\\,m/{s^2}$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37576,'physics',29,1302,3150,'The mean energy of a molecule of an ideal gas is','[{"text": "$kT$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{1}{2}kT$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{3}{2}kT$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$2\\\\,kT$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'The mean energy of a molecule of an ideal gas is<br>$\\frac{3}{2}kT$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37566,'physics',27,1382,3322,'A black body is at a temperature of $5760\\,K$. The energy of radiation emitted by the body at wavelength $250\\,nm$ is $U_{1}$, at wavelength $500\\,nm$ is $U_{2}$ and that at $1000\\,nm$ is $U_{3}$. Wien\'s constant, $b = 2.88 \\times {10^6}nmK$. Which of the following is correct?','[{"text": "$U_{1}=0$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$U_{3}=0$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$U_{1}>U_{2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$U_{2}>U_{1}$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Maximum amount of emitted radiation corresponding to ${\\lambda _m} = \\frac{b}{T}$<br>${\\lambda _m} = \\frac{{2.88 \\times {{10}^6}nmK}}{{5760\\,K}} = 500\\,nm$<br>i.e., $U_{2}$ corresponds to maximum value <br><img src=\'/img/data/physics/27_37566_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' /> <br>From the graph ${U_1} < {U_2} > {U_3}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2016,'NEET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37568,'physics',29,1302,3150,'Pressure of an ideal gas is increased by keeping temperature constant. The kinetic energy of molecules','[{"text": "Decreases", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Increases", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Remains same", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Increases or decreases depending on the nature of gas.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'The kinetic energy of molecules of an ideal gas is<br>$K.E = \\frac{3}{2}N{k_B}\\,T$<br>From the above expression it is clear that $\\mathrm{E}$ depends on the temperature only. Thus, on increasing the pressure by keeping the temperature constant, the kinetic energy of molecules remains same.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37569,'physics',27,1382,3322,'The power radiated by a black body is $P$ and it radiates maximum energy at wavelength, $\\lambda_{0}$. If the temperature of the black body is now changed so that it radiates maximum energy at wavelength $\\dfrac{3}{4} \\lambda_{0}$, the power radiated by it becomes $n P$. The value of $n$ is','[{"text": "$\\\\dfrac{256}{81}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{81}{256}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{4}{3}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{3}{4}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Wein\'s displacement law: $T \\propto \\dfrac{1}{\\lambda}$<br>$\\Rightarrow \\dfrac{T^{\\prime}}{T}=\\dfrac{\\lambda}{\\lambda^{\\prime}}=\\dfrac{4}{3}$<br>Power $P \\alpha T^{4}$<br>$\\dfrac{P^{\\prime}}{P}=\\left(\\dfrac{T^{\\prime}}{T}\\right)^{4}=\\left(\\dfrac{4}{3}\\right)^{4}=\\dfrac{256}{81}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2018,'NEET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37570,'physics',26,1133,2710,'A cubical block of steel of each side equal to 1 is floating on mercury in vessel. The densities of steel and mercury are ${\\rho _s}$ and ${\\rho _m}$. The height of the block above the mercury level is given by','[{"text": "$\\\\left( {1 + \\\\frac{{{\\\\rho _s}}}{{{\\\\rho _m}}}} \\\\right)l$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\left( {1 - \\\\frac{{{\\\\rho _s}}}{{{\\\\rho _m}}}} \\\\right)l$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\left( {1 + \\\\frac{{{\\\\rho _{\\\\rm{m}}}}}{{{\\\\rho _{\\\\rm{s}}}}}} \\\\right)l$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "v$\\\\left( {1 - \\\\frac{{{\\\\rho _m}}}{{{\\\\rho _s}}}} \\\\right)l$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'<br>${f_i} = \\frac{{{\\rho _{{\\rm{solid }}}}}}{{{\\rho _{{\\rm{liquid }}}}}} = \\frac{{{\\rho _s}}}{{{\\rho _m}}}$<br>$\\therefore {h_i} = \\frac{{{\\rho _s}}}{{{\\rho _m}}}l$<br>$$\\therefore$$ Height above the mercury level.<br>$ = \\frac{{{\\rho _s}}}{{{\\rho _m}}} \\cdot l$<br>$ = \\left( {1 - \\frac{{{\\rho _s}}}{{{\\rho _m}}}} \\right)l$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37572,'physics',29,1302,3150,'The average translational kinetic energy of the molecules of a gas will be doubled if at constant','[{"text": "Temperature, its pressure is doubled", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Volume its pressure is doubled", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Temperature, its volume is doubled", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Pressure, its volume is halved<br>", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'At constant volume when pressure is doubled then temperature is also doubled. As $K . E \\propto T$ so K.E is also doubled',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37573,'physics',27,1380,3306,'A copper rod of $88\\,cm$ and an aluminium rod of unknown length have their increase in length independent of increase in temperature. The length of aluminium rod is :$\\left(\\alpha_{c u}=1.7 \\times 10^{-5} K^{-1}\\right.$ and $\\left.\\alpha_{A l}=2.2 \\times 10^{-5} K^{-1}\\right)$','[{"text": "$6.8\\\\,cm$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$113.9\\\\,cm$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$88\\\\,cm$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$68\\\\,cm$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Given that $\\Delta L_{C u}=\\Delta L_{A l}$<br>${\\alpha _{Cu}}{L_{Cu}} = {\\alpha _{Al}}{L_{Al}}$<br>$1.7 \\times {10^{ - 5}} \\times 88\\,cm = 2.2 \\times {10^{ - 5}} \\times {L_{Al}}$<br>${L_{Al}} = \\frac{{1.7 \\times 88}}{{2.2}} = 68\\,cm$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2019,'NEET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37574,'physics',26,1136,2718,'The cylindrical tube of a spray pump has a cross-section of $8\\,c{m^2}$, one end of which has 40 fine holes each of area ${10^{ - 8}}\\,{m^2}$. If the liquid flows inside the tube with a speed of $0.15\\,m{s^{ - 1}}$, the speed with which the liquid is ejected through the holes is','[{"text": "$50\\\\,m\\\\,{s^{ - 1}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$5\\\\,m\\\\,{s^{ - 1}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$0.05\\\\,m\\\\,{s^{ - 1}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$0.5\\\\,m\\\\,{s^{ - 1}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'From equation of continuity,<br>$$\\mathrm{A} v=\\mathrm{constant}$$<br>$$\\therefore$$ For tube, $\\left( {8 \\times {{10}^{ - 4}}} \\right) \\times \\left( {\\frac{{0.15}}{{60}}} \\right) = {A_1}{v_1}$<br>For holes, $\\left( {40 \\times {{10}^{ - 8}}} \\right) \\times v = {A_2}{v_2}$<br>$$\\therefore 40 \\times 10^{-8} \\times v-\\dfrac{8 \\times 10^{-4} \\times 0.15}{60}$$<br>$ \\Rightarrow v = \\frac{{8 \\times {{10}^{ - 4}} \\times 0.15}}{{40 \\times {{10}^{ - 8}} \\times 60}} = 5\\,m{s^{ - 1}}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2007,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37579,'physics',26,1133,2710,'A wooden block of mass $8\\,kg$ is tied to a string attached to the bottom of the tank. In the equilibrium the block is completely immersed in water. If relative density of wood is 0.8 and $g = $ $10\\,m{s^{ - 2}}$, the tension $T$, in the string is','[{"text": "$120\\\\,N$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$100\\\\,N$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$80\\\\,N$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$20\\\\,N$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Upthrust $$F=w+T$$<br><img src=\'/img/data/physics/26_37579_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' /> <br>$T = F - W$<br>$ = V{\\rho _w}g - V{\\rho _b}g$<br>$ = \\left( {{\\rho _w} - {\\rho _b}} \\right)Vg$<br>$ = (1000 - 800)\\left( {\\frac{8}{{800}}} \\right)(10)$<br>$ = 20\\,N$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37580,'physics',29,1302,3150,'A gas mixture consists of molecules of type 1,2 and 3 with molecular masses $m_{1}>m_{2}>m_{3}$. Which gas has maximum average translational kinetic energy?','[{"text": "2", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "3", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "1", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "All has same average", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Kinetic energy depends only on temperature.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37581,'physics',27,1381,3314,'The quantities of heat required to raise the temperature of two solid copper spheres of radii $r_{1}$ and ${r_2}\\left( {{r_1} = 1.5\\,{r_2}} \\right)$ through $1\\,K$ are in the ratio:','[{"text": "$9 / 4$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$3 / 2$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$5 / 3$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$27 / 8$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Heat supplied $\\Delta Q=m s \\Delta T$<br>For same material \'s\' same.<br>$\\Delta Q \\propto m$ and $m=\\dfrac{4}{3} \\pi r^{3} \\rho$<br>$\\Delta Q \\propto r^{3}$<br>$\\dfrac{\\Delta Q_{1}}{\\Delta Q_{2}}=\\left(\\dfrac{r_{1}}{r_{2}}\\right)^{3}=\\left(\\dfrac{1.5}{1}\\right)^{3}=\\dfrac{27}{8}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2020,'NEET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37582,'physics',26,1134,2713,'A flow of liquid is streamline if the Reynold number is','[{"text": "between 2000 to 3000", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "less than 1000", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "between 4000 to 5000", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "greater than 1000", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'If Reynold number is less than 1000, the flow is streamline. If Reynold number is greater than 2000, the flow is turbulent. If Reynold number lies between 1000 and 2000, the flow becomes unsteady.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2014,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37584,'physics',29,1304,3154,'If the internal energy of $n_{1}$ moles of $He$ at temperature $10\\,\\,T$ is equal to the internal energy of $n_{2}$ mole of hydrogen at temperature $6\\,\\,T$. The ratio of $\\dfrac{n_{1}}{n_{2}}$ is','[{"text": "1", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "2", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$3 / 5$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$5 / 3$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Internal energy of $n_{1}$ moles of $He$ is<br>$U_{1}=n_{1} \\dfrac{3}{2} R(10 T)$<br>Internal energy of $n_{2}$ moles of hydrogen is<br>$U_{2}=n_{2} \\dfrac{5}{2} R(6 T)$<br>Given that $U_{1}=U_{2}$<br>$\\begin{aligned}& n_{1} C_{V_{1}} T_{1}=n_{2} C_{V_{2}} T_{2} \\\\& C_{V_{1}}=\\dfrac{3}{2} R, \\quad C_{V_{2}}=\\dfrac{5}{2} R \\\\& n_{1} \\dfrac{3}{2} R(10 T)=n_{2} \\dfrac{5}{2} R(6 T) \\\\& \\dfrac{n_{1}}{n_{2}}=1\\end{aligned}$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37594,'physics',28,1372,3293,'\\(n\\) \' moles of an ideal gas undergoes a process \\[A{\\rm{ }} \\to B\\] as shown in the figure. The maximum temperature of the gas during the process will be :<div class="matching-question"></div>','[{"text": "$\\\\dfrac{9 P_{0} V_{0}}{2 n R}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{9 P_{0} V_{0}}{n R}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{9 P_{0} V_{0}}{4 n R}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{3 P_{0} V_{0}}{2 n R}$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Equation of line is<br>$P{V_0} + {P_0}V = 3{P_0}{V_0}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\left( 1 \\right)$<br>Also $P V=n R T$<br>From the above two eq\'s<br>$T=\\dfrac{3 P_{0} V}{n R}-\\dfrac{P_{0} V^{2}}{n R V_{0}}$<br>for $T_{\\max }, \\dfrac{d T}{d V}=0 \\Rightarrow V=\\dfrac{3 V_{0}}{2}, P=\\dfrac{3 P_{0}}{2}$<br>$\\Rightarrow T_{\\max }=\\dfrac{9 P_{0} V_{0}}{4 n R}$',NULL,'Hard','Graph/Figure',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/28_37594_question_1.png'),
(37587,'physics',26,1136,2717,'A rectangular vessel when full of water, takes 10 minutes to be emptied through an orifice in its bottom. How much time will it take to be emptied when half filled with water?','[{"text": "9 minutes", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "7 minutes", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "5 minutes", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "3 minutes", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Let $A$ and $$a$$ be cross-sectional areas of the vessel and orifice respectively. Let $h$ be height of water in the vessel at time t. Let $\\frac{{dh}}{{dt}}$ represent the rate of fall of level.<br>Then $A\\left( { - \\frac{{dh}}{{dt}}} \\right) = av = a\\sqrt {2gh} $<br>$ - \\frac{{dh}}{{dt}} = \\frac{{a\\sqrt {2gh} }}{A}$<br>$ - \\int_h^0 {\\frac{1}{{\\sqrt h }}} dh = \\frac{{a\\sqrt {2g} }}{A}\\int_0^t d t{\\rm{ }}\\,{\\rm{or}}\\,{\\rm{ }} - ( - 2\\sqrt h ) = \\frac{{a\\sqrt {2gt} }}{A}$<br>$t = \\frac{A}{a}\\frac{1}{{\\sqrt {2g} }} \\times 2\\sqrt h $<br>$\\frac{{{t_1}}}{{{t_2}}} = \\sqrt {\\frac{{{h_1}}}{{\\,{h_2}}}} {\\rm{ }}\\,{\\rm{or}}\\,{\\rm{ }}{t_2} = {t_1}\\sqrt {\\frac{{{h_2}}}{{\\,{h_1}}}}  = 10\\sqrt {\\frac{1}{2}} $<br>$ = \\frac{{10}}{{\\sqrt 2 }} = 5\\sqrt 2  = 7\\,\\min $',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2006,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37588,'physics',29,1301,3147,'The pressure of a gas does not depend up on','[{"text": "Kinetic energy of its molecules", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "The temperature of gas", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "The density of the gas", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "The volume of the gas when the density remains unchanged<br>", "isCorrect": true}]',3,NULL,NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37589,'physics',27,1378,3304,'On a new scale of temperature (which is linear) and called the $W$ scale, the freezing and boiling points of water are $39^\\circ W$ and $239^\\circ W$ respectively. What will be the temperature on the new scale, corresponding to a temperature of $39^\\circ W$ on the Celsius scale?','[{"text": "$78^\\\\circ W$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$117^\\\\circ W$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$200^\\\\circ W$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$139^\\\\circ W$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'The relation between two scales are shown in the figure.<br><img src=\'/img/data/physics/27_37589_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' /> <br>$\\dfrac{(239-39)}{(100-0)}=\\dfrac{(t-39)}{(39-0)} \\Rightarrow t=117^{\\circ} \\mathrm{W}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37591,'physics',26,1136,2717,'A vessel completely filled with water has holes \' $$A$$ \' and \' $$B$$ \' at depths \' $$h$$ \' and \' $$3 h$$ \' from the top, respectively. Hole \' $$A$$ \' is a square of side \' $$L$$ \' and \' $B$ \' is circle of radius \' $r$ \'. The water flowing out per second from both the holes is same. Then \' $L$ \' is equal to','[{"text": "${r^{\\\\frac{1}{2}}} \\\\cdot {(\\\\pi )^{\\\\frac{1}{2}}} \\\\cdot {(3)^{\\\\frac{1}{2}}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$r \\\\cdot {(\\\\pi )^{\\\\frac{1}{4}}} \\\\cdot {(3)^{\\\\frac{1}{4}}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$r \\\\cdot {(\\\\pi )^{\\\\frac{1}{2}}} \\\\cdot {(3)^{\\\\frac{1}{4}}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${r^{\\\\frac{1}{2}}} \\\\cdot {(\\\\pi )^{\\\\frac{1}{3}}} \\\\cdot {(3)^{\\\\frac{1}{2}}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Given, depth of hole $A$ from the top $$=h$$<br>Depth of hole B from the top $ = 3\\,h$<br>According to question, we can draw the following diagram<br><img src=\'/img/data/physics/26_37591_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' /> <br>According the continuity equation,<br>$$A_{1} v_{1}=A_{2} v_{2}$$<br>Velocity of efflux,<br>$$\\begin{aligned}& v_{1}=\\sqrt{2 g h} \\quad \\text { (for square hole) } \\\\& v_{2}=\\sqrt{2 g(3 h)}=\\sqrt{6 g h} \\text { (for circular hole) } \\\\& L^{2} \\sqrt{2 g h}=\\pi r^{2} \\sqrt{6 g h}\\end{aligned}$$<br>$$\\left(\\because\\right.$$ area of square $$=L^{2}$$, area of circle $$\\left.=\\pi r^{2}\\right)$$<br>On squaring both sides, we get<br>$$\\begin{aligned}& 2 L^{2} g h=6 \\pi^{2} r^{4} g h \\\\& 2 L^{4}=6 \\pi^{2} r^{4} \\\\& L=\\left(3 \\pi^{2}\\right)^{1 / 4} r \\\\& L=r \\pi^{1 / 2}(3)^{1 / 4}\\end{aligned}$$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2018,'MHTCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37592,'physics',29,1300,3145,'A closed compartment containing gas is moving with some acceleration in horizontal direction. Neglect effect of gravity. Then the pressure in the compartment is','[{"text": "Same everywhere", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Lower in the front side", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Lower in the rear side", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Lower in the upper side<br>", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Pressure will be less in front portion of the compartment because in accelerated frame molecules will feel pseudo force in backward direction. Also density of gas will be more in the back portion.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37593,'physics',27,1380,3309,'A metal rod of length \' $L$ \' and cross - sectional area \' $A$ \' is heated through $T^\\circ C$. What is the force required to prevent the expansion of the rod length wise?','[{"text": "$\\\\dfrac{Y A \\\\alpha T}{(1-\\\\alpha T)}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{Y A \\\\alpha T}{(1+\\\\alpha T)}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{(1+\\\\alpha T)}{Y A \\\\alpha T}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{(1-\\\\alpha T)}{Y A \\\\alpha T}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Increase in length of the rod (if allowed to expand) is given as<br>$\\Delta L = \\alpha LT\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\left( 1 \\right)$<br>$\\therefore {\\rm{Newlength }}{L^\\prime } = L[1 + \\alpha T]\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\left( 2 \\right)$<br>Considering the force (F) needed to return the rod to its original length,<br>Young\'s modulus $Y = \\frac{{(F/A)}}{{\\left( {\\Delta L/{L^\\prime }} \\right)}} = \\frac{{F{L^\\prime }}}{{A\\Delta L}}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\left( 3 \\right)$<br>Using relation (2) and substituting in the relation (3), we get<br>$Y = \\frac{F}{A} \\cdot \\frac{{L(1 + \\alpha T)}}{{\\Delta L}}{\\rm{ }}$<br>${\\rm{or }}\\Delta L = \\frac{{FL(1 + \\alpha T)}}{{AY}}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\left( 4 \\right)$<br>From eq. (1) and (4), we get<br>$\\begin{aligned}& \\dfrac{F L(1+\\alpha T)}{A Y}=\\alpha L T \\\\& \\Rightarrow F=\\dfrac{Y A \\alpha T}{(1+\\alpha T)}\\end{aligned}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2015,'MHTCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37801,'physics',28,1371,3282,'One mole of an ideal gas undergoes an isothermal change at temperature $T$ so that its volume 7 times its initial value. $R$ is the molar gas constant. Work done by the gas during this change is','[{"text": "$RT\\\\ln 4$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$RT\\\\ln 7$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$RT\\\\ln 1$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$RT\\\\ln 3$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Work done by the gas during the isothermal process.<br>$W=n R T \\ln \\dfrac{V_{2}}{V_{1}}$<br>$=(1) R T \\ln \\left(\\dfrac{7 V}{V}\\right)=R T \\ln 7$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37595,'physics',26,1136,2717,'There is a hole of area a at the bottom of a cylindrical area $A$. Water is filled upto a height $h$ and water flows out it in $t$ second. If water is filled to a height $4\\,h$, it will flow out in time','[{"text": "$\\\\frac{t}{4}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2t$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$4t$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{t}{2}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Time to empty a tank $$\\mathrm{t}=\\dfrac{\\mathrm{A}}{a} \\sqrt{\\dfrac{2 \\mathrm{H}}{\\mathrm{g}}}$$ or $$\\quad t \\propto \\sqrt{\\mathrm{H}}$$<br>For $H = h,\\,t = t;$ and for $H = 4h,\\,t\' = 2t$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37596,'physics',29,1300,3145,'If temperature and pressure of an ideal gas at NTP both are doubled, its pressure becomes','[{"text": "$3 \\\\times {10^5} N/{m^2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${10^5} N/{m^2}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$4 \\\\times {10^5} N/{m^2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$0.5 \\\\times {10^5} N/{m^2}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'$P V=n R T \\Rightarrow P \\propto \\dfrac{T}{V}$. If $T$ and $V$ both doubled then pressure remains same, i.e.,<br>${P_2} = {P_1} = 1\\,atm = 1 \\times {10^5} N/{m^2}$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37597,'physics',27,1380,3307,'A steel scale measures the length of a copper wire as $80.0\\,cm$, when both are at $20^\\circ C$ (the calibration temperature for scale). What would be the scale read for the length of the wire when both are at $40^\\circ C$ ? (Given ${\\alpha _{{\\rm{steel }}}} = 11 \\times {10^{ - 6}}{\\mathop{\\rm per}\\nolimits} \\,^\\circ C$ and ${\\alpha _{{\\rm{copper }}}} = 17 \\times {10^{ - 6}}{\\mathop{\\rm per}\\nolimits} \\,^\\circ C$)','[{"text": "$80.0096\\\\,cm$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$80.0272\\\\,cm$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1\\\\,cm$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$25.2\\\\,cm$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'With temperature rise (same $25^\\circ C$ for both), both steel scale and copper wire expand. Hence length of copper wire w.r.to steel scale or apparent length of copper wire will increase after rise in temperature<br>${L_{app{\\rm{ }}}} = L_{Cu}^\\prime  - L_{steel{\\rm{ }}}^\\prime $<br>$ = \\left[ {{L_0}\\left( {1 + {\\alpha _{Cu}}\\Delta \\theta } \\right) - {L_0}\\left( {1 + {\\alpha _s}\\Delta \\theta } \\right)} \\right]$<br>$ \\Rightarrow \\,\\,\\,{\\mkern 1mu} {\\kern 1pt} {L_{app{\\rm{ }}}} = {L_0}\\left( {{\\alpha _{Cu}} - {\\alpha _s}} \\right)\\Delta \\theta $<br>$ = 80\\left( {17 \\times {{10}^{ - 6}} - 11 \\times {{10}^{ - 6}}} \\right) \\times 20$<br>$ = 80.0096\\,cm$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37598,'physics',28,1372,3294,'Find the heat absorbed by a system in going through the cyclic process shown in the figure<div class="matching-question"></div>','[{"text": "$ - 5\\\\,\\\\pi \\\\,J$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$ - 2.5\\\\,\\\\pi \\\\,J$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\pi \\\\,J$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$ - 10\\\\,\\\\pi \\\\,J$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'$a = \\frac{{\\left( {2 \\times {{10}^5} - 1 \\times {{10}^5}} \\right)}}{2} = \\frac{{{{10}^5}}}{2}\\,N/{m^2}$<br>$b = \\frac{{(200 - 100)}}{2}\\,c{m^3} \\Rightarrow b = 50\\,c{m^3}$<br>${W_{cycle}} =  - 2.5\\,\\pi {\\mkern 1mu} joules$.<br>$W = ( - )$ ve As $P$ is on $x$-axis and the gas follows clockwise path.<br>${W_{cycle{\\rm{ }}}} = {\\rm{ }}Area{\\rm{ }} = \\pi ab,\\Delta q = W\\left( {\\Delta {U_{cycle{\\rm{ }}}} = 0} \\right)$',NULL,'Medium','Graph/Figure',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/28_37598_question_1.png'),
(37599,'physics',26,1137,2723,'A small metal sphere of mass $$M$$ and density $$d_{1}$$, when dropped in a jar filled with liquid moves with terminal velocity after sometime. The viscous force acting on the sphere is $$\\left(d_{2}=\\right.$$ density of liquid and $g = $ gravitational acceleration)','[{"text": "$Mg\\\\left( {\\\\frac{{{d_1}}}{{{d_2}}}} \\\\right)$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$Mg\\\\left( {1 - \\\\frac{{{d_2}}}{{{d_1}}}} \\\\right)$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$Mg\\\\left( {\\\\frac{{{d_2}}}{{{d_1}}}} \\\\right)$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$Mg\\\\left( {1 - \\\\frac{{{d_1}}}{{{d_2}}}} \\\\right)$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'The motion of metal sphere is shown as <br> <img src=\'/img/data/physics/26_37599_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' /> <br> Where, $$F_{B}=$$ Buoyant force and<br>$$F_{v}=$$ viscous force <br> At terminal velocity, the net force on the sphere is zero <br> $$W=F_{B}+F_{v}$$<br>$$\\Rightarrow m g=d_{2} V g+F_{v}$$<br>$$=d_{2} \\dfrac{M}{d_{1}} g+F_{v}$$ <br> $$\\therefore F_{v}=M g\\left(1-\\dfrac{d_{2}}{d_{1}}\\right)$$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2020,'MHTCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37600,'physics',29,1301,3147,'A gas in a vessel is at the pressure $P_{0}$. If the masses of all the molecules be made half and their speed be made double, then the resultant pressure will be','[{"text": "$2 P_{0}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$4 P_{0}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$P_{0} / 2$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$P_{0}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Let $P_{0}=\\dfrac{1}{3} \\dfrac{m n}{V} c^{2}$<br>$P^{\\prime}=\\dfrac{1}{3} \\dfrac{\\left(\\dfrac{m}{2}\\right) n}{V}(2 c)^{2}=2 P_{0}$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37601,'physics',27,1381,3314,'$0.1\\,{m^3}$ of water at $80^\\circ C$ is mixed with $0.3\\,{m^3}$of water at $60^\\circ C$. The final temperature of the mixture is','[{"text": "$65^\\\\circ C$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$70^\\\\circ C$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$60^\\\\circ C$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$75^\\\\circ C$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Density of water $ = {10^3}\\,kg/{m^3}$<br>Let the final temperature of the mixture be $T$.<br>Heat lost by water at $80^\\circ C$<br>$ = 0.1 \\times {10^3} \\times c \\times (80 - T)$<br>Heat gained by water at $60^\\circ C$<br>$ = 0.3 \\times {10^3} \\times c \\times (T - 60)$<br>Heat lost $=$ Heat gain<br>$0.1 \\times {10^3} \\times c \\times (80 - T)$<br>$ = 0.3 \\times {10^3} \\times c \\times (T - 60)$.<br>$ \\Rightarrow 1 \\times (80 - T) = 3 \\times (T - 60)$<br>$ \\Rightarrow 4\\,T = 260 \\Rightarrow T = 65^\\circ C.$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2009,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37602,'physics',26,1137,2723,'A metal sphere of radius \' $$R$$ \' and density \' $$\\rho_{1}$$ \' is dropped in a liquid of density \' $$\\sigma$$ \' moves with terminal velocity \' $$v$$ \'. Another metal sphere of same radius and density \' $$\\rho_{2}$$ \' is dropped in the same liquid, its terminal velocity will be','[{"text": "$v\\\\left[ {\\\\left( {{\\\\rho _2} + \\\\sigma } \\\\right)/\\\\left( {{\\\\rho _1} + \\\\sigma } \\\\right)} \\\\right]$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$v\\\\left[ {\\\\left( {{\\\\rho _1} + \\\\sigma } \\\\right)/\\\\left( {{\\\\rho _2} + \\\\sigma } \\\\right)} \\\\right]$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$v\\\\left[ {\\\\left( {{\\\\rho _2} - \\\\sigma } \\\\right)/\\\\left( {{\\\\rho _1} - \\\\sigma } \\\\right)} \\\\right]$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$v\\\\left[ {\\\\left( {{\\\\rho _1} - \\\\sigma } \\\\right)/\\\\left( {{\\\\rho _2} - \\\\sigma } \\\\right)} \\\\right]$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'<br>The terminal velocity of the sphere<br>$v = \\frac{2}{9}{R^2}\\frac{{\\left( {{\\rho _1} - \\sigma } \\right)g}}{\\eta }\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\left( 1 \\right)$<br>Here $R$ = radius of sphere,<br>$$\\rho_{1}=$$ density of sphere<br>and $$\\sigma=$$ density of liquid<br>Now, if density of metal sphere is changed to $$\\rho_{2}$$, then terminal velocity<br>${v_2} = \\frac{2}{9}\\frac{{{R^2}\\left( {{\\rho _2} - \\sigma } \\right)g}}{\\eta }\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\left( 2 \\right)$<br>Divide eq.(1) and (2), we get<br>$$\\dfrac{v_{2}}{v}=\\dfrac{\\left(\\rho_{2}-\\sigma\\right)}{\\left(\\rho_{1}-\\sigma\\right)} \\text { or } v_{2}=\\dfrac{v\\left(\\rho_{2}-\\sigma\\right)}{\\left(\\rho_{1}-\\sigma\\right)}$$<br>Hence the terminal velocity is $$\\dfrac{v\\left(\\rho_{2}-\\sigma\\right)}{\\left(\\rho_{1}-\\sigma\\right)}$$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37603,'physics',28,1372,3294,'One mole of a diatomic ideal gas undergoes a cyclic process \\[ABC\\] as shown in figure. The process \\[BC\\] is adiabatic. The temperatures at \\[A,B\\] and \\[C\\] are \\[400\\;K,800\\;K\\] and \\[600\\;K\\] respectively. Choose the correct statement:<div class="matching-question"></div>','[{"text": "The change in internal energy in whole cyclic process in $250R$.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "The change in internal energy in the process $CA$ is $700R$.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "The change in internal energy in the process $AB$ is $350R$.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "The change in internal energy in the process $BC$ is $500R$.", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'In cyclic process, change in total internal energy is zero.<br>$\\begin{aligned}& \\Delta U_{\\text {cyclic }}=0 \\\\& \\Delta U_{B C}=n C_{V} \\Delta T=1 \\times \\dfrac{5 R}{2} \\Delta T\\end{aligned}$<br>Where, $C_{V}=$ molar specific heat at constant volume. <br>For $BC,{\\rm{ }}\\Delta T =  - 200K$<br>$\\therefore \\Delta U_{B C}=-500 R$',NULL,'Medium','Graph/Figure',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/28_37603_question_1.png'),
(37604,'physics',29,1300,3145,'Air is pumped into an automobile tube upto a pressure of $200\\,\\,kPa$ in the morning at temperature $22^\\circ \\,C$. When the air temperature rises to $42^\\circ \\,C$ and the tube expands by $2 \\%$. The pressure of the air in the tube at this temperature, will be approximately','[{"text": "$206\\\\,\\\\,kPa$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$209\\\\,\\\\,kPa$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$200\\\\,\\\\,kPa$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$212\\\\,\\\\,kPa$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'$\\dfrac{P V}{T}=R$ (constant) $\\Rightarrow \\dfrac{P_{1} V_{1}}{T_{1}}=\\dfrac{P_{2} V_{2}}{T_{2}}$<br>$ \\Rightarrow \\frac{{200 \\times V}}{{(273 + 22)}} = \\frac{{{P_2} \\times 1.02 V}}{{(273 + 42)}}$<br>$\\left[ {{V_2} = V + 0.02 V} \\right]$<br>$ \\Rightarrow {P_2} = \\frac{{200 \\times 315}}{{295 \\times 1.02}} = 209\\,k\\,Pa$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37605,'physics',27,1381,3315,'1 gram of ice is mixed with 1 gram of steam. At thermal equilibrium, the temperature of the mixture is','[{"text": "$100^\\\\circ C$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$55^\\\\circ C$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$0^\\\\circ C$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$50^\\\\circ C$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Here,<br>${m_{ice{\\rm{ }}}} = 1\\,g,\\,{m_{steam{\\rm{ }}}} = 1\\,g\\,$<br>${L_{ice{\\rm{ }}}} = 80\\,cal\\,{g^{ - 1}}\\,$<br>${L_{steam{\\rm{ }}}} = 540\\,cal\\,{g^{ - 1}}$<br>${c_{water{\\rm{ }}}} = 1\\,cal\\,{g^{ - 1}}{c^{ - 1}}$<br>Heat required to convert ice at $0^\\circ C$ to water at $100^\\circ C$ is<br>${Q_1} = {m_{ice{\\rm{ }}}}{L_{ice{\\rm{ }}}} + {m_{ice{\\rm{ }}}}{S_{water{\\rm{ }}}}\\Delta T$<br>$ = (1\\,g)\\left( {80\\,cal\\,{g^{ - 1}}} \\right) + (1\\,g)\\left( {1\\,cal\\,{g^{ - 10}}{C^{ - 1}}} \\right)$<br>$\\left( {100^\\circ C - 0^\\circ C} \\right)$<br>$ = 80cal + 100cal = 180cal$<br>Heat released by steam at $100^\\circ C$ to condense into water at $100^\\circ C$ is<br>${Q_2} = {m_{Steam{\\rm{ }}}}{L_{Steam{\\rm{ }}}} = (1\\,g)\\left( {540\\,cal\\,{g^{ - 1}}} \\right) = 540\\,cal$<br>${\\rm{As}}\\,\\,\\,\\,{Q_1} < {Q_2}$<br> whole of the steam will not condense, so the temperature of the mixture is $100^\\circ C$.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37606,'physics',28,1372,3295,'A cyclic process \\(A B C D\\) is shown in the following \\(\\mathrm{P}-\\mathrm{V}\\) diagram. Which of the following curves represent the same process ?<div class="matching-question"></div>','[{"text": "", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'${\\rm{Path}}\\,AB$<br>The path $AB$ is isobaric<br>$P = $ constant $\\Rightarrow \\dfrac{V}{T}=$ constant<br>$V \\rightarrow$ increases $\\Rightarrow T \\rightarrow$ increases<br>${\\rm{Path}}\\,BC$<br>The path $BC$ represents isothermal process<br>$T = $ constant<br>$P \\rightarrow$ Decreasing $\\Rightarrow V \\rightarrow$ Increasing<br>${\\rm{Path}}{\\mkern 1mu} \\,CD$<br>The path $CD$ is isochoric<br>$V = $ constant $\\Rightarrow \\dfrac{P}{T}=$ constant<br>$P \\rightarrow$ Decreases $\\Rightarrow T \\rightarrow$ Decreases<br>The graph between $P$ and $T$ is a straight line passing through origin.<br>${\\rm{Path}}\\,DA$<br>The path $DA$ isothermal<br>$T = $ Constant, $P \\rightarrow$ increases $\\Rightarrow V \\rightarrow$ Decreases',NULL,'Medium','Graph/Figure',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/28_37606_question_1.png'),
(37607,'physics',26,1137,2725,'Two capillary tubes of the same length but different radii $$r_{1}$$ and $$r_{2}$$ are fitted in parallel to the bottom of a vessel. The pressure head is $$p$$. What should be the radius of a single tube that can replace the two tubes so that the rate of flow is same as before','[{"text": "$$r_{1}+r_{2}$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$\\\\dfrac{r_{1} r_{2}}{r_{1}+r_{2}}$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$\\\\dfrac{r_{1}+r_{2}}{2}$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "None of these", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'$$V=V_{1}+V_{2}$$<br>$ \\Rightarrow \\frac{{\\pi p{r^4}}}{{8\\eta l}} = \\frac{{pr_1^4}}{{8\\eta l}} + \\frac{{\\pi pr_2^4}}{{8\\eta l}}$<br>$ \\Rightarrow {r^4} = r_1^4 + r_2^4$<br>$\\therefore r = {\\left( {r_1^4 + r_2^4} \\right)^{1/4}}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37608,'physics',29,1300,3145,'A ballon contains $500\\,\\,{m^3}$ of helium at $27^\\circ \\,C$ and 1 atmosphere pressure. The volume of the helium at $-3^\\circ \\,C$ temperature and 0.5 atmosphere pressure will be','[{"text": "$500\\\\,\\\\,{m^3}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$700\\\\,\\\\,{m^3}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$900\\\\,\\\\,{m^3}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$1000\\\\,\\\\,{m^3}$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'$\\frac{{{P_1}{V_1}}}{{{T_1}}} = \\frac{{{P_2}{V_2}}}{{{T_2}}} \\Rightarrow \\frac{{1 \\times 500}}{{300}} = \\frac{{0.5 \\times {V_2}}}{{270}} \\Rightarrow {V_2} = 900 \\,{m^3}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37609,'physics',27,1381,3315,'Sunrays are allowed to fall on a long of diameter $20\\,cm$. They are then brought to focus on a calorimeter containing $20\\,g$ of ice. If the absorption by the lens is negligible, the time required to melt all the ice is (solar constant $=$ $1.9/cal/\\min /c{m^2}$ and $L = 80\\,cal/g$)','[{"text": "$6.4 \\\\mathrm{~min}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$3.2 \\\\mathrm{~min}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$7.2 \\\\mathrm{~min}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2.7 \\\\mathrm{~min}$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Heat required to melt whole ice is<br>$Q = mL = 20 \\times 80$<br>$ = 1600\\,cal$<br>Energy incident per minute is<br>$P = 1.9 \\times \\frac{\\pi }{4} \\times {d^2}$<br>$P = 1.9 \\times \\frac{\\pi }{4} \\times {(20)^2} = 597cal/\\min $<br>Therefore time required to melt whole ice is<br>$t = \\frac{Q}{P} \\approx 2.7\\,\\min $',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37610,'physics',28,1372,3297,'Which of the following graphs is not possible between heat capacity and temperature of an ideal gas?<div class="matching-question"></div>','[{"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'When temperature remains constant then the process is isothermal. In isothermal process $C=\\infty$. so graph (3) is not possible.',NULL,'Hard','Graph/Figure',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(48829,'biology',80,477,962,'At which percentage of the full sunlight light saturation occurs ?','[{"text": "10 per cent", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Less than 2%", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Less than 5%", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "0.15", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Light saturation occurs at 10 per cent of the full sunlight.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37611,'physics',26,1138,2727,'A metal wire of density $$\\rho$$ floats on water surface horizontally. If it is NOT to sink in water, then maximum radius of wire is proportional to ( where $T = $ surface tension of water, $g = $ gravitational acceleration)','[{"text": "$\\\\sqrt {\\\\frac{T}{{\\\\pi \\\\rho g}}} $", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\sqrt {\\\\frac{{\\\\pi \\\\rho g}}{T}} $", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{T}{{\\\\pi \\\\rho g}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{{\\\\pi \\\\rho g}}{T}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Given, density of metal wire $$=\\rho$$<br>Surface tension of water $ = T$<br>If $$l$$ is the length of the wire and $$F$$ the total force either side of the wire, then<br>$F = Tl\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\left( 1 \\right)$<br>Also, $\\quad F = mg\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\left( 2 \\right)$<br>From eqs.(1) and (2), we get<br>$$T l=m g$$<br>$$T l=v \\rho g\\left[\\because \\operatorname{density}(\\rho)=\\dfrac{m}{v}\\right]$$<br>$$T l=\\pi r^{2} l \\rho g$$<br>$$r^{2}=\\dfrac{T}{\\pi \\rho g} \\Rightarrow r=\\sqrt{\\dfrac{T}{\\pi \\rho g}}$$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2018,'MHTCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37612,'physics',29,1300,3145,'The change in the magnitude of the volume of an ideal gas when a small additional pressure $\\Delta P$ is applied at a constant temperature, is the same as the change when the temperature is reduced by a small quantity $\\Delta T$ at constant pressure. The intial temperature and pressure of the gas were $100 K$ and $2\\,\\,atm$ respectively. If $|\\Delta T|=C|\\Delta P|$, then value of $C$  in $K/atm$  is -','[{"text": "12", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "50", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "40", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "25", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'In first case,<br>From ideal gas equation,<br>$P V=n R T$<br>$P \\Delta V+V \\Delta P=0$ (As temperature is constant)<br>$\\Delta V =  - \\frac{{\\Delta P}}{P}V\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,(1)$<br>In second case, using ideal gas equation again<br>$P\\Delta V = nR\\Delta T$<br>$\\Delta V = \\frac{{nR\\Delta T}}{P}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,(2)$<br>Equating (1), and (2), we get<br>$\\dfrac{n R \\Delta T}{P}=-\\dfrac{\\Delta P}{P} V \\Rightarrow \\Delta T=\\Delta P \\dfrac{V}{n R}$<br>Given that $|\\Delta T|=C|\\Delta P|$, we have<br>$C = \\frac{V}{{nR}} = \\frac{T}{P} = \\frac{{100\\,K}}{{2\\,\\,atm}} = 50\\,K/atm$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37613,'physics',27,1382,3318,'Two slabs are of the thicknesses $d_{1}$ and $d_{2}$. Their thermal conductivities are ${K_1}$ and ${K_2}$ respectively. They are in series. The free ends of the combination of these two slabs are kept at temperatures $\\theta_{1}$ and $\\theta_{2}$. Assume $\\theta_{1}>\\theta_{2}$. The temperature $\\theta$ of their common junction is','[{"text": "$\\\\dfrac{K_{1} \\\\theta_{1}+K_{2} \\\\theta_{2}}{\\\\theta_{1}+\\\\theta_{2}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{K_{1} \\\\theta_{1} d_{1}+K_{2} \\\\theta_{2} d_{2}}{K_{1} d_{1}+K_{2} d_{2}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{K_{1} \\\\theta_{1} d_{2}+K_{2} \\\\theta_{2} d_{1}}{K_{1} d_{2}+K_{2} d_{1}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{K_{1} \\\\theta_{1}+K_{2} \\\\theta_{2}}{K_{1}+K_{2}}$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'<br><img src=\'/img/data/physics/27_37613_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' /> <br>For the first slab<br>Heat current, $i_{1}=\\dfrac{K_{1}\\left(\\theta_{1}-\\theta\\right) A}{d_{1}}$<br>For the second slab<br>Heat current, $i_{2}=\\dfrac{K_{2}\\left(\\theta-\\theta_{2}\\right) A}{d_{2}}$<br>As the slabs are connected in series same amount of heat current flows through the slabs. $\\therefore i_{1}=i_{2}$<br>$\\begin{aligned}& \\dfrac{K_{1}\\left(\\theta_{1}-\\theta\\right) A}{d_{1}}=\\dfrac{K_{2}\\left(\\theta-\\theta_{2}\\right) A}{d_{2}} \\\\& \\theta=\\dfrac{K_{1} \\theta_{1} d_{2}+K_{2} \\theta_{2} d_{1}}{K_{2} d_{1}+K_{1} d_{2}}\\end{aligned}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37614,'physics',26,1138,2728,'If the surface tension of a soap solution is $3 \\times {10^{ - 2}}\\,N/m$ then the work done in forming a soap film of  $20\\,\\,cm \\times 5\\,\\,cm$ will be','[{"text": "$6 \\\\times {10^{ - 2}}\\\\,J$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$6\\\\,J$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$6 \\\\times {10^{ - 4}}\\\\,J$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$6 \\\\times {10^{ - 3}}\\\\,J$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Given, surface tension, $T = 3 \\times {10^{ - 2}}\\,N/m$ and surface area of soap film,<br>$A = 20\\,cm \\times 5\\,cm$<br>$ = 100 \\times {10^{ - 4}}\\,{m^2}$<br>The work done in forming a soap film,<br>$W = 2(TA)$<br>$$=2\\left(3 \\times 10^{-2} \\times 100 \\times 10^{-4}\\right)$$<br>$ = 6 \\times {10^{ - 4}}\\,J$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2020,'MHTCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37615,'physics',28,1372,3298,'An ideal gas is initially at temperature \\(T\\) and Volume \\[V\\]. Its volume is increased by \\(\\Delta V\\) due to an increase in temperature \\(\\Delta T\\), pressure remaining constant. The quantity \\(\\delta=\\dfrac{\\Delta V}{V \\Delta T}\\) varies with temperature as<div class="matching-question"></div>','[{"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Given that $P = $ Constant<br>$\\Rightarrow \\dfrac{V}{T}=K=$ constant $\\Rightarrow \\Delta V=K \\Delta T$<br>$\\delta=\\dfrac{\\Delta V}{V \\Delta T}=\\dfrac{1}{V}(K)=\\dfrac{K}{V}=\\dfrac{1}{T}$<br>The graph between $\\delta  = \\frac{1}{T}$ and $T$ is hyperbolic.',NULL,'Hard','Graph/Figure',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37616,'physics',29,1301,3148,'1 mole of ${H_2}$  and 1 mole of $He$ at a pressure of ${P_0}$ and $2{P_0}$  respectively and a temperatures ${T_0}$ are kept in two different containers. The ratio of their rms velocities is ${c_{{H_2}}}:{c_{He}} = $','[{"text": "$1: 1$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\sqrt{10}: \\\\sqrt{3}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$5: 3$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2: \\\\sqrt{2}$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Their average translational K.E is $\\frac{3}{2}{k_B}T$.<br>${k_B} \\to $  Boltzmann\'s constant<br>$\\frac{1}{2}{m_{{H_2}}}c_{{H_2}}^2 = \\frac{1}{2}{m_{He}}c_{He}^2 = \\frac{3}{2}{k_B}T$<br>${c_{{H_2}}}:{c_{He}} = \\sqrt {{m_{He}}} :\\sqrt {{m_{{H_2}}}}  = \\sqrt 4 \\,\\,:\\sqrt 2  = 2:\\sqrt 2 $',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37617,'physics',27,1382,3318,'Three rods of same dimensions have thermal conductivities $3\\,K,2\\,K$ and $K$. They are arranged as shown, with their ends at $100^\\circ C,50^\\circ C$ and $0^\\circ C$. The temperature of their junction is','[{"text": "$75^\\\\circ C$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{{200^\\\\circ }}{3}C$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$40^\\\\circ C$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{{100^\\\\circ }}{3}C$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Suppose $\\theta$ be the temperature of junction. ${i_1},{i_2}$, and ${i_3}$ are heat currents. Then ${i_1} = {i_2} + {i_3}$<br><img src=\'/img/data/physics/27_37617_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' /> <br>${\\rm{or }}\\frac{{100 - 0}}{{(l/3KA)}} = \\frac{{0 - 50}}{{(l/KA)}} + \\frac{{\\theta  - 0}}{{(l/KA)}}{\\rm{ }}$<br>${\\rm{or }}3(100 - \\theta ) = 2(\\theta  - 50) + \\theta $<br>Solving we get $\\theta  = \\frac{{200^\\circ }}{3}C$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/27_37617_question_1.png'),
(47275,'biology',102,775,1680,'The organism A and B are example of:','[{"text": "Convergent evolution", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Divergent evolution", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Adaptive convergence", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both (1) and (3)", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Australian marsupials and placental mammals are suitable examples of adaptive radiation and convergent evolution',NULL,'Easy','MCQ type-3',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','biology/102_47275_question_1.png'),
(37619,'physics',26,1138,2728,'The surface energy of a liquid drop is $E$. It is sprayed into 1000 equal droplets. Then its surface energy becomes','[{"text": "$E$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "10 $E$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "100 $E$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "1000 $E$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Let $$r$$ be the radius of smaller drops and $$R$$ the radius of bigger drop. Then by equating volume we have<br>$$1000\\left(\\dfrac{4}{3} \\pi r^{3}\\right)=\\dfrac{4}{3} \\pi R^{3}$$<br>or $$r=\\dfrac{R}{10}$$<br>Surface energy $$\\propto$$ surface area<br>$E = 4\\pi {R^2}$<br>or $E = 1000\\left( {4\\pi {r^2}} \\right)$<br>$E = 10\\left( {4\\pi {R^2}} \\right)$<br>$ = 10\\,E$<br>As surface area has increased 10times, surface energy will also become ten times',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37620,'physics',29,1301,3148,'Calculate the rms speed of smoke particles each of mass $5 \\times {10^{ - 17}} kg$ in thier Brownian motion in air at NTP $\\left(k=1.38 \\times 10^{-23} J K^{-1}\\right)$','[{"text": "$1.5\\\\,\\\\,m{s^{ - 1}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1.5\\\\,\\\\,mm\\\\,{s^{ - 1}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1.5\\\\,\\\\,km{s^{ - 1}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1.5\\\\,cm{s^{ - 1}}$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'At NTP or STP<br>$T = 273 \\,K$<br>As,  $c{\\rm{ }} = \\sqrt {\\frac{{3kT}}{m}}  = \\sqrt {\\frac{{3 \\times 1.38 \\times {{10}^{ - 23}} \\times 273}}{{5 \\times {{10}^{ - 17}}}}} $<br>$ = 15 \\times {10^{ - 3}}m{s^{ - 1}} = 1.5\\,\\,cm{s^{ - 1}}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37621,'physics',27,1382,3318,'The coefficient of the thermal conductivity of copper is 9 times that of steel. In the compositecylindrical bar shown in the figure, what will be the temperature at the junction of copper and steel?','[{"text": "$25^\\\\circ C$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$33^\\\\circ C$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$75^\\\\circ C$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$67^\\\\circ C$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Let $T$ be the temperature of the junction. As rate of heat flow in both rods is same<br>${i_{cu}} = {i_{steel{\\rm{ }}}}$<br>$\\frac{{ - {K_{cu}}A(T - 100)}}{{18\\,cm}} = \\frac{{ - {K_{steel{\\rm{ }}}}A(0 - T)}}{{6\\,cm}}$<br>$\\frac{{ - 9{K_{steel{\\rm{ }}}}A(T - 100)}}{{18\\,cm}} = \\frac{{ - {K_{steel{\\rm{ }}}}A(0 - T)}}{{6\\,cm}}$<br>$T = 75^\\circ C$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2008,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/27_37621_question_1.png'),
(37622,'physics',28,1372,3293,'Consider a process shown in the figure. During this process the work done by the system<div class="matching-question"></div>','[{"text": "Continuously decreases", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Continuously increases", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "First increases, then decreases", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "first decreases, then increases", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'As the volume is continuously increasing and the work of expansion is always positive, so the work done by the system continuously increases.',NULL,'Medium','Graph/Figure',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/28_37622_question_1.png'),
(37623,'physics',26,1138,2728,'A certain number of spherical drops of a liquid of radius $$r$$ coalesce to form a single drop of radius $$R$$ and volume $$V$$. If $$T$$ is the surface tension of the liquid, then an energy of','[{"text": "$4VT\\\\left( {\\\\frac{1}{r} - \\\\frac{1}{R}} \\\\right)$ is released", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$3VT\\\\left( {\\\\frac{1}{r} - \\\\frac{1}{R}} \\\\right)$ is released", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$3VT\\\\left( {\\\\frac{1}{r} + \\\\frac{1}{R}} \\\\right)$ is absorbed", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Energy is neither released nor absorbed", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Let $$R$$ is the radius of the bigger drop and $$N$$ drops are combined to form the bigger drop.<br>$$N \\dfrac{4}{3} \\pi r^{3}=\\dfrac{4}{3} \\pi R^{3}$$<br>$N{r^3} = {R^3}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,(1)$<br>The energy released<br>$$E=T\\left[A_{i}-A_{f}\\right]$$<br>$$E=T\\left[N 4 \\pi r^{2}-4 \\pi R^{2}\\right]$$<br>$$=4 \\pi R^{2} T\\left[\\dfrac{N r^{2}}{R^{2}}-1\\right]$$<br>$ = 3\\left( {\\frac{4}{3}\\pi {R^3}} \\right)T\\left[ {\\frac{{N{r^2}}}{{{R^3}}} - \\frac{1}{R}} \\right]$<br>$$=3 V T\\left[\\dfrac{1}{r}-\\dfrac{1}{R}\\right]$$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37624,'physics',29,1303,3151,'The temperature of argon, kept in a vessel, is raised by  $1^\\circ C$ at a constant volume. The total heat supplied to the gas is a combination of translational and rotational energies. Their respective shares are','[{"text": "$40 \\\\%$ and $60 \\\\%$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$60 \\\\%$ and $40 \\\\%$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$505$ and  $50{\\\\text{ }}\\\\% $", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$100 \\\\%$ and $0 \\\\%$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Argon is a monoatomic gas so it has only translational energy.<br>Translational : Rotational $=100 \\%: 0 \\%$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37625,'physics',27,1382,3318,'In a steady state, the temperature at the end $A$ and $B$ of $20\\,cm$ long rod $AB$  are $100^\\circ C$ and $0^\\circ C$. The temperature of a point $9\\,cm$ from $A$ is','[{"text": "$45^\\\\circ C$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$55^\\\\circ C$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$5^\\\\circ C$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$65^\\\\circ C$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'<br>$H = \\frac{{100 - 0}}{{{R_{AB}}}} = {\\text{Heat}}\\,{\\text{current}} = \\frac{{100}}{{{R_{AB}}}}$<br><img src=\'/img/data/physics/27_37625_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' /> <br>$100 - {\\theta _C} = H \\cdot {R_{AC}} = \\frac{{100}}{{{R_{AB}}}} \\cdot {R_{AC}}$<br>$ = \\frac{{100 \\times 9}}{{20}} = 45$<br>$\\therefore {\\theta _C} = 55^\\circ C$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37626,'physics',28,1372,3293,'The PV diagram of \\[4{\\rm{ }}gm\\] of \\[He\\] gas for a certain process \\[A\\] to \\[B\\] is shown in the figure. What is the heat given to the gas during the process \\[A\\] to \\[B\\] ?<div class="matching-question"></div>','[{"text": "$4{P_0}\\\\,\\\\,{V_0}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$6\\\\,{P_0}\\\\,{V_0}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$4.5\\\\,{P_0}\\\\,{V_0}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2{P_0}\\\\,{V_0}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Number of moles $n=\\dfrac{4 g}{4 g}=1$<br>$\\begin{aligned}& W_{A B}=P_{0} V_{0}+\\dfrac{1}{2} P_{0} V_{0}=\\dfrac{3 P_{0} V_{0}}{2} \\\\& \\Delta U_{A B}=n c_{v} \\Delta T=\\dfrac{3}{2}\\left(2 P_{0} 2 V_{0}-P_{0} V_{0}\\right) \\\\& \\Delta U_{A B}=\\dfrac{9}{2} P_{0} V_{0} \\\\& \\Delta Q=W+\\Delta U_{A B}=6 P_{0} V_{0}\\end{aligned}$',NULL,'Medium','Graph/Figure',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/28_37626_question_1.png'),
(47789,'biology',10,269,527,'A common characteristic feature of plant sieve tube cells and most mammalian erythrocytes is','[{"text": "Absence of nucleus and haemoglobin", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Absence of nucleus and cell wall", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Absence of mitochondria", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Absence of nucleus", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Plant sieve tube cells and mammalian erythrocytes are anucleated',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37627,'physics',26,1138,2730,'In air, a charged soap bubble of radius \' $$R$$ \' breaks into 27 small soap bubbles of equal radius \' $$r$$ \'. Then the ratio of mechanical force acting per unit area of big soap bubble to that of a small soap bubble is','[{"text": "$$\\\\dfrac{1}{81}$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$\\\\dfrac{3}{1}$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$\\\\dfrac{1}{3}$$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$$\\\\dfrac{9}{1}$$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Pressure inside a soap bubble of radius $$R$$ is given by $$P=\\dfrac{4 T}{R}$$<br>where $T = $ surface tension<br>and $R = $ radius of the drop<br>Pressure inside a soap bubble, $$P=\\dfrac{4 T}{R}$$<br>If a bubble is break into 27 small soap bubbles then the volume of a single bubble of radius $$R$$ and the combined volume of 27 bubbles of radius $$r$$ would be constant.<br>$27x$ volume of small bubbles $$=$$ volume of larger bubble<br>$$\\Rightarrow 27\\left(\\dfrac{4}{3} \\pi r^{3}\\right)=\\dfrac{4}{3} \\pi R^{3}$$<br>$$\\Rightarrow 27 r^{3}=R^{3}$$<br>$ \\Rightarrow r = \\frac{R}{3}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\left( 1 \\right)$<br>Now, the pressure inside smaller soap bubble<br>$$P_{\\text {small }}=\\dfrac{4 T}{r}=\\dfrac{12 T}{R}$$ (using the relation)<br>and similarly $$P_{\\text {large }}=\\dfrac{4 t}{R}$$<br>Therefore, ratio of pressure of the smaller and larger soap bubble is given as,<br>$$\\dfrac{P_{\\text {larger }}}{P_{\\text {small }}}=\\dfrac{4 T}{R} \\times \\dfrac{R}{12 T}=\\dfrac{1}{3}$$<br>$$P_{\\text {larger }}: P_{\\text {small }}=1: 3$$<br>Hence, the ratio of mechanical force acting per unit area of big soap bubble to that of small bubble is $$1: 3$$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2019,'MHTCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37628,'physics',29,1301,3148,'$N$  molecules, each of mass $m$ of gas $A$ and $2N$ molecules each of mass $2m$ of gas $B$ are contained in the same vessel which is maintained at temperature $T$. The mean square velocity of the molecules of $B$ type is denoted \' $v{ }^{2}$ \' and the mean square velocity of $A$ type is denoted by $\\omega^{2}$ the value $\\omega^{2} / v^{2}$ is,','[{"text": "1", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "2", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$2 / 3$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1 / 2$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'$v_{B}=v=\\sqrt{\\dfrac{3 k T}{2 m}}, \\quad v_{A}=\\omega=\\sqrt{\\dfrac{3 k T}{m}}$<br>$\\dfrac{\\omega^{2}}{v^{2}}=2$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37629,'physics',27,1382,3317,'A slab of stone of area of $0.36\\,{m^2}$ and thickness $0.1\\,m$ is exposed on the lower surface to steam at $100^\\circ C$. A block of ice at $0^\\circ C$ rests on the upper surface of the slab. In one hour $4.8\\,kg$ of ice is melted. The thermal conductivity of slab is (Given latent heat of fusion of ice $ = 3.36 \\times {10^5}J\\,k{g^{ - 1}}$ )','[{"text": "$1.24\\\\,J/m/s/\\\\,^\\\\circ C$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$1.29\\\\,J/m/s/^\\\\circ C$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2.05\\\\,J/m/s/^\\\\circ C$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1.02\\\\,J/m/s/^\\\\circ C$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Rate of heat flow through the slab is $\\dfrac{Q}{t}=\\dfrac{K A}{L}\\left(T_{1}-T_{2}\\right)$<br>Rate of heat taken by ice $Q=\\dfrac{K A}{L}\\left(T_{1}-T_{2}\\right) t$<br>$Q=m L_{f}$<br>$\\dfrac{K A}{L}\\left(T_{1}-T_{2}\\right) t=m L_{f}$<br>$K=\\dfrac{m L_{f} L}{A\\left(T_{1}-T_{2}\\right) t} \\Rightarrow K=\\dfrac{4.8 \\times 3.36 \\times 10^{5} \\times 0.1}{0.36 \\times 100 \\times 3600}$<br>$ = \\frac{{4.8 \\times 3.36}}{{0.36 \\times 36}} = 1.24\\,J/m/s\\,^\\circ C$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37630,'physics',28,1372,3293,'\\[P - V\\] plots for two gases during adiabatic process are shown in the figure. Plots 1 and 2 should correspond respectively, to<div class="matching-question"></div>','[{"text": "$He$ and ${N_2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${N_2}$ and $He$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$He$ and $Ar$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${O_2}$ and ${N_2}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'For an adiabatic process, $P{V^\\gamma } = $ constant,<br>Differentiating, we have<br>$\\gamma P{V^{\\gamma  - 1}} + \\frac{{dP}}{{dV}}{V^\\gamma } = 0$ or<br>$\\frac{{dP}}{{dV}} =  - \\frac{{\\gamma P}}{V}$<br>Thus, the slope of $P - V$ curve is proportional to $\\gamma $. Now, for a diatomic gas, $\\gamma ( = 7/5)$  is less then that for a monoatomic gas for which $\\gamma  = 5/3$. Therefore, the slope of the $P - V$ curve is less for a diatomic gas than for a monoatomic gas. Hence curve 1 corresponds to diatomic gas ans curve 2 to monoatomic gas. Thus the correct choice is (2).',NULL,'Medium','Graph/Figure',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/28_37630_question_1.png'),
(37631,'physics',26,1138,2730,'Two soap bubbles of radii $$r_{1}$$ and $$r_{2}$$ equal to $4\\,cm$ and $5\\,cm$ are touching each other over a common surface ${S_1}\\,{S_2}$ (shown in figure ). Its radius will be','[{"text": "$4\\\\,cm$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$20\\\\,cm$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$5\\\\,cm$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$4.5\\\\,cm$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'<br>$r = \\frac{{{r_1}{r_2}}}{{{r_1} - {r_2}}} = \\frac{{5 \\times 4}}{{5 - 4}} = 20\\,cm$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/26_37631_question_1.png'),
(37632,'physics',29,1305,3155,'An ideal gas enclosed in a cylinder at pressure of $1\\,atm$ and temperature, $300 K$. The mean time between two successive collisions is $2 \\times {10^{ - 8}} s$. If the pressure is doubled and temperature is increased to $400 K$, the mean time between two successive collisions will be close to :','[{"text": "$2 \\\\times {10^{ - 7}} s$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1.15 \\\\times {10^{ - 8}} s$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$0.5 \\\\times {10^{ - 8}} s$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$3 \\\\times {10^{ - 6}}s$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'The mean time between two successive collisions is $t=\\dfrac{\\lambda}{v}=\\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{2} \\pi d^{2} n v}$<br>$\\begin{aligned}& \\text { where } \\quad n_{v}=\\dfrac{n N_{A}}{v}=\\dfrac{n N_{A}}{\\dfrac{n R T}{P}}=\\dfrac{N_{A} P}{R T} \\\\& \\Rightarrow \\quad t=\\dfrac{R T}{\\sqrt{2} \\pi d^{2} N_{A} P v} \\\\& \\Rightarrow \\quad t \\propto \\dfrac{\\sqrt{T}}{P} \\quad(\\text { velocity } v \\propto \\sqrt{T}) \\\\& \\dfrac{t_{1}}{t_{2}}=\\sqrt{\\dfrac{T_{1}}{T_{2}}} \\dfrac{P_{2}}{P_{1}}=\\sqrt{\\dfrac{300}{400}} \\times \\dfrac{2}{1}=\\sqrt{3} \\\\& \\Rightarrow \\quad t_{2}=\\dfrac{t_{1}}{\\sqrt{3}}=1.15 \\times 10^{-8} \\mathrm{~s}\\end{aligned}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37633,'physics',27,1382,3323,'The surface temperature of the sun which has maximum energy emission at $500\\,nm$ is $6000\\,K$.The temperature of a star which has maximum energy emission at $400\\,nm$ will be','[{"text": "$8500\\\\,K$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$4500\\\\,K$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$7500\\\\,K$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$6500\\\\,K$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'By Wien\'s displacement law, ${\\lambda _m}T = $constant<br>$\\Rightarrow \\lambda_{1} T_{1}=\\lambda_{2} T_{2}$<br>$ \\Rightarrow {T_2} = \\frac{{500 \\times 6000}}{{400}} = 7500\\,K$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37652,'physics',29,1301,3147,'Energy of all molecules of a monoatomic gas having a volume $V$ and pressure $P$ is $\\dfrac{3}{2} P V$. The total translational kinetic energy of all molecules of a diatomic gas as the same volume and pressure is','[{"text": "$\\\\dfrac{1}{2} P V$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{3}{2} P V$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{5}{2} P V$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$3 P V$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Translational kinetic energy of all molecules of diatomic gas will also be $\\dfrac{3}{2} P V$, because all the gases have same translational (degree of freedom 3).',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37634,'physics',28,1372,3294,'The work done by the gas in the cyclic process is<div class="matching-question"></div>','[{"text": "$ - 75\\\\,\\\\pi \\\\,kJ$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$ - 50\\\\,\\\\pi \\\\,kJ$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$ - 40\\\\,\\\\pi \\\\,kJ$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$ - 20\\\\,\\\\pi \\\\,kJ$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'$\\left|W_{\\text {cycle }}\\right|=$ Area of the cycle $=\\pi r^{2}=\\pi r_{V} r_{P}$<br>Pressure radius $\\left( {{r_P}} \\right) = \\frac{{(20 - 10)}}{2}k\\,Pa = 5k\\,Pa$<br>Volume radius $\\left( {{r_V}} \\right) = \\frac{{(40 - 10)}}{2}{m^3} = 15{m^3}$<br>$W_{\\text {cycle }}=-75 \\pi K J$<br>$W=(+) v e$ (clock wise direction when $P$ is on $y - $ axis)<br>$W=(-)$ ve (clock wise direction when $P$ is on $x$ - axis)',NULL,'Hard','Graph/Figure',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/28_37634_question_1.png'),
(37635,'physics',26,1138,2731,'Two capillary tubes of different diameters are dipped in water. The rise of water is','[{"text": "the same in both tubes", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "greater in the tube of larger diameter", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "greater in the tube of smaller diameter", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "independent of the diameter of the tube", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'The rise of water in a capillary tube is<br>$h = \\frac{{2\\,T\\cos \\theta }}{{r\\rho g}}$<br>$h \\propto \\frac{1}{r}$<br>Hence, the rise of water is greater for the tube of smaller diameter.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2011,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37636,'physics',29,1303,3152,'The value of molar specific heat at constant pressure for one mole of triatomic gas (triangular arrangement) at temperature $T\\,K$  is $(R=$ universal gas constant)','[{"text": "$\\\\dfrac{2}{7} R$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$3 R$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$4 R$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{5}{2} R$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Here, the degrees of freedom $F=6$,<br>$C_{P}=\\left(1+\\dfrac{F}{2}\\right) R=\\left(1+\\dfrac{6}{2}\\right) R=4 R$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37637,'physics',27,1382,3323,'If a black body emits 0.5 joules of energy per second when it is at $27^\\circ C$, then the amount of energy emitted by it when it is at $627^\\circ C$ will be','[{"text": "$13.5\\\\,J$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$135\\\\,J$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$40.5\\\\,J$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$162\\\\,J$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'The energy emitted by a black body per second is given by $U = \\sigma {T^4}$.<br>$\\therefore \\frac{{{U_2}}}{{{U_1}}} = \\frac{{{{(273 + 627)}^4}}}{{{{(273 + 27)}^4}}} = \\frac{{{{(900)}^4}}}{{{{(300)}^4}}}$<br>$ \\Rightarrow {U_2} = 0.5 \\times \\frac{{{{(900)}^4}}}{{{{(300)}^4}}} = 0.5 \\times 81 = 40.5\\,J$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2008,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37638,'physics',28,1372,3294,'In the following indicator diagram, the net amount of work done will be :<div class="matching-question"></div>','[{"text": "positive", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Negative", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "zero", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Infinity", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'The clockwise cycle area is more than the area of anticlockwise cycle.',NULL,'Hard','Graph/Figure',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/28_37638_question_1.png'),
(37639,'physics',26,1138,2731,'When one end of the capillary is dipped in water, the height of water column is \' $$h$$ \'. The upward force of 105 dyne due to surface tension is balanced by the force due to the weight of water column. The inner circumference of the capillary is ( surface tension of water \\[ = 7 \\times {10^{ - 2}}\\;N/m\\] )<div class="matching-question"></div>','[{"text": "$1.5\\\\,cm$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$2\\\\,cm$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2.5\\\\,cm$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$3\\\\,cm$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Given, $F = 105$ dyne $ = 105 \\times {10^{ - 5}}\\,N$<br>$T = 7 \\times {10^{ - 2}}\\,N/m$<br>As we know, circumfernce of the capillary $x$ surface tension $$=$$ upward force<br>$$2 \\pi r T=F$$<br>$2\\pi rT = \\frac{F}{T} = \\frac{{105 \\times {{10}^{ - 5}}}}{{7 \\times {{10}^{ - 2}}}} = 15 \\times {10^{ - 3}}\\,m$<br>$ = 1.5 \\times {10^{ - 2}}\\,m = 1.5\\,cm$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37640,'physics',29,1303,3152,'For a gas if ratio of specific heats at constant pressure and volume is $\\gamma$ then value of degrees of freedom is','[{"text": "$\\\\dfrac{9}{2}(\\\\gamma-1)$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{2}{\\\\gamma-1}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{3 \\\\gamma-1}{2 \\\\gamma-1}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{25}{2}(\\\\gamma-1)$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'$\\gamma=1+\\dfrac{2}{f}, \\Rightarrow \\gamma-1=\\dfrac{2}{f} \\Rightarrow \\dfrac{f}{2}=\\dfrac{1}{\\gamma-1}$<br>$\\Rightarrow f=\\dfrac{2}{\\gamma-1}$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37641,'physics',27,1382,3321,'Heat energy is incident on the surface at the rate of $1000\\,J/\\min $. If coefficient of absorption is 0.8 and coefficient of reflection is 0.1, then heat energy transmitted by the surface in $5\\,{\\rm{min}}$ is','[{"text": "$100\\\\,J$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$500\\\\,J$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$700\\\\,J$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$900\\\\,J$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Given that, rate of incident energy $\\left( {\\mathop {{\\rm{ }}E}\\limits^. } \\right) = 1000J/\\min $<br>We know that,<br>$1 = a + r + t$<br>Here, coefficient of absorption $\\left( a \\right) = 0.8$<br>Coeficient of reflection  $\\left( r \\right) = 0.1$, and coefficient of transmission $ = t$<br>$1 = 0.8 + 0.1 + t$<br>$ \\Rightarrow t = 1 - 0.9 = 0.1$<br>The amount of transmitted heat energy in 5 min<br>${Q_t} = E \\times t \\times \\left( {time} \\right) = 1000 \\times 0.1 \\times 5 = 500J$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2018,'MHTCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37642,'physics',28,1372,3294,'The above \\[P - V\\] diagram represents the thermodynamic cycle of an engine, operating with an ideal monoatomic gas. The amount of heat, extracted from the source in a single cycle is :<div class="matching-question"></div>','[{"text": "$P_{0} V_{0}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\left(\\\\dfrac{13}{2}\\\\right) P_{0} V_{0}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\left(\\\\dfrac{11}{2}\\\\right) P_{0} V_{0}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$4 P_{0} V_{0}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Consider the cyclic process $A B C D A$<br><img src=\'/img/data/physics/28_37642_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />   <br>${\\rm{Path}}\\,AB$<br>$\\begin{aligned}& \\Delta U=\\dfrac{P_{2} V_{2}-P_{1} V_{1}}{\\gamma-1}=\\dfrac{2 P_{0} V_{0}-P_{0} V_{0}}{\\dfrac{5}{3}-1} \\\\& \\Delta U=\\dfrac{3 P_{0} V_{0}}{2}, W=0 \\\\& \\Delta Q_{A B}=\\Delta U+W=\\dfrac{3 P_{0} V_{0}}{2}\\end{aligned}$<br>${\\rm{Path}}\\,BC$<br>$\\begin{aligned}& \\Delta U=\\dfrac{4 P_{0} V_{0}-2 P_{0} V_{0}}{\\dfrac{2}{3}}=3 P_{0} V_{0} \\\\& W=2 P_{0}\\left(2 V_{0}-V_{0}\\right)=2 P_{0} V_{0} \\\\& \\Delta Q_{B C}=5 P_{0} V_{0}\\end{aligned}$<br>${\\rm{Path}}{\\mkern 1mu} \\,CD$<br>$\\Delta U=\\dfrac{2 P_{0} V_{0}-4 P_{0} V_{0}}{\\dfrac{2}{3}}=-3 P_{0} V_{0}$<br>$W = 0\\,\\,\\Delta {Q_{CD}} =  - 3{P_0}{V_0}$<br>${\\rm{Path}}\\,DA$<br>$\\begin{aligned}& \\Delta U=\\dfrac{P_{0} V_{0}-2 P_{0} V_{0}}{\\dfrac{2}{3}}=\\dfrac{-3 P_{0} V_{0}}{2} \\\\& W=-P_{0} V_{0} \\\\& \\Delta Q_{D A}=\\dfrac{-5 P_{0} V_{0}}{2}\\end{aligned}$<br>In the paths $AB$ and $BC$ gas is absorbing the heat.<br>$\\Delta Q_{\\text {absorbed }}=\\dfrac{3 P_{0} V_{0}}{2}+5 P_{0} V_{0}=\\dfrac{13 P_{0} V_{0}}{2}$',NULL,'Hard','Graph/Figure',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/28_37642_question_1.png'),
(37643,'physics',26,1138,2731,'Water rises in a capillary tube to a certain height such that the upward force due to surface tension is balanced by $75 \\times {10^{ - 4}}\\,N$ force due to the weight of the liquid. If the surface tension of water is $6 \\times {10^{ - 2}}\\,N/m$, the inner circumference of the capillary must be','[{"text": "$1.25 \\\\times {10^{ - 2}}\\\\,m$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$0.50 \\\\times {10^{ - 2}}\\\\,m$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$6.50 \\\\times {10^{ - 2}}\\\\,m$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$12.50 \\\\times {10^{ - 2}}\\\\,m$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'$$6 \\times 10^{-2} \\times$$ Circumference $$=$$ force<br>$$\\therefore$$ circumference $$=\\dfrac{75 \\times 10^{4}}{6 \\times 10^{2}}$$<br>$ = 12.50 \\times {10^{ - 2}}\\,m$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37644,'physics',29,1303,3152,'One mole of ideal monoatomic gas $(\\gamma=5 / 3)$ is mixed with one mole of diatomic gas $(\\gamma=7 / 5)$. What is $\\gamma$ for the mixture? $\\gamma$ denotes the ratio of specific heat at constant pressure, to that at constant volume','[{"text": "$3 / 2$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$35 / 23$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$23 / 15$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$4 / 3$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'${\\gamma _{mix{\\rm{ }}}} = \\frac{{{C_{{P_{mix{\\rm{ }}}}}}}}{{{C_{{V_{mix{\\rm{ }}}}}}}} = \\frac{{\\frac{{{n_1}{C_{{V_1}}} + {n_2}{C_{{V_2}}}}}{{{n_1} + {n_2}}}}}{{\\frac{{{n_1}{C_{{P_1}}} + {n_2}{C_{{P_2}}}}}{{{n_1} + {n_2}}}}}$<br>Where  ${C_V} = \\frac{R}{{\\gamma  - 1}}{\\rm{ }}\\& {\\rm{ }}{C_P} = \\frac{{\\gamma R}}{{\\gamma  - 1}}$<br>${\\gamma _{mix{\\rm{ }}}} = \\frac{{\\frac{{{n_1}{\\gamma _1}}}{{{\\gamma _1} - 1}} + \\frac{{{n_2}{\\gamma _2}}}{{{\\gamma _2} - 1}}}}{{\\frac{{{n_1}}}{{{\\gamma _1} - 1}} + \\frac{{{n_2}}}{{{\\gamma _2} - 1}}}}$<br>$ = \\frac{{\\frac{{1 \\times \\frac{5}{3}}}{{\\left( {\\frac{5}{3} - 1} \\right)}} \\times \\frac{{1 \\times \\frac{7}{5}}}{{\\left( {\\frac{7}{5} - 1} \\right)}}}}{{\\frac{1}{{\\left( {\\frac{5}{3} - 1} \\right)}} + \\frac{1}{{\\left( {\\frac{7}{5} - 1} \\right)}}}} = \\frac{3}{2} = 1.5$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37645,'physics',27,1382,3323,'A sphere, a cube and a thin circular plate all of same material and same mass initially heated to same high temperature are allowed to cool down under similar conditions. Then the','[{"text": "Plate will cool the fastest and sphere the slowest", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Cube will cool the fastest and plate the slowest", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Plate will cool the fastest and cube the slowest", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Sphere will cool the fastest and cube the slowest.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'The rate of heat transfer is proportional to surface area. For a given fixed volume of material, the plate has the largest surface area and the sphere has the smallest surface area. So the circular plate will cool fastest and sphere the slowest.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2020,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37646,'physics',28,1372,3295,'An ideal gas undergoes a thermodynamic cycle as shown in the figure. Which of the following graph represents the same cycle?<div class="matching-question"></div>','[{"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'${\\rm{Path}}\\,AB$<br>The path $AB$ passes through the origin $V=m T$ ($\\mathrm{m}$- slope) $ \\Rightarrow P = $ constant <br>$V \\rightarrow$ increases, $\\mathrm{T}$ - increases.<br>${\\rm{Path}}\\,BC$<br>The path $BC$ is isochoric and hence $V = $ constant $ \\Rightarrow P = mT$ ($m$ - constant )<br>$T$ - decreases $ \\Rightarrow P$ - decreases<br>${\\rm{Path}}{\\mkern 1mu} \\,CA$<br>The path $CA$ is isothermal<br>$PV = $ constant<br>$V$ - decreases $ \\Rightarrow P$ - increases',NULL,'Hard','Graph/Figure',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/28_37646_question_1.png'),
(37647,'physics',26,1133,2705,'The pressure at the bottom of a liquid tank is not proportional to the','[{"text": "acceleration due to gravity", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "density of the liquid", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "height of the liquid", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "area of the liquid surface", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'The pressure at the bottom of a liquid tank is, $$P=P_{\\text {atm }}+\\rho g h$$<br>Thus, it is independent of the area of the liquid surface.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2018,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37648,'physics',29,1303,3152,'If $C_{P}$ and $C_{V}$ denote the specific heats of nitrogen per unit mass at constant pressure and constant volume respectively, then','[{"text": "$C_{P}-C_{V}=28 R$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$C_{P}-C_{V}=R / 28$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$C_{P}-C_{V}=R / 14$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$C_{P}-C_{V}=R$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Molar specific heat $C^{\\prime}=\\dfrac{\\Delta Q}{n \\Delta T}$<br>Specific heat $C=\\dfrac{\\Delta Q}{m \\Delta T}$<br>$\\begin{aligned}& \\dfrac{C^{\\prime}}{C}=\\dfrac{m}{n}=M(\\text { Molecular weight }) \\\\& C=\\dfrac{C^{\\prime}}{M} \\\\& C_{P}^{\\prime}-C_{V}^{1}=R \\\\& M C_{P}-M C_{V}=R \\Rightarrow C_{P}-C_{V}=\\dfrac{R}{M}\\end{aligned}$<br>For nitrogen $M=28$<br>$C_{P}-C_{V}=\\dfrac{R}{28}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37649,'physics',27,1382,3323,'Two spherical black bodies of radius $r_{1}$ and $r_{2}$ with surface temperature $T_{1}$ and $T_{2}$ respectively, radiate same power, then $r_{1}: r_{2}$ is','[{"text": "$\\\\left(\\\\dfrac{T_{2}}{T_{1}}\\\\right)^{4}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\left(\\\\dfrac{T_{2}}{T_{1}}\\\\right)^{2}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\left(\\\\dfrac{T_{1}}{T_{2}}\\\\right)^{2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\left(\\\\dfrac{T_{1}}{T_{2}}\\\\right)^{4}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'The power radiated by the black body<br>$E=\\sigma A T^{4}$<br>It is given that, both spherical bodies radiate same power<br>$\\therefore E_{1}=E_{2}$<br>$T_{1}^{4} r_{1}^{2}=T_{2}^{4} r_{2}^{2}\\left(\\because\\right.$ for spherical body, $\\left.A=4 \\pi r^{2}\\right)$<br>$\\Rightarrow \\dfrac{r_{1}}{r_{2}}=\\left(\\dfrac{T_{2}}{T_{1}}\\right)^{2}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2020,'MHTCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37650,'physics',28,1371,3283,'A cylinder with a movable piston contains 3 moles of hydrogen at standard temperature and pressure. The walls of the cylinder are made of heat insulator, and the piston is insulated by having a pile of sand on it By what factor does the pressure of the gas increase, if the gas is compressed to half its original volume?','[{"text": "1.40", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "1.60", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "2.64", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "1.94", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Let initial volume of the gas in the cylinder be $V$.<br>$\\therefore \\quad V_{1}=V$<br>and $V_{2}=V / 2$<br>As hydrogen is a diatomic gas<br>$\\therefore \\quad \\gamma=\\dfrac{7}{5}=1.4$<br>For an adiabatic change, $p_{1} V_{1}^{\\gamma}=p_{2} V_{2}^{\\gamma}$<br>$\\therefore \\dfrac{p_{2}}{p_{1}}=\\left(\\dfrac{V_{1}}{V_{2}}\\right)^{\\gamma}=(2)^{1.4}=2.64$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,'NCERT Exemplar','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37651,'physics',26,1133,2708,'Hydraulic lift works on the basis of','[{"text": "Stoke\'s law", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Bernoulli\'s law", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Toricelli\'s law", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Pascal\'s law", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Hydraulic lift is one of the applications of Pascal\'s law.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2017,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37653,'physics',27,1382,3322,'The wavelength for maximum energy emission by a star is $289.8\\,nm$. Then intensity of radiation for the star is (Given : Stefan\'s constant $ = 5.67 \\times {10^{ - 8}}W{m^{ - 2}}\\,{K^{ - 4}}$, Wien\'s constant, $b = 2898\\mu \\,mK$','[{"text": "$5.67 \\\\times {10^{ - 12}}W{m^{ - 2}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$10.67 \\\\times {10^{14}}W{m^{ - 2}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$5.67 \\\\times {10^8}W{m^{ - 2}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$10.67 \\\\times {10^7}W{m^{ - 2}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Given, wavelength for maximum energy emission<br>${\\lambda _m} = 289.8\\,nm = 2898 \\times {10^{ - 10}}\\,m$<br>Stefan\'s constant $\\sigma  = 5.67 \\times {10^{ - 8}}W{m^{ - 2}}\\,{K^{ - 4}}$<br>Wien\'s constant $b = 2898\\mu mK = 2898 \\times {10^{ - 6}}mK$ According to Wien\'s displacement law, $\\lambda_{m}$ is given by<br>${\\lambda _m} = \\frac{b}{T} \\Rightarrow T = \\frac{b}{{{\\lambda _m}}}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\left( 1 \\right)$<br>Substituting given values in eq.(1), we get<br>$T = \\frac{{2898 \\times {{10}^{ - 6}}}}{{2898 \\times {{10}^{ - 10}}}} = {10^4}\\,K\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\left( 2 \\right)$<br>According to Stefan\'s law, the energy radiated from a source is given by<br>$E = \\sigma Ae{T^4}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\left( 3 \\right)$<br>Where, $A = $ area of source<br>$\\mathrm{e}=\\text { emissivity }$<br>The intensity of radiation emitted<br>$I=\\dfrac{E}{A}=\\sigma e T^{4} \\quad \\text { [from eq.(3)] }$<br>Assuming the star to be a blackbody radiator, $e = 1$.<br>$ \\Rightarrow I = \\sigma {T^4}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\left( 4 \\right)$<br>Substituting the value of $T$ from eq.(2) in eq.(4) we get,<br>$I = \\sigma {\\left( {{{10}^4}} \\right)^4} = 5.67 \\times {10^{ - 8}} \\times {10^{16}} = 5.67 \\times {10^8}W{m^{ - 2}}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2019,'MHTCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37654,'physics',28,1372,3294,'An ideal gas undergoes cyclic process \\(A B C D A\\) as shown in given \\[P - V\\] diagram. The amount of work done by the gas is<div class="matching-question"></div>','[{"text": "$6\\\\,{p_0}{V_0}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$ - 2\\\\,{p_0}{V_0}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$ + 2\\\\,{p_0}{V_0}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$ + 4\\\\,{p_0}{V_0}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Work done by the gas is equal to area of rectangle $A B C D A,=A B \\times B C=\\left(2 V_{0}\\right) p_{0}=2 p_{0} V_{0}$<br>For anticlockwise direction work is (-)ve work<br>$\\therefore$ Work done by the gas $=-2 p_{0} V_{0}$',NULL,'Hard','Graph/Figure',NULL,'NCERT Exemplar','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/28_37654_question_1.png'),
(37655,'physics',26,1133,2705,'A metal sphere connected by a string is dipped in a liqiud of density $$\\rho$$ as shown in figure. The pressure at the bottom of the vessel will be, $\\left( {{\\rho _o} = } \\right.$ atmospheric pressure)','[{"text": "$p = {p_0} + \\\\rho gh$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$p > {p_0} + \\\\rho gh$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$p < {p_0} + \\\\rho gh$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${p_0}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Upthrust on sphere from the liqiud makes equal and opposite pair of forces. hence there will be no effect on the pressure at bottom of vessel. Or $$\\mathrm{p}=\\mathrm{p}_{0}+\\rho g h$$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/26_37655_question_1.png'),
(37656,'physics',29,1301,3147,'A spherical balloon of volume $V$ contains helium at pressure P. How many moles of helium are in the balloon if the average kinetic energy of the helium atoms is $\\bar{K}$ ?','[{"text": "$\\\\dfrac{3 P V}{\\\\bar{K} N_{A}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{2 P V}{\\\\bar{K} N_{A}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{3 P V}{2 \\\\bar{K} N_{A}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{5 P V}{3 \\\\bar{K} N_{A}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'We know that<br>$PV = \\frac{1}{3}mN{v^2} = \\frac{2}{3}N\\left( {\\frac{1}{2}m{v^2}} \\right)$<br>$PV = \\frac{2}{3}N\\bar K$<br>$N = \\frac{3}{2}\\frac{{PV}}{{\\bar K}}$<br>$n = \\frac{N}{{{N_A}}} = \\frac{3}{2}\\frac{{PV}}{{\\bar K{N_A}}}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37657,'physics',27,1382,3323,'A black rectangular surfae of area $A$ emits energy $E$ per second at $27^\\circ C$. If length and breadth are reduced to $\\dfrac{1}{3}^{\\text {rd }}$ of initial value and temperature is raised to $327^\\circ C$, then energy emitted per second becomes','[{"text": "$\\\\dfrac{4 E}{9}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{7 E}{9}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{10 E}{9}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{16 E}{9}$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Rate of energy emitted per second from the surface,<br>$E=e \\sigma A T^{4}$<br>[ where symbols have their usual meanings]<br>When $l$ and $b$ change to $\\dfrac{l}{3}$ and $\\dfrac{b}{3}$,<br>$A^{\\prime}=\\dfrac{l b}{9}=\\dfrac{A}{9}[\\because A=l b]$<br>Now for two different cases<br>$\\dfrac{E^{\\prime}}{E}=\\dfrac{A^{\\prime}}{A} \\dfrac{(227+373)^{4}}{(27+273)^{4}}=\\dfrac{1}{9}\\left(\\dfrac{600}{300}\\right)^{4}$<br>$\\therefore E^{\\prime}=\\dfrac{1}{9} \\times(2)^{4} \\times E \\Rightarrow E^{\\prime}=\\dfrac{16 E}{9}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2016,'MHTCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37658,'physics',28,1371,3289,'Consider two containers $A$ and $B$ containing identical gases at the same pressure, volume and temperature. The gas in container  $A$ is compressed to half of its original volume isothermally while the gas in container $B$ is compressed to half of its original value adiabatically. The ratio of final pressure of gas in $B$ to that of gas in $A$ is','[{"text": "$2^{\\\\gamma-1}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\left(\\\\dfrac{1}{2}\\\\right)^{\\\\gamma-1}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\left(\\\\dfrac{1}{1-\\\\gamma}\\\\right)^{2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\left(\\\\dfrac{1}{\\\\gamma-1}\\\\right)^{2}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'When the compression is isothermal for gas in $A$,<br>$p_{2} V_{2}=p_{1} V_{1}$<br>$p_{2}=\\dfrac{p_{1} V_{1}}{V_{2}}=p_{1} \\dfrac{V_{1}}{V_{1} / 2}=2 p_{1}$<br>For gas in $B$, when compression is adiabatic,<br>$\\begin{aligned}& p_{2}{ }^{\\prime} V_{2}^{\\gamma}{ }^{\\prime}=p_{1} V_{1}^{\\gamma} \\\\& p_{2}{ }^{\\prime}=p_{1}\\left(\\dfrac{V_{1}}{V_{2}{ }^{\\prime}}\\right)=p_{1}\\left(\\dfrac{V_{1}}{V_{1} / 2}\\right)^{\\gamma}=2^{\\gamma} p_{1} \\\\& \\therefore \\dfrac{p_{2}{ }^{\\prime}}{p_{2}}=\\dfrac{2^{\\gamma} p_{1}}{2 p_{1}}=2^{\\gamma-1}\\end{aligned}$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,'NCERT Exemplar','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37659,'physics',26,1133,2710,'An aluminium sphere is dipped into water. Which of the following is true?','[{"text": "Buoyancy in water at $0^\\\\circ C$ will be same as that in water at $4^\\\\circ C.$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Buoyancy will be less in water at $0^\\\\circ C$ than that in water at $4^\\\\circ C.$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Buoyancy may be more or less in water at $4^\\\\circ C$ depending on the radius of the sphere", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Buoyancy will be more in water at $0^\\\\circ C$ than that in water at $4^\\\\circ C.$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Density of water is maximum at $4^\\circ C$, therefore buoyancy at $4^\\circ C$ will be maximum',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2019,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38168,'physics',28,1371,3282,'One mole of an ideal monoatomic gas is compressed isothermally in a rigid vessel to double its pressure at room temperature, $27^\\circ C$. The work done on the gas will be:','[{"text": "$300\\\\,R$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$300\\\\,R\\\\ln 2$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$300\\\\,\\\\ln 6$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$300\\\\,R\\\\ln 7$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Work done on gas $=n R T \\ln \\left(\\dfrac{p_{f}}{p_{i}}\\right)=R(300) \\ln (2)=300 R \\ln 2$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2018,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37660,'physics',29,1303,3152,'The temperature of 5 moles of a gas which was held at constant volume was changed from $100^\\circ \\,C$  to $120^\\circ \\,C$. The change in internal energy was found to be  $80\\,\\,Joules$. The total heat capacity of the gas at constant volume will be equal to','[{"text": "$8\\\\,\\\\,J/K$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$0.8\\\\,\\\\,J/K$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$0.4\\\\,\\\\,J/K$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$4.0\\\\,\\\\,J/K$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'$(\\Delta Q)_{V}=n C_{V} \\Delta T=W \\Delta T$<br>$\\begin{gathered}{[W=\\text { Total heat capacity }]} \\\\\\Rightarrow W=\\dfrac{(\\Delta Q)_{V}}{\\Delta T}=\\dfrac{\\Delta U}{\\Delta T}\\left[\\because(\\Delta Q)_{V}=\\Delta U\\right] \\\\\\Rightarrow W=\\dfrac{80}{(120-100)}=4 J / K\\end{gathered}$<br>Molar heat capacity is$\\frac{4}{5}\\frac{J}{{K \\cdot mol}}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37661,'physics',27,1382,3323,'Assuming the sun to have a spherical outer surface of radius $r$, radiating like a black body at temperature $t^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C}$, the power received by a unit surface (normal to the incident rays) at a distance $R$ from the centre of the sun is','[{"text": "$\\\\dfrac{4 \\\\pi r^{2} \\\\sigma t^{4}}{R^{2}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{r^{2} \\\\sigma(t+273)^{4}}{4 \\\\pi R^{2}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{16 \\\\pi^{2} r^{2} \\\\sigma t^{4}}{R^{2}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{r^{2} \\\\sigma(t+273)^{4}}{R^{2}}$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'From Stefan\'s law, the rate at which energy is<br>radiated by sun at its surface is<br>$\\begin{aligned}I=\\dfrac{P}{4 \\pi R^{2}}=\\dfrac{\\sigma \\times 4 \\pi r^{2} T^{4}}{4 \\pi R^{2}}=\\dfrac{\\sigma r^{2} T^{4}}{R^{2}} \\\\\\Rightarrow I=\\dfrac{\\sigma r^{2}(t+273)^{4}}{R^{2}}\\end{aligned}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37663,'physics',26,1133,2710,'Two solids $$P$$ and $$Q$$ float in water. It is observed that $P$ floats with half of its volume immersed and $$Q$$ floats with $$\\dfrac{2^{\\text {rd }}}{3}$$ of its volume is immersed. The ratio of densities of $P$ and $Q$ is','[{"text": "$$\\\\dfrac{4}{3}$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$\\\\dfrac{3}{4}$$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$$\\\\dfrac{2}{3}$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$\\\\dfrac{3}{2}$$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'For $$P$$,<br>Let ${V_P}$ and ${\\rho _p}$ be volume and density of solid $P$ respectively.<br>As the solid $P$ is floating in water,<br>$$\\therefore$$ Weight of $P = $ weight of water displaced by $P$<br>${V_p}{\\rho _p}g = \\frac{{{V_P}}}{2}{\\rho _{{\\rm{water}}}}g$<br>${\\rho _p} = \\frac{1}{2}{\\rho _{{\\rm{water}}}}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} \\left( 1 \\right)$<br>Similarly, for solid $Q$<br>${V_Q}{\\rho _Q}g = \\frac{2}{3}\\,{V_Q}{\\rho _{{\\text{water }}}}g$<br>${\\rho _Q} = \\frac{2}{3}{\\rho _{{\\rm{water }}}}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\left( 2 \\right)$<br>From equation (1) & (2)<br>$\\frac{{{\\rho _P}}}{{{\\rho _Q}}} = \\frac{3}{4}$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2013,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37664,'physics',29,1306,3156,'A thermally insulated vessel contains an ideal gas of molecular mass $M$ and ratio of specific heats $\\gamma$. It is moving with speed $v$ and it\'s suddenly brought to rest. Assuming no heat is lost to the surrounding, its temperature increases by','[{"text": "$\\\\dfrac{(\\\\gamma-1)}{2 \\\\gamma R} M v^{2} K$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{\\\\gamma M^{2} v}{2 R} K$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{(\\\\gamma-1)}{2 R} M v^{2} K$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{(\\\\gamma-1)}{2(\\\\gamma+1) R} M v^{2} K$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Here, work done is zero.<br>So, loss in kinetic energy $=$ Change in internal energy of gas<br>$\\frac{1}{2}m{v^2} = n{C_V}\\Delta T = n\\frac{R}{{\\gamma  - 1}}\\Delta T$<br>$\\frac{1}{2}m{v^2} = \\frac{m}{M}\\frac{R}{{\\gamma  - 1}}\\Delta T$<br>$\\therefore \\Delta T = \\frac{{M{v^2}(\\gamma  - 1)}}{{2R}}K\\quad (K - {\\rm{ }}Kelvin{\\rm{ }})$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37665,'physics',27,1382,3323,'The total radiant energy per unit area normal to the direction of incidence, received at a distance $R$  from the centre of a star of radius $r$, whose outer surface radiates as a black body at a temeprature $TK$ is given by (where $\\sigma$ is Stefan\'s constant)','[{"text": "$\\\\dfrac{\\\\sigma r T^{4}}{R^{2}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{\\\\sigma r^{2} T^{4}}{R^{2}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\sigma r^{4} T^{4}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{\\\\sigma r^{4} T^{4}}{R^{4}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'In reaching a distance $R$ this energy will be spread over a sphere of radius $R$, so the intensity of radiation will be given by<br>$S=\\dfrac{P}{4 \\pi R^{2}}=\\dfrac{4 \\pi r^{2} \\sigma T^{4}}{4 \\pi R^{2}}=\\dfrac{r^{2} \\sigma T^{4}}{R^{2}}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37667,'physics',26,1133,2710,'A body weight 50 grams in air and 40 grams in water. How much would it weigh in a liquid of specific gravity 1.5 ?','[{"text": "65 grams", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "45 grams", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "30 grams", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "35 grams", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Let $$v$$ be the volume and $$\\rho$$ be the density of the body.<br>In air $\\rho Vg = 50g\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\left( 1 \\right)$<br>In water $(\\rho  - 1)Vg = 40g\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\left( 2 \\right)$<br>In liquid $(\\rho  - 1.5)Vg = xg\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\left( 3 \\right)$<br>From the three eq\'s we get<br>$\\rho  = 5\\,\\& \\,x = 35$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2008,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37668,'physics',29,1302,3150,'A light container having a diatomic gas enclosed with in is moving with velocity $v$. Mass of the gas is  $M$ and number of moles is  $n$. The kinetic energy of the gas w.r.to ground is','[{"text": "$\\\\dfrac{1}{2} M v^{2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{5}{2} n R T$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{1}{2} M v^{2}+\\\\dfrac{5}{2} n R T$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{1}{2} n R T$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'The kinetic energy of gas w.r.t. centre of mass of the system is the kinetic energy of molecules due to their random motion. This will be the internal energy of the gas.<br>$\\mathrm{K} . \\mathrm{E} .=\\dfrac{5}{2} n R T$<br>Given that<br>Mass of gas $ = M$, Temperature $ = T$<br>Kinetic energy of gas w.r.t. ground $=$ Kinetic energy of centre of mass w.r.t. ground + Kinetic energy of gas w.r.t. centre of mass.<br>$K . E=\\dfrac{1}{2} M v^{2}+\\dfrac{5}{2} n R T$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37669,'physics',27,1382,3323,'A spherical black body with radius $12\\,cm$ radiates $640\\,W$ power at $500\\,K$. If the radius is halved and the temperature doubled, the power radiated in watts would be','[{"text": "$5120\\\\,W$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$640\\\\,W$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2560\\\\,W$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$1280\\\\,W$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'<br>$\\begin{aligned}& \\dfrac{P_{1}}{P_{2}}=\\dfrac{A_{1} T_{1}^{4}}{A_{2} T_{2}^{4}} \\\\& P_{2}=\\dfrac{A_{2}}{A_{1}}\\left(\\dfrac{T_{2}}{T_{1}}\\right)^{4} P_{1} \\\\& =\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right)^{2}(2)^{4}(640) \\\\& =2560 W\\end{aligned}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37670,'physics',28,1371,3283,'Compressed air in the tube of a wheel of a cycle at normal temperature suddenly starts coming out from a puncture. The air inside','[{"text": "Starts becoming hotter", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Remains at the same temperature", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Starts becoming cooler", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "May become hotter or cooler depending upon the amount of water vapour present", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'This is an adiabatic expansion. So temperature falls.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,'NCERT Exemplar','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37671,'physics',26,1139,2734,'If there was no gravity, which of the following will not be there for a fluid?','[{"text": "Viscosity", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Surface tension", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Pressure", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Archimedes\'s upward thrust", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'For a fluid, Archimedes\'s upward thrust will not be present if there was not gravity.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2006,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37672,'physics',29,1304,3154,'Two thermally insulated vessels 1 and 2 are filled with air at temperatures $\\left(T_{1}, T_{2}\\right)$ volume $\\left(V_{1}, V_{2}\\right)$ and pressure $\\left(P_{1}, P_{2}\\right)$ respectively. If the valve joining the two vessels is opened, the temperature inside the vessel at equilibrium will be','[{"text": "${T_1}\\\\,\\\\,and\\\\,\\\\,{T_2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\left( {{T_1} + {T_2}} \\\\right)/2$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{T_{1} T_{2} P\\\\left(V_{1}+V_{2}\\\\right)}{P_{1} V_{1} T_{2}+P_{2} V_{2} T_{1}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{T_{1} T_{2}\\\\left(P_{1} V_{1}+P_{2} V_{2}\\\\right)}{P_{1} V_{1} T_{1}+P_{2} V_{2} T_{2}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'The number of moles of the system remains same,<br>$\\dfrac{P_{1} V_{1}}{R T_{1}}+\\dfrac{P_{2} V_{2}}{R T_{2}}=\\dfrac{P\\left(V_{1}+V_{2}\\right)}{R T} \\Rightarrow T=\\dfrac{P\\left(V_{1}+V_{2}\\right) T_{1} T_{2}}{\\left(P_{1} V_{1} T_{2}+P_{2} V_{2} T_{1}\\right)}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37673,'physics',27,1382,3323,'If a body coated at $600\\,K$ surrounded by atmosphere at $300\\,K$ has cooling rate $r_{0}$, the same body at $900\\,K$ surrounded by the same atmosphere, will have cooling rate equal to','[{"text": "$\\\\dfrac{16}{3} r_{0}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{8}{16} r_{0}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$16 r_{0}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$4 r_{0}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'<br>Cooling rate $ \\propto {T^4} - T_0^4$<br>$\\begin{aligned}& r=\\left\\{\\dfrac{(900)^{4}-(300)^{4}}{(600)^{4}-(300)^{4}}\\right\\} r_{0} \\\\& =\\dfrac{16}{3} r_{0}\\end{aligned}$<br>',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37674,'physics',28,1369,3279,'Study the following statements.<br>I. $\\Delta Q$ and $W$ are path independent<br>II. $\\Delta Q-W$ is path dependent<br>III. When $\\Delta U=0$, then heat supplied to the system is used entirely for doing work on the surrounding.<br>Choose the correct statement','[{"text": "Only I", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Only II", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Only III", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both I and II", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Since $U$ is a state function, $\\Delta U$ depends only on the initial & final states and not on the path taken by the gas to go from one state to other. However, $\\Delta Q$ and $W$, in general depend on the path taken to go from the initial to final states.<br>Thus, from the first law of thermodynamics, i.e.<br>$\\Delta Q-W=\\Delta U$<br>$\\text { It is clear that } \\Delta Q-W \\text { is path }$independent.<br>Also, when $\\Delta U=0 \\Rightarrow \\Delta Q=W$<br>Hence, heat supplied to the system is used up entirely for doing work on the surrounding.<br>So option (3) is correct.',NULL,'Hard','MCQ type-2',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37675,'physics',26,1133,2710,'A body floats in a liquid containing in a beaker. The whole system shown in figure is falling under gravity, the upthrust on the body due to the liquid is','[{"text": "zero", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "equal to weight of the body in air", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "equal to weight of the liquid displaced", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "equal to weight of the immersed part of the body", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'In a freely falling vessel upthrust is zero, because effective value of $$g$$ is zero',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/26_37675_question_1.jpg'),
(37676,'physics',29,1304,3154,'Two identical vessels contain the same gas at pressures $P_{1}$ and $P_{2}$ at absoulte temperatures $T_{1}$ and $T_{2}$, respectively. On joining the vessels with a small tube as shown in the figure, the gas reaches a common temperature $T$ and $a$ common pressure $P$. Determine the ratio $P / T$.','[{"text": "$\\\\dfrac{1}{2}\\\\left[\\\\dfrac{P_{1} T_{1}+P_{2} T_{2}}{T_{1} T_{2}}\\\\right]$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\left[\\\\dfrac{P_{1} T_{1}+P_{2} T_{2}}{T_{1} T_{2}}\\\\right]$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{1}{2}\\\\left[\\\\dfrac{P_{1} T_{2}+P_{2} T_{1}}{T_{1} T_{2}}\\\\right]$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\left[\\\\dfrac{P_{1} T_{2}+P_{2} T_{1}}{T_{1} T_{2}}\\\\right]$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'On combining the two vessels the total number of moles remains constant, i.e., $n=n_{1}+n_{2}$<br>Using gas equation, we can write,<br>$n_{1}=\\dfrac{P_{1} V}{R T_{1}} ; n_{2}=\\dfrac{P_{2} V}{R T_{2}}$<br>and $n=\\dfrac{P(2 V)}{R T}$<br>where $ V$ is the volume of each vessel.<br>Thus $\\dfrac{P(2 V)}{R T}=\\dfrac{P_{1} V}{R T_{1}}+\\dfrac{P_{2} V}{R T_{2}}$ or $\\dfrac{P}{T}=\\dfrac{1}{2}\\left[\\dfrac{P_{1}}{T_{1}}+\\dfrac{P_{2}}{T_{2}}\\right]$<br>$ = \\frac{1}{2}\\left[ {\\frac{{{P_1}{T_2} + {P_2}{T_1}}}{{{T_1}{T_2}}}} \\right]$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/29_37676_question_1.jpg'),
(37678,'physics',28,1371,3282,'For an isothermal process,<br>I. Pressure of a given mass of a gas varies inversely as its volume.<br>II. During expansion of gas, work is done by the gas on environment.<br>III. During compression heat is released<br>Choose the correct statement','[{"text": "Only II", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both I and II", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both II and III", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "I, II and III", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'For an isothermal process, temperature is fixed, so <br>$p V=\\text { constant } \\Rightarrow P \\propto \\dfrac{1}{V}$<br>In an isothermal expansion, gas absorbs heat and does work on the environment.<br>In an isothermal compression, work is done on the gas by the environment and heat is released.<br>So, option (4) is correct.',NULL,'Hard','MCQ type-2',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37679,'physics',26,1133,2710,'A balloon has volume of  $1000\\,\\,{m^3}$. It is filled with hydrogen $(\\rho  = 0.09\\,g/L)$. If the density of air is $1.29\\,g/L$, it can lift a total weight of','[{"text": "$600\\\\,kg$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1200\\\\,kg$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$300\\\\,kg$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1800\\\\,kg$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'<br>$1\\,g/L = 1\\,kg/{m^3}$<br>Upthrust $$=$$ total downward force<br>$\\therefore 1000 \\times 1.29 \\times g = (1000 \\times 0.09 \\times g + mg)$<br>$\\therefore m = 1200\\,kg$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37680,'physics',29,1304,3154,'Two spherical vessels of equal volume, are connected by a narrow tube. The apparatus contains an ideal gas at one atmosphere and at temperature $300 K$. Now if one vessel is immersed in a bath of constant temperature $600 K$ and the other in a bath of constant temperature $300 K$. Then the common pressure will be','[{"text": "$\\\\frac{4}{5} \\\\,atm$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1 \\\\,atm$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{3}{4} \\\\,atm$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{4}{3} \\\\,atm$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'The number of gas particles remain constant<br>${n_i} = {n_f}$<br>$\\frac{{P(2V)}}{{R{T_1}}} = \\frac{{{P^\\prime }V}}{{R{T_1}}} + \\frac{{{P^\\prime }V}}{{R{T_2}}} \\Rightarrow \\frac{{2P}}{{R{T_1}}} = \\frac{{{P^\\prime }}}{R}\\left[ {\\frac{{{T_1} + {T_2}}}{{{T_1}{T_2}}}} \\right]$<br>${P^\\prime } = \\frac{{2P{T_2}}}{{\\left( {{T_1} + {T_2}} \\right)}} = \\frac{{2 \\times 1 \\times 600}}{{(300 + 600)}} = \\frac{4}{3} atm$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/29_37680_question_1.png'),
(37681,'physics',27,1380,3306,'A metal rod of length $L$, cross - sectional area $A$, Young\'s modulus $Y$ and coefficient of linear expansion $\\alpha$ is heated to $t^\\circ C$. The work that can be performed by the rod when heated is','[{"text": "$\\\\dfrac{Y A \\\\alpha L t^{2}}{2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{Y A \\\\alpha^{2} L t^{2}}{2}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{Y A \\\\alpha^{2} L^{2} t^{2}}{2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{Y A \\\\alpha L t}{2}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Increase  in  length if the rod is allowed to expand freely, $\\Delta L = L\\alpha t$<br>Work that can be performed by the rod,<br>$\\Delta W$= change in potential energy of the rod<br>$ = \\frac{1}{2} \\times ({\\rm{strain}}\\,{\\rm{energy}}\\,{\\rm{per}}\\,{\\rm{unit}}\\,{\\rm{volume)}}\\, \\times \\,{\\rm{volume}}$<br>$ = \\frac{1}{2} \\times Y \\times {\\left( {{\\rm{strain}}} \\right)^2} \\times AL$<br>$ = \\frac{1}{2} \\times Y \\times {\\left( {\\frac{{\\Delta L}}{L}} \\right)^2} \\times AL = \\frac{1}{2} \\times Y \\times {\\left( {\\alpha t} \\right)^2} \\times AL$<br>$ = \\frac{{YA{\\alpha ^2}L{t^2}}}{2}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37683,'physics',26,1133,2710,'A boat having a length $3\\,m$ and breadth $2\\,m$ is floating on a lake. The boat sinks by $1\\,cm$ when a man gets on it. The mass of the man is','[{"text": "$60\\\\,kg$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$72\\\\,kg$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$52\\\\,kg$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$65\\\\,kg$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Weight of man = extra upthrust<br>$\\therefore mg = \\left( {3 \\times 2 \\times {{10}^{ - 2}}} \\right){(10)^3}(10){\\text{ }}$<br>${\\text{or }}\\quad m = 60\\,kg$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37684,'physics',29,1300,3145,'A gas at  $27^\\circ \\,C$ temperature and 30 atmospheric presure is allowed to expand to the atmospheric pressure. If the volume becomes 10 times its initial volume, then the final temperature becomes','[{"text": "$273^\\\\circ \\\\,C$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$173^\\\\circ \\\\,C$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$100^\\\\circ \\\\,C$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$-173^\\\\circ \\\\,C$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'$\\dfrac{P_{1} V_{1}}{T_{1}}=\\dfrac{P_{2} V_{2}}{T_{2}} \\Rightarrow T_{2}=\\dfrac{P_{2} V_{2}}{P_{1} V_{1}} \\times T_{1}$<br>$ \\Rightarrow \\quad {T_2} = \\frac{1}{{30}} \\times \\frac{{10}}{1} \\times 300 = 100\\,\\,K =  - 173^\\circ C$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37685,'physics',27,1384,3326,'Triple point of water is','[{"text": "$273.16^\\\\circ F$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$273.16 K$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$273.16^\\\\circ C$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$273.16 R$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,NULL,NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(48880,'biology',80,475,959,'In the bundle sheath cells of ${C_4}$ plants, the Calvin pathway is carried out for the synthesis of _','[{"text": "Amino acids", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Lipids", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Carbohydrates", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "All of the above", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Released $C{O_2}$ is fixed by Calvin pathway for the synthesis of carbohydrates like glucose.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37686,'physics',28,1371,3282,'<div class="matching-question"><div class="column-left">In an isothermal expansion, the gas absorbs heat and does work while in an isothermal compression, work is done on the gas by the environment and heat is released.<br></div><div class="column-right">In an isothermal process, there is no change in internal energy of an ideal gas.</div></div>','[{"text": "Statement A is correct but Statement B is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Statement A is incorrect but Statement B is correct.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both statements are correct.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Statements are incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'From first law of thermodynamics, in isothermal expansion heat supplied to the gas equals the work done by the gas. In isothermal process temperature is constant. So option (3) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ type-2',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37687,'physics',26,1133,2710,'A body measures $5\\,N$ in air and $2\\,N$ when put in water. The buoyant force is','[{"text": "$7\\\\,N$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$9\\\\,N$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$3\\\\,N$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "none of these", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'$F = {W_{{\\text{air }}}} - {W_{{\\text{water }}}}$ or $\\Delta W$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37688,'physics',29,1300,3145,'A bottle is inverted and dipped into a tank. At the bottom of the tank, $3 / 5$ th part of the bottle is filled with water. The depth of the tank is','[{"text": "$20 \\\\,m$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$15 \\\\,m$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$10 \\\\,m$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$5 \\\\,m$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'We assume that the pressure on the surface of water is $1 \\,\\,atm$ and process is isothermal.<br>${P_1}{V_1} = {P_2}{V_2}$<br>$1 \\times {V_1} = {P_2}\\frac{2}{5}V$<br>${P_2} = \\frac{5}{2} = 2.5 \\,\\,atm$<br>$\\Delta P = 1.5 \\,\\,atm$<br>$1.5 \\times {10^5}\\frac{N}{{{m^2}}} = \\rho gh$<br>$h = \\frac{{1.5 \\times {{10}^5}}}{{{{10}^3} \\times 10}} = 15\\,\\,m$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37689,'physics',27,1384,3326,'The calories of heat developed in $200\\,W$ bulb in $7\\,{\\rm{min}}$ is estimated','[{"text": "15000", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "100", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "1000", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "20000", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'$H = \\frac{{200 \\times 7 \\times 60}}{{4.18}}cal$<br>$ = 20095.7 \\approx 20,000\\,cal$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37691,'physics',26,1133,2710,'A raft of mass $M = 600\\,kg$ floats in calm water with $7\\,cm$ submerged. When a man stands on the raft, $8.4\\,cm$ are submerged, the man\'s mass is','[{"text": "$30\\\\,kg$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$60\\\\,kg$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$90\\\\,kg$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$120\\\\,kg$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Upthrust on $7\\,cm = 600\\,kg$<br>$$\\therefore$$ Upthrusty on further $1.4\\,cm = \\frac{{600}}{7} \\times 1.4 = 120\\,kg$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37692,'physics',29,1300,3145,'The pressure is $P$, volume $V$ and temperature $T$ of a gas in the jar $A$ and the other gas in the jar $B$ is at pressure $2 P$, volume $V / 4$ and temperature $2 T$, then the ratio of the number of molecules in the jar $A$ and $B$  will be','[{"text": "$1: 1$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1: 2$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2: 1$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$4: 1$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'$P V=N k T \\Rightarrow \\dfrac{N_{A}}{N_{B}}=\\dfrac{P_{A} V_{A}}{P_{B} V_{B}} \\times \\dfrac{T_{B}}{T_{A}}$<br>$\\Rightarrow \\dfrac{N_{A}}{N_{B}}=\\dfrac{P \\times V \\times(2 T)}{2 P \\times \\dfrac{V}{4} \\times T}=\\dfrac{4}{1}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37693,'physics',27,1381,3315,'Steam at $100^\\circ C$ is passed into $20\\,g$ of water at $10^\\circ C$. When water acquires a temperature of $80^\\circ C$, the mass of water present will be [Take specific heat of water $ = 1\\,cal\\,{g^{ - 1}}\\,^\\circ {C^{ - 1}}$ and latent heat of steam $ = 540\\,cal\\,{g^{ - 1}}$ ]','[{"text": "$24\\\\,g$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$31.5\\\\,g$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$42.5\\\\,g$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$22.5\\\\,g$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Heat given by the water = Heat lost by steam<br>$\\begin{aligned}& m_{w} s_{w} \\Delta T_{1}=m L+m s_{w} \\Delta T_{2} \\\\& 20 \\times 1 \\times(80-10)=m \\times 540+m \\times 1 \\times(100-80) \\\\& \\Rightarrow 1400=560 m \\Rightarrow m=2.5 g\\end{aligned}$<br>Total mass of water $ = 20 + 2.5 = 22.5\\,g.$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37694,'physics',28,1371,3281,'<div class="matching-question"><div class="column-left">Thermodynamic processes in nature are irreversible.<br></div><div class="column-right">Dissipative effects cannot be eliminated.</div></div>','[{"text": "Statement A is correct but Statement B is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Statement A is incorrect but Statement B is correct.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both statements are correct.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Statements are incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'The thermodynamic process is irreversible as there always occurs a loss of energy due to energy spent in working against the dissipative force, which is not recovered back. So option (3) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ type-2',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37695,'physics',26,1133,2710,'A body of density $$\\rho$$ is dropped from rest from a height $h$ into a lake of density $$\\sigma(\\sigma>\\rho)$$. The maximum depth the body sinks inside the liquid is (neglect viscous effect of liquid)','[{"text": "$$\\\\dfrac{h \\\\rho}{\\\\sigma-\\\\rho}$$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$$\\\\dfrac{h \\\\sigma}{\\\\sigma-\\\\rho}$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$\\\\dfrac{h \\\\rho}{\\\\sigma}$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$\\\\dfrac{h \\\\sigma}{\\\\rho}$$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'<br>$v = \\sqrt {2gh} $<br>Retardation in liqiud $a = \\frac{{V\\sigma g - V\\rho g}}{V} = \\left( {\\frac{{\\sigma  - \\rho }}{\\rho }} \\right)g$<br>$\\therefore {d_{{\\text{max}}}} = \\frac{{{v^2}}}{{2a}} = \\frac{{2gh}}{{2 - \\frac{{\\rho h}}{{\\pi  - \\rho }}}} = \\frac{{\\rho h}}{{\\sigma  - \\rho }}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37745,'physics',27,1382,3319,'The dimensional formula for the thermal resistance is','[{"text": "$\\\\left[ {{M^{ - 1}}\\\\,{L^{ - 2}}\\\\,{T^3}\\\\theta } \\\\right]$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\left[ {M{L^2}\\\\,T\\\\theta } \\\\right]$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\left[ {{M^{ - 1}}\\\\,{L^2}\\\\,{T^3}\\\\theta } \\\\right]$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "None of these", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'<br>$R = \\frac{{{\\text{Temperature}}\\,{\\text{difference}}}}{{{\\text{Heat}}\\,{\\text{current}}}}$<br>$\\therefore R = \\frac{{[\\theta ]}}{{\\left[ {M{L^2}\\,{T^{ - 2}}/T} \\right]}}$<br>$ = \\left[ {{M^{ - 1}}\\,{L^{ - 2}}\\,{T^3}\\theta } \\right]$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37696,'physics',29,1300,3145,'When an air bubble of radius \' \\(r\\) \' rises from the bottom to the surface of a lake, its radius becomes \\(5 r / 4\\) (the pressure of the atmosphere is equal to the \\[10 \\,{\\mkern 1mu} m\\] height of water column). If the temperature is constant and the surface tension is neglected, the depth of the lake is<div class="matching-question"></div>','[{"text": "$3.53\\\\,{\\\\mkern 1mu} m$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$6.53\\\\,{\\\\mkern 1mu} m$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$9.53\\\\,{\\\\mkern 1mu} m$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$12.53\\\\,{\\\\mkern 1mu} m$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'According to Boyle\'s law<br>${\\left( {{P_1}{V_1}} \\right)_{At{\\rm{ }}top{\\rm{ }}of{\\rm{ }}the{\\rm{ }}lake{\\rm{ }}}} = {\\left( {{P_2}{V_2}} \\right)_{At{\\rm{ }}the{\\rm{ }}bottom{\\rm{ }}of{\\rm{ }}the{\\rm{ }}lake{\\rm{ }}}}$<br>$ \\Rightarrow {P_1}{V_1} = \\left( {{P_1} + h} \\right){V_2}$<br>$ \\Rightarrow 10 \\times \\frac{4}{3}\\pi {\\left( {\\frac{{5r}}{4}} \\right)^3} = (10 + h) \\times \\frac{{4\\pi }}{3}{r^3}$<br>$h = \\frac{{610}}{{64}} = 9.53\\,\\,m$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37697,'physics',27,1382,3317,'The thickness of a metallic plate is $0.4\\,cm$. The temperature between its two surface is $20^\\circ C$. The quantity of heat flowing per second is 50 $cal$ from $5\\,c{m^2}$ area. In $CGS$ system, the coefficient of thermal conductivity will be','[{"text": "0.4", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "0.6", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "0.2", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "0.5", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'<br>$\\frac{Q}{t} = \\frac{{KA(\\Delta \\theta )}}{l} \\Rightarrow 50 = \\frac{{5 \\times 20\\,K}}{{0.4}}$<br>$ \\Rightarrow K = \\frac{1}{5} = 0.2$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37698,'physics',28,1371,3283,'<div class="matching-question"><div class="column-left">In an adiabatic process, change in internal energy is equal to magnitude of work done by the gas in the process.<br></div><div class="column-right">The temperature remains constant in an adiabatic process.</div></div>','[{"text": "Statement A is correct but Statement B is incorrect.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Statement A is incorrect but Statement B is correct.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both statements are correct.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Statements are incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'In an adiabatic process $d Q=0$.<br>$\\therefore \\quad d U=-d W \\quad(\\therefore d Q=d U+d W)$<br>In an adiabatic process, temperature either rises or falls. So option (1) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ type-2',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37699,'physics',26,1135,2714,'An ideal fluid flows through a pipe of circular cross section with diameters $5\\,cm$ and $10\\,cm$ as shown. The ratio of velocities of fluid at $$A$$ and $B$ is','[{"text": "$$4: 1$$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$$1: 4$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$2: 1$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$1: 2$$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'<br><img src=\'/img/data/physics/26_37699_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' /> <br>Using continuity equation, ${A_1}{v_1} = {A_2}{v_2}$<br>$\\frac{{{v_1}}}{{{v_2}}} = \\frac{{{A_2}}}{{\\,{A_1}}} = \\frac{{\\pi r_2^2}}{{\\pi r_1^2}} = {\\left( {\\frac{{{r_2}}}{{{r_1}}}} \\right)^2} = \\frac{4}{1}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2016,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/26_37699_question_1.png'),
(37700,'physics',29,1300,3145,'Initially a gas of diatomic molecules is contained in a cylinder of volume $V$ at a pressure $P$ and temperature $250 K$. Assuming that $50 \\%$ of the molecules get dissociated causing a change in number of moles. The pressure of the resulting gas at temperature $1000 K$, when contained in a volume $2 V$ is given by ${P_2}$. The ratio ${P_2}/{P_1}$ is-','[{"text": "3", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "8", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "12", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "25", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Using ideal gas equation, $P V=n R T$ <br>$ \\Rightarrow {P_1}V = nR \\times 250\\left[ {\\because {T_1} = 250 K} \\right]\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,(1)$<br>when 50% of n is $\\left( {\\frac{n}{2}} \\right)$ is<br>dissociated then total moles will be <br>$\\frac{n}{2} + \\left( {\\frac{n}{2} + \\frac{n}{2}} \\right) = \\frac{{3n}}{2}$<br>${P_2}(2V) = \\frac{{3n}}{2}R \\times 1000\\left[ {\\because {T_2} = 1000K} \\right]\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,(2)$<br>From eq\'s (1) &nbsp;&amp; (2)<br>$\\begin{aligned}&amp; \\dfrac{P_{1}}{2 P_{2}}=\\dfrac{2 \\times 250}{3 \\times 1000} \\Rightarrow \\dfrac{P_{1}}{P_{2}}=\\dfrac{1}{3} \\\\&amp; \\Rightarrow \\dfrac{P_{2}}{P_{1}}=3 .\\end{aligned}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37701,'physics',28,1369,3279,'<div class="matching-question"><div class="column-left">First law of thermodynamics says that heat flows from lower temperature to higher temperature.<br></div><div class="column-right">Heat supplied to a system is always equal to the increase in its internal energy.</div></div>','[{"text": "Statement A is correct but Statement B is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Statement A is incorrect but Statement B is correct.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both statements are correct.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Statements are incorrect.", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'First law of thermodynamics tells only about the conversion of mechanical energy into the heat energy and vice versa. $\\Delta Q=\\Delta U$ (isochoric process). So option (4) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ type-2',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37702,'physics',27,1382,3317,'Four rods with different radii $r$ and length $l$ are used to connect two heat reservoirs at different temperatures. Which one will conduct most heat?','[{"text": "$r = 1\\\\,cm,l = 1\\\\,m$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$r = 1\\\\,cm,l = \\\\frac{1}{2}\\\\,m$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$r = 1\\\\,cm,l = 2\\\\,m$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$r = 2\\\\,cm,l = \\\\frac{1}{2}\\\\,m$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Rate of heat conduction through a rod of radius $r$ and length $l$,<br>$i=\\dfrac{K\\left(\\pi r^{2}\\right)\\left(T_{2}-T_{1}\\right)}{l}$<br>For given $K\\,{\\rm{and}}\\,\\left( {{T_2} - {T_1}} \\right),i \\propto \\frac{{{r^2}}}{l}$<br>Hence, rod with $r = 2\\,cm$ and $l = \\frac{1}{2}\\,m$ will conduct more heat.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37703,'physics',26,1135,2714,'Horizontal tube of non-uniform cross-section has radii of $0.1\\,m$ and $0.05\\,m$ respectively at $M$ and $N$. For a streamline flow of liquid the rate of liquid flows is','[{"text": "continuously changes with time", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "greater at $M$ than at $N$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "greater at $N$ than at $M$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "same at $M$ and $N$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'According to principle of continuity<br>$Av = {\\text{ constant }}$<br>$ \\Rightarrow {A_1}{v_1} = {A_2}{v_2}$<br>Therefore, the rate of flow of fluid is same at $M$ and $N$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2009,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/26_37703_question_1.png'),
(37704,'physics',29,1300,3145,'A vessel containing 5 $liters$ of a gas at $0.8 \\,m$ of mercury pressure is connected to an evacuated vessel of volume 3 $liters$. The resultant pressure inside will be (assuming isothermal condition).','[{"text": "$0.5 \\\\,m$ of mercury", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{4}{3}\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,m$ of mercury", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{3}{4}\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,m$ of mercury", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2.0 \\\\,m$ of mercury", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'As temperature is constant<br>${P_1}{V_1} = {P_2}{V_2}$<br>$0.8 \\times 5 = P \\times (3 + 5) \\Rightarrow P = 0.5 \\,m.$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37705,'physics',27,1382,3317,'The two ends of a rod of length $L$ and a uniform cross-section area $A$ are kept at two temperatures $T_{1}$ and $T_{2}\\left(T_{1}>T_{2}\\right)$. The rate of heat transfer, $\\dfrac{d Q}{d t}$, through the rod in a steady state is given by','[{"text": "$\\\\dfrac{d Q}{d t}=\\\\dfrac{K L\\\\left(T_{1}-T_{2}\\\\right)}{A}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{d Q}{d t}=\\\\dfrac{K\\\\left(T_{1}-T_{2}\\\\right)}{L A}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{d Q}{d t}=K L A\\\\left(T_{1}-T_{2}\\\\right)$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{d Q}{d t}=\\\\dfrac{K A\\\\left(T_{1}-T_{2}\\\\right)}{L}$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'In steady state the rate of heat flowing from one face to the other face $t$ is given by<br>$\\dfrac{d Q}{d t}=\\dfrac{K A\\left(T_{1}-T_{2}\\right)}{L}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37706,'physics',28,1371,3283,'<div class="matching-question"><div class="column-left">The temperature of a gas does not change, when it undergoes an adiabatic process.<br></div><div class="column-right">During adiabatic process, heat energy is exchanged between a system and surroundings.</div></div>','[{"text": "Statement A is correct but Statement B is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Statement A is incorrect but Statement B is correct.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both statements are correct.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Statements are incorrect.", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Temperature decreases during adiabatic expansion while it increases during adiabatic compression. Heat change is zero in adiabatic process. So option (4) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ type-2',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37707,'physics',26,1136,2717,'A cylindrical container containing water has a small hole at height of $H = 8\\,cm$ from the bottom and at a depth of $2\\,cm$ from the top surface of the liquid. The maximum horizontal distance travelled by the water before it hits the ground$$(x)$$ is','[{"text": "$4\\\\,cm$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$8\\\\,cm$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$6\\\\,cm$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$4\\\\sqrt 2 \\\\,cm$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Velocity of water coming out of the hole<br>$v = \\sqrt {2gh}  = \\sqrt {2 \\times 9.8 \\times 2}  = 6.26\\,cm/s$<br>Time required to fall $ = \\sqrt {\\frac{{2H}}{g}}  = \\sqrt {\\frac{{2 \\times 8}}{{9.8}}}  = 1.277\\,s$<br>Horizontal distance covered by<br>${\\text{ water }} = 6.26 \\times 1.277 = 8\\,cm$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2019,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37708,'physics',29,1301,3147,'In outer space there are 10 molecules per $c{m^3}$ on the average and the temperature there is $3 K$. The average pressure of this light gas is','[{"text": "${10^5}N{m^{ - 2}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$5 \\\\times {10^{ - 14}}N{m^{ - 2}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$0.4 \\\\times {10^{ - 16}}N{m^{ - 2}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$4.14 \\\\times {10^{ - 16}}N{m^{ - 2}}$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'$P=\\dfrac{n K T}{V}=\\dfrac{10 \\times 1.38 \\times 10^{-23} \\times 3}{10^{-6}}$<br>$ = 4.14 \\times {10^{ - 16}}N{m^{ - 2}}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37709,'physics',27,1382,3317,'On heating one end of a rod, the temperature of whole rod will be uniform when','[{"text": "$K = 1$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$K = 0$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$K = 100$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$K = \\\\infty $", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'<br>$\\frac{{dQ}}{{dT}} =  - KA\\frac{{d\\theta }}{{dx}};\\,{\\rm{when}}\\,K = \\infty ,\\frac{{d\\theta }}{{dx}} = 0$<br>i.e., $\\theta$ is independent of $x$ i.e., constant or uniform',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL);
INSERT INTO `qb_questions` (`id`, `subject_id`, `chapter_id`, `topic_id`, `subtopic_id`, `question_html`, `options`, `correct_index`, `explanation`, `explanation_image_url`, `difficulty`, `qtype`, `year`, `tag`, `created_at`, `question_image_url`) VALUES
(37710,'physics',28,1371,3282,'<div class="matching-question"><div class="column-left">The isothermal curves intersect each other at a certain point.</div><div class="column-right">The isothermal changes takes place slowly, so the isothermal curves have very little slope.</div></div>','[{"text": "Statement A is correct but Statement B is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Statement A is incorrect but Statement B is correct.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both statements are correct.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Statements are incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Isothermal curves never intersect each other. Isothermal changes take place slowly, as they have a very little slope. So option (2) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ type-2',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37711,'physics',26,1136,2717,'A large open tank containing water has two holes to its wall. A square hole of side a is made at a depth $$y$$ and a circular hole of radius $$r$$ is made at a depth $16y$ from the surface of water. If equal amount of water comes out through both the holes per second, then the relation between $$r$$ and $a$ will be','[{"text": "$$r=\\\\dfrac{a}{2 \\\\sqrt{\\\\pi}}$$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$$r=\\\\dfrac{a}{2 \\\\pi}$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$r=\\\\dfrac{2 a}{\\\\pi}$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$r=\\\\dfrac{2 a}{\\\\sqrt{\\\\pi}}$$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Using principle of continuity<br>${A_1}{v_1} = {A_2}{v_2}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\left( 1 \\right)$<br>Where, $$A_{1}$$ is the area of square hole $$A_{1}=a^{2}$$<br>$$A_{2}$$ is the area of circular hole, $$A_{2}=\\pi r^{2}$$<br>$$v_{1}$$ is the velocity at depth $y,{v_1} = \\sqrt {2gy} $<br>and $$v_{2}$$ is the velocity at depth $16y$, $$v_{2}=\\sqrt{2 g(16 y)}$$<br>Substituting values in eq.(1), we get<br>$$\\begin{aligned}& a^{2}(\\sqrt{2 g y})=\\pi r^{2}(\\sqrt{2 g(16 y)}) \\\\& a^{2}(\\sqrt{2 g y})=4 \\pi r^{2}(\\sqrt{2 g y})\\end{aligned}$$<br>$$r^{2}=\\dfrac{a^{2}}{4 \\pi}$$<br>$$\\therefore r=\\dfrac{a}{2 \\sqrt{\\pi}}$$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37712,'physics',29,1301,3147,'The mass of hydrogen molecules is<br>$3.32 \\times {10^{ - 27}} kg$. If $10^{23}$ hydrogen molecules}<br>strike per second at on $2\\,\\,c{m^2}$ area of a rigid wall at an angle of $45^{\\circ}$ from the normal and are rebound back with speed of $1000\\,\\,m/s$, then the pressure exerted on the wall is (Assume collision is elastic)','[{"text": "$23.4 \\\\times {10^3} Pa$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2.34 \\\\times {10^3} Pa$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$0.23 \\\\times {10^3} Pa$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2\\\\,Pa$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Given that the collision is elastic. The component of the linear momentum along the surface remains same and the perpendicular component gets reversed.<br>$\\Delta P=2 m v_{N}$<br>Where $v_{N}$ is the compound of velocity along normal direction. The pressure acting on the wall is $P=\\dfrac{F}{A}=\\dfrac{2 N m V_{N}}{A}$ (N. number of molecules striking per second)<br>$\\begin{aligned}P & =\\dfrac{2 \\times 10^{23} \\times 3.32 \\times 10^{-27} \\times 1000}{\\sqrt{2} \\times 2 \\times 10^{-4}} \\\\& =2.34 \\times 10^{3} \\text { Pascal }\\end{aligned}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/29_37712_question_1.jpg'),
(37776,'physics',28,1372,3293,'An ideal gas undergoes four different processes from the same initial state as shown in the figure below. Those processes are adiabatic, isothermal, isobaric and isochoric. The curve which represents the adiabatic process among \\(1,2,3\\) and 4 is<div class="matching-question"></div>','[{"text": "2", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "3", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "4", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "1", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'1 : Isochoric $(\\because \\mathrm{V}=$ constant $)$<br>2: Adiabatic $\\left(\\because\\right.$ non linear $P V^{r}=$ constant $)$<br>3: Isothermal  $$(\\because P \\propto \\frac{1}{V}\\,\\,{\\text{with}}\\,\\,T = $$ constant )<br>4: Isobaric ($$\\because P = $$ constant )',NULL,'Medium','Graph/Figure',2022,'NEET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/28_37776_question_1.png'),
(37713,'physics',27,1382,3318,'$A,B$ and $C$ are the three identical conductors but made from different materials. They are kept in contact as shown. Their thermal conductivities are $K,2\\,K$ and $K/2$. The free end of $A$ is at $100^\\circ C$ and the free end of $C$ is at $0^\\circ C$. During steady state, the temperature of the junction of $A$ and $B$ is nearly____ $^\\circ C$.','[{"text": "71", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "29", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "63", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "37", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Given that ${K_A} = K,{K_B} = 2\\,K,\\,{K_C} = \\frac{K}{2}$<br><img src=\'/img/data/physics/27_37713_explanation_1.jpg\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' /> <br>Let $L$ be the length and A be the area of cross-section of each conductor respectively. Let ${T_1}$ and ${T_2}$ be temperatures of $AB$ and $BC$ junctions respectively.<br>At the steady state, ${i_A} = {i_B} = {i_C}$<br>For the junction $AB$<br>$\\frac{{{K_A}A\\left( {100 - {T_1}} \\right)}}{L} = \\frac{{{K_B}A\\left( {{T_1} - {T_2}} \\right)}}{L}$<br>$\\frac{{KA\\left( {100 - {T_1}} \\right)}}{L} = \\frac{{2KA\\left( {{T_1} - {T_2}} \\right)}}{L}$<br>$\\therefore \\left( {100 - {T_1}} \\right) = 2\\left( {\\,{T_1} - {T_2}} \\right)$<br>$100 - {T_1} = 2\\,{T_1} - 2\\,{T_2};2\\,{T_2} = 3\\,{T_1} - 100\\,$<br>${T_2} = \\frac{3}{2}\\,{T_1} - 50\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\left( 1 \\right)$<br>For the junction $BC$<br>$\\dfrac{K_{B} A\\left(T_{1}-T_{2}\\right)}{L}=\\dfrac{K_{C} A\\left(T_{2}-0\\right)}{L}$<br>$\\dfrac{2 K A\\left(T_{1}-T_{2}\\right)}{L}=\\dfrac{(K / 2) A\\left(T_{2}\\right)}{L}$ <br>$ \\Rightarrow 4{T_1} = 5{T_2}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\left( 2 \\right)$<br>From eq\'s 1 & 2 we get<br>${T_1} = \\frac{{500^\\circ C}}{7} = 71^\\circ C$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2012,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/27_37713_question_1.png'),
(37714,'physics',28,1371,3281,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Rotating blade in a liquid stops after a certain time is a irreversible process.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Irreversibility arises as the system attains a non-equilibrium state.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Irreversibility mainly arises due to dissipative factor like friction, viscosity, etc and as many processes takes the system to non-equilibrium state.<br>So, when the blade is rotated in a liquid, then due to the viscosity, the system becomes irreversible as it stops after a certain time. Thus, it is a irreversible process. Irreversibility arises as the system attains equilibrium state. Assertion is correct reason is false. So option (3) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37715,'physics',26,1136,2715,'Two light balls are suspended as shown in figure. When a stream of air passes through the space between them, the distance between the balls will','[{"text": "remain same", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "increase", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "may increase or decrease, depending on speed of air", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "decrease", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'When a stream of air passes through the space between the balls, the pressure reduce between them as compared to the atmospheric pressure on either side. So, according to Bernoulli\'s theorem, the pressure energy should be constant and is compensated by movement of balls towards each other. So, the distance between the balls will decrease.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2019,'MHTCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/26_37715_question_1.png'),
(37716,'physics',29,1301,3148,'If the $rms$ speed, of molecules of an ideal gas is \' $v$ \', what is the average speed of the molecules at the same temperature','[{"text": "$\\\\sqrt{\\\\dfrac{8}{3 \\\\pi}} v$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\sqrt{\\\\dfrac{2}{3}} v$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$v$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{3 v}{2}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'${v_{rms{\\rm{ }}}} = \\sqrt {3RT/M} ,{V_{avg{\\rm{ }}}} = \\sqrt {\\frac{{8RT}}{{\\pi M}}} $',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37717,'physics',28,1369,3279,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> We can not change the temperature of a body without giving (or taking) heat to (or from) it.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> According to principle of conservation of energy, total energy of a system should remain conserved.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but reason is correct.", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'We can change the temperature of a body without giving (or taking) heat to (or from) it. For example in an adiabatic compression temperature rises and in an adiabatic expansion temperature falls, although no heat is given or taken from the system. Option (4) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37718,'physics',27,1382,3318,'Three rods made of the same material and having same cross - sectional area but different length $10\\,cm,20\\,cm$ and $30\\,cm$ are joined as shown. The temperature of the junction is','[{"text": "$10.8^\\\\circ C$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$14.6^\\\\circ C$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$16.4^\\\\circ C$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$18.2^\\\\circ C$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Let $\\theta$ be the temperature of junction. ${i_1},{i_2}$, and ${i_3}$ are heat currents. Then ${i_1} = {i_2} + {i_3}$<br><img src=\'/img/data/physics/27_37718_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' /> <br>${\\rm{or }}\\frac{{30 - \\theta }}{{(30/KA)}} = \\frac{{\\theta  - 20}}{{(20/KA)}} + \\frac{{\\theta  - 10}}{{(10/KA)}}$<br>${\\rm{ or }}2(30 - \\theta ) = 3(\\theta  - 20) + (\\theta  - 10){\\rm{ }}$<br>${\\rm{or }}\\theta  = 16.36^\\circ C \\approx \\theta  = 16.4^\\circ C$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/27_37718_question_1.png'),
(37719,'physics',26,1136,2717,'The velocity of efflux of a liqiud through an orifice in the bottom of a tank does not depend upon','[{"text": "Density of liqiud", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Height of the liqiud column above orifice", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Acceleration due to gravity", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "None of these", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Velocity of efflux $$=\\sqrt{2 g h}$$, which is independent of density of liqiud.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37777,'physics',29,1302,3150,'A sealed container with negligible coefficient of volumetric expansion contains helium (a monoatomic gas). When it is heated from $300\\,\\,K$ to $600\\,\\,K$, the average K.E. of helium atoms is','[{"text": "Halved", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Increased by factor $\\\\sqrt{2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Doubled", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Unchanged", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Kinetic energy $\\propto$ Temperature. Hence if temperature is doubles, kinetic energy will also be doubled.',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37720,'physics',29,1305,3155,'The speed of a molecule of gas in a cubical vessel of side $5 m$ is $15 \\,m{s^{ - 1}}$. This molecule is constantly colliding with the walls of a container. The collision frequency with a particular wall will be (Assume that molecule strikes the wall normally)','[{"text": "0.2 per second", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "1.5 per second", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "2.5 per second", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "5 per second", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'A molecule has to travel a distance of 10 $m$ to strike a surface second time.<br><img src=\'/img/data/physics/29_37720_explanation_1.jpg\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' /> <br>$\\Delta t=\\dfrac{\\Delta s}{v}=\\dfrac{10 m}{15 m / s}=\\dfrac{2}{3} s$<br>In $\\dfrac{2}{3} s \\rightarrow 1$ collision<br>$1 s \\rightarrow \\dfrac{3}{2}$ collision<br>',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37721,'physics',28,1371,3287,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> In cyclic process, $\\Delta U=0$.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Work done in a cyclic path is always zero.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'In cyclic process, the system returns to its initial state so $\\Delta U=0$ work is a path function so $W_{\\text {cycle }}$ need not to be zero.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37722,'physics',27,1382,3318,'In the figure $A B C$ is a conducting rod whose lateral surfaces are insulated. The length of the section $AB$ is one - half of that of $BC$, and the respective thermal conductivities of the two sections are as given in the figure. If the ends $A$ and $C$ are maintained at $0^\\circ C$ and $70^\\circ C$ respectively, the temperature of junction $B$ in the steady state is','[{"text": "$30^\\\\circ C$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$40^\\\\circ C$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$50^\\\\circ C$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$60^\\\\circ C$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Heat currents in both the rods are equal<br>or ${H_{CB}} = {H_{BA}}$<br>or $\\frac{{{\\theta _C} - {\\theta _B}}}{{(2l/3KA)}} = \\frac{{{\\theta _B} - {\\theta _A}}}{{(l/3KA)}}$<br>$\\therefore \\frac{3}{2}\\left( {70 - {\\theta _B}} \\right) = 2\\left( {{\\theta _B} - 0} \\right)$<br>or ${\\theta _B} = 30^\\circ C$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/27_37722_question_1.png'),
(37723,'physics',26,1136,2717,'A hole is made at the bottom of the tank filled with water<br>$\\left( {density = 1000kg/{m^3}} \\right)$. If the total pressure at the bottom of the tank is three atomspheres ( 1 atmosphere $ = {10^5}\\,N/{m^2}$ ), then the velocity of efflux is','[{"text": "$\\\\sqrt {400} \\\\,m/s$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\sqrt {200} \\\\,m/s$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\sqrt {600} \\\\,m/s$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\sqrt {500} \\\\,m/s$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Difference in pressure energy = difference in kinetic energy<br>$\\therefore \\left( {{p_0} - {p_0}} \\right) = \\frac{1}{2}\\rho {v^2}$<br>or $\\quad v = 2\\sqrt {\\frac{{{p_0}}}{\\rho }} $<br>$$=2 \\sqrt{\\dfrac{10^{5}}{10^{3}}}$$<br>$ = 20\\,m/s$ or $\\sqrt {400} \\,m/s$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37724,'physics',29,1301,3149,'The gas having average speed four times as that of $\\mathrm{SO}_{2}$ (molecular mass 64) is','[{"text": "$C{H_4}$  (molecular mass 16)", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${O_2}$ (molecular mass 32)", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${H_2}$ (molecular mass 2)", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$He$  (molecular mass 4 )", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'As temperature is constant<br>${v_{av}} \\propto \\frac{1}{{\\sqrt M }} \\Rightarrow \\frac{{{v_{Gas}}}}{{{v_{S{O_2}}}}} = \\sqrt {\\frac{{{M_{S{O_2}}}}}{{{M_{Gas}}}}}  \\Rightarrow \\frac{4}{1} = \\sqrt {\\frac{{64}}{{{M_{Gas}}}}} $<br>${M_{Gas}} = 4,i.e.,\\,\\,gas\\,\\,is\\,\\,He.$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37726,'physics',27,1382,3318,'Two identical rods $AC$ and $CB$ made of two different metals having thermal conductivities in the ratio $2: 3$ are kept in contact with each other at the end $C$as shown in the figure. $A$ is at $100^\\circ C$ and $B$ is at $25^\\circ C$. Then the junction $C$ is at','[{"text": "$55^\\\\circ C$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$60^\\\\circ C$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$75^\\\\circ C$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$50^\\\\circ C$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Rate of heat conduction<br>$i=K A \\dfrac{\\left(\\theta_{1}-\\theta_{2}\\right)}{d}$<br>Let temperature of junction $=\\theta$<br>$\\begin{aligned}\\therefore \\quad & \\dfrac{K_{1} A(100-\\theta)}{d}=\\dfrac{K_{2} A(\\theta-25)}{d} \\\\& \\dfrac{K_{1}}{K_{2}}=\\dfrac{\\theta-25}{100-\\theta} \\Rightarrow \\dfrac{2}{3}=\\dfrac{\\theta-25}{100-\\theta}\\end{aligned}$<br>$ \\Rightarrow \\theta  = 55^\\circ C.$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2007,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/27_37726_question_1.png'),
(37727,'physics',26,1136,2717,'If the velocity head of a stream of water is equal to $10\\,cm$ then its speed of flow is approximately','[{"text": "$1.0\\\\,m/s$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1.4\\\\,m/s$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$140\\\\,m/s$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$10\\\\,m/s$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'<br>$\\frac{1}{2}\\rho {v^2} = \\rho gh$<br>$\\therefore v = \\sqrt {2gh}  = \\sqrt {2 \\times 10 \\times 0.1}  = \\sqrt 2 \\,m/s$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37728,'physics',29,1303,3152,'For a gas $\\dfrac{R}{C_{V}}=0.5$. This gas is made up of molecules which are','[{"text": "Diatomic", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Polyatomic", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Monoatomic", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Mixture of diatomic and polyatomic molecules", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'$C_{V}=\\dfrac{R}{0.5}=\\dfrac{R}{\\gamma-1} \\Rightarrow \\gamma=1.5$<br>The given gas is monoatomic<br>',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37778,'physics',27,1382,3323,'A black body at $227^\\circ C$ radiates heat at the rate of $7\\,cal \\cdot c{m^{ - 2}}\\,{s^{ - 1}}.$ At a temperature of $727^\\circ C$, the rate of heat radiated in the same units will be','[{"text": "60", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "50", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "112", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "80", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Apply Stefan\'s law of radiation, $E=e \\sigma A T^{4}$<br>$ \\Rightarrow \\frac{{{E_1}}}{{{E_2}}} = {\\left( {\\frac{{{T_1}}}{{{T_2}}}} \\right)^4}$<br>$ \\Rightarrow {E_2} = 7{\\left[ {\\frac{{273 + 727}}{{273 + 227}}} \\right]^4} = {\\left( {\\frac{{1000}}{{500}}} \\right)^4} \\times 7$<br>$ \\Rightarrow {E_x} = 112\\,cal\\,c{m^{ - 2}}\\,{s^{ - 1}}.$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37729,'physics',28,1371,3283,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> The adiabatic curves intersect each other at a certain point.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Rapid changes of gases can be treated as adiabatic changes','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but reason is correct.", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Adiabatic curves never intersect each other as they are always parallel. Rapid processes are always adiabatic as there is no time for exchange of heat. So option (4) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37730,'physics',27,1382,3318,'Two rods of copper and brass $\\left(K_{C}>K_{B}\\right)$ of same length and area of cross - sectional are joined as shown. End $A$ is kept at $100^\\circ C$ and end $B$ at $0^\\circ C$. The temperature at the junction','[{"text": "Will be more than $50^\\\\circ C$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Will be less than $50^\\\\circ C$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Will be $50^\\\\circ C$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "May be more or less than $50^\\\\circ C$ depending upon the size of rods", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'<br>${K_C} > {K_B}$<br>$\\therefore {R_C} < {R_B}$<br>Temperature difference across copper $ = H{R_C} = {(TD)_C}$<br>And Temperature difference across brass $ = H{R_B} = {(TD)_B}$<br>Heat current $H$ will be same in both, as they are in series<br>Since $R_{C} < R_{B}$<br>$\\therefore {(TD)_C} < {(TD)_B}$<br>or temperature of junction is more than $50^\\circ C$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/27_37730_question_1.png'),
(37731,'physics',26,1136,2717,'The level of water in a tank is $5\\,m$ high. A hole of area $1\\,c{m^2}$ is made at the bottom of the tank. The rate of leakage of water from the hole is $\\left( {g = 10\\,m/{s^2}} \\right)$','[{"text": "${10^{ - 3}}\\\\,{m^3}/s$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${10^{ - 4}}\\\\,{m^3}/s$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$10\\\\,{m^3}/s$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${10^{ - 2}}\\\\,{m^3}/s$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Rate of leakage of water from the hole<br>$ = Av = A\\sqrt {2gh} $<br>$ = {10^{ - 4}}\\sqrt {2 \\times 10 \\times 5} $<br>$ = {10^{ - 3}}\\,{m^3}/s$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37732,'physics',29,1303,3152,'For an ideal gas of diatomic molecules','[{"text": "$C_{P}=\\\\dfrac{5}{2} R$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$C_{V}=\\\\dfrac{3}{2} R$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$C_{P}-C_{V}=2 R$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$C_{P}=\\\\dfrac{7}{2} R$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'$C_{P}=\\left(\\dfrac{f}{2}+1\\right) R=\\left(\\dfrac{5}{2}+1\\right) R=\\dfrac{7}{2} R$<br>',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37734,'physics',27,1382,3319,'Three identical rods $AB$ and $C$ are placed end to end. A temperature difference is maintained between the free ends of $A$ and $C$. The thermal conductivity of $B$ is thrice that of $C$ and half of that of A. The effective thermal conductivity of thesystem will be $\\left( {{K_A}} \\right.$ is the thermal conductivity of rod 1)','[{"text": "$\\\\dfrac{1}{3} K_{A}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$3 \\\\mathrm{~K}_{\\\\mathrm{A}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2 \\\\mathrm{~K}_{\\\\mathrm{A}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{2}{3} K_{A}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'<br><img src=\'/img/data/physics/27_37734_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' /> <br>Let $L$ be length and $A$ be area of cross-section of each rod respectively.<br>Given: ${K_B} = 3\\,{K_C},{K_B} = \\frac{1}{2}{K_A}$<br>Thermal resistance of the rods are<br>${R_A} = \\frac{L}{{{K_A}A}}$<br>${R_B} = \\frac{L}{{{K_B}A}}$<br>${R_C} = \\frac{L}{{{K_C}A}}$<br>The electrical equivalent circuit is as shown in the figure.<br><img src=\'/img/data/physics/27_37734_explanation_2.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' /> <br>Here, the rods are connected in series.<br>$\\therefore {R_S} = {R_A} + {R_B} + {R_C}$<br>Let ${K_{eff{\\rm{ }}}}$ be effective thermal conductivity of the system.<br>$\\begin{aligned}& \\dfrac{3 L}{K_{\\text {eff }} A}=\\dfrac{L}{K_{A} A}+\\dfrac{L}{K_{B} A}+\\dfrac{L}{K_{C} A} \\\\& \\dfrac{3}{K_{\\text {eff }}}=\\dfrac{1}{K_{A}}+\\dfrac{1}{\\dfrac{K_{A}}{2}}+\\dfrac{1}{\\dfrac{K_{A}}{6}} \\\\& \\dfrac{3}{K_{\\text {eff }}}=\\dfrac{1}{K_{A}}[1+2+6] \\\\& \\Rightarrow K_{\\text {eff }}=\\dfrac{3}{9} K_{A}=\\dfrac{1}{3} K_{A}\\end{aligned}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2011,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37735,'physics',26,1136,2717,'The pressure of water in a pipe when tap is closed is $5.5 \\times {10^5}\\,N/{m^2}$. When tap gets open, pressure reduces to $5 \\times {10^5}\\,N/{m^2}$. The velocity with which water comes out on opening the tap is','[{"text": "$10\\\\,m/s$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$5\\\\,m/s$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$20\\\\,m/s$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$15\\\\,m/s$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Decrease in pressure energy $$=$$ increase in kinetic energy<br>${\\text{ or }}\\quad \\Delta p = \\frac{1}{2}\\rho {v^2}$<br>$\\therefore v = \\sqrt {\\frac{{2(\\Delta p)}}{\\rho }} $<br>$ = \\sqrt {\\frac{{2 \\times 0.5 \\times {{10}^5}}}{{{{10}^3}}}} $<br>$ = 10\\,m/s$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37736,'physics',29,1303,3152,'The heat capacity per mole of water is ( $R$ is universal gas constant)','[{"text": "$9 R$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{9}{2} R$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$6 R$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$5 R$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'We treat water like a solid. According to Dulong-Petit law the heat capacity of many solids at room temperature is equal to $3R$. Water molecule has three atoms, two hydrogen and one oxygen.<br>$\\therefore$ Heat capacity per mole of water is<br>$C=9 R \\text {. }$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(39504,'physics',30,1361,3268,'During the phenomenon of resonance','[{"text": "The amplitude of oscillation becomes large", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "The frequency of oscillation becomes large", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "The time period of oscillation becomes large", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "all of the above", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'During the phenomenon of resonance the amplitude of oscillation becomes large.<br>Because applied frequency is equal to natural frequency.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37737,'physics',28,1371,3282,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Change of state is an example of isothermal process.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Change of state from solid to liquid occurs only at melting point of solid and change of state from liquid to gas occurs only at boiling point of liquid. thus, there is no change of temperature during change of state.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Option (1) is correct.',NULL,'Hard','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37738,'physics',27,1382,3317,'A cylindrical metallic rod in thermal contact with two reservoirs of heat at its two ends conducts an amount of heat $Q$ in time t. The metallic rod is melted and the material is formed into a rod of half the radius of the original rod. What is the amount of heat conducted by the new rod when placed in thermal contact with the two reservoirs in time t?','[{"text": "$Q/4$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$Q / 16$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$2Q$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$Q/2$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'In steady state the amount of heat flowing from one face to the other face in time $t$ is given by<br>$Q=\\dfrac{K A\\left(\\theta_{1}-\\theta_{2}\\right) t}{l}$<br>Where $K$ is coefficient of thermal conductivity of material of rod<br>$ \\Rightarrow \\frac{Q}{t} \\propto \\frac{A}{l} \\propto \\frac{{{r^2}}}{l}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\left( 1 \\right)$<br>As the metallic rod is melted and the meterial is formed into a rod of half the radius<br>${V_1} = {V_2} \\Rightarrow \\pi r_1^2{l_1} = \\pi r_2^2{l_2} \\Rightarrow {l_1} = \\frac{{{l_2}}}{4}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\left( 2 \\right)$<br>From Eqs. (1) and (2)<br>$\\begin{aligned}& \\dfrac{Q}{Q_{2}}=\\dfrac{r_{1}^{2}}{l_{1}} \\times \\dfrac{l_{2}}{r_{2}^{2}}=\\dfrac{r_{1}^{2}}{l_{1}} \\times \\dfrac{4 l_{1}}{\\left(r_{1} / 2\\right)^{2}} \\\\& \\Rightarrow Q=16 Q_{2} \\Rightarrow Q_{2}=\\dfrac{Q}{16}\\end{aligned}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37739,'physics',26,1136,2717,'A tank is filled to a height $$H$$. The range of water coming out of a hole which is a depth $H/4$ from the surface of water level is','[{"text": "$\\\\frac{{2H}}{{\\\\sqrt 3 }}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{{\\\\sqrt 3 H}}{2}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\sqrt 3 H$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{{3H}}{4}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'$v = 2\\sqrt {H(H - h)}  = 2\\sqrt {\\frac{H}{3}}  \\times \\frac{{3H}}{4} = \\frac{{\\sqrt 3 H}}{2}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37740,'physics',29,1303,3152,'The value of the gas constant $(R)$ calculated from the perfect gas equation is 8.32 joules $/ \\mathrm{g}$ mole $K$, whereas its value calculated from the knowledge of $C_{P}$ and $C_{V}$ of the gas is $1.98{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} cal/g$ mole of $K$. From this data, the value of $J$ is','[{"text": "$4.18\\\\,\\\\,J/cal$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$4.22\\\\,\\\\,J/cal$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$4.20\\\\,\\\\,J/cal$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$4.16\\\\,\\\\,J/cal$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'We know that $C_{P}-C_{V}=\\dfrac{R}{J}$<br>$ \\Rightarrow \\quad J = \\frac{R}{{{C_P} - {C_V}}}{\\rm{ }}$<br>${C_P} - {C_V} = 1.98\\frac{{cal}}{{g - mol - K}}$<br>$R=8.32 \\dfrac{J}{g-m o l-K}$<br>$\\therefore J = \\frac{{8.32}}{{1.98}} = 4.20\\,\\,J/cal$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37742,'physics',27,1382,3319,'Two rods of same length and material transfer a given amount of heat in $12s$, when they are joined end to end. But when they are joined lengthwise, then they will transfer same heat in same conditions in','[{"text": "$24\\\\,s$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$3\\\\,s$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1.5\\\\,s$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$48\\\\,s$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Let the heat transferred be $Q$<br>When rods are joined end to end. Heat transferred by each rod<br>$ Q = \\frac{{K(2\\,A)\\Delta \\theta }}{l} \\times 12\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\left( 1 \\right)$<br>When rods are joined lengthwise,<br>$Q = \\frac{{KA\\Delta \\theta }}{{2l}}t\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\left( 2 \\right)$<br>From eqs.(1) and (2) we get $t = 48\\,s$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37743,'physics',26,1137,2723,'Eight equal drops of water are falling through air with a steady velocity of $10\\,cm\\,{s^{ - 1}}$. If the drops combine to form a single drop big in size, then the terminal velocity of this big drop is','[{"text": "$40\\\\,cm\\\\,{s^{ - 1}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$10\\\\,cm\\\\,{s^{ - 1}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$30\\\\,cm\\\\,{s^{ - 1}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$80\\\\,cm\\\\,{s^{ - 1}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Let $$r$$ be radius of each small water droplet and $R$ be radius of a big drop.<br>Volume of big drop $$=8 \\times$$ Volume of each<br>${\\text{ small}}\\,{\\text{droplets}}\\frac{4}{3}\\pi {R^3}{\\text{ }} = 8 \\times \\frac{4}{3}\\pi {r^3}R{\\text{ }} = 2r$<br>Let terminal velocity of small droplet be $$v_{1}$$ and of big drop be $$v_{2}$$.<br>Terminal velocity, $$v$$ is<br>where the symbols have their usual meaning<br>$v\\mu {r^2}$<br>$\\frac{{{v_2}}}{{{v_1}}} = \\frac{{r_2^2}}{{r_1^2}} = {\\left( {\\frac{{2r}}{r}} \\right)^2} = 4$<br>$$\\Rightarrow \\quad v_{2}=4 v_{1}$$<br>or $ = 4 \\times 10\\,cm\\,{s^{ - 1}} = 40\\,cm\\,{s^{ - 1}}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2011,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37744,'physics',29,1303,3152,'One mole of monoatomic gas and three moles of diatomic gas are put together in a container. The molar specific heat (in $J K^{-1} \\mathrm{~mol}^{-1}$ ) at constant volume is $\\left( {R = 8.3\\,\\,J{K^{ - 1}}\\,mo{l^{ - 1}}} \\right)$','[{"text": "18.7", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "18.9", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "19.2", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "None of the above", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'$C_{V \\text { mix }}=\\dfrac{n_{1} C_{V_{1}}+n_{2} C_{V_{2}}}{n_{1}+n_{2}}=\\dfrac{1 \\times \\dfrac{3}{2} R+3 \\times \\dfrac{5}{2} R}{1+3}$<br>$ = 18.7J\\,{k^{ - 1}} mo{l^{ - 1}}$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(42713,'physics',36,908,2097,'The inductance of a solenoid $0.5\\,m$ long of cross sectional area $20\\,c{m^2}$ and with 500 turns is:','[{"text": "$12.5\\\\,mH$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1.25\\\\,mH$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$15.0\\\\,mH$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$0.12\\\\,mH$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'$L = \\frac{{{\\mu _0}{N^2}A}}{l} = \\frac{{4\\pi  \\times {{10}^{ - 7}} \\times {{(500)}^2} \\times 20 \\times {{10}^{ - 4}}}}{{0.5}}{\\rm{ }}$<br>$ = 1.25\\,mH$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37746,'physics',28,1371,3283,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> In adiabatic process, work is independent of the path.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> In adiabatic process, work done is equal to negative of change in internal energy.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'In adiabatic process, heat transfer to the thermodynamic system is zero, i.e., $\\Delta Q=0$.<br>Hence, by first law of thermodynamics,<br>$\\begin{gathered}\\Delta Q=0=\\Delta U+\\Delta W \\\\\\therefore \\quad W=-\\Delta U\\end{gathered}$<br>So, work done is equal to negative of change in internal energy.<br>Since, the internal energy is a function of thermodynamic state of the system.<br>It does not depend on the path, therefore work done is also independent of path in adiabatic process.<br>Therefore, Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37747,'physics',26,1137,2723,'The ratio of the terminal velocities of two drops of radii $R$ and $R/2$ is','[{"text": "2", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "1", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$1 / 2$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "4", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'${v_T} \\propto {r^2}$<br>$\\therefore \\frac{{{{\\left( {{v_T}} \\right)}_1}}}{{{{\\left( {{v_T}} \\right)}_2}}} = {\\left( {\\frac{R}{{R/2}}} \\right)^2} = 4$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37748,'physics',29,1303,3152,'Two moles of oxygen is mixed with eight mole of helium. The effective specific heat of the mixture at constant volume is','[{"text": "$1.7{\\\\rm{ }}\\\\,R$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$1.4{\\\\rm{ }}\\\\,R$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1.3{\\\\rm{ }}\\\\,R$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1.9{\\\\rm{ }}\\\\,R$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'For mixture of gases, let specific heat be $C_{V}$<br>$C_{V}=\\dfrac{n_{1}\\left(C_{V}\\right)_{1}+n_{2}\\left(C_{V}\\right)_{2}}{n_{1}+n_{2}}$<br>where $\\left(C_{V}\\right)_{1}=\\dfrac{5 R}{2},\\left(C_{V}\\right)_{2}=\\dfrac{3 R}{2}$<br>$=\\dfrac{2 \\times \\dfrac{5 R}{2}+8 \\times \\dfrac{3 R}{2}}{2+8}=\\dfrac{17 R}{10}=1.7 R$<br>',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37750,'physics',27,1382,3323,'Two stars $A$ and $B$ radiate maximum energy at the wavelengths of $360\\,nm$ and $480\\,nm$ respectively. Then the ratio of the surface temperatures of $A$ and $B$ is','[{"text": "$3: 4$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$81: 256$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$4: 3$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$256: 81$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'<br>According to Wein\'s displacement law<br>${\\lambda _{\\max }}T = $ constant<br>$\\therefore \\frac{{{\\lambda _A}}}{{{\\lambda _B}}} = \\frac{{{T_B}}}{{{T_A}}} \\Rightarrow \\frac{{{T_A}}}{{{T_B}}} = \\frac{{{\\lambda _B}}}{{{\\lambda _A}}} = \\frac{{480\\,nm}}{{360\\,nm}} = \\frac{4}{3}$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2013,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37751,'physics',26,1137,2723,'An air bubbles rises from te bottom of a lake of large depth. The rising speed of air bubble will','[{"text": "go on increasing till it reaches surface", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "go on decreasing till it reaches surface", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "increase in the beginning then will become constant", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "be constant all throughout", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Velocity becomes constant when terminal velocity is attained.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37752,'physics',29,1303,3152,'If $C_{P}$ and $C_{V}$ denote the specific heats of oxygen per unit mass at constant pressure and constant volume respectively. then','[{"text": "$C_{P}-C_{V}=R / 16$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$C_{P}-C_{V}=R / 32$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$C_{P}-C_{V}=R$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$C_{P}-C_{V}=32 R$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'$C_{P}-C_{V}=\\dfrac{R}{M}$<br>Where $C_{P} \\& C_{V}$ are specific heat capacities. $M$ is the molecular weight, $M=32$ for oxygen gas<br>',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37753,'physics',28,1371,3289,'A gas is compressed isothermally to half its initial volume. The same gas is compressed separately through an adiabatic process untill its volume is again reduced to half. Then,','[{"text": "Compressing the gas isothermally will require more work to be done.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Compressing the gas through adiabatic process will require more work to be done.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Compressing the gas isothermally or adiabatically will require the same amount of work.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Which of the case(whether compressing through isothermal or through adiabatic process) requires more work will depend upon the atomicity of the gas.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'${W_{ext}} = $ negative of area with volume-axis<br><img src=\'/img/data/physics/28_37753_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />  <br>$W$ ( adiabatic ) > $W$ ( isothermal )',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2016,'NEET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37754,'physics',27,1382,3322,'A piece of iron is heated in a flame. It first becomes dull red then becomes reddish yellow finally turns to white hot. The correct explanation for the above observation is possible by using.','[{"text": "Stefan\'s law", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Weinn\'s displacement law", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Kirchhoff\'s law", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Newton\'s law of cooling", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Wein\'s displacement law, $\\lambda_{m} T=$ constant<br>$\\Rightarrow \\lambda_{m}=\\dfrac{1}{T}$<br>So as temperature increase $\\lambda$ decrease.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37755,'physics',26,1138,2727,'A $10\\,cm$ long wire is placed horizontally on the surface of water and is gently pulled up with a factor of $2 \\times {10^2}\\,N$ to know the wire in equilibrium. The surface tension, in $N{m^{ - 1}}$ of water is','[{"text": "0.1", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "0.2", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "0.001", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "0.002", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'<br>$T = \\frac{F}{{2l}} = \\frac{{2 \\times {{10}^{ - 2}}}}{{2 \\times 10 \\times {{10}^{ - 2}}}} = 0.1\\,N/m$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37756,'physics',29,1303,3152,'$C_{P}$ and $C_{V}$ are specific heats at constant pressure and constant volume respectively. It is observed that $C_{p}-C_{\\mathrm{v}}=a$ for hydrogen gas $C_{p}-C_{\\mathrm{v}}=b$ for nitrogen gas. The correct relation between $a$ and $\\mathrm{b}$ :','[{"text": "$a = 14\\\\,\\\\,b$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$a = 28\\\\,\\\\,b$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$a = \\\\frac{1}{{14}}b$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$a = b$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Molar specific heat $C^{\\prime}=\\dfrac{\\Delta Q}{n \\Delta T}$<br>Specific heat $C=\\dfrac{\\Delta Q}{m \\Delta T}$<br>$\\begin{aligned}& \\dfrac{C^{\\prime}}{C}=\\dfrac{m}{n}=M(\\text { Molecular weight }) \\\\& C=\\dfrac{C^{\\prime}}{M} \\\\& C_{P}^{\\prime}-C_{V}^{\\prime}=R \\\\& M C_{P}-M C_{V}=R \\\\& C_{P}-C_{V}=\\dfrac{R}{M}\\end{aligned}$<br>For hydrogen $M = 2$<br>$C_{P}-C_{V}=\\dfrac{R}{2}=a$<br>For nitrogen $M = 28$<br>$\\begin{aligned}& \\Rightarrow C_{P}-C_{V}=\\dfrac{R}{28}=b \\\\& \\dfrac{a}{b}=14\\end{aligned}$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37758,'physics',26,1138,2729,'If the angle of contact is less than $$90^{\\circ}$$ the pressure just inside the surface of a meniscus','[{"text": "is less than atmospheric pressure", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "is greater than atmospheric pressure", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "is same as the atmospheric pressure", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "None of the above", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'${p_1} < {p_2}$ for $$\\theta < 90^{\\circ}$$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37759,'physics',27,1382,3323,'The amount of heat energy radiated by a metal at temperature $T$ is $E$. When the temperature is increased to $3T$, energy radiated is','[{"text": "$81E$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$9E$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$3E$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$27E$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'According to Stefan\'s law<br>$E \\propto T^{4}$<br>$\\therefore \\dfrac{E_{1}}{E_{2}}=\\left(\\dfrac{T}{3 T}\\right)^{4}=\\dfrac{1}{81} \\Rightarrow E_{2}=81 E$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37760,'physics',28,1369,3279,'A sample of $0.1\\,g$ of water at $100^\\circ C$ and normal pressure $\\left( {1.013 \\times {{10}^5}\\,N{m^{ - 2}}} \\right)$ requires $54\\,cal$ of heat energy to convert to steam at $100^\\circ C$. If the volume of the steam produced is $167.1\\,cc$, the change in internal energy of the sample, is','[{"text": "$42.2\\\\,J$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$84.5\\\\,J$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$208.7\\\\,J$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$104.3\\\\,J$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Density of water $\\rho  = 1\\,gm/cc$<br>$m = 0.1\\,gm$<br>Initial Volume $ = 0.1\\,c{m^3}$<br>Volume of steam $ = 167.1\\,c{m^3}$<br>$W = P\\Delta V = 1.013 \\times {10^5} \\times 167 \\times {10^{ - 6}} = 16.9\\,\\,J$<br>The heat required is<br>$\\Delta Q = 54\\,cal = 54 \\times 4.18 = 225.7$<br>$\\Delta U = \\Delta Q - W = 208.8\\,J$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2018,'NEET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37761,'physics',29,1302,3150,'A gas mixture consists of 2 moles of oxygen and 4 moles of argon at temperature $T$. Neglecting all vibrational modes, the total internal energy of the system is','[{"text": "$4 R T$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$15 R T$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$9 R T$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$11 R T$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Total internal energy of system <br>$\\begin{aligned}& =U_{\\text {oxygen }}+U_{\\text {argon }}=n_{1} \\dfrac{F_{1}}{2} R T+n_{2} \\dfrac{F_{2}}{2} R T \\\\& =2 \\dfrac{5}{2} R T+4 \\dfrac{3}{2} R T=5 R T+6 R T=11 R T\\end{aligned}$<br>[As $F_{1}=5$ (for oxygen) and $F_{2}=3$ (for argon) $]$.',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37762,'physics',27,1382,3323,'The unit of Stefan\'s constant is','[{"text": "$W\\\\,{m^{ - 2}}\\\\,{K^{ - 1}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$W\\\\,m\\\\,{K^{ - 4}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$W\\\\,{m^{ - 2}}\\\\,{K^{ - 4}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$N\\\\,{m^{ - 2}}\\\\,{K^*}$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'According to Stefan\'s law, the energy radiated per second, $U = \\sigma A{T^4}$<br>$\\therefore \\sigma  = \\frac{U}{{A{T^4}}} = \\frac{W}{{{m^2}{K^4}}} = W{m^{ - 2}}{K^{ - 4}}$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2006,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37763,'physics',26,1138,2728,'The work done in blowing a soap bubble of radius $R$ is $W$. The workdone in blowing a bubble of radius $$2 R$$ of the same soap solution is','[{"text": "$\\\\frac{W}{4}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{W}{2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$4\\\\,W$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$2\\\\,W$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'The workdone in blowing a soap bubble $W = T \\times A$<br>where, $T$ is the surface tension and $A$ is the area of soap bubble<br>$$W=T \\times 4 \\pi R^{2}$$<br>$$W \\propto R^{2}$$<br>So, $$\\dfrac{W_{1}}{W_{2}}=\\dfrac{R_{1}^{2}}{R_{2}^{2}}$$<br>$$\\Rightarrow W_{2}=W_{1}\\left(\\dfrac{2 R}{R}\\right)^{2}=4 W$$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2020,'MHTCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37764,'physics',28,1371,3284,'The volume \\[(V)\\] of a monatomic gas varies with its temperature \\[(T)\\], as shown in the graph. The ratio of work done by the gas, to the heat absorbed by it, when it undergoes a change from state \\[A\\] to state \\[B\\], is<div class="matching-question"></div>','[{"text": "$\\\\dfrac{1}{3}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{2}{7}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{2}{3}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{2}{5}$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'<br><img src=\'/img/data/physics/28_37764_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />   <br>The given process is isobaric.<br>$\\begin{aligned}& W=P \\Delta V=n R \\Delta T \\\\& \\Delta U=\\dfrac{f}{2} n R \\Delta T \\\\& \\Delta Q=\\Delta U+W=\\left(\\dfrac{f}{2}+1\\right) n R \\Delta T\\end{aligned}$<br>Ratio $\\dfrac{W}{\\Delta Q}=\\dfrac{1}{\\dfrac{f}{2}+1}=\\dfrac{2}{f+2}=\\dfrac{2}{5}$<br>For monoatomic gas $f=3$',NULL,'Medium','Graph/Figure',2018,'NEET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/28_37764_question_1.png'),
(37765,'physics',29,1304,3154,'One mole of helium and two moles of hydrogen are mixed at temperature $T$. The total internal energy of the mixture is','[{"text": "$\\\\dfrac{5}{2} R T$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{13 R T}{2}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{10}{2} R T$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{3}{2} R T$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'$U=n_{1} C_{V_{1}} T+n_{2} C_{V_{2}} T=(1) \\dfrac{3}{2} R T+(2) \\dfrac{5}{2} R T$<br>$=\\dfrac{13 R T}{2}$<br>',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37789,'physics',28,1371,3289,'If $\\Delta U$ and $-\\Delta W$ represent the increase in internal energy and work done by the system respectively in a thermodynamic process, which of the following is true?','[{"text": "$\\\\Delta U=-\\\\Delta W$, in an adiabatic process", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\Delta U=\\\\Delta W$, in an isothermal process", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\Delta U=\\\\Delta W$, in an adiabatic process", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\Delta U=-\\\\Delta W$, is an isothermal process", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'From the first law of thermodynamics<br>$Q=\\Delta U+W$<br>For adiabatic process $Q = 0 \\Rightarrow \\Delta U =  - W$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37766,'physics',26,1138,2728,'Two small drops of mercury each of radius $$r$$ coalesce to form a large single drop. The ratio of the total surface energies before and after the change is','[{"text": "$$\\\\sqrt{2}: 1$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$2^{\\\\frac{1}{3}}: 1$$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$$2: 1$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$2^{\\\\frac{2}{3}}: 1$$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'When two small drops of mercury coalesce to form a large drop, then volume remains constant<br>$2 \\times \\frac{{4\\pi {r^3}}}{3} = \\frac{4}{3}\\pi {R^3} \\Rightarrow R = {2^{\\frac{1}{3}}}r\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\left( 1 \\right)$<br>The total surface energy before change is<br>${U_1} = 2 \\times T \\times \\Delta A = 2 \\times T \\times 4\\pi {r^2}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\left( 2 \\right)$<br>and total surface energy after change is<br>${U_2} = T \\times \\Delta A = T \\times 4\\pi {R^2}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\left( 3 \\right)$<br>Dividing eq.(2) by (3), we get<br>$$\\dfrac{U_{1}}{U_{2}}=\\dfrac{2 \\times T \\times 4 \\pi r^{2}}{T \\times 4 \\pi R^{2}}=\\dfrac{2 r^{2}}{2^{\\frac{2}{3}} r^{2}}$$ [using eq .(1)]<br>$$=\\dfrac{2^{\\frac{1}{3}}}{1}$$ or $$2^{\\frac{1}{3}}: 1$$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2020,'MHTCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37767,'physics',27,1382,3323,'The surface temperature of the stars is determined using','[{"text": "Planck\'s law", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Wein\'s displacement law", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Rayleigh-Jeans law", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Kirchhoff\'s law.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'The surface temperature of the stars is determined by Wein\'s displacement law.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2009,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37768,'physics',28,1371,3283,'In which of the following processes, heat is neither absorbed nor released by a system?','[{"text": "Isothermal", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Adiabatic", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Isobaric", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Isochoric", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'In adiabatic process, heat exchange does not take place .',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2019,'NEET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37769,'physics',29,1302,3150,'The average kinetic energy of a helium atom at $30^\\circ C$  is','[{"text": "Less than $1\\\\,\\\\,eV$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "A few $k\\\\,\\\\,eV$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$50 - 60\\\\,\\\\,eV$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$13.6\\\\,\\\\,eV$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Average kinetic energy $E=\\dfrac{F}{2} k T=\\dfrac{3}{2} k T$<br>$ \\Rightarrow E{\\rm{ }} = \\frac{3}{2} \\times \\left( {1.38 \\times {{10}^{ - 23}}} \\right)(273 + 30)$<br>$ = 6.27 \\times {10^{ - 21}} J$<br>$ = 0.039\\,eV < 1\\,eV$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37770,'physics',27,1382,3322,'A black body radiates heat at temperatures $T_{1}$ and $T_{2}\\left(T_{1}>T_{2}\\right)$. The frequency correspoding to maximum energy is','[{"text": "More at $T_{1}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "More at $T_{2}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Equal for $T_{1}$ and $T_{2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Independent of $T_{1}$ and $T_{2}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'If $\\lambda_{m}$ is wavelength corresponding to maximum energy emission for a blackbody at temperature $T$, then according to the Wien\'s displacement law,<br>${\\lambda _m}T = {\\rm{constant}}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\left( 1 \\right)$<br>Since frequency $v_{m}=\\dfrac{c}{\\lambda_{m}}$, using eq. (1),<br>$\\frac{T}{{{v_m}}} = $ constant $\\Rightarrow v_{m} \\propto T$<br>Hence $T_{2}>T_{1} \\Rightarrow v_{2}>v_{1}$.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2015,'MHTCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37771,'physics',26,1138,2728,'Which one of the following statement is correct?','[{"text": "surface energy is potential energy per unit length", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "surface tension is work done per unit area", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "surface tension is work done per unit length", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "surface energy is work done per unit force", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Surface tension is the force applied per unit length or work done (or energy) per unit area of a liquid surface. While surface energy is the amount of work done per unit area by the force.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2019,'MHTCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37772,'physics',28,1371,3290,'Two cylinders $A$ and $B$ of equal capacity are connected to each other via a stop cock. A contains an ideal gas at standard temperature and pressure. $B$ is completely evacuated. The entire system is thermally insulated. The stop cock is suddenly opened. The process is:','[{"text": "adiabatic", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "isochoric", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "isobaric", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "isothermal", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'$\\Delta U=0$. Hence $U_{i}=U_{f} \\Rightarrow \\Delta T=0$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2020,'NEET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37773,'physics',29,1302,3150,'A monoatomic ideal gas occupies a volume of $4{\\rm{ }}\\,{m^3}$  at a pressure of $\\frac{2}{3} \\times {10^6} Pa.$ The energy of the gas:','[{"text": "$4 \\\\times {10^6} \\\\,J$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$6 \\\\times {10^4} \\\\,J$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${10^8} \\\\,J$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$3 \\\\times {10^2} \\\\,J$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Energy of the gas, $E$<br>$ = \\frac{f}{2}nRT = \\frac{f}{2}PV$<br>$ = \\frac{3}{2}\\left( {\\frac{2}{3} \\times {{10}^6}} \\right)(4) = 4 \\times {10^6} J$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37774,'physics',27,1382,3323,'A black body is at $727^\\circ C$. It emits energy at a rate which is proportional to','[{"text": "$(727)^{2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$(1000)^{4}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$(1000)^{2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$(727)^{4}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Amount of heat energy radiated per second by unit area of a black body is directly proportional to fourth power of absolute temperature.<br>According to stefan\'s law,<br>$\\begin{aligned}& E \\propto T^{4} \\\\& \\Rightarrow E \\propto(727+273)^{4} \\Rightarrow E \\propto(1000)^{4}\\end{aligned}$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37775,'physics',26,1138,2728,'A big water drop is formed by the combination of \' $$n$$ \' small water drops of equal radii. The ratio of the surface energy of \' $$n$$ \' drops to the surface energy of big drop is','[{"text": "$$n^{2}: 1$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$n: 1$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$\\\\sqrt{n}: 1$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$\\\\sqrt[3]{n}: 1$$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Volume of big drop $ = n$ ( volume of small drop)<br>$$\\Rightarrow \\dfrac{4}{3} \\pi R^{3}=n \\cdot \\dfrac{4}{3} \\pi r^{3}$$<br>Where $R = $ radius of big water drop<br>$r = $ radius of small water drop<br>$ \\Rightarrow {R^3} = n{r^3}$<br>$ \\Rightarrow R = {n^{1/3}}r\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\left( 1 \\right)$<br>Surface energy of $$n$$ drops.<br>$$E_{2}=n \\times 4 \\pi r^{2} \\times T$$<br>Surface energy of big drop<br>$$\\begin{aligned}& E_{1}=4 \\pi R^{2} T \\\\& \\therefore \\dfrac{E_{2}}{E_{1}}=\\dfrac{n r^{2}}{R^{2}}=\\dfrac{n r^{2}}{\\left(n^{1 / 3} \\cdot r\\right)^{2}} \\\\& =\\dfrac{n r^{2}}{n^{2 / 3} \\cdot r^{2}}=n^{1 / 3} \\quad[\\because \\text { from eq. }(1)] \\\\& E_{2}: E_{1}=\\sqrt[3]{n}: 1\\end{aligned}$$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2017,'MHTCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37779,'physics',26,1138,2728,'A liquid drop having surface energy $$E$$ is spread into 512 droplets of same size. The final surface energy of the droplets is','[{"text": "$2\\\\,E$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$4\\\\,E$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$8\\\\,E$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$12\\\\,E$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'According to question, the surface area of the liquid drop is given by $$A=4 \\pi R^{2}$$ and surface energy is $E$<br>When the drop of splits into 512 droplets, the surface area becomes<br>${A_2} = 512 \\times 4\\pi {r^2}\\quad [r = {\\text{ radius}}\\,{\\text{of}}\\,{\\text{smaller}}\\,{\\text{drop }}]$<br>$r$ can be calculated by comparing the total volume of bigger and all smaller droplets<br>$$\\text { i.e., } \\dfrac{4}{3} \\pi R^{3}=512 \\times 4 \\pi r^{3} \\Rightarrow r=\\dfrac{R}{8}$$<br>Hence, total area of smaller droplets is given by<br>$$A_{1}=512 \\times 4 \\times \\pi \\times\\left(\\dfrac{R}{8}\\right)^{2}=8 A$$<br>Change in surface area is given by $$A_{2}-A_{1}$$<br>$$\\begin{aligned}& =4 \\pi \\times\\left(\\dfrac{512 \\times R^{2}}{64}-R^{2}\\right) \\\\& =4 \\pi\\left(8 R^{2}-R^{2}\\right)=7 R^{2}\\end{aligned}$$<br>Surface energy,$E = AT[A = $ area, $T = $ tension $$]$$<br>So, $$\\dfrac{E_{n}}{E_{o}}=\\dfrac{A_{1} \\times T}{A \\times T}=\\dfrac{8 A}{A}=8$$ <br>$$\\therefore E_{n}=8 E$$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2016,'MHTCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37780,'physics',28,1371,3282,'In which of the processes, does the internal energy of the system remain constant?','[{"text": "Isobaric", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Isothermal", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Adiabatic", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Isochoric", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'The internal energy of a system remains a constant when the temperature does not change.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2008,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37781,'physics',29,1302,3150,'A gas mixture consists of 2 moles of oxygen and 4 moles of argon at temperature T. Assuming the gases to be ideal the total internal energy (in units of RT) of the mixture is :','[{"text": "11", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "13", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "20", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "12", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Total energy of the gas mixture,<br>$E_{\\mathrm{m} i \\mathrm{x}}=\\dfrac{f_{1} n_{1} R T_{1}}{2}+\\dfrac{f_{2} n_{2} R T_{2}}{2}$<br>Given that<br>$\\begin{gathered}n_{1}=2, n_{2}=4 \\\\f_{1}=5, f_{2}=3 \\\\=\\dfrac{5}{2} \\times 2 R T+\\dfrac{3}{2} \\times 4 R T=11 R T\\end{gathered}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37782,'physics',26,1138,2730,'The excess of pressure, due to surface tension, on a spherical liquid drop of radius \' $$R$$ \' is proportional to','[{"text": "$$R^{-1}$$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "R", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$R^{-2}$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$R^{2}$$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'The excess of pressure due to surface tension on a spherical liquid drop is given by<br>$p = \\frac{{2T}}{R}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\left( 1 \\right)$<br>Where, $T = $ surface tension and<br>$R = $ radius of liquid drop So, from eq.(1), we get<br>$$p \\propto \\dfrac{1}{R} \\text { or } p \\propto R^{-1}$$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2019,'MHTCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37783,'physics',27,1382,3323,'If the radius of a star is $R$ and it acts as a black body, what would be the temperature of the star, in which the rate of energy production is $Q$? (where $\\sigma$ is Stefan\'s constant)','[{"text": "$\\\\left(\\\\dfrac{Q}{4 \\\\pi R^{2} \\\\sigma}\\\\right)^{1 / 4}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{Q}{4 \\\\pi R^{2} \\\\sigma}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\left(\\\\dfrac{Q}{4 \\\\pi R^{2} \\\\sigma}\\\\right)$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\left(4 \\\\pi R^{2} \\\\sigma / \\\\sigma\\\\right)^{1 / 4}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'If $R$ is the of radius of the star and at its temperature, then the energy emitted by the star per second through radiation in accordance with Stefan\'s law will be given by<br>$Q=4 \\pi R^{2} \\sigma T^{4} \\Rightarrow T=\\left(\\dfrac{Q}{4 \\pi R^{2} \\sigma}\\right)^{1 / 4}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37784,'physics',28,1369,3279,'In a given process of an ideal gas, $dW = 0,\\,\\,dQ < 0$ then for the gas','[{"text": "The temperature will decrease", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "The volume will increase", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "The pressure will remain constant", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "The temperature will increase", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'From first law of thermodynamics<br>$d Q=d U+d W$<br>$dQ = dU$ (Given $dw = 0$)<br>Since, $dQ < 0 \\Rightarrow dU < 0$<br>Hence, temperature will decrease',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37785,'physics',29,1304,3154,'Three perfect gases at absolute temperatures $T_{1}, T_{2}$ and $T_{3}$ are mixed. The masses of molecules are $m_{1}, m_{2}$ and $m_{3}$ and number of molecules are $n_{1}, n_{2}$ and $n_{3}$ respectively. Assuming no loss of energy, the final temperature of the mixture is:','[{"text": "$\\\\dfrac{n_{1} T_{1}+n_{2} T_{2}+n_{3} T_{3}}{n_{1}+n_{2}+n_{3}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{n_{1} T_{1}^{2}+n_{2} T_{2}^{2}+n_{3} T_{3}^{2}}{n_{1} T_{1}+n_{2} T_{2}+n_{3} T_{3}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{n_{1}^{2} T_{1}^{2}+n_{2}^{2} T_{2}^{2}+n_{3}^{2} T_{3}^{2}}{n_{1} T_{1}+n_{2} T_{2}+n_{3} T_{3}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{\\\\left(T_{1}+T_{2}+T_{3}\\\\right)}{3}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Number of moles of first gas $=\\dfrac{n_{1}}{N_{A}}$<br>Number of of moles of second gas $=\\dfrac{n_{2}}{N_{A}}$<br>Number of of moles of third gas $=\\dfrac{n_{3}}{N_{A}}$<br>By conserving total internal energy<br>$\\begin{gathered}U_{f}=U_{i} \\\\\\dfrac{n_{1}}{N_{A}} R T_{1}+\\dfrac{n_{2}}{N_{A}} R T_{2}+\\dfrac{n_{3}}{N_{A}} R T_{3}=\\dfrac{n_{1}+n_{2}+n_{3}}{N_{A}} R T_{\\text {mix }} \\\\T_{m i x}=\\dfrac{n_{1} T_{1}+n_{2} T_{2}+n_{3} T_{3}}{n_{1}+n_{2}+n_{3}}\\end{gathered}$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37786,'physics',26,1138,2728,'The surface tension of a soap solution is $2 \\times {10^{ - 2}}\\,N/m$. To blow a bubble of radius $1\\,cm$, the work done is','[{"text": "$4\\\\pi  \\\\times {10^{ - 6}}J$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$8\\\\pi  \\\\times {10^{ - 6}}J$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$12\\\\pi  \\\\times {10^{ - 6}}J$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$16\\\\pi  \\\\times {10^{ - 6}}J$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'<br>$W = 8\\pi {R^2}\\,T = 8 \\times \\pi  \\times {\\left( {{{10}^{ - 2}}} \\right)^2} \\times 2 \\times {10^{ - 2}}$<br>$ = 16\\pi  \\times {10^{ - 6}}\\,J$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37787,'physics',27,1382,3323,'The temperature of body is increased from $27^\\circ C$ to $127^\\circ C$. The radiation emitted by it increase by a factor of','[{"text": "$\\\\dfrac{256}{81}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{15}{9}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{4}{5}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{12}{27}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'$E \\propto {T^4},\\,{T_1} = 300\\,K,\\,{T_2} = 400\\,K$<br>$\\frac{{{E_2}}}{{{E_1}}} = {\\left( {\\frac{{{T_2}}}{{{T_1}}}} \\right)^4}$<br>$ = {\\left( {\\frac{{400}}{{300}}} \\right)^4}$<br>$ = \\frac{{256}}{{81}}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37788,'physics',29,1302,3150,'The average translational kinetic energy of ${O_2}$ (molar mass 32) at a particular temperature is $0.048\\,eV$. The average translational kinetic energy of $N_{2}$ (molar mass 28) molecules in $eV$ at the same temperature is','[{"text": "0.003", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "0.0015", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "0.768", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "0.048", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'$K \\cdot E=\\dfrac{3}{2} R T$<br>K.E is independent of molecular mass for a diatomic gas.<br>So $K.E$ of ${N_2}$ gas is $0.048\\,eV$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37791,'physics',27,1382,3323,'Two spheres of the same material have radii $1\\,m$ and $4\\,m$, temperatures $4000\\,K$ and $2000\\,K$ respectively. Then the ratio of energy radiated per second by the first sphere as compared to that by the second is','[{"text": "$4: 1$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2: 1$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1: 1$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$1: 4$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'<br>$\\frac{{{P_1}}}{{{P_2}}} = \\frac{{{A_1}\\,T_1^4}}{{\\,{A_2}\\,T_2^4}} = \\frac{{4\\pi {{(1)}^2}{{(4000)}^4}}}{{4\\pi {{(4)}^2}{{(2000)}^4}}} = \\frac{1}{1}$<br>${\\rm{or }}\\,\\,\\,{\\mkern 1mu} {\\kern 1pt} {P_1} = {P_2}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37792,'physics',28,1371,3289,'An ideal gas is compressed to $\\dfrac{1}{4} t h$ its initial volume by means of several process. Which of the process results in the maximum work done on the gas?','[{"text": "Isobaric", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Adiabatic", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Isobaric", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Isochoric", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'<br><img src=\'/img/data/physics/28_37792_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />  <br>Work done $=$ Negative of area under $P - V$ curve So, largest work takes place in adiabatic process.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37793,'physics',29,1304,3154,'Cubical tanks $X$ and $Y$ have the same volumes and share a common fixed perfectly conducting wall. There is $1 \\mathrm{gm}$ of helium in tank $X$ and 2 gm of helium in tank $Y$. Which of the following is the same for both samples treating them as ideal gases?','[{"text": "The number of molecular collisions per second on the common wall", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "The average speed of the molecules", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "The pressure exerted by the helium", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "The density of the helium", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'${v_{avg{\\rm{ }}}} = \\sqrt {\\frac{{8RT}}{{\\pi M}}} $ (T- same for both gases)<br>Pressure and density depend on number of particles in the container.<br>So ${v_{avg}}$ is same for both gases',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/29_37793_question_1.jpg'),
(37794,'physics',26,1138,2729,'If a water drop is kept between two glass plates, then its shape is','[{"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "None of this", "isCorrect": false}]',2,NULL,NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37795,'physics',27,1382,3323,'The area of the hole of heat furnace is ${10^{ - 4}}\\,{m^2}$. It radiates $1.58 \\times {10^5}cal$ of heat per hour. If the emissivity of the furnace is 0.80 , then its temperature is','[{"text": "$1500\\\\,K$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2000\\\\,K$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2500\\\\,K$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$3000\\\\,K$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'According to Stefan\'s law $E=\\sigma \\varepsilon A T^{4}$<br>$ \\Rightarrow \\frac{{1.58 \\times {{10}^5} \\times 4.2}}{{60 \\times 60}} = 5.67 \\times {10^{ - 8}} \\times {10^{ - 4}} \\times 0.8 \\times {T^4}$<br>$T = 2500\\,K$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37796,'physics',29,1304,3154,'Figure shows two flasks connected to each other. The volume of the flask 1 is twice that of flask 2. The system is filled with an ideal gas at temperature $100 \\mathrm{~K}$ and $200 \\mathrm{~K}$ respectively. If the mass of the gas in 1 be $m$ then what is the mass of the gas in flask 2','[{"text": "$m / 4$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$m / 2$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$m$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$m / 8$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'$P V=\\dfrac{m}{M} R T$<br>As the gas is in equilibrium the pressure in both flasks must be same.<br>$\\begin{aligned}& \\Rightarrow V \\propto m T \\Rightarrow \\dfrac{V_{1}}{V_{2}}=\\dfrac{m_{1}}{m_{2}} \\cdot \\dfrac{T_{1}}{T_{2}} \\\\& \\Rightarrow \\dfrac{2 V}{V}=\\dfrac{m}{m_{2}} \\times \\dfrac{100}{200} \\Rightarrow m_{2}=\\dfrac{m}{4}\\end{aligned}$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/29_37796_question_1.jpg'),
(37797,'physics',28,1372,3293,'A gas at pressure $6 \\times {10^5}N{m^{ - 2}}$ and volume $1\\,{m^3}$ and its pressure falls to $4 \\times {10^5}N{m^{ - 2}}$ when its volume is $3\\,{m^3}$. Given that the indicator diagram is a straight line, work done by the system is','[{"text": "$6 \\\\times {10^5}\\\\,J$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$3 \\\\times {10^5}\\\\,J$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$4 \\\\times {10^5}\\\\,J$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$10 \\\\times {10^5}\\\\,J$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'<br><img src=\'/img/data/physics/28_37797_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />  <br>Work done by the system $=$ area of rectangle $BCDE + $ area of $\\Delta ABC$<br>$=4 \\times 10^{5} \\times 2+\\dfrac{2 \\times 10^{5} \\times 2}{2}$<br>$W = 10 \\times {10^5}\\,J$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37798,'physics',27,1382,3323,'A spherical black body with a radius of $12\\,cm$ radiates $440\\,W$ power at $500\\,K$. If the radius were halved and the temperature doubled, the power radiated in watt would be','[{"text": "440", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "1320", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "880", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "1760", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Radiated power by black body<br>$P = \\frac{Q}{T} = A\\sigma {T^4}$<br>$ \\Rightarrow P \\propto A{T^4} \\propto {r^2}\\,{T^4}$<br>$ \\Rightarrow \\frac{{{P_1}}}{{{P_2}}} = {\\left( {\\frac{{{r_1}}}{{{r_2}}}} \\right)^2}{\\left( {\\frac{{{T_1}}}{{\\,{T_2}}}} \\right)^4}$<br>$ \\Rightarrow \\frac{{440}}{{{P_2}}} = {\\left( {\\frac{{12}}{6}} \\right)^2}{\\left( {\\frac{{500}}{{1000}}} \\right)^4}$<br>$ \\Rightarrow {P_2} = 1760\\,W$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37799,'physics',26,1138,2730,'An air bubble of radius $1mm$ is formed inside water at a depth $10\\,m$ below free surface ( where air pressure  is ${10^5}N/{m^2}$ ). The pressure inside the bubble is (surface tension of water =$2 \\times {10^{ - 7}}N/m$ ).','[{"text": "$2.28 \\\\times {10^5}N/{m^2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2.0028 \\\\times {10^5}N/{m^2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2.14 \\\\times {10^5}N/{m^2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2.0014 \\\\times {10^5}N/{m^2}$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Pressure inside the bubble<br>$$=$$ Pressure outside it $ + \\frac{{2\\,T}}{r}$<br>$ = \\left( {{p_0} + \\rho gh} \\right) + \\frac{{2\\,T}}{r}$<br>$ = {10^5} + \\left( {{{10}^3} \\times 10 \\times 10} \\right) + \\frac{{2 \\times 7 \\times {{10}^{ - 2}}}}{{{{10}^{ - 3}}}}$<br>$ = 2.0014 \\times {10^5}\\,N/{m^2}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37800,'physics',29,1304,3154,'Two identical containers joined by a small pipe initially contain the same gas at pressure $p_{0}$ and absolute temperature $T_{0}$. One container is now maintained at the same temperature while the other is heated to $2 T_{0}$. The common pressure of the gases will be','[{"text": "$\\\\dfrac{4}{3} p_{0}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{3}{2} p_{0}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2 p_{0}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{5}{3} p_{0}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Final pressure in the containers will be the same<br>$\\dfrac{P V_{A}}{R T_{A}}+\\dfrac{P V_{B}}{R T_{B}}=\\dfrac{P_{0} V_{A}}{R T_{0}}+\\dfrac{P_{0} V_{B}}{R T_{0}}$<br><br>$\\begin{aligned}& \\Rightarrow \\dfrac{P}{2 T_{0}}+\\dfrac{P}{T_{0}}=\\dfrac{P_{0}}{T_{0}}+\\dfrac{P_{0}}{T_{0}} \\quad\\left[\\because V_{A}=V_{B}\\right] \\\\& \\Rightarrow P=\\dfrac{4 P_{0}}{3}\\end{aligned}$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37802,'physics',26,1138,2731,'In a capillary tube having area of cross - section A, water rises to height $h$. If cross - sectional area is reduced to $\\frac{A}{9}$, the rise of water in the capillary tube is','[{"text": "4$h$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "3$h$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "2$h$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$h$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'For a capillary tube,<br>$rh = $ constant<br>where, $$r=$$ radius of capillary tube<br>and $$h=$$ height of rised water in capillary tube According to the question<br>$$r_{1} h_{1}=r_{2} h_{2}$$<br>$ \\Rightarrow \\frac{{{r_1}}}{{{r_2}}} = \\frac{{{h_2}}}{{{h_1}}}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\left( 1 \\right)$<br>In the first condition,<br>${A_1} = \\pi r_1^2\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\left( 2 \\right)$<br>In the second condition,<br>${A_2} = \\pi r_2^2{\\text{ }}$<br>${\\rm{or }}\\frac{A}{9} = \\pi r_2^2\\left[ {{\\rm{ as}},{\\rm{ }}{A_2} = \\frac{A}{9}} \\right]\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\left( 3 \\right)$<br>On dividing eq.(2) by (3), we get<br>$$9=\\dfrac{r_{1}^{2}}{r_{2}^{2}} \\operatorname{or} \\dfrac{r_{1}}{r_{2}}=\\sqrt{9} \\Rightarrow \\dfrac{r_{1}}{r_{2}}=3$$<br>from eq.(1), we get<br>$$\\dfrac{h_{2}}{h_{1}}=3 \\Rightarrow h_{2}=3 h_{1}$$<br><br>$$h_{2}=3 h$$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37804,'physics',29,1300,3145,'Volume - temperature graph at atmospheric pressure for a monoatomic gas \\[\\left( {V\\,\\,in\\,\\,{m^3},T\\,\\,in{\\text{ }}^\\circ C} \\right)\\] is<div class="matching-question"></div>','[{"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'The ideal gas equation is<br>$PV = n\\,RT$<br>When $P$ is constant<br>$V=\\left(\\dfrac{n R}{P}\\right) T$<br>Where $T$ is in kelvin<br>$T=T_{C}+273$<br>$T_{C}$ - Temperature in degree centigrade<br>$V=\\left(\\dfrac{n R}{P}\\right)\\left(T_{C}+273\\right)$<br>The graph between $V \\& T_{C}$ is a straight line having positive slope and it does not pass through origin',NULL,'Easy','Graph/Figure',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37805,'physics',28,1372,3294,'An ideal gas is taken via path \\(A B C A\\) as shown in figure. The net workdone in the whole cycle is<div class="matching-question"></div>','[{"text": "$6\\\\,{P_1}\\\\,{V_1}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "zero", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$3\\\\,{P_1}\\\\,{V_1}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$ - 3\\\\,{P_1}\\\\,{V_1}$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'The work done in a $PV$ diagram is the area enclosed.<br>The work done $ = 1/2\\left( {3\\,{V_1} - {V_1}} \\right) \\cdot \\left( {4{P_1} - {P_1}} \\right)$<br>$ \\Rightarrow W =  - 3{P_1}\\,{V_1}$<br>If the direction of change is anti clockwise then work done is negative.',NULL,'Medium','Graph/Figure',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/28_37805_question_1.png'),
(37807,'physics',26,1138,2731,'Two capillary tubes $P$ and $Q$ are dipped vertically in water. The height of water level in capillary tube $P$ is  $\\frac{{{2^{nd}}}}{3}$ of the height in capillary tube $Q$. The ratio of their diameter is ____','[{"text": "$2\\\\,:\\\\,3$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$3\\\\,:\\\\,2$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$3\\\\,:\\\\,4$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$4\\\\,:\\\\,3$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'<br>$$\\begin{gathered}h=\\dfrac{2 T \\cos \\theta}{\\rho r g} \\\\\\Rightarrow \\quad \\dfrac{h_{p}}{h_{q}}=\\dfrac{r_{q}}{r_{p}} \\\\\\dfrac{\\dfrac{2}{3} h_{q}}{h_{q}}=\\dfrac{r_{q}}{r_{p}} \\\\\\Rightarrow \\dfrac{r_{q}}{r_{p}}=\\dfrac{2}{3} \\\\\\Rightarrow \\dfrac{d_{p}}{d_{q}}=\\dfrac{3}{2}\\end{gathered}$$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37808,'physics',28,1369,3279,'An electric heater supplies heat to a system at a rate of $120 \\mathrm{~W}$. If system performs work at a rate of $80\\,J{s^{ - 1}}$','[{"text": "$30\\\\,J{s^{ - 1}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$40\\\\,J{s^{ - 1}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$50\\\\,J{s^{ - 1}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$60\\\\,J{s^{ - 1}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'According to first Law of thermodynamic $\\Delta Q=\\Delta U+\\Delta W$<br>$\\therefore \\dfrac{\\Delta Q}{\\Delta t}=\\dfrac{\\Delta U}{\\Delta t}+\\dfrac{\\Delta W}{\\Delta t}$<br>Here, $\\frac{{\\Delta Q}}{{\\Delta t}} = 120W,\\frac{{\\Delta W}}{{\\Delta t}} = 80J{s^{ - 1}}$<br>$\\therefore \\frac{{\\Delta U}}{{\\Delta t}} = 120 - 80 = 40\\,J{s^{ - 1}}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37809,'physics',29,1300,3145,'Which one the following graphs represents the behaviour of an ideal gas at constant temperature<div class="matching-question"></div>','[{"text": "", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'For an ideal gas at constant temperature $PV=$ copnstant i.e . PV doesn\'t vary with $V.$',NULL,'Easy','Graph/Figure',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37810,'physics',27,1380,3308,'Two resistances of equal magnitude $R$ and having temperature coefficient $\\alpha$ respectively are connected in parallel. The temperature coefficient of the parallel combination is, approximately:','[{"text": "$\\\\dfrac{\\\\alpha}{4}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2 \\\\alpha$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{\\\\alpha}{2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\alpha$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'As the resistors are connected in parallel<br>$\\begin{aligned}& \\dfrac{1}{R_{e q}}=\\dfrac{1}{R_{1}}+\\dfrac{1}{R_{2}} \\\\& \\dfrac{1}{R_{e q}}=\\dfrac{1}{R(1+\\alpha \\Delta T)}+\\dfrac{1}{R(1+\\alpha \\Delta T)} \\\\& \\quad \\dfrac{1}{R_{e q}}=\\dfrac{1}{R}[2-(2 \\alpha) \\Delta T] \\\\& R_{e q}=\\dfrac{R}{[2-(2 \\alpha) \\Delta T]} \\\\& R_{e q}=R[2-(2 \\alpha) \\Delta T]^{-1}=R[2+(2 \\alpha) \\Delta T] \\\\& R_{e q}=2 R[1+\\alpha \\Delta T] \\\\& =2 R\\left[1+\\alpha_{e q} \\Delta T\\right] \\\\& \\Rightarrow \\alpha_{e q}=\\alpha\\end{aligned}$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37811,'physics',26,1138,2731,'Water rises in a capillary tube of radius r upto a height h. The mass of water in a capillary is m. The mass of water that will rise in a capillary of radius $\\frac{r}{4}$ will be','[{"text": "$4\\\\,m$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{m}{4}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$m$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{4}{m}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'The height of water column in a capillary tube is given by<br>$h = \\frac{{2T\\cos \\theta }}{{\\rho rg}}$<br>$ \\Rightarrow h \\propto \\frac{1}{r}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\left( 1 \\right)$<br>Also, mass of liquid in a column of radius $$r$$ and height $$h$$, is<br>$m = \\left( {\\pi {r^2}h} \\right)\\rho $<br>$ \\Rightarrow m \\propto {r^2}h\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\left( 2 \\right)$<br>From eq.(1) and (2), we get<br>$$\\begin{aligned}& m \\propto r^{2} \\times \\dfrac{1}{r} \\\\& m \\propto r \\\\& \\Rightarrow \\dfrac{m^{\\prime}}{m}=\\dfrac{\\dfrac{r}{4}}{r} \\Rightarrow m^{\\prime}=\\dfrac{m}{4}\\end{aligned}$$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2020,'MHTCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37812,'physics',29,1300,3145,'Under constant temperature, graph between \\(P\\) and \\[1/V\\]  is<div class="matching-question"></div>','[{"text": "Hyperbola", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Parabola", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Circle", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Straight line", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'At constant temperature $PV = $ constant $ \\Rightarrow P \\propto \\frac{1}{V}.$<br> So the graph between ${\\rm{ }}P$ and  $\\frac{1}{V}$  is a straight line.',NULL,'Easy','Graph/Figure',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37813,'physics',28,1372,3294,'A gas is taken through the cycle \\[A \\to B \\to C \\to A\\], as shown in the figure.<br>What is the net work done by the gas?<div class="matching-question"></div>','[{"text": "$2000 \\\\mathrm{~J}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1000 \\\\mathrm{~J}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Zero", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$-2000 \\\\mathrm{~J}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Work done in $P - V$ diagram is equal to area enclosed in $P - V$ curve <br>$ \\Rightarrow W = \\frac{1}{2} \\times 5 \\times {10^{ - 3}} \\times 4 \\times {10^5} = 1000\\,\\,J$',NULL,'Medium','Graph/Figure',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/28_37813_question_1.png'),
(37814,'physics',26,1138,2731,'Water rises in plant fibers due to','[{"text": "capillarity", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "viscosity", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "fluid pressure", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "osmosis", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Water rises in plant fibers due to capillarity.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2007,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37815,'physics',27,1380,3306,'A planar frame made of thin uniform rods, the length of section $A B$ and $E F$ is $l_{1}$ and its thermal linear coefficient of expansion is $\\alpha_{1}$. The length of section $C D$ is $l_{2}$ and its thermal linear coefficient of expansion is $\\alpha_{2} . C B$ and $D E$ are of same length having thermal linear coefficient of expansion $\\alpha_{2}$. Points $A, B, E$ and $F$ reside on same line, that is, sections $A B$ and $E F$ overlap. Then the ratio of $\\dfrac{l_{1}}{l_{2}}$, for which the distance between end $A$ and $F$ remains the same at all temperatures, is:','[{"text": "$\\\\dfrac{2 \\\\alpha_{1}}{\\\\alpha_{2}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{2 \\\\alpha_{2}}{\\\\alpha_{1}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{\\\\alpha_{2}}{2 \\\\alpha_{1}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{\\\\alpha_{1}}{2 \\\\alpha_{2}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'For distance between $A$ and $F$ to remain constant, extension in $C D=$ extension in $A B+$ extension in $E F$<br>$\\begin{aligned}& \\therefore \\quad \\Delta l_{2}=2 \\Delta l_{1} \\Rightarrow l_{2} \\alpha_{2} \\Delta \\theta=2 l_{1} \\alpha_{1} \\Delta \\theta \\\\& \\Rightarrow \\quad \\dfrac{l_{1}}{l_{2}}=\\dfrac{\\alpha_{2}}{2 \\alpha_{1}}\\end{aligned}$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/27_37815_question_1.png'),
(37816,'physics',28,1371,3289,'$310\\,J$ of heat is required to raise the temperature of 2 moles of an ideal gas at constant pressure from $25^\\circ C$ to $35^\\circ C$. The amount of heat required to raise the temperature of the gas through the same range at constant volume is','[{"text": "$384\\\\,J$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$144\\\\,J$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$276\\\\,J$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$452\\\\,J$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'At constant pressure, $\\Delta Q = n{C_p}\\Delta T$<br>$ \\Rightarrow 310 = 2 \\times {C_P} \\times (35 - 25) = 20{C_P}$ or ${C_P} = \\frac{{310}}{{20}}$<br>At constant volume, heat required $ = n{C_V}\\Delta T$<br>$\\Delta Q = 2 \\times \\left( {{C_P} - R} \\right) \\times (35 - 25)$<br>$\\Delta Q = 2 \\times (15.5 - 8.3) \\times 10$<br>$\\Delta Q = 2 \\times 7.2 \\times 10 = 144\\,\\,J$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2007,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37817,'physics',29,1300,3145,'Pressure versus temperature graph of an ideal gas of equal number of moles of different volumes is plotted as shown in Fig.<div class="matching-question"></div>','[{"text": "$V_{1}=V_{2}, V_{3}=V_{4}$ and $V_{2} < V_{3}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$V_{1}=V_{2}, V_{3}=V_{4}$ and $V_{2}>V_{3}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$V_{4}>V_{3}>V_{2}>V_{1}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$V_{1}=V_{2}=V_{3}=V_{4}$<br>", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'As the slope is constant for any line<br>${V_1} = {V_2}\\,\\,\\& \\,\\,{V_3} = {V_4}$<br>From the given graph it can be observed that the slope of line 34 is more than the slope of line 12<br>$\\begin{aligned}& m_{34}>m_{12} \\\\& \\dfrac{n R}{V_{34}}>\\dfrac{n R}{V_{12}} \\\\& V_{12}>V_{34}\\end{aligned}$<br>$ \\Rightarrow \\left( {{V_1} = {V_2}} \\right) > \\left( {{V_3} = {V_4}} \\right)$',NULL,'Medium','Graph/Figure',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/29_37817_question_1.png'),
(37831,'physics',26,1133,2707,'A narrow tube completely filled with a liquid is lying on a series of tyres as shown in figure. Assuming no sliding between any surfaces, the value of acceleration of the cylinders for which liquid will not come out of the tube from anywhere is given by','[{"text": "$$\\\\dfrac{g H}{L}$$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$$\\\\dfrac{g H}{2 L}$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$\\\\dfrac{g H}{\\\\sqrt{2} L}$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$\\\\dfrac{2 g H}{L}$$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'If the liquid does not come out then the pressure exerted by vertical and horizontal columns must be balanced at the bending point.<br>$$(\\rho a L) A=(\\rho g H) A$$<br>$$a=\\dfrac{g H}{L}$$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/26_37831_question_1.png'),
(37818,'physics',27,1380,3306,'A copper and a tungsten plate having a thickness $d$ each are riveted together so that at $0^\\circ C$ they form a flat bimetallic plate. Find the average radius of the curvature of this plate at temperature T. The coefficient of linear expansion for Copper and tungsten are $\\alpha_{C u}$ and $\\alpha_{t}$.','[{"text": "$\\\\delta \\\\dfrac{\\\\left[2+\\\\left(\\\\alpha_{c}+\\\\alpha_{t}\\\\right) T\\\\right]}{\\\\left[2\\\\left(\\\\alpha_{c}-\\\\alpha_{t}\\\\right) T\\\\right]}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\delta \\\\dfrac{\\\\left[1+\\\\left(\\\\alpha_{c}+\\\\alpha_{t}\\\\right) T\\\\right]}{\\\\left[\\\\left(\\\\alpha_{c}-\\\\alpha_{t}\\\\right) T\\\\right]}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\delta \\\\dfrac{\\\\left[2+\\\\left(\\\\alpha_{c}+\\\\alpha_{t}\\\\right) T\\\\right]}{\\\\left[\\\\left(\\\\alpha_{c}-\\\\alpha_{t}\\\\right) T\\\\right]}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\delta \\\\dfrac{\\\\left[1+\\\\left(\\\\alpha_{c}+\\\\alpha_{t}\\\\right) T\\\\right]}{\\\\left[2\\\\left(\\\\alpha_{c}-\\\\alpha_{t}\\\\right) T\\\\right]}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'The length of copper plate at a temperature $T$ is $l_{C}=l_{0}\\left(1+\\alpha_{C} T\\right)$, where $l_{0}$ is the length of copper plate at $0^\\circ C$. The length of the tungsten plate is $l_{t}=l_{0}\\left(1+\\alpha_{t} T\\right)$ <img src=\'/img/data/physics/27_37818_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' /> <br>Length of copper plate $l_{C}=\\phi(R+\\delta / 2)$<br>Consequently,<br>$\\phi (R + \\delta /2) = {l_0}\\left( {1 + {\\alpha _C}T} \\right)\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\left( 1 \\right)$<br>Similarly for tungsten plate<br>$\\phi (R - \\delta /2) = {l_0}\\left( {1 + {\\alpha _t}T} \\right)\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\left( 2 \\right)$<br>To eliminate the unknown quantities, and $l_{0}$ we divide the equation (1) by (2)<br>$\\begin{aligned}& \\Rightarrow \\dfrac{(R+\\delta / 2)}{(R-\\delta / 2)}=\\dfrac{\\left(1+\\alpha_{C} T\\right)}{\\left(1+\\alpha_{t} T\\right)} \\\\& \\Rightarrow R=\\delta \\dfrac{\\left[2+\\left(\\alpha_{c}+\\alpha_{t}\\right) T\\right]}{\\left[2\\left(\\alpha_{c}-\\alpha_{t}\\right) T\\right]}\\end{aligned}$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37819,'physics',26,1138,2731,'A liquid rises to a height of 1.8$cm$ in a glass capillary $A$. Another glass capillary $B$ having diameter 90% of capillary $A$ is immersed in the same liquid. The rise of liquid in capillary $B$ is','[{"text": "$1.4\\\\,cm$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1.8\\\\,cm$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2.0\\\\,cm$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$2.2\\\\,cm$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Height of liquid in a capillary tube,<br>$$h=\\dfrac{2 S \\cos \\theta}{r \\rho g}$$<br>where $S = $ surface tension<br>$$\\rho=$$ density of the liquid<br>$$r=$$ radius of the capillary tube<br>For capillary $A$, ${h_A} = \\frac{{2S\\cos \\theta }}{{{r_A}\\rho g}}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\left( 1 \\right)$<br>For capillary $B,\\,{h_B} = \\frac{{2S\\cos \\theta }}{{{r_B}\\rho g}}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\left( 2 \\right)$<br>from eq.(1) and (2), we get<br>${h_A}{r_A} = {h_B}{r_B}$<br>$ \\Rightarrow (1.8\\,cm)\\left( {{r_A}} \\right) = \\left( {{h_B}} \\right)\\left( {\\frac{{90}}{{100}}{r_A}} \\right){\\text{ }}$<br>${\\text{or }}(1.8\\,cm) = {h_B} \\times \\frac{9}{{10}}{\\text{ }}$<br>${\\text{or }}{h_B} = \\frac{{10}}{9} \\times 1.8\\,cm = 2\\,cm$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2015,'MHTCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37820,'physics',28,1371,3285,'What amount of heat must be supplied to $35\\,g$ of oxygen at room temperature to raise its temperature. by $80^\\circ C$ at constant volume (molecular mass of oxygen is 32 and $R = 8.3\\,J\\,mo{l^{ - 1}}{k^{ - 1}}$)','[{"text": "$1.52\\\\,kJ$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$3.23\\\\,kJ$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1.81\\\\,kJ$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$1.62\\\\,kJ$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Here mass of oxygen $(\\mathrm{m})=35 \\mathrm{~g}$, molar mass of $\\mathrm{O}_{2}(\\mathrm{M})=32 \\mathrm{~g} \\mathrm{~mol}^{-1}$ rise in temperature, $\\Delta T = 80^\\circ C$<br>$\\therefore$ number of moles $n = \\frac{m}{M} = \\frac{{35}}{{32}} = 1.09\\,mol$ <br>As oxygen is a diatomic gas, then molar specific heat at constant volume is $C_{v}=\\dfrac{5}{2} R$ and amount of heat supplied to gas $Q = n{C_v}\\Delta T$<br>$ = 1.09 \\times \\frac{5}{2}R \\times 80 = 1.09 \\times \\frac{5}{2} \\times 8.3 \\times 80$<br>$ = 1.81\\,kJ$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37821,'physics',29,1300,3145,'Two different isotherms representing the relationship between pressure \\[P\\] and volume \\[V\\] at a same temperature of the same ideal gas are shown for masses \\(m_{1}\\) and \\(m_{2}\\) then<div class="matching-question"></div>','[{"text": "$m_{1}=m_{2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$m_{1}>m_{2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$m_{1} < m_{2}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "All of the above are possible", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'The ideal gas equation is<br>$P V=n R T$<br>$P V=\\dfrac{m}{M} R T$<br>The given curve is an isothermal curve. Temperature is same for both curves.<br>$\\dfrac{P V}{m}=\\text { Constant }$<br>At a given pressure<br><img src=\'/img/data/physics/29_37821_explanation_1.jpg\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' /> <br>${V_2} > {V_1}{\\rm{ }}(from{\\rm{ }}graph)$<br>$\\frac{V}{m} = $Constant ($P$ is constant )<br>$ \\Rightarrow {m_2} > {m_1}$',NULL,'Medium','Graph/Figure',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/29_37821_question_1.png'),
(37822,'physics',26,1138,2731,'A capillary tube of radius $R$ is immersed in water and water rises in it to a height $H$. Mass of water in the capillary tube is $M$. If the radius of the tube is doubled, mass of water that will rise in the capillary tube will now be','[{"text": "$2\\\\,M$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$M$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$M/2$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$4\\\\,M$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Mass of liquid in capillary tube<br>$$M=\\pi R^{2} h \\times \\rho=\\pi R^{2} \\rho \\dfrac{2 T \\cos \\theta}{\\rho R g}$$<br>$$\\therefore \\quad M \\propto R$$. If radius becomes double, then mass will become twice.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37823,'physics',27,1380,3307,'Two scales having different coefficients of linear expansion are caliberated at room temperature. Now the temperature of the scales are increased by $20^\\circ C$  then $15\\,cm$ mark of first scale coincides with $12\\,cm$ mark of second scale. Find the mark on first scale that coincides with $10\\,cm$ mark on second scale.','[{"text": "$10\\\\,cm$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$12\\\\,cm$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$11\\\\,cm$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$12.5\\\\,cm$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'After expansion as $15\\,cm\\,\\,\\& \\,{\\rm{ }}12\\,cm$ coincide then<br>$15\\,cm\\left( {1 + {\\alpha _1}\\Delta T} \\right) = 12\\,cm\\left( {1 + {\\alpha _2}\\Delta T} \\right)\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\left( 1 \\right)$<br>Let $x$ is the mark on the first scale that coincides with $10\\,cm$ mark on second scale<br>$x\\left( {1 + {\\alpha _1}\\Delta T} \\right) = 10\\,cm\\left( {1 + {\\alpha _2}\\Delta T} \\right)\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\left( 2 \\right)$<br>${\\rm{Eq(1)}}\\,{\\rm{/}}\\,{\\rm{Eq(2)}}$<br>$\\frac{{15}}{x} = \\frac{{12}}{{10}} \\Rightarrow x = 12.5\\,cm$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/27_37823_question_1.png'),
(37824,'physics',28,1369,3279,'When heat energy of $1500\\,J$ is supplied to a gas at constant pressure, $2.1 \\times {10^5}N{m^{ - 2}}$ the change in its volume is $2.5 \\times {10^{ - 3}}\\,{m^3}$. The increase in its internal energy is','[{"text": "$450\\\\,J$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$525\\\\,J$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$975\\\\,J$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$2025\\\\,J$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Heat supplied to a gas,<br>$\\Delta Q = 1500J$<br>Work done by the gas,<br>$\\Delta W = P\\Delta V = 2.1 \\times {10^5} \\times {N^{ - 2}} \\times 2.5 \\times {10^{ - 3}}\\,{m^3}$<br>$ = 5.25 \\times {10^2}Nm = 525\\,J$<br>According to first law of thermodynamics,<br>$\\Delta Q=\\Delta W+\\Delta U$<br>$\\Delta U=\\Delta Q-\\Delta W$<br>$ = 1500\\,J - 525\\,J = 975\\,J$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37825,'physics',29,1305,3155,'If \\(\\lambda\\) represents mean free path of a gas particle pressure \\[P\\], absolute temperature \\[T\\] and density \\(\\rho\\) of an ideal gas then predict the correct graph.<div class="matching-question"></div>','[{"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "All of the above", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'The mean free path is given by<br>$\\lambda=\\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{2} \\pi n d^{2}}$<br>$n$ - number of particles per unit volume<br>$d$ - diameter of a particle<br>$\\begin{aligned}& n=\\dfrac{N}{V}=\\dfrac{P N_{A}}{R T}=\\dfrac{P}{K_{B} T} \\\\& \\lambda=\\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\dfrac{K_{B} T}{\\pi d^{2} P}\\end{aligned}$<br>So the graph between $\\lambda {\\rm{ }}\\,\\& \\,\\,T$ is a straight line passing through origin. Graph between $\\lambda {\\rm{ }}\\,\\& \\,\\,P$  is hyperbolic. <br>$\\lambda=\\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{2} \\pi n d^{2}}=\\dfrac{m_{0}}{\\sqrt{2} \\pi\\left(m_{0} n\\right) d^{2}}=\\dfrac{m_{0}}{\\sqrt{2} \\pi d^{2} \\rho}$<br>Where $m_{0}$ - mass of a gas particle<br>$m_{0} n$ - Total mass per unit volume<br>$\\left(m_{0} n=\\rho\\right)$<br>$\\lambda \\,\\,\\alpha \\,\\,\\frac{1}{\\rho }$ The graph between $\\lambda {\\rm{ }}\\,\\& \\,\\,\\rho $ is hyperbolic.<br>So option (4) is correct.',NULL,'Easy','Graph/Figure',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37826,'physics',26,1133,2705,'Two identical cylindrical vessels with their bases at the same level, each contains a liquid of density $$\\rho$$. The height of the liquid in one vessel is $$h_{1}$$ and that in the other $$h_{2}$$. The area of either base is $A$. The work done by gravity in equalizing the levels when the vessels are inter connected is','[{"text": "$$\\\\operatorname{A\\\\rho g}\\\\left(\\\\dfrac{h_{1}-h_{2}}{4}\\\\right)^{2}$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$\\\\operatorname{A\\\\rho g}\\\\left(\\\\dfrac{h_{1}-h_{2}}{4}\\\\right)$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$\\\\operatorname{A\\\\rho g}\\\\left(\\\\dfrac{h_{1}-h_{2}}{2}\\\\right)$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$\\\\operatorname{A\\\\rho g}\\\\left(\\\\dfrac{h_{1}-h_{2}}{2}\\\\right)^{2}$$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'The center gravity of liquid in the first column is at a height $$\\dfrac{h_{1}}{2}$$ and that in the second column is $$\\dfrac{h_{2}}{2}$$<br>Let initial Potential Energy be $U$,<br>$$\\begin{aligned}& \\Rightarrow U_{1}=\\left(A h_{1} \\rho\\right) g\\left(\\dfrac{h_{1}}{2}\\right)+\\left(A h_{2} \\rho\\right) g\\left(\\dfrac{h_{2}}{2}\\right) \\\\& \\Rightarrow U_{1}=\\dfrac{1}{2} A \\rho g\\left(h_{1}^{2}+h_{2}^{2}\\right)\\end{aligned}$$<br>When the water column in the two equalises then equivalent height is $$\\dfrac{h_{1}+h_{2}}{2}$$ and the center of gravity in both the columns is $$\\dfrac{h_{1}+h_{2}}{4}$$.<br>If $$U_{2}$$ be the final potential energy in both the columns then<br>${U_2} = 2\\left[ {A\\left( {\\frac{{{h_1} + {h_2}}}{2}} \\right)\\rho \\left( {\\frac{{{h_1} + {h_2}}}{4}g} \\right)} \\right]$<br>$ \\Rightarrow {U_2} = \\frac{1}{4}A\\rho g{\\left( {{h_1} + {h_2}} \\right)^2}$<br>Since work done is equal to the decrease in potential energy.<br>So, $$W=U_{1}-U_{2} \\Rightarrow W=A \\rho g\\left(\\dfrac{h_{1}-h_{2}}{2}\\right)^{2}$$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37827,'physics',27,1380,3307,'A resistance $R$ carries a current $I$. The rate of heat loss to the surroundings is $\\lambda\\left(T-T_{0}\\right)$ where $\\lambda$ is a constant, $T$ is the temperature of the resistance, and $T_{0}$ is the temperature of the atmosphere. If the coefficient of linear expansion is $\\alpha$, the strain in the resistance is :','[{"text": "Proportional to the length of the resistance wire", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Equal to $\\\\dfrac{\\\\alpha}{\\\\lambda} I^{2} R$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Equals to $\\\\dfrac{1}{2} \\\\dfrac{\\\\alpha}{\\\\lambda} I^{2} R$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Equal to $\\\\alpha \\\\lambda I R$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'The power loss is given by<br>$\\begin{aligned}& I^{2} R=\\lambda\\left(T-T_{0}\\right) ; \\text { Strain }=\\dfrac{\\Delta l}{l}=\\alpha\\left(T-T_{0}\\right) \\\\& \\dfrac{I^{2} R}{\\left(\\dfrac{\\Delta l}{l}\\right)}=\\dfrac{\\lambda\\left(T-T_{0}\\right)}{\\alpha\\left(T-T_{0}\\right)} \\\\& \\dfrac{I^{2} R}{\\left(\\dfrac{\\Delta l}{l}\\right)}=\\dfrac{\\lambda}{\\alpha} \\Rightarrow \\dfrac{\\Delta l}{l}=\\dfrac{\\alpha I^{2} R}{\\lambda}\\end{aligned}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37828,'physics',28,1371,3282,'An ideal gas system undergoes an isothermal process, then the work done during the process is','[{"text": "$n R T \\\\ln \\\\dfrac{V_{2}}{V_{1}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$n R T \\\\ln \\\\dfrac{V_{1}}{V_{2}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2 n R T \\\\ln \\\\dfrac{V_{2}}{V_{1}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2 n R T \\\\ln \\\\left(\\\\dfrac{V_{1}}{V_{2}}\\\\right)$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Let an ideal gas goes isothermally from its initial state $\\left(P_{1}, V_{1}\\right)$ to the final state $\\left(P_{2}, V_{2}\\right)$, then work done<br>$W = \\int\\limits_{{V_1}}^{{V_2}} P dV$. Taking $P V=n R T$ or $P=\\dfrac{n R T}{V}$<br>Then, $W = \\int\\limits_{{V_1}}^{{V_2}} {\\frac{{nRT}}{V}} dV = nRT\\int\\limits_{{V_1}}^{{V_2}} {\\frac{{dV}}{V}}  = nRT\\ln \\frac{{{V_2}}}{{{V_1}}}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37829,'physics',29,1300,3145,'Which one of the following graph is correct at constant pressure<div class="matching-question"></div>','[{"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'According to ideal gas equation<br>$P V=n R T \\quad \\text { or } \\dfrac{V}{T}=\\dfrac{n R}{P}$<br>At constant pressure<br>$\\dfrac{V}{T}=\\text { constant }$<br>Hence graph (2) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Graph/Figure',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37830,'physics',27,1380,3306,'Two steel rods and an aluminium rod of length $l_{0}$ and equal cross section are joined rigidly at their eand as shown in the figure below. All the rods are in a state of zero tension at $0^\\circ C$. Find the length of the system when the temperature is raised to $\\Delta T$. Coefficient of linear expansion of aluminium and steel are $\\alpha_{a}$ and $\\alpha_{s}$ respectively. Young\'s modulus of aluminium and steel are $Y_{a}$ and $Y_{s}$ respectively $\\left(\\alpha_{s}>\\alpha_{a}\\right)$.','[{"text": "$l_{0} \\\\Delta T\\\\left[\\\\alpha_{s}+\\\\alpha_{a}\\\\right]$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{l_{0} \\\\Delta T\\\\left[2 Y_{s} \\\\alpha_{s}+Y_{a} \\\\alpha_{a}\\\\right]}{\\\\left[Y_{a}+2 Y_{s}\\\\right]}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{l_{0} \\\\Delta T\\\\left[Y_{s} \\\\alpha_{s}+Y_{a} \\\\alpha_{a}\\\\right]}{\\\\left[Y_{a}+Y_{s}\\\\right]}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{l_{0} \\\\Delta T\\\\left[2 \\\\alpha_{s}+\\\\alpha_{a}\\\\right] Y_{s} Y_{a}}{\\\\left[Y_{a}+Y_{s}\\\\right]}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'If the three rods are free to expand then their extensions are<br>$\\begin{aligned}& \\Delta l_{s}=l_{0} \\alpha_{s} \\Delta T \\\\& \\Delta l_{a}=l_{0} \\alpha_{a} \\Delta T\\end{aligned}$<br><img src=\'/img/data/physics/27_37830_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' /> <br>fig (1)- Initial position of the combination before rising temperature.<br>fig (2)- Expected natural extensions in the rods after rising temperature.<br>fig (3)- Final position of the combination after rising temperature.<br>As the three rods are rigidly fixed they will have a common extension $\\Delta l$. As $\\alpha_{s}>\\alpha_{a}$ the steel rods try to exert force in forward direction on the aluminium rod.<br>As the system is in equilibrium<br>${F_a} = 2{F_s}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\left( 1 \\right)$<br>The strain in steel rods<br>${\\rm{strain }} = \\frac{{\\left( {{l_0}{\\alpha _s}\\Delta T - \\Delta l} \\right)}}{{{l_0}}}$<br>$ \\Rightarrow \\,\\,\\,{\\mkern 1mu} {\\kern 1pt} {F_s} = \\frac{{{Y_s}A\\left( {{l_0}{\\alpha _s}\\Delta T - \\Delta l} \\right)}}{{{l_0}}}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\left( 2 \\right)$<br>Similarly<br>${F_a} = \\frac{{{Y_a}A\\left( {\\Delta l - {l_0}{\\alpha _a}\\Delta T} \\right)}}{{{l_0}}}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\left( 3 \\right)$<br>From the three eq\'s we get<br>$\\Delta l=\\dfrac{l_{0} \\Delta T\\left(2 Y_{s} \\alpha_{s}+Y_{a} \\alpha_{a}\\right)}{\\left[Y_{a}+2 Y_{s}\\right]}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/27_37830_question_1.png'),
(37832,'physics',29,1300,3145,'For an ideal gas pressure \\[P\\] varies with volume according to the graph shown. The variation of \\[T\\] with \\[V\\] is represented by<div class="matching-question"></div>','[{"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'As the straight line has negative slope the general expression for the equation of line is<br>$P=-C_{1} V+C_{2}$<br>Where ${C_1}\\,\\,\\& \\,\\,{C_2}$ are $( + ){\\rm{ }}ve$  constants<br>From ideal gas equation we can write the above equation as<br>$\\begin{aligned}& \\dfrac{n R T}{V}=-C_{1} V+C_{2} \\\\& T=\\dfrac{-C_{1}}{n R} V^{2}+\\dfrac{C_{2}}{n R} V\\end{aligned}$<br>The equation represents a curve.<br>Differentiating the equation w.r.to $V$<br>$\\begin{aligned}& \\dfrac{d T}{d V}=-\\dfrac{2 C_{1}}{n R} V+\\dfrac{C_{2}}{n R} \\\\& \\dfrac{d^{2} T}{d V^{2}}=-\\dfrac{2 C_{1}}{n R}\\end{aligned}$<br>Which represents a maxima.The correct graph is (2).',NULL,'Easy','Graph/Figure',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/29_37832_question_1.png'),
(37833,'physics',28,1371,3283,'A cycle tyre bursts suddenly. What is the type of this process?','[{"text": "Isochoric", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Isothermal", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Isobaric", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Adiabatic", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Sudden bursting of the cycle tyre is an adiabatic process.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2014,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37834,'physics',26,1133,2707,'On a smooth inclined plane, making an angle $$\\alpha$$ with horizontal, a trolley containing a liquid of density $$\\rho$$ slicles down. What is the angle of inclination $$\\theta$$ of free surface with horizontal?','[{"text": "$$\\\\theta=\\\\left(\\\\dfrac{\\\\pi}{2}-\\\\alpha\\\\right)$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$\\\\theta=\\\\alpha / 3$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$\\\\theta=\\\\left(\\\\dfrac{\\\\pi}{4}-\\\\alpha\\\\right)$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$\\\\theta=\\\\alpha$$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'The force $$m g$$ and pseudo force $$m a$$ act on a liquid particle on the surface as shown in the figure<br><img src=\'/img/data/physics/26_37834_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' /> <br>As the trolley moves down with acceleration $$g \\sin \\alpha$$ on the inclined plane so $$a=g \\sin \\alpha$$. The pseudo force $$m a(=m g \\sin \\alpha)$$ is balanced by $$m g \\sin \\theta$$ (gravitational force component). The only unbalanced force is $$m g \\cos \\alpha$$. A liquid surface is always present perpendicular to net force so surface of the liquid is parallel to the inclined plane. So $$\\theta=\\alpha$$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/26_37834_question_1.png'),
(37835,'physics',27,1380,3312,'A one litre flask contains certain quantity of mercury. If the volume of air inside the flask remains the same at all temperatures then the volume of mercury in the flask is (volume expansion coefficient of mercury is 20 times that of flask)','[{"text": "$50\\\\,cc$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$100\\\\,cc$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$200\\\\,cc$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$150\\\\,cc$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Let $V_{c_{o}}$ and $V_{l_{o}}$ are the initial volumes of container and liquid $V_{c_{o}}-V_{l_{o}}=$ constant (Given)<br>$V_{c}-V_{l}=V_{c_{0}}\\left(1+\\gamma_{c} \\Delta T\\right)-V_{l_{0}}\\left(1+\\gamma_{l} \\Delta T\\right)$<br>$\\begin{aligned}& V_{c}-V_{l}=V_{c_{0}}-V_{l_{0}}+V_{c_{0}} \\gamma_{c} \\Delta T-V_{l_{0}} \\gamma_{l} \\Delta T \\\\& V_{c_{0}} \\gamma_{c}=V_{l_{0}} \\gamma_{l}\\end{aligned}$<br>Given $V_{c_{0}}=1000 c c, \\gamma_{l}=20 \\gamma_{c}$<br>$\\Rightarrow V_{l_{0}}=50 c c$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37836,'physics',29,1300,3145,'From the following \\(\\mathrm{P}-\\mathrm{T}\\) graph what inference can be drawn<div class="matching-question"></div>','[{"text": "$V_{2} < V_{1}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$V_{2}>V_{1}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$V_{2}=V_{1}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "None of these", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'As<br>$\\theta_{2}>\\theta_{1} \\Rightarrow \\tan \\theta_{2}>\\tan \\theta_{1} \\Rightarrow\\left(\\dfrac{T}{P}\\right)_{2}>\\left(\\dfrac{T}{P}\\right)_{1}$<br>From $P V=n R T ; \\dfrac{T}{P} \\propto V \\Rightarrow V_{2}>V_{1}$.',NULL,'Medium','Graph/Figure',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/29_37836_question_1.jpg'),
(37838,'physics',26,1133,2710,'A uniform solid cylinder of density $0.8\\,g/c{m^3}$ floats in equilibrium in a combination of two nonmixing liquids $$A$$ and $$B$$ with its axis vertical. The densities of the liquids $A$ and $B$ are $0.7\\,g/c{m^3}$ and  $1.2\\,g/c{m^3}$ respectively. The height of liquid $A$ is ${h_A} = 1.2\\,cm$. The length of the cylinder immersed in liquid $B$ is ${h_B} = 0.8\\,cm$. Cross sectional area $ = 1\\,c{m^2}$<br>Atmospheric pressure $ = {10^5}\\,N/{m^2}$<br>Find $$h$$, the length of the part of the cylinder in air.','[{"text": "$10\\\\,cm$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$25\\\\,cm$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$0.25\\\\,cm$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$1\\\\,cm$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'As the cylinder is in equilibrium $$F_{g}=F_{b}$$ $$0.8(h+1.2+0.8)=1.2 \\times 0.7+0.8 \\times 1.2$$<br>$ \\Rightarrow h = 0.25\\,cm$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/26_37838_question_1.png'),
(37895,'physics',28,1371,3284,'One mole of ${O_2}$ gas is heated at constant pressure starting at $27^\\circ C$. How much energy must be added to the gas as heat to double its volume?','[{"text": "$750\\\\,R$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{Zero}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1050\\\\,R$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$450\\\\,R$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Here, ${V_1} = V,\\,\\,{V_2} = 2\\,V$<br>${T_1} = 27^\\circ C = 300\\,K$<br>At constant pressure<br>$\\frac{{{V_1}}}{{{T_1}}} = \\frac{{{V_2}}}{{{T_2}}}\\quad \\therefore {T_2} = \\left( {\\frac{{{V_2}}}{{{V_1}}}} \\right) \\times {T_1} = 600\\,K$<br>Heat absorbed by the gas at constant pressure<br>$ = n{C_p}\\Delta T = 1 \\times \\frac{7}{2}R \\times 300 = 1050R.$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2019,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37839,'physics',27,1380,3311,'As shown in the picture a cylinder with volumetric thermal expansion coefficient of ${\\gamma _s} = 3 \\times {10^{ - 6}}/^\\circ C$ is completely submerged and floating in a fluid at the temperature $50^\\circ C$. The fluid has volumetric thermal expansion coefficient of ${\\gamma _l} = 8 \\times {10^{ - 5}}/^\\circ C$.<br>If we cool down the cylinder and fluid to $0^\\circ C$ what percentage of the cylinder\'s height will be out of the fluid?','[{"text": "$0.84 \\\\%$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1.42 \\\\%$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$0.78 \\\\%$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$0.37 \\\\%$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'As the cylinder is completely submerged in the liquid the densities of cylinder and liquid are same at $50^\\circ C$. Let $\\rho_{0}$ is the initial density. After decreasing the temperature<br>${\\rho _s} = \\frac{{{\\rho _0}}}{{\\left( {1 - {\\gamma _s}50} \\right)}}\\,\\& \\,{\\mkern 1mu} {\\kern 1pt} {\\rho _l} = \\frac{{{\\rho _0}}}{{\\left( {1 - {\\gamma _l}50} \\right)}}$<br>At equilibrium of the cylinder<br>$\\begin{gathered}F_{g}=F_{b} \\\\m g=\\rho_{l} V^{\\prime} g \\\\\\rho_{s} V g=\\rho_{l} V^{\\prime} g \\\\\\rho_{s} h A g=\\rho_{l} h^{\\prime} A g \\\\\\dfrac{h^{\\prime}}{h}=\\dfrac{\\rho_{s}}{\\rho_{l}}\\end{gathered}$<br>$h$ - total height of the cylinder<br>$h^{\\prime}$ - height of the cylinder present inside the liquid.<br>$\\begin{aligned}\\dfrac{h^{\\prime}}{h} & =\\dfrac{\\left(1-\\gamma_{l} 50\\right)}{\\left(1-\\gamma_{s} 50\\right)}=\\left(1-\\gamma_{l} 50\\right)\\left(1-\\gamma_{s} 50\\right)^{-1} \\\\\\dfrac{h^{\\prime}}{h} & =\\left(1-\\gamma_{l} 50\\right)\\left(1+\\gamma_{s} 50\\right) \\\\\\dfrac{h^{\\prime}}{h} & =\\left[1-\\left(\\gamma_{l}-\\gamma_{s}\\right) 50\\right] \\\\\\dfrac{h^{\\prime}}{h} & =\\left[1-7.7 \\times 10^{-5} \\times 50\\right] \\\\\\dfrac{h^{\\prime}}{h} & =0.99615\\end{aligned}$<br>The percentage of the cylinder present outside is $(1-0.99615) \\times 100=0.38 \\%$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/27_37839_question_1.jpg'),
(37840,'physics',29,1300,3145,'The correct graph between PV and P of one mol of gas at constant temperature will be<div class="matching-question"></div>','[{"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'$P{\\rm{ }}V{\\rm{ }} = {\\rm{ }}n{\\rm{ }}RT$<br>When $T = $ constant<br>$PV = $Constant $\\Rightarrow$ option (4) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Graph/Figure',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37842,'physics',26,1133,2710,'A hollow cylinder of mass $m$ made heavy at its bottom is floating vertically in water. It is tilled from its vertical position through an angle $$\\theta$$ and is left. The restoring force acting on it is','[{"text": "$$m g \\\\cos \\\\theta$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$\\\\dfrac{m g}{\\\\cos \\\\theta}$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$m g\\\\left[\\\\dfrac{1}{\\\\cos \\\\theta}-1\\\\right]$$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$$m g\\\\left[\\\\dfrac{1}{\\\\cos \\\\theta}+1\\\\right]$$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Let $$l$$ be the length of the cylinder, present inside water initially. Let $A$ be the cross-sectional area of the cylinder. Equating weight of the cylinder with the upthrust, we get<br>$mg = Alpg \\Rightarrow m = Alp$<br><img src=\'/img/data/physics/26_37842_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' /> <br>When the cylinder is tilted through an angle $$\\theta$$, length of cylinder in water $$=\\dfrac{l}{\\cos \\theta}$$<br>Weight of water displaced $$=\\dfrac{l}{\\cos \\theta} A \\rho g$$<br>Restoring force $$=\\dfrac{l A \\rho g}{\\cos \\theta}-l A \\rho g$$<br>$$=\\operatorname{lA\\rho g}\\left[\\dfrac{1}{\\cos \\theta}-1\\right]=m g\\left[\\dfrac{1}{\\cos \\theta}-1\\right]$$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37843,'physics',27,1380,3309,'A wooden wheel of radius $R$  is made of two semicircular part (see figure). The two parts are held together by ring made of a metal strip cross sectional area $A$ and length $L$. $L$ is slightly less than $2 \\pi R$. To fit the ring on the wheel, it is heated so that its temperature rises by $\\Delta T$ and it just steps over the wheel. As it cools down to surrounding temperature, it presses the semicircular parts together. If the cofficient of linear eaxpansion of the metal is $\\alpha$, and its Young\'s modulus is Y, the force that one part of the wheel applies on the other part is:','[{"text": "$2 \\\\pi S Y \\\\alpha \\\\Delta T$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$S Y \\\\alpha \\\\Delta T$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\pi S Y \\\\alpha \\\\Delta T$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2 S Y \\\\alpha \\\\Delta T$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'The Youngs modulus is given as<br>$Y=\\dfrac{F / A}{\\Delta L / L}$<br>Here it is given as<br>$Y = \\frac{F}{{A2\\pi \\Delta R}} \\times 2\\pi R\\,\\,\\,{\\mkern 1mu} {\\kern 1pt} [L = 2\\pi R]$<br>$ \\Rightarrow Y = \\frac{{FR}}{{S \\cdot \\Delta R}}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\left( 1 \\right)$<br>The coefficient of linear expansion is given as<br>$\\alpha  = \\frac{{\\Delta R}}{{R\\Delta T}}$<br>$ \\Rightarrow \\frac{{\\Delta R}}{R} = \\alpha .\\Delta t \\Rightarrow \\frac{R}{{\\Delta R}} = \\frac{1}{{\\alpha  \\cdot \\Delta T}}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\left( 2 \\right)$<br>From equations (1) and (2)<br>$Y=\\dfrac{F}{S . \\alpha \\Delta T} \\Rightarrow F=Y . S . \\alpha \\Delta T$<br>$\\therefore$ The ring presses the wheel from both sides, Thus $F_{\\text {net }}=2 F=2 Y S \\alpha \\Delta T$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/27_37843_question_1.jpg'),
(37844,'physics',29,1300,3145,'At a constant pressure, of the following graphs the one which represents the variation of the density of an ideal gas with the absolute temperature \\(T\\), is<div class="matching-question"></div>','[{"text": "II", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "I", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "IV", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "III", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'When Pressure is constant<br>$\\begin{aligned}& P V=n R T \\\\& P \\dfrac{m}{\\rho}=\\dfrac{m}{M} R T \\\\& P=\\dfrac{\\rho}{M} R T \\Rightarrow \\rho T=\\text { Constant }\\end{aligned}$',NULL,'Medium','Graph/Figure',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/29_37844_question_1.jpg'),
(37861,'physics',28,1367,3277,'Which of the following is not a thermodynamic co-ordinate?','[{"text": "Gas constant (R)", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Pressure (P)", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Volume (V)", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Temperature (T)", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'$P,{\\rm{ }}V{\\rm{ }}\\& {\\rm{ }}T$ are the thermodynamic co-ordinates used to describe the state of the system whereas gas constant $(\\mathrm{R})$ is a universal gas constant whose value is $8.314\\,J\\,mo{l^{ - 1}}\\,{K^{ - 1}}.$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2009,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37846,'physics',26,1133,2710,'If level of the liquid starts decreasing slowly when the level of liquid is at a height $$h_{1}$$ above the cylinder the block starts moving up. At what value of $$h_{1}$$, will the block rise:','[{"text": "$$\\\\dfrac{5 H}{9}$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$\\\\dfrac{4 H}{9}$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$\\\\dfrac{4 H}{3}$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$\\\\dfrac{5 H}{3}$$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'At equilibrium the pressure force on the block is balanced by its weight<br><img src=\'/img/data/physics/26_37846_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' /> <br>Here the effect of atmospheric pressure is not considered since $$P_{a}$$ effect is there both on top $$\\&$$ base of the block. $$F_{P_{1}}$$ and $$F_{P_{2}}$$ are pressure forces.<br>$$\\begin{aligned}& F_{P_{1}}+F_{g}=F_{P_{2}} \\\\& \\rho g h_{1} \\pi(2 r)^{2}+\\dfrac{\\rho}{3} g H \\pi(2 r)^{2} \\\\& =\\rho g\\left(h_{1}+H\\right) \\pi\\left[(2 r)^{2}-r^{2}\\right] \\\\& h_{1}=\\dfrac{5 H}{3}\\end{aligned}$$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/26_37846_question_1.png'),
(37847,'physics',27,1380,3306,'As a result of a temperature rise of $32^\\circ C$, a bar with a crack at its centre buckles upward as shown in the figure. If the fixed distance is ${L_0} = 3.77\\,m$ and the coefficient of linear expansion is $25 \\times {10^{ - 6}}/^\\circ C$, find $x$, the distance to which the centre rises.','[{"text": "$6.0\\\\,cm\\\\,$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$3.5\\\\,cm\\\\,$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$4\\\\,cm\\\\,$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$7.5\\\\,cm\\\\,$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Let $\\dfrac{L}{2}$ is the length of each part after rising temperature. From the right angle triangle<br>${\\left( {\\frac{L}{2}} \\right)^2} = {x^2} + {\\left( {\\frac{{{L_0}}}{2}} \\right)^2}$<br>$\\frac{{{L^2}}}{4} = {x^2} + \\frac{{L_0^2}}{4}$<br>${x^2} = \\frac{1}{4}\\left( {{L^2} - L_0^2} \\right)$<br>$x = \\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt {{L^2} - L_0^2}  = \\frac{{{L_0}}}{2}\\sqrt {{{(1 + \\alpha \\Delta T)}^2} - 1} $<br>$x = \\frac{{{L_0}}}{2}\\sqrt {1 + 2\\alpha \\Delta T - 1}  = \\frac{{{L_0}}}{2}\\sqrt {2\\alpha \\Delta T} $<br>$x = \\frac{{3.77}}{2} \\times \\sqrt {2 \\times 25 \\times {{10}^{ - 6}} \\times 32} $<br>$x = 7.5\\,cm$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/27_37847_question_1.png'),
(37848,'physics',29,1300,3145,'The adjoining figure shows graph of pressure and volume of a gas at two temperatures \\(T_{1}\\) and \\(T_{2}\\). Which of the following inference is correct<div class="matching-question"></div>','[{"text": "$T_{1}=T_{2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$T_{1}>T_{2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$T_{1} < T_{2}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "No interference can be drawn<br>", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'For a given pressure, volume will be more if temperature is more $\\dfrac{V}{T}=$ Constant<br><img src=\'/img/data/physics/29_37848_explanation_1.jpg\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' /> <br>From the graph it is clear that $V_{2}>V_{1} \\Rightarrow T_{2}>T_{1}$.',NULL,'Easy','Graph/Figure',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/29_37848_question_1.png'),
(37850,'physics',26,1133,2710,'A hemispherical portion of radius $$R$$ is removed from the bottom of a cylinder of radius $R$. The volume of the remaining cylinder is $V$ and its mass $M$. It is suspended by a string in a liquid of density $$\\rho$$ where it stays vertical. The upper surface of the cylinder is at a depth $$h$$ below the liquid surface. The force on the bottom of the cylinder by the liquid is:','[{"text": "$$M g-V \\\\rho g$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$M g$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$\\\\rho g\\\\left(V+\\\\pi R^{2} h\\\\right)$$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$$M g+\\\\pi R^{2} h \\\\rho g$$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Let $$F_{b}(=\\rho V g)$$ is the buoyancy force on the cylinder. Let $$F$$ is the pressure force exerted by the liquid on the base of the cylinder.<br>From the definition of buoyancy force<br>$$\\begin{aligned}& F_{b}=F-\\pi R^{2} \\rho g h \\\\& \\rho V g=F-\\pi R^{2} \\rho g h \\\\& \\Rightarrow \\quad F=\\rho g V+\\pi R^{2} \\rho g h\\end{aligned}$$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/26_37850_question_1.png'),
(37851,'physics',27,1380,3307,'A steel scale measures the lenght of a copper rod as $80.0\\,cm\\,$, when both are at $20^\\circ C$ (the calibration temperature for scale). What would be the scale read for the length of the rod when both are at $40^\\circ C$ ? (Given ${\\alpha _{{\\rm{steel }}}} = 11 \\times {10^{ - 6}}{\\rm{per }}^\\circ C$ and ${\\alpha _{{\\rm{copper }}}} = 11 \\times {10^{ - 6}}{\\rm{per }}^\\circ C$ )','[{"text": "$80.0272\\\\,cm$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$80.0096\\\\,cm$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$25.2\\\\,cm$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1\\\\,cm$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'The initial positions of the rod and scale are shown in the figure.<br><img src=\'/img/data/physics/27_37851_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' /> <br>Given that $\\alpha_{\\mathrm{Cu}}>\\alpha_{\\text {steel }}$<br>Let $x^{\\text {th }}$ centimeter mark on the scale coincides with the right end of the $cu$-rod.<br>After expansion $x\\left( {1 + {\\alpha _s}\\Delta T} \\right) = 80\\,cm\\left( {1 + {\\alpha _{Cu}}\\Delta T} \\right)$<br>$x \\approx 80.0096\\,cm{\\rm{ }}$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/27_37851_question_1.png'),
(37852,'physics',29,1300,3145,'A cylinder containing an ideal gas is in vertical position and has a piston of mass $M$ that is able to move up or down without friction. If the temperature is increased.','[{"text": "Both $p$ and $V$ of the gas will change", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Only p will increases according to Charles\'law", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$V$ will change but not $p$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$p$ will change but not $V$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'As piston is able to move up or down without friction, therfore, when temperature is increased, piston moves out, increasing the volume $V $, The pressure $P$ on the gas remains the same, because of fixed mass.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,'NCERT Exemplar','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/29_37852_question_1.jpg'),
(37854,'physics',26,1133,2710,'A solid cone of height $$H$$ and base radius $$H / 2$$ floats in a liquid of density $$\\rho$$. It is hanging from the ceiling with the help of a string. The force by the fluid on the curved surface of the cone is ( $$P_{0}=$$ atmospheric pressure)','[{"text": "$$\\\\pi H^{2}\\\\left(\\\\dfrac{P_{0}}{4}+\\\\dfrac{\\\\rho g H}{3}\\\\right)$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$\\\\pi H^{2}\\\\left(\\\\dfrac{P_{0}}{4}+\\\\dfrac{\\\\rho g H}{6}\\\\right)$$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$$\\\\dfrac{\\\\pi H^{2}}{4}\\\\left(\\\\dfrac{P_{0}}{4}+\\\\rho g H\\\\right)$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$\\\\dfrac{\\\\pi H^{2}}{4}\\\\left(P_{0}+\\\\rho g H\\\\right)$$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Buoyancy force $$=$$ Net pressure force<br>$$\\begin{aligned}\\dfrac{1}{3} \\pi\\left(\\dfrac{H}{2}\\right)^{2} H \\rho g & =\\left(P_{0}+\\rho g H\\right) \\pi\\left(\\dfrac{H}{2}\\right)^{2}-F_{v} \\\\& =P_{0} \\dfrac{\\pi H^{2}}{4}+\\dfrac{\\rho g H^{3} \\pi}{6}\\end{aligned}$$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/26_37854_question_1.png'),
(37855,'physics',27,1380,3309,'Two rods are joined between fixed supports as shown in the figure. Condition for no change in the lengths of individual rods with the increase of temperature will be<br>$\\left(\\alpha_{1}, \\alpha_{2}=\\right.$ linear expansion coefficients $A_{1}, A_{2}=$ Area of rods $Y_{1}, Y_{2}=$ Young modulus)','[{"text": "$\\\\dfrac{A_{1}}{A_{2}}=\\\\dfrac{\\\\alpha_{1} Y_{1}}{\\\\alpha_{2} Y_{2}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{A_{1}}{A_{2}}=\\\\dfrac{L_{1} \\\\alpha_{1} Y_{1}}{L_{2} \\\\alpha_{2} Y_{2}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{A_{1}}{A_{2}}=\\\\dfrac{L_{2} \\\\alpha_{2} Y_{2}}{L_{1} \\\\alpha_{1} Y_{1}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{A_{1}}{A_{2}}=\\\\dfrac{\\\\alpha_{2} Y_{2}}{\\\\alpha_{1} Y_{1}}$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'$Y = \\frac{{{\\rm{ Stress }}}}{{{\\rm{ Strain }}}} = \\frac{{T/A}}{{\\Delta l/l}}$<br>$T = \\frac{{Y \\cdot \\Delta l}}{l}A = Y \\cdot A\\alpha \\Delta T$<br>In both the rods tension will be same so<br>$\\begin{aligned}& T_{1}=T_{2}, \\text { Hence } Y_{1} A_{1} \\alpha_{1} \\Delta T=Y_{2} A_{2} \\alpha_{2} \\Delta T \\\\& \\dfrac{A_{1}}{A_{2}}=\\dfrac{Y_{2} \\alpha_{2}}{Y_{1} \\alpha_{1}}\\end{aligned}$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/27_37855_question_1.png'),
(37856,'physics',29,1300,3145,'Volume versus temperature graphs for a mass of an ideal gas are shown in the figure at two different values of constant pressure. What can be inferred about relation between $p_{1}$ and $p_{2}$ ?','[{"text": "$p_{1}>p_{2}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$p_{1}=p_{2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$p_{1} < p_{2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Date is insufficient<br>", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'When pressure of an ideal gas is constant, Charles\' law is obeyed i.e.,<br>$V \\propto T \\text { or } \\dfrac{V}{T}=\\text { constant }=\\dfrac{1}{p}$<br>From the slope of curve shown in figure $p_{1}>p_{2}$.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,'NCERT Exemplar','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/29_37856_question_1.jpg'),
(37858,'physics',26,1133,2710,'Two solid bodies form a balance and the arm is in horizontal position. The mass of one body is $36\\,g$ and its density is $9\\,g/c{m^3}$. If the mass of the other is $48\\,g$, its density in $g/c{m^3}$ is','[{"text": "$$\\\\dfrac{3}{2}$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$\\\\dfrac{4}{3}$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "5", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "3", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Let $T$ be the tension in the string.<br>$$\\begin{aligned}& T=m g-F_{b}=m g-m_{\\ell} g=\\left(m-\\rho_{\\ell} V\\right) g \\\\& \\rho_{\\ell}=\\text { density of water. }\\end{aligned}$$<br>As the system is in equilibrium<br>${T_1} = {T_2}$<br>$\\left( {{m_1} - {\\rho _\\ell }{V_1}} \\right)g = \\left( {{m_2} - {\\rho _\\ell }{V_2}} \\right)g$<br>$36 - 1 \\times \\frac{{36}}{9} = 48 - 1 \\times \\frac{{48}}{{{\\rho _2}}}$<br>$ \\Rightarrow {\\rho _2} = 3\\,g/c{m^3}$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/26_37858_question_1.jpg'),
(37859,'physics',27,1380,3309,'Two rods of different metals having the same area of cross-section $A$ are placed between the two massive walls as shown in figure. The first rod has a length $l_{1}$, coefficient of linear expansion $\\alpha_{1}$ and Young\'s modulus $Y_{1}$. The corresponding quantities for second rod are $l_{2}, \\alpha_{2}$ and $Y_{2}$. The temperature of both the rods is now raised by $\\Delta T$. The displacement of the junction is','[{"text": "$l_{1} l_{2} \\\\Delta T$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{l_{1} l_{2} \\\\Delta T\\\\left(Y_{1} \\\\alpha_{1} \\\\sim Y_{2} \\\\alpha_{2}\\\\right)}{Y_{1} l_{2}+Y_{2} l_{1}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{l_{1} l_{2} \\\\Delta T\\\\left(Y_{1} l_{2}+Y_{2} l_{1}\\\\right)}{Y_{1} \\\\alpha_{1}+Y_{2} \\\\alpha_{2}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{l_{1} l_{2}\\\\left(Y_{1} \\\\alpha_{1}-Y_{2} \\\\alpha_{2}\\\\right) \\\\Delta T}{Y_{1} l_{1}+Y_{2} l_{2}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Let $\\Delta l$ is the displacement of the junction towards right. If the rods are free to expand without any constrains then the two rods should expand by ${l_1}{\\alpha _1}\\Delta T\\,\\& \\,{\\rm{ }}{l_2}{\\alpha _2}\\Delta T$ as shown in the figure.<br><img src=\'/img/data/physics/27_37859_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' /> <br>fig (1)-Initial position of the combination before rising temperature.<br>fig (2)- Expected natural extensions in the rods after rising temperature.<br>fig (3)- Final position of the combination after rising temperature.<br>By comparing fig (2) & fig (3) we can conclude that both rods are in compression.<br>$\\text { Strain }=\\dfrac{l_{1} \\alpha_{1} \\Delta T-\\Delta l}{l_{1}}$<br>Force exerted by the first rod at the junction<br>$F_{1}=\\dfrac{Y_{1} A\\left(l_{1} \\alpha_{1} \\Delta T-\\Delta l\\right)}{l_{1}}$<br>Strain in the second rod<br>$\\text { Strain }=\\dfrac{l_{2} \\alpha_{2} \\Delta T+\\Delta l}{l_{2}}$<br>Force exerted by the second is<br>$F_{2}=\\dfrac{Y_{2} A\\left(l_{2} \\alpha_{2} \\Delta T+\\Delta l\\right)}{l_{2}}$<br>As the system is in equilibrium $F_{1}=F_{2}$<br>$\\Rightarrow \\Delta l=\\dfrac{l_{1} l_{2} \\Delta T\\left(Y_{1} \\alpha_{1}-Y_{2} \\alpha_{2}\\right)}{Y_{1} l_{2}+Y_{2} l_{1}}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/27_37859_question_1.png'),
(37860,'physics',29,1301,3147,'A cube vessel (with faces horizontal + vertical) contains an ideal gas at NTP. The vessel is being carried by a rocket which is moving at a speed of $500\\,\\,m{s^{ - 1}}$ in vertical direction. The pressure of the gas inside the vessel as observed by us on the ground','[{"text": "Remains the same because $500\\\\,\\\\,m{s^{ - 1}}$ is very much smaller than $v_{r m s}$ of the gas", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Remains the same because motion of the vessel at a whole does not effect the relative motion of the gas molecules and the walls.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Will increases by a factor equal to $v_{r m s}^{2}+(500)^{2} . / v_{r m s}^{2}$ where $v_{r m s}$ was the original mean square velocity of the gas.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Will be different on the top wall and bottom wall of the vessel.<br>", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'The pressure of the gas inside the vessel, as observed by us, on the ground remains the same. This is because the rocket is moving with constant velocity. Hence pseudo force does not act on the gas particle.',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,'NCERT Exemplar','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37862,'physics',26,1135,2714,'An L-shaped glass tube is just immersed in flowing water such that its opening is pointing against flowing water. If the speed of water current is $$v$$, then','[{"text": "The water in the tube rises to height $$\\\\dfrac{v^{2}}{2 g}$$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "The water in the tube rises to height $$\\\\dfrac{g}{2 v^{2}}$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "The water in the tube does not rise at all", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "None of these", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'When the liquid is in horizontal part of the tube it has velocity $$v$$. When it starts moving vertically its velocity decreases because of gravitational force. The total height travelled by the liquid calculated from conservation of mechanical energy. For a liquid particle of mass $$m$$<br>$$\\begin{gathered}m g(h+H)=\\dfrac{1}{2} m v^{2} \\\\h+H=\\dfrac{v^{2}}{2 g}\\end{gathered}$$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/26_37862_question_1.png'),
(37863,'physics',27,1380,3311,'A solid body of linear expansion coefficient $2 \\times {10^{ - 5}}{\\left( {^\\circ C} \\right)^{ - 1}}$ floats in a liquid with $20 \\%$ of its volume out of the liquid at a temperature $20^\\circ C$. The volumetric expansion coefficient of liquid is 3 times that of the solid body\'s volumetric expansion coefficient. If the temperature is increased to $70^\\circ C$, the new dfraction of the body\'s volume out of the liquid will be approximately;','[{"text": "$19.7 \\\\%$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$20.1 \\\\%$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$19.5 \\\\%$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$20.7 \\\\%$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Let $V_{S}$ is the volume submerged, $V_{T}$ is the total volume of the solid<br>$\\dfrac{V_{S}}{V_{T}}=\\dfrac{\\rho_{b}}{\\rho_{L}}=0.8$<br>$\\rho_{b}-$ density of block; $\\rho_{L}-$ density of liquid<br>$\\begin{aligned}\\Delta T & =50^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C} \\\\\\dfrac{V_{S}^{\\prime}}{V_{T}^{\\prime}} & =\\dfrac{\\rho_{b}^{\\prime}}{\\rho_{L}^{\\prime}}=\\dfrac{\\rho_{b}}{\\rho_{L}} \\times \\dfrac{1+\\gamma_{L} \\Delta T}{1+\\gamma_{b} \\Delta T} \\\\& =0.8 \\times\\left(\\dfrac{1+18 \\times 10^{-5} \\times 50}{1+6 \\times 10^{-5} \\times 50}\\right) \\\\& =0.8 \\times\\left(\\dfrac{1+9 \\times 10^{-3}}{1+3 \\times 10^{-3}}\\right) \\\\& =0.8 \\times\\left(1+6 \\times 10^{-3}\\right) \\\\\\dfrac{V_{T}^{\\prime}-V_{S}^{\\prime}}{V_{T}^{\\prime}} & =1-0.8-0.8 \\times 6 \\times 10^{-3} \\\\= & 0.1952\\end{aligned}$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37864,'physics',29,1300,3145,'1 mole of $H_{2}$ gas is contained in a box of volume $V = 100\\,{\\mkern 1mu} {m^3}\\,\\,at{\\text{ }}T = 300{\\mkern 1mu} K$. The gas is heated to a temperature of $T = 3000\\,K$ and the gas gets converted to a gas of hydrogen atoms The final pressure would be (considering all gases to be ideal)','[{"text": "Same as the pressure initially", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "2 times the pressure initially", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "10 times the pressure initially", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "20 times the pressure initially<br>", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Applying standard gas equation,<br>$\\begin{aligned}& \\dfrac{p_{1} V_{1}}{n_{1} T_{1}}=\\dfrac{p_{2} V_{2}}{n_{2} T_{2}} \\\\& \\dfrac{p_{2}}{p_{1}}=\\dfrac{V_{1}}{V_{2}} \\cdot \\dfrac{T_{2}}{T_{1}} \\cdot \\dfrac{n_{2}}{n_{1}}\\end{aligned}$<br>As $\\dfrac{T_{2}}{T_{1}}=\\dfrac{3000}{300}=10$ and every molecule of<br>${H_2}$ splits into hydrogen atoms, doubling the number, therefore $n_{2}=2 n_{1}$<br>$\\Rightarrow \\dfrac{p_{2}}{p_{1}}=2 \\times 10=20 .$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,'NCERT Exemplar','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37865,'physics',26,1135,2714,'Consider a horizontally oriented syringe containing water located at a height of $1.25\\,m$ above the ground. The diameter of the plunger is $8\\,mm$ and the diameter of the nozzle is $2\\,mm$. The plunger is pushed with a constant speed of $0.25\\,m/s$. Find the horizontal range of water stream on the ground. Take $g = 10\\,m/{s^2}$','[{"text": "$$4 m$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$2 m$$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$16\\\\,m$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1\\\\,m$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'From equation continuity<br>${A_1}{v_1} = {A_2}{v_2}$<br>$\\pi {\\left( {\\frac{8}{2}} \\right)^2}(0.25) = \\pi {\\left( {\\frac{2}{2}} \\right)^2} \\times {v_2} \\Rightarrow {v_2} = 4\\,m/s$<br>The horizontal displacement is<br>$$\\begin{aligned}& x=v_{2} t=4 t \\\\& \\because \\quad t=\\sqrt{\\dfrac{2 h}{g}}=\\sqrt{\\dfrac{2 \\times 1.25}{10}}=\\dfrac{1}{2} s \\\\& \\Rightarrow \\quad x=4 \\times \\dfrac{1}{2}=2 m\\end{aligned}$$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/26_37865_question_1.png'),
(37866,'physics',27,1380,3311,'A solid cube is first floating in a liquid. The coefficient of linear expansion of cube is $\\alpha$ and the coefficient of volume expansion of liquid is $\\gamma$. On increasing the temperature of (liquid + cube) system, the cube will sink if','[{"text": "$\\\\gamma=3 \\\\alpha$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\gamma>3 \\\\alpha$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\gamma < 3 \\\\alpha$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\gamma=2 \\\\alpha$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'If $\\gamma>3 \\alpha$ then for a same temperature rise the liquid expands more than solid and the liquid density decreases. So the cube should submerge more to balance its weight',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/27_37866_question_1.png'),
(37867,'physics',28,1371,3289,'In thermodynamic processes which of the following statements is not true?','[{"text": "In an adiabatic processes the system is insulated from the surroundings", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "In an isochoric process pressure remains constant", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "In an isothermal process the temperature remains constant", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "In an adiabatic process $p V^{\\\\gamma}=$ constant", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'For an adiabatic process there should not be any exchange of heat between the system and its surroundings all walls of the container must be perfectly insulated. In adiabatic process gases obey $p V^{\\gamma}=$ constant. In an isochoric process volume remains constant and for isobaric process pressure remains constant.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37868,'physics',29,1301,3149,'A vessel of volume $V$ contains a mixture of 1 mole of hydrogen and 1 mole of oxygen (both considered as ideal). Let $f_{1}(v) d v$, denote the function for molecules of ${H_2}$ with speed between $v$ and $(v+d v)$ and $f_{2}(v) d v$ for oxygen. Then','[{"text": "$f_{2}(v)+f_{2}(v)=f(v)$ obeys the Maxwell\'s distribution law", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$f_{1}(v), f_{2}(v)$ will obey the Maxwell\'s distribution law", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Neither $f_{1}(v)$, nor $f_{2}(v)$ will obey the Maxwell\'s distribution law", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$f_{2}(v)$ and $f_{1}(v)$ will be the same<br>", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'As the vessel contains both hydrogen and oxygen, therfore, as per Maxwell\'s law of speed distribution, $f_{1}(v)$ and $f_{2}(v)$ will obey the law separately. (In general<br>$f(v)=$ constant $\\times v^{2} e^{-\\left(m v^{2} / 2 K T\\right)}$<br>Where $m = $ mass of one molecule)',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,'NCERT Exemplar','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38253,'physics',29,1301,3148,'At what temperature will oxygen molecules have the same root mean square speed as hydrogen molecules at $200 K$ ?','[{"text": "$527^\\\\circ C$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1327^\\\\circ C$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2127^\\\\circ C$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2927^\\\\circ C$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'$V_{r m s}=\\sqrt{\\dfrac{3 K T}{M}} \\Rightarrow T \\propto M$<br>$\\Rightarrow \\dfrac{T_{1}}{T_{2}}=\\dfrac{2}{32}$<br>$\\Rightarrow T_{2}=16 T_{1}=3200 \\mathrm{~K}$<br>$ = 2927^\\circ C$.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37869,'physics',26,1136,2717,'A water tank having a uniform cross-section area has an orifice of area of cross-section $a$ at the bottom through which water is discharging. There is constant inflow of $Q$ into the tank through another pipe. If $a\\sqrt {2\\,g} = K$, the time taken for the water level to rise from $h_{1}$ to $h_{2}$ will be','[{"text": "$t = \\\\frac{A}{{2{K^2}}}\\\\left[ \\\\begin{array}{l}<br>K\\\\left( {\\\\sqrt {{h_1}} &nbsp;- \\\\sqrt {{h_2}} } \\\\right) - Q\\\\\\\\<br>\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\mkern 1mu} {\\\\rm{In}}{\\\\mkern 1mu} \\\\left( {\\\\frac{{Q - K\\\\sqrt {{h_2}} }}{{Q - K\\\\sqrt {{h_1}} }}} \\\\right)<br>\\\\end{array} \\\\right]$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$t=\\\\dfrac{3 A}{K^{2}}\\\\left[K\\\\left(\\\\sqrt{h_{1}}-\\\\sqrt{h_{2}}\\\\right)-Q \\\\operatorname{In}\\\\left(\\\\dfrac{Q-K \\\\sqrt{h_{2}}}{Q-K \\\\sqrt{h_{1}}}\\\\right)\\\\right]$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$t=\\\\dfrac{2 A}{K^{2}}\\\\left[K\\\\left(\\\\sqrt{h_{1}}-\\\\sqrt{h_{2}}\\\\right)-Q \\\\operatorname{In}\\\\left(\\\\dfrac{Q-K \\\\sqrt{h_{2}}}{Q-K \\\\sqrt{h_{1}}}\\\\right)\\\\right]$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$t=\\\\dfrac{A}{K^{2}}\\\\left[K\\\\left(\\\\sqrt{h_{1}}-\\\\sqrt{h_{2}}\\\\right)-Q \\\\operatorname{In}\\\\left(\\\\dfrac{Q-K \\\\sqrt{h_{2}}}{Q-K \\\\sqrt{h_{1}}}\\\\right)\\\\right]$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'$\\dfrac{d V}{d t}=(Q-a v)$<br>$\\frac{{Adh}}{{dt}} = (Q - av) \\Rightarrow \\int_{{h_1}}^{{h_2}} {\\frac{{Adh}}{{Q - K\\sqrt h }}} = \\int\\limits_0^t {dt} $<br>$t = \\frac{{2A}}{{{K^2}}}\\left[ {K\\left( {\\sqrt {{h_1}} - \\sqrt {{h_2}} } \\right) - Q\\ln \\left( {\\frac{{Q - K\\sqrt {{h_2}} }}{{Q - K\\sqrt {{h_1}} }}} \\right)} \\right]$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37871,'physics',28,1372,3298,'A point on $P-V$ diagram represents','[{"text": "The condition of a system", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Work done on or by the system", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Work done in a cyclic process", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "A thermodynamics process", "isCorrect": false}]',0,NULL,NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37872,'physics',29,1300,3145,'An inflated rubber balloon contains one mole of an ideal gas, has a pressure $P$, volume $V$and temperature $T$. If the temperature rises to 1.1 $T$, and the volume is increased to $1.05 \\,\\,V$. The final pressure will be','[{"text": "$1.1\\\\,\\\\,P$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$P$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Less than $P$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Between $p$ and $1.1\\\\,\\\\,P$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Here, $p_{1}=p, V_{1}=V, T_{1}=T$<br>$\\begin{aligned}& \\text { from } \\dfrac{p_{1} V_{1}}{T_{1}}=\\dfrac{p_{2} V_{2}}{T_{2}} \\\\& p_{2}=p_{1} \\dfrac{V_{1}}{V_{2}} \\cdot \\dfrac{T_{2}}{T_{1}} \\\\& =p \\times \\dfrac{V}{1.05 V} \\times 1.1 \\dfrac{T}{T}=1.1 \\dfrac{p}{1.05} \\\\& p_{2}=1.05 p \\text {. }\\end{aligned}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,'NCERT Exemplar','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37873,'physics',26,1136,2717,'Water is filled in a uniform fixed container of area of cross section \' $$A$$ \'. A hole of cross section area \' $$a$$ \' is made in the container at a height of $$20 \\mathrm{~m}$$ above the base. Water streams out horizontally and hits a small block placed at some distance from the container. With what speed (in $$\\mathrm{ms}^{-1}$$ ) the block should be moved such that water streams always hits the block.<br>$$\\left(\\text { Given } \\dfrac{a}{A}=\\dfrac{1}{20}\\right) \\text {. }$$<br>[Height of the water level in the container is sufficient, take $$\\mathrm{g}=10 \\mathrm{~ms}^{-2}$$ ]','[{"text": "$1\\\\,m/s$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$2\\\\,m/s$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1.5\\\\,m/s$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1.8\\\\,m/s$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Velocity of efflux $$v=\\sqrt{2 g y}$$<br>Range $$x=\\sqrt{2 g y} \\times \\sqrt{\\dfrac{2 h}{g}}$$<br><img src=\'/img/data/physics/26_37873_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' /> <br>The velocity of the block is $$\\left(\\dfrac{d x}{d t}\\right)$$.<br>$\\therefore {v_b} = \\frac{{dx}}{{dt}} = \\sqrt {\\frac{{2h}}{g}}  \\times \\sqrt {2g}  \\times \\frac{1}{{2\\sqrt y }}\\frac{{dy}}{{dt}}$<br>${v_b} = \\frac{{\\sqrt h }}{{\\sqrt y }} \\cdot \\frac{{dy}}{{dt}}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\left( 1 \\right)$<br>Using equation of continuity <br>$\\frac{{Ady}}{{dt}} = a\\sqrt {2gy} \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\left( 2 \\right)$<br>Equation (1) and (2) $$v_{b}=\\dfrac{\\sqrt{h}}{\\sqrt{y}} \\times \\dfrac{a}{y} \\sqrt{2 g y}$$<br>$$v_{b}=\\sqrt{2 g h} \\times \\dfrac{a}{y}=20 \\times \\dfrac{1}{20}=1 m s^{-1} .$$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37875,'physics',28,1369,3279,'The amount of heat energy required to raise the temperature of $1\\,g$ of Helium at NTP, from ${T_1}\\,K$ to ${T_2}\\,K$ is','[{"text": "$\\\\frac{3}{8}\\\\,{N_a}{k_B}\\\\left( {{T_2} - {T_1}} \\\\right)$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{3}{2}\\\\,{N_a}{k_B}\\\\left( {{T_2} - {T_1}} \\\\right)$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{3}{4}\\\\,{N_a}{k_B}\\\\left( {{T_2} - {T_1}} \\\\right)$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{3}{4}\\\\,{N_a}{k_B}\\\\left( {\\\\frac{{{T_2}}}{{\\\\,{T_1}}}} \\\\right)$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'The amount of heat energy required,<br>$Q = \\frac{f}{2}nR\\Delta T$<br>$ \\Rightarrow Q = \\frac{3}{2} \\times \\frac{1}{4} \\times {k_B}{N_a}\\Delta T = \\frac{3}{8}\\,{N_a}{k_B}\\left( {{T_2} - {T_1}} \\right)$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37876,'physics',29,1300,3145,'An oxygen cylinder of volume $30{\\mkern 1mu} \\,L$ has an initial gauge pressure of $15\\,\\,atm$ and a temperature of $27^\\circ C$. After some oxygen is withdrawn from the cylinder the gauge pressure drops to $17^\\circ C$. The mass of oxygen taken out of the cylinder $\\left( {R = 8.31\\,\\,J\\,mo{l^{ - 1}} {K^{ - 1}}} \\right)$. Moleculer mass of<br>$\\left( {{O_2} = 32u} \\right)$ is','[{"text": "$0.14\\\\, g$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$0.02 \\\\,g$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$0.14 \\\\,\\\\,kg$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$0.014 \\\\,\\\\,kg$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Absolute pressure ${p_1} = (15 + 1)\\,atm$<br>$[\\because$ Absolute pressure $=$ Gauge pressure + atmospheric pressure]<br>$ = 16 \\times 1.013 \\times {10^5} Pa$<br>${V_1} = 30\\,L = 30 \\times {10^{ - 3}}\\,\\,{m^3}$<br>$T_{1}=273.15+27=300.15 K$<br>Using ideal gas equation<br>$\\begin{aligned}& n=\\dfrac{p_{1} V_{1}}{R T}=\\dfrac{16 \\times 1.013 \\times 10^{5} \\times 30 \\times 10^{-3}}{8.314 \\times 300.15} \\\\& =19.48\\end{aligned}$<br>Final ${p_2} = (11 + 1) = 12\\,\\, atm$<br>$ = 12 \\times 1.013 \\times {10^5}Pa$<br>${V_2} = 30L = 30 \\times {10^{ - 3}}{m^3}$<br>${T_2} = 273.15 + 17 = 290.15 K$<br>Number of moles<br>$\\begin{aligned}& \\quad=\\dfrac{p_{1} V_{1}}{R T}=\\dfrac{12 \\times 1.013 \\times 10^{5} \\times 30 \\times 10^{-3}}{8.314 \\times 300.15} \\\\& =14.61\\end{aligned}$<br>Hence, mole removed $=19.48-14.61=4.87$<br>Mass removed $ = 4.36 \\times 32 \\,g = 0.1558 \\,kg$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,'NCERT Exemplar','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37877,'physics',26,1136,2717,'An ideal fluid flows in the pipe as shown in the figure. The pressure in the fluid at the bottom $$P_{2}$$ is the same as it is at the top $$P_{1}$$. If the velocity at the top ${v_1} = 2\\,m/s$. Then the ratio of areas $$A_{1} / A_{2}$$ is:','[{"text": "$$4: 1$$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$$2: 1$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$4: 3$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$8: 1$$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Using equation of continuity, we have<br>$$v_{2}=\\dfrac{A_{1}}{A_{2}} v_{1}$$<br>From Bernoulli\'s theorem,<br>$$\\begin{aligned}& P_{0}+\\rho g h_{1}+\\dfrac{1}{2} \\rho v_{1}^{2}=P_{0}+\\rho g h_{2}+\\dfrac{1}{2} \\rho v_{2}^{2} \\\\\\Rightarrow & g\\left(h_{1}-h_{2}\\right)=\\dfrac{1}{2}\\left(v_{2}^{2}-v_{1}^{2}\\right) \\\\\\Rightarrow & 60=\\left(\\dfrac{A_{1}^{2}}{A_{2}^{2}}-1\\right) v_{1}^{2} \\\\\\Rightarrow & \\dfrac{A_{1}}{A_{2}}=\\dfrac{4}{1}\\end{aligned}$$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/26_37877_question_1.jpg'),
(37878,'physics',27,1380,3306,'The variation of lengths of two metal rods \\[A\\] and \\[B\\] with change in temperature are shown in figure. The coefficient of linear expansion \\(\\alpha_{A}\\) for the metal \\[A\\] will be: (Given \\[{\\alpha _B} = 9 \\times {10^{ - 6}}/^\\circ C\\] )<div class="matching-question"></div>','[{"text": "${\\\\alpha _A} = 3 \\\\times {10^{ - 6}}/^\\\\circ C$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\alpha _A} = 3 \\\\times {10^{ - 6}}/^\\\\circ C$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\alpha _A} = 27 \\\\times {10^{ - 6}}/^\\\\circ C$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\alpha _A} = 27 \\\\times {10^{ - 6}}/^\\\\circ C$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Slope of the line<br>$A=\\dfrac{1006-1000}{T}=\\dfrac{\\Delta L}{\\Delta T}=L_{0} \\alpha_{A}$<br>$\\frac{6}{T} = 1000\\,mm\\,{\\alpha _A}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\left( 1 \\right)$<br>similarly for line $B$<br>$\\frac{2}{T} = 1002\\,mm\\,{\\alpha _B}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\left( 2 \\right)$<br>From (1) & (2) $\\alpha_{A}=3 \\alpha_{B}$',NULL,'Hard','Graph/Figure',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/27_37878_question_1.png'),
(37879,'physics',28,1372,3294,'One mole of an ideal gas is taken from \\[A\\] to \\[B\\], from \\[B\\] to \\[C\\] and then back to \\[A\\]. The variation of its volume with temperature for that change is as shown.<br>Its pressure at \\[A\\] is \\[{P_0}\\], volume is \\[{V_0}\\]. Then, the internal energy<div class="matching-question"></div>','[{"text": "At $A$ is more than at $B$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "At $C$ is less than at $B$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "At $B$ is more than at $A$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "At $A$ and $B$ are equal", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'The internal energy of an ideal gas is only dependent upon its temperature.<br>From the graph ${T_A} = {T_B} \\Rightarrow {U_A} = {U_B}$',NULL,'Medium','Graph/Figure',2012,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/28_37879_question_1.png'),
(37880,'physics',29,1301,3148,'At what temperature is the root mean square speed of an atom in an argon gas cylinder equal to the rms speed of a helium gas atom at $ - 20^\\circ C$ ? (Atomic mass of  $Ar = 39.9\\,\\,u\\,\\,of{\\text{ }}He = 4.0\\,\\,u)$','[{"text": "$3.52 \\\\times {10^3}\\\\,\\\\,K$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2.52 \\\\times {10^2}\\\\,\\\\,K$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$2.52 \\\\times {10^4}\\\\,\\\\,K$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$4.52 \\\\times {10^5}\\\\,\\\\,K$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Root mean square speed of argon atom<br>${\\left( {{v_{rms}}} \\right)_{Ar}} = \\sqrt {\\frac{{3R{T_{Ar}}}}{{{M_{Ar}}}}} \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,(1)$<br>Root mean square speed of helium atom<br>${\\left( {{v_{rms}}} \\right)_{He}} = \\sqrt {\\frac{{3R{T_{He}}}}{{{M_{He}}}}} \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,(2)$<br>Dividing Eq. (1) by Eq. (2)<br>$\\dfrac{\\left(v_{r m s}\\right)_{A r}}{\\left(v_{r m s}\\right)_{H e}}=\\sqrt{\\dfrac{3 R T_{A r}}{M_{A r}} \\times \\dfrac{M_{H e}}{3 R T_{H e}}}$<br>$\\begin{gathered}\\text { Given }\\left(v_{r m s}\\right)_{A r}=\\left(v_{r m s}\\right)_{H e} \\\\\\Rightarrow 1=\\sqrt{\\left(\\dfrac{T_{A r}}{T_{H e}}\\right)\\left(\\dfrac{M_{H e}}{M_{A r}}\\right)} \\\\\\Rightarrow \\dfrac{M_{H e}}{M_{A r}}=\\dfrac{T_{H e}}{T_{A r}} \\\\T_{A r}=253.15\\left(\\dfrac{39.9}{4}\\right)=2.52 \\times 10^{2} \\mathrm{~K} .\\end{gathered}$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,'NCERT Exemplar','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37881,'physics',26,1136,2719,'A cylindrical container of radius \' $$R$$ \' and height \' $$h$$ \' is completely filled with a liqiud. Two horizontal $L$ shaped pipes of small cross-section area \' $$a$$ \' are connected to the cylinder as shown in the figure. Now the two pipes are opened and fluid starts coming out of the pipes horizontally in opposite directions. Then the torque due to ejected liquid on the system is:','[{"text": "$8agh\\\\rho R$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$4agh\\\\rho R$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$2agh\\\\rho R$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "None of these", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'$$\\tau=\\tau_{\\text {due to } F_{1}}+\\tau_{\\text {due to } F_{2}}$$<br>$$\\begin{aligned}& =F_{1}(R+R)+F_{2}(R+R) \\\\& =\\left(F_{1}+F_{2}\\right) 2 R \\quad F_{1}=F_{2} \\quad \\text { (Both torques are }\\end{aligned}$$additive, forms a couple)<br>$$\\begin{aligned}\\tau & =2 F \\cdot 2 R \\quad\\left(F=a v^{2} \\rho, v=\\sqrt{2 g\\left(\\dfrac{h}{2}\\right)}\\right) \\\\& =4 a 2 g \\dfrac{h}{2} \\rho \\cdot R=4 a \\rho g h R\\end{aligned}$$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/26_37881_question_1.png'),
(37882,'physics',27,1380,3312,'The ratio of the coefficient of volume expansion of a glass container to that of a viscous kept inside the container is $1: 4$. What dfraction of the inner volume of the container should the liquid occupy so that the volume of the remaining vacant space will be same at all temperatures?','[{"text": "$2: 5$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1: 4$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$1: 64$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1: 8$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Given that $\\dfrac{\\gamma_{c}}{\\gamma_{l}}=\\dfrac{1}{4}$<br>If the vacant space remains constant then change in volumes of liquid and total container must be equal<br>$\\begin{aligned}& \\Delta V_{l}=\\Delta V_{c} \\Rightarrow x \\gamma_{l} \\Delta T=V_{0} \\gamma_{c} \\Delta T \\\\& \\dfrac{x}{V_{0}}=\\dfrac{\\gamma_{c}}{\\gamma_{l}}=\\dfrac{1}{4}\\end{aligned}$<br>( $x$ - initial volume of the liquid)<br>( $V_{0}$ - initial volume of the container)',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',2013,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37883,'physics',28,1372,3294,'A thermodynamic system undergoes a cyclic process \\[ABC\\] as shown in the diagram. The work done by the system per cycle is<div class="matching-question"></div>','[{"text": "$ - 750\\\\,J$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$750\\\\,J$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1250\\\\,J$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$ - 1250\\\\,J$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Work done $=$ Area under $P-V$ curve<br>$ = \\frac{1}{2} \\times 300 \\times 5 = 750\\,J$<br>Since the work done by the system is in anticlockwise direction, work done will be negative i.e., $ - 750\\,J$.',NULL,'Medium','Graph/Figure',2019,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/28_37883_question_1.png'),
(37884,'physics',29,1299,3144,'I. The volume of gas particles is negligible in comparision with the volume of container.<br>II. According to kinetic theory, the interatomic forces are negligible in gases.<br>III. According to kinetic theory, the gas consists of rapidly moving atoms.<br>Which of the following statements are correct?','[{"text": "Both I and II", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Only III", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both II and III", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "I, II and III", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'option (4) is correct',NULL,'Medium','MCQ type-2',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37885,'physics',26,1136,2719,'Water (density $$\\rho$$ ) is flowing through the uniform tube of cross-sectional area $A$ with a constant speed $$v$$ as shown in the figure. The magnitude of force exerted by the water on the curved corner of the tube is (neglect viscous forces)','[{"text": "$$2 \\\\rho A v^{2}$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$\\\\sqrt{3} \\\\rho A v^{2}$$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$$\\\\dfrac{\\\\rho A v^{2}}{\\\\sqrt{2}}$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$\\\\sqrt{2} \\\\rho A v^{2}$$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'The thrust force is $$F=\\dfrac{d m}{d t}\\left|\\vec{v}_{2}-\\vec{v}_{1}\\right|$$<br>$$=A v \\rho\\left|\\vec{v}_{2}-\\vec{v}_{1}\\right| \\quad\\left|v_{2}\\right|=\\left|v_{1}\\right|$$<br>angle between $$v_{2}, v_{1}$$ is $$120^{\\circ}$$<br>$$=A v \\rho(\\sqrt{3} v)$$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/26_37885_question_1.png'),
(37886,'physics',27,1380,3307,'On a linear temperature scale $Y$, water freezes at $ - 160^\\circ Y$ and boils at $ - 500^\\circ Y$. On this Y scale, a temperature of $340\\,K$ would be read as: (Water freezes at $273\\,K$ and boils at $373\\,K$)','[{"text": "$-73.7^{\\\\circ} Y$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$-233.7^{\\\\circ} Y$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$-86.3^{\\\\circ} Y$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$ - 106.3^\\\\circ Y$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'The two scales are shown in the figure.<br><img src=\'/img/data/physics/27_37886_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' /> <br>From the figure we can write<br>$\\begin{aligned}& \\dfrac{-50^{\\circ} Y-\\left(-160^{\\circ} Y\\right)}{x-\\left(-160^{\\circ} Y\\right)}=\\dfrac{37 K-273 K}{340 K-273 K} \\\\& \\Rightarrow \\quad x=-86.3^{\\circ} Y\\end{aligned}$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',2013,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37887,'physics',28,1372,3294,'A thermodynamic system is taken through the cycle \\(A B C D\\) as shown in the figure. Heat rejected by the gas during the cycle is<div class="matching-question"></div>','[{"text": "$PV$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2\\\\,PV$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$4\\\\,PV$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1/2\\\\,PV$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'From the first law of thermodynamics $Q=\\Delta U+\\Delta W$<br>For cyclic process $\\Delta U=0$<br>Hence $Q = W$<br>Work done $W = $ area under $P - V$ diagram<br>$ \\Rightarrow Q =  - 2\\,PV.$',NULL,'Medium','Graph/Figure',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/28_37887_question_1.png'),
(37888,'physics',29,1306,3156,'I. In solids and liquids, the interatomic forces are important.<br>II. In solids and liquids, the atoms attract when they are at a few angstroms but repel when they come closer.<br>Which of the following statements are correct?','[{"text": "Only I", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Only II", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both I and II", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Neither I nor II", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'In liquids and solids atoms or molecules present close by each other so the interatomic or molecular forces will show effect on their physical properties like melting, boiling etc. So option (3) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ type-2',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37889,'physics',26,1136,2720,'In the siphon as shown, which one of the options is not correct, if $$h_{2}>h_{1}, h_{3} < h_{1}$$ ?','[{"text": "$$p_{E} < p_{D}$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$p_{E}>p_{C}$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$p_{B}>p_{C}$$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$$p_{B} < p_{E}$$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Pressure is minimum at the highest point of the tube. Pressures are same at $B$ and $C$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/26_37889_question_1.png'),
(37890,'physics',27,1380,3307,'A simple pendulum made of a bob of mass $m$ and a metallic wire of negligible mass has time period $2\\,s$ at $T = 0^\\circ C$. If the temperature of the wire is increased and the corresponding change in its time period is plotted against its temperature, the resulting graph is a line of slop $S$. If the coefficient of linear expansion of metal is $\\alpha$ then the value of $S$ is :','[{"text": "$\\\\dfrac{1}{\\\\alpha}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2 \\\\alpha$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{\\\\alpha}{2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\alpha$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'The initial time period is<br>$t_{\\mathrm{o}}=2=2 \\pi \\sqrt{\\dfrac{l_{0}}{g}}$<br>The initial time period after rise in temperature is<br>$\\begin{aligned}& t=2 \\pi \\sqrt{\\dfrac{l_{\\mathrm{o}}(1+\\alpha T)}{g}} \\\\& =2(1+\\alpha T)^{1 / 2}=2+\\alpha T \\\\& t-t_{0}=\\alpha T \\Rightarrow \\Delta t=\\alpha T\\end{aligned}$<br><img src=\'/img/data/physics/27_37890_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',2016,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37891,'physics',28,1372,3293,'One mole of an ideal diatomic gas undergoes a transition from \\[A\\] to \\[B\\] along a path \\[AB\\] as shown in the figure. The change in internal energy of the gas during the transition is<div class="matching-question"></div>','[{"text": "$20\\\\,kJ$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$ - 20\\\\,kJ$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$20\\\\,J$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$ - 12\\\\,kJ$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'The change in internal energy of the gas<br>For diatomic gas $\\gamma=\\dfrac{7}{5}$<br>$\\Delta U = \\frac{{{P_2}\\,{V_2} - {P_1}\\,{V_1}}}{{\\gamma  - 1}} = \\frac{{2 \\times 6 - 4 \\times 5}}{{\\frac{7}{5} - 1}} =  - 20\\,kJ$',NULL,'Medium','Graph/Figure',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/28_37891_question_1.png'),
(37892,'physics',29,1299,3144,'<div class="matching-question"><div class="column-left">Molecules of air do not fall and settle on the ground due to gravity.<br></div><div class="column-right">Air molecules undergo inelastic collisions.</div></div>','[{"text": "Statement A is correct but Statement B is incorrect.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Statement A is incorrect but Statement B is correct.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both statements are correct.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Statements are incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Option (1) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ type-2',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37893,'physics',26,1136,2720,'In the siphon system shown $V$ refers to velocity and $p$ refers to pressure. then;','[{"text": "$$V_{E}>V_{A}$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$p_{D}=p_{o}-\\\\rho g\\\\left(h_{1}+h_{2}\\\\right)$$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$$V_{E} \\\\neq V_{B} \\\\neq V_{E}$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$V_{E}=5 V_{A}+V_{B}$$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'From the equation of continuity $AV = $ constant the velocity of the liquid is same everywhere inside the tube as cross sectional area is constant.<br>$$V_{A}=V_{B}=V_{C}=V_{D}=V_{E}$$<br>Take the reference line at $E$. By applying Bernoulli\'s equation at $D$ and $E$ we get<br>$$\\begin{gathered}p_{D}+\\dfrac{1}{2} \\rho V^{2}+\\rho g\\left(h_{1}+h_{2}\\right)=p_{o}+\\dfrac{1}{2} \\rho V^{2}+\\rho g(0) \\\\p_{D}=p_{o}-\\rho g\\left(h_{1}+h_{2}\\right)\\end{gathered}$$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/26_37893_question_1.png'),
(37894,'physics',27,1380,3309,'A compressive force, $F$ is applied at the two ends of a long thin steel rod. It is heated, simultaneously, Such that its temperature  increases by $\\Delta T$. The net charge in its length is zero. Let $l$ be the length of the rod, A its area of cross-section, $Y$ its Young\'s modulus, and $\\alpha$ its coefficient of linear expansions. then $F$ is equal to:','[{"text": "$l^{2} Y \\\\alpha \\\\Delta T$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$l A Y \\\\alpha \\\\Delta T$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$A Y \\\\propto \\\\Delta T$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{A Y}{\\\\alpha \\\\Delta T}$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Due to thermal expansion, change in length<br>$\\begin{gathered}Y=\\dfrac{F / A}{\\Delta l / l} \\\\F=A Y \\alpha \\Delta T \\quad\\left(\\dfrac{\\Delta l}{l}=\\alpha \\Delta T\\right)\\end{gathered}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2017,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38254,'physics',27,1378,3304,'The reading of Centigrade thermometer coincides with that of Fahrenheit thermometer in a liquid. The temperature of the liquid is','[{"text": "$ - 40^\\\\circ C$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$ 0^\\\\circ C$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$ 100^\\\\circ C$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$ 300^\\\\circ C$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'$\\left[\\dfrac{C-32}{180}=\\dfrac{C-0}{100}\\right]$<br>$\\Rightarrow 5 C-160=9 C$<br>$\\Rightarrow C=-40$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37896,'physics',29,1303,3152,'<div class="matching-question"><div class="column-left">The ratio of specific heat of a gas at constant pressure and specific heat at constant volume for a diatomic gas is more than that for a monoatomic gas<br></div><div class="column-right">The molecules of a monoatomic gas have more degree of freedom than those of a diatomic gas.</div></div>','[{"text": "Statement A is correct but Statement B is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Statement A is incorrect but Statement B is correct.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both statements are correct.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Statements are incorrect.", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'For a monoatomic gas, number of degree of freedom $f=3$, and for a diatomic gas, $f=5$,<br>As, $\\dfrac{C_{P}}{C_{V}}=\\gamma=1+\\dfrac{2}{f}$<br>For monoatomic gas, $\\dfrac{C_{P}}{C_{V}}=\\dfrac{5}{3}=1.67$ and<br>For diatomic gas, $\\dfrac{C_{P}}{C_{V}}=\\dfrac{7}{5}=1.4$<br>$\\left(\\dfrac{C_{P}}{C_{V}}\\right)_{\\text {monoatomic }}>\\left(\\dfrac{C_{P}}{C_{V}}\\right)_{\\text {diatomic }}$<br>Both statements are wrong. So option (4) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ type-2',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37897,'physics',26,1136,2720,'A pipe of uniform cross-section is used to siphon an ideal fluid of density $$\\rho$$ from one tank to another, as shown below. The lower end of the pipe is above the liquid level in the receiving tank. If the atmospheric pressure is $$p_{a}$$, then','[{"text": "Pressure at $$6=$$ pressure at 1", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Pressure at 4 less than pressure at 1", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Pressure at $$4=$$ pressure at 3", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "All the above are true", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Take the reference line at point 1 . By applying Bernoulli\'s equation at the points 6 and 1 we get<br>$$\\begin{gathered}p_{a}+\\dfrac{1}{2} \\rho(0)^{2}+\\rho g h_{2}=p_{a}+\\dfrac{1}{2} \\rho V_{1}^{2}+\\rho g(0) \\\\v_{1}=\\sqrt{2 g h_{2}}\\end{gathered}$$<br>From the equation of continuity<br>$$V_{1}=V_{2}=V_{3}=V_{4}=V_{5}$$<br>By applying Bernoulli\'s equation at 1 and 2<br>$$\\begin{gathered}p_{a}+\\dfrac{1}{2} \\rho V_{1}^{2}+\\rho g(0)=p_{2}+\\dfrac{1}{2} \\rho V_{2}^{2}+\\rho g h_{2} \\\\p_{2}=p_{a}-\\rho g h_{2}\\end{gathered}$$<br>similarly we can prove<br>$$\\begin{aligned}& p_{3}=p_{4}=p_{a}-\\rho g\\left(h_{1}+h_{2}\\right) \\\\& p_{5}=p_{a}-\\rho g h_{2} \\\\& p_{3}=p_{4}=p_{a}-\\rho g\\left(h_{1}+h_{2}\\right) \\\\& p_{5}=p_{a}-\\rho g h_{2}\\end{aligned}$$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/26_37897_question_1.png'),
(37898,'physics',27,1380,3309,'A steel rail of length $5\\,m$ and area of cross section $40\\,c{m^2}$ is prevented from expanding along its length while the temperature rises by $10^\\circ C$. If coefficient of linear expansion and young\'s modulus of steel are $1.2 \\times {10^{ - 5}}\\,{K^{ - 1}}$ and $2 \\times {10^{11}}N{m^{ - 2}}$ respectively, the force developed in the rail is approxmately:','[{"text": "$2 \\\\times {10^7}\\\\,N$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2 \\\\times {10^9}\\\\,N$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$3 \\\\times {10^{ - 5}}\\\\,N$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1 \\\\times {10^5}\\\\,N$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'$Y=\\dfrac{F / A}{\\Delta l / l} \\Rightarrow F=\\dfrac{Y A \\Delta l}{l}$<br>$F=Y A \\alpha \\Delta T$<br>( where $\\alpha \\Delta T$ is the thermal strain )<br>$ = 2 \\times {10^{11}} \\times 40 \\times {10^{ - 4}} \\times 1.2 \\times {10^{ - 5}} \\times 10$<br>$ = 9.6 \\times {10^4} = 1 \\times {10^5}\\,N$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',2017,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37899,'physics',28,1371,3284,'How much heat energy in joules must be supplied to $14\\,g$ of nitrogen at room temperature to raise its temperature by $40^\\circ C$ at constant pressure? (Mol. wt. of ${N_2} = 28{\\rm{ }}g$)','[{"text": "$50\\\\,R$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$60\\\\,R$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$70\\\\,R$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$80\\\\,R$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'As nitrogen is diatomic, its molar specific heat at constant pressure is<br>$C_{p}=\\dfrac{7}{2} R$<br>No. of moles, $n=\\dfrac{14}{28}=\\dfrac{1}{2}$<br>$\\therefore \\Delta Q=n C_{p} \\Delta T$<br>$=\\dfrac{1}{2} \\times \\dfrac{7}{2} R \\times 40=70 R$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37900,'physics',29,1299,3144,'<div class="matching-question"><div class="column-left">In case of collision of gas molecules in a given amount of gas, total kinetic energy is not conserved.<br></div><div class="column-right">All collisions of the gas molecules in a given amount of gas are elastic.</div></div>','[{"text": "Statement A is correct but Statement B is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Statement A is incorrect but Statement B is correct.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both statements are correct.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Statements are incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Option (2) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ type-2',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37901,'physics',26,1136,2720,'In the siphon system shown below find the pressure at point 2 .','[{"text": "$70.5\\\\,kPa$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$8.5\\\\,kPa$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$125.5\\\\,kPa$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$15.5\\\\,kPa$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Take the reference line at point 3<br>By applying Bernoulli\'s equation at 1 and 3 we get<br>${p_o} + \\frac{1}{2}\\rho \\left( {{0^2}} \\right) + \\rho g(3)$<br>$ = {p_o} + \\frac{1}{2}\\rho V_3^2 + \\rho g(0){V_3}$<br>$ = \\sqrt {6g}  = 7.76\\,m/s$<br>By applying equation of continuity at 2 and 3 we get<br>${V_2} = \\frac{{{V_3}{A_3}}}{{{A_2}}} = 3.03\\,m/s$<br>By applying Bernoulli\'s equation at 2 and 3<br>${p_2} + \\frac{1}{2}\\rho V_2^2 + \\rho g(0) = {p_o} + \\frac{1}{2}\\rho V_3^2 + \\rho g(0)$<br>${p_2} = 125.5\\,k{p_a}$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/26_37901_question_1.png'),
(37902,'physics',27,1380,3309,'A rod, of length $L$ at room temperature and uniform area of cross-section $A$, is made of a metal having coefficient of linear expansion $\\alpha /^\\circ C$. It is observed that an external compressive force $F$, is applied on each of its ends, prevents any change in the length of the rod, when its temperature rises by $\\Delta TK$. Young\'s modulus $Y$, for this metal is','[{"text": "$\\\\frac{{2\\\\,F}}{{\\\\,A\\\\alpha \\\\Delta T}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{F}{A \\\\alpha \\\\Delta T}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{F}{2 A \\\\alpha \\\\Delta T}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{F}{{\\\\,A\\\\alpha (\\\\Delta T - 273)}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'As the temperature of rod rises, <br>Extension in the rod, $\\Delta L = L\\alpha \\Delta T$<br>Young\'s modulus of the rod, $Y = \\frac{{F/A}}{{\\Delta L/L}}$<br>$Y = \\frac{{FL}}{{A\\Delta L}} = \\frac{{FL}}{{AL\\alpha \\Delta T}} = \\frac{F}{{A\\alpha \\Delta T}}$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',2019,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37903,'physics',28,1371,3282,'In the equation $P V^{\\gamma}=$ constant, the value of $\\gamma$ is unity. Then the process is','[{"text": "Isothermal", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Adiabatic", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Isobaric", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Irreversible", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'$P V=$ constant represent isothermal process',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(46429,'physics',40,240,479,'A hydrogen atom in ground state is given an energy of  $10.2\\,eV.$ How many spectral lines will be emitted due to transition of electrons?','[{"text": "3", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "6", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "10", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "1", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'$\\Delta E=13.6\\left(\\dfrac{1}{1^{2}}-\\dfrac{1}{n^{2}}\\right)=10.2$<br>$\\Rightarrow n=2$<br>No. of spectral lines $=\\dfrac{n(n-1)}{2}=\\dfrac{2(2-1)}{2}=1$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2024,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37904,'physics',29,1302,3150,'<div class="matching-question"><div class="column-left">Average kinetic energy per molecule of any ideal monoatomic gas is $\\dfrac{1}{2} k_{B} T$<br></div><div class="column-right">Average kinetic energy for a monoatomic gas depends on nature of the gas.</div></div>','[{"text": "Statement A is correct but Statement B is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Statement A is incorrect but Statement B is correct.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both statements are correct.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Statements are incorrect.", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'$K.{E_{avg}}$ for a monoatomic gas is $\\dfrac{3}{2} k_{B} T$. It depends on temperature, not on the nature of the gas. So both statements are wrong. option (4) is correct.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ type-2',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37905,'physics',26,1133,2708,'A triangular lamina of area $$A$$ and height $$h$$ is immersed in a liquid of density $$\\rho$$ in a vertical plane with its base touching the surface of the liquid. The pressure force on one surface of the lamina is','[{"text": "$$\\\\dfrac{1}{2} A \\\\rho g h$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$\\\\dfrac{1}{3} A \\\\rho g h$$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$$\\\\dfrac{1}{6} \\\\mathrm{~A} \\\\rho g \\\\mathrm{~h}$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$\\\\dfrac{2}{3} A \\\\rho g h$$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Thrust on<br>lamina $$=$$ pressure at centroid $$\\times$$ Area<br>$$=\\dfrac{h \\rho g}{3} \\times A=\\dfrac{1}{3} A \\rho g h$$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37906,'physics',27,1380,3306,'A thin bimetallic strip is rigidly attached on its bottom surface as shown. Coefficient of linear expansion of strip A is more than strip B. If temperature of system is decreased, find correct option among the following','[{"text": "Bends towards left", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Bends towards right", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Neither bends nor shrinks", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Shrinks but does not bends", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'As metal A contracts more than metal $B\\left(\\because \\alpha_{A}>\\alpha_{B}\\right)$, the bimetallic strip bends more towards the side of $A$. So ption (1) is correct',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2021,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/27_37906_question_1.png'),
(37907,'physics',28,1371,3282,'During an isothermal expansion, a confined ideal gas does $ - 150\\,J$ of work against its surroundings. This implies that','[{"text": "$300\\\\,J$ of heat has been added to the gas", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "No heat is transferred because the process is isothermal", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$150\\\\,J$ of heat has been added to the gas", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$150\\\\,J$ heat has been removed from the gas", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'From the first law of the thermodynamics<br>$\\Delta U=Q-W$<br>For isothermaal processes, $\\Delta U=0$<br>$\\therefore Q=W$<br>Given, $W = 150\\,J$ (Work done by the gas)<br>Where $Q$ is positive, the heat is added to the gas.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37908,'physics',29,1301,3149,'<div class="matching-question"><div class="column-left">The ratio of rms speed and average speed of a gas molecules at a given temperature is $\\sqrt{3}: \\sqrt{2}$<br></div><div class="column-right">$c_{r m s} < c_{a v}$.</div></div>','[{"text": "Statement A is correct but Statement B is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Statement A is incorrect but Statement B is correct.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both statements are correct.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Statements are incorrect.", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'$c_{r m s}=\\sqrt{\\dfrac{3 k T}{m}}$ and ${c_{avg{\\rm{ }}}} = \\sqrt {\\frac{{8kT}}{{\\pi m}}} $<br>$\\therefore \\dfrac{c_{r m s}}{c_{\\text {avg }}}=\\dfrac{\\sqrt{3}}{(\\sqrt{8 / \\pi})}>1$<br>Both statements are wrong. So option (4) is correct.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ type-2',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37909,'physics',26,1133,2708,'A tube in vertical plane is shown in figure. It is filled with a liquid of density $$\\rho$$ and its end $B$ is closed. Then the force exerted by the fluid on the tube at end $B$ will be: [Neglect atmospheric pressure and assume the radius of the tube to be negligible in comparison to $$l]$$','[{"text": "$$\\\\rho g l A_{0}$$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "0", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Cannot be determined", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$2 \\\\rho g l A_{0}$$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'$$F=\\Delta P \\cdot A=\\rho g l A_{0}$$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/26_37909_question_1.png'),
(37910,'physics',27,1381,3314,'A thermally insulated vessel contains two liquids at temperatures $T_{1}$ and $T_{2}$ respectively and specific heats $c_{1}$ and $c_{2}$ separated by a non-conducting partition. The partition is removed and the difference between the initial temperature of one of the liquids and the temperature $T$ established in the vessel turns out to be equal to half the difference between the initial temperature of the liquids. Determine the ratio $\\dfrac{m_{1}}{m_{2}}$ (massess of the liquids)','[{"text": "$\\\\dfrac{c_{2}}{c_{1}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{c_{2}+c_{1}}{c_{2}-c_{1}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{c_{2}-c_{1}}{c_{2}+c_{1}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{2 c_{1}-c_{2}}{c_{2}+c_{1}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Given that<br>$T-T_{2}=T_{1}-T=\\dfrac{\\left(T_{1}-T_{2}\\right)}{2}$<br>where given $T$ is temperature of the mixture <br>Apply heat lost $=$ heat gained<br>$\\begin{aligned}& m_{1} c_{1}\\left(T_{1}-T\\right)=m_{2} c_{2}\\left(T-T_{2}\\right) \\\\& \\dfrac{m_{1}}{m_{2}}=\\dfrac{c_{2}\\left(T-T_{2}\\right)}{c_{1}\\left(T_{1}-T\\right)}=\\dfrac{c_{2}}{c_{1}}\\end{aligned}$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37911,'physics',28,1371,3283,'In an adiabatic expansion of an ideal gas the product of pressure and volume','[{"text": "Remains constant", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "At first increases and then decreases", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Decreases", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Increases.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'For an adiabatic process, $P V^{\\gamma}=$ constant<br>${\\rm{ or }}PV = \\frac{{{\\rm{ constant }}}}{{{V^{\\gamma  - 1}}}} \\Rightarrow PV \\propto \\frac{1}{{{V^{\\gamma  - 1}}}}$<br>Since it is an adiabatic expansion, $V$ increases, therefore $PV$ decreases.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37912,'physics',29,1303,3151,'<div class="matching-question"><div class="column-left">Each vibrational mode has one degree of freedom.<br></div><div class="column-right">By law of equipartition of energy, the energy for each degree of freedom in thermal equilibrium is $3 k_{B} T$.</div></div>','[{"text": "Statement A is correct but Statement B is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Statement A is incorrect but Statement B is correct.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both statements are correct.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Statements are incorrect.", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'By law of equipartition of energy, the energy for each degree of freedom in thermal equilibrium is $\\dfrac{1}{2} k_{B} T$. Each vibrational mode gives 2 degrees of freedom i.e., kinetic and potential energy modes, corresponding to the energy. Both statements are wrong. So option (4) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ type-2',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37913,'physics',26,1133,2705,'The $U$-tube shown has a uniform cross-section. A liquid is filled in the two arms upto heights $$h_{1}$$ and $$h_{2}$$ and then the liquid is allowed to move. Neglect viscosity and surface tension. When the levels equalize in the two arms, the liquid will','[{"text": "Be at rest", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Be moving with an acceleration of<br>$$g\\\\left(\\\\dfrac{h_{1}-h_{2}}{h_{1}+h_{2}+h}\\\\right)$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Be moving with a velocity of<br>$$\\\\left(h_{1}-h_{2}\\\\right) \\\\sqrt{\\\\dfrac{g}{2\\\\left(h_{1}+h_{2}+h\\\\right)}}$$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Exert a net force to the right on the tube", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'From the above problem we can write the decrease in gravitational potential energy as<br>$\\Delta U = {\\left( {\\frac{{{h_1} - {h_2}}}{2}} \\right)^2}A\\rho g\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\left( 1 \\right)$<br>The mass of the entire liquid $$=\\left(h_{1}+h_{2}+h\\right) A \\rho$$<br>If this moves with a velocity $$v$$,<br>Kinetic energy $ = \\frac{1}{2}\\left( {{h_1} + {h_2} + h} \\right)A\\rho {v^2}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\left( 2 \\right)$<br>From eq\'s (1) & (2)<br>$$v=\\left(h_{1}-h_{2}\\right) \\sqrt{\\dfrac{g}{2\\left(h_{1}+h_{2}+h\\right)}}$$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/26_37913_question_1.png'),
(37914,'physics',27,1381,3315,'An ice cube of mass $0.1Kg$ at $0^\\circ C$  is placed in an isolated container which is at $227^\\circ C$. The specific heat $c$ of the container varies with temperature $T$ according to the empirical relation $c=A+B T$, where $A = 100\\,cal/Kg - K$ and $B = 2 \\times {10^{ - 2}}cal/Kg - {K^2}$. If the final temperature of the container is $27^\\circ C$, find the mass of the container. (Latent heat of fusion for water $ = 8 \\times {10^4}cal/Kg$ specific heat of water $\\left. { = {{10}^3}cal/Kg - K} \\right)$.','[{"text": "$0.621\\\\,kg$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$0.495\\\\,kg$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$0.336\\\\,kg$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$0.224\\\\,kg$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Heat received by ice is<br>${Q_1} = mL + mc\\Delta T = 10700\\,cal.$<br>Heat lost by the container is<br>${Q_2}{\\text{ }} = \\int_{300}^{500} m (A + BT)dT = m\\left[ {AT + \\frac{{B{T^2}}}{2}} \\right]_{300}^{500} = 21600\\,m$<br>By principle of calorimetry,<br>${Q_1} = {Q_2} \\Rightarrow m = 0.495\\,kg$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37915,'physics',28,1371,3289,'The slopes of isothermal and adiabatic curves are related as','[{"text": "Isothermal curve slope = adiabatic curve slope", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Isothermal curve slope $=\\\\gamma^{x}$ adiabatic curve slope", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Adiabatic curve slope $=\\\\gamma^{x}$ isothermal curve slope", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Adiabatic curve slope $=\\\\dfrac{1}{2} \\\\times$ isothermal curve slope", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'<br>$\\dfrac{\\text { Slope of adiabatic curve }}{\\text { Slope of isothermal curve }}=\\dfrac{(d P / d V)_{\\text {adi }}}{(d P / d V)_{\\text {iso }}}=+\\gamma$<br>So slope to adiabatic curve is $\\gamma\\left(=\\dfrac{C_{P}}{C_{V}}\\right)$ times of isothermal curve',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37916,'physics',29,1302,3150,'<div class="matching-question"><div class="column-left">In a mixture of gases at a fixed temperature, the heavier molecule has the lower average speed.<br></div><div class="column-right">Temperature of a gas is a measure of the average kinetic energy of a molecule.</div></div>','[{"text": "Statement A is correct but Statement B is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Statement A is incorrect but Statement B is correct.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both statements are correct.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Statements are incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'$\\dfrac{1}{2} m v_{\\text {avg }}^{2}=\\sqrt{\\dfrac{8 R T}{\\pi M}} \\Rightarrow v_{\\text {avg }} \\propto \\dfrac{1}{m}$<br>So statement A is correct.$K.{\\rm{ }}{E_{avg{\\rm{ }}}} \\propto T$<br>Statement B is also correct. So option (3) is correct.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ type-2',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37917,'physics',26,1133,2705,'An astronaut is standing at the north pole of a newly discovered planet of radius, $$R$$. He holds a container full of liquid having mass $$m$$ and volume, $V$. At the surface of the liquid pressure is $$P_{0}$$ and at a depth $d$ below the surface, pressure is $P$. From this information determine the mass of the planet is','[{"text": "$$\\\\dfrac{\\\\left(P+P_{0}\\\\right) R^{2} V}{2 G d m}$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$\\\\dfrac{\\\\left(P+P_{0}\\\\right) V R^{2}}{G d m}$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$\\\\dfrac{\\\\left(P-P_{0}\\\\right) R^{2} V}{G d m}$$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$$\\\\dfrac{\\\\left(P-P_{0}\\\\right) R^{2} V}{2 G d m}$$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'$$\\rho g d=\\left(p-p_{0}\\right)$$<br>$$\\begin{aligned}& \\Rightarrow g=\\dfrac{\\left(p-p_{0}\\right)}{d\\left(\\dfrac{m}{V}\\right)} \\\\& \\Rightarrow \\dfrac{G M}{R^{2}}=\\dfrac{\\left(p-p_{0}\\right) V}{d m} \\\\& \\Rightarrow M=\\dfrac{\\left(p-p_{0}\\right) V R^{2}}{G d m}\\end{aligned}$$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37918,'physics',27,1381,3314,'A water cooler of storage capacity 120 liters can cool water at a constant rate of $P$ watts. In a closed circulation system, the water from the cooler is used to cool an external device that generates constantly $3\\,kW$ of heat (thermal load). The temperature of water fed into the device cannot exceed of $30^\\circ C$ and the entire stored 120 liters of water is initially cooled to $10^\\circ C$. The entire system is thermally insulated. The minimum value of $P$ (in watts) for which the device can be operated for 3 hours is (Specific heat of water is $4.2\\,KJ\\,k{g^{ - 1}}\\,{K^{ - 1}}$ and the density of water is $1000\\,kg\\,{m^{ - 3}}$ )','[{"text": "2067", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "1600", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "3933", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "2533", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'In the entire process heater is heating water and cooler is removing heat from water. As $P_{\\text {heater }}>P_{\\text {cooler }}$ the difference will make the water to rise its temperature.<br>$\\left( {{P_{{\\rm{heater }}}} - {P_{{\\rm{cooler }}}}} \\right) \\times t = ms\\Delta T$<br>$ \\Rightarrow \\left( {3 \\times {{10}^3} - P} \\right) \\times 3 \\times 3600 = 120 \\times 4.2 \\times {10^3} \\times 20$<br>$ \\Rightarrow p = 2067\\,W$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37919,'physics',28,1371,3283,'A monoatomic gas is suddenly compressed to (1/81) of its initial volume adiabatically. The ratio of its final pressure to the initial pressure is (given the ratio of the specific heat of the given gas to be $5 / 3$ )','[{"text": "32", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "40", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "243", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "8", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'$P_{1} V_{1}^{\\gamma}=P_{2} V_{2}^{\\gamma}$<br>$\\begin{aligned}& \\Rightarrow \\dfrac{P_{2}}{P_{1}}=\\left(\\dfrac{V_{1}}{V_{2}}\\right)^{\\gamma}=(81)^{\\frac{5}{3}}=\\left(3^{3}\\right)^{\\frac{5}{3}} \\\\& =243\\end{aligned}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37920,'physics',29,1302,3150,'<div class="matching-question"><div class="column-left">For a mixture of non reactive ideal gases, the total pressure gets contribution from each gas in the mixture.<br></div><div class="column-right">In equilibrium, the average translational kinetic energy of the molecules of different gases will be equal.</div></div>','[{"text": "Statement A is correct but Statement B is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Statement A is incorrect but Statement B is correct.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both statements are correct.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Statements are incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Option (3) is correct.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ type-2',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37921,'physics',26,1133,2707,'A rectangular box containing water is accelerated upward at $3\\,m/{s^2}$ on an inclined plane making $$30^{\\circ}$$ to the horizontal. The slope of the free liquid surface is','[{"text": "0.23", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$$1 / \\\\sqrt{3}$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$\\\\sqrt{3}$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "0.32", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'The pseudo force components $$m a \\cos 30^{\\circ}$$ $$\\& m a \\sin 30^{\\circ}$$ are shown for a liquid element on the surface.<br><img src=\'/img/data/physics/26_37921_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' /> <br>The surface of the liquid is always perpendicular to resultant force<br>$$\\tan \\theta=\\dfrac{a_{x}}{a_{y}+g}=\\dfrac{a \\cos 30^{\\circ}}{a \\sin 30^{\\circ}+g}=\\dfrac{\\dfrac{3 \\times \\sqrt{3}}{2}}{\\dfrac{3}{2}+9.8}=0.23$$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/26_37921_question_1.png'),
(37922,'physics',27,1381,3314,'Four spheres $A,\\,B,\\,C$ and $D$ have their radii in arithmetic progression and the specific heat capacities of their substances are geometric progression. If the ratios of heat capacities of $D$ and $B$ to that of $C$ and $A$ are as $8: 27$ the ratio of masses of $B$ and $A$ is (Assume same density for all spheres)','[{"text": "$4: 1$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1: 8$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1: 2$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$8: 1$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Let the radii of the spheres be $\\mathrm{R}$, $R+a, R+2 a$ and $R+3 a$ where $a$ is a constant and the specific heat capacities be $s, s r$, $s r^{2}$ and $s r^{3}$ where $r$ is another constant.<br>Given that $\\left(\\dfrac{\\text { heat capacity of } D}{\\text { heat capacity of } B}\\right):\\left(\\dfrac{\\text { heat capacity of } C}{\\text { heat capacity of } A}\\right)=8: 27$Heat capacity $=$ mass $\\times$ specific heat<br>$\\begin{aligned}& {\\left[\\dfrac{(R+3 a)^{3} s r^{3}}{(R+a)^{3} s r}\\right]:\\left[\\dfrac{(R+2 a)^{3} s r^{2}}{R^{3} s}\\right]=8: 27 } \\\\\\Rightarrow \\quad & R=a \\\\& \\dfrac{m_{2}}{m_{1}}=\\dfrac{(4 / 3) \\pi(R+R)^{3} \\rho}{(4 / 3) \\pi(R)^{3} \\rho}=\\dfrac{8}{1}\\end{aligned}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL);
INSERT INTO `qb_questions` (`id`, `subject_id`, `chapter_id`, `topic_id`, `subtopic_id`, `question_html`, `options`, `correct_index`, `explanation`, `explanation_image_url`, `difficulty`, `qtype`, `year`, `tag`, `created_at`, `question_image_url`) VALUES
(37923,'physics',28,1371,3283,'During an adiabatic process, the pressure of a gas is found to be proportional to the cube of its temperature. The ratio of $\\dfrac{C_{P}}{C_{V}}$ for the gas is','[{"text": "$\\\\dfrac{4}{3}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{5}{3}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{3}{2}$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Given adiabatic process $P \\propto T^{3}$.<br>Ideal gas equation  $ \\Rightarrow T = \\frac{{PV}}{{nR}}$<br>$P \\propto {T^3}$<br>$P \\propto {(PV)^3} \\Rightarrow {P^2}\\,{V^3} = P{V^{\\frac{3}{2}}} = $ constant<br>Comparing with $P{V^\\gamma } = $ constant<br>Hence, $\\gamma=\\dfrac{3}{2}$.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37924,'physics',29,1303,3151,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Equal masses of helium and oxygen gases are given equal quantities of heat. There will be a greater rise in the temperature of helium compared to that of oxygen.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> The molecular weight of oxygen is more than the molecular weight of helium.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Helium is a monatomic gas, while oxygen is diatomic. Therefore, the heat given to helium will be totally used up in increasing the translational kinetic energy of its molecules; whereas the heat given to oxygen will be used up in increasing the translational kinetic energy of the molecule and also in increasing the kinetic energy of rotation and vibration. Hence temperature rise of helium is more. Option (2) is correct.',NULL,'Easy','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37925,'physics',26,1133,2707,'The acceleration $$a$$ of the vertical $$U$$-tube is','[{"text": "$$g$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$g / 2$$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$2\\\\,g$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "zero", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'$$\\tan \\theta=\\dfrac{3 b-b}{4 b}=\\dfrac{a}{g} \\Rightarrow a=\\dfrac{g}{2}$$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/26_37925_question_1.png'),
(37926,'physics',27,1381,3315,'A thermally insulated vessel contains some water at $0^\\circ C$. The vessel is connected to a vacuum pump to pump out water vapour. This results in some water getting forzen. It is given latent heat of vaporisation of water at $0^\\circ C = 21 \\times {10^5}\\,J/kg$ and latent heat of freezing of water $ = 3.36 \\times {10^5}\\,J/kg$. The maximum percentage amount of water vapour that will be solidified in this manner will be','[{"text": "$24.36 \\\\%$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$33.6 \\\\%$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$21 \\\\%$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$86.2 \\\\%$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'As the system is thermally isolated, the heat required for vaporisation is extracted from water getting frozen. Let $x$ be the maximum fraction of water that gets soldified. The $(1-x)$ is the dfraction that gets vaporised.<br>$\\begin{aligned}& \\therefore x \\times 3.36 \\times 10^{5}=(1-x) \\times 21 \\times 10^{5} \\\\& \\Rightarrow x=0.862 .\\end{aligned}$<br>So option (4) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37927,'physics',28,1371,3283,'During an adiabatic process, the cube of the pressure is found to be inversely proportional to the fourth power of the volume. Then the ratio of specific heats is','[{"text": "1", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "1.33", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "1.67", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "1.4", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'<br>$P V^{\\gamma}=$ constant for an adiabatic process<br>Given that<br>${P^3} \\times {V^4} = $ constant<br>$\\mathrm{PV}^{4 / 3}=$ constant.<br>$ \\Rightarrow \\quad g = \\frac{4}{3} = 1.33$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2007,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37928,'physics',29,1300,3145,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Air pressure in a car tyre increases during driving.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Absolute zero temperature is not zero energy temperature.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'When a person is driving a car then the temperature of air inside the tyre is increases because of motion. From the Gay Lussac\'s law,  $P \\propto T$<br>Hence, when temperature increase the pressure also increases. Only the energy of translatory motion of molecules is represented by temperature. Other forms of energy such as intermolecular potential energy, etc. are not represented by temperature. Hence at absolute zero, the translatory motion of molecules ceases but other forms of molecular energy do not become zero. Therfore absolute zero temperature is not the temperature of zeroenergy. At absolute zero molecular motion ceases. Option (2) is correct.',NULL,'Easy','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37929,'physics',26,1133,2707,'A sealed tank containing a liquid of density $$\\rho$$ moves with a horizontal acceleration $$a$$, as shown in the figure. The difference in pressure between the points $A$ and $B$ is','[{"text": "$$h \\\\rho g$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$l \\\\rho a$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$h \\\\rho g-l \\\\rho a$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$h \\\\rho g+l \\\\rho a$$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Consider an element of the liquid of width $$d x$$ and area of cross-section $$\\alpha$$, at a distance $$x$$ from the front of the tank.<br><img src=\'/img/data/physics/26_37929_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' /> <br>Mass of the element $$=d m=(\\alpha d x) \\rho$$.<br>Net force to the right on the element<br>$$=(p+d p) \\alpha-p \\alpha=\\alpha d p$$<br>From Newton\'s $$2^{\\text {nd }}$$ law<br>$$\\therefore \\alpha d p=(\\rho \\alpha d x) a$$<br>$$d p=\\rho a d x$$<br>$$\\int_{A}^{C} d p=\\int_{A}^{C} \\rho a d x \\text { or } p_{C}-p_{A}=\\rho a l$$<br>(Refer to the figure in the question to identify $A$, $B,C)$<br>$$\\begin{aligned}& \\text { Also } p_{B}-p_{C}=\\rho g h \\\\& \\begin{aligned}p_{B}-\\left(p_{A}+\\rho a l\\right)=\\rho g h \\\\p_{B}-p_{A}=h \\rho g+l \\rho a\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}$$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/26_37929_question_1.png'),
(37930,'physics',27,1381,3315,'A metal piece weighing $15\\,g$ is heated to $100^\\circ C$ and then immersed in a mixture of ice and water. At the thermal equilibrium the volume of the mixture is found to be reduced by $0.15\\,c{m^3}$ with the temperature of mixture remaining constant. Find the specific heat of the metal. Given specific gravity of ice $=0.92$, specific gravity of water at $0^\\circ C = 1.0$, latent heat of fusion of ice $ = 80\\,cal - {g^{ - 1}}.$','[{"text": "$0.242\\\\,cal/gm^\\\\circ C$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$0.092\\\\,cal/gm^\\\\circ C$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$0.124\\\\,cal/gm^\\\\circ C$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$0.162\\\\,cal/gm^\\\\circ C$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'The mixture has both ice and water. When the metal piece is placed some part of ice melts. Let $m$ is the mass of ice that melts. The change in the volume of ice (or the mixture) is<br>$\\frac{m}{{{\\rho _{{\\rm{ice }}}}}} - \\frac{m}{{{\\rho _{{{\\rm{H}}_2}{\\rm{O}}}}}} = 0.15 \\Rightarrow m = \\frac{{0.15 \\times 0.92}}{{0.08}}$<br>$\\therefore {H_{{\\rm{lost }}}} = {H_{{\\rm{gained }}}} \\Rightarrow 15 \\times s \\times 100 = \\frac{{0.15 \\times 0.92}}{{0.08}} \\times 80$<br>$S = 0.092\\,cal/gm\\,^\\circ C.$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37931,'physics',28,1371,3283,'A mass of diatomic gas $(\\gamma=1.4)$ at a pressure of $2\\,atm$ is compressed adiabatically so that its temperature rise from $27^\\circ C$ to $927^\\circ C$. The pressure of the gas in final state is','[{"text": "$28\\\\,atm$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$68.7\\\\,atm$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$256\\\\,atm$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$8\\\\,atm$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'${T_1} = 273 + 27 = 300\\,K$<br>${T_2} = 273 + 927 = 1200\\,K$<br>Gas equation for adiabatic process<br>$\\begin{aligned}& P V^{\\gamma}=\\text { constant } \\\\& p\\left(\\dfrac{T}{p}\\right)^{\\gamma}=\\text { constant } \\quad(\\because P V=R T) \\\\& \\dfrac{p_{2}}{p_{1}}=\\left(\\dfrac{T_{2}}{T_{1}}\\right)^{\\frac{\\gamma}{\\gamma-1}} \\text { or } p_{2}=p_{1}\\left(\\dfrac{T_{2}}{T_{1}}\\right)^{\\frac{\\gamma}{\\gamma-1}}\\end{aligned}$<br>${p_2} = 2{\\left( {\\frac{{1200}}{{300}}} \\right)^{\\frac{{1.4}}{{1.4 - 1}}}}$<br>${p_2} = 256\\,atm.$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37932,'physics',29,1301,3148,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> When speed of sound in a gas is $c$, then $c_{\\text {rms }}=\\sqrt{\\dfrac{3}{\\gamma}} \\times c$<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> $c=\\sqrt{\\dfrac{\\gamma p}{\\rho}}$','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'We know, <br>$c_{\\mathrm{rms}}=\\sqrt{\\dfrac{3 p}{\\rho}} \\text { and } c=\\sqrt{\\dfrac{\\gamma p}{\\rho}} \\Rightarrow \\dfrac{c_{\\mathrm{rms}}}{c}=\\sqrt{\\dfrac{3}{\\gamma}}$',NULL,'Easy','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37933,'physics',26,1133,2710,'A wooden rod of uniform cross-section and of length $120\\,cm$ is hinged at the bottom of the tank which is filled with water to a height of 40 $cm$. In the equilibrium position, the rod makes an angle of $$60^{\\circ}$$ with the vertical. The centre of buoyancy is located on the rod at a distance (from the hinge) of :','[{"text": "$40\\\\,cm$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$20\\\\,cm$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$75\\\\,cm$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$60\\\\,cm$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'OP is the portion of the rod immersed in water. <br> <img src=\'/img/data/physics/26_37933_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' /> <br> $\\cos 60^\\circ  = \\frac{{40}}{{OP}}$ <br> $ \\Rightarrow OP = \\frac{{40}}{{\\cos 60^\\circ }}\\,cm = \\frac{{40}}{{1/2}}\\,cm = 80\\,cm$ <br> The centre of buoyancy is at the centre of the immersed part of the rod. So, the required distance is $40\\,cm$.',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37934,'physics',27,1381,3314,'The specific heat capacity of a metal at low temperature $\\left( T \\right)$ is given as<br>$C_{p}\\left(k H K^{-1} k^{-1}\\right)=32\\left(\\dfrac{T}{400}\\right)^{3}$<br>A 100 gram vessel of this metal is to be cooled from $20^\\circ K\\,{\\rm{to}}\\,4^\\circ K$ by a special refrigerator operating at room temperature $\\left( {27^\\circ C} \\right)$. The amount of work required to cool the vessel is','[{"text": "Greater than $0.148\\\\,kJ$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Between $0.148\\\\,kJ$ and $0.028\\\\,kJ$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Less than $0.028\\\\,kJ$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Equal to $0.002\\\\,kJ$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Required work = Energy released<br>Here, $Q = \\int m c\\,dT$<br>$=\\int_{20}^{4} 0.1 \\times 32 \\times\\left(\\dfrac{T^{3}}{400^{3}}\\right) d T \\approx 0.002 k J$<br>Therefore, required work $ = 0.002\\,kJ$.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37936,'physics',29,1301,3149,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> The root mean square and most probable speeds of the molecules in a gas are the same.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> The maxwell distribution for the speed of molecules in a gas is symmetrical.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but reason is correct.", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'$v_{\\mathrm{rms}}>v_{\\mathrm{mp}}$',NULL,'Easy','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37937,'physics',26,1133,2710,'In the arrangement as shown, $$m_{B}=3 m$$, density of liquid is $$\\rho$$ and density of block $B$ is $$2 \\rho$$. The system is released from rest so that block $$B$$ moves up when it is inside the liquid and moves down when it is outside of liquid with the same magnitude of acceleration. Find the mass of block $A$.','[{"text": "$$\\\\dfrac{7}{4} m$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$2 m$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$\\\\dfrac{9}{2} m$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$\\\\dfrac{9}{4} m$$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Acceleration when block $$B$$ is in the liquid<br>$$\\begin{aligned}& a_{1}=\\dfrac{m_{A} g-\\left(m_{B} g-\\text { upthrust }\\right)}{\\left(m_{A}+3 m\\right)} \\\\& =\\dfrac{m_{A} g-\\left(3 m g-\\dfrac{3 m}{2 \\rho} \\rho g\\right)}{\\left(m_{A}+3 m\\right)}(\\uparrow)\\end{aligned}$$<br>Accelaration when block $B$ is outside of the liquid<br>$$a_{2}=\\dfrac{-m_{A} g+(3 m g)}{\\left(m_{A}+3 m\\right)}(\\downarrow)$$<br>Given $$a_{1}=a_{2}$$, we get<br>$$\\begin{aligned}& m_{A} g-\\dfrac{3}{2} m g=3 m g-m_{A} g \\\\& \\Rightarrow 2 m_{A} g=\\dfrac{9}{2} m g \\Rightarrow m_{A}=\\left(\\dfrac{9}{4}\\right) m\\end{aligned}$$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/26_37937_question_1.png'),
(37940,'physics',29,1303,3151,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> The molecule of a monoatomic gas has three degrees of freedom at room temperature.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> The molecule of a diatomic gas has five degree of freedom.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'A monoatomic gas molecule (like $\\mathrm{He}$ ) consists of single atom. It has translational motion in any direction in space. Thus, it has 3 translational degrees of freedom. The molecules of a diatomic gas (like $\\left.\\mathrm{O}_{2}, \\mathrm{CO}_{2}, \\mathrm{H}_{2}\\right)$ cannot only translate but also rorate about any one of the three co-ordinate axes. Hence, it can have two rotational degrees of freedom. Thus, a diatomic molecule has 5 degrees of freedom: 3 translational and 2 rotational.',NULL,'Easy','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37941,'physics',26,1133,2710,'A uniform cylinder of length $$L$$ and mass $$M$$ having cross-sectional area $A$ is suspended, with its length vertical, from a fixed point by a massless spring such that it is half submerged in a liquid of density $$\\sigma$$ at equilibrium position. The extension $$x_{0}$$ of the spring when it is in equilibrium is:','[{"text": "$$\\\\dfrac{M g}{k}$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$\\\\dfrac{M g}{k}\\\\left(1-\\\\dfrac{L A \\\\sigma}{M}\\\\right)$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$\\\\dfrac{M g}{k}\\\\left(1-\\\\dfrac{L A \\\\sigma}{2 M}\\\\right)$$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$$\\\\dfrac{M g}{k}\\\\left(1+\\\\dfrac{L A \\\\sigma}{M}\\\\right)$$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'From figure, $$k x_{0}+F_{B}=M g$$<br><img src=\'/img/data/physics/26_37941_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' /> <br>$$k x_{0}+\\sigma \\dfrac{L}{2} A g=M g$$<br>$$\\begin{aligned}& \\Rightarrow k x_{0}=M g-\\sigma \\dfrac{L}{2} A g \\\\& \\Rightarrow x_{0}=\\dfrac{M g-\\dfrac{\\sigma L A g}{2}}{k}=\\dfrac{M g}{k}\\left(1-\\dfrac{L A \\sigma}{2 M}\\right)\\end{aligned}$$<br>Hence, extension of the spring when it is in equilibrium is, $$x_{0}=\\dfrac{M g}{k}\\left(1-\\dfrac{L A \\sigma}{2 M}\\right)$$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37944,'physics',29,1301,3148,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> The rms velocity of gas molecules is doubled, when temperature of gas becomes four times.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> $c \\propto \\sqrt{T}$.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'The rms velocity of gas molecules is given by<br>$v_{\\text {rms }}=c=\\sqrt{\\dfrac{3 k T}{m}}$<br>Hence, it is clear that when temperature becomes four times rms velocity will be two times.',NULL,'Easy','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37945,'physics',26,1135,2714,'A piston of a syringe pushes a liquid with a speed of $1\\,cm/\\sec $. The radii of the syringe tube and the needle are $R = 1\\,cm$ and $r = 0.5\\,mm$ respectively. The velocity of the liquid coming out of the needle is','[{"text": "$20\\\\,cm/\\\\sec $", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$10\\\\,cm/\\\\sec $", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2\\\\,cm/\\\\sec $", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$400\\\\,cm/\\\\sec $", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'By equation of continuity<br>$Av = a{v^\\prime }$<br>$ \\Rightarrow {v^\\prime } = \\left( {\\frac{A}{a}} \\right)v = \\left( {\\frac{{\\pi {R^2}}}{{\\pi {r^2}}}} \\right) \\times v$<br>$ \\Rightarrow {v^\\prime } = 400\\,cm/\\sec $',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/26_37945_question_1.png'),
(37948,'physics',29,1303,3152,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> A gas has a unique value of specific heat.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> $C_{P} / C_{V}=\\gamma$','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but reason is correct.", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Specific heat of a gas depends on the type of $C_{P} / C_{V}=\\gamma$. So option (4) is correct.',NULL,'Easy','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37949,'physics',26,1136,2715,'A tap water pipe is bent as shown in the diagram. Sections 1 and 2 of the pipe are horizontal but section 2 is placed higher at a height of $H$. The diameter of the pipe of section 1 is 1.5 times larger than the diameter of section 2. Indicate which of the following is the most complete answer for relationship between the hydrostatic pressure measured by the gauges in sections 1 and 2 .','[{"text": "$$P_{1}>P_{2}$$ because water flows slower in section 1 , and section 1 is lower than section 2.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$$P_{1} < P_{2}$$ because water flows lower in section 1.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$P_{1}>P_{2}$$ because section 1 is lower than section 2.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$P_{1}>P_{2}$$ because water flows slower in section 1.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'By applying Bernoulli\'s equation and equation of continuity at 1 and 2<br>$$\\begin{aligned}& P_{1}+\\dfrac{1}{2} \\rho v_{1}^{2}=P_{2}+\\dfrac{1}{2} \\rho v_{2}^{2}+\\rho g H \\\\& v_{1} A_{1}=v_{2} A_{2}\\end{aligned}$$<br>Given $$A_{1}>A_{2} \\Rightarrow v_{2}>v_{1}$$<br>$$\\Rightarrow P_{1}>P_{2}$$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/26_37949_question_1.png'),
(37952,'physics',29,1303,3152,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Specific heat of gas at constant pressure $\\left(C_{P}\\right)$ is greater than its specific heat at constant volume $\\left(C_{V}\\right)$.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> At constant pressure, some heat is spent in expansion of the gas.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'$C_{V}$ is used in increasing the internal energy of the gas while $C_{P}$ is used in two ways (i) to change the internal energy and (ii) to do expansion of gas. Hence $C_{P}>C_{V}$.<br>So option (1) is correct.',NULL,'Easy','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37953,'physics',26,1136,2719,'Two streams of water flow through the $U$-shaped tubes shown. The tube on the left has cross-sectional area $A$, and the speed of the water going through it is $$v$$; the tube on the right has cross-sectional area $$\\dfrac{A}{2}$$. If the net force on the tube assembly is zero, what must be the speed $$v^{\\prime}$$ of the water going through the tube on the right? Neglect gravity, and assume that the speed of the water in each tube is the same upon entry and exit.','[{"text": "$$\\\\sqrt{2} v$$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$$v$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$1 / 2 v$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$2 v$$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'The thrust force exerted by a liquid of density $$\\rho$$ flowing through a cross section of area $$\\mathrm{A}$$ is $$\\rho A v^{2}$$ ( $$v$$ - velocity of the liquid).<br>As the momentum of the liquid gets reversed the net force on the left tube is $$F_{1}=2 \\rho A v^{2}$$<br>Force on the right tube is $$F_{2}=2 \\rho\\left(\\dfrac{A}{2}\\right) v^{\\prime 2}$$<br>$$\\Rightarrow F_{1}=F_{2} \\Rightarrow v^{\\prime}=\\sqrt{2} v$$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/26_37953_question_1.png'),
(37954,'physics',27,1381,3314,'A mass of $50\\,g$ of water in a closed vessel, with surroundings at a constant temperature takes 2 minutes to cool from $30^\\circ C$ to $25^\\circ C$. A mass of $100\\,g$ of another liquid in an identical vessel with identical surroundings takes the same time to cool from $30^\\circ C$ to $25^\\circ C$. The specific heat of the liquid is: (The water equivalent of the vessel is $30\\,g$.)','[{"text": "$2.0\\\\,kcal/kg$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$7\\\\,kcal/kg$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$3\\\\,kcal/g$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$0.5\\\\,kcal/kg$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'As the surrounding is identical, vessel is identical time taken to cool both water and liquid (from $30^\\circ C$ to $25^\\circ C$ ) is same 2 minutes, therefore<br>$\\begin{gathered}\\left(\\dfrac{d Q}{d t}\\right)_{\\text {water }}=\\left(\\dfrac{d Q}{d t}\\right)_{\\text {liquid }} \\\\\\dfrac{\\left(m_{w} c_{w}+W\\right) \\Delta T}{t}=\\dfrac{\\left(m_{l} c_{l}+W\\right) \\Delta T}{t}\\end{gathered}$<br>$(W = $ water equivalent of the vessel)<br>or, $m_{w} c_{w}=m_{l} c_{l}$<br>$\\therefore$ Specific heat of liquid, $c_{l}=\\dfrac{m_{W} c_{W}}{m_{l}}$<br>$ = \\frac{{50 \\times 1}}{{100}} = 0.5\\,k\\,cal/kg$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',2013,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37964,'physics',29,1301,3148,'The molecules of a given mass of a gas have r.m.s., velocity of $200\\,\\,m{s^{ - 1}}$ at $27^\\circ C$ and $1.0 \\times {10^5}N{m^{ - 2}}$ pressure. When the temperature and pressure of the gas are respectively, $27^\\circ C$ and $0.05 \\times {10^5}N{m^{ - 2}},$ the r.m.s., velocity of its molecules in $m{s^{ - 1}}$  is.','[{"text": "$100 \\\\sqrt{2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{400}{\\\\sqrt{3}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{100 \\\\sqrt{2}}{3}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{100}{3}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'$v\\,\\,\\alpha \\,\\sqrt T  \\Rightarrow \\frac{v}{{200}} = \\sqrt {\\frac{{400}}{{300}}}  \\Rightarrow v = \\frac{{200 \\times 2}}{{\\sqrt 3 }} m/s$<br>$ \\Rightarrow v = \\frac{{400}}{{\\sqrt 3 }} \\,m/s$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2016,'NEET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37956,'physics',29,1305,3155,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Mean free path of a gas molecule varies inversely as density of the gas.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Mean free path varies inversely as temperature of the gas.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'The mean free path of a gas molecule is the average distance between two successive collisions. It is represented by $\\lambda$.<br>$\\lambda=\\dfrac{k_{B} T}{\\sqrt{2} \\pi \\sigma^{2} \\rho}$<br>Here, $\\sigma=$ diameter of molecule $k_{B}=$ Boltzmann\'s constant<br>Hence, mean free path varies inversely as density of the gas. The mean free path varies directly as the temperature and inversely as the pressure of the gas. So option (3) is correct.',NULL,'Easy','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37957,'physics',26,1136,2719,'An engine pumps water continuously through a hose. Water leaves the hose with a velocity $$v$$ and $$m$$ is the mass per unit length of the water jet. What is the rate at which kinetic energy is imparted to water','[{"text": "$$m v^{3}$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$\\\\dfrac{1}{2} m v^{3}$$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$$\\\\dfrac{1}{2} m v^{2}$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$\\\\dfrac{1}{2} m^{2} v^{2}$$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'<br>$$\\begin{aligned}& \\dfrac{d k}{d t}=\\dfrac{d}{d t}\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2} M v^{2}\\right)=\\dfrac{v^{2}}{2} \\cdot \\dfrac{d M}{d t}=\\dfrac{v^{2}}{2}\\left(\\dfrac{d M}{d l} \\times \\dfrac{d l}{d t}\\right) \\\\& \\Rightarrow \\dfrac{d k}{d t}=\\dfrac{1}{2} m v^{2} \\times \\dfrac{d l}{d t}=\\dfrac{1}{2} m v^{3} \\text { (Given that } \\dfrac{d M}{d t}=m \\text { ) }\\end{aligned}$$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37959,'physics',27,1381,3315,'$500\\,g$ of water and $100\\,g$ of ice at $0^\\circ C$ are in a calorimeter whose water equivalent $40\\,g.10\\,g$ of steam at $100^\\circ C$ is added to it. then water in the calorimeter is: $($ Latent heat of ice $ = 80\\,cal/g$, Latent heat of steam $ = 540\\,cal/g$ )','[{"text": "$580\\\\,g$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$590\\\\,g$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$600\\\\,g$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$610\\\\,g$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'The heat released by steam when it converts from $100^\\circ C$ to $0^\\circ C$.<br>$\\Delta Q=10 \\times 540+10 \\times 1 \\times 100$<br>The mass of the ice that melts is<br>$\\Delta Q=6400=m 80 \\Rightarrow m=80 g$<br>Water in calorimeter $ = 500 + 80 + 10 = 590\\,g$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',2013,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37960,'physics',29,1303,3153,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Heat capacity of a solid per mole is $3 R$.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> In solids the average K.E of an atom is $3 k_{B} T$.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'In a cubic lattice the atoms can oscillate in three dimensions. So option (1) is correct.',NULL,'Easy','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37961,'physics',26,1136,2720,'In a siphon arrangement the water level in the supply reservoir is above the free end of $$a$$ pipe as shown in the figure. If the diameter of the pipe is \' $$d$$ \' (constant) what is the rate of the discharge of water.','[{"text": "$$\\\\pi d^{2} \\\\sqrt{g h}$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$\\\\pi d^{2} \\\\sqrt{8 g h}$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$\\\\pi d^{2} \\\\sqrt{\\\\dfrac{g h}{8}}$$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$$\\\\pi d^{2} \\\\sqrt{2 g h}$$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Point (1) is on the free surface of water in the supply reservoir. and point (2) is just inside the pipe. Applying Bernoulli\'s equation between (1) and (2),(Taking the reference line at (2))<br>$$\\begin{aligned}p_{1}+\\dfrac{1}{2} \\rho\\left(0^{2}\\right)+\\rho g h & =p_{2}+\\dfrac{1}{2} \\rho V_{2}^{2}+\\rho g(0) \\\\p_{1} & =p_{2}=p_{o} \\\\V_{2} & =\\sqrt{2 g h}\\end{aligned}$$<br>The volume rate of flow through the pipe is $$Q=A V_{2}$$<br>$$A=\\dfrac{\\pi d^{2}}{4} \\quad V_{2}=\\sqrt{2 g h}$$<br>$$Q=\\pi d^{2} \\sqrt{\\dfrac{g h}{8}}$$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/26_37961_question_1.png'),
(37963,'physics',27,1381,3315,'A kettle with $2\\,L$ water at $27^\\circ C$ is heated by operating coil heater of power $1\\,kW$. The heat is lost to the atmosphere at constant rate $160\\,J/$, when it is open. In how much time will water be heated to $77^\\circ C(sp$. heat of water $ = 4.2\\,kJ/kg$ ) with lid open?','[{"text": "$14 \\\\mathrm{~min}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$7 \\\\mathrm{~min}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$8\\\\min 20\\\\,s$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$6\\\\min 2\\\\,s$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Energy given by heater must be equal to the sum of energy gained by water and energy lost from the lid.<br>$Pt = ms\\Delta T + {\\rm{ energy}}\\,{\\rm{lost }}$<br>$1000t = 2 \\times \\left( {4.2 \\times {{10}^3}} \\right) \\times 50 + 160t$<br>$840t = 8.4 \\times {10^3} \\times 50$<br>$ \\Rightarrow t = 500\\,s = 8\\min 20\\,s.$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',2014,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38257,'physics',29,1301,3148,'A mixture of two gases is contained in a vessel. The gas-I is monoatomic and gas-II is diatomic. The ratio of their molecular weights is $1: 4$. The ratio of R.M.S. speeds of molecules of two gases will be','[{"text": "2", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "4", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "8", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "16", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'$V \\propto \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{M}} \\Rightarrow \\dfrac{V_{1}}{V_{2}}=\\dfrac{1}{2} \\Rightarrow V_{2}=2 V_{1}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37965,'physics',26,1136,2720,'A siphon has a uniform circular base of diameter $8/\\sqrt \\pi  cm$ with its crest $A,1.8\\,m$ above the water level vessel B is of large cross section $\\left( {g = 10\\,m/{s^2}} \\right)$ and atmospheric pressure ${P_0} = \\left( {{{10}^5}\\,N/{m^2}} \\right)$. Predict the wrong option.','[{"text": "Velocity of flow through pipe is $6\\\\sqrt 2 \\\\,m/s$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Discharge rate of flow through pipe is $96\\\\sqrt 2  \\\\times {10^{ - 4}}\\\\,{m^3}/s$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Velocity of flow through pipe is $6\\\\,m/s$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Pressure at $A$ is $0.46 \\\\times {10^5}\\\\,N/{m^2}$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'By applying Bernoulli\'s equation at $B$ and $C$ we get (reference line is taken at $C$ )<br>${p_o} + \\frac{1}{2}\\rho \\left( {{0^2}} \\right) + \\rho g(3.6)$<br>$ = {p_o} + \\frac{1}{2}\\rho {V^2} + \\rho g(0)$<br>$V = 6\\sqrt 2 \\,m/s$<br>By applying Bernoulli\'s equation at $B$ and $A$ we get<br>${p_o} + \\frac{1}{2}\\rho \\left( {{0^2}} \\right) + \\rho g(3.6)$<br>$ = {p_A} + \\frac{1}{2}\\rho \\left( {{V^2}} \\right) + \\rho g(3.6 + 1.8)$<br>${p_A} = 4.6 \\times {10^4}\\,N/{m^2}$<br>Discharge rate $ = AV = \\pi {\\left( {\\frac{d}{2}} \\right)^2} \\times V$<br>$ = 96\\sqrt 2  \\times {10^{ - 4}}\\,{m^3}/s$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/26_37965_question_1.png'),
(37967,'physics',27,1381,3315,'An experiment takes 10 minutes to raise the temperature of water in a container from $0^\\circ C$ to $100^\\circ C$ and another 55 minutes to convert it totally into steam by a heater supplying heat at a uniform rate. Neglecting the specific heat of the container and taking specific heat of water to be $1\\,cal\\,g - \\,^\\circ C$, the heat of vapourisation according to this experiment will come out to be','[{"text": "$560\\\\,cal/g$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$550\\\\,cal/g$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$540\\\\,cal/g$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$530\\\\,cal/g$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'As $P t=m c \\Delta T$<br>$P \\times 10 \\times 60 = mc \\times 1 \\times 100\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\left( 1 \\right)$<br>and $P \\times 55 \\times 60 = mL\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\left( 2 \\right)$<br>Dividing equation (1) by (2) we get<br>$\\frac{{10}}{{55}} = \\frac{{100}}{L}$<br>$\\therefore L = 550\\,cal./g.$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',2015,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37968,'physics',29,1301,3148,'At what temperature will the rms speed of oxygen molecules become just sufficient for escaping from the Earth\'s atmosphere? (Given : Mass of oxygen molecule $(m) = 2.76 \\times {10^{ - 26}} kg$, Boltzmann\'s constant ${k_B} = 1.38 \\times {10^{ - 23}}J{K^{ - 1}})$','[{"text": "$5.016 \\\\times 10^{4} K$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1.254 \\\\times 10^{4} K$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$83.6 \\\\times 10^{3} K$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$2.508 \\\\times 10^{4} K$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'${v_{rms}} = {v_{escape{\\rm{ }}}}$<br>$\\sqrt{\\dfrac{3 K T}{m}}=11.2 \\mathrm{~km} / \\mathrm{s}$<br>$\\dfrac{3 \\times 1.38 \\times 10^{-23} \\times T}{2.76 \\times 10^{-26}}=\\left(11.2 \\times 10^{3}\\right)^{2} ;$<br>$T = \\frac{{2.76 \\times 125.44 \\times {{10}^6}}}{{3 \\times 1.38 \\times {{10}^3}}} = 83.6 \\times {10^3} K$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2018,'NEET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37969,'physics',26,1136,2720,'In the figure shown find the height $h$ at which the bubbles of water will form at point 2 .<br>$$\\left(g=10 m / s^{2}\\right)$$','[{"text": "$1\\\\,m$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$3\\\\,m$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$6\\\\,m$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$4\\\\,m$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'The velocity of the liquid that comes out at point (1) is ${V_1} = \\sqrt {2g(6m)}  = 10.95\\,m/s$.<br>By applying Bernoulli\'s equation at (1) & (2)<br>$$P_{2}+\\dfrac{1}{2} \\rho V_{2}^{2}+\\rho g(h+6 m)=P_{1}+\\dfrac{1}{2} \\rho V_{1}^{2}+\\rho g(0)$$<br>We know that $$V_{1}=V_{2}$$<br>${P_1} = {P_o} = {10^5}\\,N/{m^2}$<br>When water bubbles form at (2)<br>${P_2} \\approx 0$<br>$\\rho g(h + 6m) = {10^5}\\quad \\left( {g = 10\\,m/{s^2}} \\right)$<br>$(h + 6m) = 10mh = 4m$<br>i.e., when $$h=4 m$$ the liquid flow will become discontinuous.',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/26_37969_question_1.png'),
(37971,'physics',27,1381,3314,'In an experiment a sphere of aluminium of mass $0.20\\,kg$ is heated upto $150^\\circ C$. Immediately, it is put into water of volume $150\\,cc$ at $25^\\circ C$ kept in a calorimeter of water equivalent to $0.025\\,kg$. Final temperature of the system is $40^\\circ C$. The specific heat of aluminium is:','[{"text": "$378\\\\,J/kg - \\\\,^\\\\circ C$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$315\\\\,J/kg - \\\\,^\\\\circ C$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$476\\\\,J/kg - \\\\,^\\\\circ C$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$499\\\\,J/kg - \\\\,^\\\\circ C$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'According to principle of calorimetry,<br>${Q_{{\\rm{given }}}} = {Q_{{\\rm{used }}}}$<br>$0.2 \\times s \\times (150 - 40)$<br>$ = 0.15 \\times 1(40 - 25) + 0.025(40 - 25)$<br>$s = \\frac{{0.175(15)}}{{0.2 \\times 110}} = 0.1193\\frac{{cal}}{{g\\,^\\circ C}} = 499.46\\frac{J}{{kg\\,^\\circ C}}$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',2017,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37972,'physics',29,1302,3150,'Increase in temperature of a gas filled in a container would lead to :','[{"text": "Increase in its mass", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Increase in its kinetic energy", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Decrease in its pressure", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Decrease in intermolecular distance", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Increase in temperature would lead to the increase in kinetic energy of an ideal gas<br>$U=\\dfrac{F}{2} n R T$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2019,'NEET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37975,'physics',27,1381,3314,'A $100\\,g$ of iron nail is hit by a $1.5\\,kg$ hammer striking at a velocity of $60\\,m{s^{ - 1}}$. What will be the rise in the temperature of the nail if one fourth of energy of the hammer goes into heating the nail? (Given specific heat of iron $ = 420\\,J/(kg\\,^\\circ C)$)','[{"text": "$675^\\\\circ C$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1600^\\\\circ C$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$160.7^\\\\circ C$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$6.75^\\\\circ C$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'<br> $\\dfrac{1}{4}($ kinetic energy of hammer $)=(m s \\Delta T)_{\\text {iron }}$<br>$ \\Rightarrow \\frac{1}{2} \\times 1.5 \\times {60^2} \\times \\frac{1}{4} = 0.1 \\times 420 \\times \\Delta T$<br>$ \\Rightarrow \\Delta T = 160.7^\\circ C$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',2022,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37976,'physics',29,1302,3150,'The average thermal energy for a mono-atomic gas is : ( $K_{B}$ is Boltzmann constant and $T$ absolute temperature)','[{"text": "$\\\\dfrac{3}{2} k_{B} T$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{5}{2} k_{B} T$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{7}{2} k_{B} T$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{1}{2} k_{B} T$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Average thermal energy $=\\dfrac{3}{2} k_{B} T$<br>where 3 is translational degree of freedom. For monoatomic gas total degree of freedom $f = 3$  (translational degree of freedom)',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2020,'NEET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37979,'physics',27,1382,3319,'The container $A$ is constantly maintained at$100^\\circ C$ and container $B$ contains ice at $0^\\circ C$. Different rods are used to connect them. For a rod made of copper, it takes 30 minutes for the ice to melt and for a rod of steel of same crosssection taken in different experiment it takes 60 minutes for ice to melt. When these rods are simultaneously connected in parallel, the ice melts in:','[{"text": "20 minutes", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "45 minutes", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "15 minutes", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "90 minutes", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'$Q = $ it where $i=$ heat flow rate<br>$=\\dfrac{\\Delta T}{R}=\\dfrac{100}{R}$<br>$Q=\\left(\\dfrac{100}{R_{1}}\\right)(30) \\Rightarrow R_{1}=\\left(\\dfrac{100}{Q}\\right) \\times 30$<br>Also, $Q=\\left(\\dfrac{100}{R_{2}}\\right) \\times 30 \\Rightarrow R_{2}=\\left(\\dfrac{100}{Q}\\right) \\times 60$<br>When the rods are connected in parallel<br>Now, $Q=\\left(\\dfrac{1}{R_{1}}+\\dfrac{1}{R_{2}}\\right) 100 t$<br>$\\begin{aligned}& \\dfrac{1}{R_{1}}+\\dfrac{1}{R_{2}}=\\dfrac{Q}{3000}+\\dfrac{Q}{6000}=\\dfrac{Q}{2000} \\\\& Q=\\dfrac{Q \\times 100 t}{2000} \\Rightarrow t=20 \\mathrm{~min}\\end{aligned}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37980,'physics',29,1305,3155,'The mean free path for a gas, with molecular diameter $d$ and number density $n$ can be expressed as:','[{"text": "$\\\\dfrac{1}{\\\\sqrt{2} n \\\\pi d^{2}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{1}{\\\\sqrt{2} n^{2} \\\\pi d^{2}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{1}{\\\\sqrt{2} n^{2} \\\\pi^{2} d^{2}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{1}{\\\\sqrt{2} n \\\\pi d}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Mean free path for a gas sample<br>$\\lambda_{m}=\\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{2} \\pi d^{2} n}$<br>where $d$ is diameter of a gas molecule and $n$ is molecular density',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2020,'NEET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37983,'physics',27,1382,3317,'The rate of heat flow through a cross section of the rod shown in the figure is, $\\left(T_{2}>T_{1}\\right.$ and thermal conductivity of the material of the rod is $K$.)','[{"text": "$\\\\dfrac{K \\\\pi\\\\left(r_{2}-r_{1}\\\\right)^{2}\\\\left(T_{2}-T_{1}\\\\right)}{L}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{K \\\\pi\\\\left(r_{2}-r_{1}\\\\right)^{2}\\\\left(T_{2}-T_{1}\\\\right)}{2 L}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{K \\\\pi\\\\left(r_{2}-r_{1}\\\\right)^{2}\\\\left(T_{2}-T_{1}\\\\right)}{4 L}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{K \\\\pi r_{1} r_{2}\\\\left(T_{2}-T_{1}\\\\right)}{L}$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Consider a circular disc of radius r present at a distance $x$ from the front end of the cylinder as shown in the figure<br><img src=\'/img/data/physics/27_37983_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' /> <br>The rate of heat flow across the disc is<br>$\\begin{aligned}& i=\\dfrac{K \\pi r^{2} d T}{d x} \\\\& \\int \\dfrac{d x}{r^{2}}=\\dfrac{K \\pi}{i} \\int d T\\end{aligned}$<br>From the figure<br>$\\tan \\theta=\\dfrac{r-r_{1}}{x}=\\dfrac{r_{2}-r}{L}$<br>$\\begin{aligned}& \\Rightarrow \\quad r-r_{1}=x \\tan \\theta \\\\& d r=d x \\tan \\theta \\\\& \\dfrac{1}{\\tan \\theta} \\int_{r_{1}}^{r_{2}} \\dfrac{d r}{r^{2}}=\\dfrac{K \\pi}{i} \\int_{1_{1}}^{r_{2}} d T \\\\& \\dfrac{1}{\\tan \\theta}\\left[\\dfrac{1}{r_{1}}-\\dfrac{1}{r_{2}}\\right]=\\dfrac{K \\pi}{i}\\left[T_{2}-T_{1}\\right] \\\\& \\dfrac{L}{r_{2}-r_{1}}\\left[\\dfrac{r_{2}-r_{1}}{r_{1} r_{2}}\\right]=\\dfrac{K \\pi}{i}\\left[T_{2}-T_{1}\\right] \\\\& i=\\dfrac{K \\pi r_{1} r_{2}\\left[T_{2}-T_{1}\\right]}{L}\\end{aligned}$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/27_37983_question_1.png'),
(37984,'physics',29,1300,3145,'A cylinder contains hydrogen gas at pressure of $249\\,\\,kpa$ and temperature $27^\\circ C$. Its density is: $\\left( {R = 8.3 \\,J mo{l^{ - 1}} {K^{ - 1}}} \\right)$','[{"text": "$0.2\\\\,\\\\,kg\\\\,\\\\,/\\\\,\\\\,{m^3}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$0.1\\\\,\\\\,kg\\\\,\\\\,/\\\\,\\\\,{m^3}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$0.02\\\\,\\\\,kg\\\\,\\\\,/\\\\,\\\\,{m^3}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$0.5\\\\,\\\\,kg\\\\,\\\\,/\\\\,\\\\,{m^3}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'For an ideal gas sample<br>$\\dfrac{P}{\\rho}=\\dfrac{R T}{M}(M-$ Molecular Weight $)$<br>$\\rho=\\dfrac{P M}{R T}=\\dfrac{249 \\times 10^{3} \\times 2 \\times 10^{-3}}{8.314 \\times 300}=0.199$<br>$\\rho  = 0.2\\,\\,kg/{m^3}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2020,'NEET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37985,'physics',28,1371,3283,'One mole of an ideal gas at an initial temperature of $TK$ does $6R$ joules of work adiabatically. If the ratio of specific heat of this gas at constant pressure and at constant volume is $5 / 3$, the final temperature of gas will be','[{"text": "$(T+2.4) K$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$(T-2.4) K$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$(T+4) K$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$(T-4) K$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'In adiabatic process $\\Delta Q=0$<br>$\\begin{aligned}& \\Delta Q=\\Delta U+W \\\\& W=-\\Delta U=-n C_{V}\\left(T_{2}-T_{1}\\right) \\\\& W=n C_{V}\\left(T_{1}-T_{2}\\right) \\\\& 6 R=\\dfrac{3}{2} R\\left(T-T_{2}\\right) \\\\& T_{2}=(T-4) K\\end{aligned}$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37987,'physics',27,1382,3317,'A rod of length $l$ and cross-sectional area $A$ has a variable conductivity given by $K=\\alpha T$ where $\\alpha$ is a positive constant and $T$ is temperature in kelvin. Two ends of the rod are maintained at temperatures $T_{1}$ and $T_{2}\\left(T_{1}>T_{2}\\right)$. Heat current flowing through the rod will be','[{"text": "$\\\\dfrac{A \\\\alpha\\\\left(T_{1}^{2}+T_{2}^{2}\\\\right)}{3 l}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{A \\\\alpha\\\\left(T_{1}^{2}-T_{2}^{2}\\\\right)}{2 l}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{A \\\\alpha\\\\left(T_{1}^{2}-T_{2}^{2}\\\\right)}{6 l}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{A \\\\alpha\\\\left(T_{1}^{2}+T_{2}^{2}\\\\right)}{l}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Heat current:<br>$\\begin{aligned}& i=-k A \\dfrac{d T}{d x} \\\\& i d x=-k A d T\\end{aligned}$<br>Given that<br>$\\begin{aligned}& K=\\alpha T \\\\& i \\int_{0}^{l} d x=-A \\alpha \\int_{T_{1}}^{T_{2}} T d T \\\\& i l=-A \\alpha \\dfrac{\\left(T_{2}^{2}-T_{1}^{2}\\right)}{2} \\Rightarrow i=\\dfrac{A \\alpha\\left(T_{1}^{2}-T_{2}^{2}\\right)}{2 l}\\end{aligned}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37988,'physics',29,1302,3150,'Match Column - I and Column - II and choose the correct match from the given choices.<div class="matching-question"><div class="column-left">A Root mean square speed of gas molecules<br>B Pressure exerted by ideal gas<br>C Average kinetic energy of a molecule<br>D Total energy of 1 mole of a diatomic gas</div><div class="column-right">P  $\\dfrac{1}{3} n m v^{2}$<br>Q  $\\sqrt {\\frac{{3RT}}{M}} $<br>R  $\\frac{5}{2}RT$<br>S  $\\frac{3}{2}{k_B}T$</div></div>','[{"text": "${\\\\rm{A - Q,}}\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\rm{B - R,}}\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\rm{C - S,}}\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\rm{D - P}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{A - Q,}}\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\rm{B - P,}}\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\rm{C - S,}}\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\rm{D - R}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{A - R,}}\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\rm{B - Q,}}\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\rm{C - P,}}\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\rm{D - S}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{A - R,}}\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\rm{B - P,}}\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\rm{C - S,}}\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\rm{D - Q}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Root mean square speed  ${v_{rms}} = \\sqrt {\\frac{{3RT}}{M}} $<br>Pressure exerted by gas $P=\\dfrac{1}{3}(m n) v_{r m s}^{2}$<br>Average kinetic energy of molecule $=\\dfrac{3}{2} K_{B} T$<br>Total internal energy of 1 mole of diatomic gas $=\\dfrac{5}{2} R T$',NULL,'Medium','Match the following',2021,'NEET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37989,'physics',28,1371,3283,'Consider a spherical shell of radius $R$ at temperature $T$. The black body radiation inside it can be considered as an ideal gas of photons with internal energy per unit volume $u=\\dfrac{U}{V} \\propto T^{4}$ and pressure $p=\\dfrac{1}{3}\\left(\\dfrac{U}{V}\\right)$. If the shell now undergoes an adiabatic expansion the relation between $T$ and $R$ is:','[{"text": "$T \\\\propto \\\\dfrac{1}{R}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$T \\\\propto \\\\dfrac{1}{R^{3}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$T \\\\propto e^{-R}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$T \\\\propto e^{-3 R}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'As, $P=\\dfrac{1}{3}\\left(\\dfrac{U}{V}\\right)$<br>But $\\dfrac{U}{V}=K T^{4} \\quad(K$ is a constant $)$<br>So, $\\quad P=\\dfrac{1}{3} K T^{4}$<br>$\\Rightarrow \\dfrac{n R T}{V}=\\dfrac{1}{3} K T^{4} \\quad[\\text { As } P V=n R T]$<br><br>$\\dfrac{4}{3} \\pi R^{3} T^{3}=\\text { constant } \\quad\\left(V=\\dfrac{4}{3} \\pi R^{3}\\right)$<br>Therefore, $T \\propto \\dfrac{1}{R}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37991,'physics',27,1382,3318,'Three rods of identical area of cross-section and made from the same metal form the sides of an isosceles triangle $ABC$ right angle at $B$. The points $A$ and $B$ are maintained at temperatures $T$ and $\\sqrt{2} T$ respectively. In the steady state the temperature of the point $C$ is $T_{C}$. Assuming that only heat conduction takes place, $\\dfrac{T_{C}}{T}$ is equal to $\\left(T_{c}>T\\right)$','[{"text": "$\\\\dfrac{3}{(\\\\sqrt{2}+1)}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{1}{\\\\sqrt{3}(2-1)}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{1}{(\\\\sqrt{2}+1)}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{1}{2(\\\\sqrt{2}-1)}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'$T_{B}>T_{A} \\Rightarrow$ Heat will flow from $B$ to $A$ via two paths (i) $B$ to $A$ (ii) and along $BCA$ as shown.<br><img src=\'/img/data/physics/27_37991_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' /> <br>Rate of flow of heat in path $BCA$ will be same <br>$\\begin{aligned}\\text { i.e., } & \\left(\\dfrac{Q}{t}\\right)_{B C}=\\left(\\dfrac{Q}{t}\\right)_{C A} \\\\\\Rightarrow & \\dfrac{K\\left(\\sqrt{2} T-T_{C}\\right) A}{a}=\\dfrac{K\\left(T_{C}-T\\right) A}{\\sqrt{2} a} \\\\\\Rightarrow & \\dfrac{T_{C}}{T}=\\dfrac{3}{1+\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37992,'physics',29,1305,3155,'The volume occupied by the molecules contained in $4.5{\\text{ }}kg$ of water at STP is (intermolecular forces vanish)','[{"text": "$5.6 \\\\times {10^3}\\\\,{m^3}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$5.6 \\\\times {10^{ - 3}} \\\\,{m^3}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$5.6\\\\,\\\\,{m^3}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$5.6 \\\\times {10^6}\\\\,{m^3}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'$\\mathrm{V}=($ no. of moles) $(22.4$ litre $)$<br>$ = \\frac{{{\\rm{ }}mass{\\rm{ }}}}{{{\\rm{ }}molar{\\rm{ }}mass{\\rm{ }}}}\\left( {22.4 \\times {{10}^{ - 3}}\\,{m^3}} \\right)$<br>$ = \\frac{{4.5 \\times {{10}^3}}}{{18}} \\times 22.4 \\times {10^{ - 3}} {m^3} = 5.6 \\,{m^3}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2022,'NEET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37994,'physics',28,1371,3286,'Find the pressure at which temperature attains its maximum value if the relation between pressure and volume for an ideal is $P=P_{0}+(1-\\alpha) V^{2} ; \\alpha>1$','[{"text": "$\\\\dfrac{P_{0}}{3}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{2 P_{0}}{3}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{4 P_{0}}{3}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$P_{0}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'$P V=n R T$<br>$\\begin{gathered}\\Rightarrow \\quad P_{0}+\\left(1-\\alpha V^{2}\\right) V=n R T \\\\\\Rightarrow \\quad T=\\dfrac{\\left[P_{0} V+(1-\\alpha) V^{3}\\right]}{n R} \\\\\\dfrac{d T}{d V}=\\dfrac{P_{0}}{n R}+\\dfrac{3 V^{2}(1-\\alpha)}{n R}=0 \\\\\\Rightarrow \\quad P_{0}=3(\\alpha-1) V^{2} \\\\\\Rightarrow \\quad V^{2}=\\dfrac{P_{3}}{3(\\alpha-1)} \\\\\\Rightarrow \\quad P=P_{0}+(1-\\alpha) \\dfrac{P_{0}}{3(\\alpha-1)}=P_{0}-\\dfrac{P_{0}}{3}=\\dfrac{2 P_{0}}{3}\\end{gathered}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38007,'physics',27,1382,3323,'Consider two identical homogenous spheres, $A$ and $B$, with the same initial temperatures. One of them is at rest on a horizontal plane, while the second one hangs on a thread. The same quantity of heat has been supplied to both balls. Which of the following statement is true about the temperatures of $A$and $B$ ?','[{"text": "$T_{A} < T_{B}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$T_{A}>T_{B}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$T_{A}=T_{B}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Can\'t be predicted", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'As heat is given both expands by the same values. $C.M$ of Sphere $B$ raises up and $C.M$ of sphere $A$ goes down slightly. The raise in $P.E$ of $B$ should come from the expense of heat supplied. Whereas decrease in $P.E$ of $A$ will be converted into heat.<br>$\\Rightarrow T_{A}>T_{B}$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/27_38007_question_1.png'),
(38351,'physics',26,1138,2731,'Two capillary tubes $A$ and $B$ of diameter $1\\,mm$ and $2\\,mm$ respectively are dipped vertically in a liquid. If the capillary rise in $A$ is $6\\,mm$, then the capillary rise in $B$ is','[{"text": "$2\\\\,cm$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$3\\\\,cm$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$4\\\\,cm$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$6\\\\,cm$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'For capillary rise, according, to Zurin\'s law $h_{1} r_{1}=h_{2} r_{2}$<br>$6 \\times 1 = {h_2} \\times 2 \\Rightarrow {h_2} = 3\\,cm$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2021,'MHTCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37995,'physics',27,1382,3318,'The figure shows the cross-section of the outer wall of a house built in a hill-resort to keep the house insulated from the freezing temperature of outside. The wall consists of teak wood of thickness $L_{1}$ and brick of thickness $\\left(L_{2}=5 L_{1}\\right)$, sandwiching two layers of an unknown material with identical thermal conductivities and thickness. The thermal conductivity of teak wood is $K_{1}$ and that of brick is $\\left(K_{2}=5 K\\right)$. Heat conduction through the wall has reached a steady state with the temperature of three surface being known. $\\left(T_{1}=25 C, T_{2}=20 C\\right.$ and $\\left.T_{5}=-20 C\\right)$. Find the interface temperatures $T_{3}$ and $T_{4}$.','[{"text": "$2.5^\\\\circ C, -  = 15^\\\\circ C$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$2.5^\\\\circ C,10^\\\\circ C$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$5^\\\\circ C, - 10^\\\\circ C$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2.5^\\\\circ C, - 5^\\\\circ C$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Let interface area be $A$, then the thermal resistance of wood, $R_{1}=\\dfrac{L_{1}}{K_{1} A}$ and that of brick wall<br>$R_{2}=\\dfrac{L_{2}}{K_{2} A}=\\dfrac{5 L_{1}}{5 K_{1} A}=R_{1}$<br>Let thermal resistance of the each sandwitch layer<br>$=R$. Then the above wall can be visualised as a circuit<br><img src=\'/img/data/physics/27_37995_explanation_1.jpg\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' /> <br>Thermal current through each wall is same.<br>Hence $\\dfrac{25-20}{R_{1}}=\\dfrac{20-T_{3}}{R}=\\dfrac{T_{3}-T_{4}}{R}=\\dfrac{T_{4}+20}{R_{1}}$<br>$ \\Rightarrow 25 - 20 = {T_4} + 20 \\Rightarrow {T_4} =  - 15^\\circ C$<br>also, $20-T_{3}=T_{3}-T_{4} \\Rightarrow T_{3}=\\dfrac{20+T_{4}}{2}=2.5^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/27_37995_question_1.png'),
(37996,'physics',29,1301,3148,'The curve between absolute temperature and \\(v_{r m s}^{2}\\) is<div class="matching-question"></div>','[{"text": "", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'$v_{r m s}=\\sqrt{\\dfrac{3 R T}{M}} \\Rightarrow v_{r m s}^{2} \\propto T$',NULL,'Medium','Graph/Figure',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37997,'physics',28,1371,3290,'Two cylinders fitted with pistons and placed as shown, connected with string through a small tube of negligible volume, are filled with a gas at pressure $p_{o}$ and temperature $T_{o}$. The radius of smaller cylinder is half of the other. If the temperature is increased to $2 T_{o}$, find the pressure, if the piston of bigger cylinder moves towards left by 1 metre?','[{"text": "$\\\\dfrac{4}{5} P_{o}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{3}{5} P$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{2}{5} P_{o}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{5}{4} P_{o}$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'When the bigger piston displaces towards left by $1\\,m$ the smaller piston also displace by $1\\,m$ towards left.<br>The length of the left side cylinder becomes $2\\,m$ and right side cylinder becomes $0\\,m$.<br>The initial volume ${V_o} = \\pi {R^2}(1m) + \\frac{{\\pi {R^2}}}{4}(1\\,m)$<br>The final volume $V=\\pi R^{2}(2 m)$<br>As the number of moles are equal initially and finally.<br>$\\begin{aligned}& n_{i}=n_{f} \\\\& \\qquad \\dfrac{P_{o} V_{o}}{T_{o}}=\\dfrac{P V}{2 T_{o}} \\Rightarrow P=\\dfrac{5 P_{o}}{4}\\end{aligned}$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/28_37997_question_1.png'),
(37998,'physics',26,1135,2714,'A cylindrical tank of height \\[1\\;m\\] and crosssectional area \\[A = 4000\\;c{m^2}\\] is initially empty. It is kept under a tap of cross-sectional area \\[1\\;c{m^2}\\]. Water starts flowing from the tap at \\[t = 0\\], with a speed \\[ = 2\\;m/s\\]. There is a small hole in the base of the tank of cross-sectional area \\[0.5\\;c{m^2}\\]. The variation of height of water in tank (in meters) with time $$t$$ is best depicted by:<div class="matching-question"></div>','[{"text": "", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'<br>$$\\begin{array}{r}4000 \\times 10^{-4} \\cdot \\dfrac{d h}{d t}=-0.5 \\times 10^{-4} \\sqrt{2 \\times 10 \\times h}+10^{-4} \\times 2 \\\\\\Rightarrow \\dfrac{d h}{d t}=5 \\times 10^{-4}-\\left(\\dfrac{5 \\times \\sqrt{20} \\times 10^{-4}}{4}\\right) \\times \\sqrt{h}\\end{array}$$<br>when $$h=$$ costant, <br>$$\\dfrac{d h}{d t}=0, \\quad h=\\left(\\dfrac{5 \\times 4}{5 \\times \\sqrt{20}}\\right)^{2}=0.8 m$$',NULL,'Medium','Graph/Figure',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(37999,'physics',27,1382,3323,'Parallel rays of light of intensity $I = 912W{m^{ - 2}}$ are incident on a spherical black body kept in surroundings of temperature $300\\,K$. Take StefanBoltzmann constant $\\sigma  = 5.7 \\times {10^{ - 8}}{W^{ - 2}}M\\,{K^4}$ and assume that the energy exchange with the surroundings is only through radiation. The final steady state temperature of the black body is close to','[{"text": "$1550\\\\,K$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$330\\\\,K$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$660\\\\,K$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$990\\\\,K$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'In steady state the radiation absorbed by the sphere is equal to radiation looses by it.<br>As the parallel beam falls on the sphere the radiation that falls is $I\\left(\\pi R^{2}\\right)$<br>$I\\pi {R^2} = \\sigma \\left( {{T^4} - T_0^4} \\right)4\\pi {R^2}$<br>$ \\Rightarrow I = \\sigma \\left( {{T^4} - T_0^4} \\right)4$<br>$ \\Rightarrow {T^4} - T_0^4 = 40 \\times {10^8}$<br>$ \\Rightarrow {T^4} - 81 \\times {10^8} = 40 \\times {10^8}$<br>$ \\Rightarrow {T^4} = 121 \\times {10^8}$<br>$ \\Rightarrow T \\approx 330\\,K.$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38000,'physics',29,1300,3145,'An ideal gas of molar mass $M$ is located in the uniform gravitational field. Find the gas pressure as a function of height $h, p=p_{0}$ at $h=0$ and the temperature varies with height as $T=T_{0}(1-a h)$, where $a+v e$ constant.','[{"text": "$P_{0}(1-a h)$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$P_{0}(1-a h)^{M g / a R T_{0}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{P_{0}(1-a h) a R T_{0}}{M g}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{P_{0}}{(1-a h)}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'We know that the variation of pressure with height of a fluid is given by<br>From gas law $dp = \\, - \\rho gdh\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,(1)$<br>$pV = \\frac{m}{M}RT{\\rm{ }}or{\\rm{ }}p = \\frac{\\rho }{M}RT$<br>$\\rho {\\rm{ }} = \\frac{{pM}}{{RT}}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,(2)$<br>Putting Eq. (2) in Eq. (1)<br>$\\begin{gathered}d p=\\dfrac{-p M g}{R T} d h \\\\\\dfrac{d p}{p}=\\dfrac{-M g d h}{R T_{0}(1-a h)} \\\\\\Rightarrow \\int_{p_{0}}^{p} \\dfrac{d p}{p}=\\dfrac{-M g}{R T_{0}} \\int_{0}^{h} \\dfrac{d h}{(1-a h)} \\\\\\Rightarrow \\ln \\dfrac{p}{p_{0}}=\\dfrac{M g}{a R T_{0}} \\ln (1-a h)=\\ln (1-a h)^{M g / a R T_{0}}\\end{gathered}$<br>Hence, $p=p_{0}(1-a h)^{M g / a R T_{0}}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38001,'physics',26,1139,2734,'The diagram shows a cup of tea seen from above. The tea has been stirred and is now rotating without turbulence. A graph showing the speed $$v$$ with which the liquid is crossing points at a distance \\[X\\] from \\[O\\] along a radius \\[OX\\] would look like<div class="matching-question"></div>','[{"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'When we stir the tea the cup is at rest and the particles of the tea which are in contact with the walls of the cup also be at rest. When we move from center to circumference, the velocity of liquid goes on decreasing and finally becomes zero.',NULL,'Hard','Graph/Figure',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/26_38001_question_1.jpg'),
(38002,'physics',28,1371,3290,'In the given figure, an adiabatic cylindrical tube of $2 V_{o}$ is divided in two equal parts by a frictionless adiabatic separator. An ideal gas in left side of a tube having pressure $P_{1}$ and temperature $T_{1}$ where as in the right side having pressure $P_{2}$ and temperature $T_{2} . C_{p} / C_{v}=\\gamma$ is the same for both the gases. The separator is slide slowly and is released at a position where it can stay in equilibrium. find the final volume of left part.','[{"text": "$\\\\dfrac{V_{o} P_{1}}{P_{2}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{V_{o} P_{1}}{P_{1}+P_{2}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{2 V_{o} P_{1}^{1 / \\\\gamma}}{P_{1}^{1 / \\\\gamma}+P_{2}^{1 / \\\\gamma}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{2 V_{o} P_{1}^{1 / \\\\gamma}}{P_{2}^{1 / \\\\gamma}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'An external agent displaces the system to the equilibrium position & we can\'t use conservation of energy for the system.<br>As the changes take place under adiabatic conditions.<br>${P_1}V_0^\\gamma  = PV_1^\\gamma \\quad \\& \\,\\,\\,{P_2}V_0^\\gamma  = PV_2^\\gamma $<br>$\\frac{{{P_1}}}{{V_1^\\gamma }} = \\frac{{{P_2}}}{{V_2^\\gamma }} \\Rightarrow {P_1}V_1^\\gamma  = {P_2}V_2^\\gamma $<br>$P_1^{1/\\gamma }{V_1} = P_2^{1/\\gamma }{V_2}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\left( 1 \\right)$<br>We know that ${V_1} + {V_2} = 2{V_0}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\left( 2 \\right)$<br>$V_{1}=\\dfrac{2 V_{0} P_{1}^{1 / \\gamma}}{P_{1}^{1 / \\gamma}+P_{2}^{1 / \\gamma}}$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/28_38002_question_1.png'),
(38003,'physics',27,1382,3323,'Three very large plates of same area are kept parallel and close to each other. They are considered as ideal black surfaces and have very high thermal conductivity. The first and third plates are maintained at temperature $3\\,T$ and $2\\,T$ respectively. The temperature of the middle (i.e. second) plate under steady state condition is','[{"text": "$\\\\left(\\\\dfrac{97}{4}\\\\right)^{\\\\frac{1}{4}} T$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\left(\\\\dfrac{65}{2}\\\\right)^{\\\\frac{1}{4}} T$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\left(\\\\dfrac{97}{2}\\\\right)^{\\\\frac{1}{4}} T$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$(97)^{\\\\frac{1}{4}} T$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'The three plates are shown in the figure<br><img src=\'/img/data/physics/27_38003_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' /> <br>Let $T^{\\prime}$ is the temperature of the middle plate.<br>Here $T^{\\prime}$ should by lower than $3\\,T$ and higher than $2\\,T$<br>In steady state energy absorbed by middle plate is equal to energy released by middle plate.<br>$\\begin{gathered}\\sigma A(3 T)^{4}-\\sigma A\\left(T^{\\prime}\\right)^{4}=\\sigma A\\left(T^{\\prime}\\right)^{4}-\\sigma A(2 T)^{4} \\$3 T)^{4}-\\left(T^{\\prime}\\right)^{4}=\\left(T^{\\prime}\\right)^{4}-(2 T)^{4} \\\\2\\left(T^{\\prime}\\right)^{4}=(16+81) T^{4} \\\\T^{\\prime}=\\left(\\dfrac{97}{2}\\right)^{1 / 4} T .\\end{gathered}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38004,'physics',29,1300,3145,'A vessel of volume $V$ is evacuated by means of a piston air pump. One piston stroke captures the volume $\\Delta V$. How many strokes are needed to reduce the pressure in the vessel $\\eta$ times the initial value? Assume that the temperature is constant and the gas is ideal.','[{"text": "$\\\\dfrac{\\\\log _{e} \\\\eta}{\\\\log _{e}\\\\left[\\\\dfrac{V+\\\\Delta V}{V}\\\\right]}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\log _{e}\\\\left[\\\\dfrac{V+\\\\Delta V}{V}\\\\right]$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\log \\\\eta\\\\left[\\\\dfrac{V+\\\\Delta V}{V}\\\\right]$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "None of the above options", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Let $\\rho_{1}$ be the density after first stroke.<br>As the mass remains constant<br>$V \\rho=(V+\\Delta V) \\rho_{1} \\text { or } \\quad \\rho_{1}=V \\rho /(V+\\Delta V)$<br>where $\\rho$ is the initial density of the gas.<br>Similarly, if $\\rho_{2}$ be the density after second stroke, then<br>$V \\rho_{1}=(V+\\Delta V) \\rho_{2}$<br>or $\\quad \\rho_{2}=\\left(\\dfrac{V}{V+\\Delta V}\\right) \\rho_{1}=\\left(\\dfrac{V}{V+\\Delta V}\\right)^{2} \\rho$<br>So, after $n^{\\text {th }}$ stroke,<br>$\\rho_{n}=\\left(\\dfrac{V}{V+\\Delta V}\\right)^{n} \\rho \\text { or } \\rho_{n}=\\left(\\dfrac{V}{V+\\Delta V}\\right)^{n} \\rho$<br>As temperature is constant in this process<br>$p V=\\dfrac{m}{N} R T=\\dfrac{\\rho V}{M} R T .$<br>$p = \\frac{\\rho }{M}RT \\Rightarrow p\\,\\,\\alpha \\,\\,\\rho $<br>$ \\Rightarrow \\frac{{{p_n}}}{p} = {\\left( {\\frac{V}{{V + \\Delta V}}} \\right)^n}{\\rm{ }}or{\\rm{ }}\\frac{1}{\\eta } = {\\left( {\\frac{V}{{V + \\Delta V}}} \\right)^n}$<br>By taking log on both sides<br>$n=\\dfrac{\\log _{e} \\eta}{\\log _{e}\\left[\\dfrac{(V+\\Delta V)}{V}\\right]}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38005,'physics',28,1371,3290,'A piston can freely move inside a horizontal cylinder closed from both ends. Initially, the piston separates the inside space of the cylinder into two equal parts each of volume $V_{0}$, in which an ideal gas is contained under the same pressure $P_{0}$ and at the same temperature. What work has to be performed in order to increase isothermally the volume of one part of gas $\\eta$ times compared to that of the other by slowly moving the piston?','[{"text": "$P_{0} V_{0}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$-P_{0} V_{0}\\\\left[\\\\ln \\\\left(V_{0}^{2}-\\\\left(\\\\dfrac{\\\\eta-1}{\\\\eta+1}\\\\right) V_{0}^{2}\\\\right)-\\\\ln V_{0}^{2}\\\\right]$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{P_{0} V_{0}}{\\\\eta}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$-P_{0} V_{0}\\\\left[\\\\ln \\\\left(\\\\dfrac{V_{0}^{2}}{\\\\eta}\\\\right)-\\\\ln \\\\left(V_{0}^{2}\\\\right)\\\\right]$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'In equilibrium position, $P_{1} A+F_{\\text {agent }}=P_{2} A$ $F_{\\text {agent }}=\\left(P_{2}-P_{1}\\right) A$ <br><img src=\'/img/data/physics/28_38005_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />  <br>Elementary workdone by the agent<br>${F_{agent{\\rm{ }}}}dx = \\left( {{P_2} - {P_1}} \\right)A \\times dx = \\left( {{P_2} - {P_1}} \\right)dV\\,\\,\\,\\,\\left( 1 \\right)$<br>Applying $\\mathrm{PV}=$ constant for two parts, we have $P_{1}\\left(V_{0}+A x\\right)=P_{0} V_{0}$ and $P_{2}\\left(V_{0}-A x\\right)=P_{0} V_{0}$<br>$P_{1}=\\dfrac{P_{0} V_{0}}{\\left(V_{0}+A x\\right)}$ and $P_{2}=\\dfrac{P_{0} V_{0}}{\\left(V_{0}-A x\\right)}$<br>$\\therefore P_{2}-P_{1}=\\dfrac{P_{0} V_{0}(2 A x)}{V_{0}^{2}-A^{2} x^{2}}=\\dfrac{2 P_{0} V_{0} V}{V_{0}^{2}-V^{2}} \\quad(V=A x)$<br>When the volume of the left end is $\\eta$ times the volume of right end, we have<br>$\\left( {{V_0} + V} \\right) = \\eta \\left( {{V_0} - V} \\right)$<br>$V = \\left( {\\frac{{\\eta  - 1}}{{\\eta  + 1}}} \\right){V_0}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\left( 2 \\right)$<br>The workdone by the agent is given by<br>$\\begin{aligned}& W=\\int_{0}^{V}\\left(P_{2}-P_{1}\\right) d V=\\int_{0}^{V} \\dfrac{2 P_{0} V_{0} V}{V_{0}^{2}-V^{2}} d V \\\\& =-P_{0} V_{0}\\left[\\ln \\left(V_{0}^{2}-V^{2}\\right)\\right]_{0}^{V} \\\\& =-P_{0} V_{0}\\left[\\ln \\left(V_{0}^{2}-V^{2}\\right)-\\ln V_{0}^{2}\\right] \\\\& =-P_{0} V_{0}\\left[\\ln \\left\\{V_{0}^{2}-\\left(\\dfrac{\\eta-1}{\\eta+1}\\right) V_{0}^{2}\\right\\}-\\ln V_{0}^{2}\\right]\\end{aligned}$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/28_38005_question_1.png'),
(38006,'physics',26,1133,2710,'A cubical wooden block of side $$L$$ and relative density 0.5 is floating on the surface of water as shown in the figure. A string is attached by pulley to it as shown in figure. The graph of tension \\[T\\] in the string vs distance of the bottom of the block from the free surface of the water when the end \\[A\\] of the rope is slowly being pulled up will be (block is moving very slowly):<div class="matching-question"></div>','[{"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Initially the cube is floating so buoyancy force is equal to weight and tension in the string is zero. When the block is pulled inside the water the buoyancy force increases linearly and hence tension also increases. When the block completely submerges in water the tension in the string remain constant.',NULL,'Medium','Graph/Figure',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/26_38006_question_1.png'),
(38008,'physics',29,1301,3147,'Consider an ideal gas confined in an isolated closed chamber. As the gas undergoes an adiabatic expansion the average time of collision between molecules increases as $V^{q}$, where $V$ is the volume of the gas. The value of $q$ is : $\\left( {\\gamma  = \\frac{{{C_p}}}{{{C_v}}}} \\right)$','[{"text": "$\\\\dfrac{3 \\\\gamma-5}{6}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{\\\\gamma+1}{2}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{\\\\gamma-1}{2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{3 \\\\gamma+5}{6}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Average time between collisions<br>$\\begin{aligned}& =\\dfrac{\\text { Mean free path }}{v_{\\text {rms }}} \\\\& t=\\dfrac{1}{\\dfrac{\\pi d^{2} N / V}{\\sqrt{\\dfrac{3 R T}{M}}}} ; t=\\dfrac{C V}{\\sqrt{T}} \\quad\\left(\\text { where } C=\\dfrac{\\sqrt{M}}{\\pi d^{2} N \\sqrt{3 R}}\\right) \\\\& \\Rightarrow T \\propto \\dfrac{V^{2}}{t^{2}}\\end{aligned}$<br>Given that $T V^{\\gamma-1}=k$<br>$\\begin{aligned}& \\dfrac{V^{2}}{t^{2}} V^{\\gamma-1}=k \\Rightarrow \\dfrac{V^{\\gamma+1}}{t^{2}}=k \\\\& t \\propto V^{\\frac{\\gamma+1}{2}} \\Rightarrow q=\\dfrac{\\gamma+1}{2}\\end{aligned}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38009,'physics',28,1371,3291,'A resistance coil connected to an external battery is placed inside an adiabatic cylinder fitted with a frictionless piston of mass $m$ and containing an ideal gas. A current $i$ flows through the coil which has a resistance $R$. At what speed must the piston move upward in order that the temperature of the gas remains unchanged? Neglect atmospheric pressure.','[{"text": "$\\\\dfrac{R m g}{i^{2}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{i^{2} m}{R g}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{i^{2} R}{m g}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{m g}{i^{2} R}$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'The input electrical power and output mechanical powers must be same $m g \\times v=i^{2} R$.',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/28_38009_question_1.png'),
(38010,'physics',26,1136,2717,'A small hole is made at the bottom of a symmetrical jar as shown in figure. A liquid is filled into the jar upto a certain height. The rate of descension of liquid is independent of the level of liquid in the jar. The surface of jar is a surface of revolution of the curve:<div class="matching-question"></div>','[{"text": "$$y=k x^{4}$$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$$y=k x^{2}$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$y=k x^{3}$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$y=k x^{5}$$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Let $A$ is the cross sectional area of the hole. The area of the surface of water is $$\\pi x^{2}$$.<br>From the equation of continuity<br>$$\\pi x^{2} \\dfrac{d y}{d t}=A \\sqrt{2 g y}$$<br>Given that $$\\dfrac{d y}{d t}=$$ constant $$\\Rightarrow x^{2}=\\dfrac{A \\sqrt{2 g}}{\\pi} \\sqrt{y}$$<br>$$K x^{4}=y \\quad$$ where $$\\quad K=\\dfrac{\\pi^{2}}{2 g A^{2}}$$',NULL,'Medium','Graph/Figure',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/26_38010_question_1.png'),
(38011,'physics',27,1382,3323,'A metal is heated in a furnace where a sensor is kept above the metal surface to read the power radiated $P$ by the metal. The sensor has a scale that displays $\\log _{2}\\left(P / P_{0}\\right)$, where $P_{0}$ is a constant. When the metal surface is at a temperature of $487^\\circ C$, the sensor shows a value 1. Assume that the emissivity of the metallic surface remains constant. What is the value displayed by the sensor when the temperature of the metal surface is raised to $2767^\\circ C$','[{"text": "18", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "9", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "90", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "42", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Power radiated $P=e \\sigma A T^{4}$<br>$\\begin{aligned}& \\text { At } 487^{\\circ} C ; P_{1}=e \\sigma A(760)^{4} \\\\& \\text { Given, } \\log _{2} \\dfrac{P_{1}}{P_{0}}=1 \\Rightarrow P_{0}=\\dfrac{P_{1}}{2}\\end{aligned}$<br>At $2767^{\\circ} C ; P_{2}=e \\sigma A(3040)^{4}$ <br>$\\begin{aligned}\\text { Reading } & =\\log _{2}\\left(\\dfrac{P_{2}}{P_{0}}\\right)=\\log _{2}\\left[\\dfrac{e \\sigma A(3040)^{4} \\times 2}{e \\sigma A(760)^{4}}\\right] \\\\& =\\log _{2}\\left(4^{4} \\times 2\\right)=9\\end{aligned}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38012,'physics',29,1301,3147,'One kg of a diatomic gas is at a pressure of $8 \\times {10^4} N/{m^2}$. The density of the gas is $4 kg\\,\\,/\\,\\,{m^3}$. What is the energy of the gas due to its thermal motion','[{"text": "$3 \\\\times {10^4}\\\\,\\\\,J$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$5 \\\\times {10^4}\\\\,\\\\,J$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$6 \\\\times {10^4}\\\\,\\\\,J$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$7 \\\\times {10^4}\\\\,\\\\,J$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'$K \\cdot E=\\dfrac{5}{2} n R T=\\dfrac{5}{2} P V$<br>$K.E = \\frac{5}{2}P\\left( {\\frac{m}{\\rho }} \\right) = \\frac{5}{2} \\times 8 \\times {10^4} \\times \\frac{1}{4} = 5 \\times {10^4} J$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38013,'physics',28,1371,3291,'An ideal gas at \\[NTP\\] is enclosed in an adiabatic vertical cylinder having an area of cross section \\[A = 27\\;c{m^2}\\] between two light movable piston as shown in the fig. Spring with force constant \\[k = 3700\\;N/m\\] is in a relaxed state initially. Now the lower piston is moved upwards a distance \\(h / 2\\), \\(h\\) being the initial length of gas column. It is observed that the upper piston moves up by a distance \\(h / 16\\). Final temperature of gas \\[ = 4/3 \\times 273\\,K\\]. Take \\(\\gamma\\) for the gas to be \\(3 / 2\\). The value of \\(h\\) is:<div class="matching-question"></div>','[{"text": "$1.4\\\\,m$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1\\\\,m$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2\\\\,m$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1.6\\\\,m$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Final volume of gas<br>$V^{\\prime}=A\\left(h-\\dfrac{h}{2}+\\dfrac{h}{16}\\right)=\\dfrac{9 A h}{16}$<br>As the gas has to balance the additional spring force<br>Final pressure of the gas is<br>$P^{\\prime}=p_{a}+\\dfrac{K \\cdot h / 16}{A}=\\left(10^{5}+\\dfrac{37 \\times 10^{6} \\times h}{16 \\times 27}\\right) N / m^{2}$<br>Now, $\\dfrac{P^{\\prime} V^{\\prime}}{T^{\\prime}}=\\dfrac{P V}{T}$<br>$\\Rightarrow \\dfrac{\\left(10^{5}+\\dfrac{37 \\times 10^{6} \\times h}{16 \\times 27}\\right) \\times\\left(\\dfrac{9 \\times A h}{16}\\right)}{\\left(\\dfrac{4}{3} \\times 273\\right)}=\\dfrac{10^{5} \\times A h}{273}$<br>Solving, $h = 1.6\\,m.$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/28_38013_question_1.png'),
(38014,'physics',26,1133,2710,'Two ice cubes of side \\[10\\;cm\\], having cavity of volume \\[20\\;c{m^3}\\] at centre of cube but filled with different materials \\[A\\] and \\[B\\] respectively. The specific gravity of material \\[A\\] is 4.9 and specific gravity of material B is 1.9. Now these cubes are placed in two different vessels of same base area as shown in figure. The water level before putting blocks in vessels are same. Assume that ice melts uniformly from all sides and with same constant rate in both the vessels (specific gravity of ice $$=0.9$$ ) Choose the correct graph showing the variation of heights of waterlevel in two vessels with time:<div class="matching-question"></div>','[{"text": "", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Specific gravity of $A$ is more than that of $B$.<br>$$\\Rightarrow h_{1}>h_{2}$$<br>When the ice starts melting the level of water remain constant in both containers. After some time the level decreases in both containers and finally levels will be equal when both the materials $A$ and $B$ sink.',NULL,'Medium','Graph/Figure',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/26_38014_question_1.png'),
(38015,'physics',27,1382,3317,'Two ends of a conducting rod of varying cross-section are maintained at $100^\\circ C$ and $0^\\circ C$ respectively. In steady state','[{"text": "Temperature difference may be equal or different depending upon thermal conductivity of the rod.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Temperature difference across $AB$ is less than that across $CD$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Temperature difference across $AB$ and $CD$are equal", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Temperature difference across $A B$ is greater", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Rate of flow of heat is constant throughout the rod.<br>Temperature difference is given by<br>$\\Delta T=i R$<br>where $R=\\dfrac{l}{K A} \\Rightarrow R \\propto \\dfrac{1}{A}$<br>Area across $CD$ is less. Therefore, $\\Delta T$ across $CD$ will be more.',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/27_38015_question_1.png'),
(38016,'physics',29,1304,3154,'A horizontal uniform glass tube of \\[100\\,\\,cm\\], length sealed at both ends contain \\[10\\,\\,cm\\] mercury column in the middle. The temperature and pressure of air on either side of mercury column are respectively \\[81^\\circ C\\] and \\[76 \\,\\,cm\\] of mercury. If the air column at one end is kept at \\[0^\\circ C\\] and the other end at \\[273^\\circ C\\], the pressure of air which is at \\[0^\\circ C\\]  is (in \\[cm\\]  of \\[Hg)\\]<div class="matching-question"></div>','[{"text": "76", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "68.2", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "102.4", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "122", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Length of air column on both sides is 45 $cm$ when one side is at $0^\\circ C$ and the other is at $273^\\circ C$.<br>The pressure must be same on both sides. Hence<br>$\\dfrac{T}{V}=\\text { constant }$<br><br>$\\begin{aligned}& \\dfrac{T}{l}=\\text { constant }(V=A l) \\\\& \\dfrac{l_{1}}{T_{1}}=\\dfrac{l_{2}}{T_{2}} \\Rightarrow \\dfrac{l_{1}}{273}=\\dfrac{l_{2}}{(273+273)} \\Rightarrow l_{1}=\\dfrac{l_{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}$<br>Also ${l_1} + {l_2} = 90\\,\\,cm \\Rightarrow {l_1} = 30{\\mkern 1mu} \\,{\\mkern 1mu} cm{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} and\\,\\,{l_2} = 60{\\mkern 1mu} \\,\\,cm$<br>Applying gas equation to the side at $0^\\circ C$, we get<br>$\\frac{{{P_1}{l_1}}}{{{T_1}}} = \\frac{{{P_0}{l_0}}}{{{T_0}}} \\Rightarrow \\frac{{{P_1} \\times 30}}{{273}} = \\frac{{76 \\times 45}}{{273 + 81}} \\Rightarrow {P_1} = 102.4 \\,cm$<br>Where ${P_0}\\,\\,\\& \\,{T_0}$ are the initial pressure and temperature.',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/29_38016_question_1.png'),
(38017,'physics',28,1371,3291,'A horizontal insulated cylindrical vessel of length $2 l$ is separated by a thin insulating piston into two equal parts as shown in fig. Each part contains $n$ moles of an ideal monoatomic gas at temperature $T$. The piston is connected to end faces of the cylinder with the help of two springs each of spring constant $k$. Initially both the springs are in relaxed state. The temperature of left part of cylinder is kept constant by some external means while heat is supplied to right part with the help of filament. If energy (heat) $Q$ is supplied to right part so that the piston moves to the left by $\\dfrac{l}{4}$, then the change in internal energy of right part of cylinder is:','[{"text": "$\\\\dfrac{15}{16} k l^{2}+2 n R T$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{15}{16} k l^{2}+n R T$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{3 n R}{2}\\\\left[\\\\dfrac{5 k l^{2}}{8 n R}+\\\\dfrac{2 T}{3}\\\\right]$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "None of these", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'As heat $Q$ supplied to right part, the piston moves towards left thus compressing the left spring and elongating right spring. To keep the temperature of left part constant some energy say $Q^{\\prime}$ is transferred from gas to external arrangement (which is used to keep its temperature constant).<br>As temperature of left part is constant so change in internal energy of left part is zero.<br>Let $T_{f}$ is the final temperature of right part<br>$\\Delta U=n C_{V} \\Delta T=n C_{V}\\left(T_{f}-T\\right)$<br>Where $T_{f}$ is the final equilibrium temperature of right part. <br><img src=\'/img/data/physics/28_38017_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />  <br>Using ideal gas equation<br>$\\begin{aligned}& \\dfrac{p_{1} \\times A \\times \\dfrac{3 l}{4}}{R T}=\\dfrac{p_{0} A l}{R T} \\quad \\text { (Left part) } \\\\& \\dfrac{p_{2} \\times A \\times \\dfrac{5 l}{4}}{R T_{f}}=\\dfrac{p_{0} A l}{R T} \\quad \\text { (Right part) }\\end{aligned}$<br>From equilibrium of piston,<br>$\\begin{gathered}p_{2}-p_{1}=\\dfrac{k l}{4 A}+\\dfrac{k l}{4 A}=\\dfrac{k l}{2 A} \\\\P_{2}=P_{1}+\\dfrac{k l}{2 A}=\\dfrac{4 P_{0}}{3}+\\dfrac{k l}{2 A} \\\\\\dfrac{P_{2}}{P_{0}}=\\dfrac{4}{3}+\\dfrac{k l}{2 A P_{0}} \\\\\\Rightarrow T_{f}=\\dfrac{5 T}{4}\\left[\\dfrac{k l^{2}}{2 n R T}+\\dfrac{4}{3}\\right] \\text { where } p_{0}=\\dfrac{n R T}{A l} \\\\T_{f}=\\dfrac{5 k l^{2}}{8 n R}+\\dfrac{5 T}{3} \\\\\\Delta U=n \\times \\dfrac{3 R}{2} \\times\\left(T_{f}-T\\right)=\\dfrac{3 n R}{2}\\left[\\dfrac{5 k l^{2}}{8 n R}+\\dfrac{2 T}{3}\\right]\\end{gathered}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/28_38017_question_1.png'),
(38018,'physics',26,1136,2716,'Air of density $1.2\\,kg{m^{ - 3}}$ is blowing across the horizontal wings of an aeroplane in such away that its speeds above and below the wings are $150\\,m{s^{ - 1}}$ and $100\\,m{s^{ - 1}}$, respectively. The pressure difference between the upper and lower sides of the wings, is:','[{"text": "$60\\\\,N{m^{ - 2}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$180\\\\,N{m^{ - 2}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$7500\\\\,N{m^{ - 2}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$12500\\\\,N{m^{ - 2}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Pressure difference $$P_{2}-P_{1}=\\dfrac{1}{2} \\rho\\left(v_{2}^{2}-v_{1}^{2}\\right)$$<br>$ = \\frac{1}{2} \\times 1.2\\left( {{{(150)}^2} - {{(100)}^2}} \\right) = 7500\\,N{m^{ - 2}}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2013,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38019,'physics',27,1382,3319,'The ends $Q$ and $R$ of two thin wires, $P Q$ and $RS$, are joined together. Initially each of the wires has a length of $1m$ at $10^\\circ C$. Now the end $P$ is maintained at $10^\\circ C$, while the end $S$ is heated and maintained at $400^\\circ C$. The system is thermally insulted from its surroundings. If the thermal conductivity of wire $PQ$ is twice that of the wire RS and the coefficient of linear thermal expansion of $PQ$ is $1.2 \\times {10^{ - 5}}\\,{K^{ - 1}}$, the change in length of the wire $P Q$ is','[{"text": "$2.34\\\\,mm$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$0.78\\\\,mm$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$0.90\\\\,mm$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1.56\\\\,mm$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'From the given data;<br><img src=\'/img/data/physics/27_38019_explanation_1.jpg\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' /> <br>Thermal resistance $R=\\dfrac{l}{K A}$<br>$\\dfrac{R_{P Q}}{R_{R S}}=\\dfrac{K_{R S}}{K_{P Q}}=\\dfrac{1}{2}$<br>Let $T$ is the temperature of the junction. As rate of heat flow is same in both parts.<br>so, $T - 10 = \\frac{{400 - T}}{2} \\Rightarrow T = 140^\\circ C$<br>The temperature of the first rod as a function of distance $x$ is<br>$T(x)=10+130 x$<br>Each part of the rod has different change in temperature w.r.to the initial value.<br>The change in temperature of the rod as a function of $x$ w.r.to the initial temperature of the rod is<br>$\\Rightarrow \\Delta T=130 x$<br>Extension in a small element of length $d x$ is<br>$d l=d x \\alpha \\Delta T=130 \\alpha x d x$<br>The total extension is<br>$\\Delta l = 130\\alpha \\int_0^1 x dx = \\frac{{130}}{2} \\times 1.2 \\times {10^{ - 5}} \\times 1$<br>$ \\Rightarrow \\Delta \\ell  = 0.78\\,mm.$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38076,'physics',29,1300,3145,'The densities at points \\[A\\] and \\[B\\] are \\(\\rho_{0}\\) and \\(\\dfrac{3 \\rho_{0}}{2}\\). Find the value of \\(x\\) on \\[P\\]-axis.<div class="matching-question"></div>','[{"text": "$\\\\dfrac{3}{2} P_{0}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2 P_{0}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$4 P_{0}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$3 P_{0}$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'$\\rho=\\dfrac{P M}{R T} \\Rightarrow \\rho \\propto(P / T)$<br>$\\begin{aligned}& \\rho_{A}=\\rho_{0} \\text { and } \\rho_{B}=\\dfrac{3}{2} \\rho_{0} \\\\& \\left(P_{B} / T_{B}\\right)=\\dfrac{3}{2}\\left(P_{A} / T_{A}\\right) \\\\& \\left(x / 2 T_{0}\\right)=\\left(\\dfrac{3}{2}\\right)\\left(P_{0} / T_{0}\\right) \\\\& x=3 P_{0}\\end{aligned}$',NULL,'Hard','Graph/Figure',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/29_38076_question_1.png'),
(38020,'physics',29,1304,3154,'Two diathermic pistons divide an adiabatic container in three equal parts as shown. An ideal gas is present in the three parts $A,B{\\text{ }}\\& {\\text{ }}C$ having initial pressures as shown and having same temperatures. Now the pistions are released. Then the final equilibrium length of part A after long time will be :','[{"text": "$L / 8$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$L / 4$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$L / 6$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{L} / 5$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'After reaching the equilibrium states the temperature and pressure in three parts must be same.<br>i.e. $P V=n R T \\Rightarrow \\dfrac{V}{n}=\\dfrac{R T}{P}=\\dfrac{1}{K}=$ constant<br>Let $\\dfrac{n}{V}=K$<br>By conserving the number of moles<br>$n_{1}+n_{2}+n_{3}=K\\left(V_{1}+V_{2}+V_{3}\\right)$<br>Let $T_{0}$ is the initial temperature of the gas in three regions.<br>$\\dfrac{P(V / 3)}{R T_{0}}+\\dfrac{2 P(V / 3)}{R T_{0}}+\\dfrac{P(V / 3)}{R T_{0}}=K V$<br>Where $T_{0}$ is the temperature in three parts.<br>$\\Rightarrow K=\\dfrac{4 P}{3 R T_{0}}$<br>The volume of the first part is<br>$V_{1}=\\dfrac{n_{1}}{K}=\\dfrac{V}{4}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/29_38020_question_1.png'),
(38022,'physics',26,1136,2717,'A cylindrical vessel of cross-section A contains water to a height $$h$$. There is a hole in the bottom of radius \' $$a$$ \'. The time in which it will be emptied is:','[{"text": "$$\\\\dfrac{2 A}{\\\\pi a^{2}} \\\\sqrt{\\\\dfrac{h}{g}}$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$\\\\dfrac{\\\\sqrt{2} A}{\\\\pi a^{2}} \\\\sqrt{\\\\dfrac{h}{g}}$$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$$\\\\dfrac{2 \\\\sqrt{2} A}{\\\\pi a^{2}} \\\\sqrt{\\\\dfrac{h}{g}}$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$\\\\dfrac{A}{\\\\sqrt{2} \\\\pi a^{2}} \\\\sqrt{\\\\dfrac{h}{g}}$$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Let the rate of falling water level be $$-\\dfrac{d h}{d t}$$<br>Initially at<br>$$\\begin{gathered}t=0 ; h=h \\\\t=t ; h=0\\end{gathered}$$<br>Then, $$A\\left(-\\dfrac{d h}{d t}\\right)=\\pi a^{2} \\cdot v=\\pi a^{2} \\sqrt{2 g h}$$<br>$$d t=-\\dfrac{A}{\\pi a^{2} \\sqrt{2 g h}} d h$$<br>Integrating both sides<br>$$\\begin{aligned}& \\int_{0}^{t} d t=-\\dfrac{A}{\\sqrt{2 g} \\pi a^{2}} \\int_{h}^{0} h^{-1 / 2} d h \\\\& {[t]_{0}^{t}=-\\dfrac{A}{\\sqrt{2 g} \\pi a^{2}} \\cdot\\left[\\dfrac{h^{1 / 2}}{1 / 2}\\right]_{h}^{0} \\Rightarrow t=\\dfrac{\\sqrt{2} A}{\\pi a^{2}} \\sqrt{\\dfrac{h}{g}}}\\end{aligned}$$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',2014,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38023,'physics',27,1382,3318,'A slab consists of two parallel layers of copper and brass of the same thickness and having thermal conductivities in the ratio $1: 4$. The free end of brass is at $100^\\circ C$ and that of copper at $0^\\circ C$. the temperature of interface is','[{"text": "$20^\\\\circ C$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$60^\\\\circ C$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$40^\\\\circ C$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$80^\\\\circ C$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Let $T$ is the temperature of the interface.<br>${K_{{\\rm{Copper }}}} = 4K\\,\\& \\,{K_{{\\rm{brass }}}} = K$<br>As the rate of heat flow is same in two slabs<br>$\\frac{{4K.A(100 - T)}}{l} = \\frac{{KA(T - 0)}}{l}$<br>$T = 80^\\circ C$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38024,'physics',29,1300,3145,'Find the minimum attainable pressure of ideal gas in the process $T=T_{0}+\\alpha V^{2}$, where $T_{0}$, and $\\alpha$ are $+v e$ constants and  $V$ is the volume of one mole of gas.','[{"text": "$R \\\\sqrt{T_{0}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$4 R \\\\sqrt{T_{0}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2 R \\\\sqrt{\\\\alpha T_{0}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{\\\\sqrt{\\\\alpha T_{0}}}{R}$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Given that<br>$T=T_{0}+\\alpha V^{2}$<br>For one mole of the gas, $V=R T / p$<br>$T=T_{0}+\\alpha\\left(\\dfrac{R^{2} T^{2}}{p^{2}}\\right)$<br>Solving it for $p{\\rm{ }},$ we get<br>$p = \\sqrt \\alpha  RT{\\left( {T - {T_0}} \\right)^{ - 1/2}}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,(1)$<br>Differentiating it with respect to $T$, we get<br>$\\dfrac{d p}{d T}=\\sqrt{\\alpha} \\cdot R\\left[\\left(T-T_{0}\\right)^{-1 / 2}-\\dfrac{1}{2} T\\left(T-T_{0}\\right)^{-3 / 2}\\right]$<br>Conditions for minimum pressure is that<br>$(d p / d T)=0 . \\text { Hence },$<br>$\\begin{gathered}\\sqrt{\\alpha} R\\left[\\left(T-T_{0}\\right)^{-1 / 2}-\\dfrac{1}{2} T\\left(T-T_{0}\\right)^{-3 / 2}\\right]=0 \\\\\\left(T-T_{0}\\right)^{-1 / 2}-\\dfrac{1}{2} T\\left(T-T_{0}\\right)^{-3 / 2}=0 \\quad(\\because \\sqrt{\\alpha} R \\neq 0)\\end{gathered}$<br>Now, Solving we get,<br>$T=2 T_{0}$<br>Substituting the value of $T=2 T_{0}$ in Eq. (1) we get<br>${p_{\\min }} = \\sqrt \\alpha  R\\left( {2{T_0}} \\right){\\left[ {2{T_0} - {T_0}} \\right]^{ - 1/2}}$<br>$or\\,\\,{p_{\\min }} = {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} \\,2R\\sqrt {\\left( {\\alpha {T_0}} \\right)} $',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38025,'physics',26,1136,2717,'A tank with a small hole at the bottom has been filled with water and kerosene (specific gravity 0.8 ). The height of water is $3\\,m$ and that of kerosene $2\\,m$. When the hole is opended the velocity of fluid coming out from it is nearly:<br>(take $g = 10\\,m{s^{ - 2}}$ and density of water<br>$ = {10^3}kg{m^{ - 3}}$ )','[{"text": "$10.7\\\\,m{s^{ - 1}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$9.6\\\\,m{s^{ - 1}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$8.5\\\\,m{s^{ - 1}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$7.6\\\\,m{s^{ - 1}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'By applying the bernoulli\'s equation at the top and the base<br><img src=\'/img/data/physics/26_38025_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' /> <br>$$P_{0}+\\dfrac{1}{2} 0.8\\left(v_{1}\\right)^{2}+1 \\times g \\times 3+0.8 \\times g \\times 2=P_{0}+\\dfrac{1}{2} 0.8 v_{2}^{2}$$<br>Here $$v_{1}=0$$<br>$4.6g = \\frac{1}{2}(0.8)v_2^2 \\Rightarrow v_2^2 = 115 \\Rightarrow {v_2} = 10.7\\,m/s$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',2014,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38027,'physics',27,1382,3323,'A planet is at an average distance $d$ from the sun, and its average surface temperature is $T$. Assume that the planet receives energy only from the sun, and loses energy only through radiation from its surface. Neglect atmospheric effects. If $T \\propto d^{-n}$ the value of $n$ is','[{"text": "$\\\\dfrac{1}{4}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{1}{2}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "1", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "2", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'let $P = $ power radiated by the sun, $R = $ radius of planet.<br>Energy received by planet $=\\dfrac{P}{4 \\pi d^{2}} \\times \\pi R^{2}$<br>Energy radiated by planet $=\\left(4 \\pi R^{2}\\right) \\sigma T^{2}$.<br>For thermal equilibrium, $\\dfrac{P}{4 \\pi d^{2}} \\times \\pi R^{2}=4 \\pi R^{2} \\sigma T^{4}$.<br>$\\Rightarrow \\quad T^{4} \\propto \\dfrac{1}{d^{2}} \\quad \\text { or } T^{4} \\propto d^{-1 / 2}$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38028,'physics',29,1304,3154,'A closed gas cylinder is divided into two parts by a piston held tight. The pressure and volume of gas in two parts respectively are $(P,5{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} V)$ and $(10{\\rm{ }}P,{\\rm{ }}V)$. If now the piston is left free and the system undergoes isothermal process, then the volume of the gas in two parts respectively are','[{"text": "$3{\\\\rm{ }}V,{\\\\rm{ }}3{\\\\rm{ }}V$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2 V,4 V$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$5 V, V$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$4 V,2 V$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'<img src=\'/img/data/physics/29_38028_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />   When the piston is allowed to move the gases are kept separated but the pressure has to be equal. $\\left(P_{1}=P_{2}\\right)$ and final volume $x$ and $(6V - x)$, the no of moles are same in initial and final position at each parts. <br>$\\begin{aligned}& \\because \\quad P_{1}=P_{2} \\quad P V=n_{1} R T \\\\& \\dfrac{n_{1} R T}{x}=\\dfrac{n_{2} R T}{6 V-x}\\end{aligned}$<br>where $n_{1}=\\dfrac{5 P V}{R T}, n_{2}=\\dfrac{10 P V}{R T}$<br>$\\begin{aligned}& \\dfrac{n_{1}}{x}=\\dfrac{n_{2}}{6 V-x} \\\\& \\Rightarrow \\dfrac{5 P V}{x R T}=\\dfrac{10 P V}{(6 V-x) R T} \\Rightarrow \\dfrac{1}{x}=\\dfrac{2}{6 V-x} \\\\& \\Rightarrow 6 V-x=2 x \\Rightarrow x=2 V \\text { and } \\\\& \\quad 6 V-x \\Rightarrow 6 V-2 V=4 V \\\\& \\therefore \\quad(2 V, 4 V)\\end{aligned}$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38029,'physics',28,1371,3289,'An ideal gas expand isothermally from a volume $V_{1}$ to $V_{2}$ and then compressed to original volume $V_{1}$ adiabatically. Initial pressure is $P_{1}$ and finally pressure is $P_{3}$. The total work done is $W$. Then','[{"text": "$P_{3} < P_{1}, W < 0$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$P_{3} < P_{1}, W>0$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$P_{3}=P_{1}, W=0$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$P_{3}>P_{1}, W < 0$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'From graph it is clear that $P_{3}>P_{1}$.<br><img src=\'/img/data/physics/28_38029_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />  <br>Since area under adiabatic process $(BCED)$ is greater than that of isothermal process $(ABDE)$. Therefore net work done<br>$\\begin{aligned}& W=W_{A B}+W_{B C} \\\\& W_{A B}=(+) v e ; W_{B C}=(-) v e \\Rightarrow W=(-) v e\\end{aligned}$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38030,'physics',26,1136,2719,'Water is flowing at a speed of $1.5\\,m{s^{ - 1}}$ through horizontal tube of cross-sectional area ${10^{ - 2}}\\,{m^2}$ and you are trying to stop the flow by your plam. Assuming that the water stops immediately after hitting the plam, the minimum force that you must exert should be (density of water $ = {10^3}kg{m^{ - 3}}$ )','[{"text": "$11.25\\\\,N$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$22.5\\\\,N$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$33.7\\\\,N$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$45\\\\,N$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'The thrust force exerted by the liquid on the hand is<br>$$F=\\rho A v^{2}=10^{3} \\times 10^{-2} \\times(1.5)^{2} \\quad F=22.5 N$$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2014,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38031,'physics',27,1382,3323,'A space object has the shape of a sphere of radius $R$. Heat sources ensure that the heat evolution at a constant rate is distributed uniformly over its volume. The amount of heat liberated by a unit surface area is proportional to the fourth power of thermodynamic temperature. If the power liberated is same and radius is made half of the initial value then ratio of final temperature to initial temperature is','[{"text": "2.19", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "0.414", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "1.414", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "4.19", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'The heat radiated per unit time is<br>$\\frac{{dQ}}{{dt}}\\alpha {R^2}{T^4}$<br>$ \\Rightarrow {R^2}{T^4} = {\\text{ constant }}$<br>${\\left( {\\frac{{{T_1}}}{{{T_2}}}} \\right)^4} = {\\left( {\\frac{{{R_2}}}{{{R_1}}}} \\right)^2}$<br>$\\begin{aligned}& \\dfrac{T_{1}}{T_{2}}=\\left(\\dfrac{R_{2}}{R_{1}}\\right)^{1 / 2} \\\\& T_{2}=T_{1}\\left(\\dfrac{R_{1}}{R_{2}}\\right)^{1 / 2}=T_{1}\\left(\\dfrac{R_{1}}{R_{1} / 2}\\right)^{1 / 2} \\\\& T_{2}=\\sqrt{2} T_{1}=1.414 T_{1}\\end{aligned}$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38032,'physics',29,1304,3154,'Two glass spheres of equal volume are connected by a small tube containing a small amount of mercury as shown in figure. The spheres are sealed at $20^\\circ C$ with exactly 1 litre of air in each side. If the cross sectional area of tube is $5\\,m{m^2}$ how far will the mercury be displaced if the temperature of one sphere is raised by $0^\\circ C$ while the other is maintained at $20^\\circ C$','[{"text": "$2.4 \\\\,\\\\,cm$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$5.4 \\\\,\\\\,cm$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$3.4 \\\\,\\\\,cm$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$1.4 \\\\,\\\\,cm$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'The initial and final positions of the two gases are shown in the figure below :<br><img src=\'/img/data/physics/29_38032_explanation_1.jpg\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' /> <br>Let the mercury is displaced by $x$. Then<br>$dV = xS\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,(1)$<br>Where $S = 5 \\,\\,m{m^2} = 5 \\times {10^{ - 2}} c{m^2}$<br>In equilbrium, pressure on both sides is say $P^{\\prime}$. Then<br>${P^\\prime }(V - dV) = nRT\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,(2)$<br>and ${P^\\prime }(V + dV) = nR(T + dT)\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,(3)$<br>Dividing (3) by (2) we get<br>$\\dfrac{V+d V}{V-d V}=\\dfrac{T+d T}{T}$<br>Here $V = 1{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} litre = {10^3}c{m^3}.$<br>$T = 293\\,K\\,\\,and{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} T = 0.1\\,K$<br>Solving, we get<br>$dV = 0.17 c{m^3}$<br>Hence $x = \\frac{{dV}}{S} = \\frac{{0.17}}{{5 \\times {{10}^{ - 2}}}} = 3.4\\, cm$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/29_38032_question_1.jpg'),
(38033,'physics',28,1371,3290,'An insulated container has two chambers separated by a valve. Chamber I contains an ideal gas and the chamber II has vacuum. If the gas expands then predict the incorrect option','[{"text": "It does not represent isothermal process", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "It does not represent adiabatic process", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Isobaric process", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Temperature remains constant", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'In free expansion $\\Delta Q=0, \\Delta T=0$, but it doesn\'t represent isothermal and adiabatic processes. Temperature remains constant and it does not represent isobaric process.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/28_38033_question_1.png'),
(38034,'physics',26,1135,2714,'Consider a water jar of radius $R$ that has water filled up to height $H$ and is kept on a stand of height $$h$$ (see figure). Through a hole of radius $$r$$ $(r <  < R)$ at its bottom, the water leaks out and the stream of water coming down towards the ground has a shape like a funnel as shown in the figure. If the radius of the cross-section of water stream when it hits the ground is $$x$$. Then:','[{"text": "$$x=r\\\\left(\\\\dfrac{H}{H+h}\\\\right)^{\\\\frac{1}{4}}$$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$$x=r\\\\left(\\\\dfrac{H}{H+h}\\\\right)$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$x=r\\\\left(\\\\dfrac{H}{H+h}\\\\right)^{2}$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$x=r\\\\left(\\\\dfrac{H}{H+h}\\\\right)^{\\\\frac{1}{2}}$$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'From Bernoulli\'s Principle,<br>The velocity that comes out from the hole is<br>$$v_{1}=\\sqrt{2 g h}$$<br>Let $$v_{2}$$ is the velocity after falling height $$h$$<br>$$\\begin{gathered}a_{1} v_{1}=a_{2} v_{2} \\\\\\pi r^{2} \\sqrt{2 g H}=\\pi x^{2} v_{2} \\\\\\dfrac{r^{2}}{x^{2}} \\sqrt{2 g H}=v_{2} \\\\v_{2}^{2}-v_{1}^{2}=2 g h \\quad \\Rightarrow v_{2}^{2}=2 g H+2 g h \\\\2 g H+2 g h=\\dfrac{r^{4}}{x^{4}} 2 g H \\Rightarrow x=r\\left[\\dfrac{H}{H+h}\\right]^{\\frac{1}{4}}\\end{gathered}$$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2016,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/26_38034_question_1.jpg'),
(38035,'physics',27,1382,3323,'The solar constant for the earth is $\\Sigma$. The surface temperature of the sun is $TK$. The sun subtends an angle $\\theta$ at the earth.','[{"text": "$\\\\Sigma \\\\propto T^{4}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\Sigma \\\\propto T^{2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\Sigma \\\\propto \\\\theta^{2}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\Sigma \\\\propto \\\\theta^{3}$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Let $R = $ radius of the sun, $d = $ distance of the earth from the sun.<br>Power radiated by the sun $=\\left(4 \\pi R^{2}\\right) \\sigma T^{4}=P$.<br><img src=\'/img/data/physics/27_38035_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' /> <br>Energy received per unit area per second normally on the earth.<br>$\\begin{aligned}& \\Sigma=\\dfrac{P}{4 \\pi d^{2}}=\\dfrac{4 \\pi R^{2} \\sigma T^{4}}{4 \\pi d^{2}} \\\\& =\\left(\\sigma T^{4}\\right)\\left(\\dfrac{R}{d}\\right)^{2}=\\dfrac{1}{4} \\sigma T^{4}\\left(\\dfrac{2 R}{d}\\right)^{2}\\end{aligned}$<br>Angle subtended by the sun at the earth is<br>$\\theta  = \\frac{{2R}}{d}$<br>$ \\Rightarrow \\,\\,\\,\\sum  \\propto {\\mkern 1mu} {\\kern 1pt} {\\theta ^2}.$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38036,'physics',29,1304,3154,'In the given figure a glass tube lies horizontally with the middle \\[20\\,\\,cm\\] containing mercury. The two ends of the tube contains air at \\[27^\\circ C\\] and at a pressure \\[76 \\,\\,cm\\] of mercury. Now the air column on one side is maintained at \\[0^\\circ C\\] and the other side is maintained at \\[127^\\circ C\\]. Find the new length of the air column on the cooler side. Neglect the changes in the volume of mercury and of the glass.<div class="matching-question"></div>','[{"text": "$36.25\\\\,\\\\,cm$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$32.45\\\\,\\\\,cm$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$26.55\\\\,\\\\,cm$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$28.55\\\\,\\\\,cm$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'The number of moles present on both sides are same. After reaching mechanical equilibrium the pressures on both sides are same.<br>The temperatures on both sides are given as<br>${T_1} = 273 K{\\rm{ }}\\,\\& \\,\\,{T_2} = 400\\,K$<br>${P_1} = {P_2}$<br>$\\frac{{nR{T_1}}}{{{V_1}}} = \\frac{{nR{T_2}}}{{{V_2}}}$<br>Let $x$ is the displacement of the mercury column towards the cooler side.<br>${V_1} = A(40\\,\\,cm - x)$<br>${V_2} = A(40\\,\\,cm + x)$<br>A - Area of cross section of the tube.<br>$ \\Rightarrow \\frac{{273}}{{40 - x}} = \\frac{{400}}{{40 + x}}$<br>$ \\Rightarrow x = 7.55 \\,cm$<br>The length of air column on cooler side is $32.45\\,\\,cm$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/29_38036_question_1.png'),
(38037,'physics',26,1133,2705,'A thin uniform tube is bent into a circle of radius $$r$$ in the vertical plane. Equal volumes of two immiscible liquids, whose densities are $$\\rho_{1}$$ and $$\\rho_{2}\\left(\\rho_{1}>\\rho_{2}\\right)$$, fill half the circle. The angle $$\\theta$$ between the radius vector passing through the common interface and the vertical is:','[{"text": "$$\\\\theta=\\\\tan ^{-1}\\\\left(\\\\dfrac{\\\\rho_{1}+\\\\rho_{2}}{\\\\rho_{1}-\\\\rho_{2}}\\\\right)$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$\\\\theta=\\\\tan ^{-1}\\\\left[\\\\left(\\\\dfrac{\\\\rho_{1}-\\\\rho_{2}}{\\\\rho_{1}+\\\\rho_{2}}\\\\right)\\\\right]$$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$$\\\\theta=\\\\tan ^{-1}\\\\left(\\\\dfrac{\\\\rho_{2}}{\\\\rho_{1}}\\\\right)$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$\\\\theta=\\\\tan ^{-1}\\\\left(\\\\dfrac{\\\\rho_{1}}{\\\\rho_{2}}\\\\right)$$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'<br><img src=\'/img/data/physics/26_38037_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' /> <br>Equating the pressure at $$\\mathrm{G}$$ from both sides<br>$$\\begin{aligned}& \\rho_{1} g(R-R \\sin \\theta)=\\rho_{1} g(R-R \\cos \\theta)+\\rho_{2} g R \\sin \\theta \\\\& +\\rho_{2} g R \\cos \\theta \\\\& \\rho_{1} g R(\\cos \\theta-\\sin \\theta)=\\rho_{2} g R(\\cos \\theta+\\sin \\theta) \\\\& \\rho_{1}(1-\\tan \\theta)=\\rho_{2}(1+\\tan \\theta) \\\\& \\Rightarrow \\theta=\\tan ^{-1}\\left(\\dfrac{\\rho_{1}-\\rho_{2}}{\\rho_{1}+\\rho_{2}}\\right)\\end{aligned}$$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',2018,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38038,'physics',28,1371,3290,'An adiabatic cylindrical tube is fitted with an adiabatic separator as shown in figure. Initially separator is in equilibrium and divides a tube in two equal parts. The separator can be slide into the tube by an external mechanism. An ideal gas $(\\gamma=1.5)$ is injected in the two sides at equal pressures and temperatures. Now separator is slide to a position where it divides the tube in the ratio $7: 3$. Find the ratio of the temperatures in the two parts of the vessel.','[{"text": "$\\\\sqrt{3}: \\\\sqrt{2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\sqrt{1}: \\\\sqrt{2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\sqrt{3}: \\\\sqrt{7}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\sqrt{7}: \\\\sqrt{3}$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'From adiabatic relation between temperature and volume<br>$T_{i} V_{i}^{(\\gamma-1)}=T_{f} V_{f}^{(\\gamma-1)}$<br>Let $V_{0}$ and $T_{0}$ are the volume of the gas and temperature initially in each part. After displacing the adiabatic separator the new volumes are $V_{1}$ and $V_{2}$ and temperatures are $T_{1}$ and $T_{2}$<br>Given that $\\dfrac{V_{1}}{V_{2}}=\\dfrac{7}{3}$<br>$V_{1}+V_{2}=2 V_{0} \\Rightarrow V_{1}=\\dfrac{14 V_{0}}{10} \\& V_{2}=\\dfrac{6 V_{0}}{10}$<br>$ \\Rightarrow {T_0}V_0^{\\gamma  - 1} = {T_1}{\\left( {\\frac{{14{V_0}}}{{10}}} \\right)^{\\gamma  - 1}}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\left( 1 \\right)$<br>${T_0}V_0^{\\gamma  - 1} = {T_2}{\\left( {\\frac{{6{V_0}}}{{10}}} \\right)^{\\gamma  - 1}}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\left( 2 \\right)$<br>From (1) & (2) we get $\\dfrac{T_{1}}{T_{2}}=\\dfrac{\\sqrt{3}}{\\sqrt{7}}$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/28_38038_question_1.png'),
(38039,'physics',27,1382,3323,'Four spheres $A$, $B$, $C$ and $D$ are of same radius but made of different metals. Their densities are in ratio $6: 3: 4: 5$ and specific heats are in ratio $2: 5: 4: 6$. These are initially kept at the same temperature and placed in the same surroundings. The sphere which has the slowest rate of cooling is:','[{"text": "$C$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$B$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$D$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$A$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'The rate of heat flow is<br>$\\dfrac{d Q}{d t}=e \\sigma\\left(4 \\pi R^{2}\\right) \\Delta T$<br>where $R$ is the radius of the sphere and $\\Delta T = {T^4} - T_0^4\\,\\,\\,{\\mkern 1mu} {\\kern 1pt} T\\,\\& \\,{T_0}$ are the temperature of the body and surrounding<br>$\\dfrac{d T}{d t}=\\dfrac{4 \\pi e \\sigma R^{2}}{m c} \\Delta T=\\dfrac{4 \\pi e \\sigma R^{2}}{\\rho \\dfrac{4}{3} \\pi R^{3} c}(\\Delta T)=\\dfrac{3 e \\sigma \\Delta T}{\\rho R c}$<br>$c$ - heat capacity<br>The rate of cooling $\\dfrac{d T}{d t}=\\dfrac{k}{R c}$<br>where $k$ is a constant $)$<br>For the material $D$ the value of $RC$ is maximum and rate of cooling is minimum.',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38041,'physics',28,1371,3290,'A cylindrical tube with adiabatic walls having volume $2 V_{o}$ contains an ideal monoatomic gas as shown in the figure. The tube is divided into two equal parts by a fixed super conducting wall. Initially the pressure and the temperature are $P_{o}, T_{o}$ on the left and $2 P_{o}, 2 T_{o}$ on the right. When system is left side for sufficient amount of time the temperature on both sides becomes equal. How much heat has flown from the gas on the right to the gas on the left?','[{"text": "$\\\\dfrac{3 P_{o} V_{o}}{4}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{P_{o} V_{o}}{4}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{P_{o} V_{o}}{3}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{P_{o} V_{o}}{6}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'At thermal equilibrium the final temperatures on both sides are equal. Let $T$ is the final temperature. The pressures on both sides are also equal and $P$ is the final pressure on both sides.<br>The internal energy of a monoatomic gas is $U=n C_{v} T=n \\dfrac{3}{2} R T$<br>By conserving the energy of the system initially and finally.<br>$\\dfrac{3}{2} n R T_{0}+\\dfrac{3}{2} n R\\left(2 T_{0}\\right)=\\dfrac{3}{2} n R T+\\dfrac{3}{2} n R T$<br>(No. of moles are same on both sides)<br>$\\Rightarrow T=\\dfrac{3 T_{0}}{2}$<br>The heat that has flown from right side to the left side is<br>$\\Delta \\mu=\\dfrac{3}{2} n R T-\\dfrac{3}{2} n R T_{0}=\\dfrac{3}{4} n R T_{0}=\\dfrac{3 P_{0} V_{0}}{4}$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/28_38041_question_1.png'),
(38042,'physics',26,1133,2707,'A long cylindrical vessel is half filled with a liquid. When the vessel is rotated about its own vertical axis, the liquid rises up near the wall. If the radius of vessel is $5\\,cm$ and its rotational speed is 2 rotations per second, then the difference in the heights between the centre and the sides, in $$\\mathrm{cm}$$, will be','[{"text": "0.4", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "2.0", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "0.1", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "1.2", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'The linear speed of the liquid at the sides is $$r \\omega$$. At the centre of the liquid and on the top surface the pressure is equal to atmospheric pressure. $$\\rho \\dfrac{r^{2} \\omega^{2}}{2}=\\rho g h$$<br>$2gh = {\\omega ^2}{r^2}$<br>$ \\Rightarrow h = \\frac{{{{(2 \\times 2\\pi )}^2}{{\\left( {5 \\times {{10}^{ - 2}}} \\right)}^2}}}{{2 \\times 10}} \\approx 0.02\\,m$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',2019,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38045,'physics',28,1371,3290,'A horizontal frictionless piston, of negligible mass and heat capacity, divides a vertical insulated cylinder into two halves. Each half of the cylinder contains 1 mole of air at standard temperature and pressure $p_{o}$. A load of weight $W$ is now suspended from the piston, as shown in the figure. It pulls the piston down and comes to rest after a few oscillations. How large a volume does the compressed air in the lower part of the cylinder ultimately occupy if $W$ is very large?','[{"text": "$15\\\\,\\\\% $", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$35\\\\,\\\\% $", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$45\\\\,\\\\% $", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$5\\\\,\\\\% $", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Let $A$ denote the cross-sectional area of the piston and $y$ be the vertical displacement between its initial and final equilibrium positions<br><img src=\'/img/data/physics/28_38045_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />  <br>The decrease in potential energy of the weight is equal to increase in internal energy of the air inside the cylinder. Conservation of energy between the initial and the final states gives<br>$Wy = \\frac{5}{2}\\left[ {{p_1}A(h - y) + {p_2}A(h + y)} \\right]$<br>$\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, - \\left[ {2{p_0}Ah} \\right]\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\left( 1 \\right)$<br>where $p_{1}$ is the final pressure in the lower part of the cylinder and $p_{2}$ that in the upper part.<br>The internal energy of a gas made up of diatomic molecules has been written in the form $\\dfrac{5}{2} p V$.<br>As $W$ is very large the decrease in potential energy will be converted into internal energy.<br>The initial internal energy of the gas is negligible in comparsion with final energy.<br>$Wy = \\frac{5}{2}\\left[ {{p_1}A(h - y) + {p_2}A(h + y)} \\right]\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\left( 2 \\right)$<br>When the load is finally at rest,<br>$\\left( {{p_1} - {p_2}} \\right)A = W\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\left( 3 \\right)$<br>The temperatures and the masses of the gases in the two halves are identical, and so their internal energies must be equal :<br>$\\frac{5}{2}{p_1}A(h - y) = \\frac{5}{2}{p_2}A(h + y)\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\left( 4 \\right)$<br>Equation (1), (2), (3) & (4) yield $y=\\sqrt{\\dfrac{5}{7}} h$ for the displacement of the piston, i.e., the gas in the lower part is compressed to 15 percent of its original volume.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/28_38045_question_1.png'),
(38049,'physics',28,1371,3291,'A thermally insulated rigid container contains an ideal gas heated by a filament of resistance $100\\,\\Omega $ through a current of $1\\,A$ for $5\\,\\min $ then change in internal energy is','[{"text": "$0\\\\,kJ$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$10\\\\,kJ$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$20\\\\,kJ$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$30\\\\,kJ$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Volume of the ideal gas is constant so<br>$W = P\\Delta V = 0$<br>$\\Delta Q = \\Delta U \\Rightarrow \\Delta U = {i^2}Rt$<br>$ = {1^2} \\times 100 \\times 5 \\times 60 = 30 \\times {10^3} = 30\\,kJ.$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38075,'physics',27,1382,3323,'A silver ball, painted black is kept inside a box which is maintained at a temperature of \\[27^\\circ C\\]. The ball is maintained initially at a constant temperature of \\[127^\\circ C\\] by making the radiation to fall on it through a small hole in the box. Later on due to some chemical reaction between silver and paint, the paint uniformly evaporates from the surface of ball exposing the silver. If same amount of radiation continues to fall on ball, then temperature of ball as a function of time is shown as: (Assume emissivity of silver is zero)<div class="matching-question"></div>','[{"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'As emissivity of sliver is zero $(e = 0)$ so the total radiation that falls on the sphere will be completely absorbed and hence its temperature will remain constant since the sphere acts like a good emitter (Good absorbers are good emitters).',NULL,'Medium','Graph/Figure',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/27_38075_question_1.png'),
(38258,'physics',27,1378,3304,'A centigrade and a Fehrenheit thermometer are dipped in boiling water. The water temperature is lowered until the Fahrenheit thermometer registers $140^\\circ $. What is the fall in temperature is registered by the Centigrade thermometer','[{"text": "$30^{\\\\circ}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$40^{\\\\circ}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$60^{\\\\circ}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$80^{\\\\circ}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'$\\dfrac{F-32}{180}=\\dfrac{C-0}{100}$<br>$\\Rightarrow \\dfrac{72}{180}=\\dfrac{\\Delta C}{100}$<br>$ \\Rightarrow \\Delta C = 40^\\circ C$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38053,'physics',28,1371,3291,'A container of volume $4{\\rm{ }}{V_0}$ made of a perfectly non-conducting material is divided into two equal parts by a fixed rigid wall whose lower half is non-conducting and upper half is purely conducting. The right side of the wall is divided into equal parts (initially) by means of a massless non-conducting piston free to move as shown. Section $A$ contains $2\\,mol$ of a gas while the section $B$ and $C$ contain $1\\,mol$ each of the same gas $(\\gamma=1.5)$ at pressure $P_{0}$. The heater in left part is switched on till the final pressure in section $C$ becomes $125 / 27 P_{0}$. Calculate: Final temperature in part $A$ :','[{"text": "$\\\\dfrac{P_{0} V_{0}}{R}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{12 P_{0} V_{0}}{13 R}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{205 P_{0} V_{0}}{27 R}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{105 P_{0} V_{0}}{13 R}$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'The final volume of the compartment $\\mathrm{C}$ is<br>$\\begin{aligned}& P_{0} V_{0}^{\\gamma}=\\dfrac{125 P_{0}}{27} V_{C}^{\\gamma} \\Rightarrow V_{C}^{\\gamma}=\\dfrac{27}{125} V_{0}^{\\gamma} \\\\& V_{C}=\\left(\\dfrac{27}{125}\\right)^{\\frac{1}{\\gamma}} V_{0}=\\left(\\dfrac{27}{125}\\right)^{\\frac{2}{3}} V_{0} \\Rightarrow V_{C}=\\dfrac{9 V_{0}}{25}\\end{aligned}$<br>The final volume of compartment $B$ is<br>$V_{B}=2 V_{0}-\\dfrac{9 V_{0}}{25}=\\dfrac{41 V_{0}}{25}$<br>The final temerature of compartment $\\mathrm{B}$ is<br>$\\begin{aligned}& T_{B}=\\dfrac{P_{B} V_{B}}{n R} \\\\& P_{B}=P_{C}=\\dfrac{125 P_{0}}{27} \\text { (Mechanical equilibrium) } \\\\& V_{B}=\\dfrac{41 V_{0}}{25}, n=1 \\\\& \\Rightarrow \\quad T_{B}=\\dfrac{\\dfrac{125 P_{0}}{27} \\times \\dfrac{41 V_{0}}{25}}{R}=\\dfrac{205 P_{0} V_{0}}{27 R} \\\\& T_{A}=T_{B}=\\dfrac{205 P_{0} V_{0}}{27 R}\\end{aligned}$<br>Since the wall between $A$ & $B$ is conducting in nature.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/28_38053_question_1.png'),
(38060,'physics',29,1300,3145,'Pressure versus temperature graphs of an ideal gas are as shown in figure. Choose the wrong statement<div class="matching-question"></div>','[{"text": "Density of gas is increasing in graph (II)", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Density of gas is decreasing in graph (I)", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Density of gas is constant in graph (III)", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "None of these", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'$\\rho=\\dfrac{P M}{R T}$<br>Density $\\rho$ remains constant when $P / T$ or volume remains constant.<br>In graph (I), pressure is increasing at constant temperature hence density is increasing. In graph (II) density is decreasing. In graph (III) $\\dfrac{P}{T}=$ Constant so density remain constant.',NULL,'Medium','Graph/Figure',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/29_38060_question_1.png'),
(38064,'physics',29,1300,3145,'The density \\((\\rho)\\) versus pressure \\((P)\\) of a given mass of an ideal gas is shown at two temperatures \\(T_{1}\\) and \\(T_{2}\\) Then relation between \\(T_{1}\\) and \\(T_{2}\\) may be<div class="matching-question"></div>','[{"text": "$T_{2}>T_{1}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$T_{1}>T_{2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$T_{1}=T_{2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "All the three are possible", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'According to ideal gas equation $P V=$ nRT<br>$P V=\\dfrac{m}{M} R T, P=\\dfrac{\\rho}{M} R T \\Rightarrow \\dfrac{\\rho}{P} \\propto \\dfrac{1}{T}$<br>Here, $\\dfrac{\\rho}{P}$ represents the slope of the graph.<br>As $\\left(\\dfrac{\\rho}{P}\\right)_{1}>\\left(\\dfrac{\\rho}{P}\\right)_{2}$<br>Hence $T_{2}>T_{1}$.',NULL,'Hard','Graph/Figure',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/29_38064_question_1.jpg'),
(38067,'physics',27,1382,3324,'If a piece of metal is heated to temperature \\(\\theta\\) and then allowed to cool in a room which is at temperature \\(\\theta_{0}\\), the graph between the temperature \\[T\\] of the metal and time \\[t\\] will be closest to<div class="matching-question"></div>','[{"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'According to Newton\'s law of cooling, the temperature goes on decreasing with time non-linearly and finally it reaches temperature $\\theta_{0}$.',NULL,'Hard','Graph/Figure',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38068,'physics',29,1300,3145,'An ideal gas is initially at temperature \\(T\\) and volume \\(V\\). Its volume is increased by \\(\\Delta V\\) due to an increase in temperature \\(\\Delta T\\), pressure remaining constant. The quantity<br>\\(\\delta=\\Delta V /(V \\Delta T)\\) varies with temperature as (\\(T\\) is in kelvin)<div class="matching-question"></div>','[{"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'From ideal gas equation<br>$PV = nRT\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,(1)$<br>or $P\\Delta V = nR\\Delta T\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,(2)$<br>Dividing equation (2) by (1) we get<br>$\\begin{aligned}& \\dfrac{\\Delta V}{V}=\\dfrac{\\Delta T}{T} \\Rightarrow \\dfrac{\\Delta V}{V \\Delta T}=\\dfrac{1}{T}=\\delta \\quad \\text { [Given] } \\\\& \\therefore \\delta=\\dfrac{1}{T} . \\text { So the graph between } \\delta \\text { and T }\\end{aligned}$<br>will be rectangular hyperbola.',NULL,'Hard','Graph/Figure',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38071,'physics',27,1382,3321,'The graph shown in the adjacent diagram, represents the variation of temperature \\((T)\\) of two bodies, \\(x\\) and y having same surface area, with time \\[(t)\\] due to the emission of radiation. Find the correct relation between the emissivity (\\[e\\]) and absorptivity (\\[a\\]) of the two bodies<div class="matching-question"></div>','[{"text": "${e_x} < {e_y}\\\\,\\\\& \\\\,{a_x} > {a_y}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${e_x} > {e_y}\\\\,\\\\& \\\\,{a_x} < {a_y}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${e_x} < {e_y}\\\\,\\\\& \\\\,{a_x} < {a_y}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${e_x} > {e_y}\\\\,\\\\& \\\\,{a_x} > {a_y}$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Rate of cooling $\\left(-\\dfrac{d T}{d t}\\right) \\propto$ emissivity (e)<br>From graph, $\\left(-\\dfrac{d T}{d t}\\right)_{x}>\\left(-\\dfrac{d T}{d t}\\right)_{y} \\Rightarrow e_{x}>e_{y}$<br>As good absorbers are good emitters<br>So emissivity $(e) \\propto$ Absorptive power $(a) \\Rightarrow a_{x}>a_{y}$',NULL,'Hard','Graph/Figure',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/27_38071_question_1.png'),
(38072,'physics',29,1300,3146,'PV versus \\(T\\)  graph of equal mass of \\[{H_2},He\\] and  \\[C{O_2}\\]  is shown in the figure. Choose the correct alternative<div class="matching-question"></div>','[{"text": "3 corresponds to ${H_2},2$ to $He$ and 1 to $C{O_2}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "1 corresponds to $C{O_2},2$ to${H_2}$ and 3 to $He$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "1 corresponds to $He,3$ to ${H_2}$ and 2 to $C{O_2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "1 corresponds to $He,2$ to ${H_2}$ and 3 to $C{O_2}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'The ideal gas equation is<br>$PV = nRT$<br>As the graph is between $PV$ and $T$ so $nR$ represents slope.<br>From the graph $n_{3}>n_{2}>n_{1}$<br>$\\dfrac{m}{M_{3}}>\\dfrac{m}{M_{2}}>\\dfrac{m}{M_{1}} \\Rightarrow M_{1}>M_{2}>M_{3}$<br>$M_{1}$ corresponds to $\\mathrm{CO}_{2}, M_{2}$ corresponds to $He$  and $M_{3}$ corresponds to ${H_2}$',NULL,'Hard','Graph/Figure',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/29_38072_question_1.jpg'),
(39505,'physics',3,313,577,'A ball is thrown straight up in the air. For which situation are both the instantaneous velocity and the acceleration zero?','[{"text": "Halfway up and halfway down", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "On the way up", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "At the top of its flight path", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "None of the above", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Once the ball has left the thrower’s hand, it is a freely falling body with a constant, non-zero, acceleration of $a =  - g$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38080,'physics',29,1300,3146,'The figure below shows the plot of \\(\\dfrac{P V}{n T}\\) versus \\[P\\] for<br>oxygen gas at two different temperatures.<br>Read the following statements concerning the above curves: (i) The dotted line corresponds to the \'ideal \' gas behaviour.<br>(ii) \\[{T_1} > {T_2}\\]<br>(iii) The value of \\[\\frac{{pV}}{{nT}}\\] at the point where the curves meet in the y- axis is the same for all gases.<br>Which of the above statement is true<div class="matching-question"></div>','[{"text": "(i) and (ii) only", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "(i) only", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "(i), (ii) & (iii)", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "None of these", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'(i) the dotted line in the diagram shows that there is no deviation in the value of $\\dfrac{P V}{n T}$ for different temperatures $T_{1}$ and $T_{2}$ for increaing pressure, so this gas behaves ideally.<br>(ii) We know at high temperature, the deviation of the gas is less and at low temperature the deviation of gas is more. In the graph, deviation for $T_{2}$ is more than for $T_{1}$. Thus, $T_{1}>T_{2}$<br>(iii) The two curves intersect at dotted line, so the value of $\\dfrac{P V}{n T}$ at that point on the $y$- axis is same for all gases.',NULL,'Hard','Graph/Figure',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/29_38080_question_1.png'),
(38083,'physics',27,1382,3322,'According to Wien\'s displacement law:<div class="matching-question"></div>','[{"text": "Areas are in the ratio: $A_{1}: A_{2}: A_{3}=T_{1}^{2}: T_{2}^{2}: T_{3}^{2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Total area under the three graphs $A_{1}: A_{2}: A_{3}$ are equal", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Areas are in the ratio: $A_{1}: A_{2}: A_{3}=T_{1}^{4}: T_{2}^{4}: T_{3}^{4}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$T_{3}>T_{2}>T_{1}$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'$A \\propto T^{4}$ by stefan\'s Boltzmann law not by Wien\'s displacement law.<br>As $\\lambda_{m} \\alpha \\dfrac{1}{T} \\Rightarrow T_{3}>T_{2}>T_{1}$',NULL,'Hard','Graph/Figure',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/27_38083_question_1.png'),
(38084,'physics',29,1300,3146,'A fixed amount of nitrogen gas (1 mole) is taken and is subjected to pressure and temperature variation. The experiment is performed at high pressure as well as high temperatures. The results obtained are shown in the figures. The correct variation of \\[PV/RT{\\rm{ }}with{\\rm{ }}P{\\rm{ }}\\] will be exhibited by<div class="matching-question"></div>','[{"text": "4", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "3", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "2", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "1", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'At high pressure intermoleculer attraction among molecules increase and hence the gas deviates from ideal behaviour. Path (1) is not possible as it represents ideal gas behaviour. Path(4) is also not possible as it deviates before the pressure increases to a higher value. Path (2) is more possible than path (3) since in path (2) the gas deviates slowly from ideal behaviour as pressure increases.',NULL,'Hard','Graph/Figure',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/29_38084_question_1.png'),
(38087,'physics',27,1382,3323,'Which of the following graphs correctly represents the relation between \\[\\ln E\\] and \\[\\ln T\\] where \\[E\\] is the amount of radition emitted per unit time from unit area of a body and \\[T\\] is the absolute temperature<div class="matching-question"></div>','[{"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'According to Stefan\'s law $E=\\sigma T^{4}$<br>$\\Rightarrow \\ln E=\\ln \\sigma+4 \\ln T \\Rightarrow \\ln E=4 \\ln T+\\ln \\sigma$<br>The graph between $\\ln E$ and $\\ln T$ will be straight line, having positive slope $(m = 4)$ and intercept on $\\ln E$ axis is equal to $\\ln \\sigma$',NULL,'Hard','Graph/Figure',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38088,'physics',29,1301,3149,'For an ideal gas a graph is drawn between number of molecules at a particular speed \\(\\left(\\dfrac{d N}{d v}\\right)\\) and speed of the molecules \\((v)\\). Predict the correct option.<div class="matching-question"></div>','[{"text": "Point A represents the most probable speed $\\\\left(v_{m p}\\\\right)$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "The area under the graph represents total number of molecules", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Number of molecules having speed less than $v_{m p}$ is less than the number of molecules having speeds more than $v_{m p}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "All the above", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'The maximum value of $\\dfrac{d N}{d v}$ represents most probable speed of gas particles. Area of the graph is $A=\\int \\dfrac{d N}{d v} d v=$ Total no of particles.<br>It is always true that area of the graph on the right side of $v_{m p}$ is more than the area on the left side of $v_{m p}$.',NULL,'Hard','Graph/Figure',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/29_38088_question_1.png'),
(38090,'physics',28,1372,3294,'A cylinder containing an ideal gas and closed by a movable piston is submerged in an ice water mixture. The piston is quickly pushed down from position (1) to position (2) (process \\[AB\\]). The piston is held at position (2) until the gas is again at \\(0^{\\circ} C\\) (process \\[BC\\] ). The position is slowly raised back to position (1) process \\[(CA)\\]. Which of the following \\[P - V\\] diagrams will correctly represent the processes \\[AB,BC\\] and \\[CA\\] and the cycle \\[ABCA\\]?<div class="matching-question"></div>','[{"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'The process $AB$ is a rapid compression and hence it can be treated as adiabatic compression. The process $BC$ is isochoric and the process $CA$ is isothermal expansion.',NULL,'Hard','Graph/Figure',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/28_38090_question_1.png'),
(48980,'biology',80,473,953,'The electrons in the reaction centre of PS-I are','[{"text": "Excited simultaneously with PS-II", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Excited simultaneously with P680", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Excited simultaneously with P700", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both 1 and 2", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Electrons in PS-I/P700 are excited simultaneously with PS-II/P680',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38092,'physics',28,1372,3296,'A graph is drawn between coefficient of volume expansion of the gas \\(\\alpha\\) and temperature \\[T\\] of a gas having \\[n = 2\\] and \\(\\gamma=1.5\\). Given that \\[VT = K\\] where \\[K\\] is a constant. Find the area of the graph between \\(|\\alpha|\\) and \\[T\\], w.r.to \\[T\\]- axis from \\(\\dfrac{T_{\\mathrm{o}}}{3}\\) to \\(T_{\\text {o }}\\)<div class="matching-question"></div>','[{"text": "$\\\\ln \\\\,(1/3)$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\ln \\\\,(2)$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\ln \\\\,(6)$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\ln \\\\,(3)$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Given that $\\mathrm{VT}=\\mathrm{K}$ on differentiating the equation<br>$V d T+T d V=0 \\Rightarrow V d T=-T d V$<br>$\\dfrac{d V}{d T}=\\dfrac{-V}{T} \\Rightarrow \\alpha=\\dfrac{1}{V} \\dfrac{d V}{d T}=\\dfrac{-1}{T}$<br>Area $=\\int|\\alpha| d t=\\int_{T_{0} / 3}^{T_{0}}\\left(\\dfrac{1}{T}\\right) d T=\\ln (3)$',NULL,'Hard','Graph/Figure',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/28_38092_question_1.png'),
(38094,'physics',29,1301,3148,'In the isothermal expansion of  $10\\,\\,g$ of gas from volume $V$ to $2 V$  the work done by the gas is $575 \\,\\,J$. What is the root mean square speed of the molecules of the gas at that temperature?','[{"text": "$398\\\\,\\\\,m/s$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$520\\\\,\\\\,m/s$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$499 \\\\,\\\\,m/s$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$532 \\\\,\\\\,m/s$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'In isothermal process temperature remains constant.<br>${v_{rms}} = \\sqrt {\\frac{{3RT}}{M}}  = \\sqrt {\\frac{{3PV}}{{Mn}}}  = \\sqrt {\\frac{{3PV}}{m}} \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,(1)$<br>Isothermal work done is<br>$W=n R T \\ln \\dfrac{V_{2}}{V_{1}}=P V \\ln 2$<br>Given that $W=575 J=P V \\ln 2$<br>$ \\Rightarrow \\quad PV = \\frac{{575}}{{\\ln (2)}}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,(2)$<br>From eq\'s (1) & (2) we get<br>${v_{rms}} = \\sqrt {\\frac{{3 \\times 575}}{{m(\\ln 2)}}}  = \\sqrt {\\frac{{3 \\times 575}}{{{{10}^{ - 2}} \\times 0.693}}}  = 499\\,m/s$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',2013,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38097,'physics',28,1372,3298,'On a \\[T - P\\] diagram, two moles of ideal gas perform process \\[AB\\] and \\[CD\\]. If the work done by the gas in the process \\[AB\\] is two times the work done in the process \\[CD\\] then what is the value of \\(T_{1} / T_{2}\\) ?<div class="matching-question"></div>','[{"text": "1", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1 / 2$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "4", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "2", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Let slope of line $AC$ is $m_{1} \\&$ that of line $DB$ is $m_{2}$, then slope $(m)=\\dfrac{T \\text { coordinate }}{P \\text { coordinate }}$<br>$\\Rightarrow P$-coordinate $=\\dfrac{T-\\text { coordinate }}{m}$ $P_{A}=\\dfrac{T_{1}}{m_{1}}, P_{B}=\\dfrac{T_{1}}{m_{2}}, P_{C}=\\dfrac{T_{2}}{m_{1}} \\& P_{D}=\\dfrac{T_{2}}{m_{2}}$<br><img src=\'/img/data/physics/28_38097_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />  <br>Given that ${W_{AB}} = 2{W_{CD}}$<br>$nR{T_1}\\ln \\frac{{{P_A}}}{{{P_B}}} = 2nR{T_2}\\ln \\frac{{{P_C}}}{{{P_D}}}$<br>$nR{T_1}\\ln \\frac{{{m_2}}}{{{m_1}}} = 2nR{T_2}\\ln \\frac{{{m_2}}}{{{m_1}}}\\frac{{{T_1}}}{{{T_2}}} = 2$',NULL,'Hard','Graph/Figure',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/28_38097_question_1.png'),
(38098,'physics',29,1305,3155,'Modern vaccum pumps can evacuate a vessel down to a pressure of $4.0 \\times {10^{ - 15}} atm$. At room temperature (300 K). Taking $R = 8.0\\,J{\\rm{ }}{K^{ - 1}} mol{e^{ - 1}}$, $1 atm = {10^5} Pa$  and ${N_{Avogadro{\\rm{ }}}} = 6 \\times {10^{23}} mol{e^{ - 1}}$, the mean distance between molecules of gas in an evacuated vessel will be of the order of:','[{"text": "$0.2\\\\,\\\\,\\\\mu m$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$0.2 \\\\,\\\\,mm$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$0.2\\\\,\\\\,cm$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$0.2 \\\\,\\\\,nm$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'From the given information we calculate the average volume occupied by each molecule (not the volume of molecule). The average volume occupied or share of each molecule is equal to the ratio of total volume divides by total number of molecules. This volume can be approximated as a cube of side length $(\\lambda)$. The cube root of the average volume per molecule is equal to $(\\lambda)$. Here the value $(\\lambda)$ is also called as mean free path and it is equal to average distance between two molecules of a gas as shown in the figure.<br><img src=\'/img/data/physics/29_38098_explanation_1.jpg\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' /> <br>Ideal gas equation is<br>$P V=n R T=\\dfrac{N}{N_{A}} R T$<br>Given that<br>$R = 8\\frac{J}{{{\\rm{ }}K.mole{\\rm{ }}}},T = 300 K$<br>${N_A} = 6 \\times {10^{23}} mol{e^{ - 1}}$<br>$P = 4.0 \\times {10^{ - 15}} \\times {10^5}\\,N/{m^2}$<br>$\\frac{V}{N} = \\frac{{RT}}{{P{N_A}}} = \\frac{{8 \\times 300}}{{4.0 \\times {{10}^{ - 15}} \\times {{10}^5} \\times 6 \\times {{10}^{23}}}}$<br>${\\lambda ^3} = \\frac{V}{N} = {10^{ - 11}} {m^3}$<br>$\\lambda  = 2.15 \\times {10^{ - 4}} = 0.215\\,\\,mm$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2014,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(46444,'physics',40,239,478,'An electron revolving in $n^{\\text {th }}$ Bohr orbit has magnetic moment $\\mu_{n}$. If $\\mu_{n} \\propto n^{x}$, the value of $x$ is','[{"text": "0", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "2", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "3", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "1", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'An electron revolving in $n^{\\text {th }}$ Bohr orbit has magnetic moment, $\\mu_{n}=\\dfrac{n e h}{4 \\pi m}$<br>So, $\\mu_{n} \\propto n^{1}$. On comparing, $x=1$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',2024,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38100,'physics',26,1137,2722,'A long cylinder of radius $R_{1}$ is displaced along its axis with a constant velocity $v_{0}$ inside a stationary co-axial cylinder of radius $R_{2}$ as shown in the figure. The space between the cylinders is filled with viscous liquid. Find the velocity of the liquid as a function of the distance $r$ from the axis of the cylinders.','[{"text": "$+v_{0} \\\\ln \\\\left(\\\\dfrac{R_{2}}{r}\\\\right)$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{v_{0}}{\\\\ln \\\\left(\\\\dfrac{R_{2}}{R_{1}}\\\\right)}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$v_{0} \\\\ln \\\\left(\\\\dfrac{R_{2}}{R_{1}}\\\\right)$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$v_{0} \\\\dfrac{\\\\ln \\\\left(R_{2} / r\\\\right)}{\\\\ln \\\\left(R_{2} / R_{1}\\\\right)}$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Let $v$ is the velocity of a spherical shell or radius $r$<br>The viscous force between two adjacent layers having radius $r$ is<br><img src=\'/img/data/physics/26_38100_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' /> <br>where $L$ is the length of the cylinder. $d v$ is the relative velocity between two layers which are separated by $d r$.<br>As we move towards right $v$ decreases and $r$ increases.<br>$dv = ( - )ve\\,\\& \\,dr = ( + )ve$<br>$f =  - 2\\pi rL\\eta \\frac{{dv}}{{dr}}$<br>$\\int_{{R_1}}^r {\\frac{{dr}}{r}}  = \\frac{{ - 2\\pi L\\eta }}{f}\\int_{{v_0}}^v d v$<br>$\\ln \\frac{r}{{{R_1}}} = \\frac{{2\\pi L\\eta \\left( {{v_0} - v} \\right)}}{f}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\left( 1 \\right)$<br>$\\int_{{R_1}}^{{R_2}} {\\frac{{dr}}{r}}  = \\frac{{ - 2\\pi L\\eta }}{f}\\int_{{v_0}}^0 d v$<br>$\\ln \\frac{{{R_2}}}{{{R_1}}} = \\frac{{2\\pi L\\eta {v_0}}}{f}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\left( 2 \\right)$<br>From the above two equations we get<br>$\\dfrac{\\ln \\left(r / R_{1}\\right)}{\\ln \\left(R_{2} / R_{1}\\right)}=\\left(\\dfrac{v_{0}-v}{v_{0}}\\right)$<br>$v=v_{0}\\left[1-\\dfrac{\\ln \\left(r / R_{1}\\right)}{\\ln \\left(R_{2} / R_{1}\\right)}\\right]=\\dfrac{v_{0} \\ln \\left(R_{2} / r\\right)}{\\ln \\left(R_{2} / R_{1}\\right)}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/26_38100_question_1.png'),
(38101,'physics',28,1377,3303,'One of the straight lines in the Fig. depicts the dependence of the work done \\(|W|\\) on the temperature variations \\(\\mathrm{T}\\) for an isobaric process. The two of following are the adiabatic curves for argon and nitrogen. The isobaric process which depicts both the gases is:<div class="matching-question"></div>','[{"text": "2", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "1", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "4", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "3", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'For adiabatic process, $\\Delta W_{A}=\\dfrac{n f}{2} R \\Delta T$ and for isobaric process $\\Delta W_{I}=n R \\Delta T$. As minimum value of $f=3$ so slope of the line is less for isobaric process. Line -4 represents isobaric. Line- 2 represents adiabatic $\\left(N_{2}\\right)$. Line -3 represents adiabatic $(A r)$',NULL,'Hard','Graph/Figure',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/28_38101_question_1.png'),
(38102,'physics',29,1301,3148,'At room temperature a diatomic gas is found to have an r.m.s speed of $1930 \\,\\,m{s^{ - 1}}$. The gas is','[{"text": "${H_2}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$C{l_2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${O_2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${F_2}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'$\\because c = \\sqrt {\\frac{{3RT}}{M}} $<br>${(1930)^2} = \\frac{{3 \\times 8.314 \\times 300}}{M}$<br>$M = \\frac{{3 \\times 8.314 \\times 300}}{{1930 \\times 1930}} \\approx 2 g$<br>The gas is ${H_2}$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',2014,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38104,'physics',28,1372,3294,'A certain amount of gas is taken through a cyclic process (\\[A{\\rm{ }}B{\\rm{ }}C{\\rm{ }}D{\\rm{ }}A\\]) that has two isobars, one isochore and one isothermal. The cycle can be represented on a \\[P - V\\] indicator diagram as:<div class="matching-question"></div>','[{"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'$P - V$ indicator diagram for isobaric<br><img src=\'/img/data/physics/28_38104_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />  <br>$\\mathrm{P}-\\mathrm{V}$ indicator diagram for isochoric process<br><img src=\'/img/data/physics/28_38104_explanation_2.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />  <br>$\\mathrm{P}-\\mathrm{V}$ indicator diagram for isothermal process<br><img src=\'/img/data/physics/28_38104_explanation_3.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />  <br>So the correct option is (3)',NULL,'Hard','Graph/Figure',2013,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38105,'physics',26,1137,2722,'A fluid with viscosity $\\eta$ fills the space between two along co-axial cylinders of radii $R_{1}$ and $R_{2}$. The inner cylinder is stationary while the outer one is rotated with a constant angular velocity $\\omega_{2}$. The fluid flow is laminar. Taking into account that the friction force acting on a unit area of cylindrical surface radius $r$ is defined by the formula $\\sigma=\\eta r\\left(\\dfrac{d \\omega}{d r}\\right)$, find the angular velocity of rotating fluid as a function of radius $r$.','[{"text": "$\\\\omega_{2}\\\\left[\\\\dfrac{1}{R_{1}^{2}}-\\\\dfrac{1}{r^{2}}\\\\right] \\\\dfrac{R_{1}^{2} R_{2}^{2}}{R_{2}^{2}-R_{1}^{2}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{\\\\omega_{2} R_{1} R_{2}}{R_{2}-R_{1}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{\\\\omega_{2} R_{1}^{2} R_{2}^{2}}{R_{2}^{2}-R_{1}^{2}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "None of these", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Consider a thin cylindrical shell of radius $r$. Let $L$ is the length of the cylinder. The viscous force present between two adjacent cylinders separated by $d r$ and having radius $r$ is<br>$f=\\eta 2 \\pi r L r \\dfrac{d \\omega}{d r}$<br>The torque $\\tau=\\eta 2 \\pi L r^{3} \\dfrac{d \\omega}{d r}$<br>$\\int_{{R_2}}^r {\\frac{{dr}}{{{r^3}}}}  = \\frac{{2\\pi \\eta L}}{\\tau }\\int_{{\\omega _2}}^\\omega  d \\omega \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\left( 1 \\right)$<br>$\\int_{{R_2}}^{{R_1}} {\\frac{1}{{{r^3}}}} dr = \\frac{{2\\pi \\eta L}}{\\tau }\\int_{{\\omega _2}}^0 d \\omega \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\left( 2 \\right)$<br>Dividing the two equations we get<br>$\\begin{aligned}& \\dfrac{\\dfrac{1}{r^{2}}-\\dfrac{1}{R_{2}^{2}}}{\\dfrac{1}{R_{1}^{2}}-\\dfrac{1}{R_{2}^{2}}}=\\dfrac{\\omega-\\omega_{2}}{-\\omega_{2}} \\\\& \\dfrac{\\omega}{\\omega_{2}}=1-\\dfrac{\\dfrac{1}{r^{2}}-\\dfrac{1}{R_{2}^{2}}}{\\dfrac{1}{R_{1}^{2}}-\\dfrac{1}{R_{2}^{2}}} \\\\& \\omega=\\omega_{2}\\left[\\dfrac{1}{R_{1}^{2}}-\\dfrac{1}{r^{2}}\\right] \\dfrac{R_{1}^{2} R_{2}^{2}}{R_{2}^{2}-R_{1}^{2}}\\end{aligned}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/26_38105_question_1.png'),
(38106,'physics',29,1301,3148,'A gas molecule of mass $M$  at the surface of the Earth has kinetic energy equivalent to $0^\\circ C$. If it were to go up straight without colliding with any other molecules, how high it would rise? Assume that the height attained is much less than radius of the earth. ( $k_{B}$ is Boltzmann constant).','[{"text": "0", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{273 k_{B}}{2 M g}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{546 k_{B}}{3 M g}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{819 k_{B}}{2 M g}$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Kinetic energy of each molecule,<br>$K . E=\\dfrac{3}{2} K_{B} T$<br>Given that $T = 273 K$<br>Height attained by the gas molecule, $h = ?$<br>$\\begin{aligned}& K \\cdot E=\\dfrac{3}{2} K_{B}(273)=\\dfrac{819 K_{B}}{2} \\\\& K \\cdot E=P \\cdot E \\\\& \\Rightarrow \\dfrac{819 K_{B}}{2}=M g h \\Rightarrow h=\\dfrac{819 K_{B}}{2 M g}\\end{aligned}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2014,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38107,'physics',27,1382,3323,'A black coloured solid sphere of radius $R$ and mass $M$ is inside a cavity with vaccum inside. The walls of the cavity are maintained at temperature $T_{0}$. The initial temperature of the sphere is $3 T_{0}$. If the specific heat of the material of the sphere varies as $\\alpha T^{3}$ per unit mass with the temperature $T$ of the sphere, where $\\alpha$ is a constant, then the time taken for the sphere to cool down to temperature $2 T_{0}$ will be ( $\\sigma$ is Stefan Boltzmann constant)','[{"text": "$\\\\frac{{M\\\\alpha }}{{4\\\\pi {R^2}\\\\sigma }}\\\\,\\\\ln \\\\,\\\\left( {\\\\frac{3}{2}} \\\\right)$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{M \\\\alpha}{4 \\\\pi R^{2} \\\\sigma} \\\\ln \\\\left(\\\\dfrac{16}{3}\\\\right)$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{M \\\\alpha}{16 \\\\pi R^{2} \\\\sigma} \\\\ln \\\\left(\\\\dfrac{16}{3}\\\\right)$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{M \\\\alpha}{16 \\\\pi R^{2} \\\\sigma} \\\\ln \\\\left(\\\\dfrac{3}{2}\\\\right)$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Let $T$ is the temperature of sphere at some arbitrary time. The rate of heat looses by the sphere is<br>$\\frac{{dQ}}{{dt}} = \\sigma A\\left( {{T^4} - T_0^4} \\right)$<br>$\\frac{{ - M\\left( {\\alpha {T^3}} \\right)dT}}{{dt}} = \\sigma A\\left( {{T^4} - T_0^4} \\right)$<br>$\\int {\\frac{{{T^3}dT}}{{\\left( {{T^4} - T_0^4} \\right)}}}  =  - \\int {\\frac{{\\sigma Adt}}{{M\\alpha }}} $<br>Let $x=T^{4}-T_{0}^{4}$<br>$dx = 4{T^3}dT$<br>$\\frac{1}{4}\\int_{3{T_0}}^{2{T_0}} {\\frac{1}{x}} dx =  - \\frac{{\\sigma A}}{{M\\alpha }}\\int_0^t d t$<br>$[\\ln (x)]_{3{T_0}}^{2{T_0}} = \\left[ {\\ln \\left( {{T^4} - T_0^4} \\right)} \\right]_{3{T_0}}^{2{T_0}} =  - \\frac{{4\\sigma At}}{{M\\alpha }}$<br>$\\ln \\left( {\\frac{{16T_0^4 - T_0^4}}{{81T_0^4 - T_0^4}}} \\right) =  - \\frac{{4\\sigma At}}{{M\\alpha }}$<br>$\\ln \\left( {\\frac{{15}}{{80}}} \\right) =  - \\frac{{4\\sigma At}}{{M\\alpha }}t =  - \\frac{{M\\alpha }}{{4\\sigma A}}$<br>$\\ln \\left( {\\frac{{16}}{3}} \\right) = \\frac{{M\\alpha }}{{16\\pi {R^2}\\sigma }}\\ln \\left( {\\frac{{16}}{3}} \\right)$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2014,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38108,'physics',28,1371,3283,'An ideal gas at atmospheric pressure is adiabatically compressed so that its density becomes 32 times of its initial value. If the final pressure of gas is 128 atmospheres, the value of \' $\\gamma$ \' of the gas is:','[{"text": "1.5", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "1.4", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "1.3", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "1.6", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Volume of the gas<br>$V=\\dfrac{m}{d}$<br>$P V^{\\gamma}=$ constant<br>$\\dfrac{P^{\\prime}}{P}=\\left(\\dfrac{V}{V^{\\prime}}\\right)^{\\gamma}=\\left(\\dfrac{d^{\\prime}}{d}\\right)^{\\gamma}$<br>$128=(32)^{\\gamma}$<br>$\\therefore \\gamma=\\dfrac{7}{5}=1.4$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2013,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38109,'physics',26,1137,2723,'A small steel sphere of mass $m$ is falling vertically through a viscous liquid with a constant speed $v$. Which row in the table correctly describes the changes with time in the kinetic energy and gravitational potential energy of the sphere?<br>Kinetic Energy<br>$\\begin{array}{*{20}{c}}\n{{\\rm{Kinetic}}\\,{\\rm{Energy}}}&{{\\rm{Gravitational}}\\,{\\rm{Potential}}\\,{\\rm{Energy}}}\n\\end{array}$','[{"text": "$\\\\begin{array}{*{20}{c}}\\n{{\\\\rm{Constant}}{\\\\mkern 1mu} \\\\,{\\\\rm{and}}\\\\,{\\\\mkern 1mu} {\\\\rm{equal}}}&{{\\\\rm{Decreases}}\\\\,{\\\\mkern 1mu} {\\\\rm{at}}{\\\\mkern 1mu} \\\\,{\\\\rm{a}}{\\\\mkern 1mu} \\\\,{\\\\rm{rate}}{\\\\mkern 1mu} \\\\,{\\\\rm{of}}}\\\\\\\\\\n{{\\\\rm{to}}\\\\,{\\\\mkern 1mu} \\\\frac{1}{2}m{v^2}}&{mgv}\\n\\\\end{array}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\begin{array}{*{20}{c}}\\n{{\\\\rm{Constant}}\\\\,{\\\\rm{and}}\\\\,{\\\\rm{equal}}}&{{\\\\rm{Decreases}}\\\\,{\\\\rm{at}}\\\\,{\\\\rm{a}}\\\\,{\\\\rm{rate}}\\\\,}\\\\\\\\\\n{{\\\\rm{to}}\\\\,\\\\frac{1}{2}m{v^2}}&{{\\\\rm{of}}\\\\,\\\\left( {mgv + \\\\frac{1}{2}m{v^2}} \\\\right)}\\n\\\\end{array}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\begin{array}{*{20}{c}}\\n{{\\\\rm{Increases}}\\\\,{\\\\mkern 1mu} {\\\\rm{at}}\\\\,{\\\\mkern 1mu} {\\\\rm{a}}\\\\,{\\\\mkern 1mu} {\\\\rm{rate}}}&{{\\\\rm{Decreases}}{\\\\mkern 1mu} \\\\,{\\\\rm{at}}{\\\\mkern 1mu} \\\\,{\\\\rm{a}}{\\\\mkern 1mu} \\\\,{\\\\rm{rate}}{\\\\mkern 1mu} }\\\\\\\\\\n{}&{{\\\\rm{of}}\\\\,{\\\\mkern 1mu} mgv\\\\,{\\\\mkern 1mu} {\\\\rm{of}}\\\\,{\\\\mkern 1mu} mgv}\\n\\\\end{array}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\begin{array}{*{20}{c}}\\n{{\\\\rm{Increases}}\\\\,{\\\\rm{at}}\\\\,{\\\\rm{a}}\\\\,{\\\\rm{rate}}}&{{\\\\rm{Decreases}}\\\\,{\\\\rm{at}}\\\\,{\\\\rm{a}}\\\\,{\\\\rm{rate}}\\\\,}\\\\\\\\\\n{}&{{\\\\rm{of}}\\\\,mgv\\\\,{\\\\rm{of}}\\\\,\\\\left( {\\\\frac{1}{2}m{v^2} + mgv} \\\\right)}\\n\\\\end{array}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'$\\Delta KE = 0,{\\mkern 1mu} {\\rm{since}}\\,v = $ constant<br>$KE = \\frac{1}{2}m{v^2}$<br>Mass is falling down so potential energy decrease at rate<br>$=m g\\left(\\dfrac{d h}{d t}\\right)=m g v \\quad[\\text { decreases }]$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38110,'physics',29,1303,3153,'Using equipartition of energy, the specific heat $(in{\\text{ }}J\\,k{g^{ - 1}} {K^{ - 1}})$ of aluminium at room temperature can be estimated to be (atomic weight of aluminium $=27$ )','[{"text": "410", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "25", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "1850", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "925", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'According to Dulong-Petit law many solids at room temperature $C_{V}=3 R$. For solids when heat is given their volume approximately remain constant.<br>$\\Delta U=\\Delta Q$<br>For on mole<br>Using equipartition of energy, we have<br>$3RT = MCT$<br>$C = \\frac{{3R}}{M} = \\frac{{3 \\times 8.314}}{{27 \\times {{10}^{ - 3}}}}$<br>$\\therefore C = 925\\,\\,J/kgK$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',2015,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38111,'physics',27,1382,3318,'Temperature difference of $120^\\circ C$ is maintained between the two ends of a uniform $\\operatorname{rod} A B$ of length $2 L$. Another bent $\\operatorname{rod} P Q$, of same crosssection as $A B$ and length $\\frac{{3\\,L}}{2}$, is connected across $A B$ (see figure). In steady state, temperature difference between $P$ and $Q$ will be close to','[{"text": "$35^\\\\circ C$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$45^\\\\circ C$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$60^\\\\circ C$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$75^\\\\circ C$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Assume $R$ be the thermal resistance of uniform rod of length $L$.<br><img src=\'/img/data/physics/27_38111_explanation_1.jpg\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' /> <br><br><img src=\'/img/data/physics/27_38111_explanation_2.jpg\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' /> <br>Heat current: $I=\\dfrac{\\Delta T}{R_{e q}}=\\dfrac{120}{8 R / 5}=\\dfrac{75}{R}$<br>${(\\Delta T)_{PQ}} = I\\left( {\\frac{{3R}}{5}} \\right) = \\frac{{75}}{R} \\times \\frac{{3R}}{5} = 45^\\circ C$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',2019,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/27_38111_question_1.png'),
(38112,'physics',28,1371,3288,'There are two identical chambers, completely thermally insulated from surroundings. Both chambers have a partition wall dividing the chambers in two compartments. Compartment 1 is filled with an ideal gas and compartment 3 is filled with a real gas. Compartments 2 and 4 are vacuum. A small hole (orifice) is made in the partition walls and the gases are allowed to expand in vacuum.<div class="matching-question"><div class="column-left">No change in the temperature of the gas takes place when ideal gas expands in vacuum. However, the temperature of real gas goes down (cooling) when it expands in vacuum.</div><div class="column-right">The internal energy of an ideal gas is only kinetic. The internal energy of a real gas is kinetic as well as potential.</div></div>','[{"text": "Statement-1 is false and statement-2 is true.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Statement-1 and statement-2 both are true. <br>Statement- 2 is the correct explanation of  Statement-1.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Statement-1 is true and statement-2 is false.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Statement-1 and  statement-2 both are true. <br> Statement 2 is not correct explanation of  Statement-1.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'In ideal gases the molecules are considered as point particles and for point particles, there is no internal excitation, no vibration and no rotation. For an ideal gas the internal energy can only be translational kinetic energy and for real gas both kinetic as well as potential energy. The internal energy of an ideal gas is a function of temperature only so when ideal gas undergoes free expansion $W=0, \\Delta Q=0$ (insulated chambers) $\\Rightarrow \\Delta U=0 \\quad$ so temperature of the ideal gas remain constant. Whereas internal energy of a real gas is a function of both temperature and pressure. When a real gas undergoes free expansion the gas gets cooled.',NULL,'Hard','MCQ type-2',2013,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/28_38112_question_1.png'),
(38113,'physics',26,1137,2723,'Spherical particles of pollen are shaken up in water and allowed to settle. The depth of water is $2 \\times {10^{ - 2}}\\,m$. What is the diameter of the largest particles remaining in suspension one hour later? Density of pollen $ = 1.8 \\times {10^3}kg{m^{ - 3}}$, viscosity of water $=1 \\times 10^{-2}$ poise and density of water $ = 1 \\times {10^3}\\,kg\\,{m^3}$.','[{"text": "$1.74\\\\,\\\\mu m$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2.4\\\\,\\\\mu m$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$0.8\\\\,\\\\mu m$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$3.54\\\\,\\\\mu m$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'After shaking different particles take different times to settle down. Here all the particles have to travel same distance. We assume that the particles reach terminal velocity immediately but they have different values because of different radii.<br>We know the terminal velocity $v=\\dfrac{s}{t}$<br>$\\therefore \\dfrac{s}{t}=\\dfrac{2}{9} \\dfrac{r^{2}(\\rho-\\sigma) g}{\\eta} \\Rightarrow r^{2}=\\dfrac{9 s}{2 t} \\dfrac{\\eta}{(\\rho-\\sigma) g}$<br>Given $s = 2 \\times {10^{ - 2}}\\,m,t = 3600\\,s$<br>$\\therefore \\quad \\eta  = 1 \\times {10^{ - 2}}{\\rm{poise }} = 1 \\times {10^{ - 3}}\\,kg\\,{m^{ - 1}}\\,{s^{ - 1}}$<br>Substituting given values, we get<br>${r^2} = \\frac{9}{2} \\times \\frac{{2 \\times {{10}^{ - 2}}}}{{3600}} \\times \\frac{{1 \\times {{10}^{ - 3}}}}{{\\left( {1.8 \\times {{10}^3} - 1 \\times {{10}^3}} \\right) \\times 10}}$<br>$\\therefore \\quad r = \\sqrt {\\frac{{100}}{{32}}}  \\times {10^{ - 6}}\\,m = 1.77 \\times {10^{ - 6}}\\,m$<br>Diameter $D=2 r=2 \\times 1.77 \\mu m=3.54 \\mu m$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38114,'physics',29,1301,3147,'In an ideal gas at temperature $T$, the average force that a molecule applies on the walls of a closed container depends on$T$  as  ${T^q}$. A good estimate for $q$ is:','[{"text": "$\\\\dfrac{1}{2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "2", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "1", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{1}{4}$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Pressure, $P=\\dfrac{1}{3} \\dfrac{m N}{V} v_{r m s}^{2}$<br>$P \\propto\\left(v_{r m s}\\right)^{2} \\propto T$<br>So, force $\\propto\\left(v_{r m s}\\right)^{2} \\propto T$<br>i.e., Value of $q = 1$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',2015,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38115,'physics',27,1382,3319,'Two metallic blocks $M_{1}$ and $M_{2}$ of same area of cross-section are connected to each other (as shown in figure). If the thermal conductivity of $M_{2}$ is $K$ then the thermal conductivity of $M_{1}$ will be : [Assume steady state heat conduction]','[{"text": "$10\\\\,K$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$8\\\\,K$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$12.5\\\\,K$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2\\\\,K$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'The rate of transfer of heat<br>$\\frac{{\\Delta Q}}{{\\Delta t}} = KA\\left( {\\frac{{\\Delta T}}{l}} \\right)$<br>$\\frac{{\\Delta {T_1}}}{{\\Delta {T_2}}} = \\frac{{{\\ell _1}}}{{{K_1}}} \\times \\frac{{{K_2}}}{{{l_2}}} = \\frac{{16}}{{{K_1}}} \\times \\frac{K}{8}$<br>$\\frac{{20}}{{80}} = \\frac{{16}}{{{K_1}}} \\times \\frac{K}{8}$<br>$ \\Rightarrow {K_1} = 8K$<br>Note: Here $K$ is not kelvin.',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',2022,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/27_38115_question_1.jpg'),
(38116,'physics',28,1369,3279,'Given that $1\\,g$ of water in liquid phase has volume $1\\,c{m^3}$ and vapour phase $1671\\,c{m^3}$ at atmospheric pressure and the latent heat of vaporization of water is $2256\\,J/g$; the change in the internal energy in joules for $1\\,g$ of water at $373\\,K$ when it changes from liquid phase to vapour phase at the same temperature is:','[{"text": "2256", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "167", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "2089", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "1", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'$\\Delta Q=\\Delta U+W$<br>$\\Delta U=\\Delta Q-W$$\\Delta U=m L-P\\left(V_{2}-V_{1}\\right)$<br>$ = 1g\\left( {2256\\frac{J}{g}} \\right) - {10^5} \\times (1671 - 1) \\times {10^{ - 6}}\\,{m^3}$<br>$ = 2089$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',2013,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38117,'physics',26,1137,2723,'A spherical solid ball of volume $V$ is made of a material of density $\\rho_{1}$. It is falling through a liquid of density $\\rho_{2}\\left(\\rho_{2} < \\rho_{1}\\right)$. Assume that the liquid applies a viscous force on the ball that is proportional to the square of its speed $v$, i.e., $F_{\\text {viscous }}=-k v^{2}(k>0)$. The terminal speed of the ball is','[{"text": "$\\\\sqrt {\\\\frac{{{V_g}\\\\left( {{\\\\rho _1} - {\\\\rho _2}} \\\\right)}}{k}} $", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{{Vg{\\\\rho _1}}}{k}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\sqrt {\\\\frac{{Vg{\\\\rho _1}}}{k}} $", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{{Vg\\\\left( {{\\\\rho _1} - {\\\\rho _2}} \\\\right)}}{k}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'The condition for terminal speed $\\left(v_{t}\\right)$ is<br>Weight $=$ Buoyant force + Viscous force<br>$\\therefore \\quad v \\rho_{1} g=v \\rho_{2} g+k v_{t}^{2}$<br>$\\therefore v_{t}=\\sqrt{\\dfrac{\\operatorname{Vg}\\left(\\rho_{1}-\\rho_{2}\\right)}{k}}$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38118,'physics',29,1303,3152,'An ideal gas has molecules with 5 degrees of freedom. The ratio of specific heats at constant pressure $\\left(C_{p}\\right)$ and at constant volume $\\left(C_{V}\\right)$ is:','[{"text": "6", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{7}{2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{5}{2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{7}{5}$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'The ratio of specific heats at constant pressure $\\left(C_{P}\\right)$ and constant volume $\\left(C_{V}\\right)$<br>$\\dfrac{C_{P}}{C_{V}}=\\gamma=\\left(1+\\dfrac{2}{F}\\right)$<br>where $F$ is degree of freedom<br>$\\dfrac{C_{P}}{C_{V}}=\\left(1+\\dfrac{2}{5}\\right)=\\dfrac{7}{5}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2017,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38120,'physics',28,1371,3283,'During an adiabatic compression, $830\\,J$ of work is done on $2{\\rm{ }}moles$ of a diatomic ideal gas to reduced its volume by $50\\,\\% $. The change in its temperature is nearly : $\\left( {R = 8.3\\,J{K^{ - 1}}\\,mo{l^{ - 1}}} \\right)$','[{"text": "$40\\\\,K$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$33\\\\,K$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$20\\\\,K$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$14\\\\,K$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Work done $W = 830\\,J$<br>No.of moles of gas, $n=2$<br>For diatomic gas $\\gamma=1.4$<br>Work done during an adiabatic change<br>$W = \\frac{{nR\\left( {{T_1} - {T_2}} \\right)}}{{\\gamma  - 1}}$<br>$ \\Rightarrow 830 = \\frac{{2 \\times 8.3(\\Delta T)}}{{1.4 - 1}} = \\frac{{2 \\times 8.3(\\Delta T)}}{{0.4}}$<br>$ \\Rightarrow \\Delta T = \\frac{{830 \\times 0.4}}{{2 \\times 8.3}} = 20\\,K$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2014,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38121,'physics',26,1137,2724,'A tube of length $l$ and radius $R$ carries a steady flow of fluid whose density is $\\rho$ and viscosity $\\eta$. The fluid flow velocity depends on the distance $r$ from the axis of the tube as $v=v_{0}\\left(1-r^{2} / R^{2}\\right)$. Find the mass of the fluid flowing across the section of the tube per unit time.','[{"text": "$\\\\frac{{\\\\pi \\\\rho {v_0}{R^3}}}{3}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2\\\\pi \\\\rho {v_0}{R^2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{{\\\\pi \\\\rho {v_0}{R^2}}}{2}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{{\\\\pi \\\\rho {v_0}{R^3}}}{4}$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Consider the cross-section view of the tube.<br><img src=\'/img/data/physics/26_38121_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' /> <br>The small rate of flow of liquid across the circular of radius $r$ and width $d r$ is<br>$d Q=\\rho(d A) v$<br>$\\begin{aligned}& d Q=\\rho(2 \\pi r) d r v=2 \\pi \\rho v_{0}\\left(r-\\dfrac{r^{3}}{R^{2}}\\right) d r \\\\& Q=2 \\pi \\rho v_{0}\\left[\\int_{0}^{R} r d r-\\dfrac{1}{R^{2}} \\int_{0}^{R} r^{3} d r\\right] \\\\& =2 \\pi \\rho v_{0}\\left[\\dfrac{R^{2}}{2}-\\dfrac{1}{R^{2}} \\dfrac{R^{4}}{4}\\right]=\\dfrac{\\pi \\rho v_{0} R^{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38122,'physics',29,1301,3147,'$N$  moles of a diatomic gas in a cylinder are at a temperature $T$. Heat is supplied to the cylinder such that temperature remains constant but $n$ moles of the diatomic gas get converted into monoatomic gas. What is the change in the total kinetic energy of the gas?','[{"text": "$\\\\frac{1}{2}\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,nRT$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "0", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{3}{2}\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,nRT$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{5}{2}\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,nRT$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Energy associated with $N$ moles of diatomic gas,<br>$U_{i}=N \\dfrac{5}{2} R T$<br>Energy associated with $\\mathrm{n}$ moles of monoatomic gas<br>$=n \\dfrac{3}{2} R T$<br>Total energy when $\\mathrm{n}$ moles of diatiomic gas converted into monoatomic<br>$\\begin{aligned}\\left(U_{f}\\right)= & 2 n \\dfrac{3}{2} R T+(N-n) \\dfrac{5}{2} R T \\\\= & \\dfrac{1}{2} n R T+\\dfrac{5}{2} N R T\\end{aligned}$<br>Change in total kinetic energy of the gas<br>$\\Delta U=\\dfrac{1}{2} n R T$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',2017,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38124,'physics',28,1371,3289,'A gas is compressed from a volume of $2 m^{3}$ to a volume of $1\\,{m^3}$ at a constant pressure of $100\\,N/{m^2}$. Then it is heated at constant volume by supplying $150\\,J$ of energy. As a result, the internal energy of the gas:','[{"text": "Increases by $250\\\\,J$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Decreases by $250\\\\,J$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Increases by $50\\\\,J$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Decreases by $50\\\\,J$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'From $1^{s t}$ law of thermodynamics<br>$\\Delta Q=\\Delta U+W \\Rightarrow \\Delta Q=\\Delta U+P \\Delta V$<br>$150=\\Delta U+100(1-2)$<br>$\\therefore \\Delta U=150+100=250 J$<br>Thus the internal energy of the gas increases by $250\\,J$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',2014,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38125,'physics',29,1301,3148,'A mixture of 2 moles of helium gas $(atomic\\,{\\mkern 1mu} mass = 4\\,\\,u)$, and 1 mole of argon gas $(atomic\\,{\\mkern 1mu} mass = 40\\,\\,u)$  is kept at $300 K$ in a container. The ratio of their rms speeds $\\left[\\dfrac{v_{\\mathrm{mm}}(\\text { helium })}{v_{\\mathrm{rms}}(\\operatorname{argon})}\\right]$ is close to','[{"text": "2.24", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "0.32", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "0.45", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "3.16", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'${v_{ms}} = \\sqrt {\\frac{{3RT}}{m}} $<br>$\\dfrac{\\left(v_{\\mathrm{rms}}\\right)_{\\mathrm{He}}}{\\left(v_{\\mathrm{rms}}\\right)_{\\mathrm{Ar}}}=\\sqrt{\\dfrac{40}{4}}=\\sqrt{10}=3.16$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2019,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38126,'physics',26,1137,2722,'Figure shows a long solid cylinder of radius $R$ inside a long hollow tube of inner radius $R^{\\prime}$. A viscous liquid of coefficient of viscosity $\\eta$ is filled in the gap between the cylinder and the hollow tube. If the solid cylinder is moved with velocity $v$ parallel to its length inside the fixed hollow tube, find the force required per unit length to be applied on the solid cylinder, assuming uniform velocity gradient in the liquid.','[{"text": "$\\\\eta \\\\left( {2\\\\pi R} \\\\right)\\\\left( {\\\\frac{v}{{R\' - R}}} \\\\right)$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\eta \\\\left( {2\\\\pi R\'} \\\\right)\\\\left( {\\\\frac{v}{{R\' - R}}} \\\\right)$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\eta \\\\left( {4\\\\pi R} \\\\right)\\\\left( {\\\\frac{v}{{R\' + R}}} \\\\right)$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\eta \\\\left( {4\\\\pi R\'} \\\\right)\\\\left( {\\\\frac{v}{{R\' + R}}} \\\\right)$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'The viscous force on the cylinder is<br>$\\begin{aligned}& F=-\\eta A \\dfrac{d v}{d x}=-(2 \\pi R h \\eta) \\dfrac{v-0}{R-R^{\\prime}} \\\\& \\Rightarrow \\dfrac{F}{h}=\\eta(2 \\pi R) \\dfrac{v}{R^{\\prime}-R}\\end{aligned}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/26_38126_question_1.jpg'),
(38128,'physics',28,1371,3291,'An ideal monoatomic gas in confined in a cylinder by a spring loaded piston of cross section $8.0 \\times {10^{ - 3}}\\,{m^2}$. Initially the gas is at $300\\,K$ and occupies a volume of $2.4 \\times {10^{ - 3}}\\,{m^3}$ and spring is in its relaxed state as shown in figure. The gas is heated by a small heater until the piston moves out slowly by $0.1\\,m$. The force constant of the spring is $8000\\,N/m$ and the atmospheric pressure is $1.0 \\times {10^5}\\,N/{m^2}$. The cylinder and the piston are thermally insulated. The piston and the spring are massless and there is no friction between the piston and the cylinder. The final temperature of the gas will be : (Neglect the heat loss through the lead wires of the heater. The heat capacity of the heater coil is also negligible):-','[{"text": "$300\\\\,K$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$800\\\\,K$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$500\\\\,K$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1000\\\\,K$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Area of the piston is $A = 8 \\times {10^{ - 3}}\\,{m^2}$<br>${T_1} = 300\\,K;{V_1} = 2.4 \\times {10^{ - 3}}\\,{m^3}\\,$<br>${V_2} = {V_1} + A\\Delta x$<br>$ = 2.4 \\times {10^{ - 3}} + 8 \\times {10^{ - 3}} \\times 0.1 = 3.2 \\times {10^{ - 3}}\\,{m^3}\\,$<br>$K = 8000\\,N/m;{T_2} = ?;{P_1} = {10^5}\\,N/{m^2}$<br>Initially the piston is in equilibrium. Let ${P_0}$ is the atmospheric pressure.<br>${P_0} = {P_1}$ (pressure of the gas)<br>After expansion again the piston is in equilibrium<br>${P_2} = {P_0} + \\frac{{Kx}}{A}$<br>${P_2} = {P_1} + \\frac{{Kx}}{{\\,A}} = {10^5} + \\frac{{8000 \\times 0.1}}{{8 \\times {{10}^{ - 3}}}} = 2 \\times {10^5}\\,N/{m^2}$<br>$\\frac{{{P_1}\\,{V_1}}}{{\\,{T_1}}} = \\frac{{{P_2}\\,{V_2}}}{{\\,{T_2}}}$<br>$\\frac{{{{10}^5} \\times 2.4 \\times {{10}^{ - 3}}}}{{300}} = \\frac{{2 \\times {{10}^5} \\times 3.2 \\times {{10}^{ - 3}}}}{{\\,{T_2}}}\\,$<br>${T_2} = \\frac{{3.2 \\times 300}}{{1.2}} = 800\\,K$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2014,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/28_38128_question_1.png'),
(38129,'physics',29,1305,3155,'An ideal gas is enclosed in a cylinder at pressure of $2 \\,atm$  and temperature, $300\\, K$. The mean time between two successive collisions is $6 \\times {10^{ - 8}} s$. If the pressure is doubled and temperature is increased to $500 \\,K$, the mean time between two successive collisions will be close to','[{"text": "$4 \\\\times 10^{-8} \\\\mathrm{~s}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$3 \\\\times 10^{-6} \\\\mathrm{~s}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$0.5 \\\\times 10^{-8} \\\\mathrm{~s}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2 \\\\times 10^{-7} \\\\mathrm{~s}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Mean time $(\\tau)$ between two successive collisions<br>$ \\Rightarrow \\tau  \\propto \\frac{1}{{\\sqrt T }}\\frac{T}{P} = \\frac{{\\sqrt T }}{P}\\therefore \\frac{{{\\tau _1}}}{{{\\tau _2}}} = \\frac{{{P_2}}}{{{P_1}}}\\sqrt {\\frac{{{T_1}}}{{\\,{T_2}}}} $<br>$ \\Rightarrow {\\tau _2} = \\frac{{6 \\times {{10}^{ - 8}}}}{2}\\sqrt {\\frac{{500}}{{300}}} $<br>$ = \\sqrt {15}  \\times {10^{ - 8}} \\approx 4 \\times {10^{ - 8}} s$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2019,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38144,'physics',28,1369,3279,'$200\\,g$ water is heated from $40^\\circ C$ to $60^\\circ C$. Ignoring the slight expansion of water, the change in its internal energy is close to (Given specific heat of water $ = 4184\\,J/kg\\,K):$','[{"text": "$167.4\\\\,\\\\,kJ$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$8.4\\\\,\\\\,kJ$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$4.2\\\\,\\\\,kJ$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$16.7\\\\,kJ$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Volume of water does not change, no work is done on or by the system $(W=0)$<br>According to first law of thermodynamics<br>$Q=\\Delta U+W$<br>For Isochoric process $Q=\\Delta U$<br>$\\Delta U = mc\\Delta T = 2 \\times 4184 \\times 20 = 16.7\\,k\\,J.$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2016,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38130,'physics',26,1137,2722,'In the figure shown, a thin disc of radius $R$ is is located on the surface of an oil filled container. The distance between the disc and the lower base of the container is $D$. The disc is driven at constant angular speed $\\omega$ about the axis passing through the centre of the disc. The viscosity of the oil is $\\eta$. Find the torque required to drive the disc.','[{"text": "$\\\\frac{{4\\\\pi \\\\eta \\\\omega {R^4}}}{D}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{{2\\\\pi \\\\eta \\\\omega {R^4}}}{D}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{{\\\\pi \\\\eta \\\\omega {R^4}}}{D}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{{\\\\pi \\\\eta \\\\omega {R^4}}}{{2D}}$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'The top view of the disc is shown in the figure. Consider a circular slice having radius $r$ and with $d r$. The linear velocity of any point on the slice is $r \\omega$.<br><img src=\'/img/data/physics/26_38130_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' /> <br>The small viscous force on the circular slice is<br>$d f=\\eta d A \\dfrac{v}{D}=\\eta(2 \\pi r d r) \\dfrac{r \\omega}{D}$<br>The torque<br>$\\begin{aligned}d \\tau=d f r=\\dfrac{2 \\pi \\eta \\omega r^{3} d r}{D} \\Rightarrow \\tau & =\\dfrac{2 \\pi \\eta \\omega}{D} \\int_{0}^{R} r^{3} d r \\\\& =\\dfrac{\\pi \\eta \\omega R^{4}}{2 D}\\end{aligned}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/26_38130_question_1.png'),
(38132,'physics',28,1371,3284,'The equation of state for a gas is given by<br>$P V=n R T+\\alpha V$, where $\\mathrm{n}$ is the number of moles and $\\alpha$ is a positive constant. The initial temperature and pressure of one mole of the gas contained in a cylinder are $T_{0}$ and $P_{0}$ respectively. The work done by the gas when its temperature doubles isobarically will be:','[{"text": "$\\\\dfrac{P_{0} T_{0} R}{P_{0}-\\\\alpha}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{P_{0} T_{0} R}{P_{0}+\\\\alpha}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${P_0}{T_0}R\\\\,In\\\\,2$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$P_{0} T_{0} R$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Given $PV = nRT + \\alpha V\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\left( 1 \\right)$<br>$\\Rightarrow(P-\\alpha) V=n R T$<br>$V=\\dfrac{n R T}{(P-\\alpha)}$<br>$d V=\\dfrac{n R d T}{(P-\\alpha)}$<br>Now work done is $\\int d W=\\int P d V$<br>From (i) $P d V=n R d T+\\alpha d V$ ( $P=$ constant $)$<br>$\\begin{aligned}& \\int d W=\\int n R d T+\\alpha d V \\\\& \\quad=\\int n R d T+\\dfrac{\\int \\alpha(n R d T)}{P-\\alpha} \\\\& =n R \\int_{T_{0}}^{2 T_{0}} d T+\\dfrac{\\alpha}{P_{0}-\\alpha} n R \\int_{T_{0}}^{2 T_{0}} d T\\left(P=P_{0}=\\text { constant }\\right) \\\\& =n R T_{0}+\\dfrac{\\alpha}{P_{0}-\\alpha} \\cdot n R T_{0}=\\dfrac{n R T_{0} P_{0}}{\\left(P_{0}-\\alpha\\right)} \\\\& \\therefore \\quad W=\\dfrac{R T_{0} P_{0}}{\\left(P_{0}-\\alpha\\right)}(n=1)\\end{aligned}$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',2014,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38133,'physics',29,1303,3152,'Two moles of an ideal gas with $\\dfrac{C_{P}}{C_{V}}=\\dfrac{5}{3}$ are mixed with 3 moles of another ideal gas with $\\dfrac{C_{P}}{C_{V}}=\\dfrac{4}{3}$. The value of $\\dfrac{C_{P}}{C_{V}}$ for the mixture is','[{"text": "1.50", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "1.42", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "1.47", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "1.45", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'${\\gamma _{\\max }} = \\frac{{{n_1}{C_{{P_1}}} + {n_2}{C_{{P_2}}}}}{{{n_1}{C_{{V_1}}} + {n_2}{C_{{V_2}}}}}$<br>${C_P} = \\left( {\\frac{\\gamma }{{\\gamma  - 1}}} \\right)R$<br>${C_V} = \\frac{R}{{\\gamma  - 1}}$<br>${\\gamma _{mix{\\text{ }}}} = \\frac{{{n_1}\\frac{{{\\gamma _1}}}{{{\\gamma _1} - 1}} + \\frac{{{n_2}{\\gamma _2}}}{{{\\gamma _2} - 1}}}}{{\\frac{{{n_1}}}{{{\\gamma _1} - 1}} + \\frac{{{n_2}}}{{{\\gamma _2} - 1}}}}$<br>${n_1} = 2,{\\gamma _1} = \\frac{5}{3},{n_2} = 3,{\\gamma _2} = \\frac{4}{3}$<br> We get<br>${\\gamma _{mix{\\text{ }}}} = 1.42$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2020,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38134,'physics',26,1137,2723,'A small iron sphere is dropped from a great height. It attains its terminal velocity after having fallen $32\\,m$. Then, it covers the rest of the path with terminal velocity only. The work done by air friction during the first $32\\,m$ of fall is $W_{1}$. The work done by air friction during the subsequent $32\\,m$ fall is $W_{2}$. Then','[{"text": "${W_1} < {W_2}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${W_1} > {W_2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${W_2} = 32{W_1}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${W_1} = {W_2}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'In both cases the sphere travels same distance. The viscous force in the first part gradually increases from zero and reaches maximum. In the second part the the maximum viscous force acts throughout the motion. So work done in second part is more than the work done in the first part.<br>$W_{2}>W_{1}$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38137,'physics',29,1305,3155,'In a vessel containing an ideal gas the pressure is $1.1 \\times {10^5} Pa$, temperature is $27^\\circ C$ & diameter of molecules is $0.8 \\,nm$. Find the mean free path of the molecules if Boltzman constant is $1.38 \\times {10^{ - 23}} J/K$','[{"text": "$13.2 \\\\,\\\\,nm$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$132 \\\\,\\\\,nm$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$32 \\\\,\\\\,nm$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1.32 \\\\,\\\\,nm$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'${\\lambda _{mean{\\text{ }}}} = \\frac{1}{{\\sqrt 2 \\pi {d^2}\\left( {\\frac{N}{V}} \\right)}}$<br>$and,{\\text{ }}\\quad PV = N{K_B}T$<br>$\\therefore {\\lambda _{mean{\\text{ }}}} = \\frac{{{K_B}T}}{{\\sqrt 2 \\pi {d^2}P}}$<br>$ = \\frac{{1.38 \\times {{10}^{ - 23}} \\times 300}}{{\\sqrt 2  \\times 3.14 \\times 0.64 \\times {{10}^{ - 18}} \\times 1.1 \\times {{10}^5}}}$<br>$ = 132 \\times {10^{ - 10}}m = 13.2\\,\\,nm$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',2021,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38140,'physics',28,1372,3295,'An ideal gas goes through a reversible cycle \\(\\mathrm{a} \\rightarrow b \\rightarrow c \\rightarrow d\\) has the V-T diagram shown below. Process \\(d \\rightarrow a\\) and \\(b \\rightarrow c\\) are adiabatic.<br> <br>The corresponding \\[P - V\\] diagram for the process is (all figures are schematic and not drawn to scale):<div class="matching-question"></div>','[{"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'In $VT$ graph<br>ab-process: Isobaric, volume and temperature increases.<br>bc-Process : Adiabatic, volume increases and pressure decreases.<br>cd-process : Isobaric, volume decreases.<br>da-process: Adiabatic, volume decreases and pressure increases.<br>The above processes correctly represented in $P - V$ digram (2).',NULL,'Hard','Graph/Figure',2015,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/28_38140_question_1.png'),
(38155,'physics',26,1137,2723,'In an experiment, a small steel ball falls through a liquid at a constant speed of  $10\\,cm/s$. If the steel ball is pulled upward with a force equal to twice its effective weight, how fast will it move upward?','[{"text": "$5\\\\,cm/s$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Zero", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$10\\\\,cm/s$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$20\\\\,cm/s$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'The forces exerted on the steel ball are shown in the figure.<br><img src=\'/img/data/physics/26_38155_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' /> <br>where $F_{g}-F_{b}$ is the effective force on the sphere.<br>$6\\pi \\eta rv = {F_g} - {F_b}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\left( 1 \\right)$<br>$6\\pi \\eta r{v^\\prime } + {F_g} - {F_b} = 2\\left( {{F_g} - {F_b}} \\right)\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\left( 2 \\right)$<br>From the above two equations<br>(put (1) in (2))<br>$ \\Rightarrow {v^\\prime } = v = 10\\,cm/s$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',2013,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38156,'physics',28,1371,3284,'The ratio of work done by an ideal monoatomic gas to the heat supplied to it in an isobaric process is:','[{"text": "$\\\\dfrac{2}{5}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{3}{2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{3}{5}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{2}{3}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Efficiency of heat engine is given by<br>$\\eta=\\dfrac{W}{Q}=\\dfrac{P \\Delta V}{n C_{P} \\Delta T}=\\dfrac{n R \\Delta T}{n C_{P} \\Delta T}=\\dfrac{R}{C_{P}}=\\dfrac{R}{\\dfrac{5 R}{2}}=\\dfrac{2}{5}$<br>For monoatomic gas $C_{P}=\\dfrac{5}{2} R$.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2016,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38159,'physics',26,1137,2722,'The velocity of water in a river is $18\\,km/hr$ near the surface. If the river is $5\\,m$ deep, find the shearing stress between the horizontal layers of water. The co-efficient of viscosity of water $=10^{-2}$ poise.','[{"text": "${10^{ - 1}}\\\\,N/{m^2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${10^{ - 2}}\\\\,N/{m^2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${10^{ - 3}}\\\\,N/{m^2}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${10^{ - 4}}\\\\,N/{m^2}$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'<br>$\\eta  = {10^{ - 2}}{\\rm{ }}poise{\\rm{ }} = {10^{ - 3}}\\frac{N}{{{m^2}}}\\,s$<br>$v = 18\\,km/h = \\frac{{18000}}{{3600}} = 5\\,m/s$<br>$l = 5\\,m$<br>Strain rate $=\\dfrac{v}{l}$<br>Coefficient of viscosity, $\\eta=\\dfrac{\\text { shearing stress }}{\\text { strain rate }}$<br>$\\therefore$ Shearing strees $=\\eta \\times$ strain rate<br>$ = {10^{ - 3}} \\times \\frac{5}{5} = {10^{ - 3}}N{m^{ - 2}}$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',2014,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38160,'physics',28,1372,3295,'For the \\[P - V\\] diagram given for an ideal gas, out of the following which one correctly represents the \\[T - P\\] diagram?<div class="matching-question"></div>','[{"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'From $P-V$ graph,<br>$P \\propto \\dfrac{1}{V}, P V=$ constant $\\Rightarrow \\mathrm{T}=$ constant<br>The correct graph is option (3)',NULL,'Hard','Graph/Figure',2017,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/28_38160_question_1.png'),
(38163,'physics',26,1137,2722,'Which of the following option correctly describes the variation of the speed \\[v\\] and acceleration \' \\(a\\) \' of a point mass falling vertically in a viscous medium that applies a force \\[F =  - kv\\], where \'\\[k\\] \' is constant, on the body ? (Graphs are schematic and not drawn to scale)<div class="matching-question"></div>','[{"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'The acceleration of the body is<br>$a = g - \\alpha v\\left( {\\alpha  = \\frac{K}{m}} \\right)$<br>$\\frac{{dv}}{{dt}} = g - \\alpha v$<br>$\\int_0^v {\\frac{{dv}}{{g - \\alpha v}}} $<br>$ = \\int_0^t {dt}  \\Rightarrow \\ln \\left( {\\frac{{g - \\alpha v}}{v}} \\right) =  - \\alpha t$<br>$v = {v_0}\\left( {1 - {e^{ - \\alpha t}}} \\right),\\quad a = \\frac{{dv}}{{dt}} = {v_0}\\alpha {e^{ - \\alpha t}}$<br>where $v_{0}=\\dfrac{g}{\\alpha}$',NULL,'Hard','Graph/Figure',2016,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38164,'physics',28,1372,3294,'An engine operates by taking \\(n\\) moles of an ideal gas through the cycle ABCDA shown in figure. The thermal efficiency of the engine is: (Take \\(C_{V}=1.5 R\\), where \\(\\mathrm{R}\\) is gas constant)<div class="matching-question"></div>','[{"text": "0.24", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "0.15", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "0.32", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "0.08", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'The net work done in the cycle is equal to the area enclosed in the graph $\\Rightarrow W_{\\text {cycle }}=P_{0} V_{0}$<br>$Path\\,AB$<br>$\\begin{aligned}\\Delta Q_{A B} & =\\Delta U+W \\\\& =\\dfrac{P_{2} V_{2}-P_{1} V_{1}}{\\gamma^{2}-1}+0=\\dfrac{P_{0} V_{0}}{\\dfrac{5}{3}-1}=\\dfrac{3 P_{0} V_{0}}{2} \\\\\\Delta Q_{B C} & =\\Delta U+W \\\\& =\\dfrac{4 P_{0} V_{0}-2 P_{0} V_{0}}{\\dfrac{5}{3}-1}+2 P_{0} V_{0}=5 P_{0} V_{0}\\end{aligned}$<br>In the paths $CD\\& DA,{\\mkern 1mu} \\,\\Delta Q$ is (-) ve $\\eta  = \\frac{{{W_{cycle{\\rm{ }}}}}}{{{Q_{absorbed{\\rm{ }}}}}} = \\frac{{{P_0}{V_0}}}{{\\frac{{3{P_0}{V_0}}}{2} + 5{P_0}{V_0}}} = \\frac{2}{{13}} = 0.15$',NULL,'Hard','Graph/Figure',2017,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/28_38164_question_1.png'),
(38167,'physics',26,1137,2725,'Two tubes of radii $r_{1}$ and $r_{2}$ and lengths $l_{1}$ and $l_{2}$, respectively, are connected in series and a liquid flows through each of them in stream line conditions. $P_{1}$ and $P_{2}$ are pressure differences across the two tubes. If $P_{2}$ is $4 P_{1}$ and $l_{2}$ is $\\dfrac{l_{1}}{4}$, then the radius $r_{2}$ will be equal to -','[{"text": "$4{r_1}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${r_1}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2{r_1}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{{{r_1}}}{2}$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'$Q=\\dfrac{\\pi}{8} \\dfrac{p r^{4}}{\\eta L}$<br>The rate of flow is same in both tubes<br>$\\begin{aligned}\\dfrac{p_{1} r_{1}^{4}}{L_{1}} & =\\dfrac{p_{2} r_{2}^{4}}{L_{2}} \\\\\\dfrac{p_{1} r_{1}^{4}}{L_{1}} & =\\dfrac{4 p_{1} r_{2}^{4}}{\\dfrac{L_{1}}{4}} \\Rightarrow r_{2}^{4}=\\dfrac{r_{1}^{4}}{16} \\\\r_{2} & =\\dfrac{r_{1}}{2}\\end{aligned}$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',2017,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38170,'physics',27,1381,3315,'$100\\,g$ of ice at $0^\\circ {\\rm{ }}C$ is mixed with $100\\,g$ of water at $100^\\circ {\\rm{ }}C$. The final temperature of the mixture is [Take ${L_f} = 3.36 \\times {10^5}\\,J\\,k{g^{ - 1}}$ and $\\left. {{S_w} = 4.2 \\times {{10}^3}\\,J\\,k{g^{ - 1}}{k^{ - 1}}} \\right]$','[{"text": "$50^\\\\circ {\\\\rm{ }}C$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1^\\\\circ {\\\\rm{ }}C$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$40^\\\\circ {\\\\rm{ }}C$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$10^\\\\circ {\\\\rm{ }}C$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'According to method of mixture:<br>$m_{\\text {Ice }} L+m_{\\text {Ice }} s_{w}(t-0)=m_{w} s_{w}(100-t)$<br>$100 \\times 80+100 \\times 1(t-0)=100 \\times 1(100-t)$<br>$\\Rightarrow 80+t=100-t$<br>$ \\Rightarrow t = {\\rm{ }}10^\\circ {\\rm{ }}C$.<br>Correct option is (4).',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2023,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38171,'physics',26,1138,2727,'A container of width $2 a$ is filled with a liquid. A thin wire of mass per unit length $\\lambda$ is gently placed over the liquid surface in the middle of surface as shown. As a result the liquid surface is depressed by a distance $y(y <  < a)$. Determine the surface tension of the liquid.','[{"text": "$\\\\frac{{\\\\lambda ag}}{y}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{{\\\\lambda ag}}{{2y}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{{\\\\lambda ag}}{{3y}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{{\\\\lambda ag}}{{4y}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'The free-body diagram of wire is showing in figure.<br><img src=\'/img/data/physics/26_38171_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' /> <br>$\\sin \\theta \\approx \\tan \\theta=\\dfrac{y}{a}$<br>Let $l$ be the length of the wire. If $\\lambda$ is mass per unit length of wire, the weight of wire $\\lambda l g=2 T l \\sin \\theta$ (acting vertically downward)<br>$\\Rightarrow T=\\dfrac{\\lambda \\mathrm{g}}{2 \\sin \\theta}=\\dfrac{\\lambda a \\mathrm{~g}}{2 y}$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/26_38171_question_1.png'),
(38172,'physics',28,1370,3280,'Two moles of helium are mixed with $n$ moles of hydrogen. If $\\dfrac{C_{P}}{C_{V}}=\\dfrac{3}{2}$ for the mixture then the value of $n$ is','[{"text": "1", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "3", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "2", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$3 / 2$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'$\\left(\\dfrac{C_{P}}{C_{V}}\\right)_{\\text {mip }}=\\dfrac{n_{1} C_{P_{1}}+n_{2} C_{P_{2}}}{n_{1} C_{V_{1}}+n_{2} C_{V_{2}}}$<br>$n_{1}=2, n_{2}=n$<br>$C_{P_{1}}=\\dfrac{5}{2} R \\quad C_{P_{2}}=\\dfrac{7}{2} R$<br>$C_{V_{1}}=\\dfrac{3}{2} R \\quad C_{V_{2}}=\\dfrac{5}{2} R$<br>$\\left(\\dfrac{C_{P}}{C_{V}}\\right)_{\\text {mix }}=\\dfrac{3}{2}$<br>$\\dfrac{3}{2}=\\dfrac{10+7 n}{6+5 n}$<br>$\\Rightarrow n=2$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',2018,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38174,'physics',26,1138,2730,'Calculate the pressure inside a small air bubble of radius $0.01\\,mm$ situated at a depth of $h = 20\\,m$ below the free surface of liquid of density ${\\rho _1} = {10^3}\\,kg/{m^3},{\\rho _2} = 8000\\,kg/{m^3}$ and surface tension ${T_2} = 7.5 \\times {10^{ - 2}}\\,N/m$. The thickness of the first liquid is ${h_1} = 15\\,m\\,{\\rm{and}}\\,{h_2} = 25\\,m$.','[{"text": "${10^5}\\\\,Pa$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2 \\\\times {10^5}\\\\,Pa$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$6.6 \\\\times {10^5}Pa$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$12 \\\\times {10^5}Pa$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'In side the air bubble the pressure $=$ Pressure due to atmosphere + Pressure due to upper liquid layer + Pressure due to lower liquid layer + Pressure due to surface tension.<br>$ = {P_0} + {h_1}{\\rho _1}g + \\left( {h - {h_1}} \\right){\\rho _2}g + \\frac{{2T}}{r}$<br>$ = {10^5} + 15 \\times {10^3} \\times 10 + (20 - 15)8000 \\times 10\\,\\,\\,{\\mkern 1mu} $<br>${\\kern 1pt}  + \\frac{{2 \\times 7.5 \\times {{10}^{ - 2}}}}{{0.01 \\times {{10}^{ - 3}}}}$<br>$ = {10^5} + 1.5 \\times {10^5} + 4 \\times {10^5} + 15 \\times {10^3}$<br>$ = {10^5}(1 + 1.5 + 4 + 0.15) = 6.65 \\times {10^5}\\,Pa$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/26_38174_question_1.png'),
(38175,'physics',27,1380,3306,'A bimetallic strip consists of metals $X$ and $Y$. It is mounted rigidly at the base as shown. The metal $X$ has a higher coefficient of expansion compared to that for metal $Y$. When bimetallic strip is placed in a cold bath,','[{"text": "It will bend towards the right", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "It will bend towards the left", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "It will not bend but shrink", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "It will neither bend nor shrink", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'The metal $X$ has a higher coefficient of expansion compared to that for metal $Y$.  <img src=\'/img/data/physics/27_38175_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />  Bending takes place. As both $X$ and $Y$ are of same width, outer circumference is to be more than inner circumference.<br>Hence, the strip overall will bend towards the left.<br>So correct option is (2).',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2006,'AIIMS','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/27_38175_question_1.png'),
(38177,'physics',29,1301,3148,'The temperature of a gas is $-50^\\circ {\\rm{ }}C$. To what temeprature the gas should be heated so that the rms speed is increased by 3 times?','[{"text": "$3295^\\\\circ {\\\\rm{ }}C$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$3097\\\\,K$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$223\\\\,K$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$669^\\\\circ {\\\\rm{ }}C$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'$v_{r m s} \\propto \\sqrt{T}$<br>$\\dfrac{v_{1}}{v_{2}}=\\sqrt{\\dfrac{T_{1}}{T_{2}}}$<br>Let initial speed be $v$<br>As speed is increased by 3 times so final speed become $4 v$.<br>$\\dfrac{v}{4 v}=\\sqrt{\\dfrac{273-50}{T}}$<br>$\\Rightarrow \\sqrt{\\dfrac{223}{T}}=\\dfrac{1}{4}$<br>$\\Rightarrow T=16 \\times 223$<br>$ = 3568\\,K$<br>$ \\Rightarrow t = (3568 - 273)^\\circ C = 3295^\\circ C$.<br>Correct option is (1).',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2023,'NEET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38178,'physics',26,1138,2727,'Water is filled up to a height \\(h\\) in a beaker of radius \\(R\\) as shown in the figure. The density of water is \\(\\rho\\), the surface tension of water is \\(T\\) and the atmospheric pressure is \\(P_{0}\\). Consider a vertical section \\(A B C D\\) of the water column through a diameter of the beaker. The force of water on one side of this section by water on the other side of this section has magnitude<div class="matching-question"></div>','[{"text": "$\\\\left| {2{P_0}Rh + \\\\pi {R^2}\\\\rho gh - 2RT} \\\\right|$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\left| {2{P_0}Rh + R\\\\rho g{h^2} - 2RT} \\\\right|$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\left| {{P_0}\\\\pi {R^2} + R\\\\rho g{h^2} + 2RT} \\\\right|$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\left| {{P_0}\\\\pi {R^2} + R\\\\rho g{h^2} - 2RT} \\\\right|$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Net force $=$ Average pressure $\\times$ Area $-T \\times 2 R$<br>$ = \\left( {{P_0} + \\rho g\\frac{h}{2}} \\right)(2Rh) - T2R$<br>$ = \\left| {2{P_0}Rh + R\\rho g{h^2} - 2RT} \\right|$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/26_38178_question_1.png'),
(38179,'physics',27,1380,3307,'A clock with a metal pendulum beating seconds keeps correct time at $0^\\circ {\\rm{C}}$. If it loses $12.5\\,s$ a day at $25^\\circ C$, the coefficient of linear expansion of metal pendulum is','[{"text": "$\\\\frac{1}{{86400}}/^\\\\circ C$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{1}{{43200}}/^\\\\circ {\\\\rm{C}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{1}{{14400}}/^\\\\circ {\\\\rm{C}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{1}{{28800}}/^\\\\circ {\\\\rm{C}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Number of seconds lost in a day,<br>$\\Delta t=\\dfrac{1}{2} \\alpha \\Delta \\theta \\times 86400$<br>The coefficient of linear expansion of metal pendulum,<br>$\\alpha=\\dfrac{2 \\Delta t}{\\Delta \\theta \\times 86400}=\\dfrac{2 \\times 12.5}{25 \\times 86400}$<br>$ \\Rightarrow \\alpha  = \\frac{1}{{86400}}/^\\circ C$<br>So, correct option is (1).',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',2010,'AIIMS','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38180,'physics',28,1372,3294,'A gas can be taken from \\(A\\) to \\(B\\) via two different processes \\(A C B\\) and \\(A D B\\). When path \\(A C B\\) is used \\[60\\;J\\] of heat flows into the system and \\[30\\,J\\] of work is done by the system. If path \\(A D B\\) is used work done by the system is \\[10\\,J\\]. The heat flow into the system in path \\(A D B\\) is<div class="matching-question"></div>','[{"text": "$100\\\\,J$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$80\\\\,J$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$40\\\\,J$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$20\\\\,J$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'$\\Delta Q = \\Delta U + W$<br>$\\Delta U = \\Delta Q - W$<br>${(\\Delta U)_{ACB}} = {(\\Delta U)_{ADB}}$<br>$ \\Rightarrow 60 - 30 = \\Delta Q - 10$<br>$ \\Rightarrow \\Delta Q = 40\\,\\,J$',NULL,'Hard','Graph/Figure',2019,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/28_38180_question_1.png'),
(38181,'physics',29,1301,3147,'Pressure of ideal gas at constant volume is proportional to','[{"text": "total energy of the gas", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "average kinetic energy of the molecules", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "force between the molecules", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "average potential energy of the molecules", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'$P=\\left(\\dfrac{M}{3 V}\\right) v_{r m s}^{2}$<br>$\\Rightarrow P=\\dfrac{2}{3} E$<br>$\\Rightarrow P \\propto E$<br>Correct option is (2).',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2023,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38182,'physics',26,1138,2728,'Consider a liquid drop of radius $R$ and density $\\rho$. Now the drop is blown into $(N)$ large number of bubbles each having radius $R$ and surface thickness $\\dfrac{R}{1000}$. If $T$ is the surface tension of the liquid find the work done by the external agent to blow the bubbles.','[{"text": "$1000\\\\,T\\\\pi {R^2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$100\\\\,T\\\\pi {R^2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$8000\\\\,T\\\\pi {R^2}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$2000\\\\,T\\\\pi {R^2}$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'From conservation of volume of the liquid<br>$\\begin{aligned}& \\dfrac{4}{3} \\pi R^{3}=N\\left(4 \\pi R^{2}\\right) \\dfrac{R}{1000} \\\\& \\Rightarrow \\quad N=1000\\end{aligned}$<br>i.e., 1000 bubbles can be blown from the drop<br>$W = T(\\Delta A)$<br>${A_f} = 1000\\left( {2 \\times 4\\pi {R^2}} \\right)$<br>${A_i} = 4\\pi {R^2}$<br>$W = T(2000 - 1)4\\pi {R^2} \\approx 8000\\,T\\pi {R^2}$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/26_38182_question_1.png'),
(38183,'physics',27,1380,3312,'A non-conducting body floats in a liquid at $20^\\circ C$with $2 / 3$ of its volume immersed in the liquid. When liquid temperature is increased to $100^\\circ C,3{\\rm{/}}4$ of body\'s volume is immersed in the liquid. Then, the coefficient of real expansion of the liquid is (neglecting the expansion of container of the liquid)','[{"text": "$15.6 \\\\times {10^{ - 4}}\\\\,^\\\\circ {C^{ - 1}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$156 \\\\times {10^{ - 4}}\\\\,^\\\\circ {C^{ - 1}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1.56 \\\\times {10^{ - 4}}\\\\,^\\\\circ {C^{ - 1}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$0.156 \\\\times {10^{ - 4}}\\\\,^\\\\circ {C^{ - 1}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Coefficient of real expansion,<br>$\\gamma_{R}=\\dfrac{V_{2}-V_{1}}{V_{1}\\left(t_{2}-t_{1}\\right)}$<br>Here ${V_2} = \\frac{3}{4}{V_0},\\,{V_1} = \\frac{2}{3}{V_0}$<br>and$\\left( {{t_2} - {t_1}} \\right) = (100 - 20) = 80^\\circ C$<br>$\\therefore {\\gamma _R} = \\frac{{\\left( {\\frac{3}{4} - \\frac{2}{3}} \\right)}}{{\\frac{2}{3}(80)}} = \\frac{1}{{640}}$<br>$ = 15.6 \\times {10^{ - 4}}^\\circ {C^{ - 1}}$<br>So, correct option is (1).',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',2012,'AIIMS','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38184,'physics',29,1300,3146,'If pressure of $C{O_2}$ (real gas) in a container is given by $p=\\dfrac{R T}{2 V-b}-\\dfrac{9}{4 b^{2}}$, then mass of the gas in container is','[{"text": "$11\\\\,g$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$22\\\\,g$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$33\\\\,g$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$44\\\\,g$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'van der Waal\'s gas equation for $\\mu$ mole of real gas is<br>$\\left(p+\\dfrac{\\mu^{2} a}{V^{2}}\\right)(V-\\mu b)=\\mu R T$<br>$\\Rightarrow p=\\left(\\dfrac{\\mu R T}{V-\\mu b}\\right)-\\dfrac{\\mu^{2} a}{V^{2}}$<br>Given equation,<br>$p=\\left(\\dfrac{R T}{2 V-b}-\\dfrac{9}{4 b^{2}}\\right)$<br>On comparing the given equation with this standard equation, we get $\\mu=\\dfrac{1}{2}$<br>As, $\\mu  = \\frac{m}{M}$<br>where, $m=$ mass of gas and<br>$M=$ molecular weight of the gas.<br>$m = \\mu M = \\frac{1}{2} \\times 44 = 22\\,g$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',2010,'AIIMS','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38185,'physics',28,1371,3283,'A litre of dry air at STP expands adiabatically to volume of 3 litres. If $\\gamma=1.40$, the work done by air is $\\left(3^{1.4}=4.6555\\right)$ [Take air to be an ideal gas]','[{"text": "$100.8\\\\,J$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$48\\\\,J$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$90.5\\\\,J$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$60.7\\\\,J$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'$P_{i} V_{i}^{\\gamma}=P_{f} V_{f}^{\\gamma}$<br>${P_f}\\,\\, = \\frac{{{P_i}}}{{{3^\\gamma }}} = \\frac{{{{10}^5}}}{{{3^{1.4}}}}$<br>$W\\,\\, = \\frac{{{P_f}{V_f} - {P_i}{V_i}}}{{1 - \\gamma }}$<br>$\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, = \\frac{{\\frac{{{{10}^5}}}{{{3^{1.4}}}} \\times 3 \\times {{10}^{ - 3}} - {{10}^5} \\times {{10}^{ - 3}}}}{{1 - 1.4}}$<br>$W \\approx 90.5\\,J$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',2020,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38192,'physics',29,1300,3146,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> $N{H_3}$ is liquified more easily than $C{O_2}$.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Critical temperature of $N{H_3}$ is more than $C{O_2}$.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of  Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'A gas can be liquified by applying pressure only when it is cooled below critical temperature. Critical temperature of $N{H_3}$ is more than $C{O_2}$, i.e. ${T_{N{H_3}}} = 405\\,K$ and ${T_{C{O_2}}} = 304.1\\,K$.<br>Therefore, $N{H_3}$ is liquified more easily than $C{O_2}$. Hence, both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.',NULL,'Hard','Assertion and Reason',2019,'AIIMS','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(39836,'physics',32,484,993,'The capacity of a condenser is $4\\mu F$  and its  potential is 100 $V$. The energy released on  discharging it fully will be :-','[{"text": "$0.04J$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$0.02J$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$0.05J$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$0.06J$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'The energy stored in the capacitor will  be released completely.<br>$U = \\frac{1}{2}C{V^2} = \\frac{1}{2} \\times 4 \\times {10^{ - 6}} \\times {\\left( {{{10}^2}} \\right)^2} = 0.02J$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38186,'physics',27,1380,3311,'The coefficient of cubical expansion of mercury is $0.00018/^\\circ C$ and that of brass $0.00006/^\\circ C$. If a barometer having a brass scale were to read $74.5\\,cm$ at $30^\\circ C$, find the true barometric height at$0^\\circ C$. The scale is supposed to be correct at $15^\\circ C$.','[{"text": "$74.122\\\\,cm$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$79.152\\\\,cm$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$42.161\\\\,cm$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$142.39\\\\,cm$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'As, ${\\alpha _{brass{\\rm{ }}}} = \\frac{{\\gamma {\\,_{brass{\\rm{ }}}}}}{3} = \\frac{{0.00006}}{3}$<br>$ = 0.00002 = 2 \\times {10^{ - 5}}{\\rm{/}}{}^0C$<br>The brass scale is true at $15^\\circ C$, therefore at $30^\\circ C$, its graduations will increase in length and so observed reading will be less than actual reading at $30^{\\circ}$.<br>$\\therefore$ The change in reading,<br>$\\Delta l = l{\\alpha _{{\\rm{brass }}}}(\\Delta T)$<br>$ = 74.5 \\times 2 \\times {10^{ - 5}}(30 - 15)$<br>$ = 0.02235\\,cm$<br>$\\therefore \\quad$ Actual reading at $30^\\circ C$<br>${l_{30}} = {l_{{\\rm{observed }}}} + \\Delta l$<br>$ = 74.5 + 0.02235$<br>$ = 74.522\\,cm$<br>Assuming area of cross - section A to be constant, we have<br>$V_{0} \\rho_{0}=V_{30} \\rho_{30}$<br>$\\Rightarrow A h_{0} \\rho_{0}=A h_{30} \\rho_{30}$<br>$\\Rightarrow$ true height at $0^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C}$ will be ;<br>$h_{0}=h_{30} \\dfrac{\\rho_{30}}{\\rho_{0}}=\\dfrac{h_{30}}{\\left(1+\\gamma_{H g} \\Delta T\\right)}$<br>Here, $V_{2}=\\dfrac{3}{4} V_{0}, V_{1}=\\dfrac{2}{3} V_{0}$<br>$ \\Rightarrow {h_0} = \\frac{{74.522}}{{1 + 0.00018 \\times 30}} = \\frac{{74.522}}{{1.0054}}$<br>$ = 74.122\\,cm$<br>So, correct option is (1).',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2017,'AIIMS','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38187,'physics',26,1138,2730,'A soap bubble is being blown on a tube of radius $1\\,cm$. The surface tension of the soap solution is $0.05\\,N/m$ and the bubble makes an angle of $60^{\\circ}$ with the tube as shown. The excess of pressure over the atmospheric pressure in the tube is:','[{"text": "$5\\\\,Pa$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1\\\\,Pa$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$10\\\\,Pa$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$20\\\\,Pa$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'The radius of the bubble is given by<br><img src=\'/img/data/physics/26_38187_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' /> <br>From the figure $R \\cos 60^{\\circ}=r$<br>The radius of the bubble is $R = 2r = 2\\,cm$<br>The pressure difference is<br>$\\Delta P=\\dfrac{4 T}{R}=\\dfrac{4 \\times 0.05}{2 \\times 10^{-2}}=10 P a$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/26_38187_question_1.png'),
(38188,'physics',29,1300,3145,'An ideal gas initially at pressure $1{\\rm{ }}bar$ is being compressed from $30\\,{m^3}$ to $10\\,{m^3}$ volume and its temperature decreases from $320\\,K$ to $280\\,K$, then find the value of final pressure of the gas.','[{"text": "$2.625\\\\,bar$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$3.4\\\\,bar$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1.325\\\\,bar$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$4.5\\\\,bar$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Given, initial pressure of ideal gas,<br>${p_1} = 1\\,bar = 1N{m^{ - 2}}$<br>Initial volume, ${V_1} = 30\\,{m^3}$<br>Final volume, ${V_2} = 10\\,{m^3}$<br>Initial temperature, ${T_1} = 320\\,K$<br>Final temperature, ${T_2} = 280\\,K$<br>Final pressure of gas, $p_{2}=$?<br>By ideal gas equation,<br>$\\dfrac{p_{1} V_{1}}{T_{1}}=\\dfrac{p_{2} V_{2}}{T_{2}}$<br>$ \\Rightarrow \\frac{{1 \\times 30}}{{320}} = \\frac{{{p_2} \\times 10}}{{280}}$<br>$ \\Rightarrow {p_2} = 2.625\\,bar$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2019,'AIIMS','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38189,'physics',28,1369,3279,'Work and heat are:','[{"text": "Path function", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Point function", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Extensive function", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Intensive function", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Work and heat are path functions.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2021,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38190,'physics',27,1380,3311,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> In pressure-temperature $(P-T)$ phase diagram of water, the slope of the melting curve is found to be negative.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Ice contracts on melting to water<br>','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'In the pressure-temperature $(P-T)$ phase diagram of water, the melting curve typically has a negative slope.  <img src=\'/img/data/physics/27_38190_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />  On either side of melting curve (AB), we have ice and water. By supplying heat, we increase the temperature of ice. Clearly, pressure is decreasing in that process.<br>$\\Rightarrow$ Slope is negative.<br>This is due to the change of phase, as the transition from ice to liquid water involves a decrease in volume (contraction), which is why the slope is negative. So, both the Assertion and Reason are correct, and the Reason provides the correct explanation for the Assertion.<br>So, correct option is (1).',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',2005,'AIIMS','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38191,'physics',26,1138,2728,'A soap bubble of radius \' $r$ \' and surface tension \' $T$ \' is given a charge. The new radius \' $R$ \' of the bubble is related to its initial radius $r$ by the equation, $\\left(P_{0}\\right.$ is the atmospheric pressure & $V$ is the final potential of the bubble','[{"text": "$P_{0}\\\\left(R^{3}-r^{3}\\\\right)+4 T\\\\left(R^{2}-r^{2}\\\\right)-\\\\dfrac{\\\\varepsilon_{0} V^{2} R}{2}=0$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$P_{0}\\\\left(R^{3}-r^{3}\\\\right)+4 T\\\\left(R^{2}-r^{2}\\\\right)=0$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$P_{0}\\\\left(R^{3}-r^{3}\\\\right)-\\\\dfrac{\\\\varepsilon_{0} V^{2} R}{2}=0$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${P_0}{R^3} + 4T\\\\left( {{R^2} - {r^2}} \\\\right) = 0$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'The pressures on the bubble are shown in figure<br><img src=\'/img/data/physics/26_38191_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' /> <br>${P_1} - {P_0} = \\frac{{4T}}{r}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\left( 1 \\right)$<br>${P_2} - {P_0} + \\frac{{{\\sigma ^2}}}{{2{\\varepsilon _0}}} = \\frac{{4T}}{R}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\left( 2 \\right)$<br>From isothermal condition,<br>${P_1}{V_1} = {P_2}{V_2}\\,\\,\\,{\\mkern 1mu} {\\kern 1pt} {P_1}{r^3} = {P_2}{R^3}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\left( 3 \\right)$<br>The potential of the sphere is<br>$V = \\frac{q}{{4\\pi {\\varepsilon _0}R}} = \\frac{{\\sigma R}}{{{\\varepsilon _0}}}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\left( 4 \\right)$<br>From Eq\'s (1), (2), (3) & (4)<br>we get $P_{0}\\left(R^{3}-r^{3}\\right)+4 T\\left(R^{2}-r^{2}\\right)-\\dfrac{\\varepsilon_{0} V^{2} R}{2}=0$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38244,'physics',29,1301,3148,'Given molecular weight of hydrogen molecule is $M = 2.016 \\times {10^{ - 3}}\\,kg{\\rm{/}}mol$. Calculate the root mean square speed of hydrogen molecules $\\left( {{H_2}} \\right)$ at $373.15\\,K\\left( {100^\\circ C} \\right)$,','[{"text": "$2.15\\\\,km{\\\\rm{/}}s$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$3.25\\\\,km{\\\\rm{/}}s$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$4.22\\\\,km{\\\\rm{/}}s$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1.25\\\\,km{\\\\rm{/}}s$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'$V_{r . m . s .}=\\sqrt{\\dfrac{3 R T}{M}}$<br>$C = \\sqrt {\\frac{{3 \\times 8.314 \\times 373.15}}{{2.016 \\times {{10}^{ - 3}}}}}  = \\sqrt {4.617 \\times {{10}^6}} $<br>$ = 2.148\\,km{\\rm{/}}s$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38194,'physics',26,1138,2727,'Bubbles are made by dipping a circular ring of radius $b$ in a soap solution and then blowing air on the film formed on the ring. Assume that the blown air is in the form of a cylinder of radius $b$. It has speed $v$ and stops after striking the surface of the bubble being formed. The bubble grows spherically. Let the radius $R$ of the bubble $(>>b)$, so that the air strikes the bubbles surface perpendicularly. The surface tension of the solution is $T$ and air density is $\\rho$. The radius of the bubble when it separates from the ring is (neglect the mass of the bubble).','[{"text": "$\\\\dfrac{4 T}{\\\\rho V^{2}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{T}{\\\\rho V^{2}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{T}{4 \\\\rho V^{2}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{{2T}}{{\\\\rho {V^2}}}$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'The force exerted by the blown air on the bubble is $F=\\rho A V^{2}=\\rho\\left(\\pi b^{2}\\right) V^{2}$<br>when the bubble detaches<br><img src=\'/img/data/physics/26_38194_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' /> <br>The surface tension force is<br>$F_{x}=F \\sin \\theta=(2 \\pi b) T\\left(\\dfrac{b}{R}\\right)=\\dfrac{2 \\pi b^{2} T}{R}$<br>The radius of the bubble is $R=\\dfrac{2 T}{\\rho V^{2}}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/26_38194_question_1.png'),
(38195,'physics',27,1381,3315,'Equal masses of two liquids \\(A\\) and \\(B\\) contained in vessels of negligible heat capacity are supplied heat at the same rate. The temperature-time graphs for the two liquids are shown below<div class="matching-question"></div>','[{"text": "$s_{A}>s_{B}, L_{A} < L_{B}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$s_{A}>s_{B}, L_{A}>L_{B}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$s_{A} < s_{B}, L_{A} < L_{B}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$s_{A} < s_{B}, L_{A}>L_{B}$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'It is clear from the graph,<br>Denote $\\Theta=$ temperature, $t=$ time<br>horizontal lenghts $a b>c d$  <img src=\'/img/data/physics/27_38195_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />  $\\Rightarrow$ Latent heat of liquid $A\\left(L_{A}\\right)>$ Latent heat of liquid $B\\left(L_{B}\\right)$<br>But, slope $A>$ slope $B$<br>$\\Rightarrow s_{A} < s_{B}$<br>So, correct option is (3).',NULL,'Hard','Graph/Figure',2009,'AIIMS','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/27_38195_question_1.png'),
(38196,'physics',29,1301,3148,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> The root mean square and most probable speeds of the molecules in a gas are the same.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> The Maxwell distribution for the speed of molecules in a gas is symmetrical.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of  Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are incorrect.", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'According to kinetic theory of gases molecules do exhibit random motion, hence assertion is incorrect. Also Maxwell\'s velocity distribution is asymmetric.  So correct option is (4).',NULL,'Hard','Assertion and Reason',2006,'AIIMS','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38198,'physics',26,1138,2730,'There is a soap bubble in which there exists yet another soap bubble. It is given that the outside pressure is zero. If due to some reason the inner bubble bursts then the radius of bigger bubble will','[{"text": "Remain $2R$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Become $3R$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Become $\\\\sqrt{5} R$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Become $4R$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Let $P_{1}$ and $P_{2}$ be the pressures inside the two bubbles as shown in figure<br><img src=\'/img/data/physics/26_38198_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' /> <br>Let $S$ is the coefficient of surface tension pressure difference across small bubble<br>$P_{2}-P_{o}=\\dfrac{4 S}{2 R} \\Rightarrow P_{2}=\\dfrac{4 S}{2 R}$<br>Pressure difference across large bubble<br>$P_{1}-P_{2}=\\dfrac{4 S}{R} \\Rightarrow P_{1}=\\dfrac{6 S}{R}$<br>Let $n_{1}$ and $n_{2}$ are the number of moles in the two bubbles $n$ is the number of moles in the bigger bubble after the first bubble bursts.<br>${n_1} + {n_2} = n$<br>$\\frac{{{P_1}{V_1}}}{{RT}} + \\frac{{{P_2}{V_2}}}{{RT}} = \\frac{{PV}}{{RT}}\\,\\,\\,{\\mkern 1mu} {\\kern 1pt} (\\,T = {\\rm{ constant }})$<br>${P_1}{V_1} + {P_2}{V_2} = PV$<br>$\\frac{{6S}}{R}\\left( {\\frac{4}{3}\\pi {R^3}} \\right) + \\frac{{4S}}{{2R}}\\left( {\\frac{4}{3}\\pi {{(2R)}^3} - \\frac{4}{3}\\pi {R^3}} \\right) = \\frac{{4S}}{r}\\frac{4}{3}\\pi {R^3}$<br>$r = \\sqrt 5 R$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/26_38198_question_1.png'),
(38246,'physics',27,1378,3304,'Oxygen boils at $ - 183^\\circ C$. This temperature is approximately.','[{"text": "$215^\\\\circ F$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$-297^\\\\circ F$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$329^\\\\circ F$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$361^\\\\circ F$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'According to the formula:<br>$\\begin{aligned}& \\dfrac{C}{5}=\\dfrac{F-32}{9} \\Rightarrow \\dfrac{-183}{5}=\\dfrac{F-32}{9} \\\\& F=-297^{\\circ} F\\end{aligned}$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38199,'physics',27,1382,3319,'Two slabs $A$ and $B$ of different materials but of the same thickness are joined end to end to form a composite slab. The thermal conductivities of A and B are $K_{1}$ and $K_{2}$, respectively. A steady temperature difference of $12^\\circ C$ is maintained across the composite slab. If $K_{1}=\\dfrac{K_{2}}{2}$, the temperature difference across slab $A$ is','[{"text": "$4^\\\\circ C$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$6^\\\\circ C$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$8^\\\\circ C$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$10^\\\\circ C$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Given situation can be shown in figure as  <img src=\'/img/data/physics/27_38199_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />   Rate of heat flow through rod is given as<br>$\\dfrac{d Q}{d t}=\\dfrac{\\Delta T}{l / K A}=\\dfrac{K A(\\Delta T)}{l}$<br>As, rate of heat flow is same for all sections<br>$\\left(\\dfrac{d Q}{d T}\\right)_{P Q}=\\left(\\dfrac{d Q}{d T}\\right)_{P R}$<br>$\\Rightarrow\\left(\\dfrac{100-T_{Q}}{\\dfrac{L}{K_{1} A}}\\right)=\\left(\\dfrac{T_{P}-T_{R}}{\\dfrac{L}{K_{1} A}+\\dfrac{L}{K_{2} A}}\\right)$<br>But, given $K_{1}=\\dfrac{K_{2}}{2}$<br>$\\Rightarrow \\dfrac{\\left(100-T_{Q}\\right)}{\\dfrac{1}{K_{1}}}=\\dfrac{12}{\\dfrac{1}{K_{1}}+\\dfrac{1}{2 K_{1}}}$<br>$\\Rightarrow\\left(100-T_{Q}\\right)=\\dfrac{2.12}{3}=8$<br>$ \\Rightarrow {T_P} - {T_Q} = 8^\\circ C\\,\\,\\,\\left( {\\because {T_P} = 100^\\circ C} \\right)$<br>So, correct option is (3)',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',2012,'AIIMS','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38200,'physics',29,1302,3150,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> If a gas container in motion is suddenly stopped, the temperature of the gas rises.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> The kinetic energy of ordered mechanical motion is converted into the kinetic energy of random motion of gas molecules.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of  Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'The motion of the container is known as the ordered motion of the gas and zig-zag motion of gas molecules within the container is called disordered/random motion, like brownian motion. When the container suddenly stops, ordered kinetic energy gets converted into disordered kinetic energy which increases the temperature of the gas.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',2010,'AIIMS','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38202,'physics',27,1382,3321,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> A body that is a good radiator is also a good absorber of radiation at a given wavelength.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> According to Kirchhoff\'s law, the absorptivity of a body is equal to its emissivity at a given wavelength<br>','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'That a body that is a good radiator is also a good absorber of radiation at a given wavelength. This is in accordance with Kirchhoff\'s law. This means that a good radiator (high emissivity) is also a good absorber (high absorptivity) of radiation, and it\'s a fundamental principle in radiative heat transfer. Therefore, both the Assertion and Reason are correct, and the Reason correctly explains the Assertion.<br>So, correct option is (1)',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',2005,'AIIMS','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38203,'physics',26,1138,2731,'A capillary tube is immersed vertically in water and the height of the water column is \\[x\\]. When this arrangement is taken into a mine of depth \\[d\\], the height of the water column is \\[y\\]. If \\[R\\] is the radius of the earth, the ratio \\(\\dfrac{x}{y}\\) is<div class="matching-question"></div>','[{"text": "$\\\\left(1-\\\\dfrac{d}{R}\\\\right)$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\left(\\\\dfrac{R+d}{R-d}\\\\right)$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\left(\\\\dfrac{R-d}{R+d}\\\\right)$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\left(1+\\\\dfrac{d}{R}\\\\right)$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'The capillary raise<br>$\\begin{aligned}& h=\\dfrac{2 T \\cos \\theta}{\\rho r g}, x=\\dfrac{2 T \\cos \\theta}{\\rho r g}, y=\\dfrac{2 T \\cos \\theta}{\\rho r g^{\\prime}} \\\\& \\dfrac{x}{y}=\\dfrac{g^{\\prime}}{g} \\\\& g^{\\prime}=\\dfrac{G M}{R^{3}}(R-d)=g\\left[1-\\dfrac{d}{R}\\right] \\\\& \\dfrac{x}{y}=\\left[1-\\dfrac{d}{R}\\right]\\end{aligned}$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38205,'physics',29,1300,3146,'Critical temperature of $C{O_2}$ is $31.2^\\circ C$. In summer, the room temperature is $40^\\circ C$, then','[{"text": "$C{O_2}$ cannot be liquified", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$C{O_2}$ can be liquified with increase of pressure", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$C{O_2}$ can be liquified with decrease of pressure", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$C{O_2}$ can be liquified, if temperature of $C{O_2}$ is decreased below $31.2^\\\\circ C$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Gases cannot be liquified above critical temperature, however large the pressure may be hence $C{O_2}$ cannot be liquified.',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',2013,'AIIMS','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38206,'physics',27,1382,3322,'Three objects colored black, grey and white can withstand hostile conditions upto $2800^\\circ C$. These objects are thrown into a furnace where each of them attains a temperature of $2000^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C}$. Which object will glow brightest?','[{"text": "The white object", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "The black object", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "All glow with equal brightness", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Gray object", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'An ideal black body absorbs all the radiations incident upon it and has an emissivity $\\in$ equal to unity. If a black body and an identical another body are kept at the same temperature, then the block body will radiate maximum power.  <img src=\'/img/data/physics/27_38206_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />  For ideal black body $\\in(\\lambda)=\\epsilon_{0}=1$<br>For grey body; $\\in(\\lambda)=\\epsilon^{\\prime} < 1$<br>Hence, the black object at a temperature of $2000^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C}$, will glow brightest.<br>So, correct option is (2)',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',2006,'AIIMS','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49036,'biology',80,470,949,'Photosynthesis is a','[{"text": "Single-step process", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Two-step process", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Multi-step process", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "None of the above", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Photosynthesis is a multi-step process.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38207,'physics',26,1138,2731,'Water in a clean aquarium forms a meniscus, as illustrated in figure <br> <br> Find the difference in height $h$ between the centre and the edge of the meniscus. The surface tension of water is $T = 0.073\\,N{m^{ - 1}}.$','[{"text": "$2\\\\,mm$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$4\\\\,mm$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$6\\\\,mm$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1\\\\,mm$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'The pressure of the water changes linearly with the increase in height. At the bottom of the meniscus it is equal to the external atmospheric pressure $P_{0}$, and at the top to $P_{0}-\\rho g h$. The average pressure exerted on the wall is $P_{\\text {average }}=P_{0}-\\rho g h / 2$.<br><img src=\'/img/data/physics/26_38207_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' /> <br>Consider the horizontal forces acting on the volume of water enclosed by the dashed lines in the Figure.<br>The wall pushes it to the right with force $F_{1}$, the external air pushes it to the left with force $F_{2}=l P_{0} h$, and the surface tension of the rest of the water pulls it to the right with a force $F_{3}=l T$. The resultant of these forces has to be zero,<br>That is, $\\left(P_{0}-\\dfrac{1}{2} \\rho g h\\right) l h+l T=l P_{0} h$<br>$h = \\sqrt {\\frac{{2T}}{{\\rho g}}}  = \\sqrt {\\frac{{2 \\times 0.073}}{{1000 \\times 10}}}  = 0.0038\\,m.$<br>Water rises by approximately $4\\,mm$ up the wall of the aquarium.',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/26_38207_question_1.png'),
(38209,'physics',29,1300,3145,'If two vessels $A$ and $B$ contain the same gas but the volume of vessel $A$ is twice that of $B$ and temperature and pressure of gas $A$ is twice that of gas in $B$, then the ratio of gas molecules in $A$ and $B$ is','[{"text": "$1: 2$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1: 4$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$4: 1$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2: 1$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'The number of molecules <br>$(N) = \\frac{{PV}}{{KT}}$<br>$\\frac{{{N_A}}}{{{N_B}}} = \\left( {\\frac{{{P_A}}}{{{P_B}}}} \\right)\\left( {\\frac{{{V_A}}}{{{V_B}}}} \\right)\\left( {\\frac{{{T_B}}}{{{T_A}}}} \\right)$<br>$\\frac{{{N_A}}}{{{N_B}}} = (2)(2)\\left( {\\frac{1}{2}} \\right) = 2$ $i.e.\\,2:1$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2021,'MHTCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38210,'physics',27,1382,3324,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Perspiration from human body helps in cooling the body.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> A thin layer of water on the skin enhances its emissivity.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Because during perspiration, the process of sweating, helps in cooling the human body. When we sweat, the sweat evaporates from the skin\'s surface, taking heat from the body and cooling it down. This is a well-known mechanism for temperature regulation in the body.<br>A thin layer of water on the skin does not enhance its emissivity. In fact, it would have the opposite effect. Emissivity is a measure of how efficiently a surface radiates thermal energy, and water on the skin would likely decrease emissivity rather than enhance it. So, while A is correct, $\\mathrm{R}$ is not the correct explanation for $\\mathrm{A}$.<br>So, correct option is (3)',NULL,'Hard','Assertion and Reason',2006,'AIIMS','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38211,'physics',26,1138,2731,'A glass capillary tube is of the shape of truncated cone with an apex angle $\\alpha$ so that its two ends have cross-sections of different radii. When dipped in water vertically, water rises in it to a height $h$, where the radius of its cross-section is $b$. If the surface tension of water is $S$, its density is $\\rho$, and its contact angle with glass is $\\theta$, the value of $h$ will be ( $g$ is the acceleration due to gravity)','[{"text": "$\\\\dfrac{2 S}{b \\\\rho g} \\\\cos (\\\\theta-\\\\alpha)$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{2 S}{b \\\\rho g} \\\\cos (\\\\theta+\\\\alpha)$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{2 S}{b \\\\rho g} \\\\cos (\\\\theta-\\\\alpha / 2)$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{2 S}{b \\\\rho g} \\\\cos (\\\\theta+\\\\alpha / 2)$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Let $R$ be the radius of the meniscus formed with a contact angle $\\theta$. By geometry this radius makes an angle $\\theta+\\dfrac{\\alpha}{}$ with horizontal<br><img src=\'/img/data/physics/26_38211_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' /> <br>$\\begin{aligned}& \\cos \\left(\\theta+\\dfrac{\\alpha}{2}\\right)=\\dfrac{b}{R} \\\\& P_{0}-P_{1}=\\dfrac{2 S}{R} \\quad \\& \\quad P_{2}-P_{1}=\\rho g h\\end{aligned}$<br>From the above equations<br>$h=\\dfrac{2 S}{\\rho g R}=\\dfrac{2 S \\cos \\left(\\theta+\\dfrac{\\alpha}{2}\\right)}{\\rho g b}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/26_38211_question_1.png');
INSERT INTO `qb_questions` (`id`, `subject_id`, `chapter_id`, `topic_id`, `subtopic_id`, `question_html`, `options`, `correct_index`, `explanation`, `explanation_image_url`, `difficulty`, `qtype`, `year`, `tag`, `created_at`, `question_image_url`) VALUES
(38213,'physics',29,1301,3147,'$10^{23}$ molecules of a gas, each having a mass of $3 \\times {10^{ - 27}}\\,kg$ strike per second per sq. $cm$ of a rigid wall at an angle of $60^{\\circ}$ with the normal and rebound with a velocity of $500\\,m{\\rm{/}}s$. The pressure exerted by the gas molecules on the wall is<br>$\\left[ {\\sin 30^\\circ  = \\cos 60^\\circ  = 0.5,\\cos 30^\\circ  = \\sin 60^\\circ  = \\sqrt 3 {\\rm{/}}2} \\right]$','[{"text": "$500\\\\,N{\\\\rm{/}}{m^2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2000\\\\,N{\\\\rm{/}}{m^2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1500\\\\,N{\\\\rm{/}}{m^2}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$1000\\\\,N{\\\\rm{/}}{m^2}$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'<img src=\'/img/data/physics/29_38213_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />  Mass of gas molecule, $m = 3 \\times {10^{ - 27}}\\,kg$<br>Velocity of gas molecule, $v = 500\\,m{\\rm{/}}s$<br>Number of molecules in gas, $n=10^{23}$<br>Change in momentum,<br>$\\Delta p=m v \\cos 60^{\\circ}+m v \\cos 60^{\\circ}$<br>$\\Delta p=2 m v \\cos 60^{\\circ}$<br>$\\Delta p=2 \\times 3 \\times 10^{-27} \\times 500 \\times \\dfrac{1}{2}$<br>$ = 15 \\times {10^{ - 25}}\\,kg\\,m{\\rm{/}}\\sec $<br>For \' $N$ \' molecule change in momentum,<br>$\\Delta p\' = \\Delta p \\times N = 15 \\times {10^{ - 25}} \\times {10^{23}}$<br>$ \\Rightarrow {\\left( {\\Delta p} \\right)^\\prime } = 15 \\times {10^{ - 2}}\\,kg\\,m{\\rm{/}}\\sec $<br>Area, $A = 1\\,c{m^2} = {10^{ - 4}}\\,{m^2}$<br>$P = $ Pressure exerted by gas molecule $ = \\frac{{{{\\left( {\\Delta p} \\right)}^\\prime }}}{A}$<br>$ \\Rightarrow P = \\frac{{15 \\times {{10}^{ - 2}}}}{{{{10}^{ - 4}}}} = 15 \\times {10^2}\\,N{\\rm{/}}{m^2} = 1500\\,N{\\rm{/}}{m^2}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2022,'MHTCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38214,'physics',26,1138,2727,'The figure shows a soap film in which a closed elastic thread is lying. The film inside the thread is pricked. Now the sliding wire is moved out so that the surface area increases. The radius of the circle formed by elastic thread will','[{"text": "Increase", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Decrease", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Remains same", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Data insufficient", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'The force exerted by film on wire or thread depends only on the nature of material of the film and not on its surface area. Hence the radius of circle formed by elastic thread does not change.',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/26_38214_question_1.jpg'),
(38215,'physics',27,1382,3324,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Like light radiation, thermal radiations are also electromagnetic radiation.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> The thermal radiations require no medium for propagation.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Both light radiation and thermal radiations are indeed forms of electromagnetic radiation. Light radiation falls within the visible region of the electromagnetic spectrum, while thermal radiation primarily consists of infrared radiation. Both do not require a medium for propagation. They can travel through a vacuum, which is in contrast to mechanical waves like sound waves, which need a medium for propagation.<br>However, while both $\\mathrm{A}$ and $\\mathrm{R}$ are correct, $\\mathrm{R}$ does not provide a direct explanation of $A$. The fact that thermal radiations require no medium for propagation is a general characteristic of electromagnetic waves but does not directly explain why light radiation and thermal radiation are both forms of electromagnetic radiation.<br>So, correct option is (2)',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',2010,'AIIMS','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38216,'physics',29,1301,3148,'If the absolute temperature of a gas is increased 5 times, the r.m.s. velocity of the gas molecules will be','[{"text": "10 times", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "5 times", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\sqrt{5}$ times", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "25 times", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'The  r.m.s velocity of the gas molecules<br>${v_{rms}} = \\sqrt {\\frac{{3RT}}{M}} \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\left( 1 \\right)$<br>Now Temperature \' $T$ \' increased to $5 T$.<br>${v_{rms}} = \\sqrt {\\frac{{3R(5T)}}{M}}  = \\sqrt 5 \\,{v_{rms}}$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2022,'MHTCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38218,'physics',27,1382,3322,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> For higher temperatures, the peak emission wavelength of a black body shifts to lower wavelengths.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Peak emission wavelengths of a black body is proportional to the fourth - power of temperature','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'For higher temperatures, the peak emission wavelength of a black body indeed shifts to lower wavelengths.<br>Denote $\\mathrm{I}=$ Spectral radiation intensity <img src=\'/img/data/physics/27_38218_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />  Here $5000 K>4000 K>2000 K$<br>Peak for $5000 K$ is towards shorter $\\lambda$ value $\\Rightarrow(A)$ is true<br>This is as per Wien\'s displacement law<br>$\\lambda_{\\max } T=$ constant<br>$\\Rightarrow \\lambda_{\\max } \\alpha \\dfrac{1}{1}$<br>Here \'max\' means peak<br>$\\Rightarrow \\lambda_{\\max }$ is not proportional to fourth-power of $T$.<br>$\\Rightarrow R$ is false<br>So, correct option is (3)',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',2005,'AIIMS','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38219,'physics',26,1138,2728,'A cylinder with a movable piston contains air under a pressure $p_{1}$ and a soap bubble of radius \' $r$ \'. The pressure $p_{2}$ to which the air should be compressed by slowly pushing the piston into the cylinder for the soap bubble to reduce its size by half will be : (The surface tension is $\\sigma$ and the temperature $T$ is maintained constant)','[{"text": "$\\\\left[8 p_{1}+\\\\dfrac{24 \\\\sigma}{r}\\\\right]$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\left[4 p_{1}+\\\\dfrac{24 \\\\sigma}{r}\\\\right]$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\left[2 p_{1}+\\\\dfrac{24 \\\\sigma}{r}\\\\right]$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\left[2 p_{1}+\\\\dfrac{12 \\\\sigma}{r}\\\\right]$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'The pressure difference across the soap bubble is<br>Initially ${P_i} - {P_1} = \\frac{{4\\sigma }}{r}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\left( 1 \\right)$<br>After pushing the piston<br>${P_f} - {P_2} = \\frac{{4\\sigma }}{{(r/2)}}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\left( 2 \\right)$<br>As the bubble is compressed isothermally<br>${P_i}\\frac{4}{3}\\pi {r^3} = {P_f}\\frac{4}{3}\\pi {\\left( {\\frac{r}{2}} \\right)^3}$<br>${P_f} = 8{P_i}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\left( 3 \\right)$<br>From the Eq\'s (1), (2) & (3) we get<br>$P_{2}=8 P_{1}+\\dfrac{24 \\sigma}{r}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38221,'physics',29,1300,3145,'A closed vessel contains $8\\,gm$ of oxygen and $7\\,gm$ of nitrogen. The total pressure is $10{\\mkern 1mu} \\,atm$ at the given temperature. If all the oxygen is removed from the system without permitting any change in temperature, then the pressure will becomes (in atmosphere):','[{"text": "$70{\\\\rm{/}}15$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$80{\\\\rm{/}}15$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "5", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$70{\\\\rm{/}}8$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'$P V=n R T$<br>$\\frac{{{P_1}}}{{{P_2}}} = \\frac{{{n_1}}}{{{n_2}}} = \\frac{{\\frac{8}{{32}} + \\frac{7}{{28}}}}{{\\frac{7}{{28}}}} = 2$<br>$ \\Rightarrow {P_2} = \\frac{{{P_1}}}{2} = 5Atm.$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38222,'physics',26,1138,2730,'Two soap bubbles of radii $R_{1}$ and $R_{2}$ kept in atmosphere are combined isothermally to form a big bubble of radius $R$. The expression for surface tension will be','[{"text": "$\\\\dfrac{P_{o}\\\\left(R^{3}+R_{1}^{3}+R_{2}^{3}\\\\right)}{4\\\\left(R^{2}+R_{1}^{2}+R_{2}^{2}\\\\right)}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{P_{o}\\\\left(R_{1}^{3}+R_{2}^{3}-R^{3}\\\\right)}{4\\\\left(R^{2}-R_{1}^{2}-R_{2}^{2}\\\\right)}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$P_{o}\\\\left(R_{1}^{3}+R_{2}^{3}-R^{3}\\\\right)$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$4 P_{o}\\\\left(R_{1}^{3}+R_{2}^{3}-R^{3}\\\\right)$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'As number of moles remains constant<br>$\\begin{aligned}& n=n_{1}+n_{2} \\\\& \\dfrac{P V}{R T}=\\dfrac{P_{1} V_{1}}{R T}+\\dfrac{P_{2} V_{2}}{R T} \\\\& P V=P_{1} V_{1}+P_{2} V_{2}\\end{aligned}$<br>$\\left( {{P_o} + \\frac{{4T}}{R}} \\right)\\frac{4}{3}\\pi {R^3} = \\left( {{P_o} + \\frac{{4T}}{{{R_1}}}} \\right)\\frac{4}{3}\\pi R_1^3$<br>$\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, + \\left( {{P_o} + \\frac{{4T}}{{{R_2}}}} \\right)\\frac{4}{3}\\pi R_2^3$<br>$\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{P_o}\\left( {R_1^3 + R_2^3 - {R^3}} \\right) = 4T\\left( {{R^2} - R_1^2 - R_2^2} \\right)$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38223,'physics',27,1382,3322,'Which of the following is the \\(\\lambda_{m}-T\\) graph for a perfectly black body?<div class="matching-question"></div>','[{"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'The equation of Wien\'s displacement law<br>i.e. $\\lambda_{m} T=$ constant<br>For perfectly black body, $\\lambda_{m} \\propto \\dfrac{1}{T}$<br>So, graph is rectangular hyperbola.<br>So, correct option is (4).',NULL,'Medium','Graph/Figure',2013,'AIIMS','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38225,'physics',29,1300,3145,'A vessel of volume $V=5.0$ litre contains $1.4\\,gm$ of nitrogen at temperature $T = 1800\\,K$. Find the pressure of the gas if $30 \\%$ of its molecule are dissociated into atom at this temperature.','[{"text": "$1.94 \\\\times {10^5}\\\\,N{\\\\rm{/}}{m^2}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$0.54 \\\\times {10^5}\\\\,N{\\\\rm{/}}{m^2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2.62 \\\\times {10^5}\\\\,N{\\\\rm{/}}{m^2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$3.75 \\\\times {10^5}\\\\,N{\\\\rm{/}}{m^2}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'$P V=\\left(n_{1}+n_{2}\\right) R T$<br>$\\dfrac{P_{1}}{P_{2}}=\\dfrac{n_{1}}{n_{1}+n_{2}}=\\dfrac{\\dfrac{1.4}{28}}{\\dfrac{0.98}{28}+\\dfrac{0.42}{14}}$<br>$=\\dfrac{1.4}{28} \\dfrac{(28)}{0.98+0.84}=\\dfrac{1.4}{1.82}$<br>$\\Rightarrow P_{2}=\\dfrac{1.82}{1.4} P_{1}=1.3 P_{1}$<br>$=\\dfrac{13}{10} \\dfrac{\\mu R T}{V}=\\dfrac{13}{10} \\times \\dfrac{1}{20} \\times \\dfrac{25}{3} \\times \\dfrac{1800}{5 \\times 10^{-3}}$<br>$\\Rightarrow P_{2}=13 \\times 3 \\times 5 \\times 10^{3}=195 \\times 10^{3}$<br>$ = 1.95 \\times {10^5}\\,N{\\rm{/}}{m^2}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38226,'physics',26,1138,2730,'When liquid medicine of density $\\rho$ is to be put in the eye, it is done with the help of a dropper. As the bulb on the top of the dropper is pressed, a drop forms at the opening of the dropper. We wish to estimate the size of the drop. We first assume that the drop formed at the opening is spherical because that requires a minimum increase in its surface energy. To determine the size, we calculate the net vertical force due to the surface tension $T$ when the radius of the drop is $R$. When the force becomes smaller than the weight of the drop, the drop gets detached from the dropper. If the radius of the opening of the dropper is $r$, the vertical force due to the surface tension on the drop of radius $R$ (assuming $r <  < R$) is','[{"text": "$2 \\\\pi r T$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2 \\\\pi R T$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{2 \\\\pi r^{2} T}{R}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{2 \\\\pi R^{2} T}{r}$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'The drop that is formed is shown in the figure <br> <img src=\'/img/data/physics/26_38226_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' /> <br> The free body diagram of the drop is <br> <img src=\'/img/data/physics/26_38226_explanation_2.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' /> <br>$\\begin{aligned}& F_{y}=F \\sin \\theta=(2 \\pi r T) \\sin \\theta \\\\& =(2 \\pi r T)\\left(\\dfrac{r}{R}\\right)=\\dfrac{2 \\pi r^{2} T}{R}\\end{aligned}$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38228,'physics',29,1300,3145,'The figure shows graphs of pressure versus density for an ideal gas at two temperatures \\(T_{1}\\) and \\(T_{2}\\), then<div class="matching-question"></div>','[{"text": "$T_{1}>T_{2}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$T_{1}=T_{2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$T_{1} < T_{2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "None", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'<br>$P V=n R T=\\dfrac{m}{M} R T$<br>$\\dfrac{P}{m / V}=\\dfrac{R T}{M}$<br>$\\dfrac{P}{\\rho} \\propto T \\Rightarrow T_{1}>T_{2}$',NULL,'Medium','Graph/Figure',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/29_38228_question_1.png'),
(38230,'physics',26,1138,2730,'On heating water, bubbles beings formed at the bottom of the vessel detach and rise. Take the bubbles to be spheres of radius $R$ and making a circular contact of radius $r$ with the bottom of vessel. If $r <  < R$ and the surface tension of water is $T$, value of $r$ just before bubbles detach is (density of water is $\\rho$ )','[{"text": "$R^{2} \\\\sqrt{\\\\dfrac{2 \\\\rho_{w} g}{3 T}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$R^{2} \\\\sqrt{\\\\dfrac{\\\\rho_{w} g}{6 T}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$R^{2} \\\\sqrt{\\\\dfrac{\\\\rho_{w} g}{T}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$R^{2} \\\\sqrt{\\\\dfrac{3 \\\\rho_{w} g}{T}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'The bubble will detach if the upward buoyancy force is balanced by the downward surface tension force.<br><img src=\'/img/data/physics/26_38230_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' /> <br>$\\left( {{\\rho _W}} \\right)\\left( {\\frac{4}{3}\\pi {R^3}} \\right)g = \\left( T \\right)\\left( {2\\pi r} \\right)\\sin \\theta $<br>$\\begin{aligned}& \\Rightarrow \\quad \\sin \\theta=\\dfrac{r}{R} \\\\& \\text { Solving, } r=\\sqrt{\\dfrac{2 \\rho_{W} R^{4} g}{3 T}}=R^{2} \\sqrt{\\dfrac{2 \\rho_{W} g}{3 T}}\\end{aligned}$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/26_38230_question_1.png'),
(38231,'physics',27,1382,3324,'Calculate radiation power of sphere whose temperature is $227^\\circ C$, radius $2\\,m$ and emissivity 0.8 .','[{"text": "$142.5\\\\,kW$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$1500\\\\,W$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1255\\\\,W$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1575\\\\,W$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Temperature of sphere, $T=227^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C}$<br>$\\Rightarrow T=273+227=500 K$<br>Radius, $r=2 m$<br>Emissivity, $\\in=0.8$<br>$P=$ Radiation power of sphere<br>$\\Rightarrow P=$ Radiated energy per second<br>$\\begin{aligned}& =\\sigma A \\in T^{4} \\\\& =5.67 \\times 10^{-8} \\times 4 \\pi \\times 2^{2} \\times 0.8 \\times(500)^{4}\\end{aligned}$<br>$ = 142502.6\\,W = 142.5\\,kW$<br>So, correct option is (1)',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2019,'AIIMS','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38233,'physics',29,1300,3145,'Two monoatomic gas $A$ and $B$ occupying the same volume $V$ are at the same temperature $T$ and pressure $P$. If they are mixed, the resulting mixture has volume $V$ and temperature $T$. The pressure of the mixture is','[{"text": "$P$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{P}{2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$4 P$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2 P$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'$\\frac{{{P_1}{V_1}}}{{{V_1}}} = \\frac{{{P_{mix{\\rm{ }}}}{V_{mix{\\rm{ }}}}}}{{{T_{mix{\\rm{ }}}}}}$<br>${T_1} = {T_{mix{\\rm{ }}}} = T$ and ${V_1} = V + V,{V_{mix{\\rm{ }}}} = V$<br>$\\dfrac{P(2 V)}{T}=\\dfrac{P_{m i x} V}{T}$<br>${P_{mix{\\rm{ }}}} = 2P$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38248,'physics',29,1301,3148,'Root mean square velocity of a particle is $V$ at pressure $P$. If pressure is increased two times, then the rms velocity becomes','[{"text": "$2\\\\,V$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$3\\\\,V$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$0.5\\\\,V$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$V$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Root mean square velocity does not depend upon pressure. Hence, the rms velocity remains same.',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38234,'physics',27,1382,3323,'If temperature of sun $ = 6000\\,K$, radius of sun is $7.2 \\times {10^5}\\,km$, radius of earth $ = 6000\\,km$ and distance between earth and sun $ = 15 \\times {10^7}\\,km$. Find intensity of light on earth.','[{"text": "$19.2 \\\\times 10^{16}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$12.2 \\\\times 10^{16}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$18.3 \\\\times 10^{16}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$9.2 \\\\times 10^{16}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Given, temperature of sun,<br>${T_S} = 6000\\,K$<br>Radius of sun,<br>${R_s} = 7.2 \\times {10^5}\\,km = 7.2 \\times {10^8}\\,m$<br>Radius of earth,<br>${R_e} = 6000\\,km = 600 \\times {10^3}\\,m$<br>Distance between earth and sun,<br>$d = 15 \\times {10^7}\\,km = 15 \\times {10^{10}}\\,m$<br>Intensity of light on the earth,<br>$I=\\dfrac{\\text { Total energy emitted by sun }}{4 \\pi d^{2}} \\times\\left(\\pi R_{e}{ }^{2}\\right)$<br>$\\Rightarrow I=\\dfrac{\\sigma\\left(T_{s}^{4} \\cdot 4 \\pi R_{s}^{2} \\times \\pi R_{e}^{2}\\right)}{4 \\pi d^{2}}$<br>$=\\dfrac{\\sigma T_{s}^{4} R_{s}^{2} \\times \\pi R_{e}^{2}}{d^{2}}$<br>$ = \\frac{{\\sigma T_s^4R_s^2 \\times \\pi {R_e}^2}}{{{d^2}}}$<br>$ = \\frac{{\\left( \\begin{array}{l}\n5.67 \\times {10^{ - 8}} \\times {\\left( {6000} \\right)^4} \\times {\\left( {7.2 \\times {{10}^8}} \\right)^2} \\times \\\\\n3.14 \\times {\\left( {6000 \\times {{10}^3}} \\right)^2}\n\\end{array} \\right)}}{{{{\\left( {15 \\times {{10}^{10}}} \\right)}^2}}}$<br>$ \\Rightarrow I = 19.2 \\times {10^{16}}$  (in proper unit)<br>So, correct option is (1).',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2019,'AIIMS','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38235,'physics',26,1138,2731,'A cube of side $a$ and mass $m$ just floats on the surface of water as shown in the figure. The surface tension and density of water are $T$ and $\\rho$, respectively. If angle of contact between cube and water surface is zero, find the distance $h$ (in metres) between the lower face of cube and surface of the water.','[{"text": "$\\\\dfrac{m g+a T}{a^{2} \\\\rho g}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{m g+4 a T}{a^{2} \\\\rho g}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{m g+2 a T}{a^{2} \\\\rho g}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{4 a T}{a \\\\rho g}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'At equilibrium $m g+F_{S T}=F_{b}$<br>$m g+4 a T=a^{2} h \\rho g$<br><img src=\'/img/data/physics/26_38235_explanation_1.jpg\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' /> <br>$h=\\dfrac{m g+4 a T}{a^{2} \\rho g}$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/26_38235_question_1.png'),
(38237,'physics',29,1301,3147,'A cylinder of capacity 20 litres is filled with ${H_2}$ gas. The total average kinetic energy of translatory motion of its molecules is $1.5 \\times {10^5}\\,J$. The pressure of hydrogen in the cylinder is','[{"text": "$2 \\\\times {10^6}\\\\,N{\\\\rm{/}}{m^2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$3 \\\\times {10^6}\\\\,N{\\\\rm{/}}{m^2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$4 \\\\times {10^6}\\\\,N{\\\\rm{/}}{m^2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$5 \\\\times {10^6}\\\\,N{\\\\rm{/}}{m^2}$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'$E=\\dfrac{3}{2} P V$<br>$ = \\frac{{2E}}{{3V}} = \\frac{{2 \\times 1.5 \\times {{10}^5}}}{{3 \\times 20 \\times {{10}^{ - 3}}}}$<br>$ = \\frac{1}{{20}} \\times {10^8} = 5 \\times {10^6}\\,N\\,{m^{ - 2}}.$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38238,'physics',27,1382,3319,'Two rods of same length and transfer a given amount of heat in 12 second, when they are joined as shown in figure (1)<br>But when they are joined as shown in figure(2) then they will transfer same heat in same conditions in','[{"text": "${\\\\text{24  }}s$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\text{13  }}s$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\text{15  }}s$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\text{48  }}s$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'$Q=$ constant<br>$\\frac{{K\\Delta \\theta {t_1}\\left( {{A_1} + {A_2}} \\right)}}{l} = \\frac{{K\\Delta \\theta {t_2}A}}{{{l_1} + {l_2}}}$<br>$ \\Rightarrow \\frac{{{t_1}2A}}{l} = \\frac{{{t_2}A}}{{2l}}$<br>$({\\rm{by}}\\,{\\rm{data }}{l_1} = {l_2} = l,{A_1} = {A_2} = A)$<br>$ \\Rightarrow 12 \\times 2 \\times 2 = {t_2}$<br>$ \\Rightarrow {t_2} = 48\\,\\sec $',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2021,'MHTCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/27_38238_question_1.jpg'),
(38239,'physics',26,1138,2731,'The inside diameters of the two arms of a Utube are $1.5\\,mm$ and $1.0\\,mm$ respectively. Now if it is partially filled with water having surface tension of $0.0736\\,N/m$ and zero contact angle. What will be the difference in the levels of the two arms.','[{"text": "$10\\\\,mm$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$5\\\\,mm$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$4\\\\,mm$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2\\\\,mm$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Let $P_{0}$ is the atmospheric pressure and $P_{1}$ and $P_{2}$ are the pressures just below the surfaces. As the liquid is in equilibrium the pressure is same at everywhere in the horizontal tube.<br><img src=\'/img/data/physics/26_38239_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' /> <br>${P_0} - {P_1} = \\frac{{2T\\cos \\theta }}{{{r_1}}}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\left( 1 \\right)$<br>${P_0} - {P_2} = \\frac{{2T\\cos \\theta }}{{{r_2}}}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\left( 2 \\right)$<br>${P_L} = {P_R}\\,\\& \\,\\,\\theta  = 0^\\circ $<br>${P_1} + \\rho g{h_1} = {P_2} + \\rho g{h_2}$<br>${P_0} - \\frac{{2T}}{{{r_1}}} + \\rho g{h_1} = {P_0} - \\frac{{2T}}{{{r_2}}} + \\rho g{h_2}$<br>${h_2} - {h_1} = \\frac{{2T}}{{\\rho g}}\\left[ {\\frac{1}{{{r_2}}} - \\frac{1}{{{r_1}}}} \\right]$<br>${r_1} = \\frac{{1.0}}{2}\\,mm\\,\\,\\,\\& {\\mkern 1mu} {\\kern 1pt} \\,\\,{\\mkern 1mu} {\\kern 1pt} {r_2} = \\frac{{1.5}}{2}\\,mm$<br>$T = 0.0736\\,N/m$<br>$ \\Rightarrow {h_2} - {h_1} = 10\\,mm$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/26_38239_question_1.png'),
(38241,'physics',29,1301,3148,'To what temperature should the hydrogen at $327^{\\circ}$ be cooled at constant pressure, so that the root mean square velocity of its molecules become half of its previous value.','[{"text": "$ - 123^\\\\circ C$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$ 23^\\\\circ C$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$ - 100^\\\\circ C$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$ 0^\\\\circ C$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'$V_{r m s} \\propto \\sqrt{T}$<br>$\\frac{{{C_1}}}{{{C_2}}} = \\sqrt {\\frac{{{T_1}}}{{{T_2}}}}  \\Rightarrow 2 = \\sqrt {\\frac{{{T_1}}}{{{T_2}}}} $<br>$ \\Rightarrow {T_2} = \\frac{{{T_1}}}{4} = \\frac{{600}}{4} = 150\\,K$<br>$ \\Rightarrow {T_2} =  - 123^\\circ C.$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38261,'physics',29,1301,3148,'The average translational kinetic energy and the rms speed of molecules of a sample of oxygen gas at $300\\,K$ are $6.21 \\times {10^{ - 21}}\\,J$ and $484\\,m{s^{ - 1}}$ respectively. The corresponding values at $600\\,K$ are nearly (assuming ideal gas behaviour)','[{"text": "$12.42 \\\\times {10^{ - 21}}\\\\,J,968\\\\,m{s^{ - 1}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$8.78 \\\\times {10^{ - 21}}\\\\,J,684\\\\,m{s^{ - 1}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$6.21 \\\\times {10^{ - 21}}\\\\,J,968\\\\,m{s^{ - 1}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$12.42 \\\\times {10^{ - 21}}\\\\,J,682\\\\,m{s^{ - 1}}$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'$E \\propto T$ and $v_{r m s} \\propto \\sqrt{T}$<br>$\\frac{{{E_1}}}{{{E_2}}} = \\frac{{300}}{{600}} \\Rightarrow {E_2} = 2{E_1} = 12.42 \\times {10^{ - 21}}\\,J$<br>$\\frac{{{v_1}}}{{{v_2}}} = \\sqrt {\\frac{{{T_1}}}{{{T_2}}}}  \\Rightarrow {v_2} = \\sqrt 2 {v_1}$<br>$ = 1.41 \\times 484 = 682.44\\,m{\\rm{/}}s.$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38262,'physics',27,1380,3307,'A clock while keeps correct time at $ 30^\\circ C$ has a pendulum rod made of brass. The number of seconds it gains (or) loses per second when the temperature falls to $ 10^\\circ C$ is<br>$\\left[\\alpha\\right.$ brass $\\left. { = 18 \\times {{10}^{ - 6}}/^\\circ C} \\right]$','[{"text": "$18 \\\\times {10^{ - 6}}\\\\,\\\\sec $", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$18 \\\\times {10^{ - 5}}\\\\,{\\\\rm{sec}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$0.0018{\\\\mkern 1mu} \\\\,{\\\\rm{sec}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$9 \\\\times {10^{ - 6}}\\\\,{\\\\rm{sec}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'The loss or gain in time per day is<br>$\\begin{aligned}\\dfrac{\\Delta T}{T} & =\\dfrac{1}{2} \\alpha \\Delta \\theta \\\\& =\\dfrac{1}{2} \\times 18 \\times 10^{-6} \\times 20 \\\\& =180 \\times 10^{-6}=18 \\times 10^{-5} .\\end{aligned}$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38264,'physics',29,1301,3148,'Five gas molecules chosen at random are found to have speeds of $500,600,700,800$ and $900\\,m{\\rm{/}}s$. Then:','[{"text": "The rms speed and the average speed are the same.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "The average speed is $474\\\\,m{\\\\rm{/}}s$ higher than the rms speed.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "The rms speed is $474\\\\,m{\\\\rm{/}}s$ lower than the average speed.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "The rms speed is $14\\\\,m{\\\\rm{/}}s$ higher than the average speed.", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'$v_{r m s}=\\sqrt{\\dfrac{\\sum v^{2}}{n}}, v_{\\text {avg }}=\\dfrac{\\sum v}{n}$<br>${v_{{\\rm{avg }}}} = \\frac{{500 + 600 + 700 + 800 + 900}}{5}$<br>$ = 20 \\times 35 = 700\\,m{\\rm{/}}s$<br>$\\sqrt { < {v^2} > }  = 100\\sqrt {\\frac{{25 + 36 + 49 + 64 + 81}}{5}} $<br>$ = 100\\sqrt {51}  = 714\\,m{\\rm{/}}s$<br>So rms speed is $14\\,\\,m{\\rm{/}}s$ is higher than average speed.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38265,'physics',27,1380,3306,'Two metal rods have coefficients of linear expansion $1.1 \\times {10^{ - 5}}/^\\circ C$ and $1.65 \\times {10^{ - 5}}/^\\circ C$ respectively. The difference in lengths is $10{\\rm{ }}cm$ at all temperatures. Their initial lengths must be respectively .','[{"text": "$40\\\\,cm\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\rm{and}}\\\\,\\\\,50\\\\,cm$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$40\\\\,cm\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\rm{and}}\\\\,\\\\,30\\\\,cm$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$50\\\\,cm\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\rm{and}}\\\\,\\\\,60\\\\,cm$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$30\\\\,cm\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\rm{and}}\\\\,\\\\,20\\\\,cm$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Given that the difference in lengths remain same<br>$\\Delta l_{1}=\\Delta l_{2}$<br>$l_{1} \\alpha_{1} \\Delta \\theta=l_{2} \\alpha_{2} \\Delta \\theta$<br>$ \\Rightarrow {l_1}{\\alpha _1} = {l_2}{\\alpha _2}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\left( 1 \\right)$<br>${l_1} - {l_2} = 10{\\rm{ }}({\\rm{Given}})\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\left( 2 \\right)$<br>From eq\'s (1) and (2)<br>$\\Rightarrow l_{1}-\\dfrac{l_{1} \\alpha_{1}}{\\alpha_{2}}=10$<br>$ \\Rightarrow {l_1} = \\frac{{10{\\alpha _2}}}{{{\\alpha _2} - {\\alpha _1}}} = \\frac{{10 \\times 1.65 \\times {{10}^{ - 5}}}}{{0.55 \\times {{10}^{ - 5}}}} = 30\\,cm$<br>$ \\Rightarrow {l_2} = 20\\,cm$.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38267,'physics',26,1138,2731,'Wax is coated on the inner wall of a capillary tube and the tube is then dipped in water. Then, compared to the unwaxed capillary, the angle of contact $\\theta$ and the height $h$ upto which water rises change. These changes are:','[{"text": "$\\\\theta$ increases and $h$ also increases", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\theta$ decreases and $h$ also decreases", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\theta$ increaes and $h$ decreases", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\theta$ decreases and $h$ increases", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'The capillary rise<br>$h=\\dfrac{2 T \\cos \\theta}{\\rho r g}$<br>When water is on a waxy or oily surface $\\cos \\theta$ is negative i.e., $90^{\\circ} < \\theta < 180^{\\circ}$ i.e., angle of contact $\\theta$ increases And for $\\theta>90^{\\circ}$ liquid level in capillary tube fall i.e., $h$ decreases.',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',2013,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38268,'physics',29,1301,3149,'The respective speeds of five molecules are 2 , $1.5,1.6,1.6$ and$1.2\\,km{\\rm{/sec}}$. The most probable speed in $km{\\rm{/sec}}$ will be','[{"text": "2", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "1.58", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "1.6", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "1.31", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'$V_{r m s}=\\sqrt{\\dfrac{3}{2}} V_{m p}$<br>$\\Rightarrow V_{m p}=\\sqrt{\\dfrac{2}{3}} V_{r m s}$<br>$V_{r m s}=\\sqrt{\\dfrac{4+2.25+2.56+2.56+1.44}{5}}=\\sqrt{2.562}$<br>$ \\Rightarrow {V_{mp}} = \\sqrt {\\frac{2}{3} \\times 2.562}  = \\sqrt {1.708}  = 1.30\\,km{\\rm{/}}s$<br>$ \\approx 1.31\\,$So correct option is (4).',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38269,'physics',27,1380,3306,'A bimetal made of copper and iron strips welded together is straight at room temperature. It is held vertically so that the iron strip is towards the left hand and copper strip is towards right hand. The bimetal strip is then heated. The bimetal strip will $\\left( {{\\alpha _{cu}} > {\\alpha _{Fe}}} \\right)$','[{"text": "Remain straight", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Bend towards right", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Bend towards left", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Have no change", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'<img src=\'/img/data/physics/27_38269_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' /> More a metal bend on convex shape when heated. So correct option is (3).',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38271,'physics',26,1138,2731,'This question has statement- 1 and statement-2. Of the four choices given after the statements, choose the one that best describes the two statements.<br>Statement-1: A capillary is dipped in a liquid and liquid rises to a height $h$ in it. As temperature of the liquid is raised, the height $h$ increases (if the density of the liquid and the angle of contact remain the same).<br>Statement-2: Surface tension of a liquid decreases with the rise in its temperature.','[{"text": "Statement-1 is ture, Statement-2 is true; Statement-2 is not the correct explanation for Statement-1.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Statement-1, is false, Statement-2 is true", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Statement-1 is true, Statement-2 is false.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Statement-1 is ture, Statement-2 is true; Statement- 2 is the correct explanation for Statement-1.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Surface tension of a liquid decreases with the rise in temperature. At the boiling point of liquid, surface tension is zero.<br>Capillary rise $h=\\dfrac{2 T \\cos \\theta}{r d g}$<br>As surface tension $T$ decreases with rise in temperature hence capillary rise also decreases.',NULL,'Hard','MCQ type-2',2013,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38272,'physics',29,1301,3148,'The root mean square speed of hydrogen molecule is $v_{1}$ at temperature $T K$. The most probable speed of Oxygen molecule is $v_{2}$ at same temperature. Then $\\dfrac{v_{1}}{v_{2}}$ is equal to:','[{"text": "4", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$2 \\\\sqrt{6}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$3 \\\\sqrt{6}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "2", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'For hydrogen ${v_H} = \\sqrt {\\frac{{3RT}}{{{M_H}}}} \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\left( 1 \\right)$<br>For oxygen ${v_0} = \\sqrt {\\frac{{3RT}}{{{M_0}}}} \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\left( 2 \\right)$<br>Given $v_{H}=v_{1}$ and $v_{0}=v_{2}$ at same temperature<br>$\\begin{gathered}\\therefore \\dfrac{v_{1}}{v_{2}}=\\sqrt{\\dfrac{M_{0}}{M_{H}}} \\\\M_{0}=32, M_{H}=2 \\\\\\therefore \\dfrac{v_{1}}{v_{2}}=\\sqrt{\\dfrac{32}{2}}=\\sqrt{16}=4 .\\end{gathered}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38273,'physics',27,1380,3306,'If a cylinder of diameter $1.0\\,cm$ at $30^\\circ C$ is to be slid into a hole of diameter $0.9997\\,cm$ in a steel plate present at the same temperature, the minimum required rise in the temperature of the plate is : (Coefficient of linear expansion of steel $\\left. { = 12 \\times {{10}^{ - 6}}/^\\circ C} \\right)$','[{"text": "$25^\\\\circ C$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$35^\\\\circ C$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$45^\\\\circ C$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$55^\\\\circ C$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'$r_{2}=r_{1}(1+\\alpha \\Delta \\theta) \\Rightarrow \\Delta r=r \\alpha(\\Delta \\theta)$<br>$\\Delta r = 0.5\\,cm - 0.49985\\,cm = 1.5 \\times {10^{ - 4}}\\,cm$<br>$1.5 \\times {10^{ - 4}}\\,cm = 0.5\\,cm \\times 12 \\times {10^{ - 6}} \\times \\Delta \\theta $<br>$ \\Rightarrow \\Delta \\theta  = 25^\\circ C$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38275,'physics',26,1138,2730,'A capillary tube is immersed vertically in water and the height of the water column is \\[x\\]. When this arrangement is taken into a mine of depth \\[d\\], the height of the water column is \\[y\\]. If \\[R\\] is the radius of earth, the ratio \\(\\dfrac{x}{y}\\) is :<div class="matching-question"></div>','[{"text": "$\\\\left( {1 - \\\\frac{{2d}}{R}} \\\\right)$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\left( {1 - \\\\frac{d}{R}} \\\\right)$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\left( {\\\\frac{{R - d}}{{R + d}}} \\\\right)$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\left( {\\\\frac{{R + d}}{{R - d}}} \\\\right)$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'The height rised by the liquid is<br>$h=\\dfrac{2 T}{\\rho r g}$<br>On the surface of the earth $x=\\dfrac{2 T}{\\rho r g}$<br>At a depth $d$ below the surface of earth the height rised is<br>$\\begin{aligned}& y=\\dfrac{2 T}{\\rho r g^{\\prime}} \\\\& \\dfrac{x}{y}=\\dfrac{g^{\\prime}}{g}\\end{aligned}$<br>where $g^{\\prime}=\\dfrac{G M}{R^{3}}[R-d]=g\\left[1-\\dfrac{d}{R}\\right]$<br>$\\dfrac{x}{y}=\\left[1-\\dfrac{d}{R}\\right]$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',2014,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38276,'physics',29,1303,3152,'A diatomic gas molecule has translational, rotational and vibrational degrees of freedom.<br>Then $\\dfrac{C_{P}}{C_{V}}$ is','[{"text": "1.67", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "1.4", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "1.33", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "1.29", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'For a diatomic gas having translational, rotational and vibrational degrees of freedom.<br>$C_{V}=\\dfrac{7 R}{2}, C_{P}=\\dfrac{9 R}{2} \\Rightarrow \\dfrac{C_{P}}{C_{V}}=\\dfrac{9}{7}=1.29$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38277,'physics',27,1380,3306,'The diameter of iron wheel is $1\\,cm$. If its temperature is increased by $700^\\circ C$ what is the increase in circumference of the wheel?<br>$\\left( {\\alpha  = 12 \\times {{10}^{ - 6}}/^\\circ C} \\right)$','[{"text": "$0.0264\\\\,cm$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$0.264\\\\,cm$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2.64\\\\,cm$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$26.4\\\\,cm$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Increase is circumference $=\\pi D \\alpha \\Delta t$ where $D=$ diameter of the wheel<br>circumference, $C = \\pi D$<br>Increase is circumference, $\\Delta C = \\left( {\\alpha \\,\\Delta \\,T} \\right)\\pi D$<br>$ = 12 \\times {10^{ - 6}} \\times 400 \\times \\pi  \\times 1\\,cm$<br>$ = 0.0264\\,cm$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38278,'physics',26,1138,2731,'Two identical soap bubbles are combined isothermally to form a big bubble of radius $R$. If $V$ and $S$ are changes in volume and area and $P_{o}$ is atmospheric pressure then the correct relation is','[{"text": "$3 P_{o} V+4 T S=0$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$P_{o} V+T S=0$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$P_{o} V+4 T S=0$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$4 P_{o} V+3 T S=0$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'As the number of moles remain constant<br>$\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,n = {n_1} + {n_2}$<br>$\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\frac{{PV}}{{RT}} = \\frac{{{P_1}{V_1}}}{{RT}} + \\frac{{{P_2}{V_2}}}{{RT}}$<br>$\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,PV = {P_1}{V_1} + {P_2}{V_2}$<br>$\\left( {{P_0} + \\frac{{4T}}{R}} \\right)\\frac{4}{3}\\pi {R^3} = \\left( {{P_0} + \\frac{{4T}}{r}} \\right)\\frac{4}{3}\\pi {r^3}$<br>$\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, + \\left( {{P_0} + \\frac{{4T}}{r}} \\right)\\frac{4}{3}\\pi {r^3}$<br>$\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{P_0}\\left( {\\frac{8}{3}\\pi {r^3} - \\frac{4}{3}\\pi {R^3}} \\right) = \\frac{{4T}}{3}\\left( {4\\pi {R^2} - 8\\pi {r^2}} \\right)$<br>$\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{P_0}V = \\frac{{ - 4T}}{3}S$<br>$\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,3{P_0}V + 4TS = 0$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',2014,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38280,'physics',29,1303,3152,'Which of the following statement given below are correct for monoatomic gas?<br>I. $C_{V}=\\dfrac{3}{2} R$<br>II. $C_{P}=\\dfrac{5}{2} R$<br>III. $C_{P}-C_{V}=2 R$<br>IV. $\\dfrac{C_{P}}{C_{V}}=\\dfrac{5}{3}$','[{"text": "I only", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "I, II and IV", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "III only", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "None", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'For a monoatomic gas having only translational degrees of freedom.<br>$C_{P}=\\dfrac{5}{2} R \\quad C_{V}=\\dfrac{3}{2} R \\quad \\therefore \\dfrac{C_{P}}{C_{V}}=\\dfrac{5}{3}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38281,'physics',27,1380,3310,'A thin copper wire of length $L$ increases in length by one percent when heated from<br>${t_1}^\\circ C$ and ${t_2}^\\circ C$. The percentage change in area when a thin copper plate having dimension $2 L \\times L$ is heated from ${t_1}^\\circ C$ to ${t_2}^\\circ C$ is','[{"text": "$1 \\\\%$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$3 \\\\%$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2 \\\\%$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$4 \\\\%$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'$\\dfrac{\\Delta l}{l} \\times 100=1$<br>$\\Rightarrow \\alpha(\\Delta t)=10^{-2}$<br>$\\Rightarrow \\dfrac{\\Delta A}{A} \\times 100=2 \\alpha(\\Delta t) 100=2$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38289,'physics',27,1380,3310,'The volume of a metal block is $4000\\,c{m^3}$ at 273 $K$. What would be its volume at $373 K$ ? (give $\\alpha$ of the metal is $18 \\times {10^{ - 6}}\\,{K^{ - 1}}$)','[{"text": "$5.001\\\\,{m^3}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$4.8 \\\\times {10^{ - 3}}\\\\,{m^3}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$4.002 \\\\times {10^{ - 3}}\\\\,{m^3}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$1.002 \\\\times {10^{ - 2}}\\\\,{m^3}$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'$V_{t}=V_{0}(1+\\gamma \\Delta T) \\Rightarrow V_{0}=4 \\times 10^{3} \\mathrm{~m}^{3}$<br>$\\alpha=\\dfrac{\\gamma}{3} \\Rightarrow \\gamma=3 \\alpha=3 \\times 18 \\times 10^{-6}$<br>$\\therefore V_{t}=V_{0}(1+\\gamma \\Delta T)$<br>$\\Rightarrow V_{t}=4 \\times 10^{-3}\\left(1+3 \\times 18 \\times 10^{-6} \\times 10^{2}\\right)$<br>$V=4 \\times 10^{-3}\\left(1+5.4 \\times 10^{-3}\\right)$<br>$V = 4.002 \\times {10^{ - 3}}\\,{m^3}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38282,'physics',26,1138,2728,'A large number of liquid drops each of radius \' $a$ \' coalesce to form a single spherical drop of radius \' $b$ \'. The energy released in the process is converted into kinetic energy of the big drop formed. The speed of big drop is (The density of liquid is $\\rho$ and surface tension is $T$ )','[{"text": "$\\\\sqrt {\\\\frac{{6T}}{\\\\rho }\\\\left[ {\\\\frac{1}{a} - \\\\frac{1}{b}} \\\\right]} $", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\sqrt {\\\\frac{{4T}}{\\\\rho }\\\\left[ {\\\\frac{1}{a} - \\\\frac{1}{b}} \\\\right]} $", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\sqrt {\\\\frac{{8T}}{\\\\rho }\\\\left[ {\\\\frac{1}{a} - \\\\frac{1}{b}} \\\\right]} $", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\sqrt {\\\\frac{{5T}}{\\\\rho }\\\\left[ {\\\\frac{1}{a} - \\\\frac{1}{b}} \\\\right]} $", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Let $N$ be the number of drops each having radius \' $a$ \'. When they coalesce the total volume remains constant<br>$\\begin{aligned}& N \\dfrac{4}{3} \\pi a^{3}=\\dfrac{4}{3} \\pi b^{3} \\\\& b=a N^{1 / 3}\\end{aligned}$<br>The change in surface area is<br>$\\Delta A=N 4 \\pi a^{2}-4 \\pi b^{2}$<br>$\\Delta A=4 \\pi\\left[N a^{2}-a^{2} N^{2 / 3}\\right]=4 \\pi a^{2}\\left[N-N^{2 / 3}\\right]$<br>The change in K.E $=T \\Delta A=4 \\pi a^{2} T\\left[N-N^{2 / 3}\\right]$ <br>$\\begin{aligned}& \\dfrac{1}{2}\\left(\\rho \\dfrac{4}{3} \\pi b^{3}\\right) V^{2}=\\dfrac{1}{2}\\left(N \\rho \\dfrac{4}{3} \\pi a^{3}\\right) V^{2} \\\\& =4 \\pi a^{2} T\\left[N-N^{2 / 3}\\right] \\\\& \\Rightarrow V=\\sqrt{\\dfrac{6 T}{a \\rho}\\left[1-\\dfrac{1}{N^{2 / 3}}\\right]}=\\sqrt{\\dfrac{6 T}{\\rho a}\\left[1-\\dfrac{a}{b}\\right]} \\\\& \\Rightarrow V=\\sqrt{\\dfrac{6 T}{\\rho}\\left[\\dfrac{1}{a}-\\dfrac{1}{b}\\right]} \\\\& \\left(b=a N^{1 / 3}\\right)\\end{aligned}$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',2014,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38284,'physics',29,1303,3152,'How much heat energy in joules must be supplied to $14\\,g$ of ${N_2}$ at room temperature to rise its temperature by $40^\\circ C$ at constant pressure?','[{"text": "$50\\\\,R$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$60\\\\,R$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$70\\\\,R$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$80\\\\,R$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'$Q_{\\text {constant } P}=n C_{P} \\Delta T$<br>$\\begin{aligned}& =\\dfrac{1}{2} \\times \\dfrac{7}{2} R \\times 40 \\\\& =70 R\\end{aligned}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38285,'physics',27,1380,3309,'A rod is fixed between two rigid supports at $50^\\circ C$. The coefficient of linear expansion of material of $rod\\,2 \\times {10^{ - 5}}/^\\circ C$ and young\'s modulus is $1.5 \\times {10^{11}}\\,N{\\rm{/}}{m^2}$. What is the stress developed in the rod if temperature of rod becomes $70^\\circ C$?','[{"text": "$3 \\\\times {10^4}\\\\,N{\\\\rm{/}}{m^2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$6 \\\\times {10^7}\\\\,N{\\\\rm{/}}{m^2}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$9 \\\\times {10^{12}}\\\\,N{\\\\rm{/}}{m^2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$11 \\\\times {10^{11}}\\\\,N{\\\\rm{/}}{m^2}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Stress developed in the rod due to heating is $\\gamma \\alpha \\Delta \\theta$<br>Stress $=2 \\times 10^{-5} \\times 1.5 \\times 10^{11} \\times 20^{\\circ}$<br>$ = 40 \\times 1.5 \\times {10^6} = 6 \\times {10^7}\\,N{\\rm{/}}{m^2}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38287,'physics',26,1138,2730,'An air bubble of radius $0.1\\,cm$ is in a liquid having surface tension $0.06\\,N/m$ and density ${10^3}\\,kg/{m^3}$. The pressure inside the bubble is $1100N{m^{ - 2}}$ greater than the atmospheric pressure. At what depth is the bubble below the surface of the liquid? $\\left( {g = 9.8\\,m{s^{ - 2}}} \\right)$','[{"text": "$0.1\\\\,m$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$0.25\\\\,m$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$0.20\\\\,m$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$0.15\\\\,m$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Let $h$ is the depth of the bubble. $P_{a}$ is the atmospheric pressure. The pressure inside the bubble.<br>${P_2} = {P_a} + 1100\\,\\,({\\rm{ Given }})\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\left( 1 \\right)$<br>We know that<br>${P_2} - {P_1} = \\frac{{2T}}{r}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\left( 2 \\right)$<br>${P_1} = {P_a} + \\rho gh\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\left( 3 \\right)$<br>Where $P_{2} \\& P_{1}$ are the pressures outside & inside the bubbles<br>From the above equations<br>$\\rho gh + \\frac{{2T}}{r} = 1100$<br>$h \\times {10^3} \\times 4.8 = 1100 - \\frac{{2 \\times 0.06}}{{0.1 \\times {{10}^{ - 2}}}} = 980$<br>$h = \\frac{{980}}{{9.8 \\times {{10}^3}}} = 0.1\\,m$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',2014,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38288,'physics',29,1303,3152,'At ordinary temperature, the molecules of an ideal gas have only translational and rotational kinetic energies. At high temperature they may also have vibrational energy as a result of this at high temperature.','[{"text": "$C_{V} < \\\\dfrac{3 R}{2}$, monoatomic", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$C_{V}=\\\\dfrac{3 R}{2}$, monoatomic", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$C_{V} < \\\\dfrac{5 R}{2}$, diatomic", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$C_{V}=\\\\dfrac{5 R}{2}$, diatomic", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'$C_{V}=\\dfrac{f R}{2}$, degree of freedom for diatomic gas and polyatomic gas at room temperature is $f$ but at higher temperature their degree of freedom increases by one due to vibrational motion of molecule for diatomic gas and more then one for polyatomic gas. but there is no effect of by on degree of freedom of ideal gas or monoatomic gas. A $f = 3$ for monoatomic gas.<br>$\\therefore {C_V} = \\frac{3}{2}R$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38290,'physics',28,1371,3290,'Two rigid boxes containing different ideal gases are placed on table. Box $A$ contains one mole of nitrogen at temperature $T_{0}$, while box $B$ contains one mole of helium at temperature $(7 / 3) T_{0}$. The boxes are then put into thermal contact with each other, and heat flows between them until the gases reach a common final temperature (ignore the heat capacity of boxes). Then, the final temperature of the gases $T_{f}$ in terms of $T_{0}$ is','[{"text": "$T_{f}=\\\\dfrac{3}{7} T_{0}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$T_{f}=\\\\dfrac{7}{3} T_{0}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$T_{f}=\\\\dfrac{3}{2} T_{0}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$T_{f}=\\\\dfrac{5}{2} T_{0}$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'The system of two rigid boxes is shown in figure   <img src=\'/img/data/physics/28_38290_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />  We know that, $\\Delta U=\\dfrac{1}{2} f n R \\Delta T$<br>$n=1$<br>$f=5$ for nitrogen and $f=3$ for helium<br>Change in internal energy at box $A$,<br>i.e. $\\Delta U_{A}=1 \\times \\dfrac{5 R}{2}\\left(T_{f}-T_{0}\\right)$<br>Change in internal energy at box $B$,<br>i.e. $\\Delta U_{B}=1 \\times \\dfrac{3 R}{2}\\left(T_{f}-\\dfrac{7}{3} T_{0}\\right)$<br>Now, $\\Delta U_{A}+\\Delta U_{B}=0$<br>$\\begin{aligned}& \\dfrac{5 R}{2}\\left(T_{f}-T_{0}\\right)+\\dfrac{3 R}{2}\\left(T_{f}-\\dfrac{7 T_{0}}{3}\\right)=0 \\\\& 5 T_{f}-5 T_{0}+3 T_{f}-7 T_{0}=0 \\\\& \\Rightarrow 8 T_{f}=12 T_{0} \\\\& \\Rightarrow T_{f}=\\dfrac{12}{8} T_{0}=\\dfrac{3}{2} T_{0}\\end{aligned}$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',2010,'AIIMS','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38291,'physics',26,1138,2732,'It two glass plates have water between them and are separated by very small distance (see figure), it is very diffucult to pull them apart. It is because the water in between forms cylindrical surface on the side that gives rise to lower pressure in the water in comparison to atmosphere. If the radius of the cylindrical surface is $R$ and surface tension of water is $T$ then the pressure in water between the plates is lower by :','[{"text": "$\\\\dfrac{T}{4 R}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{2 T}{R}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{4 T}{R}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{T}{R}$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Let $r$ be the radius of the liquid coverage<br><img src=\'/img/data/physics/26_38291_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' /> <br>The pressure difference between inside and outside the liquid is<br>$\\Delta P=T\\left[\\dfrac{1}{r} \\sim \\dfrac{1}{R}\\right]$<br>As $r >  > R \\Rightarrow \\frac{1}{r} \\sim \\frac{1}{R} \\approx \\frac{1}{R}$<br>$\\Delta P \\approx \\dfrac{T}{R}$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',2015,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/26_38291_question_1.png'),
(38292,'physics',29,1303,3152,'The molar specific heat at constant pressure of an ideal gas is $\\dfrac{7}{2} R$. The ratio of specific heat at constant pressure to that at constant volume is','[{"text": "$\\\\dfrac{7}{5}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{8}{7}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{5}{7}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{9}{7}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Use Mayer\'s equation<br>$\\begin{aligned}& C_{P}-C_{V}=R \\\\& C_{V}=\\dfrac{7}{2} R-R=\\dfrac{5}{2} R \\\\& \\gamma=\\dfrac{C_{P}}{C_{V}}=\\dfrac{\\dfrac{7}{2} R}{\\dfrac{5}{2} R} \\Rightarrow \\gamma=\\dfrac{7}{5}\\end{aligned}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38293,'physics',27,1380,3310,'A sheet of brass is $100\\,cm$ long and $40\\,cm$ broad at $273\\,K$. The area of the surface increase by 2 $c{m^2}$ at $100^\\circ C$. What is the coefficient of linear expansion of brass?','[{"text": "$5 \\\\times {10^{ - 6}}^\\\\circ {C^{ - 1}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2.5 \\\\times {10^{ - 6}}^\\\\circ {C^{ - 1}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$3 \\\\times {10^{ - 6}}^\\\\circ {C^{ - 1}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2 \\\\times {10^{ - 5}}^\\\\circ {C^{ - 1}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'<br>$\\frac{{\\Delta A}}{{{A_0}\\Delta T}} = \\beta  = \\frac{2}{{4000 \\times 100}} = 5 \\times {10^{ - 6}}^\\circ {C^{ - 1}}$<br>$\\alpha  = \\frac{\\beta }{2} = 2.5 \\times {10^{ - 6}}{\\rm{/}}^\\circ C$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38294,'physics',26,1138,2728,'The amount of energy required to form a soap bubble of radius $2\\,cm$ from a soap solution is nearly: (surface tension of soap solution $=0.03$ $N m^{-1}$ )','[{"text": "$5.06 \\\\times {10^{ - 4}}\\\\,J$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$3.01 \\\\times {10^{ - 4}}\\\\,J$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$50.1 \\\\times {10^{ - 4}}\\\\,J$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$30.16 \\\\times {10^{ - 4}}\\\\,J$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Soap bubble has two surfaces.<br>$E = 2T\\left( {4\\pi {R^2}} \\right) = 8\\pi T{R^2}$<br>$ = 8(0.03)(3.14){\\left( {2 \\times {{10}^{ - 2}}} \\right)^2}$<br>$ = 3.01 \\times {10^{ - 4}}\\,J.$<br>Correct option is (2).',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2023,'NEET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38295,'physics',28,1371,3289,'A perfect gas goes from state $A$ to state $B$ by absorbing $8 \\times {10^5}\\,J$ of heat and doing $6.5 \\times {10^5}\\,J$ of external work. It is now transferred between the same two states in another process in which it absorbs ${10^5}\\,J$ of heat. In the second process,','[{"text": "Work done on the gas is ${10^5}\\\\,J$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Work done on the gas is $0.5 \\\\times {10^5}\\\\,J$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Work done by the gas is ${10^5}\\\\,J$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Work done by the gas is $0.5 \\\\times {10^5}\\\\,J$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'According to first law of thermodynamics,<br>$d U=d Q-d W$<br>$dU = 8 \\times {10^5} - 6.5 \\times {10^5} = 1.5 \\times {10^5}\\,J$<br>In the second process, $d U$ remains the same.<br>$\\therefore d W=d Q-d U$<br>$=1 \\times 10^{5}-1.5 \\times 10^{5}$<br>$dW =  - 0.5 \\times {10^5}\\,J \\simeq 0.5 \\times {10^5}\\,J$<br>$\\therefore$ Work done on the gas is $0.5 \\times {10^5}\\,J$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',2011,'AIIMS','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38296,'physics',29,1303,3152,'A container contain $0.1\\,mol$ of $H_{2}$ and $0.1\\,mol$ of ${O_2}$. If the gases are in thermal equilibrium then','[{"text": "Only the average kinetic energy of the molecule of ${H_2}$ and ${O_2}$ is same.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Average speed of the molecule of ${H_2}$ and ${O_2}$ is same.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Only the specific heat at constant pressure of two gases is same.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "The specific heat at constant pressure and the kinetic energy are same for both the gases.", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'The specific heat at constant pressure $\\left(C_{P}\\right)$ is the amount of heat required to raise the temperature of one gram through $1^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C}$ when the pressure of the gas is kept constant. Again, the mean kinetic energy per molecule (3/2) $k T$ depends only upon temperature. Clearly both the specific heats at constant pressure and mean kinetic energy are depending on the temperature which is again same for the two gases.',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38297,'physics',27,1380,3310,'Ratio among linear expansion coefficient $(\\alpha)$, areal expansion $(\\beta)$ and volume expansion coefficient $(\\gamma)$ is','[{"text": "$1: 2: 3$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$3: 2: 1$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2: 3: 1$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$3: 1: 2$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'<br>$\\alpha=\\dfrac{\\beta}{2}=\\dfrac{\\gamma}{3} \\Rightarrow \\alpha: \\beta: \\gamma=1: 2: 3$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38298,'physics',28,1371,3282,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> In isothermal process, whole of the heat energy supplied to the body is converted into internal energy.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> According to the first law of thermodynamics, $\\Delta Q=\\Delta U+\\Delta W$','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'In an isothermal process, the temperature being constant, change in internal energy would be zero. Hence heat supplied would be utilised to do external mechanical work. So assertion is incorrect.<br>Reason is correct as the given equation represents first law of thermodynamics.<br>So, correct option is (4).',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',2018,'AIIMS','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38299,'physics',26,1136,2718,'The venturi-meter works on:','[{"text": "Bernoulli\'s principle", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "The principle of parallel axes", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "The principle of perpendicular axes", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Huygen\'s principle", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Venturimeter works on Bernoulli\'s principle. Correct option is (1).',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2023,'NEET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38300,'physics',29,1302,3150,'If at the same temperature and pressure, the densities of two diatomic gases are $d_{1}$ and $d_{2}$ respectively. The ratio of mean kinetic energy per molecules of gases will be','[{"text": "$1: 1$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$d_{1}: d_{2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\sqrt{d_{1}}: \\\\sqrt{d_{2}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\sqrt{d_{2}}: \\\\sqrt{d_{1}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'At a given temperature $(T)$ all the ideal gas molecules no matter what their masses, have the same average translational kinetic energy.<br>$E=\\dfrac{3}{2} K T$<br>So $E$ does not depend upon density $\\dfrac{E_{1}}{E_{2}}=\\dfrac{1}{1}$.',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38301,'physics',27,1380,3310,'A metal plate of area $1.2\\,{m^2}$ increases its area by $2.4 \\times {10^{ - 4}}\\,{m^2}$ when it is heated from $0^\\circ C$ to $100^\\circ C$. The coefficient of cubical expansion of the metal expressed in per $^\\circ C$ is','[{"text": "$2 \\\\times 10^{-6}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$4 \\\\times 10^{-6}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$6 \\\\times 10^{-6}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$3 \\\\times 10^{-6}$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'<br>$\\beta=\\dfrac{A_{2}-A_{1}}{A_{1}\\left(t_{2}-t_{1}\\right)}, \\gamma=\\dfrac{3}{2} \\beta$<br>$\\Rightarrow 2.4 \\times 10^{-4}=1.2 \\times \\dfrac{2 \\gamma}{3} \\times 100$<br>$\\Rightarrow \\gamma=3 \\times 10^{-6}$.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38302,'physics',28,1371,3289,'An ideal gas of mass $m$ in a state $A$ goes to another state $B$ via three different processes as shown in figure. If $Q_{1}, Q_{2}$ and $Q_{3}$ denote the heat absorbed by the gas along the three paths, then','[{"text": "$Q_{1} < Q_{2} < Q_{3}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$Q_{1} < Q_{2}=Q_{3}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$Q_{1}=Q_{2}>Q_{3}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$Q_{1}>Q_{2}>Q_{3}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Initial and final states are same in all the process.<br>Hence, $\\Delta U=0$ is same for each case.<br>$\\therefore \\Delta Q=\\Delta W$<br>Area enclosed by curve with volume,<br>$\\because(\\text { Area })_{1} < (\\text { Area })_{2} < (\\text { Area })_{3}$<br>$\\therefore Q_{1} < Q_{2} < Q_{3}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2018,'AIIMS','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/28_38302_question_1.png'),
(38303,'physics',26,1136,2717,'A closed water tank has cross-sectional area $A$. It has a small hole at a depth of $h$ from the free surface of water. The radius of the hole is $r$ so that $r <  < \\sqrt{\\dfrac{A}{\\pi}}$. If $P_{0}$ is the pressure inside the tank above water level, and $P_{a}$ is the atmospheric pressure, the rate of flow of the water coming out of the hole is $[\\rho$ is the density of water]','[{"text": "$\\\\pi r^{2} \\\\sqrt{2 g H}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\pi r^{2} \\\\sqrt{g h+\\\\dfrac{2\\\\left(P_{0}-P_{a}\\\\right)}{\\\\rho}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\pi r^{2} \\\\sqrt{2 g h}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\pi r^{2} \\\\sqrt{2 g h+\\\\dfrac{2\\\\left(P_{0}-P_{a}\\\\right)}{\\\\rho}}$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'$v=$ Velocity of efflux $=\\sqrt{2 g h+\\dfrac{2\\left(P_{0}-P_{a}\\right)}{\\rho}}$<br>Denote $Q=$ Rate of flow<br>From equation of continuity $Q=A v$.<br>$\\Rightarrow Q=\\left(\\pi r^{2}\\right)\\left[\\sqrt{2 g h+\\dfrac{2\\left(P_{0}-P_{a}\\right)}{\\rho}}\\right]$.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2023,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/26_38303_question_1.png'),
(38304,'physics',29,1304,3154,'Two perfect gases at absolute temperatures $T_{1}$ and $T_{2}$ are mixed. There is no loss of energy. The temperature of mixture if masses of molecules are $m_{1}$ and $m_{2}$ and the number of molecules in the gases are $n_{1}$ and $n_{2}$ respectively, is','[{"text": "$\\\\dfrac{T_{1}+T_{2}}{2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{n_{1} T_{1}+n_{2} T_{2}}{n_{1}+n_{2}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{n_{1} T_{2}+n_{2} T_{1}}{n_{1}+n_{2}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\sqrt{T_{1} T_{2} / n_{1} n_{2}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'We know, $E=\\dfrac{3}{2} n k T$<br>where, $k=$ Boltzmann\'s constant using energy conservation,<br>$\\begin{aligned}& \\therefore \\dfrac{3}{2} n_{1} k T_{1}+\\dfrac{3}{2} n_{2} k T_{2}=\\dfrac{3}{2}\\left(n_{1}+n_{2}\\right) k T \\\\& \\Rightarrow T=\\dfrac{n_{1} T_{1}+n_{2} T_{2}}{\\left(n_{1}+n_{2}\\right)}\\end{aligned}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38305,'physics',27,1380,3310,'The coefficient of linear expansion of a metal is $1 \\times {10^{ - 5}}/^\\circ C$. The percentage increase in area of a square plate of that metal when it is heated through $100^\\circ C$ is','[{"text": "$0.02 \\\\%$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$0.1 \\\\%$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$0.001 \\\\%$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$0.2 \\\\%$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'<br>$\\beta=2 \\alpha, \\dfrac{\\Delta A}{A} \\times 100=\\beta \\Delta t 100$<br>$=2 \\times 10^{-5} \\times 100 \\times 100=0.2$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38306,'physics',28,1371,3283,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Air quickly leaking out of a balloon becomes cooler.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> The leaking air undergoes adiabatic expansion','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Adiabatic expansion always follows cooling. So, correct option is (1).',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',2005,'AIIMS','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38341,'physics',28,1371,3288,'A thermodynamic process of uncontrolled change satisfying the equation $Q=W=0$, is<br>[ $Q=$ heat supplied, $W=$ work done $]$','[{"text": "cyclic", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "isothermal", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "isochroic", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "free expansion", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'As $Q=0 ; W=0$ so there is no-work done on the system or by the system. And hence this thermodynamic process is free expansion.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2022,'MHTCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38307,'physics',26,1133,2710,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Taking into account the fact that any object which floats must have an average density less than that of water, during world war $I$, a number of cargo vessels were made of concrete.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Concrete cargo vessels were filled with air.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'The assertion is correct because concrete cargo vessels were indeed constructed during World War I.<br>This reason is also true. But, the mere fact that concrete cargo vessels were filled with air doesn\'t directly explain why they floated. The correct explanation should emphasize the principle of buoyancy, which relates to the difference in density between the vessel and the water it displaces.<br>So, correct option is (2).',NULL,'Hard','Assertion and Reason',2007,'AIIMS','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38308,'physics',29,1304,3154,'A gas mixture contains 8 moles of oxygen and 2 moles of argon at room temperature $T$. The total internal energy of the mixture is:','[{"text": "$11\\\\,RT$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$23\\\\,RT$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$22\\\\,RT$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$26\\\\,RT$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'$U=\\dfrac{n_{1} f_{1} R T}{2}+\\dfrac{n_{2} f_{2} R T}{2}$<br>$=\\dfrac{8 \\times 5 R T}{2}+\\dfrac{2 \\times 3 R T}{2}=23 R T$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38309,'physics',27,1380,3310,'The coefficient of volumetric expansion of lamina is $5 \\times {10^5}/^\\circ C$. Its coefficient of linear expansion will be :','[{"text": "$1.6 \\\\times {10^{ - 5}}/^\\\\circ C$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$1.1 \\\\times {10^5}/^\\\\circ C$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2 \\\\times {10^{ - 6}}/^\\\\circ C$q", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$3 \\\\times 10/^\\\\circ C$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'<br>$\\alpha  = \\frac{\\gamma }{3} = \\frac{{5 \\times {{10}^{ - 5}}}}{3} = 1.6 \\times {10^{ - 5}}/^\\circ C$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38310,'physics',28,1371,3282,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Molar heat capacity cannot be defined for isothermal process.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> In isothermal process, $p-V$ versus $T$ graph is a dot.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are incorrect.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Here both assertion and reason are incorrect, since<br>a) Molar heat capacity can be defined for any quasi-static process<br>b) $P V$ vs $T$ graph is a straight line parallel to $P$ or $V$ or $P V$ axis.<br>So, correct option is (3).',NULL,'Hard','Assertion and Reason',2017,'AIIMS','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38311,'physics',26,1133,2710,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> A ship floats higher in the water on a high pressure day than on a low pressure day.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Floating of ship in the water is not possible because of buoyancy force which is present due to pressure difference.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are incorrect.", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Variations in atmospheric pressure do not significantly impact the level at which a ship floats. Assertion is false.<br>Ships float in water due to buoyancy, which is a result of the weight of the water displaced by the ship being equal to the weight of the ship itself. Atmospheric pressure is not a direct factor in the buoyancy of a ship.<br>So, correct option is (4)',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',2009,'AIIMS','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38312,'physics',29,1301,3148,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> The root mean square speed of the molecule of different ideal gases at the same temperature are the same.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Root mean square value of speed does depends on the temperature.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but reason is correct.", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'$v_{r m s}=\\sqrt{\\dfrac{r R T}{M}}$ It depends on both molecular weight of gas and temperature $\\left( T \\right)$ . Assertion is false and Reason is true. So, correct option is (4).',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38313,'physics',27,1382,3319,'The temperature of the two outer surfaces of a composite slab, consisting of two materials having coefficients of thermal conductivity $K$ and $2 K$ and thickness $x$ and $4 x$ respectively are $T_{2}$ and $T_{1}\\left(T_{2}>T_{1}\\right)$. The rate of heat transfer through the slab, in a steady state is<br>$\\left(\\dfrac{A\\left(T_{2}-T_{1}\\right) K}{x}\\right) f$, with $f$ equal to:','[{"text": "1", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1 / 2$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2 / 3$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1 / 3$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'For slabs in series, we have;<br>$R_{e q}=R_{1}+R_{2}$<br>i.e., $\\dfrac{5 x}{K_{e q} A}=\\dfrac{4 x}{2 K A}+\\dfrac{x}{K A}$<br>$K_{e q}=\\dfrac{5 K}{3}$<br>Now, in steady state, rate of heat transfer through the slab<br>$=\\dfrac{K_{e q} A\\left(T_{2}-T_{1}\\right)}{5 x}=\\left(\\dfrac{A\\left(T_{2}-T_{1}\\right) K}{x}\\right) f$<br>Putting the value of $K_{e q}$ we get;<br>$f=\\dfrac{1}{3}$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/27_38313_question_1.png'),
(38314,'physics',28,1371,3283,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> In adiabatic expansion process, the product of $p$ and $V$ always decreases.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> In adiabatic expansion process, work is done by the gas at the cost of internal energy of gas.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'In an adiabatic expansion temperature decreases, hence product $P V$ decreases<br>$(\\therefore P V=\\mu R T)$. So assertion is correct. Reason is also correct and supports assertion since work is done by the system at the cost of its internal energy.<br>So, correct choice is (1).',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',2017,'AIIMS','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38315,'physics',26,1134,2713,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> For Reynolds\' number $R_{e}>2000$, the flow of fluid is turbulent<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Inertial forces are dominant compared to the viscous forces at such high Reynolds\' numbers.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'$R_{e}=$ Reynold number $=\\dfrac{\\rho v D}{\\mu}$<br>Where $\\rho=$ fluid density<br>$v=$ velocity<br>$D=$ diameter of pipe<br>$\\mu=$ kinematic viscosity<br>Theoretically,<br>$R_{e}=\\dfrac{(\\text { Inertia-force })}{(\\text { Viscous-force })}$<br>Where intertia force is due to change is momentum of mass of flowing fluid.<br>Viscous force is "friction" type. Of these, when inertial force dominates, the fluid flows faster (as there is no significant opposing force).<br>$\\Rightarrow v$ will be large and $R_{e}$ will be greater than 2000 and flow is turbulent. So, correct option is (1).',NULL,'Hard','Assertion and Reason',2005,'AIIMS','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38316,'physics',29,1300,3145,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> On reducing the volume of a gas at constant temperature, the pressure of the gas increases.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> At constant temperature, according to Boyle\'s law, volume is inversely proportional to pressure.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Boyle\'s law states that at constant temperature, the product of pressure and volume is constant. So, when the volume decreases, the pressure must increase explains this behavior. So correct option is (1).',NULL,'Easy','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38317,'physics',27,1382,3318,'The coefficient of thermal conductivity of copper is nine times that of steel in the composite cylindrical bar shown in the figure. The temperature at the junctions of copper and steel is approximately equal to?','[{"text": "$61^\\\\circ C$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$62^\\\\circ C$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$65^\\\\circ C$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$66^\\\\circ C$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'According to the relation<br>$T = \\frac{{{K_1}{T_1}{\\ell _2} + {K_2}{T_2}{\\ell _1}}}{{{K_1}{\\ell _2} + {K_2}{\\ell _1}}}$<br>$T = \\frac{{9K \\times 80 \\times 10 + K \\times 0 + 20}}{{9K \\times 10 + K \\times 20}} = \\frac{{9K \\times 800}}{{90K + 20K}}$<br>$ = \\frac{{9K \\times 800}}{{110K}}$<br>$T = 65.4^\\circ C$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/27_38317_question_1.png'),
(38319,'physics',26,1137,2723,'If the terminal speed of a sphere of gold (density $\\left. { = 19.5kg{m^{ - 3}}} \\right)$ is $0.2\\,m{s^{ - 1}}$ in viscous liquid $\\left( {density = 1.5{\\mkern 1mu} kg{m^{ - 3}}} \\right)$, find the terminal speed of a sphere of silver (density $=10.5 \\mathrm{kgm}^{-3}$) of the same size in the same liquid.','[{"text": "$0.4\\\\,m{s^{ - 1}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$0.133\\\\,m{s^{ - 1}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$0.1\\\\,m{s^{ - 1}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$0.2\\\\,m{s^{ - 1}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Terminal speed of spherical body in a viscous liquid is given by<br>$v_{T}=\\dfrac{2 r^{2}(\\rho-\\sigma) g}{9 \\eta}$<br>where, $\\rho=$ density of material of solid body and $\\sigma=$ density of liquid.<br>From given data<br>$\\dfrac{v_{T}(\\text { Ag })}{v_{T}(\\text { Gold })}=\\dfrac{\\rho_{\\text {Ag }}-\\sigma_{1}}{\\rho_{\\text {Gold }}-\\sigma_{1}}$<br>(Since size $r$ and $\\eta$ of liquid are same for both spheres)<br>$ \\Rightarrow {v_T}(Ag) = \\left( {\\frac{{10.5 - 1.5}}{{19.5 - 1.5}}} \\right) \\times 0.2$<br>$ = \\frac{9}{{18}} \\times 0.2 = 0.1\\,m{s^{ - 1}}$<br>So, correct option is (3)',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2008,'AIIMS','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38320,'physics',29,1301,3148,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> The root mean square velocity of molecules of a gas having Maxwellian distribution of velocities is higher than their most probable velocity at any temperature.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> A very small number of molecules of a gas molecules which possess very large velocities.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Most probable velocity $v_{m p}=\\sqrt{\\dfrac{2 K_{B} T}{m}}$ maximum fraction of total number of molecules possess $v_{m p}$. But a few molecules have very large velocities. <img src=\'/img/data/physics/29_38320_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' /> $\\Rightarrow v_{r m s}>v_{m p}$<br>$v_{r m s}=\\sqrt{\\dfrac{3 K_{B} T}{m}}$<br>So correct option is (1).',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38321,'physics',27,1382,3318,'Three metal rods of the same material and identical in all respects are joined as shown in the figure. The temperature at the ends are maintained as indicated. Assuming no loss of heat from the curved surfaces of the rods, the temperature at the junction $X$ would be:','[{"text": "$45^\\\\circ C$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$60^\\\\circ C$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$30^\\\\circ C$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$20^\\\\circ C$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Let $T$ be the temperature at the junction.<br>Let $L$ and $A$ be the length and area of cross-section of each rod respectively.<br>$\\therefore$ Heat current from $Y$ to $X$ is<br>${H_1} = \\frac{{KA\\left( {90^\\circ C - T} \\right)}}{L}$<br>Heat current from $Z$ to $X$ is<br>${H_2} = \\frac{{KA\\left( {90^\\circ C - T} \\right)}}{L}$<br>Heat current from $X$ to $W$ is ${H_3}\\frac{{KA\\left( {T - 0^\\circ C} \\right)}}{L}$ At the junction $X$,<br>${H_1} + {H_2} = {H_3}\\,\\,\\,{\\mkern 1mu} {\\kern 1pt} \\therefore 90^\\circ C - T + 90^\\circ C - T = T$<br>$3T = 180^\\circ C$<br>$T = 60^\\circ C$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/27_38321_question_1.png'),
(38323,'physics',26,1136,2717,'Water is filled in a tank to a height of $3\\,m$. An orifice is made at a height $52.5\\,cm$ from bottom of tank. The ratio of the cross - sectional area of the orifice and the tank is 0.1 . The square of the speed of the liquid coming out from the orifice is (take, $g = 10\\,m{s^{ - 2}}$ )','[{"text": "$50\\\\,{m^2}\\\\,{s^{ - 2}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$50.5\\\\,{m^2}\\\\,{s^{ - 2}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$51\\\\,{m^2}\\\\,{s^{ - 2}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$52\\\\,{m^2}\\\\,{s^{ - 2}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Let $A=$ area cross section - of tank,<br>$a=$ area of cross - section of hole at B<br>$v=$ velocity with which level decreases<br>$v_{0}=$ velocity of efflux  <img src=\'/img/data/physics/26_38323_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />   From equation of continuity, $A v_{0}=a v$<br>$ \\Rightarrow v = \\frac{{a{v_0}}}{A}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\left( 1 \\right)$<br>We use Bernoulli\'s theorem.<br>$\\Rightarrow p_{0}+\\rho g h_{1}+\\dfrac{1}{2} \\rho v^{2}=p_{0}+\\rho g h_{2}+\\dfrac{1}{2} \\rho v_{0}^{2}$<br>$\\Rightarrow \\rho g\\left(h_{1}-h_{2}\\right)=\\dfrac{1}{2} \\rho\\left(v_{0}{ }^{2}-v^{2}\\right)$<br>Substituting the value of $v$ from Eq. (1), we get<br>$\\Rightarrow v_{0}^{2}=\\dfrac{2 g\\left(h_{1}-h_{2}\\right)}{1-(a / A)^{2}}$<br>$ \\Rightarrow v_0^2 = \\frac{{2 \\times 10 \\times (3 - 0.525)}}{{1 - {{(0.1)}^2}}} = 50\\,{m^2}\\,{s^{ - 2}}$<br>So, correct option is (1)',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',2008,'AIIMS','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38324,'physics',29,1303,3151,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> The number of degrees of freedom of a linear triatomic molecule is 7 .<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> The number of degree of freedom depends on total number of particle in the system.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'The number $(f)$ of degrees of freedom depends on atomicity the molecular structure and can vary for different molecules. In the case of a linear triatomic molecule, there are two rotational, three translational, and two vibrational degrees of freedom that add up to a total of $7,\\left(P \\propto \\dfrac{1}{V}\\right.$ at $T=$ constant $) \\quad$ value of $f$ does not depend on total number of particles. So correct option is (3).',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38325,'physics',27,1382,3319,'Two rods of equal length and diameter have thermal conductivities 3 and 4 units respectively. If they are joined in series, the thermal conductivity of the combination in the given units would be:','[{"text": "3.43", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "4.43", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "5.43", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "2.43", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'$K_{1}=3, K_{2}=4$<br>$\\begin{aligned}& R=\\dfrac{L}{K A} \\therefore R_{1}=\\dfrac{L}{3 \\times A} R_{2}=\\dfrac{L}{4 \\times A} \\\\& \\therefore R_{e q}=\\dfrac{2 L}{K_{e q} A}=\\dfrac{L}{3 \\times A}+\\dfrac{L}{4 \\times A} \\\\& \\therefore \\dfrac{2}{K_{e q}}=\\dfrac{1}{3}+\\dfrac{1}{4} \\quad \\therefore K_{e q}=\\dfrac{24}{7}=3.43\\end{aligned}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38327,'physics',26,1136,2717,'If a small orifice is made at a height of $0.25\\,m$ from the ground, the horizontal range of water stream will be','[{"text": "$46.5\\\\,cm$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$56.6\\\\,cm$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$76.6\\\\,cm$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$86.6\\\\,cm$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Given, height of small orificc from ground is $h = 0.25\\,m$  <img src=\'/img/data/physics/26_38327_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />  Total height of water tank, $H = 1\\,m$<br>$\\therefore$ Range of water stream, is given by<br>$R = 2\\sqrt {h(H - h)} $<br>$ = 2\\sqrt {(H - 0.25)(0.25)} $<br>$ = 2\\sqrt {(1 - 0.25)0.25}  = 2\\sqrt {0.75 \\times 0.25} $<br>$ = 0.866\\,m = 86.6\\,cm$<br>So, correct option is (4)',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2019,'AIIMS','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/26_38327_question_1.png'),
(38361,'physics',28,1369,3279,'A system is taken from state $A$ to state $B$ along two different paths 1 and 2 . The heat absorbed and work done by the system along these two paths are $Q_{1}, Q_{2}$ and $W_{1}, W_{2}$ respectively then','[{"text": "$Q_{1}-W_{1}=Q_{2}-W_{2}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$Q_{1}=Q_{2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$W_{1}=W_{2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "None", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Internal energy change is path independent functions.<br>$\\Delta U_{1}=\\Delta U_{2}$<br>$Q_{1}-W_{1}=Q_{2}-W_{2}$.',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/28_38361_question_1.png'),
(38328,'physics',29,1302,3150,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Absolute zero is not the temperature corresponding to zero energy.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> The temperature at which molecular motion ceases is called absolute zero temperature.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Absolute zero is the lowest temperature at which the motion of particles reaches its minimum energy state, but it is not a state of zero energy. So correct option is (1).',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38329,'physics',27,1382,3319,'A wall is made of equally thick layers $A$ and $B$ of different materials. Thermal conductivity of $A$ is twice to that of $B$. In the steady state, the temperature difference across the wall is $36^\\circ C$. The temperature difference across the layer $A$ is:','[{"text": "$12^\\\\circ C$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$18^\\\\circ C$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$6^\\\\circ C$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$24^\\\\circ C$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'In thermal equilibrium, the rate of flow across the two layers is the same<br>$\\begin{aligned}& \\dfrac{2 K\\left(\\Delta T_{1}\\right) A}{\\ell}=\\dfrac{K\\left(\\Delta T_{2}\\right) A}{\\ell} \\\\& \\Rightarrow \\dfrac{\\Delta T_{1}}{\\Delta T_{2}}=\\dfrac{1}{2} \\Rightarrow \\dfrac{\\Delta T_{1}}{36-\\Delta T_{1}}=\\dfrac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}$<br>$\\Delta {T_1} = 12^\\circ C$.',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38330,'physics',26,1136,2715,'Determine the pressure difference $(\\Delta P)$ in tube of non - uniform cross - sectional area as shown in figure. Given:<br>${d_1} = 5\\,cm,{v_1} = 4\\,m/s,{d_2} = 2\\,cm$','[{"text": "$304200\\\\,Pa$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$304500\\\\,Pa$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$302500\\\\,Pa$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$303500\\\\,Pa$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Given,<br>diameter of tube at first end, ${d_1} = 5\\,cm$<br>$\\Rightarrow$ Radius, ${r_1} = \\frac{{{d_1}}}{2} = 2.5\\,cm$<br>$\\Rightarrow r_{1}=2.5 \\times 10^{-2} \\mathrm{~m}$<br>Diameter of tube at second end, ${d_2} = 2\\,cm$<br>$\\Rightarrow$ Radius, ${r_2} = \\frac{{{d_2}}}{2} = 1\\,cm = {10^{ - 2}}\\,cm$<br>Velocity of fluid at first end, ${v_1} = 4\\,m{\\rm{/}}s$<br>By the principle of continuity,<br>${A_1}{v_1} = {A_2}{v_2}$<br>$ \\Rightarrow \\pi r_1^2{v_1} = \\pi r_2^2{v_2}$<br>$ \\Rightarrow {\\left( {2.5 \\times {{10}^{ - 2}}} \\right)^2} \\times 4 = {\\left( {{{10}^{ - 2}}} \\right)^2} \\cdot {v_2}$<br>$ \\Rightarrow {v_2} = 25\\,m{\\rm{/}}s$<br>From Bernoulli\'s theorem,<br>$P_{1}+\\dfrac{1}{2} \\rho v_{1}^{2}=P_{2}+\\dfrac{1}{2} \\rho v_{2}^{2}$<br>$\\begin{aligned}& \\Delta P=\\dfrac{1}{2} \\rho\\left(v_{2}^{2}-v_{1}^{2}\\right) \\\\& \\quad=\\dfrac{1}{2} \\times 10^{3}\\left(25^{2}-4^{2}\\right)\\end{aligned}$<br>$\\left[\\because\\right.$ density of water, $\\left. {\\rho  = {{10}^3}\\,kg{\\rm{/}}{m^3}} \\right]$<br>$ \\Rightarrow \\Delta P = 304500\\,Pa$<br>So, correct option is (2)',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2019,'AIIMS','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/26_38330_question_1.png'),
(38331,'physics',28,1371,3289,'For an ideal gas graph is shown for three processes. Process 1,2 and 3 are respectively','[{"text": "Isobaric, adiabatic, isochoric", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Adiabatic, isobaric, isochoric", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Isochoric, adiabatic, isobaric", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Isochoric, isobaric, adiabatic", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Process (1) Isochoric process due to :<br>Work $W = 0$<br>$\\Delta V = 0$<br>$ \\Rightarrow V = $ Constant<br>Process (2) is isobaric process due to :<br>$W = P\\Delta V$<br>$W = nR\\Delta T$ (with P= constant)<br>Process (3) is adiabatic process due to :<br>$W = \\frac{{nR\\Delta T}}{{\\gamma  - 1}}$<br>Where, $0 < \\gamma  - 1 < 1$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2021,'MHTCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/28_38331_question_1.jpg'),
(38332,'physics',29,1305,3155,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> When the number density of gas molecules increases, there is a decrease in their mean free path.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> With more molecules per unit volume of the gas, the chances of molecular collisions increases, thereby decreasing mean free path.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'An increase in the number density of gas molecules results in a higher probability of molecular collisions, leading to a decrease in the mean free path. The reason correctly explains this behavior. So correct option is (1).',NULL,'Hard','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38333,'physics',27,1382,3318,'Three rods made of same material and having the same cross-section have been joined. Each rod is of the same length. The left and right ends are kept at $0^\\circ C$ and $100^\\circ C$ respectively. The temperature at the junction of the three rods will be.','[{"text": "$50^\\\\circ C$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$60^\\\\circ C$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$66.6^\\\\circ C$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$40^\\\\circ C$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'<br>$\\dfrac{Q}{t}=\\dfrac{K A \\Delta T}{x}=\\dfrac{\\Delta T}{R}\\left[R=\\dfrac{\\rho \\ell}{A}=\\dfrac{1}{K} \\dfrac{x}{A}\\right]$ $\\dfrac{Q}{t}=\\dfrac{\\Delta T}{R}$<br>As the Rods $B$ and $C$ are in Parallel combinations.<br>$\\begin{aligned}& \\dfrac{1}{R_{P}}=\\dfrac{1}{R}+\\dfrac{1}{R}=\\dfrac{2}{R} \\\\& \\therefore R_{P}=\\dfrac{R}{2}\\end{aligned}$   <img src=\'/img/data/physics/27_38333_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />   <img src=\'/img/data/physics/27_38333_explanation_2.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />   In series combination flow of heat is same<br>$\\dfrac{Q_{A B}}{t}=\\dfrac{Q_{B C}}{t}$<br>Let $T$ be the temperature of interface.<br>$\\frac{{T - 0}}{R} = \\frac{{100 - T}}{{R/2}} \\Rightarrow T - 0 = 200 - 2T$<br>$3T = 200 \\Rightarrow T = \\frac{{200}}{3} = 66.6^\\circ C$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/27_38333_question_1.png'),
(38342,'physics',27,1382,3319,'A thin brass sheet at $10^\\circ C$ and thin steel at $20^\\circ C$ have the same surface area. The common temperature at which both would have the same area (coefficient of linear expansion for brass and steel are respectively<br>$(19 \\times {10^{ - 6}}/^\\circ C$ and $11 \\times {10^{ - 6}}/^\\circ C$ )','[{"text": "$ - 3.75^\\\\circ C$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$ - 2.75^\\\\circ C$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2.75^\\\\circ C$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$3.75^\\\\circ C$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Given that initial areas of both plates are same. Let both will have same area at temperature $T$.<br>Area of Brass sheet = Area of thin  steel<br>$\\therefore {A_0}\\left( {1 + 2 \\times 19 \\times {{10}^{ - 6}}(T - 10)} \\right)$<br>$ = {A_0}\\left( {1 + 2 \\times 11 \\times {{10}^{ - 6}}(T - 20)} \\right)$<br>$ \\Rightarrow 19(T - 10) = 11(T - 20)$<br>$ \\Rightarrow T =  - 3.75^\\circ C.$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38335,'physics',26,1138,2727,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Sometimes insects can walk on water.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> The gravitational force on insect is balanced by force due to surface tension','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Sometimes insects can walk on water. This assertion is correct.<br>The gravitational force on the insect is balanced by the force due to surface tension. Surface tension is the result of cohesive forces at the surface of a liquid. It acts like an invisible "skin" on the water\'s surface, allowing some insects to be supported by this surface tension and preventing them from sinking.<br>This reason is also correct and provides the correct explanation<br>So, correct option is (1).',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',2019,'AIIMS','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38336,'physics',29,1302,3150,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> The total translational kinetic energy of all the molecules of a given mass of an ideal gas is 1.5 times the product of its pressure and its volume<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> The molecules of a gas collide with each other and the velocities of the molecules change due to the collision.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'We know $n R=N K_{B}$<br>$\\left(E_{K}\\right)_{\\text {total }}=\\dfrac{3}{2} N K_{B} T=\\dfrac{3}{2} n R T$<br>$=\\dfrac{3}{2} P V=1.5 \\mathrm{PV}$<br>Reason is separate fact<br>So correct option is (2).',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38337,'physics',28,1370,3280,'The molar specific heats of an ideal gas at constant pressure and volume are denoted by $C_{p}$ and $C_{v}$, respectively. If $\\gamma=\\dfrac{C_{p}}{C_{v}}$ and $R$ is the universal gas constant, then $C_{v}$ is equal to','[{"text": "$\\\\frac{R}{{(\\\\gamma  - 1)}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{(\\\\gamma-1)}{R}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\gamma R$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{1+\\\\gamma}{1-\\\\gamma}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'$C_{p}-C_{v}=R \\Rightarrow C_{p}=C_{v}+R$<br>$\\because \\gamma=\\dfrac{C_{p}}{C_{v}}=\\dfrac{C_{v}+R}{C_{v}}=\\dfrac{C_{v}}{C_{v}}+\\dfrac{R}{C_{v}}$<br>$\\Rightarrow \\gamma=1+\\dfrac{R}{C_{v}} \\Rightarrow \\dfrac{R}{C_{v}}=\\gamma-1 \\Rightarrow C_{v}=\\dfrac{R}{\\gamma-1}$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2021,'MHTCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38338,'physics',27,1382,3318,'Three rods of the same dimension have thermal conductivities $3 K, 2 K$ and $4 K$. They are arranged as show in diagram. There ends kept at $150^\\circ C,20^\\circ C,80^\\circ C$. The temperature of the junction is','[{"text": "$90^\\\\circ C$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$97.7^\\\\circ C$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$112^\\\\circ C$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$50^\\\\circ C$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'In parallel combination, the flow of heat is the sum of the individual.<br>$H_{1}=H_{2}+H_{3}$<br>Let us consider $T$ is the temperature of junction<br>$\\dfrac{4 K A(150-T)}{x}=\\dfrac{2 K A(T-20)}{x}+$<br>$\\dfrac{3 K A\\left(T-80^{\\circ}\\right)}{x}$<br>$4(150-T)=2(T-20)+3\\left(T-80^{\\circ}\\right)$<br>$600-4 T=2 T-40+3 T-240$<br>$600 + 280 = 9T \\Rightarrow T = \\frac{{880}}{9} = 97.7^\\circ C$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/27_38338_question_1.png'),
(38339,'physics',26,1138,2730,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Water drops take spherical shape when falling freely.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Water has minimum surface tension among all liquids.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are incorrect.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'While smaller water droplets often tend to form spherical shapes due to surface tension, larger ones can deform into various shapes, including an oval shape due to air resistance and other factors.<br>Water has relatively high surface tension compared to many other liquids. At $20^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C}$, surface tension of water $ = 0.073\\,N{\\rm{/}}m$ and that of olive oil is less than that and is $0.032\\,N{\\rm{/}}m$.<br>As both the assertion and the reason are false, So, correct option is (3).',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',2019,'AIIMS','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38340,'physics',29,1302,3150,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Vibrational energy of diatomic molecule corresponding to each degree of freedom is $K_{B} T$.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> For every molecule, vibrational degree of freedom is 2 .','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'According to law of equipartition energy in thermal equilibrium, at temperature $T$. each degree of freedom of contributes an average energy equal to $\\dfrac{1}{2} K_{B} T$.<br>The vibrational motion has two types of energy associated with the vibrations along the length of the molecule-kinetic energy and potential energy. Thus it contributes two degrees of freedom. Thus vibration energy<br>$=2 \\times \\dfrac{1}{2} K_{B} T=K_{B} T$<br>So correct option is (3).',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38343,'physics',26,1138,2730,'In an isothermal process, 2 water drops of radius $1 \\mathrm{~mm}$ are combined to form a bigger drop. Find the energy change in this process, if $T = 0.1\\,N{\\rm{/}}m$','[{"text": "$1\\\\,\\\\mu J$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$0.5\\\\,\\\\mu J$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$0.25\\\\,\\\\mu J$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$0.75\\\\,\\\\mu J$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Volume remains conserved.<br>$\\Rightarrow V_{1}=V_{2}$<br>$\\Rightarrow \\dfrac{4}{3} \\pi R^{3}=2 \\times \\dfrac{4}{3} \\pi r^{3}$<br>$\\Rightarrow R^{3}=2 r^{3}=2 \\times\\left(10^{-3}\\right)^{3}$<br>$ \\Rightarrow R = {2^{1/3}} \\times {10^{ - 3}}\\,m$<br>$\\therefore$ Change in energy during isothermal condition. (where $T$ is for surface tension.<br>$\\Delta U = T \\cdot \\Delta A = T\\left[ {{A_2} - {A_1}} \\right]$<br>$ \\Rightarrow \\Delta U = 0.1\\left[ {2 \\times 4\\pi {r^2} - 4\\pi {R^2}} \\right]$<br>$ \\Rightarrow \\Delta U = 0.1 \\times 4\\pi \\left[ {2{r^2} - {R^2}} \\right]$<br>$ \\Rightarrow \\Delta U = 0.4\\pi \\left[ {2 \\times {{10}^{ - 6}} - {2^{2/3}} \\times {{10}^{ - 6}}} \\right]$<br>$ = 0.4\\pi  \\times {10^{ - 6}} \\times 0.41$<br>$ = 0.52 \\times {10^{ - 6}}\\,J$<br>$ \\Rightarrow \\Delta U = 0.52\\,\\mu J \\simeq 0.5\\,\\mu J$<br>So, correct option is (2).',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',2019,'AIIMS','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38344,'physics',29,1303,3151,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> An undamped spring-mass system is simplest free vibration system.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> It has three degrees of freedom','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'An undamped spring-mass system, characterized by a single degree of freedom. So correct option is (3).',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38345,'physics',28,1371,3283,'In a thermodynamic system, \' $W$ \' represents the work done by the system and \' $\\Delta U$ \' is the increase in internal energy. Which of the following statements is true?','[{"text": "In an isothermal process, $\\\\Delta U=W$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "In an adiabatic process, $\\\\Delta U=-W$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "In an isothermal process, $\\\\Delta U=-W$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "In an adiabatic process, $\\\\Delta U=W$.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'According to the first law of thermodynamics<br>$\\Delta Q = \\Delta U + \\Delta W$<br>In adiabatic process<br>$\\Delta U =  - \\Delta W$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2022,'MHTCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38346,'physics',27,1382,3319,'Three rods of identical dimensions are arranged as shown. Conductivity of the material of rods is $k,$ area of cross-section of rods is $A,$ length of each rod is $l$. Thermal resistance of the system between points $A$ and $D$ is, ( Assume there are no losses due to radiation)','[{"text": "$\\\\dfrac{3 l}{k A}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{l}{3 k A}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{5 l}{2 k A}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{3 l}{2 k A}$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Let $R$ be the thermal resistance of a rod of length $\\ell$ and area of cross-section $A$.<br>$R=\\dfrac{\\ell}{K A}$   <img src=\'/img/data/physics/27_38346_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />  The resistance $\\dfrac{R}{2}$ which is connected to $B$ can be removed.<br>$\\Rightarrow R_{A D}=\\dfrac{R}{2}+R+R=\\dfrac{5 R}{2}=\\dfrac{5 l}{2 K A}$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/27_38346_question_1.png'),
(38347,'physics',26,1138,2731,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> The water rises higher in a capillary tube of small diameter than in the capillary tube of large diameter.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Height through which liquid rise in capillary tube is inversely proportional to the diameter of capillary tube','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'We know, for capillary-rise height of the column $h=\\dfrac{2 T \\cos \\theta}{\\rho g r}$  <img src=\'/img/data/physics/26_38347_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />   Where $\\theta$ is angle between $\\mathrm{AO}$ (or $\\mathrm{BO}$ ) and horizontal<br>for same $\\theta, \\rho, T$ and constant volume of $\\mathrm{g}$, we have $h r=$ constant<br>$\\Rightarrow h_{1} r_{1}=h_{2} r_{2}$<br>Here $r=$ radius of cross-section of that particular tube<br>$d=$ Diameter $=2 r$<br>$\\Rightarrow h \\propto \\dfrac{1}{d}$<br>So option (1) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',2012,'AIIMS','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38348,'physics',29,1303,3151,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> The number of degrees of freedom of non-linear triatomic molecule is 6 .<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Triatomic molecule has three translational degrees of freedom and three rotational degrees of freedom.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'The assertion and reason correctly describe the degrees of freedom for triatomic molecules. They have 3 translational degrees of freedom and 3 rotational degrees of freedom. <img src=\'/img/data/physics/29_38348_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' /> So correct option is (1).',NULL,'Easy','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38349,'physics',28,1369,3279,'In thermodynamic process, $200\\,Joules$ of heat is given to a gas and $100\\,Joules$ of work is also done on it. The change in internal energy of the gas is','[{"text": "$100\\\\,J$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$300\\\\,J$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$419\\\\,J$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$24\\\\,J$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'$\\Delta Q = \\Delta U + \\Delta W;\\Delta Q = 200\\,J\\,{\\mkern 1mu} {\\rm{and}}{\\mkern 1mu} \\,\\Delta W =  - 100\\,J$<br>$ \\Rightarrow \\Delta U = \\Delta Q - \\Delta W = 2000 - (100) = 300\\,J.$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38350,'physics',27,1382,3319,'Two metal cubes with $3\\,cm$-edges of copper and aluminium are arranged as shown in figure.<br>Thermal conductivity of copper is $401\\,W{\\rm{/}}m{\\rm{ - }}K$ and that of aluminium is $237\\,W{\\rm{/}}m{\\rm{ - }}K$. Find the total thermal current from one reservoir to the other.','[{"text": "$0.16\\\\,kW$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1.5\\\\,kW$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$0.74\\\\,kW$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$0.94\\\\,kW$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'$H=401 \\times 3 \\times 10^{-2} \\times 80+237 \\times 3 \\times 10^{-2} \\times 80$<br>$ = (401 + 237)24 \\times {10^{ - 1}}$<br>$ = 1.531\\,kW$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/27_38350_question_1.png'),
(38352,'physics',29,1306,3156,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> All molecular motion ceases at $ - 273^\\circ C$.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Temperature $ - 273^\\circ C$ cannot be attained.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'It is denoted as Zero Kelvin, but experimentally not achieved classically, assertion is true. So correct option is (2).',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38353,'physics',28,1369,3279,'If $150\\,J$ of heat is added to a system and the work done by the system is $110\\,J$, then change in internal energy will be','[{"text": "$260\\\\,J$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$150\\\\,J$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$110\\\\,J$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$40\\\\,J$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'$\\Delta Q=\\Delta U+\\Delta W$<br>$ \\Rightarrow \\Delta U = \\Delta Q - \\Delta W = 150 - 110 = 40\\,J$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38354,'physics',27,1382,3319,'As compared to the person with white skin, the person with black skin will experience','[{"text": "Less heat and more cold", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "More heat and more cold", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "More heat and less cold", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Less heat and less cold", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'A person with dark skin absorb more heat radiation and feel more heat. It also radiate more heat and feels more cold. Just like black body.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38355,'physics',29,1300,3145,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> A bubble comes from the bottom of a lake to top.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Its radius increase in the process.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Bubble comes to top as Pressure is more at bottom than at top Boyle\'s law $P \\propto \\dfrac{1}{V}$. Air is bubble expands as it rises. Radius of bubble increases. So correct option is (1).',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38356,'physics',26,1133,2710,'The total weight of a piece of wood is $6\\,kg$. In the floating state in water its $\\dfrac{1}{3}$ part remains inside the water. On this floating piece of wood what maximum weight is to be put such that the whole of the piece of wood is to be drowned in the water?','[{"text": "$15\\\\,kg$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$14\\\\,kg$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$10\\\\,kg$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$12\\\\,kg$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Weight of submerged part of the block<br>$W=\\dfrac{1}{3} v$ (Density of water) $g$<br>Excess weight, $=$ weight of water having $\\dfrac{2}{3}$ volume of the block.<br>$W^{\\prime}=\\dfrac{2}{3} v$ (Density of water) $g$<br>Dividing (2) by (1)<br>$\\frac{{W\'}}{W} = \\frac{{2/3}}{{1/3}}\\,\\,\\,{\\mkern 1mu} {\\kern 1pt} \\therefore W\' = 2\\,W$<br>$ \\Rightarrow W\' = 2 \\times 6 = 12\\,kg$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2021,'MHTCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38357,'physics',28,1369,3279,'In a thermodynamic process, pressure of a fixed mass of a gas is changed in such a manner that the gas molecules gives out $20\\,J$ of heat and $10\\,J$ of work is done on the gas. If the initial internal energy of the gas was $40\\,J$, then the final internal energy will be','[{"text": "$30\\\\,J$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$20\\\\,J$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$60\\\\,J$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$40\\\\,J$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'$\\Delta Q=\\Delta U+\\Delta W=\\left(U_{f}-U_{i}\\right)+\\Delta W$<br>$ \\Rightarrow dU = dQ - dW =  - 20 - ( - 10) =  - 10\\,J$<br>${U_f} = {U_i} + \\Delta U = 40 - 10 = 30\\,J$.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38358,'physics',27,1382,3322,'Variation of radiant energy emitted by sun, filament of tungsten lamp and welding arc as a function of its wavelength is shown in figure. Which of the following options is the correct match?<div class="matching-question"></div>','[{"text": "${\\\\rm{Sun}}\\\\,{\\\\rm{ - }}\\\\,{T_3},{\\\\rm{tungsten}}\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\rm{filament}}\\\\,{\\\\rm{ - }}\\\\,{T_1},{\\\\rm{welding}}\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\rm{arc}}\\\\,{\\\\rm{ - }}\\\\,{T_2}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{Sun}}\\\\,{\\\\rm{ - }}\\\\,{T_2},{\\\\rm{tungsten}}\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\rm{filament}}\\\\,{\\\\rm{ - }}\\\\,{T_1},{\\\\rm{welding}}\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\rm{arc}}\\\\,{\\\\rm{ - }}\\\\,{T_3}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{Sun}}\\\\,{\\\\rm{ - }}\\\\,{T_3},{\\\\rm{tungsten}}\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\rm{filament}}\\\\,{\\\\rm{ - }}\\\\,{T_2},{\\\\rm{welding}}\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\rm{arc}}\\\\,{\\\\rm{ - }}\\\\,{T_1}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{Sun}}\\\\,{\\\\rm{ - }}\\\\,{T_1},{\\\\rm{tungsten}}\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\rm{filament}}\\\\,{\\\\rm{ - }}\\\\,{T_2},{\\\\rm{welding}}\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\rm{arc}}\\\\,{\\\\rm{ - }}\\\\,{T_3}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'From Wein\'s displacement law $\\lambda_{\\max } T=b$. The knowledge of $\\lambda_{\\max }$ or $T$ will help in getting the answer. Typical temperature of tungsten filament in lamp is $3000\\,K$.<br>${T_{sum{\\rm{ }}}} = 5800\\,K$<br>${T_{welding,{\\rm{ a}}rc{\\rm{ }}}} = 6000\\,K$ to $30000\\,K$',NULL,'Hard','Graph/Figure',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/27_38358_question_1.png'),
(38359,'physics',26,1137,2723,'Eight identical small drops of water are falling down vertically through a medium, each with terminal velocity \' $V$ \'. If they combine to form a single drop, then its terminal velocity will be','[{"text": "$6\\\\,V$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$4\\\\,V$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$3\\\\,V$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$5\\\\,V$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Let radius of each drop $=r$<br>Terminal velocity, $V=\\dfrac{2}{g} \\dfrac{r^{2}}{\\eta}(\\rho-\\sigma) g$<br>Let \' $R$ \' be the radius of big drop.<br>Volume of big drop $=$ volume of 8 small drops<br>$\\Rightarrow \\dfrac{4}{3} \\pi R^{3}=8 \\times \\dfrac{4}{3} \\pi r^{3} \\Rightarrow R=2 r$<br>Let $V^{\\prime}$ be the terminal velocity of bigger drop,<br>$V^{\\prime}=\\dfrac{2 R^{2}(\\rho-\\sigma)}{9 \\eta}$<br>$\\Rightarrow \\dfrac{V^{\\prime}}{V}=\\dfrac{R^{2}}{r^{2}}=\\dfrac{(2 r)^{2}}{r^{2}}=4 \\Rightarrow V^{\\prime}=4 V$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2022,'MHTCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38360,'physics',29,1306,3156,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Specific heat capacity is the cause of formation of land and sea breeze.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> The specific heat of water is more than land.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Land has specific heat to be $\\dfrac{1}{5}^{\\text {th }}$ value of that of seawater. Water has a higher specific heat capacity than land, causing it to heat up and cool down more slowly. In case of sea breeze, air above land becomes hot and so less dense. Due to, difference in pressures between land and sea water the breezes occur. So correct option is (1).',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38362,'physics',27,1382,3322,'The spectrum of a black body at two temperatures \\[227^\\circ C\\] and \\[527^\\circ C\\] as shown in graphical representation. Let \\(A_{1}\\) and \\(A_{2}\\) be the areas under the two curves respectively. The ratio of \\(A_{2} / A_{1}\\) is:<div class="matching-question"></div>','[{"text": "$(5 / 8)^{2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$(2)^{4}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "1", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$(5 / 8)^{4}$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'<br>$\\dfrac{A_{2}}{A_{1}}=\\left(\\dfrac{T_{1}}{T_{2}}\\right)^{4}=\\left(\\dfrac{500}{800}\\right)^{4}=\\dfrac{625}{4096}=\\left(\\dfrac{5}{8}\\right)^{4}$',NULL,'Medium','Graph/Figure',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/27_38362_question_1.png'),
(38363,'physics',26,1134,2713,'When a liquid flows through a tube, the<br>Reynold\'s number is 900 , the flow of a liquid is','[{"text": "changes from turbulent flow to a streamline flow", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "changes from a streamline flow to a turbulent flow", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "streamline", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "turbulent.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'For streamline flow, Reynold\'s number, $R_{n} < 2000$.<br>Hence flow of liquid is streamline.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2022,'MHTCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38364,'physics',29,1302,3150,'The total kinetic energy of 1 mole of oxygen at $27^\\circ \\,C$ is : [Use universal gas constant $(R) = 8.31\\,J/mole{\\rm{ }}K]$','[{"text": "$5942.0\\\\,J$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$5670.5\\\\,J$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$6232.5\\\\,J$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$6845.5\\\\,J$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'$K . E .=\\dfrac{f}{2} n R T$<br>$K.E.{\\rm{ }} = \\frac{5}{2}(1)\\left( {\\frac{{25}}{3}} \\right)(300)$<br>${\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} \\{ f = 5\\,{\\rm{for}}\\,{\\rm{diatomic}}\\,{\\rm{gas}}\\} $<br>$K.E.{\\rm{ }} = 6232.5\\,J$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2024,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38365,'physics',28,1369,3279,'Unit mass of a liquid with volume $V_{1}$ is completely changed into a gas of volume $V_{2}$ at a constant external pressure $P$ and temperature $T$. If the latent heat of evaporation for the given mass is $L$, then the increase in the internal energy of the system is','[{"text": "${\\\\rm{Zero}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$P\\\\left(V_{2}-V_{1}\\\\right)$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$L-P\\\\left(V_{2}-V_{1}\\\\right)$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$L$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'$\\Delta Q=\\Delta U+P \\Delta V \\Rightarrow m l=\\Delta U+P\\left(V_{2}-V_{1}\\right)$<br>$\\Delta U=L-P\\left(V_{2}-V_{1}\\right) \\quad(\\because m=1)$.',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38367,'physics',26,1138,2731,'Two narrow tube of diameters \' $d_{1}$ \' and \' $d_{2}$ \' are joined together to form a $U$ tube open at both ends. If $U$ tube contains water, the difference in water levels in the limbs is ( $T$ is the surface tension of water, angle of contact $=$ zero and density of water $=\\rho, g=$ acceleration due to gravity)','[{"text": "$\\\\dfrac{2 T}{\\\\rho g}\\\\left[\\\\dfrac{d_{1}+d_{2}}{d_{1} d_{2}}\\\\right]$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{4 T}{\\\\rho g}\\\\left[\\\\dfrac{d_{1} d_{2}}{d_{1}+d_{2}}\\\\right]$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{2 T}{\\\\rho g}\\\\left[\\\\dfrac{d_{2}-d_{1}}{d_{1} d_{2}}\\\\right]$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{4 T}{\\\\rho g}\\\\left[\\\\dfrac{d_{2}-d_{1}}{d_{1} d_{2}}\\\\right]$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'The difference between the levels of water in the tube  $h = {h_1} - {h_2}$   <img src=\'/img/data/physics/26_38367_explanation_1.jpg\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />  $h = \\frac{{2T\\cos \\theta }}{{\\rho g}}\\left( {\\frac{1}{{{r_1}}} - \\frac{1}{{{r_2}}}} \\right)$<br>$h = \\frac{{2T\\cos 0^\\circ }}{{\\rho g}}\\left( {\\frac{2}{{{d_1}}} - \\frac{2}{{{d_2}}}} \\right)$<br>$h = \\frac{{4T}}{{\\rho g}}\\left( {\\frac{1}{{{d_1}}} - \\frac{1}{{{d_2}}}} \\right)$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',2022,'MHTCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38368,'physics',29,1302,3150,'The average kinetic energy of a monoatomic molecule is $0.414\\,eV$ at temperature : (Use ${K_B} = 1.38 \\times {10^{ - 23}}\\,J{\\rm{/}}mol - K)$','[{"text": "$1600\\\\,K$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$3200\\\\,K$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$3000\\\\,K$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1500\\\\,K$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'The internal energy of any gas is given by<br>$U=K E=\\dfrac{f}{2} k T$ (ignoring molecular interaction energy)<br>$\\frac{f}{2}kT = \\frac{3}{2}kT = 0.414 \\times 1.6 \\times {10^{ - 19}}\\,J$<br>$T=3200 K$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2024,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38369,'physics',28,1372,3294,'The $P-V$ diagram of a system undergoing thermodynamic transformation is shown in figure. The work done by the system in going from $A \\rightarrow B \\rightarrow C$ is $30\\,J$, and $40\\,J$ heat is given to the system. The change in internal energy between $A$ and $C$ is','[{"text": "$10\\\\,J$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$70\\\\,J$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$84\\\\,J$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$134\\\\,J$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Since, work is done by the system, so it is positive, therefore,<br>$\\Delta W = 30\\,J$<br>Heat given to the system, $\\Delta Q = 40\\,J$<br>using first law of thermodynamics<br>$\\Delta U = \\Delta Q - \\Delta W = 40 - 30 = 10\\,J$.',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/28_38369_question_1.png'),
(38371,'physics',29,1300,3145,'A bicycle tyre is filled with air having pressure of $270\\,kPa$ at $27^\\circ C$. The approximate pressure of the air in the tyre when the temperature increases to $36^\\circ C$ is','[{"text": "$360\\\\,kPa$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$262\\\\,kPa$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$270\\\\,kPa$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$278\\\\,kPa$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Given: ${P_1} = 270\\,kPa$<br>${T_1} = 27^\\circ C$ or $300\\,K,{T_2} = 36^\\circ C$ or<br>$309 K, P_{2}=$?<br>Assuming volume of tyre remains approximately unchanged,<br>$\\dfrac{P_{1}}{T_{1}}=\\dfrac{P_{2}}{T_{2}} \\Rightarrow P_{2}=\\dfrac{P_{1}}{T_{1}} \\times T_{2} \\Rightarrow \\dfrac{270 \\times 309}{300}$<br>${P_2} = 278\\,KPa$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2023,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38372,'physics',26,1138,2731,'If a capillary tube of radius $1 \\mathrm{~mm}$ is immersed in water, the mass of water rising in the capillary tube is \' $m$ \'. If the radius of the capillary tube is doubled, then the mass of water that rises in the same capillary tube will be','[{"text": "$3 m$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$m$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2 m$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$m / 2$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'For capillary rise, $h_{1} r_{1}=h_{2} r_{2}$<br>$\\dfrac{h_{2}}{h_{2}}=\\dfrac{r_{1}}{r_{2}}=\\dfrac{1}{2}$<br>Also $m^{\\prime}=\\pi r^{2} h_{1} \\rho$ and $m^{\\prime}=\\pi(2 r)^{2} h_{2} \\times \\rho$<br>$\\dfrac{m^{\\prime}}{m}=\\left(\\dfrac{2 r}{r}\\right)^{2} \\times \\dfrac{1}{2}=\\dfrac{4 \\times 1}{2}=\\dfrac{2}{1}$<br>$m^{\\prime}=2 m$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2022,'MHTCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38373,'physics',28,1371,3282,'A container that suits the occurence of an isothermal process should be made of','[{"text": "Copper", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Wood", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Glass", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Cloth", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'An isothermal process takes place at 	constant temperature, must be carried out in a 	vessel with conducting wall so that heat 	generated should go out at once. Correct option 	is (1).',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38375,'physics',29,1303,3152,'The ratio of molar specific heats of oxygen is','[{"text": "1.4", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "1.67", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "1.33", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "1.28", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Ratio of molar specific heats, ${\\gamma=\\dfrac{C_{P}}{C_{V}}}$ ${\\gamma=1+\\dfrac{2}{f}}$<br>${f=}$ number of degrees of freedom<br>${f=5}$ for ${O_{2}}$<br>So ${\\gamma=1+\\dfrac{2}{5}=1.4}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2024,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38376,'physics',26,1138,2728,'The surface tension of a soap solution is $\'T\'$. The work done in blowing a soap bubble of diameter $\'d\'$ to that of a diameter $\'2\\,d\'$ is','[{"text": "$6 \\\\pi d^{2} T$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$4 \\\\pi d^{2} T$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2 \\\\pi d^{2} T$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$8 \\\\pi d^{2} T$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'The amount of work done to blow a bubble is the change in surface energy of the bubble.<br>Now, $\\Delta W=2 T \\cdot d A$<br>$\\Delta W=8 \\pi T\\left[d^{2}-\\dfrac{d^{2}}{4}\\right]=8 \\pi T \\times \\dfrac{3 d^{2}}{4}=6 \\pi d^{2} T$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2022,'MHTCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38377,'physics',28,1371,3282,'One mole of an ideal gas expands at a constant temperature of $300\\,K$ from an initial volume of 10 litre a to a final volume of 20 litres. The workdone in expanding the gas is','[{"text": "$750\\\\,joules$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1728\\\\,joules$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$1500\\\\,joules$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$3456\\\\,joules$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'<br>${W_{iso}} = \\mu \\,RT\\,{\\log _e}\\frac{{{V_2}}}{{{V_1}}}$<br>$ = 1 \\times 8.31 \\times 300{\\log _e}\\frac{{20}}{{10}} = 1728\\,J$.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38379,'physics',29,1300,3145,'The following graph represents the ${T-V}$ curves of an ideal gas (where ${T}$ is the temperature and ${V}$ the volume) at three pressures ${P_{1}, P_{2}}$ and ${P_{3}}$ compared with those of Charles\'s law represented as dotted lines. Then the correct relation is:<div class="matching-question"></div>','[{"text": "${P_{3}>P_{2}>P_{1}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${P_{1}>P_{3}>P_{2}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${P_{2}>P_{1}>P_{3}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${P_{1}>P_{2}>P_{3}}$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'From ideal gas equation<br>$PV = nRT$<br>$V = \\frac{{nRT}}{P}$<br>At same temperature, curve with higher volume corresponds to lower pressure.<br>At any given temperature draw a straight line parallel to volume axis<br>${V_{3}>V_{2}>V_{1}}$<br>${\\Rightarrow P_{1}>P_{2}>P_{3}}$',NULL,'Hard','Graph/Figure',2024,'NEET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/29_38379_question_1.png'),
(38380,'physics',26,1136,2715,'An ideal liquid flows through the pipe, which is of uniform cross-section area. The speed $V_{A}$ and $V_{B}$, and pressure $P_{A}$ and $P_{B}$ at points $A$ and $B$ respectively are','[{"text": "$V_{A}=V_{B}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$P_{A}>P_{B}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$P_{A}=P_{B}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$P_{B}>P_{A}$<br>", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Pressure at $B$ is due to the water column $A B$ and pressure at $A$ which is applied by flowing liquid but $V_{A}>V_{B}$.<br>So, $P_{B}>P_{A}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/26_38380_question_1.png'),
(38381,'physics',28,1371,3282,'A cylinder fitted with a piston contains 0.2 moles of air at temperature $27^\\circ C$. The piston is pushed so slowly that the air within the cylinder remains in thermal equilibrium with the sorroundings. Find the approximate work done by the system if the final volume is twice the initial volume.','[{"text": "$543\\\\,J$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$345\\\\,J$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$453\\\\,J$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$600\\\\,J$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'<br>$W = \\mu RT{\\log _e}\\left( {\\frac{{{V_2}}}{{{V_1}}}} \\right)$<br>$ = 0.2 \\times 8.3 \\times {\\log _e}2 \\times (27 + 273) = 345\\,J$.',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38383,'physics',26,1135,2714,'In the water coming out of the mouth of a tap and falling vertically in streamline flow forms a tapering column. i.e., the area of cross-section of the liquid column decreases as it moves down at the mouth of the tap the area of cross-section is \\[2.5\\;c{m^2}\\] and the speed of water is \\[3\\;m{\\rm{/}}s\\]. The area of cross-section of the water column \\[80\\;cm\\] below the tap is:<div class="matching-question"></div>','[{"text": "$2\\\\,c{m^2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1.5\\\\,c{m^2}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$1\\\\,c{m^2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$0.5\\\\,c{m^2}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'${A_1} = 2.5\\,c{m^2},{v_1} = 3\\,m{\\rm{/}}s,\\,h = 0.8,\\,g = 10\\,m{\\rm{/}}{s^2}$<br>$v_2^2 = v_1^2 + 2{\\mkern 1mu} gh = 25\\,{\\rm{or}}\\,{v_2} = 5\\,m{\\rm{/}}s$<br>${A_1}{v_1} = {A_2}{v_2}\\,{\\rm{or}}\\,{A_2} = \\frac{{{A_1}{v_1}}}{{{v_2}}} = 1.5\\,c{m^2}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/26_38383_question_1.png'),
(38384,'physics',29,1300,3145,'Pressure versus temperature graph of an ideal gas is as shown in the figure. Density of the gas at point ${A}$ is ${\\rho_{0}}$. Density at point ${B}$ will be<div class="matching-question"></div>','[{"text": "${\\\\dfrac{1}{4} \\\\rho_{0}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${2 \\\\rho_{0}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${\\\\dfrac{2}{3} \\\\rho_{0}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${3 \\\\rho_{0}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'${P V=n R T}$<br>$\\begin{aligned}& \\dfrac{P m}{\\rho}=\\dfrac{m}{M} R T \\\\& \\Rightarrow \\rho=\\dfrac{P M}{R T}\\end{aligned}$<br>$\\rho=\\dfrac{P M_{W}}{R T} \\text { or } \\rho \\propto \\dfrac{P}{T}$<br>or ${\\left(\\dfrac{P}{T}\\right)_{A}=\\dfrac{P_{0}}{T_{0}}}$ and ${\\left(\\dfrac{P}{T}\\right)_{B}=\\dfrac{4}{2} \\dfrac{P_{0}}{T_{0}}}$<br>$\\left(\\dfrac{P}{T}\\right)_{B}=2\\left(\\dfrac{P}{T}\\right)_{A}$<br>${\\therefore \\rho_{B}=2 \\rho_{A}=2 \\rho_{0}}$',NULL,'Medium','Graph/Figure',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/29_38384_question_1.png'),
(38385,'physics',28,1371,3282,'10 moles of a ideal gas at constant temperature $600\\,K$ is compressed from $100\\,L$ to $10\\,L$. The work done in the process is','[{"text": "$12 \\\\times {10^4}\\\\,J$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$-12 \\\\times {10^4}\\\\,J$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$11.4 \\\\times {10^4}\\\\,J$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$-11.4 \\\\times {10^4}\\\\,J$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'${W_{isothermal{\\rm{ }}}} = 2.303\\,n\\,RT\\,\\log \\frac{{{V_2}}}{{{V_1,}}}$<br>$ = 2.303 \\times 10 \\times 8.3 \\times 600\\log \\frac{{10}}{{100}}$<br>$ =  - 11.4 \\times {10^4}Joule$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38386,'physics',27,1382,3321,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Coefficient of absorption of radiation of an ideal black body is 1 .<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> An ideal black body emits radiation of all wavelengths.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'"For an ideal black body, reflectance is 0 , and absorptance is 1 . It is evident that when a black body is heated, it will radiate all the energy that it absorbs". So correct option is (1).',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38387,'physics',26,1136,2719,'The tube shown is of uniform cross-section. Liquid flows through it at a constant speed in the direction shown by the arrows. The liquid exerts on the tube:','[{"text": "A net force to the right", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "A net force to the left", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "A clockwise torque", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "An anticlockwise torque", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'The forces exerted by the liquid at the bends are shown. (The liquid undergoes change of momentum only at these points, and hence the liquid and tube exert forces on each other). The two forces form a couple exerting a clockwise torque.    <img src=\'/img/data/physics/26_38387_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/26_38387_question_1.png'),
(38388,'physics',29,1305,3155,'The plot that depicts the behavior of the mean free time ${t}$ (time between two successive collisions) for the molecules of an ideal gas, as a function of temperature (T), qualitatively, is : (Graphs are schematic and not drawn to scale)<div class="matching-question"></div>','[{"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Mean free time ${=\\dfrac{\\text { Mean free path }}{\\text { Average speed }}}$<br>$\\begin{aligned}& =\\dfrac{\\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{2} \\pi D^{2} n}}{\\sqrt{\\dfrac{8 R T}{\\pi M}}} \\\\& t \\propto \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{T}}\\end{aligned}$',NULL,'Medium','Graph/Figure',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38389,'physics',28,1371,3282,'Consider \\(P-V\\) diagram for an ideal gas shown in figure: Out of the following diagrams, which figure represents the \\(T-P\\) diagram?<div class="matching-question"></div>','[{"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'According to the question given $P V=$ constant.<br>Hence, we can say that the gas is going through an isothermal process.<br>Clearly, from the graph that between process 1 and 2 temperature is constant and the gas expands and pressure decreases i.e., $P_{2} < P_{1}$<br>Correct option is (3).',NULL,'Medium','Graph/Figure',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/28_38389_question_1.png'),
(38390,'physics',27,1382,3320,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> While measuring the thermal conductivity of liquid experimentally, the upper layer is kept hot and the lower layer kept cold.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> This avoids heating of liquid by convection.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'The Fourier law can be applied to liquids if we avoid convection (and also radiation) to find thermal conductivity $K$.<br>Rate of heat transfered<br>$=\\dfrac{d Q}{d t}=q=K\\left[\\dfrac{T_{\\text {hot }}-T_{\\text {cold }}}{d}\\right] A$ <img src=\'/img/data/physics/27_38390_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' /> In the experimental measurement of thermal conductivity, it is common to keep the upper layer hot and the lower layer cold to minimize heat transfer by convection. The reason correctly explains why this approach is used to prevent convection currents from affecting the measurements. So correct option is (1).',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38391,'physics',29,1302,3150,'The amount of heat energy required to raise the temperature of ${8 g}$ of Helium at $NTP$ from ${T_{1} K}$ to ${T_{2} K}$ is','[{"text": "${\\\\dfrac{3}{4} N_{a} k_{B}\\\\left(\\\\dfrac{T_{2}}{T_{1}}\\\\right)}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\dfrac{3}{8} N_{a} k_{B}\\\\left(T_{2}-T_{1}\\\\right)}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${\\\\dfrac{3}{2} N_{a} k_{B}\\\\left(T_{2}-T_{i}\\\\right)}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\dfrac{3}{4} N_{a} k_{B}\\\\left(T_{2}-T_{1}\\\\right)}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Number of moles in ${\\lg {He}=\\dfrac{1}{4}}$<br>Amount of heat energy required to raise its temperature from ${T_{1} K}$ to ${T_{2} K}$<br>${=\\mu C_{V} \\Delta T}$<br>${\\left(\\dfrac{1}{4}\\right) \\times\\left(\\dfrac{3}{2} R\\right) \\times\\left(T_{2}-T_{1}\\right)}$<br>${=\\dfrac{3}{8} k_{B} N_{A}\\left(T_{2}-T_{1}\\right)}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38392,'physics',26,1136,2715,'At what speed, the velocity head of the water is twice the pressure head of $75\\,cm$ of $Hg$ ?','[{"text": "$10\\\\,m{\\\\rm{/}}s$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$20\\\\,m{\\\\rm{/}}s$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$30\\\\,m{\\\\rm{/}}s$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$40\\\\,m{\\\\rm{/}}s$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'<br>Pressure head $=\\dfrac{p}{\\rho g} ;$ velocity head $=\\dfrac{V^{2}}{2 g}$<br>$\\dfrac{V^{2}}{2 g}=\\dfrac{2 p}{\\rho g} \\Rightarrow V^{2}=\\dfrac{4 p^{2}}{\\rho}$<br>$V^{2}=\\dfrac{4 \\times 75 \\times 10^{-2} \\times 13.6 \\times 10^{3} \\times 9.8}{10^{3}}$<br>$=39984 \\times 10^{-2}=399$<br>$V \\approx 20\\,m{\\rm{/}}s$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38393,'physics',28,1371,3283,'During an adiabatic expansion of 2 moles of a gas, the change in internal energy was found $-50\\,J$. The work done during the process is','[{"text": "${\\\\rm{Zero}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$100\\\\,J$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$-50\\\\,J$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$50\\\\,J$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'For adiabatic process<br>$\\Delta W=-\\Delta U(\\because \\Delta Q=0)$<br>$ \\Rightarrow \\Delta W =  - ( - 50) =  + 50\\,J$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38394,'physics',27,1384,3326,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Water can be made to boil without heating.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Boiling point is lowered by increasing pressure.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'<img src=\'/img/data/physics/27_38394_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' /> To boil water without heating it, we need to decrease the external pressure until the vapor pressure of the liquid equals the atmospheric pressure. So correct option is (3).',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38395,'physics',29,1305,3155,'The mean free path of molecules of a gas (radius  ‘$r$’)  is inversely proportional to','[{"text": "${r^{3}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${r^{2}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${r}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\sqrt{r}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Mean free path ${\\lambda_{m}=\\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{2} \\pi d^{2} n}}$<br>where ${d}$ = diameter of molecule<br>${\\Rightarrow \\lambda_{m} \\propto \\dfrac{1}{r^{2}}}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38396,'physics',26,1136,2717,'A small hole is made at a height of $\\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} m$ from the bottom of the cylindrical water tank and at a depth of $h = 1.73\\,m$ from the upper level of water in the tank. what is the distance, where the water emerging from the hole strike the ground?','[{"text": "$6\\\\,m$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$4\\\\,m$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$3\\\\,m$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2\\\\,m$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'<br>$R=2 \\sqrt{H H^{\\prime}}=2 \\times \\sqrt{\\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} \\times \\sqrt{3}}=2 m$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38397,'physics',28,1371,3283,'The temperature of one mole of diatomic gas changes from $4 T$ to $T$ in adiabatic process. If $R$ is universal gas constant. Then work done','[{"text": "$\\\\dfrac{15 R T}{3}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{15 R T}{2}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{3 R T}{15}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{12 R T}{5}$<br>", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'<br>$W=\\dfrac{R}{\\gamma-1}\\left[T_{2}-T_{1}\\right]=\\dfrac{R}{\\dfrac{7}{5}-1}(4 T-T)$<br>$=\\dfrac{5 R}{2}(3 T)=\\dfrac{15 R T}{2}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38398,'physics',27,1382,3323,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> A solid sphere of copper of radius $R$ and a hollow sphere of the same material of inner radius $r$ and outer radius $R$ are heated to the same temperature and allowed to cool in the same environment. The hollow sphere cools faster.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Rate of cooling is according to Stefan\'s law which is $E \\alpha T^{4}$.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Assertion is correct but its reason is by formula for convection<br>$\\dfrac{d Q}{d t}=m s \\dfrac{d T}{d t}=h A\\left(T-T_{0}\\right)$<br>It is not by Stefan\'s law. So correct option is (3).',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(46482,'physics',42,323,593,'Which of the following semi - conducting devices is used as voltage regulator?','[{"text": "Zener diode", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Laser diode", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Photo diode", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Solar cell", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Zener diode is a device which is used as voltage regulator when operated in reverse bias',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38399,'physics',26,1136,2717,'A tank containing liquid has an orifice in one vertical side. If the center of orifice is $19.6\\,m$ below the surface level in the tank. Find the velocity of discharge assuming that is no wastage of energy.','[{"text": "$19.1\\\\,m{\\\\rm{/}}s$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$19.6\\\\,m{\\\\rm{/}}s$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$15\\\\,m{\\\\rm{/}}s$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$9.8\\\\,m{\\\\rm{/}}s$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Velocity at orifice<br>$ = \\sqrt {2gh}  = \\sqrt {2 \\times 9.8 \\times 19.6}  = 19.6\\,m{\\rm{/}}s$.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38400,'physics',29,1301,3149,'At what temperature the average speed of ${{O}_{2}}$ will be ${\\left(\\dfrac{7}{4}\\right)^{\\text {th }}}$ of rms speed of ${N_{2}}$ at ${47^{\\circ} {C}}$ ?','[{"text": "${1320 k}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${1600 k}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${850 k}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${700 k}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'${\\left(v_{O_{2}}\\right)_{a v}=\\dfrac{7}{4}\\left(v_{r m s}\\right)_{N_{2}}}$<br>${T_{1}=}$ ?, ${T_{2}=320 {~K}}$<br>${\\sqrt{\\dfrac{8 R T_{1}}{\\pi M_{1}}}=\\dfrac{7}{4} \\sqrt{\\dfrac{3 R T_{2}}{M_{2}}}}$<br>${\\dfrac{8 R T_{1}}{3.14 \\times 32}=\\dfrac{49 \\times 3 R \\times 320}{16 \\times 28} \\Rightarrow T_{1}=1320 K}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38401,'physics',28,1371,3283,'In an adiabatic process if pressure is increased by $\\dfrac{2}{3} \\%$ if $\\dfrac{C_{P}}{C_{V}}=\\dfrac{3}{2}$, then the volume decreases by about','[{"text": "$\\\\dfrac{4}{9} \\\\%$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{2}{3} \\\\%$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$4 \\\\%$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{9}{4} \\\\%$<br>", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'$P{V^\\gamma } = K, \\Rightarrow \\frac{{\\Delta P}}{P} + \\gamma \\frac{{\\Delta V}}{V} = 0$<br>$\\frac{{\\Delta V}}{V} \\times 100 = \\frac{{ - 1}}{\\gamma } \\times \\frac{{\\Delta P}}{P} \\times 100$<br>$ = \\frac{{ - 2}}{3} \\times \\frac{2}{3} =  - \\frac{4}{9}\\% $<br>So correct option is (1)',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38402,'physics',27,1382,3324,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> It is hotter over the top of a fire than at the same distance of the side.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Air surrounding the fire conducts more heat upward.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'It is indeed hotter over the top of a fire than at the same distance on the side as at top, it is both convection and radiation while on \'side ways\' only radiation. So correct option is (3).',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38403,'physics',29,1300,3145,'The expansion of an ideal gas of mass ${m}$ at constant pressure ${P}$ is given by the straight line $B.$ The expansion of the same ideal gas of mass ${3 m}$ at pressure ${3 P}$ is given by the line','[{"text": "${C}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${A}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${B}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "None of these", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'${P V=\\dfrac{m R T}{M} \\Rightarrow V=\\dfrac{m R T}{M P}}$<br>Slope ${=\\dfrac{m R}{M P}}$<br>Now, for mass ${2 m}$ and pressure ${2 p}$, slope<br>$=\\dfrac{2 m \\times R}{M \\times 2 P}$<br>${\\therefore}$ It represents the straight line ${B}$.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/29_38403_question_1.png'),
(38404,'physics',26,1135,2714,'The cylindrical tube of spray pump has a cross-section of $8\\,c{m^2}$, one end of which has 40 fine holes each of area ${10^{ - 8}}\\,{m^2}$. If the liquid flows inside the tube with a speed of $0.15\\,m\\,{\\rm{min} ^{ - 1}},$ the speed with which the liquid is ejected through the holes is','[{"text": "$50\\\\,m{s^{ - 1}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$5\\\\,m{s^{ - 1}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$0.50\\\\,m{s^{ - 1}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$0.5\\\\,m{s^{ - 1}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'From equation of continuity,<br>$a v=$ constant<br>$\\Rightarrow\\left(a_{1} v_{1}\\right)_{\\text {tube }}=\\left(a_{2} v_{2}\\right)_{\\text {holes }}$<br>$\\left(8 \\times 10^{-4}\\right) \\times\\left(\\dfrac{0.15}{60}\\right)=\\left(40 \\times 10^{-8}\\right) \\times v$<br>$ \\Rightarrow v = \\frac{{8 \\times {{10}^{ - 4}} \\times 0.15}}{{40 \\times {{10}^{ - 8}} \\times 60}} = 5\\,m{s^{ - 1}}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38405,'physics',28,1371,3283,'The $\\mathrm{P}-\\mathrm{V}$ diagram of path followed by one mole of perfect gas in a cylindrical container is shown in figure, the work done when the gas is taken from state $A$ to state $B$ is:','[{"text": "$2 P_{2} V_{1}\\\\left[1-\\\\dfrac{\\\\sqrt{V_{2}}}{\\\\sqrt{V_{1}}}\\\\right]$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2 P_{1} V_{1}\\\\left[1-\\\\dfrac{\\\\sqrt{V_{1}}}{\\\\sqrt{V_{2}}}\\\\right]$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$2 P_{1} V_{2}\\\\left[1-\\\\dfrac{\\\\sqrt{V_{1}}}{\\\\sqrt{V_{2}}}\\\\right]$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2 P_{2} V_{2}\\\\left[1-\\\\dfrac{\\\\sqrt{V_{1}}}{\\\\sqrt{V_{2}}}\\\\right]$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'For path $A$ to $B \\quad P V^{3 / 2}=$ constant $=A$ then work done<br>$\\begin{aligned}& W=\\int_{V_{1}}^{V_{2}} P d V=\\int_{V_{1}}^{V_{2}} \\dfrac{A}{V^{3 / 2}}=A\\left[\\dfrac{V^{-1 / 2}}{-1 / 2}\\right]_{V_{1}}^{V_{2}} \\\\& =-2 A\\left[V^{-1 / 2}\\right]_{V_{1}}^{V_{2}}=-2 A 2 P_{1} V_{1}\\left[1-\\dfrac{\\sqrt{V_{1}}}{\\sqrt{V_{2}}}\\right] \\\\& =2 A\\left[\\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{V_{1}}}-\\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{V_{2}}}\\right] \\\\& \\Rightarrow 2 P_{1} V_{1}^{3 / 2}\\left[\\dfrac{\\sqrt{V_{2}}-\\sqrt{V_{1}}}{\\sqrt{V_{1} V_{2}}}\\right]=2 P_{1} V_{1}\\left[1-\\dfrac{\\sqrt{V_{1}}}{\\sqrt{V_{2}}}\\right] \\\\& {\\left[\\because P_{1} V_{1}^{3 / 2}=\\text { constant }\\right] .}\\end{aligned}$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/28_38405_question_1.png'),
(38406,'physics',27,1382,3323,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> If the temperature of a star is doubled then the rate of loss of heat from it becomes 16 times.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Stars have negative specific heat capacity.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'They are separate facts. By Stefan law,<br>(Rate of loss of heat) $\\propto(T)^{4}$<br>$\\Rightarrow$ If temperature $T$ is doubled, the rate will increaseby $(2)^{4}=16$ times .<br>$\\Rightarrow$ Assertion is true.<br>For \'Reason\' (which is also true): In stars, hydrogen is converted to helium by fusion. $\\Rightarrow$ The meanmolecular mass of particles increases and core collapses resulting in \'contraction\' $\\Rightarrow$ Potential energy decreases. It cools down if temperature is increased. $\\Rightarrow$ Star has negative specific heat. So correct option is (2).',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49049,'biology',80,470,949,'Sunlight is essential for plant was concluded by','[{"text": "Joseph Priestley", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Jan Ingenhousz", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "van Niel", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Sach", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Jan Ingenhousz showed that sunlight is essential for plant process using the Priestley bell jar setup.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38407,'physics',29,1303,3152,'A mixture of ${n_{1}}$ moles of monatomic gas and  ${n_{2}}$ moles of diatomic gas has ${\\dfrac{C_{P}}{C_{V}}=\\gamma=\\dfrac{17}{11}}$.  Then','[{"text": "${n_{1}=2 n_{2}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${7 n_{1}=n_{2}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${n_{1}=4 n_{2}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${3 n_{1}=5 n_{2}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'${\\gamma=\\dfrac{n_{1} C_{R_{1}}+n_{2} C_{P_{2}}}{n_{1} C_{V_{1}}+n_{2} C_{V_{2}}} \\gamma=1.5}$ (Given)<br>$\\begin{aligned}& \\therefore C_{P_{1}}=\\dfrac{5 R}{2} ; C_{P_{2}}=\\dfrac{7 R}{2} \\\\& C_{V_{1}}=\\dfrac{3 R}{2} ; C_{V_{2}}=\\dfrac{5 R}{2} ; \\text { then } n_{1}=2 n_{2}\\end{aligned}$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38408,'physics',26,1136,2715,'Water flows along a horizontal pipe whose cross-section is not constant. The pressure is $1\\,cm$ of $H g$ where the velocity is $35\\,cm\\,{s^{ - 1}}$. At a point where the velocity is $65\\,cm\\,{s^{ - 1}}$, the pressure will be','[{"text": "$0.89\\\\,cm$ of $Hg$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$8.9\\\\,cm$ of $Hg$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$0.5\\\\,cm$ of $Hg$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1\\\\,cm$ of $Hg$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Using Bernoulli\'s theorem,<br>${p_1} + \\frac{1}{2}\\rho v_1^2 = {p_2} + \\frac{1}{2}\\rho v_2^2\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\left( 1 \\right)$<br>Here, $p_{1}=\\rho_{m} g h_{1}=13600 \\times 9.8 \\times 10^{-2}$<br>$p_{2}=13600 \\times 9.8 \\times h$<br>$\\rho  = 1000\\,kg{m^{ - 3}}$<br>${v_1} = 35 \\times {10^{ - 2}}\\,m{s^{ - 1}},{v_2} = 65 \\times {10^{ - 2}}\\,m{s^{ - 1}}$<br>$\\therefore$ From Eq.(1), we get<br>$\\Rightarrow 13600 \\times 9.8 \\times 10^{-2}+\\dfrac{1}{2} \\times 1000 \\times(0.35)^{2}$<br>$=13600 \\times 9.8 \\times h+\\dfrac{1}{2} \\times 1000 \\times(0.65)^{2}$<br>$\\Rightarrow 13600 \\times 9.8 \\times h$<br>$=13600 \\times 9.8 \\times 10^{-2}+\\dfrac{1}{2} 10^{3}\\left[(0.35)^{2}-(0.65)^{2}\\right]$<br>$\\Rightarrow 13600 \\times 9.8 \\times h$<br>$=13600 \\times 9.8 \\times 10^{-2}-500(0.3)$<br>After solving, $h = 0.89\\,cm$ of $Hg$.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38409,'physics',28,1371,3284,'An ideal gas has volume $V_{0}$ at $27^\\circ C$. It is heated at constant pressure so that its volume becomes $2 V_{0}$. The final temperature is','[{"text": "$54^\\\\circ C$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$32.6^\\\\circ C$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$327^\\\\circ C$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$150^\\\\circ C$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'$\\dfrac{T_{2}}{T_{1}}=\\dfrac{V_{2}}{V_{1}}=2 \\Rightarrow T_{2}=2 \\times T_{1}=2 \\times 300$<br>$ = 600\\,K = 327^\\circ C.{\\rm{ }}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38411,'physics',26,1137,2722,'The velocity of the surface layer of water in a river of depth $20\\,m$ is $5\\,m{\\rm{/}}s$. The shearing stress between the surface layer and the bottom layer<br>is $\\left( {\\eta  = {{10}^{ - 3}}poise} \\right)$','[{"text": "$2.5 \\\\times {10^{ - 5}}\\\\,N{\\\\rm{/}}{m^2}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$3 \\\\times {10^{ - 4}}\\\\,N{\\\\rm{/}}{m^2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$3.5 \\\\times {10^{ - 5}}\\\\,N{\\\\rm{/}}{m^2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$4 \\\\times {10^{ - 3}}\\\\,N{\\\\rm{/}}{m^2}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Velocity gradient or strain rate in liquid<br>$\\eta=\\dfrac{\\text { shearing stress }}{\\text { strain rate }}$<br>$\\Rightarrow$ shearing stress $=\\eta \\times$ strain rate<br>Shearing stress $=10^{-4} \\times \\dfrac{5}{20}=0.25 \\times 10^{-4}$<br>$ = 2.5 \\times {10^{ - 5}}\\,N{\\rm{/}}{m^2}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38412,'physics',29,1303,3151,'${5 n, n}$ and ${5 n}$ moles of a monatomic, diatomic and nonlinear polyatomic gases (which do not react chemically with each other) are mixed at room temperature. The equivalent degree of freedom of the mixture is','[{"text": "${\\\\dfrac{25}{7}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\dfrac{48}{11}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\dfrac{52}{11}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\dfrac{50}{11}}$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'${f_{\\text {eq }}=\\dfrac{f_{1} n_{1}+f_{2} n_{2}+f_{3} n_{3}}{n_{1}+n_{2}+n_{3}}}$<br>${=\\dfrac{(5 n)(3)+(n)(5)+(5 n)(6)}{5 n+n+5 n}=\\dfrac{50}{11}}$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38413,'physics',28,1371,3284,'A gas is compressed at a constant pressure of $50\\,N{\\rm{/}}{m^2}$ from a volume of $10\\,{m^3}$ to volume of $4\\,{m^3}$. Energy of $100\\,joules$ is thus added to gas by heating. Its internal energy is:','[{"text": "$450\\\\,J$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$400\\\\,J$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$350\\\\,J$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$410\\\\,J$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'As volume decreases, work is done on the gas and so $-v e$.<br>$W=50[4-10]$<br>$ =  - 300{\\rm{ }}Joule$<br>Heat supplied, $Q = 100{\\rm{ }}Joule$<br>$Q=\\Delta U+W \\quad$ (${{\\rm{1}}^{{\\rm{st}}}}$ Law of thermodynamics)<br>$\\Delta U=Q-W$<br>$=100-(-300)$<br>$ = 400{\\rm{ }}Joule.$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38415,'physics',26,1137,2723,'A spherical body falling through a viscous liquid of infinite extent ultimately attains a constant value of velocity then','[{"text": "Upthrust + weight $=$ viscous frag", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Weight + viscous drag = upthrust", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Viscous drag + upthrust $=$ weight", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Viscous drag + upthrust $>$ weight", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Viscous drag and upthrust acts upward and weight acts downward for constant velocity (terminal velocity). Viscous drag to liquid and upthrust should balance weight of the ball.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38416,'physics',29,1302,3150,'The gases carbon-monoxide $(CO)$ and oxgen ${\\left({O}_{2}\\right)}$ at the same temperature, have kinetic energies ${E_{1}}$ and ${E_{2}}$, respectively. Then','[{"text": "${E_{1}=E_{2}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${E_{1} < E_{2}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${E_{1}>E_{2}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${E_{1}}$ and ${E_{2}}$ cannot be compared", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'The gases carbon-monoxide (${C O}$) and nitrogen ${\\left(N_{2}\\right)}$ are diatomic, so both have equal kinetic energy ${\\left(\\dfrac{5}{2} k T\\right)}$, $i.\\,e.$ ${E_{1}=E_{2}}$.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38417,'physics',28,1370,3280,'The molar specific heat of oxygen at constant pressure ${C_P} = 7.03\\,cal{\\rm{/}}mol^\\circ C$ and $R = 8.31\\,J{\\rm{/}}mol^\\circ C$. The amount of heat taken by $5\\,mol$ of oxygen when heated at constant volume from $10^\\circ C$ to $20^\\circ C$ will be approximately.','[{"text": "$25\\\\,\\\\,cal$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$50\\\\,\\\\,cal$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$250\\\\,\\\\,cal$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$500\\\\,\\\\,cal$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'When volume is constant<br>$\\Delta Q = n{C_V}\\Delta T = n\\left( {{C_p} - R} \\right)\\Delta T$<br>$ = 5(7.03 - 1.9) \\times 10 = 250\\,cal$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38419,'physics',29,1300,3145,'A graph between pressure P (along \\(Y\\)-axis) and absolute temperature \\(T\\) (along \\(X\\)-axis) for equal moles of two gases has been drawn. Given that volume of second gas is more than volume of first gas. Which of the following statements is correct?<div class="matching-question"></div>','[{"text": "Slope of gas 1 is less than gas 2", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Slope of gas 1 is more than gas 2", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both have some slopes", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "None of the above", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'According to ideal gas equation,<br>$\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,PV = nRT$<br>${\\rm{or }}\\quad \\frac{p}{T} = \\frac{{nR}}{V}$<br>where $\\dfrac{p}{T}$ represents slope of the graph<br>As, the number of moles are the same for the <br>two gases,<br>$\\therefore \\quad \\dfrac{p}{T} \\propto \\dfrac{1}{V}$<br>$\\therefore V_{1}>V_{2} \\quad$ (Given)<br>$\\therefore(\\text { Slope })_{2} < (\\text { Slope })_{1}$<br>or $(\\text { Slope })_{1}>(\\text { Slope })_{2}$',NULL,'Medium','Graph/Figure',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/29_38419_question_1.png'),
(38420,'physics',26,1137,2722,'A layer of glycerin of thinkness $3\\,mm$ is present between a large surface and a surface of area of $0.2\\,{m^2}$, with what force the small surface is to be pulled, so that it can move with a velocity of $5\\,m{\\rm{/}}s$? $\\left( {\\eta  = 0.09\\,kg{m^{ - 1}}\\,{s^{ - 1}}} \\right)$.','[{"text": "$10\\\\,N$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$30\\\\,N$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$40\\\\,N$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$50\\\\,N$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'$\\eta  = 0.09kg{m^{ - 1}}\\,{s^{ - 1}},\\,dv = 5\\,m{\\rm{/}}s,$<br>$dx = 3 \\times {10^{ - 3}}\\,m,\\,A = 0.2\\,{m^2}$<br>$F = \\eta \\frac{{Adv}}{{dx}} = \\frac{{0.09 \\times 0.2 \\times 5}}{{3 \\times {{10}^{ - 3}}}} = 30\\,N$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38421,'physics',28,1371,3285,'In an isochoric if ${T_1} = 27^\\circ C$ and ${T_2} = 127^\\circ C$, then $P_{1} / P_{2}$ will be equal to','[{"text": "$9 / 59$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2 / 3$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$3 / 4$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "None of these.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'At constant volume<br>$P \\propto T \\Rightarrow \\dfrac{P_{1}}{P_{2}}=\\dfrac{T_{1}}{T_{2}} \\Rightarrow \\dfrac{P_{1}}{P_{2}}=\\dfrac{300}{400}=\\dfrac{3}{4}$.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38423,'physics',26,1137,2722,'A square plate of $0.5\\,m$ side length parallel to a second plate with a velocity of $0.5\\,m{\\rm{/}}s$, both plates being immersed in water. If the viscous force is $0.081\\,N$ and the coefficient of viscosity is 0.02 poise, distance between the plates in $cm$ is','[{"text": "$2\\\\,cm$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1\\\\,cm$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$0.3\\\\,cm$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$3\\\\,cm$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'$F=\\eta \\dfrac{A d v}{d x} \\Rightarrow d x=\\eta \\dfrac{A d v}{F}$<br>$=\\dfrac{0.002 \\times 0.25 \\times 0.5}{0.081}$<br>$\\therefore dx = 0.00308\\,m = 0.308\\,cm$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38424,'physics',29,1300,3146,'Which one of the following is not an assumption in the kinetic theory of gases?','[{"text": "The volume occupied by the molecules of the gas is negligible.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "The force of attraction between the molecules is negligible.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "The collision between molecules are elastic.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "All molecules have same speed.", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'All molecules have same speed. <br>This statement is true but this is not an assumption of kinetic theory of gases.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38425,'physics',28,1372,3294,'A thermodynamic system is taken through the cycle $P Q R S P$ process. The net work done by the system is','[{"text": "$20\\\\,J$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$-40\\\\,J$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$400\\\\,J$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$-374\\\\,J$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Work done $=$ area of the cycle.<br>$W =  - 200\\,cc \\times 200\\,KPa(( - )$ sign indicates the path is in anticlockwise direction).$ =  - 200 \\times {10^{ - 6}} \\times 200 \\times {10^3} =  - 40\\,J$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/28_38425_question_1.png'),
(38426,'physics',27,1378,3304,'The graph between two temperature scales \\(P\\) and \\(Q\\) is shown in the figure. Between upper fixed point and lower fixed point there are 150 equal divisions of scale \\(P\\) and 100 divisions on scale \\(Q\\). The relationship for conversion between the two scales is given by<div class="matching-question"></div>','[{"text": "$\\\\dfrac{t_{Q}}{150}=\\\\dfrac{t_{p}-180}{100}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{t_{P}}{180}=\\\\dfrac{t_{Q}-40}{100}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{t_{P}}{100}=\\\\dfrac{t_{Q}-180}{150}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{t_{Q}}{100}=\\\\dfrac{t_{P}-30}{150}$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'According to the concept of thermometer scale,<br>$\\frac{{{\\rm{Reading }}\\,\\,{\\rm{on}}\\,\\,{\\rm{ scale}}\\,\\,{\\rm{ - }}\\,\\,{\\rm{Lower}}\\,\\,{\\rm{ fixed }}\\,\\,{\\rm{point}}}}{{{\\rm{Upper }}\\,\\,{\\rm{fixed }}\\,\\,{\\rm{point}}\\,\\,{\\rm{ - }}\\,\\,{\\rm{Lower }}\\,\\,{\\rm{fixed }}\\,\\,{\\rm{point}}}}$<br>$=$ remains constant for any temperature scale<br>$\\dfrac{t_{P}-30}{180-30}=\\dfrac{t_{Q}-0}{100-0} \\Rightarrow \\dfrac{t_{P}-30}{150}=\\dfrac{t_{Q}}{100}$',NULL,'Hard','Graph/Figure',2023,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/27_38426_question_1.png'),
(46547,'physics',42,323,592,'The increase in the width of the depletion region in a $p$-$n$ junction diode is due to','[{"text": "Reverse bias only", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both forward bias and reverse bias", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Increase in forward current", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Forward bias only", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'In reverse bias external battery attract majority charge carriers. So width of the depletion region increase',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2020,'NEET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38427,'physics',26,1137,2723,'Ten drops of a liquid of density $\\rho$ and each of radius $r$ are falling through air with a terminal velocity $12\\,cm{\\rm{/}}s$. When the 8 drops out of 10 drops coalesce to from a single drop, the terminal velocity of the new drop will be','[{"text": "$48\\\\,cm\\\\,{s^{ - 1}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$52\\\\,cm\\\\,{s^{ - 1}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$68\\\\,cm\\\\,{s^{ - 1}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$70\\\\,cm\\\\,{s^{ - 1}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Eight drops coalesce to form one drop of radius $\\mathrm{R}$.<br>Volume of Big drop$ = \\frac{4}{3}\\pi {R^3}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\left( 1 \\right)$<br>Volume of each smaller drop $ = \\frac{4}{3}\\pi {r^3}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\left( 2 \\right)$<br>From equations (1) & (2) we get<br>$\\dfrac{4}{3} \\pi R^{3}=8 \\times \\dfrac{4}{3} \\pi r^{3} \\Rightarrow R=2 r$<br>For a liquid drop<br>${v_r} \\propto {r^2} \\Rightarrow \\frac{{{v_r}}}{{{v_R}}} = {\\left( {\\frac{r}{{2r}}} \\right)^2} = \\frac{1}{4}$<br>$ \\Rightarrow {v_R} = 4 \\times 12 = 48\\,cm\\,{s^{ - 1}}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38428,'physics',29,1300,3145,'By what percentage should the pressure of a given mass of a gas be increased, so as to decrease its volume by $10 \\%$ at a constant temperature?','[{"text": "$5 \\\\%$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$7.2 \\\\%$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$12.5 \\\\%$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$11.1 \\\\%$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'From ideal gas equation,<br>$p V=\\mu R T$<br>At constant temperature,<br>$p V=\\text { constant }$<br>$i.\\,e.$ $p V=c$<br>Hence, ${p_1}{V_1} = {p_2}{V_2}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,(1)$<br>Given, $V_{2}=V_{1}-\\dfrac{10}{100} V_{1}=(0.90) V_{1}$<br>Form Eq. (1),<br>${p_2} = \\frac{{{p_1}{V_1}}}{{{V_2}}} = \\frac{{{p_1}{V_1}}}{{0.90{V_1}}} = \\frac{{{p_1}}}{{0.90}}$<br>$ \\Rightarrow \\frac{{{p_2}}}{{{p_1}}} = \\frac{1}{{0.90}} = \\frac{{10}}{9}$<br>$\\frac{{{p_2} - {p_1}}}{{{p_1}}} = \\frac{{10 - 9}}{9} = \\left( {\\frac{1}{9}} \\right)$<br>Therefore, $\\%$ increase in pressure<br>$=\\dfrac{100 \\%}{9}=11.1 \\%$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38429,'physics',28,1372,3294,'An ideal gas is taken through a cyclic thermodynamical process through four steps. The amounts of heat involved in these steps are:${Q_1} = 5960\\,J,{Q_2} =  - 5585\\,J,{Q_3} =  - 2980\\,J$, ${Q_4} = 3645\\,J$ respectively. The corresponding works involved are ${W_1} = 2200\\,J,{W_2} =  - 825\\,J,{W_3} =  - 1100\\,J$ and $W_{4}$ respectively. The value of $W_{4}$ is :','[{"text": "$1315\\\\,J$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$275\\\\,J$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$765\\\\,J$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$675\\\\,J$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'$\\Delta Q=Q_{1}+Q_{2}+Q_{3}+Q_{4}$<br>$ = 5960 - 5585 - 2980 + 3645 = 1040\\,J.$<br>$\\Delta W=W_{1}+W_{2}+W_{3}+W_{4}$<br>$=2200-825-1100+W_{4}$<br>For cyclic process,<br>$U_{f}=U_{i}$<br>$\\Delta U=U_{f}=U_{i}=0$<br>From first law of thermodynamic<br>$\\Delta Q = \\Delta U + \\Delta W$<br>$1040 = 0 + 275 + {W_4}$<br>$\\therefore {W_4} = 765\\,J.$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38431,'physics',29,1300,3146,'When a van der Waals\' gas undergoes free expansion, then its temperature','[{"text": "decreases", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "increases", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "does not change", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "depends upon the nature of the gas", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'When a van der Waals\' gas (real gas) undergoes free expansion,<br> then its temperature decreases.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38432,'physics',26,1137,2723,'If the terminal speed of a sphere of gold (density $\\left. { = 19.5 \\times {{10}^3}\\,kg{\\rm{/}}{m^3}} \\right)$ is $0.5\\,m{\\rm{/}}s$ in a viscous liquid $\\left(\\right.$ density$\\left. { = 3\\,kg{\\rm{/}}{m^3}} \\right)$. Find the terminal velocity of a sphere of silver (density $=10.5 \\times 10^{3} \\mathrm{~kg} / \\mathrm{m}^{3}$ ) of the same size in the same liquid.','[{"text": "$0.30\\\\,m{\\\\rm{/}}s$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$0.20\\\\,m{\\\\rm{/}}s$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$0.51\\\\,m{\\\\rm{/}}s$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$0.22\\\\,m{\\\\rm{/}}s$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'$V_{T}=\\dfrac{2}{9} \\dfrac{r^{3}(\\rho-\\sigma) g}{\\eta} \\rho=$ density of sphere<br>$\\sigma=$ density of liquid<br>$\\therefore V \\propto(\\rho-\\sigma) \\Rightarrow \\dfrac{V_{g}}{V_{s}}=\\dfrac{\\rho_{g}-\\sigma}{\\rho_{s}-\\sigma}$<br>$\\dfrac{0.5}{V_{s}}=\\dfrac{19.5-3}{10.5-3}=\\dfrac{16.5}{7.5} \\Rightarrow V_{s}=\\dfrac{0.5 \\times 7.5}{16.5}$<br>${V_s} = 0.22\\,m{\\rm{/}}s$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38433,'physics',28,1372,3294,'If ${Q_{iaf}} = 80\\,cal,{W_{iaf}} = 60\\,cal$ and $W =  - 30\\,cal$ for the curved path $f \\rightarrow i$, then value of $Q$ for path $f \\rightarrow i$, will be','[{"text": "$ 50\\\\,cal$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$ 30\\\\,cal$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$ - 30\\\\,cal$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$ - 50\\\\,cal$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'For cyclic process<br>$\\Delta U = 0$<br>$80 + Q = 60 + ( - 30)$<br>$ \\Rightarrow Q =  - 50\\,cal$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/28_38433_question_1.png'),
(38434,'physics',27,1381,3314,'Heat energy of $184\\,kJ$ is given to ice of mass $600\\,g$ at $ - 12^\\circ C$. Specific heat of ice is $2222.3\\,J\\,k{g^{ - 10}}{C^{ - 1}}$ and latent heat of ice is $336\\,kJ/k{g^{ - 1}}$.<br>A. Final temperature of system will be $0^\\circ C$<br>B. Final temperature of the system will be greater than $0^\\circ C$.<br>C. The final system will have a mixture of ice and water in the ratio of $5: 1$.<br>D. The final system will have a mixture of ice and water in the ratio of $1: 5$.<br>E. The final system will have water only.<br>Choose the correct answer from the options given below:','[{"text": "A and D only", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "A and C only", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "A and E only", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "B and D only", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Given: $\\Delta Q = 184\\,kJ,m = 600\\,g,{T_1} =  - 12^\\circ C$<br>${C_{{\\text{ice}}}} = 2222.3\\,J/kg^\\circ C,\\,\\,{L_{{\\text{ice}}}} = 336\\,kJ/kg$<br>Heat used to increase temperature from $ - 12^\\circ C$ to $0^\\circ C$<br>$Q_{1}=m C \\Delta T$<br>$=\\dfrac{600}{1000} \\times 2222.3 \\times 12$<br>$ = 16000.56{\\rm{ }}J = 16{\\rm{ }}K{\\rm{ }}J$<br>Remaining amount of heat,<br>${\\Delta {Q_1} = 184000 - 16000.56}$<br>${\\Delta {Q_1} = 167999.44\\,J = 168KJ}$<br>For melting of $0.6\\,Kg$ of ice<br>$\\Delta {Q_2} = mL = \\frac{{600}}{{1000}} \\times 336000 = 201600\\,J$<br>$ = 201.6{\\rm{ }}KJ$<br>As, $\\Delta Q_{2}>\\Delta Q_{1}$ so all ice will not melt and final temperature of the mixture will be $0^\\circ C$.<br>Let amount of ice melted be $m^{\\prime}$.<br>$\\therefore 167999.44=m^{\\prime} \\times 336000$<br>${m^\\prime } = 0.4999\\,kg = 0.5\\,kg$.<br>So, mass of water is $0.4999\\,kg = 0.5\\,kg$<br>mass of ice $ = 0.600 - 0.4999 = 0.1001\\,kg$<br>Ratio of ice to water $=\\dfrac{0.1001}{0.5}=1: 5$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2023,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38464,'physics',26,1138,2730,'A small hollow sphere which has a small hole in it is immersed in water to a depth of $20\\,cm$ before any water is penetrated into it. If the surface tension of water is $0.073\\,N{m^{ - 1}}$, what is the radius of the hole?','[{"text": "$0.07\\\\,mm$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$0.04\\\\,mm$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$0.01\\\\,mm$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$0.05\\\\,mm$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'$P=\\dfrac{2 S}{R} \\Rightarrow R=\\dfrac{2 S}{P}=\\dfrac{2 \\times 0.073}{0.2 \\times 10^{3} \\times 9.8}$<br>$=0.0744 \\times 10^{-3}$<br>$R = 0.07\\,mm$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38435,'physics',26,1137,2723,'Two small spherical drops having radii in the ratio $4: 3$ fall from a height through the atmosphere. Their momenta ratio on reaching the earth has the value','[{"text": "4.4", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "1.2", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "4.21", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "5.1<br>", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'$P=m v$<br>Terminal Velocity $v \\propto r^{2} \\Rightarrow \\dfrac{V_{1}}{V_{2}}=\\left(\\dfrac{r_{1}}{r_{2}}\\right)^{2}=\\dfrac{16}{9}$<br>mass $=\\dfrac{4}{3} \\pi r^{3} \\rho \\Rightarrow m \\propto r^{3}$<br>$\\therefore$ Momentums are in ratio<br>$\\begin{aligned}\\dfrac{p_{1}}{p_{2}} & =\\dfrac{r_{1}^{3}}{r_{2}^{3}} \\times \\dfrac{V_{1}}{V_{2}}=\\left(\\dfrac{4}{3}\\right)^{3} \\times \\dfrac{16}{9} \\\\& =\\dfrac{64}{27}=\\dfrac{16}{9}=\\dfrac{1024}{243}=4: 21\\end{aligned}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38436,'physics',29,1300,3145,'An electron tube was sealed OFF during manufacture at a pressure of  $1.2 \\times {10^{ - 7}}\\,mm$ of mercury at  $27^\\circ C.$ Its volume is $100\\,c{m^3}.$ The number of molecules that remains in the tube is','[{"text": "$2 \\\\times 10^{16}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$3 \\\\times 10^{15}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$3.86 \\\\times 10^{11}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$5 \\\\times 10^{11}$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'From ideal gal equation,<br>$p V=\\mu R T$<br>Given, $p = 1.2 \\times {10^{ - 7}}\\,mm$<br>$\\quad  = 1.2 \\times {10^{ - 7}} \\times {10^{ - 3}}\\,m = 1.2 \\times {10^{ - 10}}\\,m$<br>$T = 27^\\circ C = (27 + 273)K$  and<br>$V = 100\\,c{m^3} = {10^{ - 4}}\\,{m^3}$<br>$ \\Rightarrow 1.2 \\times {10^{ - 7}} \\times 133.322 \\times {10^{ - 4}}$<br>$ = \\mu  \\times 8.31 \\times (273 + 27)$<br>$\\therefore \\quad $Number of moles, $\\mu=6.42 \\times 10^{-13}$<br>Hence, number of molecules<br>$ = 6.42 \\times {10^{ - 13}} \\times 6.02 \\times {10^{23}}$<br>$ = 3.86 \\times {10^{11}}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38437,'physics',28,1372,3294,'The heat energy absorbed by a system in going through a cyclic process shown in figure is','[{"text": "$10^{3} \\\\pi J$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$10^{2} \\\\pi J$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$10^{4} \\\\pi J$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$10^{7} \\\\pi J$<br>", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'From $P-V$ diagram work done by system in cyclic process is equal to area of the loop then Heat absorbed = Workdone $=$ area of the loop.<br>$\\pi {r^2} = \\pi {\\left( {\\frac{{30 - 10}}{2}} \\right)^2} = \\pi  \\times {10^2}\\,J$.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/28_38437_question_1.png'),
(38439,'physics',26,1138,2727,'What is the force required to take away a flat plate of radius $10\\,cm$ from the surface of water, if surface tension of water is $72 \\times {10^{ - 3}}N{m^{ - 1}}?$','[{"text": "$5 \\\\times {10^{ - 2}}\\\\,N$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$4.5 \\\\times {10^{ - 2}}\\\\,N$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$3 \\\\times {10^{ - 2}}\\\\,N$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$3 \\\\times {10^3}\\\\,N$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'<br>$F = S \\times 2\\pi r = 72 \\times {10^{ - 3}} \\times 2 \\times 3.14 \\times 10 \\times {10^{ - 2}}$<br>$ = 4.5 \\times {10^{ - 2}}\\,N$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38440,'physics',29,1300,3145,'Two vessels $A$ and $B$ having equal volume contain equal masses of hydrogen in $A$ and $B$ helium in $B$ at $300\\,K.$ Then, mark the correct statement?','[{"text": "The pressure exerted by hydrogen is half that exerted by helium", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "The pressure exerted by hydrogen is equal to that exerted by helium", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Average $K E$ of the molecule of hydrogen is half the average $K E$ of the molecules of helium", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "The pressure exerted by hydrogen is twice that exerted by helium", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'As, $p V=n R T$<br>$\\Rightarrow p_{H_{2}}=\\dfrac{m}{M_{H_{2}}} \\cdot \\dfrac{R T}{V}$<br>and $p_{H e}=\\dfrac{m}{M_{H e}} \\cdot \\dfrac{R T}{V}$<br>$\\therefore \\frac{{{p_{{H_2}}}}}{{{p_{He}}}} = \\frac{{{M_{He}}}}{{{M_{{H_2}}}}} = \\frac{4}{2} = 2$<br>$[\\because {M_{{H_2}}} = 2$  and   $\\left.M_{H e}=4\\right]$<br>$\\Rightarrow p_{\\mathrm{H}_{2}}=2 p_{\\mathrm{He}}$<br>The pressure exerted by hydrogen is twice that exerted by helium.',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38441,'physics',28,1372,3294,'An ideal gas after going through a series of four thermodynamic states in order, reaches the initial state against (cyclic process). The amounts of heat and work involved in these states are<br>${Q_1} = 6000\\,J,{Q_2} =  - 5500\\,J$<br>${Q_3} =  - 3000\\,J,{Q_2} = 3500\\,J$<br>${W_1} = 2500\\,J,{W_2} =  - 1000\\,J$<br>${W_3} =  - 1200\\,J,{W_4} = x\\,J$<br>The ratio of net work done by the gas to the total heat absorbed by the gas is $\\eta$. The value of $x$ and $\\eta$ are nearly','[{"text": "$500,7.5 \\\\%$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$700,10.5 \\\\%$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$1000,21 \\\\%$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1500,15 \\\\%$<br>", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'$\\Delta Q=Q_{1}+Q_{2}+Q_{3}+Q_{4}$<br>$=6000-5500-3000+3500$<br>$\\Delta Q = 1000\\,J$<br>Total work done by ideal gas, i.e.,<br>$\\begin{aligned}W & =W_{1}+W_{2}+W_{3}+W_{4} \\\\& =2500-1000-1200+x=300+x\\end{aligned}$<br>For cyclic process, $\\Delta U=U_{f}-U_{i}=0$<br>So, from first law of thermodynamics,<br>i.e., $\\Delta Q=\\Delta U+W$<br>$\\Delta Q=W \\quad(\\therefore \\Delta U=0)$<br>We have, $1000=(300+x)$<br>i.e., $x=1000-300=700 \\mathrm{~J}$<br>Efficiency of the cycle is defined as<br>$\\eta=\\dfrac{\\text { Work done }}{\\text { heat Input }}$<br>$=\\dfrac{W}{Q_{1}+Q_{4}}=\\dfrac{\\Delta Q}{\\left(Q_{1}+Q_{4}\\right)}$<br>$=\\dfrac{1000}{(6000+3500)}=\\dfrac{1000}{9500} \\times 100$<br>$=0.105 \\times 100=10.5 \\%$<br>Correct option is (2).',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38443,'physics',26,1138,2728,'The surface tension of a liquid is $10\\,N{\\rm{/}}m$. If a thin film of the area $0.05\\,{m^2}$ is formed on a loop. Then the surface energy will be','[{"text": "$5\\\\,J$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$3\\\\,J$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2\\\\,J$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1\\\\,J$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Surface energy $=S \\times \\Delta A$<br>$ = 2 \\times 10 \\times 0.05 = 1\\,J$<br>(Because film has two surface)',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38444,'physics',29,1300,3145,'One litre of an ideal gas at $27^\\circ C$ is heated at a constant pressure to the $297^\\circ C.$ Then, the final volume is the approximately','[{"text": "$1.2\\\\,L$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1.9\\\\,L$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$19 L$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2.4 L$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'By Charles\' law,<br>$\\dfrac{V_{1}}{V_{2}}=\\dfrac{T_{1}}{T_{2}}$<br>So, $\\quad V_{2}=\\dfrac{V_{1} T_{2}}{T_{1}}$<br>Given, $V_{1}=1 L$,<br>${T_1} = 27^\\circ C = (27 + 273)K$  and<br>${T_2} = 297^\\circ C = (297 + 273)K$<br>Putting these values, we get<br>$\\therefore \\quad {V_2} = 1 \\times \\frac{{(297 + 273)}}{{(27 + 273)}}$<br>$ = \\frac{{570}}{{300}} = 1.9L$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38445,'physics',28,1372,3293,'$\\quad P-V$ diagram of an ideal gas is as shown in figure. Work done by the gas in the process $A B C D$ is:','[{"text": "$4 P_{0} V_{0}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2 P_{0} V_{0}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$3 P_{0} V_{0}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$P_{0} V_{0}$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'$W_{A B}=-P_{0} V_{0}$<br>$W_{B C}=0$ and $W_{C D}=4 P_{0} V_{0}$<br>$\\therefore W_{A B C D}=-P_{0} V_{0}+0+4 P_{0} V_{0}=3 P_{0} V_{0}$.',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/28_38445_question_1.png'),
(38447,'physics',29,1305,3155,'The mean free path of collision of gas molecules varies with its diameter $(d)$ of the molecules as','[{"text": "$d^{-1}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$d^{-2}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$d^{-3}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$d^{-4}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Mean free path of gas molecules,<br>$\\lambda=\\dfrac{\\lambda_{1}+\\lambda_{2}+\\cdots+\\lambda_{n}}{n}$<br>$\\lambda  = \\frac{1}{{\\sqrt 2 \\pi n{d^2}}}$<br>$ \\Rightarrow \\quad \\lambda  \\propto {d^{ - 2}}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38448,'physics',26,1138,2728,'The work done in increasing the size of a soap film from $11\\,cm \\times 7\\,cm$ to $20\\,cm \\times 8\\,cm$ is $8 \\times {10^{ - 2}}\\,J$. The surface tension of the film is','[{"text": "$4.8\\\\,N{\\\\rm{/}}m$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$5\\\\,N{\\\\rm{/}}m$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$6.2\\\\,N{\\\\rm{/}}m$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "None", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'As we know that $W=T \\times \\Delta A \\Rightarrow T=\\dfrac{W}{\\Delta A}$<br>$T = \\frac{{8 \\times {{10}^{ - 2}}}}{{2 \\times \\left[ {(20 \\times 8){{10}^{ - 4}} - (11 \\times 7){{10}^{ - 4}}} \\right]}}$<br>$ = \\frac{{8 \\times {{10}^{ - 2}}}}{{2\\left( {160 \\times {{10}^{ - 4}} - 77 \\times {{10}^{ - 4}}} \\right)}}$<br>$T = \\frac{{8 \\times {{10}^{ - 2}}}}{{2\\left( {83 \\times {{10}^{ - 4}}} \\right)}} = \\frac{{4 \\times {{10}^{ - 2}}}}{{83 \\times {{10}^{ - 4}}}} = 0.048 \\times {10^2}$<br>$ = 4.8\\,N{\\rm{/}}m$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38450,'physics',27,1381,3315,'$1\\,kg$ of water at $100^\\circ C$ is converted into steam at $100^\\circ C$ by boiling at atmospheric pressure. The volume of water changes from $1.00 \\times {10^{ - 3}}\\,{m^3}$ as a liquid to $1.671\\,{m^3}$ as steam. The change in internal energy of the system during the process will be (Given latent heat of vaporisation $ = 2257\\,kJ/kg$, Atmospheric pressure $ = 1 \\times {10^5}\\,Pa$)','[{"text": "$ + 2090\\\\,kJ$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$ - 2426\\\\,kJ$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$ + 2476\\\\,kJ$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$ - 2090\\\\,kJ$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'From first law of thermodynamics, $\\Delta Q=\\Delta U+\\Delta W$. Water expands against constant atmosphere pressure of $1 \\times 10^{5}$ pascal.<br>$\\therefore \\Delta U=\\Delta Q-\\Delta W=m L_{v}-P \\Delta V$<br>$ = (1\\,kg)\\left( {2257 \\times {{10}^3}\\,J/kg} \\right)$<br>$ - \\left( {1 \\times {{10}^5}\\,Pa} \\right)\\left( {1.671\\,{m^3} - 1 \\times {{10}^{ - 3}}\\,{m^3}} \\right)$<br>$ = 2257 \\times {10^3}\\,J - 167 \\times {10^3}\\,J = 2090\\,kJ$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',2023,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38451,'physics',26,1138,2728,'The surface tension of soap solution is $0.05\\,N{\\rm{/}}m$. The work done in blowing to form a soap bubble of surface area $80\\,c{m^2}$ is','[{"text": "$2 \\\\times {10^{ - 4}}\\\\,J$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$8 \\\\times {10^{ - 4}}\\\\,J$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$7 \\\\times {10^{ - 3}}\\\\,J$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$3 \\\\times {10^{ - 4}}\\\\,J$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'$W=(T \\times \\Delta A) \\times 2=\\left(0.05 \\times 80 \\times 10^{-4}\\right) \\times 2$<br>$\\therefore W=8 \\times 10^{-4} J$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38452,'physics',29,1301,3148,'At what temperature is the $rms$ velocity of a hydrogen molecule equal to that of an oxygen molecule at $47^\\circ C$ ?','[{"text": "$80 K$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$-73 K$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$3 K$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$20 K$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'We know that, $v_{\\text {rms }}=\\sqrt{\\dfrac{3 R T}{M}}$<br>In the given problem, $\\nu_{\\mathrm{rms}}$ and $R$ are constants.<br>$\\frac{T}{M} = {\\rm{ constant}}$<br>$\\frac{{{T_H}}}{{{M_H}}} = \\frac{{{T_O}}}{{{M_O}}}$<br>$\\left[ \\begin{array}{l}\n\\therefore {M_H} = 2,{M_O} = 32\\\\\n{{\\dot T}_O} = 47^\\circ {\\rm{C}} = 320\\,{\\rm{K}}\n\\end{array} \\right]$<br>$ \\Rightarrow \\frac{{{T_H}}}{2} = \\frac{{273 + 47}}{{32}}$<br>$ \\Rightarrow \\frac{T}{2} = \\frac{{320}}{{32}} = 10$<br>$\\therefore T = 20\\,{\\rm{K}}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38454,'physics',27,1378,3304,'On a temperature scale \' $X$ \', the boiling point of water is $65^\\circ X$ and the freezing point is $ - 15^\\circ X$. Assume that the $X$ scale is linear. The equivalent temperature corresponding to $ - 95^\\circ X$ on the Farenheit scale would be','[{"text": "$ - 48^\\\\circ F$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$ - 63^\\\\circ F$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$ - 112^\\\\circ F$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$ - 148^\\\\circ F$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'$\\dfrac{X-X_{\\text {freeze }}}{X_{\\text {boil }}-X_{\\text {freeze }}}=\\dfrac{t-32^{\\circ}}{212^{\\circ}-32^{\\circ}}$<br>as $\\dfrac{\\text { Reading on scale }- \\text { Lower fixed point }}{\\text { Upper fixed point }- \\text { Lower fixed point }}$<br>$=\\text { constant for any scale. }$<br>$\\dfrac{-95^{\\circ}-\\left(-15^{\\circ}\\right)}{65^{\\circ}-\\left(-15^{\\circ}\\right)}=\\dfrac{t-32^{\\circ}}{180^{\\circ}} ; \\dfrac{-80^{\\circ}}{80^{\\circ}}=\\dfrac{t-32^{\\circ}}{180^{\\circ}}$<br>$t=-180^{\\circ}+32^{\\circ} ; t=-148^{\\circ} F$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',2023,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38455,'physics',29,1301,3148,'When temperature of an ideal gas is increased from $27^\\circ C$ to  $227^\\circ C,$ its $rms$ speed is changed from $400\\,m{s^{ - 1}}$ to $v_{s}$. Then $v_{s}$ is','[{"text": "$516\\\\,m{s^{ - 1}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$450\\\\,m{s^{ - 1}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$310\\\\,m{s^{ - 1}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$746\\\\,m{s^{ - 1}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'We know that, $rms$ speed is directly<br> proportional to square root of temperature.<br>$v_{r m s} \\propto \\sqrt{T}$<br>Hence, $\\dfrac{v_{\\text {rms (1) }}}{v_{\\text {rms (2) }}}=\\sqrt{\\dfrac{T_{1}}{T_{2}}}$<br>Given,  ${T_1} = 27^\\circ C = (27 + 273)K,$<br>${T_2} = 227^\\circ {\\rm{C}} = (227 + 273)K{v_{{\\rm{rms}}(1)}}$<br>$ = 400\\,m{s^{ - 1}}\\,\\,{\\text{and}}\\,\\,{v_s} = {v_{rms(2)}} = ?$<br>$ \\Rightarrow \\quad \\frac{{400}}{{{v_{{\\rm{rms}}(2)}}}} = \\frac{{\\sqrt {27 + 273} }}{{\\sqrt {227 + 273} }} = \\sqrt {\\frac{{300}}{{500}}} $<br>$ \\Rightarrow \\quad {v_{{\\rm{rms}}(2)}} = {v_s} = \\sqrt {\\frac{{500}}{{300}}}  \\times 400$<br>$ = 1.29 \\times 400$<br>$ = 516.39\\,m{s^{ - 1}} \\simeq 516\\,m{s^{ - 1}}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38456,'physics',26,1138,2731,'A capillary tube of $2\\,mm$ diameter. What is the height to which water rises, if surface tension of water is $7.4 \\times {10^{ - 3}}N{m^{ - 1}}$ and angle of contact is $0^\\circ ?$','[{"text": "$0.25\\\\,mm$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1.51\\\\,mm$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$0.4\\\\,mm$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$0.5\\\\,mm$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'$h = \\frac{{2\\,S\\,\\cos \\,\\theta }}{{\\rho rg}}$<br>${\\rm{Where}}\\,\\,\\,\\,S = {\\rm{Surface}}\\,{\\rm{tension}}$<br>$\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\rho \\,{\\rm{ = }}\\,{\\rm{Denity of liquid}}$<br>$\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,r\\,{\\rm{ = }}\\,{\\rm{Radius of capillory tube}}$<br>$h = \\frac{{2{\\mkern 1mu} S\\cos \\theta }}{{\\rho rg}} = \\frac{{2 \\times 7.4 \\times {{10}^{ - 3}} \\times \\cos 0^\\circ }}{{{{10}^3} \\times {{10}^{ - 3}} \\times 9.8}}$<br>$ = 1.51 \\times {10^{ - 3}}$<br>$ = 1.51\\,mm$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38459,'physics',26,1138,2731,'The lower end of a glass capillary tube is dipped in water. Water rises to a height of \\[8\\;cm\\]. The tube is then placed such that height of \\[4\\;cm\\] is only above the surface. The height of water column and angle of contact will be.<div class="matching-question"></div>','[{"text": "$2\\\\,cm,\\\\,60^\\\\circ $", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$4\\\\,cm,\\\\,120^\\\\circ $", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$4\\\\,cm,\\\\,60^\\\\circ $", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$2\\\\,cm,\\\\,30^\\\\circ $", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Water rises in capillary tube due to force of adhesion between solid and liquid molecules. Tube is broken at height of $4\\,cm$, so force of adhesion is present till $4\\,cm$.<br>So, height of water column is $4\\,cm$.<br>$\\begin{aligned}& \\therefore \\dfrac{h}{\\cos \\theta}=\\dfrac{h_{1}}{\\cos \\theta^{\\prime}}\\left(\\dfrac{h}{\\cos \\theta}=\\dfrac{2 S}{\\rho r g}\\right) \\\\& \\therefore \\dfrac{8}{\\cos 0^{\\circ}}=\\dfrac{4}{\\cos \\theta} \\Rightarrow \\cos \\theta=\\dfrac{1}{2} \\Rightarrow \\theta=60^{\\circ}\\end{aligned}$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38460,'physics',29,1301,3148,'If at $NTP$ velocity of sound in a gas is $1150\\,m{s^{ - 1}}$ then the $rms$ velocity of gas molecules at $NTP$  is (take, $R = 8.3\\,J\\,mo{l^{ - 1}}{K^{ - 1}},$<br> ${C_p} = 4.8\\,cal\\,mo{l^{ - 1}}{K^{ - 1}}$)','[{"text": "$1600\\\\,m{s^{ - 1}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1532.19\\\\,m{s^{ - 1}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$160\\\\,m{s^{ - 1}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "zero", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'As we know,<br>$R=\\dfrac{8.3}{4.2} \\operatorname{calg~mol}^{-1} K^{-1}$<br>and $C_{V}=C_{p}-R$<br>$=\\left(4.8-\\dfrac{8.3}{4.2}\\right)=2.824$<br>Also, $\\quad \\gamma=\\dfrac{C_{p}}{C_{V}}=\\dfrac{4.8}{2.824}=1.69$<br>Since, $\\quad v=\\sqrt{\\left(\\dfrac{3}{\\gamma}\\right)} v_{s}=\\sqrt{\\dfrac{3}{1.69}} \\times 1150$<br>$ = 1532.19\\,\\,m{s^{ - 1}}$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38463,'physics',29,1301,3148,'The temperature of ${H_2}$ at which the $rms$ velocity of its molecules is seven times, the $rms$ velocity of the molecules of nitrogen gas at $300\\,K$ is','[{"text": "$2100 K$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1700 K$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1350 K$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1050 K$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'As, $v_{r m s}=\\sqrt{\\dfrac{3 R T}{M}}$<br>$\\Rightarrow \\dfrac{v_{1}}{v_{2}}=\\sqrt{\\dfrac{T_{1}}{M_{1}} \\times \\dfrac{M_{2}}{T_{2}}}$<br>$\\Rightarrow \\dfrac{v_{H}}{v_{N}}=\\sqrt{\\dfrac{T_{H}}{M_{H}} \\times \\dfrac{M_{N}}{T_{N}}}$<br>$\\Rightarrow 7=\\sqrt{\\dfrac{T_{H} \\times 28}{2 \\times 300}}$  $\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,$ (given)<br>or $49=\\dfrac{T_{H} \\times 28}{600}$<br>$\\therefore \\quad {T_H} = \\frac{{49 \\times 600}}{{28}} = 1050\\,K$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38466,'physics',27,1381,3315,'A solid cube of mass ${m}$ at a temperature ${\\theta_{0}}$ is heated at a constant rate. It becomes liquid at temperature ${\\theta_{1}}$ and vapour at temperature ${\\theta_{2}}$. Let ${s_{1}}$ and ${s_{2}}$ be specific heats in its solid and liquid states respectively. If ${L_{f}}$ and ${L_{v}}$ are latent heats of fusion and vaporisation respectively, then the minimum heat energy supplied to the cube until it vaporises is','[{"text": "${m s_{1}\\\\left(\\\\theta_{1}-\\\\theta_{0}\\\\right)+m s_{2}\\\\left(\\\\theta_{2}-\\\\theta_{1}\\\\right)}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${m L_{f}+m s_{2}\\\\left(\\\\theta_{2}-\\\\theta_{1}\\\\right)+m L_{v}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${m s_{1}\\\\left(\\\\theta_{1}-\\\\theta_{0}\\\\right)+m L_{f}+m s_{2}\\\\left(\\\\theta_{2}-\\\\theta_{1}\\\\right)+m L_{v}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${m s_{1}\\\\left(\\\\theta_{1}-\\\\theta_{0}\\\\right)+m L_{f}+m s_{2}\\\\left(\\\\theta_{2}-\\\\theta_{0}\\\\right)+m L_{v}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Heat energy required for conversion of solid cube into vapours is given by<br>$Q = m{s_1}\\left( {{\\theta _1} - {\\theta _0}} \\right) + m{L_f} + m{S_2}\\left( {{\\theta _2} - {\\theta _1}} \\right) + m{L_v}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2024,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38467,'physics',29,1301,3148,'The ratio of root mean square velocities of ${O_3}$ and ${O_2}$ is','[{"text": "$1: 1$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2: 3$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$3: 2$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\sqrt{2}: \\\\sqrt{3}$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'As, $v_{r m s}=\\sqrt{\\dfrac{3 R T}{M}}$<br>$ \\Rightarrow {v_{rms}} \\propto \\frac{1}{{\\sqrt M }}$<br>$\\frac{{{{\\left( {{v_{rms}}} \\right)}_{{O_3}}}}}{{{{\\left( {{v_{rms}}} \\right)}_{{O_2}}}}} = \\sqrt {\\frac{{{M_{{O_2}}}}}{{{M_{{O_3}}}}}}  = \\sqrt {\\left( {\\frac{{32}}{{48}}} \\right)}  = \\sqrt 2 :\\sqrt 3 $',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL);
INSERT INTO `qb_questions` (`id`, `subject_id`, `chapter_id`, `topic_id`, `subtopic_id`, `question_html`, `options`, `correct_index`, `explanation`, `explanation_image_url`, `difficulty`, `qtype`, `year`, `tag`, `created_at`, `question_image_url`) VALUES
(38468,'physics',26,1138,2730,'The excess pressure inside a spherical drop of water is four times that of another drop. Then their respective mass ratio is','[{"text": "$1: 16$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$8: 1$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1: 4$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1: 64$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Excess pressure inside spherical drop <br>$P=\\dfrac{2 S}{R}$<br>$P_{1}=\\dfrac{2 T}{R_{1}}, P_{2}=\\dfrac{2 T}{R_{2}}$<br>$\\therefore \\dfrac{2 T}{R_{1}}=4 \\times \\dfrac{2 T}{R_{2}} \\Rightarrow R_{2}=4 R_{1}$<br>$\\dfrac{m_{1}}{m_{2}}=\\dfrac{V_{1} \\rho}{V_{2} \\rho}=\\dfrac{4 / 3 \\pi R_{1}^{3} \\rho}{4 / 3 \\pi R_{2}^{3} \\rho}=\\dfrac{R_{1}^{3}}{64 R_{1}^{3}}=\\dfrac{1}{64}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38469,'physics',28,1370,3280,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> A gas does not have a unique value of specific heat.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Specific heat is defined as the amount of heat required to raise the temperature of unit mass of the substance through unit degree.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'There are $C_{p}$ and $C_{v}$ for gas. Each is (at constant $P$ or constant $V$ ) the amount of heat needed to raise the temperature of a unit mass by one degree, depending on the specific heating conditions. So correct option is (2)',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38470,'physics',27,1380,3309,'A metallic bar of Young\'s modulus, ${0.5 \\times 10^{11} {Nm}^{-2}}$ and coefficient of linear thermal expansion ${10^{-5^{\\circ}} {C}^{-1}}$, length 1 m and area of cross-section ${10^{-3} {~m}^{2}}$ is heated from ${0^{\\circ} {C}}$ to ${100^{\\circ} {C}}$ without expansion or bending. The compressive force developed in it is:','[{"text": "${5 \\\\times 10^{3} {~N}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${50 \\\\times 10^{3} {~N}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${100 \\\\times 10^{3} {~N}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${2 \\\\times 10^{3} {~N}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Thermal strain ${=}$ Longitudinal strain ${=\\alpha \\Delta T}$<br>${\\Rightarrow}$ Longitudinal strain, ${=10^{-5} \\times 10^{2}=10^{-3}}$<br>${\\Rightarrow}$ Compressive stress ${=}$ Strain ${\\times}$ Young\'s Modulus${=10^{-3} \\times 0.5 \\times 10^{11}}$<br>${=0.5 \\times 10^{8}}$<br>${\\Rightarrow}$ Compressive force<br>${=0.5 \\times 10^{8} \\times 10^{-3}=50 \\times 10^{3} {~N}}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2024,'NEET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38471,'physics',29,1304,3154,'$10\\,mol$ of an ideal monatomic gas at $10^\\circ C$ is mixed with $20\\,mol$ of another monatomic gas at $20^\\circ C.$ Then, the temperature of the mixture is','[{"text": "$15.5^\\\\circ C$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$15^\\\\circ C$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$16^\\\\circ C$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$16.6^\\\\circ C$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Temperature of the mixture is given by<br>$T=\\dfrac{n_{1} T_{1}+n_{2} T_{2}}{n_{1}+n_{2}}$<br>Given, $n_{1}=10 \\mathrm{~mol}, n_{2}=20 \\mathrm{~mol}$,<br>${T_1} = 10^\\circ C{\\mkern 1mu} \\,\\,{\\mkern 1mu} {\\text{and}}\\,\\,{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {T_2} = 20^\\circ C$<br>$ = \\frac{{10 \\times 10 + 20 \\times 20}}{{10 + 20}}$<br>$ = \\frac{{500}}{{30}} \\approx 16.6^\\circ C$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38472,'physics',26,1138,2730,'Two soap bubbles of radii $r_{1}$ and $r_{2}$ equals to $6\\,cm$ and $8\\,cm$ are touching each other over a common surface $S_{1} S_{2}$ whose radius will be','[{"text": "$24\\\\,cm$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$22\\\\,cm$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$12\\\\,cm$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$10\\\\,cm$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'According to the relation<br>$r = \\frac{{{r_1}{r_2}}}{{{r_2} - {r_1}}} = \\frac{{6 \\times 8}}{{8 - 6}} = \\frac{{48}}{2} = 24\\,cm$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/26_38472_question_1.png'),
(38473,'physics',28,1369,3279,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> The heat supplied to a system is not always equal to the sum of increase in its internal energy and work done by the system.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> When a system changes from one thermal equilibrium state to another, some heat is absorbed by it.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'By first law of thermodynamics, stating that heat supplied to a system equals the increase in internal energy and the external work done $\\left( {\\Delta Q = \\Delta U + \\Delta W} \\right)$. Only, for isochoric process, (constant volume, $\\Delta W=0$ ), all supplied heat increases internal energy. In other processes, heat addition can contribute to both internal energy and work. So, given are different facts. So correct option is (2).',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(48046,'biology',10,261,515,'Cell wall in plants consists of','[{"text": "Lignin, hemicellulose, pectin and lipid", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Cellulose, hemicellulose, pectins and proteins.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Lignin, hemicelluloses, protein and lipid", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Hemicellulose, cellulose, tubulin and lignin", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Cell wall consists of cellulose, hemicellulose, pectins and proteins.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38474,'physics',27,1378,3304,'Two temperature scales ${A}$ and ${B}$ are released by ${\\dfrac{A-42}{110}=\\dfrac{B-72}{220}}$. At which temperature two scales have the same reading?','[{"text": "${-42^{\\\\circ}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${-72^{\\\\circ}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${+12^{\\\\circ}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${-40^{\\\\circ}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Let at temperature ${T}$ both the scales ${A}$ and ${B}$ have the same reading.<br>${\\dfrac{T-42}{110}=\\dfrac{T-72}{220}}$<br>${\\Rightarrow 2 T-84=T-72}$<br>${\\Rightarrow T=+12^{\\circ}}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38475,'physics',26,1138,2728,'What is the work done in breaking a drop of water of $4\\,mm$ diameter into 1000 drops of same size, surface tension of water is $72 \\times {10^{ - 3}}N{m^{ - 1}}?$','[{"text": "$1.125 \\\\times {10^{ - 4}}\\\\,J$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1.125 \\\\times {10^{ - 5}}\\\\,J$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$3.25 \\\\times {10^{ - 5}}\\\\,J$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$3.25 \\\\times {10^{ - 6}}\\\\,J$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Volume remains constant. Let $R$ and $r$ be the radius of bigger and smaller drops respective $-1 y$.$\\dfrac{4}{3} \\pi R^{3}=10^{3} \\times \\dfrac{4}{3} \\pi r^{3}$<br>$R = 10 \\times r \\Rightarrow r = \\frac{R}{{10}} = \\frac{{2 \\times {{10}^{ - 3}}}}{{10}} = 2 \\times {10^{ - 4}}\\,m$<br>Work done in breaking drop into 1000 drops.<br>$W=S \\times\\left(10^{3} \\times 4 \\pi r^{2}-4 \\pi R^{2}\\right)$<br>$=S \\times 4 \\pi\\left(10^{3} \\times r^{2}-R^{2}\\right)$<br>$W=72 \\times 10^{-3} \\times 4 \\times 3.14\\left[\\begin{array}{c}10^{3} \\times\\left(2 \\times 10^{-4}\\right)^{2}- \\\\ \\left(2 \\times 10^{-3}\\right)^{2}\\end{array}\\right]$<br>$W=0.904\\left(10^{3} \\times 4 \\times 10^{-8}-4 \\times 10^{-6}\\right)$<br>$W=0.904\\left(4 \\times 10^{-5}-4 \\times 10^{-6}\\right)$<br>$W = 0.904 \\times 4 \\times {10^{ - 5}}(1 - 0.1) = 3.25 \\times {10^{ - 5}}\\,J$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38476,'physics',29,1301,3148,'The molecules of a given mass of gas have a root mean square velocity of $200\\,m\\,{s^{ - 1}}$ at $27^\\circ C$ and $1.0 \\times {10^5}\\,N{m^{ - 2}}$ pressure. When the temperature is $127^\\circ C$ and the pressure $0.5 \\times {10^5}N{m^{ - 2}}$, the root mean square velocity in $m{s^{ - 1}}$, is','[{"text": "$\\\\dfrac{400}{\\\\sqrt{3}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$100 \\\\sqrt{2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{100 \\\\sqrt{2}}{3}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{100}{3}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'$\\frac{{{c_2}}}{{{c_1}}} = \\sqrt {\\frac{{400}}{{300}}}  = \\frac{2}{{\\sqrt 3 }}$<br>$ \\Rightarrow {c_2} = \\frac{2}{{\\sqrt 3 }} \\times 200 = \\frac{{400}}{{\\sqrt 3 }}\\,m{s^{ - 1}}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38478,'physics',27,1380,3310,'Two large holes are cut in a metal sheet. If this is heated, distances ${A B}$ and ${B C}$, (as shown)','[{"text": "both will increase", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "both will decrease", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${A B}$ increases ${B C}$ decreases", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${A B}$ decreases, ${B C}$ increases", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'On heating, the expansion will take place, hence both the distances will increase.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/27_38478_question_1.png'),
(38479,'physics',26,1138,2730,'If excess of pressure inside a soap bubble of radius \\[2\\;cm\\] is balanced by that due to column of oil of (specific gravity \\[ = 0.8)2\\;mm\\] high. What is the surface tension of soap bubble?<div class="matching-question"></div>','[{"text": "$12 \\\\times {10^2}\\\\,N{\\\\rm{/}}m$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$6 \\\\times {10^{ - 2}}\\\\,N{\\\\rm{/}}m$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$8.12 \\\\times {10^{ - 2}}\\\\,N{\\\\rm{/}}m$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$7.84 \\\\times {10^{ - 2}}\\\\,N{\\\\rm{/}}m$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'$P=h \\rho g=2 \\times 10^{-3} \\times 0.8 \\times 10^{3} \\times 9.8$<br>(sp. gravity $=\\dfrac{\\rho_{\\ell}}{P_{W}}$ )<br>$P = 15.68\\,Pa$<br>For soap bubble. $P=\\dfrac{4 S}{R} \\Rightarrow 15.68=\\dfrac{4 \\times S}{2 \\times 10^{-2}}$<br>$\\therefore S = 7.84 \\times {10^{ - 2}}\\,N{\\rm{/}}m$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38480,'physics',29,1304,3154,'3 moles of an ideal gas at a temperature of $27^\\circ C$ are mixed with 2 moles of an ideal gas at a temperature $227^\\circ C$, determine the equilibrium temperature of the mixture, assuming no loss of energy.','[{"text": "$327^\\\\circ C$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$107^\\\\circ C$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$318^\\\\circ C$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$410^\\\\circ C$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Energy possessed by the ideal gas at $27^\\circ C$ is<br>${E_1} = 3\\left( {\\frac{3}{2}R \\times 300} \\right) = \\frac{{2700R}}{2}$<br>Energy possessed by the ideal gas at $227^\\circ C$ is<br>$E_{2}=2\\left(\\dfrac{3 R}{2} \\times 500\\right)=1500 R$<br>If $T$ be the equilibrium temperature, of the mixture, then its energy will be ${E_m} = 5\\left( {\\frac{{3RT}}{2}} \\right)$<br>Since, energy remains conserved, $E_{m}=E_{1}+E_{2}$<br>or $5\\left( {\\frac{{3RT}}{2}} \\right) = \\frac{{2700R}}{2} + 1500R$<br>or $T = 380\\,K\\,\\,or\\,\\,107^\\circ C$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38481,'physics',28,1377,3303,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> If an electric fan be switched on in a closed room, the air of the room will be hot.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> There will be evaporation of sweat by a human there.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'When an electric fan is activated within an enclosed room, the air temperature will actually rise. This happens because the motion of the fan accelerates the speed of air molecules. Both fan and human body follow laws of thermodynamics. However, our sensation of feeling cold is primarily attributed to the evaporative cooling effect resulting from the sweat on our skin as heat is transferred from body to sweat. So correct option is (2).',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38482,'physics',27,1380,3306,'The variation of lengths of two metal rods ${A}$ and ${B}$ with change in temperature is shown in the figure. The ratio of ${\\dfrac{\\alpha_{A}}{\\alpha_{B}}}$ is','[{"text": "${\\\\dfrac{3}{2}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\dfrac{2}{3}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${\\\\dfrac{4}{3}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\dfrac{3}{4}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'${l=l_{0}(1+\\alpha \\Delta T) \\Rightarrow l-l_{0}=l_{0} \\alpha \\Delta T}$<br>$ \\Rightarrow \\frac{{{l_0}{\\alpha _A}\\Delta T}}{{{l_0}{\\alpha _B}\\Delta T}} = \\frac{{104 - 100}}{{106 - 100}} = \\frac{4}{6}$<br>$ \\Rightarrow \\frac{{{\\alpha _A}}}{{{\\alpha _B}}} = \\frac{2}{3}$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/27_38482_question_1.png'),
(38483,'physics',26,1138,2730,'The radius of spherical drop of water is $1\\,mm$. If surface tension of water be $70 \\times {10^{ - 3}}N{m^{ - 1}}$, the pressure difference inside and outside the drop will be','[{"text": "$70\\\\,N{m^{ - 2}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$140\\\\,N{m^{ - 2}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$280\\\\,N{m^{ - 2}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Zero", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'<br>Excess pressure $=\\dfrac{2 T}{r}$<br>$=\\dfrac{2 \\times 70 \\times 10^{-3}}{1 \\times 10^{-3}}$<br>$ = 140\\,N{m^{ - 2}}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38484,'physics',29,1303,3152,'One mole of a gas occupies $22.4\\,lit$ at $N.T.P.$ Calculate the difference between two molar specific heats of the gas. $J = 4200\\,J{\\rm{/}}k\\,cal$.','[{"text": "$1.979\\\\,k\\\\,cal{\\\\rm{/}}k\\\\,mol\\\\,K$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$2.378\\\\,k\\\\,cal{\\\\rm{/}}k\\\\,mol\\\\,K$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$4.569\\\\,k\\\\,cal/k\\\\,mol\\\\,K$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$3.028\\\\,k\\\\,cal{\\\\rm{/}}k\\\\,mol\\\\,K$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'$V = 22.4\\,\\,{\\text{litre}} = 22.4 \\times {10^{ - 3}}\\,{m^3},\\,$<br>$J = 4200\\,J/k{\\mkern 1mu} cal$  by ideal gas equation for one mole of a gas,$R = \\frac{{PV}}{T} = \\frac{{1.013 \\times {{10}^5} \\times 22.4 \\times {{10}^{ - 3}}}}{{273}}$<br>${C_p} - {C_v} = \\frac{R}{J} = \\frac{{1.013 \\times {{10}^5} \\times 22.4}}{{273 \\times 4200}} = 1.979$<br>$k\\,cal{\\rm{/}}k\\,mol\\,K$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38485,'physics',28,1371,3282,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> The internal energy of an isothermal process does not change<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> The internal energy of a system depends only on pressure of the system.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'For an ideal gas undergoing an isothermal process,<br>$\\Rightarrow T=\\mathrm{constant}$<br>$\\Rightarrow \\Delta T=0$ Assertion is correct. The internal energy of a system depends on temperature only Reason is false. So correct option is (3).',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38486,'physics',27,1381,3315,'$1\\,kg$  of ice at ${-10^{\\circ} {C}}$ is mixed with $4.4{\\rm{ }}kg$ of water at ${30^{\\circ} {C}}$. The final temperature of mixture is (specific heat of ice is ${2100 {~J} / {kg} / {k}}$ )','[{"text": "${2.3^{\\\\circ} {C}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${4.4^{\\\\circ} {C}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${5.3^{\\\\circ} {C}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${8.7^{\\\\circ} {C}}$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Let temperature of mixture be ${\\theta}$, then heat lost ${=}$ heat gained<br>$\\begin{aligned}4.4 \\times 1 \\times & (30=\\theta)=1 \\times 0.5 \\times(10) \\\\+ & 1 \\times 80+1 \\times 1 \\times(\\theta-0)\\end{aligned}$<br>${\\Rightarrow \\theta=8.7^{\\circ} {C}}$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38487,'physics',26,1138,2730,'What should be the pressure inside a small air bubble of $0.1\\,mm$ radius situated just below the water surface? (surface tension of water $ = 7.2 \\times {10^{ - 2}}N{m^{ - 1}}$ and atmospheric pressure $ = 1.013 \\times {10^5}N{m^{ - 2}})$','[{"text": "$2.012 \\\\times {10^5}N{m^{ - 2}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2.012 \\\\times {10^4}N{m^{ - 2}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1.027 \\\\times {10^5}N{m^{ - 2}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$1.027 \\\\times {10^4}N{m^{ - 2}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Excess pressure inside the air bubble is given by.<br>$\\Delta P = {P_i} - {P_o} = \\frac{{2T}}{r}$<br>${P_i} = {P_o} + \\frac{{2T}}{r}$<br>$ = 1.013 \\times {10^5} + \\frac{{2 \\times 7.2 \\times {{10}^{ - 2}}}}{{0.1 \\times {{10}^{ - 3}}}}$<br>$ = 1.013 \\times {10^5} + 0.0144 \\times {10^5}$<br>$ = 1.027 \\times {10^5}N{m^{ - 2}}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38488,'physics',29,1304,3154,'Four mole of hydrogen, two mole of helium and one mole of water vapour form an ideal gas mixture. What is the molar specific heat at constant pressure of mixture?','[{"text": "$\\\\dfrac{16}{7} R \\\\quad$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{7}{16} R$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$R$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{23}{7} R$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'${C_v}$ for hydrogen $ = \\frac{5}{2}R$<br>${C_v}$ for helium $=\\dfrac{3 R}{2}$<br>${C_v}$ for water vapour $ = \\frac{{6R}}{2} = 3R$<br>$\\therefore \\,\\,\\,{\\mkern 1mu} {\\kern 1pt} {\\left( {{C_v}} \\right)_{mix{\\rm{ }}}}$<br>$=\\dfrac{4 \\times \\dfrac{5}{2} R+2 \\times \\dfrac{3}{2} R+1 \\times 3 R}{4+2+1}=\\dfrac{16}{7} R$<br>$\\therefore {C_p} = {C_v} + R$<br>$C_{p}=\\dfrac{16}{7} R+R$ or $C_{p}=\\dfrac{23}{7} R$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38489,'physics',28,1371,3283,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> An adiabatic process is an isoentropic process.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Change in entropy is zero in case of adiabatic process.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'In adiabatic process $\\Delta S=\\dfrac{\\Delta Q}{T}=0$, since there is no heat exchange $(\\Delta Q=0)$ (with the surroundings).<br>$\\Rightarrow S=$ constant<br>So, this process is called isentropic<br>So correct option is (1).',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38490,'physics',27,1381,3315,'Steam at ${100^{\\circ} {C}}$ is passed into $20{\\rm{ }}g$ of water at ${10^{\\circ} {C}}$. When water acquires a temperature of ${80^{\\circ} {C}}$, the mass of water present will be [Take specific heat of water ${=1 {calg}^{-1^{\\circ}} {C}^{-1}}$ and latent heat of steam ${=540 {cal} {g}^{-1}}$ ]','[{"text": "$24\\\\,g$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$31.5\\\\,g$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$42.5\\\\,g$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$22.5\\\\,g$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'${m_{1} L_{v}+m_{1} s \\Delta \\theta=m_{2} s \\theta}$<br>${m_{1} \\times 540+m_{1} \\times 1 \\times 20=20 \\times 1 \\times 70}$<br>${m_{1} \\times 560=1400}$<br>${m_{1} \\approx 2.5 {~g}}$<br>${\\therefore}$ Total mass of water ${=20+2.5=22.5 {gm}}$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38491,'physics',26,1138,2731,'If $h$ is the height of capillary rise and $r$ is the radius of the capillary tube, then which of the following relation will be correct?','[{"text": "$h r=$ constant", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$h / r^{2}=$ constant", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$h r^{2}=$ constant", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$h / r=$ constant", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'$h = \\frac{{2S\\cos \\theta }}{{\\rho rg}} \\Rightarrow hr = \\frac{{2S\\cos \\theta }}{{\\rho g}}$<br>$ \\Rightarrow hr = {\\rm{constant}}$<br>$[S.\\theta \\,p,g\\,{\\rm{are}}\\,{\\rm{constant}}]$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38492,'physics',28,1371,3283,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Work done by a gas in isothermal expansion is more than the work done by the gas in the same expansion, adiabatically.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Temperature remains constant in isothermal expansion and not in adiabatic expansion.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Adiabatic $\\Delta Q=0$<br>$\\Rightarrow|\\Delta U|=|\\Delta W|$ (by first law of thermodynamics) Isothermal $\\Delta T=0$<br>$\\Rightarrow \\Delta Q=\\Delta W$<br>$\\Rightarrow$ complete $\\Delta Q$ is used as work in isothermal.<br>$\\Rightarrow$ work done in isothermal expansion is greater than in adiabatic expansion. However, the reason lacks precision, attributing the difference to temperature alone, while it\'s influenced by the change in internal energy. So correct option is (2).',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38493,'physics',29,1300,3146,'A real gas behaves as an ideal gas','[{"text": "at very low pressure and high temperature", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "high pressure and low temperature", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "high temperature and high pressure", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "low pressure and low temperature", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'A real gas behaves as an ideal gas at very low pressure and at high temperature.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38494,'physics',27,1382,3318,'Four rods of same material, having the same cross-sections and lengths are joined, as shown. The temperature of junction of four rods will be','[{"text": "${20^{\\\\circ} {C}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${30^{\\\\circ} {C}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${45^{\\\\circ} {C}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${60^{\\\\circ} {C}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'${\\dfrac{K A(90-\\theta)}{l}+\\dfrac{K A(\\theta-30)}{l}+\\dfrac{K A(\\theta-0)}{l}}$<br>$+\\dfrac{K A(\\theta-60)}{l}=0$<br>$\\Rightarrow \\quad \\theta=45^{\\circ} {C}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/27_38494_question_1.png'),
(38495,'physics',26,1138,2731,'What is the diameter of a capillary tube in which mercury is depressed by $1.21\\,cm$ and surface tension for mercury is $540 \\times {10^{ - 3}}N{m^{ - 1}}$ the angle of constant is $120^{\\circ}$ and density of mercury is<br>$13.6 \\times {10^3}\\,kg\\,{m^{ - 3}}?$','[{"text": "$0.06\\\\,mm$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$2\\\\,mm$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$10\\\\,mm$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$0.67\\\\,mm$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'$h=\\dfrac{2 S \\cos \\theta}{r \\rho g} \\Rightarrow r=\\dfrac{2 S \\cos \\theta^{0}}{h \\rho g}$<br>$D=\\dfrac{4 S \\cos \\theta}{h \\rho g}$<br>$\\therefore D=\\dfrac{4 \\times 540 \\times 10^{-3} \\times \\cos 120^{\\circ}}{-1.21 \\times 10^{-2} \\times 13.6 \\times 10^{3} \\times 9.8}$<br>$D=\\dfrac{4 \\times 540 \\times 10^{-3} \\times(-0.5)}{-1.21 \\times 10^{-2} \\times 13.6 \\times 10^{3} \\times 9.8}$<br>$D = \\frac{{540 \\times {{10}^{ - 4}}}}{{806.34}} = 0.06\\,mm$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38496,'physics',28,1369,3279,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> First law of thermodynamics is a restatement of the principle of conservation of energy<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Thermal energy is a thermodynamic variable.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'The first law of thermodynamics is a restatement of the conservation of energy, stating that heat absorbed equals the increase in energy and the external work done during expansion. $P, V, T$ are thermodynamic variables But thermal energy $\\Delta Q$ is not. So correct option is (3).',NULL,'Easy','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38497,'physics',29,1303,3151,'A man is climbing up a spiral staircase. His degrees of freedom are','[{"text": "1", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "2", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "3", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "4", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Motion of the man, climbing up a spiral staircase can be described by three co-ordinate axes ${x, y}$ and ${z}$. Hence the required degrees of freedom are three.<br>So correct option is (3)',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38498,'physics',27,1382,3319,'A composite rod made of three rods of equal length and cross section, as shown in figure. The thermal conductivities of materiales of rods are ${k / 2,5 k}$ and ${k}$, respectively. The end ${A}$ and end ${B}$ are at constant temperture. The effective thermal conductivity of the bar is ${\\left(T_{1}>T_{2}\\right)}$','[{"text": "${\\\\dfrac{6 k}{13}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\dfrac{15 k}{16}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${\\\\dfrac{5 k}{16}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\dfrac{2 k}{13}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'${R_{\\text {eq. }}=R_{1}+R_{2}+R_{3}}$<br>$\\begin{gathered}\\dfrac{3 l}{k_{\\text {eq. }} A}=\\dfrac{l}{k_{1} A}+\\dfrac{l}{k_{2} A}+\\dfrac{l}{k_{3} A} \\\\\\dfrac{3}{k_{\\text {eq. }}}=\\dfrac{2}{k}+\\dfrac{1}{5 k}+\\dfrac{1}{k} \\\\k_{\\text {eq. }}=\\dfrac{15 k}{16}\\end{gathered}$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/27_38498_question_1.png'),
(38499,'physics',26,1133,2705,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Pascal\'s law is the working principle of a hydraulic lift.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Pressure is equal to thrust.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'By Pascal\'s law, (if gravity effect is neglected), the pressure at every point of liquid in equilibrium of rest is same in hydraulic lift Small force ( $\\left.F_{1}\\right)$ applied on the smaller piston (of area $a_{1}$ ) will be appearing as a very large force $\\left(F_{2}\\right)$ on the larger piston (of area $a_{2}$ ).<br>$P_{1}=P_{2}$ i.e., $\\dfrac{F_{1}}{a_{1}}=\\dfrac{F_{2}}{a_{2}}$ or, $F_{2}=\\dfrac{a_{1}}{a_{2}} F_{1}$<br>As, $a_{2} \\gg a_{1}$<br>$\\therefore F_{2} \\gg F_{1}$<br>But pressure is not thrust. It is force per unit area. So correct option is (3).',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38500,'physics',29,1304,3154,'Two gases having same pressure ${P}$ and volume ${V}$ are mixed at a temperature ${T}$. If the mixture is at a temperature ${T}$ and occupies the same volume then pressure of the mixture would be','[{"text": "${P}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${2P}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${P / 2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${3 P}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'According Dalton\'s law<br>${P=P_{1}+P_{2}+\\ldots}$<br>${\\therefore \\quad}$ The pressure of the mixture ${P=P_{1}+P_{2}}$<br> ${=P+P=2 P}$.<br>So correct option is (2)',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38501,'physics',28,1367,3277,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Zeroth law of thermodynamics explains the concept of temperature.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Internal energy is dependent on temperature.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'The zeroth law of thermodynamics gives the concept of temperature via \'thermal equilibrium\', $U \\propto T$. But, they are different facts. So correct option is (2).',NULL,'Easy','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38502,'physics',27,1382,3317,'Heat is flowing through a rod of length $50\\,cm$ and area of cross-section ${5 {~cm}^{2}}$. Its ends are maintained at ${25^{\\circ} {C}}$ and ${125^{\\circ} {C}}$. The coefficient of thermal conductivity of the material of the rod is ${0.092 {kcals}^{-1} {~m}^{-10} {C}^{-1}}$. The temperature gradient in the rod is','[{"text": "${2^{\\\\circ} {C} / {cm}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${0.2^{\\\\circ} {C} / {m}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${2^{\\\\circ} {C} / {m}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${20^{\\\\circ} {C} / {m}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'${\\dfrac{d Q}{d t}=k A \\dfrac{d \\theta}{d x}}$<br>${\\dfrac{d \\theta}{d x}=\\dfrac{\\left(125-25^{\\circ} {C}\\right)}{50}=\\dfrac{100^{\\circ} {C}}{50}=2^{\\circ} {C} / {cm}}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38503,'physics',26,1133,2705,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> The blood pressure in humans is greater at the feet than at the brain.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Pressure of liquid at any point is proportional to height. Density of liquid and acceleration due to gravity.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Pressure of a liquid at any point is determined by the height of the liquid column, its density, and the acceleration due to gravity ( $P=h \\rho g$ ). In a standing person, the blood has to pump against gravity to reach the brain. There is higher blood pressure at the feet. So correct option is (1).',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38505,'physics',29,1301,3148,'The speed of 5 molecules of gas (in arbitrary units) are as follows :<br> $2,3,4,5,6$   The root mean square speed for these molecules is','[{"text": "2.91", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "4.00", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "3.52", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "4.24", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Root mean square speed${v_{\\text {rms }}=\\sqrt{\\dfrac{v_{1}^{2}+v_{2}^{2}+v_{3}^{2}+v_{4}^{2}+v_{5}^{2}+\\ldots}{n}}}$<br>${v_{\\text {rms }}}$ for 5 molecules of gas is,$\\begin{aligned}v_{{rms}} & =\\sqrt{\\dfrac{2^{2}+3^{2}+4^{2}+5^{2}+6^{2}}{5}} \\\\& =\\sqrt{\\dfrac{90}{5}}=4.24 \\text { units }\\end{aligned}$.<br>So correct option is (4)',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38506,'physics',27,1382,3318,'Two rods having same area are used to connect two reservoirs at temperature ${100^{\\circ} {C}}$ and ${0^{\\circ} {C}}$, as shown. The temperature of junction is ${70^{\\circ} {C}}$. If the rods are now interchanged, the temperature of junction will be','[{"text": "${70^{\\\\circ} {C}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${30^{\\\\circ} {C}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${50^{\\\\circ} {C}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${40^{\\\\circ} {C}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'${\\theta_{1}=\\theta_{2}}$<br>${\\dfrac{k_{1} A(100-70)}{1}=\\dfrac{k_{2} A(70-0)}{1}}$<br>$k_{1}=\\dfrac{7}{3} k_{2}$<br>When rods are interchanged<br>$\\begin{aligned}& \\dfrac{k_{2} A(100-T)}{1}=\\dfrac{k_{1} A(T-0)}{1} \\\\& \\Rightarrow \\quad T=30^{\\circ} {C}\\end{aligned}$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/27_38506_question_1.png'),
(38507,'physics',26,1133,2705,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Hydrostatic pressure is a scalar quantity<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Pressure is a type of force.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Pressure $=\\left(\\dfrac{\\text { Force }}{\\text { Area }}\\right)$<br>An applied force on a liquid results in the equal transmission of pressure in all directions within the liquid, hydrostatic pressure does not possess a fixed direction. Hydrostatic pressure is a scalar quantity. So correct option is (3).',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38508,'physics',28,1369,3279,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> The internal energy of a real gas is function of both temperature and volume.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Internal kinetic energy depends on temperature and internal potential energy depends on volume.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'If it is ideal gas, $U \\propto T$ only, But if it is real gas internal energy depends on both temperature (kinetic energy) and volume (potential energy). The reason correctly explains the factors influencing the internal energy. So correct option is (1).',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38509,'physics',29,1301,3147,'Two gases are at absolute temperatures of 300 $K$ and 350 $K$ respectively. Ratio of average kinetic energy of their molecules is','[{"text": "${7: 6}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${6: 7}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${36: 49}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${49: 36}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'At absolute temperature the average kinetic energy ${K=\\dfrac{3}{2} k_{B} T}$<br>For two gases,<br>${K_{1}=\\dfrac{3}{2} k_{B} T_{1}, K_{2}=\\dfrac{3}{2} k_{B} T_{2}}$<br>Required ratio ${\\dfrac{K_{1}}{K_{2}}=\\dfrac{T_{1}}{T_{2}} \\quad \\therefore \\quad \\dfrac{K_{1}}{K_{2}}=\\dfrac{300}{350}=\\dfrac{6}{7}}$.<br>So correct option is (2)',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38511,'physics',26,1133,2710,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> A man sitting in a boat which is floating on a pond. If the man drinks some water from the pond, the level of the water in the pond remain unchanged.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> According to Archimede\'s principle the weight of water displaced by body is equal to the weight of the body, if the body is floating.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'This is in accordance with Archimedes\' principle, which states that the weight of the water displaced by the boat (and the man) is equal to the weight of the boat and man. Therefore, removing a small amount of water from the pond does not significantly affect the water level. So correct option is (1).',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38512,'physics',29,1306,3156,'Kinetic energy of molecules is minimum in','[{"text": "solids", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "liquids", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "gases", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "none of these", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'In solids the molecules are tightly bonded $i.\\,e.$ molecule are not free to move easily. Hence the kinetic energy of molecules is minimum in solids.<br>So correct option is (1)',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38513,'physics',28,1371,3283,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> In an adiabatic expansion, the internal energy of the system get changed .<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> An adiabatic compression is a slow process.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Assertion is true. In adiabatic process (whether compression or expansion) temperature and hence internal energy change: or expansion is a rapid process. So correct option is (3).',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38515,'physics',26,1133,2710,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> A piece of ice floats in water. The level of water remains unchanged when the ice melts completely.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> According to Archimede\'s principle, the loss in weight of the body in the liquid is equal to the weight of the liquid displaced by the immersed part of the body.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'By Archimedes\' principle, the loss in weight of the ice (due to melting) in the liquid is equal to the weight of the liquid displaced by the immersed part of the ice. The volume of water displaced by the ice is exactly the same as the volume of water produced when the ice melts. So correct option is (1).',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38517,'physics',28,1367,3277,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Two systems in thermal equilibrium with a third system, are in thermal equilibrium with each other<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Heat flows spontaneously from a system at a higher temperature to a system at a lower temperature.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Assertion is Zeroth law of thermodynamics Reason follows its second law . So, correct option is (2).',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38519,'physics',26,1138,2727,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Detergents should have small angles of contact<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> If angle of contact is small detergents rinse the clothes dust in more time.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'When detergent is dissolved in water, surface tension decreases. The detergent spreads over longer part of cloth. So correct option is (3).',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38521,'physics',29,1300,3145,'As the day advances, the temperature of an open room increases from $26.85^\\circ C$ to $28^\\circ C.$ The volume of the room is $41.6\\,{m^3}$ and the atmospheric pressure in the room remains $1 \\times {10^5}\\,Pa.$ Then difference in the number of molecules present in the room after and before the heating is <br>(Take $1\\,K = 273.15^\\circ C,$<br>Universal gas constant $R = 8.32\\,J\\,mo{l^{ - 1}}\\,{K^{ - 1}},$<br>Avogadro\'s number ${N_A} = 6.023 \\times {10^{23}}$)','[{"text": "$ - 3.833 \\\\times {10^{24}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$ - 7.384 \\\\times {10^{24}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2.233 \\\\times {10^{24}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$5.833 \\\\times {10^{24}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Using ideal gas equation,<br>Before heating, at ${T_1} = 26.85 + 273.15 = 300\\,K{\\mkern 1mu} $,<br>$PV = {n_1}R \\times 300\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,(1)$<br>After heating, at ${T_2} = 28 + 273.15 = 301.15\\,K$.<br>$PV = {n_2}R \\times 301.15\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,(2)$<br>where, $n_{1}$ and $n_{2}$ are number of moles at $T$ and ${T_2}$ respectively.<br>From equations (1) and (2).<br>${n_2} - {n_1} = \\frac{{PV}}{{R \\times 301.15}} - \\frac{{PV}}{{R \\times 300}}$<br>But, number of molecules $ = n \\times {N_{A{\\rm{ }}}}$<br>$\\therefore {n_f} - {n_1} = \\left( {{n_2} - {n_1}} \\right){N_{A{\\rm{ }}}}$<br>$\\therefore {n_f} - {n_1} =  - \\frac{{PV}}{R} \\times \\left( {\\frac{{1.15}}{{301.15 \\times 300}}} \\right)$<br>$\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, \\times 6.023 \\times {10^{23}}$<br>Given: $P = {10^5}\\,Pa$ and $V = 41.6\\,m$<br>$\\therefore$ Number of molecules<br>${n_f} - {n_i} =  - \\frac{{{{10}^5} \\times 41.6 \\times 1.15 \\times 6.023 \\times {{10}^{23}}}}{{8.32 \\times 301.15 \\times 300}}$<br>$ =  - \\frac{{{{10}^5} \\times 5 \\times 1.15 \\times {{10}^{23}}}}{{50 \\times 300}}$<br>$ =  - \\frac{{1.15 \\times {{10}^{27}}}}{{300}}$<br>$\\therefore {n_f} - {n_i} =  - 3.833 \\times {10^{24}}$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38522,'physics',27,1378,3304,'If a thermometer reads freezing point of water as $20^\\circ C$ and boiling point as $150^\\circ C,$  how much thermometer read when the actual temperature is $60^\\circ C$ ?','[{"text": "$98^\\\\circ C$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$10^\\\\circ C$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$40^\\\\circ C$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$60^\\\\circ C$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Temperature on any scale can be converted into other scale by<br>$\\dfrac{\\lambda-\\text { LFP }}{\\text { UFP - LFP }}=$ constant for all scales<br>$\\frac{{x - 20}}{{150 - 20}} = \\frac{{60}}{{100}} \\Rightarrow x = 98^\\circ C$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38523,'physics',26,1138,2733,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> It is better to wash the clothes in cold soap solution.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> The surface tension of cold solution is more than the surface tension of hot solution.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but reason is correct.", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'As temperature increases surface tension decreases. The soap solution has lower surface tension compared to regular water, and its surface tension decreases further when heated. Consequently, hot soap solution can spread over a larger surface area and exhibits increased wetting power. This property allows hot soap solution to penetrate and clean clothes more effectively than ordinary water. So correct option is (4).',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38526,'physics',27,1378,3304,'If absolute zero is $ - 273.15^\\circ C$ on Celsius temperature scale, then the absolute zero on the Fahrenheit scale is','[{"text": "$ - 227.15^\\\\circ F$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$ - 453.15^\\\\circ F$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$ - 459.67^\\\\circ F$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$ - 491.67^\\\\circ F$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Celsius and Kelvin scales are related as<br>$\\frac{C}{5} = \\frac{{F - 32}}{9}$<br>$ \\Rightarrow \\frac{{ - 273.15}}{5} = \\frac{{F - 32}}{9}$<br>$ \\Rightarrow \\quad F =  - 459.67^\\circ F$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38527,'physics',26,1135,2714,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> The velocity increases. when water flowing in broader pipe enter a narrow pipe.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> According to equation of continuity, product of area and velocity is constant.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'According to the equation of continuity $v A=$ constant. The product of area and velocity is constant for a steady flow of an incompressible fluid. When water flows from a broader pipe to a narrower pipe, the area decreases, so the velocity must increase to maintain continuity of flow. So correct option is (1).',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38528,'physics',29,1303,3152,'${C_P}$ and ${C_V}$ are specific heats at constant pressure and constant volume respectively. It is observed that<br>${C_P} - {C_V} = $ a for helium gas <br>$C_{P}-C_{V}=b$ for oxygen gas<br>Such that, $b$ is $n$ times the $a$. Find out value of $n$.','[{"text": "$0.53\\\\,a$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$0.13\\\\,a$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$0.65\\\\,a$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$0.95\\\\,a$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Let molar heat capacity at constant pressure $ = {S_P}$ <br>and molar heat capacity at constant volume $ = {s_V}$<br>$\\therefore \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{s_P} - {s_V} = R$<br>Now, principal specific heat, $C = \\frac{s}{M}$<br>$\\therefore \\quad {C_P} - {C_V} = \\frac{R}{M}$<br>$\\therefore $ For $He,a = \\frac{R}{4};$ for ${O_2},\\,b = \\frac{R}{{32}}$<br>$\\therefore \\quad \\frac{b}{a} = \\frac{R}{{32}} \\times \\frac{4}{R} = \\frac{1}{8}$<br>$ \\Rightarrow b = 0.125a = 0.13a$<br>$\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{\\rm{(Rounding}}\\,{\\rm{off}}\\,{\\rm{to}}\\,{\\rm{two}}\\,{\\rm{digits)}}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38529,'physics',28,1371,3292,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> When a bottle of cold carbonated drink is opened, a slight fog forms around the opening.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Adiabatic expansion of the gas causes lowering of temperature and condensation of water vapours.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'When a bottle of cold carbonated drink is opened, the sudden drop in pressure due to adiabatic expansion causes a decrease in temperature. This drop in temperature can lead to the condensation of water vapor in the air, resulting in the formation of a fog. The reason correctly explains why this phenomenon occurs. So correct option is (1).',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38530,'physics',27,1380,3306,'The wooden wheel of radius $R$ is made of two semicircular parts (see figure). The two parts are held together by a ring made of a metal strip of cross-sectional area $S$ and length $L . L$ is slightly less than $2 \\pi R$. To fit the ring on the wheel, it is heated so that its temperature rises by $\\Delta T$ and it just steps over the wheel. As it cools down to surrounding temperature, it presses the semicircular parts together. If the coefficient of linear expansion of the metal is $\\alpha$ and its Young\'s modulus is $Y$, the force that one part of the wheel applies on the other part is','[{"text": "$2 \\\\pi S Y \\\\alpha \\\\Delta T$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$S Y \\\\alpha \\\\Delta T$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\pi S Y \\\\propto \\\\Delta T$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2 S Y \\\\alpha \\\\Delta T$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Elongation due to change in temperature.<br>$\\Delta \\,l = L\\,\\alpha \\,\\Delta T\\quad \\quad \\quad (1)$<br>where,<br>$\\alpha=$ coefficient of linear expansion which is compensated by elastic strain when temperature becomes normal,<br>$i.\\,e.\\quad \\Delta l = \\frac{{TL}}{{YS}}\\quad \\quad \\quad (2)$<br>From Eqs. (1) and (2), we get<br>$\\frac{{TL}}{{YS}} = L\\,\\alpha \\,\\Delta \\,T$<br>$T = YS\\,\\alpha \\,\\Delta T$<br>At equilibrium, force exerted by one-half on other,<br>$F=2 T=2 Y S \\alpha \\Delta T$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/27_38530_question_1.png'),
(38531,'physics',26,1137,2723,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> The velocity of fall of a man jumping with a parachute first increases and then becomes constant.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> The constant velocity of fall of man is called terminal velocity.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'As a person jumps from a height into the air while using a parachute, inertia of motion plays role. The velocity initially increases due to the dominant gravitational force, which overcomes the opposing forces of air resistance (viscous drag) and buoyancy. As the person\'s velocity continues to rise, the air resistance (viscous drag) also increases as $F_{\\text {viscous }} \\propto v$. Finally, a point is reached where the air resistance and buoyancy forces counteract the gravitational force. At this stage, the person descends with a constant velocity, which is called to as the terminal velocity. So correct option is (2).',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38533,'physics',28,1377,3303,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> In a pressure cooker the water is brought to boil. The cooker is then removed from the stove. Now on removing the lid of the pressure cooker, the water starts boiling again.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> The impurities in water bring down its boiling point.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'A pressure cooker operates by increasing the boiling point of water due to elevated vapor pressure within the sealed container.Reason is false. So correct option is (3).',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38570,'physics',27,1382,3322,'A black body has a wavelength of $\\lambda$ at temperature $2000\\,K.$ Its corresponding wavelength at temperature $3000\\,K$ will be','[{"text": "$\\\\dfrac{2 \\\\lambda}{3}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{3 \\\\lambda}{2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{4 \\\\lambda}{9}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{9 \\\\lambda}{4}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'According to Wien\'s displacement law,<br>${\\lambda _m}T = {\\text{ constant}}$<br>$\\frac{{{\\lambda _2}}}{{{\\lambda _1}}} = \\frac{{{T_1}}}{{{T_2}}}$<br>$\\frac{{{\\lambda _2}}}{\\lambda } = \\frac{{2000}}{{3000}} = \\frac{2}{3}{\\text{ }}$<br>${\\text{or }}\\quad {\\lambda _2} = \\frac{2}{3}\\lambda $',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38534,'physics',27,1380,3306,'How much should the temperature of a brass rod be increased so as to increase its length by $1 \\%$ ? (Take, $\\alpha$ for brass is $0.00002^{\\circ} C^{-1}$ )','[{"text": "$300^\\\\circ C$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$400^\\\\circ C$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$500^\\\\circ C$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$550^\\\\circ C$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Here, $\\Delta T=$ ?, $\\dfrac{\\Delta L}{L}=\\dfrac{1}{100}$,<br>$\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,a = 0.00002^\\circ {C^{ - 1}}$<br>${\\rm{ As }}\\,\\quad \\Delta L = \\alpha L\\Delta T$<br>$\\therefore \\quad \\,\\,\\alpha \\Delta T = \\frac{{\\Delta L}}{L}$<br>${\\rm{or}}\\quad \\Delta T = \\frac{{\\Delta L}}{{L\\,\\alpha }} = \\frac{1}{{100 \\times 0.00002}}$<br>$\\Delta T = \\frac{{{{10}^5}}}{{2 \\times {{10}^2}}} = 500^\\circ C$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38535,'physics',26,1136,2715,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> The size of the needle of a syringe controls a flow rate better than the thumb pressure exerted by a doctor while administering an injection.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Flow rate is independent of pressure exerted by the thumb of the doctor.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'By Bernouli equation,<br>$\\dfrac{P}{\\rho}+h g+\\dfrac{1}{2} v^{2}=\\mathrm{constant}$<br>Total energy $=E$<br>Here, $E$ determines the flow rate.<br>Note that $E$ varies with $P$, but more with $v^{2}$.<br>The reason is false. Flow rate is influenced by both the pressure applied and the size of the needle. $\\left[\\because v \\propto\\left(\\dfrac{1}{\\text { Area }}\\right)\\right]$<br>So correct option is (3).',NULL,'Hard','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38536,'physics',28,1377,3303,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Perfectly reversible systems are difficult to find in real world.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Most processes are dissipative in nature.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'In perfectly reversible system, there is no loss of energy. But real processes dissipate. So correct option is (1).',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38537,'physics',29,1300,3145,'One mole of hydrogen gas in its molecular form is heated from temperature of $200\\,K$ to $3000\\,K$ in a box of volume $2.05\\,{m^3}.$ It is observed that the gas gets converted to a gas of hydrogen atoms. Considering all gases to be ideal, the final pressure would be','[{"text": "20  times the initial pressure", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "3  times the initial pressure", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Same as  the initial pressure", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "30  times the initial pressure", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'When the ${H_2}$ molecule breaks into atoms, the number of moles would become twice.<br>$\\therefore {n_2} = 2{n_1}$<br>From ideal gas equation, $\\,\\,\\,$ $PV = nRT$<br>$\\because$ $\\,\\,\\,$Volume $(V)$ of the container is constant,<br>$\\therefore \\quad P \\propto nT$<br>$\\therefore \\quad \\frac{{{P_2}}}{{{P_1}}} = \\frac{{{n_2}{T_2}}}{{{n_1}{T_1}}} = \\frac{{\\left( {2{n_1}} \\right)(3000)}}{{{n_1}(200)}} = 30$<br>$\\therefore \\quad {P_2} = 30P$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38538,'physics',27,1380,3311,'The coefficient of real expansion of mercury is  $0.18 \\times {10^{ - 3}}{C^{\\, - 1}}.$  If the density of mercury at $0^\\circ C$ is $13.6\\,g/cc,$ its density at  $473\\,K$ will be','[{"text": "$13.12\\\\,g/cc$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$13.65\\\\,g/cc$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$13.51\\\\,g/cc$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$13.22\\\\,g/cc$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Here, $t_{1}=0^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C}=273 \\mathrm{~K}, t_{2}=473 \\mathrm{~K}$<br>$\\begin{aligned}& \\gamma_{r}=0.18 \\times 10^{-3} C^{-1} \\\\& d_{1}=13.6 \\mathrm{~g} / c c, d_{2}=?\\end{aligned}$<br>$d_{2}=\\dfrac{d_{1}}{1+\\gamma_{r}(\\Delta t)}$<br>$ = \\frac{{13.6}}{{1 + 0.18 \\times {{10}^{ - 3}} \\times (473 - 273)}}$<br>$ \\Rightarrow \\quad {d_2} = \\frac{{13.6}}{{1.036}}$<br>$ = 13.12\\,g/cc$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38539,'physics',26,1136,2719,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> A fluid flowing out of a small hole in a vessel apply a backward thrust on the vessel.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> According to the equation of continuity, the product of area and velocity remains constant.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'When a fluid flows out of a small hole, it exerts a backward thrust on the vessel (due to conservation of momentum). By the equation of continuity, the product of area and velocity of the fluid remains constant. (i.e. $v A=$ constant) Continuity equation is also due to conservation of linear momentum. So correct option is (1).',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38541,'physics',28,1371,3282,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> For an isothermal process in an ideal gas the heat absorbed by the gas is entirely used in the work done by the gas.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> During a process taking place in a system the temperature remains constant then the process is isothermal and first law of thermodynamics is applicable.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'In an isothermal process, heat absorbed is entirely used for work, but the reason doesn\'t explain this aspect, focusing on the definition of an isothermal process. So correct option is (1).',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38542,'physics',27,1380,3313,'Coefficient of cubical expansion of water is zero at','[{"text": "$0^\\\\circ C$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$4^\\\\circ C$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$15.5^\\\\circ C$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$100^\\\\circ C$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Coefficient of cubical expansion of water at $4^\\circ C$ is zero, because the density of water at $4^\\circ c$ is maximum.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(48047,'biology',10,261,515,'Which of the following wall is capable of growth in a plant cell?','[{"text": "Primary wall", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Secondary wall", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Middle lamella", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both (1) and (2)", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'The cell wall of young plant cells, the primary wall is capable of growth.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38543,'physics',26,1138,2733,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> A mercury barometer always reads less than the actual pressure.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> The density of liquid varies with the rise of temperature.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Assertion is correct. Because of \'surface tension\', mercury in tube gets depressed. Volume expands with rise in temperature. $\\Rightarrow$ Density varies. \'Reason\' is different fact. So correct option is (2).',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38544,'physics',28,1370,3280,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> $C_{P}$ is always greater than $C_{V}$, in gases.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Work done by gas at constant pressure is more than at constant volume.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'$C_{p}$ is greater than $C_{v}$ for gases, and the reason correctly attributes this to the greater work done by the gas at constant pressure compared to constant volume. So correct option is (1).',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38545,'physics',29,1306,3156,'A container fitted with a piston, carrying $1.4\\,kg$ nitrogen gas, is moving at a constant speed of $57.6\\,km{\\rm{/}}hr.$ The change in temperature of the nitrogen gas when the container suddenly stops is <br>(Take $R = 8\\,J\\,mo{l^{ - 1}}\\,{K^{ - 1}},$Molecular weight of  ${N_2} = 28\\,g$) <br>[Ignore the energy loss due to friction]','[{"text": "$0.18^\\\\circ C$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$0.38^\\\\circ C$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$0.67^\\\\circ C$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$0.92^\\\\circ C$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'For diatomic gas like nitrogen,<br>$\\Delta U = \\frac{5}{2}nR\\Delta T$<br> When the container suddenly stops, the $K.E.$ of mechanical motion is converted into internal energy of the gas.<br>$\\therefore \\frac{1}{2}m{v^2} = \\frac{5}{2}nR\\Delta T$$\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\left( \\begin{gathered}\n  \\because \\Delta {\\text{V}} = 0 \\\\ \n  \\therefore \\Delta {\\text{Q}} = \\Delta {\\text{U}} \\\\ \n\\end{gathered}  \\right)$<br>$\\therefore \\quad \\Delta T = \\frac{{m{v^2}}}{{5nR}}$<br>but $\\frac{m}{n} = M$<br>$\\therefore \\Delta T = \\frac{{M{v^2}}}{{5R}}$<br>Given, $v = 57.6\\,km/hr = 16\\,m/s$<br>$\\therefore \\Delta T = \\frac{{28 \\times {{(16)}^2} \\times {{10}^{ - 3}}}}{{5 \\times 8}}$<br>$ = 5.6 \\times 16 \\times 2 \\times {10^{ - 3}}$<br>$ = 0.1792\\,K \\approx 0.18\\,K$<br>As, $\\Delta T$ is equivalent for both the units $^\\circ C$ and  $K,{\\mkern 1mu} $<br>${\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} \\Delta T = 0.18^\\circ C$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38546,'physics',27,1380,3306,'A vertical column \\[50\\;cm\\] long at \\[50^\\circ C\\] balances another column of same liquid \\[60\\;cm\\] long at  \\[100^\\circ C.\\] The coefficient of absolute expansion of the liquid is<div class="matching-question"></div>','[{"text": "$0.005/^\\\\circ C$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$0.0005/^\\\\circ C$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$0.002/^\\\\circ C$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$0.0002/^\\\\circ C$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'$\\dfrac{h_{1}}{h_{2}}=\\dfrac{\\rho_{2}}{\\rho_{1}}=\\dfrac{\\left(1+\\gamma \\theta_{1}\\right)}{\\left(1+\\gamma \\theta_{2}\\right)}$<br>$\\left[ {\\because \\rho  = \\frac{{{\\rho _0}}}{{(1 + \\gamma \\,\\theta )}}} \\right]$<br>$ \\Rightarrow \\frac{{50}}{{60}} = \\frac{{1 + 50\\,\\gamma }}{{1 + 100\\,\\gamma }} \\Rightarrow r = 0.005/^\\circ C$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38547,'physics',26,1138,2732,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> A large force is required to draw apart normally two glass plates enclosing a thin water film.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> There is surface tension in above scenario.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'In this scenario, atmospheric pressure is not a relevant factor because it exerts forces in all directions. The significant force at play between two glass surfaces in contact with water is the adhesive force. Adhesive forces pertain to interactions between two dissimilar substances, Here polar nature of water molecule plays role. Due to surface tension of water, glass plates try to stick together. Due to the extensive surface area of the glass slide, the resultant force is substantial. Large force is needed separate two glass plates connected by a thin film of water.So correct option is (1).',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38548,'physics',28,1371,3283,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> During rapid pumping of air in tyres air inside the tyre is hotter than atmospheric air.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Adiabatic process occurs at very high rate.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'During rapid pumping of air into tires, the adiabatic process occurs at a high rate, leading to an increase in temperature. The air inside the tire becomes hotter than atmospheric air. So correct option is (1).',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38550,'physics',27,1381,3315,'In an energy recycling process, $X$ gram of steam at $100^\\circ C$  becomes water at  $100^\\circ C$  which converts $Y$ gram of ice at $0^\\circ C$ into water at $100^\\circ C.$ The ratio of  $X$  and  $Y$  will be','[{"text": "$\\\\dfrac{1}{3}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{2}{3}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "3", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "2", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Specific heat of water<br>$ = 4200\\,J\\,kg\\,{K^{ - 1}}$<br>Specific latent heat of fusion<br>$ = 3.36 \\times {10^5}J\\,k{g^{ - 1}}$<br>Specific latent heat of vaporisation<br>$\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, = 22.68 \\times {10^5}J\\,\\,k{g^{ - 1}}$<br>$\\therefore \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{Q_1} = X \\times {10^{ - 3}} \\times 22.68 \\times {10^5}$<br>${\\rm{Also}}\\,\\,\\,\\,{Q_2} = Y \\times {10^{ - 3}} \\times 3.36 \\times {10^5}$<br>$\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, + Y \\times {10^{ - 3}} \\times 4200 \\times 100$<br>$\\therefore {Q_1} - {Q_2} \\Rightarrow \\frac{X}{Y} = \\frac{{7.56}}{{22.68}} = \\frac{1}{3}$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38551,'physics',26,1138,2733,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> The impurities always decrease the surface tension of a liquid.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> The change in surface tension of the liquid depends upon the degree of contamination of the impurity.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Insoluble impurities like detergent generally decrease the surface tension of a liquid. The extent of this decrease depends on the type and degree of contamination by the impurity. So correct option is (1).',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38554,'physics',27,1380,3313,'For a certain thermocouple, if the temperature of the cold junction is  $0^\\circ C,$  the neutral temperature and inversion temperatures are  $285^\\circ C$ and $570^\\circ C,$ respectively. If the cold junction is brought to $10^\\circ C,$ then the new neutral and inversion temperatures are respectively','[{"text": "$285^\\\\circ C$ and $560^\\\\circ C$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$285^\\\\circ C$ and  $570^\\\\circ C$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$295^\\\\circ C$ and $560^\\\\circ C$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$275^\\\\circ C$ and  $560^\\\\circ C$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Neutral temperature remains same.<br> Inversion temperature,  ${T_i} = 2{T_n} - {T_c} = 2 \\times 285 - 10 = 560^\\circ C$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38555,'physics',26,1138,2733,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> The concept of surface tension is held only for liquids.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Surface tension occurs for gases.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Liquids form definite surface. Gases do not have surface. The intermolecular distance among gas molecules is considerably greater than that among liquid molecules. Consequently, the cohesive forces among gas molecules are very weak, whereas they are much stronger in liquids. Hence, the concept of surface tension applies to liquids but not to gases. So correct option is (3).',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38556,'physics',28,1370,3280,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> The specific heat of a gas in an adiabatic process is zero and in an isothermal process is infinite.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Specific heat of gas is inversely proportional change in temperature.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'$C=\\dfrac{Q}{m(\\Delta T)}$. In an adiabatic process, the specific heat $C$ is zero; $(Q=0)$<br>(i.e., no heat exchange). In an isothermal process, the specific heat is infinite due to$(\\Delta T=0)$ So, the fact that $C \\propto \\dfrac{1}{\\Delta T}$ does not sufficient to explain Assertion fully. So correct option is (2).',NULL,'Hard','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38558,'physics',27,1382,3318,'The temperature of hot and cold end of $20\\,g$ long rod in thermal steady state are at $100^\\circ C$ and $20^\\circ C$ respectively temperature of the centre of the rod is','[{"text": "$50^\\\\circ C$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$60^\\\\circ C$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$40^\\\\circ C$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$30^\\\\circ C$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'<img src=\'/img/data/physics/27_38558_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />  Rate of heat flow through the rod is given as<br>$\\frac{{dQ}}{{dt}} = \\frac{{KA(\\Delta t)}}{L}\\quad \\quad \\quad (1)$<br>As, $\\left(\\dfrac{d Q}{d t}\\right)_{A C}=\\left(\\dfrac{d Q}{d t}\\right)_{C B}$<br>$\\dfrac{K A\\left(100-T_{0}\\right)}{L / 2}=\\dfrac{K A\\left(T_{0}-20\\right)}{L / 2}$<br>$\\Rightarrow 2 T_{0}=120^{\\circ}$<br>$ \\Rightarrow {T_0} = 60^\\circ C$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38559,'physics',26,1137,2723,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> All the raindrops hit the surface of the earth with a different constant velocity.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> An object falling through a viscous medium eventually attains a terminal velocity.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'When rain drops of varying sizes fall through gravity, they eventually reach their terminal velocities $v_{t}=\\dfrac{2}{9} \\dfrac{g r^{2}\\left(\\rho-\\rho_{\\text {air }}\\right)}{\\eta}$. The viscous drag of air is playing role. The terminal velocity of a drop is proportional to the square of its radius. So, a larger raindrop will have a greater terminal velocity and therefore fall faster than a smaller raindrop. So correct option is (1).',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38560,'physics',28,1371,3288,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> On removing the valve, the air escaping from a cycle tube becomes cool.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> On removing the valve, adiabatic expansion takes place.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'When the valve is removed from a cycle tube, the air inside undergoes adiabatic expansion, which causes a decrease in temperature, making the escaping air feel cooler. So correct option is (1).',NULL,'Easy','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38562,'physics',27,1382,3318,'In the diagram, a system of two metals of equal lengths and of same cross-sectional area are joined together The coefficient of thermal conductivities of the metals are $K$ and $2 K$,  respectively. If the furnace temperature at one end is $300^\\circ C$ and ice box temperature at the other end is $0^\\circ C,$ then the junction temperature is','[{"text": "$100^\\\\circ C$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$125^\\\\circ C$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$150^\\\\circ C$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$200^\\\\circ C$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Temperature of interface,<br>$\\theta  = \\frac{{{K_1}{\\theta _1}{l_2} + {K_2}{\\theta _2}{l_1}}}{{{K_1}{l_2} + {K_2}{l_1}}}$<br>$ = \\frac{{2K \\times 0 \\times l + K \\times 300 \\times l}}{{Kl + 2Kl}}$<br>$ = \\frac{{300\\,Kl}}{{3\\,Kl}} \\Rightarrow \\theta  = 100^\\circ C$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/27_38562_question_1.png'),
(38563,'physics',26,1137,2722,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Water flows faster than honey.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> The coefficient of viscosity of water is less than honey.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Water has a lower coefficient of viscosity compared to honey. It means it offers less resistance to flow. So correct option is (1).',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38564,'physics',28,1369,3279,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> First law of thermodynamics does not state about direction of flow of heat between two temperatures.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Heat supplied to a system is always equal to the increase in its internal energy.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'The first law of thermodynamics focuses on the conversion of mechanical energy into heat energy and vice versa, without specifying the direction of heat flow $(\\Delta Q=\\Delta U+\\Delta W)$. Assertion is correct. Heat supplied to a gas can be converted into both interval energy change and work done. Reason is false. So correct option is (3).',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38565,'physics',29,1300,3145,'A vessel of volume $30\\,{m^3}$ contains an ideal gas at temperature $27^\\circ C$ and pressure $5.16$ bar. The gas is allowed to leak till its pressure falls to atmospheric pressure. Assuming that the temperature remains constant during leakage, the number of moles of the gas that have leaked are <br>(Consider, $R = 8.32\\,J\\,mo{l^{ - 1}}\\,{K^{ - 1}},$ Atmospheric pressure $ = 1bar = {10^5}\\,Pa$ )','[{"text": "$0.5 \\\\times {10^4}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$0.8 \\\\times {10^4}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$0.10 \\\\times {10^4}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$0.5 \\\\times {10^4}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Before leakage,<br>$PV = {n_1}RT\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,(1)$<br>After leakage,<br>${P^\\prime }V = {n_2}RT\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,(2)$<br>Number of moles of gas leaked is given by,<br>$\\left( {{n_1} - {n_2}} \\right)$<br>Substracting equation (2) from equation (1), <br>${n_1} - {n_2} = \\frac{{PV}}{{RT}} - \\frac{{{P^\\prime }V}}{{RT}}$<br>$\\therefore \\,\\,\\,{\\mkern 1mu} {\\kern 1pt} {n_1} - {n_2} = \\frac{V}{{RT}}\\left( {P - {P^\\prime }} \\right)$<br>$ = \\frac{{30}}{{8.32 \\times (27 + 273)}} \\times (5.16 - 1) \\times {10^5}$<br>$ = \\frac{{30 \\times 4.16 \\times {{10}^5}}}{{8.32 \\times 300}}$<br>$\\therefore {n_1} - {n_2} = 0.5 \\times {10^4}$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38566,'physics',27,1382,3319,'A cylinder of radius $r$ and of thermal conductivity $K_{1}$ is surrounded by a cylindrical shell of inner radius $r$ and outer radius $2 r$ made of a material of thermal conductivity $K_{2}$. The effective thermal conductivity of the system is','[{"text": "$\\\\dfrac{1}{3}\\\\left(K_{1}+2 K_{2}\\\\right)$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{1}{2}\\\\left(2 K_{1}+3 K_{2}\\\\right)$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{1}{3}\\\\left(3 K_{2}+2 K_{1}\\\\right)$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{1}{4}\\\\left(K_{1}+3 K_{2}\\\\right)$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Both the cylinders are in parallel, for the heat flow from one end as shown <img src=\'/img/data/physics/27_38566_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />  Hence $K_{\\text {eq }}=\\dfrac{K_{1} A_{1}+K_{2} A_{2}}{A_{1}+A_{2}}$<br>where, $A=$ area of cross-section of inner<br> cylinder $=\\pi R^{2}$<br> and $A_{2}=$ area of cross-section of cylindrical<br> shell<br>$\\begin{aligned}& =\\pi\\left\\{(2 R)^{2}-(R)^{2}\\right\\}=3 \\pi R^{2} \\\\\\Rightarrow \\quad K_{e q} & =\\dfrac{K_{1}\\left(\\pi R^{2}\\right)+K_{2}\\left(3 \\pi R^{2}\\right)}{\\pi R^{2}+3 \\pi R^{2}} \\\\& =\\dfrac{1}{4}\\left(K_{1}+3 K_{2}\\right)\\end{aligned}$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38567,'physics',26,1138,2733,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> At critical temperature, surface tension of a liquid becomes zero.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> At this temperature, intermolecular forces for liquids and gases become equal. Liquid can expand without any restriction.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'At critical temperature $\\left(t_{c}{ }^{\\circ} C\\right)$, the intermolecular forces for liquids and gases become equal, allowing the liquid to expand without any restriction. Surface tension $T_{s}$ varies as shown <img src=\'/img/data/physics/26_38567_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' /> So correct option is (1).',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38569,'physics',28,1371,3285,'$0.08\\,kg$ air is heated at constant volume through $5^\\circ \\,C$. The specific heat of air at constant volume is$0.17\\,kcal{\\rm{/}}kg^\\circ \\,C$ and$J = 4.18\\,\\,joule{\\rm{/}}cal$. The change in its internal energy is approximately.','[{"text": "$298\\\\,J$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$284\\\\,J$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$318\\\\,J$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$142\\\\,J$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'$\\Delta U=m C_{V} \\Delta t$<br>$\\left( {\\because \\Delta U = \\frac{t}{2}nR\\Delta T\\,{\\text{and}}\\,{C_v} = \\frac{t}{2}R} \\right)$<br> $ = 0.08 \\times 0.17 \\times {10^3} \\times 5$<br>$ = 0.068 \\times {10^3}cal$<br>$ = 68\\,cal = 284.2\\,J$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',2024,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38618,'physics',27,1384,3326,'If a tensile force is suddenly removed from a wire then its temperature will','[{"text": "decrease", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "increase", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "become zero", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "remains constant", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Sudden removal of tensile force on the wire, increases the kinetic energy of its molecules. Hence temperature of the wire increases.<br>So correct option is (2)',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38571,'physics',26,1138,2730,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> A large soap bubble expands while a small bubble shrinks when they are connected to each other by a capillary tube.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> The excess pressure inside a bubble (or drop) is inversely proportional to the radius.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'When a large soap bubble and a small bubble are connected by a capillary tube, the larger bubble will expand while the smaller one will shrink. The reason is also true. The excess pressure inside a bubble is indeed inversely proportional to its radius, which explains why the larger bubble expands.<br>Excess Pressure $(\\Delta P) \\propto \\dfrac{1}{R}$<br>So correct option is (1).',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38572,'physics',29,1304,3154,'Two identical glass bulbs, connected by a thin glass tube, are filled with a monoatomic gas at $S.T.P.$ One of the bulbs is placed in a vessel containing ice while other is placed in a hot bath. The pressure of the gas increases to $1.5$ times the initial pressure. What will be the temperature of the hot bath','[{"text": "$632^\\\\circ C$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$436^\\\\circ C$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$546^\\\\circ C$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$737^\\\\circ C$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'As the bulbs are connected by the tube, the number of moles of gas is conserved.<br>$i.e.,{({n_1} + {n_2}{\\rm{)}}_{{\\rm{initial}}}}{\\rm{ }} = {\\left( {{n_1} + {n_2}} \\right)_{{\\rm{final}}}}$<br>By equation of states,<br>$n = \\frac{{PV}}{{RT}}$<br>$\\therefore \\,\\,\\,{\\mkern 1mu} {\\kern 1pt} \\frac{{PV}}{{R \\times 273}} + \\frac{{PV}}{{R \\times 273}} = \\frac{{1.5PV}}{{R \\times 273}} + \\frac{{1.5PV}}{{RT}}$<br>${\\mkern 1mu} \\quad \\quad \\quad {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} (\\because \\,\\,\\,{\\text{at}}{\\mkern 1mu} \\,\\,S.T.P.\\,\\,T = 273\\,K)$<br>$\\therefore \\,\\,\\,{\\mkern 1mu} {\\kern 1pt} \\frac{2}{{273}} = \\frac{{1.5}}{{273}} + \\frac{{1.5}}{T}$<br>$\\therefore \\,\\,\\,{\\mkern 1mu} {\\kern 1pt} \\frac{{2 - 1.5}}{{273}} = \\frac{{1.5}}{{\\,T}}$<br>$\\therefore \\,\\,\\,{\\mkern 1mu} {\\kern 1pt} T = \\frac{{273 \\times 1.5}}{{0.5}} = 819\\,K = (819 - 273)^\\circ C$<br>$\\therefore T = 546^\\circ C$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38573,'physics',28,1370,3280,'$0.08\\,kg$ air is heated at constant volume through $5^\\circ C$. The specific heat of air at constant volume is $0.17k\\,cal/kg^\\circ C$ and $J = 4.18\\,joule/cal$. The change is its internal energy is approximately.','[{"text": "$298\\\\,J$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${284\\\\,J}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$284\\\\,J$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${142\\\\,J}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'$\\Delta U=m C_{V} \\Delta t$<br>$=0.08 \\times 0.17 \\times 10^{3} \\times 5$<br>$ = 0.068 \\times {10^3}cal$<br>$ = 68\\,cal$<br>$ = 68 \\times 4 \\cdot 18\\,J$<br>$ = 284 \\cdot 2\\,J$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2023,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38574,'physics',27,1382,3323,'At  $273^\\circ C,$  the emissive power of a perfect black body is $R$. What is its value at $0^\\circ C$ ?','[{"text": "$\\\\dfrac{R}{4}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{R}{16}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{R}{2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "None of these", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'From Stefan\'s law, the total radiant energy emitted per second per unit surface area of a black body is proportional to the fourth power of the absolute temperature $(T)$ of the body.<br>$\\therefore E=\\sigma T^{4}$<br>where, $\\sigma$ is Stefan\'s constant.<br>Given, $\\quad E_{1}=R$<br>$ \\Rightarrow {T_1} = 273^\\circ C = 273 + 273 = 546\\,K$<br>${T_2} = 0^\\circ C = 273\\,K$<br>$\\therefore \\dfrac{E_{1}}{E_{2}}=\\dfrac{T_{1}^{4}}{T_{2}^{4}}$<br>$\\Rightarrow \\quad E_{2}=\\dfrac{T_{2}^{4}}{T_{1}^{4}} E_{1}$<br>$\\Rightarrow \\quad E_{2}=\\dfrac{(273)^{4}}{(546)^{4}} R$<br>$\\Rightarrow \\quad E_{2}=\\dfrac{R}{16}$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38575,'physics',26,1138,2730,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Tiny drops of liquid resist deforming forces better than bigger drops.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Excess pressure inside a drop is directly proportional to surface tension and inversely to radius.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'When a liquid drop is placed on a glass plate, its shape is influenced by two forces: gravity and surface tension. For very small drops, the potential energy resulting from gravity is negligible when compared to the potential energy from surface tension. Therefore, in such cases, the drop\'s shape is solely determined by surface tension, resulting in a spherical shape.<br>$(\\Delta P) \\propto\\left[\\dfrac{\\text { surface tension }}{\\text { Radius }}\\right]$<br>So correct option is (2).',NULL,'Hard','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38576,'physics',28,1371,3292,'The pressure $(P)$ and temperature $(T)$ relationship of an ideal gas obeys the equation $P T^{2}=$ constant. The volume expansion coefficient of the gas will be','[{"text": "$3 T^{2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{3}{T^{3}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{3}{T^{2}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{3}{T}$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'From ideal gas equation, $P V=n R T$<br>or $P=\\dfrac{n R T}{V}$<br>Since, $P T^{2}=$ constant (given)<br>$ \\Rightarrow \\frac{{nR{T^3}}}{V} = K\\quad \\quad \\quad (1)$<br>$\\left(\\because P=\\dfrac{n R T}{V}\\right)$<br>$\\Rightarrow n R T^{3}=K V$<br>Differentiating both sides<br>$ \\Rightarrow 3nR{T^2} = K\\frac{{dV}}{{dT}}\\quad \\quad (2)$<br>Dividing (2) by (1)<br>$\\dfrac{3 n R T^{2} \\cdot V}{n R T^{3}}=\\dfrac{K}{K} \\dfrac{d V}{d T} \\Rightarrow \\dfrac{3}{T}=\\left(\\dfrac{d V}{d T}\\right) \\cdot \\dfrac{1}{V}$<br>$\\therefore$ volume expansion coefficient<br>$=\\dfrac{1}{V} \\dfrac{d V}{d T}=\\dfrac{3}{T}$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',2023,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38578,'physics',27,1382,3322,'Read Assertion and Reason carefully to mark the correct option  given  below.<br><br><strong>Assertion:</strong> As temperature of a black body is raised, wavelength corresponding to maximum energy reduces.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Higher temperature would mean higher energy and hence higher wavelength','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'According to Wien\'s displacement law, the wavelength corresponding to maximum emission of radiation decreases with increasing temperature,<br>i.e. $\\lambda_{m} \\propto \\dfrac{1}{T}$, when temperature of a black body is raised, wavelength decreases means its corresponding energy reduces.<br> Hence, the statement given in Reason is contradictory. Hence, option (3) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38579,'physics',26,1133,2711,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> An air bubble comes from the bottom of a lake to the top.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Radius of bubble decreases in above process.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Fluid, pressure increases with depth, the pressure $(P)$ at the top $(o)$ will be lower than that at the bottom $(p)$, given by the formula $P=P_{o}+\\rho g h$, where $\\rho$ is density, $g$ is a acceleration due to gravity, and $\\mathrm{h}$ is depth. As a result, an air bubble moves from the bottom to the top and not sideways.<br>By Boyle\'s law, $P V=$ Constant. Therefore, as pressure decreases, volume increases, meaning the radius of the bubble increases. So correct option is (3).',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38581,'physics',28,1377,3303,'$1\\,g$ of a liquid is converted to vapour at $3 \\times {10^5}\\,Pa$ pressure. If $10 \\%$ of the heat supplied is used for increasing the volume by $1600\\,c{m^3}$ during this phase change, then the increase in internal energy in the process will be','[{"text": "$432000 J$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$4320 J$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$4800 J$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$4.32 \\\\times {10^8}\\\\,J$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'By First Law of thermodynamics $Q=\\Delta U+W$.<br>Here  $W = P \\times \\Delta V$<br>$ = 3 \\times {10^5} \\times 1600 \\times {10^{ - 6}}\\,J = 480\\,J.$<br>$\\therefore$ Heat supplied $Q = \\frac{w}{{0.1}} = 4800\\,J$.<br>$\\therefore \\Delta U=Q-W=(4800-480) J=4320 J$.',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',2023,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38583,'physics',26,1134,2713,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Specific gravity of a fluid is a dimensionless quantity.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> It is the ratio of density of fluid to the density of water.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Specific gravity (or) relative density dimensionless quantity. It is defined as the ratio of the density of a fluid to the density of water. So correct option is (1).',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38585,'physics',28,1371,3292,'Match Column I with Column II. Choose the correct answer from the options given below.<div class="matching-question"><div class="column-left">A. Isothermal process<br>B. Adiabatic process<br>C. Isochoric process<br>D. Isobaric process</div><div class="column-right">P. Work done by the gas decreases internal energy<br>Q. No change in internal energy<br>R. The heat absorbed goes\npartly to increase internal energy and partly to do work<br>S. No  work is done on or by the gas</div></div>','[{"text": "${\\\\rm{A}} - {\\\\rm{P,}}\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\rm{B}} - {\\\\rm{Q,}}\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\rm{C}} - {\\\\rm{S,}}\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\rm{D}} - {\\\\rm{R}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{A}} - {\\\\rm{Q,}}\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\rm{B}} - {\\\\rm{P,}}\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\rm{C}} - {\\\\rm{S,}}\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\rm{D}} - {\\\\rm{R}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{A}} - {\\\\rm{P,}}\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\rm{B}} - {\\\\rm{Q,}}\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\rm{C}} - {\\\\rm{R,}}\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\rm{D}} - {\\\\rm{S}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{A}} - {\\\\rm{Q,}}\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\rm{B}} - {\\\\rm{P,}}\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\rm{C}} - {\\\\rm{R,}}\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\rm{D}} - {\\\\rm{S}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Isothermal process change in temperature,$\\Delta T=0$<br>So, $\\Delta U=0$<br>Adiabatic process : exchange of heat, $\\Delta Q=0$<br>So, $\\Delta Q=\\Delta U+\\Delta W, \\Delta U=-\\Delta W$<br>If work is positive, $\\Delta U$ is negative.<br>Isochoric process : $\\Delta V=0$<br>So, $\\Delta W=0$<br>Isobaric process : $\\Delta W \\neq 0, \\Delta U \\neq 0$ and<br>$\\Delta \\phi=\\Delta U+\\Delta W$,<br>So, heat absorbed goes partly to increase internal energy and partly to do work.<br>${\\rm{A}} \\to {\\rm{Q}},{\\rm{B}} \\to {\\rm{P}},{\\rm{C}} \\to {\\rm{S}},{\\rm{D}} \\to {\\rm{R}}$',NULL,'Hard','Match the following',2023,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38587,'physics',26,1134,2713,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> For Reynold number $R_{e}>2000$, the flow of fluid is turbulent.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Inertial forces are dominant compared to the viscous forces at such high Reynold numbers','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'$R_{e}=\\left[\\dfrac{\\text { Inertia force }}{\\text { Viscous force }}\\right]$<br>A Reynolds number above 2000 generally indicates turbulent flow. This is because at high Reynolds numbers, inertial forces dominate over viscous forces, leading to turbulent flow. So correct option is (1).',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(48060,'biology',10,260,514,'The most important function of plasma membrane is that it','[{"text": "Cell growth", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Cell division", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Transports the molecules across", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both (1) and (2)", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'One of the most important functions of the plasma membrane is the transport of the molecules across it',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38589,'physics',28,1372,3294,'One mole of an ideal monoatomic gas is taken round the cyclic process ${M N O M}$. The work done by the gas is','[{"text": "${4.5 P_{0} V_{0}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${4 P_{0} V_{0}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${9 P_{0} V_{0}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${2 P_{0} V_{0}}$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Work done for cyclic process is given by area of the loop.<br>Work done ${W=\\dfrac{1}{2} \\times}$ Base ${\\times}$ height<br>${=\\dfrac{1}{2}\\left(3 V_{0}-V_{0}\\right)\\left(3 P_{0}-P_{0}\\right)}$<br>${=\\dfrac{1}{2}\\left(2 V_{0}\\right)\\left(2 P_{0}\\right)}$<br>${W=2 P_{0} V_{0}}$.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2024,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/28_38589_question_1.png'),
(38590,'physics',27,1382,3323,'A sphere and a cube of same material and same volume are heated upto same temperature and allowed to cool in the same surroundings. The ratio of the amounts of radiation emitted will be','[{"text": "$1: 1$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{4}{3} \\\\pi: 1$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\left(\\\\dfrac{\\\\pi}{6}\\\\right)^{1 / 3}: 1$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{1}{2}\\\\left(\\\\dfrac{4 \\\\pi}{3}\\\\right)^{2 / 3}: 1$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Energy radiated per second is given as,<br> $P=A \\sigma \\varepsilon T^{4}$<br>$P \\propto A$<br>[As temperature of surface is constant]<br>$\\frac{{{P_1}}}{{{P_2}}} = \\frac{{{A_1}}}{{{A_2}}} = \\frac{{{A_s}}}{{{A_c}}}$<br>$ \\Rightarrow \\quad \\frac{{{P_1}}}{{{P_2}}} = \\frac{{4\\pi {R^2}}}{{6{a^2}}}\\quad \\quad \\quad (1)$<br>Also, volume of sphere $=$ volume of cube<br>$\\frac{4}{3}\\pi {R^3} = {a^3}$<br>$ \\Rightarrow \\quad \\frac{R}{a} = {\\left( {\\frac{3}{{4\\pi }}} \\right)^{1/3}}\\quad \\quad \\quad (2)$<br>From Eqs. (1) and (2), we get<br>$\\dfrac{P_{1}}{P_{2}}=\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{6}\\right)^{1 / 3}$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38591,'physics',26,1138,2727,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> A thin stainless steel needle can lay floating on a still water surface.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> The needle floats when the buoyancy force balances the weight of the object.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'"A thin stainless steel needle placed on calm water can remain afloat because the upward force arising from surface tension counters the needle\'s weight." So correct option is (3).',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38593,'physics',28,1372,3294,'A thermodynamic system is taken through the cycle abcda. The work done by the gas along the path ${b c}$ is:','[{"text": "Zero", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${30 J}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${-90 J}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${-60 J}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Path ${b c}$ is an isochoric process.<br>${\\therefore \\quad}$ Work done by gas along path ${b c}$ is zero.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2024,'NEET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/28_38593_question_1.png'),
(38594,'physics',27,1382,3324,'A planet having average surface temperature $T_{0}$ at an average distance $d$ from the sun. Assuming that the planet receives radiant energy from the sun only and it loses radiant energy only from the surface and neglecting all other atmospheric effects we conclude','[{"text": "$T_{0} \\\\propto d^{2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$T_{0} \\\\propto d^{-2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$T_{0} \\\\propto d^{1 / 2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$T_{0} \\\\propto d^{-1 / 2}$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Energy received per second by the planet $=\\dfrac{P}{4 \\pi d^{2}}\\left(\\pi R^{2}\\right)$<br>where, $P$ is power radiated by the sun and $R$ is the radius of the planet.<br>Further, energy radiated per second by the planet according to Stefan\'s law is $\\sigma\\left(4 \\pi R^{2}\\right) T_{0}^{4}$.<br> For thermal equilibrium to exist, we get<br>$\\begin{aligned}& \\dfrac{P}{4 \\pi d^{2}}\\left(\\pi R^{2}\\right)=\\sigma\\left(4 \\pi R^{2}\\right) T_{0}^{4} \\\\& \\Rightarrow \\quad T_{0}^{4} \\propto d^{-2} \\\\& \\Rightarrow \\quad T_{0} \\propto d^{-1 / 2}\\end{aligned}$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38595,'physics',26,1135,2714,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> The stream of water flowing at high speed from a garden hose pipe tends to spread like a fountain when held vertically up, but tends to narrow down when held vertically down.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> In any steady flow of an incompressible fluid, the volume flow rate of the fluid remains constant.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Assertion is correct by definition of "steady flow". Here, the volume flow rate remains constant. So correct option is (1).',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38596,'physics',29,1302,3150,'A mixture of two gases consists of $3$ moles of nitrogen and $6$ moles of neon at temperature $27^\\circ C$. Neglecting all vibrational modes, the total internal energy of the system is<br>$\\left( {R = 8.32\\,J\\,mo{l^{ - 1}}\\,{K^{ - 1}}} \\right)$','[{"text": "$3.26 \\\\times {10^4}\\\\,J$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$4.12 \\\\times {10^4}\\\\,J$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$7.33 \\\\times {10^4}\\\\,J$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2.24 \\\\times {10^4}\\\\,J$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Internal energy of a gas with $f$ degrees of freedom,<br>$U = \\frac{f}{2}nRT$<br>Now, ${f_{{N_2}}} = \\frac{5}{2},{f_{Ne}} = \\frac{3}{2}$<br>$\\therefore \\,\\,\\,{\\mkern 1mu} {\\kern 1pt} {U_{total{\\rm{ }}}} = {U_{{N_2}}} + {U_{Ne}}$<br>${\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu}  = \\frac{5}{2} \\times 3 \\times 8.32 \\times (27 + 273) + $<br>${\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} \\frac{3}{2} \\times 6 \\times 8.32 \\times (27 + 273)$<br>$\\,\\,\\,\\, = 3 \\times 8.32 \\times 300 \\times \\left( {\\frac{5}{2} + 3} \\right)$<br>$ = 4.16 \\times 900 \\times 11$<br>$ = 41184\\,J$<br>$\\therefore {U_{total{\\rm{ }}}} = 4.12 \\times {10^4}\\,J$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38597,'physics',28,1369,3279,'The change in internal energy of $3\\,mol$ of helium gas when its temperature is increased by ${2 K}$','[{"text": "$74.8\\\\,cal$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$37.4\\\\,cal$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$37.4\\\\,J$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$74.8\\\\,J$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'${\\Delta U=\\mu C_{\\nu} d T=\\mu \\times \\dfrac{3}{2} R d T}$<br>$=3 \\times \\dfrac{3}{2} \\times 8.31 \\times 2=74.8 {~J}$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38598,'physics',27,1382,3323,'The surface temperature of the sun is $T$ kelvin and the solar constant for a planet is $S$. The sun subtends an angle $\\theta$ at the planet. Then,','[{"text": "$S \\\\propto T^{4}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$S \\\\propto T^{2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$S \\\\propto \\\\theta^{2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$S \\\\propto \\\\theta$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Let radius of sun $=R$ and distance of the earth from the sun $=d$<br>Power radiated from the sun<br>$=\\left(4 \\pi R^{2}\\right) \\sigma T^{4}=P$<br>Energy received/area/s $=S=P / 4 \\pi d^{2}$<br>$\\begin{aligned}S & =4 \\pi R^{2} \\sigma \\dfrac{T^{4}}{4 \\pi d^{2}}=\\sigma T^{4} \\dfrac{R^{2}}{d^{2}} \\\\& =\\dfrac{1}{4} \\sigma T^{4}\\left(\\dfrac{2 R}{d}\\right)^{2}\\end{aligned}$<br>Angle subtended by sun at the earth,<br>$\\begin{aligned}\\theta & =2 R / d \\\\S & =\\text { constant } \\times T^{4} \\times \\theta^{2} \\\\\\Rightarrow \\quad S & \\propto T^{4}\\end{aligned}$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38599,'physics',26,1134,2713,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Turbulence is always dissipative.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Low Reynolds number promotes turbulence.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Turbulent dissipation is conversion of mechanical energy to heat via viscous forces. High Reynold number (>2000) promotes it. So correct option is (3).',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38600,'physics',28,1371,3284,'A gas at ${50 {~N} / {m}^{2}}$ pressure is compressed from ${10 m^{3}}$ to ${4 m^{3}}$ under constant pressure. Subsequently, it is given a heat of $100\\,J$. The internal energy of the gas will be','[{"text": "increased by $100\\\\,J$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "increased by $200\\\\,J$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "increased by $400\\\\,J$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "increased by $300\\\\,J$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'${d Q=0}$<br>$\\therefore dU =  - \\Delta W =  - P\\left( {{V_2} - {V_1}} \\right)$<br>$ =  - 50(4 - 10) = 300\\,\\,J$<br>Additional heat given ${=100 {~J}}$<br>${\\therefore}$ Total increment in the internal energy<br>$d U=300+100=400 {~J}$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38602,'physics',27,1382,3321,'A body at a temperature of $727^\\circ C$ and has surface area $5\\,c{m^2},$ radiates $300\\,J$ of energy each minute. The emissivity is  (given, Boltzmznn constant $ = 5.67 \\times {10^{ - 8}}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} W{m^{ - 2}}\\,{K^{\\, - 4}}$)','[{"text": "$e=0.18$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$e=0.02$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$e=0.2$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$e=0.15$<br>", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'$Q = EAt = e\\sigma \\left( {{T^4} - T_0^4} \\right)$At<br>where, $t=$ time<br>and $T_{0}=$ temperature of surroundings <br>When $T>T_{0}$,<br>$Q = e\\,\\sigma {T^4}\\,\\,{\\rm{At}}$<br>$300 = e \\times \\left( {5.67 \\times {{10}^{ - 8}}} \\right){(1000)^4}$<br>$\\left( {5.00 \\times {{10}^{ - 4}}} \\right)(60)$<br>$ \\Rightarrow e = 0.18$<br>$(\\therefore T = 273 + 727 = 1000\\,\\,K)$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38603,'physics',26,1138,2731,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> When the height of a tube is less than the liquid rise in the capillary tube, the liquid does not overflow.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> The product of the radius of the meniscus and the height of the liquid in the capillary tube always remains constant.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'When the value of $h$ decreases due to an insufficient tube length, the radius of curvature of the liquid meniscus increases, striving to uphold the constant product of $h$ and $R$, which is a finite value represented by $2 S \\rho g$. This shape prevents the liquid from overflowing. <img src=\'/img/data/physics/26_38603_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' /> In $h$ increases $R$ adjusts such that $(h R)=$ constant. So correct option is (1).',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38604,'physics',28,1371,3285,'Figure shows the pressure versus temperature curves for a given mass of a gas corresponding to two different volumes ${V_{1}}$ and ${V_{2}}$, then:','[{"text": "${V_{1}>V_{2}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${V_{1} < V_{2}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${V_{1}=V_{2}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "The information is insufficient", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Use ${P V=n R T}$<br>$\\frac{P}{T} = \\frac{{nR}}{V}$<br>$\\therefore {\\rm{ slope }} \\propto \\frac{1}{V}$<br>So correct Option is (1)',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/28_38604_question_1.png'),
(38606,'physics',27,1382,3317,'A heat flux of $4000\\,J{\\rm{/}}s$ is to be passed through a copper rod of length $10\\,cm$ and area of crosssection $100\\,sq$. $cm$. The thermal conductivity of copper is $400\\,W{\\rm{/}}mC$. The two ends of this rod must be kept at a temperature difference of-','[{"text": "$1^\\\\circ C$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$10^\\\\circ C$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$100^\\\\circ C$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$1000^\\\\circ C$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'$\\frac{{\\Delta Q}}{{\\Delta t}} = KA\\frac{{\\Delta T}}{{\\Delta x}}$<br>$4000 = 400 \\times 100 \\times {10^4}\\frac{{\\Delta T}}{{10 \\times {{10}^{ - 2}}}}$<br>or $\\Delta T = \\frac{{40000 \\times {{10}^{ - 2}}}}{{40000 \\times {{10}^{ - 4}}}} = {10^{2^\\circ }}C$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38607,'physics',26,1138,2727,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> A needle placed carefully on the surface of water may float, whereas the ball of the same material will always sink.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> The buoyancy of an object does not depend on both on the materials and shape of the object.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'When a needle is gently placed on the surface of water, it remains floating due to the surface tension of water, which prevents the needle from sinking. However, in the case of a steel ball, the surface tension of the water is insufficient to keep it afloat, causing it to sink. Buoyancy force depends on density and so on material of object. So correct option is (3).',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(48061,'biology',10,260,514,'Endocytosis is a term used for','[{"text": "When a molecule enters into the cell", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "When a molecule leaves the cell", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "When a molecule enters into and also leaves the cell", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "All of them", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Endocytosis is a cellular process in which substances are brought into the cell.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38609,'physics',28,1371,3282,'Ten moles of an ideal gas at constant temperature 500 K is compressed from 50 L to ${5 L}$. Work done in the process is (Given, ${\\left.R=8.31 J-{mol}^{-1}-K^{-1}\\right)}$','[{"text": "${-1.2 \\\\times 10^{4} J}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${-2.4 \\\\times 10^{4} J}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${-4.8 \\\\times 10^{4} {~J}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${-9.6 \\\\times 10^{4} J}$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Work done at constant temperature (i.e., isothermal process),<br>$W = 2.3\\,n\\,RT\\,\\,{\\log _{10}}\\left( {\\frac{{{V_2}}}{{{V_1}}}} \\right)$<br>$ = 2.3 \\times 10 \\times 8.31 \\times 500 \\times {\\log _{10}}\\left( {\\frac{5}{{50}}} \\right)$<br>$ =  - 9.6 \\times {10^4}\\,J$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38610,'physics',27,1380,3306,'A steel rod of length $50\\,cm$ has a cross-sectional area of $0.4\\,c{m^2}$. What force would be required to stretch this rod by the same amount as the expansion produced by heating it through $10^\\circ C$. $\\left( {\\alpha  = {{10}^{ - 5}}\\,{K^{ - 1}}} \\right.$ and $\\left. {Y = 2 \\times {{10}^{11}}\\,N{\\rm{/}}{m^2}} \\right)$','[{"text": "$600\\\\,N$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$800\\\\,N$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$500\\\\,N$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$400\\\\,N$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'We have, Force $ = YA\\,\\alpha \\,.\\,\\Delta \\theta $<br>$ = 2 \\times {10^{11}} \\times 0.4 \\times {10^{ - 4}} \\times {10^{ - 5}} \\times 10$<br>$ = 0.8 \\times {10^3} = 800\\,N$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38611,'physics',26,1133,2711,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Sudden fall of pressure at a place indicates a storm.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Air flows from higher pressure to lower pressure.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'A sudden fall in pressure indicates "an approaching storm," as air tends to flow from regions of higher pressure to lower pressure, which can lead to atmospheric disturbances and weather changes associated with storms. So correct option is (1).',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38612,'physics',29,1300,3145,'A cylindrical container is shown in figure in which a gas is enclosed. Its initial volume is ${V}$ and temperature is ${T}$. As no external pressure is applied on the light piston shown, gas pressure must be equal to the atmospheric pressure. If gas temperature is doubled, find its final volume. In its final state if piston is clamped and temperature is again doubled, the ratio of final pressure and initial pressure of the gas is','[{"text": "6", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "4", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "7", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "2", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'In the initial state the pressure, volume and temperature of gas ${P_{0} {~V}}$ and T respectively if ${P_{0}}$ is the atmospheric pressure. It is given that temperature of gas is increased to double its value $i.e.$ up to ${2 T}$. As in initial and final state pressure of gas remains constant as initially as well as finally the piston is exposed to atmospheric pressure. Thus we have from Charles and Gay Loussac law $\\dfrac{V_{1}}{T_{1}}=\\dfrac{V_{2}}{T_{2}}$<br>Here, ${V_2} = \\frac{{{V_1}{T_2}}}{{{T_1}}} = 2V$<br> $\\left[ {{\\text{As}}} \\right.$ ${V_{1}=V, T_{1}=T}$ and ${\\left.T_{2}=2 T\\right]}$<br>Thus after increasing the gas temperature to 27, its volume becomes $2\\,V $. Now the piston is clamped that means, now the volume of gas remains constant. Again its temperature is doubled from ${2 T}$ to ${4 T}$, let the pressure changes from ${P_{0}}$ to ${P^{\\prime}}$. Thus we have<br>$\\dfrac{P_{1}}{T_{1}}=\\dfrac{P_{2}}{T_{2}}$<br>or ${P_2} = {P^\\prime } = \\frac{{{P_1}{T_2}}}{{{T_1}}}$<br>$ = 2{P_0}\\left[ {{\\text{As}}{P_1} = {P_0},{T_1} = 2T\\,\\,{\\text{and}}\\,\\,{T_2} = 4T} \\right]$<br>Thus in final state gas pressure becomes ${2 P_{0}}$.',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/29_38612_question_1.png'),
(38613,'physics',28,1371,3285,'A cylinder of fixed capacity (of $44.8 \\mathrm{~L}$ ) contains 2 moles of helium gas at STP. What is the amount of heat needed to raise the temperature of the gas in the cylinder by $20^\\circ C$ ?<br> (Take, $R = 8.31{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} Jmo{l^{ - 1}}{K^{ - 1}}$)','[{"text": "$996\\\\,J$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$831 J$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$499\\\\,J$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$374 J$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Since, the volume of cylinder is fixed and helium is a monoatomic gas.<br> The internal energy $=\\mu C_{V} \\Delta T$<br>$\\begin{aligned}\\Rightarrow \\quad U & =\\dfrac{1}{2} f \\mu R \\Delta T\\left[\\therefore C_{V}=\\dfrac{1}{2} f R\\right] \\\\U & =\\dfrac{1}{2} \\times 3 \\times 2 \\times 8.31 \\times 20 \\\\& =498.6 \\approx 499 \\mathrm{~J} \\quad(\\because f=3)\\end{aligned}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38614,'physics',27,1381,3315,'Find the quantity of heat required to convert $40\\,gm$ of ice at $ - 20^\\circ C$ into water at $20^\\circ C$. Given ${L_{ice{\\rm{ }}}} = 0.336 \\times {10^6}\\,J{\\rm{/}}kg.\\,Sp$ heat of ice $ = 2100\\,J{\\rm{/}}kg{\\rm{ - }}K\\,sp$ heat of water $ = 4200\\,J{\\rm{/}}kg{\\rm{ - }}K$','[{"text": "$12480\\\\,J$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$10420\\\\,J$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$16450\\\\,J$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$18480\\\\,J$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Mass of ice $ = \\frac{{40}}{{1000}} = 0.04\\,Kg$<br>Heat required to raise the temperature of ice from $ - 20^\\circ C$ to $0^\\circ C = 0.04 \\times 2100 \\times 20 = 1680\\,J$ <br>Heat required to convert the ice into water at $0^\\circ C = mL = .04 \\times 0.336 \\times {10^6} = 13440\\,J$<br>Heat required to heat water from $0^\\circ C$ to  $20^\\circ C$  $ = .04 \\times 4200 \\times 20 = 3360\\,J$<br>Total heat required<br>$ = 1680 + 13440 + 3360 = 18480\\,J$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38615,'physics',26,1133,2709,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> A dam for water reservoir is built thicker at the bottom than at the top.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Pressure of water is very large at the bottom.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'In a dam the pressure exerted by the water increases with depth. Bottom is constructed with more materials and thickness to ensure stability. So correct option is (1)',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38617,'physics',28,1377,3303,'The state of a thermodynamic system is represented by','[{"text": "pressure only", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "volume only", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "pressure, volume and temperature", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "number of moles", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'In thermodynamics, the value of a state variable depends only on the particular state not on the path used to attain that state. Pressure $(p)$, volume $(V)$, temperature $(T)$ and mass $(m)$ are state variables.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38619,'physics',26,1134,2712,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> The path taken by a fluid particle under a steady flow is a streamline.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> The flow of fluid is said to be steady if at any given point, the quantity of fluid flowing per second through any section remains constant.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Reason describes \'steady flow\' and explains Assertion. Assertion is difinition for stream line. So correct option is (1).',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38620,'physics',28,1371,3292,'$1\\,c{m^3}$  of water at its boiling point absorbs $540{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} cal$ of heat to become steam with a volume of  $1671\\,c{m^3}.$ If the atmospheric pressure $ = 1.013 \\times {10^5}N{m^{ - 2}}$ and the mechanical equivalent of heat $ = 4.19\\,J\\,cal,$ the energy spent in this process in overcoming intermolecular forces is','[{"text": "$540{\\\\mkern 1mu} {\\\\mkern 1mu} cal$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$40{\\\\mkern 1mu} {\\\\mkern 1mu} cal$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$500{\\\\mkern 1mu} {\\\\mkern 1mu} cal$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "zero", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Change in volume, i.e.<br>$\\Delta V = {V_2} - {V_1}$<br>$ = 1671 - 1 = 1670\\,c{m^3}$<br>$ = 1670 \\times {10^{ - 6}}\\,{m^3}$<br>Work done by the system,<br>$W = p\\Delta V$<br>$ = 1.013 \\times {10^5} \\times 1670 \\times {10^{ - 6}}$<br>$ = 1691.71 \\times {10^{ - 1}}\\,J$<br>$ = 169.171\\,J$<br>(as, we know $1\\,cal = 4.2\\,J\\,)$<br>$ = \\frac{{169.171}}{{4.2}} = 40.06\\,cal$<br>Total energy spent in boiling to overcome intermolecular force $=540-40.06$<br>$ = 499.93\\,cal$<br>$ \\approx 500\\,cal$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38621,'physics',29,1300,3145,'A glass container encloses a gas at a pressure of ${8 \\times 10^{5} {~Pa}}$ and $300\\,K$ temperature. The container walls can bear a maximum pressure of ${10^{6} {~Pa}}$. If the temperature of container is gradually increased, the temperature   at which container will break is','[{"text": "$375\\\\,K$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$276\\\\,K$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$434\\\\,K$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$724\\\\,K$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'From gas law, we have for a constant volume container<br>$\\dfrac{P_{1}}{T_{1}}=\\dfrac{P_{2}}{T_{2}} \\quad \\text { or } T_{2}=\\left(\\dfrac{P_{2}}{P_{1}}\\right) T_{1}$<br>Given that ${P_{1}^{2}=8 \\times 10^{5} {~Pa}, T_{1}=300 {~K}}$<br>${P_{2}=10^{6} {~Pa}}$, then we have ${T_{2}=\\dfrac{10^{6}}{8 \\times 10^{5}} \\times 300=375 {~K}}$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38622,'physics',27,1381,3314,'The specific heat capacity of body depends on','[{"text": "the heat given", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "the temperature raised", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "mass of the body", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "the material of the body", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Specific heat capacity ${S=\\dfrac{\\Delta Q}{m \\Delta T}}$ <br>if ${\\Delta Q=1 {cal}, \\Delta T=1^{\\circ} {C}, m=1 {~kg}}$<br>i.e., the specific heat is the amount of heat per unit mass required to raise the temperature by one degree celsius.<br>The specific heat capacity of different materials is different.<br>So correct option is (4)',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38623,'physics',26,1136,2716,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> A spinning cricket ball deviates from its trajectory as it moves through air.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> The ball is moving forward, and relative to it, the air is moving backward.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'When a cricket ball is bowled in spinning fashion, it creates a pressure difference on its surfaces due to the Bernoulli\'s principle. This leads to a deviation from its initial trajectory. <img src=\'/img/data/physics/26_38623_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' /> The reason correctly explains that the ball experiences relative motion with respect to the air, causing this effect. So correct option is (1).',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38624,'physics',28,1372,3294,'The efficiency of an ideal gas with adiabatic exponent $\\gamma$ for the shown cyclic process would be','[{"text": "$\\\\dfrac{(2 \\\\ln 2-1)}{\\\\gamma /(\\\\gamma-1)}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{(1-1 \\\\ln 2)}{\\\\gamma /(\\\\gamma-1)}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{(2 \\\\ln 2+1)}{\\\\gamma /(\\\\gamma-1)}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{(2 \\\\ln 2-1)}{\\\\gamma /(\\\\gamma+1)}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'As, we know $W=p \\Delta V=n R \\Delta T$<br>$W_{B C}=-n R\\left(2 T_{0}-T_{0}\\right)=-n R T_{0}$<br>and $W_{C A}=+2 n R T_{0} \\ln 2$<br>Work done $=W_{C A}+W_{B C}$<br>$ = 2\\,nR{T_0}\\ln 2 - n\\,R{T_0}$<br>$ = n\\,R{T_0}(2\\ln 2 - 1)$<br>Also, input heat<br>$\\Delta Q_{B C}=n C_{p} \\Delta T=\\dfrac{n R \\gamma T_{0}}{\\gamma-1}$<br>Efficiency $=\\dfrac{\\text { Work }}{\\text { Input heat }}=\\dfrac{(2 \\ln 2-1)}{\\gamma /(\\gamma-1)}$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/28_38624_question_1.png'),
(38626,'physics',27,1382,3324,'In which process, the rate of transfer of heat is maximum?','[{"text": "conduction", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "convection", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "radiation", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "In all these, heat is transferred with the same speed.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'In radiation, the rate of transfer of heat is maximum because radiations are electromagnetic waves.<br>So correct option is (3)',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38627,'physics',26,1134,2713,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> The flow is turbulent for Reynolds number greater than 2000.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Turbulence dissipates kinetic energy in the form of heat.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Both are separate facts<br>Reynold number $=\\left(\\dfrac{\\text { Inertial force }}{\\text { Viscous force }}\\right)$<br>The flow is considered streamline or laminar when the Reynolds number is less than 2000, and it becomes turbulent when the Reynolds number exceeds 2000 . So correct option is (2).',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(46828,'physics',42,323,592,'Pick out the incorrect statement regarding reverse saturation current in the $p$-$n$ junction diode','[{"text": "This current doubles for every $100^\\\\circ C$ rise of temperature", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "This current is due to minority carriers", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "The current carriers are produced by thermal agitation", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Reverse saturation current is also known as leakage current", "isCorrect": false}]',0,NULL,NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38628,'physics',28,1370,3280,'${\\rm{310}}\\,\\,J$ of heat is required to raise the temperature of  $2\\,mol$  of an ideal gas at constant pressure from $25^\\circ C$ to  $35^\\circ C.$ The amount of heat required to raise the temperature of the gas through the same range at constant volume is','[{"text": "${\\\\rm{384}}\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\rm{ }}J$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{144}}\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\rm{ J}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{276}}\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\rm{ }}J$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{452}}\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\rm{ }}J$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'At constant pressure,<br>Heat required $=n C_{p} \\Delta T$<br>$\\Rightarrow 310=2 \\times C_{p} \\times(35-25)$<br>$\\Rightarrow C_{p}=\\dfrac{310}{20}=15.5 \\mathrm{~J} / \\mathrm{mol} / \\mathrm{K}$<br>Similarly, at constant volume<br>Heat required $ = n{C_V}\\Delta T$<br>$ = 2\\left( {{C_p} - R} \\right) \\times \\left( {35 - 25} \\right)$<br>$\\quad \\because {C_p} - {C_V} = R\\,]$<br>$ = 2 \\times (15.5 - 8.3) \\times 10$<br>$ = 2 \\times 7.2 \\times 10 = 144\\,J$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38630,'physics',27,1382,3322,'The sun emits a light with maximum <br> wavelength 510 $nm$ while another star ${x}$ emits a light with maximum wavelength of 350 $nm$ . What is the ratio of surface temperature of the sun and ${\\operatorname{star} x}$ ?','[{"text": "2.1", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "0.68", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "0.46", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "1.45", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Let the sun is at temperature ${T_{1}}$ and the star is at temperature ${T_{2}}$.<br>Then, from Wein\'s displacement law<br> ${\\lambda_{1} T_{1}=\\lambda_{2} T_{2}=}$ constant<br>Given, ${\\lambda_{1}=510 {~nm}, \\lambda_{2}=350 {~nm}}$<br>${\\therefore 510 {~nm} \\times T_{1}=350 {~nm} \\times T_{2}}$<br>${\\dfrac{T_{1}}{T_{2}}=\\dfrac{350}{510}=0.68}$.<br>So correct option is (2)',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38631,'physics',26,1133,2710,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> The shape of an automobile is so designed constant with that its front resembles the streamline pattern of the fluid through which it moves.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> The resistance offered by the fluid is maximum in streamlined shapes.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'When a body moves through a fluid, it experiences resistance from fluid friction, and this resistance increases with the body\'s speed. In the case of cars and aeroplanes moving through the air, they encounter air friction, which is influenced by the shape of the body. To minimize air friction, cars and planes are designed with streamlined shapes. These streamlined shapes allow for the smooth movement of air layers over the upper and lower surfaces of the body, creating lift and ultimately increasing the speed of the vehicle. So correct option is (3).',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38632,'physics',28,1371,3282,'When an ideal gas in a cylinder was compressed isothermally by a piston, the work done on the gas was found to be $1.5 \\times {10^4}\\,J.$ During the process about','[{"text": "$3.6 \\\\times {10^3}{\\\\mkern 1mu} {\\\\mkern 1mu} cal$ of heat flowed out from the gas", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$3.6 \\\\times {10^3}{\\\\mkern 1mu} {\\\\mkern 1mu} cal$ of heat flowed into the gas", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1.5 \\\\times {10^4}{\\\\mkern 1mu} {\\\\mkern 1mu} cal$ of heat flowed into the gas", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1.5 \\\\times {10^4}{\\\\mkern 1mu} {\\\\mkern 1mu} cal$  of heat flowed out from the gas", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'In isothermal compression, there is always an increase of heat which must flow out the gas.<br>$\\Delta Q = \\Delta U + \\Delta W$<br>$ \\Rightarrow \\quad \\Delta Q = \\Delta W\\quad (\\because \\Delta U = 0)$<br>$\\Delta Q = 1.5 \\times {10^4}J = \\frac{{1.5 \\times {{10}^4}}}{{4.18}}cal$<br>$ =  - 3.6 \\times {10^3}cal$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38634,'physics',27,1382,3322,'A black body has maximum wavelength ${\\lambda_{m}}$ at 2000 $K$ . Its corresponding wavelength at <br>3000 $K$ will be','[{"text": "${\\\\dfrac{16}{81} \\\\lambda_{m}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\dfrac{81}{16} \\\\lambda_{m}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\dfrac{3}{2} \\\\lambda_{m}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\dfrac{2}{3} \\\\lambda_{m}}$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'According Wein\'s displacement law,  $\\lambda_{m} T=\\text { constant }$<br>Corresponding wavelength at 3000 $K$$=\\dfrac{2000}{3000} \\lambda_{m}=\\dfrac{2}{3} \\lambda_{m}$.<br>So correct option is (4)',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38635,'physics',26,1139,2734,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Both liquids and gases are not compressible.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> For fluids density is a function of pressure.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but reason is correct.", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'<img src=\'/img/data/physics/26_38635_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' /> Liquids are primarily incompressible, and their density remains consistent under different pressures. Gases, on the other hand, experience significant changes in density as pressure varies. So correct option is (4).',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38636,'physics',28,1372,3293,'For a certain mass of gas, the isothermal curves between $p$ and $V$ at $T_{1}$ and $T_{2}$ temperatures are 1 and 2 as shown in figure. Then,','[{"text": "$T_{1}=T_{2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$T_{1} < T_{2}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$T_{1}>T_{2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Nothing can be predicted", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'In isothermal process, curves between $p$ and $V$ is such that $T_{2}$ is farther from the origin than $T_{1}$. Therefore, $T_{2}>T_{1}$.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/28_38636_question_1.png'),
(38639,'physics',26,1133,2711,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> In a pressure cooker, the water is brought to boil. The cooker is then removed from the stove. Now on removing the lid of the pressure cooker, the water starts boiling again.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> The impurities in water bring down its boiling point.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'We know that the boiling point of a liquid increases with an increase in vapor pressure. In a cooker, water with the food to be cooked is heated, raising the pressure due to confined water vapors. Consequently, water boils at a temperature higher than its standard boiling point of $100^\\circ C$. Now, when the cooker is removed from the stove and its lid is opened, the pressure decreases, causing the boiling point to return to $100^\\circ C$, and water starts boiling again. When we add small impurities which are solid to water, the boiling point increases. So correct option is (3).',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38640,'physics',28,1371,3292,'A sample of gas expands from volume $V_{1}$ to $V_{2}$. The amount of work done by the gas is greatest when the expansion is','[{"text": "adiabatic", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "isobaric", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "isothermal", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "equal in all above cases", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'The $p-V$ diagram for isobaric, isothermal and adiabatic process of an ideal gas is shown in graph below <img src=\'/img/data/physics/28_38640_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' /> In thermodynamics, for same change in volume, the work done is maximum for the curve having maximum area enclosed with the volume axis. Area enclosed by the curve $\\propto(\\text { Slope of curve })^{-1}$ <br>As shown<br>${({\\rm{ slope }})_{{\\rm{isobaric }}}} < {({\\rm{ slope }})_{{\\rm{isothermal }}}} < {({\\rm{ slope }})_{{\\rm{adiabatic }}}}$<br>$ \\Rightarrow {({\\rm{ Area }})_{{\\rm{isobaric }}}} > {({\\rm{ Area }})_{{\\rm{isothermal }}}} > {({\\rm{ Area }})_{{\\rm{adiabatic }}}}$<br>Hence, work done is maximum in isobaric process.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38641,'physics',29,1300,3145,'A vertical hollow cylinder of height \\[1.52\\,m\\] is fitted with a movable piston of negligible mass and thickness. The lower part of cylinder contains an ideal gas and the upper part is filled with mercury as shown in figure. Initially the temperature of system is \\[300\\,K\\] and the lengths of gas and mercury columns are equal. Find the temperature  to which system is raised so that half of mercury overflows. Take atmospheric pressure \\[76\\,cm\\] of \\[Hg\\] and neglect thermal expansion of mercury.<div class="matching-question"></div>','[{"text": "$237\\\\,K$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$337.5\\\\,K$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$527\\\\,K$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$437\\\\,K$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Final state of gas is shown in figure after half of mercury overflows. Let this temperature be ${T}$. Then from gas law for initial and final state of gas we have<br><img src=\'/img/data/physics/29_38641_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />  <br> $\\dfrac{P_{1} V_{1}}{T_{1}}=\\dfrac{P_{2} V_{2}}{T_{2}}$<br>Here initial pressure, volume and temperature of gas are<br>${P_1} = 76 + \\frac{h}{2} = 76 + 76 = 152\\,cm\\,\\,of\\,\\,Hg$<br>${V_1} = \\frac{h}{2}A = 76\\,A{\\text{ }}\\,{\\text{and}}\\,{\\text{ }}{T_1} = 300\\,K$<br>Similarly after heating pressure, volume and temperature of gas are<br>$\\begin{aligned}& P_{2}=76+\\dfrac{h}{4}=76+38=114 {~cm} \\text { of } {Hg} \\\\& V_{2}=\\dfrac{3 h}{4} A=114 A\\end{aligned}$<br>Thus from gas law, we have<br>$\\dfrac{152 \\times 76 {~A}}{300}=\\dfrac{114 \\times 114 {~A}}{T_{2}} \\text { or } T_{2}=337.5 {~K}$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/29_38641_question_1.png'),
(38644,'physics',28,1371,3282,'The internal energy of an ideal gas increases during an isothermal process when the gas is','[{"text": "expanded by adding more molecules to it", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "expanded by adding more heat to it", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "expanded against zero pressure", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "compressed by doing work on it", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Internal energy of an ideal gas is given by<br> $U=\\dfrac{f}{2} \\mu R T$<br>where, $f$ is degree of freedom.<br>$ = \\frac{f}{2}\\left( {\\frac{N}{{{N_A}}}} \\right)RT$<br>$ \\Rightarrow \\quad U \\propto NT$<br>In isothermal process, $T=$ constant<br>$\\Rightarrow \\quad U \\propto N$<br>i.e., internal energy increases by increasing number of molecules $N$.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38645,'physics',29,1304,3154,'Figure shows a horizontal cylindrical container of length $30\\,cm$, which is partitioned by a tight-fitting separator. The separator is diathermic but conducts heat very slowly. Initially the separator is in the state shown in figure. The temperature of left part of cylinder is $100\\,K$ and that on right part is $400\\,K$. Initially the separator is in equilibrium. As heat is conducted from right to left part, separator displaces to the right. Find  the  displacement of separator  when two gases on the parts of cylinder are in thermal equilibrium is','[{"text": "$5\\\\,cm$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$10\\\\,cm$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$20\\\\,cm$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$35\\\\,cm$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'It is given that initially the separator is in equilibrium thus pressure on both sides of gas are equal say, it is ${P i}$. If ${A}$ be the area of cross-section of cylinder, number of moles of gas in left and right part ${n_{1}}$ and ${n_{2}}$ can be given as<br>${n_{1}=\\dfrac{P_{i}(10 A)}{R(100)}}$ and ${n_{2}=\\dfrac{P_{i}(20 A)}{R(400)}}$<br>Finally if separator is displaced to right by a distance ${x}$, we have<br>${n_{1}=\\dfrac{P_{f}(10+x) A}{R T_{f}}}$ and ${n_{2}=\\dfrac{P_{f}(20-x) A}{R T_{f}}}$<br>If ${P_{f}}$ and ${T_{f}}$ be the final pressure and temperature on both sides after a long time.<br>Now if we equate the ratio of moles ${n_{1} / n_{2}}$ in initial and final state we set<br>$\\dfrac{n_{1}}{n_{2}}=\\dfrac{\\left(\\dfrac{10 A}{100}\\right)}{\\left(\\dfrac{20 A}{400}\\right)}=\\dfrac{(10+x) A}{(20-x) A}$<br>or    $2(20-x)=10+x$<br>or ${\\quad x=10 {~cm}}$<br>Thus in final state when gases in both parts are in thermal equilibrium, the piston is displaced to $10\\,cm$ right from its initial position.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/29_38645_question_1.png'),
(38646,'physics',27,1382,3318,'Two conducting cylinders are connected in series with each other. They are kept between two heat baths at temperatures ${T_1} = 200\\,K$ and ${T_2} = 400\\,K$ as shown in the figure. Radius of the smaller cylinder is half the radius of bigger one, whereas, both of them have same length $L.$ Thermal conductivities of the materials of the larger and the smaller cylinders are ${K_1}$ and ${K_2}$ respectively. In steady state, if temperature at the junction of the two cylinders is $300\\,K$ then $\\left( {\\frac{{{K_1}}}{{\\,{K_2}}}} \\right)$ will be','[{"text": "0.12", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "0.25", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "0.92", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "0.67", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Thermal resistances for the two cylinders can be given as, ${R_1} = \\frac{L}{{{K_1}{A_1}}}\\,$ and ${R_2} = \\frac{L}{{{K_2}{A_2}}}$ <img src=\'/img/data/physics/27_38646_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' /> As the cylinders are connected in series, the same amount of heat flows through both the cylinders.<br>$\\therefore \\frac{Q}{t} = \\frac{{\\Delta {\\theta _1}}}{{{R_1}}} = \\frac{{\\Delta {\\theta _2}}}{{{R_2}}}$<br>$\\therefore \\frac{{300 - 200}}{{{R_1}}} = \\frac{{400 - 300}}{{{R_2}}}$<br>$\\therefore \\quad {R_1} = {R_2}$<br>$\\therefore \\frac{L}{{{K_1}\\,{A_1}}} = \\frac{L}{{{K_2}\\,{A_2}}}$<br>$\\therefore \\quad \\frac{{{K_1}}}{{{K_2}}} = \\frac{{{A_2}}}{{{A_1}}} = \\frac{{{A_2}}}{{4{A_2}}}\\quad $<br>$\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\left( {\\because {r_2} = \\frac{{{r_1}}}{2} \\Rightarrow {A_1} = 4{A_2}} \\right)$<br>$\\therefore \\frac{{{K_1}}}{{\\,{K_2}}} = \\frac{1}{4} = 0.25$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/27_38646_question_1.png'),
(38647,'physics',26,1138,2727,'Read the Statement - A and Statement - B carefully to mark the correct option given below:<div class="matching-question"><div class="column-left">Viscosity of gases is greater than that of liquids.</div><div class="column-right">Surface tension of a liquid decreases due to the presence of insoluble impurities.</div></div>','[{"text": "Statement A is correct but Statement B is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Statement A is incorrect but Statement B is correct.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both statements are correct.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Statements are incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Viscosity represent resistance of flow & in liquids molecules are closely packed. So viscosity of liquids is higher than gases. Surface tension of liquid increases on adding soluble material as they make bond with solvent increasing density & thus increasing intermolecular attractions. But on adding insoluble impurities, surface tension of liquid decreases. So option (2) is right.',NULL,'Hard','MCQ type-2',2024,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38648,'physics',29,1302,3150,'The ratio of translational and rational kinetic energy at 100 $K$ temperature is 3:2. Find the internal energy of one mole gas at this temperature','[{"text": "$2075\\\\,J$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$3120\\\\,J$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$5128\\\\,J$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$7126\\\\,J$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'we use $\\frac{{{K_T}}}{{{K_R}}} = \\frac{{\\frac{3}{2}nRT}}{{\\frac{{(f - 3)}}{2}nRT}} = \\frac{3}{2} \\Rightarrow \\frac{3}{{f - 3}} = \\frac{3}{2}$<br>$ \\Rightarrow \\quad 6 = 3f - 9 \\Rightarrow f = 5$<br>Now $U=\\dfrac{f}{2} n R T=\\dfrac{5}{2} \\times 1 \\times 8.3 \\times 100=2075 {~J}$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38649,'physics',28,1377,3303,'At $27^\\circ C,$ a motor car tyre has pressure of  $2\\,atm.$ The temperature at which the tyre suddenly burst will be (take, $\\gamma_{\\text {air }}=1.4$ )','[{"text": "$246.1 \\\\mathrm{~K}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$250 K$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$290 K$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$248 K$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'When a system undergoes a sudden change under condition that no exchange of heat takes place between the system and surroundings, then such a process is called adiabatic. So, a tyre bursting suddenly an adiabatic process.<br>$p^{1-\\gamma} T^{\\gamma}=\\text { constant }$<br>where, $\\gamma$ is ratio of specific heats, $p$ is pressure and $T$ is temperature.<br>$\\therefore \\,\\,\\,\\,{\\left( {\\frac{{{p_2}}}{{{p_1}}}} \\right)^{\\gamma  - 1}} = {\\left( {\\frac{{{T_2}}}{{{T_1}}}} \\right)^\\gamma }$<br>$ \\Rightarrow \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{\\left( {\\frac{1}{2}} \\right)^{0.4}} = {\\left( {\\frac{{{T_2}}}{{300}}} \\right)^{1.4}}$<br>$\\therefore 0.4\\left[ {\\log 1 - \\log 2} \\right]$<br>$\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, = 1.4\\left[ {\\log {T_2} - \\log 300} \\right]$<br>$ \\Rightarrow \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{T_2} = 246.1\\,\\,\\,K$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38654,'physics',27,1382,3319,'Three rods of copper, brass and steel are welded together to form an inverted $Y$-shaped structure. End of copper rod is maintained at $100^\\circ C$ whereas ends of brass and steel are kept at $0^\\circ C.$ All the three rods have same length of $9\\,cm$ and cross section of $4.5\\,c{m^2}.$ The rods are thermally insulated from surroundings except at ends. Rate of heat flow through copper rod is <br>(Take thermal conductivities of copper, brass and steel to be $0.9,0.24$ and 0.11 $CGS$ units respectively.)','[{"text": "$12.6\\\\,calls$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$17.2\\\\,calls$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$15.5\\\\,calls$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$19.7\\\\,calls$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'<img src=\'/img/data/physics/27_38654_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />  $Q = {Q_1} + {Q_2}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,(1)$<br>But $Q = KA\\left( {\\frac{{\\Delta \\theta }}{{\\Delta x}}} \\right)t$<br> Here, for copper rod, $\\frac{Q}{t} = \\frac{{0.9 \\times 4.5 \\times (100 - T)}}{9}$<br>For brass and steel rods,$\\frac{{{Q_1} + {Q_2}}}{t} = \\frac{{\\left( \\begin{array}{l}\n[0.24 \\times 4.5 \\times (T - 0)] + \\\\\n[0.11 \\times 4.5 \\times (T - 0)]\n\\end{array} \\right)}}{9}$<br>From equation (1),<br>$0.9 \\times 4.5 \\times (100 - T) = \\left[ \\begin{array}{l}\n(0.24 \\times T) + \\\\\n(0.11 \\times T)\n\\end{array} \\right] \\times 4.5$<br>$\\therefore 0.9 \\times (100 - T) = 0.24 \\times T + 0.11 \\times T$<br>$\\therefore 90 - 0.9\\,T = 0.35\\,T$<br>$\\therefore 90 = 1.25\\,T$<br>$\\therefore T = 72^\\circ C$<br>$\\therefore \\frac{Q}{t} = \\frac{{0.9 \\times 4.5 \\times 28}}{9} = 12.6\\,\\,cal/s$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38659,'physics',26,1137,2723,'Eight equal drops of water are falling through air with a steady speed of $10\\,cm/s$. If the drops coalesce, the new velocity is','[{"text": "$10\\\\,cm/s$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$40\\\\,cm/s$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$16\\\\,cm/s$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$5\\\\,cm/s$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Terminal velocity, $v_{T}=\\dfrac{2 r^{2}(\\rho-\\sigma) g}{9 \\eta}$<br>i.e., $v_{T} \\propto r^{2} (\\because$ All other quantities are constant $)$<br>$\\Rightarrow \\dfrac{v_{T}}{v_{T}^{\\prime}}=\\dfrac{r^{2}}{R^{2}}$<br>As volume remains constant<br>$\\therefore$ Volume of the big drop $=8 \\times$ volume of each small drop<br>$\\Rightarrow \\dfrac{4}{3} \\pi R^{3}=8 \\times \\dfrac{4}{3} \\pi r^{3}$<br>$\\Rightarrow R=2 r$<br>$\\therefore \\dfrac{10}{v_{T}^{\\prime}}=\\dfrac{r^{2}}{(2 r)^{2}}=\\dfrac{r^{2}}{4 r^{2}}$<br>$v_T^\\prime  = 40\\,cm/s$<br>So, correct option is (2).',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2023,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38664,'physics',29,1304,3154,'Two closed containers of equal volume of air are initially at $1.05 \\times 10^{5} {~Pa}$, and 300 $K$ temperature. If the containers are connected by a narrow tube and one container is maintained at 300 $K$ temperature and other at 400 $K$ temperature. Find the final pressure in the containers.','[{"text": "$90\\\\,k\\\\,Pa$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$120\\\\,k\\\\,Pa$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$180\\\\,k\\\\,Pa$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$150\\\\,k\\\\,Pa$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Similar to previous example final pressure in both the containers remain same as they are connected. If initial pressure volume and temperatures of the two containers are denoted by $P_{0} V_{0}$ and $T_{0}$ and $P_{{f}}, V_{0} T_{2}$ and $P_{{f}} V_{0} T_{2}$ are the final pressure volume and temperature in the two containers then according to gas law and constant number of moles, we have$\\frac{{{P_0}\\left( {2{V_0}} \\right)}}{{{T_0}}} = \\frac{{{P_1}{V_0}}}{{{T_1}}} + \\frac{{{P_f}{V_0}}}{{{T_2}}}$<br>or $\\quad {P_f} = \\frac{{2{P_0}}}{{{T_0}}} \\times \\left( {\\frac{{{T_1}{T_2}}}{{{T_1} + {T_2}}}} \\right)$<br>Here it is given<br>${P_0} = 105\\,\\,kPa,{T_0} = 300\\,K,$<br>${T_1} = 300\\,K.$ ${T_2} = 400\\,K,$then we get<br>${P_f} = \\frac{{2 \\times 105 \\times {{10}^3} \\times 400}}{{300 + 400}}$<br>$ = 120\\,\\,kPa$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38666,'physics',27,1381,3315,'$\'M\'{\\rm{ }}kg$ of water at $80^\\circ C$ is divided into two parts so that one part of mass $\'m\'{\\rm{ }}kg$ when converted into ice at $0^\\circ C$ would release enough heat to vaporise the other part entirely, then $\\frac{m}{M}$ is equal to <br>(Take Specific heat of water $ = 1{\\mkern 1mu} \\,{\\rm{cal}}\\,{g^{ - 1}}\\,^\\circ {C^{ - 1}},$ Latent heat of fusion of ice $ = 80\\,{\\rm{cal}}\\,\\,{g^{ - 1}},$ Latent heat of steam $ = 540\\,{\\rm{cal}}\\,\\,{g^{ - 1}}$)','[{"text": "0.15", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "0.38", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "0.78", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "0.96", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Total mass of water $ = Mkg\\,$<br>mass converted to ice $ = m\\,kg$<br>$\\therefore $ mass converted to steam $ = (M - m)kg$<br>Heat lost during conversion of water to ice$=$ Heat gained during vaporisation <br>$\\therefore m{c_w}\\Delta {\\theta _i} + m{L_i} = (M - m){c_w}\\Delta {\\theta _s} + $<br>$\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,(M - m){L_s}$<br>$\\therefore m \\times 1 \\times (80 - 0) + m \\times (80)$<br>$ = (M - m) \\times 1 \\times (100 - 80) + (M - m)540$<br>$\\therefore \\quad 160\\,m = 20M - 20\\,m + 540M - 540\\,m$<br>$720\\,m = 560M$<br>$\\therefore \\quad \\frac{m}{M} = \\frac{{560}}{{720}} = \\frac{7}{9} = 0.78$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38669,'physics',28,1372,3294,'A cyclic Process of an ideal gas is shown in the figure. Let $W_{a \\rightarrow b}, W_{b \\rightarrow c}, W_{c \\rightarrow d}$ and $W_{d \\rightarrow a}$<br>represents the work done by the system during the processes $a \\rightarrow b, b \\rightarrow c, c \\rightarrow d$ and $d \\rightarrow a$ respectively. Consider the following relations<br> <br> I. $W_{a \\rightarrow b}+W_{b \\rightarrow c}+W_{c \\rightarrow d}+W_{d \\rightarrow a}>0$<br> II. $W_{a \\rightarrow b}+W_{b \\rightarrow c}+W_{c \\rightarrow a}+W_{d \\rightarrow a} < 0$<br>III. $W_{a \\rightarrow b}+W_{c \\rightarrow d}>0$<br>IV. $W_{b \\rightarrow c}+W_{d \\rightarrow a}=0 \\quad$<br> Which of the above relations is/are true?','[{"text": "I and III", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "III and IV", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "I, III and IV", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "II, III and IV", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'In Carnot cycle, in order to obtain continuous supply of work, net work done<br>As, $W_{a \\rightarrow b}$ is isothermal<br>$W_{b \\rightarrow c}$ is adiabatic<br>$W_{c \\rightarrow d}$ is isothermal (negative)<br>$W_{d \\rightarrow a}$ is adiabatic (negative)<br>Net work done during the complete cycle,<br>$W = {W_{a \\to b}} + {W_{b \\to c}} + \\left( { - {W_{c \\to d}}} \\right) + \\left( { - {W_{d \\to a}}} \\right)$<br>$W=W_{a \\rightarrow b}-W_{c \\rightarrow d}$<br>$\\left[\\operatorname{as} W_{b \\rightarrow c}=W_{d \\rightarrow a}\\right]$<br>i.e. $\\quad W_{a \\rightarrow b}+W_{c \\rightarrow d}>0$<br>${W_{a \\to b}} + {W_{b \\to c}} + {W_{c \\to d}} + {W_{d \\to a}} > 0$<br>$\\,\\,\\,\\,{W_{b \\to c}} = {W_{d \\to a}}$<br>$\\therefore \\,\\,\\,\\,{W_{b \\to c}} + {W_{d \\to a}} = 0$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/28_38669_question_1.png'),
(48062,'biology',10,260,514,'Plasma membrane is-','[{"text": "Semipermeable", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Selective / differentially permeable", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Permeable", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Non-permeable", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Plasma membrane is selectively permeable to ions and organic molecules and regulates the movement of substance in out of the cell.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38671,'physics',26,1136,2715,'The figure shows a liquid of given density flowing steadily in horizontal tube of varying cross- section. Cross sectional areas at $A$ is $1.5\\,c{m^2}$, and $B$ is $25\\,m{m^2}$, if the speed of liquid at $B$ is $60\\,cm/s$ then $\\left( {{P_A} - {P_B}} \\right)$ is (Given: ${{P_A}}$ and ${{P_B}}$ are liquid pressures at $A$ and $B$ points. Density $\\rho = 1000\\,kg\\,{m^{ - 3}},A$ and $B$ are on the axis of tube)','[{"text": "$36\\\\,Pa$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$27\\\\,Pa$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$135\\\\,Pa$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$175\\\\,Pa$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'In the given situation $A$ and $B$ lie on the same horizontal level. Now, applying Bernoulli\'s equation<br>${P_A} - {P_B} = \\frac{1}{2}\\rho \\left( {v_B^2 - v_A^2} \\right)\\,\\,\\,{\\mkern 1mu} {\\kern 1pt} \\left( {{h_A} = {h_B}} \\right){\\rm{ }}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\left( 1 \\right)$<br>Also, from equation of continuity, we have<br>$S_{A} v_{A}=S_{B} v_{B}$<br>where $S_{A}$ and $S_{B}$ are corresponding cross - sectional area. Given,<br>${S_A} = 1.5\\,c{m^2}$<br>${S_B} = 25\\,m{m^2} = 25 \\times {10^{ - 2}}\\,c{m^2}$,<br>${v_B} = 60\\,cm/s$<br>$\\therefore \\quad(1.5)\\left(v_{A}\\right)=25 \\times 10^{-2} \\times 60$<br>$ \\Rightarrow {v_A} = \\frac{{25}}{{1.5}} \\times {10^{ - 2}} \\times 60\\,cm/s$<br>$ = 10\\,cm/s$<br>Now, from eq. (1)<br>$P_{A}-P_{B}=\\dfrac{1}{2} \\times 10^{3}\\left(60^{2}-10^{2}\\right) \\times 10^{-4}$<br>$ = 175\\,Pa$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2023,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/26_38671_question_1.png'),
(38672,'physics',29,1306,3156,'The core of the sun is a plasma. It is at a temperature of The order of $10^{7} {~K}$ and contains equal number of protons the electrons. The density of the core of the Sun is $10^{5} {~kg} {~m}^{-3}$ Assume that molar mass of proton is $1 {~g} {~mol}^{-1}$ and the mass of an electron to be negligible compared to the mass of a proton. Estimate the pressure  at the core of the sun. Assume that plasma behaves like an ideal gas.','[{"text": "$3.57 \\\\times {10^{16}}\\\\,Pa$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2.68 \\\\times {10^{16}}\\\\,Pa$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1.66 \\\\times {10^{16}}\\\\,Pa$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$9.35 \\\\times {10^{16}}\\\\,Pa$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'$1 {~m}^{3}$ volume of matter has a mass of $10^{5} {~kg}$ and is mostly due to protons.<br>$n p=$ number of moles of proton in $1 {~m}^{3}$ volume.$=\\dfrac{\\text { Mass of protons }}{\\text { Molar mass of proton }}=\\dfrac{10^{5}}{10^{-3}}=10^{8} {~mol}$<br>The matter has equal number of electrons. Hence total count of particles is<br>$2{n_P} = 2 \\times {10^8}\\,{\\rm{mol}}$<br>$\\therefore \\quad P = \\frac{{nRT}}{V} = \\frac{{2 \\times {{10}^8} \\times 8.3 \\times {{10}^7}}}{1}$<br>$ = 1.66 \\times {10^{16}}\\,Pa$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38675,'physics',26,1137,2723,'Read the Assertion and Reason carefully to mark the correct options given below:<br><br><strong>Assertion:</strong> A spherical body of radius $(5 \\pm 0.1)mm$ having a particular density is falling through a liquid of constant density. The percentage error in the calculation of its terminal velocity is $4 \\%$.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> The terminal velocity of the spherical  body falling through the liquid is inversely  proportional to its radius.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'The expression for terminal velocity is given by,<br>${v_T} = \\frac{2}{9}\\frac{{{r^2}}}{\\eta }\\left( {\\rho  - {\\rho _0}} \\right)g\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\left( 1 \\right)$<br>Where, $r \\rightarrow$ radius of particle.<br>$\\rho \\rightarrow$ density of particle<br>$\\rho_{0} \\rightarrow$ density of fluid<br>$\\eta \\rightarrow$ coefficient of viscosity<br>Percentage error in terminal velocity is,<br>$\\dfrac{\\Delta v}{v} \\times 100=\\dfrac{2 \\Delta r}{r} \\times 100=\\dfrac{2(0.1)}{5} \\times 100=4 \\%$<br>From equation (1) $v_{T} \\propto r^{2}$<br>So, reason statement is incorrect.<br>Hence, Assertion is true but Reason is false.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',2023,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38677,'physics',28,1377,3303,'Choose the incorrect statement from the following.<br>S1 : The efficiency of a heat engine can be 1 , but the coefficient of performance of a refrigerator can never be infinity.<br>S2 : The first law of thermodynamics is basically the principle of conservation of energy.<br>S3 : The second law of thermodynamics does not allow several phenomena consistent with the first law.<br>S4 : A process, whose sole result is the transfer of heat from a colder object to a hotter object is impossible.','[{"text": "${\\\\rm{S 1}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$S 3$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$S 2$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$S 4$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Efficency of heat engine,<br>$\\eta=1-\\dfrac{T_{2}}{T_{1}}$<br>For  $(\\eta  = 1)\\quad \\quad ({\\theta _1} = \\infty )$<br>$\\dfrac{T_{2}}{T_{1}}$ should zero that is either, ${T_2} = 0\\,K$<br>or $T_{1}=\\infty K$<br>As source at infinite temperature or sink at $0 K$ is not attainable.<br>Therefore, efficiency of heat engine is always less than 1 .',NULL,'Hard','MCQ type-2',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38697,'physics',28,1371,3286,'An ideal gas with adiabatic exponent $(\\gamma=1.5)$ undergoes a process in which work done by the gas is same as increase in internal energy of the gas. The molar heat capacity of gas for the process is -','[{"text": "$C = 4\\\\,R$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$C = 0$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$C = 2\\\\,R$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$C = R$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'$\\int P dV = \\int {n{C_v}} dt$<br>$ \\Rightarrow dQ = 2dU \\Rightarrow nCdT = 2n{C_v}dt$<br>$ \\Rightarrow C = 2{C_v}$<br>$ \\Rightarrow \\,\\,\\,{\\mkern 1mu} {\\kern 1pt} C = \\frac{{2R}}{{1.5 - 1}} = 4R$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38683,'physics',26,1139,2734,'Match Column I with Column II. Choose the correct answer from the options given below.<div class="matching-question"><div class="column-left">A. Surface tension<br>B. Pressure<br>C. Viscosity<br>D. Impulse</div><div class="column-right">P. $kg\\,{m^{ - 1}}{s^{ - 2}}$<br>Q. $kg\\,m\\,{s^{ - 1}}$<br>R. $kg\\,{m^{ - 1}}{s^{ - 2}}$<br>S. $kg\\,{s^{ - 2}}$</div></div>','[{"text": "${\\\\rm{A  -  R}},\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\rm{B  -  S}},\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\rm{C  -  P}},\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\rm{D  -  Q}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{A  -  S}},\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\rm{B  -  R}},\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\rm{C  -  Q}},\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\rm{D  -  P}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{A  -  S}},\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\rm{B  -  R}},\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\rm{C  -  P}},\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\rm{D  -  Q}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{A  -  Q}},\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\rm{B  -  P}},\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\rm{C  -  R}},\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\rm{D  -  S}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Dimensions of surface tension,<br>$S = \\frac{F}{l} = \\left[ {M{L^0}{T^{ - 2}}} \\right]kg/{s^2}$<br>Dimensions of pressure,<br>$P = \\frac{F}{A} = \\left[ {M{L^{ - 1}}{T^{ - 2}}} \\right] = kg/m{s^2}$<br>Dimensions of viscosity,<br>$F = 6\\pi \\eta rv$<br>$\\eta  = \\frac{F}{{6\\pi r\\nu }} = \\left[ {M{L^{ - 1}}{T^{ - 1}}} \\right] = kg/m{\\rm{ - }}s$<br>Dimensions of impulse,<br>$I = Ft = \\left[ {M{L^1}{T^{ - 1}}} \\right] = kgm/s$<br>So, ${\\rm{A  -  S}},\\,\\,\\,{\\rm{B  -  R}},\\,\\,\\,{\\rm{C  -  P}},\\,\\,\\,{\\rm{D  -  Q}}$',NULL,'Medium','Match the following',2023,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38690,'physics',27,1380,3306,'Two rods $A$ and $B$ of identical dimensions are at temperature $40^\\circ C.$ If  $A$ is heated upto $160^\\circ C$ and $B$  upto $T^\\circ C$ then new lengths are the same. If the ratio of the coefficients of linear expansion of $B$ and $A$ is $2: 5$, then  find  the value of  $T$ is','[{"text": "$265^\\\\circ C$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$435^\\\\circ C$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$340^\\\\circ C$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$570^\\\\circ C$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Let initial length of identical rods be ${l_0}$. Thermal expansion in length,<br>$\\Delta l = {l_0}\\alpha (\\Delta T) = {l_0}\\alpha \\left( {{T_2} - {T_1}} \\right)$<br>So, change in length of rods are,<br>$\\Delta {l_A} = {l_0}{\\alpha _A}(160 - 40)$<br>$\\Delta {l_B} = {l_0}{\\alpha _B}(T - 40)$<br>Since new lengths are same,<br>$\\therefore \\Delta {l_A} = \\Delta {l_B}$<br>$\\therefore {l_0}{\\alpha _A} \\times 120 = {l_0}{\\alpha _B}(T - 40)$<br>$\\therefore \\frac{{{\\alpha _A}}}{{{\\alpha _B}}} = \\frac{{(T - 40)}}{{120}}$<br>$\\frac{{T - 40}}{{120}} = \\frac{5}{2}$<br>$T - 40 = \\frac{5}{2} \\times 120 = 300$<br>$T = 300 + 40 = 340^\\circ C$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38691,'physics',26,1138,2728,'Surface tension of a soap bubble is $2.0 \\times {10^{ - 2}}N{m^{ - 1}}$. Work done to increase the radius of soap bubble from $3.5\\,cm$ to $7\\,cm$ will be<br>Take $\\left[\\pi=\\dfrac{22}{7}\\right]$','[{"text": "$0.72 \\\\times {10^{ - 4}}\\\\,J$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$18.48 \\\\times {10^{ - 4}}\\\\,J$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$9.24 \\\\times {10^{ - 4}}\\\\,J$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$5.76 \\\\times {10^{ - 4}}\\\\,J$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Surface tension of a soap bubble,<br>$T = 2 \\times {10^{ - 2}}N{m^{ - 1}}$<br>Work done to increase the radius of soap bubble from $3.5\\,cm$ to $7\\,cm$.<br>$W=$ Change in surface energy<br>$=$ Surface Tension $\\left(A_{f}-A_{i}\\right)$<br>$W=2 T(\\Delta A)$<br>$W=2 T\\left(4 \\pi R_{2}^{2}-4 \\pi R_{1}^{2}\\right)$<br>$W=2 \\times 2 \\times 10^{-2} \\times 4 \\pi\\left[\\begin{array}{c}\\left(7 \\times 10^{-2}\\right)^{2} \\\\ -\\left(3.5 \\times 10^{-2}\\right)^{2}\\end{array}\\right]$<br>$ \\Rightarrow W \\approx 18.48 \\times {10^{ - 4}}\\,J$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2023,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38694,'physics',28,1371,3282,'In the equation $P{V^\\gamma } = $constant, the value of $\\gamma$ is unity. Then the process is','[{"text": "isothermal", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "adiabatic", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "isobaric", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "irreversible", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'$PV = $ constant represents isothermal process.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38695,'physics',26,1136,2716,'A fully loaded boeing aircraft has a mass $5.4 \\times {10^5}\\,kg$. Its total wing area is $500\\,{m^2}$. It is in level flight with a speed of $1080\\,km/h$. If the density of air $\\rho$ is $1.2\\,kg\\,{m^{ - 3}}$, the fractional increase in the speed of the air on the upper surface of the wing relative to the lower surface in percentage will be $\\left( {g = 10\\,m/{s^2}} \\right)$','[{"text": "10", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "16", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "6", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "8", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Here : $m = 5.4 \\times {10^5}\\,kg,A = 500\\,{m^2}$<br>$v = 1080\\,km/hr = 300\\,m/s,\\rho  = 1.2\\,kg/{m^3}$<br>$g = 10\\,m/{s^2},{P_2} - {P_1} = mg/A$<br>According to Bernoulli\'s theorem,<br>$P_{1}+\\dfrac{1}{2} \\rho v_{1}^{2}+\\rho g h_{1}=P_{2}+\\dfrac{1}{2} \\rho v_{2}^{2}+\\rho g h_{2}$<br>$P_{2}-P_{1}=\\dfrac{1}{2} \\rho\\left(v_{1}^{2}-v_{2}^{2}\\right) \\quad\\left(\\because h_{1}=h_{2}\\right)$<br>$P_{2}-P_{2}=\\dfrac{1}{2} \\rho\\left(v_{1}+v_{2}\\right)\\left(v_{1}-v_{2}\\right)$<br>Since, $v=\\dfrac{v_{1}+v_{2}}{2}$or $v_{1}+v_{2}=2 v$;<br>$P_{2}-P_{1}=\\dfrac{1}{2} \\rho\\left(v_{1}-v_{2}\\right) 2 v$<br>$ \\Rightarrow \\frac{{5.4 \\times {{10}^5} \\times 10}}{{500}}$<br>$ = \\frac{1}{2} \\times 1.2\\left( {{v_1} - {v_2}} \\right) \\times 2 \\times 300$<br>$\\Rightarrow v_{1}-v_{2}=30$<br>$\\Rightarrow 100\\left(\\dfrac{v_{1}-v_{2}}{v}\\right)=\\dfrac{30}{300} \\times 100=10 \\%$<br>So, correct option is (1).',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2023,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38703,'physics',26,1138,2731,'The height of liquid column raised in a capillary tube of certain radius when dipped in liquid \\(A\\) vertically is, \\[5\\;cm\\]. If the tube is dipped in a similar manner in another liquid \\(B\\) of surface tension and density double the values of liquid \\(A\\), the height of liquid column raised in liquid \\(B\\) would be ____ \\(m\\).<div class="matching-question"></div>','[{"text": "0.20", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "0.5", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "0.10", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "0.05", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'$h=\\dfrac{2 T \\cos \\theta}{R \\rho g}$<br>$\\therefore h \\propto \\dfrac{T}{P}$<br>So, on increasing the surface tension $T$ and density $\\rho$ by same factor 2 (by introducing another liquid) there is no change in height of liquid raised. So, correct option is (4).',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2023,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38707,'physics',26,1133,2705,'Read the Assertion and Reason carefully to mark the correct options given below:<br><br><strong>Assertion:</strong> When you squeeze one end of a tube to get toothpaste out from the other end, Pascal\'s principle is observed.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> A change in the pressure applied to  an enclosed incompressible fluid is transmitted  undiminished to every portion of the fluid and to  the walls of its container.','[{"text": "Assertion is not correct but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct ans Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is not correct.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'According to Pascal\'s law, pressure applied to an enclosed fluid, will transmitted without a change in magnitude to every point of the fluid and also to the walls of container. Due to that when any part of tooth paste\'s tube is squeezed, toothpaste squirts out and pressure applied is equally transmitted throughout the tube and when pressure reaches the open end, it forces toothpaste out through the opening. So, its one of the application of Pascal\'s law. Correct option is (2).',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',2023,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38709,'physics',28,1367,3277,'Two systems are in thermal equilibrium. The quantity which is common for them is','[{"text": "heat", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "momentum", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "specific heat", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "temperature", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Two systems are said to be in thermal equilibrium if they are at same temperature. It means there is no heat flow between these two system whatever the amount of heat a system contains.<br>So correct option is (4)',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38710,'physics',27,1381,3315,'Oil at $25^\\circ C$ is poured very slowly into a calorimeter that is at temperature of $120^\\circ C.$ The boiling point of the oil is $80^\\circ C.$ It is found that the first $5\\,g$ of the oil completely evaporates. After pouring another  $75\\,g$ of the oil the equilibrium temperature is found to be $40^\\circ C.$ The ratio of the latent heat of the oil to its speific heat will be<br>(Neglect the heat exchange with surrounding)','[{"text": "$170^\\\\circ C$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$250^\\\\circ C$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$193^\\\\circ C$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$150^\\\\circ C$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'First $5\\,g$ of the oil at $25^\\circ C$ is poured to calorimeter at $120^\\circ C$.  As the oil completely evaporates the calorimeter must have attained the temperature $ = 80^\\circ C$<br>${m_c}{c_c}\\Delta {T_c} = {m_o}{c_o}\\Delta {T_o} + {m_0}\\,L$.<br>$\\therefore {m_c} \\times {c_c} \\times (120 - 80)$<br>$ = 5 \\times {c_o} \\times (80 - 25) + 5\\,L$<br>$\\therefore {m_c}{c_c} \\times 40 = 275\\,{c_o} + 5\\,L$$\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,(1)$<br>Now, $75\\,g$ of liquid at $25^\\circ C$ is poured into calorimeter at $80^\\circ C$ the equilibrium temperature reaches to $40^\\circ C$.<br>$\\therefore {m_c} \\times {c_c} \\times (80 - 40) = 75 \\times {c_o} \\times (40 - 25)$<br>$\\therefore {m_c}{c_c} \\times 40 = 1125\\,{c_o}$$\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,(2)$<br>From equations (1)  and (2),<br>$275{c_o} + 5\\,L = 1125\\,{c_o}$<br>$\\therefore 5\\,L = 850\\,{c_o}$<br>$\\therefore \\frac{L}{{{c_o}}} = \\frac{{850}}{5} = 170^\\circ C$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38711,'physics',26,1133,2705,'An air bubble of volume $1\\,c{m^3}$ rises from the bottom of a lake $40\\,m$ deep to the surface at a temperature of $12^\\circ C$. The atmospheric pressure is $1 \\times {10^5}\\,Pa$, the density of water is $1000\\,kg/{m^3}$ and $g = 10\\,m/{s^2}$. There is no difference in the temperature of water at the depth of $40\\,m$ and on the surface. The volume of air bubble when it reaches the surface will be','[{"text": "$4\\\\,c{m^3}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$3\\\\,c{m^3}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$5\\\\,c{m^3}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$2\\\\,c{m^3}$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Given, volume of bubble at depth of $40\\,m = 1\\,c{m^3}$<br>Depth of lake,<br>$d = 40\\,m,T = 12^\\circ C$<br>${P_1} = 1 \\times {10^5}\\,Pa$;<br>Density of water $ = 1000\\,kg/{m^3};g = 10\\,m/{s^2}$ As temperature is constant at depth and on the surface, $P V=$ constant<br>$\\therefore P_{1} V_{1}=P_{2} V_{2}$<br>$P_{1} V_{1}=\\left(P_{1}+h \\rho g\\right) V_{2}$<br>$1 \\times {10^5} \\times {V_1} = \\left( \\begin{array}{l}\n1 \\times {10^5} + 40 \\times \\\\\n1000 \\times 10\n\\end{array} \\right) \\times {10^{ - 6}}$<br>$V_{1}=\\dfrac{5 \\times 10^{5} \\times 10^{-6}}{1 \\times 10^{5}}$<br>${V_1} = 5 \\times {10^{ - 6}}\\,{m^3} = 5\\,c{m^3}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2023,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38712,'physics',29,1304,3154,'A vessel of volume 3 $V$ contains a gas at pressure $4 P_{0}$ and another vessel of volume $2 V$ contains the same gas at pressure $1.5 P_{0}$. Both vessels have same temperature. When both vessels are connected by a tube of negligible volume, the equilibrium is pressure $I P_{0}$, where $I$ is an integer. Find the value of $I$.','[{"text": "2", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "1", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "6", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "3", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'No change of temperature<br>So, $\\left(n_{1}+n_{2}\\right)_{i}=\\left(n_{1}+n_{2}\\right)_{f}$<br>$\\dfrac{P_{1} V_{1}}{R T}+\\dfrac{P_{2} V_{2}}{R T}=\\dfrac{P V_{1}}{R T}+\\dfrac{P V_{2}}{R T}$<br>So, $\\quad P = \\frac{{{P_1}{V_1} + {P_2}{V_2}}}{{{V_1} + {V_2}}}$<br>$ = \\frac{{\\left( {4{P_0}} \\right)(3V) + \\left( {1.5{P_0}} \\right)(2V)}}{{5V}}$<br>$ = 3{P_0} = I{P_0}\\quad {\\rm{ (given) }}$<br>So, $I=3$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38716,'physics',29,1304,3154,'The two conducting cylinder-piston systems shown below are linked. Cylinder 1 is filled with a certain molar quantity of a monatomic ideal gas, and cylinder 2 is filled with an equal molar quantity of a diatomic ideal gas. The entire apparatus is situated inside an oven whose temperature is $T_{a}=27^{\\circ} {C}$. The cylinder volumes have the same initial value $V_{0}=100 {cc}$. When the oven temperature is slowly raised to $T_{b}=127^{\\circ} {C}$. What is the volume change $\\Delta V$  of cylinder 1 ?','[{"text": "1", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "5", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "0", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "3", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'$P_{0} V_{0}=n R T_{a}$<br>$\\begin{aligned}& \\left(P_{0}+\\Delta P\\right)\\left(V_{0}+\\Delta V\\right)=n R T_{b} \\\\& \\left(P_{0}+\\Delta P\\right)\\left(V_{0}-\\Delta V\\right)=n R T_{b} \\\\& \\Delta V=0\\end{aligned}$<br>Pressure depends on number of moles and is independent of nature of gas.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/29_38716_question_1.png'),
(38717,'physics',27,1381,3314,'A lead bullet at $277^\\circ C$ just melts when stopped by an obstacle. Assuming that $25 \\%$ of heat is absorbed by the obstacle, the minimum velocity the bullet should possess at the time of striking is <br>(Take $M.P.$ of lead $ = 327^\\circ C,$ ${c_{lead{\\rm{ }}}} = 0.03\\,{\\rm{cal/}}g^\\circ C,$ latent heat of fusion of lead $ = 5.5\\,{\\rm{cal/}}g,1\\,{\\rm{cal}} = 4.20\\,J$)','[{"text": "$165\\\\,m/s$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$280\\\\,\\\\,m/s$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$240\\\\,m/s$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$350\\\\,m/s$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Let mass of the bullet be  $\'m\'g$. Total heat required to melt the bullet is,<br>${Q_I} = mcl\\,\\Delta \\,\\theta  + mL$<br>$ = [m \\times 0.03 \\times (327 - 277)] + (m \\times 5.5)$<br>$ = (1.5 \\times m) + (m \\times 5.5)cal = (7\\,m \\times 4.20)J$<br>Loss in mechanical energy of the bullet due to obstacle $ = \\frac{1}{2}\\left( {\\,m \\times {{10}^{ - 3}}} \\right){v^2}\\,J$<br>Now, $25 \\%$ of this energy is absorbed by the obstacle.<br>$\\therefore$ Energy absorbed by the bullet,<br>${Q_2} = \\frac{{75}}{{100}} \\times \\frac{1}{2}m{v^2} \\times {10^{ - 3}} = \\frac{3}{8}m{v^2} \\times {10^{ - 3}}\\,J$<br>The bullet will melt if ${Q_2} \\ge {Q_1}$<br>$\\therefore \\quad \\frac{3}{8}mv_{\\min {\\rm{ }}}^2 \\times {10^{ - 3}} = 7\\,m \\times 4.20$<br>$\\therefore \\quad v_{\\min {\\rm{ }}}^2 = \\frac{{7\\,m \\times 4.20}}{{\\frac{3}{8}\\,m \\times {{10}^{ - 3}}}} = 78400$<br>$\\therefore \\quad v = 280\\,m/s$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38719,'physics',26,1133,2708,'A hydraulic automobile lift is designed to lift vehicles of mass $5000\\,kg$. The area of cross - section of the cylinder carrying the load is $250\\,c{m^2}$. The maximum pressure the smaller piston would have to bear is [Assume $g = 10\\,m/{s^2}$].','[{"text": "$2 \\\\times {10^{ + 6}}\\\\,Pa$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$2 \\\\times {10^{ + 5}}\\\\,Pa$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$20 \\\\times {10^{ + 6}}\\\\,Pa$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$200 \\\\times {10^{ + 6}}\\\\,Pa$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Pressure, $P=\\dfrac{F}{A}$<br>Force, $F = mg = 5000 \\times 10 = 50000\\,N$<br>Area, $A = 250\\,c{m^2} = 250 \\times {10^{ - 4}}\\,{m^2}$<br>$\\therefore$ Pressure on the piston is given by Pressure, $P=\\dfrac{50000}{250 \\times 10^{-4}}$<br>$ = 200 \\times {10^4} = 2 \\times {10^6}\\,Pa$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2023,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38724,'physics',29,1300,3145,'${\\quad P-T}$ diagram of an ideal gas having three different densities ${\\rho_{1}, \\rho_{2}, \\rho_{3}}$ (in three different cases) is shown in the figure. Which of the following is correct?','[{"text": "${\\\\rho_{1}>\\\\rho_{2}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${\\\\rho_{2} < \\\\rho_{3}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rho_{1} < \\\\rho_{2}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rho_{1}=\\\\rho_{2}=\\\\rho_{3}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'By ideal gas equation.<br>${P V=n R T}$<br>${P=\\dfrac{m}{M \\cdot V} R T=\\dfrac{m}{V} \\cdot \\dfrac{R T}{M}=\\rho \\dfrac{R T}{M}}$<br>${\\therefore}$ Slope, ${\\dfrac{P}{T} \\propto \\rho}$<br><img src=\'/img/data/physics/29_38724_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />  <br>( ${m}$ is mass of gas and ${M}$ is molecular mass of gas). For same temperature ${P \\propto \\rho}$<br>As, ${P_{1}>P_{2}>P_{3} \\Rightarrow \\rho_{1}>\\rho_{2}>\\rho_{3}}$<br>So, correct option is (1).',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2024,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/29_38724_question_1.png'),
(38726,'physics',28,1371,3283,'When there is no heat change from surroundings in a system, then the process taking place is','[{"text": "isobaric", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "isochoric", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "adiabatic", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "isothermal", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'The process in which no heat is transferred to or from the system, during the whole process, is called adiabatic process.<br>So correct option is (3)',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38737,'physics',28,1371,3282,'Two identical containers ${A}$ and ${B}$ with frictionless pistons contain the same ideal gas at the same volume ${V}$, pressure ${P}$ and temperature ${T}$. A has mass ${m_{A}}$ of gas and ${B}$ has mass ${m_{B}}$. The gas in both cylinders is allowed to expand isothermally to same final volume 2 $V$ . The final pressure in ${A}$ and ${B}$ are found to be ${P}$ and 1.5 ${P}$ respectively. Then','[{"text": "$4\\\\,{m_A} = 9\\\\,{m_B}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2\\\\,{m_A} = 3\\\\,{m_B}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$3\\\\,{m_A} = 2\\\\,{m_B}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$9\\\\,{m_A} = 4\\\\,{m_B}$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'For container $A,\\frac{{{P_A}{V_A}}}{{{n_A}}} = RT\\,\\,\\,\\,(1)$<br>For container $B,\\frac{{{P_B}{V_B}}}{{{n_B}}} = RT\\,\\,\\,\\,(2)$<br>From eqns. (1) and (2),<br>$\\frac{{{P_A}}}{{{P_B}}} = \\frac{{{n_A}}}{{{n_B}}} = \\frac{{{m_A}}}{{{m_B}}}\\quad \\quad \\left( {\\because {V_A} = {V_B}} \\right).$<br>$\\therefore \\frac{{{m_A}}}{{{m_B}}} = \\frac{2}{3} \\Rightarrow 3\\,{m_A} = 2\\,{m_B}$<br>So correct option is (3)',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38727,'physics',26,1138,2728,'A mercury drop of radius ${10^{ - 3}}\\,m$ is broken into 125 equal size droplets. Surface tension of mercury is $0.45\\,N{m^{ - 1}}$. The gain in surface energy is','[{"text": "$17.5 \\\\times {10^{ - 5}}\\\\,J$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$28 \\\\times {10^{ - 5}}\\\\,J$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2.26 \\\\times {10^{ - 5}}\\\\,J$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$5 \\\\times {10^{ - 5}}\\\\,J$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Radius of bigger drop, $R = {10^{ - 3}}\\,m$<br>Number of drops, $n=125$<br>Surface tension, $T = 0.45\\,N/m$<br>Let the radius of smaller drop is $r$.<br>Volume of bigger drop $=$ Sum of volume of smaller drops<br>So, $\\dfrac{4}{3} \\pi R^{3}=n \\times \\dfrac{4}{3} \\pi r^{3} \\Rightarrow\\left(10^{-3}\\right)^{3}=125 r^{3}$<br>${\\left( {\\frac{{{{10}^{ - 3}}}}{5}} \\right)^3} = {r^3} \\Rightarrow r = \\frac{1}{5} \\times {10^{ - 3}}\\,m$<br>Initial surface energy, $U_{i}=T \\times 4 \\pi R^{2}$<br>Final surface energy, $U_{f}=n \\times T \\times 4 \\pi r^{2}$<br>$\\Delta U=U_{f}-U_{i}=T \\times 4 \\pi\\left[n r^{2}-R^{2}\\right]$<br>$=0.45 \\times 4 \\times 3.14\\left[125 \\times \\dfrac{10^{-6}}{25}-10^{-6}\\right]$<br>$ = 2.26 \\times {10^{ - 5}}\\,J$<br>So, correct option is (3).',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2023,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38728,'physics',29,1301,3148,'A sample contains mixture of helium and oxygen gas. The ratio of root mean square speed of helium and oxygen in the sample, is','[{"text": "${\\\\dfrac{1}{32}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\dfrac{1}{2 \\\\sqrt{2}}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\dfrac{1}{4}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\dfrac{2 \\\\sqrt{2}}{1}}$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'We know that, ${M_{O_{2}}=32, M_{H e}=4}$<br>${R M S}$ velocity of a gas is given by<br>${v_{r m s}=\\sqrt{\\dfrac{3 R T}{M}} \\Rightarrow v_{r m s} \\propto \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{m}}}$<br>${\\Rightarrow \\dfrac{v_{{He}}}{v_{O_{2}}}=\\sqrt{\\dfrac{M_{O_{2}}}{M_{{He}}}}=\\sqrt{\\dfrac{32}{4}}=\\dfrac{2 \\sqrt{2}}{1}}$<br>So, correct option is (4).',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2024,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38732,'physics',29,1301,3148,'If the root mean square velocity of hydrogen molecule at a given temperature and pressure is ${2 {~km} / {s}}$, the root mean square velocity of oxygen at the same condition in ${{km} / {s}}$ is','[{"text": "1.0", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "1.5", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "2.0", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "0.5", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'$RMS$ velocity of gas particles is given by<br>$\\begin{gathered}v_{r m s}=\\sqrt{\\dfrac{3 R T}{M}} \\Rightarrow v_{r m s} \\propto \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{M}} \\\\\\Rightarrow \\dfrac{v_{O_{2}}}{v_{O_{2}}}=\\sqrt{\\dfrac{M_{H_{2}}}{M_{O_{2}}}} \\Rightarrow \\dfrac{V_{O_{2}}}{2}=\\sqrt{\\dfrac{2}{32}}\\end{gathered}$<br>${\\Rightarrow V_{{O}_{2}}=2 \\times \\dfrac{1}{4}=0.5 {~km} / {s}}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2024,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38733,'physics',27,1381,3314,'Two identical rods of copper and iron are coated with wax uniformly. When one end of each is kept at temperature of boiling water, the length upto which wax melts are $7.2\\,cm$ and $3.6\\,cm$ respectively. If thermal conductivity of copper is $0.92,$ then what is the thermal conductivity of iron?','[{"text": "0.23", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "0.57", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "0.95", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "0.62", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'$\\frac{{{K_1}}}{{{K_2}}} = \\frac{{l_1^2}}{{l_2^2}}$<br>$\\therefore \\,\\,\\,{K_2} = \\frac{{{K_1}l_2^2}}{{l_1^2}}$<br>$ = \\frac{{0.92 \\times {{(3.6)}^2}}}{{{{(7.2)}^2}}} = \\frac{{0.92}}{4} = 0.23$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38734,'physics',28,1369,3279,'First law of thermodynamics is a special case of','[{"text": "Law of conservation of energy", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Boyle\'s law", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Charle\'s law", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Gay Lussac\'s law", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'According the first law of thermodynamics,$d Q=d U+d W$<br>This equation is the most general statement of the law of conservation of energy.<br>So correct option is (1)',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38735,'physics',26,1133,2705,'Read the Statement - A and Statement - B carefully to mark the correct options given below:<div class="matching-question"><div class="column-left">Pressure in a reservoir of water is same at all points at the same level of water.</div><div class="column-right">The pressure applied to enclosed  water is transmitted in all directions equally.</div></div>','[{"text": "Both Statement A and Statement B is correct.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Statement A is incorrect but Statement B is correct.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Statement A and Statement B is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Statement A is correct but Statement B is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Pressure is same at all points at the same level in a reservoir because, pressure $=\\rho g h$.<br>Where $\\rho=$ Density of liquid,<br>$\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{\\rm{g}} = $ Acceleration due gravity ,<br>$\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{\\rm{h}} = $ Height of liquid.<br>As, $h$ is same, pressure will also be same.<br>According to Pascal\'s law, the pressure applied to enclosed water is transmitted in all directions equally.<br>Thus, both the statements are true.<br>So, correct option is (1).',NULL,'Medium','MCQ type-2',2023,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38736,'physics',29,1300,3145,'Two vessels ${A}$ and ${B}$ are of the same size and are at same temperature. ${A}$ contains ${1 g}$ of hydrogen and ${B}$ contains ${1 g}$ of oxygen. ${P_{A}}$ and ${P_{B}}$ are the pressures of the gases in ${A}$ and respectively, then ${\\dfrac{P_{A}}{P_{B}}}$ is','[{"text": "32", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "16", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "4", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "8", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'${\\rm{Vessel}}\\,\\,A\\quad \\quad \\quad \\quad \\quad {\\rm{Vessel}}\\,\\,B$<br>$1\\,g\\,\\,{\\rm{of}}\\,\\,{\\rm{hydrogen}}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,1\\,g\\,\\,{\\rm{of}}\\,\\,{\\rm{oxygen}}$<br>${\\rm{Pressure}} \\to {P_A}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{\\rm{Pressure}} \\to {P_B}$<br>According to ideal gas equation,<br>${P V=n R T}$<br>As per question volume and temperature are same for both gases, then ${P \\propto n(n=}$ Number of moles)<br>As, ${n=\\dfrac{m}{M}}$<br>For hydrogen; ${n=\\dfrac{1}{2}}$ and for oxygen, ${n=\\dfrac{1}{32}}$<br>So, pressure ${P_{A} \\propto \\dfrac{1}{2} ; P_{B} \\propto \\dfrac{1}{32} \\Rightarrow \\dfrac{P_{A}}{P_{B}}=16}$<br>So, correct option is (2).',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2024,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38739,'physics',29,1303,3151,'The translational degrees of freedom ${\\left(f_{t}\\right)}$ and rotational degrees of freedom ${\\left(f_{r}\\right)}$ of  ${\\rm{C}}{{\\rm{H}}_4}$  molecule are','[{"text": "${f_{t}=2}$ and ${f_{r}=2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${f_{t}=3}$ and ${f_{r}=2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${f_{t}=2}$ and ${f_{r}=3}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${f_{t}=3}$ and ${f_{r}=3}$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'${\\rm{C}}{{\\rm{H}}_4}$ is a polyatomic and non linear molecule. Translational degrees of freedom ${=3}$ Rotational degrees of freedom ${=3}$<br>${\\therefore \\quad f_{t}=3}$ and ${f_{r}=3}$<br>So, Correct option is (4)',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2024,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38740,'physics',26,1138,2728,'If 1000 droplets of water of surface tension $0.07\\,N/m$, having same radius $1\\,mm$ each, combine to form a single drop. In the process the released surface energy is $\\left( {{\\rm{Take }}\\,\\pi  = \\frac{{22}}{7}} \\right)$','[{"text": "$7.92 \\\\times {10^{ - 6}}\\\\,J$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$7.92 \\\\times {10^{ - 4}}\\\\,J$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$9.68 \\\\times {10^{ - 4}}\\\\,J$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$8.8 \\\\times {10^{ - 5}}\\\\,J$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Volume of a bigger single drop $=1000 \\times$ volume of a smaller drop<br>$\\Rightarrow \\dfrac{4}{3} \\pi R^{3}=1000 \\times \\dfrac{4}{3} \\pi r^{3}$<br>$\\Rightarrow R^{3}=1000 r^{3} \\Rightarrow R=10 r$<br>Decrease in the surface area,<br>$\\Delta A=1000 \\times 4 \\pi r^{2}-4 \\pi R^{2}$<br>$=1000 \\times 4 \\pi r^{2}-4 \\pi(10 r)^{2}=900 \\times 4 \\pi r^{2}$<br>Here $r = 1\\,mm = {10^{ - 3}}\\,m$,<br>$S = 0.07\\,N/m$<br>$\\Delta E=$?<br>Required energy loss in the process,<br>$\\Delta E=$ Surface tension $\\times$ change in surface area<br>$\\Delta E=S . \\Delta A$<br>$\\Delta E=0.07 \\times 900 \\times 4 \\times(22 / 7) \\times\\left(10^{-3}\\right)^{2}$<br>$\\Delta E = 7.92 \\times {10^{ - 4}}\\,J$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2023,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38741,'physics',28,1371,3283,'A monoatomic gas ${\\left(\\gamma=\\dfrac{5}{3}\\right)}$ is suddenly compressed to ${1 / 8}$ of its volume adiabatically. If the initial pressure is ${P}$, then the final pressure of the gas is','[{"text": "${\\\\dfrac{24}{5} P}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${8 P}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\dfrac{40}{3} P}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${32 P}$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'For adiabatic process,  $P{V^\\gamma } = {\\text{ constant }}\\,\\,\\,{\\mkern 1mu} {\\kern 1pt} \\therefore \\,\\,\\,{\\mkern 1mu} {\\kern 1pt} {P_1}V_1^\\gamma  = {P_2}V_2^\\gamma $<br>$P{V^\\gamma } = {P_2}{\\left( {\\frac{V}{8}} \\right)^\\gamma },$ ${\\left(\\because V_{2}=\\dfrac{V}{8}\\right)}$<br>${P=\\dfrac{P_{2}}{8^{\\gamma}}}$ or ${P_{2}=8^{\\gamma} P}$<br>${\\therefore \\quad P_{2}=8^{5 / 3} P}$  ${\\left(\\because \\gamma=\\dfrac{5}{3}\\right)}$<br>${\\Rightarrow P_{2}=32 P}$.<br>So correct option is (4)',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38742,'physics',27,1381,3314,'A $0.5\\,kg$ of mass is attached to a massless spring with $k = 600\\,N{\\rm{/}}m.$ The system is completely immersed in a vessel containing $1\\,kg$ of water. The spring is made to oscillate with amplitude of  $5\\,cm.$ When the oscillations are stopped completely, the change in the temperature of the water will be<br>(Consider, specific heat of the mass $ = 900\\,J{\\rm{/}}kg - K,$ specific heat of water $ = 4200\\,J{\\rm{/}}kg - K,$ the heat received by the vessel and spring can be neglected.)','[{"text": "$1.61 \\\\times {10^{ - 4}}\\\\,k$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$2.13 \\\\times {10^{ - 4}}\\\\,k$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$5.35 \\\\times {10^{ - 4}}\\\\,k$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$3.65 \\\\times {10^{ - 4}}\\\\,k$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'When the oscillations of the spring are stopped, the potential energy stored in the spring is converted to heat energy, thus, increasing the temperature of the water.<br>$\\therefore \\,\\,\\,\\frac{1}{2}k{x^2} = {m_w}{c_w}\\Delta T + {m_m}{c_m}\\Delta T$<br>$\\therefore \\quad \\frac{1}{2} \\times 600 \\times {(0.05)^2}$<br>$ = (1 \\times 4200 + 0.5 \\times 900)\\Delta T$<br>$\\therefore 300 \\times 25 \\times {10^{ - 4}} = (4200 + 450)\\Delta T$<br>$\\therefore 25 \\times {10^{ - 4}} = \\frac{{4650}}{{300}}\\Delta T$<br>$\\therefore \\Delta T = \\frac{{25 \\times {{10}^4}}}{{15.5}} = \\frac{{50}}{{31}} \\times {10^{ - 4}}$<br>$ = 1.6129 \\times {10^{ - 4}}\\,K$<br>$\\therefore \\Delta T = 1.61 \\times {10^{ - 4}}\\,K$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38743,'physics',26,1135,2714,'Water flows through a horizontal pipe of varying cross-section at a rate of ${0.314 {~m}^{3} {~s}^{-1}}$. The velocity of water at a point where the radius of the pipe is $10\\,cm$ is','[{"text": "${0.1 {~ms}^{-1}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${1 {~ms}^{-1}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${10 {~ms}^{-1}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${100 {~ms}^{-1}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Rate of flow is given by<br>${Q=A V}$<br>${0.314=\\pi r^{2} \\cdot V}$<br>${0.314=3.14 \\times 10^{-2} \\times V}$<br>${V=10 {~ms}^{-1}}$<br>So, correct option is (3).',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2024,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38744,'physics',29,1303,3152,'The specific heat at constant pressure of a real gas obeying ${P V^{2}=R T}$ equation is','[{"text": "${\\\\dfrac{R}{3}+C_{V}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${C_{V}+\\\\dfrac{R}{2 V}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${R}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${C_{V}+R}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Given that, ${P V^{2}=R T}$<br>For ${P=}$ constant, and ${n=1}$<br>${P(2 V) d V=R d T}$<br>or  $PdV = \\frac{{RdT}}{{2V}}\\quad \\quad (1)$<br>From first law of thermodynamics, <br>${d Q=d W+d U}$<br>or ${d Q=P d V+C_{V} d T}$ (Using equation (1))<br>or ${d Q=\\dfrac{R d T}{2 V}+C_{V} d T}$<br>or ${\\dfrac{d Q}{d T}=\\dfrac{R}{2 V}+C_{V}}$<br>${\\Rightarrow C_{P}=\\dfrac{R}{2 V}+C_{V}}$<br>So, correct option is (2).',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',2024,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38747,'physics',26,1138,2727,'A thin flat circular disc of radius 4.5 cm is placed gently over the surface of water. If surface tension of water is ${0.07 {Nm}^{-1}}$, then the excess force required to take it away from the surface is','[{"text": "$19.8\\\\,mN$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$198\\\\,N$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1.98\\\\,mN$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$99\\\\,N$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Surface tension force acts along the circumference of the disc. <img src=\'/img/data/physics/26_38747_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' /> Excess force required to balance the surface tension force is ${F_{ext}} = T \\times 2\\pi R$<br>${F_{ext}} = \\frac{7}{{100}} \\times 2 \\times 3.14 \\times \\frac{{4.5}}{{100}}$<br>$ = 197.82 \\times {10^{ - 4}} = 19.8\\,mN$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2024,'NEET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38748,'physics',29,1302,3150,'Energy of 10 non rigid diatomic molecules at temperature ${T}$ is','[{"text": "$35\\\\,RT$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${70 K_{B} T}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\dfrac{7}{2} R T}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${35 K_{B} T}$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'For non rigid diatomic molecule, degree of freedom is given by, ${f=7}$<br>Energy of one molecule ${=\\dfrac{f}{2} K_{B} T}$<br>Energy of 10 molecules<br>${=10 \\times \\dfrac{f}{2} K_{B} T=5 \\times 7 K_{B} T=35 K_{B} T}$<br>So, correct option is (4).',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',2024,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38751,'physics',29,1304,3154,'A mixture of one mole of monoatomic gas and one mole of a diatomic gas (rigid) are kept at room temperature ${\\left(27^{\\circ} {C}\\right)}$. The ratio of specific heat of gases at constant volume respectively is','[{"text": "${\\\\dfrac{3}{2}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\dfrac{5}{3}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\dfrac{7}{5}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\dfrac{3}{5}}$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Specific heat of gases at constant volume,<br>${C_{V}=\\dfrac{f}{2} R}$<br>${\\left(C_{V}\\right)_{\\text {monoatomic gas }}=\\dfrac{3}{2} R}$;<br>${\\left(C_{V}\\right)_{\\text {diatomic gas }}=\\dfrac{5}{2} R\\left(f_{\\text {mono }}=3, f_{\\text {dia }}=5\\right)}$<br>${\\because}$ Required ratio ${=\\dfrac{\\left(C_{V}\\right)_{\\text {mono }}}{\\left(C_{V}\\right)_{\\text {dia }}}=\\dfrac{3}{5}}$<br>So, correct option is (4).',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',2024,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38752,'physics',26,1135,2714,'The diagram shows a pipe of uniform crosssection in which water is flowing. The directions of flow and the volume flow rates (in ${{cm}^{3} / {s}}$ ) are shown for various portions of the pipe. The direction of flow and the volume flow rate in the portion marked ${A}$ are','[{"text": "${\\\\downarrow}$ and ${3 {~cm}^{3} / {s}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\uparrow}$ and ${7 {~cm}^{3} / {s}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\downarrow}$ and ${15 {~cm}^{3} / {s}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${\\\\uparrow}$ and ${11 {~cm}^{3} / {s}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Let the water be flowing in upward direction in pipe ${A}$ with flow rate<br>${x {~cm}^{3} / {s},(3+6+x)+4+5=3}$<br>${18+x=3}$<br>${x=-15}$<br>-ve sign indicates the opposite direction for the flow of water, i.e., downward.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/26_38752_question_1.png'),
(38755,'physics',26,1136,2715,'Water is flowing with a velocity of ${2 {~m} / {s}}$ in a horizontal pipe where cross-sectional area is ${2 \\times 10^{-2} {~m}^{2}}$ at pressure ${4 \\times 10^{4} {~Pa}}$. The pressure (in ${P a}$ ) at cross-section of area ${0.01 m^{2}}$ will be','[{"text": "32", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "3.4", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${3.4 \\\\times 10^{4}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${3.4 \\\\times 10^{5}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'${A_{1} v_{1}=A_{2} v_{2}}$<br>${2 \\times\\left(2 \\times 10^{-2}\\right)=v_{2}(0.01)}$<br>${v_{2}=4 {~m} / {s}}$<br>${P_{1}+\\dfrac{1}{2} P v_{1}^{2}=P_{2}+\\dfrac{1}{2} P v_{2}^{2}}$<br>${4 \\times 10^{4}+\\dfrac{1}{2} \\times\\left(10^{3}\\right) \\times(2)^{2}}$<br>${=P_{2}+\\dfrac{1}{2} \\times\\left(10^{3}\\right) \\times(4)^{2}}$<br>${\\Rightarrow P_{2}=4 \\times 10^{4}-6 \\times 10^{3}=3.4 \\times 10^{4} {~Pa}}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38756,'physics',29,1302,3150,'The temperature of a gas is ${-78^{\\circ} {C}}$ and the average translational kinetic energy of its molecules is ${K}$. The temperature at which the average translational kinetic energy of the molecules of the same gas becomes ${2 K}$ is','[{"text": "${127^{\\\\circ} {C}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${-39^{\\\\circ} {C}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${117^{\\\\circ} {C}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${-78^{\\\\circ} {C}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'${T_{i}=-78^{\\circ} {C}=195 {~K}}$<br>As we know that, $K.E. \\propto T,$ when kinetic energy is doubled, temperature also double.<br>${K_{f}=2 k \\Rightarrow T_{f}=2 T}$<br>${T_{f}=2 \\times 195=390 {~K}=117^{\\circ} {C}}$<br>So, correct option is (3).',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',2024,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38759,'physics',26,1136,2717,'A closed vessel have a small hole on one face of vessel near the bottom, as shown. The velocity (in ${{m} / {s}}$ ) of water coming out from the given hole at a given instant is','[{"text": "4", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "5", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "3", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "6", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'${9 P_{0}+\\rho g h=P_{0}+\\dfrac{1}{2} \\rho v^{2}}$<br>${\\Rightarrow v=5 {~m} / {s}}$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/26_38759_question_1.png');
INSERT INTO `qb_questions` (`id`, `subject_id`, `chapter_id`, `topic_id`, `subtopic_id`, `question_html`, `options`, `correct_index`, `explanation`, `explanation_image_url`, `difficulty`, `qtype`, `year`, `tag`, `created_at`, `question_image_url`) VALUES
(38760,'physics',29,1304,3154,'Two moles of a monoatomic gas is mixed with six moles of a diatomic gas. The molar specific heat of the mixture at constant volume is','[{"text": "${\\\\dfrac{3}{2} R}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\dfrac{5}{2} R}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\dfrac{9}{4} R}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${\\\\dfrac{7}{4} R}$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'${n_{1}=2, n_{2}=6}$<br>molar specific heat at constant volume,<br>$C_{V}=\\dfrac{f}{2} R$<br>${\\therefore C_{V_{1}}=\\dfrac{3}{2} R, \\quad C_{V_{2}}=\\dfrac{5}{2} R}$<br>So, molar specific heat of the mixture is<br>$\\begin{aligned}& C_{V_{\\text {mix }}}=\\dfrac{n_{1} C_{V_{1}}+n_{2} C_{V_{2}}}{n_{1}+n_{2}}=\\dfrac{2 \\times \\dfrac{3}{2} R+6 \\times \\dfrac{5}{2} R}{2+6} \\\\& =\\dfrac{3 R+15 R}{8}=\\dfrac{9}{4} R\\end{aligned}$<br>So, correct option is (3)',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',2024,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38762,'physics',27,1382,3319,'Two identical metal plates are welded end to end as shown in Fig. (i). $20\\,cal$ of heat flows through it in 4 minutes. If the plates are welded as shown in Fig. (ii), find the time (in minutes) taken by the same amount of heat to flow through the plates.','[{"text": "2", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "4", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "1", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "6", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Rate of heat flow ${\\dfrac{\\Delta Q}{\\Delta t}=\\dfrac{k A\\left(T_{1}-T_{2}\\right)}{l} \\Rightarrow \\Delta t \\propto \\dfrac{l}{A}}$<br>$\\frac{{\\Delta \\,{t_1}}}{{\\Delta \\,{t_2}}} = \\frac{{{l_1}/{A_1}}}{{{l_2}/{A_2}}} = \\frac{{{l_1}{A_2}}}{{{l_2}{A_1}}}$<br>${l_1} = 2l,\\,\\,\\,\\,{l_2} = l$<br>${A_1} = A,\\,\\,\\,\\,{A_2} = 2A$<br>$ \\Rightarrow \\,\\,\\,\\frac{{\\Delta {\\mkern 1mu} {t_1}}}{{\\Delta {\\mkern 1mu} {t_2}}} = \\frac{{2l\\left( {2A} \\right)}}{{l\\left( {{A_2}} \\right)}} = 4$<br>$ \\Rightarrow \\Delta {\\mkern 1mu} {t_2} = \\frac{{\\Delta {\\mkern 1mu} {t_1}}}{4} = \\frac{4}{4} = 1$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/27_38762_question_1.png'),
(38763,'physics',29,1304,3154,'${\\quad N}$ moles of a polyatomic gas ${(f=6)}$ must be mixed with two moles of a monoatomic gas so that the mixture behaves as a diatomic gas. The value of ${N}$ is','[{"text": "4", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "2", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "3", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "6", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Given: number of moles ${=N}$<br>Degree of freedom, of polyatomic gas ${f_{1}=6}$;<br>Degree of freedom monoatomic gas ${f_{2}=3}$<br>Degree of freedom of mixture of gas is given by<br>${f_{e q}=\\dfrac{n_{1} f_{1}+n_{2} f_{2}}{n_{1}+n_{2}}}$<br>For diatomic, ${f_{e q}=5}$<br>$\\begin{aligned}& \\therefore 5=\\dfrac{N \\times 6+2 \\times 3}{N+2} \\\\& \\Rightarrow N=4\\end{aligned}$<br>So, correct option is (1).',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',2024,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38764,'physics',26,1138,2728,'If million small drops of water coalesce into one larger drop, then the ratio of total surface energy of the larger drop to that of the smaller drops combined will be','[{"text": "${1: 10^{4}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${1: 10^{3}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${1: 10^{2}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${1: 10}$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'If radius of small drop is ${r}$, then radius of larger drop, ${R}$ is ${\\left(10^{6}\\right)^{1 / 3}}$.<br>$\\Rightarrow R=100 r$<br>$\\therefore $  ratio  is  $1:{10^2}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38765,'physics',28,1372,3294,'Amongst the two paths that may be taken by a gas to go from a state $A$ to a state $C$, in process $AB$ (as shown in figure), $360\\,J$ of heat is added to the system. In process $BC,140\\,J$ of heat is added to the system. The heat absorbed by the system in the process $AC$ will be','[{"text": "$460\\\\,J$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$300\\\\,J$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$420\\\\,J$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$530\\\\,J$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'${Q_{ABC}} = {Q_{AC}} + {W_{ABCA}}\\,$<br>In this case,<br>${W_{ABCA}} = $Area of $PV$ graph $ = \\frac{1}{2} \\times AB \\times BC$<br>$\\therefore (360 + 140) = {Q_{AC}} + \\frac{1}{2} \\times \\left( {8 \\times {{10}^4} \\times 2 \\times {{10}^{ - 3}}} \\right)$<br>$\\therefore {Q_{AC}} = 500 - 80 = 420\\,J$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/28_38765_question_1.png'),
(38767,'physics',26,1138,2730,'The volume of a spherical soap bubble ${A}$ is 8 times the volume of the soap bubble ${B}$. The ratio of the excess pressure inside the two soap bubbles will be','[{"text": "${1: 8}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${1: 6}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${1: 4}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${1: 2}$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'${\\dfrac{R_{A}}{R_{B}}=\\dfrac{2}{1}}$<br>Since ${P \\propto \\dfrac{1}{r}}$<br>${\\dfrac{P_{A}}{P_{B}}=\\dfrac{r_{B}}{r_{A}}=\\dfrac{1}{2}}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38768,'physics',29,1304,3154,'If three moles of monoatomic gas ${\\left(\\gamma=\\dfrac{5}{3}\\right)}$ is mixed with two moles of a diatomic gas ${\\left(\\gamma=\\dfrac{7}{5}\\right)}$, the value of adiabatic exponent ${\\gamma}$ for the mixture is','[{"text": "1.40", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "1.35", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "1.52", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "1.75", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Ratio of specific heats due to the mixture of gases is<br>$\\gamma_{\\text {mix }}=\\dfrac{C_{P(\\text { mix })}}{C_{V(\\text { mix })}}=\\dfrac{n_{1} C_{P_{1}}+n_{2} C_{P_{2}}}{n_{1} C_{V_{1}}+n_{2} C_{V_{2}}}$<br>As ${r=1+\\dfrac{2}{f}}$<br>${\\Rightarrow f=\\dfrac{2}{\\gamma-1}}$<br>${\\therefore C_{V}=\\dfrac{f}{2} R}$<br>${\\Rightarrow C_{V}=\\dfrac{R}{\\gamma-1}}$<br>$C_{p}=C_{V}+R$<br>${\\therefore \\dfrac{C_{P}}{C_{V}}=\\dfrac{3\\left(\\dfrac{5}{2} R\\right)+2\\left(\\dfrac{7 R}{2}\\right)}{3 \\times\\left(\\dfrac{3}{2} R\\right)+2\\left(\\dfrac{5}{2} R\\right)}}$<br>$=\\dfrac{\\dfrac{15 R}{2}+\\dfrac{14 R}{2}}{\\dfrac{9 R}{2}+\\dfrac{10 R}{2}}=\\dfrac{29}{19}=1.52$<br>So, correct option is (3).',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',2024,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38770,'physics',27,1381,3315,'In an industrial process $10\\,kg$ of water per hour is to be heated from ${20^{\\circ} {C}}$ to ${80^{\\circ} {C}}$. To do this steam at ${150^{\\circ} {C}}$ is passed from a boiler into a copper coil immersed in water. The steam condenses in the coil and is returned to the boiler as water at ${90^{\\circ} {C}}$. How many $kg$ of steam is required per hour? (Specific heat of steam ${=}$ specific heat of water ${=1 {cal} / {g}^{\\circ} {C}}$, Latent heat of vaporization ${=540 {cal} / {g}}$)','[{"text": "$1\\\\,kg$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$3\\\\,kg$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$6\\\\,kg$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2\\\\,kg$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Suppose ${m {~kg}}$ steam is required per hour<br>Heat is released by steam in following three steps<br>(i) When ${150^{\\circ} {C}}$ steam $\\overrightarrow {{Q_1}} \\,100^\\circ C$ steam  ${Q_1} = m{c_{{\\text{steam }}}}\\Delta \\theta  = m \\times 1(150 - 100) = 50\\,m\\,cal$<br>(ii) When ${100^{\\circ} {C}}$ steam $\\overrightarrow {{Q_2}} \\,100^\\circ C$ water<br>${Q_2} = m{L_v} = m \\times 540 = 540\\,m\\,cal$<br>(iii) When ${100^{\\circ} {C}}$ water $\\overrightarrow {{Q_2}} \\,90^\\circ C$ water<br>${Q_3} = m{c_w}\\Delta \\theta  = m \\times 1 \\times (100 - 90) = 10\\,m\\,cal$<br>Hence total heat given by the steam<br>$Q = {Q_1} + {Q_2} + {Q_3} = 600\\,m\\,cal\\quad \\quad {\\mkern 1mu} (1)$<br>Heat taken by $10\\,kg$ water<br>${Q^\\prime }m{c_w}\\Delta \\theta  = 10 \\times {10^3} \\times 1 \\times (80 - 20)$<br>$ = 600 \\times {10^3}cal$<br>Hence ${Q=Q^{\\prime} \\Rightarrow 600 {~m}=600 \\times 10^{3}}$<br>${\\Rightarrow m=10^{3} {~g}=1 {~kg}}$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38771,'physics',29,1306,3156,'The parameter that remains the same for molecules of all gases at a given temperature is','[{"text": "kinetic energy", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "speed", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "momentum", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "mass", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Kinetic energy is given by ${\\dfrac{3}{2} k T}$, where ${k}$ is the Boltzman constant. Thus, for a given temperature it will be same for all molecules.<br>So, correct option is (1)',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2024,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38772,'physics',26,1137,2723,'A rain drop of radius $1,5\\,mm$ experiences a drag force ${F=\\left(2 \\times 10^{-5} {v}\\right) {N}}$, while falling through air from a height 2 km with a velocity ${v}$. The terminal velocity of the rain drop will be nearly (use ${g=10 {~m} / {s}^{2}}$ )','[{"text": "${200 {~m} / {s}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${60 {~m} / {s}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${7 {~m} / {s}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${3 {~m} / {s}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'At equilibrium,<br>weight of raindrop ${=}$ Drag force on the spherical raindrop<br>$\\dfrac{4}{3} \\pi r^{3} \\rho g=2 \\times 10^{-5} v$<br>${v_T} = \\frac{{\\left( \\begin{array}{l}\n4 \\times (3.14) \\times {(1.5)^3} \\times \\\\\n{\\left( {{{10}^{ - 3}}} \\right)^3} \\times {10^3} \\times 10\n\\end{array} \\right)}}{{3 \\times 2 \\times {{10}^{ - 5}}}} = 7\\,m/s$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38775,'physics',26,1137,2722,'A space $2.5\\,cm$ wide between two large plane surfaces is filled with oil. Force required to drag a very thin plate of area ${0.5 {~m}^{2}}$ just midway the surfaces at a speed of ${0.5 {~m} / {s}}$ is $1\\,N.$   The coefficient of viscosity in ${{kg}-s / {m}^{2}}$ is','[{"text": "${5 \\\\times 10^{-2}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${2.5 \\\\times 10^{-2}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${1 \\\\times 10^{-2}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${7.5 \\\\times 10^{-2}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Velocity gradient ${=\\dfrac{0.5 \\times 2}{2.5 \\times 10^{-2}}}$<br>$\\begin{aligned}& \\text { Also, } F=2 \\eta A \\dfrac{d v}{d z}=2 \\times \\eta \\times(0.5) \\times \\dfrac{0.5}{1.25 \\times 10^{-2}} \\\\& \\quad \\Rightarrow \\quad \\eta=2.5 \\times 10^{-2} {~kg}-{sec} / {m}^{2}\\end{aligned}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/26_38775_question_1.png'),
(40134,'physics',31,1348,3245,'When two tuning forks $A$ and $B$ are sounded together $x$ beat/s are heard. Frequency $A$ is $n$. Now, when one prong of $B$ is loaded with a little wax, the number of beats/s decreases. The frequency of fork $B$ is','[{"text": "$n-x$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$n+x$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$n-2 x$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$n-x^{2}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'There are two possible frequencies $n+x$ and $n-x$. But only $n+x$ satisfied the given conditions.',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38776,'physics',29,1302,3150,'A gas mixture consists of 8 moles of argon and 6 moles of oxygen at temperature ${T}$. Neglecting all vibrational modes, the total internal energy of the system is','[{"text": "${20 R T}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${29 R T}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$27\\\\,RT$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${21 R T}$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Number of moles of argon, ${n_{1}=8}$<br>Number of moles of oxygen, ${n_{2}=6}$<br>For an ideal gas, internal energy, ${U=\\dfrac{f}{2} n R T}$ where \' ${f}$ \' is degree of freedom<br>${\\therefore U_{1}=\\dfrac{f_{1}}{2} n_{1} R T}$ and ${U_{2}=\\dfrac{f_{2}}{2} n_{2} R T}$<br>Total energy, ${U=U_{1}+U_{2}}$<br>${U=\\dfrac{f_{1}}{2} n_{1} R T+\\dfrac{f_{2}}{2} n_{2} R T}$<br>${U=\\dfrac{3}{2} \\times 8 R T+\\dfrac{5}{2} \\times 6 R T=27 R T}$<br>So, correct option is (3).',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2024,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38777,'physics',28,1377,3303,'A barometer tube has length $90\\,cm.$ It contains some air above mercury. On some hot days when temperature is $50^\\circ C$ and true atmospheric pressure is $75\\,cm$ of $Hg,$ the reading of the mercury level is $74.8\\,cm.$ If the reading is observed to be $75.1\\,cm$ on some another day when temperature is $25^\\circ C,$ then Find the true pressure on that day will be','[{"text": "$75.59\\\\,cm$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$63.56\\\\,cm$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$55.6\\\\,cm$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$86.72\\\\,cm$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'<img src=\'/img/data/physics/28_38777_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />  ${P_1} = 75 - 74.8 = 0.2\\,cm$ of $Hg$<br>${P_2} = \\left( {P\' - 75.1} \\right)cm$ of $Hg$<br>The length of barometer tube above mercury level is equivalent to volume of air in the tube in both cases<br>$\\therefore {V_1} = 90 - 74.8 = 15.2$ and ${V_2} = 14.9$<br>From equation of state, $\\frac{{{P_1}{V_1}}}{{{T_1}}} = \\frac{{{P_2}{V_2}}}{{{T_2}}}$<br>($\\because$ number of moles of gas is constant in the barometer tube)<br>$\\therefore \\frac{{0.2 \\times 15.2}}{{(50 + 273)}} = \\frac{{{P_2} \\times 14.9}}{{(25 + 273)}}$<br>$\\therefore \\quad {P_2} = \\frac{{298 \\times 0.2 \\times 15.2}}{{323 \\times 14.9}}$<br>$\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, = \\frac{{298 \\times 152 \\times 0.2}}{{323 \\times 149}}$<br>$ = \\frac{{2 \\times 8 \\times 0.2}}{{17}}$<br>$ = \\frac{{3.2}}{{17}}$<br>$\\therefore \\quad {P_2} = 0.188 = 0.19$<br>$\\therefore \\quad P\' = 75.4 + 0.19 \\approx 75.59\\,cm$ of $Hg$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38778,'physics',27,1380,3309,'A fine steel wire of length $4\\,m$ is fixed rigidly in a heavy brass frame as shown in figure. It is just taut at ${20^{\\circ} {C}}$. Find the tensile stress developed in steel wire  if whole system is heated to ${120^{\\circ} {C}}$.<br>(Given  ${\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\alpha _{{\\text{brass}}}} = 1.8 \\times {10^{ - 50}}{C^{ - 1}},$<br>${\\alpha _{{\\text{steel}}}} = 1.2 \\times {10^{ - 50}}{C^{ - 1}},$<br>${{Y_{{\\rm{steel }}}} = 20 + {{10}^{11}}N{m^{ - 2}},{Y_{{\\rm{brass }}}}}$ $\\left. { = 1.7 \\times {{10}^7}N{m^{ - 2}}} \\right)$','[{"text": "$160 \\\\times {10^6}\\\\,N{m^{ - 2}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$130 \\\\times {10^6}\\\\,N{m^{ - 2}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$110 \\\\times {10^6}\\\\,N{m^2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$120 \\\\times {10^6}\\\\,N{m^{ - 2}}$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Stress ${=Y(}$ strain ${)=Y_{S}\\left(\\alpha_{b}-\\alpha_{s}\\right) \\Delta T}$<br>$ = \\left( {2 \\times {{10}^{11}}} \\right)\\left( {0.6 \\times {{10}^{ - 5}}} \\right)(100)$<br>$ = 120 \\times {10^6}N{m^{ - 2}}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/27_38778_question_1.png'),
(38779,'physics',29,1305,3155,'If the collision frequency of hydrogen molecules in a closed chamber at ${27^{\\circ} {C}}$ is ${Z}$, then the collision frequency of the same system at ${127^{\\circ} {C}}$ is','[{"text": "${\\\\dfrac{4}{3} Z}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\dfrac{\\\\sqrt{3}}{2} Z}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\dfrac{3}{4} Z}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\dfrac{2}{\\\\sqrt{3}} Z}$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Collision time between successive collisions is ${\\tau \\alpha \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{T}}}$ and ${Z=\\dfrac{1}{\\tau}}$<br>$\\therefore Z \\propto \\sqrt T \\quad \\quad (1)$<br>Here, ${Z_{1}=Z, T_{1}=300 K, Z_{2}=}$ ? and ${T_{2}=400 {~K}}$<br>From eq. (1), we can write<br>$\\dfrac{Z_{2}}{Z_{1}}=\\sqrt{\\dfrac{T_{2}}{T_{1}}}$<br>or ${\\dfrac{Z_{2}}{Z_{1}}=\\sqrt{\\dfrac{400}{300}}}$ or ${\\dfrac{Z_{2}}{Z_{1}}=\\dfrac{2}{\\sqrt{3}}}$ or<br>${\\Rightarrow Z_{2}=\\dfrac{2}{\\sqrt{3}} Z}$<br>So, option (4) is correct.',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',2024,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38780,'physics',26,1139,2734,'A common hydrometer reads specific gravity of liquids. Compared to the 1.6 mark of the stem, the mark 1.5 will be','[{"text": "upwards", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "downwards", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "in the same place", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "may be upward or downward depending upon the hydrometer", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'In hydrometer, lower density mark is upwards and higher density mark is downwards.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38781,'physics',28,1372,3294,'The diagram given below represents $P - T$ curve of a cyclic process. If number of moles of the gas are $n = 3,$ the work done by the gas in the process is<br>(Consider $R = 8.32\\,J\\,mo{l^{ - 1}}\\,{K^{ - 1}}$)','[{"text": "$12.48\\\\,k.J$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$14.98\\\\,k.J$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$16.34\\\\,k.J$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$19.45\\\\,k.J$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'During paths $BC$ and  $DA,$ <br>$P \\propto T\\,$<br>Thus, the paths $BC$  and $DA$ are isochoric.Work done during an isochoric process is zero.<br>$\\therefore \\quad {W_{BC}} = {W_{DA}} = 0$<br>${W_{AB}} = nR\\Delta T = nR\\left( {{T_2} - {T_1}} \\right)$<br>$ = 3 \\times 8.32 \\times (573 - 373)$<br>$ = 4992\\,J$<br>Similarly, ${W_{CD}} = 3 \\times 8.32 \\times (873 - 473)$<br>$ = 9984\\,J$<br>$\\therefore $ Total work done $ = {W_{AB}} + {W_{CD}} = 4992 + 9984$<br>$ = 14976\\,J$<br>$ = 14.98\\,kJ$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/28_38781_question_1.png'),
(38783,'physics',29,1305,3155,'If ${n}$ is the number density and ${d}$ is the diameter of the molecule, then the average distance covered by a molecule between two successive collisions ($i.\\,e.$ mean free path) is represented by','[{"text": "${\\\\dfrac{1}{\\\\sqrt{2} n^{2} \\\\pi^{2} d^{2}}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\dfrac{1}{\\\\sqrt{2 n \\\\pi d^{2}}}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\dfrac{1}{\\\\sqrt{2} n \\\\pi d^{2}}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${\\\\sqrt{2} n \\\\pi d^{2}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Average distance (or mean free path) travelled by a gas molecule between two successive collisions is known as mean free path and is give as, ${\\lambda=\\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{2} n \\pi d^{2}}}$<br>So, correct option is (3).',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2024,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38828,'physics',26,1136,2721,'A rectangular vessel when full of water, takes $10 \\mathrm{~min}$ to be emptied through an orifice in its bottom. How much time will it take to be emptied when half filled with water?','[{"text": "9 min", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$7 \\\\mathrm{~min}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$5 \\\\mathrm{~min}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$3 \\\\mathrm{~min}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'If $A_{0}$ is the area of orifice at the bottom below the free surface and $A$ that of vessel, time $t$ taken to be emptied the tank,<br>$t=\\dfrac{A}{A_{0}} \\sqrt{\\dfrac{2 H}{g}}$<br>$\\therefore \\dfrac{t_{1}}{t_{2}}=\\sqrt{\\dfrac{H_{1}}{H_{2}}} \\Rightarrow \\dfrac{t}{t_{2}}=\\sqrt{2}$<br>$t_{2}=\\dfrac{t}{\\sqrt{2}}=\\dfrac{10}{\\sqrt{2}} \\approx 7 \\mathrm{~min}$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38784,'physics',26,1133,2705,'Find density of ethyl alcohol.','[{"text": "$0.83\\\\,g\\\\,c{m^{ - 3}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$0.5g\\\\,c{m^{ - 3}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1.83g\\\\,c{m^{ - 3}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$0.12g\\\\,c{m^{ - 3}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Pressure at left hand of a U-tube,<br>$p_{1}=p_{0}+\\rho_{1} g h_{1}=p_{0}+\\rho_{1} 10\\left(10 \\times 10^{-2}\\right)$<br>or $\\quad p_{1}=p_{0}+\\rho_{1}$<br>Pressure at right hand of a U-tube,<br>$p_{2}=p_{0}+\\rho_{2} g h_{2}=p_{0}+\\rho_{2} 10\\left(12 \\times 10^{-2}\\right)$<br>or $\\quad {p_2} = {p_0} + 1.2\\,\\,{\\rho _2}$<br>The mercury column in both arms of U-tube are at same level, therefore pressure in both arms will be same.<br>or $\\quad p_{1}=p_{2} \\Rightarrow p_{0}+\\rho_{1}=p_{0}+1.2 \\rho_{2}$<br>$\\therefore$ Density of ethyl alcohol,<br>${\\rho _2} = \\frac{{{\\rho _1}}}{{1.2}} = \\frac{{1000}}{{1.2}} = 833.3\\,kg\\,{m^{ - 3}}$<br>$\\quad \\quad 0.83\\,g\\,c{m^{ - 3}}$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/26_38784_question_1.png'),
(38786,'physics',26,1133,2710,'A solid floats with $1 / 4$ th of its volume above the surface of water, the density of the solid is','[{"text": "$750\\\\,kg\\\\,{m^{ - 3}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$650\\\\,kg\\\\,{m^{ - 3}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$560\\\\,kg\\\\,{m^{ - 3}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$450\\\\,kg\\\\,{m^{ - 3}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Let $V$ and $\\rho$ be volume and density of solid respectively and $\\rho^{\\prime}$ be the density of water,<br> i.e. $\\quad \\rho \' = {10^3}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} kg\\,{m^{ - 3}}$<br>Weight of body $=V \\rho g$<br>Volume of solid body outside water $=V / 4$<br>$\\therefore$ Volume of solid body inside water$=V-V / 4=3 V / 4$<br>Weight of water displaced by solid<br>$=\\dfrac{3 \\mathrm{~V}}{4} \\times 10^{3} \\times g$<br>As solid body is floating, then<br>Weight of body $=$ Weight of water displaced by it<br>$V\\rho g = \\frac{{3V}}{4} \\times {10^3}g$<br>$ \\Rightarrow \\quad \\rho  = \\frac{3}{4} \\times 1000 = 750\\,kg\\,{m^{ - 3}}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38788,'physics',29,1305,3155,'Read the Statement - A and Statement - B carefully to mark the correct options give below.<div class="matching-question"><div class="column-left">The mean free path of gas molecules is inversely proportional to square of molecular diameter.</div><div class="column-right">Average kinetic energy of gas molecules is directly proportional to absolute temperature of gas.</div></div>','[{"text": "Statement A is correct but Statement B is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Statement A incorrect but Statement B is correct", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Statements are correct", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Statements are incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Average ${K . E}$. of gas molecules ${=\\dfrac{f}{2} k T}$<br>where, ${f}$ is the degree of freedom of molecule.<br>${\\therefore(K . E) \\propto T}$<br>Also, mean free path of gas molecule<br>${(\\lambda)=\\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{2} \\pi d^{2} n}}$<br>${\\therefore \\lambda \\propto \\dfrac{1}{d^{2}}}$<br>Hence both statement ${A}$ and ${B}$ are correct.<br>So, correct option is (3).',NULL,'Medium','MCQ type-2',2024,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38790,'physics',29,1300,3145,'The temperature of a gas having ${2.0 \\times 10^{25}}$ molecules per cubic meter at $1.38\\,\\,atm$ is <br>(Given, ${k=1.38 \\times 10^{-23} J K^{-1}}$ ) is','[{"text": "$100\\\\,K$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$300\\\\,K$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$500\\\\,K$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$200\\\\,K$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Given, ${N=2 \\times 10^{25} / {m}^{3}}$<br>${P=1.38 {~atm}, k=1.38 \\times 10^{-23} {~J} / {K}}$<br>By ideal gas equation<br>${P V=n R T}$<br>${\\Rightarrow P V=\\dfrac{N}{N_{A}} R T}$<br>${\\Rightarrow P=\\dfrac{N}{V} \\cdot k T}$<br>${\\Rightarrow 1.38 \\times 1.01 \\times 10^{5}}$<br>${=2 \\times 10^{25} \\times 1.38 \\times 10^{-23} \\times T}$<br>${\\Rightarrow T \\simeq 500 K}$<br>So, correct option is (3).',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2024,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38791,'physics',27,1381,3315,'A calorimeter of water equivalent $10\\,g$ contains a liquid of mass 50 g at ${40^{\\circ} {C}}$. When ${m}$ gram of ice at ${-10^{\\circ} {C}}$ is put into the calorimeter and the mixture is allowed to attain equilibrium, the final temperature was found to be ${20^{\\circ} {C}}$. It is known that specific heat capacity of the liquid changes with temperature as ${S=\\left(1+\\dfrac{\\theta}{500}\\right) {cal} {g}^{-1}{ }^{\\circ} {C}^{-1}}$ where ${\\theta}$ is temperature in ${{ }^{\\circ} {C}}$. The specific heat capacity of ice, water and the calorimeter remains constant and values are ${S_{{\\text{ice }}}} = 0.5\\,cal{g^{ - 1}}^\\circ {C^{ - 1}};$ ${S_{{\\text{water }}}} = 1.0\\,cal{g^{ - 1}}^\\circ {C^{ - 1}}$ and latent heat of fusion of ice is ${L_{f}=80 {cal} {g}^{-1}}$. Assume no heat loss to the surrounding and calculate the value of','[{"text": "$10\\\\,g$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$12\\\\,g$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$8\\\\,g$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$15\\\\,g$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Heat gained by ice<br>$=m S_{{ice}} \\times 10+m S_{m} \\times 20 \\times m L_{f}=105 {~m} . {cal}$<br>Heat lost by calorimeter ${=10 \\times 1 \\times 20=200 {cal}}$<br>$\\text { Heat lost by liquid }=-50 \\int_{40}^{20}\\left(1+\\dfrac{\\theta}{500}\\right) d \\theta$<br>$ = 50\\left[ {\\theta  + \\frac{{{\\theta ^2}}}{{1000}}} \\right]_{20}^{40}$<br>$ = 50\\left[ {\\left( {40 + \\frac{{1600}}{{1000}}} \\right) - \\left( {20 + \\frac{{400}}{{1000}}} \\right)} \\right]$<br>$ = 50 \\times 21.2 = 1060\\,cal$<br>${\\therefore 105 {~m}=1060+200 \\Rightarrow m=12 {~g}}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38792,'physics',26,1133,2710,'An ice-berg of density $900\\,kg\\,{m^{ - 3}}$ is floating in water of density $1000{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} kg\\,{m^{ - 3}}.$ The percentage of volume of ice-berg outside the water is','[{"text": "$20 \\\\%$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$35 \\\\%$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$10 \\\\%$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$11 \\\\%$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Let the volume of ice-berg is $V$ and its density is $\\rho$. If this ice-berg floats in water with volume $V_{\\text {in }}$ inside it, then<br>$\\begin{aligned}& V_{\\text {in }} \\sigma g=V \\rho g \\\\& \\Rightarrow V_{\\text {in }}=\\left(\\dfrac{\\rho}{\\sigma}\\right) V\\end{aligned}$<br>$\\begin{aligned}\\Rightarrow V_{\\text {out }} & =V-V_{\\text {in }}=\\left(\\dfrac{\\sigma-\\rho}{\\sigma}\\right) V \\\\& =\\left(\\dfrac{1000-900}{1000}\\right) V=\\dfrac{V}{10}\\end{aligned}$<br>$\\Rightarrow \\dfrac{V_{\\text {out }}}{V}=0.1=10 \\%$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38793,'physics',28,1371,3283,'The temperature of a hypothetical ideal gas increases to $\\sqrt{3}$ times when compressed adiabatically to $\\left(\\dfrac{1}{9}\\right)^{\\text {th }}$ the volume. Its equation can be written as $P{V^x} = $ constant, where $x$ is','[{"text": "3.25", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "9.6", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "1.25", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "5.36", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'For adiabatic process,<br>$T{V^{\\gamma  - 1}} = \\,{\\text{constant}}$<br>$\\therefore \\quad \\frac{{{T_2}}}{{{T_1}}} = {\\left( {\\frac{{{V_1}}}{{{V_2}}}} \\right)^{\\gamma  - 1}}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,(1)$<br>But, ${V_2} = \\frac{{{V_1}}}{9}$ and ${T_2} = \\sqrt 3 \\quad {T_1}$<br>$\\therefore \\quad \\sqrt 3  = {(9)^{\\gamma  - 1}}$<br>$\\therefore \\quad {(9)^{\\frac{1}{4}}} = {(9)^{\\gamma  - 1}}$<br>$\\therefore \\gamma  - 1 = \\frac{1}{4}$<br>$\\therefore \\gamma  = 1 + \\frac{1}{4} = \\frac{5}{4}$<br>Also for adiabatic process,$P{V^\\gamma } = $ constant<br>$\\therefore \\quad P{V^{\\frac{5}{4}}} = $ constant<br>$\\therefore \\quad x = \\frac{5}{4} = 1.25$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38794,'physics',29,1301,3148,'The $rms$ velocity of hydrogen molecules at a certain temperature is ${300 {~m} / {s}}$. If the temperature is doubled and hydrogen gas dissociates into atomic hydrogen. The rms speed of the gas molecules  is','[{"text": "$255\\\\,m/s$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$600\\\\,m/s$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$720\\\\,m/s$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$520\\\\,m/s$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'We use  ${\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {v_{rms}} = \\sqrt {\\frac{{3RT}}{M}} $<br>$ \\Rightarrow \\,\\,\\,{\\mkern 1mu} {\\kern 1pt} \\frac{{{v_1}}}{{{v_2}}} = \\sqrt {\\frac{{{T_1}{M_2}}}{{{T_2}{M_1}}}} $<br>$ \\Rightarrow \\,\\,\\,{\\mkern 1mu} {\\kern 1pt} {v_2} = \\sqrt {\\frac{{{T_2}{M_1}}}{{{T_1}{M_2}}}} $<br>$ = 300 \\times {v_1}\\sqrt {\\frac{{2{T_1}{M_1}}}{{{T_1} \\times \\left( {{M_1}/2} \\right)}}}  = 600\\,m/s$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38796,'physics',26,1133,2710,'A $10\\,c{m^3}$ cube floats in water with a volume of  $4\\,c{m^3}$  remaining above the surface. The density of the material from which the cube is made, is','[{"text": "$0.6\\\\,g\\\\,c{m^{ - 3}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$1\\\\,g\\\\,c{m^{ - 3}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$0.4\\\\,g\\\\,c{m^{ - 3}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$0.24\\\\,g\\\\,c{m^{ - 3}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'According to Archimedes\' principle<br>$\\rho_{C} g V_{C}=\\rho_{n} g V_{C S}$<br>where, $\\rho_{C}$ and $\\rho_{w}$ are densities of cube and water respectively; and $V_{C}$ and $V_{C S}$ are volumes of whole cube and submerged cube,respectively.<br>$\\begin{aligned}& \\Rightarrow \\dfrac{\\text { Volume of cube submerged }}{\\text { Total volume of cube }} \\\\& \\quad=\\dfrac{\\text { Density of cube material }}{\\text { Density of water }}\\end{aligned}$<br>$ \\Rightarrow \\frac{{10 - 4}}{{10}} = \\frac{d}{1}$<br>$ \\Rightarrow d = \\frac{6}{{10}} = 0.6\\,g\\,c{m^{ - 3}}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38799,'physics',26,1133,2705,'A liquid $X$ of density $3.36\\,g/c{m^3}$ is poured in a  $U - $ tube in right arm with height $10\\,cm,$ which contains $Hg.$ Another liquid $Y$ is poured in left arm with height $8\\,cm.$ Upper levels of $X$ and $Y$ are same. What is the density of $Y$ ?','[{"text": "$0.8\\\\,g/cc$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$1.2\\\\,g/cc$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1.4\\\\,g/cc$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1.6\\\\,g/cc$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'As shown in figure, in the two arms of a tube atmospheric pressure remains same on surface $p p^{\\prime}$. Hence,<br>$8 \\times \\rho_{Y} \\times g+2 \\times \\rho_{H g} \\times g$<br>$ = 10 \\times {\\rho _X} \\times g$  <img src=\'/img/data/physics/26_38799_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />   $\\therefore \\quad 8 \\rho_{Y}+2 \\times 13.6=10 \\times 3.36$<br>or $\\quad {\\rho _Y} = \\frac{{33.6 - 27.2}}{8} = 0.8\\,g/cc$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/26_38799_question_1.png'),
(38800,'physics',29,1301,3148,'At which temperature the r.m.s velocity of a hydrogen molecule equal to that of an oxygen molecule at ${47^{\\circ} {C}}$ ?','[{"text": "$20\\\\,K$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${4 K}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${-73 K}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${80 K}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Root mean square velocity of gas molecules ${v_{r m s}=\\sqrt{\\dfrac{3 k_{B} T}{m}}}$<br>Given: Temperature of oxygen molecule<br>${=47^{\\circ} C=273+47=320 K}$<br>As per question the rms velocity of hydrogen molecule is equal to that of oxygen molecule at a temperature ${T_{H_{2}}}$, then<br>${v_{{H}_{2}}=v_{{O}_{2}}}$<br>${\\therefore \\sqrt{\\dfrac{3 k_{B} T_{H_{2}}}{m_{H_{2}}}}=\\sqrt{\\dfrac{3 k_{B} T_{O_{2}}}{m_{O_{2}}}} \\Rightarrow \\dfrac{T_{H_{2}}}{m_{H_{2}}}=\\dfrac{T_{O_{2}}}{m_{O_{2}}}}$<br>${\\Rightarrow T_{H_{2}}=\\dfrac{m_{H_{2}}}{m_{O_{2}}} T_{O_{2}}}$<br>Putting the values, we have<br>${T_{{H}_{2}}=\\dfrac{2}{32} \\times 320=20 {~K}}$<br>So, correct option is (1).',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2024,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38801,'physics',28,1371,3286,'An ideal gas undergoes a process such that pressure is a parabolic function of volume with the function having its minima at $P = 5\\,bar,V = 5\\,\\,d{m^3}.$ If the volume is increased from $4\\,\\,d{m^3}$  to $10\\,\\,d{m^3},$ the work done in the process is<br>($1$ bar ${ = {{10}^5}\\,Pa}$)','[{"text": "$4.2\\\\,kJ$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$7.2\\\\,kJ$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$2.5\\\\,kJ$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$9.4\\\\,kJ$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'<img src=\'/img/data/physics/28_38801_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />  The standard equation of a parabola with minima at $(h, k)$ is given as $(y-k)=(x-h)^{2}$. Thus, for given parabola, equation can be given as,<br>$(P - k) = {(V - h)^2}$<br>$\\therefore \\quad (P - 5) = {(V - 5)^2}$<br>$\\therefore \\quad (P - 5) = {V^2} - 10\\,V + 25$<br>$\\therefore \\quad P = {V^2} - 10V + 30$<br>Now, $dW = PdV$<br>$\\therefore W = \\int_4^{10} {\\left( {{V^2} - 10V + 30} \\right)} dV$<br>$W = \\left[ {\\frac{{{V^3}}}{3}} \\right]_4^{10} - 10\\left[ {\\frac{{{V^2}}}{2}} \\right]_4^{10} + 30[\\,V]_4^{10}$<br>$ = \\left( {\\frac{{1000 - 64}}{3}} \\right) - 10\\left( {\\frac{{100 - 16}}{2}} \\right) + 30(10 - 4)$<br>$ = 312 - 420 + 180$<br>$ = 72\\,bar\\,d{m^3}$<br>$ = 72 \\times {10^5} \\times {10^{ - 3}}\\,J$<br>$\\therefore \\quad W = 7.2\\,kJ$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38802,'physics',26,1133,2710,'An ice block floats in a liquid whose density is less than water. A part of block is outside the liquid. When whole of ice has melted, the liquid level will','[{"text": "rise", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "go down", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "remain same", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "first rise then go down", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Ice is lighter than water. When ice melts, the volume occupied by water is less than that of ice, due to which the level of water goes down.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38803,'physics',27,1382,3323,'When the centre of earth is at a distance of ${1.5 \\times 10^{11} {~m}}$ from the centre of sun, the intensity of solar radiation reaching at the earth\'s surface is ${1.26 {~kW} / {m}^{2}}$. There is a spherical cloud of cosmic dust, containing iron particles. The melting point for iron particles in the cloud is $2000\\,K$. Find the distance of iron particles from the centre of sun at which the iron particles start melting  (Assume sun and cloud as a black body, ${\\left.\\sigma=5.6 \\times 10^{-8} {~W} / {m}^{2} {~K}^{4}\\right)}$','[{"text": "$2.18 \\\\times {10^9}\\\\,m$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$6.25 \\\\times {10^9}\\\\,m$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1.60 \\\\times {10^9}\\\\,m$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$3.74 \\\\times {10^9}\\\\,m$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'${d_{1}=}$ Distance between sun and cloud<br>${d_{2}=}$ distance between sun and earth<br>${P_{{S}}=}$ Power of sun ${=1.26 \\times 10^{3} \\times 4 \\pi \\times d_{2}^{2}}$<br>Let the radius of particle is ${r}$ so in equilibrium<br>${P_{emission{\\text{ }}}} = {P_{absorption{\\text{ }}}}$<br>$ \\Rightarrow \\,\\,\\,{\\mkern 1mu} {\\kern 1pt} 4\\sigma {T^4} = \\pi {r^2} \\times \\frac{{{P_S}}}{{4\\pi d_1^2}}$<br>$4\\sigma {T^4} = \\frac{1}{{4\\pi d_1^2}} \\times 1.26 \\times {10^3} \\times 4\\pi d_2^2$ <img src=\'/img/data/physics/27_38803_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' /> $ \\Rightarrow \\,\\,\\,{\\mkern 1mu} {\\kern 1pt} {d_1} = \\frac{{{d_2}}}{{2{T^2}}}\\sqrt {\\frac{{1.26 \\times {{10}^3}}}{{5.6 \\times {{10}^{ - 8}}}}} $<br>$ \\Rightarrow \\,\\,\\,{\\mkern 1mu} {\\kern 1pt} {d_1} = \\frac{{1.5 \\times {{10}^{11}}}}{{2 \\times 4 \\times {{10}^6}}} \\times 15 \\times {10^4}$<br>$ \\Rightarrow \\,\\,\\,{\\mkern 1mu} {\\kern 1pt} {d_1} = 2.81 \\times {10^9}\\,m$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38806,'physics',27,1381,3315,'A student takes 50 $g$ wax (specific heat $=0.6\\,\\, {k\\,\\,cal} / {kg}^{\\circ} {C}$ ) and heats it till it boils. The graph between temperature and time is as follows. Find the heat supplied to the wax per minute.','[{"text": "$1500\\\\,cal$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$1150\\\\,cal$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1896\\\\,cal$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1760\\\\,cal$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'In the region $O A$ temperature of solid is changing with time.In the region $AB$ temperature is constant, so it represents change of state, i,e., melting of solid with melting point ${T_1}$,At $A$ melting starts and at $B$ all solid is converted into liquid. So, between $A$ and $B$ substance is partly solid and partly liquid.<br> In the region $B C$ temperature of liquid increases.<br>Since specific heat $=0.6\\,\\,\\ {kcal} / {g} \\times{ }^{\\circ} {C}$<br>$=0.6\\,\\,\\ {cal} / {g} \\times{ }^{\\circ} {C}$<br>From graph it is clear that in a minute, the temperature is raised from $0^{\\circ} {C}$ to $50^{\\circ} {C}$.<br>$\\Rightarrow$ Heat required for a minute $Q=m c_{s} \\Delta T=50 \\times 0.6 \\times 50$ = 1500 $cal$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/27_38806_question_1.png'),
(38807,'physics',26,1133,2710,'A cube made of material having a density of  $0.9 \\times {10^3}kg\\,{m^{ - 3}}$ floats between water and a liquid of density $0.7 \\times {10^3}kg\\,{m^{ - 3}}$ which is immisicible with water. what part of the cube is immersed in water?','[{"text": "$\\\\dfrac{1}{3}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{2}{3}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{3}{4}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{3}{7}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Let $l=$ side of the cube, <br>$x=$ side of cube immersed in water<br> and $l-x=$ side of cube immersed in liquid.<br> According to law of floatation,<br>$\\begin{array}{r}l^{3} \\times 0.9 \\times 10^{3} \\times g=\\left(l^{2} \\times x\\right) \\times 1000 g \\\\+l^{2}(l-x) \\times 0.7 \\times 10^{3} g \\\\{\\left[\\because V_{1} \\rho_{1} g=V_{2} \\rho_{2} g\\right]} \\\\\\Rightarrow l \\times 0.9=x+(l-x) \\times 0.7\\end{array}$<br>or $0.3 x=0.2 l$ or $\\dfrac{x}{l}=\\dfrac{2}{3}$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38810,'physics',26,1137,2723,'A rain drop of radius $0.3\\,mm$ has a terminal velocity in air $1\\,m{s^{ - 1}}.$ The viscosity of air is $18 \\times 10^{-5}$ poise. Find the viscous force on the rain drops.','[{"text": "$2.05 \\\\times {10^{ - 7}}\\\\,N$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1.018 \\\\times {10^{ - 7}}\\\\,N$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$1.05 \\\\times {10^{ - 7}}\\\\,N$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2.058 \\\\times {10^{ - 7}}\\\\,N$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Here,<br>$r = 0.3\\,mm = 0.3 \\times {10^{ - 3}}\\,m,v = 1\\,m{s^{ - 1}}$<br>$\\eta=18 \\times 10^{-5}$ poise<br>$=18 \\times 10^{-6}$ deca poise<br>Viscous force, $F=6 \\pi \\eta r v$<br>$=6 \\times \\dfrac{22}{7} \\times\\left(18 \\times 10^{-6}\\right) \\times\\left(0.3 \\times 10^{-3}\\right) \\times 1$<br>$ = 1.018 \\times {10^{ - 7}}\\,N$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38811,'physics',28,1377,3303,'Rigid vessel of negligible mass containing $3$ moles of hydrogen gas is set free from a rough inclined plane of length $\\sqrt 3 \\,km$ and angle of inclination $60^{\\circ}$. One third of heat due to friction is absorbed by the hydrogen gas. If the vessel stops just before reaching the bottom of the incline, then change in temperature of the hydrogen gas is<br>(Take $R = 8.0\\,J\\,mo{l^{ - 1}}\\,{K^{ - 1}},{C_{di}} = \\frac{5}{2}R$)','[{"text": "$0.49^\\\\circ C$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$0.95^\\\\circ C$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$0.05^\\\\circ C$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$0.35^\\\\circ C$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'<img src=\'/img/data/physics/28_38811_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />  The loss in potential energy due to friction will be converted to heat.<br>Potential energy loss $=$ Heat loss due to friction<br>i.e., heat loss $ = mgh = mgl\\sin \\alpha $<br>As, the vessel is rigid,<br>$\\Delta V = 0 \\Rightarrow \\Delta W = 0$<br>$\\therefore\\,\\,\\, \\Delta Q = \\Delta U$<br>Since, ${\\frac{1}{3}^{{\\text{rd }}}}$  of the loss is absorbed by gas,<br>$\\therefore \\quad \\frac{1}{3}mgl\\sin \\alpha  = \\frac{5}{2}nR\\,(\\Delta T)$<br>$\\therefore \\quad \\Delta T = \\frac{{2\\,mg\\,l\\,\\sin \\,\\alpha }}{{15\\,nR}} = \\frac{{2{M_0}\\,gl\\,\\sin \\,\\alpha }}{{15R}}$<br>$\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\left( {\\because n = \\frac{m}{{{M_0}}}} \\right)$<br>Where ${M_0} = $  molecular weight of ${H_2} = 2\\,g$<br>$\\therefore \\quad \\Delta T = \\frac{{2 \\times 2 \\times {{10}^{ - 3}} \\times 9.8 \\times \\sqrt 3  \\times {{10}^3} \\times \\sqrt 3 }}{{15 \\times 8.0 \\times 2}}$<br>$ = \\frac{{2 \\times 9.8}}{{5 \\times 8.0}}$<br>$\\therefore \\quad \\Delta T = 0.49^\\circ C$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38812,'physics',26,1136,2715,'At what speed will the velocity of a stream of water be equal to \\[20\\;cm\\] of mercury column ?  (Take, \\[g = 10\\;m{s^{ - 2}}\\] )<div class="matching-question"></div>','[{"text": "$6.4\\\\,m{s^{ - 1}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$7.3756\\\\,\\\\,m{s^{ - 1}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$6.4756\\\\,m{s^{ - 1}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "None of these", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Here, velocity head  $ = 20\\,cm$ of $H g$<br>$=20 \\times 13.6 \\mathrm{~cm}$ of water<br>As, velocity head $=v^{2} / 2 g$<br>$\\therefore 20 \\times 13.6=\\dfrac{v^{2}}{2 \\times 1000}$<br>$v=\\sqrt{20 \\times 13.6 \\times 2 \\times 1000}$<br>$ = 737.56\\,cm{s^{ - 1}} = 7.3756\\,\\,m{s^{ - 1}}$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38813,'physics',27,1380,3307,'A second\'s pendulum clock has a steel wire. The clock is calibrated at $20^{\\circ} {C}$. How much time   does the clock lose or gain in one week when the temperature is increased to $30^{\\circ} {C} ?\\left(\\alpha_{\\text {steel }}=1.2 \\times 10^{-5}{ }^{\\circ}\\,\\, {C}^{-1}\\right)$.','[{"text": "$23.57\\\\,s$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$36.28\\\\,s$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$46.25\\\\,s$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$27.65\\\\,s$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'The time period of second s pendulum is 2 $sec$ . As the temperature increases length time period increases.Clock becomes slow and it loses the time.The change in time period is<br>$\\Delta T = \\frac{1}{2}T\\alpha \\Delta \\theta  = \\left( {\\frac{1}{2}} \\right)(2)\\left( {1.2 \\times {{10}^{ - 5}}} \\right)$<br>$\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,({\\rm{ }}30^\\circ  - 20^\\circ ){\\rm{ }}$<br>$ = 1.2 \\times {10^{ - 4}}\\,s$<br>$\\therefore $New time period is<br>$T\' = T + \\Delta T = \\left( {2 + 1.2 \\times {{10}^{ - 4}}} \\right) = 2.00012\\,s$<br>Time lost in one week<br>$\\Delta  = \\left( {\\frac{{\\Delta T}}{{{T^\\prime }}}} \\right)t = \\frac{{\\left( {1.2 \\times {{10}^{ - 4}}} \\right)}}{{(2.00012)}}$<br>${\\rm{(}}7 \\times 24 \\times 3600{\\rm{)}} = 36.28\\,s$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38878,'physics',26,1138,2728,'Two mercury drops (each of radius $r$) merge to form a bigger drop. The surface energy of the bigger drop, if $T$ is the surface tension, is','[{"text": "${2^{5/3}}\\\\pi \\\\,{r^2}\\\\,T$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$4\\\\,\\\\pi \\\\,{r^2}\\\\,T$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2\\\\,{\\\\mkern 1mu} \\\\pi \\\\,{r^2}\\\\,T$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${2^{8/3}}\\\\pi \\\\,{r^2}\\\\,T$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'$\\dfrac{4}{3} \\pi R^{3}=2 \\times \\dfrac{4}{3} \\pi r^{3} \\Rightarrow \\mathrm{R}=2^{1 / 3} r$<br>Surface energy of bigger drop,<br>$E = 4\\,\\pi {R^2}T = 4 \\times {2^{2/3}}\\pi {r^2}T = {2^{8/3}}\\pi {r^2}T$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38816,'physics',26,1135,2714,'Water flows through a horizontal pipe of variable cross-section at the rate of  $20\\,L\\,{\\min ^{ - 1}}$ What will be the velocity of water at a point where diameter is  $4\\,cm$ ?','[{"text": "$0.2639\\\\,m{s^{ - 1}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$0.5639\\\\,m{s^{ - 1}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$0.4639\\\\,m{s^{ - 1}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$0.3639\\\\,m{s^{ - 1}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Volume of the water flowing per second, <br>$V = 20\\,L\\,{\\min ^{ - 1}}$<br>$ = \\frac{{20 \\times 1000}}{{60 \\times {{(100)}^3}}}\\,{m^3}\\,{s^{ - 1}} = \\frac{1}{3} \\times {10^{ - 3}}\\,{m^3}\\,{s^{ - 1}}$<br>Radius of the pipe,<br>$r = \\frac{4}{2} = 2\\,cm = 0.02\\,m$<br>Area of cross-section,<br>$A=\\pi r^{2}=\\dfrac{22}{7} \\times(0.02)^{2} m^{2}$<br>Let $v$ be the velocity of the flow of water at the given point. Clearly, $V=A v$<br>or $\\dfrac{1}{3} \\times 10^{-3}=\\dfrac{22}{7} \\times(0.02)^{2} \\times v$<br>$v = \\frac{{7 \\times {{10}^{ - 3}}}}{{3 \\times 22 \\times {{(0.02)}^2}}} \\simeq 0.2639\\,m{s^{ - 1}}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38817,'physics',28,1371,3291,'$7$ moles of a monoatomic ideal gas is enclosed in an adiabatic, vertical cylinder fitted with a smooth, light adiabatic piston. The piston is connected to a vertical spring of spring constant $420\\,N{\\rm{/}}m.$ The area of cross-section of the cylinder is $35\\,c{m^2}.$ Initially, the spring is at its natural length and the temperature of the gas is $30^\\circ C.$ The atmospheric pressure is ${10^5}\\,Pa.$ The gas is heated slowly for some time by means of an electric heater so as to move the piston up through $20\\,cm.$ Find the work done by the gas','[{"text": "$63.2\\\\,J$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$78.4\\\\,J$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$47.5\\\\,J$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$96.2\\\\,J$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'The work done by the gas as the piston moves through $l = 20\\,cm$ is<br>$W = \\int_0^1 {Fdx} $<br>Here the net force acting on the piston is,<br>$F = {P_a}A + kx$<br>$\\therefore \\quad W = {P_a}Al + \\frac{1}{2}k{l^2}$<br>$ = \\left( {{{10}^5}} \\right) \\times \\left( {35 \\times {{10}^{ - 4}}} \\right) \\times \\left( {20 \\times {{10}^{ - 2}}} \\right) + $<br>$\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\frac{1}{2}(420) \\times \\left( {400 \\times {{10}^{ - 4}}} \\right)$<br>$ = 70 + 8.4$<br>$\\therefore \\quad W = 78.4\\,J$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38818,'physics',26,1135,2714,'A liquid is allowed into a tube of truncated cone shape. Identify the correct statement from the following.','[{"text": "The speed is high at the wider end and low at the narrow end.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "The speed is low at the wider end and high at the narrow end.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "The speed is same at both ends in a streamline flow.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "The liquid flows with uniform velocity in the tube.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'For an incompressible liquid equation of continuity,<br>$A v=$ constant<br> $A \\propto \\dfrac{1}{v}$<br>where,<br>$A=$ area of cross-section of tube and <br>$v=$ speed of liquid.<br>So, the speed of liquid flow is not uniform, but changes with area of cross-section of tube.Therefore, at the wider end speed will be low and at the narrow end speed will be high.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38819,'physics',27,1381,3315,'A container with negligible heat capacity has 100 $g$ ice at $0^{\\circ} {C}$ is suspended in a room where temperature is $35^{\\circ} {C}$. It was found that the entire ice melted in 10 hours. Now the same container having 100 $g$ of water at $0^{\\circ} {C}$ is suspended in the same room. How much time (in $min$) will it take for the temperature of water to rise to $0.5^{\\circ} {C}$. Specific heat of water and specific latent heat of fusion of ice are $1\\,\\, {cal}\\,\\, {g}^{-1}{ }^{\\circ} {C}^{-1}$ and $80\\,\\, {cal}\\,\\, {g}^{-1}$ respectively.','[{"text": "1.27", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "5.68", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "3.75", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "10.26", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Rate of heat gain for the container and its content $=\\dfrac{m L}{10 {hr}}$<br>Since conditions are unchanged, rate of heat gain will remain same for water filled container.<br>$\\therefore m s_{w} \\dfrac{\\Delta \\theta}{\\Delta t}=\\dfrac{m L}{10 {hr}}$<br>$\\therefore \\Delta t = \\frac{{10 \\times {s_w} \\times \\Delta \\theta }}{L} = \\frac{{10 \\times 1 \\times 0.5}}{{80}}$<br>$ = \\frac{1}{{16}}hr = 3.75\\,\\min $<br>So correct option is (3).',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38822,'physics',26,1137,2723,'What is the velocity $v$ of a metallic ball of radius $r$ falling in a tank of liquid at the instant when its acceleration is one-half that of a freely falling body?(The densities of metal and of liquid are $\\rho$ and $\\sigma$, respectively and the viscosity of liquid is $\\eta$ )','[{"text": "$\\\\dfrac{r^{2} g}{9 \\\\eta}(\\\\rho-2 \\\\sigma)$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{r^{2} g}{9 \\\\eta}(2 \\\\rho-\\\\sigma)$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{r^{2} g}{9 \\\\eta}(\\\\rho-\\\\sigma)$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{2 r^{2} g}{9 \\\\eta}(\\\\rho-\\\\sigma)$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Net force on the ball = downward<br> force - upward force $=\\dfrac{m g}{2}$  <img src=\'/img/data/physics/26_38822_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />  ${\\frac{4}{3}\\pi {r^3}(\\rho  - \\sigma )g - 6\\pi \\eta rv = \\frac{{mg}}{2}}$<br>${\\frac{4}{3}\\pi {r^3}(\\rho  - \\sigma )g - 6\\pi \\eta rv = \\frac{1}{2}\\left( {\\frac{4}{3}\\pi {r^3}\\rho } \\right)g}$<br>${r^3}(\\rho  - \\sigma )g - \\frac{9}{2}\\eta v = \\frac{1}{2}{r^2}\\rho g$<br>$\\frac{9}{2}v\\eta  = {r^2}(\\rho  - \\sigma )g - \\frac{1}{2}{r^2}\\rho g$<br>$ = \\frac{1}{2}{r^2}\\rho g - {r^2}\\sigma g$<br>$\\frac{9}{2}v\\eta  = {r^2}g(\\rho  - 2\\,\\sigma )$<br>$ \\Rightarrow \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,v = \\frac{{{r^2}g}}{{9\\eta }}(\\rho  - 2\\,\\sigma )$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38823,'physics',28,1371,3284,'When $1\\,g$ of water is frozen at $0^\\circ C,$ under normal atmospheric pressure, its volume changes from $1cc$ to $1.083\\,cc.$ The change in its internal energy is $ - 800.022 \\times {10^x}\\,cal$. Find the value of $x$.','[{"text": "5", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$ - 3$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$ - 1$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$ - 7$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Given that, $ = 1\\,g$<br> Heat released by $1\\,g$ of ice on freezing,<br>$\\Delta Q = mL = 1 \\times 80 = 80\\,cal$<br>As, this heat is given out by water, it is taken negative.<br>$\\therefore \\quad \\Delta Q =  - 80\\,cal$<br>Now, $\\Delta V = {V_2} - V$<br>$ = 1.083 - 1.000$<br>$ = 0.083\\,cc$<br>$ = 0.083 \\times {10^{ - 6}}\\,{m^3}$<br>The external work done by water,<br>$\\Delta W = P(\\Delta V)$<br>$ = 1.013 \\times {10^5} \\times 0.083 \\times {10^{ - 6}}$<br>$ = 0.0084\\,J$<btr>$ = \\frac{{0.0084}}{{4.2}}cal$<br>$ = 0.002cal$<br>From first law of thermodynamics,<br>$\\Delta U = \\Delta Q - \\Delta W$<br>$ =  - 80 - 0.002$<br>$ =  - 80.002cal$<br>$ =  - 800.02 \\times {10^{ - 1}}cal$<br>$\\therefore x =  - 1$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38824,'physics',26,1137,2723,'Two raindrops of same radius $r$ falling with terminal velocity $v$ merge and from a bigger drop of radius $R$. The terminal velocity of the bigger drop is','[{"text": "$v \\\\dfrac{R}{r}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$v \\\\dfrac{R^{2}}{r^{2}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$v$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2 v$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Terminal velocity, $v=\\dfrac{2}{9} \\dfrac{r^{2}(\\rho-\\sigma) g}{\\eta}$<br>$\\therefore \\quad v \\propto r^{2}$<br>$\\Rightarrow \\quad \\dfrac{v}{V}=\\dfrac{r^{2}}{R^{2}} \\Rightarrow V=\\dfrac{v R^{2}}{r^{2}}$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38830,'physics',27,1381,3315,'In a calorimeter there is 5 $kg$ of water at $0^{\\circ} {C}$ mixed to an unknown mass of ice and the mixture is in thermal equilibrium. The water equivalent of the calorimeter is 250 $g$ . At time $t=0$, a heater is switched on which supplies heat at a constant rate to the calorimeter. The temperature of the mixture is measured at various times and the result has been plotted in the given figure. Neglect any heat loss from the mixture calorimeter system to the surrounding and calculate the initial mass of the ice. Given: Specific latent heat of fusion of ice is $L_{f}=80\\,\\, {cal}\\,\\, {g}^{-1}$ Specific heat capacity of water $=1\\,\\, {cal}\\,\\, {g}^{-1}{ }^{\\circ} {C}^{-1}$','[{"text": "$250\\\\,g$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$580\\\\,g$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$920\\\\,g$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$750\\\\,g$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Let the mass of ice in calorimeter be $m$.<br>Rate of heat supplied by heater in first 50 $min$,<br>$\\frac{{dQ}}{{dt}} = \\frac{{m{L_f}}}{{50\\,\\min }} = \\frac{{80\\,m}}{{50}}\\frac{{cal}}{{\\min }}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{\\rm{(}}1{\\rm{)}}$<br>This heat supplied in first 50 $min$ is used to melt the ice.<br>Now considering from $t=50 {~min}$ to 60 $min$<br>$\\frac{{dQ}}{{dt}} = \\left( {\\frac{\\begin{gathered}  (m + 5000 + 250) \\times  \\hfill \\\\\n  (1\\,\\,{\\text{ca}}{{\\text{l}}^{ - 1}}{\\text{g}}^\\circ {{\\text{C}}^{ - 1}}) \\times (2^\\circ {\\text{C)}} \\hfill \\\\ \\end{gathered} }{{10\\,{\\text{min}}}}} \\right)\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,(2)$<br>From (1) and (2)<br>$\\frac{{80m}}{{50}} = \\frac{{(5250 + m) \\times 2}}{{10}}$<br>$16\\,m = 10500 + 2m$<br>$ \\Rightarrow 14m = 10500$<br>$m = 750\\,g$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/27_38830_question_1.png'),
(38831,'physics',26,1137,2723,'An air bubble of radius $1\\,cm$ rises from the bottom portion through a liquid of density $1.5\\,g\\,c{c^{ - 1}}$ at a constant speed of  $0.25{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} cm\\,{s^{ - 1}}.$ If the density of air is neglected, the coefficient of viscosity of the liquid is approximately (in $P a$ ).','[{"text": "13000", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "1300", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "130", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "13", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Terminal velocity,<br>$v=\\dfrac{2}{9} \\dfrac{r^{2} \\rho g}{\\eta} \\Rightarrow \\eta=\\dfrac{2}{9} \\cdot \\dfrac{r^{2} \\rho g}{v}$<br>$=\\dfrac{2}{9} \\dfrac{\\left(1 \\times 10^{-2}\\right)^{2} \\times\\left(1.5 \\times 10^{3}\\right) \\times 9.8}{0.25 \\times 10^{-2}}$<br>$=130 \\mathrm{~Pa}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38832,'physics',28,1371,3290,'The two conducting cylinder-piston systems shown below are linked. Cylinder 1 is filled with a certain molar quantity of a monatomic ideal gas, and cylinder 2 is filled with an equal molar quantity of a diatomic ideal gas. The entire apparatus is situated inside an oven whose temperature is ${T_{a}=27^{\\circ} {C}}$. The cylinder volumes have the same initial value ${V_{0}=100 {cc}}$. When the oven temperature is slowly raised to ${T_{b}=127^{\\circ} {C}}$. The volume change ${\\Delta V}$ (in $cc$) of cylinder 1 is','[{"text": "5", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "7", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "0", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "3", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'${P_{0} V_{0}=n R T_{a}}$<br>$\\left( {{P_0} + \\Delta \\,P} \\right)\\left( {{V_0} + \\Delta V} \\right) = n\\,R{T_b}$<br>$\\left( {{P_0} + \\Delta \\,P} \\right)\\left( {{V_0} - \\Delta V} \\right) = n\\,R{T_b}$<br>$\\Delta \\,V = 0$<br>Pressure depends on number of moles and is independent of nature of gas.',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/28_38832_question_1.png'),
(38834,'physics',26,1137,2723,'Two equal drops of water are falling through air with a steady velocity $v$. If the drops coalesced what will be the new velocity?','[{"text": "$(2)^{1 / 3} v$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$(2)^{3 / 2} v$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$(2)^{2 / 3} v$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$(2)^{1 / 4} v$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Let $r$ be the radius of the each drop. The terminal velocity of drop will be given by<br>$v = \\frac{2}{9}\\frac{{{r^2}(\\rho  - \\sigma )g}}{\\eta }\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,(1)$<br>where, $\\rho$ is density of drop and $\\sigma$ is density of viscous medium of coefficient of viscosity $\\eta$. When two drops each of radius $r$ coalesce to form a new drop, then the radius of coalesced drop will be<br>$\\begin{aligned}\\dfrac{4}{3} \\pi r^{3} \\times 2 & =\\dfrac{4}{3} \\pi R^{3} \\\\\\Rightarrow \\quad R & =(2)^{1 / 3} r\\end{aligned}$<br>Hence, new terminal velocity of coalesced drop will be<br>$\\begin{equation*}v^{\\prime}=\\dfrac{2}{9}\\left[\\dfrac{\\left(2^{1 / 3} r\\right)^{2}(\\rho-\\sigma) g}{\\eta}\\right] \\tag{2}\\end{equation*}$<br>From Eqs. (1) and (2), we get<br>$\\begin{aligned}& \\dfrac{v^{\\prime}}{v}=(2)^{2 / 3} \\\\& v^{\\prime}=(2)^{2 / 3} v\\end{aligned}$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38835,'physics',28,1371,3292,'A cylinder of cross-section area ${A}$ has two pistons of negligible mass separated by distances ${l}$ loaded with spring of negligible mass. An ideal gas at temperature ${T_{1}}$ is in the cylinder where the springs are relaxed. When the gas is heated by some means its temperature becomes ${T_{2}}$ and the springs get compressed by ${\\dfrac{l}{2}}$ each. If ${P_{0}}$ is atmospheric pressure and spring constant ${k=\\dfrac{2 P_{0} A}{l}}$, then the ratio of ${T_{2}}$ and ${T_{1}}$ is','[{"text": "2", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "7", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "1", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "4", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'${\\dfrac{P_{1} V_{1}}{T_{1}}=\\dfrac{P_{2} V_{2}}{T_{2}}}$ where ${V_{1}=l A}$ and ${V_{2}=2 l A}$ and ${P_{1}=P_{0}}$ and<br>$P_{2}=\\dfrac{k x}{A}+P_{0}=2 P_{0}$<br>${\\Rightarrow \\dfrac{T_{2}}{T_{1}}=\\dfrac{P_{2} V_{2}}{P_{1} V_{1}}=4}$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/28_38835_question_1.png'),
(38836,'physics',26,1137,2723,'The terminal velocity of small sized spherical body of radius $r$ falling vertically in a viscous liquid is given by the following proportionality','[{"text": "$1 / r^{2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1 / r$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$r$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$r^{2}$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'According to formula,<br>$v=\\dfrac{2 r^{2}(\\rho-\\sigma) g}{9 \\eta}$<br>Hence, $v \\propto r^{2}$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38840,'physics',26,1136,2715,'The reading of a manometer fitted to a closed tap is  $3.5 \\times {10^5}\\,N{m^{ - 2}}.$  If the valve is opened, the reading of the manometer falls to $3 \\times {10^5}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} N{m^{ - 2}}.$ The velocity of water is','[{"text": "$1\\\\,m{s^{ - 1}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$10\\\\,m{s^{ - 1}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$100\\\\,m{s^{ - 1}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$0.1\\\\,m{s^{ - 1}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'According to Bernoulli\'s theorem,<br>$p_{1}+\\dfrac{1}{2} \\rho v_{1}^{2}=p_{2}+\\dfrac{1}{2} \\rho v_{2}^{2}$<br>$\\Rightarrow \\dfrac{1}{2} \\rho v_{2}^{2}=\\left(p_{1}-p_{2}\\right)+\\dfrac{1}{2} \\rho v_{1}^{2}$<br>$=\\left(p_{1}-p_{2}\\right) \\quad\\left(\\because v_{1}=0\\right)$<br>$\\Rightarrow \\quad v_{2}=\\sqrt{\\dfrac{2\\left(p_{1}-p_{2}\\right)}{\\rho}}$<br>$\\Rightarrow \\quad v_{2}=\\sqrt{\\dfrac{2 \\times\\left(3.5 \\times 10^{5}-3 \\times 10^{5}\\right)}{10^{3}}}$<br>$\\Rightarrow \\quad v_{2}=10 m s^{-1}$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38843,'physics',26,1136,2717,'A hole is in the bottom of the tank having water. If total pressure at the bottom is$3\\,{\\rm{atm}}\\left( {1\\,{\\rm{atm}} = {{10}^5}\\,\\,N{m^{ - 2}}} \\right)$, then velocity of water flowing from hole is','[{"text": "$\\\\sqrt {400} \\\\,m{s^{ - 1}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\sqrt {600} \\\\,m{s^{ - 1}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\sqrt {60} \\\\,m{s^{ - 1}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "None of these", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Let height of water column in the tank be $h$.<br>Total pressure $(p)=$ atmospheric pressure$\\left(p_{0}\\right)+$ pressure due to water column in tank $\\left(p^{\\prime}\\right)$<br>$\\therefore p^{\\prime}=p-p_{0}=3-1=2 \\mathrm{~atm}$<br>or $\\quad h \\rho g=2 \\times 10^{5}$<br>or $h \\times 10^{3} \\times 10=2 \\times 10^{5}$<br>or  $\\quad h = 20\\,m$<br>Hence, velocity of water coming from hole, i.e. velocity of efflux is<br>$v = \\sqrt {2gh}  = \\sqrt {2 \\times 10 \\times 20}  = \\sqrt {400} \\,m{s^{ - 1}}$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38845,'physics',26,1136,2715,'According to Bernoulli\'s equation,  $\\dfrac{p}{\\rho g}+h+\\dfrac{1}{2} \\dfrac{v^{2}}{g}=\\text { constant }$<br>The terms $A\\left(=\\dfrac{p}{\\rho g}\\right), B(=h)$ and $C\\left(=\\dfrac{v^{2}}{2 g}\\right)$ are generally called respectively','[{"text": "gravitational head, pressure head and velocity head", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "gravity, gravitational head and velocity head", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "pressure head, gravitational head and velocity head", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "gravity, pressure and velocity head", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'According to Bernoulli\'s theorem,in case of steady flow of incompressible and non-viscous liquid through a tube of non-uniform cross-section, the sum of the pressure, the potential energy per unit volume and the kinetic energy per unit volume is same at every point in the tube, i.e.<br>$p+\\rho g h+\\dfrac{1}{2} \\rho v^{2}=$ constant<br>Dividing this expression by $\\rho g$, we have<br>$\\dfrac{p}{\\rho g}+\\dfrac{v^{2}}{2 g}+h=$ constant<br>In this expression $\\dfrac{p}{p g}$ is called the<br>\'pressure head\', $\\dfrac{v^{2}}{2 g}$ the \'velocity head\' and $h$ the \'gravitational head\'.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38847,'physics',28,1372,3293,'One mole of a monoatomic ideal gas follows a process ${A B}$, as shown. The specific heat of the process is ${\\dfrac{13 R}{6}}$. The value of ${n}$ on ${P}$-axis is','[{"text": "6", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "8", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "11", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "10", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'$C = \\frac{{13R}}{6} \\Rightarrow {C_v} = \\frac{3}{2}R$<br>$C = \\frac{{\\Delta Q}}{{\\Delta T}} = \\frac{{(\\Delta U + \\Delta W)}}{{\\Delta T}}$<br>$ = \\frac{{\\Delta U}}{{\\Delta T}} + \\frac{{\\Delta W}}{{\\Delta T}} = {C_v} + \\frac{{\\Delta W}}{{\\Delta T}}$<br>So, ${\\quad \\dfrac{\\Delta W}{\\Delta T}=C-C_{v}=\\dfrac{13 R}{6}-\\dfrac{3 R}{2}=\\dfrac{2 R}{3}}$<br>$\\Delta T = {T_2} - {T_1}$<br>$ = \\frac{{\\left( {n{P_0}} \\right)\\left( {5{V_0}} \\right)}}{R} - \\frac{{\\left( {3{P_0}} \\right){V_0}}}{R} = \\frac{{{P_0}{V_0}}}{R}(5n - 3)$<br>$\\Delta W = \\frac{{\\left( {5{V_0} - {V_0}} \\right)\\left( {n{P_0} + 3{P_0}} \\right)}}{2} = 2{P_0}{V_0}(n + 3)$<br>$\\frac{{\\Delta W}}{{\\Delta T}} = \\frac{{2(n + 3)R}}{{(5n - 3)}} = \\frac{{2R}}{3}$<br>On solving, ${n=6}$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/28_38847_question_1.png'),
(38849,'physics',26,1138,2728,'The work done in blowing a soap bubble of surface tension  $0.06\\,\\,N{m^{ - 1}}$ from $2\\,cm$ radius to $5\\,cm$ radius is','[{"text": "$0.004168\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,J$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{0}}{\\\\rm{.003168}}\\\\,\\\\,J$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{0}}{\\\\rm{.003158}}\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\rm{ }}J$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{0}}{\\\\rm{.004568}}\\\\,\\\\,J$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Here, $S = 0.06\\,\\,N{m^{ - 1}}$<br>${r_1} = 2\\,cm = 0.02\\,m,\\,\\,{r_2} = 5\\,cm = 0.05\\,m$<br>Since, bubble has two surfaces.<br>Initial surface area of the bubble<br>$=2 \\times 4 \\pi r_{1}^{2}=2 \\times 4 \\pi(0.02)^{2}$<br>$ = 32\\pi  \\times {10^{ - 4}}\\,{m^2}$<br>Final surface area of the bubble<br>$=2 \\times 4 \\pi r_{2}^{2}=2 \\times 4 \\pi(0.05)^{2}$<br>$ = 200\\pi  \\times {10^{ - 4}}\\,{m^2}$<br>Increase in surface area<br>$=200 \\pi \\times 10^{-4}-32 \\pi \\times 10^{-4}$<br>$ = 168\\,\\pi  \\times {10^{ - 4}}\\,{m^2}$<br>$\\therefore$ Work done $=$ Surface tension $(S)$ $\\times$ Increase in surface area<br>$0.06 \\times 168\\,\\,\\pi  \\times {10^{ - 4}} = 0.003168\\,J$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38853,'physics',26,1138,2730,'Two soap bubbles coalesce. It is noticed that, whilst Joined together, the radii of the two bubbles are $a$ and $b$ where $a>b$. Then, the radius of curvature of interface between the two bubbles will be','[{"text": "$a-b$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$a+b$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{a b}{(a-b)}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{a b}{(a+b)}$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'According to given figure,Let the radius of curvature of the common internal film surface of the <img src=\'/img/data/physics/26_38853_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />  double bubble formed by two bubbles be $r$. Excess of pressure as compared to atmosphere inside $A$ is $(4 T / a)$. In double bubble, the pressure difference between $A$ and $B$ on either side of the common surface<br>$=\\dfrac{4 T}{b}-\\dfrac{4 T}{a}$<br>This will be equal to $(4 T / r)$.<br>$\\begin{aligned}& \\therefore \\quad \\dfrac{4 T}{r}=\\dfrac{4 T}{b}-\\dfrac{4 T}{a} \\\\& \\text { or } \\dfrac{1}{r}=\\left(\\dfrac{1}{b}-\\dfrac{1}{a}\\right)=\\dfrac{a-b}{b a} \\\\& \\text { or } \\quad r=\\dfrac{a b}{a-b}\\end{aligned}$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38857,'physics',26,1138,2728,'A liquid drop of diameter $D$ breaks into 27 small drops of equal size. If the surface tension of the liquid is $\\sigma$, then change in surface energy is','[{"text": "$\\\\pi D^{2} \\\\sigma$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2 \\\\pi D^{2} \\\\sigma$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$3 \\\\pi D^{2} \\\\sigma$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$4 \\\\pi D^{2} \\\\sigma$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Work done, $W=T \\times \\Delta A$<br>We know that, work done in splitting $n$ liquid drops,<br>$W=4 \\pi \\sigma R^{2}\\left[n^{1 / 3}-1\\right]$<br>$=4 \\pi \\sigma\\left[\\dfrac{D}{2}\\right]^{2}\\left[(27)^{1 / 3}-1\\right]$<br>$=\\pi D^{2} \\sigma\\left[(27)^{1 / 3}-1\\right]=2 \\pi D^{2} \\sigma$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38861,'physics',26,1138,2730,'A bubble of  $8\\,mm$  diameter is formed in the air. The surface tension of soap solution is $30\\,{\\mkern 1mu} dyne/cm.$ The excess pressure inside the bubble is','[{"text": "$150\\\\,{\\\\mkern 1mu} dyne/c{m^2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$300\\\\,{\\\\mkern 1mu} dyne/c{m^2}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$3 \\\\times {10^3}{\\\\mkern 1mu} dyne/c{m^2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$12\\\\,{\\\\mkern 1mu} dyne/c{m^2}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'The excess pressure inside the bubble,<br>$\\Delta \\,p = \\frac{{4T}}{r} = \\frac{{4 \\times 30}}{{0.4}} = 300{\\rm{ }}dyne/c{m^2}$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38882,'physics',26,1138,2732,'Two circular plates of radius $5\\,cm$ each, have a $0.01\\,mm$ thick water film between them. Then what will be the force required to separate these plate (S.T. of water $ = 73\\,dyne{\\rm{/}}cm$)?','[{"text": "$125\\\\,N$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$95\\\\,N$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$115\\\\,N$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$105\\\\,N$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'$F = \\frac{{2AT}}{d} = \\frac{{2 \\times \\pi  \\times {{(0.05)}^2} \\times 73 \\times {{10}^{ - 3}}}}{{0.01 \\times {{10}^{ - 3}}}}$<br>$ = 36.5\\,{\\mkern 1mu} \\pi  \\approx 115\\,\\,{\\rm{newton}}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38862,'physics',28,1371,3286,'A gas consisting of rigid diatomic molecules was expanded in a polytropic process so that the rate of collisions of the molecules against the vessel\'s wall did not change. Find the molar heat capacity of the gas in this process is found to be \'${x R}$\', the value of ${x}$ is','[{"text": "$1\\\\,R$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$7\\\\,R$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$3\\\\,R$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$8\\\\,R$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Rate of collisions with vessel wall is<br>${N_C} = \\frac{1}{6}{n_0}{V_{rms}} = \\frac{1}{6}\\left( {\\frac{N}{V}} \\right)\\sqrt {\\frac{{3RT}}{M}} $<br>As the rate of collisions with vessel wall is constant<br>${\\Rightarrow \\quad N_{c} \\propto \\dfrac{\\sqrt{T}}{V}=}$ constant<br>${\\Rightarrow \\quad \\dfrac{T}{V^{2}}=}$ Constant$\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\left( 1 \\right)$<br>As ${P V=n R T \\Rightarrow \\dfrac{T}{P V}=}$ Constant$\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\left( 2 \\right)$<br>Hence from (1) and (2); ${P V^{-1}=}$ constant<br>Molar heat capacity is given as<br>$C = \\frac{R}{{\\gamma  - 1}} + \\frac{R}{{1 - n}} = {C_v} + \\frac{R}{{1 - n}}{\\text{ }}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\left( 3 \\right)$<br>$ \\Rightarrow \\,\\,\\,{\\mkern 1mu} {\\kern 1pt} {\\text{ }}C = \\frac{{5R}}{2} + \\frac{R}{{1 - ( - 1)}} = 3R$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38864,'physics',27,1382,3319,'Two identical conducting rods are first connected independently to two vessels, one containing water at $100^{\\circ} {C}$ and the other containing ice at $0^{\\circ} {C}$. In the second case, the rods are joined end to end and connected to the same vessels. Let $q_{1}$ and $q_{2} {~g} / {s}$ be the rate of melting of ice in two cases respectively. The ratio of $q_{1} / q_{2}$ is','[{"text": "$\\\\frac{4}{1}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{2}{4}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{5}{2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{9}{1}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Initially the rods are placed in vessels as shown below.  <img src=\'/img/data/physics/27_38864_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />  $\\frac{Q}{t} = \\frac{{\\left( {{\\theta _1} - {\\theta _2}} \\right)}}{R}$<br>$ \\Rightarrow {\\left( {\\frac{Q}{t}} \\right)_1} = \\frac{{mL}}{t} = {q_1}L = \\frac{{(100 - 0)}}{{\\frac{R}{2}}}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,(1)$<br>Finally when rods are joined end to end as shown  <img src=\'/img/data/physics/27_38864_explanation_2.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />  $ \\Rightarrow {\\left( {\\frac{Q}{t}} \\right)_2} = \\frac{{mL}}{t} = {q_2}L = \\left( {\\frac{{100 - 0}}{{2R}}} \\right)\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,(2)$<br>From Eqs. (1) and (2), $\\dfrac{q_{1}}{q_{2}}=\\dfrac{4}{1}$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38865,'physics',26,1138,2730,'Two soap bubbles have radii in the ratio of $2: 1$. What is the ratio of excess pressures inside them?','[{"text": "$1: 2$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$1: 4$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2: 1$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$4: 1$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Excess pressure inside the bubble is given by, $p=4 T / r$<br>Then,<br>${p_1} = 4T/{r_1}\\quad \\quad \\quad \\,(1)$<br>${p_2} = 4T/{r_2}\\quad \\quad \\quad (2)$<br>From Eqs. (1) and (2), we have<br>$\\dfrac{p_{1}}{p_{2}}=\\dfrac{4 T / r_{1}}{4 T / r_{2}}=\\dfrac{r_{2}}{r_{1}}=\\dfrac{1}{2}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38869,'physics',26,1138,2730,'Find the difference of air pressure between the inside and outside of a soap bubble $5\\,mm$ in diameter, if the surface tension is $1.6\\,\\,N{m^{ - 1}}$.','[{"text": "$2560\\\\,\\\\,N{m^{ - 2}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$3720\\\\,\\\\,N{m^{ - 2}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1208\\\\,\\\\,N{m^{ - 2}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$10132\\\\,\\\\,N{m^{ - 2}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'The excess pressure $p$ of bubble in air is given by,<br>$p = \\frac{{4T}}{R} = \\frac{{4 \\times 1.6}}{{2.5 \\times {{10}^{ - 3}}}} = 2560\\,\\,N{m^{ - 2}}$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38870,'physics',28,1371,3289,'A sample of an ideal gas is taken through a process ${A B C D}$. It absorbs $50\\,J$ of energy during the process ${A B}$ which is an isometric process, no heat during ${B C}$ and it rejects $70\\,J$ of heat during isobaric process ${C D}$. It is also given that $40\\,J$ of work is done on the gas during the process ${B C}$. Internal energy of gas in state ${A}$ is $1500\\,J$. The internal energy  of gas in state ${C}$ is','[{"text": "$1590\\\\,J$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$2250\\\\,J$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$3555\\\\,J$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$5296\\\\,J$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Given that ${\\Delta Q_{A \\rightarrow B}=50 {~J} ; \\Delta Q_{B \\rightarrow C}=0}$<br>$\\Delta Q_{C \\rightarrow D}=-70 {~J} ; \\Delta W_{B \\rightarrow C}=40 {~J}$<br>${U_{A}=1500 {~J}}$<br>${U_{B}=U_{A}+\\Delta U_{A \\rightarrow B}=1500+50=1550 {~J}}$<br>${U_{C}=B_{B}+W_{B \\rightarrow C}=1500+40=1590 {~J}}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38872,'physics',27,1382,3317,'One end of a copper rod of uniform cross-section and of length 3.1 $m$ is kept in contact with ice and the other end with water at $100^{\\circ} {C}$. Find the distance   of a point from $100^{\\circ} {C}$ end, along its length should a temperature of $200^{\\circ} {C}$ be maintained so that in steady state, the mass of ice melting be equal to that of the steam produced in the same interval of time. Assume that the whole system is insulated from the surroundings. Latent heat of fusion of ice and vaporization of water are $80\\,\\, {cal} / {g}$ and $540\\,\\, {cal} / {g}$ respectively.','[{"text": "$40\\\\,cm$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$65\\\\,cm$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$20\\\\,cm$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$10\\\\,cm$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Rate of flow of heat is given by $\\dfrac{d Q}{d t}=\\dfrac{\\Delta \\theta}{1 / K A}$also <br>$\\dfrac{d Q}{d t}=L \\dfrac{d m}{d t}$ (where $L=$ Latent heat)<br>$\\Rightarrow \\dfrac{d m}{d t}=\\dfrac{K A}{l}\\left(\\dfrac{\\Delta \\theta}{L}\\right)$. <br>Let the desire point is at a distance $x$ from water at $100^{\\circ} {C}$ <img src=\'/img/data/physics/27_38872_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' /> $\\because \\quad$ Rate of ice melting $=$ Rate at which steam is being produced  $ \\Rightarrow {\\left( {\\frac{{dm}}{{dt}}} \\right)_{{\\rm{Steam }}}} = {\\left( {\\frac{{dm}}{{dt}}} \\right)_{{\\rm{Ice }}}}$<br>$ \\Rightarrow {\\left( {\\frac{{\\Delta \\theta }}{{Ll}}} \\right)_{{\\rm{Steam }}}} = {\\left( {\\frac{{\\Delta \\theta }}{{Ll}}} \\right)_{{\\rm{Ice }}}}$<br>$ \\Rightarrow \\frac{{(200 - 100)}}{{540 \\times x}} = \\frac{{(200 - 0)}}{{80(3.1 - x)}}$<br>$ \\Rightarrow x = 0.4\\,m = 40\\,cm$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/27_38872_question_1.png'),
(38874,'physics',26,1138,2731,'If the length of tube is less and cannot accommodate the maximum rise of liquid, then','[{"text": "liquid will form fountain", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "liquid will not rise", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "the meniscus will adjust itself, so that the water does not spill", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "None of the above<br>", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'If the length of the tube $h^{\\prime}$ is less than $h$, it is found that the liquid does not overflow. In a tube of insufficient length, the liquid rises upto the top of the tube and increases the radius of curvature of its meniscus to a value $R^{\\prime}$, i.e. smaller the length $h^{\\prime}$ of the tube, greater will be the radius of curvature $R^{\\prime}$ of the meniscus, but the liquid will never overflow.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38884,'physics',27,1382,3317,'Two different rods \\(A\\) and \\(B\\) are kept as shown in figure. The variation of temperature of different cross sections plotted in a graph shown in figure.<br>Find the ratio of thermal conductivities of \\(B\\) to \\(A\\).<div class="matching-question"></div>','[{"text": "1", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "5", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "2", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "7", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'$\\because {\\left( {\\frac{{\\Delta Q}}{{\\Delta t}}} \\right)_A} = {\\left( {\\frac{{\\Delta Q}}{{\\Delta t}}} \\right)_B}$<br>$\\therefore \\frac{{{K_A}A(100 - 70)}}{{30}}$<br>$ = \\frac{{{K_B}A(70 - 35)}}{{70}}$<br>$ \\Rightarrow {K_A} = \\frac{{{K_B}}}{2} \\Rightarrow \\frac{{{K_B}}}{{{K_A}}} = 2$',NULL,'Hard','Graph/Figure',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/27_38884_question_1.png'),
(38885,'physics',28,1370,3280,'An amount $Q$ of heat is added to a mono-atomic ideal gas in a process in which the gas performs a work $\\dfrac{Q}{2}$ on its surroundings. If the molar heat capacity for the process is \' $x$ \'R. Find the value of \' $x$ \'.','[{"text": "$1\\\\,R$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2\\\\,R$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$5\\\\,R$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$3\\\\,R$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'By first law of thermodynamics<br>$Q = W + \\Delta U$<br>$ \\Rightarrow \\quad Q/2 = \\Delta U$<br>$ \\Rightarrow \\quad \\frac{{nC\\left( {{T_2} - {T_1}} \\right)}}{2} = n\\frac{{3R}}{2}\\left( {{T_2} - {T_1}} \\right)$<br>$ \\Rightarrow \\quad C = 3R$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38886,'physics',26,1135,2714,'A liquid is flowing through a non-sectional tube with its axis horizontally. If two points $X$ and $Y$ on the axis of tube has a sectional area $2.0\\,c{m^3}$ and $25\\,m{m^3}$ respectively then find the flow velocity at $Y$ when the flow velocity at $X$ is $10\\,m{\\rm{/}}s.$','[{"text": "$20\\\\,m{\\\\rm{/}}s$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$40\\\\,m{\\\\rm{/}}s$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$80\\\\,m{\\\\rm{/}}s$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$60\\\\,m{\\\\rm{/}}s$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'According to principle of continuity<br>${v_y} = \\frac{{{v_x}{A_x}}}{{{A_y}}} = \\frac{{10(\\,m{\\rm{/}}s) \\times 2\\left( {\\,c{m^2}} \\right)}}{{25 \\times {{10}^{ - 2}}\\left( {\\,c{m^2}} \\right)}} = 80\\,m{\\rm{/}}s$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38890,'physics',26,1137,2722,'A cylindrical vessel filled with water is released on an inclined surface of angle $\\theta$ as shown in figure.The friction coefficient of surface with the vessel is $\\mu \\left( { < \\tan \\,\\theta } \\right).$ Then the contact angle made by the surface of water with the incline will be','[{"text": "$\\\\tan ^{-1} \\\\mu$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\theta-\\\\tan ^{-1} \\\\mu$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\theta+\\\\tan ^{-1} \\\\mu$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\cot ^{-1} \\\\mu$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Figure shows forces acting on a particle on the surface, with respect to vessel. <img src=\'/img/data/physics/26_38890_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' /> $\\tan \\,\\theta  = \\mu \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\therefore \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\theta  = {\\tan ^{ - 1}}\\mu $',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/26_38890_question_1.png'),
(38894,'physics',26,1133,2710,'Two pieces of metals are suspended from the arms of a balance and are found to be in equilibrium when kept immersed in water. The mass of one piece is $32\\,g$ and its density $8\\,g\\,c{m^{ - 3}}$. The density of the other is $5\\,g$ per $c{m^3}$. Then the mass of the other is','[{"text": "$28\\\\,g$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$35\\\\,g$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$21\\\\,g$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$33.6\\\\,g$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Volume of first piece of metal $ = \\frac{{32}}{8} = 4\\,c{m^3}$<br>Upthrust $ = 4\\,gf$<br>Effective weight $ = (32 - 4)gf = 28gf$<br>If $m$ be the mass of second body, volume of second body is $\\dfrac{m}{5}$<br>Now, $28 = m - \\frac{m}{5} \\Rightarrow m = 35\\,g$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38895,'physics',3,313,577,'A stone falls freely under gravity. It covers distance ${h_1},{h_2}\\,{\\rm{and}}\\,{h_3}$ in the first $5 \\, s$, the next $5 \\, s$ and the next $5 \\, s$ respectively. The relation between ${h_1},{h_2}\\,{\\rm{and}}\\,{h_3}$ is','[{"text": "${h_1} = 2{h_2} = 3{h_3}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${h_2} = 3{h_1}\\\\,{\\\\rm{and}}\\\\,{h_3} = 3{h_2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${h_1} = \\\\frac{{{h_2}}}{3} = \\\\frac{{{h_3}}}{5}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${h_1} = {h_2} = {h_3}$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'For free fall from a height, $u = 0$<br>$\\therefore $ Distance covered by stone in first $5 \\, s$,<br>${h_1} = 0 + \\frac{1}{2}\\,g{(5)^2} = \\frac{{25}}{2}g$ &emsp; (1)<br>$\\therefore $ Distance covered in first $10 \\, s$,<br>${s_2} = 0 + \\frac{1}{2}g{(10)^2} = \\frac{{100}}{2}g$<br>$\\therefore $ Distance covered in second $5 \\, s$<br>${h_2} = {s_2} - {h_1} = \\frac{{100}}{2}g - \\frac{{25}}{2}g = \\frac{{75}}{2}g$ &emsp; (2)<br>Distance covered in first $15 \\, s$,<br>${s_3} = 0 + \\frac{1}{2}g\\,{(15)^2} = \\frac{{225}}{2}g$<br>$\\therefore $ Distance covered in last $5 \\,s$,<br>${h_3} = {s_3} - {s_2} = \\frac{{225}}{2}g - \\frac{{100}}{2}g = \\frac{{125}}{2}g$ &emsp; (3)<br>From Eqs (1), (2) and (3), we get<br>${h_1}:{h_2}:{h_3} = \\frac{{25}}{2}g:\\frac{{75}}{2}g:\\frac{{125}}{2}g = 1:3:5$<br>$ \\Rightarrow \\quad {h_1} = \\frac{{{h_2}}}{3} = \\frac{{{h_3}}}{5}$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38897,'physics',28,1372,3293,'A gas is taken from state-1 to state-2 along the path shown in figure. If 70 $cal$ of heat is extracted from the gas in the process, calculate the change in internal energy of the system (in $J$).','[{"text": "$241.5{\\\\mkern 1mu} J$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$367.1\\\\,J$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$165.2\\\\,J$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$435.7\\\\,J$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'We know work done by a gas is given by the area under $P V$-curve or the area between $P V$-curve and the volume axis. Generally, we take volume on $x$-axis while plotting $P V$-curve but in figure it is taken on $y$-axis thus the work done is given by the shaded area shown in figure. In this process volume of gas decreases thus work is done on the gas and it is given as  <img src=\'/img/data/physics/28_38897_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />  $W =  - \\frac{1}{2} \\times 1.5 \\times {10^{ - 4}} \\times (2 + 5) \\times {10^5}$<br>$ =  - 52.5\\,J$<br>It is given that heat extracted in the process is 70 cal, thus<br>$Q=-70\\,\\, {cal}=70 \\times 4.2 {~J}=-294 {~J}$<br>Now from first law of thermodynamics, we have$Q=W+\\Delta U$<br>or<br> $\\Delta U=Q-W=(-294)-(-52.5)=-241.5 {~J}$Thus in the process internal energy of gas decreases by 241.5 $J$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/28_38897_question_1.png'),
(38898,'physics',26,1138,2731,'Due to capillary action, a liquid will rise in a tube if angle of contact is','[{"text": "acute", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "obtuse", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$90^{\\\\circ}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "zero", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'$h = \\frac{{2\\,T\\cos \\theta }}{{r\\rho g}}$; The liquid will rise $i.e.,$ $h$ is positive if $\\cos \\theta$ is $ + ve;$ It is so if $\\theta < 90^{\\circ}$ or $\\theta$ is acute.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(48078,'biology',10,260,514,'The lipid component of cell membrane consists of','[{"text": "Lipolipids", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Phosphoglycerides", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Hydrophobic lipids", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "None of these", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Phosphoglycerides are glycerol based phospholipids.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38900,'physics',3,314,579,'Two bodies $A$ and $B$ start from rest from the same point with a uniform acceleration of $2 \\, m{s^2}$. If $B$ starts one second later then the bodies are separated, at the end of the next second, by','[{"text": "$1 \\\\, m$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2 \\\\, m$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$3 \\\\, m$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$4 \\\\, m$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'${S_B} = \\frac{1}{2} \\times 2 \\times {\\left( 1 \\right)^2} = 1\\,m$<br>${S_A} = \\frac{1}{2} \\times 2 \\times {\\left( 2 \\right)^2} = 4\\,m$<br>$\\therefore \\quad {S_A} - {S_B} = 3\\,m$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38902,'physics',26,1136,2715,'The rate of flow of glycerine of density $1.25 \\times {10^3}\\,kg/{m^3}$ through the conical section of a pipe, if the radii of its ends are $0.1\\,m$ and $0.04\\,m$ and the pressure drop across its length is $10\\,N/{m^2}$ is','[{"text": "$5.28 \\\\times {10^{ - 4}}\\\\,{m^3}/s$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$6.28 \\\\times {10^{ - 4}}\\\\,{m^3}/s$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$7.28 \\\\times {10^{ - 4}}\\\\,{m^3}/s$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$8.28 \\\\times {10^{ - 4}}\\\\,{m^3}/s$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,': According to equation of continuity<br>$\\begin{equation*}\\dfrac{v_{2}}{v_{1}}=\\dfrac{A_{1}}{A_{2}}=\\dfrac{\\pi \\times(0.1)^{2}}{\\pi \\times(0.04)^{2}}=\\dfrac{25}{4} \\tag{1}\\end{equation*}$<br>According to Bernoulli\'s equation for horizontal tube,<br>$P_{1}+\\dfrac{1}{2} \\rho v_{1}^{2}=P_{2}+\\dfrac{1}{2} \\rho v_{2}^{2}$<br>or $v_2^2 - v_1^2 = \\frac{{({p_1} - {p_2})}}{\\rho }$<br>or $v_{2}^{2}-v_{1}^{2}=\\dfrac{(2 \\times 10)}{\\left(1.25 \\times 10^{3}\\right)}=16 \\times 10^{-3}$$\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,(2)$<br>Substituting the value of $v_{2}$ from equation (1) in<br> (2), we get<br>${\\left( {6.25{v_1}} \\right)^2} - v_1^2 = 16 \\times {10^{ - 3}}$ or $\\quad {v_1} = 0.02\\,m/s$<br>So rate of flow through the tube<br>$=A_{1} v_{1}=A_{2} v_{2}=\\pi \\times(0.1)^{2} \\times 0.02$<br>$ = 6.28 \\times {10^{ - 4}}\\,{m^3}/s$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38903,'physics',27,1378,3304,'On celsius  scale the temperature of body increases by $40^\\circ C$. The increase in temperature on Fahrenheit scale is ____ .','[{"text": "$68^\\\\circ F$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$72^\\\\circ F$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$70^\\\\circ F$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$75^\\\\circ F$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'On celcius scale temperature increases by $\\Delta C=40^{\\circ} C$<br>By the relation, $C=\\dfrac{5}{9}(F-32)$<br>$\\therefore \\Delta C=\\dfrac{5}{9} \\Delta F$<br>$\\Rightarrow 40=\\dfrac{5}{9} \\Delta F$<br>$\\Delta F=72^{\\circ} F$<br>So, option (2) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2024,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38904,'physics',3,314,579,'At a metro station, a girl walks up a stationary escalator in $20 \\, sec$. If she remains stationary on the escalator, then the escalator take her up in $30 \\, sec$. The time taken by her to walk up on the moving escalator will be','[{"text": "$12\\\\,\\\\sec $", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$10\\\\,\\\\sec $", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$25\\\\,\\\\sec $", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$60\\\\,\\\\sec $", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Let $x$ be the length of the escalator<br>Speed of the girl on stationary escalator<br>${v_g} = \\frac{x}{{20}}$<br>Speed of the escalator ${v_e} = \\frac{x}{{30}}$<br>When both the girl and the escalator move up,<br>or $t = \\frac{x}{{{v_g} + {v_e}}}$<br>$t = \\frac{x}{{\\frac{x}{{20}} + \\frac{x}{{30}}}}$<br>$\\therefore $ Time taken, $t$ = $12 \\, s$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38906,'physics',26,1137,2723,'The velocity of rain drop having radius 1 $mm$ is ${20 {~cm} {~s}^{-1}}$. The velocity of raindrops of size 3 $mm$ is','[{"text": "${60 {~cm} / {s}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${120 {~cm} / {s}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${180 {~cm} / {s}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${20 {~cm} / {s}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Terminal velocity, ${v_{t}=\\dfrac{2 r^{2}(\\rho-\\sigma)}{9 \\eta} g}$,<br>where, ${\\rho=}$ density of liquid, ${\\sigma=}$ density of air/medium and ${\\eta=}$ coefficient of viscosity of the liquid.<br>${\\Rightarrow v_{t} \\propto r^{2}}$<br>${r_{1}=1 {~mm}=10^{-3} {~m}, v_{1}=20 \\times 10^{-2} {~m} / {s}}$<br>${r_2} = 3\\,mm = 3 \\times {10^{ - 3}}\\,m,{v_2} = ?$<br>${\\dfrac{v_{1}}{v_{2}}=\\left(\\dfrac{r_{2}}{r_{1}}\\right)^{2}=\\left(\\dfrac{3}{1}\\right)^{2}}$<br>${\\Rightarrow v_{2}=9 v_{1}=9 \\times 20 \\times 10^{-2} {~m} / {s}}$<br>$\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, = 180\\,cm/s$.<br>So correct option is (3)',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38909,'physics',26,1137,2723,'The ratio of radii of two spheres are ${1: 2}$ when they are dropped in a viscous liquid. The ratio of their terminal speed is','[{"text": "${1: 1}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${2: 1}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${1: 2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${1: 4}$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Terminal velocity is directly proportional to the square of radius of the body.<br>${\\therefore v \\propto r^{2}}$<br>$\\frac{{{v_1}}}{{{v_2}}} = {\\left( {\\frac{{{r_1}}}{{{r_2}}}} \\right)^2} = {\\left( {\\frac{1}{2}} \\right)^2} = \\frac{1}{4}\\,\\,\\,\\left[ {\\because \\frac{{{r_2}}}{{{r_1}}} = \\frac{1}{2}{\\text{ Given}}} \\right]$.<br>So correct option is (4)',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38910,'physics',3,313,577,'A body dropped from the top of a tower covers a distance $7 \\, x$ in the last second of its journey where $x$ is the distance covered in first second. How much time does it takes to reach the ground?','[{"text": "$3\\\\,s$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$4\\\\,s$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$5\\\\,s$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$6\\\\,s$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'In first second distance travelled <br>$x = \\frac{1}{2} \\times g \\times {t^2} = 5\\,m$<br>(for $g = 10 \\, m/{s^2} $) in last second $7x = 35\\,m$<br>Now, ${S_t}th = \\left( {u + at - \\frac{1}{2}a} \\right)$<br>or, $35 = 0 + 10 \\times t - \\frac{1}{2} \\times 10$<br>or $t = 4\\,s$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38912,'physics',26,1138,2730,'Excess pressure inside a soap bubble is three times that of the other bubble. The ratio of their volumes will be','[{"text": "${1: 3}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${1: 9}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${1: 16}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${1: 27}$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'In first bubble, excess pressure ${\\left(P_{1}\\right)=\\dfrac{4 T}{R_{1}}}$<br>In second bubble, excess pressure ${\\left(P_{2}\\right)=\\dfrac{4 T}{R_{2}}}$Given ${P_{1}=3 P_{2}}$<br>${\\therefore \\dfrac{4 T}{R_{1}}=3 \\times \\dfrac{4 T}{R_{2}}}$ or ${\\dfrac{R_{2}}{R_{1}}=\\dfrac{1}{3}}$<br>${\\dfrac{V_{1}}{V_{2}}=\\left(\\dfrac{R_{1}}{R_{2}}\\right)^{3}=\\left(\\dfrac{1}{3}\\right)^{3}=\\dfrac{1}{27}}$.<br>So correct option is (4)',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38913,'physics',3,313,577,'A body falls from a height $h = 200\\,m$. The ratio of distance travelled in each $2 \\, s$, during $t = 0\\,{\\rm{to}}\\,t = 6\\,s$ of the journey is','[{"text": "$1:4:9$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1:2:4$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1:3:5$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$1:2:3$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'${h_1} = \\frac{1}{2} \\times g \\times {\\left( 2 \\right)^2} = 2g$<br>${h_2} = \\frac{1}{2} \\times g \\times {\\left( 4 \\right)^2} - 2g = 6g$<br>${h_3} = \\frac{1}{2} \\times g \\times {\\left( 6 \\right)^2} - 8g = 10g$<br>$\\therefore \\quad \\quad {h_1}:{h_2}:{h_3} = 1:3:5$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38914,'physics',28,1371,3291,'A rectangular box shown in the figure has a partition which can slide without friction along the length of the box. Initially each of the two chambers of the box has one mole of a monatomic gas $\\left(\\gamma=\\dfrac{5}{3}\\right)$ at a pressure $P_{0}$, volume $V_{0}$, and temperature $T_{0}$. The chamber on the left is slowly heated by an electric heater. The walls of the box and the partition are thermally insulated. Heat loss through the lead wires is negligible. The gas in the left chamber expands, pushing the partition until the final pressure in both chambers becomes $\\dfrac{243\\,\\, p_{0}}{32}$. If $P_{0} V_{0}=400 {~J}$, then find  the magnitude of work done by the gas in the right chamber.','[{"text": "$ - 470\\\\,J$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$ - 750\\\\,J$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$ - 890\\\\,J$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$550\\\\,J$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'In right chamber we use for adiabatic process<br>${P_0}V_0^{6/3} = \\frac{{243}}{{32}}{P_0}V_{fR}^{5/3}$<br>$ \\Rightarrow {V_{fR}} = {\\left( {\\frac{{32}}{{243}}} \\right)^{3/5}}{V_0} = \\frac{8}{{27}}{V_0}$<br>Using gas law we have for right chamber,<br>$T_{fR}=\\dfrac{\\dfrac{243}{32} P_{0} \\cdot \\dfrac{8}{27} V_{0} T_{0}}{P_{0} V_{0}}=\\dfrac{9}{4} T_{0}$<br>for left chamber volume, $V_{fL}=2 V_{0}-\\dfrac{8}{27} V_{0}=\\dfrac{46}{27} V_{0}$<br>using gas law, we have, $T_{fL}=\\dfrac{\\dfrac{243}{32} P_{0} \\cdot \\dfrac{46}{27} V_{0} T_{0}}{P_{0} V_{0}}=\\dfrac{207}{16} T_{0}$<br>Work done by gas for right chamber.<br>$W = \\frac{{{P_0}{V_0} - \\left( {\\frac{{243}}{{32}}{P_0}} \\right)\\left( {\\frac{8}{{27}}{V_0}} \\right)}}{{\\frac{5}{3} - 1}}$<br>$ =  - \\frac{{15}}{8}{P_0}{V_0} =  - \\frac{{15}}{8} \\times 400 =  - 750\\,J$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/28_38914_question_1.png'),
(38915,'physics',26,1138,2727,'If the temperature increases, the surface tension of a liquid','[{"text": "Decreases", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Increases", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Remains a constant", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "First increases then decreases", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'The surface tension of a liquid decreases with the rise in temperature and it disappears a few degrees below the critical temperature.<br>So correct option is (1)',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38916,'physics',3,313,578,'The acceleration $a$ in $m/{s^2}$ of particle is given by $a = 3{t^2} + 2t + 2$ where $t$ is the time. If the particle starts out with a velocity $v = 2\\,m/s$ at $t = 0$, then the velocity at the end of $2 \\, s$ is','[{"text": "$12\\\\,m/s$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$14\\\\,m/s$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$16\\\\,m/s$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$18m/s$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'$a = \\frac{{dv}}{{dt}} = 3{t^2} + 2t + 2$<br>$dv = \\left( {3{t^2} + 2t + 2} \\right)dt$<br>$\\int\\limits_2^v {dv = 3\\int\\limits_0^2 {{t^2}dt + 2} \\int\\limits_0^2 {t\\,dt + 2} } \\int\\limits_0^2 {dt} $<br>$v - 2 = 3\\left[ {\\frac{{{t^3}}}{3}} \\right]_0^2 + 2\\left[ {\\frac{{{t^2}}}{2}} \\right]_0^2 + 2\\left[ t \\right]_0^2$<br>$v - 2 = 8 + 4 + 4\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, \\Rightarrow v = 18\\,m{\\rm{/}}s$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38918,'physics',26,1133,2711,'Two miscible liquids of densities ${2.5 {~g} / {cm}^{3}}$ and ${0.8 {~g} / {cm}^{3}}$ are taken in the ratio ${2: 3}$ by mass and mixed. The average density of he resulting mixture is','[{"text": "1.27", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "1.09", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "1.72", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "2.26", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Let the mass of two liquids of densities ${2.5 {~g} / {cm}^{3}}$ and ${0.8 {~g} / {cm}^{3}}$ are $2\\,mg$ and  $3\\,mg$ respectively<br>${V_{1}=\\dfrac{m_{1}}{\\rho_{1}}=\\left(\\dfrac{2 m}{2.5}\\right) {cm}^{3}, V_{2}=\\dfrac{m_{2}}{\\rho_{2}}=\\left(\\dfrac{3 m}{0.8}\\right) {cm}^{3}}$<br>Mass of resulting mixture ${=2 m+3 m=5 m {~g}}$<br>Volume of resulting mixture $ = {V_1} + {V_2}$<br>$ = \\left( {\\frac{{2m}}{{2.5}}} \\right) + \\left( {\\frac{{3m}}{{0.8}}} \\right) = \\left( {\\frac{{1.6 + 7.5}}{{2.5 \\times 0.8}}} \\right)mc{m^3}$<br>$ = 4.55m\\,c{m^3}$<br>Average density of mixture ${=\\left(\\dfrac{5 m}{4.55 m}\\right) {g} / {cm}^{3}}$<br>$\\quad \\quad \\quad  = 1.09\\,g/c{m^3}.$<br>So correct option is (2)',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38919,'physics',3,313,577,'A body standing at the top of a tower of $20 \\, m$ height drops a stone. Assuming, $g = \\,10\\,m{s^{ - 2}},$ the velocity  with which it hits the ground is','[{"text": "$40\\\\,m/s$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$20\\\\,m/s$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$5\\\\,m/s$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$10\\\\,m/s$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Given $g = \\,10\\,m{s^{ - 2}}\\,{\\rm{and}}\\,h = 20 \\, m$<br>We have $v = \\sqrt {2gh} $<br>$ = \\sqrt {2 \\times 10 \\times 20}  = \\sqrt {400}  = 20\\,m/s$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38921,'physics',26,1133,2710,'A cube of mass ${m}$ and density ${D}$ is suspended in a liquid of density ${d < D}$ with the help of a spring of stiffness ${k}$. The elongation of spring is','[{"text": "${\\\\dfrac{m g}{k}\\\\left(1-\\\\dfrac{d}{D}\\\\right)}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${\\\\dfrac{m g}{k}\\\\left(1-\\\\dfrac{D}{d}\\\\right)}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\dfrac{m g}{k}\\\\left(1+\\\\dfrac{d}{D}\\\\right)}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\dfrac{m g}{k}\\\\left(1+\\\\dfrac{D}{d}\\\\right)}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Let volume of cube ${=V}$<br>Weight of body in liquid ${=V g(D-d)}$$\\begin{aligned}\\therefore \\quad k x & =V g(D-d)=\\dfrac{m g}{D}(D-d) \\\\x & =\\dfrac{m g}{k}\\left(\\dfrac{D-d}{D}\\right) \\\\& =\\dfrac{m g}{k}\\left(1-\\dfrac{d}{D}\\right)\\end{aligned}$.<br>So correct option is (1)',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38922,'physics',3,313,577,'If a ball is thrown vertically upward with speed $u$, the distance covered during the last $t$ second of its ascent is','[{"text": "$ut - \\\\left( {g{t^2}/2} \\\\right)$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\left( {ut + gt} \\\\right)t$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$ut$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$g{t^2}/2$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Let $t$ second of upward journey = first $t$ second of downward journey (with zero initial velocity)<br>$\\therefore $ Desired distance $ = \\frac{1}{2}g{t^2}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38923,'physics',28,1372,3294,'Two moles of an ideal monoatomic gas are taken through a cycle $A B C A$ as shown in the $P T$ diagram in figure. During the process $A B$, pressure and temperature of the gas vary such that $P T=$ constant. If $T_{0}=300 {~K}$, and the heat released by the gas in the process $A B$ (in $kJ$).<br>$(R = 8.3\\,J/mo{l^{ - K}})$','[{"text": "$17.43\\\\,kJ$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$25.38\\\\,kJ$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$10.65\\\\,kJ$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$30.18\\\\,kJ$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'For process $A B, P T=$ constant<br>$\\left( {\\frac{{{\\text{Constant}}}}{T}} \\right)V = nRT$<br>$or\\,\\,T = k\\sqrt V (k\\,\\,{\\text{is}}\\,\\,{\\text{a}}\\,\\,{\\text{constant)}}$<br>$\\Rightarrow \\quad P \\sqrt{V}=$ constant or $P V^{1 / 2}=$ constant<br>Thus, molar specific heat of gas,$C=C_{v}+\\dfrac{R}{1-\\dfrac{1}{2}}=C_{v}+2 R$<br>$=\\dfrac{3}{2} R+2 R=\\dfrac{7}{2} R$<br>Heat supplied to gas is $\\Delta Q_{A B}=2 C\\left(T_{0}-2 T_{0}\\right)=-2 C T_{0}$<br>$=-7 R T_{0}=-2100 R$<br>Hence heat released by the gas,<br>${\\left( {\\Delta {Q_{AB}}} \\right)_{{\\rm{released }}}} = 2100 \\times 8.3$<br>$ = 17430\\,J = 17.43\\,kJ$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/28_38923_question_1.png'),
(38924,'physics',26,1133,2710,'A body of mass 2 $kg$ , volume ${0.001 {~m}^{3}}$ is in a fluid of density ${\\dfrac{4}{3} {~g} / {cm}^{3}}$. The fluid at rest. The upward acceleration of the body in the fluid is (in ${{m} / {s}^{2}}$ )','[{"text": "3.3", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "The body is neutrally buoyant", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$ - 3.3$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "None of these", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Density of body  $(\\rho )$<br>$=\\dfrac{\\text { Mass }}{\\text { Volume }}=\\dfrac{2}{0.001} {~kg} / {m}^{3}$<br>Density of fluid ( ${\\sigma}$ )<br>$=\\dfrac{4}{3} {~g} / {cm}^{3}=\\dfrac{4}{3} \\times 10^{3} {~kg} / {m}^{3}$<br>Weight of body ${(W)=V(\\rho-\\sigma)}$ g$=0.001\\left(2-\\dfrac{4}{3}\\right) \\times 10^{3} \\times 10 {~N}=6.6 {~N}$<br>Downward acceleration<br>$=\\dfrac{W}{m}=\\dfrac{6.6}{2}=3.3 {~m} / {s}^{2}$<br>${\\therefore}$ Upward acceleration ${=-3.3 {~m} / {s}^{2}}$.<br>So correct option is (3)',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38925,'physics',3,312,574,'For a body moving along a straight line, the following $v-t$ graph is obtained. According to the graph, the displacement during','[{"text": "Uniform acceleration is greater than that during uniform motion", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Uniform acceleration is less than that during uniform motion", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Uniform acceleration is equal to that during uniform motion", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Uniform motion zero", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'The area  under the velocity-time curve is equal to the displacement. So the displacement  is less in uniform acceleration than in uniform  motion, as the corresponding area is less for the first case.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2021,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/3_38925_question_1.png'),
(38927,'physics',26,1135,2714,'The piston of a syringe pushes a liquid with a speed of ${1 {~cm} / {s}}$. The radii of syringe tube and needle are ${R=1 {~cm}}$ and ${r=0.5 {~mm}}$ respectively. The velocity of liquid coming out of the needle is','[{"text": "${100 {~cm} / {s}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${200 {~cm} / {s}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${400 {~cm} / {s}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${800 {~cm} / {s}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Here, for syringe ${v_{s}=1 {~cm} / {s}}$,${R=1 {~cm}=10^{-2} {~m}}$<br>For needle, ${r=0.5 {~mm}=5 \\times 10^{-6} {~m}, v_{n}=}$ ?<br>According to equation of continuity,<br><img src=\'/img/data/physics/26_38927_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />  <br>${A_s}{v_s} = {A_n}{v_n}$<br>$\\pi {R^2}{v_s} = \\pi {r^2}{v_n}$<br>$\\therefore \\quad {v_n} = {\\left( {\\frac{R}{r}} \\right)^2}{v_s} = {\\left( {\\frac{{{{10}^{ - 2}}}}{{5 \\times {{10}^{ - 4}}}}} \\right)^2} \\times {10^{ - 2}}\\,m/s$<br>$ = 400\\,cm/s$<br>So correct option is (3).',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38928,'physics',3,313,576,'The distance travelled by a particle starting from rest and moving with an acceleration $\\frac{4}{3}{\\rm{m}}{{\\rm{s}}^{{\\rm{ - 2}}}}$, in the third second is','[{"text": "$6 \\\\, m$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{{10}}{3} \\\\, m$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$4\\\\, m$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{{19}}{3} \\\\, m$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Distance travelled in ${n^{th}}$ second is given by<br>${s_n} = u + \\frac{1}{2}a(2n - 1)$<br>Here,$u = 0,\\quad a = \\frac{4}{3}$<br>$\\therefore \\quad {s_3} = 0 + \\frac{1}{2} \\times \\frac{4}{3} \\times (6 - 1)$<br> $= \\, \\frac {10}{3} \\, m$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38931,'physics',3,313,576,'A particle starts with a velocity of $2\\,m{s^{ - 1}}$ and moves in a straight line with a retardation of $0.1\\,m{s^{ - 2}}$. The first time at which the particle is $15\\,m$ from the starting point is','[{"text": "$10\\\\,\\\\,s$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$20\\\\,\\\\,s$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$30\\\\,\\\\,s$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$40\\\\,\\\\,s$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'$15 = 2t - \\frac{1}{2} \\times \\left( {0.1} \\right){t^2}\\, {\\rm{or}} \\,t = 10\\,s$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38932,'physics',28,1371,3283,'A sample of gas at temperature ${T}$ is adiabatically expanded to double its volume. Adiabatic constant for the gas is ${\\gamma=3 / 2}$. The work done by the gas in the process is ( ${\\mu=1}$ mole)','[{"text": "${R T[2 \\\\sqrt{2}-1]}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${R T[\\\\sqrt{2}-2]}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${R T[2-\\\\sqrt{2}]}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${R T[1-2 \\\\sqrt{2}]}$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Given: ${\\gamma=3 / 2, V_{1}=V, V_{2}=2 V, T_{1}=T}$<br>For adiabatic process, ${T V^{\\gamma-1}=}$ constant<br>${\\therefore T_{1} V_{1}^{\\gamma-1}=T_{2} V_{2}^{\\gamma-1}}$<br>${T\\left(\\dfrac{V}{2 V}\\right)^{3 / 2-1}=T_{2} \\Rightarrow T_{2}=T\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right)^{1 / 2}=\\dfrac{T}{\\sqrt{2}}}$<br>Work done in adiabatic process,<br>${W=\\dfrac{n R \\Delta T}{1-\\gamma}}$<br>${W=\\dfrac{1 \\times R\\left(\\dfrac{T}{\\sqrt{2}}-T\\right)}{1-\\dfrac{3}{2}}=\\dfrac{R T(\\sqrt{2}-1) \\times 2}{\\sqrt{2} \\times 1}}$<br>${\\therefore W=R T(2-\\sqrt{2})}$<br>So, correct option is (3).',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2024,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38933,'physics',26,1133,2710,'A sphere of gold of radius $1\\,cm$ and density $19.2\\,g{\\rm{/}}c{m^3}$ has a concentric spherical cavity. If it floats with its whole volume just immersed in molten aluminium of density $2.4\\,g{\\rm{/}}c{m^3}$ then volume of cavity in cubic centimetre would be   (Take $\\pi=\\dfrac{22}{7}$ )','[{"text": "$3.67\\\\,c{m^3}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$6.73\\\\,c{m^3}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2.64\\\\,c{m^3}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$7.76\\\\,c{m^3}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Sphere will float in liquid if its weight is less than or equal to weight of liquid displaced by it, <br>i.e., $\\frac{4}{3}\\pi \\left( {{R^3} - {r^3}} \\right)\\sigma g \\le \\frac{4}{3}\\pi {R^3}\\rho g$<br>Where, $R$ is radius of sphere, $r$ is radius of cavity, $\\sigma$ density of gold and $\\rho$ is density of molten aluminium.As the sphere is floating with its whole volume just immersed in liquid,<br>$\\left( {{R^3} - {r^3}} \\right)\\sigma  = {R^3}\\rho $<br>$\\therefore \\quad \\frac{{{R^3} - {r^3}}}{{{R^3}}} = \\frac{\\rho }{\\sigma } = \\frac{{2.4}}{{19.2}}$<br>$\\therefore 1 - \\frac{{{r^3}}}{{{R^3}}} = \\frac{1}{8}$<br>$\\therefore \\quad \\frac{{{r^3}}}{{{R^3}}} = 1 - \\frac{1}{8} = \\frac{7}{8}$<br>$\\therefore \\quad {r^3} = \\frac{7}{8} \\times {1^3}$<br>$\\therefore \\quad$ Volume of cavity $=\\dfrac{4}{3} \\pi r^{3}$$=\\dfrac{4}{3} \\times \\dfrac{22}{7} \\times \\dfrac{7}{8} \\times 1^{3}$<br>$\\therefore \\quad V = \\frac{{11}}{3}\\,c{m^3} = 3.67\\,c{m^3}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38934,'physics',3,313,578,'A particle initially at rest moves along the $X$-axis. Its acceleration varies with time as $a = 4t$. If it starts from the origin, the distance covered  by it in $3 \\,s$ is','[{"text": "$12\\\\,m$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$18\\\\,m$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$24\\\\,m$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$36\\\\,m$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'$dv = a\\,dt$<br>$\\therefore \\quad \\quad \\int_0^v {dv}  = \\int_0^t {4\\,t\\,dt} $<br>${\\rm{or}}\\quad \\quad v = 2{t^2}$<br>Now, $s = \\int_0^3 {v\\,dt}  = \\int_0^3 {\\left( {2{t^2}} \\right)\\,dt} $<br>$ = \\frac{2}{3}{t^2} = 18\\,m$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38935,'physics',28,1372,3294,'The heat absorbed by a system in going through the given cyclic process is','[{"text": "${19.6 J}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${61.6 J}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$431.2{\\\\text{ }}J$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${616 J}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'In cyclic process, heat absorbed by the system is equal to the work done by the system.<br>i.e., ${\\Delta Q=W(\\because \\Delta U=0)}$<br>Now, ${W=}$ area under the given circle<br>${\\therefore W=\\pi r^{2}}$<br>${\\Rightarrow W=\\pi \\times\\left(\\dfrac{\\Delta P}{2}\\right) \\times\\left(\\dfrac{\\Delta V}{2}\\right)}$<br>$ \\Rightarrow W = \\pi  \\times \\left( {\\frac{{340 - 60}}{2}} \\right) \\times {10^3}$<br>$ \\times \\left( {\\frac{{340 - 60}}{2}} \\right) \\times {10^{ - 6}}$<br>${\\Rightarrow W=\\pi \\times \\dfrac{280 \\times 280}{4} \\times 10^{-3}=61.5 {~J}}$<br>${\\therefore \\Delta Q=W=61.5 J}$<br><img src=\'/img/data/physics/28_38935_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />  <br>So, option (2) is correct.',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',2024,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/28_38935_question_1.png'),
(38937,'physics',3,313,578,'A particle starts from rest. Its acceleration "$a$" versus time \'$t$\' is shown in the figure. The maximum speed of the particle will be:','[{"text": "$80\\\\,m{s^{ - 1}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$40\\\\,m{s^{ - 1}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$18\\\\,m{s^{ - 1}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2\\\\,m{s^{ - 1}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'As the particle, starting from rest, is accelerating continuously for $10 \\, s$, its velocity will be maximum at the end of $10 \\, s$.<br>${v_{\\max }} = {\\rm{area}}\\,{\\rm{of}}\\,{\\rm{the}}\\,{\\rm{graph}}$<br>${v_{\\max }} = \\frac{1}{2}(10)\\,(8) = 40\\,m{\\rm{/}}s$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2021,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/3_38937_question_1.png'),
(38938,'physics',28,1369,3279,'A total of ${48 J}$ heat is given to one mole of helium kept in a cylinder. The temperature of helium increases by ${2^{\\circ} {C}}$. The work done by the gas is (Given, ${R=8.3 {JK}^{-1} {~mol}^{-1}}$.)','[{"text": "$24.9\\\\,J$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$48\\\\,J$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$72.9{\\\\text{ }}J$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${23.1 J}$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'<br>${\\Delta Q=48 {~J}, n=1, \\Delta T=2^{\\circ} {C}, R=8.3 {~J} / {K} {mol}}$<br>For Helium, ${C_{V}=\\dfrac{3 R}{2}}$ and ${\\Delta U=n C_{V} \\Delta T}$<br>By using first law of thermodynamics,<br>${\\Delta Q=\\Delta U+W ; 48=n C_{V} \\Delta T+W}$<br>${\\Rightarrow 48=1 \\times \\dfrac{3 R}{2} \\times 2+W}$<br>${\\Rightarrow W=23.1 J}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2024,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38940,'physics',3,312,575,'A particle moving with uniform speed $v$, changes its direction by angle $\\theta $ in time $t$.  Magnitude of its average acceleration during this time is','[{"text": "${\\\\rm{zero}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{{2v}}{t}\\\\sin \\\\frac{\\\\theta }{2}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{{v\\\\sqrt 2 }}{t}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{None}}\\\\,{\\\\rm{of}}\\\\,{\\\\rm{these}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'${a_{av}} = \\left| {\\frac{{\\Delta v}}{{\\Delta r}}} \\right| = \\frac{{\\left| {{v_f} - {v_i}} \\right|}}{t}$<br>$ = \\frac{{\\sqrt {{v^2} + {v^2} - 2v.v.\\cos \\theta } }}{t}$<br>$ = \\frac{{2v\\,\\,\\sin \\theta /2}}{t}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38942,'physics',26,1135,2714,'Water from a tap emerges vertically downwards with initial velocity $6\\,cm{\\rm{/}}s.$ Assuming constant pressure throughout the stream and a steady flow, the distance from the tap in $cm$ at which the cross-sectional area of stream becomes ${\\left( {\\frac{1}{3}} \\right)^{rd{\\rm{ }}}}$ of the cross-sectional area of stream at the tap is<br>(Take $g = 10\\,m{\\rm{/}}{s^2}$)','[{"text": "$12.2\\\\,cm$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$14.4\\\\,cm$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$16.5\\\\,cm$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$10.3\\\\,cm$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Using equation of continuity,<br>${A_1}u = {A_2}v$<br>$\\therefore \\frac{{{A_2}}}{{\\,{A_1}}} = \\frac{6}{v}$<br>Substituting ${v^2} = {u^2} + 2gh$<br>$\\frac{1}{3} = \\frac{6}{{\\sqrt {{6^2} + 2gh} }}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\left( {\\because {A_2} = \\frac{1}{3}{A_1}} \\right)$<br>$\\therefore \\sqrt {{6^2} + 2gh}  = 3 \\times 6$<br>${6^2} + 2gh = {18^2}$<br>$\\therefore \\quad 2gh = {18^2} - {6^2}$<br>$\\therefore h = \\frac{{324 - 36}}{{2 \\times 10}} = 14.4\\,cm$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38943,'physics',3,313,576,'A car moving with a velocity of $20\\,m{s^{ - 1}}$ is stopped in a distance of $40 \\, m$. If the same car is travelling at double the velocity, the distance travelled by it for same retardation is','[{"text": "$640\\\\,m$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$320\\\\,m$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1280\\\\,m$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$160\\\\,m$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Initial velocity, $u = 20\\,m{s^{ - 1}}$,<br>Final velocity, $v = 0\\,m{s^{ - 1}};$<br>Distance travelled, $s = 40\\,m$<br>${v^2} - {u^2} = 2\\,{\\rm{as}};\\,0 - {20^2} = 2 \\times a \\times 40$<br>We get,$a =  - 5\\,m{s^{ - 2}}$<br>For second case, $u = 2 \\times 20\\,m{s^{ - 1}} = 40\\,m{s^{ - 1}}$<br>$v = 0\\,m{s^{ - 1}}\\,{\\rm{and}}\\,a =  - 5\\,m{s^2}$<br>Using the third equation of motion,<br>$s = \\frac{{0 - {{\\left( {40} \\right)}^2}}}{{2\\left( { - 5} \\right)}} = 160\\,m$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2017,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38944,'physics',28,1371,3284,'A diatomic gas ${(\\gamma=1.4)}$ does ${100 J}$ of work in an isobaric expansion. The heat given to the gas is','[{"text": "$150\\\\,J$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${250 J}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$490{\\\\text{ }}J$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$350{\\\\text{ }}J$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'According to ideal gas equation, ${P V=n R T}$<br>and ${\\Delta W=P \\Delta V=n R \\Delta T}$.<br>${\\Delta Q=\\Delta U+W=\\dfrac{f}{2} n R \\Delta T+n R \\Delta T}$<br>${\\Delta Q=\\left(1+\\dfrac{f}{2}\\right) n R \\Delta T=\\dfrac{7}{2} n R \\Delta T(\\because f=5)}$<br>${\\therefore Q=\\dfrac{7}{2} \\Delta W=\\dfrac{7}{2} \\times 100=350 J}$<br>So, correct option is (4).',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',2024,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38946,'physics',3,312,574,'The motion of a particle along a straight line is described   by  equation $x = 8 + 12t - {t^3},$ where, $x$ is in metre and $t$ in sec. The retardation of the particle when its velocity becomes zero, is','[{"text": "$12\\\\,m{s^{ - 2}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{Zero}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$6\\\\,m{s^{ - 2}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$24\\\\,m{s^{ - 2}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Given, $x = 8 + 12t - {t^3}$<br>we know $v = \\frac{{dx}}{{dt}}$<br>and acceleration $a = \\frac{{dv}}{{dt}}$<br>So,$v = 12 - 3{t^2}$<br>and $a =  - \\,6t$<br>At $t = 2s$<br>$v = 0$ and $a =  - \\,6 \\times 2$<br>$a =  - \\,12\\,m/{s^2}$<br>So, retardation of the particle $ = \\,12\\,m/{s^2}$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38947,'physics',28,1371,3283,'The volume of an ideal gas ${(\\gamma=1.5)}$ is changed adiabatically from 5 litres to 4 litres. The ratio of initial pressure to final pressure is','[{"text": "${\\\\dfrac{2}{\\\\sqrt{5}}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\dfrac{4}{5}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\dfrac{16}{25}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\dfrac{8}{5 \\\\sqrt{5}}}$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Given, ${\\gamma=1.5, V_{1}=5}$ litre, ${V_{2}=4}$ litre<br>For adiabatic process,<br>${P V^{\\gamma}=}$ constant<br>${P_{1} V_{1}^{\\gamma}=P_{2} V_{2}^{\\gamma} \\Rightarrow P_{2}=P_{1}\\left(\\dfrac{V_{1}}{V_{2}}\\right)^{\\gamma}}$<br>${\\Rightarrow P_{2}=P_{1}\\left(\\dfrac{5}{4}\\right)^{1.5}}$<br>${\\therefore \\dfrac{P_{1}}{P_{2}}=\\dfrac{4}{5} \\times \\sqrt{\\dfrac{4}{5}}=\\dfrac{8}{5 \\sqrt{5}}}$<br>So, correct option is (4)',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2024,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38949,'physics',3,312,574,'The given graph shows the variation of velocity ($v$) with position ($x$) for a  particle moving along a straight line. Which of the following graph shows the variation of acceleration ($a$) with position ($x$)?','[{"text": "", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'$v =  - kx + c$ (given line with negative slope)<br>$\\frac{{dv}}{{dt}} =  - k\\frac{{dx}}{{dt}} =  - kv$<br>$ \\Rightarrow a =  - k( - kx + c) = {k^2}x - kc$<br>Slope of $a - x$ graph is positive and $a =  - ve$ at $x = 0$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/3_38949_question_1.png'),
(38950,'physics',28,1372,3294,'A real gas within a closed chamber at ${27^{\\circ} {C}, P\\left({~N} / {m}^{2}\\right)}$ undergoes the cyclic process as shown in figure. The gas obeys ${P V^{3}=R T}$ equation for the path ${A}$ to ${B}$. The net work done in the complete cycle is<br>$({\\text{assuming }}\\,R = 8\\,J/mol\\,K)$','[{"text": "${20 J}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${-20 J}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$225{\\\\text{ }}J$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$205{\\\\text{ }}J$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Given,<br>${P V^{3}=R T, T_{1}=27^{\\circ} {C}=300 {~K}, R=8 {~J} / {molK}}$<br>The net work done in complete cycle is<br>${W_{net}} = {W_{AB}} + {W_{BC}} + {W_{CA}}$<br>${W_{AB}} = \\int_{{V_1}}^{{V_2}} P  \\cdot dV = \\int_2^4 {\\frac{{RT}}{{{V^3}}}} dV$<br>$ = RT\\int_2^4 {{V^{ - 3}}} dV$<br>${W_{AB}} = \\frac{{RT}}{{ - 2}}\\left[ {\\frac{1}{{{V^2}}}} \\right]_2^4 = \\frac{{ - RT}}{2}\\left[ {\\frac{1}{{16}} - \\frac{1}{4}} \\right]$<br>$ = \\frac{{8 \\times 300}}{2}\\left( {\\frac{{4 - 1}}{{16}}} \\right)$<br>${W_{AB}} = 225\\,J{\\rm{ }}({\\rm{expansion}})$<br>${W_{BC}} = \\int_4^2 P  \\cdot dV = 10\\,(2 - 4)$<br>$ =  - 20{\\mkern 1mu} J\\,{\\mkern 1mu} ({\\rm{compression}})$<br><img src=\'/img/data/physics/28_38950_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />  <br>${W_{C A}=0(}$ As ${\\Delta V=0)}$<br>${W_{\\text {net }}=W_{A B}+W_{B C}+W_{C A}=225-20=205 J}$<br>So, correct option is (4).',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',2024,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/28_38950_question_1.png'),
(38951,'physics',26,1139,2734,'A liquid is kept in a cylindrical vessel which is being rotated about a vertical axis through the centre of the circular base. If the radius of the vessel is $4\\,cm$ and angular velocity of rotation is $0.1\\,rad{\\rm{/}}s,$ then the difference in the heights of the liquid at the centre of the vessel and the edge in $\\mu m$ is<br>(Take $g = 10\\,m{\\rm{/}}{s^2}$)','[{"text": "$0.4\\\\,\\\\mu m$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$0.8\\\\,\\\\mu m$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$0.1\\\\,\\\\mu m$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$0.3\\\\,\\\\mu m$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'<img src=\'/img/data/physics/26_38951_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />  Using Bernoulli\'s theorem,<br>${P_A} + \\frac{1}{2}\\rho v_A^2 + \\rho g{h_A}$<br>$\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, = {P_B} + \\frac{1}{2}\\rho v_B^2 + \\rho g{h_B}$.<br>As the difference between heights $h_{A}$ and $h_{B}$ is very small, potential energy associated with liquid at point $A$ and $B$ is comparable. Hence, neglecting potential energy term from Bernoulli\'s equation, we get,<br>${P_A} + \\frac{1}{2}\\rho v_A^2 = {P_B} + \\frac{1}{2}\\rho v_B^2$<br>$\\therefore {P_A} - {P_B} = \\frac{1}{2}\\rho \\left[ {v_B^2 - v_A^2} \\right]$<br>At centre of liquid velocity will be zero i.e., ${v_A} = 0$and at periphery it will be $r\\omega $ i.e., ${v_B} = r\\omega $ <br>Let difference in heights be $h$.<br>$\\therefore hpg{\\rm{ }} = \\frac{1}{2}\\rho\\, {r^2}{\\omega ^2}$<br>$\\therefore h = \\frac{{{r^2}{\\omega ^2}}}{{2\\,g}}$<br>$ = \\frac{{{{\\left( {4 \\times {{10}^{ - 2}}} \\right)}^2} \\times {{(0.1)}^2}}}{{2 \\times 10}}$<br>$ = \\frac{{16 \\times {{10}^{ - 4}} \\times {{10}^{ - 2}}}}{{20}}$<br>$ = 0.8 \\times {10^{ - 6}}\\,m$<br>$\\therefore h = 0.8\\mu m$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38952,'physics',3,312,574,'A particle moves along a straight line such that its displacement at any point time $t$ is given by $s = 3{t^3} + 7{t^2} + 14t + 5$. The acceleration of the particle at $t = 1\\,s$ is','[{"text": "$18\\\\,m/{s^2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$32\\\\,m/{s^2}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$29\\\\,m/{s^2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$24\\\\,m/{s^2}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'$v = \\frac{{ds}}{{dt}} = 9{t^2} + 14t + 14$<br>$a = \\frac{{dv}}{{dt}} = 18t + 14$<br>At $t = 1\\,s,\\,a = 32\\,m{\\rm{/}}{s^2}$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38953,'physics',28,1371,3286,'The pressure and volume of an ideal gas are related as ${P V^{3 / 2}=K}$ (constant). The work done when the gas is taken from state ${A\\left(P_{1}, V_{1}, T_{1}\\right)}$ to state ${B\\left(P_{2}, V_{2}, T_{2}\\right)}$ is','[{"text": "${2\\\\left(P_{2} V_{2}-P_{1} V_{1}\\\\right)}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${2\\\\left(P_{1} V_{1}-P_{2} V_{2}\\\\right)}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${2\\\\left(P_{2} \\\\sqrt{V_{2}}-P_{1} \\\\sqrt{V_{1}}\\\\right)}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${2\\\\left(\\\\sqrt{P_{1}} V_{1}-\\\\sqrt{P_{2}} V_{2}\\\\right)}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Given,  $P{V^{3/2}} = K\\quad \\quad (1)$<br>${A\\left(P_{1}, V_{1}, T_{1}\\right) \\rightarrow B\\left(P_{2}, V_{2}, T_{2}\\right)}$<br>For polytropic gas, ${P V^{\\gamma}=}$ constant   $\\quad \\quad (2)$<br>From (1) and (2), ${\\gamma=\\dfrac{3}{2}}$<br>Work done, ${W=\\dfrac{n R \\Delta T}{1-\\gamma}}$, here ${\\gamma=\\dfrac{3}{2}}$<br>Using ${P V=n R T}$<br>$P_{1} V_{1}=n R T_{1}$<br>${P_{2} V_{2}=n R T_{2}}$<br>${\\therefore n R \\Delta T=P_{2} V_{2}-P_{1} V_{1}}$<br>${W=\\dfrac{P_{2} V_{2}-P_{1} V_{1}}{1-\\dfrac{3}{2}}=\\dfrac{2\\left(P_{2} V_{2}-P_{1} V_{1}\\right)}{-1}}$<br>${W=2\\left(P_{1} V_{1}-P_{2} V_{2}\\right)}$<br>So, correct option is (2).',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',2024,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38955,'physics',3,313,578,'A particle starting from rest with constant acceleration travels a distance $x$ in first $2 \\, s$ and a distance $y$ in next $2 \\, s$, then','[{"text": "$y = x$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$y = 2x$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$y = 3x$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$y = 4x$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'$\\frac{1}{2} \\times \\left( a \\right){\\left( 2 \\right)^2} = x\\,\\, {\\rm{or}} \\,2a = x$<br>Now, $y = \\frac{1}{2}\\left( a \\right){\\left( 4 \\right)^2} - \\frac{1}{2}\\left( a \\right){\\left( 2 \\right)^2} = 6a = 3x$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38956,'physics',28,1372,3294,'A thermodynamic system is taken from an original state ${A}$ to an intermediate state ${B}$ by a linear process as shown in the figure. It\'s volume is then reduced to the original value from to by an isobaric process. The total work done by the gas from ${A}$ to ${B}$ and ${B}$ to ${C}$ would be','[{"text": "${1200 J}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${600 J}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${2200 J}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$800{\\\\text{ }}J$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Total work done by the gas for the process ${A B C=}$ Area under curve ${A B}$-Area under curve ${B C=}$ Area under the curve ${\\triangle A B C}$<br><img src=\'/img/data/physics/28_38956_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />  <br>Work done ${=\\dfrac{1}{2} \\times A C \\times B C}$<br>Where ${A C=8000-4000=4000}$ dyne ${/ {cm}^{2}}$<br>${=4000 \\times \\dfrac{10^{-5}}{10^{-4}}=400 {~N} / {m}^{2}}$<br>${B C=7-3=4 m^{3}}$<br>${\\therefore \\quad}$ Work done ${=\\dfrac{1}{2} \\times 400 \\times 4=800 J}$<br>So, correct option is (4).',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2024,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/28_38956_question_1.png'),
(38958,'physics',3,313,578,'The acceleration $a$ (in $m{s^{ - 2}}$) of a body, starting from rest varies with time $t$ (in second) according to the realtion $a = 3t + 4$. The velocity of the body starting from rest at time $t = 2s$ will be','[{"text": "$10\\\\,m{s^{ - 1}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$12\\\\,m{s^{ - 1}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$14\\\\,m{s^{ - 1}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$16\\\\,m{s^{ - 1}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'$dv = a\\,dt$<br>or $v = \\int_0^2 {\\left( {3t + 4} \\right)dt = 14\\,m/s} $',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38959,'physics',28,1370,3280,'Two thermodynamical processes are shown in the figure. The molar heat capacity for process ${A}$ and ${B}$ are ${C_{A}}$ and ${C_{B}}$. The molar heat capacity at constant pressure and constant volume are represented by ${C_{P}}$ and ${C_{V}}$, respectively. Choose the correct statement.','[{"text": "${C_{A}=0}$ and ${C_{B}=\\\\infty}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${C_{A}>C_{P}>C_{V}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${C_{P}>C_{V}>C_{A}=C_{B}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${C_{A}=\\\\infty, C_{B}=0}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'For process ${A}$, the given curve is adiabatic.<br>For process ${A}$<br>${P V^{\\gamma}=K}$<br>${\\log P+\\log V^{\\gamma}=\\log K}$<br>${\\therefore \\log P=-\\gamma \\log V+C_{1}}$<br>${\\Rightarrow}$ Slope ${m, \\tan \\theta=-\\gamma}$<br>${\\therefore \\theta=\\tan ^{-1}(-\\gamma)}$<br>As there is no exchange of heat, ${\\Delta Q=0}$<br>${\\Delta Q=n C_{A} \\Delta T}$<br>$0 = n{C_A}\\Delta T$<br>${C_A} = 0$<br>Similarly for process ${B}$, the given curve is isothermal slope,<br>${\\tan \\left(45^{\\circ}\\right)=1}$<br>Specific heat capacity for isothermal process is ${\\infty}$.<br>So, ${C_{B}=\\infty}$ correct option is (1).',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',2024,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/28_38959_question_1.png'),
(38960,'physics',26,1133,2705,'Two identical cylindrical vessels with their bases at the same level, contain oil of density $0.8\\,g{\\rm{/}}c{m^3}.$ The area of both vessels is $20\\,c{m^2}$ but the height of liquid in one vessel is $10\\,cm$ and in the other $20\\,cm.$ When both cylinders are connected, the work done in joule (rounding of to $2$ decimal places) by gravity in equalising the levels is','[{"text": "$0.3528\\\\,J$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$0.4258\\\\,J$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$0.6327\\\\,J$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$0.5327\\\\,J$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'<img src=\'/img/data/physics/26_38960_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />  When cylinders are connected, levels in two vessels get equalised to $x = \\frac{{10 + 20}}{2} = 15\\,cm$ Volume of water that will be transferred to $BV$<br>$ = Ax = 20 \\times 15 = 300\\,c{m^3}$<br>This causes decrease in P.E.<br>$\\therefore$ Decrease in P.E.$ = mgh$<br>$ = V\\rho gx$<br>$ = 300 \\times 0.8 \\times 980 \\times 15$<br>$ = 3.528 \\times {10^6}erg$<br>Decrease in P.E. $=$ Work done $ = 3.528 \\times {10^6}erg$<br>$ = 0.3528\\,J$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38961,'physics',30,1358,3261,'The motion of a ball bouncing between two rigid walls is','[{"text": "Non-periodic", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Oscillatory", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Linear simple harmonic", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Angular simple harmonic", "isCorrect": false}]',1,NULL,NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38962,'physics',3,312,575,'A body is moving with velocity $30\\,m/s$ towards East. After $10 \\, s$, its velocity becomes $40\\,m/s$ towards North. The average acceleration of the body is','[{"text": "$5\\\\,m/{s^2}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$7\\\\,m/{s^2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\sqrt 7 \\\\,m/{s^2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1\\\\,m/{s^2}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Average acceleration $ = \\frac{{{\\rm{Change \\, in \\, velocity }}}}{{{\\rm{Total \\, time}}}}$<br>$a = \\frac{{\\left| {{v_f} - {v_i}} \\right|}}{{\\Delta \\,t}} = \\frac{{\\sqrt {30{}^2 + {{40}^2}} }}{{10}}$<br>$ = \\frac{{\\sqrt {900 + 1600} }}{{10}} = 5\\,m{s^{ - 2}}$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38963,'physics',28,1372,3293,'Choose the correct statement for processes ${A}$ and ${B}$ shown in figure.','[{"text": "${P V^{\\\\gamma}=k}$ for process ${B}$ and ${P V=k}$ for process ${A}$.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${\\\\dfrac{T^{\\\\gamma}}{P^{\\\\gamma-1}}=k}$ for process ${A}$ and ${P V=k}$ for process ${B}$.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${P V=k}$ for process ${B}$ and ${A}$.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\dfrac{P^{\\\\gamma-1}}{T^{\\\\gamma}}=k}$ for process ${B}$ and ${T=k}$ for process ${A}$.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Slope of adiabatic curve is greater than that of an isothermal curve<br>So, process ${A}$ is isothermal<br>${\\therefore \\quad P V=k}$ for process ${A}$ and process ${B}$ is adiabatic<br>${\\therefore \\quad P V^{\\gamma}=k}$ for process ${B}$.<br><img src=\'/img/data/physics/28_38963_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2024,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/28_38963_question_1.png'),
(38965,'physics',30,1360,3267,'The restoring force of SHM is maximum when particle','[{"text": "is half way between the mean and extreme position", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "displacement is maximum", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "is at $\\\\dfrac{\\\\sqrt{3}}{2}$ times of maximum displacement", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "crosses mean position", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'We know that during SHM, the restoring force is proportional to the displacement from equilibrium position. Hence restoring force is maximum when the displacement is maximum.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38966,'physics',3,313,576,'A particle starts its motion from rest under the action of a constant force. If the distance covered in first  $10\\,s\\,{\\rm{is}}\\,{s_1}$ and that covered in the first  $20\\,s\\,{\\rm{is}}\\,{s_2}$, then','[{"text": "${s_2} = 2\\\\,{s_1}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${s_2} = 3\\\\,{s_1}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${s_2} = \\\\,{s_1}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${s_2} = 4\\\\,{s_1}$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Since, the body starts from rest $u = 0$<br>$s = \\frac{1}{2}a{t^2}$<br>Now,${s_1} = \\frac{1}{2}a\\,{(10)^2}$ &emsp; (1)<br>and ${s_2} = \\frac{1}{2}a\\,{(20)^2}$ &emsp; (2)<br>Dividing Eq. (1) and Eq. (2), we get <br>$\\frac{{{s_1}}}{{{s_2}}} = \\frac{{{{(10)}^2}}}{{{{(20)}^2}}}$<br>$ \\Rightarrow \\quad {s_2} = 4{s_1}$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38967,'physics',28,1371,3284,'The given figure represents two isobaric processes for the same mass of an ideal gas, then','[{"text": "${P_{1}=P_{2}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${P_{2} \\\\geq P_{1}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${P_{2}>P_{1}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${P_{1}>P_{2}}$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'As the given processes are isobaric, the reciprocal of the slope of processes will represent the value of pressure.<br>${P V=n R T}$<br>${V=\\left(\\dfrac{n R}{P}\\right) T}$<br>${\\therefore}$ Slope ${=\\dfrac{n R}{P}}$<br>As, slope of ${P_{2}>}$ slope of ${P_{1}}$<br>${\\therefore \\quad P_{1}>P_{2}}$<br>So, correct option is (4).',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2024,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/28_38967_question_1.png'),
(38969,'physics',30,1359,3264,'An object of mass $m$ is moving in a uniform circular motion in the $xy$ plane. The circle has radius $R$ and the object is moving around the circle with speed $v$. The motion is projected onto the $x$-axis where it appears as simple harmonic motion according to $x(t) = R\\cos (\\omega t + \\phi )$. Here $\\omega$ is equal to','[{"text": "$v / R$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$v /(R \\\\sin \\\\omega t)$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${m^2}R$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{R} / v$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Angular frequency of SHM is equal to angular velocity of particle $\\omega=v / R$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38970,'physics',3,311,573,'A body of mass $m$ moving along a straight line covers half the distance with a speed of $2\\,m{s^{ - 1}}$. The remaining half of the distance is  covered in two equal time intervals with a speed of $3\\,m{s^{ - 1}}\\,{\\rm{and}}\\,5m{s^{ - 1}}$ respectively. The average  speed of the particle for the entire journey is','[{"text": "$\\\\frac{3}{8}\\\\, m{s^{ - 1}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{8}{3}\\\\, m{s^{ - 1}}\\\\,$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{4}{3}\\\\, m{s^{ - 1}}\\\\,$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{{16}}{3} \\\\,m{s^{ - 1}}\\\\,$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Let $2 \\, S$ be the total distance travelled by the body:<br>Let  ${t_1}$ be the time taken by the body to travel  first half of the distance.<br>$\\therefore \\,{t_1} = \\frac{S}{2}$<br>Let  ${t_2}$ be the time taken by the body for each time interval for the remaining half journey.<br>$\\therefore \\,S = 3{t_2} + 5{t_2}\\,{\\rm{or}}\\,{t_2} = S/8$<br>${\\rm{Average}}\\,{\\rm{speed}} = \\frac{{{\\rm{Total}}\\,{\\rm{distance}}\\,{\\rm{travelled}}}}{{{\\rm{Total}}\\,{\\rm{time}}\\,{\\rm{taken}}}}$<br>$ = \\frac{{2S}}{{{t_1} + 2{t_2}}} = \\frac{{2S}}{{\\frac{S}{2} + 2\\left( {\\frac{S}{8}} \\right)}} = \\frac{8}{3}\\,m{s^{ - 1}}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2009,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38971,'physics',28,1371,3284,'A diatomic gas ${(\\gamma=1.4)}$ does ${200 J}$ of work when it is expanded isobarically. The heat given to the gas in the process is','[{"text": "$800\\\\,J$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${600 J}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$700\\\\,J$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${850 J}$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Work done by gas ${=200 {~J}}$<br>Gas is expanding isobarically, so pressure is constant.<br>$PV = nRT$<br>$\\therefore P{V_1} = nR{T_1}{\\text{ }}$  and $P{V_2} = nR{T_2}$<br>$ \\Rightarrow W = P\\left( {{V_2} - {V_1}} \\right) \\Rightarrow nR\\left( {{T_2} - {T_1}} \\right) = nR\\Delta T$<br>$\\therefore \\quad nR\\Delta T = 200\\quad {\\kern 1pt} {\\kern 1pt} \\,\\,\\,(1)$<br>Heat given to gas, ${Q=n C_{P} \\Delta T}$<br>Where, ${C_{P}=\\left(\\dfrac{f}{2}+1\\right) R, f=}$ degree of freedom<br>Using,$\\begin{aligned}\\gamma & =1+\\dfrac{2}{f} \\text { or } 1.4=1+\\dfrac{2}{f} \\\\& \\Rightarrow f=5 \\\\\\therefore & C_{P}=\\left(\\dfrac{5}{2}+1\\right) R=\\dfrac{7}{2} R\\end{aligned}$<br>As ${\\Delta Q=n C_{P} \\Delta T}$<br>${\\therefore \\Delta Q=n \\cdot \\dfrac{7}{2} R \\Delta T=\\dfrac{7}{2}(200)}$<br>( ${\\therefore}$ using eqn. (1))<br>${=700 {~J}}$<br>So, correct option is (3)',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',2024,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38972,'physics',26,1133,2710,'A ball whose density is $0.5 \\times {10^3}\\,kg{\\rm{/}}{m^3}$ falls into water from a height of $10\\,cm$. To what depth  does the ball sink?','[{"text": "$15\\\\,cm$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$20\\\\,cm$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$10\\\\,cm$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$30\\\\,cm$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Velocity of ball before entering the water surface,$v = \\sqrt {2gh}  = \\sqrt {2\\,g \\times 10} \\,cm/s\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,(1)$<br> When ball enters into water, due to upthrust of water the velocity of ball decreases.<br>$\\therefore$ retardation, $\\mathrm{a}=\\dfrac{\\text { apparent weight }}{\\text { mass of ball }}$$ = \\frac{{V(\\sigma  - \\rho )g}}{{V\\sigma }} = \\left( {\\frac{{\\sigma  - \\rho }}{\\sigma }} \\right)g$<br>$ = \\left( {\\frac{{0.5 - 1}}{{0.5}}} \\right) \\times g$<br>$ =  - g$<br>Let $d$ be the depth upto which ball sinks then, using III $^{\\text {rd }}$ kinematical equation,<br>$0 - {v^2} = 2 \\times  - g \\times d$<br>${{v^2} = 2gd}$<br>${\\therefore \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,2 \\times g \\times 10 = 2gd{\\rm{  }}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{\\rm{[using}}\\,\\,{\\rm{(1)]}}}$<br>${\\therefore d = 10\\,cm}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38973,'physics',30,1359,3263,'A particle is performing simple haromonic motion along $x$-axis with amplitude $4\\,cm$ and time period $1.2\\,\\sec $. The minimum time taken by the particle to move from $x = 2\\,cm$ to $x =  + 4\\,cm$ and back again is given by [Mean position is at $x=0$ ]','[{"text": "$0.4\\\\,{\\\\rm{sec}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$0.6\\\\,{\\\\rm{sec}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$0.2\\\\,{\\\\rm{sec}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$0.3\\\\,{\\\\rm{sec}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Time taken by particle to move from $x=$ 0 (mean position) to $x=4$ (extreme position)<br>$ = \\frac{T}{4} = \\frac{{1.2}}{4} = 0.3\\,s$<br>Let $t$ be the time taken by the particle to move from<br>$x = 0{\\rm{ }}to{\\rm{ }}x = 2\\,cm$<br>$y = a\\sin \\omega t \\Rightarrow 2 = 4\\sin \\frac{{2\\pi }}{T}t \\Rightarrow \\frac{1}{2} = \\sin \\frac{{2\\pi }}{{1.2}}t$<br>$\\Rightarrow \\dfrac{\\pi}{6}=\\dfrac{2 \\pi}{1.2} t \\Rightarrow t=0.1 s$.<br>Hence time to move from<br>$x=2$ to $x=4$ will be equal to $0.3-0.1=0.2 s$<br>Hence total time to move from $x=2$ to $x=4$ and back again $=2 \\times 0.2=0.4 \\mathrm{sec}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38997,'physics',30,1359,3265,'If $x, v$ and a denote the displacement, the velocity and the acceleration of a particle executing SHM of time period $T$. Then which of the following does not change with time?','[{"text": "$\\\\dfrac{a T}{v}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{a T}{x}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$3 a^{2} T^{2}+4 \\\\pi^{2} v^{2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$a T+2 \\\\pi v$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'$\\frac{{aT}}{x} = \\frac{{{\\omega ^2}xT}}{x} = \\frac{{4{\\pi ^2}}}{{{T^2}}} \\times T = \\frac{{4{\\pi ^2}}}{{\\,T}} = $ Constant',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38974,'physics',3,309,571,'The position $x$ of a particle w.r.t. time $t$ along $x$-axis is given by $x = 9{t^2} - {t^3},$ where $x$ is in metre and $t$ in sec. What will be the position of this particle when it achieves maximum speed along the $ + x$ direction?','[{"text": "$32\\\\,m$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$54\\\\,m$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$81\\\\,m$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$24\\\\,m$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Given, the position $x$  of a particle w.r.t. time $t$ along $x$- axis<br>$x = 9{t^2} - {t^3}$<br>$v = \\frac{{dx}}{{dt}} = \\frac{d}{{dt}}\\,(9{t^2} - {t^3})$<br>$v = 18t - 3{t^2}$<br>$a = \\frac{{dv}}{{dt}} = \\frac{d}{{dt}}(18t - 3{t^2})$<br>$a = 18 - 6t$<br>When speed of particle is maximum, its  acceleration is zero, i.e.,<br>$a = 0 \\Rightarrow t = 3s$<br>Putting in Eq. (1), we obtain position of particle at the time<br>$x = 9{(3)^2} - {(3)^3} = 81 - 27 = 54\\,m$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38975,'physics',30,1359,3263,'Out of the following functions representing motion of a particle which represents SHM<br>A. $y=\\sin ^{3} \\omega t$<br>B. $y=\\sin \\omega t-\\cos \\omega t$<br>C. $y=1+\\omega t+\\omega^{2} t^{2}$<br>D. $y=5 \\cos \\left(\\dfrac{3 \\pi}{4}-3 \\omega t\\right)$','[{"text": "Only (B)", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Only (A) and (B)", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Only (B) and (D)", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Only (C) does not represent SHM", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'The function $y=5 \\cos \\left(\\dfrac{3 \\pi}{4}-3 \\omega t\\right)$ represents equation of SHM with phase constant $\\dfrac{3 \\pi}{4}$<br>$\\begin{aligned}& y=\\sin \\omega t-\\cos \\omega t \\\\& \\dfrac{d y}{d t}=\\omega \\cos \\omega t+\\omega \\sin \\omega t \\\\& \\dfrac{d^{2} y}{d t^{2}}=-\\omega^{2} \\sin \\omega t+\\omega^{2} \\cos \\omega t \\\\& =-\\omega^{2}(\\sin \\omega t-\\cos \\omega t) \\\\& \\dfrac{d^{2} y}{d t^{2}}=-\\omega^{2} y\\end{aligned}$<br>The other two functions do not obey<br>$\\dfrac{d^{2} y}{d t^{2}}=-\\omega^{2} y$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38977,'physics',3,311,573,'A point traversed $3/{4^{th}}$ of the circle of radius $R$ in time $t$. The magnitude of the average velocity of the particle in this time interval is','[{"text": "$\\\\frac{{\\\\pi R}}{t}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{{3\\\\pi R}}{{2t}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{{R\\\\sqrt 2 }}{t}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{R}{{\\\\sqrt 2 t}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'${v_{av}} = \\frac{{{\\rm{Displacement}}}}{{{\\rm{Time}}}} = \\frac{{{\\sqrt 2} R}}{t}$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38979,'physics',30,1359,3265,'The velocity of a particle in simple harmonic motion at displacement $y$ from mean position is','[{"text": "$\\\\omega \\\\sqrt{a^{2}-y^{2}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\omega \\\\sqrt{a^{2}+y^{2}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\omega^{2} \\\\sqrt{a^{2}-y^{2}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\omega y$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,NULL,NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38980,'physics',3,310,572,'A person moves towards east for $4 \\, m$, then towards north for $3 \\, m$ and then moves vertically up by $5 \\, m$.  What is his distance now from the starting point?','[{"text": "$5\\\\sqrt 2 \\\\, m$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$5 \\\\, m$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$10 \\\\, m$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$20 \\\\, m$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Distance from starting point<br>$\\sqrt {{{\\left( 3 \\right)}^2} + {{\\left( 4 \\right)}^2} + {{\\left( 5 \\right)}^2}}  = 5\\sqrt 2 \\,m$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38981,'physics',26,1137,2724,'The radius of a pipe carrying a liquid decreases by $20 \\%$ because of deposits on the inner surface. By how many times would the pressure difference between the ends of the constricted pipe should be increased to original pressure difference to maintain a constant flow rate?','[{"text": "3.45", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "2.44", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "6.43", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "1.44", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Let $l$ and $r$ be the length and radius of the pipe. If $P$ is the pressure difference between the two ends of the pipe, then the rate of flow of the liquid through the pipe,<br>$V = \\frac{{\\pi {{\\Pr }^4}}}{{8\\eta l}}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,(1)$<br>Let the pressure difference be changed to $P\',$ when the radius of the pipe decreases by $20 \\%$<br> i.e., becomes $r\' = 0.8r$<br>$\\therefore \\quad V = \\frac{{\\pi P\'{r^4}}}{{8\\eta l}} = \\frac{{\\pi P\' \\times {{(0.8r)}^4}}}{{8\\eta l}}$$\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,(2)$<br>From the equations (1) and (2)<br>$\\frac{{\\pi P\' \\times {{(0.8r)}^4}}}{{8\\eta l}} = \\frac{{\\pi {{\\Pr }^4}}}{{8\\eta l}}$<br>$\\therefore \\quad \\frac{{P\'}}{P} = \\frac{{{r^4}}}{{{{(0.8r)}^4}}} = \\frac{1}{{{{(0.8)}^4}}}$<br>$ = \\frac{1}{{0.64}} \\times \\frac{1}{{0.64}}$<br>$\\therefore \\quad \\frac{{P\'}}{P} = \\frac{{25}}{{16}} \\times \\frac{{25}}{{16}} = \\frac{{625}}{{256}} = 2.44$<br>$\\therefore \\quad P\' = 2.44$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38982,'physics',30,1359,3265,'If a simple pendulum oscillates with an amplitude of $50\\,mm$ and time period of $2\\,s$, then its maximum velocity is','[{"text": "$0.15\\\\,m/s$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$0.10\\\\,m/s$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$0.26\\\\,m/s$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$0.8\\\\,m/s$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'${v_{\\max }} = a\\omega  = a \\times \\frac{{2\\pi }}{T} = \\left( {50 \\times {{10}^{ - 3}}} \\right) \\times \\frac{{2\\pi }}{2} = 0.15\\,m/s$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38983,'physics',3,310,572,'An insect crawls a distance of $4 \\, m$ along north in $10 \\, s$ and then a distance of $3 \\, m$ along east in $5 \\, s$. The average velocity of the insect is','[{"text": "$\\\\frac{7}{{15}} \\\\, m/s$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{1}{5} \\\\, m/s$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{1}{3} \\\\, m/s$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{4}{5} \\\\, m/s$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'${v_{av}} = \\frac{{{\\rm{Displacement}}}}{{{\\rm{Time}}}}$<br>$ = \\frac{{\\sqrt {{{\\left( 4 \\right)}^2} + {{\\left( 3 \\right)}^2}} }}{{10 + 5}} = \\frac{1}{3}m{\\rm{/}}s$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38985,'physics',30,1359,3265,'The maximum velocity of a particle, executing simple harmonic motion with an amplitude $7\\,mm$, is $4.4\\,m/s$. The period of oscillation is','[{"text": "$10\\\\,s$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$0.01\\\\,s$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$100\\\\,s$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$0.1\\\\,s$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Maximum velocity,<br>${v_{\\max {\\rm{ }}}} = a\\omega  \\Rightarrow {v_{\\max {\\rm{ }}}} = a \\times \\frac{{2\\pi }}{T}$<br>$ \\Rightarrow T = \\frac{{2\\pi a}}{{{v_{\\max {\\rm{ }}}}}} = \\frac{{2 \\times 3.14 \\times 7 \\times {{10}^{ - 3}}}}{{4.4}} \\approx 0.01\\,s$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38986,'physics',3,311,573,'A particle having time period (in its circular motion) $2\\pi s$ completes half revolution. Its mean speed is [given Radius of circle $ = 50\\,cm$]','[{"text": "$2.6\\\\,m/s$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1.5\\\\,m/s$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$0.5\\\\,m/s$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$0.7\\\\,m/s$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'The average or Mean speed is <br>$\\langle v\\rangle  = \\frac{{\\pi R}}{T/2} = \\frac{\\pi \\times 0.5} {\\pi} = 0.5\\,m/s$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38987,'physics',30,1359,3265,'A particle performs simple harmonic motion with amplitude $A$. Its speed is tripled at the instant when it is at a distance $\\dfrac{2 A}{3}$ from equilibrium position. The new amplitude of the motion is :','[{"text": "$3\\\\,A$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$A\\\\sqrt 3 $", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{7 A}{3}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{A}{3} \\\\sqrt{41}$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'We know that $v=\\omega \\sqrt{A^{2}-x^{2}}$<br>Initially $v=\\omega \\sqrt{A^{2}-\\left(\\dfrac{2 A}{3}\\right)^{2}}$<br>After increasing the velocity<br>$3v = \\omega \\sqrt {{{A\'}^2} - {{\\left( {\\frac{{2A}}{3}} \\right)}^2}} $<br>Where $A^{\\prime}$ is the final amplitude.<br>$\\frac{1}{3} = \\sqrt {\\frac{{{A^\\prime } - {{\\left( {\\frac{{2A}}{3}} \\right)}^2}}}{{{A^{\\prime 2}} - {{\\left( {\\frac{{2A}}{3}} \\right)}^2}}}} $<br>$9\\left[ {{A^2} - \\frac{{4{A^2}}}{9}} \\right] = {A^{\\prime 2}} - \\frac{{4{A^2}}}{9}$<br>$ \\Rightarrow {A^\\prime } = \\frac{{7A}}{3}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38988,'physics',26,1137,2722,'A metal plate of area $0.2\\,{m^2}$ is connected to a $0.01\\,kg$ of mass via a string that passes over an ideal pulley. A liquid with a film thickness of $0.4\\,\\,mm$ is placed between the plate and the table as shown in figure above. When released, the plate moves to the right with a constant speed of $0.08\\,\\,m{s^{ - 1}}.$ The coefficient of viscosity of the liquid is $n \\times {10^{ - 3}}Pa - s.$ Find $n$.<br>(Take $g = 10\\,m{\\rm{/}}{s^2}$)','[{"text": "$4.5 \\\\times {10^{ - 3}}\\\\,Pa - s$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$6.5 \\\\times {10^{ - 3}}\\\\,Pa - s$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$8.5 \\\\times {10^{ - 3}}\\\\,Pa - s$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2.5 \\\\times {10^{ - 3}}\\\\,Pa - s$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'The metal plate will move under the action of force,<br>$F = mg = 0.01 \\times 10\\,N\\,$<br>As, $F = \\eta \\,\\,A\\frac{{dv}}{{dx}}$<br>$\\therefore \\quad \\eta  = \\frac{{Fdx}}{{A\\,\\,dv}} = \\frac{{0.01 \\times 10 \\times 4 \\times {{10}^{ - 4}}}}{{0.2 \\times 0.08}}$<br>$\\therefore \\quad \\eta  = \\frac{1}{{400}} = 2.5 \\times {10^{ - 3}}\\,Pa - s$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/26_38988_question_1.png'),
(38989,'physics',3,311,573,'A car moves from $X$ to $Y$ with a uniform speed ${v_u}$ and returns to $X$ with a uniform speed ${v_d}$. The  average speed for this round trip is','[{"text": "$\\\\frac{{{v_d}{v_u}}}{{{v_d} + {v_u}}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\sqrt {{v_u}{v_d}} $", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{{2\\\\,{v_d}{v_u}}}{{{v_d} + {v_u}}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{{{v_u} + {v_d}}}{2}$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'${\\rm{Average}}\\,{\\rm{speed}} = \\frac{{{\\rm{Total \\, distance \\, travelled}}}}{{{\\rm{Time \\, taken }}}}$<br>Let ${t_{1\\,}}\\,{\\rm{and}}\\,{t_2}$  be time taken by the car to go from $X$ to $Y$ and then from $Y$ to $X$ respectively.<br>${\\rm{Then, }} \\, {t_1} + {t_2} = \\frac{{XY}}{{{v_u}}} + \\frac{{XY}}{{{v_d}}} = XY\\left( {\\frac{{{v_u} + {v_d}}}{{{v_u}{v_d}}}} \\right)$<br>Total distance travelled $ = XY + XY = 2XY$<br>Therefore, average speed of the car for this round trip is<br>${v_{av}} = \\frac{{2XY}}{{XY\\left( {\\frac{{{v_u} + {v_d}}}{{{v_u}{v_d}}}} \\right)}}$<br>or   ${v_{av}} = \\frac{{2{v_u}{v_d}}}{{{v_u} + {v_d}}}$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38990,'physics',26,1136,2717,'A spout pipe of diameter $0.5\\,cm$ is connected horizontally at the bottom of a cylindrical vessel of diameter $15\\,cm$ as shown in figure below.<br> <br>When water is poured into the vessel, it leaves the spout in the form of a fountain. Find the height to which the veritical stream of water goes, if the water level in the vessel is maintained at a constant height of $0.45\\,\\,m.$<br>(Take $g = 10\\,m{\\rm{/}}{s^2}$)','[{"text": "$0.45\\\\,m$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$0.75\\\\,m$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$0.10\\\\,m$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$0.01\\\\,m$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Depth of spout pipe,$h = 0.45\\,m$;<br>$\\therefore$ Velocity of efflux through the spout pipe,<br>$v = \\sqrt {2gh}  = \\sqrt {2 \\times 10 \\times 0.45}  = 3\\,m{s^{ - 1}}$<br>According to Bernoulli\'s theorem,<br>$\\frac{P}{\\rho } + gh + \\frac{1}{2}{v^2} = $ constant<br>At the mouth of the spout pipe and the top of fountain, pressure is same (equal to atmospheric pressure).<br>$\\therefore gh{\\rm{ }} + \\frac{1}{2}{v^2} = $ constant <br>At the mouth of the spout pipe, $h=0$ and  $v = 3\\,m{s^{ - 1}}$<br>Also, at the top of fountain, $v=0$<br>Suppose that stream of water in the form of fountain goes up to a height $h^{\\prime}$. Then,<br>$10 \\times h\' + \\frac{1}{2} \\times {(0)^2} = 10 \\times 0 + \\frac{1}{2} \\times {(3)^2}$<br>$\\therefore \\quad h\' = \\frac{1}{2} \\times \\frac{9}{{10}} = 0.45\\,m$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/26_38990_question_1.png'),
(38991,'physics',30,1359,3265,'The amplitude of a particle executing S.H.M with frequency of $60\\,Hz$ is $0.01\\,m$. the maximum value of the acceleration of the particle is','[{"text": "$144\\\\,m/{s^2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$144\\\\,{\\\\pi ^2}m/{s^2}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$288\\\\,{\\\\pi ^2}m/{s^2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{{144}}{{{\\\\pi ^2}}}\\\\,m/{s^2}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Maximum acceleration $=a \\omega^{2}=a \\times 4 \\pi^{2} n^{2}$<br>$ = 0.01 \\times 4 \\times {(\\pi )^2} \\times {(60)^2} = 144{\\pi ^2}\\,m/{s^2}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38992,'physics',3,309,571,'A particle shows distance-time curve as given in this figure. The maximum instantaneous  velocity of the particle is around the point.','[{"text": "$C$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$B$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$D$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$A$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Maximum velocity point means, the point at which slope is maximum at point $C$, slope is maximum.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/3_38992_question_1.png'),
(38994,'physics',30,1359,3265,'If the maximum velocity and maximum acceleration of a particle executing SHM are equal in magnitude, the time period will be :-','[{"text": "$12.56\\\\,{\\\\rm{sec}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1.57\\\\,{\\\\rm{sec}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$6.28\\\\,{\\\\rm{sec}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$3.14\\\\,{\\\\rm{sec}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'$v_{\\text {max }}=A \\omega, \\quad \\alpha_{\\text {max }}=A \\omega^{2}$<br>Given that ${v_{\\max {\\rm{ }}}} = {\\alpha _{\\max {\\rm{ }}}} \\Rightarrow \\omega  = 1{\\rm{ }}T = 2\\,\\pi \\,\\sec $<br>$ = 6.28\\,\\sec $',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38995,'physics',3,314,579,'A motorboat covers a given distance in 6 hours moving downstream on a river. It covers the  same distance in 10 hours moving upstream. The time it taken to cover the same distance in still water is','[{"text": "$9\\\\,{\\\\rm{hours}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$7.5\\\\,{\\\\rm{hours}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$6.5\\\\,{\\\\rm{hours}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$8\\\\,{\\\\rm{hours}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Let ${v_w}$ be the velocity of water and ${v_b}$ be the velocity of motorboat in still water.<br>The distance $x$ covered by the motorboat in moving downstream in 6 $h$ is<br>$x = ({v_b} + {v_w}) \\times 6\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,(1)$<br>Same distance $x$ covered by the motorboat in moving upstream in 10 $h$ is<br>$x = ({v_b} - {v_w}) \\times 10\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,(2)$<br>Equating (1) and (2), we get<br>$({v_b} + {v_w}) \\times 6 = ({v_b} - {v_w}) \\times 10$<br>${v_w} = \\frac{{{v_b}}}{4}$<br>$\\therefore \\,x = ({v_b} + {v_w}) \\times 6 = 7.5\\,\\,{v_b}$<br>Time taken by the motorboat to cover the same distance in still water is <br>$t = \\frac{x}{{{v_b}}} = \\frac{{7.5{v_b}}}{{{v_b}}} = 7.5\\,{\\rm{hours}}$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2010,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(38998,'physics',3,311,573,'A body falls freely for 10 sec. Its average velocity during this journey  $({\\rm{take}}\\,g = 10\\,m{s^{ - 2}})$','[{"text": "$100\\\\,m{s^{ - 1}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$10\\\\,m{s^{ - 1}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$50\\\\,m{s^{ - 1}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$5\\\\,m{s^{ - 1}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'${v_{av}} = \\frac{{{\\rm{Net}}\\,{\\rm{displacement}}}}{{{\\rm{Time}}\\,{\\rm{taken}}}}\\frac{{{s_2} - {s_1}}}{{{t_2} - {t_1}}}$<br>Here, ${t_1} = 0\\,s,{s_1} = 0\\,m,{t_2} = 10\\,s,g = 10\\,m{s^{ - 2}}$<br>${s_2} = u{t_2} + \\frac{1}{2}gt_2^2 = 0 \\times 10 + \\frac{1}{2} \\times 10 \\times {10^2} = 500\\,m$<br>$\\therefore \\,\\,\\,\\,{v_{av}} = \\frac{{500 - 0}}{{10 - 0}} = 50\\,m{s^{ - 1}}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2016,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(39000,'physics',30,1359,3265,'A $0.10\\,kg$ block oscillates back and forth along a horizontal surface. Its displacement from the origin is given by:<br>$x = (10\\,cm)\\cos (10rad/s)t + \\pi /2rad]$. What is the maximum acceleration experienced by the block','[{"text": "$10\\\\,\\\\pi m/{s^2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$10\\\\,\\\\,m/{s^2}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{{10\\\\pi }}{3}\\\\,m/{s^2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{{10\\\\pi }}{2}\\\\,m/{s^2}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'$A = 10 \\times {10^{ - 2}}\\,m\\,\\,{\\rm{and}}\\,\\,\\omega  = 10\\,rad/s$<br>${a_{\\max {\\rm{ }}}} = {\\omega ^2}A = 10 \\times {10^{ - 2}} \\times {10^2} = 10\\,m/{s^2}.$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(39001,'physics',3,314,579,'A bus is moving with a velocity $10\\,m{s^{ - 1}}$ on a straight road. A scooterist wishes to overtake the bus in $100 \\, s$. If the bus is at a distance of 1 $km$ from the scooterist, with what velocity should the scooterist chase the bus?','[{"text": "$30 \\\\, m{s^{ - 1}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$20 \\\\, m{s^{ - 1}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$40 \\\\, m{s^{ - 1}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$50 \\\\, m{s^{ - 1}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Let the velocity of the scooter be $v\\,m{s^{ - 1}}$.<br>Then $(v - 10)100 = 1000\\,{\\rm{or }}v = 20 \\, m{s^{ - 1}}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(39003,'physics',30,1359,3265,'The equation of motion of a particle executing simple harmonic motion is $a+16 \\pi^{2} x=0$. In this equation, $a$ is the linear acceleration in $m / s^{2}$ of the particle at a displacement $x$ in metre. The time period in simple harmonic motion is:','[{"text": "$\\\\frac{1}{2}\\\\,\\\\sec $", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{3}{2}\\\\,\\\\sec $", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2\\\\,\\\\sec $", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1\\\\,\\\\sec $", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'$a=-16 \\pi^{2} x$<br>Standard equation of SHM is $a=-\\omega^{2} x$<br>Hence, comparing two equation, we get $\\omega  = 4\\,\\pi $<br>and $T=\\dfrac{2 \\pi}{\\omega}=\\dfrac{2 \\pi}{4 \\pi}=\\dfrac{1}{2} \\mathrm{sec}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(39004,'physics',3,312,574,'A particle moves a distance $x$ in time $t$ according to the equation $x = {(t + 5)^{ - 1}}$. The acceleration of  particle is proportional to','[{"text": "${({\\\\rm{distance}})^{ - 2}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${({\\\\rm{distance}})^2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${({\\\\rm{velocity}})^{3/2}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${({\\\\rm{velocity}})^{2/3}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Given, <br>Distance $x = {(t + 5)^{ - 1}}$ &emsp; (1)<br>Differentiating Eq. (1) w.r.t. $t$, we get<br>$\\frac{{dx}}{{dt}} = (v) = \\frac{{ - 1}}{{{{(t + 5)}^2}}}$ &emsp; (2)<br>Again, differentiating Eq. (2) w.r.t. $t$, we get<br>$\\frac{{{d^2}x}}{{d{t^2}}} = (a) = \\frac{2}{{{{(t + 5)}^3}}}$ &emsp; (3)<br>Comparing Eqs (2) and (3) , we get<br>$a\\,\\alpha \\,{v^{3/2}}$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(39005,'physics',26,1136,2718,'A liquid flows through a horizontal tube as shown in figure. The velocities of the liquid in the two sections, which have areas of crosssection ${A_1}$ and ${A_2}$, are ${v_1}$ and ${v_2}$, respectively. The difference in the levels of the liquid in the two vertical tubes is $3\\,h.$ If  ${A_2}$ is one by four times ${A_1},v_2^2 = khg.$ Find value of $k.$','[{"text": "8.4", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "6.4", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "10.4", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "7.4", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Let $\\rho $ be density of liquid flowing in the tube.<br>${A_1}{v_1} = {A_2}{v_2}$<br>$\\frac{{{v_1}}}{{{v_2}}} = \\frac{{{A_2}}}{{{A_1}}} = \\frac{{A/4}}{A} = \\frac{1}{4} \\Rightarrow {v_1} = \\frac{{{v_2}}}{4}$<br>Using Bernoulli\'s equation,<br>${P_1} + \\frac{1}{2}\\rho v_1^2 = {P_2} + \\frac{1}{2}\\rho v_2^2$<br>${P_1} - {P_2} = \\frac{1}{2}\\rho \\left( {v_2^2 - v_1^2} \\right)$<br>$3h\\rho g = \\frac{1}{2}\\rho \\left( {v_2^2 - v_1^2} \\right)$<br>$v_2^2 - v_1^2 = 6hg$<br>$v_2^2 - {\\left( {\\frac{{{v_2}}}{4}} \\right)^2} = 6\\,hg$<br>$\\therefore \\frac{{15v_2^2}}{{16}} = 6\\,hg$<br>$\\therefore \\quad v_2^2 = \\frac{{32}}{5}hg$<br>$\\therefore \\quad v_2^2 = 6.4hg$<br>$ \\Rightarrow k = 6.4$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/26_39005_question_1.png'),
(39006,'physics',30,1359,3265,'The average acceleration of a particle performing SHM over one complete oscillation is :','[{"text": "$\\\\dfrac{\\\\omega^{2} A}{\\\\sqrt{2}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{\\\\omega^{2} A}{2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$A \\\\omega^{2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Zero", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'The average acceleration of particle performing SHM over one complete oscillation is zero. Because acceleration is a sinusoidal function and average value of sinusoidal function for complete cycle is zero.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(39007,'physics',3,312,574,'The displacement of a particle is given by $y = a + bt + c{t^2} - d{t^4}$. The initial velocity and acceleration are respectively','[{"text": "$b, - 4d$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$ - b, - 2c$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$b,2c$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$2c, - 4d$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'$y = a + bt + c{t^2} - d{t^4}$<br>$\\therefore v = \\frac{{dy}}{{dt}} = b + 2ct - 4d{t^3}$<br>and $a = \\frac{{dv}}{{dt}} = 2c - 12d{t^2}$<br>Hence, at $t = 0,{v_{initial}} = b\\,{\\rm{and}}\\,{a_{initial}} = 2c$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(39009,'physics',30,1359,3266,'The displacement of two particles executing SHM are represented by equations<br>$y_{1}=2 \\sin (10 t+\\theta), y_{2}=3 \\cos 10 t$.<br>The phase difference between the velocity of these particles is','[{"text": "$-\\\\theta$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\theta$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\theta-\\\\pi / 2$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\theta+\\\\pi / 2$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'We can find the velocities,<br>$v_{1}=\\dfrac{d y_{1}}{d t}=2 \\times 10 \\cos (10 t+\\theta)$<br>and $v_{2}=-3 \\times 10 \\sin 10 t=30 \\cos (10 t+\\pi / 2)$<br>$\\therefore$ Phase difference<br>$=(10 t+\\theta)-(10 t+\\pi / 2)=\\theta-\\pi / 2$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(39010,'physics',3,313,577,'A stone falls freely under gravity. The total distance covered by it in the last second of its journey equals the distance covered by it in first $3 \\,s$ of its motion.  The time for which the stone is in air is','[{"text": "$5\\\\,s$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$12\\\\,s$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$15\\\\,s$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$8\\\\,s$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'${S_t}th = u + at - \\frac{1}{2}a$<br>or $0 + 10\\,t - 5 = \\frac{1}{2} \\times 10 \\times {\\left( 3 \\right)^2}$<br>or $t = 5\\,s$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(39011,'physics',26,1138,2727,'A rectangular frame of $7\\,cm \\times 5\\,cm$ hangs vertically by an arm of a balance. When its edge is just dipped in soap solution in container, extra mass of $0.4\\,g$ must be placed in opposite arm to balance the pull of the film in order to just raise it. Determine surface tension of soap solution in ${\\rm{dyne/}}cm$.','[{"text": "28", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "32", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "20", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "30", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'A soap film is formed between edge length of frame and soap solution in container.<br>$\\therefore \\quad T = \\frac{{mg}}{{2l}} = \\frac{{0.4 \\times 980}}{{2 \\times 7}} = 28\\,dyne/cm$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(39012,'physics',30,1359,3266,'The equation of S.H.M is $y=a \\sin (2 \\pi n t+\\alpha)$, then its phase at time $t$ is','[{"text": "$\\\\alpha$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2 \\\\pi n t$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2 \\\\pi t$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2 \\\\pi n t+\\\\alpha$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'$y=a \\sin (2 \\pi n t+\\alpha)$.<br>Its phase at time<br>$t=2 \\pi n t+\\alpha$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(39013,'physics',3,313,577,'A person throws balls into air vertically upward in regular intervals of time of one second. The  next ball is thrown when the velocity of the ball thrown earlier becomes zero. The height to  which the balls rise is… (Assume $10\\,m{s^{ - 2}}$)','[{"text": "$5\\\\,m$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$10\\\\,m$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$7.5\\\\,m$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$20\\\\,m$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Given that time taken by the ball to reach highest point is $t = 1s$<br>When the first ball reaches the highest point;<br>using $v = u + at,$<br>where $v = 0,a =  - g,t = 1s,$<br>$0 = u - (10)(1)$<br>$u = 10\\,m/s$<br>Using ${v^2} - {u^2} = 2ah$, we get<br>${(0)^2} - {(10)^2} = 2( - 10)(h)$<br>$h = \\frac{{{{(10)}^2}}}{{20}} = 5 \\, m$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(39015,'physics',30,1360,3267,'For a particle executing S.H.M., the kinetic energy $K$ is given by $K=K_{0} \\cos ^{2} \\omega t$. The maximum value of potential energy is:','[{"text": "Zero", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$K_{0}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Not obtainable", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$K_{0} / 2$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'In S.H.M. if rest energy is zero $\\max P . E=\\max K . E=K_{0}\\left(\\cos ^{2} \\omega t\\right)_{\\max }=K_{0} \\times 1=K_{0}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(39016,'physics',3,311,573,'A cyclist starts from the centre $O$ of a circular park of radius one kilometre, reaches the edge $P$ of the park, then cycles along the circumference and returns to the centre along QO as shown in the figure. If the round trip takes ten minutes, the net displacement and average speed of the cyclist (in metre and kilometre per hour) is','[{"text": "$0,1$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{{\\\\pi  + 4}}{2},0$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$21.4,\\\\frac{{\\\\pi  + 4}}{2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$0,21.4$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Since the initial position coincides with the  final position, net displacement of the cyclist=zero<br>Average speed of the cyclist<br>$ = \\frac{{{\\rm{total}}\\,{\\rm{distance}}\\,{\\rm{travelled}}}}{{{\\rm{total}}\\,{\\rm{time}}\\,{\\rm{taken}}}}$<br>$ = \\frac{{OP + PQ + QO}}{{10}}km/\\min  = \\frac{{1 + \\frac{\\pi }{2} \\times 1 + 1}}{{10}}$<br>$ = \\frac{{\\pi  + 4}}{{20}}km/\\min  = \\frac{{\\pi  + 4}}{{20}} \\times 60\\,km/h$<br>$ = 21.4\\,km/h$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2006,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/3_39016_question_1.png'),
(39017,'physics',26,1138,2730,'There is a small hole in a hollow sphere. When it is taken to a depth of $20\\,cm$ under water, water enters in it. If the surface tension of water is $0.07\\,N{\\rm{/}}m,$ the radius of the hole in $\\mu m$ is <br>(Take $g = 10\\,m{\\rm{/}}{s^2}$)','[{"text": "$80\\\\,\\\\mu m$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$60\\\\,\\\\mu m$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$70\\\\,\\\\mu m$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$40\\\\,\\\\mu m$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'When water enters the hollow sphere, force of surface tension is balanced by normal force exerted by the liquid column,<br>$i.e.,{\\rm{ }}T \\times 2\\pi r = \\pi {r^2}h\\rho g$<br> where, $r$ is radius of hole of the sphere.<br>$\\therefore \\quad r = \\frac{{2\\,T}}{{\\,h\\rho g}} = \\frac{{2 \\times 0.07}}{{20 \\times {{10}^{ - 2}} \\times {{10}^3} \\times 10}}$<br>$\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, = 70 \\times {10^{ - 6}}\\,m$<br>$\\therefore \\quad r = 70\\mu m$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(39018,'physics',30,1360,3267,'A particle of mass $m$ is executing SHM about the origin on $x$-axis with frequency $\\sqrt{\\dfrac{k a}{\\pi m}}$, where $k$ is a constant and $a$ is the amplitude. Find its potential energy, if $x$ is the displacement at time $t$.','[{"text": "$k a^{2} x$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$ka{x^2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2 \\\\pi k x^{3}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2 \\\\pi k a x^{2}$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'$2 \\pi f=\\omega ; 2 \\pi \\sqrt{\\dfrac{k a}{\\pi m}}=\\omega$<br>Potential energy $U=\\dfrac{1}{2} m \\omega^{2} x=2 \\pi k a x^{2}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(39019,'physics',3,309,571,'The displacement of the particle starting from rest (at $t = 0$) is given by $s = 6{t^2} - {t^3}$. The time  in second at which the particle will attain zero velocity again, is','[{"text": "$2$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$4$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$6$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$8$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'$v = \\frac{{ds}}{{dt}} = 12t - 3{t^2}$<br>Velocity is zero for $t = 0$ and $t = 4s$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(39021,'physics',30,1360,3267,'The angular velocity and the amplitude of a simple pendulum is $\\omega$ and A respectively. At a displacement $x$ from the mean position if its kinetic energy is $T$ and potential energy is $V$, then the ratio of $T\\,\\,{\\rm{to}}\\,\\,V$ is','[{"text": "${x^2}{\\\\omega ^2}/\\\\left( {{A^2} - {x^2}{\\\\omega ^2}} \\\\right)$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\left( {{A^2} - {x^2}{\\\\omega ^2}} \\\\right)/{x^2}{\\\\omega ^2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$x/\\\\left( {{A^2} - {x^2}} \\\\right)$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\left( {{A^2} - {x^2}} \\\\right)/{x^2}$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Potential energy, $V=\\dfrac{1}{2} m \\omega^{2} x^{2}$<br>and kinetic energy $T=\\dfrac{1}{2} m \\omega^{2}\\left(A^{2}-x^{2}\\right)$<br>$\\therefore \\dfrac{T}{V}=\\dfrac{\\mathrm{A}^{2}-x^{2}}{x^{2}}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(39022,'physics',3,309,571,'The displacement time graphs of two moving particles make angles of $30^\\circ $ and $45^\\circ $ with the $X$-axis. The ratio of their velocities is','[{"text": "$\\\\sqrt 3 :2$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1:1$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1:2$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1:\\\\sqrt 3 $", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Slope of displacement-time graph gives velocity. <br>$\\therefore \\,\\frac{{{v_1}}}{{{v_2}}} = \\frac{{\\tan {\\theta _1}}}{{\\tan \\,{\\theta _2}}} = \\frac{{\\tan 30^\\circ }}{{\\tan \\,45^\\circ }} = \\frac{1}{{\\sqrt 3 }}$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49780,'biology',94,795,1704,'DNA from every tissue (such as blood, hairfollicle, skin, bone, saliva, sperm etc.), from an individual show the_____','[{"text": "Same degree of polymorphism", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "No degree of polymorphism", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Variable degree of polymorphism", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Same degree of monomorphism", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Same degree of polymorphism',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(39024,'physics',30,1360,3267,'The displacement of a particle executing SHM is given by $y=5 \\sin \\left(4 t+\\dfrac{\\pi}{3}\\right)$. If $T$ is the time period and mass of the particle is $2\\,g$, the kinetic energy of the particle when $t=\\dfrac{T}{4}$ is given by','[{"text": "$0.5\\\\,J$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$0.4\\\\,J$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$0.3\\\\,J$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$3\\\\,J$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'The displacement of particle, executing SHM,<br>$\\begin{aligned}& y=5 \\sin \\left(4 t+\\dfrac{\\pi}{3}\\right) \\\\& v=20 \\cos \\left(4 t+\\dfrac{\\pi}{3}\\right)\\end{aligned}$<br>Velocity at $t=\\left(\\dfrac{T}{4}\\right)$<br>$\\begin{aligned}& v=20 \\cos \\left(4 \\times \\dfrac{T}{4}+\\dfrac{\\pi}{3}\\right) \\\\& \\Rightarrow v=20 \\cos \\left(T+\\dfrac{\\pi}{3}\\right)\\end{aligned}$<br>Comparing the given equation with standard equation of SHM<br>$y=a \\sin (\\omega t+\\phi)$,<br>we get $\\omega=4$.<br>$\\text { As } T=\\dfrac{2 \\pi}{\\omega} \\Rightarrow T=\\dfrac{2 \\pi}{4} \\Rightarrow T=\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}$<br>Now, putting value of $T$ in Eq. (2), we get<br>$\\begin{aligned}v & =20 \\cos \\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}+\\dfrac{\\pi}{3}\\right)=-20 \\sin \\dfrac{\\pi}{3} \\\\& =-20 \\times \\dfrac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}=-10 \\times \\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}$<br>The kinetic energy of particle,<br>$KE = \\frac{1}{2}m{v^2}$<br>$\\because m = 2g = 2 \\times {10^{ - 3}}\\,kg$<br>$ = \\frac{1}{2} \\times 2 \\times {10^{ - 3}} \\times {( - 10\\sqrt 3 )^2}$<br>$ = {10^{ - 3}} \\times 100 \\times 3 = 3 \\times {10^{ - 1}} \\Rightarrow K.E = 0.3\\,J.$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(39025,'physics',26,1138,2731,'A uniform capillary tube of length $l$ and inner radius $r$ with its upper end sealed is submerged vertically into water. The outside pressure is ${P_0}$ and surface tension of water is $T$. When water levels inside and outside capillary coincide, length of capillary submerged into water is $\\dfrac{2 l}{\\left(2+\\dfrac{P_{0} r}{c T}\\right)}$. Find value of $c$.','[{"text": "5", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "3", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "2", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "1", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Let $x$ be the length of capillary submerged.\nIn equilibrium for air inside capillary,<br>${P_0}(l\\,A) = P\'(l - x)A$<br>\nWhere, $P\'$ is pressure in capillary after being submerged into water.<br>$\\therefore \\quad P\' = \\frac{{{P_0}l}}{{l - x}}$<br>\nSince level of water inside capillary coincides with outside, the excess pressure,<br>$\\Delta P = P\' - {P_0} = \\frac{{2\\,T}}{r}$<br>$\\therefore \\quad \\frac{{{P_0}l}}{{l - x}} - {P_0} = \\frac{{2\\,T}}{r}$<br>\nSolving above equation,<br>$x = \\frac{l}{{\\left( {1 + \\frac{{{{\\mathop P\\limits^. }_0}r}}{{2\\,T}}} \\right)}}$<br>\nComparing with given relation,<br>$\\frac{{2l}}{{2 + \\frac{{{P_0}r}}{{cT}}}} = \\frac{l}{{\\left( {1 + \\frac{{{P_0}r}}{{2\\,T}}} \\right)}}$<br>$ \\Rightarrow c = 1$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(39026,'physics',3,309,571,'Look at the graphs (1) to (4) carefully and indicate which of these possibly represents one dimensional motion of a particle?','[{"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Graphs 1 and 4 depict scenarios where velocity and position each have two values  respectively at every instant of time which is physically not possible. Graph 3 shows negative speed which is also not possible. Only Graph 2 represents a realistic one dimensional motion where the particle first accelerates, reaches a constant velocity, then decelerates and finally stops.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2006,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(39027,'physics',30,1360,3267,'The potential energy of a simple harmonic oscillator when the particle is half way to its end point is (where $E$ is the total energy)','[{"text": "$\\\\dfrac{2}{3} E$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{1}{8} E$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{1}{4} E$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{1}{2} E$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'$\\dfrac{U}{E}=\\dfrac{\\dfrac{1}{2} K y^{2}}{\\dfrac{1}{2} K a^{2}}=\\dfrac{y^{2}}{a^{2}}=\\dfrac{\\left(\\dfrac{a}{2}\\right)^{2}}{a^{2}}=\\dfrac{1}{4} \\Rightarrow U=\\dfrac{E}{4}$.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(39029,'physics',3,311,573,'A car moves from $A$ to $B$ with a speed of 30 $kmph$ and from $B$ to $A$ with a speed of 20 $kmph$. What is the average speed of the car?','[{"text": "$50\\\\,kmph$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$25\\\\,kmph$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$10\\\\,kmph$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$24\\\\,kmph$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Let $S$ be distance between $A$ and $B$.<br>Let ${t_1}$ be time taken by the car to move from $A$ to $B$ with speed ${v_1}$ and ${t_2}$ be time taken by the car to move from $B$ to $A$ with speed ${v_2}$. Then <br>${t_1} = \\frac{S}{{{v_1}}}\\,{\\rm{and}}\\,{t_2} = \\frac{S}{{{v_2}}}$<br>Average speed of the car<br>${v_{avg}} = \\frac{{{\\rm{Total}}\\,{\\rm{distance}}\\,{\\rm{travelled}}}}{{{\\rm{Total}}\\,{\\rm{time}}\\,{\\rm{taken}}}} = \\frac{{2S}}{{{t_1} + {t_2}}}$<br>$ = \\frac{{2S}}{{\\frac{S}{{{v_1}}} + \\frac{S}{{{v_2}}}}} = \\frac{{2{v_1}{v_2}}}{{{v_1} + {v_2}}}$<br>Here, ${v_1} = 30\\,kmph,{v_2} = 20\\,kmph$<br>$\\therefore {v_{avg}} = \\frac{{2 \\times 30 \\times 20}}{{30 + 20}} = 24\\,kmph$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2014,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(39030,'physics',30,1360,3267,'The average energy in one time period in simple harmonic motion is','[{"text": "$\\\\dfrac{1}{2} m \\\\omega^{2} a^{2}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$m \\\\omega^{2} a^{2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1.5\\\\,m{\\\\omega ^2}{a^2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "None of these", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Total energy in SHM is always constant and it is equal to $\\frac{1}{2}\\,m\\,{\\omega ^2}{a^2}$.<br>So $(T . E)_{\\text {avg }}=\\dfrac{1}{2} m \\omega^{2} a^{2}$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(39032,'physics',3,312,574,'The velocity of a particle moving in the positive direction of $x = $ axis varies as $v = 5\\sqrt x $.  Assuming that at $t = 0$, particle was at $x = 0$.<br>What is the acceleration of the particle?','[{"text": "$12.5\\\\,m/{s^2}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$7.5\\\\,m/{s^2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$5\\\\,m/{s^2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2.5\\\\,m/{s^2}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'${v^2} = 25 \\times \\,$ comparing with ${v^2} = 2as$, we have, $a = 12.5\\,m/{s^2}$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(39033,'physics',30,1362,3269,'A pendulum clock is taken to the bottom of a deep mine. Will it gain or lose time ? How should its length be altered to correct the time?','[{"text": "Looses time, length to be increased", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Looses time, length to be decreased", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Gains time, length to be increased", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Gains time, length to be decreased", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'As we go down to the bottom of mine $g$ value decreases. So $T$ increases and we say that it looses time since it makes less number of oscillations in a given standard interval.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(39044,'physics',3,313,576,'A particle moves in a straight line with a constant acceleration. It changes its velocity from  $10\\,m{s^{ - 1}}$ to $20\\,m{s^{ - 1}}$ while passing through a distance $135\\,m$ in $t$ sec. The value of $t$ is','[{"text": "$10$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$9$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$12$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1.8$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Using ${v^2} - {u^2} = 2\\,as$<br>${(20)^2} - {(10)^2} = 2 \\times a \\times 135$<br>$\\frac{{300}}{{270}} = a = \\frac{{10}}{9}$<br>Now, $v - u = at$<br>$20 - 10 = \\frac{{10}}{9} \\times t$<br>$t = 9\\,s$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(39034,'physics',26,1138,2731,'A capillary tube of radius $r$ is immersed in water and water rises in it to a height $h$. The mass of water in the capillary tube is $12\\,g.$  If another capillary of radius $4 r$ is immersed in water, then the mass of water that would rise in this tube will be','[{"text": "48", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "54", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "38", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "62", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Let ${h_1}$ be the capillary rise in tube of radius $r$.<br>$\\therefore {h_1} = \\frac{{2\\,T\\cos \\theta }}{{r\\rho g}}$<br>Mass of water in the first tube is<br>${m_1} = \\pi {r^2}{h_1}\\rho  = \\pi {r^2}\\left( {\\frac{{2T\\cos \\theta }}{{r\\rho g}}} \\right)\\rho $<br>$ = \\frac{{2\\pi rT\\cos \\theta }}{g}$<br>$ \\Rightarrow \\quad {m_1} \\propto r$<br>$\\therefore \\quad \\frac{{{m_2}}}{{{m_1}}} = \\frac{{{r_2}}}{{{r_1}}} = \\frac{{4r}}{r} = 4$<br>$\\therefore \\quad {m_2} = 4{m_1}$<br>$\\therefore \\quad {m_2} = 4 \\times 12 = 48\\,g$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(39035,'physics',3,312,574,'The velocity - time graph for two bodies $A$ and $B$ are shown. Then the acceleration of $A$ and $B$ are in the ratio','[{"text": "$\\\\tan 25^\\\\circ \\\\,{\\\\rm{to}}\\\\,\\\\tan 50^\\\\circ $", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\cos 25^\\\\circ \\\\,{\\\\rm{to}}\\\\,\\\\cos 50^\\\\circ $", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\tan 25^\\\\circ \\\\,{\\\\rm{to}}\\\\,\\\\tan 40^\\\\circ $", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\sin 25^\\\\circ \\\\,{\\\\rm{to}}\\\\,\\\\sin 40^\\\\circ $", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'As the slope of  $v - t$  graph gives acceleration, so acceleration $a = \\tan \\theta $<br>$\\therefore $ From the given graph<br>The acceleration of $A$ is<br>${a_A} = \\tan 25^\\circ $<br> and that of $B$ is<br>${a_B} = \\tan \\,(90^\\circ  - 40^\\circ ) = \\tan 50^\\circ $<br>$\\frac{{{a_A}}}{{{a_B}}} = \\frac{{\\tan 25^\\circ }}{{\\tan 50^\\circ }}$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2015,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/3_39035_question_1.png'),
(39036,'physics',30,1362,3269,'What effect occurs on the frequency of a pendulum, if it is taken, from the earth\'s surface to deep into a mine?','[{"text": "First increases, then decreases", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Decreases", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Increases", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "No effect", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Since, frequency $\\propto g$, and g decreases therefore frequency also decreases.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(39037,'physics',3,313,576,'A body travelling with uniform acceleration crosses two points $A$ and $B$ with velocities $20\\,m/s$ and $30\\,m/s$ respectively.  The speed of the body at mid-point of $A$ and $B$ is','[{"text": "$24\\\\,m/s\\\\,$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$255\\\\,m/s\\\\,$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$22\\\\,m/s\\\\,$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$25.5\\\\,m/s\\\\,$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'${\\left( {30} \\right)^2} = {\\left( {20} \\right)^2} + 2a\\left( {2s} \\right)\\,\\rm{or}\\,2as = 250$<br>${\\rm{Now,}}\\quad {v^2} = {\\left( {20} \\right)^2} + 2as = 650$<br>$\\therefore \\quad v = 25.5\\,m{\\rm{/}}s$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(39039,'physics',30,1362,3269,'A simple pendulum of length $\\ell$ is making oscillations with its bob immersed in a liquid of density $n$ times less than the density of the bob. What is its period?','[{"text": "$2 \\\\pi \\\\sqrt{\\\\dfrac{\\\\ell}{\\\\left(1-\\\\dfrac{1}{n}\\\\right) g}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$2 \\\\pi \\\\sqrt{\\\\dfrac{l}{n g}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2 \\\\pi \\\\sqrt{\\\\dfrac{\\\\ell}{(n-1) g}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2 \\\\pi \\\\sqrt{\\\\dfrac{n l}{g}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'$T=2 \\pi \\sqrt{\\dfrac{l}{g^{\\prime}}}$<br>$g^{\\prime}$ is the effective acceleration due to gravity Inside the liquid the effective weight is<br>$\\begin{aligned}& m g^{\\prime}=m g-F_{b}=\\rho_{s} V g-\\rho_{l} V g \\\\& \\rho_{s} V g^{\\prime}=\\rho_{s} V g-\\dfrac{\\rho_{s}}{n} V g \\\\& g^{\\prime}=g\\left(1-\\dfrac{1}{n}\\right) \\\\& \\Rightarrow T=2 \\pi \\sqrt{\\dfrac{l}{g\\left(1-\\dfrac{1}{n}\\right)}}\\end{aligned}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(39040,'physics',3,309,571,'A particle shows distance-time curve as shown in the figure. The maximum instantaneous velocity of the particle is around the point','[{"text": "$P$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$S$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$R$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$Q$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'The slope of the tangent to the distance-time curve at any point gives the instantaneous velocity at that point. Thus, the maximum instantaneous velocity of the particle is aroud the point $R$ as the slope is steepest at that point.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2018,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/3_39040_question_1.png'),
(39041,'physics',26,1138,2727,'Maximum possible mass of a greased needle of length $l$ floating on water surface of tension $T$ is $CTl.$ Find value of $C.$ (Take $g = 10\\,m{\\rm{/}}{s^2}$)','[{"text": "0.2", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "0.6", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "0.9", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "0.7", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Let the mass of the needle be $m$. As the liquid surface is distorted, the surface tension forces acing on both sides of the needle make an angle $\\theta$, say, with vertical as shown in the figure.  <img src=\'/img/data/physics/26_39041_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />  Since the forces acting on the needle are $F,F$ and $mg$, resolving the forces vertically for its equilibrium,<br>$\\sum {{F_y}}  = F\\cos \\theta  + F\\cos \\theta  - mg = 0$<br>$ \\Rightarrow m = \\frac{{2\\,F\\cos \\theta }}{g}$ where $F = Tl$<br>$\\therefore m = \\frac{{2\\,Tl\\cos \\theta }}{g}$<br>For $m$ to be maximum, $\\cos \\theta=1$<br>$\\therefore {m_{\\max }} = \\frac{{2\\,Tl}}{{\\,g}} = \\frac{{2Tl}}{{10}} = 0.2\\,Tl$<br>$ \\Rightarrow C = 0.2$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(39042,'physics',30,1362,3269,'The bob of a simple pendulum of length $L$ is released at time $t = 0$ from a position of small angular displacement. Its linear displacement at time $t$ is given by','[{"text": "$x=a \\\\sin \\\\left(2 \\\\pi \\\\sqrt{\\\\dfrac{L}{g}} t\\\\right)$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$x=a \\\\cos \\\\left(2 \\\\pi \\\\sqrt{\\\\dfrac{g}{L}} t\\\\right)$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$x=a \\\\sin \\\\left(\\\\sqrt{\\\\dfrac{g}{L}} t\\\\right)$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$x=a \\\\cos \\\\left(\\\\sqrt{\\\\dfrac{g}{L}} t\\\\right)$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'As the bob executes SHM $\\omega=\\sqrt{\\dfrac{g}{L}}$<br>$x=a \\cos \\omega t$ (since it is released from extreme position)',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(39043,'physics',31,1345,3232,'A stretched wire will produce a note of very high frequency, then the nature of the wire is','[{"text": "Thin and short wire of light material under high tension", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Thick and short wire of light material under high tension", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Thin and long wire of light material under high tension", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Thin and short wire of heavy material under high tension.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Frequency of a stretched string is<br>$f=\\dfrac{n}{2 L} \\sqrt{\\dfrac{T}{\\mu}}$<br>For $f$ to be high $L$ is small, $T$ is large and $\\mu$ is also small.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(39055,'physics',31,1343,3227,'In sinusoidal wave, the time required for a particular point to move from maximum displacement to zero displacement is $0 \\cdot 14$ second. The frequency of the wave is','[{"text": "$2.75\\\\,Hz$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$0.42\\\\,Hz$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$0.56\\\\,Hz$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1.79\\\\,Hz$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'$\\dfrac{T}{4}=0.14 \\Rightarrow T=4 \\times 0.14 s$<br>$\\therefore \\quad f=\\dfrac{1}{T}=\\dfrac{1}{4 \\times 0.14} s^{-1}=1.79 H z$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(39046,'physics',30,1362,3269,'The time period of a simple pendulum is $T$. The pendulum is oscillated with its bob immersed in a liquid of density $\\sigma$. If the density of the bob is $\\rho$ and viscous effect is neglected, the time period of the pendulum will be','[{"text": "${\\\\left( {\\\\frac{\\\\rho }{{\\\\rho  - \\\\sigma }}} \\\\right)^{1/2}}\\\\,T$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${\\\\left( {\\\\frac{\\\\rho }{\\\\sigma }} \\\\right)^{1/2}}\\\\,T$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\left( {\\\\frac{\\\\sigma }{\\\\rho }} \\\\right)^{1/2}}\\\\,T$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\left( {\\\\frac{\\\\sigma }{{\\\\rho  - \\\\sigma }}} \\\\right)^{1/2}}\\\\,T$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'${T^\\prime } = 2\\pi \\sqrt {\\frac{1}{{\\,{g_{eff{\\text{ }}}}}}}  = 2\\pi \\sqrt {\\frac{{\\rho l}}{{(\\rho  - \\sigma )g}}}  = {\\left( {\\frac{\\rho }{{\\rho  - \\sigma }}} \\right)^{1/2}}\\,T$<br>$\\left( {\\because T = 2\\pi \\sqrt {\\frac{l}{{\\,g}}} } \\right)$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(39047,'physics',31,1343,3226,'In the given progressive wave','[{"text": "The speeds of particles $B$ and $C$ are same", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "The speeds of particles $A$, $C$ and $E$ are maximum", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "The particle $F$ moves upward", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "The speed of particle $F$ is maximum", "isCorrect": false}]',1,NULL,NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/31_39047_question_1.png'),
(39048,'physics',3,310,572,'The position of a particle along $x$-axis at time $t$ is given by $x = 2 + t - 3{t^2}$. The displacement and the distance travelled in the interval $t = 0\\,{\\rm{and}}\\,t = 1$ are respectively','[{"text": "$2,2$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$ - 2,2.5$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$0,2$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$ - 2,2.1$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'$x = 2 + t - 3{t^2},\\,v = \\frac{{dx}}{{dt}} = 1 - 6t,$ velocity will become zero at time, $0 = 1 - 6 {t_0}\\,{\\rm{or}}\\,{t_0} = \\frac{1}{6}s,$<br>Since the given time $t = 1\\,s$ is greater than  <br>${t_0} = \\frac{1}{6}s,$<br>${\\rm{distance}} > \\left| {{\\rm{displacement}}} \\right|$<br>Displacement $S = {x_f} - {x_i}$<br>$ = (2 + 1 - 3) - (2 + 0 - 0) =  - 2\\,m$<br>Distance $d = \\left| {{S_0} - {t_0}} \\right| + \\left| {{S_t}_{ - {t_0}}} \\right|$<br>$ = \\frac{{{u^2}}}{{2\\left| a \\right|}} + \\frac{1}{2}\\left| a \\right|{\\left( {t - {t_0}} \\right)^2}$<br>Comparing $v = 1 - 6t$ with $v = u + ut,$ we have <br>$u = 1\\,m{\\rm{/}}s\\,{\\rm{and}}\\,a =  - 6\\,m{\\rm{/}}{s^2}$<br>$\\therefore {\\rm{Distance}} = \\frac{{{{\\left( 1 \\right)}^2}}}{{2 \\times 6}} + \\frac{1}{2} \\times 6 \\times {\\left( {1 - \\frac{1}{6}} \\right)^2}$ <br>$ = 2.1\\,m$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(39050,'physics',30,1362,3269,'A pendulum suspended from the ceiling of an elevator at rest has a time period $T_{1}$, when the elevator moves up with an acceleration $a$ its time period becomes $T_{2}$, when the elevator moves down with an acceleration $a$, its time period becomes $T_{3}$, then:-','[{"text": "$T_{1}=\\\\sqrt{\\\\left(T_{1}^{2}+T_{3}^{2}\\\\right)}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$T_{1}=\\\\sqrt{\\\\left(T_{1} T_{2}\\\\right)}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$T_{1}=\\\\dfrac{T_{2} T_{3} \\\\sqrt{2}}{\\\\sqrt{\\\\left(T_{2}^{2}+T_{3}^{2}\\\\right)}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "None of these", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'${T_1} = 2\\pi \\sqrt {\\frac{\\ell }{g}} \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,(1)$<br>${T_2} = 2\\pi \\sqrt {\\frac{\\ell }{{g + a}}} \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,(2)$<br>${T_3} = 2\\pi \\sqrt {\\frac{\\ell }{{g - a}}} \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,(3)$<br>By eliminating (2) & (3) we get<br>$\\begin{aligned}& \\dfrac{g}{4 \\pi^{2} \\ell}=\\dfrac{T_{3}^{2}+T_{2}^{2}}{2 T_{2}^{2} T_{3}^{2}} \\\\& \\Rightarrow T_{1}^{2}=\\dfrac{2 T_{2}^{2} T_{3}^{2}}{T_{3}^{2}+T_{2}^{2}} \\\\& \\Rightarrow T_{1}=\\dfrac{T_{2} T_{3} \\sqrt{2}}{\\sqrt{T_{2}^{2}+T_{3}^{2}}}\\end{aligned}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(39051,'physics',31,1343,3227,'A wave equation which gives the displacement along the $y$-direction is given by<br>$y=10^{-4} \\sin (60 t+2 x)$ where $x$ and $y$ are in metre and $t$ is time in second. This represents a wave','[{"text": "Travelling with a velocity of $30\\\\,m/s$ in the positive $x$-direction", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Of wavelength $2 \\\\pi m$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Of frequency $\\\\dfrac{30}{\\\\pi}$ hertz", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Of amplitude $2 \\\\times {10^{ - 4}}\\\\,m$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'$y=10^{-4} \\sin (60 t+2 x)$<br>$A = {10^{ - 4}}\\,m,\\omega  = 60\\,rad/\\sec ,k = 2\\,{m^{ - 1}}$<br>Speed of wave $v = \\frac{\\omega }{k} = 30\\,m/\\sec $<br>$f = \\frac{\\omega }{{2\\pi }} = \\frac{{60}}{{2\\pi }} = \\frac{{30}}{\\pi }Hz$<br>$\\lambda  = \\frac{{2\\pi }}{k} = \\pi m$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(39052,'physics',3,309,571,'The displacement-time graphs of two moving particles make angles of $30^\\circ $ and $45^\\circ $ with the $x$-axis as shown in the figure. The ratio of their respective velocities is:','[{"text": "$1:1$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1:2$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1:\\\\sqrt 3 $", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\sqrt 3 :1$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Velocity is slope of $s-t$ graph<br>$v = \\frac{{ds}}{{dt}} = \\tan \\theta $<br>$\\frac{{{v_1}}}{{{v_2}}} = \\frac{{\\tan {\\theta _1}}}{{\\tan {\\theta _2}}} = \\frac{{\\tan 30^\\circ }}{{\\tan 45^\\circ }} = \\frac{1}{{\\sqrt 3 }}$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2022,'NEET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/3_39052_question_1.png'),
(39053,'physics',26,1138,2730,'A bubble having surface tension $T$ and radius $R$ is formed on ring of radius $r(r \\ll R)$. Air of density $\\rho$ is blown inside the tube with velocity $v$ such that air molecule collides perpendicularly with the wall of the bubble and stops. The radius at which the bubble separates from the ring is $\\frac{{ZT}}{{\\rho {v^2}}}.$ Find $Z$.','[{"text": "2", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "7", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "4", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "9", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Excess pressure inside a bubble $ = \\frac{{4T}}{R}$ Let area of wall of bubble where air strikes be $A$.  <img src=\'/img/data/physics/26_39053_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />   $\\therefore \\quad$ Force due to excess pressure $ = \\frac{{4TA}}{R}$<br>Now, force due to striking air  $ = \\rho A{v^2}$<br>For bubble to separate from the ring,<br>$\\rho A v^{2}=\\dfrac{4 T A}{R} \\Rightarrow R=\\dfrac{4 T}{\\rho v^{2}}$<br>$\\Rightarrow Z=4$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL);
INSERT INTO `qb_questions` (`id`, `subject_id`, `chapter_id`, `topic_id`, `subtopic_id`, `question_html`, `options`, `correct_index`, `explanation`, `explanation_image_url`, `difficulty`, `qtype`, `year`, `tag`, `created_at`, `question_image_url`) VALUES
(39054,'physics',30,1362,3269,'Simple pendulum is executing simple harmonic motion with time period $T$. If the length of the pendulum is increased by $21 \\%$, then percentage increase in the time period of the pendulum is:','[{"text": "$13 \\\\%$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$22 \\\\%$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$10 \\\\%$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$50 \\\\%$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'The time period of a simple pendulum of a length $l$ is given by<br>$T = 2\\pi \\sqrt {\\frac{l}{g}} \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,(1)$<br>Where $g$ is acceleration due to gravity.<br>Where length of pendulum increases by $21 \\%$<br>We have $l^{\\prime}=1.21 l$<br>$\\therefore {T^\\prime } = 2\\pi \\sqrt {\\frac{{1.21l}}{g}} \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,(2)$<br>Dividing Eq. (1) by Eq. (2), we get<br>$\\dfrac{T}{T^{\\prime}}=\\sqrt{\\dfrac{1}{1.21}} \\Rightarrow T^{\\prime}=1.1 T$<br>Hence, increase in time period<br>$1.1 T-T=0.1 T$<br>Percent increase in time period <br>$=\\dfrac{0.1 T}{T} \\times 100=10 \\%$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(39056,'physics',3,313,577,'The ratio of the distances travelled by a freely falling body in the ${1^{st}},{2^{nd}},{3^{rd}}\\,{\\rm{and}}\\,{4^{th}}$ second','[{"text": "$1:4:9:16$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1:3:5:7$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$1:1:1:1$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1:2:3:4$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'${S_{nth}} = u + \\frac{a}{2}\\left( {2n - 1} \\right)$<br>$ = 0 + \\frac{a}{2}\\left( {2n - 1} \\right)$<br>${S_{nth}} \\propto \\left( {2n - 1} \\right)$<br>$ \\Rightarrow {S_{1st}},{S_{2nd}},{S_{3rd}},{S_{4th}}$<br>$ = \\left[ {2\\left( 1 \\right) - 1} \\right]:\\left[ {2\\left( 2 \\right) - 1} \\right]:\\left[ {2\\left( 3 \\right) - 1} \\right]:\\left[ {2\\left( 4 \\right) - 1} \\right]$<br>$ = 1:3:5:7$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2022,'NEET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(39058,'physics',30,1362,3270,'An object is attached to the bottom of a light vertical spring and set vibrating. The maximum speed of the object is $15\\,cm/\\sec $ and the time $\\frac{\\pi }{5}\\,\\sec $, find its amplitude','[{"text": "2.0", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "3.0", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "1.0", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "1.5", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Maximum velocity, $v_{\\max }=a \\omega$<br>${\\rm{or }}\\,\\,\\,{\\mkern 1mu} {\\kern 1pt} {v_{\\max }} = a\\left( {\\frac{{2\\pi }}{T}} \\right)$<br>$a = \\frac{{{v_{\\max }}T}}{{2\\pi }} = \\frac{{15 \\times 3.14}}{{2 \\times 3.14 \\times 5}} = 1.5\\,cm$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(39059,'physics',31,1343,3227,'At $t = 0$, a transverse wave pulse travelling in the positive $x$ direction with a speed of $2\\,m/s$ in a wire is described by the function $y=\\dfrac{6}{x^{2}}$, given that $x \\neq 0$. Transverse velocity of a particle at $x=2 m$ and $t=2$ seconds is','[{"text": "$ - 3\\\\,m/s$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$ 8\\\\,m/s$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$ - 8\\\\,m/s$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$ 3\\\\,m/s$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'$y(x, t=0)=\\dfrac{6}{x^{2}}$ then $y(x, t)=\\dfrac{6}{(x-2 t)^{2}}$<br>$ \\Rightarrow \\frac{{\\partial y}}{{\\partial t}} = \\frac{{24}}{{{{(x - 2t)}^3}}}{\\text{ at }}x = 2,t = 2$<br>${v_y} = \\frac{{24}}{{{{( - 2)}^3}}} =  - 3\\,m/s.$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(39060,'physics',3,310,572,'A wheel of radius 1 $m$ rolls forward half a revolution on a horizontal ground.  The magnitude of the displacement of the point of the wheel initially in contact with the ground is','[{"text": "$2\\\\pi $", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\sqrt 2 \\\\pi $", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\sqrt {{\\\\pi ^2} + 4} $", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\pi $", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Horizontal distance by the wheel in half revolution $ = \\pi R$<br> So the displacement of the point which was initially in contact with ground<br>$ = AA\' = \\sqrt {{{\\left( {\\pi R} \\right)}^2} + {{\\left( {2R} \\right)}^2}} $<br>$ = R\\sqrt {{\\pi ^2} + 4} $<br>$ = \\sqrt {{\\pi ^2} + 4} \\, ({\\rm{as}} \\, R = 1 \\, m)$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(39062,'physics',30,1362,3270,'If point $P$ is accelerated upwards:-','[{"text": "Time period of oscillation changes but mean position remains unchanged", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Time period of oscillation and mean position unchanged", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Time period of oscillation does not change but mean position shifts upward", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Time period of oscillation does not change but mean position shitfs downward", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Time period of a spring block system does not depend on acceleration of the frame. So correct option is (4)',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/30_39062_question_1.jpg'),
(39063,'physics',31,1343,3227,'Which of the following does not represent a travelling wave?','[{"text": "$y=y_{m} \\\\sin k(x+v t)$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$y=\\\\sin (x-v t)$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$y=f\\\\left(x^{2}-v t^{2}\\\\right)$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$y=y_{m} \\\\log (x-v t)$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'$y=f\\left(x^{2}-v t^{2}\\right)$ doesn\'t follow the standard wave equation $f(x \\pm t)$.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(39064,'physics',3,309,571,'Two cars $P$ and $Q$ start from a point at the same time in a straight line and their positions are represented by ${x_P}(t) = at + b{t^2}$ and ${x_Q}(t) = ft - {t^2}.$ At what time do the cars have the same velocity?','[{"text": "$\\\\frac{{a - f}}{{1 + b}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{{a + f}}{{2(b - 1)}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{{a + f}}{{2(1 + b)}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{{f - a}}{{2(1 + b)}}$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'$\\frac{{d{x_p}}}{{dt}} = a + 2bt$<br>$\\frac{{d{x_q}}}{{dt}} = f - 2t$<br>$a + 2bt = f - 2t$<br>$t = \\frac{{f - a}}{{\\left( {2b + 2} \\right)}}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2016,'NEET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(39066,'physics',30,1362,3271,'In figures $S_{1}$ and $S_{2}$ are identical springs. the oscillation frequency of the mass $m$ is $f$. If one spring is removed, the frequency will become','[{"text": "$2 f$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$f$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$f / \\\\sqrt{3}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$f / \\\\sqrt{2}$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Effective spring constant ${K_{eff{\\rm{ }}}} = 2K$.<br>$f=\\dfrac{1}{2 \\pi} \\sqrt{\\dfrac{2 K}{m}}$<br>When one spring is removed, $K_{\\text {eff }}$ becomes $K$ new frequency<br>$\\begin{aligned}& f^{\\prime}=\\dfrac{1}{2 \\pi} \\sqrt{\\dfrac{K}{m}} \\\\& \\dfrac{f^{\\prime}}{f}=\\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\Rightarrow f^{\\prime}=\\dfrac{f}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/30_39066_question_1.jpg'),
(39067,'physics',31,1343,3227,'The phase difference between two waves, represented by<br>${y_1} = {10^{ - 6}}\\sin \\{ 100t + (x/50) + 0.5\\} m$<br>${y_2} = {10^{ - 6}}\\cos \\{ 100t + (x/50)\\} m$<br>Where $x$ is expressed in meters and $t$ is expressed in seconds, is approximately:','[{"text": "1.07 radians", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "1.5 radians", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "0.5 radians", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "2.07 radians", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'$y_{1}=10^{-6} \\sin (100 t+(x / 50)+0.5) m$<br>$ = {10^{ - 6}}\\cos \\left( {\\frac{\\pi }{2} - (100t + x/50 + 0.5)} \\right)$<br>$ = {10^{ - 6}}\\cos (100t + x/50 + 0.5 - \\pi /2)m$<br>${y_2} = {10^{ - 6}}\\cos (100t + x/50)m$<br>$\\therefore \\phi  = \\pi /2 - 0.5 = 1.07{\\mkern 1mu} rad$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(39068,'physics',3,314,579,'Preeti reached the metro station and found that the escalator was not working. She walked up the stationary escalator in time ${t_1}.$ On other days, if she remains stationary on the moving escalator, then the escalator takes her up in time ${t_2}.$ The time taken by her to walk up on the moving escalator will be','[{"text": "$\\\\frac{{{t_1}{t_2}}}{{{t_2} - {t_1}}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{{{t_1}{t_2}}}{{{t_2} + {t_1}}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${t_1} - {t_2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{{{t_1} + {t_2}}}{2}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'$v_1 = $ velocity of  Preeti<br>$v_2 = $ velocity of escalator<br>$\\ell  = $ distance<br>$t = \\frac{\\ell }{{{{\\rm{v}}_1} + {{\\rm{v}}_2}}} = \\frac{\\ell }{{\\frac{\\ell }{{{t_1}}} + \\frac{\\ell }{{{t_2}}}}} = \\frac{{{t_1}{t_2}}}{{{t_1} + {t_2}}}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2017,'NEET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(39070,'physics',30,1362,3271,'A mass $m$ is suspended separately by two different springs in successive order, then time periods is $t_{1}$ and $t_{2}$ respectively. If $m$ is connected by both springs as shown in figure, then time period is $t_{0}$, the correct relation is','[{"text": "$t_{0}^{-2}=t_{1}^{-2}+t_{2}^{-2}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$t_{0}^{2}=t_{1}^{2}+t_{2}^{2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$t_{0}=t_{1}+t_{2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$t_{0}^{-1}=t_{1}^{-1}+t_{2}^{-1}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'As, $t_{1}=2 \\pi \\sqrt{\\dfrac{m}{k_{1}}} \\quad$ or<br>$k_{1}=\\dfrac{4 \\pi^{2} m}{t_{1}^{2}}$<br>Similarly, $k_{2}=\\dfrac{4 \\pi^{2} m}{t_{2}^{2}}$<br>$\\begin{aligned}& \\text { and } \\quad\\left(k_{1}+k_{2}\\right)=\\dfrac{4 \\pi^{2} m}{t_{0}^{2}} \\\\& \\therefore \\quad \\dfrac{4 \\pi^{2} m}{t_{0}^{2}}=\\dfrac{4 \\pi^{2} m}{t_{1}^{2}}+\\dfrac{4 \\pi^{2} m}{t_{2}^{2}} \\\\& \\text { or } \\quad \\dfrac{1}{t_{o}^{2}}=\\dfrac{1}{t_{1}^{2}}+\\dfrac{1}{t_{2}^{2}}\\end{aligned}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/30_39070_question_1.jpg'),
(39071,'physics',31,1343,3226,'If phase of the particle $A$ at time \' $t$ \' $s$ is greater than phase of next particle $B$ at that time. The direction of travel of wave is','[{"text": "$B$ to $A$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$A$ to $B$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Parallel to line $BA$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Perpendicular to line $BA$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,NULL,NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(39072,'physics',3,313,577,'When an object is shot from the bottom of a long smooth inclined plane kept at an angle $60^\\circ $ with horizontal, it can travel a distance ${x_1}$ along the plane. But when the inclination is decreased to $30^\\circ $ and the same object is shot with the same velocity, it can travel ${x_2}$ distance Then ${x_1}:{x_2}$ will be:','[{"text": "$1:\\\\sqrt 2 $", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\sqrt 2 :1$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1:\\\\sqrt 3 $", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$1:2\\\\sqrt 3 $", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Stopping distance on, an incline plane that makes an angle $\\theta $ w.r.t. horizontal is $x = \\frac{{{u^2}}}{{2g\\sin \\theta }}$<br>${x_1} = \\frac{{{u^2}}}{{2g\\sin 60^\\circ }}$ and ${x_2} = \\frac{{{u^2}}}{{2g\\sin 30^\\circ }}$<br>$ \\Rightarrow \\frac{{{x_1}}}{{{x_2}}} = \\frac{{\\sin 30^\\circ }}{{\\sin 60^\\circ }} = \\frac{{1 \\times 2}}{{2 \\times \\sqrt 3 }} = 1:\\sqrt 3 $',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',2019,'NEET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(39073,'physics',30,1362,3270,'A block of mass $M$ is suspended from a light spring of force constant $k$. Another mass $m$ moving downwards hits the mass $M$ and gets embeded in it. What will be the shift in mean position of the system.','[{"text": "$\\\\dfrac{2 M g}{k}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{M g}{K}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{m g}{K}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{2 m g}{K}$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'The initial shift in mean position of the block of mass $\\mathrm{M}$ is $x_{1}=\\dfrac{M g}{K}$ (from natural length)<br>The new mean poistion of the combined mass<br>$\\text { is } \\quad \\begin{gathered}x_{2}=\\dfrac{M g+m g}{K} \\\\\\text { shift }=\\dfrac{m g}{K}\\end{gathered}$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(39074,'physics',26,1138,2727,'A metal wire of density $\\rho$ floats on water surface horizontally. For it not to sink in water maximum possible radius of wire should be $\\frac{{{T^n}}}{{\\sqrt {\\pi \\rho g} }}$ where, $T$ is surface tension of water. Find $n$.','[{"text": "0.5", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "0.8", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "0.3", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "0.9", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'For wire to float into water, its weight should be balanced by the surface tension of the water.<br>$\\therefore \\,\\,mg = Tl\\quad ({\\rm{where}},\\,\\,l = {\\rm{length}}\\,\\,{\\rm{of}}\\,\\,{\\rm{the}}\\,\\,{\\rm{wire}})$<br>$\\therefore \\quad V\\rho g = Tl$<br>$\\therefore \\pi {r^2}l\\rho g = Tl$<br>$\\therefore \\quad {r^2} = \\frac{T}{{\\pi \\rho g}}$<br>$\\therefore \\quad r = \\sqrt {\\frac{T}{{\\pi \\rho g}}}  \\Rightarrow {T^n} = \\sqrt T $<br>$\\therefore \\quad n = \\frac{1}{2} = 0.5$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(39075,'physics',31,1344,3228,'Two blocks each having a mass of $3.2\\,kg$ are connected by a wire $CD$ and the system is suspended from the ceiling by another wire $AB$. The linear mass density of the wire $AB$ is $10gm/m$ and that of $CD$ is $8gm/m$. The ratio of wave speeds in strings $AB,CD$  is $\\sqrt{\\dfrac{8}{x}}$. The value of $x$ is','[{"text": "4", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "5", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "3", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "2", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'<br>${T_{AB}} = 64N,{T_{CD}} = 32N$<br>${v_{AB}} = \\sqrt {\\frac{{64}}{{10 \\times {{10}^{ - 3}}}}} \\,m/s,\\,\\,\\,{\\mkern 1mu} {\\kern 1pt} {v_{CD}} = \\sqrt {\\frac{{32}}{{8 \\times {{10}^{ - 3}}}}} \\,m/s$<br>$\\therefore \\frac{{{v_{AB}}}}{{{v_{CD}}}} = \\sqrt {\\frac{{64}}{{10}} \\times \\frac{8}{{32}}}  = \\sqrt {\\frac{8}{5}} \\,\\,\\,$<br>${\\mkern 1mu} {\\kern 1pt} \\therefore x = 5$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/31_39075_question_1.png'),
(39076,'physics',3,313,577,'A ball is thrown vertically downward with a velocity of  $20\\,m/s$ from the top of a tower. It  hits the ground after some time with a velocity of $80\\,m/s$.The height of the tower is:<br>$(g = 10\\,m/{s^2})$','[{"text": "$340\\\\,m$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$320\\\\,m$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$300\\\\,m$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$360\\\\,m$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'${v^2} = {u^2} + 2gh$<br>${80^2} = {20^2} + 2 \\times 10h$<br>$h = 300 \\, m$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2020,'NEET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(39078,'physics',30,1362,3271,'Match the following:<div class="matching-question"></div>','[{"text": "A - P, B - Q, C - S, D - R", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "A - S, B - R, C - P, D - Q", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "A - R, B - P,  C - S, D - Q", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "A - P, B - R, C - Q, D - S", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'In figure $A$, three springs are connected in parallel. The effective spring constant is,<br>$k_{\\text {eff }}=k_{1}+k_{2}+k_{3} \\text {. }$<br>$\\therefore T=2 \\pi \\sqrt{\\dfrac{m}{k_{1}+k_{2}+k_{3}}}$<br>In figure $B$, three identical springs are connected in parallel. The effective spring constant is<br>$k_{\\mathrm{eff}}=k+k+k=3 k \\therefore T=2 \\pi \\sqrt{\\dfrac{m}{3 k}}$<br>In figure $C$, two springs are connected in series. The effective spring constant is<br>$\\begin{aligned}& \\dfrac{1}{k_{e f f}}=\\dfrac{1}{k_{1}}+\\dfrac{1}{k_{2}}=\\dfrac{k_{2}+k_{1}}{k_{1} k_{2}} \\Rightarrow k_{e f f}=\\dfrac{k_{1} k_{2}}{k_{1}+k_{2}} \\\\& \\therefore T=2 \\pi \\sqrt{\\dfrac{m\\left(k_{1}+k_{2}\\right)}{k_{1} k_{2}}}\\end{aligned}$<br>In figure $D$, two identical springs are connected in series. The effective spring constant is<br>$\\begin{aligned}& k_{\\text {eff }}=\\dfrac{(k)(k)}{k+k}=\\dfrac{k}{2} \\\\& \\therefore T=2 \\pi \\sqrt{\\dfrac{2 m}{k}}\\end{aligned}$<br>So option (2) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Match the following',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/30_39078_question_1.jpg'),
(39113,'physics',30,1359,3263,'A particle executing simple harmonic motion along $y$-axis has its motion described by the equation $y=A \\sin (\\omega t)+B$. The amplitude of the simple harmonic motion is','[{"text": "$B$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$A$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\sqrt{A+B}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$A+B$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'The amplitude is the maximum displacement from the mean position.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(39079,'physics',31,1345,3232,'A sinusoidal wave with amplitude $y_{m}$ is travelling with speed $v$ on a string with linear density $\\mu$. The angular frequency of the wave is $\\omega$. Mark the one which is correct (only one parameter is changed at a time).','[{"text": "Doubling the frequency doubles the rate at which energy is carried along the string", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "If the amplitude were doubled, the rate at which energy is carried would be halved", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "If the amplitude were doubled, the rate at which energy is carried would be doubled", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "The rate at which energy is carried is directly proportional to the velocity of the wave", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Rate of energy flow is given by<br>$P = \\frac{1}{2}{a^2}{\\omega ^2}\\mu  \\cdot v$<br>$\\therefore \\,\\,\\,{\\mkern 1mu} {\\kern 1pt} P\\alpha v$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(39080,'physics',3,313,577,'A small block slides down on a smooth inclined plane, starting from rest at time $t = 0$. Let ${s_n}$ be the distance travelled by the block in the interval $t = n - 1$ to $t = n$. Then , the ratio $\\frac{{{S_n}}}{{{S_{n + 1}}}}$ is','[{"text": "$\\\\frac{{2n - 1}}{{2n + 1}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{{2n + 1}}{{2n - 1}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{{2n}}{{2n - 1}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{{2n - 1}}{{2n}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'$S = ut + \\frac{1}{2}a{t^2}$<br>$ \\Rightarrow {S_n} = un + \\frac{1}{2}a{n^2} - \\left[ {u\\left( {n - 1} \\right) + \\frac{1}{2}a{{\\left( {n - 1} \\right)}^2}} \\right]$<br>$ = u + \\frac{1}{2}a\\left( {2n - 1} \\right)$<br>$\\frac{{{S_n}}}{{{S_{n + 1}}}} = \\frac{{u + \\frac{1}{2}a\\left( {2n - 1} \\right)}}{{u + \\frac{1}{2}a\\left\\{ {2\\left( {n + 1} \\right) - 1} \\right\\}}}$<br>Given that $u = 0$<br>$ = \\frac{{\\frac{1}{2}a\\left( {2n - 1} \\right)}}{{\\frac{1}{2}a\\left( {2n + 1} \\right)}} = \\frac{{2n - 1}}{{2n + 1}}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2021,'NEET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(39082,'physics',30,1361,3268,'Two linear simple harmonic motions of equal amplitude and frequency are imposed on a particle along $x$ and $y$ axis respectively. The initial phase difference between them is $\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}$. The resultant path followed by the particle is:','[{"text": "A circle", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "A straight line", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "An ellipse", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "A parabola", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'$x = A\\sin \\omega t\\,\\,\\& \\,\\,y = A\\sin \\left( {\\omega  + \\frac{\\pi }{2}} \\right) = A\\cos \\omega t$<br>$\\dfrac{x^{2}}{A^{2}}+\\dfrac{y^{2}}{A^{2}}=1 \\quad \\Rightarrow x^{2}+y^{2}=A^{2} \\quad \\text { (circular) }$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(39083,'physics',31,1345,3229,'Two periodic waves of intensities $I_{1}$ and $I_{2}$ pass through a region at the same time in the same direction. The sum of the maximum and minimum intensities is','[{"text": "$\\\\left(\\\\sqrt{I_{1}}+\\\\sqrt{I_{2}}\\\\right)^{2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\left(\\\\sqrt{I_{1}}-\\\\sqrt{I_{2}}\\\\right)^{2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2\\\\left(I_{1}+I_{2}\\\\right)$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$I_{1}+I_{2}$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'${I_{\\max {\\text{ }}}} = {I_1} + {I_2} + 2\\sqrt {{I_1}{I_2}} $<br>${I_{\\max {\\text{ }}}} = {I_1} + {I_2} - 2\\sqrt {{I_1}{I_2}} $<br>$\\therefore$ Sum of maximum and minimum intensities<br>$=2\\left(I_{1}+I_{2}\\right) \\text {. }$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(39084,'physics',3,310,572,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Displacement of a body may be zero when distance travelled by it is not zero.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> The displacement is the longest distance between initial and final position.','[{"text": "Both assertion and reason are correct and reason is the correct explanation of assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both assertion and reason are correct but reason is not the correct explanation of assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'The displacement is the shortest distance between initial and final position. When final position of a body coincides with its initial position, displacement is zero, but the distance travelled is not zero. So option (3) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(39085,'physics',30,1361,3268,'A particle is subjected to two simple harmonic motions in the same direction having equal amplitudes and equal frequency. If the resulting amplitude is equal to the amplitude of individual motions, the phase difference between them is:','[{"text": "$\\\\dfrac{\\\\pi}{3}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{2 \\\\pi}{3}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{\\\\pi}{6}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{\\\\pi}{2}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'$A^{2}=a^{2}+a^{2}+2 a^{2} \\cos \\phi$<br>$\\Rightarrow a^{2}=2 a^{2}+2 a^{2} \\cos \\phi \\Rightarrow \\phi=\\dfrac{2 \\pi}{3}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(39086,'physics',26,1138,2732,'A drop of some liquid of volume $0.09\\,c{m^3}$ is placed on the surface of a glass slide. Then another glass slide is placed on it in such a way that the liquid forms a thin layer of area $30\\,c{m^2}$ between the surfaces of the two slides. To separate the slides, a force of $16 \\times 10^{5}$ dyne has to be applied normal to the surfaces. The surface tension of the liquid (in $dyne - c{m^{ - 1}}$) is','[{"text": "60", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "75", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "90", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "80", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Let thickness of layer be $t$<br>$V = At \\Rightarrow t = \\frac{V}{A}$<br>$2r = \\frac{V}{A} \\Rightarrow r = \\frac{V}{{2\\,A}}$<br>also, $\\Delta P = \\frac{T}{r}$<br>$\\therefore F = \\Delta P \\times A = \\frac{T}{r} \\times A = \\frac{T}{{\\left( {\\frac{V}{{2\\,A}}} \\right)}}A = \\frac{{2T{A^2}}}{{\\,V}}$<br>$\\therefore \\quad T = \\frac{{FV}}{{2\\,{A^2}}} = \\frac{{16 \\times {{10}^5} \\times 0.09}}{{2 \\times {{(30)}^2}}} = 80\\,dyne/cm$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(39087,'physics',31,1345,3229,'Two waves having equations<br>$x_{1}=a \\sin \\left(\\omega t+\\varphi_{1}\\right), x_{2}=a \\sin \\left(\\omega t+\\varphi_{2}\\right)$<br>If in the resultant wave the frequency and amplitude remain equal to those of superimposing waves. Then phase difference between them is','[{"text": "$\\\\dfrac{2 \\\\pi}{3}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{\\\\pi}{3}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{\\\\pi}{6}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{\\\\pi}{4}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Superposition of waves does not alter the frequency of resultant wave and resultant amplitude <br>$\\begin{aligned}& \\Rightarrow a^{2}=a^{2}+a^{2}+2 a^{2} \\cos \\varphi=2 a^{2}(1+\\cos \\varphi) \\\\& \\Rightarrow \\cos \\varphi=-1 / 2=\\cos 2 \\pi / 3 \\therefore \\varphi=2 \\pi / 3 .\\end{aligned}$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(39133,'physics',30,1359,3263,'The displacement of a particle along the $x$-axis is given by $x=a \\sin ^{2} \\omega t$. The motion of the particle corresponds to','[{"text": "Simple harmonic motion of frequency $\\\\omega / \\\\pi$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Simple harmonic motion of frequency $\\\\omega / 2 \\\\pi$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Non simple harmonic motion", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Simple harmonic motion of frequency $3 \\\\omega / 2 \\\\pi$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'$x=a \\sin ^{2} \\omega t=\\dfrac{a}{2}(1-\\cos 2 \\omega t)$.<br>Frequency $=\\dfrac{2 \\omega}{2 \\pi}=\\dfrac{\\omega}{\\pi}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(39088,'physics',3,311,573,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> The speedometer of an automobile measures the average speed of the automobile.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Average velocity is equal to total displacement per total time taken.','[{"text": "Both assertion and reason are correct and reason is the correct explanation of assertion", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both assertion and reason are correct but reason is not the correct explanation of assertion", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but reason is correct.", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Speed meter measures instantaneous speed of automobile. ${v_{avg}} = \\frac{{\\Delta s}}{{\\Delta t}}$<br>So option (4) is  correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(39089,'physics',30,1362,3272,'A coin is placed on a horizontal platform which undergoes vertical simple harmonic motion of angular frequency $\\omega$. The amplitude of oscillation is gradually increased. The coin will leave contact with the platform for the first time','[{"text": "For an amplitude of $g^{2} / \\\\omega^{2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "For an amplitude of $g / \\\\omega^{2}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "At the mean position of the platform", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "At the highest position of the platform", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'The coin will leave contact when it is at the highest point and for that condition<br>Maximum acceleration $=$ Acceleration due to gravity<br>$\\Rightarrow \\omega^{2} A=g \\quad \\Rightarrow$ Amplitude $A=\\dfrac{g}{\\omega^{2}}$.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(39091,'physics',31,1345,3232,'When a stationary wave is formed then its frequency is','[{"text": "Half that of the individual waves", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Twice that of the individual waves", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Same as that of the individual waves", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "None of these", "isCorrect": false}]',2,NULL,NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(39092,'physics',3,310,572,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Position-time graph of a stationary object is a straight line parallel to time axis.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> For a stationary object, position does not change with time.','[{"text": "Both assertion and reason are correct and reason is the correct explanation of assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both assertion and reason are correct but reason is not the correct explanation of assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Option (1) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(39093,'physics',30,1360,3267,'The amplitude of a simple harmonic oscillator is doubled. Which of the following is doubled?','[{"text": "Its frequency", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Its period", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Its maximum velocity", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Its total energy", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'${\\rm{Maximum}}\\,{\\rm{velocity}} = {v_{{\\rm{max}}}} = \\omega A$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(39095,'physics',31,1343,3227,'At a certain instant a stationary transverse wave is found to have maximum kinetic energy. The appearance of string at that instant is','[{"text": "Sinusoidal shape with amplitude $A$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Straight line", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Sinusoidal shape with amplitude $A/3$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Sinusoidal shape with amplitude $A/2$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Particles have kinetic energy maximum at mean position. So the string will be at mean position present as a straight line.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(39096,'physics',3,309,571,'If the velocity of a particle is $v = At + B{t^2},$ where $A$ and $B$ are constants, then the distance travelled by it between $1 \\, s$ and $2 \\, s$ is','[{"text": "$\\\\frac{3}{2}A + 4B$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$3A + 7B$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{3}{2}A + \\\\frac{7}{3}B$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{A}{2} + \\\\frac{B}{3}$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'$v = At + B{t^2} \\Rightarrow \\frac{{dx}}{{dt}} = At + B{t^2}$<br>$ \\Rightarrow \\int_0^x {dx}  = \\int_1^2 {\\left( {At + B{t^2}} \\right)dt} $<br>$ \\Rightarrow x = \\frac{A}{2}\\left( {{2^2} - {1^2}} \\right) + \\frac{B}{3}\\left( {{2^3} - {1^3}} \\right) = \\frac{{3A}}{2} + \\frac{{7B}}{3}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2016,'NEET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(39097,'physics',30,1358,3261,'Select the correct alternatives','[{"text": "A simple harmonic motion is necessarily periodic", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "A simple harmonic motion is necessarily oscillatory", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Oscillatory motions are all periodic.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "All of the above", "isCorrect": true}]',3,NULL,NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(39099,'physics',31,1345,3232,'When stationary waves are set up, pick out the correct statement from the following','[{"text": "All the particles in the medium are in the same phase of vibration at all times and distances", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "The particles between two consecutive nodes are in phase.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "The phase lag along the path of the wave increases as the distance from the source increases", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Only antinodes are in same phase", "isCorrect": false}]',1,NULL,NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(39100,'physics',3,310,572,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Displacement of a body is vector sum of the area under velocity-time graph.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Displacement is a vector quantity.','[{"text": "Both assertion and reason are correct and reason is the correct explanation of assertion", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both assertion and reason are correct but reason is not the correct explanation of assertion", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Displacement = velocity $ \\times $ time.<br>Displacement is a equal to the sum of the area under velocity-time graph. So option (1) is  correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(39101,'physics',30,1362,3272,'The motion which is not simple harmonic is:','[{"text": "Motion of simple pendulum", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Vertical oscillation of a spring", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Vertical oscialltion of a wooden plank floating in a liquid", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Motion of a planet around the sun", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'The motion of planets around the sun is periodic but not simple harmonic motion.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(39169,'physics',30,1359,3265,'Which one of the following statements is true for the speed \' $v$ \' and the acceleration \' $a$ \' of a particle executing simple harmonic motion','[{"text": "When \' $v$ \' is zero, $a$ is zero", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Value of $a$ is zero, whatever may be the value of \' $v$ \'", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "When \' $v$ \' is maximum, $a$ is maximum", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "When \' $v$ \' is maximum, $a$ is zero", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'In SHM if $v=v_{\\text {max }}$ then $a=0$ if $v=0$ then $a=a_{\\max }$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(39102,'physics',26,1138,2731,'A long capillary tube of radius $0.2\\,mm$ is placed vertically inside a beaker of water of surface tension $0.07\\,N{\\rm{/}}m.$ Water due to zero angle of contact rises upto height of $7.05\\,cm$ in tube. Now if tube is pushed into water so that only $5\\,cm$ of its length is above the surface, find new angle of contact in degrees between water and glass surface. (Take $\\sqrt{2}=1.41$ )','[{"text": "$45^\\\\circ $", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$55^\\\\circ $", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$70^\\\\circ $", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$65^\\\\circ $", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'<img src=\'/img/data/physics/26_39102_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />  If two points are just above and below the meniscus then, $\\rho g{h_0} = \\frac{{2\\,T}}{R}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,(1)$<br>When $\\theta  = 0;R = r$ and ${h_0} = h$<br>$\\therefore \\rho gh = \\frac{{2\\,T}}{r}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,(2)$<br>From equations (1) and (2),<br>$ \\Rightarrow \\frac{r}{R} = \\frac{{{h_0}}}{{\\,h}}$<br>$\\therefore $  New angle of contact,<br>$\\theta  = {\\cos ^{ - 1}}\\left( {\\frac{r}{R}} \\right) = {\\cos ^{ - 1}}\\left( {\\frac{{{h_0}}}{{\\,h}}} \\right)$<br>$ = {\\cos ^{ - 1}}\\left( {\\frac{5}{{7.05}}} \\right)$<br>$ = {\\cos ^{ - 1}}\\left( {\\frac{5}{{5 \\times \\sqrt 2 }}} \\right) = {\\cos ^{ - 1}}\\left( {\\frac{1}{{\\sqrt 2 }}} \\right)$<br>$\\therefore \\theta  = 45^\\circ $',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(39103,'physics',31,1345,3232,'In the standing wave shown, particles at the positions $A$ and $B$ have a phase difference of','[{"text": "$\\\\dfrac{\\\\pi}{2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$0$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\pi$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{5 \\\\pi}{6}$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Phase difference between any two points present in two adjacent loops is always equal to $\\pi$.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/31_39103_question_1.png'),
(39104,'physics',3,311,573,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> The average speed of a body over a given interval of time is equal to magnitude of the average velocity of the body in the same interval of time if a body moves in a straight line in one direction.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Because in this case distance travelled by a body is equal to the magnitude of displacement of the body.','[{"text": "Both assertion and reason are correct and reason is the correct explanation of assertion", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both assertion and reason are correct but reason is not the correct explanation of assertion", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'When a body moves on a straight path in one direction then the value of distance and displacement remains same so that average speed equals the average velocity for a given time interval. So option (1) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(39105,'physics',30,1359,3263,'A system executing SHM must posseses','[{"text": "Inertia only", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Restoration force", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both restoring force and inertia", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Only external force.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,NULL,NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(39107,'physics',31,1345,3232,'A string of length $240\\,cm$ is connected to two light rings which can freely move along two vertical rods. The maximum three wavelengths of standing waves that can be formed in the string are $cm$','[{"text": "$120,60,30$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$480,240,160$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$120,80,60$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$240,180,90$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Since both ends are free, (the free ends are<br>antinodes.<br>Hence, $240 = \\frac{\\lambda }{2}\\,\\,\\,{\\mkern 1mu} {\\kern 1pt} \\therefore \\lambda  = 480\\,cm$<br>$240 = \\lambda \\,\\,\\,{\\mkern 1mu} {\\kern 1pt} \\therefore \\lambda  = 240\\,cm$<br>$240 = \\frac{{3\\lambda }}{2}\\,\\,\\,{\\mkern 1mu} {\\kern 1pt} \\therefore \\lambda  = 160\\,cm$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(39108,'physics',3,312,574,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> In a uniformly accelerated motion,acceleration time graph is a straight line with positive slope.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Acceleration is rate of change of velocity','[{"text": "Both assertion and reason are correct and reason is the correct explanation of assertion", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both assertion and reason are correct but reason is not the correct explanation of assertion", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but reason is correct.", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Positive slope indicates that acceleration increases uniformly with time. It is not uniform.So option (4) is correct.',NULL,'Easy','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(39109,'physics',30,1359,3263,'A particle executing S.H.M of amplitude $4\\,cm$ and $T = 4\\,\\sec $. The time taken by it to move from positive extreme position to half the amplitude is','[{"text": "$1/3\\\\,\\\\sec $", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1\\\\,\\\\sec $", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\sqrt {3/2} \\\\,\\\\sec $", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2/3\\\\,\\\\sec $", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Equation of motion $y=a \\cos \\omega t$<br>$ \\Rightarrow \\frac{a}{2} = a\\cos \\omega t \\Rightarrow \\cos \\omega t = \\frac{1}{2} \\Rightarrow \\omega t = \\frac{\\pi }{3}$<br>$ \\Rightarrow \\frac{{2\\pi t}}{T} = \\frac{\\pi }{3} \\Rightarrow t = \\frac{{\\frac{\\pi }{3} \\times T}}{{2\\pi }} = \\frac{4}{{3 \\times 2}} = \\frac{2}{3}\\sec $',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(39111,'physics',31,1345,3232,'A stretched wire of length $110\\,cm$ is divided into three segments whose frequencies are in ratio 1:2:3. Their lengths must be:','[{"text": "$20\\\\,cm,30\\\\,cm,60\\\\,cm$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$60\\\\,cm,30\\\\,cm,20\\\\,cm$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$60\\\\,cm,20\\\\,cm,30\\\\,cm$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$30\\\\,cm,60\\\\,cm,20\\\\,cm$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Given, ${l_1} + {l_2} + {l_3} = 110\\,cm$<br>Here tension and linear mass density must be same for all.<br>and $f_{1} l_{1}=f_{2} l_{2}=f_{3} l_{3}$<br>$f_{1}: f_{2}: f_{3}=1: 2: 3$<br>$\\therefore \\dfrac{f_{1}}{f_{2}}=\\dfrac{1}{2}=\\dfrac{l_{2}}{l_{1}} \\Rightarrow l_{2}=\\dfrac{l_{1}}{2}$<br>and $\\frac{{{f_1}}}{{{f_3}}} = \\frac{1}{3} = \\frac{{{l_3}}}{{{l_1}}} \\Rightarrow {l_3} = \\frac{{{l_1}}}{3}$<br>$\\therefore {l_1} + \\frac{{{l_1}}}{2} + \\frac{{{l_1}}}{3} = 110 \\Rightarrow {l_1} = 60\\,cm$<br>So, ${l_1} = 60\\,cm,{l_2} = 30\\,cm,\\,{\\text{and}}\\,{\\text{ }}{l_3} = 20\\,cm$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(39112,'physics',3,310,572,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> The slope of displacement-time of a body moving with high velocity is steeper  than the slope of displacement-time graph of a body with low velocity.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Slope of displacement-time graph = Velocity of the body.','[{"text": "Both assertion and reason are correct and reason is the correct explanation of assertion", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both assertion and reason are correct but reason is not the correct explanation of assertion", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Slope of displacement-time measures velocity of an object. So option (1) is correct',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(39114,'physics',26,1137,2722,'A thin plate ${A B}$ of large area ${A}$ is placed symmetrically in a small gap of height \' ${h}$ \' filled with water of viscosity ${\\eta_{0}}$ and the plate has a constant velocity ${v}$ by applying a force ${F}$, as shown in the figure. If the gap is filled with some other liquid of viscosity ${0.75 \\eta_{0}}$ where should the plate be placed in the gap, so that the plate can again be pulled at the same constant velocity ${V}$, by applying the same force ${F}$. (Take ${h=20 {~cm}}$)','[{"text": "$2\\\\,cm$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$6\\\\,cm$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$5\\\\,cm$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$7\\\\,cm$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Case I: ${y_{1}=y_{2}=h / 2}$<br>$\\dfrac{\\partial u}{\\partial y}=\\dfrac{v}{h / 2}=\\dfrac{2 v}{h}$<br>Viscous force ${=\\dfrac{\\eta_{0} 2 {~V}}{h} {~A}}$ <img src=\'/img/data/physics/26_39114_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' /> The plate has got two sides.<br>Total force ${F=\\dfrac{4 \\eta_{c} v a}{h}}$<br>Case II: ${F=\\dfrac{\\eta v A}{y}+\\dfrac{\\eta v A}{y-h}=\\dfrac{\\eta v A h}{y(h-y)}}$<br>$\\dfrac{\\eta v A h}{y(h-y)}=\\dfrac{4 n^{2} A v}{h} ; \\dfrac{3}{16} h^{2}=y(h-y)$<br>Solving we get ${y=\\dfrac{h}{4}, \\dfrac{3 h}{4}}$<br>Hence, for minimum distance from top ${y=20 / 4=5 {~cm}}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/26_39114_question_1.png'),
(39115,'physics',31,1345,3232,'The waves formed in stretched strings are','[{"text": "Transverse", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Longitudinal", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both longitudinal and transverse", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Shock waves", "isCorrect": false}]',0,NULL,NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(39116,'physics',3,312,574,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> A body having non-zero acceleration can have a constant velocity.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Acceleration is the rate of change of velocity.','[{"text": "Both assertion and reason are correct and reason is the correct explanation of assertion", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both assertion and reason are correct but reason is not the correct explanation of assertion", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but reason is correct.", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Acceleration is the rate of change of velocity,<br> i.e., $a = \\frac{{dv}}{{dt}}$<br>So option (4) is correct.',NULL,'Easy','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(39117,'physics',30,1359,3263,'The displacement of a particle in simple harmonic motion in one time period is','[{"text": "$2\\\\,A$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$A$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{Zero}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$4\\\\,A$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'In one complete oscillation particle comes to its starting position, so displacement is zero.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(39118,'physics',26,1133,2710,'A cube of wood of mass $0.5\\,kg$ and density ${800 {~kg} {~m}^{-3}}$ is fastened to the free end of a vertical spring of spring constant ${k=50 {Nm}^{-1}}$, fixed at the bottom. Now, the entire system is completely submerged in water. The elongation or compression of the spring in equilibrium is ${\\dfrac{\\beta}{2} {~cm}}$. The value of ${\\beta}$ is <br>(Given, ${g=10 {~ms}^{-2}}$)','[{"text": "7", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "5", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "3", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "2", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'${\\rho_{s} < \\rho_{w}}$. So, block tends to move up and thus the spring has elongation.<br>${B_{F}=m g \\times k x}$<br>${V \\rho_{w} g=V \\rho_{s} g+k x}$<br>${\\dfrac{0.8}{800} \\times 1000 \\times 10=(0.5)(10)+50 x}$ <img src=\'/img/data/physics/26_39118_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' /> ${x=0.025 {~m}=2.5 {~cm}=\\dfrac{5}{2} {~cm}}$<br>${\\Rightarrow \\beta=5}$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/26_39118_question_1.png'),
(39119,'physics',31,1345,3232,'A wire under tension vibrates with a fundamental frequency of $1500\\,Hz$. If the length of the wire is doubled, the radius is halved and the wire is made to vibrate under one-ninth the tension. Then the fundamental frequency will become :-','[{"text": "$500\\\\,Hz$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$300\\\\,Hz$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$600\\\\,Hz$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$400\\\\,Hz$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'The fundamental frequency is<br>$1500 = \\frac{{\\sqrt {\\frac{T}{\\mu }} }}{{2\\ell }} = {f_0}$<br>${T^\\prime } = \\frac{T}{9}\\,\\& \\,{\\ell ^\\prime } = 2\\ell $<br>$\\mu^{\\prime}=\\dfrac{m^{\\prime}}{l^{\\prime}}=\\dfrac{\\rho \\pi r^{\\prime 2} l^{\\prime}}{l^{\\prime}}=\\dfrac{\\rho \\pi r^{2}}{4}=\\dfrac{\\mu}{4}$<br>${f^\\prime } = \\frac{{\\sqrt {\\frac{{{T^\\prime }}}{{{\\mu ^\\prime }}}} }}{{2{\\ell ^\\prime }}} = \\frac{{\\sqrt {\\frac{{\\frac{T}{9}}}{{\\frac{\\mu }{4}}}} }}{{2(2\\ell )}} = \\frac{1}{3}{f_0} = 500\\,Hz$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(39120,'physics',3,312,574,'<div class="matching-question"><div class="column-left">The acceleration of an object at a particular time is the slope of the velocity -time graph at that instant of time.</div><div class="column-right">For uniform motion acceleration is (+)ve.</div></div>','[{"text": "Statement A is correct but Statement B is incorrect.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Statement A is incorrect but Statement B is correct.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Statements are correct.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Statements are incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'$a = \\frac{{dv}}{{dt}}$ For uniform motion acceleration is zero. So option (1) is correct.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ type-2',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(39121,'physics',30,1359,3264,'A stone is swinging in a horizontal circle $0.8\\,m$ in diameter, at $30\\,rev/\\min $. A distant light causes a shadow of the stone to be formed on a nearby wall. What is the amplitude of the motion of the shadow? what is the frequency?','[{"text": "$0.4\\\\,m,1.5\\\\,Hz$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$0.4\\\\,m,0.5\\\\,Hz$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$0.8\\\\,m,0.5\\\\,Hz$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$0.2\\\\,m,0.5\\\\,Hz$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'The amplitude is the radius of the circle<br>$R = \\frac{{0.8}}{2}\\,m = 0.4\\,m.$<br>The frequency of the shadow is the same as that of angular velocity of the circular motion, so $\\omega  = 30rev/\\min  = 0.5rev/s = \\pi rad/s$<br>$\\left[ {\\begin{array}{*{20}{l}}{\\because 2\\pi  = 1\\,rev} \\\\ { \\Rightarrow \\pi  = 0.5\\,rev} \\end{array}} \\right]$<br>and $f = \\omega /2\\pi  = \\frac{\\pi }{{2\\pi }} = 0.5\\,Hz$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(39123,'physics',31,1345,3232,'A string with a mass density of $4 \\times {10^{ - 3}}\\,kg/m$ is under tension of $360\\,N$ and is fixed at both ends. One of its resonance frequencies is $375\\,Hz$. The next higher resonance frequency is$450\\,Hz$. The mass of the string is :-','[{"text": "$2 \\\\times {10^{ - 3}}\\\\,kg$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$6 \\\\times {10^{ - 3}}\\\\,kg$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$8 \\\\times {10^{ - 3}}\\\\,kg$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$4 \\\\times {10^{ - 3}}\\\\,kg$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'The frequency $f=\\dfrac{n}{2 L} \\sqrt{\\dfrac{T}{\\mu}}$<br>Given that<br>$\\frac{n}{{2L}}\\sqrt {\\frac{T}{\\mu }} {\\text{ }} \\sim \\frac{{n + 1}}{{2L}}\\sqrt {\\frac{T}{\\mu }}  = 450 - 375$<br>${f_1} = \\frac{1}{{2L}}\\sqrt {\\frac{T}{\\mu }}  = 450 - 375 = 75$<br>$L = \\frac{1}{{2 \\times 75}} \\times \\sqrt {\\frac{{360}}{{4 \\times {{10}^{ - 3}}}}}  = \\frac{{300}}{{150}} = 2\\,m$<br>$m = \\mu  \\times 2 = 8 \\times {10^{ - 3}}\\,kg$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(39124,'physics',3,311,573,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> The average velocity of the object over an interval of time is either smaller than or equal to the average speed of the object over  the same interval.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Velocity is a vector quantity and speed is a scalar quantity.','[{"text": "Both assertion and reason are correct and reason is the correct explanation of assertion", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both assertion and reason are correct but reason is not the correct explanation of assertion", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'As displacement is either smaller or equal to distance but never be greater than distance. Velocity is a vector and speed is a scalar. So  option (2) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(39125,'physics',30,1359,3263,'A particle executes simple harmonic oscillation with an amplitude $a$. The period of oscillation is $T$. The minimum time taken by the particle to travel half of the amplitude from the equilibrium position is','[{"text": "$\\\\dfrac{T}{8}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{T}{4}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{T}{2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{T}{12}$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Let the displacement equation of particle executing SHM is $y=a \\sin \\omega t$<br>As particle travels half of the amplitude from the equilibrium position, so<br>$y=\\dfrac{a}{2}$<br>Therefore, $\\dfrac{a}{2}=a \\sin \\omega t$ or $\\sin \\omega t=\\dfrac{1}{2}=\\sin \\dfrac{\\pi}{6}$<br>$\\Rightarrow t=\\dfrac{T}{12}$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(39126,'physics',26,1133,2710,'Figure shows a cubical block of side $10\\,cm$ and relative density 1.5 suspended by a wire of cross-sectional area ${10^{-6} {~m}^{2}}$. The breaking stress of the wire is ${7 \\times 10^{6} {Nm}^{-2}}$. The block is placed in a beaker of base area ${200 {~cm}^{2}}$ and initially at ${t=0}$, the top surfaces of water and block coincide. There is a pump at the bottom corner, which ejects ${2 {~cm}^{3}}$ of water per second. If the time at which wire breaks is ${p \\times 100}$ seconds, then the value of ${p}$ is <br> (Given, ${g=10 {~ms}^{-2}}$)','[{"text": "3", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "5", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "1", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "7", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'${T_{\\max }=\\left(7 \\times 10^{6}\\right)\\left(10^{-6}\\right)=7 {~N}}$<br>Initial buoyant force is ${B_{F_{1}}=(0.1)^{3}(1000)(10)=10 {~N}}$<br>Weight of the block is ${w=(0.1)^{3}(1500)(10)=15 {~N}}$<br>${\\therefore}$ Initial tension ${T_{i}=15-10=5 {~N}}$<br>At the time of breaking tension is ${T_{\\max }=7 {~N}}$<br>${\\therefore \\quad B_{F_{\\text {min }}}=15-7=8 {~N}}$ (at the time of breaking of wire)<img src=\'/img/data/physics/26_39126_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' /> ${\\therefore \\quad\\left(V_{i}\\right)(1000)(10)=8}$<br>${\\Rightarrow \\quad V_{i}=}$ volume immersed ${=8 \\times 10^{-4} {~m}^{3}}$<br>Or ${V_{i}=800 {~cm}^{3}}$<br>Volume of water to be removed ${=(200-100)(2)=200 {~cm}^{3}}$<br>${\\therefore 2 t=200}$<br>${\\Rightarrow t=100 {~s}}$<br>${\\Rightarrow p=1}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/26_39126_question_1.png'),
(39127,'physics',31,1345,3232,'The tension in a stretched string fixed at both ends is changed by $2 \\%$, the fundamental frequency is found to get changed by $15\\,Hz$. Select the incorrect statement.','[{"text": "Wavelength of the string of fundamental frequency does not change", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Velocity of propagation of wave changes by $2 \\\\%$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Velocity of propagation of wave changes by $1 \\\\%$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Original frequency is $1500\\\\,Hz$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'$f_{0}=$ fundamental frequency $=\\dfrac{1}{2 l} \\sqrt{\\dfrac{T}{\\mu}}$<br>$\\frac{{\\Delta {f_0}}}{{{f_0}}} = \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot \\frac{{\\Delta T}}{T} \\Rightarrow {f_0} = \\frac{{2 \\times \\Delta {f_0}}}{{(\\Delta T/T)}} = \\frac{{2 \\times 15}}{{(2/100)}}$<br>$ = 1500\\,Hz$<br>Also, $v=\\sqrt{\\dfrac{T}{\\mu}} \\quad \\therefore \\dfrac{\\Delta v}{v}=\\dfrac{1}{2} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\Delta T}{T}=\\dfrac{1}{2} \\times 2 \\%=1 \\%$<br>For fundamental frequency,<br>$\\dfrac{\\lambda}{2}=l \\Rightarrow \\lambda=2 l=\\text { constant } .$<br>So option (2) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(39128,'physics',3,311,573,'<div class="matching-question"><div class="column-left">The speedometer of an automobile measures the instantaneous speed of the automobile.</div><div class="column-right">Average velocity is equal to total displacement divided by total time taken.</div></div>','[{"text": "Statement A is correct but Statement B is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Statement A is incorrect but Statement B is correct.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Statements are correct.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Statements are incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Option (3) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ type-2',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(39129,'physics',30,1359,3265,'If displacement $x$ and velocity $v$ are related as $4 v^{2}=25-x^{2}$ in a SHM then time period of given SHM is (consider SI units)','[{"text": "$2 \\\\pi$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\pi$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$6 \\\\pi$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$4 \\\\pi$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Given $4 v^{2}=25-x^{2}$<br>Differentiating with respect to $t$ on both sides,<br>$8 v a=-2 x v \\Rightarrow a=-\\dfrac{x}{4}$<br>Motion is simple harmonic:<br>So, $\\omega^{2}=\\dfrac{1}{4}$ and $T=2 \\pi \\sqrt{4}=4 \\pi$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(39131,'physics',31,1345,3232,'The equation of a stationary wave is given by $y=6 \\sin (\\pi x / 3) \\cos 40 \\pi t$ where $y$ and $x$ are given in $cm$ and time $t$ in second, then the amplitude of progressive wave is','[{"text": "$2\\\\,cm$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$6\\\\,cm$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$3\\\\,cm$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$12\\\\,cm$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'For the given standing wave<br>$2A = 6{\\text{ or }}A = 3\\,cm$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(39132,'physics',3,309,571,'<div class="matching-question"><div class="column-left">The position - time graph of a body moving uniformly in a straight line is parallel to position axis.</div><div class="column-right">The position - time graph in a non - uniform motion gives constant velocity at  all instants of time.</div></div>','[{"text": "Statement A is correct but Statement B is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Statement A is incorrect but Statement B is correct.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Statements are correct.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Statements are incorrect.", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'If the position - time graph of a body moving uniformly in a straight line is parallel to position axis, it means that the position of body is changing at constant time, which  shows that the velocity of the body is infinite. In non uniform motion velocity is constant. So  both  statements are wrong and option (4) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ type-2',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(39171,'physics',31,1346,3237,'According to Laplace correction, the propagation of sound in gas takes place under','[{"text": "Isothermal condition", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Isobaric condition", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Isochoric condition", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Adiabatic condition", "isCorrect": true}]',3,NULL,NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(39135,'physics',31,1345,3232,'A standing wave pattern is formed on a string. One of the waves is given by equation $y_{1}=a \\sin \\left(\\omega t-k x+\\dfrac{\\pi}{3}\\right)$ then the equation of the other wave such that at $x=0$ a node is formed.','[{"text": "$y_{2}=a \\\\cos \\\\left(\\\\omega t+k x+\\\\dfrac{\\\\pi}{3}\\\\right)$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$y_{2}=a \\\\sin \\\\left(\\\\omega t+k x+\\\\dfrac{\\\\pi}{3}\\\\right)$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$y_{2}=a \\\\sin \\\\left(\\\\omega t+k x+\\\\dfrac{2 \\\\pi}{3}\\\\right)$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$y_{2}=a \\\\cos \\\\left(\\\\omega t+k x+\\\\dfrac{4 \\\\pi}{3}\\\\right)$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'At $x=0$ the phase difference should be $\\pi$.<br>Thus, the correct option is (4).',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(39136,'physics',3,312,574,'<div class="matching-question"><div class="column-left">A particle may be momentarily at rest and yet have non - zero acceleration.</div><div class="column-right">The zero velocity of a particle at any instant does not necessarily imply zero acceleration at that instant</div></div>','[{"text": "Statement A is correct but Statement B is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Statement A is incorrect but Statement B is correct.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Statements are correct.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Statements are incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'A particle may be momentarily at rest and yet have non - zero acceleration. For example,  freely  falling particle has zero velocity at its   uppermost point but the acceleration at that  instant continues to be the acceleration due to gravity. Option (3) is correct.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ type-2',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(39137,'physics',30,1359,3263,'$x=a \\cos ^{2} \\omega t$ or $x=\\dfrac{a}{2}-\\dfrac{a}{2} \\cos 2 \\omega t$ represents<br>(i) Periodic motion but not simple harmonic motion if $x$ is displacement<br>(ii) SHM of angular frequency $2 \\omega$','[{"text": "Only (i) is correct", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Only (ii) is correct", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both are incorrect", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both are correct", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'If $x$ is position, $x=\\dfrac{a}{2}-\\dfrac{a}{2} \\cos 2 \\omega t$. Here displacement $x$ varies simple harmonically with angular frequency $2 \\omega$.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(39138,'physics',26,1138,2730,'A bowl whose bottom has round holes of diameter $1\\,mm$ is filled with water. Assuming that surface tension acts only at holes, find the maximum height  up to which water can be filled in the vessel without leakage. (Given, surface tension of water ${=75 \\times 10^{-3} {Nm}^{-1}, g=10 {~ms}^{-2}}$ and density of water ${=1 {~g} / {cm}^{3}}$.','[{"text": "$1\\\\,cm$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$3\\\\,cm$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$5\\\\,cm$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$8\\\\,cm$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'The vertical force due to surface at the hole <img src=\'/img/data/physics/26_39138_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' /> $=T \\cos \\theta \\times L=T \\cos \\theta \\times 2 \\pi r$<br>This will balance weight.<br>${m g\\left(=\\pi r^{2} h \\rho g\\right)}$<br>${\\therefore \\quad T \\cos \\theta \\times 2 \\pi r=\\pi r^{2} h \\rho g}$<br>$\\Rightarrow \\quad h=\\dfrac{2 T \\cos \\theta}{r \\rho g}$<br>${h}$ is maximum, when ${\\cos \\theta=1}$<br>$\\therefore \\,\\,\\,{\\mkern 1mu} {\\kern 1pt} {h_{\\max }} = \\frac{{2T}}{{r\\rho g}}$<br>$ = \\frac{{2 \\times 0.075}}{{0.0005 \\times 1000 \\times 10}} = 0.03\\,m = 3\\,cm$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(39139,'physics',31,1345,3232,'One end of string, in which a tension in maintained, is fixed to a rigid support and the free end is made to oscillate transversely by a vibrator at a frequency $f$. Then maximum velocity of propagation of wave in the string will be','[{"text": "$2f\\\\,l$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$4f\\\\,l$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$6f\\\\,l$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$f\\\\,l$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'wavelength is maximum for fundamental mode $\\dfrac{\\lambda}{4}=l \\Rightarrow \\lambda_{\\max }=4 l$<br>$\\therefore \\quad v_{\\text {max }}=f \\lambda_{\\text {max }}=4 f l \\text {. }$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(39140,'physics',3,311,573,'<div class="matching-question"><div class="column-left">The average speed of an object is greater than or equal to the magnitude of the average velocity over a given time interval.</div><div class="column-right">The two are equal only if the distance and displacement are same</div></div>','[{"text": "Statement A is correct but Statement B is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Statement A is incorrect but Statement B is correct.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Statements are correct.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Statements are incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Option (3) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ type-2',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(39141,'physics',30,1359,3265,'The velocity of a particle performing simple harmonic motion, when it passes through its mean position is','[{"text": "Zero", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Infinity", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Maximum", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Minimum", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'In S.H.M at mean position velocity is maximum',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(39143,'physics',31,1345,3233,'A device used for investigating the vibration of a fixed string or wire is','[{"text": "Barometer", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Hydrometer", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Sonometer", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "None of these", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Sonometer is used to produce resonance of sound source with stretched vibrating string.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(39144,'physics',30,1359,3265,'If velocity of a particle is given as $v=\\pi \\sin \\omega t$ then find the average value of velocity from 0 to $T/2$ time interval. where $\\omega$ and $T$ are angular velocity and time period respectively :-','[{"text": "$\\\\dfrac{\\\\pi}{\\\\sqrt{2}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{\\\\pi}{2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "2", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$2 \\\\sqrt{2}$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'$v_{\\text {avg }}=\\dfrac{\\int_{0}^{T / 2} \\pi \\sin \\omega t d t}{\\int_{0}^{T / 2} d t}=-\\dfrac{2 \\pi}{\\omega T}[\\cos \\omega t]_{0}^{T / 2}=2$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(39145,'physics',3,312,574,'<div class="matching-question"><div class="column-left">Acceleration of a body can’t be changed abruptly at an instant.</div><div class="column-right">Acceleration can be produced only by change in the magnitude of the velocity.</div></div>','[{"text": "Statement A is correct but Statement B is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Statement A is incorrect but Statement B is correct.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Statements are correct.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Statements are incorrect.", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Acceleration of a body can be changed abruptly at any instant by changing  force. <br>Accleration can be produced by change in the direction of velocity also. So option (4) is correct.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ type-2',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49802,'biology',94,789,1698,'Nirenberg synthesised an RNA having 34 adenine residues ( AAAA...) and obtained a polypeptide of 11 lysine residues. it proved that genetic code of lysine is','[{"text": "Adenine", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "AA", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "AAA", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "AAAA ....A.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'$34 / 11=3$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(39147,'physics',31,1345,3233,'The given diagram shows three light pieces of paper placed on a wire that is stretched over two supports, $Q$ and $R$, a distance $4 x$ apart. When the wire is made to vibrate at a particular frequency, all the pieces of paper, except the middle one, fall off the wire. Which of the following could be the wavelength of the vibration?','[{"text": "$2 x$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$3 x$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$4 x$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$8 x$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Middle paper does not fall.<br>So there must be a node at the middle.<br>The standing wave pattern is<br><img src=\'/img/data/physics/31_39147_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' /> <br>$4x = 2 \\times \\frac{\\lambda }{2} \\Rightarrow \\lambda  = 4x.$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/31_39147_question_1.png'),
(39148,'physics',3,313,576,'<div class="matching-question"><div class="column-left">Doubling the initial velocity, the stopping distance of moving object increases by a factor of 4 (for the same deceleration).</div><div class="column-right">Stopping distance is proportional to  the initial velocity.</div></div>','[{"text": "Statement A is correct but Statement B is incorrect.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Statement A is incorrect but Statement B is correct.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Statements are correct.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Statements are incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Let the distance travelled by the vehicle before it stops be $x$, then using ${v^2} = {u^2} = 2ax$.<br>Put $v = 0$, we get $x = \\frac{{{u^2}}}{{2a}}$<br>Thus, the stopping distance is proportional to the square of the initial velocity. So option (1) is correct.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ type-2',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(39149,'physics',30,1359,3265,'In SHM there is always a constant ratio between the displacement of the body and its','[{"text": "Velocity", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Acceleration", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Mass of the particle", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "All of the above", "isCorrect": false}]',1,NULL,NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(39151,'physics',31,1345,3233,'When the string of a sonometer of length $L$ between the bridges vibrates in the first overtone, the amplitude of vibration is maximum at:','[{"text": "$(L / 4)$ and $(3 L / 4)$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$L / 2$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{L}{8}, \\\\dfrac{3 L}{8}, \\\\dfrac{5 L}{8}, \\\\dfrac{7 L}{8}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$(L / 6),(3 L / 6)$, and $(5 L / 6)$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'The string vibrates in two segments in the first overtone. Therefore, the amplitude of vibration is maximum at $(L / 4)$ and $(3 L / 4)$.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(39152,'physics',3,313,578,'In the rectilinear motion of a body accelearation is $a = 6 - 3v$, where $v$ is velocity. If the body  starts from rest, its velocity as a function of time $t$ is','[{"text": "$v = 2(1 - {e^{ - 3t}})$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$v = 2{e^{ - 3t}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$v = 2{e^{ - 5t}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$v = 3(1 - {e^{ - 5t}})$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Given, $\\frac{{dv}}{{dt}} = 6 - 3v$<br>$ \\Rightarrow \\frac{{dv}}{{6 - 3v}} = dt$<br>Integrating it, we have<br>$\\frac{{ - 1}}{3}\\left[ {\\ln [6 - 3v]} \\right]_0^v = t$<br>$\\ln \\left( {\\frac{{6 - 3v}}{6}} \\right) =  - 3t$<br>$\\frac{{6 - 3v}}{6} = {e^{ - 3t}}$<br>$1 - \\frac{v}{2} = {e^{ - 3t}} \\Rightarrow v = 2\\left( {1 - {e^{ - 3t}}} \\right)$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,'NCERT Exemplar','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(39153,'physics',30,1359,3265,'Which of the following four statements is false?','[{"text": "A body can have zero velocity and still be accelerated", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "A body can have a constant velocity and still have a varying speed", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "A body can have a constant speed and still have a varying velocity", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "The direction of the velocity of a body can change when its speed is constant", "isCorrect": false}]',1,NULL,NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(39154,'physics',26,1133,2708,'The drawing shows a hydraulic chamber with a spring (spring constant ${=1600 {~N} / {m}}$) attached to the input piston and a rock of mass $40.0\\,kg$ resting on the output plunger. The piston and plunger are nearly at the same height, and each has a negligible mass. By how much is the spring compressed  from its unstrained position?','[{"text": "$5.0 \\\\times {10^{ - 2}}\\\\,m$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$2.0 \\\\times {10^{ - 2}}\\\\,m$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$8.0 \\\\times {10^{ - 2}}\\\\,m$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$7.0 \\\\times {10^{ - 2}}\\\\,m$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Let ${F_{1}}$ and ${F_{2}}$ are the magnitudes of the force the spring exerts on the piston and the rock exerts on the plunger respectively. Initially the piston and the plunger are at the same height, so the fluid pressure at the piston is equal to the fluid pressure at the plunger ${\\dfrac{F_{1}}{A_{1}}=\\dfrac{F_{2}}{A_{2}}}$ where ${A_{1}}$ is the area of the piston and ${A_{2}}$ is the area of the plunger.<br>Therefore, the magnitude of the force ${F_{2}}$ that the rock exerts on the plunger is given by ${F_{2}=F_{1}\\left(\\dfrac{A_{2}}{A_{1}}\\right)}$<br>Here ${F_{1}=k x}$, where ${k}$ is the spring constant of the spring, and ${x}$ is the amount by which the spring is compressed from its unstrained position. and the magnitude ${F_{2}}$ of the force the rock exerts on the plunger is equal to the magnitude ${W=m g}$ where ${m}$ is the rock\'s mass and ${g}$ is the magnitude of the acceleration due to gravity.<br>Solving ${F_{2}=F_{1}\\left(\\dfrac{A_{2}}{A_{1}}\\right)}$ for ${F_{1}}$ and substituting ${F_{2}=m g}$ yields ${F_1} = {F_2}\\left( {\\frac{{{A_1}}}{{{A_2}}}} \\right) = mg\\left( {\\frac{{{A_1}}}{{{A_2}}}} \\right)\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\left( 1 \\right)$<br>Solving for ${x}$, we obtain, $x = \\frac{{{F_1}}}{k}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\left( 2 \\right)$<br>Substituting Eq. (1) into Eq. (2), we find that<br>$x = \\frac{{{F_1}}}{k} = \\frac{{mg}}{k}\\left( {\\frac{{{A_1}}}{{{A_2}}}} \\right)$<br>$ = \\frac{{(40\\,kg)\\left( {10\\,m/{s^2}} \\right)}}{{1600\\,N/m}}\\left( {\\frac{{15\\,c{m^2}}}{{75\\,c{m^2}}}} \\right) = 5.0 \\times {10^{ - 2}}\\,m$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/26_39154_question_1.png'),
(39155,'physics',31,1345,3233,'A sonometer wire supports a $4\\,kg$ load and vibrates in fundamental mode with a tuning fork of frequency $416\\,Hz$. The length of the wire between the bridges is now doubled. In order to maintain fundamental mode, the load should be changed to','[{"text": "$2\\\\,kg$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1\\\\,kg$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$16\\\\,kg$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$4\\\\,kg$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Tension in sonometer wire $ = 4\\,g$ $\\mu=$ mass per unit length<br>$v=\\dfrac{1}{2 l} \\sqrt{\\dfrac{T}{\\mu}} \\Rightarrow 416=\\dfrac{1}{2 l} \\sqrt{\\dfrac{4 g}{\\mu}}$<br>When length is doubled, i.e.,<br>Let new load $ = M$<br>As, $v^{\\prime}=v$<br>$\\frac{1}{{2{l^\\prime }}}\\sqrt {\\frac{{Mg}}{\\mu }}  = \\frac{1}{{2l}}\\sqrt {\\frac{{4g}}{\\mu }}  \\Rightarrow \\frac{1}{{4l}}\\sqrt {\\frac{{Mg}}{\\mu }}  = \\frac{1}{{2l}}\\sqrt {\\frac{{4g}}{\\mu }} $<br>$ \\Rightarrow \\sqrt M  = 2 \\times 2 \\Rightarrow M = 16\\,kg$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(39156,'physics',3,311,573,'Study the following statements.<br>I. The unit of average speed is same as that of velocity<br>II. If one or more coordinates of an object  changes with time, we say that the object   is at rest with respect to the given reference frame<br>Which of the following statement (s) is /are correct?','[{"text": "Only I", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Only II", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both I and II", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Neither I nor II", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Only statement  I  is correct but II is incorrect and since if none of the coordinates change, then the object is said to be at rest with respect to the given reference frame. So options (1) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ type-2',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(39157,'physics',30,1359,3265,'The angular velocities of three bodies in simple harmonic motion are $\\omega_{1}, \\omega_{2}, \\omega_{3}$ with thier respective amplitudes as $A_{1}, A_{2}, A_{3}$. If all the three bodies have same mass and maximum velocity, then','[{"text": "$A_{1} \\\\omega_{1}^{2}=A_{2} \\\\omega_{2}^{2}=A_{3} \\\\omega_{3}^{2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$A_{1} \\\\omega_{1}=A_{2} \\\\omega_{2}=A_{3} \\\\omega_{3}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$A_{1}^{2} \\\\omega_{1}^{2}=A_{2}^{2} \\\\omega_{2}^{2}=A_{3}^{2} \\\\omega_{3}^{2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$A_{1}^{2} \\\\omega_{1}=A_{2}^{2} \\\\omega_{2}=A_{3}^{2} \\\\omega_{3}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Velocity is same. So by using $v=a \\omega$<br>$\\Rightarrow A_{1} \\omega_{1}=A_{2} \\omega_{2}=A_{3} \\omega_{3} .$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(39159,'physics',31,1346,3236,'During propagation of longitudinal plane wave in a medium the two particles separated by a distance equivalent to one wavelength at an instant will be/have','[{"text": "In phase, same displacement", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "In phase, different displacement", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Different phase, same displacement", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Different phase, different displacement", "isCorrect": false}]',0,NULL,NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(39160,'physics',3,311,573,'Which of the following statements  is/are correct?<br>I. Distance is always positive.<br>II. Displacement can be positive, negative or zero<br>III. Average speed is always positive.<br>IV. Path length is a vector quantity whereas  displacement scalar quantity','[{"text": "Only I", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both II and III", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both I and II", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "I, II and III", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Path length is a scalar quantity as it has only magnitude but no direction whereas  displacement has magnitude as well as direction,  so displacement is vector quantity. So option (4) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ type-2',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(39161,'physics',30,1359,3265,'Acceleration of a particle, executing SHM, at it\'s mean position is','[{"text": "Varies", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Inifinity", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Zero", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Infinity", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'$A=-\\omega^{2} y$ at mean position $y = 0$<br>So acceleration is minmum (zero).',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(39163,'physics',31,1346,3236,'Match the Column I with Column II.<div class="matching-question"><div class="column-left">A. Pitch<br>B. Quality<br>C. Loudness<br>D. Nodes</div><div class="column-right">P. Waveform<br>Q. Frequency<br>R. Intensity<br>S. Zero amplitude</div></div>','[{"text": "${\\\\rm{A - P,}}\\\\,{\\\\rm{B - Q,}}\\\\,{\\\\rm{C - R,D - S}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{A - Q,}}\\\\,{\\\\rm{B - P,}}\\\\,{\\\\rm{C - R,}}\\\\,{\\\\rm{D - S}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{A  -  R,}}\\\\,{\\\\rm{B  -  P,}}\\\\,{\\\\rm{C  -  S,}}\\\\,{\\\\rm{D  -  Q}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{A - R,B - S,C - P,D - Q}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,NULL,NULL,'Easy','Match the following',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(39164,'physics',3,309,571,'<div class="matching-question"><div class="column-left">The average and instanteneous velocities have same value in a uniform motion.</div><div class="column-right">In uniform motion, the velocity of an object increases uniformly.</div></div>','[{"text": "Statement A is correct but Statement B is incorrect.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Statement A is incorrect but Statement B is correct.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Statements are correct.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Statements are incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'An object is said to be in uniform motion if it undergoes equal displacement in equal intervals of time.<br>Thus, in uniform motion average and   instantaneous velocities have same value.   Velocity is constant in uniform motion, so option  (1) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ type-2',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(39165,'physics',30,1359,3265,'In S.H.M maximum acceleration is at','[{"text": "Equilibrium", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Extreme position", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Acceleration is constant", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "None of these", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'$A_{\\max }=\\omega^{2} a$.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(39166,'physics',26,1133,2710,'A ball of density ${\\rho_{0}}$ falls from rest from a point ${P}$ onto the surface of a liquid of density ${\\rho}$ in time ${T}$. It enters the liquid, stops, moves up, and returns to ${P}$ in a total time ${3 T}$. Neglect viscosity, surface tension and splashing. Find  the ratio of ${\\dfrac{\\rho}{\\rho_{0}}}$.','[{"text": "1", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "3", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "6", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "2", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'It strikes the surface of liquid with velocity ${v_{1}=g T}$ in water (liquid) its time of flight.<br>$T = \\frac{{2{v_1}}}{{\\left( {\\frac{\\rho }{{{\\rho _0}}} - 1} \\right)g}} = \\frac{{2gT}}{{\\left( {\\frac{\\rho }{{{\\rho _0}}} - 1} \\right)g}} \\Rightarrow \\frac{\\rho }{{{\\rho _0}}} = 3$ <img src=\'/img/data/physics/26_39166_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(39167,'physics',31,1346,3236,'If the pressure amplitude is $\\Delta P_{0}$, the difference in pressures at the maximum compressed and maximum rarefied position is:','[{"text": "$\\\\dfrac{\\\\Delta P_{0}}{2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\Delta P_{0}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$4 \\\\Delta P_{0}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2 \\\\Delta P_{0}$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,NULL,NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(39168,'physics',3,314,579,'On a two lane road, car $A$ is travelling with a speed of 36 $km/h$. Two cars $B$ and $C$ approach car $A$ in opposite directions with a speed of 54 $km/h$ each. At a certain instant, when the distance AB is equal to AC, both being 1 $km$, $B$ decides to overtake $A$ before $C$ does. In this case, the acceleration of car $B$ is required to avoid an accident','[{"text": "$0.2 \\\\, m/{s^2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{0}}{\\\\rm{.1}}\\\\,m/{s^2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1 \\\\, m/{s^2}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$1.9 \\\\, m/{s^2}$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Speed of car $A\\left( {{v_A}} \\right) = 36\\,km/h = 10\\,m/s$<br>Speed of car $B$ and car $C$<br>${u_A} = {u_C} = 54 \\, km/h = 15\\,m/s$<br>Relative velocity of car $B$ w.r.t., car $A$<br>${u_{BA}} = {u_B} - {u_A} = 15 - 10 = 5 \\, m/s$<br>Relative velocity of car $C$ w.r.t., car $A$<br>${u_{CA}} = {u_C} - {u_A} = 15 - \\left( { - 10} \\right) = 25 \\, m/s$<br>Distance between car $A$ and car $B$ = $1 \\, km = 1000 \\, m$<br>Time taken by car $C$ to travel distance $AC = 1000 \\, m$<br>$ = \\frac{{1000}}{{25}} = 40 \\, s$<br>Let car $B$ starts to accelerate with an  acceleration $a$, using equation of motion.<br>$s = ut + \\frac{1}{2}a{t^2}$<br>$s = {u_{BA}}t + \\frac{1}{2}a{t^2}$<br>$1000 = 5 \\times 40 + \\frac{1}{2}a \\times {\\left( {40} \\right)^2}$<br>$ \\Rightarrow a = 1m/{s^2}$<br>Therefore, car $B$ should accelerate with an  acceleration $1 \\, m/{s^2}$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,'NCERT Exemplar','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(39172,'physics',3,311,573,'A passenger arriving in a new town wishes to go from the station to a hotel located 10 $km$ away on a straight road from the station. A dishonest cabman takes him along a circuitons path 23 $km$ long and reaches the hotel in 28 min. What is the average speed of the taxi and the magnitude of average velocity respectively (in $km/h$)?','[{"text": "$21.3\\\\,{\\\\rm{and}}\\\\,49.3\\\\,$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$21\\\\,{\\\\rm{and}}\\\\,20$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$48.3\\\\,{\\\\rm{and}}\\\\,24.43$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$49.3\\\\,{\\\\rm{and}}\\\\,21.43$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Given, shortest distance between the station and the hotel $ = 10\\,km$<br>$\\therefore $ Displacement of the taxi $ = 10\\,km$<br>Distance travelled by the taxi $ = 23\\,km$<br>Time taken by the taxi $ = 28\\,\\min  = \\frac{7}{{15}}h$<br>${\\rm{Avg}}\\,{\\rm{speed}} = \\frac{{{\\rm{Total \\, distance \\, travelled}}}}{{{\\rm{Total \\, time \\, taken}}}}$<br>$ = \\frac{{23}}{{\\left( {7/15} \\right)}} = 49.3\\,km/h <br>${\\rm{Average}}\\,{\\rm{velocity}} = \\frac{{{\\rm{ Displacement}}}}{{{\\rm{Total time }}}}$<br>$ = \\frac{{10}}{{\\left( {7/15} \\right)}} = 21.43\\,km/h$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,'NCERT Exemplar','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(39173,'physics',30,1359,3265,'For a particle in S.H.M., if the amplitude of displacement is \' $a$ \' and the amplitude of velocity is \' $v$ \' the amplitude of acceleration is','[{"text": "$\\\\dfrac{v^{2}}{a}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$v a$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{v}{a}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{v^{2}}{2 a}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Maximum velocity $v=\\omega a$<br>Maximum acceleration $f=\\omega^{2} a \\Rightarrow f=\\dfrac{v^{2}}{a}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(39174,'physics',26,1136,2718,'When air of density ${1.3 {~kg} / {m}^{3}}$ flows across the top of the tube shown in the accompanying figure, water rises in the tube to a height of $1.0\\,cm$. The speed of the air is','[{"text": "$11.6\\\\,m/s$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$14.2\\\\,m/s$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$12.4\\\\,m/s$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$13.2\\\\,m/s$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'$\\frac{1}{2}{\\rho _{air}}{v^2} = {\\rho _w}g{h_w}$<br>$\\nu  = \\sqrt {\\frac{{2{\\rho _w}g{h_w}}}{{{\\rho _{{\\rm{air }}}}}}} $<br>$ = \\sqrt {\\frac{{2 \\times {{10}^3} \\times 10 \\times {{10}^{ - 2}}}}{{1.3}}}  = 12.4\\,m/s$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/26_39174_question_1.png'),
(39175,'physics',31,1343,3226,'A boat at anchor is rocked by waves whose crests are $100\\,m$ apart and velocity is $25\\,m/\\sec $. The boat bounces up once in every','[{"text": "$75\\\\,s$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2500\\\\,s$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$0.25\\\\,s$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$4\\\\,s$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'$\\lambda=100 m \\Rightarrow t=\\dfrac{\\lambda}{v}=4 \\mathrm{sec}$.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(39176,'physics',3,311,573,'Among the four graphs, there is only one graph for which average velocity over the time interval (0, \\[T\\]) can vanish for a suitable \\[T\\]. Which one is it?<div class="matching-question"></div>','[{"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'In the graph 2, for one value of displacement, there are two timings. As a result of it, for one time, the average velocity is positive and for other time is equivalent negative. Due to it, the average velocity  can vanish for two time intervals.',NULL,'Hard','Graph/Figure',NULL,'NCERT Exemplar','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(39177,'physics',30,1359,3265,'The equation of motion for a mass at the end of particular spring is $y = 0.30\\cos 0.5t$ metre. Find displacement, velocity, and accelerartion of the mass at $t = 0$.','[{"text": "$0.3\\\\,m,0, - 0.075\\\\,m/{s^2}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$0.3\\\\,m,0.15\\\\,m/s,0$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$0.3\\\\,m,0.15\\\\,m/s,0.075\\\\,m/{s^2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$0.3\\\\,m,0,0$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'The speed<br>$v = \\frac{{dy}}{{dt}} =  - 0.15\\sin (0.50t)m/s$<br>$a = \\frac{{dv}}{{dt}} =  - 0.075\\cos (0.50t)m/{s^2}$<br>$\\therefore y = 0.30\\,m,v = 0$ and $a =  - 0.075\\,m/{s^2}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(39179,'physics',31,1346,3236,'Two sound waves have phase difference of $60^{\\circ}$, then they will have the path difference of:','[{"text": "$\\\\dfrac{\\\\lambda}{3}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$3 \\\\lambda$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\lambda$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{\\\\lambda}{6}$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'$\\Delta \\phi=\\dfrac{2 \\pi}{\\lambda} \\Delta x$<br>Where $\\lambda$ is wavelength.<br>$\\Delta \\phi=\\dfrac{\\lambda}{2 \\pi} \\times \\dfrac{\\pi}{3}=\\dfrac{\\lambda}{6}$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(39180,'physics',3,313,576,'A car moving along a straight highway with speed of $126\\,km/h$ is brought to a stop within a distance of 200 $m$. What is the retardation of the car (assumed uniform) and how long does it take for the car to stop?','[{"text": "$3.06\\\\,m/{s^2}\\\\,{\\\\rm{and}}\\\\,4.1\\\\,s$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2.06\\\\,m/{s^2}\\\\,{\\\\rm{and}}\\\\,11.4\\\\,s$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$3.06\\\\,m/{s^2}\\\\,{\\\\rm{and}}\\\\,11.4\\\\,s$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$3.06\\\\,m/{s^2}\\\\,{\\\\rm{and}}\\\\,10.4\\\\,s$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Initial velocity of the car $(u) = 126\\,km/h$<br>$ = 126 \\times \\frac{5}{{18}}m/s = 35\\,m/s$ $\\left( {\\because 1km/h = \\frac{5}{{18}}m/s} \\right)$<br>Final velocity of the car $(v) = 0$<br>Distance travelled $(s) = 200\\,m$<br>From equation of motion, ${v^2} = {u^2} + 2as$<br>or $a = \\frac{{{v^2} - {u^2}}}{{2s}} = \\frac{{0 - {{\\left( {35} \\right)}^2}}}{{2 \\times 200}} = - 3.06 \\, m/{s^2}$<br>$\\therefore $ Retardation of motion, <br>$v = u + at$<br>$t = \\frac{{v - u}}{a} = \\frac{{\\left( {0 - 35} \\right)}}{{\\left( { - 49/16} \\right)}} = 11.4\\,s$<br>$\\therefore $ Car will stop after $11.4\\,s$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,'NCERT Exemplar','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(39181,'physics',30,1359,3265,'A particle executes linear simple harmonic motion with an amplitude of $2\\,cm$. When the particle is at $1\\,cm$ from the mean position the magnitude of its velocity is equal to that of its acceleration. Then its time period in seconds is','[{"text": "$2 \\\\pi \\\\sqrt{3}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{1}{2 \\\\pi \\\\sqrt{3}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{\\\\sqrt{3}}{2 \\\\pi}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{2 \\\\pi}{\\\\sqrt{3}}$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Velocity $v=\\omega \\sqrt{A^{2}-x^{2}}$ and acceleration $=\\omega^{2} x$<br>Now given,<br>$\\begin{aligned}& \\omega^{2} x=\\omega \\sqrt{A^{2}-x^{2}} \\Rightarrow \\omega^{2} \\cdot 1=\\omega \\sqrt{2^{2}-1^{2}} \\\\& \\Rightarrow \\omega=\\sqrt{3} \\quad \\therefore T=\\dfrac{2 \\pi}{\\omega}=\\dfrac{2 \\pi}{\\sqrt{3}}\\end{aligned}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(39183,'physics',31,1343,3226,'A line of surface on which the disturbance has the same phase at all points is called:','[{"text": "Wave train", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Wave front", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Path difference", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Wave pulse", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'A wavefront is a line or surface on which the disturbance has the same phase at all points.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(39184,'physics',3,314,579,'A police van is moving on a highway with a speed of 30 $km/h$ fires a bullet at a thiefs car speeding away in the same direction with a speed of 192 $km/h$. If the muzzle speed of the bullet is 150 $m/s$, with what speed does the bullet hit the thief’s car?','[{"text": "$110\\\\,m/s$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$100\\\\,m/s$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$105\\\\,m/s$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$95\\\\,m/s$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Speed of police van<br>$\\left( {{v_p}} \\right) = 30km/h = \\frac{{25}}{3}m/s$ $\\left( {\\because 1\\,km/s = \\frac{5}{{18}}m/s} \\right)$<br>Speed of thief’s car $({v_\\gamma }) = 192\\,km/h = \\frac{{160}}{3}m/s.$<br>Muzzle speed of bullet $({v_B}) = 150\\,m/s$<br>The effective speed of the bullet<br>${v_B} = {v_B} + {v_p}$<br>$ = 150 + \\frac{{25}}{3} = \\frac{{475}}{3}m/s$<br>Speed of the bullet with which it hits the thief’s car <br>$ = \\left( {\\frac{{475}}{3} - \\frac{{160}}{3}} \\right)m/s = 105 \\, m/s$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,'NCERT Exemplar','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(39185,'physics',30,1360,3267,'Which of the following quantities are always negative in a simple harmonic motion?','[{"text": "$\\\\vec{F} \\\\cdot \\\\vec{r}$.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\vec{v} \\\\cdot \\\\vec{r}$.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\vec{a} \\\\cdot \\\\vec{r}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both (1) & (3)", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Force and acceleration will be always towards the origin. Position vector defined from the origin is always away from the mean position. $\\bar{v}$ can be towards or away from the mean position. So $\\vec F.\\vec r\\,\\,\\& \\,\\,\\vec a \\cdot \\vec r$ are (-) ve.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(39186,'physics',26,1136,2720,'A siphon has a uniform circular base of diameter $\\frac{8}{{\\sqrt \\pi  }}\\,cm$ with its crests $1.8\\,m$ above water level as in figure. The absolute pressure at the crest level  $A$ is <br>[Use ${P_{0}=10^{5} {~N} / {m}^{2}}$ and $\\left. {g = 10\\,\\,m/{s^2}} \\right]$','[{"text": "$62\\\\,k\\\\,Pa$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$46\\\\,k\\\\,Pa$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$36\\\\,k\\\\,Pa$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$56\\\\,k\\\\,Pa$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Velocity of flow ${=\\sqrt{2 g h}=\\sqrt{2 \\times 10 \\times 3.6}=6 \\sqrt{2} {~m} / {s}}$<br>Bernoulli\'s theorem between surface and ${A}$<br>${P_{atm{\\rm{ }}}} = P + \\frac{1}{2}\\rho {v^2} + \\rho gh$<br>$P = {P_{atm{\\rm{ }}}} - \\frac{1}{2}\\rho {v^2} - \\rho gh$<br>$ = {10^5} - \\frac{1}{2} \\times {10^3}{(6\\sqrt 2 )^2} - {10^3} \\times 10 \\times 1.8$<br>$ = 4.6 \\times {10^4}\\,N/{m^2} = 46\\,k\\,Pa$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/26_39186_question_1.png'),
(39187,'physics',31,1346,3237,'Among the following velocity of sound depend in a gaseous medium','[{"text": "Intensity of sound", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Amplitude and frequency of sound", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Density and elasticity of medium", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Wavelength of sound", "isCorrect": false}]',2,NULL,NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(39188,'physics',3,314,579,'A jet airplane travelling at a speed of 500 $km/h$ ejects its products of combustion at the speed of 1500 $km/h$ relative to the jet plane. What is  the velocity of the latter with respect to an observer on the ground?','[{"text": "$ - 1000\\\\,km/h$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$ - 100\\\\,km/h$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1000\\\\,km/h$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$100\\\\,km/h$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Let jet airplane be moving upwards right (+ve direction) with velocity ${v_J}$ and ejected gases be moving downwards (-ve direction) with velocity ${v_g}$ while observer be at rest on the ground i.e.,${v_0} = 0$<br>$\\therefore {v_J} = 500 \\, km/h$<br>${v_{gJ}} =  - 1500 \\, km/h \\Rightarrow {v_g} - {v_J} =  - 1500\\,kmph$<br>$\\therefore {v_g} =  - 1500 + 500 =  - 1000\\,km{h^{ - 1}}$<br>Therefore, relative velocity of the ejected gases with respect to the observer is $1000 \\, km/h$, in downward direction.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,'NCERT Exemplar','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(39189,'physics',30,1359,3265,'Match the Column I (quantity) with Column II (value) for an object executing simple harmonic motion in a horizontal plane with displacement given as \\(x=A \\cos \\omega t\\) and select the correct answer from the codes given below<div class="matching-question"><div class="column-left">A. $\\dfrac{v_{\\max }}{A}$<br>B. $\\dfrac{a_{\\max }}{A}$<br>C. $\\begin{array}{l}\\text { If object starts from } \\\\x=+A, \\text { then time to } \\\\\\text { reach at } \\dfrac{A}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{array}$<br>D. $\\begin{array}{l}\\text { If object starts from } \\\\x=0 \\text { and move towards } \\\\\\text { right, then the time to } \\\\\\text { reach at }+A / 2\\end{array}$</div><div class="column-right">P. $T / 8$<br>Q. $T / 12$<br>R. $\\omega$<br>S. $\\omega^{2}$</div></div>','[{"text": "A - R, B - P, C - S, D - Q", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "A - R, B - S, C - P, D - Q", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "A - S, B - R, C - Q, D - P", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "A - R, B - S, C - Q, D - P", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'For an object executing SHM,<br>A. ${v_{{\\rm{max }}}} = A\\omega $<br>$ \\Rightarrow \\frac{{{v_{{\\rm{max }}}}}}{A} = \\frac{{A\\omega }}{A} = \\omega $<br>B. Similarly, $a_{\\max }=A \\omega^{2}$<br>So, $\\dfrac{a_{\\max }}{A}=\\dfrac{A \\omega^{2}}{A}=\\omega^{2}$<br>C. If object starts from $x=+A$, its equation is<br>$x=A \\cos \\omega t$<br>For $x=+A / \\sqrt{2}$<br>$\\dfrac{A}{\\sqrt{2}}=A \\cos \\omega t$<br>$\\cos \\omega t=\\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}=\\cos \\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}\\right)$<br>$\\Rightarrow \\dfrac{2 \\pi}{T} \\times t=\\dfrac{\\pi}{4} \\Rightarrow t=T / 8$<br>D. If object starts from $x=0$, its equation is $x=A \\sin \\omega t$<br>For $x=+A / 2 \\Rightarrow \\dfrac{A}{2}=A \\sin \\omega t$<br>$\\therefore \\dfrac{1}{2}=\\sin \\omega t \\Rightarrow \\sin \\dfrac{\\pi}{6}=\\sin \\omega t$<br>$\\Rightarrow \\omega t=\\dfrac{\\pi}{6} \\Rightarrow t=\\dfrac{T}{12}$<br>Option (2) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Match the following',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(39191,'physics',31,1346,3237,'Sound velocity is maximum in','[{"text": "${N_2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${H_2}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${O_2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$He$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'$v \\propto \\sqrt{\\dfrac{\\gamma}{M}}$<br>Since $\\dfrac{\\gamma}{M}$ is maximum for $H_{2}$ so sound velocity is maximum in ${H_2}.$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(39192,'physics',3,314,579,'Two trains $A$ and $B$ of length 400 $m$ each are moving on two parallel tracks with a uniform speed of 72 $km/h$ in the same direction, with $A$ ahead of $B$. The driver of $B$ decides to overtake $A$ and acceleration by $1m/{s^2}.$ If after 50 $s$, the ground of $B$ just brushes past the driver of $A$, what was the original distance between them?','[{"text": "$1450\\\\,m$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1250\\\\,m$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$1350\\\\,m$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{None}}\\\\,{\\\\rm{of}}\\\\,{\\\\rm{these}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Length of each train,<br>$A = B = 400 \\, m$<br>Initial velocities of both trains,<br>${v_A} = {v_B} = 42 \\, km/h = 20\\,m/s$<br>Distance travelled by train $A$ in 50 $s$<br>${S_A} = {v_A} \\times t$<br>${S_A} = 20 \\times 50 = 1000\\,m$<br>Distance travelled by train $B$ in 50 $s$,<br>${S_B} = {u_B}t + \\frac{1}{2}{a_g}{t^2}$<br>${S_B} = 20 \\times 50 + \\frac{1}{2} \\times 1 \\times {\\left( {50} \\right)^2}$<br>= 1000 + 1250 = 2250 $m$<br>Original distance between the two trains<br>$ = {S_B} - {S_A}$<br>= 2250 - 1000 = 1250 $m$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,'NCERT Exemplar','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(39193,'physics',30,1359,3265,'A particle moves such that its acceleration $a$ is given by $a=-b x$, where $x$ is the displacement from equilibrium position and $b$ is constant. The period of oscillation is','[{"text": "$\\\\dfrac{2 \\\\pi}{\\\\sqrt{b}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$2 \\\\pi \\\\sqrt{b}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2 \\\\sqrt{\\\\dfrac{\\\\pi}{b}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{2 \\\\pi}{b}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Given that $a=-b x$<br>Comparing $a=-\\omega^{2} x \\Rightarrow \\omega^{2}=b \\Rightarrow \\omega=\\sqrt{b}$<br>$ \\Rightarrow \\frac{{2\\pi }}{T} = \\sqrt b  \\Rightarrow T = \\frac{{2\\pi }}{{\\sqrt b }}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(39194,'physics',26,1133,2705,'A tube of uniform cross-section has two vertical portions connected with a horizontal thin tube $8\\,cm$ long at their lower ends. Enough water to occupy $22\\,cm$ of the tube is poured into one branch and enough oil of specific gravity 0.8 to occupy $22\\,cm$ is poured into the other. Find the distance  of the common surface ${E}$ of the two liquids from point ${B}$.','[{"text": "$6\\\\,cm$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$4\\\\,cm$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$36\\\\,cm$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$56\\\\,cm$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'${h_{{w}}+8 {~cm}+h_{0}=22 {~cm}+22 {~cm}}$<br>$ \\Rightarrow {\\rm{ }}{h_w} + {h_0} = 36\\,cm$<br>${P_C} = {P_B} \\Rightarrow {h_0}{\\rho _0}g = {h_w}{\\rho _0}g$<br>$ \\Rightarrow {h_0} \\times 0.8 = {h_w} \\times 1$<br>$ \\Rightarrow {\\rm{ }}{h_0} = \\frac{{{h_w}}}{{0.8}} = 1.25{h_w} \\Rightarrow {h_w} + 1.25{h_w} = 36$<br>$ \\Rightarrow {h_w} = \\frac{{36}}{{2.25}} = 16\\,cm \\Rightarrow BE = 22 - 16 = 6\\,cm$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/26_39194_question_1.png'),
(39195,'physics',31,1346,3237,'The isothermal elasticity of a medium is $E_{i}$ and the adiabatic elasticity in $E_{a}$. The velocity of sound in the medium is proportional to','[{"text": "$E_{i}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$E_{a}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\sqrt{E_{i}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\sqrt{E_{a}}$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,NULL,NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(39196,'physics',3,312,574,'A body starting from rest moves along a straight line with a constant acceleration. The variation of speed (\\[v\\]) with distance (\\[s\\]) is represented by the graph.<div class="matching-question"></div>','[{"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'${v^2} - {0^2} = 2as$<br>${v^2} = 2as$',NULL,'Easy','Graph/Figure',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(39197,'physics',30,1359,3266,'Two simple harmonic motions are represented by equations<br>$y_{1}=4 \\sin (10 t+\\phi)$ and $y_{2}=5 \\cos 10 t$. What is the phase difference between their velocities?','[{"text": "$-\\\\phi$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\phi$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\left(\\\\phi-\\\\dfrac{\\\\pi}{2}\\\\right)$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\left(\\\\phi+\\\\dfrac{\\\\pi}{2}\\\\right)$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'$y_{1}=4 \\sin (10 t+\\phi)$;<br>${y_2} = 5\\cos 10t$<br>${v_1} = \\frac{{d{y_1}}}{{dt}} = 40\\cos (10t + \\phi )$<br>${v_2} = \\frac{{d{y_2}}}{{dt}} =  - 50\\sin 10t = 50\\cos (10t + \\pi /2)$<br>Phase difference between $v_{1}$ and $v_{2}=\\left(\\phi-\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\right)$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(39199,'physics',31,1346,3237,'It takes of 2.0 seconds for a sound wave to travel between two fixed points when the day temperature is $10^\\circ C$. If the temperature rise to $30^\\circ C$ the sound wave travels between the same fixed parts in','[{"text": "$2.0\\\\,{\\\\mkern 1mu} \\\\sec $", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1.9{\\\\mkern 1mu} \\\\,\\\\sec $", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$2.2\\\\,{\\\\mkern 1mu} \\\\sec $", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2.1{\\\\mkern 1mu} \\\\,\\\\sec $", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Suppose the distance between two fixed points is $d$ then<br>$t=\\dfrac{d}{v}$ also $v \\propto \\sqrt{T}=\\dfrac{t_{1}}{t_{2}}=\\dfrac{v_{2}}{v_{1}}=\\sqrt{\\dfrac{T_{2}}{T_{1}}}$<br>$\\Rightarrow \\dfrac{2}{t_{2}}=\\sqrt{\\dfrac{303}{283}} \\Rightarrow t_{2}=1.9 \\mathrm{sec}$.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(39200,'physics',3,312,574,'The acceleration time plot for a particle (starting from rest) moving on a straight line is shown in figure. For given time interval<div class="matching-question"></div>','[{"text": "The particle has turned around at $t$ = 10 $s$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "The particle has zero displacement", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "The average speed is same in both intervals 0 to 10 $s$ and 10 $s$ to 20 $s$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "The particle has non- zero average acceleration", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'As the graph is symmetric about $t$-axis average value of $a$ from $0 \\, s$ to $20 \\, s$ is zero. The average speed is same from $0 \\, s$ to $10 \\, s$ and from $10 \\, s$ to $20 \\,s$ since the area of triangles are same. From $0 \\, s$ to $10 \\, s$ the particle is in acceleration and $10 \\,s$ and $20 \\, s$ the particle is in deceleration but it is moving in same direction, so displacement is non zero.',NULL,'Medium','Graph/Figure',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/3_39200_question_1.png'),
(39201,'physics',30,1359,3266,'The phase difference between two SHM $y_{1}=10 \\sin \\left(10 \\pi t+\\dfrac{\\pi}{3}\\right)$ and $y_{2}=12 \\sin \\left(8 \\pi t+\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}\\right)$ at $t = 0.5\\,s$ is','[{"text": "$\\\\dfrac{13 \\\\pi}{12}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{11 \\\\pi}{12}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{17 \\\\pi}{12}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\pi$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Phase for $1^{s t}$ SHM: $\\phi_{1}=10 \\pi t+\\dfrac{\\pi}{3}$<br>Phase for $2^{\\text {nd }}$ SHM: $\\phi_{2}=8 \\pi t+\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}$<br>Phase difference: $\\Delta \\phi=\\phi_{1}-\\phi_{2}=2 \\pi t+\\dfrac{\\pi}{12}$<br>Put $t = 0.5{\\rm{ }}s$<br>$\\Delta \\phi=2 \\pi(0.5)+\\dfrac{\\pi}{12}=\\dfrac{13 \\pi}{12}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(39203,'physics',31,1347,3238,'In the case of closed end organ pipe','[{"text": "The maximum possible wavelength is same as that of open end organ pipe", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "The maximum possible wavelength is less than that of open end organ pipe", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "The maximum possible wavelength may be less than that of open end organ pipe", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "The maximum possible wavelength is greater than that of open end organ pipe", "isCorrect": true}]',3,NULL,NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(39204,'physics',3,312,574,'Acceleration – time graph of a body is shown in the figure. The corresponding velocity time graph of the same body is<div class="matching-question"></div>','[{"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Acceleration is constant for sometime so the velocity increases and then velocity remains constant as  $a = 0$. Again $a$ is $( + )ve$ and constant so velocity increases further.',NULL,'Medium','Graph/Figure',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/3_39204_question_1.png'),
(39205,'physics',30,1360,3267,'Total energy of a particle executing S.H.M. is ( $x$ is displacement from mean position):','[{"text": "Proportional to $x^{2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Proportional to $x$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Proportional to $x^{1 / 2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Independent of $x$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Total energy of particle in S.H.M.<br>$E=\\dfrac{1}{2} K A^{2} \\text { (independent of } x \\text { ) }$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(39207,'physics',31,1347,3239,'An open pipe is in resonance in its fundamental mode. Air, hydrogen, ethane are filled in succession in this pipe. The velocity of sound is different in these three media on account of which','[{"text": "Only the wavelength changes", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both frequency and wavelength change", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Only the frequency changes", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Amplitude change", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'$f=\\dfrac{v}{2 L}$<br>As $v$ changes falso changes since $L$  is constant.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(39208,'physics',3,310,572,'Velocity of a body starting from rest is shown in the diagram. Find the displacement and distance travelled by the body. From \\[t = 0 \\, s\\] to \\[8 \\, s\\]<div class="matching-question"></div>','[{"text": "$12m,0m$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$24m,24m$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$24m,0m$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$16m,16m$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'As velocity is positive the body is moving in only one direction throughout the time. In this case displacement and distance are same.<br>Distance $ = $ Displacement  $ = $ Area of the graph<br>$ = \\left( {\\frac{1}{2} \\times 2 \\times 4} \\right) + (4 \\times 4) + \\left( {\\frac{1}{2} \\times 2 \\times 4} \\right)$<br>$ = 24\\,m$',NULL,'Easy','Graph/Figure',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/3_39208_question_1.png'),
(39209,'physics',30,1360,3267,'A body executes simple harmonic motion. The potential energy (PE), kinetic energy (KE) and total energy (TE) are measured as a function of displacement $x$. Which of the following statement is true?','[{"text": "$PE$ is maximum when $x=0$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$TE$ is zero when $x=0$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$KE$ is maximum when $x$ is maximum", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$KE$ is maximum when $x=0$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'$P E=\\dfrac{1}{2} m \\omega^{2} x^{2}$<br>$KE = \\frac{1}{2}m{\\omega ^2}\\left( {{a^2} - {x^2}} \\right)$<br>$TE = PE + KE = \\frac{1}{2}m{\\omega ^2}{a^2}$<br>Since $PE$ is maximum at $x=a$ and $KE$ is maximum at $x=0$, therefore $TE$ remains constant throughout the motion.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(39210,'physics',26,1133,2710,'A uniform rod $A B, 12 {~m}$ long weighing 24 $kg$ , is supported at end $B$ by a flexible light string and a lead weight (of very small size) of 12 $kg$ attached at end $A$. The rod floats in water with one-half of its length submerged. Find the volume of the rod. [Take $g=10 {~m} / {s}^{2}$, density of water $=1000 {~kg} / {m}^{3}$ ]','[{"text": "$64 \\\\times {10^{ - 3}}{m^3}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$38 \\\\times {10^{ - 3}}{m^3}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$79 \\\\times {10^{ - 3}}{m^3}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$56 \\\\times {10^{ - 3}}{m^3}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'<img src=\'/img/data/physics/26_39210_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />  $T + U = {W_1} + {W_2} = 360\\,N\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,(1)$<br>$U = \\frac{V}{2}{\\rho _w}g = (V/2)\\left( {{{10}^3}} \\right)(10)$<br>or $\\quad U = 0.5 \\times {10^4}\\,V\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,(2)$<br>Taking torque about $M=0$<br>$\\begin{array}{ll}\\therefore & 120\\left(\\dfrac{l}{4}\\right)+T\\left(\\dfrac{3 l}{4}\\right)=240\\left(\\dfrac{l}{4}\\right) \\\\\\text { or } & T=\\dfrac{240-120}{3}=40 {~N}=4 g\\end{array}$<br>Now from Eqs. (1) and (2), we get<br>$V=6.4 \\times 10^{-2} {~m}^{3}=64 \\times 10^{-3} {~m}^{3}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/26_39210_question_1.png'),
(39211,'physics',31,1347,3238,'A sufficiently long closed organ pipe has a small hole at its bottom. Initially, the pipe is empty. Water is poured into the pipe at a constant rate. The fundamental frequency of the air column in the pipe<div class="matching-question"></div>','[{"text": "Continuously increase", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "First increases and then becomes constant", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Continuously decreases", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "First decreases and then becomes constant", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Fundamental frequency $f$ is $f=\\dfrac{v}{4 \\ell}$ when the pipe gets filled up $\\ell$ decreases and hence $f$ increases. When the inflow and outflow of water are equal $\\ell$ becomes constant and hence $f$ also becomes constant.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(39212,'physics',30,1360,3267,'The time period of the variation of potential energy of a particle executing SHM with period $T$ is','[{"text": "$T$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$T/4$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$T/2$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$2\\\\,T$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'The potential energy of a particle executing SHM is periodic with time period $\\dfrac{T}{2}$.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(39214,'physics',3,311,573,'\\[s-t\\] graph of a particle in motion is shown in figure. Calculate average speed and average velocity.<div class="matching-question"></div>','[{"text": "$0,0$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$10\\\\,m/s,5\\\\,m/s$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$5\\\\,m{s^{ - 1}},0$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$0,5\\\\,m/s$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'The given graph is between $s$ and $t$<br>${\\rm{Total}}\\,{\\rm{distance}} = 20 + 20 + 20 + 20 = 80\\,m$<br>Total displacement $ = 0$ (Finally the body position is zero at $t = 16\\,s$)<br>${\\rm{Average}}\\,{\\rm{speed}} = \\frac{{80 \\, m}}{{16 \\, s}} = 5 \\, m{s^{ - 1}}$<br>Average velocity $ = 0$',NULL,'Medium','Graph/Figure',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/3_39214_question_1.png'),
(39215,'physics',31,1347,3239,'If the fundamental frequency of a pipe closed at one end in $512\\,Hz$. The fundamental frequency of a pipe of the same dimensions but open at both ends will be:','[{"text": "$512\\\\,Hz$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1024\\\\,Hz$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$128\\\\,Hz$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$256\\\\,Hz$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'${f_{{\\text{closed }}}} = \\frac{v}{{4L}}$<br>${f_{{\\text{open }}}} = \\frac{v}{{2L}}$<br>$\\therefore \\,\\,\\,{\\mkern 1mu} {\\kern 1pt} {f_{{\\text{open }}}} = 2 \\times 512 = 1024\\,Hz$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(39216,'physics',30,1360,3267,'The kinetic energy of SHM is $1 / n$ time its potential energy. If the amplitude of the SHM be $A$, then what is the displacement of the particle?','[{"text": "$\\\\dfrac{A}{n}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\sqrt{\\\\dfrac{n+1}{n}} A$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\sqrt{\\\\dfrac{n}{n+1}} A$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$n A$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'$\\begin{aligned}& K E=\\dfrac{1}{n} P E \\\\& \\dfrac{1}{2} m \\omega^{2}\\left(A^{2}-x^{2}\\right)=\\dfrac{1}{n}\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2} m \\omega^{2} x^{2}\\right)\\end{aligned}$<br>$\\Rightarrow x=\\sqrt{\\dfrac{n}{n+1}} A$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(39227,'physics',31,1347,3238,'Two closed organ pipes $A$ and $B$, have the same length. $A$ is wider than $B$. The resonate in fundamental mode at frequencies $n_{A}$ and $n_{B}$ respectively then','[{"text": "$n_{A}>n_{B}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\quad n_{A} < n_{B}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Either (1) or (2) depending on the ratio of their diameters", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$n_{A}=n_{B}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'In closed organ pipe. First resonance occurs at $\\lambda / 4$.<br>So, in fundamental mode of vibration of organ pipe $\\dfrac{\\lambda}{4}=(l+0.3 d)$<br>Where $0.3 d$ is end correction.<br>Frequency of vibration $n=\\dfrac{v}{\\lambda}=\\dfrac{v}{4(l+0.3 d)}$<br>As $l$ is same, wider pipe $A$ will resonate at a lower frequency, i.e., $n_{A} < n_{B}$.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(39217,'physics',26,1136,2715,'Water is flowing in a canal speed $v=10 {~m} / {s}$. A bend tube is lowered into the canal as shown in figure. The upper end of the tube is held at a height $h_{0}=3 {~m}$ above the surface of the water. To what height \' $h$ \' above the upper end of the tube,  will the water jet spurt? Assume that flow remains ideal. (Take $g=10 {~m} / {s}^{2}$ )','[{"text": "$5\\\\,m$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2\\\\,m$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$10\\\\,m$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$7\\\\,m$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Let the depth of the tube entrance be $y$ from top surface of flowing water. Consider point 1 (at the entry) and point 2 (at the maximum height of the fountain) on a streamline. Apply Bernoulli\'s theorem<br>$P_{1}+\\rho g h_{1}+\\dfrac{1}{2} \\rho v_{1}^{2}=P_{2}+\\rho g h_{2}+\\dfrac{1}{2} \\rho v_{2}^{2}$ <img src=\'/img/data/physics/26_39217_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />  Let us consider point  1  as reference level hence, $h_{1}=0$ and $P_{1}=P_{0}+\\rho g y$, and $P_{2}=P_{0}$ where $P_{0}=$ atmospheric pressure<br>$\\left( {{P_0} + \\rho gy} \\right) + 0 + \\frac{1}{2}\\rho {v^2}$<br>$ = {P_0} + \\rho g\\left( {y + {h_0} + h} \\right) + 0$<br>[Since speed at top point 2 is zero]<br>$\\therefore \\quad \\dfrac{1}{2} \\rho v^{2}=\\rho g\\left(h_{0}+h\\right) \\quad \\therefore h=\\dfrac{v^{2}}{2 g}-h_{0}$<br>$\\Rightarrow h=\\dfrac{10^{2}}{2 g}-3=2 {~m}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/26_39217_question_1.png'),
(39218,'physics',3,309,571,'The displacement of a particle as a function of time is in fig. The graph indicates that<div class="matching-question"></div>','[{"text": "The particle starts with a certain velocity, but the motion is retarded and finally the particle stops.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "The velocity of particle is constant throughout motion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "The acceleration of the particle is constant throughout motion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "The particle starts with constant velocity, the motion is accelerated and finally the particle moves with another constant velocity.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'The slope of the graph changes, decreases continuously $\\left( {\\frac{{dx}}{{dt}} = v} \\right)$ and finally it becomes zero.',NULL,'Easy','Graph/Figure',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/3_39218_question_1.png'),
(39219,'physics',31,1347,3243,'$150,225,300,375$, is a series of resonant frequency. Which frequency is missing lower than $400\\,Hz$','[{"text": "350", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "250", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "75", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "200", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Series of resonant frequency must be integral multiple of fundamental frequency. This series is integral multiple of $75\\,Hz$. Hence missing frequency is $75\\,Hz$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(39220,'physics',30,1360,3267,'The K. E and P. E of a particle executing SHM with amplitude $A$ will be equal when its displacement is','[{"text": "$A/2$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$A\\\\sqrt 2 $", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$A\\\\sqrt {2/3} $", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$A/\\\\sqrt 2 $", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'$\\mathrm{KE}=\\mathrm{PE}$<br>$\\dfrac{1}{2} m \\omega^{2}\\left(\\mathrm{~A}^{2}-y^{2}\\right)=\\dfrac{1}{2} m \\omega^{2} y^{2} \\Rightarrow y=\\dfrac{A}{\\sqrt{2}}$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(39221,'physics',26,1136,2717,'Calculate work should be done (in $J$ ) in order to squeeze all water from a horizontally located cylinder as shown in figure during the time $t=10 {~s}$ by means of a container force acting on the piston? The volume of water in the cylinder is equal to $V=1$ lit, the cross-sectional area of the orifice is $a=0.5 {~cm}^{2}$, with a being considerably less than the pistol area. The friction and viscosity are negligibly small.','[{"text": "$3.0$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$7.0$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$4.0$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2.0$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Work done to squeeze water $=F \\cdot L$   <img src=\'/img/data/physics/26_39221_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />  If $v$ be the velocity at point $B$,then applying Bernoulli\'s theorem at points \'1\' and \'2\',we have<br>$P_{1}+\\dfrac{1}{2} \\rho v_{1}^{2}=P_{2}+\\dfrac{1}{2} \\rho v_{2}^{2}$<br>Here $P_{1}=P_{0}+\\dfrac{F}{A}$ and $P_{2}=P_{o}$<br> also $r_{1}$ is very much smaller than $v_{2}$, can be neglected hence<br>we have<br>${P_0} + \\frac{F}{A} = {P_0} + \\frac{1}{2}\\rho {v^2}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,(1)$<br>Force on piston$F = P \\cdot A = \\frac{1}{2}\\rho {v^2}A\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,(2)$<br>Volume of liquid coming through orifice per unit time $=a_{v}$<br>Volume of liquid coming through during time \' $t$ \' $V=a v t$<br>Velocity of liquid through orifice $v=\\left(\\dfrac{V}{a t}\\right)$<br>From Eqs. (1) and (2) $F=\\dfrac{1}{2} \\rho\\left(\\dfrac{V}{a t}\\right) A$<br>Thus work done is<br> $W=F L=\\dfrac{1}{2} \\rho\\left(\\dfrac{V}{a t}\\right) A L=\\dfrac{1}{2} a\\left(\\dfrac{V^{2}}{a t}\\right) V$<br>hence <br>$W = \\frac{1}{2}\\rho \\frac{{{V^3}}}{{{a^2}{t^2}}}$<br>$ = \\frac{1}{2}1000\\frac{{{{\\left( {1000 \\times {{10}^{ - 6}}} \\right)}^3}}}{{{{\\left( {0.5 \\times {{10}^{ - 4}}} \\right)}^2}{{10}^2}}} = 2.0\\,J$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/26_39221_question_1.png'),
(39222,'physics',3,309,571,'The displacement-time graph of a moving particle is shown in figure. The instantaneous velocity of the particle is negative at the point.<div class="matching-question"></div>','[{"text": "$F$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$D$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$E$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$C$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'The slope of the graph is negative at this point.',NULL,'Easy','Graph/Figure',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/3_39222_question_1.png'),
(39223,'physics',31,1347,3239,'In one metre long open pipe what is the harmonic of resonance obtained with a tuning fork of frequency $480\\,Hz$. $(v = 320\\,m/s)$','[{"text": "Third", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Fifth", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Fourth", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Sixth", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'In case of open pipe, $f=\\dfrac{n v}{2 l}$ where $n=$ order of harmonics $=$ order of mode of vibration<br>$\\Rightarrow n=\\dfrac{f \\times 2 l}{v}$<br>$ = \\frac{{480}}{{320}} \\times 2 \\times 1 = 3\\,\\,\\,{\\mkern 1mu} {\\kern 1pt} [{\\text{ Here }}v = 320\\,m/s]$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(39224,'physics',30,1360,3267,'A particle executes SHM along a straight line. It starts its motion at extreme position at $t = 0$. The time interval after which its kinetic energy will be equal to its potential energy for the first time is $(T = $ Time period)','[{"text": "$T/5$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$T / 4$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$T / 6$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$T/8$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Let $x=a \\cos \\omega t$<br>$\\begin{aligned}& \\therefore \\quad K E=\\dfrac{1}{2} m a^{2} \\omega^{2} \\sin ^{2} \\omega t \\\\& P E=\\dfrac{1}{2} m a^{2} \\omega^{2} \\cos ^{2} \\omega t\\end{aligned}$<br>when $K E=P E, \\quad \\sin ^{2} \\omega t=\\cos ^{2} \\omega t$<br>$\\begin{aligned}& \\therefore \\quad \\tan \\omega t= \\pm 1 \\\\& \\therefore \\quad \\omega t=\\dfrac{\\pi}{4} \\Rightarrow t_{\\min }=\\dfrac{\\pi}{4 \\omega}=\\dfrac{T}{8} .\\end{aligned}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(39225,'physics',3,313,577,'A body is projected vertically upward from the surface of the earth, then the velocity time graph is<div class="matching-question"></div>','[{"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'The velocity of the body as a function of time is $v = u - gt$. The graph is a straight line having $( - )ve$ slope. The correct option is (2).',NULL,'Medium','Graph/Figure',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(39228,'physics',30,1360,3267,'In SHM restoring force is $F=-k x$, where $k$ is force constant, $x$ is displacement and $A$ is amplitude of motion then total energy depends upon:','[{"text": "$k, x, M$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$k, A$ and $M$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$k, x$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$k, A$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'In SHM restoring force is<br>$F=-m \\omega^{2} x$<br>Given that $F=-k x$<br>Hence $\\quad k=m \\omega^{2}$<br>Total energy is $\\mathrm{TE}=\\dfrac{1}{2} m \\omega^{2} A^{2}=\\dfrac{1}{2} k A^{2}$.<br>Hence total energy depends upon $k$ and $A$.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(39229,'physics',3,312,574,'A particle is initially at rest, it is subjected to a linear acceleration \\[a\\] as shown in figure. The maximum speed attained by the particle is<div class="matching-question"></div>','[{"text": "$605\\\\,m/s$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$55\\\\,m/s$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$110\\\\,m/s$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$550\\\\,m/s$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Throughout the motion the body is under acceleration but with decreasing $a$ value. The velocity reaches maximum at the end <br>${v_{\\max }} = \\Delta v = {\\rm{Area}} = \\frac{1}{2} \\times 10 \\times 11 = 55 \\, m/s$',NULL,'Easy','Graph/Figure',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/3_39229_question_1.png'),
(39231,'physics',31,1347,3238,'In a resonance tube experiment, a closed organ pipe of length $120\\,cm$ is used and tuned with a tuning fork of frequency $340\\,Hz$. If water is poured into the pipe, then incorrect statement among the following [velocity of sound in air is $340\\,m/s$, neglect end correction]','[{"text": "Minimum length of water column to have the resonance is $45\\\\,cm$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "The distance between two successive nodes is $50\\\\,cm$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "The maximum length of water column to create the resonance is $95\\\\,cm$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "The distance between two successive nodes is $25\\\\,cm$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'From $v=f \\lambda$<br>$ \\Rightarrow 340 = 340 \\times \\lambda  = \\lambda  = 1\\,m$<br>For $1^{\\text {st }}$ resonance, $l_{1}=\\dfrac{\\lambda}{4}$<br>${l_1} = 25\\,cm$, so length of water column is $95\\,cm$.<br> For $2^{\\text {nd }}$ resonance, ${l_2} = \\frac{{3\\lambda }}{4} = 75\\,cm$,<br>Separation between consecutive nodes is, ${l_2} - {l_1} = 50\\,cm$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(39232,'physics',30,1360,3267,'The P.E. of a particle executing SHM at a distance $x$ from its equilibrium position is','[{"text": "$\\\\dfrac{1}{2} m \\\\omega^{2} a^{2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{1}{2} m \\\\omega^{2} x^{2}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Zero", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{1}{2} m \\\\omega^{2}\\\\left(a^{2}-x^{2}\\\\right)$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,NULL,NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(39233,'physics',3,312,574,'Study the following graphs:<br>The particle has negative acceleration<div class="matching-question"></div>','[{"text": "In graph (ii)", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "In graph (i)", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "In graph (iii)", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "In graph (iv)", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'For the graphs (i) and (iv), slope is constant, hence the velocity is constant and  hence acceleration is zero. <br>For the graph (iii), the particle’s velocity first decreases and then increases. It means negative acceleration is involved in this motion. For graph (ii) velocity increases and hence it is an accelerated motion.',NULL,'Medium','Graph/Figure',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/3_39233_question_1.png'),
(39234,'physics',26,1136,2719,'A cylindrical vessel having cross-sectional area $A=0.5 {~m}^{2}$ is filled with two liquids of density $\\rho_{1}=500 {~kg} {~m}^{-3}$ and $\\rho_{2}=$ $1000 {~kg} {~m}^{-3}$, to a height $h=50 {~cm}$ each as shown in figure. A small hole having area $a=5 {~cm}^{2}$ is made in right vertical wall at a height $y=10 {~cm}$ from the bottom. Calculate the maximum values of $F$  to keep the cylinder in static equilibrium, if coefficient of friction between the cylinder and the plane is $\\mu=0.02$. $\\left(g=10 {~ms}^{-2}\\right)$.','[{"text": "$120.1\\\\,N$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$154.5\\\\,N$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$184.5\\\\,N$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$132.5\\\\,N$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Since area $a$ of hole is very small in comparison to base area $A$ of the cylinder, therefore, velocity of liquid inside the cylinder is negligible.<br>Let velocity of efflux be $v$ and atmospheric pressure $P_{0}$.Consider two-point $A$ (inside the cylinder) and $B$ (just outside the hole) in the same horizontal line as shown in figure.<br>Pressure at $A, P_{A}=P_{0}+h \\rho_{2} g+(h-y) \\rho_{1} g$<br>Pressure at $B, P_{B}=P_{0}$  <img src=\'/img/data/physics/26_39234_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />  According to Bernoulli\'s theorem,<br>Pressure energy at $A=$ pressure energy at $B\\,+$ kinetic energy at $B$$P_{A}=P_{B}+\\dfrac{1}{2} \\rho_{1} v^{2} \\Rightarrow v=3 {~ms}^{-1}$<br>When cylinder is on smooth horizontal plane, force $F$ required to keep cylinder stationary equals horizontal thrust exerted by water jet. But that is equal to mass flowing per second $\\times$ change in velocity of this mass.<br>$F_{\\text {thrust }}=v_{\\text {rel }} \\dfrac{d m}{d t}=(v-0) \\times(a v \\rho)=a \\rho v^{2}$<br>Hence $F_{\\text {thrust }}=5 \\times 10^{-4} \\times 1000 \\times(3)^{2}=4.5 {~N}$<br>Total mass of the liquid in the cylinder is.$m=A h \\rho_{1}+A h \\rho_{2}=750 {~kg}$  <img src=\'/img/data/physics/26_39234_explanation_2.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />   Limiting friction $=\\mu m g=150 {~N}$.<br>Considering free body diagram for maximum value of force.<br>Considering vertical force, $N=m g$<br>Now considering horizontal force, ${F_{\\max {\\rm{ }}}} = {F_{{\\rm{thrust }}}} + \\mu N$<br>${F_{\\max }} = 4.5 + 150 = 154.5\\,N$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/26_39234_question_1.png'),
(39235,'physics',31,1347,3238,'Air is blown at the mouth of an open tube of length $25\\,cm$ and diameter $2\\,cm$. If the velocity of sound in air is $330\\,m{s^{ - 1}}$, then emitted frequencies are (in $Hz$ )','[{"text": "$660,1000,3300$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$660,1320,1980$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$330,990,1690$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$302,664,1320$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Fundamental frequency of open tube is, $f=\\dfrac{v}{2 L}$<br>where $v$ is the velocity of sound in air and $L$ is the length of the tube<br>$\\therefore f = \\frac{{330\\,m{s^{ - 1}}}}{{2 \\times 0.25\\,m}} = 660\\,Hz$<br>The emitted frequencies are $f, 2 f, 3 f, 4 f \\ldots$<br>i.e., ($660\\,Hz,1320\\,Hz,1980\\,Hz,2640\\,Hz, \\ldots $',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(39236,'physics',30,1360,3267,'A particle is executing SHM with an amplitude of $4\\,cm$. At what distance from equilibrium position its potential energy will be $25 \\%$ of its total energy?','[{"text": "$\\\\sqrt 2 \\\\,cm$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2\\\\sqrt 3 \\\\,cm$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2\\\\,cm$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$2\\\\sqrt 2 \\\\,cm$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'$\\dfrac{1}{2} m \\omega^{2} x^{2}=\\dfrac{1}{4} \\times \\dfrac{1}{2} m a^{2} \\omega^{2}$<br>$ \\Rightarrow \\,\\,\\,{\\mkern 1mu} {\\kern 1pt} x = \\frac{a}{2} = \\frac{4}{2}\\,cm = 2\\,cm$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(39237,'physics',3,312,574,'\\[a - t\\] graph for a particle moving in a straight line is as shown in figure. Change in velocity of the particle from \\[t = 0\\] to \\[t = 6\\] sec is<div class="matching-question"></div>','[{"text": "$10\\\\,m/s$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$4\\\\,m/s$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$12\\\\,m/s$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$8\\\\,m/s$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'$\\Delta v = {\\rm{Area}} = \\frac{1}{2} \\times 4 \\times 4 - \\frac{1}{2} \\times 2 \\times 4 = 4 \\, m/s$',NULL,'Medium','Graph/Figure',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/3_39237_question_1.png'),
(39239,'physics',31,1347,3239,'$n_{1}$ is the frequency of the pipe closed at one end and $n_{2}$ is the frequency of the pipe open at both ends. If both are joined end to end, find the fundamental frequency of closed pipe that is formed:','[{"text": "$\\\\dfrac{n_{1} n_{2}}{2 n_{2}+2 n_{1}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{n_{1} n_{2}}{n_{2}+2 n_{1}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{2 n_{1}+n_{2}}{n_{2} n_{1}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{n_{1}+2 n_{2}}{n_{2} 2 n_{1}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Frequency of closed pipe,<br>$n_{1}=\\dfrac{v}{4 l_{1}} \\Rightarrow l_{1}=\\dfrac{v}{4 n_{1}}$<br>Frequency of open pipe, $n_{2}=\\dfrac{v}{2 l_{2}} \\Rightarrow l_{2}=\\dfrac{v}{2 n_{2}}$<br>When both pipes are joined, then length of closed pipe<br>$\\begin{aligned}& l=l_{1}+l_{2} \\\\& \\dfrac{v}{4 n}=\\dfrac{v}{4 n_{1}}+\\dfrac{v}{2 n_{2}} \\\\& \\Rightarrow \\dfrac{1}{2 n}=\\dfrac{1}{2 n_{1}}+\\dfrac{1}{n_{2}} \\\\& \\Rightarrow n=\\dfrac{n_{1} n_{2}}{n_{2}+2 n_{1}}\\end{aligned}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(39240,'physics',30,1360,3267,'The total energy of the body executing simple harmonic motion is $E$ then, the kinetic energy when the displacement is at the mid point of mean and extreme position is','[{"text": "$\\\\dfrac{E}{4}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{E}{2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{\\\\sqrt{3} E}{4}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{3 E}{4}$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Total energy of SHM,<br>$E=\\dfrac{1}{2} m \\omega^{2} a^{2},(\\text { where }, a=\\text { amplitude })$<br>Kinetic energy,<br>$K=\\dfrac{1}{2} m \\omega^{2}\\left(a^{2}-y^{2}\\right) \\Rightarrow K=E-\\dfrac{1}{2} m \\omega^{2} y^{2}$<br>When $y=\\dfrac{a}{2} \\Rightarrow K=E-\\dfrac{1}{2} m \\omega^{2}\\left(\\dfrac{a^{2}}{4}\\right)=E-\\dfrac{E}{4}$<br>$K=\\dfrac{3 E}{4}$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(39242,'physics',3,312,574,'For motion of a particle, acceleration time graph is shown in figure. Then the velocity time graph is<div class="matching-question"></div>','[{"text": "", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'From $t = 0s$ to $2s$ the velocity increases and from $t = 2s$ velocity decreases and finally becomes zero. Throughout the motion $v$ is $( + )ve$',NULL,'Medium','Graph/Figure',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/3_39242_question_1.png'),
(39243,'physics',31,1347,3238,'Velocity of sound in air is \\[320\\;m{s^{ - 1}}\\]. A pipe closed at one end has a length of  \\[1\\;m\\]. Neglecting end correction, the air column in the pipe cannot resonate with sound of frequency<div class="matching-question"></div>','[{"text": "$240\\\\,Hz$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$80\\\\,Hz$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$400\\\\,Hz$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$320\\\\,Hz$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'In a closed organ pipe the fundamental frequency is<br>$f = \\frac{v}{{4L}}f = \\frac{{320\\,m{s^{ - 1}}}}{{4 \\times 1\\,{m^2}}} = 80\\,Hz$<br>In a closed organ pipe only odd harmonics are present. So, it can resonate with $80\\,Hz,240\\,Hz,{\\text{ }}400\\,Hz,560\\,Hz$.<br>Option (4) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(39244,'physics',30,1360,3267,'If $ < E > $ and $ < U > $ denote the average kinetic and the average potential energies respectively of mass describing a simple harmonic motion, over one period, then the correct relation is','[{"text": "$ < E > \\\\, = 2\\\\, < U > $", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$ < E > \\\\, = \\\\, < U > $", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$ < E > \\\\, =  -  < U > $", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$ < E >  =  - 2 < U > $", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'$E_{a v}=U_{a v}=\\dfrac{1}{4} m \\omega^{2} A^{2}$<br>$ < E >  =  < U > $',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(39245,'physics',3,312,574,'Velocity versus displacement graph of a particle moving in a straight line is shown in figure. Corresponding acceleration versus velocity graph will be:<div class="matching-question"></div>','[{"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'The relation between $v$ and $s$ is <br>$v = ms$<br>$m = \\frac{{10 - 0}}{{10 - 0}} = 1$<br>$v = s$<br>on differentiating the equation w.r.t. time $t$ <br>$\\frac{{dv}}{{dt}} = \\frac{{ds}}{{dt}}$<br>$a = v$',NULL,'Medium','Graph/Figure',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/3_39245_question_1.png'),
(39246,'physics',26,1133,2710,'A solid cylinder of height $h$ and mass $m$ is floating in a liquid of density $\\rho$ as shown in the figure. Find the acceleration of the vessel  containing liquid for which the relative downward acceleration of the completely immersed cylinder w.r.t. vessel becomes equal to one-third of that of the vessel. (Take $g=10 {~m} / {s}^{2}$)','[{"text": "$1\\\\,m{s^{ - 2}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$5\\\\,m{s^{ - 2}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$8\\\\,m{s^{ - 2}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$10\\\\,m{s^{ - 2}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Let the volume of the cylinder be $V$. When the cylinder is floating, up thrust $=$ weight. Hence,  $\\rho=\\left(\\dfrac{3}{4} V\\right) g=m g \\Rightarrow V=\\dfrac{4 m}{3 \\rho}$<br>Let the acceleration of the particle vessel be $A$ (upwards). In the reference frame of the vessel, the acceleration of the cylinder is $A \\cdot 3$.<br>${\\therefore \\quad mg + mA - {\\rm{up}}\\,\\,{\\rm{trust}} = m\\left( {\\frac{A}{3}} \\right)}$<br>${mg + mA - \\rho V{g^\\prime } = m\\left( {\\frac{A}{3}} \\right)}$<br>where $g^{\\prime}=g+A=$ effective value of $g$ for upthrust.<br>$\\therefore \\,\\,mg + mA - \\rho V(g + A) = m\\left( {\\frac{A}{3}} \\right)$<br>$ \\Rightarrow mg - \\frac{4}{3}m(g + A) =  - m\\frac{2}{3}A$<br>$A = \\left( { - \\frac{g}{2}} \\right){\\rm{ upwards }}$<br>The acceleration of the vessel should be $\\dfrac{g}{2}=\\dfrac{10}{2}=5 {~ms}^{-2}$(downwards).',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/26_39246_question_1.png'),
(39247,'physics',31,1345,3232,'For superposition of two waves, the following is correct','[{"text": "They must have the same frequency and wavelength", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "They must have equal frequencies but may have unequal wavelengths", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "They must have the same wavelength, but may have different frequencies", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "They may have different wavelength and different frequencies", "isCorrect": true}]',3,NULL,NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(39248,'physics',30,1362,3269,'In a simple pendulum if iron sphere is replaced by a wooden sphere of same mass time period.','[{"text": "Increases", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Decreases", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Remains same", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "The pendulum does not oscillate", "isCorrect": false}]',2,NULL,NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(39250,'physics',3,313,578,'A uniform moving cricket ball is turned back by hitting it with a bat for a very short time interval.  The correct representation of  variation of its  acceleration with time from the following graphs. (Take acceleration in the backward  direction as positive)<div class="matching-question"></div>','[{"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'When the bat hits the ball first the ball decelerates and then it accelerates. In both cases the bat exerts force in backward direction only so $a = ( + )ve$ throughout the motion. It increases from 0 and reaches maximum value and then decreases to zero.',NULL,'Medium','Graph/Figure',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(39252,'physics',30,1362,3269,'The period of a simple pendulum suspended from the ceiling of a car is $T$ when the car is at rest. If the car moves with a constant acceleration the period of the pendulum','[{"text": "Remains same", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Decreases", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Increases", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "First increases then decreases", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'$\\begin{aligned}& T=2 \\pi \\sqrt{\\dfrac{l}{g^{\\prime}}} \\\\& g^{\\prime}=\\sqrt{g^{2}+a^{2}}\\end{aligned}$<br>Where $a$ is the acceleration of the car<br>As $g^{\\prime}>g \\Rightarrow T$ decreases',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(39253,'physics',3,309,571,'Acceleration time graph is given below. If the body started from zero velocity find out the final velocity after 3 seconds of motion in a straight line.<div class="matching-question"></div>','[{"text": "$10\\\\,m/s$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$20\\\\,m/s$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$15\\\\,m/s$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$5\\\\,m/s$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Change in velocity $\\Delta v = \\int\\limits_0^3 {a\\,dt} $<br>$\\Delta v = $ Area of the graph<br>$\\Delta v = \\frac{1}{2} \\times 1 \\times 4 + 2 \\times 4$<br>$v - 0 = 10 \\, m/s$  (initial velocity is zero)<br>$v = 10 \\, m/s$',NULL,'Easy','Graph/Figure',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/3_39253_question_1.png'),
(39255,'physics',31,1345,3229,'Two waves having intensity $I$ and $9I$ produce interference. If the resultant intensity at a point is $7I$, what is the phase difference between the two waves?','[{"text": "$60^{\\\\circ}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$0^{\\\\circ}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$120^{\\\\circ}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$90^{\\\\circ}$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'$I_{R}=I_{1}+I_{2}+2 \\sqrt{I_{1} I_{2}} \\cos \\phi$<br>$7 I=I+9 I+2(3 I) \\cos \\phi$<br>$-3 I=6 I \\cos \\phi$<br>$\\cos \\phi=-\\dfrac{1}{2} \\Rightarrow \\varphi=120^{\\circ}$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(39256,'physics',30,1362,3269,'A simple pendulum has a hollow sphere containing mercury suspended by means of a wire. If a little mercury is drained off, the period of the pendulum will','[{"text": "Increase", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Decrease", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Remains unchanged", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Become erratic", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'The CM of the (bob + liquid) system goes down and hence $l$ increases. So $T$ also increases.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(39257,'physics',3,309,571,'Figure shows the position of a particle moving on \\[x\\]-axis as a function of time<div class="matching-question"></div>','[{"text": "The particle has come to rest 5 times", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Initial speed of particle was zero", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "The velocity remains positive for $t = 0\\\\,s$ to $t = 6s$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "The average velocity for the total period shown is negative", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'The slope of the tangent drawn to the curve is zero at five instants.<br>',NULL,'Medium','Graph/Figure',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/3_39257_question_1.png'),
(39259,'physics',31,1345,3229,'$S_{1}$ and $S_{2}$ are two coherent sources of sound of frequency $110\\,Hz$ each. they have no initial phase difference. The intensity at a point $P$ due to $S_{1}$ is $I_{0}$ and due to $S_{2}$ is $4 I_{0}$. If the velocity of sound is $330\\,m/s$ then the resultant intensity at $P$ is','[{"text": "$9 I_{0}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$I_{0}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$8 I_{0}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$3 I_{0}$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'The wavelength of sound source $=\\dfrac{330}{110}=3$ metre.<br>The phase difference between interfering waves at Pis<br>$=\\Delta x=\\dfrac{2 \\pi}{\\lambda}\\left(S_{2} P-S_{1} P\\right)=\\dfrac{2 \\pi}{3}(5-4)=\\dfrac{2 \\pi}{3}$<br>$\\therefore$ Resultant intensity at $P$ is<br>$I_{R}=I_{0}+4 I_{0}+2 \\sqrt{I_{0}} \\sqrt{4 I_{0}} \\cos \\dfrac{2 \\pi}{3}=3 I_{0}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/31_39259_question_1.png'),
(39260,'physics',30,1362,3269,'The period of a simple pendulum is doubled, when','[{"text": "the mass of the bob is doubled", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Its lengths is doubled", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "The mass of the bob and the length of the pendulum are doubled", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Its length is made four times", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'$T=2 \\pi \\sqrt{\\dfrac{l}{g}} \\Rightarrow T \\propto \\sqrt{l}$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(39261,'physics',3,309,571,'A ball is dropped from certain height on a surface. It collides elastically and comes back to initial position. If this process is repeated then velocity time graph is<div class="matching-question"></div>','[{"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Conceptual',NULL,'Medium','Graph/Figure',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(39263,'physics',31,1348,3245,'When two sound waves of frequency differing by more than $10\\,Hz$ reach our ear simultaneously','[{"text": "Interference of sound does not take place", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Beats are not produced", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Beats are produced but we cannot identify them", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "The waves destructively interfere", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Persistence of human ear is $0.1\\,s$<br>$\\therefore$ More than 10 beats can\'t be heared',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(39264,'physics',30,1362,3269,'A simple pendulum is suspended from the ceiling of a lift. When the lift is at rest its time period is $T$. With what acceleration should the lift be accelerated upwards in order to reduce its period to $T / 2$ ( $g$ acceleration due to gravity)','[{"text": "$g$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$4 g$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$3 g$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$2 g$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'$T=2 \\pi \\sqrt{\\dfrac{1}{g}}$.<br>$T^{\\prime}=\\dfrac{2 \\pi}{2} \\sqrt{\\dfrac{l}{g}} \\Rightarrow T^{\\prime}=2 \\pi \\sqrt{\\dfrac{l}{4 g}}$<br>the lift should be accelerated by $3 g$ upwards because the total acceleartion $g+3 g$ pseudo acceleration.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(39266,'physics',3,310,572,'Velocity of a body is shown in the graph. The graph is a semicircle in shape. Find the displacement and distance in time from 0 \\[s\\] to 4 \\[s\\].<div class="matching-question"></div>','[{"text": "$10\\\\,\\\\pi m$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$20\\\\,\\\\pi m$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$30\\\\,\\\\pi m$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$5\\\\,\\\\pi m$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Displacement $ = $ distance  (as the body is travelling in one direction though out time)<br>$ = {\\rm{Area}} = \\frac{{\\pi {r^2}}}{2} = \\frac{{\\pi {r_v}{r_t}}}{2}$<br>$ = \\frac{{\\pi  \\times 10 \\times 2}}{2} = 10 \\, \\pi m$<br>Here ${r_v} - $ velocity radius,  ${r_t} - $ time radius',NULL,'Medium','Graph/Figure',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/3_39266_question_1.png'),
(39434,'physics',31,1345,3232,'A piece of wire cut into two pieces $A$ and $B$ and stretched to the same tension and fixed between two rigid walls. Segment $A$ is longer than segment $B$. Which of the following quantities will always be larger for waves on $A$ than for waves on $B$','[{"text": "Amplitude of the wave", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Frequency of the fundamental mode", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Wave velocity", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Wavelength of fundamental mode", "isCorrect": true}]',3,NULL,NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(39267,'physics',31,1348,3245,'At a point, beat frequency of $n\\,Hz$ is observed. It means:','[{"text": "Amplitude of vibrations changes simple harmonically with frequency $n/2\\\\,Hz$at that point only", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Medium particles, at that point, are vibrating with frequency $n\\\\,Hz$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "At that, zero intensity is observed $2 n$ times per second", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "None of the above", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Beats are observed when intensity at a point varies with time and beat frequency is equal to the number of times the intensity reaches maximum per second.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(39268,'physics',30,1362,3269,'How does the time period of pendulum vary with length','[{"text": "${L^2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\sqrt{L}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$2 L$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{1}{\\\\sqrt{L}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Time period $T=2 \\pi \\sqrt{\\dfrac{L}{g}}$<br>$ \\Rightarrow T \\propto \\sqrt L $',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(39269,'physics',3,311,573,'Velocity of a body is given in the graph. Find the  average speed and average velocity of the body from \\[t = 0 \\,s\\] to \\[12 \\,s\\]<div class="matching-question"></div>','[{"text": "$\\\\frac{8}{3}m/s,\\\\,0$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{4}{3}m/s,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\frac{8}{3}m/s$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{{16}}{3}m/s,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\frac{8}{3}m/s$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$0,\\\\,\\\\frac{{16}}{3}m/s\\\\,$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'From $t = 0s\\,{\\rm{to}}\\,6s$ the body is travelling in one direction.<br>From $t = 0s\\,{\\rm{to}}\\,12s$ the body changes its direction at $t = 6s$<br>${\\rm{Distance}} = \\left[ {\\frac{1}{2} \\times 2 \\times 4 + 2 \\times 4 + \\frac{1}{2} \\times 2 \\times 4} \\right]$<br>$ + \\left[ {\\frac{1}{2} \\times 2 \\times 4 + 2 \\times 4 + \\frac{1}{2} \\times 2 \\times 4} \\right] = 32 \\, m$<br>${\\rm{Displacement}} = \\left[ {\\frac{1}{2} \\times 2 \\times 4 + 2 \\times 4 + \\frac{1}{2} \\times 2 \\times 4} \\right]$<br> $- \\left[ {\\frac {1}{2} \\times 2 \\times 4 + 2 \\times 4 + \\frac {1}{2} \\times 2 \\times 4} \\right] = 0$<br>${\\rm{Average}}\\,{\\rm{velocity}} = {\\rm{ }}\\frac{{{\\rm{Displacement}}}}{{{\\rm{time}}}} = \\frac{0}{{12 \\, s}} = 0$<br>${\\rm{Average  \\,  speed}} = \\frac{{{\\rm{Distance}}}}{{{\\rm{time}}}} = \\frac{{32 \\, m}}{{12 \\, s}} = \\frac{8}{3} \\, m/s$',NULL,'Medium','Graph/Figure',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/3_39269_question_1.png'),
(39271,'physics',31,1348,3245,'Two tuning forks, $A$ and $B$, produce notes of frequencies $256\\,Hz$ and $262\\,Hz$. An unknown note sounded with A produces certain beats. When the same note is sounded with $B$, the beat frequency gets doubled. The unknown frequency is','[{"text": "$250\\\\,Hz$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$252\\\\,Hz$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$254\\\\,Hz$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$256\\\\,Hz$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'$262-f=2(256-f)$<br>$f=250 H z$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(39272,'physics',30,1362,3269,'A pendulum has time period $T$ in air. When it is made to oscillate in water, it acquired a time period $T^{\\prime}=\\sqrt{2} T$. The density of the pendulum bob is equal to (density of water $=1$ )','[{"text": "$2 \\\\sqrt{2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\sqrt{2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "None of these", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'The effective acceleration of a bob in water<br>$=g^{\\prime}=g\\left(1-\\dfrac{\\sigma}{\\rho}\\right)$ where $\\sigma$ and $\\rho$ are the<br>density of water and the bob respectively. Since the period of oscillation of the bob in air and water are given as<br>$T=2 \\pi \\sqrt{\\dfrac{l}{g}} \\text { and } T^{\\prime}=2 \\pi \\sqrt{\\dfrac{l}{g^{\\prime}}}$<br>$\\therefore \\dfrac{T}{T^{\\prime}}=\\sqrt{\\dfrac{g^{\\prime}}{g}}=\\sqrt{\\dfrac{g(1-\\sigma / \\rho)}{g}}=\\sqrt{1-\\dfrac{\\sigma}{\\rho}}=\\sqrt{1-\\dfrac{1}{\\rho}}$<br>Putting $\\dfrac{T}{T^{\\prime}}=\\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}$. We obtain, $\\dfrac{1}{2}=1-\\dfrac{1}{\\rho} \\Rightarrow \\rho=2$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(39273,'physics',3,314,579,'A bird flies to and fro between two cars which move with velocities ${v_1} = 20\\,\\,m/s\\,{\\rm{and}}\\,{v_2} = 30\\,\\,m/s$. If the speed of the bird is ${v_3} = 10\\,m/s$ and the initial distance of separation between them is 2 $km$, find the total distance covered by the bird till the cars meet.','[{"text": "$2000\\\\,m$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1000\\\\,m$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$400\\\\,m$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$200\\\\,m$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Time to meet the cars: $t = \\frac{d}{{{v_1} + {v_2}}}$<br>Distance travelled by bird in this time<br>$s = {v_3}t = \\frac{{{v_3}d}}{{{v_1} + {v_2}}} = \\frac{{10 \\times 2000}}{{\\left( {20 + 30} \\right)}} = 400 \\, m$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/3_39273_question_1.png'),
(39275,'physics',31,1348,3245,'Two tuning forks of frequency $250\\,Hz$ and $256\\,Hz$ produce beats. If maximum of intensity is observed just now, after how much time the minimum is observed at the same place','[{"text": "$\\\\dfrac{1}{18} \\\\sec$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{1}{4} \\\\sec$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{1}{8} \\\\mathrm{sec}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{1}{12} \\\\mathrm{sec}$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'The time interval between two successive maximas is equal to $\\dfrac{1}{6} s$<br>For every $\\dfrac{1}{12}$ th sec<br>The minimum will be observed.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(39276,'physics',30,1362,3269,'If the length of a pendulum is made 9 times and mass of the bob is made 4 times then the value of time period becomes:','[{"text": "$(3 / 2) T$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$3\\\\,T$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$2\\\\,T$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$4\\\\,T$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'$T=2 \\pi \\sqrt{\\dfrac{l}{g}} \\Rightarrow T \\alpha \\sqrt{l}$, hence if $l$ made 9 times $T$ becomes 3 times. Also time period of simple pendulum does not depend on the mass of the bob.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(39277,'physics',3,314,579,'Two cars $A$ and $B$ are moving as shown in figure. Find their time of meeting.','[{"text": "$7s$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2s$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$4s$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$5s$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Method – 1<br>When the two cars meet each other<br>$\\left| {{S_A}} \\right| + \\left| {{S_B}} \\right| = 75$<br>$10 \\, t + 5 \\, t = 75$<br>$t = 5 \\, \\sec$.<br>Method-2<br>(Relative frame method)<br>We can solve with respect to any car, we do it with respect to car $B$<br>${\\overline v _{_{AB}}} = {\\overline v _{_A}} - {\\overline v _{_B}} = 10\\hat i - \\left( { - 5\\hat i} \\right) = 15\\hat i$<br>Here car $B$ is at rest and car $A$ is moving with $15 \\, m/s$ w.r.t. $B$<br>We can transfer the picture into relative frame as shown  in the figure<br>${s_r} = {u_r}t + \\frac{1}{2}{a_r}{t^2}({a_r} = 0)$<br>$75 = 15t \\Rightarrow t = 5\\sec .$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/3_39277_question_1.png'),
(39279,'physics',31,1348,3245,'Two open pipes of length $50\\,cm$ and $51\\,cm$ produce 6 beats when sounded together, find the speed of sound.','[{"text": "$316\\\\,m{s^{ - 1}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$330\\\\,m{s^{ - 1}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$360\\\\,m{s^{ - 1}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$306\\\\,m{s^{ - 1}}$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'$f_{1}-f_{2}=6$ or $\\dfrac{v}{2 l_{1}}-\\dfrac{v}{2 l_{2}}=6$<br>$\\frac{v}{{2(0.5)}} - \\frac{v}{{2(0.51)}} = 6{\\text{ or }}v = 306\\,m{s^{ - 1}}.$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(39280,'physics',30,1362,3269,'A simple pendulum has a time period $T$ in vacuum. Its time period when it is completely immersed in a liquid of density one-eighth of the density of material of the bob is :','[{"text": "$\\\\sqrt{\\\\dfrac{7}{8}} T$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\sqrt{\\\\dfrac{5}{8}} T$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\sqrt{\\\\dfrac{3}{8}} T$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\sqrt{\\\\dfrac{8}{7}} T$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Time period in liquid is given as<br>$\\begin{aligned}& T^{\\prime}=\\sqrt{\\dfrac{\\rho_{s}}{\\rho_{s}-\\rho_{\\ell}}} T \\\\& T^{\\prime}=\\sqrt{\\dfrac{\\rho_{s}}{\\rho_{s}-\\dfrac{\\rho_{s}}{8}}} T \\\\& T^{\\prime}=\\sqrt{\\dfrac{8}{7} T}\\end{aligned}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(39281,'physics',3,314,579,'Two trains each of length 50 $m$ are running with constant speeds on parallel tracks. While moving in same direction one overtakes the other in 40 seconds and while moving in opposite direction one crosses the other in 20 seconds. The speeds of the trains will be','[{"text": "$2.25m/s\\\\,{\\\\rm{and}}\\\\,2.75\\\\,m/s$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2.15m/s\\\\,{\\\\rm{and}}\\\\,2.85\\\\,m/s$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$3.75m/s\\\\,{\\\\rm{and}}\\\\,1.25\\\\,m/s$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$4.25m/s\\\\,{\\\\rm{and}}\\\\,0.75\\\\,m/s$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'We use the concept of relative motion<br>Let ${v_1}\\,\\& \\,{v_2}$ are velocities of the two trains<br>We know that the displacement equation in relative frame is <br>${S_r} = {u_r}t + \\frac{1}{2}{a_r}{t^2}\\left( {{a_r} = 0} \\right)$<br>${S_r} - $relative displacement, <br>When moving in opposite direction<br>$100 = \\left( {{v_1} + {v_2}} \\right)20\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,({\\rm{1}})$<br>When moving in same direction <br>$100 = \\left( {{v_1} - {v_2}} \\right)40\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,({\\rm{2}})$<br>  ${u_r} - $ relative velocity,  ${a_r} - $ relative acceleration<br>By solving (1) & (2) we get<br>${v_1} = 15/4\\,m/s\\quad {v_2} = 5/4\\,m/s$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(39283,'physics',31,1348,3245,'Two identical flutes produce fundamental notes of frequency $300\\,Hz$ at $27^\\circ C$. If the temperature of air in one flute is increased to $31^\\circ C$, the number of the beats heard per second will be','[{"text": "2", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "1", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "4", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "3", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Velocity of sound increases if the temperature increases. So with $v=n \\lambda$, if $v$ increases $n$ will increse at $27^\\circ C,{v_1} = n\\lambda ,{\\text{at }}31^\\circ C,{v_2} = (n + x)\\lambda $<br>Now using $v \\propto \\sqrt{T} \\quad\\left(\\because v=\\sqrt{\\dfrac{\\gamma R T}{M}}\\right)$<br>$\\Rightarrow \\dfrac{300+x}{300}=\\sqrt{\\dfrac{(273+31)}{(273+27)}}=\\sqrt{\\dfrac{304}{300}}=\\sqrt{\\dfrac{300+4}{300}}$<br>$\\Rightarrow 1+\\dfrac{x}{300}=\\left(1+\\dfrac{4}{300}\\right)^{1 / 2}=\\left(1+\\dfrac{1}{2} \\times \\dfrac{4}{300}\\right) \\Rightarrow x=2$. <br>$\\left[\\because(1+x)^{n}=1+n x\\right]$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(39284,'physics',30,1362,3269,'In a seconds pendulum, mass of bob is $30\\,g$. If it is replaced by $90\\,g$ mass. Then its time period will be:-','[{"text": "$2\\\\,\\\\sec $", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$1\\\\,\\\\sec $", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$3\\\\,\\\\sec $", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$4\\\\,\\\\sec $", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Time period of a pendulum does not depend on mass. Time period of seconds pendulum is $2\\,s$.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(39285,'physics',26,1133,2710,'A cube of ice floats partly in water partly in kerosene oil. The ratio of volume of ice immersed in water to that in kerosene oil (specific gravity of kerosene oil $=0.8$, specific gravity of ice $=0.9$ )','[{"text": "$1: 1$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$5: 4$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$9: 10$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$8: 9$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Weight of ice is balanced by buoyant force due to water and kerosene oil.<br>$\\therefore m g=\\rho_{k} V_{k} g+\\rho_{w} V_{w} g$<br>$\\rho_{\\mathrm{ice}} V_{\\mathrm{ice}} g=\\rho_{k} V_{k} g+\\rho_{w} V_{w} g$<br>$\\dfrac{\\rho_{\\text {ice }}}{\\rho_{w}} V_{\\text {ice }}=\\dfrac{\\rho_{k}}{\\rho_{w}} V_{k}+V_{w}$<br>$\\Rightarrow 0.9 V_{\\text {ice }}=0.8 V_{k}+V_{w}$<br>${({\\rm{Volume}})_{{\\rm{ice}}}} = {({\\rm{Volume}})_{{\\rm{water}}}} + {({\\rm{Volume}})_{{\\rm{kerosene}}}}$<br>$\\therefore \\quad 0.9\\left(V_{w}+V_{k}\\right)=0.8 V_{k}+V_{w}$<br>$0.1 V_{w}=0.1 V_{k} \\Rightarrow \\dfrac{V_{w}}{V_{k}}=\\dfrac{1}{1}$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',2024,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/26_39285_question_1.png'),
(39286,'physics',3,311,573,'For a body moving with uniform acceleration ‘$a$’, initial and final velocities in a time interval ‘$t$’ are $u$ and $v$ respectively. The average velocity in the time interval ‘$t$’ is','[{"text": "$u + at$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$u - \\\\frac{{at}}{2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$u - at$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$u + \\\\frac{{at}}{2}$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'The velocity of the body is $v = u + at$<br>As the velocity function is given<br>The average velocity is <br>$\\left\\langle v \\right\\rangle  = \\frac{{\\int\\limits_0^{t} vdt}}{t - 0} = \\frac{1}{t}\\int\\limits_0^{t} {\\left( {u + at} \\right)dt} $<br>$ = \\frac{1}{t} \\left[ {\\int\\limits_0^{t} udt + a}\\int\\limits_0^{t} tdt \\right] = u + \\frac{{at}}{2}$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(39287,'physics',31,1348,3245,'Beat phenomenon is physically meaningful only, if','[{"text": "$\\\\left|\\\\omega_{1}-\\\\omega_{2}\\\\right|>>\\\\left|\\\\omega_{1}+\\\\omega_{2}\\\\right|$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\left|\\\\omega_{1}-\\\\omega_{2}\\\\right| <  < \\\\left|\\\\omega_{1}+\\\\omega_{2}\\\\right|$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{\\\\omega_{1}}{\\\\omega_{2}} < \\\\left|\\\\omega_{1}-\\\\omega_{2}\\\\right|$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\left|\\\\omega_{1}+\\\\omega_{2}\\\\right|>>\\\\dfrac{\\\\omega_{1}}{\\\\omega_{2}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,NULL,NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(39288,'physics',30,1362,3269,'If a simple pendulum of length $\\ell$ has maximum angular displacment $\\theta$, then the maximum kinetic energy of the bob of mass $m$ is','[{"text": "$\\\\left(\\\\dfrac{1}{2}\\\\right)\\\\left(\\\\dfrac{m g}{\\\\ell}\\\\right)$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\left(\\\\dfrac{1}{2}\\\\right) m \\\\sqrt{\\\\dfrac{\\\\ell}{g}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$(1 / 2) m g \\\\ell \\\\sin \\\\theta$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$m g \\\\ell(1-\\\\cos \\\\theta)$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'As T.E of the pendulum is conserved<br>$K \\cdot E_{\\text {max }}=P \\cdot E_{\\text {max }}=m g \\ell(1-\\cos \\theta)$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(39289,'physics',3,311,573,'Between two stations, a train accelerates from rest uniformly at first, then moves with constant velocity, and finally retards uniformly to come to rest. If the ratio of time taken is $1:8:1$ and the maximum speed attained be $60\\,km/h$, then what is the average speed over the whole journey?','[{"text": "$48\\\\,km/h$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$52\\\\,km/h$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$54\\\\,km/h$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$56\\\\,km/h$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Given that the ratio of the times is<br>${t_1}:{t_2}:{t_3} = 1:8:1$<br>Let ${t_1} = k,{t_2} = 8k,{t_3} = k,$ $a$ is the acceleration and retardation in first and third parts.<br>${s_1} = \\frac{1}{2}a{t_1}^2 = \\frac{{a{k^2}}}{2},{v_1} = a{t_1} = ak$<br>${s_2} = {v_1}{t_2} = 8a{k^2}$<br>${s_3} = {v_2}{t_3} - \\frac{1}{2}a{t_3}^2({v_2} = {v_1} = ak)$<br>${s_3} = \\frac{{a{k^2}}}{2}$<br>$\\langle v\\rangle  = \\frac{{{s_1} + {s_2} + {s_3}}}{{10k}} = \\frac{{9a{k^2}}}{{10k}} = \\frac{{9ak}}{{10}} = 54\\,km/h$<br>(Given that ${v_1} = ak = 60\\,km/h$)',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(39290,'physics',26,1133,2710,'A sphere of relative density $\\sigma$ and diameter $D$ has concentric cavity of diameter $d$. The ratio of $\\dfrac{D}{d}$, if it just floats on water in a tank is','[{"text": "$\\\\left(\\\\dfrac{\\\\sigma-1}{\\\\sigma}\\\\right)^{1 / 3}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\left(\\\\dfrac{\\\\sigma}{\\\\sigma-1}\\\\right)^{1 / 3}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\left(\\\\dfrac{\\\\sigma+1}{\\\\sigma-1}\\\\right)^{1 / 3}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\left(\\\\dfrac{\\\\sigma-2}{\\\\sigma+2}\\\\right)^{1 / 3}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'<img src=\'/img/data/physics/26_39290_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' /> <br>Relative density of sphere $=\\sigma$<br>Diameter $=D$, Cavity diameter $=d$<br>Weight of sphere $(W)=\\dfrac{4}{3} \\pi \\times \\dfrac{1}{8}\\left(D^{3}-d^{3}\\right) \\sigma g$<br>Buoyant force, $F_{B}=\\dfrac{4}{3} \\pi \\dfrac{D^{3}}{8} \\times 1 \\times g$<br>For just floating, Weight = buoyant force<br>$\\therefore \\dfrac{4}{3} \\pi \\times \\dfrac{1}{8}\\left(D^{3}-d^{3}\\right) \\sigma g=\\dfrac{4}{3} \\pi \\dfrac{D^{3}}{8} \\times 1 \\times g$<br>or $\\left(D^{3}-d^{3}\\right) \\sigma=D^{3}$<br>or $1-\\dfrac{d^{3}}{D^{3}}=\\dfrac{1}{\\sigma} \\Rightarrow\\left(\\dfrac{D}{d}\\right)^{3}=\\left(\\dfrac{\\sigma}{\\sigma-1}\\right)$<br>$\\dfrac{D}{d}=\\left(\\dfrac{\\sigma}{\\sigma-1}\\right)^{1 / 3}$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',2024,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(39291,'physics',31,1349,3246,'Which of the following has high pitch in their sound','[{"text": "Mosquito", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Lion", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Woman", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Man", "isCorrect": false}]',0,NULL,NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(39292,'physics',30,1362,3269,'What should be the displacement of a simple pendulum whose amplitude is $A$, at which potential energy is $\\dfrac{1}{4}$ th of the total energy?','[{"text": "$\\\\frac{A}{{\\\\sqrt 2 }}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{A}{2}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{A}{4}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{A}{{2\\\\sqrt 2 }}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'$\\dfrac{1}{2} K x^{2}=\\dfrac{1}{4} \\times \\dfrac{1}{2} K A^{2}$<br>$x^{2}=\\dfrac{A^{2}}{4} \\Rightarrow x=\\dfrac{A}{2}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(39294,'physics',3,314,579,'Two cars $A$ and $B$ are moving with same speed of 45 $km/h$ along same direction. If a third car $C$ coming from the opposite direction with speed of 36 $km/h$ meets two cars in an interval of 5 minutes, the distance of separation of two cars $A$ and $B$ should be (in $km$)','[{"text": "$8.35$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$6.75$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$4.75$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$7.25$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Distance b/w the cars $A$ and $B$ remains constant. Let the distance ‘$x$’ <br>Velocity of $C$ w.r.t. $A$ and $B$ is $V$ = 45 + 36 = 81 $km/h$<br>Distance $ = 81 \\times \\frac{5}{{60}} = 6.75 \\, Km.$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL);
INSERT INTO `qb_questions` (`id`, `subject_id`, `chapter_id`, `topic_id`, `subtopic_id`, `question_html`, `options`, `correct_index`, `explanation`, `explanation_image_url`, `difficulty`, `qtype`, `year`, `tag`, `created_at`, `question_image_url`) VALUES
(39295,'physics',31,1349,3246,'If $T$ is the reverberation time of an auditorium of volume $V$ then','[{"text": "$T \\\\propto \\\\dfrac{1}{V^{2}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$T \\\\propto \\\\dfrac{1}{V}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$T \\\\propto V$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$T \\\\propto V^{2}$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Reverberation time $T \\propto V$.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(39296,'physics',30,1362,3270,'One end of an ideal spring of force constant $k$ is fixed at ceiling and other end is loaded with a block of mass $m$. If equilibrium elongation of the spring is $x$, then time period of vibration of the spring mass system is ( $g = $ acceleration due to gravity)','[{"text": "$2 \\\\pi \\\\sqrt{\\\\dfrac{m}{k}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2 \\\\pi \\\\sqrt{\\\\dfrac{x}{g}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2 \\\\pi \\\\sqrt{\\\\dfrac{m x}{k g}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both (1) & (2)", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Given $m g=k x$<br>$\\therefore \\dfrac{m}{k}=\\dfrac{x}{g}$<br>Now, $T=2 \\pi \\sqrt{\\dfrac{m}{k}}=2 \\pi \\sqrt{\\dfrac{x}{g}}$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(39297,'physics',3,314,579,'An elevator, in which a man is standing, is moving upward with a constant acceleration of $2\\,m/{s^2}.$ At some instant when speed of elevator is 10 $m/s$, the man drops a coin from a height of 1.5 $m$. Find the time taken by the coin to reach the floor.','[{"text": "$\\\\frac{1}{{\\\\sqrt 3 }}\\\\sec $", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1\\\\sec $", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{1}{{\\\\sqrt 2 }}\\\\sec $", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{1}{2}\\\\sec $", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'${d_{rel}} = \\frac{1}{2}{a_{rel}}{t^2} \\Rightarrow 1.5 = \\frac{1}{2}(10 + 2){t^2}$<br>$1.5 = \\frac{1}{2} \\times 12 \\times {t^2} \\Rightarrow t = \\frac{1}{2} \\, \\sec $',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(39299,'physics',31,1349,3246,'A two-fold increase in intensity of a wave implies an increases of (given: $\\log _{10} 2=0.301$ )','[{"text": "$10\\\\,dB$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2\\\\,dB$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$0.5\\\\,dB$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$3.01\\\\,dB$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'$L_{1}=10 \\log _{10} \\dfrac{I}{I_{0}}$ decibel<br>${L_2} = 10\\log \\frac{{2I}}{{{I_o}}} \\Rightarrow \\Delta L = 3.01\\,dB$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(39300,'physics',30,1362,3270,'A mass of $250\\,g$ hangs on a spring and oscillates vertically with a period of $1.1\\,s$. To double the period, what mass must be added to the $250\\,g$ ? (Ignore the mass of the spring)','[{"text": "$250\\\\,g$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$450\\\\,g$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$750\\\\,g$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$550\\\\,g$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'As, $T \\propto \\sqrt m  \\Rightarrow 1.1 \\propto \\sqrt {250} $<br>and $\\Rightarrow 2.2 \\propto \\sqrt{250+x}$ <br>$\\therefore \\frac{1}{2} = \\sqrt {\\frac{{250}}{{250 + x}}}  \\Rightarrow x = 750\\,g$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(39302,'physics',3,314,579,'Two buses $A$ and $B$ starts from the same position in the same direction. Bus $A$ moves with uniform speed of 40 $m/s$ and starts from rest with uniform acceleration of $2 \\,m/{s^2}.$ Bus $B$ starts at $t$ = 0 and bus $A$ starts at $t$ = $10 \\,s$. After buses starts moving then the time during the journey in which bus $B$ was ahead of bus $A$ is','[{"text": "$5\\\\sqrt 2 \\\\,s$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$10\\\\sqrt 2 \\\\,s$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$10\\\\,s$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$20\\\\sqrt 2 \\\\,s$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'In the time $10 \\, s$ the displacement of the $B$ is<br> $\\left( {\\frac{1}{2} \\times 2 \\times {{10}^2}} \\right)$ = $100 \\, m$<br> and its velocity is $20 \\, m/s$. By the time $A$ starts its motion $B$ is ahead of $A$ by $100 \\, m$. The time $\\Delta t$ after which $A$ and $B$ meet is <br>${v_A}\\Delta t = {v_B}\\Delta t + \\frac{1}{2}2\\Delta {t^2} + 100 \\, m$<br>${v_B} = 20 \\, m/s,\\,{v_A} = 40 \\, m/s$<br>$\\Delta {t^2} - 20\\Delta t + 100 = 0$<br>$\\Delta t = 10\\,s$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(39303,'physics',31,1349,3246,'A sound absorber attenuates the sound level by $20\\,dB$. The intensity decreases by a factor of','[{"text": "1000", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "10000", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "10", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "100", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Let ${I_1}$ & ${I_2}$ are initial and final intensities<br>$\\beta_{1}=10 \\log \\left(\\dfrac{I_{1}}{I_{0}}\\right)$<br>Later, $\\beta_{2}=10 \\log \\left(\\dfrac{I_{2}}{I_{0}}\\right)$<br>Given, $\\beta_{1}-\\beta_{2}=20$<br>$\\therefore 20 = 10\\log \\left( {\\frac{{{I_1}}}{{{I_2}}}} \\right)$<br>${I_1} = 100\\,{I_2}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(39304,'physics',26,1136,2718,'Read the Statement - A and Statement - B carefully to mark the correct options given below.<div class="matching-question"><div class="column-left">When speed of liquid is zero everywhere, pressure difference at any two points depends on equation<br>$P_{1}-P_{2}=\\rho g\\left(h_{2}-h_{1}\\right)$.</div><div class="column-right">In venturi tube shown $2 g h=v_{1}^{2}-v_{2}^{2}$<br>https://res.cloudinary.com/doe6oiv2h/image/upload/v1764957361/preptracker/questions/q_39304_col2_1764957361362.png</div></div>','[{"text": "Statement A is correct but Statement B is incorrect.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Statement A is incorrect but Statement B is correct.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Statements are correct.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Statements are incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'According to Bernoulli\'s theorem,<br>$P_{1}+\\rho g h_{1}+\\dfrac{1}{2} \\rho v_{1}^{2}=P_{2}+\\rho g h_{2}+\\dfrac{1}{2} \\rho v_{2}^{2}$<br>As per question $v_{1}=v_{2}=0$<br>So, $P_{1}-P_{2}=\\rho g\\left(h_{2}-h_{1}\\right)$<br>(Statement - A is correct)<br>For venturimeter : $P_{1}-P_{2}=\\rho g h$<br>and $P_{1}-P_{2}=\\dfrac{1}{2} \\rho v_{2}^{2}-\\dfrac{1}{2} \\rho v_{1}^{2}=\\rho g h$<br>$2 g h=v_{2}^{2}-v_{1}^{2} \\quad($ Statement -B is incorrect)<br>So, correct option is (1).',NULL,'Hard','MCQ type-2',2024,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(39305,'physics',30,1362,3270,'A block of mass $M$ is hanged from the ceiling by a spring with a spring constant $k$. It executes SHM with a period $P$. If the mass of the ball is increased by four times, the new period will be:','[{"text": "$\\\\frac{P}{4}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$4P$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$P$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2P$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'The time period of the block would be given by,<br>$\\begin{aligned}& p=2 \\pi \\sqrt{\\dfrac{M}{K}} \\\\& p^{\\prime}=2 \\pi \\sqrt{\\dfrac{4 M}{K}}=2 P\\end{aligned}$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(39306,'physics',3,313,577,'From a tower of height $H$, a particle is thrown vertically  upwards with a  speed $u$. The time taken by the particle, to hit the ground, is $n$ times that is taken by it to reach the highest point of its path. The relation between $H,u\\,{\\rm{and}}\\,n\\,{\\rm{is}}\\,:$','[{"text": "$2gH = n{u^2}(n - 2)$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$gH = (n - 2){u^2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2gH = {n^2}{u^2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$gH = {(n - 2)^2}{u^2}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Time to reach the maximum height<br>${t_1} = \\frac{u}{g}$<br>If ${t_2}$ be the time taken to hit the ground <br>$ - H = u{t_2} - \\frac{1}{2}gt_2^2$<br>But ${t_2} = n{t_1}$(given)<br>$ \\Rightarrow  - H = \\frac{{n{u^2}}}{g} - \\frac{1}{2}g\\frac{{{n^2}{u^2}}}{{{g^2}}}$<br>$ \\Rightarrow 2gH = n{u^2}(n - 2)$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(39307,'physics',31,1349,3246,'The sound intensity is $0.008\\,W/{m^2}$ at a distance of $10\\,m$ from an isotropic point source of sound. The power of the source is','[{"text": "0.8 $watt$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "2.5 $watt$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "10 $watt$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "8 $watt$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'$\\dfrac{P}{4 \\pi r^{2}}=I$<br>$ \\Rightarrow P = I \\cdot 4\\pi {r^2}$<br>$ = \\left( {0.008\\,W/{m^2}} \\right)\\left( {4 \\cdot \\pi  \\cdot {{10}^2}} \\right) = 10\\,W$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(39308,'physics',3,313,577,'A person throwing two balls in the air one after the other. He throws the second ball when the first ball is at the highest point. If he is throwing the balls every second, how high do they rise?','[{"text": "$3.75\\\\,m$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$5\\\\,m$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$1.25\\\\,m$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2.50\\\\,m$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Time of ascent <br>$ = 1s \\Rightarrow \\frac{u}{g} = 1 \\Rightarrow u = 10\\,m{s^{ - 1}}$<br>${\\rm{height }}(h) = \\frac{{{u^2}}}{{2g}} = \\frac{{{{10}^2}}}{{2 \\times 10}} = 5 \\, m$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(39309,'physics',26,1136,2715,'Correct Bernoulli\'s equation is (symbols have their usual meaning)','[{"text": "$P+\\\\rho g h+\\\\dfrac{1}{2} \\\\rho v^{2}=$ constant", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$P+\\\\dfrac{1}{2} \\\\rho g h+\\\\dfrac{1}{2} \\\\rho v^{2}=$ constant", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$P+\\\\rho g h+\\\\rho v^{2}=$ constant", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$P+m g h+\\\\dfrac{1}{2} m v^{2}=$ constant", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'According to Bernoulli\'s theorem,<br>$P+\\rho g h+\\dfrac{1}{2} \\rho v^{2}=$ constant.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2024,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(39310,'physics',30,1362,3271,'As shown in figure a simple harmonic motion oscillator having identical four springs has time period','[{"text": "$T=2 \\\\pi \\\\sqrt{\\\\dfrac{m}{2 k}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$T=2 \\\\pi \\\\sqrt{\\\\dfrac{m}{4 k}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$T=2 \\\\pi \\\\sqrt{\\\\dfrac{m}{8 k}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$T=2 \\\\pi \\\\sqrt{\\\\dfrac{m}{k}}$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'The upper two springs are in parallel their equival ent spring constant is $2 K$. Similarly the equivalent spring constant for two lower springs is $2\\,K$. The equivalent spring constant of the system is<br>$\\begin{aligned}&amp; \\dfrac{1}{k_{e q}}=\\dfrac{1}{2 k}+\\dfrac{1}{2 k} \\Rightarrow k_{e q}=k \\\\&amp; T=2 \\pi \\sqrt{\\dfrac{m}{k}}\\end{aligned}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/30_39310_question_1.png'),
(39312,'physics',26,1137,2723,'A small ball of mass $m$ and density $\\rho$ is dropped in a viscous liquid of density $\\rho_{0}$. After sometime, the ball falls with constant velocity. The viscous force on the ball is','[{"text": "$m g\\\\left(1-\\\\rho \\\\rho_{0}\\\\right)$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$m g\\\\left(1-\\\\dfrac{\\\\rho_{0}}{\\\\rho}\\\\right)$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$m g\\\\left(1+\\\\dfrac{\\\\rho}{\\\\rho_{0}}\\\\right)$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$m g\\\\left(\\\\dfrac{\\\\rho_{0}}{\\\\rho}-1\\\\right)$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'The small ball is having mass $m$ and density $\\rho$. Let the mass of liquid displaced be $m^{\\prime}$.<br>Volume of ball is $V=\\dfrac{m}{\\rho}$  $\\quad \\quad (1)$<br>Volume of liquid displaced by ball,<br><img src=\'/img/data/physics/26_39312_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />  <br>$V^{\\prime}=\\dfrac{m^{\\prime}}{\\rho_{0}}$   $\\quad \\quad (2)$<br>Volume of ball = Volume of liquid displaced<br>$\\dfrac{m}{\\rho}=\\dfrac{m^{\\prime}}{\\rho_{0}}$<br>$m^{\\prime}=\\dfrac{m}{\\rho} \\rho_{0}$   $\\quad \\quad (3)$<br>Buoyant force, $F_{B}=\\rho_{0} V^{\\prime} g=m^{\\prime} g$<br>Viscous force on the ball is, $F_{V}=m g-m^{\\prime} g$.<br>$F_{V}=m g-\\left(\\dfrac{m}{\\rho} \\rho_{0}\\right) g=m g\\left(1-\\dfrac{\\rho_{0}}{\\rho}\\right)$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',2024,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(39313,'physics',30,1362,3271,'A body connected at the end of a spring executes SHM with a time period $T_{1}$, while the corresponding period for another spring is $T_{2}$. If the period of oscillation with the two springs in series is $T$, then','[{"text": "$T=T_{1}+T_{2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$T^{2}=T_{1}^{2}+T_{2}^{2}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{1}{T}=\\\\dfrac{1}{T_{1}}+\\\\dfrac{1}{T_{2}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{1}{T^{2}}=\\\\dfrac{1}{T_{1}^{2}}+\\\\dfrac{1}{T_{2}^{2}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'In series<br>$\\dfrac{1}{K}=\\dfrac{1}{K_{1}}+\\dfrac{1}{K_{2}} \\quad K=\\dfrac{4 \\pi^{2} m}{T^{2}}$<br>As<br>$K \\propto \\dfrac{1}{T^{2}} \\Rightarrow T^{2}=T_{1}^{2}+T_{2}^{2}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(39314,'physics',3,311,573,'The average velocity of a freely falling body $({\\rm{with}}\\,u = 0)$ is numerically equal to half of the  acceleration due to gravity. The velocity of the body as it reaches the ground is','[{"text": "$g$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{g}{2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{g}{{\\\\sqrt 2 }}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\sqrt 2 g$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'${\\rm{Avg}}{\\rm{.velocity}} = \\frac{{{\\rm{Total \\, distance}}}}{{{\\rm{total \\, time}}}} = \\frac{{\\frac{1}{2}g{t^2}}}{t} = \\frac{{gt}}{2}$<br>Given that $\\frac{{gt}}{2} = \\frac{g}{2} \\Rightarrow t = 1s$<br>Velocity after reaching ground $ = gt = g$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(39316,'physics',30,1362,3272,'A block of mass $M$ is performing SHM with amplitude A on a smooth horizontal surface. At the extreme position a small block of mass $m$ falls vertically and sticks to $M$. New amplitude of oscillation will be','[{"text": "$A$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$A \\\\dfrac{M+m}{m}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$A \\\\sqrt{\\\\dfrac{M}{M+m}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$A \\\\dfrac{M}{M+m}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'At extreme position velocity of $M$ will be zero. So amplitude remains same.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(39317,'physics',3,311,573,'The velocity of a body as a function of time is $v = 10 - 5t$. Find the average velocity of the body in the time interval from $0s\\,{\\rm{to}}\\,2s$.','[{"text": "$0$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$5\\\\,m/s$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$10\\\\,m/s$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$20\\\\,m/s$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Given that $v = 10 - 5t$<br>The average velocity is $\\left\\langle v \\right\\rangle  = \\frac{{\\int_0^2 {(10 - 5t)dt} }}{{2 - 0}}$<br>$\\left\\langle v \\right\\rangle  = \\frac{1}{2}\\left[ {\\int_0^2 {10\\,dt - 5\\int_0^2 {t\\,dt} )} } \\right] = 5 \\, m/s$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(39318,'physics',26,1137,2723,'A spherical ball of radius $1 \\times {10^{ - 4}}\\,m$ and density  ${10^5}\\,kg/{m^3}$  falls freely under gravity through a distance $h$ before entering a tank of water. If after entering in water the velocity of the ball does not change, then the value of $h$ is approximately (The coefficient of viscosity of water is  $9.8 \\times {10^{ - 6}}Ns/{m^2}{\\mkern 1mu} )$','[{"text": "$2249\\\\,m$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2518\\\\,m$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$2396\\\\,m$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2296\\\\,m$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'The terminal velocity is given by:<br>$v=\\dfrac{2}{9} r^{2} \\dfrac{(\\rho-\\sigma) g}{\\eta}$<br>$v = \\frac{2}{9} \\times \\frac{{{{\\left( {{{10}^{ - 4}}} \\right)}^2}\\left( {{{10}^5} - {{10}^3}} \\right) \\times 10}}{{9.8 \\times {{10}^{ - 6}}}} = 224.5\\,m/s$<br>Velocity of ball just before entering the water tank is<br>$v = \\sqrt {2gh} $<br>$ \\Rightarrow h = \\frac{{{v^2}}}{{2g}};h = \\frac{{{{(224.5)}^2}}}{{2 \\times 10}} = 2518\\,m$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2024,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(39320,'physics',3,313,577,'A stone thrown upwards with speed $u$ attains maximum height $h$. Another stone thrown upwards from the same point with speed $2u$ attains maximum height $H$. What is the relation between $h$ and $H$?','[{"text": "$3h = H$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2h = H\\\\,$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$5h = H$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$4h = H\\\\,$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Maximum height attained $\\alpha \\,{u^2}$<br>$\\frac{H}{h} = \\frac{{\\left( {2{u^2}} \\right)}}{{{u^2}}} = 4$<br>$H = 4\\,h$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(39321,'physics',26,1137,2723,'A small spherical ball of radius \' $r$ \' falling through a viscous medium of negligible density has terminal velocity \' $v$ \'. Another ball of the same mass but of radius $2 r$; falling through the same viscous medium will have terminal velocity.','[{"text": "$\\\\dfrac{v}{4}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{v}{2}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$2 v$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$4 v$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Terminal velocity of the ball through a viscous medium,<br>$v=\\dfrac{2}{9} \\times \\dfrac{g}{\\eta}(\\rho-\\sigma) r^{2}$<br>For given viscous medium, $\\sigma=0$<br>$v=\\dfrac{2}{9} \\times \\dfrac{g}{\\eta} \\rho r^{2} \\Rightarrow v=\\dfrac{2}{9} \\times \\dfrac{g}{\\eta} \\times \\dfrac{m}{\\dfrac{4}{3} \\pi r^{3}} \\times r^{2}$<br>i.e., $v \\propto \\dfrac{1}{r}$<br>For the $2^{\\text {nd }}$ ball $r^{\\prime} \\rightarrow 2 r$<br>So, $\\dfrac{v^{\\prime}}{v}=\\dfrac{r}{r^{\\prime}} ; \\dfrac{v^{\\prime}}{v}=\\dfrac{1}{2 r} \\times \\dfrac{r}{1}=\\dfrac{1}{2}$<br>$\\Rightarrow v^{\\prime}=\\dfrac{v}{2}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2024,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(39322,'physics',30,1361,3268,'The composition of two simple harmonic motions of equal periods at right angle to each others and with a phase difference of $\\pi$ results in the displacement of the particle along.','[{"text": "Circle", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Figure of Eight", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Straight line", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Ellipse", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'$x = A\\sin \\omega t\\,\\& $<br>$\\begin{gathered}y=\\sin \\left(\\omega+\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\right)=-A \\sin (\\omega t) \\\\\\Rightarrow \\dfrac{x}{y}=-1 \\quad \\Rightarrow x=-y \\quad \\text { (straight line) }\\end{gathered}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(39325,'physics',3,313,578,'The velocity $v$ of a particle is given by the equation $v = 6{t^2} - 6{t^3}$, where $v$ is in $m/\\sec $ and $t$ is time in seconds then :-','[{"text": "At, $t = \\\\frac{2}{3}$, velocity is minimum", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Minium velocity is 2 $m$/sec", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Minium velocity is zero", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "At, $t = 1s$, velocity is minimum", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Given $v = 6{t^2} - 6{t^3}$<br>$\\frac{{dv}}{{dt}} = 12t - 18{t^2}$<br>$ \\Rightarrow \\frac{{{d^2}v}}{{d{t^2}}} = 12 - 36t$<br>When $v$ is maxima or minima<br>$\\frac{{dv}}{{dt}} = 12t - 18{t^2} = 0 \\Rightarrow t = \\frac{2}{3}s,t = 0$<br>${\\left. {\\frac{{{d^2}v}}{{d{t^2}}}} \\right|_{t = 0}} = 12({\\rm{minima}})$<br>${\\left. {\\frac{{{d^2}v}}{{d{t^2}}}} \\right|_{t = \\frac{2}{3}s}} =  - 12{\\rm{(maxima)}}$<br>${v_{\\min }} = 6{(0)^2} - 6{(0)^3} = 0$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(39326,'physics',30,1361,3268,'The motion of a particle varies with time according to the relation<br>$y=a \\sin \\omega t+a \\sin (\\omega t+\\pi / 3)$','[{"text": "The motion is oscillatory but not SHM", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "The motion is SHM with amplitude $\\\\sqrt{2} a$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "The motion is SHM with amplitude $2 a$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "The motion is SHM with amplitude $\\\\sqrt{3} a$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'$A=\\sqrt{A_{1}^{2}+A_{2}^{2}+2 A_{1} A_{2} \\cos \\phi}$<br>$\\begin{aligned}& A=\\sqrt{a^{2}+a^{2}+2 a^{2} \\cos \\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{3}\\right)} \\\\& A=\\sqrt{3} a\\end{aligned}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(39328,'physics',3,313,577,'An object is dropped from a height $h$, then the distance travelled in times $t,2t,3t$ are in the ratio','[{"text": "$1:3:5$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1:9:5$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1:2:3$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1:4:9$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'${s_1} = \\frac{1}{2}g{t^2}$<br>${s_2} = \\frac{1}{2}g{(2t)^2} = 4{s_1}$<br>${s_3} = \\frac{1}{2}g{(3t)^2} = 9{s_1}$<br>${s_1}:{s_2}:{s_3} = {s_1}:4{s_1}:9{s_1} = 1:4:9$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(39329,'physics',26,1138,2731,'Read the Statement - A and Statement - B carefully to mark the correct options given below.<div class="matching-question"><div class="column-left">The contact angle between a solid and a liquid is a property of the material of the solid and liquid as well.</div><div class="column-right">The rise of a liquid in a capillary tube does not depend on the inner radius of the tube.</div></div>','[{"text": "Statement A is correct but statement B is incorrect.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Statement A is incorrect but statement B is correct.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both statements are correct .", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both statements are incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Statement  A  is correct as the contact angle depends on the interaction between solid and liquid.<br>From the ascent formula, rise in capillary,<br>$h=\\dfrac{2 T}{\\rho g r} \\cos \\theta$<br>Thus, rise of a liquid in a capillary tube depends on surface tension $(T)$, density of liquid $(\\rho)$, contact angle $(\\theta)$ and inner radius of capillary $(r)$.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ type-2',2024,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(39330,'physics',30,1359,3263,'Which of the following expression does not represent SHM?','[{"text": "$A \\\\sin 2 \\\\omega t$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$A \\\\sin \\\\omega t+B \\\\cos 2 \\\\omega t$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$A \\\\sin \\\\omega t+B \\\\cos \\\\omega t$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$A \\\\sin ^{2} \\\\omega t$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'For SHM, $\\dfrac{d^{2} y}{d t^{2}} \\propto-y$<br>For the equation $y = $ $A\\sin \\omega t + B\\cos 2\\omega t$<br>$\\frac{{{d^2}y}}{{d{t^2}}}$ is not proportional to $(-y)$.',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(39332,'physics',26,1138,2731,'Read the Statement - A and Statement - B carefully to mark the correct options given below.<div class="matching-question"><div class="column-left">When a capillary tube is dipped into a liquid, the liquid neither rises nor falls in the capillary. The contact angle may be $0^{\\circ}$.</div><div class="column-right">The contact angle between a solid and a liquid is a property of the material of the solid and liquid as well.</div></div>','[{"text": "Statement A is correct but statement B is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Statement A is incorrect and statement B is correct.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both statements are correct.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both statements are incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Rise or fall of capillary height,<br>$h=\\dfrac{2 T}{\\rho g r} \\cos \\theta$<br>If $\\theta < 90^{\\circ}, h$ is $+v e \\Rightarrow$ rise of liquid<br>If $\\theta>90^{\\circ}, h$ is $-v e \\Rightarrow$ fall of liquid<br>For $h$ be zero, contact angle $\\theta=90^{\\circ}$.<br>(Statement A is incorrect.)<br>Contact angle between a solid and liquid depends upon the adhesive and cohesive forces between the two media. These forces are the properties of the material in contact $i.\\,e.,$ solid and  liquid, which decides the contact angle.<br>(Statement B is correct.)<br>So, option (2) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ type-2',2024,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(39333,'physics',3,313,577,'A body is projected downward with speed $20\\,m/s$ from the top of a tower of height 50 $m$. Find the time at which it hits the ground.','[{"text": "$\\\\sqrt {14}  - 2\\\\sec $", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\sqrt {10}  - 2\\\\sec $", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\sqrt {14}  + 2\\\\sec $", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\sqrt {10}  + 2\\\\sec $", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Method-1 <br>Consider the origin at the top of the tower and downward is positive and upward is negative<br>${S_0} = 0$<br>$u = 20\\,m/s$  (Going towards $\\left(  +  \\right)ve$  direction) <br>$a = g$ (Accelerated motion)<br>$s = 20t + \\frac{1}{2}g{t^2}$<br>When it hits the ground <br>$s = 50\\,m$<br>$50 = 20t + \\frac{1}{2}10{t^2}$<br>${t^2} + 4t - 10 = 0$<br>$t = \\frac{{ - 4 \\pm \\sqrt {56} }}{2} = \\sqrt {14}  - 2$<br>Method 2<br>If we do the problem graphically we can avoid  quadratic equation. When big numbers are  involved solving quadratic equation is difficult.<br>The graph between $v$ and $t$ is a straight line <br>since  $a = \\frac{{dv}}{{dt}} = g = {\\rm{constant}}$<br>The velocity when the body touches the ground is ${\\rm{v}}_{\\max }^2 = {20^2} + 2 \\times 10 \\times 50 \\Rightarrow {v_{\\max }} = 10\\sqrt {14} $<br>Total distance travelled = Area of the graph<br>$50 = 20 \\times t + \\frac{1}{2} \\times t \\times \\left( {10\\sqrt {14}  - 20} \\right)$<br>$ \\Rightarrow t = \\frac{{10}}{{2 + \\sqrt {14} }}$<br>on rationalising we get $t = \\sqrt {14}  - 2$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(39334,'physics',30,1359,3265,'The displacement of particle varies with time as $x = 12\\sin \\omega t - 16{\\sin ^3}\\omega t\\,(in{\\rm{ }}cm)$. If its motion is S.H.M., then its maximum acceleration is','[{"text": "$36\\\\,{\\\\omega ^2}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$12\\\\,{\\\\omega ^2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\sqrt {192} \\\\,{\\\\omega ^2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$133\\\\,{\\\\omega ^2}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'$x=12 \\sin \\omega t-16 \\sin ^{3} \\omega t=4\\left[3 \\sin \\omega t-4 \\sin ^{3} \\omega t\\right]$<br>$=4[\\sin 3 \\omega t]\\left(\\right.$ By using $\\left.\\sin 3 \\theta=3 \\sin \\theta-4 \\sin ^{3} \\theta\\right)$<br>$\\therefore$ Maximum acceleration<br>$A_{\\max }=(3 \\omega)^{2} \\times 4=36 \\omega^{2}$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(39336,'physics',3,314,580,'A passenger is standing $‘d’ m$ away from a bus. The bus begins to move with constant acceleration ‘$a$’ away from him. To catch the  bus the passenger runs at a constant speed $v$ towards the bus. The minimum speed of the passenger so that he may catch the bus will be','[{"text": "2 $ad$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\sqrt {ad} $", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\sqrt {2ad} $", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$ad$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Assume that the passenger will catch the bus after time $t$.<br>When the passenger catches the bus $d + {S_1} = {S_2}$<br>Displacement of bus $\\left( {{S_1}} \\right) = \\frac{1}{2}a{t^2}$<br>Displacement of passenger $\\left( {{S_2}} \\right) = ut$<br>$d + \\frac{1}{2}a{t^2} = ut \\Rightarrow t = \\frac{{u \\pm \\sqrt {{u^2} - 2ad} }}{a}$<br>Passenger will catch when $t$ is real <br>i.e.,${u^2} \\ge 2ad$<br>${u_{\\min }} = \\sqrt {2ad} $',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(39337,'physics',26,1138,2730,'Pressure inside a soap bubble is greater than the pressure outside by an amount (given : $R=$ Radius of bubble, $S=$ Surface tension of bubble).','[{"text": "$\\\\dfrac{4 S}{R}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{4 R}{S}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{S}{R}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{2 S}{R}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Inside a soap bubble, the excess pressure is given by<br>$\\Delta P=\\dfrac{4 S}{R} \\quad$ (as it has two free surfaces)<br>Where $S=$ Surface tension<br>$R=$ Radius of soap bubble',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2024,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(39338,'physics',30,1359,3263,'Two particles undergo SHM along parallel lines with tha same time period (T) and equal amplitudes. At a particular instant, one particle is at its extreme position while the other is at its mean position. They move in the same direction. They will cross each other after a further time:','[{"text": "$3 T / 8$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$T / 8$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$4 T / 3$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$T / 6$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'The two particles are shown in figure<br><img src=\'/img/data/physics/30_39338_explanation_1.jpg\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' /> <br>Where $t^{\\prime}=\\dfrac{T}{8} \\Rightarrow t=\\dfrac{3 T}{8}$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/30_39338_question_1.jpg'),
(39339,'physics',31,1345,3232,'The amplitude of vibration of any particle in a standing wave, produced along a stretched string depends on','[{"text": "Frequency of incident wave", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Time period of selected wave", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Location of particle", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Time", "isCorrect": false}]',2,NULL,NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(39340,'physics',26,1138,2730,'The excess pressure inside a soap bubble is thrice the excess pressure inside a second soap bubble. The ratio between the volume of the first and the second bubble is','[{"text": "$1: 27$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$1: 3$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1: 81$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1: 9$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'$\\Delta P_{1}=P_{1}-P_{0}=\\dfrac{4 T}{r_{1}}$   $\\quad \\quad (1)$<br>$\\Delta P_{2}=P_{2}-P_{0}=\\dfrac{4 T}{r_{2}}$   $\\quad \\quad (2)$<br>According to question, $\\Delta P_{1}=3 \\Delta P_{2}$<br>$\\therefore \\dfrac{4 T}{r_{1}}=3 \\times \\dfrac{4 T}{r_{2}} \\Rightarrow r_{2}=3 r_{1}$<br>$\\Rightarrow \\dfrac{V_{1}}{V_{2}}=\\dfrac{\\dfrac{4}{3} \\pi r_{1}^{3}}{\\dfrac{4}{3} \\pi r_{2}^{3}}=\\left(\\dfrac{r_{1}}{r_{2}}\\right)^{3}=\\left(\\dfrac{r_{1}}{3 r_{1}}\\right)^{3}=1: 27$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',2024,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(39341,'physics',3,313,576,'A body $A$  starts  from rest with an acceleration ${a_1}$. After 2 seconds, another body $B$ starts from rest with an acceleration ${a_2}$. If they travel  equal distance in the ${5^{th}}$ second, after the start of $A$, then the ratio ${a_1}:{a_2}$ is equal to:','[{"text": "$5:7$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$9:5$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$7:5$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$5:9$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'According to problem, distance travelled by body $A$ in $5^{th}$ sec and distance travelled by body $B$ in $3^{rd}$ sec of its motion are equal.<br> $0+\\frac {a_1}{2}(5^2 - 4^2) = 0 + \\frac{a_2}{2} [3^2 -2^2]$<br> $9a_1 = 5a_2 \\Rightarrow \\frac {a_1}{a_2} = \\frac {5}{9}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(39342,'physics',30,1359,3265,'A particle executing SHM according to the equation $x=A \\cos \\omega t$. Average speed of the particle during the interval $0 \\leq t \\leq \\dfrac{\\pi}{6 \\omega}$.','[{"text": "$\\\\dfrac{\\\\sqrt{3} A \\\\omega}{4}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{\\\\sqrt{3} A \\\\omega}{2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{3 A \\\\omega}{\\\\pi}(2-\\\\sqrt{3})$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{3 A \\\\omega}{\\\\pi}$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Average velocity<br>$\\begin{aligned}\\bar{v} & =\\dfrac{\\int_{0}^{t} \\dfrac{d x}{d t} \\cdot d t}{t}=\\dfrac{\\int_{0}^{t} d x}{t}=\\dfrac{x(t)-x(0)}{t} \\\\& =\\dfrac{A(\\cos \\pi / 6-1)}{\\pi / 6 \\omega}=\\dfrac{3 A \\omega}{\\pi}(\\sqrt{3}-2)\\end{aligned}$<br>since particle does not change its direction in the given interval, average speed<br>$|\\bar{v}|=\\dfrac{3 A \\omega}{\\pi}(2-\\sqrt{3})$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(39343,'physics',31,1343,3226,'A transverse wave travels along the $Z$ axis. The particles of the medium must move','[{"text": "Along the $Z$ axis", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Along the $X$ axis", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Along the $Y$ axis", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "In the $XY$ plane.", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'In transverse wave particle in the medium oscillate perpendicular to the propagation.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(39344,'physics',26,1138,2728,'A big drop is formed by coalescing 1000 small droplets of water. The surface energy will become','[{"text": "$\\\\dfrac{1^{\\\\text {th }}}{100}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{1}{10}^{\\\\mathrm{th}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "100 times", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "10 times", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Let radius of big drop is $R$ and radius of small drop is $r$. Here, volume remain constant.<br>Final volume $=$ Initial volume<br>So, $\\dfrac{4}{3} \\pi R^{3}=1000 \\cdot \\dfrac{4}{3} \\pi r^{3}$<br>$R^{3}=10^{3} r^{3} \\Rightarrow R=10 r$<br>Initial surface energy, $U_{i}=1000 \\times T \\times$ Area<br>$=1000 \\times T \\times 4 \\pi r^{2}$<br>Final surface energy,<br>$U_{f}=T \\times 4 \\pi R^{2}=T \\times 4 \\pi(10 r)^{2}$<br>$=100 \\times T \\times 4 \\pi r^{2}$<br>$\\dfrac{U_{f}}{U_{i}}=\\dfrac{100 \\times 4 \\pi r^{2}}{1000 \\times 4 \\pi r^{2}} \\Rightarrow U_{f}=\\dfrac{1}{10} U_{i}$<br>Thus, surface energy will become $1 / 10^{\\text {th }}$ times.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2024,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(39345,'physics',3,312,574,'The relation between time and distance is $t = \\alpha {x^2} + \\beta x,$ where $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants. The retardation is','[{"text": "$2\\\\alpha \\\\beta {v^3}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2\\\\alpha {v^3}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$2{\\\\beta ^2}{v^3}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2\\\\beta {v^3}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'$\\frac{{dt}}{{dx}} = 2\\alpha x + \\beta  \\Rightarrow v = \\frac{1}{{2\\alpha x + \\beta }}$<br>$\\because a = \\frac{{dv}}{{dt}} = \\frac{{dv}}{{dx}}.\\frac{{dx}}{{dt}}$<br>$a = v\\frac{{dv}}{{dx}} = \\frac{{ - v.2\\alpha }}{{{{\\left( {2\\alpha x + \\beta } \\right)}^2}}} =  - 2\\alpha .v.{v^2} =  - 2\\alpha {v^3}$<br>$\\therefore \\quad {\\rm{Retardation}} = 2\\alpha {v^3}.$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(39346,'physics',31,1343,3226,'The property required for propagation of transverse wave is','[{"text": "Longitudinal strain", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Lateral strain", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Shearing strain", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Poisson\'s ratio", "isCorrect": false}]',2,NULL,NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(39347,'physics',30,1359,3265,'A body oscillates with SHM according to the equation $x = 5\\cos \\,(2\\pi t + \\pi )$. At time $t = 1.5\\,s$, its displacement, speed and acceleration respectively is','[{"text": "$0,-10 \\\\pi,+20 \\\\pi^{2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$5,0,-20 \\\\pi^{2}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$2.5,+20 \\\\pi, 0$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$-5.0,+5 \\\\pi,-10 \\\\pi^{2}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'$x=5.0 \\cos (2 \\pi t+\\pi)$<br>Velocity $=\\dfrac{d x}{d t}=-10 \\pi \\sin (2 \\pi t+\\pi)$<br>Acceleration $=\\dfrac{d^{2} x}{d t^{2}}=-20 \\pi^{2} \\cot (2 \\pi t+\\pi)$<br>$\\therefore$ At $t = 1.5\\,\\sec ,x = 5.0\\cos 4\\pi  = 5$<br>Velocity $=\\dfrac{d x}{d t}=10 \\pi($ zero $)=0$<br>Acceleration $=\\dfrac{d^{2} x}{d t^{2}}=-20 \\pi^{2}(1)=-20 \\pi^{2}$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(39348,'physics',26,1138,2731,'Read the Statement - A and Statement - B carefully to mark the correct options given below.<div class="matching-question"><div class="column-left">If a capillary tube is immersed first in cold water and then in hot water, the height of capillary rise will be smaller in hot water.</div><div class="column-right">If a capillary tube is immersed first in cold water and then in hot water, the height of capillary rise will be smaller in cold water.</div></div>','[{"text": "Statement A is correct but statement B is incorrect", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Statement A is incorrect but statement B is correct", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both statements are correct", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both statements are incorrect", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'The height upto which a liquid rises in capillary tube is given by,<br>$h=\\dfrac{2 T \\cos \\theta}{r \\rho g} \\Rightarrow h \\propto \\dfrac{T}{r}$<br>Surface tension of hot water is less than that of cold water. When a capillary tube is immersed in hot water, it expands and its radius increases.<br>As surface tension decreases and radius increases, the height of capillary rise will be smaller in hot water than in cold water.<br>Hence, statement A is true and statement B is false. So, correct option is (1).',NULL,'Medium','MCQ type-2',2024,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(39349,'physics',3,313,576,'A car acceleration from rest at a constant rate $\\alpha $ for some time, after which it decelerates at a constant rate $\\beta $ and comes to rest. If the total time elapsed is $t$, then the maximum velocity acquired by the car is','[{"text": "$\\\\frac{{\\\\left( {\\\\alpha  + \\\\beta } \\\\right)t}}{{\\\\alpha \\\\beta }}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{{\\\\alpha \\\\beta t}}{{\\\\alpha  + \\\\beta }}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\left( {\\\\frac{{{\\\\alpha ^2} + {\\\\beta ^2}}}{{\\\\alpha \\\\beta }}} \\\\right)t$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\left( {\\\\frac{{{\\\\alpha ^2} - {\\\\beta ^2}}}{{\\\\alpha \\\\beta }}} \\\\right)t$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Let the car accelerate at  rate  $\\alpha $ for time ${t_1}$ then maximum velocity  attained,<br>$v = 0 + \\alpha {t_1} = \\alpha {t_1}$<br>Now, the card decelerates at a   rate $\\beta $ for time $(t - {t_1})$ and  finally  comes  to rest.  Then ,<br>$0 = v - \\beta (t - {t_1}) \\Rightarrow 0 = \\alpha {t_1} - \\beta t + \\beta {t_1}$<br>$ \\Rightarrow {t_1} = \\frac{\\beta }{{\\alpha  + \\beta }}t$<br>$\\therefore \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,v = \\frac{{\\alpha \\beta }}{{\\alpha  + \\beta }}t$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(39350,'physics',31,1343,3226,'The diagram below shows the propagation of a wave. Which points are in same phase?','[{"text": "$C$ and $E$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$B$ and $G$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$B$ and $F$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$F, G$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Points $B$ and $F$ are in same phase as they are $\\lambda$ distance apart.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/31_39350_question_1.png'),
(39351,'physics',30,1359,3265,'A point mass oscillates along the $x$-axis according to the law $x=x_{0} \\cos (\\omega t-\\pi / 4)$. If the acceleration of the particle is written as $a=A \\cos (\\omega t+\\delta)$, then','[{"text": "$A=x_{0} \\\\omega^{2}, \\\\delta=3 \\\\pi / 4$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$A=x_{0}, \\\\delta=-\\\\pi / 4$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$A=x_{0} \\\\omega^{2}, \\\\delta=\\\\pi / 4$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$A=x_{0} \\\\omega^{2}, \\\\delta=-\\\\pi / 4$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Here,<br>$x=x_{0} \\cos (\\omega t-\\pi / 4)$<br>$\\therefore$ Velocity, $v=\\dfrac{d x}{d t}=-x_{0} \\omega \\sin \\left(\\omega t-\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}\\right)$<br>Acceleration, $a=\\dfrac{d v}{d t}=-x_{0} \\omega^{2} \\cos \\left(\\omega t-\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}\\right)$<br>$ = {x_0}{\\omega ^2}\\cos \\left[ {\\pi  + \\left( {\\omega t - \\frac{\\pi }{4}} \\right)} \\right]$<br>$ = {x_0}{\\omega ^2}\\cos \\left( {\\omega t + \\frac{{3\\pi }}{4}} \\right)\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,(1)$<br>Acceleration, $a=A \\cos (\\omega t+\\delta)$<br>Comparing the two equations, we get<br>$A=x_{0} \\omega^{2} \\text { and } \\delta=\\dfrac{3 \\pi}{4} .$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(39352,'physics',3,313,576,'A car, starting from rest, accelerates at the rate $f$ through a distance $S$, then continues at constant speed for time $t$ and then decelerates at the rates $\\frac{f}{2}$ to come to rest. If the total distance travelled is 15 $S$, then','[{"text": "$S = \\\\frac{1}{6}f{t^2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$S = \\\\frac{1}{{72}}f{t^2}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$S = \\\\frac{1}{2}f{t^2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$S = \\\\frac{1}{4}f{t^2}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Let car start from point $A$ from rest and moves up to point $B$ with acceleration $f$<br>Velocity of car at point $B$, $\\upsilon  = \\sqrt {2f\\,S} $<br>$[{\\rm{As}}\\,{\\upsilon ^2} = {u^2} + 2as]$<br>Car moves distance $BC$ with this constant velocity in time $t$<br>$x = \\sqrt {2fS} .t[{\\rm{As}}\\,s = ut]$ &emsp; (1)<br>So the velocity of car at point $C$ also will be <br>$\\sqrt {2fS} $ and finally car stops after covering  distance $y$.<br>Distance $CD \\Rightarrow y = \\frac{{{{\\left( {\\sqrt {2fS} } \\right)}^2}}}{{2\\left( {f/2} \\right)}} = \\frac{{2fS}}{f} = 2S$ &emsp; (2)<br>So, the total distance $AD = AB + BC + CD = 45S$ [Given]<br>$ \\Rightarrow S + x + 2S = 15S \\Rightarrow x = 12 \\, S$<br>Substituting the values of $x$ in equation (1) we get<br> $x = \\sqrt {2fS} .t \\Rightarrow 12S = \\sqrt {2fS} .t \\Rightarrow 144{S^2} = 2fS.{t^2}$<br>$ \\Rightarrow S = \\frac{1}{{72}}f{t^2}$.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(39353,'physics',26,1138,2728,'A small liquid drop of radius $R$ is divided into 27 identical liquid drops. If the surface tension is $T$, then the work done in the process will be','[{"text": "$3 \\\\pi R^{2} T$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$8 \\\\pi R^{2} T$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$4 \\\\pi R^{2} T$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{1}{8} \\\\pi R^{2} T$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Radius of big drop $=R$<br>Number of drops $n=27$<br>Surface tension $=T$<br>$W=4 \\pi R^{2} T\\left(n^{\\frac{1}{3}}-1\\right)$<br>$W=4 \\pi R^{2} T\\left(27^{\\frac{1}{3}}-1\\right)$<br>$=8 \\pi R^{2} T$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',2024,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(39354,'physics',31,1343,3227,'A wave travelling in the + ve $x$-direction having displacement along $y$-direction as $1\\,m$, wavelength $2 \\pi m$ and frequency of $\\dfrac{1}{\\pi} H z$ is represented by','[{"text": "$y=\\\\sin (2 \\\\pi x+2 t)$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$y = \\\\sin (2t - x)$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$y=\\\\sin (2 \\\\pi x-2 \\\\pi t)$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$y=\\\\sin (10 \\\\pi x-20 \\\\pi t)$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'$y=A \\sin (\\omega t-k x)$<br>$\\begin{aligned}& \\omega=2 \\pi f, K=2 \\pi / \\lambda \\\\& \\omega=2, k=1\\end{aligned}$<br>$ \\Rightarrow y = A\\sin \\left( {2t - x} \\right)$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(39355,'physics',30,1359,3266,'Two pendulums have time periods T and $\\frac{{5T}}{4}$.<br>They start S.H.M. at the same time from the mean position. What will be the phase difference between them after the bigger pendulum\ncomplete one oscillation?','[{"text": "$90^{\\\\circ}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$45^{\\\\circ}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$30^{\\\\circ}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$60^{\\\\circ}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'$\\dfrac{5 T}{4}=T+\\dfrac{T}{4}$ <img src=\'/img/data/physics/30_39355_explanation_1.jpg\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' /> By the time, the bigger pendulum makes one full oscillation, the smaller pendulum will make $\\left(1+\\dfrac{1}{4}\\right)$ oscillation. The bigger pendulum will be in the mean position and the smaller one will be in the positive extreme position. Thus, phase difference $=90^{\\circ}$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(39356,'physics',3,312,574,'A particle moves a distance $x$ in time $t$ according to equation $x = {\\left( {t + 5} \\right)^{ - 1}}$. The acceleration of particle is proportional to','[{"text": "${\\\\left( {{\\\\rm{Velocity}}} \\\\right)^{3/2}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${\\\\left( {{\\\\rm{Distance}}} \\\\right)^2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\left( {{\\\\rm{Distance}}} \\\\right)^{ - 2}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\left( {{\\\\rm{Velocity}}} \\\\right)^{2/3}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'$x = \\frac{1}{{t + 5}} \\Rightarrow v = \\frac{{dx}}{{dt}} =  - \\frac{1}{{{{\\left( {t + 5} \\right)}^2}}}$<br>Acceleration, <br>$a = \\frac{{dv}}{{dt}} = \\frac{2}{{{{\\left( {t + 5} \\right)}^3}}} \\Rightarrow a\\,\\alpha {\\left( {velocity} \\right)^{3/2}}.$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(39358,'physics',31,1343,3227,'When a wave travels in a medium, displacement of a particle is given by $y=a \\sin 2 \\pi(b t-c x)$, where $a,\\,b,\\,c$ are constants. The maximum particle velocity will be twice the wave velocity if','[{"text": "$c=\\\\dfrac{1}{\\\\pi a}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$b=\\\\dfrac{1}{a c}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$b=a c$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$c=\\\\pi a$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Given, $y=a \\sin 2 \\pi(b t-c x)$<br>On comparing with general equation<br>$y=A \\sin \\left[\\dfrac{2 \\pi t}{T}-\\dfrac{2 \\pi}{\\lambda} x\\right]$<br>We get $\\dfrac{2 \\pi}{T}=\\omega=2 \\pi b$<br>and $A=a \\Rightarrow \\lambda=\\dfrac{1}{c}$ and $T=\\dfrac{1}{b}$<br>Maximum velocity,<br>$v_{\\max }=\\omega A=2 \\pi b a$<br>Wave velocity, $v=\\dfrac{\\lambda}{T}=\\dfrac{b}{c}$<br>Given, particle velocity $=2 \\times$ wave velocity<br>$2 \\pi b a=2 \\times \\dfrac{b}{c} \\Rightarrow c=\\dfrac{1}{\\pi a}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(39359,'physics',30,1359,3263,'Two pendulums of different lengths are in phase at the mean position at a certain instant. The minimum time after which they will be again in phase is $5\\,T/4$, where $T$ is the time period of shorter pendulum. Find the ratio of lengths of the two pendulums.','[{"text": "$1: 4$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1: 16$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1: 25$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$1: 2$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Let $T_{1}=\\mathrm{T}_{\\text {and }} \\mathrm{T}_{2}=K T ; \\dfrac{2 \\pi}{T} t-\\dfrac{2 \\pi}{K T} t=2 \\pi$<br>$t\\left(\\dfrac{K-1}{K T}\\right)=1 ; \\dfrac{5 T}{4}\\left(\\dfrac{K-1}{K T}\\right)=1$ which gives $K = 5$<br>If length of first pendulum $=l, T_{1}=2 \\pi \\sqrt{l / g}$<br>$T_{2}=5 \\times 2 \\pi \\sqrt{l / g}=2 \\pi \\sqrt{25 l / g}$<br>So, ratio of lengths $=1: 25$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(39360,'physics',3,314,579,'Two trains separated by a distance of 100 $km$ approach each other with constant speed 10 $km/hr$. There is a fly that starts at one train and goes to the other train and keeps on going back and forth at a constant speed of 40 $km/hr$. Find the total distance that the fly travels before the two trains meet.','[{"text": "$75\\\\,km$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$200\\\\,km$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$100\\\\,km$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$50\\\\,km$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'This problem can be solved very easily with the help of relative velocity. As the two trains approach each other the time taken to meet each other is <br>${s_r} = {v_r}t$<br>$100 \\, km = \\left( {20\\frac{{km}}{h}} \\right)t \\Rightarrow t = 5h$<br>So the fly travels for 5 $h$ with a constant speed of  40 $km/h$<br>${s_{fly}} = 40\\frac{{km}}{h} \\times 5h = 200 \\, km$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/3_39360_question_1.png'),
(39362,'physics',31,1343,3227,'Equation of a wave is $y=0.4 m \\sin (3.14 t-3.14 x)$; where $x$ is in metre and $t$ is in second. Wavelength of the wave is','[{"text": "$2\\\\,m$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$0.5\\\\,m$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$50\\\\,m$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$25\\\\,m$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Comparing the given equation with<br>$\\begin{aligned}& y=A \\sin \\left(2 \\pi f t+\\dfrac{2 \\pi}{\\lambda} x\\right) \\\\& \\dfrac{2 \\pi}{\\lambda}=3.14 \\\\& \\therefore \\lambda=2 m\\end{aligned}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(39363,'physics',30,1360,3267,'For a particle executing S.H.M, the kinetic energy $k$ is given by $k=10 \\cos ^{2} \\omega t$. The maximum value of P.E is','[{"text": "2.5", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "20", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "5", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "10", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Given that $k=10 \\cos ^{2} \\omega t$<br>$k = 10\\left( {\\frac{{1 + \\cos (2\\omega t)}}{2}} \\right) = 5[1 + \\cos 2\\omega t]$<br>${k_{\\max }} = 10\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,(\\cos (2\\omega t) = 1)$<br>${k_{\\min {\\rm{ }}}} = 0\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,(\\cos (2\\omega t) =  - 1)$<br>In S.H.M $T.{E_{\\max }} = K \\cdot {E_{\\max }} = 10$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(39364,'physics',3,309,571,'The displacement of a body along $x$-axis depends on time as $\\sqrt x  = t + 1.$ Then the velocity of body','[{"text": "Decreases with time", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Independent of time", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Increases with time", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "None of these", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'$\\sqrt x  = t + 1$<br>Squaring both sides, we get, <br>$x = {\\left( {t + 1} \\right)^2} = {t^2} + 2t + 1$<br>Differentiating it w.r.t. time $t$, we get, $\\frac{{dx}}{{dt}} = 2t + 2$<br>velocity, $v = \\frac{{dx}}{{dt}} = 2t + 2.$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(39366,'physics',31,1343,3227,'A plane progressive wave is given by $y=2 \\cos 6.284(330 t-x)$. What is period of the wave?','[{"text": "$2\\\\pi  \\\\times 330\\\\,s$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{1}{330} s$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{6.284}{330} s$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$(2 \\\\pi \\\\times 330)^{-1} s$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'We have the progressive wave given by<br>$y=2 \\cos 6.284(330 t-x)$<br>As $\\quad 6.284=2 \\pi$<br>$\\omega=6.284 \\times 330$<br>Time period $T = \\frac{{2\\pi }}{\\omega } = \\frac{1}{{330}}s$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(39367,'physics',30,1360,3267,'A particle of mass $10\\,gm$ is placed in a potential field given by $V=\\left(50 x^{2}+100\\right) J / k g$. The frequency of oscillation in cycle/sec is:','[{"text": "$\\\\dfrac{5}{\\\\pi}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{10}{\\\\pi}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{50}{\\\\pi}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{100}{\\\\pi}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Potential energy $=U=V m$<br>$\\Rightarrow U=\\left(50 x^{2}+100\\right) 10^{-2}$<br><br>$\\begin{gathered}F=-\\dfrac{d U}{d x}=-(100 x) 10^{-2} \\\\\\Rightarrow-m \\omega^{2} x=-\\left(100 \\times 10^{-2}\\right) x \\\\10 \\times 10^{-3} \\omega^{2} x=100 \\times 10^{-2} x \\\\\\Rightarrow \\omega^{2}=100 \\therefore \\omega=10 \\\\\\Rightarrow f=\\dfrac{\\omega}{2 \\pi}=\\dfrac{10}{2 \\pi}=\\dfrac{5}{\\pi}\\end{gathered}$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(39368,'physics',3,309,571,'The displacement-time graph for two particles $A$ and $B$ are straight lines inclined at angles of $30^\\circ $ and $60^\\circ $ with the time axis. The ratio of  vilocities of  ${v_A}:{v_B}$ is','[{"text": "$1:3$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$1:2$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\sqrt 3 :1$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1:\\\\sqrt 3 $", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'$\\frac{{{v_A}}}{{{v_B}}} = \\frac{{\\tan {\\theta _A}}}{{\\tan {\\theta _B}}} = \\frac{{\\tan {{30}^ \\circ }}}{{\\tan {{60}^ \\circ }}} = \\frac{{1/\\sqrt 3 }}{{\\sqrt 3 }} = \\frac{1}{3}.$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(39370,'physics',31,1343,3227,'A wave on a string is travelling and the displacement of particles on it is given by $x=A \\sin (2 t-0.1 x)$. Then the wavelength of the wave is :','[{"text": "$20 \\\\pi$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$10 \\\\pi$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$30 \\\\pi$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$40 \\\\pi$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'$x=A \\sin (2 t-0.1 x)$<br>$\\begin{gathered}\\text { also } \\quad x=A \\sin \\left(\\dfrac{2 \\pi t}{T}-\\dfrac{2 \\pi}{\\lambda} x\\right) \\\\\\Rightarrow \\dfrac{2 \\pi}{\\lambda}=0.1 \\Rightarrow \\lambda=20 \\pi\\end{gathered}$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(39371,'physics',30,1360,3267,'A particle of mass $m$ is located in a undimensional potential field where potential energy of the particle depends on the coordinate $x$ as $U(x)=\\dfrac{A}{x^{2}}-\\dfrac{B}{x}$ where $A$ and $B$ are positive contacts. Find the time period of small oscillations that the particle will perform about equilibrium position.','[{"text": "$4 \\\\pi A \\\\sqrt{\\\\dfrac{2 m A}{B^{4}}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$2 \\\\pi A \\\\sqrt{\\\\dfrac{m A}{B A}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$8 \\\\pi A \\\\sqrt{\\\\dfrac{m A}{2 B A}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\pi A \\\\sqrt{\\\\dfrac{2 m A}{B^{4}}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'The potential energy function for the mass $m$ in a undimensional potential field is given as<br>$U(x) = \\frac{A}{{{x^2}}} - \\frac{B}{x}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,(1)$<br>Since this is the potential energy of mass $m$ in a potential field, so the force acting on the mass is obtained by the relation:<br>$F =  - \\frac{{dU}}{{dx}} = \\frac{{2A}}{{{x^3}}} - \\frac{B}{{{x^2}}}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,(2)$<br>Now, the equilibrium position is given by<br>$\\begin{aligned}& F=0 \\text { or } \\dfrac{2 A}{x^{3}}-\\dfrac{B}{x^{2}}=0 \\\\& (2 A-B x)=0\\end{aligned}$<br>So, $\\quad x=\\dfrac{2 A}{B}$<br>Since $x=2 A / B$ gives stable situation<br>(because energy is minimum here; $d^{2} U / d x^{2}$ is positive), so stable equilibrium position is given as $x=\\dfrac{2 A}{B}$<br>Now if the mass is displaced slightly away from the equilibrium position, the force acting on mass $m$ is given as<br>$F=\\dfrac{2 A}{[2 A / B+\\Delta x]^{3}}-\\dfrac{B}{[2 A / B+\\Delta x]^{2}}$<br>$\\begin{aligned}& =\\dfrac{2 A}{\\left(\\dfrac{2 A}{B}\\right)^{3}\\left(1+\\dfrac{B \\Delta x}{2 A}\\right)^{3}}-\\dfrac{B}{\\left(\\dfrac{2 A}{B}\\right)^{2}\\left(1+\\dfrac{B \\Delta x}{2 A}\\right)^{2}} \\\\& =\\dfrac{2 A}{8 A^{3} / B^{3}}\\left[1+\\dfrac{B \\Delta x}{2 A}\\right]^{-3}-\\dfrac{B}{4 A^{2} / B^{2}}\\left[1+\\dfrac{B \\Delta x}{2 A}\\right]^{-2}\\end{aligned}$<br>So, using binomial expansion for small $\\Delta x$, we get<br>$\\begin{aligned}F & =\\dfrac{B^{3}}{4 A^{2}}\\left[1-\\dfrac{3 B \\Delta x}{2 A}\\right]-\\dfrac{B^{3}}{4 A^{2}}\\left[1-\\dfrac{2 B \\Delta x}{2 A}\\right] \\\\& =\\dfrac{B^{3}}{4 A^{2}}\\left[1-\\dfrac{3 B \\Delta x}{2 A}-1+\\dfrac{2 B \\Delta x}{2 A}\\right]=-\\left(\\dfrac{B^{4}}{8 A^{3}}\\right) \\Delta x\\end{aligned}$<br>We can write<br>$m\\frac{{{d^2}x}}{{d{t^2}}} =  - \\frac{{{B^4}}}{{8{A^3}}}\\Delta x$<br>$\\frac{{{d^2}x}}{{d{t^2}}} =  - \\left( {\\frac{{{B^4}}}{{8m{A^3}}}} \\right)\\Delta x\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,(3)$<br>$\\frac{{{d^2}x}}{{d{t^2}}} \\propto  - \\Delta x\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,(4)$<br>So from the above relations, it is clear that particle will perform SHM. But for any SHM,<br>$\\dfrac{d^{2} x}{d t^{2}}=-\\omega^{2} \\Delta x$<br>So, from eqs. (3) and (4), we have $\\omega^{2}=\\dfrac{B^{4}}{8 m A^{3}}$<br>$T=\\dfrac{2 \\pi}{\\omega}=2 \\pi \\sqrt{\\dfrac{8 A^{3} m}{B^{4}}}=4 \\pi A \\sqrt{\\dfrac{2 m A}{B^{4}}}$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(39372,'physics',3,313,577,'A ball is dropped into a well in which the water level is at a depth $h$ below the top. If the speed of sound is $c$, then the time after which the splash is heard will be given by','[{"text": "$h\\\\left[ {\\\\sqrt {\\\\frac{2}{{gh}}}  - \\\\frac{1}{c}} \\\\right]$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$h\\\\left[ {\\\\sqrt {\\\\frac{2}{{gh}}}  + \\\\frac{1}{c}} \\\\right]$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$h\\\\left[ {\\\\frac{2}{g} - \\\\frac{1}{c}} \\\\right]$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$h\\\\left[ {\\\\frac{2}{g} + \\\\frac{1}{c}} \\\\right]$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Time of fall $ = \\sqrt {\\frac{{2h}}{g}} $<br>Time taken by the sound to come out $ = \\frac{h}{c}$<br>Total time  $ = \\sqrt {\\frac{{2h}}{g}}  + \\frac{h}{c} = h\\left[ {\\sqrt {\\frac{2}{{gh}}}  + \\frac{1}{c}} \\right]$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(39373,'physics',31,1343,3227,'The equation of a wave is given as $y=0.07 \\sin (12 \\pi x-3000 \\pi t)$. where $x$ is in metre and $t$ in sec, then the correct statement is','[{"text": "$\\\\lambda  = 1/6m,v = 250\\\\,m/s$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$a = 0.07\\\\,m,v = 300\\\\,m/s$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$n = 1500,v = 200\\\\,m/s$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "None of these", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Comparing the given equation with<br>$y=a \\sin (\\omega t-k x)$<br>We get $\\omega=3000 \\pi \\Rightarrow n=\\dfrac{\\omega}{2 \\pi}=1500 H z$<br>and $k=\\dfrac{2 \\pi}{\\lambda}=12 \\pi \\Rightarrow \\lambda=\\dfrac{1}{6} m$<br>So, $v = n\\lambda  \\Rightarrow v = 1500 \\times \\frac{1}{6} = 250\\,m/s$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(39374,'physics',30,1362,3269,'The bob of a simple pendulum is displaced from its equilibrium position $O$ to a position $Q$ which is at height $h$ above $O$ and the bob is then released. Assuming the mass of the bob to be $m$ and time period of oscillations to be $2.0\\,\\sec $, the tension in the string when the bob passes through $O$ is','[{"text": "$m\\\\left(g+\\\\sqrt{\\\\pi^{2} g h}\\\\right)$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$m(g+\\\\pi \\\\sqrt{2 g h})$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$m\\\\left(g+\\\\sqrt{\\\\dfrac{\\\\pi^{2}}{3} g h}\\\\right)$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$m\\\\left(g+\\\\sqrt{\\\\dfrac{\\\\pi^{2}}{2} g h}\\\\right)$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Tension in the string when bob passes through lowest point<br>$T=m g+\\dfrac{m v^{2}}{r}=m g+m v \\omega \\quad(\\because v=r \\omega)$<br>Putting $v=\\sqrt{2 g h}$ and $\\omega=\\dfrac{2 \\pi}{T}=\\dfrac{2 \\pi}{2}=\\pi$ we get $T=m(g+\\pi \\sqrt{2 g h})$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/30_39374_question_1.jpg'),
(39375,'physics',3,314,579,'An express train is moving with a velocity ${v_1}$. Its driver finds another train that is moving on the same track in the same direction with velocity ${v_2}$. To escape collision, diver applies a retardation $a$ on the train, the minimum time of escaping collision will be','[{"text": "$t = \\\\frac{{{v_1} - {v_2}}}{a}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$t = \\\\frac{{v_1^2 - v_2^2}}{2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{Both}}\\\\,1\\\\,\\\\& \\\\,2$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$t\\\\,{\\\\rm{  = }}\\\\,\\\\frac{{{v_1} + {v_2}}}{2}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'As the trains are moving in the same direction, so the initial relative speed $\\left( {{v_1} - {v_2}} \\right)$ and by applying retardation final relative speed  becomes is zero.<br>From $v = u - at \\Rightarrow 0 = \\left( {{v_1} - {v_2}} \\right) - at \\Rightarrow t = \\frac{{{v_1} - {v_2}}}{a}.$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(39376,'physics',31,1343,3227,'The equation of a travelling wave is $y=60 \\cos (180 t-6 x)$ where $y$ is in $\\mu m, t$ in second and $x$ in meters. the ratio of maximum particle velocity to velocity of wave propagation is','[{"text": "$3.6 \\\\times 10^{-4}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$3.6 \\\\times 10^{-2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$3.6 \\\\times 10^{-11}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$3.6 \\\\times 10^{-6}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'$y = 60\\cos (180t - 6x)\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\left( 1 \\right)$<br>$\\omega  = 180,k = 6$<br>$v = \\frac{\\omega }{k} = \\frac{{180}}{6} = 30\\,m/s$<br>Differentiating (1) w.r.t $t$,<br>$v = \\frac{{dy}}{{dt}} =  - 60 \\times 180\\sin (180t - 6x)$<br>${v_{\\max {\\text{ }}}} = 60 \\times 180\\mu \\,m/s$<br>$\\frac{{{v_{\\max {\\text{ }}}}}}{v} = \\frac{{60 \\times 180}}{{30}} = 3.6 \\times {10^{ - 4}}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(39377,'physics',30,1362,3269,'A pendulum made of a uniform wire of cross sectional area $A$ has time period $T$. When an additional mass $M$ is added to its bob, the time period changes to ${T_M}$. If the Young\'s modulus of the material of the wire is $Y$ then $\\dfrac{1}{Y}$ is equal to: $(g = $ gravitational acceleration$)$','[{"text": "$\\\\left[\\\\left(\\\\dfrac{T_{M}}{T}\\\\right)^{2}-1\\\\right] \\\\dfrac{M g}{A}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\left[1-\\\\left(\\\\dfrac{T_{M}}{T}\\\\right)^{2}\\\\right] \\\\dfrac{A}{M g}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\left[1-\\\\left(\\\\dfrac{T}{T_{M}}\\\\right)^{2}\\\\right] \\\\dfrac{A}{M g}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\left[\\\\left(\\\\dfrac{T_{M}}{T}\\\\right)^{2}-1\\\\right] \\\\dfrac{A}{M g}$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Time period, $T=2 \\pi \\sqrt{\\dfrac{l}{g}}$<br>when additional mass $M$ is added to its bob<br>$\\begin{aligned}T_{M} & =2 \\pi \\sqrt{\\dfrac{l+\\Delta l}{g}} \\\\\\Delta l & =\\dfrac{M g l}{A Y} \\\\\\Rightarrow T_{M} & =2 \\pi \\sqrt{\\dfrac{l+\\dfrac{M g l}{A Y}}{g}} \\\\\\left(\\dfrac{T_{M}}{T}\\right)^{2} & =1+\\dfrac{M g}{A Y} \\\\\\dfrac{1}{Y} & =\\dfrac{A}{M g}\\left[\\left(\\dfrac{T_{M}}{T}\\right)^{2}-1\\right]\\end{aligned}$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(39378,'physics',3,314,579,'A man swimming downstream overcome a float at a point $M$. After travelling distance $D$ he turned back and passed the float at a distance of $D$/2 from the point $M$, then the ratio of speed of swimmer with respect to still water to the speed of the river will be','[{"text": "$2$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$3$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$4$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2.5$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Time taken by man to go from $M$ to $P$ and then $P$ to $N$ = time taken by float to go from $M$ to $N$.<br>Let $u$ is the velocity of river and $v$ is the velocity of the man w.r.t. the still water.<br>$\\frac{D}{{v + u}} + \\frac{D}{{2(v - u)}} = \\frac{D}{{2u}}$ <br>Simplify to get $\\frac{v}{u} = 3$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(39379,'physics',31,1343,3227,'The tansverse wave represented by the equation $y=\\sin [3 x-15 t]$','[{"text": "Wavelength $=4 \\\\dfrac{\\\\pi}{3}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Amplitude $=4$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Period $=\\\\dfrac{\\\\pi}{15}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Speed of propagation $=5$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Compare the given equation with standard form<br>$\\begin{aligned}& y=A \\sin \\left[\\dfrac{2 \\pi x}{\\lambda}-\\dfrac{2 \\pi t}{T}\\right] \\\\& A=1 \\\\& \\dfrac{2 \\pi}{\\lambda}=3, \\lambda=\\dfrac{2 \\pi}{3} \\text { and } \\dfrac{2 \\pi}{T}=15 \\\\& T=\\dfrac{2 \\pi}{15} \\Rightarrow v=\\dfrac{\\lambda}{T}=5\\end{aligned}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(39414,'physics',3,313,576,'A small block slides without friction down an inclined plane starting from rest. Let ${S_n}$ be the distance travelled from time $t = n - 1\\,{\\rm{to}}\\,t = n$. Then $\\frac{{{S_n}}}{{{S_{n + 1}}}}$ is','[{"text": "$\\\\frac{{2n - 1}}{{2n}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{{2n - 1}}{{2n + 1}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{{2n}}{{2n + 1}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{{2n + 1}}{{2n - 1}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'${S_n} = \\frac{1}{2}g\\sin \\theta \\left( {2n - 1} \\right),$<br>${S_{n + 1}} = \\frac{1}{2}g\\sin \\theta \\{ 2\\left( {n + 1} \\right) - 1\\} $<br>$\\frac{{{S_n}}}{{{S_{n + 1}}}} = \\frac{{2n - 1}}{{2n + 1}}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(39380,'physics',30,1362,3269,'The period of oscillation of a simple pendulum of length $l$ suspended from the roof of the vehicle which moves down without friction on an inclined plane of inclination $\\alpha$, is given by:','[{"text": "$\\\\pi \\\\sqrt{\\\\dfrac{l}{g \\\\cos \\\\alpha}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2 \\\\pi \\\\sqrt{\\\\dfrac{l}{g \\\\cos \\\\alpha}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{1}{\\\\pi} \\\\sqrt{\\\\dfrac{l}{2 g \\\\cos \\\\alpha}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{1}{2 \\\\pi} \\\\sqrt{\\\\dfrac{l}{g \\\\cos \\\\alpha}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'The vehicle goes down with acceleration $g \\sin \\alpha$. so the pendulum is in noninertial frame and Pseudo force acts on it. <img src=\'/img/data/physics/30_39380_explanation_1.jpg\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' /> So the only resultant force on the bob is $m g \\cos \\alpha$. The effective acceleration is<br>$\\begin{aligned}& g^{\\prime}=g \\cos \\alpha \\\\& T=2 \\pi \\sqrt{\\dfrac{l}{g \\cos \\alpha}}\\end{aligned}$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(39381,'physics',3,314,579,'A ball $A$ is thrown up vertically with a speed $u$ and at the same instant another ball $B$ is released from a height $h$. At time $t$, the speed of $A$ relative to $B$ is','[{"text": "$2u$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$u$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$u - gt$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\sqrt {({u^2} - gt)} $", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'At time $t$<br>Velocity of $A$, ${v_A} = u - gt$ upward<br>Velocity of $B$, ${v_B} = gt$ downward <br>If we assume that height $h$ is smaller than or equal to the maximum height reached by $A$, then at every instant ${v_A}\\,{\\rm{and}}\\,{v_B}$ are in opposite direction.<br>$\\therefore \\quad {V_{AB}} = {v_A} + {v_B}$ <br>$ = u - gt + gt$ [Speeds in opposite directions get added]<br>$ = u$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(39382,'physics',31,1344,3228,'A uniform long rope is suspended from roof. A transverse wave pulse is produced at its lower end. As the wave travels upward along the suspended rope then<br>A. Velocity of wave increases<br>B. Wavelength of wave increases<br>C. Frequency of wave remains constant','[{"text": "Only $A$ and $B$ are true", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Only $B$ and $C$ are true", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Only $A$ and $C$ are true", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$A$, $B$ and $C$ all are true", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'$v=\\sqrt{\\dfrac{T}{\\mu}}$<br>When wave travels up tension increases <br>Hence \' $v$ \' increases, and \' $\\lambda$ \' increases.<br>Frequency remains constant.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(39383,'physics',30,1362,3271,'In the figure all springs are identical having spring constant $k$and mass $m$ each. The block also has mass $m$. The frequency of oscillation of the block is:','[{"text": "$\\\\dfrac{1}{2 \\\\pi} \\\\sqrt{\\\\dfrac{3 k}{2 m}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{1}{2 \\\\pi} \\\\sqrt{\\\\dfrac{3 k}{m}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2 \\\\pi \\\\sqrt{\\\\dfrac{3 m}{3 k}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "None of these", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'If spring has the mass then:<br>$f=\\dfrac{1}{2 \\pi} \\sqrt{\\dfrac{k}{m+\\dfrac{M_{\\text {spring }}}{3}}}$<br>Spring equivalent $=3 k$ (As all are in parallel $)$<br>$f=\\dfrac{1}{2 \\pi} \\sqrt{\\dfrac{3 k}{m+\\dfrac{(3 m)}{3}}} \\Rightarrow f=\\dfrac{1}{2 \\pi} \\sqrt{\\dfrac{3 k}{2 m}}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/30_39383_question_1.jpg'),
(39384,'physics',3,314,579,'From a lift moving upward with uniform acceleration ‘$a$’ a man throws a ball vertically upward with a velocity $v$ relative to the lift. The time after which it comes back to the man is','[{"text": "$\\\\frac{{2v}}{{g - a}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{v}{{g + a}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{{2v}}{{g + a}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{{2vg}}{{{g^2} + {a^2}}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'We use the relative motion equation for displacement<br>${S_r} = {S_{{r_0}}} + {u_r}t + \\frac{1}{2}{a_r}{t^2}$<br>${S_r}$-displacement of ball w.r.t. lift   <br>Take the origin at the base of the lift <br>$ \\Rightarrow {S_{{r_0}}} = 0$<br>(Since the body is projected from the floor itself)<br>${\\vec S_r} = {\\vec S_{bl}},{\\vec u_r} = {\\vec v_{bl}} = v\\hat j$<br>${\\vec a_r} = {\\vec a_{bl}} = {\\vec a_b} - {\\vec a_l}$<br>${\\vec a_r} =  - g\\hat i - a\\hat j =  - (a + g)\\hat j$<br>When the ball strikes the floor again<br>${\\vec S_r} = 0$ (relative displacement)<br>$0 = vt\\,\\hat j - \\frac{1}{2}(a + g){t^2}\\hat j$<br>$t = \\frac{{2v}}{{a + g}}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(39385,'physics',31,1344,3228,'A circular loop of string rotates about its axis on a frictionless horizontal plane at a uniform rate so that the tangential speed of any particle of the string is $v$. If a small transverse disturbance is produced at a point of the loop, speed (relative to the string) of this disturbance travel on the string is $x v$ then $x$ is','[{"text": "3", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "2", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "1", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "4", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Suppose $F$ is the tension in the string due to its rotation. Choose a small element of the string of length $l$. If $\\mu$ is the mass per unit length of the string, then mass of the element,<br>$m=\\mu l=\\mu(R \\theta)$<br><br><img src=\'/img/data/physics/31_39385_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' /> <br>Using Newton\'s second law for the element, we have<br>$2 F \\sin \\theta / 2=\\dfrac{m v^{2}}{R}$<br>For small $\\theta, \\sin \\dfrac{\\theta}{2} \\simeq \\dfrac{\\theta}{2}$<br>$\\begin{aligned}& \\therefore 2 F \\dfrac{\\theta}{2}=\\dfrac{m v^{2}}{R} \\\\& \\therefore F=\\mu v^{2}\\end{aligned}$<br>The speed of the disturbance<br>$=\\sqrt{\\dfrac{F}{\\mu}}=\\sqrt{\\dfrac{\\mu v^{2}}{\\mu}}=v .$<br>So option (3) is correct.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/31_39385_question_1.png'),
(39386,'physics',30,1362,3270,'A spring of mass $m$ is connected to a rigid support as shown in the figure. If the free end of the spring is given velocity $v_{o}$ then kinetic energy of the spring is','[{"text": "$\\\\dfrac{m v_{o}^{2}}{4}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{m v_{o}^{2}}{3}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{1}{2}\\\\left(\\\\dfrac{m}{3}\\\\right) v_{o}^{2}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{1}{2} m v_{o}^{2}$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'The velocity of the spring increases linearly from left to right. The velocity of the spring at a distance $x$ from the left end is<br>$v=\\dfrac{v_{o}}{l} x$<br>consider a small element of length $dx$ as shown in the figure. <img src=\'/img/data/physics/30_39386_explanation_1.jpg\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' /> The kinetic energy of the element is<br>$\\begin{aligned}& d x=\\dfrac{1}{2}\\left(\\dfrac{m}{l}\\right) d x v^{2}=\\dfrac{1}{2} \\dfrac{m}{l} \\dfrac{v_{o}^{2}}{l^{2}} x^{2} d x \\\\& k=\\dfrac{m v_{o}^{2}}{2 l^{3}} \\int_{x^{2}}^{l} x^{2} d x=\\dfrac{m v_{o}^{2}}{2 l^{3}}\\left[\\dfrac{l^{3}}{3}\\right]=\\dfrac{1}{2}\\left(\\dfrac{m}{3}\\right) v_{o}^{2}\\end{aligned}$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/30_39386_question_1.jpg'),
(39387,'physics',3,314,579,'Car $A$ and $B$ are travelling as shown in the diagram. Find their time of meeting?','[{"text": "$5\\\\,s$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$25\\\\,s$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$20\\\\,s$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$23\\\\,s$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'${\\bar u_{AB}} = 10\\hat i - 5\\hat j = 5\\hat i$<br>${\\bar a_{AB}} = 5\\hat i - 5\\hat i = 0$<br>${\\bar u_{BB}} = 0,\\,{\\bar a_{BB}} = 0$<br>The relative motion is shown in the below  diagram <br>${s_r} = {u_r}t + \\frac{1}{2}{a_r}{t^2}$<br>$100 = 5(t) \\Rightarrow t = 20 \\, s$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/3_39387_question_1.png'),
(39388,'physics',31,1344,3228,'A transverse wave propagating on a stretched string of linear density $3 \\times {10^{ - 2}}\\,kg - {m^{ - 1}}$ is represented by the equation $y=0.2 \\sin (1.5 x+60 t)$. Where, $x$ is in meters and $t$ is in seconds. The tension in the string (in newton) is:','[{"text": "0.48", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "0.24", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "1.80", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "1.20", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Equation of wave,<br>$y=0.2 \\sin (1.5 x+60 t)$<br>Comparing with standard equation, we get<br>$y=A \\sin (k x+\\omega t) \\Rightarrow k=1.5 \\text { and } \\omega=60$<br>$\\therefore {\\text{Velocity}}\\,{\\text{of}}\\,{\\text{wave}},{\\text{ }}v = \\frac{\\omega }{k} = \\frac{{60}}{{1.5}} = 40\\,m/s$<br>$\\Rightarrow v=\\sqrt{\\dfrac{T}{\\mu}}$<br>Where, $\\mu$ is linear density and $T$ is tension in the string.<br>So, $T=v^{2} \\mu=(40)^{2} \\times 3 \\times 10^{-4}=0.48 N$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(39389,'physics',30,1362,3270,'A mass $M$ is attached to a horizontal spring of force constant $k$ fixed on one side to a rigid support as shown figure. The mass oscillates on a frictionless surface with time period $T$ and amplitude A. When the mass is in equilibrium position, another mass $m$ is gently placed on it and both stick together. What will be the new amplitude of oscillation?','[{"text": "$A \\\\sqrt{\\\\left(\\\\dfrac{M-m}{M}\\\\right)}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$A \\\\sqrt{\\\\left(\\\\dfrac{M}{M-m}\\\\right)}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$A \\\\sqrt{\\\\left(\\\\dfrac{M}{M+m}\\\\right)}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$A \\\\sqrt{\\\\left(\\\\dfrac{M+m}{M}\\\\right)}$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'When a mass $m$ is placed on mass $M$, the new system is of mass $=(M+m)$, attached to the spring. New time period of oscillation,<br>$\\begin{aligned}& T^{\\prime}=2 \\pi \\sqrt{\\dfrac{M+m}{k}} \\\\& T=2 \\pi \\sqrt{\\dfrac{M}{k}}\\end{aligned}$<br>Let $v=$ velocity of mass $\\mathrm{M}$ while passing through the mean position.<br>$v^{\\prime}=$ velocity of mass $(M+m)$, while passing through the mean position.<br>According to law of conservation of linear momentum<br>$M v=(M+m) v^{\\prime}$<br>At mean position, $v=A \\omega=$ and $v^{\\prime}=A^{\\prime} \\omega^{\\prime}$<br>$\\therefore M A \\omega=(M+m) A^{\\prime} \\omega^{\\prime}$<br><br>$\\text { or } \\begin{aligned}A^{\\prime} & =\\left(\\dfrac{M}{M+m}\\right) \\dfrac{\\omega}{\\omega^{\\prime}} A=\\dfrac{M}{M+m} \\times \\dfrac{T^{\\prime}}{T} \\times A \\\\& =\\left(\\dfrac{M}{M+m}\\right) \\times \\sqrt{\\dfrac{M+m}{M}} \\times A=A \\sqrt{\\dfrac{M}{M+m}}\\end{aligned}$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/30_39389_question_1.jpg'),
(39390,'physics',3,311,573,'Consider a body under S.H.M with amplitude  equal to 4 $cm$. The displacement of the body as a function of time is $x = 4cm\\sin \\left( {\\frac{\\pi }{2}t} \\right)$. The average speed and average velocity of the body in time from $t = 0s\\,{\\rm{to}}\\,t = 2s\\,$ are','[{"text": "$2\\\\,cm/s,2\\\\,cm/s$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$0,2\\\\,cm/s$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$4\\\\,cm/s,0$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$2\\\\,cm/s,4\\\\,cm/s$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'${\\rm{Avg}}\\,{\\rm{speed}} = \\frac{{{\\rm{Distance}}}}{{{\\rm{time}}}} = \\frac{{8 \\, cm}}{{2 \\, s}} = 4 \\, cm/s$<br>${\\rm{Avg}}\\,{\\rm{velocity}} = \\frac{{{\\rm{Displacement}}}}{{{\\rm{time}}}} = 0$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(39391,'physics',31,1343,3226,'A series of ocean waves, each $5.0\\,m$ from crest to crest, moving past the observer at a rate of 2 waves per second, what is the velocity of ocean waves?','[{"text": "$5.0\\\\,m/s$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2.5\\\\,m/s$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$10.0\\\\,m/s$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$8.0\\\\,m/s$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Here, $\\lambda=5.0 m, n=2$<br>$\\therefore v = n\\lambda  = 2 \\times 5.0 = 10.0\\,m/s$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(39392,'physics',30,1362,3270,'A block of mass $M$ is suspended from a light spring of force constant $k$. Another mass $m$ moving upwards with velocity $v$ shifts the mass $M$ and gets embeded in it. What will be the amplitude of the combined mass?','[{"text": "$\\\\dfrac{m v}{\\\\sqrt{(M-m) k}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{m v}{k \\\\sqrt{(M-m)}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{m v}{\\\\sqrt{(M+m) k}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{m v}{k \\\\sqrt{(M+m)}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'If $v^{\\prime}$ are the velocities of the block of mass $M$ and $(M+m)$ while passing from the mean position when executing SHM.<br>Using law of conservation of linear momentum, we have<br>$\\begin{aligned}m v & =(M+m) v^{\\prime} \\\\\\text { or } \\quad v^{\\prime} & =m v /(M+m)\\end{aligned}$<br>Also, maximum $PE = $ maximum $KE$<br>$\\begin{gathered}\\therefore \\quad \\dfrac{1}{2} k A^{\\prime 2}=\\dfrac{1}{2}(M+m) v^{\\prime 2} \\\\A^{\\prime}=\\left(\\dfrac{M+m}{k}\\right)^{1 / 2} \\times \\dfrac{m v}{(M+m)}=\\dfrac{m v}{\\sqrt{(M+m) k}}\\end{gathered}$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(39393,'physics',3,311,573,'A particle moves along a straight line and its velocity  depends on time as $v = 4t - {t^2}$. Then for first 5 $s$ the','[{"text": "Average velocity is $25/3\\\\,m/s$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Average speed is $10\\\\,m/s$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Average velocity is $5/3\\\\,m/s$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Average velocity is Zero", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'$v = 4t - {t^2}$<br>Average velocity is <br>$\\left\\langle v \\right\\rangle  = \\frac{{\\int\\limits_0^5 {vdt} }}{5} = \\frac{{\\int\\limits_0^5 {{{(4t - t)}^2}dt} }}{5} = \\frac{5}{3} \\, m/s$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(39394,'physics',31,1345,3229,'Two interfering waves have intensities in the ratio $9: 1$. Then the ratio of maximum to minimum intensity is','[{"text": "$16: 4$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$10: 8$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$100: 64$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$4: 2$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'$\\dfrac{I_{\\max }}{I_{\\min }}=\\left(\\dfrac{\\sqrt{I_{1}}+\\sqrt{I_{2}}}{\\sqrt{I_{1}}-\\sqrt{I_{2}}}\\right)^{2}=\\left(\\dfrac{\\sqrt{9}+1}{\\sqrt{9}-1}\\right)^{2}$<br>$ = {\\left( {\\frac{4}{2}} \\right)^2} = \\frac{{16}}{4} = 4$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(39395,'physics',30,1362,3271,'A particle of mass $m$ is attached to three identical springs $A,B$ and $C$ each of force constant $k$ as shown in figure. If the particle of mass $m$ is pushed slightly against the spring A and released then the time period of oscillations is (The particle is on a frictionless horizontal surface).','[{"text": "$2 \\\\pi \\\\sqrt{\\\\dfrac{m}{2 k}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$2 \\\\pi \\\\sqrt{\\\\dfrac{2 m}{k}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2 \\\\pi \\\\sqrt{\\\\dfrac{m}{3 k}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2 \\\\pi \\\\sqrt{\\\\dfrac{m}{k}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'When the particle of mass $m$ at $O$ is pushed by y in the direction of $A$ the spring $A$ will be compressed by y while spring $B$ and $C$ will be stretched by $y^{\\prime}=y \\cos 45^{\\circ}$. So that the total restoring force on the mass $m$ along $O A$. <img src=\'/img/data/physics/30_39395_explanation_1.jpg\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' /> $F_{\\text {net }}=F_{A}+F_{B} \\cos 45^{\\circ}+F_{C} \\cos 45^{\\circ}$<br>$=k y+2 k y^{\\prime} \\cos 45^{\\circ}=k y+2 k\\left(y \\cos 45^{\\circ}\\right) \\cos 45^{\\circ}=2 k y$<br>Also $F_{\\text {net }}=k^{\\prime} y \\Rightarrow k^{\\prime} y=2 k y \\Rightarrow k^{\\prime}=2 k$<br>$T=2 \\pi \\sqrt{\\dfrac{m}{k^{\\prime}}}=2 \\pi \\sqrt{\\dfrac{m}{2 k}}$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/30_39395_question_1.jpg'),
(39396,'physics',3,311,573,'A particle is projected vertically upward with velocity $u$ from a point $A$, when it returns to the point of projection','[{"text": "Its average speed is $u/2$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Average velocity is $u$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Distance covered is zero", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Its average speed is $u$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'The average speed $ = \\frac{{2H}}{T} = \\frac{{{\\rm{Total}}{\\kern 1pt} {\\kern 1pt} {\\rm{distance}}}}{{{\\rm{Time}}{\\kern 1pt} {\\kern 1pt} {\\rm{of}}{\\kern 1pt} {\\kern 1pt} {\\rm{flight}}}}$<br>$H = \\frac{{{u^2}}}{{2g}}$ (maximum height reached by the body)<br>$T = \\frac{{2u}}{g}$ (Time of flight)<br>${\\rm{Average}}\\,{\\rm{speed}} = \\frac{{2 \\cdot \\frac{{{u^2}}}{{2g}}}}{{\\left( {\\frac{{2u}}{g}} \\right)}} = \\frac{u}{2}$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(39397,'physics',31,1345,3229,'Two periodic waves of amplitude $A_{1}$ and $A_{2}$ pass through a region. If $A_{1}>A_{2}$ the difference in the maximum and minimum resultant amplitude possible is','[{"text": "$2 A_{2}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$A_{1}-A_{2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2 A_{1}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$A_{1}+A_{2}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'$A_{\\max }=A_{1}+A_{2} ; A_{\\min }=A_{1}-A_{2}$<br>$\\therefore \\quad A_{\\text {max }}-A_{\\text {min }}=A_{1}+A_{2}-A_{1}+A_{2}=2 A_{2}$.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(39398,'physics',30,1362,3272,'Consider a liquid which fills a uniform U-tube, as shown in fig up to a height $h$. Find angular frequency of small oscillations of the liquid in the U-tube.','[{"text": "$\\\\sqrt{g / 2 h}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\sqrt{g / h}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\sqrt{2 g / h}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\sqrt{3 g / 2 h}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Suppose that the liquid is displaced slightly from equilibrium so that its level rises in one arm of the tube, while it is depressed in the second arm by the same amount, $x$. <img src=\'/img/data/physics/30_39398_explanation_1.jpg\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' /> If the density of the liquid is $\\rho$, then the total mechanical energy of the liquid column is<br>The difference in the two levels is $2 x$. So the column having length $2 x$ produces restoring force on the entire liquid.<br>$\\begin{aligned}&amp; \\rho A 2 x g=\\rho A 2 h a \\\\&amp; a=-\\left(\\dfrac{g}{h}\\right) x \\Rightarrow \\omega=\\sqrt{\\dfrac{g}{h}}\\end{aligned}$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/30_39398_question_1.jpg'),
(39399,'physics',3,311,573,'A car starts from rest and moves with uniform acceleration $a$ on a straight road from time $t = 0\\,{\\rm{to}}\\,t = T$. After that, a constant deceleration brings it to rest. In this process the average speed of the car is','[{"text": "$aT$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{{aT}}{2}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{{3aT}}{2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{{aT}}{4}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'For first part,<br>$u = 0,t = T$ and acceleration $ = a$<br>$\\therefore v = 0 + aT = aT\\,{\\rm{and}}\\,{S_1} = 0 + \\frac{1}{2}a{T^2} = \\frac{1}{2}a{T^2}$<br>For second part,<br>$u = aT,$ retardation $ = {a_1},v = 0$ and time taken<br>$ = {T_1}({\\rm{let}})$<br>$\\therefore 0 = u - {a_1}{T_1} \\Rightarrow aT = {a_1}{T_1}$<br>and from ${v^2} - {u^2} = 2a{S_2} \\Rightarrow {S_2} = \\frac{{{u^2}}}{{2{a_1}}} = \\frac{1}{2}\\frac{{{a^2}{T^2}}}{{{a_1}}}$<br>${S_2} = \\frac{1}{2}aT \\times {T_1}\\quad \\left( {As\\,{a_1} = \\frac{{aT}}{{{T_1}}}} \\right)$<br>$\\therefore {v_{av}} = \\frac{{{S_1} + {S_2}}}{{T + {T_1}}} = \\frac{{\\frac{1}{2}a{T^2} + \\frac{1}{2}aT \\times {T_1}}}{{T + {T_1}}}$<br>$ = \\frac{{\\frac{1}{2}aT\\left( {T + {T_1}} \\right)}}{{T + {T_1}}} = \\frac{1}{2}aT.$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(39400,'physics',31,1345,3229,'Two waves represented by $y=a \\sin (\\omega t-k x)$ and $y_{2}=\\sqrt{3} a \\cos (\\omega t-k x)$ are superposed. The amplitude of the resultant wave is','[{"text": "$2 a$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$(2+\\\\sqrt{3}) a$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$(2-\\\\sqrt{3}) a$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2 \\\\sqrt{3} a$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Path difference $=\\pi / 2$<br>$\\therefore \\quad a_{R}=\\sqrt{a^{2}+(\\sqrt{3} a)^{2}+2 \\cdot a \\cdot \\sqrt{3} a \\cdot \\cos \\pi / 2}=2 a$.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(39401,'physics',30,1361,3268,'Three simple harmonic motions, of equal amplitudes $A$ and equal time periods, along the same line combine. The phase of the second motion is $60^{\\circ}$ ahead of the first and phase of the third motion is $60^{\\circ}$ ahead of the second. The amplitude of resultant motion is ____.','[{"text": "$\\\\dfrac{A}{2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\sqrt{3} A$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2 \\\\sqrt{2} A$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2\\\\,A$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'vector diagram of amplitudes is given below. <img src=\'/img/data/physics/30_39401_explanation_1.jpg\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' /> By treating the SHMs as vector<br>$\\bar{A}_{1}=A \\hat{i} ; \\bar{A}_{2}=\\dfrac{A}{2} \\hat{i}+\\dfrac{\\sqrt{3} A}{2} \\hat{j} ; \\bar{A}_{3}=-\\dfrac{A}{2} \\hat{i}+\\dfrac{\\sqrt{3} A}{2} \\hat{j}$<br>The resultant amplitude is $\\bar{A}_{R}=A \\hat{i}+\\sqrt{3} A \\hat{j}$<br>$A_{R}=2 A$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(39402,'physics',3,313,577,'A man throws balls with the same speed vertically upwards one after the other at an   interval of 2 $s$. What should be the speed of the throw so that more than two balls are in the sky at any  time? (Given $g$  =  9.8 $m^{-2}$)','[{"text": "Any speed less than $19.6\\\\,m{s^{ - 1}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Only with speed $19.6\\\\,m{s^{ - 1}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "More than $19.6\\\\,m{s^{ - 1}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "At least $9.8\\\\,m{s^{ - 1}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Let time taken by ball to reach maximum height be $T$.<br>Also $v = u - gT$<br>At maximum height, $v = 0\\,$<br>So, $u = gT$<br>$u = 2 \\times 9.8 = 19.6 \\, ms^{-1}$<br>hence, the speed should be more than<br>$19.6 \\, ms^{-1}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(39403,'physics',31,1345,3229,'The resultant amplitude due to superposition of two waves $y_{1}=5 \\sin (\\omega t-k x)$ and $y_{2}=-5 \\cos \\left(\\omega t-k x-150^{\\circ}\\right)$','[{"text": "$5 \\\\sqrt{3}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "5", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$5 \\\\sqrt{2+\\\\sqrt{3}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$5 \\\\sqrt{2-\\\\sqrt{3}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'$y_{1}=5 \\sin (\\omega t-k x)$<br>$\\begin{aligned}& y_{2}=5 \\sin [-(\\omega t-k x-150)] \\\\& y_{2}=5 \\sin \\left(\\omega t-k x+120^{\\circ}\\right)\\end{aligned}$<br>$\\therefore$ Resultant $=\\sqrt{5^{2}+5^{2}+2.5^{2} \\cos 120^{\\circ}}=5$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(39404,'physics',30,1361,3268,'The resultant amplitude due to superposition of three simple harmonic motions ${y_1} = 3\\sin \\omega t{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {y_2} = 5\\sin \\omega t + {37^{\\rm{o}}}$ and ${y_3} =  - 15\\cos \\omega t$ is ____.','[{"text": "$\\\\sqrt{193}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\sqrt{73}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$8 \\\\sqrt{2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$7 \\\\sqrt{3}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'$y_{3}=-15 \\cos \\omega t=-15 \\sin \\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}+\\omega t\\right)$<br>$=15 \\sin \\left(\\omega t+\\dfrac{3 \\pi}{2}\\right)=15 \\sin \\left(\\omega t+270^{\\circ}\\right)$<br>$\\therefore$ Vector diagram of amplitudes is: <img src=\'/img/data/physics/30_39404_explanation_1.jpg\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' /> $\\Sigma A_{x}=3+5 \\cos 37^{\\circ}=7$<br>$\\Sigma A_{y}=-15+5 \\sin 37^{\\circ}=-12$<br>$\\therefore A=\\sqrt{\\left(\\Sigma A_{x}\\right)^{2}+\\left(\\Sigma A_{y}\\right)^{2}}=\\sqrt{7^{2}+12^{2}}=\\sqrt{193}$ units',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(39405,'physics',3,313,577,'A body is projected vertically downwards from $A$, the top of the tower, reaches the ground in ${t_1}$ seconds. If it is projected upwards with same speed, it reaches the ground in ${t_2}$ seconds. At what time will it reach the ground if it is dropped from $A$? <br>$[{t_1} = 8\\,{\\rm{units}},\\,{t_2} = 16\\,{\\rm{units}}]$','[{"text": "$4\\\\sqrt {2\\\\,} {\\\\rm{units}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$3\\\\sqrt {2\\\\,} {\\\\rm{units}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$8\\\\sqrt {2\\\\,} {\\\\rm{units}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$16\\\\sqrt {2\\\\,} {\\\\rm{units}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'For downward motion,<br>$h = u{t_1} + \\frac{1}{2}gt_1^2$  &emsp; (1)<br>For upward motion,<br>$h = u{t_2} + \\frac{1}{2}gt_2^2$ &emsp; (2)<br>From (1) & (2)<br>$u{t_1} + \\frac{1}{2}gt_1^2 =  - u{t_2} + \\frac{1}{2}gt_2^2$<br>$ \\Rightarrow \\quad u\\left( {{t_1} + {t_2}} \\right) = \\frac{1}{2}g\\left( {t_2^2 - t_1^2} \\right)\\,$<br>$\\therefore \\quad h = \\frac{g}{2}\\left( {{t_1} - {t_1}} \\right){t_1} + \\frac{g}{2}gt_1^2 = \\frac{{g{t_2}{t_1}}}{2}$<br>$\\therefore \\,$ Required time $t = \\sqrt {\\frac{{2h}}{g}}  = \\sqrt {{t_1}{t_2}} $<br>$ = \\sqrt {8 \\times 16}  = 8\\sqrt 2 \\,$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(39406,'physics',31,1345,3232,'Standing waves can be produced','[{"text": "On a string clamped at both ends", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "On a string clamped at one end and free at the other", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "When incident wave gets reflected from a wall", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "All the above", "isCorrect": true}]',3,NULL,NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(39408,'physics',3,313,577,'A frictionless wire $AB$ is fixed on a sphere of radius $R$. A very small spherial ball slips on this wire. The time taken by this ball to slip from $A$ to $B$ is','[{"text": "$2\\\\sqrt {\\\\frac{R}{g}} $", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{{gR}}{{\\\\sqrt {g\\\\cos \\\\theta } }}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{{2\\\\sqrt {gR} }}{{g\\\\cos \\\\theta }}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2\\\\sqrt {gR} .\\\\frac{{\\\\cos \\\\theta }}{g}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Acceleration of a body along $AB$ is $g\\,\\cos \\theta \\,$<br>Distance travelled in time <br>$t\\sec  = AB = \\frac{1}{2}(g\\cos \\theta ){t^2}$<br>From <br>$\\Delta ABC,AB = 2R\\cos \\theta  \\Rightarrow 2R\\cos \\theta  = \\frac{1}{2}g\\cos \\theta {t^2}$<br>$ \\Rightarrow {t^2} = \\frac{{4R}}{g}{\\rm{ or }}t = 2\\sqrt {\\frac{R}{g}} \\,$<br> Here ‘$t$’ is independent of $\'\\theta \'\\,$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/3_39408_question_1.png'),
(39409,'physics',31,1345,3232,'In a stationary wave all the particles','[{"text": "In the region between two antinodes vibrate in same phase", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Of the medium vibrate in same phase", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "On either side of a node vibrate in same phase", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "In the region between two nodes vibrate in same phase", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'In stationary wave all the particles in one particular segment (i.e., between two nodes) vibrates in the same phase.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(39411,'physics',3,313,576,'Two cars $A$ and $B$ are travelling in the same direction with velocities ${v_{\\rm{A}}}\\,{\\rm{and}}\\,{v_B}\\left( {{v_{\\rm{A}}} > {v_{\\rm{B}}}} \\right)$. The initial seperation between the cars is $d$.  When the car $A$ applies brakes producing a uniform retardation $a$. There will be no collision when','[{"text": "$d  < \\\\frac{(v_A - v_B)^2}{2a}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$d  < \\\\frac{{v^{2}_A} - {{v^{2}}_B}}{2a}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$d  >  \\\\frac{(v_A - v_B)^2}{2a}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$d  > \\\\frac{{v^{2}_A} - {{v^{2}}_B}}{2a}$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Let $d$ is the initial separation between the cars.<br>When the two cars meet each other.<br>${v_A}t - \\frac{1}{2}a{t^2} = d + {v_B}t$<br>$\\frac{a}{2}{t^2} + \\left( {{v_B} - {v_A}} \\right)t + d = 0$<br>The cars meet when <br>${\\left( {{v_B} - {v_A}} \\right)^2} - 4\\frac{a}{2}d \\ge 0$<br>${\\left( {{v_B} - {v_A}} \\right)^2} \\ge 2ad$<br>$d \\le \\frac{{{{\\left( {{v_A} - {v_B}} \\right)}^2}}}{{2a}}$<br>The cars will not collide if $d > \\frac{{{{\\left( {{v_A} - {v_B}} \\right)}^2}}}{{2a}}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(39416,'physics',30,1359,3263,'The displacement-time equation of particle executing SHM is: $x=A \\sin (\\omega t+\\phi)$. At time $t = 0$, position of the particle is $x = A/2$ and it is moving along negative $x$-direction. Then the angle $\\phi$ can be:','[{"text": "$\\\\dfrac{\\\\pi}{6}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{\\\\pi}{4}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{5 \\\\pi}{6}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{7 \\\\pi}{6}$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'At $t=0, x=\\dfrac{A}{2}$<br>$\\dfrac{A}{2}=A \\sin \\phi \\text { or } \\phi=\\dfrac{\\pi}{6} \\text { or } \\phi=\\dfrac{5 \\pi}{6}$<br><br>$v=\\dfrac{d x}{d t}=A \\omega \\cos (\\omega t+\\phi)$<br>At $t=0, v=A \\omega \\cos \\phi$<br>Now, $\\cos \\dfrac{\\pi}{6}=\\dfrac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}$ and $\\cos \\dfrac{5 \\pi}{6}=-\\dfrac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}$<br>As $v$ is negative at $t=0, \\phi$ must be $5 \\pi / 6$.',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(39417,'physics',3,313,578,'A particle of unit mass undergoes one-dimensional motion such that its velocity varies according to $v\\left( x \\right) = b{x^{ - 2n}}$. Where $b$ and $n$ are  constants and $x$ is the position of the particle. The acceleration of the particle as function of $x$, is given by','[{"text": "$ - 2{b^2}{x^{ - 2n - 1}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$ - 2{b^2}{x^{ - 4n - 1}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$ - 2n{b^2}{x^{ - 2n + 1}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$ - 2{b^2}{x^{ - 4n + 1}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'$v\\left( x \\right) = b{x^{ - 2n}}$<br>$a = v\\frac{{dv}}{{dx}} = b{x^{ - 2n}}\\{ b\\left( { - 2n} \\right){x^{ - 2n - 1}}\\}  =  - 2{b^2}n{x^{ - 4n - 1}}.$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(39418,'physics',31,1345,3232,'At a certain instant a stationary transverse wave is found to have maximum potential energy. The appearance of the string at that instant is a (each of the component waves have amplitude $A$)','[{"text": "Sinusoidal shape with amplitude $A/3$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Sinusoidal shape with amplitude $A / 2$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Sinusoidal shape with amplitude $2\\\\,A$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Straight line", "isCorrect": false}]',2,NULL,NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(39419,'physics',30,1359,3263,'Which of the following functions represents a simple harmonic oscillation?','[{"text": "$\\\\sin \\\\omega t+\\\\sin (3 \\\\omega t)$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\sin \\\\omega t-\\\\cos \\\\omega t$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\sin \\\\omega t-\\\\sin 2 \\\\omega t$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\sin \\\\omega t+\\\\sin 2 \\\\omega t$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'One of the condition for SHM is that restoring force $(F)$ and hence acceleration $(a)$ should be proportional to displacement $(y)$.<br>Let, $\\quad y=\\sin \\omega t-\\cos \\omega t$<br>$\\begin{gathered}\\dfrac{d y}{d t}=\\omega \\cos \\omega t+\\omega \\sin \\omega t \\\\\\dfrac{d^{2} y}{d t^{2}}=-\\omega^{2} \\cos \\omega t+\\omega^{2} \\cos \\omega t \\\\\\text { or } \\quad a=-\\omega^{2}(\\sin \\omega t-\\cos \\omega t) \\\\a=-\\omega^{2} y \\Rightarrow a \\propto-y\\end{gathered}$<br>This satisfies the condition of SHM, other equations do not satisfy this condition.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(39420,'physics',3,312,574,'A car accelerates from rest at a constant for some time, after which it decelerates at a constant rate $\\beta $ and comes to rest. If the total time elapsed is $t$, then the distance travelled by the car is','[{"text": "$\\\\left( {\\\\frac{{{\\\\alpha ^2} + {\\\\beta ^2}}}{{\\\\alpha \\\\beta }}} \\\\right)t$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\left( {\\\\frac{{{\\\\alpha ^2} - {\\\\beta ^2}}}{{\\\\alpha \\\\beta }}} \\\\right)t$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\left( {\\\\frac{{\\\\alpha + \\\\beta }}{{\\\\alpha \\\\beta }}} \\\\right)t$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{1}{2}\\\\frac{{\\\\alpha \\\\beta {t^2}}}{{\\\\left( {\\\\alpha + \\\\beta } \\\\right)}}$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Let the car accelerate at rate $\\alpha $ for time ${t_1}$ then maximum velocity attained, <br>$v = 0 + \\alpha {t_1} = \\alpha {t_1}$<br>Now, the car decelerates at a rate $\\beta $ for time $\\left( {t - {t_1}} \\right)$ and finally comes to rest. Then,<br>$0 = v\\beta \\left( {t - {t_1}} \\right) \\Rightarrow 0 = \\alpha {t_1} - \\beta t + \\beta {t_1}$<br>$ \\Rightarrow {t_1} = \\frac{\\beta }{{\\alpha + \\beta }}t;\\,{S_1} = \\frac{1}{2}\\alpha \\,t_1^2$<br>${S_2} = \\frac{1}{2}\\beta t_2^2$<br>$ \\Rightarrow {S_1} + {S_2} = \\frac{1}{2}\\left( {\\alpha t_1^2 + \\beta t_2^2} \\right)$<br>$S = \\frac{1}{2}\\frac{{\\alpha \\beta }}{{\\alpha + \\beta }}{t^2}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(39421,'physics',31,1344,3228,'$\\lambda$ is maximum wavelength of a transverse wave that travels along a stretched wire whose two ends are fixed. The length of that wire is','[{"text": "$2 \\\\lambda$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\lambda$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\lambda / 2$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$3 \\\\lambda / 2$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'The string must be in fundamental mode since $\\lambda$ is maximum $\\Rightarrow \\lambda=2 l$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(39422,'physics',30,1359,3263,'A particle moves with simple harmonic motion in a straight line. In first $\\tau \\,s$, after starting from rest it travels a distance $a$, and in next $\\tau \\,s$ it travels $2 a$, in same direction, then :','[{"text": "amplitude of motion is $4 a$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "time period of oscillations is $6\\\\,\\\\tau $", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "amplitude of motion is $3 a$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "time period of oscillations is $8\\\\,\\\\tau $", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'The displacement of the particle is<br>$x=A \\cos \\omega t$<br>Given that $A(1 - \\cos \\omega \\tau ) = a$<br>$\\begin{aligned}& \\text { A }(1-\\cos 2 \\omega \\tau)=3 a \\\\& \\cos \\omega \\tau=\\left(1-\\dfrac{a}{A}\\right) \\\\& \\cos 2 \\omega \\tau=\\left(1-\\dfrac{3 a}{A}\\right) \\\\& 2\\left(1-\\dfrac{a}{A}\\right)^{2}-1=1-\\dfrac{3 a}{A} \\\\& \\dfrac{a}{A}=\\dfrac{1}{2} \\Rightarrow A=2 a \\\\& \\cos \\omega \\tau=\\dfrac{1}{2} \\\\& T=6 \\tau\\end{aligned}$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(39423,'physics',3,312,574,'A body travels for $12\\,\\sec $ starting from rest with constant acceleration. If it travels distance  ${S_1},{S_2}\\,{\\mathop{\\rm and}\\nolimits} \\,{S_3}$ in the first four seconds, second four seconds and next four seconds respectively the relation between ${S_1},{S_2}\\,{\\mathop{\\rm and}\\nolimits} \\,{S_3}$ is','[{"text": "${S_1} = \\\\frac{1}{5}{S_2} = \\\\frac{1}{3}{S_3}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$5{S_1} = 3{S_2} = {S_3}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${S_1} = \\\\frac{1}{3}{S_2} = \\\\frac{1}{5}{S_3}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${S_1} = {S_2} = {S_3}$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'If the body starts from rest and moves with constant acceleration then the ratio of distance in consecutive equal time intervals is <br>${S_1} = \\frac{1}{2}a({4^2})$<br>${S_2} = \\frac{1}{2}a({8^2} - {4^2})$<br>${S_3} = \\frac{1}{2}a({12^2} - {8^2})$<br>${S_1}:{S_2}:{S_3} = 1:3:5.$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(39462,'physics',32,486,1000,'In the adjacent figure, half of the shape between the plates of a parallel plate capacitor is filled with dielectric material of constant \\[K\\]. Then which of the plot is possible:<div class="matching-question"></div>','[{"text": "", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Suppose field in air gap is ${E_0}$ then in dielectric it is $\\frac{{{E_0}}}{K}$<br>For dielectric medium $dV =  - \\frac{{{E_0}}}{K}d{\\rm{x}} \\Rightarrow {\\rm{V = }} - \\frac{{{E_0}}}{K}{\\rm{x}}$<br>For air gap medium $V =  - {E_0}x$<br>The slope is more for air medium.',NULL,'Hard','Graph/Figure',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/32_39462_question_1.png'),
(39424,'physics',30,1359,3265,'A particle executes SHM with $x=A \\sin \\omega t$. Find the average acceleration of the particle from $t=0$ to $t=\\dfrac{T}{4}$.','[{"text": "$\\\\dfrac{6 \\\\pi A}{T^{2}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{8 \\\\pi A}{T^{2}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{4 \\\\pi A}{T^{2}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{\\\\pi A}{4 T^{2}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'$x=A \\sin \\omega t$<br>$a =  - {\\omega ^2}A\\sin \\omega t$<br>$ < a >  = \\frac{{\\int_0^{T/4}  -  {\\omega ^2}A\\sin (\\omega t)dt}}{{\\frac{T}{4} - 0}} = \\frac{{ - 4{\\omega ^2}{A^{T/4}}}}{T}\\int_0^T {\\sin } (\\omega t)dt$<br>$ < a >  = \\frac{{4{\\omega ^2}A}}{{\\omega T}}[\\cos (\\omega t)]_0^{t/4}$<br>$ < a >  = \\frac{{4\\omega A}}{T}\\left[ {\\cos \\frac{{2\\pi }}{T}\\frac{T}{4} - \\cos 0} \\right]_0^{t/4}$<br>$ = \\frac{{ - 4A\\omega }}{T} = \\frac{{ - 8\\pi A}}{{{T^2}}}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(39425,'physics',31,1345,3232,'Which of the following statements are correct w.r.to standing waves.','[{"text": "There is no forward motion of the disturbance from one Particle to the other", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "The wavelength and time period of stationary waves are the same as for the component waves", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "The distance between two consecutive nodes is $\\\\dfrac{\\\\lambda}{2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "All the above", "isCorrect": true}]',3,NULL,NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(39426,'physics',3,309,571,'A particle located at $x = 0$ at time $t = 0$, starts  moving along the positive $x$ - direction with a velocity $\'v\'$ that varies as $v = a\\sqrt x .$ The displacement of the particle varies with time as','[{"text": "$t$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${t^{3/2}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${t^3}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${t^2}$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'$v = \\alpha \\sqrt x  \\Rightarrow \\frac{{dx}}{{dt}} = \\alpha \\sqrt x  \\Rightarrow \\frac{{dx}}{{\\sqrt x }} = \\alpha \\,dt$<br>By integrating both sides $\\int {{x^{ - 1/2}}dx = \\int {\\alpha \\,dt} } $<br>$\\therefore \\,x \\propto {t^2}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(39427,'physics',30,1359,3265,'For a particle executing simple harmonic motion, determine the ratio of average acceleration of particle from extreme position to equilibrium position to the maximum acceleration.','[{"text": "$\\\\dfrac{2}{\\\\pi}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{4}{\\\\pi}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{1}{2 \\\\pi}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{1}{\\\\pi}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Let the equation of simple harmonic motion be,<br>$x=A \\sin \\omega t$<br>Acceleration $a=-\\omega^{2} x$<br>$|a|=\\omega^{2} A \\sin \\omega t$<br>Then, the reqiured ratio is $\\dfrac{\\int_{0}^{T / 4} \\omega^{2} A \\sin \\omega t d t}{\\dfrac{T}{4} \\times \\omega^{2} A}=\\dfrac{2}{\\pi}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(39428,'physics',31,1345,3229,'A steel wire is stretched between two walls and excited in its fundamental tone. Which of the following statements is correct?','[{"text": "On increasing the temperature, frequency of fundamental tone increases", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "On decreasing the temperature frequency of fundamental tone decreases", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Frequency of fundamental tone does not vary with temperature", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "On increasing temperature frequency of fundamental tone decreases", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Frequency of fundamental mode<br>$f_{0}=\\dfrac{1}{2 l} \\sqrt{\\dfrac{T}{\\mu}}$<br>If temperature increases, $T$ decreases, $f_{0}$ decreases. If temperature decreases, $T$ increases, $f_{0}$ increases.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(39429,'physics',3,309,571,'A body is thrown vertically upwards. If air resistance is to be taken into account, then the time during which the body rises is','[{"text": "Greater than the time of fall", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Twice the time of fall", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Equal to the time of fall", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Less than the time of fall", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Let the initial velocity of ball be $u$.<br>Time of rise ${t_1} = \\frac{u}{{g + a}}$ and height reached<br>$ = \\frac{{{u^2}}}{{2(g + a)}}$<br>Time of fall is given by<br>$\\frac{1}{2}(g - a)t_2^2 = \\frac{{{u^2}}}{{2(g + a)}}$<br>$ \\Rightarrow {t_2} = \\frac{u}{{\\sqrt {(g + a)(g - a)} }}$<br>$\\therefore {t_2} > {t_1}$ because $\\frac{1}{{g + a}} < \\frac{1}{{g - a}}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(39430,'physics',30,1359,3265,'If $x, v$ and a denote the displacement, the velocity and the acceleration of a particle executing simple harmonic motion of time period $T$, then, which of the following does not change with time?','[{"text": "$a T / x$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$a T / 2 \\\\pi v$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$a T / v$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$a^{2} T^{2} / 4 \\\\pi^{2} v^{2}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'For an SHM, the acceleration $a=-\\omega^{2} x$<br>where $\\omega^{2}$ is a constant. Therefore, $\\dfrac{a}{x}$ is a constant. The time period $\\mathrm{T}$ is also constant. Therefore, $\\dfrac{a T}{x}$ is a constant.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(39431,'physics',31,1345,3232,'Consider a string fixed at one end and free at the other end. The frequency of the 3rd overtone is ( $l$ is the length and $v$ is the velocity)','[{"text": "$\\\\dfrac{7 v}{4 l}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{3 v}{4 l}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{5 v}{4 l}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{v}{4 l}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'The frequency of the ${3^{rd}}$ overtone is<br>$f=\\dfrac{(2 n+1) v}{4 l}=\\dfrac{7 v}{4 l} \\quad(n=3)$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(39432,'physics',3,313,578,'Acceleration is given as a function of displacement $a = 2x - 3{x^2}$ starting with velocity is $2m/s$. Find the final velocity after a  displacement from 0 to 3 $m$.','[{"text": "$6\\\\,m{s^{ - 1}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$4\\\\sqrt 2 \\\\,m{s^{ - 1}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$5\\\\,m{s^{ - 1}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{None}}\\\\,{\\\\rm{of}}\\\\,{\\\\rm{these}}$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Given that $a = 2x - 3{x^2}$<br>$\\frac{{dv}}{{dt}} = \\frac{{dv}}{{dx}}\\frac{{dx}}{{dt}} = \\frac{{dv}}{{dx}}v = 2x - 3{x^2}$<br>$\\int\\limits_2^v {vdv = \\int\\limits_0^3 {\\left( {2x - 3{x^2}} \\right)dx} } $<br>$\\frac{{{v^2} - 4}}{2} = \\left[ {{x^2} - {x^3}} \\right]_0^3$<br>${v^2} - 4 =  - 36$<br>${v^2} =  - 32$<br>Velocity cannot be imaginary that means particles cannot move from 0 to 3 $m$. Particle will stop before reaching 3 $m$.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(39433,'physics',30,1359,3266,'Two simple harmonic motions are given by $x_{1}=a \\sin \\omega t+a \\cos \\omega t$ and $x_{2}=a \\sin \\omega t+\\dfrac{a}{\\sqrt{3}} \\cos \\omega t$.<br>The ratio of the amplitudes of first and second motion and the phase difference between them are respectively','[{"text": "$\\\\dfrac{\\\\sqrt{3}}{2}$ and $\\\\dfrac{\\\\pi}{12}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\sqrt{\\\\dfrac{3}{2}}$ and $\\\\dfrac{\\\\pi}{12}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\sqrt{\\\\dfrac{3}{2}}$ and $\\\\dfrac{\\\\pi}{6}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{2}{\\\\sqrt{3}}$ and $\\\\dfrac{\\\\pi}{12}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'<img src=\'/img/data/physics/30_39433_explanation_1.jpg\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(39435,'physics',3,314,579,'It takes one minute for a passenger standing on an escalator to reach the top. If the escalator does not move it takes him 3 minute to walk up. How long will it take for the passenger to arrive at the top if he walks up the moving escalator?','[{"text": "$40\\\\,\\\\sec $", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$35\\\\,\\\\sec $", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$30\\\\,\\\\sec $", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$45\\\\,\\\\sec $", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Let speed of elevator be ${v_e}$<br>${t_1} = \\frac{L}{{{v_e}}} \\Rightarrow 1\\min  = \\frac{L}{{{v_e}}}$<br>Let speed of person on stationary elevator be ${v_P}$ then ${t_2} = \\frac{L}{{{v_P}}} \\Rightarrow 3\\min  = \\frac{L}{{{v_P}}}$<br>when the escalator is moving:<br>${t_3} = \\frac{L}{{{v_e} + {v_P}}}\\frac{L}{{\\frac{L}{{1\\min }} + \\frac{L}{{3\\min }}}} = \\frac{3}{4}\\min  = 45 \\, s$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(39436,'physics',30,1359,3266,'The equation describing the motion of a simple harmonic oscillator along the $x$-axis is given as: $x=A \\cos (\\omega t+\\phi)$. If at time $t = 0$, the oscillator is at $x = 0$, and moving in the negative $t$ direction, then the phase angle $\\phi$ is','[{"text": "$-\\\\pi / 2$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\pi / 2$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "0", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\pi$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'$x=A \\cos (\\omega t+\\phi)$<br>$\\begin{aligned}& \\text { At } t=0, x=0 \\\\& \\therefore 0=\\cos \\phi \\\\& \\cos \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}=\\cos \\phi \\quad \\therefore \\phi=\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\end{aligned}$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(39437,'physics',3,314,579,'An elevator, whose floor to ceiling distance is equal to 2.7 $m$, starts ascending with constant acceleration of $1.2\\,m{s^{ - 2}}.$ 2 sec after the start, a bolt begins falling from the ceiling of the car. The free fall time of the bolt is','[{"text": "$\\\\sqrt {0.54} s$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1s$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$0.7s$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\sqrt 6 s$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'${S_r} = \\frac{1}{2}{a_r}{t^2} \\Rightarrow t = \\sqrt {\\frac{{2.7 \\times 2}}{{11}}}  = 0.7s$<br>${S_r} = $ relative displacement of the bolt w.r.to the elevator<br>${a_r} = $ relative acceleration of bolt w.r.t. the elevator.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(39438,'physics',31,1345,3232,'If you set up the ninth harmonic on a string fixed at a both ends, its frequency compared to the seventh harmonic','[{"text": "Equal", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Higher", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Lower", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "None of these", "isCorrect": false}]',1,NULL,NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(39439,'physics',30,1360,3267,'A particle of mass $m$ moving along the $x$-axis has a potential energy $U(x)=a+b x^{2}$ where $a$ and $b$ are positive constants. It will execute simple harmonic motion with a frequency determined by the value of','[{"text": "$b$ and $a$ alone", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$b$ alone", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$b, a$ and $m$ alone", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$b$ and $m$ alone", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'$U=a+b x^{2}$<br>$\\begin{aligned}& F=-\\dfrac{d U}{d x}=-[0+2 b x] \\\\& F=m a=-2 b x \\Rightarrow a=-\\left(\\dfrac{2 b}{m}\\right) x \\\\& \\omega=2 \\pi f=\\left(\\dfrac{2 b}{m}\\right)^{1 / 2}\\end{aligned}$<br>Hence frequency depends upon $b$ and $m$.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(39440,'physics',31,1345,3232,'Two vibrating strings of same length, same cross section area and stretched to same tension is made of materials with densities $\\rho$ and $2 \\rho$. Each string is fixed at both ends. If $f_{1}$ represents the fundamental mode of vibration of the one made with density $\\rho$ and $f_{2}$ for another, then $f_{1} / f_{2}$ is','[{"text": "$\\\\sqrt{2}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{1}{\\\\sqrt{2}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\sqrt{2}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Velocity of sound is inversely proportional to the square root of density of the medium.<br>$v=\\sqrt{\\dfrac{T}{\\mu}}=\\sqrt{\\dfrac{T}{\\rho A}}$<br>i.e., $v . \\rho=$ constant<br>$\\begin{aligned}& \\dfrac{v_{1}}{v_{2}}=\\sqrt{\\dfrac{\\rho_{2}}{\\rho_{1}}}=\\sqrt{\\dfrac{2 \\rho}{\\rho}}=\\sqrt{2} \\\\& \\dfrac{f_{1}}{f_{2}}=\\dfrac{v_{1}}{v_{1}}=\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(39441,'physics',3,313,577,'A ball is released from the top of a tower of height $h$ meters. It takes $T$ seconds to reach the ground. What is the position of the ball at  $\\frac{T}{3}$second','[{"text": "$\\\\frac{{8h}}{9}{\\\\rm{meters}}\\\\,{\\\\rm{from}}\\\\,{\\\\rm{the}}\\\\,{\\\\rm{ground}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{{7h}}{9}{\\\\rm{meters}}\\\\,{\\\\rm{from}}\\\\,{\\\\rm{the}}\\\\,{\\\\rm{ground}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{h}{9}{\\\\rm{meters}}\\\\,{\\\\rm{from}}\\\\,{\\\\rm{the}}\\\\,{\\\\rm{ground}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{{17h}}{9}{\\\\rm{meters}}\\\\,{\\\\rm{from}}\\\\,{\\\\rm{the}}\\\\,{\\\\rm{ground}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'We have, $s = ut + \\frac{1}{2}g{t^2}$<br>$ \\Rightarrow {\\rm{ }}h = \\frac{1}{2}g{T^2}\\,\\,\\left( {\\therefore u = 0} \\right)$<br>In $T/3$ second, vertical distance moved is given by $h\' = \\frac{1}{2}g{\\left( {\\frac{T}{3}} \\right)^2} \\Rightarrow h\' = \\frac{1}{2} \\times \\frac{{g{T^2}}}{9} = \\frac{h}{9}$<br>$\\therefore $ Position of ball from ground $ = h - \\frac{h}{9} = \\frac{{8h}}{9}$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(39442,'physics',30,1360,3267,'A particle of mass $m$ is executing oscillations about the origin on the $x$ - axis. Its potential energy is $U(x)=k[x]^{3}$, where $k$ is a positive constant. If the amplitude of oscillation is $a$, then its time period $T$ is','[{"text": "independent of $a$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "proportional to $\\\\dfrac{1}{\\\\sqrt{a}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "proportional to $a^{3 / 2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "proportional to $\\\\sqrt{a}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'$U(x)=k|x|^{3} \\Rightarrow F=-\\dfrac{d U}{d x}$<br>$F =  - 3k|x{|^2}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,(1)$<br>Also, for SHM, $x=a \\sin \\omega t$<br>and $\\dfrac{d^{2} x}{d t^{2}}+\\omega^{2} x=0$<br>$\\Rightarrow$ Acceleration, $a=\\dfrac{d^{2} x}{d t^{2}}=-\\omega^{2} x$<br>$ \\Rightarrow F = ma = m\\frac{{{d^2}x}}{{d{t^2}}} =  - m{\\omega ^2}x\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,(2)$<br>From Eqs. (1) and (2), we get $\\omega=\\sqrt{\\dfrac{3 k x}{m}}$<br>$\\begin{aligned}& \\Rightarrow T=\\dfrac{2 \\pi}{\\omega}=2 \\pi \\sqrt{\\dfrac{m}{3 k x}}=2 \\pi \\sqrt{\\dfrac{m}{3 k(a \\sin \\omega t)}} \\\\& \\Rightarrow T \\propto \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{a}}\\end{aligned}$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(39443,'physics',31,1345,3232,'The particle displacement (in $cm$ ) in a stationary wave is given by $y(x, t)=2 \\sin (0.1 \\pi x) \\cos (100 \\pi t)$. The distance between a node and the next antiode is','[{"text": "$10\\\\,cm$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$5\\\\,cm$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$2.5\\\\,cm$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$7.5\\\\,cm$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'$y(x, t)=2 \\sin (0.1 \\pi x) \\cos (100 \\pi t)$<br>compare with $y=A \\sin (k x) \\cos \\omega t$<br>$k = 0.1\\pi  = \\frac{{2\\pi }}{\\lambda } \\Rightarrow \\lambda  = 20\\,cm$<br>The distance between a node and next antinode<br>$ = \\frac{\\lambda }{4} = \\frac{{20}}{4} = 5\\,cm$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(39481,'physics',3,313,577,'A man standing in a lift falling under gravity releases a ball from his hand.  As seen by him,  the ball','[{"text": "Falls down", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Remains stationary", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Goes up", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Executes SHM", "isCorrect": false}]',1,NULL,NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(39444,'physics',3,314,579,'A man is 45 $m$ behind the bus, when the bus starts accelerating from rest with acceleration $2.5\\, ms^{-2}$. With what minimum velocity should the  man start running to catch the bus $B$ in time $t = 6s$?','[{"text": "$16\\\\,{{\\\\mathop{\\\\rm ms}\\\\nolimits} ^{ - 1}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$12\\\\,{{\\\\mathop{\\\\rm ms}\\\\nolimits} ^{ - 1}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$15\\\\,{{\\\\mathop{\\\\rm ms}\\\\nolimits} ^{ - 1}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$14\\\\,{{\\\\mathop{\\\\rm ms}\\\\nolimits} ^{ - 1}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'For man: $S = ({V_{\\min }}t)$ For bus: <br>$S - 45 = \\frac{1}{2} \\times 2.5 \\times {t^2}$<br>$\\therefore {V_{\\min }}t - 45 = 1.25{t^2};$<br>Here $t = 6s$<br>${V_{\\min }} \\times 6 - 45 = 1.25 \\times 36 \\Rightarrow {V_{\\min }} = \\frac{{90}}{6} = 15 \\, m{s^{ - 1}}$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(39445,'physics',30,1360,3267,'A particle of mass $m$ moves in a one-dimensional potential energy $U(x)=a x^{2}-b x^{4}$, where $a$ and $b$ are positive constants. The angular frequency of small oscillations about the minima of the potential energy is equal to','[{"text": "$2 \\\\sqrt{\\\\dfrac{a}{m}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\pi \\\\sqrt{\\\\dfrac{a}{2 b}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\sqrt{\\\\dfrac{a}{2 m}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\sqrt{\\\\dfrac{2 a}{m}}$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'$U(x)=-a x^{2}+b x^{4}$<br>$\\therefore F=-\\dfrac{d U}{d x}=-2 a x+4 b x^{3}$<br>At mean position $F=0$<br>$\\begin{gathered}\\therefore 2 a x=4 b x^{3} \\\\\\text { or } x^{2}=\\dfrac{2 a}{4 b}=\\dfrac{a}{2 b} \\Rightarrow x= \\pm \\sqrt{a / 2 b} \\\\\\text { \' } \\mathrm{U} \\text { \' is minimum at } x=-\\sqrt{\\dfrac{a}{2 b}}\\end{gathered}$<br>Now, $F=m \\dfrac{d^{2} x}{d t^{2}}$<br>$\\begin{aligned}& \\therefore \\quad m \\dfrac{d^{2} x}{d t^{2}}=-2 a x+4 b x^{3} \\\\& \\Rightarrow \\dfrac{d^{2} x}{d t^{2}}=-\\dfrac{2 a x}{m}\\left(1-\\dfrac{2 b}{a} x^{2}\\right)\\end{aligned}$<br>For small \' $x$ \',<br>$\\begin{aligned}& \\dfrac{d^{2} x}{d t^{2}}=-\\left(\\dfrac{2 a}{m}\\right) x \\\\& \\therefore \\quad \\omega=\\sqrt{2 a / m}\\end{aligned}$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(39446,'physics',31,1345,3232,'Equation of a standing wave is generally expressed as $y=2 A \\sin \\omega t \\cos k x$. In the equation, quantity $\\dfrac{\\omega}{k}$ represents','[{"text": "The speed of either of the component waves", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "A quantity that is independent of the properties of the string", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "The transeverse speed of the particles of the string", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "The speed of the standing wave", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Equation of the component waves are;<br>$y=A \\sin (\\omega t-k x) \\text { and } y=A \\sin (\\omega t+k x)$<br>For both waves the speed of component waves is $\\dfrac{\\omega}{k}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(39447,'physics',3,314,579,'Two cars are moving in the same direction with a speed of $30 kmh^{-1}$ . They are separated from each other by 5 $k$m. Third car moving in the opposite direction meets the two cars after an interval of 4 $min$. What is the speed of the third car?','[{"text": "$30\\\\, kmh^{-1}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$35\\\\, kmh^{-1}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$40\\\\,km{h^{ - 1}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$45\\\\, kmh^{-1}$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Let speed of the third car be $v \\, kmh^{-1}$ Then, relative speed of third car with respect to two cars $ = {\\rm{ }}\\left( {v + 30} \\right)\\, kmh^{-1}$<br>Two cars are separated by 5 $km$ and third car crosses them in 4 min.<br>$\\therefore v + 30 = \\frac{{5 \\times 60}}{4} = 75 \\Rightarrow v = 45 \\, kmh^{-1}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(39448,'physics',30,1362,3269,'The period of a simple pendulum, whose bob is a hollow metallic sphere, is $T_{1}$. The period is $T_{2}$ when the bob is filled with sand, $T_{3}$ when it is filled with mercury and $T_{4}$ when it is half filled with mercury. Which of the following is true?','[{"text": "$T_{1}=T_{2}=T_{3}>T_{4}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$T_{1}=T_{2}=T_{3}>T_{4}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$T_{1}=T_{2}=T_{3} < T_{4}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$T_{1}>T_{2}>T_{3}=T_{4}$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Time period of pendulum doesn\'t depends upon mass but it depends upon length (distance between point of suspension and centre of mass). In first three cases length are same so $T_{1}=T_{2}=T_{3}$ but in last case centre of mass lowers which in turn increases the length. So option (3) is correct',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/30_39448_question_1.jpg'),
(39449,'physics',31,1345,3232,'A $10\\,cm$ long rubber band obeys Hooke\'s law. When the rubber band is stretched to a total length of $12\\,cm$ the lowest resonant frequency is ${f_0}$. The rubber band is then stretched to a length of $13\\,cm$. The lowest resonant frequency will now be','[{"text": "Lower than $f_{0}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Higher than $f_{0}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "The same as $f_{0}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Changed, but the change (positive or negative) depends on the elastic constant and the original tension", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'$f_{0}=\\dfrac{1}{2 l} \\sqrt{\\dfrac{T}{\\mu}}$<br>For elastic wire $T = Kx$<br>$x \\rightarrow$ elongation from natural length<br>Hence frequency depends on $K$ and original tension',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(39450,'physics',3,314,579,'Two trains, each 50 $m$ long are travelling in opposite direction with velocity 10 $m/s$ and 15  $m/s$. The time of crossing is','[{"text": "$4s$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$2s$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2\\\\sqrt 3 s$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$4\\\\sqrt 3 s$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'${\\rm{Time}} = \\frac{{{\\rm{Total \\, length}}}}{{{\\rm{Relative \\, velocity}}}} = \\frac{{50 + 50}}{{10 + 15}} = \\frac{{100}}{{25}} = 4\\sec $',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(39451,'physics',30,1362,3269,'If $T_{1}$ and $T_{2}$ are the time-periods of oscillation of a simple pendulum on the surface of earth (of radius $R$) and at a depth $d$, then $d$ is equal to','[{"text": "$\\\\left(1-\\\\dfrac{T_{2}^{2}}{T_{1}^{2}}\\\\right) R$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\left(1-\\\\dfrac{T_{1}^{2}}{T_{2}^{2}}\\\\right) R$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\left(1-\\\\dfrac{T_{2}}{T_{1}}\\\\right) R$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\left(1-\\\\dfrac{T_{1}}{T_{2}}\\\\right) R$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'<br>$\\begin{aligned}& T_{1}=\\dfrac{k}{\\sqrt{g}} \\text { and } T_{2}=\\dfrac{k}{\\sqrt{g\\left(1-\\dfrac{d}{R}\\right)}} \\\\& \\dfrac{T_{1}}{T_{2}}=\\sqrt{1-\\dfrac{d}{R}} \\Rightarrow\\left(\\dfrac{T_{1}}{T_{2}}\\right)^{2}=1-\\dfrac{d}{R} \\\\& d=\\left[1-\\left(\\dfrac{T_{1}}{T_{2}}\\right)^{2}\\right] R\\end{aligned}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(39452,'physics',31,1345,3232,'The first overtone of a stretched wire of given length in $320\\,Hz$. The first harmonic is','[{"text": "$160\\\\,Hz$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$320\\\\,Hz$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$640\\\\,Hz$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$480\\\\,Hz$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Frequency of first overtone are second harmonic $\\left( {{n_2}} \\right) = 320\\,Hz$. So, frequency of first harmonic<br>${n_1} = \\frac{{{n_2}}}{2} = \\frac{{320}}{2} = 160\\,Hz$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(39453,'physics',3,314,579,'A train is moving slowly on a straight track with a constant speed of $1\\,m{s^{ - 1}}.$ A passenger in that train starts walking at a steady speed of $1\\,m{s^{ - 1}}$ to the back of the train in the opposite direction of the motion of the train. So to an observer standing on the platform directly in front of that passenger, the velocity of the passenger appears to be','[{"text": "$2\\\\,m{s^{ - 1}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{Zero}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$4\\\\,m{s^{ - 1}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2\\\\,m{s^{ - 1}}$ in the opposite direction of the train", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'&nbsp;The relative velocity of the passenger w.r.to Platform is &nbsp;${v_{rel}} = 2 - 2 = 0\\,m/s$.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2010,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(39454,'physics',32,479,975,'The variation of potential \\[V\\] with distance \\[x\\] from a fixed point charge is shown in figure. The electric field strength between \\[x = 0.1\\,m\\] and 0.3 \\[m\\] is<div class="matching-question"></div>','[{"text": "$ - 0.4V{m^{ - 1}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$ + 10V{m^{ - 1}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$ + 20V{m^{ - 1}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$ + 0.4V{m^{ - 1}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'As,$E = - \\frac{{ - \\Delta V}}{{\\Delta r}} = - \\frac{{(1 - 3)}}{{0.3 - 0.1}}V{m^{ - 1}} = 10V{m^{ - 1}}$',NULL,'Medium','Graph/Figure',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/32_39454_question_1.png'),
(39455,'physics',30,1362,3269,'A simple pendulum of length $l$ and having a bob of mass $M$ is suspended in a car. The car is moving on a circular track of radius $R$ with a uniform speed $v$. If the pendulum makes small oscillations about its equilibrium position, what will be its time period?','[{"text": "$2\\\\pi \\\\sqrt {l/\\\\sqrt {\\\\left( {{g^2} + \\\\frac{{{v^4}}}{{{R^2}}}} \\\\right)} } $", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$2\\\\pi \\\\sqrt {\\\\frac{{3l}}{{\\\\sqrt {g + \\\\frac{{{v^2}}}{R}} }}} $", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2 \\\\pi \\\\sqrt{\\\\dfrac{2 l}{\\\\left(g^{2}+v^{2} / R\\\\right)}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2\\\\pi \\\\sqrt {\\\\frac{{2l}}{{\\\\sqrt {\\\\left( {{g^2} + \\\\frac{{{v^2}}}{{{R^2}}}} \\\\right)} }}} $", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'The bob is subjected to two simultaneous, accelerations perpendicular to each other viz acceleration due to gravity $g$ and radial acceleration $a_{R}=\\dfrac{v^{2}}{R}$ towards the centre of the circular path. <img src=\'/img/data/physics/30_39455_explanation_1.jpg\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' /> $\\therefore$ Effective acceleration $a_{e f f}=\\sqrt{g^{2}+\\left(\\dfrac{v^{2}}{R}\\right)^{2}}$<br>$\\therefore$ Time period of the simple pendulum<br>$ = 2\\pi \\sqrt {\\frac{l}{{{a_{{\\text{eff }}}}}}} $<br>$ = 2\\pi \\sqrt {\\frac{l}{{\\sqrt {{g^2} + {{\\left( {\\frac{{{v^2}}}{R}} \\right)}^2}} }}}  = 2\\pi \\sqrt {\\frac{l}{{\\sqrt {{g^2} + \\frac{{{v^4}}}{{{R^2}}}} }}} $',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(39456,'physics',31,1345,3232,'In order to double the frequency of the fundamental note emitted by a stretched string, the length is reduced to $\\dfrac{3}{4}$ $th$ of the original length and the tension is changed. The factor by which the tension is to be changed is','[{"text": "$\\\\dfrac{8}{9}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{3}{8}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{9}{4}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{2}{3}$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'$f=\\dfrac{1}{2 l} \\sqrt{\\dfrac{T}{\\mu}} \\Rightarrow f \\propto \\dfrac{\\sqrt{T}}{l}$<br>$\\Rightarrow \\dfrac{T_{2}}{T_{1}}=\\left(\\dfrac{f_{2}}{f_{1}}\\right)^{2}\\left(\\dfrac{l_{2}}{l_{1}}\\right)^{2}=(2)^{2}\\left(\\dfrac{3}{4}\\right)^{2}=\\dfrac{9}{4}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(39457,'physics',3,314,579,'A bus is moving with a velocity $10\\,m{s^{ - 1}}$ on a straight road. A scooterist wishes to overtake the bus in 100 $s$. If the bus is at a distance of 1 $km$ from the scooterist, with what velocity should the scooterist chase the bus?','[{"text": "$30\\\\,m{s^{ - 1}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$20\\\\,m{s^{ - 1}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$40\\\\,m{s^{ - 1}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$50\\\\,m{s^{ - 1}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Let the velocity of the scooter be  $v$ $m{s^{ - 1}}$.<br>Then $(v - 10)100 = 1000\\,{\\rm{or }} \\, v = 20 \\, m{s^{ - 1}}$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(39458,'physics',32,484,991,'The graph between capacitance of an isolated spherical capacitor \\[vs \\] its radius is<div class="matching-question"></div>','[{"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'The capacitance of a spherical capacitor is<br>${\\rm{C}} = 4\\pi {\\varepsilon _0}r$<br>The graph is a straight line passing through the origin.',NULL,'Easy','Graph/Figure',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(39459,'physics',30,1362,3271,'Two springs, of force constants $k_{1}$ and $k_{2}$ are connected to a mass $m$ as shown. The frequency of oscillation of the mass is $f$. If both $k_{1}$ and $k_{2}$ are made four times their original values, the frequency of oscillation becomes.','[{"text": "$2 f$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$f / 2$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$f / 4$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$4 f$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'The two springs are in parallel.<br>$\\therefore$ Effective spring constant,<br>$k=k_{1}+k_{2}$<br>Now, frequency of oscillation is given by<br>$f = \\frac{1}{{2\\pi }}\\sqrt {\\frac{{{k_1} + {k_2}}}{m}} \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,(1)$<br>When both $k_{1}$ and $k_{2}$ are made four times their original values, the new frequency is given by<br>$\\begin{aligned}& f^{\\prime}=\\dfrac{1}{2 \\pi} \\sqrt{\\dfrac{4 k_{1}+4 k_{2}}{m}} \\\\& =\\dfrac{1}{2 \\pi} \\sqrt{\\dfrac{4\\left(k_{1}+k_{2}\\right)}{m}}=2\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2 \\pi} \\sqrt{\\dfrac{k_{1}+k_{2}}{m}}\\right)=2 f\\end{aligned}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/30_39459_question_1.jpg'),
(39460,'physics',31,1345,3232,'When a string fixed at both ends vibrates in 1 loop, 2 loops, 3 loops and 4 loops, the frequenices are in the ratio','[{"text": "$1: 2: 3: 4$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$1: 1: 1: 1$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1: 4: 9: 16$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$4: 3: 2: 1$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'When the string vibrates in $n$ loops, its frequency is $f_{n}=\\dfrac{n v}{2 L}$<br>Where $L$ is the length of the string and $v$ is the velocity of the wave.<br>$\\therefore$ When the string fixed at its both ends vibrates in 1 loop, 2 loops, 3 loops and 4 loops, the frequencies are in the ratio $1: 2: 3: 4$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(39461,'physics',3,313,577,'A particle is released from rest from a tower of height 3 $h$. The ratio of times to fall equal heights $h$, is','[{"text": "${\\\\rm{9}}\\\\,{\\\\rm{:}}\\\\,{\\\\rm{4}}\\\\,{\\\\rm{:}}\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\rm{1}}\\\\,$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\sqrt 3 :\\\\sqrt 2 :1$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$3:2\\\\,:1$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1:\\\\left( {\\\\sqrt 2  - 1} \\\\right):\\\\left( {\\\\sqrt 3  - \\\\sqrt 2 } \\\\right)$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'$h = \\frac{1}{2}gt_1^2$<br>$2h = \\frac{1}{2}gt_2^2$<br>$3h = \\frac{1}{2}gt_3^2$<br>${t_1}:({t_2} - {t_1}):({t_3} - {t_2})$<br>$\\sqrt {\\frac{{2h}}{g}} :\\sqrt {\\frac{{4h}}{g}}  - \\sqrt {\\frac{{2h}}{g}} :\\sqrt {\\frac{{6h}}{g}}  - \\sqrt {\\frac{{4h}}{g}} $<br>$1:(\\sqrt 2  - 1):(\\sqrt 3  - \\sqrt 2 )$<br>',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(39463,'physics',30,1362,3270,'A block of mass $m$ rests on a platform. The platform is given up and down SHM with an amplitude $\\mathrm{d}$. What can be the maximum frequency so that the block never leaves the platform?','[{"text": "$\\\\dfrac{1}{2 \\\\pi} \\\\sqrt{g / d}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\sqrt{g / d}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2 \\\\pi \\\\sqrt{g / d}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{1}{2 \\\\pi}(g / d)$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'For the gievn amplitude $d$ the block looses contact with the platform when maximum acceleration is equal to $g$ at the highest position.<br>$\\begin{aligned}& k d=m g \\Rightarrow m \\omega^{2} d=m g \\\\& \\Rightarrow \\omega=\\sqrt{\\dfrac{g}{d}} \\Rightarrow n=\\dfrac{1}{2 \\pi} \\sqrt{\\dfrac{g}{d}}\\end{aligned}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(39464,'physics',31,1345,3232,'The expression $y=a \\sin b x \\sin \\omega t$ represents a stationary wave. The distance between the consecutive nodes is equal to:','[{"text": "$2 \\\\pi / b$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\pi / b$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$1 / b$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\pi / 2 b$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'$y=a \\sin b x \\sin \\omega t$<br>On comparing with standard equation of stationary wave<br>$\\begin{aligned}& y=2 a \\sin \\dfrac{2 \\pi x}{\\lambda} \\cdot \\sin \\omega t, \\text { we get } \\\\& \\dfrac{2 \\pi x}{\\lambda}=b x, \\\\& \\therefore \\lambda=\\dfrac{2 \\pi}{b}\\end{aligned}$<br>The distance between constructive nodes<br>$=\\dfrac{\\lambda}{2}=\\dfrac{\\pi}{b}$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(39465,'physics',3,313,577,'From a building two balls $A$ and $B$ are thrown such that $A$ is thrown upwards and $B$ downwards (both vertically) with the same speed. If ${V_A}\\,{\\rm{and}}\\,{V_B}$ are their respective velocities on reaching the ground then :-','[{"text": "${V_B} = {V_A}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${V_B} > {V_A}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${V_B} < {V_A}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "These velocities depend on their masses", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'${V_A} = {V_B}$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(39466,'physics',32,480,978,'As per figure a point charged \\[+q\\] is placed at the origin \\[O\\] work done in taking another point charge \\[-Q\\] from the point \\[M\\] coordinates (0, \\[a\\])  to another point \\[N\\] coordinates (\\[a\\],0) along the straight path \\[MN\\] is<div class="matching-question"></div>','[{"text": "zero", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\left( {\\\\frac{Q}{{4\\\\pi {\\\\varepsilon _o}{a^2}}}} \\\\right),\\\\sqrt {2a} $", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\left( {\\\\frac{{qQ}}{{4\\\\pi {\\\\varepsilon _o}{a^2}}} \\\\cdot \\\\frac{a}{{\\\\sqrt 2 }}} \\\\right)$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\left( {\\\\frac{{ - qQ}}{{4\\\\pi {\\\\varepsilon _o}{a^2}}} \\\\cdot \\\\frac{1}{a}} \\\\right)$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'$\\Delta U = 0 \\Rightarrow {W_{ext}} = 0$',NULL,'Hard','Graph/Figure',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/32_39466_question_1.png'),
(39467,'physics',30,1362,3270,'A mass $M$, attached to a horizontal spring, executes S.H.M. with amplitude $A_{1}$. When the mass $M$ passes through its mean position then a smaller mass $m$ is placed over it and both of them move together with amplitude $A_{2}$. The ratio<br>of $\\left(\\dfrac{A_{1}}{A_{2}}\\right)$ is:','[{"text": "$\\\\left(\\\\dfrac{M}{M+m}\\\\right)^{\\\\frac{1}{2}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{M+m}{M}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\left(\\\\dfrac{M+m}{M}\\\\right)^{\\\\frac{1}{2}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{M}{M+m}$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'The net force exerted by the spring is zero at the mean point. Therefore, linear momentum must be conserved. $\\therefore M v_{1}=(M+m) v_{2}$<br>$\\begin{aligned}\\text { As } \\quad & =A \\omega=A \\sqrt{\\dfrac{k}{m}} \\\\\\Rightarrow & M A_{1} \\sqrt{\\dfrac{k}{M}}=(M+m) A_{2} \\sqrt{\\dfrac{k}{m+M}} \\\\& \\therefore\\left(V=A \\sqrt{\\dfrac{k}{M}}\\right) \\\\& \\because A_{1} \\sqrt{M}=A_{2} \\sqrt{M+m} \\\\& \\therefore \\dfrac{A_{1}}{A_{2}}=\\sqrt{\\dfrac{m+M}{M}}\\end{aligned}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(39468,'physics',31,1345,3233,'A brick is hung from a sonometer wire. If the brick is immersed in oil, then frequency of the wire will','[{"text": "Increase due to buoyancy", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Decrease", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Remains unchanged", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Increase due to viscosity of oil", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'$f \\alpha \\sqrt{T} \\alpha \\sqrt{\\text { effective weight of load }}$<br>Tension in the string decreases because of buyoancy force. So frequency decreases',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(39469,'physics',3,313,577,'A ball thrown vertically upwards after reaching a maximum height $h$ returns to the starting point after a time of 10 $s$. Its displacement is','[{"text": "$10\\\\,h$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$20\\\\,h$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Zero", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$2\\\\,h$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'The net displacement of the body is equal to zero',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(39470,'physics',32,479,975,'In the figure shown, the electric field intensity at \\[r = 1m,r = 6m,r = 9m,\\] in \\[V{m^{ - 1}}\\]  is<div class="matching-question"></div>','[{"text": "$ + 5,1.67, - 5$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$ - 5,0 + 5$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$ - 5, - 1.67, + 5$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$0,1.67,0$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'$E =  - \\frac{{\\Delta V}}{{\\Delta r}}$',NULL,'Medium','Graph/Figure',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/32_39470_question_1.png'),
(39471,'physics',30,1362,3270,'The masses $m_{1}$ and $m_{2}$ are suspended together by a massless spring of constant $k$. When the masses are in equilibrium, $m_{1}$ is removed without disturbing the system; the amplitude of vibration is -','[{"text": "$m_{2} g / k$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$m_{1} g / k$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{\\\\left(m_{2}-m_{1}\\\\right) g}{k}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{\\\\left(m_{1}+m_{2}\\\\right) g}{k}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'In equilibrium $F=\\left(m_{1}+m_{2}\\right) g$<br>By removing mass $m_{1}$<br>$\\begin{aligned}& F^{\\prime}=m_{2} g \\\\& F_{r}=F^{\\prime}-F=m_{2} g-\\left(m_{1}+m_{2}\\right) g \\\\& k A=m_{1} g \\Rightarrow A=\\dfrac{m_{1} g}{k}\\end{aligned}$<br>since the lower spring exerts force on the block only in the downward motion.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(39472,'physics',31,1345,3233,'Fundamental frequency of sonometer wire is $n$. If the length, tension and diameter of wire are tripled, the new fundamental frequency is','[{"text": "$n \\\\sqrt{3}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{n}{\\\\sqrt{3}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{n}{2 \\\\sqrt{3}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{n}{3 \\\\sqrt{3}}$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'$n=\\dfrac{1}{2 l} \\sqrt{\\dfrac{T}{\\pi r^{2} \\rho}} \\Rightarrow n \\propto \\dfrac{\\sqrt{T}}{l r}$ <br>$\\begin{aligned}& \\Rightarrow \\dfrac{n_{1}}{n_{2}}=\\sqrt{\\dfrac{T_{1}}{T_{2}}} \\times \\dfrac{l_{2}}{l_{1}} \\times \\dfrac{r_{2}}{r_{1}} \\\\& =\\sqrt{\\dfrac{T}{3 T}} \\times \\dfrac{3 l}{l} \\times \\dfrac{3 r}{r}=3 \\sqrt{3} \\Rightarrow n_{2}=\\dfrac{n}{3 \\sqrt{3}}\\end{aligned}$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(39482,'physics',32,484,993,'A graph between energy (\\[U\\]) and potential difference (\\[V\\]) for a capacitor will be<div class="matching-question"></div>','[{"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'The energy of a capacitor is $U = \\frac{1}{2}C{V^2}$<br>The graph between $U$ and $V$ is a parabola',NULL,'Easy','Graph/Figure',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(39473,'physics',3,313,577,'If a freely falling body travels in the last second a distance equal to the distance travelled by it in the first three second, the time of the travel is','[{"text": "$5\\\\,\\\\sec $", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$4\\\\,\\\\sec $", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$6\\\\,\\\\sec $", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$3\\\\,\\\\sec $", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'The distance travelled in last second.<br>${S_{Last}} = u + \\frac{g}{2}\\left( {2t - 1} \\right) = \\frac{1}{2} \\times 9.8\\left( {2t - 1} \\right) = 4.9\\left( {2t - 1} \\right)$<br>and distance travelled in first three seconds,<br>${S_{Three}} = 0 + \\frac{1}{2} \\times 9.8 \\times 9 = 44.1m$<br>According to problem ${S_{Last}} = {S_{Three}}$<br>$ \\Rightarrow 4.9\\left( {2t - 1} \\right) = 44.1 \\Rightarrow 2t - 1 = 9$<br>$ \\Rightarrow t = 5\\sec .$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(39474,'physics',32,480,979,'2\\[q\\] and 3\\[q\\] are two charges separated by a  distance 12 \\[cm\\] on \\[x\\]- axis.A third charge \\[q\\] is placed at 5 \\[cm\\] on \\[y\\] -axis as shown in the figure. Find the change in potential energy of the system if \\[q\\] is moved from initial position to a point on \\[X\\] - axis in circular path:<div class="matching-question"></div>','[{"text": "$\\\\frac{{3{q^2}}}{{4\\\\pi {\\\\varepsilon _0}}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{{{q^2}}}{{4\\\\pi {\\\\varepsilon _0}}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{{18{q^2}}}{{4\\\\pi {\\\\varepsilon _0}(91)}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{{16{q^2}}}{{4\\\\pi {\\\\varepsilon _0}(91)}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'The intial Potential energy of the system is<br>${U_1} = \\frac{{q\\left( {2q} \\right)}}{{4\\pi {\\varepsilon _0}\\left( 5 \\right)}} + \\frac{{q\\left( {3q} \\right)}}{{4\\pi {\\varepsilon _0}\\left( {13} \\right)}} + \\frac{{\\left( {2q} \\right)\\left( {3q} \\right)}}{{4\\pi {\\varepsilon _0}\\left( {12} \\right)}}$<br>${U_2} = \\frac{{q\\left( {2q} \\right)}}{{4\\pi {\\varepsilon _0}\\left( 5 \\right)}} + \\frac{{q\\left( {3q} \\right)}}{{4\\pi {\\varepsilon _0}\\left( 7 \\right)}} + \\frac{{\\left( {2q} \\right)\\left( {3q} \\right)}}{{4\\pi {\\varepsilon _0}\\left( {12} \\right)}}$<br>$\\Delta U = {U_2} - {U_1} = \\frac{{3{q^2}}}{{4\\pi {\\varepsilon _0}}}\\left[ {\\frac{1}{7} - \\frac{1}{{13}}} \\right] = \\frac{{18{q^2}}}{{4\\pi {\\varepsilon _0}\\left( {91} \\right)}}$',NULL,'Hard','Graph/Figure',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/32_39474_question_1.png'),
(39475,'physics',30,1362,3270,'Two identical particles each of mass $m$ are interconnected by a light spring of stiffness $k$, the time period for small oscillation is equal to:','[{"text": "$\\\\dfrac{\\\\pi}{2} \\\\sqrt{\\\\dfrac{m}{k}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2 \\\\pi \\\\sqrt{\\\\dfrac{m}{k}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\pi \\\\sqrt{\\\\dfrac{2 m}{k}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\pi \\\\sqrt{\\\\dfrac{m}{2 k}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'The reduced mass<br>$\\mu=\\dfrac{(m)(m)}{m+m}=\\dfrac{m}{2}$<br>The given system is equivalent to a system of a particle of mass $m/2$ connected to a spring of stiffness $k$ ragidly.<br>The required period of oscillation.<br>$T=2 \\pi \\sqrt{\\dfrac{\\mu}{k}}=2 \\pi \\sqrt{\\dfrac{m / 2}{k}}=\\pi \\sqrt{\\dfrac{2 m}{k}}$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/30_39475_question_1.jpg'),
(39476,'physics',31,1345,3233,'Where should the two bridges be set in a $110\\,cm$ wire so that it is divided into three parts and the ratio of the frequencies are $3: 2: 1$ ?','[{"text": "$30\\\\,cm$ from one end and $70\\\\,cm$ from other end", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$20\\\\,cm$ from one end and $60\\\\,cm$ from other end", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$50\\\\,cm$ from one end and $50\\\\,cm$ from other end", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$10\\\\,cm$ from one end and $40\\\\,cm$ from other end <br>", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'<br><img src=\'/img/data/physics/31_39476_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' /> <br>${n_1}:{n_2}:{n_3} = 3:2:1$<br>$n\\alpha \\frac{1}{\\ell }$ ( $T{\\mkern 1mu} $ & $\\mu $ are same )<br>${\\ell _1}:{\\ell _2}:{\\ell _3} = \\frac{1}{3}:\\frac{1}{2}:1 = 2:3:6$<br>${\\ell _1} + {\\ell _2} + {\\ell _3} = 110$<br>$ \\Rightarrow 2x + 3x + 6x = 110 \\Rightarrow x = 10$<br>$\\therefore$ The two bridges should be set at $2 x$ i.e., 20 $cm$ from one end and $6x$ i.e, $60\\,cm$ from the other end.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(39477,'physics',3,313,577,'A body is falling freely. Find the ratio of distances in ${1^{st}},{2^{nd}}\\,{\\rm{and}}\\,{3^{rd}}$ seconds.','[{"text": "$1:3:5$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$1:5:7$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1:2:3$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1:\\\\sqrt 2 :2$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Consider the downward direction as positive<br>Given $u = 0,\\,a = g$<br>${D_n} = 0 + \\frac{1}{2}a\\left( {2n - 1)} \\right)$<br>${D_1}:{D_2}:{D_3} = \\frac{1}{2} \\, g\\left( {2\\left( 1 \\right) - 1} \\right):$<br>$\\frac{1}{2} \\, g\\left( {2\\left( 2 \\right) - 1} \\right):\\frac{1}{2} \\, g\\left( {2\\left( 3 \\right) - 1} \\right)$<br>$ = 1\\,:3\\,:5$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(39478,'physics',32,478,971,'A solid sphere of radius \\[R\\] is charged uniformly. The electrostatic potential \\[V\\] is plotted as a  function of distance \\[r\\] from the centre of the sphere. Which of the following best represents  the resulting curve?<div class="matching-question"></div>','[{"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'For $(r \\le R)\\,V = \\frac{1}{{4\\pi {\\varepsilon _0}}}\\frac{Q}{{2R}}\\left( {3 - \\frac{{{r^2}}}{{{R^2}}}} \\right)$<br>For $(r \\ge R)\\,V = \\frac{1}{{4\\pi {\\varepsilon _0}}}\\frac{Q}{R}$<br>where $Q$ - total charge of the sphere',NULL,'Medium','Graph/Figure',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(39479,'physics',30,1362,3270,'Figure shows a system consisting of massless pulley, a spring of force constant $k$ and a block of mass $m$. If the block is slightly displaced vertically down from its equilibrium position and then released, the period of its vertical oscillation is','[{"text": "$\\\\pi \\\\sqrt{\\\\dfrac{m}{4 K}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2 \\\\pi \\\\sqrt{\\\\dfrac{m}{K}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$4 \\\\pi \\\\sqrt{\\\\dfrac{m}{K}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\pi \\\\sqrt{\\\\dfrac{m}{K}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Let us assume that in equilibrium condition spring is $x_{0}$ elongate from its natural length <img src=\'/img/data/physics/30_39479_explanation_1.jpg\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' /> In equilibrium $T_{0}=m g$<br>and $k x_{0}=2 T_{0}$<br>$ \\Rightarrow k{x_0} = 2mg\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,(1)$<br>If the mass $m$ moves down a distance $\\mathrm{x}$ from its equilibrium position then pulley will move down by $\\dfrac{x}{2}$. So the extra force in spring will be $\\dfrac{k x}{2}$.<br>From figure<br>$\\begin{aligned}& F_{\\text {net }}=m g-T=m g-\\dfrac{k}{2}\\left(x_{0}+\\dfrac{x}{2}\\right) \\\\& \\left(T=\\dfrac{k\\left(x_{o}+\\dfrac{x}{2}\\right)}{2}\\right) \\\\& F_{\\text {net }}=m g-\\dfrac{k x_{0}}{2}-\\dfrac{k x}{4}\\end{aligned}$<br>from eq. (1), we get<br>${F_{{\\rm{net }}}} = \\frac{{ - kx}}{4}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,(2)$<br>Comparing eq. (2) with $F=-K_{\\text {Sнм }} x$<br>then $\\quad K_{\\text {SHM }}=\\dfrac{K}{4}$<br>$\\Rightarrow T=2 \\pi \\sqrt{\\dfrac{m}{K_{\\mathrm{SHM}}}}=2 \\pi \\sqrt{\\dfrac{4 m}{K}}$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/30_39479_question_1.jpg'),
(39480,'physics',31,1346,3236,'Sound waves transfer','[{"text": "Only energy not momentum", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Energy", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Momentum", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both energy and momentum", "isCorrect": true}]',3,NULL,NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(39484,'physics',31,1346,3236,'The true statement is','[{"text": "Sound waves in air are transverse waves", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Sound wave does not required a material medium for its propagation", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Sound travels faster in gas than in solid", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Sound travels faster in solid than in gas", "isCorrect": true}]',3,NULL,NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(39485,'physics',3,313,577,'Two bodies of different masses are dropped simultaneously from the top of a tower.  If air resistance is proportional to the mass of the body, then,','[{"text": "The heavier body reaches the ground earlier", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "The lighter body reaches the ground earlier.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both the bodies reach the ground simultaneously.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Cannot be decided.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'$f = mk$<br>$mg + mk = ma$<br>$ \\Rightarrow \\,\\,a = g + k$ (where $k$ is proportionality  constant).<br>Acceleration is same for both bodies',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(39486,'physics',32,486,1000,'A parallel plate capacitor has two layers of  dielectric as shown in figure. This  capacitor is  connected across a battery. The graph which  shows the variation of electric field (\\[E\\]) and  distance (\\[x\\]) from left plate.<div class="matching-question"></div>','[{"text": "", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'The electric field remains constant inside a dielectric and the values in the two slabs are<br>${E_1} = \\frac{{{E_0}}}{2}$ (inside the first slab)<br>${E_2} = \\frac{{{E_0}}}{4}$ (inside the second slab)<br>Where ${{E_0}}$ is the electric field in the absence of slab.',NULL,'Hard','Graph/Figure',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/32_39486_question_1.png'),
(39487,'physics',30,1362,3272,'A wooden cube (density of wood \'$d$\') of side \' $l$ \' floats in a liquid of density \'$\\rho$\' with its upper and lower surface horizontal. If the cube is pushed slightly down and relased, it performs simple harmonic motion of period \'$T$\' to','[{"text": "$2 \\\\pi \\\\sqrt{\\\\dfrac{l d}{\\\\rho g}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$2 \\\\pi \\\\sqrt{\\\\dfrac{l \\\\rho}{d g}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2 \\\\pi \\\\sqrt{\\\\dfrac{l d}{(\\\\rho-d) g}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2 \\\\pi \\\\sqrt{\\\\dfrac{l \\\\rho}{(\\\\rho-d) g}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'At equilibrium $F_{b}=m g$<br>$\\rho A l_{0} g=d l g$<br>Restoring force,<br>$\\begin{aligned}& F_{r}=m g-F_{b}^{\\prime} \\\\& F=m g-\\rho A\\left(l_{0}+x\\right) g \\\\& d A l a=d A l g-\\rho A l_{0} g-\\rho g A x \\\\& a=-\\dfrac{\\rho g}{d l} x\\end{aligned}$<br>Therefore, wooden cube performs S.H.M.<br>$\\therefore \\omega=\\sqrt{\\dfrac{\\rho g}{d l}} \\Rightarrow T=2 \\pi \\sqrt{\\dfrac{l d}{\\rho g}}$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(39488,'physics',31,1346,3237,'Speed of sound at constant temperature depends on','[{"text": "Density of gas", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Pressure", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both (1) and (2)", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "None of these", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Speed of sound, doesn\'t depend upon pressure and density of the medium at constant temperature.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(39489,'physics',3,313,577,'A ball is thrown up under gravity $\\left( {g = 10\\,m/{{\\sec }^2}} \\right)$. Find its velocity after $1.0\\,\\sec $ at a height of $10\\,m$.','[{"text": "$5\\\\,m/{\\\\sec ^2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$5\\\\,m/\\\\sec $", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$15\\\\,m/\\\\sec $", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$10\\\\,m/\\\\sec $", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'$h = ut + \\frac{1}{2}a{t^2} \\Rightarrow 10 = u \\times 1 - 5$<br>${(1)^2} \\Rightarrow u = 15 \\, m{s^{ - 1}};\\,V = u + at$<br>$ = 15 - 10 \\times 1 = 5 \\, m{s^{ - 1}}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(39490,'physics',32,478,969,'A circular ring carries a uniformly distributed  positive charge. The electric field (\\[E\\]) and  potential (\\[V\\]) varies with distance (\\[r\\]) from the centre of the ring along its axis as<div class="matching-question"></div>','[{"text": "$b$ and $c$ are true", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$a,b,c,d$ are true", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$a$ is true", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$a,b,c$ are true", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'The electric field and potential on the axis of a circular charged ring is <br>$E = \\frac{{q{\\rm{x}}}}{{4\\pi {\\varepsilon _0}{{\\left( {{R^2} + {{\\rm{x}}^2}} \\right)}^{3/2}}}}$<br>$V = \\frac{q}{{4\\pi {\\varepsilon _0}\\sqrt {{R^2} + {{\\rm{x}}^2}} }}$',NULL,'Medium','Graph/Figure',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/32_39490_question_1.png'),
(39491,'physics',30,1362,3272,'A tunnel is made across the earth of radius $R$, passing through its centre. A ball is dropped from a height h in the tunnel. the motion will be periodic with time period.','[{"text": "$2 \\\\pi \\\\sqrt{\\\\dfrac{R}{g}}+4 \\\\sqrt{\\\\dfrac{2 h}{g}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$2 \\\\pi \\\\sqrt{\\\\dfrac{R}{g}}+4 \\\\sqrt{\\\\dfrac{h}{g}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2 \\\\pi \\\\sqrt{\\\\dfrac{R}{g}}+\\\\sqrt{\\\\dfrac{h}{g}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2 \\\\pi \\\\sqrt{\\\\dfrac{R}{g}}+\\\\sqrt{\\\\dfrac{2 h}{g}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'When the ball $m$ falls from a height $h$, it reaches the surface of earth in time, $t=\\sqrt{2 h / g}$. Its velocity is $v=\\sqrt{2 g h}$. It then moves into the tunnel and reaches on the other side of earth and goes again upto a height $h$ on the other side of earth. The ball again returns back and thus executes periodic motion. Outside the earth ball crosses distance $h$ four times. When the ball is in the tunnel at distance $x$ from the centre of the earth, then gravitational force acting on ball is<br>$\\begin{aligned}& \\therefore \\quad F=\\dfrac{G M m x}{R^{3}} \\\\& \\text { i.e., } F \\propto x\\end{aligned}$<br>As, this force, $F$ is directed towards the centre of earth i.e., the mean position. So, the ball will execute periodic motion about the centre of earth.<br>Here, inertia factor $=$ mass of ball $=m$<br>$\\text { Spring factor }=\\dfrac{G M m}{R^{3}}=\\dfrac{g m}{R}$<br>$\\therefore$ Time period of oscillation of ball in the tunnel is<br>$\\begin{aligned}T^{\\prime} & =2 \\pi \\sqrt{\\dfrac{\\text { inertia factor }}{\\text { spring factor }}} \\\\& =2 \\pi \\sqrt{\\dfrac{m}{g m / R}}=2 \\pi \\sqrt{\\dfrac{R}{g}}\\end{aligned}$<br>Time spent by ball outside the tunnel on both the sides will be $=4 \\sqrt{2 h / g}$<br>Therefore, total time period of oscillation of ball is<br>$=2 \\pi \\sqrt{\\dfrac{R}{g}}+4 \\sqrt{\\dfrac{2 h}{g}}$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(39492,'physics',31,1349,3246,'The persistance of sound in a hall is called','[{"text": "Resonance", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Reverberation", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Articulation", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Acoustics", "isCorrect": false}]',1,NULL,NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(39494,'physics',32,484,995,'Find the potential difference ${V_a} - {V_b}$  between  the points  $a$ and $b$ shown in the figure.','[{"text": "$ - 4V$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$ - 6V$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$ - 8V$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$ - 10V$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'A battery always supplies equal and  opposite charges from its two terminals so we  can conserve charge on a loop that has both  terminal of a battery. Consider  an indepedent  loop that has two terminals of a battery. The total charge on the loop is zero. Two possible independent loops are shown in the figure.   <img src=\'/img/data/physics/32_39494_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />    By applying Kirchoff’s law<br>$24V - \\frac{q}{{4\\mu F}} - 12V - \\frac{q}{{2\\mu F}} = 0$<br>$12 = \\frac{{3q}}{4} \\Rightarrow q = 16\\mu C$<br>The potential difference between a and b is  <br>${V_a} - {V_b} = \\frac{{ - q}}{{2\\mu F}} = \\frac{{ - 16\\mu C}}{{2\\mu F}} =  - 8V$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/32_39494_question_1.png'),
(39495,'physics',31,1349,3246,'Loudness of sound refers to','[{"text": "Velocity", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Intensity", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Wavelength", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Frequency", "isCorrect": false}]',1,NULL,NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(39496,'physics',30,1362,3272,'A round metal hoop is suspended on the edge by a hook. The hoop can oscillate side to side in the plane of the hoop, or it can oscillate back and fourth in a direction perpendicular to the plane of the hoop. For which mode will the frequency of oscillation be larger?','[{"text": "The frequency of oscillation will be the same in either mode", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Oscillations in the plane of the hoop", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Oscillations perpendicular to the plane of the hoop", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "None of these", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'$T = 2\\pi \\sqrt {\\frac{I}{{mgR}}} $<br>For side by side oscillations<br>$I_{1}=2 m R^{2}$ (Passing the centre and plane)<br>$I_{2}=\\dfrac{m R^{2}}{2}($ Passing through edge $)$<br>As $I_{1}>I_{2} \\Rightarrow T_{1}>T_{2} \\Rightarrow f_{1} < f_{2}$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(39497,'physics',3,312,574,'Match the Column I and Column II<div class="matching-question"><div class="column-left">A. Velocity<br>B. Displacement<br>C. Speed<br>D. Acceleration</div><div class="column-right">P.$\\,m/{s^2}$<br>Q. Vector<br>R. $\\,m/s$<br>S. Scalar</div></div>','[{"text": "A - Q,R,  B - Q, C - R,S, D - P,Q", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "A - Q, B - P, C - R, D - S", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "A - P,Q,R, B-R,C-S,D-P,R", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "A - Q,  B - S, C - P, D-R", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'A - Q,R,  B - Q, C - R,S, D - P,Q',NULL,'Easy','Match the following',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(39498,'physics',32,484,995,'Find the charge on $2\\mu F$ capacitor in the given circuit','[{"text": "$12\\\\mu C$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$4\\\\mu C$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$8\\\\mu C$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2\\\\mu C$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'The circuit has two independent loops. The net charge inside an independent loop must be zero. The independent loop should have two terminals of the battery inside it.  <img src=\'/img/data/physics/32_39498_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />    By applying Kirchoff’s law<br>$16V - \\frac{q}{{2\\mu F}} - 6V - \\frac{q}{{3\\mu F}} = 0$<br>$q = 12\\mu C$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/32_39498_question_1.png'),
(39499,'physics',31,1346,3236,'Match the Column I and II<div class="matching-question"><div class="column-left">A.  A region of low pressure and low density<br>B. A region of high pressure and high density<br>C. Longitudinal wave<br>D. Transverse wave</div><div class="column-right">P. Particles oscillate perpendicular to the direction of propagation<br>Q. Particles oscillate in the same direction compression<br>R. Compression<br>S. Rare faction</div></div>','[{"text": "${\\\\text{A  -  R,}}\\\\,{\\\\mkern 1mu} {\\\\text{B  -  S,}}\\\\,{\\\\mkern 1mu} {\\\\text{C  -  P,}}{\\\\mkern 1mu} \\\\,{\\\\text{D  -  Q}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\text{A  -  P,}}\\\\,{\\\\mkern 1mu} {\\\\text{B  -  Q,}}\\\\,{\\\\mkern 1mu} {\\\\text{C  -  R,}}\\\\,{\\\\mkern 1mu} {\\\\text{D  -  S}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\text{A  -  S,}}\\\\,{\\\\text{B  -  R,}}\\\\,{\\\\text{C  -  Q,}}\\\\,{\\\\text{D  -  P}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${\\\\text{A  -  R,}}\\\\,{\\\\text{B  -  S,}}\\\\,{\\\\text{C  -  Q,}}\\\\,{\\\\text{D  -  Q}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,NULL,NULL,'Easy','Match the following',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(39500,'physics',30,1361,3268,'The displacement equation of the SHM obtained by combining the motions $x_{1}=2 \\sin \\omega t$, $x_{2}=4 \\sin \\left(\\omega t+\\dfrac{\\pi}{6}\\right)$ and $x_{3}=6 \\sin \\left(\\omega t+\\dfrac{\\pi}{3}\\right)$ is.','[{"text": "$x=11.10 \\\\sin \\\\left(\\\\omega t+31.54^{\\\\circ}\\\\right)$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$x=9.24 \\\\sin \\\\left(\\\\omega t+37.1^{\\\\circ}\\\\right)$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$x=8.20 \\\\sin \\\\left(\\\\omega t+23.4^{\\\\circ}\\\\right)$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$x=12.10 \\\\sin \\\\left(\\\\omega t+19.1^{\\\\circ}\\\\right)$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'$x=x_{1}+x_{2}+x_{3}$<br>$\\begin{aligned}=2 & \\sin \\omega t+2 \\sqrt{3} \\sin \\omega t+2 \\cos \\omega t+3 \\sin \\omega t \\\\& +3 \\sqrt{3} \\cos \\omega t \\\\& =(5+2 \\sqrt{3}) \\sin \\omega t+(2+3 \\sqrt{3}) \\cos \\omega t \\\\\\therefore \\quad & x=A \\sin (\\omega t+\\phi)\\end{aligned}$<br>Where $A=\\sqrt{68+32 \\sqrt{3}}$ and $\\phi=\\tan ^{-1}\\left(\\dfrac{2+3 \\sqrt{3}}{5+2 \\sqrt{3}}\\right)$<br>$x = 11.1\\sin (\\omega t + 31.54)^\\circ $',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(39501,'physics',3,313,577,'At the maximum height of a body thrown vertically up','[{"text": "Velocity is not zero but acceleration is zero.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Acceleration is not zero but velocity is zero.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both acceleration and velocity are zero.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both acceleration and velocity are not zero.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,NULL,NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(39502,'physics',32,486,1000,'Two condensers of capacities 2$C$ and $C$ are  joined in parallel and charged upto potential $V$. The battery is removed and the condenser of  capacity $C$ is filled completely with a medium  of dielectric constant $K$. The $p$.$d$. across the  capacitors will now be','[{"text": "$\\\\frac{V}{K}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{V}{{K + 2}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{{3V}}{K}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{{3V}}{{K + 2}}$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'${q_1} = 2CV,\\,{q_2} = CV$<br>Now, condenser of capacity $C$ is filled with  dielectric $K$, therefore ${C_2} = KC$<br>As charge is conserved<br>$\\therefore {q_1} + {q_2} = (KC + 2C)V\' \\Rightarrow V\' = \\frac{{3CV}}{{(K + 2)C}} = \\frac{{3V}}{{K + 2}}$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
