(44185,'physics',38,1065,2539,'A plane electromagnetic wave travelling along the $x$-direction has a wavelength of $3\\,mm$. The variation in the electric field occurs in the $y$ direction with an amplitude $66\\,V{m^{ - 1}}$. The equation for the electric and magnetic fields as a function of $$x$$ and $$t$$ are respectively','[{"text": "$$E_{y}=11 \\\\cos 2 \\\\pi \\\\times 10^{11}\\\\left(t-\\\\dfrac{x}{c}\\\\right)$$ and$$B_{z}=11 \\\\times 10^{-7} \\\\cos 2 \\\\pi \\\\times 10^{11}\\\\left(t-\\\\dfrac{x}{c}\\\\right)$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$E_{x}=33 \\\\cos \\\\pi \\\\times 10^{11}\\\\left(t-\\\\dfrac{x}{c}\\\\right)$$ and$$B_{z}=11 \\\\times 10^{-7} \\\\cos \\\\pi \\\\times 10^{11}\\\\left(t-\\\\dfrac{x}{c}\\\\right)$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$E_{y}=33 \\\\cos \\\\pi \\\\times 10^{11}\\\\left(t-\\\\dfrac{x}{c}\\\\right)$$ and$$B_{z}=1.1 \\\\times 10^{-7} \\\\cos \\\\pi \\\\times 10^{11}\\\\left(t-\\\\dfrac{x}{c}\\\\right)$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$E_{y}=66 \\\\cos 2 \\\\pi \\\\times 10^{11}\\\\left(t-\\\\dfrac{x}{c}\\\\right)$$ and$$B_{z}=2.2 \\\\times 10^{-7} \\\\cos 2 \\\\pi \\\\times 10^{11}\\\\left(t-\\\\dfrac{x}{c}\\\\right)$$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Here, $\\lambda  = 3\\,mm = 3 \\times {10^{ - 3}}\\,m,\\,{E_0} = 66\\,V{m^{ - 1}}$<br>$\\therefore {B_0} = \\frac{{{E_0}}}{c} = \\frac{{66}}{{3 \\times {{10}^8}}} = 2.2 \\times {10^{ - 7}}\\,T$<br>As, electromagnetic wave is propagating along $x$-axis and electric field oscillation is long $y$ direction using the relation for harmonic wave<br>$$\\begin{aligned}\\mathrm{E}_{\\mathrm{y}} & =\\mathrm{E}_{0} \\cos \\dfrac{2 \\pi}{\\lambda}(\\mathrm{ct}-\\mathrm{x}) \\\\E_{y} & =E_{0} \\cos \\dfrac{2 \\pi c}{\\lambda}(t-x / c) \\\\E_{y} & =66 \\cos \\dfrac{2 \\pi \\times 3 \\times 10^{8}}{3 \\times 10^{-3}}(t-x / c) \\\\& =66 \\cos 2 \\pi \\times 10^{11}(t-x / c) \\\\B_{z} & =B_{0} \\cos \\dfrac{2 \\pi}{\\lambda}(t-x / c)\\end{aligned}$$<br>$$=2.2 \\times 10^{-7} \\cos 2 \\pi \\times 10^{11}(t-x / c)$$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44186,'physics',37,972,2260,'A direct current of 5$A$ is superimposed on an alternating current $I = 10\\sin \\omega t$ flowing through a wire. The effective value of the resulting current will be','[{"text": "$15\\\\,A$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$5\\\\sqrt 5 \\\\,A$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$5\\\\sqrt 3 \\\\,A$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$(15/2)A$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Given $l = 5 + 10\\sin \\omega t$<br>${I_{eff}} = {\\left[ {\\frac{{\\int_0^T {{I^2}dt} }}{{\\int_0^T {dt} }}} \\right]^{1/2}} = {\\left[ {\\frac{1}{T}\\int_0^T {{{(5 + 10\\sin \\omega t)}^2}dt} } \\right]^{1/2}}$<br>$ = {\\left[ {\\frac{1}{T}\\int_0^T {(25 + 100\\sin \\omega t + 100{{\\sin }^2}\\omega t)dt} } \\right]^{1/2}}$<br>But as $\\frac{1}{T}\\int_0^T {\\sin \\omega t\\,dt = 0} $<br>so ${I_{eff}} = {\\left[ {25 + \\frac{1}{2} \\times 100} \\right]^{1/2}} = 5\\sqrt 3 A$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
