(44208,'physics',39,1106,2633,'Match the following.<div class="matching-question"><div class="column-left">A. Stopping potential<br>B. Work -function<br>C. De-Broglie wavelength</div><div class="column-right">P. $\\frac{h}{p}$<br>Q. $E - {K_{high{\\text{ }}}}$<br>R. ${K_{high{\\text{ }}}}/e$</div></div>','[{"text": "${\\\\rm{A - R,}}\\\\,{\\\\rm{B - Q,}}\\\\,{\\\\rm{C - P}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{A - P,}}\\\\,{\\\\rm{B - Q,}}\\\\,{\\\\rm{C - R}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{A - Q,}}\\\\,{\\\\rm{B - P,}}\\\\,{\\\\rm{C - R}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{A - Q,}}\\\\,{\\\\rm{B - R,}}\\\\,{\\\\rm{C - P}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Option (1) is correct.',NULL,'Easy','Match the following',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44209,'physics',38,1066,2543,'Which of the following statement does not hold good for thermal radiation?','[{"text": "The wavelength changes when it travels from one medium to another.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "The frequency changes when it travels from one medium to another.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "The speed changes when it travels from one medium to antother.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "They travel in straight line in a given medium", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'When radiation travels from one medium to another, its wavelength and speed changes whereas frequency remains unchanged.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2009,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44211,'physics',37,972,2635,'Alternating current can not be measured by $dc$ ammeter because','[{"text": "$ac$ is virtual", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$ac$ cannot pass through $dc$ ammeter", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Average value of current is zero", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$ac$ changes its direction", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'In $dc$ ammter, a coil is free to rotate in the magnetic field of a fixed magnet. If an alternating current is passed through such a coil, the torque will reverse its direction each time the current changes direction and the average value of the torque will be zero.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44212,'physics',39,1104,2631,'Choose the correct statement','[{"text": "Any charged particle in rest is accompanied by matter waves.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Any uncharged particle in rest is accompanied by matter waves.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "The matter waves are waves of zero amplitude.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "The matter waves are waves of probability amplitude.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,NULL,NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44213,'physics',38,1067,2544,'Electromagnetic radiation used to sterilise milk is','[{"text": "$X$-rays", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$\\\\gamma$$ - rays", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$UV$ rays", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Radiowaves", "isCorrect": false}]',2,NULL,NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2016,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44214,'physics',37,977,2771,'A step-down transformer reduces the voltage of a transmission line from 2200 $V$ to 220 $V$. The power delivered by it is 880 $W$ and its efficiency is 88%. The input current is:','[{"text": "$4.65\\\\,mA$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$0.045A$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$0.45A$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$4.65A$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Efficiency of transformer, <br>$\\eta  = \\frac{{Output\\,power}}{{Input\\,power}}$<br>$ \\Rightarrow \\frac{{88}}{{100}} = \\frac{{880}}{{{P_i}}} \\Rightarrow {P_i} = 1000W$<br>Input current, ${I_i} = \\frac{{{P_i}}}{{{V_i}}} = \\frac{{1000}}{{2200}} = 0.45A$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44216,'physics',39,1104,2631,'The incorrect statement is','[{"text": "Material wave (de Broglie wave) can travel in vacuum.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Electromagnetic wave can travel through vacuum.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "The velocity of photon is the same as light passes through any medium.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Wavelength of de Broglie wave depends upon velocity.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Velocity of a photon depends on medium',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44217,'physics',38,1065,2539,'The source of electromagnetic wave can be a charge.','[{"text": "Moving with a constant velocity", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Moving in a circular orbit", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "At rest", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Moving parallel to the magnetic field", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Accelerated charges can produce electromagnetic waves. Since a charge moving in a circular orbit is accelerating continuously, it will emit $EM$ waves.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2011,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44218,'physics',37,977,2770,'A transformer of 100% efficiency has 200 turns in the primary and 40000 turns in secondary. It is connected to a 220 $V$ main supply and secondary feeds to a $100\\,k\\Omega $ resistance. The potential difference per turn is:','[{"text": "$1.1\\\\,V$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$25\\\\,V$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$18\\\\,V$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$11\\\\,V$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'From transformer ratio,<br>$\\frac{{{V_s}}}{{{V_p}}} = \\frac{{{N_s}}}{{{N_p}}} \\Rightarrow {V_s} = \\frac{{{V_p} \\times {N_s}}}{{{N_p}}}$<br>${V_s} = \\frac{{220 \\times 40000}}{{200}} = 44000V$<br>Potential difference per turn is<br>$\\frac{{{V_s}}}{{{N_s}}} = \\frac{{44000}}{{40000}} = 1.1V$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44219,'physics',36,906,2095,'A square loop of area $25\\,c{m^2}$ has a resistance of $10 \\Omega$. The loop is placed in uniform magnetic field of magnitude $40.0\\,T.$ The plane of loop is perpendicular to the magnetic field. The work done in pulling the loop out of the magnetic field slowly and uniformly in $1.0\\,\\sec ,$ will be','[{"text": "$1.0 \\\\times {10^{ - 3}}\\\\,J$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$2.5 \\\\times {10^{ - 3}}\\\\,J$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$5 \\\\times {10^{ - 3}}\\\\,J$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1.0 \\\\times {10^{ - 1}}\\\\,J$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Given<br>$A = 25\\,c{m^2},R = 10\\Omega ,B = 40T,t = 1\\,s$<br>Let the length of side is $l$.<br>$A = {l^2} \\Rightarrow 25 = {l^2} \\Rightarrow l = 5\\,cm$<br>so, $v = \\frac{l}{t} = \\frac{{0.05}}{1} = 0.05\\,m/s$<br>Voltage, $V = Bvl = 40 \\times 0.05 \\times 0.05 = 0.1\\,V$<br>Current, $i = \\frac{V}{R} = \\frac{{0.1}}{{10}} = 0.01\\,A$<br>Force $=\\operatorname{Bil} \\times \\sin 90^{\\circ}$<br>Work $=$ Force distance<br>$=40 \\times 0.01 \\times 0.05 \\times 0.05$<br>$W = 1 \\times {10^{ - 3}}\\,J$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2023,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44220,'physics',39,1104,2631,'Matter waves are:','[{"text": "Electromagnetic waves", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Mechanical waves", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Either mechanical or electromagnetic waves", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Neither mechanical nor electromagnetic waves", "isCorrect": true}]',3,NULL,NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44221,'physics',38,1066,2543,'$X$-rays, gamma rays and microwaves travelling in vacuum have','[{"text": "Same wavelengths but different velocities", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Same frequency but different velocities", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Same velocity but different wavelengths", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Same velocity and same frequency.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'In vacuum $X$-rays, gamma rays and microwaves travelling with same velocity, i.e., with the velocity of light $c( = 3 \\times {10^8}\\,m/s)$ but different wavelengths.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2012,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44222,'physics',37,977,2771,'A transformer with efficiency 80% works at 4 $kW$ and 100 $V$. If the secondary voltage is 200 $V$ then the primary and secondary currents are respectively:','[{"text": "$40A,16A$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$16A,40A$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$20A,40A$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$40A,10A$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Efficiency,$\\eta  = \\frac{{output\\,power}}{{Input\\,power}}$<br>$ \\Rightarrow \\eta  = \\frac{{{V_s}{I_s}}}{{{V_p}{I_p}}} \\Rightarrow \\frac{{80}}{{100}} = \\frac{{200 \\times {I_s}}}{{4000}} \\Rightarrow {I_s} = 16A$<br>Also,${V_p}{I_p} = 4000 \\Rightarrow {I_p} = \\frac{{4000}}{{100}} = 40A$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44224,'physics',39,1104,2631,'Which of the following particles - neutron, proton, electron and deuteron has the lowest energy if all have the same de Broglie wavelength?','[{"text": "Neutron", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Proton", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Electron", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Deuteron", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'$$\\lambda=\\dfrac{h}{\\sqrt{2 m E}} \\Rightarrow E=\\dfrac{h^{2}}{2 m \\lambda^{2}}$$<br>$$E \\alpha \\dfrac{1}{m}$$<br>As mass for deuteron is maximum so its energy is minimum.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44225,'physics',38,1065,2539,'Suppose that the electric field amplitude of electromagnetic wave is ${E_0} = 120\\,N{C^{ - 1}}$ and its frequency if $f = 50MHz$. Then which of the following value is incorrectly computed?','[{"text": "Magnetic field amplitude is $400\\\\,nT$.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Angular frequency of $EM$ wave is $\\\\pi  \\\\times {10^8}\\\\,rad/s$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Propagation constant (angular wave number) is $2.1\\\\,rad/m$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Wavelength of $EM$ wave is $6\\\\,m.$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'<br>Magnetic field amplitude $$B_{0}=\\dfrac{E_{0}}{c}=\\dfrac{120}{3 \\times 10^{8}}$$<br>$$=400 n T$$<br>Angular frequency $$\\omega=2 \\pi f=2 \\pi \\times 50 \\times 10^{6}$$<br>$$=\\pi \\times 10^{8} \\mathrm{rad} / \\mathrm{s}$$<br>Wave length $\\lambda  = \\frac{c}{f} = \\frac{{3 \\times {{10}^8}}}{{50 \\times {{10}^6}}} = 6\\,m$<br>Angular wave number $$k=\\dfrac{2 \\pi}{\\lambda}=\\dfrac{2 \\pi}{6}$$<br>$ \\approx 2.1\\,rad/m$<br>Hence option (3) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,'NEET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44226,'physics',37,977,2281,'Transformers are used in','[{"text": "$DC$ circuits only", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$AC$ circuits only", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both $AC$ and $DC$ circuits", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Integrated circuits", "isCorrect": false}]',1,NULL,NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44227,'physics',39,1104,2631,'Moving with the same velocity, one of the following has the longest de Broglie wavelength','[{"text": "$$\\\\beta$$-particle", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$$\\\\alpha$$-particle", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Proton", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Neutron", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'$$\\lambda=\\dfrac{h}{m v} \\Rightarrow \\lambda \\alpha \\dfrac{1}{m}$$<br>$\\therefore $ $\\beta $ particle',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44228,'physics',38,1065,2539,'An electromagnetic wave, going through vacuum is described by $$E=E_{0} \\sin (k x-\\omega t)$$.<br>Which of the following is independent of wavelength?','[{"text": "$$k \\\\omega$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$k$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$k / \\\\omega$$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$$\\\\omega$$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'$$\\dfrac{K}{\\omega}=\\dfrac{1}{v}$$<br>Where $$v$$ is the velocity of electromagnetic wave, which is independent of wavelength of a wave but depends upon the nature of medium of propagation of wave.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2010,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44229,'physics',37,977,2281,'The core of a transformer is laminated so that','[{"text": "Energy loss due to eddy currents may be \\treduced", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Rusting of the core may be prevented", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Change in flux may be increased", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Ratio of voltage in the primary to that in \\tthe secondary may be increased", "isCorrect": false}]',0,NULL,NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
