(44231,'physics',37,977,2770,'A step up transformer is connected on the primary side to a $DC$ battery which can deliver a large current. If a bulb is connected in the secondary, then','[{"text": "The bulb will glow very bright", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "The bulb will get fused", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "The bulb will glow, but with less brightness", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "The bulb will not glow", "isCorrect": true}]',3,NULL,NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44232,'physics',38,1065,2540,'A plane electromagnetic wave of frequency 20 $MHz$ travels through a space along $x$ direction. If the electric field vector at a certain point in space is $6\\,\\,V\\,{m^{ - 1}}$, what is the magnetic field vector at that point?','[{"text": "$2\\\\,\\\\,T$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2 \\\\times {10^{ - 8}}\\\\,\\\\,T$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{1}{2}\\\\,\\\\,T$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{1}{2} \\\\times {10^{ - 8}}\\\\,\\\\,T$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'The magnitude of magnetic field is<br>$B = \\frac{E}{c} = \\frac{{6V{m^{ - 1}}}}{{3 \\times {{10}^8}\\,m{s^{ - 1}}}} = 2 \\times {10^{ - 8}}\\,T$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2014,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44233,'physics',39,1104,2631,'An electron of mass $$m$$ when accelerated through a potential difference has de Broglie wavelength $$\\lambda$$. The de Broglie wavelength associated with a proton of mass $M$ accelerated through the same potential difference will be','[{"text": "$\\\\frac{{\\\\lambda mM}}{{m + M}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\lambda \\\\sqrt {\\\\frac{M}{m}} $", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\lambda \\\\sqrt {\\\\frac{m}{M}} $", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\lambda $", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'<br>As, $$\\lambda=\\dfrac{h}{\\sqrt{2 m E}}=\\dfrac{h}{\\sqrt{2 m q V}} \\Rightarrow \\lambda \\propto \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{m}}$$<br>where, $$m=$$ mass of the charge<br>$$\\begin{aligned}& \\Rightarrow \\dfrac{\\lambda_{1}}{\\lambda_{2}}=\\sqrt{\\dfrac{M}{m}} \\\\& \\Rightarrow \\lambda_{2}=\\lambda \\sqrt{\\dfrac{m}{M}} \\quad\\left(\\because \\lambda_{1}=\\lambda\\right)\\end{aligned}$$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44234,'physics',37,977,2281,'A transformer changes the voltage','[{"text": "Without changing the current and frequency", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Without changing the current but changes the frequency", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Without changing the frequency but changes the current", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Without changing the frequency as well as the current", "isCorrect": false}]',2,NULL,NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44235,'physics',38,1065,2539,'The electric field associated with an electromagnetic wave in vacuum is given by $\\overrightarrow E  = \\hat i40\\cos \\left( {kz - 6 \\times {{10}^8}t} \\right)$, where $$E, z$$ and $$t$$ are in volt $/m$, metre and second respectively. The value of wave vector $$k$$ is','[{"text": "$$2 m^{-1}$$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$$6 m^{-1}$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$3 m^{-1}$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$0.5 m^{-1}$$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Electromagnetic wave equation is given by<br>$E = {E_0}\\cos (kz - \\omega t)\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,(1)$<br>Speed of electromagnetic wave, $$v=\\dfrac{\\omega}{k}$$<br>Given, equation is<br>$E = \\hat i40\\cos \\left( {kz - 6 \\times {{10}^8}t} \\right)\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,(2)$<br>Comparing Eqs. (1) and (2), we get<br>$$\\omega=6 \\times 10^{8} \\text { and } E_{0}=40 \\hat{i}$$<br>Here, wave factor, $k = \\frac{\\omega }{v} = \\frac{{6 \\times {{10}^8}}}{{3 \\times {{10}^8}}} = 2\\,{m^{ - 1}}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,'NEET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44236,'physics',39,1104,2631,'The ratio of de-Broglie wavelengths of molecules of hydrogen and helium which are at temperature $27^\\circ C$ and $127^\\circ C$ respectively is','[{"text": "1", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\sqrt {\\\\frac{8}{3}} $", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$$\\\\sqrt{\\\\dfrac{3}{8}}$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$\\\\dfrac{1}{2}$$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Wavelength $\\lambda  = \\frac{h}{{\\sqrt {3mKT} }}$<br>$ = \\frac{{{\\lambda _H}}}{{{\\lambda _{He}}}} = \\sqrt {\\frac{{{m_{He}}{T_{He}}}}{{{m_H}{T_H}}}}  = \\sqrt {\\frac{{4(273 + 127)}}{{2(273 + 27)}}}  = \\sqrt {\\frac{8}{3}} $',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44237,'physics',37,976,2280,'The natural frequency of a $L$-$C$ circuit is equal to','[{"text": "$\\\\frac{1}{{2\\\\pi }}\\\\sqrt {LC} $", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{1}{{2\\\\pi \\\\sqrt {LC} }}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{1}{{2\\\\pi }}\\\\sqrt {\\\\frac{C}{L}} $", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{1}{{2\\\\pi }}\\\\sqrt {\\\\frac{L}{C}} $", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'$\\omega  = \\frac{1}{{\\sqrt {LC} }} \\Rightarrow f = \\frac{1}{{2\\pi \\sqrt {LC} }}$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44238,'physics',38,1067,2544,'The condition under which a microwave oven heats up a food item containing water molecules most efficiently is','[{"text": "The frequency of the microwave must match the resonant frequency of the water molecules", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Infra-red waves produce heating in a microwave oven", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "The frequency of the microwave has no relation with natural frequency of water molecules", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Microwave are heat waves, so always produce heating", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'It is an electromagnetic wave. The frequency of the microwave oven must match the resonant frequency of the water molecules.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44239,'physics',39,1104,2631,'The de Broglie wavelength of a neutron at $27^\\circ C$ is $$\\lambda_{0}$$. What will be its wavelength at $$927^{\\circ} C$$ ?','[{"text": "$$\\\\lambda / 3$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$3 \\\\lambda / 2$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$\\\\lambda / 4$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$\\\\lambda / 2$$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'The de Broglie wavelength associated with it is,<br>$$\\lambda=\\dfrac{h}{\\sqrt{2 m E}}=\\dfrac{h}{\\sqrt{2 m \\times \\dfrac{3}{2} k T}}$$<br>i.e., $$\\quad \\lambda \\propto \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{T}}$$<br>$$\\therefore \\quad \\dfrac{\\lambda_{927}}{\\lambda_{27}}=\\sqrt{\\dfrac{27+273}{927+273}}=\\sqrt{\\dfrac{300}{1200}}=\\dfrac{1}{2}$$<br>$$\\Rightarrow \\lambda_{927}=\\dfrac{\\lambda_{27}}{2}=\\dfrac{\\lambda}{2}$$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44240,'physics',37,976,2280,'A fully charged capacitor $C$ with initial charge ${q_0}$ is connected to a coil of self inductance $L$ at $t = 0$.The time at which the energy is stored equally between the electric and the magnetic fields is','[{"text": "$\\\\frac{\\\\pi }{4}\\\\sqrt {LC} $", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\sqrt {LC} $", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2\\\\pi \\\\sqrt {LC} $", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\pi \\\\sqrt {LC} $", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'As initial charge is maximum <br>$q = {q_0}\\cos \\omega t$<br>$ \\Rightarrow i = \\frac{{dq}}{{dt}} =  - \\omega {q_0}\\sin \\omega t$<br>Given $\\frac{1}{2}L{i^2} = \\frac{{{q^2}}}{{2C}}$<br>$ \\Rightarrow \\frac{1}{2}L{(\\omega {q_0}\\sin \\omega t)^2} = \\frac{{{{({q_0}\\cos \\omega t)}^2}}}{{2C}}$<br>But,$\\omega  = \\frac{1}{{\\sqrt {LC} }} \\Rightarrow \\tan \\omega t = 1$<br>$\\omega t = \\frac{\\pi }{4}\\quad  \\Rightarrow t = \\frac{\\pi }{{4\\omega }} = \\frac{\\pi }{4}\\sqrt {LC} $',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44241,'physics',38,1065,2541,'A light beam of intensity $20\\,W\\,c{m^{ - 2}}$ is incident normally on a perfectly reflecting surface of sides $25\\,cm \\times 15\\,cm$. The momentum imparted to the surface by the light per second is','[{"text": "$$5 \\\\times 10^{-5} \\\\mathrm{~kg} \\\\mathrm{~ms}^{-1}$$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$$1.2 \\\\times 10^{-5} \\\\mathrm{~kg} \\\\mathrm{~ms}^{-1}$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$2 \\\\times 10^{-5} \\\\mathrm{~kg} \\\\mathrm{~ms}^{-1}$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$1 \\\\times 10^{-5} \\\\mathrm{~kg} \\\\mathrm{~ms}^{-1}$$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'<br>Given that, $I = 20\\,\\,W\\,c{m^{ - 2}},$<br>Area $ = 25\\,cm \\times 15\\,cm$<br>For a perfectly reflecting surface,<br>$\\dot P = \\frac{{2\\dot E}}{c}$<br>$\\dot E = I \\times A = 20 \\times 25 \\times 15 = 7500\\,W$<br>$\\therefore \\quad \\dot P = \\frac{{2 \\times 7500}}{{3 \\times {{10}^8}}} = 5 \\times {10^{ - 5}}\\,kg\\,m{s^{ - 1}}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2020,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44242,'physics',39,1104,2631,'The de-Broglie wavelength of an electron and the wavelength of a photon are the same. The ratio between the energy of the photon and the momentum of electron is ($c$ = velocity of light, $h$ = Planck\'s constant):','[{"text": "$h$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$c$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{1}{h}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{1}{c}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'For photon, $$E=\\dfrac{h c}{\\lambda}$$<br>But $$\\lambda_{\\text {Photon }}=\\lambda_{e}=\\dfrac{h}{P_{e}}\\left(\\lambda_{\\text {Photon }}=\\lambda\\right)$$<br>$$\\Rightarrow \\dfrac{E}{P_{e}}=\\dfrac{h c / \\lambda}{h / \\lambda}=c$$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44243,'physics',37,973,2268,'A coil has an inductance of 0.7 henry and is joined in series with a resistance of $220\\Omega $. When the alternating emf of $220V$ at 50 $Hz$ is applied to it then the phase through which current lags behind the applied emf will be','[{"text": "$30^\\\\circ $", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$45^\\\\circ $", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$60^\\\\circ $", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$None\\\\,\\\\,of\\\\,\\\\,these$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'$L = 0.7\\,H,R = 220\\Omega ,$<br>${E_0} = 220V,v = 50Hz$<br>This is an $L - R$ circuit<br>Phase difference,<br>$\\tan \\phi  = \\frac{{{X_L}}}{R} = \\frac{{\\omega L}}{R} = \\frac{{2\\pi vL}}{R}$<br>$[{X_L} = 2\\pi vL = 2 \\times \\frac{{22}}{7} \\times 50 \\times 0.7 = 220\\Omega ]$<br>$ = \\frac{{220}}{{220}} = 1 \\Rightarrow \\phi  = {45^0}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45957,'physics',41,406,806,'$99\\% $ of a radioactive element will decay between','[{"text": "7 and 8 half-lives", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "6 and 7 half-lives", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "8 and 9 half-lives", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "9 half-lives", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'$N = {N_0}{\\left( {\\frac{1}{2}} \\right)^n} = \\frac{1}{{100}}$<br>$ \\Rightarrow {2^n} = 100.$<br>$n$ comes out in between 6 and 7.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44244,'physics',38,1065,2539,'Which of the following statement is false for the properties of electromagnetic waves?','[{"text": "The energy in electromagnetic wave is divided equally between electric and magnetic vectors.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "These waves do not require any material medium for propagation.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both electric and magnetic field vectors are parallel to each other and perpendicular to the direction of propagation of wave.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both electric and magnetic field vectors attain the maxima and minima at the same place and same time.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'The time varying electric and magnetic fields are mutually perpendicular to each other and also perpendicular to the direction of propagation.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44245,'physics',39,1104,2631,'The ratio of the respective de-Broglie wavelength associated with electrons accelerated from rest with the voltages $100\\,V,\\,200\\,V$ and $300\\,V$ is','[{"text": "$$1: 2: 3$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$1: 4: 9$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$1: \\\\dfrac{1}{\\\\sqrt{2}}: \\\\dfrac{1}{\\\\sqrt{3}}$$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$$1: \\\\dfrac{1}{2}: \\\\dfrac{1}{3}$$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'As we know, de-Broglie wavelength<br>$$\\begin{gathered}\\lambda=\\dfrac{h}{\\sqrt{2 m e V}} \\text { or } \\lambda=\\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{V}} \\\\\\therefore \\lambda_{1}: \\lambda_{2}: \\lambda_{3}=\\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{V_{1}}}: \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{V_{2}}}: \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{V_{3}}} \\\\=\\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{100}}: \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{200}}: \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{300}} \\\\=1: \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}: \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\end{gathered}$$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44246,'physics',37,974,2274,'The power factor of an $R$-$L$ circuit is $\\frac{1}{{\\sqrt 2 }}$. If the frequency of $AC$ is doubled, then what will be the power factor?','[{"text": "$\\\\frac{1}{{\\\\sqrt 3 }}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{1}{{\\\\sqrt 5 }}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{1}{{\\\\sqrt 7 }}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{1}{{\\\\sqrt {11} }}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Power factor,<br>$\\cos \\phi  = \\frac{1}{{\\sqrt 2 }} = \\frac{R}{{\\sqrt {{R^2} + {\\omega ^2}{L^2}} }} \\Rightarrow {\\omega ^2}{L^2} = {R^2}$<br>When $\\omega $ is doubled, we find <br>$\\cos \\phi \' = \\frac{R}{{\\sqrt {{R^2} + 4{\\omega ^2}{L^2}} }} = \\frac{R}{{\\sqrt {{R^2} + 4{R^2}} }} = \\frac{1}{{\\sqrt 5 }}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44247,'physics',38,1065,2539,'An electromagnetic wave is travelling in $x$-direction with electric field vector given by, $\\overrightarrow E  = {E_0}\\sin (Kx - \\omega t)\\widehat j$. The correct expression for magnetic field vector is','[{"text": "$\\\\overrightarrow {{B_Y}}  = \\\\frac{{{E_0}}}{c}\\\\sin (Kx - \\\\omega t)\\\\hat k$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\overrightarrow {{B_Y}}  = {E_0}c\\\\sin (Kx - \\\\omega t)\\\\hat k$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\overrightarrow {{B_z}}  = \\\\frac{{{E_0}}}{c}\\\\sin (Kx - \\\\omega t)\\\\hat k$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\overrightarrow {{B_z}}  = {E_0}c\\\\sin (Kx - \\\\omega t)\\\\hat k$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'<br><img src=\'/img/data/physics/38_44247_explanation_1.jpg\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' /> <br>The electromagnetic wave is propagating along $$x$$-axis and $E$ component is along $$y$$-axis. So B component should be along $$z$$-axis. The correct option is (3).',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2019,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44248,'physics',39,1104,2631,'If the kinetic energy of the particle is increased by 16 times, the percentage change in the de-Broglie wavelength of the particle is:','[{"text": "$$25 \\\\%$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$75 \\\\%$$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$$60 \\\\%$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$50 \\\\%$$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'For de-Broglie wavelength,<br>$$\\lambda=\\dfrac{h}{p}=\\dfrac{h}{\\sqrt{2 m K}} \\Rightarrow \\lambda \\alpha \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{K}}$$<br>Hence, $$\\lambda_{2}=\\dfrac{\\lambda_{1}}{4}$$<br>$$\\dfrac{\\lambda_{2}-\\lambda_{1}}{\\lambda_{1}} \\times 100=75 \\%$$<br>There is $$75 \\%$$ change in the wavelength.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44249,'physics',37,974,2272,'In an  $AC$ circuit with voltage $V$ and current $I$, the power dissipated is','[{"text": "Depends on the phase between $V$ and $I$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{1}{2}VI$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{1}{{\\\\sqrt 2 }}VI$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$VI$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'$$\\because \\,$$ $P = VI\\cos \\phi ,\\therefore P \\propto \\cos \\phi $',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44250,'physics',38,1065,2539,'The elecric field part of an electromagnetic wave in a medium is represented by $$E_{X}=0$$;<br>${E_y} = 2.5\\frac{N}{C}\\cos \\left[ {\\left( {2\\pi  \\times {{10}^6}\\frac{{rad}}{m}} \\right)t} \\right.$<br>$\\left. { - \\left( {\\pi  \\times {{10}^{ - 2}}\\frac{{rad}}{s}} \\right)x} \\right];\\quad {E_z} = 0.{\\rm{ The wave is }}$','[{"text": "Moving along-$x$-direction with frequency ${10^6}HZ$ and wavelength $100\\\\,m$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Moving along $$y$$-direction with frequency $2\\\\pi  \\\\times {10^6}HZ$ and wavelength $200\\\\,m$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Moving along $$x$$-direction with frequency ${10^6}HZ$ and wavelength $200\\\\,m$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Moving along $$x$$-direction with frequency ${10^6}HZ$ and wavelength $300\\\\,m$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Comparing the given equation<br>$$E_{y}=2.5 \\dfrac{N}{C} \\cos \\left[\\left(2 \\pi \\times 10^{6} \\dfrac{\\mathrm{rad}}{\\mathrm{m}}\\right) t-\\left(\\pi \\times 10^{-2} \\dfrac{\\mathrm{rad}}{\\mathrm{sec}}\\right) x\\right]$$<br>With the standard equation<br>$$E_{y}=E_{0} \\cos (\\omega t-k x)$$<br>we get<br>$\\omega  = 2\\pi f = 2\\pi  \\times {10^6}$<br>$\\therefore \\quad f = {10^6}\\,Hz$<br>Moreover, we know that<br>$$\\dfrac{2 \\pi}{\\lambda}=k=\\pi \\times 10^{-2} m^{-1} \\Rightarrow \\lambda=200 m$$<br>As the direction of field of electromagnetic wave is in $y$-direction so, the wave is moving along positive $x$-direction with frequency ${10^6}HZ$ and wavelength $200\\,m$.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44251,'physics',39,1104,2631,'An electron and a photon, each has a wavelength of $1.2\\mathop A\\limits^o $. What is the ratio of their energies?','[{"text": "$$1: 10^{2}$$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$$1: 10$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$1: 10^{4}$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$1: 10^{3}$$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'$$\\dfrac{E_{e}}{E_{p h}}=\\dfrac{h^{2}}{2 m \\lambda^{2}} \\times \\dfrac{\\lambda}{h c}=\\dfrac{h}{2 m \\lambda c}$$<br>$$\\begin{aligned}& =\\dfrac{6.6 \\times 10^{-34}}{2 \\times 9.1 \\times 10^{-31} \\times 1.2 \\times 10^{-10} \\times 3 \\times 10^{8}} \\\\& =\\dfrac{1}{100}\\end{aligned}$$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44252,'physics',37,974,2272,'If the power factor changes from $\\frac{1}{2}$ to $\\frac{1}{4}$ keeping resistance unchanged then what is the increase in impedance in $AC$?','[{"text": "$40\\\\% $", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$50\\\\% $", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$25\\\\% $", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$100\\\\% $", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Power factor, $\\cos \\phi  = \\frac{R}{Z}$<br>If $R$ is constant, then $\\cos \\phi \\,\\alpha \\frac{1}{Z}$<br>$\\therefore \\frac{{{Z_1}}}{{{Z_2}}} = \\frac{{\\cos {\\phi _2}}}{{\\cos {\\phi _1}}} = \\frac{{1/4}}{{1/2}} = \\frac{1}{2} \\Rightarrow {Z_2} = 2{Z_1}$<br>$\\therefore $ Percentage change  $ = \\frac{{2{Z_1} - {Z_1}}}{{{Z_1}}} \\times 100 = 100\\% $',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44253,'physics',38,1066,2543,'Match List - I with List - II<br>Choose the correct answer from the options given below<div class="matching-question"><div class="column-left">A. AM radio waves<br>B. Microwaves<br>C. Infrared radiations<br>D. $X$-rays</div><div class="column-right">P. ${10^{ - 10}}\\,m$<br>Q. ${10^2}\\,m$<br>R. ${10^{ - 2}}\\,m$<br>S. ${10^{ - 4}}\\,m$</div></div>','[{"text": "(A) - (R), (B) - (Q), (C) - (P), (D) - (S)", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "(A) - (R), (B) - (S), (C) - (Q), (D) - (R)", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "(A) - (Q), (B) - (R), (C) - (S), (D) - (P)", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "(A) - (S), (B) - (R), (C) - (Q), (D) - (P)", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'${\\rm{(A)Radiowave(Q)}} \\approx {10^2}\\,m{\\rm{(Q)}}$<br>${\\rm{(B)Microwave}} \\approx {\\rm{(R)}}{10^{ - 2}}\\,m{\\rm{(R)}}$<br>${\\rm{(C)Infrared}}\\,{\\rm{radiations}} \\approx {\\rm{(S)}}{10^{ - 4}}\\,m{\\rm{(S)}}$<br>$(D)X - ray(P) \\approx \\mathop A\\limits^o  = {10^{ - 10}}\\,m(P)$<br>$$\\Rightarrow$$ (A) - (Q), (B) - (R), (C) - (S), (D) - (P)',NULL,'Medium','Match the following',2022,'NEET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44254,'physics',39,1103,2630,'An electron and photon have same wavelength. If $$E$$ is the energy of photon and $$P$$ is the momentum of electron, then the magnitude of $$\\dfrac{E}{p}$$ in $SI$ unit is','[{"text": "$$3.0 \\\\times 10^{8}$$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$$3.33 \\\\times 10^{-9}$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$9 \\\\times 10^{16}$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$1.1 \\\\times 10^{-19}$$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'For electron, $p = \\frac{h}{\\lambda }$; and for photon, $E = \\frac{{hc}}{\\lambda }$<br>$\\therefore \\quad \\frac{E}{p} = \\frac{{hc/\\lambda }}{{h/\\lambda }} = c = 3 \\times {10^8}\\,m{s^{ - 1}}$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44255,'physics',37,974,2272,'In an ac circuit, $V$ and $I$ are given by $V = 100\\sin (100t)volts,$ $I = 100\\sin \\left( {100t + \\frac{\\pi }{3}} \\right)mA$. The power dissipated in the circuit is','[{"text": "$2.5\\\\,watt$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$5\\\\,watt$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${10^4}\\\\,watt$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$10\\\\,watt$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'$P = {V_{r.m.s.}} \\times {i_{r.m.s.}} \\times \\cos \\phi $<br>$ = \\frac{{100}}{{\\sqrt 2 }} \\times \\frac{{100 \\times {{10}^{ - 3}}}}{{\\sqrt 2 }} \\times \\cos \\frac{\\pi }{3}$<br>$ = \\frac{{{{10}^4} \\times {{10}^{ - 3}}}}{2} \\times \\frac{1}{2} = \\frac{{10}}{4} = 2.5\\,Watt$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44256,'physics',38,1065,2539,'When light propagates through a material medium of relative permittivity $$\\epsilon_{\\mathrm{r}}$$ and relative permeability $$\\mu_{r}$$, the velocity of light, $$v$$ is given by: (c-velocity of light in vacuum)','[{"text": "$v = {\\\\rm{ }}\\\\sqrt {\\\\frac{{{\\\\mu _r}}}{{{ \\\\in _r}}}} $", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$v = \\\\sqrt {\\\\frac{{{ \\\\in _r}}}{{{\\\\mu _r}}}} $", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$v = \\\\frac{c}{{\\\\sqrt {{ \\\\in _r}{\\\\mu _r}} }}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$v = c$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'$n = \\sqrt {{ \\in _r}{\\mu _r}} $<br>$n = \\frac{c}{v} \\Rightarrow v = \\frac{c}{n}$<br>$v = \\left( {\\frac{c}{{\\sqrt {{ \\in _r}{\\mu _r}} }}} \\right)$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2022,'NEET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44257,'physics',39,1103,2630,'A photosensitive surface is receiving light of wavelength $5000\\mathop A\\limits^o $ at the rate of ${10^{ - 7}}J{s^{ - 1}}$. The number of photons ejected per second is:','[{"text": "$$2.5 \\\\times 10^{12}$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$2.5 \\\\times 10^{11}$$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$$2.5 \\\\times 10^{10}$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$2.5 \\\\times 10^{9}$$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'$$E=n h v=n\\left(\\dfrac{h c}{\\lambda}\\right) \\Rightarrow \\dfrac{E}{t}=\\left(\\dfrac{n}{t}\\right)\\left(\\dfrac{h c}{\\lambda}\\right)$$<br>$ \\Rightarrow {10^{ - 7}} = \\left( {\\frac{n}{t}} \\right)\\frac{{6.626 \\times {{10}^{ - 34}} \\times 3 \\times {{10}^8}}}{{5000 \\times {{10}^{ - 10}}}}$<br>$ \\Rightarrow \\frac{n}{t} = \\frac{{5000 \\times {{10}^{ - 10}} \\times {{10}^{ - 7}}}}{{6.626 \\times {{10}^{ - 34}} \\times 3 \\times {{10}^8}}} = 2.5 \\times {10^{11}}\\,{s^{ - 1}}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44258,'physics',37,974,2273,'A sinusoidal $AC$ current flows through a resistor of resistance $R$. If the peak current is ${I_{p,}}$ then the power dissipated is','[{"text": "$\\\\frac{4}{\\\\pi }I_p^2R$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{1}{\\\\pi }I_p^2R$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{1}{2}I_p^2R$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$I_p^2R\\\\cos \\\\theta $", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'${\\rm{Power}} = I_{rms}^2R = {\\left( {\\frac{{{I_p}}}{{\\sqrt 2 }}} \\right)^2}R = \\frac{{I_p^2R}}{2}$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44259,'physics',38,1065,2540,'For a plane electromagnetic wave propagating in $x$-direction, which one of the following combination gives the correct possible direction for electric field ($E$) and magnetic field ($B$) respectively?','[{"text": "$$-j+k,-j-k$$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$$j+k,-j-k$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$-j+k,-j+k$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$j+k, j+k$$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'$\\overrightarrow E  \\times \\overrightarrow B $ should be along x axis<br>(1) $$\\left|\\begin{array}{ccc}\\hat{i} & \\hat{j} & \\hat{k} \\\\ 0 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1 & -1\\end{array}\\right|=2 i$$<br>(2) $$\\left|\\begin{array}{ccc}\\hat{i} & \\hat{j} & \\hat{k} \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1 & -1\\end{array}\\right|=0$$<br>(3) $$\\left|\\begin{array}{ccc}\\hat{i} & \\hat{j} & \\hat{k} \\\\ 0 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 1\\end{array}\\right|=0$$<br>(4) $$\\left|\\begin{array}{lll}\\hat{i} & \\hat{j} & \\hat{k} \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{array}\\right|=0$$<br>Option (1) is correct.',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',2021,'NEET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44260,'physics',39,1103,2630,'A radio transmitter radiates $1\\,kW$ power at a wavelength $198.6\\,m$. How many photons does it emit per second?','[{"text": "$$10^{20}$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$10^{10}$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$10^{40}$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$10^{30}$$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Number of photons, $$n=\\dfrac{P}{E}=\\dfrac{P}{h v}=\\dfrac{P \\lambda}{h c}$$<br>$$=\\dfrac{1000 \\times 198.6}{6.6 \\times 10^{-34} \\times 3 \\times 10^{8}} \\approx 10^{30}$$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44261,'physics',37,974,2767,'Current in the circuit is wattless, if','[{"text": "Resistance in the circuit is zero", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Inductance in the circuit is zero", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Resistance and inductance both are zero", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Current is alternating", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Because power $ = {i^2}R,\\,if{\\rm{ }}R = 0,then{\\rm{ }}P = 0.$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44262,'physics',38,1065,2542,'Light with an average flux of $20\\,\\,W/c{m^2}$ falls on a non-reflectiong surface at normal incidence having surface area $20\\,c{m^2}$. The energy received by the surface during time span of 1 minute is:','[{"text": "$12 \\\\times {10^3}J$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$24 \\\\times {10^3}J$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$48 \\\\times {10^3}J$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$10 \\\\times {10^3}J$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'$$I=\\dfrac{E}{A t}$$<br>$E = IAt$<br>$ = \\frac{{20}}{{{{10}^{ - 4}}}} \\times 20 \\times {10^{ - 4}} \\times 60$<br>$ = 24 \\times {10^3}\\,J$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2020,'NEET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44264,'physics',37,974,2766,'In a series \\[LCR\\] circuit, the voltage leads current. Now the driving frequency is decreased slightly. Match columns I and II.<div class="matching-question"><div class="column-left">A. Current amplitude<br>B. Phase constant<br>C. Power developed in resistor<br>D. Impedance</div><div class="column-right">P. Increases<br>Q. Decreases<br>R. Remains same<br>S. May increase or decrease</div></div>','[{"text": "A - P,Q,\\tB - Q,\\tC - R,S\\tD - P", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "A - P,\\tB - Q,\\tC - P,\\tD - Q", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "A - P,      B - R, \\t  C - P,\\tD - Q", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "A - Q,     B - R,\\t  C - S,\\tD - P", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Given that voltage leads current. Hence ${X_L} > X{}_C$ <br>$\\tan \\phi  = \\frac{{{X_L} - {X_C}}}{R},{X_L} = \\omega L,{X_C} = \\frac{1}{{\\omega C}}$<br>If $\\omega $ decreases slightly then ${X_L}$ decreases and  ${X_C}$ increases.${{X_L} - {X_C}}$  decreases<br>$ \\Rightarrow \\,\\tan \\phi \\,\\,{\\mathop{\\rm and}\\nolimits} \\,\\,\\phi $ also decrease<br>$Z = \\sqrt {{{\\left( {{X_L} - {X_C}} \\right)}^2} + {R^2}} $ also decreases<br>As peak voltage ${V_0}$ remains constant so ${i_0}$ is given by ${i_0} = \\frac{{{V_0}}}{Z}$ (increases)<br>The average power ($P$) is <br>$ < P >  = \\frac{{V_{rms}^2}}{Z}\\cos \\phi  = \\frac{{V_{rms}^2R}}{{{Z^2}}} = \\frac{{V_0^2R}}{{2{Z^2}}}$<br>As $Z$ decreases so  ($P$)  increases. So, correct option is (2).',NULL,'Easy','Match the following',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44265,'physics',38,1066,2543,'Which colour of the light has the longest wavelength?','[{"text": "Red", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Blue", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Green", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Violet", "isCorrect": false}]',0,NULL,NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2019,'NEET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44266,'physics',39,1103,2630,'What will be the number of photons emitted per second by a $10\\,W$ sodium vapour lamp assuming that $$90 \\%$$ of the consumed energy is converted into light? [Wavelength of sodium light is 590 $\\left. {nm,h = 6.63 \\times {{10}^{ - 34}}\\,J - s} \\right]$','[{"text": "$$0.267 \\\\times 10^{19}$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$0.267 \\\\times 10^{18}$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$0.267 \\\\times 10^{17}$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$0.267 \\\\times 10^{20}$$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Energy of photon,<br>$$E=\\dfrac{h c}{\\lambda}=\\dfrac{6.63 \\times 10^{-34} \\times 3 \\times 10^{8}}{590 \\times 10^{-9}}=\\dfrac{6.63 \\times 3}{59} \\times 10^{-18}$$<br>Light energy produced per second<br>$ = \\frac{{90}}{{100}} \\times 10 = 9\\,W$<br>$$\\therefore$$ Number of photons emitted per sec<br>$$=\\dfrac{9 \\times 59}{6.63 \\times 3 \\times 10^{-18}}=2.67 \\times 10^{19}$$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44267,'physics',37,973,2268,'In a circuit $L,C,R$ connected in series with an alternating voltage source of frequency $f$.  The current leads the voltage by $45^\\circ $. The value of $C$ is','[{"text": "$\\\\frac{1}{{2\\\\pi f(2\\\\pi fL + R)}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{1}{{\\\\pi f(2\\\\pi fL - R)}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{1}{{2\\\\pi f(2\\\\pi fL - R)}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{1}{{\\\\pi f(2\\\\pi fL + R)}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'$\\tan 45 = \\frac{{\\frac{1}{{2\\pi fC}} - 2\\pi fL}}{R}$<br>or  $C = \\frac{1}{{2\\pi f(2\\pi fL + R)}}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44268,'physics',38,1065,2542,'The ratio of contributions made by the electric field and magnetic field components to the intensity of an electromagnetic wave is :$(e = $ speed of electromagnetic waves)','[{"text": "$$1: 1$$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$1:c$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$1: c^{2}$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$c:1$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'In $EM$ waves, electric field and magnetic field have same energy density and same intensities.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2020,'NEET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44269,'physics',37,975,2278,'A 100$V$, $AC$ source of frequency 500 $Hz$ is connected to an $L$-$C$-$R$ circuit with $L - 8.1\\,mH,C = 12.5\\mu F,R = 10\\Omega $ all connected in series. What is the quality factor of circuit?','[{"text": "$2.02$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2.5434$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$20.54$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$200.54$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'The Q-factor of series resonant circuit is given as,<br>$Q = \\frac{{{\\omega _0}L}}{R}$<br>Here, $L = 8.1\\,mH,C = 12.5\\mu F,R = 10\\,\\Omega ,f = 500\\,Hz$<br>$\\therefore Q = \\frac{{2\\pi fL}}{R} = \\frac{{2 \\times \\pi  \\times 500 \\times 8.1 \\times {{10}^{ - 3}}}}{{10}} = 2.5434$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44270,'physics',39,1102,2628,'In photoelectric effect, which of the following property of incident light will not affect the stopping potential','[{"text": "Frequency", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Wavelength", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Energy", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Intensity", "isCorrect": true}]',3,NULL,NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44271,'physics',38,1065,2540,'In an electromagnetic wave in free space the root mean square value of the electric field is ${E_{rms}} = 6\\,V/m$. The peak value of the magnetic field is :-','[{"text": "$2.83 \\\\times {10^{ - 8}}\\\\,T$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$0.70 \\\\times {10^{ - 8}}\\\\,T$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$4.23 \\\\times {10^{ - 8}}\\\\,T$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1.41 \\\\times {10^{ - 8}}\\\\,T$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'$$\\mathrm{E}_{0}=\\mathrm{CB}_{0}$$<br>${E_{{\\rm{rms}}}} = \\frac{{{E_0}}}{{\\sqrt 2 }}$<br>$ \\Rightarrow {E_{{\\rm{rms}}}} = \\sqrt 2  = C{B_0}$<br>$ \\Rightarrow {B_0} = \\frac{{{E_{{\\rm{rms}}}}\\sqrt 2 }}{C} = \\frac{{6 \\times \\sqrt 2 }}{{3 \\times {{10}^8}}} = 2.83 \\times {10^{ - 8}}T$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2017,'NEET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44272,'physics',37,973,2268,'An inductor $L$, a capacitor of $20\\,\\mu F$ and a resistor of $10\\Omega $ are connected in series with an $AC$ source of frequency 50 $Hz$. If the current is in phase with the voltage, then the inductance of the inductor is:','[{"text": "$2.00\\\\,H$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$0.51\\\\,H$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$1.5\\\\,H$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$0.99\\\\,H$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'In an $LCR$ circuit, the current and the voltage are in phase $(\\phi  = 0)$ ,when <br>$\\tan \\phi  = \\frac{{\\omega L - \\frac{1}{{\\omega C}}}}{R} = 0 \\Rightarrow \\omega L = \\frac{1}{{\\omega C}}$<br>$ \\Rightarrow L = \\frac{1}{{{\\omega ^2}C}}$<br>Here,$\\omega  = 2\\pi f = 2 \\times 3.14 \\times 50{s^{ - 1}} = 314{s^{ - 1}}$ and $C = 20\\mu F = 20 \\times {10^{ - 6}}F$<br>$\\therefore L = \\frac{1}{{{{(314{s^{ - 1}})}^2} \\times (20 \\times {{10}^{ - 6}}F)}} = 0.51H$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44273,'physics',39,1102,2628,'If the frequency of light in a photoelectric experiment is doubled, the stopping potential will','[{"text": "Be doubled", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Be halved", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Become more than double", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Become less than double", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'$$e V_{s}=h v-\\phi$$<br>$$\\begin{aligned}e V_{s}^{1} & =2 h v-\\phi \\\\& =2\\left(e V_{s}+\\phi\\right)-\\phi \\\\e V_{s}^{1} & =e\\left(2 V_{s}\\right)+\\phi \\\\V_{s}^{1} & >2 V_{s}\\end{aligned}$$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44274,'physics',38,1065,2539,'An em wave is propagating in a medium with a velocity $$\\vec{v}=v \\hat{i}$$ The instantaneous oscillating electric field of this em wave is along $$+y$$ axis. Then the direction of oscillating magnetic field of the em wave will be along','[{"text": "$$-y$$ direction", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$-x$$ direction", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$+z$$ direction", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$$-z$$ direction", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'The wave propagates with $v$ along $ + x$ direction along the direction of pointing vector $\\overrightarrow S $<br>$\\overrightarrow S  = \\frac{{\\vec E \\times \\vec B}}{{{\\mu _0}}}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\left[ {{\\rm{given}}\\,\\overrightarrow E \\,{\\mathop{\\rm is}\\nolimits} \\,{\\rm{along}}\\, + y} \\right]$<br>$B$ will be along $ + z$.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2018,'NEET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44275,'physics',37,974,2766,'As given in the figure, a series circuit connected across a 100$V$, 60 $Hz$ line consists of a capacitor of capacitive reactance $30\\Omega $, a resistor of $44\\Omega $ and a coil of inductive reactance $90\\Omega $ and resistance $36\\Omega $. The power dissipated in the coil is:','[{"text": "$80\\\\,W$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$176\\\\,W$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$144\\\\,W$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$0\\\\,W$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'<img src=\'/img/data/physics/37_44275_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' /> <br> $Z = \\sqrt {{{\\left( {{R_1} + {R_2}} \\right)}^2} + {{\\left( {{X_L} - {X_C}} \\right)}^2}} $<br>$Z = \\sqrt {{{\\left( {80} \\right)}^2} + {{\\left( {60} \\right)}^2}}  = 100$ <br>Current,$I = \\frac{V}{Z} = \\frac{{100}}{{100}} = 1A$ <br> Power dissipated in the coil, <br> ${P_{av}} = {I^2}{R_2} = {\\left( 1 \\right)^2} \\times 80 = 80W$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/37_44275_question_1.png'),
(44276,'physics',39,1106,2633,'Match Column -I (Fundamental Experiment) with Column-II (Its conclusion) and select the correct option from the choice given below the list.<div class="matching-question"><div class="column-left">A. Frank-hertz experiment<br>B. Photoeletric experiment<br>C. Davisson-Germer experiment</div><div class="column-right">P. Particle nature of light<br>Q. Discrete energy levels of atom<br>R. Wave nature of electron</div></div>','[{"text": "${\\\\rm{A - Q,}}\\\\,{\\\\rm{ B - P,}}\\\\,{\\\\rm{ C - R}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{A - S,}}\\\\,{\\\\rm{B - Q,}}\\\\,{\\\\rm{ C - R}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{A - Q,}}\\\\,{\\\\rm{B - S,}}\\\\,{\\\\rm{C - R}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{A - R,}}\\\\,{\\\\rm{B - P,}}\\\\,{\\\\rm{C - Q}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'A-Q, B-P, C-R<br>Frank-Hertz. experiment $$\\rightarrow$$ Discrete energy levels of atoms.<br>Photoelectric experiment $$\\rightarrow$$ Particle nature of light<br>Davisson-Germer experiment $$\\rightarrow$$ Wave nature of electron.',NULL,'Easy','Match the following',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44277,'physics',38,1065,2539,'Out of the following options which one can be used to produce a propagating electromagnetic wave?','[{"text": "A charge moving at constant velocity", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "A stationary charge", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "A chargeless particle", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "An accelerating charge", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'To generate electromagnetic waves we need accelerating charged particle.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2016,'NEET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44278,'physics',37,973,2268,'In an $LCR$ series ac circuit the voltage across $L$,$C$ and $R$ is 10 $V$ each. If the inductor is short circuited, the voltage across the capacitor would become','[{"text": "$\\\\frac{{10}}{{\\\\sqrt 2 }}V$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{{20}}{{\\\\sqrt 2 }}V$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$20\\\\sqrt 2 V$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$10\\\\,V$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'At resonance ${V_L} = {V_C} = {V_R} = 10volt$ given ${V_{source}} = 10\\,volt$ and $R = {X_C} = {X_L}$<br>So when $L$ is shorted<br>$ \\Rightarrow {10^2} = V_C^2 + V_R^2$<br>${V_C} = {V_R} = \\frac{{10}}{{\\sqrt 2 }}\\,volt$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44279,'physics',39,1102,2627,'Intensity of light incident on a photo sensitive surface is doubled. Then','[{"text": "The number of emitted electrons is tripled", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "The number of emitted electrons is doubled", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "The $K.E$ of emitted electrons is doubled", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "The momentum of emitted electrons is doubled", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Intensity $$\\alpha$$ no. of photoelectrons',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44280,'physics',38,1066,2543,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> $$\\gamma$$ - rays are more energetic than $X$-rays.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> $$\\gamma$$ - rays are of nuclear origin but $X$ rays are produced due to sudden deceleration of high energy electron while falling on a metal of high atomic number.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Both $$A$$ and $$R$$ are true, but $$R$$ is not correct explanation of A. In fact, the energy of $$\\gamma$$ - rays is more than $X$-rays because the frequency of $$\\gamma$$ - rays is higher than that of $X$-rays and $$E=$$ $$h v$$. So option (2) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44281,'physics',37,973,2268,'The incorrect statement for $L$-$R$-$C$ series circuit is','[{"text": "The potential difference across the resistance and  the appleid e.m.f. are in same phase at resonance.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "The phase difference across inductor and resistor is $90^\\\\circ .$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "The phase difference between the potential difference across capacitor and potential difference across inductance is $90^\\\\circ .$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "The phase difference between potential \\tdifference across capacitor and potential difference across resistance is $90^\\\\circ .$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,NULL,NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44282,'physics',39,1102,2627,'A point source of light is used in a photoelectric effect. If the source is moved farther from the emitting metal, the stopping potential','[{"text": "Will increase", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Will decrease", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Will remain constant", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Will either increase or decrease", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Stopping potential does not depend on intensity.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44324,'physics',37,973,2262,'A resistance of 20 $ohm$ is connected to a source of an alternating potential $V = 220\\sin (100\\pi t)$. The time taken by the current to change from zero value to $r$.$m$.$s$. value is','[{"text": "$2.5 \\\\times {10^{ - 3}}\\\\sec $", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$25 \\\\times {10^{ - 3}}\\\\sec $", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$0.25\\\\sec $", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$0.2\\\\sec $", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Let the current through the resistor is  <br>$i = {i_o}\\sin 100\\pi t \\Rightarrow \\frac{1}{{\\sqrt 2 }} \\times {i_o}$<br>$ = {i_o}\\sin 100\\pi t$<br>$ \\Rightarrow \\sin \\frac{\\pi }{4} = \\sin 100\\pi t$<br>$ \\Rightarrow t = \\frac{1}{{400}}\\sec  = 2.5 \\times {10^{ - 3}}\\sec .$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44283,'physics',38,1065,2540,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> In an electromagnetic wave the direction of the magnetic field induction $$B$$ is parallel to the electric field $$E$$.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Electric field vector $$E$$ and magnetic field vector $$B$$, have the same frequency.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but reason is correct.", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'In an electromagnetic wave, the direction of $$E$$ and $$B$$ are perpendicular to each other and they have same frequency.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44284,'physics',37,973,2267,'Time period of  $LC$ circuit is','[{"text": "$2\\\\pi \\\\sqrt {LC} $", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{1}{{2\\\\pi {L^2}{C^2}}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{1}{{2\\\\pi LC}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{{LC}}{{2\\\\pi }}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'$\\omega  = \\frac{1}{{\\sqrt {LC} }} = \\frac{{2\\pi }}{T}$<br>$T = 2\\pi \\sqrt {LC} $',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44285,'physics',39,1102,2626,'The best suitable metal for photoelectric effect is','[{"text": "Iron", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Steel", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Aluminium", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Cesium", "isCorrect": true}]',3,NULL,NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44286,'physics',38,1065,2540,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> The changing electric field produces a magnetic field.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> A changing magnetic field produces an electric field.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'The changing electric field produces a magnetic field. It is accounted by AmpereMaxwell\'s law. The charging magnetic field also produces electric field. So option (2) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44287,'physics',37,973,2268,'An inductance $L$ and capacitance $C$ and resistance $R$ are connected in series across an $AC$ source of angular frequency $\\omega $.If ${\\omega ^2} > \\frac{1}{{LC}}$ then','[{"text": "Emf leads the current", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both the emf and the current are in phase", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Current leads the emf", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Emf lags behind the current", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'If $\\omega  > {\\omega _o}\\left[ {{\\omega _o} = \\frac{1}{{\\sqrt {LC} }}} \\right], \\Rightarrow {X_L} > {X_C}$<br>So voltage leads current.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44288,'physics',39,1102,2626,'The work function of a metal','[{"text": "Is different for different metals", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Is the same for all the metals", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Depends on the frequency of the light", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Depends on the intensity of the incident light", "isCorrect": false}]',0,NULL,NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44289,'physics',38,1065,2539,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> The light can travel in vacuum but sound cannot do so.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Light is an electromagnetic wave and sound is a mechanical wave.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Option (1) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44290,'physics',40,240,479,'Hydrogen atom in ground state is excited by a monochromatic radiation of $\\lambda  = 975\\mathop A\\limits^ \\circ  $ Number of spectral lines in the resulting spectrum emitted will be','[{"text": "$10$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$6$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$2$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$3$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Energy provided to the ground state electron<br>$E = \\frac{{hc}}{\\lambda } = \\frac{{6.6 \\times {{10}^{ - 34}} \\times 3 \\times {{10}^8}}}{{975 \\times {{10}^{ - 10}}}} = \\frac{{6.6 \\times 3}}{{975}} \\times {10^{ - 16}}$<br>$ = 0.020 \\times {10^{ - 16}} = 2 \\times {10^{ - 18}}J$<br>${\\rm{ = }}\\frac{{20 \\times {{10}^{ - 19}}}}{{1.6 \\times {{10}^{ - 19}}}}eV = {\\rm{12}}{\\rm{.75}}eV$<br>It means the electron jumps to $n = 4$ from $n = 1.$<br>Number of spectral lines emitted<br>$ = \\frac{{n\\left( {n - 1} \\right)}}{2} = \\frac{{4\\left( {4 - 1} \\right)}}{2} = 6$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44291,'physics',37,973,2266,'A resistor and a capacitor are connected in series with an $a$.$c$. source. If the potential drop across the capacitor is 8 $V$ and that across resistor is 6 $V$, the applied voltage is','[{"text": "$17\\\\,V$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$10\\\\,V$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$7\\\\,V$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$9\\\\,V$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Let the applied voltage be $V$ volt.<br>Here,${V_R} = 6V,{V_C} = 8V$<br>$V = \\sqrt {V_R^2 + V_C^2}  = \\sqrt {{{(6)}^2} + {{(8)}^2}}  = 10V$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44292,'physics',39,1102,2626,'Emission of electrons in photoelectric effect is possible, if','[{"text": "Metal surface is highly polished", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "The incident light is of sufficiently high intensity", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "The light is incident at right angles to the surface", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "The incident light is of sufficiently low wavelength", "isCorrect": true}]',3,NULL,NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44293,'physics',38,1067,2544,'<div class="matching-question"><div class="column-left">One should not use metal containers in a microwave oven.<br></div><div class="column-right">Only because metal may melt from heating.</div></div>','[{"text": "Statement A is correct but Statement B is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Statement A is incorrect but Statement B is correct.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both statements are correct.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Statements are incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'When microwaves hit metal objects, they get reflected. This continuous reflection causes a fire. So optoin (3) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ type-2',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44342,'physics',37,972,2261,'An $AC$ source is $240 - V - 60\\,Hz$. The value of voltage after $\\frac{1}{{720}}s$ starting from zero will be:','[{"text": "$20.2\\\\,V$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$42.4\\\\,V$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$169.7\\\\,V$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$106.8\\\\,V$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'$V = {V_0}\\sin \\omega t \\Rightarrow V = {V_{rms}}\\sqrt 2 \\sin \\omega t$<br>After,$t = \\frac{1}{{720}}s,$<br>$V = 240\\sqrt 2 \\sin 2\\pi v\\,t = 240\\sqrt 2 \\sin 2\\pi  \\times 60 \\times \\frac{1}{{720}}$<br>$ = 120\\sqrt 2  = 169.7V$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44294,'physics',37,973,2266,'The impedance of a $R - C$ circuit is ${Z_1}$ for a frequency $f$ and ${Z_2}$ for frequency $2f$. Then ${Z_1}/{Z_2}$ is','[{"text": "$ > 2$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$ < 2$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$1/2$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'${Z_1} = \\sqrt {{R^2} + {{\\left( {\\frac{1}{{2\\pi fC}}} \\right)}^2}} ;\\,{Z_2} = \\sqrt {{R^2} + {{\\left( {\\frac{1}{{4\\pi fC}}} \\right)}^2}} $<br>$\\frac{{{Z_1}}}{{{Z_2}}} = \\frac{{\\sqrt {1 + \\frac{1}{{4{\\pi ^2}{f^2}{C^2}{R^2}}}} }}{{\\sqrt {1 + \\frac{1}{{16{\\pi ^2}{f^2}{C^2}{R^2}}}} }}$<br>or $\\frac{{{Z_1}}}{{{Z_2}}} = \\frac{{\\sqrt {1 + \\frac{1}{{4k}}} }}{{\\sqrt {1 + \\frac{1}{{16k}}} }} = \\sqrt {\\frac{{4k + 1}}{{16k + 1}}}  \\cdot 2$ and <br>$\\sqrt {\\frac{{4k + 1}}{{16k + 1}}}  < 1.$<br>$ \\Rightarrow \\frac{{{z_1}}}{{{z_2}}} < 2$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44295,'physics',40,240,479,'An electron of stationary hydrogen atom jumps from 4$th$ energy level to ground level. The velocity that the photon acquired as a result of electron transition will be ( where $H = $ Planck’s constant, $R = $ Rydberg’s constant, $m = $ mass of photons)','[{"text": "$\\\\frac{{9Rh}}{{16m}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{{11Rh}}{{16m}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{{13Rh}}{{16m}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{{15Rh}}{{16m}}$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Given,${n_1} = 1,{n_2} = 4$<br>We know that,<br>$\\frac{1}{\\lambda } = R\\left( {\\frac{1}{{n_1^2}} - \\frac{1}{{n_2^2}}} \\right)$<br>$\\frac{1}{\\lambda } = R\\left( {\\frac{1}{{{{\\left( 1 \\right)}^2}}} - \\frac{1}{{{{\\left( 4 \\right)}^2}}}} \\right)$<br>$\\frac{1}{\\lambda } = R\\left( {1 - \\frac{1}{{16}}} \\right)$<br>$\\frac{1}{\\lambda } = R\\left( {\\frac{{16 - 1}}{{16}}} \\right)$<br>$\\frac{1}{\\lambda } = \\frac{{15R}}{{16}}$<br>$\\lambda  = \\frac{{16}}{{15R}}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,(1)$<br>But $p = \\frac{h}{\\lambda }\\left[ {mv = \\frac{h}{\\lambda }} \\right]$<br>$v = \\frac{h}{{m\\lambda }}$ <br>$v = \\frac{h}{m} \\times \\frac{{15R}}{{16}}\\,\\,{\\rm{[from eq}}{\\rm{.(1)]}}$<br>$v = \\frac{{15hR}}{{16m}}$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',2018,'MHTCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44296,'physics',39,1102,2626,'The photo electrons emitted from the surface of sodium metal are','[{"text": "Of speeds from 0 to a certain maximum", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Of same de Broglie wavelength", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Of same kinetic energy", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Of same frequency", "isCorrect": false}]',0,NULL,NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44297,'physics',38,1065,2539,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> The difference between various types of electromagnetic waves lies in their wavelength or frequencies.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Electromagnetic waves travel with different speeds in different mediums.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'The basic difference between various types of electromagnetic waves lies in their wavelengths or frequencies, since all of them travel through vaccum with the same speed. Velocity of electromagnetic waves depend on medium. Option (2) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44298,'physics',37,973,2268,'The reading of voltmeter in the circuit shown will be:','[{"text": "$2\\\\,A$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2.4\\\\,A$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Zero", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$1.7A$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Given ${X_L} = {X_C} = 5\\Omega $,this is the condition of resonance, So ${V_L} = {V_C}$, so net voltage across $L$ and $C$ combination will be zero.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/37_44298_question_1.png'),
(44299,'physics',39,1102,2626,'The process of photoelectric emission depends on','[{"text": "Temperature of incident light", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Nature of surface", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Speed of emitted photo electrons", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Speed of the incident light", "isCorrect": false}]',1,NULL,NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44300,'physics',40,240,479,'The wavelength of the first line in Balmer series in hydrogen spectrum is $\'\\lambda \'$. What is the wavelength of the  second line in the same series?','[{"text": "$\\\\frac{{20}}{{27}}\\\\lambda $", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{3}{{16}}\\\\lambda $", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{5}{{16}}\\\\lambda $", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{3}{4}\\\\lambda $", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Wavelength in Balmer series of hydrogen spectrum is given by relation<br>$\\frac{1}{\\lambda } = R\\left[ {\\frac{1}{{{2^2}}} - \\frac{1}{{{n^2}}}} \\right]$ where $n = 3,4,5$<br>So, wavelength of first line<br>$\\frac{1}{{{\\lambda _1}}} = R\\left[ {\\frac{1}{4} - \\frac{1}{{{3^2}}}} \\right]$<br>$ = \\frac{{5R}}{{36}}$<br>$ \\Rightarrow R = \\frac{{36}}{{5\\lambda }}\\left( {\\because {\\lambda _1} = \\lambda ,given} \\right)\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,(1)$<br>Similarly wavelength of second line,<br>$\\frac{1}{{{\\lambda _2}}} = R\\left[ {\\frac{1}{4} - \\frac{1}{{{4^2}}}} \\right] = \\frac{{3R}}{{16}}$<br>$ \\Rightarrow {\\lambda _2} = \\frac{{16}}{{3R}}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,(2)$<br>From eq,(1) and eq,(2), we get<br>${\\lambda _2} = \\frac{{20}}{{27}}\\lambda $<br>Hence, the wavelength of the second line in the same series is $\\frac{{20}}{{27}}\\lambda $',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2019,'MHTCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44301,'physics',38,1065,2539,'<div class="matching-question"><div class="column-left">The velocity of electromagnetic waves depends on electric and magnetic properties of the medium.<br></div><div class="column-right">Velocity of electromagnetic waves in free space is constant.</div></div>','[{"text": "Statement A is correct but Statement B is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Statement A is incorrect but Statement B is correct.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both statements are correct.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Statements are incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Option (3) is correct',NULL,'Medium','MCQ type-2',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
