(44308,'physics',38,1065,2541,'<div class="matching-question"><div class="column-left">Electromagnetic waves exert radiation pressure.<br></div><div class="column-right">Electromagnetic waves carry energy.</div></div>','[{"text": "Statement A is correct but Statement B is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Statement A is incorrect but Statement B is correct.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both statements are correct.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Statements are incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Electromagnetic waves have linear momentum as well as energy. This concludes that they can exert radiation pressure by falling beam of electromagnetic radiation on an object. So option (3) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ type-2',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44309,'physics',37,973,2264,'The capacity of a pure capacitor is 1 farad. In $DC$ circuits, its effective resistance will be','[{"text": "$1/2\\\\,ohm$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1\\\\,ohm$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{Zero}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Infinite", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'${X_C} = \\frac{1}{{2\\pi v\\,C}} = \\frac{1}{0} = \\infty $',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44310,'physics',40,240,479,'The ratio of energies of photons produced due to transition of electron of hydrogen atom from its (i) second to first energy level and (ii) highest energy level  to second level is respectively','[{"text": "$2.5:1$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$3:1$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$2:1$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$4:1$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'The energy of photons is given by <br>$E = Rhc\\left( {\\frac{1}{{n_1^2}} - \\frac{1}{{n_2^2}}} \\right)\\,\\,$<br>Where, $R$ is Rydberg constant, $h$ is Planck’s constant and $c\\,$ is the speed of light<br>Energy of photon produced from second to first energy level<br>${E_1} = Rhc\\left( {\\frac{1}{{{1^2}}} - \\frac{1}{{{2^2}}}} \\right)\\,$<br>${E_1} = \\frac{3}{4}Rhc\\,$<br>Energy of photon produced from higher energy level (i.e.,$\\infty $ ) to second level<br>${E_2} = Rhc\\left( {\\frac{1}{{{2^2}}} - \\frac{1}{\\infty }} \\right) = \\frac{1}{4}Rhc\\,\\,$<br>$\\therefore \\frac{{{E_1}}}{{{E_2}}} = \\frac{{\\frac{3}{4}Rhc}}{{\\frac{1}{4}Rhc}} = \\frac{3}{1}or3:1$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2020,'MHTCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44311,'physics',39,1102,2626,'The function of photoelectric cell is','[{"text": "To convert electrical energy into light energy", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "To convert light energy into electrical energy", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "To convert mechanical energy into electrical energy", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "To convert $DC$ into $AC$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,NULL,NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44312,'physics',38,1065,2539,'<div class="matching-question"><div class="column-left">Electromagnetic waves are transverse in nature.<br></div><div class="column-right">The electric and magnetic fields in electromagnetic waves are perpendicular to each other and the direction of propagation.</div></div>','[{"text": "Statement A is correct but Statement B is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Statement A is incorrect but Statement B is correct.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both statements are correct.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Statements are incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Transverse waves are those waves in which the particles for the medium oscillate perpendicular to the direction of wave propagation. So option (3) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ type-2',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44313,'physics',37,973,2264,'If a capacitor is connected to two different $A$.$C$. generators, then the value of capacitive reactance is','[{"text": "Directly proportional to frequency", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Inversely proportional to frequency", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Independent of frequency", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Inversely proportional to the square of frequency", "isCorrect": false}]',1,NULL,NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44315,'physics',40,240,479,'What is the wavelength of light for the least energetic photon emitted in the Lyman series of the hydrogen spectrum? (take $hc = 1240\\,eVnm$)','[{"text": "$102\\\\,nm$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$150\\\\,nm$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$82\\\\,nm$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$122\\\\,nm$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'For the Lyman series, the  transition that produces the least energetic photon is the transition from the $n = 2$ level to the $n = 1$ level<br>$\\therefore $ The wavelength for the least energetic photon<br>$\\lambda  = \\frac{{hc}}{{{E_2} - {E_1}}}$<br>Here, $hc = 1240\\,eV\\,nm$<br>Energy for $n = 1$ level,<br>${E_1} =  - \\frac{{13.6}}{{{1^2}}}eV =  - 13.6\\,\\,eV$ $\\left( {as\\,\\,{E_n} =  - \\frac{{13.6}}{{{n^2}}}eV} \\right)$<br>Similarly, energy for $n = 2$ level,<br>${E_2} =  - \\frac{{13.6}}{{{2^2}}}eV =  - 3.4eV$<br>$\\therefore \\lambda  = \\frac{{1240}}{{ - 3.4 - \\left( { - 13.6} \\right)}}nm = \\frac{{1240}}{{10.2}}\\,nm \\approx 122\\,nm$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2015,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44316,'physics',38,1066,2543,'Which of the following statements are correct?<br>I. The wavelength of microwave is greater than that of UV-rays.<br>II. The wavelength of infrared rays is lesser than that of UV-rays<br>III. The wavelength of microwave is lesser than that of infrared rays.<br>IV. Gamma rays have shortest wavelengths in the electromagnetic spectrum.','[{"text": "I and II", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "II and III", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "III and IV", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "I and IV", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'${\\lambda _\\gamma } < {\\lambda _{UV}} < {\\lambda _{IR}} < {\\lambda _{MW}}.$<br>So option (4) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ type-2',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44317,'physics',37,973,2264,'At low frequency a condenser offers','[{"text": "High impedance", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Low impedance", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Zero impedance", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Impedance of condenser is independent of frequency", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'${X_C}\\,\\alpha \\frac{1}{\\omega }$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44318,'physics',39,1102,2626,'When ultraviolet radiation is incident on a surface, no photoelectrons are emitted. If a second beam causes emission of photoelectrons, it may consist of :','[{"text": "Radio waves", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Infrared rays", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Visible light rays", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$X$-rays", "isCorrect": true}]',3,NULL,NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44319,'physics',38,1067,2544,'Which of the following statement(s) is/are correct?<br>I. Radio waves are produced by the accelerated motion of charges in conducting wires.<br>II. Radio waves are used in radio and television communication systems.<br>III. Cellular phones use radio waves to transmit voice communication in the ultra high frequency.','[{"text": "I and III", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "II and III", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "I and II", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "All of these", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Radio waves are produced by the accelerated motion of charges in conducting wires. They are used in radio and television communication systems. Cellular phones use radio waves to transmit voice communication in the Ultra High Frequency ($UHF$) band.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ type-2',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44320,'physics',37,973,2263,'The value of the current through an inductance of 1$H$ and of negligible resistance, when connected through an $AC$ source of  200 $V$ and 50 $Hz$, is','[{"text": "$1.637\\\\,A$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$0.637\\\\,A$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$2.637\\\\,A$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$3.637\\\\,A$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'$i = \\frac{V}{{{X_L}}} = \\frac{{200}}{{\\omega L}} = \\frac{{200}}{{2\\pi  \\times 50 \\times 1}} = 0.637A$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44321,'physics',40,240,479,'The figure shows the energy level of certain atom. When the electron deexcites from $3E$ to $E$, an electromagnetic wave of wavelength $\\lambda $ is emitted.  What is the wavelength of the electromagnetic wave emitted when the electron deexcites from $5E/3$ to $E$?','[{"text": "$3\\\\lambda $", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$2\\\\lambda $", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$5\\\\lambda $", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$3\\\\lambda /5$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'From figure,<br>$\\frac{{hc}}{\\lambda } = 3E - E = 2E\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,(1)$<br>$\\frac{{{\\rm{hc}}}}{{{\\lambda ^1}}} = \\frac{{5E}}{3} - E = \\frac{2}{3}E\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,(2)$<br>Divide (1) by (2), we get<br>$\\frac{{\\lambda \'}}{\\lambda } = 3\\,$ or $\\lambda \' = 3\\lambda \\,$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2013,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/40_44321_question_1.png'),
(44322,'physics',39,1102,3092,'Match the quantities of Column I with their values in Column II and choose the correct option from the codes given below.<div class="matching-question"><div class="column-left">A. Maximum number of photons absorbed by an electron in a photo electric effect<br>B. Number of photons each of energy $E$ emitted by a source of power $P$ per second<br>C. Energy of a photon<br>D. Planck\'s constant in terms of work function and threshold frequency</div><div class="column-right">P. $\\frac{{{\\phi _0}}}{{{v_0}}}$<br>Q. $\\frac{P}{E}$<br>R. $hv$<br>S. 1</div></div>','[{"text": "${\\\\rm{A - Q,}}\\\\,{\\\\rm{B - S,}}\\\\,{\\\\rm{ C - P,}}\\\\,{\\\\rm{D - Q}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{A - S,}}\\\\,{\\\\rm{B - Q,}}\\\\,{\\\\rm{C - R,}}\\\\,{\\\\rm{D - P}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{A - Q,}}\\\\,{\\\\rm{B - S,}}\\\\,{\\\\rm{ C - R,}}\\\\,{\\\\rm{D - P}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{A - R,}}\\\\,{\\\\rm{B - P,}}\\\\,{\\\\rm{C - Q,}}\\\\,{\\\\rm{D - S}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'<br>A. An electron absorbs a single photon of light and if the energy carried by photon is more than work function of the metal, the electron is emitted.<br>B. From a source,<br>Total emitted power $$=P($$ total energy per second)<br>Energy of each photon $$=E$$<br>So, number of photons emitted by the source $$=$$ $$P / E$$.<br>C. Energy of a photons $$=E=h v$$<br>D. For the cut-off or threshold frequency, the energy $$h v_{0}$$ of the incident radiation must be equal to work function $$\\phi_{0}$$, so that $$h v_{0}=\\phi_{0}$$<br>$$\\Rightarrow h=\\dfrac{\\phi_{0}}{v_{0}}$$<br>So option (2) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Match the following',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44323,'physics',38,1067,2544,'I. Electromagnetic waves are capable to carry energy from one place to another.<br>II. The radio and TV signals from broadcasting stations carry energy.<br>III. Light carries energy from the sun to the earth, thus making life possible on the earth.<br>Which of the following statement(s) is/are correct?<br>Choose the correct option.','[{"text": "I and II", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "II and III", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "I and III", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "All of these", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'The great technological importance of electromagnetic waves stems from their capability to carry energy from one place to another. The radio and TV signals from broadcasting stations carry energy. Light carries energy from the sun to the earth, thus making life possible on the earth.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ type-2',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44325,'physics',40,240,479,'When an electron jumps from the orbit $n = 2$ to $n = 4$, then wavelength of the radiations absorbed will be ( $R$ is Rydberg’s constant)','[{"text": "$16/3R$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$16/5R$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$5R/16$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$3R/16$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'According to Rydberg formula<br>$\\frac{1}{\\lambda } = R\\left[ {\\frac{1}{{n_i^2}} - \\frac{1}{{n_f^2}}} \\right]$<br>Therefore, the wavelength corresponds to  transition from ${n_i} = 2$ to ${n_f} = 4$ is<br>$\\frac{1}{\\lambda } = R\\left[ {\\frac{1}{{{2^2}}} - \\frac{1}{{{4^2}}}} \\right]$<br>or ,$\\,\\frac{1}{\\lambda } = \\frac{{3R}}{{16}} \\Rightarrow \\lambda  = \\frac{{16}}{{3R}}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2011,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44326,'physics',39,1102,2629,'The threshold frequency for a certain photosensitive metal is ${\\nu _0}$. When it is illuminated by light of frequency $2{\\nu _0}$, the maximum velocity of photoelectrons is ${v_0}$. What will be the maximum velocity of the electrons when the same metal is illuminated by light of frequency $5{\\nu _0}$','[{"text": "$$\\\\sqrt{2} V_{0}$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$2 V_{0}$$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$$2 \\\\sqrt{2} V_{0}$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$4 V_{0}$$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Maximum velocity of photo electron is<br>${V_{{\\rm{max}}}} = \\sqrt {\\frac{{2h\\left( {\\upsilon  - {\\upsilon _0}} \\right)}}{m}} $<br>${V_o} = \\sqrt {\\frac{{2h\\left( {2{\\upsilon _0} - {\\upsilon _0}} \\right)}}{m}} \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\left( {given,\\upsilon  = 2{\\upsilon _0}} \\right)$<br>${V_0} = \\sqrt {\\frac{{2h{\\upsilon _0}}}{m}} \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,(1)$<br>For the  incident frequency $5{\\upsilon _0}$<br>$h\\left( {5{\\upsilon _0}} \\right) - \\phi  = \\frac{1}{2}m{V^2}$<br>$h\\left( {5{\\upsilon _0}} \\right) - h{\\upsilon _0} = \\frac{1}{2}m{V^2}$<br>$V = 2\\sqrt {\\frac{{2h\\beta {\\upsilon _0}}}{m}}  = 2{\\upsilon _0}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44327,'physics',38,1065,2539,'I. No material medium is involved in the vibrations of the electric and magnetic fields.<br>II. Sound waves in air are longitudinal waves of compression and refraction.<br>III. Transverse waves on the surface of water consist of water moving up and down as the wave spreads horizontally and radially onwards.<br>Which of the following statements are correct?','[{"text": "I and II", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "I and III", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "II and III", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "I, II and III", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Electromagnetic waves are self-sustaining oscillation of electric and magnetic fields in free space, or vacuum. A medium is not involved in the vibration of the electric and magnetic fields of electromagnetic waves. Sound waves in air are longitudinal waves of compression and raredfraction. Transverse waves on the surface of water consist of water moving up and down as the wave spreads horizontally and radially onwards.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ type-2',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44328,'physics',37,973,2262,'$220\\,V,50\\,Hz\\,AC$ is applied to a resistor. The instantaneous value of voltage is:','[{"text": "$220\\\\sqrt 2 \\\\sin 100\\\\pi t$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$220\\\\sin 100\\\\pi t$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$220\\\\sqrt 2 \\\\sin 50\\\\pi t$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$220\\\\sin 50\\\\pi t$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'We  know that, instantaneous voltage,<br>$E = {E_0}\\sin \\omega t$<br>But $\\omega  = 2\\pi v$<br>$\\therefore E = {E_0}\\sin 2\\pi vt\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,(1)$<br>Given ${E_0} = 220V\\,and\\,v = 50Hz$<br>On putting these values in Eq. (1), we get <br>$E = 220\\sin 2 \\times \\pi  \\times 50t = 220\\sin 100\\pi t$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44329,'physics',39,1102,2629,'In a photoelectric cell, the wavelength of incident light is changed from $4000\\mathop A\\limits^o $ to $3600\\mathop A\\limits^o $. The change in stopping potential will be:','[{"text": "$0.14\\\\,V$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$0.24\\\\,V$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$0.35\\\\,V$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$0.44\\\\,V$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'$e{V_S} = \\frac{{hc}}{\\lambda } - \\phi $<br>$e\\left( {{V_{{S_1}}} - {V_{{S_2}}}} \\right) = hc\\left[ {\\frac{1}{{{\\lambda _1}}} - \\frac{1}{{{\\lambda _2}}}} \\right]$<br>${V_{{S_2}}} - {V_{{S_1}}} = \\frac{{hc}}{e}\\left[ {\\frac{1}{{{\\lambda _2}}} - \\frac{1}{{{\\lambda _1}}}} \\right]$<br>$ = \\frac{{6.62 \\times {{10}^{ - 34}} \\times 3 \\times {{10}^8}}}{{1.6 \\times {{10}^{ - 19}}}} \\times \\left[ {\\frac{{{{10}^{ + 10}}}}{{3600}} - \\frac{{{{10}^{ + 10}}}}{{4000}}} \\right]$<br>$ = 0.24\\,V$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44330,'physics',40,240,479,'The frequencies for series limit of Balmer and Paschen series respectively are $\'{v_1}\'$ and $\'{v_3}\'$. If frequency of first line of Balmer series is  then the relation between $\'{v_1}\',\'{v_2}\'$ and  $\'{v_3}\'$ is','[{"text": "${v_1} - {v_2} = {v_3}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${v_1} + {v_3} = {v_2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${v_1} + {v_2} = {v_3}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${v_1} - {v_3} = 2{v_1}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'As we know, $v = n\\lambda $<br>$ \\Rightarrow \\frac{1}{\\lambda } = \\frac{n}{v} \\Rightarrow \\frac{1}{\\lambda } = R\\left( {\\frac{1}{{n_1^2}} - \\frac{1}{{n_2^2}}} \\right)$<br>$ \\Rightarrow v = Rc\\left( {\\frac{1}{{n_1^2}} - \\frac{1}{{n_2^2}}} \\right)$<br>Therefore, <br>${v_2} = Rc\\left( {\\frac{1}{{{2^2}}} - \\frac{1}{{{3^2}}}} \\right) = Rc\\left( {\\frac{1}{4} - \\frac{1}{9}} \\right)$             (1)<br>${v_1} = Rc\\left( {\\frac{1}{{{2^2}}}} \\right) = \\frac{{Rc}}{4}$<br>${v_3} = Rc\\left( {\\frac{1}{{{3^2}}}} \\right) = \\frac{{Rc}}{9}$<br>$ \\Rightarrow {v_1} - {v_3} = Rc\\left( {\\frac{1}{4} - \\frac{1}{9}} \\right)$                    (2)<br>from eq.(1) and eq.(2), we get<br>${v_1} - {v_3} = {v_2}$<br>$ \\Rightarrow {v_1} - {v_2} = {v_3}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2017,'MHTCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44331,'physics',38,1065,2541,'Light with an energy flux of $20\\,W/c{m^2}$ falls on a non-reflecting surface at normal incidence. If the surface has an area of $30\\,c{m^2}$, the total momentum delivered (for complete absorption) during $30\\,\\min $ is','[{"text": "$1.08 \\\\times {10^7}\\\\,kg - m/s$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$108 \\\\times {10^4}\\\\,kg - m/s$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$36 \\\\times {10^{ - 4}}\\\\,kg - m/s$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$36 \\\\times {10^{ - 5}}\\\\,kg - m/s$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'$F = {P_{{\\rm{rad }}}} \\times {\\rm{Area}}$<br>$ = \\frac{I}{c} \\times {\\rm{ Area }}$<br>$ = \\frac{{20}}{{3 \\times {{10}^8}}} \\times 30 = 2 \\times {10^{ - 6}}\\,N$<br>$$=$$ Change in momentum in $1\\,\\sec $<br>$$\\therefore$$ Momentum delivered to the surface in $30\\,\\min $<br>$ = 2 \\times {10^{ - 6}} \\times 30 \\times 60 = 36 \\times {10^{ - 4}}\\,kg\\,m\\,{s^{ - 1}}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,'NCERT Exempler','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44332,'physics',37,972,2634,'Find the intial phase angle for $V = 2\\sin \\omega t + 3\\cos \\omega t$ is','[{"text": "${\\\\tan ^{ - 1}}\\\\left( {\\\\frac{3}{2}} \\\\right)$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${\\\\tan ^{ - 1}}\\\\left( {\\\\frac{4}{5}} \\\\right)$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\tan ^{ - 1}}\\\\left( {\\\\frac{1}{2}} \\\\right)$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\tan ^{ - 1}}\\\\left( {\\\\frac{3}{4}} \\\\right)$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Comparing the given equation<br>$2\\sin \\omega t + 3\\cos \\omega t$ with ${V_0}\\sin \\left( {\\omega t + \\phi } \\right)$<br>${V_0}\\sin \\omega t\\cos \\phi  + {V_0}\\cos \\omega t\\sin \\phi $<br>$ \\Rightarrow \\,{V_0}\\cos \\phi  = 2$ and ${V_0}\\sin \\phi  = 3$<br>$ \\Rightarrow \\,\\,\\tan \\phi  = \\frac{3}{2}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44333,'physics',39,1103,2630,'Minimum light intensity that can be perceived by normal human eye is about ${10^{ - 10}}W{m^{ - 2}}$. What is the minimum number of photons of wavelength $660\\,nm$ that must enter the pupil in $1\\,s$ for one to see the object? Area of cross-section of the pupil is ${10^{ - 4}}\\,{m^2}$.','[{"text": "$$3.3 \\\\times 10^{3}$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$3.3 \\\\times 10^{2}$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$3.3 \\\\times 10^{5}$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$3.3 \\\\times 10^{4}$$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Intensity,<br>$I = {10^{ - 10}}W{m^{ - 2}} = {10^{ - 10}}J{s^{ - 1}}\\,{m^{ - 2}}$<br>Let the number of photons required be $$n$$.<br>Then, $$\\dfrac{n h v}{10^{-4}}=10^{-10}$$<br>Hence, $$n=10^{-14} \\dfrac{\\lambda}{h c}$$<br>$$=\\dfrac{10^{-14} \\times 660 \\times 10^{-9}}{6.6 \\times 10^{-34} \\times 3 \\times 10^{8}}=3.3 \\times 10^{4}$$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44334,'physics',38,1065,2539,'An electromagnetic wave travels in vacuum alongz-direction $$E=\\left(E_{1} \\hat{i}+E_{2} \\hat{j}\\right) \\cos (k z-\\omega t)$$. Choose the correct options from the following:','[{"text": "The associated magnetic field is given as<br>$$B=\\\\dfrac{1}{c}\\\\left(E_{1} \\\\hat{i}-E_{2} \\\\hat{j}\\\\right) \\\\cos (k z-\\\\omega t)$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "The associated magnetic field is given as<br>$B{\\\\rm{ }} = \\\\frac{1}{c}\\\\left( {{E_1}\\\\hat i - {E_2}\\\\hat j} \\\\right)\\\\cos (kz - \\\\omega t){\\\\rm{ }}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$B{\\\\rm{ }} = \\\\frac{1}{c}\\\\left( {{E_2}\\\\hat i - {E_1}\\\\hat j} \\\\right)\\\\cos (kz - \\\\omega t){\\\\rm{ }}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$B{\\\\rm{ }} = \\\\frac{1}{c}\\\\left( {{E_2}\\\\hat i + {E_1}\\\\hat j} \\\\right)\\\\cos (kz - \\\\omega t)$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Here, in electromagnetic wave, the electric field vector is given as,<br>$$\\bar{E}=\\left(E_{1} \\hat{i}+E_{2} \\hat{j}\\right) \\cos (k z-\\omega t)$$<br>In electromagnetic wave, the associated magnetic field vector, $$B_{o}=\\dfrac{E_{o}}{c}$$<br>where $$E_{0}=\\sqrt{E_{1}^{2}+E_{2}^{2}}, \\vec{B}=\\dfrac{E_{2} \\hat{i}+E_{1} \\hat{j}}{\\mathrm{c}} \\cos (k z-\\omega t)$$<br>As $$\\bar{E} \\& \\bar{B}$$ are perpendicular to each other.<br>$$\\Rightarrow \\overrightarrow{\\mathrm{E}_{2}} \\cdot \\overrightarrow{\\mathrm{B}}=0$$<br>So option (3) is correct.',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,'NCERT Exempler','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44335,'physics',37,972,2634,'Voltage and current in an $ac$ circuit are given by $V = 5\\sin \\left( {100\\pi t - \\frac{\\pi }{6}} \\right)$ and $I = 4\\sin \\left( {100\\pi t + \\frac{\\pi }{6}} \\right)$','[{"text": "Voltage leads the current by $60^\\\\circ $", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Current leads the voltage by $30^\\\\circ $", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Current leads the voltage by $60^\\\\circ $", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Voltage leads the current by $30^\\\\circ $", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Phase difference<br>$\\Delta \\phi  = {\\phi _2} - {\\phi _1} = \\frac{\\pi }{6} - \\left( {\\frac{{ - \\pi }}{6}} \\right) = \\frac{\\pi }{3}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44336,'physics',40,240,479,'If an electron in hydrogen atom jumps from an orbit of level $n = 3$ to an orbit of level $n = 2$, the emitted radiation has a frequency ($R = $ Rydberg constant, $c = $ velocity of light)','[{"text": "$\\\\frac{{Rc}}{{25}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{{5Rc}}{{36}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{{3Rc}}{{27}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{{8Rc}}{9}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'When an electron jumps from higher level to lower energy level ${n_2}$, the frequency of the emitted radiation is $v = Rc\\left[ {\\frac{1}{{n_2^2}} - \\frac{1}{{n_1^2}}} \\right]$<br>$\\therefore $ For $n = 3$ to $n = 2$<br>$v = Rc\\left[ {\\frac{1}{{{2^2}}} - \\frac{1}{{{3^2}}}} \\right] = \\frac{{5Rc}}{{36}}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2015,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44337,'physics',39,1103,2630,'A proton is about 1840 times heavier than an electron. When it is accelerated by a potential difference of $1kV$, its kinetic energy will be:','[{"text": "$1/1840\\\\,keV$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1840\\\\,keV$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$920\\\\,keV$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1\\\\,keV$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'When a charge $$q$$ is accelerated by a potential difference of $V$, the energy imparted to it is $qV$. In the given problem potential difference $ = 1kV$.<br>Charge on the proton $$=1.6 \\times 10^{-19}$$ coulomb. The energy imparted $ = 1keV.$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44338,'physics',38,1065,2541,'A plane electromagnetic wave of wave intensity $6\\,W/{m^2}$  strikes a small mirror of area  $40\\,c{m^2}$, held perpendicular to the approaching wave. The momentum transferred by wave to the mirror each second will be','[{"text": "$4.8 \\\\times {10^{ - 8}}\\\\,kg - m/s$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$3.2 \\\\times {10^{ - 9}}\\\\,kg - m/s$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$6.4 \\\\times {10^{ - 7}}\\\\,kg - m/s$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1.6 \\\\times {10^{ - 10}}\\\\,kg - m/s$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'$P = \\frac{{2U}}{c} = \\frac{{2{I_{av}}A}}{c} = 1.6 \\times {10^{ - 10}}\\,kg \\cdot m/s$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44339,'physics',37,972,2260,'If $E = 100\\sin (100t)$ volt and $I = 100\\sin \\left( {100t + \\frac{\\pi }{3}} \\right)mA$ are the instantaneous values of voltage and current, then the $r$.$m$.$s$. values of voltage and current are respectively.','[{"text": "$141.4\\\\,V,141.4\\\\,A$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$141.4\\\\,V,141.4\\\\,mA$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$70.7\\\\,V,70.7A$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$70.7\\\\,V,70.7mA$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'The rms value of voltage is <br>${E_{rms}} = \\frac{{{E_o}}}{{\\sqrt 2 }} = \\frac{{100}}{{\\sqrt 2 }}V = 70.7V$<br>The rms value of current is<br>${I_{rms}} = \\frac{{{I_o}}}{{\\sqrt 2 }} = \\frac{{100}}{{\\sqrt 2 }}mA = 70.7mA$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44340,'physics',40,240,479,'Hydrogen atom from excited state comes to the ground state by emitting a photon of wavelength $\\lambda $.If $R$ is the Rydberg constant, the principal quantum number $n$ of the excited state is','[{"text": "$\\\\sqrt {\\\\frac{{\\\\lambda R}}{{\\\\lambda R - 1}}} $", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\sqrt {\\\\frac{\\\\lambda }{{\\\\lambda R - 1}}} $", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\sqrt {\\\\frac{{\\\\lambda {R^2}}}{{\\\\lambda R - 1}}} $", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\sqrt {\\\\frac{{\\\\lambda R}}{{\\\\lambda  - 1}}} $", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'According to Rydberg’s formula<br>$\\frac{1}{\\lambda } = R\\left( {\\frac{1}{{n_f^2}} - \\frac{1}{{n_i^2}}} \\right)$<br>Here, ${n_f} = 1,\\,\\,\\,{n_i} = n$<br>$\\therefore \\frac{1}{\\lambda } = R\\left( {\\frac{1}{{{1^2}}} - \\frac{1}{{{n^2}}}} \\right)$<br>$ \\Rightarrow \\frac{1}{{{n^2}}} = 1 - \\frac{1}{{\\lambda R}} = \\frac{{\\lambda R - 1}}{{\\lambda R}}$<br>$ \\Rightarrow n = \\sqrt {\\frac{{\\lambda R}}{{\\lambda R - 1}}} $',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2009,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44341,'physics',39,1103,2630,'A photon of wavelength $4000\\mathop A\\limits^o $ is passing through vacuum. The effective mass and momentum of the photon are respectively','[{"text": "$5 \\\\times {10^{ - 35}}\\\\,kg,1.5 \\\\times {10^{ - 26}}\\\\,kg - m/s$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$5 \\\\times {10^{ - 36}}\\\\,kg,1.5 \\\\times {10^{ - 27}}\\\\,kg - m/s$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$5 \\\\times {10^{ - 36}}\\\\,kg,1.67 \\\\times {10^{ - 43}}\\\\,kg - m/s$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Zero, $1.5 \\\\times {10^{ - 26}}\\\\,kg - m/s$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'$p = \\frac{h}{\\lambda } = \\frac{{6.6 \\times {{10}^{ - 34}}}}{{4400 \\times {{10}^{ - 10}}}} = 1.5 \\times {10^{ - 27}}\\,kg - m/s$<br>and mass $m = \\frac{p}{c} = \\frac{{1.5 \\times {{10}^{ - 27}}}}{{3 \\times {{10}^8}}} = 5 \\times {10^{ - 36}}\\,kg$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44343,'physics',38,1066,2543,'One requires $11eV$ of energy to dissociate a carbon monoxide molecule into carbon and oxygen atoms. The minimum frequency of the appropriate electromagnetic radiation to achieve the dissociation lies in','[{"text": "Infrared region", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Visible region", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Microwave region", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Ultraviolet region", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Given, energy required to dissociate a carbon monoxide molecule into carbon and oxygen atoms.<br>$E = 11e\\,V$<br>We know that, $E = h\\upsilon $, where<br>$h = 6.62 \\times {10^{ - 34}}J - s;\\upsilon  = {\\rm{ frequency }}$<br>$ \\Rightarrow 11e\\upsilon  = h\\upsilon $<br>$ \\Rightarrow \\quad \\upsilon  = \\frac{{11 \\times 1 \\cdot 6 \\times {{10}^{ - 19}}}}{h}\\,J$<br>$E = h\\upsilon $<br>$ = \\frac{{11 \\times 1 \\cdot 6 \\times {{10}^{ - 19}}}}{{6 \\cdot 62 \\times {{10}^{ - 34}}}}\\,J = 2.65 \\times {10^{15}}\\,Hz$<br>This frequency belongs to ultraviolet region.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,'NCERT Exempler','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44344,'physics',40,240,479,'For Balmer series, wavelengthh of first line is  ${\\lambda _1}$ and for Brackett series, wavelength of first line is ${\\lambda _2}$, then $\\frac{{{\\lambda _1}}}{{{\\lambda _2}}}$ is','[{"text": "$0.081$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$0.162$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$0.198$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$0.238$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'For Balmer series, wavelength of first line $\\left( {{\\lambda _1}} \\right)$ is given by <br>$\\frac{1}{{{\\lambda _1}}} = R\\left( {\\frac{1}{{{2^2}}} - \\frac{1}{{{3^2}}}} \\right) = \\frac{{5R}}{{36}}$<br>Where, $R$ is Rydberg constant<br>For Brackett series, wavelength of first line $\\left( {{\\lambda _2}} \\right)$is given by<br>$\\frac{1}{{\\left( {{\\lambda _2}} \\right)}} = R\\left( {\\frac{1}{{{4^2}}} - \\frac{1}{{{5^2}}}} \\right) = \\frac{{9R}}{{400}}$<br>$\\therefore \\frac{{{\\lambda _1}}}{{{\\lambda _2}}} = \\frac{{1/{\\lambda _2}}}{{1/{\\lambda _1}}} = \\frac{{9R/400}}{{5R/36}} = 0.162$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2015,'MHTCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44345,'physics',39,1104,2631,'An electron of mass $$m$$ and charge $e$ initially at rest gets accelerated by a constant electric field $$E$$. The rate of change of de Broglie wavelength of this electron at time $$t$$ ignoring relativistic effect is','[{"text": "$$\\\\dfrac{-e E t}{E}$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$\\\\dfrac{-h}{e E t^{2}}$$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$$\\\\dfrac{-h}{e E}$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$\\\\dfrac{-m h}{e E t^{2}}$$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Here, $$u=0 ; a=\\dfrac{e E}{m}$$;<br>$\\therefore \\quad v = u + at = 0 + \\frac{{eE}}{m}t$ (from equation of motion) de Broglie wavelength,<br>$\\lambda  = \\frac{h}{{mv}} = \\frac{h}{{m(eEt/m)}} = \\frac{h}{{eEt}}$<br>Rate of change of de Broglie wavelength,<br>$$\\dfrac{d \\lambda}{d t}=\\dfrac{h}{e E}\\left(-\\dfrac{1}{t^{2}}\\right)=\\dfrac{-h}{e E t^{2}}$$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44346,'physics',37,972,2261,'The $rms$ value of an $ac$ of 50 $Hz$ is 10$A$. The time taken by an alternating current in reaching from zero to maximum value or the peak value will be','[{"text": "$5 \\\\times {10^{ - 3}}\\\\,s\\\\,{\\\\rm{and}}\\\\,14.14A$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$1 \\\\times {10^{ - 2}}s\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\rm{and}}\\\\,7.07A$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2 \\\\times {10^{ - 2}}s\\\\,{\\\\rm{and}}\\\\,14.14A$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$5 \\\\times {10^{ - 3}}s\\\\,{\\\\rm{and}}\\\\,7.07A$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Time for reaching maximum or peak value from 0<br>$ = \\frac{T}{4} = \\frac{1}{4} \\times \\frac{1}{{50}}s = \\frac{1}{{200}}s = 5 \\times {10^{ - 3}}s$<br>${I_o} = 10\\sqrt 2 A = 14.14A$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44347,'physics',38,1065,2539,'An electromagnetic wave travels along $z$-axis. Which of the following pairs of space and time varying fields would generate such wave?','[{"text": "${E_x},{B_y}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$$E_{y}, B_{x}$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$E_{y}, B_{z}$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${E_z},\\\\,{B_x}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'$$E_{x}$$ and $$B_{y}$$ would generate a plane electro-magnetic wave travelling in z-direction. $i$,e., $E$ is along $x$-axis and $B$ is along $y$-axis.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44348,'physics',39,1102,2629,'Radiations of two photon energies twice and five times the work function of metal are incident successively on the metal surface. The ratio of the maximum velocity of the photoelectrons emitted in the two cases will be:','[{"text": "$$1: 2$$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$$1: 1$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$1: 4$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$1: 3$$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'$$E-\\phi_{0}=\\dfrac{1}{2} m v^{2}$$<br>$2{\\phi _0} - {\\phi _0} = \\frac{1}{2}mv_1^2\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,(1)$<br>$5{\\phi _0} - {\\phi _0} = \\frac{1}{2}mv_2^2\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,(2)$<br>Therefore, from eqs. (1) and (2), we get<br>$$\\dfrac{2 \\phi_{0}-\\phi_{0}}{5 \\phi_{0}-\\phi_{0}}=\\dfrac{v_{1}^{2}}{v_{2}^{2}} \\Rightarrow \\dfrac{v_{1}}{v_{2}}=\\dfrac{1}{2}$$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44349,'physics',37,972,2259,'An alternating voltage is represented as $E = 20\\sin \\,300t$. The average value of voltage over one cycle will be','[{"text": "$10\\\\,volt$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Zero", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{{20}}{{\\\\sqrt 2 }}{\\\\rm{volt}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$20\\\\sqrt 2 {\\\\rm{volt}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,NULL,NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44350,'physics',38,1065,2542,'An intense light source radiates uniformly in all directions. At a distance $5\\,m$ from the source, the radiation pressure on absorbing surface is $9 \\times {10^{ - 6}}\\,Pa$. Find the total average power output.','[{"text": "$8.5 \\\\times {10^5}\\\\,W$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$8.5 \\\\times {10^3}\\\\,W$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$6.5 \\\\times {10^3}\\\\,W$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$6.5 \\\\times {10^5}\\\\,W$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'$$p_{\\text {rad }}=\\dfrac{I}{c}$$ and Power $$=I 4 \\pi \\mathrm{r}^{2}$$<br>$ = {p_{{\\rm{rad }}}} \\times c4\\pi {r^2}$<br>$ = 9 \\times {10^{ - 6}} \\times 3 \\times {10^8} \\times 12.56 \\times 25$<br>$ = 8.5 \\times {10^5}\\,W$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44351,'physics',40,239,478,'The ground state energy of hydrogen atom is  $ - 13.6\\,eV$. To send its electron to the first excited state, its excitation energy required is','[{"text": "$6.8\\\\,eV$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$3.4\\\\,eV$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{Zero}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$10.2\\\\,eV$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'In the ground state, $n = 1$<br>For $n = 2$<br>$E = \\frac{{ - 13.6}}{{{2^2}}} =  - 3.4\\,eV$<br>So,$ - 3.4\\,eV$ energy of electron in the first excited state  is<br> An  additonal energy of $10.2\\,eV$ is required to the  excite electron',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44382,'physics',37,973,2268,'If the reading of voltmeter ${V_1}$ is 40 $V$, what is the reading of voltmeter ${V_2}$?','[{"text": "$40\\\\,V$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$20\\\\,V$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$30\\\\,V$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$10\\\\,V$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'$AC$ voltmeter reads rms value of voltage. RMS Voltage applied $ = \\frac{{{V_o}}}{{\\sqrt 2 }} = 50V$<br>Reading of voltmeter ${V_2}$ is $x$, then<br>$\\sqrt {{x^2} + {{40}^2}}  = 50V$<br>$ \\Rightarrow x = 30V$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/37_44382_question_1.png'),
(44352,'physics',39,1102,2629,'The velocity of the most energetic electrons emitted from a metallic surface is doubled when the frequency $$v$$ of the incident radiation is doubled. The work function of this metal is:','[{"text": "$$\\\\dfrac{h v}{3}$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$\\\\dfrac{h v}{4}$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$\\\\dfrac{2 h v}{3}$$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$$\\\\dfrac{h v}{2}$$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Given that ${k_0} = hv - {\\phi _0}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,(1)$<br>${k_0} \\times 4 = 2hv - {\\phi _0}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,(2)$<br>From (1), and (2)<br>$$\\begin{aligned}& 4 \\times h v-4 \\phi_{0}=2 h v-\\phi_{0} \\\\& 2 h v=3 \\phi_{0} \\\\& \\phi_{0}=\\dfrac{2 h v}{3}\\end{aligned}$$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44353,'physics',37,972,2635,'In case of $AC$ circuits the relation $V = iZ$, where $Z$ is impedance, can directly applied to','[{"text": "Peak values of voltage and current only", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Rms values of voltage and current only", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Instantaneous values of voltage and current only", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both 1 and 2 are true", "isCorrect": true}]',3,NULL,NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44354,'physics',38,1065,2541,'A radiation of $200\\,W$ is incident on a surface which is $$60 \\%$$ reflecting and $$40 \\%$$ absorbing. The total force on the surface is','[{"text": "$1.07 \\\\times {10^{ - 6}}\\\\,N$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$1.07 \\\\times {10^{ - 7}}\\\\,N$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1.03 \\\\times {10^{ - 7}}\\\\,N$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1.3 \\\\times {10^{ - 6}}\\\\,N$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'$$P_{r a d}=(1+\\rho) \\dfrac{I}{c}$$ where<br>$$\\rho=$$ coefficient of reflection<br>$$F=(1+\\rho) \\dfrac{I}{C} \\times A$$<br>$\\therefore F = (1 + 0.6)\\frac{{200}}{{3 \\times {{10}^8} \\times A}} \\times A = 1.07 \\times {10^{ - 6}}\\,N$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44355,'physics',40,241,480,'The ionisation energy of an electron in the ground state of helium atom is $24.6\\,eV$. The energy required to remove both the elctron is','[{"text": "$51.8\\\\,eV$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$79\\\\,eV$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$38.2\\\\,eV$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$49.2\\\\,eV$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'First ionization energy of helium atom is <br>${I_1} = 24.6\\,eV$<br>Second ionization energy of helium atom is <br>${I_2} = \\frac{{13.6{{\\left( 2 \\right)}^2}}}{{{{\\left( 1 \\right)}^2}}} = 54.4eV$<br>$\\therefore $ Energy required to remove both the electrons from helium atom is<br>$ = {I_1} + {I_2} = 24.6\\,eV + 54.4\\,eV = 79\\,\\,eV$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2013,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44356,'physics',39,1102,2629,'Work-function for caesium metal is \\[2.14\\,eV.\\] Let a beam of light of frequency \\[6 \\times {10^{14}}\\;Hz\\] is incident over the metal surface. Now, match the following columns and choose the correct option<div class="matching-question"><div class="column-left">A. maximum $KE$ of emitted photoelectrons (in $eV$)<br>B. Minimum $KE$ of emitted photoelectrons (in $eV$<br>C. Stopping potential of material ( in $mV$) is<br>D. Maximum speed of the emitted photoelectrons ( in $km{s^{ - 1}}$)</div><div class="column-right">P. 334 $km{s^{ - 1}}$<br>Q. 345 $mV$<br>R. $0.345\\,eV$<br>S. 0</div></div>','[{"text": "${\\\\rm{A - S,}}\\\\,{\\\\rm{B - R,}}\\\\,{\\\\rm{C - Q,}}\\\\,{\\\\rm{D - P}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{A - R,}}\\\\,{\\\\rm{B - S,}}\\\\,{\\\\rm{C - Q,}}\\\\,{\\\\rm{D - P}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{A - R,}}\\\\,{\\\\rm{B - P,}}\\\\,{\\\\rm{C - S,}}\\\\,{\\\\rm{D - P}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{A - Q,}}\\\\,{\\\\rm{B - P,}}\\\\,{\\\\rm{C - S,}}\\\\,{\\\\rm{D - R}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'<br>Maximum $K.E$ is given by<br>${K_{{\\rm{max}}}} = hf - {\\phi _0}$<br>$ = \\frac{{6.62 \\times {{10}^{ - 34}} \\times 6 \\times {{10}^{14}}}}{{1.6 \\times {{10}^{ - 19}}}} - 2.14$<br>$ = 2.485 - 2.140 = 0.345\\,eV$<br>${K_{{\\rm{min}}}} = 0,({\\rm{so}}\\,B \\to 4)$<br>Stopping potential, ${V_0} = \\frac{{{K_{{\\rm{max}}}}}}{e} = \\frac{{0.345eV}}{e}$<br>$ \\Rightarrow {V_0} = 0.345\\,V = 345\\,mV$<br>Maximum speed of emitted electrons,<br>${K_{{\\rm{max}}}} = \\frac{1}{2}mv_{{\\rm{max}}}^{\\rm{2}}{\\rm{ }}$<br>${\\rm{or}}\\,\\,{v_{{\\rm{max}}}} = \\sqrt {\\frac{{2{K_{{\\rm{max}}}}}}{m}}  = \\sqrt {\\frac{{2 \\times 0.345 \\times 1.6 \\times {{10}^{ - 19}}}}{{9.1 \\times {{10}^{ - 31}}}}} $<br>$ = 3.34 \\times {10^5}\\,m{s^{ - 1}} = 334\\,km{s^{ - 1}}$<br>So option (2) is correct.',NULL,'Hard','Match the following',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44357,'physics',37,975,2278,'In an $LCR$ series resonant circuit which one of the following cannot be the expression for the $Q $- factor?','[{"text": "$\\\\frac{R}{{LC}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\sqrt {\\\\frac{L}{C}} \\\\frac{1}{R}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{1}{{\\\\omega CR}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{{\\\\omega L}}{R}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'The Q-factor $Q = \\frac{1}{R}\\sqrt {\\frac{L}{C}} $<br>$\\omega  = \\frac{1}{{\\sqrt {LC} }}$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44358,'physics',38,1065,2540,'In an apparatus, the electric field was found to oscillate with amplitude of $18\\,V/m$. The amplitude of the oscillating magnetic field will be','[{"text": "$11 \\\\times {10^{ - 11}}\\\\,T$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$6 \\\\times {10^{ - 8}}\\\\,T$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$4 \\\\times {10^{ - 6}}\\\\,T$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$9 \\\\times {10^{ - 9}}\\\\,T$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'${B_0} = \\frac{{{E_0}}}{c} = \\frac{{18}}{{3 \\times {{10}^8}}} = 6 \\times {10^{ - 8}}\\,T$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44359,'physics',40,240,479,'When the electron in hydrogen atom jumps from fourth Bohr orbit to second Bohr orbit, one gets the','[{"text": "Second line of Balmer series", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "First line of Balmer series", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "First line of Pfund series", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Second line of Paschen  series", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'The wavelength of line in case of Balmer series is given by<br>$\\frac{1}{\\lambda } = R\\left( {\\frac{1}{{{2^2}}} - \\frac{1}{{{n^2}}}} \\right)$ where $n = 3,4,5$<br>and $R = $ Rydberg constant<br>So, for Balmer series, the transition takes from third orbit to second for first line spectrum, fourth orbit to second for second line spectrum and so on. Hence, given transition represents second line of Balmer series',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2019,'MHTCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44360,'physics',37,978,2282,'$L$, $C$ and $R$ represent physical quantities inductance, capacitance and resistance respectively. The combination representing  dimension of frequency is','[{"text": "$\\\\frac{C}{L}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\left( {\\\\frac{L}{C}} \\\\right)^{ - 1/2}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\left( {LC} \\\\right)^{ - 1/2}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$LC$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Frequency $ = \\frac{1}{{2\\pi \\sqrt {LC} }}$<br>So the combination which represents dimension of frequency is $\\frac{1}{{\\sqrt {LC} }} = {\\left( {LC} \\right)^{ - 1/2}}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44383,'physics',39,1102,2627,'The curve between photoelectric current (\\[i\\]) and frequency $$(v)$$ is<div class="matching-question"></div>','[{"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'For a minimum frequency called threshold frequency the photoelectrons are emitted from the surface. If we increase the frequency the current increases and it reaches a saturation value.',NULL,'Medium','Graph/Figure',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44361,'physics',39,1102,2628,'The curve drawn between velocity and frequency of photon in vacuum will be a','[{"text": "Straight line parallel to frequency axis", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Straight line parallel to velocity axis", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Straight line passing through origin and making an angle of $$45^{\\\\circ}$$ with frequency axis", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Hyperbola", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Velocity of a photon (light) does not depend upon frequency. Hence the graph between velocity and frequency will be a straight line<br><img src=\'/img/data/physics/39_44361_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44362,'physics',38,1065,2539,'A plane electromagnetic wave propagating in the $$x$$ direction has a wavelength of $5.0\\,mm$. The electric field is in the y-direction and its maximum magnitude is $30\\,V/m$. Find equation for the electric field as a function of $$x$$ and $t.$','[{"text": "$$E=30 \\\\sin \\\\left[12 \\\\pi \\\\times 10^{10} t-400 \\\\pi x\\\\right]$$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$$E=15 \\\\sin \\\\left[400 \\\\pi t-12 \\\\pi \\\\times 10^{10} x\\\\right]$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$E=30 \\\\sin \\\\left[48 \\\\pi \\\\times 10^{10} t-1200 \\\\pi x\\\\right]$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$E=15 \\\\sin \\\\left[48 \\\\pi \\\\times 10^{10} t-1200 \\\\pi x\\\\right]$$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'The equation for the electric field is<br>$$E=E_{0} \\sin \\omega\\left(t-\\dfrac{x}{c}\\right)$$<br>We have, $$\\omega=2 \\pi v=\\dfrac{2 \\pi}{\\lambda} c$$<br>Thus, $$E=E_{0} \\sin \\left[\\dfrac{2 \\pi}{\\lambda}(c t-x)\\right]$$<br>$ = (30\\,V/m)\\sin \\left[ {\\frac{{2\\pi }}{{5\\,mm}}(ct - x)} \\right]$<br>$ = 30\\,V/m\\sin \\left[ {12\\pi  \\times {{10}^{10}}t - 400\\pi x} \\right]$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44363,'physics',37,975,2275,'A capacitor, a resistor and a 40 $mH$ inductor are connected in series to an $ac$ source of frequency 60 $Hz$. Calculate the capacitance of the capacitor, if the current is in phase with the voltage.','[{"text": "$125\\\\,\\\\mu F$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$176\\\\,\\\\mu F$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$15\\\\,\\\\mu F$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$45\\\\,\\\\mu F$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'If the current is in phase with the voltage,<br>${V_L} = {V_C} \\Rightarrow {X_L} = {X_C}$<br>$ \\Rightarrow \\frac{1}{{2\\pi \\sqrt {LC} }} = f$<br>$4{\\pi ^2}{f^2}LC = 1 \\Rightarrow C = \\frac{1}{{4{\\pi ^2}{f^2}L}} = 175.7\\mu F$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44364,'physics',39,1102,2628,'For a photoelectric cell the graph showing the variation of cutoff voltage $$\\left(V_{S}\\right)$$ with frequency (\\[v\\]) of incident light is best represented by<div class="matching-question"></div>','[{"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'According to Einstein\'s equation<br>$$\\begin{aligned}& h v=\\phi+K_{\\max } \\\\& \\Rightarrow V_{S}=\\left(\\dfrac{h}{e}\\right) v-\\dfrac{\\phi}{e}\\end{aligned}$$<br>This is the equation of straight line having positive slope ($h/e$) and intercept on $$-V_{S}$$ axis, equal to $$\\dfrac{\\phi}{e}$$. The correct graph is (4).',NULL,'Medium','Graph/Figure',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44365,'physics',40,240,479,'The ratio of  minimum wavelengths of Lyman and Balmer series will be','[{"text": "$5$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$10$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1.25$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$0.25$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Wavelength is inversely proportional to energy difference and therefore minimum  wavelength is for highest energy difference For Lyman series, maximum energy difference<br>$(for\\,n = 1 \\to n = \\infty ) = {E_0}/{1^2} = {E_0}$<br>For Balmer series,  maximum energy difference<br>$(for\\,\\,n = 2 \\to n = \\infty ) = {E_0}/{2^2} = {E_0}/4$<br>$\\therefore $ Ratio of  minimum wavelengths is $1/4 = 0.25$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2011,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44366,'physics',38,1064,2538,'The diameter of the condenser plate is $4\\,cm$. It is charged by an external current of $0.2\\,A$ . The maximum magnetic field induced in the gap','[{"text": "$$2 \\\\mu T$$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$$8 \\\\mu T$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$4 \\\\mu T$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$6 \\\\mu T$$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'$$B=\\dfrac{\\mu_{0} i}{2 \\pi r}=2 \\mu T(\\mathrm{r}=$$ radius of the plate $$)$$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44367,'physics',37,973,2267,'Find the capacity of a condenser to run a 30 $V$, 10 $W$ lamp when connected in series with an alternating emf of  200$V$ and frequency 50 $C/s$.','[{"text": "$4.9\\\\,\\\\mu F$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$3.2\\\\,\\\\mu F$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1.8\\\\,\\\\mu F$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$0.5\\\\,\\\\mu F$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'${X_C} = \\frac{1}{{\\omega C}} = \\frac{1}{{100\\pi C}}$<br>$P = \\frac{{{V^2}}}{R} \\Rightarrow R = \\frac{{900}}{{10}} = 90\\Omega $<br>$Z = \\sqrt {{{90}^2} + {{\\left( {\\frac{1}{{100\\pi C}}} \\right)}^2}} $<br>${V^2} = V_C^2 + V_R^2 \\Rightarrow {V_C} = \\sqrt {{{220}^2} - {{30}^2}} $<br>or ${V_C} = 218\\,volt$<br>$i = \\frac{{{V_c}}}{{{X_c}}} = \\frac{V}{z} \\Rightarrow \\frac{{218}}{{(\\frac{1}{{100}}\\pi c)}}$ $ = \\frac{{220}}{{\\sqrt {8100 + {{\\left( {\\frac{1}{{100\\pi C}}} \\right)}^2}} }}$<br>$ \\Rightarrow C = 4.8\\mu F$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44368,'physics',39,1102,2628,'The correct curve between the stopping potential $$\\left(V_{o}\\right)$$ and intensity of incident light (\\[I\\]) is<div class="matching-question"></div>','[{"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Stopping potential depends on frequency and it doesn\'t depend on intensity. The correct graph is (2).',NULL,'Medium','Graph/Figure',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL);
INSERT INTO `qb_questions` (`id`, `subject_id`, `chapter_id`, `topic_id`, `subtopic_id`, `question_html`, `options`, `correct_index`, `explanation`, `explanation_image_url`, `difficulty`, `qtype`, `year`, `tag`, `created_at`, `question_image_url`) VALUES
(44369,'physics',38,1064,2537,'To establish an instantaneous displacement current of $2\\,A$ in the space between two parallel plate of $$1 \\mu F$$ capacitor, the potential difference across the capacitor plates will have to be changed at the rate of','[{"text": "$2 \\\\times {10^4}\\\\,V/s$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$4 \\\\times {10^4}\\\\,V/s$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$4 \\\\times {10^6}\\\\,V/s$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2 \\\\times {10^6}\\\\,V/s$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'${I_d} = {\\varepsilon _0}\\frac{{d{\\phi _E}}}{{dt}} = {\\varepsilon _0}\\frac{d}{{dt}}(EA) = {\\varepsilon _0}\\,A\\frac{d}{{dt}}\\left( {\\frac{V}{d}} \\right)$<br>or $\\quad {I_d} = \\frac{{{\\varepsilon _0}\\,A}}{{\\,d}}\\frac{{dV}}{{dt}} = C\\frac{{dV}}{{dt}}$<br>$\\therefore \\frac{{dV}}{{dt}} = \\frac{{{I_d}}}{C} = \\frac{2}{{{{10}^{ - 6}}}} = 2 \\times {10^6}\\,V/s$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44370,'physics',40,241,480,'The energy levels with transitions for the atom are shown. The transitions corresponding to emission of radiation of maximum and minimum wavelength are respectively','[{"text": "$B,C$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$A,C$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$C,D$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$A,D$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'As, we know that the difference of energy between two level is equal to the energy of photon<br>$\\Delta E = hv = \\frac{{hc}}{\\lambda } \\Rightarrow \\lambda  = \\frac{{hc}}{{\\Delta E}}$<br>or $\\lambda  \\propto \\frac{1}{{\\Delta E}}$<br>The value of $\\Delta E$ is minimum for $A$, so radiation corresponding to $A$ have maximum wavelength and value of $\\Delta E$ is maximum for $D$, so radiation corresponding to $D$ have minimum wavelength',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2020,'MHTCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/40_44370_question_1.png'),
(44372,'physics',39,1102,2627,'The curve between current (\\[i\\]) and potential difference $$(V)$$ for a photo cell will be<div class="matching-question"></div>','[{"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'In photocell, as the potential difference between cathode and anode increases current through the circuit increases but after some time constant current (saturation current) flows through the circuit even if potential difference still increases. At a particular negative potential (stopping potential $$V_{o}$$ ) of anode, photoelectric current is zero.',NULL,'Medium','Graph/Figure',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44373,'physics',38,1064,2537,'The displacement current flows in the dielectric of a capacitor when the potential difference across its plate','[{"text": "Is changing with time", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Displacement current does not depend on potential difference", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Becomes zero", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Has assumed a constant value", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'$$\\mathrm{i}_{d}=\\varepsilon_{0} \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d} \\phi}{\\mathrm{dt}}=\\varepsilon_{0} \\dfrac{\\operatorname{Ad} E}{\\mathrm{dt}}$$<br>$$\\Rightarrow \\mathrm{i}_{d} \\neq 0 \\text { whenever } \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d} E}{\\mathrm{dt}} \\neq 0$$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44374,'physics',40,240,479,'Electron in hydrogen atom first jumps from third excited state to second excited state and then from second excited to the first excited state. The ratio of the wavelengths  ${\\lambda _1}:{\\lambda _2}$ emitted in the two cases is','[{"text": "$20/7$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$7/5$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$27/20$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$27/5$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'We know that, $\\frac{1}{\\lambda } = R\\left[ {\\frac{1}{{n_1^2}} - \\frac{1}{{n_2^2}}} \\right]$<br>So, for  ${\\lambda _1}$<br>$ \\Rightarrow \\quad \\frac{1}{{{\\lambda _1}}} = R\\left[ {\\frac{1}{{{{\\left( 3 \\right)}^2}}} - \\frac{1}{{{{\\left( 4 \\right)}^2}}}} \\right]$<br>$\\frac{1}{{{\\lambda _1}}} = R\\left[ {\\frac{7}{{144}}} \\right]$                          (1)<br>Similarly, for ${\\lambda _2}$<br>$ \\Rightarrow \\quad \\frac{1}{{{\\lambda _2}}} = R\\left[ {\\frac{1}{{{{\\left( 2 \\right)}^2}}} - \\frac{1}{{{{\\left( 3 \\right)}^2}}}} \\right]$<br>$\\frac{1}{{{\\lambda _2}}} = R\\left[ {\\frac{5}{{36}}} \\right]$                    (2)<br>Hence, from Eqs. (1) and (2), we get<br>$\\frac{{{\\lambda _1}}}{{{\\lambda _2}}} = \\frac{{20}}{7}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44375,'physics',37,973,2268,'The following series $L - C - R$ circuit, when driven by an emf source of angular frequency 100 $k$-rad/s, the circuit effectively behaves like:','[{"text": "purely resistive circuit", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "series $R - L$ circuit", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "series $R - C$ circuit", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "series $L - C$ circuit with $R = 0$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Resonance frequency <br>${\\omega _0} = \\frac{1}{{\\sqrt {LC} }} = \\frac{1}{{\\sqrt {100 \\times {{10}^{ - 6}} \\times 1 \\times {{10}^{ - 6}}} }}$<br>$ = \\frac{1}{{\\sqrt {{{10}^{ - 10}}} }} = {10^5}rad/s$<br>Now, given $\\omega  = 100k - rad/s$<br>Here,${\\omega _0} = \\omega $<br>Thus, the circuit behaves like a purely resistive circuit.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/37_44375_question_1.png'),
(44376,'physics',39,1102,2627,'The value of stopping potential in the following diagram<div class="matching-question"></div>','[{"text": "$ - 8\\\\,V$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$ - 6\\\\,V$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$ - 4\\\\,V$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$ - 2\\\\,V$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Stopping potential is the potential where the current is zero.<br>$\\therefore  - 8v$',NULL,'Medium','Graph/Figure',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/39_44376_question_1.png'),
(44377,'physics',38,1065,2539,'A wave is propagating in a medium of dielectric constant 2 and relative permeability 50 . The wave impedance is','[{"text": "$$376.6 \\\\Omega$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$3776 \\\\Omega$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$5 \\\\Omega$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$1883 \\\\Omega$$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Wave impedance<br>$$z=\\sqrt{\\dfrac{\\mu}{\\varepsilon}}=\\sqrt{\\dfrac{\\mu_{r} \\mu_{o}}{\\varepsilon_{r} \\varepsilon_{o}}} \\sqrt{\\dfrac{\\mu_{r}}{\\varepsilon_{r}}} c \\mu_{o}=1883 \\Omega$$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44378,'physics',37,973,2268,'In the series $L - C - R$ circuit shown, the impedance is:','[{"text": "$200\\\\Omega $", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$100\\\\Omega $", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$300\\\\Omega $", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$500\\\\Omega $", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'${X_L} = 2\\pi fL = 2\\pi \\left( {\\frac{{100}}{\\pi }} \\right) \\times \\frac{1}{2} = 100\\Omega $<br>${X_C} = \\frac{1}{{2\\pi fC}} = \\frac{1}{{2\\pi \\left( {\\frac{{100}}{\\pi }} \\right)10 \\times {{10}^{ - 6}}}} = 500\\Omega $<br>$Z = \\sqrt {{R^2} + {{\\left( {{X_C} - {X_L}} \\right)}^2}} $<br>$Z = \\sqrt {{{\\left( {300} \\right)}^2} + {{\\left( {400} \\right)}^2}}  = 500\\Omega $',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/37_44378_question_1.png'),
(44379,'physics',40,240,479,'The wavelength of the first line of Lyman series for hydrogen atom is equal to that of the second line of Balmer series for a hydrogen like ion. The atomic number Z of hydrogen-like ion is','[{"text": "$1$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$4$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$3$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'For Lyman series for $H$-atom<br>$\\frac{1}{\\lambda } = R\\left( {\\frac{1}{{{1^2}}} - \\frac{1}{{{2^2}}}} \\right)$<br>For $H$-like ion,               $\\frac{1}{\\lambda } = {Z^2}R\\left( {\\frac{1}{{{2^2}}} - \\frac{1}{{{4^2}}}} \\right)$<br>$\\therefore \\quad \\left( {\\frac{1}{{{1^2}}} - \\frac{1}{{{2^2}}}} \\right) = {Z^2}\\left( {\\frac{1}{4} - \\frac{1}{{16}}} \\right)$<br>$ \\Rightarrow Z = 2$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44380,'physics',39,1102,2629,'For the photoelectric effect, the maximum kinetic energy $$E_{k}$$ of the emitted photoelectrons is plotted against the frequency $$v$$ of the incident photons as shown in the figure. The slope of the curve gives<div class="matching-question"></div>','[{"text": "Charge of the electron", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Work function of the metal", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Planck\'s constant", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Ratio of the Planck\'s constant to electronic charge", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Comparing Einstein\'s equation $$K_{\\max }=h v-\\phi$$, with $$y=m x+c$$, we get slope, $$m=h$$',NULL,'Medium','Graph/Figure',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/39_44380_question_1.png'),
(44381,'physics',38,1064,2537,'The Maxwell\'s four equations are written as<br>(i) $$\\oint_{s} \\bar{E} \\cdot d \\bar{s}=\\dfrac{q}{\\varepsilon_{o}}$$<br>(ii) $$\\oint_{S} B . d s=0$$<br>(iii) $$\\oint_{S} E \\cdot d l=\\dfrac{d}{d t} \\int_{S} B \\cdot d s$$<br>(iv) $$\\int \\bar{B} \\cdot d \\bar{l}=\\mu_{0} I+\\mu_{0} \\varepsilon_{0} \\dfrac{d}{d t} \\int \\bar{E} \\cdot \\mathrm{d} \\bar{s}$$<br>The correct equations are','[{"text": "(i), (ii)", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "(i) and (iii) only", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "(i), (ii), (iii)", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "(i) and (iv) only", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Equation (i) is Gauss\'s law and equation (iv) is Ampere\'s law. Here, two equations are Maxwell\'s equations. For equations (ii) and (iii) vector forms are missing so they are not correct.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44384,'physics',40,240,479,'${v_1}$ is the frequency of the series limit of Lyman series,  ${v_2}$ is the frequency of the first line of Lyman series and  ${v_3}$ is the frequency of the series limit of the Balmer series. Then','[{"text": "${v_1} - {v_2} = {v_3}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${v_1} = {v_2} - {v_3}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{1}{{{v_2}}} = \\\\frac{1}{{{v_1}}} + \\\\frac{1}{{{v_3}}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{1}{{{v_1}}} = \\\\frac{1}{{{v_2}}} + \\\\frac{1}{{{v_3}}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'For Lyman series<br>$v = Rc\\left[ {\\frac{1}{{{1^2}}} - \\frac{1}{{{n^2}}}} \\right]$<br>where $n = 2,3,4.....$<br>For the series limit of Lyman series $n = \\infty $<br>$\\therefore {v_1} = Rc\\left[ {\\frac{1}{{{1^2}}} - \\frac{1}{{{\\infty ^2}}}} \\right] = Rc\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,(1)$<br>For the first line of Lyman series, $n = 2$<br>$\\therefore {v_2} = Rc\\left[ {\\frac{1}{{{1^2}}} - \\frac{1}{{{2^2}}}} \\right] = \\frac{3}{4}Rc\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,(2)$<br>For Balmer series<br>$v = Rc\\left[ {\\frac{1}{{{2^2}}} - \\frac{1}{{{n^2}}}} \\right]$<br>where $n = 3,4,5....$<br>For the series limit of Balmer series $n = \\infty $<br>$\\therefore {v_3} = Rc\\left[ {\\frac{1}{{{2^2}}} - \\frac{1}{{{\\infty ^2}}}} \\right] = \\frac{{Rc}}{4}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,(3)$<br>From equations (1), (2) and (3), we get<br>${v_1} = {v_2} + {v_3}$ or ${v_1} - {v_2} = {v_3}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2010,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44385,'physics',38,1065,2542,'A long straight wire of resistance $R$, radius \' $$a$$ \' and length \' $$l$$ \' carries a constant current \' $$I$$ \'. The poynting vector for the wire will be-','[{"text": "$$\\\\dfrac{I R^{2}}{a l}$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$\\\\dfrac{I^{2} R}{a l}$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$\\\\dfrac{I R}{2 \\\\pi a l}$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$\\\\dfrac{I^{2} R}{2 \\\\pi a l}$$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'If $$V$$ is the potential difference across the wire, then $$\\mathrm{E}=\\dfrac{\\mathrm{V}}{\\ell}=\\dfrac{\\mathrm{IR}}{\\ell}$$ and magnetic field at the surface of wire $$B=\\dfrac{\\mu_{0} I}{2 \\pi a}$$. Hence pointing vector, directed radially inwards, is given by<br>$$S=\\dfrac{E B}{\\mu_{0}}=\\dfrac{I R}{\\mu_{0} \\ell} \\times \\dfrac{\\mu_{0} I}{2 \\pi a}=\\dfrac{I^{2} R}{2 \\pi a l}$$<br>Hence the correct answer will be (4).',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44386,'physics',37,974,2273,'Power factor value is one for','[{"text": "Pure inductor", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Pure resistor", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Either an inductor or a capacitor", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Pure capacitor", "isCorrect": false}]',1,NULL,NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44387,'physics',39,1102,2629,'The figure shows the plot of the stopping potential versus the frequency of the light used in an experiment on photoelectric effect. The ratio $$h / e$$ is<div class="matching-question"></div>','[{"text": "${10^{ - 15}}Vs$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2 \\\\times {10^{ - 15}}Vs$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$3 \\\\times {10^{ - 15}}Vs$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$4.14 \\\\times {10^{ - 15}}Vs$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'$e{V_s} = hv - {\\phi _o}$<br>$$V_{s}=\\dfrac{h}{e} v-\\dfrac{\\phi_{o}}{e}$$<br>Now, $$\\quad \\dfrac{h}{e}=$$ slope $$=\\dfrac{1.656}{4 \\times 10^{14}}$$<br>$$\\begin{aligned}& =0.414 \\times 10^{-14} V s \\\\& =4.14 \\times 10^{-15} V s\\end{aligned}$$',NULL,'Medium','Graph/Figure',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/39_44387_question_1.png'),
(44388,'physics',38,1065,2541,'Light with an energy flux of $18\\,W/c{m^2}$ falls on a non- reflecting suface at normal incidence. If the surface has an area of $20\\,c{m^2}$, find the average force exerted on the suface during a 30 minute time span.','[{"text": "$1.2 \\\\times {10^{ - 8}}\\\\,N$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1.2 \\\\times {10^{ - 7}}\\\\,N$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1.2 \\\\times {10^{ - 6}}\\\\,N$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$1.2 \\\\times {10^{ - 5}}\\\\,N$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'The total energy falling on the surface is<br>$U = \\left( {18\\,W/c{m^2}} \\right) \\times 20\\,c{m^2} \\times (30 \\times 60)\\infty $<br>$ = 6.48 \\times {10^5}\\,J$<br>Therefore, the total momentum delivered (for complete absorption) is<br>$p = \\frac{U}{c} = \\frac{{6.48 \\times {{10}^5}\\,J}}{{3 \\times {{10}^8}\\,m/s}} = 2.16 \\times {10^{ - 3}}\\,kg/ms$<br>The average force exerted on the surface is<br>$F = \\frac{p}{t} = \\frac{{2.16 \\times {{10}^{ - 3}}}}{{0.18 \\times {{10}^4}}} = 1.2 \\times {10^{ - 6}}\\,N$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44389,'physics',40,240,479,'Total energy of electron in an excited state of hydrogen atom is $ - 3.4\\,eV$. The kinetic and potential energy of electron in this state','[{"text": "$K =  - 3.4\\\\,{\\\\text{ }}eV\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\text{ }}U =  - 6.8\\\\,{\\\\text{ }}eV$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$K = 3.4\\\\,{\\\\text{ }}eV\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\text{ }}U =  - 6.8\\\\,{\\\\text{ }}eV$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$K =  - 6.8\\\\,eV\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\text{ }}U =  + 3.4\\\\,{\\\\text{ }}eV$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$K =  + 10.2\\\\,eV\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\text{ }}U =  - 13.6\\\\,{\\\\text{ }}eV$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Total energy of electron in an excited state, $E =  - 3.4\\,eV$<br>$\\therefore \\,K =  - E = 3.4\\,eV$<br>$V = E - K =  - 3.4 - 3.4 =  - 6.8\\,eV$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2016,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44390,'physics',37,974,2274,'For a series $L - C - R$ circuit at resonance, the statement which is not true?','[{"text": "Peak energy stored by a capacitor = peak energy stored by an inductor.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Average power = apparent power", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Wattless current is zero", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Power factor is zero", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'For a series $L - C - R$ circuit at resonance, <br>Phase difference,$\\phi  = 0^\\circ  \\Rightarrow $ Power factor =<br>$\\cos \\phi  = 1$ and peak energy stored in capacitor <br>${U_C} = \\frac{1}{2}CV_{(c)}^2$<br>${U_c} = \\frac{1}{2}C{I^2}X_c^2 = \\frac{1}{2}C{I^2}\\frac{1}{{\\omega _0^2{C^2}}}$<br>$ = \\frac{1}{2}{I^2}\\frac{{LC}}{C} = \\frac{1}{2}L{I^2} = {U_L}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44391,'physics',39,1102,2627,'In the following diagram if $$V_{2}>V_{1}$$ then (for same photo metal)<div class="matching-question"></div>','[{"text": "$$\\\\lambda_{1}=\\\\sqrt{\\\\lambda_{2}}$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$\\\\lambda_{1} < \\\\lambda_{2}$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$\\\\lambda_{1}=\\\\lambda_{2}$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$\\\\lambda_{1}>\\\\lambda_{2}$$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,NULL,NULL,'Medium','Graph/Figure',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/39_44391_question_1.png'),
(44401,'physics',37,974,2766,'The power factor of $LCR$ circuit at resonance is','[{"text": "$0.5$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$0.707$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${\\\\mathop{\\\\rm Zero}\\\\nolimits} $", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'At resonance, $LCR$ circuit behaves as purely resistive circuit. For purely resistive circuit power factor = 1',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(46001,'physics',42,323,593,'There is a sudden increase in current in zener diode is','[{"text": "Due to rupture of bonds", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Resistance of depletion layer becomes less", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Due to high doping", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Due to less doping", "isCorrect": false}]',0,NULL,NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44392,'physics',38,1065,2540,'An electromagnetic wave in vacuum has the electric and magnetic field $\\overrightarrow E $ and $\\overrightarrow B $ which are always perpendicular to each other. The direction of polarisation is given by $$\\vec{X}$$ and that of wave propagation by $\\overrightarrow k $. Then','[{"text": "${\\\\rm{\\\\vec X||\\\\vec B}}\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\rm{and}}\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\rm{\\\\vec k||\\\\vec B \\\\times \\\\vec E}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$\\\\overrightarrow{\\\\mathrm{X}} \\\\| \\\\overrightarrow{\\\\mathrm{E}}$$ and $$\\\\overrightarrow{\\\\mathrm{k}} \\\\| \\\\overrightarrow{\\\\mathrm{E}} \\\\times \\\\overrightarrow{\\\\mathrm{B}}$$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$$\\\\overrightarrow{\\\\mathrm{X}} \\\\| \\\\overrightarrow{\\\\mathrm{B}}$$ and $$\\\\overrightarrow{\\\\mathrm{k}} \\\\| \\\\overrightarrow{\\\\mathrm{E}} \\\\times \\\\overrightarrow{\\\\mathrm{B}}$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$\\\\overrightarrow{\\\\mathrm{X}} \\\\| \\\\overrightarrow{\\\\mathrm{E}}$$ and $$\\\\overrightarrow{\\\\mathrm{k}} \\\\| \\\\overrightarrow{\\\\mathrm{B}} \\\\times \\\\overrightarrow{\\\\mathrm{C}}$$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Plane of oscillations of electric field is the plane of polarization.<br>Direction of propagation $$\\bar{K}$$ is always present along the direction of $$\\bar{E} \\times \\bar{B}$$.',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44393,'physics',37,974,2273,'A bulb is connected first with $dc$ and then $ac$ of same voltage it will shine brightly with','[{"text": "Equally with both", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$AC$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$DC$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Brightness will be in the ratio 1/1.4", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Brightness $ \\propto {P_{consumed}} \\propto \\frac{1}{R}.$<br>For bulb,${R_{AC}} = {R_{DC}},$  so brightness will be equal in both the cases.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44394,'physics',40,240,479,'The energy (in $eV$) required to excite an electron from $n = 2$ to $n = 4$ state in hydrogen atom','[{"text": "$ + 2.55$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$ - 3.4$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$ + 4.25$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$ - 0.85$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'In hydrogen atom, the energy required to excite an electron from $n = 2$ to $n = 4$ state,<br>${E_4} - {E_2} = \\frac{{ - 13.6}}{{{4^2}}} - \\frac{{ - 13.6}}{{{2^2}}}$<br>$ =  - 0.85 + 3.4 = 2.55\\,\\,eV$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2017,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44395,'physics',39,1102,2627,'The anode voltage of a photocell is kept fixed. The wavelength $$\\lambda$$ of the light falling on the cathode is gradually changed. The plate current \\[i\\] of the photocell varies as follows:<div class="matching-question"></div>','[{"text": "", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'On increasing wavelength of light the photoelectric current decreases and at a certain wavelength (cut off) above which photoelectric current stops.',NULL,'Medium','Graph/Figure',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44396,'physics',38,1065,2540,'In a plane electromagnetic wave, the electric field oscillated sinusoidally at a frequency of $2 \\times {10^{10}}\\,Hz$ and amplitude $48\\,V/m$. The amplitude of oscillating magnetic field will be','[{"text": "$\\\\frac{1}{{12}} \\\\times {10^{ - 7}}\\\\,Wb/{m^2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$16 \\\\times {10^{ - 8}}\\\\,Wb/{m^2}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{1}{{16}} \\\\times {10^{ - 8}}\\\\,Wb/{m^2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$12 \\\\times {10^{ - 7}}\\\\,Wb/{m^2}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'${B_0} = \\frac{{{E_0}}}{c} = 16 \\times {10^8}\\,Wb/{m^2}$<br>$(c = 3 \\times {10^8}m/s)$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44397,'physics',37,974,2767,'Match the following<div class="matching-question"><div class="column-left">A. Resistive circuit<br>B. Purely inductive or capacitive circuit<br>C. Power dissipated at resonance in L-C-R circuit</div><div class="column-right">P. No dissipation<br>Q. Wattless current<br>R. Maximum power dissipation</div></div>','[{"text": "A - Q,\\tB - P,\\tC - R", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "A - R,\\tB - Q,P,\\tC - R", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "A - P,      B - Q, \\t  C - R", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "A - P,      B - R,\\t  C - Q", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Resistive circuit : If the circuit contains only pure R, it is called resistive. In that case $\\phi  = 0,\\,\\,\\cos \\,\\,\\phi  = 1.$There is maximum power dissipation.<br>Purely inductive or capacitive circuit. If the circuit contains only an inductor or capacitor, we know that, the phase difference between voltage and current is $\\pi /2$.<br>Therefore,$\\cos \\phi  = 0$ and no power is dissipated even though a current is flowing in the circuit. This current is sometimes referred to as wattless current.<br>Power dissipated at resonance in $L - C - R$ circuit : At resonance <br>${X_C} - {X_L} = 0,\\,\\,{\\mathop{\\rm and}\\nolimits} \\,\\,\\phi  = 0$ Therefore,$\\cos \\,\\phi  = 1\\,\\,{\\mathop{\\rm and}\\nolimits} \\,\\,P = {I^2}Z = {I^2}R$ .That is maximum power is dissipated in a circuit (through $R$) at resonance',NULL,'Easy','Match the following',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44398,'physics',40,240,479,'If $n$ is the orbit number of the electron in a hydrogen atom, the correct statement among the following is','[{"text": "Electron energy increases as $n$ increases", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Hydrogen emits infrared rays for the electron transition from $n = \\\\infty $ to $n = 1$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Electron energy is zero for  $n = 1$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Electron energy varies as ${n^2}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'In a hydrogen atom the energy of electrons in ${n^{th}}$ orbit is<br>${E_n} =  - \\frac{{13.6}}{{{n^2}}}eV$<br>From the above expression it is clear that electron',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2012,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44399,'physics',39,1102,2629,'For two different metals a graph is drawn between stopping potential and frequency predict the correct option.<div class="matching-question"></div>','[{"text": "$Na$ and $Al$ both have the same threshold frequency", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Maximum kinetic energy for both the metals depend linearly on the frequency", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "The stopping potentials are same for $Na$ and $Al$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$Al$ is a better photo sensitive material than $Na$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Stopping potential equals to maximum kinetic energy. Since stopping potential is varying linearly with the frequency, therefore max. $KE$ for both the metals also vary linearly with frequency. As $x$ and $y$ intercepts are different then threshold frequency, work function and stopping potentials are different for both the metals.',NULL,'Medium','Graph/Figure',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/39_44399_question_1.png'),
(44400,'physics',38,1064,2537,'A parallel plate capacitor of plate separation $2\\,mm$ is connected in an electric circuit having source voltage $400\\,V$. If the plate area is $60\\,c{m^2}$, then the value of displacement current for ${10^{ - 6}}\\sec $ will be $\\left[ {In\\,{{10}^{ - 6}}\\,s} \\right.$ voltage across capacitor becomes 400 volt]','[{"text": "$1.062 \\\\times {10^{ - 4}}amp$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1.062 \\\\times {10^{ - 2}}amp$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$1.062\\\\,amp$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1.062 \\\\times {10^{ - 3}}\\\\,amp$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'$$I_{d}=\\varepsilon_{0} \\dfrac{d \\phi_{E}}{d t}=\\varepsilon_{0} \\dfrac{E \\mathrm{~A}}{t}=\\dfrac{\\varepsilon_{0} \\mathrm{~A}}{t} \\dfrac{V}{\\mathrm{~d}}$$<br>${I_d} = \\frac{{8.85 \\times {{10}^{ - 12}} \\times 400 \\times 60 \\times {{10}^{ - 4}}}}{{2 \\times {{10}^{ - 3}} \\times {{10}^{ - 6}}}}$<br>$ = 1.062 \\times {10^{ - 2}}amp$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44402,'physics',39,1104,2631,'What is the de Broglie wavelength of a nitrogen molecule in air at $300\\,K$ ? Assume that the molecule is moving with the root-mean-square speed of molecules at this temperature.(Atomic mass of nitrogen $ = 14.0076\\,u$ )','[{"text": "$0.09\\\\,nm$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$0.01\\\\,nm$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$0.2\\\\,nm$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$0.03\\\\,nm$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Given, temperature $T = 300\\,K$<br>and Boltzmann\'s constant $ = 1.38 \\times {10^{ - 23}}\\,J/k$<br>Molecular weight of nitrogen molecule<br>$m = 28.0152\\,u$<br>$ = 28.0152 \\times 1.67 \\times {10^{ - 27}}\\,kg$<br>Mean kinetic energy of molecules<br>$\\frac{1}{2}m{v^2} = \\frac{3}{2}KT$<br>or $\\quad v = \\sqrt {\\frac{{3KT}}{m}} $<br>$ = \\sqrt {\\frac{{3 \\times 1.38 \\times {{10}^{ - 23}} \\times 300}}{{28.0152 \\times 1.66 \\times {{10}^{ - 27}}}}} $<br>$ = 516.78\\,m/s$<br>de Broglie wavelength,<br>$\\lambda  = \\frac{h}{{mv}}$<br>$\\lambda  = \\frac{{6.63 \\times {{10}^{ - 34}}}}{{28.0152 \\times 1.67 \\times {{10}^{ - 27}} \\times 516.78}} = 0.028\\,nm$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,'NCERT Exempler','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44403,'physics',40,240,479,'Energy of an electron in the second orbit of hydrogen atom is ${E_2}$ . The energy of electron in the third orbit of  $H{e^ + }$ will be','[{"text": "$\\\\frac{9}{{16}}{E_2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{{16}}{9}{E_2}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{3}{{16}}{E_2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{{16}}{3}{E_2}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'For hydrogen-like atom, energy of electron at orbit with principal quantum number $n$,<br>$E = {E_0}\\frac{{{Z^2}}}{{{n^2}}}$, where ${E_0} =  - 13.6eV$<br>For second orbit of hydrogen $(Z = 1,n = 2)$<br>$E = {E_0}\\frac{{{1^2}}}{{{2^2}}} = \\frac{{{E_0}}}{4} = {E_2}({\\rm{given}})$ $\\therefore \\,{E_0} = 4{E_2}$<br>For third orbit of $H{e^ + }(Z = 2,n = 3)$<br>$E = {E_0}\\frac{{{2^2}}}{{{3^2}}} = \\frac{4}{9}{E_0} = \\frac{4}{9} \\times 4{E_2} = \\frac{{16}}{9}{E_2}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44404,'physics',37,974,2274,'A resistor and an inductor are connected to an ac supply of $120V$ and $50Hz$. The current in the circuit is $3A$. If the power consumed in the circuit is $108W$, then the resistance in the circuit is','[{"text": "$360\\\\Omega $", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$40\\\\Omega $", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$12\\\\Omega $", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\sqrt {(52 \\\\times 28)} \\\\Omega $", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'In an ac circuit, a pure inductor does not consume any power. Therefore, power is consumed by the resistor only.<br>$\\therefore P = {V_{rms}}{I_{rms}}\\cos \\phi  = I_{rms}^2Z\\left( {\\frac{R}{Z}} \\right) = I_{rms}^2R$<br> or $108 = {\\left( 3 \\right)^2}R\\,or\\,R = 12\\Omega $',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44405,'physics',38,1064,2538,'A condenser of capacity $50\\,pF$ is connected to an AC supply of $220\\,V\\,\\,50\\,Hz$. The rms value of magnetic field at a distance of $5\\,cm$ from the axis is','[{"text": "$\\\\frac{{11}}{5}\\\\pi  \\\\times {10^{ - 12}}\\\\,T$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$22\\\\pi  \\\\times {10^{ - 12}}\\\\,T$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$22\\\\pi  \\\\times {10^{ - 14}}\\\\,T$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$44\\\\pi  \\\\times {10^{ - 13}}\\\\,T$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'$$i_{r m s}=\\dfrac{V_{r m s}}{X_{C}}, B_{r m s}=\\dfrac{\\mu_{0} i_{r m s}}{2 \\pi r}$$<br>$$\\begin{aligned}& B_{r m s}=\\dfrac{\\mu_{o}}{2 \\pi r}\\left(\\dfrac{V_{r m s}}{X_{C}}\\right) \\\\& =\\dfrac{\\mu_{o} 2 \\pi v c}{2 \\pi r}=44 \\pi \\times 10^{-13} T\\end{aligned}$$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44406,'physics',39,1104,2631,'An electron (of mass $$m$$ ) with an initial velocity $v = {v_0}\\widehat i$ is moving in an electric field $E = {E_0}\\widehat j$. If ${\\lambda _0} = h/m{v_0}$, it\'s de Broglie wavelength at time $$t$$ is given by','[{"text": "$$\\\\lambda_{0} \\\\sqrt{1+\\\\dfrac{e^{2} E^{2} t^{2}}{m^{2} v_{0}^{2}}}$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$\\\\lambda_{0}$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{{{\\\\lambda _0}}}{{\\\\left( {1 + \\\\frac{{{e^2}E_0^2{t^2}}}{{{m^2}v_0^2}}} \\\\right)}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{{{\\\\lambda _0}}}{{\\\\sqrt {1 + \\\\frac{{{e^2}E_0^2{t^2}}}{{{m^2}v_0^2}}} }}$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Initial de Broglie wavelength of electron,<br>${\\lambda _0} = \\frac{h}{{m{v_0}}}$<br>Force on electron in electric field,<br>$\\bar F =  - e\\bar E =  - e{E_0}\\widehat j$<br>Acceleration of electron, $\\bar a = \\frac{{\\bar F}}{{\\,m}} =  - \\frac{{e{E_0}}}{{\\,m}}\\widehat j$<br>It is acting along negative $$Y$$-axis.<br>The initial velocity of electron along $$X$$-axis<br>${v_{{X_0}}} = {v_0}\\widehat i.{\\rm{ }}$<br>Initial velocity of electron along $$Y$$-axis,<br>${v_{{Y_0}}} = 0$.<br>Velocity of electron after time $$t$$ along $$X$$-axis,<br>${v_X} = {v_0}\\widehat i$<br>( $$\\therefore$$ There is no acceleration of electron along $$X$$-axis )<br>Velocity of electron after time $$t$$ along $$Y$$-axis,<br>${v_Y} = 0 + \\left( { - \\frac{{e{E_0}}}{m}\\widehat j} \\right)t =  - \\frac{{e{E_0}}}{m}t\\widehat j$<br>Magnitude of velocity of electron after time $$t$$ is<br>$|v| = \\sqrt {v_X^2 + v_Y^2}  = \\sqrt {v_0^2 + {{\\left( {\\frac{{ - e{E_0}}}{m}t} \\right)}^2}} $<br>$ = {v_0}\\sqrt {1 + \\frac{{{e^2}E_0^2{t^2}}}{{{m^2}v_0^2}}} $<br>de Broglie wavelength,<br>${\\lambda ^\\prime } = \\frac{h}{{mv}} = \\frac{h}{{m{v_0}\\sqrt {1 + {e^2}E_0^2{t^2}/\\left( {{m^2}v_0^2} \\right)} }}$<br>$ = \\frac{{{\\lambda _0}}}{{\\sqrt {1 + {e^2}E_0^2{t^2}/\\left( {{m^2}v_0^2} \\right)} }}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,'NCERT Exempler','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44407,'physics',37,974,2273,'A group of electric lamps having a total power rating of 1000 watt is supplied by an $AC$ voltage $E = 200\\sin \\left( {3\\,10t + 60^\\circ } \\right).$ Then the rms value of the circuit current is[ Here $i = {i_o}\\sin 310t$]','[{"text": "$20\\\\sqrt 2 A$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$10A$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$20A$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$10\\\\sqrt 2 A$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'$P = \\frac{1}{2}{V_0}{i_0}\\cos \\phi  \\Rightarrow 1000 = \\frac{1}{2} \\times 200 \\times {i_0}\\cos 60^\\circ $<br>$ \\Rightarrow {i_0} = 20A \\Rightarrow {i_{rms}} = \\frac{{{i_0}}}{{\\sqrt 2 }} = \\frac{{20}}{{\\sqrt 2 }} = 10\\sqrt 2 A.$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44408,'physics',40,240,479,'Spectrum of sunlight is an example for','[{"text": "Band emission spectrum", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Line absorption spectrum", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Continuous emission spectrum", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Continuous absorption spectrum", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'The spectrum of sunlight, when passed through a prism or diffraction grating, shows a continuous spectrum of colors with dark lines superimposed on it. These dark lines, known as absorption lines or Fraunhofer lines, are due to the absorption of specific wavelengths of light by elements present in the Sun\'s outer layers. These elements absorb light at specific wavelengths, creating dark lines in the otherwise continuous spectrum. This phenomenon is known as a line absorption spectrum.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2012,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44449,'physics',37,977,2770,'In a step down transformer, the number of turns in the primary is always','[{"text": "Greater than the number of turns in the secondary", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Less than the number of turns in the secondary", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Equal to the number of turns in the secondary", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Either greater than or less than the number of turns  in the secondary", "isCorrect": false}]',0,NULL,NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44409,'physics',38,1066,2543,'Match Column I (Electromagnetic wave type) with Column II (Its association/application).<div class="matching-question"><div class="column-left">A.  Infrared waves<br>B. Radio waves<br>C. X-rays<br>D. Ultraviolet</div><div class="column-right">P. To treat muscular strain<br>Q. For broadcasting<br>R. To detect fracture of bones<br>S. Absorbed by the ozone layer of the atmosphere</div></div>','[{"text": "${\\\\rm{A  -  S,}}\\\\,{\\\\rm{B  -  R,}}\\\\,{\\\\rm{C  -  Q,}}\\\\,{\\\\rm{D  -  P}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{A - P,}}\\\\,{\\\\rm{B - Q,}}\\\\,{\\\\rm{C - S,}}\\\\,{\\\\rm{D - R}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{A  -  R,}}\\\\,{\\\\rm{B  -  Q,}}\\\\,{\\\\rm{C  -  P,}}\\\\,{\\\\rm{D  -  S}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{A - P,}}\\\\,{\\\\rm{B - Q,}}\\\\,{\\\\rm{C - R,}}\\\\,{\\\\rm{D - S}}$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,NULL,NULL,'Medium','Match the following',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44410,'physics',39,1104,2631,'An electron is moving with an initial velocity $v = {v_0}\\widehat i$ in a magnetic field $B = {B_0}j$. Then it\'s de Broglie wavelength','[{"text": "Increases with time", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Remains constant", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Increases and decreases periodically", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Decreases with time", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Here, when a particle is moving in a magnetic field then its speed remains constant. So the magnitude of $$v$$ will not change, i.e., momentum ( $mv$ ) will remain constant in magnitude. Hence de Broglie wavelength $\\lambda  = h/mv$ remains constant.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,'NCERT Exempler','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44411,'physics',37,974,2766,'A series $LCR$ circuit is connected across an ac source $E = 10\\sin \\left[ {100\\pi t + \\frac{\\pi }{6}} \\right]$.Current from the supply is $I = 2\\sin \\left[ {100\\pi t + \\frac{\\pi }{{12}}} \\right]$.What is the average power dissipated? (in watt)','[{"text": "$5$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$8$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{{20}}{{\\\\sqrt 2 }}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$5\\\\sqrt 2 $", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Phase difference between voltage and current<br>$\\phi  = \\frac{\\pi }{{12}} - \\left( { - \\frac{\\pi }{6}} \\right) = \\frac{\\pi }{4}$<br>$ \\Rightarrow $ Power factor $ = \\cos \\phi  = \\frac{1}{{\\sqrt 2 }}$<br>Average power dissipated $ = \\frac{{{V_m}{I_m}}}{2}\\cos \\phi $<br>$ = \\frac{{10 \\times 2}}{2} \\times \\frac{1}{{\\sqrt 2 }} = 5\\sqrt 2 \\,watt$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44412,'physics',38,1065,2539,'A plane electromagnetic wave of frequency 40 $MHz$ travels in free space in the $X$-direction. At some point instant, the electric field $\\overrightarrow E $ has its maximum value of $750\\,N/C$ in $Y$-direction. The wavelength of the wave is-','[{"text": "$5.5\\\\,m$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$9.5\\\\,m$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$7.5\\\\,m$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$3.5\\\\,m$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'$\\lambda  = \\frac{c}{f} = \\frac{{3 \\times {{10}^8}}}{{4 \\times {{10}^7}}} = 7.5\\,m$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44413,'physics',40,240,479,'Pick out the incorrect statement from  the following:','[{"text": "Mercury vapour lamp produces line emission spectrum", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Oil flame produces line emission spectrum", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Band spectrum helps us to study molecular structure", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Sunlight spectrum is an example for line absorption spectrum", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Statements (1), (3), and (4) are correct while statement (2) is incorrect',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2013,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44414,'physics',39,1104,2631,'The wavelength of a photon needed to remove a proton from a nucleus which is bound to the nucleus with $1M\\,eV$ energy is nearly','[{"text": "$1.2\\\\,nm$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1.2 \\\\times {10^{ - 3}}\\\\,nm$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$1.2 \\\\times {10^{ - 6}}\\\\,nm$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1.2 \\\\times 10\\\\,nm$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Given in the equation,<br>Energy of a photon, $E = 1MeV \\Rightarrow {10^6}eV$<br>Now, $hc$ $ = 1240\\,eVnm$<br>Now, $$E=\\dfrac{h c}{\\lambda}$$<br>$ \\Rightarrow \\lambda  = \\frac{{hc}}{E} = \\frac{{1240eVnm}}{{{{10}^6}eV}} = 1.24 \\times {10^{ - 3}}\\,nm$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,'NCERT Exempler','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44415,'physics',37,974,2272,'The instantaneous value of alternating current and voltages in a circuit are given as<br>$I = \\frac{1}{{\\sqrt 2 }}\\sin (100\\pi t)amp$<br>$e = \\frac{1}{{\\sqrt 2 }}\\sin (100\\pi t + \\pi /3)volt$<br>The average power in watts consumed in the circuit is:','[{"text": "$\\\\frac{1}{4}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{{\\\\sqrt 3 }}{4}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{1}{2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{1}{8}$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Given equations are $i = \\frac{1}{{\\sqrt 2 }}\\sin (100\\pi t)$<br> and $e = \\frac{1}{{\\sqrt 2 }}\\sin (100\\pi t + \\pi /3) \\Rightarrow {I_0} =  - \\frac{1}{{\\sqrt 2 }}$<br>${P_{av}} = {V_{rms}} \\times {I_{rms}}\\cos \\phi  = \\frac{1}{2} \\times \\frac{1}{2} \\times \\cos 60^\\circ $<br>$$\\left( {\\because {I_{rms}} = \\frac{{{I_0}}}{{\\sqrt 2 }}\\,and\\,{V_{rms}} = \\frac{{{V_0}}}{{\\sqrt 2 }}} \\right)$$<br>$ = \\frac{1}{2} \\times \\frac{1}{2} \\times \\frac{1}{2} = \\frac{1}{8}W$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44416,'physics',38,1066,2543,'Which wave is not electromagnetic in nature?','[{"text": "Micro", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Radio", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "X-ray", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Sound wave", "isCorrect": true}]',3,NULL,NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44417,'physics',39,1104,2631,'A proton, a neutron, an electron and an $$\\alpha$$-particle have same energy. Then, their de Broglie wavelength compare as','[{"text": "${\\\\lambda _p} = {\\\\lambda _n} > {\\\\lambda _e} > {\\\\lambda _\\\\alpha }$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\lambda _\\\\alpha } < {\\\\lambda _p} = {\\\\lambda _n} < {\\\\lambda _e}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${\\\\lambda _e} < {\\\\lambda _p} = {\\\\lambda _n} > {\\\\lambda _\\\\alpha }$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\lambda _e} = {\\\\lambda _p} = {\\\\lambda _n} = {\\\\lambda _\\\\alpha }$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'We know that the relation between $$\\lambda$$ and $E$ is given by<br>$\\lambda  = \\frac{h}{{\\sqrt {2mE} }}$<br>Here, for the given value of energy $E,\\frac{h}{{\\sqrt {2E} }}$ is a constant.<br>Thus, $\\lambda  = \\frac{h}{{\\sqrt m }}$<br>$\\therefore {\\lambda _p}:{\\lambda _n}:{\\lambda _e}:{\\lambda _\\alpha } = \\frac{1}{{\\sqrt {{m_p}} }}:\\frac{1}{{\\sqrt {{m_n}} }}:\\frac{1}{{\\sqrt {{m_e}} }}:\\frac{1}{{\\sqrt {{m_\\alpha }} }}$<br>Since, $$m_{p}=m_{n}$$, hence $$\\lambda_{p}=\\lambda_{n}$$<br>As, ${m_\\alpha } > {m_p}$, therefore ${\\lambda _\\alpha } < {\\lambda _p}$<br>As, ${m_e} < {m_n}$, therefore ${\\lambda _e} > {\\lambda _n}$<br>Hence, $$\\lambda_{\\alpha} < \\lambda_{p}=\\lambda_{n} < \\lambda_{\\text {e }}$$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,'NCERT Exempler','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44418,'physics',37,974,2273,'An electric heater rated $225V$ and $550W$ is connected to $AC$ mains. The current drawn by it is:','[{"text": "$0.8\\\\,A$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2.0\\\\,A$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$0.4\\\\,A$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1.25\\\\,A$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'$$\\because P = VI \\Rightarrow I = \\frac{P}{V} = \\frac{{550}}{{225}} = 2.0A$$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44419,'physics',40,240,479,'The energy of a hydrogen atom in the ground state is  $ - 13.6\\,eV$. The energy of a $H{e^ + }$ ion in the first excited state will be','[{"text": "$ - 6.8\\\\,eV$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$ - 13.6\\\\,eV$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$ - 27.2\\\\,eV$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$ - 54.4\\\\,eV$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Energy $E$ of an atom with principal quantum number $n$ is given by  $E = \\frac{{ - 13.6}}{{{n^2}}}{Z^2}$<br>For first excited state $n = 2$ and for $H{e^ + },Z = 2$<br>So $E = \\frac{{ - 13.6 \\times {{\\left( 2 \\right)}^2}}}{{{{\\left( 2 \\right)}^2}}}$<br>$ =  - 13.6\\,eV$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44420,'physics',38,1064,2537,'Maxwell\'s equations describe the fundamental laws of','[{"text": "Electricity only", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Magnetism only", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Mechanics only", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "both 1 and 2", "isCorrect": true}]',3,NULL,NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44421,'physics',39,1101,2625,'Energy required by an electron for electron emission can be supplied to a free electron by<br>I. Hammering the metal surface<br>II. Heating the metal surface<br>III. Applying electric filed<br>IV. Applying magnetic field','[{"text": "Only I", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "I, II and IV", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "II, III and IV", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "II and III", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'The minimum energy required for the electron emission from the metal surface can be supplied to the free electrons by anyone of the following physical processes.<br>(i) By suitably heating, sufficient thermal energy can be imparted to the free electrons to enable them to come out of the metal.<br>(ii) By applying a very strong electric field (of the order of ${10^8}V{m^{ - 1}}$ ) to a metal, electrons can be pulled out of the metal, as in a spark plug.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ type-2',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44422,'physics',37,974,2272,'In a $AC$ circuit the voltage and current are described by $V = 100\\left( {319t - \\frac{\\pi }{6}} \\right)volts$ and $i = 200\\sin \\left( {319t + \\frac{\\pi }{6}} \\right)mA$ respectively. The average power dissipated in the circuit is:','[{"text": "$2.5\\\\,{\\\\mathop{\\\\rm watts}\\\\nolimits} $", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$5.0\\\\,{\\\\mathop{\\\\rm watts}\\\\nolimits} $", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$10.0\\\\,{\\\\mathop{\\\\rm watts}\\\\nolimits} $", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$50.0\\\\,{\\\\mathop{\\\\rm watts}\\\\nolimits} $", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'$P = \\frac{{{V_0}{i_0}\\cos \\phi }}{2} = \\frac{{100 \\times 200 \\times {{10}^{ - 3}}}}{2}\\cos \\frac{\\pi }{3} = 5W$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44423,'physics',40,239,478,'The total energy of an electron revolving in the second orbit of hydrogen atom is','[{"text": "$ - 13.6\\\\,eV$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$ - 1.51\\\\,eV$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$ - 3.4\\\\,eV$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{zero}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'The total energy of an electron revolving in the ${n^{th}}$ orbit of hydrogen atom is<br>${E_n} =  - \\frac{{13.6}}{{{n^2}}}eV$<br>For the second orbit , $n = 2$<br>$\\therefore {E_2} =  - \\frac{{13.6}}{{{2^2}}}eV =  - 3.4\\,eV$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2018,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44424,'physics',38,1065,2539,'Electromagnetic waves are transverse in nature is evident by','[{"text": "Polarisation", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Interference", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Reflection", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Diffraction", "isCorrect": false}]',0,NULL,NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44425,'physics',39,1106,2633,'Which of the following statements are true?<br>I. In the interaction with matter, radiation behaves as if it is made up of particles called photons.<br>II. Each photon has energy $$E=h v$$ and momentum $$p=h v / c$$<br>III. Photons are electrically neutral and are not deflected by electric and magnetic fields.<br>IV. In a photon-particle collision, photon number is conserved','[{"text": "I and II", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "I, II and III", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "I, III and IV", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "I, II and IV", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'(i) In interaction of radiation with matter, radiation behaves as if it is made up of particles called photons.<br>(ii) Each photon has energy $$E(=h v)$$ and momentum $$p(=h v / c)$$ where $$c$$ is the speed of light.<br>(iii) Photons are electrically neutral and are not deflected by electric and magnetic fields.<br>(iv) In photon-particle collision (such as photoelectron collision), the total energy and total momentum are conserved.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ type-2',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44426,'physics',37,975,2275,'An $L - C - R$ series circuit consists of a resistance of $10\\Omega $, a capacitor of reactance $60\\Omega $ and an inductor coil. The circuit is found to resonate when put across a $300V$, $100Hz$ supply. The inductance of coil is (take, $\\pi  = 3$):','[{"text": "$0.1\\\\,H$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$0.01\\\\,H$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$0.2H$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$0.02H$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'${\\omega _0} = 2\\pi n$<br><br>${\\omega _0} = 2 \\times 3 \\times 100 = 600rad/s$ ($$\\because $$ $\\pi  = 3$)<br>Further ${\\omega _0} = \\frac{1}{{\\sqrt {LC} }}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,(1)$<br>Also,<br>${X_C} = \\frac{1}{{{\\omega _0}C}} = 60\\Omega  \\Rightarrow C = \\frac{1}{{{\\omega _0} \\times 60}} = \\frac{1}{{600 \\times 60}}$<br>$ \\Rightarrow C = \\frac{1}{{36 \\times {{10}^3}}}F$<br>So, put values in eq. (1), we get<br>$600 = \\frac{1}{{\\sqrt {L\\left( {\\frac{1}{{36 \\times {{10}^3}}}} \\right)} }} \\Rightarrow 36 \\times {10^4} = \\frac{{36 \\times {{10}^3}}}{L}$<br>$\\therefore \\quad L = \\frac{{36 \\times {{10}^3}}}{{36 \\times {{10}^4}}} = \\frac{1}{{10}} = 0.1H$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44427,'physics',38,1065,2542,'Energy stored in electromagnetic oscillations is in the form of','[{"text": "Electrical energy", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Magnetic energy", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both 1 and 2", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Neither of the above", "isCorrect": false}]',2,NULL,NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44428,'physics',40,239,478,'An electron is moving in an orbit of a hydrogen atom from which there can be a maximum of six transitions. An electron is moving in an orbit of another hydrogen atom from which there can be a maximum of three transitions. The ratio of the velocity of the electron in these two orbits is','[{"text": "$1/2$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2/1$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$5/4$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$3/4$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Number of spectral lines obtained due to transition of electron from ${n^{th}}$ orbit to lower orbits is<br>$N = \\frac{{n\\left( {n - 1} \\right)}}{2}$<br>In first case, $N = 6$<br>$\\therefore 6 = n\\frac{{\\left( {n - 1} \\right)}}{2} \\Rightarrow n = 4$<br>In the second case,$N = 3$<br>$\\therefore 3 = \\,\\,n\\,\\,\\frac{{\\left( {n - 1} \\right)}}{2} \\Rightarrow n = 3$<br>Velocity of electron in hydrogen atom in ${n^{th}}$ orbit is<br>${v_n} = \\frac{{{e^2}}}{{2{\\varepsilon _0}nh}} \\Rightarrow {v_n} \\propto \\frac{1}{n}$<br>$\\therefore \\frac{{{v_4}}}{{{v_3}}} = \\frac{3}{4}$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',2010,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44429,'physics',39,1102,3092,'Experimental study of photoelectric effect shows that<br>I. Photocurrent $$\\propto$$ intensity of light<br>II. Saturation current $$\\propto$$ intensity of light<br>III. Photoemission occurs only at frequency greater than thresold frequency.','[{"text": "Only I is correct", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "I and II are correct", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "I and III are correct", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "I, II and III are correct", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'(I) For a given photosensitive material and frequency of incident radiation (above the threshold frequency), the photoelectric current is directly proportional to the intensity of incident light, till saturation.<br>(II) Saturation current is found to be proportional to the intensity of incident radiation whereas the stopping potential is independent of its intensity.<br>(III) A certain minimum cutt-off frequency of the incident radiation, called the threshold frequency, below which no emission of photoelectrons takes place, no matter how intense the incident light is. Above the threshold frequency, the stopping potential or equivalently the maximum kinetic energy of the emitted photoelectrons increases linearly with the frequency of the incident radiation, but is independent of its intensity.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ type-2',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44430,'physics',37,976,2280,'The frequency of the output signal becomes $x$ times by doubling of the value of the capacitance in the $LC$ oscillator circuit. The value of $x$ is','[{"text": "$\\\\frac{1}{{\\\\sqrt 2 }}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$2$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\sqrt 2 $", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{1}{2}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'$f = \\frac{1}{{2\\pi }}\\sqrt {\\frac{1}{{LC}}} $<br>$f\' = \\frac{1}{{2\\pi }}\\sqrt {\\frac{1}{{2(LC)}}}  = \\frac{f}{{\\sqrt 2 }}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44431,'physics',38,1066,2543,'Select wrong statement from the followingElectromagnetic waves','[{"text": "Are transverse", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Travel with same speed in all media", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Travel with the speed of light", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Are produced by accelerating charge", "isCorrect": false}]',1,NULL,NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44432,'physics',39,1101,2625,'<div class="matching-question"><div class="column-left">The photoelectrons produced by a monochromatic light beam incident on a metal surface have a spread in their kinetic energies.<br></div><div class="column-right">The work function of the metal is its characteristics property.</div></div>','[{"text": "Statement A is correct but Statement B is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Statement A is incorrect but Statement B is correct.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both statements are correct.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both statements are incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'The kinetic energy of emitted photoelectrons varies from zero to a maximum value. Work function depends on nature of metal.<br>Option (3) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ type-2',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44433,'physics',40,240,479,'An electron of a stationary hydrogen atom passes from the fifth energy level to the ground level. The velocity that the atom acquired as a result of photon emission will be [$m = $ mass of atom]','[{"text": "$\\\\frac{{25\\\\,hR}}{{24\\\\,m}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{{24\\\\,hR}}{{25\\\\,m}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{{24\\\\,m}}{{25\\\\,hR}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{{25\\\\,m}}{{24\\\\,hR}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'The wavelength of emitted photon is<br>$\\frac{1}{\\lambda } = R\\left[ {\\frac{1}{{n_1^2}} - \\frac{1}{{n_2^2}}} \\right]$<br>$\\frac{1}{\\lambda } = R\\left[ {\\frac{1}{{{1^2}}} - \\frac{1}{{{5^2}}}} \\right] \\Rightarrow \\frac{{24}}{{25}}R = \\frac{1}{\\lambda }$<br>As,$p = \\frac{h}{\\lambda }$<br>$v = \\frac{h}{{m\\lambda }} = \\frac{{24}}{{25}}\\frac{{Rh}}{m}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44434,'physics',37,976,2280,'A $16\\,\\mu F$  capacitor is charged  to a 20 volt potential. The battery is then disconnected  and pure 40  $mH$ coil is connected across the capacitor  so that $LC$ oscillations are setup. The maximum  current in the  coil is','[{"text": "$40\\\\,mA$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2A$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$0.2A$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$0.4A$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'The charge on the capacitor as a function of time is $q = {q_0}\\cos \\omega t$<br>$i = \\frac{{dq}}{{dt}} =  - {q_0}\\omega .\\sin \\omega t$<br>${i_{\\max }} = C\\omega V = V\\sqrt {\\frac{C}{L}}  = 20\\sqrt {\\frac{{16 \\times {{10}^{ - 6}}}}{{40 \\times {{10}^{ - 3}}}}}  = 0.4A$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44435,'physics',38,1066,2543,'Which of the following rays is emmitted by a human body?','[{"text": "Visible light", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$UV$ rays", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$X$-rays", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$IR$ rays", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Temperature of the body is $37^\\circ C$ and it emits heat radiation which falls in $IR$ and microwave region.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44436,'physics',39,1102,2626,'<div class="matching-question"><div class="column-left">Photosensitivity of a metal is high if its work function is small<br></div><div class="column-right">Work function is $$h v_{0}$$ where $$v_{0}$$ is the threshold frequency.</div></div>','[{"text": "Statement A is correct but Statement B is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Statement A is incorrect but Statement B is correct.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both statements are correct.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both statements are incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Less work function means less energy is required for ejecting out the electrons.<br>Option (3) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ type-2',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44437,'physics',37,976,2280,'In an $L$-$C$ circuit, the capacitor has maximum charge ${q_0}$. The value of ${\\left. {\\frac{{di}}{{dt}}} \\right|_{\\max }}$ is:','[{"text": "$\\\\frac{{{q_0}}}{{LC}} - 1$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{{{q_0}}}{{\\\\sqrt {LC} }}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{{{q_0}}}{{LC}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$None\\\\,\\\\,of\\\\,\\\\,these$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'$q = {q_o}\\cos \\omega t$<br>$i = {q_o}\\omega \\sin \\omega t$<br>${i_{\\max }} = {q_o}\\omega $<br>${i_{\\max }} = \\frac{{{q_o}}}{{\\sqrt {LC} }}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44438,'physics',40,239,478,'What is the energy of the electron revolving in third orbit expressed in $eV$?','[{"text": "$ - \\\\,4.53\\\\,eV$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$ - \\\\,1.51\\\\,eV$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$ - \\\\,4\\\\,eV$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$ - \\\\,3.4\\\\,eV$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Energy of the electron in  ${n^{th}}$ orbit is<br>${E_n} =  - \\frac{{13.6}}{{{n^2}}}eV$<br>For third orbit, $n = 3$<br>$\\therefore \\,\\,{E_3} =  - \\frac{{13.6}}{{{3^2}}}eV =  - 1.51\\,eV$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2014,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44450,'physics',38,1065,2542,'The intensity of solar radiation at the earth\'s surface is $1KW{m^{ - 2}}$. The power entering the pupil of an eye of diameter $0.5\\,cm$ is','[{"text": "$9.8\\\\,mW$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$19.6\\\\,mW$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$4.9\\\\,mW$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$39.2\\\\,mW$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'$P = IA = I\\pi {R^2} = 19.6\\,\\,mW$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44439,'physics',38,1066,2543,'If  ${\\lambda _v},{\\lambda _x}$ and ${\\lambda _m}$ represent the wavelengths of visible light, $x$ - rays and microwaves respectively, then :','[{"text": "$$\\\\lambda_{v}>\\\\lambda_{m}>\\\\lambda_{x}$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\lambda _v} > {\\\\lambda _x} > {\\\\lambda _m}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\lambda _m} > {\\\\lambda _v} > {\\\\lambda _x}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${\\\\lambda _m} > {\\\\lambda _x} > {\\\\lambda _v}$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Wavelength order of given rays are listed below:<br>$\\begin{array}{*{20}{c}}\n{rays}&{wavelengths(\\mathop A\\limits^o )}\\\\\n{{\\rm{Visible}}\\,{\\rm{light}}}&{{\\rm{4000 - 7900}}}\\\\\n{{\\rm{X - rays}}}&{{\\rm{1 - 100}}}\\\\\n{{\\rm{Microwaves}}}&{{\\rm{of}}\\,{\\rm{the}}\\,{\\rm{order}}\\,{\\rm{of}}\\,{\\rm{cm}}}\\\\\n{{\\rm{Obviously,}}}&{{\\lambda _m} > {\\lambda _v} > {\\lambda _x}}\n\\end{array}$<br>${\\rm{Note}}$ : Visible light, $X$-rays and microwaves are all electromagnetic waves.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44440,'physics',39,1102,2629,'<div class="matching-question"><div class="column-left">The kinetic energy of phototelectrons emitted from metal surface does not depend on the intensity of incident photon<br></div><div class="column-right">The ejection of electrons from metallic surface is not possible with frequency of incident photons below the threshold frequency.</div></div>','[{"text": "Statement A is correct but Statement B is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Statement A is incorrect but Statement B is correct.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both statements are correct.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Statements are incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Option (3) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ type-2',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44441,'physics',37,976,2280,'In an oscillations $LC$ circuit, $L = 3.00\\,mH$ and $C = 2.70\\mu F$. At $t = 0$ the charge on the capacitor is zero and the current is 2.00 $A$. The maximum charge that will appear on the capacitor will be','[{"text": "$1.8 \\\\times {10^{ - 5}}C$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$18 \\\\times {10^{ - 5}}C$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$9 \\\\times {10^{ - 5}}C$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$90 \\\\times {10^{ - 5}}C$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Equating maximum energy stored in the in inductor and the capacitor, <br>$\\frac{1}{2}LI_0^2 = \\frac{{Q_0^2}}{{2C}} \\Rightarrow {Q_0} = {I_0}\\sqrt {LC} $<br>$ = 2 \\times \\sqrt {3 \\times {{10}^{ - 3}} \\times 2.7 \\times {{10}^{ - 6}}}  = 18 \\times {10^{ - 5}}C$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44442,'physics',38,1065,2542,'During the propagation of electromagnetic waves in a medium:','[{"text": "Electric energy density is equal to the magnetic energy density.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both electric and magnetic energy densities are zero.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Electric energy density is double of the magnetic energy density.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Electric energy density is half of the magnetic energy density.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,NULL,NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44443,'physics',40,240,479,'Continuous emission spectrum is produced by','[{"text": "Incandescent electric lamp", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Mercury vapour lamp", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Sodium vapour lamp", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "The sun", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Incandescent electric lamp gives  continuous emission spectrum. Mercury and sodium vapour lamp give line  emission spectrum. The Fraunhofer lines seen in the sun’s spectrum constitute an absorption spectrum.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44444,'physics',39,1102,2626,'<div class="matching-question"><div class="column-left">In photon-particle collision the total energy and total momentum are conserved.<br></div><div class="column-right">The number of photons are conserved in a collision.</div></div>','[{"text": "Statement A is correct but Statement B is incorrect.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Statement A is incorrect but Statement B is correct.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both statements are correct.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Statements are incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'In a photon-particle collision such as photon electron collision, the total energy and total momentum are conserved. However the number of photons may not be conserved in a collision. The photon may be absorbed or a new photon may be created. So option (1) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ type-2',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44445,'physics',37,977,2770,'For an ideal transformer ratio of output to the input power is always','[{"text": "Greater than one", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Equal to one", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Less than one", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Zero", "isCorrect": false}]',1,NULL,NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44446,'physics',38,1065,2542,'The amplitude of electric field in a parallel light beam of intensity $4W{m^{ - 2}}$ is','[{"text": "$49.5\\\\,N{C^{ - 1}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$35.5\\\\,N{C^{ - 1}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$45.5\\\\,N{C^{ - 1}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$55.5\\\\,N{C^{ - 1}}$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'$$\\mathrm{I}=\\dfrac{1}{2} \\varepsilon_{0} \\mathrm{E}_{0}^{2} \\mathrm{c}$$ or $$\\mathrm{E}_{0}=\\sqrt{\\dfrac{2 I}{\\varepsilon_{0} \\mathrm{c}}}$$<br>$ = \\sqrt {\\frac{{2 \\times 4}}{{8.85 \\times {{10}^{ - 12}} \\times 3 \\times {{10}^8}}}}  = 55.5\\,N{C^{ - 1}}$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44447,'physics',40,241,480,'Using Bohr’s quantisation condition, what is the rotational energy in the second orbit for a 	diatomic molecule? ( $I = $ moment of inertia of diatomic molecule, $h = $ Planck’s constant)','[{"text": "$\\\\frac{h}{{2I{\\\\pi ^2}}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{{{h^2}}}{{2I{\\\\pi ^2}}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{{{h^2}}}{{2{I^2}{\\\\pi ^2}}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{h}{{2{I^2}\\\\pi }}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'The angular momentum of diatomic molecule is <br>$L = I\\omega  = \\frac{{nh}}{{2\\pi }}$<br>For Second orbit, $L = \\frac{{2h}}{{2\\pi }} = \\frac{h}{\\pi }$<br>Rotational energy <br>$ = \\frac{1}{2}I{\\omega ^2} = \\frac{{{{\\left( {I\\omega } \\right)}^2}}}{{2I}} = \\frac{{{L^2}}}{{2I}} = {\\left( {\\frac{h}{\\pi }} \\right)^2} \\times \\frac{1}{{2I}} = \\frac{{{h^2}}}{{2I{\\pi ^2}}}$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',2020,'MHTCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44448,'physics',39,1102,2629,'<div class="matching-question"><div class="column-left">If the frequency of the incident light on a metal surface is doubled, the kinetic energy of emitted electrons is more than doubled.<br></div><div class="column-right">The metal will provide additional energy to the emitted photoelectron for light of higher frequency than that for lower frequency.</div></div>','[{"text": "Statement A is correct but Statement B is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Statement A is incorrect but Statement B is correct.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both statements are correct.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Statements are incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Option (3) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ type-2',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44451,'physics',39,1102,2626,'<div class="matching-question"><div class="column-left">Photoelectric effect supports the quantum nature of light.<br></div><div class="column-right">Photoelectric emission is instantaneous.</div></div>','[{"text": "Statement A is correct but Statement B is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Statement A is incorrect but Statement B is correct.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both statements are correct.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Statements are incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Photoelectric effect is observed only when the frequency of the incident radiation is more than a characteristic value depending upon the metal. Small amount of energy given slowly over a long time does not liberate photoelectrons. Thus photoelectric emission is an instantaneous process. So option (3) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ type-2',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44452,'physics',37,977,2770,'A step up transformer is used to','[{"text": "Increase the current and increase the voltage", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Decrease the current and increase the voltage", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Increase the current and decrease the voltage", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Decrease the current and decrease the voltage", "isCorrect": false}]',1,NULL,NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44453,'physics',40,239,478,'When electron jumps from $n = 4$ level to $n = 1$ level, the angular momentum of electron changes by','[{"text": "$\\\\frac{h}{{2\\\\pi }}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{{2h}}{{2\\\\pi }}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{{3h}}{{2\\\\pi }}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{{4h}}{{2\\\\pi }}$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Angular momentum of electron in an orbit,<br>$L = \\frac{{nh}}{{2\\pi }}$<br>For $n = 4,{L_4} = \\frac{{4h}}{{2\\pi }}$<br>For $n = 1,\\,{L_1} = \\frac{h}{{2\\pi }}$<br>Change in angular momentum $ = {L_4} - {L_1}$<br>$ = \\frac{{4h}}{{2\\pi }} - \\frac{h}{{2\\pi }} = \\frac{{3h}}{{2\\pi }}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2016,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44454,'physics',38,1066,2543,'An electromagnetic radiation has an energy $14.4KeV$. To which region of electromagnetic spectrum does it belong?','[{"text": "Visible region", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "X-rays region", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Infra-red region", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$\\\\gamma$$ - rays region", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'$\\lambda  = \\frac{{hc}}{E} = 2.8 \\times {10^{ - 11}}\\,m$<br>This wavelength corresponds to $$\\gamma$$ - rays.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44455,'physics',39,1102,2626,'<div class="matching-question"><div class="column-left">Though light of a single frequency (monochromatic) is incident on a metal, the energies of emmited photoelectrons are different.<br></div><div class="column-right">The energy of electrons emitted from inside the metal surface is lost in collision with the other atoms in the metal.</div></div>','[{"text": "Statement A is correct but Statement B is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Statement A is incorrect but Statement B is correct.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both statements are correct.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Statements are incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'When a light of single frequency falls on the electron of inner layer of metal, the electron comes out of the metal surface after a large number of collisions with atoms of its upper layer. Energy of the photoelectrons will be lost during collisions. Photoelectrons will have different energies. So option (3) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ type-2',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44456,'physics',37,977,2771,'To reduce the iron losses in a transformer, the core must be made of a material having','[{"text": "High retentivity, high coercivity", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Low retentivity, low coercivity", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "High coercivity, low retentivity", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "High retentivity, low coercivity", "isCorrect": false}]',1,NULL,NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44457,'physics',40,240,479,'Ratio of longest wavelengths corresponding to Lyman and Balmer series in hydrogen spectrum is','[{"text": "$\\\\frac{9}{{31}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{7}{{29}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{5}{{27}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{3}{{23}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Wavelength for Lyman series<br>${\\lambda _L} = \\frac{1}{{R\\left( {1 - \\frac{1}{4}} \\right)}} = \\frac{4}{{3R}}$<br>Wavelength for Balmer series<br>${\\lambda _B} = \\frac{1}{{R\\left( {\\frac{1}{4} - \\frac{1}{9}} \\right)}} = \\frac{1}{{R\\left( {\\frac{5}{{36}}} \\right)}} = \\frac{{36}}{{5R}}$<br>$\\therefore \\quad \\frac{{{\\lambda _L}}}{{{\\lambda _B}}} = \\frac{4}{{3R}} \\times \\frac{{5R}}{{36}} = \\frac{5}{{27}}$<br>$ \\Rightarrow \\,{\\lambda _L}:{\\lambda _B} = 5:27$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44458,'physics',38,1065,2542,'A radio station on the surface of the earth radiates $50\\,kW$. If transmitter radiates equally in all directions above the surface of the earth find the amplitude of electric field detected $100\\,km$ away. [In the downward direction assume no radiation]','[{"text": "$2.45 \\\\times {10^{ - 1}}\\\\,V{m^{ - 1}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2.45 \\\\times {10^{ - 3}}\\\\,V{m^{ - 1}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2.45 \\\\times {10^{ - 2}}\\\\,V{m^{ - 1}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$2.45\\\\,V{m^{ - 1}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'$$I=\\dfrac{P}{2 \\pi r^{2}}$$<br>$I = \\frac{{{\\varepsilon _0}E_0^2c}}{2} = \\frac{P}{{2\\pi {r^2}}}$<br>${E_0} = \\sqrt {\\frac{P}{{{\\varepsilon _0}c\\pi {r^2}}}}  = \\sqrt {\\frac{{50 \\times {{10}^3}}}{{8.85 \\times {{10}^{ - 12}} \\times 3 \\times {{10}^8} \\times {{10}^{10}} \\times 3.14}}} $<br>$ = 2.45 \\times {10^{ - 2}}\\,V{m^{ - 1}}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44459,'physics',37,977,2770,'Transformer ratio of a step-up transformer is','[{"text": "greater than one", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "less than one", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "equal to 1", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "zero", "isCorrect": false}]',0,NULL,NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44460,'physics',39,1102,2626,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Work function of copper is greater than the work function of sodium, but both have same value of threshold frequency and threshold wavelength.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> The frequency is inversely proportional to wavelength.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but reason is correct.", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'When work function of copper is greater than the work function of sodium, then<br>${\\phi _{Cu}} > {\\phi _{Na}}$<br>${\\left( {h{v_0}} \\right)_{Cu}} > {\\left( {h{v_0}} \\right)_{Na}}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,(1)$<br>${\\left( {\\frac{{hc}}{{{\\lambda _0}}}} \\right)_{Cu}} > {\\left( {\\frac{{hc}}{{{\\lambda _0}}}} \\right)_{Na}}$<br>${\\left( {{\\lambda _0}} \\right)_{Na}} > {\\left( {{\\lambda _0}} \\right)_{Cu}}\\,\\& \\,{\\left( {{v_0}} \\right)_{Cu}} > {\\left( {{v_0}} \\right)_{Na}}$<br>So option (4) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44461,'physics',38,1065,2542,'Predict the correct relation between average energy density in electric field ${\\mu _E}$ magnetic field $$u_{B}$$','[{"text": "$$u_{E}=u_{B}$$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$$u_{E}=\\\\dfrac{1}{2} u_{B}$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$\\\\dfrac{1}{2} u_{E}=u_{B}$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$u_{E}=4 u_{B}$$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Average energy density of $$E$$<br>$$u_{E}=\\dfrac{1}{4} \\varepsilon_{0} E_{0}^{2}$$<br>Average energy density of $B$<br>$$\\begin{gathered}u_{B}=\\dfrac{1}{4} \\dfrac{B_{0}^{2}}{\\mu_{0}} \\\\\\because \\mathrm{c}=\\dfrac{\\mathrm{E}_{0}}{\\mathrm{~B}_{0}} \\mathrm{So}, \\\\u_{E}=\\dfrac{1}{4} \\varepsilon_{0}\\left(c B_{0}\\right)^{2}=\\dfrac{1}{4} \\varepsilon_{0} \\dfrac{1}{\\left(\\mu_{0} \\varepsilon_{0}\\right)} \\times B_{0}^{2}=\\dfrac{1}{4} \\dfrac{\\mathrm{B}_{0}^{2}}{\\mu_{0}} \\\\u_{E}=u_{B}\\end{gathered}$$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44462,'physics',40,239,478,'In hydrogen atom, electron excites from ground state to higher energy state and its orbital velocity is reduced to ${\\left( {\\frac{1}{3}} \\right)^{rd}}$ of  its initial value. The radius of the orbit in the ground state is $R$. The radius of the orbit in that higher state is ......','[{"text": "$2R$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$3R$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$27R$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$9R$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'As per problem,<br>$\\frac{{{v_h}}}{{{v_g}}} = \\frac{1}{3}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,(1)$<br>Where subscripts $h$ and $g$ denotes higher energy state and ground state.<br>Orbital velocity of electron in the ${n^{th}}$ orbit is<br>${v_n} = \\frac{{{e^2}}}{{2{\\varepsilon _0}nh}}$ or ${v_n} \\propto \\frac{1}{n}$<br>For ground state,  $n = 1$<br>$\\therefore \\frac{{{v_h}}}{{{v_g}}} = \\frac{1}{n}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,(2)$<br>Equating (1) and (2), we get $n = 3$ (higher energy state)<br>Radius of ${n^{th}}$ orbit is<br>${r_n} = \\frac{{{n^2}{h^2}{\\varepsilon _0}}}{{\\pi {e^2}m}}$ or ${r_n} \\propto {n^2}$<br>$\\therefore \\frac{{{r_3}}}{{{r_1}}} = \\frac{{{{\\left( 3 \\right)}^2}}}{{{{\\left( 1 \\right)}^2}}} = 9$<br>${r_3} = 9{r_1} = 9R$                  $(\\because \\,\\,{\\text{given}}\\,\\,{{\\text{r}}_1} = {\\text{R}})$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44463,'physics',37,977,2281,'Quantity  that remains unchanged in a transformer is','[{"text": "current", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "voltage", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "frequency", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "None of these", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Frequency remains unchanged in a transformer.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44464,'physics',39,1104,2631,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> The de Broglie wavelength of a molecule varies inversely as the square root of temperature.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> The root mean square velocity of the molecule depends on the temperature.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'de Broglie wavelength associated with gas molecule varies as<br>$$\\lambda \\propto \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{T}}$$<br>Also, root mean square velocity of gas molecule is,<br>${v_{{\\rm{rms}}}} = \\sqrt {\\frac{{3RT}}{M}} $<br>Momentum $$p=\\dfrac{h}{\\lambda}=m v_{r m s}$$.<br>So option (2) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44465,'physics',38,1065,2540,'The magnetic field in travelling EM wave has a peak value of $20nT$. The peak value of electric field strength is','[{"text": "$6\\\\,V/m$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$3\\\\,V/m$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$9\\\\,V/m$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$12\\\\,V/m$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'${E_0} = {B_0}c = 20 \\times {10^{ - 9}} \\times 3 \\times {10^8} = 6\\,\\,V/m$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44466,'physics',37,977,2770,'The magnitude of the $e$.$m$.$f$. across the secondary coil of a transformer does not depend on','[{"text": "The number of the turns in the primary", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "The number of the turns in the secondary", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "The magnitude of the e.m.f applied across the primary", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "The resistance of the primary and the secondary", "isCorrect": true}]',3,NULL,NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44467,'physics',40,239,478,'The force acting on the electrons in hydrogen atom (Bohr’s theory) is related to the principle quantum number $n$ as','[{"text": "${n^{ - 4}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${n^4}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${n^{ - 2}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${n^2}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'The electron moves around the nucleus due to centripetal force provided by the electrostatic force, which is given by<br>${F_e} = \\frac{{Z{e^2}}}{{4\\pi {\\varepsilon _0}r_n^2}} \\Rightarrow {F_e} \\propto \\frac{1}{{r_n^2}}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,(1)$<br>Also, radius of orbit,<br>${r_n} = \\left( {\\frac{{{h^2}{\\varepsilon _0}}}{{\\pi m{e^2}}}} \\right)\\frac{{{n^2}}}{Z}$<br>$ \\Rightarrow {r_n} \\propto {n^2}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,(2)$<br>From eq.(1) and eq.(2), we get<br>${F_e} \\propto \\frac{1}{{{{\\left( {{n^2}} \\right)}^2}}} \\Rightarrow {F_e} \\propto {n^{ - 4}}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2020,'MHTCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44468,'physics',39,1102,2628,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Stopping potential is a measure of $KE$ of photoelectron.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> $$W=e V_{s}=\\dfrac{1}{2} m v^{2}=K E$$','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'It is a fact that, greater is $KE$ of photoelectron, greater is the potential required to stop it. Hence, stopping potential is a measure of $KE$ of photoelectron. It can be understood from the relation, $e{V_s} = KE$. So option (1) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44469,'physics',38,1065,2541,'Find the radiation pressure of solar radiation on the surface of earth. Solar constant is $1.4\\,kW{m^{ - 2}}$ (Assume complete absorption)','[{"text": "$9.4 \\\\times {10^{ - 6}}\\\\,Pa$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$4.7 \\\\times {10^{ - 6}}\\\\,Pa$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$4.7 \\\\times {10^{ - 5}}\\\\,Pa.$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2.37 \\\\times {10^{ - 6}}\\\\,Pa$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'${P_{{\\rm{rad}}}} = \\frac{I}{c} = \\frac{{1.4 \\times {{10}^3}}}{{3 \\times {{10}^8}}} = 4.7 \\times {10^{ - 6}}\\,Pa$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44470,'physics',37,977,2770,'The turns ratio of a transformer is given as 2 : 3. If the current through the primary coil is 3 $A$, thus calculate the current through load resistance.','[{"text": "$1\\\\,A$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$4.5\\\\,A$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2\\\\,A$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$1.5\\\\,A$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Given $\\frac{{{N_p}}}{{{N_s}}} = \\frac{2}{3},{I_p} = 3A \\Rightarrow {I_s} = \\frac{{{N_p}}}{{{N_s}}}{I_p} = \\frac{2}{3} \\times 3 = 2A$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44471,'physics',39,1102,2626,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> The threshold frequency of photoelectric effect supports the particle nature of sunlight.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> If frequency of incident light is less than the threshold frequency, electrons are not emitted from metal surface.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Option (2) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44472,'physics',40,241,480,'An electron in an excited state of $L{i^{2 + }}$ ion has angular momentum $\\frac{{3h}}{{2\\pi }}$. The de Broglie wavelength of electron in this state is $P\\pi {a_0}$ (where ${a_0}$ Bohr radius  ). The value of $P$ is','[{"text": "$3$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$1$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$4$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Angular momentum $L = 3\\frac{h}{{2\\pi }}({\\rm{given}})$<br>$\\therefore \\quad n = 3$<br>de Broglie wavelength for $n = 3$,<br>$\\lambda  = \\frac{{2\\pi r}}{n} = \\frac{{2\\pi r}}{3}$<br>${\\rm{As}}\\,\\,Z = 3\\,{\\rm{for}}\\,L{i^{ +  + }}$<br>$r = {a_0}\\frac{{{n^2}}}{Z} = {a_0}\\frac{{{3^2}}}{3} = 3{a_0}$<br>Hence $\\lambda  = \\frac{{2\\pi  \\times 3{a_0}}}{3} = 2\\pi {a_0}$<br>$\\therefore \\quad P = 2$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',2021,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44473,'physics',38,1065,2541,'An earth-orbiting satellite has solar energy collecting panel with total area $5\\,{m^2}$. If solar radiations are perpendicular and completely absorbed find the average force associated with the radiation pressure. &nbsp;$\\left( {{\\rm{Solar}}\\,{\\rm{constant}} = 1.4 \\times {{10}^3}\\frac{W}{{{m^2}}}} \\right)$','[{"text": "$2.33 \\\\times {10^{ - 5}}\\\\,N$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$2.33 \\\\times {10^3}\\\\,N$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2.33 \\\\times {10^{ - 6}}\\\\,N$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2.33 \\\\times {10^{ - 4}}\\\\,N$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Power $=I$ Area $ = 1.4 \\times {10^3} \\times 5\\,\\,W$<br>$F = \\frac{{{\\rm{Power}}}}{{\\rm{c}}} = \\frac{{1.4 \\times {{10}^3} \\times 5}}{{3 \\times {{10}^8}}} = 2.33 \\times {10^{ - 5}}\\,N$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44474,'physics',37,977,2771,'A current of 5 $A$ is flowing at 220 $V$ in the primary coil of a transformer. If the voltage produced in the secondary coil is 2200 $V$ and 50% of power is lost, then the current is secondary will be:','[{"text": "$2.5\\\\,A$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$5\\\\,A$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$0.25\\\\,A$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$0.5\\\\,A$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Given,${V_p} = 220V,{V_s} = 2200V,{I_p} = 5A$ and power loss $ = 50\\% $<br>i.e.,$\\eta \\%  = \\frac{{{P_{out}}}}{{{P_{in}}}} \\times 100 = \\frac{{{V_s}{I_s}}}{{{V_p}{I_p}}} \\times 100$<br>$50 = \\frac{{2200 \\times {I_s}}}{{220 \\times 5}} \\times 100 \\Rightarrow {I_s} = 0.25A$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44475,'physics',39,1101,2625,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Cathode rays produce fluorescence in glass and colour of glow depends on nature of glass.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Cathode rays excite glass electrons and they on de-excitation emits radiation in visible region.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Cathode ray particles when strike the electrons of glass atom, the electrons of glass atom are excited and move to higher energy levels. On de-excitation, they fall to their ground state and release energy. As energy levels are characteristics of glass, glow depends on glass. So option (1) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44476,'physics',40,239,478,'The magnetic moment of electron due to orbital motion is proportional to ( $n = $ principal quantum numbers)','[{"text": "$\\\\frac{1}{{{n^2}}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{1}{n}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${n^2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$n$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Magnetic moment is given as,<br>Magnetic moment $\\left( {{M_0}} \\right) = \\frac{e}{{2{m_e}}} \\times L\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,(1)$<br>where, $e = $ electronic charge<br> ${m_e} = $ mass of electron<br>and $L = $ orbital angular momentum<br>As, we know $L = \\frac{{nh}}{{2\\pi }}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,(2)$<br>Where, $n = $ prinicpal quantum number<br>and $h = $ planck’s constant<br>$ \\Rightarrow L \\propto n$<br>Therefore, from relation (1) and (2), we get<br>${M_0} \\propto n$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2017,'MHTCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44477,'physics',38,1065,2542,'Consider the following two statements regarding a linearly polarised plane electromagnetic wave.<br>(i) Electric field and the magnetic field have equal average values.<br>(ii) Electric energy and the magnetic energy have equal average values.','[{"text": "(ii) is true", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both are false", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both are true", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "(i) is true", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Electric and magnetic field energies have equal average values.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44478,'physics',37,977,2770,'The number of turns in primary and secondary coils of a transformer are 5 and 10 and mutual inductance of transformer is 25 $H$, Now, if the number of turns in primary and secondary are 10 and 5, the new mutual inductance will be:','[{"text": "$6.25\\\\,H$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$12.5\\\\,H$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$25\\\\,H$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$50\\\\,H$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Mutual inductance of transformer<br>$M\\alpha {N_1}{N_2} \\Rightarrow \\frac{{{M_1}}}{{{M_2}}} = \\frac{{\\left( {{N_1}{N_2}} \\right)I}}{{\\left( {{N_1}{N_2}} \\right)II}}$<br>$\\frac{{25}}{{{M_2}}} = \\frac{{5 \\times 10}}{{10 \\times 5}} \\Rightarrow {M_2} = 25H$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44479,'physics',39,1102,2627,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Photoelectric current depends on the intensity of incident light.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Number of photoelectrons emitted per second is directly proportional to the intensity of incident radiation.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'The number of photoelectrons emitted per second is directly proportional to the intensity of incident radiation. So option (1) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44481,'physics',37,972,2634,'If ${i_1} = 3\\sin \\omega t$ and ${i_2} = 4\\cos \\omega t,$ then ${i_3}$ is','[{"text": "$5\\\\sin (\\\\omega t + 53^\\\\circ )$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$5\\\\sin (\\\\omega t + 45^\\\\circ )$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$ - 5\\\\sin (\\\\omega t + 53^\\\\circ )$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$5\\\\sin (\\\\omega t + 37^\\\\circ )$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'${i_3} = {i_1} + {i_2} = 3\\sin \\omega t + 4\\cos \\omega t$<br>$ = 3\\sin \\omega t + 4\\sin (\\omega t + {90^o})$<br>${i_{{3_{Max}}}} = \\sqrt {{3^2} + {4^2}}  = 5$<br>$\\tan \\phi  = \\frac{4}{3} \\Rightarrow \\phi  = {53^o}$<br>So ${i_3} = {i_{{3_{Max}}}}\\sin (\\omega t + \\phi ) = 5\\sin (\\omega t + {53^o})$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/37_44481_question_1.png'),
(44482,'physics',40,239,478,'The period of revolution of an elctron revolving in ${n^{th}}$ orbit of $H$ - atoms is proportional to','[{"text": "${n^3}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "independent of $n$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${n^2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1/n$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Frequency of electron in ${n^{th}}$ orbit<br>$f = \\frac{{4{\\pi ^2}{Z^2}{e^4}m{K^2}}}{{{n^3}{h^3}}} \\Rightarrow f \\propto \\frac{1}{{{n^3}}}$<br>$\\therefore \\,\\,{\\rm{Time}}\\,{\\rm{period}}\\,T = \\frac{1}{f}\\,i.e.,\\,T \\propto {n^3}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2020,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44483,'physics',39,1104,2631,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> A wave is a moving, dynamic disturbance of matter or energy in an organised and periodic way.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Light is defined as the electro magnetic wave within the section of the electromagnetic spectrum.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but Reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Option (2) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44484,'physics',38,1065,2539,'A standing em wave frequency $2.2 \\times {10^{10}}\\,Hz$ is produced in a certain material and two adjacent nodal planes of magnetic field are $3.5\\,mm$ apart. Find speed of wave in this material.','[{"text": "$1.79 \\\\times {10^8}\\\\,m{s^{ - 1}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$3.08 \\\\times {10^8}\\\\,m{s^{ - 1}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2.81 \\\\times {10^8}\\\\,m{s^{ - 1}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1.54 \\\\times {10^8}\\\\,m{s^{ - 1}}$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'$\\frac{\\lambda }{2} = 3.5\\,mm$<br>$\\lambda  = 7.0\\,mm$<br>$c = f\\lambda  = 2.2 \\times {10^{10}} \\times 0.7 \\times {10^{ - 2}}$<br>$ = 1.54 \\times {10^8}\\,m{s^{ - 1}}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44485,'physics',37,972,2261,'If reading of an ammeter is 20$A$, then the peak value of current is:','[{"text": "$\\\\frac{{20}}{{\\\\sqrt 2 }}A$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{5}{{\\\\sqrt 2 }}A$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$20\\\\sqrt 2 A$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$20\\\\sqrt 2 $", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Ammeter reads the root mean square value of current $({I_{rms}})$ is related to the peak value of current $({I_0})$ by the relation<br>${I_{rms}} = \\frac{{{I_0}}}{{\\sqrt 2 }} \\Rightarrow {I_0} = \\sqrt 2  \\times {I_{rms}} = 20\\sqrt 2 A$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44486,'physics',39,1105,2632,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> In Davisson-Germer experiment wavelength associated with the beam decreases with increase in of speed of electrons.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Wavelength associated with electron beam in Davisson-Germer experiment is given by $$\\lambda=\\dfrac{h}{\\sqrt{2 m k}}$$.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'In Davisson and Germer experminant, de-Broglie wavelength associated with electron beam is<br>$$\\lambda=\\dfrac{h}{\\sqrt{2 m k}}$$<br>where, $K$ is $KE$ and $$m$$ is mass of electron. Option (1) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44487,'physics',40,239,478,'Frequency of revolution of an electron revolving in ${n^{th}}$ orbit of $H$ - atom is proportional to','[{"text": "$n$  independent of $n$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1/{n^2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1/{n^3}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$n$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'$T = \\frac{{2\\pi r}}{v} \\Rightarrow \\frac{1}{f} = \\frac{{2\\pi r}}{v}$ or $f = \\frac{v}{{2\\pi r}}$<br>where, $f = $ frequency of revolution of electron.<br>Hence for $H - $ like atom $v \\propto \\frac{1}{n}$ and $r \\propto {n^2}$<br>$ \\Rightarrow f \\propto \\frac{1}{{{n^2} \\times n}} \\Rightarrow f \\propto \\frac{1}{{{n^3}}}$<br>$\\therefore \\lambda  = \\frac{{2 \\times \\pi  \\times 0.53}}{1}\\mathop A\\limits^0  \\approx 3.33\\mathop A\\limits^0 $',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2019,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44488,'physics',38,1065,2539,'An electromagnetic wave going through vacuum is described by $$E=E_{0} \\sin (k x-\\omega t)$$;<br>$$B=B_{0} \\sin (k x-\\omega t)$$. Which of the following is true?','[{"text": "$$E_{0} k=B_{0} \\\\omega$$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$$E_{0} \\\\omega=B_{0} k$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$E_{0} k=2 B_{0} \\\\omega$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$E_{0} B_{0}=\\\\omega k$$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'As, $$\\dfrac{E_{0}}{B_{0}}=c$$ also $$k=\\dfrac{2 \\pi}{\\lambda}$$<br>Their relation gives, $$E_{0} k=B_{0} \\omega$$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44489,'physics',37,973,2263,'A coil of inductance $L$ has an inductive reactance of ${X_L}$ in an $AC$ circuit in which the  effective current is $I$. The coil is made from a super-conducting material and has no resistance. The rate at which power is dissipated in the coil is','[{"text": "$I{X_L}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$0$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$IX_L^2$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${I^2}{X_L}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'For a purely inductive circuit $P = 0$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44490,'physics',39,1102,2626,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> In photoelectric effect, cathode or emitter plate is usually coated with barium oxide.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Coating prevents cathode from erosion.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Sensitivity of a photoelectric materials greatly depends on its surface characteristics. When emitter plate is coated with a materials of low work function, photoemission occurs even at low frequency. So option (3) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44492,'physics',37,974,2767,'A coil has an inductance of $0.7H$ and is joined in series with a resistance of $220\\Omega $. When an alternating emf of $220V$ at $50cps$ is applied to it, then the wattless component of the current in the circuit is','[{"text": "$7\\\\,A$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$0.7\\\\,A$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$5\\\\,A$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$0.5\\\\,A$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'The phasor diagram is<br>${X_L} = \\omega L = 2\\pi  \\times 50 \\times 0.7 = 220\\Omega $<br>$Z = \\sqrt {X_L^2 + {R^2}} $<br>or $Z = \\sqrt {{{220}^2} + {{220}^2}}  = 220\\sqrt 2 \\Omega $<br>${i_{rms}} = \\frac{{220}}{{220\\sqrt 2 }} = \\frac{1}{{\\sqrt 2 }}A$<br>Wattless current $ = {i_{rms}}\\sin \\phi  = \\frac{1}{{\\sqrt 2 }} \\times \\frac{1}{{\\sqrt 2 }} = \\frac{1}{2}A$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44493,'physics',38,1065,2539,'A radio wave of frequency $90\\,MHz(FM)$ enters a ferrite rod. If $$\\varepsilon_{\\mathrm{r}}=10^{3}$$ and $$\\mu_{\\mathrm{r}}=10$$ then the velocity and wavelength of radio wave in ferrite are','[{"text": "$3 \\\\times {10^6}\\\\,\\\\,m{s^{ - 1}},3.33 \\\\times {10^{ - 2}}\\\\,\\\\,m$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$3 \\\\times {10^6}\\\\,m{s^{ - 1}},3.33 \\\\times {10^{ - 3}}\\\\,m$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$3 \\\\times {10^6}\\\\,m{s^{ - 1}},3.33 \\\\times {10^{ - 1}}\\\\,m$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "None of these", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'${v_{{\\rm{ferrite }}}} = \\frac{c}{{\\sqrt {{\\varepsilon _r}{\\mu _r}} }} = \\frac{{3 \\times {{10}^8}}}{{\\sqrt {{{10}^3} \\times 10} }} = 3 \\times {10^6}\\,m{s^1}$<br>${\\lambda _{{\\rm{ferrite }}}} = \\frac{{{v_{{\\rm{ferrite }}}}}}{f} = \\frac{{3 \\times {{10}^6}}}{{90 \\times {{10}^6}}} = 3.33 \\times {10^{ - 2}}\\,m$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44494,'physics',39,1101,2625,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> In Millikan experiment for the determination of charge on an electron, oil drop of any size can be used.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Millikan\'s experiment determines the charge of electron.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but reason is correct.", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Millikan experiment is performed with small oil drops. It is used to measure charge of electron. Option (4) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44495,'physics',37,973,2269,'An inductor $L$ and a capacitor $C$ are connected in the circuit as shown in the figure. The frequency of the power supply is equal to the resonant frequency of the circuit. Which ammeter will read zero ampere?','[{"text": "${A_3}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${A_1}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${A_2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "None of these", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'The capacitor and inductor are in parallel.<br>At resonance  ${i_C} = {i_L}$ and they have a phase difference of  $\\pi .$ The net current through the circuit is zero',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/37_44495_question_1.png'),
(44496,'physics',38,1064,2538,'An $AC$ peak voltage of $2\\,V$ having a frequency of  $50\\,KHz$  is applied to a condenser of a capacity of $10\\,\\mu F$. The maximum value of the magnetic field between the plates of the condenser if the radius of plate is $10\\,cm$ is','[{"text": "$$4 \\\\pi \\\\mu T$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$40 \\\\pi \\\\mu T$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$2 \\\\mu T$$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$$0.4 p \\\\mu$$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'$$B=\\dfrac{\\mu_{o}}{2 \\pi R} i=\\dfrac{\\mu_{o}}{2 \\pi R} \\dfrac{d}{d t}\\left[q_{o} \\sin \\omega t\\right]$$<br>$$B=\\dfrac{\\mu_{o}}{2 \\pi R} q_{o} \\omega \\cos \\omega t$$<br>$$\\begin{aligned}& B=\\dfrac{\\mu_{o}}{2 \\pi R} q_{o} \\omega \\quad\\left(q_{o}=C V_{o}\\right) \\\\& B_{\\max }=2 \\mu T\\end{aligned}$$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44497,'physics',40,239,478,'Angular momentum of an electron in hydrogen atom is $3h/2\\pi $ ( $h$ is the planck’s constant). The $K$.$E$. of the electron is','[{"text": "$3.4\\\\,eV$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$6.8\\\\,eV$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$4.35\\\\,eV$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1.51\\\\,eV$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Given, angular momentum,<br>$L = \\frac{{nh}}{{2\\pi }} = \\frac{{3h}}{{2\\pi }}$<br>$\\therefore n = 3$<br>Total energy of electron in ${n^{th}}$ orbit, is<br>${E_T} =  - \\frac{{13.6}}{{{n^2}}}eV$<br>$ \\Rightarrow {E_T} =  - \\frac{{13.6}}{{{3^2}}}\\,\\,eV =  - 1.51\\,\\,eV(\\because n = 3)$<br>Now, kinetic energy of electron in orbit is<br>${E_K} = \\left| {{E_T}} \\right|$<br>$\\therefore {E_k} = 1.51\\,eV$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2020,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44498,'physics',39,1104,2631,'An electron of mass $$m$$ and $a$ photon have same energy $E$. The ratio of de Broglie wavelengths associated with them is :','[{"text": "$$\\\\dfrac{1}{c}\\\\left(\\\\dfrac{E}{2 m}\\\\right)^{\\\\frac{1}{2}}$$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${\\\\left( {\\\\frac{E}{{2m}}} \\\\right)^{ - \\\\frac{1}{2}}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$\\\\left(\\\\dfrac{E}{2 m}\\\\right)^{\\\\frac{1}{2}}$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$\\\\dfrac{1}{x c}\\\\left(\\\\dfrac{2 m}{E}\\\\right)^{\\\\frac{1}{2}}$$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'For electron $$\\lambda_{e}=\\dfrac{h}{\\sqrt{2 m E}}$$<br>for Photon $$E=P c \\Rightarrow \\lambda_{P h}=\\dfrac{h c}{E}$$<br>$$\\Rightarrow \\dfrac{\\lambda_{e}}{\\lambda_{P h}}=\\dfrac{h}{\\sqrt{2 m E}} \\times \\dfrac{E}{h c}=\\left(\\dfrac{E}{2 m}\\right)^{1 / 2} \\dfrac{1}{c}$$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2016,'NEET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44499,'physics',37,973,2269,'In the circuit shown in fig. If both the bulbs ${B_1}$ and ${B_2}$ are identical,','[{"text": "${B_1}$  will be brighter than ${B_2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Their brightness will be the same", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${B_2}$ will be brighter than ${B_1}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Only ${B_2}$ will glow because the capacitor has infinite impedance", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'${X_C} = \\frac{1}{{\\omega C}} = \\frac{{{{10}^6}}}{{100\\pi  \\times 100}} = \\frac{{20}}{\\pi }\\Omega $<br>${X_L} = \\omega L = 100\\pi  \\times {10^{ - 2}} = \\pi \\Omega $<br>${X_C} > {X_L} \\Rightarrow {X_{RC}} > {X_{LC}}$<br>${i_2} > {i_1}$ so bulb ${B_2}$ glows brighter then ${B_1}$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/37_44499_question_1.png'),
(44500,'physics',38,1064,2538,'A condenser has two conducting plates of radius $10\\,cm$ separated by a distance of $5\\,mm$. It is charged with a constant current of $0.15\\,A$. The magnetic field at a point $2\\,cm$  from the axis in the gap is','[{"text": "$3 \\\\times {10^{ - 8}}\\\\,T$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$3 \\\\times {10^{ - 6}}\\\\,T$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$6 \\\\times {10^{ - 8}}\\\\,T$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$1.5 \\\\times {10^{ - 6}}\\\\,T$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'$B = \\frac{{{\\mu _0}i{r^2}}}{{2\\pi {R^2}}} = 6 \\times {10^{ - 8}}\\,T$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(47136,'biology',102,772,1677,'Select the false statement from the following.','[{"text": "Big bang theory is used to explain the end of the universe.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Expansion of the universe after the explosion lead to a decrease in temperature.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Hydrogen and Helium formed sometime later.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Gases condensed under gravitation and formed galaxies.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Big bang theory is used to explain the beginning the universe.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44501,'physics',40,242,481,'The de - Broglie wavelength associated with electron of hydrogen atom in the ground state is','[{"text": "$6.26\\\\mathop A\\\\limits^ \\\\circ  $", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$10\\\\mathop A\\\\limits^ \\\\circ  $", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$0.3\\\\mathop A\\\\limits^ \\\\circ  $", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$3.3\\\\mathop A\\\\limits^ \\\\circ  $", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'According to Bohr’s postulate<br>$2\\pi r = n\\lambda $<br>$\\therefore $ The de-Broglie wavelength<br>$\\lambda  = \\frac{{2\\pi r}}{n} = 2\\pi r$ $\\,(\\because \\,n = 1\\,\\,\\,at\\,\\,ground\\,\\,state)$<br>$ \\Rightarrow \\lambda  = 2 \\times 3.14 \\times 0.53\\mathop A\\limits^0  = 3.33\\mathop A\\limits^0 $',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2020,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44502,'physics',39,1102,2629,'When a metallic surface is illuminated with radiation of wavelength $$\\lambda$$, the stopping potential is $V$. If the same surface is illuminated with radiation of wavelength $$2 \\lambda$$, the stopping potential is $\\frac{V}{4}$. The threshold wavelength for the metallic surface is:-','[{"text": "$$4 \\\\lambda$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$5 \\\\lambda$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$\\\\dfrac{5}{2} \\\\lambda$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$3 \\\\lambda$$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Given that<br>$eV = \\frac{{hc}}{\\lambda } - \\frac{{hc}}{{{\\lambda _0}}}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,(1)$<br>$eV/4 = \\frac{{hc}}{{2\\lambda }} - \\frac{{hc}}{{{\\lambda _0}}}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,(2)$<br>From equation (1) and (2)<br>$$\\begin{aligned}& \\lambda_{0}=3 \\lambda \\\\& \\left(h=M L^{2} T^{-1}, G=M^{-1} L^{3} T^{-2}\\right)\\end{aligned}$$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2016,'NEET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44503,'physics',37,975,2276,'In the figure shown, three $AC$ voltmeters are connected. At resonance,','[{"text": "${V_3} = 0$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${V_1} = {V_2} \\\\ne 0$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${V_2} = 0$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${V_1} = 0$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'At resonance, ${V_L}$ and ${V_C}$ are equal in magnitude but have phase difference of  relative to each other. <br>$\\therefore {V_{LC}} = {V_L} - {V_C} = 0$<br>Hence, voltmeter  ${V_2}$ read 0 volt.<br>So correct option is (4).',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/37_44503_question_1.png'),
(44504,'physics',38,1064,2537,'A parallel-plate capacitor with plate area $A$ and separation between the plates $d$, is charged by a constant current $i$. Consider a plane surface of area $A/2$ parallel to the plates and drawn symmetrically between the plates. Find the displacement current through this area.','[{"text": "$$\\\\dfrac{i}{2}$$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$$\\\\dfrac{i}{3}$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$i$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$\\\\dfrac{2 i}{3}$$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Suppose the charge on the capacitor at time $$t$$ is $$Q$$. The electric field between the plates of the capacitor is $$E=\\dfrac{Q}{\\varepsilon_{0} A}$$.<br>The flux through the area considered is<br>$$\\Phi_{E}=\\dfrac{Q}{\\varepsilon_{0} A} \\cdot \\dfrac{A}{2}=\\dfrac{Q}{2 \\varepsilon_{0}}$$<br>The displacement current is<br>$$i_{d}=\\varepsilon_{0} \\dfrac{d \\Phi_{E}}{d t}=\\varepsilon_{0}\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2 \\varepsilon_{0}}\\right) \\dfrac{d Q}{d t}=\\dfrac{i}{2}$$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44505,'physics',40,238,477,'In a Rutherford scattering experiment when a projectile of charge  ${Z_1}$ and mass ${M_1}$ approaches a target nucleus of charge  ${Z_2}$ and mass ${M_2},$ the distance of closest approach is ${r_0}.$The energy of the projectile is','[{"text": "Directly proportional to ${Z_1}{Z_2}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Directly proportional to ${M_1} \\\\times {M_2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Directly proportional to mass ${M_1}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Inversely proportional to ${Z_1}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'A particle of mass  ${M_1}$ and charge  ${Z_1}$ possess initial velocity  $u,$ when it is at a large distance from the nucleus of an atom having atomic number ${Z_2}$. At the distance of closest approach, the kinetic energy of particle is completely converted to potential energy, Mathematically,$\\frac{1}{2}{M_1}{u^2} = \\frac{1}{{4\\pi {\\varepsilon _0}}}\\frac{{{Z_1}{Z_2}}}{{{r_0}}}$<br>So, the energy of the particle is directly proportional to ${Z_1}{Z_2}.$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44506,'physics',37,975,2276,'An ideal resistance $R$, ideal inductance $L$, ideal capacitance $C$ and $AC$ voltmeters ${V_1},{V_2},{V_3}$ and ${V_4}$ are connected to an $AC$ source as shown. At resonance','[{"text": "Reading in ${V_3} = $ reading in ${V_1}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Reading in  ${V_1} = $ reading in ${V_2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Reading in  ${V_2} = $ reading in ${V_4}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "None of these", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'At resonance,<br>Voltage across $L$ = Voltage across $C$<br>Reading in ${V_2} = $ Reading in ${V_3}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/37_44506_question_1.png'),
(44507,'physics',39,1102,2629,'Photons with energy $5\\,eV$ are incident on a cathode $C$ in a photoelectric cell. The maximum energy of emitted photoelectrons is $2\\,eV$. When photons of energy $6\\,eV$ are incident on $C$, no photoelectrons will reach the anode $A$, the stopping potential of $A$ relative to $C$ is','[{"text": "$ + 3\\\\,V$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$ + 4\\\\,V$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$ - 1\\\\,V$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$ - 3\\\\,V$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'In first case ${E_p} = 5\\,eV$<br>$K.{E_{{\\rm{max}}}} = 2\\,eV$<br>$\\therefore \\phi  = 3\\,eV$<br>In second case $K.{E_{\\max }} = 6 - 3 = 3\\,eV$<br>$$\\therefore$$ Stopping voltage $ =  - 3\\,V$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2016,'NEET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44508,'physics',38,1064,2537,'The area of each plate of a parallel plate condenser is $144\\,c{m^2}$. The electrical field in the gap between the plates changes at the rate of  ${10^{12}}V{m^{ - 1}}\\,{s^{ - 1}}$. The displacement current is','[{"text": "$$\\\\dfrac{1}{10 \\\\pi A}$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$\\\\dfrac{0.4}{\\\\pi} A$$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$$\\\\dfrac{4}{\\\\pi} A$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$\\\\dfrac{40}{\\\\pi} A$$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'$$I_{d}=\\varepsilon_{0} A \\dfrac{d E}{d t}=\\dfrac{0.4}{\\pi} A$$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44509,'physics',37,975,2276,'Which voltmeter will give zero reading at resonance?','[{"text": "${V_2}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${V_3}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${V_1}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{None}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'At resonance, the voltage across $L$ and $C$ are equal but opposite in phase. The reading of ${V_2}$ is zero',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/37_44509_question_1.png'),
(44510,'physics',39,1104,2631,'The de Broglie wavelength of a neutron in thermal equilibrium with heavy water at a temperature $\\,T$ (Kelvin) and mass $\\,m$, is :-','[{"text": "$$\\\\lambda=\\\\dfrac{h}{\\\\sqrt{3 m K T}}$$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$$\\\\lambda=\\\\dfrac{h}{\\\\sqrt{m K T}}$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$\\\\lambda=\\\\dfrac{h}{\\\\sqrt{2 m K T}}$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$\\\\lambda=\\\\dfrac{h}{\\\\sqrt{3 m T}}$$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Kinetic energy of thermal neutron with equilibrium is $\\frac{3}{2}KT$<br>$\\lambda  = \\frac{h}{{mv}} = \\frac{h}{{\\sqrt {2\\,m\\,K \\cdot E} }} = \\frac{h}{{\\sqrt {2\\,m\\left( {\\frac{3}{2}KT} \\right)} }} = \\frac{h}{{\\sqrt {3mkT} }}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2017,'NEET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44511,'physics',40,239,478,'When an electron in hydrogen atom revolves in stationary orbit, it','[{"text": "Does not radiate light through its velocity changes", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Does not radiate light and velocity remains unchanged", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Radiates light but its velocity is unchanged", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Radiates light with the change of energy", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'According to Bohr’s quantisation principle, if an electron revolves in a stationary orbit which has fixed energy, will not radiate light. Since,the direction of velocity changes, so we can say its velocity changes',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2016,'MHTCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44512,'physics',38,1064,2537,'A condenser having circular plates having radius $2\\,cm$ and separated by a distance of $3\\,mm$. It is charged with a current of $0.1\\,A$. The rate at which the potential difference between the plates change is','[{"text": "$1.8 \\\\times {10^{10}}\\\\,V/s$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2.7 \\\\times {10^6}\\\\,V/s$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$9 \\\\times {10^{10}}\\\\,V/s$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2.7 \\\\times {10^{10}}\\\\,V/s$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'$\\frac{{dV}}{{dt}} = \\frac{{d{I_d}}}{{{\\varepsilon _0}A}} = 2.7 \\times {10^{10}}\\,V/S$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44513,'physics',37,974,2273,'A 100 $W$/200 $V$ bulb and an inductor are connected in series to a $220V/50Hz$ supply. Find the power consumed by the bulb. $(L = 1H)$','[{"text": "$74\\\\,W$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$84\\\\,W$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$100\\\\,W$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$92W$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Given $R = \\frac{{{{(200)}^2}}}{{100}} = 400\\Omega $<br>$P = {I^2}R = {\\left( {\\frac{V}{{|Z|}}} \\right)^2}R = {\\left( {\\frac{{220}}{{\\sqrt {{{(400)}^2} + {{(100\\pi )}^2}} }}} \\right)^2} \\times 400$<br>$ = 74.46W$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44514,'physics',39,1102,2629,'The photoelectric threshold wavelength of silver is $3250 \\times {10^{ - 10}}\\,m$. The velocity of the electron ejected from a silver surface by ultraviolet light of wavelength $2536 \\times {10^{ - 10}}\\,m$ is :-<br>(Given $h = 4.14 \\times {10^{ - 15}}eVs$ and $c = 3 \\times {10^8}\\,m{s^{ - 1}}$ )','[{"text": "$ \\\\approx 6 \\\\times {10^6}\\\\,m{s^{ - 1}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$ \\\\approx 61 \\\\times {10^3}\\\\,m{s^{ - 1}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$ \\\\approx 0.3 \\\\times {10^6}\\\\,m{s^{ - 1}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$ \\\\approx 6 \\\\times {10^5}\\\\,m{s^{ - 1}}$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'${\\lambda _0} = 3250\\mathop A\\limits^o ,\\lambda  = 2536\\mathop A\\limits^o $<br>$\\frac{1}{2}m{v^2} = hc\\left[ {\\frac{1}{\\lambda } - \\frac{1}{{{\\lambda _0}}}} \\right]$<br>$ \\Rightarrow v = \\sqrt {\\frac{{2hc}}{m}\\left[ {\\frac{1}{\\lambda } - \\frac{1}{{{\\lambda _0}}}} \\right]} $<br>$ = 0.6 \\times {10^6}\\,m/s = 6 \\times {10^5}\\,m/s$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2017,'NEET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44515,'physics',38,1064,2537,'Displacement current is continuous','[{"text": "When electric field is changing in the circuit", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "When magnetic field is changing in the circuit", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "In both types of fields.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Through wire and resistance only", "isCorrect": false}]',0,NULL,NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44516,'physics',40,238,477,'The scientist who is credited with the discovery of ‘nucleus’ in an atom is','[{"text": "Niels Bohr", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Rutherford", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "J.J.Thomson", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Balmer", "isCorrect": false}]',1,NULL,NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44517,'physics',37,974,2273,'In an  $LR$- circuit,  the inductive reactance is equal to to the resistance $R$ of the circuit. An e.m.f. $E = {E_0}\\cos \\left( {\\omega t} \\right)$  applied to the circuit. The power consumed in the circuit is','[{"text": "$\\\\frac{{E_0^2}}{{2R}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{{E_0^2}}{R}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{{E_0^2}}{{8R}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{{E_0^2}}{{4R}}$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'$P = {E_{rms}}{i_{rms}}\\cos \\phi  = \\frac{{{E_0}}}{{\\sqrt 2 }} \\times \\frac{{{i_0}}}{{\\sqrt 2 }} \\times \\frac{R}{Z}$<br>$ \\Rightarrow \\frac{{{E_0}}}{{\\sqrt 2 }} \\times \\frac{{{E_0}}}{{Z\\sqrt 2 }} \\times \\frac{R}{Z} \\Rightarrow P = \\frac{{E_0^2R}}{{2{Z^2}}}$<br>Given ${X_L} = R$ so,$Z = \\sqrt 2 R \\Rightarrow P = \\frac{{E_0^2}}{{4R}}$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44518,'physics',39,1102,2629,'When the light of frequency $2 {\\nu_0}$ (where ${\\nu_0}$ is threshold frequency), is incident on a metal plate, the maximum velocity of electrons emitted is <i>v</i>${_1}$. When the frequency of the incident radiation is increased to $5{\\nu_0}$ the maximum velocity of electrons emitted from the same plate is <i>v</i>${_2}$. The ratio of <i>v</i>${_1}$ to <i>v</i>${_2}$ is','[{"text": "$$4: 1$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$2: 1$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$1: 4$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$1: 2$$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'${E_1} = hv - \\varphi  = h\\left( {2{\\upsilon _0}} \\right) - \\upsilon {h_0} = \\upsilon {h_0}$<br>${E_2} = 5h{\\upsilon _0} - {h_0}\\upsilon  = 4h{\\upsilon _0}$<br>$\\frac{{{E_1}}}{{{E_2}}} = \\frac{{v_1^2}}{{v_2^2}} = \\frac{1}{4}$<br>$\\frac{{{v_1}}}{{{v_2}}} = \\frac{1}{2}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2018,'NEET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44519,'physics',38,1066,2543,'The nature of electromagnetic wave is','[{"text": "Longitudinal", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Longitudinal stationary", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Transverse", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Transverse stationary", "isCorrect": false}]',2,NULL,NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44520,'physics',40,238,477,'A beam of fast moving alpha particles were directed towards a thin film of gold. The parts 	$A,B$ and $C$ of  the transmitted and reflected beams corresponding to the incident parts $A,B$ and $C$ of the beam are shownin the adjoining diagram. The number of alpha particles in','[{"text": "$A\'$ will be minimum and in $B\'$  maximum", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$C\'$ will be minimum and in $B\'$  maximum", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$B\'$ will be minimum and in $C\'$  maximum", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$A\'$ will be maximum and in  $B\'$ minimum", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Most of the $\\alpha  - $ particle will pass through the gold film undeflcted and only very small number of $\\alpha  - $ particles are reflected by $180^\\circ $. Hence, the number of  $\\alpha $ particles must be maximum in transmitted beam $A\'$ and minimum in reflected beam $B\'$.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2020,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/40_44520_question_1.png'),
(44521,'physics',37,974,2272,'The potential difference across an instrument in an $AC$ circuit of frequency $f$ is $V$ and the current flowing through it is $i$ such that $V = 5\\cos (2\\pi ft)$ Volt and $i = 2\\sin (2\\pi ft)amp.$ The power disspated in the instrument is','[{"text": "$10\\\\,W$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$5\\\\,W$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2.5\\\\,W$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{zero}}$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'As $V = 5\\cos (2\\pi ft) = 5\\sin (2\\pi ft + \\pi /2)$ and $i = 2\\sin (2\\pi ft)$<br>$\\therefore $ Phase difference between $V$ and $i$ is $\\theta  = \\frac{\\pi }{2}$ <br>Average power,  $P = {V_{rms}}{i_{rms}} \\times \\cos \\theta  = 0$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44522,'physics',39,1104,2631,'An electron of mass $$m$$ with an initial velocity $\\overrightarrow v  = {v_0}\\hat i\\left( {{v_0} > 0} \\right)$ enters an electric field $\\vec E =  - {E_0}\\hat i\\left( {{E_0} = } \\right.$ constant $$\\left.>0\\right)$$ at $$\\mathrm{t}=0$$. If $$\\lambda_{0}$$ is its de-Broglie wavelength initially, then its de-Broglie wavelength at time $t$ is','[{"text": "$$\\\\lambda_{0} t$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$\\\\lambda_{0}$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\lambda _0}\\\\left( {1 + \\\\frac{{e{E_0}}}{{m{v_0}}}t} \\\\right)$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{{{\\\\lambda _0}}}{{\\\\left( {1 + \\\\frac{{e{E_0}}}{{m{v_0}}}t} \\\\right)}}$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'$$\\vec{F}=q \\vec{E}=e E_{0} \\hat{i}$$<br>$\\vec a = \\frac{{\\vec F}}{m} = \\frac{{e{E_0}}}{m}\\hat i$<br>$v = {v_0} + at$<br>$v = {v_0} + \\frac{{e{E_0}}}{m}t$<br>$P = m{v_0} + e{E_0}t$<br>$\\lambda  = \\frac{h}{P} = \\frac{h}{{m{v_0} + e{E_0}t}} = \\frac{{\\frac{h}{{m{v_0}}}}}{{1 + \\frac{{e{E_0}t}}{{m{v_0}}}}} = \\frac{{{\\lambda _0}}}{{1 + \\frac{{e{E_0}}}{{m{v_0}}}t}}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2018,'NEET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44523,'physics',38,1066,2543,'The velocity of all radio waves in free space is $3 \\times {10^8}\\,m/s$. The frequency of a radio wave of wavelength $150\\,m$ is :','[{"text": "$1MHz$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$20\\\\,kHz$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2\\\\,MHz$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$2\\\\,kHz$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Velocity of electromagnetic waves in free space and wavelength is<br>$v = 3 \\times {10^8}\\,m/s\\lambda  = 150\\,m$<br>Using the relation for the frequency of radiowaves is given by<br>$ = \\frac{v}{\\lambda } = \\frac{{3 \\times {{10}^8}}}{{150}} = 2 \\times {10^6}\\,Hz = 2MHz$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44524,'physics',37,973,2268,'Consider the circuit shown in figure given below and match the columns.<div class="matching-question"><div class="column-left">A. For $\\omega  = 5,000\\,rad/\\sec $<br>B. For $\\omega  = 2,500\\,rad/\\sec $<br>C. For $\\omega  = 10,000\\,rad/\\sec $</div><div class="column-right">P. Current leads the voltage<br>Q. The current and voltage in circuit are in same phase<br>R. Voltage leads the current.</div></div>','[{"text": "A - Q,\\tB - P,\\tC - R", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "A - P,\\tB - Q,\\tC - P", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "A - P,     B - R, \\t  C - Q", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "A - R,     B - P,\\t  C - Q", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'For $\\omega  = 5000\\,rad/\\sec $<br>${X_L} = \\omega L = 5000 \\times 4 \\times {10^{ - 3}} = 20\\Omega $<br>${X_C} = \\frac{1}{{\\omega C}} = \\frac{1}{{5000 \\times 10 \\times {{10}^{ - 2}}}} = 20\\Omega $<br>${X_L} = {X_C}$ so the circuit is in resonance and phase difference between current and voltage is zero.<br>For $\\omega  = 2500\\,rad/\\sec $<br>${X_L} = \\omega L = 2500 \\times 4 \\times {10^{ - 3}} = 10\\Omega $<br>${X_C} = \\frac{1}{{\\omega C}} = \\frac{1}{{2500 \\times 10 \\times {{10}^{ - 6}}}} = 40\\Omega $<br>${X_C} > {X_L}$ current leads the voltage <br>For $\\omega  = 10000\\,rad/\\sec $<br>${X_L} = {10^4} \\times 4 \\times {10^{ - 3}} = 40\\Omega $<br>${X_C} = \\frac{1}{{{{10}^4} \\times 10 \\times {{10}^{ - 6}}}} = 10\\Omega $<br>${X_L} > {X_C}$ voltage leads the current. So option (1) is correct.',NULL,'Hard','Match the following',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/37_44524_question_1.png'),
(44525,'physics',39,1104,2631,'An electron is accelerated through a potential difference of 10,000 $V$. Its de Broglie wavelength is, (nearly) : $\\left( {{m_e} = 9 \\times {{10}^{ - 31}}\\,kg} \\right)$','[{"text": "$12.2 \\\\times {10^{ - 13}}\\\\,m$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$12.2 \\\\times {10^{ - 12}}\\\\,m$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$12.2 \\\\times {10^{ - 14}}\\\\,m$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$12.2\\\\,nm$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'For an electron accelerated through a potential $V$<br>$\\lambda  = \\frac{{12.27}}{{\\sqrt V }}\\mathop A\\limits^o  = \\frac{{12.27 \\times {{10}^{ - 10}}}}{{\\sqrt {10000} }} = 12.27 \\times {10^{ - 12}}\\,m$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2019,'NEET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44526,'physics',40,240,479,'The ionisation energy of the electron in the hydrogen atom in its ground state is $13.6\\,eV$. The atoms are excited to higher energy levels to emit radiations of 6 wavelengths. Maximum wavelength of emitted radiations corresponds to the transistion between','[{"text": "$n = 3\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\rm{to}}\\\\,\\\\,n = 1$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$n = 3\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\rm{to}}\\\\,\\\\,n = 2$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$n = 4\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\rm{to}}\\\\,\\\\,n = 3$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$n = 2\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\rm{to}}\\\\,\\\\,n = 1$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Number of spectral lines  $N = \\frac{{n\\left( {n - 1} \\right)}}{2} = 6$<br>${n^2} - n - 12 = 0 \\Rightarrow n = 4$<br>For maximum wavelength the electron should undergo transition from ${4^{th}}$ orbit to ${3^{rd}}$ orbit',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44527,'physics',38,1066,2543,'The electromagnetic waves do not transport','[{"text": "Energy", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Charge", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Momentum", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Information", "isCorrect": false}]',1,NULL,NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44528,'physics',37,977,2770,'The turns ratio of a transformer is given as 8 : 3. If the current through the primary coil is 3 $A$, then calculate the current through load resistance.','[{"text": "$2.5\\\\,A$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1.125\\\\,A$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$2A$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1.5A$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'$\\frac{{{N_s}}}{{{N_p}}} = \\frac{{{i_p}}}{{{i_s}}}\\,or\\,\\frac{8}{3} = \\frac{3}{{{i_s}}} \\Rightarrow {i_s} = 1.125A$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44529,'physics',39,1102,2629,'Light of frequency 1.5 times the threshold frequecncy is incident on a photosensitive material. What will be the photoelectric current if the frequency is halved and intensity is doulbled?','[{"text": "four times", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "one-forth", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "zero", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "doubled", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Given that $$\\phi_{0}=h v_{0}$$<br>$$\\begin{aligned}& K_{1}=1.5 h v_{0}-\\phi_{0}=0.5 h v_{0} \\\\& K_{2}=\\dfrac{1.5}{2} h v_{0}-h v_{0}=-0.25 h v_{0}\\end{aligned}$$<br>Kinetic energy can never be negative<br>So, no emission and $i = 0$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2020,'NEET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44530,'physics',38,1066,2543,'Which of the following statements is not correct?','[{"text": "Photographic plates are sensitive to infrared rays.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Photographic plates are sensitive to ultraviolet rays.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Infra-red rays are invisible but can cast shadows like visible light.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Infra-red photons have more energy than photons of visible light.", "isCorrect": true}]',3,NULL,NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44540,'physics',39,1103,2630,'The number of photons per second on an average emitted by the source of monochromatic light of wavelength $600\\,nm$, when it delivers the power of $$3.3 \\times 10^{-3}$$ $watt$ be $h = \\left( {6.6 \\times {{10}^{ - 34}}Js} \\right)$','[{"text": "$$10^{17}$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$10^{16}$$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$$10^{15}$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$10^{18}$$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'${\\rm{power}}\\,(P) = n\\left( {\\frac{{hc}}{\\lambda }} \\right)$<br>$$n=\\dfrac{p \\lambda}{h c}=\\dfrac{3.3 \\times 10^{-3} \\times 6 \\times 10^{-7}}{6.6 \\times 10^{-34} \\times 3 \\times 10^{8}}=10^{16}$$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2021,'NEET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44531,'physics',40,245,487,'If the electron in hydrogen atom jumps from second Bohr orbit to ground state and difference between energies of the two states is radiated in the form of photons. If the work function of the material is 4.2$eV$, then stopping potential is<br>[energy of electron in ${n^{th}}$ orbit $ =  - \\frac{{13.6}}{{{n^2}}}eV$]','[{"text": "$2\\\\,V$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$4\\\\,V$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$6V$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$8V$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'The energy difference between two states is given as<br>$\\Delta E = {E_2} - {E_1} = \\frac{{ - 13.6}}{{{2^2}}} - \\left( {\\frac{{ - 13.6}}{{{1^2}}}} \\right)$<br>$ \\Rightarrow \\Delta E = \\frac{{13.6}}{{{1^2}}} - \\frac{{13.6}}{{{2^2}}}$<br>$ \\Rightarrow \\Delta E = 13.6\\left[ {\\frac{{4 - 1}}{4}} \\right] = 13.6 \\times \\frac{3}{4}$<br>$\\therefore \\Delta E = 10.2eV$<br>since, the energy is radiated in form of photons, so we have<br>energy of photons $ = hv = 10.2\\,eV$<br>From Einstein’s photoelectric equation, we have<br>$hv = {\\phi _0} + e{V_s}$<br>Substituting the given values,<br>$\\therefore 10.2eV = 4.2eV + e{V_s}$<br>$ \\Rightarrow 6eV = e{V_s}$<br>${V_s} = 6V$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44532,'physics',37,977,2770,'The primary and secondary coils of a transformer have 50 and 1500 turns, respectively. If the magnetic flux $\\phi $ linked with the primary coil is given by $\\phi  = {\\phi _0} + 4t$, where $\\phi $ is in weber, $t$ is time in second and $\\phi $ is a constant, then the output voltage across the secondary coil is','[{"text": "$90\\\\,V$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$120\\\\,V$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$220\\\\,V$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$30\\\\,V$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'The magnetic flux linked with the primary coil is given by <br>$\\phi  = {\\phi _0} + 4t$<br>So voltage across primary coil <br>${V_p} = \\frac{{d\\phi }}{{dt}} = \\frac{d}{{dt}}({\\phi _0} + 4t) = 4V$(as ${\\phi _0} = $ constant)<br>Also, we have<br>${N_p} = 50\\,\\,and\\,{N_s} = 1500$<br>From relation <br>$\\frac{{{V_s}}}{{{V_p}}} = \\frac{{{N_s}}}{{{N_p}}} \\Rightarrow {V_s} = {V_p}\\frac{{{N_s}}}{{{N_p}}} = 4\\left( {\\frac{{1500}}{{50}}} \\right) = 120V$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44533,'physics',39,1104,2631,'An electron is accelerated from rest through a potential difference of $$ volt. If the de Broglie wavelength of the electron is $1.227 \\times {10^{ - 2}}\\,nm$, the potential difference is:','[{"text": "${10^2}\\\\,V$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${10^3}\\\\,V$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${10^4}\\\\,V$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$10\\\\,V$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'$\\lambda  = \\frac{{12.27}}{{\\sqrt V }}\\mathop A\\limits^o $<br>$\\sqrt v  = {10^2} \\Rightarrow v = {10^4}{\\rm{ }}volt{\\rm{ }}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2020,'NEET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44534,'physics',38,1066,2543,'Radio waves and visible light in vacuum have','[{"text": "Same velocity but different wavelength", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Continuous emission spectrum", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Band absorption spectrum", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Line emission spectrum", "isCorrect": false}]',0,NULL,NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44535,'physics',40,240,479,'Electrons in a certain energy level $n = {n_1}$ , can emit 3 spectral lines. When they are in another energy level $n = {n_2}$. They can emit 6 spectral lines. The orbital speed of the electrons in the two orbits are in the ratio','[{"text": "$4:3$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$3:4$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2:1$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1:2$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Number of emission spectral lines<br>$N = \\frac{{n\\left( {n - 1} \\right)}}{2}$<br>$\\therefore \\quad 3 = \\frac{{{n_1}\\left( {{n_1} - 1} \\right)}}{2}$  in first case.<br>or $n_1^2 - {n_1} - 6 = 0\\, \\Rightarrow \\,\\left( {{n_1} - 3} \\right)\\left( {{n_1} + 2} \\right) = 0$<br>Taking positive root<br>$\\therefore {n_1} = 3$                     (1)<br>In second case, $6 = \\frac{{{n_2}\\left( {{n_2} - 1} \\right)}}{2}$<br>or $n_2^2 - {n_2} - 12 = 0\\,\\, \\Rightarrow \\,\\,\\left( {{n_2} - 4} \\right)\\left( {{n_2} + 3} \\right) = 0$<br>Taking positive root,  ${n_2} = 4$                              (2)<br>Velocity of electron,$v = \\frac{{2\\pi KZ{e^2}}}{{nh}}$<br>$\\therefore \\,\\,\\,\\frac{{{v_1}}}{{{v_2}}} = \\frac{{{n_2}}}{{{n_1}}} = \\frac{4}{3}$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',2007,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44536,'physics',37,972,2260,'An alternating voltage given as $V = 100\\sqrt 2 \\sin 100t$ volt is applied to a capacitor of  $1\\mu F.$The current reading of the ammeter will be equal to____$mA$','[{"text": "$20$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$80$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$10$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$40$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'$\\omega  = 100\\,rad\\,{s^{ - 1}};\\quad {X_C} = \\frac{1}{{\\omega C}} = {10^4}\\Omega $<br>${i_{rms}} = \\frac{{{V_{rms}}}}{{{X_C}}} = \\frac{{\\left( {\\frac{{100\\sqrt 2 }}{{\\sqrt 2 }}} \\right)}}{{{{10}^4}}} = 10mA$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44537,'physics',39,1104,2631,'An electromagnetic wave of wavelength \' $$\\lambda$$ \', is incident on a photosensitive surface of negligible work function. If \' $m$ \' is the mass of photoelectron emitted from the surface has deBroglie wavelength \' $$\\lambda_{d}$$ \' then','[{"text": "${\\\\lambda _d} = \\\\left( {\\\\frac{{2mc}}{h}} \\\\right){\\\\lambda ^2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\lambda _d} = \\\\left( {\\\\frac{{2mc}}{h}} \\\\right)\\\\lambda _d^2$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\lambda  = \\\\left( {\\\\frac{{2h}}{{mc}}} \\\\right)\\\\lambda _d^2$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\lambda  = \\\\left( {\\\\frac{{2m}}{{hc}}} \\\\right)\\\\lambda _d^2$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'$$\\dfrac{h c}{\\lambda}=\\dfrac{1}{2} m v^{2}+\\phi$$<br>Given that $$\\phi \\approx 0$$<br>$$\\dfrac{h c}{\\lambda}=\\dfrac{1}{2} m v^{2}$$<br>$$\\begin{aligned}& \\dfrac{h c}{\\lambda}=\\dfrac{P^{2}}{2 m} \\\\& P=\\sqrt{\\dfrac{2 m h c}{\\lambda}} \\\\& \\lambda_{d}=\\dfrac{h}{p}=\\dfrac{h}{\\sqrt{\\dfrac{2 m h c}{\\lambda}}} \\\\& =\\sqrt{\\dfrac{h^{2} \\lambda}{2 m h c}}=\\sqrt{\\dfrac{h \\lambda}{2 m c}} \\\\& \\lambda_{d}^{2}=\\dfrac{h \\lambda}{2 m c} \\Rightarrow \\lambda=\\dfrac{2 m c}{h} \\lambda_{d}^{2}\\end{aligned}$$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2021,'NEET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44538,'physics',38,1066,2543,'Which of the following rays are not electromagnetic waves?','[{"text": "$$\\\\beta$$-rays", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$X$-rays", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$\\\\gamma$$ - rays", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Heat rays", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'$$\\beta$$-rays are fast moving electrons. So, they are not electromagnetic waves.<br>$X$-rays are electromagnetic waves having wavelengths from about ${10^{ - 8}}\\,m$ to ${10^{ - 10}}\\,m$<br>$$\\gamma$$ - rays are electromagnetic waves<br>wavelength ranging from about ${10^{ - 10}}\\,m\\,{\\rm{to}}\\,{10^{ - 14}}\\,m$ Heat rays can travel through vacuum by radiation process. They are electromagnetic waves.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44539,'physics',37,972,2260,'When  a voltage measuring device is connected to $AC$ mains, the meter shows the steady input voltage of  220 $V$. This means','[{"text": "The meter  is calibrated to read $\\\\sqrt { < {v^2} > } .$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "The pointer of the meter is stuck by some mechanical defect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Input voltage cannot be $AC$ voltage, but a $DC$ voltage.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Maximum input voltage is $220V$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'The voltmeter connected to $AC$ mains is calibrated to read root mean square value of $AC$ voltage,  i.e, $\\sqrt { < {v^2} > } $.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44541,'physics',40,239,478,'The total energy of electron in the ground state of hydrogen atom is $ - 13.6\\,eV$. The kinetic energy of an electron in the first excited state is','[{"text": "$6.8\\\\,eV$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$3.4\\\\,eV$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$1.7\\\\,eV$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$13.6\\\\,eV$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'The energy of hydrogen atom when the electron revolves in  ${n^{th}}$ orbit is given by<br>$T.E = \\frac{{ - 13.6{Z^2}}}{{{n^2}}}eV$                $[Z = 1]$<br>For $n = 2,$<br>$E = \\frac{{ - 13.6}}{{{2^2}}} =  - 3.4\\,eV$<br>So, kinetic energy of electron in the first excited state  is<br>$KE =  - T.E =  - \\left( { - 3.4} \\right) = 3.4\\,eV$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44542,'physics',38,1066,2543,'Which of the following has minimum wavelength?','[{"text": "$$\\\\gamma$$ - rays", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$X$-rays", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Ultraviolet rays", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Cosmic rays", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'The wavelength order of waves are given below:<br>$\\begin{array}{*{20}{c}}\n{}&{waves}&{wavelength(inA^\\circ )}\\\\\n{(1)}&{{\\rm{X - rays}}}&{{\\rm{1}}\\,{\\rm{to}}\\,{\\rm{100}}}\\\\\n{(2)}&{{\\rm{Ultravoilet}}\\,{\\rm{rays}}}&{{\\rm{100}}\\,{\\rm{to}}\\,{\\rm{4000}}}\\\\\n{(3)}&{{\\rm{\\gamma  - rays}}}&{{\\rm{0}}{\\rm{.001}}\\,{\\rm{to}}\\,{\\rm{1}}}\\\\\n{(4)}&{{\\rm{Cosmic}}\\,{\\rm{rays}}}&{{\\rm{upto}}\\,{\\rm{4 \\times 1}}{{\\rm{0}}^{{\\rm{ - 3}}}}}\n\\end{array}$<br>Thus, Cosmic rays have the minimum wavelength',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44543,'physics',37,973,2264,'Same current is flowing in two alternating circuits. The first circuit contains only inductance and the other contains only a capacitor. If the frequency of the $e$.$m$.$f$. of $AC$ is increased, the effect on the value of the current  will','[{"text": "Decrease in the first circuit and increases in the other", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Decrease in both the circuits", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Increase in both the circuits", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Increase in the first circuit and decreases in the other", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'For the first circuit $i = \\frac{V}{Z} = \\frac{V}{{\\omega L}}$<br>$\\therefore $ Increase in $\\omega $ will cause a decrease in $i$.<br>For the second circuit$i = \\frac{V}{{\\left( {\\frac{1}{{\\omega C}}} \\right)}} = V\\omega C$<br>Increase in  $\\omega $ will increase $i$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44544,'physics',39,1104,2631,'The graph which shows the variation of the de Broglie wavelength $$(\\lambda)$$ of a particle and its associated momentum (\\[p\\]) is<div class="matching-question"></div>','[{"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'$$\\lambda=\\dfrac{h}{p}$$<br>Graph will be hyperbolic<br><img src=\'/img/data/physics/39_44544_explanation_1.jpg\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' /> <br>Note: If it is to be linear, it is to be of $$y=m x$$ type<br>or $$y=m x+c$$ type<br>Here $$y=\\lambda, x=p$$<br>But we have $$\\lambda \\propto \\dfrac{1}{p}$$<br>$$\\Rightarrow$$ Curved as in (3)',NULL,'Medium','Graph/Figure',2022,'NEET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44545,'physics',38,1065,2542,'A laser beam can be focussed on an area equal to the square of its wavelength. A He-Ne laser radiates energy at the rate of $1\\,mW$ and its wavelength is $600\\,nm$. The intensity of focussed beam will be','[{"text": "$2.8 \\\\times {10^{13}}\\\\,W/{m^2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$3.2 \\\\times {10^{13}}\\\\,W/{m^2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2.7 \\\\times {10^9}\\\\,W/{m^2}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$3.2 \\\\times {10^9}\\\\,W/{m^2}$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'$I = \\frac{P}{A} = \\frac{P}{{{\\lambda ^2}}} = 2.7 \\times {10^9}\\,W/{m^2}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44546,'physics',40,239,478,'If the speed of an electron of hydrogen atom in the ground state is $2.2 \\times {10^6}m/s$, then its speed in the third excited state will be','[{"text": "$5.5 \\\\times {10^6}m/s$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$5.5 \\\\times {10^5}m/s$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$8.8 \\\\times {10^5}m/s$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$6.8 \\\\times {10^6}m/s$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Speed of electron of hydrogen atom in ground state,<br>${v_1} = 2.2 \\times {10^6}m/s$<br>Since,$v \\propto \\frac{1}{n}$<br>$\\therefore \\frac{{{v_1}}}{{{v_2}}} = \\frac{{{n_1}}}{{{n_2}}} = \\frac{{2.2 \\times {{10}^6}}}{{{v_2}}}$<br>[ for third excited state,${n_2} = 4$ ]<br>$ \\Rightarrow {v_2} = \\frac{{2.2 \\times {{10}^6}}}{4} = 0.55 \\times {10^6}m/s$<br>$ = 5.5 \\times {10^5}m/s$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2019,'MHTCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44547,'physics',37,973,2263,'When 100 volt $DC$ is applied across a coil, a current of $1\\,amp$  flows through it. When 100 volt $AC$ at $50\\,c/s$  is applied to the same coil, only 0.5 ampere current flows. The impedance 	of the coil is','[{"text": "$300\\\\Omega $", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$100\\\\Omega $", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$400\\\\Omega $", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$200\\\\Omega $", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'When $DC$ is supplied <br>$R = \\frac{V}{i} = \\frac{{100}}{1} = 100\\,\\Omega C$<br>When $AC$ is supplied $Z = \\frac{V}{i} = \\frac{{100}}{{0.5}} = 200\\,\\Omega $',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44548,'physics',39,1104,2631,'The kinetic energy of an electron gets tripled then the de Broglie wavelength associated with it changes by a factor','[{"text": "$$\\\\dfrac{1}{3}$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$\\\\sqrt{3}$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$\\\\dfrac{1}{\\\\sqrt{3}}$$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "3", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'For an electron,<br>de Broglie wavelength $\\lambda  = \\frac{h}{{\\sqrt {2mK} }}$<br>where $h = $ Planck\'s constant, $m = $ mass of an electron, $k = $ kinetic energy of an electron<br>Since $$h$$ and $$m$$ are constants, $\\lambda  \\propto \\frac{1}{{\\sqrt K }}$<br>$ \\Rightarrow \\frac{\\lambda }{{{\\lambda ^\\prime }}} = \\sqrt {\\frac{{{K^\\prime }}}{K}}  = \\sqrt {\\frac{{3\\,K}}{k}} {\\rm{ or }}{\\lambda ^\\prime } = \\frac{\\lambda }{{\\sqrt 3 }}$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44549,'physics',38,1065,2542,'The sun delivers ${10^3}\\,W/{m^2}$ of electromagnetic flux to the earth\'s surface. The total power that is incident on a roof of dimensions $8\\,m \\times 20\\,m$, will be','[{"text": "$3.4 \\\\times {10^4}\\\\,W$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$6.4 \\\\times {10^3}\\\\,W$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1.6 \\\\times {10^5}\\\\,W$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "None of these", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'$P = {10^3} \\times 160 = 1.6 \\times {10^5}\\,W$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44550,'physics',40,240,479,'For hydrogen atom, the energy of radiation emitted in the transition from 4$th$ excited state to 2$nd$ excited state, according to Bohr’s theory is','[{"text": "$0.567\\\\,eV$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$0.667\\\\,eV$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$0.967\\\\,eV$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$1.267\\\\,eV$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Using,${E_n} = \\frac{{ - 13.6}}{{{n^2}}}eV$<br>According to given question,<br>Energies in ${4^{th}}$ and ${2^{nd}}$ excited state are <br>${E_{3rd}} = \\frac{{ - 13.6}}{{{3^2}}} = \\frac{{13.6}}{9}eV =  - 1.51\\,eV$<br>${E_{5th}} = \\frac{{ - 13.6}}{{{5^2}}} =  - 0.544$<br>Energy radiation emitted in the transition<br>$\\Delta E = {E_{5th}} - {E_{3rd}} =  - 1.511 + 0.544 = 0.967\\,eV$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',2015,'MHTCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44552,'physics',39,1104,2631,'The additional energy that should be given to an electron to reduce its de - Broglie wavelength from $1\\,nm$ to $0.5\\,nm$ is','[{"text": "2 times the initial kinetic energy", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "3 times the initial kinetic energy", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "0.5 times the initial kinetic energy", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "4 times the initial kinetic energy", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'de Broglie wavelength of an electron having kinetic energy $K$ is<br>$\\lambda  = \\frac{h}{{\\sqrt {2\\,{m_e}K} }}$ where ${m_e}$ is the mass of an electron<br>$\\therefore \\quad {\\lambda ^2} = \\frac{{{h^2}}}{{2\\,{m_e}K}}{\\rm{ }}or{\\rm{ }}K = \\frac{h}{{2\\,{m_e}{\\lambda ^2}}} \\Rightarrow K \\propto \\frac{1}{{{\\lambda ^2}}}$<br>$\\therefore \\frac{{{K^\\prime }}}{K} = {\\left( {\\frac{\\lambda }{{{\\lambda ^\\prime }}}} \\right)^2} = {\\left( {\\frac{{1\\,nm}}{{0.5\\,nm}}} \\right)^2} = 4$<br>${K^\\prime } = 4K$<br>Additional energy given $$=$$<br>${K^\\prime } - K = 4\\,K - K = 3\\,K$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2005,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44553,'physics',38,1065,2542,'A point source of electromagnetic radiation has an average power output of $800\\,W$. The maximum value of electric field at a distance $3.5\\,m$ from the source will be $62.6\\,V/m,$ the energy density at a distance $3.5\\,m$ from the source will be -(in joule $/{m^3}$ )','[{"text": "$$1.73 \\\\times 10^{-6}$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$1.73 \\\\times 10^{-7}$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$1.73 \\\\times 10^{-5}$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$1.73 \\\\times 10^{-8}$$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Energy density<br>$u = \\frac{1}{2}{\\varepsilon _0}E_m^2 = 1.73 \\times {10^{ - 8}}\\,J/{m^3}$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44554,'physics',37,973,2265,'In the circuit given in the figure, find the current 	through the circuit','[{"text": "$15.1\\\\,A$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$28.2\\\\,A$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$14.2\\\\,A$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$7.8\\\\,A$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'${i_{rms}} = \\frac{{{V_{rms}}}}{X} = \\frac{{240}}{{\\sqrt {{{40}^2} + {{(2\\pi  \\times 100 \\times 12 \\times {{10}^{ - 3}})}^2}} }}$<br>$ = 28.2\\,A$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/37_44554_question_1.png'),
(44555,'physics',40,240,479,'Which of the following spectral series of hydrogen atom is lying in visible range of electromagnetic wave?','[{"text": "Lyman series", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Paschen series", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Balmer series", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Pfund series", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'The Balmer series lies in the visible region. The Lyman series is in the ultraviolet region, and Paschen and Pfund series are in the infrared region.<br>$v = \\frac{h}{{m\\lambda }} = \\frac{{24}}{{25}}\\frac{{Rh}}{m}$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2014,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44556,'physics',39,1104,2631,'The de Broglie wavelength of an electron accelerated to a potential of $400\\,V$ is approximately','[{"text": "$0.03\\\\,nm$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$0.04\\\\,nm$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$0.12\\\\,nm$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$0.06\\\\,nm$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'de Broglie wavelength $\\lambda  = \\frac{h}{p} = \\frac{h}{{\\sqrt {2meV} }}$<br>here, $V = 400\\,V$<br>$\\lambda  = \\frac{{6.6 \\times {{10}^{ - 34}}}}{{\\sqrt {2 \\times 9.1 \\times {{10}^{ - 31}} \\times 1.6 \\times {{10}^{ - 19}} \\times 400} }}$<br>$ \\approx 0.06 \\times {10^{ - 9}}\\,m = 0.06\\,nm$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2016,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44557,'physics',38,1065,2539,'A light beam travelling in the $$x$$-direction is described by the electric field  ${E_y} = (300\\,V/m)\\sin \\omega \\,(t - x/c)$. An electron is constrained to move along the $$y$$-direction with a speed of $2.0 \\times {10^7}\\,m/s$. Find the maximum magnetic force on the electron.','[{"text": "$3.2 \\\\times {10^{ - 18}}\\\\,N$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$3.2 \\\\times {10^{ - 16}}\\\\,N$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1.6 \\\\times {10^{ - 18}}\\\\,N$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1.6 \\\\times {10^{ - 16}}\\\\,N$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'The maximum electric field is ${E_0} = 300\\,V/m$. The maximum magnetic field is<br>${B_0} = \\frac{{{E_0}}}{c} = \\frac{{300\\,V/m}}{{3 \\times {{10}^8}\\,m/s}} = {10^{ - 6}}\\,T$<br>The maximum magnetic force on the electron is $\\quad {F_b} = |q\\overrightarrow \\upsilon   \\times \\vec B{|_{\\max }} = q\\upsilon {B_0}$<br>$ = \\left( {1.6 \\times {{10}^{ - 19}}} \\right) \\times \\left( {2.0 \\times {{10}^7}\\,m/s} \\right) \\times \\left( {{{10}^{ - 6}}\\,T} \\right)$<br>$ = 3.2 \\times {10^{ - 18}}\\,N$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44558,'physics',37,973,2265,'A $60{\\rm{ }}W/120{\\rm{ }}V$ bulb is connected to a $240V/60{\\rm{ }}Hz$ supply with an inductance in series. Find the value of inductance so that bulb gets correct voltage.','[{"text": "$2\\\\sqrt 3 H$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\pi H$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{{2\\\\sqrt 3 }}{\\\\pi }H$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{{2.3}}{\\\\pi }H$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'$R = \\frac{{{{(120)}^2}}}{{60}} = 240\\Omega $<br> The current through the resistor is <br>$i = \\frac{P}{V} = \\frac{{60W}}{{120V}} = 0.5A$<br>$Z = \\frac{{{V_{rms}}}}{{{i_{rms}}}} = \\frac{{240}}{{0.5}} = 480\\Omega $<br>${X_L} = \\sqrt {{{480}^2} - {{240}^2}}  = 240\\sqrt 3 \\Omega $<br>$L = \\frac{{240\\sqrt 3 }}{{60 \\times 2\\pi }} = \\frac{{2\\sqrt 3 }}{\\pi }H$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44559,'physics',39,1104,2631,'An electron of mass $$m$$ has de - Brogile wavelength $$\\lambda$$ when accelerated through potential difference $V$. When proton of mass $M$, is accelerated through potential difference $9\\,V$, the de - Brogile wavelength associated with it will be (assume that wavelength is determined at low voltage)','[{"text": "$\\\\frac{\\\\lambda }{3}\\\\sqrt {\\\\frac{M}{m}} $", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{\\\\lambda }{3}\\\\frac{M}{m}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{\\\\lambda }{3}\\\\sqrt {\\\\frac{m}{M}} $", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{\\\\lambda }{3}\\\\frac{m}{M}$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'According to question,<br>Mass of electron $ = m$<br>Wavelength of electron $$=\\lambda$$<br>Potential difference in case of electron $ = V$<br>From de - Brogile relation,<br>$$\\begin{aligned}& \\lambda=\\dfrac{h}{p} \\Rightarrow h=\\dfrac{h}{\\sqrt{2 m K E}}=\\dfrac{h}{\\sqrt{2 m q V}} \\\\& \\Rightarrow \\lambda \\propto \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{q V m}}\\end{aligned}$$<br>For electron, $\\lambda  \\propto \\frac{1}{{\\sqrt {eVm} }}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,(1)$<br>For proton, ${\\lambda _1} = \\frac{1}{{\\sqrt {e9VM} }}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,(2)$<br>Where, $e$ is the charge on proton, potential differnce $ = 9\\,V$<br>Mass of proton $ = m$<br>From eq.(1) and (2), we get<br>$$\\dfrac{\\lambda}{\\lambda_{1}}=\\sqrt{\\dfrac{9 V M e}{e V m}} \\Rightarrow \\lambda_{1}=\\dfrac{\\lambda}{3} \\sqrt{\\dfrac{m}{M}}$$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2016,'MHTCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44560,'physics',40,240,479,'The spectrum of an oil flame is an example for','[{"text": "Line emission spectrum", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Continuous emission spectrum", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Line absorption spectrum", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Band emission spectrum", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'The spectrum of an oil flame is a continuous emission spectrum over a wide band ranging from ultraviolet to infrared.<br>$\\frac{{\\lambda \'}}{\\lambda } = 3$ or $\\lambda \' = 3\\lambda $',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44561,'physics',38,1065,2540,'The velocity of electromagnetic wave is along the direction of:','[{"text": "$$\\\\vec{B}$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{{\\\\overrightarrow E  \\\\times \\\\overrightarrow B }}{{{\\\\mu _0}}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{{\\\\overrightarrow B  \\\\times \\\\overrightarrow E }}{{{\\\\mu _0}}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$E$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'An electromagnetic wave is the wave composed of the oscillations of electric and magnetic fields in mutually perpendicular planes and these oscllilations are perpendicular to the direction of propagations of wave.<br>The direction of propagation of electromagnetic wave is given by poynting vector<br>Along $$\\vec{S}=\\overrightarrow{\\mathrm{E}} \\times \\vec{H}=\\dfrac{\\overrightarrow{\\mathrm{E}} \\times \\overrightarrow{\\mathrm{B}}}{\\mu_{0}}$$ this is parallel to $\\overrightarrow E  \\times \\overrightarrow B $',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44562,'physics',37,973,2269,'For the circuit shown in fig, the ammeter ${A_2}$ reads 1.6$A$ and ammeter ${A_3}$ reads $0.4A$. Then','[{"text": "The ammeter ${A_1}$ reads 1.2$A$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\omega  = \\\\frac{4}{{\\\\sqrt {LC} }}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "The ammeter ${A_1}$ reads 2$A$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$f = \\\\frac{{2\\\\pi }}{{\\\\sqrt {LC} }}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'The phasor diagrams of the both inductor and  capacitor are shown in the figure. <img src=\'/img/data/physics/37_44562_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />  As the capacitor and inductor are in parallel<br>$\\Delta {V_L} = \\Delta {V_C}$<br>The resultant phasor is  <img src=\'/img/data/physics/37_44562_explanation_2.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />  $i = {i_L} \\sim {i_C} = 1.2A$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/37_44562_question_1.png'),
(44563,'physics',39,1104,2631,'An electron of mass $$m_{e}$$ and a proton of mass ${m_p}$ are moving with the same speed. The ratio of their de - Broglie\'s wavelength $$\\dfrac{\\lambda_{e}}{\\lambda_{p}}$$ is','[{"text": "1", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "1836", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$$\\\\dfrac{1}{1836}$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "918", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'de Broglie wavelength $\\lambda  = \\frac{h}{{mv}}$<br>where $m$ is the mass and $$v$$ is the speed of the particle.<br>As electron and proton both are moving with same speed, therefore the ratio of their de Broglie wavelengths is<br>$\\frac{{{\\lambda _e}}}{{{\\lambda _p}}} = \\frac{{{m_p}}}{{{m_e}}} = \\frac{{1.6726 \\times {{10}^{ - 27}}\\,kg}}{{9.1094 \\times {{10}^{ - 31}}\\,kg}} \\approx 1836$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44564,'physics',38,1065,2541,'The sun delivers $\\overrightarrow {{{10}^3}\\,W/{m^2}} $ of electromagnetic flux to the earth\'s surface. The total power that is incident on a roof of dimensions $8\\,m \\times 20\\,m$ is $1.6 \\times {10^5}\\,W$, the radiation force on the roof will be (Assume complete absorption)','[{"text": "$7.33 \\\\times {10^{ - 3}}\\\\,N$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$5.33 \\\\times {10^{ - 4}}\\\\,N$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$9.33 \\\\times {10^{ - 2}}\\\\,N$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$3.33 \\\\times {10^{ - 5}}\\\\,N$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'$F = {P_{rod}}A = \\frac{{IA}}{c} = \\frac{{{{10}^3} \\times 160}}{{3 \\times {{10}^8}}} = 5.33 \\times {10^{ - 4}}\\,N$<br>(or)  $F = $ total power / velocity of light',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44565,'physics',40,239,478,'A hydrogen atom in ground state absorbs $10.2\\,eV$ of energy. The orbital angular momentum of the electron is increased by','[{"text": "$3.16 \\\\times {10^{ - 34}}\\\\,Js$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1.05 \\\\times {10^{ - 34}}Js$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$4.22 \\\\times {10^{ - 34}}{\\\\rm{Js}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2.11 \\\\times {10^{ - 34}}Js$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Energy absorbed  during electron transition from<br>$n = 1$ to $n = 2$<br>$\\Delta E = 13.6 \\times \\left( {\\frac{1}{{{1^2}}} - \\frac{1}{{{2^2}}}} \\right)eV = 10.2\\,eV$<br>As the hydrogen atom in the given problem also absorbs 10.2 $eV$, the only possible electron transition is from $n = 1$ to $n = 2$.<br>$\\therefore $ Change in angular momentum<br>$\\Delta L = \\frac{{(2 - 1)h}}{{2\\pi }} = \\frac{{6.6 \\times {{10}^{ - 34}}}}{{2 \\times 3.14}} = 1.05 \\times {10^{ - 34}}Js$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',2019,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44566,'physics',37,973,2268,'For a series $RLC$ circuit $R = {X_L} = 2{X_C}$ the impedence of the circuit and phase difference (between) $V$ and $i$ will be','[{"text": "$\\\\sqrt 5 {X_C},{\\\\tan ^{ - 1}}\\\\left( 2 \\\\right)$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\sqrt 5 R,{\\\\tan ^{ - 1}}\\\\left( {\\\\frac{1}{2}} \\\\right)$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{{\\\\sqrt 5 R}}{2},{\\\\tan ^{ - 1}}\\\\left( 2 \\\\right)$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{{\\\\sqrt 5 R}}{2},{\\\\tan ^{ - 1}}\\\\left( {\\\\frac{1}{2}} \\\\right)$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'${X_L} = R,{X_C} = R/2$<br>$\\therefore \\tan \\phi  = \\frac{{{X_L} - {X_C}}}{R} = \\frac{{R - \\frac{R}{2}}}{R} = \\frac{1}{2}$<br>$ \\Rightarrow \\phi  = {\\tan ^{ - 1}}\\left( {1/2} \\right)$<br>Also $Z = \\sqrt {{R^2} + {{\\left( {{X_L} - {X_C}} \\right)}^2}}  = \\sqrt {{R^2} + \\frac{{{R^2}}}{4}}  = \\frac{{\\sqrt 5 }}{2}R$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44567,'physics',39,1102,2626,'The electromagnetic theory of light failed to explain','[{"text": "Photoelectric effect", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Polarisation", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Diffraction", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Interference", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'The electromagnetic theory of light failed to explain photoelectric effect',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2007,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44568,'physics',38,1065,2540,'A plane electromagnetic wave of frequency 25 $MHz$ travels in free space along the $x$-direction. At a perpendicular point in space and time, $E = 6.3j\\,V/m$. What is $B$ at that point?','[{"text": "$5.1 \\\\times {10^{ - 8}}\\\\,\\\\hat kT$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$4.1 \\\\times {10^{ - 8}}\\\\,\\\\hat kT$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$3.1 \\\\times {10^{ - 8}}\\\\,\\\\hat kT$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2.1 \\\\times {10^{ - 8}}\\\\,\\\\hat kT$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'The magnitude of $$B$$ is $$B=\\dfrac{E}{C}$$<br>$ = \\frac{{6.3\\,V/M}}{{3 \\times {{10}^8}\\,m/s}} = 2.1 \\times {10^{ - 8}}\\,T$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44569,'physics',37,973,2268,'The following series $L$ - $C$ - $R$ circuit, when driven by an e.m.f. source of angular frequency 70 kilo-radians per second, the circuit effectively behaves like','[{"text": "Series $L{\\\\rm{ }} - C{\\\\rm{ }}$ circuit with $R = {V_3}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Series $R - C$ circuit", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Series $R - L$ circuit", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Purely resistive circuit", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Impedence $Z = \\sqrt {{{({X_L} \\sim {X_C})}^2} + {R^2}} $<br>$ \\Rightarrow Z = \\sqrt {{{\\left( {\\omega L \\sim \\frac{1}{{\\omega C}}} \\right)}^2} + {R^2}} $<br>Inductive reactance<br>${X_L} = \\omega L = 70 \\times {10^3} \\times 100 \\times {10^{ - 6}} = 7\\Omega $<br>Capacitive reactance<br>${X_C} = \\frac{1}{{\\omega C}} = \\frac{1}{{70 \\times {{10}^3} \\times 1 \\times {{10}^{ - 6}}}} = \\frac{{100}}{7}$<br>$ \\Rightarrow {X_C} > {X_L}$<br>The circuit behaves like an $R - C$ circuit.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/37_44569_question_1.png'),
(44570,'physics',40,239,478,'The period of revolution of an electron in the ground state of hydrogen atom is $T$. The period of revolution of the electron in the first excited state is','[{"text": "$2T$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$4T$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$6T$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$8T$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'The period of revolution of an electron in ${n^{th}}$ orbit of hydrogen atom is<br>${T_n} = \\frac{{2\\pi {r_n}}}{{{v_n}}} = \\frac{{4\\varepsilon _0^2{h^3}{n^3}}}{{m{e^4}}}$<br>$\\left( {as\\,\\,{r_n} = \\frac{{{n^2}{h^2}{\\varepsilon _0}}}{{\\pi m{e^2}}},{v_n} = \\frac{{{e^2}}}{{2{\\varepsilon _0}hn}}} \\right)$<br>or  ${T_n} \\propto {n^3}$<br>For ground state, $n = 1$<br>and for first excited state, $n = 2$<br>$\\therefore \\frac{{{T_2}}}{{{T_1}}} = \\frac{{{2^3}}}{{{1^3}}} = 8\\,\\,\\,{\\rm{or}}\\,\\,{T_2} = 8{T_1}$<br>But as per question ${T_1} = T$<br>$\\therefore {T_2} = 8T$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2018,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44571,'physics',39,1103,2630,'Monochromatic light of frequency $6.0 \\times {10^{14}}\\,Hz$ is produced by a laser. The power emitted is $2 \\times {10^{ - 3}}\\,W$. The number of photons emitted, on the average, by the source per second is','[{"text": "$$5 \\\\times 10^{16}$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$5 \\\\times 10^{17}$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$5 \\\\times 10^{14}$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$5 \\\\times 10^{15}$$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Power emitted,<br>$P = 2 \\times {10^{ - 3}}\\,W$<br>Energy of photon,<br>$E = hv$<br>$ = 6.6 \\times {10^{ - 34}} \\times 6 \\times {10^{14}}\\,J$<br>Here, $$h$$ being Planck\'s constant<br>Number of photons emitted per second is given<br>by $$n=\\dfrac{\\operatorname{power}(P)}{\\operatorname{Energy}(E)}=\\dfrac{2 \\times 10^{-3}}{6.6 \\times 10^{-34} \\times 6 \\times 10^{14}}=5 \\times 10^{15}$$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44572,'physics',38,1065,2539,'The magnetic field in a plane electromagnetic wave is given by<br>${B_y} = 2 \\times {10^{ - 7}}\\sin \\left( {0.5 \\times {{10}^3}x - 1.5 \\times {{10}^{11}}t} \\right)T.$<br>The wavelength and frequency of the wave respectively are','[{"text": "$1.9\\\\,cm,29GHz$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$3.2\\\\,cm,39GH$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1.26\\\\,cm,23.9GHz$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$2.13\\\\,cm,28.72GHz$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'(1)Comparing the given equation with<br>${B_y} = {B_0}\\sin \\left( {\\frac{{2\\pi }}{\\lambda }x - 2\\pi ft} \\right)$<br>we get, $\\lambda  = \\frac{{2\\pi }}{{0.5 \\times {{10}^3}}}\\,m = 1.26\\,cm$,<br>and $\\frac{1}{{\\,T}} = \\upsilon  = \\left( {1.5 \\times {{10}^{11}}} \\right)/2\\pi  = 23.9\\,GHz$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL);
INSERT INTO `qb_questions` (`id`, `subject_id`, `chapter_id`, `topic_id`, `subtopic_id`, `question_html`, `options`, `correct_index`, `explanation`, `explanation_image_url`, `difficulty`, `qtype`, `year`, `tag`, `created_at`, `question_image_url`) VALUES
(44573,'physics',37,975,2275,'The resonant frequency of a circuit is $f$. If the capacitance is made 4 times the initial values, then the resonant frequency will become','[{"text": "$f/4$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$f/2$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$2f$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$f$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'$f = \\frac{1}{{2\\pi \\sqrt {LC} }} \\Rightarrow f \\propto \\frac{1}{{\\sqrt C }}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44574,'physics',39,1103,2630,'A $200\\,W$ sodium street lamp emits yellow light of wavelength $0.6\\,\\mu m$. Assuming it to be $$25 \\%$$ efficient in converting electrical energy to light, the number of photons of yellow light it emits per second is','[{"text": "$$6 \\\\times 10^{18}$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$1.5 \\\\times 10^{20}$$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$$3 \\\\times 10^{19}$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$62 \\\\times 10^{20}$$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Efficient power $$(P)$$ is given by<br>$P = \\frac{N}{t} \\times \\frac{{hc}}{\\lambda }\\quad \\left( {\\begin{array}{*{20}{c}}\n{{\\rm{N = total}}\\,{\\rm{number}}\\,{\\rm{of}}}\\\\\n{{\\rm{photons}}}\n\\end{array}} \\right)$<br>$\\frac{N}{t} = \\frac{{P \\times \\lambda }}{{hc}} = \\frac{{50 \\times 0.6 \\times {{10}^{ - 6}}}}{{6.6 \\times {{10}^{ - 34}} \\times 3 \\times {{10}^8}}} = 1.5 \\times {10^{20}}$<br>$[\\because 25\\% {\\text{ of }}200\\,W = 50\\,W]$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44575,'physics',40,239,478,'The ratio of speed of an electron in the ground state in Bohr’s first orbit of hydrogen atom to velocity of light ($c$) is ($h = $ Planck’s constant,  ${\\varepsilon _0} = $ permitivity of free space, $e = $charge on electron)','[{"text": "$\\\\frac{{2{e^2}{\\\\varepsilon _0}}}{{hc}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{{{e^3}}}{{2{\\\\varepsilon _0}hc}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{{{e^2}}}{{2{\\\\varepsilon _0}hc}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{{2{\\\\varepsilon _0}hc}}{{{e^2}}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'The speed of electron in the ${n^{th}}$ state of hydrogen atom is <br>${v_n} = \\left( {\\frac{{{e^2}}}{{2h{\\varepsilon _0}}}} \\right)\\frac{1}{n}m/s$<br>For ground state $(n = 1),{v_1} = \\left( {\\frac{{{e^2}}}{{2h{\\varepsilon _0}}}} \\right)m/s$<br>$\\therefore \\frac{{{v_1}}}{c} = \\frac{{{e^2}}}{{2{\\varepsilon _0}hc}}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2020,'MHTCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44576,'physics',38,1065,2539,'A plane electromagnetic wave propagating in the $$x$$-direction has a wavelength of  $60\\,mm$. The electric field is in the y-direction and its maximum magnitude is $$33 \\mathrm{Vm}^{-1}$$. The equation for the electric field as function of $x$ and $t$ is','[{"text": "$$11 \\\\sin \\\\pi \\\\times 10^{10}(t-x / c)$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$33 \\\\sin \\\\pi \\\\times 10^{10}(t-x / c)$$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$$11 \\\\sin \\\\pi(t-x / c)$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$33 \\\\sin \\\\pi(t-x / c)$$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'$\\omega  = 2\\pi v = \\frac{{2\\pi c}}{\\lambda } = \\frac{{2\\pi  \\times 3 \\times {{10}^8}}}{{60 \\times {{10}^{ - 3}}}}\\pi  = \\pi  \\times {10^{10}}rad/s$<br>${E_y} = {E_0}\\sin \\omega \\left( {t - \\frac{x}{c}} \\right) = 33\\sin \\pi  \\times {10^{10}}(t - x/c)$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44577,'physics',37,973,2268,'In $LCR$ circuit $R = 50\\,{\\mathop{\\rm ohm}\\nolimits} $. When capacitance $C$ is removed, the current lags behind the voltage by $\\pi /6$ . When inductance $L$ is removed, the current leads the voltage by  $\\pi /6$.The impedance of the circuit is','[{"text": "$100\\\\,{\\\\mathop{\\\\rm ohm}\\\\nolimits} $", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$50\\\\,{\\\\mathop{\\\\rm ohm}\\\\nolimits} $", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$400\\\\,{\\\\mathop{\\\\rm ohm}\\\\nolimits} $", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$200\\\\,{\\\\mathop{\\\\rm ohm}\\\\nolimits} $", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'When $C$ is removed circuit becomes $RL$ circuit  hence<br>$\\tan \\frac{\\pi }{6} = \\frac{{{X_L}}}{R}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,(1)$<br>When $L$ is removed circuit becomes $RC$ circuit hence<br>$\\tan \\frac{\\pi }{6} = \\frac{{{X_C}}}{R}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,(2)$<br>From equations (1) and (2) we obtain ${X_L} = {X_C}.$<br>In resonance $Z = R = 50\\Omega $',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44578,'physics',39,1102,2628,'Light of wavelength $$\\lambda$$ which is less than threshold wavelength is incident on a photosensitive material. If incident wavelength is decreased, then stopping potential will','[{"text": "Increase", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Decrease", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Be zero", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Become exactly half", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'According to photoelectric equation<br>$\\frac{{hc}}{\\lambda } - \\phi  = \\frac{1}{2}m{v^2} = e{V_S}$<br>Symbols have their usual meaning<br>If we decrease the wavelength $$\\lambda$$, then stopping potential ${V_S}$ will increase.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2016,'MHTCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44580,'physics',37,974,2274,'A virtual current of 4$A$ and 50 $Hz$ flows in an $AC$ circuit containing a coil. The power consumed in the coil is  240$W$. If the virtual voltage across the coil is 100$V$ its inductance will be','[{"text": "$\\\\frac{1}{{5\\\\pi }}H$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{1}{{3\\\\pi }}H$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{1}{{9\\\\pi }}H$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{1}{{7\\\\pi }}H$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'$R = \\frac{P}{{i_{rms}^2}} = \\frac{{240}}{{16}} = 15\\Omega $<br>$Z = \\frac{V}{i} = \\frac{{100}}{4} = 25\\Omega $<br>Now ${X_L} = \\sqrt {{Z^2} - {R^2}}  = \\sqrt {{{\\left( {25} \\right)}^2} - {{\\left( {15} \\right)}^2}}  = 20\\Omega $<br>$\\therefore 2\\pi vL = 20 \\Rightarrow L = \\frac{{20}}{{2\\pi  \\times 50}} = \\frac{1}{{5\\pi }}Hz.$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44581,'physics',40,239,478,'Bohr model is applied to a particle of mass $\'m\'$ and charge $\'q\'$ is moving in a plane under the influence of a transverse magnetic field $\'B\'$. The energy of the charged particle in the  ${n^{th}}$ level will be ($h = $ planck’s constant)','[{"text": "$2nhqB/\\\\pi m$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$nhqB/2\\\\pi m$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$nhqB/4\\\\pi m$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$nhqB/\\\\pi m$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'For a particle moving in a magnetic field, then applied two forces are equal <br>Centripetal force $\\left( {{F_c}} \\right) = $ magnetic force $\\left( {{F_m}} \\right)$<br>$ \\Rightarrow \\frac{{m{v^2}}}{r} = qvB$<br>$ \\Rightarrow m{v^2} = qB\\left( {vr} \\right)\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,(1)$<br>Also, from Bohr’s model<br>$mvr = \\frac{{nh}}{{2\\pi }}$<br>$\\therefore vr = \\frac{{nh}}{{2\\pi m}}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,(2)$<br>From eq.(1) and eq.(2), we get<br>$m{v^2} = \\frac{{nh}}{{2\\pi m}}.qB\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,(3)$<br>Energy of the electron moving in ${n^{th}}$ orbit,<br>$E = \\frac{1}{2}m{v^2} = \\frac{1}{2}\\frac{{nhqB}}{{\\pi m}}$  (Using (3))<br>$ \\Rightarrow E = \\frac{{nhqB}}{{4\\pi m}}$<br>Hence the energy of the charged particle in the ${n^{th}}$ level will be $\\frac{{nhqB}}{{4\\pi m}}$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2019,'MHTCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44582,'physics',39,1102,2628,'In a photocell, frequency of incident radiation is increased by keeping other factors constant $\\left( {\\nu  > {\\nu _0}} \\right)$, the stopping potential','[{"text": "Decreases", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Increases", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Becomes zero", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "First decreases and the increases", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'We know that<br>$$e V_{0}=h v-h v_{0}$$<br>or $$e V_{0}=h\\left(v-v_{0}\\right)$$<br>or $$V_{0}=\\dfrac{h}{e}\\left(v-v_{0}\\right)$$<br>Here $$V_{0}=$$ stopping potential<br>and $V = $ frequency of incident radiation<br>Therefore, stopping potential is directly proportional to the frequency of incident radiation.<br>Hence, stopping potential $$V_{0}$$ is increased.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2018,'MHTCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44583,'physics',38,1064,2537,'Match the Column I and Column II.<div class="matching-question"><div class="column-left">A. $\\oint {\\overrightarrow E } .d\\overrightarrow A  = Q/{\\varepsilon _0}$<br>B. $\\oint {\\overrightarrow B } .d\\overrightarrow A  = 0$<br>C. $\\oint {\\overrightarrow E } .\\overrightarrow {dl}  =  - \\frac{{d{\\phi _E}}}{{dt}}$<br>D. $\\oint {\\overrightarrow E } .\\overrightarrow {dl}  = {u_0}{i_c}$ $\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, + {u_0}{\\varepsilon _0}\\frac{{d{\\phi _E}}}{{dt}}$</div><div class="column-right">P. Faraday\'s law<br>Q. Ampere-Maxwell law<br>R. Gauss law for electricity<br>S. Gauss law for magnetism</div></div>','[{"text": "${\\\\rm{A - S,}}\\\\,{\\\\rm{B - R,}}\\\\,{\\\\rm{C - Q,}}\\\\,{\\\\rm{D - P}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{A - R,}}\\\\,{\\\\rm{B - Q,}}\\\\,{\\\\rm{C - P,}}\\\\,{\\\\rm{D - S}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{A - R,}}\\\\,{\\\\rm{B - S,}}\\\\,{\\\\rm{C - P,}}\\\\,{\\\\rm{D - Q}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{A  -  P,}}\\\\,{\\\\rm{B  -  Q,}}\\\\,{\\\\rm{C  -  R,}}\\\\,{\\\\rm{D  -  S}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Correct option is (3)',NULL,'Medium','Match the following',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44584,'physics',37,974,2767,'The r.m.s. current in an $AC$ circuit is 2$A$. If the wattless current be $\\sqrt 3 A$, what is the power factor?','[{"text": "$\\\\frac{1}{{\\\\sqrt 2 }}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{1}{{\\\\sqrt 3 }}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{1}{3}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{1}{2}$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Let $\\phi $ is the angle made by $V$ w.r.to $i$ <br><img src=\'/img/data/physics/37_44584_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' /> <br>The Wattless current is ${i_{rms}}\\sin \\phi $ <br>${i_{WL}} = {i_{rms}}\\sin \\phi \\Rightarrow \\sqrt 3 = 2\\sin \\phi \\Rightarrow \\sin \\phi = \\frac{{\\sqrt 3 }}{2}$ <br>$ \\Rightarrow \\theta = 60^\\circ \\Rightarrow \\cos \\phi = \\cos 60^\\circ = \\frac{1}{2}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44585,'physics',40,238,477,'An $\\alpha  - $ particle of energy $5\\,MeV$ is scattered through $180^\\circ $ by gold nucleus. The distance of closest approach is of the order of','[{"text": "${10^{ - 12}}cm$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${10^{ - 16}}cm$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${10^{ - 10}}cm$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${10^{ - 14}}cm$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'At the distance of closest approach $d$, initial kinetic energy of $\\alpha $ particle<br>$ = $ Potential energy of  $\\alpha $ particle and gold nucleus<br>i.e., $K = \\frac{1}{{4\\pi {\\varepsilon _0}}}\\frac{{\\left( {2e} \\right)\\left( {Ze} \\right)}}{d} = \\frac{{2Z{e^2}}}{{4\\pi {\\varepsilon _0}d}}$<br>${\\rm{or}},\\,\\,d = \\frac{{2Z{e^2}}}{{4\\pi {\\varepsilon _0}K}}$<br>Here, $K = 5MeV = 5 \\times 1.6 \\times {10^{ - 13}}J$<br>$\\left( {\\because \\,\\,\\,1\\,\\,MeV = 1.6 \\times {{10}^{ - 13}}J} \\right)$<br>For gold, $Z = 79$<br>$\\therefore \\,\\,d = \\frac{{(2)(9 \\times {{10}^9}\\,N{m^2}{C^{ - 2}})(79){{(1.6 \\times {{10}^{ - 19}}C)}^2}}}{{(5 \\times 1.6 \\times {{10}^{ - 13}}J)}}$<br>$ = 4.55 \\times {10^{ - 14}}m = 4.55 \\times {10^{ - 12}}cm$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',2015,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44586,'physics',39,1102,2628,'According to Einstein\'s photoelectric equation, the graph of $K.E$. of the photoelectron emitted from the metal versus the frequency of the incident radiation gives a straight line graph, whose slope','[{"text": "Depends on the intensity of the incident radiation", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Depends on the nature of the metal and also on the intensity of incident radiation", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Is same for all metals and independent of the intensity of the incident radiation", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Depends on the nature of the metal", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'According to Einstein\'s photoelectric equation<br>$K.{E_{\\max }} = hv - {\\phi _0}$<br>Where $h = $ Planck\'s constant<br>$$v=$$ frequency of incident light<br>$$\\phi_{0}=$$ work function of a metal<br>Compare it with the equation of straight line<br>$y = mx + c$<br>We get, slope of the graph $$=h$$<br>Hence slope is independent of nature of metal or incident radiation',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2010,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44587,'physics',38,1064,2537,'Which of the following statements is/are correct?<br>(I) Displacement current happens because of changing electric field.<br>(II) Displacement current happens because of motion of charged particles.','[{"text": "both (I) and (II) are correct", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "only (I) is correct", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "only (II) is correct", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "both (I) and (II) are incorrect", "isCorrect": false}]',1,NULL,NULL,'Easy','MCQ type-2',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44589,'physics',40,239,478,'Let ${T_1}$ and  ${T_2}$ be the energy of an electron in the first and second excited states of hydrogen atom, respectively. According to the Bohr’s model of an atom, the ratio ${T_1}:{T_2}$ is','[{"text": "$4:1$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$4:9$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$9:4$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$1:4$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'First excited state $ \\Rightarrow n = 2$<br>Note: If $n = 1$ we call it ground state<br>${T_1} =  - 13.6\\frac{{{z^2}}}{{{n^2}}} =  - \\frac{{13.6}}{4}eV$ (for ${n^{th}}$ state)<br>Second excited state $ \\Rightarrow n = 3$<br>${T_2} =  - 13.6\\frac{{{z^2}}}{{{n^2}}} =  - \\frac{{13.6}}{9}eV$<br>${T_1}:{T_2} = \\frac{1}{4}:\\frac{1}{9} = 9:4$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2022,'NEET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44590,'physics',39,1102,2629,'Which one of the following graphs represents the variation of maximum kinetic energy $$\\left(E_{k}\\right)$$ of the emitted electrons with frequency $$v$$ in photoelectric effect correctly?<div class="matching-question"></div>','[{"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'According to the equation of photoelectricity, $$\\quad E_{k}=h v-h v_{0}$$,<br>where $$v_{0}=$$ threshold frequency. The above relationship is valid for $$v \\geq h v_{0}$$. For $$v < v_{0}, E_{k}=0$$.<br>Hence (4) correctly represents the relationship between $$E_{k}$$ and $$v$$.',NULL,'Easy','Graph/Figure',2007,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44591,'physics',38,1064,2537,'A parallel plate condenser consists of two circular plates each of radius $2\\,cm$ separated by a distance of  $0.1\\,mm$. A time varying potential difference of $5 \\times {10^3}\\,V/s$ is applied across the plates of the condenser. The displacement current is','[{"text": "$5.56 \\\\times {10^{ - 5}}\\\\,A$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2.28 \\\\times {10^{ - 3}}\\\\,A$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$5.56 \\\\times {10^{ - 7}}\\\\,A$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$5.50\\\\,A$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'${I_d} = \\frac{{{\\varepsilon _0}A}}{d} \\times \\frac{{dV}}{{dt}}$<br>$ = \\frac{{8.85 \\times {{10}^{ - 12}} \\times \\pi  \\times 4 \\times {{10}^{ - 4}} \\times 5 \\times {{10}^3}}}{{1 \\times {{10}^{ - 4}}}}$<br>$ = 5.56 \\times {10^{ - 7}}A$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44592,'physics',37,974,2766,'In an \\[LCR\\] series circuit connected to an \\[ac\\] source, the supply voltage is<br>\\[V = {V_0}\\sin \\left( {100\\pi t + \\frac{\\pi }{6}} \\right) \\cdot {V_L} = 40V,\\]<br>\\[{V_R} = 40V,\\;Z = 5\\Omega \\;{\\mathop{\\rm and}\\nolimits} R = 4\\Omega \\] Then match the column I and II.<div class="matching-question"><div class="column-left">A. Peak current (in A)<br>B. ${V_0}$ (in volts)<br>C. ${X_L}({\\mathop{\\rm in}\\nolimits} \\,\\Omega )$<br>D. ${X_C}({\\mathop{\\rm in}\\nolimits} \\,\\Omega )$</div><div class="column-right">P. $10\\sqrt 2 $<br>Q. $50\\sqrt 2 $<br>R. $4$<br>S. $1$</div></div>','[{"text": "A - P,\\tB - Q,\\tC - R,\\tD - S", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "A - Q,\\tB - P,\\tC - R,\\tD - S", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "A - Q,     B - R, \\t  C - P,\\tD - S", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "A - S,     B - R,\\t  C - Q,\\tD - P", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Given that <br>${V_L} = 40V,\\,{V_R} = 40V,\\,\\,Z = 5\\Omega $<br>$R = 4\\Omega $<br>${i_{rms}} = \\frac{{40V}}{{4\\Omega }} = 100\\,amp$<br>${i_0} = 10\\sqrt 2 $<br>$ \\Rightarrow {V_0} = {i_0}Z = \\,\\left( {10\\sqrt 2 } \\right)5 = 50\\sqrt 2 $<br>$ \\Rightarrow {X_L} = \\frac{{{V_L}}}{{{i_{rms}}}} = \\frac{{40}}{{10}} = 4\\Omega $<br>$ \\Rightarrow {Z^2} = {\\left( {{X_L} - {X_C}} \\right)^2} + {R^2}$<br>${\\left( {4 - {X_C}} \\right)^2} = 25 - 16$<br>$4 - {X_C} = 3 \\Rightarrow {X_C} = 1\\Omega $<br>So option (1) is correct.',NULL,'Hard','Match the following',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/37_44592_question_1.png'),
(44593,'physics',39,1102,2626,'$A$ and $B$ are two metals with threshold frequencies $1.8 \\times {10^{14}}\\,Hz$ and $2.2 \\times {10^{14}}\\,Hz$.<br>Two identical photons of energy $0.825\\,eV$ each are incident on them. Then photoelectrons are emitted in<br>$({\\rm{Take}}\\,h = 6.6 \\times {10^{ - 34}}Js)$','[{"text": "$B$ alone", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$A$ alone", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Neither $A$ nor $B$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both $A$ and $B$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Minimum energy of photon needed for emission of electron $ = \\frac{{h{v_0}}}{e}eV$<br>$$\\therefore$$ For metal $A$, ${E_A} = \\frac{{\\left( {6.6 \\times {{10}^{ - 34}}} \\right) \\times \\left( {1.8 \\times {{10}^{14}}} \\right)}}{{1.6 \\times {{10}^{ - 19}}}} = 0.74\\,eV$<br>Similarly for metal $B$,<br>${E_B} = \\frac{{\\left( {6.6 \\times {{10}^{ - 34}}} \\right) \\times \\left( {2.2 \\times {{10}^{14}}} \\right)}}{{1.6 \\times {{10}^{ - 19}}}} = 0.91\\,eV$<br>Incident energy $0.825\\,eV$ is greater than ${E_A}$ $(0.74eV)$ but less than ${E_B}(0.91\\,eV)$<br>Hence, photoelectrons are emitted in $A$ alone',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2007,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44594,'physics',38,1064,2537,'An $ac$ source is connected to a capacitor $C$.<br>Due to decrease in its operating frequency:','[{"text": "displacement current increases", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "displacement current decreases", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "capacitive reactance remains constant", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "capacitive reactance decreases.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'$i_{C}=i_{D}=\\dfrac{V_{0}}{X_{C}} \\sin \\omega t$<br>$i_{C}=i_{D}=\\left(V_{0} \\omega C\\right) \\sin \\omega t$<br>$\\Rightarrow i_{C}=i_{D}=V_{0}(2 \\pi f) C \\sin \\omega t$<br>$\\Rightarrow i_{C}=i_{D}=V(2 \\pi f) C \\sin \\omega t$.<br>$\\Rightarrow$ If $f$ decreases, $i_{C}$ also decreases.<br>Correct option is (2).',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2023,'NEET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44595,'physics',40,238,477,'Rutherford’s atomic model could account for','[{"text": "Stability of atoms", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Origin of spectra", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "The positively charged central core of an atom", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Concept of stationary orbits", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'According to Rutherford’s atomic model the entire positive charge and most of the mass of the atom is concentrated in a small volume called the nucleus with electrons revolving around the nucleus just as planets revolve around the sun.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2011,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44596,'physics',37,976,2280,'An $LC$ circuit contains a 20 $mH$ inductor and a $50\\mu F$ capacitor with an initial charge of 10 $mC$. The resistance of the circuit is negligible. At the instant the circuit is closed be $t = 0$. At what time is the energy stored completely magnetic?','[{"text": "$t = 6.28ms$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$t = 1.54ms$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$t = 0$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$t = 3.14\\\\,ms$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'For $L - C$, oscillator,<br>$T = 2\\pi \\sqrt {LC} $<br>At time $t = \\frac{T}{4}$ energy stored is completely magnetic<br>$\\,t = \\frac{T}{4} = \\frac{{2\\pi \\sqrt {LC} }}{4}$<br>$ \\Rightarrow t = \\frac{{\\pi \\sqrt {20 \\times {{10}^{ - 3}} \\times 50 \\times {{10}^{ - 6}}} }}{2}$<br>$ = \\frac{{\\pi \\sqrt {1 \\times {{10}^{ - 6}}} }}{2} = 1.54\\,ms$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(47930,'biology',10,265,523,'In both 70s and 80s ribosomes &ldquo;s&rdquo; stands for','[{"text": "Sedimentation co-efficient", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Svedberg&rsquo;s unit", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both a and b", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "None of these", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'The eukaryotic ribosomes are 80s while prokaryotic ribosomes are 70s; here s (Svedberg unit) stands for Sedimentation coefficient.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44597,'physics',39,1102,2629,'When a piece of metal is illuminated by a monochromatic light of wavelength $$\\lambda$$, then stopping potential is $3\\,{V_s}$. When same surface is illuminated by light of wavelength $$2 \\lambda$$, then stopping potential becomes ${V_s}$. The value of threshold wavelength for photoelectric emission will be','[{"text": "$$(4 \\\\lambda) / 3$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$6 \\\\lambda$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$4 \\\\lambda$$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$$8 \\\\lambda$$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'From photoelectricity equation,<br>$\\frac{{hc}}{\\lambda } = \\frac{{hc}}{{{\\lambda _0}}} + e{V_s}$<br>where $$\\lambda=$$ wavelength of incident light,<br>$$\\lambda_{0}=$$ threshold wavelength<br>$$V_{s}=$$ stopping potential<br>For the given problem,<br>$\\frac{{hc}}{\\lambda } = \\frac{{hc}}{{{\\lambda _0}}} + e \\cdot 3\\,{V_s}\\quad {\\text{ }}(1){\\text{ and }}$<br>$\\frac{{hc}}{{2\\lambda }} = \\frac{{hc}}{{{\\lambda _0}}} + e \\cdot {V_s}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,(2)$<br>Eliminating ${V_S}$ from these two equations,<br>$$\\dfrac{h c}{\\lambda_{0}}=\\dfrac{h c}{4 \\lambda} \\Rightarrow \\lambda_{0}=4 \\lambda$$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2008,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44598,'physics',38,1065,2540,'In a plane electromagnetic wave travelling in free space, the elctric field component oscillates sinusoidally at a frequency of $2.0 \\times {10^{10}}\\,Hz$ and amplitude $48V{m^{ - 1}}$. Then the amplitude of oscillating magnetic field is: (Speed of light in free space $ = 3 \\times {10^8}\\,m{s^{ - 1}})$','[{"text": "$1.6 \\\\times {10^8}\\\\,T$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1.6 \\\\times {10^{ - 7}}\\\\,T$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$1.6 \\\\times {10^{ - 6}}\\\\,T$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1.6 \\\\times {10^{ - 9}}\\\\,T$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'$c = \\frac{{{E_0}}}{{{B_0}}}$<br>$ \\Rightarrow {B_0} = \\frac{{{E_0}}}{c} = \\frac{{48}}{{3 \\times {{10}^8}}} = 1.6 \\times {10^{ - 7}}\\,{\\rm{T}}$.<br>Correct option is (2).',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2023,'NEET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44599,'physics',37,976,2280,'The natural frequency of the circuit shown in the figure','[{"text": "$\\\\frac{1}{{2\\\\pi \\\\sqrt {LC} }}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{2}{{\\\\pi \\\\sqrt {LC} }}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{1}{{\\\\pi \\\\sqrt {LC} }}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "None of these", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'${L_{eq}} = L + L = 2L$<br>${C_{eq}} = \\frac{{{C_1}{C_2}}}{{{C_1} + {C_2}}} = \\frac{C}{2}$<br>$f = \\frac{1}{{2\\pi \\sqrt {{L_{eq}}{C_{eq}}} }} = \\frac{1}{{2\\pi \\sqrt {2L \\times \\frac{L}{2}} }} = \\frac{1}{{2\\pi \\sqrt {LC} }}$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/37_44599_question_1.png'),
(44600,'physics',40,239,478,'The ratio of kinetic energy to the total energy of an  electron in a Bohr orbit of the hydrogen atom 	is','[{"text": "$2: - 1$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1: - 2$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1: - 1$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$1:1$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'In Bohrs orbit<br>$KE = \\frac{{{e^2}}}{{8\\pi \\,{\\varepsilon _0}r}}$<br>$TE =  - \\frac{{{e^2}}}{{8\\pi \\,{\\varepsilon _0}r}}$<br>$\\frac{{KE}}{{TE}} = \\frac{1}{{ - 1}}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2018,'NEET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44601,'physics',39,1106,2633,'$$\\lambda_{1}$$ and $$\\lambda_{2}$$ are used to illuminate the slits. $$\\beta_{1}$$ and $$\\beta_{2}$$ are the corresponding fringe widths. The wavelength $$\\lambda_{1}$$ can produce photoelectric effect when incident on a metal. But the wavelength $$\\lambda_{2}$$ cannot produce photoelectric effect. The correct relation between $$\\beta_{1}$$ and $$\\beta_{2}$$ is','[{"text": "$$\\\\beta_{1} < \\\\beta_{2}$$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$$\\\\beta_{1}=\\\\beta_{2}$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$\\\\beta_{1}>\\\\beta_{2}$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$\\\\beta_{1} \\\\geq \\\\beta_{2}$$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Fringe width, $$\\beta=\\dfrac{\\lambda D}{d}$$<br>$$\\therefore \\beta \\propto \\lambda \\quad \\therefore \\dfrac{\\beta_{1}}{\\beta_{2}}=\\dfrac{\\lambda_{1}}{\\lambda_{2}}$$<br>$$\\lambda_{1}$$ can produce photoelectric effect and $$\\lambda_{2}$$ cannot produce photoelectric effect<br>$$\\Rightarrow \\lambda_{1} < \\lambda_{2} \\therefore \\beta_{1} < \\beta_{2}$$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',2013,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44602,'physics',38,1065,2540,'The ratio of the magnitudes of electric field to the magnetic field of an electromagnetic wave is of the order of','[{"text": "${10^{ - 5}}\\\\,m{s^{ - 1}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${10^8}\\\\,m{s^{ - 1}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${10^{ - 8}}\\\\,m{s^{ - 1}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${10^5}\\\\,m{s^{ - 1}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'$\\frac{{{E_{peak{\\rm{ }}}}}}{{{B_{peak{\\rm{ }}}}}} = c = 3 \\times {10^8}\\,m/s$ (in free space).<br>Correct option is (2).',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2023,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44603,'physics',37,977,2770,'The coefficient of mutual inductance between the primary and secondary coils of transformer is 5 $H$. A current of 10 $A$ is cut-off in 0.5 $s$. The induced $emf$ is','[{"text": "$10\\\\,V$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1\\\\,V$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$100\\\\,V$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$5\\\\,V$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'From Faraday’s law of electromagnetic induction,  the induced emf  is given by<br>$\\varepsilon  =  - M\\frac{{di}}{{dt}}$<br>Given,$M = 5H,di = 10A,dt = 0.5\\,s$<br>$\\therefore \\varepsilon  =  - 5 \\times \\frac{{10}}{{0.5}} =  - 100V$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44604,'physics',40,239,478,'The total energy of an electron in an atom in an orbit is $ - 3.4\\,eV$. Its kinetic and potential energies are, respectively:','[{"text": "$ - 3.4\\\\,eV, - 3.4\\\\,eV$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$ - 3.4\\\\,eV, - 6.8\\\\,eV$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$3.4\\\\,eV, - 6.8\\\\,eV$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$3.4\\\\,eV,3.4\\\\,eV$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'In Bohr’s model of $H$ atom<br>$\\therefore \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,K.E = \\left| {T \\cdot E} \\right| = \\frac{{\\left| U \\right|}}{2}$<br>Given $K.E. = 3.4\\,eV \\Rightarrow U =  - 6.8\\,eV$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2019,'NEET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44605,'physics',39,1102,2629,'The maximum kinetic energy of the photoelectrons depends only on','[{"text": "Incident angle", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Potential", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Pressure", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Frequency", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'using ${E_k} = hv - h{v_0},$ maximum kinetic energy of photoelctrons depends only on frequency.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2014,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44606,'physics',37,977,2770,'A step-down transformer has 50 turns on secondary and 1000 turns on primary winding. If a transformer is connected to 220 $V$, 1 $A$  $AC$ source, then what is output current of the transformer?','[{"text": "$\\\\frac{1}{{20}}A$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$20A$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$100A$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2A$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'We know that,<br>$\\frac{{{N_s}}}{{{N_p}}} = \\frac{{{V_s}}}{{{V_p}}}$<br>Given,${N_s} = 50,{N_p} = 1000$<br>${V_p} = 220V,{I_s} = ?$<br>$\\frac{{50}}{{1000}} = \\frac{{{V_s}}}{{220}} \\Rightarrow {V_s} = \\frac{{50 \\times 220}}{{1000}} = 11V$<br>But ${V_s}{I_s} = {V_p}{I_p} \\Rightarrow 11 \\times {I_s} = 220 \\times 1$<br>${I_s} = \\frac{{220}}{{11}} = 20A$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44607,'physics',38,1065,2541,'The pressure exerted by an electromagnetic wave of intensity, $I\\left( {W{m^{ - 2}}} \\right)$ on a non-reflecting surface is','[{"text": "$I c$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$I c^{2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$I / c$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$I / c^{2}$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'When a surface intercepts electromagnetic radiation, a force and a pressure are exerted on the surface. As the surface is non-reflecting, so it is completely absorbed and in such case the<br>force is, $F=\\dfrac{I A}{c}$<br>The radiation pressure is the force per unit area,<br>$p=\\dfrac{F}{A}=\\dfrac{I}{c}$<br>where, $c=$ speed of light',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,'AIIMS','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44608,'physics',39,1102,2629,'Light of two different frequencies whose photons have energies $1\\,eV$ and $2.5\\,eV$ respectively, successively illuminate a metallic surface whose work function is $0.5\\,eV$. Ratio of maximum speeds of emitted electrons will be','[{"text": "$$1: 4$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$1: 1$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$1: 5$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$1: 2$$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'According to Einstein\'s photoelectric equation, the maximum kinetic energy of emitted photoelectrons is<br>${K_{{\\text{max }}}} = hf - {\\phi _0}$<br>where $hf$ is the energy of incident photon and $$\\phi_{0}$$ is the work function<br>${\\text{ But }}{K_{{\\text{max }}}} = \\frac{1}{2}\\,m{v_{{\\text{ma}}{{\\text{x}}^{\\text{2}}}}}$<br>$\\therefore \\frac{1}{2}\\,mv_{\\max {\\text{ }}}^2 = hf - {\\phi _0}$<br>As per question<br>$\\frac{1}{2}\\,mv_{{\\text{ma}}{{\\text{x}}_{\\text{1}}}}^2 = 1\\,eV - 0.5\\,eV = 0.5\\,eV\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,(1)$<br>$\\frac{1}{2}\\,mv_{{\\text{ma}}{{\\text{x}}_{\\text{2}}}}^{\\text{2}} = 2.5eV - 0.5eV = 2eV\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,(2)$<br>Dividing eqn. (1) by eqn. (2), we get<br>$\\frac{{v_{{{\\max }_1}}^2}}{{v_{{{\\max }_2}}^2}} = \\frac{{0.5eV}}{{2eV}} = \\frac{1}{4}$<br>$\\frac{{{v_{{{\\max }_1}}}}}{{{v_{{{\\max }_2}}}}} = \\sqrt {\\frac{1}{4}}  = \\frac{1}{2}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2015,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44609,'physics',40,241,480,'For which one  of the following, Bohr model is not valid?','[{"text": "Singly ionized helium atom $(H{e^ + })$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Deuteron atom", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Singly ionized neon atom $(N{e^ + })$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Hydrogen atom", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Bohr model is applicable for only single electron species. <br>',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2020,'NEET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44610,'physics',38,1065,2542,'An electromagnetic wave passes through space and its equation is given by $E = {E_0}\\sin (\\omega t - kx)$  where $E$ is electric field. Energy density of electromagnetic wave in space is','[{"text": "$\\\\frac{1}{2}{\\\\varepsilon _0}E_0^2$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{1}{4}{\\\\varepsilon _0}E_0^2$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\varepsilon _0}E_0^2$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2{\\\\varepsilon _0}E_0^2$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Energy density  for the wave, <br>${u_E} = {\\varepsilon _0}E_{rms}^2 = {\\varepsilon _0}{\\left( {\\frac{{{E_0}}}{{\\sqrt 2 }}} \\right)^2} = \\frac{1}{2}{\\varepsilon _0}E_0^2$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2021,'MHTCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44611,'physics',37,972,2260,'The current \\[vs\\] time graph is shown in the figure. Find the average and \\[rms\\] currents<div class="matching-question"></div>','[{"text": "$\\\\frac{{{i_0}}}{2},\\\\frac{{{i_0}}}{{\\\\sqrt 2 }}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${i_0},\\\\frac{{{i_0}}}{2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${i_0},\\\\frac{{{i_0}}}{{\\\\sqrt 2 }}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{{{i_0}}}{2},{i_0}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'${i_{avg}} = \\frac{{\\int\\limits_0^T {i{\\mkern 1mu} dt} }}{T} = \\frac{1}{T}\\left[ {\\int\\limits_0^T {i{\\mkern 1mu} dt} } \\right]$<br>$ = \\frac{1}{T}\\left[ {\\int\\limits_0^{T/2} {i{\\mkern 1mu} dt + \\int\\limits_{T/2}^T {\\left( 0 \\right)dt} } } \\right]$<br>${i_{avg}} = \\frac{1}{T}\\int\\limits_0^{T/2} {idt}  = \\int\\limits_0^{T/2} {{i_0}dt = \\frac{{{i_0}}}{2}} $<br>$ < {i^2} > {\\mkern 1mu}  = {\\mkern 1mu} \\frac{{\\int\\limits_0^T {{i^2}{\\mkern 1mu} dt} }}{T} = \\frac{1}{T}\\left[ {\\int\\limits_0^T {{i^2}{\\mkern 1mu} dt} } \\right]$<br>$ = \\frac{1}{T}\\left[ {\\int\\limits_0^{T/2} {i_0^2{\\mkern 1mu} dt + \\int\\limits_{T/2}^T {\\left( 0 \\right)dt} } } \\right]$<br>$ < {i^2} > \\, = \\frac{{i_0^2}}{T}\\int\\limits_0^{T/2} {dt}  = \\frac{{i_0^2}}{2} \\Rightarrow {i_{rms}} = \\frac{{{i_0}}}{{\\sqrt 2 }}$',NULL,'Hard','Graph/Figure',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/37_44611_question_1.png'),
(44612,'physics',39,1102,2628,'The variation of photocurrent with collector potential for different frequencies of incident radiation. $$v_{1}, v_{2}$$ and $$v_{3}$$ is as shown in the graph, then<div class="matching-question"></div>','[{"text": "$$v_{1}=v_{2}=v_{3}$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$v_{1}>v_{2}>v_{3}$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$v_{1} < v_{2} < v_{3}$$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$$v_{3}=\\\\dfrac{v_{1}+v_{2}}{2}$$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'${\\rm{As}}\\,\\,e{V_s} = {K_{{\\rm{max}}}} = hv - {\\phi _0}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,(1)$<br>Saturation current is same. It means each source of radiation has same intensity. But corresponding stopping potential is different so they have different frequencies.<br>So, from eqn. (1) and given graph we conclude that ${v_1} < {v_2} < {v_3}\\quad \\left( {\\because \\left| {{{\\text{V}}_{01}}} \\right| < \\left| {{{\\text{V}}_{02}}} \\right| < \\left| {{{\\text{V}}_{03}}} \\right|} \\right)$',NULL,'Medium','Graph/Figure',2016,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/39_44612_question_1.png'),
(44613,'physics',37,973,2264,'A constant voltage at different frequencies is applied across a capacitance C as shown in the figure. Which of the following graphs depicts the variation of current with frequency?<div class="matching-question"></div>','[{"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'For capacitive circuits ${X_C} = \\frac{1}{{\\omega C}}$<br>$\\therefore I = \\frac{V}{{{X_C}}} = V\\omega C \\Rightarrow I \\propto \\omega $',NULL,'Medium','Graph/Figure',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/37_44613_question_1.png'),
(44614,'physics',38,1064,2537,'In order to establish an instantaneous displacement current of $1\\,mA$ in the space between the plates of $2\\,\\mu F$ parallel plate capacitor, the potential difference need to apply is:','[{"text": "$100\\\\,V{s^{ - 1}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$200\\\\,V{s^{ - 1}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$300\\\\,V{s^{ - 1}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$500\\\\,V{s^{ - 1}}$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'${i_d} = 1m(a)\\,\\,\\,{\\mkern 1mu} {\\kern 1pt} C = 2\\,\\mu F$<br>${i_d} = \\frac{{dq}}{{dt}} = \\frac{{\\Delta (cv)}}{{\\Delta t}}$<br>${i_d} = c\\frac{{\\Delta v}}{{\\Delta t}}or\\frac{{\\Delta v}}{{\\Delta t}} = \\frac{{{{10}^{ - 3}}}}{{2 \\times {{10}^{ - 6}}}} = 500\\,V{\\rm{/}}{s^{ - 1}}.$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44642,'physics',39,1104,2631,'The energy of an electron having de - Brogile wavelength $$\\lambda$$ is ( where $$h=$$ Planck\'s constant, $$\\mathrm{m}=$$ mass of electron)','[{"text": "$$\\\\dfrac{h}{2 m \\\\lambda}$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$\\\\dfrac{h^{2}}{2 m \\\\lambda^{2}}$$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$$\\\\dfrac{h^{2}}{2 m^{2} \\\\lambda^{2}}$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$\\\\dfrac{h^{2}}{2 m^{2} \\\\lambda}$$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'We know that<br>$$\\lambda=\\dfrac{h}{\\sqrt{2 m(K E)}}$$<br>on taking square on both sides, we get<br>${\\lambda ^2} = \\frac{{{h^2}}}{{2m(KE)}}$<br>$KE = \\frac{{{h^2}}}{{2m{\\lambda ^2}}}$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44615,'physics',40,240,479,'The ratio of wavelengths of the last line of Balmer series and the last line of Lyman series is:','[{"text": "$1$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$4$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$0.5$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'For last line of Balmer : ${n_1} = 2\\,\\,\\& \\,\\,{n_2} = \\infty $<br>$\\frac{1}{{{\\lambda _B}}} = R{Z^2}\\left[ {\\frac{1}{{n_1^2}} - \\frac{1}{{n_2^2}}} \\right] = R{(1)^2}\\left[ {\\frac{1}{{{2^2}}} - \\frac{1}{{{\\infty ^2}}}} \\right]$<br>${\\lambda _B} = \\frac{4}{R}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,(1)$<br>For last line of Lyman series : ${n_1} = 1\\,\\,\\& \\,\\,{n_2} = \\infty $<br>$\\frac{1}{{{\\lambda _L}}} = R{Z^2}\\left[ {\\frac{1}{{n_1^2}} - \\frac{1}{{n_2^2}}} \\right] = R{(1)^2}\\left[ {\\frac{1}{{{1^2}}} - \\frac{1}{{{\\infty ^2}}}} \\right]$<br>${\\lambda _L} = 1/R\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,(2)$<br>$\\frac{{{\\lambda _B}}}{{{\\lambda _L}}} = \\frac{{\\left( {4/R} \\right)}}{{(1/R)}} = 4$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2017,'NEET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44616,'physics',39,1102,2629,'The work function of a metal is $1\\,eV$. Light of wavelength $3000\\,\\mathop A\\limits^o $ is incident on this metal surface. The velocity of emitted photoelectrons will be','[{"text": "$10\\\\,m{s^{ - 1}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1 \\\\times {10^3}\\\\,m{s^{ - 1}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1 \\\\times {10^4}\\\\,m{s^{ - 1}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1 \\\\times {10^6}\\\\,m{s^{ - 1}}$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'From the photoelectricity equation,<br>$$\\dfrac{1}{2} m v^{2}=\\dfrac{h c}{\\lambda}-W_{0} \\Rightarrow v=\\sqrt{\\dfrac{2}{m}\\left(\\dfrac{h c}{\\lambda}-W_{0}\\right)}$$<br>Given, <br>$\\lambda  = 3000\\mathop A\\limits^o  = 3 \\times {10^{ - 7}}\\,m,{W_0} = 1eV = 1.6 \\times {10^{ - 9}}\\,J$<br>Also, $m = 9.11 \\times {10^{ - 31}}\\,kg$ and $hc = 1.99 \\times {10^{ - 25}}Jm$<br>$\\therefore \\quad v = \\sqrt {\\frac{2}{{9.11 \\times {{10}^{ - 31}}}} \\times \\left( \\begin{array}{l}\n\\frac{{1.99 \\times {{10}^{ - 25}}}}{{3 \\times {{10}^{ - 7}}}}\\\\\n - 1.6 \\times {10^{ - 19}}\n\\end{array} \\right)} m{s^{ - 1}}$<br>$ \\approx 1 \\times {10^6}\\,m{s^{ - 1}}.$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2021,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44617,'physics',37,973,2264,'The graph given below depict the dependence of two reactive impedances \\[{X_1}\\;and\\;{X_2}\\] on the frequency of the alternating e.m.f. applied individually to them. We can then say that<div class="matching-question"></div>','[{"text": "${X_1}$ is an inductor and ${X_2}$ is a capacitor", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${X_1}$ is a resistor and ${X_2}$  is a capacitor", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${X_1}$  is a capacitor and ${X_2}$ is an inductor", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${X_1}$ is an inductor and ${X_2}$ is a resistor", "isCorrect": false}]',2,NULL,NULL,'Medium','Graph/Figure',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/37_44617_question_1.png'),
(44618,'physics',38,1065,2539,'The electric field part of an electromagnetic wave in vacuum is $E=3.1 N C^{-1} \\cos [(1.8 \\mathrm{rad} /$ $\\left.\\left.m^{-1}\\right) y+\\left(5.4 \\times 10^{8} \\mathrm{rad} / \\mathrm{s}^{-1}\\right) t\\right] i$.<br>The wavelength of this part of electromagnetic wave is:','[{"text": "$1.5\\\\,m$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2\\\\,m$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2.5\\\\,m$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$3.5\\\\,m$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'$E=3.1 \\cos \\left[\\left(1.8 y+5.4 \\times 10^{8}\\right] i N / C\\right.$<br>Standard equation:<br>$\\begin{aligned}& E=E_{0} \\cos [k x-\\omega t] N / C \\\\& \\therefore k=\\dfrac{2 \\pi}{\\lambda}=1.8 \\\\& \\lambda=\\dfrac{2 \\pi}{1.8}=3.49 \\simeq 3.5 .\\end{aligned}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44619,'physics',40,240,479,'Given the value of Rydberg constant is ${10^7}{m^{ - 1}},$ the wave number of the last line of the Balmer series in hydrogen spectrum will be:','[{"text": "$0.025 \\\\times {10^4}{m^{ - 1}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$0.5 \\\\times {10^7}{m^{ - 1}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$0.25 \\\\times {10^7}{m^{ - 1}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$2.5 \\\\times {10^7}{m^{ - 1}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'$\\frac{1}{\\lambda } = R{Z^2}\\left( {\\frac{1}{{n_2^2}} - \\frac{1}{{n_1^2}}} \\right) = {10^7} \\times {1^2}\\left( {\\frac{1}{{{2^2}}} - \\frac{1}{{{\\infty ^2}}}} \\right)$<br>$ \\Rightarrow \\frac{1}{\\lambda } = 0.25 \\times {10^7}{m^{ - 1}}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2016,'NEET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44620,'physics',39,1102,2629,'The maximum velocity of the photoelectron emitted by the metal surface is $v$. Charge and mass of the photoelectron is denoted by $e$ and $m$ respectively. The stopping potential in volt is','[{"text": "$$\\\\dfrac{v^{2}}{2\\\\left(\\\\dfrac{e}{m}\\\\right)}$$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$$\\\\dfrac{v^{2}}{\\\\left(\\\\dfrac{m}{e}\\\\right)}$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$\\\\dfrac{v^{2}}{2\\\\left(\\\\dfrac{m}{e}\\\\right)}$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$\\\\dfrac{v^{2}}{\\\\left(\\\\dfrac{e}{m}\\\\right)}$$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'At stopping potential, the maximum velocity of emitted photoelectron from metal surface is given by<br>$$K_{\\max }=\\dfrac{1}{2} m v^{2}=e v_{0}$$<br>$$\\therefore$$ Stopping potential, $$v_{0}=\\dfrac{m v^{2}}{2 e}=\\dfrac{v^{2}}{2\\left(\\dfrac{e}{m}\\right)}$$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2020,'MHTCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44621,'physics',37,973,2268,'The figure shows variation of \\[R\\], \\[\\;{X_L}\\;and\\;{X_C}\\] with frequency \\[f\\] in a series \\[L\\], \\[C\\], \\[R\\] circuit. Then for what frequency point, the circuit is inductive?<div class="matching-question"></div>','[{"text": "$A$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$B$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$C$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{All}}\\\\,{\\\\rm{points}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'At $A:{X_C} > {X_L}$<br>At $B:{X_C} = {X_L}$<br>At $C:{X_C} < {X_L}$',NULL,'Medium','Graph/Figure',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/37_44621_question_1.png'),
(44622,'physics',38,1066,2543,'Which rays are not is the portion of $e m$ waves?','[{"text": "$X$-rays", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Microwaves", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\alpha$-rays", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Radio-waves", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'$\\alpha$-rays is nothing but nucleus of helium moving at higher speed.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44623,'physics',39,1102,2629,'On a photo sensitive material, when frequency of incident radiation is increased by $$30 \\%$$, kinetic energy of emitted photoelectrons increases from $0.4\\,eV$ to $0.9\\,eV$. The work function of the surface is','[{"text": "$1\\\\,eV$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1.267\\\\,eV$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$1.4\\\\,eV$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1.8\\\\,eV$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'According to the Einstein\'s equation<br>$$K E_{\\max }=h v_{0}-\\phi_{0}$$<br>Where $$K E_{\\max }=$$ maximum kinetic energy and<br>$$\\phi_{0}=\\text { work function }$$<br>Initially $h{v_0} = 0.4 + {\\phi _0}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,(1)$<br>When the frequency of incident radiation os increased by $$30 \\%$$, then<br>$1.3hv = 0.9 + {\\phi _0}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,(2)$<br>Solving eq.(1) and eq.(2), we get<br>$0.3{\\phi _0} = 0.9 - 1.3(0.4)$<br>$\\therefore {\\phi _0} = \\frac{{0.38}}{{0.3}} = 1.267eV$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2017,'MHTCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(48315,'biology',101,573,1152,'Present increase in India\'s population has not been due to decline in','[{"text": "Infant mortality rate", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Number of people reaching reproductive age", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Death rate", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Maternal mortality rate", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Present increase in India\'s population has not been due to decline in Number of people reaching reproductive age.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44624,'physics',37,975,2277,'Which one of the following curves represents the variation of impedance (\\[Z\\]) with frequency f in series \\[LCR\\] circuit?<div class="matching-question"></div>','[{"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'$Z = \\sqrt {{R^3} + {{\\left( {2\\pi fL - \\frac{1}{{2\\pi fC}}} \\right)}^2}} $<br>From the above equation at $f = 0 \\Rightarrow z = \\infty $<br>When $f = \\frac{1}{{2\\pi \\sqrt {LC} }}$ (resonant frequency)<br>$ \\Rightarrow Z = R$<br>For $f > \\frac{1}{{2\\pi \\sqrt {LC} }} \\Rightarrow Z$ starts increasing.i.e., for frequency $0 - {f_r},Z$ decreases and for ${f_r}to\\,\\infty ,Z$ increases.',NULL,'Medium','Graph/Figure',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44625,'physics',40,238,477,'If an electron in a hydrogen atom jumps from the 3$rd$ orbit to the 2$nd$ orbit, it emits a photon of wavelength $(\\lambda )$. When it jumps from the 4$th$ orbit to the 3$rd$ orbit, the corresponding wavelength of the photon will be','[{"text": "$\\\\frac{{16}}{{25}}\\\\lambda $", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{9}{{16}}\\\\lambda $", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{{20}}{7}\\\\lambda $", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{{20}}{{13}}\\\\lambda $", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'$\\frac{1}{\\lambda } = \\left( R \\right)\\left( {\\frac{1}{{{2^2}}} - \\frac{1}{{{3^2}}}} \\right) \\Rightarrow \\frac{1}{\\lambda } = R\\left( {\\frac{5}{{36}}} \\right)$<br>$\\therefore \\frac{1}{{\\lambda \'}} = R\\left( {\\frac{1}{9} - \\frac{1}{{16}}} \\right) = R\\frac{7}{{9 \\times 16}}$<br>$\\therefore \\lambda \' = \\lambda \\frac{{5 \\times 9 \\times 16}}{{36 \\times 7}} \\Rightarrow \\lambda \' = \\frac{{20}}{7}\\lambda $',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2016,'NEET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44626,'physics',38,1066,2543,'Red light differes from blue light in its-','[{"text": "Speed", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Frequency", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Intensity", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Amplitude", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Red and blue lights differ in their frequency and wavelength.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44627,'physics',39,1102,2629,'A $5\\,W$ source emits monochromatic light of wavelength $5000\\mathop A\\limits^o $. When placed $0.5\\,m$ away, it liberates photoelectrons from a photosensitive metallic surface. When the source is moved to a distance of $1.0\\,m$, the number of photoelectrons liberated will be reduced by a factor of','[{"text": "8", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "16", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "2", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "4", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Intensity of light is inversely proportional to square of distance of source<br>$$\\text { i.e., } \\quad I \\propto \\dfrac{1}{r^{2}}$$<br>For two different situations,<br>$$\\dfrac{I_{2}}{I_{1}}=\\dfrac{\\left(r_{1}\\right)^{2}}{\\left(r_{2}\\right)^{2}}$$<br>Given, $\\quad {r_1} = 0.5\\,m,\\quad {r_2} = 1.0\\,m$<br>Therefore,<br>$$\\dfrac{I_{2}}{I_{1}}=\\dfrac{(0.5)^{2}}{(1)^{2}}=\\dfrac{1}{4}$$<br>Since, number of photoelectrons emitted per second is directly proportional to intensity, so number of electrons emitted would decrease by factor of 4 .',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44628,'physics',37,972,2261,'If the rms current in a 50 $Hz$ $AC$ circuit is 5 $A$, the value of the current 1/300 seconds after its value becomes zero is','[{"text": "$5\\\\sqrt {3/2} \\\\,{\\\\rm{A}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$5\\\\sqrt 2 \\\\,{\\\\rm{A}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$5/\\\\sqrt 2 \\\\,{\\\\rm{A}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$5/6\\\\,{\\\\rm{A}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Here,$\\upsilon  = 50Hz,{I_{rms}} = 5A,I = ?$<br>${I_0} = 5\\sqrt 2 {\\kern 1pt} A$<br>From  $I = {I_0}\\sin \\omega {\\kern 1pt} t$<br>$ = 5\\sqrt 2 \\sin 100\\,\\pi  \\times \\frac{1}{{300}}$<br>$ = 5\\sqrt 2 \\sin \\frac{\\pi }{3} = 5\\sqrt 2  \\times \\frac{{\\sqrt 3 }}{2} = 5\\sqrt {\\frac{3}{2}} \\,{\\rm{A}}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,'NCERT Exemplar','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44629,'physics',38,1065,2539,'A beam of light travelling along $x$-axis is described by magnetic field<br>$B_{z}=5 \\times 10^{-9} T \\sin \\omega(t-Y / C) .$<br>What is the maximum electric and magnetic forces on a charge $i.e$., $\\alpha$-particle moving along $y$-axis with a speed of $3 \\times {10^8}\\,m{\\rm{/}}s$. (charge on electron $ = 1.6 \\times {10^{ - 19}}C$).','[{"text": "$4.80 \\\\times {10^{ - 17}}\\\\,N,4.80 \\\\times {10^{ - 17}}\\\\,N$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$4.85 \\\\times {10^{ - 16}}\\\\,N,5.6 \\\\times {10^{ - 17}}\\\\,N$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$9.2 \\\\times {10^6}\\\\,N,9.2 \\\\times {10^3}\\\\,N$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$4.8 \\\\times {10^{ - 19}}\\\\,N,4.8 \\\\times {10^{ - 19}}\\\\,N$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'$q=$ Charge on $\\alpha$-particle $(q)=2 \\times e$.<br>Maximum force on $\\alpha$-particle due to electric field<br>$F_{E}=q \\times E_{0} .$<br>$=3.2 \\times 10^{-19} \\times 1.5$<br>${F_E} = 4.80 \\times {10^{ - 19}}\\,N$<br>Maximum force on $\\alpha$ - particle due to magnetic field $F_{B}=q v B_{0}$<br>$ = 3.2 \\times {10^{ - 19}} \\times 3 \\times {10^8} \\times {10^{ - 9}} \\times 5$<br>${F_B} = 4.80 \\times {10^{ - 19}}\\,N.$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44630,'physics',40,238,477,'When an $\\alpha  - $ particle of mass $\'m\'$ moving with velocity $\'v\'$ bombards on a heavy nucleus of charge $\'Ze\'$, its distance of closest approach from the nucleus depends on $m$ as:','[{"text": "$\\\\frac{1}{m}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{1}{{\\\\sqrt m }}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{1}{{{m^2}}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$m$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'At closest distance of approach, the kinetic energy of the particle will convert completely into electrostatic potential energy.<br>$ \\Rightarrow \\frac{1}{2}m{v^2} = \\frac{{KQq}}{d} \\Rightarrow d \\propto \\frac{1}{m}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2016,'NEET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44631,'physics',39,1102,2627,'The number of photoelectrons emitted for light of a frequency $$v$$ (higher than the threshold frequency $$v_{0}$$ ) is proportional to','[{"text": "Intensity of light", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Threshold frequency $$\\\\left(v_{0}\\\\right)$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Frequency of light", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$v-v_{0}$$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'If intensity increases, number of photoelectrons increases.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44632,'physics',37,975,2278,'Obtain the resonant frequency $\\omega $ of a series $L - C - R$ circuit with $L = 2.0\\,H,C = 32\\mu F$  and $R = 10\\Omega $ What is the Q-value of this circuit?','[{"text": "$23$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$17$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$25$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$18$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Given $L = 2H,C = 32\\mu F,R = 10\\,\\Omega .$<br>Resonant angular frequency <br>${\\omega _r} = \\frac{1}{{\\sqrt {LC} }} = \\frac{1}{{\\sqrt {2 \\times 32 \\times {{10}^{ - 6}}} }} = 125\\,{\\rm{rad/s}}$<br>$Q$-factor of this circuit,<br>$Q = \\frac{1}{R}\\sqrt {\\frac{L}{C}}  = \\frac{1}{{10}}\\sqrt {\\frac{2}{{32 \\times {{10}^{ - 6}}}}}  = \\frac{{{{10}^3}}}{{40}} = 25$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,'NCERT Exemplar','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(46002,'physics',41,406,809,'Which of the following is a correct statement?','[{"text": "Gamma rays are high energy neutrons", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Beta rays are same as cathode rays", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Protons and neutrons are singly ionized helium atoms", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Alpha particles are singly ionized helium atoms", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Both the $\\beta  - $rays and the cathode rays are electrons.$\\gamma  - $ rays are $EM$ waves, $\\alpha  - $ particles are doubly ionized helium atoms.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44633,'physics',38,1065,2539,'A plane electromagnetic wave of frequency $25 \\mathrm{MHz}$ travel in free space along the $x$ direction. At a particular point in space and time, the electric vector is $\\vec{E}=6.3 \\mathrm{~V} / \\mathrm{m} \\hat{j}$. What is the $\\vec{B}$ at this point?','[{"text": "$2 \\\\times {10^{ - 8}}\\\\hat k$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2.1 \\\\times {10^{ - 8}}\\\\hat k\\\\,T$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$2.1 \\\\times {10^{ - 8}}\\\\hat J\\\\,T$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2 \\\\times {10^{ - 8}}\\\\hat J\\\\,T$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'As we know<br>$B = \\frac{E}{C} = \\frac{{6.3}}{{3 \\times {{10}^8}}} = 2.1 \\times {10^{ - 8}}\\,T$<br>As $\\vec{E}$ is along $y$-direction and wave is travelling along $x$-direction, therefore $\\vec{B}$ is along $z$ direction because in propagation of $e m$ wave $E\\,\\,\\& \\,\\,B$ fields are $\\perp$ to each other.<br>$\\vec B = 2.1 \\times {10^{ - 8}}\\hat k\\,T$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44634,'physics',40,240,479,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> The electron in the hydrogen atom passes from energy level $n = 4$ to the $n = 1$ level. The maximum number of photons that can be emitted are six.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> The photons are emitted when electrons make a transition from the higher energy state to the lower energy state.','[{"text": "Both assertion and reason are correct and reason is the correct explanation of assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both assertion and reason are correct but reason is not the correct explanation of assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Max. number of spectral  lines $ = \\frac{{n(n - 1)}}{2}$<br>$ = \\frac{{4(4 - 1)}}{2} = 6$.  So option (2) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44635,'physics',39,1104,2631,'The de - Brogile wavelength $$\\lambda$$ of a particle','[{"text": "Is proportional to mass", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Is proportional to impulse", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Is inversely proportional to impulse", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Does not depend on impule", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'de - Brogile wavelength $$(\\lambda)$$ of a particle is related to its momentum ($p$) as $$\\lambda=\\dfrac{h}{p}$$<br>where, $h$ is Planck\'s constant<br>${\\text{ or }}\\lambda  = \\frac{h}{{mv}}\\quad [\\because p = mv\\} $<br>$\\quad \\lambda  = \\frac{h}{{m\\frac{v}{t}.t}} = \\frac{h}{{ma \\cdot t}} = \\frac{h}{{F \\cdot t}}$<br>$[\\because F = ma]$<br>$(\\lambda )$ is inversely proportional to impulse $$(F)$$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44636,'physics',37,976,2280,'A radio can tune over the frequency range of a portion of  MW broadcast bond; (800 $kHz$ to 1200 $kHz$). If its $LC$ circuit has an effective inductance of $220\\,\\mu H$,  what must be the range  of its variable capacitor? <br>[Hint: For tuning the natural frequency i.e, the frequency of free oscillations of the $LC$ circuit should be equal to the frequency of the radiowave.]','[{"text": "$44\\\\,{\\\\mathop{\\\\rm to}\\\\nolimits} \\\\,208\\\\,{\\\\mathop{\\\\rm pF}\\\\nolimits} $", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$63\\\\,{\\\\mathop{\\\\rm to}\\\\nolimits} \\\\,168\\\\,{\\\\mathop{\\\\rm pF}\\\\nolimits} $", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$87.8\\\\,{\\\\mathop{\\\\rm to}\\\\nolimits} \\\\,198\\\\,{\\\\mathop{\\\\rm pF}\\\\nolimits} $", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$99\\\\,{\\\\mathop{\\\\rm to}\\\\nolimits} \\\\,190\\\\,{\\\\mathop{\\\\rm pF}\\\\nolimits} $", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Given, minimum frequency<br>${f_1} = 800\\,kHz = 8 \\times {10^5}Hz$<br>Inductance $L = 200\\,\\mu H = 2 \\times {10^{ - 4}}H$<br>Maximum frequency ${f_2} = 1200\\,kHz = 12 \\times {10^5}Hz$<br>For tuning, the natural frequency is equal to the frequency of oscillations that means it is the case of resonance.<br>Fequency of oscillations $f = \\frac{1}{{2\\pi \\sqrt {LC} }}$<br>For capacitance ${C_1},{f_1} = \\frac{1}{{2\\pi \\sqrt {L{C_1}} }}$<br>${C_1} = \\frac{1}{{4{\\pi ^2}{f_1}^2L}} = 197.7pF$<br>For capacitance ${C_2},{f_2} = \\frac{1}{{2\\pi \\sqrt {L{C_2}} }}$<br>${C_2} = \\frac{1}{{4{\\pi ^2}{f_2}^2L}} = 87.8\\,pF$<br>Thus, the range of capacitor is 87.8 $pF$ to 197.7 $pF$.',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,'NCERT Exemplar','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44637,'physics',38,1065,2539,'The speed of electromagnetic wave in a medium of dielectric constant 2.25 and relative permeability 4 is:','[{"text": "${10^8}\\\\,m{\\\\rm{/}}s$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$2.5 \\\\times {10^8}\\\\,m{\\\\rm{/}}s$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2 \\\\times {10^8}\\\\,m{\\\\rm{/}}s$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$3 \\\\times {10^8}\\\\,m{\\\\rm{/}}s$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'The speed of electromagnetic wave in a medium is-<br>$v = \\frac{1}{{\\sqrt {\\mu \\varepsilon } }} = \\frac{1}{{\\sqrt {{\\mu _0}{\\varepsilon _0}{\\mu _r}{\\varepsilon _r}} }} = \\frac{c}{{\\sqrt {{\\mu _r}{\\varepsilon _r}} }}$<br>$v = \\frac{{3 \\times {{10}^8}}}{{\\sqrt {4 \\times 2.25} }} = 1 \\times {10^8}\\,m{\\rm{/}}s.$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44638,'physics',39,1106,2633,'G.P. Thomson experimentally confirmed the existence of matter waves by the phenomena','[{"text": "Diffraction", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Refraction", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Polarisation", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Scattering", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'$G.P$ Thomson experimentally confirmed the existence of matter waves (de Broglie\'s hypothesis) by demonstrating that electron beams are diffracted when they pass through thin metallic foils.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44639,'physics',37,975,2275,'Which of the following combinations should be selected for better tuning of an $L-C-R$ circuit used for communication ?','[{"text": "$R = 25\\\\,\\\\Omega ,L = 2.5\\\\,H,C = 45\\\\,\\\\mu F$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$R = 20\\\\Omega ,L = 1.5\\\\,H,C = 35\\\\,\\\\mu F$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$R = 25\\\\Omega ,L = 1.5\\\\,H,C = 45\\\\,\\\\mu F$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$R = 15\\\\Omega ,L = 3.5\\\\,H,C = 30\\\\,\\\\mu F$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'For better tuning the resonance curve should be sharp. When $Q$-factor is large, the sharpness of resonance curve is more and vice-verse.<br>$Q = \\frac{1}{R}\\sqrt {\\frac{L}{C}} $<br>For large value of $Q$, $R$ should be small, $L$ should be high and $C$ should be low. These conditions are best satisfied by the values in option (4)',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,'NCERT Exemplar','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44640,'physics',40,245,487,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ particles are accelerated through same potential difference. Finally both particles have same linear momentum.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Linear momentum $ = \\sqrt {KE \\times 2 \\times mass} $','[{"text": "Both assertion and reason are correct and reason is the correct explanation of assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both assertion and reason are correct but reason is not the correct explanation of assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but reason is correct.", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'From energy conservation <br>$q\\Delta V = \\frac{1}{2}m{v^2} = \\frac{{{p^2}}}{{2m}}$<br>$ \\Rightarrow p = \\sqrt {(q\\Delta V)2m}  = \\sqrt {K.E \\times 2m} $<br>$ \\Rightarrow {p_\\alpha } > {p_\\beta }$<br>Assertion is wrong. Reason is correct. Option (4) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44643,'physics',37,975,2275,'To reduce the resonant frequency in an $L-C-R$ series circuit with a generator','[{"text": "The iron core of the inductor should be removed", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Dielectric in the capacitor should be removed", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "The generator frequency should be reduced", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Another capacitor should be added in parallel to the first", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Reasonant frequency in an $L - C - R$ series circuit is <br>${\\upsilon _r} = \\frac{1}{{2\\pi \\sqrt {LC} }}$<br>To reduce ${\\upsilon _r};C$ can be increased, by adding another capacitor in parallel to the first capacitor',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,'NCERT Exemplar','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44644,'physics',38,1065,2542,'The magnetic field of a beam emerging from a filter facing a flood light is given by $B=12 \\times 10^{-8} \\sin \\left(1.20 \\times 10^{7} z-3.60 \\times 10^{15} t\\right) T$. The average intensity of the beam is:','[{"text": "$1.71\\\\,W{m^{ - 2}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$2.1\\\\,W{m^{ - 2}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$3.2\\\\,W{m^{ - 2}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2.9\\\\,W{m^{ - 2}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'$I_{a v}=\\dfrac{1}{2} \\dfrac{B_{0}^{2} c}{\\mu_{0}}$<br>$ = \\frac{1}{2} \\times \\frac{{\\left( {12 \\times {{10}^{ - 8}}} \\right) \\times 3 \\times {{10}^8}}}{{4\\pi  \\times {{10}^{ - 7}}}}$<br>$ = 1.71\\,W{m^{ - 2}}.$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44645,'physics',40,238,477,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Large angle of scattering of alpha particles led to the discovery of atomic nucleus.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Entire positive charge of atom is 	concentrated in the central core.','[{"text": "Both assertion and reason are correct and reason is the correct explanation of assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both assertion and reason are correct but reason is not the correct explanation of assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'$\\alpha  - $ particle is positively charged, so is the nucleus, so the large angle of scattering of $\\alpha  - $ particle shows that the nucleus is positively charged and concentrated in the central core. So option (1) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44646,'physics',37,977,2770,'The output of a step-down transformer is measured to be 24 $V$ when connected to a 12 $W$ light bulb. The value of the peak current is','[{"text": "$\\\\sqrt 2 \\\\,A$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{1}{{\\\\sqrt 2 \\\\,}}A$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$2\\\\sqrt 2 \\\\,A$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2\\\\,A$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Secondary voltage ${V_S} = 24V$<br>Power associated with secondary ${P_S} = 12\\,W$<br>${I_S} = \\frac{{{P_S}}}{{{V_S}}} = \\frac{{12}}{{24}} = \\frac{1}{2}A$<br>Peak value of the current is<br>${I_0} = {I_S}\\sqrt 2  = \\frac{1}{{\\sqrt 2 }}A$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,'NCERT Exemplar','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44647,'physics',39,1104,2631,'The de Broglie wavelength associated with a particle of mass $m$, moving with a velocity $v$ and energy $E$ is given by','[{"text": "$$h / m v^{2}$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$m v / h^{2}$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$h / \\\\sqrt{2 m E}$$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$$\\\\sqrt{2 m E} / h^{2}$$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'$\\lambda  = \\frac{h}{P} = \\frac{h}{{\\sqrt {2mE} }}$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2015,'MHTCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44648,'physics',38,1065,2539,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> The electromagnetic waves of shorter wavelength can travel longer distance on earth\' surface than those of longer wavelengths.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Shorter the wavelength, shorter is the velocity of electromagnetic waves propagation.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Short $\\lambda$ waves have low attenuation as they have high frequency and so high energy. So they travel long distances. Reason is false as all electromagnetic waves haves same $v=c$. So correct option is (3).',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44650,'physics',37,973,2264,'I. When a capacitor is connected to a voltage source in a $DC$ circuit, current will flow for the short time required to charge the capacitor.<br>II. A capacitor in a $DC$ circuit will limit or oppose the current as it charges.<br>III. When the capacitor is fully charged, the current in the circuit falls to zero.<br>Which of the above statements are incorrect? Choose the correct option.','[{"text": "I, II and III", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "II and III", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "I and II", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "None of these", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'When a capacitor is connected to a voltage source in a $DC$ circuit, current will flow for the short time required to charge the capacitor. As charge accumulates on the capacitor plates, the voltage across them increases, opposing the current. That is, a capacitor in a $DC$ circuit will limit or oppose the current as it charges. When the capacitor is fully charged, the current in the circuit falls to zero. Option (4) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ type-2',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44651,'physics',39,1104,2631,'What is the de Broglie wavelength of the electron accelerated through a potential difference of 100 $volt$?','[{"text": "$0.1227\\\\mathop A\\\\limits^o $", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$12.27\\\\mathop A\\\\limits^o $", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$0.001227\\\\mathop A\\\\limits^o $", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1.227\\\\mathop A\\\\limits^o $", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'de Broglie wavelength of the electron accelerated through a potential difference of $V$ volt is<br>$\\lambda  = \\frac{{12.27}}{{\\sqrt V }}\\mathop A\\limits^o $<br>For $V = 100\\,volt$<br>$\\lambda  = \\frac{{12.27}}{{\\sqrt {100} }}\\mathop A\\limits^o  = 1.227\\mathop A\\limits^o $',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2014,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(46004,'physics',42,323,593,'The current flow in a Zener diode is mainly due to','[{"text": "Thermally generated charge carriers", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Minority charge carriers", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Direct rupture of covalent bonds", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Ions", "isCorrect": false}]',2,NULL,NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44652,'physics',38,1067,2544,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> The surface (ground) wave propagation is used for radio broadcasting.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> The surface waves travel directly from transmitting antenna to receiver antenna through atmosphere.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Surface wave propagation is favored in medium-wave radio ($500\\,KHz$ to $1500\\,KHz$) broadcasting for reliable, quality coverage over long distances, near the Earth\'s curvature, making it cost-effective and practical for urban and regional broadcasting. So correct option is (1).',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44653,'physics',37,977,2281,'Consider the statements.<br>I. Most of the electrical devices we use require $AC$ voltage.<br>II. Most of the electrical energy sold by power companies is transmitted and distributed as alternating current<br>III. $AC$ voltages can be easily and efficiently converted from one voltage to the other by means of transformers.','[{"text": "I is correct, II and III are incorrect", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "I, III are correct, II is incorrect", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "I, II are correct, III is incorrect", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "I, II and III are correct", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Option (4) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ type-2',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44654,'physics',39,1104,2631,'If an electron and a proton have the same de-Broglie wavelength, then the kinetic energy of the electron is','[{"text": "More than that of a proton", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Equal to that of a proton", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Zero", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Less than that of a proton.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Using $$E=\\dfrac{p^{2}}{2 m}$$ and $$\\lambda=\\dfrac{h}{p}$$,<br>$$\\begin{gathered}\\quad E=\\dfrac{h^{2}}{2 m \\lambda^{2}} \\\\\\therefore \\text { For same } \\lambda, \\dfrac{E_{e}}{E_{p}}=\\dfrac{m_{p}}{m_{e}},\\end{gathered}$$<br>As ${m_p} >  > {m_e},{E_e} >  > {E_p}$.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2008,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44656,'physics',38,1067,2544,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> The television broadcasting becomes weaker with increasing distance.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> The power transmitted from $T V$ transmitter varies inversely as the distance of the receiver','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Power $\\propto \\dfrac{1}{r^{2}}\\left(\\operatorname{not} \\dfrac{1}{r}\\right)$<br>$T V$ signals weaken with distance following the inverse square law, diminishing in strength. As they spread over a larger area. additional transmitters or repeaters will be needed to extend coverage. So correct option is (3).',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44657,'physics',37,973,2264,'<div class="matching-question"><div class="column-left">A capacitor is connected to a 	direct current source. Its reactance is infinite</div><div class="column-right">Reactance of a capacitor is given by<br>${X_C} = \\frac{1}{{\\omega C}}$</div></div>','[{"text": "Statement A is correct but Statement B is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Statement A is incorrect but Statement B is correct.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Statements are correct.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Statements are incorrect.", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'As ${X_C} = \\frac{1}{{\\omega C}},$ so far<br>$\\omega  = 0,\\,{X_C} \\to \\infty $',NULL,'Medium','MCQ type-2',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44658,'physics',39,1104,2631,'A proton and an alpha particle are accelerated under the same potential difference. The ratio of de - Broglie wavelengths of the proton and the alpha particle is','[{"text": "$$\\\\sqrt{8}$$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$$\\\\dfrac{1}{\\\\sqrt{8}}$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "1", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "2", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'When a charged particle of charge $$q$$ and mass $$m$$ is accelerated under a potential difference $V$, let $$v$$ be velocity acquired by the particle<br>Then<br>$qV = \\frac{1}{2}\\,m{v^2} \\Rightarrow mv = \\sqrt {2mqV} $<br>$$\\therefore$$ de Broglie wavlength $\\lambda  = \\frac{h}{{mv}} = \\frac{h}{{\\sqrt {2mqV} }}$<br>$\\lambda  \\propto \\frac{1}{{\\sqrt {mq} }}$ for the same value of $V$<br>$\\therefore \\frac{{{\\lambda _p}}}{{{\\lambda _\\alpha }}} = \\frac{{\\sqrt {{m_\\alpha }{q_\\alpha }} }}{{\\sqrt {{m_p}{q_p}} }} = \\sqrt {\\frac{{4\\,m}}{{\\,m}} \\times \\frac{{2e}}{e}}  = \\sqrt 8 $',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2012,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44659,'physics',38,1067,2544,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Propagation of electromagnetic waves (of $2\\,MHz$ to $30\\,MHz$) is done by Sky wave method.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> These waves are reflected back to earth by ionosphere.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Ionosphere her its refrective index $(n)$ given by $n=n_{o}\\left[\\dfrac{1-(80.5 \\mathrm{Ne})}{f^{2}}\\right]^{1 / 2}$ where $n_{0}=$ that of free space. here, $N_{e}$ is electron - density which increases as these waves be deep into ionosphere. So correct option is (1).',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(48316,'biology',101,573,1152,'The marriageable age of female must be _______years and that of male must be _____ years respectively.','[{"text": "16,18", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "18,20", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "18,21", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "17,22", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'The marriageable age of female must be 18 years and of male must be 21 years.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44661,'physics',37,977,2281,'<div class="matching-question"><div class="column-left">A laminated core is used in transformers to increase eddy currents.</div><div class="column-right">The efficiency of a transformer increases with increase in eddy currents.</div></div>','[{"text": "Statement A is correct but Statement B is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Statement A is incorrect but Statement B is correct.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Statements are correct.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Statements are incorrect.", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Large eddy currents are produced in non-	laminated iron core of the trnasformer by the induced emf, as the resistance of bulk iron core is very small. Laminating the core substantially reduces the eddy currents. Eddy current 	heats up the core of the transformer. More the eddy currents greater is the loss of energy and the efficiency goes down.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ type-2',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44662,'physics',39,1104,2631,'According to de - Brogile hypothesis, the wavelength associated with moving electron of mass \' $m$ \' is $$\\lambda_{e}$$. Using mass energy relation and Planck\'s quantum theory, the wavelength associated with photon is $$\\lambda_{p}$$. If the energy ($E$) electron and photon is same, then relation between $$\\lambda_{e}$$ and $$\\lambda_{p}$$ is','[{"text": "$$\\\\lambda_{p} \\\\propto \\\\lambda_{e}$$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$$\\\\lambda_{p} \\\\propto \\\\lambda_{e}^{2}$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$\\\\lambda_{p} \\\\propto \\\\sqrt{\\\\lambda_{e}}$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$\\\\lambda_{p} \\\\propto \\\\dfrac{1}{\\\\lambda_{e}}$$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'The energy of photon is given as<br>${E_p} = \\frac{{hc}}{{{\\lambda _p}}}$<br>$\\therefore {\\lambda _p} = \\frac{{hc}}{{{E_p}}}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,(1)$<br>Energy of an moving electron is given as<br>${E_e} = m{c^2} = pc \\Rightarrow p = \\frac{{{E_p}}}{c}$<br>$\\therefore {\\lambda _e} = \\frac{h}{p} = \\frac{{hc}}{{{E_e}}}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,(2)$<br>Given, $$E_{p}=E_{e}$$<br>Therefore, from eq.(1) and eq.(2), we get $$\\lambda_{p} \\propto \\lambda_{e}$$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2017,'MHTCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44663,'physics',38,1067,2544,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Satellite for remote sensing.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Such satellite shall be in polar orbit.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Satellites in polar orbits at 879 kilometers altitude pass over the same Earth location at a specific local time, ensuring consistent lighting and atmospheric conditions. So correct option is (1).',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44665,'physics',37,977,2770,'<div class="matching-question"><div class="column-left">An ideal transformer does not vary the power.</div><div class="column-right">A transformer is used to step-up or step-down $ac$ voltages.</div></div>','[{"text": "Statement A is correct but Statement B is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Statement A is incorrect but Statement B is correct.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Statements are correct.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Statements are incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Option (3) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ type-2',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44666,'physics',39,1104,2631,'A particle is dropped from a height \' $$H$$ \'. The de Broglie wavelength of the particle depends on height as','[{"text": "$H$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${H^{ - 1/2}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${H^0}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${H^{1/2}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'When the particle is dropped from height $H$, then the velocity at lowest point is $v = \\sqrt {2gH} $<br>de Broglie\'s wavelength $\\lambda  = \\frac{h}{{mv}} = \\frac{h}{{\\,m\\sqrt {2gH} }}$<br>$ \\Rightarrow \\lambda  \\propto \\frac{h}{{\\sqrt H }}$ or $\\lambda  \\propto {H^{^{ - \\frac{1}{2}}}}$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2019,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44667,'physics',38,1067,2544,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> It is necessary to use satellite for long distance T.V. transmission.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> The television signals are low. frequency signals.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'$T V$ singnals are of high-frequency, with minimal ionospheric reflection, rely on tall antennas for local coverage, but for long-distance transmission, satellites serve as crucial relay stations. Satellites in space facilitate global $T V$ broadcasts by receiving signals from Earth, overcoming geographical barriers and ionospheric limitations. So correct option is (3).',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(48317,'biology',101,573,1152,'Young couples in the urban working areas have adopted the _____norm.','[{"text": "One child", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Two child", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "No child", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Three child", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Young couples in the urban working areas have adopted the one child norm.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44668,'physics',37,974,2767,'<div class="matching-question"><div class="column-left">In a purely inductive or capacitive circuit, the current is referred to as wattless current.</div><div class="column-right">No power is dissipated in a purely inductive or capacitive circuit even though a current is flowing in the circuit.</div></div>','[{"text": "Statement A is correct but Statement B is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Statement A is incorrect but Statement B is correct.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Statements are correct.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Statements are incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'In a purely inductive or capacitive circuit, power factor, $\\cos \\,\\phi = 0$ and no power is dissipated even though a current is flowing in the circuit. In such cases, current is referred to as wattless current.<br>Option (3) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ type-2',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44670,'physics',39,1104,2631,'A proton and an $$\\alpha$$ particle are accelerated through the same potential difference $V$. The ratio of their de Broglie wavelength is','[{"text": "$$\\\\sqrt{2}$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$2 \\\\sqrt{2}$$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$$\\\\sqrt{3}$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$2 \\\\sqrt{3}$$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'de Broglie wavelength of a particle of mass $m$ and charge $q$ accelerated through a potential difference $V$ is<br>$\\lambda  = \\frac{h}{{\\sqrt {2mqV} }}$<br>Since the potential difference $V$ is the same for both the particles,<br>$\\lambda  \\propto \\frac{1}{{\\sqrt {mq} }}$<br>Thus<br>$$\\dfrac{\\lambda_{p}}{\\lambda_{\\alpha}}=\\sqrt{\\dfrac{m_{\\alpha} q_{\\alpha}}{m_{p} q_{p}}}=\\sqrt{\\left(\\dfrac{4 m_{p}}{m_{p}}\\right)\\left(\\dfrac{2 e}{e}\\right)}=\\dfrac{\\sqrt{8}}{1}=2 \\sqrt{2}$$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2018,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44671,'physics',38,1065,2539,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> In Hertz experiment, the electric vector of radiation produced by the source gap is parallel to the gap.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Production of sparks between the detector gap is maximum when it is placed perpendicular to the source gap.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Hertz observed maximum spark production when the detector gap was parallel to the source gap, indicating the electric vector\'s alignment perpendicular to both gaps and the radiation propagation. So correct option is (3).',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44672,'physics',37,974,2272,'<div class="matching-question"><div class="column-left">The power in an ac circuit is minimum if the circuit has only a resistor.</div><div class="column-right">Power of a circuit is independent of the phase angle.</div></div>','[{"text": "Statement A is correct but Statement B is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Statement A is incorrect but Statement B is correct.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Statements are correct.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Statements are incorrect.", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Power in a series ac circuit consisting of $L$, $C$ and $R$ is given by $P = {I_{rms}}{V_{rms}}\\cos \\phi $ where <br>$\\phi  = {\\tan ^{ - 1}}\\left( {\\frac{{\\left| {{X_L} - {X_C}} \\right|}}{R}} \\right)$<br>For a purely resistive circuit <br>${X_L} = 0\\,\\,\\,{\\mathop{\\rm and}\\nolimits} \\,\\,\\,{X_C} = 0\\,$ .Therefore $\\tan \\phi  = 0\\,\\,{\\rm{or}}\\,\\,\\phi  = 0$ and thereby $\\cos \\phi  = 1$. The power is maximum as $\\cos \\,\\phi $ is maximum. Power depends on the phase angle through the power factor $\\cos \\,\\phi $. So option (4) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ type-2',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44673,'physics',39,1104,2631,'The figure shows standing de Broglie waves due to the revolvtion of electron in a certain orbit of hydrogen atom. Then the expression for the orbit radius is (all notations have their ususal meanings)','[{"text": "$\\\\frac{{{h^2}{\\\\varepsilon _0}}}{{\\\\pi m{e^2}}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{{4\\\\,{h^2}{\\\\varepsilon _0}}}{{\\\\pi m{e^2}}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{{9\\\\,{h^2}{\\\\varepsilon _0}}}{{\\\\pi m{e^2}}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{{36\\\\,{h^2}{\\\\varepsilon _0}}}{{\\\\pi m{e^2}}}$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Number of standing waves in the orbit $$=n=6$$ (from the given figure)<br>Radius of orbit with principal quantum number $n$,<br>${r_n} = \\frac{{{n^2}{h^2}{\\varepsilon _0}}}{{m{e^2}\\pi }} = \\frac{{36{h^2}{\\varepsilon _0}}}{{\\pi m{e^2}}}$<br>None of the options match the correct answer.',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',2021,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/39_44673_question_1.png'),
(44674,'physics',40,239,478,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> In hydrogen atom as $n$ increases, energy level comes closer and closer.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Energy of an electron in hydrogen atom is ${E_n} = \\frac{{ - 13.6}}{{{n^2}}}eV$','[{"text": "Both assertion and reason are correct and reason is the correct explanation of assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both assertion and reason are correct but reason is not the correct explanation of assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'${E_n} =  - \\frac{{13.6}}{{{n^2}}}ev$<br>As $n$ increases energy of the orbits come close to each other. So option (1) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44683,'physics',37,977,2281,'<div class="matching-question"><div class="column-left">When a current flows in the coil of a transformer then its core becomes hot.</div><div class="column-right">The core of transformer is made of soft-iron.</div></div>','[{"text": "Statement A is correct but Statement B is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Statement A is incorrect but Statement B is correct.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Statements are correct.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Statements are incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'When current flows in the coil eddy currents will be developed in the core which is made up of iron. The eddy currents heats the core. So option (3) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ type-2',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44675,'physics',38,1068,2545,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> $X$-ray astronomy is possible only from satellites orbiting the earth.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Efficiency of $X$-ray telescope is large as compared to terre-strail telescope.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'$X$-rays are absorbed by Earth\'s atmosphere, if used in ground-based observatories. Placing $X$ - ray telescopes in space satellites eliminates atmospheric interference, enabling discoveries in $X$-ray astronomy. So correct option is (3).',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44676,'physics',37,977,2770,'<div class="matching-question"><div class="column-left">The power is produced when a transformer steps up the voltage.</div><div class="column-right">In an ideal transformer $VI = {\\mathop{\\rm constant}\\nolimits} $</div></div>','[{"text": "Statement A is correct but Statement B is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Statement A is incorrect but Statement B is correct.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Statements are correct.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Statements are incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Transformer cannot produce power, but it transfer from primary to secondary.Power $P = VI$ is constant for an ideal transformer. So option (3) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ type-2',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44677,'physics',39,1104,2631,'de Broglie wavelength of a body of mass $1\\,kg$ moving with velocity of $2000\\,m/s$ is','[{"text": "$1.5 \\\\times {10^7}\\\\,m$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$3.32 \\\\times {10^{ - 27}}\\\\,m$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$0.55 \\\\times {10^{ - 22}}\\\\,m$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "None of these", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'$$\\lambda=\\dfrac{h}{m v}=\\dfrac{6.6 \\times 10^{-34}}{1 \\times 2000}$$<br>$ = 3.3 \\times {10^{ - 27}}\\,m$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44678,'physics',38,1067,2544,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Short wave bands are used for transmission of "D waves" to a large distance.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Short waves are reflected by ionosphere.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Short waves, with wavelengths from $30\\,km$ to $30\\,cm$, are vital for long-distance radio transmission, utilizing the ionosphere\'s ( 80 to 400 $km$) reflection to enable global connectivity. D wave band has range $110\\,\\,170{\\mkern 1mu} GHz$. So correct option is (1).',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44679,'physics',40,240,479,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Balmer series lies in the visible region of electromagnetic spectrum.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> $\\frac{1}{\\lambda } = R\\left( {\\frac{1}{{{2^2}}} - \\frac{1}{{{n^2}}}} \\right),$  where $n = 3,4,5...$','[{"text": "Both assertion and reason are correct and reason is the correct explanation of assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both assertion and reason are correct but reason is not the correct explanation of assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'The wavelength in Balmer series is given by<br>$\\frac{1}{\\lambda } = R\\left( {\\frac{1}{{{2^2}}} - \\frac{1}{{{n^2}}}} \\right),n = 3,4,5...$<br>$\\frac{1}{{{\\lambda _{\\max }}}} = R\\left( {\\frac{1}{{{2^2}}} - \\frac{1}{{{3^2}}}} \\right)$<br>${\\lambda _{\\max }} = \\frac{{36}}{{5R}} = \\frac{{36 \\times 1}}{{5 \\times 1.097 \\times {{10}^7}}} = 6563\\mathop {\\rm{A}}\\limits^ \\circ  $<br>and  $\\frac{1}{{{\\lambda _{\\min }}}} = R\\left( {\\frac{1}{{{2^2}}} - \\frac{1}{{{\\infty ^2}}}} \\right)$<br>${\\lambda _{\\min }} = \\frac{4}{R} = \\frac{4}{{1.097 \\times {{10}^7}}} = 3646\\mathop {\\rm{A}}\\limits^ \\circ  $<br>So the Balmer series lies in visible region',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44680,'physics',37,975,2276,'<div class="matching-question"><div class="column-left">Resonance is exhibited by a circuit only if both L and C are present in the circuit.</div><div class="column-right">In resonance the voltage across 	L and C cancel each other, both being out of phase.</div></div>','[{"text": "Statement A is correct but Statement B is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Statement A is incorrect but Statement B is correct.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Statements are correct.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Statements are incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'At resonance ${X_L} = {X_C}$ which is only possible if the circuit has both inductor and capacitor. So option (3) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ type-2',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44681,'physics',39,1104,2631,'If the kinetic energy of the particle is increased to 16 times its previous value, the percentage change in the de Broglie wavelength of the particle is','[{"text": "25", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "50", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "75", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "60", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'For de Broglie wavelength,<br>$$\\lambda=\\dfrac{h}{p}=\\dfrac{h}{\\sqrt{2 m K}}$$<br>For ${1^{st}}$ case, $$\\quad \\lambda_{1}=\\dfrac{h}{p}=\\dfrac{h}{\\sqrt{2 m K}}$$<br>For ${2^{nd}}$ case, $$\\lambda_{2}=\\dfrac{h}{\\sqrt{2 m 16 K}}=\\dfrac{h}{4 \\sqrt{2 m K}}=\\dfrac{\\lambda_{1}}{4}$$<br>So, $$75 \\%$$ change in the wavelength takes place.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44682,'physics',38,1067,2544,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Ultraviolet radiations are of higher frequency waves are dangerous to human being.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Ultraviolet radiation are absorbed by the atmosphere.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Ultraviolet $(U V)$ waves, with wavelengths from $4000\\,\\mathop {{\\rm{ }}A}\\limits^{\\,\\,^\\circ } $ to $100\\,\\mathop {{\\rm{ }}A}\\limits^{\\,\\,^\\circ } $, are absorbed by the Earth\'s atmosphere, (converting ${O_2}$ to ${O_3}$) contributing to the ozone layer formation and protecting against harmful $U V$ radiation. While shorter $UVC$wavelengths are absorbed, $UVB$ and $UVA$ reaching the Earth\'s surface pose healthrisks, emphasizing the need for protective measures like sunscreen and sunglasses. So correct option is (2).',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44684,'physics',40,239,478,'<div class="matching-question"><div class="column-left">The total energy of an electron revolving in any stationary orbit is negative.</div><div class="column-right">Kinetic energy of revolving electron can be $( - ve)$ also.</div></div>','[{"text": "Statement A is correct but Statement B is incorrect.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Statement A is incorrect but Statement B is correct.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Statements are correct.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Statements are incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Energy can have positive or negative values. In the atom the electrons are bound. The negative  energy of the revolving electrons does not allow the electrons to leave the atom. Kinetic energy of the electron is always $( + )ve$.  So option (1) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ type-2',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44685,'physics',39,1103,2630,'A wave is associated with matter when it is','[{"text": "Stationary", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "In motion with a velocity", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "In motion with speed of light", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "In motion with speed greater than that of light", "isCorrect": false}]',1,NULL,NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44686,'physics',38,1065,2539,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Radio waves can be polarised.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Sound waves cannot be polarised.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Radio waves exhibit polarization due to transverse oscillations of electric and magnetic fields perpendicular to propagation, while longitudinal sound waves lack polarization as particle oscillations align with wave motion. So correct option is (2).',NULL,'Easy','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44687,'physics',37,973,2263,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> The alternating current lags behind the e.m.f. by a phase angle of $\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ when $AC$ flows through an inductor.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> The inductive reactance increases as the frequency of $AC$ source decreases.','[{"text": "Both assertion and reason are correct and reason isthe correct explanation of assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both assertion and reason are correct but reason is not the correct explanation of assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'When $AC$ flows through an inductor current lags behind the e.m.f., by phase of  $\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ Inductive reactance is, ${X_L} = \\omega L = \\pi 2fL,$ so when frequency increases, correspondingly inductive reactance also increase.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44688,'physics',39,1103,2630,'A photon in motion has a mass:','[{"text": "$$c / h v$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$v / h$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$h v$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$h v / c^{2}$$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'$$m=\\dfrac{h v}{c^{2}}$$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44689,'physics',40,238,477,'<div class="matching-question"><div class="column-left">The trajectory traced by an incident particle depends on the impact parameter of collision.</div><div class="column-right">The impact parameter is the perpendicular distance of the initial velocity 	vector of the incident particle from the centre of the target nucleus.</div></div>','[{"text": "Statement A is correct but Statement B is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Statement A is incorrect but Statement B is correct.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Statements are correct.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Statements are incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Option (3) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ type-2',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44690,'physics',38,1067,2544,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Micro waves are used in radar.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> These waves have very small wavelength.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Radar needs singnal in particular direction and so short wavelength is required. Microwaves, with their short wavelengths in the millimeter range, are ideal for radar systems, enabling the generation of precise and directional beams crucial for accurate target detection in military, meteorological, and air traffic control applications. So correct option is (1).',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44691,'physics',37,973,2265,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Average value of ac over a complete cycle is always zero.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> A choke coil consists of an inductor having some resistance R.','[{"text": "Both assertion and reason are correct and reason is the correct explanation of assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both assertion and reason are correct but reason is not the correct explanation of assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'The mean or average value of alternating current or e.m.f. during a full cycle is always equal to zero.<br>So average value over a full cycle is always 	zero. Option (2) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44692,'physics',39,1103,2630,'$$n$$ \' photons of wavelength \' $$\\lambda$$ \' are absorbed by a black body of mass \' $m$ \'. The momentum gained by the body is','[{"text": "$\\\\frac{h}{{m\\\\lambda }}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{{mnh}}{\\\\lambda }$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{{nh}}{{m\\\\lambda }}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{{nh}}{\\\\lambda }$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'<br>Energy of $n$ protons, $E = \\frac{{nhc}}{\\lambda }$<br>Momentum gained by the body, $p = \\frac{E}{c}$<br>$ = \\frac{{nhc}}{{\\lambda c}} = \\frac{{nh}}{\\lambda }$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2012,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44708,'physics',37,973,2265,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> A capacitor of suitable capacitance can be used in an ac circuit in place of the choke coil.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> A capacitor blocks $dc$ and allows $ac$ only','[{"text": "Both assertion and reason are correct and reason is the correct explanation of assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both assertion and reason are correct but reason is not the correct explanation of assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'We can use a capacitor of suitable capacitance as a choke coil because average power consumed per cycle in a ideal capacitoris zero. Therefore, like a choke coil, a condenser can reduce power loss. Capacitor blocks dc and allows ac. So option (2) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44693,'physics',38,1065,2542,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> The electric energy and magnetic energy have equal average values in linearly polarised plane em waves.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> The electric field and magnetic field have equal average values in linearly polarised plane em waves.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Linearly polarized plane electromagnetic waves exhibit a fundamental balance, with average values of electric and magnetic fields as well as electric and magnetic energy being equal, highlighting their harmonious coexistence during propagation. So correct option is (2).',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44694,'physics',37,974,2272,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> When an $AC$ circuit contains resistor only, its power is minimum.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Power of an $AC$ circuit depends on phase angle.','[{"text": "Both assertion and reason are correct and reason isthe correct explanation of assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both assertion and reason are correct but reason is not the correct explanation of assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but reason is correct.", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'The power of an $AC$ circuit is given by $p = EI\\cos \\phi $ where $\\cos \\phi $ is power factor and $\\phi $  is phase angle. In case of circuit containing resistance only, phase angle is zero and power factor is equal to one. Therefore power is maximum in case of circuit containing only resistor. So option (4) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44695,'physics',40,239,478,'<div class="matching-question"><div class="column-left">Bohr&rsquo;s second postulate states that the stationary orbits are those for which the angular momentum is integral multiple of $\\frac{h}{{2\\pi }}$.</div><div class="column-right">Angular momentum of electron in 2$nd$ orbit is $\\frac{{2h}}{\\pi }$</div></div>','[{"text": "Statement A is correct but Statement B is incorrect.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Statement A is incorrect but Statement B is correct.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Statements are correct.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Statements are incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Bohr&rsquo;s second postulate states that the stationary orbits are those for which the angular momentum is some integral multiple of $\\frac{h}{{2\\pi }}$ i.e.,$L = n\\frac{h}{{2\\pi }}$where $n$ is an integer called a quantum number. Angular momentum in the 2$nd$ orbit is $\\frac{{2h}}{{2\\pi }} = \\frac{h}{\\pi }$. So option 1 is correct.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ type-2',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44696,'physics',39,1103,2630,'A source $$S_{1}$$ is producing, $$10^{15}$$ photon $/s$ of wavelength $5000\\mathop A\\limits^o .$ Another source $$S_{2}$$ is producing $$1.02 \\times 10^{15}$$ photons per second of wavelength $5100\\mathop A\\limits^o .$ Then, (power of $$S_{2}$$ ) / (power of $$S_{1}$$ ) is equal to','[{"text": "1.02", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "1.04", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "1.00", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "0.98", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Number of photons emitted per second is given by<br>$$\\begin{aligned}n= & \\dfrac{P}{\\left(\\dfrac{h c}{\\lambda}\\right)} \\\\& {\\left[\\begin{array}{l}P=\\text { power } \\\\\\dfrac{h c}{\\lambda}=\\text { Energy }\\end{array}\\right] }\\end{aligned}$$<br>So,<br>$$P=\\dfrac{n h c}{\\lambda} \\Rightarrow \\dfrac{P_{2}}{P_{1}}=\\dfrac{n_{2} \\lambda_{1}}{n_{1} \\lambda_{2}}=\\dfrac{1.02 \\times 10^{15} \\times 5000}{10^{15} \\times 5100}=1$$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44697,'physics',37,973,2263,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> If the frequency of alternating current in an $AC$ circuit consisting of an inductance coil is increased then current gets decreased.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> The inductive reactance is directly proportional to frequency of alternating current.','[{"text": "Both assertion and reason are correct and reason isthe correct explanation of assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both assertion and reason are correct but reason is not the correct explanation of assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'In pure inductive circuit<br>${i_L} = \\frac{{{V_L}}}{{{X_L}}},{X_L} = \\omega L$ as $\\omega $ increases ${i_L}$ decreases. So option (1) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44698,'physics',38,1065,2539,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> The eye is most sensitive for the light of frequency $5.36 \\times {10^{14}}\\,Hz.$<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> The frequency of light is directly proportional to wavelength of light.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'The human eye\'s cone cells are most sensitive to a frequency $(f)$ of approximately $5.36 \\times {10^{14}}\\,Hz$, corresponding to the greenishyellow region of the visible spectrum with a wavelength of $560\\,nm.$ This specific frequency defines the peak sensitivity of the eye to light.<br>But reason is false as $f=\\dfrac{c}{\\lambda}$. So correct option is (3).',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44699,'physics',40,240,479,'<div class="matching-question"><div class="column-left">Hydrogen atom consists of only one electron but its emission spectrum has many lines.</div><div class="column-right">Only Lyman series is found in the absorption spectrum of hydrogen atom whereas in the emission spectrum, all the series are found.</div></div>','[{"text": "Statement A is correct but Statement B is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Statement A is incorrect but Statement B is correct.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Statements are correct.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Statements are incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'When the atom gets appopriate energy from outside, then this electron rises to some higher energy level. Now it can return either directly to  the lower energy level or come to the lowest energy level after passing through other lower energy levels hence all possible transitions take place in the source and many lines are seen in the spectrum.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ type-2',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44735,'physics',40,240,479,'A set of atoms in an excited state decays','[{"text": "Into a lower state only when excited by an external  electric field", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "In general to any of the states with lower energy", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "To emit photons only when they collide", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "All together simultaneously into a lower state", "isCorrect": false}]',1,NULL,NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,'NCERT Exemplar','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44700,'physics',39,1103,2630,'A particle of mass $1mg$ has the same wavelength as an electron moving with a velocity of $3 \\times {10^6}\\,m{s^{ - 1}}$. The velocity of the particle is (mass of electron $ = 9.1 \\times {10^{ - 31}}\\,kg$ )','[{"text": "$9.1 \\\\times {10^{ - 2}}\\\\,m{s^{ - 1}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2.7 \\\\times {10^{ - 18}}\\\\,m{s^{ - 1}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$3 \\\\times {10^{ - 31}}\\\\,m{s^{ - 1}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$3 \\\\times {10^{31}}\\\\,m{s^{ - 1}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'According to de Broglie relation,<br>$$\\lambda=\\dfrac{h}{p}$$<br>Where, electron is given by<br>For electron $$\\lambda_{e}=\\dfrac{h}{p_{e}}$$<br>but $$\\lambda=\\lambda_{e}$$, so $$p=p_{e}$$<br>${m_e}{v_e} = mv \\Rightarrow v = \\frac{{{m_e}{v_e}}}{m}$<br>Here, $\\quad {m_e} = 9.1 \\times {10^{ - 31}}\\,kg$,<br>${v_e} = 3 \\times {10^6}\\,m{s^{ - 1}}$<br>and $\\quad m = 1 \\times {10^{ - 6}}\\,kg$<br>$\\therefore \\quad v = \\frac{{9.1 \\times {{10}^{ - 31}} \\times 3 \\times {{10}^6}}}{{1 \\times {{10}^{ - 6}}}} = 2.7 \\times {10^{ - 18}}\\,m/s$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44701,'physics',37,975,2275,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> In series L-C-R circuit resonance can take place.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Resonance takes place if inductive and capacitive reactances are equal and opposite.','[{"text": "Both assertion and reason are correct and reason isthe correct explanation of assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both assertion and reason are correct but reason is not the correct explanation of assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Option (1) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44702,'physics',38,1065,2539,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Velocity of light is constant in all media.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Light is an electromagnetic wave .','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but reason is correct.", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'The speed of light in a medium is determined by its redfractive index, indicating how much it slows down compared to the speed in a vacuum. Reason is well known fact. So correct option is (4).',NULL,'Easy','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44703,'physics',39,1103,2630,'Monochromatic light of wavelength $667\\,nm$ is produced by a helium neon laser. The power emitted is $9\\,mW$. The number of photons arriving per second on the average at a target irradiated by this beam is','[{"text": "$$9 \\\\times 10^{15}$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$3 \\\\times 10^{19}$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$3 \\\\times 10^{16}$$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$$9 \\\\times 10^{17}$$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Here, $\\lambda  = 667 \\times {10^{ - 9}}\\,m,P = 9 \\times {10^{ - 3}}\\,W$<br>$$\\begin{aligned}& \\text { Power }=\\dfrac{\\operatorname{energy}(E)}{\\operatorname{time}(t)} \\\\& =\\dfrac{N h c}{\\lambda}\\end{aligned}$$<br>where $N$ - total number of photons emitted per second<br>So, $N = \\frac{{P \\times \\lambda }}{{hc}}$<br>$ = \\frac{{9 \\times {{10}^{ - 3}} \\times 667 \\times {{10}^{ - 9}}}}{{6.6 \\times {{10}^{ - 34}} \\times 3 \\times {{10}^8}}} = 3 \\times {10^{16}}/s$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44704,'physics',40,239,478,'<div class="matching-question"><div class="column-left">Bohr had to postulate that the electrons in stationary orbits around the nucleus do not radiate.</div><div class="column-right">According to classical physics all accelerating electrons radiate.</div></div>','[{"text": "Statement A is correct but Statement B is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Statement A is incorrect but Statement B is correct.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Statements are correct.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Statements are incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Bohr postulated that electrons in stationary orbits around the nucleus do not radiate. According to this the moving electrons radiates only when they go from one orbit to the next lower orbit. Accelerated charges radiate photons. So option (3) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ type-2',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44705,'physics',37,973,2265,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> An inductance and a resistance are connected in series with an $AC$ circuit. In this circuit the current and the potential difference across the resistance lag behind potential difference across the inductance<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> In $LR$ circuit voltage leads the current by phase angle which depends on the value of inductance and resistance','[{"text": "Both assertion and reason are correct and reason is the correct explanation of assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both assertion and reason are correct but reason is not the correct explanation of assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'In $LR$ circuits voltage across $L$ leads the current and voltage across resistor by  $\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ The net voltage leads the net current by  $\\phi $ given by $\\tan \\phi  = \\frac{{{X_L}}}{{{X_R}}} = \\frac{{\\omega L}}{R}$<br>So $\\phi $ depends on both  ${X_L}$ and  ${X_R}$. So option (2) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44706,'physics',38,1064,2537,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> When variable frequency A.C. source is connected to a capacitor, displacement current comes into play.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> There is rate of change in electric flux.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Varying alternating current changes electricfield between the plates $\\dfrac{d \\phi_{E}}{d t} \\neq 0$.<br>$\\Rightarrow I($ displecement $)=\\mu_{o} \\in_{o} \\dfrac{d \\phi_{E}}{d t} \\neq 0$.<br>So correct option is (1).',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44707,'physics',39,1102,2629,'In a photoelectric experiment, the maximum velocity of photoelectrons emitted','[{"text": "Depends on intensity of incident radiation", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Does not depend on cathode material", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Depends on frequency of incident radiation", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Does not depend on wavelength of incident radiation", "isCorrect": false}]',2,NULL,NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(46021,'physics',41,406,809,'$_{92}{U^{238}}$ absorbs a neutron. The product emits electron. This product further emits an electron. The product is','[{"text": "$_{94}P{u^{239}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$_{90}P{u^{239}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$_{93}P{u^{237}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$_{94}P{u^{237}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'$_{92}{U^{238}}{ + _0}{n^1}{ \\to _{ - 1}}{e^0}{ + _{93}}N{p^{239}}{ \\to _{ - 1}}{e^0}{ + _{94}}P{u^{239}}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44709,'physics',38,1065,2539,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Accelerated charge radiate electromagnetic waves.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Oscillating electric and magnetic fields regenerate each other.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Oscillating charges generate alternating electric fields, inducing oscillating magnetic fields, resulting in the self-perpetuating cycle of electromagnetic wave generation. So correct option is (1).',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44710,'physics',40,239,478,'<div class="matching-question"><div class="column-left">Electrons in the atom are held due to coulomb forces.</div><div class="column-right">The atom is stable only because the centripetal force due to Coulomb’s law is balanced by the centrifugal force</div></div>','[{"text": "Statement A is correct but Statement B is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Statement A is incorrect but Statement B is correct.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Statements are correct.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Statements are incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'According to postulates of Bohr’s atom model the electron revolves around the nucleus in fixed orbit of definite radii. As long as the electron is in a certain orbit it does not radiate any energy.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ type-2',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44711,'physics',39,1102,2628,'The stopping potential of the photoelectrons, from a photo cell is','[{"text": "Directly proportional to intensity of incident light", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Directly proportional to frequency of incident light", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Inversely proportional to frequency of incident light", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Inversely proportional to intensity of incident light", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'The stopping potential of photoelectrons is potential needed to stop the electrons from reaching the collector depends only on the frequency of incident radiation. It is directly proportional to it.<br>This is beacuse, more the frequency of incident light, more will be the energy of the photons incident photons. $(E = hv)$<br>Thus, more will be the energy (kinetic) acquired the electrons<br>More the kinetic energy of emitted electrons, higher is the potential needed to stop them (stopping potential)',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2019,'MHTCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44712,'physics',37,973,2264,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> $AC$ generators are based upon EMI principle.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Resistance offered by a capacitor for $DC$ current is infinity.','[{"text": "Both assertion and reason are correct and reason isthe correct explanation of assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both assertion and reason are correct but reason is not the correct explanation of assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'${X_C} = \\frac{1}{{\\omega C}},\\omega  = \\frac{{2\\pi }}{T}$<br>As $T = 0$ for dc current then  $\\omega $ is zero and ${X_L}$  is infinite that is why a capacitor does not allow dc current. So option (2) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44713,'physics',38,1065,2542,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> In an electromagnetic wave, magnitude of magnetic field vector is equal to the magnitude of electric field vector.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Energy of electromagnetic waves is shared equally by the electric and magnetic fields.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but reason is correct.", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'The equation $\\frac{E}{B} = c = 3 \\times {10^8}\\,m{\\rm{/}}s$ signifies that the electric field\'s magnitude $(E)$ is much greater than the magnetic field\'s magnitude $(B)$ in electromagnetic waves. Despite this, both fields share energy equally during wave propagation, emphasizing their collaborative role in transporting electromagnetic energy. So correct option is (4).',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44714,'physics',40,245,487,'$\\alpha  - $ particles have<br>I. mass equals to that of helium atom<br>II. Charge of $ + 2e$ units<br>III. Size less than ${10^{ - 2}}m$<br>IV. Speed equal to that of speed of light<br>Choose the correct option from the codes given 	below.','[{"text": "I and II", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "II and III", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "I, II and III", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "I, II and IV", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Option (1) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ type-2',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44715,'physics',39,1102,2629,'A metal surface is illuminated by light of given intensity and frequency to cause photoemission. if the intensity of illumination is reduced to one fourth of its original value then the maximum $KE$ of the emitted photoelectrons would be','[{"text": "Twice the original value", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Four times the original value", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "One fourth of the original value", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Unchanged", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'The maximum kinetic energy of photoelectrons is given by $K{E_{{\\text{max}}}} = h\\left( {v - {v_0}} \\right)\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,(1)$<br>Where, $h = $ planck\'s constant<br>$v = $ frequency of radiation<br>$$v_{0}=$$ threshold frequency<br>It can be seen from eq.(1), that the maximum $KE$ of emitted photoelectron is proportional to the frequency of radiation and is independent of the intensity of radiation, so it remains unchanged',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2019,'MHTCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44716,'physics',37,974,2272,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> When capacitive reactance is smaller than the inductive reactance in LCR 	circuit, e.m.f. leads the current.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Power is zero when power factor is 1.','[{"text": "Both assertion and reason are correct and reason isthe correct explanation of assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both assertion and reason are correct but reason is not the correct explanation of assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'$\\tan \\phi  = \\frac{{{X_L} - {X_C}}}{R} = \\frac{{\\omega L - \\frac{1}{{\\omega C}}}}{R}$<br>When ${X_L} > {X_C}$ then $\\tan \\phi $ is positive, i.e., $\\phi $ ispositive (between 0 and $\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ ). Hence e.m.f. 	leads the current. So option (3) is correct.',NULL,'Easy','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44717,'physics',38,1068,2545,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Sound waves require a material medium for propagation.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Sound waves are not electromagnetic waves.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Sound waves, as mechanical waves, need a physical medium for propagation, disturbing particles to transmit energy. In contrast, electromagnetic waves, including light and radiation, can travel through a vacuum without relying on a material medium, showcasing distinctive propagation characteristics. So correct option is (2).',NULL,'Easy','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44718,'physics',39,1102,2626,'The maximum kinetic energy of emitted electrons in a photoelectric effect does not depend upon','[{"text": "Intensity", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Work function", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Wavelength", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Frequency", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'The maximum kinetic energy of emitted electron $ = hv - {W_0}$ where $hv$ is the energy of incident photon and ${W_0}$ is the work function. Hence, maximum $K.E$. depends only on $$v$$, $$\\lambda(=c / v)$$ and $$W_{0}$$ and not on intensity.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2008,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44719,'physics',37,973,2263,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Choke coil is preferred over a resistor to adjust current in an $AC$ circuit.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Power factor for inductance is zero.','[{"text": "Both assertion and reason are correct and reason isthe correct explanation of assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both assertion and reason are correct but reason is not the correct explanation of assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'If resistor is used in controlling $AC$ supply,electrical energy will be wasted in the form of heat energy across the resistance wire. However, $AC$ supply can be controlled with choke without any wastage of energy. This is because, power factor $\\left( {\\cos \\phi } \\right)$  for resistance is unity and is zero for an inductance. <br>${\\rm{[}}P = EI\\cos \\phi {\\rm{]}}$ .So option (1) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44720,'physics',40,238,477,'Trajectory of an $\\alpha  - $ particle in Geiger-Marsden experiment is explained by using<br>I. Coulomb’s law            II. Newton’s law<br>III. Gauss’ law	     IV. Faraday’s law<br>Choose the correct option from the codes given below.','[{"text": "I and II", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "I and III", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "I and IV", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "I, II and IV", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Option (1) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ type-2',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44721,'physics',38,1066,2543,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Infrared waves are sometimes referred as heat waves.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Infrared rays have wavelengths less than $10\\,nm$','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Infrared waves, known as heat waves, are absorbed by materials with water molecules, elevating the thermal motion of the molecules and causing heating. Wavelength of infrared waves lies between $700\\,nm$ to $1\\,nm$.So correct option is (3).',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44722,'physics',37,973,2264,'A small signal voltage $V\\left( t \\right) = {V_0}\\sin \\omega t$ is applied across an ideal capacitor $C$','[{"text": "Current  $I(t)$, lags voltage $V(t)$  by $90^\\\\circ $", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Over a full cycle the capacitor $C$ does not consume   any energy from the voltage source", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Current $I(t)$ is in phase with voltage $V(t)$.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Current $I(t)$ leads voltage $V(t)$ by $180^\\\\circ $", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Power $ = {V_{rms}}.{I_{rms}}\\cos \\phi $<br>$\\therefore $ Power consumed  $ = 0\\,{\\rm{Because}}\\,\\phi  = {\\rm{ }}90^\\circ {\\rm{ }}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2016,'NEET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44723,'physics',39,1102,2626,'The maximum kinetic energy of emitted photoelectrons depends on','[{"text": "Intensity of incident radiation", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Frequency of incident radiation", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Speed of incident radiation", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Number of photons in the incident rotation", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'According to Einstein\'s photoelectric equation,<br>${K_{\\max }} = hv - {\\phi _0}$<br>Where $$v$$ is the frequency of incident radiation and $$\\phi_{0}$$ is the work function of a metal<br>Thus, the maximum kinetic energy $\\left( {{K_{\\max }}} \\right)$ of emitted photoelectrons depends on frequency of incident radiation but not on its intensity, speed or number of photons in the incident radiation',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44724,'physics',38,1066,2543,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> $4\\,KHz$ electromagnetic wave is radio wave.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> $10\\,GHz$ corresponds to microwave range','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Micro waves are in range $1\\,GHz$ to $300\\,GHz$. Radio waves are in range $3\\,KHz$ to $1\\,GHz$. So correct option is (2).',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44725,'physics',40,240,479,'The Balmer series for the $H$-atom can be observed,<br>I. If we measure the frequencies of light emitted when an excited atom falls to ground state<br>II. If we measure the frequencies of light emitted due to transition between excited states and first excited state<br>III. In any transition of a $H$-atom<br>IV. Frequencies of higher values will be closely packed<br>Choose the correct option from the codes given below','[{"text": "I and II", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "II and III", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "I and III", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "II and IV", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Option (4) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ type-2',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(48331,'biology',101,574,1153,'Lactational amenorrhea - what is not true?','[{"text": "A natural method of contraception", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "OCPs have to be used at this time", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Method effective up to maximum 6 months", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Side effects nil; chances of failure high", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'OCPs have to be used at this time is not true.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44726,'physics',37,974,2766,'An inductor 20 $mH$, a capacitor $50\\mu F$ and a resistor $40\\Omega $ are connected in series across a source of emf  $V = 10\\,\\sin \\,340t$. The power loss in $A.C.$ circuit is','[{"text": "$0.51\\\\,W$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$0.67\\\\,W$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$0.76\\\\,W$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$0.89\\\\,W$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'${X_C} = \\frac{1}{{\\omega C}} = \\frac{1}{{340 \\times 50 \\times {{10}^{ - 6}}}} = 58.8\\Omega $<br>${X_L} = \\omega L = 340 \\times 20 \\times {10^{ - 3}} = 6.8\\Omega $<br>$Z = \\sqrt {{R^2} + {{\\left( {{X_C} - {X_L}} \\right)}^2}} $<br>$P = i_{rms}^2R = {\\left( {\\frac{{{V_{rms}}}}{Z}} \\right)^2}R$<br>$ = \\left( {\\frac{{10/\\sqrt 2 }}{{\\sqrt {4304} }}} \\right) \\times 40 = \\frac{{50 \\times 40}}{{4304}} = 0.47\\,W$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2016,'NEET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44727,'physics',39,1102,2627,'Light of certain frequency and intensity incident on a photosensitive material causes photoelectric effect. If both the frequency and intensity are doubled, the photoelectric saturation current becomes','[{"text": "Halved", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Quadrupled", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Unchanged", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Doubled", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Photoelectric current is proportional to intensity of incident radiation and hence it gets doubled.<br>The photoelectric saturation current does not depend on frequency of the incident light',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2019,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44728,'physics',38,1065,2539,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Static crashes are heard on radio, when a lightning flash occurs.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Light and radio waves travel with different speeds.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'During a lightning flash, interference occurs between these lightning-generated waves and ordinary radio waves, traveling at the same speed, causes static crashes during thunderstorms, disrupting radio signal reception. So correct option is (3).',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44729,'physics',40,239,478,'Using the Bohr’s model to calculate the speed 	of the electron in a hydrogen atom in the $n = 1,2$ and $3$ levels.','[{"text": "$2.19 \\\\times {10^6}\\\\,m/s,1.1 \\\\times {10^6}\\\\,m/s{\\\\rm{ }}and\\\\,7.3 \\\\times {10^5}m/s$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$2.2 \\\\times {10^6}\\\\,m/s,1.9 \\\\times {10^6}\\\\,m/s\\\\,\\\\,and\\\\,7.5 \\\\times {10^4}m/s$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$4.2 \\\\times {10^4}\\\\,m/s,3.2 \\\\times {10^4}\\\\,m/s\\\\,\\\\,and\\\\,7.2 \\\\times {10^6}m/s$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2 \\\\times {10^9}\\\\,m/s,3.2 \\\\times {10^5}\\\\,m/s\\\\,\\\\,and\\\\,7 \\\\times {10^5}m/s$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Speed of the electron in Bohr’s $n{\\rm{th}}$ orbit is $v = \\frac{{Z{e^2}}}{{2{\\varepsilon _0}nh}}$<br>For $n = 1,$<br>${v_1} = 2.19 \\times {10^6}m/s$<br>For $n = 2,{v_2} = 1.095 \\times {10^6}m/s$<br>For $n = 3,{v_3} = 7.3 \\times {10^5}m/s$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,'NCERT Exemplar','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44730,'physics',37,973,2266,'A  $100\\Omega $ resistance and a capacitor of  $100\\Omega $ reactance are connected in series across a 220$V$ source. When the capacitor is 50% charged, the peak value of the displacement current is','[{"text": "$2.2\\\\,A$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$11A$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$4.4A$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$11\\\\sqrt 2 A$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'$Z = \\sqrt {{R^2} + X_C^2}  = \\sqrt {{{100}^2} + {{100}^2}}  = 100\\sqrt 2 $<br>${i_0} = \\frac{{{V_0}}}{Z} = \\frac{{{V_{rms}}\\sqrt 2 }}{Z} = \\frac{{220\\sqrt 2 }}{{100\\sqrt 2 }} = 2.2A$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2016,'NEET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44731,'physics',39,1102,2629,'The work function of a surface of a photosensitive material is $6.2\\,eV$. The wavelength of the incident radiation for which the stopping potential is $5\\,V$ lies in the','[{"text": "Visible region", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$X$ - ray region", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Infrared region", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Ultraviolet region", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'According to Einstein\'s photoelectric equation,<br>$$h v=K E+W_{0}$$<br>As maximum $KE$ of ejected electrons is given by<br>$$K E=e V_{0}$$<br>Where, $$V_{0}$$ is stopping potential<br>$hv = 5eV + 6.2eV$<br>$\\left[ {\\because e{V_0} = 5eV} \\right]$<br>${\\text{ }} = {\\text{ }}11.2\\,eV\\quad ({\\text{lies}}\\,{\\text{in X - ray}}\\,{\\text{region}})$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44732,'physics',38,1065,2539,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> In electromagnetic waves, electric field and magnetic field lines are perpendicular to each other<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Electric field and magnetic field vectors do not have any link in these waves.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Electromagnetic waves, characterized by sinusoidal electric and magnetic field variations, propagate in free space without a medium. The two vector fields are perpendicular to each other.So correct option is (3).',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44733,'physics',37,974,2766,'A inductor 20 $mH$, a capacitor $100\\mu F$ and a resistor  $5\\Omega $ are connected in series across a source of emf, $V = 10\\,\\sin \\,314t$. The power loss  in the circuit is','[{"text": "$2.74\\\\,W$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1.13\\\\,W$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$0.\\\\,43\\\\,W$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$0.\\\\,79\\\\,W$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'$L = 20\\,mH$<br>${X_L} = \\omega L = 314 \\times 20 \\times {10^{ - 3}} = 6.28\\,\\Omega $<br>$C = 100\\,\\mu F$<br>${X_C} = \\frac{1}{{\\omega \\,C}} = \\frac{1}{{314 \\times 100 \\times {{10}^{ - 6}}}} = \\frac{{{{10}^4}}}{{314}} = 31.84\\,\\Omega $<br>$Z = \\sqrt {{R^2} + {{({X_L} - {X_C})}^2}}  = \\sqrt {{{(50)}^2} + {{(25)}^2}}  = 25\\sqrt 5 $<br>The average power loss is<br>$P = {V_{rms}} \\times {I_{RMS}} \\times \\cos \\phi $<br>$ = {V_{rms}} \\times \\frac{{{V_{rms}}}}{Z} \\times \\frac{R}{Z} = \\frac{{{{({V_{rms}})}^2} \\times R}}{{{Z^2}}} = \\frac{{{{\\left( {\\frac{{10}}{{\\sqrt 2 }}} \\right)}^2} \\times 50}}{{{{\\left( {25\\sqrt 5 } \\right)}^2}}}$<br>$ = 0.8W$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',2018,'NEET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44734,'physics',39,1102,2629,'When light of wavelength $300\\,nm$ falls on a photoelectric emitter, photoelectrons are liberated. For another emitter, light of wavelength $600\\,nm$ is sufficient for liberating photoelectrons. The ratio of the work function of the two emitters is','[{"text": "$$1: 2$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$2: 1$$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$$4: 1$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$1: 4$$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Work function $\\phi  = hc/\\lambda $<br>$$\\therefore \\phi \\propto \\dfrac{1}{\\lambda} \\text { or, } \\dfrac{\\phi_{1}}{\\phi_{2}}=\\dfrac{\\lambda_{2}}{\\lambda_{1}}=\\dfrac{600}{300}=\\dfrac{2}{1}$$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2006,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44736,'physics',38,1066,2543,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Waves of wavelength $10\\,cm$ are of Microwave type.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> The electromagnetic waves are transverse in nature.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Microwaves have $\\lambda$ range of ${10^{ - 3}}\\,m$ to ${10^{ - 1}}\\,m$. We know ${10^{ - 1}}\\,m = 10\\,cm$. Em waves are transverse in nature. So correct option is (3).',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44737,'physics',37,974,2766,'A series $LCR$ circuit is connected to a voltage source. When $L$ is removed from the circuit, the phase difference between current and voltage is $\\frac{\\pi }{3}$. If instead $C$ is removed from the circuit, the phase difference is again  $\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ between current and voltage. The power factor of the circuit is:','[{"text": "$0.5$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1.0$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$ - 1.0$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "zero", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'When $L$ removed $\\tan \\phi  = \\frac{{{X_C}}}{R}$<br>$\\frac{{{X_c}}}{R} = \\frac{{{X_L}}}{R} \\Rightarrow $ Resonance<br>$Z = R$<br>$\\cos \\phi  = \\frac{R}{Z} = \\frac{R}{R} = 1$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',2020,'NEET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44738,'physics',39,1102,2629,'Maximum velocity of the photoelectrons emitted by a metal surface $1.2 \\times {10^6}\\,m{s^{ - 1}}$. Assuming the specific charge of the electron to be $1.8 \\times {10^{11}}Ck{g^{ - 1}},$ the value of the stopping potential in volt will be','[{"text": "2", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "3", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "4", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "6", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Specific charge of electron,<br>$e/m = 1.8 \\times {10^{11}}Ck{g^{ - 1}}$<br>Maximum kinetic energy of photoelectrons<br>$\\frac{1}{2}m{v_{{{\\max }^2}}} = e{V_s}$<br>where ${V_s}$ is the stopping potential<br>$ \\Rightarrow {V_s} = \\frac{{{v_{{{\\max }^2}}}}}{{2(e/m)}} = \\frac{{{{\\left( {1.2 \\times {{10}^6}} \\right)}^2}}}{{2 \\times 1.8 \\times {{10}^{11}}}} = 4\\,V$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2006,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44739,'physics',38,1065,2539,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> When a charged particle moves in a circular path, it produces electromagnetic wave.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Charged particle moving in circular path has acceleration.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,') In circular path, direction of velocity of charge is charging as a function of time and so it has acceleration. Accelerated charges emit electromagnetic waves. So correct option is (1).',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44740,'physics',40,238,477,'The observations of Geiger-Marsden experiment are<br>I. Many of $\\alpha  - $ particles pass straight through the gold foil<br>II. Only about $0.1\\% $ of $\\alpha $ particles scatter by more than $1^\\circ $<br>III. About 1 in 8000 of $\\alpha  - $ particles is deflected more than $90^\\circ $<br>IV. Very few  $\\alpha  - $  particles are reflected back<br>Choose the correct option from the codes given below.','[{"text": "I, II and IV", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "I, II and III", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "II, III and IV", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "I, II, III and IV", "isCorrect": true}]',3,NULL,NULL,'Medium','MCQ type-2',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44741,'physics',37,973,2264,'A $40\\,\\mu F$ capacitor is connected to a 200$V$, 50 $Hz$ $ac$ supply. The rms value of the current in the circuit is, nearly:','[{"text": "$2.05\\\\,A$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2.5\\\\,A$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$25.1\\\\,A$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1.7\\\\,A$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'$I = \\frac{V}{{{X_C}}} = \\frac{V}{{1/C\\omega }} = VC\\omega $<br>$ = 200 \\times 40 \\times {10^{ - 6}} \\times 2\\pi  \\times 50$<br>$ = 2.5A$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2020,'NEET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44742,'physics',39,1102,2629,'The photoelectric threshold wavelength for silver is $$\\lambda_{0}$$. The energy of the electron ejected from the surface of silver by an incident wavelength $$\\lambda\\left(\\lambda < \\lambda_{0}\\right)$$ will be','[{"text": "$hc\\\\left( {{\\\\lambda _0} - \\\\lambda } \\\\right)$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{{hc}}{{{\\\\lambda _0} - \\\\lambda }}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{h}{c}\\\\left( {\\\\frac{{{\\\\lambda _0} - \\\\lambda }}{{\\\\lambda {\\\\lambda _0}}}} \\\\right)$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$hc\\\\left( {\\\\frac{{{\\\\lambda _0} - \\\\lambda }}{{\\\\lambda {\\\\lambda _0}}}} \\\\right)$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'According to Einstein\'s photoelectric equation<br>$K = \\frac{{hc}}{\\lambda } - \\frac{{hc}}{{{\\lambda _0}}}$<br>$ = hc\\left( {\\frac{1}{\\lambda } - \\frac{1}{{{\\lambda _0}}}} \\right) = hc\\left( {\\frac{{{\\lambda _0} - \\lambda }}{{\\lambda {\\lambda _0}}}} \\right)$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44743,'physics',38,1065,2541,'An object is placed in a medium of redfractive index 3. An electromagnetic wave of intensity $6 \\times {10^8}\\,W{\\rm{/}}{m^2}$ falls normally on the object and it is absorbed completely. The radiation pressure on the object would be (speed of light in free space $ = 3 \\times {10^8}\\,m{\\rm{/}}s$):','[{"text": "$2\\\\,N{m^{ - 2}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$18\\\\,N{m^{ - 2}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$6\\\\,N{m^{ - 2}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$36\\\\,N{m^{ - 2}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'$P=\\dfrac{\\Delta P_{\\text {momentum }}}{\\Delta t \\times A}=$ pressure<br>$P=\\dfrac{E}{V} \\times \\dfrac{1}{A \\times \\Delta t}=\\dfrac{l}{V} \\quad\\left[E=m v^{2}\\right]$<br>$P=\\dfrac{6 \\times 10^{6}}{c / \\mu} \\quad\\left[V=\\dfrac{c}{\\mu}\\right]$<br>$P=\\dfrac{6 \\times 10^{6}}{3 \\times 10^{8}} \\times 3$<br>$ = 6\\,N{\\rm{/}}{m^2}$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',2024,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44744,'physics',37,973,2264,'A capacitor of capacitance $‘C’$, is connected across an $ac$ source of voltage $V$, given by $V = {V_0}\\sin \\omega t$. The displacement current between the plates of the capacitor, would then be given by','[{"text": "${I_d} = \\\\frac{{{V_0}}}{{\\\\omega C}}\\\\cos \\\\omega t$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${I_d} = \\\\frac{{{V_0}}}{{\\\\omega C}}\\\\sin \\\\omega t$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${I_d} = {V_0}\\\\omega C\\\\sin \\\\omega t$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${I_d} = {V_0}\\\\omega C\\\\cos \\\\omega t$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'${I_d} = {\\varepsilon _0}\\frac{{d\\phi }}{{dt}} = {\\varepsilon _0}\\frac{{AdE}}{{dt}} = {\\varepsilon _0}A\\frac{d}{{dt}}\\left( {\\frac{V}{d}} \\right)$<br>$ = \\frac{{{\\varepsilon _0}A}}{d}\\frac{d}{{dt}}\\left( V \\right)$<br>$ = C\\frac{d}{{dt}}\\left( {{V_0}\\sin \\omega t} \\right)$<br>$ = {V_0}\\omega C\\cos \\omega t$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2021,'NEET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44745,'physics',40,239,478,'For the ground state the electron in the $H$-atom has an angular momentum $h$, according to the simple Bohr model. Angular momentum is a vector and hence there will be infinitely many orbits with the vector pointing in all possible directions. In actuality, this is not true,','[{"text": "Because angular momentum must be in the direction of spin of electron", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Because Bohr model gives incorrect values of angular momentum", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Because only one of these would have a minimum  energy", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Because electrons go around only in circular orbits", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Bohr model, does not give correct values of angular momentum of revolving electron. It gives only the magnitude of angular momentum, which is a vector. So the given statement in the question is not true.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,'NCERT Exemplar','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44746,'physics',39,1102,2629,'Maximum velocity of the photoelectron emitted by a metal is $1.8 \\times {10^6}\\,m{s^{ - 1}}$. Take the value of specific charge of the electron is $1.8 \\times {10^{11}}Ck{g^{ - 1}}$. Then the stopping potential in volt is','[{"text": "1", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "3", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "9", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "6", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'As $\\frac{1}{2}\\,m{v_{{{\\max }^2}}} = e{V_s}$<br>where $$v_{\\max }$$ is the maximum velocity of the electron and ${V_s}$ is the stopping potential<br>${V_s} = \\frac{1}{2}\\frac{m}{e}{v_{{{\\max }^2}}}$<br>here, $\\frac{e}{m} = 1.8 \\times {10^{11}}Ck{g^{ - 1}},{v_{\\max }} = 1.8 \\times {10^6}\\,m{s^{ - 1}}$<br>$\\therefore {V_s} = \\frac{1}{2} \\times \\frac{1}{{1.8 \\times {{10}^{11}}}} \\times {\\left( {1.8 \\times {{10}^6}} \\right)^2} = 9\\,V$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2013,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44747,'physics',38,1065,2542,'A plane electromagnetic wave propagating in $x$-direction is described by ${E_y} = \\left( {200\\,V{\\mkern 1mu} {m^{ - 1}}} \\right)\\sin \\left[ {1.5 \\times {{10}^7}t - 0.05x} \\right];$ Theintensity of the wave is :&lt;br&gt;(Use ${ \\in _0} = 8.85 \\times {10^{ - 12}}{C^2}\\,{N^{ - 1}}\\,{m^{ - 2}}$)','[{"text": "$35.4\\\\,\\\\,W{m^{ - 2}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$26.6\\\\,\\\\,W{m^{ - 2}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$106.2\\\\,\\\\,W{m^{ - 2}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$53.1\\\\,\\\\,W{m^{ - 2}}$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Average intensity of $E M$ wave in terms of Electric field is given by<br>$I=\\dfrac{1}{2} c \\varepsilon_{0} E_{0}^{2}$<br>where $c = $Speed of $EM$ wave in vaccum<br>$\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{E_0} = $Amplitude of electric field<br>$ = \\frac{1}{2} \\times 3 \\times {10^8} \\times 8.85 \\times {10^{ - 12}} \\times {(200)^2}$<br>$Z = 53.1\\,W{\\rm{/}}{m^2}$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',2024,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44748,'physics',37,977,2770,'A step down transformer connected to an ac mains supply of 220 $V$ is made to operate at 11 $V$, 44 $W$ lamp. Ignoring power losses in the transformer, what is the current in the primary circuit ?','[{"text": "$0.4\\\\,A$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2\\\\,A$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$4\\\\,A$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$0.2\\\\,A$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Given that<br>${v_p} = 220\\,V$<br>${v_s} = 11\\,\\,V$<br>${V_s}{I_s} = {V_p}{I_p}$<br>$44 = \\left( {220} \\right){I_p}$<br>${I_p} = \\frac{{44}}{{220}} = 0.2A$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2021,'NEET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44749,'physics',39,1102,2628,'From the following graph of photo current aganist collector plate potential, for two different intensities of light $$I_{1}$$ and $$I_{2}$$ one can conclude<div class="matching-question"></div>','[{"text": "$$I_{1}=I_{2}$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$I_{1}>I_{2}$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${I_1} < {I_2}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "comparsion is not possible", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'For a given frequency of incident radiation, light of higher intensity produces larger value of current. Hence $$I_{2}>I_{1}$$.',NULL,'Medium','Graph/Figure',2017,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/39_44749_question_1.png'),
(44750,'physics',40,239,478,'The binding energy of a $H$-atom, considering an electron moving around a fixed nuclei (proton), is $B = \\frac{{m{e^4}}}{{8{n^2}\\varepsilon _0^2{h^2}}} \\cdot $ ($m$- mass of electron).  If one decides to work in a frame of reference where the electron is at rest, the proton would be moving around it. By similar arguments, the binding energy would be $B =  - \\frac{{M{e^4}}}{{8{n^2}\\varepsilon _0^2{h^2}}} \\cdot $ ($M = $ proton mass). This last expression is not correct because','[{"text": "The frame in which the electron is at rest is not inertial.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Bohr-quantisation applies only to electron", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$n$ would not be integral", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "The motion of the proton would not be in circular  orbits, even approximately.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,NULL,NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,'NCERT Exemplar','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44751,'physics',38,1065,2539,'If $\\vec{E}$ and $\\vec{K}$ represent electric field and propagation vectors of the EM waves in vacuum, then magnetic field vector is given by ($\\omega$ - angular frequency)','[{"text": "$\\\\dfrac{1}{\\\\omega}(\\\\vec{K} \\\\times \\\\vec{E})$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\vec{K} \\\\times \\\\vec{E}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\omega(\\\\vec{E} \\\\times \\\\vec{K})$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\omega(\\\\vec{K} \\\\times \\\\vec{E})$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Here, the magnetic field vector will be along $\\hat{K} \\times \\vec{E}$, Where $\\hat{K}$ is the unit vector along the direction of propagation.<br>Also, $|\\vec{B}|=\\dfrac{|\\vec{E}|}{c} \\Rightarrow \\vec{B}=\\left(\\dfrac{E}{c}\\right)<br>\\dfrac{\\hat{K} \\times \\vec{E}}{E}$<br>$=\\dfrac{\\hat{K} \\times \\vec{E}}{c}=\\dfrac{\\hat{K} \\times \\vec{E}}{\\omega / K} \\quad\\left[\\because c=\\dfrac{\\omega}{K}\\right]$<br>$=\\dfrac{K \\hat{K} \\times \\vec{E}}{\\omega}=\\dfrac{\\vec{K} \\times \\vec{E}}{\\omega} \\quad[\\because K \\hat{K}=\\vec{K}]$<br>So option (1) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2023,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44752,'physics',37,975,2278,'A series $LCR$ circuit containing 5.0$H$ inductor, $80\\mu F$  capacitor and $40\\Omega $ resistor is connected to 230$V$ variable frequency ac source . The angular frequencies of the source at which power transferred to the circuit is half the power at the resonant angular frequency are likely to be :','[{"text": "50 $rad/s$ and 25 $rad/s$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "46$rad/s$ and 54 $rad/s$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "42$rad/s$ and 58 $rad/s$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "25 $rad/s$ and 75 $rad/s$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Resonant angular frequency<br>${\\omega _0} = \\frac{1}{{\\sqrt {LC} }} = \\frac{1}{{\\sqrt {5 \\times 80 \\times {{10}^{ - 6}}} }}$<br>${\\omega _0} = 50\\,rad/\\sec $<br>The frequencies at which the power is half of the maximum power is<br>${\\omega _n} = {\\omega _0} \\pm \\frac{R}{{2L}}$<br>$ = 50 \\pm 4$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',2021,'NEET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44818,'physics',37,973,2268,'In a $LCR$ series circuit, the potential difference between the terminals of the inductance is 60$V$. between the terminals of the capacitor is 30$V$ and that across the resisistance is 40$V$. Then, supply voltage will be equal to','[{"text": "$50\\\\,V$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$70\\\\,V$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$130\\\\,V$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$10\\\\,V$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'In $LCR$ series circuit<br>$V = \\sqrt {V_R^2 + {{({V_L} - {V_C})}^2}}  = \\sqrt {{{(40)}^2} + {{(60 - 30)}^2}} $<br>$ = \\sqrt {{{(40)}^2} + {{(30)}^2}} $ $ = \\sqrt {1600 + 900}  = 50V$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2006,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44753,'physics',39,1102,2628,'The following graph represents the variation of photocurrent with anode potential for a metal surface. Here $$I_{1}, I_{2}$$ and $$I_{3}$$ represents intensities and $$\\gamma_{1}, \\gamma_{2}$$ and $$\\gamma_{3}$$ represent frequency for curves 1, 2 and 3 respectively then<div class="matching-question"></div>','[{"text": "$$\\\\gamma_{1}=\\\\gamma_{2}$$ and $$I_{1}=I_{2}$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$\\\\gamma_{2}=\\\\gamma_{3}$$ and $$I_{1}=I_{3}$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$\\\\gamma_{1}=\\\\gamma_{2}$$ and $$I_{1} \\\\neq I_{2}$$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$$\\\\gamma_{1}=\\\\gamma_{3}$$ and $$I_{1}=I_{3}$$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'For 1 and 2, the stopping potentials are the same. Hence frequencies of incident radiation are also same.<br>$$\\therefore \\quad \\gamma_{1}=\\gamma_{2}$$<br>But for 1 and 2, saturation photocurrents are different and hence the corresponding intensities are also different.<br>$\\therefore \\quad {I_1} \\ne {I_2}$',NULL,'Medium','Graph/Figure',2020,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/39_44753_question_1.png'),
(44754,'physics',40,241,480,'Taking the Bohr radius as ${a_0} = 53\\,pm,$ the radius of $L{i^{ +  + }}$ ion in its ground state, on the basis of  Bohr’s  model, will be about','[{"text": "$27\\\\,pm$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$53\\\\,pm$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$13\\\\,pm$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$18\\\\,pm$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'On the basis of Bohr’s model, <br>$r = \\frac{{{n^2}{h^2}{\\varepsilon _0}}}{{\\pi m{e^2}Z}} = {a_0}\\frac{{{n^2}}}{Z}$<br>For $L{i^{ +  + }}$ ion, $Z = 3;\\,n = 1$ for ground state<br>Given, ${a_0} = 53\\,pm$<br>$\\therefore \\,\\,\\,r = \\frac{{53 \\times {1^2}}}{3} = 18\\,pm$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,'NCERT Exemplar','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44755,'physics',38,1065,2540,'The electric field and magnetic field components of an electromagnetic wave going through vacuum is described by<br>$E_{x}=E_{0} \\sin (k z-\\omega t) ; B_{y}=B_{0} \\sin (k z-\\omega t)$<br>then the correct relation between $E_{0}$ and $B_{0}$ is given by','[{"text": "$E_{0}=k B_{0}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\omega E_{0}=k B_{0}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$k E_{0}=\\\\omega B_{0}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$E_{0} B_{0}=\\\\omega k$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Given, $E_{x}=E_{0} \\sin (k z-\\omega t)$$B_{x}=B_{0} \\sin (k z-\\omega t)$<br>The magnitude of $\\vec{E}$ and $\\vec{B}$ are related as, $\\dfrac{E}{B}=c \\Rightarrow \\dfrac{E_{0}}{B_{0}}=c=$ velocity of E.M wave,<br>$c=f \\lambda=\\left(\\dfrac{\\omega}{2 \\pi}\\right)\\left(\\dfrac{2 \\pi}{k}\\right)=\\dfrac{\\omega}{k}$<br>$\\Rightarrow \\dfrac{E_{0}}{B_{0}}=\\dfrac{\\omega}{k} \\Rightarrow k E_{0}=\\omega B_{0}$<br>So, correct option is (3).',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2023,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44756,'physics',37,973,2268,'An inductor of inductance $L$, a capacitor of capacitance $C$ and a resistor of resistance $‘R’$ are connected in series to an ac source of potential difference $‘V’$ volts as shown in figure. Potential difference across $L, C$ and $R$ is 40$V$, 10$V$ and 40$V$, respectively. The amplitude of current flowing through $LCR$ series circuit is $10\\sqrt 2 A$. The impedance of the circuit is:','[{"text": "$5\\\\sqrt 2 \\\\,\\\\Omega $", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$4\\\\,\\\\Omega $", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$5\\\\,\\\\Omega $", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$4\\\\sqrt 2 \\\\,\\\\Omega $", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'$V = \\sqrt {V_R^2 + {{\\left( {{V_L} - {V_C}} \\right)}^2}}  = \\sqrt {{{40}^2} + {{\\left( {40 - 10} \\right)}^2}} $<br>$ = \\sqrt {{{40}^2} + {{30}^2}}  = 50\\,volt$<br>$z = \\frac{{{V_{rms}}}}{{{I_{rms}}}} = \\frac{{50}}{{\\left( {\\frac{{10\\sqrt 2 }}{{\\sqrt 2 }}} \\right)}} = 5\\,\\Omega $',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',2021,'NEET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/37_44756_question_1.png'),
(44757,'physics',39,1102,2629,'If the maximum kinetic energy of emitted electrons in photoelectric effect is $3.2 \\times {10^{ - 19}}\\,J$ and the work - function for metal is $6.63 \\times {10^{ - 19}}\\,J$, then the stopping potential and threshold wavelength respectively are [ planck\'s constant $ h = 6.63 \\times {10^{34}}\\,J - s$ [velocity of light $c = 3 \\times {10^8}\\frac{m}{s}$, ] [charge of electron $\\left. { = 1.6 \\times {{10}^{ - 19}}C} \\right]$','[{"text": "$4V,6000\\\\mathop A\\\\limits^o $", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$3\\\\,V,4000\\\\mathop A\\\\limits^o $", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2\\\\,V,3000\\\\mathop A\\\\limits^o $", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$1V,1000\\\\mathop A\\\\limits^o $", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'The maximum kinetic energy emitted photoelectron is<br>$$K E_{\\max }=e V_{s}$$<br>where, $$V_{s}=$$ stopping potential<br>$ \\Rightarrow {V_s} = \\frac{{{K_{\\max }}}}{e} = \\frac{{3.2 \\times {{10}^{ - 19}}}}{{1.6 \\times {{10}^{ - 19}}}} = 2\\,V$<br>Also, the work - function of a metal is<br>$$\\phi=\\dfrac{1242}{\\lambda_{0}(n m)} e V$$<br>${\\lambda _0} = \\frac{{1242 \\times 1.6 \\times {{10}^{ - 19}}}}{\\phi }nm$<br>$ = \\frac{{1242 \\times 1.6 \\times {{10}^{ - 19}}}}{{6.63 \\times {{10}^{ - 19}}}}\\,nm$<br>$ = 3000\\mathop A\\limits^o $',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2020,'MHTCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44758,'physics',38,1065,2540,'A plane electromagnetic wave of frequency $20\\,MHz$ propagates in free space along positive $x$-direction. At a particular space and time, $\\vec{E}=6.6 \\hat{j} V / m$. What is $\\vec{B}$ at this point?','[{"text": "$2.2 \\\\times {10^{ - 8}}\\\\,\\\\hat kT$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$2.2 \\\\times {10^{ - 8}}\\\\,\\\\hat i\\\\,T$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$ - 2.2 \\\\times {10^{ - 8}}\\\\,\\\\hat kT$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$ - 2.2 \\\\times {10^{ - 8}}\\\\,\\\\hat i\\\\,T$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'The magnitude of $B$ is,<br>$B=\\dfrac{E}{c}=\\dfrac{6.6}{3 \\times 10^{8}}=2.2 \\times 10^{-8} {~T}$<br>Direction of $\\vec{B}$ is along $+\\hat{z}$ direction.<br>So, $\\vec{B}=2.2 \\times 10^{-8} \\hat{k} T$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2023,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44759,'physics',37,972,2261,'The peak voltage of the $ac$ source is equal to','[{"text": "The rms value of the ac source", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\sqrt 2 $ times the rms value of the ac source", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$1/\\\\sqrt 2 $ time the rms value fo the ac source", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "the value of voltage supplied to the circuit.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Peak voltage is $\\sqrt 2 $ times rms voltage in alternating current.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2022,'NEET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44761,'physics',39,1102,2629,'The graph of stopping potential $$v_{s}$$ against frequency $$v_{0}$$ of incident radiation is plotted for two different metals \\[P\\] and \\[Q\\] as shown in the graph. $$\\phi_{p}$$ and $$\\phi_{Q}$$ are work - functions of \\[P\\] and \\[Q\\] respectively, then<div class="matching-question"></div>','[{"text": "$$\\\\phi_{P}>\\\\phi_{Q}$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$\\\\phi_{P} < \\\\phi_{Q}$$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$$\\\\phi_{P}=\\\\phi_{Q}$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$v_{0}^{\\\\prime} < v_{0}$$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'The work - function of a surface is<br>$$\\phi=h v_{0}$$<br>where, $h = $ planck\'s constant<br>and $$v_{0}=$$ threshold frequency<br>From graph it is clear that<br>$$\\begin{aligned}& \\left(v_{0}\\right)_{P} < \\left(v_{0}\\right)_{Q} \\\\& \\therefore \\phi_{P} < \\phi_{Q}\\end{aligned}$$',NULL,'Medium','Graph/Figure',2020,'MHTCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/39_44761_question_1.png'),
(44762,'physics',38,1067,2544,'Read the Assertion and Reason carefully to mark the correct options given below:<br><br><strong>Assertion:</strong> EM waves used for optical communication have longer wavelengths than that of microwave, employed in Radar technology.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Infrared EM waves are more energetic than microwaves, (used in Radar).','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but reason is correct.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Radio waves are used for optical communication have shorter wavelength than microwaves. Hence, assertion is incorrect. Infrared rays have higher frequency and energy than microwaves. Hence, reason is true.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',2023,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44763,'physics',37,975,2275,'A series $LCR$ circuit with inductance 10 $H$, capacitance $10\\mu F$, resistance $50\\Omega $ is conencted to an $ac$ source of voltage,$V = 200\\,\\sin (100\\,t)\\,volt$ . If the resonant frequency of the $LCR$ circuit is ${v_0}$ and the frequency of the $ac$ soruce is $v$, then','[{"text": "${v_o} = v = \\\\frac{{50}}{\\\\pi }Hz$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${v_o} = \\\\frac{{50}}{\\\\pi }Hz,\\\\,v = 50\\\\,Hz$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$v = 100\\\\,Hz;\\\\,{v_o} = \\\\frac{{100}}{\\\\pi }Hz$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${v_o} = v = 50\\\\,Hz$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'$\\omega  = 100$<br>$v = \\frac{\\omega }{{2\\pi }} = \\frac{{100}}{{2\\pi }} = \\frac{{50}}{\\pi }Hz$<br>Resonance frequency<br>${v_0} = \\frac{1}{{2\\pi \\sqrt {LC} }} = \\frac{1}{{2\\pi }}\\sqrt {\\frac{1}{{10 \\times 10 \\times {{10}^{ - 6}}}}} $<br>$ = \\frac{{50}}{\\pi }Hz$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',2022,'NEET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44765,'physics',39,1102,2629,'The light of wavelength $$\\lambda$$ incident on the surface of metal having work function $$\\phi$$ emits the electrons. The maximum velocity of electrons emitted is [ $c = $ velocity of light, $h = $ planck\'s constant, $m = $ mass of electron]','[{"text": "$\\\\left[ {\\\\frac{{2(hv - \\\\phi )\\\\lambda }}{{mc}}} \\\\right]$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\left[ {\\\\frac{{2(hv - \\\\lambda \\\\phi )}}{{m\\\\lambda }}} \\\\right]^{1/2}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${\\\\left[ {\\\\frac{{2(hv - \\\\lambda )}}{{m\\\\lambda }}} \\\\right]^{1/2}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\left[ {\\\\frac{{2(hv - \\\\phi )}}{{m\\\\lambda }}} \\\\right]$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'In photoelectric effect, the maximum kinetic energy possessed by the particle,<br>$$\\begin{array}{r}K E_{\\max }=h v-\\phi \\\\\\dfrac{1}{2} m v^{2}=\\dfrac{h c}{\\lambda}-\\phi\\left(\\because v=\\dfrac{c}{\\lambda}\\right) \\\\\\Rightarrow v^{2}=\\dfrac{2(h c-\\phi \\lambda)}{\\lambda m} \\\\v=\\left[\\dfrac{2(h c-\\phi \\lambda)}{\\lambda m}\\right]^{1 / 2}\\end{array}$$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2020,'MHTCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44766,'physics',38,1068,2545,'Which of the following Maxwell\'s equation is valid for time varying conditions but not valid for static conditions?','[{"text": "$\\\\oint \\\\vec{E} \\\\cdot d \\\\vec{l}=0$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\oint \\\\vec{E} \\\\cdot d \\\\vec{l}=-\\\\dfrac{\\\\partial \\\\phi_{B}}{\\\\partial t}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\oint \\\\vec{B} \\\\cdot d \\\\vec{l}=\\\\mu_{0} I$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\oint \\\\vec{D} \\\\cdot d \\\\vec{A}=Q$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'For time varying conditions, the Maxwell equation is given by<br>$\\oint \\vec{E} \\cdot \\overrightarrow{d l}=-\\dfrac{\\partial \\phi_{B}}{\\partial t}=$ Induced $emf$ in a loop and $\\vec{E}$ is the induced non-electrostatic electric field produced by variable magnetic field.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2023,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44767,'physics',37,972,2260,'The frequency of an alternating current is 50 $Hz$, What is the minimum time taken by current to reach its peak value from $rms$ value ?','[{"text": "$0.2\\\\,{\\\\rm{s}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$5 \\\\times {10^{ - 3}}\\\\,{\\\\rm{s}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$10 \\\\times {10^{ - 3}}\\\\,{\\\\rm{s}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2.5 \\\\times {10^{ - 3}}\\\\,{\\\\rm{s}}$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Alternating current $I = {I_0}\\sin (2\\pi vt)$, where $v = 50\\,Hz$<br>If the current, starting from zero, reaches rms  value at time ${t_1},$<br>${I_0}{\\rm{/}}\\sqrt 2  = {I_0}\\sin \\,(2\\pi v{t_1})$<br>$ \\Rightarrow \\sin \\,(2\\pi v{t_1}) = 1/\\sqrt 2  = \\sin \\,\\left( {\\frac{\\pi }{4}} \\right)$<br>$\\therefore \\quad {t_1} = 1{\\rm{/}}(8v)$<br>Similarly if the current reaches peak value at time ${t_2},$<br>${I_0} = {I_0}\\sin \\,(2\\pi v{t_2}) \\Rightarrow \\sin \\,(2\\pi v{t_2}) = 1 = \\sin (\\pi {\\rm{/}}2)$<br>$\\therefore \\quad {t_2} = 1{\\rm{/}}(4v)$<br>Hence time to reach peak value from rms value:<br>${t_2} - {t_1} = 1{\\rm{/}}(8v) = 1{\\rm{/}}(8 \\times 50){\\rm{s}}$<br>$ = 1{\\rm{/}}400\\,s = 2.5 \\times {10^{ - 3}}s$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44768,'physics',39,1102,2629,'The maximum velocity of the photoelectrons emitted by the metal surface is \' $$v$$ \'. Charge and mass of the photoelectron is denoted by \' $e$ \' and \' $m$ \' respectively. The stopping potential in $volt$ is','[{"text": "$\\\\frac{{{v^2}}}{{2\\\\left( {\\\\frac{m}{e}} \\\\right)}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{{{v^2}}}{{2\\\\left( {\\\\frac{e}{m}} \\\\right)}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{{{v^2}}}{{\\\\left( {\\\\frac{e}{m}} \\\\right)}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{{{v^2}}}{{\\\\left( {\\\\frac{m}{e}} \\\\right)}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Given, maximum velocity of a photoelectron $ = v$<br>charge of photoelectron $ = e$<br>and mass of photoelectron $ = m$<br>Let, the stopping potential of the photoelectron $ = V$<br>Then, the maximum kinetic energy<br>$$\\begin{array}{r}K E_{\\max }=e V \\Rightarrow \\dfrac{1}{2} m v^{2}=e v \\\\\\left(\\because K E=\\dfrac{1}{2} m v^{2}\\right) \\\\\\Rightarrow V=\\dfrac{v^{2} m}{2 e} \\text { or } V=\\dfrac{v^{2}}{2\\left(\\dfrac{e}{m}\\right)}\\end{array}$$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2020,'MHTCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44770,'physics',38,1065,2539,'In an electromagnetic wave, at an instant and at a particular position, the electric field is along the negative $z$-axis and magnetic field is along the positive $x$-axis. Then the direction of propagation of electromagnetic wave is','[{"text": "positive $z$-axis", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "positive $y$-axis", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "negative $y$-axis", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "at $45^{\\\\circ}$ angle from positive -axis.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'For an electromagnetic wave, the direction of propagation is given by, the direction of the vector $\\vec{E} \\times \\vec{B}$.<br>$e=E \\times B$<br>As, $\\vec{E}$ is in $-z$ direction and $\\vec{B}$ is in $+x$ direction. So, $\\vec{E} \\times \\vec{B}=(-E \\hat{K}) \\times(B \\hat{i})$<br>$=E B(-\\hat{j})$<br>Thus, it is evident that electromagnetic wave will propagate in negative $y$-axis.',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',2023,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44771,'physics',37,972,2260,'A multimeter reads a voltage of a certain $A$.$C$. source as 100$V$. What is the peak value of voltage of $A$.$C$. source ?','[{"text": "$141.4\\\\,{\\\\rm{V}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$200\\\\,{\\\\rm{V}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$400\\\\,{\\\\rm{V}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$100\\\\,{\\\\rm{V}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'The multimeter reads the rms value of $A.C$ source.<br>$\\therefore \\,{V_{rms}} = 100V$<br>The peak voltage of the source is<br>${V_0} = \\sqrt 2 {V_{rms}} = \\sqrt 2  \\times 100\\,V = 141.4\\,V$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2014,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44772,'physics',39,1102,2629,'When photons of energy hv fall on a metal plate of work function \' $$W_{0}$$ \', photoelectrons of maximum kinetic energy $\'K\'$ are ejected. If the frequency of the radiation is doubled, the maximum kinetic energy of the ejected photo electrons will be','[{"text": "$$K+W_{0}$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$K + hv$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$K$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2\\\\,K$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Given, energy of photon $$=$$ $hv$,<br>work function $$=W_{0}$$ and<br>Maximum kinetic energy $ = K$<br>So, from the Einstein\'s photoelectric equation<br>$$E=K+W_{0} \\Rightarrow h v=K+W_{0}$$<br>$ \\Rightarrow K = hv - {W_0}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,(1)$<br>If frequency of the radiation is doubled, then Einstein\'s Photoelectric equation changed as<br>${K^\\prime } = h(2v) - {W_0}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,(2)$<br>by subtracting the eq.(1) from (2), we get<br>$$\\begin{aligned}& K^{\\prime}-k=h(2 v)-h v-W_{0}+W_{0} \\\\& \\Rightarrow K^{\\prime}=K+h v\\end{aligned}$$<br>So, option (2) is correct',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2019,'MHTCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44773,'physics',37,972,2259,'Find the average value of current (in $A$) shown graphically in figure from $t = 0$ to $t = 2s$','[{"text": "$5{\\\\rm{A}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$2{\\\\rm{A}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1{\\\\rm{A}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$3{\\\\rm{A}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'From the $i - t$ graph, area from $t = 0$ to $t = 2s$<br>$\\frac{1}{2} \\times 2 \\times 10 = 10As$<br>$\\therefore $ Average current  $ = \\frac{{10}}{2} = 5A$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/37_44773_question_1.png'),
(44774,'physics',38,1066,2543,'Match Column-I with Column-II of electromagnetic waves with corresponding wavelength range. Choose the correct answer from the options\ngiven below.<div class="matching-question"><div class="column-left">A. Microwave<br>B. Ultraviolet<br>C. $X$-rays<br>D. Infra - red</div><div class="column-right">P. $400\\,nm$ to $1\\,nm$<br>Q. $1\\,nm\\,{10^{ - 3}}nm$<br>R. $1\\,nm$ to $700\\,nm$<br>S. $0.1\\,m$ to $1\\,nm$</div></div>','[{"text": "${\\\\rm{A}} - {\\\\rm{S}},\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\rm{B}} - {\\\\rm{P}},\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\rm{C}} - {\\\\rm{R}},\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\rm{D}} - {\\\\rm{Q}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{A}} - {\\\\rm{S}},\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\rm{B}} - {\\\\rm{P}},\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\rm{C}} - {\\\\rm{Q}},\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\rm{D}} - {\\\\rm{R}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{A}} - {\\\\rm{S}},\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\rm{B}} - {\\\\rm{Q}},\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\rm{C}} - {\\\\rm{P}},\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\rm{D}} - {\\\\rm{R}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{A}} - {\\\\rm{P}},\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\rm{B}} - {\\\\rm{S}},\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\rm{C}} - {\\\\rm{Q}},\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\rm{D}} - {\\\\rm{R}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Microwaves $-0.1 {~m}$ to $1 {~mm}$<br>Ultraviolet $-400 {~nm}$ to $1 {~nm} ; X$-rays $-1 {~nm}$  to $10^{-3} {~nm}$ Infrared $-1 {~mm}$ to $700 {~nm}$.<br>So, A - S, B - P, C - Q, D - R',NULL,'Hard','Match the following',2023,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL);
INSERT INTO `qb_questions` (`id`, `subject_id`, `chapter_id`, `topic_id`, `subtopic_id`, `question_html`, `options`, `correct_index`, `explanation`, `explanation_image_url`, `difficulty`, `qtype`, `year`, `tag`, `created_at`, `question_image_url`) VALUES
(44776,'physics',39,1102,2629,'When a certain metal surface is illuminated with a light of wavelength $$\\lambda$$, the stopping potential is $V$, when the same surface is illuminated by light of wavelength $$2 \\lambda$$, the stoping potential is $$\\left(\\dfrac{V}{3}\\right)$$. The threshold wavelength for the surface is','[{"text": "$$\\\\dfrac{8 \\\\lambda}{3}$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$\\\\dfrac{4 \\\\lambda}{3}$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$4 \\\\lambda$$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$$6 \\\\lambda$$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Given a metal, wavelength of light used $$=\\lambda$$<br>Stopping potential $ = V$<br>If $$\\lambda_{0}$$ be the threshold wavelength, then maximum kinetic energy of emitted electrons<br>${K_{\\max }} = hc\\left( {\\frac{1}{\\lambda } - \\frac{1}{{{\\lambda _0}}}} \\right)\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,(1)$<br>Again, wavelength of used light $$\\lambda^{\\prime}=2 \\lambda$$<br>Stopping potential $$V^{\\prime}=\\dfrac{V}{3}$$<br>then $$K E_{\\max }=h c\\left(\\dfrac{1}{\\lambda^{\\prime}}-\\dfrac{1}{\\lambda_{0}}\\right)$$<br>$$\\Rightarrow e V^{\\prime}=h c\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2 \\lambda}-\\dfrac{1}{\\lambda_{0}}\\right)$$<br>$ \\Rightarrow \\frac{{eV}}{3} = hc\\left( {\\frac{1}{{2\\lambda }} - \\frac{1}{{{\\lambda _0}}}} \\right)\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,(2)$<br>From eq.(1) and (2), we have<br>$$\\Rightarrow \\dfrac{e V}{\\dfrac{e V}{3}}=\\dfrac{h c\\left(\\dfrac{1}{\\lambda}-\\dfrac{1}{\\lambda_{0}}\\right)}{h c\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2 \\lambda}-\\dfrac{1}{\\lambda_{0}}\\right)}$$<br>$$3\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2 \\lambda}-\\dfrac{1}{\\lambda_{0}}\\right)=\\dfrac{1}{\\lambda}-\\dfrac{1}{\\lambda_{0}}$$<br>$$\\Rightarrow \\lambda_{0}=4 \\lambda$$<br>So threshold wavelength is 4 times of wavelength of light.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2019,'MHTCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44777,'physics',37,973,2263,'The reactance of a coil when used in the $AC$ power supply (220 $V$, 50 $Hz$) is  $50\\Omega $. The inductance of the coil is nearly:','[{"text": "$0.16H$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$0.22H$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2.2H$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1.6H$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Reactance of the coil or inductive reactance is given as,<br>${X_L} = \\omega L = 2\\pi fL$<br>where, $f$ is frequency<br>Given ${X_L} = 50\\Omega ,f = 50cps$<br>$\\therefore L = \\frac{{{X_L}}}{\\omega } = \\frac{{{X_L}}}{{2\\pi f}} = \\frac{{50}}{{2\\pi  \\times 50}} = \\frac{1}{{2 \\times 3.14}} = 0.16H$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44778,'physics',38,1065,2539,'An electromagnetic wave is transporting energy in the negative $z$ direction. At a certain point and certain time the direction of electric field of the wave is along positive $y$ direction. What will be the direction of the magnetic field of the wave at that point and instant?','[{"text": "Positive direction of $z$.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Negative direction of $x$.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Positive direction of $x$.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Negative direction of $y$.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Direction of wave propagation, $\\vec{v}=v(-\\hat{k})$ direction of electric field, $\\vec{E}=E \\hat{j}$<br>As $\\vec{v}=\\vec{E} \\times \\vec{B}$<br>So,  $\\vec v\\,( - \\hat k) = \\vec E\\,\\hat j \\times \\vec B\\,\\hat i$<br>So, direction of $\\vec{B}$ is along $+x$ axis.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2023,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44779,'physics',40,242,481,'The de Broglie wave present in third Bohr orbit is<div class="matching-question"></div>','[{"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'$2\\pi r = n\\lambda ,\\,n = 3$<br>The circumference is equal to three times the wavelength of the electron.',NULL,'Medium','Graph/Figure',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44780,'physics',39,1102,2629,'In photoelectric emission process from a metal of work function $1.8\\,eV$, the kinetic energy of most energetic electrons is $0.5\\,eV$. The corresponding stopping potential is','[{"text": "$0.5\\\\,V$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$1.3\\\\,V$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1.8\\\\,V$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2.3\\\\,V$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'As we know that stopping potential gives the maximum $KE$ of ejected electrons, so<br>$K{E_{\\max {\\text{ }}}} = e{V_0} = 1.6 \\times {10^{ - 19}} \\times {V_0}$<br>$ \\Rightarrow \\quad {V_0} = 0.5\\,V$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44781,'physics',37,973,2263,'The voltage across a pure inductor is represented by the following diagram. Which one of the following diagrams will represent the current?','[{"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": true}]',3,NULL,NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/37_44781_question_1.png'),
(44782,'physics',38,1068,2545,'Match Column I with Column II. Choose the correct answer from the options given below.<div class="matching-question"><div class="column-left">A. Gauss\'s law in Electrostatics<br>B. Faraday\'s law<br>C. Gauss\'s law nagnetism<br>D. Ampere Maxwell Law</div><div class="column-right">P.  $\\oint {\\vec E.{\\mkern 1mu} \\,d\\vec l}  =  - \\frac{{d{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\phi _B}}}{{dt}}$<br>Q. $\\oint {\\vec B.\\,d\\vec A}  = 0$<br>R. $\\oint {\\vec B.\\,d\\vec l = } {\\mu _0}{i_c} + {\\mu _0}{\\varepsilon _0}\\frac{{d{\\phi _E}}}{{dt}}$<br>S. $\\oint {\\vec E.\\,d\\vec s = \\frac{{{q_{net}}}}{{{{\\rm{\\varepsilon }}_0}}}} $</div></div>','[{"text": "${\\\\rm{A}} - {\\\\rm{P}},\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\rm{B}} - {\\\\rm{Q}},\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\rm{C}} - {\\\\rm{R}},\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\rm{D}} - {\\\\rm{S}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{A}} - {\\\\rm{S}},\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\rm{B}} - {\\\\rm{P}},\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\rm{C}} - {\\\\rm{Q}},\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\rm{D}} - {\\\\rm{R}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{A}} - {\\\\rm{R}},\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\rm{B}} - {\\\\rm{S}},\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\rm{C}} - {\\\\rm{P}},\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\rm{D}} - {\\\\rm{Q}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{A}} - {\\\\rm{Q}},\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\rm{B}} - {\\\\rm{R}},\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\rm{C}} - {\\\\rm{S}},\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\rm{D}} - {\\\\rm{P}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Gauss\'s law in electrostatics<br>$\\Rightarrow \\phi=\\oint \\vec{E} \\cdot d \\vec{s}=\\dfrac{q_{\\text {net }}}{\\varepsilon_{0}}$<br>Faraday\'s law $\\Rightarrow \\oint \\vec{E} \\cdot d \\vec{l}=-\\dfrac{d \\phi_{B}}{d t}$<br>Gauss\'s law in magnetism, $\\oint \\vec{B} \\cdot d \\vec{A}=0$, as the net magnetic pole strength enclosed by the Gaussion surface is zero.<br>Ampere\'s Maxwell law,<br>$\\oint {\\vec B}  \\cdot d\\vec l = {\\mu _0}{i_c} + {\\mu _0}{\\varepsilon _0}\\frac{{d{\\phi _E}}}{{dt}}$<br>${\\rm{A}} - {\\rm{S}},{\\rm{B}} - {\\rm{P}},{\\rm{C}} - {\\rm{Q}},{\\rm{D}} - {\\rm{R}}$',NULL,'Hard','Match the following',2023,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44790,'physics',38,1065,2539,'Read the Statement - A and Statement - B carefully to mark the correct options given below:<div class="matching-question"><div class="column-left">Electromagnetic waves are not deflected by electric and magnetic field.</div><div class="column-right">The amplitude of electric field and the magnetic field in electromagnetic waves are related to each other as $E_{0}=\\sqrt{\\dfrac{\\mu_{0}}{\\varepsilon_{0}}} B_{0}$.</div></div>','[{"text": "Both Statement A and  Statement B are incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Statement A and  Statement B are correct.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Statement A is correct but Statement B is incorrect.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Statement A is in correct but Statement B is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Statement A: True, as the electromagnetic waves are stream of photons which are not charged particles, so they are not deflected  by electric and magnetic fields.<br>Statement B : False, as $E_{0}=\\sqrt{\\dfrac{1}{\\mu_{0} \\varepsilon_{0}}} \\cdot B_{0}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ type-2',2023,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44783,'physics',39,1102,2629,'When light of wavelength $$\\lambda$$ is incident on photosensitive surface, the stopping potential is $V$. When light of wavelength $$3 \\lambda$$ is incident on same surface, the stopping potential is $$\\dfrac{V}{6}$$. Threshold wavelength for the surface is','[{"text": "$$2 \\\\lambda$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$3 \\\\lambda$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$4 \\\\lambda$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$5 \\\\lambda$$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'From Einstein\'s photoelectric equation, $$\\dfrac{h c}{\\lambda}-\\phi=e V$$<br>In first case, for $$\\lambda$$ stopping potential is $V$, i.e., equation becomes<br>$\\frac{{hc}}{\\lambda } - \\phi  = eV\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,(1)$<br>Similarly in second case, for $$3 \\lambda$$ stopping potential is $$\\dfrac{V}{6}$$<br>i.e., equation becomes<br>$$\\begin{aligned}& \\dfrac{h c}{3 \\lambda}-\\phi=\\dfrac{e V}{6} \\\\& \\Rightarrow \\dfrac{2 h c}{\\lambda}-\\phi=e V\\end{aligned}$$<br>From eq.(1) and eq.(2) by subtracting<br>$$\\begin{aligned}& \\dfrac{2 h c}{\\lambda}=e V+6 \\phi \\\\& \\dfrac{h c}{\\lambda}=e V+\\phi \\\\& \\dfrac{h c}{\\lambda}=5 \\phi\\end{aligned}$$<br>and $$\\phi=\\dfrac{h c}{\\lambda_{0}}$$<br>Thus, $$\\lambda_{0}=5 \\lambda$$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44784,'physics',37,973,2264,'A capacitor of capacitance $10\\mu F$ is connected to an $AC$ source and an $AC$ ammeter. If the source voltage varies as $V = 50\\sqrt 2 \\sin 100t$ the reading of the ammeter is','[{"text": "$50\\\\,mA$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$70.7\\\\,mA$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$5.0\\\\,mA$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$7.07\\\\,mA$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Given, $C = 10\\mu F = {10^{ - 5}}F,{V_0} = 50\\sqrt 2 V$<br>$\\omega  = 100\\,rad\\,{s^{ - 1}}$<br>$\\therefore \\,\\,{X_C} = \\frac{1}{{\\omega C}} = \\frac{1}{{100 \\times {{10}^{ - 5}}}} = 1000\\,\\Omega $<br>$ \\Rightarrow {\\rm{Z}} = {{\\rm{X}}_{\\rm{C}}} = 1000\\,\\Omega $<br>$\\therefore \\,\\,{I_{rms}} = \\frac{{{V_{rms}}}}{Z} = \\frac{{{V_0}{\\rm{/}}\\sqrt 2 }}{Z} = \\frac{{50\\sqrt 2 }}{{\\sqrt 2  \\times 1000}}A$<br>$ = 005A = 50\\,mA$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44786,'physics',38,1065,2539,'Which of the following are true?<br>(A) Speed of light in vacuum is dependent on the direction of propagation.<br>(B) Speed of light in a medium is independent of the wavelength of light.<br>(C) The speed of light is independent of the motion of the source.<br>(D) The speed of light in a medium is independent of intensity.<br>Choose the connect answer from the options given below.','[{"text": "B and D only", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "C and D only", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "A and C only", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "B and C only", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'C and D only.The speed of light is always independent of the motion of source as $c=1 / \\sqrt{\\mu_{0} \\in_{0}}$.<br>The speed of light in a medium is independent of intensity as $v=c / n$, where $n$ is the refractive index of the medium.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2023,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44787,'physics',39,1102,2629,'When monochromatic light of wavelength $$\\lambda$$ is incident on a metallic surface, the stopping potential for photoelectric current is $$3 V_{0}$$. When the same surface is illuminated with light of wavelength $$2 \\lambda$$, the stopping potential is $$V_{0}$$<br>The threshold wavelength for this surface when photoelectric effect takes place is','[{"text": "$$\\\\lambda$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$2 \\\\lambda$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$3 \\\\lambda$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$4 \\\\lambda$$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'From photoelectric equation,<br>$$h v-h v_{0}=e v_{0} \\text { or } \\dfrac{h c}{\\lambda}-\\dfrac{h c}{\\lambda_{0}}=e v_{0}$$<br>Case I: $hc\\left( {\\frac{1}{\\lambda } - \\frac{1}{{{\\lambda _0}}}} \\right) = e\\left( {3{V_0}} \\right)\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,(1)$<br>Case II : $hc\\left( {\\frac{1}{{2\\lambda }} - \\frac{1}{{{\\lambda _0}}}} \\right) = e{V_0}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,(2)$<br>On dividing eq.(1) by eq.(2), we get <br>$$\\begin{aligned}& \\dfrac{\\left(\\lambda_{0}-\\lambda\\right)}{\\left(\\lambda_{0}-2 \\lambda\\right) / 2}=\\dfrac{3}{1} \\\\& \\text { or } 2 \\lambda_{0}-2 \\lambda=3 \\lambda_{0}-6 \\lambda \\\\& \\Rightarrow \\lambda_{0}=4 \\lambda\\end{aligned}$$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2015,'MHTCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44788,'physics',37,973,2264,'An alternating voltage $e = 200\\sqrt 2 \\sin (100t)$  volt is connected to $1\\mu F$ capacitor through $AC$ ammeter. The reading of ammeter is','[{"text": "$5mA$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$10mA$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$15mA$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$20mA$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Given,$e = 200\\sqrt 2 \\sin \\left( {100t} \\right)$          (1)<br>Capacitance of capacitor (C) $ = 1\\mu F = 1 \\times {10^{ - 6}}F$<br>From eq. (1), peak voltage,<br>${V_0} = 200\\sqrt 2 \\,{\\rm{V}}$<br>$\\omega  = 100\\,{\\rm{rad/s}}$<br>Now,${I_{rms}} = \\frac{{{V_{rms}}}}{{{X_C}}}$<br>$$\\because $$ ${V_{rms}} = \\frac{{{V_0}}}{{\\sqrt 2 }} \\Rightarrow {I_{rms}} = \\frac{{{V_0}}}{{\\sqrt 2 {X_C}}}$<br>$$ \\Rightarrow {I_{rms}} = \\frac{{{V_0}\\omega C}}{{\\sqrt 2 }}\\left( {\\because {X_C} = \\frac{1}{{\\omega C}}} \\right)$$<br>$ \\Rightarrow {I_{rms}} = \\frac{{200 \\times \\sqrt 2  \\times 100 \\times 1 \\times {{10}^{ - 6}}}}{{\\sqrt 2 }}$<br>$ \\Rightarrow {I_{rms}} = 20 \\times {10^{ - 3}}A = 20mA$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2018,'MHTCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44789,'physics',39,1102,2626,'Find the maximum wavelength of light that can cause photoelectric effect in Lithium (work function $ = 2.5\\,eV$ )','[{"text": "$497\\\\,nm$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$397\\\\,nm$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$749\\\\,nm$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$237\\\\,nm$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Work function of lithium, ${\\phi _0} = 2.5\\,eV$<br>$\\lambda  = \\frac{{hc}}{{{\\phi _0}}} = \\frac{{\\left( {6.62 \\times {{10}^{ - 34}}J.{s^{ - 1}}} \\right) \\times \\left( {3 \\times {{10}^8}\\,m/s} \\right)}}{{2.5 \\times 1.6 \\times {{10}^{ - 19}}\\,J}}$<br>$ = 497\\,nm$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44799,'physics',37,973,2265,'An inductor of inductance $L$ and resistor $R$ are joined together in series and connected by a source of frequency $\\omega $. The power dissipated in the circuit is','[{"text": "$\\\\frac{V}{{{R^2}{\\\\omega ^2}{L^2}}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{{{R^2} + {\\\\omega ^2}{L^2}}}{V}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{{{V^2}R}}{{\\\\sqrt {{R^2} + {\\\\omega ^2}{L^2}} }}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{{{V^2}R}}{{{R^2} + {\\\\omega ^2}{L^2}}}$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'${\\rm{Power}}\\,{\\rm{P}} = {\\rm{VI}}\\cos \\phi  = {\\rm{V}} \\cdot \\frac{{\\rm{V}}}{{\\rm{Z}}} \\cdot \\frac{{\\rm{R}}}{{\\rm{Z}}} = \\frac{{{{\\rm{V}}^{\\rm{2}}}{\\rm{R}}}}{{{{\\rm{Z}}^{\\rm{2}}}}}$<br>As impendance $Z = \\sqrt {{\\omega ^2}{L^2} + {R^2}} ,$<br>$P = \\frac{{{V^2}R}}{{({\\omega ^2}{L^2} + {R^2})}}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2019,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44792,'physics',37,973,2265,'A choke is preferred to a resistance for limiting current in $AC$ circuit because','[{"text": "Choke is cheap", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "There is no wastage of power", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Choke is compact in size", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Choke is a good absorber of heat", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'A choke acts as an inductor when alternating current flows through it. The inductor offers purely reactive impedance to the $AC$, reducing the current without consuming any power as the current and voltage across the inductor have quarter cycle phase difference. Due to very low ohmic loss, using a choke in a $AC$ circuit minimises power wastage.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2006,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44793,'physics',39,1102,2629,'Monochromatic radiation emitted when electron on hydrogen atom jumps from first excited state to the ground state irradiates a photosensitive material. The stopping potential is measured to be $3.57\\,V$. The threshold frequency of the material is','[{"text": "$1.6 \\\\times {10^{15}}\\\\,Hz$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$4 \\\\times {10^{15}}\\\\,Hz$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2.5 \\\\times {10^{15}}\\\\,Hz$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$5 \\\\times {10^{15}}\\\\,Hz$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'When an electron in hydrogen atom jumps from first excited state $$(n=2)$$ to ground state $$(n=1)$$ energy is released and is given by where, $\\quad E = 10.2eV$<br>Now, from photoelectric equation, work function,<br>$\\phi  = E - 3.57eV = hv$<br>$v = \\frac{{E - 3.57eV}}{h} = \\frac{{(10.2 - 3.57)eV}}{{6.67 \\times {{10}^{ - 34}}}}$<br>$v = \\frac{{6.63 \\times 1.6 \\times {{10}^{ - 19}}}}{{6.67 \\times {{10}^{ - 34}}}} = 1.6 \\times {10^{15}}\\,Hz$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44794,'physics',38,1065,2541,'A small object at rest, absorbs a light pulse of power $20\\,mW$ and duration $300\\,ns$. Assuming speed of light as $3 \\times {10^8}\\,m/s$, the momentum of the object becomes equal to','[{"text": "$2 \\\\times {10^{ - 17}}\\\\,kg\\\\,m/s$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$3 \\\\times {10^{ - 17}}\\\\,kg\\\\,m/s$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$0.5 \\\\times {10^{ - 17}}\\\\,kg\\\\,m/s$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1 \\\\times {10^{ - 17}}\\\\,kg\\\\,m/s$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'$U$ ( energy $)=$ Power $\\times$ time<br>$ = \\left( {20 \\times {{10}^{ - 3}}} \\right) \\times \\left( {300 \\times {{10}^{ - 9}}\\,s} \\right)$<br>$ = 6 \\times {10^{ - 9}}{\\rm{ joules}}$<br>Momentum gained by the object is,<br>$p=\\dfrac{U}{c}=\\dfrac{6 \\times 10^{-9}}{3 \\times 10^{8}}$$=2 \\times 10^{-17} {~kg} {~ms}^{-1}$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',2023,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44796,'physics',37,973,2265,'In the $A.C$. circuit shown, keeping $‘K’$ pressed,if an iron rod is inserted into the coil, the bulb in the circuit.','[{"text": "Gets damaged", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Glows less brightly", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Glows with same brightness (as before the rod is inserted)", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Glows more brightly", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'As the presence of the iron rod increases the self-inductance and hence the inductive reactance ofthe coil, the net impedance of the circuit increases and that causes the current in the circuit to decrease.<br>$\\therefore $ The bulb glows less brightly.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/37_44796_question_1.png'),
(44797,'physics',39,1102,2629,'Energy of the incident photon on the metal surface is $3\\,W$ and then $5\\,W$, where $W$ is the work function for that metal. The ratio of velocities of emitted photoelectrons is','[{"text": "$$1: 4$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$1: 2$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$1: \\\\sqrt{2}$$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$$1: 1$$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'In photoelectric emission,<br>Kinetic energy of emitted electron<br>= energy of incident photon - work - function<br>$$\\begin{gathered}\\Rightarrow \\dfrac{1}{2} m v^{2}=E-W \\\\\\text { or } v=\\sqrt{\\dfrac{2(E-W)}{m}} \\\\\\therefore \\dfrac{v_{1}}{v_{2}}=\\sqrt{\\dfrac{2\\left(E_{1}-W\\right)}{m}} \\times \\sqrt{\\dfrac{m}{2\\left(E_{2}-W\\right)}}=\\sqrt{\\dfrac{E_{1}-W}{E_{2}-W}} \\\\=\\sqrt{\\dfrac{3 W-W}{5 W-W}}=\\sqrt{\\dfrac{2}{4}}=\\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\text { or } 1: \\sqrt{2}\\end{gathered}$$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2020,'MHTCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44798,'physics',38,1065,2542,'A point source of $100\\,W$ emits light with $5 \\%$ efficiency. At a distance of $5\\,m$ from the source, the intensity produced by the electric field component is','[{"text": "$\\\\dfrac{1}{40 \\\\pi} \\\\dfrac{W}{m^{2}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{1}{20 \\\\pi} \\\\dfrac{W}{m^{2}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{1}{2 \\\\pi} \\\\dfrac{W}{m^{2}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{1}{10 \\\\pi} \\\\dfrac{W}{m^{2}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Effective power emitted by the source<br>$P_{e}=100 \\times \\dfrac{5}{100}$ Watt $=5$ Watt.<br>Intensity at a distance of $5 {~m}$ form the source is given by<br>$I = \\frac{{{P_e}}}{{4\\pi {r^2}}} = \\frac{5}{{4\\pi {{(5)}^2}}}W/{m^2} = \\frac{1}{{20\\pi }}.$<br>$50 \\%$ of this energy is carried by electric field and $50 \\%$ is carried by magnetic field.<br>$\\therefore$ Intensity produced by electric field component is given by  ${I_E} = \\frac{1}{2},{I_e} = \\frac{1}{{40\\pi }}W/{m^2}.$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2023,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44801,'physics',39,1104,2631,'A particle of mass $$M$$ at rest decays into two particles of masses $$m_{1}$$ and $$m_{2}$$, having non-zero velocities. The ratio of the de-Broglie wavelengths of the particles, $$\\lambda_{1} / \\lambda_{2}$$, is','[{"text": "$$m_{2} / m_{1}$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$m_{1} / m_{2}$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$\\\\sqrt{m_{2}} / \\\\sqrt{m_{1}}$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "1.0", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'de-Broglie wavelength<br>$$\\lambda=\\dfrac{h}{p} \\therefore \\dfrac{\\lambda_{1}}{\\lambda_{2}}=\\dfrac{p_{2}}{p_{1}}$$<br>And momentum is conserved in decay so $$p_{1}=p_{2}$$ and $$\\dfrac{\\lambda_{1}}{\\lambda_{2}}=1$$.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44802,'physics',38,1065,2542,'The energy density associated with electric field $\\vec{E}$ and magnetic field $\\vec{B}$ of an electromagnetic wave in free space is given by $\\left(\\varepsilon_{0}\\right.$ - permittivity of free space, $\\mu_{0}$ - permeability of free space)','[{"text": "${u_E} = \\\\frac{{{E^2}}}{{2{\\\\varepsilon _0}}},{u_B} = \\\\frac{{{\\\\mu _0}{B^2}}}{2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${u_E} = \\\\frac{{{\\\\varepsilon _0}{E^2}}}{2},{u_B} = \\\\frac{{{B^2}}}{{2{\\\\mu _0}}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${u_E} = \\\\frac{{{\\\\varepsilon _0}{E^2}}}{2},{u_B} = \\\\frac{{{\\\\mu _0}{B^2}}}{2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${u_E} = \\\\frac{{{E^2}}}{{2{\\\\varepsilon _0}}},{u_B} = \\\\frac{{{B^2}}}{{2{\\\\mu _0}}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'In free space, the energy density of a static field $E$ is  ${u_E} = \\frac{1}{2}{\\varepsilon _0}{E^2},$  and the energy density of magnetic field is  ${u_B} = \\frac{1}{{2{\\mu _0}}}{B^2}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2023,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44803,'physics',37,974,2274,'A resistor of $500\\Omega $, an inductance of 0.5 $H$ are in series with an $a.c.$ voltage source which is given by $V = 100\\sqrt 2 \\sin (1000t)$. The power factor of the combination is','[{"text": "$\\\\frac{1}{{\\\\sqrt 2 }}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{1}{{\\\\sqrt 3 }}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$0.5$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$0.6$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Here,$R = 500\\Omega ,L = 0.5\\,H$<br>Compare $V = 100\\sqrt 2 \\sin (1000t)$ with<br>$V = {V_0}\\sin \\omega t$,<br>we get,   $\\omega  = $ 1000 rad/s<br>The inductive reactance is <br>${X_L} = \\omega L = (1000)(0.5) = 500\\Omega $<br>Impedance of the $RL$ circuit is<br>$Z = \\sqrt {{R^2} + X_L^2} $ $ = \\sqrt {{{(500\\Omega )}^2} + {{(500\\Omega )}^2}} $<br>$ = 500\\sqrt 2 \\Omega $<br>Power factor, $\\cos \\phi  = \\frac{R}{Z} = \\frac{{500\\Omega }}{{500\\sqrt 2 \\Omega }} = \\frac{1}{{\\sqrt 2 }}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2012,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44804,'physics',40,239,478,'The graph between wave number \\[{\\bar \\nu }\\] and angular frequency \\[\\omega \\] is<div class="matching-question"></div>','[{"text": "", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'$\\omega  = 2\\pi v = \\frac{{2\\pi c}}{\\lambda } = 2\\pi c{\\rm{\\bar \\nu }} \\Rightarrow \\omega  \\propto {\\rm{\\bar \\nu }}$',NULL,'Medium','Graph/Figure',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44805,'physics',38,1065,2540,'The amplitude of magnetic field in an electromagnetic wave propagating along $y$-axis is $6.0 \\times {10^{ - 7}}\\,T$. The maximum value of electric field in the electromagnetic wave is','[{"text": "$2 \\\\times {10^{15}}V{m^{ - 1}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$6.0 \\\\times {10^{ - 7}}\\\\,V\\\\,{m^{ - 1}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$5 \\\\times {10^{14}}V{m^{ - 1}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$180\\\\,V{m^{ - 1}}$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'For a electromagnetic wave, amplitude of electric and magnetic field are related by,<br>$\\dfrac{E_{m}}{B_{m}}=c$<br>$\\therefore E_{m}=B_{m} \\times C$<br>$=6 \\times 10^{-7} \\times 3 \\times 10^{8}=18 \\times 10 ; E=180 {Vm}^{-1}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2023,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44806,'physics',39,1104,2631,'An electron is 2000 times lighter than a proton. Both are moving such that their matter waves have same wavelength. The ratio of their kinetic energies in approximation is:','[{"text": "$$1: 1$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$1: 2000$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$2000: 1$$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$$1: 200$$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Since both have same de-Broglie wavelength, hence both must have equal value of momentum.<br>Since $$E=\\dfrac{P^{2}}{2 m}$$<br>$$\\dfrac{E_{e}}{E_{P}}=\\dfrac{m_{p}}{m_{e}}=2000$$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44807,'physics',37,973,2267,'Out of the following graphs, which graphs shows the correct relation(graphical representation) for $LC$ parallel resonant circuit?','[{"text": "$1$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$3$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$4$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'In a parallel resonant circuit, at resonating frequency, the current would be minimum.<br>This is correctly depicted in the graph (4).',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2017,'MHTCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/37_44807_question_1.png'),
(44808,'physics',38,1065,2542,'The electric field in an electromagnetic wave is given as $\\vec{E}=20 \\sin \\omega\\left(t-\\dfrac{x}{c}\\right) \\hat{j} N C^{-1}$.<br>Where $\\omega$ and $c$ are angular frequency and velocity of electromagnetic wave respectively. The energy contained in a volume of $5 \\times {10^{ - 4}}\\,{m^3}$ will be<br>(Given ${\\varepsilon _0} = 8.85 \\times {10^{ - 12}}{C^2}/N{m^2}$)','[{"text": "$17.7 \\\\times {10^{ - 13}}\\\\,J$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$88.5 \\\\times {10^{ - 13}}\\\\,J$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$8.85 \\\\times {10^{ - 13}}\\\\,J$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$28.5 \\\\times {10^{ - 13}}\\\\,J$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'$\\vec{E}=20 \\sin \\omega\\left(t-\\dfrac{x}{c}\\right) \\hat{j} N / C$<br>From the given equation, $E_{0}=20 {~N} / {C}$<br>Volume $=5 \\times 10^{-4} {~m}^{3}$<br>Average energy density of an em wave<br>$=\\dfrac{1}{2} \\varepsilon_{0} E_{0}^{2}$<br>Energy stored $=\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2} \\varepsilon_{0} E_{0}^{2}\\right)($ volume $)$<br>$=\\dfrac{1}{2} \\times 8.85 \\times 10^{-12} \\times(20)^{2} \\times\\left(5 \\times 10^{-4}\\right) J$<br>$=8.85 \\times 10^{-13} {~J}$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',2023,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44809,'physics',39,1104,2631,'A photon and electron have same de Broglie wavelength. Given that $v$ is the speed of electron and $$c$$ is the velocity of light. $$E_{e}, E_{p}$$ are the kinetic energies of electron and photon respectively. $$p_{e}, p_{h}$$ are the momentum of electron and photon respectively. Then which of the following relation is correct?','[{"text": "$\\\\frac{{{E_e}}}{{{E_p}}} = \\\\frac{{2c}}{v}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{{{P_e}}}{{{P_h}}} = \\\\frac{{2c}}{v}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{{{E_e}}}{{{E_p}}} = \\\\frac{v}{{2c}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{{{P_e}}}{{{P_h}}} = \\\\frac{c}{{2v}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'As,<br>${E_e} = \\frac{1}{2}m{v^2} = \\frac{1}{2}(mv)v = \\frac{1}{2}\\left( {\\frac{h}{\\lambda }} \\right)v$<br>and ${\\quad {E_p} = \\frac{{hc}}{\\lambda };}$<br>$\\therefore \\quad \\frac{{{E_e}}}{{{E_p}}} = \\frac{v}{{2c}}$<br>Now, ${p_e} = mv = \\frac{h}{\\lambda }$<br>and $$\\quad p_{P}=\\dfrac{h}{\\lambda} \\therefore \\dfrac{p_{e}}{p_{P}}=1$$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44811,'physics',37,974,2766,'Power dissipated in an $L-C-R$ series circuit connected to an $AC$ source of emf  $\\varepsilon $ is','[{"text": "$\\\\frac{{{\\\\varepsilon ^2}R}}{{\\\\left[ {{R^2} + {{\\\\left( {L\\\\omega  - \\\\frac{1}{{C\\\\omega }}} \\\\right)}^2}} \\\\right]}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{{{\\\\varepsilon ^2}\\\\left[ {{R^2} + {{\\\\left( {L\\\\omega  - \\\\frac{1}{{C\\\\omega }}} \\\\right)}^2}} \\\\right]}}{R}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{{{\\\\varepsilon ^2}R}}{{\\\\sqrt {{R^2} + {{\\\\left( {L\\\\omega  - \\\\frac{1}{{C\\\\omega }}} \\\\right)}^2}} }}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{{{\\\\varepsilon ^2}\\\\sqrt {{R^2} + {{\\\\left( {L\\\\omega  - \\\\frac{1}{{C\\\\omega }}} \\\\right)}^2}} }}{R}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Power dissipated in series $LCR$<br>$P = {I^2}_{rms}R$<br>$ = \\frac{{\\varepsilon _{rms}^2R}}{{{{\\left| Z \\right|}^2}}} = \\frac{{{\\varepsilon ^2}R}}{{\\left[ {{R^2} + {{\\left( {\\omega L - \\frac{1}{{\\omega C}}} \\right)}^2}} \\right]}}$<br>As ${I_{rms}} = \\frac{{{V_{rms}}}}{Z}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44812,'physics',39,1104,2631,'An electron (mass $$m$$ ) with an initial velocity $v = {v_0}\\hat i\\left( {{v_0} > 0} \\right)$ in an electric field $$E=E_{0} \\hat{i}$$ $$\\left(E_{0}=\\right.$$ constant $$\\left.>0\\right)$$. It\'s de Broglie wavelength at time $$t$$ is given by','[{"text": "$$\\\\lambda_{0}$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$\\\\lambda_{0} t$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{{{\\\\lambda _0}}}{{m\\\\left( {1 + \\\\frac{{e{E_0}t}}{{m{v_0}}}} \\\\right)}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${\\\\lambda _0}\\\\left( {1 + \\\\frac{{e{E_0}t}}{{m{v_0}}}} \\\\right)$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Initial de Broglie wavelength of electron,<br>${\\lambda _0} = \\frac{h}{{m{v_0}}}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,(1)$<br>Force on electron in electric field,<br>$\\overline F  =  - e\\overline E  =  - e\\left[ { - {E_0}\\widehat i} \\right] = e{E_0}\\widehat i$<br>Acceleration of electron $a = \\frac{F}{m} = \\frac{{e{E_0}}}{{\\,m}}$<br>Velocity of electron after time $$t$$,<br>$v = {v_0} + \\left( {\\frac{{e{E_0}}}{{\\,m}}} \\right)t$<br>$ = \\left( {{v_0} + \\frac{{e{E_0}}}{{\\,m}}t} \\right) = {v_0}\\left( {1 + \\frac{{e{E_0}}}{{m{v_0}}}t} \\right)$<br>de Broglie wavelength associated with electron at time $$t$$ is<br>$\\lambda  = \\frac{h}{{m{v_0}}} = \\frac{h}{{m\\left[ {{v_0}\\left( {1 + \\frac{{e{E_0}}}{{m{v_0}}}t} \\right)} \\right]}} = \\frac{{{\\lambda _0}}}{{m\\left[ {1 + \\frac{{e{E_0}}}{{m{v_0}}}t} \\right]}}$<br>[from Eq. (1)]',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44813,'physics',38,1066,2543,'Match Column I with Column II.  Choose the correct answer from the options given below.<div class="matching-question"><div class="column-left">A. Microwave<br>B. Gamma rays<br>C. Radio waves<br>D. $X$-rays</div><div class="column-right">P. Radioactive decay of nucleus<br>Q. Rapid acceleration and deceleration of electrons in aerials<br>R. Inner shell electrons<br>S. Klystron valve</div></div>','[{"text": "${\\\\rm{A}} - {\\\\rm{R}},{\\\\mkern 1mu} {\\\\mkern 1mu} {\\\\mkern 1mu} {\\\\mkern 1mu} {\\\\mkern 1mu} {\\\\rm{B}} - {\\\\rm{Q}},{\\\\mkern 1mu} {\\\\mkern 1mu} {\\\\mkern 1mu} {\\\\mkern 1mu} {\\\\mkern 1mu} {\\\\rm{C}} - {\\\\rm{R}},{\\\\mkern 1mu} {\\\\mkern 1mu} {\\\\mkern 1mu} {\\\\mkern 1mu} {\\\\mkern 1mu} {\\\\rm{D}} - {\\\\rm{S}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{A}} - {\\\\rm{P}},\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\rm{B}} - {\\\\rm{R}},\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\rm{C}} - {\\\\rm{S}},\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\rm{D}} - {\\\\rm{Q}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{A}} - {\\\\rm{S}},\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\rm{B}} - {\\\\rm{P}},\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\rm{C}} - {\\\\rm{Q}},\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\rm{D}} - {\\\\rm{R}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{A}} - {\\\\rm{S}},\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\rm{B}} - {\\\\rm{R}},\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\rm{C}} - {\\\\rm{Q}},\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\rm{D}} - {\\\\rm{P}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Microwaves $\\rightarrow$ Klystron valve<br>Gamma rays $\\rightarrow$ Radioactive decay of nucleus<br>Radio waves $\\rightarrow$ Rapid acceleration and deceleration of electron in aerials<br>$X$-rays $\\rightarrow$ Inner shell electrons<br>Hence, ${\\rm{A}} - {\\rm{S}},\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{\\rm{B}} - {\\rm{P}},\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{\\rm{C}} - {\\rm{Q}},\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{\\rm{D}} - {\\rm{R}}$',NULL,'Hard','Match the following',2023,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44814,'physics',37,974,2272,'In an $AC$ circuit the emf $(V)$ and the current $(i)$ at any instant are given respectively by $V = {V_0}\\,\\sin \\,\\omega t$,$i = {i_0}\\sin \\left( {\\omega t - \\phi } \\right)$. The average power in the circuit over one cycle of $AC$ is','[{"text": "$\\\\frac{{{V_0}{i_0}}}{2}\\\\sin \\\\phi $", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${V_0}{i_0}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{{{V_0}{i_0}}}{2}\\\\cos \\\\phi $", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{{{V_0}{i_0}}}{2}$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'${P_{av}} = {V_{rms}}.{i_{rms}}\\cos \\phi  = \\frac{1}{2}{V_0}{i_0}\\cos \\phi $',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44816,'physics',38,1065,2542,'The ratio of average electric energy density and total average energy density of electromagnetic wave is','[{"text": "2", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "3", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{1}{2}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "1", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Average electric energy density = average magnetic energy density $=\\dfrac{1}{2}$ total average energy density<br>So, the ratio of,  <br>$\\dfrac{\\text { average electric energy density }}{\\text { total average energy density }}=\\dfrac{1}{2}$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2023,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44817,'physics',39,1104,2631,'The potential energy of a particle of mass $$m$$ is given by<br>$$U(x)= \\begin{cases}E_{0}: & 0 \\leq x \\leq 1 \\\\ 0 ; & x>1\\end{cases}$$<br>$$\\lambda_{1}$$ and $$\\lambda_{2}$$ are the de Broglie wavelength of the particle, when $$0 \\leq x \\leq 1$$ and $$x>1$$ respectively. If the total energy of particle is $$2 E_{0}$$, the ratio $$\\dfrac{\\lambda_{1}}{\\lambda_{2}}$$ will be','[{"text": "1", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "2", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$\\\\dfrac{1}{\\\\sqrt{2}}$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$\\\\sqrt{2}$$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'$$K . E .=2 E_{0}-E_{0}=E_{0}($$ for $$0 \\leq x \\leq 1)$$<br>$$\\Rightarrow \\quad \\lambda_{1}=\\dfrac{h}{\\sqrt{2 m E_{0}}}$$<br>$$K . E .=2 E_{0}(\\text { for } x>1)$$<br>$$\\Rightarrow \\lambda_{2}=\\dfrac{h}{\\sqrt{4 m E_{0}}} \\Rightarrow \\dfrac{\\lambda_{1}}{\\lambda_{2}}=\\sqrt{2}$$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44820,'physics',38,1065,2542,'The energy of an electromagnetic wave contained in a small volume oscillates with','[{"text": "zero frequency", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "double the frequency of the wave", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "half the frequency of the wave", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "the frequency of the wave.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Let the electric field is $E=E_{0} \\sin (\\omega t-k x)$<br>The energy density due to electric field is<br>${U_E} = \\frac{1}{2}{\\varepsilon _0}{E^2} = \\frac{1}{2}{\\varepsilon _0}E_0^2{\\sin ^2}(\\omega t - kx)$<br>$U_{E}=\\dfrac{1}{4} \\varepsilon_{0} E_{0}^{2}[1-\\cos 2(\\omega t-k x)]$<br>$\\left[\\cos 2 \\theta=1-2 \\sin ^{2} \\theta\\right]$<br>Again, $B=B_{0} \\sin (\\omega t-k x)$.<br>Thus, $U_{B}=\\dfrac{1}{4} \\dfrac{B_{0}^{2}}{\\mu_{0}}[1-\\cos 2(\\omega t-k x)]$<br>So, the frequency of $U_{E}$ and $U_{B}$ is double the frequency of electromagnetic wave.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2023,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44821,'physics',39,1102,2629,'The work function of metals $$A$$ and $$B$$ are in the ratio of 1:2. If light of frequencies $$f$$ and $$2 f$$ are incident on the surfaces of $A$ and $B$ respectively, the ratio of the maximum kinetic energies of photoelectrons emitted is ( $$f$$ is greater than threshold frequency of $$\\mathrm{A}, 2 f$$ is greater than threshold frequency of $B$)','[{"text": "$$1: 2$$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$$1: 1$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$1: 4$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$1: 3$$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'$E = {W_0} + {K_{\\max }}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,(1)$<br>$$\\Rightarrow h f=W_{A}+K_{A}$$<br>and $\\quad 2hf = {W_B} + {K_B} = 2{W_A} + {K_B}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,(2)$<br>$$\\left(\\because \\dfrac{W_{A}}{W_{B}}=\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right)$$<br>Dividing equation (1) by (2)<br>$$\\dfrac{1}{2}=\\dfrac{W_{A}+K_{A}}{2 W_{A}+K_{B}} \\Rightarrow \\dfrac{K_{A}}{K_{B}}=\\dfrac{1}{2}$$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44822,'physics',37,973,2268,'In a series $LCR$ circuit, the potential drop across $L,C$ and $R$ respectively 40$V$, 120$V$ and 60$V$. Then the source voltage is','[{"text": "$220\\\\,V$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$160\\\\,V$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$180\\\\,V$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$100\\\\,V$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Here,${V_L} = 40V,{V_C} = 120V,{V_R} = 60V$<br>Source voltage,$V = \\sqrt {V_R^2 + {{({V_L} - {V_C})}^2}} $<br>$V = \\sqrt {{{(60)}^2} + {{(40 - 120)}^2}} $<br>$V = \\sqrt {{{(60)}^2} + {{(80)}^2}}  = 100\\,V$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44823,'physics',38,1068,2545,'If a source of electromagnetic radiation having power $15\\,kW$ produces $10^{16}$ photons per second, the radiation belongs to a part of spectrum is<br> (Take Planck constant  $h = 6 \\times {10^{ - 34}}\\,Js\\,)$','[{"text": "Micro waves", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Gamma rays", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Radio wave", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Ultraviolet rays", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Power, $P=15 {~kW}$<br>$P=\\dfrac{E}{t}=\\dfrac{n h c}{\\lambda}$<br>$\\lambda=\\dfrac{n h c}{P}=\\dfrac{10^{16} \\times 6.63 \\times 10^{-34} \\times 3 \\times 10^{8}}{15 \\times 10^{3}}$<br>$\\lambda=1.32 \\times 10^{-13} {~m}$<br>$\\lambda < 10^{-12}$ lies in range of Gamma rays',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',2023,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44824,'physics',39,1102,2629,'Given that a photon of light of wavelength $10,000\\mathop A\\limits^o $ has an energy equal to $1.23\\,eV$. When light of wavelength $5000\\mathop A\\limits^o $ and intensity $$I_{0}$$ falls on a photoelectric cell, the surface current is $0.40 \\times {10^{ - 6}}\\mathop A\\limits^o $ and the stopping potential is $1.36\\,V$, then the work function is','[{"text": "$0.55\\\\,eV$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1.10\\\\,eV$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$1.53\\\\,eV$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$0.43\\\\,eV$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'As, $$E=\\dfrac{h c}{\\lambda}$$ or $$E \\propto \\dfrac{1}{\\lambda}$$<br>$\\therefore \\frac{{{E_2}}}{{{E_1}}} = \\frac{{{\\lambda _1}}}{{{\\lambda _2}}}{\\text{ }}$<br>${\\text{or}}\\quad {E_2} = {E_1} \\times \\frac{{{\\lambda _1}}}{{{\\lambda _2}}} = 1.23 \\times \\frac{{10000}}{{5000}} = 2.46\\,eV$<br>Now, $hv - {\\phi _0} = \\frac{1}{2}m{v_{{{\\max }^2}}} = e\\,{V_s}$<br>${\\phi _0} = h{v_2} - e\\,{V_s} = {E_2} - e\\,{V_s}{\\text{ }}$<br>${\\text{or }}\\quad  = 2.46 - 1.36 = 1.10\\,eV$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44826,'physics',37,973,2268,'In the given circuit the peak voltages across $C$, $L$ and $R$ are 30$V$, 110 $V$ and 60 $V$ respectively. The rms value of the applied voltage is','[{"text": "$70.7\\\\,V$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$141\\\\,V$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$100\\\\,V$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$200\\\\,V$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Here,${V_C} = 30V,{V_L} = 110\\,V,{V_R} = 60V$<br>Then ${V_0} = \\sqrt {V_R^2 + {{({V_L} - {V_C})}^2}} $<br>$ = \\sqrt {{{60}^2} + {{(110 - 30)}^2}} $<br>$ \\Rightarrow {V_0} = 100\\,V$<br>$\\therefore \\,\\,{V_{rms}} = \\frac{{{V_0}}}{{\\sqrt 2 }} = \\frac{{100}}{{\\sqrt 2 }} \\approx 70.7\\,V$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2020,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/37_44826_question_1.png'),
(44851,'physics',38,1065,2539,'The property which is not of an electromagnetic wave travelling in free space is that:','[{"text": "They are transverse in nature", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "The energy density in electric field is equal to energy density in magnetic field", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "They travel with a speed equal to ${\\\\dfrac{1}{\\\\sqrt{\\\\mu_{0} \\\\varepsilon_{0}}}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "They originate from charges moving with uniform speed", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'The EM waves are originated from accelerating charge. The charge moving with uniform velocity produces steady state magnetic field. Correct option is (4).',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2024,'NEET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44828,'physics',39,1102,2629,'When photons of wavelength $$\\lambda_{1}$$ are incident on an isolated sphere, the corresponding stopping potential is found to be $V$. When photons of wavelength $$\\lambda_{2}$$ are used, the corresponding stopping potential was thrice that of the above value. If light of wavelength $$\\lambda_{3}$$ is used then find the stopping potential for this case:','[{"text": "$\\\\frac{{hc}}{e}\\\\left[ {\\\\frac{1}{{{\\\\lambda _3}}} + \\\\frac{1}{{{\\\\lambda _2}}} - \\\\frac{1}{{{\\\\lambda _1}}}} \\\\right]$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$\\\\dfrac{h c}{e}\\\\left[\\\\dfrac{1}{\\\\lambda_{3}}+\\\\dfrac{1}{2 \\\\lambda_{2}}-\\\\dfrac{1}{\\\\lambda_{1}}\\\\right]$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$\\\\dfrac{h c}{e}\\\\left[\\\\dfrac{1}{\\\\lambda_{3}}-\\\\dfrac{1}{\\\\lambda_{2}}-\\\\dfrac{1}{\\\\lambda_{1}}\\\\right]$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$\\\\dfrac{h c}{e}\\\\left[\\\\dfrac{1}{\\\\lambda_{3}}+\\\\dfrac{1}{2 \\\\lambda_{2}}-\\\\dfrac{3}{2 \\\\lambda_{1}}\\\\right]$$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Let the threshold wavelength for sphere be $$\\lambda_{0}$$. According to Einstein\'s photoelectric equation<br>$e{V_s} = \\frac{{hc}}{\\lambda } - \\frac{{hc}}{{{\\lambda _0}}}$<br>$\\therefore eV = \\frac{{hc}}{{{\\lambda _1}}} - \\frac{{hc}}{{{\\lambda _0}}}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,(1)$<br>$3eV = \\frac{{hc}}{{{\\lambda _2}}} - \\frac{{hc}}{{{\\lambda _0}}}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,(2)$<br>$e{V^\\prime } = \\frac{{hc}}{{{\\lambda _3}}} - \\frac{{hc}}{{{\\lambda _0}}}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,(3)$<br>From eqns. (1) and (2)<br>$$\\dfrac{h c}{\\lambda_{0}}=\\dfrac{3 h c}{2 \\lambda_{1}}-\\dfrac{h c}{2 \\lambda_{2}}$$<br>Substituting in eqn. (3)<br>$$\\begin{gathered}e V^{\\prime}=\\dfrac{h c}{\\lambda_{3}}-\\dfrac{3 h c}{2 \\lambda_{1}}+\\dfrac{h c}{2 \\lambda_{2}} \\text { or } \\\\V^{\\prime}=\\dfrac{h c}{e}\\left[\\dfrac{1}{\\lambda_{3}}-\\dfrac{3}{2 \\lambda_{1}}+\\dfrac{1}{2 \\lambda_{2}}\\right]\\end{gathered}$$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44829,'physics',37,973,2268,'In the given circuit, the reading of voltmeter ${V_1}$ and  ${V_2}$ are $300V$ each. The readings of  voltmeter ${V_3}$ and ammeter $A$ are respectively','[{"text": "$220\\\\,V,2.0\\\\,A$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$220\\\\,V,2.2\\\\,A$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$100\\\\,V,2.0\\\\,A$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$150\\\\,V,2.2\\\\,A$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'For series $LCR$ circuit<br>Voltage $V = \\sqrt {{V_R}^2 + {{\\left( {{V_{_L}} - {V_c}} \\right)}^2}} $<br>Since,${V_L} = {V_C}$<br>Hence $V = {V_R} = 220V$<br>Also current $i = \\frac{V}{R} = \\frac{{220}}{{100}} = 2.2A$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/37_44829_question_1.png'),
(44830,'physics',40,243,482,'An electron with  $KE\\,\\,6\\,eV$ is incident on a hydrogen atom in its ground state. The collision','[{"text": "May be partially inelastic", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Must be completely inelastic", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "May be partially elastic", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Must be elastic", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'The minimum energy required for excitation of hydrogen atom is 10.2 $eV$. Here the $K$.$E$ of the incoming electron is less than 10.2  $eV$. So excitation does not take place. The $K$.$E$ of the electron will be retained as $K$.$E$ for the system. The collision must be perfectly elastic.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44831,'physics',39,1102,2629,'Radiation of wavelength $$\\lambda$$, is incident on a photocell. The fastest emitted electron has speed $$v$$. If the wavelength is changed to $$\\dfrac{3 \\lambda}{4}$$, the speed of the fastest emitted electron will be:','[{"text": "$$>v\\\\left(\\\\dfrac{4}{3}\\\\right)^{1 / 2}$$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$$ < v\\\\left(\\\\dfrac{4}{3}\\\\right)^{1 / 2}$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$=v\\\\left(\\\\dfrac{4}{3}\\\\right)^{1 / 2}$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$=v\\\\left(\\\\dfrac{3}{4}\\\\right)^{1 / 2}$$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'According to Einstein\'s photoelectric effectmaximum kinetic energy of a photoelectron,<br>$$K E=\\dfrac{1}{2} m v^{2}=\\dfrac{h c}{\\lambda}-\\phi$$<br>Assume speed of faster photoelectron is $$v^{\\prime}$$<br>when incident photon has wavelength $$\\dfrac{3 \\lambda}{4}$$.<br>$$\\therefore \\dfrac{1}{2} m v^{\\prime 2}=\\dfrac{4 h c}{3 \\lambda}-\\phi$$<br>or, $$\\quad \\dfrac{1}{2} m v^{\\prime 2}=\\dfrac{4}{3}\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2} m v^{2}+\\phi\\right)-\\phi$$<br>$$\\begin{aligned}& v^{\\prime}=\\sqrt{\\dfrac{4}{3} v^{2}+\\dfrac{2 \\phi}{3 m}} \\\\& \\therefore v^{\\prime}>\\sqrt{\\dfrac{4}{3}} v\\end{aligned}$$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44832,'physics',38,1067,2544,'Match Column I with Column II. Choose the correct answer from the options given below:<div class="matching-question"><div class="column-left">A. Microwave<br>B. UV rays<br>C. Infra-red light<br>D. $X$-rays</div><div class="column-right">P. Physiotherapy<br>Q. Treatment of cancer<br>R. Lasik eye surgery<br>S. Aircraft navigation</div></div>','[{"text": "${\\\\rm{A}} - {\\\\rm{S}},\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\rm{B}} - {\\\\rm{P}},\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\rm{C}} - {\\\\rm{Q}},\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\rm{D}} - {\\\\rm{R}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{A}} - {\\\\rm{Q}},\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\rm{B}} - {\\\\rm{S}},\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\rm{C}} - {\\\\rm{R}},\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\rm{D}} - {\\\\rm{P}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{A}} - {\\\\rm{S}},\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\rm{B}} - {\\\\rm{R}},\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\rm{C}} - {\\\\rm{P}},\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\rm{D}} - {\\\\rm{Q}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{A}} - {\\\\rm{R}},\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\rm{B}} - {\\\\rm{Q}},\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\rm{C}} - {\\\\rm{P}},\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\rm{D}} - {\\\\rm{S}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Properties of em waves are Microwaves are used in air craft navigation. (A)-(S)<br>UV rays are used in lasik eye surgery. (B)-(R)<br>Infrared lamps used in the treatment of muscular complaints. (C)-(P)<br>$X$-rays used in the treatment of cancer as $X$-rays can pass through flesh but not through bones in medical diagnosis. (D)-(Q)',NULL,'Medium','Match the following',2023,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44833,'physics',37,977,2771,'A 220 $V$ input is supplied to a transformer. The output circuit draws a current of 2.0 $A$ at 440 $V$. If the ratio of output to input power is 0.8, then the current drawn by primary winding is','[{"text": "$3.6\\\\,A$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$5.0\\\\,A$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$2.5A$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2.8A$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Given, input voltage supplied to transformer,<br>${V_1} = 220V$<br>Current at output circuit of transformer,<br>${I_2} = 2A$<br>${V_2} = 440V$<br>Given,$\\frac{{{\\rm{Output}}\\,{\\rm{power}}\\left( {{P_2}} \\right)}}{{{\\rm{Input}}\\,{\\rm{power}}\\left( {{P_1}} \\right)}} = 0.8 \\Rightarrow \\frac{{{V_2}{I_2}}}{{{V_1}{I_1}}} = 0.8$<br>$ \\Rightarrow \\frac{{440 \\times 2}}{{220 \\times {I_1}}} = 0.8$<br>$ \\Rightarrow {I_1} = \\frac{4}{{0.8}} = 5A$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2019,'MHTCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44835,'physics',39,1102,2629,'A photon of energy $E$ ejects a photoelectron from a metal surface whose work function is $$W_{0}$$. If this electron enters into a uniform magnetic field of induction $B$ in a direction perpendicular to the field and describes a circular path of radius $$r$$, then the radius $$r$$ is given by','[{"text": "$\\\\frac{{\\\\sqrt {2e\\\\left( {E - {W_0}} \\\\right)} }}{{mB}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{{\\\\sqrt {2e\\\\left( {{W_0} - E} \\\\right)} }}{{eB}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$\\\\sqrt{\\\\dfrac{2 m W_{0}}{e B}}$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$\\\\dfrac{\\\\sqrt{2 m\\\\left(E-W_{0}\\\\right)}}{e B}$$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'We know that Einstein equation, <br>$E - {W_0} = \\frac{1}{2}m{v^2}$<br>$ \\Rightarrow \\sqrt {\\frac{{2\\left( {E - {W_0}} \\right)}}{m}}  = v$<br>and a charged particle placed in uniform magnetic field experience a force<br>$$\\begin{aligned}& F=\\dfrac{m v^{2}}{r} \\\\& \\Rightarrow e v B=\\dfrac{m v^{2}}{r} \\\\& \\Rightarrow r=\\dfrac{m v}{e B}=\\dfrac{\\sqrt{2 m\\left(E-W_{0}\\right)}}{e B}\\end{aligned}$$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44837,'physics',37,977,2770,'Alternating current of peak value $\\left( {\\frac{2}{\\pi }} \\right)$  ampere flows through the primary coil of the transformer. The coefficient of mutual inductance between primary and secondary coil is 1$H$. The peak emf induced in secondary coil is (frequency of $AC = 50Hz$)','[{"text": "$100\\\\,V$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$200\\\\,V$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$300\\\\,V$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$400\\\\,V$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'According to question, peak value of current<br>${I_0} = \\frac{2}{\\pi }A$<br>Frequency of $AC$, $f = 50\\,Hz$<br>Coefficient of mutual inductance $ = 1H$<br>Induced emf in secondary coil is given by<br>${E_{\\rm{S}}} = M.\\frac{{dI}}{{dt}}\\,\\,\\left[ {where,I = {I_0}\\sin \\omega t} \\right]$<br>$ \\Rightarrow {E_{\\rm{S}}} = M\\omega {I_0}\\cos \\omega t$<br>$ = 1 \\times 2\\pi  \\times 50 \\times \\frac{2}{\\pi }\\cos \\left( {2\\pi  \\times 50 \\times t} \\right)\\,$  $$\\,\\left[ {\\because \\omega  = 2\\pi {\\text{f}}} \\right]$$<br>$ = 200\\cos \\left( {100\\pi t} \\right)V$<br>Hence maximum emf induced in secondary is<br>${({E_S})_{\\max }} = 200V$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2016,'MHTCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44838,'physics',38,1066,2543,'The waves emitted when a metal target is bombarded with high energy electrons are','[{"text": "Radio Waves", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Microwaves", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$X$-rays", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Infrared rays", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'$X$-rays are produced by bombarding a metal target with high energy electrons.',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',2023,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44839,'physics',39,1104,2631,'If the momentum of an electron is changed by $$\\Delta p$$, then the de-Broglie wavelength associated with its changes by $$0.5 \\%$$. The initial momentum of electron will be:','[{"text": "$$\\\\dfrac{\\\\Delta p}{199}$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$\\\\dfrac{\\\\Delta p}{200}$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$400 \\\\Delta p$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$200 \\\\Delta p$$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'$$\\lambda=\\dfrac{h}{P}$$<br>$$\\begin{aligned}& \\dfrac{\\Delta \\lambda}{\\lambda}=\\dfrac{-\\Delta P}{P} \\\\& \\dfrac{\\Delta \\lambda}{\\lambda} \\times 100=\\dfrac{-\\Delta P}{P} \\times 100 \\\\& 0.5=\\dfrac{-\\Delta P}{P} \\times 100 \\\\& P=-200 \\Delta P \\\\& {[P]=200 \\Delta P}\\end{aligned}$$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44840,'physics',37,977,2771,'A transformer having efficiency of 90% is working on 200 $V$ and 3 $KW$ power supply. The current in the secondary coil is 6 $A$ and voltage in the primary coil is 200 $V$. The voltaege in the secondary coil and current in the primary coil respectively are','[{"text": "$600\\\\,V,15\\\\,A$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$450\\\\,V,13.5\\\\,A$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$450\\\\,V,15\\\\,A$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$300\\\\,V,15\\\\,A$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Intial power $ = 3000{\\kern 1pt} W$<br>As efficiency is 90%, then final power <br>$ = 3000 \\times \\frac{{90}}{{100}} = 2700{\\kern 1pt} W$<br>$ \\Rightarrow \\left. \\begin{array}{l} \\quad {V_1}{I_1} = 3000W\\\\ \\quad {V_2}{I_2} = 2700W \\end{array} \\right]$<br>${V_2} = \\frac{{2700}}{6} = \\frac{{900}}{2}$ $$[\\because {I_2} = 6A]$$<br>$ \\Rightarrow \\quad {V_2} = 450V$<br>and ${I_1} = \\frac{{3000}}{{200}}$<br>$$\\left[ {\\because {V_1} = 200V} \\right]$$<br>$ \\Rightarrow \\quad {I_1} = 15A$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44841,'physics',40,241,480,'As an electron makes a transition from an excited state to the ground state of a hydrogen-like atom/ion:','[{"text": "kinetic energy, potential energy and total energy decreases", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "kinetic energy decreases, potential energy increases but total energy remains same", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "kinetic energy and total energy decreases but potential energy increases", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Its kinetic energy increases but potential energy and total energy decreases", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'As electron goes to ground state, total 	energy decreases.<br>$TE =  - KE\\,\\,\\& \\,\\,PE = 2TE$<br>So, kinetic energy increases but potential energy and total energy decreases.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44842,'physics',39,1104,2631,'If we express the energy of a photon in $KeV$ and the wavelength in angstroms, then energy of a photon can be calculated from the relation','[{"text": "$$E=12.4 h / \\\\lambda$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$E = 12.4\\\\,hv$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$E=h v$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$E=12.4 / \\\\lambda$$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Energy of photon<br>$E = \\frac{{hc}}{\\lambda }({\\text{ }}Joules{\\text{ }}) = \\frac{{hc}}{{e\\lambda }}(eV)$<br>$ \\Rightarrow E(eV) = \\frac{\\begin{gathered}\n  6.6 \\times {10^{ - 34}} \\times 3 \\times {10^8} \\times \\left( {\\frac{1}{{1.6 \\times {{10}^{ - 29}}}}eV} \\right) \\hfill \\\\\n  \\left( {\\frac{1}{{{{10}^{ - 10}}}}\\mathop A\\limits^o } \\right) \\hfill \\\\ \n\\end{gathered} }{{\\lambda (\\mathop A\\limits^o )}}$<br>$E(eV) = \\frac{{12375}}{{\\lambda \\mathop A\\limits^o }}$<br>$ \\Rightarrow E(KeV) = \\frac{{12.37}}{{\\lambda (\\mathop A\\limits^o )}} \\approx \\frac{{12.4}}{\\lambda }$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44843,'physics',38,1065,2541,'Electromagnetic waves are incident normally on a perfectly reflecting surface having surface area A . If ${I}$ is the intensity of the incident electromagnetic radiation and ${c}$ is the speed of light in vacuum, the force exerted by the electromagnetic wave on the reflecting surface is','[{"text": "${\\\\dfrac{2 I A}{c}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${\\\\dfrac{I A}{c}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\dfrac{I A}{2 c}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\dfrac{I}{2 A c}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'For perfectly reflecting surface, force is given by ${F=\\dfrac{2 I A}{c}}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2024,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44852,'physics',37,973,2262,'A 100 $W$ bulb is connected to an $AC$ source of 220 $V$, 50 $Hz$. Then the current flowing through the bulb is','[{"text": "$\\\\frac{5}{{11}}A$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{1}{2}A$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2A$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{3}{4}A$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Power of the bulb, $P = 100W$<br>rms value of voltage, ${V_{rms}} = 220Hz$<br>The current flowing through the bulb is<br>${I_{rms}} = \\frac{P}{{{V_{rms}}}} = \\frac{{100W}}{{220V}} = \\frac{5}{{11}}A$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44844,'physics',37,972,2261,'An alternating voltage is given by $E = 100\\sin \\left( {{\\rm{\\omega t}} + \\frac{\\pi }{6}} \\right)V$.The voltage will be maximum for the first time when $t$ is [$T = $ periodic time]','[{"text": "$\\\\frac{T}{{12}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{T}{2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{T}{6}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{T}{3}$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Given, alternating voltage is<br>$E = 100\\sin \\left( {\\omega t + \\frac{\\pi }{6}} \\right)V$<br>The voltage will be maximum at time $t$, when<br>$\\sin \\left( {\\omega t + \\frac{\\pi }{6}} \\right) = 1$<br>$ \\Rightarrow \\omega t + \\frac{\\pi }{6} = {\\sin ^{ - 1}}\\left( 1 \\right) = \\frac{\\pi }{2}$<br>$ \\Rightarrow \\omega t = \\frac{\\pi }{3}$<br>$$ \\Rightarrow t = \\frac{\\pi }{{3\\omega }} = \\frac{{\\pi  \\times T}}{{3 \\times 2\\pi }}\\,\\,\\left[ {\\because \\omega  = \\frac{{2\\pi }}{T}} \\right]$$<br>$ \\Rightarrow t = \\frac{T}{6}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2019,'MHTCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44845,'physics',40,245,487,'The radiation corresponding to $3 \\to 2$ transition of hydrogen atom falls on a metal surface to produce photoelectrons. These  electrons are made to enter a magnetic field of $3 \\times {10^{ - 4}}T.$  If 	the radius of the largest circular path followed by these electrons is $10.0\\,mm$, the work function of the metal is close to :','[{"text": "$3.1\\\\,eV$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2.1\\\\,eV$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1.1\\\\,eV$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$0.7\\\\,eV$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'$\\frac{{m{v^2}}}{R} = qvB$<br>$K{E_{(\\max )}} = \\frac{{{{(mv)}^2}}}{{2m}} = \\frac{{{q^2}{B^2}{R^2}}}{{2m}}$<br>$ = \\frac{{{{(1.6 \\times {{10}^{ - 19}})}^2} \\times {{(3 \\times {{10}^{ - 4}})}^2} \\times {{({{10}^{ - 2}})}^2}}}{{2 \\times 9.1 \\times {{10}^{ - 31}}}} = 1.266 \\times {10^{ - 19}}J$<br>$ = 0.8eV$<br>$hv = 13.6\\left[ {\\frac{1}{4} - \\frac{1}{9}} \\right]$<br>$\\therefore \\,\\quad W = hv - K{E_{(\\max )}}$<br>$ = 13.6\\left( {\\frac{5}{{36}}} \\right) - 0.8 = 1.1eV$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44846,'physics',39,1102,2629,'Anode voltage is at $ + 4\\,V$. Incident radiation has frequency $1.4 \\times {10^{15}}\\,Hz$ and work function of the photo cathode is $2.8\\,eV$. Find the minimum and maximum kinetic energy of photoelectrons in $eV$ that reach the collector.','[{"text": "0, 2", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "4, 7", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "2, 5", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "0, 7", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'<br>$hv = \\frac{{6.625 \\times {{10}^{ - 34}} \\times 1.4 \\times {{10}^{15}}}}{{1.6 \\times {{10}^{ - 19}}}} = 5.8eV$<br>${(KE)_{\\max }} = hv - \\phi  = 5.8 - 2.8 = 3$<br>Since anode voltage is $4\\,V$, the electron emitted with zero will acquire an energy $ = 4eV$ and the electrons emitted with $3eV$ will acquire $3 + 4 = 7eV$<br>$$\\therefore K E_{\\min }=4 e V \\text { and } K E_{\\max }=7 e V$$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44847,'physics',38,1064,2537,'A parallel plate capacitor is charged by connecting it to a battery through a resistor. If ${I}$ is the current in the circuit, then in the gap between the plates:','[{"text": "There is no current", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Displacement current of magnitude equal to ${I}$ flows in the same direction as ${I}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Displacement current of magnitude equal to ${I}$ flows in a direction opposite to that of ${I}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Displacement current of magnitude greater than I flows but can be in any direction", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'From modified Ampere\'s law<br>$\\oint B . d l=\\mu_{0}\\left(I_{C}+I_{D}\\right)$ <img src=\'/img/data/physics/38_44847_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' /> For Loop ${L_1}\\quad {I_C} \\ne 0$ and ${I_D} = 0$<br>For Loop ${L_2}\\quad {I_C} = 0$ and ${I_D} \\ne 0$<br>Due to $KCL\\quad {I_C} = {I_D} = I$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2024,'NEET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44848,'physics',37,972,2260,'In  India, the normal domestic power supply $AC$ is 220 $V$, 50 $Hz$, Here 220 $V$ and 50 $Hz$ refer to','[{"text": "Peak value of voltage and frequency", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Rms value of voltage and frequency", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Mean value of voltage and frequency", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Peak, value of voltage and angular frequency.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'220 $V$ and 50 $Hz$ refer to rms value of voltage and frequency.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44849,'physics',40,243,482,'A moving hydrogen atom makes a head-on collision with a stationary hydrogen atom. Before collision, both atoms are in ground state and after collision they move together. What is the minimum value of the kinetic energy of the moving hydrogen atom, such that one of the atoms reaches one of the excitation state?','[{"text": "$20.4\\\\,eV$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$10.2\\\\,eV$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$30.6\\\\,eV$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{Any}}\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\rm{value}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Method-1<br>From conservation of linear momentum<br>$mu + 0 = (m + m){\\rm{v}} \\Rightarrow {\\rm{v}} = \\frac{u}{2};$<br>In this case loss in $KE$ will be maximum. Now this  $\\Delta {K_{\\max }}$ must be greater than the energy required to excite<br>$\\,\\left| {{{(\\Delta K)}_{\\max }}} \\right| = \\left| {\\frac{1}{2}2m{{\\left( {\\frac{u}{2}} \\right)}^2} - \\frac{1}{2}m{u^2}} \\right| = \\frac{1}{4}m{u^2}$<br>$\\frac{1}{4}m{u^2} \\ge 10.2\\,ev \\Rightarrow \\frac{1}{2}m{u^2} \\ge 20.4\\,eV$<br>Therefore, the minimum kinetic energy of moving hydrogen is  $20.4\\,eV$.<br>Method-2 <br>The threshold energy ($K.{E_{\\min }}$) required for the moving $H$-atom is <br>$K.{E_{Th}} = {E_a}\\left[ {1 + \\frac{m}{M}} \\right]$<br> ${E_a} - $ Energy of excitation, $m$- mass of moving  particle<br>$M-$ Mass of particle at rest <br>$ \\Rightarrow K.{E_{Th}} = 10.2eV\\left[ {1 + \\frac{1}{1}} \\right] = 20.4eV$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/40_44849_question_1.png'),
(44850,'physics',39,1102,2629,'A metal surface is illuminated by light of two different wavelengths $248\\,nm$ and $310\\,nm$. The maximum speeds of the photoelectrons corresponding to these wavelengths are $$u_{1}$$ and $$u_{2}$$ respectively. If $$u_{1}: u_{2}=2: 1$$ and $hc = 1240\\,eV - nm$, the work function of the metal is nearly','[{"text": "$3.2\\\\,eV$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$3.7\\\\,eV$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$2.5\\\\,eV$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2.8\\\\,eV$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'From Einstein\'s photoelectric equation, kinetic energy of photoelectrons,<br>$$K=\\dfrac{1}{2} m v^{2}=\\dfrac{h c}{\\lambda}-\\phi$$<br>For $$\\lambda=248 n m, v=u_{1}$$<br>$\\therefore {K_1} = \\frac{1}{2}mu_1^2 = \\frac{{hc}}{{248}} - \\phi \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,(1)$<br>For $\\lambda  = 310\\,nm,v = {u_2}$<br>$\\therefore {K_2} = \\frac{1}{2}mu_2^2 = \\frac{{hc}}{{310}} - \\phi \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,(2)$<br>Divide eqn. (1) by eq. (2),<br>$$\\dfrac{u_{1}^{2}}{u_{2}^{2}}=\\dfrac{\\dfrac{h c}{248}-\\phi}{\\dfrac{h c}{310}-\\phi}=\\dfrac{\\dfrac{1240}{248}-\\phi}{\\dfrac{1240}{310}-\\phi}$$<br>$$\\Rightarrow\\left(\\dfrac{2}{1}\\right)^{2}=\\dfrac{5-\\phi}{4-\\phi}$$<br>$ \\Rightarrow \\phi  = 3.7eV$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(47137,'biology',102,772,1677,'During early times, UV rays from the sun broke_______','[{"text": "Water into hydrogen and oxygen", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Carbon dioxide into carbon and oxygen", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Water into protons and electrons", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Ammonia into nitrogen and hydrogen", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'The UV rays from the sun brokeup water into Hydrogen and Oxygen and the lighter $\\mathrm{H}_{2}$ escaped.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44853,'physics',37,973,2263,'A coil has inductance 2$H$. The ratio of its reactance, when it is connected first to an $AC$ source and then to $DC$ source, is','[{"text": "zero", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "1", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "less than 1", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "infinity", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Given, inductance of coil, $L = 2H$<br>Reactance of coil, when it is connected to $AC$ source,<br>${\\left( {{X_L}} \\right)_{AC}} = \\omega L$ where $\\omega  = $ angular frequency<br>For $DC$ source, inductor coil behaves as pure conductor, hence ${\\left( {{X_L}} \\right)_{DC}} = 0$<br>$\\therefore \\frac{{{{\\left( {{X_L}} \\right)}_{AC}}}}{{{{\\left( {{X_L}} \\right)}_{DC}}}} = \\frac{{\\omega {\\rm{L}}}}{0} = \\infty $ (infinity)',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2019,'MHTCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44854,'physics',38,1064,2537,'The charge on a parallel plate capacitor is varying as ${q=q_{0} \\sin 2 \\pi n t}$ The plates are very large and close to each other. Neglecting the edge effects, the displacement current through the capacitor is','[{"text": "${\\\\dfrac{q}{\\\\varepsilon_{0} A}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\dfrac{q_{0}}{\\\\varepsilon_{0}} \\\\sin 2 \\\\pi n t}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${2 \\\\pi n q_{0} \\\\cos 2 \\\\pi n t}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${\\\\dfrac{2 \\\\pi n q_{0}}{\\\\varepsilon} \\\\cos 2 \\\\pi n t}$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'${I_{D}=\\dfrac{d q}{d t}=\\dfrac{d}{d t} q_{0} \\sin 2 \\pi n t=2 \\pi n q_{0} \\cos 2 \\pi n t}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44855,'physics',39,1102,2629,'Photons of energy $5\\,eV$ are incident on cathode. These electrons are accelerated through external voltage $5\\,V$. Electrons reaching the anode have maximum kinetic energy $8\\,eV$. The work function of the metal is','[{"text": "$2\\\\,eV$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$3\\\\,eV$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$4\\\\,eV$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1\\\\,eV$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'$$K E_{\\max }=(5-\\phi) e V$$<br>When these electrons are accelerated through $5\\,V$, they will reach the anode with maximum energy<br>$ = (5 - \\phi  + 5)eV$<br>$10 - \\phi  = 8,\\phi  = 2eV$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/39_44855_question_1.png'),
(44856,'physics',40,240,479,'Some energy levels of a atom are shown in the figure. The ratio of the wavelengths $r = {\\lambda _1}/{\\lambda _2}$ , is given by :','[{"text": "$r = \\\\frac{2}{3}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$r = \\\\frac{1}{3}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$r = \\\\frac{3}{4}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$r = \\\\frac{4}{3}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'We know that energy difference between two orbits is<br>Using $\\Delta E = \\frac{{hc}}{\\lambda }$<br>For ${\\lambda _1}$      $ - E - ( - 2E) = \\frac{{hc}}{{{\\lambda _1}}} \\Rightarrow {\\lambda _1} = \\frac{{hc}}{E}$<br>For ${{\\lambda _2}}$    $ - E - ( - \\frac{{4E}}{3}) = \\frac{{hc}}{{{\\lambda _2}}}$<br>${\\lambda _2} = \\frac{{3hc}}{E} \\Rightarrow \\frac{{{\\lambda _1}}}{{{\\lambda _2}}} = r = \\frac{1}{3}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/40_44856_question_1.png'),
(44857,'physics',37,973,2263,'Same current is flowing in two $AC$ circuits. First contains only inductance and second contains only capacitance. If frequency of $AC$ is increased for both, the current will','[{"text": "Increase in first circuit and decrease in  second", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Increase in both circuits", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Decrease in both circuits", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Decrease in first circuit and increase in second", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Current in the inductive circuit,<br>$I = \\frac{V}{{{X_L}}} = \\frac{V}{{\\omega L}} \\Rightarrow {I_{inductive}} \\propto \\frac{1}{\\omega }\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,(1)$<br>Similarly, for capacitive circuit<br>$I = \\frac{V}{{{X_C}}} = \\omega CV \\Rightarrow {I_{capacitive}} \\propto \\omega {\\rm{ }}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,(2)$<br>When frequency of $AC$ is increased,<br>from eq.(1)${I_{inductive}}$  decreases and from eq.(2) ${I_{capacitive}}$ increases.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2015,'MHTCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44858,'physics',38,1064,2537,'A parallel plate capacitor with plate area ${A}$ and seperation ${d}$ is charged by a constant current $i$. Consider a plane surface of area ${A / 4}$, parallel to the plates, and drawn symetrically between the plates. What is the displacement current through this area?','[{"text": "${i}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${2 i}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${i / 4}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${i / 2}$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Electric field between the plates of the capacitor is given by<br>${E=\\dfrac{\\sigma}{\\varepsilon_{0}}=\\dfrac{q}{A \\varepsilon_{0}}}$<br>Flux through the area,<br>${\\phi=\\dfrac{q}{A \\varepsilon_{0}} \\times \\dfrac{A}{4}=\\dfrac{q}{4 \\varepsilon_{0}}}$<br>Displacement current, ${i_{d}=\\varepsilon_{0} \\dfrac{d \\phi_{E}}{d t}}$<br>${=\\varepsilon_{0} \\times \\dfrac{d}{d t}\\left(\\dfrac{q}{4 \\varepsilon_{0}}\\right)=\\dfrac{i}{4}}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44859,'physics',39,1102,2629,'Photoelectric emission is observed from a metallic surface for frequencies $$v_{1}$$ and $$v_{2}$$ of the incident light rays $$\\left(v_{1}>v_{2}\\right)$$. If the maximum values of kinetic energy of the photoelectrons emitted in the two cases are in the ratio of $$1: k$$, then the threshold frequency of the metallic surface is','[{"text": "$\\\\frac{{k{v_1} - {v_2}}}{{k - 1}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$$\\\\dfrac{v_{1}-v_{2}}{k-1}$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$\\\\dfrac{v_{2}-v_{1}}{k}$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$\\\\dfrac{k v_{2}-v_{1}}{k-1}$$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'By using $$h v-h v_{0}=k_{\\max }$$<br>$ \\Rightarrow h\\left( {{v_1} - {v_0}} \\right) = {k_1}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,(1)$<br>And $h\\left( {{v_2} - {v_0}} \\right) = {k_2}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,(2)$<br>$$\\Rightarrow \\dfrac{v_{1}-v_{0}}{v_{2}-v_{0}}=\\dfrac{k_{1}}{k_{2}}=\\dfrac{1}{k} \\text {, hence, } v_{0}=\\dfrac{k v_{1}-v_{2}}{k-1} \\text {. }$$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44860,'physics',40,239,478,'The average $KE$ of molecules in a gas at temperature $T$  is  $\\frac{3}{2}KT$.Find the temperature at which the average $KE$ of molecules equal to binding energy of  $H$-atom','[{"text": "${\\\\rm{1}}{\\\\rm{.05}} \\\\times {\\\\rm{1}}{{\\\\rm{0}}^4}K$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{1}}{\\\\rm{.05}} \\\\times {\\\\rm{1}}{{\\\\rm{0}}^5}K$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{1}}{\\\\rm{.05}} \\\\times {\\\\rm{1}}{{\\\\rm{0}}^3}K$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{None }}\\\\,{\\\\rm{of }}\\\\,{\\\\rm{these}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'$\\frac{3}{2}KT = 13.6\\,e{\\rm{V}}$<br>$ \\Rightarrow \\frac{3}{2} \\times \\frac{{1.38 \\times {{10}^{ - 23}}}}{{1.6 \\times {{10}^{ - 19}}}} \\times T = 13.6$<br>$T = 1.05 \\times {10^5}K$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44861,'physics',37,973,2263,'Which of the following represents the variation of inductive reactance $({X_L})$ with the frequency of voltage source $(v)$?','[{"text": "", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'The inductive reactance is<br>${X_L} = \\omega L = 2\\pi vL(as\\,\\omega  = 2\\pi v)$<br>As ${X_L} \\propto v,$ the graph between ${X_L}$ and $v$ is a straight line through origin.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2018,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44863,'physics',39,1102,2629,'When a metal surface is illuminated by light of wavelength $400\\,nm$ and $200\\,nm$, the maximum velocities of the photoelectrons ejected are $v$ and 2 $v$ respectively. The work function of the metal is ( $$h=$$ planck\'s constant, $$c=$$ velocity of light in air)','[{"text": "$2.5\\\\,hc \\\\times {10^6}\\\\,J$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$hc \\\\times {10^6}\\\\,J$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1.6\\\\,hc \\\\times {10^6}\\\\,J$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$0.5\\\\,hc \\\\times {10^6}\\\\,J$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'By using $$\\dfrac{h c}{\\lambda}=\\phi+\\dfrac{1}{2} m v^{2}$$<br>$ \\Rightarrow \\frac{{hc}}{{400 \\times {{10}^{ - 9}}}} = \\phi  + \\frac{1}{2}m{v^2}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,(1)$<br>and $\\frac{{hc}}{{200 \\times {{10}^{ - 9}}}} = \\phi  + \\frac{1}{2}m{(2v)^2}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,(2)$<br>On solving (1) and (2)<br>$\\frac{1}{2}m{v^2} = \\frac{{hc}}{3}\\left[ {\\frac{1}{{200 \\times {{10}^{ - 9}}}} - \\frac{1}{{400 \\times {{10}^{ - 9}}}}} \\right]\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,(3)$<br>From equations (1) and (3), $\\phi  = 1.6hc \\times {10^6}\\,J$.',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44864,'physics',40,240,479,'If the series limit frequency of the lyman series is  ${v_L},$ then the series limit frequency of the P-fund series is :','[{"text": "$16\\\\,{v_L}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\,{v_L}/16$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\,{v_L}/25$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$25\\\\,{v_L}$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'$hv = {K_0}\\left[ {\\frac{1}{{{n_1}^2}} - \\frac{1}{{{n_2}^2}}} \\right]$<br>For Lyman series series limit ${n_2} = \\infty ,{n_1} = 1$<br>$h{v_L} = {K_0}[1]\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,(1)$<br>for-p-fund series<br>For p-fund series limit ${n_2} = \\infty ,{n_1} = 5$<br>$h{v_p} = {K_0}\\left[ {\\frac{1}{{25}}} \\right]\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,(2)$<br>From equation’s (1) and (2)<br>$\\frac{{{v_L}}}{{{v_p}}} = \\frac{{25}}{1} \\Rightarrow {v_p} = {v_L}/25$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44865,'physics',37,973,2264,'Which of the following curves correctly represents the variation of capacitive reactance ${X_C}$ with frequency $f$ ?','[{"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}]',1,NULL,NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44866,'physics',38,1065,2539,'The electric field of an electromagnetic wave in free space is given by:<br>${E=10 \\cos \\left(10^{7} t+k x\\right) V / m}$<br>where ${t}$ and ${x}$ are in seconds and metres, respectively. It can be inferred that<br>(a) the wavelength ${\\lambda}$ is $188.4\\,m$<br>(b) the wave number ${k}$ is  $0.33\\,rad/m$<br>(c) the wave amplitude is $10\\,V/m$<br>(d) the wave is propagating along ${+x}$-direction.<br>Which one of the following pairs of statements is correct?','[{"text": "(a) and (b)", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "(b) and (c)", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "(a) and (c)", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "(c) and (d)", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'${A=10 {~V} / {m} ; \\omega=10^{7} \\Rightarrow \\omega=2 \\pi f=\\dfrac{2 \\pi c}{\\lambda}}$<br>${\\Rightarrow \\lambda=\\dfrac{2 \\pi c}{\\omega}=188.4 {~m}}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44867,'physics',39,1102,2629,'Two identical metal plates show photoelectric effect by a light of wavelength ${\\lambda _A}$ falls on plate A and $$\\lambda_{B}$$ on plate $$B\\left(\\lambda_{A}=2 \\lambda_{B}\\right)$$. The maximum kinetic energy is','[{"text": "$$K_{A} < \\\\dfrac{K_{B}}{2}$$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$$3 K_{A}=K_{B}$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$K_{A}=\\\\dfrac{K_{B}}{2}$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$K_{A}=2 K_{B}$$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'We know, $$K_{A}=\\dfrac{h c}{\\lambda_{A}}-\\phi_{0}$$<br>and $$\\quad K_{B}=\\dfrac{h c}{\\lambda_{B}}-\\phi_{0}$$<br>$$\\therefore \\dfrac{K_{A}}{K_{B}} < \\dfrac{1}{2}$$<br>or $$\\quad K_{A} < K_{B} / 2$$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44868,'physics',37,973,2268,'A capacitor and an inductance coil are connected in separate $AC$ circuits with a bulb glowing in both the circuits. The bulb glows more brightly when','[{"text": "An iron rod is introduced into the inductance  coil", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "The number of turns in the inductance coil  is increased", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Separation between the plates of the capacitor is increased.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "A dielectric is introduced into the gap between the plates of the capacitor.", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'The bulb glows more brightly when current flowing through the bulb increases.<br>For the first two options, the current decreases as the increase in self-inductance causes increase in inductive reactance.<br>The current decreases for the third option also as the decrease in capacitance causes 	increase in capacitive reactance.<br>The bulb glows more brightly for option (4) due to increase in current as the capacitive reactance decreases due to increase in its capacitance.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44870,'physics',39,1102,2628,'A 5 watt source emits monochromatic light of wavelength $5000\\mathop A\\limits^o $. When placed $0.5\\,m$ away, it liberates photoelectrons from a photosensitive metallic surface. When the source is moved to a distance of $2.0\\,m$, the number of photoelectrons liberated will be reduced by a factor of','[{"text": "16", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "8", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "4", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "2", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Number of emitted electrons $$N_{E}$$ $$\\alpha$$ Intensity<br>$$\\begin{gathered}\\alpha \\dfrac{1}{(\\text { Distance })^{2}} \\\\\\dfrac{N_{1}}{N_{2}}=\\left(\\dfrac{r_{2}}{r_{1}}\\right)^{2}=\\left(\\dfrac{2}{0.5}\\right)^{2}=16\\end{gathered}$$<br>Therefore, as distance is doubled, $N$ decreases by $$(1 / 16)$$ times.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44883,'physics',37,974,2274,'The power factor of $R-L$ circuit is $\\frac{1}{{\\sqrt 3 }}$. If the inductive reactance is $2\\Omega $. The value of resistance is','[{"text": "$0.5\\\\Omega $", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{1}{{\\\\sqrt 2 }}\\\\Omega $", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2\\\\Omega $", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\sqrt 2 \\\\Omega $", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Here, power factor, $\\cos \\phi  = \\frac{1}{{\\sqrt 3 }}$<br>$\\therefore \\,\\,\\tan \\phi  = \\sqrt {{{(1{\\rm{/}}\\cos \\phi )}^2} - 1}  = \\sqrt 2 $<br>For $R - L$ circuit, $\\tan \\phi  = \\frac{{{X_L}}}{R}$<br>$R = \\frac{{{X_L}}}{{\\tan \\phi }} = \\frac{2}{{\\sqrt 2 }} = \\sqrt 2 \\Omega $',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2020,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44871,'physics',38,1064,2537,'Figure shows a capacitor made of two circular plates each of radius $12\\,cm$, separated by $5.0\\,mm$. The capacitor is being charged by an external source (not shown in the figure). The charging current is constant and equal to $0.15\\,A$, then which of the following is correct?<br>(Given: ${\\varepsilon_{0}=8.85 \\times 10^{-12} {C}^{2} N^{-1} {~m}^{-2}}$ )','[{"text": "The rate of change of potential difference between the plates is ${1.87 \\\\times 10^{9} {Vs}^{-1}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "The displacement current across the plates is $0.30\\\\,A$.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "The capacitance of the capacitor is $75\\\\,pF$.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "None of these", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Area, ${A=\\pi R^{2}=3.14 \\times(0.12)^{2} m^{2}}$<br>${C=\\dfrac{\\varepsilon_{0} A}{d}=\\dfrac{8.85 \\times 10^{-12} \\times 3.14 \\times(0.12)^{2}}{5 \\times 10^{-3}}}$<br>${=80.1 \\times 10^{-12} F=80.1 p F}$<br>${I=C \\dfrac{d V}{d t} \\quad\\left(\\because I=\\dfrac{d q}{d t}\\right)}$<br>${\\Rightarrow \\dfrac{d V}{d t}=\\dfrac{I}{C}=\\dfrac{0.15}{80.1 \\times 10^{-12}}}$<br>${=1.87 \\times 10^{9} {~V} {~s}^{-1}}$<br>Displacement current is equal to conduction current $i.e.,{\\rm{ }}0.15{\\rm{ }}A$.',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/38_44871_question_1.png'),
(44872,'physics',37,973,2265,'An $AC$ voltage is applied to a resistance $R$ and an inductor $L$ in series. If $R$ and the inductive reactance are both equal to $3\\Omega $ the phase difference between the applied voltage and the current in the circuit is','[{"text": "$\\\\frac{\\\\pi }{4}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{\\\\pi }{6}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{\\\\pi }{2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{Zero}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'As we know that <br>$\\tan \\phi  = \\frac{{{X_L}}}{R} = \\frac{{\\omega L}}{R}$<br>$\\tan \\phi  = \\frac{3}{3} \\Rightarrow \\quad \\phi  = \\frac{\\pi }{4}rad$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44873,'physics',38,1065,2539,'In a free space, electron is placed in the path of a<br>plane electromagnetic wave, it will start moving along','[{"text": "centre of earth", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "equator of earth", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "magnetic field", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "electric field", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'The electron placed in the path of<br>electromagnetic wave will experience<br>force due to electric field vector and<br>not due to magnetic field vector.<br>Hence, it will move along the electric field.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44876,'physics',37,973,2265,'An $AC$ circuit contains resistance of  ${\\rm{12}}\\Omega $ and  inductive reactance ${\\rm{5}}\\Omega $ . The phase angle between current and potential difference will be','[{"text": "${\\\\sin ^{ - 1}}\\\\left( {\\\\frac{{12}}{{13}}} \\\\right)$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\cos ^{ - 1}}\\\\left( {\\\\frac{5}{{12}}} \\\\right)$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\sin ^{ - 1}}\\\\left( {\\\\frac{5}{{12}}} \\\\right)$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\cos ^{ - 1}}\\\\left( {\\\\frac{{12}}{{13}}} \\\\right)$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Given,$R = 12\\Omega $ and ${X_L} = 5\\Omega $<br>Therefore, Impedance, <br>$Z = \\sqrt {{{\\left( {12} \\right)}^2} + {{\\left( 5 \\right)}^2}}  = 13\\Omega $<br>If $\\phi $ is the phase angle between current and voltage, <br>Power factor $ = \\cos \\phi  = \\frac{R}{Z}$<br>$ \\Rightarrow \\phi  = {\\cos ^{ - 1}}\\left( {\\frac{R}{Z}} \\right) = {\\cos ^{ - 1}}\\left( {\\frac{{12}}{{13}}} \\right)$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2020,'MHTCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44877,'physics',38,1065,2542,'The average magnetic energy density of an<br>electromagnetic wave of wave length $\\lambda $ travelling in  free space is given by','[{"text": "$\\\\,\\\\frac{{{B^2}}}{{2\\\\lambda }}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{{{B^2}}}{{2{\\\\mu _0}}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{{2{B^2}}}{{{\\\\mu _0}\\\\lambda }}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{B}{{{\\\\mu _0}\\\\lambda }}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Energy density of an electromagnetic wave,<br>$\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,U = \\frac{1}{2}{\\varepsilon _0}{E^2} + \\frac{1}{2}\\frac{{{B^2}}}{{{\\mu _0}}}$<br>So, the magnetic energy density is<br>$\\frac{{{B^2}}}{{2{\\mu _0}}}.$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44880,'physics',37,973,2265,'A coil of self-inductance $L$ is connected in series with a bulb $B$ and an $AC$ source. Brightness of the bulb decreases when','[{"text": "Number of turns in the coil is reduced", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "A capacitance of reactance ${X_C} = {X_L}$ is induced in  the  same circuit", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Frequency of the $AC$ source is decreased", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "An iron rod is inserted in the coil", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'When a bulb of resistance $R$ is connected in series with a coil of self-inductance $L$, then current in the circuit is given by<br>$I = \\frac{E}{{\\sqrt {{\\omega ^2}{L^2} + {R^2}} }},$<br>where $E$ is the voltage of an $AC$ source<br>As $L = \\frac{{{\\mu _0}{\\mu _r}{N^2}A}}{l} \\Rightarrow \\quad L \\propto {\\kern 1pt} {\\mu _r}$<br>When iron rod is inserted, $L$ increases, therefore current $I$ decreases.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44881,'physics',38,1065,2539,'If ${v_\\gamma },{v_X}$ and ${v_M}$ are the speeds of $\\gamma $-rays, $X$-rays<br>and microwaves respectively in vacuum, then','[{"text": "${v_\\\\gamma } > {v_M} > {v_X}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${v_\\\\gamma } > {v_X} > {v_M}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${v_\\\\gamma } = {v_X} = {v_M}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "None of these", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'$\\gamma $-rays, $X$-rays and microwaves<br>respectively are electromagnetic<br>waves. They travel with the speed of<br>light in vacuum.<br>Hence, $\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{v_\\gamma } = {v_X} = {v_M}$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44885,'physics',38,1066,2543,'The magnetic field of plane<br>electromagnetic wave is given by<br>${B_y} = 2 \\times {10^{ - 7}}\\sin \\left( {0.5 \\times {{10}^3}x + 1.5 \\times {{10}^{11}}t} \\right)T$.<br>This electromagnetic wave is','[{"text": "a visible light", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "an infrared wave", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "a microwave", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "a radio wave", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'We have,<br>${B_y} = 2 \\times {10^{ - 7}}\\sin \\left( {0.5 \\times {{10}^3}x + 1.5 \\times {{10}^{11}}t} \\right)$<br>Comparing with the standard equation<br>${B_y} = {B_0}\\sin (kx + \\omega t)$, we get<br>$\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,k = 0.5 \\times {10^3}$<br>$ \\Rightarrow \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\lambda  = \\frac{{2\\pi }}{k}$<br>$\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, = \\frac{{2\\pi }}{{0.5 \\times {{10}^3}}}\\,$<br>${\\mkern 1mu} \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, = 0.01256\\,m$<br>The wavelength range of microwaves is<br>${10^{ - 3}}$ to $0.1\\,m.$ The wavelength of this<br>wave lies between ${10^{ - 3}}$ to $0.1\\,m$, so the<br>equation represents a microwave.',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44887,'physics',37,973,2265,'A coil of inductive reactance $1{\\rm{/}}\\sqrt 3 \\Omega $ and resistance $1\\Omega $ is connected to a 200 $V$, 50 $Hz$ $A.C$ supply. The time lag between maximum voltage and current is','[{"text": "$\\\\frac{1}{{600}}s$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{1}{{200}}s$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{1}{{300}}s$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{1}{{500}}s$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Given,${X_L} = \\frac{1}{{\\sqrt 3 }}\\Omega ,R = 1\\Omega ,V = 200V,v = 50Hz$<br>In series $LR$ circuit, phase lag $\\phi $ is given by<br>$\\tan \\phi  = \\frac{{{X_L}}}{R} = \\frac{{\\frac{1}{{\\sqrt 3 }}}}{1} = \\frac{1}{{\\sqrt 3 }} \\Rightarrow \\phi  = \\frac{\\pi }{6}$<br>$\\therefore \\,2\\pi v\\Delta t = \\frac{\\pi }{6},$ (where, $\\Delta t$ is time lag)<br>$ \\Rightarrow \\Delta t = \\frac{\\pi }{{6 \\times 2 \\times \\pi  \\times 50}} = \\frac{1}{{600}}s$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2017,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44888,'physics',38,1065,2539,'Which of the following is not a property of light?','[{"text": "It requires a material medium for propagation.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "It can travel through vacuum.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "It involves transportation of energy.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "It has finite speed.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Light waves do not require a material medium for their propagation. Also, they travel through vacuum. A metallic plate kept in the path of light rays gets heated up, showing that light involves transportation of energy. Also, speed of light is $3 \\times {10^8}\\,m{s^{ - 1}},$ which is finite.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44891,'physics',37,973,2266,'A resistor and  a capacitor are connected in series with an $a.c.$ source. If the potential drop across the capacitor is 5$V$ and that across resistor is 12$V$, the applied voltage is','[{"text": "$13\\\\,V$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$17\\\\,V$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$5\\\\,V$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$12\\\\,V = 13V$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'<img src=\'/img/data/physics/37_44891_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' /> <br> Given,${V_R} = 12V,{V_C} = 5V$ <br> $\\therefore $ Applied voltage $V = \\sqrt {V_R^2 + V_C^2}  = \\sqrt {{{(12)}^2} + {{(5)}^2}} $ <br> $ = 13V$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2009,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44892,'physics',38,1065,2541,'A plane electromagnetic wave is incident on a material surface. If the wave delivers momentum $p$ and energy $E$, then','[{"text": "$p = 0,E = 0$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$p \\\\ne 0,E \\\\ne 0$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$p \\\\ne 0,E = 0$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$p = 0,E \\\\ne 0$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'An electromagnetic wave has both energy and momentum.<br>$\\therefore p \\ne 0,E \\ne 0$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44893,'physics',37,973,2269,'The $L - C$ parallel resonant circuit','[{"text": "Has a very high impedance", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Has a very high current", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Acts as resistance of very low value", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Has zero impedance", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'In case of parallel resonance $L - C$ circuit the capacitive and inductive reactance equal to each other, the total impedance of the circuit increase to infinity meaning the circuit draws no current from the $AC$ power source',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2016,'MHTCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44915,'physics',38,1066,2543,'Which of the following rays is emitted by a human body?','[{"text": "X-rays", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "UV rays", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Visible rays", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "IR rays", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Generally, temperature of human body is $37^\\circ C$ corresponding to which IR and microwave radiations are emitted from the human body.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44896,'physics',38,1065,2540,'A plane electromagnetic wave travels in free space along $x$-axis. At a particular point in space, the electric field along $y$-axis is $9.3\\,V\\,{m^{ - 1}}$. The magnetic induction ($B$) along $z$-axis is','[{"text": "$3.1 \\\\times {10^{ - 8}}\\\\,T$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$3 \\\\times {10^{ - 5}}\\\\,T$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$3 \\\\times {10^{ - 6}}\\\\,T$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$9.3 \\\\times {10^{ - 6}}\\\\,T$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'$c = \\frac{E}{B}$<br>$ \\Rightarrow B = \\frac{E}{c} = \\frac{{9.3}}{{3 \\times {{10}^8}}} = 3.1 \\times {10^{ - 8}}\\,T$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44897,'physics',37,976,2280,'In the given circuit, the resonant frequency is','[{"text": "$1592\\\\,Hz$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$15910\\\\,Hz$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$15.92\\\\,Hz$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$159.2\\\\,Hz$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Resonant frequency,<br>$v = \\frac{1}{{2\\pi \\sqrt {LC} }}$<br>$ = \\frac{1}{{2 \\times 3.14 \\times \\sqrt {0.5 \\times {{10}^{ - 3}} \\times 20 \\times {{10}^{ - 6}}} }} \\approx 1592\\,\\,Hz$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/37_44897_question_1.png'),
(44900,'physics',38,1065,2540,'A plane electromagnetic wave is propagating along the $z$ direction. If the electric field component of this wave is in the direction $(\\hat{i}+\\hat{j})$, then which of the following is the direction of the magnetic field component?','[{"text": "$(-\\\\hat{i}+\\\\hat{j})$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$(\\\\hat{i}-\\\\hat{j})$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$(-\\\\hat{i}-\\\\hat{j})$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$(\\\\hat{i}+\\\\hat{k})$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'The magnetic field component is perpendicular to the direction of propagation and the direction of electric field.<br>Using vector algebra, $\\vec{E} \\times \\vec{B}$ should be in $z$-direction<br>$\\therefore \\quad(\\hat{i}+\\hat{j}) \\times(-\\hat{i}+\\hat{j})=2 \\hat{k}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44901,'physics',37,974,2273,'The average power dissipated in pure inductor is','[{"text": "$V{I^2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{zero}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{1}{2}VI$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{{V{I^2}}}{4}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'The average power dissipated in a pure inductor is zero, as the phase angle between voltage and current is always $\\pi /2$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44903,'physics',38,1068,2545,'Beyond which frequency, the ionosphere bends any incident electromagnetic radiation but do not reflect it back towards the earth?','[{"text": "$50MHz$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$40MHz$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$30MHz$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$20MHz$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'The ionosphere can reflect electromagnetic waves of frequency less than  $40\\,MHz$ but do not reflect electromagnetic waves of frequency more than $40MHz$.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44904,'physics',40,243,482,'Consider a hydrogen atom present at rest in ground state. A fast moving neutron collides with the atom. Find the threshold energy of the neutron for excitations  ${n_1} = 2\\,\\,{\\rm{to}}\\,\\,{n_2} = 3$ $\\left( {{m_N} \\simeq {m_H}} \\right)$','[{"text": "$24.2\\\\,eV$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$12.4\\\\,eV$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$10.2\\\\,eV$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$20.4\\\\,eV$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'The threshold energy<br>${E_{Th}} = {E_a}\\left[ {1 + \\frac{m}{M}} \\right]$<br>$m = {m_n}$ & $M = {m_H}$<br>${E_a} = 10.2eV\\,\\,\\& \\,\\,{m_n} \\approx {m_H}$<br>In the following table threshold energies are given for different excitations of $H$-atom<br>It can be observed from the above table that ${E_{Th}} = 24.2\\,eV$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44905,'physics',37,974,2272,'The average power dissipated in $AC$ circuit is 2 Watt. If a current flowing through a circuit is 2$A$ and impedance is $1\\Omega $, what is the power factor of the $AC$ circuit ?','[{"text": "$0$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$0.5$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{1}{{\\\\sqrt 2 }}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Average power dissipated in $A.C$. circuit is <br>$P = VI\\cos \\phi  = {I^2}Z\\cos \\phi $<br>where $\\cos \\phi $ is the power factor of the circuit.<br>$ \\Rightarrow \\cos \\phi  = \\frac{P}{{{I^2}Z}}$<br>Here,$P = 2W,I = 2A,Z = 1\\Omega $<br>$\\therefore \\,\\cos \\phi  = \\frac{2}{{{{(2)}^2}(1)}} = \\frac{1}{2} = 0.5$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2014,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44939,'physics',40,238,477,'The diagram shows the path of four $\\alpha  - $ particles of the same energy being scattered by the nucleus of an atom simultaneously. Which of these are/is not physically possible','[{"text": "$2\\\\,{\\\\rm{and}}\\\\,3$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$3\\\\,{\\\\rm{and}}\\\\,4$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$4\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\rm{only}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$1\\\\,{\\\\rm{and}}\\\\,4$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'$\\alpha  - $ particles cannot be attracted by the nucleus.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/40_44939_question_1.png'),
(44906,'physics',39,1102,2629,'The stopping potential $$V_{s}$$ for photoelectric emission from a metal surface is plotted along \\[Y\\]-axis and frequency $$v$$ of incident light along \\[X\\]-axis. A straight line is obtained as shown. Planck\'s constant is given by<div class="matching-question"></div>','[{"text": "Slope of the line", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Product of slope on the line and charge on the electron", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Product of intercept along $Y$-axis and mass of the electron", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Product of slope and mass of electron", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'$$K_{\\max }=h v-\\phi$$<br>$$\\Rightarrow e V_{S}=h v-\\phi \\Rightarrow V_{S}=\\dfrac{h}{e} v-\\dfrac{\\phi}{e}$$<br>Comparing this equation with $$y=m x+c$$, we get slope $$m=\\dfrac{h}{e} \\Rightarrow h=m \\times e$$.',NULL,'Hard','Graph/Figure',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/39_44906_question_1.png'),
(44907,'physics',38,1068,2545,'The amplitude of an electromagnetic wave in vacuum is doubled with no other changes made to the wave. As a result of this doubling of the amplitude, which of the following statement is correct?','[{"text": "The frequency of the wave changes only.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "The wavelength of the wave changes only.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "The speed of wave propagation changes", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "None of the above is correct.", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'The speed of an electromagnetic wave in vacuum is<br>$c = \\frac{1}{{\\sqrt {{\\mu _0}{\\varepsilon _0}} }} = 3 \\times {10^8}\\,m\\,{s^{ - 1}}$<br>It is independent of amplitude, frequency and wavelength of the electromagnetic wave.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44908,'physics',37,974,2272,'In an $A.C.$ circuit $V$ and $I$ are given by $V = 150\\sin (150t)$ volt and $I = 150\\sin (150t + \\frac{\\pi }{3})$ ampere. The power dissipated in the circuit is','[{"text": "$106\\\\,W$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$150\\\\,W$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$5625\\\\,W$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{zero}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Comparing $V = 150\\sin (150t)$ with<br>$V = {V_0}\\sin \\omega t,$ we get<br>${V_0} = 150V$<br>Comparing $I = 150\\sin \\left( {150t + \\frac{\\pi }{3}} \\right)$ with<br>$I = {I_0}\\sin (\\omega t + \\phi )$ we get<br>${I_0} = 150A,\\phi  = \\frac{\\pi }{3} = 60^\\circ $<br>The power dissipated in  $A.C$ circuit is<br>$P = \\frac{1}{2}{V_0}{I_0}\\cos \\phi  = \\frac{1}{2} \\times 150 \\times 150 \\times \\cos 60^\\circ $<br>$ = \\frac{1}{2} \\times 150 \\times 150 \\times \\frac{1}{2} = 5265\\,W$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44909,'physics',40,243,482,'A  $H{e^ + }$ ion is at rest and is in ground state. A neutron with intial kinetic energy $K$ collides head on with the  $H{e^ + }$ ion. Find minimum value of $K$ so that there can be an inelastic collision between these two particles.','[{"text": "$51\\\\,eV$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$30.6\\\\,eV$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$20.4\\\\,eV$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$40.8\\\\,eV$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Method-1<br>From conservation of linear momentum<br>$mu = (m + 4m)v \\Rightarrow v = \\frac{u}{5};$<br>In this case loss in $KE$ will be maximum. Now this  $\\Delta {K_{\\max }}$ must be greater than the energy received to excite<br>$\\left| {\\Delta {K_{\\max }}} \\right| = \\frac{1}{2}m{u^2} - \\frac{1}{2}(5m){\\left( {\\frac{u}{5}} \\right)^2} = \\frac{1}{2}m{u^2}\\left( {1 - \\frac{1}{5}} \\right)$<br>$ = \\frac{1}{2}m{u^2} \\cdot \\frac{4}{5}$<br>$\\therefore \\frac{1}{2}m{u^2} \\cdot \\frac{4}{5} \\ge 40.8 \\Rightarrow \\frac{1}{2}m{u^2} \\ge 51\\,eV$<br>Hence, minimum value of  $K$ for an inelastic collision: ${K_{\\min }} = 51\\,eV.$<br>Method-2 <br>The Thereshold energy <br>$K.{E_{Th}} = {E_a}\\left[ {1 + \\frac{m}{M}} \\right]\\,(M \\approx 4m)$<br>${E_1} = \\frac{{ - 13.6 \\times {2^2}}}{{{1^2}}},\\,{E_2} = \\frac{{ - 13.6 \\times {2^2}}}{{{2^2}}}$<br>$ \\Rightarrow {E_a} = {E_1} - {E_2}$<br>$ \\Rightarrow {E_a} = 13.6 \\times 3eV \\Rightarrow K.{E_{Th}} = \\frac{{5{E_a}}}{4} = 51eV$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/40_44909_question_1.png'),
(44910,'physics',39,1102,2629,'In a photoelectric emission, electrons are ejected from metals \\[X\\]and \\[Y\\] by light of frequency $$v$$. The potential difference \\[V\\] required to stop the electrons is measured for various frequencies. If \\[Y\\] has a greater work function than \\[X\\], which graph illustrates the expected results?<div class="matching-question"></div>','[{"text": "", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'$$e V_{S}=h v-\\phi$$<br>$$\\begin{aligned}& V_{S}=\\left(\\dfrac{h v}{e}\\right)-\\left(\\dfrac{\\phi}{e}\\right) \\\\& \\phi_{y}>\\phi_{x}\\end{aligned}$$<br>The $y$-intercept for the metal-$Y$ is more than for metal- $$X$$<br>So option (1) is correct.',NULL,'Hard','Graph/Figure',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44911,'physics',38,1065,2542,'In an electromagnetic wave, the average energy density associated with magnetic field is','[{"text": "$\\\\dfrac{L i_{0}^{2}}{2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{B^{2}}{2 \\\\mu_{0}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{\\\\mu_{0} B^{2}}{2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{\\\\mu_{0}}{2 B^{2}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'$U_{B}=\\dfrac{B^{2}}{2 \\mu_{0}}$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44912,'physics',37,975,2276,'For a series $LCR$ circuit at resonance, the statement which is not true is','[{"text": "Wattless current is zero", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Power factor is zero", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Peak energy stored by a capacitor $ = $ peak energy stored by an inductor", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Average power $ = $ apparent power", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'For a series $LCR$ circuit at resonance, total reactance is zero and impedance is purely resistive. The phase angle  $\\phi $ between the current and voltage is zero. Hence power factor $\\cos \\phi  = \\cos 0^\\circ  = 1$ .Therefore statement (2) is wrong.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2009,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44913,'physics',39,1102,2629,'The graph between \\[\\log E\\] and $$\\log \\lambda$$ will be ( \\[E\\] - energy of photon and $$\\lambda$$ - wavelength of an electron)<div class="matching-question"></div>','[{"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Kinetic energy $$E=\\dfrac{1}{2} m v^{2}$$<br>$$\\begin{aligned}& E=\\dfrac{P^{2}}{2 m}=\\dfrac{h^{2}}{2 m \\lambda^{2}}=\\dfrac{C}{\\lambda^{2}} \\\\& \\log E=\\log C-2 \\log \\lambda \\\\& \\log \\lambda=\\dfrac{1}{2} \\log C-\\dfrac{1}{2} \\log E\\end{aligned}$$<br>The correct graph is (3).',NULL,'Hard','Graph/Figure',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44914,'physics',40,241,480,'In a hydrogen-like atom electron make transition from an energy level with quantum number $n$ to another with quantum number $(n - 1)$. If $n >  > 1$, the frequency of radiation emitted is proportional to :','[{"text": "$\\\\frac{1}{n}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{1}{{{n^2}}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{1}{{{n^{\\\\frac{3}{2}}}}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{1}{{{n^3}}}$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'$\\Delta E = hv$<br>$v = \\frac{{\\Delta E}}{h} = k\\left[ {\\frac{1}{{{{(n - 1)}^2}}} - \\frac{1}{{{n^2}}}} \\right] = \\frac{{k[{n^2} - {n^2} - 1 + 2n]}}{{{n^2}{{(n - 1)}^2}}}$<br>$ = \\frac{{k2n}}{{{n^2}{{(n - 1)}^2}}} \\approx \\frac{{2k}}{{{n^3}}}\\,\\alpha \\,\\frac{1}{{{n^3}}}(n - 1 \\approx n)$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44916,'physics',37,975,2276,'In an $LCR$ circuit, at resonance','[{"text": "The impedance is maximum", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "The current leads the voltage by $\\\\pi /2$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "The current and voltage are in phase", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "The current is minimum", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'In an $LCR$ circuit  at resonance, the phase angle $(\\phi )$ between current and voltage is  zero as the total reactance is zero.<br>Thus the current and voltage are in phase. The current is maximum and the impedance is minimum at resonance in $LCR$ circuit.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44917,'physics',39,1102,2627,'Photoelectric effect experiments are performed using three different metal plates \\[p,q\\] and \\[r\\] having work functions \\[{\\phi _p} = 2.0\\,eV,\\,{\\phi _q} = 2.5\\,eV\\] and \\[{\\phi _r} = 3.0\\,eV\\], respectively. A light beam containing wavelengths of \\[550\\;nm,\\,450\\;nm\\] and \\[350\\;nm\\] with equal intensities illuminates each of the plates. The correct \\[i - V\\] graph for the experiment is<br>[Take \\[hc = 1240\\,eVnm\\] ]<div class="matching-question"></div>','[{"text": "", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'${E_{{\\lambda _1} - 550\\,mm}} = \\frac{{1240}}{{550}}eV = 2.25eV$<br>${E_{{\\lambda _2} - 450\\,nm}} = \\frac{{1240}}{{450}}eV = 2.8eV$<br>${E_{{\\lambda _3} - 350\\,nm}} = \\frac{{1240}}{{350}}eV = 3.5eV$<br>For metal $r;{\\lambda _3}$ is able to generate photoelectrons<br>For metal $q;{\\lambda _2}$ and $$\\lambda_{3}$$ are able to generate photoelectrons.<br>For metal $p$; all wavelengths are able to generate photoelectrons.<br>Hence photoelectric current will be maximum for $p$ and least for $r$.',NULL,'Hard','Graph/Figure',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44918,'physics',40,239,478,'Let the $PE$ of hydrogen atom in the ground state be zero. Then its total energy in the first excited state will be','[{"text": "$23.8\\\\,eV$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$27.2\\\\,eV$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$10.2\\\\,eV$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$12.6\\\\,eV$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'When the $P$.$E$ is taken as zero (Old-Frame) at infinity  then the values of $P$.$E$, $K$.$E$ & $T$.$E$ for $H$-atom are shown as follows <br>$P.E = 2T.E\\,\\& K.E =  - T.E$ <img src=\'/img/data/physics/40_44918_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />  ${\\rm{T}}{\\rm{.E}} = \\frac{{ - 13.6eV}}{{{n^2}}}$  <img src=\'/img/data/physics/40_44918_explanation_2.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />  The above relations between $P$.$E$, $T$.$E$ & $K$.$E$ are not valid in the new frame.<br>Note: If we change the reference point for $P$.$E$ then the absolute $K$.$E$ and difference in $T$.$E$ must remain same.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44920,'physics',37,973,2268,'The value of alternating emf in the given circuit will be','[{"text": "$100\\\\,V$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$20\\\\,V$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$220\\\\,V$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$140\\\\,V$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'${V_L}$ and ${V_C}$ are in opposite phase with each other and both have $\\pi /2$ phase difference with ${V_R}$.<br>$\\therefore E = \\sqrt {V_R^2 + {{\\left( {{V_L} - {V_C}} \\right)}^2}} $<br>$ = \\sqrt {{{80}^2} + {{(40 - 100)}^2}}  = 100\\,V$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2008,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/37_44920_question_1.png'),
(44921,'physics',39,1102,2629,'According to Einstein\'s photoelectric equation, the plot of the kinetic energy of the emitted photo electrons from a metal versus the frequency, of the incident radiation gives a straight line whose slope<div class="matching-question"></div>','[{"text": "Is the same for all metals and independent of the intensity of the radiation", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Depends on the intensity of the radiation", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Depends both on the intensity of the radiation and the metal used", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Depends on the nature of the metals used", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'$$h v=h v_{o}+K E_{\\max } \\Rightarrow K E_{\\max }=h v-h v_{o}$$<br>On comparing this equation with $$y=m x+c$$ we get $$m=h=$$ Universal constant.<br>So the slope does not depend on anything.',NULL,'Hard','Graph/Figure',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44922,'physics',38,1064,2537,'In order to establish an instantaneous displacement current of $1 {~mA}$ in the space between the parallel plates of  $4\\,\\mu F$  capacitor what should be the rate of change of potential difference  ?','[{"text": "$350\\\\,V{s^{ - 1}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$250\\\\,V{s^{ - 1}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$100\\\\,V{s^{ - 1}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$190\\\\,V{s^{ - 1}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'${I_D} = {I_C} = \\frac{d}{{dt}}CV = C\\frac{{dV}}{{dt}}$<br>$\\therefore \\quad \\dfrac{{dV}}{{dt}}=\\dfrac{{I}_{{D}}}{{C}}=\\dfrac{10^{-3}}{4 \\times 10^{-6}}=250 {~V} {~s}^{-1}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44923,'physics',37,973,2268,'The readings of ammeter and voltemeter in the following circuit are respectively','[{"text": "$2.7{\\\\rm{ }}A,{\\\\rm{ }}220{\\\\rm{ }}V$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{1}}{\\\\rm{.2 }}A,{\\\\rm{ 1}}20{\\\\rm{ }}V$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{2}}{\\\\rm{.2 }}A,{\\\\rm{ 2}}20{\\\\rm{ }}V$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{1}}{\\\\rm{.5 }}A,{\\\\rm{ 10}}0{\\\\rm{ }}V$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'As,${V_L} = {V_C} = 50V$,the given circuit is in resonance.<br>Hence 220V will be the voltage across the resistance.<br>$\\therefore $ Current in the circuit $ = \\frac{{220}}{{100}} = 2.2A$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/37_44923_question_1.png'),
(44925,'physics',39,1102,2628,'A point source causes photoelectric effect from a small metal plate. Which of the curves in figure may represent the saturation of photo-current as a function of the distance between the source and the metal?<div class="matching-question"></div>','[{"text": "$A$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$B$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$C$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$D$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Saturation current is inversely proportional to the square of distance of cathode from point source.',NULL,'Hard','Graph/Figure',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/39_44925_question_1.png'),
(44926,'physics',37,973,2268,'What will be the reading in the voltmeter and ammeter of the circuit shown ?','[{"text": "$90V,\\\\,2A$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$0V,\\\\,2A$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$90V,\\\\,1A$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$0V,\\\\,1A$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'As,${X_L} = {X_C}$ the circuit is in resonance.<br>$\\therefore $ Voltmeter reading $ = {V_L} - {V_C} = 0V$<br>$\\therefore $ Ammeter reading $ = \\frac{V}{R} = \\frac{{90}}{{45}} = 2A$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/37_44926_question_1.png'),
(44928,'physics',39,1102,2629,'The correct graph between the maximum energy of a photoelectron and the inverse of wavelength of the incident radiation is given by the curve<div class="matching-question"></div>','[{"text": "$A$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$B$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$C$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "None of the above", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'$$K_{\\max }=h v-\\phi=\\dfrac{h c}{\\lambda}-\\phi$$ i.e., graph between $$K_{\\max }$$ and $$\\dfrac{1}{\\lambda}$$ will be straight line having slope ($hc$) and intercept $$\\phi$$ on -$KE$ axis.<br>So option (1) is correct.',NULL,'Hard','Graph/Figure',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/39_44928_question_1.png'),
(44930,'physics',37,975,2276,'An alternating emf of 0.2$V$ is applied across an $LCR$ series circuit having  $R = 4\\Omega ,C = 80\\mu F$ and $L = 200mH$. At resonance the voltage drop across the inductor is','[{"text": "$10V$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2.5\\\\,V$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$1\\\\,V$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$5V$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Given,${V_{rms}} = 0.2V,R = 4\\Omega $<br>$C = 80\\mu F = 80 \\times {10^{ - 6}}F$<br>and $L = 200mH = 200 \\times {10^{ - 3}}H$<br>The impedance of the series $LCR$ circuit,<br>$Z = \\sqrt {{R^2} + {{\\left( {{X_L} - {X_C}} \\right)}^2}} $<br>At resonance, ${X_L} = {X_C}$<br>$ \\Rightarrow Z = R = 4\\Omega $<br>Therefore, voltage drop across inductor,<br>${\\left( {{V_{rms}}} \\right)_L} = {I_{rms}} \\times {X_L} = \\frac{{{V_{rms}}}}{Z} \\times \\omega L$<br>$ = \\frac{{{V_{rms}}}}{R} \\times \\frac{L}{{\\sqrt {LC} }}$ $$\\,\\left( {\\because \\,{\\text{At}}\\,{\\text{resonance}}\\,\\,\\omega  = \\frac{1}{{\\sqrt {LC} }}} \\right)$$<br>$ = \\frac{{{V_{rms}}}}{R} \\times \\sqrt {\\frac{L}{C}} $<br>$ = \\frac{{0.2}}{4} \\times \\sqrt {\\frac{{200 \\times {{10}^{ - 3}}}}{{80 \\times {{10}^{ - 6}}}}}  = 2.5V$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44931,'physics',38,1065,2539,'The electric field part of an electromagnetic wave in vacuum is,<br>${E}=3.1 \\cos \\left[(1.8) {y}+\\left(5.4 \\times 10^{8}\\right) {t}\\right] \\hat{{i}} \\dfrac{{N}}{{C}}$<br>What is the wavelength   of this part of electromagnetic wave?','[{"text": "$3.49\\\\,m$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$5.62\\\\,m$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1.78\\\\,m$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$7.54\\\\,m$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Comparing the given equation with,<br>$E = {E_0}\\cos \\left( {ky + \\omega t} \\right)$<br>We get, ${k}=1.8 \\,{rad}\\, {m}^{-1}$,<br>Now, $\\lambda  = \\frac{{2\\pi }}{k} = \\frac{{2 \\times 22}}{{1.8 \\times 7}} = 3.49\\,m$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44932,'physics',39,1102,2629,'A copper ball of radius $1\\,cm$ and work function $4.47\\,eV$ is irradiated with ultraviolet radiation of wavelength $2500\\mathop A\\limits^o $. The effect of irradiation results in the emission of electrons from the ball. Further the ball will acquire charge and due to this there will be a finite value of the potential on the ball. The charge acquired by the ball is:','[{"text": "$5.5 \\\\times {10^{ - 13}}C$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$7.5 \\\\times {10^{ - 13}}C$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$4.5 \\\\times {10^{ - 12}}C$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2.5 \\\\times {10^{ - 11}}C$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'When the sphere acquires a maximum charge at which the emitted photoelectrons can\'t escape from the sphere due to electrostatic attraction. The electron $K.E$ converts into $P.E$. The $K.E$ of the photoelectron is<br>$K.E = hv - \\phi  = P.E$<br>$\\frac{{hc}}{\\lambda } - 4.47eV = \\frac{1}{{4\\pi {\\varepsilon _0}}}\\frac{{\\left( {1.6 \\times {{10}^{ - 19}}} \\right)q}}{{{{10}^{ - 2}}\\,m}}$<br>$ \\Rightarrow q = 5.5 \\times {10^{ - 13}}C$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',2013,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44934,'physics',37,973,2268,'In a series $LCR$ circuit $R = 300\\Omega ,L = 0.9H,C = 2\\mu F,\\omega  = 1000rad/s$.The impedance of the circuit is','[{"text": "$500\\\\Omega $", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$1300\\\\Omega $", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$400\\\\Omega $", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$900\\\\Omega $", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Impedance for series $LCR$ circuit is given by $Z = \\sqrt {{R^2} + {{\\left( {\\omega L - \\frac{1}{{\\omega C}}} \\right)}^2}} $<br>Given, resistance,$R = 300\\Omega $  inductance $L = 0.9H,$ capacitance $C = 2\\mu F = 2 \\times {10^{ - 6}}F$ and angular frequency $\\omega  = 1000rad/s$<br>Substituting the given values in the above equation, we get <br>$ \\Rightarrow Z = \\sqrt {{{300}^2} + {{\\left( {1000 \\times 0.9 - \\frac{1}{{1000 \\times 2 \\times {{10}^{ - 6}}}}} \\right)}^2}} $<br>$ \\Rightarrow Z = \\sqrt {{{300}^2} + {{\\left( {900 - 500} \\right)}^2}}  = 500\\Omega $',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2019,'MHTCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44936,'physics',39,1102,2629,'In an experiment on photoelectric effect, a student plots stopping potential \\[{V_o}\\] against reciprocal of the wavelength $$\\lambda$$ of the incident light of two different metals \\[A\\] and \\[B\\]. These are shown in the figure. <br> <br> Looking at the graphs, you can most appropriately say that:<div class="matching-question"></div>','[{"text": "Work function of metal $B$ is greater than that of metal $A$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "For light of certain wavelength falling on both metal, maximum kinetic energy of electrons emitted from $A$ will be greater than those emitted from $$B$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Work function of metal $A$ is greater than that of metal $B$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Student data is not correct", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'$\\frac{{hc}}{\\lambda } - \\phi  = e{V_0} \\Rightarrow {V_0} = \\frac{{hc}}{{e\\lambda }} - \\frac{\\phi }{e}$<br>$$\\left|\\phi_{A}\\right|>\\left|\\phi_{B}\\right|$$',NULL,'Hard','Graph/Figure',2013,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/39_44936_question_1.png'),
(44937,'physics',37,974,2766,'In series LCR circuit $R = 18\\Omega $  and impedance is $33\\Omega $. An rms voltage 220$V$ is applied across the circuit. The true power consumed in $AC$ circuit is','[{"text": "$220W$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$400W$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$600W$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$800W$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Given,$R = 18\\Omega ,Z = 33\\Omega ,{V_{rms}} = 220V$<br>The power consumed in an $AC$ circuit is given as,<br>$P = {{\\rm{V}}_{rms}}.{I_{rms}}.\\cos \\phi  = {{\\rm{V}}_{rms}}.\\frac{{{{\\rm{V}}_{rms}}}}{Z}.\\frac{R}{Z}$<br>$ = \\frac{{220 \\times 220 \\times 18}}{{33 \\times 33}} = 20 \\times 20 \\times 2 = 800W$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2017,'MHTCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44940,'physics',39,1104,2631,'A photon of wavelength $$\\lambda$$ is scattered from an electron, which was at rest. The wavelength shift $$\\Delta \\lambda$$ is three times of $$\\lambda$$ and the angle of scattering $$\\theta$$ is $$60^{\\circ}$$. The angle at which the electron recoiled is $$\\phi$$. The value of $$\\tan \\phi$$ is : (electron speed is much smaller than the speed of light)','[{"text": "0.16", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "0.28", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "0.25", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "0.22", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Given that $$\\Delta \\lambda=3 \\lambda \\Rightarrow \\lambda^{\\prime}=4 \\lambda$$<br>The scattering is shown in the figure<br><img src=\'/img/data/physics/39_44940_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' /> <br>${m_0}v\\sin \\phi  = \\frac{h}{{{\\lambda ^\\prime }}}\\sin \\theta  = \\frac{h}{{4\\lambda }}\\sin \\theta \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,(1)$<br>$$\\dfrac{h}{\\lambda^{\\prime}} \\cos \\theta+m_{0} v \\cos \\phi=\\dfrac{h}{\\lambda}$$<br>${m_0}v\\cos \\phi  = \\frac{h}{\\lambda } - \\frac{h}{{4\\lambda }}\\cos \\theta \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,(2)$<br>From (1) & (2)<br>$$\\tan \\phi=\\dfrac{\\dfrac{1}{4} \\sin \\theta}{1-\\dfrac{1}{4} \\cos \\theta}=\\dfrac{\\sqrt{3}}{7} \\simeq 0.25$$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',2014,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44941,'physics',37,975,2275,'What is the value of inductance $L$ for which the current is maximum in a series $LCR$ circuit with $\\omega  = 1000\\,{s^{ - 1}},C = 10\\mu F$?','[{"text": "$100\\\\,mH$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Cannot be calculated unless R is known", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1\\\\,mH$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$10\\\\,mH$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Current in $LCR$ series circuit,<br>$i = \\frac{V}{{\\sqrt {{R^2} + {{\\left( {{X_L} - {X_C}} \\right)}^2}} }}$<br>where, $V$ is rms value of voltage $R$ is resistance,${X_L}$  is inductive reactance and ${X_C}$ is capacitive reactance. For current to be maximum, denominator should be minimum which can be done, i.e., during the resonance of series $LCR$ circuit<br>${X_L} = {X_C}\\quad {\\rm{i}}{\\rm{.e,}}\\quad \\omega L = \\frac{1}{{\\omega C}}$<br>or $L = \\frac{1}{{{\\omega ^2}C}}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,(1)$<br>Given,$\\omega  = 1000{s^{ - 1}},\\quad C = 10\\mu F = 10 \\times {10^{ - 6}}\\,F$<br>Hence,$L = \\frac{1}{{{{\\left( {1000} \\right)}^2} \\times 10 \\times {{10}^{ - 6}}}}$<br>$ = 0.1\\,H = 100\\,mH$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44943,'physics',39,1104,2631,'For which of the following particles will it be most difficult to experimentally verify the de Broglie relationship?','[{"text": "a dust particle", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "an electron", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "a proton", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "an $$\\\\alpha$$-particle", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'If mass of a particle is more, then its de Broglie\'s wavelength is small and hence it is difficult to find $$\\lambda$$ for a dust particle',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',2014,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44945,'physics',37,977,2281,'A transformer works on the principle of','[{"text": "Self induction", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Electrical inertia", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Mutual induction", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Magnetic effect of the electrical current.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'A transformer is based on the principle of mutual induction.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2011,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44947,'physics',39,1102,2629,'A beam of light has two wavelengths $4972\\mathop A\\limits^o $  and $6216\\mathop A\\limits^o $ with a total intensity of $3.6 \\times {10^{ - 3}}\\,W\\,{m^{ - 2}}$ equally distributed among the two wavelengths. The beam falls normally on an area of $1\\,c{m^2}$ of a clean metallic surface of work function $2.3\\,eV$. Assume that there is no loss of the light by reflection and that each capable photon ejects one electron. The number of photoelectrons liberated in $2\\,s$ is approximately:-','[{"text": "$$15 \\\\times 10^{11}$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$6 \\\\times 10^{11}$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$9 \\\\times 10^{11}$$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$$11 \\\\times 10^{11}$$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'As the work function of metal is $2.3eV$ only the wave having wavelength $4972\\mathop A\\limits^o $ is capable of producing photoelectrons. So only half of the incident energy is useful.<br>The no of photons $$=$$ No of photoelectrons<br>$$=\\dfrac{1.8 \\times 10^{-3} \\times 10^{-4} \\times 2 \\times 4972 \\times 10^{-10}}{6.62 \\times 10^{-34} \\times 3 \\times 10^{8}}=9 \\times 10^{11}$$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',2014,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44948,'physics',37,977,2770,'A step up transformer has 300 turns of primary winding and 450 turns of secondary winding . A primary is connected to 150 $V$ and the current flowing through it is 9 $A$. The current and voltage in the secondary are','[{"text": "$13.5\\\\,A,100\\\\,V$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$13.5\\\\,A,225\\\\,V$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$4.5\\\\,A,100\\\\,V$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$6.0\\\\,A,225\\\\,V$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Given, number of turns in primary winding, <br>${N_P} = 300$.<br>Number of turns in secondary winding,<br>${N_S} = 450$.<br>Primary voltage,${V_P} = 150V$.<br>Primary current, ${I_P} = 9A$.<br>For step - up transformer<br>$\\frac{{{V_S}}}{{{V_P}}} = \\frac{{{N_S}}}{{{N_P}}}$<br>$ \\Rightarrow \\frac{{{V_S}}}{{150}} = \\frac{{450}}{{300}}$<br>$ \\Rightarrow {V_S} = \\frac{{450}}{{300}} \\times 150 = 225V$<br>Again,${V_P}{I_P} = {V_S}{I_S}$ (assuming ideal transformer)<br>$ \\Rightarrow 150 \\times 9 = 225 \\times {I_S}$<br>$ \\Rightarrow {I_S} = \\frac{{1350}}{{225}} = 6.0A$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2020,'MHTCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44962,'physics',39,1102,2629,'The maximum velocity of the photoelectrons emitted from the surface is $v$ when the light of frequency $$n$$ falls on a metal surface. If the incident frequency is increased to 3 $n$, the maximum velocity of the ejected photoelectrons will be -','[{"text": "More than $\\\\sqrt 3 \\\\,v$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Equal to $\\\\sqrt 3 \\\\,v$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$v$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Less than $\\\\sqrt 3 v$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'$$E_{1}=h n-\\phi ; \\quad E_{2}=3 h n-\\phi$$<br>$$\\begin{aligned}& E_{2}=3\\left(E_{1}+\\phi\\right)-\\phi \\\\& E_{2}=3 E_{1}+2 \\phi \\\\& \\dfrac{1}{2} m v_{2}^{2}=3\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2} m v_{1}^{2}\\right)+2 \\phi \\\\& v_{2}=\\sqrt{3} v_{1}+c\\end{aligned}$$<br>Where $c$ is a constant.',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',2017,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44950,'physics',38,1068,2545,'Sea water at frequency $v=4 \\times 10^{8} {~Hz}$ has permittivity  $\\varepsilon  \\approx 80\\,{\\varepsilon _0},$  permeability $\\mu \\approx \\mu_{0}$ and resistivity $\\rho=0.25\\, \\Omega-{m}$. Imagine a parallel plate capacitor immersed in sea water and driven by an alternating voltage source ${V}({t})={V}_{0} \\sin (2 \\,\\pi {vt}$ ). What is the ratio of amplitude of the conduction current density to the displacement current density?','[{"text": "0.44", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "0.69", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "0.98", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "0.2", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'$V(t) = {V_0}\\sin 2\\pi vt$<br>Let distance between the plates $={d}$.<br>Electric field $=\\dfrac{{V}({t})}{{d}}=\\dfrac{{V}_{0}}{{~d}} \\sin 2 \\pi {vt}$<br>Conducting current density <br>$J_{c}=\\dfrac{E}{\\rho}=\\dfrac{V_{0}}{\\rho d} \\,\\sin 2 \\pi v t=J_{0 c} \\sin 2 \\pi v t$<br>where, ${J}_{0 {c}}$ is maximum conduction current density.<br>Displacement current density,<br>${J}_{{d}}=\\varepsilon \\dfrac{{d}}{{dt}}\\left[\\dfrac{{V}_{0}}{{~d}} \\sin (2 \\pi {vt})\\right]=\\dfrac{2 \\pi v \\varepsilon}{{d}} {V}_{0} \\cos (2 \\pi v {t})$<br>$J_{d}=J_{0_{d}} \\cos (2 \\pi v t)$<br>$\\dfrac{J_{0_{d}}}{J_{0_{c}}}=\\dfrac{\\dfrac{2 \\pi \\vee \\varepsilon V_{0}}{d}}{\\dfrac{V_{0}}{\\rho d}}$<br>$=2 \\pi v \\varepsilon \\rho$<br>$=2 \\pi \\times 4 \\times 10^{8} \\times 80 \\,\\varepsilon_{0} \\times 0.25$<br>$ = \\frac{{4 \\times {{10}^9}}}{{9 \\times {{10}^9}}} = \\frac{4}{9}\\quad \\left( {\\frac{1}{{4\\pi {\\varepsilon _0}}} = 9 \\times {{10}^9}N{m^2}/{C^2}} \\right)$<br>$=0.44$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44951,'physics',39,1104,2631,'A parallel beam of electrons travelling in $x$ direction falls on a slit of width $d$ (see figure). If after passing the slit, an electron acquires momentum $$p_{y}$$ in the $y$-direction then for a majority of electrons passing through the slit ($h$ is Planck\'s constant) :','[{"text": "$$\\\\left|p_{y}\\\\right| d>>h$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$\\\\left|p_{y}\\\\right| d \\\\simeq h$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$\\\\left|p_{y}\\\\right| d>h$$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$$\\\\left|p_{y}\\\\right| d < h$$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'$$d \\sin \\theta=\\lambda \\Rightarrow \\sin \\theta=\\dfrac{\\lambda}{d} < 1 \\Rightarrow \\lambda < d$$<br>$\\frac{h}{{\\left| {{p_y}} \\right|}} < d \\Rightarrow h < \\left| {{p_y}} \\right|d$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',2015,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/39_44951_question_1.png'),
(44952,'physics',37,977,2770,'A step-up transformer operates on a 230 $V$ line and a load current of 2 $A$. The ratio of primary and secondary windings is 1 : 25. Then the current in the primary is','[{"text": "$25\\\\,A$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$50\\\\,A$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$15\\\\,A$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$12.5\\\\,A$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Load current ${I_S} = 2A,$<br> Turns ratio (primary to secondary)  $ = \\,\\,\\frac{{{N_P}}}{{{N_S}}} = \\frac{1}{{25}}$<br>For an ideal transformer, $\\frac{{{I_P}}}{{{I_S}}} = \\frac{{{N_S}}}{{{N_P}}}$<br>$\\therefore $ Current in the primary, <br>${I_P} = \\left( {\\frac{{{N_S}}}{{{N_P}}}} \\right){I_S} = \\left( {\\frac{{25}}{1}} \\right)(2A) = 50A$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2018,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44955,'physics',39,1104,2631,'The de Broglie wavelength associated with the electron in the $n = 4$ level is :','[{"text": "${\\\\frac{1}{4}^{th}}$ of the de Broglie wavelength of the electron in the ground state", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Four times the de Broglie wavelength of the electron in the ground state", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Two times the de Broglie wavelength of the electron in the ground state", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Half of the de Broglie wavelength of the electron in the ground state", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'de Broglie wavelength of electron<br>$\\lambda  = \\frac{h}{{mv}}$<br>As we know, $v\\,\\alpha \\frac{1}{n}$ So, $\\lambda \\,\\alpha \\,n \\Rightarrow {\\lambda _4} = 4{\\lambda _1}$<br>$$\\lambda_{1}$$ is the de Broglie wavelength of the electron in the ground state.',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',2015,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44956,'physics',37,977,2770,'An ideal transformer converts 200 $V$ $AC$ to 3.3 $kV$ $AC$ to transmit a power of 4.4 $kW$. If primary coil has 600 turns, then alternating current in secondary coil is','[{"text": "$\\\\frac{1}{3}A$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{4}{3}A$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{5}{3}A$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{7}{3}A$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Output voltage $\\left( {{V_S}} \\right) = 3.3 \\times {10^3}V$<br>Power ($P$) $ = 4.4kW$<br>$ = 4.4 \\times {10^3}W$<br>For a ideal transformer,<br>$P = {V_{\\rm{S}}} \\times {I_{\\rm{S}}} \\Rightarrow {I_{\\rm{S}}} = \\frac{P}{{{V_{\\rm{S}}}}}$<br>$ \\Rightarrow {I_S} = \\frac{{4.4 \\times {{10}^3}}}{{3.3 \\times {{10}^3}}} = \\frac{4}{3}A$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2018,'MHTCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44958,'physics',39,1104,2631,'de-Broglie wavelength of an electron accelerated by voltage of $50\\,V$ is close to $\\left( {|e| = 1.6 \\times {{10}^{ - 19}}C,{m_e} = 9.1 \\times {{10}^{ - 31}}\\,kg} \\right.$, $\\left. {h = 6.6 \\times {{10}^{ - 34}}Js} \\right)$:','[{"text": "$1.7\\\\mathop A\\\\limits^o $", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$1.2\\\\mathop A\\\\limits^o $", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$0.5\\\\mathop A\\\\limits^o $", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2.4\\\\mathop A\\\\limits^o $", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Momentum $$P=\\sqrt{2 m E}$$<br>$\\lambda  = \\frac{h}{p} = \\frac{h}{{\\sqrt {2mE} }}$<br>$ = \\frac{{6.6 \\times {{10}^{ - 34}}}}{{\\sqrt {2 \\times 9.1 \\times {{10}^{ - 31}} \\times 1.6 \\times {{10}^{ - 19}} \\times 50} }}$<br>$ = 1.7 \\times {10^{ - 10}}\\,m = 1.7\\mathop A\\limits^o $',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',2015,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44960,'physics',37,977,2771,'A current of 5$A$ is flowing at 220 $V$ in the primary coil of a transformer. If the voltage produced in the secondary coil is 2200 $V$ and 50% of power is lost, then the current in the secondary will be','[{"text": "$0.25\\\\,A$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$0.5\\\\,A$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2.5\\\\,A$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$5\\\\,A$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Given the power output is 50% of the input power, ${i_s}{V_s} = (1/2){i_p}{V_p}$<br>Also given ${i_p} = 5A,{V_p} = 220V$ and ${V_s} = 2200V$<br>${i_S} = \\frac{1}{2}\\frac{{{i_P}{V_P}}}{{{V_S}}} \\Rightarrow {i_S} = \\frac{1}{2} \\times \\frac{{5A \\times 220V}}{{2200\\,V}}$<br>${i_S} = 0.25\\,A$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2008,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44963,'physics',37,977,2771,'For a transformer, the turns ratio is 3 and its efficiency is 0.75. The current flowing in the primary coil is 2$A$ and the voltage applied to it is 100$V$. Then the voltage and the current flowing in the secondary coil are   respectively.','[{"text": "$150{\\\\rm{ }}V,{\\\\rm{ }}1.5{\\\\rm{ }}A$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$300{\\\\rm{ }}V,{\\\\rm{ 0}}.5{\\\\rm{ }}A$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$300{\\\\rm{ }}V,{\\\\rm{ 1}}.5{\\\\rm{ }}A$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$150{\\\\rm{ }}V,{\\\\rm{ 0}}.5{\\\\rm{ }}A$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Given,$\\frac{{{N_S}}}{{{N_P}}} = 3,\\eta  = 0.75m{I_P} = 2A,{V_P} = 100V$<br>For a transformer, turns ratio = voltage ratio<br>$ \\Rightarrow \\,\\,\\frac{{{N_S}}}{{{N_P}}} = \\frac{{{V_S}}}{{{V_P}}}$<br>$\\therefore \\,\\,{V_S} = \\frac{{{N_S}}}{{{N_P}}} \\times {V_P} = 3 \\times 100\\,V = 300\\,V$<br>Efficiency of a transformer,<br>$\\eta  = \\frac{{Output\\,power}}{{Input\\,power}} = \\frac{{{V_S}{I_S}}}{{{V_P}{I_P}}}$<br>$\\therefore \\,\\,0.75 = \\frac{{(300V)({I_S})}}{{(100V)(2A)}}$<br>${I_s} = \\frac{{1.5}}{3}A = 0.5A$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2013,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44965,'physics',38,1065,2542,'A  $27{\\mkern 1mu} \\,mW$  laser beam has a cross-sectional area of ${10 {~mm}^{2}}$. Find the magnitude of the maximum electric field   in this electromagnetic wave is given by [Given permittivity of space ${\\varepsilon_{0}=9 \\times 10^{-12}}$ $SI$ units, Speed of light ${\\left.c=3 \\times 10^{8} {~m} / {s}\\right]}$ :','[{"text": "$3.1\\\\,k\\\\,V/m$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1.4\\\\,k\\\\,V/m$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$5.2\\\\,k\\\\,V/m$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$7.4\\\\,k\\\\,V/m$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Intensity of $EM$ wave is given by<br>$I = \\frac{{{\\rm{ Power }}}}{{{\\rm{ Area }}}} = \\frac{1}{2}{\\varepsilon _0}E_0^2C = \\frac{{27 \\times {{10}^{ - 3}}}}{{10 \\times {{10}^{ - 6}}}}$<br>$ = \\frac{1}{2} \\times 9 \\times {10^{ - 12}} \\times {E^2} \\times 3 \\times {10^8}$<br>$E = \\sqrt 2  \\times {10^3}kV/m = 1.4\\,kV/m$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44966,'physics',39,1104,2631,'The de-Broglie wavelength $$\\left(\\lambda_{B}\\right)$$ associated with the electron orbiting in the second excited state of hydrogen atom is related to that in the ground state $$\\left(\\lambda_{G}\\right)$$ by:','[{"text": "$$\\\\lambda_{B}=3 \\\\lambda_{G}$$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$$\\\\lambda_{B}=2 \\\\lambda_{G}$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$\\\\lambda_{B}=3 \\\\lambda_{G / 3}$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$\\\\lambda_{B}=3 \\\\lambda_{G / 2}$$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'$$\\lambda \\alpha \\dfrac{1}{P} \\Rightarrow \\lambda \\alpha \\dfrac{1}{v}$$<br>The velocity<br>$$v \\alpha \\dfrac{1}{n} \\Rightarrow \\lambda \\alpha n \\Rightarrow \\dfrac{\\lambda_{B}}{\\lambda_{G}}=\\dfrac{n_{B}}{n_{G}}=3$$<br>$\\therefore {\\lambda _B} = 3{\\lambda _G}$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',2018,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44967,'physics',37,977,2770,'A step down transformer has 50 turns on secondary and 1000 turns on primary winding. If a transofrmer is connected to 220$V$, 1$A$  $AC$. source, what is output current of the transformer?','[{"text": "$100\\\\,A$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{1}{{20}}\\\\,A$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2\\\\,A$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$20\\\\,A$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'$\\frac{{{I_P}}}{{{I_S}}} = \\frac{{{N_S}}}{{{N_P}}},$<br>where subscripts ‘$P$’ and ‘$S$’ represenet primary and secondary.<br>$\\therefore \\,\\,{I_S} = \\left( {\\frac{{{N_P}}}{{{N_S}}}} \\right){I_P}$<br>Here,${I_P} = 1A,{N_P} = 1000,{N_S} = 50$<br>$\\therefore \\,{I_S} = \\left( {\\frac{{1000}}{{50}}} \\right)(1\\,A) = 20A$<br>Hence the output current of the transformer is $20A$.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2014,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44970,'physics',39,1102,2629,'Surface of certain metal is first illuminated with light of wavelength ${\\lambda _1} = 350\\,nm$ and then, by light of wavelength ${\\lambda _2} = 540\\,nm$. It is found that the maximum speed of the photo electrons in the two cases differ by a factor of 2 . The work function of the metal (in $eV$ ) is close to $$\\left(\\right.$$ Energy of photon $\\left. { = \\frac{{1240}}{{\\lambda (in\\,nm)}}eV} \\right)$','[{"text": "5.6", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "2.5", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "1.4", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "1.8", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'${E_1} = \\frac{{1240}}{{350}}eV;{E_2} = \\frac{{1240}}{{540}}eV$<br>Also, ${v_1} = 2{v_2}$<br>Using Einstein photoelectric equation,<br>$E - \\phi  = \\frac{1}{2}m{v^2}$<br>$\\frac{{{E_1} - \\phi }}{{{E_2} - \\phi }} = \\frac{{v_1^2}}{{v_2^2}} = 4 \\Rightarrow {E_1} - \\phi  = 4{E_2} - 4\\phi $<br>$\\phi  = \\frac{{4{E_2} - {E_1}}}{3}$<br>$ = \\frac{{1240}}{3}\\left( {\\frac{4}{{540}} - \\frac{1}{{350}}} \\right) \\approx 1.88eV$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',2019,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44971,'physics',37,977,2771,'A 220 $V$ input is supplied to a transformer. The output circuit draws a current of  2.0 $A$ at 440 $V$. If the efficiency of the transformer is 80%, the current drawn by the primary windings of the transformer is','[{"text": "$2.5\\\\,A$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$3.6\\\\,A$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2.8\\\\,A$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$5.0\\\\,A$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Efficiency is defined as the ratio of output power and input power<br>i.e., $\\eta \\%  = \\frac{{{P_{out}}}}{{{P_{input}}}} \\times 100 = \\frac{{{V_s}{i_s}}}{{{V_p}{i_p}}} \\times 100$<b>$80 = \\frac{{2 \\times 440}}{{220 \\times {i_p}}} \\times 100 \\Rightarrow \\quad {i_p} = 5A$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44973,'physics',39,1102,2629,'When a certain photosensitive surface is illuminated with monochromatic light of frequency $$v$$, the stopping potential for the photo current is $\\frac{{ - {V_0}}}{2}$. When the surface is illuminated by monochromatic light of frequency $$\\dfrac{v}{2}$$, the stopping potential is $ - {V_0}$. The threshold frequency for photoelectric emission is','[{"text": "$$\\\\dfrac{4 v}{3}$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$2 v$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$\\\\dfrac{5 v}{3}$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$\\\\dfrac{3 v}{2}$$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Einstein\'s photoelectric equation in two cases is given by<br>$\\frac{{e\\,{V_0}}}{2} = hv - h{v_0}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,(1)$<br>$e{V_0} = h\\frac{v}{2} - h{v_0}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,(2)$<br>From eqn. (1) and (2),<br>$\\frac{1}{2} = \\frac{{hv - h{v_0}}}{{hv/2 - h{v_0}}} \\Rightarrow {v_0} = \\frac{3}{2}v$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',2019,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44975,'physics',37,977,2771,'A transformer is used to light a 100 $W$ and 110$V$ lamp from a 220 $V$ mains. If the main current is 0.5 $A$, the efficiency of the transformer is approximately','[{"text": "$50\\\\% $", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$10\\\\% $", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$90\\\\% $", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$30\\\\% $", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'$\\eta  = \\frac{{{\\rm{Output power}}}}{{{\\rm{Input power}}}}$<br>$\\eta  = \\frac{{{V_s}{I_s}}}{{{V_p}{I_p}}}$<br>Given,${V_s}{I_s} = 100\\,W,\\,{V_p} = 220\\,V,\\,{I_P} = 0.5A$<br>Hence,$\\eta  = \\frac{{100}}{{220 \\times 0.5}} = 0.90 = 90\\% $',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44977,'physics',39,1102,2629,'In photoelectric effect stopping potential for electromagnetic radiations depends on','[{"text": "Amplitude", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Intensity", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Phase", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Frequency", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'The stopping potential in photoelectric effect depends on frequency of incident radiation.',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',2021,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44978,'physics',37,976,2280,'$LC$ oscillations are similar and analogous to the mechanical oscillations of a block attached to a spring. The electrical equivalent of the force constant of the spring is','[{"text": "Reciprocal of capacitive reactance", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Capacitive reactance", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Reciprocal of capacitance", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Capacitance", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Equation describing $LC$ circuit oscillation:<br>$L\\frac{{{d^2}q}}{{d{t^2}}} + \\frac{1}{C}q = 0$<br>Equation describing spring-mass system oscillation:<br>$m\\frac{{{d^2}x}}{{d{t^2}}} + kx = 0$<br> Hence $k$ is equivalent to $1/C$.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44979,'physics',40,239,478,'The effective current due to the rotation of electron in  atom depends on $Z$ and $n$ as','[{"text": "$\\\\frac{Z}{{{n^2}}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{Z}{n}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{{{Z^2}}}{{{n^3}}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{{{Z^2}}}{n}$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'$i = \\frac{e}{T} = \\frac{{e{{\\rm{v}}_n}}}{{2\\pi {r_n}}} = \\frac{{m{Z^2}{e^5}}}{{4\\varepsilon _0^2{h^3}{n^3}}}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44980,'physics',38,1065,2540,'A parallel-plate capacitor made of circular plates, each of radius ${R=6.0 {~cm}}$, has a capacitance $C = 100\\,pF$. The capacitor is connected to a $230\\,V\\,a.c.$ supply with a (angular) frequency of  $300\\,rad/s.$  Determine the amplitude of ${B}$ (in ${10^{-11} {~T}}$ ) at a point $3.0\\,cm$ from the axis between the plates.','[{"text": "1.63", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "5.98", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "3.65", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "7.24", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'We are given that, $C = 100\\,pF = 100 \\times {10^{ - 12}}\\,F = {10^{ - 10}}\\,F,$<br>${V_{rms}} = 230\\,\\,V,\\omega  = 300\\,rad/s.$<br>Capacitive reactance, ${X_{C}=\\dfrac{1}{\\omega C}=\\dfrac{1}{300 \\times 10^{-10}}=\\dfrac{10^{8}}{3} \\Omega}$<br>If ${I_{{rms}}}$ is the $rms$ value of the conduction current,<br>$I_{{rms}}=\\dfrac{V_{{rms}}}{X_{C}}=\\dfrac{230}{10^{8} / 3}=6.9 \\times 10^{-6} {~A}=6.9 \\mu {A}$<br>The conduction current is equal to the displacement even in case of $ac$.<br>Let ${I_{0}}$ be the peak value (amplitude) of the conduction current $(I)$ Clearly, ${I_{{rms}}=\\dfrac{I_{0}}{\\sqrt{2}}}$<br>Or ${\\quad I_{0}=I_{\\text {rms }} \\times \\sqrt{2}=(6.9 \\mu A) \\times 1.414=9.76 \\mu {A}}$<br>Obviously, peak value (amplitude) of ${I_{d}}$ is also the same, $i.e.,$ ${9.76 \\mu {A}}$.<br>Consider a circular path of radius ${3.0 {~cm}(=0.03 {~m})}$ parallel to the plates of the capacitor and with its centre ${(O)}$ on the axis of the plates.<br>According to the Ampere\'s law,<br>$\\oint {\\vec B}  \\cdot \\overrightarrow {d\\ell }  = {\\mu _0}\\left( {I + {I_d}} \\right){\\rm{ }}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\left( 1 \\right)$<br>Now ${\\oint \\vec{B} \\cdot \\overrightarrow{d \\ell}=B \\times}$ circumference of the circle of radius  $0.03\\,m = B(2\\pi  \\times 0.03)\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\left( 2 \\right)$<br>As there is no conduction current in the plates,<br>$I = 0{\\rm{ }}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\left( 3 \\right)$<br>Further, ${I_{d}=\\dfrac{9.76(\\mu {A})}{\\pi(0.06)^{2}} \\times \\pi(0.03)^{2}}$<br>$ = 2.44\\,\\mu A = 2.44 \\times {10^{ - 6}}\\,A\\quad \\quad (4)$<br>From Eqs. (1), (2), (3) and (4),<br>$B(2\\pi  \\times 0.03) = {\\mu _0}\\left( {2.44 \\times {{10}^{ - 6}}} \\right)$<br>Or $B = \\left( {\\frac{{{\\mu _0}}}{{2\\pi }}} \\right)\\frac{{2.44 \\times {{10}^{ - 6}}}}{{0.03}}$<br>$ = \\left( {2 \\times {{10}^{ - 7}}} \\right)\\left( {81.3 \\times {{10}^{ - 6}}} \\right)$<br>$ = 1.63 \\times {10^{ - 11}}\\,T$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/38_44980_question_1.png'),
(44981,'physics',39,1104,2631,'If the electron and proton are accelerated by same potential difference $100\\,V$, then find the ratio of their de-Broglie wavelengths<br>${m_e} = 9.1 \\times {10^{ - 31}}\\,kg$<br>${m_p} = 1.6825 \\times {10^{ - 27}}\\,kg$','[{"text": "43", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$$\\\\dfrac{1}{43}$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$\\\\dfrac{40}{1}$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$\\\\dfrac{1}{40}$$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'An electron and a proton, accelerated through the same potential difference $$V$$, acquire same kinetic energy $K( = eV)$. For a particle of mass $m$ and kinetic energy $K$, de-Broglie wavelength: $$\\lambda=\\dfrac{h}{m v}=\\dfrac{h}{\\sqrt{2 K m}}=\\dfrac{h}{\\sqrt{2 e V m}}$$<br>$$\\Rightarrow \\dfrac{\\lambda_{e}}{\\lambda_{p}}=\\sqrt{\\dfrac{m_{p}}{m_{e}}} \\approx 43 .$$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',2021,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44982,'physics',37,974,2274,'For the circuit shown in the figure the rms value of voltages across $$\\mathrm{R}$$ and coil are $$E_{1}$$ and $$E_{2}$$, respectively.<br>The power (thermal) developed across the coil is','[{"text": "$$\\\\dfrac{E-E_{1}^{2}-E_{2}^{2}}{2 R}$$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$$\\\\dfrac{E^{2}}{2 R}$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$\\\\dfrac{E-E_{1}^{2}}{2 R}$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$\\\\dfrac{\\\\left(E-E_{1}\\\\right)^{2}}{2 R}$$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Draw the phasor diagram.<br>$$E^{2}=E_{1}^{2}+E_{2}^{2}+2 E_{1} E_{2} \\cos \\theta$$<br><img src=\'/img/data/physics/37_44982_explanation_1.jpg\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' /> <br>Thermal power developed in coil is $$P=E_{2} \\cos \\theta \\times I$$ and<br>$$I=\\dfrac{E_{1}}{R} \\Rightarrow P=\\dfrac{E_{1} E_{2}}{R} \\cos \\theta=\\dfrac{E^{2}-E_{1}^{2}-E_{2}^{2}}{2 R}$$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/37_44982_question_1.jpg'),
(44983,'physics',38,1065,2540,'The electric field in an electromagnetic wave is given by $\\vec{E}=\\hat{i} 40 \\cos \\omega\\left(t-\\dfrac{z}{c}\\right) N C^{-1}$. The magnetic field induction of this wave is (in $S I$ unit)','[{"text": "$\\\\vec{B}=\\\\hat{k} \\\\dfrac{40}{c} \\\\cos \\\\omega\\\\left(t-\\\\dfrac{z}{c}\\\\right)$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\vec{B}=\\\\hat{i} \\\\dfrac{40}{c} \\\\cos \\\\omega\\\\left(t-\\\\dfrac{z}{c}\\\\right)$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\vec{B}=\\\\hat{j} 40 \\\\cos \\\\omega\\\\left(t-\\\\dfrac{z}{c}\\\\right)$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\vec{B}=\\\\hat{j} \\\\dfrac{40}{c} \\\\cos \\\\omega\\\\left(t-\\\\dfrac{z}{c}\\\\right)$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'$\\vec{E}=\\hat{i} 40 \\cos \\omega\\left(t-\\dfrac{z}{c}\\right) N / C$<br>$\\vec{E}$ is along $+X$ axis: $\\vec{v}$ is along $+Z$ axis ;<br>Direction of $E M$ wave is given by<br> $\\vec{v}=\\vec{E} \\times \\vec{B}$<br>So, $\\vec{B}$ is along $+Y$ axis, $\\dfrac{E}{B}=c$<br>$\\Rightarrow B=\\dfrac{E}{c}=\\dfrac{40}{3 \\times 10^{8}}$<br>$\\therefore \\vec{B}=\\dfrac{40}{c} \\hat{j} \\cos \\omega\\left(t-\\dfrac{z}{c}\\right)$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2024,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44986,'physics',37,973,2266,'The diagram shows a capacitor $C$ and a resistor $R$ connected in series to an ac source. $$V_{1}$$ and $$V_{2}$$ are voltmeters and $A$ is an ammeter<br>Consider the fallowing statements<br>I. Reading in $A$ and ${V_2}$ are always in phase.<br>II. Reading ${V_1}$ is ahead in phase with reading in ${V_2}$.<br>III. Reading in $A$ and ${V_1}$ are always in phase.<br>Which of these statements are/is correct.','[{"text": "II and III only", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "I and II only", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "I only", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "II only", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Phasor diagram of an $RC$ circuit is<br><img src=\'/img/data/physics/37_44986_explanation_1.jpg\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' /> <br>So only I is correct',NULL,'Hard','MCQ type-2',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/37_44986_question_1.jpg'),
(44987,'physics',38,1064,2537,'An alternating voltage of amplitude $40\\,V$ and frequency $4\\,k\\,Hz$ is applied directly across the capacitor of  $12\\,\\mu F.$  The maximum displacement current between the plates of the capacitor is nearly','[{"text": "$13\\\\,A$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$8\\\\,A$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$10\\\\,A$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$12\\\\,A$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Given,  ${V_0} = 40\\,V,$  $f = 4k\\,Hz = 4 \\times {10^3}\\,Hz$  $C = 12 \\times {10^{ - 6}}\\,F$<br>Voltage at any time \' $t$ \' is given by,<br>$V = {V_0}\\sin \\,(\\omega t) = {V_0}\\sin \\,(2\\pi ft)$<br>$\\therefore V = 40\\,\\sin \\left( {2\\pi  \\times 4 \\times {{10}^3}} \\right)$<br>$ = 40\\,\\sin \\left( {8\\pi  \\times {{10}^3}t} \\right)V$<br>Displacement current, $I_{d}=$ Conduction current, $I_{C}$<br>$\\therefore I_{d}=C \\dfrac{d V}{d t}$<br>$=12 \\times 10^{-6} \\times\\left[40 \\times 8 \\pi \\times 10^{3} \\cos \\left(8 \\pi \\times 10^{3} t\\right)\\right]$<br>$=12.05 \\cos \\left(8 \\pi \\times 10^{3} t\\right)$<br>$\\therefore$ Maximum displacement current,<br>${\\left( {{I_d}} \\right)_{\\max }} = 12.05\\,A\\quad \\left( {\\therefore {\\text{For}}{{\\left( {{I_d}} \\right)}_{\\max ,}}\\cos \\theta  = 1} \\right)$<br>So, option (4) is correct',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2024,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44988,'physics',37,973,2266,'If  $10\\,mV/1kHz$ input is given to a series $RC$ circuit then output voltage across the capacitor is','[{"text": "$10\\\\,mV$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$7.5\\\\,mV$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$8.5\\\\,mV$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "None of these", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'$$\\omega=2000 \\pi ; X_{C}=\\dfrac{1}{2000 \\pi \\times 10^{-8}}=\\dfrac{10^{5}}{2 \\pi}$$<br>$Z = \\sqrt {X_C^2 + {R^2}}  \\simeq \\frac{{{{10}^5}}}{{2\\pi }}$<br>$\\therefore \\quad {V_C} \\simeq {V_{in}} = 10mV$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/37_44988_question_1.jpg'),
(44989,'physics',39,1102,2629,'The light of two different frequencies whose photons have energis $3.8\\,eV$ and $1.4\\,eV$ respectively, illuminate a metallic surface whose work function is $0.6\\,eV$. The ratio of maximum speeds of emitted electrons (for these two frequencies) respectively will be:','[{"text": "$$1: 1$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$2: 1$$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$$4: 1$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$1: 4$$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'<br>$$\\begin{aligned}& W_{0}=h v_{0}=h v-\\dfrac{1}{2} m v_{\\max }^{2} \\\\& W_{0}=h v_{0}=(h v)\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2} m v_{\\max }^{2}\\right) \\\\& \\dfrac{v_{\\max 1}}{v_{\\max 2}}=\\sqrt{\\dfrac{3.8-0.6}{1.4-0.6}}=2\\end{aligned}$$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',2022,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44991,'physics',38,1065,2539,'Electromagnetic waves travel in a medium with speed of  $1.5 \\times {10^8}\\,m{s^{ - 1}}.$  The relative permeability of the medium is 2.0 . The relative permittivity will be','[{"text": "2", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "1", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "5", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "4", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Let the relative permittivity be $\\varepsilon_{r}$.<br>Speed of an EM wave in a medium is given by<br>$v=\\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{\\mu_{r} \\varepsilon_{r}} \\sqrt{\\mu_{0} \\varepsilon_{0}}}$<br>$\\Rightarrow v=\\dfrac{c}{\\sqrt{\\mu_{r} \\varepsilon_{r}}}$<br>$\\therefore \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{\\mu _r}{\\varepsilon _r} = {\\left( {\\frac{c}{v}} \\right)^2}$<br>$\\Rightarrow \\mu_{r} \\varepsilon_{r}=\\left(\\dfrac{3 \\times 10^{8}}{1.5 \\times 10^{8}}\\right)^{2}$<br>$ \\Rightarrow {\\varepsilon _r} = \\frac{4}{{{\\mu _r}}} = \\frac{4}{2} = 2\\quad (\\therefore {\\mu _r} = 2.0\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,({\\text{given}}))$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2024,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44992,'physics',37,973,2268,'The readings of ammeter and voltmeter in the following circuit are respectively $\\left( {{V_{{\\rm{source }}}} = 220\\,V} \\right)$','[{"text": "$2.2\\\\,A,\\\\,220\\\\,V$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$1.7\\\\,A,\\\\,200\\\\,V$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1.5\\\\,A,\\\\,100\\\\,V$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2\\\\,A,\\\\,200\\\\,V$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'$$\\begin{aligned}& V=\\sqrt{V_{R}^{2}+\\left(V_{L}-V_{C}\\right)^{2}} \\\\& \\because V_{R}=V \\quad \\because V_{L}=V_{C}\\end{aligned}$$<br>$$\\therefore$$ Reading of voltmeter $$=220 \\mathrm{~V}$$<br>Reading of ammeter $$I_{r m s}=\\dfrac{E_{r m s}}{Z}=\\dfrac{220}{100}=2.2 \\mathrm{~A}$$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/37_44992_question_1.png'),
(44995,'physics',38,1065,2542,'In the given electromagnetic wave $E_{y}=600$ sin $(\\omega t-k x) V m^{-1}$, intensity of the associated light beam is  $({\\text{in}}\\,\\,W/{m^2})$ <br>(Given ${\\varepsilon _0} = 9 \\times {10^{ - 12}}{C^2}{N^{ - 1}}\\,{m^{ - 2}})$','[{"text": "729", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "243", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "486", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "972", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Given, $E_{y}=600 \\sin (\\omega t-k x)$ and<br>${\\varepsilon _0} = 9 \\times {10^{ - 12}}{C^2}/N{m^2}$<br>Intensity of $EM$ wave is given by,<br>$I=\\dfrac{1}{2} \\varepsilon_{0} E_{0}^{2} c=\\dfrac{1}{2} \\dfrac{B_{0}^{2}}{\\mu_{0}} c$<br>Intensity, $I=\\dfrac{1}{2} \\varepsilon_{0} E_{0}^{2} c$<br>$ = \\frac{1}{2} \\times 9 \\times {10^{ - 12}} \\times {600^2} \\times 3 \\times {10^8} = 486\\,W/{m^2}$<br>So, correct option is (3).',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',2024,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(48362,'biology',101,574,1153,'Arrange the following intra uterine devices (IUDs) in chronological order, non medical IUDs, copper IUDs and hormonal IUDs.','[{"text": "First, second, third", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Second, first, third", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Third, first, second", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "First, third, second", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'First, second, third',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(44996,'physics',37,973,2269,'Two identical incandescent light bulbs are connected as shown in figure. When the circuit is an AC voltage source of frequency $$f$$, which of the following observations will be correct','[{"text": "Bulb $$b_{1}$$ will blink and bulb $$b_{2}$$ will be ON constantly", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Bulb $$b_{1}$$ will light up initially and goes off, bulb $$b_{2}$$ will be ON constantly", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both bulbs will glow alternatively", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both bulbs will glow with same brightness provided $$f=\\\\dfrac{1}{2 \\\\pi} \\\\sqrt{(1 / L C)}$$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'This is a parallel circuit. For oscillation, the energy in  $L$and $C$ will be alternately maximum.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/37_44996_question_1.jpg'),
(44998,'physics',38,1065,2541,'Average force exerted on a non-reflecting surface at normal incidence is  $2.4 \\times {10^{ - 4}}\\,N.$  If  $360\\,W/c{m^2}$  is the light energy flux during span  of 1 hour 30 minutes, then the area of the surface is','[{"text": "$20\\\\,{m^2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$0.1\\\\,\\\\,{m^2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$0.02\\\\,{m^2}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$0.2\\\\,{m^2}$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Force on a non-reflecting surface at normal incidence is given by $F=\\dfrac{I A}{c}$<br>$\\therefore A = \\frac{{Fc}}{I} = \\frac{{2.4 \\times {{10}^{ - 4}} \\times 3 \\times {{10}^8} \\times {{10}^{ - 4}}}}{{360}}$<br>$ = 0.02\\,{m^2}$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',2024,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45000,'physics',37,974,2274,'An alternating current generator has an internal resistance $$R_{g}$$ and an internal reactance $$X_{g}$$. It is used to supply power to a passive load consisting of a resistance $$R_{g}$$ and a reactance $$X_{L}$$. For maximum power to be delivered from the generator to the load, the value of $$X_{L}$$ is equal to','[{"text": "$$X_{g}$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$R_{g}$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$-X_{g}$$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "zero", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'For delivering maximum power from the generator to the passive load, total reactance must vanish,<br>$$\\text { i.e., } X_{L}+X_{g}=0 \\text { or } X_{L}=-X_{g}$$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45002,'physics',38,1065,2540,'A plane $E M$ wave is propagating along $x$ direction. It has a wavelength of  $4\\,mm.$ If electric field is in $y$ direction with the maximum magnitude of  $60\\,V{m^{ - 1}},$  the equation for magnetic field is','[{"text": "$\\\\vec{B}_{z}=2 \\\\times 10^{-7} \\\\sin \\\\left[\\\\dfrac{\\\\pi}{2} \\\\times 10^{3}\\\\left(x-3 \\\\times 10^{8} t\\\\right)\\\\right] \\\\hat{k} T$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\vec{B}_{x}=60 \\\\sin \\\\left[\\\\dfrac{\\\\pi}{2}\\\\left(x-3 \\\\times 10^{8} t\\\\right)\\\\right] \\\\hat{i} T$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\vec{B}_{z}=60 \\\\sin \\\\left[\\\\dfrac{\\\\pi}{2}\\\\left(x-3 \\\\times 10^{8} t\\\\right)\\\\right] \\\\hat{k} T$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\vec{B}_{z}=2 \\\\times 10^{-7} \\\\sin \\\\left[\\\\dfrac{\\\\pi}{2}\\\\left(x-3 \\\\times 10^{8} t\\\\right)\\\\right] \\\\hat{k} T$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Given that,  $\\lambda  = 4\\,mm,{E_0} = 60\\,V/m$<br>Amplitude magnetic field is given by<br>$B_{0}=\\dfrac{E_{0}}{c}=\\dfrac{60}{3 \\times 10^{8}}=2 \\times 10^{-7}$<br>Since wave is travelling in $+x$ direction and electric field is along $+y$ axis. So, $B$ is along $z$ - axis.<br>General equation of $\\vec{B}$ is given by<br>$\\vec{B}=B_{0} \\sin [k(x-v t] \\hat{k} T$<br>$f=\\dfrac{c}{\\lambda}=\\dfrac{3 \\times 10^{8}}{4 \\times 10^{-3}}=\\dfrac{3}{4} \\times 10^{11}$<br>$\\omega  = 2\\pi f = 2\\pi  \\times \\frac{3}{4} \\times {10^{11}} = \\frac{\\pi }{2} \\times {10^3}c$<br>and $k=\\dfrac{2 \\pi}{\\lambda}=\\dfrac{2 \\pi}{4 \\times 10^{-3}}=\\dfrac{\\pi}{2} \\times 10^{3}$<br>So, $\\overrightarrow{B_{z}}=2 \\times 10^{-7} \\sin \\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{2} \\times 10^{3}\\left(x-3 \\times 10^{8} t\\right)\\right) \\hat{k} T$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',2024,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45004,'physics',37,977,2770,'A transformer has 500 primary turns and 10 secondary turns. If the secondary has a resistive load of $$15 \\Omega$$, the currents in the primary and secondary respectively, are:','[{"text": "$0.16\\\\,A,\\\\,3.2 \\\\times {10^{ - 3}}\\\\,A$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$3.2 \\\\times {10^{ - 3}}\\\\,A,\\\\,0.16\\\\,A$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$$0.16 A, 0.16 A$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$3.2 \\\\times {10^{ - 3}}\\\\,A,\\\\,3.2 \\\\times {10^{ - 3}}\\\\,A$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'We have,<br>$$\\begin{aligned}& \\dfrac{N_{s}}{N_{p}}=\\dfrac{I_{p}}{I_{s}}, \\dfrac{10}{500}=\\dfrac{I_{p}}{I_{s}} \\Rightarrow \\dfrac{I_{p}}{I_{s}}=\\dfrac{1}{50} \\\\& \\Rightarrow I_{s}=50 I_{p}\\end{aligned}$$<br>This condition is satisfied only when current in primary is $3.2 \\times {10^{ - 3}}\\,A$ and in secondary $0.16\\,A.$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45006,'physics',38,1065,2540,'The magnetic field in a plane electromagnetic wave is <br> ${B_y} = \\left( {3.5 \\times {{10}^{ - 7}}} \\right)\\sin \\left( {1.5 \\times {{10}^3}x + 0.5 \\times {{10}^{11}}t} \\right)T.$<br>The corresponding electric field will be','[{"text": "${E_\\\\gamma } = 10.5\\\\sin \\\\left( {1.5 \\\\times {{10}^3}x + 0.5 \\\\times {{10}^{11}}t} \\\\right)V{m^{ - 1}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${E_z} = 1.17\\\\sin \\\\left( {1.5 \\\\times {{10}^3}x + 0.5 \\\\times {{10}^{11}}t} \\\\right)V{m^{ - 1}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${E_y} = 1.17\\\\sin \\\\left( {1.5 \\\\times {{10}^3}x + 0.5 \\\\times {{10}^{11}}t} \\\\right)V{m^{ - 1}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${E_z} = 105\\\\sin \\\\left( {1.5 \\\\times {{10}^3}x + 0.5 \\\\times {{10}^{11}}t} \\\\right)V{m^{ - 1}}$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'The magnetic field of an $EM$ wave propagating along $x$-axis is given by,  ${B_y} = {B_0}\\sin \\,(kx + \\omega t)\\,T.$  On comparing the above equation with the given electromagnetic wave equation we get,  ${B_0} = 3.5 \\times {10^{ - 7}}\\,\\,J.$ The electric field equation is given by,<br>$E=E_{0} \\sin (k x+\\omega t) V / m$ Also,<br>Relation between $E$ and $B$ is given by $\\dfrac{E}{B}=c$<br>$\\therefore {E_0} = {B_0}c = 3 \\times 3.5 \\times {10^8} \\times {10^{ - 7}} = 105\\,V/{m^{ - 1}}$<br>$E = {E_0}\\sin \\left( {1.5 \\times {{10}^3}x + 0.5 \\times {{10}^{11}}t} \\right)V/{m^{ - 1}}$<br>${E_z} = 105\\sin \\left( {1.5 \\times {{10}^3}x + 0.5 \\times {{10}^{11}}t} \\right)V/{m^{ - 1}}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2024,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(47138,'biology',102,772,1677,'Formation of earth took place approximately years ago','[{"text": "4.5 billion years ago", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "4.5 million years ago", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "20 billion years ago", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "20 million years ago", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'The Earth is thought to have been formed about 4.6 billion years ago by collisions in the giant disc-shaped cloud of material that also formed the Sun.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45007,'physics',37,972,2260,'A direct current of $2\\,A$ is superimposed on an alternating current $$I=5 \\sin \\omega t$$ flowing through a wire. The rms value of the resulting current will be','[{"text": "4.5", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "13.5", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "16.5", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "12.5", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Given $$I=2+5 \\sin \\omega t$$<br>$$\\begin{aligned}& I_{r m s}=\\left[\\dfrac{\\int_{0}^{T} I^{2} d t}{\\int_{0}^{T} d t}\\right] \\\\& =\\left[\\dfrac{1}{T} \\int_{0}^{T}(2+5 \\sin \\omega t)^{2} d t\\right]^{1 / 2} \\\\& =\\left[\\dfrac{1}{T} \\int_{0}^{T}\\left(4+25 \\sin ^{2} \\omega t+20 \\sin \\omega t\\right) d t\\right]^{1 / 2}\\end{aligned}$$<br>But $$\\int_{0}^{T} \\sin \\omega t d t=0$$<br>$$\\begin{aligned}& I_{r m s}=\\dfrac{1}{T} 4 T+\\dfrac{25}{T} \\int_{0}^{T} \\sin ^{2} \\omega t d t \\\\& =4+\\dfrac{25}{T}\\left(\\dfrac{T}{2}\\right)=16.5\\end{aligned}$$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45010,'physics',38,1064,2537,'A parallel plate capacitor has a capacitance $C = 200\\,pF.$ It is connected to $230\\,V$ ac supply with an angular frequency $300\\,rad/s.$ The rms value of conduction current in the circuit and displacement current in the capacitor respectively are','[{"text": "$13.8\\\\,\\\\mu \\\\,A$  and  $138\\\\,\\\\mu \\\\,A$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$14.3\\\\,\\\\mu \\\\,A$  and  $143\\\\,\\\\mu \\\\,A$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$13.8\\\\,\\\\mu \\\\,A$  and  $13.8\\\\,\\\\mu \\\\,A$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$1.38\\\\,\\\\mu \\\\,A$  and  $1.38\\\\,\\\\mu \\\\,A$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'$C = 200\\,pF,\\,\\,\\,\\omega  = 300\\,rad/s,\\,\\,V = 230\\,\\,V$<br><img src=\'/img/data/physics/38_45010_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />  <br>$I=\\dfrac{V}{X_{C}}=\\omega C V=300 \\times 200 \\times 10^{-12} \\times 230$<br>$I = 13.8\\,\\mu \\,A$<br>The magnitude of conduction current and displacement current is same.',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',2024,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45011,'physics',37,973,2266,'An $ac$ source of angular frequency $$\\omega$$ is fed across a resistor $$r$$ and a capacitor $$\\mathrm{C}$$ in series. The current registered is I. If the frequency of source is changed to $$\\omega / 3$$ (maintaining the same voltage), the current in the circuit is found to be halved. Calculate the ratio of reactance to resistance at the original frequency $$\\omega$$','[{"text": "$$\\\\sqrt{\\\\dfrac{4}{5}}$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$\\\\sqrt{\\\\dfrac{2}{5}}$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$\\\\sqrt{\\\\dfrac{3}{5}}$$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$$\\\\sqrt{\\\\dfrac{1}{5}}$$<br>", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'At angular frequency $$\\omega$$, the current in $RC$ circuit is given by<br>${i_{rms}} = \\frac{{{V_{rms}}}}{{\\sqrt {{R^2} + {{\\left( {\\frac{1}{{\\omega C}}} \\right)}^2}} }}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,(1)$<br>Also $\\frac{{{i_{rms}}}}{2} = \\frac{{{V_{rms}}}}{{\\sqrt {{R^2} + {{\\left( {\\frac{1}{{\\frac{\\omega }{3}C}}} \\right)}^2}} }} = \\frac{{{V_{rms}}}}{{\\sqrt {{R^2} + \\frac{9}{{{\\omega ^2}{C^2}}}} }}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,(2)$<br>From equation (1) and (2) we get<br>$$\\begin{aligned}& 3 R^{2}=\\dfrac{5}{\\omega^{2} C^{2}} \\\\& \\Rightarrow \\dfrac{\\dfrac{1}{\\omega C}}{R}=\\sqrt{\\dfrac{3}{5}} \\Rightarrow \\dfrac{X_{C}}{R}=\\sqrt{\\dfrac{3}{5}}\\end{aligned}$$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45014,'physics',37,973,2267,'An LC circuit has $L = 5H$ and $$C=20 \\mu F$$. $$V=5 \\times 10^{-3} \\cos \\omega t$$ is appled. $$\\omega$$ is twice the resonant frequency. Find the maximum charge stored in the capacitor.','[{"text": "$11.3\\\\,nC$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$23.2\\\\,nC$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$33.3\\\\,nC$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$66.6\\\\,nC$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'$\\omega  = 2{\\omega _o} = 2 \\times \\frac{1}{{\\sqrt {LC} }} = 200\\,rad{s^{ - 1}}$<br>${X_L} = \\omega L = 200 \\times 5 = 1000\\Omega $<br>${X_C} = \\frac{1}{{\\omega C}} = \\frac{1}{{200 \\times 20 \\times {{10}^{ - 6}}}} = 250\\Omega $<br>$I = \\frac{{5 \\times {{10}^{ - 3}}}}{{\\sqrt {{{\\left( {{X_L} - {X_C}} \\right)}^2}} }} = \\frac{{5 \\times {{10}^{ - 3}}}}{{\\sqrt {{{(1000 - 250)}^2}} }}$<br>$ = \\frac{{5 \\times {{10}^{ - 3}}}}{{750}} = \\frac{1}{{15}} \\times {10^{ - 4}}\\,A$<br>${V_C} = I{X_C}{\\rm{ and }}Q = C{V_C} = 33.3\\,nC$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/37_45014_question_1.jpg'),
(45015,'physics',38,1065,2539,'If frequency of an electromagnetic wave is $60\\,M\\,Hz$ and it travels in air along $z$ direction then the corresponding electric and magnetic field vectors will be mutually perpendicular to each other and the wavelength of the wave  $({\\rm{in}}\\,\\,m)$  is','[{"text": "2.5", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "5", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "10", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "2", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Frequency,  $f = 60M\\,Hz$<br>Wavelength,  $\\lambda  = \\frac{c}{f} = \\frac{{3 \\times {{10}^8}}}{{60 \\times {{10}^6}}} = 5\\,m$<br>So, correct option is (2).',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2024,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45017,'physics',37,974,2766,'A series LCR circuit with a resistance of $$100 \\sqrt{5} \\Omega$$ is connected to an ac source of  $200\\,\\,V$. When the capacitor is removed from the circuit, current lags behind emf by $$45^{\\circ}$$. When the inductor is removed from the circuit keeping the capacitor and resistor in the circuit, current leads by an angle of $$\\tan ^{-1}\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right)$$. Calculate the power dissipated in LCR circuit.','[{"text": "$64\\\\sqrt 5 \\\\,\\\\,W$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$5\\\\sqrt 5 \\\\,\\\\,W$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$12\\\\sqrt 5 \\\\,\\\\,W$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$5\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\rm{W}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'When capacitor is removed,<br>$\\tan \\phi  = \\frac{{{X_L}}}{R}({\\rm{It}}\\,{\\rm{is}}\\,L - R\\,{\\rm{circuit}})$<br>$\\tan \\phi  = \\tan 45^\\circ {\\rm{ }} = \\frac{{{X_L}}}{R} \\Rightarrow {X_L} = R$<br>When inductor is removed,<br>$$\\tan \\phi=\\dfrac{X_{C}}{R}=\\dfrac{1}{2}=\\dfrac{X_{C}}{R} \\Rightarrow X_{C}=\\dfrac{R}{2}$$<br>Impedance $$Z=\\sqrt{R^{2}+\\left(X_{L}-X_{C}\\right)^{2}}$$<br>$$=\\sqrt{R^{2}+\\left(R-\\dfrac{R}{2}\\right)^{2}}=\\dfrac{\\sqrt{5}}{2} R$$<br>Now, $$I_{r m s}=\\dfrac{V_{r m s}}{Z}=\\dfrac{200}{250}=0.8 \\mathrm{~A}, \\cos \\phi=\\dfrac{R}{Z}=\\dfrac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}$$<br>${P_{av}} = {V_{rms}}{I_{rms}}\\cos \\phi  = 64\\sqrt 5 \\,\\,W$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(46549,'physics',41,408,813,'In a nuclear reactor using $U^{235}$, the power output is $9.6\\,MW$. If energy released per fission of $U^{235}$ is $200\\,MeV$, then the number of fissions per second is ($1\\,eV = 1.6 \\times {10^{ - 19}}J$)','[{"text": "$3 \\\\times 10^{11}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$3 \\\\times 10^{17}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$3 \\\\times 10^{23}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$3 \\\\times 10^{18}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'${n=\\dfrac{9.6 \\times 10^{6}}{200 \\times 10^{6} \\times 1.6 \\times 10^{-19}}=3 \\times 10^{17}}$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45018,'physics',38,1068,2545,'Match the Column I and Column II<br> <br>Choose the correct answer from the options given below :<div class="matching-question"></div>','[{"text": "A – Q,     B – R, \\tC – P, \\tD – S", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "A – P,      B – Q, \\tC – R, \\tD – S", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "A – S,      B – R, \\tC – P, \\tD – Q", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "A – S,      B – P, \\tC – R, \\tD – Q", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'All the mentioned laws and respective equations are given by<br>Ampere - Maxwell law is given by,<br>$\\oint \\vec{B} d \\vec{l}=\\mu_{0} i_{c}+\\mu_{0} \\varepsilon_{0} \\dfrac{d \\phi_{E}}{d t}$<br>Gauss\'s law for electricity is given by, <br>$\\oint \\vec{E} \\cdot d \\vec{A}=\\dfrac{Q}{\\varepsilon_{0}}$<br>Gauss\'s law for magnetism is, $\\oint \\vec{B} \\cdot d \\vec{A}=0$<br>Faraday\'s law is $\\oint \\vec{E} \\cdot d \\vec{l}=\\dfrac{d \\phi_{B}}{d t}$<br>So, correct option is (3).',NULL,'Medium','Match the following',2024,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/38_45018_question_1.png'),
(45021,'physics',37,973,2268,'In the circuit shown in figure neglecting source resistance the voltmeter and ammeter reading will respectively be','[{"text": "$0\\\\,V,\\\\,8\\\\,A$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$150\\\\,V,\\\\,3\\\\,A$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$0\\\\,V,\\\\,3\\\\,A$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$150\\\\,V,\\\\,6\\\\,A$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'The voltage $$V_{L}$$ and $$V_{C}$$ are equal and opposite so voltmeter reading will be zero.<br>Also $$R=30 \\Omega, X_{L}=X_{C}=25 \\Omega$$<br>$ \\Rightarrow i = \\frac{V}{{\\sqrt {{R^2} + {{\\left( {{X_L} - {X_C}} \\right)}^2}} }} = \\frac{V}{R} = \\frac{{240}}{{30}} = 8\\,A$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/37_45021_question_1.jpg'),
(45022,'physics',38,1065,2540,'A plane electromagnetic wave of frequency $35\\,M\\,Hz$ travels in free space along the $x$ - direction.  At a particular point (in space and time)  $\\vec E = 9.6\\,\\hat j\\,\\,V/m.$ The value of magnetic field at this point is','[{"text": "$9.6 \\\\times 10^{-8} \\\\hat{k} T$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$3.2 \\\\times {10^{ - 8}}\\\\,\\\\hat i\\\\,\\\\,T$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$3.2 \\\\times 10^{-8} \\\\hat{k} T$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$9.6\\\\hat j\\\\,T$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Given :  $f = 35M\\,Hz,\\vec E = 9.6\\hat j\\,\\,V/m$<br>Wave is travelling along $x$-axis, $\\vec{n}=\\vec{E} \\times \\vec{B}$<br>So, direction of $\\vec{B}$ is along $\\hat{k}$.<br>$\\vec{B}=\\dfrac{\\vec{E}}{c}=\\dfrac{9.6}{3 \\times 10^{8}}=3.2 \\times 10^{-8} ; \\vec{B}=3.2 \\times 10^{-8} \\hat{k}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2024,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45023,'physics',40,241,480,'The ionisation energy of 10 times ionised sodium atom is','[{"text": "$\\\\frac{{13.6}}{{11}}eV$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{{13.6}}{{{{(11)}^2}}}eV$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$13.6 \\\\times {(11)^2}eV$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$13.6eV$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'${E_n} =  - 13.6\\frac{{{Z^2}}}{{{n^2}}}$<br>Here, $Z = 11$ for $Na$-atom, 10 electrons are removed already, so it is 10 times ionised. For the last electron to be removed,<br>$\\therefore {E_n} =  - \\frac{{13.6{{(11)}^2}}}{{{{(1)}^2}}}eV \\Rightarrow {E_n} =  - 13.6 \\times {(11)^2}eV$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45024,'physics',39,1102,2626,'The work functions of Caesium $(C s)$, Potassium $(K)$ and Sodium $(Na)$ are $2.14\\,eV,2.30\\,eV\\,{\\rm{and }}2.75\\,eV$ respectively. If incident electromagnetic radiation has an incident energy of $2.20\\,eV$, which of these photosensitive surfaces may emit photoelectrons?','[{"text": "Both $Na$ and $K$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$K$ only", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$Na$ only", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$Cs$ only", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Given energy of photon$E = 2.20\\,eV$.<br>Only for $C s, W_{0}=2.14 < E$.<br>$\\Rightarrow$ Only $C s$ emits photoelectrons.<br>Correct option is (4).',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2023,'NEET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45025,'physics',37,975,2276,'In the joining ac circuit the voltmeter whose reading will be zero at resonance is','[{"text": "$$V_{4}$$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$$V_{3}$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$V_{2}$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$V_{1}$$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'At resonance net voltage across $$L$$ and $$C$$ is zero',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/37_45025_question_1.jpg'),
(45026,'physics',38,1065,2540,'The electric field of an electromagnetic wave in free space is represented as $\\vec{E}=E_{0} \\cos (\\omega t-k z) \\hat{i}$. The corresponding magnetic induction vector will be','[{"text": "$\\\\vec{B}=\\\\dfrac{E_{0}}{c} \\\\cos (\\\\omega t-k z) \\\\hat{j}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\vec{B}=E_{0} c \\\\cos (\\\\omega t+k z) \\\\hat{j}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\vec{B}=E_{0} c \\\\cos (\\\\omega t-k z) \\\\hat{j}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\vec{B}=\\\\dfrac{E_{0}}{c} \\\\cos (\\\\omega t+k z) \\\\hat{j}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Given: $\\vec{E}=E_{0} \\cos (\\omega t-k z) \\hat{i}$<br>From the equation of $\\vec{E}$, we can see the direction of $EM$ wave is $+Z$ - direction<br>$\\dfrac{E_{0}}{B_{0}}=c \\Rightarrow B_{0}=\\dfrac{E_{0}}{c}$<br>$\\vec{E} \\times \\vec{B}=\\hat{k} \\Rightarrow \\hat{i} \\times \\vec{B}=\\hat{k} \\Rightarrow \\vec{B}$ is along $+\\hat{j}$<br>$\\therefore \\quad \\vec{B}=\\dfrac{E_{0}}{c} \\cos (\\omega t-k z) \\hat{j}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2024,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45027,'physics',39,1102,2628,'In an experiment to study photo-electric effect the observed variation of stopping potential with frequency of incident radiation is as shown in the figure. The slope and \\(y\\)-intercept are respectively<div class="matching-question"></div>','[{"text": "$\\\\dfrac{h v}{e},-\\\\dfrac{h}{e}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$h v,-h v_{0}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{h}{e},-\\\\dfrac{h v_{0}}{e}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{h v}{e}, v_{0}$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'By Einstein\'s photo electric equation:<br>$e{V_0} = h\\upsilon  - h{\\upsilon _0}$<br>$ \\Rightarrow {V_0} = \\frac{h}{e}\\upsilon  - \\frac{h}{e}{\\upsilon _0}$<br>$\\Rightarrow$ Graph between $\\upsilon $ and $V_{0}$ is straight line shown.<img src=\'/img/data/physics/39_45027_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' /> Its slope$ = \\frac{h}{e},$<br>Its $y - {\\rm{intercept}} = \\frac{{ - h{\\upsilon _0}}}{e}$<br>Correct option is (3).',NULL,'Medium','Graph/Figure',2023,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/39_45027_question_1.png'),
(45028,'physics',40,240,479,'Match the Column-I and Column-II<div class="matching-question"><div class="column-left">A. $n = 5\\,\\,to\\,\\,n = 2$<br>B. $n = 8\\,\\,to\\,\\,n = 4$<br>C. $n = 3\\,\\,to\\,\\,n = 1$<br>D. $n = 4\\,\\,to\\,\\,n = 3$</div><div class="column-right">P. Lyman series<br>Q. Brackett series<br>R. Paschen<br>S. Balmer</div></div>','[{"text": "A-Q, \\tB-R, \\t\\tC-P, \\tD-S", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "A-S, \\tB-Q, \\t\\tC-P, \\tD-R", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "A-R, \\tB-Q, \\t\\tC-S, \\tD-P,S", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "A-P,R, \\tB-S, \\t\\tC-R, \\tD-P", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Option (2) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Match the following',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45030,'physics',38,1065,2541,'If the total energy transferred to a surface in time $t$ is  $6.48 \\times {10^5}\\,J,$ then the magnitude of the total momentum delivered to this surface for complete absorption will be','[{"text": "$1.58 \\\\times {10^{ - 3}}\\\\,kg\\\\,m/s$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$4.32 \\\\times {10^{ - 3}}\\\\,kg\\\\,m/s$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2.16 \\\\times {10^{ - 3}}\\\\,kg\\\\,m/s$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$2.46 \\\\times {10^{ - 3}}\\\\,kg\\\\,m/s$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Total energy transferred to a surface,<br>$E = 6.4 \\times {10^5}\\,J$<br>Total momentum transferred to a surface for absorption is given by<br>$p=\\dfrac{E}{c}$<br>$p = \\frac{{6.48 \\times {{10}^5}\\,J}}{{3 \\times {{10}^8}\\,m{s^{ - 1}}}}$<br>$ \\Rightarrow p = 2.16 \\times {10^{ - 3}}\\,kg\\,m{s^{ - 1}}$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',2024,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45031,'physics',39,1106,2633,'A $60\\,W$ source emits monochromatic light of wavelength $662.5\\,nm$. The number of photons emitted per second is','[{"text": "$5 \\\\times 10^{26}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2 \\\\times 10^{29}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$5 \\\\times 10^{17}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2 \\\\times 10^{20}$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'$E = \\frac{{12400}}{{\\lambda \\left( {\\mathop {{\\rm{ }}A}\\limits^{\\,\\,^\\circ } } \\right)}}{\\rm{where }}E{\\rm{ will be in }}eV.$<br>$\\Rightarrow E($ in $J)=\\dfrac{12400}{6625} \\times 1.6 \\times 10^{-19}$<br>$ \\Rightarrow E \\simeq 32 \\times {10^{ - 19}}\\,J$<br>Denote $N=$ total number of photons.<br>$ \\Rightarrow \\frac{N}{t}{\\rm{ }} = \\frac{P}{E} = \\frac{{60}}{{3 \\times {{10}^{ - 19}}}}$<br>$ = 20 \\times {10^{19}}$<br>$ = 2 \\times {10^{20}}.$<br>$\\Rightarrow$ Number of photons per second.<br>Correct option is (4).',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2023,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45033,'physics',38,1065,2542,'In a plane $E M$ wave, the electric field oscillates sinusoidally at a frequency of $5 \\times {10^{10}}\\,Hz$ and have an amplitude of  $50\\,V{m^{ - 1}}.$ The total average energy density of the electromagnetic field of the wave is [Use  ${\\varepsilon _0} = 8.85 \\times {10^{ - 12}}{C^2}/N{m^2}\\,J\\,]$','[{"text": "$2.212 \\\\times {10^{ - 10}}J\\\\,{m^{ - 3}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$4.425 \\\\times {10^{ - 8}}J\\\\,{m^{ - 3}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1.106 \\\\times {10^{ - 8}}J\\\\,{m^{ - 3}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$2.212 \\\\times {10^{ - 8}}J\\\\,{m^{ - 3}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Given,<br>$f = 5 \\times {10^{10}}\\,Hz,{E_0} = 50\\,V/m$<br>Total average density of $EM$ wave $u_{T}=\\dfrac{1}{2} \\varepsilon_{0}$<br>$E_{0}^{2}=\\dfrac{1}{2} \\dfrac{B_{0}^{2}}{\\mu_{0}}$<br>Here, we will use, $u_{T}=\\dfrac{1}{2} \\varepsilon_{0} E_{0}^{2}$<br>$ = \\frac{1}{2} \\times 8.85 \\times {10^{ - 12}} \\times 2500 = 1.106 \\times {10^{ - 8}}J{m^{ - 3}}$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',2024,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45034,'physics',40,241,480,'As per Bohr model the minimum energy (in $eV$) required to remove the electron from the ground state of doubly ionised lithium $(Z = 3)$ is','[{"text": "$1.51$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$13.6$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$40.8$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$122.4$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Ionsiation energy<br>$ = {Z^2}Rhc\\left( {\\frac{1}{{{{\\left( 1 \\right)}^2}}} - \\frac{1}{{{{\\left( \\infty  \\right)}^2}}}} \\right)$<br>$ = {Z^2}Rhc = {\\left( 3 \\right)^2} \\times 13.6eV = 122.4eV$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45035,'physics',39,1102,2628,'The threshold frequency of a metal with workfunction $6.63\\,\\,eV$ is:','[{"text": "$1.6 \\\\times {10^{12}}\\\\,\\\\,Hz$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$16 \\\\times {10^{12}}\\\\,\\\\,Hz$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$16 \\\\times {10^{15}}\\\\,\\\\,Hz$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1.6 \\\\times {10^{15}}\\\\,\\\\,Hz$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Using the basic concept of photoelectric effect, we can solve the given question<br>$\\phi  = 6.63\\,\\,eV$<br>$h v_{t h}=\\phi$<br>$v_{\\text {th }}=\\dfrac{6.63 \\times 1.6 \\times 10^{-19}}{6.63 \\times 10^{-34}} H z$<br>${v_{th{\\rm{ }}}} = 1.6 \\times {10^{15}}\\,Hz$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2024,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45037,'physics',38,1065,2542,'Read the Statement - A and Statement - B  carefully to mark the correct options given 	below<div class="matching-question"><div class="column-left">Electromagnetic waves carry energy as they travel through space and this energy is equally shared by the electric and magnetic fields.</div><div class="column-right">When electromagnetic waves strike a surface, a pressure is exerted on the surface.</div></div>','[{"text": "Statement A is correct but statement B is \\t  incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Statement A is incorrect but statement B is \\t  correct.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both statements are correct.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both statements are incorrect", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Electromagnetic waves carry energy when travel through space and this energy is equal divided by electric field vectors and magnetic field vectors. Electromagnetic waves are  composed of photons which have momentum and energy when photon is incident on a surface, its momentum changes and hence it exerts force on the surface giving rise to pressure. So, correct option is (3).',NULL,'Medium','MCQ type-2',2024,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45038,'physics',40,240,479,'Figure represents in simplified form of some  lower energy levels of the hydrogen atom. If the transition of an electron from ${E_1}\\,\\,{\\rm{to}}\\,\\,{E_2}$ were associated with the emision of blue light, which one of the following transitions could be associated with the emission of red light?','[{"text": "${E_4}\\\\,to\\\\,{E_1}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${E_3}\\\\,to\\\\,{E_1}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${E_3}\\\\,to\\\\,{E_2}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${E_1}\\\\,to\\\\,{E_3}$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Blue light falls in the Balmer series and it is obtained when the atom makes a transition from ${E_4}\\,to\\,{E_3}$. Red light also falls in the Balmer series and it has a lower frequency compared to blue light. By quantum theory of radiation, $E = hf$. Thus, red light is associated with a smaller energy change from a lower energy level (compared to ${E_4}$) to the first excited state ${E_2}$. Hence, the only possible transition that results in the emission of red light is the ${E_3}\\,to\\,{E_2}$ transition.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/40_45038_question_1.png'),
(45039,'physics',39,1102,2628,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> If work function of a material increases by some mechanism, it requires greater energy to excite the electrons from its surface.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> A plot of stopping potential $\\left(\\mathrm{V}_{0}\\right)$ versus frequency $(f)$ for different metals, has greater slope for metals with greater work functions.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Einstein equation for photoelectric effect is:<br>$h{f_{incident{\\rm{ }}}} = {W_0} + \\frac{1}{2}{m_e}v_e^2$<br>If value of $W_{0}$ increases, the incident photons hall have more $\\left( {h{f_{incident}}} \\right)$ so as to eject electron. They have same slope but intercepts are different. <img src=\'/img/data/physics/39_45039_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' /> But, the plots have Slope $=\\dfrac{h}{e}=$ constant<br>So correct option is (3).',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45041,'physics',38,1066,2543,'Arrange the following in the ascending order of wavelength.<br>(I) Gamma rays $\\left(\\lambda_{1}\\right)$<br>(II) $X$ - rays $\\left(\\lambda_{2}\\right)$<br>(III) Infrared waves $\\left(\\lambda_{3}\\right)$<br>(IV) Microwaves $\\left(\\lambda_{4}\\right)$<br>Choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:','[{"text": "$\\\\lambda_{4} < \\\\lambda_{3} < \\\\lambda_{2} < \\\\lambda_{1}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\lambda_{4} < \\\\lambda_{3} < \\\\lambda_{1} < \\\\lambda_{2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\lambda_{2} < \\\\lambda_{1} < \\\\lambda_{4} < \\\\lambda_{3}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\lambda _1} < {\\\\lambda _2} < {\\\\lambda _3} < {\\\\lambda _4}$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'$EM$ waves in the ascending order of wavelength is Gamma rays  $\\left( {{\\lambda _1}} \\right),$  $X$ - rays $\\left(\\lambda_{2}\\right)$, Infrared rays $\\left(\\lambda_{3}\\right)$, Microwaves $\\left(\\lambda_{4}\\right)$. So, correct option is (4).',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2024,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45042,'physics',39,1102,2626,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> When a certain wavelength of light falls on a soft metal surface, it ejects electrons.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Light has wave nature.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Photon behaves like particle in photoelectric effect, but is electromagnetic wave in light. So correct option is (2).',NULL,'Easy','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL);
INSERT INTO `qb_questions` (`id`, `subject_id`, `chapter_id`, `topic_id`, `subtopic_id`, `question_html`, `options`, `correct_index`, `explanation`, `explanation_image_url`, `difficulty`, `qtype`, `year`, `tag`, `created_at`, `question_image_url`) VALUES
(45044,'physics',40,240,479,'In figure the energy levels of the hydrogen atom have been shown along with some transitions marking $A,B,C$. The transitions $A,B$ and $C$ respectively represents:','[{"text": "the first member of the Lyman series, thrid line of Balmer series and second line of  Paschen series.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "the ionisation potential of $H$, second line of Balmer series and third line of Paschen series.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "the series limit of Lyman series, second line of Balmer series and second  of line Paschen series.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "the series limit of Lyman series, third line of Balmer series and second line of \\tPaschen series.", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'The transition mark, $A$ represents series limit of Lyman series, $B$ represents third member of Balmer series and $C$ represents second member of Paschen series.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/40_45044_question_1.png'),
(45045,'physics',38,1066,2543,'Match the Column I and Column II.<br> <br>Choose the correct answer from the options given below :<div class="matching-question"></div>','[{"text": "A – P,     B – R, \\tC – Q, \\tD – S", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "A – Q,    B – P,  \\tC – S, \\tD – R", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "A – R,    B – Q, \\tC – S, \\tD – P", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "A – S,     B – R, \\tC – Q, \\tD – P", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'The correct sequence of $EM$ wave and their respective wavelength range is given by<br>  A – R,    B – Q, 	C – S, 	D – P',NULL,'Hard','Match the following',2024,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/38_45045_question_1.png'),
(45046,'physics',39,1103,2630,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> An electron microscope is based on de Broglie hypothesis.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> A beam of electrons behaves as a wave which can be converged by electric and magnetic lenses.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Electron microscopes, based on in de Broglie\'s wavelength concept, utilize short electron wavelengths for high-resolution imaging. Their superiority over optical microscopes lies in the ability to resolve atomic and molecular details. So correct option is (1).',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45048,'physics',40,240,479,'In hydrogen atom, electron makes transition from $n = 4$ to $n = 1$ level. Recoil momentum of the $H$ atom will be','[{"text": "$3.4 \\\\times {10^{ - 27}}N - \\\\sec $", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$6.8 \\\\times {10^{ - 27}}N - \\\\sec $", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$3.4 \\\\times {10^{ - 24}}N - \\\\sec $", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$6.8 \\\\times {10^{ - 24}}N - \\\\sec $", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Recoil momentum $ = $ Momentum of photon<br>$ = \\frac{h}{\\lambda } = hR\\left( {\\frac{1}{{n_1^2}} - \\frac{1}{{n_2^2}}} \\right)$<br>$ = \\frac{{hR \\times 15}}{{16}} = 6.8 \\times {10^{ - 27}}N \\times \\sec $',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45049,'physics',38,1065,2541,'A totally reflecting, small plane mirror placed horizontally faces a parallel beam of light as shown in the figure. The mass of the mirror is $20\\,g$. Assume that there is no absorption in the lens and that ${30 \\%}$ of the light emitted by the source goes through the lens. The power of the source  needed to support the weight of the mirror is','[{"text": "$80\\\\,MW$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$100\\\\,MW$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$130\\\\,MW$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$150\\\\,MW$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'As ${30 \\%}$ light is incident and reflected by mirror, force due to reflection of light on mirror is<br>$F = 2 \\times \\frac{{0.3P}}{c} = \\frac{{0.6\\,P}}{c}$<br>To support the weight of mirror, we use ${\\dfrac{0.6 P}{c}=m g}$<br>$ \\Rightarrow \\,\\,\\,{\\mkern 1mu} {\\kern 1pt} P = \\frac{{mgc}}{{0.6}}$<br>$ = \\frac{{20 \\times {{10}^{ - 3}} \\times 10 \\times 3 \\times {{10}^8}}}{{0.6}} = {10^8}{\\rm{ watt }}$<br>$=100\\,MW$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/38_45049_question_1.png'),
(45050,'physics',39,1104,2631,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Both interference and diffraction shown by electrons are due to concept of matter wave.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Electrons moving at proper velocities have their matter waves associated with themselves.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Matter waves, introduced by de Broglie, describe the dual nature of particles, explaining quantum phenomenae. So correct option is (1).',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(46550,'physics',42,323,591,'In a $p$-$n$ junction diode, change in temperature due to heating','[{"text": "Does not affect resistance of $p$-$n$ junction", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Affects the overall $V$ - $I$ characteristics of $p$-$n$ junction", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Affects only forward resistance", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Affects only reverse resistance", "isCorrect": false}]',1,NULL,NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2018,'NEET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45052,'physics',38,1065,2541,'A plate of mass 10 $g$ is in equilibrium in air due to the force exerted by a light beam on the plate. Calculate power of the beam (in $MW$). Assume that the plate is perfectly absorbing.','[{"text": "$30\\\\,MW$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$50\\\\,MW$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$10\\\\,MW$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$40\\\\,MW$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'For equilibrium, the force exerted by the light beam should balance the weight of plate.<br>${F_{{\\rm{photon }}}} = mg$<br>$\\left. {\\left( {{F_{{\\text{photon}}}} = \\frac{{IA}}{c} = \\frac{P}{c}} \\right.,{\\text{where}}\\,\\,{\\text{power}}\\,\\,P = IA} \\right)$<br>$ \\Rightarrow \\frac{P}{c} = 10 \\times {10^{ - 3}} \\times 10$<br>$ \\Rightarrow P = 30 \\times {10^6}\\,W = 30\\,\\,MW$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/38_45052_question_1.png'),
(45053,'physics',40,240,479,'Maximum energy is released during which of the following transitions?','[{"text": "$n = 1\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\rm{to}}\\\\,\\\\,n = 2$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$n = 2\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\rm{to}}\\\\,\\\\,n = 6$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$n = 2\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\rm{to}}\\\\,\\\\,n = 1$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$n = 6\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\rm{to}}\\\\,\\\\,n = 2$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'When transition is from upper state to 	lower state, the energy is evolved. <br>In option (3),<br>$E = Rch\\left( {\\frac{1}{{{1^2}}} - \\frac{1}{{{2^2}}}} \\right) = \\frac{3}{4}Rch$<br>In option (4),$E = Rch\\left( {\\frac{1}{{{2^2}}} - \\frac{1}{{{6^2}}}} \\right) = \\frac{2}{9}Rch$<br>Thus, maximum energy is evolved in option (3), When transition takes place between $n = 2$ to $n = 1$.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45055,'physics',39,1102,2627,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> When ultraviolet light is incident on a photocell, its stopping potential is $V_{0}$ and the maximum kinetic energy of the photoelectrons is $E_{k \\text { max }}$. When the ultraviolet light is replaced by $X$-rays, both $V_{0}$ and $E_{k \\text { max }}$ increase.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Photoelectrons are emitted with fixed kinetic energy which is equal to $h v-W$.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'According to Einstein\'s photoelectric equation, increasing from ultraviolet light to $X$ rays raises both the maximum kinetic energy $E_{k \\text { max }}$ and so stopping potential $\\left(V_{0}\\right)$ of emitted photoelectrons due to higher energy X-ray photons. Note that $X$-rays have more frequency and have more energy than $U V$. The emitted Photoelectrons will have kinetic energy ranging from 0 to maximum value. So correct option is (3).',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45057,'physics',40,241,480,'Energy levels $A,B$ and $C$ of a certain atom correspond to increasing values of energy, i.e.,${E_A} < {E_B} < {E_C}.$  If  ${\\lambda _1},{\\lambda _2}$ and ${\\lambda _3}$ are wavelength corresponding to  transitions $C$ to $B,B$ to $A$ and $C$ to $A$ respectively then','[{"text": "${\\\\lambda _3} = \\\\frac{{{\\\\lambda _1}{\\\\lambda _2}}}{{{\\\\lambda _1} + {\\\\lambda _2}}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${\\\\lambda _3} = {\\\\lambda _1} + {\\\\lambda _2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\lambda _3^2 = \\\\lambda _1^2 + \\\\lambda _2^2$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\lambda _1} + {\\\\lambda _2} + {\\\\lambda _3}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'${E_C} - {E_A} = {E_C} - {E_B} + {E_B} - {E_A}$  (From fig).<br>$\\frac{{hc}}{{{\\lambda _3}}} = \\frac{{hc}}{{{\\lambda _1}}} + \\frac{{hc}}{{{\\lambda _2}}} \\Rightarrow \\frac{1}{{{\\lambda _3}}} = \\frac{1}{{{\\lambda _1}}} + \\frac{1}{{{\\lambda _2}}}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/40_45057_question_1.png'),
(45058,'physics',37,972,2260,'The current vs time graph is shown in the figure. The average and rms currents are. (for one cycle)<div class="matching-question"></div>','[{"text": "$$0, \\\\dfrac{i_{0}}{2}$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$0, \\\\dfrac{i_{0}}{\\\\sqrt{2}}$$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$$\\\\dfrac{i_{0}}{2} \\\\dfrac{i_{0}}{\\\\sqrt{2}}$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$i_{0}, \\\\dfrac{i_{0}}{\\\\sqrt{2}}$$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'$$i_{\\text {avg }}=\\dfrac{\\int_{0}^{T} i d t}{T}=\\dfrac{1}{T}\\left[\\int_{0}^{T} i_{0} \\sin \\omega t d t\\right]=\\dfrac{-1}{\\omega T}[\\cos \\omega t]_{0}^{T}$$<br>$$i_{\\text {avg }}=0$$<br>$$\\begin{aligned}& \\left\\langle i^{2}\\right\\rangle=\\dfrac{\\int_{0}^{T} i^{2} d t}{T}=\\dfrac{1}{T}\\left[\\int_{0}^{T} i_{0}^{2} \\sin ^{2}(\\omega t) d t\\right] \\\\& =\\dfrac{i_{0}^{2}}{T} \\int_{0}^{T} \\sin ^{2}(\\omega t) d t\\end{aligned}$$<br>$\\left\\langle {{i^2}} \\right\\rangle  = \\frac{{i_0^2}}{{2T}}\\int_0^T {\\left[ {\\frac{{1 - \\cos (2\\omega t)}}{2}} \\right]} dt$<br>$ = \\frac{{i_0^2}}{{2T}}\\int_0^T {\\left[ {1 - \\cos (2\\omega t)} \\right]} dt$<br>$\\left\\langle {{i^2}} \\right\\rangle  = \\frac{{i_0^2}}{2} = {i_{rms}} = \\frac{{{i_0}}}{{\\sqrt 2 }}$',NULL,'Hard','Graph/Figure',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/37_45058_question_1.png'),
(45059,'physics',39,1103,2630,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> The de Broglie equation has significance for any microscopic particle.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> The de Broglie wavelength is inversely proportional to the mass of the object if velocity is constant.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'$\\lambda=\\dfrac{h}{m v}$. Here $\\mathrm{h}$ indicates quantum or microscopic nature. $\\lambda$ is appreciable only if $m$ is low. The de Broglie equation extends wave particle duality to all microscopic particles, predicting their associated wavelengths based on momentum. It plays a crucial role in explaining quantum effects, facilitating techno-logical applications in diffraction, interference, and high precision measurements. The deBroglie equation is a fundamental concept in quantum mechanics. So correct option is (1) .',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45069,'physics',37,972,2260,'The voltage time \\[(V - t)\\] graph for triangular wave having peak value $$V_{o}$$ is as shown in figure.<br>The rms value of \\[V\\] in time interval from \\[t = 0\\] to \\[T/4\\] is<div class="matching-question"></div>','[{"text": "$$\\\\dfrac{V_{o}}{\\\\sqrt{3}}$$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$$\\\\dfrac{V_{o}}{2}$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$\\\\dfrac{V_{o}}{\\\\sqrt{2}}$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "None of these", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'The equation of the line from 0 to $$\\dfrac{T}{4}$$ is<br>$$\\begin{gathered}V=\\dfrac{V_{o}}{T / 4} t \\Rightarrow V=\\dfrac{4 V_{o}}{T} t \\\\V_{r m s}=\\sqrt{ < V^{2}>} \\\\ < V^{2}>=\\left(\\dfrac{4 V_{o}}{T}\\right)^{2}\\left\\{\\dfrac{\\int_{0}^{T / 4} t^{2} d t}{\\int_{0}^{T / 4} d t}\\right\\} \\Rightarrow V_{r m s}=\\dfrac{V_{o}}{\\sqrt{3}}\\end{gathered}$$',NULL,'Hard','Graph/Figure',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/37_45069_question_1.png'),
(48378,'biology',101,574,1153,'Which one is a sterilization method?','[{"text": "Diaphragm", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Loop", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Tubectomy", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Rhythm method", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Tubectomy is a sterilization method for females.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45061,'physics',37,972,2260,'The current vs time is shown in the graph. The given graph is half wave rectified. Find the rms and average currents.<div class="matching-question"></div>','[{"text": "$$\\\\dfrac{i_{0}}{2}, \\\\dfrac{i_{0}}{\\\\pi}$$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$$i_{0}, i_{0}$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$\\\\dfrac{i_{0}}{\\\\pi}, \\\\dfrac{i_{0}}{4}$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$\\\\dfrac{i_{0}}{\\\\pi}, \\\\dfrac{i_{0}}{\\\\pi}$$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'${i_{avg{\\rm{ }}}} = \\frac{{\\int_0^{2\\pi /\\omega } i dt}}{{\\left( {\\frac{{2\\pi }}{\\omega }} \\right)}}$<br>$ = \\frac{\\omega }{{2\\pi }}\\left[ {\\int_0^{\\pi /\\omega } {{i_0}} \\sin (\\omega t)dt + \\int_{\\pi /\\omega }^{2\\pi /\\omega } {(0)} dt} \\right]$<br>${i_{avg{\\rm{ }}}} = \\frac{{{i_0}}}{{2\\pi }}[ - \\cos \\omega t]_0^\\pi  =  - \\frac{{{i_0}}}{{2\\pi }}\\left[ {\\cos \\left( {\\omega \\frac{\\pi }{\\omega }} \\right) - \\cos 0} \\right]$<br>${i_{avg}} = \\frac{{2{i_0}}}{{2\\pi }} = \\frac{{{i_0}}}{\\pi }$<br>$\\left\\langle {{i^2}} \\right\\rangle  = \\frac{\\omega }{{2\\pi }}\\int_0^{2\\pi /\\omega } {{i^2}} dt$<br>$ = \\frac{\\omega }{{2\\pi }}\\left[ {\\int_0^{\\pi /\\omega } {i_0^2} {{\\sin }^2}(\\omega t)dt + \\int_{\\pi /\\omega }^{2\\pi /\\omega } {(0)} dt} \\right]$<br>$\\left\\langle {{i^2}} \\right\\rangle  = \\frac{{\\omega i_0^2}}{{2\\pi }}\\left[ {\\int_0^{\\pi /\\omega } {{{\\sin }^2}} (\\omega t)dt} \\right]$<br>$\\int_0^{\\pi /\\omega } {{{\\sin }^2}} (\\omega t)dt = \\frac{1}{2}\\int_0^{\\pi /\\omega } {(1 - \\cos (} 2\\omega t))dt = \\frac{\\pi }{{2\\omega }}$<br>$ \\Rightarrow \\left\\langle {{i^2}} \\right\\rangle  = \\frac{{\\omega i_0^2}}{{2\\pi }} \\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{{2\\omega }} = \\frac{{i_o^2}}{4} \\Rightarrow {i_{rms}} = \\frac{{{i_0}}}{2}$',NULL,'Hard','Graph/Figure',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/37_45061_question_1.png'),
(45062,'physics',39,1103,2630,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> A photon has no rest mass, yet it carries definite momentum.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Momentum of photon is due to its energy and hence its equivalent mass.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'$m_{0}=0$ for photon. But $m=\\dfrac{h}{\\lambda v}$<br>We also know $p=m v$<br>$E=h v=\\dfrac{h c}{\\lambda}$<br>$\\Rightarrow p=\\dfrac{E}{c}$<br>So correct option is (1).',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45063,'physics',40,240,479,'If ${\\lambda _1}\\,and\\,{\\lambda _2}$ are the wavelength of the Second member of the Lyman and Paschen series respectively, then ${\\lambda _1}\\,:{\\lambda _2}$ is','[{"text": "$1:3$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1:30$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$7:50$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$18:225$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'For second line of Lyman series,${n_1} = 1$ and ${n_2} = 3$<br>$\\therefore \\quad \\frac{1}{{{\\lambda _1}}} = R\\left( {\\frac{1}{{{1^2}}} - \\frac{1}{{{3^2}}}} \\right) = R\\left( {1 - \\frac{1}{9}} \\right) = \\frac{{8R}}{9}$<br>For second line of paschen series, ${n_1} = 3$ and ${n_2} = 5$<br>$\\therefore \\quad \\frac{1}{{{\\lambda _2}}} = R\\left( {\\frac{1}{{{3^2}}} - \\frac{1}{{{5^2}}}} \\right) = R\\left( {\\frac{1}{9} - \\frac{1}{{25}}} \\right) = \\frac{{16R}}{{225}}$<br>$\\therefore \\quad \\frac{{{\\lambda _1}}}{{{\\lambda _2}}} = \\frac{{16R}}{{225}} \\times \\frac{9}{{8R}} = \\frac{{18}}{{225}}$<br>${\\lambda _1}:{\\lambda _2} = 18:225$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45065,'physics',37,973,2268,'An \\[AC\\] source of variable frequency \\[f\\] is connected to \\[LCR\\] series circuit. Which one of the graphs in figure represents the variation of current \\[I\\] in the circuit with frequency \\[f\\]?<div class="matching-question"></div>','[{"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}]',2,NULL,NULL,'Hard','Graph/Figure',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45066,'physics',39,1102,2628,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Stopping potential depends upon the frequency of incident light but is independent of the intensity of the light.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> The maximum kinetic energy of the photoelectrons depends on stopping potential.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Einstein gave $h f=h f_{0}+\\dfrac{1}{2} m v_{\\text {max }}^{2}$<br>Denote: $E_{k \\max }=\\dfrac{1}{2} m v_{\\max }^{2}$<br>The stopping potential $\\left(V_{0}\\right)$ serves as a measure of the maximum kinetic energy of emitted photoelectrons, and it is typically measured in electronvolts $\\left( {eV} \\right)$. The value of $E_{k \\text { max }}$ is contingent upon the frequency of the incident light but remains independent of its intensity. So correct option is (2).',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45067,'physics',40,240,479,'Given the longest wavelength in Lyman series 	as  $1240A^\\circ $, the highest frequency emitted in Balmer series is','[{"text": "$8 \\\\times {10^{14}}Hz$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$8 \\\\times {10^{12}}Hz$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$8 \\\\times {10^{10}}Hz$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$8 \\\\times {10^3}Hz$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'The wavelength of different spectral lines of Lyman series is given by<br>$\\frac{1}{\\lambda } = R\\left[ {\\frac{1}{{{1^2}}} - \\frac{1}{{n_2^2}}} \\right]$ where ${n_2} = 2,3,4,....$<br>For longest wavelength of Lyman series ${n_2} = 2$<br>$\\therefore \\frac{1}{{{\\lambda _L}}} = R\\left[ {\\frac{1}{{{1^2}}} - \\frac{1}{{{2^2}}}} \\right] = R\\left[ {\\frac{3}{4}} \\right]$<br>Highest frequency of Balmer series correspond to the smallest wavelength of Balmer series. The wavelength of different spectral lines of Balmer series is given by<br>$\\frac{1}{\\lambda } = R\\left[ {\\frac{1}{{{2^2}}} - \\frac{1}{{n_2^2}}} \\right]{\\rm{where}}\\,\\,{n_2} = 3,4,5,...$<br>For smallest wavelength of Balmer series<br>${n_2} = \\infty $<br>$\\therefore \\frac{1}{{{\\lambda _S}}} = R\\left[ {\\frac{1}{{{2^2}}} - \\frac{1}{{{\\infty ^2}}}} \\right] = \\frac{R}{4}$<br>$\\therefore \\frac{{{\\lambda _S}}}{{{\\lambda _L}}} = \\frac{{3R}}{4} \\times \\frac{4}{R} = 3$<br>or ${\\lambda _S} = 3{\\lambda _L} = 3 \\times 1240A^\\circ $<br>Highest frequency emitted in Balmer series is<br>${v_H} = \\frac{c}{{{\\lambda _S}}} = \\frac{{3 \\times {{10}^8}}}{{3 \\times 1240 \\times {{10}^{ - 10}}}} = 8 \\times {10^{14}}Hz$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45084,'physics',40,242,481,'The de-Broglie wavelength of an electron in the ground state of the hydrogen atom is:','[{"text": "$\\\\pi {r^2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2\\\\pi r$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\pi r$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\sqrt {2\\\\pi r} $", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'According to de-Broglie’s  theory,<br>$2\\pi r = n\\lambda $ For, $n = 1,\\,\\,\\,\\lambda  = 2\\pi r$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(48379,'biology',101,574,1153,'Which is related to males?','[{"text": "Oral pill", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Tubectomy", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Vasectomy", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "None of these", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Vasectomy is a sterilization method for males.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45070,'physics',39,1102,2626,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Work function of different metals is different.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Work function is the minimum energy required by an electron to just escape from the metal surface.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'The work function $W_{0}$ of metals varies based on factors such as electronic band structure, atomic arrangement, surface conditions, density of states, and electron affinity. Work function is the minimum energy required by an electron to just escape from metal surface. So correct option is (2).',NULL,'Easy','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45071,'physics',37,973,2269,'When an \\[AC\\] source of emf $$e=e_{o} \\sin (100 t)$$ is connected across a circuit, the phase difference between the emf e and the current $$i$$ in the circuit is observed to be $$\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}$$, as shown in the diagram. If the circuit consists of \\[R - C\\] or \\[R - L\\] or \\[L - C\\] in series, find the relationship between the two elements.<div class="matching-question"></div>','[{"text": "$R = 1\\\\,k\\\\Omega ,C = 10\\\\,\\\\mu F$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$R=1 k \\\\Omega, C=1 \\\\mu F$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$R = 1\\\\,k\\\\Omega ,L = 10H$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$$R=1 k \\\\Omega, L=1 H$$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'As the current $$i$$ lags the emf $e$ by $$\\pi / 4$$, it is an $L - R$ circuit.<br>$\\quad \\tan \\phi  = \\frac{{{X_L}}}{R}{\\rm{ or }}\\tan \\frac{\\pi }{4} = \\frac{{\\omega L}}{R}$<br>$\\therefore R = \\omega L$<br>${\\rm{As }}\\quad \\omega  = 100rad/s{\\rm{ }}$<br>${\\rm{If}}\\quad L = 10H\\,{\\rm{then}}\\,R = 1\\,K\\Omega $',NULL,'Hard','Graph/Figure',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/37_45071_question_1.jpg'),
(45072,'physics',39,1103,2630,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> The de-Broglie wavelength of particle having kinetic energy $E_{k}$ is $\\lambda$.If its kinetic energy becomes $4 E_{k}$ then its new wavelength would be $\\lambda / 2$.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> The de-Broglie wavelength is inversely proportional to square root of the kinetic energy.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'$\\lambda=\\dfrac{h}{p}=\\sqrt{2 m E_{k}}$<br>$\\Rightarrow$ if $E_{k}^{\\prime}=4 E_{k}$ then $\\lambda^{\\prime}=\\left(\\dfrac{\\lambda}{2}\\right)$<br>So correct option is (1).',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45073,'physics',40,240,479,'An electron jumps from the 4$th$ orbit to 2$nd$ orbit of the hydrogen atom. Give, Rydberg’s constant $R = {10^7}{m^{ - 1}}$, the frequency in hertz of emitted radiation will be','[{"text": "$\\\\frac{3}{{16}} \\\\times {10^5}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{3}{{16}} \\\\times {10^{15}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{9}{{16}} \\\\times {10^{15}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{3}{4} \\\\times {10^{15}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'We know that, frequency <br>$v = \\frac{c}{\\lambda } = c.R\\left( {\\frac{1}{{n_1^2}} - \\frac{1}{{n_2^2}}} \\right)$<br>$ = 3 \\times {10^8} \\times {10^7}\\left( {\\frac{1}{{{2^2}}} - \\frac{1}{{{4^2}}}} \\right) = \\frac{9}{{16}} \\times {10^{15}}Hz$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45074,'physics',37,976,2280,'In the circuit shown in the figure the rms currents $$I_{1}, I_{2}$$, and $$I_{3}$$ are altered by varying the frequency fof the oscillator.<br>The output voltage of the oscillator remains sinusoidal and has a fixed amplitude.<br>Which curves in figure correctly indicate the variation with frequency of the currents $$I_{1}, I_{2}$$, and $$I_{3}$$ ?<div class="matching-question"></div>','[{"text": "$$Q \\\\quad Q \\\\quad Q$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$R \\\\quad Q \\\\quad Q$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$Q \\\\quad P \\\\quad R$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$Q \\\\quad R \\\\quad P$$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Reactance of the inductor $O$ is given by $$X_{L}=2 \\pi f L$$.<br>Therefore, rms current through inductor $L$ is $$I_{2}$$ $$I_{2}=\\dfrac{V}{2 \\pi f L} \\propto \\dfrac{1}{f}$$ where $V$ is the rms of the supply voltage.<br>Reactance of the capacitor $C$ is given by<br>$$X_{C}=\\dfrac{1}{2 \\pi f C}$$<br>Therefore, rms current through the capacitor is<br>$$I_{3}=\\dfrac{V}{X_{C}}=2 \\pi f C V \\propto f$$<br>From the phasor diagram the two currents are opposite in phase<br><img src=\'/img/data/physics/37_45074_explanation_1.jpg\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' /> <br>The two currents get subtracted.<br>The total current $$=\\left(\\dfrac{V}{2 \\pi f L}-2 \\pi f C V\\right)$$<br>Thus, $$I_{2}$$ is the best represented by curve $R$.<br>$$I_{3}$$ is best represented by curve $P$.<br>$$I_{1}$$ is best represented by curve $Q.$',NULL,'Hard','Graph/Figure',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/37_45074_question_1.jpg'),
(45075,'physics',39,1102,2626,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> The relative velocity of two photons travelling in opposite direction is the velocity of light.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> The rest mass of a photon is zero.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Photons have zero rest mass and always travel at the speed of light. No material body can exceed this speed. So, if two photons move in opposite directions, their relative velocity to each other remains the speed of light. So correct option is (2).',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45076,'physics',40,240,479,'The spectral series of the hydrogen spectrum that lies in the ultraviolet region is the','[{"text": "P fund series", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Balmer series", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Lyman series", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Paschen series", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Lyman series lies in the $UV$ region.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45085,'physics',37,973,2263,'In pure inductive circuit, the curves between frequency \\[f\\] and and reciprocal of inductive reactance $$1 / X_{L}$$ is<div class="matching-question"></div>','[{"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'$$X_{L}=2 \\pi f L \\Rightarrow X_{L} \\propto f \\Rightarrow \\dfrac{f}{X_{L}}=$$ constant',NULL,'Hard','Graph/Figure',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(48380,'biology',101,574,1153,'Which of the following method of contraception has minimum failure rate?','[{"text": "IUD", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Oral pill", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Cervical cap", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Vasectomy", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Vasectomy can fail if the doctor misses the vas deferens during the procedure, hence it has minimum failure rate.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45077,'physics',37,972,2260,'The average and effective values for the wave shape shown in the figure are:<div class="matching-question"></div>','[{"text": "$$\\\\dfrac{2}{\\\\pi} V_{m}$$ and $$\\\\dfrac{V_{m}}{2}$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$\\\\dfrac{V_{m}}{\\\\pi}$$ and $$\\\\dfrac{V_{m}}{\\\\sqrt{2}}$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$\\\\dfrac{2}{\\\\pi} V_{m}$$ and $$\\\\dfrac{V_{m}}{\\\\sqrt{2}}$$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$$\\\\dfrac{V_{m}}{\\\\pi \\\\sqrt{2}}$$ and $$\\\\dfrac{V_{m}}{\\\\sqrt{2}}$$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'The equation of the graph from 0 to $$\\pi$$ is $$V=V_{m} \\sin \\theta$$<br>The average value is ${V_{avg{\\rm{ }}}} = \\frac{{\\int_0^\\pi  V d\\theta }}{\\pi }$<br>$$\\begin{aligned}& V_{\\text {avg }}=\\dfrac{V_{m}}{\\pi} \\int_{0}^{\\pi} \\sin \\theta d \\theta=\\dfrac{2 V_{m}}{\\pi} \\\\& V_{\\text {eff }}^{2}=\\dfrac{V_{m}^{2}}{\\pi} \\int_{0}^{\\pi} \\sin ^{2} \\theta d \\theta=\\dfrac{V_{m}^{2}}{2} \\\\& V_{\\text {eff }}=V_{m} / \\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}$$',NULL,'Hard','Graph/Figure',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/37_45077_question_1.jpg'),
(45078,'physics',39,1102,2626,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> The threshold frequency of photoelectric effect supports the particle nature of light.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> If frequency of incident light is iess than the threshold frequency, electrons are emitted from metal surface.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'In the photoelectric effect, photoelectrons are emitted only when the frequency of incident light exceeds a specific threshold, irrespective of light intensity. It is as per Einstein equation $h v=h v_{0}+\\dfrac{1}{2} m v_{\\max }^{2}$. So correct option is (3).',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45079,'physics',37,972,2260,'If a direct current of value a ampere is superimposed on an alternative current $$I=b \\sin \\omega t$$ flowing through a wire, what is the effective value of the resulting current in the circuit?<div class="matching-question"></div>','[{"text": "$$\\\\left[a^{2}-\\\\dfrac{1}{2} b^{2}\\\\right]^{1 / 2}$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$\\\\left[a^{2}+b^{2}\\\\right]^{1 / 2}$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$\\\\left[\\\\dfrac{a^{2}}{2}+b^{2}\\\\right]^{1 / 2}$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$\\\\left[a^{2}+\\\\dfrac{1}{2} b^{2}\\\\right]^{1 / 2}$$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'As current at any instant in the circuit is<br>$$\\begin{gathered}I=I_{d c}+I_{a c}=a+b \\sin \\omega t \\\\I_{e f f}=\\left[\\dfrac{\\int_{0}^{T} I^{2} d t}{\\int_{0}^{T} d t}\\right]^{1 / 2}=\\left[\\dfrac{1}{T} \\int_{0}^{T}(a+b \\sin \\omega t)^{2} d t\\right]^{1 / 2}\\end{gathered}$$<br>i.e., $$I_{e f f}=\\left[\\dfrac{1}{T} \\int_{0}^{T}\\left(a^{2}+2 a b \\sin \\omega t+b^{2} \\sin ^{2} \\omega t\\right) d t\\right]^{1 / 2}$$<br>but as $$\\dfrac{1}{T} \\int_{0}^{T} \\sin \\omega t d t=0$$ and $$\\dfrac{1}{T} \\int_{0}^{T} \\sin ^{2} \\omega t d t=\\dfrac{1}{2}$$<br>So, $$I_{e f f}=\\left[a^{2}+\\dfrac{1}{2} b^{2}\\right]^{1 / 2}$$',NULL,'Hard','Graph/Figure',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/37_45079_question_1.jpg'),
(45080,'physics',40,240,479,'In terms of Rydberg’s constant  $R$ the wave number of the first Balmer line is','[{"text": "$3R$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$R$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{{8R}}{9}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{{5R}}{{36}}$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Wave number $ = \\frac{1}{\\lambda } = R\\left( {\\frac{1}{{n_1^2}} - \\frac{1}{{n_2^2}}} \\right)$<br>For first Balmer line ${n_1} = 2,{n_2} = 3$<br>$\\therefore $ Wave number<br>$ = R\\left( {\\frac{1}{{{2^2}}} - \\frac{1}{{{3^2}}}} \\right) = R\\left( {\\frac{{9 - 4}}{{9 \\times 4}}} \\right) = \\frac{{5R}}{{36}}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45081,'physics',39,1102,2626,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Some photographic plates are not affected by red light but are immediately blackened by white light.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> The wavelength of red light is less than the wavelength of many components of white light.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'we know ${\\lambda _{red{\\rm{ }}}} = 7000\\mathop {{\\rm{ }}A}\\limits^{\\,\\,^\\circ } $<br>$E=(h c / \\lambda)$<br>Red light photons lack sufficient energy to overcome the work function of a photographic plate, while components like blue and violet light in white light exceed the work function. Therefore, distinct photographic plates are used to capture the varied energy levels present in different regions of the light spectrum. So correct option is (3).',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45082,'physics',37,973,2271,'The graph given below depict the dependence of two reactive impedances $$X_{1}$$ and $$X_{2}$$ on the frequency of the alternating e.m.f. applied individually to them. We can then say that<div class="matching-question"></div>','[{"text": "$$X_{1}$$ is an inductor and $$X_{2}$$ is a capacitor", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$X_{1}$$ is a resistor and $$X_{2}$$ is a capacitor", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$X_{1}$$ is a capacitor and $$X_{2}$$ is an inductor", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$$X_{1}$$ is an inductor and $$X_{2}$$ is a resistor", "isCorrect": false}]',2,NULL,NULL,'Hard','Graph/Figure',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/37_45082_question_1.png'),
(45083,'physics',39,1106,2633,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> An electron microscope can achieve better resolving power than an optical microscope.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> The de-Broglie wavelength of the electrons emitted from an electron gun with velocity $5 \\times {10^5}\\,m{\\rm{/}}s$ is much less than $500\\,nm.$','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Electron microscopes achieve superior resolving power due to their use of electrons with shorter wavelengths compared to optical microscopes using visible light. Optical wavelength are from $4500\\,\\mathop {{\\rm{ }}A}\\limits^{\\,\\,^\\circ } $ to $7000\\,\\mathop {{\\rm{ }}A}\\limits^{\\,\\,^\\circ } $. But electron is a matter wave (when moving) with wavelength $\\lambda=\\dfrac{h}{m_{e} v_{e}}$.<br>Given,<br>$v_{e}=5 \\times 10^{5} \\mathrm{~m} / \\mathrm{s} \\Rightarrow \\lambda_{e}=\\dfrac{6.6 \\times 10^{-34}}{9 \\times 10^{-31} \\times 5 \\times 10^{5}}$<br>$ \\Rightarrow {\\lambda _e} = 0.147 \\times {10^{ - 8}}\\,m = 1.47\\,nm = 14.7\\,\\mathop {{\\rm{ }}A}\\limits^{\\,\\,^\\circ } $<br>This is very much less than the lower limit $450\\,nm$ of optical visible region. Then, by Rayleigh\'s criterion, Resolving power $=\\dfrac{d}{1.22 \\lambda} \\propto \\dfrac{1}{\\lambda}$<br>$\\Rightarrow$ Electron microscope has very high resolving power compared to optical device. So correct option is (1).',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45086,'physics',39,1102,2626,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Work function of metal is $4.2\\,eV$. Emission of electrons is possible if two photons each of energy $2.5\\,eV$ strike an electron of the metal.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> For photoelectric emission the energy of each photon should be greater than the work function of the metal.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but reason is correct.", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Assertion is wrong. There is very low probability for two photons, (each with an energy of $2.5\\,eV$), to simultaneously collide with the same electron within the metal, so that their total energy $(2.5 + 2.5) = 5\\,eV$ is available to over come $4.2\\,eV$ of $W_{0}$ and then eject photo electrons. Reason is correct since energy of incident radiation should be more than work function. So correct option is (4).',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45087,'physics',40,241,480,'Ionisation energy of a hydrogen-like ion $A$ is greater  than that of another hydrogen like ion $B$. If  ${Z_A}$ and ${Z_B}$ represent atomic numbers of $A$  and $B$ then','[{"text": "${Z_B} > {Z_A}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${Z_A} > {Z_B}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${Z_A} = {Z_B}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{Can\'t be predicted}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Ionisation energy of a hydrogen atom is<br>$E = \\frac{{13.6{Z^2}eV}}{{{n^2}}}$<br>As ${E_A} > {E_B} \\Rightarrow {Z_A} > {Z_B}$ ($n$ is same for both)',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45088,'physics',37,974,2766,'A series $LR$ circuit is connected to an ac source of frequency $$\\omega$$ and the inductive reactance is equal to $2R$. A capacitance of capacitive reactance equal to $$R$$ is added in series with $$L$$ and  $R$. The ratio of the new power factor to the old one is :','[{"text": "$$\\\\sqrt{\\\\dfrac{3}{2}}$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$\\\\sqrt{\\\\dfrac{2}{5}}$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$\\\\sqrt{\\\\dfrac{2}{3}}$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$\\\\sqrt{\\\\dfrac{5}{2}}$$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'The initial Power factor is<br>$$\\phi_{1}=\\dfrac{\\mathrm{R}}{\\sqrt{\\mathrm{R}^{2}+\\mathrm{X}_{\\mathrm{L}}^{2}}}=\\dfrac{\\mathrm{R}}{\\sqrt{\\mathrm{R}^{2}+(2 \\mathrm{R})^{2}}}=\\dfrac{\\mathrm{R}}{\\sqrt{5} \\mathrm{R}}$$<br>The final Power factor is<br>$$=\\dfrac{R}{\\sqrt{R^{2}+\\left(X_{L}-X_{C}\\right)^{2}}}=\\dfrac{R}{\\sqrt{R^{2}+(2 R-R)^{2}}}=\\dfrac{R}{\\sqrt{2} R}$$<br>$$\\therefore \\dfrac{\\text { Final power factor }}{\\text { Initial power factor }}=\\dfrac{\\dfrac{\\mathrm{R}}{\\sqrt{2} \\mathrm{R}}}{\\dfrac{\\mathrm{R}}{\\sqrt{5} \\mathrm{R}}}=\\sqrt{\\dfrac{5}{2}}$$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',2013,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45089,'physics',39,1101,2625,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Photoelectric effect is from metals of low atomic number while thermionic emission is from metals of high atomic number.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> The process of thermionic emission is temperature dependent, but photoelectric emission is independent of temperature.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Metals with low atomic number like Li, Cs show photoelectric effect. Tungsten (like high Z) shows thermionic emission. In photoelectric emission, electrons of soft metals gain energy from absorbed photons, and get ejected. But, in thermionic emission, electrons (of heavy tungsten) acquire energy from heat, making it temperatre-dependent. So correct option is (2).',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45090,'physics',37,973,2263,'A sinusoidal voltage $V(t) = 100\\sin (500t)$ is applied across a pure inductance of  $L = 0.02H$. The current through the coil:-','[{"text": "$10\\\\cos (500t)$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$-10 \\\\cos (500 t)$$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$$10 \\\\sin (500 t)$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$-10 \\\\sin (500 t)$$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'$$X_{L}=\\omega L=500 \\times 0.02=10 \\Omega$$<br>$$\\begin{aligned}& i_{0}=\\dfrac{V_{0}}{X_{L}}=10 A \\\\& \\mathrm{i}=10 \\sin [\\omega \\mathrm{t}-\\pi / 2]=-10 \\cos 500 \\mathrm{t}\\end{aligned}$$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',2014,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45091,'physics',40,239,478,'Angular momentum $(L)$ and radius $(r)$ of a hydrogen atom are related as:','[{"text": "$Lr = {\\\\rm{constant}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$L{r^2} = {\\\\rm{constant}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$L{r^4} = {\\\\rm{constant}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{None}}\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\rm{of}}\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\rm{these}}$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'$r = \\frac{{{\\varepsilon _0}{n^2}{h^2}}}{{{e^2}\\pi m}}$<br>$ = \\frac{{{\\varepsilon _0}{{(2\\pi L)}^2}}}{{{e^2}\\pi m}}(L = n\\frac{h}{{2\\pi }}\\,\\,\\,or\\,\\,nh = 2\\pi L)$<br>$\\because $ $L{r^{\\frac{{ - 1}}{2}}} = {\\rm{constant}}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45092,'physics',39,1102,2628,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> In the process of photoelectric emission. the photo electrons emitted from the surface of the metal have maximum kinetic energy.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> The photon transfers its whole energy to the electron of the atom in photoelectric effect.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'$v_{\\max }$ in Einstein photo electric effect equation refers to electrons from surface. Photoelectrons that are emitted from the surface of a metal possess the highest kinetic energy. In contrast, the photoelectrons emitted from within the metal may lose a portion of their energy due to collisions with other atoms within the metal. So correct option is (1).',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45093,'physics',37,973,2268,'When the rms voltages ${V_L}$, ${V_C}$ and ${V_R}$ are measured respectively across the inductor $L$, the capacitor $C$ and the resistor $R$ in a series $LCR$ circuit connected to an $AC$ source, it is found that the ratio ${V_L}:{V_C}:{V_R} = 1:2:3$. If the rms voltage of the AC source is $100\\,V,$ then ${V_R}$ is close to :','[{"text": "$50\\\\,V$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$100\\\\,V$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$70\\\\,V$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$94\\\\,V$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'The rms current is<br>$$\\begin{aligned}& I_{r m s}=\\dfrac{V_{r m s}}{Z}=\\dfrac{V_{r m s}}{\\sqrt{R^{2}+\\left(X_{L}-X_{C}\\right)^{2}}} \\\\& \\dfrac{100}{\\sqrt{9 \\mathrm{x}^{2}+\\mathrm{x}^{2}}}=\\dfrac{100}{\\sqrt{10 \\mathrm{x}^{2}}}\\end{aligned}$$<br>Since $$\\mathrm{V}_{\\mathrm{L}}: \\mathrm{V}_{\\mathrm{C}}: \\mathrm{V}_{\\mathrm{R}}=1: 2: 3$$<br>$$\\mathrm{X}_{\\mathrm{L}}: \\mathrm{X}_{\\mathrm{C}}: \\mathrm{X}_{\\mathrm{R}}=1: 2: 3=\\mathrm{x}: 2 \\mathrm{x}: 3 \\mathrm{x}$$<br>Now $$V_{R}=I(3 x)=\\dfrac{100}{\\sqrt{10 x^{2}}} \\cdot 3 x \\approx 94.87 \\mathrm{~V}$$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',2014,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45094,'physics',39,1103,2630,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> The de-Broglie wavelength of a molecule varies inversely as the square root of temperature.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> The root mean square velocity of the molecule is inversely proportonal to the temperature.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'We know: $E_{k}=\\left(\\dfrac{3}{2} K_{B} T\\right)$,<br>$p=\\sqrt{2 m E_{k}}$<br>$\\Rightarrow \\lambda=\\dfrac{h}{p}=\\left[\\dfrac{h}{\\sqrt{3 m K_{B} T}}\\right]$<br>The de Broglie wavelength associated with gas molecules varies inversely with the square root of temperature. Reason is false as<br>$V_{r m s}=\\sqrt{\\dfrac{3 R T}{M}}$.<br>So correct option is (3).',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45095,'physics',40,239,478,'An electron is moving round the nucleus of a hydrogen atom in a circular orbit of radius $r$. The coulomb force ${\\vec F}$ between the two is','[{"text": "$K\\\\frac{{{e^2}}}{{{r^2}}}\\\\vec r$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$ - K\\\\frac{{{e^2}}}{{{r^3}}}\\\\vec r$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$K\\\\frac{{{e^2}}}{{{r^3}}}\\\\hat r$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$ - K\\\\frac{{{e^2}}}{{{r^2}}}\\\\hat r$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Coulomb’s force on electron by the proton is towards proton and is given by<br>$\\frac{{(e)(e)\\hat r}}{{4\\pi {\\varepsilon _0}{r^2}}} = \\frac{{{e^2}\\vec r}}{{4\\pi {\\varepsilon _0}{r^3}}}$<br>$\\therefore \\vec F =  - K\\frac{{{e^2}\\vec r}}{{{r^3}}}$<br>($ - ve$ sign shows force is towards centre of circle)',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45096,'physics',37,972,2635,'The AC voltage across a resistance can be measured using a:','[{"text": "Hot wire voltmeter", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Moving coil galvanometer", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Potential coil galvanometer", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Moving magnet galvanometer", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Hot wire voltmeter is used to measure $AC$ voltage.',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',2015,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45097,'physics',39,1103,2630,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Mass of moving photon varies inversely as the wavelength.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Energy of the particle with mass is <br>$\\left[\\right.$Mass $\\left.\\times(\\text { Speed of light })^{2}\\right]$','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Photon has zero rest-mass. It has mass when moving with $\\mathrm{c}$ as given by de Broglie formula $\\lambda=\\dfrac{h}{m c}$. Then $E=m c^{2}$ is a separate fact. So correct option is (2).',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45098,'physics',37,973,2268,'For the $LCR$ circuit, shown here, the current is observed to lead the applied voltage. An additional capacitor $$C^{\\prime}$$, when joined with the capacitor $C$ present in the circuit, makes the power factor of the circuit unity. The capacitor $$C^{\\prime}$$, must have been connected in:','[{"text": "Series with $C$ and has a magnitude<br>$\\\\frac{C}{{\\\\left( {{\\\\omega ^2}LC - 1} \\\\right)}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Series with $C$ and has a magnitude<br>$\\\\frac{{1 - {\\\\omega ^2}LC}}{{{\\\\omega ^2}\\\\,L}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Parallel with $C$ and has a magnitude<br>$\\\\frac{{1 - {\\\\omega ^2}LC}}{{{\\\\omega ^2}\\\\,L}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Parallel with $C$ and has a magnitude<br>$\\\\frac{C}{{\\\\left( {{\\\\omega ^2}LC - 1} \\\\right)}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Power factor<br>$$\\cos \\phi=\\dfrac{\\mathrm{R}}{\\sqrt{\\mathrm{R}^{2}+\\left[\\omega \\mathrm{L}-\\dfrac{1}{\\omega\\left(\\mathrm{C}+\\mathrm{C}^{\\prime}\\right)}\\right]^{2}}}=1$$<br>On solving we get,<br>$$\\omega L=\\dfrac{1}{\\omega(C+C)} \\Rightarrow C^{\\prime}=\\dfrac{1-\\omega^{2} L C}{\\omega^{2} L}$$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',2015,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/37_45098_question_1.jpg'),
(45099,'physics',40,239,478,'Match the Column-I and Column-II.<div class="matching-question"><div class="column-left">A. Radius of ${n^{th}}$ orbit<br>B. Velocity of electron in ${n^{th}}$ orbit<br>C. Potential energy in ${n^{th}}$ orbit<br>D. Kinetic energy in ${n^{th}}$ orbit</div><div class="column-right">P. $\\frac{{z{e^2}}}{{2{\\varepsilon _0}hn}}$<br>Q. $\\frac{{ - z{e^4}m}}{{4{n^2}{h^2}\\varepsilon _0^2}}$<br>R. $\\frac{{z{e^2}m}}{{8{n^2}{h^2}\\varepsilon _0^2}}$<br>S. $\\frac{{{\\varepsilon _0}{n^2}{h^2}}}{{\\pi m{e^2}z}}$</div></div>','[{"text": "A-S, \\tB-P, \\t\\tC-Q, \\tD-R", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "A-S, \\tB-R, \\t\\tC-P, \\tD-Q", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "A-Q, \\tB-P, \\t\\tC-S, \\tD-R", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "A-S, \\tB-Q, \\t\\tC-P, \\tD-R", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Option (1) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Match the following',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45100,'physics',39,1102,2627,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> The energy $(E)$ and momenturn $(p)$ of a photon are related by $p=E / c$<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> The photon behaves like a particle','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'$E=h v=\\dfrac{h v}{\\lambda}$, but $\\lambda=\\dfrac{h}{p}$ (by de Broglie) Therefore, the relationship between energy $(E)$ and momentum $(p)$ for a photon is $p=\\dfrac{E}{c}$. So correct option is (1).',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45101,'physics',37,973,2268,'A sinusoidal voltage of peak value $283\\,V$ and angular frequency $320/s$  is applied to a series $LCR$ circuit. Given that $R = 5\\Omega ,L = 25mH$ and $C = 1000\\,\\mu F$. The total impendance, and phase difference between the voltage across the source and the current will respectively be-','[{"text": "$$10 \\\\Omega$$ and $$\\\\tan ^{-1}\\\\left(\\\\dfrac{5}{3}\\\\right)$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$7 \\\\Omega$$ and $$45^{\\\\circ}$$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$$7 \\\\Omega$$ and $$\\\\tan ^{-1}\\\\left(\\\\dfrac{5}{3}\\\\right)$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$10 \\\\Omega$$ and $$\\\\tan ^{-1}\\\\left(\\\\dfrac{8}{3}\\\\right)$$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'$$V_{0}=283 \\mathrm{~V}, \\omega=320 \\mathrm{~S}^{-1}$$<br>$$\\begin{aligned}& X_{L}=320 \\times 25 \\times 10^{-3}=8 \\Omega \\\\& X_{C}=\\dfrac{1}{\\omega C}=\\dfrac{1}{320 \\times 1000 \\times 10^{-6}}=3.1 \\Omega \\\\& R=5 \\Omega \\\\& Z=\\sqrt{R^{2}+\\left(X_{L}-X_{C}\\right)^{2}}=7 \\Omega \\\\& \\tan \\phi=\\dfrac{X_{L}-X_{C}}{R} \\Rightarrow \\phi=45^{\\circ}\\end{aligned}$$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',2017,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45102,'physics',40,239,478,'The ground state energy of hydrogen atom is $ - 13.6eV$. What is the potential energy?','[{"text": "$0eV$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$ - 27.2eV$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$1eV$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2eV$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'$P.E = 2\\left( {T.E} \\right) = 2 \\times ( - 13.6eV) =  - 27.2eV$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45103,'physics',39,1102,2629,'Photoelectric emission is observed from a metallic surface for frequencies $v_{1}$ and $v_{2}$ of the incident light $\\left(v_{1}>v_{2}\\right)$. If the maximum values of kinetic energy of the photoelectrons emitted in the two cases are in the ratio $1: 2 n$, then the threshold frequency of the metallic surface is:','[{"text": "$\\\\dfrac{\\\\left(v_{1}-v_{2}\\\\right)}{(n-1)}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{\\\\left(2 n v_{1}-v_{2}\\\\right)}{(2 n-1)}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{\\\\left(n v_{2}-v_{1}\\\\right)}{(n-1)}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{\\\\left(v_{1}-v_{2}\\\\right)}{n}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'As we know, $h v-h v_{0}=K_{\\max }$<br>$h\\left(v_{1}-v_{0}\\right)=K_{1}$ and $h\\left(v_{2}-v_{0}\\right)=K_{2}$<br>$\\therefore \\dfrac{v_{1}-v_{0}}{v_{2}-v_{0}}=\\dfrac{K_{1}}{K_{2}}=\\dfrac{1}{2 n}$ or $v_{0}=\\dfrac{\\left(2 v_{1}-v_{2}\\right)}{(2 n-1)}$.',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45104,'physics',37,977,2771,'A power transmission line feeds input power at $2300\\,V$ to a step down transformer with its primary windings having 4000 turns giving the output power at $230\\,V$. If the current in the primary of the transformer is $5\\,A$ and its efficiency is $$90 \\%$$ the output current would be:','[{"text": "$45\\\\,A$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$50\\\\,A$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$20\\\\,A$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$25\\\\,A$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Efficiency $$\\eta=0.9=\\dfrac{P_{S}}{P_{P}}$$<br>${V_S}{I_S} = 0.9 \\times {V_P}{I_P}$<br>${I_S} = \\frac{{0.9 \\times 2300 \\times 5}}{{230}} = 45\\,A$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',2018,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45105,'physics',39,1102,2629,'Light of two different frequencies whose photons have energies $2.5\\,eV$ and $8.5\\,eV$ respectively, illuminative a metallic surface whose work function is $0.5\\,eV$successively. Ratio of maximum speeds of emitted electrons will be:','[{"text": "$1: 2$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$1: 1$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1: 5$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1: 4$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Kinetic energy, $K=\\phi-\\phi_{0}$<br>Here, ${K_1} = 2.5 - 0.5 = 2\\,eV$<br>${K_2} = 8.5 - 0.5 = 8\\,eV$<br>$\\therefore \\frac{{{K_1}}}{{{K_2}}} = \\frac{2}{8} = \\frac{1}{4}{\\rm{ or }}\\frac{{v_1^2}}{{v_2^2}} = \\frac{1}{4}$<br>${\\rm{or }}\\frac{{{v_1}}}{{{v_2}}} = \\sqrt {\\frac{1}{4}}  = \\frac{1}{2}.$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45106,'physics',40,239,478,'The ground state energy of hydrogen atom is $ - 13.6eV$. The kinetic energy of the electron in this state is:','[{"text": "$1.85\\\\,eV$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$13.6\\\\,eV$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$6.8\\\\,eV$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$3.4\\\\,eV$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'$K.E =  - \\left( {T.E} \\right) =  - ( - 13.6)eV = 13.6eV$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45107,'physics',37,978,2282,'Let $L,R,C$ and $V$ represent inductance, resistance, capacitance and voltage, respectively. The dimension of $\\frac{L}{{RCV}}$ in SI units will be','[{"text": "$\\\\left[ {L{A^{ - 2}}} \\\\right]$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\left[ {L{A^2}} \\\\right]$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\left[ {{A^{ - 1}}} \\\\right]$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "[$LTA$]", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'For $LR$ circuit, time $$=\\left[\\dfrac{L}{R}\\right]=[T]$$<br>For capacitor $C,[q] = [CV] = [AT]$<br>${\\rm{ So}},{\\rm{ }}\\left[ {\\frac{L}{{RCV}}} \\right] = \\frac{{[T]}}{{[AT]}} = \\left[ {{A^{ - 1}}} \\right]$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',2019,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45108,'physics',39,1102,2629,'A photosensitive metallic surface has work function, $h v_{0}$. If photons of energy $2 h v_{0}$ fall on this surface, the electrons come out with a maximum velocity of $4 \\times {10^6}\\,m{\\rm{/}}s$. When the photon energy is increased to $5 h v_{0}$, then maximum velocity of photoelectrons will be:','[{"text": "$2 \\\\times {10^7}\\\\,m{\\\\rm{/}}s$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2 \\\\times {10^6}\\\\,m{\\\\rm{/}}s$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$8 \\\\times {10^5}\\\\,m{\\\\rm{/}}s$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$8 \\\\times {10^6}\\\\,m{\\\\rm{/}}s$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'$\\dfrac{1}{2} m v^{2}=E-W=h\\left(v-v_{0}\\right)$<br>$\\Rightarrow \\dfrac{v_{1}^{2}}{v_{2}^{2}}=\\dfrac{2 h v_{0}-h v_{0}}{5 h v_{0}-h v_{0}}=\\dfrac{h v_{0}}{4 h v_{0}}=\\dfrac{1}{4}$<br>$\\Rightarrow \\dfrac{v_{1}^{2}}{v_{2}^{2}}=\\dfrac{1}{4} \\Rightarrow \\dfrac{v_{1}}{v_{2}}=\\dfrac{1}{2}$<br>$\\Rightarrow v_{2}=2 v_{1}$<br>$ \\Rightarrow {v_2} = 8 \\times {10^6}\\,m{\\rm{/}}s.$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45110,'physics',40,239,478,'The time of revolution of an electron around a nucleus of charge $Ze$ in ${n^{th}}$  Bohr orbit is directly proportional to','[{"text": "$\\\\frac{{{n^3}}}{{{Z^2}}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{Z}{n}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{{{n^2}}}{Z}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$n$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'$T = \\frac{{2\\pi r}}{v},r = \\frac{{{n^2}{h^2}{\\varepsilon _0}}}{{\\pi mZ{e^2}}}\\,\\,\\,\\& \\,\\,v = \\frac{{Z{e^2}}}{{2{\\varepsilon _0}nh}}$<br>$T = (2\\pi )\\frac{{\\left( {\\frac{{{n^2}{h^2}{\\varepsilon _0}}}{{\\pi mZ{e^2}}}} \\right)}}{{\\left( {\\frac{{Z{e^2}}}{{2{\\varepsilon _0}nh}}} \\right)}} \\Rightarrow T \\propto \\frac{{{n^3}}}{{{Z^2}}}$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45111,'physics',39,1102,2628,'When a monochromatic source of light is at a distance of $0.2\\,m$ from a photoelectric cell, the cut-off voltage and the saturation current are respectively $0.6\\,V$ and $18\\,ma$. If the same source is placed $0.6\\,m$ away from the cell, then:','[{"text": "The stopping potential will be $0.2\\\\,V$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "The stopping potential will be $1.8\\\\,V$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "The saturation current will be $0.6\\\\,mA$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "The saturation current will be $2.0\\\\,mA$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'When the source is 3 times farther, number of photons falling on the surface becomes $\\dfrac{1}{9}$ th but the frequency remains same. Hence stopping potential will be same i.e., $0.6\\,V$ and saturation current become $\\dfrac{1}{9} \\times 18 m A=2 m A$.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45112,'physics',37,977,2770,'In a transformer primary voltage is $220\\,V$ and secondary current is $0.11\\,A$ with output power $60\\,W$','[{"text": "Transformer is step up", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Transformer is step down", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Transformer is auxiliary", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "None of these", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Voltage across the secondary of the transformer:<br>$$\\begin{aligned}& \\mathrm{V}_{\\mathrm{s}}=\\dfrac{60 \\mathrm{~W}}{0.11 \\mathrm{~A}} \\approx 545 \\mathrm{~V}, \\mathrm{~V}_{\\mathrm{p}}=220 \\text { volt (given) } \\\\& \\because \\mathrm{V}_{\\mathrm{s}}>\\mathrm{V}_{\\mathrm{p}} \\text { so it is a step up transformer }\\end{aligned}$$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',2021,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/37_45112_question_1.jpg'),
(45113,'physics',40,239,478,'Product of velocity and square of time period of electrons, in ${n^{th}}$  of  hydrogen atom orbit is proportional to:','[{"text": "${n^3}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{1}{{{n^2}}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${n^2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${n^5}$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'We know that, the period $\\alpha \\,{n^3}$<br>Velocity $\\alpha \\,\\frac{1}{n}$<br>$ \\Rightarrow {\\left( {Time\\,period} \\right)^2} \\times velocity\\,\\alpha \\,{n^5}$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45114,'physics',39,1102,2627,'Light rays of wavelengths $6000 \\mathop A^{~~\\circ} $ and of photon intensity $39.6\\,watts{\\rm{/}}{m^2}$ is incident on a metal surface. If only one percent of photons incident on the surface emit photo electrons, then the number of electrons emitted per second per unit area from the surface will be<br>[Planks constant $ = 6.64 \\times {10^{ - 34}}\\,J - s$; Velocity of light $ = 3 \\times {10^8}\\,m{s^{ - 1}}$]','[{"text": "$12 \\\\times 10^{18}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$10 \\\\times 10^{18}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$12 \\\\times 10^{17}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$12 \\\\times 10^{15}$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'$n_{e}=10^{-6} \\times \\dfrac{I \\lambda}{h c}$<br>$\\begin{aligned}& =\\dfrac{10^{-6} \\times 39.6 \\times 6 \\times 10^{-7}}{6.6 \\times 10^{-34} \\times 3 \\times 10^{8}} \\\\& =12 \\times 10^{13} .\\end{aligned}$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45115,'physics',37,973,2262,'A resistance of $$40 \\Omega$$ is connected to a source of alternating current rated  $220\\,V,\\,50\\,Hz$. Find the time taken by the current to change from its maximum value to rms value:','[{"text": "$2.5\\\\,ms$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$1.25\\\\,ms$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2.5\\\\,s$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$0.25\\\\,s$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'<br>Let $$t_{1}$$ be the time at which the current reaches the maximum value.<br>$$\\begin{aligned}& I_{\\max } \\sin \\left(\\omega t_{1}\\right)=I_{\\text {max }} \\\\& \\sin \\left(\\omega t_{1}\\right)=1 \\\\& \\omega t_{1}=\\dfrac{\\pi}{2} \\\\& \\Rightarrow t_{1}=\\dfrac{\\pi}{2 \\omega}\\end{aligned}$$<br>Let ${t_2}$ be the time at which the current reaches the rms value from maximum value.<br>${I_{\\max }}\\sin \\left( {\\omega {t_2}} \\right) = \\frac{{{I_{\\max }}}}{{\\sqrt 2 }}$<br>$\\sin \\left( {\\omega {t_2}} \\right) = \\frac{1}{{\\sqrt 2 }}$<br>$\\omega {t_2} = \\frac{\\pi }{4},\\frac{{3\\pi }}{4}$<br>$\\omega {t_2} = \\frac{{3\\pi }}{4}\\quad \\left( {\\frac{{\\rm{\\pi }}}{{\\rm{4}}}{\\rm{is}}\\,{\\rm{not}}\\,{\\rm{allowed}}} \\right).$<br>${t_2} = \\frac{{3\\pi }}{{4\\omega }}$<br>${t_2} - {t_1} = \\frac{{3\\pi }}{{4\\omega }} - \\frac{\\pi }{{2\\omega }} = \\frac{\\pi }{{4\\omega }}$<br>$ = \\frac{\\pi }{{4 \\times 2\\pi  \\times 50}}$<br>$ = 2.5\\,ms$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',2022,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45116,'physics',39,1102,3092,'Ultraviolet light of wavelength $300\\,nm$ and intensity $1.0\\,W{\\rm{/}}{m^2}$ falls on the surface of a photoelectric material. If one percent of the incident photons produce photoelectrons, then the number of photoelectrons emitted from an area of $1.0\\,c{m^2}$ of the surface is nearly','[{"text": "$9.61 \\\\times 10^{14}$ per second", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$4.12 \\\\times 10^{13}$ per second", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1.51 \\\\times 10^{12}$ per second", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$2.13 \\\\times 10^{11}$ per second", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'If \' $n$ \' is the no. of photons incidenting per unit time per unit area on a metal surface then<br>$I=n \\dfrac{h c}{\\lambda} \\Rightarrow n=\\dfrac{I \\lambda}{h c}$<br>$\\%$ of this number cause photo electric emission<br>$\\Rightarrow N=\\dfrac{1}{100} n$<br>$\\Rightarrow$ no. of photo electrons emitted from an are $1\\,c{m^2}$ is given by<br>$n_{e}=10^{-4} \\times \\dfrac{1}{100} n$<br>$\\Rightarrow n_{e}=10^{-6} \\times \\dfrac{I \\lambda}{h c}$<br>$\\approx 10^{-6} \\times \\dfrac{1 \\times 300 \\times 10^{-9}}{20 \\times 10^{-26}}$<br>$=15 \\times 10^{11}=1.5 \\times 10^{12}$.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45118,'physics',40,239,478,'The radius of the smallest electron orbit in hydrogen like ion is $(0.5 \\times {10^{ - 10}}/4)$, then it is:','[{"text": "${\\\\rm{hydrogen}}\\\\,{\\\\rm{atom}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$H{e^ + }$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$L{i^{2 + }}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$B{e^{3 + }}$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'For hydrogen like atom the radius of ${n^{th}}$ orbit, $r_n^2 = \\frac{{{n^2}}}{Z}{a_0}$<br>Here, ${a_0} = 0.51 \\times {10^{ - 10}}m,r_n^2 = \\frac{{0.51 \\times {{10}^{ - 10}}}}{4}m$<br>In the ground state,  $n = 1$<br>$\\therefore \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\frac{{0.51 \\times {{10}^{ - 10}}}}{4} = \\frac{{{1^2}}}{Z} \\times 0.51 \\times {10^{ - 10}} \\Rightarrow Z = 4$<br>So, the atom is triply ionised beryllium $(B{e^{3 + }})$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45119,'physics',41,406,809,'A nuclear reaction is given by $_z{X^A}{ \\to _{z + 1}}{Y^A}{ + _{ - 1}}{e^0} + \\bar v$, represents:','[{"text": "${\\\\mathop{\\\\rm Fission}\\\\nolimits} $", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\beta - decay$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\alpha - decay$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\mathop{\\\\rm Fusion}\\\\nolimits} $", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'$_{ - 1}{e^0}$ is known as ${\\beta ^ - }$ particle so it is known as $\\beta $ -decay.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45120,'physics',39,1102,2628,'When a metal surface is illuminated by a monochromatic light of wave length $\\lambda$, then the stopping potential difference required is $3\\,V$. When the same surface is illuminated by the light of wavelength $2 \\lambda$, then the stopping potential required is $V$. Then the threshold wavelength of the given metal surface will be','[{"text": "$6 \\\\lambda$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$4 \\\\lambda / 3$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$4 \\\\lambda$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$8 \\\\lambda$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'$h c\\left[\\dfrac{1}{\\lambda}-\\dfrac{1}{\\lambda_{0}}\\right]=3 e V$<br>$h c\\left[\\dfrac{1}{2 \\lambda}-\\dfrac{1}{\\lambda_{0}}\\right]=1 e V$<br>$\\Rightarrow \\dfrac{1 / \\lambda-1 / \\lambda_{0}}{1 / 2 \\lambda-1 / \\lambda_{0}}=3 \\Rightarrow \\dfrac{1}{\\lambda}-\\dfrac{1}{\\lambda_{0}}=\\dfrac{3}{2 \\lambda}-\\dfrac{3}{\\lambda_{0}}$<br>$\\Rightarrow \\dfrac{1}{2 \\lambda}=\\dfrac{2}{\\lambda_{0}} \\Rightarrow \\lambda_{0}=4 \\lambda$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45121,'physics',37,974,2274,'Given below are two statements:<br>Statement-I: The reactance of an ac circuit is zero. It is possible that the circuit contains a capacitor and an inductor.<br>Statement-II : In ac circuit, the average power delivered by the source never becomes zero.<br>In the light of the above statements, choose the correct answer from the above statements, choose the correct answer from the options given below:','[{"text": "Both Statement I and Statement II are true.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Statement I and Statement II are false.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Statement I is true but Statement II in false.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Staement I is false but Statement II is true.<br>", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'<br>$$X=$$ Reactance of circuit $$=\\left(X_{L}-X_{C}\\right)$$<br>$$X$$ can be zero when $$X_{L}=X_{C}$$<br>In a.c circuit, average power $$P=V_{r m s} I_{r m s} \\cos \\phi$$<br>$$V_{r m s} \\neq 0, I_{r m s} \\neq 0$$ but $$(\\cos \\phi)$$ can be zero<br>$$\\left(\\phi=\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\right)$$<br>$P$ can be zero.<br>Then it is called \'wattless current\'.<br>Statement (1) is true.<br>',NULL,'Hard','MCQ type-2',2022,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45122,'physics',40,239,478,'For an electron in the seventh orbit of hydrogen, the moment of momentum as per Bohr’s model is','[{"text": "$\\\\frac{{7h}}{{2\\\\pi }}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{{2h}}{\\\\pi }$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{{5h}}{{2\\\\pi }}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2\\\\pi h$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'We know from Bohr’s theory that in an orbit angular momentum $ = n \\cdot \\frac{h}{{2\\pi }}$<br>For second orbit $n = 7$<br>So, angular momentum $ = 7 \\times \\frac{h}{{2\\pi }} = \\frac{{7h}}{{2\\pi }}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45124,'physics',39,1102,2629,'A photometal is illuminated by lights of wavelengths $\\lambda_{1}$ and $\\lambda_{2}$ respectively. The maximum kinetic energies of electrons emitted in the two cases are $E_{1}$ and $E_{2}$ respectively. The work function of metal is.','[{"text": "$\\\\dfrac{E_{2} \\\\lambda_{1}+E_{1} \\\\lambda_{2}}{\\\\lambda_{1}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{E_{1} \\\\lambda_{1}+E_{2} \\\\lambda_{2}}{\\\\lambda_{1}+\\\\lambda_{2}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{E_{1} \\\\lambda_{1}+E_{2} \\\\lambda_{2}}{\\\\lambda_{1}-\\\\lambda_{2}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{E_{2} \\\\lambda_{2}-E_{1} \\\\lambda_{1}}{\\\\lambda_{1}-\\\\lambda_{2}}$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'$\\dfrac{h c}{\\lambda_{1}}=W+E_{1} ; \\dfrac{h c}{\\lambda_{2}}=W+E_{2}$<br>$\\Rightarrow W_{1} \\lambda_{1}+\\lambda_{1} E_{1}=W_{2} \\lambda_{2}+\\lambda_{2} E_{2}$<br>$\\Rightarrow W \\lambda_{1}+\\lambda_{1} E_{1}=W \\lambda_{2}+E_{2} \\lambda_{2}$<br>$\\Rightarrow W=\\dfrac{E_{2} \\lambda_{2}-E_{1} \\lambda_{1}}{\\lambda_{1}-\\lambda_{2}}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45125,'physics',41,406,809,'The particle $A$ is converted to $C$ via following reactions then<br>$A \\to B + {}_2H{e^4}$<br>$B \\to C + {2_{ - 1}}{e^0}$','[{"text": "$A$ and $C$ are isobars", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$A$ and $C$ are isotopes", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$A$ and $B$ are isobars", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$A$ and $B$ are isotopes", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'By adding the given two reactions<br>$A \\to C{ + _2}H{e^4} + 2\\mathop e\\limits_{ - 1}^0 $<br>$A$ and $C$ have same atomic numbers but different neutron numbers. The given $A$ and $C$ are isotopes',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45126,'physics',40,241,480,'The angular momentum of an electron in the ${2^{nd}}$ excited state of Helium ion $(H{e^ + })$ is','[{"text": "$\\\\frac{h}{{2\\\\pi }}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{{2h}}{{2\\\\pi }}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{{3h}}{{2\\\\pi }}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{{4h}}{{2\\\\pi }}$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Angular momentum in the second exicted state $\\frac{{nh}}{{2\\pi }} = \\frac{{3h}}{{2\\pi }}\\left( {n = 3} \\right)$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45127,'physics',39,1102,2628,'A metal of work function $4\\,eV$ is exposed to a radiation of wavelength $140 \\times {10^{ - 9}}\\,m$. Then the stopping potential required is $\\left( {h = 6.63 \\times {{10}^{ - 34}}\\,Js} \\right.$ and $c = 3 \\times {10^8}\\,m{\\rm{/}}\\left. s \\right)$','[{"text": "$6.42\\\\,V$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2.94\\\\,V$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$4.86\\\\,V$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$3.2\\\\,V$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'${V_0} = \\frac{{\\left[ {\\frac{{12400}}{{\\lambda {\\rm{ in }}\\mathop {{\\rm{ }}A}\\limits^{\\,\\,^\\circ } }} - {\\phi _0}} \\right]}}{e}$<br>$\\Rightarrow V_{0}=\\left[\\dfrac{12400}{1400}-4\\right]$<br>$=\\left(\\dfrac{124}{14}-4\\right)=\\dfrac{124-56}{14}$<br>$ = \\frac{{68}}{{14}} = \\frac{{34}}{7} = 4.86\\,Volts$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45129,'physics',41,407,811,'In the following reaction<br>$_{12}M{g^{24}} + {}_2{\\rm{H}}{{\\rm{e}}^4} \\to {}_{14}{\\rm{S}}{{\\rm{i}}^X} + {}_0{n^1},X$ is','[{"text": "$26$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$22$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$28$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$27$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'By equating the mass numbers. <br>$X + 1 = 24 + 4 \\Rightarrow X = 27.$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45130,'physics',39,1102,3092,'A laser used to weld detached retains emits light with a wavelength $652\\,nm$ in pulses that are of $20\\,ms$ duration. The average power during each pulse is $0.6\\,W$. The energy in each pulse and in a single photon are','[{"text": "$7.5 \\\\times {10^{15}}eV,2.7\\\\,eV$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$6.5 \\\\times {10^{16}}eV,2.9\\\\,eV$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$6.5 \\\\times {10^{16}}eV,2.7\\\\,eV$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$7.5 \\\\times {10^{16}}eV,1.9\\\\,eV$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'$E=p t ; E($ in each photon $)=\\dfrac{h c}{\\lambda}$<br>$\\lambda  = 652\\,nm$<br>$E = Pt \\Rightarrow E = (0.6)20 \\times {10^{ - 3}} = 12 \\times {10^{ - 3}}\\,J$<br>$ \\Rightarrow E = \\frac{{12 \\times {{10}^{ - 3}}}}{{1.6 \\times {{10}^{ - 19}}}} = \\frac{{12}}{6} \\times \\frac{{{{10}^{ - 3}}}}{{{{10}^{ - 20}}}}$<br>$ = \\frac{3}{4} \\times {10^{17}} = 0.75 \\times {10^{17}}$<br>$ = 7.5 \\times {10^{16}}eV$<br>Energy of photon $ = \\frac{{hc}}{\\lambda } = \\frac{{1240}}{{652}} = 1.9\\,eV$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45131,'physics',40,239,478,'Orbital acceleration of electron is','[{"text": "$\\\\frac{{4{n^2}{h^2}}}{{{\\\\pi ^2}{m^2}{r^3}}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{{{n^2}{h^2}}}{{4{\\\\pi ^2}{m^2}{r^3}}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{{4{n^2}{h^2}}}{{4{\\\\pi ^2}{m^2}{r^3}}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{{{n^2}{h^2}}}{{2{n^2}{r^3}}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'$mvr = \\frac{{nh}}{{2\\pi }} \\Rightarrow v = \\frac{{nh}}{{2\\pi mr}} \\Rightarrow \\frac{{{v^2}}}{r} = \\frac{{{n^2}{h^2}}}{{4{\\pi ^2}{m^2}{r^3}}}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45132,'physics',41,404,803,'The binding energies of nuclei $X$ and $Y$ are ${E_1}$ and ${E_2}$ respecitvely. Two atoms of $X$ fuse to give one atom of $Y$ and an energy $Q$ is released. Then','[{"text": "$Q = 2{E_1} - {E_2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$Q = {E_2} - 2{E_1}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$Q < 2{E_1} - {E_2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$Q > {E_2} - 2{E_1}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'During fusion, binding energy of daughter nucleus is always greater than the total binding energy of the parent nuclei. The difference of binding energies is released. <br>Hence , $Q = {E_2} - 2{E_1}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45134,'physics',39,1102,2629,'Two photons of energies twice and thrice the work function of a metal are incident on the metal surface. Then the ratio of maximum velocities of the photoelectrons emitted in the two cases respectively, is','[{"text": "$\\\\sqrt{2}: 1$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\sqrt{3}: 1$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\sqrt{3}: \\\\sqrt{2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1: \\\\sqrt{2}$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'$\\frac{{{V_1}}}{{{V_2}}} = \\sqrt {\\frac{{{E_1} - {\\phi _0}}}{{{E_2} - {\\phi _0}}}}  = \\frac{{{V_1}}}{{{V_2}}}$<br>$ = \\sqrt {\\frac{{2\\phi  - \\phi }}{{3\\phi  - \\phi }}}  = \\frac{1}{{\\sqrt 2 }}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45135,'physics',41,404,804,'$\\gamma  - {\\mathop{\\rm rays}\\nolimits} $ radiation can be used to create electron-positron pair. In this process of pair production, $\\gamma  - {\\mathop{\\rm rays}\\nolimits} $ energy cannot be less than','[{"text": "$5.0\\\\,MeV$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1.02\\\\,MeV$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$4.02\\\\,MeV$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$15.0\\\\,MeV$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Energy released by $\\gamma  - {\\mathop{\\rm rays}\\nolimits} $  for pair production must be greater than $1.02\\,MeV$<br>$\\because $ Energy of each electron is $0.51\\,MeV$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45137,'physics',40,239,478,'For electron moving in ${n^{th}}$  orbit of the atom, the angular velocity is proportional to:','[{"text": "$n$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{1}{n}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${n^3}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{1}{{{n^3}}}$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'${\\rm{v}}\\,\\alpha \\frac{1}{n}\\,\\,\\& \\,r\\,\\alpha \\,{n^2}$<br>$v = r\\omega  \\Rightarrow \\omega  = \\frac{v}{r}$<br>$\\omega \\,\\alpha \\frac{1}{{{n^3}}}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45138,'physics',39,1102,2628,'A radiation of wavelength $2500 \\mathop A^{~~\\circ} $ is incident on a metal plate whose work function is $3.5\\,eV$. Then the potential required to stop the fastest photo electrons emitted by the surface is  $\\left( {h = 6.63 \\times {{10}^{ - 34}}Js\\,\\,\\& \\,\\,{\\mkern 1mu} c = 3 \\times {{10}^8}m{\\rm{/}}s} \\right)$','[{"text": "$1.86\\\\,V$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$3.00\\\\,V$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1.46\\\\,V$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$2.15\\\\,V$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'$e V_{0}=\\dfrac{h c}{\\lambda}-\\phi$<br>$E = \\frac{{hc}}{\\lambda } = \\frac{{6.6 \\times {{10}^{ - 34}} \\times 3 \\times {{10}^8}}}{{2500 \\times {{10}^{ - 10}}}}$<br>$ = \\frac{{20 \\times {{10}^{ - 16}}}}{{2500}}\\,J$<br>$ = \\frac{{20 \\times {{10}^{ - 16}}}}{{2500 \\times 1.6 \\times 10\\,h - 19}} = 5\\,eV$<br>$ \\Rightarrow e{V_0} = (5 - 3.5)\\,eV = 1.5\\,eV$<br>$ \\Rightarrow {V_0} = 1.5\\,V.$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45139,'physics',41,406,809,'${}_{86}{A^{222}} \\to {}_{84}{B^{210}}.$ In this reaction how many  $\\alpha $ and  $\\beta $ particles are emitted is','[{"text": "$\\\\alpha ,3\\\\beta $", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$6\\\\alpha ,3\\\\beta $", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$3\\\\alpha ,6\\\\beta $", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$3\\\\alpha ,4\\\\beta $", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'${}_{86}^{222}A \\to {}_{84}^{210}B + x{}_2^4\\alpha  + y{}_{ - 1}^0\\beta $<br>By equating the atomic and mass numbers<br>$86 = 84 + 2x - y$<br>$222 = 210 + 4x$<br>$ \\Rightarrow x = 3,\\,y = 4$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45140,'physics',40,239,478,'What will be the angular momentum in 4$th$ orbit, if $L$ is the angular momentum of the electron in the 2$nd$ orbit of hydrogen atom?','[{"text": "$2L$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{3}{2}L$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{2}{3}L$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{L}{2}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'We know that, ${L_n} = \\frac{{nh}}{{2\\pi }}$<br>Angular momentum in 2$nd$ orbit is $L$.<br>Hence,$L = \\frac{{2h}}{{2\\pi }} = \\frac{h}{\\pi }$<br>The angular momentum in 4th orbit is <br>${L_4} = \\frac{{4h}}{{2\\pi }} = \\frac{{2h}}{\\pi } = 2L$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45142,'physics',39,1102,3092,'A photo sensitive surface is receiving light of wavelength $5000 \\mathop A^{~~\\circ} $ at the rate of ${10^{ - 7}}J{s^{ - 1}}$. The number of photoelectrons ejected per second is (given each photon can eject one electron)','[{"text": "$2.5 \\\\times 10^{12}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2.5 \\\\times 10^{11}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$2.5 \\\\times 10^{10}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2.5 \\\\times 10^{9}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'According to the Plank\'s quantum law<br>$E=n h v=n\\left(\\dfrac{h c}{\\lambda}\\right)$<br>$\\frac{E}{t} = \\frac{n}{t}\\left( {\\frac{{hc}}{\\lambda }} \\right) \\Rightarrow {10^{ - 7}}$<br>$ = \\left( {\\frac{n}{t}} \\right)\\frac{{6.6 \\times {{10}^{ - 34}} \\times 3 \\times {{10}^8}}}{{5000 \\times {{10}^{ - 10}}}}$<br>$\\frac{n}{t} = \\frac{{5000 \\times {{10}^{ - 10}} \\times {{10}^{ - 7}}}}{{6.6 \\times {{10}^{ - 34}} \\times 3 \\times {{10}^8}}} = 2.5 \\times {10^{11}}{\\rm{sec}}.$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45143,'physics',41,404,803,'The binding energy per nucleon is maximum in the case of','[{"text": "$_{26}^{56}{\\\\rm{Fe}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$_2^4{\\\\rm{He}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$_{92}^{235}{\\\\rm{U}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$_{56}^{141}{\\\\rm{Ba}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Binding energy per nucleon increases with atomic number and is maximum for iron. After that it decreases.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45145,'physics',39,1102,2629,'Work function of nickel is $5.01\\,eV$. When ultraviolet radiation of wavelength $2000 \\mathop A^{~~\\circ} $ is incident on it, electrons are emitted. What will be the maximum velecity of emitted electrons?','[{"text": "$3 \\\\times {10^8}\\\\,m{\\\\rm{/}}s$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$6.46 \\\\times {10^5}\\\\,m{\\\\rm{/}}s$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$10.36 \\\\times {10^5}\\\\,m{\\\\rm{/}}s$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$8.54 \\\\times {10^6}\\\\,m{\\\\rm{/}}s$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Energy corresponding to $2000 \\mathop A^{~~\\circ} $.<br>$E = \\frac{{12375}}{{2000}}eV = 6.2\\,eV$<br>Maximum kinetic energy.<br>$K=h v-h v_{0}$<br>$K = E - {W_0} \\Rightarrow {K_{\\max }}$<br>$ = (6.2 - 5.01)\\,eV = 1.19\\,eV$<br>Now, $K_{\\max }=\\dfrac{1}{2} m v^{2}{ }_{\\max }$<br>$1.19 \\times 1.6 \\times 10^{-19}=\\dfrac{1}{2} \\times 9.1 \\times 10^{-31} \\times V_{\\max }^{2}$<br>$v_{\\max }^{2}=\\dfrac{1.19 \\times 10^{-19} \\times 1.6 \\times 2}{9.1 \\times 10^{-31}}$<br>$ = 0.418 \\times {10^{12}} = 418 \\times {10^{10}}$<br>${v_{\\max }} = 6.46 \\times {10^5}\\,m{\\rm{/}}s$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45146,'physics',40,239,478,'The radius of hydrogen atom, when it is in second excited state becomes:','[{"text": "Half", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Double", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Four times", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Nine times", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'$r\\,\\alpha \\,{n^2}$<br>$\\frac{{{r_1}}}{{{r_2}}} = \\frac{{n_1^2}}{{n_2^2}} \\Rightarrow \\frac{{{r_1}}}{{{r_2}}} = \\frac{1}{{{3^2}}}$<br>${r_2} = 9{r_1}$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45147,'physics',41,404,803,'Average binding energy per nucleon over a wide range is','[{"text": "$8\\\\,MeV$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$8.8\\\\,MeV$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$5.6\\\\,MeV$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1.1\\\\,MeV$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,NULL,NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45148,'physics',39,1102,2627,'When the distance of a point light source from a photocell is $r_{1}$, photoelectric current is $I_{1}$. If the distance becomes $r_{2}$, then the current is $I_{2}$. The ratio $\\left(I_{1}: I_{2}\\right)$ is equal to','[{"text": "$r_{2}^{2}: r_{1}^{2}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$r_{2}: r_{1}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$r_{1}^{2}: r_{2}^{2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$r_{1}: r_{2}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Photo electric current $(I) \\propto$ intensity and, intensity $\\propto \\dfrac{1}{r}$.<br>$\\Rightarrow(I) \\propto \\dfrac{1}{r^{2}} \\Rightarrow \\dfrac{I_{1}}{I_{2}}=\\dfrac{r_{2}^{2}}{r_{1}^{2}}$.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45149,'physics',41,404,803,'The wrong statement about binding energy is','[{"text": "It is the sum of the rest mass energies of  nucleons minus the rest mass energy of the  nucleus.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "It is the energy released when the nucleons combine to form a nucleus.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "It is the energy required to break a given nucleus into its constituent  nucleons.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "It is the sum of the kinetic energies of all the nucleons in the nucleus.", "isCorrect": true}]',3,NULL,NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45150,'physics',40,241,480,'Which of the following parameters is same for all hydrogen-like atoms and ions in their ground states?','[{"text": "Radius of the orbit", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Speed of the electron", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Energy of the atom", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Orbital angular momentum of the electron", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'For all $H$-like atoms in ground state<br>$L = \\frac{h}{{2\\pi }}$<br>',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45152,'physics',39,1102,2627,'A point source causes photoelectric effect from a small metal plate. Which of the following curves may reperesent the saturation photo current as a function of the distance between the source and the metal?<div class="matching-question"></div>','[{"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'With distance intensity will fall as $\\dfrac{1}{r^{2}}$.<br>Current $ \\propto $ Intensity $ \\propto $ $\\dfrac{1}{r^{2}}$. <br>So, Correct option is (4).',NULL,'Medium','Graph/Figure',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45153,'physics',41,404,803,'If ${M_0}$ is the mass of an oxygen isotope $_8{O^{17}},{m_p}\\,and\\,{m_n}$ are the masses of a proton and a neutron respectively the nuclear binding energy of the isotope is:','[{"text": "$({M_0} - 8{m_p}){c^2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$(9{m_n} + 8{m_p} - {M_0}){c^2}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${M_0}{c^2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$({M_0} - 17{m_n}){c^2}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Binding energy,<br>$B.E. = (8{m_p} + 9{m_n} - {M_0}){c^2}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45155,'physics',39,1102,2628,'The stopping potential as a function of frequency of incident radiation is plotted for two different photo electric surfaces $A$ and $B$. The graphs show the work function of $A$ is<div class="matching-question"></div>','[{"text": "Greater than that of $B$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Smaller than that of $B$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Same as that of $B$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "No comparision can be done from given graphs", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'<img src=\'/img/data/physics/39_45155_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />  Since $\\beta$ is greater, work function of $B$ will be greater.',NULL,'Hard','Graph/Figure',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/39_45155_question_1.png'),
(45156,'physics',40,238,477,'An alpha nucleus of energy $\\frac{1}{2}m{v^2}$ bombard a heavy nuclear target of charge $Ze$. Then, the distance of closest approach for the alpha nucleus will be proportional to:','[{"text": "${v^2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1/m$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$1/{v^4}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1/Ze$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'For the distance of closest approach, we can write,<br>$\\frac{1}{2}m{v^2} = \\frac{{K \\times (2e)(Ze)}}{r} \\Rightarrow r\\alpha \\frac{1}{m}$<br>Where $r$ is the distance of closest approach.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45157,'physics',41,404,802,'A nucleus with mass number 220 initially at rest emits an $\\alpha $-particle. If the $Q$ value of the reaction is $5.5\\,MeV$, calculate the kinetic energy of the $\\alpha $-particle:','[{"text": "$4.4\\\\,MeV$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$5.4\\\\,MeV$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$5.6\\\\,MeV$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$4.5\\\\,MeV$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Linear momentum is conserved.<br>$\\therefore {p_1} = {p_2}$<br>But $p = \\sqrt {2mK} $ where $K = $ kinetic energy<br>$\\therefore \\sqrt {2(216m){K_1}}  = \\sqrt {2(4m){K_2}} $<br>$216{K_1} = 4{K_2} \\Rightarrow {K_2} = 54{K_1}$               (1)<br>Given  ${K_1} + {K_2} = 5.5MeV$                       (2)<br>From (1) and (2)<br>or ${K_1} = \\frac{1}{{10}}MeV$ and ${K_2} = 5.4MeV$<br>$\\therefore $ Kinetic energy of $\\alpha $ particle $ = 5.4\\,MeV$.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45159,'physics',39,1103,2630,'Which of the following figure represent the variation of particle momentum $(P)$ and associated de-Brogle wavelength $(\\lambda)$ ?<div class="matching-question"></div>','[{"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'de-brogle wavelength, $\\lambda=\\dfrac{h}{p}$ or $\\lambda \\propto \\dfrac{1}{p}$<br>Hence curve (4) is the correct option.',NULL,'Easy','Graph/Figure',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45160,'physics',41,404,802,'$M,{M_n}\\& {M_p}$ denotes the masses of a nucleus of $_z{X^A},$ a neutron, and a proton respectively. If the nucleus is separated into its individual protons and neutrons then','[{"text": "$M = (A - Z){M_n} + Z{M_p}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$M = Z{M_n} + (A - Z){M_p}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$M > (A - Z){M_n} + Z{M_p}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$M < (A - Z){M_n} + Z{M_p}$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,NULL,NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45161,'physics',40,239,478,'Bohr’s atomic model assumes','[{"text": "The nucleus is inside the atom at rest", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Electron in a quantised  orbit will not radiate energy", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Mass of the electron remains constant", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "1, 2, 3 are correct", "isCorrect": true}]',3,NULL,NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45162,'physics',39,1103,2630,'If a proton and electron have the same de-broglie wavelength then:','[{"text": "Kinetic energy of electron $ < $ Kinetic energy of proton.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Kinetic energy of electron= Kinetic energy of proton.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Momentum of electron $>$ Momentum of proton.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Momentum of electron = Momentum of proton.", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'$\\lambda=\\dfrac{h}{p} \\Rightarrow$ same de-Broglie wave length means same linear momentum.<br>$\\lambda=\\dfrac{h}{\\sqrt{2 m(K E)}} \\Rightarrow$ for same de-Broglie wave length $K E$ would be different.<br>Since $e^{-}$has less mass its $K E$ would be more.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45163,'physics',41,404,802,'Let ${m_p}$ be the mass of a proton, ${m_n}$ the mass of a neturon, ${M_1}$ be the mass of $_{10}N{e^{20}}$ nucleus Then','[{"text": "${M_1} = 10\\\\left( {{m_p} + {m_n}} \\\\right)$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${M_1} > 10\\\\left( {{m_p} + {m_n}} \\\\right)$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${M_1} = 20{m_n}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${M_1} < 10({m_p} + {m_n})$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Since the mass of nucleus is less than that the sum of masses of nucleons forming the nucleus $\\therefore {M_1} < 10{m_p} + 10{m_n}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45164,'physics',40,238,477,'If a cathod ray tube has a potential difference $V$ volt between the cathode and anode, then the speed $v$ of cathode rays is given by','[{"text": "$v \\\\propto \\\\sqrt V $", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$v \\\\propto {V^2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$v \\\\propto V$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$v \\\\propto {V^{ - 1}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'$qV = \\frac{1}{2}m{v^2}{\\rm{ or }}\\,v = \\sqrt {2qV/m} $<br>i.e., $v \\propto \\sqrt V $',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45166,'physics',41,404,802,'The difference between the mass of a nucleus and the combined mass of its nucleons is','[{"text": "Zero", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Positive", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Negative", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Zero, positive or negative", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Mass of a nucleus is always less than the 	mass of its constituent nucleons.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45167,'physics',39,1103,2630,'If the mass of neutron $ = 1.7 \\times {10^{ - 27}}\\,kg$. Then the de - broglie wavelength of neutron of energy $3\\,eV$ is:','[{"text": "$1.6 \\\\times {10^{ - 10}}\\\\,m$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1.6 \\\\times {10^{ - 11}}\\\\,m$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$1.4 \\\\times {10^{ - 10}}\\\\,m$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1.4 \\\\times {10^{ - 11}}\\\\,m$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'For a neutron, $m = 1.7 \\times {10^{ - 17}}\\,kg$<br>$\\begin{aligned}& \\lambda=\\dfrac{h}{\\sqrt{2 m E}} \\\\& \\lambda=\\dfrac{6.6 \\times 10^{-34}}{\\sqrt{2 \\times 1.7 \\times 10^{-29} \\times 3 \\times 1.6 \\times 10^{-19}}}\\end{aligned}$<br>$ = \\frac{{6.6 \\times {{10}^{ - 34}}}}{{{{10}^{ - 23}}\\sqrt {2 \\times 1.7 \\times 4.8} }}$<br>$ = \\frac{{6.6 \\times {{10}^{11}}}}{{\\sqrt {3.4 \\times 4.8} }} = 1.633 \\times {10^{11}}\\,m$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45170,'physics',41,403,800,'Atomic weight of  Boron is $10.81$ and it has two isotopes  ${}_5{B^{10}}$ and ${}_5{B^{11}}.$ Then the ratio of  isotopes in nature would be','[{"text": "$10:11$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$19:81$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$81:19$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$15:16$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'The number of ${}^{10}B$ type atoms $ = x$<br>And the number of ${}^{11}B$ type atoms $ = y$<br>Weight of ${}^{10}B$ type atoms $ = 10x$<br>Weight of  ${}^{11}B$ type atoms $ = 11y$<br>Total number of atoms $ = x + y$<br>$\\therefore $ Atomic weight $ = \\frac{{10x + 11y}}{{x + y}} = 10.81$<br>$ \\Rightarrow 10x + 11y = 10.81x + 10.81y$<br>$ \\Rightarrow 0.81x = 0.19y \\Rightarrow \\frac{x}{y} = \\frac{{19}}{{81}}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45171,'physics',39,1103,2630,'Wavelength associated with a photon having energy $4.14\\,eV$ is','[{"text": "$3000 \\\\mathop A^{~~\\\\circ} $", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$4000 \\\\mathop A^{~~\\\\circ} $", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$5000 \\\\mathop A^{~~\\\\circ} $", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$3800 \\\\mathop A^{~~\\\\circ} $", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'$\\lambda  = \\frac{{hc}}{E} = \\frac{{6.62 \\times {{10}^{ - 34}} \\times 3 \\times {{10}^8}}}{{4.14 \\times 1.6 \\times {{10}^{ - 19}}}}m$<br>$ = 3000\\mathop {{\\rm{ }}A}\\limits^{\\,\\,^\\circ } $',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45173,'physics',41,405,805,'Two nucleons are at a separation of $1\\,fm$. The net force between them is  ${F_1},$ if both are neutrons, ${F_2}$ if both are protons and  ${F_3}$ if one is a proton and the other is a neutron.','[{"text": "${F_2} > {F_1} > {F_3}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${F_1} > {F_2} > {F_3}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${F_1} = {F_2} > {F_3}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${F_1} = {F_3} > {F_2}$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Nuclear force of attraction between any two nucleons $\\left( {n - n,p - p,p - n} \\right)$ is same. The difference comes up only due to electrostatic force of repulsion between two protons.<br>$\\therefore \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{F_1} = {F_3} \\ne {F_2}$<br>Coulombic repulsion takes place between $p - p$<br>$\\therefore \\,\\,\\,{F_1} = {F_3} > {F_2}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45174,'physics',39,1103,2630,'The de Broglie wavelength of an electron having $80\\,eV$ of energy is nearly $\\left( {1\\,eV = 1.6 \\times {{10}^{ - 19}}\\,J} \\right.$, Mass of electron $ = 9 \\times {10^{ - 31}}\\,kg$, Plank\'s constant $ = 6.6 \\times {10^{ - 34}}\\left. {Js} \\right)$ (nearly)','[{"text": "$140 \\\\mathop A^{~~\\\\circ} $", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$0.14 \\\\mathop A^{~~\\\\circ} $", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$14 \\\\mathop A^{~~\\\\circ} $", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1.4 \\\\mathop A^{~~\\\\circ} $", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'$\\lambda=\\dfrac{h}{\\sqrt{2 m E}}$<br>$\\lambda  = \\frac{{6.6 \\times {{10}^{ - 34}}}}{{\\sqrt {2 \\times 9 \\times {{10}^{ - 31}} \\times 80 \\times 1.6 \\times {{10}^{ - 19}}} }}$ <br>$ = \\frac{{6.6 \\times {{10}^{ - 34}}}}{{16 \\times 3 \\times {{10}^{ - 25}}}}$ <br>$ = \\frac{{6.6}}{{16 \\times 3}} \\times {10^{ - 9}} = \\frac{{66}}{{16 \\times 3}}$ $ \\mathop A^{~~\\circ}  $<br>$ = 1.375$ $ \\mathop A^{~~\\circ}  $ $ \\approx 1.4$ $ \\mathop A^{~~\\circ}  $',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45175,'physics',40,245,487,'The classification of discrete energy levels in  atoms was first given experimentally by','[{"text": "Thomson’s experiment", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Millikan’s oil drop experiment", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Frank - Hertz experiment", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Leonard experiment", "isCorrect": false}]',2,NULL,NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45177,'physics',41,403,800,'Observe the following statements regarding isotones <br>I. $^{39}{K_{19}}$ and $^{40}C{a_{20}}$ are isotones <br>II. Nuclides having different atomic numbers $(z)$ and mass number $(A)$ but same number of neutrons $(n)$ are called Isotones <br>III. $^{19}{F_9}$ and $^{23}N{a_{11}}$ are isotones <br>The correct answer is','[{"text": "I, II and III are correct", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "only I and II are correct", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "only I and III are correct", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "only II and III are correct", "isCorrect": false}]',1,NULL,NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45178,'physics',39,1103,2630,'If de-Broglie wavelength associated with electron is $100 \\mathop A^{~~\\circ} $ its linear momentum (in $g\\,cm\\,{s^{ - 1}}$) is','[{"text": "$6.6 \\\\times 10^{-32}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$6.6 \\\\times 10^{-29}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$6.6 \\\\times 10^{-25}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$6.6 \\\\times 10^{-21}$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'$P = \\frac{h}{\\lambda } = \\frac{{6.6 \\times {{10}^{ - 34}}}}{{1 \\times {{10}^{ - 10}}}}$<br>$ = 6.6 \\times {10^{ - 24}}\\,kg\\,m{\\rm{/}}s$<br>$ = 6.6 \\times {10^{ - 26}} \\times 1000 \\times 100\\,g\\,cm{\\rm{/}}s$<br>$ = 6.6 \\times {10^{ - 21}}\\,g\\,cm{\\rm{/}}s.{\\rm{ }}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45181,'physics',41,403,800,'The nucleus of ${}_{56}B{a^{141}}$ contains','[{"text": "85 protons, 56 neutrons", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "55 protons, 86 neutrons", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "56 protons, 85 neutrons", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "86 protons, 55neutrons", "isCorrect": false}]',2,NULL,NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45182,'physics',39,1103,2630,'If $m$ is the mass of an electron and $c$ is the speed of light the ratio of the wavelengths of photon of energy $E$ to that of the electron of the same energy:','[{"text": "$c \\\\sqrt{\\\\dfrac{2 m}{E}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\sqrt{\\\\dfrac{2 m}{E}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\sqrt{\\\\dfrac{2 m}{c E}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\sqrt{\\\\dfrac{m}{E}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Energy of photon $E=h v=\\dfrac{h c}{\\lambda_{p}}$<br>$\\lambda_{p}=\\dfrac{h c}{E}$<br>Wavelength of electron $\\lambda_{e}=\\dfrac{h}{\\sqrt{2 m E}}$<br>$\\therefore \\dfrac{\\lambda_{p}}{\\lambda_{e}}=\\dfrac{h c}{E} \\times \\dfrac{\\sqrt{2 m E}}{h}=c \\sqrt{\\dfrac{2 m}{E}} \\text {. }$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45183,'physics',40,238,477,'According to classical theory, the path of electron in Rutherford  atomic model is','[{"text": "Straight line", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Spiral", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Circular", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Parabolic", "isCorrect": false}]',1,NULL,NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45185,'physics',41,403,801,'The radius of germanium $(Ge)$ nuclide is measured to be twice the radius of ${}_4B{e^9}$. The number of nucelons in $Ge$ are','[{"text": "$73$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$74$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$75$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$72$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Let radius of ${}_4B{e^9}$ nuclues be $r$. Then, radius of germanium $(Ge)$ nucleus will be $2r$. <br>Radius of nucleus is given by $R = {R_0}{A^{1/3}}$<br>$\\therefore \\frac{{{R_1}}}{{{R_2}}} = {\\left( {\\frac{{{A_1}}}{{{A_2}}}} \\right)^{1/3}} \\Rightarrow \\frac{r}{{2r}} = {\\left( {\\frac{9}{{{A_2}}}} \\right)^{1/3}}\\,$ $\\,\\,(\\because {A_1} = 9)$<br>$ \\Rightarrow {\\left( {\\frac{1}{2}} \\right)^3} = \\frac{9}{{{A_2}}} \\Rightarrow {A_2} = 72$<br>Thus, in germanium $(Ge)$ nucleus 72 nucleons are present.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45186,'physics',39,1103,2630,'A particle having a de Broglie wavelength of $1.0 \\mathop A^{~~\\circ} $ is associated with a momentum of (given $h = 6.6 \\times {10^{ - 34}}Js$)','[{"text": "$6.6 \\\\times {10^{ - 26}}\\\\,kg\\\\,m{\\\\rm{/}}s$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$6.6 \\\\times {10^{ - 25}}\\\\,kg\\\\,m{\\\\rm{/}}s$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$6.6 \\\\times {10^{ - 24}}\\\\,kg\\\\,m{\\\\rm{/}}s$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$6.6 \\\\times {10^{ - 22}}\\\\,kg\\\\,m{\\\\rm{/}}s$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'$P = \\frac{h}{\\lambda } = \\frac{{6.6 \\times {{10}^{ - 34}}}}{{1 \\times {{10}^{ - 10}}}}$<br>$ = 6.6 \\times {10^{ - 24}}\\,kg\\,m{\\rm{/}}s$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45188,'physics',41,403,800,'Match the following columns.<div class="matching-question"><div class="column-left">A. Isotopes<br>B. Isobars<br>C. Isotones<br>D. Nucleons</div><div class="column-right">P. Mass number same but different atomic number<br>Q. Atomic number same but different mass number<br>R. Number of neutrons plus number of protons<br>S. Number of neutrons same but different atomic number</div></div>','[{"text": "A - R B - P C - Q D - S", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "A - Q B - P C - S D - R", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "A - P B - Q C - R D - S", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "A - P B - R C - Q D - S", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Option (2) is correct',NULL,'Medium','Match the following',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45189,'physics',39,1103,2630,'The de-Broglie wavelength associated with a charged particle when it is accelerated through a potential difference of 150 volt is $1 \\mathop A^{~~\\circ} $. what will be the de-Broglie wavelength associated with the same particle when it is accelerated through a potential difference of $4500\\,V$?','[{"text": "$\\\\dfrac{1}{\\\\sqrt{3}}\\\\mathop A^{~~\\\\circ} $", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{1}{\\\\sqrt{30}}\\\\mathop A^{~~\\\\circ} $", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{1}{\\\\sqrt{300}} \\\\mathop A^{~~\\\\circ} $", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{1}{3} \\\\mathop A^{~~\\\\circ} $", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'As we know $\\lambda=\\dfrac{h}{\\sqrt{2 m k}}, K=e V_{0}$<br>$\\lambda \\propto \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{K}}, K \\propto V_{0}$<br>$\\lambda \\propto \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{V_{0}}}$<br>$\\dfrac{\\lambda_{2}}{\\lambda_{1}}=\\sqrt{\\dfrac{V_{1}}{V_{2}}} \\Rightarrow \\lambda_{2} \\sqrt{\\dfrac{150}{4500}} \\mathop A^{~~\\circ} =\\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{30}} \\mathop A^{~~\\circ} $.',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45190,'physics',40,238,477,'In scattering experiment the force that scatters particles is','[{"text": "Nuclear force", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Coulomb’s force", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both(1)and(2)", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Gravitational force", "isCorrect": false}]',1,NULL,NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45192,'physics',41,405,805,'The origin of nuclear force between nucleons is due to the exchange of','[{"text": "Mesons", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Photons", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Positrons", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Electrons", "isCorrect": false}]',0,NULL,NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45193,'physics',39,1103,2630,'If the kinetic energy of an electron gets tripled then the de-Broglie wavelength associated with it changes by a factor:','[{"text": "$1 / 3$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\sqrt{3}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{1}{\\\\sqrt{3}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "3", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'For an electron $\\lambda=\\dfrac{h}{\\sqrt{2 m k}}$<br>$\\begin{aligned}& \\therefore \\lambda \\propto \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{k}} \\\\& \\therefore \\dfrac{\\lambda^{\\prime}}{\\lambda}=\\dfrac{\\sqrt{k}}{\\sqrt{3 k}} \\Rightarrow \\lambda^{\\prime}=\\dfrac{\\lambda}{\\sqrt{3}} .\\end{aligned}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45194,'physics',40,238,477,'The angular  momentum of the  $\\alpha $-particles which are scattered through large angles by the heavier nuclei is conserved because of the','[{"text": "Nature of repulsive forces", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Conservation of kinetic energy", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Conservation of potential energy", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "There is no external torque", "isCorrect": true}]',3,NULL,NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45196,'physics',41,403,801,'The radius of $_{13}^{27}Al$ is 3.6 Fermi. Find the radius of $_{52}^{125}Te$ nucleus.','[{"text": "8 Fermi", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "6 Fermi", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "5 Fermi", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "4 Fermi", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'$\\frac{{{R_1}}}{{{R_2}}} = \\frac{{A_1^{1/3}}}{{A_2^{1/3}}} \\Rightarrow {R_2} = {\\left( {\\frac{{125}}{{27}}} \\right)^{1/3}} \\times 3.6 = 6\\,{\\rm{Fermi}}{\\rm{.}}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45211,'physics',41,404,803,'The curve of binding energy per nucleon as a function of atomic mass number has a sharp peak for helium nucleus. This implies that helium','[{"text": "Is very stable", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Can easily be broken up", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Is radioactive", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Can be used as fissionable material", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'The elements with large value of  $B$.$E$ versus mass number plot are very tightly bound and hence, are stable. And the elements with low value of $B$.$E$ versus mass number are less tightly bound and hence, are unstable.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45197,'physics',39,1102,2628,'In photoelectric experiment the stopping potential was measured to be \' $V_{1}$ \' and \' $V_{2}$ \' volt with incident light of wavelength \' $\\lambda$ \' and $\\dfrac{\\lambda}{2}$ respectively. The value of $V_{2}$ is ( $\\phi_{0}=$ work functionn, $e=$ electronic charge)','[{"text": "$2 V_{1}+\\\\dfrac{\\\\phi_{0}}{e}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$V_{1}-\\\\dfrac{2 \\\\phi_{0}}{e}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$V_{1}+\\\\dfrac{2 \\\\phi_{0}}{e}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2 V_{1}-\\\\dfrac{\\\\phi_{0}}{e}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Photoelectric equation is, $e V=h v-\\phi$<br>Here, $\\phi$ work function<br>$\\dfrac{h c}{\\lambda}=e V+\\phi$<br>Case I : $\\frac{{hc}}{\\lambda } = e{V_1} + \\phi \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\left( 1 \\right)$<br>Case II $: \\dfrac{h c}{\\dfrac{\\lambda}{2}}=e V_{2}+\\phi$<br>where ${\\lambda _2} = \\frac{\\lambda }{2}$<br>$\\frac{{2hc}}{\\lambda } = e{V_2} + \\phi \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\left( 2 \\right)$<br>$\\Rightarrow 2 e V_{1}+2 \\phi=e V_{2}+\\phi \\Rightarrow 2 e V_{1}+\\phi=e V_{2}$<br>$\\Rightarrow 2 V_{1}+\\dfrac{\\phi}{e}=V_{2}$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',2022,'MHTCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45200,'physics',41,403,801,'The radius of the nucleus is proportional to, (if $A$ is the atomic mass number)','[{"text": "$A$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${A^3}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${A^{1/3}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${A^{2/3}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'$R = {R_0}{A^{1/3}}$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45201,'physics',39,1102,2629,'Light of two different frequencies whose photons have energies $1.3\\,eV$ and $2.8\\,eV$ respectively, successfully illumminate a metallic surface whose work function is $0.8\\,eV$. the ratio of maximum speeds of emitted electrons will be','[{"text": "$1: 2$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$1: 5$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1: 4$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1: 3$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'For maximum speed of photo-electrons,<br>$\\dfrac{1}{2} m v^{2}=E_{\\text {photon }}-\\phi$<br>Here, $\\phi  = 0.8\\,eV$<br>For first photon, we have<br>Energy of photons, ${E_1} = 1.3\\,eV$<br>$ \\Rightarrow \\frac{1}{2}mv_1^2 = 1.3\\,eV - 0.8\\,eV$<br>$ \\Rightarrow \\frac{1}{2}mv_1^2 = 0.5{\\mkern 1mu} \\,eV\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\left( 1 \\right)$<br>Energy of photons, ${E_2} = 2.8\\,eV$<br>$ \\Rightarrow \\frac{1}{2}mv_2^2 = 2.8{\\mkern 1mu} eV - 0.8{\\mkern 1mu} eV = 2{\\mkern 1mu} eV\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\left( 2 \\right)$<br>Then from equation (1) and (2), we have<br>$\\dfrac{v_{1}^{2}}{v_{2}{ }^{2}}=\\dfrac{0.5 \\mathrm{eV}}{2 \\mathrm{eV}}=\\dfrac{1}{4} ; \\dfrac{v_{1}}{v_{2}}=\\dfrac{1}{2}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2022,'MHTCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45203,'physics',41,405,805,'The strong interaction exists in','[{"text": "Gravitational forces", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Electrostatic force of attraction", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Nuclear forces", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Magnetic force on a moving charge", "isCorrect": false}]',2,NULL,NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45204,'physics',39,1102,2627,'For a given photosensitive material and frequency ( \\(>\\) threshold frequency) of incident radiation, the photoelectric current varies with the intensity of incident light as<div class="matching-question"></div>','[{"text": "", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'For a given photosensitive material and with a source of constant frequency  $v\\left( {v > {v_{\\text{0}}}} \\right)$  of incident radiation, the photo current increases with increases in intensity of incident light.<br>$ \\Rightarrow I\\,\\alpha \\,i$<br>$ \\Rightarrow $ Graph as in (1)',NULL,'Medium','Graph/Figure',2021,'MHTCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45207,'physics',41,406,809,'Three $\\alpha {\\rm{ - }}$particle and one  $\\beta {\\rm{ - }}$ particle decaying takes place in series from an isotope $_{88}R{a^{236}}.$ Finally the isotope obtained will be','[{"text": "${}_{86}{X^{222}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${}_{83}{X^{224}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${}_{83}{X^{215}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${}_{84}{X^{220}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'${}_{88}^{236}Ra \\to {}_x^yX + 3{}_2^4\\alpha  + {}_{ - 1}^0\\beta $<br>By equating the atomic and mass numbers.<br>$88 = x + 6 - 1 \\Rightarrow x = 83$<br>$236 = y + 12 \\Rightarrow y = 224$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45208,'physics',39,1103,2630,'The graph between the energy \\(\\log E\\) of an electron and its de - Broglie wavelength \\(\\log \\lambda\\) will be<div class="matching-question"></div>','[{"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'As we know that, wavelength of a particle<br>$\\begin{aligned}& \\lambda=\\dfrac{h}{\\sqrt{2 m E_{K}}}=\\dfrac{h}{\\sqrt{2 m}} \\cdot \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{E_{K}}} \\\\& \\Rightarrow \\log \\lambda=\\log \\left(\\dfrac{h}{\\sqrt{2 m}} \\cdot \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{E_{K}}}\\right) \\\\& \\Rightarrow \\log \\lambda=\\log \\dfrac{h}{\\sqrt{2 m}}+\\log \\dfrac{1}{E_{K}^{1 / 2}} \\\\& \\Rightarrow \\log \\lambda=\\log \\left(\\dfrac{h}{\\sqrt{2 m}}\\right)-\\dfrac{1}{2} \\log E_{K}\\end{aligned}$<br>$\\Rightarrow \\log \\lambda=-\\dfrac{1}{2} \\log E_{K}+\\log \\dfrac{h}{\\sqrt{2 m}}$<br>Comparing the above equation with the equation of straight line. i.e.<br>$y=m x+c$.<br>Here, $E_{K}=E$<br>We can conclude that, $\\log \\lambda$ varies linearly with $\\log E$, with slope ($-1 / 2$ ). It is correctly shown in graph (3).',NULL,'Medium','Graph/Figure',2018,'AIIMS','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45212,'physics',39,1103,2630,'Photon and electron are given same energy $\\left( {{{10}^{ - 20}}\\,J} \\right)$. Wavelength associated with photon and electron are $\\lambda_{p}$ and $\\lambda_{e}$, the correct statement will be','[{"text": "$\\\\lambda_{p}>\\\\lambda_{e}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\lambda_{p} < \\\\lambda_{e}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\lambda_{p}=\\\\lambda_{e}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{\\\\lambda_{e}}{\\\\lambda_{p}}=c$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'We have, $\\lambda_{p}=h c / E ; E=$ energy given to photon<br>$\\lambda_{e}=\\dfrac{h}{\\sqrt{2 M_{e} E}}$<br>$E=$ energy given to electron<br>$\\Rightarrow \\dfrac{\\lambda_{p}}{\\lambda_{c}}=c \\sqrt{\\dfrac{2 M_{e}}{E}}>1 \\Rightarrow \\lambda_{p}>\\lambda_{e}$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',2012,'AIIMS','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45215,'physics',41,404,803,'The total binding energy of $_1{H^2}{,_2}H{e^4}{,_{25}}F{e^{56}}\\,and{\\,_{92}}{U^{235}}$ nuclei are $2.22,28.3,492\\,and\\,786\\,MeV$ respectively. Identify the most stable nucleus of the following:','[{"text": "$_{28}F{e^{56}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$_1{H^2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$_{92}{U^{238}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$_2H{e^4}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Binding energy per nucleon<br>For $_1{H^2},\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{E_1} = \\frac{{2.22}}{2} = 1.11MeV$<br>For ${\\,_2}H{e^4},\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{E_2} = \\frac{{28.3}}{4} = 7.08MeV$<br>For ${\\,_{28}}F{e^{56}},\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{E_3} = \\frac{{492}}{{56}} = 8.80MeV$<br>For ${\\,_{92}}{U^{235}},\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{E_4} = \\frac{{786}}{{235}} = 3.35MeV$<br>Hence, it is obvious that ${}_{28}F{e^{56}}$ is most stable.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45216,'physics',39,1103,2630,'An electron of mass $m$ when accelerated through a potential difference $V$ has de Broglie wavelength $\\lambda$. The de - Broglie wavelength associated with a proton of mass $M$ accelerated through the same potential difference will be.','[{"text": "$\\\\lambda \\\\dfrac{m}{M}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\lambda \\\\sqrt{\\\\dfrac{m}{M}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\lambda \\\\dfrac{M}{m}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\lambda \\\\sqrt{\\\\dfrac{M}{m}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'As, $\\lambda=\\dfrac{h}{\\sqrt{2 m E}}$<br>$\\Rightarrow \\lambda \\propto \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{m}} \\Rightarrow \\dfrac{\\lambda_{1}}{\\lambda_{2}}=\\sqrt{\\dfrac{m_{2}}{m_{1}}}$<br>So, $\\dfrac{\\lambda}{\\lambda_{2}}=\\sqrt{\\dfrac{M}{m}}$<br>$\\therefore \\lambda_{2}=\\lambda \\sqrt{\\left(\\dfrac{m}{M}\\right)}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2010,'AIIMS','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45217,'physics',37,975,2276,'In a series $L C R$ circuit, the inductance $L$ is 10 $m H$, capacitance $C$ is $1 \\mu F$ and resistance $R\\,{\\rm{is}}\\,100\\Omega $. The frequency at which resonance occurs is','[{"text": "$15.9\\\\,kHz$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1.59\\\\,rad/s$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1.59\\\\,kHz$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$15.9\\\\,rad/s$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'At resonance the two reactances are equal<br>$X_{L}=X_{C}$.<br>$\\Rightarrow \\omega L=\\dfrac{1}{\\omega C}$<br>$\\Rightarrow \\omega=\\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{L C}}$<br>$\\Rightarrow f=\\dfrac{1}{2 \\pi \\sqrt{L C}}$<br>$ \\Rightarrow f = \\frac{1}{{2\\pi \\sqrt {10 \\times {{10}^{ - 3}} \\times {{10}^{ - 6}}} }} = 1.59\\,kHz$<br>Correct option is (3).',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2023,'NEET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45218,'physics',41,406,809,'During ${\\beta ^ - }$ - decay, a neutron inside nucleus converts into proton, electron and $X$. The particle $X$ is','[{"text": "${\\\\pi ^ + }$- meson", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Neutrino", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Anti-neutrino", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${\\\\pi ^ - }$- meson", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'The neutron decay is<br>${}_0^1n \\to {}_1^1P + {}_{ - 1}^0e + \\bar v$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45219,'physics',39,1103,2630,'A particle of mass $M$ at rest decays into two particles of masses $m_{1}$ and $m_{2}$ having non zero velocities. The ratio of the de - Broglie wavelengths of the particles $\\lambda_{1} / \\lambda_{2}$ is','[{"text": "$m_{1} / m_{2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$m_{2} / m_{1}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "1", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\sqrt{m_{2}} / \\\\sqrt{m_{1}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'As no external force acts on system momentum of system remains conserved<br>$0=m_{1} v_{1}-m_{2} v_{2}$<br>$m_{1} v_{1}=m_{2} v_{2} \\Rightarrow\\left(\\dfrac{m_{2} v_{2}}{m_{1} v_{1}}\\right)=1$<br>$\\dfrac{\\lambda_{1}}{\\lambda_{2}}=\\dfrac{\\dfrac{h}{m_{1} v_{1}}}{\\dfrac{h}{m_{2} v_{2}}}=1$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2006,'AIIMS','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45221,'physics',37,977,2770,'$A{\\rm{ }}12\\,V,60\\,W$ lamp is connected to the secondary of a step down transformer, whose primary is connected to $ac$ mains of $220\\,V$. Assuming the transformer to be ideal, what is the current in the primary winding?','[{"text": "$2.7\\\\,A$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$3.7\\\\,A$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$0.37\\\\,A$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$0.27\\\\,A$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'For ideal transformer<br>${V_S}{I_S} = {V_P}{I_P}$<br>$ \\Rightarrow {P_{{\\rm{out }}}} = {P_{{\\rm{in }}}}$<br>$ \\Rightarrow 60 = 220 \\times {I_P}$<br>$ \\Rightarrow {I_P} = \\frac{{60}}{{220}} = 0.27\\,A$<br>Correct option is (4).',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2023,'NEET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45222,'physics',41,404,803,'Maximum value of binding energy per nucleon is','[{"text": "$8\\\\,MeV$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$8.8\\\\,MeV$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$7.6\\\\,MeV$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1.1\\\\,MeV$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,NULL,NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45223,'physics',39,1105,2632,'Davisson - Germer\'s electron diffraction arrangement is as shown','[{"text": "$A$-electron source, $B$-metal crystal, $C$-reflector, $D$-detector", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$A$-electron source, $B$-hollow tube, $C$-wall, D-reflector", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$A$-electron gun, $B$-electron accelerator, $C$-detector, $D$-counter", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$A$-electron gun, $B$-collimating and accelerating tube, $C$-metal target, $D$-movable collector", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'<img src=\'/img/data/physics/39_45223_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' /> So, correct option is (4)',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2007,'AIIMS','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/39_45223_question_1.png'),
(45226,'physics',37,973,2268,'The net impedance of circuit (as shown in figure) will be:','[{"text": "$15 \\\\Omega$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$5 \\\\sqrt{5} \\\\Omega$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$25 \\\\Omega$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$10 \\\\sqrt{2} \\\\Omega$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'$X_{L}=\\dfrac{50}{\\pi} \\times 10^{-3} \\times 2 \\pi \\times 50=5 \\Omega$<br>$X_{C}=\\dfrac{1}{2 \\pi \\times 50 \\times \\dfrac{10^{3}}{\\pi} \\times 10^{-6}}=10 \\Omega$<br>$Z=\\sqrt{R^{2}+\\left(X_{L}-X_{C}\\right)^{2}}$<br>$=\\sqrt{(10)^{2}+(5)^{2}}=5 \\sqrt{5} \\Omega$<br>Correct option is (2).',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2023,'NEET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/37_45226_question_1.png'),
(45227,'physics',39,1103,2630,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> The energy $(E)$ and momentum $(p)$ of a photon are related by $p=E / c$.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> The photon behaves like a particle.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'The energy (E) and momentum (p) of a photon are related by $p = E/c$. This assertion is correct.<br>The photon behaves like a particle. This reason is also correct. Photons are particles of light and electromagnetic radiation, and they exhibit both wave-like and particle-like behavior. They can be thought of as discrete packets of energy (quanta) and can interact with matter as if they were particles.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',2005,'AIIMS','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45229,'physics',41,404,803,'Let ${E_1}$ and ${E_2}$ be the binding energies of two nuclei $\\,A$ and $B$. It is observed that two nuclei of  $\\,A$ combine together to form a $B$ nucleus. This observation is correct only if','[{"text": "${E_1} > {E_2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${E_2} > {E_1}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${E_2} > 2{E_1}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${\\\\mathop{\\\\rm Nothing}\\\\nolimits} \\\\,can\\\\,be\\\\,said$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Nuclear reaction occurs only when the energy is released, which is possible only when ${E_2} > 2{E_1}$.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45230,'physics',39,1102,2626,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Photoelectric effect can take place only with an electron bound in the atom.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Electron is a fermion, whereas proton is boson.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'The photoelectric effect can take place only with an electron bound in the atom. This assertion is correct. The photoelectric effect involves the ejection of electrons (photoelectrons) from a material when it is exposed to light. These electrons are typically bound within the material, and it is their interaction with the incident photons of light that leads to the photoelectric effect.<br>An electron is a fermion, whereas a proton is a boson. This part of the reason is not directly relevant to the photoelectric effect. While the classification of particles as fermions or bosons is an important concept in quantum physics, it is not the determining factor for the occurrence of the photoelectric effect. The key factor is the interaction between the electrons (fermions) and the incident light.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',2017,'AIIMS','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45231,'physics',41,404,803,'When the number of nucleons in a  nucleus increases the binding energy per nucleon','[{"text": "Increase continuously with mass number", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Decreases continuously with mass number", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Remains constant with mass number", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "First increases and then decreases with increase in mass number", "isCorrect": true}]',3,NULL,NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45233,'physics',37,977,2770,'An ideal transformer has a turns ratio of 10 , when the primary is connected to $220\\,V,50\\,Hz\\,ac$ source, the power output is','[{"text": "equal to power input", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "zero", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "10 times the power input", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{1}{10}^{\\\\text {th }}$ the power input", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Transformer is stated to be ideal.<br>$\\Rightarrow$ Power constant.<br>$P_{\\text {in }}=P_{\\text {out }}$.<br>Correct option is (1).',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2023,'KECT','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45234,'physics',39,1102,3092,'If $5 \\%$ of the energy supplied to a bulb is irradiated as visible light, how many quanta are emitted per second by a $100{\\rm{ }}W$ lamp? (Assume, wavelength of visible light as ${5.6 \\times {{10}^{ - 5}}\\,cm}$)','[{"text": "$1.4 \\\\times 10^{19}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$3 \\\\times 10^{3}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1.4 \\\\times 10^{-19}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$3 \\\\times 10^{4}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Energy radiated as visible light<br>$ = \\frac{5}{{100}} \\times 100 = 5\\,J{s^{ - 1}}$<br>Let $n$ be the number of photons emitted per second. Then,<br>$n h v=E=5$<br>$\\therefore n = \\frac{{5\\lambda }}{{hc}} = \\frac{{5 \\times 5.6 \\times {{10}^{ - 7}}}}{{\\left( {6.62 \\times {{10}^{ - 34}}\\left( {3 \\times {{10}^8}} \\right)} \\right)}}$<br>$\\left[ {\\because E = \\frac{{hc}}{\\lambda }} \\right]$<br>$\\Rightarrow n=1.4 \\times 10^{19}$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',2011,'AIIMS','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45237,'physics',39,1102,2629,'Work function of nickel is $5.01\\,eV$. When ultraviolet radiation of wavelength $2000\\,\\mathop {{\\rm{ }}A}\\limits^{\\,\\,^\\circ } $ is incident on it, electrons are emitted. What will be the maximum velocity of emitted electrons?','[{"text": "$3 \\\\times {10^8}\\\\,m{s^{ - 1}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$6.46 \\\\times {10^5}\\\\,m{s^{ - 1}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$10.36 \\\\times {10^5}\\\\,m{s^{ - 1}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$8.54 \\\\times {10^6}\\\\,m{s^{ - 1}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Energy corresponding to $2000\\,\\mathop {{\\rm{ }}A}\\limits^{\\,\\,^\\circ } $<br>$E = \\frac{{12375}}{{\\lambda ({\\rm{in}}\\mathop {{\\rm{ }}A}\\limits^{\\,\\,^\\circ } )}} = \\frac{{12375}}{{2000}}eV = 6.2\\,eV$<br>Maximum kinetic energy<br>${\\left( {{E_K}} \\right)_{\\max }} = hv - h{v_0}$<br>$ \\Rightarrow K = E - W$<br>${\\left( {{E_K}} \\right)_{\\max }} = (6.2 - 5.01)\\,eV = 1.19\\,eV$<br>Now, $\\quad\\left(E_{K}\\right)_{\\max }=\\dfrac{1}{2} m v_{\\max }^{2}$<br>$\\Rightarrow 1.19 \\times 1.6 \\times 10^{-19}=1 / 2 \\times 9.1 \\times 10^{-31} \\times v_{\\max }^{2}$<br>$\\Rightarrow v_{\\max }^{2}=\\dfrac{1.19 \\times 1.6 \\times 10^{-19} \\times 2}{9.1 \\times 10^{-31}}$<br>$=0.418 \\times 10^{12}=41.8 \\times 10^{10}$<br>$ \\Rightarrow {v_{\\max }} = 6.46 \\times {10^5}\\,m{s^{ - 1}}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2011,'AIIMS','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45239,'physics',41,404,803,'In a nuclear reaction some mass converts into energy. In this reaction total $B$.$E$ of reactants when compared with that of products is','[{"text": "always greater", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "always less", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "either greater or less", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "always equal", "isCorrect": false}]',1,NULL,NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45240,'physics',37,975,2275,'Total impedance of a series \\(L C R\\) circuit varies with angular frequency of the \\(A C\\) source connected to it as shown in the graph. The quality factor \\(Q\\) of the series \\(L C R\\) circuit is<div class="matching-question"></div>','[{"text": "5", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "1", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "0.4", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "2.5", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'$Q = \\frac{{{\\omega _L} - {\\omega _c}}}{{{\\omega _0}}}$<br>$=\\left(\\dfrac{600-400}{500}\\right)=0.4 \\text {. }$<br>Correct option is (3).',NULL,'Easy','Graph/Figure',2023,'KECT','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/37_45240_question_1.png'),
(45241,'physics',39,1102,2629,'The work functions for metals $A, B$ and $C$ are respectively, $1.92\\,eV,2\\,eV$ and $5\\,eV$. According to Einstein\'s equation, the metals which will emit photoelectrons for a radiation of wavelength $4100$ $ \\mathop A^{~~\\circ}  $ is/are','[{"text": "Only $A$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Only $C$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$A$ and $B$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "None of these", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Energy incident by the radiation of wavelength $4000\\,\\mathop {{\\rm{ }}A}\\limits^{\\,\\,^\\circ } $ is<br>$E = \\frac{{hc}}{\\lambda } = \\frac{{6.67 \\times {{10}^{ - 34}} \\times 3 \\times {{10}^8}}}{{4100 \\times {{10}^{ - 10}}}}$<br>$ = 4.8 \\times {10^{ - 19}}\\,J$<br>$ = \\frac{{4.8 \\times {{10}^{ - 19}}}}{{1.6 \\times {{10}^{ - 19}}}}eV = 3eV$<br>Now, we have ${W_A} = 1.92\\,eV$,<br>${W_B} = 2\\,eV$ and ${W_C} = 5\\,eV$<br>Since, $W_{A} < E$ and $W_{B} < E$<br>Hence, $A$ and $B$ will emit photoelectrons.<br> Metal $C$ will emit electrons. So, correct option is (2)',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2011,'AIIMS','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45243,'physics',41,404,802,'One microgram of matter converted into energy will be given','[{"text": "$9 \\\\times {10^3}J$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$90J$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$9 \\\\times {10^5}J$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$9 \\\\times {10^7}J$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'$E = \\Delta m{c^2} = {10^{ - 9}} \\times {(3 \\times {10^8})^2} = 9 \\times {10^7}J$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45244,'physics',37,972,2260,'An $A C$ ammeter is used to measure current in a circuit. When a given direct current passes through the circuit, the $A C$ ammeter reads $3 A$. When another alternating current passes through the circuit, the $A C$ ammeter reads $4 A$. Then, the reading of this ammeter, if $D C$ and $A C$ flow through the circuit simultaneously, is','[{"text": "$3\\\\,A$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$4\\\\,A$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$7\\\\,A$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$5\\\\,A$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Quantity of heat liberated in the ammeter of resistance $R$<br>$\\left( {I} \\right)$ due to direct current of $3\\,A$<br>$=\\left[(3)^{2} R / J\\right]$<br>$\\left( {II} \\right)$ due to alternating current of $4\\,A$<br>$=\\left[(4)^{2} R / J\\right]$<br>$\\therefore$ Total heat produced per second<br>$=\\dfrac{(3)^{2} R}{J}+\\dfrac{(4)^{2} R}{J}=\\dfrac{25 R}{J}$<br>Let the equivalent alternating current be $I_{r m}$ then<br>$\\frac{{I_{rms}^2R}}{J} = \\frac{{25R}}{J} \\Rightarrow {I_{rms}} = 5\\,A$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2011,'AIIMS','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45245,'physics',39,1102,2628,'Light of wavelength $330\\,nm$ falling on piece of metal ejects electrons with sufficient energy which requires voltage $V_{0}$ to prevent them from reaching a collector. In the same set up, light of wavelength $220\\,nm$, ejects electrons which required twice the voltage $V_{0}$ to stop them in reaching a collector. The numerical value of voltage $V_{0}$ is','[{"text": "$\\\\frac{{16}}{{15}}\\\\,V$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{{15}}{{16}}\\\\,V$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{{15}}{{8}}\\\\,V$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{{8}}{{15}}\\\\,V$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Let $W$ be the work function of metal, then $e V_{0}=\\dfrac{h c}{330 \\times 10^{-9}}-W$<br>and $e\\left(2 V_{0}\\right)=\\dfrac{h c}{220 \\times 10^{-9}}-W$<br>Solving these two equations, we get<br>${V_0} = \\frac{{{{10}^9} \\times h \\times c}}{{110 \\times e \\times 6}}$<br>$ = \\frac{{{{10}^9} \\times 6.6 \\times {{10}^{ - 34}} \\times 3 \\times {{10}^8}}}{{110 \\times 1.6 \\times {{10}^{ - 19}} \\times 6}} = \\frac{{15}}{8}\\,V$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',2009,'AIIMS','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(48381,'biology',101,574,1153,'Emergency contraceptives could be used to avoid possible pregnancy due to','[{"text": "MTP", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Rape", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Casual unprotected intercourse", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both 2 and 3", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Emergency contraceptives could be used to avoid possible pregnancy due to<br>(2) Rape (3) Casual unprotected intercourse',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45246,'physics',41,407,811,'A heavy nucleus at rest breaks into two fragments which fly off with velocities in the ratio 8:1. The ratio of radii of the fragments is','[{"text": "$1:4$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1:2$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$2:1$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$4:1$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Let  ${m_1}$ and ${m_2}$are the masses of two fragments. <br>By conservation of momentum ${m_1}{v_1} = {m_2}{v_2}$<br>$ \\Rightarrow \\frac{{{v_1}}}{{{v_2}}} = \\frac{8}{1} = \\frac{{{m_2}}}{{{m_1}}} = \\frac{{m{A_2}}}{{m{A_1}}} = \\frac{{{A_2}}}{{{A_1}}}$                      (1)<br>Where $m$ is the mass of each nucleon.<br>Also from $r \\propto {A^{1/3}} \\Rightarrow \\frac{{{r_1}}}{{{r_2}}} = {\\left( {\\frac{{{A_1}}}{{{A_2}}}} \\right)^{1/3}} = {\\left( {\\frac{1}{8}} \\right)^{1/3}} = \\frac{1}{2}.$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45248,'physics',37,974,2766,'An inductor $20\\,mH$, a capacitor $100\\,\\mu F$ and a resistor $50\\,\\Omega $ are connected in series across a source of $emf\\,V = 10\\sin 314\\,t$. The power loss in the circuit is','[{"text": "$2.74\\\\,W$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$0.43\\\\,W$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$0.79\\\\,W$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$1.13\\\\,W$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Given, inductance, $L = 20\\,mH$<br>$ = 20 \\times {10^{ - 3}}H$<br>Capacitance, $C = 100\\mu F = 100 \\times {10^{ - 6}}\\,F$<br>Resistance, $R = 50\\,\\Omega $<br>$emf,{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} V = 10\\sin 314{\\mkern 1mu} t\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\left( 1 \\right)$<br>$\\because$ The general equation of emf is given as<br>$V = {V_0}\\sin \\omega t\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\left( 2 \\right)$<br>On comparing Eqs. (1) and (2), we get<br>${V_0} = 10\\,V$<br>$\\omega  = 314\\,rad{s^{ - 1}}$<br>The power loss associated with the g iven $AC$ circuit is given as<br>$P = {V_{rms}}{I_{rms}}\\cos \\phi $<br>$ = {V_{rms}}\\left( {\\frac{{{V_{rms}}}}{Z}} \\right)\\left( {\\frac{R}{Z}} \\right)$<br>$ = {\\left( {\\frac{{{V_{rms}}}}{Z}} \\right)^2}R = {\\left( {\\frac{{{V_0}}}{{\\sqrt 2  \\cdot Z}}} \\right)^2}R\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\left( 1 \\right)$<br>$\\therefore $ Impedence, $Z = \\sqrt {{R^2} + {{\\left( {{X_L} - {X_C}} \\right)}^2}} $<br>$ = \\sqrt {{R^2} + {{\\left( {\\omega L - \\frac{1}{{\\omega C}}} \\right)}^2}} $<br>$\\therefore $ Substituting the given values in the above equation, we get<br>$ = \\sqrt {{{\\left( {50} \\right)}^2} + {{\\left[ \\begin{array}{l}\n\\left( {314 \\times 20 \\times {{10}^{ - 3}}} \\right) - \\\\\n\\frac{1}{{314 \\times {{10}^{ - 4}}}}\n\\end{array} \\right]}^2}} $<br>$ = \\sqrt {2500 + {{\\left[ {6280 \\times {{10}^{ - 3}} - 0.00318 \\times {{10}^4}} \\right]}^2}} $<br>$ = \\sqrt {2500 + {{\\left( {25.56} \\right)}^2}} $<br>$ = 56.15\\Omega  \\simeq 56\\Omega $<br>Now, substituting the values in Eq. ,(1) we get<br>$P = {\\left( {\\frac{{10}}{{\\sqrt 2  \\times 56}}} \\right)^2} \\times 50 = \\frac{{100}}{{2 \\times 3136}} \\times 50$<br>$ = 0.79\\,W$<br>Thus, the power loss in the circuit is $0.79\\,W.$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',2018,'AIIMS','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45249,'physics',39,1104,2631,'The curve drawn between velocity and frequency of photon in vacuum will be a','[{"text": "Straight line parallel to frequency axis", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Straight line parallel to velocity axis", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Straight line passing through origin and making an angle of $45^{\\\\circ}$ with frequency axis", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Hyperbola", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'The curve drawn between velocity $(v)$ and frequency $(f)$ of photon in vacuum will be a straight line parallel to frequency axis as velocity of photon in a vacuum is constant and it is independent of frequency.  <img src=\'/img/data/physics/39_45249_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2009,'AIIMS','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45250,'physics',41,404,802,'The mass  defect in a particular nuclear reaction is $0.3g$. The amount of energy liberated (in $kWh$) is','[{"text": "$1.56 \\\\times {10^6}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2.5 \\\\times {10^6}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$3 \\\\times {10^6}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$7.5 \\\\times {10^6}$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'According to mass energy equivalence,<br>$E = \\Delta m{c^2} = \\frac{{0.3}}{{1000}} \\times {(3 \\times {10^8})^2} = 2.7 \\times {10^{13}}J$<br>$ = \\frac{{2.7 \\times {{10}^{13}}}}{{3.6 \\times {{10}^6}}} = 7.5 \\times {10^6}kWh$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45252,'physics',37,973,2265,'The figure shows three circuits with identical batteries, inductors and resistance. Rank the circuits according to the currents through the battery just after the switch is closed, greatest first.','[{"text": "$i_{2}>i_{3}>i_{1}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$i_{2}>i_{1}>i_{3}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$i_{1}>i_{2}>i_{3}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$i_{1}>i_{3}>i_{2}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'In circuit (1), on closing the switch, the current in the inductor is zero due to selfinduction,$i.e.$ $i_{1}=0$.<br>In circuit (2), on closing the switch, the current in the inductor is zero dut to self-induction.  <img src=\'/img/data/physics/37_45252_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />   Therefore, $i_{2}=i=\\dfrac{E}{R_{1}}$<br>In circuit (3), on closing the switch, the current in the inductor is again zero due to the same reason. <img src=\'/img/data/physics/37_45252_explanation_2.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />   Therefore, $i_{3}=i=\\dfrac{E}{R_{1}+R_{2}}$<br>Thus, it is obvious that, $i_{2}>i_{3}>i_{1}(=0)$.',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',2007,'AIIMS','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/37_45252_question_1.png'),
(45253,'physics',41,407,811,'A stationary Thorium nucleus $(A = 200,Z = 90)$ emits an alpha particle with kinetic energy. What is the kinetic energy of the recoiling nucleus?','[{"text": "$\\\\frac{{{E_\\\\alpha }}}{{108}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{{{E_\\\\alpha }}}{{110}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{{{E_\\\\alpha }}}{{55}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{{{E_\\\\alpha }}}{{54}}$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'The reaction can be represented by <br>$_{90}^{220}Th \\to _2^4He + _{88}^{216}Ra$<br>By conservation of momentum, <br>$ \\Rightarrow {m_\\alpha }{v_\\alpha } = {m_R}{v_R}$<br>$ \\Rightarrow \\frac{{{v_R}}}{{{v_\\alpha }}} = \\frac{{{m_\\alpha }}}{{{m_R}}} = \\frac{4}{{216}} = \\frac{1}{{54}}$<br>The kinetic energy of $Ra,\\,{E_R}$, is related to the kinetic energy of alpha particle ${E_\\alpha }$ by<br>$\\frac{{{E_R}}}{{{E_\\alpha }}} = \\frac{{\\frac{1}{2}{m_R}v_R^2}}{{\\frac{1}{2}{m_\\alpha }v_\\alpha ^2}} = \\left( {\\frac{{{m_R}}}{{{m_\\alpha }}}} \\right){\\left( {\\frac{{{v_R}}}{{{v_\\alpha }}}} \\right)^2} = \\left( {\\frac{{{m_R}}}{{{m_\\alpha }}}} \\right){\\left( {\\frac{{{m_\\alpha }}}{{{m_R}}}} \\right)^2}$<br>$ = \\frac{{{m_\\alpha }}}{{{m_R}}} = \\frac{1}{{54}}$<br>$\\therefore {E_R} = \\frac{{{E_\\alpha }}}{{54}}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45254,'physics',39,1102,2629,'Let $K_{1}$ be the maximum kinetic energy of photoelectrons emitted by light of wavelength $\\lambda_{1}$ and $K_{2}$ corresponding to wavelength $\\lambda_{2}$. If $\\lambda_{1}=2 \\lambda_{2}$, then','[{"text": "$2 K_{1}=K_{2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$K_{1}=2 K_{2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$K_{1} < K_{2} / 2$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$K_{1}>2 K_{2}$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'$k_{1} < k_{2} / 2$<br>$K \\cdot E=\\dfrac{P^{2}}{2 m}$<br>$\\begin{aligned}& P=\\sqrt{2 m k} \\quad P \\propto \\sqrt{k} \\\\& \\dfrac{h}{\\lambda} \\propto \\sqrt{k} \\quad \\lambda \\propto \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{k}} \\\\& \\dfrac{\\lambda_{1}}{\\lambda_{2}}=\\sqrt{\\dfrac{k_{2}}{k_{1}}} \\quad \\therefore \\lambda_{1}=2 \\lambda_{2} \\\\& \\therefore \\dfrac{2 \\lambda_{2}}{\\lambda_{1}}=\\sqrt{\\dfrac{k_{2}}{k_{1}}} \\\\& 4=\\dfrac{k_{2}}{k_{1}} \\\\& k_{1}=\\dfrac{k_{2}}{4} \\quad \\therefore k_{1} < \\dfrac{k_{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2009,'AIIMS','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45255,'physics',37,973,2267,'A capacitor of capacitance $1\\,\\mu F$ is charged to potential of $1\\,V$. It is connected in parallel to an inductor of inductance ${10^{ - 3}}H$. The maximum current that will flow in the circuit has the value.','[{"text": "$\\\\sqrt {1000} \\\\,mA$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$1\\\\,A$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1\\\\,mA$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1000\\\\,mA$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Charge on the capacitor,<br>${q_0} = CV = 1 \\times {10^{ - 6}} \\times 1 = {10^{ - 6}}C$<br>Here, $q=q_{0} \\sin \\omega t$<br>or $I_{0}=\\omega q_{0}=$ maximum current<br>Now, $\\omega=\\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{L C}}=\\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{10^{-9}}}=\\left(10^{9}\\right)^{1 / 2}$<br>$L = {10^{ - 3}}H,C = {10^{ - 6}}\\,F$<br>$\\therefore I_{0}=\\left(10^{9}\\right)^{1 / 2} \\times\\left(1 \\times 10^{-6}\\right)$<br>$ = \\sqrt {1000} \\,mA$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2010,'AIIMS','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45256,'physics',40,241,480,'The ionisation energy of $L{i^{2 + }}$ is equal to','[{"text": "$9hcR$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$6hcR$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2hcR$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$hcR$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'As, ionisation energy $ = Rch{Z^2}$<br>For lithium $Z = 3$<br>$\\therefore $ Ionisation energy $ = {(3)^2}Rch = 9Rch$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45257,'physics',41,404,802,'One requires energy ${E_n}$ to remove a nucleon from a nucleus and an energy  ${E_e}$ to remove an electron from the orbit of an atom. Then','[{"text": "${E_n} < {E_e}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${E_n} > {E_e}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${E_n} \\\\geqslant {E_e}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${E_n} = {E_0}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Nuclear force is stronger than Coulomb force. So energy required to remove a nucleon is more than energy required to remove an electron.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45258,'physics',39,1102,3092,'Energy from the sun is received on earth at the rate of $ = 2\\,cal\\,c{m^{ - 2}}\\,{\\rm{mi}}{{\\rm{n}}^{ - 1}}$. If average wavelength of solar light be taken as $5500\\,\\mathop {{\\rm{ }}A}\\limits^{\\,\\,^\\circ } $, then how many photons are received on the earth $\\left( {c{m^{ - 2}}\\,{\\rm{mi}}{{\\rm{n}}^{ - 1}}} \\right)$?<br>(Take, $h = 6.6 \\times {10^{ - 34}}\\,J - s,1\\,cal = 4.2\\,J$)','[{"text": "$1.5 \\\\times 10^{13}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2.9 \\\\times 10^{13}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2.3 \\\\times 10^{19}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$1.75 \\\\times 10^{19}$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Energy received from the sun<br>$ = 2\\,cal\\,c{m^{ - 2}}\\,{\\rm{mi}}{{\\rm{n}}^{ - 1}}$<br>$ = 8.4\\,{\\mkern 1mu} J - c{m^{ - 2}}\\,{\\rm{mi}}{{\\rm{n}}^{ - 1}}$<br>Energy of one photon received from sun,<br>$E = \\frac{{hc}}{\\lambda } = \\frac{{6.6 \\times {{10}^{ - 34}} \\times 3 \\times {{10}^8}}}{{5500 \\times {{10}^{ - 10}}}}$<br>$ = 3.6 \\times {10^{ - 19}}\\,J$<br>$\\therefore$ Number of photons reaching the earth per $c{m^2}$ per minute will be<br>$n=\\dfrac{\\text { Energy received from sun }}{\\text { Energy of one photon }}$<br>$n=\\dfrac{8.4}{3.6 \\times 10^{-19}}=2.3 \\times 10^{19}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2007,'AIIMS','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45259,'physics',37,973,2266,'A series $R-C$ circuit is connected to $A C$ voltage source. Consider two cases; $(A)$ When $C$ is without a dielectric medium and $(B)$ When $C$ is filled with dielectric of constant 4 . The current $I_{R}$ through the resistor and voltage $V_{C}$ across the capacitor are compared in two cases. Which of the following is true?','[{"text": "$I_{R}^{A}>I_{R}^{B}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$I_{R}^{A} < I_{R}^{B}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$V_{C}^{A} < V_{C}^{B}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "None of these", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'For circuit $A$,<br>Impedance, $Z_{A}=\\sqrt{R^{2}+\\dfrac{1}{\\omega^{2} C^{2}}}$<br>Current in circuit,<br>$I_R^A = \\frac{V}{{\\sqrt {{R^2} + \\left( {1/{\\omega ^2}{C^2}} \\right)} }}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\left( 1 \\right)$<br>Potential difference across $C$,<br>$V_{C}^{A}=I_{R}^{A} \\times \\dfrac{1}{\\omega C}=\\dfrac{V}{\\sqrt{(R \\omega C)^{2}+1}}$<br>For circuit $B$,<br>Impedance, $Z_{B}=\\sqrt{R^{2}+\\dfrac{1}{(4 \\omega)^{2} C^{2}}}$<br>Current in circuit,<br>$I_{R}^{B}=\\dfrac{V}{\\sqrt{R^{2}+\\left[1 /(4 \\omega C)^{2}\\right]}}$<br>Potential difference across $C$,<br>$V_C^B = I_R^B \\times \\frac{1}{{4\\omega C}} = \\frac{V}{{\\sqrt {{{(4R\\omega C)}^2} + 1} }}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\left( 2 \\right)$<br>Hence, from above Eqs. (1) and (2), we conclude that because numerator of $I_{R}^{B}$ is increased from $I_{R}^{A}$ by a factor of 2 .<br>So, $I_{R}^{B}>I_{R}^{A}$ and $V_{C}^{A}>V_{C}^{B}$.',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',2017,'AIIMS','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45260,'physics',41,403,800,'Outside a nucleus','[{"text": "Proton and neutron both are stable", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Neutron is stable", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Neither neutron nor proton is stable", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Neutron is unstable", "isCorrect": true}]',3,NULL,NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45261,'physics',39,1106,2633,'An $X$-ray pulse of wavelength $4.9\\,\\mathop {{\\rm{ }}A}\\limits^{\\,\\,^\\circ } $ is sent through a section of Wilson cloud chamber containing a super saturated gas and tracks of photoelectron ejected from the gaseous atoms are observed. Two groups of tracks of lengths $1.40\\,cm$ and $2.02\\,cm$ are noted. If the range energy relation for cloud chamber is given by $R=\\alpha E$ with $\\alpha  = 1\\,cmke{V^{ - 1}}$, obtain the binding energies of the two levels from which electrons are emitted.<br>(Take, $h = 6.63 \\times {10^{ - 34}}\\,J - s$,<br>$1eV = 1.6 \\times {10^{ - 19}}\\,J$)','[{"text": "$0.52\\\\,keV$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$0.75\\\\,eV$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$0.52\\\\,eV$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$0.75\\\\,keV$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Binding energy, $W=\\dfrac{h c}{\\lambda}-E$<br>Here, $\\lambda  = 4.9 \\times {10^{ - 10}}\\,m$,<br>${R_1} = 1.40\\,cm,{R_2} = 2.02\\,cm$,<br>Give $R=\\propto E$<br>$\\alpha  = 1\\,cm\\,ke{V^{ - 1}}$<br>For cloud chamber, the range-energy relation is<br>$R = \\alpha E{\\rm{ or }}E = \\frac{R}{\\alpha }$<br>$\\therefore {E_1} = \\frac{{{R_1}}}{\\alpha } = \\frac{{140}}{1} = 1.40{\\mkern 1mu} keV{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} \\left( 1 \\right)$<br>and ${E_2} = \\frac{{{R_2}}}{\\alpha } = \\frac{{2.02}}{1} = 2.02{\\mkern 1mu} \\,keV{\\mkern 1mu} \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\left( 2 \\right)$<br>Energy of the incident photon, $h v$<br>$ = \\frac{{hc}}{\\lambda } = \\frac{{6.63 \\times {{10}^{ - 34}} \\times 3 \\times {{10}^8}}}{{4.9 \\times {{10}^{ - 10}}}}\\,J$<br>$ = \\frac{{6.63 \\times 3 \\times {{10}^{ - 16}}}}{{4.9 \\times 1.6 \\times {{10}^{ - 19}}}}eV$<br>$ = 2.54\\,{\\mkern 1mu} keV{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} \\left( 3 \\right)$<br>From Einstein\'s photoelectric equation,<br>$h v=W+E$<br>$\\therefore$ Binding energy, $W=h v-E$<br>From Eqs. (1),(2) and (3), we get<br>${W_1} = 2.54 - 1.40 = 1.14\\,keV$<br>and ${W_2} = 2.54 - 2.02 = 0.52\\,keV$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',2007,'AIIMS','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45262,'physics',37,973,2266,'In a series $R-C$ circuit shown in figure, the applied voltage is $10\\,V$ and the voltage across capacitor is found to be $8\\,V$. Then, the voltage across $R$ and the phase difference between current and the applied voltage will respectively be','[{"text": "$6\\\\,V,{\\\\tan ^{ - 1}}\\\\left( {\\\\frac{4}{3}} \\\\right)$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$3\\\\,V,{\\\\tan ^{ - 1}}\\\\left( {\\\\frac{3}{4}} \\\\right)$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$6\\\\,V,{\\\\tan ^{ - 1}}\\\\left( {\\\\frac{5}{3}} \\\\right)$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "None of these", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'We know that, for series $R-C$ circuit<br>$V^{2}=V_{C}^{2}+V_{R}^{2}$  <img src=\'/img/data/physics/37_45262_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />   $100=64+V_{R}^{2}$<br>$ \\Rightarrow V_R^2 = 36 \\Rightarrow {V_R} = \\sqrt {36}  = 6\\,V$<br>Also, $\\tan \\phi=\\dfrac{V_{C}}{V_{R}} \\Rightarrow \\tan \\phi=\\dfrac{8}{6}$<br>$\\tan \\phi=\\dfrac{4}{3}$<br>$\\therefore \\phi=\\tan ^{-1}\\left(\\dfrac{4}{3}\\right)$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2018,'AIIMS','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/37_45262_question_1.png'),
(45263,'physics',40,240,479,'The shortest wavelenght of Paschen, Balmer and Lyman series are in the ratio:','[{"text": "$9:1:4$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1:4:9$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$9:4:1$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$1:9:4$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'As we know<br>$\\frac{1}{\\lambda } = R\\left[ {\\frac{1}{{n_1^2}} - \\frac{1}{{n_2^2}}} \\right]$ for shortest wavelength<br>${n_2} = \\infty $<br>Hence for Lyamn<br>$\\frac{1}{{{\\lambda _L}}} = R\\left[ {\\frac{1}{{{1^2}}} - \\frac{1}{{{\\infty ^2}}}} \\right] \\Rightarrow {\\lambda _L} = \\frac{1}{R}$<br>For Balmer<br>$\\frac{1}{{{\\lambda _B}}} = R\\left[ {\\frac{1}{{{2^2}}} - \\frac{1}{{{\\infty ^2}}}} \\right] \\Rightarrow {\\lambda _B} = \\frac{4}{R}$<br>For Paschen<br>$\\frac{1}{{{\\lambda _P}}} = R\\left[ {\\frac{1}{{{3^2}}} - \\frac{1}{{{\\infty ^2}}}} \\right] \\Rightarrow {\\lambda _P} = \\frac{9}{R}$<br>Hence, ratio<br>${\\lambda _P}:{\\lambda _B}:{\\lambda _L}::9:4:1$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45264,'physics',41,403,800,'Which of the following is most unstable?','[{"text": "Protons", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Electrons", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\alpha $ -particle", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Neutrons", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Neutrons are unstable and they decay by emitting an electron and antineutrino to become proton.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45265,'physics',39,1102,2626,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Photoelectric emission is an instantaneous process.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> There is no time lag between incidence of light and emission of photoelectron.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Photoelectric emission is an instantaneous process. This assertion is correct.<br>There is no time lag between the incidence of light and the emission of a photoelectron. This reason is also correct and provides the correct explanation for Assertion (A).<br>In the photoelectric effect, when a photon of light strikes a metal surface, it can instantaneously release a photoelectron without any time delay.<br>Therefore, option (1) is the correct choice.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',2006,'AIIMS','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45266,'physics',37,974,2274,'If maximum energy is stored in a capactior at $t=0$, then find the time after which current in the circuit will be maximum.','[{"text": "$\\\\frac{\\\\pi }{2}\\\\,ms$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{\\\\pi }{4}\\\\,ms$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\pi \\\\,ms$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2\\\\,ms$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Given, $L = 25\\,mH$,<br>$ = 25 \\times {10^{ - 3}}H$<br>$C = 10\\,\\mu F = {10^{ - 5}}\\,F$<br>If $T$ be the time period in $L-C$ oscillation, then<br>$T = 2\\pi \\sqrt {LC} $<br>$ = 2\\pi \\sqrt {25 \\times {{10}^{ - 3}} \\times {{10}^{ - 5}}} $<br>$ = \\pi  \\times {10^{ - 3}}\\,s = \\pi \\,ms$<br>Current in the circuit will be maximum when<br>$t=\\dfrac{T}{4}=\\dfrac{\\pi}{4} m s$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2019,'AIIMS','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/37_45266_question_1.png'),
(45267,'physics',41,404,802,'Mass defect of an atom refers to','[{"text": "Inaccurate measurement of mass of neutrons", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Mass annihilated to produce energy to bind  the nucleons", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Packing fraction", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Difference in the number of neutrons and protons in the nucleus", "isCorrect": false}]',1,NULL,NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45268,'physics',40,245,487,'If elements with principal quantum number $n > 4$ were not allowed in nature, then the number of possible elements would be','[{"text": "$32$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$60$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$18$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$4$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Number of elements in an orbit having principle quantum number $n$ is $2{n^2}$. The total number of elements having $n < 5$ are $2 + 8 + 18 + 32 = 60$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45269,'physics',39,1101,2625,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> The specific charge for positive rays is a characteristic constant.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> The specific charge depends on charge and mass of positive ions present in positive rays','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'The specific charge of a positive ion corresponding to one gas is fixed but it is different for different gases.<br>The specific charge depends on charge and mass of positive ions present in positive rays<br>So correct option is (2).',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',2010,'AIIMS','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45270,'physics',37,973,2268,'In an $L-C-R$ series circuit, source voltage is $120\\,V$ and voltage across inductor $50\\,V$ and that across resistance is $40\\,V$, then determine voltage across the capacitor. (Given current lags the source voltage).','[{"text": "$V_{C}=10(5-8 \\\\sqrt{2})$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$V_{C}=10(5+8 \\\\sqrt{2})$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$V_{C}=20(5+8 \\\\sqrt{2})$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$V_{C}=10(5+7 \\\\sqrt{2})$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Given, in $L-C-R$ series circuit,<br>Source voltage (resultant voltage), $V = 120\\,V$<br>Voltage across inductor, ${V_L} = 50\\,V$<br>Voltage across resistor, ${V_R} = 40\\,V$<br>In $L-C-R$ series circuit,<br>$V=\\sqrt{V_{R}^{2}+\\left(V_{L}-V_{C}\\right)^{2}}$<br>or $120=\\sqrt{40^{2}+\\left(50-V_{C}\\right)^{2}}$<br>$\\begin{aligned}& 120^{2}=40^{2}+\\left(50-V_{C}\\right)^{2} \\\\\\Rightarrow & \\left(50-V_{C}\\right)^{2}=12800\\end{aligned}$<br>$\\Rightarrow 50-V_{C}=80 \\sqrt{2}$<br>$\\Rightarrow V_{C}=10(5-8 \\sqrt{2})$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2019,'AIIMS','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45272,'physics',39,1102,2628,'From the photoelectric effect experiment, following observations are made. Identify which of these are correct.<br>A. The stopping potential depends only on the work function of the metal.<br>B. The saturation current increases as the intensity of incident light increases.<br>C. The maximum kinetic energy of a photo electron depends on the intensity of the incident light.<br>D. Photoelectric effect can be explained using wave theory of light<br>Choose the correct answer from the options given below','[{"text": "A, C, D only", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "B only", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "A, B, D only", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "B, C only", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'By Einstein\'s photoelectric equation.<br>$V_{0}=\\dfrac{h}{e} v-\\dfrac{\\phi}{e}$ Evidently $V_{0}$ depends upon  frequency $(v)$ of incident light and work function of target metal surface.<br>For incident light of given frequency, as intensity increases, more number of photons strikes the target metal surface per unit times. As a result more number of electrons are ejected per unit time which leads to increase in photo current.<br>As $(K . E)_{\\max }=h v-\\phi$, maximum kinetic energy of photo electrons depends on frequency $(v)$ of incident light. photo electic effect can be explained by particle theory of light only.',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',2023,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45273,'physics',40,240,479,'Atomic hydrogen is excited to the ${n^{th}}$ energy level.The maximum number of spectral lines which it can emit while returning to the ground state, is:','[{"text": "$\\\\frac{1}{2}n(n - 1)$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{1}{2}n(n + 1)$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$n(n - 1)$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$n(n + 1)$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,NULL,NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45274,'physics',37,973,2271,'When the $K$ is pressed at $t=0$ which of the following statements about the current $I$ in the resistor $A B$ of the given circuit is true?','[{"text": "$I = 2\\\\,mA$ at all $t$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "I oscilaltes between $1\\\\,mA$ and $2\\\\,mA$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$I = 1\\\\,mA$ at all $t$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "At $t = 0,I = 2\\\\,mA$ and with time, it goes to $1\\\\,mA$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'At time, $t=0$, i.e. when capacitor is charging,<br>Current, $I = \\frac{2}{{1000}} = 2\\,mA$<br>When capacitor is full charged, no current will pass through it, hence current through the circuit,<br>$I = \\frac{2}{{2000}} = 1\\,mA$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2008,'AIIMS','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/37_45274_question_1.png'),
(45275,'physics',41,403,801,'If the radius of a nucleus of mass number 3 is $R$, then the radius of a nucleus of mass number 81 is','[{"text": "$3R$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$9R$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${(27)^{1/2}}R$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$27R$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'$R\\,\\,\\alpha \\,\\,{A^{1/3}}$<br>$ \\Rightarrow \\frac{{{R_1}}}{{{R_2}}} = {\\left( {\\frac{{{A_1}}}{{{A_2}}}} \\right)^{1/3}} \\Rightarrow \\frac{{{R_1}}}{{{R_2}}} = {\\left( {\\frac{3}{{81}}} \\right)^{1/3}} \\Rightarrow \\frac{{{R_1}}}{{{R_2}}} = \\frac{1}{3}$<br>$\\therefore {R_2} = 3R = 3{R_1}$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45276,'physics',39,1102,2626,'Match Column I with Column II<div class="matching-question"><div class="column-left">A. Planck’s constant $(h)$<br>B. Stopping potential \n$({V_S})$<br>C. Work function $(\\phi )$<br>D. Momentum $(p)$</div><div class="column-right">P. $\\left[ {{M^1}{L^{ - 2}}{T^{ - 1}}} \\right]$<br>Q. $\\left[ {{M^1}{L^1}{T^{ - 1}}} \\right]$<br>R. $\\left[ {{M^1}{L^2}{T^{ - 1}}} \\right]$<br>S. $\\left[ {{M^1}{L^2}{T^{ - 3}}{A^{ - 1}}} \\right]$</div></div>','[{"text": "${\\\\rm{A}} - {\\\\rm{P,}}\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\rm{B}} - {\\\\rm{R,}}\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\rm{C}} - {\\\\rm{S,}}\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\rm{D}} - {\\\\rm{Q}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{A}} - {\\\\rm{R,}}\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\rm{B}} - {\\\\rm{S,}}\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\rm{C}} - {\\\\rm{P,}}\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\rm{D}} - {\\\\rm{Q}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{A}} - {\\\\rm{R,}}\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\rm{B}} - {\\\\rm{P,}}\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\rm{C}} - {\\\\rm{Q,}}\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\rm{D}} - {\\\\rm{S}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{A}} - {\\\\rm{Q,}}\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\rm{B}} - {\\\\rm{S,}}\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\rm{C}} - {\\\\rm{R,}}\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\rm{D}} - {\\\\rm{P}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'We know that $E=h v \\Rightarrow[E]=[h][v]$ <br>where  $E = $ energy of a photon.<br>$\\Rightarrow[h]=\\dfrac{[E]}{[v]}=\\dfrac{\\left[M L^{2} T^{-2}\\right]}{[1 / T]}=\\left[M L^{2} T^{-1}\\right]$<br>$\\Rightarrow[A-R]$<br>$V=$ Potential $\\dfrac{\\text { work done }}{\\text { charge moved }}=\\dfrac{W}{Q}$<br>$\\Rightarrow[V]=\\dfrac{[W]}{[Q]}=\\dfrac{\\left[M L^{2} T^{-2}\\right]}{[A T]}$<br>$ = \\left[ {M{L^2}\\,{T^{ - 3}}\\,{A^{ - 1}}} \\right]$<br>$\\Rightarrow[B-S]$<br>Work function $\\left(W_{0}\\right.$ or $\\phi$) is the energy requried to remove one electron from the surface of a metal.<br>$\\therefore [\\phi ] = [E] = \\left[ {M{L^2}\\,{T^{ - 2}}} \\right]$<br>$\\Rightarrow[C-P]$<br>Momentum, $p=m v$<br>$[p] = [m][v] = ML{T^{ - 1}}$<br>$\\Rightarrow[D-Q]$<br>So option (2) is correct.',NULL,'Hard','Match the following',2023,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45277,'physics',37,977,2770,'A transformer with turns ratio $\\dfrac{N_{1}}{N_{2}}=\\dfrac{50}{1}$ is connected to a $120\\,V\\,AC$ supply. If primary and secondary circuit resistance are $1.5\\,k\\,\\Omega $ and $1\\,\\Omega $, then find out power of output.','[{"text": "$5.76\\\\,W$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$11.4\\\\,W$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2.89\\\\,W$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$7.56\\\\,W$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Given, turn ratio of a transformer,<br>$\\dfrac{N_{1}}{N_{2}}=\\dfrac{50}{1}$<br>$\\Rightarrow N_{1}=50 N_{2}$<br>Since, $\\dfrac{N_{1}}{N_{2}}=\\dfrac{V_{1}}{V_{2}}$<br>$50 = \\frac{{120}}{{{V_2}}}\\left[ {{V_1} = 120\\,V(} \\right.$given$\\left.)\\right]$<br>$ \\Rightarrow {V_2} = \\frac{{12}}{5}\\,V$<br>Output power at secondary coil,<br>$P_{s}=\\dfrac{V_{2}^{2}}{R_{2}} \\quad\\left[R_{2}=1 \\Omega(\\right.$ given $\\left.)\\right]$<br>$=\\dfrac{\\left(\\dfrac{12}{5}\\right)^{2}}{1}=\\dfrac{144}{25}$<br>$ = 5.76\\,W$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',2019,'AIIMS','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45278,'physics',40,241,480,'Which energy state of the triply ionised beryllium has the same electron orbital radius as that of ground state of hydrogen? Given $Z$ for $Be{\\rm{ }} = {\\rm{ }}4.$','[{"text": "$n = 1$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$n = 2$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$n = 3$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$n = 4$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'For an atom of atomic number $Z$, radius of ${n^{th}}$ orbit is given by<br>${r_n} = \\frac{{k\\,{n^2}}}{Z}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,(1)$<br>where $k = {\\rm{constant}}$<br>For ground state of hydrogen,$Z = 1,n = 1,$ so that ${r_1} = k$<br>Let $n$ be the energy state of  $B{e^{ + 3}}$ for which orbital radius is ${r_1}.$<br>Put $Z = 4$ and ${r_{\\rm{n}}} = {r_1} = k$ in Eq.(1)<br>${r_1} = \\frac{{{r_1}\\,{n^2}}}{4} \\Rightarrow n = 2$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45279,'physics',41,403,800,'Number of neutrons in ${C^{12}}\\,$ and ${C^{14}}$ are:','[{"text": "$8\\\\,\\\\operatorname{and} \\\\,6$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$6\\\\,\\\\operatorname{and} \\\\,8$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$6\\\\,\\\\operatorname{and} \\\\,6$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$8\\\\,\\\\operatorname{and} \\\\,8$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Number of neutrons $ = $ Mass number - Atomic number<br>For ${C^{12}},\\,\\,\\, = 12 - 6 = 6$<br>For ${C^{14}},\\,\\,\\, = 14 - 6 = 8$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45280,'physics',39,1104,2631,'The ratio of the de-Broglie wavelengths of proton and electron having same kinetic energy (Assume $m_{p}=m_{e} \\times 1849$)','[{"text": "$2: 43$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1: 43$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$1: 30$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1: 62$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Kinetic energy, of a particle of mass $m$ is given by $E_{k}=\\dfrac{1}{2} m v^{2}$ where $v$ is the velocity of the particle.<br>As, $p=m v=\\sqrt{2 m E_{k}}$<br>$\\therefore$ de-broglie wavelength of the moving particle is given by, $\\lambda=\\dfrac{h}{p}=\\dfrac{h}{\\sqrt{2 m E_{k}}}$<br>For proton, $\\lambda_{p}=\\dfrac{h}{\\sqrt{2 m_{p} E_{k}}}$; for electron,<br>$\\lambda_{e}=\\dfrac{h}{\\sqrt{2 m_{e} E_{k}}}$<br>So, $\\dfrac{\\lambda_{p}}{\\lambda_{e}}=\\dfrac{\\sqrt{m_{e}}}{\\sqrt{m_{p}}}=\\sqrt{\\dfrac{m_{e}}{m_{e} \\times 1849}}$;<br>$\\dfrac{\\lambda_{p}}{\\lambda_{e}}=\\sqrt{\\dfrac{1}{1849}}=\\dfrac{1}{43}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2023,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45281,'physics',37,977,2770,'The primary of a transformer has 400 turns while the secondary has 2000 turns. If the power output from the secondary at $1000\\,V$ is $12\\,kw$, what is the primary voltage?','[{"text": "$200\\\\,V$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$300\\\\,V$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$400\\\\,V$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$500\\\\,V$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'${N_P} = 400,{N_S} = 2000\\,\\,{\\rm{and}}{\\mkern 1mu} \\,{\\mkern 1mu} {V_S} = 1000\\,V$. We know, for transformer,<br>$\\frac{{{V_P}}}{{{V_S}}} = \\frac{{{N_P}}}{{{N_S}}}$<br>${V_P} = \\frac{{{V_S} \\times {N_P}}}{{{N_S}}} = \\frac{{1000 \\times 400}}{{2000}} = 200\\,V$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2021,'MHTCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45282,'physics',41,403,800,'The nuclei  $_6{C^{13}}$ and $_7{N^{14}}$ can be described as','[{"text": "Isotones", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Isobars", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Isomers", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Isotopes", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Isotones have same neutron number',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45283,'physics',40,241,480,'A hydrogen atom and a $L{i^{ +  + }}$ ion are both in the second excited state. If  ${L_H}$ and ${L_{Li}}$ are their respective electronic angular momenta, and ${E_H}$ and ${E_{Li}}$ are their respective energies, then','[{"text": "${L_H} > {L_{Li}}\\\\,{\\\\rm{and}}\\\\,\\\\,\\\\left| {{E_H}} \\\\right| > \\\\left| {{E_{Li}}} \\\\right|$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${L_H} = {L_{Li}}\\\\,{\\\\rm{and}}\\\\,\\\\,\\\\left| {{E_H}} \\\\right| < \\\\left| {{E_{Li}}} \\\\right|$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${L_H} = {L_{Li}}\\\\,{\\\\rm{and}}\\\\,\\\\,\\\\left| {{E_H}} \\\\right| > \\\\left| {{E_{Li}}} \\\\right|$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${L_H} < {L_{Li}}\\\\,{\\\\rm{and}}\\\\,\\\\,\\\\left| {{E_H}} \\\\right| < \\\\left| {{E_{Li}}} \\\\right|$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'$L = \\frac{{nh}}{{2\\pi }},\\,\\left| E \\right|\\,\\alpha \\,\\frac{{{Z^2}}}{{{n^2}}}$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45284,'physics',39,1104,2631,'A proton and an $\\alpha$-particle are accelerated from rest by $2\\,V$ and $4\\,V$ potentials respectively. The ratio of their de-Broglie wavelength is','[{"text": "$16: 1$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$8: 1$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2: 1$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$4: 1$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'If a charged particle of mass $m$, carrying a charge $Q$ is accelerated through a potential difference of \' $V$ \' volt, then the kinetic energy acquired by the particle is given by<br>$\\dfrac{1}{2} m u^{2}=Q \\cdot V \\Rightarrow u=\\sqrt{\\dfrac{2 Q V}{m}}$<br>$\\therefore$ de Broglie wavelength of the charged particle is $\\lambda=\\dfrac{h}{m V}=\\dfrac{h}{\\sqrt{2 Q m V} \\text {. }}$<br>$\\therefore \\dfrac{\\lambda_{p}}{\\lambda_{\\alpha}} \\sqrt{\\dfrac{Q_{\\alpha}}{Q_{p}}} \\cdot \\sqrt{\\dfrac{m_{\\alpha}}{m_{p}}} \\cdot \\sqrt{\\dfrac{V_{\\alpha}}{V_{p}}}$<br>$=\\sqrt{\\dfrac{2 e}{e}} \\cdot \\sqrt{\\dfrac{4 m p}{m p}} \\cdot \\sqrt{\\dfrac{4 V}{2 V}}=4$.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2023,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45285,'physics',37,973,2268,'In an experiment, $200\\,V$ A.C. is applied at the ends of an $L C R$ circuit. The circuit consists of an inductive reactance $\\left( {{X_L}} \\right) = 50\\,\\Omega $, capacitive reactance $\\left( {{X_C}} \\right) = 50\\,\\Omega $ and ohmic resistance $(R) = 10\\,{\\mkern 1mu} \\Omega $. The impedance of the circuit is','[{"text": "$10\\\\,{\\\\mkern 1mu} \\\\Omega $", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$20\\\\,{\\\\mkern 1mu} \\\\Omega $", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$30\\\\,{\\\\mkern 1mu} \\\\Omega $", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$40\\\\,{\\\\mkern 1mu} \\\\Omega $", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'The impedance of the $L C R$ circuit<br>$(Z)=\\sqrt{\\left\\{\\left(X_{L}-X_{C}\\right)^{2}+R^{2}\\right\\}}$<br>$ \\Rightarrow Z = \\sqrt {\\left\\{ {{{(50 - 50)}^2} + {{(10)}^2}} \\right\\}} $<br>$ \\Rightarrow Z = 10\\,{\\mkern 1mu} \\Omega $',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2021,'MHTCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45286,'physics',41,403,800,'Match the following nuclei with the type of characteristic shown.<div class="matching-question"><div class="column-left">A. Isotopes<br>B. Isobars<br>C. Isotones</div><div class="column-right">P. $_8{O^{18}}{,_9}{F^{19}}$<br>Q. $_8{O^{17}}{,_9}{F^{17}}$<br>R. $_{92}{U^{235}}{,_{92}}{U^{237}}$</div></div>','[{"text": "A - P\\tB - R\\t       C - Q", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "A - Q\\tB - P\\t       C - R", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "A - R\\tB - Q        C - P", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "A - R\\tB - P\\t       C - Q", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'For  $_{92}^{235}{\\text{U}}$ and $_{92}^{237}{\\text{U}}$, $Z = 92$; Number of protons equal are isotopes. In $_8^{17}{\\text{O}}$ and $_9^{17}{\\text{F}}$, $A = 17$, Mass numbers are same $ \\Rightarrow $ Isobars. In $_8^{18}{\\text{O}}$ and $_9^{19}{\\text{F}}$, $N = A - Z = 10$ for both  Since, number of neutrons are same, so there are isotones.',NULL,'Medium','Match the following',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45287,'physics',39,1102,2626,'The difference between threshold wavelengths for two metal surfaces $A$ and $B$ having work function ${\\phi _A} = 9eV$ and ${\\phi _B} = 4.5eV$ in $nm$ is Given, $hc = 1242eVnm$','[{"text": "276", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "540", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "264", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "138", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Work function is given by, $\\phi=\\dfrac{h c}{\\lambda_{0}}$<br>where $\\lambda_{0}$ is threshold wavelength of incident light.<br>$\\Rightarrow \\lambda_{A}=\\dfrac{h c}{\\phi_{A}} ; \\lambda_{B}=\\dfrac{h c}{\\phi_{B}}$<br>$\\therefore \\lambda_{B}-\\lambda_{A}=h c\\left(\\dfrac{1}{\\phi_{B}}-\\dfrac{1}{\\phi_{A}}\\right)$<br>$=\\left(\\dfrac{1242 e V n m}{e V}\\right)\\left(\\dfrac{1}{4.5}-\\dfrac{1}{9}\\right) n m$<br>$=(1242)\\left(\\dfrac{9-4.5}{9 \\times 4.5}\\right) n m$<br>$ \\Rightarrow {\\lambda _B} - {\\lambda _A} = 138\\,nm$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',2023,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45288,'physics',40,240,479,'An electron in a hydrogen atom makes a transition from  first excited state to ground state. The equivalent current due to circulating 	electron:','[{"text": "increasing 2 times", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "increases 4 times", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "increases 8 times", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "remains the same", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'$i = \\frac{e}{T} = \\frac{e}{{4\\varepsilon _0^2{n^3}{h^3}}}\\left( {m{z^2}{e^4}} \\right)$<br>$i\\alpha \\frac{1}{{{n^3}}}$<br>$\\frac{{{i_1}}}{{{i_2}}} = {\\left( {\\frac{{{n_2}}}{{{n_1}}}} \\right)^3}$<br>$ \\Rightarrow {i_2} = {i_1}{\\left( {\\frac{{{n_1}}}{{{n_2}}}} \\right)^3}$<br>${i_2} = {i_1}{\\left( {\\frac{2}{1}} \\right)^3} = 8{i_1}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL);
INSERT INTO `qb_questions` (`id`, `subject_id`, `chapter_id`, `topic_id`, `subtopic_id`, `question_html`, `options`, `correct_index`, `explanation`, `explanation_image_url`, `difficulty`, `qtype`, `year`, `tag`, `created_at`, `question_image_url`) VALUES
(45289,'physics',37,975,2275,'A circuit of negligible resistance has an inductor of $0.16\\,H$ and capacitor of $25\\,\\mu {\\rm{F}}$ connected in series with an alternating voltage source. The resonating frequency of the circuit is','[{"text": "$\\\\dfrac{200}{\\\\pi}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{250}{\\\\pi}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{400}{\\\\pi}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{150}{\\\\pi}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'At resonance, $X_{C}=X_{L}$<br>$\\dfrac{1}{\\omega C}=\\omega L \\Rightarrow \\omega^{2}=\\dfrac{1}{L C}$<br>$ \\Rightarrow \\omega  = \\frac{1}{{\\sqrt {LC} }} = \\frac{1}{{\\sqrt {(0.16) \\times \\left( {25 \\times {{10}^{ - 6}}} \\right)} }}$<br>$ \\Rightarrow \\omega  = 2\\pi v = \\frac{1}{{2 \\times {{10}^{ - 3}}}} \\Rightarrow v = \\frac{{250}}{\\pi }\\,Hz$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2021,'MHTCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45290,'physics',41,403,800,'Nucleus of an atom whose atomic mass is 24 consists of','[{"text": "11 electrons, 13 protons and 11 neutrons", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "11 electrons, 11 protons and 13 neutrons", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "11  protons, 13 electrons", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "11  protons, 13 neutrons", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Nucleus does not contain electron. So the only possible option is (4)',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45291,'physics',39,1102,2629,'Read the Statement - A and Statement - B carefully to mark the correct options given below:<div class="matching-question"><div class="column-left">Out of microwaves, infrared rays and ultraviolet rays, ultraviolet rays are the most effective for the emission of electrons from a metallic surface.</div><div class="column-right">Above the threshold frequency, the maximum kinetic energy of photoelectrons is inversely proportional to the frequency of the incident light.</div></div>','[{"text": "Both Statement A and Statement B are correct.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Statement A is correct but Statement B is incorrect.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Statement A and Statement B are incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Statement A is  incorrect but Statement B is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Energy of a photon of ultraviolet rays is more then that of microwaves and infrared rays.<br>Therefore the quantity $(h v-\\phi)$ is maximum for ultraviolet rays. Hence ultraviolet ray are most effective for emission of photo electrons.<br>Maximum kinetic energy of photo electron is given by, $K . E=h v-\\phi$<br>Where, $\\phi$ is the work function and $v$ is the frequency of incident light.<br>Thus, statement $\\mathrm{A}$ is true but statement $\\mathrm{B}$ is false',NULL,'Medium','MCQ type-2',2023,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45292,'physics',37,972,2634,'The instantaneous value of current and voltage in an $A C$ circuit are given by<br>$I = 6\\sin (100\\pi t + \\pi /4){\\rm{ and}}$<br>$V = 5\\sin (100\\pi t - \\pi {\\rm{/}}4){\\rm{ then}}$','[{"text": "voltage leads the current by $45^{\\\\circ}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "current leads the voltage by $45^{\\\\circ}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "voltage leads the current by $90^{\\\\circ}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "current leads the voltage by $90^{\\\\circ}$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'The phase difference between instantaneous values of current and voltage is $\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}-\\left(\\dfrac{-\\pi}{4}\\right)=\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}$<br>$ \\Rightarrow $ the current leads the voltage by $90^{\\circ}$.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2022,'MHTCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45293,'physics',40,240,479,'An electron makes a transition from orbit  $n = 4$ to the orbit $n = 2d$ of a hydrogen atom. The wave number of the emitted radiations ($R = $ Rydberg’s constant) will be','[{"text": "$\\\\frac{{3R}}{{16}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{{4R}}{{16}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{{2R}}{{16}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{{16}}{{3R}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Wave number <br>$\\frac{1}{\\lambda } = R\\left[ {\\frac{1}{{n_1^2}} - \\frac{1}{{n_2^2}}} \\right] = R\\left[ {\\frac{1}{4} - \\frac{1}{{16}}} \\right] = \\frac{{3R}}{{16}}$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45294,'physics',41,403,800,'Which one of the following pairs of nuclei are 	isotones?','[{"text": "$_{34}S{e^{74}}{,_{31}}G{a^{71}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$_{42}M{o^{92}}{,_{40}}Z{r^{92}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$_{38}S{r^{84}}{,_{38}}S{r^{86}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$_{20}C{a^{40}}{,_{16}}{S^{32}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Nuclei of $_{34}S{e^{74}}\\,and{\\,_{31}}G{a^{71}}$ are isotones as $A - Z = 74 - 34 = 71 - 31 = 40$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45296,'physics',37,975,2275,'In an \\(L C R\\) series circuit, if the angular frequency is gradually increased then match the following columns<div class="matching-question"></div>','[{"text": "$\\\\mathrm{A}-\\\\mathrm{P}, \\\\quad \\\\mathrm{B}-\\\\mathrm{R}, \\\\quad \\\\mathrm{C}-\\\\mathrm{S}, \\\\quad \\\\mathrm{D}-\\\\mathrm{Q}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{A}-\\\\mathrm{S}, \\\\quad \\\\mathrm{B}-\\\\mathrm{P}, \\\\quad \\\\mathrm{C}-\\\\mathrm{Q}, \\\\quad \\\\mathrm{D}-\\\\mathrm{R}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{A}-\\\\mathrm{P}, \\\\quad \\\\mathrm{B}-\\\\mathrm{S}, \\\\quad \\\\mathrm{C}-\\\\mathrm{Q}, \\\\quad \\\\mathrm{D}-\\\\mathrm{R}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{A}-\\\\mathrm{Q}, \\\\quad \\\\mathrm{B}-\\\\mathrm{R}, \\\\quad \\\\mathrm{C}-\\\\mathrm{P}, \\\\quad \\\\mathrm{D}-\\\\mathrm{S}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'As $X_{C}=\\dfrac{1}{\\omega C}$ $ \\Rightarrow {X_C} \\propto \\frac{1}{\\omega }$<br>$ \\Rightarrow $ if $\\omega$ increased, then $X_{C}$ decreases.<br>$ \\Rightarrow \\left( {A - S} \\right)$<br>${X_L} = \\omega L{\\rm{ }} \\Rightarrow {\\rm{ }}{X_L} \\propto \\omega $<br>$ \\Rightarrow \\left( {B - P} \\right)$<br>$ \\Rightarrow $ As $\\omega$ increases, $X_{L}$ will be increases.<br>$R$ will be same as it not a function of $\\omega$.<br>$ \\Rightarrow \\left( {C - Q} \\right)$<br>Total impedance will be minimum at resonance.<br>$ \\Rightarrow (D - R)$',NULL,'Hard','Match the following',2022,'MHTCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/37_45296_question_1.jpg'),
(45297,'physics',41,403,800,'$A$ and $B$ are isotopes.  $B$ and $C$ are isobars. All three are radioactive. Which one of the following is true?','[{"text": "$A$, $B$ and $C$ must belong to the same element.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$A$, $B$ and $C$ may belong to the same element.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "It is possible that $A$ will change to $B$ through a radioactive-decay process.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "It is possible that $B$ will change to $C$ through a radioactive-decay process.", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Isotopes have same atomic number and different neutron number. Isobars have different atomic number and same mass number.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45298,'physics',39,1104,2631,'The de-Broglie wavelength of an electron having kinetic energy $E$ is $\\lambda$. If the kinetic energy of electron becomes $\\dfrac{E}{4}$, then its de-Broglie wavelength will be','[{"text": "$\\\\dfrac{\\\\lambda}{\\\\sqrt{2}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2 \\\\lambda$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\sqrt{2} \\\\lambda$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{\\\\lambda}{2}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'If $p$ be the linear momentum of a moving particle and $m$ be its mass, then its kinetic energy is given by $E=p^{2} / 2 m$<br>$\\therefore p=\\sqrt{2 m E}$.<br>Therefore de Broglie equation is given by<br>$\\lambda=\\dfrac{h}{p}=\\dfrac{h}{\\sqrt{2 m E}} \\Rightarrow \\lambda \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{E}}$.<br>$\\therefore \\dfrac{\\lambda^{\\prime}}{\\lambda}=\\sqrt{\\dfrac{E}{E^{\\prime}}}=\\sqrt{\\dfrac{E}{E / 4}}=2 \\Rightarrow \\lambda^{\\prime}=2 \\lambda$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2023,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45299,'physics',40,241,480,'If the energy of hydrogen atom in nth orbit ${E_n}$, then energy in the nth orbit of a single ionized helium atom will be:','[{"text": "$4{E_n}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${E_n}/4$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2{E_n}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${E_n}/2$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'The energy of the electron in the ${n^{th}}$ orbit is $E\\,\\alpha \\,{Z^2}$<br>Given,${Z_H} = 1,{Z_{He}} = 2$<br>$\\frac{{{E_H}}}{{{E_{He}}}} = \\frac{{Z_H^2}}{{Z_{He}^2}} = \\frac{1}{4} \\Rightarrow {E_{He}} = 4{E_H}$<br>Given,${E_H} = {E_n} \\Rightarrow {E_{He}} = 4{E_n}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45300,'physics',37,976,2280,'In $L C$ parallel resonance circuit, choose the wrong statement.','[{"text": "At resonance, current is minimum.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "At resonance, impedance is maximum.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Resonance occurs when inductive and capcitive reactance is same.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "At resonance, resonate frequency $=\\\\sqrt{L C}$.", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'In $L C$ parallel resonance circuit, impedance<br>$\\left( Z \\right) = \\frac{{{X_C},{X_L}}}{{{X_C} - {X_L}}}$<br>At resonance, ${X_C} = {X_L}$ then $Z \\to \\infty $<br>$ \\Rightarrow $ The impedance is maximum<br>$ \\Rightarrow $ Current is zero (or) minimum<br>At resonance ${X_L} = {X_C}$<br>$\\omega L = \\frac{1}{{\\omega C}}$<br>$\\omega  = \\frac{1}{{\\sqrt {LC} }}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2022,'MHTCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45301,'physics',41,403,801,'The radius of a nucleus mainly depends on','[{"text": "Proton number", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Electron Number", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Mass number", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Neutron number", "isCorrect": false}]',2,NULL,NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45302,'physics',39,1104,2631,'Electron beam used in an electron microscope, when accelerated by a voltage of $20\\,kV$, has a de-Broglie wavelength of $\\lambda_{0}$. If the voltage is increased to $40\\,kV$, then the de-Broglie wavelength associated with the electron beam would be','[{"text": "$3 \\\\lambda_{0}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$9 \\\\lambda_{0}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{\\\\lambda_{0}}{\\\\sqrt{2}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{\\\\lambda_{0}}{2}$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'de-Broglie wavelength, is given by<br>$\\lambda=\\dfrac{h}{\\sqrt{2 M e V}}$<br>$\\lambda \\propto \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{V}}$ So, $\\dfrac{\\lambda_{2}}{\\lambda_{1}}=\\sqrt{\\dfrac{V_{1}}{V_{2}}}=\\sqrt{\\dfrac{20}{40}}=\\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}$<br>$\\lambda_{2}=\\dfrac{\\lambda_{0}}{\\sqrt{2}}$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',2023,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45303,'physics',37,972,2261,'In an $a.c$ circuit, peak value of voltage is $423\\,volts,$ its effective voltage is:','[{"text": "$400\\\\,V$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$323\\\\,V$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$300\\\\,V$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$340\\\\,V$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'We know,<br>The $r.m.s.$ or effective voltage is $V_{m s}=\\dfrac{V_{0}}{\\sqrt{2}}$<br>$ \\Rightarrow {V_{rms}} = \\frac{{423}}{{\\sqrt 2 }} = 300\\,V$<br>So, correct option is (3).',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45304,'physics',40,240,479,'The fine structure of hydrogen spectrum can be explained by','[{"text": "The presence of neutrons in the nucleus", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "The finite size of nucleus", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "The orbital angular momentum of electrons", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "The spin angular momentum of electrons", "isCorrect": true}]',3,NULL,NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45305,'physics',41,403,801,'The nucleus which has radius one-third of the radius of $O{s^{189}}$ is:','[{"text": "$B{e^9}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$L{i^7}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${F^{19}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${C^{12}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'The radius of a nucleus is given by the 	relation, $r = {r_0}{(A)^{1/3}}$<br>where, ${r_0} = $ constant<br>For $O{s^{189}},{A_1} = 189,{r_1} = r$<br>Thus,using ${r_2} = \\frac{r}{3}$, we know that,<br>$\\frac{{{r_1}}}{{{r_2}}} = {\\left( {\\frac{{{A_1}}}{{{A_2}}}} \\right)^{1/3}} \\Rightarrow 3 = {\\left( {\\frac{{189}}{{{A_2}}}} \\right)^{1/3}}$<br>$ \\Rightarrow 27 = \\frac{{189}}{{{A_2}}} \\Rightarrow {A_2} = 7$<br>Thus, the answer is $L{i^7}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45306,'physics',39,1102,2629,'Read the Statement - A and Statement - B carefully to mark the correct options given below:<div class="matching-question"><div class="column-left">Stopping potential in photoelectric effect does not depend on the power of the light source.</div><div class="column-right">For a given metal, the maximum kinetic energy of the photoelectron depends on the wavelength of the incident light.</div></div>','[{"text": "Both Statement A and Statement B are correct.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Statement A is correct but Statement B is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Statement A and Statement B are incorrect.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Statement A is correct but Statement B is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'If a source of light emits photons at constant frequency, then its power is directly proportional to the number of photons emitted from the source per second. Since stopping potential<br>$V=\\dfrac{h}{e} v-\\dfrac{\\phi}{e}$, it does not depend upon the power of source as $v$ is constant. For photo electric effect, from Einstein\'s equation,<br>$\\dfrac{h c}{\\lambda}=\\phi+(K E)_{\\max } \\Rightarrow(K E)_{\\max }=\\left(\\dfrac{h c}{\\lambda}=\\phi\\right)$<br>Hence option (3) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ type-2',2023,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45307,'physics',37,972,2261,'The peak value of $220\\,volts,$ of $a.c$ mains is:','[{"text": "$155.6\\\\,V$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$200\\\\,V$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$311\\\\,V$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$440\\\\,V$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'$V_{r m s}=\\dfrac{V_{0}}{\\sqrt{2}}$<br>$\\Rightarrow$ Peak value is $V_{0}=\\sqrt{2} \\times V_{r m s}$<br>$=1.41 \\times 220$<br>${V_0} = 311\\,V$<br>So correct option is (3).',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45308,'physics',41,403,801,'The volume occupied by an atom is greater than the volume of the nucleus by a factor of about','[{"text": "${10^{10}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${10^{15}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${10^1}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${10^5}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Radius of atom $ \\approx {10^{ - 10}}m$<br>Radius of nucleus $ \\approx {10^{ - 15}}m$<br>$\\frac{{Volume\\,of\\,atom}}{{Volume\\,of\\,nucleus}} = \\frac{{\\frac{4}{3}\\pi r_1^3}}{{\\frac{4}{3}\\pi r_2^3}} = {\\left( {\\frac{{{{10}^{ - 10}}}}{{{{10}^{ - 15}}}}} \\right)^3} = {10^{15}}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45309,'physics',40,241,480,'Consider the spectral line resulting from the transition $n = 2$ to $n = 1$ in the atoms and the ions 	given below. The shortest wavelength is produced by','[{"text": "Hydrogen atom", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Deuterium atom", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Singly ionised helium", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Doubly ionised lithium", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'$\\Delta E = \\frac{{hc}}{\\lambda } = 13.6{z^2}\\left[ {\\frac{1}{{{1^2}}} - \\frac{1}{{{2^2}}}} \\right]$<br>$ \\Rightarrow \\lambda \\alpha \\frac{1}{{{z^2}}}$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45310,'physics',39,1102,2626,'The threshold wavelength for photoelectric emission from a material is $5500$ $ \\mathop A^{~~\\circ}  $ . Photoelectrons will be emitted, when this material is illuminated with monochromatic radiation from a<br>A. $75 W$ infra-red lamp<br>B. $10 W$ infra-red lamp<br>C. $75 W$ ultra-violet lamp<br>D. 10W ultra-violet lamp<br>Choose the correct answer from the options given below.','[{"text": "C only", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "B and C only", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "C and D only", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "A and D only", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Wavelength $5500$ $ \\mathop A^{~~\\circ}  $ belongs to yellow light. wavelength of infrared light is more then $5500$ $ \\mathop A^{~~\\circ}  $ and that of ultraviolet light is less then $5500$ $ \\mathop A^{~~\\circ}  $. Hence option (3) is correct.',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',2023,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45311,'physics',37,972,2260,'The frequency of an alternating voltage is $50\\,cycles{\\rm{/}}\\sec $ and its amplitude is $120\\,V$. Then the $r.m.s$ value of voltage is:','[{"text": "$101.37\\\\,V$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$84.8\\\\,V$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$70.7\\\\,V$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$56.5\\\\,V$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'We know that<br>${V_{rms}} = \\frac{{{V_0}}}{{\\sqrt 2 }} = \\frac{{120}}{{1.41}} = 84.8\\,V{\\rm{ (nearly) }}$<br>So, correct option is (2).',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45312,'physics',41,408,814,'Nuclear fusion requires high temperature because','[{"text": "All nuclear reactions absorb heat", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "The particles cannot come together unless  they are moving rapidly", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "The binding energy must be  supplied from an external  source", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "The mass defect must be supplied", "isCorrect": false}]',1,NULL,NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45313,'physics',39,1104,2631,'The ratio of de-Broglie wavelength of an $\\alpha$ particle and a proton accelerated from rest by the same potential is $\\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{m}}$, the value of $m$ is','[{"text": "16", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "2", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "4", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "8", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Here: $m_{\\alpha}=4 m_{P}, q_{\\alpha}=2 q_{P}$,<br>$V=$ Potential difference.<br>$\\lambda=\\dfrac{h}{\\sqrt{2 m q V}}$ <br>so, $\\dfrac{\\lambda_{\\alpha}}{\\lambda_{P}}=\\sqrt{\\dfrac{2 m_{P} q_{P} V}{2 m_{\\alpha} q_{\\alpha} V}}=\\sqrt{\\dfrac{m_{P} \\cdot q_{P}}{4 m_{P} \\times 2 q_{P}}}=\\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{8}}$<br>So, $m=8$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',2023,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45314,'physics',40,239,478,'An electron moves towards a nucleus at the focus of an elliptical orbit with velocity $v$. Its angular momentum with respect to nucleus is','[{"text": "Always zero", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Always remains constant", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Changes with time", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Can not be determined", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Angular momentum of the incident electron w.r. to nucleus is <br>$L = mv \\times $ perpendicular distance<br>$ = mv \\times \\left( 0 \\right) = 0.$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45315,'physics',37,972,2260,'In an $A C$ circuit, the $r m s$ value of the current, $I_{r m s}$, is related to the peak current $I_{0}$ as','[{"text": "$I_{r m s}=\\\\dfrac{1}{\\\\pi} I_{0}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$I_{r m s}=\\\\dfrac{1}{\\\\sqrt{2}} I_{0}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$I_{r m s}=\\\\sqrt{2} I_{0}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$I_{r m s}=\\\\pi I_{0}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'$I_{r m s}=\\dfrac{I_{0}}{\\sqrt{2}}$<br>So, correct option is (2).',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45316,'physics',41,408,814,'Which of these is a fusion reaction?','[{"text": "$_{92}^{238}{\\\\rm{U}} \\\\to _{82}^{206}{\\\\rm{Pb  + 8}}\\\\left( {_2^4{\\\\rm{He}}} \\\\right) + 6\\\\left( {_{ - 1}^0\\\\beta } \\\\right)$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$_1^3{\\\\rm{H}} + _1^2{\\\\rm{H}} \\\\to _2^4{\\\\rm{He}} + _1^1n$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$_{92}^{238}{\\\\rm{U}} \\\\to _{82}^{206}{\\\\rm{Pb  + 8}}\\\\left( {_2^4{\\\\rm{He}}} \\\\right) + 6\\\\left( {_{ - 1}^0\\\\beta } \\\\right)$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\mathop{\\\\rm None}\\\\nolimits} \\\\,\\\\,{\\\\mathop{\\\\rm of}\\\\nolimits} \\\\,\\\\,{\\\\mathop{\\\\rm these}\\\\nolimits} $", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'In fusion reaction, two lighter nuclei combines.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45317,'physics',39,1104,2631,'An electron accelerated through a potential difference $V_{1}$ has a de-Broglie wavelength of $\\lambda$. When the potential is changed to $V_{2}$, its deBroglie wavelength increases by $50 \\%$. The value of $\\left(\\dfrac{V_{1}}{V_{2}}\\right)$ is equal to','[{"text": "3", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{9}{4}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "4", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{3}{2}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'When a charged particle of mass $m$, carrying a charge $Q$ is accelerated. Through a potential difference of $V$, its final linear momentum is given by<br>$\\dfrac{p^{2}}{2 m}=Q V \\Rightarrow p=\\sqrt{2 m Q V}$.<br>$\\therefore$ de Broglie wavelength $\\lambda=\\dfrac{h}{p}=\\dfrac{h}{\\sqrt{2 m Q V}}$<br>$\\therefore \\dfrac{\\lambda_{2}}{\\lambda_{1}}=\\sqrt{\\dfrac{V_{1}}{V_{2}}}$<br>$\\therefore \\dfrac{V_{1}}{V_{2}}=\\left(\\dfrac{\\lambda_{2}}{\\lambda_{1}}\\right)^{2}=\\left(\\dfrac{1.5}{\\lambda}\\right)^{2}=\\dfrac{9}{4}$.',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',2023,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45318,'physics',37,972,2261,'The peak voltage of $440\\,Volt$ $A C$ mains is equal to (in $Volt$)','[{"text": "155.6", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "220.0", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "622.0", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "440.0", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'$V_{0}=\\sqrt{2} \\cdot V_{\\text {r.m.s }}=\\sqrt{2} \\times 440=622$ $Volt$<br>So correct option is (3).',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45319,'physics',40,241,480,'The de-Broglie wavelength of an electron in 4th orbit is ($r = $ radius of 1st orbit)','[{"text": "$2\\\\pi r$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$4\\\\pi r$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$8\\\\pi r$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$16\\\\pi r$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'According to de Broglie’s quantisation condition,<br>$2\\pi r = n\\lambda $<br>$ \\Rightarrow \\lambda  = \\frac{{2\\pi r}}{n}$<br>$r\\,\\alpha \\,{n^2}$<br>$\\frac{{{r_1}}}{{{r_2}}} = {\\left( {\\frac{1}{4}} \\right)^2} \\Rightarrow {r_2} = 16{r_1}$<br>Hence, $\\lambda  = \\frac{{2\\pi [16{r_1}]}}{4} = 8\\pi {r_1} = 8\\pi r$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45320,'physics',41,405,805,'Two protons attract each other when','[{"text": "The distance between them is  ${10^{ - 10}}m$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "The distance between them is ${10^{ - 1}}m$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "The distance between them is ${10^{ - 15}}m$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "The distance between them is ${10^{ - 6}}m$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,NULL,NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45474,'physics',39,1104,2631,'Calculate the momentum and de-Broglie wavelength of electrons accelerated through a potential difference of $56\\,V.$','[{"text": "$\\\\lambda  = 0.164\\\\,nm$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\lambda  = 1\\\\,nm$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\lambda  = 0.5\\\\,nm$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\lambda  = 5\\\\,mm$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Given, potential difference,<br>$V = 56\\,V$<br>de-Broglie wavelength of electron,<br>$\\lambda  = \\frac{{12.27}}{{\\sqrt V }}\\mathop A\\limits^ \\circ   = \\frac{{12.27}}{{\\sqrt {56} }} = 0.164 \\times {10^{ - 9}}\\,m$<br>$ = 0.164\\,nm$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45321,'physics',39,1104,2631,'The kinetic energy of an electron, $\\alpha$-particle and a proton are given as $4 K, 2 K$ and $K$ respectively. The de-Broglie wavelength associated with electron $\\left(\\lambda_{e}\\right), \\alpha$-particle $\\left(\\lambda_{\\alpha}\\right)$ and the proton $\\left(\\lambda_{p}\\right)$ are as follows :','[{"text": "$\\\\lambda_{\\\\alpha}>\\\\lambda_{p}>\\\\lambda_{e}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\lambda_{\\\\alpha}=\\\\lambda_{p} < \\\\lambda_{e}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\lambda_{\\\\alpha} < \\\\lambda_{p} < \\\\lambda_{e}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\lambda_{\\\\alpha}=\\\\lambda_{p}>\\\\lambda_{e}$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'As, $\\lambda=\\dfrac{h}{\\sqrt{2 m(K E)}} ; \\lambda \\propto \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{m(K E)}}$<br>Let mass of proton $m_{p}=1$.<br>For $\\alpha$-particle, $\\lambda=\\dfrac{h}{\\sqrt{4 \\times 2 K}}$ or $\\lambda=\\dfrac{h}{\\sqrt{8 K}}$<br>For electron, $\\lambda=\\dfrac{h}{\\sqrt{\\dfrac{1}{1840} \\times 4 K}}$ or $\\sqrt{\\dfrac{1840}{4 K}} h$<br>For proton, $\\lambda=\\dfrac{h}{\\sqrt{K}}$<br>As $\\alpha$-particle is heavier than both proton and electron.<br>$\\therefore \\lambda_{\\alpha} < \\lambda_{p} < \\lambda_{e}$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',2023,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
