(45324,'physics',40,239,478,'For atomic model of hydrogen atom given by Niels Bohr, match the following proportionalities.<div class="matching-question"><div class="column-left">A. Angular momentum<br>B. Velocity of electron<br>C. Radius of electron<br>D. Energy of electron</div><div class="column-right">P. $1/n$<br>Q. ${n^2}$<br>R. $1/{n^2}$<br>S. $n$</div></div>','[{"text": "A-P, \\tB-Q, \\t\\tC-R, \\tD-S", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "A-S, \\tB-R, \\t\\tC-Q, \\tD-P", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "A-S, \\tB-P, \\t\\tC-R, \\tD-Q", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "A-S, \\tB-P, \\t\\tC-Q, \\tD-R", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'As we know, angular momentum <br>$(L) = \\frac{{nh}}{{2\\pi }} \\Rightarrow L \\propto n$<br>Radius of an electron<br>${r_n} = \\frac{{{\\varepsilon _0}{n^2}{h^2}}}{{\\pi m{e^2}z}} \\Rightarrow {r_n} \\propto {n^2}$<br>Velocity of electrons<br>${v_n} = \\frac{{z{e^2}}}{{2{\\varepsilon _0}nh}} \\Rightarrow {v_n} \\propto 1/n$<br>Potential energy i.e., ${E_n} =  - \\frac{{m{e^4}}}{{4{n^2}\\varepsilon _0^2{h^2}}}$<br>${E_n} \\propto 1/{n^2}$<br>So option (4) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Match the following',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45325,'physics',39,1102,2628,'The work functions of Aluminium and Gold are $4.1\\,eV$ and $5.1\\,eV$ respectively. The ratio of the slope of the stopping potential versus frequency plot for Gold to that of Aluminium is','[{"text": "1.24", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "1", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "1.5", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "2", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'From Einstein\'s equation, $h v=\\phi+e V_{0}$$\\Rightarrow V_{0}=\\left(\\dfrac{h}{e}\\right) v-\\left(\\dfrac{\\phi}{e}\\right)$<br>$\\therefore$ slope $=\\dfrac{h}{e}$.<br>As the slope remain constant the ratio of slopes between any two metals is equal to 1.',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',2023,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45326,'physics',37,972,2260,'The peak value of $A C$ is $2\\sqrt 2 \\,A$. Its apparent value will be','[{"text": "$1\\\\,A$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2\\\\,A$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$4\\\\,A$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Zero", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'$I_{r m s}=\\dfrac{I_{0}}{\\sqrt{2}}$<br>So correct option is (2).',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45327,'physics',41,405,805,'Nuclear forces are','[{"text": "Non-central forces", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Saturated", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Spin dependent", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "All the above", "isCorrect": true}]',3,NULL,NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45328,'physics',39,1102,2629,'In photoelectric effect<br>A. The photocurrent is proportional to the intensity of the incident radiation.<br>B. Maximum Kinetic energy with which photoelectrons are emitted depends on the intensity of incident light.<br>C. Max. KE. with which photoelectrons are emitted depends on the frequency of incident light.<br>D. The emission of photoelectrons require a minimum threshold intensity of incident radiation.<br>E. Max K.E. of the phoelectrons is independent of the frequency of the incident light.<br>Choose the correct answer from the options given below.','[{"text": "B and C only", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "A and E only", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "A and C only", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "A and B only", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'In photoelectric effect, photo current is directly proportional to the number of photo electrons emitted per second and the number of photo electrons emitted per second is directly proportional to the intensity of the incident radiation.The maximum kinetic energy of photo electrons depends on the frequency of incident light and hence there is no effect of intensity of light on the kinetic energy of emitted photo electrons.<br>So, correct option is (3).',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',2023,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45329,'physics',40,239,478,'Potential energy $(P{E_n})$ and kinetic energy $(K{E_n})$ of electron in ${n^{th}}$  orbit are related as:','[{"text": "$P{E_n} = K{E_n}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$P{E_n} =  - 2K{E_n}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$P{E_n} = 2K{E_n}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$P{E_n} =  - K{E_n}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,NULL,NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45330,'physics',37,972,2260,'In an a.c circuit, the $r m s$ voltage is $100\\sqrt 2 \\,V$. The peak value of voltage and its mean value during a positive half cycle are','[{"text": "$200\\\\,V,127.4\\\\,V$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$100\\\\,V,127.4\\\\,V$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$120\\\\,V,150\\\\,V$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$200\\\\,V,150\\\\,V$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'$V_{r m s}=100 \\sqrt{2} V, V_{0}=$ ?, $V_{m}=$ ?<br>Where subscripts 0 and $m$ are for \'peak\' and \'mean\'.<br>${V_0} = \\sqrt 2 {V_0} = \\sqrt 2 (100\\sqrt 2 ) = 200\\,V$<br>${V_m} = \\frac{{2{V_0}}}{\\pi } = \\frac{{2 \\times 200}}{{3.14}} = 127.4\\,V$<br>So, correct option is (1).',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45331,'physics',41,404,802,'Nuclear reactions obey the law of conservation of','[{"text": "Mass and energy", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Charge", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Momentum", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "All the above", "isCorrect": true}]',3,NULL,NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(46551,'physics',41,404,802,'If total energy of electron is $3.555\\,MeV$ then its rest mass energy is','[{"text": "$3.555\\\\,MeV$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$3.045\\\\,MeV$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$0.00055\\\\,MeV$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$0.511\\\\,MeV$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'${E=\\Delta m \\times c^{2}}$<br>${\\Delta E=\\dfrac{9.1 \\times 10^{-31} \\times 9 \\times 10^{16}}{1.6 \\times 10^{-19}} {eV}}$<br>${E=0.511 {MeV}}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45332,'physics',39,1102,2627,'The variation of stopping potential \\(\\left(V_{0}\\right)\\) as a function of the frequency \\((v)\\) of the incident light for a metal is shown in figure. The work function of the surface is<div class="matching-question"></div>','[{"text": "$18.6\\\\,eV$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2.07\\\\,eV$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$2.98\\\\,eV$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1.36\\\\,eV$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'From the graph it is evident that threshold frequency ${v_0} = 5 \\times {10^4}\\,Hz$.<br>$\\therefore \\quad \\phi=h v_{0}$<br>$ = 6.6 \\times {10^{ - 34}} \\times 5 \\times {10^{14}} = 33 \\times {10^{ - 20}}\\,J$<br>$\\phi  = \\frac{{33 \\times {{10}^{ - 20}}}}{{1.6 \\times {{10}^{ - 19}}}} = 2.07eV$',NULL,'Medium','Graph/Figure',2023,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/39_45332_question_1.png'),
(45333,'physics',37,972,2260,'If the instantaneous current in a circuit is given by $I=2 \\cos (\\omega t+\\phi)$ ampere, the $r m s$ value of the current is','[{"text": "$2\\\\,A$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\sqrt 2 \\\\,A$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$2\\\\sqrt 2 \\\\,A$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Zero", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'${I_{rms}} = \\frac{{{I_0}}}{{\\sqrt 2 }} = \\frac{2}{{\\sqrt 2 }} = \\sqrt 2 \\,A$<br>So, correct option is (2).',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45334,'physics',40,239,478,'According to Bohr’s theory, the expressions for the kinetic and potential energy of an electron revolving in an orbit is given respectively by','[{"text": "$ + \\\\frac{{8\\\\pi {\\\\varepsilon _0}{e^2}}}{r}{\\\\rm{and}} - \\\\frac{{4\\\\pi {\\\\varepsilon _0}{e^2}}}{r}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$ + \\\\frac{{{e^2}}}{{8\\\\pi {\\\\varepsilon _0}r}}{\\\\rm{and}} - \\\\frac{{{e^2}}}{{4\\\\pi {\\\\varepsilon _0}r}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$ + \\\\frac{{{e^2}}}{{8\\\\pi {\\\\varepsilon _0}r}}{\\\\rm{and + }}\\\\frac{{{e^2}}}{{4\\\\pi {\\\\varepsilon _0}r}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$ - \\\\frac{{{e^2}}}{{8\\\\pi {\\\\varepsilon _0}r}}{\\\\rm{ and  - }}\\\\frac{{{e^2}}}{{4\\\\pi {\\\\varepsilon _0}r}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'$P.E =  - \\frac{{k{e^2}}}{r} =  - \\frac{{{e^2}}}{{4\\pi {\\varepsilon _0}r}};K.E =  - \\frac{1}{2}(P.E) = \\frac{{{e^2}}}{{8\\pi {\\varepsilon _0}r}}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45335,'physics',41,408,812,'Most of the energy released in the fission is carried by','[{"text": "Neutrons", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Fission fragments", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Neutrons and fragments carry equally", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Positrons", "isCorrect": false}]',1,NULL,NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45336,'physics',39,1102,2628,'A metallic surface is illuminated with radiation of wavelength $\\lambda$, the stopping potential is $V_{0}$. If the same surface is illuminated with radiation of wavelength $2 \\lambda$, the stopping potential becomes $\\dfrac{V_{0}}{4}$. The threshold wavelength for this metallic surface will be','[{"text": "$\\\\dfrac{3}{2} \\\\lambda$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$4 \\\\lambda$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{\\\\lambda}{4}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$3 \\\\lambda$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'From Einstein\'s equation of photoelectric effect<br>$e{V_0} = \\frac{{hc}}{\\lambda } - {\\phi _0} = \\frac{{hc}}{\\lambda } - \\frac{{hc}}{{{\\lambda _0}}}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\left( 1 \\right)$<br>$\\frac{{e{V_0}}}{4} = \\frac{{hc}}{{2\\lambda }} - \\frac{{hc}}{{{\\lambda _0}}}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\left( 2 \\right)$<br>By equation (1) and equation (2)<br>$\\Rightarrow \\dfrac{1}{4}\\left(\\dfrac{h c}{\\lambda}-\\dfrac{h c}{\\lambda_{0}}\\right)=\\dfrac{h c}{2 \\lambda}-\\dfrac{h c}{\\lambda_{0}}$<br>$\\dfrac{1}{\\lambda_{0}}-\\dfrac{1}{4 \\lambda_{0}}=\\dfrac{1}{2 \\lambda}-\\dfrac{1}{4 \\lambda} ; \\dfrac{3}{4 \\lambda_{0}}=\\dfrac{1}{4 \\lambda} \\Rightarrow \\lambda_{0}=3 \\lambda$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',2023,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45337,'physics',37,972,2261,'Alternating current in circuit is given by $I = {I_0}\\sin 2\\pi \\,nt$. Then the time taken by the current to rise from zero to r.m.s. value is equal to','[{"text": "${1{\\\\rm{/}}2\\\\,n}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${1{\\\\rm{/}}\\\\,n}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${1{\\\\rm{/}}4\\\\,n}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${1{\\\\rm{/}}8\\\\,n}$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'$I = {I_0}\\sin (2\\pi \\,nt)$<br>$\\frac{{{I_0}}}{{\\sqrt 2 }} = {I_0}\\sin (2\\pi \\,nt)$<br>$ \\Rightarrow \\sin (2\\pi \\,nt) = \\frac{1}{{\\sqrt 2 }} = \\sin \\frac{\\pi }{4}$<br>$ \\Rightarrow 2\\pi \\,nt = \\frac{\\pi }{4}$<br>$\\Rightarrow t=\\dfrac{1}{8 n}$<br>So, correct option is (4).',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45338,'physics',40,239,478,'The ratio of magnetic dipole moment to angular momentum in a hydrogen  atom is','[{"text": "$\\\\frac{e}{m}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{e}{{2m}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{e}{{3m}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{{2e}}{m}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Magnetic moment <br>$\\mu  = iA = \\frac{q}{T}\\pi {r^2} = \\frac{{q\\omega {r^2}}}{2}$<br>Angular momentum<br>$L = m{r^2}\\omega $<br>$\\frac{\\mu }{L} = \\frac{q}{{2m}} = \\frac{e}{{2m}}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45339,'physics',41,408,812,'In the given nuclear reaction A, B, C, D, E represents<br>${}_{{\\text{92}}}{{\\text{U}}^{{\\text{238}}}}\\xrightarrow{{{\\rm {- \\alpha}}}}{}_{\\text{B}}{\\text{T}}{{\\text{h}}^{\\text{A}}}\\xrightarrow{{{\\rm {- \\beta}}}}{}_{\\text{D}}{\\text{P}}{{\\text{a}}^{\\text{C}}}\\xrightarrow{{{\\text{ - E}}}}{}_{{\\text{92}}}{{\\text{U}}^{{\\text{234}}}}$','[{"text": "$A = 234,\\\\,B = 90,\\\\,C = 238,\\\\,D = 94,E = \\\\alpha $", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$A = 234,\\\\,B = 90,\\\\,C = 234,\\\\,D = 91,E = \\\\beta $", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$A = 234,\\\\,B = 90,\\\\,C = 234,\\\\,D = 93,E = \\\\alpha $", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$A = 238,\\\\,B = 93,\\\\,C = 234,\\\\,D = 91,E = \\\\beta $", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'$_{92}{U^{238}}{\\xrightarrow{{ - \\alpha }}_{90}}T{h^{234}}{\\xrightarrow{{ - {\\beta ^ - }}}_{91}}P{a^{234}}{\\xrightarrow{{ - {\\beta ^ - }}}_{92}}{U^{234}}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45340,'physics',39,1104,2631,'A proton moving with one tenth of velocity of light has a certain de-Broglie wavelength of $\\lambda$. An alpha particle having certain kinetic energy has the same de- Broglie wavelength $\\lambda$. The ratio of kinetic energy of proton and that of alpha particle is','[{"text": "$1: 4$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$4: 1$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$1: 2$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2: 1$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'As $V=\\dfrac{c}{10}$, we can assume that the velocity of the proton is in non - relativistic range.<br>$K E=\\dfrac{p^{2}}{2 m}=\\left(\\dfrac{h}{\\lambda}\\right)^{2} \\times \\dfrac{1}{2 m}=\\dfrac{h^{2}}{2 m \\lambda^{2}}$<br>Ratio of kinetic energy of proton and that alpha particle is $\\dfrac{K E_{p}}{K E_{\\alpha}}=\\dfrac{m_{\\mu}}{m_{p}}=\\dfrac{4 m_{p}}{m_{p}}=4: 1$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',2023,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45341,'physics',37,972,2260,'Determine the virtual value of $a c$ current shown in figure.','[{"text": "$3\\\\,A$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$4\\\\,A$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$5\\\\,A$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2\\\\,A$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'$I_{r. m . s}=\\sqrt{\\dfrac{I_{1}{ }^{2}+I_{2}{ }^{2}+I_{3}{ }^{2}}{N}}=\\sqrt{\\dfrac{2^{2}+2^{2}+2^{2}}{3}}$<br>${I_{r.m.s}} = 2\\,A$<br>So, correct option is (4).',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/37_45341_question_1.png'),
(45342,'physics',40,239,478,'The electron of a hydrogen atom revolves around the proton in a circular nth orbit of radius ${r_n} = {\\varepsilon _0}{n^2}{h^2}/(\\pi m{e^2})$ with speed,${v_n} = \\frac{{{e^2}}}{{2{\\varepsilon _0}nh}}$ . The current due to the circulating charge is proportional to','[{"text": "${e^2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${e^3}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${e^5}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${e^6}$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Time period of electron, <br>$T = \\frac{{2\\pi r}}{v} = \\frac{{\\frac{{2\\pi  \\times {\\varepsilon _0}{n^2}{h^2}}}{{\\pi m{e^2}}}}}{{\\frac{{{e^2}}}{{2{\\varepsilon _0}nh}}}} = \\frac{{4\\varepsilon _0^2{n^3}{h^3}}}{{m{e^4}}}$<br>Now currrent,<br>$i = \\frac{e}{T} = \\frac{e}{{4\\varepsilon _0^2{n^3}{h^3}/m{e^4}}} = \\frac{{m{e^5}}}{{4\\varepsilon _0^2{n^3}{h^3}}} \\Rightarrow i\\,\\,\\alpha \\,\\,{e^5}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45343,'physics',41,408,814,'Inside the sun','[{"text": "Four nuclei of hydrogen combine to form two nuclei  of helium", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Four nuclei of hydrogen combine to form four nuclei of helium", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Four nuclei of hydrogen combine to form one nucleus of helium", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Four nuclei of hydrogen  is transformed into one  nucleus of helium", "isCorrect": false}]',2,NULL,NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45344,'physics',39,1102,2628,'The threshold frequency of a metal is $f_{0}$. When the light of frequency $2 f_{0}$ is incident on the metal plate, the maximum velocity of photoelectrons is $v_{1}$. When the frequency of incident radiation is increased to $5 f_{0}$, the maximum velocity of photoelectrons emitted is $v_{2}$. The ratio of $v_{1}$ to $v_{2}$ is','[{"text": "$\\\\dfrac{v_{1}}{v_{2}}=\\\\dfrac{1}{4}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{v_{1}}{v_{2}}=\\\\dfrac{1}{16}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{v_{1}}{v_{2}}=\\\\dfrac{1}{8}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{v_{1}}{v_{2}}=\\\\dfrac{1}{2}$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Threshold frequency $=f_{0}$<br>According to the Einstein\'s photoelectric equation,<br>$K E_{\\max }=E-\\phi$<br>Here $\\phi=h f_{0}$.<br>Case I : $\\dfrac{1}{2} m v_{1}^{2}=h \\times 2 f_{0}-h f_{0}$ or $\\dfrac{1}{2} m v_{1}^{2}=h f_{0}$<br>Case II : $\\dfrac{1}{2} m v_{2}^{2}=h \\times 5 f_{0}-h f_{0}$ or $\\dfrac{1}{2} m v_{2}^{2}=4 h f_{0}$<br>From case I and case II,<br>$\\dfrac{\\dfrac{1}{2} m v_{1}^{2}}{\\dfrac{1}{2} m v_{2}^{2}}=\\dfrac{1}{4} \\Rightarrow \\dfrac{v_{1}^{2}}{v_{2}^{2}}=\\dfrac{1}{4} \\Rightarrow \\dfrac{v_{1}}{v_{2}}=\\dfrac{1}{2}$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',2023,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45345,'physics',37,973,2263,'An inductive reactance of an inductor of $\\dfrac{1}{\\pi} H$ at $50\\,Hz$ frequency is:','[{"text": "$\\\\dfrac{50}{\\\\pi} \\\\Omega$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{\\\\pi}{50} \\\\Omega$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$100\\\\,\\\\Omega $", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$50\\\\,\\\\Omega $", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'$X_{L}=L \\omega=(2 \\pi v L)$<br>$ \\Rightarrow {X_L} = 2 \\times \\pi  \\times \\frac{1}{\\pi } \\times 50 = 2 \\times 50 = 100\\,\\Omega $<br>So, correct option is (3).',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45346,'physics',41,406,806,'A mixture consists of two radioactive materials ${A_1}$ and ${A_2}$ with half lives of $20\\,s$ and $10\\,s$ respectively. Initially the mixture has $40g$ of  ${A_1}$ and $160g$ of ${A_2}$. The amount of the two in the mixture will become equal after','[{"text": "$60\\\\,s$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$80\\\\,s$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$20\\\\,s$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$40\\\\,s$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'The masses of  ${A_1}$ and ${A_2}$ are<br>${m_{{A_1}}} = 40\\,{e^{ - \\lambda t}}\\& \\,{m_{{A_2}}} = 160\\,{e^{ - \\lambda t}}$<br>When ${m_{{A_1}}} = {m_{{A_2}}}$<br>${e^{ - {\\lambda _1}t}} = 4{e^{ - {\\lambda _2}t}}$<br>$4 = {e^{({\\lambda _2} - {\\lambda _1})t}}$<br>$t = \\frac{{\\ln 4}}{{{\\lambda _2} - {\\lambda _1}}} = 40\\,s$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45347,'physics',39,1104,2631,'The de-Broglie wavelength of a molecule in a gas at room temperature $(300 K)$ is $\\lambda_{1}$. If the temperature of the gas is increased to $600\\,K$, then the de Broglie wavelength of the same gas molecule becomes','[{"text": "$\\\\sqrt{2} \\\\lambda$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{1}{2} \\\\lambda_{1}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2 \\\\lambda_{1}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{1}{\\\\sqrt{2}} \\\\lambda_{1}$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'$T_{1}=300 K, \\lambda_{1}$<br>$T_{2}=600 K, \\lambda_{2}=$?<br>de-Broglie wavelength is given by<br>$\\lambda=\\dfrac{h}{P}=\\dfrac{h}{\\sqrt{2 m K E}}=\\dfrac{h}{\\sqrt{2 m \\times \\dfrac{3}{2} K T}}$<br>Here $K E=$ Average $K E$ of each molecule $=\\dfrac{3}{2} K T$.<br>$\\lambda \\propto \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{T}} \\Rightarrow \\dfrac{\\lambda_{2}}{\\lambda_{1}}=\\sqrt{\\dfrac{T_{1}}{T_{2}}}=\\sqrt{\\dfrac{300}{600}}=\\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}$<br>or $\\lambda_{2}=\\dfrac{\\lambda_{1}}{\\sqrt{2}}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2023,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45348,'physics',40,239,478,'If the radius of first Bohr orbit is $r$, then radius of second orbit will be','[{"text": "$2r$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{r}{2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$4r$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\sqrt {2r} $", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'$r\\,\\alpha \\,{n^2}$<br>$\\frac{{{r_1}}}{{{r_2}}} = {\\left( {\\frac{1}{2}} \\right)^2} \\Rightarrow {r_2} = 4{r_1}$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45349,'physics',37,973,2265,'A coil of $200\\,\\Omega $ resistance and $1\\,H$ inductance is connected to an a.c source of frequency $\\frac{{100}}{\\pi }Hz$, phase angle between potential and current will be:','[{"text": "$30^{\\\\circ}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$45^{\\\\circ}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$60^{\\\\circ}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$90^{\\\\circ}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'$\\cos \\phi=\\dfrac{R}{Z}=\\dfrac{R}{\\sqrt{(R)^{2}+\\omega^{2} L^{2}}}$<br>$\\omega=2 \\pi v=2 \\pi\\left(\\dfrac{100}{\\pi}\\right)=200 H z$<br>$\\Rightarrow \\cos \\phi=\\dfrac{200}{\\sqrt{(200)^{2}+(200)^{2} \\times(1)^{2}}}$<br>$\\Rightarrow \\cos \\phi=\\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}=\\phi=45^{\\circ}$<br>So, correct option is (2).',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45350,'physics',41,408,813,'In a nuclear reactor of the function of the  moderator is to decrease','[{"text": "Number of  neutrons", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Speed of neutrons", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Escape of neutrons", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Temperature of the reactor", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'In nuclear reactor, the moderator is used to decrease the speed of neutrons',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2017,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45370,'physics',39,1102,2626,'The photoelectric work function for photo metal is $2.4\\,eV$. Among the four wavelengths, the wavelength of light for which photo emission does not take place is','[{"text": "$200\\\\,nm$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$300\\\\,nm$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$700\\\\,nm$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$400\\\\,nm$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'For photo electric emission wavelength ${(\\lambda)}$ of radiation must be less than threshold wavelength  $({\\lambda _0}).$<br>${\\omega _0} = 2.4\\,eV$<br>${\\lambda _0} = \\frac{{12400}}{{{\\omega _0}}} = \\frac{{12400}}{{2.4}}$<br>${\\lambda _0} = 5166{A^0}$<br>$ = 516.6nm = {\\text{maximum}}$<br>${\\therefore}$ For ${\\lambda=700 {~nm}}$ photo electric effect does not take place. Correct option is (3).',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2024,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45351,'physics',39,1105,2632,'Read the Assertion and Reason carefully to mark the correct options given below:<br><br><strong>Assertion:</strong> The beam of electrons show wave nature and exhibit interference and diffraction.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Davisson Germer Experimentally verified the wave nature of electrons.<br>In the light of the above statement choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'The Davission and Germer experiment showed that electron beams can undergo diffraction when passed through the atomic crystals. This shows that the electrons behaves as waves can exhibit interference and diffraction.',NULL,'Hard','Assertion and Reason',2023,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45352,'physics',37,973,2268,'In $A C$ series circuit, the resistance, inductive reactance and capacitance reactance are $3\\,\\Omega ,10\\,\\Omega ,14\\,\\Omega $ respectively. The impedence of the circuit is:','[{"text": "$5\\\\,\\\\Omega $", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$4\\\\,\\\\Omega $", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$7\\\\,\\\\Omega $", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$10\\\\,\\\\Omega $", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Here, Resistance, $R, = 3\\,\\Omega $<br>Inductive reflectance, ${X_L} = 10\\,\\Omega $<br>Capacitive reactance, $X_{C}=14 \\Omega$<br>The impedence of the series $L C R$ circuit is:<br>$Z=\\sqrt{R^{2}+\\left(X_{C}-X_{L}\\right)^{2}}=\\sqrt{(3)^{2}+(14-10)^{2}}$<br>$ \\Rightarrow Z = 5\\,\\Omega $<br>So, correct option is (1).',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45353,'physics',40,239,478,'The electron in hydrogen atom jumps from ${1^{st}}$ orbit to the ${5^{th}}$ orbit. Change in angular momentum of electron is','[{"text": "$\\\\frac{{2h}}{\\\\pi }$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{{3h}}{{2\\\\pi }}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{h}{\\\\pi }$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{h}{{2\\\\pi }}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Angular momentum of electron is <br>$L = \\frac{{nh}}{{2\\pi }}$<br>change in $L$ is $\\Delta L = \\frac{{5h}}{{2\\pi }} - \\frac{h}{{2\\pi }} = \\frac{{2h}}{\\pi }$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45354,'physics',41,406,806,'Which one of the following nuclei has shorter mean life?','[{"text": "$C$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$A$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${\\\\text{Same for all}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$B$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'As $\\left| {\\frac{{dN}}{{dt}}} \\right|$ (activity) drops sharply in case of $A$ so disintegration constant  is higher for $A$<br>Average life (Mean life) $ = \\frac{1}{\\lambda }$<br>So, average life will be shortest for $A$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/41_45354_question_1.png'),
(45355,'physics',39,1102,2626,'The two metals $A$ and $B$ are exposed to radiation of wavelength $350\\,nm$. The work functions of metals $A$ and $B$ are $4.8\\,eV$ and $2.2\\,eV$. Then choose the correct option.','[{"text": "Both metals $A$ and $B$ will emit photoelectrons.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Metal $B$ will not emit photo-electrons.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both metals $A$ and $B$ will not emit photoelectrons.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Metal $A$ will not emit photo-electrons.", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Energy of each photon of incident radiation $ = \\frac{{hc}}{\\lambda } = \\frac{{12400}}{{3500}}eV = 3.54eV$<br>Since $3.54eV$ is less than, $\\phi_{A}$, option (4) is correct',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2023,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45356,'physics',37,973,2267,'What is the mechanical equivalent of spring constant $K$ in $L C$ oscillating circuit?','[{"text": "$\\\\dfrac{1}{L}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{1}{C}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{L}{C}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{1}{L C}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Compare the equation of electromagnetic energy of $L C$ circuit (a.c. source) with oscillations.<br>$U=\\dfrac{1}{2} L i^{2}+\\dfrac{1}{2} \\dfrac{q^{2}}{C}$<br>with the mechanical energy of spring - mass system:<br>$E_{\\text {mech }}=\\dfrac{1}{2} m v^{2}+\\dfrac{1}{2} k x^{2}$<br>$\\Rightarrow$ The mechanical equivalent will be $k=\\dfrac{1}{C}$.<br>So, the correct option is (2).',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45357,'physics',41,406,806,'Two radioactive substances $A$ and $B$ have decay constants $5\\lambda $ and  $\\lambda $ respectively. At $t = 0$ they have the same number of nuclei. The ratio of number of nuclei of $A$ to those of $B$ will be  ${\\left( {\\frac{1}{e}} \\right)^2}$ after a time interval','[{"text": "$\\\\frac{1}{{4\\\\lambda }}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{1}{{2\\\\lambda }}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$2\\\\lambda $", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$4\\\\lambda $", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'The concentrations of $A$ and $B$ are<br>${N_1} = {N_0}{e^{ - 5\\lambda t}}$                   (1)<br>${N_2} = {N_0}{e^{ - \\lambda t}}$                     (2)<br>Dividing Eq. (1) by Eq. (2), we obtain<br>$\\frac{{{N_1}}}{{{N_2}}} = {e^{( - 5\\lambda  + \\lambda )t}} = {e^{ - 4\\lambda t}} = \\frac{1}{{{e^{4\\,\\lambda t}}}} = \\frac{1}{{{e^2}}}$<br>Comparing the power, we get<br>$2 = 4\\lambda t$ or $t = \\frac{2}{{4\\lambda }} = \\frac{1}{{2\\lambda }}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45358,'physics',40,239,478,'The main defect of Bohr’s atomic model is','[{"text": "Can’t explain Zeeman effect", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Can’t explain spectra of more complex atoms", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Can’t explain fine structure of spectral lines", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "All of the above", "isCorrect": true}]',3,NULL,NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45359,'physics',39,1104,2631,'Proton $(P)$ and electron (e) will have same de-Broglie wavelength when the ratio of their momentum is (assume, $m_{P}=1849 m_{e}$ ).','[{"text": "$1: 43$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$43: 1$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1: 1849$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1: 1$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'De-Broglie wavelength, $\\lambda=\\dfrac{h}{p}$, where to is the linear momentum of particle.<br>Given $: \\dfrac{\\lambda_{\\text {proton }}}{\\lambda_{\\text {electron }}}=1$<br>$\\dfrac{h / p_{\\text {proton }}}{h / p_{\\text {electron }}}=1 \\Rightarrow \\dfrac{p_{\\text {proton }}}{p_{\\text {electron }}}=1$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2023,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45388,'physics',40,238,477,'The incorrect statement regarding Rutherford’s atomic model is','[{"text": "Atom contains nucleus", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Size of nucleus is very small in comparison to that of atom", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Nucleus contains about 70 % mass of the atom", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Electrons revolve round the nucleus with uniform speed", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'A nucleus contains more than 99.9% of the mass of the atom.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(48382,'biology',101,574,1153,'Which one can be used to control human population?','[{"text": "MTP only", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Tubectomy and Vasectomy", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Vasectomy only", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "All the above", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Tubectomy and Vasectomy are sterilization methods for both females and males respectively.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45360,'physics',37,973,2267,'In an oscillating system, a restoring force is a must. In an $L-C$ curcuit restoring force is provided by:','[{"text": "Capacitor", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Inductor", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Resistance", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "both (1) and (2)", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'In osciallting system, total energy is constant. Here, charge $(q)$ across $C$ and current (i) across $L$ is oscillating.<br>When $\\mathrm{i}$ is maximum, $q$ on $C$ is zero and all energy is stored in $L$. Then, as current starts decreasing, $q$ starts increasing .<br>$\\Rightarrow$ In this case, restoring force is provided by $L$. However, as charge reaches its maximum value, the values of $i$ tends to be zero and all energy will be stored in $C$. When is starts increasing, $q$ on $C$ will start decreasing. Hence, in this case, the restoring force is provided by C.<br>$\\Rightarrow$ Both $L$ and $C$ are essential to provide restoring force<br>So, the correct option is (4).',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45361,'physics',41,406,806,'Two radioactive materials  ${X_1}$ and  ${X_2}$ have decay constants  $5\\lambda $ and  $\\lambda $ respectively. If initially they have the same number of nuclei, then the ratio of the number of nuclei of ${X_1}$ to that of ${X_2}$ will be $\\frac{1}{e}$ after a time','[{"text": "$\\\\lambda $", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{1}{2}\\\\lambda $", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{e}{\\\\lambda }$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{1}{{4\\\\lambda }}$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'The concentrations of ${X_1}$ and ${X_2}$ are<br>${N_1} = {N_0}{\\mkern 1mu} {e^{ - 5\\lambda t}}\\,\\,\\,\\,(1)$<br>${N_2} = {N_0}{\\mkern 1mu} {e^{ - \\lambda t}}\\,\\,\\,\\,(2)$<br>Dividing Eq. (1) by Eq. (2), we obtain<br>$\\frac{{{N_1}}}{{{N_2}}} = \\frac{1}{e} = \\frac{{{e^{ - 5\\lambda t}}}}{{{e^{ - \\lambda t}}}} = {e^{ - 4\\lambda t}}$<br>$\\therefore \\quad {e^{ - 1}} = {e^{ - 4\\lambda t}} \\Rightarrow 1 = 4\\lambda t$<br>$ \\Rightarrow t = \\frac{1}{{4\\lambda }}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45362,'physics',39,1104,2631,'An $\\alpha$-particle, a proton and an electron have the same kinetic energy. Which one of the following is correct in case of their de-Broglie wavelength?','[{"text": "$\\\\lambda_{\\\\alpha}=\\\\lambda_{p}=\\\\lambda_{e}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\lambda_{\\\\alpha} < \\\\lambda_{p} < \\\\lambda_{e}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\lambda_{\\\\alpha}>\\\\lambda_{p}>\\\\lambda_{e}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\lambda_{\\\\alpha}>\\\\lambda_{p} < \\\\lambda_{c}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'$p^{2} / 2 m=E_{k} \\Rightarrow p=\\sqrt{2 m E_{k}}$.<br>$\\therefore \\lambda=$ De-Broglie wavelength $=\\dfrac{h}{p}=\\dfrac{h}{\\sqrt{2 m E_{k}}}$<br>$\\propto \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{m}}$ for same kinetic energy $E_{k}$.<br>As mass of electron is minimum out of given particles, so it has largest wavelength.<br>And mass of alpha particle is maximum, so its de-Broglie wavelength is shortest.<br>Hence, $\\lambda_{\\alpha} < \\lambda_{p} < \\lambda_{e}$<br>So, correct option is (2).',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2023,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45363,'physics',40,240,479,'Hydrogen atom does not emit $X$-rays because:','[{"text": "Its energy levels are too close to each other", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Its energy levels are too far apart", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "It has a very small mass", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "It has a single electron", "isCorrect": false}]',0,NULL,NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45364,'physics',37,974,2769,'Power factor varies between:','[{"text": "0 to 1", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "1 to 2", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "1 to 1.5", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "2 to 2.5", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Power factor is $\\cos \\phi$, and $\\cos \\phi$, varies in between $\\cos 0^{\\circ}$ to $\\cos 90^{\\circ}$, which gives range from 1 to 0 .',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45365,'physics',41,408,814,'The binding energy / nucleon of deuteron $\\left( {_1{H^2}} \\right)$ and the helium atom $\\left( {_2H{e^4}} \\right)$ are 1.1 $MeV$ and 7 $MeV$ respectively. If the two deuteron atoms fuse to form a single helium atom, then the 	energy released is','[{"text": "$26.9\\\\,MeV$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$25.8\\\\,MeV$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$23.6\\\\,MeV$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$12.9\\\\,MeV$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Binding energy of deuteron $\\left( {_1{H^2}} \\right)$<br>$ = 2 \\times 1.1\\,MeV = 2.2\\,MeV$<br>Binding energy of helium atom $\\left( {_2H{e^4}} \\right)$<br>$ = 4 \\times 7MeV = 28\\,MeV$<br>$_1{H^2}{ + _1}{H^2}{ \\to _2}H{e^4} + $ energy released<br>Energy released<br>$ = $ $BE$ of helium $ - 2 \\times BE$ of deuteron<br>$ = 28\\,MeV - 2 \\times 2.2\\,MeV$<br>$ = 28\\,MeV - 4.4\\,MeV = 23.6\\,MeV$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2013,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45366,'physics',39,1102,2629,'Light of energy ${E}$ falls normally on a metal of work function ${E / 3}$. The kinetic energies ${(K)}$ of the photo electrons are','[{"text": "${K=\\\\dfrac{2 E}{3}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${K=\\\\dfrac{E}{3}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${0 \\\\leq K \\\\leq \\\\dfrac{2 E}{3}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${0 \\\\leq K \\\\leq \\\\dfrac{E}{3}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'From Einstein\'s equation for photoelectric emission, maximum kinetic energy of photo electrons is given by<br>$K{E_{\\max }} = E - {\\omega _0}$<br>$ = E - \\frac{E}{3} = \\frac{{2E}}{3}$<br>${\\therefore K E}$ lies from 0 to ${\\dfrac{2 E}{3}}$.<br>Correct option is (3).',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2024,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45367,'physics',37,975,2276,'At resonance the peak value of current in $L-C-R$ series circuit is','[{"text": "$E_{0} / Z$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{E_{0}}{\\\\sqrt{R^{2}+\\\\left(\\\\omega L-\\\\dfrac{1}{\\\\omega C}\\\\right)^{2}}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\sqrt{2} \\\\sqrt{R^{2}+\\\\left(\\\\omega^{2} L-\\\\dfrac{1}{\\\\omega^{2} C^{2}}\\\\right)^{2}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{E_{0}}{\\\\sqrt{2} R}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'$Z=\\sqrt{R^{2}+\\left(X_{L}-X_{C}\\right)^{2}}$<br>At resonance, $X_{L}=X_{C}$<br>$\\Rightarrow Z=R$<br>$\\Rightarrow I_{\\text {peak }}=\\dfrac{E_{0}}{Z}$<br>So, correct option is (1).',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45368,'physics',40,238,477,'If an $\\alpha $-particle of mass $m$, charged $q$ and velocity $v$ is incident on a nucleus of charge $Q$ and mass $m$, then the distance of closest  approach is:','[{"text": "$\\\\frac{{Qq}}{{4\\\\pi {\\\\varepsilon _0}{m^2}}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{{Qq}}{{2\\\\pi {\\\\varepsilon _0}m{v^2}}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{{Qqm{v^2}}}{2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{{Qq}}{{m{v^2}}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'$\\frac{1}{2}m{v^2} = \\frac{1}{{4\\pi {\\varepsilon _0}}}\\frac{{Qq}}{{{r_0}}} \\Rightarrow {r_0} = \\frac{{Qq}}{{2\\pi {\\varepsilon _0}m{v^2}}}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45369,'physics',41,408,814,'A certain mass of hydrogen is changed to helium by the process of fusion. The mass defect in fusion reaction is 0.02866 $u$. The energy liberated per $u $ is<br>(given $1u = 931\\,MeV$)','[{"text": "$2.67\\\\,MeV$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$26.7\\\\,MeV$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$6.675\\\\,MeV$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$13.35\\\\,MeV$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Energy liberated $E = \\Delta m{c^2}$<br>$ = 0.028664\\,u \\times 931\\frac{{MeV}}{u} = 26.7MeV$<br>As four nucleons are involved in the reaction the energy liberated per each nucleon (1$u$) is<br>$\\frac{{26.7MeV}}{4} = 6.675MeV$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45371,'physics',37,973,2268,'A coil of self - inductance $\\left(\\dfrac{1}{\\pi}\\right) H$ is connected in series with a $300\\,\\Omega $ resistance. A voltage of $200\\,V$ at frequency $200\\,Hz$ is applied to this combination. The phase difference between the voltage and the current will be','[{"text": "$\\\\tan ^{-1}\\\\left(\\\\dfrac{4}{3}\\\\right)$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\tan ^{-1}\\\\left(\\\\dfrac{3}{4}\\\\right)$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\tan ^{-1}\\\\left(\\\\dfrac{1}{4}\\\\right)$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\tan ^{-1}\\\\left(\\\\dfrac{5}{4}\\\\right)$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'In the $L R$ series circuit, $\\tan \\phi=\\dfrac{X_{L}}{R}$  <img src=\'/img/data/physics/37_45371_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />  Where $\\phi$ is required phase difference $X_{L}=L \\omega$<br>$\\begin{aligned}& =L(2 \\pi f) \\\\& =\\left(\\dfrac{1}{\\pi}\\right)(2 \\pi)(200) \\\\& =400 \\Omega\\end{aligned}$<br>$\\begin{aligned}\\phi & =\\tan ^{-1}\\left(\\dfrac{X_{L}}{R}\\right) \\\\& =\\tan ^{-1}\\left(\\dfrac{400}{300}\\right)=\\tan ^{-1}\\left(\\dfrac{4}{3}\\right)\\end{aligned}$<br>So, the correct option is (1).',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45372,'physics',41,406,806,'Activity of a radioactive sample decreases to ${(1/3)^{rd}}$ of its original value in 3 days. Then, in 9 days its activity will become','[{"text": "$(1/27)$ of the original value", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$(1/9)$ of the original value", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$(1/18)$ of the original value", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$(1/3)$ of the original value", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Just as the time required for  the activity to be 	reduced to half the original value is called half  life, to be reduced to 1/3 of the  original activity  can be called 1/3 life <br>There are 3 one third lives in 9 days, hence\n the activity will be reduced to ${\\left( {1/3} \\right)^3} = 1/27$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2006,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45373,'physics',40,238,477,'In Rutherford scattering experiment, what will be the correct angle for $\\alpha $ scattering for an impact parameter $b = 0$?','[{"text": "$90^\\\\circ $", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$270^\\\\circ $", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$0^\\\\circ $", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$180^\\\\circ $", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'We know that impact parameter, <br>$b\\,\\alpha \\,\\cot \\frac{\\theta }{2} \\Rightarrow b = K\\,\\cot \\frac{\\theta }{2}$<br>Here, $K$ is constant $b = 0$. Hence,$\\theta  = 180^\\circ $',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45374,'physics',39,1104,2631,'The graph which shows the variation of ${\\left(\\dfrac{1}{\\lambda^{2}}\\right)}$ and its kinetic energy, ${E}$ is (where ${\\lambda}$ is de Broglie wavelength of a free particle):<div class="matching-question"></div>','[{"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'de-Broglie wavelength<br>${\\lambda=\\dfrac{h}{P}=\\dfrac{h}{m v}=\\dfrac{h}{\\sqrt{2 m E}}}$ where ${E=\\dfrac{1}{2} m v^{2}}$<br>Squaring both sides,<br>$\\lambda^{2}=\\dfrac{h^{2}}{2 m E} \\Rightarrow \\dfrac{1}{\\lambda^{2}}=(\\text { constant }) E$<br>Graph passes through origin with constant slope. Correct option is (4).',NULL,'Medium','Graph/Figure',2024,'NEET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45375,'physics',37,973,2268,'In a series $L C R$ circuit $R = 10\\,\\Omega $ and the impedance $Z = 20\\,\\Omega $. Then the phase difference between the current and the voltage is','[{"text": "$60^{\\\\circ}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$30^{\\\\circ}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$45^{\\\\circ}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$90^{\\\\circ}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'$\\cos \\phi=\\dfrac{R}{Z}=\\dfrac{10}{20}=\\dfrac{1}{2}$<br>$\\Rightarrow \\phi=60^{\\circ}$<br>So, correct option is (1).',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45376,'physics',41,406,806,'A radioactive sample of half life 10 days contains 1000$x$ nuclei. Number of original nuclei present after 5 days is','[{"text": "$707x$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$750x$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$500x$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$250x$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Half life of sample  ${\\tau _{1/2}} = 10$ days<br>Initial number of nuclei, ${N_0} = 1000x,t = 5$ days<br>$N(t) = {N_0}{\\left( {\\frac{1}{2}} \\right)^{\\frac{t}{{{\\tau _{\\frac{1}{2}}}}}}}$<br>$ = {N_0}{\\left( {\\frac{1}{2}} \\right)^{\\frac{5}{{10}}}}$<br>$N\\left( t \\right) = \\frac{{{N_0}}}{{\\sqrt 2 }} = \\frac{{1000x}}{{\\sqrt 2 }} = 707x$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2016,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45377,'physics',40,238,477,'Match the following<div class="matching-question"><div class="column-left">A. Thomson<br>B. Rutherford<br>C. Bohr<br>D. Sommerfeld</div><div class="column-right">P. Radiating circular orbit<br>Q. Radiating SHM of electron<br>R. Eliptical orbit<br>S. Non-radiating orbit</div></div>','[{"text": "A-P,Q, \\tB-R, \\t\\tC-S, \\tD-Q", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "A-Q, \\tB-P, \\t\\tC-S, \\tD-R,S", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "A-Q,S, \\tB-P, \\t\\tC-R, \\tD-Q", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "A-Q, \\tB-P, \\t\\tC-S, \\tD-R", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Option (2) is correct.',NULL,'Easy','Match the following',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45378,'physics',39,1102,2626,'If ${c}$ is the velocity of light in free space, the correct statements about photon among the following are:<br>A. The energy of a photon is ${E=h v}$.<br>B. The velocity of a photon is ${c}$.<br>C. The momentum of a photon, ${p=\\dfrac{h v}{c}}$.<br>D. In a photon-electron collision, both total energy and total momentum are conserved.<br>E. Photon possesses positive charge.<br>Choose the correct answer from the options given below:','[{"text": "A and B only", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "A, B, C and D only", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "A, C and D only", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "A, B, D and E only", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'If ${c}$ is the velocity of light so, ${E=h v}$<br>Velocity of photon is equal to velocity of light ${(c)}$.<br>$\\lambda=\\dfrac{h}{p} \\Rightarrow p=\\dfrac{h}{\\lambda} \\Rightarrow p=\\dfrac{h v}{c}$<br>In photon-electron collision both total energy and total momentum are conserved.Correct option is (2).',NULL,'Hard','MCQ type-2',2024,'NEET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45379,'physics',37,973,2265,'In the circuit shown, a $30\\,V$ d.c. source gives a current $2.0\\,A$ as recorded in the ammeter $A$ and $30\\,V$ a.c. source of frequency gives a current $1.2\\,V$. The inductive reactance is (in $ohms$)','[{"text": "30", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "20", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$5 \\\\sqrt{34}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "40", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'When $d.c.$ source, $R=\\dfrac{V}{1}=\\dfrac{30}{2}=15 \\Omega$<br>When $a.c.$ source, $Z=\\dfrac{30}{1.2}=25 \\Omega$<br>We know for $L-R$ circuit $Z^{2}=R_{2}^{2}+X_{L}^{2}$<br>$X_{L}=\\sqrt{(25)^{2}-(15)^{2}}=\\sqrt{625-225}=20 \\Omega$<br>So, correct option is (2).',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/37_45379_question_1.png'),
(45431,'physics',41,406,809,'The particles emitted in the decay of ${}_{92}^{238}U$ to ${}_{92}^{234}U$','[{"text": "${\\\\text{2}}\\\\alpha {\\\\text{ and 2}}\\\\beta $", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1\\\\alpha {\\\\text{ and 2}}\\\\beta $", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$1\\\\alpha {\\\\text{ and 1}}\\\\beta $", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1\\\\alpha {\\\\text{ only}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'${}_{92}^{238}U \\to {}_{92}^{234}U + x{}_2^4\\alpha  + y{}_{ - 1}^0\\beta $<br>Using law of conservation of charges and masses<br> $238 = 234 + 4x\\,{\\text{or}}\\,x = 1$ and $92 = 92 + 2x - y$ or $y = 2x = 2$<br>Therefore, one  $\\alpha $ and two $\\beta $ particles are emitted',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2017,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45380,'physics',41,406,806,'The masses of two radioactive substances are same and their half lives are 1 year and 2 years respectively. The ratio of their activities after 6 years will be','[{"text": "$1:3$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1:6$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1:4$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$1:2$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Let us assume that the initial number of radioactive nuclei are the same<br>Sample $ - 1$: Half - life  ${T_{1/2}} = 1$ year<br>$\\therefore $ After $t = 6$ years, number of nuclei for 1st sample <br>${N_1} = {N_0}{\\left( {\\frac{1}{2}} \\right)^{\\frac{t}{{{T_{1/2}}}}}} = {N_0}{\\left( {\\frac{1}{2}} \\right)^{\\frac{6}{1}}} = \\frac{{{N_0}}}{{64}}$<br>Sample $ - 2$: Half life  ${T_{1/2}} = 2$ years<br>$\\therefore $ After $t = 6$ years, number of nuclei for 2nd sample<br>${N_2} = {N_0}{\\left( {\\frac{1}{2}} \\right)^{\\frac{t}{{{T_{1/2}}}}}} = {N_0}{\\left( {\\frac{1}{2}} \\right)^{\\frac{6}{2}}} = \\frac{{{N_0}}}{8}$<br>$\\frac{{d{N_1}}}{{dt}} =  - {\\lambda _1}.\\frac{{{N_0}}}{{64}} \\Rightarrow \\frac{{d{N_1}}}{{dt}} =  - \\frac{{0.693}}{{1yr}}.\\frac{{{N_0}}}{{64}}$<br>$\\frac{{d{N_2}}}{{dt}} =  - {\\lambda _2}.\\frac{{{N_0}}}{8} \\Rightarrow \\frac{{d{N_2}}}{{dt}} =  - \\frac{{0.693}}{{2yr}}.\\frac{{{N_0}}}{8}$<br>$\\therefore $ Ratio of activities $ = \\frac{{2 \\times 8}}{{1 \\times 64}} = \\frac{1}{4}$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2008,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45381,'physics',39,1102,2627,'Light of power $1.5\\,mW$ and wavelength $400\\,nm$ is directed at a photoelectric cell. If ${0.10 \\%}$ of the incident photons produce photoelectrons, the current in the cell is','[{"text": "$0.36\\\\,\\\\mu A$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$0.48\\\\,{\\\\mkern 1mu} \\\\mu A$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$0.42\\\\,mA$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$0.32\\\\,mA$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Number of photoelectron emitted per second, ${n=\\dfrac{P}{h c / \\lambda}}$<br>${=\\dfrac{1.5 \\times 10^{-3} {~W} \\times\\left(10^{-3}\\right)}{\\dfrac{1240({~nm})({eV})}{400({~nm})} \\times e({~V} / {s})}}$<br>${=\\dfrac{0.48}{e} \\times 10^{-6}}$<br>${\\therefore}$ Photo current ${=n e=0.48 \\mu {A}}$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45382,'physics',37,973,2271,'In a black box of unknown elements ( $L, C$ or $R$ or any other combination) an $A C$ voltage $E=E_{0} \\sin (\\omega t+\\phi)$ is applied and current in the circuit was found to be $i=i_{0} \\sin (\\omega t+\\phi+\\pi / 4)$. Then the unknown elements in the box may be','[{"text": "Only capacitor", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Inductor and resistor both", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Either capacitor, resistor and inductor or only capacitor and resistor", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Only resistor", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Here current leads the voltage by $\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}$. So, there is reactance which is capacitive.<br>$\\Rightarrow X=\\left(X_{C}-X_{L}\\right)$ with $X_{L}>X_{C}$ or $X=X_{C}$<br>$\\Rightarrow(L C R)$ or $(C R)$ is possible with $X_{C}>X_{L}$.<br>By the given expression, $I$ leads $E\\left(\\right.$ by $\\left.\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}\\right)$<br>In each of options, it will be as below:<br>(1) If only $C$, the voltage shall lag behind current by $\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}$.<br>(2) If only $L$ and $R$, the overall voltage will lead the current by some phase angle.<br>(3) With only $C$ and $R$, the net voltage will lag current.<br>It is possible for voltage to lag by $\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}$ for some voltage of $C$ and $R$. Similarly, with all three elements of $L-C-R$, voltage can lag current, provided $V_{C}>V_{R}$.<br>(4) In pure resistor case, voltage and current are in phase.<br>So, option (3) is correct .',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/37_45382_question_1.png'),
(45384,'physics',41,406,806,'A radioactive substance contains 10,000 nuclei and its half - life period is 20 days. The number of nuclei present at the end of 10 days is','[{"text": "$7070$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$9000$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$8000$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$7500$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'${N_0} = $ Initial number of nuclei <br>${T_{1/2}} = $ Half -life  $N = $ Number of nuclei at time $t$<br>$\\frac{N}{{{N_0}}} = {\\left( {\\frac{1}{2}} \\right)^{\\frac{t}{{{T_{1/2}}}}}}$ or $\\frac{N}{{10000}} = {\\left( {\\frac{1}{2}} \\right)^{\\frac{{10}}{{20}}}}$<br>or $N = \\frac{{10000 \\times 1}}{{\\sqrt 2 }} = \\frac{{10000}}{{1.414}} = 7070$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45385,'physics',39,1102,2626,'A photon of wavelength $4400$ $ \\mathop A^{~~\\circ}  $ is passing through vaccum. The effective mass and momentum of the photon are respectively','[{"text": "${5 \\\\times 10^{-36} {~kg}, 1.5 \\\\times 10^{-27} {~kg} {~ms}^{-1}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${5 \\\\times 10^{-35} {~kg}, 1.5 \\\\times 10^{-26} {~kg} {~ms}^{-1}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "zero, ${1.5 \\\\times 10^{-26} {~kg} {~ms}^{-1}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${5 \\\\times 10^{-36} {~kg}, 1.67 \\\\times 10^{-43} {~kg} {~ms}^{-1}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'from de Broglie equation,<br>$P = \\frac{h}{\\lambda } = \\frac{{6.6 \\times {{10}^{ - 34}}}}{{4400 \\times {{10}^{ - 10}}}} = 1.5 \\times {10^{ - 27}}\\,kg\\,m{s^{ - 1}}$<br>For photon, velocity  $ = c = 3 \\times {10^{ - 8}}\\,kg\\,m{s^{ - 1}}$<br>$\\therefore \\quad m = \\frac{P}{c} = \\frac{{1.5 \\times {{10}^{ - 27}}}}{{3 \\times {{10}^{ - 8}}}} = 5 \\times {10^{ - 36}}\\,kg$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45386,'physics',37,974,2274,'An $A$. $C$ voltage $E = 200\\sin 300\\,t$ is applied across a series combination of $R = 10\\,\\Omega $ and $L = 800\\,mH$. Calculate the power factor of the circuit.','[{"text": "0.0315", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "0.416", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "0.0416", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "0.417", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'$X_{L}=\\omega L=300 \\times 800 \\times 10^{-3}=240 \\Omega$  <img src=\'/img/data/physics/37_45386_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />  $Z=\\sqrt{R^{2}+X_{L}^{2}}=\\sqrt{(10)^{2}+(240)^{2}}=240.2 \\Omega$<br>Power factor, $\\cos \\phi=\\dfrac{R}{Z}=\\dfrac{10}{240.2}=0.0416$<br>So, correct option is (3).',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45387,'physics',41,406,806,'The fraction of the initial number of radioactive nuclei which remain undecayed after half of a half - life of the radioactive sample is','[{"text": "$\\\\frac{1}{4}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{1}{{2\\\\sqrt 2 }}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{1}{2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{1}{{\\\\sqrt 2 }}$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'${N_0}$ is the initial number of radioactive nuclei and $N$  is the number of nuclei that remains undecayed.<br>Thus, $\\frac{N}{{{N_0}}} = {\\left( {\\frac{1}{2}} \\right)^n}$<br>where $n$ is the number of half lives<br>As $n = \\frac{t}{{{T_{1/2}}}} = \\frac{{\\frac{1}{2}{T_{1/2}}}}{{{T_{1/2}}}} = \\frac{1}{2}$<br>$\\therefore \\frac{N}{{{N_0}}} = {\\left( {\\frac{1}{2}} \\right)^{1/2}} = \\frac{1}{{\\sqrt 2 }}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2011,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45389,'physics',37,974,2766,'Calculate the value of inductance which should be connected in series with a capacitance of $5\\,\\mu F$ and a resistance of $10\\,\\Omega $ and an $a.c$ source of $50\\,Hz$, so that the power factor of the circuit is unity.','[{"text": "$2.028\\\\,H$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2.029\\\\,H$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2.026{\\\\mkern 1mu} H$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$2.01\\\\,H$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'$L = ?,C = 5{\\mkern 1mu} \\mu F = 5 \\times {10^{ - 6}}\\,F,R$<br>$ = 10{\\mkern 1mu} \\Omega ,v = 50\\,Hz$<br>Power factor is unity, when $X_{L}=X_{C}$<br>$\\omega L=\\dfrac{1}{\\omega C}$ or $L=\\dfrac{1}{\\omega^{2} C}$<br>$\\Rightarrow L=\\dfrac{1}{(2 \\pi \\times 50)^{2} \\times 5 \\times 10^{-6}}$<br>$ \\Rightarrow L = 2.026\\,H$<br>So, correct option is (3).',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45390,'physics',39,1102,2626,'A $2\\,mW$ laser operates at a wavelength of $500\\,nm$. The number of photons that will be emitted per second is [Given Planck\'s constant  $h = 6.6 \\times {10^{ - 34}}\\,Js,$ speed of light ${\\left.c=3.0 \\times 10^{8} {~m} / {s}\\right]}$','[{"text": "${2 \\\\times 10^{16}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${1.5 \\\\times 10^{16}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${5 \\\\times 10^{15}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${1 \\\\times 10^{16}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'${P=\\dfrac{n h c}{\\lambda}}$<br>${n=\\dfrac{P \\lambda}{h c}}$<br>${=\\dfrac{2 \\times 10^{-3} \\times 500 \\times 10^{-9}}{6.6 \\times 10^{-34} \\times 3 \\times 10^{8}}=1.5 \\times 10^{16}}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45391,'physics',41,406,806,'Decay constants of two radio - active  samples  $A$ and $B$ are 15$x$ and 3$x$ respectively. They have equal number of initial nuclei. The ratio of the number of nuclei left in $A$ and $B$ after a time 1/6$x$ is','[{"text": "$e$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${e^2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${e^{ - 1}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${e^{ - 2}}$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'According to radioactive decay<br>$N = {N_0}{e^{ - \\lambda t}}$<br>Where<br>${N_0} = $ initial number of nuclei at $t = 0$<br>$N = $ number of nuclei left undecayed after time $t$<br>$\\lambda  = $ decay constant<br>For $A$,  ${N_A} = {N_{0A}}{e^{ - \\left( {15x} \\right)\\left( {1/6x} \\right)}} = {N_{0A}}{e^{ - 5/2}}$<br>For $B$, ${N_B} = {N_{0B}}{e^{ - \\left( {3x} \\right)\\left( {1/6x} \\right)}} = {N_{0B}}{e^{ - 1/2}}$<br>$\\therefore \\frac{{{N_A}}}{{{N_B}}} = \\frac{{{e^{ - 5/2}}}}{{{e^{ - 1/2}}}} = {e^{ - 2}}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2012,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45393,'physics',37,974,2274,'The coefficient of induction of a choke coil is $0.1\\,H$ and resistance is $12\\,\\Omega $. If it is connected to an a.c source of frequency $60\\,Hz$. Then the power factor will be:','[{"text": "0.35", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "0.30", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "0.28", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "0.24", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'As we know (with $\\omega=2 \\pi f$ )<br>$\\cos \\phi=\\dfrac{R}{Z}=\\dfrac{R}{\\sqrt{R^{2}+\\omega^{2} L^{2}}}$<br>$=\\dfrac{12}{\\sqrt{(12)^{2}+4 \\times \\pi^{2} \\times(60)^{2} \\times(0.1)^{2}}}$<br>$\\Rightarrow \\cos \\phi=0.30$<br>So, correct option is (2).',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45394,'physics',39,1103,2630,'A $200\\,W$ sodium street lamp emits yellow light of wavelength ${0.6 \\mu {m}}$. Assuming it to be ${25 \\%}$ efficient in converting electrical energy to light, the number of photons of yellow light it emits per second is','[{"text": "${62 \\\\times 10^{20}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${3 \\\\times 10^{19}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${1.5 \\\\times 10^{20}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${6 \\\\times 10^{18}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Power converted to light<br>${P_{1}=\\dfrac{25}{100} \\times 200=50 {~W}}$<br>Energy of photon ${E=\\dfrac{h c}{\\lambda}=3.31 \\times 10^{-20}}$<br>Now, ${{n}=\\dfrac{\\text { Amount of power converted to light }}{\\text { Energy of photon }}}$<br>${=\\dfrac{50}{33.1 \\times 10^{-20}}=1.5 \\times 10^{20}}$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45395,'physics',41,406,806,'In a radioactive disintegrations, the ratio of initial number of atoms to the number of atoms present at an instant of time equal to its mean life is','[{"text": "$1/{e^2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1/e$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$e$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${e^2}$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Let the initial number of atoms at time $t = 0$ be ${N_0}$.<br>Let the number of atoms at any instant of time $t$ be $N$.<br>According to radioactive decay law, <br>$N = {N_0}{e^{ - \\lambda t}}$<br>Mean life $\\tau  = 1/\\lambda $, where  $\\lambda $ is the decay constant<br>here $t = \\tau $  (given)<br>$\\therefore N = {N_0}{e^{ - \\frac{1}{\\tau } \\times \\tau }}$ or  $N = {N_0}{e^{ - 1}}$ or $\\frac{{{N_0}}}{N} = e$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2010,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45396,'physics',37,973,2268,'In an $L-C-R$ series circuit, $R = \\sqrt 5 \\,\\Omega ,{X_L} = 9\\,\\Omega ,{X_C} = 7\\,\\Omega $. If applied voltage in the circuit is $50\\,V$ then impedance of the circuit (in $ohm$) will be','[{"text": "2", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "3", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$2 \\\\sqrt{5}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$3 \\\\sqrt{5}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'$Z=\\sqrt{R^{2}+\\left(X_{L}-X_{C}\\right)^{2}}$<br>$\\Rightarrow|Z|=\\sqrt{5+4}=3 \\Omega$<br>So, correct option is (2).',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45397,'physics',39,1104,2631,'A proton and photon both have same energies of ${E=100 {ke} V}$. If the de broglie wavelength of proton and photon be ${\\lambda_{1}}$ and ${\\lambda_{2}}$, then ${\\lambda_{1} / \\lambda_{2}}$ is proportional to','[{"text": "${E^{-1 / 2}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${E^{1 / 2}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${E^{-1}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${E}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'${\\dfrac{\\lambda_{1}}{\\lambda_{2}}=\\dfrac{\\dfrac{h}{\\sqrt{2 m E}}}{\\dfrac{h c}{E}}}$<br>${\\Rightarrow \\dfrac{\\lambda_{1}}{\\lambda_{2}} \\propto E^{1 / 2}}$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45420,'physics',39,1104,2631,'Velocity of a proton is ${\\dfrac{c}{20}}$. Associated de broglie wavelength is','[{"text": "${2.64 \\\\times 10^{-24} {~mm}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${2.64 \\\\times 10^{-24} {~cm}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2.64 \\\\times {10^{ - 14}}$  $ \\\\mathop A^{~~\\\\circ}  $", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${2.64 \\\\times 10^{-14} {~m}}$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'${\\lambda=\\dfrac{h}{m v}}$<br>${\\because v=\\dfrac{c}{20}=\\dfrac{3 \\times 10^{8}}{20}=1.5 \\times 10^{7} {~m} / {s}}$<br>${h=6.62 \\times 10^{-34} {Js}, m=1.67 \\times 10^{-27} {~kg}}$<br>$\\therefore \\lambda  = \\frac{{6.62 \\times {{10}^{ - 34}}}}{{1.6 \\times {{10}^{ - 27}} \\times 1.5 \\times {{10}^7}}}$<br>$ = 2.64 \\times {10^{ - 14}}\\,m$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45398,'physics',40,240,479,'In the line spectra of hydrogen atom, the difference between the largest and the shortest wavelengths of the Lyman series is $304\\mathop A\\limits^o $. The corresponding difference for the Paschan series in $\\mathop A\\limits^o $  is : ____.','[{"text": "10533", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "1055", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "3550", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "15501", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'From Bohr\'s formula for hydrogen atom,<br>$\\frac{1}{\\lambda } = R\\left( {\\frac{1}{{n_1^2}} - \\frac{1}{{n_2^2}}} \\right)$<br>$R = 1.097 \\times {10^7}\\,{m^{ - 1}}$<br>For Lyman series<br>$\\frac{1}{{{\\lambda _{\\min {\\text{ }}}}}} = R(1) = R\\quad \\because {n_2} = \\infty ,{n_1} = 1$<br>$\\frac{1}{{{\\lambda _{\\max {\\text{ }}}}}} = R\\left\\{ {1 - \\frac{1}{4}} \\right\\} = \\frac{{3R}}{4}\\quad \\because {n_1} = 2,{n_1} = 1$<br>$\\therefore {\\lambda _{\\max {\\text{ }}}} - {\\lambda _{\\min {\\text{ }}}} = \\frac{4}{{3R}} - \\frac{1}{R} = \\frac{1}{{3R}} = 304$ (given)<br>For Paschen series<br>${\\lambda _{{{\\max }^1}}} = R\\left( {\\frac{1}{9} - \\frac{1}{{16}}} \\right) = \\frac{{7R}}{{16 \\times 9}}$ and<br>$\\lambda _{\\min {\\text{ }}}^1 = R\\left( {\\frac{1}{9}} \\right)$<br>$\\lambda _{\\max {\\text{ }}}^1 - \\lambda _{\\min {\\text{ }}}^1 = \\frac{{16 \\times 9}}{{7R}} - \\frac{9}{R} = \\frac{{81}}{{7R}}$<br>$\\lambda _{\\max {\\text{ }}}^1 - \\lambda _{\\min {\\text{ }}}^1 = \\frac{{81}}{{7R}} = \\frac{{81 \\times 3}}{{7 \\times 3R}} = \\frac{{81 \\times 3}}{7} \\times 304$<br>${\\lambda _{{{\\max }^1}}} - \\lambda _{\\min {\\text{ }}}^1 = 10553.14$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2020,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45399,'physics',41,406,806,'A radioactive element has half life of 15 years. What is the fraction that will decay in 30 years?','[{"text": "$0.75$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$0.85$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$0.25$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$0.5$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Given, that half life  ${T_{1/2}} = 15$ yrs and time, $t = 30$ yrs<br>Fraction of nucleus undecayed after $n$ half lives,<br>$\\frac{N}{{{N_0}}} = {\\left( {\\frac{1}{2}} \\right)^n} = {\\left( {\\frac{1}{2}} \\right)^{t/{t_{1/2}}}} = {\\left( {\\frac{1}{2}} \\right)^{30/15}} = \\frac{1}{4} = 0.25$<br>$\\therefore $ Fraction that will decay $ = (1 - 0.25) = 0.75$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45400,'physics',37,974,2273,'An $a c$ supply gives ${(30\\,V)_{r,m.s}}$ which passed through a $100\\,\\Omega $ resistance. The power dissipated in it is:','[{"text": "$30\\\\,W$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$90\\\\,W$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$40\\\\,W$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$9\\\\,W$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'$P = \\left( {\\frac{{V_{rms}^2}}{R}} \\right) = \\frac{{{{(30)}^2}}}{{100}} = \\frac{{900}}{{100}} = 9\\,W$<br>So, correct option is (4).',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45401,'physics',39,1104,2631,'A particle ${P}$ is formed due to a completely inelastic collision of particles ${x}$ and ${y}$, having de-Broglie wavelengths ${\\lambda_{x}}$ and ${\\lambda_{y}}$ respectively. If ${x}$ and ${y}$ were moving in opposite directions, then the de Broglie wavelength of ${p}$ is','[{"text": "${\\\\lambda_{x}+\\\\lambda_{y}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\dfrac{\\\\lambda_{x} \\\\lambda_{y}}{\\\\lambda_{x}+\\\\lambda_{y}}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\dfrac{\\\\lambda_{x} \\\\lambda_{y}}{\\\\left|\\\\lambda_{x}-\\\\lambda_{y}\\\\right|}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${\\\\lambda_{x}-\\\\lambda_{y}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'<img src=\'/img/data/physics/39_45401_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' /> By momentum conservation,<br>${p_{x}-p_{y}=p_{p}}$<br>${\\dfrac{h}{\\lambda_{x}}-\\dfrac{h}{\\lambda_{y}}=\\dfrac{h}{\\lambda_{p}}}$<br>${\\lambda_{p}=\\dfrac{\\lambda_{x} \\lambda_{y}}{\\left|\\lambda_{x}-\\lambda_{y}\\right|}}$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45402,'physics',41,406,806,'A radioactive substance emits 100 beta particles in the first 2 seconds and 50 beta particles in the next 2 seconds. The mean life of the sample is','[{"text": "4 seconds", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "2 seconds", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{2}{{0.693}}$ second", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$2 \\\\times 0.693$ second", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'The half life of the radioactive substance is ${T_{1/2}} = 2$ seconds since activity has halved in 2 seconds from 100 to 50.<br>Mean life $ = \\frac{{{T_{1/2}}}}{{\\ln 2}} = \\frac{2}{{0.693}}$ seconds',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2013,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45404,'physics',37,974,2273,'The average power dissipation in a pure capacitance $a.c$ circuit is:','[{"text": "$\\\\dfrac{C V^{2}}{2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{CV}^{2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{C V^{2}}{4}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Zero", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Average power in $a c$ circuit is given by:<br>$P=V_{r m s} I_{r m s} \\cos \\phi$<br>For pure capacitor circuit $\\phi=90^{\\circ} \\Rightarrow \\cos \\phi=0$<br>$\\Rightarrow P=0$. So, correct option is (4).',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45405,'physics',39,1104,2631,'Two particles are moving at right angle to each other, and their de Broglie wavelengths are ${\\lambda_{1}}$ and ${\\lambda_{2}}$, respectively. The particles suffer perfectly inelastic collision. The de Broglie wavelength ${\\lambda}$ of the final particle is given by','[{"text": "${\\\\lambda=\\\\dfrac{\\\\lambda_{1}+\\\\lambda_{2}}{2}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\dfrac{2}{\\\\lambda}=\\\\dfrac{1}{\\\\lambda_{1}}+\\\\dfrac{1}{\\\\lambda_{2}}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\lambda=\\\\sqrt{\\\\lambda_{1} \\\\lambda_{2}}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\dfrac{1}{\\\\lambda^{2}}=\\\\dfrac{1}{\\\\lambda_{1}^{2}}+\\\\dfrac{1}{\\\\lambda_{2}^{2}}}$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'<img src=\'/img/data/physics/39_45405_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' /> ${\\vec{p}_{1}=\\dfrac{h}{\\lambda_{1}} \\hat{i}}$ and ${\\vec{p}_{2}=\\dfrac{h}{\\lambda_{2}} \\hat{j} n}$<br>Using momentum conservation,<br>${\\vec{p}=\\vec{p}_{1}+\\vec{p}_{2}}$<br>${=\\dfrac{h}{\\lambda_{1}} \\hat{i}+\\dfrac{h}{\\lambda_{2}} \\hat{j}}$<br>${|\\vec{p}|=\\sqrt{\\left(\\dfrac{h}{\\lambda_{1}}\\right)^{2}+\\left(\\dfrac{h}{\\lambda_{2}}\\right)^{2}}}$<br>${\\dfrac{h}{\\lambda}=\\sqrt{\\left(\\dfrac{h}{\\lambda_{1}}\\right)^{2}+\\left(\\dfrac{h}{\\lambda_{2}}\\right)^{2}}}$<br>${\\dfrac{1}{\\lambda^{2}}=\\dfrac{1}{\\lambda_{1}^{2}}+\\dfrac{1}{\\lambda_{2}^{2}}}$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45406,'physics',41,406,806,'The relation between half life ($T$) and decay constant ($\\lambda $) is','[{"text": "$\\\\lambda T = {\\\\log _e}2$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\lambda T = 1$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\lambda  = \\\\log 2T$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\lambda T = \\\\frac{1}{2}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'The relation between half life ($T$) and decay constant $\\left( \\lambda  \\right)$ is <br>$T = \\frac{{\\ln 2}}{\\lambda } = \\frac{{{{\\log }_e}2}}{\\lambda }$<br>or $T\\lambda  = {\\log _e}2$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2014,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45407,'physics',37,974,2273,'A choke coil has negligible resistance. The alternating potential drop across it is $220\\,volts$ and the current is $5\\,mA.$ The power consumed is','[{"text": "$220 \\\\times \\\\frac{5}{{1000}}\\\\,W$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{{220}}{5}\\\\,W$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Zero", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$2.20 \\\\times 5\\\\,W$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Phase angle $=\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}$ (between $V$ and $I$ )<br>$\\Rightarrow \\cos \\phi=0$<br>$\\Rightarrow \\cos \\phi=0$<br>$\\Rightarrow\\langle P\\rangle=0$<br>Since $\\langle P\\rangle=V I \\cos \\phi$<br>Average power is zero.<br>So, correct option is (3).',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45409,'physics',39,1102,2628,'The stopping potential of most energetic photoelectron emitted from a metal becomes three times of initial value when the wavelength of incident radiation is reduced from ${\\lambda_{1}}$ to ${\\lambda_{2}}$. The work function of metal is','[{"text": "${\\\\dfrac{h c}{2 \\\\lambda_{1} \\\\lambda_{2}}\\\\left(2 \\\\lambda_{1}-\\\\lambda_{2}\\\\right)}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\dfrac{h c}{3 \\\\lambda_{1} \\\\lambda_{2}}\\\\left(3 \\\\lambda_{2}-\\\\lambda_{1}\\\\right)}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\dfrac{h c}{2 \\\\lambda_{1} \\\\lambda_{2}}\\\\left(3 \\\\lambda_{2}-\\\\lambda_{1}\\\\right)}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${\\\\dfrac{h c}{2 \\\\lambda_{1} \\\\lambda_{2}}\\\\left(\\\\lambda_{1}-3 \\\\lambda_{2}\\\\right)}$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'${\\dfrac{h c}{\\lambda}=\\phi_{0}+e V_{0}}$ (Photoelectric equation)<br>${\\Rightarrow \\dfrac{h c}{\\lambda}=\\phi_{0}+e V_{0}}$ and ${\\dfrac{h c}{\\lambda_{2}}=\\phi_{0}+3 e V_{0}}$<br>${\\Rightarrow \\dfrac{h c}{\\lambda_{2}}=\\phi_{0}+3\\left(\\dfrac{h c}{\\lambda_{1}}-\\phi_{0}\\right)}$<br>${\\Rightarrow \\quad \\phi_{0}=\\dfrac{h c}{2 \\lambda_{1} \\lambda_{2}}\\left(3 \\lambda_{2}-\\lambda_{1}\\right)}$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45410,'physics',41,406,806,'The half life of tritium is 12.5 years. What mass of tritium of initial mass 64 $mg$ will remain undecayed after 50 years?','[{"text": "$32\\\\,mg$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$8\\\\,mg$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$16\\\\,mg$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$4\\\\,mg$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Here,<br>Half life of tritium, ${T_{1/2}} = 12.5$ years<br>Initial mass of tritium, ${m_0} = 64mg$<br>Time, $t = 50$ years<br>Mass of tritium remained undecayed after 50 years is,<br>$m = {m_0}{\\left( {\\frac{1}{2}} \\right)^{t/{T_{1/2}}}} = \\left( {64mg} \\right){\\left( {\\frac{1}{2}} \\right)^{50years/12.5years}}$<br>$ = \\left( {64mg} \\right){\\left( {\\frac{1}{2}} \\right)^4} = \\frac{{64mg}}{{16}} = 4mg$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2018,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45411,'physics',37,974,2272,'In an $A.C.$ circuit, the instantaneous values of $e.m.f.$ and current are $E = 200\\sin 314\\,t\\,volts$and $I=\\sin (314 t+\\pi / 3)$ ampere then the average power consumed in watts is','[{"text": "200", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "100", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "0", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "50", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'$P_{\\text {org }}=I_{r m s} V_{r m s} \\cos \\phi$<br>$\\Rightarrow P_{\\text {avg }}=\\langle P\\rangle=\\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\times \\dfrac{200}{\\sqrt{2}} \\cos 60^{\\circ}$<br>$ = 50\\,W$.<br>So, correct option is (4).',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45412,'physics',39,1103,2630,'Consider Sun is producing power ${P}$ in the form of monochromatic radiations of wavelength ${\\lambda}$, and the distance between Sun and Earth is ${d}$ ($d\\,\\, >  > $ earth radius). Calculate the number of photon per cubic meter just above the earth surface','[{"text": "${\\\\dfrac{P \\\\lambda}{4 \\\\pi d^{2} h c^{2}}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${\\\\dfrac{P \\\\lambda}{\\\\pi d^{2} h c}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\dfrac{4 P \\\\lambda}{h c^{2}}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\dfrac{P \\\\lambda}{h c^{2}}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Photons per second, ${n=\\dfrac{P \\lambda}{h c}}$<br>Number of photons per second per unit area.<br>${\\left(\\dfrac{n}{A}\\right)=\\dfrac{P \\lambda}{4 \\pi d^{2}(h c)}}$<br>Number of photons per cubic metre${=\\dfrac{P \\lambda}{4 \\pi d^{2} h c^{2}}}$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45414,'physics',41,406,809,'An element $X$ decays into element $Z$ by two step process<br>$X \\to Y + {}_2^4He,Y \\to Z + 2{e^ - }$','[{"text": "$X$ and $Z$ are isobars", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$X$ and $Y$ are isotopes", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$X$ and $Z$ are isotones", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$X$ and $Z$ are isotopes", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'$X$ and $Z$ have same number of protons, so they are isotopes',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2016,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45415,'physics',37,977,2770,'How much current is drawn by the primary coil of a transformer which steps down $220\\,volt$ to $22\\,volt$ to operate a device with an impedance of $220\\,\\Omega $?','[{"text": "$0.01\\\\,A$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$0.001\\\\,A$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$0.0001\\\\,A$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1\\\\,A$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'$I_{p}=$ ? ${V_p} = 220\\,V$<br>${V_s} = 22\\,V,{R_s} = 220\\,\\Omega $<br>$\\therefore {I_s} = \\frac{{{V_s}}}{{{R_s}}} = \\frac{{22}}{{220}} = 0.1\\,A$<br>By principle of transformer, $\\dfrac{I_{p}}{I_{s}}=\\dfrac{V_{s}}{V_{p}}$<br>$ \\Rightarrow {I_p} = \\frac{{{V_s}}}{{{V_p}}} \\times {I_s} = \\frac{{22 \\times 0.1}}{{220}} = 0.01\\,A$<br>So, correct option is (1).',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45416,'physics',39,1102,2629,'Radiation of wavelength $6561$ $ \\mathop A^{~~\\circ}  $ falls on a metal surface to produce photoelectrons. The electrons are made to enter a uniform magnetic field of ${3 \\times 10^{-4} {~T}}$. If the radius of the largest circular path followed by the electrons is  $10\\,mm,$  the work function of the metal is close to','[{"text": "$1.8\\\\,eV$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1.1\\\\,eV$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$0.8\\\\,eV$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1.6\\\\,eV$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Let the work function of metal surface be ${\\phi}$.<br>${\\therefore \\quad K E_{\\max }=\\dfrac{h c}{\\lambda}-\\phi}$<br>Again, ${R_{\\max }=\\dfrac{\\sqrt{2 m K E_{\\max }}}{q B}=\\dfrac{\\sqrt{2 m\\left(\\dfrac{h c}{\\lambda}-\\phi\\right)}}{q B}}$<br>${\\therefore \\quad \\dfrac{R_{\\max } q^{2} B^{2}}{2 m}=\\dfrac{h c}{\\lambda}-\\phi}$<br>${\\therefore \\phi=\\dfrac{h c}{\\lambda}-\\dfrac{R_{\\max }^{2} q^{2} B^{2}}{2 m}=1.0899 {eV} \\approx 1.1 {eV}}$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45417,'physics',41,406,809,'$_{92}{U^{235}}$ undergoes successive disintegrations with end product of $_{82}{P^{203}}$. The number of  $\\alpha $ and  $\\beta $ particles emitted are','[{"text": "$\\\\alpha  = 8,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\beta  = 6$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\alpha  = 3,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\beta  = 3$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\alpha  = 6,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\beta  = 4$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\alpha  = 6,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\beta  = 0$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'$_{92}{U^{235}}$ disintegration end product is  $_{82}{P^{203}}$ with  $\\alpha $ and  $\\beta $ particles emitted.<br>$\\Delta A = 235 - 203 = 32$<br>$\\therefore $ 8 alpha particles are emitted. The charge ($Z$)  should be $92 - 16 = 76$<br>But as the final charge is 82, six  ${\\beta ^ - }$ particles are also emitted to make the final atomic number $Z = 82$.<br>$\\therefore $  8 alpha particles and six  ${\\beta ^ - }$ are emitted',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2008,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45419,'physics',37,977,2770,'A transformer rated at $10\\,kW$ is used to connect a $5\\,KV$ transmission line to $240\\,V$ circuit. The ratio of turns in the windings of a transformer?','[{"text": "5", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "20.8", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "104", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "40", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'$5000\\,V$ to be connected by $240\\,V$ so, This is a step down transformer.<br>$\\dfrac{N_{p}}{N_{s}}=\\dfrac{5000}{240}=20.8$<br>$\\Rightarrow$ Primary has 20.8 times the turns in the secondary. So, correct option is (2).',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45421,'physics',41,406,809,'A radioactive decay can form an isotope of the original nucleus with the emission of particles','[{"text": "One  $\\\\alpha $ and two $\\\\beta $", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Four $\\\\alpha $ and one $\\\\beta $", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "One  $\\\\alpha $ and four $\\\\beta $", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "One $\\\\alpha $  and one $\\\\beta $", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'As the emission of an alpha particle decreases the atomic number and mass number by 2 and 4 respectively while the emission of a beta particle increases the atomic number by 1 but mass number remains the same.So, the emission of one alpha particle and two beta particles from  the nucleus can form an isotope of the original nucleus.<br>It may be represented as<br>$_Z{X^A}{\\xrightarrow{\\alpha }_{Z - 2}}{Y^{A - 4}}{\\xrightarrow{{2{\\beta ^ - }}}_z}{X^{A - 4}}$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2015,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45422,'physics',37,977,2770,'The ratio of secondary to the primary turns in a transformer is $3: 2$. If the power output be $P$, then the input power neglecting all losses must be equal to:','[{"text": "${5{\\\\rm{ }}P}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1.5\\\\,P$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$P$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${2{\\\\rm{/}}5{\\\\rm{ }}P}$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'As we know $\\eta=\\dfrac{\\text { Output power }}{\\text { Input power }}$<br>Whatever be ratio of turns (as losses are neglected).<br>Efficiency $\\eta=1$<br>$\\Rightarrow P_{o}=P_{i}$<br>So, correct option is (3).',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45424,'physics',41,406,809,'A nucleus ${}_n^mX$ emits one $\\alpha  - $ particle and two ${\\beta ^ - }$particles. The resulting nucleus is','[{"text": "${}_n^{m - 6}Z$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${}_{n - 2}^{m - 4}\\\\,Y$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${}_n^{m - 4}\\\\,X$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${}_{n - 4}^{m - 6}\\\\,Z$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'${}_n^mX{\\xrightarrow{{ - \\alpha }}_{n - 2}}{X^{m - 4}}{\\xrightarrow{{ - 2\\beta }}_n}{X^{m - 4}}$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45425,'physics',39,1102,2628,'In an experiment of photoelectric effect, the stopping potentials were measured to be ${V_{1}}$ and ${V_{2}}$ with incident lights of wavelengths ${\\lambda_{1}}$ and ${\\lambda_{2}}$, respectively. The expression for ${V_{1}-V_{2}}$ is','[{"text": "${\\\\dfrac{h c}{e}\\\\left(\\\\dfrac{\\\\lambda_{1} \\\\lambda_{2}}{\\\\lambda_{1}-\\\\lambda_{2}}\\\\right)}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\dfrac{h c}{e}\\\\left(\\\\dfrac{1}{\\\\lambda_{1}}-\\\\dfrac{1}{\\\\lambda_{2}}\\\\right)}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${\\\\dfrac{h e}{c}\\\\left(\\\\dfrac{1}{\\\\lambda_{1}}-\\\\dfrac{1}{\\\\lambda_{2}}\\\\right)}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\dfrac{h e}{c \\\\lambda_{1} \\\\lambda_{2}}\\\\left(\\\\lambda_{1}-\\\\lambda_{2}\\\\right)}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'${V_1} = \\frac{{hc}}{{e{\\lambda _1}}} - \\frac{\\phi }{e}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\left( 1 \\right)$<br>${V_2} = \\frac{{hc}}{{e{\\lambda _2}}} - \\frac{\\phi }{e}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\left( 2 \\right)$<br>From Eqs. (1) and (2),<br>${V_{1}-V_{2}=\\dfrac{h c}{e}\\left[\\dfrac{1}{\\lambda_{1}}-\\dfrac{1}{\\lambda_{2}}\\right]}$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45426,'physics',37,977,2771,'A transformer of efficiency $90 \\%$ draws an input power of $4\\,kW$. An electrical appliances connected across the secondary draws a current of $6\\,A$. The impedance of the device is:','[{"text": "$60\\\\,\\\\Omega $", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$50\\\\,\\\\Omega $", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$80\\\\,\\\\Omega $", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$100\\\\,\\\\Omega $", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Given that:<br>Input power $ = 4\\,kW$:<br>Efficiency is $90 \\%$<br>$\\Rightarrow$ Output power $ = 0.9 \\times 4\\,kW = 3.6\\,kW$<br>$3.6\\,kW = {I^2}R = 36 \\times R$<br>$R = \\frac{{3.6\\,kW}}{{36}} = \\frac{{3.6 \\times {{10}^3}}}{{36}} = {10^2}\\Omega  = 100\\,\\Omega $<br>So, correct option is (4).',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45428,'physics',41,406,809,'In a radio active decay, an element  $_Z{X^A}$ emits four  $\\alpha $ particles, three  $\\beta $ particles and eight  gamma photons. The atomic number and mass number of the resulting final nucleus are','[{"text": "$Z - 11,A - 16$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$Z - 5,A - 13$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$Z - 5,A - 16$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$Z - 8,A - 13$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Emission of $\\alpha $ particle decreases the atomic number and mass number by 2 and 4 respectively. Emission of  ${\\beta ^ - }$ particle increases the atomic number by 1 while the mass number remains unchanged. After emission of  $\\gamma $ particle both the atomic number and the mass number remain unchanged<br>$\\therefore $ After the emission of four $\\alpha $ particles, three ${\\beta ^ - }$ particles and eight   $\\gamma $ photons:<br>Decrease in atomic number $ = 4 \\times 2 - 3 \\times 1 = 5$<br>Decrease in mass number  $ = 4 \\times 4 - 0 = 16$<br>Thus, the atomic number and mass number of resulting final nucleus are $(Z - 5)$ and $(A - 16)$ respectively.<br>$\\therefore $ The resulting final nucleus is $_{Z - 5}{Y^{A - 16}}$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45429,'physics',39,1102,2628,'Using light of wavelength $6000$ $ \\mathop A^{~~\\circ}  $, stopping potential is obtained to be $2.4\\,volt$ for photoelectric cell. If light of wavelength $4000$ $ \\mathop A^{~~\\circ}  $ is used, then stopping potential would be','[{"text": "$2.9\\\\,V$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1.9\\\\,V$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$3.43\\\\,V$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$9.4\\\\,V$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'${V_{0}=\\dfrac{h c}{e \\lambda}-\\dfrac{\\phi_{0}}{e}}$<br>$ \\Rightarrow 2.4 = \\frac{{hc}}{{6000 \\times {{10}^{ - 10}}e}} - \\frac{{{\\phi _0}}}{e}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\left( 1 \\right)$<br>${V_0} = \\frac{{hc}}{{4000 \\times {{10}^{ - 10}}e}} - \\frac{{{\\phi _0}}}{e}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\left( 2 \\right)$<br>Subtracting Eq. (2) from Eq. (1), we get<br>${2.4-V_{0}=\\dfrac{h c}{e}\\left[\\dfrac{1}{6000 \\times 10^{-10}}-\\dfrac{1}{4000 \\times 10^{-10}}\\right]}$<br>${\\Rightarrow 2.4-V_{0}=\\dfrac{6.62 \\times 10^{-34} \\times 3 \\times 10^{8}}{10^{-7} \\times 1.6 \\times 10^{-19}}\\left[\\dfrac{4-6}{24}\\right]}$<br>${\\Rightarrow V_{0}=2.4+\\dfrac{6.62 \\times 10^{-34} \\times 3 \\times 10^{8}}{12 \\times 10^{-7} \\times 1.6 \\times 10^{-19}}}$<br>${=2.4+1.03=3.43 {~V}}$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45430,'physics',37,977,2770,'An ideal transformer has 500 and 5000 turn in primary and secondary windings respectively. If the primary voltage is connected to a $6\\,V$ battery then the secondary voltage is:','[{"text": "$0\\\\,V$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$60\\\\,V$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$0.6\\\\,V$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$6\\\\,V$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Secondary voltage will be zero because transformer works only on a.c but battery means d.c.<br>$\\Rightarrow V_{s}=0$. So, correct option is (1).',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45464,'physics',37,973,2268,'In the series $L-C-R$ circuit figure the voltmeter and ammeter readings are','[{"text": "$V = 100\\\\,volt,I = 2\\\\,amp$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$V = 100\\\\,volt,I = 5\\\\,amp$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$V = 1000\\\\,volt,I = 2\\\\,amp$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$V = 300\\\\,volt,I = 1\\\\,amp$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'${I_{r.m.s}} = \\frac{{{V_{r.m.s}}}}{Z} = \\frac{{{V_{r.m.s}}}}{R} = \\frac{{100}}{{50}} = 2\\,A$<br>Voltmeter reads $r m s$ value viz $100\\,V$.<br>So, correct option is (1).',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/37_45464_question_1.png'),
(45432,'physics',39,1102,2626,'The work function of a substance is 4 ${\\rm{ }}eV$. The longest wavelength of light that can cause photoelectron emission from this substance is approximately','[{"text": "$540\\\\,nm$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$400\\\\,nm$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$310\\\\,nm$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$220\\\\,nm$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Given, work function, $\\phi  = 4\\,e\\,V$<br>Longest wavelength of light $ = {\\lambda _m}$<br>$\\therefore \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\frac{{hc}}{{{\\lambda _m}}} = \\phi $<br>$\\therefore \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{\\lambda _m} = \\frac{{hc}}{\\phi } = \\frac{{\\left( {6.63 \\times {{10}^{ - 34}} \\times 3 \\times {{10}^8}} \\right)}}{{4.0 \\times 1.6 \\times {{10}^{ - 19}}}}$<br>$\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, = 310\\,nm$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45434,'physics',37,977,2770,'The transformer ratio of a transformer is $10: 1$. If the primary voltage is $440\\,V$, secondary emf is','[{"text": "$44\\\\,V$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$440\\\\,V$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$4400\\\\,V$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$44000\\\\,V$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'$\\dfrac{N_{S}}{N_{p}}=\\dfrac{V_{s}}{V_{p}} \\Rightarrow V_{s}=(10)(440)$<br>$ \\Rightarrow {V_s} = 4400\\,V$<br>So, correct option is (3).',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45435,'physics',41,406,809,'Pick out the correct statements from the following.<br>(a)  Electron emission during decay is always accompained by neutrino<br>(b)  Nuclear force is charge independent<br>(c)  fusion is the chief source of stellar energy','[{"text": "(a), (b) are correct", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "(a), (c) are correct", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "only (a) is correct", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "(b), (c) are correct", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Electron emission during ${\\beta ^ - }$ decay is always accompanied by antineutrino emission.<br>Hence , statement (a) is incorrect <br>While statements (b) and (c) are correct',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45436,'physics',39,1102,2626,'Choose the correct statements','[{"text": "Photoelectric effect can take place from bound electrons.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Photoelectric effect can take place from free electrons.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Photoelectric effect can take place from bounded or free electrons.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "None of the above", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Photoelectric effect can take place from free electrons.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45438,'physics',37,977,2770,'A transformer steps up an $A.C.$ voltage from $230\\,V$ to $2300\\,V$. If the number of turns in the secondary coil is 1000 , the number of turns in the primary coil will be','[{"text": "100", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "10,000", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "500", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "1000", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'$\\dfrac{N_{s}}{N_{p}}=\\dfrac{V_{s}}{V_{p}}$<br>$\\begin{aligned}& \\Rightarrow \\dfrac{1000}{N_{p}}=\\dfrac{2300}{230} \\\\& \\Rightarrow N_{p}=100\\end{aligned}$<br>So, the correct option is (1).',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45439,'physics',41,406,809,'The number of beta particles emitted by a radioactive substance is twice the number of alpha particles emitted by it. The resulting daughter is an','[{"text": "Isotope of parent", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Isomer of parent", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Isotone of parent", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Isobar of parent", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Let the radioactive substance be ${}_Z^AX$ .Radioactive transition is given by ${}_Z^AX\\xrightarrow{{ - \\alpha }}{}_{Z - 2}^{A - 4}X\\xrightarrow{{ - 2\\beta }}{}_Z^{A - 4}X$<br>The atoms of element having same atomic number but different mass numbers are called isotopes.<br>So, ${}_Z^AX$ and ${}_Z^{A - 4}X$ are isotopes.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45440,'physics',39,1102,2626,'When light of wavelength $300\\,nm$ falls on a photoelectric emitter, photoelectrons are liberated. For another emitter, light of wavelength $600\\,nm$ is sufficient for liberating photoelectrons. The ratio of the work function of the two emitters is','[{"text": "$1: 2$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2: 1$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$4: 1$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1: 4$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Given, $\\,\\,{\\lambda _1} = 300\\,nm$<br>and $\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{\\lambda _2} = 600\\,nm$<br>We know that, work function,<br>$\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\phi  = hc/\\lambda $<br>So, to find ratio between them,<br>$\\frac{{{\\phi _1}}}{{{\\phi _2}}} = \\frac{{hc}}{{{\\lambda _1}}} \\times \\frac{{{\\lambda _2}}}{{hc}} = \\frac{{{\\lambda _2}}}{{{\\lambda _1}}} = \\frac{{600}}{{300}} = 2:1$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45441,'physics',37,977,2770,'The transformer ratio of a transformer is 5 . If the primary voltage of the transformer is $400\\,V$, $50\\,Hz$, the secondary voltage will be','[{"text": "$2000\\\\,V,250\\\\,Hz$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$80\\\\,V,50\\\\,Hz$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$80\\\\,V,10\\\\,Hz$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2000\\\\,V,50\\\\,Hz$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'${V_s} = ({\\rm{turn}}\\,\\,{\\rm{ratio}}){V_p}$<br>$ = (5)(400) = 2000\\,V$<br>But frequency of secondary voltage will be the same as that of primary voltage.<br>$\\Rightarrow V_{s}$ will have $50\\,Hz$ only. So, the correct option is (4).',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45443,'physics',41,406,809,'When a neutron disintegrates to give a  ${\\beta ^ - }$ particle,','[{"text": "A  neutrino alone is emitted", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "A  proton and neutrino are emitted", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "A  proton alone is emitted", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "A  proton and an antineutrino are emitted", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'In  ${\\beta ^ - }$ decay, a neutron disintegrates into a proton, an electron, and an anti-neutrino as  shown:<br>$\\mathop n\\limits_{neutron}  \\to \\mathop p\\limits_{proton}  + \\mathop {{e^ - }}\\limits_{electron}  + \\mathop {\\overline v }\\limits_{antineutrino} $',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2010,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45444,'physics',39,1103,2630,'Calculate the linear momentum of a $3\\,MeV$ photon.','[{"text": "$0.01\\\\,eV - s/m$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$0.02\\\\,eV - s/m$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$0.03\\\\,eV - s/m$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$0.04\\\\,eV - s/m$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Momentum, $p = \\frac{E}{c} = \\frac{{3MeV}}{{3 \\times {{10}^8}\\,m/s}}$<br>$ = \\frac{{3 \\times {{10}^6}eV}}{{3 \\times {{10}^8}\\,m/s}} = 0.01eV - s/m$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45445,'physics',37,977,2771,'A step-up transformer works on $220\\,V$ and gives $2\\,A$ to an external resistor. The turn ratio between the primary and secondary coils is 2:25.<br>Assuming 100% efficiency, find the secondary voltage. Primary current and power delivered.','[{"text": "$2750\\\\,V,20\\\\,A,5500\\\\,W$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$2750\\\\,V,20\\\\,A,5000\\\\,W$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2570\\\\,V,25\\\\,A,550\\\\,W$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2750\\\\,V,20\\\\,A,55\\\\,W$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'$\\dfrac{N_{p}}{N_{s}}=\\dfrac{2}{25}$ (given)<br>$\\Rightarrow \\dfrac{V_{p}}{V_{s}}=\\dfrac{2}{25}$<br>$ \\Rightarrow {V_s} = \\left( {\\frac{{25}}{2}} \\right)(220) = 2750\\,V$<br>$P_{O}=V_{s} I_{s}$<br>$=(2750)(2)$<br>$ \\Rightarrow {P_o} = 5500\\,W$<br>$\\dfrac{N_{s}}{N_{p}}=\\dfrac{I_{p}}{I_{s}} \\quad\\left(\\because \\dfrac{I_{p}}{I_{s}}=\\dfrac{V_{s}}{V_{p}}\\right)$<br>$\\Rightarrow \\dfrac{1000}{100}=\\dfrac{I_{p}}{2}$<br>$ \\Rightarrow {I_p} = 20\\,A$<br>So, the correct option is (1).',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45446,'physics',41,404,803,'Assume the graph of specific binding energy versus mass number is as shown in the figure. Using this graph, select the correct choice from the following:','[{"text": "Fusion of two nuclei of mass number lying in  the range of 100 < A < 200 will release energy", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Fusion of two nuclei of mass number lying in the range of 51 < A < 100 will release energy", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Fusion of two nuclei of mass number lying in the range of 1< A < 50 will release energy", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Fission of the nucleus of mass number lying in  the range of 100 < A < 200 will release energy when broken into two fragments.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'When two nuclei of mass number lying in the 	range of 51 < A < 100 are combined then a nucleus is formed in the range of 102 < A < 200 which has higher value of specific binding energy and therefore releases energy.',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',2010,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/41_45446_question_1.png'),
(45448,'physics',39,1102,3092,'In a $p - n$ junction photocell, the value of the photo-electromotive force produced by monochromatic light is proportional to','[{"text": "the barrier voltage at the $p - n$ junction", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "this intensity of the light falling on the cell", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "the frequency of the light falling on the cell", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "the voltage applied at the $p - n$ junction", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'In a photoconductive cell, when monochromatic light is incident on the transparent metallic film, a force produced called the photoelectromotive, stimulates the emission of an electric current when photovoltaic action creates a potential difference between two points.The magnitude of this current depends upon the intensity of incident light. Hence, photo-electromotive force produced by monochromatic light is proportional to the intensity of the light falling on the cell.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45449,'physics',37,972,2261,'The average current of a sinusoidally varying alternating current of peak value $5\\,A$ with initial phase zero, between the instants $t=T / 8$ to $t=T / 4$ is (where \' $T$ \' is time period)','[{"text": "$\\\\frac{{10}}{\\\\pi }\\\\sqrt 2 \\\\,A$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{5}{\\\\pi }\\\\sqrt 2 \\\\,A$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{{20\\\\sqrt 2 }}{\\\\pi }A$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{{10}}{\\\\pi }A$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'$ < i>=\\dfrac{\\int_{T / 8}^{T / 4} i d t}{\\int_{T / 8}^{T / 4} d t}$<br>Then, $i=5 \\sin \\omega t$.<br>So, correct option is (1).',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45450,'physics',41,404,803,'The mass defect of ${}_2^4He$ is 0.03$u$. The binding energy per nucleon of helium(in $MeV$) is','[{"text": "$69.825$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$6.9825$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$2.793$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$27.93$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'The mass defect of ${}_2^4He,\\Delta m = 0.03u$<br>The binding energy per nucleon $ = \\frac{{\\Delta m \\times 931}}{4}MeV$<br>$ = \\frac{{0.03 \\times 931}}{4}MeV = 6.9825MeV$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2017,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45451,'physics',39,1102,2629,'Light of frequency $v$ falls on material of threshold frequency $v_{0}$. Maximum kinetic energy of emitted electron is proportional to','[{"text": "$v-v_{0}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$v$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\sqrt{v-v_{0}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$v_{0}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'When light falls on a metallic surface, then it results ejection of photoelectron. In this process, conservation of energy hold. Thus, from law of conservation of energy, energy imparted by the photon $=$ maximum kinetic energy of the emitted electron + work function of the metal<br>or $\\quad hv = {(KE)_{\\max }} + \\phi $<br>But $\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\phi  = h{v_0}$<br>where, $v_{0}$ being threshold frequency.<br>or $\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{(KE)_{\\max }} = h\\nu  - h{v_0}$<br>$\\, \\Rightarrow {({\\rm{KE}})_{\\max }} \\propto v - {v_0}$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45452,'physics',40,240,479,'In hydrogen spectrum, the shortest wavelength in the Balmer series is $\\lambda$. The shortset wavelength in the Bracket series is:','[{"text": "$4 \\\\lambda$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$9 \\\\lambda$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$16 \\\\lambda$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2 \\\\lambda$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Denote $R=$ Rydberg constant.<br>Shortest wavelength in Balmer series is for transition of $e^{-}$from $n=\\infty$ to $n=2$.<br>$\\frac{1}{\\lambda } = R{Z^2}\\left[ {\\frac{1}{{{2^2}}} - \\frac{1}{{{\\infty ^2}}}} \\right]$<br>$ \\Rightarrow \\frac{1}{\\lambda } = \\frac{R}{4}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,(1)$<br>Shortest wavelength in Balmer series is for transition of $e^{-}$from $n=\\infty$ to $n=4$.<br>$\\frac{1}{{{\\lambda ^\\prime }}} = R{(1)^2}\\left[ {\\frac{1}{{{4^2}}} - \\frac{1}{{{\\infty ^2}}}} \\right] \\Rightarrow \\frac{1}{{{\\lambda ^\\prime }}} = \\frac{R}{{16}}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,(2)$<br>Divide eq. (1) by eq. (2)<br>$\\Rightarrow \\dfrac{\\lambda^{\\prime}}{\\lambda}=\\dfrac{R}{4} \\times \\dfrac{16}{R} \\Rightarrow \\lambda^{\\prime}=4 \\lambda$<br>Correct option is (1).',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2023,'NEET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45453,'physics',37,972,2259,'An alternating current \' $i$ \' is given by $i=i_{0} \\sin 2 \\pi(t / T+1 / 4)$. Then the avg current in the first one quarter time period is','[{"text": "$\\\\dfrac{2 i_{0}}{\\\\pi}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{I_{0}}{\\\\pi}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{I_{0}}{2 \\\\pi}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{3 I_{0}}{\\\\pi}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'$ < i>=\\dfrac{\\int_{0}^{T / 4} i d t}{\\int_{0}^{T / 4} d t}$<br>Then, use given expression for $i$.<br>So, correct option is (1).',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45454,'physics',41,404,803,'The mass of  ${}_3^7Li$ nucleus is 0.042 $u$ less than the sum of the masses of all its nucleus. The binding energy per nucleon of  ${}_3^7Li$ nucleus is nearly','[{"text": "$46\\\\,MeV$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$23\\\\,MeV$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$3.9\\\\,MeV$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$5.6\\\\,MeV$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'If $m = 1\\,u,\\,c = 3 \\times {10^8}m{s^{ - 1}}$, then<br>$E = 931\\,MeV$ i.e., $1\\,u = 931\\,MeV$<br>Binding energy $ = 0.042 \\times 931 = 39.10\\,MeV$<br>$\\therefore $ Binding ebergy per nucleon $ = \\frac{{39.10}}{7} = 5.58 \\approx 5.6\\,MeV$<br>into a lighter nucleus with higher specific binding energy,${E_2} > {E_1}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45455,'physics',39,1104,2631,'The wavelength $\\lambda$ of a photon and the de-Broglie wavelength of an electron have the same value. Find the ratio of energy of photon to the kinetic energy of electron in terms of mass $m$, speed of light $c$ and Planck\'s constant $(h)$.','[{"text": "$\\\\lambda mc/h$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$hmc/\\\\lambda $", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2hmc/\\\\lambda $", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2\\\\lambda mc/h$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'The de-Broglie wavelength of an electron,<br>$\\lambda  = \\frac{h}{{mv}} \\Rightarrow v = \\frac{h}{{m\\lambda }}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\left( 1 \\right)$<br>Energy of photon,<br>${E_p} = \\frac{{hc}}{\\lambda }\\left( {{\\text{since,}}\\lambda \\,{\\text{is}}\\,{\\text{same}}} \\right)\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\left( 2 \\right)$<br>Kinetic energy of photon to kinetic<br>energy of electron<br>$\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, = \\frac{{{E_p}}}{{{E_e}}} = \\frac{{\\frac{{hc}}{\\lambda }}}{{\\left( {\\frac{1}{2}} \\right)m{v^2}}} = \\frac{{2hc}}{{\\lambda m{v^2}}}$<br>Substituting the value of $v$ from<br>Eq. (1), we get<br>$\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\frac{{{E_p}}}{{{E_e}}} = \\frac{{2hc}}{{\\lambda m{{\\left( {\\frac{h}{{m\\lambda }}} \\right)}^2}}} = \\frac{{2\\lambda mc}}{h}$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45456,'physics',37,973,2265,'A $100\\,\\Omega $ resistance is connected in series with a $4\\,H$ inductor. The voltage across the resistor is ${V_R} = 2\\sin (1000\\,t)V$. The voltage across the inductor is','[{"text": "$80\\\\sin \\\\left( {1000\\\\,t + \\\\frac{\\\\pi }{2}} \\\\right)$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$40\\\\sin \\\\left( {1000\\\\,t + \\\\frac{\\\\pi }{2}} \\\\right)$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$80\\\\sin \\\\left( {1000\\\\,t - \\\\frac{\\\\pi }{2}} \\\\right)$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$40\\\\sin \\\\left( {1000\\\\,t - \\\\frac{\\\\pi }{2}} \\\\right)$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'$i=\\dfrac{\\left(V_{0}\\right)_{R}}{R}$,<br>$V_{L}=\\left(V_{0}\\right)_{L} \\sin \\left(\\omega t+\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\right)$<br>$\\left(V_{0}\\right)_{L}=X_{L} i$  <img src=\'/img/data/physics/37_45456_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />  Here $\\left(V_{0}\\right)_{R}=2$ Volt<br>$X_{L}=L \\omega$<br>$L = 2{\\rm{ }}H$<br>$\\omega  = 1000\\,rad{\\rm{/}}s$<br>Also note that $V_{L}$ leads $V_{R}$ by $(\\pi / 2)$.<br>So, correct option is (1).',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45457,'physics',40,239,478,'The radius of inner most orbit of hydrogen atom is $5.3 \\times {10^{ - 11}}\\,m$. What is the radius of third allowed orbit of hydrogen atom?','[{"text": "1.06 $ \\\\mathop A^{~~\\\\circ}  $", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "1.59 $ \\\\mathop A^{~~\\\\circ}  $", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "4.77 $ \\\\mathop A^{~~\\\\circ}  $", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "0.53 $ \\\\mathop A^{~~\\\\circ}  $", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Radius of $n^{\\text {th }}$ orbit in Hydrogen Atom<br>${r_n} = 0.53 \\times \\frac{{{n^2}}}{Z}\\mathop {{\\rm{ }}A}\\limits^{\\,\\,^\\circ } $<br>For $H, Z=1$<br>$\\Rightarrow$ Radius of third orbit<br>${r_3} = 0.53 \\times \\frac{{{{(3)}^2}}}{{(1)}}\\mathop {{\\rm{ }}A}\\limits^{\\,\\,^\\circ }  = 4.77\\mathop {{\\rm{ }}A}\\limits^{\\,\\,^\\circ } $<br>Correct option is (3).',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2023,'NEET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45458,'physics',41,404,803,'A radioactive nucleus has specific binding energy $\'{E_1}\'$. It emits an $\\alpha $ particle. The resulting nucleus has specific binding energy $\'{E_2}\'$ Then','[{"text": "${E_2} = {E_1}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${E_2} < {E_1}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${E_2} > {E_1}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${E_2} = 0$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,NULL,NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2012,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45459,'physics',39,1104,2631,'Nuclear radii may be measured by scattering high energy electrons from nuclei. What is the de-Broglie wavelength for $200\\,M{\\mkern 1mu} eV$ electrons?','[{"text": "$8.28\\\\,Fm$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$7.98\\\\,Fm$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$6.45\\\\,Fm$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$6.20\\\\,Fm$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'de-Broglie wavelength,<br>$\\lambda  = \\frac{{hc}}{E} = \\frac{{1240}}{{200 \\times {{10}^6}}} = 6.20 \\times {10^{ - 6}}\\,nm$<br>$ = 6.20\\,Fm$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45460,'physics',37,973,2268,'In the following circuit, the values of current flowing in the circuit at $t=0$ and $t=\\infty$ will respectively be','[{"text": "$8\\\\,A{\\\\mkern 1mu} {\\\\mkern 1mu} {\\\\rm{and}}\\\\,\\\\,0\\\\,A$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$0\\\\,A{\\\\mkern 1mu} {\\\\mkern 1mu} {\\\\rm{and}}\\\\,\\\\,0\\\\,A$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$8\\\\,A\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\rm{and}}\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\rm{8}}\\\\,A$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$0\\\\,A\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\rm{and}}\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\rm{8}}\\\\,A$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'$I=\\dfrac{E}{Z}=\\dfrac{E}{\\sqrt{R^{2}+\\left[2 \\pi f L-\\dfrac{1}{2 \\pi f C}\\right]^{2}}}$ <img src=\'/img/data/physics/37_45460_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />  Due to shape, in beginning $L$ opposite the flow and as $t \\rightarrow \\infty, C$ opposes flow of current.<br>$\\Rightarrow i(t=0)=0$<br>$i(t \\rightarrow \\infty)=0$.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/37_45460_question_1.png'),
(45461,'physics',41,404,803,'Pick out the correct statement from the 	following:','[{"text": "Energy released per unit mass of the reactant  is less in case of  fusion reaction", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Packing fraction may be positive or may be negative", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$P{u^{239}}$  is not suitable for a fission reaction", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "For stable nucleus, the specific binding energy  is low", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Packing fraction may be positive or negative.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2010,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45462,'physics',40,240,479,'Three energy levels of hydrogen atom and the corresponding wavelength of the emitted radiation due to different electron transition are as shown. Then.','[{"text": "$\\\\lambda_{2}=\\\\lambda_{1}+\\\\lambda_{3}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\lambda_{2}=\\\\dfrac{\\\\lambda_{1} \\\\lambda_{3}}{\\\\lambda_{1}+\\\\lambda_{3}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\lambda_{3}=\\\\dfrac{\\\\lambda_{1} \\\\lambda_{2}}{\\\\lambda_{1}+\\\\lambda_{2}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\lambda_{1}=\\\\dfrac{\\\\lambda_{2} \\\\lambda_{3}}{\\\\lambda_{2}+\\\\lambda_{3}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'$E=\\dfrac{h c}{\\lambda}$ implies<br>$\\dfrac{1}{\\lambda_{2}}=\\dfrac{1}{\\lambda_{1}}+\\dfrac{1}{\\lambda_{3}}$<br>$\\Rightarrow \\lambda_{2}=\\dfrac{\\lambda_{1} \\lambda_{3}}{\\lambda_{1}+\\lambda_{3}}$.<br>Correct option is (2).',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2023,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/40_45462_question_1.png'),
(45463,'physics',39,1104,2631,'If $K$ be the kinetic energy and $m$ be the mass of a moving particle, then the de-Broglie wavelength of the particle is','[{"text": "$\\\\lambda  = \\\\frac{h}{{\\\\sqrt {mK} }}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\lambda  = \\\\frac{{2h}}{{\\\\sqrt {mK} }}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\lambda  = \\\\frac{h}{{2\\\\sqrt {mK} }}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\lambda  = \\\\frac{h}{{\\\\sqrt {2mK} }}$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Given, $K=$ kinetic energy, $m=$ mass<br>Then, $\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,K = \\frac{1}{2}m{v^2}{\\rm{ }}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\left( 1 \\right)$<br>But we know that, $\\lambda  = \\frac{h}{{mv}}{\\rm{ }}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\left( 2 \\right)$<br>Now, from Eq. (1), ${v^2} = \\frac{{2K}}{m}$<br>$ \\Rightarrow \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,v = \\sqrt {2K/m} $<br>Putting this in Eq. (2), we get<br>$\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, = \\frac{h}{{\\sqrt {2mK} }}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45465,'physics',41,404,803,'A nucleus ${}_Z^AX$ has mass represented by $m(A,Z)$. If  ${m_p}$ and ${m_n}$ denote the mass of proton and neutron respectively and $BE$ the binding energy (in $MeV$), then','[{"text": "$BE = \\\\left[ {Z{m_p} + (A - Z){m_n} - m(A,Z)} \\\\right]{c^2}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$BE = \\\\left[ {m(A,Z) - N{m_p} - (A - Z){m_n}} \\\\right]{c^2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$BE = \\\\left[ {m(A,Z) - Z{m_p} - (A - Z){m_n}} \\\\right]{c^2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$BE = \\\\left[ {Z{m_p} + A{m_n} - m(A,Z)} \\\\right]{c^2}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'$m(A,Z)$ is the mass of the atom of mass number $A$ and atomic number $Z$. Hence, the binding energy of the nucleus is<br>$BE = \\left[ {Z{m_p} + N{m_n} - m(A,Z)} \\right]{c^2}$<br>$BE = \\left[ {Z{m_p} + (A - Z){m_n} - m(A,Z)} \\right]{c^2}$<br>Where,$N = A - Z = $  number of neutrons.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45466,'physics',39,1104,2631,'The de-Broglie wavelength of neutrons in thermal equilibrium is','[{"text": "$\\\\frac{{30.8}}{{\\\\sqrt T }}\\\\mathop A\\\\limits^ \\\\circ  $", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{{3.08}}{{\\\\sqrt T }}\\\\mathop A\\\\limits^ \\\\circ  $", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{{0.308}}{{\\\\sqrt T }}\\\\mathop A\\\\limits^ \\\\circ  $", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{{0.0308}}{{\\\\sqrt T }}\\\\mathop A\\\\limits^ \\\\circ  $", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'de - Brogliewavelength,<br>$\\lambda  = \\frac{h}{{\\sqrt {3mkT} }}$<br>where, $T = $ temperature<br>and $\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,k = $ Boltzmann constant<br>$ = \\frac{{6.62 \\times {{10}^{ - 34}}}}{{\\sqrt {3 \\times 1.67 \\times {{10}^{ - 27}} \\times 1.38 \\times {{10}^{ - 23}}\\,T} }}$<br>$ = \\frac{{6.62 \\times {{10}^{ - 34}}}}{{2.15 \\times {{10}^{ - 25}}\\sqrt T }}$<br>${\\rm{ = }}\\frac{{{\\rm{3}}.{\\rm{08}}}}{{\\sqrt {\\rm{T}} }} \\times {\\rm{1}}{{\\rm{0}}^{{\\rm{ - 9}}}}{\\rm{ = }}\\frac{{{\\rm{30}}.{\\rm{8}}}}{{\\sqrt {\\rm{T}} }}\\mathop A\\limits^ \\circ  $',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45467,'physics',40,238,477,'In the Rutherford\'s alpha scattering experiment, as the impact parameter increases, the scattering angle of the alpha particle','[{"text": "decreases", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "increases", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "remains the same", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "is always $90^{\\\\circ}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Impact parameter $b$ can be written as:<br>$b = \\frac{1}{{4\\pi {\\varepsilon _0}}}\\frac{{Z{e^2}\\cot (\\theta /2)}}{{\\left( {\\frac{1}{2}m{\\upsilon ^2}} \\right)}}$ <img src=\'/img/data/physics/40_45467_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' /> If impact meter increases then scattering 		angle decreases.					Correct option is (1).',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2023,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45468,'physics',37,973,2268,'In the given figure, the instantaneous value of alternating $e.m.f.$ is $V=14.14 \\sin \\omega t$. The reading of voltmeter in $volt$ will be','[{"text": "141.4", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "10", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "200.0", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "70.7", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Reading of voltmeter in $r m s$ value<br>$\\Rightarrow V_{r m s}=\\dfrac{V_{\\text {peak }}}{\\sqrt{2}}$<br>$ \\Rightarrow {V_{rms}} = \\frac{{14.14}}{{\\sqrt 2 }} = 10\\,V$<br>So, correct option is (2).',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/37_45468_question_1.png'),
(45469,'physics',41,407,811,'A nucleus  $_Z{X^A}$ emits an  $\\alpha $ particle with velocity $v$. The recoil speed of the daughter nucleus is','[{"text": "$\\\\frac{{A - 4}}{{4v}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{{4v}}{{A - 4}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$v$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{v}{4}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'According to law of conservation of linear momentum<br>$0 = {m_D}{v_D} + {m_\\alpha }{v_\\alpha }$($\\because $ The parent nucleus is initially at rest)<br>Where subscripts $D$ and  $\\alpha $ represent daughter nucleus  and $\\alpha $-particle respectively<br>$0 = \\left( {A - 4} \\right){v_D} + 4v$ ( $\\because $ Mass of a nucleus is approximately proportional to its mass number)<br>${v_D} =  - \\frac{{4v}}{{\\left( {A - 4} \\right)}}$<br>Negative sign shows the recoil of the daughter nucleus',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2013,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45470,'physics',39,1104,2631,'An electron and a photon possess the same de-Broglie wavelength. If ${E_e}$ and ${E_P}$ are the energies of electron and photon respectively and $v$ and $c$ are their respective velocities, then $\\frac{{{E_e}}}{{{E_p}}}$ is equal to','[{"text": "$\\\\frac{v}{c}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{v}{{2c}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{v}{{3c}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{v}{{4c}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'de-Broglie wavelength,<br>$\\lambda  = \\frac{h}{{\\sqrt {2m{E_e}} }} = \\frac{{hc}}{{{E_p}}}$ or $2m{E_e} = \\frac{{E_p^2}}{{{c^2}}}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\left( 1 \\right)$<br>But $\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{E_e} = \\frac{1}{2}m{v^2}$ or $m = \\frac{{2{E_e}}}{{{v^2}}}$<br>From Eq. (1), we get<br>$2\\left[ {\\frac{{2{E_e}}}{{{v^2}}}} \\right]{E_e} = \\frac{{E_p^2}}{{{c^2}}}$   or   $\\frac{{4E_e^2}}{{{v^2}}} = \\frac{{E_p^2}}{{{c^2}}}$<br>or ${\\rm{ }}\\,\\,\\,{\\mkern 1mu} {\\kern 1pt} \\,\\,\\,\\frac{{E_e^2}}{{E_p^2}} = \\frac{{{v^2}}}{{4{c^2}}}$   or   ${\\mkern 1mu} {\\kern 1pt} \\frac{{{E_e}}}{{{E_p}}} = \\frac{v}{{2c}}$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45471,'physics',37,975,2275,'In a series resonant $L C R$ circuit, the voltage across $R$ is $100\\,V$ and $R = 1\\,k\\Omega $ with $C = 2\\,\\mu {\\rm{F}}$. The resonant frequency $\\omega$ is $200\\,rad{\\rm{/}}s$. At resonance, the voltage across $L$ is:','[{"text": "$40\\\\,V$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$250\\\\,V$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$4 \\\\times {10^{ - 3}}\\\\,V$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$25\\\\,mV$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'$I = \\frac{V}{R} = \\frac{{100}}{{1000}} = 0.1\\,A$<br>In resonance condition, $V=V_{R}$<br>${V_L} = {V_C} = \\left( {I{X_C}} \\right) = \\frac{I}{{\\omega C}}$<br>$ = \\frac{{0.1}}{{200 \\times 2 \\times {{10}^{ - 6}}}} = 250\\,V$<br>So, correct option is (2).',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45472,'physics',40,239,478,'The radius of third stationary orbit of electron for Bohr\'s atom is $R$. The radius of the fourth stationary orbit will be :','[{"text": "$\\\\dfrac{3}{4} R$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{9}{16} R$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{16}{9}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{4}{3} R$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Radius of Bohr\'s atom is given by<br>$R=R_{0} \\dfrac{n^{2}}{Z}$ where $n=$ Orbit number<br>$\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,Z = $ Atomic number<br>$R_{3}=R=R_{0} \\dfrac{n^{2}}{Z}=R_{0} \\dfrac{(3)^{2}}{1}$<br>$R_{4}=R_{0}(4)^{2}$<br>$\\dfrac{R_{4}}{R}=\\dfrac{4^{2}}{3^{2}}$<br>$R_{4}=\\dfrac{16}{9} R$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2024,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45473,'physics',41,407,811,'A radioactive nucleus of mass $M$ emits a photon of frequency $v$ and the nucleus recoils. The recoil energy will be','[{"text": "${h^2}{v^2}/2M{c^2}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{Zero}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$hv$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$M{c^2} - hv$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Momentum of a photon $p = \\frac{{hv}}{c}$<br>Hence, recoil energy of the nucleus is $E = \\frac{{{p^2}}}{{2M}}$ ($M$ is the mass of the nucleus)<br>$\\therefore \\quad E = \\frac{{{{\\left( {\\frac{{hv}}{c}} \\right)}^2}}}{{2M}} \\Rightarrow E = \\frac{{{h^2}{v^2}}}{{2M{c^2}}}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45475,'physics',37,972,2634,'The variation of the instantaneous current $(I)$ and the instantaneous $emf$ $(V)$ in a circuit is as shown in figurre. Which of the following statement is correct.','[{"text": "The voltage lag behind the current by $\\\\pi / 2$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "The voltage leads the current by $\\\\pi / 2$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "The voltage and the current are in phase", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "The voltage leads the current by $\\\\pi$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'At $t=0$, phase of the voltage is zero, while phase of the current is $\\dfrac{-\\pi}{2}$, i.e., voltage leads by $\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}$. So, correct option is (2).',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/37_45475_question_1.png'),
(45476,'physics',41,407,811,'A nucleus of mass 20$u$ emits a $\\gamma $ photon of energy 6 $MeV$. If the emission is assumed to occur when nucleus is free and at rest, then the nucleus will have kinetic energy nearest to ( take $1u = 1.6 \\times {10^{ - 27}}\\,kg$)','[{"text": "$10\\\\,keV$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1\\\\,keV$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$0.1\\\\,keV$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$100\\\\,keV$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Mass of nucleus, $M = 20u$<br>${E_1} = 6MeV = 6 \\times 1.6 \\times {10^{ - 13}}J$<br>Using momentum conservation principle<br>$0 = Mv + \\frac{{{E_\\gamma }}}{c}$<br>$\\therefore v =  - \\frac{{{E_\\gamma }}}{{Mc}} =  - \\frac{{6 \\times 1.6 \\times {{10}^{ - 13}}}}{{20 \\times 1.6 \\times {{10}^{ - 27}} \\times 3 \\times {{10}^8}}}$<br>$ =  - {10^5}m{s^{ - 1}}$<br>$K$.$E$ of nucleus, $K = \\frac{1}{2}M{v^2}$<br>$ = \\frac{1}{2} \\times 20 \\times 1.6 \\times {10^{ - 27}} \\times {\\left( {{{10}^5}} \\right)^2} = 16 \\times {10^{ - 17}}J$<br>$ = \\frac{{16 \\times {{10}^{ - 17}}}}{{1.6 \\times {{10}^{ - 16}}}}keV = 1\\,\\,keV$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45478,'physics',39,1104,2631,'Which of these particles (having the same kinetic energy) has the largest de-Broglie wavelength?','[{"text": "Electron", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\alpha$-particle", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Proton", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Neutron", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'de-Broglie wavelength,<br>$\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\lambda {\\rm{ = }}\\frac{h}{{mv}} = \\frac{h}{{\\sqrt {2mE} }}$<br>$E$ is same for all, so $\\lambda  \\propto \\frac{1}{{\\sqrt m }}$<br>Hence, de-Broglie wavelength will be<br>maximum for particle with lesser mass.<br>Mass of the given particels in<br>increasing order are given as<br>$\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{m_e} < {m_p} < {m_n} < {m_\\alpha }$<br>Thus, de-Broglie wavelength will be maximum for electron.',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45479,'physics',37,972,2260,'The $r.m.s$ voltage of the wave from shown is:','[{"text": "$10\\\\,V$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$7\\\\,V$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$6.37\\\\,V$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$11\\\\,V$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'$V_{r m s}=\\sqrt{\\dfrac{\\left(V_{1}\\right)^{2}+\\left(V_{2}\\right)^{3}+\\left(V_{3}\\right)^{2}}{\\text { Number of loops }}}$<br>$V_{r m s}=\\sqrt{\\dfrac{(10)^{2}+(10)^{3}+(10)^{2}}{3}}$<br>${V_{rms}} = \\sqrt {\\frac{{300}}{3}}  = \\sqrt {100}  = 10\\,V$.<br>So, correct option is (1).',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/37_45479_question_1.png'),
(45480,'physics',41,404,802,'The energy equivalent to a substance of mass 	1$g$ is','[{"text": "$18 \\\\times {10^{13}}J$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$9 \\\\times {10^{13}}J$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$18 \\\\times {10^6}J$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$9 \\\\times {10^6}J$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Here, <br>Mass, $m = 1,\\,g = {10^{ - 3}}kg$<br>According to Einstein’s mass energy relation, 	the energy equivalent of mass $m$ is <br>$E = m{c^2}$ ( where $c$ is the speed of light in vaccum)<br>$ = \\left( {{{10}^{ - 3}}kg} \\right){\\left( {3 \\times {{10}^8}m{s^{ - 1}}} \\right)^2}$<br>$ = {10^{ - 3}} \\times 9 \\times {10^{16}}J = 9 \\times {10^{13}}J$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45481,'physics',37,974,2272,'A $100\\,\\Omega $ electric iron is connected to $200\\,V$, $50\\,Hz\\,ac$ source. The average power delivered to iron, peak power and energy spent in one minute will be','[{"text": "$400\\\\,W,800\\\\,W,12 \\\\times {10^5}\\\\,J$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$400\\\\,W,900\\\\,W,1.2 \\\\times {10^5}\\\\,J$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$500\\\\,W,800\\\\,W,6 \\\\times {10^5}\\\\,J$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$400\\\\,W,900\\\\,W,60 \\\\times {10^5}\\\\,J$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Here, $R = 100\\Omega ,{V_{rms}} = 200\\,V,f = 50\\,Hz$<br>$\\langle P\\rangle=$ Average power $/$ cycle $=V_{r m s} I_{r m s}=\\dfrac{V_{r m s}^{2}}{R}$<br>$\\langle P\\rangle=\\dfrac{(200)^{2}}{100}=400$ $watt$<br>$P_{0}=$ Peak power $/$ cycle<br>$=V_{0} I_{0}=\\sqrt{2} V_{r m s} \\times \\sqrt{2} I_{r m s}$<br>$ \\Rightarrow {P_0} = 2 \\times 400 = 800\\,W$<br>But, the frequency is $50\\,Hz$<br>$\\Rightarrow$ Average power spent in one second $=\\langle P\\rangle f=400 \\times 50$<br>$\\therefore$ Energy spent in one minute $=U=\\langle P\\rangle f(60)$<br>$ \\Rightarrow U = 400 \\times 50 \\times 60 = 12 \\times {10^5}\\,J$.<br>So, correct option is (1).',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45482,'physics',39,1104,2631,'A material particle with a rest mass $m_{0}$ is moving with speed of light $c$. The de-Broglie wavelength associated is given by','[{"text": "$h/{m_0}c$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${m_0}c/h$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "zero", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "infinite", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'$\\lambda  = \\frac{h}{{mv}}$<br>where, $m = \\frac{{{m_0}}}{{\\sqrt {1 - {v^2}/{c^2}} }}$<br>As $\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,v = c,$ then<br>$ \\Rightarrow \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,m = \\frac{{{m_0}}}{{\\sqrt {1 - \\frac{{{c^2}}}{{{c^2}}}} }} = \\frac{{{m_0}}}{{\\sqrt {1 - 1} }} = \\frac{{{m_0}}}{0}$<br>$ \\Rightarrow \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,m = \\infty ,$<br>Hence, ${\\rm{ }}\\lambda  = \\frac{h}{\\infty } = 0$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45484,'physics',41,403,800,'Number of neutrons in ${C^{12}}\\,{\\rm{and}}\\,{C^{14}}$ are:','[{"text": "8 and 6", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "6 and 8", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "6 and 6", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "8 and 8", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Number of neutrons $ = $ Mass number $ - $ Atomic number<br>For ${C^{12}},\\,\\,\\, = 12 - 6 = 6$<br>For ${C^{14}},\\,\\,\\, = 14 - 6 = 8$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45485,'physics',37,974,2766,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> By only knowing the power factor 	for a given LCR circuit it is not possible to tell 	whether the applied alternating e.m.f. leads or 	lags the current.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> $\\cos \\theta  = \\cos ( - \\theta )$','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'This is because the reason provides a valid explanation for why knowing the power factor alone does not determine whether the e.m.f. leads or lags the current in an $L C R$ circuit. For that, we shall know whether $X_{L}>X_{C}$ (or) $X_{C}>X_{L}$. So correct option is (1).',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45486,'physics',39,1104,2631,'If alpha, beta and gamma rays carry same momentum, which has the longest wavelength?','[{"text": "Alpha rays", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Beta rays", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Gamma rays", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "All have same wavelength", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'We know that, $\\lambda  = h/p$<br>Given, ${\\mkern 1mu} {\\kern 1pt} \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{p_\\alpha } = {p_\\beta } = {p_\\gamma }$<br>So, all have same wavelength.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45488,'physics',41,403,801,'If ${r_1}$ and ${r_2}$ are the radii of the atomic nuclei of mass numbers 64 and 125 respectively, then the ratio $\\left( {{r_1}/{r_2}} \\right)$ is','[{"text": "$\\\\frac{{64}}{{125}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\sqrt {\\\\frac{{64}}{{125}}} $", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{5}{4}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{4}{5}$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Nuclear radius ,$r = {r_0}{\\left( A \\right)^{1/3}}$<br>$\\therefore \\,\\,\\,\\frac{{{r_1}}}{{{r_2}}} = {\\left( {\\frac{{{A_1}}}{{{A_2}}}} \\right)^{1/3}} = {\\left( {\\frac{{64}}{{125}}} \\right)^{1/3}} = {\\left( {\\frac{{{4^3}}}{{{5^3}}}} \\right)^{1/3}} = \\frac{4}{5}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2006,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45489,'physics',37,975,2278,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> In a series LCR circuit, at resonace 	condition power consumed by circuit is non-zero.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> At resonance, impedance of the circuit 	is maximum.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'At resonance, in reality, resonance minimizes impedance, not effective resistance, leading to increased power consumption.$X_{L}=X_{C} \\Rightarrow Z=Z_{\\min }=R$ So correct option is (3).',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45490,'physics',39,1103,2630,'A radio transmitter radiated $1\\,kW$ power at a wavelength $198.6\\,m$. How many photons does it emit per second?','[{"text": "$10^{10}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$10^{20}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$10^{30}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$10^{40}$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Given, $P=1 \\mathrm{~kW}=1000 \\mathrm{~W}$ and $\\lambda=198.6 \\mathrm{~m}$Number of photons emitted per second$\\begin{aligned}n & =\\dfrac{P}{E}=\\dfrac{P}{h v}=\\dfrac{P \\lambda}{h c} \\\\& =\\dfrac{1000 \\times 198.6}{6.6 \\times 10^{-34} \\times 3 \\times 10^{8}}=10^{30}\\end{aligned}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45492,'physics',41,405,805,'Two protons are kept at a separation of 10 $nm$. Let  ${F_n}$ and ${F_e}$ be the nuclear force and the electromagnetic force between them','[{"text": "${F_e} <  < {F_n}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${F_e}$ and ${F_n}$ differ only slightly", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${F_e} = {F_n}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${F_e} >  > {F_n}$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Since nuclear force is a short range force $\\left( { = {{10}^{ - 15}}m} \\right)$, for a separation of ${10^{ - 8}}m$, nuclear force between protons will  be negligible $\\therefore {F_e} >  > {F_n}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2020,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45493,'physics',37,973,2265,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> In series $L-R$ circuit, the voltage across $L$ leads the current in circuit.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> In series $L-R$ circuit current leads the voltage across $L.$','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'The voltage across the inductor leads the current in a series $L-R$ circuit. When $I=0, V_{L}=$ maximum when $\\phi=\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}, I$ is maximum and $V_{L} \\rightarrow 0$<img src=\'/img/data/physics/37_45493_explanation_1.jpg\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' /> We can also state that current in $L$ lags behind voltage. So correct option is (3).',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45495,'physics',41,403,801,'The ratio of the nuclear radii of elements with mass numbers 216 and 125 is','[{"text": "$6:5$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\sqrt {216} :\\\\sqrt {125} $", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$216:125$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "none of these", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'For a nucleus of mass number  $A$, its radius is given by <br>$R = {R_0}{A^{1/3}}$<br> Hence for two elements with $A = 216$ and $A = 125$,<br>$\\therefore \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\frac{{R\\left( {^{216}X} \\right)}}{{R\\left( {^{125}Y} \\right)}} = \\frac{{\\sqrt[3]{{216}}}}{{\\sqrt[3]{{125}}}} = \\frac{6}{5}$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2008,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45496,'physics',37,973,2266,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Given circuit: <img src=\'/img/data/physics/37_45496_qtext_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' /> Then ${V_C}$ in the circuit $ = 2\\,V$<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Kirchoff voltage law rule can be 	applied to AC circuits also.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but reason is correct.", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Reason is true as kirchoffs voltage law $(K V L)$ is fundamentally for conservation of electrical energy. Both $a . c$ and d.c shall follow it. In circuit of \'Assertion\' the values of voltages indicated are taken as rms values of those alternating quantities. But, we shall add them vectorially. Across $C$, we have $V_{C}=\\sqrt{V_{R}+V_{C}}$<br>$10=\\sqrt{8^{2}+V_{C}^{2}} \\Rightarrow V_{C}=4 V$<br>Assertion is wrong<br>So, correct option is (4).',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45497,'physics',40,245,487,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> In photoemissive cell inert gas is used.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Inert gas in the photoemissive cell gives greater current.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'The photoemissive cell can be either evacuated or filled with an inert gas at low pressure. Introducing an inert gas into the cell can result in a higher current, but it may also introduce a time lag in the cell\'s response to rapid changes in radiation. So correct option is $(1)$.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45498,'physics',39,1104,2631,'If $E_{1}, E_{2}, E_{3}$ are the respective kinetic energies of an electron, an alpha-particle and a proton, each having the same de-Broglie wavelength, then','[{"text": "${E_1} > {E_3} > {E_2}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$E_{2}>E_{3}>E_{1}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${E_1} > {E_2} > {E_3}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${E_1} = {E_2} = {E_3}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'According to relation, $E=\\dfrac{1}{2} m v^{2}$<br>$\\sqrt {\\frac{{2E}}{m}}  = v$<br>$\\lambda  = \\frac{h}{{\\sqrt {2mE} }}$<br>Because ${m_1} < {m_3} < {m_2}$<br>So for same $\\lambda, E_{1}>E_{3}>E_{2}$.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45499,'physics',41,403,801,'The volume of a nucleus is directly proportional to','[{"text": "$A$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${A^3}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\sqrt A $", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${A^{1/3}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Radius of nucleus $R = {R_0}{A^{1/3}}$<br>where ${R_0} = 1.2 \\times {10^{ - 15}}m$<br>and $A$ is the mass number of nucleus<br>$\\therefore $ Volume of nucleus $ = \\frac{4}{3}\\pi {R^3} = \\left( {\\frac{4}{3}\\pi R_0^3} \\right)A$<br>$\\therefore $ Volume is proportional to $A$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2007,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45500,'physics',37,972,2260,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Heat generated when same resistance $(R)$ is connected with $A C$ source of $V_{r m s}$ will not be same of $D C$ source (with $\\left.V_{D C}=V_{r m s}\\right)$.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> $R.M.S.$ voltage is greater than maximum $A C$ volatge.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'$P_{a c} < P_{D C}$<br>Here $P_{a c}=\\dfrac{V_{r m s}^{2}}{R}$<br>$P_{D C}=\\dfrac{V_{D C}^{2}}{R}$<br>$V_{r m s}=\\dfrac{V_{0}}{\\sqrt{2}} < V_{0}$<br>In Assertion: $V_{r m s}=V_{D C}$<br>So correct option is (3).',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45501,'physics',39,1104,2631,'If the momentum of electron is changed by $P$, then the de Broglie wavelength associated with it changes by $0.5 \\%$. The initial momentum of electron will be','[{"text": "$200 P$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$400 P$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{P}{200}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$100 P$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'$\\frac{{d\\lambda }}{\\lambda } =  - \\frac{{dp}}{P}$<br>$\\frac{{0.5}}{{100}} = \\frac{P}{{{P^\\prime }}} \\Rightarrow {P^\\prime } = 200P$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45502,'physics',40,245,487,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Isotope of an element can be separated by using a mass spectrometer.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Separation of isotope is possible because of the difference in electron numbers of isotope.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'The atomic number, which corresponds to the number of electrons or protons, remains the same in isotopes of an element. <img src=\'/img/data/physics/40_45502_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' /> Two circles are shown. Their radii $r_{1}$ and $r_{2}$ are different due to different values of<br>$(Q / M) \\text { in formula } r=(M v / Q B)$<br>Isotopes of an element can be differentiated based on their distinct atomic weights using a mass spectrograph. So correct option is (3).',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45510,'physics',41,406,806,'A radio isotope $X $with a half-life  $1.4 \\times {10^9}$ yr decays to $Y$ which is stable. A sample of the rock from a cave was found to contain $X$ and $Y$ in the ratio 1:7. The age of the rock is','[{"text": "$1.96 \\\\times {10^9}\\\\,yr$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$3.92 \\\\times {10^9}\\\\,yr$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$4.20 \\\\times {10^9}\\\\,yr$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$8.40 \\\\times {10^9}\\\\,yr$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Given that <br>$\\frac{{{N_x}}}{{{N_y}}} = \\frac{1}{7}$<br>$\\frac{{{N_0}{e^{ - \\lambda t}}}}{{{N_0} - {N_0}{e^{ - \\lambda t}}}} = \\frac{1}{7}$<br>$8{e^{ - \\lambda t}} = 1$<br>$t = \\frac{{\\ln 8}}{\\lambda } = 4.2 \\times {10^9}y$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45503,'physics',41,407,811,'A nucleus at rest splits into two nuclear parts having radii in the ratio 1:2. Their velocities are in the ratio','[{"text": "$6:1$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2:1$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$8:1$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$4:1$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Let  ${A_1}$ and  ${A_2}$ be the mass number of the two nuclear parts. Their radii are given by<br>${R_1} = {R_0}A_1^{1/3}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,(1)$<br>and ${R_2} = {R_0}A_2^{1/3}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,(2)$<br>Dividing eqn. (1) by eqn(2), we get<br>$\\frac{{{R_1}}}{{{R_2}}} = {\\left( {\\frac{{{A_1}}}{{{A_2}}}} \\right)^{1/3}}or\\frac{{{A_1}}}{{{A_2}}} = {\\left( {\\frac{{{R_1}}}{{{R_2}}}} \\right)^3}$<br>As $\\frac{{{R_1}}}{{{R_2}}} = \\frac{1}{2}$ (given) <br>$\\therefore \\frac{{{A_1}}}{{{A_2}}} = {\\left( {\\frac{1}{2}} \\right)^3} = \\frac{1}{8}$<br>Hence the ratio of their masses is <br>$\\frac{{{m_1}}}{{{m_2}}} = \\frac{1}{8}$              (3)<br>($\\therefore $ mass is approximately proprtional to $A$)<br>According to law of conservation of linear momentum<br>magnitude of  ${{\\vec p}_1} = $ magnitude of ${{\\vec p}_2}$<br>i.e., ${m_1}{v_1} = {m_2}{v_2}$<br>or $\\frac{{{v_1}}}{{{v_2}}} = \\frac{{{m_2}}}{{{m_1}}} = \\frac{8}{1}$    (using (3))',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2015,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL);
INSERT INTO `qb_questions` (`id`, `subject_id`, `chapter_id`, `topic_id`, `subtopic_id`, `question_html`, `options`, `correct_index`, `explanation`, `explanation_image_url`, `difficulty`, `qtype`, `year`, `tag`, `created_at`, `question_image_url`) VALUES
(45504,'physics',37,973,2268,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> When frequency is greater than resonance frequency in a series $L C R$ circuit, it will be an inductive circuit.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Applied volatge will lead the current in a circuit having pure inductor.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'The current vs frequency in an LCR series circuit is. <img src=\'/img/data/physics/37_45504_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />  $V=I Z$<br>$Z=\\sqrt{\\left(X_{L}-X_{C}\\right)^{2}+R^{2}}$<br>$f_{0}=$ resonant frequency<br>At resonance $X_{L}=X_{C}$<br>At $f>f_{0}$, circuit is inductive as $X_{L}>X_{C}$<br>$\\Rightarrow V_{L}=V_{C}$<br>${V_{applied{\\rm{ }}}}$ is leading I by $\\phi . V_{L}$ also leads I but by $\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}$ <img src=\'/img/data/physics/37_45504_explanation_2.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' /> So correct option is (2).',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45505,'physics',39,1102,2629,'The photoelectric threshold frequency of a metal is $v_{0}$. When light of frequency $4 v_{0}$ is incident on the metal, the maximum kinetic energy of the emitted photoelectrons is','[{"text": "$4\\\\, h v_{0}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$3\\\\, h v_{0}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{h v_{0}}{4}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{h v_{0}}{2}$.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'As, $h v=W+K_{\\max }$<br>Here $W=h v_{0}$ and $h v=h\\left(4 v_{0}\\right)$<br>$\\Rightarrow \\quad K_{\\max }=h v-W=4 h v_{0}-h v_{0}=3 h v_{0}$.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45506,'physics',40,239,478,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Bohr\'s atomic model cannot be used to explain multiple electron species<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> It does not take inter-electronic interactions into account.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Bohr\'s atomic model, while successful for hydrogen, inadequately addresses multi-electron atoms or species due to its single-electron assumption and neglect of electron-electron repulsion. So correct option is (1).',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45507,'physics',41,408,813,'In a nuclear reactor heavy nuclei is not used as moderators because','[{"text": "They will break up", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Elastic collision of neutrons with heavy nuclei will not slow them down", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "The net weight of the reactor would be unbearably high", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Substance with heavy nuclei do not occur in  liquid or  gaseous state at room temperature", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Since the function of a moderator is to reduce the speed of neutrons, materials with lighter nuclei are chosen for this purpose so as to maximise the reduction in kinetic energy of neutrons. For a near-elastic collision with heavy nucleus, the speed of a neutron may not reduce much.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2021,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45508,'physics',37,973,2268,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> When capacitive reactance is smaller than the inductive reactance in series $L C R$ circuit, e.m.f. leads the current.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> The phase angle between the applied $e.m.f$ and alternating current in $L$ the circuit is zero in that case.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'$X_{C} < X_{L}$<br>$\\Rightarrow X_{L}>X_{C}$<br>$\\Rightarrow$ Circuit is inductive and so $V$ leads with $\\phi \\neq 0$. So correct option is (3).',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45509,'physics',39,1102,2628,'Silver has a work function of $4.7eV$. When ultraviolet light of wavelength $100\\,nm$ is incident on it a potential of $7.7\\,V$ is required to stop the photoelectrons from reaching the collector plate. How much potential will be required to stop photoelectrons, when light of wavelength $200\\, nm$ is incident on it?','[{"text": "$15.4\\\\,V$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2.35\\\\,V$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$3.85\\\\,V$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1.5\\\\,V$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'According to Einstein\'s photoelectric equation<br>$e V_{0}=\\dfrac{h c}{\\lambda}-\\phi_{0}$<br>where $V_{0}$ is the stopping potential, $\\lambda$ is the incident wavelength and $\\phi_{0}$ is the work function of the metal.<br>or $\\dfrac{h c}{\\lambda}=e V_{0}+\\phi_{0}$<br>For wavelengths $\\lambda_{1}$ and $\\lambda_{2}$,<br>$\\frac{{hc}}{{{\\lambda _1}}} = e{V_{01}} + {\\phi _0}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,(1)$<br>and $\\frac{{hc}}{{{\\lambda _2}}} = e{V_{02}} + {\\phi _0}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,(2)$<br>Dividing eqn. (1) by eqn. (2), we get<br>$\\dfrac{\\lambda_{2}}{\\lambda_{1}}=\\dfrac{e V_{01}+\\phi_{0}}{e V_{02}+\\phi_{0}}$<br>Here, ${\\lambda _1} = 100\\,nm,{\\lambda _2} = 200\\,nm$<br>$\\therefore \\quad \\frac{{200\\,nm}}{{100\\,nm}} = \\frac{{e{V_{01}} + {\\phi _0}}}{{e{V_{02}} + {\\phi _0}}}$<br>$2 = \\frac{{e{V_{01}} + {\\phi _0}}}{{e{V_{02}} + {\\phi _0}}} \\Rightarrow e{V_{02}} = \\frac{{e{V_{01}} - {\\phi _0}}}{2}$<br> As ${V_{01}} = 7.7V$ and ${\\phi _0} = 4.7eV$(given) <br>$\\therefore e{V_{02}} = \\frac{{e(7.7V) - 4.7eV}}{2}$<br>$ = \\frac{{7.7eV - 4.7eV}}{2} = \\frac{{3\\,eV}}{2} = 1.5\\,eV$<br>${V_{02}} = 1.5V$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45511,'physics',37,978,2282,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> An alternating current cannot produce magnetic field.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Alternating current varies with time. across $L$ leads the current in circuit .','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but reason is correct.", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'An alternating current, like direct current, produces a magnetic field, But the difference is that the magnitude and direction of the field change continuously with time in case of alternating current. So correct option is (4).',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45513,'physics',39,1102,2627,'If the distance of $100\\,W$ lamp is increased from a photocell, the saturation current $i$ in the photocell varies with the distance $d$ as','[{"text": "$i \\\\propto d^{2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$i \\\\propto d$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$i \\\\propto \\\\dfrac{1}{d}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$i \\\\propto \\\\dfrac{1}{d^{2}}$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Saturation current depends upon the intensity of light which is inversely proportional to the square of the distance of the lamp from the photosensitive surface.<br>Thus $i \\propto \\dfrac{1}{d^{2}}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45514,'physics',37,973,2264,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Capacitor serves as a block for $D C$ and offers an easy path of $A C$.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Capacitive reactance is inversely proportional to frequency (incase of $A C$).','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'$X_{C}=\\dfrac{1}{2 \\pi f C}$ This is because the reason accurately explains how a capacitor blocks $D C$ we know $f=0$ for Direct current $(D C)$.<br>$\\Rightarrow X_{C} \\rightarrow \\infty$ while allowing $\\mathrm{AC}$ to pass through. as $X_{C}$ is finite. So correct option is (1).',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45515,'physics',41,408,814,'Fusion reaction takes place at high temperature because','[{"text": "Kinetic energy is high enough to overcome the  coulomb repulsion between nuclei", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Atoms get ionised at high temperature", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Nuclei break up at high temperature", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Molecules break up at high temperature", "isCorrect": false}]',0,NULL,NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45516,'physics',40,245,487,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> In a metal all the free electrons are in same energy band.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Electrons do not obey Pauli\'s exclusion principle.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Metals are solids (except $Hg$). They have single energy band (for carriers viz. electrons) in the sense that valence band and conduction band overlop: The band formation is any solids is due to extension of Pauli exclusion principle which is originally for atoms<br>$\\Rightarrow$ Reason is false. So correct option is (3).',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45517,'physics',39,1102,3092,'The following process is known as $h v \\rightarrow e^{+}+e^{-}$','[{"text": "pair production", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "photoelectric effect", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Compton effect", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Zeeman effect", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'The given process is known as pair production. In this process a photon of sufficient energy materializes into an electron $\\left(e^{-}\\right)$and a positron $({e^ + }).$ It is illustrated in figure. <img src=\'/img/data/physics/39_45517_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45518,'physics',37,978,2282,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> The $D C$ and $A C$ both can be measured by a hot wire instruments.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> The hot wire instrument is based on the principle is magnetic effect of current.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'In hot wire instruments can measure both $D C$ and $A C$ based on the heating effect of current, (deflection $\\propto i^{2}$ ) ${\\rm{[}}$Here $i=I_{D C}$ (or) $\\left. {i = {I_{rms}}} \\right]$. So correct option is (3).',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45525,'physics',40,245,487,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> LASER is used to measure distant objects.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> LASER is highly coherent source of light.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Laser beams, are highly monochromatic nature and coherent, and intense beams So, they can do precise distance measurements. So correct option is (1).',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45519,'physics',41,408,814,'The thermonuclear reaction of hydrogen inside the stars is taking place by a cycle of operations. The particular element which acts as a catalyst is','[{"text": "Nitrogen", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Oxygen", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Helium", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Carbon", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'The carbon cycle for energy generation in stars is represented by the following sets of reactions:<br>${}_6^{12}C + {}_1^1H \\to {}_7^{13}N + \\gamma  + 1.93MeV\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,(1)$<br>${}_7^{13}N \\to {}_6^{13}C + {e^ + } + v + 1.20MeV\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,(2)$<br>${}_6^{13}C + {}_1^1H \\to {}_7^{14}N + \\gamma  + 7.6MeV\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,(3)$<br>${}_7^{14}N + {}_1^1H \\to {}_8^{15}O + \\gamma  + 7.39MeV\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,(4)$<br>${}_8^{15}O \\to {}_7^{15}N + {e^ + } + v + 1.71MeV\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,(5)$<br>${}_7^{15}N + {}_1^1H \\to {}_6^{12}C + {}_2^4He + 4.99MeV\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,(6)$<br>In this set of reactions carbon is not destroyed but acts only as a catalyst',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2011,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45520,'physics',39,1102,2628,'When a certain metallic surface is illuminated with monochromatic light of wavelength $\\lambda$, the stopping potential for photoelectric current is $3 V_{0}$. When the same surface is illuminated with a light of wavelength $2 \\lambda$, the stopping potential is $V_{0}$. The threshold wavelength for this surface for photoelectric effect is','[{"text": "$4 \\\\lambda$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$6 \\\\lambda$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$8 \\\\lambda$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{4}{3} \\\\lambda$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'According to Einstein\'s photoelectric equation<br>$e V_{0}=\\dfrac{h c}{\\lambda}-\\phi_{0}$<br>where $V_{0}$ is the stopping potential, $\\lambda$ is the wavelength of incident light and $\\phi_{0}$ is the work function of the surface.<br>As per question<br>$e\\left( {3{V_0}} \\right) = \\frac{{hc}}{\\lambda } - {\\phi _0}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,(1)$<br>and $e{V_0} = \\frac{{hc}}{{2\\lambda }} - {\\phi _0}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,(2)$<br>Multiplying eqn. (2) by 3 , we get<br>$e\\left( {3{V_0}} \\right) = \\frac{{3hc}}{{2\\lambda }} - 3{\\phi _0}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,(3)$<br>Equating eqns. (1) and (3), we get<br>$\\dfrac{h c}{\\lambda}-\\phi_{0}=\\dfrac{3 h c}{2 \\lambda}-3 \\phi_{0}$ or $\\phi_{0}=\\dfrac{h c}{4 \\lambda}$<br>$\\therefore \\quad$ The threshold wavelength is<br>$\\lambda_{0}=\\dfrac{h c}{\\phi_{0}}=\\dfrac{h c}{(h c / 4 \\lambda)}=4 \\lambda$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45522,'physics',41,408,813,'On bombarding ${U^{235}}$ by slow neutron, 200 $MeV$ energy is released. If the power output of atomic rector is 1.6 $MW$, then the rate of fission will be','[{"text": "$8 \\\\times {10^{16}}/s$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$20 \\\\times {10^{16}}/s$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$5 \\\\times {10^{22}}/s$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$5 \\\\times {10^{16}}/s$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Energy released per fission of uranium nucleus<br>$ = 200 \\times {10^6} \\times 1.6 \\times {10^{ - 19}}J$<br>Power output $ = 1.6 \\times {10^6}$<br>$\\therefore $ Number of fission/s <br>$ = \\frac{{{\\rm{Power}}\\,{\\rm{output}}}}{{{\\rm{Energy}}\\,{\\rm{release}}\\,{\\rm{per}}\\,{\\rm{fission}}}}$<br>$ = \\frac{{1.6 \\times {{10}^6}}}{{200 \\times {{10}^6} \\times 1.6 \\times {{10}^{ - 19}}}} = 5 \\times {10^{16}}/s$<br>This is the rate of fission',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2008,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45523,'physics',37,978,2282,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> $A C$ is more dangerous than $D C$<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Frequency of $A C$ is dangerous for human body.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Body acts as capacitor, $X_{C}=\\dfrac{1}{2 \\pi f C}$ is high for low supply frequency 50 to $60\\,Hz$. For why Hence, $A C$, especially at low frequencies, can be more dangerous to the human body due to its effects on the heart\'s electrical rhythms. But , $D C$ has $f=0 \\Rightarrow X_{C} \\rightarrow \\infty$. So correct option is $(1)$.',NULL,'Easy','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45524,'physics',39,1102,2626,'$A$ and $B$ are two metals with threshold frequencies $1.8 \\times {10^{14}}\\,Hz$ and $2.2 \\times {10^{14}}\\,Hz$. Two identical photons of energy $0.825\\,eV$ each are incident on them. Then photoelectrons are emitted by<br>(Take $h = 6.6 \\times {10^{ - 34}}\\,J\\,s$ )','[{"text": "$B$ alone", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "A alone", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "neither $A$ nor $B$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "both $A$ and $B$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Here,<br>Threshold frequency of  $A,$<br>${v_{0A}} = 1.8 \\times {10^{14}}\\,Hz$<br>Threshold frequency of  $B,$<br>${v_{0B}} = 2.2 \\times {10^{14}}\\,Hz$<br>The work function of $A$ is<br>${\\phi _{0A}} = h{v_{0A}} = \\left( {6.6 \\times {{10}^{ - 34}}\\,J\\,s} \\right)\\left( {1.8 \\times {{10}^{14}}\\,Hz} \\right)$<br>$ = 11.88 \\times {10^{ - 20}}\\,J$<br>$ = \\frac{{11.88 \\times {{10}^{ - 20}}}}{{1.6 \\times {{10}^{ - 19}}}}eV\\left( {1eV = 1.6 \\times {{10}^{ - 19}}\\,J} \\right)$<br>$ = 0.742\\,eV$<br>and that of $B$ is<br>${\\phi _{OB}} = h{v_{OB}} = \\left( {6.6 \\times {{10}^{ - 34}}\\,J\\,s} \\right)\\left( {2.2 \\times {{10}^{14}}\\,Hz} \\right)$<br>$ = 14.52 \\times {10^{ - 20}}\\,J$<br>$ = \\frac{{14.52 \\times {{10}^{ - 20}}}}{{1.6 \\times {{10}^{ - 19}}}}eV = 0.908\\,eV$<br>Since the incident energy $0.825\\,eV$ is greater than $0.742\\,eV$ but less than $0.908\\,eV,$ so photoelectrons are emitted by $A$ alone.',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45558,'physics',39,1104,2631,'When a proton is accelerated with 1 volt potential difference, its kinetic energy is','[{"text": "1840 $eV$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "1 $eV$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "184 $eV$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "18400 $eV$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Kinetic energy of a proton accelerated through a potential difference ${V}$ is <br> ${K E=e V}$<br>${K E=1.6 \\times 10^{-19} \\times 1 {~J}=1.6 \\times 10^{-19} {~J}=1 {eV}}$.<br>So correct option is (2)',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45526,'physics',41,406,806,'The half-life of a radioactive isotope $X$ is 50 yr. It decays to another element Y which is stable. The two elements $X$ and $Y$ were found to be in the ratio of 1:15 in a sample of a given rock. The age of the rock was estimated to be','[{"text": "$250\\\\,yr$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$100\\\\,yr$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$200\\\\,yr$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$150\\\\,yr$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Let ${N_0}$ is the initial concentration of  $X$.<br> The concentration of $X$ at time $t$ is<br>${N_X} = {N_0}{e^{ - \\lambda t}}$<br>${N_Y} = {N_0} - {N_0}{e^{ - \\lambda t}}$<br>$\\frac{{{N_X}}}{{{N_Y}}} = \\frac{{{N_0}{e^{ - \\lambda t}}}}{{{N_0} - {N_0}{e^{ - \\lambda t}}}} = \\frac{1}{{15}}$<br>$ \\Rightarrow 1 = 16{e^{ - \\lambda t}} \\Rightarrow t = \\frac{{\\ln 16}}{\\lambda } = 200yr$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45527,'physics',37,973,2266,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> An electric lamp connected in series with variable capacitor and $A C$ source, its brightness increases with increase in capacitance.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Capacitive reactance decreases with increases in capacitance of capacitor.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'More the value of $C$, more $I$ and hence more the brightness of the lamp in an $A C$ circuit.$X_{C}=\\dfrac{1}{2 \\pi f C}$. Variable capacitor has varying $C$.<br>Then, $I = \\frac{{{E_{{\\rm{supply }}}}}}{{\\sqrt {{R^2} + X_C^2} }}$<br>So correct option is (1).',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45528,'physics',39,1104,2631,'The kinetic energy of an electron get tripled, then the de Broglie wavelength associated with it changes by a factor','[{"text": "$\\\\dfrac{1}{3}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\sqrt{3}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{1}{\\\\sqrt{3}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "3", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'de Broglie wavelength associated with an electron is<br>$\\lambda=\\dfrac{h}{\\sqrt{2 m K}}$<br>where $m$ be mass of the electron and $K$ its kinetic energy.<br>or $\\lambda \\propto \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{K}}$<br>$\\therefore \\quad \\dfrac{\\lambda^{\\prime}}{\\lambda}=\\sqrt{\\dfrac{K}{K^{\\prime}}}=\\sqrt{\\dfrac{K}{3 K}}=\\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}$<br>$\\lambda^{\\prime}=\\dfrac{\\lambda}{\\sqrt{3}}$<br>Hence, de Broglie wavelength changes by $\\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}$.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45529,'physics',41,406,806,'The acitivity of a radioactive sample is measured as ${N_0}/e$ counts per minute at  $t = 5\\min .$ The time (in minute) at which the activity reduces to half its value is','[{"text": "${\\\\log _2}2/5$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$5{\\\\log _e}2$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$5{\\\\log _{10}}2$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{5}{{{{\\\\log }_e}2}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Fraction remains after $n$ half lives<br>$\\frac{N}{{{N_0}}} = {\\left( {\\frac{1}{2}} \\right)^n} = {\\left( {\\frac{1}{2}} \\right)^{t/T}}$<br>Where $T$ is the half life<br>Given, $N = \\frac{{{N_0}}}{e} \\Rightarrow \\frac{{{N_0}}}{{e{N_0}}} = {\\left( {\\frac{1}{2}} \\right)^{5/T}}$<br>or $\\frac{1}{e} = {\\left( {\\frac{1}{2}} \\right)^{5/T}}$<br>Taking log on both sides, we get<br>$\\log 1 - \\log e = \\frac{5}{T}\\log \\frac{1}{2}$<br>$ - 1 = \\frac{5}{T}( - \\log 2)$<br>$ \\Rightarrow \\quad T = 5{\\log _e}2$<br>The time after which activity reduces to half is $5\\log 2$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45531,'physics',37,973,2271,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> The quantity $L / R$ possesses dimensions of time.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> To reduce the rate of increases of current through a solenoid, can be increased the time constant $(L / R)$.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'$\\dfrac{d I}{d t}=$ Rate of change of current <img src=\'/img/data/physics/37_45531_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' /> Here $E-I R-L \\dfrac{d I}{d t}=0$<br>$I=\\dfrac{E}{R}\\left(1-e^{-t R / L}\\right)$ Where $\\tau=\\dfrac{L}{R}$<br>$V_{L}=\\left(\\dfrac{E}{R}\\right) e^{-t / \\tau}$<br>$\\left|V_{L}\\right|=L \\dfrac{d I}{d t}$<br>If $\\tau\\left(=\\dfrac{L}{R}\\right)$ is increased, rate of increases in $I_{L}$ decreases.<br>$\\left(\\dfrac{d I}{d t}\\right) \\propto \\dfrac{1}{\\tau}\\left(\\right.$ Here $\\left.I=I_{L}\\right)$<br>So correct option is (2).',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45532,'physics',39,1102,2628,'In photoelectric emission process from a metal of work function $1.8\\,eV$, the kinetic energy of most energetic electrons is $0.5\\,eV$. The corresponding stopping potential is','[{"text": "$1.3\\\\,V$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$0.5\\\\,V$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$2.3\\\\,V$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1.8\\\\,V$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Here,${K_{\\max }} = 0.5\\,eV$<br>If $V_{0}$ is the stopping potential, then<br>$K_{\\max }=e V_{0}$<br>$\\therefore \\quad 0.5\\,eV = e{V_0}$ or ${V_0} = 0.5\\,V$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45533,'physics',41,406,806,'A radioactive element has rate of disintegration 10,000 disintegrations per minute at a particular instant. After four minutes it becomes 2500 disintegrations per minute. The decay constant per minute is','[{"text": "$0.2{\\\\log _e}2$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$0.5{\\\\log _e}2$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$0.6{\\\\log _e}2$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$0.8{\\\\log _e}2$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Given,${N_0} = 10000$ disintegration/min<br>$N = 2500$ disintegration/min<br>$t = 4\\,\\min $<br>From the radioactive decay law, we have<br>$\\frac{N}{{{N_0}}} = {e^{ - \\lambda t}}$<br>$\\therefore \\,\\,\\,\\frac{{2500}}{{10000}} = {e^{ - \\lambda  \\times 4}}$<br>$ \\Rightarrow \\frac{1}{4} = {e^{ - 4\\lambda }} \\Rightarrow {e^{4\\lambda }} = 4$<br>$ \\Rightarrow 4\\lambda  = {\\log _e}4 \\Rightarrow 4\\lambda  = {\\log _e}{2^2}$<br>$ \\Rightarrow 4\\lambda  = 2{\\log _e}2$<br>$\\lambda  = \\frac{2}{4}{\\log _e}2$<br>$\\therefore \\lambda  = 0.5{\\log _e}2$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2017,'MHTCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45534,'physics',37,973,2262,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Faraday\'s laws are consequences of conservation of energy.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> In a purely resistive $A C$ circuit, the current lags behind the $e.m.f$ in phase.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'In pure $R$ circuit $I, E$ are in same phase. So correct option is (3).',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45535,'physics',39,1102,2629,'The frequency of incident light falling on a photosensitive metal plate is doubled, the kinetic energy of the emitted photoelectrons is','[{"text": "double the earlier value", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "unchanged", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "more than doubled", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "less than doubled", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'According to Einstein\'s photoelectric eqn, the kinetic energy of photoelectron,<br>$K E=h v-\\phi$<br>If frequency of incident light is doubled then,<br>$K E^{\\prime}=h v^{\\prime}-\\phi=2 h v-\\phi=2(h v-\\phi)+\\phi$<br>$K E^{\\prime}=2 K E+\\phi$.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45537,'physics',41,406,806,'A radioactive sample ${S_1}$ having the activity  ${A_1}$has twice the number of nuclei as another sample  of ${S_2}$ activity ${A_2}$. If  ${A_2} = 2{A_1}$, then the ratio of half life of ${S_1}$ to the life of ${S_2}$  is','[{"text": "$4$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$2$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$0.25$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$0.75$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Activity , $A = \\lambda N = \\frac{{0.693}}{T}N$<br>Where $T$ is the half life of a radioactive sample, $N$ is the number of nuclei and  $\\lambda $ is the decay constant.<br>$\\therefore \\frac{{{A_1}}}{{{A_2}}} = \\frac{{{N_1}}}{{{T_1}}} \\times \\frac{{{T_2}}}{{{N_2}}}$<br>$\\frac{{{T_1}}}{{{T_2}}} = \\frac{{{A_2}}}{{{A_1}}} \\times \\frac{{{N_1}}}{{{N_2}}} = \\frac{{2{A_1}}}{{{A_1}}} \\times \\frac{{2{N_2}}}{{{N_2}}} = \\frac{4}{1}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2010,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45538,'physics',37,975,2276,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> At resonance, $L C R$ series circuit has a maximum current.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> At resonance, in $L C R$ series circuit, the current and $e.m.f$ are in phase with each other and impedance is minimum.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'<img src=\'/img/data/physics/37_45538_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' /> In series LCR circuit, at resonant $f_{0}$, we have I and emf in phase as $X_{C}=X_{L}$ and circuit is resistive. $\\Rightarrow$ the current is at its maximum. So correct option is (1).',NULL,'Easy','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45539,'physics',39,1103,2630,'An electron accelerated under a potential difference $V$ volt has a certain wavelength $\\lambda$. Mass of proton is some 2000 times of the mass of the electron. If the proton has to have the same wavelength $\\lambda$, then it will have to be accelerated under a potential difference of','[{"text": "$V$ volt", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2000 \\\\mathrm{~V}$ volt", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{V}{2000}$ volt", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\sqrt {2000} \\\\,V\\\\,{\\\\text{volt}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Wavelength of a particle,<br>$\\lambda=\\dfrac{h}{p}=\\dfrac{h}{\\sqrt{2 m q V}}$<br>Here, $\\lambda_{p}=\\lambda_{e}$<br>$\\dfrac{h}{\\sqrt{2 m_{p} q_{p} V_{p}}}=\\dfrac{h}{\\sqrt{2 m_{e} q_{e} V_{e}}}$<br>${m_e}{q_e}{V_e} = {m_p}{q_p}{V_p}\\,{\\text{or}}\\,{V_p} = \\left( {\\frac{{{m_e}}}{{{m_p}}}} \\right)\\left( {\\frac{{{q_e}}}{{{q_p}}}} \\right){V_e}$<br>Here, ${V_e} = V,{m_p} = 2000\\,{m_e},{q_e} = {q_p} = e$<br>$\\therefore \\quad V_{p}=\\dfrac{1}{2000} \\times 1 \\times V=\\dfrac{V}{2000}$ volt.',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45540,'physics',41,406,806,'The half life of a radioactive substance is 20 minutes. The time taken between 50% decay and 87.5% decay of the substance will be','[{"text": "40  minutes", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "10 minutes", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "30 minutes", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "25 minutes", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'After 50% decay, 50% $ = 1/2$ of the original material remains, i.e., the original material has undergone one half life $ = 20$ minutes.<br>After 87.5% decay, 12.5%$ = 1/8$ of the original material remains. Since $\\frac{1}{8} = {\\left( {\\frac{1}{2}} \\right)^3} = \\frac{1}{2} \\times \\frac{1}{2} \\times \\frac{1}{2}$, the original material has undergone 3 consecutive half lives $ = 20 \\times 3 = 60$ minutes<br>Therefore, time difference between 50% decay and 87.5% decay $ = 60 - 20 = 40$ minutres.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2015,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45542,'physics',37,978,2282,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> An inductor acts as a perfect conductor for D.C.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> D.C. remains constant in direction.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'$X_{L}=2 \\pi f L$, then $f=0$ for $D C \\Rightarrow X_{L}=0$ for $D C$. An inductor behaves like a perfect conductor (metallic wire) for $D . C$. due to its zero inductive reactance. Then, reason is general fact. So correct option is (2).',NULL,'Easy','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45543,'physics',39,1104,2631,'The ratio of momentum of an electron and $\\alpha$-particle which are accelerated from rest by a potential difference of $100\\,V$ is','[{"text": "1", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\sqrt{\\\\left(\\\\dfrac{2 m_{e}}{m_{\\\\alpha}}\\\\right)}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\sqrt{\\\\left(\\\\dfrac{m_{e}}{m_{\\\\alpha}}\\\\right)}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\sqrt{\\\\left(\\\\dfrac{m_{e}}{2 m_{\\\\alpha}}\\\\right)}$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'$P = \\sqrt {2\\,mKE}  = \\sqrt {2\\,mqV} $<br>$\\therefore \\dfrac{P_{e}}{P_{\\alpha}}=\\dfrac{\\sqrt{2 m_{e} q_{e} V_{e}}}{\\sqrt{2 m_{\\alpha} q_{\\alpha} V_{\\alpha}}}$<br>Here, $V_{c}=V_{\\alpha}=100, q_{\\alpha}=2 q_{e}$<br>So, $\\dfrac{P_{e}}{P_{\\alpha}}=\\sqrt{\\dfrac{m_{e}}{m_{\\alpha}} \\dfrac{1}{2} \\times 1}=\\sqrt{\\dfrac{m_{e}}{2 m_{\\alpha}}}$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45544,'physics',41,406,806,'1 curie represents','[{"text": "$3.7 \\\\times {10^7}$ disintegrations per second", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$3.7 \\\\times {10^{10}}$ disintegrations per second", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${10^6}$ disintegrations per second", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1$ disintegrations per second", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'1 curie = $ = 1\\,Ci$<br>$ = 3.7 \\times {10^{10}}$ decays per second<br>$ = 3.7 \\times {10^{10}}\\,Bq = 37\\,GBq$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2011,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45545,'physics',37,977,2770,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Step-down transformer decreases the output voltage.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Transformer obeys the law of conservation of energy.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Step-down transformer ${V_{oupput{\\rm{ }}}} < {V_{input{\\rm{ }}}}$ transformer obey\'s law of conservation of energy. So correct option is (2).',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45546,'physics',40,240,479,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Between any two given energy levels, the number of absorption transitions is always less than the number of emission transitions.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Absorption transitions start from the lowest energy level only and may end at any higher energy level. But emission transitions may start trom any higher energy level and end at any energy level below it.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'In electronic transitions, absorption transitions (such as Lyman) occur from the ground state to higher-energy states, while emission transitions offer more possibilities, leading to a higher count of emission transitions (such as Balmer). So correct option is (1).',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45547,'physics',39,1102,2629,'The maximum velocity of electrons emitted from a metal surface is $v$, when frequency of light falling on it is $f$. The maximum velocity when frequency becomes $4 f$ is','[{"text": "$2 v$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$>2 v$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$ < 2 v$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "between $2 v$ and $4 v$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Maximum velocity of photoelectron,<br>$v_{\\max }=v=\\sqrt{\\dfrac{2(E-\\phi)}{m}}=\\sqrt{\\dfrac{2(h f-\\phi)}{m}}$<br>If the frequency becomes $4 f$ then maximum velocity of photoelectron,<br>$\\begin{aligned}& v^{\\prime}=\\sqrt{\\dfrac{2(h \\times 4 f-\\phi)}{m}}=2 \\sqrt{\\dfrac{2(h f-\\phi / 4)}{m}} <br>\\\\& \\therefore \\quad v^{\\prime}>2 v\\end{aligned}$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45548,'physics',41,406,809,'A radioactive element forms its own isotope after 3 consecutive disintegrations. The particles emitted are','[{"text": "$3\\\\beta $ paricles", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2\\\\beta $ particles and $1\\\\alpha $ particle", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$2\\\\beta $ particles and $1\\\\gamma $ particle", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2\\\\alpha $ particles and $1\\\\beta $ particle", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Any isotope of the element  $_Z{X^A}$ should have same number of protons in its nucleus, i.e., the  isotope should have same $Z$.<br>Emission of alpha particle decreases $Z$ by 2. <br>Emission of beta particle increases $Z$ by 1.<br>The emission of one $\\alpha $ particle and two  $\\beta $ particles maintain the same $Z$.<br>Hence for isotope formation two  $\\beta $ particles and one $\\alpha $ particle are emitted',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2007,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45549,'physics',37,978,2282,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Long distance transmission of $A C$. carried out at extremely high voltage<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> For large distance,voltage has to be large.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'To minimize losses in power high voltage is used for long distance transitions $P=V i$. To have $i$ minimum(less power loss) $V$ has to be large So correct option is (1).',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45566,'physics',39,1102,2629,'Ultraviolet radiations of 6.2 $eV$ fall on an aluminium surface (work function 4.2 $eV$ ) The kinetic energy in joules of the fastest electron emitted is approximately','[{"text": "${3.2 \\\\times 10^{-21} {~J}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${3.2 \\\\times 10^{-19} {~J}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${3.2 \\\\times 10^{-17} {~J}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${3.2 \\\\times 10^{-15} {~J}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Kinetic energy of the fastest electron emitted will be<br>$K{E_{\\max }} = {E_i} - {W_0}$<br>$ \\Rightarrow K{E_{\\max }} = 6.2 eV - 4.2 eV = 2 eV$<br>$ \\Rightarrow K{E_{{\\rm{max }}}} = 2 \\times 1.6 \\times {10^{ - 19}}\\,J = 3.2 \\times {10^{ - 19}}\\,J$.<br>So correct option is (2)',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45550,'physics',40,241,480,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> There is a difference in the spectrum of sunlight when passed through "yellow-glass" and that of the light from a sodium vapour lamp.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> The sunlight always gives continuous spectrum with absorption lines (the Fraunhofer lines).','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Continuous spectrum in yellow glass results in a continuous yellow region. But, it is the emission line spectrum of sodium vapor lamps that display distinct yellow lines during atomic transitions Reason is a different fact. So correct option is (2).',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45551,'physics',39,1102,2628,'The collector plate in an experiment on photoelectric effect is kept vertically above the emitter plate. Light source is put on and a saturation photo-current is recorded. An electric field is switched on which has a vertically downward direction, then','[{"text": "the photo-current will increase", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "the kinetic energy of the electrons will increase", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "the stopping potential will decrease", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "the threshold wavelength will increase", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'In electric field, photoelectron will experience force and accelerate opposite to the field so its $KE$ increases $(i.e.,$ stopping potential will increase), no change in photoelectric current and threshold wavelength.<img src=\'/img/data/physics/39_45551_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45552,'physics',41,406,809,'In the nuclear decay given below<br>${}_Z^AX \\to {}_{Z + 1}^AY \\to {}_{Z - 1}^{A - 4}{B^*} \\to {}_{Z - 1}^{A - 4}B$<br>the particles emitted in the sequence are','[{"text": "$\\\\beta ,\\\\gamma ,\\\\alpha $", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\gamma ,\\\\beta ,\\\\alpha $", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\beta ,\\\\alpha ,\\\\gamma $", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\alpha ,\\\\beta ,\\\\gamma $", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'The following nuclear reactions are possible<br>${}_Z^AX \\to {}_{Z + 1}^AY + \\beta _{ - 1}^0$<br>${}_{Z + 1}^AY \\to {}_{Z - 1}^{A - 4}\\beta  + \\alpha _2^4$<br>${}_{Z - 1}^{A - 4}\\beta  \\to {}_{Z - 1}^{A - 4}\\beta  + \\gamma _0^0$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45553,'physics',37,978,2282,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> To damp the oscillation metallic frames is used in the dead beat galvanometer.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Principle of electromagnetic damping used in dead beat galvanometers.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Due to eddy current, coil may come to equilibrium instantly. Electromagnetic damping is employed in deadbeat galvanometers by using metallic frames. So correct option is (1).',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45554,'physics',39,1102,2629,'Light of wavelength 5000 $ \\mathop A^{~~\\circ}  $ falls on a sensitive plate with photoelectric work function of 1.9 $eV$. The kinetic energy of the photoelectron emitted will be','[{"text": "0.58 $eV$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "2.48 $eV$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "1.24 $eV$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "1.16 $eV$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Kinetic energy of the emitted photoelectron is given by photoelectric equation. ${K E=E_{i}-W_{o}}$<br>Here, ${E_{i}=}$ energy of incident radiation ${=\\dfrac{h c}{\\lambda}}$<br>${W_{o}=}$ work function<br>$KE = \\frac{{hc}}{\\lambda } - {W_o}$<br>$ = \\frac{{6.6 \\times {{10}^{ - 34}} \\times 3 \\times {{10}^8}}}{{5000 \\times {{10}^{ - 10}}}}$<br>$\\,\\,\\,\\, - 1.9 \\times 1.6 \\times {10^{ - 19}}\\,J$<br>$ = \\frac{{1.98 \\times {{10}^{ - 25}}}}{{5 \\times {{10}^{ - 7}}}} - 3.04 \\times {10^{ - 19}}\\,J$<br>$ = 3.96 \\times {10^{ - 19}} - 3.04 \\times {10^{ - 19}}\\,J$<br>$ = \\frac{{0.92 \\times {{10}^{ - 19}}}}{{1.6 \\times {{10}^{ - 19}}}}eV = 0.575eV$<br>$\\,\\,\\,\\, \\approx 0.58eV$.<br>So correct option is (1)',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45555,'physics',41,406,809,'The end product of decay $_{90}T{h^{232}}$ is $_{82}P{b^{208}}$. The number of  $\\alpha $ and  $\\beta $ particles emitted are respectively','[{"text": "$6,0$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$3,3$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$4,6$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$6,4$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'$_{90}T{h^{232}}{ \\to _{82}}P{b^{208}}$<br>Change in atomic mass $ = 232 - 208 = 24$<br>Number of $\\alpha $- particles emitted $ = 24/4 = 6$<br>Atomic number will decrease by $6 \\times 2 = 12$<br>Atomic number will be increase by 4 due to emission $4\\beta $ particles.<br>So, net decrease in atomic number $ = 12 - 4 = 8$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45556,'physics',40,241,480,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Smoky flame of Bunsen burner gives continuous spectrum whereas its blue flame gives band spectrum.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> The band spectrum consists of coloured bands of light on a dark background.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'When the flame of a Bunsen burner appears smoky, it indicates that there are carbon particles within it that are in an incandescent state. As a result, the flame emits (like grey body)a continuous spectrum of light. However, when the burner produces a blue flame, it signifies the presence of carbon and cyanogen, in a molecular state. This type of flame yields a band spectrum of light. So correct option is (2).',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45557,'physics',37,978,2282,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> An electric heater is heated first by direct and then by alternating currents. For both the currents, the potential difference across the ends of the heater is the same. The rate of production of heat will be different in two cases.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> The resistance of a coil in alternating curent will be more than the resistance of a coil in direct current.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Element of heater is coil with both $L$ and $R$.$\\Rightarrow Z=\\sqrt{R^{2}+L^{2} \\omega^{2}}$ will be more when $A C$ is used (compared to $R$ of $D C$ ). So correct option is (1).',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45559,'physics',41,406,809,'A radioactive nucleus emits $4\\alpha $- particles and $7\\beta $ - particles in succession. The ratio of number of neutrons of that of protons, is [$A = $ mass number, $Z = $ atomic number]','[{"text": "$\\\\frac{{A - Z - 13}}{{Z - 2}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{{A - Z - 15}}{{Z - 1}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{{A - Z - 13}}{{Z - 1}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{{A - Z - 11}}{{Z - 2}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Let us assume, a particle $X$ having atomic number $Z$ and mass number $A$ <br>when an $\\alpha $- particle is emitted by a nucleus, then its atomic number decreases by 2 and mass number decreases by 4. So, for given case<br>$_Z{X^A}{\\xrightarrow{{4\\alpha  - particle}}_{Z - 8}}{Y^{A - 16}}$<br>When an $\\beta $-particle is emitted by a nucleus its atomic number increases by one and mass number remains unchanged. So, for given case<br>$_{Z - 8}{Y^{A - 16}}{\\xrightarrow{{7\\beta  - particle}}_{Z - 1}}{Z^{A - 16}}$<br>$\\therefore \\frac{{{\\rm{Number}}\\,{\\rm{of}}\\,{\\rm{neutrons}}}}{{{\\rm{Number}}\\,{\\rm{of}}\\,{\\rm{protons}}}} = \\frac{{\\left( {A - 16} \\right) - \\left( {Z - 1} \\right)}}{{\\left( {Z - 1} \\right)}}$<br>$ = \\frac{{A - Z - 15}}{{Z - 1}}$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',2020,'MHTCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45560,'physics',37,977,2281,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> We use a thick wire in the secondary coil of a step down transformer to reduce the production of heat.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> When the plane of the armature is parallel to the line of force of magnetic field, the magnitude of induced $e.m.f$ is maximum.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'A step-down transformer converts electrical energy from a high voltage to one at a low voltage. (Subscript $p$ for primary and $s$ for secondary)<br>$\\Rightarrow V_{s} < V_{p}$<br>$\\Rightarrow I_{s}>I_{p}(\\because P=V I=$ constant $)$<br>In order to produce less heat in secondary, we shall use a wire of less resistance and so "thick wire" ($i.e.,$ $r$ high in $A=\\pi r^{2}$ of formule $\\left.R=\\rho \\dfrac{l}{A}\\right)$<br>radiation, $\\left(\\dfrac{d \\phi}{d t}\\right)$ will be maximum when plane of armature is parallel to lines of force of magnetic field. (and then $\\left|V_{\\text {ind }}\\right|$ in maximum then).Both are facts, but assertion is true for a different explanation. So correct option is (2).',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45561,'physics',40,239,478,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> In a hydrogen atom energy of emitted photon corresponding to transition from $n=2$ to $n=1$ is much greater as compared to transition from $n=\\infty$ to $n=2$.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Wavelength of photon is directly proportional to the energy of emitted photon.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'$E_{n_{1} \\rightarrow n_{2}}=-13.6\\left[\\dfrac{1}{n_{2}^{2}}-\\dfrac{1}{n_{1}^{2}}\\right]$;<br>For $n_{1}=2$ and $n_{2}=1$<br>${(\\Delta E)_{2.1}}{\\rm{ by }}{E_2} \\to {E_1} =  - 13.6 \\times \\frac{3}{4} =  - 10.2\\,eV$<br>${(\\Delta E)_{\\infty 2}}{\\rm{ by }}{E_\\infty } \\to {E_2} =  - (13.6)\\left[ {\\frac{1}{{{2^2}}} - \\frac{1}{{{\\infty ^2}}}} \\right]$<br>$ =  - 3.4\\,eV$<br>Numerically, $(\\Delta E)_{21}>(\\Delta E)_{\\infty 2}$<br>$\\Delta E=\\dfrac{h c}{\\lambda} \\Rightarrow \\Delta E \\propto \\dfrac{1}{\\lambda}$<br>$\\Rightarrow(\\Delta E)$ is not having direct proportionality with $\\lambda$. So correct option is (3).',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45562,'physics',39,1102,2629,'The work function for a metal is 4 $eV$ . To eject the photo electrons with zero velocity, the wavelength of incident light should be','[{"text": "2700 $ \\\\mathop A^{~~\\\\circ}  $", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "3125 $ \\\\mathop A^{~~\\\\circ}  $", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "1700 $ \\\\mathop A^{~~\\\\circ}  $", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "5900 $ \\\\mathop A^{~~\\\\circ}  $", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Kinetic energy of emitted photoelectron is<br>${K E=\\dfrac{h c}{\\lambda}-W_{o}}$<br>For zero speed of electron, ${K E=0}$<br>$ \\Rightarrow \\lambda  = \\frac{{hc}}{{{W_0}}} = \\frac{{6.62 \\times {{10}^{ - 34}} \\times 3 \\times {{10}^8}}}{{4 \\times 1.6 \\times {{10}^{ - 19}}}}$ <br>$\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, = 3.1031 \\times {10^{ - 7}}\\,m \\approx 3125$ $ \\mathop A^{~~\\circ}  $.<br>So correct option is (2)',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45563,'physics',41,406,809,'During a $\\beta $ decay','[{"text": "A neutron in the nucleus decays emitting \\tan electron", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "A proton in the nucleus decays emitting an  electron", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "An atomic electron is ejected", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "An electron which is already present within the nucleus is ejected", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'In  ${\\beta ^ - }$ decay<br>$_Z{X^A}{ \\to _{Z + 1}}{Y^A} + {e^ - } + \\overline v $<br>Thus a neutron in the nucleus decays into a proton and an electron along with the emission of an antineutrino',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45564,'physics',37,974,2273,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> No power loss is associated with pure capacitor in ac circuit.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> No current is flowing in this circuit.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'$P = {V_{rms}}{I_{rms}}\\cos \\phi  = 0$<br>$\\left(\\because\\right.$ Here $\\left.\\phi=90^{\\circ}\\right)$ But current is indeed flowing in circuit. So correct option is (3).',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45565,'physics',40,239,478,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> The electron in the hydrogen atom passes from energy level $n=4$ to the $n=1$ level. The maximum and minimum number of photons that can be emitted are six and one respectively.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> The photons are emitted when electron make a transition from the lower energy state to the higher energy state.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'The maximum number of photon emissions<br>$N_{\\text {max }}=\\dfrac{n(n-1)}{2}$<br>$=\\dfrac{4(4-1)}{2}=6$<br>Photons are emitted when electron have a transition from higher to lower state. The minimum is 1 for a direct jump. So correct option is (3).',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45567,'physics',41,406,809,'$\\beta $  decay means emission of electron from','[{"text": "Innermost electron orbit", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "A stable nucleus", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Outermost electron orbit", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Radioactive nucleus", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'In beta minus $\\left( {{\\beta ^ - }} \\right)$ decay, an electron is emitted by the radioactive nucleus, as in the decay<br>${}_{15}^{32}P \\to {}_{16}^{32}S{ + _{ - 1}}{e^0} + \\overline v $<br>where  $\\overline v $ is the antineutrino<br>In beta plus $\\left( {{\\beta ^ + }} \\right)$ decay, a positron is emitted by the radioactive nucleus, as in the decay <br>${}_{11}^{22}Na \\to {}_{10}^{22}Ne + {e^ + } + v$<br>where $v$ is the neutrino',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2009,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45568,'physics',37,977,2770,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Transformers are used only in alternating current source not in direct current.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Only A.C. can be stepped up or down by means of transformers.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Transformers are primarily used with $A C$ sources due to the changing magnetic field required for mutual induction, which is not present in $D C$ sources. So correct option is (1).',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45569,'physics',40,239,478,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Electrons in the atom are held due to coulomb forces.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> The atom is stable only because the centripetal force due to Coulomb\'s law is balanced by the centrifugal force.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Positive nucleus exhibits attractive coulomb force on electrons. Bohr\'s atomic model proposed electrons orbiting the nucleus without emitting radiation as long as they\'re in fixed (stationary) orbits. So correct option is (3).',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45570,'physics',39,1102,2626,'Wavelength of a 1 $KeV$ photon is ${1.24 \\times 10^{-9} {~m}}$. What is the frequency of 1 $KeV$ photon?','[{"text": "${2.4 \\\\times 10^{20} {~Hz}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${1.24 \\\\times 10^{15} {~Hz}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${1.24 \\\\times 10^{18} {~Hz}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${2.4 \\\\times 10^{23} {~Hz}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Energy of the photon is given by ${E=h v}$ <br>Here ${h=}$ Planck\'s constant<br>${v=}$ frequency of photon<br>${{v}=\\dfrac{E}{h}=\\dfrac{10^{6} {eV}}{6.6 \\times 10^{-34} {Js}}}$<br>${v=\\dfrac{10^{6} \\times 1.6 \\times 10^{-19}}{6.6 \\times 10^{-34}} {~Hz}}$<br>$v = 0.2424 \\times {10^{21}}\\,Hz = 2.4 \\times {10^{20}}\\,Hz$.<br>So correct option is (1)',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45571,'physics',41,404,803,'Mass numbers of the elements $A, B, C$ and $D$ are 30, 60, 90 and 120 respectively. The specific binding energy of them are 5 $MeV$, 8.5 $MeV$, 8 $MeV$ and 7 $MeV$ respectively. Then, in which 	of the following reaction/s energy is released?<br>$(a)\\,\\,D \\to 2B\\,\\,\\,\\,(b)\\,\\,C \\to B + A$<br>$(c){\\rm{ }}B \\to 2A$','[{"text": "Only in (a)", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "In (b), (c)", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "In (a), (c)", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "In (a), (b), (c)", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'The first reaction results in release of energy because $D \\to 2B$ is the only process in which total binding energy increases.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2012,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45572,'physics',37,977,2770,'Primary side of a transformer is connected to $230\\,\\,V,50\\,\\,Hz$ supply. Turns ratio of primary to secondary winding is $10: 1$. Load resistance connected to secondary side is $46\\,\\Omega .$ The power consumed in it is:','[{"text": "$10.0\\\\,\\\\,W$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$11.5\\\\,\\\\,W$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$12.5\\\\,\\\\,W$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$12.0\\\\,\\\\,W$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Induced voltage $\\propto$ Number of turns in winding.<br>$\\therefore \\dfrac{V_{2}}{V_{1}}=\\dfrac{N_{2}}{N_{1}}=\\dfrac{1}{10}$<br>${V_2} = \\frac{{{V_1}}}{{10}} = \\frac{{230}}{{10}} = 23\\,\\,V$<img src=\'/img/data/physics/37_45572_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' /> $P_{L}=\\dfrac{V_{2}^{2}}{R_{L}}=\\dfrac{(23)^{2}}{46}$<br>${P_L} = 11.5\\,\\,W$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',2024,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45573,'physics',41,404,803,'The binding energy per nucleon of  ${}_3^7Li$ and ${}_2^4He$ 	nuclei are 5.60 $MeV$ and 7.06 $MeV$,  respectively. In the nuclear reaction ${}_3^7Li + {}_1^1H \\to 2{}_2^4He + Q,$ the value of energy  $Q$ released is','[{"text": "$19.6\\\\,MeV$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$ - 2.4\\\\,MeV$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$8.4\\\\,MeV$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$17.3\\\\,MeV$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'The binding energy for  ${}_1{H^1}$ is taken as zero since it has only one nucleon <br>${}_3^7Li + {}_1^1H \\to {}_2^4He + Q$<br>$Q = 2(4 \\times 7.06) - (7 \\times 5.60)$<br>$ = (8 \\times 7.06) - (7 \\times 5.60)$<br>$Q = 17.28\\,MeV = 17.3\\,MeV$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45574,'physics',39,1102,2626,'If mean wavelength of light radiated by 100 $W$ lamp is 5000 $ \\mathop A^{~~\\circ}  $, the number of photons radiated per second would be','[{"text": "${2.5 \\\\times 10^{20}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${2.5 \\\\times 10^{22}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${3 \\\\times 10^{23}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${5 \\\\times 10^{17}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Power of the lamp ${=100 {~W}}$<br>Energy radiated by the lamp in one second ${=100 {~J}}$<br> Energy of the emitted light with wavelength ${\\lambda}$,<br>$\\begin{align*}& E=\\dfrac{h c}{\\lambda} \\\\& E=\\dfrac{6.6 \\times 10^{-34} \\times 3 \\times 10^{8}}{5000 \\times 10^{-10}}=3.96 \\times 10^{-19} {~J}\\end{align*}$<br>Number of photons emitted ${=\\dfrac{100 {~J}}{3.96 \\times 10^{-19} {~J}}}$<br>${=0.2525 \\times 10^{21}=2.5 \\times 10^{20}}$.<br>So correct option is (1)',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45584,'physics',41,403,801,'If the nucleus ${}_{13}^{27}Al$ has a nuclear radius of about $3.6\\,fm$, then ${}_{52}^{125}Te$ would have its radius approximately as','[{"text": "$6.0\\\\,fm$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$12.0\\\\,fm$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$9.6\\\\,fm$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$4.8\\\\,fm$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'$R = {R_0}{A^{1/3}}$<br>Therefore, $\\frac{{{R_{Al}}}}{{{R_{Te}}}} = \\frac{{{R_0}{{({A_{Al}})}^{1/3}}}}{{{R_0}{{({A_{Te}})}^{1/3}}}}$<br>$\\frac{{{R_{Al}}}}{{{R_{Te}}}} = \\frac{{{{({A_{Al}})}^{1/3}}}}{{{{({A_{Te}})}^{1/3}}}} = \\frac{{{{(27)}^{1/3}}}}{{{{(125)}^{1/3}}}} = \\frac{3}{5}$<br>$ \\Rightarrow {R_{Te}} = \\frac{5}{3} \\times {R_{Al}} = \\frac{5}{3} \\times 3.6 = 6\\,fm$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45576,'physics',40,238,477,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> The positively charged nucleus of an atom has a radius of order of ${10^{ - 15}}\\,m$.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> In $\\alpha$-particle scattering experiment, the distance of closest approach for $\\alpha$-particle $ \\approx {10^{ - 15}}\\,m$.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'These length-scales indicate short-range of nuclear forces. In Rutherford\'s alpha particle scattering experiment, a small number of alpha particles were observed to scatter back through large angles, even approaching 180 degrees. Such large-angle scattering indicated that the nucleus at centre of atom possessed a high positive charge. So correct option is (1).',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45577,'physics',41,403,801,'The radius of  $_{29}C{u^{64}}$ nucleus in Fermi is (given ${R_0} = 1.2 \\times {10^{ - 15}}m$)','[{"text": "$4.8$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$1.2$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$7.7$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$9.6$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Nuclear radius $R = {R_0}{A^{1/3}}$<br>Where  ${R_0}\\left( { = 1.2 \\times {{10}^{ - 15}}m} \\right)$ is a constant and $A$ is the mass number <br>${R_{Cu}} = \\left( {1.2 \\times {{10}^{ - 15}}m} \\right){\\left( {64} \\right)^{1/3}}$<br>$ = \\left( {1.2 \\times {{10}^{ - 15}}m} \\right){\\left( {{4^3}} \\right)^{1/3}}$<br>$ = 4.8 \\times {10^{ - 15}}m = 4.8fm$$\\left( {\\because 1fm = {{10}^{ - 15}}m = 1\\,{\\text{Fermi}}} \\right)$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2012,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45578,'physics',39,1102,2628,'The slope of graph between frequency of incident light and stopping potential for a given surface will be','[{"text": "${h}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$eh$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\dfrac{h}{e}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${e}$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'K.E. of a emitted photoelectron when incident with a radiation of frequency ${v}$ is <br>$KE = hv - {W_o} \\Rightarrow e{V_o} = hv - {W_o}({V_o} = $ stopping potential) <br>$ \\Rightarrow {V_o} = \\left( {\\frac{h}{e}} \\right)v - {W_o}$<br>This is the equation of a straight line with slope $\\left( {\\frac{h}{e}} \\right)$.<br>So correct option is (3)',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45579,'physics',37,975,2275,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> In series $L C R$ circuit resonance can take place.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Resonance takes place if inductance and capacitive reactances are equal and opposite.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Resonance occurs when the inductive reactance $\\left(X_{L}\\right)$ and capacitive reactance $\\left(X_{C}\\right)$ are equal in magnitude and opposite in phase, resulting in the impedance being purely resistive. At this point, the circuit allows for maximum current flow and is in a state of resonance. Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion. So, correct option is (1).',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45580,'physics',40,238,477,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> In experiment by Rutherford used $\\alpha$-particles for scattering.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> The force of repulsion between atomic nucleus and $\\alpha$-particle varies with distance according to inverse square law.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Rutherford used $\\alpha$-particles for scattering experiments. Rutherford confirmed that the repulsive force on $\\alpha$-particle due to nucleus varies with distance as per inverse square law and so positive charges are concentrated at the centre (not distributed throughout the atom). So correct option is (2).',NULL,'Easy','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45581,'physics',41,405,805,'Which graph in the following diagram correctly represents the potential energy of a pair of nucleons as a function of their separation?','[{"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Potential energy is negative when the nucleons are far a part. When they come close  $P.E$ value increases. $P.E$ is also minimum at a critical separation and it is measured in $MeV$.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2021,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45582,'physics',39,1103,2630,'A photocell is illuminated by a small bright source placed 1$m$ away. When the same source is placed ${\\dfrac{1}{2} {~m}}$ away, the number of electrons emitted would','[{"text": "increase by a factor of 2", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "increase by a factor of 4", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "increase by a factor of 8", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "increase by a factor of 16", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Number of electrons emitted is inversely proportional to the square of distance, $i.e.,$ ${n \\propto \\dfrac{1}{r^{2}}}$.<br>$\\therefore \\,\\,\\,{n_1} \\propto \\frac{1}{{{{(1)}^2}}}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,(1)$<br>Also, ${n_2} \\propto \\frac{1}{{{{\\left( {\\frac{1}{2}} \\right)}^2}}}{\\mkern 1mu} \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,(2)$<br>Dividing eqs. (1) and (2), we get <br>${\\dfrac{n_{1}}{n_{2}}=\\dfrac{1}{4} \\Rightarrow n_{2}=4 n_{1}}$.<br>So correct option is (2)',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45583,'physics',37,977,2281,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> A transformer cannot work on $D C$ supply.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> $D C$ changes neither in magnitude nor in direction.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Transformers operate on the principle of electromagnetic induction, where a changing magnetic field in the primary coil induces a voltage in the secondary coil. In alternating current $(A C)$, the current regularly changes direction and magnitude, creating the necessary changing magnetic field for transformer action. However, direct current $(D C)$ remains constant in both magnitude and direction. So, \'Reason\' accounts for \'Assertion\'. So correct option is (1).',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45586,'physics',39,1102,3092,'A photon of wavelength 6630 $ \\mathop A^{~~\\circ}  $ is incident on a totally reflecting surface. The momentum delivered by the photon is equal to','[{"text": "${6.63 \\\\times 10^{-27} {~kg} {~m} / {s}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${2 \\\\times 10^{-27} {~kg} {~m} / {s}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${3.33 \\\\times 10^{-27} {~kg} {~m} / {s}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${10^{-27} {~kg} {~m} / {s}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Momentum of the photon is<br>${\\Rightarrow p=\\dfrac{h}{\\lambda}}$<br>Here ${h=}$ Planck\'s constant<br>Momentum delivered by the photon<br>${=2 p=\\dfrac{2 \\times 6.63 \\times 10^{-34}}{6630 \\times 10^{-10}}=2 \\times 10^{-27} {~kg} {~m} / {s}}$.<br>So correct option is (2)',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45588,'physics',41,404,803,'A nucleus with mass number 240 breaks into two fragments each of mass number 120, the binding energy per nucleon of unfragmented nuclei is 7.6 $MeV$ while that of fragmentsis 8.5 $MeV$. The total gain in the Binding Energy in the process is','[{"text": "$9.4\\\\,MeV$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$804\\\\,MeV$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$216\\\\,MeV$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$0.9\\\\,MeV$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'${X^{240}} \\to 2{Y^{120}} + Q$<br>$Q = B{E_P} - B{E_R}$<br>$ = 240\\left( {8.5} \\right) - 240 \\times 7.6\\,MeV$<br>$ = 240 \\times 0.9\\,MeV = 216\\,MeV$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2021,'NEET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45589,'physics',39,1104,2631,'Electron behaves as wave because they can be','[{"text": "deflected by an electric field", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "deflected by magnetic field", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "diffracted by a crystal", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "they ionize a gas", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Electrons show the diffraction phenomenon therefore they can behave as a wave also.<br>So correct option is (3)',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45590,'physics',37,975,2278,'Read the Statement - A and Statement - B carefully to mark the correct options given below:<div class="matching-question"><div class="column-left">When the frequency of an a.c. source in a series $L C R$ circuit increases, the current in the circuit first increases, attains a maximum value and then decreases.</div><div class="column-right">In a series $L C R$ circuit, the value of power factor at resonance is one.</div></div>','[{"text": "Statement A is incorrect but Statement B is correct.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Statement A and Statement B are correct.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Statement A is correct but Statement B is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Statement A and Statement B are incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'The power factor at resonance is one, hence statement -B is true. When frequency increases, current in the circuit first increases, then attains a maximum value and then decreases. <img src=\'/img/data/physics/37_45590_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' /> Thus, As suggested by the graph<br>Statement $A$ is correct.<br>$Z=\\sqrt{R^{2}+\\left(X_{L}-X_{C}\\right)^{2}} ; I=\\dfrac{V}{Z}$<br>So, when impedance decreases current increases.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ type-2',2023,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45591,'physics',40,239,478,'Two $H$ atoms in the ground state collide in elastically. The maximum amount by which their combined kinetic energy is reduced is:','[{"text": "$10.20\\\\,eV$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$20.40\\\\,eV$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$13.6\\\\,eV$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$27.2\\\\,eV$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'The total energy associated with the two $H$-atoms in the ground state collide in elastically. $ = 2 \\times (13.6\\,eV) = 27.2\\,eV$<br>The maximum amount by which their combined kinetic energy is reduced when any of them goes into first excited state after the inelastic collision.<br>The total energy associated with the two $H$ atoms after the collision.<br>$ = \\left( {\\frac{{13.6}}{{{2^2}}}} \\right) + (13.6) = 17.0\\,eV$<br>Therefore, maximum loss of their combined kinetic energy $ = 27.2 - 17.0 = 10.2\\,eV.$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45592,'physics',41,407,811,'In the given nuclear reaction, the element $X $ is $_{11}^{22}Na \\to X + {e^ + } + v$','[{"text": "$_{10}^{23}Ne$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$_{10}^{22}Ne$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$_{12}^{22}Mg$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$_{11}^{23}Na$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'$_{11}^{22}Na \\to X + {e^ + } + v$<br>This is  ${\\beta ^ + } - $ decay<br>(In this, $A$ remains same while $Z$ decreases by 1)<br>$_{11}^{22}Na \\to _{10}^{22}Ne + {e^ + } + v$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2022,'NEET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45593,'physics',37,975,2276,'Read the Statement - A and Statement - B carefully to mark the correct options given below:<div class="matching-question"><div class="column-left">An AC circuit undergoes electrical resonance if it contains either a capacitor or an inductor.</div><div class="column-right">An AC circuit containing a pure capacitor or a pure inductor consumes high power due to its non-zero power factor.</div></div>','[{"text": "Both Statement A and Statement B are incorrect.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Statement A is correct but Statement B is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Statement A is incorrect but Statement B is correct.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Statement A and Statement B are correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Electrical resonance phenomenon is exhibited by a circuit, only when voltage across inductor $L$ and capacitor $C$ will cancel out each other (as, they are out of phase).<br>The power consumed by pure capacitor and pure inductor is zero.<br> As, voltage and current in both the cases are out of phase with each other by $90^{\\circ}$. Therefore, the power factor of both is zero and they have no internal resistance.<br>Hence, both statement A and statement B are false.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ type-2',2023,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45594,'physics',39,1104,2631,'For a particle of mass ${m}$ moving with kinetic energy ${E}$, the de Broglie wavelength is','[{"text": "${h / 2 m E}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${h \\\\sqrt{2 m E}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${h / \\\\sqrt{2 m E}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${h \\\\sqrt{2} / m E}$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'de-Broglie wavelength ${\\lambda=\\dfrac{h}{p}}$<br>Also, ${E=\\dfrac{p^{2}}{2 m} \\Rightarrow p=\\sqrt{2 m E}}$<br>${\\therefore \\lambda=\\dfrac{h}{\\sqrt{2 m E}}}$ ${(E=}$ kinetic energy).<br>So correct option is (3)',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45606,'physics',40,240,479,'If an electron in hydrogen atom jumps from an orbit of level $n=3$ to an orbit of level $n=2$ then emitted radiation has a frequency:','[{"text": "$\\\\dfrac{R c}{9}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{R c}{25}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{8 R c}{9}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{5 R c}{36}$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'$v = \\frac{c}{\\lambda }\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\left( 1 \\right)$<br>$\\dfrac{1}{\\lambda}=(Z)^{2} R\\left[\\dfrac{1}{n_{1}^{2}}-\\dfrac{1}{n_{2}^{2}}\\right]$<br>$\\dfrac{1}{\\lambda}=(1)^{2} R\\left[\\dfrac{1}{9}-\\dfrac{1}{4}\\right]$<br>$\\dfrac{1}{\\lambda}=R\\left[\\dfrac{4-9}{36}\\right]$<br>$\\dfrac{1}{\\lambda}=R\\left[\\dfrac{-5}{36}\\right]$<br>By putting the value of $\\dfrac{1}{\\lambda}$ in eq. (1)<br>$v=\\dfrac{-5 R c}{36}[-v e$ sign indicates emission $]$.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45595,'physics',40,239,478,'An electron is in an excited state in a hydrogen like atom. It has a total energy $ - 34\\,eV$. The kinetic energy of the electron is $E$ and its de-Broglie wavelength is $\\lambda$.<br>Then: (Take ${{m_e} = 9.1 \\times {{10}^{ - 31}}\\,kg}$)','[{"text": "$E = 6.8eV,\\\\lambda  = 6.6 \\\\times {10^{ - 10}}\\\\,m$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$E = 3.4eV,\\\\lambda  = 6.6 \\\\times {10^{ - 10}}\\\\,m$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$E = 3.4eV,\\\\lambda  = 6.6 \\\\times {10^{ - 11}}\\\\,m$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$E = 6.8eV,\\\\lambda  = 6.6 \\\\times {10^{ - 11}}\\\\,m$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'$P \\cdot E=-2 \\times K . E$<br>$=-2 E$<br>$\\therefore$ Total energy $ =  - 2E + E =  - E =  - 3.4\\,eV$.<br>$\\therefore E = 3.4\\,eV \\Rightarrow E = 3.4 \\times 1.6 \\times {10^{ - 19}}\\,J$<br>Let $P=$ momentum and $m=$ mass of the electron.<br>$\\therefore E = \\frac{{{P^2}}}{{2m}}{\\rm{ or }}P = \\sqrt {2mE} $<br>$\\lambda  = \\frac{h}{P} = \\frac{h}{{\\sqrt {2mE} }}$<br>$ = \\frac{{6.6 \\times {{10}^{ - 34}}}}{{\\sqrt {2 \\times 9.1 \\times {{10}^{ - 31}} \\times 0.34 \\times 1.6 \\times {{10}^{ - 19}}} }}$<br>$\\lambda  = 6.6 \\times {10^{ - 10}}\\,m.$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45596,'physics',41,403,801,'A nucleus of mass number 189 splits into two nuclei having mass numbers 125 and 64. The ratio of radius of two daughter nuclei respectively is','[{"text": "$4:5$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$5:4$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$25:16$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1:1$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Nuclear radius:<br>$R = {R_0}{\\left( A \\right)^{1/3}}$<br>$\\frac{{R\\left( {125} \\right)}}{{R\\left( {64} \\right)}} = \\frac{{{R_0}{{\\left( {125} \\right)}^{1/3}}}}{{{R_0}{{\\left( {64} \\right)}^{1/3}}}} = \\frac{5}{4}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2022,'NEET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45597,'physics',37,976,2280,'In an $L C$ oscillator, if values of inductance and capacitance become twice and eight times, respectively, then the resonant frequency of oscillator becomes $x$ times its initial resonant frequency $\\omega_{0}$. The value of $x$ is','[{"text": "$1 / 4$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "16", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1 / 16$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "4", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Here, $L_{1}=L, C_{1}=C$,<br>Resonant frequency, ${\\omega _0} = \\frac{1}{{\\sqrt {LC} }}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\left( 1 \\right)$<br>Now, $L_{2}=2 L, C_{2}=8 C$<br>So, $\\omega_{2}=\\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{2 L \\times 8 C}}=\\dfrac{1}{4} \\times \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{L C}}=\\dfrac{\\omega_{0}}{4}$<br>$\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{\\rm{(from (1))}}\\,$<br>So, $x=1 / 4$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2023,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45598,'physics',39,1104,2631,'If we consider electrons and protons of the same wavelength, they will have the same','[{"text": "velocity", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "energy", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "momentum", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "angular momentum", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'The momentum of particle is given by ${p=\\dfrac{h}{\\lambda}}$. Since ${\\lambda}$ is same for both the electrons and protons, the value of ${p}$ for both is the same.<br>So correct option is (3)',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45599,'physics',41,406,806,'The half-life of a radioactive nuclide is 100 hours. The fraction of original activity that will remain after150 hours would be','[{"text": "$\\\\frac{1}{{2\\\\sqrt 2 }}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{2}{3}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{2}{{3\\\\sqrt 2 }}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1/2$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Rate of decay is<br>$A = {A_0}{e^{ - \\lambda t}}$<br>$\\frac{A}{{{A_0}}} = {e^{ - \\lambda \\left( {150} \\right)}}$<br>$ = {e^{ - \\,\\,\\frac{{\\ell n2}}{{{t_{1/2}}}}\\left( {150} \\right)}} = {e^{ - \\,\\frac{{\\ell n2}}{{100}} \\times 150}}$<br>$ = {e^{ - \\,\\frac{3}{2}\\ell n2}} = {e^{\\ell n{{\\left( {{2^{3/2}}} \\right)}^{ - 1}}}}$<br>$ = {\\left( {{2^{3/2}}} \\right)^{ - 1}} = {\\left( {2\\sqrt 2 } \\right)^{ - 1}} = \\frac{1}{{2\\sqrt 2 }}$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2021,'NEET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45600,'physics',40,239,478,'An electron in a hydrogen atom makes a transition from $n=n_{1}$ to $n=n_{2}$. The time period of the electron in the initial state is eight times that in the final state. The possible values of $n_{1}$ and $n_{2}$ are:','[{"text": "$n_{1}=4, n_{2}=2$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$n_{1}=8, n_{2}=2$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$n_{1}=8, n_{2}=1$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$n_{1}=6, n_{2}=2$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'In the $n^{\\text {th }}$ orbit, let $r_{n}=$ radius and<br>$v_{n}=$ speed of electron.<br>Time period, $T_{n}=\\dfrac{2 \\pi r_{n}}{V_{n}} \\propto \\dfrac{r_{n}}{V_{n}}$<br>Now, $r_{n} \\propto n^{2}$ and $V_{n} \\propto \\dfrac{1}{n}$<br>$\\therefore \\dfrac{r_{n}}{V_{n}} \\propto n^{3}$ or $T_{n} \\propto n^{3}$<br>$8=\\left(\\dfrac{n_{1}}{n_{2}}\\right)^{3} \\Rightarrow \\dfrac{n_{1}}{n_{2}}=2 \\Rightarrow n_{1}=2 n_{2}$<br>$\\begin{aligned}\\Rightarrow n_{1} & =4 \\\\n_{2} & =2\\end{aligned}$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45602,'physics',39,1104,2631,'The momentum of a proton is ${2.5 \\times 10^{-29} {~kg}-{m} / {s}}$. Its frequency will be','[{"text": "${1.14 \\\\times 10^{13} {~Hz}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${3.5 \\\\times 10^{3} {~Hz}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${6.8 \\\\times 10^{3} {~Hz}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${1.04 \\\\times 10^{12} {~Hz}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Momentum ( ${p}$ ) of the proton is<br>$2.5 \\times {10^{ - 29}}\\,kg\\,m/s$<br>Wavelength ${(\\lambda)}$ of the proton will be ${\\lambda=\\dfrac{h}{p}}$<br>Here, ${h=}$ Planck\'s constant ${=6.6 \\times 10^{-34} {~J} {~s}}$<br>${\\lambda=\\dfrac{6.6 \\times 10^{-34}}{2.5 \\times 10^{-29}} {~m}=2.64 \\times 10^{-5} {~m}}$<br>Now, frequency ${(v)}$ of the proton will be ${v=\\dfrac{c}{\\lambda}=\\dfrac{3 \\times 10^{8}}{2.64 \\times 10^{-5}}=1.14 \\times 10^{13} {~Hz}}$.<br>So correct option is (1)',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45603,'physics',41,408,812,'When a uranium isotope ${}_{92}^{235}U$ is bombarded witha neutron, ,it generates ${}_{36}^{89}Kr$, three neutrons and:','[{"text": "${}_{40}^{91}Zr$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${}_{36}^{101}Kr$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${}_{36}^{103}Kr$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${}_{56}^{144}Ba$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'$_{{\\rm{92}}}^{235}U + _0^1n \\to _{36}^{89}Kr + _{56}^{144}Ba + 3_{{}^0}^1n + Q$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2020,'NEET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45605,'physics',39,1104,2631,'The kinetic energy of an electron is 5 $eV$ . The de Broglie wavelength associated with it${\\left(h=6.6 \\times 10^{-34} {~J} {~s}, m_{e}=9.1 \\times 10^{-31} {~kg}\\right)}$ is','[{"text": "5.47 $ \\\\mathop A^{~~\\\\circ}  $", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "10.9 $ \\\\mathop A^{~~\\\\circ}  $", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "2.7 $ \\\\mathop A^{~~\\\\circ}  $", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "none of these", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Kinetic energy, ${E=5 {eV}}$<br>${\\therefore \\lambda=h / \\sqrt{2 m E}}$<br>$ = \\frac{{6.63 \\times {{10}^{ - 34}}}}{{\\sqrt {2 \\times 9.1 \\times {{10}^{ - 31}} \\times 5 \\times 1.6 \\times {{10}^{ - 19}}} }} = 5.47$$ \\mathop A^{~~\\circ}  $.<br>So correct option is (1)',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45607,'physics',41,406,806,'A radioactive nucleus  ${}_Z^AX$ undergoes spontaneous decay in the sequence  $_Z^AX{ \\to _{Z - 1}}B{ \\to _{Z - 3}}C{ \\to _{Z - 2}}D,$ where $Z$ is the atomic number of element $X$. The possible decay particles in the sequence are:','[{"text": "$\\\\alpha ,{\\\\beta ^ + },{\\\\beta ^ - }$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\beta ^ + },\\\\alpha ,{\\\\beta ^ - }$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${\\\\beta ^ - },\\\\alpha ,{\\\\beta ^ + }$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\alpha ,{\\\\beta ^ - },{\\\\beta ^ + }$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'$_Z{X^A}{\\xrightarrow[{ - {\\beta ^ + }}]{}_{Z - 1}}B{\\xrightarrow[{ - \\alpha }]{}_{Z - 3}}C{\\xrightarrow[{ - {\\beta ^{ - 1}}}]{}_{Z - 2}}D$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2021,'NEET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45608,'physics',37,973,2268,'For the given figures, choose the correct options','[{"text": "The rms current in circuit figure (ii) can never be larger than that in (i).", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "At resonance, current in figure (ii) is less than that in (i).", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "The rms current in figure (i) is always equal to that in (ii).", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "The rms current in circuit (ii) can be larger than that in (i).", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'<img src=\'/img/data/physics/37_45608_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' /> ${I_{rms}} = \\frac{{220}}{{40}} = 5.5\\,A{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,[{\\rm{In}}\\,\\,{\\rm{Fg}}\\,\\,{\\rm{ - }}\\,\\,{\\rm{i}}]$<br>$X_{C}=\\dfrac{1 \\times 10^{6}}{2 \\times \\pi \\times 50 \\times 0.5}=6369.4 \\Omega$<br>$X_{L}=2 \\times \\pi \\times 50 \\times 50 \\times 10^{-3}=15.7 \\Omega$<br>$Z=\\sqrt{R^{2}+\\left(X_{L}-X_{C}\\right)^{2}}$<br>$\\Rightarrow Z=\\sqrt{40^{2}+(6369.4-15.7)^{2}}$<br>$Z=6353.8 \\Omega$<br>${I_{rms}} = \\frac{{220}}{{6353.8}} = 0.03\\,A\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{\\mkern 1mu} [{\\rm{In}}\\,\\,{\\rm{Fg}}\\,\\,{\\rm{ - }}\\,\\,{\\rm{ii}}]$<br>At, resonance, $I = \\frac{{220}}{{40}} = 5.5\\,A$<br>So $\\left(I_{r m s}\\right)_{\\max }$ in Fg-(ii) is $5.5\\,A$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2023,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/37_45608_question_1.png'),
(45609,'physics',39,1104,2631,'If the kinetic energy of a free electron doubles, its de-Broglie wavelength changes by a factor of','[{"text": "2", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\dfrac{1}{2}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\sqrt{2}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\dfrac{1}{\\\\sqrt{2}}}$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'If kinetic energy of free electron ${K^{\\prime} \\rightarrow 2 K}$ (initial K.E.) then de-Broglie wavelength changes by<br>$\\lambda^{\\prime}=\\dfrac{h}{\\sqrt{2 m K^{\\prime}}}=\\dfrac{h}{\\sqrt{2 m 2 K}}=\\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\lambda$.<br>So correct option is (4)',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45610,'physics',40,239,478,'A set of atoms in an excited state decays:','[{"text": "In general to any of the states with lower energy.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Into a lower state only when excited by an external electric field.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "All together simultaneously into a lower state.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "To emit photons only when they collide.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'A set of atoms in an excited state decays in general to any of the states with lower energy.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45611,'physics',41,406,809,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> An $\\alpha $-particle is emitted when uranium 238 decays into thorium.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> The decay of uranium 238 to thorium is represented by $_{92}^{238}U \\to \\,_{90}^{234}Th + \\,_2^4He$. The helium nuclei is called an alpha particles.','[{"text": "Both assertion and reason are correct and reason is the correct explanation of assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both assertion and reason are correct but reason is not the correct explanation of assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Option (1) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45613,'physics',39,1104,2631,'The de-Broglie wavelength of an electron in the first orbit of Bohr\'s hydrogen is equal to','[{"text": "radius of orbit", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "perimeter of orbit", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "diameter of orbit", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "half the perimeter of orbit", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'de-Broglie wavelength ${(\\lambda)}$ of an electron is given as $\\lambda  = \\frac{h}{p} = \\frac{h}{{mv}}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,(1)$<br>Also, from Bohr\'s quantization rule, the angular momentum is <br>${L=m v r=\\dfrac{n h}{2 \\pi}}$<br>For first Bohr\'s orbit ${(n=1), \\lambda=2 \\pi r}$ i.e., equal to the perimeter of orbit.<br>So correct option is (2)',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45614,'physics',40,239,478,'The relation between the orbit radius and the electron velocity for a dynamically stable orbits in a hydrogen atom is (where all notations have their usual meanings):','[{"text": "$v=\\\\sqrt{\\\\dfrac{4 \\\\pi \\\\varepsilon_{0}}{m e^{2} e}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$r=\\\\sqrt{\\\\dfrac{e^{2}}{4 \\\\pi \\\\varepsilon_{0} v}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$v=\\\\sqrt{\\\\dfrac{e^{2}}{4 \\\\pi \\\\varepsilon_{0} m r}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$r=\\\\sqrt{\\\\dfrac{v e^{2}}{4 \\\\pi \\\\varepsilon_{0} m}}$.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Centripetal force is provided by the electrostatic force of attraction between the revolving electrons and the nucleus in an atom.<br>$F_{e}=F_{c}$<br>$\\therefore \\dfrac{m v^{2}}{r}=\\dfrac{e^{2}}{4 \\pi \\varepsilon_{0} r^{2}}$<br>or $v^{2}=\\dfrac{e^{2}}{4 \\pi \\varepsilon_{0} r m} \\Rightarrow v=\\dfrac{e}{\\sqrt{4 \\pi \\varepsilon_{0} m r}}$.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45615,'physics',41,406,806,'The half-life of a radioactive substance is 30 minutes. The time (in minutes) taken between 40% decay and 85% decay of the same radioactive substance is','[{"text": "$15$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$30$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$45$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$60$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Given that ${t_{_{\\frac{1}{2}}}} = 30\\min $<br>$\\therefore \\lambda  = \\frac{{\\ln 2}}{{30}}$<br>$0.6{N_o} = {N_o}{e^{ - \\lambda {t_1}}},0.15{N_o} = {N_o}{e^{ - \\lambda {t_2}}}$<br>$4 = {e^{\\lambda \\left( {{t_2} - {t_1}} \\right)}}$<br>$\\therefore 2\\ln 2 = \\lambda \\left( {{t_2} - {t_1}} \\right) \\Rightarrow ({t_2} - {t_1}) = 60\\min $',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2016,'NEET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45616,'physics',37,973,2268,'In the given circuit, rms value of current $\\left(I_{r m s}\\right)$ through the resistor $R$ is','[{"text": "$20\\\\,A$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{1}{2}\\\\,A$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2\\\\,A$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$2\\\\sqrt 2 \\\\,A$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'As, $Z=\\sqrt{R^{2}+\\left(X_{L}-X_{C}\\right)^{2}}$<br>$Z=\\sqrt{100+\\left(200^{2}-100\\right)^{2}}=100 \\sqrt{2} \\Omega$<br>${I_{rms}} = \\frac{{{V_{rms}}}}{Z} = \\frac{{200\\sqrt 2 V}}{{100\\sqrt 2 }} = 2\\,A$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2023,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/37_45616_question_1.png'),
(45618,'physics',41,406,806,'For a radioactive material, half-life is 10 minutes. If initially there are 600 number of nuclei, the time taken (in minutes) for the disintegration of 450 nuclei','[{"text": "$30$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$15$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$10$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$20$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'$\\lambda  = \\frac{{0.693}}{{10}} = 0.0693$<br>Now, $N = {N_0}{e^{ - \\lambda t}}$<br>$600 - 450 = 600\\,{e^{ - 0.0693\\,t}}$<br>$150 = 600\\,{e^{ - 0.0693\\,t}}$<br>$\\ln 4 = 0.0693\\,t$<br>$t = \\frac{{1.3863}}{{0.0693}} = 20\\min $',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2018,'NEET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45621,'physics',40,240,479,'The following diagram indicates the energy levels of a certain atom when the system moves from $2\\,E$ level to $E$, a photon of wavelength $\\lambda$ is emitted. The wavelength of photon produced during its transistion from $\\dfrac{4 E}{3}$ level to $E$ is:','[{"text": "$\\\\dfrac{\\\\lambda}{3}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{3 \\\\lambda}{4}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{4 \\\\lambda}{3}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$3 \\\\lambda$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'$2 E-E=\\dfrac{h c}{\\lambda} \\Rightarrow E=\\dfrac{h c}{\\lambda}$<br>$\\begin{aligned}& \\dfrac{4 E}{3}-3=\\dfrac{h c}{\\lambda^{\\prime}} \\Rightarrow \\dfrac{E}{3}=\\dfrac{h c}{\\lambda^{\\prime}} \\\\& \\therefore \\dfrac{\\lambda^{\\prime}}{\\lambda}=3 \\Rightarrow \\lambda^{\\prime}=3 \\lambda .\\end{aligned}$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/40_45621_question_1.png'),
(45622,'physics',41,403,800,'$\\alpha $-particle consists of :','[{"text": "2 protons and 2 neutrons only", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "2 electrons, 2 protons and 2 neutrons", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "2 electrons and 4 protons only", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "2 protons only", "isCorrect": false}]',0,NULL,NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2019,'NEET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45623,'physics',37,973,2264,'A capacitor of capacitance $150.0 \\mu F$ is connected to an alternating source of emf given by $E=36 \\sin (120 \\pi t) V$. The maximum value of current in the circuit is approximately equal to','[{"text": "$2\\\\sqrt 2 \\\\,A$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2\\\\,A$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{1}{\\\\sqrt{2}} A$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\sqrt{2} A$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Given : Capacitance, $C=150 \\mu F$,<br>$E=36 \\sin (120 \\pi t) V$<br>$\\therefore i=\\dfrac{E}{X_{C}}$ and $X_{C}=\\dfrac{1}{\\omega c}$<br>$\\Rightarrow i=\\omega C E_{m} \\cos (\\omega t)$<br>For maximum $i, \\cos \\omega t=1$<br>${i_{\\max }} = \\omega C{E_m} = 120\\pi  \\times 150 \\times {10^{ - 6}} \\times 36\\,A$<br>$i = 2.03\\,A$ <img src=\'/img/data/physics/37_45623_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2023,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45625,'physics',40,239,478,'What is the angular momentum of an electron in an orbit of hydrogen atom if its energy is $ - 0.544\\,eV$.','[{"text": "$\\\\dfrac{h}{\\\\pi}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{2 h}{\\\\pi}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{5 h}{2 \\\\pi}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{7 h}{2 \\\\pi}$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'By using $E=-\\dfrac{13.6}{n^{2}} \\mathrm{eV} \\quad$ (for $H_{2}$ atom)<br>$\\Rightarrow-0.544=-\\dfrac{13.6}{n^{2}} \\Rightarrow n^{2}=25 \\quad n=5$<br>$\\therefore$ Angular momentum $=n \\dfrac{h}{2 \\pi}=\\dfrac{5 h}{2 \\pi}$.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45626,'physics',41,404,802,'The energy equivalent of $0.5\\,g$ of a substance is:','[{"text": "$4.5 \\\\times {10^{13}}J$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$1.5 \\\\times {10^{13}}J$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$0.5 \\\\times {10^{13}}J$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$3.5 \\\\times {10^{13}}J$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'$E = m{c^2}$<br>$ = 0.5 \\times {10^{ - 3}} \\times 9 \\times {10^{16}}$<br>$ = 4.5 \\times {10^{13}}J$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2020,'NEET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45629,'physics',41,406,810,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Cobalt -60 is useful in cancer therapy.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Cobalt-60 is source of $\\gamma $-radiations capable of killing cancerous cell.','[{"text": "Both assertion and reason are correct and reason is the correct explanation of assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both assertion and reason are correct but reason is not the correct explanation of assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Cobalt-60 is a source of $\\gamma $-radiations. These radiation s kill cancer cells more rapidly than others because cancer cells are undifferentiated. However, healthy cells in vicinty of cancer cells are also damaged. <br>Hence, option (1) is true.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45630,'physics',40,239,478,'Which of the following statements are true regarding Bohr\'s model of hydrogen atom?<br>A. Orbiting speed of electron decreases as it shifts to discrete orbits away from the nucleus.<br>B. Radii of allowed orbits of electron are inversely proportional to the square of principle quantum number.<br>C. Frequency with which electrons orbits around the nucleus in discrete orbits is inversely proportional to the cube of principal quantum number.<br>D. Binding force with which the electron is bound to the nucleus increases as it shifts to outer orbits.','[{"text": "A and C", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "B and D", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "A, B and C", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "B, C and D", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'(A) Orbiting speed of electron decreases so it shifts to discrete orbits away from the nucleus. Actually, the velocity of electron increase with increase in atomic numbers.<br>The speed of electron in hydrogen atom is the orbital speed of electron.<br>It is given by $r = \\frac{{2.18 \\times {{10}^6}}}{n}\\,m{\\rm{/}}s$.<br>Thus $r$ is indirectly proportional to $n$.<br>Thus when $n$ will increase $r$ will decrease.<br>Hence, it is a correct statement.<br>(B) Radii of allowed orbits of electron are proportional to the principal quantum. In a hydrogen atom, the radius of orbit is given by formula<br>$r=0.529 n^{2}$<br>Thus $r$ is directly proportional to $n^{2}$. Hence it is an incorrect statement.<br>(C) Frequency with which electrons orbits around the nucleus indiscrete orbits is inversly proportional to the principal quantum member. Frequency of electron is given by the formula<br>$f=\\dfrac{6.56 \\times 10^{15}}{n^{3}}$<br>Thus frequency is inversely proportional to $n$. Hence, it is correct statement.<br>(D) Binding force with which the electron is bound to the nucleus increases on it shifts to outer orbits.<br>The energy will always decrease when electron shifts to outer orbits.<br>This is hence incorrect statement.<br>Hence (1) A and C is the correct answer.',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45633,'physics',41,408,812,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Energy is released in nuclear fission.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Total binding energy of the fission fragments is larger than the total binding energy of the parent nucleus.','[{"text": "Both assertion and reason are correct and reason is the correct explanation of assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both assertion and reason are correct but reason is not the correct explanation of assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'When atoms of an element are bombarded by neutrons, the atomic nuclei are disintegrated and emit lighter particles. This process is called nuclear fission. The mass of the nuclei obtained by the fission is less than the mass of the disintegrated nucleus and lost mass reappears in the form of heat accroding to Einstein mass energy relation. The total binding energy of the fission fragments is larger than the total binding energy of the parent nucleus, because fission occurs when total mass energy decreases that is  $\\Delta {E_{bn}}$ will increase. Option (1) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45634,'physics',37,973,2271,'As per the given graph, choose the correct representation for curve \\(A\\) and curve \\(B\\). Where \\(X_{C}=\\) reactance of pure capacitive circuit connected with \\[A.C.\\] source<br>\\(X_{L}=\\) reactance of pure inductive circuit connected with \\(A.C.\\) source<br>\\(R=\\) impedance of pure resistive circuit connected with \\[A.C.\\] source<br>\\(Z=\\) Impedance of the \\(L C R\\) series circuit<div class="matching-question"></div>','[{"text": "$A=X_{L}, B=Z$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$A=X_{C}, B=R$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$A=X_{C}, B=X_{L}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$A=X_{L}, B=R$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'As, $X_{C}=\\dfrac{1}{\\omega C}=\\dfrac{1}{(2 \\pi f) C} \\quad \\therefore \\quad X_{C} \\propto \\dfrac{1}{f}$<br>$\\therefore$ Curve $A$ shows $X_{C} \\propto \\dfrac{1}{f}$.<br>$X_{L}=\\omega L=(2 \\pi f) L$<br>$\\therefore X_{L} \\propto f$<br>$\\therefore$ Curve $B$ shows $X_{L} \\propto f$',NULL,'Easy','Graph/Figure',2023,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/37_45634_question_1.png'),
(45635,'physics',40,239,478,'The electric current $I$ created by the electron in the ground state of $H$ atom using Bohr model in terms of Bohr radius $\\left(a_{0}\\right)$ and velocity $\\left(v_{0}\\right)$ of electron in first orbit is:','[{"text": "$\\\\dfrac{e v_{0}}{2 \\\\pi a_{0}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{2 \\\\pi a_{0}}{e v_{0}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{2 \\\\pi a_{0}}{v_{0}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{v_{0}}{2 \\\\pi a_{0}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'In the ground state of hydrogen atom, suppose, $a_{0}=$ Bohr radius.<br>$T=\\dfrac{2 \\pi a_{0}}{v_{0}}$<br>$\\therefore$ Current created, $I=\\dfrac{e}{T}=\\dfrac{e v_{0}}{2 \\pi a_{0}}$.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45636,'physics',39,1102,2629,'On a photosensitive material, when frequency of incident radiation is increased by $40 \\%$, kinetic energy of emitted photoelectrons increases from $0.4 \\,{eV}$ to $1.2 \\,{eV}$. The work function  of the material is','[{"text": "$1.6\\\\,eV$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$3.2\\\\,eV$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$5.1\\\\,eV$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2.7\\\\,eV$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'According to Einstein\'s photoelectric equation,<br> $h\\nu  = K.E. + {\\phi _0}$<br> Initially,<br>$h\\nu  = 0.4 + {\\phi _0}{\\rm{ }}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{\\rm{(1)}}$<br>When frequency is increased by $40 \\%$,<br>$1.4\\,hv = 1.2 + {\\phi _0}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,(2)$<br>Subtracting equation (1) from equation (2),<br>$1.4 {~h} v-{h} v=1.2-0.4$<br>$\\therefore \\quad 0.4 \\,{hv}=0.8$<br>$\\therefore h v=2$$\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,(3)$<br>From equations (1) and (3),<br>$2=0.4+\\phi_{0}$<br>$\\therefore \\phi_{0}=2-0.4=1.6 \\,{eV}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45637,'physics',41,406,806,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> If the half-life of a radioactive substance is 40 days then  25%  substance decays in 20 days<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> $N = {N_0}{\\left( {\\frac{1}{2}} \\right)^n}$','[{"text": "Both assertion and reason are correct and reason is the correct explanation of assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both assertion and reason are correct but reason is not the correct explanation of assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but reason is correct.", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'In 20 days 50% of the substance decays.<br>$N = \\frac{{{N_0}}}{{{2^n}}}$<br>option (4) is correct',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45638,'physics',37,978,2282,'Match Column I with Column II. Choose the correct answer from the options given below.<div class="matching-question"><div class="column-left">A. $A.C$ generator<br>B. Transformer<br>C. Resonance phenomenon to occur<br>D. Sharpness of resonance</div><div class="column-right">P. Presence of both $L$ and $C$<br>Q. Electromagnetic induction<br>R. Quality factor<br>S. Mutual induction</div></div>','[{"text": "${\\\\rm{A}} - {\\\\rm{S}},\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\rm{B}} - {\\\\rm{R}},\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\rm{C}} - {\\\\rm{P}},\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\rm{D}} - {\\\\rm{Q}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{A}} - {\\\\rm{Q}},\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\rm{B}} - {\\\\rm{S}},\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\rm{C}} - {\\\\rm{P}},\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\rm{D}} - {\\\\rm{R}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{A}} - {\\\\rm{Q}},\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\rm{B}} - {\\\\rm{P}},\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\rm{C}} - {\\\\rm{R}},\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\rm{D}} - {\\\\rm{S}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{A}} - {\\\\rm{S}},\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\rm{B}} - {\\\\rm{Q}},\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\rm{C}} - {\\\\rm{P}},\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\rm{D}} - {\\\\rm{R}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'$A C$ generator works on the principle of electromagnetic induction. $EMF$ is induced in rotating armature.<br>Transformer works on the principle of mutual induction. Primary and secondary coils are not electrically coupled, they are magnetically coupled.<br>Resonant frequency is given by $f_{0}=\\dfrac{1}{2 \\pi \\sqrt{L C}}$<br>Hence, we can say that resonance occurs in presence of both $L$ and $C$ and it occurs when $X_{L}=X_{C}$.<br>Sharpness of resonance gives quality factor<br>$Q=\\dfrac{1}{R} \\sqrt{\\dfrac{L}{C}}$<br>$\\Rightarrow(\\mathrm{A})-(\\mathrm{Q}),(\\mathrm{B})-(\\mathrm{S}),(\\mathrm{C})-(\\mathrm{P}),(\\mathrm{D})-(\\mathrm{R})$',NULL,'Medium','Match the following',2023,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45639,'physics',40,238,477,'The trajectory of an $\\alpha$-particle can be computed by employing:','[{"text": "Newton\'s second law of motion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Coulomb\'s law for electrostatic force of repulsion between the $\\\\alpha$-particle and the positively charges nucleus.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both of these.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "None of these.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'For situation at sub atomic level also basic laws are applicable.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45641,'physics',41,406,809,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Electron capture occurs more often than positron emission in heavy elements.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Heavy elements exhibit radioactivity.','[{"text": "Both assertion and reason are correct and reason is the correct explanation of assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both assertion and reason are correct but reason is not the correct explanation of assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Electron capture is energitically more favourable than positron emission in heavy nuclei. So option (2) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45656,'physics',41,403,800,'<div class="matching-question"><div class="column-left">The detection of neutrions is extremely difficult.</div><div class="column-right">Neutrions only interact very weakly with matter.</div></div>','[{"text": "Statement A is correct but Statement B is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Statement A is incorrect but Statement B is correct.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Statements are correct.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Statements are incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Neutrons are uncharged particles with almost no mass. So they are difficult to detect since they  interact weakly with matter. So option (3) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ type-2',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45643,'physics',37,974,2769,'Read the Statement - A and Statement - B carefully to mark the correct options given below:<div class="matching-question"><div class="column-left">Maximum power is dissipated in a circuit containing an inductor, a capacitor and a resistor connected in series with an $A C$ source, when resonance occurs.</div><div class="column-right">Maximum power is dissipated in a circuit containing pure resistor due to zero phase difference between current and voltage.</div></div>','[{"text": "Statement A is correct but Statement B is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Statement A and Statement B are incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Statement A and Statement B are correct.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Statement A is incorrect but Statement B is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'<img src=\'/img/data/physics/37_45643_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />  <br>For $L C R$ circuit, $Z=\\left[R^{2}+\\left(X_{L}-X_{C}\\right)^{2}\\right]^{1 / 2}$<br>In series $L C R$ circuit, at resonance, impedance is minimum  $i.e.,$  $Z=R$. So maximum power is dissipated.<br>As the phase difference between current and voltage is $90^{\\circ}$ in case of inductor and capacitors. So the power consumed by them is zero and thus maximum power is dissipated by resistor only.<br>Both statements $A$  and  $B$ are true.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ type-2',2023,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45644,'physics',41,406,809,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> The mass of $\\beta $-particles when they are emitted is higher than the mass of electrons obtained by other means.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> $\\beta $-particles  and electron, both are similar particles.','[{"text": "Both assertion and reason are correct and reason is the correct explanation of assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both assertion and reason are correct but reason is not the correct explanation of assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'$\\beta $-particles are emitted with energy with very high velocity. So, according to Einstein’s theory of relativity, the mass of a $\\beta $-particle is much higher compared to its rest mass $\\left( {{m_0}} \\right).$ But $\\beta {\\rm{ - }}$particle and electron both are similar particles. So option (2) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45646,'physics',40,238,477,'The Rutherford $\\alpha$-particle experiment shows that most of the $\\alpha$-particles pass through almost unscattered. While some are scattered through large angles. What information does it give about the structure of the atom?','[{"text": "Atom is hollow.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "The whole mass of the atom is concentrated in a small centre called nucleus.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Nucleus is positively charged.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "All of the above.", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'All the options given are the conclusion of the experiment.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45647,'physics',41,403,801,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> All nuclei are not of same size.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Size depends on atomic mass.','[{"text": "Both assertion and reason are correct and reason is the correct explanation of assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both assertion and reason are correct but reason is not the correct explanation of assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'The radius of nucleus is given by $R = {R_0}{{\\rm{A}}^{1/3}},$  where ${R_0}$ is a constant  $ = 1.1 \\times {10^{ - 15}}m.$ For different nuclei mass number $A$ is different, therefore $R$ is different. So option (1) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45650,'physics',40,239,478,'In case of a hydrogen atom the ratio of areas of electrons orbits corresponding to first excited state and ground state is','[{"text": "$16: 1$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$18: 1$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$4: 1$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2: 1$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'$r \\propto n^{2}, A=\\pi r^{2}$<br>$\\dfrac{A_{1}}{A_{0}}=\\dfrac{\\pi\\left(r_{1}\\right)^{2}}{\\pi\\left(r_{0}\\right)^{2}}=\\dfrac{\\left(n_{2}\\right)^{4}}{\\left(n_{1}\\right)^{4}}=\\dfrac{16}{1}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45651,'physics',41,404,802,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> 1 $amu$ is equivalent to 931 $MeV$.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Mass and energy relation is $E = m{c^2}.$','[{"text": "Both assertion and reason are correct and reason is the correct explanation of assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both assertion and reason are correct but reason is not the correct explanation of assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Substituing  $m = 1\\,amu = 1.67 \\times {10^{ - 17}}kg$ and $c = 3 \\times {10^8}m{s^{ - 1}}$ in the energy-mass equivalence relation <br>$E = m{c^2} = 931MeV$<br>So option (1) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45652,'physics',37,973,2263,'An alternating voltage source $V=260 \\sin (628 t)$ is connected across a pure inductor of $5\\,mH$. Inductive reactance in the circuit is','[{"text": "$3.14 \\\\Omega$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$6.28 \\\\Omega$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$0.318 \\\\Omega$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$0.5 \\\\Omega$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Given, $V=260 \\sin (628 t)$<br>$L = 5mH = 5 \\times {10^{ - 3}}H$<br>${V_0} = 260\\,V,\\omega  = 628\\,rad/s = $ Angular frequancy<br>$v = \\frac{\\omega }{{2\\pi }} = \\frac{{628}}{{2 \\times 3.14}} = 100\\,Hz$<br>Inductive reactance, $X_{L}=2 \\pi v L$<br>$=2 \\times 3.14 \\times 100 \\times 5 \\times 10^{-3}=3.14 \\Omega$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2023,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45653,'physics',39,1102,2628,'Photons with energy $4 {eV}$ are incident on a cathode ${C}$ in a photoelectric cell. The maximum energy of emitted photoelectrons is $2 \\,{eV}$. When photons of energy $6 \\,{eV}$ are incident on ${C}$ and no photoelectrons reaches the anode ${A}$, then the stopping potential is','[{"text": "$1\\\\,V$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$6\\\\,V$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$4\\\\,V$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$2\\\\,V$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'We know,<br>${\\left( {K.E.} \\right)_{\\max }} = h\\nu  - {\\phi _0}$<br>$\\therefore {\\rm{ }}2eV = 4eV - {\\phi _0}$<br>$\\therefore {\\rm{ }}\\,\\,{\\phi _0} = 2eV$<br>Hence, when ${h} v=6 {eV}$,<br>${(K.E.)_{\\max }} = 6\\,eV - 2\\,eV = 4\\,eV$<br>Also, ${(K.E.)_{\\max }} = e{V_0} = 4\\,eV$<br>$\\Rightarrow {V}_{0}=4 {~V}$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45654,'physics',40,238,477,'The order of the size of nucleus and Bohr radius of an atom respectively are:','[{"text": "${10^{ - 14}}\\\\,m,{10^{ - 10}}\\\\,m$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${10^{ - 10}}\\\\,m,{10^{ - 8}}\\\\,m$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${10^{ - 20}}\\\\,m,{10^{ - 16}}\\\\,m$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${10^{ - 8}}\\\\,m,{10^{ - 6}}\\\\,m$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Radius of first bohr\'s orbit such that $H$ atom is $0.53 \\times {10^{ - 10}}\\,m$ and diameter of nucleus is in the range of ${10^{ - 14}}\\,m.$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45658,'physics',37,974,2766,'In a series $L R$ circuit with $X_{L}=R$, power factor is $P_{1}$. If a capacitor of capacitance $C$ with $X_{C}=X_{L}$ is added to the circuit the power factor becomes $P_{2}$. The ratio of $P_{1}$ to $P_{2}$ will be','[{"text": "$1: 3$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1: 2$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1: 1$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1: \\\\sqrt{2}$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'When ${X_L} = R$, power factor is,<br>${P_1} = \\frac{R}{{\\sqrt {X_L^2 + {R^2}} }} = \\frac{R}{{\\sqrt {{R^2} + {R^2}} }} = \\frac{1}{{\\sqrt 2 }}$<br>${P_2} = 1$ <img src=\'/img/data/physics/37_45658_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />   <br>${P_2} = 1$  (resonance condition)<br>For ${X_C} = {X_L}$ the circuit resonates and  power factor becomes 1.<br>$\\therefore \\frac{{{P_1}}}{{{P_2}}} = \\frac{1}{{\\sqrt 2 }}$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',2023,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45659,'physics',41,403,800,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Heavy nuclides tend to have more number of neutrons than protons.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> In heavy nuclei, as there is coulomb repulsion between protons, so excess of neutrons are preferrable.','[{"text": "Both assertion and reason are correct and reason is the correct explanation of assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both assertion and reason are correct but reason is not the correct explanation of assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Option (1) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45660,'physics',39,1102,2628,'For intensity $I$ of a light of wavelength $6000$$ \\mathop A^{~~\\circ}  $, the photoelectron saturation current is $0.40 \\,\\mu {A}$ and stopping potential is $1.37 {~V}$. The work function of metal is  <br> (Take ${h}=4.14 \\times 10^{-15} {eVs}$ )','[{"text": "$0.4\\\\,eV$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$0.7\\\\,eV$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$0.9\\\\,eV$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$0.5\\\\,eV$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'$E = \\frac{{4.14 \\times {{10}^{ - 15}} \\times 3 \\times {{10}^8}}}{{6000 \\times {{10}^{ - 10}}}} = 2.07eV\\,$<br>$K.E.{{\\rm{ }}_{\\max {\\rm{ }}}} = e{V_0} = 1.37eV$<br>By using  $E = {\\phi _0} + {\\rm{K}}.{{\\rm{E}}_{{\\rm{max}}}},$  we get,<br>$2.07=\\phi_{0}+1.37$<br>$\\therefore \\phi_{0}=0.7 {eV}$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45661,'physics',40,239,478,'If the radius of the first Bohr\'s orbit is $x$, then de-Broglie wavelength of electron in 3rd orbit is nearly:','[{"text": "$2\\\\,\\\\pi x$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$6\\\\,\\\\pi x$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$9\\\\,x$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$x / 3$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'$m v r=\\dfrac{n h}{2 \\pi}\\left(r=\\dfrac{r_{0} n^{2}}{Z}\\right) r_{0}=x$<br>$2 \\pi r=n \\lambda \\Rightarrow \\lambda=\\dfrac{2 \\pi r}{n}=6 \\pi x$.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45662,'physics',37,975,2278,'Which of the following combinations should be selected for better tuning of an ${L C R}$ circuit used for communication?','[{"text": "$R = 20\\\\,\\\\Omega ,L = 1.5H,C = 35\\\\,\\\\mu F$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$R = 25\\\\,\\\\Omega ,L = 2.5H,C = 45\\\\,\\\\mu F$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$R = 25\\\\,\\\\Omega ,L = 1.5H,C = 45\\\\,\\\\mu F$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$R = 15\\\\,\\\\Omega ,L = 3.5H,C = 30\\\\,\\\\mu F$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'For good communication ${Q}$ factor should be high ${Q=\\dfrac{1}{R} \\sqrt{\\dfrac{L}{C}}}$<br>From the given values in options-<br>$\\dfrac{1}{5} \\sqrt{\\dfrac{3.5}{30}}=0.022$<br>So, correct option is (4).',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2024,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45663,'physics',41,403,800,'<div class="matching-question"><div class="column-left">Isotopes of an element can be seperated by using a mass spectrometer.</div><div class="column-right">Separation of isotopes is possible because of difference in mass number of isotopes.</div></div>','[{"text": "Statement A is correct but Statement B is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Statement A is incorrect but Statement B is correct.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Statements are correct.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Statements are incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'A mass spectrometer ionizes atoms with a high energy electron beam and then deflects the ions through a magnetic field based on their mass to charge ratio. So option (3) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ type-2',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45665,'physics',40,239,478,'What is the $K.E.$ and $P.E.$ of electron in the first orbit of hydrogen atom? Given $e = 1.6 \\times {10^{ - 19}}C$, $r = 0.53 \\times {10^{ - 10}}\\,m:$','[{"text": "$13.58\\\\,eV, - 27.16\\\\,eV$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$ - 13.58\\\\,eV,27.16\\\\,eV$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$13.58\\\\,J,27.16\\\\,eV$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$27.16\\\\,eV,27.16\\\\,eV$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'$K . E$. of electron $\\dfrac{K Z e^{2}}{2 r}=\\dfrac{K e^{2}}{2 r}(\\because Z=1)$<br>$K.E. = \\frac{{9 \\times {{10}^9}{{\\left( {1.6 \\times {{10}^{ - 19}}} \\right)}^2}}}{{2 \\times 0.53 \\times {{10}^{ - 10}}}}Joule$<br>${ = \\frac{{9 \\times 2.56 \\times {{10}^{ - 19}}}}{{1.06 \\times 1.6 \\times {{10}^{ - 19}}}}eV = 13.58\\,eV}$<br>${P.E{\\rm{ }}\\,\\,{\\rm{of }}\\,\\,\\,{\\rm{electron }} = \\frac{{ - KZ{e^2}}}{r} =  - 2(K.E)}$<br>${ = 2 \\times (13.58) =  - 27.16\\,eV.}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45666,'physics',37,975,2277,'In an ${L C R}$ series circuit, the value of only capacitance ${C}$ is varied. The resulting variation of resonance frequency ${f_{0}}$ as a function of can be represented as<div class="matching-question"></div>','[{"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'${f_{0}=\\dfrac{1}{2 \\pi} \\sqrt{\\dfrac{L}{C}}}$<br>${\\therefore f_{0} \\propto \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{C}}}$<br>So, correct option is (4).',NULL,'Medium','Graph/Figure',2024,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45667,'physics',41,404,803,'<div class="matching-question"><div class="column-left">The binding energy per nucleon, for nuclei with atomic mass number $A$ > 100, decrease with $A$.</div><div class="column-right">Repulsive forces among nuclei are more for heavier nuclei.</div></div>','[{"text": "Statement A is correct but Statement B is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Statement A is incorrect but Statement B is correct.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Statements are correct.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Statements are incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,NULL,NULL,'Medium','MCQ type-2',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45669,'physics',41,406,809,'<div class="matching-question"><div class="column-left">All radioactive nuclei must emit ${\\beta ^{ - 1}}$ particles.</div><div class="column-right">Electron exist inside the nucleus.</div></div>','[{"text": "Statement A is correct but Statement B is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Statement A is incorrect but Statement B is correct.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Statements are correct.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Statements are incorrect.", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Electrons present outside the nucleus. All the radioactive nuclei do not emit  ${\\beta ^ - }$ -particles.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ type-2',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45674,'physics',41,403,800,'<div class="matching-question"><div class="column-left">The whole mass of the atom is concentrated in the nucleus.</div><div class="column-right">The mass of a nucleus can be either less than or more than the sum of the masses of nucleons present in it.</div></div>','[{"text": "Statement A is correct but Statement B is incorrect.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Statement A is incorrect but Statement B is correct.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Statements are correct.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Statements are incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Mass of a nucleus is always less than the total mass of nucleons. Option (1) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ type-2',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45675,'physics',40,238,477,'A proton approaches another proton at rest with speed $v_{0}$. Assume impact parameter to be zero. Their closest distance of approach is (mass of proton is $m$ )','[{"text": "$\\\\dfrac{e^{2}}{4 \\\\pi \\\\varepsilon m v_{0}^{2}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{e^{2}}{\\\\pi \\\\varepsilon_{0} m v_{0}^{2}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{e^{2}}{m v_{0}^{2}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Zero", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Use: Law of Conservation of Linear momentum and energy.<br>$i.e.,$ $m v_{0}=2 m v$<br>$\\dfrac{1}{2} m v_{0}^{2}-\\dfrac{1}{2} m v^{2} 2=\\dfrac{1}{4 \\pi \\in_{0}} \\dfrac{e^{2}}{d}$<br>$\\Rightarrow \\dfrac{1}{2} m v_{0}^{2}-\\dfrac{m v_{0}^{2}}{4}=\\dfrac{e^{2}}{4 \\pi \\in_{0} d}$<br>$\\Rightarrow \\dfrac{m v_{0}^{2}}{4}=\\dfrac{e^{2}}{4 \\pi \\in_{0} d} \\Rightarrow d=\\dfrac{e^{2}}{\\pi \\in_{0} m v_{0}^{2}}$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45677,'physics',37,973,2264,'A ${10 \\mu F}$ capacitor is connected to a ${210 {~V}, 50 {~Hz}}$ source as shown in figure. The peak current in the circuit is nearly ${(\\pi=3.14)}$ :','[{"text": "$0.58\\\\,A$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$0.93\\\\,A$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$1.20\\\\,A$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$0.35\\\\,A$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Capacitive Reactance<br>${X_C} = \\frac{1}{{\\omega C}} = \\frac{1}{{2\\pi fC}}$<br>$ = \\frac{1}{{2 \\times 3.14 \\times 50 \\times 10 \\times {{10}^{ - 6}}}}$<br>$ = \\frac{{1000}}{{3.14}}$<br>${V_{rms}} = 210\\,V$<br>${i_{rms}} = \\frac{{{V_{rms}}}}{C} = \\frac{{210}}{C}$<br>Peak current<br>$=\\sqrt{2} i_{{rms}}=\\sqrt{2} \\times \\dfrac{210}{1000} \\times 3.14=0.93 A$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2024,'NEET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/37_45677_question_1.png'),
(45678,'physics',41,403,801,'Which of the following are correct?<br>I. Nuclear density is costant for all matter.<br>II. Nuclear density is around $2.3 \\times {10^{17}}kg/{m^3}$<br>III.	Mass of ordinary matter is mainly due to 	nucleus.','[{"text": "I, II and III", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "II and III", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "I and II", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Only I", "isCorrect": false}]',0,NULL,NULL,'Medium','MCQ type-2',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45680,'physics',40,238,477,'A closest distance of approach of an $\\alpha$ particle travelling with a velocity $v$ towards $A l_{13}$ nucleus is $d$. The closest distance of approach of an alpha particle travelling with velocity $4 v$ towards $F{e_{26}}$ nucleus is','[{"text": "$d / 2$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$d / 4$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$d / 8$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$d / 16$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'$\\dfrac{1}{2} m v^{2}=\\dfrac{1}{4 \\pi \\varepsilon_{0}} \\times \\dfrac{q_{1} q_{2}}{r}$<br>$r \\propto \\dfrac{z^{2}}{v^{2}}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45681,'physics',37,972,2261,'The peak value of the alternating current given by ${I=4 \\sin \\omega t+4 \\sin (\\omega t+2 \\pi / 3)}$ is','[{"text": "${4 \\\\sqrt{2}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${2 \\\\sqrt{2}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "8", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "4", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'${I=4 \\sin \\omega t+4 \\sin \\left(\\omega t+\\dfrac{2 \\pi}{3}\\right)}$<br>${=4\\left(\\sin \\omega t+\\sin \\left(\\omega t+\\dfrac{2 \\pi}{3}\\right)\\right)}$<br>$ = 4\\left[ \\begin{array}{l}\n2 \\cdot \\sin \\frac{{\\left( {\\omega t + \\omega t + \\frac{{2\\pi }}{3}} \\right)}}{2} \\cdot \\\\\n\\cos \\frac{{\\left( {\\omega t + \\frac{{2\\pi }}{3} - \\omega t} \\right)}}{2}\n\\end{array} \\right]$<br>${=8\\left[\\sin \\left(\\omega t+\\dfrac{\\pi}{3}\\right) \\cdot \\cos \\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{3}\\right)\\right]}$<br>${=4 \\sin \\left(\\omega t+\\dfrac{\\pi}{3}\\right)}$<br>Peak current ${=4 {~A}}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45682,'physics',41,403,800,'Which of the following statements are correct?<br>I. Atoms of isotopes have same electronic structure.<br>II. Atoms of isotopes occupies same place in periodic table.<br>III. Atoms of isotopes have same number of protons.<br>IV.	Atoms of isotopes have same number of 	neutrons.','[{"text": "I and II", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "I, II and III", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "I, II, III and IV", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "II and IV", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Isotopes have same atomic number. In atoms number of electrons and protons are equal. The chemical properties of elements depend on their electronic structure. As the atoms of isotopes have identical electronic structure they have identical chemical behaviour and are placed in the same location in the periodic table.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ type-2',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45684,'physics',37,972,2259,'A complex current wave is given by ${i=5+5 \\sin (100 \\omega t) A}$. Its average value over one time period is given as','[{"text": "$10\\\\,A$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$5\\\\,A$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${\\\\sqrt{50} A}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "0", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'${i=5+5 \\sin (100 \\omega t)}$<br>average value,<br>$ < i >  = 5 + 5 < \\sin (100\\omega t) > $<br>In one time period, $ < \\sin (100\\omega t) >  = 0$<br>$ < i >  = 5\\,A$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45685,'physics',41,403,800,'<div class="matching-question"><div class="column-left">Neutrons penetrate matter more readily as compared to protons.</div><div class="column-right">Neutrons are slightly more massive than protons.</div></div>','[{"text": "Statement A is correct but Statement B is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Statement A is incorrect but Statement B is correct.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Statements are correct.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Statements are incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'As neutrons have no charge so their penetration power is more than protons and 	electrons.  So option (3) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ type-2',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45686,'physics',40,238,477,'In a head on collision between an $\\alpha$-particle and gold nucleus, the closest distance of approach is $41.3\\,fm$. Calculate the energy of $\\alpha$-particle. $(z$ of gold $=79)$','[{"text": "$5.51\\\\,eV$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$5.51\\\\,MeV$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$5.51\\\\,J$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$5.51\\\\,KJ$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'$E = \\frac{{(Ze)(2e)}}{{4\\pi {\\varepsilon _0}r}} = \\frac{{\\left( \\begin{array}{l}\n9 \\times {10^9} \\times 79 \\times \\\\\n2{\\left( {1.6 \\times {{10}^{ - 19}}} \\right)^2}\n\\end{array} \\right)}}{{41.3 \\times {{10}^{ - 15}}}}$<br>$ = \\frac{{9 \\times 79 \\times 3.2 \\times 1.6 \\times {{10}^{ - 14}}}}{{41.3}}\\,J$<br>$ = 8.814 \\times {10^{ - 13}}{\\rm{ }}Joule{\\rm{ }} = \\frac{{8.814 \\times {{10}^{ - 13}}}}{{1.6 \\times {{10}^{ - 13}}}}$<br>$E = 5.51\\,MeV.$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45687,'physics',39,1104,2631,'If the momentum of an electron is increased by $p_{m}$ then the de Broglie wavelength associated with it changes by $0.5 \\%$. Then   find  the initial momentum of the electron is','[{"text": "$156\\\\,pm$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$199\\\\,pm$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$179\\\\,pm$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$185\\\\,pm$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Let $p$ be initial momentum of electron,<br> $p^{\\prime}=p+p_{m}$<br>$\\therefore \\quad \\lambda^{\\prime}=\\lambda-\\dfrac{0.5}{100} \\lambda=\\left(1-\\dfrac{0.5}{100}\\right) \\lambda$<br>$\\therefore \\dfrac{\\lambda^{\\prime}}{\\lambda}=\\dfrac{{p}}{{p}^{\\prime}}$<br>$\\therefore\\left(1-\\dfrac{0.5}{100}\\right)=\\dfrac{p}{p+p_{m}}$<br>$\\therefore\\left(\\dfrac{99.5}{100}\\right)=\\dfrac{{p}}{{p}+{p}_{{m}}}$<br>$\\therefore \\quad 99.5 \\,{p}+99.5 \\,{p}_{{m}}=100 \\,{p}$<br>$\\therefore 0.5 \\,{p}=99.5 \\,{p}_{{m}}$<br>$\\therefore {p}=199 \\,{p}_{{m}}$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45688,'physics',37,972,2261,'The peak value of an alternating $e.m.f.$ ${E}$ is given by ${E=E_{0} \\cos \\omega t}$ is $10\\,volts$ and its frequency is $50\\,Hz$. At time ${t=\\dfrac{1}{600} s}$, the instantaneous emf is','[{"text": "$10\\\\,V$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${5 \\\\sqrt{3} V}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$5\\\\,V$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1\\\\,V$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'${E=E_{o} \\cos \\omega t}$<br>${E_{o}=10 {~V}}$<br>${f=50 H_{Z}}$<br>${\\Rightarrow \\omega=2 \\pi \\times 50=100 \\pi}$<br>${t=\\dfrac{1}{600} s}$<br>The instantaneous value of emf<br>${=10 \\cos \\left(100 \\pi \\times \\dfrac{1}{600}\\right)}$<br>${=10 \\cos \\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{6}\\right)=5 \\sqrt{3} {~V}}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45689,'physics',41,406,806,'In one half-life time duration<br>I. Activity of a sample reduced to half of its initial value.<br>II. Number of radio active nuclei present is reduced to half of its initial value.<br>III. Mass of sample is reduced to half of its initial value.<br>Out of these, correct statements are','[{"text": "I and III", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "I and II", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "I, II and III", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "II and III", "isCorrect": false}]',1,NULL,NULL,'Medium','MCQ type-2',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45690,'physics',40,238,477,'When an $\\alpha$-particle happens to come near a nucleus the intense electric field of these scatters it through a large angle then what is the effect of atomic electrons on $\\alpha$-particle?','[{"text": "Repel $\\\\alpha$-particle lightly.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Deviate $\\\\alpha$-particle from their path.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Due to the electron cloud it blocks the pathof $\\\\alpha$-particle.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Do not appreciably effect.", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Since electrons are distributed throughout, their charge is not localised and hence no appreciable effect on \' $\\alpha$ \' particles.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45692,'physics',41,406,809,'In $\\alpha $- decay which of these are true?<br>I.  $_2^4He$ is emitted.<br>II.	 Mass number of daughter nucleus decreases by 4.<br>III.	Atomic number of daughter nucleus decreases by 2<br>IV.  $_2^4He$ is electrically neutral.','[{"text": "I and II", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "I, II and IV", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "I, II and III", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "I, II, III and IV", "isCorrect": false}]',2,NULL,NULL,'Medium','MCQ type-2',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45693,'physics',37,972,2260,'A periodic voltage ${V}$ varies with time ${t}$, as shown in the figure. ${T}$ is the time period. The rms value of the voltage is','[{"text": "${\\\\dfrac{V_{0}}{8}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\dfrac{V_{0}}{2}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${V_{0}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\dfrac{V_{0}}{4}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Root mean square value<br>${\\langle V\\rangle=\\sqrt{\\dfrac{\\int_{0}^{T / 4} V_{0}^{2} d t}{\\int_{0}^{T} d t}}=\\sqrt{\\dfrac{V_{0}^{2}\\left(\\dfrac{T}{4}\\right)}{T}}}$<br>${=\\sqrt{\\dfrac{V_{0}^{2}}{4}}=\\dfrac{V_{0}}{2}}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/37_45693_question_1.png'),
(45694,'physics',39,1102,2629,'A metal surface of threshold frequency $10^{15} {~Hz}$ is illuminated by a light of frequency $v\\left(v>v_{0}\\right)$. Most energetic electrons thus emitted enter normally in a magnetic field of intensity $2.5 {~T}$. If the magnetic force acting on these electrons is $10^{-8}$ dyne, then the difference between the frequency of incident radiation and the threshold frequency is $4.3 \\times 10^{{x}} {Hz}$. Find the value of $x$.<br>(Take ${h}=6.6 \\times 10^{-34} {Js}$ )','[{"text": "11", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "13", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "15", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "17", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Given that the electrons enter the magnetic field normally,<br>$\\therefore \\theta  = 90^\\circ $<br>$\\therefore$ Magnetic force acting on electrons will be,<br>${F}={qvB} \\sin \\theta={qvB}$<br>$\\therefore \\quad v=\\dfrac{F}{q B}$<br>Force, ${F}=10^{-8}$ dyne $=10^{-13} {~N}$<br>$\\therefore \\quad v=\\dfrac{10^{-13}}{1.6 \\times 10^{-19} \\times 2.5}=2.5 \\times 10^{5} {~m} / {s}$<br>Also, by using Einstein\'s photoelectric equation, <br>we know<br>$K.{E_{\\max {\\rm{ }}}} = h\\left( {v - {v_0}} \\right)$<br>$\\therefore \\quad \\dfrac{1}{2} m v_{\\max }^{2}={h}\\left(v-v_{0}\\right)$<br>$\\therefore \\quad \\dfrac{1}{2} \\times 9.1 \\times 10^{-31} \\times\\left(2.5 \\times 10^{5}\\right)^{2}={h}\\left(v-v_{0}\\right)$<br>$\\therefore \\quad 28.44 \\times 10^{-21}={h}\\left(v-v_{0}\\right)$<br>$\\therefore \\quad v-v_{0}=\\dfrac{28.44 \\times 10^{-21}}{6.6 \\times 10^{-34}}$<br> $=4.3 \\times 10^{13} {~Hz}$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45707,'physics',37,973,2264,'In ac circuit, an alternating voltage ${e=100 \\sqrt{2} \\sin 200 t}$ volt is connected to a capacitor of capacity ${1 \\mu F}$. The rms value of the current in the circuit is','[{"text": "$10\\\\,mA$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$100\\\\,mA$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$200\\\\,mA$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$20\\\\,mA$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'${X_{C}=\\dfrac{1}{\\omega C}=\\dfrac{1}{200 \\times 10^{-6}}=0.5 \\times 10^{4}}$<br>${=5 \\times 10^{3} \\Omega}$<br>${V_{\\text {rms }}=\\dfrac{V_{0}}{\\sqrt{2}}=100}$<br>${I_{r m s}=\\dfrac{V_{m s}}{X_{C}}=\\dfrac{100}{5 \\times 10^{3}}=20 \\times 10^{-3} {~A}=20 {~mA}}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45696,'physics',37,973,2262,'Three alternating voltage sources ${V_{1}=3 \\sin \\omega t}$ volt, ${V_{2}=5 \\sin \\left(\\omega t+\\phi_{1}\\right)}$ volt and ${V_{3}=5 \\sin \\left(\\omega t-\\phi_{2}\\right)}$ volt connected across a resistance ${R=\\sqrt{\\dfrac{7}{3}} \\Omega}$, as shown in the figure, where ${\\phi_{1}}$ and ${\\phi_{2}}$ correspond to ${30^{\\circ}}$ and ${127^{\\circ}}$. Find the peak current (in Amp) through the resistor.','[{"text": "${1 A}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${2 A}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${3 A}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${\\\\sqrt{3} A}$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'${V_{1}=3 \\sin \\omega t ; V_{2}=5 \\sin \\left(\\omega t+\\phi_{1}\\right)}$;<br>$V_{3}=5 \\sin \\left(\\omega t-\\phi_{2}\\right)$ <img src=\'/img/data/physics/37_45696_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' /> ${V}$ max ${={\\sqrt{\\left(\\dfrac{5 \\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right)^{2}}}^{2}+(1.5)^{2}=\\sqrt{21}}$<br>${I \\max =\\dfrac{V_{\\max }}{R}=\\dfrac{\\sqrt{21}}{\\sqrt{\\dfrac{7}{3}}}=\\sqrt{\\dfrac{21 \\times 3}{7}}=3 {~A}}$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/37_45696_question_1.png'),
(45697,'physics',41,406,806,'Suppose we consider a large number of containers each containing initially 10000 atoms of a radioactive material with a half-life of 1 year. After 1 year','[{"text": "All the containers will contain the same number of atoms of the material but that number will only be approximately 5000.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "The containers will in general have different numbers of the atoms of the material but their average will be close to 5000.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "All the containers will have 5000 atoms of the materials.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "None of the containers can have more than 5000 atoms.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Radioactivity is spontaneous activity of the radioactive material. In $t = 1$ year $ = $ half-life of material; on the average, half the number of atoms will decay. Therefore, the containers will in general have different number of atoms of the material, but their average will be close to 5000.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,'NCERT Exemplar','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45699,'physics',37,973,2265,'When ${100 V}$ dc is supplied across ${a}$ solenoid, a current of $1.0\\,A$ flows in it. When $100\\,volts$ ac is applied across the same coil, the current drops to $0.5\\,A$. If the frequency of ${a c}$ source is $50\\,Hz$, then the impedance and inductance of the solenoid are','[{"text": "${200 \\\\Omega}$ and $0.55\\\\,H$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${100 \\\\Omega}$ and $0.86\\\\,H$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${200 \\\\Omega}$ and $1.0\\\\,H$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${100 \\\\Omega}$ and $0.93\\\\,H$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'For dc, ${R=\\dfrac{V}{i}=\\dfrac{100}{1}=100 \\Omega}$<br>For ac, ${Z=\\dfrac{V}{i}=\\dfrac{100}{0.5}=200 \\Omega}$<br>${\\because Z=\\sqrt{R^{2}+(\\omega L)^{2}}}$.<br>${\\Rightarrow 200=\\sqrt{(100)^{2}+4 \\pi^{2}(50)^{2} L^{2}}}$<br>${L=0.55 H}$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45700,'physics',41,406,806,'The half-life period of radium is 1600 yr. The fraction of a sample of radium that would remain after 6400 yr is','[{"text": "$\\\\frac{1}{{16}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{1}{2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{1}{4}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{1}{8}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'$N = {N_0}{e^{ - \\lambda t}} = {N_0}{e^{\\frac{{ - \\ln 2}}{{1600}} \\times 6400}} = {N_0}{e^{ - {{\\ln }_e}16}}$<br>$ \\Rightarrow N = \\frac{{{N_0}}}{{16}} \\Rightarrow \\frac{N}{{{N_0}}} = \\frac{1}{{16}}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,'NCERT Exemplar','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45701,'physics',40,239,478,'The value of ground state energy of a $H$-atom is $ - 13.6\\,eV$. The energy required to take an electron from ground state to the first excited state is:','[{"text": "$ - 10.2\\\\,eV$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$ + 10.2\\\\,eV$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$ - 17\\\\,eV$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$ - 3.4\\\\,eV$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Required energy,<br>$E{\\rm{ }} = {E_f} - {E_i}$<br>$ =  - 3.4 - ( - 13.6)$<br>$ =  + 10.2\\,eV.$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45703,'physics',41,406,806,'A radioactive isotope has a half-life of $T$ years. How long will it take the activity to reduce to 3.125% and 1%?','[{"text": "$9.5\\\\,T\\\\,{\\\\rm{and}}\\\\,5\\\\,T$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$5\\\\,T\\\\,{\\\\rm{and}}\\\\,6.65\\\\,T$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$4.5\\\\,T\\\\,{\\\\rm{and}}\\\\,7.5\\\\,T$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$5\\\\,T\\\\,{\\\\rm{and}}\\\\,9.5\\\\,T$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Given, half-time ${T_{1/2}} = T\\,$<br>$A = 3.125\\% \\,\\,{\\rm{of}}\\,{A_0}$<br>$\\therefore \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\frac{A}{{{A_{\\rm{0}}}}} = \\frac{{3.125}}{{100}} = \\frac{1}{{32}}$<br>We know that<br>$\\frac{A}{{{A_0}}} = {\\left( {\\frac{1}{2}} \\right)^n} \\Rightarrow \\frac{1}{{32}} = {\\left( {\\frac{1}{2}} \\right)^n}$<br>$ \\Rightarrow {\\left( {\\frac{1}{2}} \\right)^5} = {\\left( {\\frac{1}{2}} \\right)^n} \\Rightarrow n = 5$<br>So, time $t = n \\times {T_{1/2}} = 5\\,T$<br>After 5 half-time period activity reduces to 	3.125% of initial activity.<br>Now, given $A = {\\rm{1\\% }}\\,\\,{\\rm{of}}\\,\\,{A_0}$<br>$\\therefore \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\frac{A}{{{A_{\\rm{0}}}}} = \\frac{1}{{100}}$<br>We know that $\\frac{A}{{{A_{\\rm{0}}}}} = {e^{ - \\lambda t}} \\Rightarrow \\frac{1}{{100}} = {e^{ - \\lambda t}}$<br>Taking  on both sides, we get<br>$lo{g_e}1 - lo{g_e}100 =  - \\lambda t{\\log _e}e$<br>$ \\Rightarrow t = \\frac{{4.6006}}{\\lambda }$<br>Also, we know that $\\lambda  = \\frac{{0.693}}{{{T_{1/2}}}} = \\frac{{0.693}}{T}$<br>$t = \\frac{{4.606}}{{0.693}} = 6.66\\,T$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,'NCERT Exemplar','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45704,'physics',37,973,2265,'A coil of self-inductance ${L}$ is connected in series with a bulb ${B}$ and an ac source. Brightness of the bulb decreases when','[{"text": "an iron rod is inserted in the coil", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "frequency of the ac source is decreased", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "number of turns in the coil is reduced", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "a capacitance of reactance ${X_{C}=X_{L}}$ is included in the same circuit", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'<img src=\'/img/data/physics/37_45704_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' /> Brightness of the bulb<br>(i) decreases when an iron rod is inserted in the coil as impedance of circuit increases<br>(ii) increases when frequency of the ac source is decreased as impedance of circuit decreases<br>(iii) Increases when number of turns in the coil is reduced as impedance of circuit decreases<br>(iv) Increases when a capacitance of reactance ${X_{C}=X_{L}}$ is included in the circuit as impedance of circuit decreases',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45708,'physics',41,406,806,'The count rate for a sample of radioactive material was measured at different times and this has been shown in figure with scale given. The half-life of the material and the total count in the first half value period, respectively are<div class="matching-question"></div>','[{"text": "4 h and 9000 (approximately)", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "3 h and 14100 (approximately)", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "3 h and 235 (approximately)", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "10 h and 157 (approximately)", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'As the count rate falls from 100 to 50 in 3h so the half-life is 3 hours. Taking average count per minute in the first half value period as $(100 + 50)/2$ i.e., 75<br>Total number of counts during this period<br>$ = 75 \\times 3 \\times 60 = 13500$<br>which is closest to the given result (14100).',NULL,'Medium','Graph/Figure',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/41_45708_question_1.png'),
(45710,'physics',37,973,2266,'A ${110 {~V}, 60 {~W}}$ lamp is run from a $220\\,V $  ac mains using a capacitor in series with the lamp instead of a resistor, then the voltage across the capacitor is about','[{"text": "$110\\\\,V$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$190\\\\,V$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$220\\\\,V$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$311\\\\,V$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'${V_{R}^{2}+V_{C}^{2}=V^{2}}$<br>${\\Rightarrow V_{C}=\\sqrt{V^{2}-V_{R}^{2}}}$<br>${V_{C}=\\sqrt{(200)^{2}-(110)^{2}}=190 {~V}}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45711,'physics',41,404,803,'Binding energy per nucleon \\[vs\\] mass number curve for nuclei is shown. \\[W, X, Y\\] and \\[Z\\] are four nuclei indicated on the curve. The process that would release energy is<div class="matching-question"></div>','[{"text": "$Y \\\\to 2Z$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$W \\\\to X + Z$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$W \\\\to 2Y$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$X \\\\to Y + Z$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Energy will be released when stability increases. This will happen when binding energy per nucleon increases. In the following table $B$.$E$ for all the four reactions are shown.<br>The $B$.$E$ of products is more than that of reactants in reaction 3. The reaction 3 is possible.',NULL,'Hard','Graph/Figure',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/41_45711_question_1.png'),
(45712,'physics',40,239,478,'The total energy of an $e^{-}$in the second orbit of $H$-atom is $ - 3.4\\,eV$. Its potential energy is:','[{"text": "$ + 3.4\\\\,eV$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$ + 6.8\\\\,eV$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$ - 6.8\\\\,eV$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$ - 1.7\\\\,eV$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'$P.E. = 2\\,T.E. = 2 \\times ( - 3.4) =  - 6.8\\,eV.$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45714,'physics',37,973,2271,'The vector diagram of current and voltage for a circuit is given. The components of the circuit will be','[{"text": "${L C R}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${L R}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${L C R}$ or ${L R}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "None of these", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'From the phase diagram, it is clear that current is lagging with respect to  ${E_{rms}}.$  This happens in  $LCR$  or  $LR$  circuit.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/37_45714_question_1.png'),
(45715,'physics',41,407,811,'Tritium is an isotope of hydrogen whose nucleus Triton contains 2 neutrons and 1 proton. Free neutrons decay into  $p + \\bar e + \\bar v.$ If one of the neutrons in Triton decays, it would transform into $H{e^3}$ nucleus. This does not happen. This is because','[{"text": "The electron created in the beta decay process cannot remain in the nucleus.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Because free neutrons decay due to external perturbations which is absent in a triton nucleus.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Triton energy is less than that of a  nucleus.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both the neutrons in triton have to decay simultaneously resulting in a nucleus with 3 protons, which is not a $H{e^3}$ nucleus.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Tritium  $ \\to {}_1{H^3}.$ The nucleus contain 1 proton and 2 neutrons. If one neutron decays  $n \\to p + {e^ - } + \\overline v ,$ the nucleus will have 2 protons and one neutron, i.e., tritium will transform into ${}_2H{e^3}.$ This does not happen because triton energy is less than that of  ${}_2H{e^3}$ nucleus, i.e., transformation is not allowed energetically.',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,'NCERT Exemplar','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45716,'physics',40,239,478,'The magnetic moment $\\left( {{m_{orb{\\rm{ }}}}} \\right)$ of a revolving electron around the nucleus varies with the principal quantum number $(n)$ as','[{"text": "$m_{\\\\text {orb }} \\\\propto n^{2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$m_{\\\\text {orb }} \\\\propto \\\\dfrac{1}{n^{2}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$m_{\\\\text {orb }} \\\\propto \\\\dfrac{1}{n}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$m_{\\\\text {orb }} \\\\propto n$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'For revolving electron magnetic moment<br>$m_{o r b}=\\dfrac{e_{L}}{2 m_{e}}$<br>where $L$ is angular momentum and $m_{e}$ is mass of electron.<br>By Bohr theory, $L=n\\left(\\dfrac{h}{2 \\pi}\\right)$<br>$\\Rightarrow m_{o r b}=\\dfrac{e_{n} h}{4 \\pi m}$<br>$\\Rightarrow m_{o r b} \\propto n$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2022,'MHTCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45718,'physics',37,973,2268,'The reading of ammeter in the circuit is','[{"text": "$2\\\\,A$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$2.4\\\\,A$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "zero", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1.7\\\\,A$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'${X_L} = {X_C} = 5\\,\\Omega $<br>$\\Rightarrow V_{L}=V_{C}$<br>The net voltage across ${L}$ and ${C}$ combination is zero. Voltage drop across ${R=110 {~V}}$<br>${i=\\dfrac{110}{55}=2 {~A}}$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/37_45718_question_1.png'),
(45719,'physics',41,406,806,'The graph shows the number of particles  \\[{N_t}\\] emitted per second by a radioactive source as a function of time \\[t\\]. The relationship between \\[{N_t}\\] and \\[t\\] is (take \\[\\ln \\,20 = 3\\])<div class="matching-question"></div>','[{"text": "${N_t} = 1000{e^{ - 20t}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${N_t} = 20{e^{ - 20t}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${N_t} = 3{e^{ - 0.05t}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${N_t} = 20{e^{ - 0.05t}}$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'The graph can be represented by the linear equation as <br>$\\frac{{\\ln {N_t} - 3}}{{t - 0}} = \\frac{{0 - 3}}{{60 - 0}}$<br>$ \\Rightarrow \\quad \\ln {N_t} - \\ln 20 =  - \\frac{1}{{20}}t$  $\\left( {{{\\ln }_e}20 \\approx 3} \\right)$<br>$ \\Rightarrow \\quad \\ln \\frac{{{N_t}}}{{20}} =  - 0.05t$<br>Hence,${N_t} = 20{e^{ - 0.05t}}$',NULL,'Medium','Graph/Figure',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/41_45719_question_1.png'),
(45729,'physics',41,406,806,'Radioactive element decays to form a stable nuclide, then the rate of decay of reactant  \\[\\left| {\\frac{{dN}}{{dt}}} \\right|\\] will vary with time (\\[t\\]) as shown in figure<div class="matching-question"></div>','[{"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'$N = {N_o}{e^{ - \\lambda t}}$<br>$\\frac{{dN}}{{dt}} =  - {N_o}\\lambda {e^{ - \\lambda t}}$<br>i.e., Rate of decay  $\\left| {\\frac{{dN}}{{dt}}} \\right|$ varies exponentially with time ($t$).',NULL,'Medium','Graph/Figure',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45720,'physics',40,239,478,'Assuming the atom is in the ground state, the expression for the magnetic field at a point nucleus in hydrogen atom due to circular motion of elecrton is<br>[ $\\mu_{0} \\rightarrow$ permeability of free space,<br>$m \\rightarrow$ mass of electrons]<br>$\\left[\\epsilon_{0} \\rightarrow\\right.$ permittivity of free space,<br>$h \\rightarrow$ planck\'s constant]','[{"text": "$\\\\dfrac{\\\\mu_{0} e^{3} \\\\pi m^{2}}{8 \\\\varepsilon_{0}{ }^{2} h^{4}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{\\\\mu_{0} e^{2} \\\\pi m^{4}}{6 \\\\varepsilon_{0}{ }^{3} h^{4}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{\\\\mu_{0} e^{7} \\\\pi m^{2}}{8 \\\\varepsilon_{0}{ }^{3} h^{5}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{\\\\mu_{0} e^{3} \\\\pi m^{3}}{6 \\\\varepsilon_{0}{ }^{3} h^{3}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'<img src=\'/img/data/physics/40_45720_explanation_1.jpg\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />  $B = \\frac{{{\\mu _0}i}}{{2r}}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\left( 1 \\right)$<br>$i = \\frac{{ev}}{{2\\pi r}}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\left( 2 \\right)$<br>From (1) and (2)<br>$ \\Rightarrow B = \\frac{{{\\mu _0}ev}}{{4\\pi {r^2}}}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\left( 3 \\right)$<br>$F=\\dfrac{m v^{2}}{r}=\\dfrac{1}{4 \\pi \\varepsilon_{0}} \\dfrac{e^{2}}{r^{2}}$<br>$ \\Rightarrow v = {\\left( {\\frac{{{e^2}}}{{4\\pi {\\varepsilon _0}mr}}} \\right)^{1/2}}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\left( 4 \\right)$<br>We know $m v r=\\dfrac{n h}{2 \\pi}$ (by Bohr theory)<br>$\\Rightarrow m\\left(\\dfrac{e^{2}}{4 \\pi \\varepsilon_{0} m r}\\right)^{1 / 2} r=\\dfrac{n h}{2 \\pi}$<br>$\\Rightarrow \\dfrac{m^{2} e^{2}}{4 \\pi \\varepsilon_{0} m r} r^{2}=\\dfrac{n^{2} h^{2}}{4 \\pi}$<br>$\\Rightarrow r=\\dfrac{n^{2} h^{2} \\varepsilon_{0}}{\\pi m e^{2}}$<br>From eqn (4)<br>$v=\\sqrt{\\dfrac{e^{2}}{\\left(4 \\pi \\varepsilon_{0} m\\right)\\left(\\dfrac{n^{2} h^{2} \\varepsilon_{0}}{\\pi m e^{2}}\\right)}}$<br>$v=\\dfrac{e^{2}}{2 n \\varepsilon_{0} h}$<br>From eqn (3)<br>$B=\\dfrac{\\mu_{0} e}{4 \\pi r^{2}} \\cdot \\dfrac{e^{2}}{2 n \\varepsilon_{0} h}$<br>$B = \\frac{{{\\mu _0}{e^3} \\times {\\pi ^2}{m^2}{e^4}}}{{4\\pi \\,2n{\\varepsilon _0}h{n^2}{h^4}\\varepsilon _0^2}}$<br>$\\Rightarrow B=\\dfrac{\\mu_{0} e^{7} m^{2} \\pi}{8 \\varepsilon_{0}^{3} h^{5}}$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',2022,'MHTCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45721,'physics',39,1104,2631,'The de Broglie wavelength of an electron moving with a velocity of $2 \\times 10^{8} {~m} {~s}^{-1}$ is equal to that of a photon. Find the ratio of the kinetic energy of the photon to that of the electron.','[{"text": "1", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "5", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "7", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "3", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Speed of photon  $\\left( c \\right) = 3 \\times {10^8}\\,m{s^{ - 1}}.$<br> Let $\\lambda$ be the wavelength of the photon.<br>$\\therefore \\lambda=\\dfrac{{h}}{{mc}}$ also, $\\lambda_{{e}}=\\dfrac{{h}}{{m}_{{e}} {v}}$ and $\\lambda=\\lambda_{{e}}$<br>Now, $\\frac{{{\\rm{ K}}{\\rm{.E}}{\\rm{. of \\,photon }}}}{{{\\rm{ K}}{\\rm{.E}}{\\rm{. of\\, electron }}}} = \\frac{{hv}}{{(1/2){m_e}{v^2}}}$<br>$ = \\frac{{2hc}}{{{m_e}{v^2}\\lambda }}$<br>$ = \\frac{{2c}}{v}\\quad \\,\\,\\,\\,\\left( {\\because {\\lambda _e} = \\frac{h}{{{m_e}v}}} \\right)$<br>$ = \\frac{{2 \\times 3 \\times {{10}^8}}}{{2 \\times {{10}^8}}} = 3$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45722,'physics',37,973,2265,'The current in the shown circuit is found to be $4\\sin \\left( {314t - \\frac{\\pi }{4}} \\right)\\,A$. Find the value of inductance.','[{"text": "${1 H}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${2 H}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${3 H}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${4 H}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'${\\tan \\phi=\\dfrac{\\omega L}{R} \\Rightarrow L=\\dfrac{R \\tan \\theta}{\\omega}}$<br>${=\\dfrac{314 \\times \\tan 45^{\\circ}}{314}=1 H}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/37_45722_question_1.png'),
(45723,'physics',41,406,806,'The fraction \\[f\\] of radioactive material that has decayed in time \\[t\\], varies with time \\[t\\]. The correct variation is given by the curve<div class="matching-question"></div>','[{"text": "$A$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$B$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$C$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$D$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Number of atoms undecayed $N = {N_o}{e^{ - \\lambda t}}$ number of atoms decayed <br>$ = {N_o} - N = {N_o}(1 - {e^{ - \\lambda t}})$<br>$ \\Rightarrow $  Fraction of atoms that got decayed in time $t$ is <br>$f = \\frac{{{N_o} - N}}{{{N_o}}} = 1 - {e^{ - \\lambda t}}$<br>i.e., fraction will rise up to 1, following exponential path as shown for curve ($B$).',NULL,'Medium','Graph/Figure',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/41_45723_question_1.png'),
(45724,'physics',40,240,479,'For wavelength of visible radiation of hydrogen spectrum Balmer gave an equation as $\\lambda  = \\frac{{\\left( {k{m^2}} \\right)}}{{\\left( {{m^2} - 4} \\right)}}$, where \'$m$\' is principal quantum number of energy level. The value of $k$ in terms of Rydberg\'s constant $R$ is','[{"text": "$\\\\dfrac{4}{R}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{R}{4}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$4 R$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$R$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Balmer series wavelength is given by,<br>$\\frac{1}{\\lambda } = R\\left( {\\frac{1}{2} - \\frac{1}{{{m^2}}}} \\right)\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\left( 1 \\right)$<br>But, by data,<br>$\\lambda=\\dfrac{k m^{2}}{\\left(m^{2}-4\\right)}$<br>using value of \' $\\lambda$ \' in equation (1), we get<br>$\\Rightarrow \\dfrac{m^{2}-4}{k m^{2}}=R\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2^{2}}-\\dfrac{1}{m^{2}}\\right)$<br>$\\Rightarrow \\dfrac{m^{2}-4}{k m^{2}}=R\\left(\\dfrac{m^{2}-4}{4 m^{2}}\\right)$<br>$\\Rightarrow k=\\dfrac{4}{R}$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',2022,'MHTCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45725,'physics',41,404,803,'The above is a plot of binding energy per nucleon \\[{E_b},\\] against the nuclear mass \\[M;A,\\,B,\\,C,\\,D,\\,E\\,\\& \\,F\\] correspond to different nuclei. Consider four reactions<br>\\[(i)A + B \\to C + \\varepsilon \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,(ii)C \\to A + B + \\varepsilon \\]<br>\\[(iii){\\rm{ }}D + E \\to F + \\varepsilon {\\rm{ }}\\,\\,(iv){\\rm{ }}F \\to D + E + \\varepsilon \\]<br>Where \\[\\varepsilon \\] is the energy released? In which reactions is \\[\\varepsilon \\] positive?<div class="matching-question"></div>','[{"text": "(i) and (iv)", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "(i) and (iii)", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "(ii) and (iv)", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "(ii) and (iii)", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'${I^{st}}$ reaction is fusion and $I{V^{th}}$ fission.',NULL,'Medium','Graph/Figure',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/41_45725_question_1.png'),
(45726,'physics',37,973,2265,'The emf ${E=4 \\cos 1000 t}$ volts is applied to an ${L R}$ circuit, containing inductance of $3\\,mH$ and resistance of ${4 \\Omega}$. The amplitude of current is','[{"text": "${4 \\\\sqrt{7} A}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1.0\\\\,A$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\dfrac{4}{7} {~A}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$0.8\\\\,A$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'$ = \\sqrt {{R^2} + {\\omega ^2}{L^2}} $<br>$ = \\sqrt {{{(4)}^2} + {{\\left( {1000 \\times 3 \\times {{10}^{ - 3}}} \\right)}^2}} $<br>$=5 \\Omega$<br>${E=4 \\cos (1000 t)}$<br>${\\Rightarrow \\omega=1000 {rad} / {s}}$, and, ${E_{o}=4 {~V}}$<br>current, ${I=\\dfrac{V}{Z}=\\dfrac{4}{5}=0.8 {~A}}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45728,'physics',37,975,2276,'An $LCR$ series circuit is at resonance with $10\\,V$, each across ${L, C}$, and ${R}$. If the resistance is halved the respective voltages across ${L, C}$, and ${R}$ are','[{"text": "${10 {~V}, 10 {~V}}$, and $10\\\\,V$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${20 {~V}, 20 {~V}}$, and $5\\\\,V$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${20 {~V}, 20 {~V}}$, and $10\\\\,V$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${5 {~V}, 5 {~V}}$, and $5\\\\,V$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'${V_{L}=V_{C} \\Rightarrow X_{L}=X_{C}}$<br>${i_{\\text {max }}=\\dfrac{V}{R}}$<br>If ${R}$ is halved, then current will be doubled and ${V_{L}=V_{C}=2(10)=20 {~V}}$<br>${V_{R}=I R=2 I \\times \\dfrac{R}{2}=I R=V_{R}=10 {~V}}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(48394,'biology',101,575,1154,'Intentional or voluntary termination of pregnancy before full term is called________','[{"text": "Medical transfer of pregnancy", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Medical terminal pregnancy", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Medical Termination of Pregnancy", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "None of these", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Intentional or voluntary termination of pregnancy before full term is called MTP',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45731,'physics',40,240,479,'The transition from the state $n=4$ to $n=3$ in a hydrogen like atom results in ultraviolet radiation. Infrared radiation will be obtained in the transition from','[{"text": "$2 \\\\rightarrow 1$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$3 \\\\rightarrow 2$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$4 \\\\rightarrow 2$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$5 \\\\rightarrow 4$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'<img src=\'/img/data/physics/40_45731_explanation_1.jpg\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />  For $n_{i} \\rightarrow n_{f}$, we know<br>$\\Delta E \\propto\\left[\\dfrac{1}{n_{i}^{2}}-\\dfrac{1}{n_{f}^{2}}\\right]$<br>For $4 \\rightarrow 3$, it is given as $U V$<br>$(\\Delta E)_{U V} \\propto\\left[\\dfrac{1}{4^{2}}-\\dfrac{1}{3^{2}}\\right]$<br>$=(0.11) K$ where $K$ is constant<br>We know frequency of UV radiation is more than that of IR radiation<br>$\\Rightarrow$ The correct choice will have value of<br>$(\\Delta E)$ less than 0.11 .<br>$\\Rightarrow$ Calculate $(\\Delta E)$ as below for each choice<br>For $2 \\rightarrow 1,(\\Delta E) \\propto\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2^{2}}-\\dfrac{1}{1^{2}}\\right)$<br>$\\Rightarrow(\\Delta E)=(0.75) K$ which is greater than<br>$(\\Delta E)$ for $U V$<br>Hence, $2 \\rightarrow 1$, is not the correct choice<br>For $3 \\rightarrow 2$, we have $(\\Delta E) \\propto\\left(\\dfrac{1}{3^{2}}-\\dfrac{1}{2^{2}}\\right)$<br>$\\Delta E=(0.14) K$<br>$>(\\Delta E)_{U V}$<br>$\\Rightarrow 3 \\rightarrow 2$, is not the correct choice<br>For $4 \\rightarrow 2$, we have $(\\Delta E)=(0.187) K$<br>$>(\\Delta E)_{U V}$<br>For $5 \\rightarrow 4$, we have $(\\Delta E) \\propto\\left(\\dfrac{1}{5^{2}}-\\dfrac{1}{4^{2}}\\right)$<br>$=(0.0225) K$<br>$ < (\\Delta E)_{U V}$<br>$\\Rightarrow(5 \\rightarrow 4)$ is the correct transition.',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',2021,'MHTCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45732,'physics',37,975,2275,'In a series resonant ${L C R}$ circuit, if ${L}$ is increased by ${25 \\%}$ and ${C}$ is decreased by ${20 \\%}$, then the resonant frequency will','[{"text": "increase by ${10 \\\\%}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "decrease by ${10 \\\\%}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "remain unchanged", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "increase by ${2.5 \\\\%}$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'We know ${v_{0}=\\dfrac{1}{2 \\pi \\sqrt{L C}}}$<br>${L^{\\prime}=L+25 \\%}$ of ${L=L+\\dfrac{L}{4}=\\dfrac{5 L}{4} ;}$<br>${C^{\\prime}=C-20 \\%}$ of ${C=C-\\dfrac{C}{5}=\\dfrac{4 C}{5}}$<br>Hence<br>$v_0^\\prime  = \\frac{1}{{2\\pi \\sqrt {{L^\\prime }{C^\\prime }} }} = \\frac{1}{{2\\pi \\sqrt {\\frac{{5L}}{4} \\times \\frac{{4C}}{5}} }}$<br>$ = \\frac{1}{{2\\pi \\sqrt {LC} }} = {v_0}$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45733,'physics',41,409,815,'A proton of mass \\[m\\] collides with  \\[H{e^ + }\\] ion present in ground state. If  \\[{E_a}\\] is the excitation energy of \\[H{e^ + }\\] ion, then the graph between threshold energy of proton and its mass is<div class="matching-question"></div>','[{"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'The threshold energy is <br>${E_{Th}} = {E_a}\\left[ {1 + \\frac{m}{M}} \\right]$<br>$M$- mass of the $H{e^ + }$<br>$m$ - mass of photon',NULL,'Hard','Graph/Figure',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45736,'physics',41,406,806,'A radioactive sample undergoes decay as per the following graph. At time \\[t = 0\\], the number of undecayed nuclei is  \\[{N_o}.\\] Calculate the number of nuclei left after \\[1\\,h\\].<div class="matching-question"></div>','[{"text": "${N_o}/{e^8}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${N_o}/{e^{10}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${N_o}/{e^{12}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${N_o}/{e^{14}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'At $t = 0,N = {N_o}$<br>At $t = 3.47,N = {N_o}/2$<br>${N_o}/2$  is the sample left after one half-life<br>$\\therefore \\quad {t_{1/2}} = 3.47\\min $<br>$ \\Rightarrow \\,\\frac{{0.693}}{\\lambda } = 3.47\\,\\min \\, \\Rightarrow \\,\\lambda  = 0.2\\, {\\rm {min} ^{ - 1}}$<br>$ \\Rightarrow At\\,t = 60\\min $<br>$\\therefore \\quad N = {N_o}{e^{ - \\lambda t}} = {N_o}{e^{ - 0.2 \\times 60}} = \\frac{{{N_o}}}{{{e^{12}}}}$',NULL,'Medium','Graph/Figure',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/41_45736_question_1.png'),
(45737,'physics',37,973,2268,'In the circuit shown, resistance ${R=100 \\Omega}$, inductance ${L=\\dfrac{2}{\\pi} H}$, and capacitance ${C=\\dfrac{8}{\\pi} \\mu F}$ are connected in series with an ac source of 200 volt and frequency ${f}$. If the readings of the hot wire voltmeters ${V_{1}}$ and ${V_{2}}$ are same, then choose the incorrect option.','[{"text": "${f=125 {~Hz}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${f=250 \\\\pi {Hz}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Current through ${R}$ is ${2 A}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${V_{1}=V_{2}=1000}$ volts", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'${V_{1}=V_{2} \\Rightarrow X_{C}=X_{L}}$<br>${\\Rightarrow f=\\dfrac{1}{2 \\pi \\sqrt{L C}}=125 {~Hz}}$<br>${I_{0}=\\dfrac{V_{0}}{R}=\\dfrac{200}{100}\\left(Z=R\\right.}$ as ${\\left.X_{C}=X_{L}\\right)=2 {~A}}$<br>${V_1} = {V_2} = i{X_L} = i \\times (\\omega L)$<br>$ = 2 \\times 2\\pi  \\times 125 \\times 2/\\pi $<br>${=1000}$ volts. So incorrect option is (2).',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/37_45737_question_1.png'),
(45740,'physics',37,973,2268,'In the given ac circuit, which of the following is incorrect?','[{"text": "Voltage across resistance is lagging by ${90^{\\\\circ}}$ than the voltage across capacitor.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "voltage across capacitor is lagging by ${180^{\\\\circ}}$ than voltage across inductor.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "voltage across inductor is leading by ${90^{\\\\circ}}$ than voltage across resistance.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Resistance of the circuit is not equal to reactance of circuit.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Since the circuit is at resonance, the current in the circuit is in the phase with applied voltage. Voltage across inductor leads the current by ${\\pi / 2}$ and across a capacitor lags by ${\\pi / 2}$. So, the voltage across resistance is lagging by ${90^{\\circ}}$ than the voltage across capacitor.',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/37_45740_question_1.png'),
(45741,'physics',41,406,806,'The \\[\\ln N - t\\] curve for radioactive material is<div class="matching-question"></div>','[{"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'We know that<br>$N = {N_o}{e^{ - \\lambda t}}$<br>$\\ln N = \\ln \\left( {{N_o}{e^{ - \\lambda t}}} \\right) = \\ln {N_o} + \\ln {e^{ - \\lambda t}}$<br>$\\ln N = \\ln {N_o} - \\lambda t$<br>The graph between ln $N$ and $t$ is a straight line whose slope is $ - \\lambda $',NULL,'Medium','Graph/Figure',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45742,'physics',39,1102,2629,'Light of two different frequencies whose photons have energies $1\\,eV$ and $2.5\\,eV$ respectively, successively illuminates a metal of work function $0.5\\,eV$. If the maximum kinetic energies of emitted electron is ${K_{1}}$ and ${K_{2}}$ respectively. The ratio ${\\dfrac{K_{2}}{K_{1}}}$ is','[{"text": "$\\\\frac{3}{2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{1}{4}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{1}{6}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{4}{3}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'By using ${E=W_{0}+K_{\\max } \\Rightarrow K_{\\max }=E-W_{0}}$<br>Hence, ${K_{1}=1-0.5=0.5}$<br>and ${\\quad K_{2}=2.5-0.5=2 \\Rightarrow \\dfrac{K_{1}}{K_{2}}=\\dfrac{1}{4}}$.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45743,'physics',41,406,806,'A radioactive sample has \\[{N_o}\\] active atoms at \\[t = 0.\\] If the rate of disintegration at any time is \\[R\\] and the number of atoms is \\[N\\], then the ratio \\[R/N\\] varies with time as<div class="matching-question"></div>','[{"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Rate $R =  - \\frac{{dN}}{{dt}} = \\lambda {N_o}{e^{ - \\lambda t}} = \\lambda N$<br>$ \\Rightarrow \\frac{R}{N} = \\lambda $ (constant)<br>i.e., graph between  $\\frac{R}{N}$ and $t$, be a straight line parallel to the time axis.',NULL,'Medium','Graph/Figure',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45744,'physics',37,973,2268,'In the given circuit, the reading of voltmeter ${V_{1}}$ and ${V_{2}}$ are $300\\,V$ each. The reading of the voltmeter ${V_{3}}$ and ammeter ${A}$ respectively are','[{"text": "${100 {~V}, 2.0 {~A}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${150 {~V}, 2.2 {~A}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${220 {~V}, 2.2 {~A}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${220 {~V}, 2.0 {~A}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'<img src=\'/img/data/physics/37_45744_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' /> ${=V_{R}^{2}+\\left(V_{L}-V_{C}\\right)^{2}}$<br>${V=\\sqrt{V_{R}^{2}+\\left(V_{L}-V_{C}\\right)^{2}}}$<br>${V=\\sqrt{V_{R}^{2}} \\quad\\left(\\therefore V_{L}=V_{C}\\right)}$<br>${\\Rightarrow \\quad V=220 {~V}}$<br>Also, ${i=\\dfrac{V}{R}}$<br>$ = \\frac{{220}}{{100}}$<br>$ = 2.2\\,A$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/37_45744_question_1.png'),
(45746,'physics',40,239,478,'$15\\,eV$ is given to electron in 4 th orbit, then find its final energy when it comes out of $H$-atom','[{"text": "$14.15\\\\,eV$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$13.6\\\\,eV$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$12.08\\\\,eV$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$15.85\\\\,eV$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Given, total energy given to $e^{-}$in 4 th orbit, $E = 15\\,eV$<br>Energy of $n t h$ orbit of $H$-atom,<br>$E^{\\prime}=-\\dfrac{13.6}{n^{2}}$<br>So, energy of 4 th orbit of $H$-atom,<br>$E\' = \\frac{{ - 13.6}}{{{4^2}}} =  - 0.85\\,eV$<br>$\\therefore$ Ionisation energy of 4 th orbit $=-E^{\\prime}$<br>$ = ( - 0.85\\,eV)$<br>$ = 0.85\\,eV$<br>$\\therefore$ Final energy of electron, when it comes out of $H$-atom,<br>$E=$ Total energy given to electron - Ionisation energy of 4th orbit,<br>$ = 15 - 0.85 = 14.15\\,eV$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',2019,'AIIMS','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45747,'physics',37,973,2265,'A coil has resistance ${30 \\Omega}$ and inductive reactance $20\\,Ohm$ at $50\\,Hz$ frequency. If an ac source of $200\\,V$ and $100\\,Hz$ is connected across the coil, the current in the coil will be','[{"text": "$2.0\\\\,A$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$4.0\\\\,A$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$8.0\\\\,A$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\dfrac{20}{\\\\sqrt{13}} {~A}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Total resistance of coil<br>$=30+20=50 \\Omega$<br>$i=\\dfrac{V}{\\text { Resistance }} \\Rightarrow i=\\dfrac{200}{50} \\quad i=4 {~A}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45748,'physics',41,406,806,'A radioactive element \\[A\\] decay into stable element \\[B\\], initially a fresh sample of \\[A\\] is available. In this sample variation in number of nuclei of  \\[B\\] with time is shown by<div class="matching-question"></div>','[{"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'The concentration of $A$ is given by $N = {N_o}{e^{ - \\lambda t}}$<br>The concentration of $B$ is ${N_B} = {N_o} - {N_o}{e^{ - \\lambda t}}$',NULL,'Medium','Graph/Figure',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45749,'physics',39,1102,2629,'In the figure shown electromagnetic radiations of wavelength $200\\,nm$ are incident on the metallic plate ${A}$. The photo electrons are accelerated by a potential difference $10\\,V$. These electrons strike another metal plate ${B}$ from which electromagnetic radiations are emitted. The minimum wavelength of the emitted photons is $100\\,nm$. If the work function of the metal \' ${A}$ \' is found to be $1.9\\,NeV$. The value of ${N}$ is ____ . Use $hv = 12400\\,eV$ $ \\mathop A^{~~\\circ} $, use $Rch = 13.6\\,eV.$','[{"text": "$2.5\\\\,eV$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$5.2\\\\,eV$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1.8\\\\,eV$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$3.8\\\\,eV$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Energy of incident photons ${=\\dfrac{12400}{2000} {eV}}$.<br>Maximum kinetic energy of ejected electrons ${=\\dfrac{12400}{2000}-\\phi}$<br>Maximum kinetic energy of electrons striking plate<br>$B=\\dfrac{12400}{2000}-\\phi+10$<br>Minimum wavelength of photons emitted from<br>$B = \\frac{{12400}}{{1000}}$<br>$\\therefore \\,\\,\\,{\\mkern 1mu} {\\kern 1pt} {\\rm{ }}\\frac{{12400}}{{2000}} - \\phi  + 10 = \\frac{{12400}}{{1000}}\\therefore \\phi  = 3.8\\,eV$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/39_45749_question_1.png'),
(47139,'biology',102,772,1677,'How long did the earth exist before life appeared on it?','[{"text": "500 million years ago", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "1 billion years ago", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "10 billion years ago", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "4 billion years ago", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'fossil evidence The age of Earth is about 4.54 billion years; the earliest undisputed evidence of life on Earth dates from at least 3.5 billion years ago.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45750,'physics',40,239,478,'The energy $E$ of a hydrogen atom with principal quantum number $n$ is given by $E = \\frac{{13.6}}{{{n^2}}}eV$. The energy of a photon ejected when the electron jumps from $n=3$ state to $n=2$ state of hydrogen is approximately','[{"text": "$1.5\\\\,eV$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$0.85\\\\,eV$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$3.3\\\\,eV$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1.9\\\\,eV$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'The energy of ejected photon,<br>$E=R h c\\left(\\dfrac{1}{n_{1}^{2}}-\\dfrac{1}{n_{2}^{2}}\\right)$<br>where, transistion takes place from $n_{1}$ to $n_{2}$.<br>$ = 13.6\\left( {\\frac{1}{{{2^2}}} - \\frac{1}{{{3^2}}}} \\right)$<br>$ = 13.6 \\times \\frac{5}{{36}} = 1.9\\,eV$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2013,'AIIMS','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45751,'physics',37,974,2272,'In an ac circuit, the current is given by ${i=5 \\sin (100 t-\\pi / 2)}$ and the ac potential is ${V=200 \\sin (100) t}$, the power consumption is','[{"text": "$20\\\\,W$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$40\\\\,W$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$100\\\\,W$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$0\\\\,W$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Phase angle between ${V}$ and ${I}$ is ${90^{\\circ}}$.<br>$ < P >  = {V_{{\\rm{rms }}}}{I_{{\\rm{rms }}}}\\cos \\frac{\\pi }{2} = 0$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45752,'physics',41,409,815,'Consider a nuclear reaction in which the target particle is ${}_9{F^{19}}$ and it is at rest initially. A fast moving neutron ${}_0{n^1}$ collides with ${}_9{F^{19}}.$ If $Q$ value of the reaction is $4\\,MeV$ then find the threshold energy of the neutron.','[{"text": "$2.0\\\\,MeV$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$8.0\\\\,MeV$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$4.0\\\\,MeV$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$4.2\\\\,MeV$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'${E_{Th}} = Q\\left[ {1 + \\frac{m}{M}} \\right]$<br>we can approximate that<br>$m = {m_n} = 1\\,amu$<br>$M = {m_F} = 19\\,amu$<br>Given that<br>$Q = 4MeV$<br>${E_{Th}} = 4MeV\\left[ {1 + \\frac{1}{{19}}} \\right] = 4.2MeV$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45754,'physics',37,974,2272,'The rms current in an ac circuit is ${2 A}$. If the wattless current be ${\\sqrt{3} A}$. What is power factor?','[{"text": "${\\\\dfrac{1}{\\\\sqrt{3}}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\dfrac{1}{\\\\sqrt{2}}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\dfrac{1}{2}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${\\\\dfrac{1}{3}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'${I_{W L}=I_{r m s} \\sin \\phi \\Rightarrow \\sqrt{3}=2 \\sin \\phi}$<br>${\\Rightarrow \\sin \\phi=\\dfrac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\Rightarrow \\phi=60^{\\circ}}$<br>Power factor, ${\\cos \\phi=\\cos 60^{\\circ}=\\dfrac{1}{2}}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL);
INSERT INTO `qb_questions` (`id`, `subject_id`, `chapter_id`, `topic_id`, `subtopic_id`, `question_html`, `options`, `correct_index`, `explanation`, `explanation_image_url`, `difficulty`, `qtype`, `year`, `tag`, `created_at`, `question_image_url`) VALUES
(45755,'physics',41,409,815,'Consider a nuclear reaction with target particle of mass $2m$ which is present at rest. The mass of  moving particle is $m$.  The energy required for the reaction to take place is $4MeV$. If the moving particle carries an energy of $5MeV$ then the nature of collision is','[{"text": "Elastic", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Inelastic", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Partially elastic", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Information is insufficient.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'${E_{Th}} = Q\\left[ {1 + \\frac{m}{{2m}}} \\right] = 4\\left[ {\\frac{3}{2}} \\right]$<br>${E_{Th}} = 6MeV$<br>As  $E = 5\\,MeV$ so reaction does not take place and the collision is elastic, since the $K$.$E$ of system does not convert into another form.',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45756,'physics',40,240,479,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> It is essential that all the lines available in the emission spectrum will also be available in the absorption spectrum.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> The spectrum of hydrogen atom is only absorption spectrum.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are incorrect.", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'${\\rm{Assertion:}}$ It is not essential that all the lines available in the emission spectrum will also be available in the absorption spectrum.<br>${\\rm{Reason:}}$ The spectrum of hydrogen atom may not be absorption spectrum.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',2009,'AIIMS','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45758,'physics',37,974,2766,'In series LR circuit, ${X_{L}=3 R}$. Now, a capacitor with ${X_{C}=R}$ is added in series, the ratio of new to old power factor is','[{"text": "${\\\\sqrt{2}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${\\\\dfrac{1}{\\\\sqrt{2}}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "2", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "1", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Old power factor ${(\\cos \\phi)=\\dfrac{R}{Z}=\\dfrac{R}{\\sqrt{R^{2}+X_{L}^{2}}}=\\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}}$<br>$\\left(\\because X_{L}=3 R \\text { and } X_{C}=R\\right)$<br>New power factor<br>$\\left( {\\cos {\\phi ^\\prime }} \\right) = \\frac{R}{{{Z^\\prime }}} = \\frac{R}{{\\sqrt {{R^2} + {{\\left( {{X_L} - {X_C}} \\right)}^2}} }} = \\frac{1}{{\\sqrt 5 }}$<br>$ \\Rightarrow \\frac{{\\cos \\phi }}{{\\cos {\\phi ^\\prime }}} = \\sqrt 2 $',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45759,'physics',41,408,814,'Consider fusion of $He$ plasma. At what temperature fusion at a distance $2\\,fm$ is possible?','[{"text": "$4.45 \\\\times {10^{10}}K$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$4.23 \\\\times {10^5}K$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2.23 \\\\times {10^3}K$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "None of these", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Let $T$ is the temperature at which the particles have enough kinetic energy to come close.<br>The kinetic energy of a $He$ particle at absolute temperature $T$ is $\\frac{3}{2}KT.$ When these particles come close the $K$.$E$ converts into $P$.$E$.<br>$2 \\times \\frac{3}{2}kT = \\frac{{{{\\left( {2e} \\right)}^2}}}{{4\\pi {\\varepsilon _0}r}}$<br>$ \\Rightarrow T = \\frac{{8 \\times {{\\left( {1.6 \\times {{10}^{ - 19}}} \\right)}^2} \\times 9 \\times {{10}^9}}}{{6 \\times 1.38 \\times {{10}^{ - 23}} \\times 2 \\times {{10}^{ - 15}}}}$<br>$ = 4.45 \\times {10^{10}}K$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45761,'physics',37,974,2274,'In a series ${L R}$ circuit, the voltage drop across inductor is $8\\,V$ and across resistor is $6\\,V$ . The voltage applied and power factor of circuit respectively are','[{"text": "${14 V, 0.8}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${10 {~V}, 0.8}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${10 {~V}, 0.6}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${14 V, 0.6}$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'${V_{L}=8 V, V_{R}=6 V}$,<br>${V=\\sqrt{V_{L}^{2}+V_{R}^{2}}=10 V}$<br>Power factor, ${\\cos \\phi=\\dfrac{V_{R}}{V}=\\dfrac{6}{10}=0.6}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45784,'physics',37,977,2770,'The output voltage of an ideal transformer, connected to a $240\\,V$ ac mains is $24\\,V$. When this transformer is used to light a bulb with rating ${24 {~V}, 24 {~W}}$, calculate the current in the primary coil of the circuit.','[{"text": "$0.1\\\\,A$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$0.2\\\\,A$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$0.5\\\\,A$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$0.4\\\\,A$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'${E_{P}=240 {~V}, E_{S}=24 {~V}}$,<br>$E_{S} I_{S}=24 {~W}$<br>Current in primary coil ${I_{P}=\\dfrac{E_{S} I_{S}}{E_{P}}=\\dfrac{24}{240}=0.1 {~A}}$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45762,'physics',41,408,814,'Assuming that about $20\\,MeV$ of energy is released per fusion reaction<br>${}_1{H^2} + {}_1{H^2} \\to {}_0{n^1} + {}_2H{e^3}$<br>Then the mass of ${}_1{H^2}$ consumed per day in a fusion reactor of power 1 megawatt will 	approximately be','[{"text": "$0.18\\\\,g$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$0.001\\\\,g$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$10.0\\\\,g$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1000\\\\,g$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'$P = {10^6}{\\rm{Watt}}$<br>1 day $ = {\\rm{24}} \\times {\\rm{36}} \\times {\\rm{1}}{{\\rm{0}}^2}\\sec $<br>Energy produced<br>$U = 24 \\times 36 \\times {10^8}{\\rm{joule}}$<br>Energy released per fusion reaction<br>$ = 20\\,MeV = {\\rm{32}} \\times {\\rm{1}}{{\\rm{0}}^{ - 13}}{\\rm{joule}}$<br>Number of ${}_1{H^2}$  atoms used<br>$ = \\frac{{2 \\times 24 \\times 36 \\times {{10}^8}}}{{32 \\times {{10}^{ - 13}}}} = 54 \\times {10^{21}}$<br>Mass of $6 \\times {10^{23}}$ atoms $ = 2g$<br>$\\therefore $ Mass of $54 \\times {10^{21}}$ atoms<br>$ = \\frac{2}{{6 \\times {{10}^{23}}}} \\times 54 \\times {10^{21}} = 0.18g$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45763,'physics',40,239,478,'The energy of hydrogen atom in ground state is $13.6\\,eV$ The ionisation energy of singly ionised helium $H{e^{2 + }}$ will be','[{"text": "$13.6\\\\,eV$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$3.4\\\\,eV$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$27.2\\\\,eV$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$54.4\\\\,eV$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Energy of ionised $H{e^{2 + }}$,<br>$E = \\left( {{E_0}} \\right){Z^2} = (13.6)(4) = 54.4\\,eV$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2009,'AIIMS','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45765,'physics',37,974,2274,'A resistance ${R}$ draws power ${P}$ when connected to an ac source. If an inductance is now placed in series with the resistance, such that the impedance of the circuit becomes ${Z}$, the power drawn will be','[{"text": "${P \\\\sqrt{\\\\dfrac{R}{Z}}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${P\\\\left(\\\\dfrac{R}{Z}\\\\right)}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${P}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${P\\\\left(\\\\dfrac{R}{Z}\\\\right)^{2}}$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'<img src=\'/img/data/physics/37_45765_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' /> ${\\Rightarrow V^{2}=P R ; \\quad P^{\\prime}=\\dfrac{V^{2}}{Z} \\cdot \\dfrac{R}{Z}}$ [From phasor diagram]<br>${\\Rightarrow P^{\\prime}=\\dfrac{(P R) R}{Z^{2}}}$<br>$P^{\\prime}=\\left(\\dfrac{R}{Z}\\right)^{2} P$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45766,'physics',41,408,812,'Assuming that about $200\\,MeV$ of energy is released per fission of ${}_{92}{{\\rm{U}}^{235}}$ nuclei, then the mass of  ${{\\rm{U}}^{235}}$ consumed per day in a fission reactor of power 1 megawatt will be approximately:','[{"text": "$10,000\\\\,g$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${10^{ - 2}}\\\\,g$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1\\\\,g$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$100\\\\,g$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Power $P$ of fission reaction,<br>$P = {10^6}{\\rm{watt}} = {10^6}{\\rm{joule /sec}}$<br>Time $ = t = 1{\\rm{day = }}\\,{\\rm{24}} \\times {\\rm{3600}}\\sec $<br>Energy produced , $U = Pt$<br>${\\rm{U}} = {10^6} \\times 24 \\times 3600 = 24 \\times 36 \\times {10^8}{\\rm{joule}}$<br>Energy released per fission of ${{\\rm{U}}^{235}}$<br>$ = 200MeV = 32 \\times {10^{ - 12}}\\,J$<br>Number of  ${U^{235}}$ atoms used<br>$ = \\frac{{24 \\times 36 \\times {{10}^8}}}{{32 \\times {{10}^{ - 12}}}} = 27 \\times {10^{20}}$<br>Mass of $6 \\times {10^{23}}$ atoms of  ${U^{235}} = 235{\\rm{g}}$<br>Mass of $27 \\times {10^{20}}$  atoms of  ${U^{235}}$<br>$ = \\left( {\\frac{{235}}{{6 \\times {{10}^{23}}}}} \\right)\\left( {27 \\times {{10}^{20}}} \\right) = 1g$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45767,'physics',40,239,478,'In the Bohr\'s model of a hydrogen atom, the centripetal force is furnished by the Coulomb attraction between the proton and the electron. If $a_{0}$ is the radius of the ground state orbit, $m$ is the mass, $e$ is the charge on electron and $\\varepsilon_{0}$ is the permittivity of free space, the speed of the electron is','[{"text": "$\\\\dfrac{e}{\\\\sqrt{\\\\varepsilon_{0} a_{0} m}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Zero", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{e}{\\\\sqrt{4 \\\\pi \\\\varepsilon_{0} a_{0} m}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{\\\\sqrt{4 \\\\varepsilon_{0} a_{0} m}}{e}$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'We can write the expression for the circular motion of electron around the nucleus<br>${F_{electrostatic{\\rm{ }}}} = {F_{centripetal{\\rm{ }}}}$<br>$ \\Rightarrow \\frac{1}{{4\\pi {\\varepsilon _0}}}\\frac{{{e^2}}}{{a_0^2}} = \\frac{{m{v^2}}}{{{a_0}}}$<br>$ \\Rightarrow {v^2} = \\frac{1}{{4\\pi {\\varepsilon _0}}}\\frac{{{e^2}}}{{m{a_0}}}$<br>$ \\Rightarrow v = \\frac{e}{{\\sqrt {4\\pi {\\varepsilon _0}{a_0}m} }}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2009,'AIIMS','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45769,'physics',37,977,2770,'In a step-up transformer, the ratio of turns of the primary and the secondary is ${1: 25}$. If the input voltage is $230\\,V$ and the current through the load is ${2 A}$, then the current in the primary coil is','[{"text": "$50\\\\,A$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$25\\\\,A$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$12.5\\\\,A$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$6.25\\\\,A$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'${\\dfrac{N_{p}}{N_{S}}=\\dfrac{I_{S}}{I_{P}} \\Rightarrow \\dfrac{1}{25}=\\dfrac{2}{I_{P}}}$<br>$I_{P}=50 {~A}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45770,'physics',41,408,813,'What is the power output of $_{92}{U^{235}}$ reactor if it takes 30 days to use up $2g$ of fuel and if each fission gives $185\\,MeV$ of usable energy?<br>Avogadro’ number $ = 6.02 \\times {10^{26}}mo{\\ell ^{ - 1}}$','[{"text": "$45\\\\,MW$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$58.46\\\\,MW$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$72\\\\,MW$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$92\\\\,MW$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Number of atoms in $2kg$ fuel<br>$\\frac{2}{{235}} \\times 6.02 \\times {10^{26}} = 5.12 \\times {10^2}$<br>Number of atoms fissioned in one second<br>$ = \\frac{{5.12 \\times {{10}^{24}}}}{{30 \\times 24 \\times 60 \\times 60}}$<br>$ = 1.975 \\times {10^{18}}{s^{ - 1}}$<br>Each fission gives $185\\,MeV$. Hence, energy obtained in one second,<br>$P = 185 \\times 1.975 \\times {10^{18}}MeV{s^{ - 1}}$<br>$ = 185 \\times 1.975 \\times {10^{18}} \\times 1.6 \\times {10^{ - 19}}j{s^{ - 1}}$<br>$ = 58.46$ Megawatt',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45771,'physics',39,1104,2631,'Calculate the ratio of the accelerating potential required to accelerate (i) a proton and (ii) an ${\\alpha}$-particle to have the same de-Broglie wavelength associated with them.<br>[Given: Mass of proton ${=1.6 \\times 10^{-27} {~kg}}$; Mass of ${\\alpha}$-particle ${\\left.=6.4 \\times 10^{-27} {~kg}\\right]}$','[{"text": "4", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "8", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "10", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "5", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'If a particle is accelerated through a potential difference $V$, then<br> $q V=\\dfrac{1}{2} m v^{2}=\\dfrac{p^{2}}{2 m}$ or $p=\\sqrt{2 m q V}$<br>$\\lambda=\\dfrac{h}{p}=\\dfrac{h}{\\sqrt{2 m q V}}$<br>As $\\lambda_{p}=\\lambda_{\\alpha}$<br> or $\\dfrac{h}{\\sqrt{2 m_{p} q_{p} V_{p}}}=\\dfrac{h}{\\sqrt{2 m_{a} q_{a} V_{\\sigma}}}$<br>or $\\dfrac{V_{p}}{V_{a}}=\\dfrac{m_{a} q_{a}}{m_{p} q_{p}}=\\dfrac{4 m_{p} \\cdot 2 e}{m_{p} \\cdot e}=8$.',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(48395,'biology',101,575,1154,'Nearly________ MTPs are preformed in a year all over the world.','[{"text": "45 to 50 million", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "45 to 50 billion", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "45 to 50 hundreds", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "45 to 50 lacs", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Nearly 45 to 50 million MTPs are preformed in a year all over the world.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45773,'physics',37,977,2771,'A transformer connected to $220\\,V$ line shows an output of $2\\,A$ at $11000\\,V$. The efficiency is ${100 \\%}$. The current drawn from the line is','[{"text": "$100\\\\,A$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$200\\\\,A$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$22\\\\,A$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$11\\\\,A$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'${\\eta \\%=\\dfrac{P_{\\text {out }}}{P_{\\text {in }}} \\times 100 \\% \\Rightarrow 100=\\dfrac{e_{2} I_{2}}{e_{1} I_{1}} \\times 100}$<br>$1=\\dfrac{11000 \\times 2}{220 \\times I_{1}} \\Rightarrow I_{1}=100 {~A}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45774,'physics',41,406,806,'A radioactive nuclide can decay simultaneously by two  different processes which have decay constants ${\\lambda _1}$ and ${\\lambda _2}.$ The effective decay constant of the nuclide is $\\lambda .$','[{"text": "$\\\\lambda  = \\\\frac{1}{2}({\\\\lambda _1} + {\\\\lambda _2})$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\lambda  = {\\\\lambda _1} + {\\\\lambda _2}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\lambda  = \\\\sqrt {{\\\\lambda _1}{\\\\lambda _2}} $", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{1}{\\\\lambda } = \\\\frac{1}{{{\\\\lambda _1}}} + \\\\frac{1}{{{\\\\lambda _2}}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'If $N$ is concentration of the nuclide at time $t$ then <br>$\\frac{{dN}}{{dt}} =  - {\\lambda _1}N - {\\lambda _2}N =  - \\left( {{\\lambda _1} + {\\lambda _2}} \\right)N =  - \\lambda N$<br>Effective decay constant $(\\lambda ) = \\left( {{\\lambda _1} + {\\lambda _2}} \\right)$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45776,'physics',41,406,806,'In a sample of rock there are two radioactive elements $A$ and $B$ having initial concentration ratio 1:4. If ${\\lambda _A}$ and ${\\lambda _B}$ are rate constants then the time at which the concentration of both $A$ and $B$ are equal.','[{"text": "$\\\\frac{{{\\\\lambda _A}{\\\\lambda _B}}}{{{\\\\lambda _B} - {\\\\lambda _A}}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{1}{{{\\\\lambda _B} - {\\\\lambda _A}}}{\\\\rm{ln}}2$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{1}{{{\\\\lambda _A} - {\\\\lambda _B}}}\\\\ln \\\\left( {\\\\frac{1}{4}} \\\\right)$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{1}{{{\\\\lambda _B} - {\\\\lambda _A}}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'The concentrations of $A$ and $B$ at time $t$ are ${N_A} = {N_{{0_A}}}{e^{ - {\\lambda _A}t}}$ & ${N_B} = {N_{{o_B}}}{e^{ - {\\lambda _B}t}}$<br>Given that $\\frac{{{N_{{o_A}}}}}{{{N_{{0_B}}}}} = \\frac{1}{4}$<br>${N_{{0_A}}}{e^{ - {\\lambda _A}t}} = {N_{{o_B}}}{e^{ - {\\lambda _B}t}}$<br>$\\frac{1}{4} = {e^{({\\lambda _A} - {\\lambda _B})t}}$<br>$\\ln \\left( {\\frac{1}{4}} \\right) = ({\\lambda _A} - {\\lambda _B})t \\Rightarrow t = \\frac{1}{{{\\lambda _A} - {\\lambda _B}}}\\ln \\left( {\\frac{1}{4}} \\right)$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45777,'physics',37,977,2770,'A power transformer is used to step up an alternating emf of $220\\,volt$ to $11\\,kV$ to transmit $4.4\\,kW$ of power. If the primary coil has 1000 turns, what is the current in the secondary coil?','[{"text": "$4\\\\,A$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$0.4\\\\,A$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$0.04\\\\,A$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$0.2\\\\,A$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'${I_{s}=P_{S} / V_{S}}$<br>$\\Rightarrow I_{S}=\\dfrac{4.4 \\times 10^{3}}{11 \\times 10^{3}}=0.4 {~A}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45778,'physics',40,240,479,'A photon is emitted in transition from $n=4$ to $n=1$ level in hydrogen atom. The corresponding wavelength for this transition is (given, $h = 4 \\times {10^{ - 15}}eV - s$)','[{"text": "$974\\\\,nm$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$99.3\\\\,nm$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$941\\\\,nm$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$94.1\\\\,nm$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Energy of emitted photon is given by,<br>$\\Delta E=h v=\\dfrac{h c}{\\lambda}$<br>$E_{1}-E_{2}=\\dfrac{h c}{\\lambda}$<br>As, $E=\\dfrac{-13.6 Z^{2}}{n^{2}} \\quad[Z=1$ for hydrogen atom $]$<br>$ \\Rightarrow \\frac{{ - 13.6{{(1)}^2}}}{{{{(4)}^2}}} - \\left( {\\frac{{13.6{{(1)}^2}}}{{{{(1)}^2}}}} \\right)$<br>$ = \\frac{{\\left( {4 \\times {{10}^{ - 15}}} \\right)\\left( {3 \\times {{10}^8}} \\right)}}{\\lambda }$<br>$ \\Rightarrow \\frac{1}{\\lambda } = 1.0625 \\times {10^7}\\,{m^{ - 1}}$<br>$\\lambda  = 0.941 \\times {10^{ - 7}}\\,m$<br>$ \\Rightarrow \\lambda  = 94.1\\,nm$.<br>So , correct option is (4).',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',2023,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45780,'physics',37,977,2770,'A power transmission line feeds input power at $2300\\,V$ to a step-down transformer having, $4000$ turns in its primary. What should be the number of turns in the secondary coil to get output power at $230\\,V$ ?','[{"text": "400", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "200", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "600", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "800", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'${E_{p}=2300 {~V} ; N_{P}=4000, E_{S}=230 {~V}}$<br>$\\frac{{{E_S}}}{{{E_P}}} = \\frac{{{N_S}}}{{{N_P}}}$<br>$\\therefore {N_S} = {N_P} \\times \\frac{{{E_S}}}{{{E_P}}} = 4000 \\times \\frac{{230}}{{2300}} = 400$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45781,'physics',41,406,806,'The radioactivity of a given sample of scotch due to tritium (half-life 12 years) was found to be only 3% of that measured in a recently purchased bottle marked “7 years old”. The sample must have been prepared about','[{"text": "67 years back", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "220 years back", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "300 years back", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "400 years back", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'After one half-life period, the activity of 	Tritium becomes 50%.<br>After 2 half-life period 25%<br>After 5 half-life period 3.12% $ \\approx $ 3%<br>It is  $5 \\times 12$ years  $ + 7$ years i.e., 67 years only.',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45782,'physics',39,1104,2631,'The radius of an ${\\alpha}$-particle moving in a circle in a constant magnetic field is half of the radius of an electron moving in circular path in the same field. The de-Broglie wavelength of ${\\alpha}$-particle is ${n}$ times that of the electron. Find ${n}$ (an integer).','[{"text": "1", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "3", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "5", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "2", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'$r \\propto \\dfrac{p}{q} \\quad\\left(\\right.$ since, $\\left.r=\\dfrac{p}{B q}\\right)$<br>where $p=$ momentum, Given $r_{\\alpha}=\\dfrac{1}{2} r_{e}$<br>$\\dfrac{p_{\\alpha}}{B 2 e}=\\dfrac{1}{2}\\left(\\dfrac{p_{e}}{B e}\\right)$<br>or $p_{\\alpha}=p_{e}$<br>$\\lambda \\propto \\dfrac{1}{p} \\quad\\left(\\text { since, } \\lambda=\\dfrac{h}{p}\\right)$<br>So, $\\lambda_{\\alpha}=\\lambda_{e}$<br>or $n=1$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45785,'physics',41,406,806,'Half-lives of two radioactive elements $A$ and $B$ are 20 minutes and 40 minutes, respectively. Initially, the samples have equal number of nuclei. After 80 minutes, the ratio of decayed numbers of $A$ and $B$ nuclei will be:','[{"text": "$1:16$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$4:1$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1:4$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$5:4$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Half-life of $A$, ${T_{1/2(A)}} = 20\\min $<br>Half-life of $B$, ${T_{1/2(B)}} = 40\\min $<br>Initially, number of nuclei in each sample $ = N$<br>Now, 80 min $ = 4{T_{1/2(A)}} = 2{T_{1/2(B)}}$<br>Number of active nuclei after four half-lives of $A$,  ${N_A} = \\frac{N}{{{2^4}}} = \\frac{N}{{16}}$<br>$\\therefore $ Number of decayed nuclei $ = N - {N_A} = \\frac{{15}}{6}N$<br>Number of active nuclei after two half-lives of $B$ ${N_B} = \\frac{N}{{{2^2}}} = \\frac{N}{4}$<br>$\\therefore $ Number of decayed nuclei $ = N - {N_B} = \\frac{3}{4}N$<br>$\\therefore {\\mathop{\\rm Re}\\nolimits} {\\rm{quired}}\\,{\\rm{ratio}} = \\frac{{\\frac{{15}}{{16}}N}}{{\\frac{3}{4}N}} = \\frac{5}{4}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45787,'physics',37,974,2273,'An ac voltage given by ${V=70 \\sin \\left(100 \\pi t+\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}\\right) V}$ is connected across ${10 \\Omega}$ resistor. The power consumed will be','[{"text": "$490\\\\,W$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$245\\\\,W$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$350\\\\,W$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "zero", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'${P=\\dfrac{V_{r m s}^{2}}{R}}$<br>${=\\left(\\dfrac{70}{\\sqrt{2}}\\right)^{2} \\times \\dfrac{1}{10}}$<br>${=\\dfrac{50 \\times 70}{2 \\times 10}}$<br>${=245 W}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45788,'physics',41,406,806,'After 300 days, the activity of a radioactive sample is 5000 $dps$ (disintegration per second). The  activity becomes 2500 $dps$ after another 150 days. The initial activity of the sample (in $dps$) is','[{"text": "$20000$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$10000$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$7000$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$25000$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'As, actively it changed from 5000 $dps$ to 2500 $dps$ in 150 day means ${T_{1/2}} = 150$ day<br>$ \\Rightarrow 300\\,\\,day = 2{T_{1/2}}$<br>So, initial activity $2 \\times 5000 \\times 2 = 20000\\,\\,dps$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45789,'physics',40,240,479,'A $12.1\\,eV$ electron beam is used to bombard gaseous hydrogen at room temperature. The number of spectral lines emitted will be','[{"text": "2", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "3", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "4", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "1", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'When an electron of the beam, after hitting a hydrogen atom, completely looses its energy, the hydrogen atom jumps to the maximum excited state and its energy becomes<br>$( - 13 \\cdot 6 + 12.1)ev$ $i.e.$ $ - 1 \\cdot 5\\,ev$.<br>$\\therefore-\\dfrac{13 \\cdot 6}{n^{2}}=-1 \\cdot 5 \\quad \\Rightarrow n=3$.<br>So the hydrogen atom jumps to the second excited state.<br>$\\therefore$ Total number of spectral lines<br>$N=\\dfrac{n(n-1)}{2}=\\dfrac{3(3-1)}{2}=3$.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2023,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45791,'physics',37,973,2268,'In an LCR series circuit, ${R=10 \\Omega, X_{L}=10 \\Omega}$, and ${X_{C}=8 \\Omega}$. The impedance of the circuit is','[{"text": "$10.2\\\\,\\\\Omega $", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$18.2\\\\,\\\\Omega $", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$10\\\\,\\\\Omega $", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2\\\\,\\\\Omega $", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'${Z=\\sqrt{\\left(X_{L}-X_{C}\\right)^{2}+R^{2}}}$<br>$\\begin{aligned}& =\\sqrt{(10-8)^{2}+(10)^{2}} \\\\& =\\sqrt{4+100}=10.2 \\Omega\\end{aligned}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45792,'physics',41,406,808,'A bone fragment found in a cave contains 0.21 times as much $_6^{14}C$ as an equal amount of carbon in air when the organism containing bone died. Find the approximate age of fragment ${t_{1/2}}$ of $^{14}C = 5730$ years.','[{"text": "$1.3 \\\\times {10^4}y$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$1.15 \\\\times {10^4}y$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1.4 \\\\times {10^4}y$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1.24 \\\\times {10^4}y$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'$\\lambda  = \\frac{{0.693}}{{{t_{t/2}}}} = \\frac{{0.693}}{{5730}} = 1.209 \\times {10^{ - 4}}{y^{ - 1}}$<br>Let  ${m_0}$ is the initial mass of the $\\mathop C\\limits^{14} .$ After time $t$ the mass $m = {m_0}{e^{ - \\lambda t}}$<br>$0.21\\,{m_0} = {m_0}{e^{ - \\lambda t}}$<br>$0.21\\, = {e^{ - \\lambda t}} \\Rightarrow \\lambda  = 1.3 \\times {10^4}y$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45794,'physics',39,1104,2631,'An electron of mass ${m}$ when accelerated through a potential difference ${V}$, has de Broglie wavelength ${\\lambda}$. The de-Broglie wavelength associated with a particle of mass ${m / 4}$ and charge ${e}$ accelerated through the same potential difference is ${n \\lambda}$. Then find the value of ${n}$.','[{"text": "2", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "4", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "8", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "6", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Momentum of electron, $p_{e}=\\sqrt{2 m e V}$<br>Momentum of particle, $p_{p}=\\sqrt{2 \\dfrac{m}{4} \\cdot {eV}}$<br>$\\therefore \\quad \\dfrac{\\lambda_{p}}{\\lambda_{e}}=\\dfrac{h / p_{p}}{h / p_{e}}=\\sqrt{\\dfrac{m}{m / 4}}=2$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45795,'physics',37,975,2276,'At resonance, the voltage drop across the resistor ${R}$ is','[{"text": "$20\\\\,V$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$220\\\\,V$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$200\\\\,V$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$10\\\\,V$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'At resonance,<br>$V_{L}=V_{C}$<br>So, ${V_{R}=}$ applied voltage<br>$ = 220\\,V$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/37_45795_question_1.png'),
(45796,'physics',41,406,806,'The radioactivity of  a sample is $x$ at time ${t_1}$ and is $y$ at time ${t_2}$. If the mean life of the specimen is $\\tau $, the number of atoms that have disintegrated in the time interval $({t_2} - {t_1})$ is:','[{"text": "$x - y$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$(x - y)/\\\\tau $", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$(x - y)\\\\tau $", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$x{t_1} - y{t_2}$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'The activity, $\\frac{{dN}}{{dt}} =  - \\lambda N$<br>As per question, $x =  - \\lambda {N_{{t_1}}},y =  - \\lambda {N_{{t_2}}}$<br>$ \\Rightarrow {N_{{t_1}}} =  - \\frac{x}{\\lambda },{N_{{t_2}}} =  - \\frac{y}{\\lambda }$<br>The number of atoms disintegrated in time interval<br>${N_{{t_2}}} - {N_{{t_1}}} =  - \\frac{y}{\\lambda } - \\left( { - \\frac{x}{\\lambda }} \\right) = \\frac{{x - y}}{\\lambda } = (x - y)\\tau $<br>Where, $\\tau  = $ mean life of the sample.',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45799,'physics',37,973,2263,'An electric lamp marked $200\\,V$ dc consumes a current of $10\\,A$. When connected to ${200 {~V}, 50 {~Hz}}$ ac, the current is $5\\,A$. The reactance of the lamp is','[{"text": "$20\\\\sqrt 3 \\\\,\\\\Omega $", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$20\\\\,\\\\Omega $", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$40\\\\,\\\\Omega $", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$10\\\\sqrt 3 \\\\,\\\\Omega $", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'${Z=\\sqrt{X_{L}^{2}+R^{2}}}$<br>For dc, ${R=\\dfrac{V}{I}=\\dfrac{200}{10}=20 \\Omega}$<br>For ac, ${Z=\\dfrac{V}{i}=\\dfrac{200}{5}=40 \\Omega}$<br>${Z=\\sqrt{X_{L}^{2}+R^{2}}}$<br>${(40)^{2}=X_{L}^{2}+(20)^{2}}$<br>${X_{L}^{2}=1600-400=1200}$<br>${X_{L}=20 \\sqrt{3} \\Omega}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45801,'physics',39,1102,2629,'Photons of energy 5$eV$ are incident on the cathode. Electrons reaching the anode have kinetic energies varying from 6 $eV$ to 8 $eV$ Find the work function of the metal','[{"text": "$4\\\\,eV$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$7\\\\,eV$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2\\\\,eV$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$5\\\\,eV$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'$K_{\\max }=(5-\\phi) {eV}$<br>When electrons are accelerated through 5 $V$ , they will reach the anode with energy $=(5-\\phi+5) {eV}$<br>$\\therefore \\quad 10-\\phi=8$<br>or $\\phi=2\\,\\, {eV}$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/39_45801_question_1.png'),
(45802,'physics',37,972,2260,'The instantaneous current in an ${A C}$ circuit is ${i=5 \\sqrt{2} \\sin \\left(100 t+\\dfrac{\\pi}{3}\\right)}$. The rms value of current is','[{"text": "$5\\\\sqrt 2 \\\\,A$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$5\\\\,A$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\sqrt 2 \\\\,A$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$3.185\\\\,A$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'${i_{r m s}=\\dfrac{i_{0}}{\\sqrt{2}}=\\dfrac{5 \\sqrt{2}}{\\sqrt{2}}=5 {~A}}$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45803,'physics',41,406,809,'The $\\gamma $-rays are originated from','[{"text": "excited nucleus", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "outermost shell of atom", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "innermost shell of atom", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "none of the above", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'$\\gamma $-rays are originated from excited nucleus.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45805,'physics',37,975,2275,'For the series $LCR$ circuit shown in the figure, the resonance frequency is','[{"text": "$2500\\\\,rad/s$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$250\\\\,rad/s$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$25\\\\,rad/s$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2.5\\\\,rad/s$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'${V=\\dfrac{1}{2 \\pi \\sqrt{L C}}}$<br>${\\omega=\\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{L C}}=\\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{8 \\times 10^{-3} \\times 20 \\times 10^{-6}}}}$<br>$ = \\frac{1}{{\\sqrt {16 \\times {{10}^{ - 8}}} }} = \\frac{1}{{4 \\times {{10}^{ - 4}}}} = 2500\\,rad/s$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/37_45805_question_1.png'),
(45807,'physics',41,406,809,'A radioactive nucleus (initial mass number $A$ and atomic number $Z$) emits ${\\rm{3}}\\alpha {\\rm{ - }}$ particles and 2 positrons. the ratio of number of neutrons to that of protons in the final nucleus will be','[{"text": "$\\\\frac{{A - Z - 8}}{{Z - 4}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{{A - Z - 4}}{{Z - 8}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{{A - Z - 12}}{{Z - 4}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{{A - Z - 4}}{{Z - 2}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'As a result of emission of $1\\,\\alpha {\\rm{ - }}$particle, the mass number decreases by 4 units and atomic number decreases by 2 units. And by the 	emmission of 1 positron the atomic number decreases by 1 unit but mass number remains constant.<br>$\\therefore $ Mass number of final nucleus $ = A - 12$<br>Atomic number of final nucleus $ = Z - 8$<br>$\\therefore $ Number of neutrons $ = (A - 12) - (Z - 8)$<br>$ = A - Z - 4$<br>$\\therefore $ Required ratio $ = \\frac{{A - Z - 4}}{{Z - 8}}$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45809,'physics',37,974,2274,'In an ac circuit, the reactance of a coil is ${\\sqrt{3}}$ times its resistance. The phase difference between the voltage and current through the coil is','[{"text": "${\\\\dfrac{\\\\pi}{3}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${\\\\dfrac{\\\\pi}{2}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\dfrac{\\\\pi}{4}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\dfrac{\\\\pi}{6}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'${X_{L}=\\sqrt{3} R}$ (Given) <img src=\'/img/data/physics/37_45809_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' /> ${\\tan \\phi=\\dfrac{X_{L}}{R}}$<br>${\\tan \\phi=\\dfrac{\\sqrt{3} R}{R}}$<br>${\\phi=60^{\\circ}=\\dfrac{\\pi}{3}}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45810,'physics',39,1104,2631,'Calculate de-Broglie wavelength  for an average helium atom in a furnace at $400\\,K.$ Given mass of helium $ = 4.002\\,amu.$','[{"text": "0.35", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "0.63", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "0.92", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "0.43", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'de-Broglie wavelength, $\\lambda=\\dfrac{h}{p}$<br>Mean $KE$ of helium atom $=\\dfrac{3 k T}{2}$<br>or $E=\\dfrac{3 \\times\\left(1.38 \\times 10^{-23}\\right) \\times 400}{2}$<br>In terms of momentum, the energy is given by $E=\\dfrac{p^{2}}{2 m}$<br>or $\\quad p^{2}=2 m E$<br>or $p=\\sqrt{2 m E}$<br>$\\therefore \\quad p = \\sqrt {\\begin{array}{*{20}{l}}{2 \\times \\left( {4.002 \\times 1.66 \\times {{10}^{ - 27}}} \\right) \\times 3 \\times }\\\\{\\left( {1.38 \\times {{10}^{ - 23}}} \\right) \\times 200}\\end{array}} $<br>(where mass of helium   $4.000\\,amu = 4.002 \\times 1.66 \\times {10^{ - 27}}\\,kg\\,)$<br>Now, $\\lambda=\\dfrac{6.625 \\times 10^{-34}}{p}$<br>Substituting the value of $p$ and solving it, we get<br>$\\lambda=0.63 \\times 10^{-10} {~m}$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45811,'physics',41,406,809,'In which of the following processes, the number of protons in the nucleus increase?','[{"text": "$\\\\alpha $-decay", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\beta ^ - }$-decay", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${\\\\beta ^ + }$-decay", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$k-$ capture", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'For $\\alpha  - decay{:_x}{A^y}{ \\to _{x - 2}}{B^{y - 4}} + \\alpha $<br>For ${\\beta ^ - }{:_x}{A^y}{ \\to _{x + 1}}{B^y}{ + _{ - 1}}{\\beta ^0}$<br>For ${\\beta ^ + }{:_x}{A^y}{ \\to _{x - 1}}{B^y}{ + _{ + 1}}{\\beta ^0}$<br>For $k$-cpature, there will be no change in the number of protons. Hence, in $\\beta $ decay.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45812,'physics',37,973,2268,'In the series ac circuit shown in the figure, resistor, inductor, and capacitors have values ${10 \\Omega, 50 \\Omega}$, and ${50 \\Omega}$, respectively. Potential difference across the resistor is','[{"text": "$10\\\\sqrt 2 \\\\,V$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$20\\\\,V$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "zero", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$40\\\\,V$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Resonance condition, ${X_{L}=X_{C}}$<br>So, ${E_{R}=E=\\dfrac{20 {~V}}{\\sqrt{2}}=10 \\sqrt{2} {~V}}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/37_45812_question_1.png'),
(45813,'physics',40,240,479,'The energy levels of an atom is shown in figure. Which one of these transitons will result in the emission of a photon of wavelength $124.1\\,nm?$ Given $\\left( {h = 6.62 \\times {{10}^{ - 34}}Js} \\right)$','[{"text": "$D$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$B$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$A$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$C$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Wavelength, of emitted photon is given by $\\lambda=\\dfrac{h c}{\\Delta E}$ where $\\Delta E=$ difference in energy between two levels.<br>$\\Delta {E_A} = 2.2 - 0 = 2.2eV$<br>$\\Delta {E_B} = 5.2 - 0 = 5.2\\,eV$<br>$\\Delta {E_C} =  - 2.2 - ( - 5.2) = 3\\,eV$<br>$\\Delta {E_D} = 10.0 - 0 = 10\\,eV$<br>$\\lambda_{A}=\\dfrac{6.62 \\times 10^{-34} \\times 3 \\times 10^{8}}{2.2 \\times 1.6 \\times 10^{-19}}$<br>$ = 564 \\times {10^{ - 9}}\\,m = 564\\,nm$<br>$\\lambda_{B}=\\dfrac{6.62 \\times 10^{-34} \\times 3 \\times 10^{8}}{5.2 \\times 1.6 \\times 10^{-19}}$<br>$ = 238.65 \\times {10^{ - 9}}\\,m = 238.65\\,nm$<br>$\\lambda_{C}=\\dfrac{6.62 \\times 10^{-34} \\times 3 \\times 10^{8}}{3 \\times 1.6 \\times 10^{-19}}$<br>$ = 413.66 \\times {10^{ - 9}}\\,m = 413.66\\,nm$<br>$\\lambda_{D}=\\dfrac{6.62 \\times 10^{-34} \\times 3 \\times 10^{8}}{10 \\times 1.6 \\times 10^{-19}}$<br>$ = 124.1 \\times {10^{ - 9}}\\,m = 124.1\\,nm$<br>So, correct option is (1)',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',2023,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/40_45813_question_1.png'),
(45818,'physics',39,1102,2627,'Which figure shows the correct variation of applied potential difference \\((V)\\) with photoelectric current (i) at two different intensities of light  \\[({I_1} < {I_2})\\]  of same wavelengths?<div class="matching-question"></div>','[{"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Intensity,   $I_{1} < I_{2}$<br>As the light has same wavelength, so stopping potential is same.<br>Saturation current $i \\propto I$<br>$\\therefore$ saturation current corresponding to $I_{2}$ will be greater than that for $I_{1}$.<br>So, correct option is (3).',NULL,'Medium','Graph/Figure',2024,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45819,'physics',40,239,478,'Speed of an electron in Bohr\'s $7^{\\text {th }}$ orbit for Hydrogen atom is $3.6 \\times {10^6}\\,m/s$. The corresponding speed of the electron in $3^{\\text {rd }}$ orbit, in $m / s$ is','[{"text": "$1.8 \\\\times 10^{6}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$3.6 \\\\times 10^{6}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$7.5 \\\\times 10^{6}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$8.4 \\\\times 10^{6}$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'The speed of electron in Bohr orbit of a hydrogen - like atom is given by $V_{n}=V_{o} \\dfrac{Z}{n}$ where $V_{o}$ is the speed in ground state.<br>For $H$-atom, $Z=1$<br>Here, ${v_7} = 3.6 \\times {10^6}\\,m/s$<br>Therefore $\\dfrac{v_{7}}{v_{3}}=\\dfrac{3}{7}$<br>or $v_{3}=\\dfrac{\\left(3.6 \\times 10^{6}\\right) \\times 7}{3}$<br>$ = 8.4 \\times {10^6}\\,m/s$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2023,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45820,'physics',37,974,2274,'The power factor of an $R - L$ circuit is $1/\\sqrt 2 $. If the frequency of $AC$ is doubled, then what will be the power factor?','[{"text": "$1 / \\\\sqrt{3}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1 / \\\\sqrt{5}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$1 / \\\\sqrt{7}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1 / \\\\sqrt{11}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Power factor,<br>$\\cos \\phi=\\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}=\\dfrac{R}{\\sqrt{R^{2}+\\omega^{2} L^{2}}}$<br>This gives, $\\omega L=R$.<br>When $\\omega$ is doubled, we find,<br>$\\cos {\\phi ^\\prime } = \\frac{R}{{\\sqrt {{R^2} + 4{\\omega ^2}{L^2}} }}$<br>$\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, = \\frac{R}{{\\sqrt {{R^2} + 4{R^2}} }} = \\frac{1}{{\\sqrt 5 }}$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45821,'physics',41,404,803,'The binding energy of deutron $_1^2{\\text{H}}$ is $1.112\\,MeV$ per nucleon and an $\\alpha $-particle ${}_2^4He$ has a binding energy of  $7.047\\,MeV$ per nucleon. Then in the fusion reaction $_1^2{\\text{H}} + _1^2{\\text{H}} \\to _2^4{\\text{He}} + Q,$ the energy $Q$ released is','[{"text": "$11.9\\\\,MeV$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1\\\\,MeV$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$931\\\\,MeV$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$23.8\\\\,MeV$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'The $Q$ value of the reaction is  <br>$Q = 4\\left[ {7.047 - 1.112} \\right]MeV = 23.8MeV$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45822,'physics',39,1102,2628,'Read Assertion and Reason carefully to    mark the correct option  given  below.<br><br><strong>Assertion:</strong> Number of photons increases with increase in frequency of light.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Maximum kinetic energy of emitted 	electrons increases with the frequency of 		incident radiation.','[{"text": "Both  Assertion and Reason  are true \\t \\t  and Reason is the correct explanation of  \\t  Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but  \\t  Reason is not the correct explanation of \\t  Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is true but Reason is false.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is false but Reason is true.", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'As number of photons depends on the intensity of light but not on the frequency. Thus, assertion is incorrect.<br>$K E_{\\max }=h v-\\phi_{0}$, so reason is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',2024,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45823,'physics',40,240,479,'The energy levels of an hydrogen atom are shown below. The transition corresponding to emission of shortest wavelength is','[{"text": "$D$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$B$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$C$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$A$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'We know wavelength of emitted photon is given by $\\lambda=\\dfrac{h c}{\\Delta E}$, where $\\Delta E$ is the energy difference between two stable energy levels. From the given energy levels we can say that transition $D$, corresponds to emission of shortest wavelength because shortest wavelength corresponds to highest energy difference (de-excitation) which will occur in $D$, as $n=3$ to $n=1$.<br>So correct option is (1).',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2023,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/40_45823_question_1.png'),
(45824,'physics',37,974,2272,'The power factor in a circuit connected to an AC power supply has a value which is','[{"text": "unity when the circuit contains only inductance", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "unity when the circuit contains only resistance", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "zero when the circuit contains an ideal resistance only", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "unity when the circuit contains an ideal capacitance only", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Power factor,  $\\cos \\phi  = \\frac{R}{Z}$<br>When circuit contains only resistance,<br>then $Z = R$<br>$\\therefore \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\cos \\phi  = \\frac{R}{R} = 1$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45825,'physics',41,406,809,'$_{90}^{232}Th$ an isotope of thorium decays in ten stages emitting six  $\\alpha $-particle and  four  $\\beta $- particle in all. The end product of the decay is','[{"text": "$_{82}^{209}{\\\\text{Pb}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$_{90}^{232}{\\\\text{Th}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$_{82}^{208}{\\\\text{Pb}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$_{82}^{206}{\\\\text{Pb}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'${}_{90}^{232}Th\\xrightarrow{{ - 6\\alpha }}{}_{78}^{208}Pt\\xrightarrow{{ - 4{\\beta ^ - }}}{}_{82}^{208}Pb$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45826,'physics',39,1102,2628,'Read the Statement -A and Statement -B 	carefully to mark the correct options given 	below:<div class="matching-question"><div class="column-left">Figure shows the variation of stopping potential $(V)$  with frequency $(f)$ for the two photo  sensitive materials  ${M_1}$ and ${M_2}.$  The slope gives value of  $\\frac{h}{e}\\,\\,,$  where  $h$  is Planck’s   constant,  $e$  is the charge of electron.<br>https://res.cloudinary.com/doe6oiv2h/image/upload/v1764950838/preptracker/questions/q_45826_col1_1764950838184.png</div><div class="column-right">${M_2}$  will emit photoelectrons of greater kinetic energy for the incident radiation having same frequency.</div></div>','[{"text": "Statement A is correct but Statement B is \\t\\tincorrect.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Statement A is incorrect but Statement B is \\t  correct.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Statements are correct.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Statements are incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Slope of the graph between stopping potential $V_{0}$ and frequency $f$ equals $h / e$ (using Einstein\'s photoelectric equation).<br>$\\Rightarrow V_{0} \\propto f$<br>For material $M_{1}$, emitted electrons will have greater kinetic energy. This is because $M_{1}$ has lower threshold frequency and have lower value of $\\phi\\left(=h f_{0}\\right)$. So, option (1) is correct.',NULL,'Hard','MCQ type-2',2024,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45827,'physics',37,976,2280,'Find resonance frequency in the given circuit','[{"text": "$\\\\dfrac{1}{\\\\sqrt{L C}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{2}{\\\\sqrt{L C}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{1}{2 \\\\sqrt{L C}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{4}{\\\\sqrt{L C}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'In the given circuit diagram,<br>both inductors of inductance $L$ are<br>parallel to each other, hence<br>$\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{L_{eg}} = \\frac{{{L_1}{L_2}}}{{{L_1} + {L_2}}} = \\frac{{L \\cdot L}}{{L + L}} = \\frac{L}{2}$<br>Similarly,<br>$\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{C_{eg}} = {C_1} + {C_2} = C + C = 2C$<br>$\\therefore $   Reasonance frequency<br>${\\omega _r} = \\frac{1}{{\\sqrt {{L_{eg}}.\\,{C_{eg}}} }} = \\sqrt {\\frac{1}{{\\frac{L}{2}.\\,\\,2C}}} $<br>$\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, = \\sqrt {\\frac{1}{{LC}}}  = \\frac{1}{{\\sqrt {LC} }}$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/37_45827_question_1.png'),
(45829,'physics',41,404,803,'The binding energies per nucleon of ${\\text{L}}{{\\text{i}}^7}$ and  ${\\text{H}}{{\\text{e}}^4}$ are $5.6\\,\\,{\\text{MeV}}$ and $7.06\\,\\,{\\text{MeV}}$ respectively, then the energy of the reaction ${\\text{L}}{{\\text{i}}^7} + p \\to 2\\left[ {{}_2{\\text{H}}{{\\text{e}}^4}} \\right]$ will be','[{"text": "$39.2\\\\,MeV$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$17.28\\\\,MeV$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$1.46\\\\,MeV$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$28.24\\\\,MeV$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'The reaction is ${}_3{\\text{L}}{{\\text{i}}^7} + {}_1{p^1} \\to 2\\left[ {{}_2{\\text{H}}{{\\text{e}}^4}} \\right]$<br>${E_p} = 2\\left( {4 \\times 7.06} \\right) - 7 \\times 5.6$<br>$ = 56.48 - 39.2 = 17.28{\\mkern 1mu} {\\rm{MeV}}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45830,'physics',39,1102,2628,'Which of the following statement is not true about stopping potential $\\left(V_{0}\\right)$ ?','[{"text": "It depends on the nature of emitter material.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "It increases with increase in intensity of the incident light.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "It depends upon frequency of the incident light.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "It is $1 / e$ times the maximum kinetic energy of electrons emitted.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Stopping potential does not depend on the intensity of incident radiation. If frequency of the incident radiation increases, $V_{0}$ increases.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2024,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45831,'physics',37,976,2280,'For high frequency $L-C$ circuit, the maximum charge on the capacitor is $Q$. The charge on the capacitor, when the energy is stored equally between the electric and magnetic fields is','[{"text": "$Q / 2$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$Q / \\\\sqrt{2}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$Q / \\\\sqrt{3}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$Q / 3$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Energy stored in a capacitor of<br>capacitance $C$, charge $Q$ is<br>$\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,U = \\frac{1}{2}\\frac{{{Q^2}}}{C}$<br>Since, energy is stored equally between<br>electric and magnetic fields,<br>$\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,E = \\frac{1}{2}\\left( {\\frac{1}{2}\\frac{{{Q^2}}}{C}} \\right)$<br>Now, $\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\frac{{{Q^{\\prime 2}}}}{{2C}} = \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot \\frac{{{Q^2}}}{{2C}} \\Rightarrow {Q^\\prime } = \\frac{Q}{{\\sqrt 2 }}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45832,'physics',41,405,805,'Potential energy for a pair of nucleons versus separation between the nucleons is plotted below.<br>Match the following columns<div class="matching-question"><div class="column-left">A. Force between nucleons is zero<br>B. Force between nucleons is attractive<br>C. Force between nucleons is repulsive<br>D. Potential energy is maximum</div><div class="column-right">P. $r > {r_0}$<br>Q. $r < {r_0}$<br>R. $r = {r_0}$<br>S. $r = 0$</div></div>','[{"text": "A - R\\tB - P\\t       C - Q        D - S", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "A - R\\tB - Q\\t       C - P         D - S", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "A - S\\tB - R        C - Q        D - P", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "A - R\\tB - P\\t       C - Q        D - R", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'A rough plot of the potential energy between two nucleons as a function of distance shows  that the potential energy is a minimum at a distance ${r_0}$. This means that the force is attractive for distances larger than ${r_0}$ and repulsive if they are separated by distances less than ${r_0}$. As  $r \\to 0$ the potential energy reaches maximum value.',NULL,'Hard','Match the following',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/41_45832_question_1.png'),
(45833,'physics',40,241,480,'A small particle of mass $m$ moves in such a way that its potential energy $U=\\dfrac{1}{2} m \\omega^{2} r^{2}$, where $\\omega$ is constant and $r$ is the distance of the particle from origin. Assuming Bohr\'s quantization of momentum and circular orbit, the radius of $n^{\\text {th }}$ orbit will be proportional to','[{"text": "$\\\\sqrt{n}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$n^{2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$n$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{1}{n}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Mass of particle is $m$,<br>$P E(U)=\\dfrac{1}{2} m \\omega^{2} r^{2}=c r^{2}$<br>Force acting on the particle is given by,<br>$F=-\\dfrac{d U}{d r}=-m \\omega^{2} r$ or $F \\propto r$<br>$m \\omega^{2} r=\\dfrac{m v^{2}}{r}$<br>$v = r\\omega \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\left( 1 \\right)$<br>By Bohr\'s quantization of momentum<br>$L=m v r=\\dfrac{n h}{2 \\pi}$<br>$v = \\frac{{nh}}{{2\\pi r}}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\left( 2 \\right)$<br>By equation (1) and (2), we get<br>$r\\omega  = \\frac{{nh}}{{2\\pi r}}$<br>$ \\Rightarrow {r^2} \\propto n$<br>$ \\Rightarrow r \\propto \\sqrt n $',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',2023,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45834,'physics',39,1102,2629,'In photoelectric experiment energy of $2.48\\,eV$  irradiates a photo sensitive material. The stopping potential was measured to be $0.5\\,V.$ Work function of the photo sensitive material is','[{"text": "$1.68\\\\,eV$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2.48\\\\,eV$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$0.5\\\\,eV$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1.98\\\\,eV$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'According to Einstein\'s photoelectric equation,<br>$e{V_0} = hv - \\phi \\quad \\quad \\quad (1)$<br>where $V_{0}$ is the stopping potential and $\\phi$ is the work function.<br>Here,  ${V_0} = 0.5\\,\\,V,hv = 2.48\\,eV$<br>Substituting given values in equation (1), we get<br>$0.5\\,eV = 2.48\\,eV - \\phi $<br>$ \\Rightarrow \\phi  = 1.98\\,eV$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2024,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45835,'physics',37,973,2268,'When an AC voltage is applied to an $L-C-R$ circuit, which of the following is true?','[{"text": "$I$ and $V$ are out of phase with each other in $R$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$I$ and $V$ are in phase in $L$ with in $C$, they are out of phase", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$I$ and $V$ are out of phase in both, $C$ and $L$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$I$ and $V$ are out of phase in $L$ and in phase in $C$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'$I$ and $V$ are out of the phase in both, $C$ and $L$ is true.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45836,'physics',41,404,802,'Assume that a neutron breaks into a proton and 	an electron. The energy absorbed during this process is: (mass of neutron  	$ = 1.6725 \\times {10^{ - 27}}kg,$ mass of proton $ = 1.6725 \\times {10^{ - 27}}kg,$ mass of electron $ = 9 \\times {10^{ - 31}}kg$).','[{"text": "$ - 0.51\\\\,MeV$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$ - 7.10\\\\,MeV$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$ - 6.30\\\\,MeV$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$ - 5.4\\\\,MeV$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'${}_0^1n \\to {}_1^1P{ + _{ - 1}}{e^0} + \\bar v + Q$<br>The mass defect during the process<br>$\\Delta m = {m_n} - {m_H} - {m_e} = 1.6725 \\times {10^{27}}$<br>$ - (1.6725 \\times {10^{ - 27}} + 9 \\times {10^{ - 31}}kg)$<br>$ =  - 9 \\times {10^{ - 31}}kg$<br>Here  $\\Delta m$ is $( - )ve$ so the reaction absorbs energy.The energy absorbed during the process<br>$E = \\Delta m{c^2}$<br>$E = 9 \\times {10^{ - 31}} \\times 9 \\times {10^{16}} = 81 \\times {10^{ - 15}}{\\text{joules}}$<br>$E = \\frac{{81 \\times {{10}^{ - 15}}}}{{1.6 \\times {{10}^{ - 19}}}} = 0.511{\\mkern 1mu} $ $Mev$<br>The correct answer is $ - 0.511\\,MeV$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45837,'physics',39,1102,2626,'Which of the following phenomena does not explain by wave nature of light?<br>(I) reflection<br>(II) diffraction<br>(III) photoelectric effect<br> (IV) interference <br>(V) polarization<br>Choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below.','[{"text": "I, III only", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "V only", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "II, IV only", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "III only", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Photoelectric effect established particle nature of light. So, correct option is (4).',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2024,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45839,'physics',37,973,2265,'In an $L-R$ circuit, the value of $L$ is $\\left( {\\frac{{0.4}}{\\pi }} \\right)H$ and the valueof $R$ is $30 \\Omega$. If in the circuit, an alternating emf of $200\\,V$ at 50 cycle $/ \\mathrm{s}$ is connected, the impedance of the circuit and current will be','[{"text": "$11.4 \\\\Omega, 17.5 \\\\mathrm{~A}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$30.7 \\\\Omega, 6.5 \\\\mathrm{~A}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$40.4 \\\\Omega, 5 \\\\mathrm{~A}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$50 \\\\Omega, 4$ A", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Given, ${X_L} = \\omega L = 2\\pi fL$<br>$\\quad \\quad \\quad  = 2\\pi  \\times 50 \\times \\frac{{0.4}}{\\pi } = 40\\,\\Omega $<br>$\\quad \\quad R = 30\\,\\Omega $<br>$ \\Rightarrow \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,Z = \\sqrt {{R^2} + X_L^2} $<br>$\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, = \\sqrt {{{(30)}^2} + {{(40)}^2}}  = 50\\Omega {\\text{ }}$<br>and $\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{i_{ms}} = \\frac{{{V_{ms}}}}{Z} = \\frac{{200}}{{50}} = 4\\,A$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45840,'physics',41,409,815,'Consider a nuclear reaction with target particle at rest and its mass is $M$. The mass of  moving particle is m and velocity is $u$. The energy required for the reaction to take place is $Q$. Find the threshold energy of the particle of mass $m$.','[{"text": "$Q\\\\left[ {1 + \\\\frac{M}{m}} \\\\right]$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$Q\\\\left[ {M + m} \\\\right]$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$Q\\\\left[ {1 + \\\\frac{m}{M}} \\\\right]$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{{Qm}}{M}$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'For the requirement of least $K$.$E$ (Threshold energy) the nature of collision is inelastic. <br>From consevation of linear momentum<br>$mu + M(0) = (m + M)v\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,(1)$<br>From conservation of energy<br>$\\frac{1}{2}mu = \\frac{1}{2}(m + M){v^2} + Q\\,\\,\\,\\,(2)$<br>Where $Q$ is the energy required for nuclear reaction which has to come from $K$.$E$ of the incoming particle.<br>From (1) & (2)<br>$\\frac{1}{2}m{u^2} = \\frac{1}{2}(m + M)\\frac{{{m^2}{u^2}}}{{{{(m + M)}^2}}} + Q$<br>$\\frac{1}{2}m{u^2} = \\frac{1}{2}\\frac{{{m^2}{u^2}}}{{(m + M)}} + Q$<br>$\\frac{1}{2}m{u^2}\\left[ {1 - \\frac{m}{{m + M}}} \\right] = Q$<br>${E_{Th}} = Q\\left[ {1 + \\frac{m}{M}} \\right]$       $\\left( {\\frac{1}{2}m{u^2} = {E_{Th}}} \\right)$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45841,'physics',39,1102,2629,'When $U V$ light of wavelength $300\\,nm$  is incident on the metal surface having work function $2.13\\,eV,$ electron emission takes  place. The stopping potential is  $({\\text{Given}}\\,\\,hc = 1240\\,eV\\,nm)$','[{"text": "$4.1\\\\,V$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1.5\\\\,V$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2\\\\,V$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$4\\\\,V$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Given:  $\\lambda  = 300\\,nm,\\phi  = 2.13\\,eV$<br>Using Einstein\'s equation for photoelectric effect,<br>$hc = 1240\\,eV\\,nm$<br>$\\dfrac{h c}{\\lambda}=\\phi+e V_{0}$<br>$\\dfrac{1240}{300}=2.13+e V_{0}$<br>$4.13-2.13=e V_{0}$<br>${V_0} = 2\\,V$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2024,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45842,'physics',37,975,2275,'In an $L-C-R$ series, AC circuit at resonance,','[{"text": "the capacitive reactance is more than the inductive reactance", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "the capacitive reactance equals the inductive reactance", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "the capacitive reactance is less than the inductive reactance", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "the power dissipated is minimum", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'The impedance of a series $L - C - R$<br>circuit is<br>$\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,Z = \\sqrt {{{\\left( {{X_L} - {X_C}} \\right)}^2} + {R^2}} $<br>At resonance (series resonant circuit),<br>$\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{X_L} = {X_C}$<br>$ \\Rightarrow \\,\\,{Z_{{\\text{min }}}} = R,i.e.$ circuit behaves as<br>resistive circuit.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45844,'physics',41,408,814,'A star initially has  ${10^{40}}$ deuterons. It produces energy via the processes<br>$_1{H^2}{ + _1}{H^2}{ \\to _1}{H^3} + p$<br>$_1{H^2}{ + _1}{H^3}{ \\to _2}H{e^4} + n$<br>The masses of the nuclei are as follows:<br>$M({H^2}) = 2.014\\,amu;M(p) = 1.007\\,amu;$<br>$M(n) = 1.008\\,amu;M(H{e^4}) = 4.001\\,amu$<br>If the average power radiated by the star is ${10^{16}}W,$ the deuteron supply of the star is exhausted in a time of the order of','[{"text": "${10^8}\\\\sec $", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${10^6}\\\\sec $", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${10^{16}}\\\\sec $", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${10^{12}}\\\\sec $", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'The net reaction is <br>${3_1}{H^2} \\to {}_2H{e^4} + p + n$<br>Mass defect $ = 3 \\times 2.014 - 4.001 - 1.007 - 1.008$<br>$Q = 0.026\\,amu = 0.026 \\times 931 \\times {10^6} \\times 1.6 \\times {10^{ - 19}}J$<br>$ = 3.87 \\times {10^{ - 12}}J$<br>Power radiated by the star $ = {10^{16}}W$<br>A single reaction consumes 3-deuterons. <br>So 3- deuterons produce $ - 3.87 \\times {10^{ - 12}}J$<br>1-deuteron produce $ - 1.29 \\times {10^{ - 12}}J$<br>Star produces  $ - {10^{16}}J$ in $1s$<br>No of deuterons consumed per second<br>$\\frac{{{{10}^{16}}}}{{1.29 \\times {{10}^{ - 12}}}} = \\frac{1}{{1.29}} \\times {10^{28}}$<br>Deuteron supply exhausts in <br>$\\frac{{{{10}^{40}}}}{{0.775 \\times {{10}^{28}}}} = 1.29 \\times {10^{12}}\\,{\\text{s}}$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45845,'physics',39,1102,2629,'$U V$ light of $4.13\\,eV$ is incident on a photosensitive metal surface having work function $3.13\\,eV.$ The maximum kinetic energy of ejected photoelectrons will be','[{"text": "$4.13\\\\,eV$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1\\\\,eV$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$3.13\\\\,eV$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$7.26\\\\,eV$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'By Einstein\'s photoelectric equation,<br>$K{E_{\\max }} = E - \\phi  = 4 \\cdot 13 - 3.13$<br>$ \\Rightarrow K.{E_{\\max }} = 1\\,eV$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2024,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45846,'physics',37,973,2268,'In a series $L-C-R$ circuit, resistance $R=10 \\Omega$ and the impedance $Z=10 \\Omega$. The phase difference between the current and the voltage is','[{"text": "$0^{\\\\circ}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$30^{\\\\circ}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$45^{\\\\circ}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$60^{\\\\circ}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Impedance,<br>$Z = \\sqrt {{R^2} + {{\\left( {{X_L} - {X_C}} \\right)}^2}} $<br>$\\therefore \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,10 = \\sqrt {{{(10)}^2} + {{\\left( {{X_L} - {X_C}} \\right)}^2}} $<br>$\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,100 = 100 + {\\left( {{X_L} - {X_C}} \\right)^2}$<br>$ \\Rightarrow \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{X_L} - {X_C} = 0\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\left( 1 \\right)$<br>Let $\\phi $ is the phase difference between<br>current and voltage.<br>$\\therefore \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\tan {\\mkern 1mu} \\phi {\\mkern 1mu}  = \\frac{{{X_L} - {X_C}}}{R}$<br>$ \\Rightarrow \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\tan \\phi  = \\frac{0}{R}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,$ [from Eq. (1)]<br>$\\therefore {\\mkern 1mu} \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\phi  = {0^ \\circ }$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45847,'physics',41,408,814,'Calculate the energy which can be obtained from 1 $kg$ of  ${H_2}O$ through the fusion reaction $_1^2H + _1^2H \\to _1^3H + p.$ Assume $1.5 \\times {10^{ - 2}}\\% $ of the water contains deuterium. The whole deuterium is consumed in the fusion reaction. (Mass of ${}_1{H^2} = 2.01410,\\,{}_1{H^3} = 3.01605,\\,P = 1.008$ in $amu$)','[{"text": "$2820 \\\\times {10^4}\\\\,{\\\\text{J}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2820\\\\,{\\\\text{J}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2820\\\\, \\\\times {10^8}\\\\,{\\\\text{J}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2820\\\\, \\\\times {10^6}\\\\,{\\\\text{J}}$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'${{\\text{D}}_{\\text{2}}}{\\text{O}} = 1 \\times 1.5 \\times {10^{ - 4}}{\\text{Kg}} = 0.15{\\text{g}}$<br>Number of  $_1^2H = \\frac{{0.15 \\times 6.023 \\times {{10}^{23}}}}{{20}} = 4.5 \\times {10^{21}}$<br>$ \\Rightarrow Q = \\left( {2 \\times 2.01410 - 3.01605 - 1.008} \\right)$<br>$( \\times 931.5 \\times 4.5 \\times {10^{21}} \\times 1.6 \\times {10^{ - 13}}{\\text{J)}}$<br>$ = 0.004155 \\times 931.5 \\times 7.2 \\times {10^8} = 2820{\\mkern 1mu} M\\,J$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45849,'physics',39,1102,2626,'Monochromatic light of frequency $6 \\times {10^{14}}\\,Hz$ is produced by a laser. The power emitted is  $2 \\times {10^{ - 3}}\\,W.$  How many photons per second on an average, are emitted by the source ? $({\\text{Given}}\\,\\,h = 6.63 \\times {10^{ - 34}}\\,J\\,)$','[{"text": "$5 \\\\times 10^{15}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$7 \\\\times 10^{16}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$6 \\\\times 10^{15}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$9 \\\\times 10^{18}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Given : Frequency,  $v = 6 \\times {10^{14}}\\,Hz\\,;$<br>Power,  $P = 2 \\times {10^{ - 3}}\\,W$<br>As, $P=\\dfrac{n h v}{t}$<br>So, number of photons emitted by the source per sec,<br>$\\dfrac{n}{t}=\\dfrac{P}{h v}=\\dfrac{2 \\times 10^{-3}}{6.63 \\times 10^{-34} \\times 6 \\times 10^{14}}$<br>$=0.050 \\times 10^{17}=5 \\times 10^{15}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2024,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45850,'physics',37,972,2260,'An alternating current of rms value $10\\,A$ is passed through a $12 \\Omega$ resistor. The maximum potential difference across the resistor is','[{"text": "$20\\\\,V$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$90\\\\,V$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$169.68\\\\,V$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "None of these", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Given, ${I_{rms}} = 10\\,A$<br>and $\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,R = 12\\,\\Omega $<br>$\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{E_{rms}} = {I_{rms}} \\times R$<br>$\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, = 10 \\times 12 = 120\\,V$<br>Now, maximum potential difference<br>$\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, = {E_{rms}} \\times \\sqrt 2  = 120 \\times \\sqrt 2 $<br>$\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, = 169.68\\,V$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45851,'physics',41,408,814,'Which of the following is the fusion reaction?','[{"text": "${}_0{n^1} + {}_{\\\\text{7}}{{\\\\text{N}}^{14}} \\\\to {}_6{{\\\\text{C}}^{14}} + {}_1{{\\\\text{H}}^1}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${}_0{n^1} + {}_{92}{{\\\\text{U}}^{238}} \\\\to {}_{93}{\\\\text{N}}{{\\\\text{p}}^{239}} + {\\\\beta ^{ - 1}} + \\\\gamma $", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${}_1{{\\\\text{H}}^2} + {}_1{{\\\\text{H}}^2} \\\\to {}_2{\\\\text{H}}{{\\\\text{e}}^4}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${}_1{{\\\\text{H}}^3} \\\\to {}_2{\\\\text{H}}{{\\\\text{e}}^3} + {\\\\beta ^{ - 1}} + \\\\gamma $", "isCorrect": false}]',2,NULL,NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45852,'physics',39,1102,2626,'In a photoelectric effect experiment, a light of frequency 1.5 times the threshold frequency is made to fall on the surface of photo  sensitive material. Now if the frequency is halved and intensity is doubled, the number of photo electrons emitted will be','[{"text": "halved", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "quadrupled", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Zero", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "doubled", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Given, $v=1.5 v_{0}$<br>Now, when the incident frequency is reduced to half of its initial value, then<br>$v^{\\prime}=0.75 v_{0} \\Rightarrow v^{\\prime} < v_{0}$<br>$i.\\,e.,$  incident frequency  <  threshold frequency so no photoelectrons will be emitted.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2024,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45854,'physics',37,973,2263,'An AC voltage is applied to a pure inductor $L$, drives a current in the inductor. The current in the inductor would be','[{"text": "ahead of the voltage by $\\\\pi / 2$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "lagging the voltage by $\\\\pi / 2$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "ahead of the voltage by $\\\\pi / 4$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "lagging the voltage by $3 \\\\pi / 4$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'In an AC circuit $L$ only , alternating<br>current $I$ lags behind the alternating<br>voltage $E$ by a phase angle of ${90^ \\circ },i.e.$ by $\\pi / 2$.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45855,'physics',41,408,812,'To generate a power of 3.2 megawatt, the number of fissions of ${{\\text{U}}^{235}}$ per minute is (Energy released per fission $ = 200\\,MeV,leV = 1.6 \\times {10^{ - 19}}J$)','[{"text": "$6 \\\\times {10^{17}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$6 \\\\times {10^{18}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$5 \\\\times {10^{14}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$5 \\\\times {10^{12}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Number of fissions per second<br>${\\rm{ = }}\\frac{{{\\rm{Power}}\\,\\,{\\rm{output}}}}{{{\\rm{Energy}}\\,\\,{\\rm{released}}\\,\\,{\\rm{per}}\\,\\,{\\rm{fission}}}}$<br>$ = \\frac{{3.2 \\times {{10}^6}}}{{200 \\times {{10}^6} \\times 1.6 \\times {{10}^{ - 19}}}} = 1 \\times {10^{17}}$<br>$ \\Rightarrow $ Number of fission per minute<br>$ = 60 \\times 1 \\times {10^{17}} = 6 \\times {10^{18}}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45856,'physics',39,1104,2631,'A proton and an electron are associated with same de-Broglie wavelength. The ratio of their kinetic energies is <br>(Assume  $h = 6.63 \\times {10^{ - 34}}Js,$  ${m_e} = 9.0 \\times {10^{ - 31}}\\,kg$ and  ${m_p} = 1836\\,\\,{\\text{times}}\\,\\,{m_e})$','[{"text": "1:1836", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$1: \\\\dfrac{1}{1836}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1: \\\\sqrt{1836}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1: \\\\dfrac{1}{\\\\sqrt{1836}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'de-Broglie wavelength, $\\lambda=\\dfrac{h}{p}=\\dfrac{h}{\\sqrt{2 m K E}}$<br>For same wavelength, $m K E=$ same<br>$\\Rightarrow m_{P} K E_{P}=m_{e} K E_{e}$<br>$\\dfrac{K E_{p}}{K E_{e}}=\\dfrac{m_{e}}{m_{p}}=1: 1836$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',2024,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45857,'physics',37,975,2278,'In an $L-C-R$ series $A C$ circuit, if $\\omega_{0}$ is the resonant angular frequency, then the quality factor ( $Q$-factor) is given by<br>$L / C R$','[{"text": "$\\\\omega_{0} L / C$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$(1 / R) \\\\sqrt{\\\\dfrac{L}{C}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\omega_{0} C / R$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$L / \\\\omega_{0} R$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Quality factor, $Q = \\frac{{{X_L}}}{R} = \\frac{{{\\omega _0}L}}{R}$<br>At resonance, ${\\omega _0} = \\frac{1}{{\\sqrt {LC} }}$<br>$\\therefore \\,\\,\\,\\,Q = \\frac{L}{R} \\times \\frac{1}{{\\sqrt {LC} }} = \\frac{1}{R}\\sqrt {\\frac{L}{C}} $',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45858,'physics',40,241,480,'An electron of a hydrogen like atom, having $Z=4$, jumps from $4^{\\text {th }}$ energy state to $2^{\\text {nd }}$ energy state. The energy released in this process, will be (Given $Rch = 13.6\\,eV$) Where, $R=$ Rydberg constant, $c=$ Speed of light in vacuum, $h=$ Planck\'s constant','[{"text": "$40.8\\\\,eV$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$13.6\\\\,eV$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$3.4\\\\,eV$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$10.5\\\\,eV$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'As, $Z=4, n_{f}=2, n_{i}=4$<br>The energy released is given by<br>$\\Delta E=13.6 Z^{2}\\left[\\dfrac{1}{n_{f}^{2}}-\\dfrac{1}{n_{i}^{2}}\\right]=13.6 \\times 4^{2}\\left[\\dfrac{1}{4}-\\dfrac{1}{16}\\right]$<br>$\\Delta E = 13.6 \\times 16\\left[ {\\frac{{4 - 1}}{{16}}} \\right]$<br>$ = 13.6 \\times 3 = 40.8\\,eV$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',2023,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45859,'physics',41,406,806,'The half life of a radioactive substance is 20 minutes. The approximate time interval  $({t_2} - {t_1})$ between  the time  ${t_2}$ when  $\\frac{2}{3}$ of it had decayed and time  ${t_1}$ when  $\\frac{1}{3}$ of it had decayed has','[{"text": "$14\\\\,\\\\min $", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$20\\\\,\\\\min $", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$28\\\\,\\\\min $", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$7\\\\,\\\\min $", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Number of undecayed nuclei after time ${t_2};$<br>$\\frac{{{N_0}}}{3} = {N_0}{e^{ - \\lambda {t_2}}}$                     (1)<br>Number of undecayed nuclei after time ${t_1};$<br>$\\frac{{2{N_0}}}{3} = {N_0}{e^{ - \\lambda {t_1}}}$                (2)<br>From(1) ${e^{ - \\lambda {t_2}}} = \\frac{1}{3}$ <br>$ \\Rightarrow  - \\lambda {t_2} = {\\log _e}\\left( {\\frac{1}{3}} \\right)$                (3)<br>From (2) $ - {e^{ - \\lambda {t_1}}} = \\frac{2}{3}$<br>$ \\Rightarrow  - \\lambda {t_1} = {\\log _e}\\left( {\\frac{2}{3}} \\right)$                     (4)<br>Solving (3) and (4), we get<br>${t_2} - {t_1} = \\frac{1}{\\lambda }\\left[ {{{\\log }_e}\\left( {\\frac{2}{3}} \\right) - {{\\log }_e}\\left( {\\frac{1}{3}} \\right)} \\right] = \\frac{{{{\\log }_e}(2)}}{\\lambda } = 20\\min $',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45860,'physics',39,1104,2631,'A proton and an electron have the same de-Broglie wavelength. If $K_{p}$ and $K_{e}$ be the kinetic energies of proton and electron respectively, then choose the correct relation.','[{"text": "$K_{p}=K_{e}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$K_{p}>K_{e}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$K_{p}=K_{e}^{2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$K_{p} < K_{e}$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'de-Broglie wavelength,<br>$\\lambda=\\dfrac{h}{p}$. If $\\lambda$ is same, so momentum $p$ will be same.<br>Now, kinetice energy $K=\\dfrac{p^{2}}{2 m}$<br>$\\therefore K \\propto \\dfrac{1}{m} ; m_{p}>m_{e} \\Rightarrow K_{p} < K_{e}$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',2024,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45861,'physics',37,975,2276,'In $L-C-R$ resonant circuit, what is the phase angle $\\phi$ ?','[{"text": "$90^{\\\\circ}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$180^{\\\\circ}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$0^{\\\\circ}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$60^{\\\\circ}$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'In an $L - C - R$ circuit, phase angle is<br>defined as $\\cos \\phi  = \\frac{R}{Z}$<br>where, $Z = \\sqrt {{R^2} + {{\\left( {{X_L} - {X_C}} \\right)}^2}} $<br>When circuit is resonant,<br>$\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{X_L} = {X_C} \\Rightarrow Z = R$<br>So,  $\\cos \\phi  = \\frac{R}{R} = 1,\\phi  = \\mathop 0\\limits^ \\circ  $',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45863,'physics',41,408,813,'An atomic power nuclear reactor can deliver $300\\,MW$. The energy released due to fission of each nucleus of uranium atom  ${{\\text{U}}^{238}}$ is $170\\,MeV$. The number of uranium atoms fissioned per hour will be','[{"text": "$4 \\\\times {10^{22}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$30 \\\\times 1{0^{25}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$5 \\\\times 1{0^{15}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$10 \\\\times 1{0^{20}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'$P = \\frac{{NE}}{t} \\Rightarrow 300 \\times {10^6}$<br>$ = \\frac{{N \\times 170 \\times {{10}^6} \\times 1.6 \\times {{10}^{ - 19}}}}{t}$<br>$\\therefore $ Number of atoms per sec  $\\frac{N}{t} = 1.102 \\times {10^{19}}$<br>Number of atoms per hour $ = 1.02 \\times {10^{19}} \\times 3600$<br>$ = 3.97 \\times {10^{22}}.$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45864,'physics',39,1104,2631,'A proton, an electron and an alpha particle have the same energies. Their de-Broglie wavelengths will be compared as','[{"text": "$\\\\lambda_{e}>\\\\lambda_{\\\\alpha}>\\\\lambda_{p}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\lambda_{p}>\\\\lambda_{e}>\\\\lambda_{\\\\alpha}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\lambda_{\\\\alpha} < \\\\lambda_{p} < \\\\lambda_{e}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\lambda_{p} < \\\\lambda_{e} < \\\\lambda_{\\\\alpha}$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'$\\lambda=\\dfrac{h}{p}=\\dfrac{h}{\\sqrt{2 m K}}$<br>$\\lambda \\propto \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{m}}$ or $K$ is constant<br>Since, $m_{e} < m_{p} < m_{\\alpha}$<br>$\\therefore \\quad {\\lambda _e} > {\\lambda _p} > {\\lambda _\\alpha } \\Rightarrow {\\lambda _\\alpha } < {\\lambda _p} < {\\lambda _e}$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',2024,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45865,'physics',37,973,2266,'In $R-C$ circuit, $\\omega  = 100rad{s^{ - 1}},R = 100\\Omega ,C = 20\\mu F$ .What is impedance?','[{"text": "$510\\\\,\\\\Omega $", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$200\\\\,\\\\Omega $", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$250\\\\,\\\\Omega $", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$300\\\\,\\\\Omega $", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Impedance for an $R$ - $C$ circuit is<br>given by<br>$Z\\,\\, = \\sqrt {{R^2} + X_C^2}  = \\sqrt {{R^2} + {{\\left( {\\frac{1}{{\\omega C}}} \\right)}^2}} $<br>$\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, = \\sqrt {{{(100)}^2} + {{\\left( {\\frac{1}{{100 \\times 20 \\times {{10}^{ - 6}}}}} \\right)}^2}} $<br>$\\,\\,\\,\\, = \\sqrt {{{10}^4} + 25 \\times {{10}^4}} $<br>$\\quad  = 100\\sqrt {26}  = 510\\,\\Omega $',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45866,'physics',41,406,806,'A human body excretes certain material by a law similar to radioactivity. The body excretes half the amount injected in $24h$. Find the time in which activity falls to ${\\text{3}}\\mu {\\text{Ci}}$. If a person is injected technitium  $\\left( {{t_{1/2}} = 6h} \\right)$ and its activity just after the injection is $6\\mu {\\text{Ci}}{\\text{.}}$','[{"text": "${\\\\text{6}}\\\\,h$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$4.8\\\\,h$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$6.3\\\\,h$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\operatorname{None}  \\\\,of these$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'The person looses the radioactive material both  by excretion and by disintegration.The effective rate constant is<br>$\\lambda  = {\\lambda _1} + {\\lambda _2} \\Rightarrow \\frac{1}{t} = \\frac{1}{{{t_1}}} + \\frac{1}{{{t_2}}}$<br>$t = \\frac{{{t_1}{t_2}}}{{{t_1} + {t_2}}} = \\frac{{24 \\times 6}}{{24 + 6}} = 4.8\\,h$<br>Where $t$ is the new half life. The concentration falls from $6\\mu ci$ to $3\\mu ci$ in $4.8\\,h$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45867,'physics',40,239,478,'The radius of electron\'s second stationary orbit in Bohr\'s atom is $R$. The radius of $3^{\\text {rd }}$ orbit will be','[{"text": "$\\\\dfrac{R}{3}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$9 R$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2.25 R$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$3 R$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'In a hydrogen atom $R_{n}=R_{0} n^{2}$, where $R_{0}$ is the radius of atom in its ground state. Radius of $2^{\\text {nd }}$ orbit $=R ; A s, R_{n} \\propto n^{2}$<br>$\\frac{{{R_2}}}{{{R_3}}} = \\frac{4}{9} \\Rightarrow {R_3} = \\frac{9}{4}{R_2};$<br>${R_3} = \\frac{9}{4}(R)\\quad \\left( {\\because {R_2} = R} \\right)$<br>${R_3} = 2.25R$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2023,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45868,'physics',39,1104,2631,'The de-Broglie wavelength of a proton and an $\\alpha$-particle are $\\lambda$ and $2 \\lambda$ respectively. The ratio of the velocities of proton and $\\alpha$-particle will be','[{"text": "$1: 8$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$4: 1$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$8: 1$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$1: 2$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'$\\lambda_{P}=\\lambda, \\lambda_{\\alpha}=2 \\lambda$<br>The de-Broglie\'s theorem<br>$\\lambda=\\dfrac{h}{m v}$<br>$m_{P}=m_{e}, m_{\\alpha}=4 m_{e}$<br>$\\dfrac{\\lambda_{P}}{\\lambda_{\\alpha}}=\\dfrac{m_{\\alpha} v_{\\alpha}}{m_{P} v_{P}} \\Rightarrow \\dfrac{\\lambda}{2 \\lambda}=\\dfrac{4 m \\cdot v_{\\alpha}}{m \\cdot v_{P}}$<br>$\\dfrac{1}{2}=4 \\dfrac{v_{\\alpha}}{v_{P}} \\Rightarrow \\dfrac{v_{P}}{v_{\\alpha}}=\\dfrac{8}{1}$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',2024,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45869,'physics',37,975,2276,'In an $L-C-R$ series AC circuit, the voltage across each of the components $L, C$ and $R$ is $50 \\mathrm{~V}$. The voltage across the $L-C$ combination will be','[{"text": "$50\\\\,V$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$50\\\\sqrt 2 \\\\,V$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$100\\\\,V$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "zero", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'In an $L-C-R$ series AC circuit, the voltage across inductor $L$ leads the current by $90^{\\circ}$ and the voltage across capacitor $C$ lags behind the current by $90^{\\circ}$. <br><img src=\'/img/data/physics/37_45869_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />  <br>Hence, the voltage across $L-C$ combination will be zero.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45870,'physics',41,406,806,'A radioactive nucleus is being produced at a constant rate $\\alpha $ per second. Its decay constant is $\\lambda .$  If ${N_0}$ are  the number of nuclei at time $t = 0,$ then maximum number of nuclei possible are','[{"text": "${N_0}\\\\,\\\\frac{\\\\alpha }{\\\\gamma }$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{\\\\alpha }{\\\\lambda }$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\,\\\\frac{\\\\gamma }{\\\\alpha } + {N_0}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${N_0}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'The rate of change of $N$ is<br>$\\frac{{dN}}{{dt}} = {\\left( {\\frac{{dN}}{{dt}}} \\right)_{formation}} - {\\left( {\\frac{{dN}}{{dt}}} \\right)_{decay}}$<br>$\\frac{{dN}}{{dt}} = \\alpha  - \\lambda N$<br>When $N$ becomes maximum<br>$\\frac{{dN}}{{dt}} = 0 \\Rightarrow \\alpha  - \\lambda {N_{\\max }} = 0$<br>${N_{\\max }} = \\frac{\\alpha }{\\lambda }$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45871,'physics',39,1104,2631,'The de-Broglie wavelength of an electron is the same as that of a photon. If velocity of electron is $25 \\%$ of the velocity of light, then the ratio of $K.E.$ of electron and  $K.E.$ of photon will be','[{"text": "$\\\\dfrac{1}{8}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{1}{1}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{8}{1}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{1}{4}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'$\\lambda_{e}=\\lambda_{\\text {photon }}$<br>Velocity of electron $=25 \\%$ velocity of light<br>Kinetic energy of electron<br>$=E_{k}=\\dfrac{1}{2} p v_{e}=\\dfrac{h v_{e}}{2 \\lambda_{e}}\\left[\\because p=\\dfrac{h}{\\lambda}\\right]$<br>and $K . E$. of photon $=\\dfrac{h c}{\\lambda_{p}}$<br>$\\therefore$ Ratio $=\\dfrac{\\text { K.E. of electron }}{\\text { K.E. of photon }}=\\dfrac{h v_{e}}{2 \\lambda_{e}} \\dfrac{\\lambda_{p}}{h c}$<br>(Given, $\\lambda_{e}=\\lambda_{p}$ )<br>$ = \\frac{{{v_e}}}{{2\\,c}} = \\frac{{0.25\\,c}}{{2\\,c}} = \\frac{{25}}{{200}} = \\frac{1}{8}\\quad \\left( {{v_e} = 0.25\\,c} \\right)$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',2024,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45872,'physics',37,973,2265,'An inductive coil has a resistance of $100 \\Omega$. When an $A C$ signal of frequency $1000\\,Hz$ is applied to the coil, the voltage leads the current by $45^{\\circ}$. The inductance of the coil is','[{"text": "$\\\\dfrac{1}{10 \\\\pi}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{1}{20 \\\\pi}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{1}{40 \\\\pi}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{1}{60 \\\\pi}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'$\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\tan \\phi  = \\frac{{{X_L}}}{R}$<br>$ \\Rightarrow \\tan 45^\\circ  = \\frac{{L\\omega }}{R}$<br>$ \\Rightarrow \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,L = \\frac{R}{\\omega }\\,{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} \\because \\tan 45^\\circ  = 1$<br>$\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, = \\frac{{100}}{{2\\pi (1000)}} = \\frac{1}{{20\\pi }}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45874,'physics',41,406,806,'A small quantity of solution containing  $N{a^{24}}$ radio nuclide of activity 1 microcurie is injected into the blood of a person. A sample of the blood  of volume $1c{m^3}$ taken after 5 hours shows an activity of 296 disintegration per minute. What will be the total volume of the blood in the body of the person? Assume that the radioactive solution mixes uniformly in the blood of the person (Take 1 Curie $ = 3.7 \\times {10^{10}}$ disintegration per second and ${e^{ - \\lambda t}} = 0.7927;$ where $\\lambda  = $ disintegration constant)','[{"text": "2 litres", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "5.94 litres", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "1 litres", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "317 litres", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Let ${R_0}$ is initial activity of whole blood.<br>${R_0} = $ Intial activity  $ = 1$ micro curie<br>$ = 3.7 \\times {10^4}dps$<br> $r = $ Activity in $1\\,c{m^3}$ of  blood at time $t = 5\\,{\\text{hrs,}}$<br>$ = \\frac{{296}}{6}$$dps$ $ = $ 4.93 $dps$ <br>$R = $  Activity of whole blood at time $t = 5\\,{\\text{hr,}}$<br>Total volume should be $V = \\frac{R}{r} = \\frac{{{R_0}{e^{ - \\lambda t}}}}{r}$<br>$ = \\frac{{3.7 \\times {{10}^4} \\times 0.7927}}{{4.93}}$<br>$ = 5.94\\,\\operatorname{liters} $',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45875,'physics',37,976,2280,'An $L-C$ circuit is in the state of resonance. If $C = 0.1\\mu F$ and $L = 0.25H$, neglecting ohmic resistance of circuit, then what is the frequency of oscillations?','[{"text": "$1007\\\\,Hz$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$100\\\\,Hz$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$109\\\\,Hz$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$500\\\\,Hz$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'From the formula, the frequency of<br>oscillation is<br>$f = \\frac{1}{{2\\pi \\sqrt {LC} }} = \\frac{1}{{2\\pi \\sqrt {0.25 \\times 0.1 \\times {{10}^{ - 6}}} }}$<br>$\\,\\,\\,\\, \\approx 1007\\,Hz$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45896,'physics',41,406,809,'In the given reaction<br>${}_Z{X^A} \\to {}_{Z + 1}{Y^A} \\to {}_{Z - 1}{K^{A - 4}} \\to {}_{Z - 1}{K^{A - 4}}$<br>Radioactive radiations are emitted in the sequence','[{"text": "$\\\\beta ,\\\\alpha ,\\\\gamma $", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\alpha ,\\\\beta ,\\\\gamma $", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\beta ,\\\\gamma ,\\\\alpha $", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\gamma ,\\\\alpha ,\\\\beta $", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'${}_Z{X^A}\\xrightarrow{{_{ - 1}{\\beta ^0}}}{}_{Z + 1}{Y^A}\\xrightarrow{{\\left( \\alpha  \\right)}}{}_{Z - 1}{K^{A - 4}}\\xrightarrow{\\gamma }{}_{Z - 1}{K^{A - 4}}$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45877,'physics',41,406,808,'The fossil bone has a $^{14}C{:^{12}}C$ ratio, which is $\\left( {\\frac{1}{{16}}} \\right)$ of that in a living animal bone. If the half-life time of $^{14}C$ is 5730 years then the age of the fossil bone is','[{"text": "11460 years", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "17190 years", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "22920 years", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "45840 years", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Let ${A_{14}}/{A_{12}}$ be the initial ratio of $^{14}C{,^{12}}C$ . Let it be $\\frac{1}{{16}}{A_{14}}/{A_{12}}$ in the fossil. As ${A_{12}}$ remains constant, ${A_{14}}$ must have reduced by $\\frac{1}{{16}}$ during the period.<br>No. of half lives required $ = 4$<br>Age of fossil $ = 4 \\times 5730 = 22920$ years',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45878,'physics',37,975,2275,'A circuit consists of a resistor, an inductor and a capacitor connected in series. If the resistance of a resistor is $25 \\Omega$ and the value of inductive reactance is equal to capacitive reactance, then the net impedance of the circuit will be','[{"text": "$5\\\\,\\\\Omega $", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$25\\\\,\\\\Omega $", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$125\\\\,\\\\Omega $", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$625\\\\,\\\\Omega $", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'The impedance of the $L-C-R$ circuit<br>is given by<br>$\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,Z = \\sqrt {{R^2} + {{\\left( {{X_L} - {X_C}} \\right)}^2}} $<br>$or\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,Z = \\sqrt {{R^2} + {{\\left( {{X_L} - {X_L}} \\right)}^2}}  = R$<br>$\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\left( {\\because {X_L} = {X_C}} \\right)$<br>$\\therefore Z = 25\\Omega \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\left( {\\because R = 25\\Omega } \\right)$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45879,'physics',41,406,808,'Carbon 14 decays with half-life of about 5,800 years. In a sample of bone, the ratio of carbon 14 to carbon  12 is found to be $\\frac{1}{4}$ of what it is in free air. This bone may belong to a period about  $x$ centuries ago, where $x$ is nearest to','[{"text": "$58$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2 \\\\times 58$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$3 \\\\times 58$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$58/2$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'To become $\\frac{1}{4}$$th$,  it requires time of two half lives i.e., $t = 2\\left( {{T_{1/2}}} \\right) = 2 \\times 58$ Centuries',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45880,'physics',40,238,477,'In alpha particle scattering experiment, if ${v}$ is the initial velocity of the particle, then the distance of closest approach is ${d}$. If the velocity is doubled, then the distance of closest approach becomes','[{"text": "${4 d}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${2 d}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\dfrac{d}{2}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\dfrac{d}{4}}$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Relation between velocity of alpha particle and distance of closest approach is given by<br>${v^{2} \\propto \\dfrac{1}{d}}$<br>${\\dfrac{d_{2}}{d_{1}}=\\left(\\dfrac{v_{1}}{v_{2}}\\right)^{2}=\\left(\\dfrac{v}{2 v}\\right)^{2}}$<br>${\\therefore d_{2}=\\dfrac{d}{4}}$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',2024,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45881,'physics',37,977,2770,'A transformer has 500 primary turns and 10 secondary turns. If the secondary has a resistive load of $15 \\Omega$, the currents in the primary and secondary respectively, are','[{"text": "$0.16\\\\,A,3.2 \\\\times {10^{ - 3}}\\\\,A$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$3.2 \\\\times {10^{ - 3}}\\\\,A,0.16\\\\,A$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$0.16\\\\,A,0.16\\\\,A$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$3.2 \\\\times {10^{ - 3}}\\\\,A,3.2 \\\\times {10^{ - 3}}\\\\,A$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'We have, $\\frac{{{N_s}}}{{{N_p}}} = \\frac{{{i_p}}}{{{i_s}}} \\Rightarrow \\frac{{10}}{{500}} = \\frac{{{i_p}}}{{{i_s}}}$<br>$ \\Rightarrow \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\frac{{{i_p}}}{{{i_s}}} = \\frac{1}{{50}} \\Rightarrow {i_s} = 50{i_p}$<br>This condition is satisfied only when<br>current in primary is $3.2 \\times {10^{ - 3}}\\,A$ and<br>in secondary $0.16\\,A.$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45882,'physics',41,406,806,'A radioactive isotope is being produced at a constant rate $X$. Half-life of the radioactive substance is $Y$. After some time, the number of radioactive nuclei become constant. The value of this constant is','[{"text": "$\\\\frac{{XY}}{{\\\\ell n(2)}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$XY$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$(XY)\\\\ell n(2)$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{X}{Y}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Number of nuclei become constant, when<br>$X = \\lambda N \\Rightarrow N = \\frac{X}{\\lambda }$<br>$ = \\frac{{\\frac{X}{{\\ell n\\,2}}}}{Y} = \\frac{{XY}}{{\\ell n(2)}}$ (Given ${T_{12}}/Y$)',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45883,'physics',37,974,2272,'An alternating voltage $V = {V_0}\\sin \\omega t$ is applied across a circuit. As a result, a current $I = {I_0}\\sin (\\omega t - \\pi /2)$ flows in it. The power consumed per cycle is','[{"text": "zero", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$0.5\\\\,{V_0}{I_0}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$0.707\\\\,{V_0}{I_0}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1.414\\\\,{V_0}{I_0}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'The phase angle between voltage $V$ and current $I$ is $\\pi / 2$.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45884,'physics',40,239,478,'The ratio of area of first excited state to ground state of orbit of hydrogen atom is','[{"text": "${1: 16}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${1: 4}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${4: 1}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${16: 1}$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Radius of electron orbit is related with the orbit number as ${r \\propto n^{2}}$.<br>${\\therefore}$ Area ${\\propto r^{2} \\propto n^{4}}$<br>${\\dfrac{A_{1}}{A_{2}}=\\left(\\dfrac{n_{1}}{n_{2}}\\right)^{4}=\\left(\\dfrac{2}{1}\\right)=\\dfrac{16}{1}}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2024,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45885,'physics',41,406,809,'A nucleus $X$ undergoes following transformation<br>$X\\xrightarrow{{ - 2\\alpha }}Y$<br>$Y\\xrightarrow{{ - 4{\\beta ^ - }}}Z$<br>then','[{"text": "$X$ and $Z$ are isobars", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$X$ and $Y$ are isotopes", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$X$ and $Z$ are isotones", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$X$ and $Z$ are isotopes", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'${}_Z^AX\\xrightarrow{{ - 2\\alpha }}{}_{Z - 4}^{A - 8}Y$<br>${}_{Z - 4}^{A - 8}Y\\xrightarrow{{ - 4\\beta }}{}_Z^{A - 8}Z$<br>$X$ & $Z$ are isotopes',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45886,'physics',37,973,2268,'A resistance $R$ and inductance $L$ and a capacitor $C$ all are connected in series with an $AC$ supply. The resistance of $R$ is 16 ohm and for a given frequency, the inductive reactance of $L$ is 24 ohm and capacitive reactance of $C$ is $12\\,ohm$. If the current in the circuit is $5\\,amp$., find the potential difference across $R, L$ and $C$.','[{"text": "$30,20,50\\\\,volt$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$40,100,60\\\\,volt$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$70,110,60\\\\,volt$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$80,120,60\\\\,volt$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'${V_R} = i\\,R = 5 \\times 16 = 80\\,Volt$<br>${V_L} = i \\times (\\omega L) = 5 \\times 24 = 120\\,Volt$<br>${V_C} = i \\times (1/\\omega C) = 5 \\times 12 = 60\\,Volt$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45887,'physics',40,239,478,'Read the Statement - A  and  Statement - B  carefully to mark correct options given below<div class="matching-question"><div class="column-left">Atoms are electrically neutral as they contain equal number of positive and negative charges.</div><div class="column-right">Atoms of each element are stable and emit their characteristic spectrum.</div></div>','[{"text": "Statement A is correct but Statement B is incorrect.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Statement A is incorrect but Statement B is correct.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both statements are correct.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Statements are incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Statement A is true as atoms are electrically neutral because they contain equal number of positive and negative charges.<br>Statement B is wrong as atom of some of the elements are stable and emit characteristic spectrum. But this statement is not true for every atom. Correct option is (1).',NULL,'Hard','MCQ type-2',2024,'NEET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45888,'physics',41,406,809,'An atom of mass number $A$ and atomic number $Z$ emits successively $a$  $\\gamma $-ray, a $\\beta $-particle, and $\\alpha $-particle and $a$ $\\gamma $-ray. The mass number and the atomic number of the end product are:','[{"text": "$A - 4,Z - A$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$A,Z + 1$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$A - 4,Z + 2$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$A - 4,Z - 1$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'When an atom of mass number $A$ and atomic number $Z$ emits $\\gamma $-rays, no change take place in atomic and mass number, but when a $\\beta $-particle is emitted, atomic mass number remain same, but atomic number increase by 1. When an alpha particle is emitted, atomic number is decreased by 2 and atomic mass number is decreased by 4.<br>$_Z^A{X^ * }\\xrightarrow{{\\gamma  - ray}}_Z^AX\\xrightarrow{{\\beta  - particle}}_{Z + 1}^AY\\xrightarrow{{\\alpha  - particle}}$<br>$_{Z - 1}^{A - 4}{Z^ * }\\xrightarrow{{\\gamma  - ray}}_{Z - 1}^{A - 4}Z$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45889,'physics',37,972,2260,'The instantaneous current from an $a.c.$ source is $I = 6\\sin 314\\,t$. What is the rms value of the current?','[{"text": "$3\\\\sqrt 2 \\\\,amp$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$2\\\\sqrt 2 \\\\,amp$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\sqrt 2 \\\\,amp$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2\\\\,amp$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'${I_{rms}} = \\frac{{{I_0}}}{{\\sqrt 2 }} = \\frac{6}{{\\sqrt 2 }} = 3\\sqrt 2 \\,amp.$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45890,'physics',41,404,803,'The binding energies per nucleon for a deutron and an $\\alpha $-particle  are  ${x_1}$ and  ${x_2}$ respectively. What will be the energy  $Q$ released in the following reaction?<br>${}_1{{\\text{H}}^2} + {}_1{{\\text{H}}^2} \\to {}_2{\\text{H}}{{\\text{e}}^4} + {\\text{Q}}$','[{"text": "${\\\\text{4}}\\\\left( {{x_2} - {x_1}} \\\\right)$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${\\\\text{4}}\\\\left( {{x_1} + {x_2}} \\\\right)$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2\\\\left( {{x_2} - {x_1}} \\\\right)$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2\\\\left( {{x_1} + {x_2}} \\\\right)$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'$Q = 4\\left( {{x_2} - {x_1}} \\right)$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45891,'physics',40,240,479,'Match the following columns. Column-I (Spectral Lines of Hydrogen  for transitions from) Column-II (Wavelengths \\[(nm)\\])<div class="matching-question"><div class="column-left">A. ${n_2} = 3$ to ${n_1} = 2$<br>B. ${n_2} = 4$ to ${n_1} = 2$<br>C. ${n_2} = 5$ to ${n_1} = 2$<br>D. ${n_2} = 6$ to ${n_1} = 2$</div><div class="column-right">P. 410.2<br>Q. 434.1<br>R. 656.3<br>S. 486.1</div></div>','[{"text": "A-Q, B-P, C-S, D-R", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "A-R, B-S, C-Q, D-P", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "A-S, B-R, C-P, D-Q", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "A-P, B-Q, C-R, D-S", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Energy difference between spectral lines is<br>$\\Delta E = \\frac{{hc}}{\\lambda }{\\rm{ }}$<br>$\\therefore \\lambda  \\propto \\frac{1}{{\\Delta E}}$<br>${(\\Delta E)_{6-2}>(\\Delta E)_{5-2}>(\\Delta E)_{4-2}>(\\Delta E)_{3-2}}$<br>${\\lambda_{6-2} < \\lambda_{5-2} < \\lambda_{4-2} < \\lambda_{3-2}}$<br>A-R, B-S, C-Q, D-P',NULL,'Hard','Match the following',2024,'NEET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45892,'physics',37,973,2265,'A coil has resistance $30\\,ohm$ and inductive reactance $20\\,ohm$ at $50\\,Hz$ frequency. If an ac source, of $200\\,volt$, $100\\,Hz$, is connected across the coil, the current in the coil will be','[{"text": "$4.0\\\\,A$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$8.0\\\\,A$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$7.2\\\\,A$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2.0\\\\,A$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'If $\\omega=50 \\times 2 \\pi$ then $\\omega L = 20\\,\\Omega $<br>If $\\omega^{\\prime}=100 \\times 2 \\pi$ then ${\\omega ^\\prime }L = 40\\,\\Omega $<br>Current flowing in the coil is<br>$I = \\frac{{200}}{Z} = \\frac{{200}}{{\\sqrt {{R^2} + {{\\left( {{\\omega ^\\prime }L} \\right)}^2}} }} = 4\\,A$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45893,'physics',41,404,803,'Mass numbers of the elements $A,B,C$ and $D$ are 30, 60, 90 and 120 respectively. The specific binding energy of them are $5\\,MeV,8.5\\,MeV,8\\,MeV$ and $7\\,MeV$ respectively. Then, in which of the following reactions energy is released?  $(i){\\rm{ }}D \\to 2B\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,(ii){\\rm{ }}C \\to B + A$<br>  (iii) $B \\to 2A$','[{"text": "In (ii), (iii)", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Only in (i)", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "In (i), (iii)", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "In (i), (ii) and (iii)", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'If the energy is to be released then the products should have more $B$.$E$ (More stable) than the reactants.<br>$D \\to 2B$<br>$120 \\times 7 \\to 2 \\times 60 \\times 8.5\\,\\,\\left( {\\operatorname{possible} } \\right)$<br>$C \\to B + A$<br>$90 \\times 8 \\to 60 \\times 8.5 + 30 \\times 5\\,\\,\\left( {\\operatorname{not} \\,possible} \\right)$<br>$B \\to 2A$<br>$60 \\times 8.5 \\to 2 \\times 30 \\times 5\\,\\,\\left( {not\\,possible} \\right)$<br>In only reaction (i) $D \\to 2B$ the $B$.$E$ is more for products.',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45894,'physics',40,240,479,'The transition from the state ${n=3}$ to ${n=1}$ in a hydrogen like atom results in ultraviolet radiation. Infrared radiation will be obtained in the transition from','[{"text": "${4 \\\\rightarrow 2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${4 \\\\rightarrow 3}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${2 \\\\rightarrow 1}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${3 \\\\rightarrow 2}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Lyman series produces ${U V}$ radiation. Balmer series produces visible radiation. Pachen series produces infrared radiation Least energetic photon in Paschen series is obtained from ${n_{i}=4}$ to ${n_{f}=3}$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45895,'physics',37,977,2770,'The primary winding of a transformer has 100 turns and its secondary winding has 200 turns. The primary is connected to an $A.C.$ supply of $120\\,V$ and the current flowing in it is $10\\,A$. The voltage and the current in the secondary are','[{"text": "$240\\\\,V,5\\\\,A$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$240\\\\,V,10\\\\,A$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$60\\\\,V,20\\\\,A$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$120\\\\,V,20\\\\,A$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'${E_s} = {E_p} \\times \\left( {\\frac{{{n_s}}}{{{n_p}}}} \\right) = 240\\,V$<br>${I_s} = {I_p}\\left( {\\frac{{{n_p}}}{{{n_s}}}} \\right) = 5\\,amp$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45903,'physics',37,977,2770,'Turn ratio is $1: 25$. The step up transformer operates at $230\\,V$and current through secondary is $2\\,A$. Then current in primary is','[{"text": "$25\\\\,A$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$100\\\\,A$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$50\\\\,A$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$20\\\\,A$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Here, $\\frac{{{N_P}}}{{{N_S}}} = \\frac{1}{{25}},{V_P} = 230\\,V,{I_S} = 2\\,A$ <br>For an ideal transformer<br>$\\dfrac{N_{P}}{N_{S}}=\\dfrac{V_{P}}{I_{S}}=\\dfrac{I_{S}}{I_{P}} \\quad$ or $\\dfrac{N_{P}}{N_{S}}=\\dfrac{I_{S}}{I_{P}}$<br>or${I_P} = {I_S} \\times \\frac{{{N_S}}}{{{N_P}}} = 2\\,A \\times \\frac{{25}}{1} = 50\\,A$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45904,'physics',41,410,816,'Match the following columns.<div class="matching-question"><div class="column-left">A. Nuclear fusion<br>B. Nuclear fission<br>C. ${\\beta ^ + }$ decay<br>D. Mass- energy equivalence</div><div class="column-right">P. $E = m{c^2}$<br>Q. Generally possible for nuclei with low atomic number<br>R. Generally possible for nuclei with higher atomic number<br>S. A neutrino is released</div></div>','[{"text": "A - Q\\tB - R\\t       C - S        D - P", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "A - S\\tB - P\\t       C - Q        D - S", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "A - P\\tB - R\\t       C - Q        D - S", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "A - R\\tB - S\\t       C - Q        D - P", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Option (1) is correct',NULL,'Medium','Match the following',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45905,'physics',40,239,478,'The binding energy of an electron with ${n=4}$ in the ${H}$-atom is-','[{"text": "$13.6\\\\,eV$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$3.4\\\\,eV$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1.51\\\\,eV$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$0.85\\\\,eV$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Binding energy (B.E.) ${=\\dfrac{13.6}{n^{2}} {eV}}$<br>For ${n=4}$, B.E. ${=0.85 {eV}}$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45906,'physics',37,973,2265,'The phase difference between the current and voltage is','[{"text": "$45^{\\\\circ}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$30^{\\\\circ}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$50^{\\\\circ}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$90^{\\\\circ}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'The phase difference for an $L R$ circuit between current and voltage is $\\tan ^{-1}\\left(\\dfrac{\\omega L}{R}\\right)$<br>$\\Rightarrow$ Phase difference,<br>$\\phi=\\tan ^{-1}\\left(\\dfrac{100 \\times 20 \\times 10^{-3}}{2}\\right)$<br>$\\Rightarrow \\phi=\\tan ^{-1} 1$ or $\\phi=45^{\\circ}$ where the current is lagging behind the voltage.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/37_45906_question_1.png'),
(45907,'physics',41,408,814,'Nuclear fusion is possible','[{"text": "only betweem light nuclei", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "only between heavy nuclei", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "between both light and heavy nuclei", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "only between nuclei which are stable against $\\\\beta $-decay", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Nuclear fusion is possible only between light nuclei as they become more stable by acquring greater atomic number.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45908,'physics',37,974,2274,'A coil of resistance $10\\, \\Omega$ and an inductance $5\\,H$ is connected to a $100\\,V$ battery. The energy stored in the coil is','[{"text": "325 erg", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$125\\\\,J$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "250 erg", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$250\\\\,J$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Here,$R = 10\\,\\Omega ,L = 5H,V = 100\\,V$<br>The current in the coil is<br>$I = \\frac{V}{R} = \\frac{{100\\,V}}{{10\\,\\Omega }} = 10\\,A$<br>The energy stored in the coil is<br>$U = \\frac{1}{2}L{I^2} = \\frac{1}{2}(5H){(10\\,A)^2} = 250\\,J$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45909,'physics',40,239,478,'${E_n}$  and ${J_{n}}$ denote the total energy and the angular momentum of an electron in the ${n^{\\text {th }}}$ allowed orbit of a Bohr atom. Choose the correct relation between ${E_{n}}$ and ${J_{n}}$','[{"text": "${E_{n} \\\\propto J_{n}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${E_{n} \\\\propto 1 / J_{n}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${E_{n} \\\\propto J_{n}^{2}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${E_{n} \\\\propto 1 / J_{n}^{2}}$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Energy ${E_{n} \\propto \\dfrac{1}{n^{2}}}$<br>Angular momentum, ${J_{n} \\propto n}$<br>${E_{n} \\propto \\dfrac{1}{J_{n}^{2}}}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45910,'physics',41,408,814,'Hydrogen bomb is based upon','[{"text": "fission", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "fusion", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "chemical reaction", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "transmutation", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Hydrogen bomb is based on nuclear fusion.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45911,'physics',37,976,2280,'A circuit has a self inductance of $1H$ and carries a current of $2\\,A$. To prevent sparking, when the circuit is switched off, a capacitor which can withstand $400\\,V$ is used. The least capacitance of capacitor connected across the switch must be equal to','[{"text": "$50\\\\,\\\\mu F$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$25\\\\,\\\\mu F$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$100\\\\,\\\\mu F$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$12.5\\\\,\\\\mu F$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'The least capacitance is such that the energy stored in capacitor is equal to that stored in inductance.<br>If $C$ be required capacitance, then<br>$\\dfrac{1}{2} C V^{2}=\\dfrac{1}{2} L I^{2} \\text { or } C=\\dfrac{L I^{2}}{V^{2}}$<br>Here, $L = 1H,I = 2\\,A,V = 400\\,V$<br>$\\therefore \\quad C{\\rm{ }} = \\frac{{(1H){{(2\\,A)}^2}}}{{{{(400\\,V)}^2}}} = 0.25 \\times {10^{ - 4}}\\,F$<br> $\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,$$ = 25 \\times {10^{ - 6}}\\,F = 25\\mu F$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45912,'physics',41,408,814,'As the age of star increases','[{"text": "Helium quantity increases", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Helium quantity decreases", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Helium quantity does not change", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Helium, Hydrogen both quantities increase", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'In nuclear fusion large amount of $He$ is created from hydrogen in stars. As the age of a star increases then concentration of $He$ also increases.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45913,'physics',40,239,478,'If in the first orbit of a hydrogen atom, the total energy of the electron is ${-21.76 \\times 10^{-19} {~J}}$, then its electric potential energy will be','[{"text": "${-43.52 \\\\times 10^{-19} {~J}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${-21.76 \\\\times 10^{-19} J}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${-10.88 \\\\times 10^{-19} {~J}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${-13.6 \\\\times 10^{-19} {~J}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Potential energy ${=2}$ (Total energy)<br>$\\begin{aligned}& =2 \\times-21.76 \\times 10^{-19} \\\\& =-43.52 \\times 10^{-19} {~J}\\end{aligned}$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(46022,'physics',37,973,2269,'For the circuit shown in figure, the current in the inductance is 0.8 $A$ while in the capacitance is 0.6 $A$ . What is the current drawn from the source  ?','[{"text": "$0.2\\\\,A$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$0.7\\\\,A$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$0.5\\\\,A$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$0.3\\\\,A$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'As $I_{L}=\\dfrac{\\varepsilon}{X_{L}} \\sin \\left(\\omega t-\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\right)=-0.8 \\cos \\omega t$,<br>${I_C} = \\frac{\\varepsilon }{{{X_C}}}\\sin \\left( {\\omega t + \\frac{\\pi }{2}} \\right) =  + 0.6\\cos \\omega t,$<br>$I = {I_L} + {I_C} =  - 0.2\\cos \\omega t$<br>$\\left| {{I_0}} \\right| = 0.2\\,\\,A$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/37_46022_question_1.png'),
(45914,'physics',37,975,2275,'An $L C R$ circuit contains $R = 50\\,\\Omega ,L = 1mH$ and $C = 0.1\\mu F$. The impedance of the circuit will be minimum for a frequency of','[{"text": "$\\\\frac{{{{10}^5}}}{{2\\\\pi }}Hz$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{{{{10}^6}}}{{2\\\\pi }}Hz$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2\\\\pi  \\\\times {10^5}\\\\,Hz$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2\\\\pi  \\\\times {10^6}\\\\,Hz$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'The impedance of the $L C R$ circuit will be minimum for a resonant frequency $v_{r}$.<br>$v_{r}=\\dfrac{1}{2 \\pi \\sqrt{L C}}$<br>Here, $L = 1mH = 1 \\times {10^{ - 3}}H$<br>$C = 0.1\\mu F = 0.1 \\times {10^{ - 6}}\\,F$<br>$\\therefore {v_r} = \\frac{1}{{2\\pi \\sqrt {\\left( {1 \\times {{10}^{ - 3}}H} \\right)\\left( {0.1 \\times {{10}^{ - 6}}F} \\right)} }}$<br>$ = \\frac{{{{10}^5}}}{{2\\pi }}Hz$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45915,'physics',41,408,814,'The nuclear fusion reaction between deuterium and tritium takes place','[{"text": "at low temperature amd low pressure", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "at very high temperature and very high  pressure", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "when the temperature is near absolute zero", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "at ordinary temperature and pressure.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'The nuclear fusion reaction between deuterium and tritium  takes place at very high temperature and very high pressure.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45916,'physics',40,239,478,'When a hydrogen atom is excited from ground state to first excited state, then which of the following options incorrect?','[{"text": "The kinetic energy increases by $10.2\\\\,eV$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "The kinetic energy decreases by $10.2\\\\,eV$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "The potential energy increases by $20.4\\\\,eV$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "The angular momentum increases by $1.05 \\\\times 10^{-34} {Js}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'${|\\Delta K|=13.6\\left[\\dfrac{1}{1^{2}}-\\dfrac{1}{2^{2}}\\right]=10.2 {eV}}$<br>${|\\Delta U|=27.2\\left[\\dfrac{1}{1^{2}}-\\dfrac{1}{2^{2}}\\right]=20.4 {eV}}$<br>${\\Delta L=\\dfrac{2 h}{2 \\pi}-\\dfrac{h}{2 \\pi}=\\dfrac{h}{2 \\pi}}$<br>So correct option is (1).',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45917,'physics',37,977,2770,'The turns ratio of a transformer is $3: 2$. If the current through the primary coil is $3\\,A$, then the current through load resistance is','[{"text": "$1\\\\,A$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$4.5\\\\,A$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2\\\\,A$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$1.5\\\\,A$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'For a transformer,<br>$\\dfrac{I_{p}}{I_{s}}=\\dfrac{N_{s}}{N_{p}}$ or $I_{s}=\\left(\\dfrac{N_{p}}{N_{s}}\\right) I_{p}$<br>where $I_{p}, N_{p}$ be the current and number of turns in the primary coil and $I_{s}, N_{s}$ be corresponding quantities in the secondary coil.<br>Here, ${I_p} = 3A,\\frac{{{N_s}}}{{{N_p}}} = \\frac{3}{2}.$<br>$\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\left( {\\because {\\text{Turns}}\\,{\\text{ratio}} = \\frac{{{N_s}}}{{{N_p}}}} \\right)$<br>$\\therefore \\quad {I_s} = \\left( {\\frac{2}{3}} \\right)(3\\,A) = 2\\,A$<br>Hence the current through load resistance is  $2\\,A$.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45918,'physics',41,408,813,'Thermal neutrons are those which','[{"text": "Move with high velocities", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Are at very high temperature", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Are at rest", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Have kinetic energies similar to those of surrounding molecules", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'When fast moving neutrons pass through a moderator, they collide with the molecules of the moderator. As a result of this the neutrons are in thermal equillibrium with the surrounding molecules of moderator. These neutrons are called thermal neutrons.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45919,'physics',37,973,2265,'In an AC circuit, the potential difference across an inductance and resistance joined in series are respectively $16\\,V$ and $20\\,V$. The total potential difference across the circuit is','[{"text": "$20.0\\\\,V$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$25.6\\\\,V$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$31.9\\\\,V$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$33.6\\\\,V$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Here,${V_L} = 16\\,V,{V_R} = 20\\,V$<br>The potential difference across the series<br>$L R$ circuit is $V=\\sqrt{V_{L}^{2}+V_{R}^{2}}$<br>$ = \\sqrt {{{(16\\,V)}^2} + {{(20\\,V)}^2}}  = 25.6\\,V$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45920,'physics',40,242,481,'An electron is in an excited state of hydrogen-like atom has a total energy of $-3.4eV$. If the kinetic energy of the electron is ${E}$ and its de Broglie wavelength is ${\\lambda}$, then','[{"text": "${E=6.8 {eV}, \\\\lambda=6.6 \\\\times 10^{-10} {~m}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${E=3.4 {eV}, \\\\lambda=6.6 \\\\times 10^{-10} {~m}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${E=3.4 {eV}, \\\\lambda=6.6 \\\\times 10^{-11} {~m}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${E=6.8 {eV}, \\\\lambda=6.6 \\\\times 10^{-11} {~m}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'$K.E. = |T.E| = 3.4\\,eV$<br>$\\lambda  = \\frac{{12.27}}{{\\sqrt V }} = \\frac{{12.27}}{{\\sqrt {3.4} }} = 6.6$ $ \\mathop A^{~~\\circ}  $<br>So, correct option is (2).',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
