(45929,'physics',40,240,479,'In the figure, six lines of emission spectrum are shown. Which of them will be absent in the absorption spectrum?','[{"text": "${1,2,3}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${1,4,6}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${4,5,6}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${1,2,3,4,5,6}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'The photon\'s with energies equal to that required for upward transition ${A \\rightarrow X, A \\rightarrow B}$ and ${A \\rightarrow C}$ would be absorbed, these photons shall be absent from absorption spectrum.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/40_45929_question_1.png'),
(45930,'physics',37,977,2770,'The output voltage of a transformer connected to $220\\, V$ line is $1100\\,V$ at $2 A$ current. Its efficiency is $100\\% $. The current coming from the line is','[{"text": "$20\\\\,A$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$10\\\\,A$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$11\\\\,A$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$22\\\\,A$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Here, $\\eta=100 \\%=1$<br>$\\eta=\\dfrac{P_{S}}{P_{P}} \\Rightarrow P_{S}=P_{P}$ or $V_{S} I_{S}=V_{P} I_{P}$<br>or $1100 \\times 2=220 \\times I_{P}$<br>$\\therefore \\quad {I_P} = 10\\,A$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45931,'physics',41,408,812,'In a fission process, nucleus $A$ divides into two nuclei  $B$ and $C$, their binding energies being ${E_a},{E_b}$ and ${E_c}$ respectively. Then','[{"text": "${E_b} + {E_c} < {E_a}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${E_b}.{E_c} = {E_a}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${E_b} + {E_c} > {E_a}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${E_b} + {E_c} = {E_a}$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,NULL,NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45933,'physics',37,972,2260,'Using an $AC$ voltmeter the potential difference in the electrical line in a house is read to be $234\\,V$. If line frequency is known to be 50<br> cycles $/s$, the equation for the line voltage is','[{"text": "$V=165 \\\\sin (100\\\\, \\\\pi t)$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$V=331 \\\\sin (100\\\\, \\\\pi t)$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$V=220 \\\\sin (100\\\\, \\\\pi t)$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$V=440 \\\\sin (100\\\\, \\\\pi t)$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Here, ${V_{rms}} = 234\\,V$,<br>${V_0} = {V_{rms}}\\sqrt 2  = 234\\sqrt 2  = 331\\,V$<br>$\\omega  = 50\\,{\\text{cycles}}\\,{\\text{/s}} = 100\\,\\pi \\,rad{s^{ - 1}}$<br>$\\therefore \\quad$ Line voltage eqn., $V=V_{0} \\sin \\omega t$<br>$V=331 \\sin (100 \\,\\pi t)$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45934,'physics',41,406,809,'In the disintegration series $_{92}^{238}{\\text{U}}\\xrightarrow{{ - \\alpha }}X\\xrightarrow{{ - {\\beta ^ - }}}_Z^AY$ the values of $Z$ and $A$ respectively will be','[{"text": "$88,230$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$92,236$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$91,234$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$90,234$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'${}_{92}{{\\text{U}}^{238}}\\xrightarrow{{ - \\alpha }}{}_{90}{X^{234}}\\xrightarrow{{ - {\\beta ^ - }}}{}_{91}{Y^{234}}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45937,'physics',37,977,2770,'In a step-up transformer, the turn ratio is 1 : 2. Aleclanche cell (emf=1.5 $V$) is connected across the primary. The voltage developed in the secondary will be','[{"text": "3.0 $V$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "0.75$V$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "1.5 $V$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "zero", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Given turn ratio,<br>${\\dfrac{N_{p}}{N_{S}}=\\dfrac{1}{2}}$<br>Also, ${V_{p}=1.5 {~V}}$<br>${\\because}$ Transformer operates over only alternating source of emf and here voltage is applied by Leclanche cell. <br>${\\therefore \\quad}$ Voltage across secondary will be zero.<br>So correct option is (4)',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
