(45951,'physics',42,324,594,'A photodiode is a special purpose $p$-$n$ junction diode fabricated with a transparent window to allow light to fall on the diode. It is operated under','[{"text": "Reverse bias", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Forward bias", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both reverse and forward bias", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "No biasing is required", "isCorrect": false}]',0,NULL,NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45953,'physics',41,406,806,'The half-life of a radioactive substance is 48 hours. How much time will it take to disintegrate to its $\\frac{1}{{16}}th$ part?','[{"text": "$16\\\\,h$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$12\\\\,h$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$192\\\\,h$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$48\\\\,h$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'$\\frac{N}{{{N_0}}} = {\\left( {\\frac{1}{2}} \\right)^n} \\Rightarrow \\frac{1}{{16}} = {\\left( {\\frac{1}{2}} \\right)^n}$<br>$ \\Rightarrow n = 4 \\Rightarrow t = 4 \\times T = 192\\,{\\mathop{\\rm hours}\\nolimits} {\\rm{.}}$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45954,'physics',40,245,487,'If elements with principal quantum number $n>4$ were not allowed in nature, then the number of possible elements would be','[{"text": "32", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "60", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "18", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "4", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'If all the elements having $n>4$ are removed the number of elements that will be present in the periodic table are calculated as<br>(1) $n=1$, represents $K$ shell and the number of elements having $K$ shell $=2$ [in accordance with $2 n^{2}$ ]<br>(2) $n=2$, represents $L$ shell and the number of elements having $L$ shell $=8$<br>(3) $n=3$, represents $M$ shell and the number of elements having $M$ shell $=18$<br>(4) $n=4$, represents $N$ shell and the number of elements having $N$ shell$=32$<br>So, the total number of elements having $n < 5$ are $2+8+18+32=60$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45958,'physics',40,239,478,'The energy of an electron in excited hydrogen atom is $ - 3.4\\,eV$. Then, according to Bohr\'s theory, the angular momentum of the electron is','[{"text": "$2.1 \\\\times {10^{ - 34}}\\\\,J - s$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$3 \\\\times {10^{ - 34}}\\\\,J - s$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2 \\\\times {10^{ - 34}}\\\\,J - s$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$0.5 \\\\times {10^{ - 34}}\\\\,J - s$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'According to the question,<br>energy, $E =  - 3.4eV$<br>This corresponds to $n=2$.<br>So, the angular momentum of the electron,<br> $m v r=\\dfrac{n h}{2 \\pi}=\\dfrac{2 \\times 6.626 \\times 10^{-34}}{2 \\times 3.14}$<br>$\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, = \\frac{{6.626 \\times {{10}^{ - 34}}}}{{3.14}}$<br>$\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, = 2.1 \\times {10^{ - 34}}\\,J - s$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45960,'physics',37,974,2272,'In an $A.C.$ circuit, $V$ and $I$ are given by${V}=50 \\sin [1000 {t}]$ volt and$I=500 \\sin \\left[1000 t+\\dfrac{\\pi}{3}\\right] {mA}$. What would the power dissipated in the circuit  ?','[{"text": "$5.6\\\\,W$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$13.7\\\\,W$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$8.6\\\\,W$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$10.8\\\\,W$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'$P = {V_{rms{\\rm{ }}}} \\times {I_{rms{\\rm{ }}}} \\times \\cos \\phi $<br>$ = \\frac{{{V_0}}}{{\\sqrt 2 }} \\times \\frac{{{I_0}}}{{\\sqrt 2 }} \\times \\cos \\phi $<br>$ = \\frac{{50}}{{\\sqrt 2 }} \\times \\frac{{500 \\times {{10}^{ - 3}}}}{{\\sqrt 2 }} \\times \\cos \\frac{\\pi }{3}$<br>$\\therefore \\quad P=12.5 \\times \\dfrac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}=10.8 {~W}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45961,'physics',41,406,806,'Acitivity of radioactive element decreased to one third of original activity ${R_0}$ in 9 years. After further 9 years, its acitivity will be','[{"text": "$\\\\frac{2}{3}{R_0}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${R_0}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${R_0}/9$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${R_0}/6$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Activity ${\\rm{R  =  }}{{\\rm{R}}_0}\\,{e^{ - \\lambda t}}$<br>$\\frac{{{{\\rm{R}}_{\\rm{0}}}}}{{\\rm{3}}} = {{\\rm{R}}_0}\\,{e^{ - \\lambda  \\times 9}} \\Rightarrow {e^{ - 9\\lambda }} = \\frac{1}{3}$<br>After further 9 years $R = {R_0}{e^{ - 18\\lambda }} = {R_0}{e^{ - 9\\lambda }}{e^{ - 9\\lambda }}$<br>$ = {R_0}\\left( {\\frac{1}{3}} \\right)\\left( {\\frac{1}{3}} \\right) = \\frac{{{R_0}}}{9}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45964,'physics',40,239,478,'The radius of an electron orbit in a hydrogen atom is of the order of','[{"text": "${10^{ - 8}}\\\\,m$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${10^{ - 9}}\\\\,m$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${10^{ - 11}}\\\\,m$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${10^{ - 10}}\\\\,m$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Radius of $n$th orbit,  ${r_n} = 0.53\\frac{{{n^2}}}{Z}$ $ \\mathop A^{~~\\circ}  $<br>Here, $n=1$ and for hydrogen atom, $Z=1$<br>$\\therefore {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {r_1} = 0.53\\frac{{{{(1)}^2}}}{1}$  $ \\mathop A^{~~\\circ}  $   $ = 0.53 \\times {10^{ - 10}}\\,m$<br>$\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, = 5.3 \\times {10^{ - 11}}\\,m$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45965,'physics',41,406,806,'1 milli curie is equal to','[{"text": "$3.7 \\\\times {10^7}$ disintegration/sec", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$3 \\\\times {10^{10}}$ disintegration/sec", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$3.7 \\\\times {10^7}$ disintegration/sec", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$5 \\\\times {10^7}$ disintegration/sec", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'1 milli curie $ = {10^{ - 3}} \\times 3.7 \\times {10^{10}}$<br>$ = 3.7 \\times {10^7}$ disintegration/sec',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45968,'physics',41,406,806,'The half life period of a radioactive element $X$ is same as the mean life time of another radioactive element $Y$. Initially both of them have the same number of atoms. Then','[{"text": "$X$ and $Y$ decay at the same rate always", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$X$and $Y$ have the same decay rate initially", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$X$ will decay at a faster rate than $Y$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$Y$ will decay at a faster rate than $X$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'${({T_{1/2}})_x} = {({t_{mean}})_y}$<br>$ \\Rightarrow \\frac{{0.693}}{{{\\lambda _x}}} = \\frac{1}{{{\\lambda _y}}} \\Rightarrow {\\lambda _x} = 0.693{\\lambda _y}{\\rm{ }} \\Rightarrow {\\rm{ }}{\\lambda _x} < {\\lambda _y}$<br>Also rate of decay $ = \\lambda N$<br>Initially number of atoms $(N)$ of both are equal but since  ${\\rm{ }}{\\lambda _x} < {\\lambda _y},$ therefore, $y$ will decay at a faster rate than $x$.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45969,'physics',40,245,487,'White light is passed through a dilute solution of potassium permanganate. The spectrum produced by the emergent light is','[{"text": "band emission spectrum", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "line emission spectrum", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "band absorption spectrum", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "line absorption spectrum", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'The spectrum produced by emergent light is band absorption spectrum.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45970,'physics',37,972,2259,'Find the average value of current (in $A$) shown graphically in figure from ${t=0}$ to ${t=2 {~s}}$.','[{"text": "$5\\\\,A$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$3\\\\,A$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$7\\\\,A$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$9\\\\,A$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'From the ${i-t}$ graph (figure), area from ${t=0}$ to ${t=2 {~s}}$<br>$\\frac{1}{2} \\times 2 \\times 10 = 10$ As<br>${\\therefore}$ Average current ${=\\dfrac{10}{2}=5 {~A}}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/37_45970_question_1.png'),
(45972,'physics',41,408,812,'If $200\\,MeV$ of energy is released in the fission of 1 nucleus of $_{92}{U^{235}}$, then the number of nuclei that undergo fission to produce energy of $10\\,kWh$  in $1s$, is:','[{"text": "$11.25 \\\\times {10^{18}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$22.5 \\\\times {10^{17}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$11.25 \\\\times {10^{17}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$22.5 \\\\times {10^{18}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'As $P = n\\left( {\\frac{E}{t}} \\right)$<br>$ \\Rightarrow 10kWh = n\\left[ {\\frac{{200MeV}}{t}} \\right]$<br>$ \\Rightarrow 10 \\times {10^3} \\times 60 \\times 60 = n \\times 200 \\times {10^6} \\times 1.6 \\times {10^{ - 19}}$<br>$(\\because t = 1s)$<br>$\\therefore n = \\frac{{10 \\times {{10}^3} \\times 60 \\times 60}}{{200 \\times {{10}^6} \\times 1.6 \\times {{10}^{ - 19}}}} = 11.25 \\times {10^{17}}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45973,'physics',40,241,480,'If the energy of hydrogen atom in $n$ th orbit is $E_{n}$, then energy in the $n$th orbit of a singly ionised helium atom will be','[{"text": "$4 E_{n}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$E_{n} / 4$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2 E_{n}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$E_{n} / 2$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Since, we know, the energy of an<br>electron in H-like atom in $n$th orbit,<br>$\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{E_n} = \\frac{{ - 13.6}}{{{n^2}}}{Z^2}$<br>For hydrogen atom,<br>${\\mkern 1mu} \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{E_{{n_1}}} = \\frac{{ - 13.6}}{{{n^2}}}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\left( {\\,\\rlap{--} Z = 1} \\right)\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\left( 1 \\right)$<br>For ${\\text{H}}{{\\text{e}}^ + }$ ion,<br>$\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{E_{{n_2}}} = \\frac{{ - 13.6{{(2)}^2}}}{{{n^2}}}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\left( 2 \\right)$<br>From Eqs. (1) and (2), we get<br>$\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\frac{{{E_{{n_2}}}}}{{{E_{{n_1}}}}} = {(2)^2}$<br>$ \\Rightarrow \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{E_{{n_2}}} = 4{E_{{n_1}}}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45976,'physics',41,406,806,'The half-life of $^{215}At$ is $100\\,\\mu s$ . The time taken for the radioactivity of a sample of $^{215}At$ to  decay $1/16th$ of its initial value is','[{"text": "$400\\\\,\\\\mu s$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$6.3\\\\,\\\\mu s$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$40\\\\,\\\\mu s$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$300\\\\,\\\\mu s$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'For radioactive disintegration,<br>$N = {N_0}{\\left( {\\frac{1}{2}} \\right)^n},$ where $n = $ number of half-lives<br>$\\frac{{{N_0}}}{{16}} = {N_0}{\\left( {\\frac{1}{2}} \\right)^n}\\,\\,or\\,{\\left( {\\frac{1}{2}} \\right)^4} = {\\left( {\\frac{1}{2}} \\right)^n}$<br>$\\therefore n = 4$<br>$\\therefore t = 4 \\times 100\\,\\mu s$<br>$ \\Rightarrow t = 400\\,\\mu s$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45979,'physics',40,239,478,'In which of the following systems will the radius of the first orbit $(n=1$ ) be minimum?','[{"text": "Deuterium atom", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Hydrogen atom", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Doubly ionised lithium", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Singly ionised helium", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Radius of $n$th Bohr\'s orbit of<br>hydrogen like atom,<br>${\\mkern 1mu} \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{r_n} \\propto \\frac{{{n^2}}}{Z}$<br>where, $Z$ is the atomic number.<br>For first orbit.<br>$\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,n = 1$<br>$\\therefore \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{r_n} \\propto \\frac{1}{Z}$<br>Now, for radius of 1 st orbit to be minimum, its atomic number must be greater. Here, in the problem, atomic number $Z$ is greater for double ionised lithium, $i.e.\\,\\,Z = 3$. Hence, for the radius of 1 st orbit will be minimum.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45980,'physics',41,406,806,'Half-life of a radioactive substance is 5 min. The time between 20% and 80% decay will be:','[{"text": "$20\\\\,\\\\min $", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$30\\\\,\\\\min $", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$10\\\\,\\\\min $", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$25\\\\,\\\\min $", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'For 20% decay,  ${N_1} = 0.8{N_0}$<br>For 80% decay, ${N_2} = 0.2{N_0}$<br>$ \\Rightarrow {N_2} = \\frac{{{N_1}}}{4}$<br>$ \\Rightarrow 2$ Half-lives have take place between 80% decay and 20% decay<br>$t = 2{T_{1/2}} = 2 \\times 5 = 10\\min $',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45983,'physics',41,406,806,'Mean life of a radioactive sample is 100$s$. Then, its half-life (in min) is','[{"text": "$0.693$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${10^{ - 4}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1.155$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Mean life, $\\tau  = {T_{1/2}}/0.693$<br>$\\therefore \\,\\,\\,\\,{T_{1/2}} = \\frac{{100}}{{1.44}} = 69.44s = \\frac{{69.44}}{{60}} = 1.155\\min $',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45984,'physics',40,239,478,'The Bohr model of atoms','[{"text": "assumes that the angular momentum of electrons is quantised", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "uses Einstein\'s photoelectric equation", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "predicts continuous emission spectra for atoms", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "predicts the same emission spectra for all types of atoms", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'According to Bohr\'s hypothesis, electron can revolve only in those orbits in which its angular momentum is an integral multiple of $\\dfrac{h}{2 \\pi}$, where $h$ being the Planck\'s constant.In these orbits, angular momentum of electron can have magnitude as $\\frac{{h\'}}{{2\\pi \'}}\\frac{{2h}}{{2\\pi \'}}\\frac{{3h}}{{2\\pi }}, \\ldots ,$ etc, but can never have magnitude as $\\dfrac{1.5 h}{2 \\pi^{\\prime}}, \\dfrac{2.5 h}{2 \\pi^{\\prime}}, \\dfrac{3.5 h}{2 \\pi}, \\ldots$,<br>etc. This is called the quantisation of angular momentum.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(46051,'physics',41,408,812,'Nuclear energy is released in fission since binding energy per nucleon is','[{"text": "smaller for fission fragments than for parent nucleus", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "the same for fission fragments and parent nucleus", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "larger for fission fragments than for parent nucleus", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "sometimes larger and sometimes smaller", "isCorrect": false}]',2,NULL,NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45985,'physics',37,973,2266,'A series ${R}$ - ${C}$ combination is connected to an ac voltage of angular frequency $\\omega  = 500\\,rad{s^{ - 1}}$. If the impedance of the ${R-C}$ circuit is ${R \\sqrt{1.25}}$, the time constant  of the circuit is','[{"text": "$1\\\\,ms$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$7\\\\,ms$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$4\\\\,ms$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$3\\\\,ms$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Given, $\\omega  = 500\\,rad{s^{ - 1}}$ and ${Z=R \\sqrt{1.25}}$<br>As impedance. ${Z=\\sqrt{R^{2}+\\left(\\dfrac{1}{\\omega C}\\right)^{2}}=R \\sqrt{1.25}}$<br>${R^{2}(1.25)=R^{2}+\\left(\\dfrac{1}{\\omega C}\\right)^{2} \\Rightarrow \\dfrac{1}{\\omega C}=0.5 R}$<br>or time constant (in millisecond), $i.e.$<br>${R C=\\dfrac{1}{0.5 \\times 500 {~s}^{-1}}=0.004 {~s}=4 {~ms}}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45986,'physics',42,325,596,'The maximum efficiency of full-wave rectifier is','[{"text": "$81.2\\\\% $", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$100\\\\% $", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$40.2\\\\,\\\\% $", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$25.20\\\\,\\\\% $", "isCorrect": false}]',0,NULL,NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45987,'physics',41,406,806,'If the half-life of any sample of radioactive susbtance is 4 days, then the fraction of sample will remain undecayed after 2 days, will be','[{"text": "$\\\\sqrt 2 $", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{1}{{\\\\sqrt 2 }}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{{\\\\sqrt 2  - 1}}{{\\\\sqrt 2 }}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{1}{2}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'As we know, $N = {N_0}{\\left( {\\frac{1}{2}} \\right)^n}$<br>Where, $n$ is number of half-lives.<br>$n = \\frac{t}{{{T_{1/2}}}} = \\frac{2}{4} = \\frac{1}{2}$<br>$ \\Rightarrow \\frac{N}{{{N_0}}} = {\\left( {\\frac{1}{2}} \\right)^{1/2}} = \\frac{1}{{\\sqrt 2 }}$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45989,'physics',40,240,479,'In terms of Rydberg constant $R$, the waves number of the first Balmer line is','[{"text": "$R$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$3 R$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{5 R}{36}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{8 R}{9}$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Wave number is given by$v=\\dfrac{1}{\\lambda}=R\\left(\\dfrac{1}{n_{1}^{2}}-\\dfrac{1}{n_{2}^{2}}\\right)$<br>${\\rm{Given}}\\quad {n_1} = 2,{n_2} = 3{\\rm{ }}$<br>$ \\Rightarrow v = R\\left( {\\frac{1}{{{2^2}}} - \\frac{1}{{{3^2}}}} \\right)$<br>$\\therefore \\quad v = \\frac{{5R}}{{36}}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45991,'physics',41,406,806,'During mean life of a radioactive element, the fraction that disintegrates is','[{"text": "$\\\\frac{1}{e}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{{e - 1}}{e}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$e$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{e}{{e - 1}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'By using  $N = {N_0}{e^{ - \\lambda t}}$ and average life 	time $t = \\frac{1}{\\lambda },$ <br>$N = {N_0}{e^{ - \\lambda  \\times (1/\\lambda )}} = {N_0}{e^{ - 1}} \\Rightarrow \\frac{N}{{{N_0}}} = {e^{ - 1}} = \\frac{1}{e}$<br>Now disintegrated fraction <br>$ = 1 - \\frac{N}{{{N_0}}} = 1 - \\frac{1}{e} = \\frac{{e - 1}}{e}$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45993,'physics',42,323,593,'Zener diode is used as','[{"text": "DC voltage stabiliser", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "AC voltage stabiliser", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Amplifier", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Detector", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'For a wide range of values of load resistance, the current in the Zener diode may change but the voltage across it remains unaffected. Thus the output voltage across the Zener diode is a regulated voltage.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45994,'physics',40,240,479,'Calculate the highest frequency of the emitted photon in the Paschen series of spectral lines of the hydrogen atom.','[{"text": "$3.7 \\\\times {10^{14}}\\\\,Hz$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$9.1 \\\\times {10^{15}}\\\\,Hz$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$10.23 \\\\times {10^{14}}\\\\,Hz$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$29.7 \\\\times {10^{15}}\\\\,Hz$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'The frequencies of the emitted<br>photon in the Paschen series are given by,<br>$v=\\operatorname{Rc}\\left(\\dfrac{1}{3^{2}}-\\dfrac{1}{n^{2}}\\right)$, where $n=4,5,6, \\ldots .$.<br>The highest frequency corresponds to<br>$n=\\infty$.<br>$\\therefore v_{\\text {highest }}=\\dfrac{R c}{9}=\\dfrac{1.097 \\times 10^{7} \\times 3 \\times 10^{8}}{9}$<br>$\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, = 0.37 \\times {10^{15}}\\,{s^{ - 1}}$<br>$\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, = 3.7 \\times {10^{14}}\\,{s^{ - 1}}$<br>$\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, = 3.7 \\times {10^{14}}\\,Hz$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45995,'physics',41,406,806,'$N$ atoms of a radioactive element emit $n$ alpha particles per second. The half-life of the element is','[{"text": "$\\\\frac{N}{n}\\\\sec $", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{n}{N}\\\\sec $", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{{0.693n}}{N}\\\\sec $", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{{0.693N}}{n}\\\\sec $", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'$\\frac{{dN}}{{dt}} =  - \\lambda N \\Rightarrow n =  - \\lambda N$ $\\left( {{\\rm{Given}}\\frac{{dN}}{{dt}} = n} \\right)$<br>$\\therefore \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\lambda  =  - \\frac{n}{N}$<br>$\\therefore \\,$ Half life $ = \\frac{{0.693}}{\\lambda } = \\frac{{0.693N}}{n}\\,\\,$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45997,'physics',42,325,595,'If the input frequency of half-wave rectifier is $n Hz$ $ac$, then its output is','[{"text": "A constant $dc$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$n$/2 $Hz$ pulsating $dc$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$n Hz$ pulsating $dc$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "2$n$ $Hz$ pulsating $dc$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'For half wave rectifier output wave form has the same frequency.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(45998,'physics',41,406,806,'The half-life of radium is 1620 years and its atomic weight is 266. The number of atoms that will decay from its $1g$ sample per second will be:','[{"text": "$3.1 \\\\times {10^{10}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$3.6 \\\\times {10^{12}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$3.6 \\\\times {10^{10}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$31.1 \\\\times {10^{15}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'According to Avogadro’s hypothesis,<br>${N_0} = \\frac{{6.02 \\times {{10}^{23}}}}{{266}} = 2.66 \\times {10^{21}}$<br>Half - time $ = T = \\frac{{0.693}}{\\lambda } = 1620\\,{\\rm{years}}$<br>$\\therefore \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\lambda  = \\frac{{0.693}}{{1620 \\times 3.16 \\times {{10}^7}}}$<br>$\\frac{{dN}}{{dt}} = \\lambda {N_0} = 3.6 \\times {10^{10}}$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(46006,'physics',41,406,806,'The units of radioactivity is','[{"text": "Fermi", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Farad", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Curie", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Hertz", "isCorrect": false}]',2,NULL,NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(46007,'physics',37,973,2268,'In the circuit shown in figure, the voltage across resistance ${R}$, box ${A}$ and box ${B}$ are represented as  $V_{R}=V \\sin (\\omega t), V_{A}=\\sqrt{2} V \\sin \\left(\\omega t+\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}\\right)$<br>and ${V_{B}=V \\sin \\left(\\omega t+\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\right)}$<br>Find the phase difference (in degrees) between current and the applied voltage.','[{"text": "$30^\\\\circ $", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$45^\\\\circ $", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$29^\\\\circ $", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$70^\\\\circ $", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'In the figure shown, $V_{0}$ represents the phasor associated with the source voltage.<br>$\\tan \\phi=\\dfrac{2 V}{2 V}=1$  <img src=\'/img/data/physics/37_46007_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/37_46007_question_1.png'),
(46008,'physics',42,323,593,'The main cause of Zener breakdown is','[{"text": "The base semiconductor being germanium", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Production of electron - hole pairs due to thermal exitation", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Low doping", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "High doping", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Zener diode is similar to $p$-$n$ junction diode in forward bias, condition, But in reverse bias condition it conducts above its breakdown 	voltage/threshold.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
