(46032,'physics',41,406,809,'In a reaction $_{92}B{e^{234}}{ \\to _{88}}{Y^{218}}$, the number of $\\alpha \\,$ and $\\beta $ particles emitted are','[{"text": "$4,\\\\,\\\\,4$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$1,\\\\,\\\\,6$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$4,\\\\,\\\\,8$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$4,\\\\,\\\\,2$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'$\\mathop {Be}\\limits_{92}^{234}  \\to \\mathop Y\\limits_{88}^{218}  + x\\mathop \\alpha \\limits_2^4  + y\\mathop \\beta \\limits_{ - 1}^o $<br>By balancing the proton and mass numbers on both sides.<br>$92 = 88 + 2x - y\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,(1)$<br>$234 = 218 + 4x\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,(2)$<br>From eq’s (1) & (2)<br>$x = 4\\& y = 4$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(46034,'physics',40,240,479,'Taking Rydberg\'s constant ${R_H} = 1.097 \\times {10^7}\\,m$, first and second wavelength of Balmer series in hydrogen spectrum is','[{"text": "$2000$  $ \\\\mathop A^{~~\\\\circ}  $,  $3000$ $ \\\\mathop A^{~~\\\\circ}  $", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1575$ $ \\\\mathop A^{~~\\\\circ}  $ ,    $2960$ $ \\\\mathop A^{~~\\\\circ}  $", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$6529$ $ \\\\mathop A^{~~\\\\circ}  $ ,          $4280$  $ \\\\mathop A^{~~\\\\circ}  $", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$6552$  $ \\\\mathop A^{~~\\\\circ}  $,          $4863$  $ \\\\mathop A^{~~\\\\circ}  $", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'$\\frac{1}{\\lambda } = R\\left[ {\\frac{1}{{n_1^2}} - \\frac{1}{{n_2^2}}} \\right]$. For first wavelength, ${n_1} = 2,{n_2} = 3$<br>$ \\Rightarrow {\\lambda _1} = 6563$  $ \\mathop A^{~~\\circ}  $<br>For second wavelength, ${n_1} = 2,{n_2} = 4 \\Rightarrow {\\lambda _2} = 4861$ $ \\mathop A^{~~\\circ}  $<br>So correct option is (4).',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(46053,'physics',42,323,592,'If ‘$p$’ region of a semiconductor is connected to negative and ‘$n$’ region to positive pole, it is said to be-','[{"text": "Directed biased", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Unbiased", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Forward biased", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Reverse biased", "isCorrect": true}]',3,NULL,NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(46035,'physics',42,323,591,'In a $p$-$n$ junction the depletion region is 400$nm$ wide and electric field of  $5 \\times {10^5}\\,V{m^{ - 1}}$ exists in it. The minimum energy of a conduction electron, which can diffuse from $n$-side to the $p$-side is','[{"text": "$4\\\\,eV$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$5\\\\,eV$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$0.4\\\\,eV$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$0.2\\\\,eV$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'$W = Vq = E(dq)$<br>$ = 5 \\times {10^5} \\times 400 \\times {10^{ - 9}} \\times 1.6 \\times {10^{ - 19}}J$<br>$ = \\frac{{5 \\times {{10}^5} \\times 400 \\times {{10}^{ - 9}} \\times 1.6 \\times {{10}^{ - 19}}J}}{{1.6 \\times {{10}^{ - 19}}}}eV$<br>$ = 2000 \\times {10^{ - 4}}eV = 0.2\\,eV$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(46036,'physics',41,406,809,'Neutron decay in free space is given as follows:<br>$_0{n^1}{ \\to _1}{P^1}{ + _{ - 1}}{e^0} + [\\,]$<br>Then the parenthesis represents a','[{"text": "Antineutrino", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Graviton", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Photon", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Neutrino", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'An electron is accompained by an antineutrino.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(46038,'physics',42,329,604,'A $PN$ junction diode cannot be used','[{"text": "As rectifier", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "For converting light energy to electric energy", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "For getting light radiation", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "For increasing the amplitude of an $ac$ signal", "isCorrect": true}]',3,NULL,NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(46039,'physics',40,240,479,'The ionisation potential of $H$-atom is $13.6\\,V$. When it is excited from ground state by monochromatic radiations of  $970.6$  $ \\mathop A^{~~\\circ}  $  the number of emission lines will be (according to Bohr\'s theory)','[{"text": "10", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "8", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "6", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "4", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'$\\frac{1}{\\lambda } = R\\left[ {\\frac{1}{{n_1^2}} - \\frac{1}{{n_2^2}}} \\right] \\Rightarrow {n_2} = 4$<br>$\\therefore$ Number of emission line<br>$N = \\frac{{n(n - 1)}}{2} = \\frac{{4 \\times 3}}{2} = 6$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(46040,'physics',41,406,809,'In the nuclear reaction $_{85}{X^{297}} \\to Y + 4\\alpha ,Y$ is','[{"text": "$_{77}{Y^{281}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$_{76}{Y^{287}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$_{77}{Y^{285}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$_{77}{Y^{289}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'${}_{85}{X^{297}}{ \\to _A}{Y^B} + {4_2}{\\alpha ^4}$<br>By equating the atomic and mass numbers<br>$85 = A + 8 \\Rightarrow A = 77$<br>$297 = B + 16 \\Rightarrow B = 281$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(46041,'physics',37,978,2282,'The graph given here represents an idealized sinusoidal current flow between a cloud at a height of 1 \\[km\\] and the earth, during a lightning discharge. Value of ${I_{0}}$ is 157 \\[kA\\] and ${T=0.2 {~ms}}$. Assume that discharge happens when the electric field in the air between the cloud and the earth becomes equal to the breakdown field of air i.e., ${E_{0}=3 \\times 10^{6} {~V} / {m}}$. Assume that the entire charge on the cloud is released during the lightning and estimate the capacitance of the cloud-Earth system (in \\[nF\\] ).<div class="matching-question"></div>','[{"text": "6.67", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "8.25", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "4.98", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "2.67", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Charge released $Q=\\int_{0}^{T} I_{0} \\sin \\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{T} t\\right) d t$<br>$=\\dfrac{2 I_{0} T}{\\pi}=\\dfrac{2 \\times 157 \\times 10^{3} \\times 0.2 \\times 10^{-3}}{3.14}=20 {C}$<br>Electric field at the time of discharge $E=3 \\times 10^{6} {~V} / {m}$<br>Potential difference between the cloud and the earth<br>$V=E d=3 \\times 10^{6} \\times 10^{3}=3 \\times 10^{9} \\text { volt }$<br>$\\therefore \\quad$ Capacitance <br>$C=\\dfrac{Q}{V}=\\dfrac{20}{3 \\times 10^{9}}=6.67 \\times 10^{-9} {~F}$',NULL,'Medium','Graph/Figure',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/37_46041_question_1.png'),
(46042,'physics',42,323,592,'The reverse saturation current in a $PN$ junction diode is due to only the','[{"text": "Minority carriers", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Majority carriers", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Acceptor ions", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Donor ions", "isCorrect": false}]',0,NULL,NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(46043,'physics',40,241,480,'The angular momentum of an electron in first orbit of $L{i^{ +  + }}$ ion is -','[{"text": "$\\\\frac{{3\\\\,h}}{{2\\\\pi }}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{{9\\\\,h}}{{2\\\\pi }}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{h}{{2\\\\pi }}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{h}{{6\\\\pi }}$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Angular momentum $(mvr)$<br>$ = n \\cdot \\frac{h}{{2\\pi }} = \\frac{h}{{2\\pi }}(n = 1)$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(46044,'physics',41,406,809,'Which of the following cannot be emitted by radioactive substances during their decay?','[{"text": "Protons", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\gamma $ rays", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Helium nuclei", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Electrons", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'During radioactive decay $\\alpha $ or $\\beta $ or $\\gamma $	particles are emitted. So protons are not emitted.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(46046,'physics',42,323,593,'The main cause of avalenche breakdown is','[{"text": "Collision by ionisation", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "High doping", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Recombination of electrons and holes", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Low doping", "isCorrect": false}]',0,NULL,NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(46047,'physics',41,406,806,'A count rate meter shows a count of 240 per minute from a given radioactive source. One hour later the meter shows a count rate of 30 per minute. the half-life of the source is','[{"text": "$80\\\\,\\\\min $", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$120\\\\,\\\\min $", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$20\\\\,\\\\min $", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$30\\\\,\\\\min $", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'$A = {A_0}{\\left( {\\frac{1}{2}} \\right)^n} \\Rightarrow 30 = 240{\\left( {\\frac{1}{2}} \\right)^n} \\Rightarrow {\\left( {\\frac{1}{2}} \\right)^3} = {\\left( {\\frac{1}{2}} \\right)^n}$<br>$ \\Rightarrow n = 3$<br>$\\therefore \\frac{t}{{{T_{1/2}}}} = 3 \\Rightarrow {T_{1/2}} = \\frac{t}{3} = \\frac{1}{3}hr = 20\\min .$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(46049,'physics',40,242,481,'The de Broglie wavelength of the electron in the first Bohr orbit of the hydrogen atom is','[{"text": "equal to the diameter of the first orbit", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "equal to the circumference of the first orbit", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "equal to half the circumference of the first orbit", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "independent of the size of the first orbit", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'$2\\pi {r_n} = n\\lambda $<br>For $n = 1$<br>$2\\pi {r_1} = \\lambda $<br>So correct option is (4).',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(46050,'physics',42,322,589,'The difference in the variation of resistance with temperature in a metal and a semiconductor arises essentially due to the difference in the :','[{"text": "Crystal structure", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Variation of the number of charge carriers with temperature", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Type of bonding", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Variation of scattering mechanism with temperature", "isCorrect": false}]',1,NULL,NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(46054,'physics',40,240,479,'If the electron in the hydrogen atom jumps from third orbit to second orbit, the wavelength of the emitted radiation in term of Rydberg constant is','[{"text": "$\\\\dfrac{6}{5 R}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{36}{5 R}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{64}{7 R}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "none of these", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'The wavelength of the emitted radiation for the electron transition from $n_{i}$ to $n_{f}$ $\\left(n_{f} < n_{i}\\right)$ is<br>$\\dfrac{1}{\\lambda}=R\\left[\\dfrac{1}{n_{f}^{2}}-\\dfrac{1}{n_{i}^{2}}\\right]$<br>Here, $n_{i}=3, n_{f}=2$<br>$\\therefore \\quad \\dfrac{1}{\\lambda}=R\\left[\\dfrac{1}{2^{2}}-\\dfrac{1}{3^{2}}\\right]$<br>$=R\\left[\\dfrac{1}{4}-\\dfrac{1}{9}\\right]=R\\left[\\dfrac{9-4}{36}\\right]=\\dfrac{5 R}{36}$<br>or $\\lambda=\\dfrac{36}{5 R}$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(46055,'physics',41,406,806,'When a radioactive substance is subjected to a vacuum, the rate of disintegration per second','[{"text": "increases considerably", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "is not affected", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "increases only if the products are gases", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "suffers a slight decrease", "isCorrect": false}]',1,NULL,NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(46057,'physics',42,323,591,'The thickness of depletion layer in a typical $p$-$n$ junction diode is in the order of','[{"text": "${10^{ - 4}}m$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${10^{ - 2}}m$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${10^{ - 6}}m$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${10^{ - 1}}m$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,NULL,NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(46058,'physics',40,239,478,'As the electron in Bohr\'s orbit of hydrogen atom passes from state $n=2$ to $n=1$, the kinetic energy $(K)$ and the potential energy $(U)$ changes as','[{"text": "$K$ four fold, $U$ also four fold", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$K$ two fold, $U$ also two fold", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$K$ four fold, $U$ two fold", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$K$ two fold, $U$ four fold", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'The kinetic energy of an electron in ${n^{th{\\rm{ }}}}$ orbit is<br>$K_{n}=\\dfrac{m e^{4}}{8 n^{2} \\varepsilon_{0}^{2} h^{2}}$ or $K_{n} \\propto \\dfrac{1}{n^{2}}$<br>and potential energy of the electron in ${n^{th{\\rm{ }}}}$ orbit is <br>$U_{n}=-\\dfrac{m e^{4}}{4 n^{2} \\varepsilon_{0}^{2} h^{2}}$ or $U_{n} \\propto \\dfrac{1}{n^{2}}$<br> $\\therefore \\quad$ When the electron passes from $n=2$ to$n=1$, then<br>$\\dfrac{K_{2}}{K_{1}}=\\dfrac{(1)^{2}}{(2)^{2}}=\\dfrac{1}{4}$ or $K_{1}=4 K_{2}$<br>and $\\dfrac{U_{2}}{U_{1}}=\\dfrac{(1)^{2}}{(2)^{2}}=\\dfrac{1}{4}$ or $U_{1}=4 U_{2}$<br>Thus, both $K$ and $U$ change as four fold.',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(46059,'physics',41,406,810,'A sample of radioactive material is used to provide desired doses of radiation for medical purposes. The total time for which the sample can be used will depend','[{"text": "Only on the number of times radiation is drawn  from it", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Only on the intensity of doses drawn from it", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "On both (1) and (2)", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Neither on (1) nor on (2)", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Radioactivity of a sample does not depend on how many times we use it. It is a continuous process and its rate does not depend on external factors.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(46060,'physics',37,973,2270,'In an ac circuit, the instantaneous current is zero, when the instantaneous voltage is maximum. In this case, the source may be connected to<br>I. pure inductor.<br>II. pure capacitor.<br>III. pure resistor.<br>IV. combination of an inductor and capacitor.<br>Choose the correct answer from the options given below:','[{"text": "I and II only", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "I, II and IV only", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "I, II and III only", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "II, III and IV only", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Instantaneous current is (minimum or) zero in case of pure inductor, pure capacitor or a combination of inductor and capacitor as there is phase difference of $90^{\\circ}$ or $\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}$ radian between the voltage $\\&$ the current in these combinations. Current is maximum in case of pure resistor. So, correct option is (2).',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2024,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(46061,'physics',42,323,592,'When $p$-$n$ junction is foward biased, the current across the junction is mainly due to','[{"text": "Diffusion of charges", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Drifting of charges", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both diffusion and drifting of charges", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Holes only", "isCorrect": false}]',0,NULL,NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(46062,'physics',41,403,801,'The graph of  ln  $(R/{R_0})$ versus ln $A$ ($R = $ radius) of nucleus ($A$ is atomic number).','[{"text": "Straight line", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Parabola", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Ellipse", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Circle", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'$R = {R_0}{A^{1/3}}$<br>$\\frac{R}{{{R_0}}} = {A^{1/3}} \\Rightarrow \\ln \\frac{R}{{{R_0}}} = \\frac{1}{3}\\ln A$<br>The graph between  $\\ln \\left( {\\frac{R}{{{R_0}}}} \\right)$ and  $\\ln A$ is a straight line.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(46063,'physics',37,973,2268,'Match the Column I and Column II<br> <br>Choose the correct answer from the options given below :<div class="matching-question"></div>','[{"text": "A – S,     B – P, \\tC – R, \\tD – Q", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "A – P,     B – S, \\tC – Q, \\tD – R", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "A – S,     B – P, \\tC – Q, \\tD – R", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "A – P,     B – S, \\tC – R, \\tD – Q", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'For A : Current leads by $\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}$;<br><img src=\'/img/data/physics/37_46063_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />  <br>For $B$ : Voltage leads by $\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}$;<br><img src=\'/img/data/physics/37_46063_explanation_2.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />  <br>For $C$ : In series $L C R$ circuit resonance, current and voltage are in same phase.<br><img src=\'/img/data/physics/37_46063_explanation_3.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />  <br>For $D:$ In $L C R$ series circuit, $\\theta$ is the angle between voltage and current.<br><img src=\'/img/data/physics/37_46063_explanation_4.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />',NULL,'Medium','Match the following',2024,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/37_46063_question_1.png'),
(46066,'physics',41,408,812,'The energy released by the fission of one uranium atom is $200\\,MeV$. The number of fission per second required to produce $3.2\\,mW$ of power is: (take, $1eV = 1.6 \\times {10^{ - 19}}J$)','[{"text": "${10^7}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${10^{10}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${10^6}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${10^8}$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'We have the energy released by fission of one uranium atom is $200\\,MeV$.<br>So,$E = 200 \\times {10^6} \\times 1.6 \\times {10^{ - 19}} = 3.2 \\times {10^{ - 11}}J$<br>The power required $ = 3.2 \\times {10^{ - 3}}W$<br>Thus, the number of fission required is equal to $\\frac{{3.2 \\times {{10}^{ - 3}}}}{{3.2 \\times {{10}^{ - 11}}}} = {10^8}$ fissions.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(46067,'physics',37,973,2268,'A series $L C R$ circuit is subjected to an ac signal of  $200\\,V,50\\,Hz.$ If the voltage across the inductor $(L = 10\\,mH)$ is  $31.4\\,V,$  then the current in this circuit is _____ .','[{"text": "$68\\\\,A$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$10\\\\,A$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$63\\\\,A$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$10\\\\,mA$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'<img src=\'/img/data/physics/37_46067_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />  <br>Given, $L = 10\\,mH,{V_L} = 31.4\\,V$  and  $f = 50\\,Hz$<br> Inductive reactance, $X_{L}=\\omega L=2 \\pi f L$<br>$ = 2\\pi  \\times 50 \\times 10 \\times {10^{ - 3}} = \\pi \\,\\Omega $<br>Voltage across the inductor, $V_{L}=i X_{L}$ where $i$ is current flowing in the circuit,<br>$\\therefore i=\\dfrac{V_{L}}{X_{L}}$<br>$\\Rightarrow i=\\dfrac{31.4}{\\pi}$<br>$ \\Rightarrow i = 10\\,A$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',2024,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(46068,'physics',42,323,592,'The diffusion current in a $p$-$n$ junction is greater than the drift current when the junction is','[{"text": "Forward biased", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Reverse biased", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Unbiased", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both forward and reverse biased", "isCorrect": false}]',0,NULL,NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(46070,'physics',41,406,809,'In the given nuclear reaction, how many $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ particles are emitted ${}_{92}{X^{235}}_{82}{Y^{207}}$?','[{"text": "$4\\\\alpha \\\\,{\\\\mathop{\\\\rm particles}\\\\nolimits}  \\\\,and\\\\,\\\\,3\\\\beta \\\\,{\\\\mathop{\\\\rm particles}\\\\nolimits} $", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$3\\\\alpha \\\\,{\\\\mathop{\\\\rm particles}\\\\nolimits}  \\\\,and\\\\,\\\\,2\\\\beta \\\\,{\\\\mathop{\\\\rm particles}\\\\nolimits} $", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$7\\\\alpha \\\\,{\\\\mathop{\\\\rm particles}\\\\nolimits}  \\\\,and\\\\,\\\\,4\\\\beta \\\\,{\\\\mathop{\\\\rm particles}\\\\nolimits} $", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$6\\\\alpha \\\\,{\\\\mathop{\\\\rm particles}\\\\nolimits}  \\\\,and\\\\,\\\\,4\\\\beta \\\\,{\\\\mathop{\\\\rm particles}\\\\nolimits} $", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'${n_\\alpha } = \\frac{{A - A\'}}{4} = \\frac{{235 - 207}}{4} = 7$<br>${n_\\beta } = (2{n_\\alpha } - Z + Z\') = (2 \\times 7 - 92 + 82) = 4$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(46071,'physics',37,975,2276,'Read the Statement   A and Statement   B carefully to mark the correct options given below.<div class="matching-question"><div class="column-left">In an $LCR$ series circuit, current is maximum at resonance .</div><div class="column-right">Current in a purely resistive circuit can never be less than that in a series $LCR$ circuit when connected to same voltage source.</div></div>','[{"text": "Statement A is correct but Statement B is   incorrect", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Statement A is incorrect but Statement B is  correct", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Statements are true", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Statements are incorrect", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'In a series $L C R$ circuit, current is maximum at resonance.<br>At resonance, $X_{L}=X_{C}$,<br>$\\therefore$ Impedance,<br>$Z=\\sqrt{R^{2}+\\left(X_{L}-X_{C}\\right)^{2}}=\\sqrt{R^{2}}$<br>or $Z=R$<br>$\\Rightarrow Z$ is minimum at resonance<br>$\\therefore \\quad I_{m}=\\dfrac{V_{m}}{Z}=\\dfrac{V_{m}}{R}$<br>In a purely resistive circuit, $I=\\dfrac{V}{R}$ and in a series LCR circuit, $I^{\\prime}=\\dfrac{V}{Z}$<br>$\\Rightarrow I>I^{\\prime}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ type-2',2024,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(46072,'physics',42,323,592,'In forward bias the depletion layer behaves like','[{"text": "An insulator", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "A conductor", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "A semiconductor", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Capacitor", "isCorrect": false}]',1,NULL,NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(46073,'physics',40,239,478,'The electron of hydrogen atom is considered to be revolving round a proton in circular orbit of radius $\\hbar^{2} / m e^{2}$ with velocity $e^{2} / \\hbar$, where $\\hbar=h / 2 \\pi$. The current $i$ is','[{"text": "$\\\\dfrac{4 \\\\pi^{2} m e^{5}}{h^{2}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{4 \\\\pi^{2} m e^{5}}{h^{3}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{4 \\\\pi^{2} m^{2} e^{2}}{h^{3}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{4 \\\\pi^{2} m^{2} e^{5}}{h^{3}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Current, $i=\\dfrac{Q}{t}=\\dfrac{e}{T}=\\dfrac{e}{2 \\pi r / v}$<br>$\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,$$=\\dfrac{e v}{2 \\pi r}$$\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,(1)$<br>Here, $r=\\dfrac{\\hbar^{2}}{m e^{2}}, v=\\dfrac{e^{2}}{\\hbar}, \\hbar=\\dfrac{h}{2 \\pi}$<br>Eqn. (1) becomes,<br>$i = \\frac{{e \\times \\frac{{{e^2}}}{\\hbar }}}{{2\\pi \\frac{{{\\hbar ^2}}}{{m{e^2}}}}} = \\frac{{m{e^5}}}{{2\\pi {\\hbar ^3}}} = \\frac{{4{\\pi ^2}m{e^5}}}{{{h^3}}}$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(46075,'physics',37,975,2276,'A $LCR$ circuit is at resonance for a capacitor $C$, inductance $L$ and resistance $R$. Now the value of resistance is halved keeping all other parameters same. The current amplitude at resonance will be now','[{"text": "double", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "halved", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "zero", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "same", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'The impedance of $LCR$ circuit is given by, $Z=\\sqrt{R^{2}+\\left(X_{L}-X_{C}\\right)^{2}}$<br>At resonance, $X_{L}=X_{C}$<br>$Z=R$<br>$\\therefore Z_{1}=R_{1}$ and $Z_{2}=R_{2}$<br><img src=\'/img/data/physics/37_46075_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />  <br>$\\Rightarrow I_{1}=\\dfrac{V}{Z_{1}}=\\dfrac{V}{R} ; \\quad I_{2}=\\dfrac{V}{Z_{2}}=\\dfrac{2 V}{R}$<br>$\\therefore I_{2}=2 I_{1}$<br>Current amplitude will be double',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2024,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(46076,'physics',42,323,592,'$p$-$n$ junction in reverse bias behaves like','[{"text": "An inductor", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "A condenser", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Amplifier", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "An off switch", "isCorrect": true}]',3,NULL,NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(46078,'physics',37,973,2265,'A coil of negligible resistance is connected in series with $90\\,\\Omega $ resistor across $120\\,V,60\\,Hz$ supply. A voltmeter reads $36\\,V$ across resistance. Inductance of the coil is','[{"text": "$0.286\\\\,H$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2.86\\\\,H$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$0.76\\\\,H$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$0.91\\\\,H$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'<img src=\'/img/data/physics/37_46078_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />  <br>$\\left( {{V_{rms}}} \\right) = {i_{rms}} \\times R$<br>$\\Rightarrow i_{r m s}=\\dfrac{36}{90} A \\quad\\left(\\therefore V_{r m s}=36 V\\right.$ (given)<br>Now,  $Z = \\frac{{{V_{rms{\\text{ }}}}}}{{{i_{rms{\\text{ }}}}}} = \\frac{{120}}{{36}} \\times 90 = 300\\,\\Omega $<br>$Z=\\sqrt{R^{2}+X_{L}^{2}}$<br>$300=\\sqrt{(90)^{2}+X_{L}^{2}}$<br>As $X_{L}=\\omega L \\Rightarrow X_{L}=286.181 \\Omega$<br>$\\therefore \\omega L=286.181$<br>$2 \\pi \\times 60 \\times L=286.181$<br>$L = 0.759H \\approx 0.76H$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2024,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(46080,'physics',42,323,592,'On increasing reverse voltage in a $p$-$n$ junction diode the value of reverse current will','[{"text": "Gradually increase", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "First remain constant and then suddenly increase", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Remain constant", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Gradually decrease", "isCorrect": false}]',1,NULL,NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(46082,'physics',37,973,2266,'A bulb and a capacitor are connected in series across an ac supply. A dielectric is then placed between the plates of the capacitor. The glow of the bulb','[{"text": "increases", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "becomes zero", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "decreases", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "remains same", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'When dielectric is inserted, $X_{c}=\\dfrac{1}{\\omega C}$ decreases, as capacitance increases due to insertion of dielectric and then $Z=\\sqrt{X_{c}^{2}+R^{2}}$ decreases, so current $\\left(I=\\dfrac{V}{Z}\\right)$ increases and brightness of bulb increases. So, correct option is (1).',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2024,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(46083,'physics',42,321,588,'In semiconductors the forbidden energy gap between Valence band and Conduction band is of the order of','[{"text": "$1\\\\,eV$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$5\\\\,eV$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1\\\\,keV$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1\\\\,MeV$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,NULL,NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(46086,'physics',37,975,2275,'In series $LCR$ circuit, the capacitance is changed from $C$ to $4\\,C.$ To keep the resonance frequency unchanged, the new inductance should be','[{"text": "reduced by $\\\\dfrac{1}{4} L$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "reduced by $\\\\dfrac{3}{4} L$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "increased by $4 L$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "increased by $2 L$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'${C_1} = C,{C_2} = 4\\,C$<br>Let $L_{1}=L, L_{2}=$ ?<br>Resonant frequency is given by<br>$\\omega=\\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{L C}}$<br>As per question<br>$\\omega_{1}=\\omega_{2}$<br>$\\Rightarrow \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{L_{1} C_{1}}}=\\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{L_{2} C_{2}}}$<br>$L \\times C=L_{2} \\times 4 C$<br>$\\Rightarrow L_{2}=\\dfrac{L}{4}$<br>So, inductance is reduced by, $L-\\dfrac{L}{4}=\\dfrac{3}{4} L$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2024,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(46087,'physics',42,322,590,'If  ${n_e}$ and ${n_h}$ are the number of electrons and holes in a semiconductor heavily doped with phosphorus, then','[{"text": "${n_e} <  < {n_h}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${n_e} >  > {n_h}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${n_e} = {n_h}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${n_e} \\\\le {n_h}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Phosphorus is pentavalent. After doping no of electrons will be more than no of holes.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(46088,'physics',40,239,478,'The ionization potential of a hydrogen atom is 13.6 $eV$ . What will be the energy of electron in the second orbit?','[{"text": "10.02 $eV$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$ - 3.40\\\\,eV$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "1.51 $eV$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$ - 0.85\\\\,eV$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Energy of the electron in the ${n^{\\text {th }}}$ orbit is ${E_{n}=\\dfrac{E_{1}}{n^{2}}}$. For second orbit, ${n=2}$<br>${\\Rightarrow \\quad E_{2}=\\dfrac{-13.6}{4}=-3.4 {eV}\\left(E_{1}=-13.6   {eV}\\right)}$.<br>So correct option is (2)',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(46090,'physics',37,976,2280,'A capacitor of capacitance $100\\,\\mu F$ is charged to a potential of $12\\,V$ and connected to a $6.4\\,mH$ inductor to produce oscillations. The maximum current in the circuit would be','[{"text": "$3.2\\\\,A$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1.5\\\\,A$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$1.2\\\\,A$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2.0\\\\,A$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'$C = 100\\,\\mu F = 100 \\times {10^{ - 6}}\\,F = {10^{ - 4}}\\,F$<br>$V = 12\\,V$<br>$L = 6.4\\,mH = 6.4 \\times {10^{ - 3}}H$<br><img src=\'/img/data/physics/37_46090_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />  <br>Total energy is conserved.<br>max energy in $C=$ max energy in $L$<br>$\\Rightarrow \\dfrac{1}{2} C V^{2}=\\dfrac{1}{2} L i^{2}$<br>$\\Rightarrow i=\\sqrt{\\dfrac{C}{L}} V$<br>$ \\Rightarrow i = \\sqrt {\\frac{{{{10}^{ - 4}}}}{{6.4 \\times {{10}^{ - 3}}}}}  \\times 12 = \\frac{{12}}{8} = 1.5\\,A$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2024,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(46091,'physics',42,321,588,'The forbidden energy band gap in conductors, semiconductors and insulators are  $E{G_1},E{G_2},$ and  $E{G_3}$ respectively. The relation among them is','[{"text": "$E{G_1} < E{G_2} < E{G_3}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$E{G_1} = E{G_2} = E{G_3}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$E{G_1} < E{G_2} > E{G_3}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$E{G_1} > E{G_2} > E{G_3}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'In insulators, the forbidden energy gap is very large, in case of semiconductor it is moderate and in conductors the energy gap is zero.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(46093,'physics',37,974,2272,'In an a.c. circuit, voltage and current are given by $V=100 \\sin (100 t) V$ and  $I=100 \\sin \\left(100 t+\\dfrac{\\pi}{3}\\right) m A$ respectively.  The average power dissipated in one cycle is','[{"text": "$10\\\\,W$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$5\\\\,W$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$25\\\\,W$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2.5\\\\,W$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Given, $V=100 \\sin (100 t) V$<br>$I=100 \\sin (100 t+\\pi / 3) m A$<br>Phase difference between $V$ and $I, \\phi=\\dfrac{\\pi}{3}$<br>${V_0} = 100\\,V,{I_0} = 100\\,mA,$<br>Average power dissipated in one cycle is<br>$P_{a v}=I_{r m s} \\times V_{r m s} \\cos \\phi$<br>$P_{a v}=\\dfrac{100}{\\sqrt{2}} \\times \\dfrac{100}{\\sqrt{2}}\\left(\\cos \\dfrac{\\pi}{3}\\right) \\times 10^{-3}$<br>$ = \\frac{{10}}{2} \\times \\frac{1}{2} = 2.5\\,W$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',2024,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(46094,'physics',42,322,589,'If an intrinsic semiconductor is heated, the ratio of free electrons to holes is','[{"text": "Greater than one", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Less than one", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Equal to one", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Dercease and becomes zero", "isCorrect": false}]',2,NULL,NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(46095,'physics',40,240,479,'The first line of Balmer series has wavelength 6563 $ \\mathop A^{~~\\circ}  $. What will be the wavelength of the first member of Lyman series?','[{"text": "1215.4 $ \\\\mathop A^{~~\\\\circ}  $", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "2500 $ \\\\mathop A^{~~\\\\circ}  $", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "7500 $ \\\\mathop A^{~~\\\\circ}  $", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "600 $ \\\\mathop A^{~~\\\\circ}  $", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'When an electron makes a transition from ${m^{\\text {th }}}$ orbit to ${n^{\\text {th }}}$ orbit, wavelength of the emitted light is given by<br>${\\dfrac{1}{\\lambda}=R\\left(\\dfrac{1}{n^{2}}-\\dfrac{1}{m^{2}}\\right)}$<br>Here, ${R=}$ Rydberg constant<br>For first line of Balmer series of hydrogen atom ${[n=2}$ and ${m=3]}$<br>$\\frac{1}{\\lambda } = R\\left( {\\frac{1}{4} - \\frac{1}{9}} \\right) = R\\left( {\\frac{5}{{36}}} \\right){\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} (1)$<br> For first line of Lyman series, ${n=1}$ and ${m=2}$<br>$\\frac{1}{{{\\lambda ^\\prime }}} = R\\left( {1 - \\frac{1}{4}} \\right) = R\\left( {\\frac{3}{4}} \\right){\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} (2)$<br>Dividing eq (1) by (2), we get ${\\dfrac{\\lambda^{\\prime}}{\\lambda}=\\dfrac{5}{27}}$<br>$ \\Rightarrow {\\lambda ^\\prime } = \\frac{5}{{27}} \\times 6563$$ \\mathop A^{~~\\circ}  $ $ = 1215.4$$ \\mathop A^{~~\\circ}  $ . <br>So correct option is (1)',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(46096,'physics',37,974,2274,'A series $LR$ circuit connected with an $ac$ source $E=(25 \\sin 1000 t) V$ has a power factor of $\\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}$. If the source of emf is changed to $E=(20 \\sin 2000 t) V$, the new power factor of the circuit will be','[{"text": "$\\\\dfrac{1}{\\\\sqrt{7}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{1}{\\\\sqrt{5}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{1}{\\\\sqrt{2}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{1}{\\\\sqrt{3}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Power factor of a series $L R$ circuit is<br>$\\cos \\phi=\\dfrac{R}{Z}=\\dfrac{R}{\\sqrt{R^{2}+\\omega^{2} L^{2}}}$<br>$E_{1}=25 \\sin (1000 t) V$<br>Given: $\\cos \\phi_{1}=\\dfrac{R}{\\sqrt{R^{2}+(1000)^{2} L^{2}}}=\\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}$<br>$\\left( {\\because {\\omega _1} = 1000\\,rad/s} \\right)$<br>$\\Rightarrow \\dfrac{R^{2}}{R^{2}+(1000)^{2} L^{2}}=\\dfrac{1}{2}$<br>$\\Rightarrow R^{2}=(1000)^{2} L^{2} \\Rightarrow R=1000 L$<br>${E_2} = 20\\sin \\,(2000\\,t)V\\left( {\\because {\\omega _2} = 2000\\,rad/s} \\right)$<br>Now, $\\cos \\phi_{2}=\\dfrac{R}{\\sqrt{R^{2}+(2000)^{2} L^{2}}}$<br>Substituting the value of $R$, we have<br>$\\cos \\phi_{2}=\\dfrac{1000 L}{\\sqrt{(1000)^{2} L^{2}+(2000)^{2} L^{2}}}=\\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2024,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(46098,'physics',42,322,589,'An intrinsic semiconductor at absolute zero  temperature behaves as','[{"text": "A metallic conductor", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "A semiconductor", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "An insulator", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "A superconductor", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'At absolute zero a intrinsic semiconductor behaves like an insulator',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(46099,'physics',40,240,479,'The ratio of the long wavelength limits of the Lyman and Balmer series of hydrogen is','[{"text": "${27: 5}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${5: 27}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${4: 1}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${1: 4}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'When an electron makes a transition from ${m^{\\text {th }}}$ orbit to ${n^{\\text {th }}}$ orbit, wavelength of the emitted light is given by<br>${\\dfrac{1}{\\lambda}=R\\left(\\dfrac{1}{n^{2}}-\\dfrac{1}{m^{2}}\\right)}$<br>Here, ${R=}$ Rydberg constant<br>For long wavelength limits, electron must make a transition from the nearest possible state i.e., ${n=2}$ and ${m=3}$<br>For first line of Balmer series of hydrogen atom ${[n=2}$ and ${m=3]}$<br>$\\frac{1}{\\lambda } = R\\left( {\\frac{1}{4} - \\frac{1}{9}} \\right) = R\\left( {\\frac{5}{{36}}} \\right)\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,(1)$<br>For first line of Lyman series, ${n=1}$ and ${m=2}$<br> $\\frac{1}{{{\\lambda ^\\prime }}} = R\\left( {1 - \\frac{1}{4}} \\right) = R\\left( {\\frac{3}{4}} \\right)\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,(2)$<br>Dividing eq (1) by (2), we get ${\\dfrac{\\lambda^{\\prime}}{\\lambda}=\\dfrac{5}{27}}$.<br>So correct option is (2)',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(46100,'physics',41,406,806,'The half life of a radioactive substance is 20 minutes. In how much time, the activity of substance drops to $\\left(\\dfrac{1}{16}\\right)^{\\text {th }}$ of its initial value?','[{"text": "40 minutes", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "60 minutes", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "80 minutes", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "20 minutes", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Half life $T=20 \\mathrm{~min}$.<br>Denote $A=$ Activity<br>Left fraction $=\\dfrac{1}{16}$<br>$\\Rightarrow \\dfrac{A_{2}}{A_{1}}=\\dfrac{1}{2^{n}}$<br>$\\Rightarrow \\dfrac{1}{16}=\\dfrac{1}{2^{n}} \\Rightarrow n=4$<br>$t=n T \\quad$ where $T=T_{1 / 2}=$ Half-life<br>$\\Rightarrow t=4 \\times 20=80 \\mathrm{~min}$.<br>Correct option is (3).',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2023,'NEET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(46101,'physics',37,973,2262,'An alternating voltage $V(t) = 220\\sin 100\\,\\pi t$ volt is applied to a purely resistive load of  $50\\,\\Omega \\,.$  The time taken for the current to rise from half of the peak value to the peak value is','[{"text": "$5\\\\,ms$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$7.2\\\\,ms$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$3.3\\\\,ms$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$2.2\\\\,ms$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Given,  $V(t) = 220\\,\\,\\sin \\,(100\\,\\pi t)$<br>As $V$ and $I$ have no phase difference<br>$\\therefore I(t)=\\dfrac{V(t)}{R}=\\dfrac{220}{50} \\sin (100 \\pi t)$<br>$I(t)=I_{m} \\sin (\\omega t)$<br>for $\\omega=100 \\pi$<br>$I_{m}=I_{m} \\sin \\left(\\omega t_{1}\\right)$<br>For peak value,  ${t_1} = \\frac{\\pi }{2} \\times \\frac{1}{{100\\,\\pi }}$<br>$\\left[\\sin \\theta\\right.$ is $\\max$ at $\\left.\\theta=\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\right]$<br>$t_{1}=\\dfrac{1}{200} s$<br>Now, for half of the peak value,<br>$I = \\frac{{{I_m}}}{2} \\Rightarrow \\frac{{{I_m}}}{2} = {I_m}\\,\\,\\sin \\,(100\\,\\pi t)$<br>$ \\Rightarrow \\frac{1}{2} = \\sin \\left( {100\\,\\pi {t_2}} \\right)$<br>For half of the peak value,<br>${t_2} = \\frac{\\pi }{6} \\times \\frac{1}{{100\\,\\pi }} = \\frac{1}{{600}}\\,s$<br>Time taken for current to rise from half of the peak value to the peak value is, $t_{\\text {req }}=t_{1}-t_{2}$<br>$=\\dfrac{1}{200}-\\dfrac{1}{600}=\\dfrac{3-1}{600}=\\dfrac{2}{600} s=\\dfrac{1}{300} s$<br>${t_{req}} = 3.3\\,ms$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2024,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(46102,'physics',42,322,589,'The relation between number of free electrons ($n$) in a semiconductor and temperature ($T$) is given by','[{"text": "$n \\\\propto T$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$n \\\\propto {T^2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$n \\\\propto \\\\sqrt T $", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$n \\\\propto {T^{3/2}}$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Number of electrons reaching from valence band to conduction band $n = A{T^{3/2}}{e^{\\frac{{ - \\Delta {E_g}}}{{2kT}}}}$<br>$k - $ Boltzman constant<br>$A - $ constant<br>$T - $ Absolute temperature<br>$\\Delta {E_g} - $ Energy gap between $CB$ & $VB$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(46103,'physics',40,245,487,'In Millikan\'s oil drop experiment, the charge on oil drop is calculated to ${6.35 \\times 10^{-19} {C}}$. The number of excess electrons on the drop is','[{"text": "3.9", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "4.2", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "4", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "6", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Total charge, ${Q=6.35 \\times 10^{-19} {C}}$<br>${\\because \\quad Q=n e \\quad(n=}$ number of electrons)<br>$\\therefore n = \\frac{Q}{e} = \\frac{{6.35 \\times {{10}^{ - 19}}}}{{1.6 \\times {{10}^{ - 19}}}} \\simeq 4$<br>( ${\\because}$ we can not take number of electrons in decimal.)<br>So correct option is (3).',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(46104,'physics',37,974,2766,'An $AC$  voltage $V=20 \\sin 200 \\pi t$ is applied to a series $LCR$ circuit which drives a current $I=10 \\sin \\left(200 \\pi t+\\dfrac{\\pi}{3}\\right)$. The average power dissipated is','[{"text": "$50\\\\,W$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$200\\\\,W$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$173.2\\\\,W$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$21.6\\\\,{W^ - }$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'$V = 20\\,\\,\\sin 200\\,\\pi t,I = 10\\sin \\left( {200\\,\\pi t + \\frac{\\pi }{3}} \\right)$<br>Average power dissipated in a cycle is given by<br>$P=V_{r m s} I_{r m s} \\cos \\phi$<br>Where $\\cos \\phi=$ Power factor<br>$P=\\dfrac{V_{0}}{\\sqrt{2}} \\dfrac{I_{0}}{\\sqrt{2}} \\cos \\phi$<br>Here, $V_{0}=20, I_{0}=10 ; \\phi=\\dfrac{\\pi}{3}$<br>$P = \\frac{{20}}{{\\sqrt 2 }} \\times \\frac{{10}}{{\\sqrt 2 }}\\cos \\frac{\\pi }{3} = 50\\,W$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2024,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(46105,'physics',41,406,809,'In the following equation representing $\\beta^{-}$decay, the number of neutrons in the nucleus $X$ is<br>${ }_{83}^{210} B i \\rightarrow X+e^{-1}+\\bar{v}$.','[{"text": "125", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "84", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "126", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "127", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Here neutron is converted to proton. So, $Z$ increases by 1.<br>$\\Rightarrow Z$ of $X=83+1=84$.<br>$N=A-Z=210-84=126$.<br>Correct option is (3).',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2023,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(46106,'physics',42,321,588,'Band gap in insulator is of the order','[{"text": "$6\\\\,eV$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$0.60\\\\,eV$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$ - 6\\\\,eV$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$0\\\\,eV$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,NULL,NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(46108,'physics',41,404,802,'A nucleus with mass number 220 initially at rest emits an alpha particle. If the $Q$ value of reaction is $5.5\\,MeV$, calculate the value of kinetic energy of alpha particle.','[{"text": "$7.4\\\\,MeV$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$4.5\\\\,MeV$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$6.5\\\\,MeV$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$5.4\\\\,MeV$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Required $\\left(E_{K}\\right)_{\\alpha}$ is<br>$\\Rightarrow\\left(E_{K}\\right)_{\\alpha}=Q\\left(\\dfrac{A-4}{A}\\right)$<br>$\\Rightarrow\\left(E_{K}\\right)_{\\alpha}=5.5 \\times \\dfrac{216}{220}$<br>$ = 5.4\\,MeV$.<br>Correct option is (4).',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2023,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(46109,'physics',42,322,589,'At zero Kelvin a piece of germanium','[{"text": "Becomes good conductor", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Becomes semiconductor", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Has maximum conductivity", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Becomes bad conductor", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'At zero Kelvin, there is no thermal agitation and therefore no electrons from valence band are able to shift to conduction band.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(46110,'physics',40,241,480,'The ratio of ionization energy of Bohr\'s hydrogen atom and Bohr\'s hydrogen like Lithium atom is','[{"text": "${1: 1}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${1: 3}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${1: 9}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${1: 27}$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Ionization energy is the energy required to ionize an atom, is proportional to atomic number and number of orbit, i.e<br>$E \\propto \\frac{{{Z^2}}}{{{n^2}}} \\Rightarrow E = \\frac{{k{Z^2}}}{{{n^2}}}$<br>For hydrogen atom ${Z=1}$ and lithium ${Z=3}$<br>$\\frac{{{E_H}}}{{{E_{Li}}}} = \\frac{k}{{k{{(3)}^2}}}({\\text{say}}  n = 1)$<br>$\\frac{{{E_H}}}{{{E_{Li}}}} = \\frac{1}{9}$<br>So correct option is (3)',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(46112,'physics',41,406,806,'A radioactive sample has half-life of 3 years. The time required for the activity of the sample to reduce to $\\dfrac{1}{5}^{\\text {th }}$ of its initial value is about','[{"text": "15 years", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "5 years", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "10 years", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "7 years", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'$\\dfrac{N}{N_{0}}=e^{-\\lambda t} \\Rightarrow \\dfrac{1}{5}=e^{-\\lambda t}$<br>$\\Rightarrow \\lambda t=\\log e^{5}$<br>$\\Rightarrow t=\\dfrac{3}{0.693} \\log e^{5}$<br>$=6.96 \\approx 7 \\text { years }$<br>Correct option is (4).',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2023,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(46113,'physics',42,322,589,'In intrinsic semiconductor conductivity is','[{"text": "low", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "average", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "high", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "very low", "isCorrect": false}]',0,NULL,NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(46114,'physics',40,239,478,'An electron in Bohr\'s hydrogen atom has an energy of $ - 3.4$ $eV$ . The angular momentum of the electron is','[{"text": "${\\\\dfrac{h}{\\\\pi}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${\\\\dfrac{h}{2 \\\\pi}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\dfrac{n h}{2 \\\\pi}}$ ( ${n}$ is an integer)", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\dfrac{2 h}{\\\\pi}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Energy of the electron in the ${n^{\\text {th }}}$ orbit is ${E_{n}=\\dfrac{E_{1}}{n^{2}}=\\dfrac{-13.6}{n^{2}} {eV}}$<br>For ${n=2, E_{2}=\\dfrac{-13.6}{4} {eV}=-3.4 {eV}}$<br>Angular momentum of the electron in the ${n^{\\text {th }}}$ orbit is<br>$L = mvr = \\frac{{nh}}{{2\\pi }} \\Rightarrow L = \\frac{{2h}}{{2\\pi }} = \\frac{h}{\\pi }\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,(n = 2)$.<br>So correct option is (1)',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(46132,'physics',41,406,809,'The mother and daughter elements, with emission of $\\gamma$ -rays are','[{"text": "Isotopes", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Isobars", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Isomers", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Isodiapheres", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'The mother and daughter elements after the emission of $\\gamma$-rays are called isomers having same atomic number and mass number. Isotopes have same atomic number but different atomic mass and isobars have same mass number but different atomic number. Isodiaphers have same difference in proton and neutron numbers.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2013,'AIIMS','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(46116,'physics',41,406,808,'The fossil has a ${ }^{14} C:{ }^{12} C$ ratio, which is $\\left[\\dfrac{1}{16}\\right]$ of that in a living animal bone. If the half-life of $^{14}C$ is $5730\\,yr$, then the age of the fossil bone is','[{"text": "$11460\\\\,yr$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$17190\\\\,yr$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$22920\\\\,yr$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$45840\\\\,yr$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'After $n$ half -lives (i.e. at $t=n T_{\\frac{1}{2}}$), the number of nuclides left undecayed,<br>$N=N_{0}(1 / 2)^{n}$<br>Given, $\\dfrac{N}{N_{0}}=\\dfrac{1}{16}$<br>$\\Rightarrow \\dfrac{1}{16}=\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right)^{n}$<br>$\\Rightarrow\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right)^{4}=\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right)^{n}$<br>Equating the powers, we obtain, $n=4$<br>i.e. $\\dfrac{t}{T_{\\frac{1}{2}}}=4$<br>$\\Rightarrow t=4 T_{\\frac{1}{2}}$<br>$\\therefore t = 4 \\times 5730 = 22920\\,yr$<br>$\\left( {\\because {T_{\\frac{1}{2}}} = 5730\\,yr} \\right)$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2006,'AIIMS','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(46117,'physics',42,322,590,'The hole in a $p$-type semiconductor is','[{"text": "An electron deficiency", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "An electron excess", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "An atom deficiency", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Positive ion", "isCorrect": false}]',0,NULL,NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(46119,'physics',41,406,809,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Cobalt-60 is useful in cancer therapy.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Cobalt-60 is source of $\\gamma$-radiations capable of killing cancerous cell.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Cobalt-60 is indeed used in radiotherapy for cancer treatment. Cobalt-60 is a radioactive isotope that emits $\\gamma$-radiations, which are used to target and kill cancer cells during radiotherapy.<br>Both Assertion and Reason are correct, and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion. So option (1) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',2006,'AIIMS','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
