(46123,'physics',41,406,809,'The energy specturm of $\\beta$-particles [number $N(E)$ as a function of $\\beta$ - energy $E$] emitted from a radioactive source is<div class="matching-question"></div>','[{"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Energy specturm of emitted $\\beta$-particles from a radioactive source is drawn as  <img src=\'/img/data/physics/41_46123_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />',NULL,'Medium','Graph/Figure',2007,'AIIMS','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(46125,'physics',42,322,590,'The mobility of free electrons is greater than that of free holes because','[{"text": "They carry negative charge", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "They are light", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Their mutal collisions are less", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "They require low energy to continue their motion", "isCorrect": true}]',3,NULL,NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(46127,'physics',41,406,809,'We have seen that a gamma ray dose of $3\\,Gy$ is lethal to half the people exposed to it. If the equivalent energy were absorbed as heat, what rise in body temperature would result?','[{"text": "$300\\\\,\\\\mu K$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$700\\\\,\\\\mu K$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$455\\\\,\\\\mu K$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$390\\\\,\\\\mu K$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'We can relate an absorbed energy $Q$ and the resulting temperature increase $\\Delta T$ with relation $Q=m r(\\Delta T)$. In this equation, $m$ is the mass of the material absorbing the energy and $r$ is the specific heat of that material. An absorbed dose of $3\\,Gy$ corresponds to an absorbed energy per unit mass of $3\\,J\\,k{g^{ - 1}}$. Let us assume that $r$ is the specific heat of human body, is the same as that of water,<br>$4180\\,J\\,k{g^{ - 1}}\\,{K^{ - 1}}$.<br>Then, we find that<br>$\\Delta T = \\frac{{Q{\\rm{/}}m}}{r} = \\frac{3}{{4180}}$<br>$ = 7.2 \\times {10^{ - 4}}\\,K \\simeq 700\\,\\mu K$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',2007,'AIIMS','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(46128,'physics',42,322,589,'An electric field is applied across a semiconductor. Let $n$ be the number of charge carriers. As temperature increases, $n$ will','[{"text": "Increase", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Decrease", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Does not change", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "May increase or decrease", "isCorrect": false}]',0,NULL,NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(46129,'physics',40,240,479,'If a hydrogen atom initially at the ground state absorbs a photon and jumps to $4^{\\text {th }}$ excited state then the wavelength of photon is $9.48 \\times 10^{{N}}$.  Find  the value of \' $N$ \' is<br>(Take Planck\'s constant $=6.6 \\times 10^{-34} {Js}$ )','[{"text": "$ - 4$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$ - 8$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$ - 12$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$ - 10$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Here,${n_1} = 1,$ and $n_{2}=5$<br>Energy of absorbed photon, ${E}={E}_{2}-{E}_{1}$<br>Since, $E_{n}=-\\dfrac{13.6}{n^{2}} e V$<br>Then, ${E}_{2}-{E}_{1}=-\\dfrac{13.6}{(5)^{2}}-\\left[-\\dfrac{13.6}{(1)^{2}}\\right]$<br>$=-\\dfrac{13.6}{25}+13.6$<br>$=-0.544+13.6$<br>$=13.056 \\,\\,{eV}$<br>$=13.056 \\times 1.6 \\times 10^{-19} {~J}$<br>But $E_{2}-E_{1}=\\dfrac{h c}{\\lambda}$<br>$\\therefore \\lambda=\\dfrac{h c}{{E}_{2}-{E}_{1}}=\\dfrac{6.6 \\times 10^{-34} \\times 3 \\times 10^{8}}{13.056 \\times 1.6 \\times 10^{-19}}$<br>$=9.48 \\times 10^{-8} {~m}$<br>$\\therefore \\quad {N}=-8$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(46130,'physics',41,407,811,'A nucleus at rest breaks into two nuclear parts which have their velocities ratio equal to $2: 1$. What will be the ratio of their radii of the nuclei?','[{"text": "$2^{1 / 3}: 1$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1: 2^{1 / 3}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$2^{3 / 2}: 1$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1: 2^{3 / 2}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'According to the law of conservation linear momentum,<br>$\\begin{aligned}& m_{1} v_{1}=m_{2} v_{2} \\\\& \\therefore \\dfrac{m_{1}}{m_{2}}=\\dfrac{v_{2}}{v_{1}}=\\dfrac{1}{2} \\quad\\left(\\text { given }, \\dfrac{v_{1}}{v_{2}}=2\\right)\\end{aligned}$<br>Since, we know<br>$\\begin{aligned}& R=R \\cdot(A)^{1 / 3} \\\\& A \\approx m\\end{aligned}$<br>Thus, $\\dfrac{R_{1}}{R_{2}}=\\left(\\dfrac{m_{1}}{m_{2}}\\right)^{1 / 3}=\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right)^{1 / 3}$<br>$\\therefore R_{1}: R_{2}=1: 2^{1 / 3}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2009,'AIIMS','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(46131,'physics',42,322,590,'In $n$-type semiconductors the electron concentration is equal to','[{"text": "Density of donor atoms", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Density of acceptor atoms", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Density of both type of atoms", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Neither density of acceptor atoms nor density of donor atoms", "isCorrect": false}]',0,NULL,NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(46135,'physics',41,406,809,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> In $\\alpha$ -decay, atomic number of daughter nucleus reduces by a unit from the parents nucleus.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> An $\\alpha$ -particle carries four units of mass.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but reason is correct.", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'In $\\alpha$-decay, the atomic number (the number of protons) of the daughter nucleus decreases by 2 units, not by 1 unit. So, the assertion is not correct.<br>An $\\alpha$-particle consists of two protons and two neutrons, which gives it a mass of four atomic mass units (amu). This is a valid and accurate statement. So option (4) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',2017,'AIIMS','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(46138,'physics',41,406,809,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Radioactive nuclei emits $\\beta$ particles.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Electron exist inside the nucleus.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Radioactive nuclei can emit $\\beta$-particles, which are electrons $\\left(\\beta^{-}\\right)$or positrons $\\left(\\beta^{+}\\right)$, as part of the process of radioactive decay.<br>Electrons do not exist inside the nucleus of an atom. Electrons are negatively charged subatomic particles that orbit the nucleus in electron shells or energy levels, but they are not found within the nucleus. The nucleus primarily contains protons and neutrons.<br>So option (3) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',2018,'AIIMS','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(46140,'physics',40,245,487,'The ground state energy of an atom is $-217.8 \\,{eV}$. Photon emitted during the transition of electron from $n=3$ to $n=1$ state, is incident on a photosensitive material of unknown work function. The photoelectrons are emitted from the materials with a maximum kinetic energy of $190.29 {eV}$. The threshold wavelength of the material used is<br>(Take ${h}=6.62 \\times 10^{-34} {Js}$ ]','[{"text": "$3750\\\\,$$ \\\\mathop A^{~~\\\\circ}  $", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$1275\\\\,$$ \\\\mathop A^{~~\\\\circ}  $", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$5347\\\\,$$ \\\\mathop A^{~~\\\\circ}  $", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$7255\\\\,$$ \\\\mathop A^{~~\\\\circ}  $", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'For transition from $n=3$ to $n=1$,<br>Energy of the emitted photon,<br>$h u=E_{3}-E_{1}$<br>$ = \\left[ {\\frac{{ - 217.8}}{{{3^2}}} - \\left( {\\frac{{ - 217.8}}{{{1^2}}}} \\right)} \\right]eV$<br>$ = [ - 24.2 + 217.8]eV$<br>$ = 193.6\\,eV$<br>From Einstein\'s photoelectric equation,<br>${h} u={K}_{\\text {max }}+{W}_{0}$<br>$\\therefore \\quad {W}_{0}={hu}-{K}_{\\max }$<br>$ = (193.6 - 190.29)eV$<br>$ = 3.31eV$<br>${\\text{But }}{\\lambda _0} = \\frac{{hc}}{{{W_0}}}$<br>$ = \\frac{{6.62 \\times {{10}^{ - 34}} \\times 3 \\times {{10}^8}}}{{3.31 \\times 1.6 \\times {{10}^{ - 19}}}}$<br>$ = 3.75 \\times {10^{ - 7}}\\,m$,<br>$ = 3750$$ \\mathop A^{~~\\circ}  $',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(46141,'physics',41,406,806,'If half-life of an element is $69.3\\,h$, then how much of its percent will decay in $10\\,th$ to $11\\,th\\,h$? (Initial activity $ = 50\\,\\mu \\,Ci)$','[{"text": "$1 \\\\%$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$2 \\\\%$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$3 \\\\%$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$4 \\\\%$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Given, half-life of an element, ${T_{1/2}} = 69.3\\,h$<br>Let initial amount (at $t=0$) of radioactive element is $N_{0}$<br>Active nuclei at $t = 10\\,h$,<br>$N_{1}=N_{0} e^{-10 \\lambda}$<br>Active nuclei at $t=11 \\mathrm{~h}, N_{2}=N_{0} e^{-11 \\lambda}$<br>$\\%$ decay in 10 th to 11 th hours<br>$=\\dfrac{N_{1}-N_{2}}{N_{1}} \\times 100$<br>$=\\dfrac{N_{0} e^{-10 \\lambda}-N_{0} e^{-11 \\lambda}}{N_{0} e^{-10 \\lambda}} \\times 100$<br>$=\\left(1-e^{-\\lambda}\\right) \\times 100$<br>$=\\left[1-e^{\\frac{-0.693}{69.3}}\\right] \\times 100 \\quad\\left[\\because\\right.$ where, $\\left.\\lambda=\\dfrac{0.693}{T_{1 / 2}}\\right]$<br>$\\begin{aligned}& =\\left[1-e^{-0.01}\\right] \\times 100 \\\\& =[1-0.99] \\times 100 \\\\& =0.01 \\times 100=1 \\%\\end{aligned}$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2019,'AIIMS','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(46143,'physics',41,410,816,'A neutron breaks into a proton, an electron ( $\\beta$ particle) and anti-neutrino<br>$\\left({ }_{0} n^{1} \\rightarrow{ }_{1} H^{1}+{ }_{1} \\beta^{0}+\\bar{v}\\right)$. The energy released in the process in $MeV$ is','[{"text": "$731\\\\,MeV$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$7.31\\\\,MeV$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$0.731\\\\,MeV$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$1.17\\\\,MeV$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'According to the question,<br>${ }_{0} n^{1} \\rightarrow{ }_{1} H^{1}+{ }_{1} \\beta^{0}+\\bar{v}$<br>The energy released can be calculated by using,<br>$\\Delta E=\\Delta m c^{2}=\\left({ }_{1} H^{1}+{ }_{1} \\beta^{0}\\right)-m\\left({ }_{0} n^{1}\\right)$<br>By putting values, we get,<br>$\\Delta E \\simeq 0.731\\,MeV$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2005,'AIIMS','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(48397,'biology',101,575,1154,'MTPs are considered relatively safe during _______ trimester of pregnancy.','[{"text": "${{\\\\rm{2}}^{{\\\\rm{nd}}}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${{\\\\rm{1}}^{{\\\\rm{st}}}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${{\\\\rm{3}}^{{\\\\rm{rd}}}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "All of these", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'MTPs are considered relatively safe during ${{\\rm{1}}^{{\\rm{st}}}}$ trimester of pregnancy.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(46146,'physics',41,404,803,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> The binding energy per nucleon, for nuclei with atomic mass number $A>100$, decrease with $A$.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> The nuclear forces are weak for heavier nuclei.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'It is a well-established fact that the binding energy per nucleon decreases for heavy nuclei $(\\mathrm{A}>100)$. This phenomenon is primarily due to the increasing influence of the Coulomb repulsion between protons as the number of protons in the nucleus increases. The Coulomb repulsion counteracts the attractive nuclear force, leading to a decrease in the binding energy per nucleon. The nuclear forces, often referred to as the strong nuclear force, are responsible for holding the nucleus together. The strong nuclear force is not weak for heavier nuclei; it is the dominant force that overcomes the electrostatic repulsion between protons and maintains nuclear stability. The decrease in binding energy per nucleon for heavy nuclei is primarily due to the increasing Coulomb repulsion between protons, not the weakness of the nuclear forces.<br>The binding energy per nucleon decreases in heavy nuclei due to the Coulomb repulsion, not the weakness of the nuclear forces. So, the assertion is correct, but the reason provided is incorrect. So option (3) is correct',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',2006,'AIIMS','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(46148,'physics',42,324,594,'A light-emitting diode (LED) has a voltage drop of  $2V$ across it and passes a current of  $10\\,\\mu A,$ when it operates with a  $6V$ battery with a limiting resistor $R$. What is the value of $R$?','[{"text": "$200\\\\,\\\\Omega $", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$100\\\\,\\\\Omega $", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$400\\\\,\\\\Omega $", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$400\\\\,k\\\\Omega $", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'$I{\\text{ }} = \\frac{V}{R}$ or $R{\\text{ }} = \\frac{V}{I}$<br>$R = \\frac{{\\left( {6 - 2} \\right)V}}{{10 \\times {{10}^{ - 6}}A}} = 400\\,k\\Omega $',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(46149,'physics',41,408,812,'A nuclear explosive is designed to deliver $1\\,MW$ power in the form of heat energy. If the explosion is designed with nuclear fuel consisting of $U^{235}$ to run a reactor at this power level for one year, then the amount of fuel needed is (take, energy per fission is $200\\,MeV$)','[{"text": "$1\\\\,kg$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$0.01\\\\,kg$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$3.84\\\\,kg$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$0.384\\\\,kg$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Energy released per fission,<br>$E = 200\\,MeV = 200 \\times 26 \\times {10^{ - 13}}$<br>$ = 3.2 \\times {10^{ - 11}}\\,J$<br>Total energy required to run a $1\\,MW$ reaction for one year $ = {10^6}\\,J\\,{S^{ - 1}} \\times (365 \\times 24 \\times 60 \\times 60)$<br>$ = 3.15 \\times {10^{13}}\\,J$<br>Since, 1 fission (atom of $U^{235}$ ) produces<br>$3.2 \\times 10^{-11} J$ of energy.<br>So, total number of $U^{235}$ atoms required is<br>$\\dfrac{3.15 \\times 10^{13}}{3.2 \\times 10^{-11}}=9.84 \\times 10^{23}$.<br>$\\because$ Mass of $U^{235}$ containing $9.84 \\times 10^{23}$ atom,<br>$M=\\dfrac{235}{6.02 \\times 10^{23}}=9.84 \\times 10^{23}$<br>$ = 384\\,g = 0.384\\,kg$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',2017,'AIIMS','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(46151,'physics',40,245,487,'If the wavelength of first transition of the Balmer series is displaced by $\\Delta \\lambda  = 0.2$$ \\mathop A^{~~\\circ}  $, then the velocity of the excited hydrogen atoms is found to be ${N} \\times 10^{3} {~m} / {s}$. When their radiations are observed at an angle $\\theta=60^{\\circ}$ to their motion, then the value of ${N}$ is<br>[Take ${R}=10^{7} {~m}^{-1}$ ]','[{"text": "12.62", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "10.78", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "16.67", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "19.47", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'The wavelength of the first Balmer line is<br>\n$\\lambda  = \\frac{1}{{\\bar v}} = \\frac{1}{{R\\left( {\\frac{1}{{{2^2}}} - \\frac{1}{{{3^2}}}} \\right)}} = \\frac{{36}}{{5R}}m$<br>In general, by Doppler effect $\\lambda \' = \\left[ {\\frac{{c - {v_s}}}{{c - {v_o}}}} \\right]\\lambda $<br>Here $\\lambda \' = \\left[ {\\frac{{c - v\\cos \\theta }}{{c - 0}}} \\right]\\lambda $<br>$\\therefore \\quad \\Delta \\lambda  = \\left| {\\lambda \' - \\lambda } \\right| = \\frac{{v\\cos \\theta }}{c}\\lambda $<br>$\\therefore \\quad v = \\frac{{c\\Delta \\lambda }}{{\\lambda \\cos \\theta }} = \\frac{{3 \\times {{10}^8} \\times 0.2 \\times {{10}^{ - 10}}}}{{\\left( {\\frac{{36}}{{5R}}} \\right) \\times \\cos 60^\\circ {\\rm{ }}}}$<br>$ = \\frac{{0.6 \\times {{10}^{ - 2}} \\times 5 \\times {{10}^7} \\times 2}}{{36 \\times 1}}$<br>$ = 16.67 \\times {10^3}\\,m/s$<br>$\\therefore N = 16.67$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(46152,'physics',41,406,810,'Outside a nucleus,','[{"text": "Neutron is stable", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Neutron is unstable", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Proton and neutron both are stable", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Proton and neutron both are unstable", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'The neutron has no net charge and mass is slightly larger than proton. Protons and neutrons constitute the nuclei of atoms. Outside the nucleus the neutron is unstable. The life of neutron outside the nucleus is about 932 seconds. ( $\\simeq 15.5$ minutes).<br>After this period, $n \\rightarrow p+e^{-}+\\bar{v}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2013,'AIIMS','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(46154,'physics',41,404,803,'Binding energy per nucleon plot against the mass number for stable nuclei is shown in the figure. Which curve is correct?','[{"text": "$A$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$B$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$C$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$D$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'The mass number increases, binding energy per nucleon increases up to $\\operatorname{Fe}(A=56)$. After atomic number greater than 56 , binding energy per nucleon decreases.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2021,'MHTCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/41_46154_question_1.png'),
(46156,'physics',42,324,594,'Symbolic representation of photodiode is','[{"text": "", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'In photodiode, it is illuminated by light radiations, which in turn produces electric current.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(46157,'physics',41,406,806,'The radioactivity of a sample is $R_{1}$ at a time $T_{1}$ and $R_{2}$ at a time $T_{2}$. If the half-life of the specimen is $T$, the number of atoms that have disintegrated in the time $\\left(T_{1}-T_{2}\\right)$ is proportional to','[{"text": "$\\\\left(R_{1} T_{1}-R_{2} T_{2}\\\\right)$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\left(R_{1}-R_{2}\\\\right)$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\left(R_{1}-R_{2}\\\\right) / T$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\left(R_{1}-R_{2}\\\\right) T$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Given symbol $T=T_{1 / 2}=\\dfrac{0.693}{\\lambda}$<br>$\\Rightarrow$ we can write $\\lambda=\\left(\\dfrac{0.693}{T}\\right)$<br>$\\Rightarrow$ Radioactivity $R=\\lambda N$<br>$R_{1}=\\lambda N_{1}$<br>$R_{2}=\\lambda N_{2}$<br>(Since $\\lambda$ is same as only one sample is involved)<br>Subtracting : $\\left(R_{1}-R_{2}\\right)=\\lambda\\left(N_{1}-N_{2}\\right)$<br>$\\Rightarrow\\left(N_{1}-N_{2}\\right)=\\left(\\dfrac{R_{1}-R_{2}}{\\lambda}\\right)$<br>$=\\left(\\dfrac{R_{1}-R_{2}}{0.693}\\right)(T)$<br>$\\Rightarrow$ Required $\\left(N_{1}-N_{2}\\right) \\propto\\left[\\left(R_{1}-R_{2}\\right) T\\right]$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2022,'MHTCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(46159,'physics',40,240,479,'If wavelength of photon emitted due to transition of an electron from the third orbit to the first orbit in a hydrogen atom is $\\lambda$, then the wavelength of photon emitted due to transition of electrons from the fourth orbit to the second orbit will be ${N}$ times $\\lambda$. What is the value of ${N}$ ?','[{"text": "$2.65\\\\,\\\\lambda $", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$7.35\\\\,\\\\lambda $", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$5.47\\\\,\\\\lambda $", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$4.74\\\\,\\\\lambda $", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Transition of electrons from the third orbit to the first orbit,<br>$\\frac{1}{\\lambda } = R\\left[ {\\frac{1}{{{1^2}}} - \\frac{1}{{{3^2}}}} \\right]$<br>$\\Rightarrow \\lambda=\\dfrac{9}{8 {R}}$<br>Transition of electrons from the fourth orbit to the second orbit,<br>$\\frac{1}{{\\lambda \'}} = R\\left[ {\\frac{1}{{{2^2}}} - \\frac{1}{{{4^2}}}} \\right]\\quad  \\Rightarrow \\lambda \' = \\frac{{16}}{{3R}}$<br>Now, $\\dfrac{\\lambda^{\\prime}}{\\lambda}=\\dfrac{16}{3 {R}} \\times \\dfrac{8 {R}}{9}=\\dfrac{128}{27}$<br>$\\Rightarrow \\lambda^{\\prime}=\\dfrac{128}{27} \\lambda=4.74\\, \\lambda$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(46160,'physics',41,406,806,'For a radioactive substance, fraction of its initial quantity $\\left(N_{0}\\right)$ which will disintegrate in its average life time is about $(e=2.71)$','[{"text": "$\\\\left(\\\\dfrac{1}{3}\\\\right) N_{0}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\left(\\\\dfrac{2}{3}\\\\right) N_{0}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$(0.9) N_{0}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\left(\\\\dfrac{1}{2}\\\\right) N_{0}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Let the amount of radioactive substance initially be \' $N_{0}$ \' and amount remaining be \' $N$ \'.<br>We know $N=N_{0} e^{-\\lambda t}$<br>Average lifetime $=\\tau$<br>$t=\\tau=\\dfrac{1}{\\lambda} \\Rightarrow N=N_{0} e^{-\\dfrac{\\lambda}{\\lambda}}=\\dfrac{N_{0}}{e}$<br>Fraction of remaining substance, $\\dfrac{N}{N_{0}}=\\dfrac{1}{e}$<br>$f=$ Fraction of disintegrated substance<br>$=1-\\dfrac{N}{N_{0}}$<br>$\\Rightarrow f=1-\\dfrac{1}{e}=1-\\dfrac{1}{2.71}=\\dfrac{2}{3}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2022,'MHTCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(46163,'physics',41,403,801,'The ratio of radii of 2 nuclei with mass number 1 and 64 respectively is:','[{"text": "$1 / 64$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1 / 4$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$1 / 8$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1 / 2$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'$\\Rightarrow R \\propto A^{1 / 3}$<br>$\\dfrac{R_{1}}{R_{2}}=\\left[\\dfrac{A_{1}}{A_{2}}\\right]^{1 / 3}=\\left[\\dfrac{1}{64}\\right]^{1 / 3}=\\dfrac{1}{4}$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(46167,'physics',41,403,801,'Two nuclei have mass number in ratio of $1: 4$. The ratio of their nuclear densities is:','[{"text": "$1: 4$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1: 2$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1: 64$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1: 1$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'The density of matter in nuclei is independent of mass number and same for all nuclei. Hence, ratio is $1: 1$.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(46380,'physics',42,323,592,'The width of the depletion region in a $P$ - $N$ junction diode is','[{"text": "Increased by reverse bias", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Increased by forward bias", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Decreased by reverse bias", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Independent of the bias voltage", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'The width of the depletion region in a $P$ - $N$ junction diode is increased by reverse bias and decreased by forward bias',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(46171,'physics',41,403,800,'Number of protons and neutrons in $T H_{90}^{234}$ respectively are:','[{"text": "90,234", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "144,90", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "90,144", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "234,90", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'$P=90, n=234-90=144$.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(46173,'physics',40,239,478,'Magnetic field at the centre (at nucleus) of the hydrogen like atoms (atomic number $={Z}$ ) due to the motion of electron in $n^{\\text {th }}$ orbit is proportional to $n^{p} Z^{q}$. What is the value of $q-p$ ?','[{"text": "4", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "8", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "2", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "10", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'${B_n} = \\frac{{{\\mu _0}{I_n}}}{{2{r_n}}}$<br>${B_n} = \\frac{{{\\mu _0}e}}{{2{r_n}T}}$<br>$B_{n}=\\dfrac{\\mu_{0} e}{2 r_{n}\\left(\\dfrac{2 \\pi r_{n}}{v_{n}}\\right)}=\\dfrac{\\mu_{0} e v_{n}}{4 \\pi r_{n}^{2}}$<br>$B_{n} \\propto \\dfrac{v_{n}}{\\left(r_{n}\\right)^{2}}$<br>But $v \\propto \\dfrac{Z}{n}$ and $r_{n} \\propto \\dfrac{n^{2}}{Z}$<br>$\\therefore \\quad {B}_{{n}} \\propto \\dfrac{{Z}^{3}}{{n}^{5}}$<br>$B_{n} \\propto {n}^{-5} Z^{3}$<br>$\\therefore {\\mkern 1mu} \\,\\,\\,q = 3,p =  - 5$<br>$\\therefore q-p=3-(-5)=8$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
