(46194,'physics',41,403,800,'If the mass number of an atom is $A=40$ and its electron configuration is $1\\,{s^2},2\\,{s^2},2\\,{p^6},3\\,{s^2},3\\,{p^6}$, the number of neutrons and protons in its nucleus will be:','[{"text": "22,18", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "18,22", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "20,20", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "18,18", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'No. of protons $=2+2+6+2+6=18$<br>No. of neutrons $=40-18=22$.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(46196,'physics',42,329,604,'The plate current will be zero in a triode, if the negative potential applied on grid is: (here $\\mu $ is amplification factor,${R_p}$  is plate resistance, ${V_p}$ is plate potential)','[{"text": "$\\\\mu \\\\,{V_p}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${V_p}/\\\\mu $", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${V_p}/{R_p}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${V_p}{R_p}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'$\\mu  = \\left( {\\frac{{{V_p} - 0}}{{ - {V_g} - 0}}} \\right) = \\frac{{{V_p}}}{{ - {V_g}}}$<br>$ \\Rightarrow  - {V_g} = \\frac{{{V_p}}}{\\mu }$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(46197,'physics',41,403,801,'The ratio of the nuclear radii of the gold isotope ${}_{79}^{197}Au$ and silver isotope ${}_{47}^{107}Ag$ is:','[{"text": "1.23", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "0.216", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "4", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "0.612", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'$\\dfrac{R_{1}}{R_{2}}=\\left(\\dfrac{A_{1}}{A_{2}}\\right)^{1 / 3}=\\left(\\dfrac{197}{107}\\right)^{1 / 3}=1.255 \\approx 1.23$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(46200,'physics',42,324,594,'The energy gap of silicon is 1.14 $eV$. The maximum wavelength at which silicon will begin absorbing energy is','[{"text": "$1088.8\\\\,\\\\mathop A\\\\limits^ \\\\circ  $", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$10888\\\\,\\\\mathop A\\\\limits^ \\\\circ  $", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$10.888\\\\,\\\\mathop A\\\\limits^ \\\\circ  $", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$108.88\\\\,\\\\mathop A\\\\limits^ \\\\circ  $", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'${\\lambda _{\\max }} = \\frac{{hc}}{E} = \\frac{{6.6 \\times {{10}^{ - 34}} \\times 3 \\times {{10}^8}}}{{1.14 \\times 1.6 \\times {{10}^{ - 19}}}} = 10888\\mathop A\\limits^ \\circ  $',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(46203,'physics',41,404,803,'The binding energy per nucleon of dueterium and helium nuclei are $1.1\\,MeV$ and $7\\,MeV$ respectively. When two deuterium nuclei fuse to form a helium nucleus the energy released in the fusion is:','[{"text": "$24.6\\\\,MeV$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$25.1\\\\,MeV$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$22.3\\\\,MeV$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$23.6\\\\,MeV$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'${ }_{1} H^{2}+{ }_{1} H^{2} \\rightarrow 2 H e^{4}+\\Delta E$<br>The binding energy per nucleon of a deuterium $ = 1.1\\,MeV$.<br>$\\therefore$ Total binding energy $ = 4 \\times 1.1 = 4.4\\,MeV$.<br>The binding energy per nucleon of a helium nuclei $ = 7\\,MeV$.<br>$\\therefore$ Total binding energy $ = 4 \\times 7 = 28\\,MeV$.<br>Hence, energy released.<br>$\\Delta E = (28 - 4.4)\\,MeV = 23.6\\,MeV.$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
