(46756,'physics',42,323,592,'The circuit shown in figure contains two diodes each with a forward resistance of 50 $ohm$ and with infinite reverse resistance. If the battery voltage is 6 $V$, the current through the 100 $ohm$ resistance is','[{"text": "$0.01\\\\,A$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$0.02\\\\,A$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$0.03\\\\,A$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$0.04\\\\,A$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'As per given circuit, diode ${D_1}$ is forward biased and offers a resistance of 50 ohm. Diode ${D_2}$ is reverse biased and as its corresponding resistance is infinite, no current flows through it. Thus the equivalent circuit is as shown in Figure. As all the three resistances are  in series,the current through the circuit is<br>$I = \\frac{{6V}}{{\\left( {50 + 150 + 100} \\right)\\Omega }} = \\frac{6}{{300}}A = 0.02A$<br>Current through ${D_2}$ is zero<br>Current through ${D_1}$ and $100k\\Omega $ is $ = 0.02A$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/42_46756_question_1.png'),
(46758,'physics',42,323,592,'Find the current passing through the battery.','[{"text": "$1\\\\,A$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$2\\\\,A$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$0.5\\\\,A$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$4\\\\,A$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'The lower diode is reverse biased and 	hence current does not pass through it. The equivalent resistance is $15\\Omega $. The net current flows through the battery is <br>$i = \\frac{{15}}{{15}} = 1A$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/42_46758_question_1.png'),
(46759,'physics',41,408,814,'Find the minimum kinetic energy (in eV ) of an<br>${\\alpha}$-particle to cause the reaction ${{ }^{14} {~N}(\\alpha, p){ }^{17} {O}}$. The masses of ${{ }^{14} {~N},{ }^{4} {He},{ }^{1} {H}}$ and ${{ }^{17} {O}}$ are respectively $14.00307{\\rm{ }}u,{\\rm{ }}4.00260{\\rm{ }}u,{\\rm{ }}1.007.83{\\rm{ }}u$ and $16.99913{\\rm{ }}u.$','[{"text": "$1.54\\\\,MeV$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$3.62\\\\,MeV$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$5.74\\\\,MeV$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$8.64\\\\,MeV$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Since the masses are given in atomic mass units, it is easiest to proceed by finding the mass difference between reactants and products in the same units and then multiplying by ${931.5 {MeV} / {u}}$. Thus, we have<br>$Q = (14.00307\\,u + 4.00260\\,u$<br>$ - 1.00783\\,u - 16.99913\\,u)\\left( {931.5\\frac{{MeV}}{u}} \\right)$<br>$ = {\\rm{ }} - 1.20\\,MeV$<br>${Q}$ value is negative. It means reaction is endothermic<br>So, the minimum kinetic energy of ${\\alpha}$-particle to initiate this reaction would be,<br>${K_{\\min }} = |Q|\\left( {\\frac{{{m_\\alpha }}}{{{m_N}}} + 1} \\right)$<br>$ = (1.20)\\left( {\\frac{{4.00260}}{{14.00307}} + 1} \\right) = 1.54\\,MeV$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(46760,'physics',42,323,592,'Diode is forward biased and the applied voltage is greater than the potential barrier then<br>I) resistance of the junction in the forward bias decreases<br>II)   potential barrier remains same<br>III) width of barrier decreases<br>IV) $p$-type is connected to (-)$ve$ terminal','[{"text": "All are true", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "All are false", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "I, III, IV are true", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "I,II, III are true", "isCorrect": true}]',3,NULL,NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(46762,'physics',42,323,592,'A $p$ - $n$ junction diode has breakdown voltage of 28$V$. If applied external voltage in reverse bias is 40$V$ the current through it is','[{"text": "Zero", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Infinite", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "10$A$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "15$A$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'The diode cannot offer any resistance 	when it breaks down.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(46765,'physics',42,323,592,'Two similar $p$-$n$ junctions can be connected in three different ways as shown in the figures. The two connections across which the potential difference is same are','[{"text": "Circuits $A$ and $B$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Circuits $B$ and $C$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Circuits $A$ and $C$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "All the circuits", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'In circuit ‘$A$’,  $N$ is connected with $N$, which is not a series combination of $p$-$n$ junction. In circuit ‘$B$’, each $p$-$n$ junction is forward biased. <br>In circuit ‘$C$’, each $p$-$n$ junction is reverse biased, potential will be distributed equally across two diodes in $B$ and $C$.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/42_46765_question_1.png'),
(46767,'physics',42,322,590,'An $n$-type semiconductor has valence bond is 20$meV$ below the conduction band. In a thermal collision, transferrable energy is $KT$. The value of $T$ for which electrons start to jump in conduction band is :','[{"text": "$232\\\\,K$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$348\\\\,K$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$400\\\\,K$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$600\\\\,K$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'$KT = 20 \\times {10^{ - 3}}eV$<br>$\\therefore T = \\frac{{20 \\times {{10}^{ - 3}}}}{k}eV = \\frac{{20 \\times {{10}^{ - 3}}eV}}{{8.62 \\times {{10}^{ - 5}}eV/K}}$<br>$ = 232\\,K$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(46769,'physics',42,321,588,'Which of the energy band diagrams shown in the figure corresponds to that of a semiconductor?','[{"text": "", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'In semiconductors, the forbidden energy gap between the valence band and conduction band is very small, almost equal to $kT$.Moreover, valenceband is completely filled where as conduction band is empty.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(46771,'physics',42,322,590,'A pure semiconductor has equal electron and 	hole concentration of   ${10^{16}}{m^{ - 3}}.$ Doping by indium increases ${n_h}$ to  $4.5 \\times {10^{22}}{m^{ - 3}}.$ What is  ${n_e}$ in the doped semiconductor?','[{"text": "${10^{22}}{m^{ - 3}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${10^6}{m^{ - 3}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$4.5 \\\\times {10^{22}}{m^{ - 3}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{{{{10}^{32}}}}{{4.5 \\\\times {{10}^{22}}}}{m^{ - 3}}$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'${n_e}{n_h} = {n^2}$ or ${n_e} = \\frac{{{n^2}}}{{{n_h}}} = \\frac{{{{10}^{16}} \\times {{10}^{16}}}}{{4.5 \\times {{10}^{22}}}}$<br>$ = \\frac{{{{10}^{32}}}}{{4.5 \\times {{10}^{22}}}}{m^{ - 3}}$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(46772,'physics',42,323,593,'Consider the following statements $A$ and $B$ and identify the correct answer<br>$A$: Germanium is preferred over silicon in the construction of zener diode.<br>$B$: Germanium has high thermal stability than silicon in the construction of Zener diode.','[{"text": "Both ($A$) and ($B$) are true", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both ($A$) and ($B$) are false", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "($A$) is true but ($B$) is false", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "($A$) is false but ($B$) is true", "isCorrect": false}]',1,NULL,NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
