(46776,'physics',41,406,806,'A nucleus of ${U_{x_{1}}}$ has a half-life of 24.1 days. How long   a sample of ${U_{x_{1}}}$ take to change to ${90 \\%}$ of it to ${U_{x_{2}}}$ ?','[{"text": "$30\\\\,d$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$50\\\\,d$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$90\\\\,d$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$80\\\\,d$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Here, $T_{1 / 2}=24.1$ days,<br> $N_{0}=100$,$N=(100-90)=10$<br>Thus, $\\dfrac{N_{0}}{N}=\\dfrac{100}{10}=10$<br>As $\\frac{{{N_0}}}{N} = {2^{t/{T_{1/2}}}} \\Rightarrow 10 = {2^{t/{T_{l/2}}}}$<br>or $\\log 10 = \\log {2^{t/{T_{1/2}}}}$<br>$ \\Rightarrow t = \\frac{1}{{0.3010}} \\times 24.1\\,d = 80\\,d$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(46777,'physics',42,322,589,'Among the following,the wrong statement in 	the case of semiconductor is','[{"text": "Resistivity is in between that of a conductor and  insulator", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Temperature coefficient of resistance is negative", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Doping increases conductivity", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "At absolute zero temperature it behaves like a conductor", "isCorrect": true}]',3,NULL,NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(46779,'physics',42,322,589,'Fermi level of energy of an intrinsic semiconductor lies','[{"text": "In the middle of the forbidden gap", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Below the middle of forbidden gap", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Above the middle of forbidden gap", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Outside the forbidden gap", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'In Instrinsic semiconductor, the number of holes in valence band is equal to the number of  ${e^ - }$ in the conduction band. Hence probability of occupation of energy in both bands are equal. i.e., fermi level for intrinsic semiconductor lies in the middle of  forbidden band.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(46780,'physics',41,406,806,'The mean lives of a radioactive substance are 1620 years and 405 years for ${\\alpha}$-emission and ${\\beta}$-emission respectively. Find out the time (in years) during which three fourth of a sample will decay if it is decaying both by ${\\alpha}$-emission and ${\\beta}$-emission simultaneously. (Given: ${\\ln 2=0.693}$ )','[{"text": "$449\\\\,yr$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$765\\\\,yr$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$269\\\\,yr$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$949\\\\,yr$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'The decay constants for $\\alpha$ and $\\beta$ emissions are $1 / 1620$ and $1 / 405$ per year respectively.<br>In this case effective decay constant for both decays simultaneously is<br>$\\lambda=\\lambda_{\\alpha}+\\lambda_{\\beta}$<br>$\\Rightarrow \\lambda=\\dfrac{1}{1620}+\\dfrac{1}{405}=\\dfrac{1}{324}$ year $^{-1}$<br>Let $t$ be time in which the given sample decays three fourth.<br>Therefore, the fraction of sample undecayed in time $t$ is $1 / 4$. Hence <br>$N=i_{v_{0}} / 4$<br>Now from decay equation<br>$N=N_{0} e^{-\\lambda t} \\Rightarrow \\dfrac{N_{0}}{4}=N_{0} e^{-\\lambda t}$<br>$\\Rightarrow t=\\dfrac{\\ln (4)}{\\lambda}=1.386 \\times 324=449\\,\\, {yr}$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(46781,'physics',42,322,590,'If the ratio of the concentration of electrons to that of holes in a semiconductor is  $\\frac{7}{5}$ and the ratio of currents is  $\\frac{7}{4},$ then the ratio of drift velocities is','[{"text": "$5/8$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$4/5$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$5/4$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$4/7$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'$I = {n_e}A{v_d}$<br>$\\frac{{{I_e}}}{{{I_h}}} = \\frac{{{n_e} \\times {{\\left( {{v_d}} \\right)}_e}}}{{{n_h} \\times {{\\left( {{v_d}} \\right)}_h}}}$<br>Here $\\frac{{{n_e}}}{{{n_h}}} = \\frac{7}{5},\\frac{{{I_e}}}{{{I_h}}} = \\frac{7}{4}\\therefore \\frac{7}{4} = \\frac{7}{5} \\times \\frac{{{{\\left( {{v_d}} \\right)}_e}}}{{{{\\left( {{v_d}} \\right)}_h}}} \\Rightarrow \\frac{{{{\\left( {{v_d}} \\right)}_e}}}{{{{\\left( {{v_d}} \\right)}_h}}} = \\frac{5}{4}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(46783,'physics',42,321,587,'If the resistivity of copper is $1.7 \\times {10^{ - 6}}\\Omega \\,cm$, then the mobility of electrons in copper, if each atom of copper contributes one free electron for conduction, is [The atomic weight of copper is 63.54 and its density is 8.96 $g$/$cc$]:','[{"text": "$23.36\\\\,c{m^2}/Vs$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$503.03\\\\,c{m^2}/Vs$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$43.25\\\\,c{m^2}/Vs$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$88.0\\\\,c{m^2}/Vs$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Mobility of electron $\\mu  = \\frac{\\sigma }{{ne}}$    -   (1)<br>also resistivity $\\rho  = \\frac{1}{\\sigma }$          -(2)<br>From Eqs. (i) and (ii) , we get $\\mu  = \\frac{1}{{ne\\rho }}$<br>Here, $n = $ number of free electrons per unit volume <br>$n = \\frac{{{N_0} \\times d}}{{atomic\\,weight}}$<br>$n = \\frac{{6.023 \\times {{10}^{23}} \\times 8.96}}{{63.54}} = 8.5 \\times {10^{22}}$<br>From Eqs. (3) and (4), we get   <br>$\\mu  = \\frac{1}{{8.5 \\times {{10}^{22}} \\times 1.6 \\times {{10}^{ - 19}} \\times 1.7 \\times {{10}^{ - 6}}}}$<br>$\\therefore \\mu  = 43.25c{m^2}/Vs$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(46786,'physics',41,406,806,'A human body excretes (removes by waste discharge, sweating etc.) certain materials by a law similar to radioactivity. If technetium is injected in some form in a human body, the body excretes half the amount in 24 hours. A patient is given an injection containing ${{ }^{99} {Tc}}$. This isotope is radioactive with a half-life of 6 hours. The activity from the body just after the injection is $6\\,\\mu Ci.$ How much time (in hours) will elapse before the activity falls to $3\\,\\mu Ci$ ?','[{"text": "$1.5\\\\,hrs$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$7.3\\\\,hrs$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$4.8\\\\,hrs$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$9.6\\\\,hrs$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'If in time $t$ total atoms inside the body, $N_{\\text {body }}=N_{0}(2)^{-t / 2 t}$<br>If after time $t, N_{A}=\\dfrac{N_{0}}{2}$<br>Total active atoms inside body at time  $t,$<br>${N_A} = {N_{{\\text{body }}}}{(2)^{ - t/6}}$<br>If after time $t$,$N_{A}=\\dfrac{N_{0}}{2}$<br>$\\frac{{{N_0}}}{2} = {N_0}{(2)^{ - t/24}} \\cdot {(2)^{t/6}}$<br>$ \\Rightarrow t\\left( {\\frac{1}{{24}} + \\frac{1}{6}} \\right) = 1$<br>$\\Rightarrow t=\\dfrac{24}{5}=4.8 {hrs}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(46817,'physics',42,325,595,'In the circuit shown in figure the maximum output voltage ${V_0}$ is','[{"text": "$5V$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$0\\\\,V$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{5}{{\\\\sqrt 2 }}V$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$10\\\\,V$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'For the positive half cycle of input the 	resulting network is shown below<br>$ \\Rightarrow {\\left( {{V_0}} \\right)_{\\max }} = \\frac{1}{2}{\\left( {{V_i}} \\right)_{\\max }} = \\frac{1}{2} \\times 10 = 5\\,V$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/42_46817_question_1.png'),
(46790,'physics',41,408,813,'In the process of nuclear fission of 1 gram uranium, the mass lost is 0.92 milligram. The efficiency of power house run by the fission reactor is ${10 \\%}$. To obtain 400-megawatt power from the power house, how much uranium  will be required per hour? ${\\left(c=3 \\times 10^{8} {~m} / {s}\\right)}$','[{"text": "$174\\\\,gm$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$235\\\\,gm$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$134\\\\,gm$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$364\\\\,gm$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Power to be obtained from power house $=400 {MW}$<br> In this case energy obtained per hour<br> $=400 {MW} \\times 1$ hour $=\\left(400 \\times 10^{6} {~W}\\right) \\times 3600 {~s}=144 \\times 10^{10} {~J}$<br>Here only $10 \\%$ of output is utilized. In order to obtain $144 \\times 10^{10}$ joule of useful energy.<br>The output energy from the power house.<br>$E=\\dfrac{\\left(144 \\times 10^{10}\\right) \\times 100}{10}=144 \\times 10^{11} {~J}$<br>Let this energy be obtained from a mass-loss of $\\Delta m {~kg}$.<br>Hence $(\\Delta m) c^{2}=144 \\times 10^{11} {~J}$<br>or $\\Delta m=\\dfrac{144 \\times 10^{11}}{\\left(3 \\times 10^{8}\\right)^{2}}=16 \\times 10^{-1} {~kg}=0.16 {gm}$<br>Since 0.92 milli gram $\\left( { = 0.92 \\times {{10}^{ - 3}}\\,g} \\right)$ mass is lost in 1 $g$ uranium,<br> hence for a mass loss of 0.16 $g$ the uranium required.<br>$\\Delta m^{\\prime}=\\dfrac{1 \\times 0.16}{0.92 \\times 10^{-3}}=174 {gm}$<br>Thus, to run the power house, 174 $gm$ uranium is required per hour.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(46794,'physics',41,406,806,'The activity of a radioactive element decreases to one third of the original activity ${A_{0}}$ in a period of nine years. After a further lapse of 9 years, if activity is found to be ${\\dfrac{A_{0}}{N}}$. Find the value of ${N}$.','[{"text": "4", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "9", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "5", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "12", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'$\\dfrac{A_{0}}{3}=A_{0}\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right)^{\\frac{9}{T_{1 / 2}}}$<br>$\\dfrac{A_{0}}{3}=A_{0}\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right)^{\\frac{9}{T_{1 / 2}}}$<br>$A^{\\prime}=\\dfrac{A_{0}}{3}\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right)^{\\frac{9}{T_{1 / 2}}}$<br>Dividing $\\dfrac{A^{\\prime} \\times 3}{A_{0}}=\\dfrac{1}{3}$ or $A^{\\prime}=\\dfrac{A_{0}}{9}$<br>Hence $N=9$.',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(46800,'physics',41,408,812,'A nucleus at rest disintegrates inte two smaller nuclei with their masses in the ratio of 2: 1. After disintegration they will move','[{"text": "in opposite directions with speed in the ratio of $1: 2$ respectively", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "in the same directions with same speed", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "in opposite directions with the same speed", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "in opposite directions with speed in the ratio of 2: 1 respectively", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'${m_1}:{m_2} = 2:1$<br>Using the conservation of momentum, $\\overrightarrow{p_{i}}=\\overrightarrow{p_{f}}$<br>$0 = {m_1}\\overrightarrow {{v_1}}  + {m_2}\\overrightarrow {{v_2}} $<br>$ \\Rightarrow {m_1}{{\\vec v}_1} =  - {m_2}\\overrightarrow {{v_2}} $<br>$\\frac{{{v_1}}}{{{v_2}}} = \\left| { - \\frac{{{m_2}}}{{{m_1}}}} \\right| = \\left| {\\frac{1}{2}} \\right| = 1:2$<br>Minus sign shows that they move in opposite directions.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2024,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(46803,'physics',41,403,801,'The mass number of nucleus having radius equal to half of the radius of nucleus with mass number 192 is','[{"text": "20", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "32", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "24", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "40", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Radius of nucleus, $R=R_{0} A^{1 / 3}$<br>$ \\Rightarrow R \\propto {A^{1/3}} \\Rightarrow \\frac{{{R_2}}}{{{R_1}}} = {\\left( {\\frac{{{A_{\\,2}}}}{{{A_1}}}} \\right)^{1/3}}$<br>$\\frac{{R/2}}{R} = {\\left( {\\frac{{{A_{\\,2}}}}{{192}}} \\right)^{1/3}} \\Rightarrow {A_{\\,2}} = \\frac{{192}}{{{2^3}}} = 24$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2024,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(46804,'physics',41,404,803,'Binding energy of a certain nucleus is  $18 \\times {10^8}\\,J.$  How much is the difference between total mass of all the nucleons and nuclear mass of the given nucleus?','[{"text": "$0.2{\\\\mkern 1mu} \\\\,\\\\mu g$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$10\\\\,\\\\mu g$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2\\\\,\\\\mu g$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$20\\\\,\\\\mu g$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'The binding energy of nucleus, $B E=\\Delta m c^{2}$<br>$\\Delta \\,m = \\frac{{BE}}{{{c^2}}} = \\frac{{18 \\times {{10}^8}}}{{{{\\left( {3 \\times {{10}^8}} \\right)}^2}}} = \\frac{{18 \\times {{10}^8}}}{{9 \\times {{10}^{16}}}}$<br>$ = \\frac{2}{{{{10}^8}}}\\,kg = 2 \\times {10^{ - 8}}\\,kg$<br>$ = 2 \\times {10^{ - 8}} \\times 1000\\,g$<br>$ = 2 \\times {10^{ - 5}}\\,g = 20\\mu \\,g$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2024,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(46806,'physics',42,323,593,'From the circuit shown below, the maximum and minimum values of zener diode current are','[{"text": "$6\\\\,mA,5mA$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$14\\\\,mA,5mA$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$9mA,1mA$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$3mA,2mA$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'When potential is 80$V$ the drop across Zener and Load is 50 $V$ <br>$\\therefore $ Load current $ = \\frac{{50}}{{10k}} = 5mA$<br> and current across $5k\\Omega $<br>$\\frac{{30V}}{{5k\\Omega }} = 6mA$<br>${i_{Zener}} + {i_{load}} = 6mA$<br>${i_{Zener}} = 6mA - {i_{load}}$<br>$ = 6mA - 5mA = 1mA$<br>Similarly when potential is 120 $V$<br>${i_R} = \\frac{{120 - 50}}{{5k}} = \\frac{{70}}{{5k}} = 14mA$<br>${i_Z}{\\rm{ }} = {\\rm{ }}{i_R}{\\rm{ }} - {\\rm{ }}{i_{load}} = 14 - 5 = 9mA$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/42_46806_question_1.png'),
(46807,'physics',41,404,803,'If $M_{0}$ is the mass of isotope ${ }_{5}^{12} B, M_{p}$ and $M_{n}$ are the masses of proton and neutron, then nuclear binding energy of isotope is','[{"text": "$\\\\left(5 M_{p}+7 M_{n}-M_{0}\\\\right) c^{2}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\left(M_{0}-5 M_{p}\\\\right) c^{2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\left(M_{0}-12 M_{n}\\\\right) c^{2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\left(M_{0}-5 M_{p}-7 M_{n}\\\\right) c^{2}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'As we know,<br>$B.E. = \\Delta \\,m{c^2}$<br>Here $\\Delta m=Z M_{p}+(A-Z) M_{n}-M_{0}$<br>$\\Delta m=\\left(5 M_{p}+7 M_{n}-M_{0}\\right)$<br>So, $B \\cdot E .=\\left(5 M_{p}+7 M_{n}-M_{0}\\right) c^{2}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2024,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(46809,'physics',41,404,802,'The energy equivalent of $1 g$ of substance is','[{"text": "$5.6\\\\,eV$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$5.6 \\\\times {10^{12}}\\\\,MeV$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$11.2 \\\\times {10^{24}}\\\\,MeV$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$5.6 \\\\times {10^{26}}\\\\,MeV$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'$E=m c^{2}=1 \\times 10^{-3} \\times\\left(3 \\times 10^{8}\\right)^{2} J$<br>$ = \\frac{{1 \\times {{10}^{ - 3}} \\times 3 \\times {{10}^8} \\times 3 \\times {{10}^8}}}{{1.6 \\times {{10}^{ - 19}}}}\\,\\,eV$<br>$\\quad \\left( {\\because 1\\,J = \\frac{1}{{1.6 \\times {{10}^{ - 19}}}}\\,\\,eV} \\right)$<br>$E = 56.169 \\times {10^{31}}eV$<br>$E = 5.6 \\times {10^{26}}MeV$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2024,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(46810,'physics',41,410,816,'From the statements given below :<br>(I) The angular momentum of an electron in $n^{\\text {th }}$ orbit is an integral multiple of $\\hbar$.<br>(II) Nuclear forces do not obey inverse square law.<br>(III) Nuclear forces are spin dependent.<br>(IV) Nuclear forces are non-central and charge independent.<br>(V) Stability of nucleus is inversely proportional to the value of packing  fraction.<br>Choose the correct answer from the options given below.','[{"text": "(II), (III), (IV), (V) only", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "(I), (III), (IV), (V) only", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "(I), (II), (III), (V) only", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "(I), (II), (III), (IV) and (V) only", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'According to Bohr hypothesis,  $L=m v r=n \\hbar$ and nuclear forces are spin dependent, non-central and charge independent. Also, stability of nucleus is inversely proportional to the value of packing  fraction. Nuclear force does not follow inverse sqaure law.',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',2024,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(46813,'physics',42,325,595,'Which is the correct diagram of a half-wave rectifier?','[{"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": true}]',3,NULL,NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(46815,'physics',42,325,595,'The peak voltage in the output of a half-wave 	diode rectifier fed with a sinusoidal signal without filter is 10$V$. The $d$.$c$. component of the output voltage is','[{"text": "$10/\\\\sqrt 2 \\\\,\\\\,V$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$10/\\\\pi \\\\,\\\\,V$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$10\\\\,V$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$20/\\\\pi \\\\,\\\\,V$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'${V_{dc}} = \\frac{{{V_m}}}{\\pi } = \\frac{{10}}{\\pi }volt$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(46818,'physics',41,408,812,'Which of the following nuclear fragments corresponding to nuclear fission between neutron $\\left({ }_{0}^{1} n\\right)$ and uranium isotope $\\left({ }_{92}^{235} U\\right)$ is correct?','[{"text": "$_{56}^{141}Ba + _{36}^{92}Kr + 3_0^1n$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$_{51}^{153}Sb + _{41}^{99}Nb + 3_0^1n$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$_{56}^{144}Ba + _{36}^{89}Kr + 4_0^1n$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$_{56}^{140}Xe + _{38}^{94}Sr + 3_0^1n$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'${ }_{92} U^{235}+{ }_{0} n^{1} \\rightarrow{ }_{56} B a^{141}+{ }_{36} K r^{92}+3_{0} n^{1}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2024,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(46819,'physics',42,323,592,'${V_A}\\,$ and $\\,{V_B}$ denote potentials of $A$ and $B$, then the equivalent resistance between $A$ and $B$ in the adjoining circuit is','[{"text": "$5\\\\Omega \\\\,\\\\,{\\\\rm{if}}\\\\,\\\\,{V_A} > {V_B}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$30\\\\Omega \\\\,\\\\,{\\\\rm{if}}\\\\,\\\\,{V_A} < {V_B}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{Both}}\\\\,{\\\\rm{(1)}}\\\\,{\\\\rm{and}}\\\\,{\\\\rm{(2)}}\\\\,$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{Neither (1) nor (2)}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'For a diode  $R = \\infty $ in the case of reverse bias and $R = 0$ in case of forward bias',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/42_46819_question_1.png'),
(46820,'physics',41,406,809,'In a hypothetical fission reaction,<br>${ }_{92} X^{236} \\rightarrow_{56} Y^{141}+{ }_{36} Z^{92}+3 R$<br>The identity of emitted particles $(R)$ is','[{"text": "$\\\\gamma$-radiations", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "electron", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "proton", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "neutron", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'$92=56+36+Z_{R} \\Rightarrow Z_{R}=0$<br>$\\therefore \\quad R$ is a neutral particle.<br>$A_{R}=\\dfrac{236-(141+92)}{3}=1$<br>So, the emitted particle $(R)$ is neutron.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2024,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(46821,'physics',42,323,592,'4 ideal diodes are connected as shown in the circuit the current through $50\\,\\Omega $ is','[{"text": "$0\\\\,A$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$0.5\\\\,A$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$0.6\\\\,A$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1\\\\,A$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'$i = \\frac{V}{R} = \\frac{0}{{50}} = 0$  (Potential difference across $50\\,\\Omega $ is zero).',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/42_46821_question_1.png'),
(46822,'physics',42,324,594,'The electrical conductivity of a semiconductor 	increases when electromagnetic radiation of wavelength shorter than 2480 $nm$ is incident on it. The forbidden band energy for the semiconductor is (in $eV$)','[{"text": "$0.5$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$0.9$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$0.7$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1.1$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'$E = \\frac{{hc}}{\\lambda } = \\frac{{12000}}{{24800{A^0}}}\\left( {{e_v}} \\right) = \\frac{1}{2} = 0.5eV$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(46823,'physics',41,408,814,'The energy released in the fusion of  $2\\,kg$  of hydrogen deep in the sun is $E_{H}$ and the energy released in the fission of $2\\,kg$ of  $^{235}U$  is $E_{U}$. The ratio $\\dfrac{E_{H}}{E_{U}}$ is approximately  (Consider the fusion reaction as,  $4_1^1H + 2{e^ - } \\to _2^4He + 2v + 6\\gamma  + 26.7MeV$ energy released in the fission reaction of ${ }^{235} U$ is  $200\\,MeV$ per fission nucleus and  ${N_A} = 6.023 \\times {10^{23}}\\,)$','[{"text": "15.04", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "25.6", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "7.62", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "9.13", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Energy released in each fussion is<br>$ = 26.7\\,MeV$<br>No of atoms in  $2\\,kg$  is $=N_{A} \\times \\dfrac{m}{M}=N_{A} \\times \\dfrac{2000}{1}$<br>No of fusion is $=\\dfrac{N_{A}}{4} \\times 2000$<br>Total energy released is<br>${E_H} = \\frac{{2000}}{4} \\times {N_A} \\times 26.7\\,MeV$<br>Energy released by  $2\\,\\,kg\\,{U^{235}}$  is<br>$E_{U}=\\dfrac{2000}{235} \\times N_{A} \\times 200$<br>So, $\\dfrac{E_{H}}{E_{U}}=\\dfrac{235 \\times 6.675}{200} \\approx 7.62$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',2024,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL);
INSERT INTO `qb_questions` (`id`, `subject_id`, `chapter_id`, `topic_id`, `subtopic_id`, `question_html`, `options`, `correct_index`, `explanation`, `explanation_image_url`, `difficulty`, `qtype`, `year`, `tag`, `created_at`, `question_image_url`) VALUES
(46824,'physics',42,323,592,'Two ideal junction diodes ${D_1},{D_2}$ are connected as shown in the figure. A 3$V$ battery is connected between $A$ and $B$. The current supplied by the battery if its positive terminal is connected to $A$ is','[{"text": "$0.1\\\\,A$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$0.3\\\\,A$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$0.9\\\\,A$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$90\\\\,A$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'If positive terminal is connected to $A$, ${D_1}$ is in forward bias and ${D_2}$ is in reverse bias. So $20\\Omega $ is ineffective.<br>$\\therefore i = \\frac{V}{R} = \\frac{3}{{10}} = 0.3A$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/42_46824_question_1.png'),
(46825,'physics',41,408,814,'The explosive in a Hydrogen bomb is a mixture of $_1{H^2},\\,{\\,_1}{H^3}$  and ${ }_{3} L i^{6}$ in some condensed form.<br>The chain reaction is given by<br>${ }_{3} L i^{6}+{ }_{0} n^{1} \\rightarrow{ }_{2} H e^{4}+{ }_{1} H^{3}$<br>${ }_{1} H^{2}+{ }_{1} H^{3} \\rightarrow{ }_{2} H e^{4}+{ }_{0} n^{1}$. During the explosion the energy released is approximately<br> [Given : $M\\left( {Li} \\right) = 6.01690\\,amu,$<br>$M\\left( {_1{H^2}} \\right) = 2.01471\\,amu,$<br>$M\\left( {_2H{e^4}} \\right) = 4.00388\\,amu,$<br>${\\rm{and}}\\,\\,\\,\\,1\\,amu = 931.5\\,MeV$','[{"text": "$28.12\\\\,MeV$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$22.22\\\\,MeV$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$12.64\\\\,MeV$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$16.48\\\\,MeV$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'${\\text{Adding}}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\frac{\\begin{gathered}\n  _3L{i^6}{ + _0}{n^1}{ \\to _2}H{e^4}{ + _1}{H^3} \\hfill \\\\\n  _1{H^2}{ + _1}{H^3}{ \\to _2}H{e^4}{ + _0}{n^1} \\hfill \\\\ \n\\end{gathered} }{{_3L{i^6}{ + _1}{H^2} \\to 2\\left( {_2H{e^4}} \\right)}}$<br>Energy released in process,<br>$Q = \\left( {{M_{{\\text{reactants }}}} - {M_{{\\text{products }}}}} \\right) \\times 931.5MeV$<br>$Q=\\left[M\\left({ }_{3} L i^{6}\\right)+M\\left({ }_{1} H^{2}\\right)-2 \\times M\\left({ }_{2} H e^{4}\\right)\\right]$<br>$ \\times \\,\\,931.5\\,MeV$<br>$Q=[(6.01690+2.01471-2 \\times 4.00388)]$<br>$ \\times \\,\\,931.5\\,MeV$<br>$Q = 22.216\\,MeV \\approx 22.22\\,MeV$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',2024,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(46826,'physics',42,322,590,'Which of the following energy band diagrams 	shows the $N$-type semiconductor?','[{"text": "", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'In $N$-type semiconductor impurity energy level lies just below the conduction band.',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(46827,'physics',41,408,812,'In a nuclear fission reaction of an isotope of mass $M$. Three similar daughter nuclei of same mass are formed. The speed of a daughter nuclei in terms of mass defect $\\Delta M$ will be','[{"text": "$\\\\dfrac{\\\\Delta M c^{2}}{3}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\sqrt{\\\\dfrac{2 c \\\\Delta M}{M}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$c \\\\sqrt{\\\\dfrac{2 \\\\Delta M}{M}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$c \\\\sqrt{\\\\dfrac{3 \\\\Delta M}{M}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'As,  $E = \\Delta {\\mkern 1mu} M{c^2}\\quad \\quad (1)$<br>As, after fission, 3 similar daughter nuclei of same mass are formed, their kinetic energy will be<br>$E = 3 \\times \\frac{1}{2}\\left( {\\frac{M}{3}} \\right){v^2}\\quad \\quad (2)$<br>By comparing (1) and (2)<br>$\\Delta M c^{2}=\\dfrac{1}{2} M v^{2}$<br>${v^2} = \\frac{{2\\,\\Delta \\,M{c^2}}}{M} \\Rightarrow v = \\sqrt {\\frac{{2\\,\\Delta \\,M{c^2}}}{M}} $<br>$ \\Rightarrow v = c\\sqrt {\\frac{{2\\,\\Delta \\,M}}{M}} $',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',2024,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(46835,'physics',42,322,589,'A $Ge$ specimen is doped with $Al$. The concentration of acceptor atoms is ${10^{21}}\\,atoms/{m^3}.$ Given that the intrinsic concentration of electron-hole pairs is  ${10^{19}}/{m^3},$ the concentration of electrons in the specimen is','[{"text": "${10^{17}}/{m^3}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${10^{15}}/{m^3}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${10^4}/{m^3}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${10^2}/{m^3}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'When $Ge$ specimen is doped with $Al$, then concentration of acceptor atoms is also called concentration of holes. Using formula, ${n_i}^2 = {n_0}{p_0}$  where  ${n_1} = $ concentration of electron hole pair $ = {10^{19}}{m^{ - 3}}$<br>${n_0} = $ concentration of electron<br>${p_0} = $ concentration of holes $ = {10^{21}}{\\rm{atom}}\\,\\,{m^{ - 3}}$<br>$ \\Rightarrow {\\left( {{{10}^{19}}} \\right)^2} = {10^{21}} \\times {n_0}$<br>$ \\Rightarrow {n_0} = {10^{17}}{m^{ - 3}}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(46836,'physics',42,322,589,'Assume that the number of hole-electron pair in an intrinsic semiconductor is proportional to ${e^{ - \\Delta E/2KT}}$.  Here  $\\Delta E = $ energy gap and $k = 8.62 \\times {10^{ - 5}}eV/kelvin.$ The energy gap for silicon is 1.1$eV$. The ratio of electron hole pairs at 300$K$ and 400 $K$ is:','[{"text": "${e^{ - 5.31}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${e^{ - 5}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$e$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${e^2}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'$\\frac{{{N_1}}}{{{N_2}}} = \\frac{{{e^{ - \\Delta E/2K{T_1}}}}}{{{e^{ - \\Delta E/2K{T_2}}}}}$<br>$ = {e^{\\frac{{\\Delta E}}{{2K}}\\left( {\\frac{1}{{{T_2}}} - \\frac{1}{{{T_1}}}} \\right)}}$<br>$ = \\frac{{1.1eV}}{{{e^2} \\times 8.62 \\times {{10}^{ - 5}}eV}}\\left( {\\frac{1}{{400}} - \\frac{1}{{300}}} \\right) = \\,{e^{ - 5.31}}$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(46837,'physics',42,322,590,'Pure  $Si$ at 300 $K$ has electron $({n_e})$ concentrations of $1.5 \\times {10^{16}}{m^{ - 3}}$ Doping by indium increases ${n_h}$ to  $4.5 \\times {10^{22}}{m^{ - 3}}.$ ${n_e}$ in the doped  $Si$ is','[{"text": "$5 \\\\times {10^9}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$7 \\\\times {10^9}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$9 \\\\times {10^9}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$8 \\\\times {10^9}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'We know that for a doped semiconductor in thermal equilibrium, we have <br>${n_e}{n_h} = n_i^2$<br>As per given data, ${n_i} = 1.5 \\times {10^{16}}{m^{ - 3}}$<br>${n_h} = 4.5 \\times {10^{22}}{m^{ - 3}}$<br>Thus ${n_e} = \\frac{{n_i^2}}{{{n_h}}} = \\frac{{{{\\left( {1.5 \\times {{10}^{16}}} \\right)}^2}{m^{ - 6}}}}{{4.5 \\times {{10}^{22}}{m^{ - 3}}}} = 5.0 \\times {10^9}{m^{ - 3}}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(46865,'physics',42,324,594,'Given below are two statements<div class="matching-question"><div class="column-left">Photovoltaic devices can convert optical radiation into electricity.</div><div class="column-right">Zener diode is designed to operate under reverse bias in breakdown region.<br>\nIn the light of the above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:</div></div>','[{"text": "Both Statement A and Statement B are incorrect", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Statement A is correct but Statement B is \\t\\tincorrect", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Statement A is incorect but Statement B is correct", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Statement A and Statement B are \\t\\t\\tcorrect.", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'For Statement A: Photocell/solar cell convert light energy into electric energy/current.<br>For Statement B: We use zener diode in reverse biased condition. When reverse biased voltage is more than break down volatge then it acts as stablizer.<br>Correct option is (4).',NULL,'Medium','MCQ type-2',2023,'NEET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(46866,'physics',42,325,596,'A full wave rectifier circuit consists of two $p-n$ junction diodes, a centre-tapped transformer, capacitor and a load resistance. Which of these components remove the ac ripple from the rectified output?','[{"text": "$p - n$ junction diodes", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Capacitor", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Load resistance", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "A centre-tapped transformer", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Capacitor is used to remove $A C$ ripples from output of rectifier.<br>Correct option is (2).',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2023,'NEET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(46867,'physics',42,325,596,'A full-wave rectifier with diodes $D_{1}$ and $D_{2}$ is used to rectify $50\\,Hz$ alternating voltage. The diode $D_{1}$ conducts____ times in one second.','[{"text": "75", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "50", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "100", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "25", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'A Full wave rectifier shows output for both the half cycles of $A C$ input. The output frequency of waveform across $D_{1}$ diode is $50\\,Hz$ and $D_{2}$ diode is $50\\,Hz$ and so for total output it is $100\\,Hz$.<br>Correct option is (2).',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2023,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(46869,'physics',42,323,592,'When a $p-n$ junction diode is in forward bias, which type of charge carriers flows in the connecting wire?','[{"text": "Protons", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Holes", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Free electrons", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Ions", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'In forward bias of $p-n$ junction diode electrons are attracted towards positive terminal of battery, electrons get captured into the hole and leaves the hole.<br>Correct option is (3).',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2023,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(46870,'physics',42,324,594,'The energy gap of an $L E D$ is $2.4\\,eV$. When the $L E D$ is switched \' $O N$ \', the momentum of the emitted photons is','[{"text": "$1.28 \\\\times {10^{ - 11}}\\\\,kg \\\\cdot m \\\\cdot {s^{ - 1}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$0.64 \\\\times {10^{ - 27}}\\\\,kg \\\\cdot m \\\\cdot {s^{ - 1}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1.28 \\\\times {10^{ - 27}}\\\\,kg.m \\\\cdot {s^{ - 1}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$2.56 \\\\times {10^{ - 27}}\\\\,kg \\\\cdot m \\\\cdot {s^{ - 1}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Momentum $p=\\dfrac{h}{\\lambda}$<br>$\\Rightarrow E_{g}=\\dfrac{h c}{\\lambda}=c p \\Rightarrow p=\\dfrac{E_{g}}{c}=\\dfrac{2.4 \\times 1.6 \\times 10^{-19}}{3 \\times 10^{8}}$<br>Hence, $p = 1.28 \\times {10^{ - 27}}\\,kg - m{s^{ - 1}}$.<br>Correct option is (3).',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2023,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(46871,'physics',42,323,592,'Which of the following circuits is reverse -biased?','[{"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'In reverse biasing, the $p$ and $n$ part of diode are connected to lower and higher potentials respectively.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2024,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(46876,'physics',42,324,594,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Photodiode and photovoltaic cell are based on the same principle.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Both use same method of operations to work.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Photodiodes and photovoltaic cells are based on semiconductor principles, but the Reason is not a correct explanation as they operate differently: a photodiode converts light into current, while a photovoltaic cell converts light into voltage. So correct option is (2).',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(46877,'physics',42,322,590,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> At a fixed temperature, silicon will have a minimum conductivity when it has a smaller acceptor doping.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> The conductivity of an intrinsic semiconductor is slightly higher than that of a lightly doped $p$-type.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'There is possibility of collisions of carriers with impurities. The conductivity of an intrinsic semiconductor is lower than that of a lightly doped $p$-type semiconductor. So correct option is (3).',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(46878,'physics',42,329,604,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> A pure semiconductor has negative temperature coefficient of resistance.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> On raising the temperature, more charge carriers are released, conductance increases and resistance decreases.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'The negative temperature coefficient of resistance in pure semiconductors is due to that the increase in temperature releases more charge carriers, leading to increased conductance and decreased resistance. So correct option is (1).',NULL,'Easy','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(46921,'physics',42,325,595,'In a half wave rectifier output is taken across a $90\\,ohm$ load resistor. If the resistance of diode in forward biased condition is $10\\,ohm$, the efficiency of rectification of $a c$ power into $d c$ power is','[{"text": "$40.6 \\\\%$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$81.2 \\\\%$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$73.08 \\\\%$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$36.54 \\\\%$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'$\\eta=\\dfrac{0.406 R_{L}}{r_{f}+R_{L}}$<br>$=\\dfrac{0.406 \\times 90}{10+90}=\\dfrac{0.406 \\times 90}{100}=0.3654$<br>$\\Rightarrow \\%=36.54$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(46881,'physics',42,324,594,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> A photocell is called an electric eye.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> When light is incident on some semiconductor its electrical resistance is reduced.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Photo-conductive cells operate on the principle that when light strikes certain semiconductors, their electrical resistance decreases. These cells find applications in solar batteries. Photo cell can distinguish between weak light intense light.<br>$\\Rightarrow$ It gives a measure of intensity of light interms of photo current. Hence it is called electric eye. So correct option is (2).',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(46884,'physics',42,323,592,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> During reverse biasing a diode doesn\'t conduct current.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> It narrows the depletion layer.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'When a diode is subjected to reverse bias, the depletion layer increses. So, there is an increase in resistance, Hence, So correct option is (3).',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(46885,'physics',42,324,594,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Light Emitting Diode (LED) emit spontaneous radiation.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> LED are forward-biased $pn$ junction.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Energy bands in intrinsic or doped semiconductor can emit photon. In Light Emitting Diodes (LEDs), the emitted light\'s color is determined by the semiconductor material\'s band gap $\\lambda=\\left(\\dfrac{h c}{E_{g}}\\right)$. The emitted light is spontaneous in all directions So correct option is (2).',NULL,'Easy','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(46886,'physics',42,325,595,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> The half-wave rectifier work only for positive half cycle of ac<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> In half-wave rectifier only one diode is used.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'In the context of a half-wave rectifier, a single diode becomes biased only during the positive half of the alternating current (AC) cycle. During the negative half of the AC cycle, the diode becomes reverse-biased, resulting in no corresponding output. Hence, the designationas a "half-wave rectifier. So correct option is (1).',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(46925,'physics',42,325,595,'In a half wave rectifier circuit operating from $50\\,Hz$ mains frequency, the fundamental frequency in the ripple would be:','[{"text": "$25\\\\,Hz$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$50\\\\,Hz$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$70.7\\\\,Hz$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$100\\\\,Hz$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'As the output voltage obtained in a half wave rectifier circuit has a single variation in one cycle of $a c$ voltage, hence the fundamental frequency in the ripple of output voltage would be $ = 50\\,Hz$.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(46889,'physics',42,323,591,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> The majority current carriers diffuse from a region of higher concentration to a region of lower concentration.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> The direction of diffusion current in a junction diode is from $n$-region to $p$-region.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'The direction of diffusion current is positively charged particles move from the $p$-type region to the $n$-type region of a diode. So correct option is (3).',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(46890,'physics',42,321,588,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Diamond behaves like an insulator.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> There is a large energy gap between valence band and conduction band of diamond.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'For diamond, ${E_g} = 5.8\\,eV$<br>For $Si\\,{E_g} = 1.1\\,eV$<br>In insulators, the forbidden energy gap is significantly large. When an electric field is applied to such a solid, electrons find it challenging to acquire the substantial amount of energy required to bridge this gap.<br>$\\Rightarrow$ no electron flow occurs. So correct option is (1).',NULL,'Easy','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(46891,'physics',42,323,592,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> The current through the $pn$ junction is not same in forward and reversed bias.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> We can measure the potential barrier of a $pn$ junction by putting a sensitive voltmeter across its terminal.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'As there are no free charge carriers, the potential barrier of a $pn$ junction cannot be directly measured by connecting a sensitive voltmeter across its terminals. Moreover, without the application of a forward bias, the $pn$ junction effectively presents infinite resistance. So correct option is (3).',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(46892,'physics',42,323,592,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> The resistance of $pn$ junction is low when forward biased and is high when reverse baised.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> In reversed biased. the depletion layer is reduced.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Forward biasing a p-n junction reduces resistance due to a substantial increase in forward current and decreased depletion layer width. Reverse biasing increases resistance and the depletion layer widens. So correct option is $(3)$.',NULL,'Easy','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(46893,'physics',42,329,604,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> $pn$ junction diode can be used even ultra high frequencies.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Capacitive reactance of a $pn$ junction diode increases as frequency increases.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'The capacitive reactance, denoted by $X_{c}$, is inversely proportional to the angular frequency ù and the capacitance $C$. It can be expressed as $\\mathrm{X}_{c}=\\dfrac{1}{2 \\pi f C}$, where \' $f$ \' is the frequency. Therefore, as the frequency increases, the capacitive reactance decreases. So correct option is (3).',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(46922,'physics',42,323,592,'For the given circuit of $pn$ junction diode, which is correct?','[{"text": "In forward bias the voltage across $R$ is $V$.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "In reverse bias the voltage across $R$ is $V$.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "In forward bias the voltage across $R$ is $2 \\\\mathrm{~V}$.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "In reverse bias the voltage across $R$ is $2 V$.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'In forward bias the resistance of diode is neglible and hence applied voltage will be across resistance. In reverse bias current does not pass through the circuit. So potential difference across resistor is zero. Correct option is (1)',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/42_46922_question_1.png'),
(46894,'physics',42,321,588,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> The probability of electrons to be found in the conduction band of an intrinsic semiconductor at a finite temperature decrease exponentially with increasing band gap.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> It will be more difficult for the electron to cross over the large band gap while going from valence band to conduction band.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'We know $\\sigma=\\sigma_{0} e^{-E g / 2 K_{B} T}$ In order for an electron to make transition from the valence band to the conduction band, it requires energy that is equal to or greater than the energy gap (forbidden band) between these two bands. As the energy gap increases, it becomes difficult for an electron to acquire the necessary energy to make this jump. So correct option is (1).',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(46895,'physics',42,322,590,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> When a semiconductor is doped with a donor impurity, the hole concentration decreases and the electron concentration increases.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> A donor impurity is an atom with valence electrons of five always.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Doping with a donor impurity (like $\\mathrm{P}, \\mathrm{As}$ ) in a semiconductor creates n-type semiconductors, where the extra free electron increases electron concentration. So correct option is (2).',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(46896,'physics',42,323,592,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> In a semiconductor diode, the reverse biased current is due to drift of free electrons and holes.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> The drift of electrons and holes is due to thermal excitations.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'<img src=\'/img/data/physics/42_46896_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />   Applying reverse bias to a p-n junction restricts diffusion current but allows microampere-level drift current due to thermal excitations of electrons and holes. <img src=\'/img/data/physics/42_46896_explanation_2.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />  So correct option is (2).',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(46897,'physics',42,329,604,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> At absolute zero the conductivity of semiconductor is zero.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> In a semiconductor there are no free electrons at any temperature.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'At $T=0 K$, the covalent bonds between atoms in a semiconductor completely hold electrons. So , at $0\\,K$, a semiconductor has no free electrons, and it acts as if insulator. As as temperature increases, more electrons gain energy to enter the conduction band. The variation of electrical conductivity $\\sigma$ with temperature is given below. $\\sigma=\\sigma_{0} e^{-E g / 2 K_{B} T}$ (Note that $T$ is in denominator in exponential) <img src=\'/img/data/physics/42_46897_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' /> It may also be noted for used conductor (metalic),<br>where $\\sigma=\\dfrac{\\sigma e^{2} \\tau}{m}$ <img src=\'/img/data/physics/42_46897_explanation_2.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' /> Collision time $\\tau$ decreases as temperature increases. So correct option is (3).',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(46898,'physics',42,323,591,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> The depletion layer in the $pn$ junction is free from mobile charge carriers.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> There is no electric field across the junction barrier.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'As a result of the diffusion of holes from the $p$-region to the $n$-region and the diffusion of electrons from the $n$-region to the $p$-region, an electric field is established across the junction barrier. $V_{b}=($ Potential difference across barrier) <img src=\'/img/data/physics/42_46898_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' /> So correct option is (3).',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(46899,'physics',42,321,587,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Crystalline solids are anisotropic and amorphous solids are isotropic.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> The amorphous solids do not have a regularity in their external shape.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Single crystals (such as Nacl) are anisotropic with respect to mechanical properties. Tourmaline like crystals show anisotropy while exhibiting polarization with light. Reason is a separate fact. So correct option is (2).',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(46923,'physics',42,323,592,'A two Volts battery forward biases a diode however there is a drop of $0.5\\,V$ across the diode which is independent of current. Also a current greater then $10\\,mA$ produces large joule loss and damages diode. If diode is to be operated at $5\\,mA$, the series resistance to be put is','[{"text": "$3\\\\,k\\\\Omega $", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$300\\\\,k\\\\Omega $", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$300\\\\,\\\\Omega $", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$200\\\\,\\\\Omega $", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'When $P-N$ junction is operated at $5\\,mA$.<br>$\\begin{aligned}& (2-0.5)=5 \\times 10^{-3} \\times R \\\\& R=300 \\Omega\\end{aligned}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/42_46923_question_1.png'),
(46901,'physics',42,323,592,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> The value of current through $p{\\rm{ - }}n$ junction (ideal diode) in the given figure will be $10\\,mA$. https://res.cloudinary.com/doe6oiv2h/image/upload/v1764956599/preptracker/questions/q_46901_col1_1764956599128.png<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> In the above figure, $p$-side is at higher  potential than $n$-side than $n$-side so, $pn$ junction is forward biased.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'The p-side of the $pn$ junction is maintained at a higher potential ( +5 V) compared to the nside, $(+2 V)$<br>$\\Rightarrow$ It is a forward-biased p-n junction. diode has zero resistance (as diode is ideal).<br>$I=\\dfrac{(5-2)}{300}=10^{-2} \\mathrm{~A}=10 \\mathrm{~mA}$<br>So correct option is (1).',NULL,'Easy','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(46902,'physics',42,324,594,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> A $pn$ junction with reverse bias can be used as a photo-diode to measure light intensity.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> In a reverse bias condition the current is small but it is more sensitive to change in incident light intensity.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Due to reverse bias, width of deplection layer increases in a photodiode. This generateselectron-hole pairs a sort of photo electric effect when exposed to light with energy greater than the bandgap. The resulting change in reverse saturation current enables the measurement of light intensity. So correct option is (1).',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(46903,'physics',42,329,604,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Diode lasers are used as optical sources in optical communication.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Diode lasers consume less energy.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Laser diodes, semiconductor-based with a thin doped layer, are efficient light sources for optical communication, capable of high-power radiation and energy-efficient operation, when coupled into optical fibers. So correct option is (2).',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(46906,'physics',42,323,592,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> In the following circuit the potential drop across the resistance is zero. https://res.cloudinary.com/doe6oiv2h/image/upload/v1764956600/preptracker/questions/q_46906_col1_1764956599911.png<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> The  resistance is of  low value.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'$p$\' side of diode is connected to more negative $(-5 V)$ than $n$ side of $(-2 V)$. Diode is reverse biased. Both assertion and reason are separately true. However, potential difference across the resistance is zero, because diode is in reverse biasing hence no current flows. So correct option is (2).',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(46909,'physics',42,324,594,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> A $pn$ photodiode is made from a semiconductor for which ${E_g} = 2.8\\,eV$. This photo diode will not detect the wavelength of $600\\,nm$.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> A $P N$ photodiode detects wavelength $\\lambda$ if $\\left(\\dfrac{h c}{\\lambda}\\right)$ is less than $E_{g}$','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'It can detect if $\\left(\\dfrac{h c}{\\lambda}\\right)>E_{g}$. By data, photon incident is of energy $\\left( {\\frac{{1200}}{{600}}} \\right) = 2\\,eV$ (Since we know $h c=1200 \\mathrm{eV}-n m$) As this is less than $0.8\\,eV\\left( { = {E_g}} \\right)$, the photodiode cannot detect the light.The given energy gap (bandgap) of $2.8\\,eV$ corresponds to a shorter wavelength than $6000{\\rm{ }}nm$, indicating that it won\'t detect $600{\\rm{ }}nm$. So correct option is (3).',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(46911,'physics',42,329,604,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> In vacuum tubes (valves), vacuum is necessary for the movement of electrons between electrodes otherwise electrons collide with air particle and loses their energy.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> In semiconductors devices, external heating or vacuum is not required.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Old age vaccum tubes use thermionic emission of electrons. In vacuum tubes, the presence of a vacuum is essential for their operation. In contrast, semiconductor devices operate independently of heating or vacuum conditions. Semi conductors when doped by suitable element have two types of carriers viz. electrons and holes. So correct option is (2).',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(46924,'physics',42,323,592,'Current in the circuit will be:','[{"text": "$\\\\dfrac{5}{40}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{5}{50}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{5}{10}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{5}{20}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'<img src=\'/img/data/physics/42_46924_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />  $I=\\dfrac{5}{50}$.',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/42_46924_question_1.png'),
(46913,'physics',42,323,593,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Zener diode works on a principle of breakdown voltage.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Current increases suddenly after breakdown voltage.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'When the reverse voltage applied to the Zener diode $\\left(V_{z}\\right)$ equals or exceeds the breakdown voltage, a significant and rapid increase in reverse current $\\left(I_{z}\\right)$ occurs. So correct option is (1).',NULL,'Easy','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(46915,'physics',42,321,588,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Silicon is preferred to germanium for making semiconductor devices.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> The energy gap for germanium is more than the energy gap of silicon.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'The energy gap $\\left(E_{g}\\right)$ for germanium is smaller at $0.72\\,eV$, while silicon has a larger energy gap of $1.1\\,eV$. This distinction makes silicon the preferred choice over germanium for manufacturing semiconductor devices. So correct option is (3).',NULL,'Easy','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(46916,'physics',42,323,592,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> The dominant mechanism for motion of charge cariers in forward and reverse biased silicon $pn$ junction is drift in both forward and reverse bias.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> In reverse biasing, no current flows through the junction.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but reason is correct.", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'During reverse bias in a $pn$ junction, majority carriers are impeded, allowing minority carriers to flow, resulting in a microampererange $(m A)$ current. So correct option is (4).',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(46917,'physics',42,321,587,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> The crystalline solids have a sharp melting point.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> All the bonds between the atoms or molecules of a crystalline solids are equally strong, that they get broken at the same temperature.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'At a specific temperature, all the bonds in crystalline solids break simultaneously, resulting in the characteristic sharp melting point of these materials. So, melting takes place sharply. So correct option is (1).',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(46918,'physics',42,322,590,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> An $N$-type semiconductor has a large number of electrons but still it is electrically neutral.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> An $N$-type semiconductor is obtained by doping an intrinsic semiconductor with a trivalent impurity.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'An $N$-Type semiconductor is created by introducing a pentavalent impurity (like P, As) into an intrinsic semiconductor. Such $N$-type material is neutral as there is equilibrium of total electrons with total number of protons. However, if biased, the excess electrons donated will be available for conduction. ($Si$ or $Ge$) So correct option is (3).',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(46919,'physics',42,322,590,'A silicon specimen is made into a $p$-type semiconductor by doping on an average one indium atom per $5 \\times 10^{7}$ silicon atoms. If the number density of atoms in the silicon specimen is $5 \\times {10^{28}}{\\rm{atom/}}c{m^3}$, then the number of accepter atoms in silicon per cubic centimeter will be:','[{"text": "$2.5 \\\\times {10^{30}}{\\\\rm{atom/}}c{m^3}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2.5 \\\\times {10^{35}}{\\\\rm{atom/}}c{m^3}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1.0 \\\\times {10^{13}}{\\\\rm{atom/}}c{m^3}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1.0 \\\\times {10^{15}}{\\\\rm{atom/}}c{m^3}$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Nunber density of atoms in silicon specimen $ = 5 \\times {10^{28}}atom{\\rm{/}}{m^3}$ $ = 5 \\times {10^{22}}atom{\\rm{/}}{cm^3}$<br>Since 1 atom of indium is doped in $5 \\times 10^{7}$ silicon atoms, so total number of indium atom doped per $c{m^3}$ of silicon will be<br>$n = \\frac{{5 \\times {{10}^{22}}}}{{5 \\times {{10}^7}}} = 1.0 \\times {10^{15}}{\\rm{ }}atom{\\rm{/}}c{m^3}.$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(46920,'physics',42,323,592,'If $V_{A}$ and $V_{B}$ denote the potentials of $A$ and $B$, then the equivalent resistance between $A$ and $B$ in the adjoint electric circuit is','[{"text": "$10\\\\,ohm$ if $V_{A}>V_{B}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$5\\\\,ohm$ if $V_{A} < V_{B}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$5\\\\,ohm$ if $V_{A}>V_{B}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$20\\\\,ohm$ if $V_{A}>V_{B}$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'If $V_{A}>V_{B}$, the diode gets forward biased and has nearly zero resistance.<br>$\\Rightarrow$ Given resistors will be in parallel.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/42_46920_question_1.png'),
(46928,'physics',42,323,592,'In the circuit shown if drift current for the diode is $20\\,\\mu A$, the potential difference across the diode is:','[{"text": "$2\\\\,V$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$4.5\\\\,V$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$4\\\\,V$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$2.5\\\\,V$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Diode is reverse biased, so only drift current due to minority carriers which is $20\\,\\mu A$ will flow. Potential drop across Resistor<br>$V = 15\\Omega  \\times 20\\,\\mu A$<br>$ = 300\\,\\mu V = 0.0003\\,V$<br>Potential difference across the diode<br>$ = 4 - 0.0003 = 3.99 \\approx 4\\,V.$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/42_46928_question_1.png'),
(46929,'physics',42,325,596,'In a full wave junction diode rectifier the input $a c$ has $r m s$ value of $20\\,V$. The transformer used is a step up transformer having primary and secondary turn ratio $1: 2$. The $d c$ voltage in the rectified output is:','[{"text": "$12\\\\,V$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$24\\\\,V$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$36\\\\,V$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$42\\\\,V$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Input ${V_{rms}} = 20\\,V$<br>Peak value of input voltage $V_{0}=\\sqrt{2} V_{r m s}$<br>$ = \\sqrt 2  \\times 20 \\Rightarrow 28.28\\,V$<br>Since the transformer is a set up transformer having transformer ratio $1: 2$ the maximum value of output voltage of the transformer applied the diode will be<br>${V_0} = 2 \\times 28.28\\,V$<br>$\\frac{{2{V_0}}}{\\pi } = \\frac{{2 \\times 2 \\times 28.28}}{{\\frac{{22}}{7}}} = 36\\,V$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(46930,'physics',42,322,590,'In $n$-type semiconductor when all donor states<br>are filled, then the net charge density in the donor states becomes:','[{"text": "1", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$>1$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$ < 1$, but not zero", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Zero", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'If all the donor states in $n$-type semiconductor are filled, the number of electrons in donor states will increase. Due to it the charge density in donor states will become more than one.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(46931,'physics',42,323,592,'A diode connected to an external resistance and an e.m.f. Assuming that the barrier potential developed in diode is $0.5\\,V$. What will be the value of current in the circuit in milliampere?','[{"text": "$40\\\\,mA$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$4\\\\,mA$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$0.4\\\\,mA$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$400\\\\,mA$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Effective voltage in the circuit<br>$V = 4.5 - 0.5 = 4\\,V$<br>Current in the circuit<br>$I = \\frac{V}{R} = \\frac{4}{{100}} = 0.04\\,A$<br>$I = 0.04A = 40\\,mA.$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/42_46931_question_1.png'),
(46932,'physics',42,322,590,'A pure $S i$ crystal has $5 \\times 10^{22}$ atoms ${m^{ - 3}}$. It is doped by 1 ppm concentration of pentavalent atoms. The number of holes is: $\\left( {{n_i} = 1.5 \\times {{10}^{16}}\\,{m^{ - 3}}} \\right)$','[{"text": "$2.5 \\\\times {10^6}\\\\,{m^{ - 3}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$4.5 \\\\times {10^9}\\\\,{m^{ - 3}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$6 \\\\times {10^9}\\\\,{m^{ - 3}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$4 \\\\times {10^6}\\\\,{m^{ - 3}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Electron and hole concentration in a semiconductor in thermal equilibrium is<br>$n_{e} n_{h}=n_{i}^{2}$<br>or ${n_h} = \\frac{{n_i^2}}{{{n_e}}} \\Rightarrow \\frac{{{{\\left( {1.5 \\times {{10}^{16}}} \\right)}^2}}}{{5 \\times {{10}^{22}}}} = 4.5 \\times {10^9}\\,{m^{ - 3}}.$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(46933,'physics',42,322,589,'An intrinsic semiconductor has a resistivity of $0.50\\,\\Omega m$ at room temperature. Find the intrinsic carrier concentration if the mobilities of electrons and holes are $0.39\\,{m^2}{V^{ - 1}}{S^{ - 1}}$ and $0.11\\,{m^2}{V^{ - 1}}{S^{ - 1}}$ respectively.','[{"text": "$1.2 \\\\times {10^{18}}\\\\,{m^{ - 3}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2.5 \\\\times {10^{19}}\\\\,{m^{ - 3}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$1.9 \\\\times {10^{20}}\\\\,{m^{ - 3}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$30 \\\\times {10^{19}}\\\\,{m^{ - 3}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'$\\rho  = 0.50\\,\\Omega m$<br>${\\mu _e} = 0.39\\,{m^2}{V^{ - 1}}\\,{s^{ - 1}}$<br>${\\mu _h} = 0.11\\,{m^2}{V^{ - 1}}\\,{s^{ - 1}}$<br>Resistivity of intrinsic semiconductor is<br>$\\dfrac{1}{\\rho}=e\\left(\\mu_{e} n_{i}+n_{i} \\mu_{h}\\right)$<br>$\\Rightarrow n_{i}=\\dfrac{1}{\\rho e\\left(\\mu_{e}+\\mu_{h}\\right)}$<br>$n_{i}=\\dfrac{1}{(0.5)\\left(1.6 \\times 10^{-19}\\right)(0.39+0.11)}$<br>$ = 2.5 \\times {10^{19}}\\,{m^{ - 3}}.$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(46934,'physics',42,322,589,'In an intrinsic semiconductor, the number of conduction electron is $6 \\times 10^{19}$ per cubic meter. How many holes are there in a sample of size $1\\,cm \\times 1\\,cm \\times 2\\,mm$?','[{"text": "$5 \\\\times 10^{2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$12 \\\\times 10^{-12}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$12 \\\\times 10^{12}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$4 \\\\times 10^{8}$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Number of density of electron,<br>${n_e} = 6 \\times {10^{19}}\\,{m^{ - 3}}$.<br>Volume of sample, $V = 1\\,cm \\times 1\\,cm \\times 2\\,mm$<br>$ = {10^{ - 2}}\\,m \\times {10^{ - 2}}\\,m \\times 2 \\times {10^{ - 3}}\\,m$<br>$ = 2 \\times {10^{ - 7}}\\,{m^3}$<br>$\\therefore$ No. of electrons in the given sample $=n_{e} \\times v$.<br>$\\begin{aligned}& =6 \\times 10^{19} \\times 2 \\times 10^{-7} \\\\& =12 \\times 10^{12} .\\end{aligned}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(46935,'physics',42,324,594,'In a $P$-type semiconductor, the acceptor level is $57\\,MeV$, above the valence $\\operatorname{ban}(d)$. The maximum wave length of light required to produce a hole will be','[{"text": "$57 \\\\mathop A^{~~\\\\circ}   $", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$57 \\\\times 10^{-3}  \\\\mathop A^{~~\\\\circ}  $", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2171000  \\\\mathop A^{~~\\\\circ}  $", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$11.61 \\\\times 10  \\\\mathop A^{~~\\\\circ}  $", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'$E=(h c / \\lambda)$<br>$\\Rightarrow \\lambda=\\dfrac{h c}{E} \\approx \\dfrac{12400}{57 \\times 10^{6}}$<br>$ = 217.1 \\times {10^{ - 6}}\\,m$<br>$ = 217.1 \\times {10^4} = 2171000\\mathop {{\\rm{ }}A}\\limits^{\\,\\,^\\circ } .$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(46936,'physics',42,321,587,'Mobility of electrons in $N$-type $G e$ is $5000\\,\\,c{m^2}{\\rm{/}}volt\\,\\sec $ and conductivity $5\\,mho{\\rm{/}}cm$. If effect of holes is negligible then impurity concentration will be','[{"text": "$6,25 \\\\times {10^{15}}{\\\\rm{/}}c{m^3}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$9.25 \\\\times {10^{14}}{\\\\rm{/}}c{m^3}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$6 \\\\times {10^{13}}{\\\\rm{/}}c{m^3}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$9 \\\\times {10^{13}}{\\\\rm{/}}c{m^3}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'$\\sigma=e n_{e} \\mu_{e} \\Rightarrow n_{e}=\\dfrac{\\sigma}{e \\mu_{e}}$<br>$ = \\frac{5}{{1.6 \\times {{10}^{ - 19}} \\times 5000}} = 6.25 \\times {10^{15}}{\\rm{/}}c{m^3}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(46997,'physics',42,323,592,'Two ideal diodes connected as shown in the figure. The current flowing through the ${R_{2}}$ resistance will be','[{"text": "$0.5\\\\,A$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$3\\\\,A$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2\\\\,A$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1.25\\\\,A$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'${D_{1}}$ is ${R B}$ and ${D_{2}}$ is ${F B}$. So, circuit will look much shown in figure below <img src=\'/img/data/physics/42_46997_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' /> We have, ${i=\\dfrac{V}{R_{\\text {eq }}}=\\dfrac{5}{4}=1.25 {~A}}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/42_46997_question_1.png'),
(46937,'physics',42,322,590,'The number densities of electrons and holes in pure silicon at $27^\\circ C$ are equal and its value is $1.5 \\times {10^{16}}\\,{m^{ - 3}}$. On doping with indium, the hole density increases to $4.5 \\times {10^{27}}\\,{m^{ - 3}}$. The electron density in doped silicon will be:','[{"text": "$50 \\\\times {10^9}\\\\,{m^{ - 3}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$5 \\\\times {10^4}\\\\,{m^{ - 3}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${10^8}\\\\,{m^{ - 3}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${10^7}\\\\,{m^{ - 3}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'$n_{i}^{2}=n_{e} n_{h}$<br>$\\therefore {n_e} = \\frac{{n_i^2}}{{{n_h}}} = \\frac{{2.25 \\times {{10}^{32}}}}{{4.5 \\times {{10}^{27}}}} = 5 \\times {10^4}\\,{m^{ - 3}}.$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(46938,'physics',42,325,595,'A $pn$ junction diode as shown in the figure can act as a rectifier, an alternating current source $V$ is connected in the circuit. The current $(I)$ in the resistor $(R)$ can be shown by:<div class="matching-question"></div>','[{"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'The $pn$ junction acts as a half-wave rectifier.',NULL,'Medium','Graph/Figure',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/42_46938_question_1.png'),
(46939,'physics',42,321,587,'If the ratio of concentration of electrons to that of holes in a semiconductor is (4/7) and the ratio of currents is (5/7), then the ratio of their drift velocities is:','[{"text": "$3 / 5$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$4 / 5$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$5 / 4$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$4 / 3$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'$\\frac{{{I_h}}}{{{I_e}}} = \\frac{{{n_h}eA{v_h}}}{{{n_e}eA{v_e}}} = \\frac{4}{7} \\times \\frac{{{v_h}}}{{{v_e}}}$<br>$ \\Rightarrow \\frac{{{v_h}}}{{{v_e}}} = \\frac{7}{4} \\times \\frac{5}{7} = \\frac{5}{4}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(46940,'physics',42,321,587,'What will be conductivity of pure silicon crystal at $300\\,K$ temperature. If electron hole pairs per $c{m^3}$ is $1.072 \\times 10^{10}$ at this temperature, ${\\mu _n} = 1350\\,c{m^2}{\\rm{/}}volt\\,{\\rm{ - }}\\,s$ and ${\\mu _p} = 480\\,c{m^2}{\\rm{/}}volt\\,{\\rm{ - }}\\,s$','[{"text": "$3.14 \\\\times {10^{ - 6}}\\\\,mho{\\\\rm{/}}cm$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$3 \\\\times {10^{ - 6}}\\\\,mho{\\\\rm{/}}cm$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${10^{ - 6}}\\\\,mho{\\\\rm{/}}cm$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${10^6}\\\\,mho{\\\\rm{/}}cm$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'$\\sigma=e n\\left(\\mu_{e}+\\mu_{n}\\right)$<br>$=1.6 \\times 10^{-19} \\times 1.072 \\times 10^{10} \\times(1350+480)$<br>$ = 3.14 \\times {10^{ - 6}}\\Omega {\\rm{/}}cm.$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(46941,'physics',42,321,587,'In sample of pure silicon ${10^{13}}atom{\\rm{/}}c{m^3}$ is mixed of phosphorus. If all donar atoms are active then what will be resistivity at $20^\\circ C$ if mobility of electron is $1200\\,\\,c{m^2}{\\rm{/}}volts\\,{\\rm{ - }}\\,s$','[{"text": "$0.5209\\\\,ohm\\\\,cm$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$5.209\\\\,ohm\\\\,cm$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$52.09\\\\,ohm\\\\,cm$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$520.9\\\\,ohm\\\\,cm$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'$\\dfrac{1}{\\rho}=e n_{e} \\mu_{e}$<br>$\\rho  = \\frac{1}{{1.6 \\times {{10}^{ - 19}} \\times {{10}^{ - 13}} \\times 1200}}$<br>$ = 520.9\\,\\Omega \\,cm$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(46942,'physics',42,321,588,'A $pn$ photodiode is fabricated from a semiconductor with a band gap of $2.5\\,eV$. The signal wavelength is:','[{"text": "$6000$ $ \\\\mathop A^{~~\\\\circ}  $", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$6000\\\\,nm$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$4000\\\\,nm$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$5000$ $ \\\\mathop A^{~~\\\\circ}  $", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'$E_{g}=\\dfrac{h c}{\\lambda} \\Rightarrow \\lambda=\\dfrac{6.6 \\times 10^{-34} \\times 3 \\times 10^{8}}{2.5 \\times 1.6 \\times 10^{-19}}$<br>$ \\Rightarrow \\lambda  = 4.95 \\times {10^{ - 7}}\\,m \\approx 5000\\mathop {{\\rm{ }}A}\\limits^{\\,\\,^\\circ } .$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(46945,'physics',42,322,590,'A pure semiconductor has equal electron and hole concentration of ${10^{16}}\\,{m^{ - 3}}$. Doping by indium increases $n_{h}$ to $5 \\times {10^{22}}\\,{m^{ - 3}}$. Then, the value $n_{c}$ in the doped semiconductor is','[{"text": "${10^6}{\\\\rm{/}}{m^3}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${10^{22}}{\\\\rm{/}}{m^3}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2 \\\\times {10^6}{\\\\rm{/}}{m^3}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2 \\\\times {10^9}{\\\\rm{/}}{m^3}$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Here, ${n_i} = {10^{16}}\\,{m^{ - 3}},{n_h} = 5 \\times {10^{22}}\\,{m^{ - 3}}$<br>we know that $n_{e} n_{h}=n_{i}^{2}$<br>$ \\Rightarrow {n_e} = \\frac{{n_i^2}}{{{n_h}}} = \\frac{{{{\\left( {{{10}^{16}}\\,{m^{ - 3}}} \\right)}^2}}}{{5 \\times {{10}^{22}}\\,{m^{ - 3}}}} = 2 \\times {10^9}\\,{m^{ - 3}}$<br>So, correct option is (4)',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2021,'MHTCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(46954,'physics',42,324,594,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Photodiode and solar cell works on same mechanism<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Area is large for solar cell.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'A $pn$ junction photodiode can be operated under photovoltaic conditions similar to that of a solar cell. The current - voltage characteristics of a photodiode and solar cell under illumination are also similar.<br>When light energy falls on the solar cell, then it converts solar energy into electrical energy.<br>But the area of solar cell is larger, so that it can absorb more amount of sunlight and hence more amount of electrical energy is obtained.<br>Hence, Assertion and Reason both are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',2019,'AIIMS','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(46955,'physics',42,323,593,'If voltage across a zener diode is $6\\,V$, then find out the value of maximum resistance in this condition.','[{"text": "$2\\\\,k\\\\Omega $", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$3\\\\,k\\\\Omega $", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$5\\\\,k\\\\Omega $", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$4\\\\,k\\\\Omega $", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Given, voltage across zener diode, ${V_Z} = 6\\,V$<br>Since, zener diode provide stabilised supply of $6\\,V$.   <img src=\'/img/data/physics/42_46955_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />  In this case, the value of $R$ should be chosen such that negligible current flows through the zener diode. $\\therefore I=I_{Z}+I_{L}=0+I_{L}$<br>Here, $I=\\dfrac{E}{R+R_{L}}$<br>$\\Rightarrow I=\\dfrac{30}{R+10^{3}}$<br>But, ${V_Z}{R_L} = 6 \\times {10^{ - 3}}\\,A$<br>Note that ${V_z}$ gets across RL. It is also in data ${I_L} = 6\\,m$<br>$\\Rightarrow 6 \\times 10^{-3} A=\\dfrac{30}{R+10^{3}}$<br>$ \\Rightarrow R = 4 \\times {10^{3\\,}}\\Omega  = 4\\,k\\Omega $',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',2019,'AIIMS','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/42_46955_question_1.png'),
(46956,'physics',42,323,591,'When two semiconductors of $p$-type and $n$-type are brought into contact, they form a $pn$ junction which acts like a','[{"text": "Conductor", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Oscillator", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Amplifer", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Rectifier", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'A $pn$ junction acts as a rectifier.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2013,'AIIMS','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(46957,'physics',42,323,592,'The diode used at a constant potential drop of $0.5\\,V$ at all currents and maximum power rating of $100\\,mW$. What resistance must be connected in series to diode to so that current in circuit is maximum?','[{"text": "$200\\\\,\\\\Omega $", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$6.67\\\\,\\\\Omega $", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$5\\\\,\\\\Omega $", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$15\\\\,\\\\Omega $", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Current passing in the circuit,<br>$I = \\frac{P}{V} = \\frac{{100 \\times {{10}^{ - 3}}}}{{0.5}} = 0.2\\,A$<br>Value of connected series resistance,<br>$\\begin{aligned}& R=\\left(\\dfrac{1.5-0.5}{0.2}\\right)=\\dfrac{1}{0.2} \\\\& \\Rightarrow R=5 \\Omega\\end{aligned}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2018,'AIIMS','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/42_46957_question_1.png'),
(46958,'physics',42,323,591,'The curve between charge density \\((\\rho)\\) and distances near \\(pn\\) junction, will be<div class="matching-question"></div>','[{"text": "", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'The $n$-side of $pn$ junction has stationary positive ions and $p$-side has stationary negative ions. Hence, the correct graph between charge density and distance near $pn$ junction is shown in option (1).',NULL,'Medium','Graph/Figure',2009,'AIIMS','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(46959,'physics',42,324,594,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Light Emitting Diode ( $L E D)$ emits spontaneous radiation.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> $L E D$ are forward biased $pn$ junctions.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'When a junction diode is forward biased as shown in figure, energy is released at the junction due to recombination of electrons and holes. In the junction diode made of Gallium arsenide(GaAs) or Indium phosphide(InP), the energy is released in visible region. Such a junction diode is called light emitting diode ($L E D)$. It is heavily doped $pn$ junction diode. So, the radiations emitted are spontaneous in forward biasing.   <img src=\'/img/data/physics/42_46959_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',2008,'AIIMS','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(46960,'physics',42,323,592,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> The value of current through $pn$ junction in the given figure will be $10\\,mA$.   https://res.cloudinary.com/doe6oiv2h/image/upload/v1764956611/preptracker/questions/q_46960_col1_1764956610534.png<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> In the above figure, $p$-side is at higher potential than $n$-side.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reaction is the correct explanation of Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanaiton of Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'In the given figure, $p$ side of $pn$ junction is at higher potential than $n$-side, $ \\Rightarrow p{\\rm{ - }}n$ junction is forward biased. Taking its resistance to be zero (for ideal diode)and applying Ohm\'s law, we get<br>$I = \\frac{V}{R} = \\frac{{5 - 2}}{{300}} = {10^{ - 2}}\\,A = 10\\,mA$',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',2008,'AIIMS','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(46998,'physics',42,323,592,'Find out current through resistance of ${2 k \\Omega}$','[{"text": "$2.75\\\\,mA$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$3\\\\,mA$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$35\\\\,mA$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2\\\\,mA$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'${i=\\dfrac{[4-(-2)-0.5]}{2 \\times 10^{3}}=\\dfrac{6-0.5}{2} \\times 10^{-3}}$<br>$=\\dfrac{5.5}{2} \\times 10^{-3}=2.75 {~mA}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/42_46998_question_1.png'),
(46961,'physics',42,324,594,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> A $pn$ junction with reverse bias can be used as a photodiode to measure light intensity.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> In a reverse bias condition, the current is small but it is more sensitive to change in incident light intensity.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but reason is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'At the $pn$ junction with reverse bias, there exists a junction field which at equilibrium does not permit the flow of charge carriers across the junction. When such a $pn$ diode is illuminated with light photons having energy, $h v>E_{g}$ but different intensities $I_{1}, I_{2}, I_{3}$, etc, the electron and hole pairs generated in the depletion layer (or near the junction) are made to flow across the junction.<br>This produces current in the circuit depending on light intensity. Thus, a revers biased $pn$ junction is used as a photodiode to measure light intensity.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',2006,'AIIMS','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(46962,'physics',42,322,590,'A specimen of silicon is to be made $p$-type semiconductor, for this one atom of indium, on an average, is doped in $5 \\times 10^{7}$ silicon atoms. If the number density of silicon is $5 \\times {10^{28}}atom{\\rm{/}}{{\\rm{m}}^3}$, then the number of acceptor atoms per $c{m^3}$ will be','[{"text": "$2.5 \\\\times 10^{30}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1.0 \\\\times 10^{13}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1.0 \\\\times 10^{15}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$2.5 \\\\times 10^{36}$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Given, one Indium atom was doped in $5 \\times 10^{7}$ Silicon atoms.<br>Number density of Silicon<br>$ = 5 \\times {10^{28}}atom\\,{m^{ - 3}}$<br>$ \\approx 5 \\times {10^{22}}atom\\,c{m^{ - 3}}$<br>Number of acceptor atoms per $\\,c{m^{ - 3}}$ given by<br>${N_A} = \\frac{{5 \\times {{10}^{22}}}}{{5 \\times {{10}^7}}} = 1 \\times {10^{15}}\\,c{m^{ - 3}}$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',2017,'AIIMS','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(46963,'physics',42,321,588,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> The energy gap between the valence band and conduction band is greater in silicon than in germanium.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Thermal energy produces fewer minority carriers in silicon than in germanium.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'The energy gap between valence band and conduction band in Germanium is $0.76\\,eV$ and the energy gap between valence band and conduction band in Silicon is $1.1\\,eV$.<br>Also, it is true that thermal energy produces fewer minority carriers in Silicon than in Germanium.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',2007,'AIIMS','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(46964,'physics',42,321,587,'In a semiconducting material, the mobilities of electrons and holes are $\\mu_{e}$ and $\\mu_{h}$ respectively, Which of the following is true?','[{"text": "$\\\\mu_{e}>\\\\mu_{h}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\mu_{e} < \\\\mu_{h}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mu_{e}=\\\\mu_{h}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mu_{e} < 0, \\\\mu_{h}>0$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Hole is massive than electron, and so mobility of holes is less than that of electrons,<br>$\\Rightarrow \\mu_{h} < \\mu_{e}$.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2006,'AIIMS','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(46965,'physics',42,324,594,'Read the Assertion and Reason carefully to mark the correct options given below:<br><br><strong>Assertion:</strong> Photo diodes are preferably operated in reverse bias condition for light intensity measurement.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> The current in the forward bias is more than the current in the reverse bias for a $p-n$ junction diode.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but reason is correct.", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'When a $p-n$ junction diode is forward biased, the width of depletion region decreases. But, when the diode is reverse biased the width of depletion region increases. Due to this increased width the incident light gets more surface area and more number of electrons can be emitted. This will lead to more photo current. So, photodiodes work in reverse biased condition.<br>Also, we know that the current in forward biased $p-n$, junction is more than the current in reverse biased $p-n$, junction. Hence, both assertion and reason are true.<br>So option (4) is correct.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',2023,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(46971,'physics',42,324,594,'Read the Assertion and Reason carefully to mark the correct options given below:<br><br><strong>Assertion:</strong> Photo diodes are used in forward bias usually for measuring the light intensity.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> For a $p-n$ junction diode, at applied voltage $V$ the current in the forward bias is more than the current in the reverse bias for $\\left|V_{Z}\\right|> \\pm V \\geq\\left|V_{0}\\right|$ where $V_{0}$ and $V_{Z}$ are the threshold voltage and the breakdown voltage.','[{"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but reason is correct.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Assertion is false as photodiodes are used in reverse bias only so that the more photocurrent can be generated. Reason is true. So, correct option is (2).',NULL,'Hard','Assertion and Reason',2023,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(46972,'physics',42,323,591,'Read the Statement - A and Statement - B carefully to mark the correct options given below:<div class="matching-question"><div class="column-left">When a Si sample is doped with Boron, it becomes $P$-type and when doped by   Arsenic it becomes $N$-type semiconductor such that $P$-type has excess holes and $N$-type has excess electrons.</div><div class="column-right">When such $P$-type and $N$-type semiconductors, are fused to make a junction, a current will automatically flow which can be detected with an externally connected ammeter.</div></div>','[{"text": "Statement A is correct but Statement B is incorrect.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Statement A is incorrect but Statement B is correct.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both statements are correct.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Statements are incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Statement A is correct.<br>When $P N$ junction is formed, an electric field is generated from $N$ side to $P$ side due to which barrier potential arises and majority charge carriers from $P$ - type and $N$ - type cannot flow through the junction due to barrier potential so current is zero unless we apply forward bias voltage. So, correct option is (1).',NULL,'Medium','MCQ type-2',2023,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(46976,'physics',42,323,592,'Read the Assertion and Reason carefully to mark the correct options given below:<br><br><strong>Assertion:</strong> Diffusion current in a $p-n$ junction is greater than the drift current in magnitude if the junction is forward biased.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Diffusion current in a $p-n$ junction is from the $n$-side to the $p$-side if the junction is forward biased. In the light of the above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is incorrect but reason is correct.", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'As, diffusion current is due to the movement of majority charge carriers and drift current is due to movement of minority charge carriers under electric field. When junction is forward biased, the movement of electron is eased from $n$ side to $p$ side which increases the diffusion current and direction of diffusion current is from $p$-region to $n$-region.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',2023,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(46996,'physics',42,323,592,'Which one of the following represent reverse bias diode','[{"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'For ${R B, P}$ side is at low potential and ${N}$ side is at high potential.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(46979,'physics',42,323,592,'If each diode has a forward bias resistance of $25 \\Omega$ in the below circuit, which of the following option is correct?','[{"text": "$\\\\dfrac{I_{1}}{I_{2}}=1$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{I_{2}}{I_{3}}=1$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{I_{1}}{I_{2}}=2$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\dfrac{I_{3}}{I_{4}}=1$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Diode $D_{2}$ is reversed biased. So, the new circuit is given by <img src=\'/img/data/physics/42_46979_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' /> For the circuit, two resistors of resistance $125 \\Omega$ are in parallel. Resultant of $125 \\Omega$ is in series with $25 \\Omega$.<br>$R_{e q}=\\dfrac{150 \\times 150}{300}+25=100 \\Omega$<br>${I_1} = \\frac{5}{{100}} = 0.05\\,A$<br>${I_2} = {I_4} = \\frac{{0.05}}{2} = 0.025\\,A$<br>$\\dfrac{I_{1}}{I_{2}}=2$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2023,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/42_46979_question_1.png'),
(46981,'physics',42,321,588,'Match Column I with Column II. Choose the correct answer from the options given below<div class="matching-question"><div class="column-left">A. Intrinsic \nsemiconductor<br>B. $n$ - type \nsemiconductor<br>C. $p$ - type \nsemiconductor<br>D. Metal</div><div class="column-right">P. Fermi-level near the \nvalence band.<br>Q. Fermi-level in the\nmiddle of valence \nand conduction \nbands.<br>R. Fermi-level near the \nconduction band.<br>S. Fermi-level inside \nthe conduction band.</div></div>','[{"text": "$\\\\mathrm{A}-\\\\mathrm{P}, \\\\mathrm{B}-\\\\mathrm{Q}, \\\\mathrm{C}-\\\\mathrm{R}, \\\\mathrm{D}-\\\\mathrm{S}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{A}-\\\\mathrm{R}, \\\\mathrm{B}-\\\\mathrm{P}, \\\\mathrm{C}-\\\\mathrm{Q}, \\\\mathrm{D}-\\\\mathrm{S}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{A}-\\\\mathrm{Q}, \\\\mathrm{B}-\\\\mathrm{P}, \\\\mathrm{C}-\\\\mathrm{R}, \\\\mathrm{D}-\\\\mathrm{S}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{A}-\\\\mathrm{Q}, \\\\mathrm{B}-\\\\mathrm{R}, \\\\mathrm{C}-\\\\mathrm{P}, \\\\mathrm{D}-\\\\mathrm{S}$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Intrinsic semiconductor (Pure semiconductor) $\\rightarrow$ Fermi-level in the middle of valence and conduction band.<br>$n$-type semiconductor $\\rightarrow$ Fermi-level near the conduction band.<br>Extrinsic semiconductor is of two types<br>$p$-type semiconductor $\\rightarrow$ Fermi-level near the valence band.<br>Metals $\\rightarrow$ Fermi-level inside the conduction band<br>Hence,  $\\mathrm{A}-\\mathrm{Q}, \\mathrm{B}-\\mathrm{R}, \\mathrm{C}-\\mathrm{P}, \\mathrm{D}-\\mathrm{S}$',NULL,'Medium','Match the following',2023,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(46982,'physics',42,323,593,'Choose the correct statement about zener diode','[{"text": "It works as a voltage regulator in reverse bias and behaves like simple $p-n$ junction diode in forward bias.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "It works as a voltage regulator only in forward bias.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "It works as a voltage regulator in forward bias and behaves like simple $p-n$ junction diode in reverse bias", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "It works as a voltage regulator only in forward bias.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Zener diode works as a voltage regulator in reverse bias and a $p-n$ junction diode in forward bias. The main application of zener diode is voltage regulation.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2023,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(46983,'physics',42,323,593,'A zener diode of power rating $1.6 \\mathrm{~W}$ is to be used as voltage regulator. If the zener diode has a breakdown of $8\\,V$ and it has to regulate voltage fluctuating between $3\\,V$ and $10\\,V$. The value of resistance $R_{s}$ for safe operation of diode will be','[{"text": "$10 \\\\Omega$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$13 \\\\Omega$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$13.3 \\\\Omega$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$12 \\\\Omega$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'$P = 1.6\\,W,{V_{breakdown{\\rm{ }}}} = 8\\,V$<br>Voltage fluctuating between $3\\,V - 10\\,V$ <img src=\'/img/data/physics/42_46983_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' /> Current, $I = \\frac{P}{{{V_{Breakdown{\\rm{ }}}}}} = \\frac{{1.6}}{8} = 0.2\\,A$<br>$R_{S}=\\dfrac{V_{R}}{I}=\\dfrac{10-8}{0.2}=10 \\Omega$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',2023,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/42_46983_question_1.png'),
(46984,'physics',42,329,604,'The effect of increase in temperature on the number of electrons in conduction band ( $n_{e}$ ) and resistance of a semiconductor will be as','[{"text": "$n_{e}$ decreases, resistance increases", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both $n_{e}$ and resistance increases", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$n_{e}$ increases, resistance decreases", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both $n_{e}$ and resistance decreases", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'As temperature increases the electrons in valence band get excited and jump in to conduction band and hence the number of ${e^ - }s$ increases in conduction band. So, conductivity of semiconductor increases and resistivity decreases.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2023,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(46987,'physics',42,324,594,'Which one of the following statement is not correct in the case of light emitting diodes?<br>(A) It is a heavily doped $p-n$ junction.<br>(B) It emits light only when it is forward biased.<br>(C) It emits light only when it is reverse biased.<br>(D) The energy of the light emitted is equal to or slightly less than the energy gap of the semiconductor used.<br>Choose the correct answer from the options given below:','[{"text": "$B$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$C$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$A$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$C$ and $D$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'An LED works when it is forward biased. In forword biased LED when a free electron (which is in conduction band) recombines with a hole (which is in valence band), a photon of light is emitted.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ type-2',2023,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(46988,'physics',42,323,591,'Depletion region in an unbiased semiconductor diode is a region consisting of','[{"text": "both free electrons and holes", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "neither free electrons nor holes", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "only free electrons", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "only holes", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'As recombination of holes and electrons takes place, it is free from charge carriers.Correct option is (2).',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2024,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(46989,'physics',42,321,588,'The upper level of valence band and lower level of conduction band overlap in the case of','[{"text": "silicon", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "copper", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "carbon", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "germanium", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Copper is a conductor, In conductors, valence band and conduction band are overlapped.Correct option is (2).',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2024,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(46990,'physics',42,323,593,'In the diagram shown, the Zener diode has a reverse breakdown voltage of ${V_{Z}}$. The current through the load resistance ${R_{L}}$ is ${I_{L}}$. The current through the Zener diode is','[{"text": "${\\\\dfrac{V_{0}-V_{Z}}{R_{S}}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\dfrac{V_{0}-V_{Z}}{R_{L}}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\dfrac{V_{Z}}{R_{L}}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\left(\\\\dfrac{V_{0}-V_{Z}}{R_{S}}\\\\right)-I_{L}}$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Using ${K C L}$<br>${I=I_{Z}+I_{L}}$<br>${\\Rightarrow I_{Z}=I-I_{L}}$<br>${\\Rightarrow I_{Z}=I-I_{L}=\\left(\\dfrac{V_{0}-V_{Z}}{R_{S}}\\right)-I_{L}}$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',2024,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/42_46990_question_1.png'),
(46991,'physics',42,323,592,'A ${p-n}$ junction diode is connected to a battery of emf $5.7\\,V$ in series with a resistant $5\\,k\\Omega $ such that it is forward biased. If the barrier potential of the diode is $0.7\\,V$, neglecting the diode resistance, the current in the circuit is','[{"text": "$1.14\\\\,mA$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1\\\\,mA$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$1\\\\,A$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1.14\\\\,A$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'<img src=\'/img/data/physics/42_46991_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' /> $i = \\frac{{5.7 - 0.7}}{{5 \\times {{10}^3}}} = 1\\,mA$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2024,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(46993,'physics',42,324,594,'Consider the following statements ${A}$ and ${B}$ and identify the correct answer: A. For a solar-cell, the I-V characteristics lies in the IV quadrant of the given graph.<br>B. In a reverse biased $pn$ junction diode, the current measured in $\\left( {\\mu A} \\right)$, is due to majority charge carriers.','[{"text": "${A}$ is correct but ${B}$ is incorrect", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${A}$ is incorrect but ${B}$ is correct", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both ${A}$ and ${B}$ are correct", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both ${A}$ and ${B}$ are incorrect", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'A: Solar cell characteristics <img src=\'/img/data/physics/42_46993_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' /> B: In reverse biased $pn$ junction diode, the  current measured in $(\\mu A)$, is due to minority  charge carrier. Correct option is (1).',NULL,'Medium','MCQ type-2',2024,'NEET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/42_46993_question_1.png'),
(46995,'physics',42,323,592,'Choose the correct circuit which can achieve the bridge balance.','[{"text": "", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'The given circuit is a wheatstone bridge. The diode conducts when it is forward biased. In circuits (1) and (3) the diode is forward biased. In option (1)<br>${\\dfrac{10}{15}=\\dfrac{10}{5+R_{D}}}$ (Balancing condition)<br>The diode can conduct and have resistance<br>${R_{D}=10 \\Omega}$<br>In option (3)<br>${\\dfrac{10}{15}=\\dfrac{10}{\\dfrac{5 \\times R_{D}}{5+R_{D}}}}$ (Balancing condition)<br>${\\Rightarrow R_{D}=(-) v e}$ which is not possible.<br>So option (1) is correct.',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',2024,'NEET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(46999,'physics',42,323,592,'Two ideal diodes are connected to a battery as shown in the circuit. The current supplied by the battery in circuit','[{"text": "$0.5\\\\,A$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$0.25\\\\,A$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$1\\\\,A$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2\\\\,A$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Diode ${D_{1}}$ and ${D_{3}}$ are forward biased and ${D_{2}}$ is reverse biased. So, circuit will look like as shown below <img src=\'/img/data/physics/42_46999_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' /> We have,<br>$i=\\dfrac{5}{10+10} \\Rightarrow i=\\dfrac{5}{20}=0.25 {~A}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/42_46999_question_1.png'),
(47000,'physics',42,323,592,'In the circuit, if the forward voltage drop for the diode is $0.5\\,V$, the current will be','[{"text": "$3.4\\\\,mA$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$2\\\\,mA$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2.5\\\\,mA$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$3\\\\,mA$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'We have,<br>${I=\\dfrac{8-0.5}{2.2 \\times 10^{3}}=\\dfrac{7.5}{2.2} {~mA}=3.4 {~mA}}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/42_47000_question_1.png'),
(47001,'physics',42,323,593,'What is value of current ${I}$ in given circuit','[{"text": "$6\\\\,mA$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$4\\\\,mA$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$10\\\\,mA$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "zero", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Voltage across Zener diode<br>${=16 \\times \\dfrac{1 {k} \\Omega}{(1+1) {k} \\Omega}=8 {~V}}$,<br>which is greater than ${V z}$<br>So, Zener diode is in ${O N}$ state.<br>Then, ${I=\\dfrac{(16-6)}{1}-\\dfrac{6}{1}=10-6=4 {~mA}}$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/42_47001_question_1.png'),
(47002,'physics',42,323,593,'A zener diode having breakdown voltage equal to $10\\,V$, is used in a voltage regulator circuit shown in figure. The current flowing through the diode is','[{"text": "$10\\\\,mA$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$5\\\\,mA$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$15\\\\,mA$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$20\\\\,mA$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Voltage across zener diode<br>${=15 \\times \\dfrac{1}{1+0.2}=\\dfrac{15}{1.2}}$<br>${=12.5 {~V}}$<br>Clearly, voltage across Zener diode is greater then Zener breakdown voltage. So, diode is in ${O N}$ state. <img src=\'/img/data/physics/42_47002_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' /> Now, ${i=5 / 200=0.025 A=25 m A}$<br>and, ${i_{L}=10 / 10^{3}=10 {~mA}}$<br>So, ${i_{Z}=i-i_{L}=25-10=15 {~mA}}$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/42_47002_question_1.png'),
(47003,'physics',42,323,593,'In the circuit below, the current through zener diode is','[{"text": "$2\\\\,mA$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$3\\\\,mA$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$4\\\\,mA$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$5\\\\,mA$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Current through load ${=\\dfrac{10 {~V}}{5 {k} \\Omega}=2 {~mA}}$<br>Current through main circuit<br>${=\\dfrac{(90-10) V}{16 k \\Omega}=5 m A}$<br>So, current through diode ${=5 m A-2 m A=3 m A}$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/42_47003_question_1.png'),
(47004,'physics',42,329,604,'The typical ionisation energy of a donor in silicon is','[{"text": "$10.0\\\\,eV$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1.0\\\\,eV$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$0.1\\\\,eV$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$0.001\\\\,eV$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'The typical ionisation energy of donor in silicon is of the order of $0.1\\,eV.$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(47005,'physics',42,321,588,'Based on the energy band description, a solid can be classified as an insulator. The energy gap between the valence band and conduction band is','[{"text": "$3eV < {E_g} < 6eV$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${E_g} > 6eV$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${E_g} < 3eV$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${E_g} = 0$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'In insulators, the forbidden energy gap is quite large,  $e.g.$  the forbidden energy gap for diamond is $6\\,eV,$ which means that minimum of $6\\,eV$ energy is required to make the electron jump from the completely filled valence band to the conduction band.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(47006,'physics',42,321,588,'Band gap of silicon is ${E_g}(Si)$ of germanium is ${E_g}(Ge)$ and of carbon is ${E_g}(C)$. The correct order of band gap is','[{"text": "${E_g}(Si) < {E_g}(Ge) < {E_g}(C)$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${E_g}(Si) > {E_g}(Ge) < {E_g}(C)$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${E_g}(Si) < {E_g}(Ge) > {E_g}(C)$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${E_g}(Si) > {E_g}(Ge) > {E_g}(C)$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Energy band gap at $0\\,K$ in $eV$ for silicon and germanium is $1.17\\,eV$ and $0.74\\,eV,$ respectively. Band gap for carbon is much higher $\\left( {5.2\\,eV} \\right)$.Thus, correct sequence is<br>${E_g}(Si) > {E_g}(Ge) < {E_g}(C)$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(47007,'physics',42,322,590,'Suitable impurities are added to a semiconductor depending on its use. This is done to','[{"text": "increase its life", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "enable it to withstand high voltage", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "increase its electrical conductivity", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "increase its electrical resistivity", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Impurities added to semiconductors increase their electrical conductivity by increasing the number of charge carriers, $i.e.{\\rm{ }}$ holes and electrons.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(47008,'physics',42,322,589,'In intrinsic semiconductor at room temperature, number of electrons and holes are','[{"text": "equal", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "zero", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "unequal", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "infinite", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'At room temperature, due to thermal vibrations, the few bonds of intrinsic semiconductor are broken, producing equal number of electrons and holes in the semiconductor.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(47009,'physics',42,322,590,'Would there be any advantage to add $n$-type or $p$-type impurities to copper?','[{"text": "Yes", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "No", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "May be", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Information is insufficient", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Pure $Cu$ is already an excellent conductor, since it has a partially filled conduction band.<br>Further more, $Cu$ forms a metallic crystal as compared to the covalent crystals of silicon or germanium, so the scheme of using an impurity to donate or accept an electron does not work for copper.<br>In fact adding impurities to copper decreases the conductivity, because an impurity tends to scatter electrons, impeding the flow of current<br>.So, there is no advantage of adding $n$-type or $p$-type impurities to copper.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(47010,'physics',42,322,590,'In semiconductor, the concentrations of electrons and holes are $8 \\times {10^{18}}\\,{m^{ - 3}}$ and $5 \\times {10^{18}}\\,{m^{ - 3}}$ respectively. If the mobilities of electrons and holes are $2.3\\,{m^2}\\,{V^{ - 1}}\\,{s^{ - 1}}$ and $0.01\\,{m^2}\\,{V^{ - 1}}\\,{s^{ - 1}}$ respectively, then semiconductor is','[{"text": "$n - $type and its resistivity is $2.95\\\\Omega  - m$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$p - $type and its resistivity is $0.034\\\\Omega  - m$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$n - $type and its resistivity is $0.34\\\\Omega  - m$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$p - $type and its resistivity is $3.4\\\\Omega  - m$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'As, $n_{e}>h_{h}$, so semiconductor is $n - {\\rm{type}}.$<br>Also, conductivity $(\\sigma ) = \\frac{1}{{{\\text{resistivity }}(\\rho )}}$<br>$\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\sigma  = e\\left( {{n_e}{\\mu _e} + {n_h}{\\mu _h}} \\right)$<br>$\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, = 1.6 \\times {10^{ - 19}}$<br>$\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, \\times \\,\\left[ {8 \\times {{10}^{18}} \\times 2.3 + 5 \\times {{10}^{18}} \\times 0.01} \\right]$<br>$\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, = 2.95\\Omega  - m$<br>So, $\\rho  = \\frac{1}{\\sigma } = \\frac{1}{{2.95}} = 0.34\\Omega  - m$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(47011,'physics',42,321,587,'Choose the only false statement from the following.','[{"text": "Substances with energy gap of the order of $10\\\\,eV$ are insulators.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "The conductivity of a semiconductor increases with increase in temperature.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "In conductors, the valence and conduction bands may overlap.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "The resistivity of a semiconductor increases with increase in temperature.", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'In semiconductor, resistivity decreases with increase in temperature.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(47012,'physics',42,322,590,'In a semiconductor, electron concentration is $7 \\times {10^{13}}\\,c{m^{ - 3}}$ and hole concentration is $5 \\times {10^{12}}\\,c{m^{ - 3}}$. Then the semiconductor is','[{"text": "$n - {\\\\rm{type}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$p$-type", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "intrinsic", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$p-n$ type", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Given, ${n_e} = 7 \\times {10^{13}}\\,c{m^{ - 3}} = 70 \\times {10^{12}}\\,c{m^{ - 3}}$<br>${n_h} = 5 \\times {10^{12}}\\,c{m^{ - 3}}$<br>${n_e} > {n_h}$<br>So, the semiconductor must be $n - $ type.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(47013,'physics',42,323,592,'In a reverse biased $p - n$  junction, when the applied bias voltage is equal to the breakdown voltage, then','[{"text": "current remains constant while voltage increases sharply", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "voltage remains constant while current increases sharply", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "current and voltage increase", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "current and voltage decrease", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'In a reverse biased $p - n$ junction, when the applied bias voltage is equal to the breakdown voltage, then voltage remains constant while current increases sharply.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(47014,'physics',42,325,596,'The output form of a full wave rectifier is','[{"text": "an AC voltage", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "a DC voltage", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "zero", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "a pulsating unidirectional voltage", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Output from of a full-wave rectifier is a pulsating unidirectional voltage.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(47015,'physics',42,324,594,'For detecting intensity of light, we use','[{"text": "photodiode in forward bias", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "photodiode in reverse bias", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "LED in forward bias", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "LED in reverse bias", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'To detect the intensity of light photodiode is used in reverse bias.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(47016,'physics',42,323,592,'What is the current through an ideal $p-n$ junction diode shown in figure below ?','[{"text": "Zero", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$10\\\\,mA$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$20\\\\,mA$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$50\\\\,mA$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Applying KVL, $1 + 100\\,i - 3 = 0$<br>$\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,i = \\frac{2}{{100}} \\times \\frac{{1000}}{{1000}}$<br>$\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, = 20 \\times {10^{ - 3}}\\,A$<br>$\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, = 20\\,mA$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/42_47016_question_1.png'),
(47017,'physics',42,323,593,'Zener diode acts as','[{"text": "voltage regulator in reverse biasing", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "voltage regulator in forward biasing", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "current regulator in reverse biasing", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "current regulator in forward biasing", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Zener diode works as a voltage regulator under reverse biasing condition. It is based on the fact that in the breakdown region, for quite large changes in the zener current, the voltage across the diode remains almost constant.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(47018,'physics',42,323,592,'Of the diodes shown in the following diagrams, which one is reverse biased?','[{"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'In reverse biasing condition, the potential at $p$-side is lower than that at $n$-side. In option (3), the $p$-side is grounded or at zero potential and $n$-side is at $ + 5\\,V$ which is higher. So, it is reverse biased.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ type-3',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(47019,'physics',42,323,592,'In forward bias, the width of depletion layer is','[{"text": "decreases with decrease in potential barrier voltage", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "increases with increase in potential barrier voltage", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "independent of potential barrier voltage", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "None of the above", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'In forward biasing, the forward bias voltage opposes the potential barrier ${V_B}$. Due to it, the potential barrier is considerably reduced and the depletion region becomes thin. The potential barrier at some forward voltage $\\left( {0.1\\,{\\mkern 1mu} {\\text{to}}\\,{\\mkern 1mu} 0.3\\,V} \\right)$, is eliminated altogether.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(47020,'physics',42,323,592,'Potential drop across forward junction $p-n$ diode is $0.7\\,V$. If a battery of $4\\,V$ is applied, calculate the resistance to be put in series, if the maximum current in the circuit is $5\\,mA$.','[{"text": "$660\\\\,\\\\Omega $", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$350\\\\,\\\\Omega $", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$475\\\\,\\\\Omega $", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$500\\\\,\\\\Omega $", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'The circuit can be drawn as  <img src=\'/img/data/physics/42_47020_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />   Applying KVL, $ - 0.7 + 4 - 5 \\times {10^{ - 3}}R = 0$<br>$ \\Rightarrow R = 660\\Omega $',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(47034,'physics',42,329,604,'8 bits form','[{"text": "1 byte", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "1 diction", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "1 nibble", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "1 binary", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'8 bits form 1 byte.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(47035,'physics',42,323,592,'Assuming the diodes to be of silicon with forward resistance zero, the current I in the following circuit is','[{"text": "0", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$9.65\\\\,mA$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$10\\\\,mA$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$10.35\\\\,mA$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'$I = \\frac{V}{R} = \\frac{{20}}{{2 \\times {{10}^3}}} = 10 \\times {10^{ - 3}}\\,$<br>$A = 10\\,mA$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/42_47035_question_1.png'),
(47036,'physics',42,323,593,'A zener diode, having breakdown voltage equal to $15\\,V$, is used in a voltage regulator circuit shown in figure. The current through the diode is','[{"text": "$10\\\\,mA$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$15\\\\,mA$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$20\\\\,mA$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$5\\\\,mA$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Voltage across zener diode is constant. <img src=\'/img/data/physics/42_47036_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' /> Current in $1\\,k\\Omega $ resistor,<br> $\\,{\\left( i \\right)_{1k\\Omega }} = \\frac{{15volt}}{{1k\\Omega }} = 15\\,mA$<br>Current in ${250\\,\\Omega }$ resistor,<br>${(i)_{250\\Omega }} = \\frac{{(20 - 15)V}}{{250\\Omega }} = \\frac{{5\\,V}}{{250\\Omega }}$<br>$ = \\frac{{20}}{{1000}}\\,A = 20\\,mA$<br>$\\therefore \\,\\,{\\left( i \\right)_{zener\\,diode}} = \\left( {20 - 15} \\right) = 5\\,mA.$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/42_47036_question_1.png'),
(47037,'physics',42,323,591,'In an unbiased $n{\\text{ - }}p$ junction electrons diffuse from $n$-region to $p$-region because :','[{"text": "holes in $p$-region attract them", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "electrons travel across the junction due to potential difference", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "only electrons move from $n$ to $p$ region and not the vice-versa", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "electron concentration in $n$-region is more compared to that in $p$-region", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Electrons in an unbiased $p{\\rm{ - }}n$ junction, diffuse from $n$-region $i.e.,$ higher electron concentration to $p$-region $i.e.,$ low electron concentration region.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(47042,'physics',42,323,592,'The resistance of a germanium junction diode whose $V-I$ is shown in figure is$\\left( {{V_k} = 0.3\\,V} \\right)$','[{"text": "$5\\\\, \\\\mathrm{k} \\\\Omega$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$0.2\\\\, \\\\mathrm{k} \\\\Omega$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$2.3\\\\, \\\\mathrm{k} \\\\Omega$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\left(\\\\dfrac{10}{2.3}\\\\right)\\\\, \\\\mathrm{k} \\\\Omega$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'<img src=\'/img/data/physics/42_47042_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />  From graph,<br>Resistance of the germanium junction diode is<br>$R = \\frac{{\\Delta V}}{{\\Delta I}} = \\frac{{2.3\\,V - 0.3\\,V}}{{10\\,mA - 0}} = \\frac{{2\\,V}}{{10 \\times {{10}^{ - 3}}\\,A}}$<br>$=0.2 \\times 10^{3} \\Omega=0.2\\, \\mathrm{k} \\Omega$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/42_47042_question_1.png'),
(47043,'physics',42,321,588,'In a semiconductor, separation between conduction and valence band is of the order of','[{"text": "$0\\\\,eV$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1\\\\,eV$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$10\\\\,eV$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$50\\\\,eV$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'In a semiconductor, separation between conduction and valence band $(i.e.$ energy gap) is the order of $1eV$.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(47044,'physics',42,323,592,'The current in the circuit will be','[{"text": "$\\\\frac{5}{{40}}\\\\,A$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{5}{{50}}\\\\,A$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{5}{{10}}\\\\,A$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{5}{{20}}\\\\,A$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'In the given circuit, diode in the lower branch is forward biased so it conducts and diode in the upper branch is reverse biased so it does not conduct.<br>$\\therefore$ The total resistance in the circuit<br>$=20\\, \\Omega+30\\, \\Omega=50\\, \\Omega$<br>The current in the circuit is $I = \\frac{{5\\,V}}{{50\\,\\Omega }} = \\frac{5}{{50}}\\,A$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/42_47044_question_1.png'),
(47046,'physics',42,322,590,'Mobilities of electrons and holes in a sample of intrinsic germanium at room temperature are $0.36\\,{m^2}\\,{V^{ - 1}}\\,{s^{ - 1}}$ and $0.17\\,{m^2}\\,{V^{ - 1}}\\,{s^{ - 1}}$. The electron and hole densities are each equal to $2.5 \\times {10^{19}}\\,{m^{ - 3}}$. The electrical conductivity of germanium is','[{"text": "$4.24\\\\,S\\\\,{m^{ - 1}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$2.12\\\\,S\\\\,{m^{ - 1}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$1.09\\\\,S\\\\,{m^{ - 1}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$0.47\\\\,S\\\\,{m^{ - 1}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'The electrical conductivity of germanium is<br>$\\sigma=e\\left[n_{e} \\mu_{e}+n_{h} \\mu_{h}\\right]$<br>Here,${\\mu _e} = 0.36\\,{m^2}{V^{ - 1}}\\,{s^{ - 1}}$<br>${\\mu _h} = 0.17\\,{m^2}\\,{V^{ - 1}}\\,{s^{ - 1}}$ <br>${n_e} = {n_h} = 2.5 \\times {10^{19}}\\,{m^{ - 3}}$<br>$\\therefore \\quad \\sigma  = \\left( {1.6 \\times {{10}^{ - 19}}C} \\right)\\left( {0.36\\,{m^2}\\,{V^{ - 1}}\\,{s^{ - 1}}} \\right)$<br>$\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\left( {2.5 \\times {{10}^{19}}{m^{ - 3}}} \\right) + $<br>$\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\left( {0.17{m^2}{V^{ - 1}}{s^{ - 1}}} \\right)\\left( {2.5 \\times {{10}^{19}}{m^{ - 3}}} \\right)$<br>$ = 2.12\\,S{m^{ - 1}}$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(47048,'physics',42,325,596,'The output waveform of a full wave rectifier is','[{"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'The output waveform of a full wave rectifier is represented by $(3).$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(47050,'physics',42,323,593,'Zener diode is used for','[{"text": "producing oscillations in an oscillator", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "amplification", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "stabilisation", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "rectification", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Zener diode has an unusual reverse current characteristic which is particularly suitable for voltage regulating purposes. Due to this characteristic, it is used as voltage stabilizer in electronics.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(47051,'physics',42,322,590,'A potential difference of $2 V$ is applied between the opposite faces of a Ge crystal plate of area $1\\,c{m^2}$ and thickness $0.5\\,mm$. If the concentration of electrons in $Ge$ is $2 \\times {10^{19}}/{m^3}$ and mobilities of electrons and holes are $0.36\\,{m^2}\\,{V^{ - 1}}\\,{s^{ - 1}}$ and $0.14\\,{m^2}\\,{V^{ - 1}}\\,{s^{ - 1}}$ respectively, then the current flowing through the plate will be','[{"text": "$0.25\\\\,A$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$0.45\\\\,A$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$0.56\\\\,A$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$0.64\\\\,A$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Conductivity, $\\sigma=n e\\left(\\mu_{e}+\\mu_{h}\\right)$<br>$=2 \\times 10^{19} \\times 1.6 \\times 10^{-19}(0.36+0.14)$<br>$ = 1.6{(\\Omega m)^{ - 1}}$<br>As, $R=\\rho \\dfrac{l}{A}=\\dfrac{l}{\\sigma A}=\\dfrac{0.5 \\times 10^{-3}}{1.6 \\times 10^{-4}}=\\dfrac{25}{8} \\Omega$<br>$\\therefore \\quad i = \\frac{V}{R} = \\frac{2}{{25/8}} = \\frac{{16}}{{25}}\\,A = 0.64\\,A$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(47059,'physics',42,323,591,'In a ${p-n}$ junction having depletion layer of thickness ${10 \\mu {m}}$, the potential across it is 0.1 $V$ . The electric field (in ${{V} {m}^{-1}}$ ) is','[{"text": "${10^{7}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${10^{-6}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${10^{4}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${10^{-5}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Thickness of depletion layer,<br>$d=10 \\mu {m}=10 \\times 10^{-6} {~m}$<br>Potential across ${p-n}$ junction, ${V=0.1 {~V}}$<br>${\\therefore}$  The electric field,<br>$E=\\dfrac{V}{d}=\\dfrac{0.1 {~V}}{10 \\times 10^{-6} {~m}}=10^{4} {~V} {~m}^{-1}$.<br>So correct option is (3)',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(47060,'physics',42,323,592,'The diode used in the circuit shown in the figure has a constant voltage drop of 0.5 $V$ at all currents and a maximum power rating of 100 milliwatts. What should be the value of the resistor ${R}$, connected in series with the diode, for obtaining maximum current?','[{"text": "$1.5\\\\,\\\\Omega $", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$5\\\\,\\\\Omega $", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$6.67\\\\,\\\\Omega $", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$200\\\\,\\\\Omega $", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Current in diode,<br>$I=\\dfrac{P}{V}=\\dfrac{100 {~mW}}{0.5 {~V}}=0.2 {~A}$<br>Voltage drop across resistance,<br>$R=(1.5-0.5) {V}=1 {~V}$<br>${\\therefore}$ Resistance  $R = \\frac{{{\\text{ Voltage drop }}}}{{{\\text{ Current }}}} = \\frac{{1\\,V}}{{0.2\\,A}} = 5\\,\\Omega .$<br>So correct option is (2)',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/42_47060_question_1.png'),
(47063,'physics',42,323,592,'In forward biasing of junction diode, the width of depletion layer','[{"text": "decreases", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "increases", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "remains unchanged", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "none of these", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'When ${p-n}$ junction diode is forward bias, the applied electric field opposes the barrier electric field. As a result, width of depletion layer is reduced.<br>So correct option is (1)',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(47066,'physics',42,323,592,'In the circuit shown, the diodes have a forward resistance of $50\\,\\Omega $ and infinite backward resistance. The current through the $100\\,\\Omega $ resistance is','[{"text": "0", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "0.02 $A$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "0.03 $A$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "0.036 $A$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'In the circuit, the upper diode ${D_{1}}$ is forward biased and the lower diode ${D_{2}}$ is reverse biased. Thus there will be no current through lower diode junction.Total resistance of the circuit<br>$ = (150 + 50 + 100) = 300\\,\\Omega $<br>Current in circuit,  $I = \\frac{V}{R} = \\frac{6}{{300}} = 0.02\\,\\Omega .$<br>So correct option is (2)',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/42_47066_question_1.png'),
(47067,'physics',42,322,590,'The impurity atoms with which pure silicon should be doped to make a ${p}$-type semiconductor are','[{"text": "Phosphorus", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Antimony", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Boron", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Silver", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'${p}$-type semiconductor is obtained when silicon is be doped with trivalent impurity like ${{Al}, {B}}$, In etc.<br>So correct option is (3)',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(47068,'physics',42,321,588,'The energy of a photon ${(\\lambda=589 {~nm})}$ equals the band gap of a semi-conducting material. The minimum energy required to create a hole-electron pair is','[{"text": "4.2 $eV$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "2.1 $eV$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "1.2 $eV$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "0.98 $eV$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'The minimum energy required to create a hole-electron pair is ${E=\\dfrac{h c}{\\lambda}}$<br> where, ${h=6.6 \\times 10^{-34} {~J} {~s}}$<br>$\\begin{gathered}c=3 \\times 10^{8} {~m} {~s}^{-1} \\\\\\lambda=589 {~nm}=589 \\times 10^{-9} {~m} \\\\\\therefore \\quad E=\\dfrac{6.6 \\times 10^{-34} \\times 3 \\times 10^{8}}{589 \\times 10^{-9} \\times 1.6 \\times 10^{-19}} {eV}=2.1 {eV}\\end{gathered}$.<br>So correct option is (2)',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(47072,'physics',42,323,592,'In the circuit below, $A$ and $B$ represent two inputs and ${C}$ represents the output. The circuit represents a logic gate for which, values of $A,B$ and $C$ are as follows: Determine the value of $x$. (Consider all the compound in the circuit to be ideal.)','[{"text": "$1\\\\,V$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$7\\\\,V$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$3\\\\,V$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$5\\\\,V$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'In the given circuit, the gate conducts when any of the diodes conduct. Thus, it represents an $OR$ gate. For $OR$ gate, the output is high when either or both the inputs are high.<br>$\\therefore \\,\\,\\,\\,x = 5\\,V$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/42_47072_question_1.png'),
(47075,'physics',42,329,604,'The band gap in germanium is ${E}_{{g}}=0.64 \\,{eV}$. Assuming that the number of hole-electron pairs is proportional to ${e}^{\\left(-{E}_{{g}} / 2 k {~T}\\right)}$, find the fractional increase in the number of charge carriers in pure germanium as the temperature is increased from $300 {~K}$ to $384 {~K}$. <br>[Take ${k = 1.4 \\times {{10}^{ - 23}},\\ln (14.4) = \\frac{8}{3}}$]','[{"text": "13.4", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "17.2", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "10.6", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "15.4", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'If  ${n_1}$ is the number of charge carriers at temperature $T_{1}$ and ${n}_{2}$ at ${T}_{2}$, then,<br>$n_{1}=n_{0} e^{\\left(-E_{{g}} / 2 k T_{1}\\right)}$ and $n_{2}=n_{0} e^{\\left(-{E}_{{g}} / 2 k T_{2}\\right)}$.<br>The fractional increase as the temperature is raised from $T_{1}$ to $T_{2}$ is, $\\Delta n=\\dfrac{n_{2}-n_{1}}{n_{1}}=\\left(\\dfrac{n_{2}}{n_{1}}-1\\right)$<br>$\\therefore \\quad \\Delta {n}=\\left[{e}^{\\frac{{E}_{{g}}}{2 {k}}\\left(\\dfrac{1}{{~T}_{1}}-\\dfrac{1}{{~T}_{2}}\\right)}-1\\right]$$\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,(1)$<br>Now,<br>$\\frac{{{E_g}}}{{2k}}\\left( {\\frac{1}{{\\,{T_1}}} - \\frac{1}{{\\,{T_2}}}} \\right)$<br>$ = \\frac{{0.64 \\times 1.6 \\times {{10}^{ - 19}}}}{{2 \\times 1.4 \\times {{10}^{ - 23}}}}\\left( {\\frac{1}{{300}} - \\frac{1}{{384}}} \\right)$<br>$ = \\frac{{32 \\times 8 \\times {{10}^2}}}{7}\\left( {\\frac{{84}}{{384 \\times 300}}} \\right)$<br>$ = \\frac{{32 \\times 12 \\times 8}}{{384 \\times 3}} = \\frac{8}{3}$<br>$\\therefore \\quad {e}^{\\frac{E_{{g}}}{2 k}\\left(\\dfrac{1}{T_{1}}-\\dfrac{1}{T_{2}}\\right)}={e}^{\\frac{8}{3}}$<br>Given that, $\\ln (14.4)=\\dfrac{8}{3}$<br>${\\therefore \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{e^{\\frac{8}{3}}} = 14.4}$<br>${\\therefore \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\Delta n = (14.4 - 1){\\rm{  }}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{\\rm{[From(1)]}}}$<br>${\\therefore \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\Delta n = 13.4}$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(47080,'physics',42,323,592,'At $0.3 {~V}$ and $0.7 {~V}$, the diodes ${Ge}$ and ${Si}$ become conductor respectively. In given figure, if ends of diode Ge are reversed, the change in potential ${V}_{0}$ will be','[{"text": "$0.4\\\\,V$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$0.9\\\\,V$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$0.1\\\\,V$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$0.7\\\\,V$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Initially $Ge$ and ${Si}$ are both forward biased. So, current will effectively pass through Ge diode with a voltage drop of $0.3 {~V}$.   <img src=\'/img/data/physics/42_47080_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />   $\\therefore$ Initial output voltage,<br>$V_{0}=10-0.3=9.7 {~V}$<br>If ${Ge}$ is reversed biased, then only Si diode will conduct.<br>In this condition, output voltage<br>${V}_{0}=10-0.7=9.3 {~V}$  <br><img src=\'/img/data/physics/42_47080_explanation_2.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />  <br>$\\therefore$ Change in output voltage $=9.7-9.3=0.4 {~V}$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/42_47080_question_1.png'),
(47084,'physics',42,323,593,'Zener diode has contact potential of $1 {~V}$ in the absence of biasing. It undergoes zener breakdown for an electric field of $2.5 \\times 10^{6} {~V} / {m}$ at the depletion region of ${p}-{n}$ junction. If the width of the depletion region is $1.2 \\,\\mu {m}$, what should be the reverse bias potential   for zener breakdown to occur?','[{"text": "$2\\\\,V$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$7\\\\,V$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$5\\\\,V$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$3\\\\,V$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'The breakdown field $ = 2.5 \\times {10^6}\\,V/m$,  width of depletion region $=1.2 \\times 10^{-6} {~m}$<br>$\\therefore {V}_{\\text {breakdown }}={E} \\times {d}=2.5 \\times 10^{6} \\times 1.2 \\times 10^{-6}$<br>$\\therefore {V}_{\\text {breakdown }}=3 {~V}$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(47086,'physics',42,323,592,'In the circuits shown below, the ratio $I_{1}: I_{2}$ is equal to ____  .<br>(Assume both the diodes to be ideal)','[{"text": "1.65", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "3.47", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "9.36", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "5.16", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'In figure (a), diode $D$ is forward biased. Hence, the current will flow directly through the diode and equivalent circuit would be,  <img src=\'/img/data/physics/42_47086_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />  $\\therefore \\quad {I}_{1}=\\dfrac{{V}}{{R}}=\\dfrac{5}{10 \\times 10^{3}}=\\dfrac{1}{2} {~mA}$$\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,(1)$<br>In figure $(b)$, diode ${D}$ is reverse biased. <br>Hence, the current will flow through the branch containing $10 \\,{k} \\Omega$ resistor  <img src=\'/img/data/physics/42_47086_explanation_2.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />  $\\therefore \\quad {I}_{2}=\\dfrac{5}{(6.5+10) \\times 10^{3}}$<br>$\\therefore \\quad {I}_{2}=\\dfrac{1}{3.3} {~mA}$$\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,(2)$<br>From (1) and (2),<br>$\\dfrac{I_{1}}{I_{2}}=\\dfrac{3.3}{2}=1.65$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/42_47086_question_1.png'),
(47090,'physics',42,323,592,'The graph of  \\[I - V\\]  characteristics of a  \\[p - n\\]  junction diode is shown below. If the diode is connected to a signal generator of peak voltage \\(22 {~V}\\), then the dc current across the load of \\(4.8 \\,\\Omega\\) will be   (Take \\(\\pi=22 / 7\\) )<div class="matching-question"></div>','[{"text": "$3.27\\\\,A$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1.25\\\\,A$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$5.62\\\\,A$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$4.65\\\\,A$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'The current at $2{\\rm{ }}V$ is $250 {~mA}$ and at $2.2 {~V}$ it is $500 {~mA}$. The dynamic resistance in this region is,<br>${r_d} = \\frac{{\\Delta V}}{{\\Delta I}}$<br>$ = \\frac{{(2.2 - 2)}}{{(500 - 250) \\times {{10}^{ - 3}}}} = \\frac{4}{5}\\Omega $<br>Now, when the diode is connected to ac supply it works as a half-wave rectifier giving a $dc$ output. For half-wave rectifier, peak current is, <br>$I_{0}=\\dfrac{V_{0}}{r_{d}+R_{L}}$<br>$=\\dfrac{22}{\\dfrac{4}{5}+4.8}$<br>$=\\dfrac{22}{5.6} {~A}$<br>$\\therefore \\quad {I}_{{dc}}=\\dfrac{{I}_{0}}{\\pi}=\\dfrac{22}{5.6 \\times \\dfrac{22}{7}}$<br>$=\\dfrac{5}{4}$<br>$=1.25 {~A}$',NULL,'Hard','Graph/Figure',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/42_47090_question_1.png'),
(47097,'physics',42,323,592,'Assuming that the junction diode is ideal, the current through the diode in $mA$ is','[{"text": "$10\\\\,mA$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$20\\\\,mA$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$18\\\\,mA$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$30\\\\,mA$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'$I=\\dfrac{3-1}{100}=20 {~mA}$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/42_47097_question_1.png'),
(47102,'physics',42,322,590,'If the ratio of the concentration of electrons to that of holes in a semiconductor is ${\\dfrac{7}{5}}$ and the ratio of currents is ${\\dfrac{7}{4}}$, then what is the ratio of their drift velocities?','[{"text": "$\\\\frac{2}{3}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{4}{7}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{5}{4}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{1}{5}$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'$I = neA{v_d} \\Rightarrow {v_d} \\propto \\frac{I}{n}$<br>$\\frac{{{v_{{d_1}}}}}{{{v_{{d_2}}}}} = \\frac{{{I_1}}}{{{I_2}}} \\times \\frac{{{n_2}}}{{{n_1}}} = \\frac{7}{4} \\times \\frac{5}{7} = \\frac{5}{4}$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(47108,'physics',42,322,589,'The acceptor level of a  $p$ -type semiconductor is  $6\\,eV.$ The maximum wavelength of light which can create a hole would be (Given $hc = 1242\\,eV\\,nm)$','[{"text": "$407\\\\,nm$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$207\\\\,nm$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$10.3.5\\\\,nm$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$414\\\\,nm$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'$E = 6\\,eV$<br>$\\lambda=\\dfrac{h c}{E}$ or $\\lambda=\\dfrac{1242}{E(\\text { in } e V)} n m$<br>$ \\Rightarrow \\lambda  = \\frac{{1242}}{6}\\,nm = 207\\,nm$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',2024,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(47109,'physics',42,323,592,'The value of net resistance of the network as shown in the given figure is','[{"text": "$\\\\left(\\\\dfrac{15}{4}\\\\right) \\\\Omega$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$6\\\\,\\\\Omega $", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\left(\\\\dfrac{30}{11}\\\\right) \\\\Omega$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\left(\\\\dfrac{5}{2}\\\\right) \\\\Omega$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Here $D_{2}$ is reverse biased, so  $5\\,\\Omega $  branch is removed and $D_{1}$ is Forward biased so $D_{1}$ is replaced by conducting wire<br><img src=\'/img/data/physics/42_47109_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />  <br>Now,  $10\\,\\Omega $  and  $15\\,\\Omega $  are parallel<br>So, ${R_{eq}} = \\frac{{15 \\times 10}}{{25}} = 6\\,\\Omega $',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2024,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/42_47109_question_1.png'),
(47110,'physics',42,323,592,'Which of the diode circuit shows correct biasing used for the measurement of dynamic resistance of $p-n$ junction diode?','[{"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'To measure the dynamic resistance, diode should be forward biased. For forward bias $V_{P}>V_{n}$.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2024,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(47111,'physics',42,323,592,'In the given circuit, the voltage across load resistance $\\left(R_{L}\\right)$ is','[{"text": "$14.00\\\\,V$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$9.00\\\\,V$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$8.50\\\\,V$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$8.75\\\\,V$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Voltage drop across $D_{2}$ is  ${V_{{B_2}}} = 0.7\\,\\,V$  and voltage drop across $D_{1}$ is  ${V_{{B_1}}} = 0.3\\,\\,V.$<br><img src=\'/img/data/physics/42_47111_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />  <br>Total current through the circuit is,<br>$I=\\dfrac{V-V_{B_{1}}-V_{B_{2}}}{R_{e f f}}$<br>$I = \\frac{{(15 - 0.7 - 0.3)}}{{(1.5 + 2.5) \\times {{10}^3}}} = \\frac{{14}}{{4 \\times {{10}^3}}} = 3.5 \\times {10^{ - 3}}\\,A$<br>Voltage across load resistance is, ${V_L} = I{R_L}$<br>${V_L} = 3.5 \\times {10^{ - 3}} \\times 2.5 \\times {10^3} \\Rightarrow {V_L} = 8.75\\,V$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2024,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/42_47111_question_1.png'),
(47112,'physics',42,324,594,'A light emitting diode ( $L E D)$ is fabricated using Ga as semiconducting material whose band gap is $1.42\\,eV.$ The wavelength of light emitted from the $LED$ is','[{"text": "$1234\\\\,nm$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1400\\\\,nm$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$875\\\\,nm$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$650\\\\,nm$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Given, $E = 1.42\\,eV.$  We know that  $\\lambda  = \\frac{{1240}}{{E({\\text{ in }}eV)}}nm$<br>As,  $ \\Rightarrow \\lambda  = \\frac{{1240}}{{1.42}} \\simeq 875\\,nm$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2024,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(47113,'physics',42,324,594,'The $I-V$ characteristics of an electronic device shown in the figure. The device is','[{"text": "a diode which can be used as a rectifier", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "a transistor which can be used as an amplifier", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "a solar cell", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "a Zener diode which can be used as a voltage regulator", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'It is the characteristic of Zener diode. $i.\\,e.,$  Zener diode has sharpe breakdown in the reverse bias',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2024,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/42_47113_question_1.png'),
(47114,'physics',42,323,593,'In the given circuit if the power rating of Zener  diode is $10\\,mW,$ the value of series resistance $R_{s}$ to regulate the input unregulated supply is','[{"text": "$5\\\\,k\\\\,\\\\Omega $", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$10\\\\,k\\\\,\\\\Omega $", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$0.42\\\\,k\\\\,\\\\Omega $", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$1\\\\,k\\\\,\\\\Omega $", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'<img src=\'/img/data/physics/42_47114_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />  <br>Voltage across $R_{s}$<br>$V\' = {V_s} - {V_z} = 8 - 5 = 3\\,V$<br>Current though load resistor $R_{L}$ is<br>$i\'\' = \\frac{5}{{1000}} = 5\\,mA$<br>Maximum current through zener diode,<br>$i\' = \\frac{{{P_{{\\text{zener }}}}}}{{{V_{{\\text{zener }}}}}} = \\frac{{10 \\times {{10}^{ - 3}}}}{5} = 2\\,mA$<br>So,  $i = i\' + i\'\' = 2 + 5 = 7\\,mA$<br>So, ${R_s} = \\frac{{V\'}}{i} = \\frac{3}{{7 \\times {{10}^{ - 3}}}} = \\frac{{3000}}{7}\\Omega  = 0.42\\,k\\,\\Omega $',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',2024,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/42_47114_question_1.png'),
(47115,'physics',42,324,594,'Conductivity of a photodiode starts changing only if the wavelength of incident light is less than  $660\\,nm.$  The band gap of photodiode is found to be  $\\left( {\\frac{X}{8}} \\right)eV.$ The value of $X$ is<br>(Given, $h = 6.6 \\times {10^{ - 34}}\\,Js,\\,\\,e = 1.6 \\times {10^{ - 19}}C{\\mkern 1mu} \\,)$','[{"text": "11", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "13", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "15", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "21", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Given, $\\lambda_{c}=$ critical wavelength  $ = 660\\,nm$  Energy band gap,<br>$E_{g}=\\dfrac{h c}{\\lambda}=\\dfrac{6.6 \\times 10^{-34} \\times 3 \\times 10^{8}}{660 \\times 10^{-9}}$<br>$ = 3 \\times {10^{ - 19}}\\,J = \\frac{{3 \\times {{10}^{ - 19}}}}{{1.6 \\times {{10}^{ - 19}}}}eV$<br>$ = \\frac{{30}}{{16}} = \\frac{{15}}{8}eV$<br>$\\Rightarrow$ So, $X=15$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',2024,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(47116,'physics',42,323,593,'In the given circuit, the breakdown voltage of the Zener diode is  $3.0\\,V.$  What is the value of $I_{Z}$ ?','[{"text": "$10\\\\,mA$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$7\\\\,mA$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$5.5\\\\,mA$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$3.3\\\\,mA$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'<img src=\'/img/data/physics/42_47116_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />  <br>${V_{{\\text{in }}}} = 10\\,V,{V_Z} = 3\\,V$<br>$R = 1000\\,\\Omega ,{R_L} = 2000\\,\\Omega $<br>$I_{Z}=\\dfrac{V_{\\text {in }}-V_{Z}}{R}-\\dfrac{V_{Z}}{R_{L}}$<br>$I_{Z}=\\dfrac{10-3}{1000}-\\dfrac{3}{2000}=7 \\times 10^{-3}-1.5 \\times 10^{-3}$<br>$ = 5.5 \\times {10^{ - 3}}\\,A = 5.5\\,\\,mA$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',2024,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/42_47116_question_1.png'),
(47117,'physics',42,323,593,'A Zener diode of breakdown voltage $10\\,V$ is used as a voltage regulator as shown in the figure. The current through the Zener diode is','[{"text": "$50\\\\,mA$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$20\\\\,mA$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$0$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$30\\\\,mA$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'<img src=\'/img/data/physics/42_47117_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />  <br>${V_i} = 20\\,V,{V_Z} = 10\\,V,{R_L} = 500\\,\\Omega ,R = 200\\,\\Omega ,$<br>As, $I=I_{Z}+I_{L}$,<br>$ \\Rightarrow {I_Z} = I - {I_L} = \\frac{{{V_i} - {V_Z}}}{R} - \\frac{{{V_z}}}{{{R_L}}}$<br>$ = \\frac{{20 - 10}}{{200}} - \\frac{{10}}{{500}}$<br>$ \\Rightarrow {I_Z} = \\frac{1}{{20}} - \\frac{1}{{50}} = \\frac{{5 - 2}}{{100}} = \\frac{3}{{100}}\\,A = 0.03\\,A$<br>$ \\Rightarrow \\quad {I_Z} = 30 \\times {10^{ - 3}}\\,A = 30\\,mA$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2024,'JEE','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','physics/42_47117_question_1.png'),
(47128,'biology',102,772,1677,'Evolutionary biology is the','[{"text": "Study of history of life forms in the universe.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Study of history of life forms on earth.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Study of history of earth.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Study of history of a single life", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Discipline of biology concerned with the processes and patterns of biological evolution especially in relation to the diversity of organisms and how they change over time.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
