(47180,'biology',102,773,1678,'An alternative name of Cosmozoic Theory is','[{"text": "Abiotic theory", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Big-Bang theory", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Panspermia theory", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Chemical evolution theory", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'The cosmogonic hypothesis is additionally called a theory of panspermia.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(47181,'biology',102,773,1678,'Theory of evolution by natural selection was proposed by','[{"text": "Darwin", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Aristotle", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Lamarck", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Hugo devries", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Theory of evolution by natural selection was first formulated by Charles Darwin.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(47182,'biology',102,773,1678,'The fitness according to Darwin is','[{"text": "Physical fitness", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Reproductive fitness", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Emotional fitness", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "All of these", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'reproductive fitness Darwin\'s fitness refers only to reproductive fitness. It is the capacity of a variant type of organism to invade and displace the resident population in competition for the available resources. The invading organism possesses suitable variations and is able to transfer these variations to the next generation because of reproductive fitness. According to him, those organisms who are better fit in an environment, produce more progeny than others. These organisms will survive more and hence get selected by nature. This will in turn increase their progeny in the successive generations in a population. He called it as natural selection and implied it as a mechanism of evolution.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(47183,'biology',102,773,1678,'Charles Darwin concluded that<br>i. Existing living forms share similarities to varying degrees not only among themselves but also with life forms that existed millions of years ago.<br>ii. Many such life forms do not exist anymore. There had been extinctions of different life forms in the years gone by just as new forms of life arose at different periods of history of earth.<br>iii. There has been fast evolution of life forms.','[{"text": "i and ii are correct", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "ii and iii are correct", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "iii is correct", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "All are correct", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Based on the observations during a voyage on H.M.S Beagle, Darwin concluded that existing living forms share similarities to varying degrees not only among themselves but also with the life forms that existed millions of years ago. This is because the organisms seen in the present have descended from a few common ancestors. Well-adapted individuals leave more offspring than poorly adapted individuals. According to Darwin, this fitness refers ultimately and only to reproductive fitness. So, both the statements are correct.',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(47194,'biology',102,774,1679,'Fossils are useful in','[{"text": "Studying history of organisms", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Studying extinct organisms", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both (1) and (2)", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "None of the above", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Provide important evidence for evolution and the adaptation of plants and animals to their environments. Fossil evidence provides a record of how creatures evolved and how this process can be represented by a \'tree of life\', showing that all species are related to each other.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(47184,'biology',102,773,1678,'Which one of the following is a feature postulated by Darwin?','[{"text": "Earth is about 4000 years old.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Diversity will be same in future.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "All living organsim that we see today were created as such", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Many life forms do not exist anymore", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'The four postulates presented by Darwin in On the Origin of Species by Means of Natural Selection, or the Preservation of Favored Races in the Struggle for Life (eventually shortened to On the Origin of Species) are as follows:<br>1) Individuals within species are variable;<br>2) Some of these variations are passed on to offspring;<br>3) In every generation, more offspring are produced than can survive; and<br>4) The survival and reproduction of individuals are not random; the individuals who survive and go on to reproduce the most are those with the most favorable variation, and they are naturally selected. It follows logically from these that the characteristics of the population will change with each subsequent generation until the population becomes distinctly different from the original; this process is known as evolution.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(47185,'biology',102,773,1678,'All organisms share the same genetic code. This statement is in evidence that','[{"text": "All the organisms looks similar", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "The evolution which has occured was very fast", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "The evolution occurs gradually", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "All the organisms are descended from a common ancestor", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'The genetic code provides evidence for the theory of evolution because It is nearly the same among all organisms Closely related species have more similar DNA than species that are distantly related. Amino acids are always coded for by (he same sequence of nitrogenous bases (such GGU coding for glycine).',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(47186,'biology',102,773,1678,'Darwinian concept of survival of fittest suggests that those who are better fit in an environment,','[{"text": "Leave more progeny than others", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Leave less progeny than others", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Does not affect the rate of reproduction", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Leave highly reduced progeny than others", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Theory of natural selection was put forward by Charles Darwin. The theory suggests that those who are better fit in an environment are hence selected by nature. This leads to the mechanism of evolution.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(47187,'biology',102,773,1678,'Alfred Wallace, a_____ worked in_____','[{"text": "Naturalist, Galapagos Island", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Naturalist, Malay Archipelago", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Botanist, Malay Archipelago", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Sailor, Malay Archipelago", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Alfred Wallace worked in Malay Archipelago. He came to similar conclusions around the same time as Darwin. He played a pivotal role in developing the theory of natural selection. But over time, Charles Darwin became almost universally thought of as the father of evolution. So, the correct answer is \'Malay Archipelago\'.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(47188,'biology',102,773,1678,'The theory of natural selection given by Charles Darwin was further supported by','[{"text": "Lamarck", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Alfred Wallace", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Darwin", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Oparian and Haldane", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Darwin and a scientific contemporary of his, Alfred Russell Wallace, proposed that evolution occurs because of a phenomenon called natural selection.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(47189,'biology',102,773,1678,'Darwin and Wallace were able to draw similar conclusions. They were','[{"text": "All existing life forms share similarities and common ancestors", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "The ancestors of existing life forms were present at different periods in the history of earth", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "The geological history of earth correlates with the biological history of earth", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "All of them", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'British naturalist Alfred Russel Wallace worked on a diversity of plants and animals of Malay Archipelago, reached at the same conclusion as Darwin. Wallace\'s book, "Contributions to the Theory of Natural Selection", was published in 1870.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(47190,'biology',102,773,1678,'Which of the following statements are presented correctly? i. Alfred Wallace, a naturalist- Malay Archipelago<br>ii. Charles Darwin -HMS Beagle Galapogas Island','[{"text": "Only i is correct", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Only ii is correct", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both are correct", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both are incorrect", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Alfred Wallace, a naturalist-Malay Archipelago Charles Darwin-HMS beagle Galapagos Island',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(47191,'biology',102,774,1679,'Evidence that evolution of life forms has indeed taken place on earth has come from','[{"text": "Fossil studies (palaeontological evidences)", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Morphological and comparative anatomical studies", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Biochemical studies", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "All of the above", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Life began on Earth at least 3.5 to 4 billion years ago, and it has been evolving ever since. Darwin stated the theory of evolution by natural selection, fossil studies, morphological and comparative anatomical. Studies and as well as biochemical studies presenting a great deal of evidence to support his theory. Evolution is a change in the characteristics of living things over time.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(47192,'biology',102,774,1679,'A fossil is a','[{"text": "Stuffed animal", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Remains of hard parts of life-forms found in rocks", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Inorganic relic of the past", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Laboratory preserved animal", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Fossils is the remains or traces of any organism that lived in the geological past.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(47193,'biology',102,774,1679,'A study of fossils in different sedimentary layers indicates','[{"text": "Historical period in which they existed", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Geological period in which they existed", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Only extinct organisms", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "All of the above", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'A study of fossils in different sedimentary layers indicates the geological period in which they existed. This study shows that life-forms varied over time and certain life forms are restricted to certain geological time-spans. Hence new forms of life have arisen at different times in the history of Earth.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(47215,'biology',102,774,1679,'Evolutionary history of an organism is also known as','[{"text": "Phylogeny", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Paleontology", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Ontogeny", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Phycology", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Phylogeny is the origin and diversification of any taxon or the evolutionary history of its origin and diversification.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(47195,'biology',102,774,1679,'Age of fossils in the past was generally determined by radio-carbon method and other methods involving radioactive elements found in the rocks. More precise methods, which were used recently and led to the revision of the evolutionary period for different groups of organisms, includes','[{"text": "Study of carbohydrates / proteins in fossils", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Study of the conditions of fossilization", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Electron Spin Resonance (ESR) and fossil DNA", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Study of carbohydrates / proteins in rocks.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Electron spin resonance (ESR) has been used for absolute dating of archaeological materials such as quartz, and fossil remains. The dating range is dependent on the nature and state of conservation of the sample and the surrounding environment but is between a few thousands and a couple of million years. This method is very definite one.',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(47196,'biology',102,774,1679,'Fossils in the earth support evolution. Which of the following sentences support the following line?','[{"text": "A study of fossils in different sedimentary layers indicates the geological period in which the organism existed", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "They represent extinct organisms (e.g., Dinosaurs)", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "The study showed that life-forms varied over time and certain life forms are restricted to certain geological timespans.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "All of them", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Fossils are the preserved remains of previously living organisms or their traces, dating from the distant past. The fossil record is not, alas, complete or unbroken: most organisms never fossilize, and even the organisms that do fossilize are rarely found by humans. Nonetheless, the fossils that humans have collected offer unique insights into evolution over long timescales. Fossils document the existence of now-extinct species, showing that different organisms have lived on Earth during different periods of the planet\'s history. They can also help scientists reconstruct the evolutionary histories of present-day species.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(47197,'biology',102,774,1679,'Rocks form sediments and a cross-section of earth\'s crust indicates<br>(I) The arrangement of sediments one over the other during the long history of earth.<br>(II) It contains fossils of different life-forms who probably died during the formation of the particular sediment.','[{"text": "Only I is correct", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Only II is correct", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both are correct", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Irrelevant statement", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Sedimentary rocks cover most of the Earth\'s rocky surface, but they only make up a very small percentage of the planet\'s crust. They often contains fossils of plants and animals millions of years old. Limestone is often made from the fossilized remains of ocean life that died millions of years ago. These rocks form layers called strata which can often be seen in exposed cliffs.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(47198,'biology',102,774,1679,'Mark the incorrect statements about the evolution of life forms on the behalf of fossil records?','[{"text": "New life forms have arisen at different times in the history of earth.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Diversity was same since creation and will be same in future also", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Some life forms are restricted to certain geological periods", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Life forms varied over the period of time", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Due to mutation or many other factors, variations happens and diversity is increasing and it will increase in future too.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(47199,'biology',102,774,1679,'Identify the correct statement<br>1. Rocks form sediments and a cross-section of earth\'s crust indicates the arrangement of sediments one over the other during the long history of earth.<br>2.Different-aged rock sediments contain living remains of different life-forms who probably died during the formation of the particular sediment.<br>3. All of them appear similar to modern organisms','[{"text": "1 is correct only", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "1 and 2 are correct", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "2 and 3 are correct", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "All are correct", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'1 . Rocks form sediments and a cross-section of earth\'s crust indicates the arrangement of sediments one over the other during the long history of earth.<br>2. Different-aged rock sediments contain fossils of different life-forms who probably died during the formation of the particular sediment.<br>3. Some of them appear similar to modern organisms',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(47200,'biology',102,774,1679,'Palaeontological evidences are the evidences from:','[{"text": "Rocks which form sediments and a crosssection of earth\'s crust indicates the arrangement of sediments one over the other during the long history of earth", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "A study of fossils in different sedimentary layers indicates the geological period in which they existed.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Distribution of plants and animals which are present today", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both 1 and 2", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Paleontological evidence include the fossils of extinct organisms as well as the fossil records of biological activity .',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(47201,'biology',102,774,1679,'Age of evolution of man is measured by','[{"text": "Electron microscope", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Chemical reaction", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Ultraviolet Radiation", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Radioactive dating", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Radio dating is a technique used to date materials such as rocks or carbon. This method is also used find the age of fossilized life.',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(47202,'biology',102,774,1679,'The preserved fossil remains of Archaeopteryx show that','[{"text": "Reptiles gave rise to birds during Permian periods", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "It was flying reptile in the Triassic period", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Reptiles gave rise to birds during Jurassic period", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "It was a flying reptile in the Permian period", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Archaeopteryx is an earliest bird which is extinct now. It is considered to be missing link between reptiles and birds because it has the characters of both reptile and bird.',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(47203,'biology',102,774,1679,'<i>Archaeopteryx</i> lived in Mesozoic era is a connecting link between_______','[{"text": "Bird and reptile", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Bird and mammal", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Reptile and amphibia", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Reptile and fish", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Archaeopteryx is the bird like dinosaurs which lived in late Jurassic period (Mesozoic era). It was the first primitive bird.',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(47204,'biology',102,774,1679,'Identify the following dinosaur.','[{"text": "Branchiosaurus", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Stegosaurus", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Archeopteryx", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Triceratops", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Stegosaurus',NULL,'Easy','MCQ type-3',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','biology/102_47204_question_1.png'),
(47205,'biology',102,774,1679,'Which of the following animals show characters of bird and reptiles?','[{"text": "A", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "B", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{C}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "D", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Monotremes are mammals but they lay eggs instead of giving birth to live young, like marsupials and placental mammals. Monotremes are similar to reptilia in having shoulder girdle and egg. Although they share some reptilian features, Monotremes possess all the major mammalian characteristics, like air breathing, endothermy (i.e., they are warmblooded), mammary glands, a furred body, a single bone in the lower jaw and three bones in the middle ear.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ type-3',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','biology/102_47205_question_1.png'),
(47206,'biology',102,774,1679,'A family tree of dinosaurs and their living modern day counterpart organisms like crocodiles and birds are given below. Identify 1,2, 3 and 4.','[{"text": "1 - Stegosaurus,2 - Brachiosaurus, 3 - Tyrannosaurus,4 - Triceratops", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "1 - Triceratops,2 - Tyrannosaurus, 3 - Stegosaurus, 4 - Brachiosaurus", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "1 - Stegosaurus, 2 - Triceratops, 3 - Brachiosaurus, 4 - Tyrannosaurus", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "1 - Triceratops, 2 - Brachiosaurus, 3 - Stegosaurus, 4 - Tyrannosaurus", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'1 - Triceratops, 2 - Tyrannosaurus, 3 - Stegosaurus,<br>4 - Brachiosaurus',NULL,'Easy','MCQ type-3',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','biology/102_47206_question_1.png'),
(47207,'biology',102,774,1679,'Embryological support for evolution was given by','[{"text": "Charles Darwin", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Karl Haeckel", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Urey and Miller", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Ernst Haeckel", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Ernst Haeckel -Embryological support for evolution was proposed by Ernst Haeckel.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(47208,'biology',102,774,1679,'Recapitulation theory / Biogenetic law was proposed by______','[{"text": "Charles Darwin", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Urey and Miller", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Ernst Haeckel", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Karl Haeckel", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'The biogenetic law is a hypothesis of improvement and advancement proposed by Ernst Haeckel in Germany during the 1860s. It is one of a few summarization speculations, which set that the phases of advancement for a creature fetus are equivalent to other creatures\' grown-up stages.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(47209,'biology',102,774,1679,'Which theory states Ontogeny repeats Phylogeny?','[{"text": "Recapitulation theory", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Biogenetic law", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both 1 and 2", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Bioinformatics law", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Haeckel formulated his theory as "Ontogeny recapitulates phylogeny". The notion later became simply known as the recapitulation theory. Ontogeny is the growth (size change) and development (structure change) of an individual organism; phylogeny is the evolutionary history of a species.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(47210,'biology',102,774,1679,'Who disapproved the Recapitulation theory by careful study?','[{"text": "Charles Darwin", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Karl Ernst von Baer", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Karl Haeckel", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Ernst Haeckel", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Von Baer\'s laws, published in 1828, responded to Johann Friedrich Heckel\'s recapitulation theory. Heckel worked at the University of Halle in Halle, Germany. In 1808, Heckel published his recapitulation theory. In his text, Heckel claimed that throughout ontogeny, embryos pass through successive stages that represent the adult forms of less complex organisms. He said that more complex organisms went through developmental stages that chronologically replayed the scala naturae, a hierarchical system of classification that places the least complex organisms on the bottom of the classification and the more complex organisms on the top. Later, Heckel\'s theory was termed the Heckel-Serres law, because Antoine Étienne Reynaud Augustin Serres, a physician in France, independently published a similar theory in 1821.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(47211,'biology',102,774,1679,'Karl Ernst von Baer noted that','[{"text": "Embryos never pass through the adult stages of other animals", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Adults never pass through the embryo stages of other animals", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Embryos always pass through the adult stages of other animals", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "None of the above", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Von Baer\'s laws of embryology are four rules discovered by Karl Ernst von Baer to explain the observed pattern of embryonic development in different species.<br>Charles Robert Darwin (February 12, 1809, to April 19, 1882) was a naturalist and biologist known for his theory of evolution and the process of natural selection.<br>Ernst Haeckel known for the famous statement "ontogeny recapitulates phylogeny", he also invented many words commonly used by biologists today, such as phylum, phylogeny, and ecology.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(47212,'biology',102,774,1679,'Presence of gills in the tadpole of frog indicates that the','[{"text": "Frogs will have gills in the embryo only", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Frogs evolved from gilled ancestors", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Gills are a respiratory organ", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Fishes evolved from frog-like ancestors", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Presence of gills in the tadpole of frog indicates that frogs evolved from gilled ancestors. Tadpoles look like fish, and they breathe under the water using gills, as fish do.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(47213,'biology',102,774,1679,'Embryological support for evolution given by Ernst Haeckel includes the','[{"text": "Presence of gill slits all over the body in adult human", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Absence of vestigial gill slits in invertebrates\'s embryo", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Absence of vestigial gill slits in human\'s embryo", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Presence of row of vestigial slits in embryo of all vertebrates behind the head", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Recapitulation theory says that embryological development repeats path of evolution. Gill slits are present in the fishes and the aquatic amphibians and from them gradually the reptiles and mammals have evolved.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(47214,'biology',102,774,1679,'In the developmental history of mammalian heart, it is observed that it passes through a two chambered fish like heart, three chambered frog like heart and finally four chambered stage. To which hypothesis can the above cited statement be approximated?','[{"text": "Lamarcks principle", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Biogenetic law", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Hardy -Weinberg law", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Mendelian principles", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'According to biogenetic law, during the development of animal heart it passes through ancestral adult stages.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(47217,'biology',102,774,1679,'Recapitulation theory is suggested by one of the following','[{"text": "The young one of bird is ammonotelic but the adult is uricotelic", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Human embryo and adult have hair", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "The tadpole gills for respiration like fishes but adult frog has no gills", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both (1) and (3)", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Theory of recapitulation also called biogenetic law or embryological parallelism. It is a theory which tied embryology with evolution. It stated that before they are born, organisms pass through developmental stages that look like adult animals of other species.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(47218,'biology',102,774,1679,'Which of the following statement is correct?','[{"text": "Gills are organs of secretion", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "There is no evidence of existence of gills during embryogenesis of mammals", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "All plants cells and animal cells are totipotent", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Ontogeny recapitulates phylogeny", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Ontogeny repeats Phylogeny is phrase coined by Ernst Haeckel. It means that development of an organism (ontogeny) expresses all the intermediate forms of its ancestors throughout evolution.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(47219,'biology',102,774,1679,'Which of the following is true?','[{"text": "Human hands and bats wings are analogous organs", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Flipper of seal and wing of birds are homologous organs", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Wings of birds and insects are homologous organs", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Human hands and birds wings are analogous organs", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Flipper of seal and wing of birds are homologous organs i.e they are having same structure but different function. Both the flipper of seal and wings of birds are modified limbs, but Flippers are helpful in swimming while the wings are meant for flying.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(47220,'biology',102,774,1679,'Divergent evolution gives rise to','[{"text": "Homologous organs", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Analogous organs", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both (1) and (2)", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "None of these", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Divergent evolution leads to homologous structures/organs. Improvement of various practical structures from a typical familial shape because of their movement to various natural surroundings and adaption to new prerequisites is called disparate or versatile radiation.<br>For instance - Darwin\'s finches of the Galapagos Islands had regular precursors however now have distinctive kinds of adjusted bills as per their nourishment propensity.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(47221,'biology',102,774,1679,'Which option is not an example of analogous organs?','[{"text": "Wings of butterfly and birds", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Eyes of octopus and mammals", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Onion and ginger", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Potato and sweet potato", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Thorns of bougainvilla and tendril of Cucurbita as well as onion and ginger do not have the same function and thus, could not be called as analogous organs.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(47222,'biology',102,774,1679,'Tendril of &nbsp;pea and tendril of cucurbits bougainvilla is an example of','[{"text": "Vestigial organs", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Analogous organs", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Homologus organs", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Atavism", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Tendril of cucurbits pea and tendril of cucurbits bougainvilla is an example of Analogous organs',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(47223,'biology',102,774,1679,'Which one among the following is an example for homology?','[{"text": "Roots of sweet potato and potato", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Wings of butterfly and birds", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Flippers of penguins and dolphins", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Hearts and brains of vertebrate", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Vertebrate\'s hearts and brains are the examples of homologous organ.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(47224,'biology',102,774,1679,'The following diagram represents homologous organ, which part of the plant represents homology?','[{"text": "Root tendrils", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Leaf tendrils", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Stem tendrils", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Spine tendrils", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Homology indicates common ancestry. In plants also, the thorn and tendrils of Bougainvillea and Cucurbita represents homology.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ type-3',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','biology/102_47224_question_1.png'),
(47225,'biology',102,774,1679,'What does presence of homologous organs in different animals indicate?','[{"text": "Different ancestry", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Common ancestry", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Sometimes different ancestry", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Sometimes common but finally different ancestry", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'The presence of homologous organs in different groups of animals indicates their common origin or ancestry and degree of closeness to the difference among various groups.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(47226,'biology',102,774,1679,'How many examples in the list given below represent homology?<br>(i) Forelimbs of cheetah and human<br>(ii) Thorn of bougainvillea and tendril of cucurbita<br>(iii) Eyes of octopus and mammals<br>(iv) Brain of vertebrates <br>(v) Tendrils of cucurbita and pea<br>(vi) Sweet potato and potato','[{"text": "Two", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Three", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Four", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Five", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Homology indicates Common ancestry. Other examples are vertebrate hearts or brains. In plants also, the thorn and tendrils of Bougainvillea and Cucurbita represent Homology ( Homology is based on divergent evolution whereas Analogy refers to a situation exactly opposite. Wings of butterfly and of birds Look alike. They are not anatomically<br>Similar structures though they perform Similar functions. Hence, analogous Structures are a result of convergent Evolution – different structures evolving. For the same function and hence having Similarity. Other examples of analogy are The eye of the octopus and of mammals Or the flippers of Penguins and Dolphins. One can say that it is the similar habitat That has resulted in selection of similar Adaptive features in different groups of Organisms but towards the same function: Sweet potato (root modification) and Potato (stem modification) is another Example for analogy.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(47227,'biology',102,774,1679,'Which one of the following groups of structure organs have similar function?','[{"text": "Incisors of rat, gizzard of cockroach and tube feet of starfish", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Nephridia in earthworm, Malpighian tubules in cockroach and urinary tubules in rat", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Antennae of cockroach, tympanum of frog and clitellum of earthworm", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Typhlosole in earthworm, intestinal villa in rat and contractile vacuole in&nbsp;<em>Amoeba</em>.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Nephridia in earthworm, Malpighian tubule in cockroach and urinary tubules in rat have similar functions. They are homologous organs, they have similar function (excretion of nitrogenous wastes) But differ in their origin.',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(47229,'biology',102,774,1679,'Which of the following structures are \'homologous\' from the evolutionary point of view?','[{"text": "Petals of rose and the anther of an apple blossom.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Thorn of bougainvillea and tendrils of cucurbita", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Spines of a cactus and spines of a porcupine.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Flagellum of <em>Euglena </em>and the flagella of <em>Paramecium</em>", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Both Flagellum of <em>Euglena </em>and the cilium of <em>Paramecium </em>are similar infunction (locomotion) and are Analogous to each other.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(47230,'biology',102,774,1679,'Which of the following set of structures includes only analogous organs?','[{"text": "Hind legs of horse", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Wings of butterfly, housefly and bat", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Hands of man, monkey and kangaroo", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Mandibles of cockroach, mosquito and honeybee", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Wings of butterfly, housefly, and bat perform same function but they are different anatomically.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(47231,'biology',102,774,1679,'Evolutionary convergence is characterized by','[{"text": "Development of characteristics by random mating", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Development of a common set of characteristics in group of different ancestry", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Replacement of common characteristics in different groups", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Development of dissimilar characteristics in closely related groups", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Convergent evolution is development of similar adaptive functional structures in unrelated groups of organisms. It also shows analogy.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(47232,'biology',102,774,1679,'Tendrils of cucurbita and thorns  of bougainvillea are examples of','[{"text": "Vestigial organs", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Analogous organs", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Homologus organs", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Atavism", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Thorns of Bougainvillea and tendrils of Cucurbita are examples of homologous organs. They arise from the axillary buds. They perform different types of functions. The thorn is for providing protection against the grazing animals. The tendrils help in providing support while climbing. They are anatomically similar. But the functions are different.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(47233,'biology',102,774,1679,'Which one of the following describes correctly the homologous structures?','[{"text": "Organs appearing only in embryonic stage and disappearing later in the adult", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Organs that have no function now, but had an important function in ancestors", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Organs with anatomical dissimilarities but performing same functions", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Organs with anatomical similarities but performing different functions", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Homologous organs are the organs which are similar anatomically but vary functionally.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(47234,'biology',102,774,1679,'Some statements are given for wings of bat and butterfly<br>(i) They are anatomically similar<br>(ii) They are homologus structure<br>(iii) They perform same functions<br>(iv) They perform different functions<br>(v) They are a result of convergent evolution<br>(vi) They indicate common ancestry which of the above statements are correct?','[{"text": "i, ii iii", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "i, ii, iv", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "iii, v", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "iii, v, vi", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Wing of bat and wings of butterfly should not be considered as homologous organs because they have different structure and origin but have the same function of flying so they are analogous organs.',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(47235,'biology',102,774,1679,'Which one of the following options gives one correct example each of convergent evolution and divergent evolution<br>&emsp; $\\begin{array}{*{20}{c}}\n{\\begin{array}{*{20}{l}}\n{{\\rm{Divergent}}}\n\\end{array}\\,\\begin{array}{*{20}{l}}\n{{\\rm{Evolution}}}\n\\end{array}}&{\\begin{array}{*{20}{l}}\n{{\\rm{Convergent}}}\n\\end{array}\\,\\begin{array}{*{20}{l}}\n{{\\rm{Evolution}}}\n\\end{array}}\n\\end{array}$','[{"text": "$\\\\begin{array}{l}\\n\\\\begin{array}{*{20}{c}}\\n{\\\\begin{array}{*{20}{l}}\\n{{\\\\rm{Bones}}\\\\,{\\\\rm{of}}\\\\,{\\\\rm{forelimbs}}\\\\,{\\\\rm{of}}\\\\,\\\\,}\\n\\\\end{array}}&{\\\\begin{array}{*{20}{l}}\\n{{\\\\rm{Eyes}}\\\\,{\\\\rm{of}}\\\\,{\\\\rm{octopus}}}\\n\\\\end{array}\\\\,}\\n\\\\end{array}\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\begin{array}{*{20}{c}}\\n{\\\\begin{array}{*{20}{l}}\\n{\\\\begin{array}{*{20}{l}}\\n{\\\\begin{array}{*{20}{l}}\\n{{\\\\rm{whale}}\\\\,{\\\\rm{and}}\\\\,{\\\\rm{cheetah}}}\\n\\\\end{array}}\\n\\\\end{array}}\\n\\\\end{array}}&{\\\\,\\\\,\\\\begin{array}{*{20}{l}}\\n{\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\rm{and}}\\\\,{\\\\rm{mammals}}}\\n\\\\end{array}}\\n\\\\end{array}\\n\\\\end{array}$<br><br>", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\begin{array}{l}\\n\\\\begin{array}{*{20}{c}}\\n{\\\\begin{array}{*{20}{l}}\\n{\\\\begin{array}{*{20}{l}}\\n{\\\\begin{array}{*{20}{l}}\\n{{\\\\rm{Wings}}\\\\,{\\\\rm{of}}\\\\,{\\\\rm{butterfly}}\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,}\\n\\\\end{array}}\\n\\\\end{array}}\\n\\\\end{array}}&{\\\\begin{array}{*{20}{l}}\\n{{\\\\rm{Brain}}\\\\,{\\\\rm{and}}\\\\,{\\\\rm{heart}}\\\\,{\\\\rm{of}}}\\n\\\\end{array}}\\n\\\\end{array}\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\begin{array}{*{20}{c}}\\n{\\\\begin{array}{*{20}{l}}\\n{\\\\begin{array}{*{20}{l}}\\n{\\\\begin{array}{*{20}{l}}\\n{\\\\begin{array}{*{20}{l}}\\n{{\\\\rm{and}}\\\\,{\\\\rm{sparrow}}}\\n\\\\end{array}\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,}\\n\\\\end{array}}\\n\\\\end{array}}\\n\\\\end{array}}&{\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\begin{array}{*{20}{l}}\\n{\\\\,{\\\\rm{vertebrates}}}\\n\\\\end{array}}\\n\\\\end{array}\\n\\\\end{array}$<br><br>", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\begin{array}{l}\\n\\\\begin{array}{*{20}{c}}\\n{\\\\begin{array}{*{20}{l}}\\n{\\\\begin{array}{*{20}{l}}\\n{\\\\begin{array}{*{20}{l}}\\n{\\\\begin{array}{*{20}{l}}\\n{{\\\\rm{Potato}}\\\\,{\\\\rm{and}}\\\\,{\\\\rm{sweet}}}\\n\\\\end{array}\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,}\\n\\\\end{array}}\\n\\\\end{array}}\\n\\\\end{array}}&{\\\\begin{array}{*{20}{l}}\\n{{\\\\rm{Thorn}}\\\\,{\\\\rm{of}}\\\\,{\\\\rm{bougainvillea}}\\\\,{\\\\rm{and}}}\\n\\\\end{array}}\\n\\\\end{array}\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\begin{array}{*{20}{c}}\\n{\\\\begin{array}{*{20}{l}}\\n{\\\\begin{array}{*{20}{l}}\\n{\\\\begin{array}{*{20}{l}}\\n{\\\\begin{array}{*{20}{l}}\\n{{\\\\rm{potato}}}\\n\\\\end{array}\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,}\\n\\\\end{array}}\\n\\\\end{array}}\\n\\\\end{array}}&{\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\begin{array}{*{20}{l}}\\n{\\\\,\\\\begin{array}{*{20}{l}}\\n{\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\begin{array}{*{20}{l}}\\n{\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\rm{tendrils}}\\\\,{\\\\rm{of}}\\\\,{\\\\rm{cucurbita}}}\\n\\\\end{array}}\\n\\\\end{array}}\\n\\\\end{array}}\\n\\\\end{array}\\n\\\\end{array}$<br><br>", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\begin{array}{l}\\n\\\\begin{array}{*{20}{c}}\\n{\\\\begin{array}{*{20}{l}}\\n{\\\\begin{array}{*{20}{l}}\\n{\\\\begin{array}{*{20}{l}}\\n{\\\\begin{array}{*{20}{l}}\\n{{\\\\rm{Flippers}}\\\\,{\\\\rm{of}}\\\\,{\\\\rm{penguins}}}\\n\\\\end{array}\\\\,\\\\,}\\n\\\\end{array}}\\n\\\\end{array}}\\n\\\\end{array}}&{\\\\begin{array}{*{20}{l}}\\n{{\\\\rm{Wings}}\\\\,{\\\\rm{of}}\\\\,{\\\\rm{butterfly}}}\\n\\\\end{array}}\\n\\\\end{array}\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\begin{array}{*{20}{c}}\\n{\\\\begin{array}{*{20}{l}}\\n{\\\\begin{array}{*{20}{l}}\\n{\\\\begin{array}{*{20}{l}}\\n{\\\\begin{array}{*{20}{l}}\\n{{\\\\rm{and}}\\\\,{\\\\rm{dolphins}}}\\n\\\\end{array}\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,}\\n\\\\end{array}}\\n\\\\end{array}}\\n\\\\end{array}}&{\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\begin{array}{*{20}{l}}\\n{\\\\,\\\\begin{array}{*{20}{l}}\\n{\\\\,\\\\begin{array}{*{20}{l}}\\n{{\\\\rm{and}}\\\\,{\\\\rm{birds}}}\\n\\\\end{array}}\\n\\\\end{array}}\\n\\\\end{array}}\\n\\\\end{array}\\n\\\\end{array}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'In Convergent evolution, structures are not similar but perform similar functions resulting in analogous organs. In divergent evolution, origin of structures is same but it performs different functions which results in homologous organs.',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(47236,'biology',102,774,1679,'Some examples are given below:<br>(a) Hearts of mammals<br>(b) Thorn of bougainvillea and thorn of <i>Opuntia</i><br>(c) Wings of birds and butterfly<br>(d) Eyes of the octopus and mammals<br>(e) Flippers of penguin and dolphins<br>(f) Ginger and potato<br>Which of these are examples of analogous structures in animals?','[{"text": "a, b, c", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "b,c,d", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "c,d,e", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "d,e,f", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Some examples of analogous structures are wings of birds and butterflies, flippers of Penguins and Dolphins, eyes of the octopus and of mammals, sweet potato.',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(47237,'biology',102,774,1679,'Some of the evidences of organic evolution are','[{"text": "Occurrence of analogous and vestigial organs", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Occurrence of homologous and analogous organs in different animals", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Occurrence of only analogous organs in different animals", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Occurrence of homologous and vestigial organs in different animals", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Evidences are only way which can prove all proposed theories of evolution so far.Evidences are fossils, comparative anatomy and embryo development pattern. Comparative anatomy explains about the organisms evolved as a result of natural selection or genetic drift and organisms having same ancestors. Two types of comparative ancestors are homologous and analogous organs.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(47238,'biology',102,774,1679,'Identify the correct statement','[{"text": "Paired fins of fish and flippers of whale are analogous", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Wings of birds and insects are homologous", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Caecum and appendix in rabbit and man are analogous", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "<i>Archaeopteryx lithographica</i> is missing link between birds and mammals", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Fins of fishes & flippers of whales are functionally similar.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(47239,'biology',102,774,1679,'Phylloclade and thorn of <i>Opuntia</i> is an example of','[{"text": "Vestigial organs", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Analogous organs", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Homologus organs", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "None of the above", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Another type of modification seen is thorn, seen in citrus plants. Phylloclade is also a type of stem modification where the flattened leaf-like structures are formed, which helps in photosynthesis, examples are cactus opuntia.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(47240,'biology',102,774,1679,'The similar habitat that has resulted in selection of similar adaptive features in different groups of organisms but towards the same function. It explains','[{"text": "Divergent evolution", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Convergent evolution", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Adaptive evolution", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Chemical evolution", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'One can say that it is the similar habitat that has resulted in selection of similar adaptive features in different groups of organisms but towards the same function. An example of convergent evolution is the similar nature of the flight/ wings of insects, birds, pteridosaurs, and bats.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(47241,'biology',102,774,1679,'The following statements guide us to a feature<br>I. Similarities in proteins and genes performing a given function among diverse organisms.<br>II. The biochemical similarities as structural similarities among diverse organisms.','[{"text": "Uncommon ancestry", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Extinct ancestry", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Divergent ancestry", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Common ancestry", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Similarities in proteins and genes performing a given function among diverse organisms give clue to common ancestry.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(47242,'biology',102,774,1679,'Change in peppered moths support','[{"text": "Natural selection acts on favourable variations which appear among individuals.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Environment has a role in evolution", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Heritable variations arise due to the changes in the gene complex", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Acquired characters during life of an individual are inherited.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Organism provided with favourable variations will succeed in struggle for existence and fitness of organism for survival is decidedby Natural selection. Here the Selected organism with favourable variations survives. Variation in peppered moth turned to be an useful variation as it helped the moth to camouflage on tree bark.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(47243,'biology',102,774,1679,'Example of industrial melanism','[{"text": "<i>Archaeopteryx</i>", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Darwins Finches", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "<i>Tyrannosaurus</i> rex", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Peppered moth", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Industrial Melanism refers to the evolution of dark body colour in animal species that live in habitats blackened by industrial soot. Peppered moth is the good example.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(47244,'biology',102,774,1679,'In the post industrialisation period in England:','[{"text": "Melanised moths were more in number than white-winged moths.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "White-winged moths were more in number than melanised moths.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both had an equal number.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both became extinct.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Many examples were given to describe natural selection<br>(a) Industrial melanism<br>(b) Antibiotics or drug resistance<br>(c) Herbicide resistance',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(47245,'biology',102,774,1679,'There were more white-winged moths on trees than dark-winged or melanised moths during ____  period in England.','[{"text": "1850 s, i.e., after industrialisation set in", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "1920, i.e., before industrialisation set in", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "1850 s, i.e., before industrialisation set in", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "1920, i.e., after industrialisation set in", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'In a collection of moths made in 1850 s,i.e., before industrialisation set in, it was observed that there were more white-winged moths on trees than dark-winged or melanised moths.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(47246,'biology',102,774,1679,'Why did the white winged moth survived compared to dark-coloured moth before industrial revolution?','[{"text": "Thin growth of almost white-coloured lichen covered the trees", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Thick growth of almost white-coloured lichen covered the trees", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Thick growth of almost white-coloured mycorrhizae covered the trees", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Thin growth of almost white-coloured mycorrhizae covered the trees", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'As the trees darkened with soot, the lightcoloured moths were easier to see. They were eaten by birds more and more, while the rare dark coloured moths blended in better on the darker trees.This made the dark coloured moths have a higher survival rate.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(47247,'biology',102,774,1679,'The observations about the number of white winged and dark-winged moths in England during different environmental conditions shows that:','[{"text": "Predators can\'t spot a moth against contrasting background", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "No variant is completely wiped out", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "The areas where industrialisation did not occur, the count of melanic moths was high", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Industrialisation did not have any effect on the number of moths", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Due to industrialization, the air pollution increased and lichens growth is declined so the black colored moths are well camouflaged themselves into the bark of trees that are covered by the industrial pollution and reproduce but white peppered moth are visible and more visible to predators so naturally declined. This is caused as humans evolve themselves but that increase the pollution and lead to a wrong or negative way for the moth.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(47248,'biology',102,774,1679,'The process of industrial melanism demonstrates','[{"text": "Adaptive radiation", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Artificial selection", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Natural selection", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Geographical isolation", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'The process of industrial melanism demostrates natural selection.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(47249,'biology',102,774,1679,'Which one is an example of evolution by anthropogenic action?','[{"text": "Industrial melanism", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Selection of herbicide resistant varieties of weeds", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Selection of antibiotic resistant microbes on a petri dish", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "All of these", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Two examples of evolution by anthropogenic action are industrial melanism, overuse of weedicides and pesticides.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(47251,'biology',102,774,1679,'Which is not an example of anthropogenic evolution','[{"text": "Excess use of herbicides, pesticides", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Drug resistant eukaryotic organisms", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Industrial melanism of <i>Biston betularia</i>", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Industrial melanism of <i>Darwins finches</i>", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Australian marsupials within Australian island Anthropogenic action are the activities that are produced or caused by humans, used in a negative way. Two examples of evolution by anthropogenic action are industrial melanism, overuse of weedicides and pesticides.<br>Adaptive radiation of marsupials in Australia, convergent evolution of placental mammals in Australia, evolution of finches in Galapagos islands are examples of evolution due to environmental changes and do not involve human intervention.<br>Herbicide resistant weeds, drug resistant eukaryotes and man-created breeds of domesticated animals like dogs are examples of evolution by anthropogenic action.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(47252,'biology',102,774,1679,'Evolution is','[{"text": "Discontinuous process", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Stochastic process", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both (1) and (2)", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Non-essential process", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Abstract Evolution is a stochastic process, resulting from a combination of deterministic and random factors. We present results from a general theory of directional evolution that reveals how random variation in fitness, heritability, and migration influence directional evolution.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(47253,'biology',102,774,1679,'Evolution by anthropogenic action tells us','[{"text": "Evolution is not a directed process in the sense of determinism.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "It is a non- stochastic process based on chance events in nature and chance mutation in the organisms.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "It is a stochastic process based on chance events in nature and chance mutation in the organisms.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both 1 and 3", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Anthropogenic actions refer to the activities that are caused by human beings. Anthropogenic action also referred to as human activities has commenced to the evolution of a number of new species in a short time scale. This hastens the method of evolution.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(47254,'biology',102,774,1679,'In rural areas, the count of melanic moths was low even post 1920. How did it happen?','[{"text": "Melanic moths were not able to camouflage themselves as there was no pollution in rural areas.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Melanic moths changed their location", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Melanic moths started hiding in pit holes.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "None of them", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'In 1920, there were more dark-winged moths in The same area, i.e., the proportion was reversed.The explanation put forth for this observation was that \'predators will Spot a moth against a contrasting background\'. During post-Industrialisation period, the tree trunks became dark due to industrial Smoke and soot\'s. Under this condition the white-winged moth did not Survive due to predators, dark-winged or melanised moth survived.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(47255,'biology',102,774,1679,'The flowering insect represents a particular phenomenon in England. Identify the correct option.','[{"text": "Mutation", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Natural selection", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Adaptive radiation", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Convergent evolution", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Natural election comes from England.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ type-3',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','biology/102_47255_question_1.png'),
(47256,'biology',102,774,1679,'Industrial melanism is an example for','[{"text": "Mutation", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Natural selection", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Artificial selection", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "All the above", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'The selection of dark coloured peppered moth in industrial area than pale forms is natural selection.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(47257,'biology',102,774,1679,'Industrial melanism is a supportive mechanism as it provides','[{"text": "Defensive adaptation of skin against ultraviolet radiations", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Drug resistance", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Darkening of skin due to smoke from industries", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Protective resemblance with the surroundings", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'The peppered moth (Biston betularia) occurs in two forms-pale colour moth and black colour moth. Before the industrial revolution occurs that is when pollution is less, the pale colour moth were common as they were not easily identified among the light coloured lichens growing on the trees. But after industries were set-up, which killed off the lichens and blackened the trees with soot. On this dark background, the pale moths were no longer well-camouflaged and were easily caught by birds so black colour moth survives and becomes common. This is an example of natural selection. As the environment selects the black colour moth so their number.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(47258,'biology',102,775,1680,'Galapagos finches are associated with','[{"text": "Lamarck", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Darwin", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Miller", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Hardy-Weinberg", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Darwin\'s finches (also known as the Galapagos finches) are a group of about fifteen species of passerine birds.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(47259,'biology',102,775,1680,'The different varieties of beaks in black birds observed by Darwin were present in:','[{"text": "Galapagos island, South America", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Australia", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "England, South America", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Galapagos island, North America", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Darwin\'s finches are small birds which were studied by Darwin on Galapagos island. The original ancestral species of Darwin\'s finch were seed eating types. As time passed competition arose among the individuals due to food scarcity and an increased population of birds. The food available in different niches were different like fruits, cactus, insects, etc. In order to utilise the food and reduce competition, many other forms with altered beaks arose from the original seed eating variety.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(47260,'biology',102,775,1680,'The development of various functional structures from a common ancestral form is called','[{"text": "Chemical evolution", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Adaptive radiation", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Non-adaptive radiation", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Mutational evolution", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Development of different functional structures from a common ancestral form is called adaptive radiation.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(47261,'biology',102,775,1680,'The process by which different type of finches were evolved in Galapagos islands is a result of','[{"text": "Adaptive radiation", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Non -adaptive radiation", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Industrial melanism", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Convergent evolution", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Development of different functional structures from a common ancestral form is called adaptive radiation.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(47262,'biology',102,775,1680,'On Galapagos island the original finches from which other finches arose were','[{"text": "Seed-eating", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Insectivorous", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Vegetarian", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Carnivorous", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Darwin\'s finches show a variety of beaks suited for eating large seeds, flying insects and cactus seeds. Ancestral seed-eating stock of Darwin\'s finches radiated out from South American mainland to different geographical areas of the Galapagos Islands, where they found competitor for new habitats.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(47263,'biology',102,775,1680,'Which one of the following aspects of evolution is shown by Darwin\'s finches?','[{"text": "Isolation due to geographical barrier", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Industrial melanism", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Genetic evidence", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Embryological evidence", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'On his visit to the Galapagos Islands, Charles Darwin found a few types of finches that fluctuated from island to island, which helped him to build up his hypothesis of normal choice. They additionally explored developmental changes in Darwin\'s finches.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(47264,'biology',102,775,1680,'The diversity in the type of finches and adaptation to different feeding habits on the Galapagos islands, as observed by Darwin, provides an evidence of','[{"text": "Origin of species by natural selection", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Commensalism", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Predation", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Origin of species by artificial selection", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Origin of species by natural selection.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(47265,'biology',102,775,1680,'There is a difference in one morphological character in the birds. The part involved in the change is','[{"text": "Size of eye", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Shape of beak", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Size of bird size", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Size of wings.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Shape of beak from the original seed-eating features, many Other forms with altered beaks arose, enabling them to become insectivorous And vegetarian finches (Tis process of evolution of different Species in a given geographical area starting from a point and literally Radiating to other areas of geography (habitats) is called adaptive radiation.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ type-3',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','biology/102_47265_question_1.png'),
(47266,'biology',102,775,1680,'From the original seed-eating features, many other forms with altered beaks arose, enabling them to become','[{"text": "Insectivorous", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Vegetarian finches", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Tortoise", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both 1 and 2", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'From the original seed-eating features, many other forms with altered beaks arose, enabling them to become insectivorous and vegetarian finches.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(47267,'biology',102,775,1680,'Australian marsupials are the example of','[{"text": "Homologous radiation", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Analogous radiation", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Adaptive radiation", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Convergent radiation", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'In evolutionary biology, adaptive radiation is a process in which organisms diversify rapidly from an ancestral species into several new forms, particularly when a change in the environment makes new resources available, creates new challenges, or opens new environmental niches. In evolutionary biology, convergent evolution is the process whereby organisms not closely related (not monophyletic), independently evolve similar traits as a result of having to adapt to similar environments or ecological niches. Placental mammals that are present in Australia also exhibit adaptive radiation in evolving into varieties of such placental mammals each of which appears to be \'similar\' to a corresponding marsupial.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(47268,'biology',102,775,1680,'What does the given image depicts?','[{"text": "Variety of beaks of Darwins finches", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Variety of birds of African island", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Variety of beaks of finches found in Galapagos island.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both (1) and (3)", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Shape of beak. From the original sedating features, many other forms with altered beaks arose, enabling them to become insectivorous And vegetarian finches (This process of evolution of different Species in a given geographical area starting from a point and literally Radiating to other areas of geography (habitats) is called adaptive radiation.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ type-3',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','biology/102_47268_question_1.png'),
(47269,'biology',102,775,1680,'When more than one adaptive radiation appeared to have occurred in an isolated geographical area, it is','[{"text": "Convergent evolution", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Divergent evolution", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Natural selection", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Genetic drift", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Convergent evolution. Adaptive radiation refers to an evolutionary process in which different species evolve successfully to adapt to their environment on inhabiting new areas. While convergent evolution is an evolutionary process in which different structures of different species are evolved for the same function. Hence during adaptive radiation, the changes are determined by the environment. The organism evolves to survive in a particular environment. Therefore, if two different adaptive radiation occurs in an area, Then it is most likely that both the evolved species will have similar adaptations to survive in the particular area. Hence, they will develop similar structures to perform similar functions which show convergent evolution.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(47270,'biology',102,775,1680,'The classical example of adaptive radiation in development of new species is','[{"text": "Darwin\'s finches", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Marsupials of Australia", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Locomotion in mammals", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "All of the above", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'The classical example of adaptive radiation during formation of new species is marsupials of Australia, Darwin\'s finches and giant Tortoise.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(47271,'biology',102,775,1680,'Example of convergent evolution is','[{"text": "Darwin finches and marsupial mouse", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Placental wolf and Tasmanian wolf", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Lemur and Spotted cuscus", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both 2 and 3", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Both humans and octopuses have cameralike eyes with an iris, a lens and a retina - all essential parts of an imaging device. And both bats and birds have wings. As similar as these traits may appear, a closer look reveals their independent origins.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(47272,'biology',102,775,1680,'What does the following diagram represent?','[{"text": "Natural selection", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Marsupial radiation", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Placental radiation", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Mutation", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Marsupial radiation',NULL,'Easy','MCQ type-3',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','biology/102_47272_question_1.png'),
(47277,'biology',102,775,1680,'Adaptive radiation refers to','[{"text": "Power of adaptation in an individual to a variety of environment", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Migration of members of a species to different geographical areas", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Evolution of different species from a common ancestor", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Adaptation due to geographical non- isolation", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Adaptive radiation is a process in which organisms diversify rapidly from an ancestral species into a multitude of new forms, particularly when a change in the environment makes new resources available, creates new challenge',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(47278,'biology',102,775,1680,'Identify the example of adaptive radiation in development of new species','[{"text": "Elimination of <i>Biston betularia</i> and appearance of <i>Biston carbonaria</i>", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Origin of Koala bear, <i>Banded anteater</i>, Sugar glider, Tiger cat from ancestral marsupial", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Example of Dinosaurs", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Origin of mainland finch from finches on Galapagos islands", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Adaptive radiation is the evolutionary diversification from a single ancestral (prototype)populationofdescendantpopulation into more and more numerous adaptive zones and ecological niches. It may involve both Anagenesis and cladogenesis. The examples of adaptive radiations are Darwin finches of Galapagos islands, Australian marsupials and limbs of mammals.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(47279,'biology',102,775,1680,'Which among these animal groups do exist in Galapagos island?','[{"text": "Finches", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Tortoises", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Dinosaurs", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both 1 and 2", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Finches and Tortoises exist in Galapagos Island.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(47280,'biology',102,775,1680,'Adaptive radiation is an example for','[{"text": "Stabilizing selection", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Diversifying selection", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Directional selection", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "None of the above", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Adaptive radiation is the development of different functional structures from a common ancestor. Hence it is example for diversifying selection.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(47281,'biology',102,775,1680,'The diversity in the type of beaks of finches adapted to different feeding habits on Galapagos islands as observed by Darwin, provides evidence for','[{"text": "Interspecific competition", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Origin of species by natural selection", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Origin of species by mutation", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Intraspecific competition", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'When Charles Darwin went on a voyage towards the Galapagos Island he saw variations in the kind of bird called as Finches. Their beak was of different size and shape according to its different food habit. He concluded that the ancestral finches on reaching the different islands occupied empty ecological niches and in the absence of competition they evolved into different species. This provided evidences for the origin of species by natural selection.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(47282,'biology',102,775,1680,'Example of australian marsupial is','[{"text": "Wombat", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Spotted cuscus", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Lemur", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "1 and 2", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Australian marsupials can be categorised by what they eat into 3 groups: Dasyurids these are the meat-eating marsupials: quolls, the tasmanian devil, tasmanian tiger, numbats, dunnarts, antechinus. Peramelemorphs - these are the omnivorous marsupials: bilbies and bandicoots.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(47283,'biology',102,775,1680,'The given figure represents','[{"text": "Divergent evolution", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Adaptive radiation", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Convergent evolution", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Classification", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Convergent evolution.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ type-3',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','biology/102_47283_question_1.png'),
(47284,'biology',102,776,1681,'Evolution by natural selection, in a true sense would have started when cellular forms of life with differences in_______ originated on earth.','[{"text": "Chemical capability", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Metabolic capability", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Physical capability", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "None of these", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Evolution by natural selection, in a true sense would have started when cellular forms of life with differences in metabolic capability originated on earth. The essence of Darwinian theory about evolution is natural selection. The rate of appearance of new forms is linked to the life cycle or the life span',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(47285,'biology',102,776,1681,'Antibiotic resistant bacteria in a petridish subjected to a certain antibiotic is an example of','[{"text": "Adaptive radiation", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Pre-existing variation in the population", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Transformation", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Convergent evolution", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Appearance of antibodies resistant bacteria is example of pre-existing variation.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(47286,'biology',102,776,1681,'Survival of the fittest is possible due to the','[{"text": "Production of the best species", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Adaptive ability which is genetic in origin", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Adaptive ability which is non- genetic in origin", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Inheritance of acquired characters", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'When favorable variations came in the environment and one species is more fitted to that change then that $\\&$ species will be more fitted to environment and in successive generation that character transfers and it became main character of the species.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(47287,'biology',102,776,1681,'Which of the following are the two key concepts of Darwinian theory of evolution?','[{"text": "Branching descent and mutation", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Adaptive radiation and branching ascent", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Mutation and natural selection", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Branching descent and natural selection", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'According to Darwin\'s theory of evolution, nature selects the fittest. Fitness is based on characteristics which are inherited. Therefore, there must be a genetic basis for getting selected and to evolve. Some organisms are better adapted to survive in an otherwise hostile environment. Adaptive ability is inherited. It has a genetic basis. Fitness is the end result of the ability to adapt and get selected by nature. Therefore, branching descent (inheritance) and natural selection are the two key concepts of Darwin\'s theory of evolution.So, the correct answer is \'Branching descent and natural selection.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(47290,'biology',102,776,1681,'In a culture plate streptomycin resistant strain can be developed by using','[{"text": "Complete medium with streptomycin", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Minimal medium and streptomycin", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Only minimal medium", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Only complete medium", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'If an organism has the ability to produce mutant strains resistant to antibiotics, the nature of mutation, whether it is spontaneous or induced have to be tested. Replica plating technique isolates both nutritional mutants and antibiotic resistant mutants. To develop antibiotic resistant strain we need a complete medium and a antibiotic.',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(47291,'biology',102,776,1681,'Micro organisms are shifted from a culture plate (no antibiotics) to a different culture plate (with antibiotics). What conclusion can be inferred?&nbsp;','[{"text": "Already existed adaptive mutations provide selection advantage to the individuals of a population", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Already existed harmful mutations provides selection advantage to the individuals of a population", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Individuals of a population develop new characters in response to changed environment which helps in their selection", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Any change in environment doesn\'t affect the individuals of a population", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Any change in environment doesn\'t affect the individuals of a population.',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(47292,'biology',102,776,1681,'Experements involving shifting of micro organisms from culture plate (antibiotics) to culture plate (with antibiotics) indicate.','[{"text": "Characters are heritable", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Natural selection plays an important role in fixing a mutation of survival value", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Genes are independent entities", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Bacteria can multiply in culture medium", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Lederbergs replica plating experiment suggest that natural selection plays an important role in fixing mutation of survival rate.Lederberg gave an evidence that drug penicillin and streptomycin permitted preexisting mutations to express themselves.',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(47293,'biology',102,776,1681,'Select the correct statement from the following<br>I. Fitness is the start of the ability to adapt and get selected by nature.<br>II. The rate of appearance of new forms is linked to the life cycle or the life span.','[{"text": "Only I", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Only II", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both I and II", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "None of them", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'The essence of Darwinian theory about evolution is natural selection. Fitness is the end result of the ability to adapt and get selected by nature Example. A colony of bacteria growing on a given medium has built in variation in terms of ability to utilise a feed component.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(47294,'biology',102,776,1681,'Lamarck was a','[{"text": "A Russian naturalist", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "A French botanist", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "A French naturalist", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "A Russian biologist", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Jean Baptiste Lamarck (1744-1829) is one of the best-known early evolutionists. Unlike Darwin, Lamarck believed that living things evolved in a continuously upward direction, from dead matter, through simple to more complex forms, towards human "perfection." Species didn\'t die out in extinctions, Lamarck claimed. He was a French Naturalist.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(47295,'biology',102,776,1681,'Whose theory of evolution believes that every organism has an internal vital force?','[{"text": "De Vries theory", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Lamarckism", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Darwinism", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Weismann theory", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'According to Lamarckism, living organisms and their parts tend to increase in size continuously due to internal forces of life.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(47296,'biology',102,776,1681,'Lamarck explained his use and disuse theory with the example of','[{"text": "Marsupians", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Giraffee", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Finches", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Tortoise", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Lamarck use or disuse theory states that change in the environment causes changes in the needs of organisms living in that environment, which in turn causes changes in their behavior. Altered behavior leads to lesser or greater use of structures leads to enlarge or shrink. He explained it with the example of giraffe. Giraffee which were having scarcity of short plants, started stretching its necks and legs for eating the leaves on the tall trees which made its neck and legs to stretch which lead to change its neck and leg into a long neck and tall legs respectively.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(47297,'biology',102,776,1681,'The present giraffe has a long neck as compared to its ancestors. Lamarck believed it could be due to','[{"text": "Natural selection", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Branching descent", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Inheritance of acquired characters", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Mutation", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'According to Lamarck, the giraffe got its long neck because its ancestors stretched theirs to eat leaves that were just out of reach. This stretching of the neck was passed on to their offspring, over generations, until it reached its current length.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(47298,'biology',102,776,1681,'The \'use and disuse theory\' was proposed by','[{"text": "Malthus", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Francis Darwin", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Louis Pasteur", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Lamarck", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Charles Darwin proposed the principle of natural selection, August Weismann proposed the theory of continuity of germ plasm, Hugo de Vries proposed mutation theory of evolution, and Jean Baptiste de Lamarck proposed the use and disuse theory of evolution.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(47299,'biology',102,776,1681,'The phenomenon which Lamarck fails to explain is','[{"text": "Exceptionally long neck of giraffe", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "An intellectual having a dull son", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Loss of vision in cave dwelling mammals", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Absence of all the four limbs amongst Amphibians", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Some amphibians are devoid of all of the four limbs, it is explained by Lamarckism.<br>The ancestors of giraffe were bearing a small neck and fore-limbs and were like horses. But as they were living in places with no surface vegetation, they had to stretch their neck and fore-limbs to take the leaves for food, which resulted in the slight elongation of these parts, it is explained by Lamarckism.<br>The ancestors of cave dwellers had normal eye sight. On account of living under continuous dark conditions, the animal lost their power to see, it is explained by Lamarckism.<br>However, Dull progeny of Nobel Prize winners cannot be explained by Lamarckism. It could not explain that why the kids of Nobel Laureates have dull children.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(47300,'biology',102,776,1681,'Who said that evolution of the life forms has occurred but driven by use and disuse of organs:','[{"text": "Darwin - French naturalist", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Lamarck - French naturalist", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "De Vries - French naturalist", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Malthus - French naturalist", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'The biological evolution theory of use and disuse of organs was propounded by Lamarck. $\\mathrm{He}$ is a French naturalist who gave the theory of evolution. His theory is also known as Lamarckism.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(47301,'biology',102,776,1681,'Which one of the following is not a concept of Lamarck?','[{"text": "If an organ is used constantly it will continuously increase its size", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Environmental pressure causes variation", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Rate and survival of organism is different due to variation", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Inheritance of acquired characters", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Rate and survival of organism is different due to variation is not a concept of Lamarckism.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(47302,'biology',102,776,1681,'Lamarckism was not well accepted at todays time because','[{"text": "His mechanism lack reasonable evidences", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Organisms pass down traits through predetermined genetic information, not based on environmental adaptations during their lifetime.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Weismanns experiment disproved his theories", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "All the above", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Because it was tested and found to be wrong. Same reason chemists don\'t accept the phlogiston theory of fire. Or astronomers the geocentric theory of the Solar System. Or doctors the "bad air" theory of malaria. It\'s not accepted because it was wrong, and demonstrated as such.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(47303,'biology',102,776,1681,'Lamarckism states that','[{"text": "Organism can pass on characteristics that it has acquired during its lifetime to its offspring", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Individuals lose characteristics if they do not require or use", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Acquired traits were heritable", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "All the above", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Lamarckism, a theory of evolution based on the principle that physical changes in organisms during their lifetime - such as greater development of an organ or a part through increased use - could be transmitted to their offspring.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(47304,'biology',102,776,1681,'Lamarckism cannot explain','[{"text": "Heterophylly", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Webbed toes in aquatic birds", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Weak muscles in the son of a wrestler", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Long narrow and limbless body of snakes", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Lamarckian theory is also called as theory of inheritance of acquired characters or use and disuse theory. These theories cannot explain weak muscles in the son of a wrestler .',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(47305,'biology',102,776,1681,'According to Lamarckism, long necked Giraffes evolved because','[{"text": "Humans preferred only long necked ones", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Short necks suddenly changed into long necks", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Nature selected only long necked ones", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Stretching of necks over many generation by short necked ones", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Lamarckian theory suggested that Short necked giraffe started stretching their neck to eat the leaves on the tree and as a result of this, after generations long necked giraffe evolved.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(47306,'biology',102,776,1681,'Which is not a basic concept of Lamarckism?','[{"text": "Struggle for existence", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Use and disuse of organs", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Inheritance of aquired characters", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Effect of environment and new needs", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Lamarckism never explains about variation in due course of time and survival of organism. It explains about the characteristics acquired by animals as a result of continuous use of the physical trait.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(47307,'biology',102,776,1681,'Darwin could not explain the:','[{"text": "Arrival of fittest", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Inheritance of variations", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Origin of variations", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "1 and 3", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Darwin had no knowledge of genetics and principles of inheritance. Hence, he could not explain the origin of variations in animals and plants.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(47308,'biology',102,776,1681,'Theory of natural selection of Darwin was based on','[{"text": "Variations, which are inherited will give rise to new species", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Good traits are preserved", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Prodigility of reproduction, mass production of species, large number of birth rate and survival of the fittest.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Mass conservation of mutations.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Darwins theory of natural selection is based on over production, struggle for existence, variations and survival of the fittest.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(47309,'biology',102,776,1681,'Darwin explains all the following except','[{"text": "Organisms tend to produce more number of offspring than can survive", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Within each species there are variations", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Offspring with better traits that overcome competition are best suited for the environment", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Variations may or may not be inherited from parents to offspring through genes", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Darwin\'s theory cannot explain the inheritance of variations from parents to offsprings through the genes. Darwin didn\'t have any idea regarding the genes nor genetics. It was gregor mendel who discovered the basic principles of heredity.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(47310,'biology',102,776,1681,'Natural selection is based on all of the following except','[{"text": "Individuals must adapt to their environment", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "The fittest individuals leave the most offspring", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Variation exists within populations", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Populations tend to produce more offspring than the environment can support", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'According to natural selection, if the individual is fit it gets adapts. Only the best adapted individual survives if not then it gets eliminated.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(47311,'biology',102,776,1681,'Which statement is correct?','[{"text": "Darwinian theory - Use and disuse theory", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Lamarck theory - Struggle for existence", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Darwinian theory - Survival of the fittest", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Lamarck theory - Theory of continuity of germplasm", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Darwinian Theory explained about Overproduction, Natural Selection, Struggle for existence and survival of the fittest.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(47312,'biology',102,776,1681,'Darwin differed from Lamarck in his proposal that','[{"text": "Evolution leads to adaptation", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Species are not fixed", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Life on earth has a long evolutionary history", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Inherent variations in the population are more important in evolution than variation acquired during individual lifetimes", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Unlike Lamarck, who said that traits could develop and change during an animal\'s lifetime, Darwin believed that individuals were simply born with different traits and that these differences were mostly random.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(47313,'biology',102,776,1681,'The more suitable reason for the development of resistance against pesticides in insects damaging a crop is','[{"text": "Directed mutation", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Acquired heritable changes", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Random mutations", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Genetic recombination", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Genetic recombination is the production of new gene combination by cross over, independent assortment of chromosomes during gametogenesis and their random rearrangement during fertilization; it does not add new trait but only produce newcombinations of already present traits which make option A incorrect. Acquired changes are non-heritable as they are epigenetic which makes option B incorrect. First exposure to the pesticide is followed survival of small proportion of the pest population due to their distinct genetic makeup. Owing to their survival and thereby reproductive advantage, these individuals pass the genes for resistance to the next generation. Repeated uses of the pesticide lead to increased population of less-susceptible individuals. Thus, the random mutation in less proportion of pest population is favoured and pesticide resistance is developed which makes option $\\mathrm{C}$ correct. Natural mutations are always random, not directed towards a particular change. These random mutations, if provide reproductive or survival advantage to individual, are then favoured by natural selection.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(47314,'biology',102,776,1681,'Which one of the following phenomena supports Darwins concept of natural selection in organic evolution?','[{"text": "Production of Dolly the sheep by cloning", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Development of transgenic animals", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Development of organs from stem cells for organ transplantation", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Prevalence of pesticide resistant insects", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Prevalence of pesticide resistant insects supports the natural selection theory. Example: Some years ago DDT was introduced against house hold pest. After some years they gained resistance against DDT and appeared some DDT resistant mosquitos. Later on in the population, mutant strains got well established and DDT resistant mosquito\'s replaced DDT sensitive mosquitos.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(47315,'biology',102,776,1681,'Darwins theory gets recognition because','[{"text": "It tells about the natural selection", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "It tells about the arrival of the fittest", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "It is logical sequence of events that should have occurred", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Darwin was very famous and well known", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'According to Charles Darwin, evolution is a very slow and gradual process. He concluded that evolution took place over a very long period of time. As we talk about the time period in evolution we usually refer to billions of years. The generation of a species from another takes long time. It is a very steady process as the changes and adaptation take a long time to stabilize and give rise to a new species.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(47316,'biology',102,776,1681,'Which one of the following sequences was proposed by Darwin and Wallace for organic evolution?','[{"text": "Variation, natural selection, overproduction, constancy of population size", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Overproduction, constancy of population size, variations, natural selection.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Variations, constancy of population size, overproduction, natural selection", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Overproduction, Variations, constancy of population size, natural selection", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'The sequence proposed by Darwin and Wallace for organic evolution is overproduction, variations, the constancy of population size, natural selection.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(47317,'biology',102,776,1681,'Darwins theory suggests','[{"text": "Size of organs increase with ageing", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "There should be some genetic basis of inheritance", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Development of organs is due to will power of the organism.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Useful organs become strong and developed while useless organs become extinct", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'There should be some genetic basis of inheritance.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(47318,'biology',102,776,1681,'In which respect does Darwin\'s theory is wrong?','[{"text": "Survival of the fittest", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Arrival of the fittest", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "High efficiency of reproduction", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Origin of species", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Darwin failed to explain arrival of fittest. But explained survival of fittest.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(47319,'biology',102,776,1681,'Select the incorrect statement among the followings:','[{"text": "Darwin formed his theory on the basis of branching descent and natural selection.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Darwin concluded that existing living forms share similarities to varying degrees not only among themselves but also with life forms that existed millions of years ago.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Darwin did not believe in the concept of inheritance of acquired characters.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "According to Darwin variations which cause the evolution are small and directional.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'In On the Origin of Species, Charles Darwin accepted the principle of the inheritance of acquired characteristics as one of the factors contributory to evolution.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(47320,'biology',102,776,1681,'Darwin believed that the giraffe has long neck because','[{"text": "They were always like that", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "The catastrophes eliminated short necked forms", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Its ancestors stretched their needs to get food", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Ancestral giraffes with slightly longer necks than others got more food and left more food surviving adapted offsprings", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Charles Darwin was the first to propose that giraffes evolved into the elegantly longnecked creatures they are because successive generations realized that extra vertebrae helped them get access to tender leaves on top of trees.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(47321,'biology',102,776,1681,'Persons who do hard manual work with their hands are likely to develop','[{"text": "Greater number of sweat pores in their hands", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Greater quantity of melanin all over the body", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Thick subcutaneous fat in their palms", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Thick epidermis on their palms", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Thick epidermis on his palms.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(47322,'biology',102,776,1681,'Find the incorrect statement','[{"text": "Natural selection is based on certain observations which are theoretical.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "It is possible that the work of Thomas Malthus on populations influenced Darwin.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "According to Darwin, any population has built in variation in characteristics", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Darwin highlighted the importance of small and gradual differences in evolution, whereas deVries focused an abrupt and drastic changes as cause of origin of species.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Natural selection is based on certain observations or features which are factual: Natural resources are limited: Due to over population, there is scarcity in the basic resources like food and shelter in any population.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(47323,'biology',102,776,1681,'Select the correct statement','[{"text": "All mammals except whales and camels have seven cervical vertebrae", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Mutation are random and directional", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Darwinism variation are small and directionless", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Fitness is the end result of the ability to adapt and gets selected by nature", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Fitness is a result of selection and proliferation of only those organisms which were most suitably adapted to the environment and get selected by nature.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(47324,'biology',102,776,1681,'Darwin\'s and Lamarck\'s theories of evolution both suggest that','[{"text": "Earth is 6000 years old", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "The main mechanism of evolution is the inheritance of acquired character", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "The interaction of organisms with their environment is important in the evolutionary process", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Species are fixed", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Both Lamarck and Darwin believed that living things had hereditary traits, traits they could pass on to their offspring. They believed that some traits were more useful than others, and that over time the more useful trait would become more common.<br>The difference is that Lamarck believed that the changes an organism experienced during its life could be passed on. So for instance if a giraffe stretched its neck its entire life, it would have longer necked children.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(47325,'biology',102,776,1681,'Which of the following is correct?','[{"text": "Natural selection is not necessary for evolution", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Natural selection is the basis for evolutionary change", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Natural selection discriminates variations", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Natural selection is just a imaginative concept which don\'t work in the real world", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'The natural selection brings a remarkable change in the morphology, anatomy and genetic of a living organism which helps them to adjust in a better manner in the environment and coordinate with the fluctuating conditions with time. These changes create variation which is carried from one generation to generation.It is a key mechanism of evolution.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(47326,'biology',102,776,1681,'Improved race of horse is developed due to','[{"text": "Natural selection", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Artificial selection", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Protective selection", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Environmental selection", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Artificial selection is the intentional reproduction of individuals in a population that have desired traits Ex: Improved race of Horse.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(47327,'biology',102,777,1682,'Theory of saltation was given by','[{"text": "H.J. Miller", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Hugo de Vries", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "G Mendel", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "J.B.S. Haldane", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Saltation is a sudden change from one generation to the next .',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(47328,'biology',102,777,1682,'\'Mutation theory\' was given by','[{"text": "Hugo de Vries", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Darwin", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Lamarck", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Malthus", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Advanced at the beginning of the 20th century by botanist and geneticist Hugo de Vries in his Die Mutations theory (1901-03; The Mutation Theory), mutation theory joined two seemingly opposed traditions of evolutionary thought.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(47329,'biology',102,777,1682,'Hugo deVries based on his work on_______ plant gave the idea of mutations.','[{"text": "<i>Oenothera Lamarckiana</i>", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Evening primrose", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both 1 and 2", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "4\'o clock plant", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'<i>Oenothera lamarckiana</i> is the botanical name of evening primrose.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(47330,'biology',102,777,1682,'Mutation is very common when it is present in','[{"text": "Dominant condition", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Constant in population", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Recessive condition", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "None of these", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Dominant characters are expressed either in homozygous or heterozygous state.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(47331,'biology',102,778,1683,'Match the following columns.<div class="matching-question"><div class="column-left">A. Genetic drift<br>B. Natural selection<br>C. Gene flow<br>D. Mutation</div><div class="column-right">P. Change in the population’s allele frequency due to chance alone<br>Q. Selection of better traits<br>R. Immigation or emigration changes the allele frequency<br>S. Saltation</div></div>','[{"text": "$\\\\mathrm{A}-\\\\mathrm{R}, \\\\mathrm{B}-\\\\mathrm{P}, \\\\mathrm{C}-\\\\mathrm{Q}, \\\\mathrm{D}-\\\\mathrm{S}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{A}-\\\\mathrm{S}, \\\\mathrm{B}-\\\\mathrm{R}, \\\\mathrm{C}-\\\\mathrm{P}, \\\\mathrm{D}-\\\\mathrm{Q}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{A}-\\\\mathrm{P},  \\\\mathrm{B}-\\\\mathrm{Q},  \\\\mathrm{C}-\\\\mathrm{R},  \\\\mathrm{D}-\\\\mathrm{S}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{A}-\\\\mathrm{Q}, \\\\mathrm{B}-\\\\mathrm{P}, \\\\mathrm{C}-\\\\mathrm{R}, \\\\mathrm{D}-\\\\mathrm{S}$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'A - genetic drift-change in the populations allele frequency due to chance alone, B - Natural SelectionSelection of better traits, C - Gene flow-immigation or emigration changes the allele frequency, D- Mutation-saltation',NULL,'Easy','Match the following',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(47332,'biology',102,778,1683,'The common and continuous source of variations in a population:','[{"text": "Gene recombination", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Mutation", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Genetic drift", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Gene flow", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Gene recombination occurs during meiosis during gamete formation,which is a common and continuous source of variations',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(48401,'biology',101,575,1154,'MTPs are harmful to mother and foetus during _______ trimester of pregnancy.','[{"text": "${{\\\\rm{1}}^{{\\\\rm{st}}}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${{\\\\rm{3}}^{{\\\\rm{rd}}}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${{\\\\rm{2}}^{{\\\\rm{nd}}}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Unrelated", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'MTPs are harmful to mother and foetus during ${{\\rm{2}}^{{\\rm{nd}}}}$ trimester of pregnancy.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(47333,'biology',102,777,1682,'The source of variations in a sexually reproducing population:','[{"text": "Mutation", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Gene recombination", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both (1) and (2)", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Hybridization", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'The three main sources of genetic variation arising from sexual reproduction are: Crossing over (in prophase I) Random assortment of chromosomes (in metaphase I) Random fusion of gametes from different parents.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(47334,'biology',102,777,1682,'According to Hugo de Vries theory, evolution is','[{"text": "Discontinuous", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Sudden", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Continuous", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both (1) and (2)", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'According to De Vries theory of evolution: Mutation or discontinuous variation is the raw material of evolution. The mutation appears suddenly and produced their effect immediately. Mutants are different from the parents and there is no intermediate stage between the two.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(47335,'biology',102,777,1682,'According to Darwin, variations are','[{"text": "Heritable and slow", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Non-heritable and slow", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Mutational and slow", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Environmental and fast", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Mutations are random and directionless, while, as per Darwin, variations are small and directional. The theory of Darwin\'s mechanism of evolution starts with the variation between organisms within a genus. Individuals of one generation vary from each other qualitatively.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(47336,'biology',102,777,1682,'Only three statements are correct among the given five statements. Identify them:<br>I.  Evolution for Darwin was gradual while deVries believed mutation caused speciation and hence called it saltation<BR>II. Members of a population don\'t vary in characteristics<BR>III.  Fitness is the end result of ability to adapt and get selected by nature.<br>IV. Evolution is a single step process called saltation<br>V. Evolution is not a directed process in the sense of determinism.','[{"text": "I, III, V", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "I, II, III", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "III, IV, V", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "IV, V, III", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'The correct option is D Saltation causes evolution as per DeVries while evolution is gradual according to Darwin, Biological evolution refers to the gradual changes that occur in the inheritable characters of organisms over several generations. Both Hugo de Vries and Darwin had different views on evolution and its mechanism.',NULL,'Hard','MCQ type-2',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(47337,'biology',102,777,1682,'Match the following columns.<div class="matching-question"><div class="column-left">A. Darwin<br>B. Lamarck<br>C. Pasteur<br>D. De Vries</div><div class="column-right">P. Branching descent<br>Q. Inheritance of  aquired character<br>R. Disapproved spontaneous theory<br>S. Mutational theory of inheritance</div></div>','[{"text": "$\\\\mathrm{A}-\\\\mathrm{R}, \\\\mathrm{B}-\\\\mathrm{P}, \\\\mathrm{C}-\\\\mathrm{Q}, \\\\mathrm{D}-\\\\mathrm{S}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{A}-\\\\mathrm{P}, \\\\mathrm{B}-\\\\mathrm{Q}, \\\\mathrm{C}-\\\\mathrm{R},  \\\\mathrm{D}-\\\\mathrm{S}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{A}-\\\\mathrm{P}, \\\\mathrm{B}-\\\\mathrm{R}, \\\\mathrm{C}-\\\\mathrm{Q}, \\\\mathrm{D}-\\\\mathrm{S}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{A}-\\\\mathrm{Q}, \\\\mathrm{B}-\\\\mathrm{P}, \\\\mathrm{C}-\\\\mathrm{R}, \\\\mathrm{D}-\\\\mathrm{S}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Darwin-branching descent, Lamarck - inheritance of acquired character, Pasteur - disapproved spontaneous theory, De vries - Mutational theory of inheritance',NULL,'Easy','Match the following',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(47338,'biology',102,777,1682,'Select the most appropriate statement<br>I. Darwinian variations are small and directional.<br>II. Evolution for Darwin was gradual','[{"text": "Only I is correct", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Only II is correct", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both I and II are correct", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "None are correct", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Darwinism is the theory of evolution given by Charles Darwin which states that a species or organism evolve due to natural selection which increases their ability to adapt, reproduce and compete i.e it increases the chances of survival and reproduction based on the fitness of organisms. His observation and conclusions were published in the book origin of species are based on natural selection',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(47339,'biology',102,777,1682,'Hugo de Vries is known for_____','[{"text": "Mutation alone", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Saltation theory alone", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Independently studying Mendels law", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "All of them", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Hugo de Vries, in full Hugo Marie de Vries, (born February 16, 1848, Haarlem, Netherlands - died May 21, 1935, near Amsterdam), Dutch botanist and geneticist who introduced the experimental study of organic evolution.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(47340,'biology',102,776,1681,'Read four statements (I-IV) carefully, find the correct statement:<br>I.  Natural resources are limited.<br>II.  Populations are stable in size except for seasonal fluctuations.<br>III.  Members of a population vary in characteristics.<br>IV.  There is no competition for resources among the individuals of a population.','[{"text": "I, III, IV", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "I, II,III", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "I, II, IV", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "II, III, IV", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'According to Darwin\'s theory of natural selection, all organisms have the potential to reproduce limitlessly irrespective of the fact that each species maintain a constant density over time. However, the struggle for existence, i.e. the competition among individuals for available resources to survive and reproduce, put a check on species density and maintains it at the constant level. Survival of fittest is unequal ability of individuals to survive and reproduce based on suitability of their inherited characters to prevailing environmental conditions. It imparts the reproductive advantage to certain organisms which account for major part of the gene pool of the species. Thus, natural selection favors the individual with survival and/or reproductive advantage over others under existing environmental condition. Accordingly, variation',NULL,'Easy','MCQ type-2',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(47364,'biology',102,778,1683,'Identify the correct statement','[{"text": "Genetic variability is produced by gene mutations", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Accumulation of useful mutations through ages has led to the creation of new species of living beings", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Genetic variability provides raw materials for the operation of natural selection and reproductive isolation", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "All the above", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Genetic variability is the change in the sequence of the gens due to mutations. These mutations has led to new species generation. The so evolved species are selected in the environment based on natural selection.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(47341,'biology',102,777,1682,'Fill in the blanks :<br>Mutations are_______ while Darwinian variations are_______','[{"text": "Constant and directionless, small and directional.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Random and directional, small and directional.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Random and directionless, small and directional.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Random and directionless, small and directionless", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Random and directionless, small and directional, Hugo de Vries, based on his work on Oenothera lamarckiana or evening primrose, proposed the mutation theory. In this theory, he explained that new species evolve by sudden and distinct heritable changes called mutations. These mutations can occur at any time hence are random and can be useful, harmful or have no effects at all on the organisms hence are directionless. As the mutations are produced occasionally without prior precedent, they are termed as discontinuous heritable changes in the genetic material. On the other hand, Darwin\'s theory states that new variations appear in every generation (continuous) and such small variations accumulate over several generations by natural selection until the offspring become significantly different, thus evolving into a new species. Thus, Darwinian variations were considered small and directional.Hugo de Vries believed that it is mutation which causes evolution and not the minor variations (heritable) that Darwin discussed in his theory.Mutations are random and directionless while Darwinian variations are small and directional.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(47342,'biology',102,778,1683,'Hardy-Weinberg law is applicable to','[{"text": "Small population", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Genetic equilibrium", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Genetic drift", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Non-random mating", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'The Hardy-Weinberg equilibrium is a principle stating that the genetic variation in a population will remain constant from one generation to the next in the absence of disturbing factors. Hardy Weinberg law is applicable to genetic equilibrium.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(47343,'biology',102,778,1683,'Hardy - Weinberg principle is expresed by following equation:','[{"text": "$\\\\mathrm{p}^{2}-\\\\mathrm{q}^{2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{p}^{2}+\\\\mathrm{q}^{2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${{\\\\rm{p}}^{\\\\rm{2}}}{\\\\rm{ + 2pq + }}{{\\\\rm{q}}^{\\\\rm{2}}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{p}^{2}-2 \\\\mathrm{pq}-\\\\mathrm{q}^{2}$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'In the equation, ${{\\rm{p}}^{\\rm{2}}}$ represents the frequency of the homozygous genotype AA, ${{\\rm{q}}^{\\rm{2}}}$ represents the frequency of the homozygous genotype aa, and $2 \\mathrm{pq}$ represents the frequency of the heterozygous genotype Aa. In addition, the sum of the allele frequencies for all the alleles at the locus must be 1, so ${\\rm{p + q = 1}}$.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(47344,'biology',102,778,1683,'The frequency of occurrence of alleles of a gene or a locus is supposed to remain______ and even remain the_____ through generations.','[{"text": "Non-fixed, same", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Fixed, same", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Fixed, Different", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Non-Fixed, Different", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Hardy-Weinberg principle states that the frequency of occurrence of alleles of a gene in a population remains constant through generations unless disturbances such as mutations, nonrandom mating etc.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(47345,'biology',102,778,1683,'Hardy-Weinberg principle says that allele frequencies in a population are______ and is______ from generation to generation.','[{"text": "Stable, constant", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Unstable, constant", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Stable, unconstant", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Unstable, unconstant", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'According to hardy-Weinberg principle, allele frequencies in a population are stable and is constant from generation to generation.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(47346,'biology',102,778,1683,'The gene pool represents_____ in a population and it remains a constant.','[{"text": "Total genes only", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Dominant alleles only", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Recessive alleles only", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Total genes and their alleles", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Gene pool refers to the combination of all the genes (including alleles) present in a reproducing population or species. A large gene pool has extensive genomic diversity and is better able to withstand environmental challenges.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(47347,'biology',102,778,1683,'What is the sum total of all the allelic frequencies?','[{"text": "0", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Variable", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "1", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Infinity", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'The sum of all the frequencies for all classes is equal to the number of elements in the given data and that summation is termed as the cumulative frequency which defines the number of entries of that statistical data.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(47348,'biology',102,778,1683,'Randomly mating population has an established frequency of $36 \\%$ for organisms that are homozygous recessive for a given trait. The frequency of this recessive allele in the gene pool is','[{"text": "6", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "36", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "0.6", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "16", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'We know that the frequency of the recessive homozygote genotype is $\\mathrm{q}^{2}$.$\\mathrm{q}^{2}=36 \\%=0.36$<br>$q=0.6$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(47349,'biology',102,778,1683,'The frequency of two alleles in a gene pool is 0.19 (1) and 0.81(1). Assume that the population is in Hardy-Weinberg equilibrium. The percentage of heterozygous individuals in the population is','[{"text": "0.81", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "0.31", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "0.19", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "1", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'According to the Hardy-Weinberg Equilibrium equation, heterozygotes are represented by the $2 \\mathrm{pq}$ term.Therefore, the number of heterozygous individuals $(\\mathrm{Aa})$ is equal to $2 \\mathrm{pq}$ which equals $2 \\times 0.19 \\times 0.81=0.31$ or $31 \\%$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(47350,'biology',102,778,1683,'In a population that is in Hardy-Weinberg equilibrium, the frequency of the recessive homozygote genotype of a certain trait is 0.09 . Percentage of individuals homozygous for the dominant allele is','[{"text": "0.09", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "0.30", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "0.70", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "0.49", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'We know that the frequency of the recessive homozygote genotype is $\\mathrm{q}^{2}$ and equal to 0.09 .<br>${\\rm{q = }}\\sqrt {{{\\rm{q}}^{\\rm{2}}}} {\\rm{ = }}\\sqrt {{\\rm{0}}{\\rm{.09}}} {\\rm{ = 0}}{\\rm{.30}}$<br>we also know that<br> ${\\rm{p + q = 1}}$<br>Thus, ${\\rm{p = 1 - q}}$<br>${\\rm{p = 1 - 0}}{\\rm{.30 = 0}}{\\rm{.70}}$<br>The homozygote dominants are represented by<br>$\\mathrm{p}^{2}=(0.70)^{2}=0.49$ or $49 \\%$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(47352,'biology',102,778,1683,'According to Hardy Weinberg principle if p and q represent the frequency of the allele A and allele a respectively, then what will be the frequency of AA individuals in the population?','[{"text": "${{\\\\rm{p}}^{\\\\rm{2}}}{\\\\rm{ + 2pq + }}{{\\\\rm{q}}^{\\\\rm{2}}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{p + q}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{2 p q}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${{\\\\rm{p}}^{\\\\rm{2}}}$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Allele frequency is determined by the relative frequency of a specific allele an autosomal locus in a population, in accordance with the Hardy-Weinberg equilibrium rule the allelic frequencies (frequency of recessive and dominant allele) in a particular population will not change from one generation to the next, if there is no mutation, assortative mating, selection, migration, and genetic drift.According to the Hardy-Weinberg equation $\\mathrm{p}^{2}+2 \\mathrm{pq}+\\mathrm{q}^{2}=1$, where $\\mathrm{p}$ denotes dominant allele frequency, $q$ denotes recessive allele frequency, the $\\mathrm{p}^{2}=$ frequency of the homozygous dominant allele, the $\\mathrm{q}^{2}=$ frequency of the recessive allele, the $2 \\mathrm{pq}=$ frequency of the heterozygous allele.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(47353,'biology',102,778,1683,'Which type of natural selection eliminates extreme individuals and maintains normal individuals?','[{"text": "Directional", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Non-directional", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Disruptive", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Stabilising", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Stabilizing selection is the opposite of disruptive selection. Instead of favoring individuals with extreme phenotypes, it favors the intermediate variants. Stabilizing selection tends to remove the more severe phenotypes, resulting in the reproductive success of the norm or average phenotypes.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(47354,'biology',102,778,1683,'Select the correct statement from the following<br>I. Disturbance in Hardy-Weinberg equilibrium, i.e., change of frequency of alleles in a population would then be interpreted as resulting in evolution.<br>II. The original drifted population becomes founders and the effect is called founder effect','[{"text": "Statement I only", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Statement II only", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both of them", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "None of them", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'According to Hardy-Weinberg principle, disturbance in genetic equilibrium or change of frequency of alleles in a population can be interpreted as accumulation or change in variations that results in evolution.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(47355,'biology',102,778,1683,'If the gene flow occurs by chance, it is called?','[{"text": "Genetic shift", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Genetic drift", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Genetic migration", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "None of them", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Genetic drift is defined as the change in gene frequency that occurs by chance in a small population. It provides diversity to a population without any adaptations.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(47356,'biology',102,778,1683,'On critical analysis which of the following has resulted in changed frequency of genes and alleles in future generation?','[{"text": "Variation due to mutation", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Variation due to recombination", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Gene flow or genetic drift", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "All of these", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'The Hardy-Weinberg law states<br>1) The population size is very large.<br>2) No genes are input from other sources i.e. no gene flow takes place.<br>3) No mutation takes place.<br>4) No natural selection occurs.<br>5) Random mating is occurring.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(47357,'biology',102,778,1683,'One of the following is also called as "Sewall Wright Effect"','[{"text": "Genetic drift", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Genetic flow", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Gene pool", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Isolation", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Theory of Genetic drift was proposed by Sewall Wright in 1930. Hence it is also called as "Sewall Wright Effect".',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(47358,'biology',102,778,1683,'Genetic drift operates only in','[{"text": "Smaller population", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Island population", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Larger population", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Mid-sized population", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Genetic drift is an evolutionary force operating in small populations.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(47359,'biology',102,778,1683,'Which of the following cannot affect the Hardy-Weinberg equilibrium?','[{"text": "Genetic drift", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Mutation", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Gene migration", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Random mating", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Five basic assumptions of Hardy-Weinberg law are: random mating, infinite (large) population size, no mutation, no gene flow and no selection. While, genetic drift occurs in small size population. Genetic drift is a type of evolution that is not necessarily adaptive. It does not specifically selection for traits that are fit for the environment. Hence, answer is "Genetic drift".',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(47360,'biology',102,778,1683,'Random genetic drift in a population probably results from','[{"text": "Large population size", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Highly genetically variable individuals", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Constant low mutation rate", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Interbreeding within this population", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Interbreeding within this population results in Random genetic drift .',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(47361,'biology',102,778,1683,'According to Neo-Darwinism theory which of the following is responsible for the origin of new species?','[{"text": "Useful variations", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Hybridisation", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Mutations", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Mutation together with natural selection", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Neo-Darwinism is theory of evolution that represents a synthesis of Charles darwins theory in terms of natural selection and modern population genetics.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(47362,'biology',102,778,1683,'Evolution is principally a process of','[{"text": "Inherited somatic modifications", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Sudden disruptive change", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Modification and speciation", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Somatic mutation and genetic recombination", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Evolution is a process of modification and speciation.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(47363,'biology',102,778,1683,'Which of the following is false in case of a species?','[{"text": "Variation occur among members of a species", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Gene flow does not occur between the populations of a species", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Members of a species can interbreed", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Every species is reproductively isolated from every other species", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Gene flow is the transfer of alleles from one population to another population through immigration of individuals.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(47365,'biology',102,778,1683,'Which one of the following supports Hardy-Weinberg equilibrium?','[{"text": "Gene migration", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Genetic drift", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Natural selection", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "None of them", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Any factor, which can disturb the frequency of alleles in a population is likely to affect the Hardy-Weinberg equilibrium.The alleles in a population can be altered by mutation, migration, genetic drift, recombination, and natural selection.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(47366,'biology',102,778,1683,'Gene flow by chance results in','[{"text": "Genetic drift", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Variation", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Gene migration", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Mutation", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'The effect of gene flow is to reduce genetic differences between populations, thereby preventing or delaying the evolution of the populations in different geographical areas into separate species of the pathogen.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(47367,'biology',102,778,1683,'Genetic drift occurs when a few individuals of a species colonise an island. This particular phenomenon is known as','[{"text": "The bottleneck effect", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "The founder effect", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "The swan neck effect", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Mutation", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'The founder effect is one form of genetic drift. This is the phenomenon in which there is a derivation of a new population from a small isolated group of individuals. The new population is genetically different from the parent populations. Pitcairn island is a group of volcanic islands. The calamities result in the development of the different human population on this island. This is an example of Founder\'s effect.',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(47368,'biology',102,778,1683,'In which condition, gene ratio remains constant in a species?','[{"text": "Gene flow", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Natural selection", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Random mating", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Sexual selection", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'According to Hardy-Weinberg\'s principle the gene and genotypic frequencies in a population in a large random mating population will remain constant from generation to generation in the absence of migration, mutation and selection. So, the correct answer is \'Random mating\'',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(47369,'biology',102,778,1683,'Match the following columns.<div class="matching-question"><div class="column-left">A. Wallace<br>B. Malthus<br>C. Hardy-Weinberg Law<br>D. Industrial melanism</div><div class="column-right">P. Archipelago<br>Q. Influenced Darwin<br>R. England<br>S. ${{\\rm{p}}^{\\rm{2}}}{\\rm{ + 2pq + }}{{\\rm{q}}^{\\rm{2}}}{\\rm{ = 1}}$</div></div>','[{"text": "$\\\\mathrm{A}-\\\\mathrm{R}, \\\\mathrm{B}-\\\\mathrm{P}, \\\\mathrm{C}-\\\\mathrm{Q}, \\\\mathrm{D}-\\\\mathrm{S}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{A}-\\\\mathrm{S}, \\\\mathrm{B}-\\\\mathrm{R}, \\\\mathrm{C}-\\\\mathrm{P}, \\\\mathrm{D}-\\\\mathrm{Q}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{A}-\\\\mathrm{P}, \\\\mathrm{B}-\\\\mathrm{R}, \\\\mathrm{C}-\\\\mathrm{Q}, \\\\mathrm{D}-\\\\mathrm{S}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{A}-\\\\mathrm{P},  \\\\mathrm{B}-\\\\mathrm{Q},  \\\\mathrm{C}-\\\\mathrm{S},  \\\\mathrm{D}-\\\\mathrm{R}$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Wallace-Archipelago<br>Malthus-Influenced Darwin<br>Hardy weinberg law $-\\mathrm{p}^{2}+\\mathrm{q}^{2}+2 \\mathrm{pq}=1$Industrial melanism-England',NULL,'Easy','Match the following',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(47370,'biology',102,778,1683,'The Hardy-Weinberg law of equilibrium was based on the following','[{"text": "Random mating, natural selection, gene flow", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Random mating, genetic drift, gene flow", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Non-random mating, mutation, gene flow", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Random mating, no mutation, no gene flow", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'The Hardy-Weinberg principle relies on a number of assumptions: (1) random mating (i.e, population structure is absent and matings occur in proportion to genotype frequencies), (2) the absence of natural selection, (3) a very large population size (i.e., genetic drift is negligible), (4) no gene flow or migration,',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(47371,'biology',102,778,1683,'Which of the following statement is false?','[{"text": "Microbial experiments show that pre- existing advantageous mutations when selected will result in observation of new phenotypes. Over few generations, this would result in Speciation.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "The sum total of genes of all the species in an area constitutes the gene pool.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "The gene frequency refers to the proportion of an allele in the gene pool as compared with other alleles.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "None of the above", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Gene pool is the set of all genes (genetic information)of a single species in any population.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(47372,'biology',102,778,1683,'According to modern synthesis theory, the unit of evolution','[{"text": "Genus", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Population", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Individual", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Species", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'The ultimate effect of evolution is a group of organisms of the same species living in specific area at a specific time-population',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(47373,'biology',102,778,1683,'A new species of organism is formed','[{"text": "When an organism is isolated from the rest of the species by geographical barrier", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "When a group of organism can no longer interbreed with other closely related organisms", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "When drastic climate changes leading to structural changes in the given population", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "When a series of mutation occur to cause an organism to be different from the others in population, but it still shows successful interbreeding", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'New species form by speciation, in which an ancestral population splits into two or more genetically distinct descendant populations. Speciation involves reproductive isolation of groups within the original population and accumulation of genetic differences between the two groups.',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(47374,'biology',102,778,1683,'Phylogenetic evolution refers to','[{"text": "Similar character", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Natural affinity of genes", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Similar habitat", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Genetic relationship and evolutionary sequence", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'The result of these analyses is a phylogeny, a diagrammatic hypothesis about the history of the evolutionary relationships of a group of organisms. Hence, Phylogenetic evolution refers to Genetic relationship and evolutionary sequence. So, the correct answer is \'Genetic relationship and evolutionary sequence\'.',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(48442,'biology',101,577,1156,'Which is the method in which a sperm is directly injected into the ovum?','[{"text": "GIFT", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "ICSI", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "ZIFT", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "ET", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'ICSI method in which a sperm is directly injected into the ovum.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(47375,'biology',102,778,1683,'Which is not true for directional selection?','[{"text": "Extreme value, single is selected", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Mean value never change", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Industrial melanism is an example", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Peak shifts in one direction", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Directional selection is one type of natural selection in which the phenotype (the observable characteristics) of the species tends toward one extreme rather the mean phenotype or the opposite extreme phenotype.\'shifts allele frequency in a steady, consistent direction\'',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(47376,'biology',102,778,1683,'In disruptive type of natural selection','[{"text": "Homozygous as well as heterozygous character can be preferred", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Homozygous character is preferred at one end", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Heterozygous character is preferred", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Homozygous characters are preferred at both ends", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Disruptive selection is a type of natural selection that selects against the average individual in a population. The makeup of this type of population would show phenotypes (individuals with groups of traits) of both extremes but have very few individuals in the middle.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(47377,'biology',102,778,1683,'Match the following columns.<div class="matching-question"><div class="column-left">A. Stabilisation<br>B. Directional selection<br>C. Disruption selection</div><div class="column-right">P. Industrial melanism<br>Q. More individuals possess heterozygous character<br>R. More individuals possess homozygous character</div></div>','[{"text": "$\\\\mathrm{A}-\\\\mathrm{R}, \\\\mathrm{B}-\\\\mathrm{P}, \\\\mathrm{C}-\\\\mathrm{Q}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{A}-\\\\mathrm{Q}, \\\\mathrm{B}-\\\\mathrm{P}, \\\\mathrm{C}-\\\\mathrm{R}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{A}-\\\\mathrm{P}, \\\\mathrm{B}-\\\\mathrm{R}, \\\\mathrm{C}-\\\\mathrm{Q}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{A}-\\\\mathrm{Q}, \\\\mathrm{B}-\\\\mathrm{R}, \\\\mathrm{C}-\\\\mathrm{P}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Stabilisation-More individual possess heterozygous character<br>Directional selection-Industrial melanism<br>Disruption selection-More individuals possess homozygous character',NULL,'Easy','Match the following',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(47378,'biology',102,779,1684,'Which is the age of prokaryotic microbes?','[{"text": "Proterozoic", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Paleozoic", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Precambrian", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Mesozoic", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Prokaryotic microbes originated about 3900-3500 million years ago duringPrecambrian era.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(47379,'biology',102,779,1684,'About______ the first cellular forms of life appeared on earth.','[{"text": "3000 (mya)", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "2000 (mya)", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "2000 (bya)", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "3000 (bya)", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'The first cellular form of life originated about 4 billion years ago. 3 billion years ago, 2000 million years ago and 50,000 years ago is after the origination of life on earth. So, before 4 billion years ago, cellular form of life did not possibly originated or present.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(47380,'biology',102,779,1684,'Select the correct option<br>I. The mechanism of how non-cellular aggregates of giant macromolecules could evolve into cells with membranous envelope is not known.<br>II. Some of these cells had the ability to release $\\mathrm{CO}_{2}$.','[{"text": "I is correct", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "II is correct", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both are correct", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "None are correct", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Some of the cells had ability to release $\\mathrm{CO}_{2}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(47381,'biology',102,779,1684,'Jawless fishes probably evolved around','[{"text": "350 bya", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "200 mya", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "350 mya", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "100 mya", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Jawless fish probably evolved around 350 mya.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(47382,'biology',102,779,1684,'Which two periods are correctly matched with the dominance of organisms in that particular period?<div class="matching-question"></div>','[{"text": "$\\\\begin{array}{*{20}{c}}\\n{\\\\left( {\\\\rm{A}} \\\\right)\\\\,{\\\\rm{Devonian}}\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,}&{\\\\rm{ - }}&{{\\\\rm{Reptiles}}\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,}\\\\\\\\\\n{\\\\left( {\\\\rm{B}} \\\\right)\\\\,{\\\\rm{Jurassic}}\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,}&{\\\\rm{ - }}&{{\\\\rm{Dinosaurs}}}\\n\\\\end{array}$<br><br>", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\begin{array}{*{20}{c}}\\n{\\\\left( {\\\\rm{A}} \\\\right)\\\\,{\\\\rm{Carboniferous}}}&{\\\\rm{ - }}&{{\\\\rm{Amphibians}}}\\\\\\\\\\n{\\\\begin{array}{*{20}{l}}\\n{\\\\left( {\\\\rm{B}} \\\\right)\\\\,{\\\\rm{Devonian}}\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,}\\n\\\\end{array}}&{\\\\rm{ - }}&{\\\\begin{array}{*{20}{l}}\\n{{\\\\rm{Fishes}}\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,}\\n\\\\end{array}}\\n\\\\end{array}$<br><br>", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\begin{array}{*{20}{c}}\\n{\\\\left( {\\\\rm{A}} \\\\right)\\\\,\\\\begin{array}{*{20}{l}}\\n{{\\\\rm{Devonian}}\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,}\\n\\\\end{array}}&{\\\\rm{ - }}&{{\\\\rm{Amphibians}}}\\\\\\\\\\n{\\\\begin{array}{*{20}{l}}\\n{\\\\left( {\\\\rm{B}} \\\\right)\\\\,\\\\begin{array}{*{20}{l}}\\n{{\\\\rm{Carboniferous}}}\\n\\\\end{array}}\\n\\\\end{array}}&{\\\\rm{ - }}&{\\\\begin{array}{*{20}{l}}\\n{\\\\begin{array}{*{20}{l}}\\n{{\\\\rm{Dinosaurs}}}\\n\\\\end{array}\\\\,\\\\,}\\n\\\\end{array}}\\n\\\\end{array}$<br><br>", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\begin{array}{*{20}{c}}\\n{\\\\left( {\\\\rm{A}} \\\\right)\\\\,\\\\begin{array}{*{20}{l}}\\n{{\\\\rm{Jurassic}}\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,}\\n\\\\end{array}}&{\\\\rm{ - }}&{{\\\\rm{Dinosaurs}}}\\\\\\\\\\n{\\\\begin{array}{*{20}{l}}\\n{\\\\left( {\\\\rm{B}} \\\\right)\\\\,\\\\begin{array}{*{20}{l}}\\n{{\\\\rm{Carboniferous}}}\\n\\\\end{array}}\\n\\\\end{array}}&{\\\\rm{ - }}&{\\\\begin{array}{*{20}{l}}\\n{\\\\begin{array}{*{20}{l}}\\n{\\\\begin{array}{*{20}{l}}\\n{{\\\\rm{Fishes}}}\\n\\\\end{array}\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,}\\n\\\\end{array}\\\\,\\\\,}\\n\\\\end{array}}\\n\\\\end{array}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'(A) Carboniferous-Amphibians<br>(B) Devonian-Fishes',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(47383,'biology',102,779,1684,'Name the ancestor shared by birds, crocodiles and dinosaurs?','[{"text": "Thecodonts", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Dinosaurs", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Pelycosaurs", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Sauropsids", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Extinct archosaurs include non-avian dinosaurs, pterosaurs, and extinct relatives of crocodilians. Modern paleontologists define Archosauria as a crown group that includes the most recent common ancestor of living birds and crocodilians, and all of its descendants.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(47384,'biology',102,779,1684,'Which is amongst the following show common ancestry line of mammals?','[{"text": "Therapsids", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Synapsids", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Pelycosaurs", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "All of these", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Given sequence shows ancestry line of mammals: Synapsids $\\rightarrow$ Pelycosaurs $\\rightarrow$ Therapsids $\\rightarrow$ Mammals .',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(47399,'biology',102,779,1684,'The most recent era in geological time scale is','[{"text": "Palaeozoic", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Cenozoic", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Mesozoic", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Azoic", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'The further subdivision of the eras into 12 "periods" is based on identifiable but less profound changes in life-forms. In the most recent era, the Cenozoic, there is a further subdivision of time into epochs.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(47385,'biology',102,779,1684,'Which is incorrectly matched?<div class="matching-question"></div>','[{"text": "Jawless fish probably evolved around 350 mya.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "320 mya - Jawless fishes evolved", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "2000 mya - First cellular form of life appeared", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "65 mya - Dinosaurs disappeared", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'About 2000 million years ago the first cellular form of life appeared on earth. Before 500 mya, only invertebrates were formed, later around 350 mya Jawless fish evolved being the first vertebrate.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(47386,'biology',102,779,1684,'Identify the missing period.','[{"text": "A - Carboniferous,B - Cretaceous, C - Jurassic", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "A - Triassic,B - Permian, C - Jurassic", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "A - Carboniferous, B - Permian, C - Jurassic", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "A- Carboniferous, B - Permian, C - Triassic", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'A-Carboniferous, B-Permian, C-Jurassic',NULL,'Easy','MCQ type-3',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','biology/102_47386_question_1.png');
INSERT INTO `qb_questions` (`id`, `subject_id`, `chapter_id`, `topic_id`, `subtopic_id`, `question_html`, `options`, `correct_index`, `explanation`, `explanation_image_url`, `difficulty`, `qtype`, `year`, `tag`, `created_at`, `question_image_url`) VALUES
(47387,'biology',102,779,1684,'1938, a fish caught in happened to be a Coelacanth which was thought to be extinct.','[{"text": "North Africa", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "South Africa", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "South America", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "North America", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'In 1938, a fish caught in South Africa, happended to be a coelacanth which as thought to be extinct. These animals are called lobefins evolved into the first amphibians.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(47388,'biology',102,779,1684,'Choose the incorrect pair.','[{"text": "1938-Coelacanth which was thought to be extinct.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Seaweeds existed - Around 320 mya", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "First organisms that invaded land -Plants", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Fish with stout and strong fins - Around 320 mya", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Fish with stout and strong fins could move on land and go back to water. This was about 350 mya.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(47389,'biology',102,779,1684,'Name the animal that evolved into the first probable amphibians and lived on both land and water.','[{"text": "Tyrannosaurus rex", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Coelacanth (lobefins)", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Therapsids", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Sauropsid", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Lobefins were the first animals to be declared as amphibians.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(47390,'biology',102,779,1684,'Thecodonts became extinct during','[{"text": "Cenozoic era", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Mesozoic era", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Palaeozoic era", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Proterozoic era", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'With the emergence of the running prey population, thecodonts were unable to meet their food requirements. They could not escape direct interactions with the dinosaurs during the condition of extreme stress. They began to decline and soon became extinct at the end of the Triassic',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(47391,'biology',102,779,1684,'Which period in geological time scale is considered the golden era of fish?','[{"text": "Carboniferous", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Silurian", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Devonian", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Jurassic", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'The Devonian, part of the Paleozoic era, is otherwise known as the Age of Fishes, as it spawned a remarkable variety of fish. The most formidable of them were the armored placoderms, a group that first appeared during the Silurian with powerful jaws lined with bladelike plates that acted as teeth.',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(47392,'biology',102,779,1684,'Identify the missing time period.','[{"text": "A - 150,&emsp; B - 100,&emsp; C -200", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "A - 150,&emsp; B - 200,&emsp; C -100", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "A - 300,&emsp; B - 350,&emsp; C -250", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "A - 250,&emsp; B - 350,&emsp; C -300", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'A-150,  B-200, C-100',NULL,'Easy','MCQ type-3',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','biology/102_47392_question_1.png'),
(47393,'biology',102,779,1684,'Identify the missing ancestors.','[{"text": "A - Chlorophyte ancestor,B - Rhynia type,C - Tracheophyte", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "A - Psilophyton,B - Rhynia type,C - Tracheophyte", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "A - Psilophyton,B - Tracheophyte,C - Chlorophyte", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "A - Psilophyton,B - Rhynia,C - Chlorophyte", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'A-Psilophyton B-Rhynia type C-Tracheophyte',NULL,'Easy','MCQ type-3',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','biology/102_47393_question_1.png'),
(47394,'biology',102,779,1684,'The Jurassic period belongs to which era?','[{"text": "Cenozoic", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Mesozoic", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Palaeozoic", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Proterozoic", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Jurassic Period, second of three periods of the Mesozoic Era. Extending from 201.3 million to 145 million years ago, it immediately followed the Triassic Period (251.9 million to 201.3 million years ago) and was succeeded by the Cretaceous Period (145 million to 66 million years ago).',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(47395,'biology',102,779,1684,'During which period did the first seed plant appear (Progymnosperms)?','[{"text": "Carboniferous", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Devonian", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Silurian", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Cretaceous", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'The fossil plant Elkinsia polymorpha, a "seed fern" from the Devonian period (about 400 million years ago) is considered the earliest seed plant known to date.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(47396,'biology',102,779,1684,'The Cenozoic era is often designated as','[{"text": "Age of dinosaurs", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Age of reptiles", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Age of mammals", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Age of amphibians", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'The Cenozoic is sometimes called the Age of Mammals, because the largest land animals have been mammals during that time.',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(47397,'biology',102,779,1684,'Which of the following is an extinct animal?','[{"text": "<i>Tyranosaurrus</i>", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "<i>Pelycosaurs</i>", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "<i>Archaeopteryx</i>", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "All of them", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Dodo and Dinosaur are extinct species.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(47398,'biology',102,779,1684,'Mesozoic era is called the age of','[{"text": "Fishes", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Reptiles", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Amphibians", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Birds", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'During the Mesozoic, or "Middle Life" era, life diversified rapidly and giant reptiles, dinosaurs and other monstrous beasts roamed the Earth. The period, which spans from about 252 million years ago to about 66 million years ago, was also known as the age of reptiles or the age of dinosaurs.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(47402,'biology',102,779,1684,'Dinosaurs became extinct during ____ time period.','[{"text": "Jurassic period", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Triassic period", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Permian Period", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Cretaceous period", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Dinosaurs went extinct about 65 million years ago (at the end of the Cretaceous Period), after living on Earth for about 165 million years.Jurassic Period (201 to 145 million years ago). Many large land animals were wiped out but the dinosaurs survived, giving them the opportunity to evolve into a wide variety of forms and increase in number.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(47403,'biology',102,779,1684,'Match the column I with Column II<div class="matching-question"><div class="column-left">A. Mesozoic<br>B. Devonian<br>C. Palacocene<br>D. Permian</div><div class="column-right">P. First amphibians<br>Q. 300-250 mya<br>R. 160 million years<br>S. Radiation of primitive mammals</div></div>','[{"text": "$\\\\mathrm{A}-\\\\mathrm{R}, \\\\mathrm{B}-\\\\mathrm{P}, \\\\mathrm{C}-\\\\mathrm{R}, \\\\mathrm{D}-\\\\mathrm{Q}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{A}-\\\\mathrm{R}, \\\\mathrm{B}-\\\\mathrm{P}, \\\\mathrm{C}-\\\\mathrm{S}, \\\\mathrm{D}-\\\\mathrm{Q}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{A}-\\\\mathrm{P}, \\\\mathrm{B}-\\\\mathrm{R}, \\\\mathrm{C}-\\\\mathrm{Q}, \\\\mathrm{D}-\\\\mathrm{S}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{A}-\\\\mathrm{Q}, \\\\mathrm{B}-\\\\mathrm{P}, \\\\mathrm{C}-\\\\mathrm{R}, \\\\mathrm{D}-\\\\mathrm{S}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Mesozoic-160 million years<br>Devonian-first amphibians<br>Palacocene-radiation of primitive mammals<br>Permian-300-250mya',NULL,'Easy','Match the following',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(47404,'biology',102,779,1684,'Choose the incorrectly matched pair.<div class="matching-question"></div>','[{"text": "Reptiles - Thin-shelled eggs which do not dry up", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Ichthyosaurs - Fish like reptiles", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Dinosaurs - May be evolved into birds", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "<i>Tyrannosaurus rex</i> - 20 feet in height", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'The amphibian evolved into reptiles. Reptiles lay thick shelled eggs which do not dry up in sun unlike those of amphibian',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(47405,'biology',102,779,1684,'The first mammals were like','[{"text": "Shrews", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Ichthyosaurs", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Marsupials", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Lobefins", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'The first mammals were like shrews. Morganucodontids were thought to be one of the first mammals to evolve. They evolved around 250 million years ago, in the late Triassic period and lived with dinosaurs.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(47406,'biology',102,779,1684,'Dinosaurs were abundant in','[{"text": "Permian", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Jurassic", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Devonian", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Pleistocene", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Jurassic period is known as "Age of Dinosaurs".',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(47407,'biology',102,779,1684,'Coal has been mainly formed by','[{"text": "Angiosperms", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Giant ferns", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Pteriodophytes", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both 2 and 3", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Giant ferns (Pteridophytes) fell to form coal deposits.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(47408,'biology',102,779,1684,'The origin of first mammals occurred','[{"text": "600 million years ago", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "1600 million years ago", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "220 million years ago", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "100 million years ago", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'The origin of first mammals occurred 220 million years ago.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(47409,'biology',102,779,1684,'The characteristics of mammals were','[{"text": "Viviparous and protected their unborn young inside the mother\'s body.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Mammals were more intelligent in sensing and avoiding danger at least", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Had dagger like teeth", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "1 and 2", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Mammals have hair or fur; are warmblooded; most are born alive; the young are fed milk produced by the mother\'s mammary glands; and they have a more complex brain than other animals.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(47410,'biology',102,779,1684,'Continental drift explains','[{"text": "Geographical upheavals", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Mass extinction", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Distribution of fossils on earth", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "All the above", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Continental drift is the movement of the earths continents relative to each other, thus appearing to drift across the ocean bed.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(47411,'biology',102,779,1684,'The pouched mammals of Australia survived  from other mammals because of ?','[{"text": "Continental collapse", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Continental variation", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Continental drift", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Continental evolution", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'During evolution pouched mammals of Australia survived. Due to lack of competition from any other mammal due to continental drift.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(47412,'biology',102,779,1684,'The most successful story is evolution of man with characters such as-','[{"text": "Avoiding danger", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Language skill alone", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Language skill and self-consciousnes", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Being omnivores", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'The most successful story is the evolution of man with language skills and self-consciousness.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(47413,'biology',102,779,1684,'Match the following columns.<div class="matching-question"><div class="column-left">A. <i>Ichthyosaurus</i><br>B. Coelacanth<br>C. Giant pteridophytes<br>D. Dinosaurs</div><div class="column-right">P. Caught in South Africa in 1938<br>Q. Fell to form coal deposits<br>R. Disappeared about 65 mya<br>S. Fish-like reptiles in 200 mya</div></div>','[{"text": "$\\\\mathrm{A}-\\\\mathrm{R}, \\\\mathrm{B}-\\\\mathrm{P}, \\\\mathrm{C}-\\\\mathrm{Q}, \\\\mathrm{D}-\\\\mathrm{S}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{A}-\\\\mathrm{S}, \\\\mathrm{B}-\\\\mathrm{R}, \\\\mathrm{C}-\\\\mathrm{P}, \\\\mathrm{D}-\\\\mathrm{Q}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{A}-\\\\mathrm{P}, \\\\mathrm{B}-\\\\mathrm{R}, \\\\mathrm{C}-\\\\mathrm{Q}, \\\\mathrm{D}-\\\\mathrm{S}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{A}-\\\\mathrm{S},  \\\\mathrm{B}-\\\\mathrm{P}, \\\\mathrm{C}-\\\\mathrm{Q}, \\\\mathrm{D}-\\\\mathrm{R}$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'<i>Ichthyosaurus</i>-fish like reptiles in 200 mya Coelacanth-caught in South Africa in 1938, Giant pteridophytes-fell to form coal deposits, Dinosaurs-disappeared about $65 \\mathrm{mya}$',NULL,'Easy','Match the following',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(47414,'biology',102,780,1685,'Evolution of man took place in','[{"text": "Australia", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Africa", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Central America", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Central Asia", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Humans first evolved in Africa and much of the human evolution occurred on that continent. The fossils of early humans who lived between 6 and 2 million years ago come entirely from Africa.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(47415,'biology',102,780,1685,'The common ancestor of apes and human is','[{"text": "<i>Homo neanderthelensis</i>", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "<i>Dryopithecus</i>", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "<i>Homo erectus</i>", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "<i>Ramapitheccus</i>", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Dryopithecus is considered as the common ancestor of the great apes and man. These are believed to have lived during Miocene(about 16 million years ago). The fossils of Dryopithecus were discovered from many parts of Africa, Europe and Sivalik hills of India.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(47416,'biology',102,780,1685,'Which was the first human being like creature?','[{"text": "Ramapithecus", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Dryopithecus", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Homo habilis", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Australopithecines", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Homo habilis.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(47417,'biology',102,780,1685,'Which statement is not true about Ramapithecus and Dryopithecus?','[{"text": "They existed about 15 million years ago", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Dryopithecus was more man like", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Ramapithecus was more Ape like", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both 2 and 3", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Dryopithecus was more ape-like while Ramapithecus was more man-like.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(47418,'biology',102,780,1685,'The only true statement about the prehistoric man <i>Homo habilis</i> is:','[{"text": "It was the first man who used fire", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "They probably did not eat meat.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "It was the first man who painted cave walls", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "It was the first man who used hides to protect body", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Homo habilis probably did not eat meat.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(47419,'biology',102,780,1685,'About how many years ago <i>Drypithecus</i> and <i>Ramapithecus</i> were existed?','[{"text": "15 million years ago", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "15 billion years ago", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "15 thousand years ago", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "18,000 years ago", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'They were present about 15 million years ago in Miocene epoch. They were fossil primate dating from the Middle and Late Miocene epochs (about 16.6 million to 5.3 million years ago).',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(47420,'biology',102,780,1685,'Which is not correctly matched among the following options?<div class="matching-question"></div>','[{"text": "<i>Homo habilis</i> - Brain capacity 650-800 cc", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "<i>Homo erectus</i> - large brain around $600 \\\\mathrm{cc}$.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Neanderthal man - Brain size of $1400 \\\\mathrm{cc}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Modern <i>Homo sapiens</i> - During ice age between 75,000-10,000 years ago", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Brain size in H. Erectus averages about $950 \\mathrm{~cm}$ while in a series of Middle Pleistocene crania from Africa and Europe, volume is about $1230 \\mathrm{~cm}$.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(47421,'biology',102,780,1685,'The first man who used to hides to protect their body:','[{"text": "Homo sapiens", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Neanderthal man", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Australopithecines", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Homo habilis", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'The Neanderthal man with a brain size of $1400 \\mathrm{cc}$ lived in near east and central Asia between 1,00,000-40,000 years back. They used hides to protect their body and buried their dead.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(47422,'biology',102,780,1685,'Pre historic cave arts were developed by:','[{"text": "<i>Homo habilis</i>", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Neanderthalensis", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Australopithecines", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "<i>Homo erectus</i>", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'The oldest known cave painting is a red hand stencil in Maltravieso cave, Cáceres, Spain. It has been dated using the uraniumthorium method to older than 64,000 years and was made by a Neanderthal.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(47423,'biology',102,780,1685,'Which statement is not true about Australopithecines?','[{"text": "They lived approx 2 million years ago", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "They hunted with stones", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "They probably lived in West African grasslands", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "They ate fruit", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'They probably lived in east African grassland.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(47424,'biology',102,780,1685,'Which statement is not true about <i>Homo habilis</i>?','[{"text": "First human-like being the hominid", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "They probably did not eat meat.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "The brain capacities were between 650 $800 \\\\mathrm{cc}$.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "They ate fruit", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'They showed the hominid features.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(47425,'biology',102,780,1685,'Which is correctly matched?<div class="matching-question"></div>','[{"text": "Modern man - First man who started agriculture", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Neanderthal man - First man who started to buried dead bodies", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Australopithecines ate meat", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Homo habilis probably ate only meat", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Neanderthal Man used hides to protect their body and buried their Neanderthal Man builts huts and used animal hides as clothing.So, the correct option is \'Neanderthal Man\'.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(47426,'biology',102,780,1685,'The correct sequence of evolution of man:<br>I. Homo habilis<br>II. Java Man<br>III. Modern Man<br>IV. Dryopithecus','[{"text": "II, I, III, IV", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "IV, I, II, III", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "I, II, III, IV", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "I, II, IV, III", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Australopithecus, Neanderthal Man, CroMagnon Man, Modern Man\'',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(47427,'biology',102,780,1685,'Which is considered as the connecting link between apes and men?','[{"text": "<i>Homo erectus</i>", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Dryopithecus", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Australopithecus", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "<i>Homo habilis</i>", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Australopithecus had features of man as well as apes as they were bipedal, were adapted to hold things. So they can be considered as connecting link between man and apes. So the correct answer is \'Australopithecus\'',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(47428,'biology',102,780,1685,'Australopithecus lives in','[{"text": "Central African grasslands, 2 mya", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "East African grasslands, 2 mya", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "West African grasslands, 15 mya", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "South African grasslands, 15 mya", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Australopithecus africanus is an extinct species of australopithecine which lived between about 3.3 and 2.1 million years ago in the Late Pliocene to Early Pleistocene of South Africa. The species has been rec overed from Taung, Sterkfontein, Makapansgat, and Gladysvale.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(47431,'biology',102,780,1685,'Cave art by prehistoric man was evolved about','[{"text": "5,000 years ago", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "10,000 years ago", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "18,000 years ago", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "40,000 years ago", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Cave art, generally, the numerous paintings and engravings found in caves and shelters dating back to the Ice Age (Upper Paleolithic), roughly between 40,000 and 14,000 years ago. See also rock art. The first painted cave acknowledged as being Paleolithic, meaning from the Stone Age, was Altamira in Spain.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(47432,'biology',102,780,1685,'Most of the cave paintings discovered are associated with','[{"text": "Peking", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Java", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Cromagnon", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Neanderthal", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Cro-Magnon man is closest ancestor of modern man. It lived in france and spain. It made paintings inside cave and ornaments of ivory. It had aesthetic sense.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(47433,'biology',102,780,1685,'What kind of evidence suggested that man is more closely related with chimpanzee than with other hominoid apes?','[{"text": "Evidence from DNA extracted from sex chromosomes, autosomes, and mitochondria", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Evidence from fossil remains and the fossil mitochondrial DNA alone", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Evidence from DNA from sex chromosomes only", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Comparison of chromosome morphology only", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'DNA extracted from sex chromosomes, autosomes, and mitochondria are the evidences which suggest that man is more closely related with chimpanzee than with other hominoid apes.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(47434,'biology',102,780,1685,'Select the correct statement','[{"text": "<i>Australopithecus</i> is the real ancestor of man", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "<i>Homo erectus</i> is the immediate ancestor of man", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Cro-Magnon mans fossil has been found in Ethiopia", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Cro-Magnon man is the most recent ancestor of Homosapiens", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'The features showed by Neanderthal man showed resemblance to the modern man and so were called the subspecies of the Homo sapiens. The DNA of the men differs from the modern man by $0.12 \\%$. The brain capacity of the Neanderthal man was higher than the modern man. However, the Cro-magnon man developed from the Neanderthal man and gave rise to the Homo sapiens and so the Cromagnon man is the direct ancestor of the Homo sapiens.',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(47435,'biology',102,779,1684,'Jurassic period of Mesozoic era is characterized by','[{"text": "Dinosaurs become extinct and angiosperms appear", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Flowering plants and first dinosaurs appear", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Radiation of reptiles and origin of mammal like reptiles", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Gymnosperms are dominant plants and first birds appear", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'In Jurassic period, gymnosperms were dominant and the plants included Cycads, Ginkgo and conifers.',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(47436,'biology',102,780,1685,'Which of the following changes for man in the course of evolution is probably useless?','[{"text": "Development of being erect", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Development of cranial capacity", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Development of opposable thumb", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Loss of tail", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Loss of tail is perhaps a relatively useless change for man in the course of evolution. The volume of cranial cavity and size of the skull increased in order to accommodate the large and complex brain.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(47437,'biology',102,780,1685,'Match the columns I and II.<div class="matching-question"><div class="column-left">A.<i>Homo habilis</i><br>B. <i>Homo neanderthalensis</i><br>C. <i>Homo erectus</i><br>D. <i>Homo sapiens</i></div><div class="column-right">P. 900 cc<br>Q. 650-800 cc<br>R. 1400 cc<br>S. 1350 cc</div></div>','[{"text": "$\\\\mathrm{A}-\\\\mathrm{R}, \\\\mathrm{B}-\\\\mathrm{P}, \\\\mathrm{C}-\\\\mathrm{Q}, \\\\mathrm{D}-\\\\mathrm{S}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{A}-\\\\mathrm{S}, \\\\mathrm{B}-\\\\mathrm{R}, \\\\mathrm{C}-\\\\mathrm{P},  \\\\mathrm{D}-\\\\mathrm{Q}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{A}-\\\\mathrm{P}, \\\\mathrm{B}-\\\\mathrm{R}, \\\\mathrm{C}-\\\\mathrm{Q}, \\\\mathrm{D}-\\\\mathrm{S}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{A}-\\\\mathrm{Q}, \\\\mathrm{B}-\\\\mathrm{R}, \\\\mathrm{C}-\\\\mathrm{P}, \\\\mathrm{D}-\\\\mathrm{S}$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'<i>Homo habilis</i>-650-800cc, <i>Homo neanderthalensis,</i>- $1400 \\mathrm{cc}$ <i>Homo erectus</i>-900cc, <i>Homo sapiens</i>-1350cc',NULL,'Easy','Match the following',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(47438,'biology',102,772,1677,'In the early atmosphere, the organic molecules once formed accumulated in water and their degradation was extremely slow. Why such transformation is not possible in the present atmosphere?','[{"text": "Microbes will decompose living particles that arise by more chance", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Reducing atmosphere will reduce the molecules", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Oxidising atmosphere will oxidize molecules", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both (1) and (3)", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'In the early atmosphere, the organic molecules once formed accumulated in water and their degradation was extremely slow because there were no microbes to decompose living particles and atmosphere was reducing. But this transformation is not possible in the present atmosphere beacause in the present atmosphere microbes will decompose living particles that arise by more chance and todays oxidizing atmosphere will oxidise molecules.',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(47439,'biology',102,779,1684,'Which of the following statements regarding the evolution of plants and animals are correct?<br>I. Reptiles evolved into birds<br>II. Fish with stout and strong fins could move on land and go back to water. This was about 350 million years ago.<br>III. Giant ferns fell to form coal deposits slowly.<br>IV. About 65 billion years ago dinosaurs died out.<br>V. <i>Archaeopteryx</i> is the connecting link between birds and reptiles.<br>The correct answer is','[{"text": "I and II", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "III and IV", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "V and I", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "I, II, III, & V", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'About 65 million years ago dinosaurs died out.',NULL,'Hard','MCQ type-2',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(47440,'biology',102,774,1679,'The change of the lighter coloured variety of peppered moth,&nbsp;<em>Biston betularia</em> to its darker variety (<em>carbonaria</em>) is due to','[{"text": "Deletion of a segment of genes due to industrial pollution", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Translocation of a block of genes in chromosomes in response to heavy carbons", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Industrial carbon deposited on the barks of the trees resulting in darker variety being more camouflaged", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Mutation of single mendelian gene for survival in smoke laden industrial environment.", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Impact of industrial carbon over melanin formation\'.',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(47441,'biology',102,772,1677,'Which of the following is not evidence for the role of endosymbiosis in the origin of eukaryotes?','[{"text": "The DNA in the eukaryotes nucleus codes for some enzymes in mitochondria", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Mitochondria reproduced by binary fission", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Chloroplasts have their own DNA.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "The inner membrane of a chloroplast is similar to prokaryotic membrane.", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Origin of a eukaryotic cell is by a symbiotic origin or by invagination. As per symbiotic origin of eukaryotic cell, primitive aerobic bacteria was engulfed by anaerobic predator cell and it could not digest the engulfed particle. So the engulfed anaerobic particle got itself established into the host cell as an symbiont (Endosymbiont). Predator host cell became an eukaryotic cell. The predator host cell which captured both aerobic bacteria and blue green algae became an eukaryotic plant cell.Aerobic bacteria established itself as mitochondria and blue green algae established itself as chloroplast.',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(47442,'biology',102,778,1683,'Read the following four (i-iv) statements :<br>i. Natural selection is a process in which heritable variations enabling better survival are enabled to reproduce greater number of progeny.<br>ii. When migration of a section of population to another place and population occurs, gene frequencies changes<br>iii. If loss of gene migration happens multiple times there would be a gene flow<br>iv. If loss of considerable gene from a small population occurs by chance it is called as genetic drift<br>How many of the above statements are true:-','[{"text": "Two", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "One", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Four", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Three", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'It is a process in which heritable variations help in the survival of an organism, enabling it to reproduce and give rise to a large number of offspring. There may be changes in the frequency of genes and alleles in future generations. It leads to the formation of new species.',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(47443,'biology',102,774,1679,'Which of these statements is true?','[{"text": "A higher percentage of dark moths were recaptured in the country compared to dark moths recaptured in the city.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "A higher percentage of light moths were recaptured in the city compared to dark moths recaptured in the city.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "A higher percentage of light moths were recaptured in the country compared to dark moths recaptured in the country.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "A higher percentage of light and dark moths were recaptured in the country compared to light and dark moths recaptured in the city.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'A higher percentage of light moths were recaptured in the country compared to dark moths recaptured in the country.',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','biology/102_47443_question_1.png'),
(47444,'biology',102,775,1680,'Darwin\'s finches are best example of adaptive radiation. Which of the following best describe this adaptive radiation?','[{"text": "The evolutionary process that allows for the change that occur within the same lineage.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "The genetic variability that can be found among individuals of same species.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "A sudden diversification of a group of organism from closely related species.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "The evolutionary process by which different forms, adapted to different niches, arose from a common ancestors.", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Presence of evolution of different species in a given geographical area starting from a point and radiating to other habitats is called Adaptive Radiation.',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(47445,'biology',102,776,1681,'Which is not a concept of Lamarksism?','[{"text": "Rate and survival of organisms are different due to variations.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Environmental pressure produces variations.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "An organ in constant use will grow in size.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Inheritance of acquired characters.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Lamark\'s theory is often called Inheritance of acquired characters theory of use and dis use of organ.<br>It includes 4 postulates<br>1) Internal forces tend to increase size of the body.<br>2) Formation of new organs is the result of need (felt by organism)<br>3) Development & power of action of an organ is directly proportional to its use and<br>4) All changes acquired by organism is transmitted by inheritance.',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(47446,'biology',102,777,1682,'Mark the correct statements.<br>I. Wings of insects and bats are analogous.<br>II. Homology in vertebrate brain indicates common ancestry.<br>III. Theory of aquired character was given by de Vries.<br>IV. After industrialisation, the white moth population decreased due to predators.<br>The correct option is','[{"text": "I, II and III", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "I, III and IV", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "II, III and IV", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "I, II and IV", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'I, II and IV are correct.',NULL,'Hard','MCQ type-2',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(47447,'biology',102,776,1681,'According to Darwinism, fitness level of which category of microbes is highest?','[{"text": "Which do not divide but is capable to resist any change in medium", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Which divide slowly and make few hundred individuals in an hour, all are sensitive to surroundings", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Which divide and make two types of colonies i.e., resistant and sensitive", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Which divide fast and make millions of individuals within hours, They have built in variations in terms of ability to utilize a feed component", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Fitness according to Darwin refers ultimately and only to the reproductive fitness of an organism. This fitness enables an organism to survive better, produce more progeny, and expand and save its own gene pool.',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(47448,'biology',102,779,1684,'Which of the following is incorrect?','[{"text": "Lobefin fish evolved into first amphibians.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Ichthosaurus evolved about 200 mya.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Stegosaurus was 20 feet in height and had huge fearsome dagger like teeth.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Reptiles of different shapes and size dominated on earth around 200 mya.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Tyrannosaurus rex was 20 feet in height and had huge fearsome dagger like teeth. Lobefin fish evolved into first amphibians. Theses fish had thick and stout fins, and could move on land move back to water. Around 200 mya, some reptiles went back to water and developed fish like characters, to form ichthyosaur. Reptiles of different shapes and sizes dominated on earth around 200 mya. Mesozoic era is called age of reptiles.',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(47486,'biology',102,778,1683,'Which type of selection explains industrial melanism observed in moth, <i>Biston betularia</i>','[{"text": "Stabilising", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Directional", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Disruptive", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Artificial", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Directional selection: Under this selection, individuals at one end of the frequency distribution do well light and hence more number of individuals of that type will be present in next generation.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,'NCERT Exemplar','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(47449,'biology',102,774,1679,'Read the following three statement (I to III) and mark the right option<br>I. The thorns in Bougainvillea and tendrils in cucurbits represent divergent evolution<br>II. The similarity in the eyes of octopus and monkeys is the result of convergent evolution<br>III. The potato and sweet potato are the examples of homology','[{"text": "II and III correct", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "I and II correct", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "I and III correct", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "All I, II and III correct", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'The potato (stem modification) and sweet potato (root modification) are the examples of analogy. Analogy is the similarity of function and superficial resemblance of structures that have different origins.',NULL,'Hard','MCQ type-2',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(47450,'biology',102,772,1677,'Mark the incorrect statement about the first noncellular form of life?','[{"text": "These capsules have molecules with no ability of self replication.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "They would have been giant molecules and the first living giant molecules was probably a RNA", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Originated about 3 billion years back", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "It was a cluster of nucleoprotein surrounded by lipid coat", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'The Capsules had molecules which had ability of self replication.',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(47451,'biology',102,774,1679,'"Given below are four statements (A - D) each with one or two blanks. Select the option which correctly fills up the blanks in two statements.<br>(A) Wings of butterfly and birds look alike and are the results of ____(i)____volution.<br>(B) Miller showed that ${\\rm{C}}{{\\rm{H}}_{\\rm{4}}}{\\rm{,}}\\,{{\\rm{H}}_{\\rm{2}}}{\\rm{,}}\\,{\\rm{N}}{{\\rm{H}}_{\\rm{3}}}$ and____ (i)____ when exposed to electric discharge in a flask resulted in formation of ____ (ii)____.<br>(C) Vermiform appendix is a ____(i)____organ and an ____(ii)____evidence of evolution.<br>(D) According to Darwin evolution took place due to ____(i)____and ____(ii)____ of the fittest.<br>Options:','[{"text": "(A) - (i) convergent,<br>(B) - (i) Oxygen, (ii) nucleosides.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "(B) - (i) Water vapour, (ii) Amino acids,<br>(C) - (i) vestigial, (ii) Anatomical.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "(C) - (i) vestigial, (ii) anatomical,<br>(D) - (i) Mutations, (ii) Multiplication.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "(D) - (i) small variations, (ii) survival,<br>(C) - (i) convergent, (ii) adaptive", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Wings of butterfly and birds look alike and are the results of Convergent evolution. According to Darwin evolution took place due to small variations and survival of the fittest.',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(47452,'biology',102,779,1684,'Some major events in the early history of life are mentioned here.<br>i. First animal<br>ii. First eukaryotes iii. First Genes<br>iv. First heterotrophic prokaryotes<br>v. First autotrophic prokaryotes.<br>Identify the option which places them in correct order','[{"text": "ii, iii, iv, i, v", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "iii, iv, v, ii, i", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "iv, iii, i, ii, v", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "v, ii, iii, iv, i", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Organisms have evolved from simpler forms to complex form. Hence the order of the events are genes first, then autotrophic prokaryotes, then heterotrophic prokaryotes, then eukaryotes and then animals',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(47453,'biology',102,777,1682,'Read the following three statements (I to III ) and mark the most appropriate option<BR>I. The fitness in the \'survival of the fittest\' is based upon the characteristics that are inherited<BR>II. Darwin\'s variations were small and directional<BR>III. The fitness is the end result of ability to adapt','[{"text": "Only II and III correct", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Only I and II correct", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Only I and III correct", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "All I, II, and III correct", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'All are correct',NULL,'Hard','MCQ type-2',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(47454,'biology',102,772,1677,'Read the following statements and select the correct once<BR>I. The early belief of the spontaneous origin of life was disproved by Louis Pasteur<br>II. Founder of \'theory of catastophism is Georges Cuvier<br>III. Cosmozoic theory of the origin of life was proposed by Saurez.<br>IV. Idea of life originates from pre-existing life is referred to as biogenesis theory.','[{"text": "I, II and III", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "I, II and IV", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "I, III and IV", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "I and IV", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Father Suarez proposed theory of special creation.',NULL,'Hard','MCQ type-2',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(47455,'biology',102,778,1683,'Which of the following statements are incorrect?<br>I. Hugo de vries called mutations as saltation because they brought speciation.<br>II. Genetic drift is directional and can cause elimination of certain alleles in the population.<br>III. Genetic bottleneck effect leads to sharp increase in the size of population or anthropogene due to environmental reasons.<br>IV. Founder effect results in formation of a different genotype in a new settlement.','[{"text": "I and IV", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "III and IV", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "I and III", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "II and III", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Genetic drift is a mechanism of evolution in which allele frequencies of a population change over generations due to chance (sampling error).Genetic drift occurs in all populations of non-infinite size, but its effects are strongest in small populations. Genetic drift may result in the loss of some alleles (including beneficial ones) and the fixation, or rise to $100 \\% 100 \\% 100$, percent frequency, of other alleles. Genetic drift can have major effects when a population is sharply reduced in size by a natural disaster (bottleneck effect) or when a small group splits off from the main population to found a colony (founder effect).',NULL,'Hard','MCQ type-2',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(47456,'biology',102,775,1680,'An Australian mole is actually a marsupial rather than a placental animal like the North American or European mole. The two animals are similar in appearance because','[{"text": "The selection pressures on both were similar", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "They have undergone a long period of coevolution", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "There are practically no placental mammals in Australia", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Marsupials and placental mammals are closely related", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Australian mole resemble to that of the mole of Europe or North America. This is the result of convergent evolution, in which similar features develop separately in different species due to same selection pressures.',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(47457,'biology',102,776,1681,'Giraffes have long neck this length will not further increase any more from generations to generations this is because of natural selection.','[{"text": "Directional", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Stabilizing", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Disruptive", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Centrifugal", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Once the average value of fitness (Phenotype) meets the enivornmental changes, directional selection will be over taken by stabilizing natural selection.',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(47458,'biology',102,779,1684,'We know much about fossil fishes, snails and corals but not much about ancient sea weeds. Why do you suppose this is the case?','[{"text": "A mass extinction wiped out the sea weeds but animals survived.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Plants moved onto land leaving only animals in the sea.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "There were no sea weeds in the ancient oceans.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Sea weeds were too soft to fossilize well.", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Sea weeds include multicellular algae and they are too soft to fossilize.',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(47459,'biology',102,776,1681,'"Frequency of resistant viruses increases in population by using medication". Which of the following options is/are correct explaining the above statement?','[{"text": "Nature favours those characteristics which provide advantage in the current environment", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Drugs create resistant pathogens", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Nature selects resistant individuals that are already present in populations and survived.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both (1) and (3)", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'This is according to use and disuse theory, i.e., if a organ is used more often then it gets stronger. The person who does the work in fields and hold the sickles etc in the hand, for them the epidermis on palm becomes thicker.',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(47460,'biology',102,777,1682,'Find the incorrect statement','[{"text": "Evolution at genetic level like sickle cell anemia is macroevolution", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Whales and seals do not contain gill slits in adults as their adaptation to aquatic life is secondary", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "According to Darwin, any population has built in variation in characteristics", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Darwin highlighted the importance of small and gradual differences in evolution, whereas devries focused an abrupt and drastic changes ancause of origin of species.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Indeed, the sickle-cell variant (i.e., the $\\mathrm{HbS}$ allele) has been identified in four distinct genetic backgrounds in different African populations, suggesting that the same mutation arose independently several times through convergent evolution.',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(47461,'biology',102,778,1683,'Which of the following statement is false?','[{"text": "Speciation is generally a function of time, for tropics, unlike temperate regions subjected to frequent glaciations for past.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "The sum total of genes of all the species in an area constitutes the gene pool.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "The gene frequency refers to the proportion of an allele in the gene pool as compared with other alleles.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both (1) and (3)", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Gene pool is the set of all genes (genetic information) of a single species in any population.',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(47462,'biology',102,778,1683,'A potential damage to a population that has been greatly reduced in number is the','[{"text": "Loss of genetic variability", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Tendency towards assortative mating", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Reduced gene flow", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Hardy - Weinberg disequilibrium", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Potential damage to a population that has been greatly reduced in number is loss in its genetic variability (Bottle neck effect)',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(47463,'biology',102,778,1683,'Selection will not eliminate a lethal recessive allele from a large population of diploid organisms because:','[{"text": "Gene fixation will occur in the population", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Heterozygotes are at a selective advantage", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "The allele will have some good effects and these will become dominant", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "There will always be some heterozygote carrier for the allele", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Heterozygotic condition is when one gene is dominant and another gene is the recessive. In such conditions lethal gene will be recessive and dominant gene will express itself. The recessive gene can be expressed in the next generation and selection cannot eliminate lethal recessive allele from large population of diploid organisms. Recessive lethals produce effect only in homozygous condition, their heterozygotes are normal.',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(47464,'biology',102,772,1677,'Which of the following is the correct sequence of events in the origin of life.<br>i. Formation of protobionts<br>ii. Synthesis of organic monomers.<br>iii. Synthesis of organic polymers.<br>iv. Formation of DNA- based genetic systems.','[{"text": "i, ii, iii, iv", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "i, iii, ii, iv", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "ii, iii, i, iv", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "ii, iii, iv, i", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'The correct sequence of events in the origin of life - (1) Synthesis of organic monomers, (2) Synthesis of organic polymer, (3) Formation of protobionts, (4) Formation of DNA-based genetic systems.',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(47465,'biology',102,778,1683,'In a hypothetical human population in HardyWeinberg equilibrium, the frequencies of the alleles IA, IB and i are respectively $0.1,0.3$ and 0.6 . What percentage of the population is expected to have \' $\\mathrm{B}$ \' type blood?','[{"text": "27", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "9", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "36", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "45", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'frequency of $I^{\\mathrm{A}}=0.1$<br>${{\\rm{I}}^{\\rm{B}}}{\\rm{ = 0}}{\\rm{.3}}$<br>${\\rm{I = 0}}{\\rm{.6}}$<br>Percentage of population with $\\mathrm{B}$ type blood<br>$=\\mathrm{I}^{\\mathrm{B}} \\mathrm{I}^{\\mathrm{B}}+\\mathrm{I}^{\\mathrm{B}} \\mathrm{I}^{6}$<br>$=(0.3 \\times 0.3)+(0.3 \\times 0.6)$<br>$=(0.09)+(0.18)$<br>$=0.27$<br>$=27 \\%$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(47466,'biology',102,776,1681,'Read the given statements<br>I. Theory of pangenesis was given by Lamarck to explain the inheritance of acquired character.<br>II. According to Ernst Haeckel, Ontogeny is recapitulation of phylogeny i.e., the development of an organism reveals the evolutionary history.<br>III. In modern synthetic theory, the unit of evolution is individual.<br>IV. Wings of bats and birds are homologous organ.','[{"text": "I, II and V are correct", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "II and IV are correct", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "I, IV and V are correct", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "I, II, and IV are correct", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Theory of pangenesis was given by Darwin to explain the inheritance of characters from parents to off springs. Haeckel gave a biogenetic Law stating "Ontogeny recapitulates phylogeny". Wings of bats and birds are homologous organ.',NULL,'Hard','MCQ type-2',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(47497,'biology',102,772,1677,'<div class="matching-question"><div class="column-left">Earliest organisms that appeared on Earth were nongreen and heterotrophic<br></div><div class="column-right">The first cellular form of life did not possibly originate till about 2000 million years ago</div></div>','[{"text": "Statement A is correct but Statement B is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Statement A is incorrect but Statement B is correct.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both statements are correct.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Statements are incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Both are correct.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ type-2',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(47467,'biology',102,778,1683,'In a population of 1,000 individuals, 400 are homozygous $\\mathrm{Rh}+(\\mathrm{DD}), 240$ are heterozygous $\\mathrm{Rh}+(\\mathrm{Dd})$ and 360 are $\\mathrm{Rh}-(\\mathrm{dd})$. The frequency of recessive allele (d) in that population is','[{"text": "0.48", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "0.6", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "0.52", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "0.96", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Total populations $=1,000$<br>homozygous dominant $(\\mathrm{Rh}+)(\\mathrm{DD})=400=\\mathrm{P} 2$<br>heterozygous $\\quad(\\mathrm{Rh}+)(\\mathrm{Dd})=240=2 \\mathrm{Pq}$<br>homozygous recessive (Rh-) (dd) $360=\\mathrm{q} 2$<br>frequency of homozygous recessive individuals<br>$=360 / 100=0.36$<br>$\\mathrm{q} 2=0.36$<br>${\\rm{q = }}\\sqrt {{{\\rm{q}}^{\\rm{2}}}} {\\rm{ = 0}}{\\rm{.6}}$<br>frequency of recessive allele $(\\mathrm{q})=0.6$.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(47468,'biology',102,773,1678,'Mark the incorrect statements','[{"text": "Homology in vertebrates brain indicates their common ancestry", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "The idea of survival of fittest of Alfred R Wallace was based on his studies on Galapagos islands", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "The fitness of the individuals, according to Charles Darwin, means reproductive fitness", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "All of the above", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Alfred.R.Wallace divided the whole world into 6 major biogeographical realms.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(47469,'biology',102,778,1683,'Fishermen use nets of a large mesh size to capture larger adult fish, leaving smaller adults. This would result in','[{"text": "Disruptive selection as more fish acquire peripheral character value at both ends of the distribution curve", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Stabilizing selection as individuals with mean weight have maximum chance of survival", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Directional selection leading to an increase in the mean weight", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Directional selection leading to a decrease in the mean weight", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Higher mortality of large adult fish (compared to small adult fish) leads to decreased reproductive success of larger fish. The mean size of the fish body would gradually decrease over generations. This is directional selection which eliminates one extreme phenotype from an array of phenotypes.',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(47470,'biology',102,780,1685,'Study the following table and identify the correct option for A, B and C.','[{"text": "A - 700cc, B - 3400 years ago, C - Homo erectus", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "A - 500cc, B - 11000 years ago, C - Homo habilis", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "A - 800cc, B - 50,000 years ago, C - Ramapithecus", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "A - 500cc, B - 100,000 years ago, C - Homo erectus", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'A-500cc,<br>B-100,000 years ago,<br>C-Homo erectus',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','biology/102_47470_question_1.png'),
(47471,'biology',102,780,1685,'Which of the following statements are True (T) or False (F).<br>I. Homo sapiens arose in Africa and moved across continents and developed into distinct races.<br>II. Agriculture came around 18000 years ago and human settlement started.<br>III. During ice- age between 75000-10000 years ago modern Homo sapiens arose<br>IV. Neanderthal man had a brain size of 1400 cc and lived between 100000-40000 years back.','[{"text": "${\\\\rm{I - T,}}\\\\,{\\\\rm{II - F,}}\\\\,{\\\\rm{III - T,}}\\\\,{\\\\rm{IV - F}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{I - T,}}\\\\,{\\\\rm{II - F,}}\\\\,{\\\\rm{III - F,}}\\\\,{\\\\rm{IV - T}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{I - T,}}\\\\,{\\\\rm{II - F,}}\\\\,{\\\\rm{III - T,}}\\\\,{\\\\rm{IV - T}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{I - T,}}\\\\,{\\\\rm{II - F,}}\\\\,{\\\\rm{III - F,}}\\\\,{\\\\rm{IV - F}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'The cradle of human evolution is Africa. So, Homo sapiens arose in Africa and moved to different continents to develop into distinct races.Agriculture came around 10,000 years ago, and human settlement started.Modern Homo sapiens arose between 75000-10000 years ago Neanderthal man was present in central and eastern Asia between 100,000 -40,000 years back. It has a brain size of around 1400 cc.',NULL,'Hard','MCQ type-2',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(47472,'biology',102,780,1685,'Mark the correct statements','[{"text": "Agriculture came around 18000 yrs back", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "The skull of modern human resembles more closely to baby chimpanzee", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Homo erectus lived in east and central Asia and used hides to protect their bodies", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "All of these", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Agriculture came around 10,000 Yrs ago, lived in central asias Java mostly.',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(47473,'biology',102,780,1685,'Arrange the following human ancestors in ascending order, from earliest to latest:<br>i. Homo erectus<br>ii. Australopithecus iii. Homo habilis<br>iv. Homo neanderthalensis','[{"text": "ii, i, iii, iv", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "i, ii, iii, iv", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "iv, iii, i, ii", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "ii, iii, i, iv", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Homo habilis (650-800 cc), Homo erectus (850-1200 cc),Neanderthal man (1450 cc), Cro-Magnon man (1600 cc).',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(47474,'biology',102,778,1683,'Read the following statements and select the correct option.<br>I. Branching descent and natural selection are the two key concepts of Darwinian Theory of Evolution<br>II. When more individuals of a population acquire a mean character value, it is called disruption.<br>III. Hardy-Weinberg equilibrium says that allele frequencies in a population are stable and is constant from generation to generation.<br>IV. Genetic drift changes the existing gene or allelic frequency in future generations','[{"text": "III alone is correct", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "II, III and IV are correct", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "I, III and IV are correct", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both I and III are correct", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'When more individuals of a population acquire a mean character value, it is called stabilizing.',NULL,'Hard','MCQ type-2',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(47475,'biology',102,780,1685,'Consider the following statements.<br>I. Homo habilis: The brain capacities were between 650-800cc.<br>II. Erect man or Homo erectus is skull was flatter than that of modern man.<br>III. Homo erectus probably ate meat<br>IV. Neanderthal man is regarded as most recent ancestor of today\'s man.<br>Choose the correct statements.','[{"text": "I, III and IV", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "I, II and III", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "I and II", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "I and III", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Homo erectus is the extinct species of human ancestor that most likely originated in Africa. The brain was smaller and the teeth larger than in modern humans. Members of Homo erectus have a cranial capacity of 600-800 cc.',NULL,'Hard','MCQ type-2',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(47553,'biology',102,774,1679,'Which one of the following are analogous structures?','[{"text": "Wings of Bat and Wings of Pigeon.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Gills of Prawn and Lungs of Man.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Thorns of <i>Bougainvillea</i> and Tendrils of <i>Cucurbita</i>", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Flippers of Dolphin and Legs of Horse", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Analogous organs are those that perform similar functions but have entirely different embryonic origins (anatomical)',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2014,'AIPMT','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(47476,'biology',102,779,1684,'Vivaparity is considered to be more evolved because','[{"text": "The young ones are left on their own", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "The young ones are protected", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "The young ones are protected inside the mother\'s body and are looked after they are born leading to more chances of survival", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "The embryo takes a long time to develop", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'As the young progenies are protected inside the body of the mother as well as they are looked after they are born, leads to more chances of survival, viviparity is considered to be more evolved.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,'NCERT Exemplar','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(47477,'biology',102,774,1679,'Animal husbandry and plant breeding programmes are the examples of','[{"text": "Reverse evolution", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Artifical selection", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Mutation", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Natural selection", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Animal husbandry and plant breeding programmes are the examples of artificial selection. The artificial selection is the modification of species by selective breeding. Animals or plants with desirable characteristics are interbred with the aim of altering the genotype and producing a new strain of the organism for a specific purpose.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,'NCERT Exemplar','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(47478,'biology',102,776,1681,'Variations during mutations of meiotic recombinations are','[{"text": "Random and directionless", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Random and directional", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Small and directional", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Random, small and directional", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Hugo de Vries (1901) put forward a theory of evolution, called mutation theory. The theory states that evolution is a jerky process where new varieties and species are formed by mutations (discontinuous variations) that function as raw material of evolution.Mutations appear all of a sudden. They become operational immediately. Mutations appear in all conceivable directions.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,'NCERT Exemplar','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(47479,'biology',102,774,1679,'Palaentological evidences for evolution refer to the','[{"text": "Development of embryo", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Homologus organs", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Fossils", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Analogous organs", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Palaentology is the study of extinct organisms, including their structure, environment, evolution and distribution, as revealed by their fossil remains. Fossils are the remains or traces of any organism that lived in the geological past. In general only the hard parts of organisms become fossilised (e.g., bones, teeth, shells and wood) but under certain circumstances the entire organisms is preserved.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,'NCERT Exemplar','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(47480,'biology',102,774,1679,'Analogous organs arise due to','[{"text": "Divergent evolution", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Artificial selection", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Genetic drift", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Convergent evolution", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'The organs which have similar functions but are different in their structural details and origin are called analogous organs. The analogous structures are the results of convergent evolution. The wings of an insect are analogous to wings of a bird. It is due the fact that the basic structure of the wings of the insects is different from the wings of bird. However, their function is similar.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,'NCERT Exemplar','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(47481,'biology',102,774,1679,'The bones of forelimbs of whale, bat, cheetah and man are similar in structure, because','[{"text": "One organism has given rise to another", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "They share a common ancestor", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "They perform the same function", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "They have biochemical similarities", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'The bones of forelimbs of whale, bate, cheetah, and man are similar in structure because they are homologous organs. The organs which have the same fundamental structure but are different in functions are called homologous organs.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,'NCERT Exemplar','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(47482,'biology',102,779,1684,'Which of the following is an example for link species','[{"text": "Lobed finfish", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Sea weed", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Dodo bird", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Chimpanzee", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Lung fish (lobed fin fish) forms a link between fishes and amphibians and hence are link species.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,'NCERT Exemplar','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(47483,'biology',102,772,1677,'In 1953 S. L. Miller created primitive earth condition in the laboratory and gave experimental evidence for origin of first form of life from pre-existing non-living organic molecules. The primitive earth conditions created include','[{"text": "Low temperature, volcanic storms, atmosphere rich in oxygen", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Low temperature, volcanic storms, reducing atmosphere", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "High temperature, volcanic storms, non- reducing atmosphere", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "High temperature, volcanic storms, reducing atmosphere containing $\\\\mathrm{CH}_{4}$, $\\\\mathrm{NH}_{3}$ etc.", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'The conditions on earth were - high temperature, volcanic storms, reducing atmosphere containing $\\mathrm{CH}_{4}, \\mathrm{NH}_{3}$, etc. In 1953, S.L. Miller, an American scientist created similar conditions in a laboratory scale. He created electric discharge in a closed flask containing $\\mathrm{CH}_{4}, \\mathrm{H}_{2}, \\mathrm{NH}_{3}$ and water vapour at 8000C.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,'NCERT Exemplar','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(47484,'biology',102,774,1679,'Appearance of antibiotic-resistant bacteria is an example of','[{"text": "Adaptive radiation", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Transduction", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Pre-existing variation in the population", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Divergent evolution", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'When a bacterial population encounters a particular antibiotic, those sensitive to it die. But some bacteria having mutations become resistant to antibiotics. Such resistant bacteria survive and multiply quickly as the competing bacteria have died. Soon the resistance providing genes become wide spread and entire bacterial population become resistant. Appearance of antibodies resistant bacteria is example of pre-existing variation.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,'NCERT Exemplar','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(47485,'biology',102,776,1681,'Match the scientists listed under column \' I \' with ideas listed Column \'II\'<div class="matching-question"><div class="column-left">A. Darwin<br>B. Oparin<br>C. Lamarck<br>D. August Weisman</div><div class="column-right">P. Abiogenesis<br>Q. Use and disuse of organs<br>R. Germ plasm theory and neodarwinism<br>S. Evolution by natural selection</div></div>','[{"text": "A - P, B - S, C - Q, D - R", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "A - S, B - P, C - Q, D - R", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "A - Q, B - S, C - R, D - P", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "A - S, B - R, C - Q, D - P", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Darwin = related with evolution by natural selection.<br>Oparin $=$ related with abiogenesis theory.<br>Lamarck $=$ related with use and disuse of organs.<br>A. Weisman $=$ A substance present in the germ cell carries hereditary information.',NULL,'Medium','Match the following',NULL,'NCERT Exemplar','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(47487,'biology',102,772,1677,'Evolution of life shows that life forms had a trend of moving from','[{"text": "Land of water", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Dryland to wet land", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Fresh water to sea water", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Water to land", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Evolution of life shows a trend of moving from water to land .The early vertebrates were fishes lived in only water. Some fishes have attempted a change in their mode of life from aquatic to terrestrial and evolved into amphibians. Certain amphibians then transformed into the reptiles which are true terrestrial vertebrates. Some of the reptiles latter evolved into birds and than mammals.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,'NCERT Exemplar','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(47488,'biology',102,774,1679,'Which of the following is used as an atmospheric pollution indicator','[{"text": "Lepidoptera", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Lichens", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Lycopersicon", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Lycopodium", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Lichens can be used as an atmospheric pollution indicator as they are sensitive to oxides of nitrogen and sulphur can\'t grow well.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,'NCERT Exemplar','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(47489,'biology',102,778,1683,'${{\\rm{(p + q)}}^{\\rm{2}}}{\\rm{ = }}{{\\rm{p}}^{\\rm{2}}}{\\rm{ + 2pq + }}{{\\rm{q}}^{\\rm{2}}}{\\rm{ = 1}}$ represents an equation used in','[{"text": "Population genetics", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Mendelian genetics", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Biometrics", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Molecular genetics", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'${{\\rm{(p + q)}}^{\\rm{2}}}{\\rm{ = }}{{\\rm{p}}^{\\rm{2}}}{\\rm{ + 2pq + }}{{\\rm{q}}^{\\rm{2}}}{\\rm{ = 1}}$ is a mathematical representation of \'Hardy-Weinberg principle\' used in population genetics. It states that the allelic and genotypic frequencies of a population are stable and is constant over generations.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,'NCERT Exemplar','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(47490,'biology',102,778,1683,'For the MN-blood group system, the frequencies of $\\mathrm{M}$ and $\\mathrm{N}$ alleles are 0.7 and 0.3 respectively. The expected frequency of $\\mathrm{MN}$-blood group bearing organisms is likely to be','[{"text": "$42 \\\\%$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$49 \\\\%$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$9 \\\\%$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$58 \\\\%$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'The expected frequency of M N blood group bearings organisms is likely to be $42 \\%$.<br>According to Haedy-Weinberg equation,$\\mathrm{p}^{2}+2 \\mathrm{pq}+\\mathrm{q}^{2}=1$<br>where ${\\rm{p = }}$ Frequency of $\\mathrm{M}$ alleles.<br>$\\mathrm{p}^{2}=$ Frequency of homozygous<br>dominant individuals.<br>${\\rm{q = }}$ frequency of $\\mathrm{N}$ alleles<br>$\\mathrm{q}^{2}=$ Frequency of homozygous recessive individuals.<br>Frequency of heterozygous individuals.<br>So, $(0.7)^{2}+(0.3)^{2}+2 \\mathrm{pq}=1$<br>$0.49+0.09+2 \\mathrm{pq}=1$<br>$\\therefore 2 p q=0.42=$<br>Frequency of heterozygous individuals.i.e., $42 \\%$.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,'NCERT Exemplar','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(47491,'biology',102,780,1685,'The most accepted line of descent in human evolution is','[{"text": "Australopithecus $\\\\rightarrow$ Ramapithecus $\\\\rightarrow$ Homo sapiens $\\\\rightarrow$ Homo habilis", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Homo erectus $\\\\rightarrow$ Homo habilis $\\\\rightarrow$ Homo sapiens", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Ramapithecus $\\\\rightarrow$ Homohabilis $\\\\rightarrow$ Homo erectus $\\\\rightarrow$ Homo sapiens", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Australopithecus $\\\\rightarrow$ Ramapithecus $\\\\rightarrow$ Homo erectus $\\\\rightarrow$ Homo habilis $\\\\rightarrow$ Homo sapiens", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'The most accepted line of descent in human evolution is Ramapithecus, Homo habilis, Homo erectus, Homo sapiens.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,'NCERT Exemplar','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(47492,'biology',102,772,1677,'The theory of spontaneous generation stated that','[{"text": "Life arose from living forms only", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Life can arise from both living and non- living", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Life can arise from non-living things only", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Life arises spontaneously, neither from living nor from the non-living", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'As per the theory of spontaneous generation or Abiogenesis theory, life can arise from nonliving things only. stated that life can arise from non-living things only.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,'NCERT Exemplar','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(47493,'biology',102,774,1679,'Fossils are generally found in','[{"text": "Sedimentary rocks", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Igneous rocks", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Metamorphic rocks", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Any type of rock", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Fossils are generally found in sedimentary rocks, which are formed by the gradual deposition of slit, sand or calcium carbonate over millions of years in regions such as lakes or sea during their formation, the dead animals are carried to the sea or large lake, sink down and get buried in the rocks and converted into fossils.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,'NCERT Exemplar','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(47494,'biology',102,772,1677,'<div class="matching-question"><div class="column-left">Theory of chemical evolution proposed that life came from pre-existing non-living organic molecules.<br></div><div class="column-right">Primitive earth conditions led to the production of organic molecules.</div></div>','[{"text": "Statement A is correct but Statement B is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Statement A is incorrect but Statement B is correct.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both statements are correct.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Statements are incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Both are correct.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ type-2',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
