(48900,'biology',80,475,959,'Kranz means ____ and is a ____ of the arrangement of cells','[{"text": "Wreath, refraction", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Wreath, reflection", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Bead, refraction", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Bead, reflection", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Kranz means ‘wreath’ and is a reflection of the arrangement of cells.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(48901,'biology',80,475,959,'Bundle sheath cells of  ${C_4}$ plants show _','[{"text": "Thick walled cells and intercellular spaces", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Thin walled cells and granal chloroplasts", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Thick walled cells and no intercellular spaces", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Thin walled cells and no intercellular spaces", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Bundle sheath cells pf ${C_4}$ plants show thick walls impervious to gaseous exchange and no intercellular spaces.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(48902,'biology',80,475,959,'The special type of leaf anatomy present in the ${C_4}$ pathway plants is called?','[{"text": "Kranz anatomy", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Crown anatomy", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Either 1 or 2", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "None of these", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Special anatomy in the leaves of  ${C_4}$ plants is known as Kranz anatomy.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(48903,'biology',80,475,959,'What is incorrect statement about bundle sheath cells?','[{"text": "No intercellular spaces", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Large number of chloroplasts", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Thick walls", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Single layer", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'The bundle sheath cells may form several layers around the vascular bundles; they are characterised by having a large number of chloroplasts, thick walls impervious to gaseous exchange and no intercellular spaces.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(48904,'biology',80,475,959,'The large cells around the vascular bundles of the  ${C_4}$ pathway plants are called?','[{"text": "Mesophyll cells", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Bundle sheath cells", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Passage cells", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both 1 and 2", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'The particularly large cells around the vascular bundles of the  ${C_4}$ plants are called bundle sheath cells, and the leaves which have such anatomy are said to have ‘Kranz’ anatomy.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(48905,'biology',80,475,959,'Which of the following statements are correct about ${C_4}$ plants?<br>(1) Do not tolerate high temperature <br>(2) They have a special type of leaf anatomy<br>(3) They lack a process called photorespiration<br>(4) Have lesser productivity of biomass<br>(5) Show a response to high light intensities','[{"text": "1,2,5", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "2,3,5", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "2,3,4", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "All are correct", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'${C_4}$ plants are special: They have a special type of leaf anatomy, they tolerate higher temperatures, they show a response to highlight intensities, they lack a process called photorespiration and have greater productivity of biomass.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ type-2',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(48906,'biology',80,475,959,'Plants that are adapted to dry tropical regions have?','[{"text": "${C_3}$ cycle", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${C_4}$  cycle", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${C_2}$  cycle", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both 2 and 3", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Plants that are adapted to dry tropical regions have the  ${C_4}$ pathway',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(48907,'biology',80,475,959,'The first $C{O_2}$ fixation product of the  ${C_4}$  pathway is?','[{"text": "Citric acid", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Malic acid", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Oxaloacetic acid", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Aspartic acid", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Plants have the  ${C_4}$ oxaloacetic acid as the first $C{O_2}$ fixation product.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(48908,'biology',80,475,959,'${C_4}$  pathway is also known as?','[{"text": "Hatch and Slack Pathway", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Ranson and Thomas Pathway", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "EMP Pathway", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "TCA Cycle", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Named after the Australian botanists, ${C_4}$  pathway is also known as Hatch and Slack pathway.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(48909,'biology',80,474,958,'The given diagram represents Calvin cycle. Identify the three stages (A-C) of Calvin cycle.','[{"text": "A - Carboxylation, B - Reduction, C - Regeneration", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "A - Reduction, B - Regeneration, C - Carboxylation", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "A - Regeneration, B - Carboxylation, C - Reduction", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "A - Reduction, B - Carboxylation, C - Regeneration", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'A. Carboxylation<br>B. Reduction<br>C. Regeneration of RuBP',NULL,'Easy','MCQ type-3',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','biology/80_48909_question_1.png'),
(48910,'biology',80,474,958,'The given diagram represents the calvin cycle.Name the  $C{O_2}$ acceptor (A) and first stable product (B) of  ${C_3}$ cycle respectively.','[{"text": "A - Phosphoglyceric acid, B - Phosphoenol pyruvate", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "A - RuBP, B - Phosphoglyceric acid", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "A - RuBP, B - Oxaloacetic acid", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "A - Phosphoenol pyruvate, B - Oxaloacetic acid", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'A. $C{O_2}$ acceptor - RuBisCO<br>B. First stable product - 3-PGA',NULL,'Easy','MCQ type-3',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','biology/80_48910_question_1.png'),
(48911,'biology',80,474,958,'In which plant Calvin experimented the radioactive isotopy to discover the stable product of  ${C_3}$  cycle?','[{"text": "<i>Chlorella</i> - a green algae", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "<i>Cholera</i> - a green algae", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Sugarcane - a green algae", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Tobacco- a monocot", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Calvin used the unicellular green algae, Chlorella to discover the first stable product (3-PGA) of  ${C_3}$ cycle by radioactivity.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(48912,'biology',80,474,958,'How many Calvin cycle forms one hexose molecule?','[{"text": "2", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "6", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "4", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "8", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Hexose, a 6 carbon containing molecule can be formed by 6 turns of Calvin Cycle.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(48913,'biology',80,474,958,'In  ${C_3}$  plants, the first stable product of photosynthesis during dark reaction is','[{"text": "3-phosphoglyceric acid", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Phosphoglyceraldehyde", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Malic acid", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Oxaloacetic acid", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'RuBP undergoes carboxylation to form the first stable product of Calvin Cycle, a ${C_3}$ acid named 3-PGA (phosphoglyceric acid).',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(48914,'biology',80,474,958,'During photosynthesis, when PGA is changed into phosphoglyceraldehyde, which of the following reaction occurs?','[{"text": "Oxidation", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Reduction", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Electrolysis", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Hydrolysis", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Second step of Calvin cycle is a series of reaction where 3-PGA undergoes reduction. Conversion of PGA into phosphoglyceraldehyde is a reduction reaction.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(48915,'biology',80,474,958,'In Calvin cycle, if one molecule of RuBP is carboxylated than how many PGA molecule will be formed?','[{"text": "1", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "3", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "4", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "2", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'One molecule of RuBP gets carboxylated to form 2 molecules of PGA.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(48916,'biology',80,474,958,'How many turns of Calvin cycle produces one molecule of glucose?','[{"text": "Eight", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Six", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Three", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "One", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'To make one molecule of glucose 6 turns of the Calvin cycle are required.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(48917,'biology',80,474,958,'In dark cycle, one molecule of glucose formation needs','[{"text": "12 ATP and 12 NADPH", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "14 ATP and 12 NADPH", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "16 ATP and 12 NADPH", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "18 ATP and 12 NADPH", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'For every  $C{O_2}$  molecule entering the Calvin Cycle, 3 ATP and 2 NADPH are required. For 1 glucose molecule, 6  $C{O_2}$  molecules will enter the Calvin Cycle. 6 turns of the Calvin Cycle will need 18 ATP and 12 NADPH.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(48918,'biology',80,474,958,'In dark reaction, regeneration of RuBP needs','[{"text": "2 molecules of ATP", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "1 moleule of ATP", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "3 molecules of ATP", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "4 nolecules of ATP", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'The regeneration steps in dark reaction require one ATP for phosphorylation to form RuBP.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(48919,'biology',80,474,958,'The first stable product of Calvin cycle has','[{"text": "2 carbon atoms", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "3 carbon atoms", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "4 carbon atoms", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "6 carbon atoms", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'The first stable product of Calvin Cycle is a  ${C_3}$  acid (three carbon containing acid). Carboxylation of RuBP results in the formation of 2 molecules of 3-PGA (Phosphoglyceric acid)',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(48920,'biology',80,474,958,'Every  $C{O_2}$ molecule entering the Calvin cycle requires','[{"text": "2 molecules of NADPH and 3 molecules of ATP for its fixation", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "2 molecules of NADPH and 2 molecules of ATP for its fixation", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Variable amount of ATP", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Only ATP", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'For every  $C{O_2}$ molecule entering the Calvin cycle, 3 molecules of ATP and 2 of NADPH are required.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(48921,'biology',80,474,958,'Which process of Calvin cycle requires RuBP?','[{"text": "Carboxylation", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Reduction", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Regeneration", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "None of these", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'RuBP, a 5-C ketose sugar is the carbon accepting molecule in the first step - Carboxylation of Calvin Cycle. Carboxylation is the most crucial step of the Calvin cycle where  $C{O_2}$  is utilised for the carboxylation of\nRuBP.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(48922,'biology',80,474,958,'The initial enzyme of Calvin cycle is','[{"text": "Ribulose 1,5 diphosphate carboxylase", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Ribulose 1,3 diphosphate carboxylase", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Hexokinase", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Cytochrome oxidase", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'First step of Calvin cycle is carboxylation. The substrate Ribulose 1,5- biphosphate is catalysed by carboxylase enzyme. Ribulose 1,5- diphosphate carboxylase is used.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(48923,'biology',80,474,958,'Carboxylation (${C_3}$ cycle) is catalysed by','[{"text": "Carboxylase", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "RuBP carboxylase", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "RuBP oxygenase", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both 2 and 3", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Carboxylation of RuBP is catalysed by RuBisCO or RuBP carboxylase oxygenase. It results in the formation of two molecules of 3-PGA (3-Phosphoglyceric acid)',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(48924,'biology',80,474,958,'Calvin cycle occurs in','[{"text": "Stroma", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Cytoplasm", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Mitochondria", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Thylakoid", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Calvin/${C_3}$  cycle, being a part of dark reaction occurs in stroma of chloroplast.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(48925,'biology',80,474,958,'Ribulose diphosphate carboxylase enzyme catalyses the carboxylation reaction between','[{"text": "Oxaloacetic acid and acetyl CoA", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$C{O_2}$  and ribulose 1,5 diphosphate", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$C{O_2}$  and ribulose 1,3 diphosphate", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$C{O_2}$ and ribulose 1,4 diphosphate", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Carboxylation is the most crucial step of the Calvin cycle where  $C{O_2}$ is utilised for the carboxylation of Ribulose 1,5 - diphosphate. This reaction is catalysed by the enzyme RuBP carboxylase which results in the formation of two molecules of 3-PGA.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(48926,'biology',80,474,958,'Calvin cycle includes','[{"text": "Carboxylation", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Reduction", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Regeneration of RuBP", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "All of these", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Calvin cycle comprises of 3 stages - carboxylation, reduction and regeneration of RuBP',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(48927,'biology',80,474,957,'Ribulose bisphosphate is a','[{"text": "5-carbon sugar", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Ketose sugar", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$C{O_2}$  acceptor", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "All of them", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'RuBP is a 5-carbon ketose sugar which acts as the primary acceptor of  $C{O_2}$ in Calvin Cycle.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(48928,'biology',80,474,957,'The similarity between  ${C_3}$ and  ${C_4}$ pathway is:','[{"text": "Both are equally efficient.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Organic acid is formed as the first product of  $C{O_2}$  fixation.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both take place in mesophyll cell", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both takes place in light", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'In both the cycles, organic acid is formed as first stable product. Those plants in which the first product of  $C{O_2}$  fication is a  ${C_3}$ acid (PGA), i.e., the  ${C_3}$  pathway, and those in which the first product was a  ${C_4}$  acid (OAA), i.e., the  ${C_4}$  pathway.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(48929,'biology',80,474,957,'During the dark reaction, the acceptor of $C{O_2}$  is','[{"text": "$NADP{H_2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "RuBP", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${H_2}O$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$C{O_2}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'The primary acceptor molecule of  $C{O_2}$  is a 5-carbon ketose sugar – ribulose bisphosphate (RuBP).',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(48930,'biology',80,474,957,'Why Calvin cycle is called  ${C_3}$-cycle?','[{"text": "Primary  $C{O_2}$ acceptor is  ${C_3}$ compound", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Many intermediate compounds are ${C_3}$ compound", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "1st stable product is 3 PGA which is a ${C_3}$ compound", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "3 molecules of sugar are formed", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'The first product formed in Calvin Cycle was 3-phosphoglyceric acid or in short PGA. It is a three carbon containing or  ${C_3}$ acid. Hence, calvin cycle is also known as  ${C_3}$ cycle.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(48931,'biology',80,474,957,'Which pathway is common to all the photosynthetic plant?','[{"text": "${C_3}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${C_4}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "1 and 2", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "None of them", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'The Calvin pathway occurs in all photosynthetic plants; it does not matter whether they have ${C_3}$ or ${C_4}$ (or any other) pathways.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(48932,'biology',80,474,957,'Biosynthetic phase of life uses all except _________ that is produced in photochemical phase.','[{"text": "ATP", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "NADPH", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${O_2}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$C{O_2}$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Dark/Biosynthetic phase does not directly depend on the presence of light but is dependent on the products of the light reaction, i.e., ATP, NADPH, $C{O_2}$  and  ${H_2}O$. ${O_2}$ is not required.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(48933,'biology',80,473,956,'The given diagram shows synthesis of ATP in the chloroplast. This mechanism is explained by-','[{"text": "Splitting of water", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Photo phosphorylation", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Chemiosmotic hypothesis", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Electron transport system", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'The given figure explains the formstion of ATP by chemiosmotic hypothesis.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ type-3',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','biology/80_48933_question_1.png'),
(48934,'biology',80,474,957,'ATP and NADPH produced in light reaction are used immediately for','[{"text": "Synthesis of sugar in dark phase", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Synthesis of sugar in biosynthetic phase", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Reduction of carbon dioxide", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "All of them", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'ATP and NADPH formed in light reactions are used to drive the processes leading to the synthesis of food, more accurately, sugars in dark/biosynthetic phase. This is carried out by Calvin cycle where the fixation of C$C{O_2}$  occurs.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
