(49031,'biology',80,471,950,'When intensity of light is low, chloroplasts align themselves in the mesophyll cell in such a way that their flat surfaces are','[{"text": "Antiparallel to the cell wall", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Perpendicular to the cell wall", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Parallel to the cell wall", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Middle in the cell", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Usually the chloroplasts align themselves parallel to the walls of the mesophyll cells, such that they get the optimum quantity of the incident light.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49032,'biology',80,471,950,'With in chloroplast, the membranous system is made up of','[{"text": "Grana", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Stroma lamellae", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Matrix- Stroma", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "1 and 2", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Within the chloroplast there is a membranous system consisting of grana, the stroma lamellae',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49033,'biology',80,471,950,'Large number of chloroplast are present in which of the following cells?','[{"text": "Parenchymatous cell", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Mesophyll cell", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Stem cell", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Xylem", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Mesophyll cells in the leaves have a large number of chloroplasts.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49034,'biology',80,471,950,'The membranous system of grana is responsible for','[{"text": "Trapping light energy but no ATP and  $NADP{H_2}$  formation", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Trapping light energy and also for fixation of $C{O_2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "For ATP and  $NADP{H_2}$  formation but not for light trapping", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "For light capturing and also for $NADP{H_2}$  and ATP formation", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'The membrane system is responsible for trapping the light energy and also for the synthesis of ATP and  $NADP{H_2}$.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49035,'biology',80,471,950,'Photosynthesis occurs in','[{"text": "Green leaf", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Green stem", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Mesophyll cells", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "All of them", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Photosynthesis does take place in the green leaves of plants (mesophyll cells) but it does so also in other green parts of the plants like green stem.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49037,'biology',80,470,949,'Match the following Columns I & II and choose the correct option.<div class="matching-question"><div class="column-left">A. T.W Engelmann<br>B. Jan Ingenhousz<br>C. Joseph Priestley<br>D. Cornelius van Neil<br>E. Julius von Sachs</div><div class="column-right">P. Bell Jar Experiment<br>Q. Evolution of oxygen<br>R. Glucose is stored as starch<br>S. Light and chlorophyll play a role in photosynthesis<br>T. ${O_2}$  is releasedfrom ${H_2}O$</div></div>','[{"text": "A-P, B-Q, C-R, D-T, E-S", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "A-Q, B-P, C-S, D-T, E-R", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "A-Q, B-S, C-P, D-R, E-T", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "A-Q, B-S, C-P, D-T, E-R", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'A. T.W Engelmann - Q. Evolution of oxygen<br>B. Jan Ingenhousz - S. Light and chlorophyll play a role in photosynthesis<br>C. Joseph Priestley - P. Bell Jar experiment<br>D. Cornelius van Neil - T.  ${O_2}$  is released from  ${H_2}O$<br>E. Julius von Sachs - R. Glucose is stored as starch',NULL,'Easy','Match the following',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49038,'biology',80,470,949,'Match the following Columns I & II and choose the correct option.<div class="matching-question"><div class="column-left">A. Jan Ingenhousz<br>B. Joseph Priestley<br>C. T.W Engelmann<br>D. Julius von Sachs</div><div class="column-right">P. First action spectrum of photosynthesis<br>Q. Glucose is produced in the green parts of the plants<br>R. Sunlight is essential to the plant processes<br>S. Plants restore the air whatever breathing animals and burning candles remove</div></div>','[{"text": "A-R , B-S , C-P , D-Q", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "A-S , B-P , C-Q , D-R", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "A-R , B-Q , C-P , D-S", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "A-R , B-S , C-Q , D-P", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'A. Jan Ingenhousz - R. Sunlight is essential to the plant processes<br>B.Joseph Priestley - S. Plants restore the air whatever breathing animals and burning candles remove<br>C. T.W Engelmann - P. First action spectrum of photosynthesis<br>D. Julius von Sachs - Q. Glucose is produced in the green parts of the plants.',NULL,'Easy','Match the following',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49039,'biology',80,470,949,'Match the following Columns I & II and choose the correct option.<div class="matching-question"><div class="column-left">A. Joseph Priestley<br>B. Jan Ingenhousz<br>C. T.W Engelmann<br>D. Cornelius van Neil</div><div class="column-right">P. 1897-1985<br>Q. 1730-1799<br>R. 1733-1804<br>S. 1843-1909</div></div>','[{"text": "A-Q, B-R, C-P, D-S", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "A-R, B-Q, C-S, D-P", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "A-R, B-S, C-Q, D-P", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "A-Q, B-S, C-P, D-R", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'A. Joseph Priestley - R. 1733 to 1804<br>B.  Jan Ingenhousz - Q. 1730 to 1799<br>C. T.W Engelmann - S. 1843 to 1909<br>D. Cornelius van Neil - P. 1897 to 1985',NULL,'Easy','Match the following',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49040,'biology',80,470,949,'By using a _________ isotope it can be proved that  ${O_2}$  is released from water during photosynthesis','[{"text": "Radioactive", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Heavy", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both (1) and (2)", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "None of these", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'The  ${O_2}$  released is from water; this was proved using radio isotope techniques.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49041,'biology',80,470,949,'Purple and green sulphur bacteria performs','[{"text": "Oxygenic photosynthesis", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Anoxygenic photosynthesis", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Nitrogen fixation", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Chemosynthesis", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Purple and green sulphur bacteria use  ${H_2}S$  as their hydrogen donor. Hence, no oxygen is released. They perform anoxygenic photosynthesis.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49042,'biology',80,470,949,'What are the probable oxidation product of  ${H_2}S$ in purple and green sulphur bacteria?','[{"text": "Sulphur", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Sulphate", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "1 and 2", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "None of these", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'When  ${H_2}S$  is the hydrogen donor for purple and green sulphur bacteria, the oxidation product is sulphur or sulphate depending on the organism',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49043,'biology',80,470,949,'Van Neil performed the experiment on','[{"text": "Mint plant", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Green algae", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Green plants", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Purple and Green bacteria", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Cornelius van Neil, a microbiologist performed experiments on green and purple bacteria.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49044,'biology',80,470,949,'Cornelius Van Neil was a','[{"text": "Botanist", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Microbiologist", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Biochemist", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Biologist", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Cornelius van Neil was a microbiologist.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49045,'biology',80,470,949,'Who demostrated that photosynthesis is\nessentially a light-dependent reaction in which hydrogen from a suitable oxidizable compound reduces  $C{O_2}$  to carbohydrates?','[{"text": "Engelmann", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Von Sachs", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "C. B. Van Niel", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Jan Ingenhousz", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'A milestone contribution to the understanding of photosynthesis was that made by a microbiologist, Cornelius van Neil (1897-1985), who, based on his studies of purple and green bacteria, demonstrated that\nphotosynthesis is essentially a light-dependent reaction in which hydrogen from a suitable oxidisable compound reduces carbon dioxide to carbohydrates.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49046,'biology',80,470,949,'Which of the following pigments resemble the action spectrum produced on the basis of Engelmann experiment?','[{"text": "Chlorophyll a only", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Chlorophyll a, Chlorophyll b", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Chlorophyll b, Xanthophyll", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Carotenoids only", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'The action spectrum described by T. W. Engelmann resembles roughly the absorption spectra of chlorophyll a and b.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49047,'biology',80,470,949,'A first action spectrum of photosynthesis was described on the basis of experiment performed by T. W. Engelmann on _________ algae.','[{"text": "Green", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Purple and green sulphur bacteria", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Brown", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Blue green", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'T. W. Engelmann performed an experiment on <em>Cladophora</em>, a green alga and derived the first action spectrum of photosynthesis.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49048,'biology',80,470,949,'The first action spectrum of photosynthesis was observed by','[{"text": "Jan Ingenhousz", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Priestley", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "TW Engelmann", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Cornelius van Niel", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'The first action spectrum of photosynthesis was described by T. W. Engelmann.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49051,'biology',80,470,949,'Jan Ingenhousz did an experiment with an aquatic plant and concluded that','[{"text": "Plants release  ${O_2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Plants release  $C{O_2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Only green parts of plant release  ${O_2}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Only green parts of plant release  $C{O_2}$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Ingenhousz in an elegant experiment with an aquatic plant showed that in bright sunlight, small bubbles were formed around the green parts while in the dark they did not. Later he identified these bubbles to be of oxygen. Hence he showed that it is only the green part of the plants that could release oxygen.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49052,'biology',80,470,949,'The given diagram shows a series of experiments performed by Priestley. Identify the hypothesis given by Priestley?','[{"text": "Plants restore to the air whatever breathing animals and burning candles remove", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "It is only the green part of the plants that could release oxygen", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Sunlight is essential to the plant process", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "All the above", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Priestley hypothesised that plants restore to the air whatever breathing animals and burning candles remove. Ingenhousz showed that sunlight is essential for plant process. He also found that only green parts of the plants could release oxygen.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ type-3',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','biology/80_49052_question_1.png'),
(49053,'biology',80,470,949,'Find out correct statement about the given diagram. A-Scientists, B-Year.','[{"text": "A - Jan Ingenhousz, B - 1972", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "A - Joseph Priestley,  B - 1860", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "A - Joseph Priestley , B - 1770", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "A - Julius von Sachs, B - 1860", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Bell Jar experiment was performed by Joseph Priestley in 1770.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ type-3',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','biology/80_49053_question_1.png'),
(49054,'biology',80,470,949,'Who proved that light is essential for plant process that purifies foul air by placing Priestly setup once in light and once in dark?','[{"text": "Jan Ingenhousz", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Joseph Priestley", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Von Sachs", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Cornelius Van Niel", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Using a similar setup as the one used by Priestley, but by placing it once in the dark and once in the sunlight, Jan Ingenhousz showed that sunlight is essential to the plant process.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49055,'biology',80,470,949,'_______ performed an elegant experiment with an _______ plant to show that in bright sunlight, small bubble, were formed around the green parts, while in dark they do not die.','[{"text": "Priestly, &emsp; Aquatic", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Ingenhousz, &emsp;  Terrestrial", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Priestly,  &emsp; Terrestrial", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Ingenhousz, &emsp; Aquatic", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Ingenhousz in an elegant experiment with an aquatic plant showed that in bright sunlight, small bubbles were formed around the green parts while in the dark they did not.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49056,'biology',80,470,949,'_________ in _______performed a series of experiments that revealed the essential role of air in growth of green plants.','[{"text": "Jan Ingenhousz, 1770", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Joseph Priestley, 1770", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Joseph Priestley, 1733", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Cornelius Van Niel, 1733", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Joseph Priestley in 1770 performed a series of experiments that revealed the essential role of air in the growth of green plants.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49057,'biology',80,470,949,'Bell Jar experiment was performed by','[{"text": "Jan Ingenhousz", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Joseph Priestley", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Von Sachs", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Cornelius Van Niel", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Bell Jar experiment was performed by Joseph Priestley.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49058,'biology',80,470,949,'The scientist, who discovered oxygen is','[{"text": "Priestley, 1774", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Ingenhousz, 1774", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Priestley, 1770", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Ingenhousz, 1770", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Joseph Priestley discovered oxygen in 1774.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL);
INSERT INTO `qb_questions` (`id`, `subject_id`, `chapter_id`, `topic_id`, `subtopic_id`, `question_html`, `options`, `correct_index`, `explanation`, `explanation_image_url`, `difficulty`, `qtype`, `year`, `tag`, `created_at`, `question_image_url`) VALUES
(49059,'biology',80,469,948,'Which of the following substance is used in half leaf experiment to absorb  $C{O_2}$?','[{"text": "$KS{O_4}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$KOH$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$HN{O_3}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Starch", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'KOH (Potassium hydroxide) is used in Moll’s half leaf experiment. It absorbs $C{O_2}$ and helps us prove that carbon dioxide is required for photosynthesis.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49060,'biology',80,469,948,'Half leaf experiment proved that _________ is required for photosynthesis.','[{"text": "Carbon dioxide", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Chlorophyll", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Light", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Oxygen", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Moll’s half leaf experiment proved that carbon dioxide is essential for photosynthesis.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49061,'biology',80,469,948,'In an experiment, a leaf was partially covered with black paper, and was exposed to light. On testing these leaves for starch, it was seen that uncovered part turned__________.','[{"text": "Blue-black", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Brown", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Black-red", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "1 and 2", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'The uncovered or exposed part of the leaf turned blue-black indicating presence of starch. The blue colour is a positive starch test result. This depicts that sunlight is essential for photosynthesis.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49062,'biology',80,469,948,'Which of the following is required for photosynthesis?','[{"text": "Chlorophyll", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$C{O_2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Light", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "All of them", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Chlorophyll (green pigment of the leaf), light and  $C{O_2}$  are required for photosynthesis to occur.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49063,'biology',80,469,948,'An experiment using two leaves, a variegated leaf or a leaf that was covered with black paper and one that was exposed to light on testing these leaves for starch shows that photosynthesis occurs only in _________ part of leaf and in the_________ of sunlight','[{"text": "Non-green, presence", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Green, presence", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Non-green, absence", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Green, absence", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'An experiment using two leaves, a variegated leaf or a leaf that was covered with black paper and one that was exposed to light on testing these leaves for starch shows that photosynthesis occurs only in green parts of leaf and in the presence of sunlight.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49065,'biology',80,469,948,'Photosynthesis is important because','[{"text": "It is the primary source of all food on earth", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Release of  ${O_2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "1 and 2", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "It releases ATP for work", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Photosynthesis is important due to two reasons: it is the primary source of all food on earth. It is also responsible for the release of oxygen into the atmosphere by green plants',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49066,'biology',80,469,948,'Photosynthesis is a','[{"text": "Physical process", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Chemical process", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Photochemical process", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Physico–chemical process", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Photosynthesis is a physico–chemical process by which the plants use light energy to drive the synthesis of organic compounds.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49067,'biology',80,469,948,'All living forms on earth depend on _________ for energy.','[{"text": "Sun", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Water", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Celestial bodies like meteorites", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Moon", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'All living forms on earth depend on sunlight for energy.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49068,'biology',80,476,960,'CAM helps plants in','[{"text": "Secondary growth", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Disease resistance", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Reproduction", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Conserving water", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'CAM plants are succulent xerophytes, which need to conserve water. In these plants stomata remains closed during daytime and open during night which helps to check transpiration.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2011,'AIPMT','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49069,'biology',80,471,950,'In land plants, the guard cells differ from other epidermal cells in having','[{"text": "Cytoskeleton", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Mitochondria", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Endoplasmic reticulum", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Chloroplasts", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'The guard cells are bean-shaped in surface view, while the epidermal cells are irregular in shape. The guard cells contain chloroplasts, so they can manufacture food by photosynthesis (The epidermal cells do not\ncontain chloroplasts)',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2011,'AIPMT','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49070,'biology',80,476,960,'The correct sequence of cell organelles during photorespiration is','[{"text": "Chloroplast &rarr; golgicomplex &rarr; mitochondria", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Chloroplast &rarr; rough endoplasmic reticulum &rarr; dictyosomes", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Chloroplast &rarr; peroxisome &rarr; mitochondria", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Chloroplast &rarr; vacuole &rarr; peroxisome", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Photorespiration requires the sequence of chloroplast &rarr; peroxisome &rarr; mitochondria.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2012,'AIPMT','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49071,'biology',80,476,960,'A process that makes important difference between ${C_3}$ and ${C_4}$ plants is','[{"text": "Transpiration", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Glycolysis", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Photosynthesis", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Photorespiration", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Photorespiration is light-dependent process which occurs in ${C_3}$ plants. During this process uptake of ${O_2}$ and release of $C{O_2}$ takes place which is an opposite process of photosynthesis. In ${C_4}$ plants photorespiration does not take place. It has kranz anatomy.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2012,'AIPMT','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49072,'biology',80,470,949,'Anoxygenic photosynthesis is characteristic of','[{"text": "<em>Rhodospirillum</em>", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "<em>Spirogyra</em>", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "<em>Chlamydomonas</em>", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "<em>Ulva</em>", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Anoxygenic photosynthesis is a type of photosynthesis in which ${O_2}$ is not released. <em>Rhodospirillum </em>is a purple non- sulphur bacteria. It helps in organism to trap light energy and store it as chemical energy. Anoxygenic photosynthesis also occurs in green sulphur bacteria and heliobacteria.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2014,'AIPMT','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49073,'biology',80,473,954,'The oxygen evolved during photosynthesis comes from water molecules. which one of the following pairs of elements is involved in this reaction?','[{"text": "Maganesium and Chlorine", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Manganese and Chlorine", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Manganese and Potassium", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Magnesium and Molybdenum", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Photolysis of water requires the ions – manganese and chlorine.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2015,'AIPMT','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49074,'biology',80,471,950,'The structures that are formed by stacking of organized flattened membranous sacs in the chloroplasts are','[{"text": "Grana", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Stroma lamellae", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Stroma", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Cristae", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Flat membranous structures known as thylakoids stack one on one to form grana.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2015,'AIPMT','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49075,'biology',80,476,960,'The process which makes major difference between ${C_3}$ and ${C_4}$ plants is','[{"text": "Glycolysis", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Calvin cycle", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Photorespiration", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Respiration", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'The process of photorespiration is also called as photosynthetic carbon oxidative cycle or PCO cycle. It is a process associated with photosynthetic tissues (green plants). Photorespiration is light-dependent process\nwhich occurs in ${C_3}$ plants. In ${C_4}$ plants photorespiration does not take place.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2016,'NEET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49076,'biology',80,473,956,'In a chloroplast the highest number of protons are found in','[{"text": "Antennae complex", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Stroma", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Lumen of thylakoids", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Inter membrane space", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'A proton gradient is established across the membrane of thylakoid. Highest number of protons are accumulated in the lumen of thylakoids.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2016,'NEET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49077,'biology',80,472,951,'Water soluble pigments found in plant cell vacuoles are','[{"text": "Xanthophylls", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Chlorophylls", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Carotenoids", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Anthocyanins", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Anthocyanins are water soluble pigments found in plant cell vacuoles.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2016,'NEET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49097,'biology',80,473,955,'Assimilatory power is:','[{"text": "$\\\\mathrm{NADPH}_{2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "ATP", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "ATP and $\\\\mathrm{NADPH}_{2}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{FADH}_{2}$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'The products of light reactions are oxygen, NADPH and ATP which are also known as assimilatory power. NADPH and ATP are further utilized in biosynthetic reactions taking place in the stroma and fixing carbon dioxide (or reducing it) to synthesize sugars.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2013,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49078,'biology',80,472,951,'Emerson’s enhancement effect and Red drop have been instrumental in the discovery of','[{"text": "Oxidative phosphorylation", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Photophosphorylation and non-cyvlic electron transport", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Two photosystems operating simultaneously", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Photophosphorylation and cyclic electron transport", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'The reduction in the rate of photosynthesis when a monochromatic beam of more than 680nm is used is known as the Emerson effect or Red drop effect. When a monochromatic beam of two different wavelengths are applied simultaneously, the rate of photosynthesis will be more than the total sum of photosynthesis carried put seperately using 2 light beams. This is known as the Emerson’s enhancement effect. It was given by Robert Emerson. It gave the conclusion that photosynthesis process will involve two light reactions, one carried out at a higher wavelength and another carried out at a lower wavelength.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2016,'NEET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49079,'biology',80,475,959,'A plant in your garden avoids photorespiratory losses, has improved water use efficiency, shows high rates of photosynthesis at high temperatures and has improved efficiency of nitrogen utilization. In which of the following physiological groups would you assign this plant?','[{"text": "${C_3}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${C_4}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "CAM", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Nitrogen fixer", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'All these features points towards ${C_4}$ plants. They lack photorespiration, lose less water compared to ${C_3}$ plants, show tolerance to high temperature and evolved an efficient nitogren utilization mechanism.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2016,'NEET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49080,'biology',80,469,948,'Water vapour comes out from the plant leaf through the stomatal opening. Through the same stomatal opening carbon di oxide diffuses into the plant during photosynthesis. Reason out the above statements using one of the following options','[{"text": "Both processes cannot happen simultaneously", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both processes can happen together\\nbecause the diffusion coefficient of water and $C{O_2}$ is different", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "The above processes happen only during night time", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "One process occurs during day time and other at night.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Evolution of water vapour and intake of carbon dioxide are simultaneous processes because their diffusion coefficients are different. Much more water leaves the leaf compared to the $C{O_2}$ intake.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2016,'NEET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49081,'biology',80,477,964,'With reference to factors affecting the rate of photosynthesis, Which of the following statements is not correct?','[{"text": "Increasing atmospheric $C{O_2}$ concentration up to $0.05\\\\% $ can enhance $C{O_2}$ fixation rate", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${C_3}$ plants respond to higher temperatures with enhanced photosynthesis while ${C_4}$ plants have much lower temperature optimum", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Tomato is a greenhouse crop which can be grown $C{O_2}$ - enriched atmosphere for higher yield", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Light saturation for $C{O_2}$ fixation occurs at $10\\\\% $ of full sunlight", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'In ${C_3}$ plants, due to increased photorespiration, photosynthesis is decreased at higher temperature. ${C_4}$ plants have higher temperature optimum because of the presence of pyruvate phosphate dikinase enzyme, which is sensitive to low temperature.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2017,'NEET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49082,'biology',80,474,957,'Phosphoenol pyruvate (PEP) is the primary $C{O_2}$ acceptor in:','[{"text": "${C_4}$ plants", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${C_2}$ plants", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${C_3}$ and ${C_4}$ plants", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${C_3}$  plants", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Phosphoenol pyruvate (PEP) is 3C\ncompound which serves as primary $C{O_2}$ acceptor in the mesophyll cell cytoplasm of ${C_4}$ plants.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2017,'NEET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49083,'biology',80,473,952,'Which of the following is not a product of light reaction of photosynthesis?','[{"text": "NADPH", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "NADH", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "ATP", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Oxygen", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Products of light reactions are ATP, NADPH and Oxygen. NADH is a product of respiration process',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2018,'NEET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49084,'biology',80,470,949,'Oxygen is not produced during photosynthesis by','[{"text": "<i>Cycas</i>", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "<i>Nostoc</i>", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Green sulphur bacteria", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "<i>Chara</i>", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'In Purple and green sulphur bacteria, instead of water (${H_2}O$), Hydrogen sulphide (${H_2}S$) is used as hydrogen donor. Hence the product of oxidation is sulphur or sulphate depending upon the organism and its not oxygen.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2018,'NEET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49085,'biology',80,475,959,'The first stable product of $C{O_2}$ fixation in sorghum is:','[{"text": "Pyruvic acid", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Oxaloacetic acid", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Succinic acid", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Phosphoglyceric acid", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Oxaloacetate (OAA), malate, and aspartate (Asp) are substrates for the ${C_4}$ acid cycle that underpins the $C{O_2}$ concentrating mechanism of ${C_4}$ photosynthesis. In this cycle, OAA is the immediate, short-lived, product of the initial $C{O_2}$ fixation step in ${C_4}$ leaf mesophyll cells',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2021,'NEET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49086,'biology',80,473,955,'Which of the following statements is incorrect?','[{"text": "Both ATP and $NADP{H^ + }\\\\,{H^ + }$ are synthesized during non-cyclic photophosphorylation.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Stroma lamellae have PS I only and lack NADP reductase.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Grana lamellae have both PS I and PS II.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Cyclic photophosphorylation involves both PS I and PS II.", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Cyclic photophosphorylation involves only with PSI whereas noncyclic photophosphorylation is with both PSI and PSII.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2021,'NEET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49087,'biology',80,475,959,'Given below are two statements:<div class="matching-question"><div class="column-left">The primary $C{O_2}$ acceptor in ${C_4}$ plants is phosphoenolpyruvate and is found in the mesophyll cells.</div><div class="column-right">Mesophyll cells of ${C_4}$ plants lack RuBisCo enzyme.<br>In the light of the above statements, choose the correct answer from the options given below.</div></div>','[{"text": "Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Statement I is correct but statement II is incorrect", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Statement I is incorrect but statement II is correct", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Statement I and Statement II are correct", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'The primary $C{O_2}$ acceptor in ${C_4}$ plant is PEP and is found in the mesophyll cells. Mesophyll cells of ${C_4}$ plants lack RuBiSCO enzyme.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ type-2',2022,'NEET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49088,'biology',80,475,959,'What is the role of large bundle shealth cells found around the vascular bundles in ${C_4}$ plants?','[{"text": "To increase the number of chloroplast for the operation of Calvin cycle", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "To enable the plant to tolerate high temperature", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "To protect the vascular tissue from high light intensity", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "To provide the site for photorespiratory pathway", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'The bundle sheath cells may form several layers around the vascular bundles; they are characterized by having a large number of chloroplasts, thick walls impervious to gaseous exchange and no intercellular spaces.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2022,'NEET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49089,'biology',80,473,952,'The reaction centre in PS II has an absorption maxima at','[{"text": "680 nm", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "700 nm", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "660 nm", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "780 nm", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'In PS-I, the reaction centre chlorophyll a\nhas an absorption peak at 700 nm, while in PSII, reaction centre has an absorption maxima at\n680 nm.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2023,'NEET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49090,'biology',80,473,956,'Which of the following combinations is required for chemiosmosis?','[{"text": "Membrane, proton pump, proton gradient, ATP synthase", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Membrane, proton pump, proton gradient, NADP synthase", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Proton pump, electron gradient, ATP synthase", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Proton pump, electron gradient, NADP synthase", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Chemiosmosis requires a membrane, a\nproton pump, a proton gradient and ATP\nsynthase.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2023,'NEET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49091,'biology',80,474,958,'How many ATP and ${\\rm{NADP}}{{\\rm{H}}_{\\rm{2}}}$ are required for the synthesis of one molecule of Glucose during Calvin cycle?','[{"text": "12 ATP and 12 ${\\\\rm{NADP}}{{\\\\rm{H}}_{\\\\rm{2}}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "18 ATP and 12 ${\\\\rm{NADP}}{{\\\\rm{H}}_{\\\\rm{2}}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "12 ATP and 16 ${\\\\rm{NADP}}{{\\\\rm{H}}_{\\\\rm{2}}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "18 ATP and 16 ${\\\\rm{NADP}}{{\\\\rm{H}}_{\\\\rm{2}}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'For every ${\\rm{C}}{{\\rm{O}}_{\\rm{2}}}$ molecule entering the Calvin cycle, 3 molecules of ATP and 2 of  ${\\rm{NADP}}{{\\rm{H}}_{\\rm{2}}}$ are required. For the synhtesis of one molecule of glucose, 6 turns of the cycle are required. Thus, ATP and ${\\rm{NADP}}{{\\rm{H}}_{\\rm{2}}}$ molecules required for synthesis of one molecule of glucose during Calvin cycle will be<br>$ \\to {\\rm{6 \\times }}\\left[ {\\begin{array}{*{20}{c}}\n{{\\rm{3ATP}}}\\\\\n{{\\rm{2NADP}}{{\\rm{H}}_{\\rm{2}}}}\n\\end{array}} \\right]{\\rm{ = }}\\begin{array}{*{20}{c}}\n{{\\rm{18ATP}}\\,{\\rm{and}}}\\\\\n{{\\rm{12NADP}}{{\\rm{H}}_{\\rm{2}}}}\n\\end{array}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2023,'NEET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49092,'biology',80,473,955,'Photosynthesis cannot continue for long if during light reaction, only cyclic photophosphorylation takes place. This is because','[{"text": "There is unidirectional cyclic movement of the electrons", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "There is no evolution of $\\\\mathrm{O}_{2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Only ATP is formed, $\\\\mathrm{NADPH}^{+}+\\\\mathrm{H}^{+}$is not formed", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Photosystem I stops getting excited at a wavelength of light beyond $680 \\\\mathrm{~nm}$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Assimilatory power, i.e., ATP and $\\mathrm{NADPH}_{2}$ are produced during light reaction of photosynthesis. As only cyclic photophosphorylation takes place only ATP is formed and photosynthesis cannot continue for long because in absence of $\\mathrm{NADPH}_{2}$. Dark reaction of photosynthesis do not take place.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2008,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49093,'biology',80,473,955,'Which statement about photosynthesis is false?','[{"text": "Photosynthesis is a redox process in which water is oxidised and carbon dioxide is reduced.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "The enzymes required for carbon fixation are located only in the grana of chloroplasts.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "In green plants, both PS I and PS II are required for the formation of $\\\\mathrm{NADPH}+\\\\mathrm{H}^{+}$.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "The electron carriers involved in photophosphorylation are located on the thylakoid membranes.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'The Calvin cycle or dark reaction causing carbon dioxide fixation takes place in the stroma of chloroplasts because it has enzymes essential for fixation $\\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ of and synthesis of sugar.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2009,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49094,'biology',80,470,949,'In an experiment demonstrating the evolution of oxygen in <i>Hydrilla</i>, sodium bicarbonate is added to water in the experimental set-up. What would happen if all other conditions are favourable?','[{"text": "Amount of oxygen evolved increases as the availability of carbon dioxide increases.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Amount of oxygen evolved decreases as the availability of carbon dioxide increases.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Amount of oxygen evolved increases as carbon dioxide in water is absorbed by sodium bicarbonate.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Amount of oxygen evolved decreases as carbon dioxide in water is absorbed by sodium bicarbonate.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'The addition of sodium bicarbonate to water in the given experimental set-up causes the availability of more carbon dioxide for photosynthesis thus amount of oxygen evolved in expected to increase.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2009,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49095,'biology',80,475,959,'In $\\mathrm{C}_{4}$ pathway, the $\\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ fixation in mesophyll cells is carried out by the enzyme:','[{"text": "Rubisco", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "PEP carboxylase", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Pyruvate decarboxylase", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Pyruvate dehydrogenase", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'In $\\mathrm{C}_{4}$ plants, the carbon dioxide that enters the mesophyll cells is accepted by a 3 -carbon molecule called phospho enol pyruvate (PEP). The enzyme phosphoenol pyruvate carboxylase (PEPcase) catalyses the carboxylation of PEP.<br>$\\mathrm{PEP}+\\mathrm{CO}_{2}+\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O} \\xrightarrow{\\text { PEP carboxylase }} \\mathrm{OAA}+\\mathrm{P}_{\\mathrm{i}}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2010,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49096,'biology',80,474,958,'In a condensed schematic representation of Dark reaction of photosynthesis given below, steps are indicated by alphabets. Select the option where the alphabets are correctly identified.<br>','[{"text": "A=${\\\\rm{C}}{{\\\\rm{O}}_2}$ fixation, B=Reduction,\\nC=Phosphorylation, D=Regeneration", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "A=Regeneration, B= ${\\\\rm{C}}{{\\\\rm{O}}_2}$ fixation,\\nC=Reduction, D=Phosphorylation", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "A= ${\\\\rm{C}}{{\\\\rm{O}}_2}$ fixation, B=Phosphorylation,\\nC=Reduction, D=Regeneration", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "A= ${\\\\rm{C}}{{\\\\rm{O}}_2}$ fixation, B=Phosphorylation,\\nC=Regeneration, D=Reduction", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'<img src=\'/img/data/biology/80_49096_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />',NULL,'Medium','MCQ type-3',2012,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','biology/80_49096_question_1.png'),
(49098,'biology',80,473,953,'Electrons from excited chlorophyll molecules of photosystem II are accepted first by:','[{"text": "Ferredoxin", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Pheophytin", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Cytochrome b", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Cytochorme f", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'The electrons ejected from P680 in PSII by the absorption of photons are replaced with electrons abstracted from $\\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O}$ by an Mn containing complex which is the oxygen evolving centre; (OEC), thus resulting in formation of $\\mathrm{O}_{2}$ and $4 \\mathrm{H}^{+}$. Each ejected electron passes downhill through a chain of electron carriers to pheophytin molecules.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2013,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49099,'biology',80,476,960,'Read the statements and choose the correct option.<br><div class="matching-question"><div class="column-left">Photorespiration decreases photosynthetic output.<br></div><div class="column-right">In photorespiratory pathway, neither ATP nor NADPH is produced.</div></div>','[{"text": "Both the Statements A and B are correct.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both the Statements A and B are wrong.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Statement A is correct and Statement B is wrong.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Statement B is correct, and Statement A is wrong.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Photorespiration light dependent reaction in $\\mathrm{C}-3$ plants where oxygen is consumed, and carbon dioxide is reduced. Neither ATP nor NADPH is produced.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ type-2',2014,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49100,'biology',80,475,959,'Match the organic compounds listed under Column I with the explanation given under Column II; choose the appropriate option from the given choices.<div class="matching-question"><div class="column-left">A. Phosphoenol pyruvate (PEP)<br>B. Ribulose biphosphate (RuBP)<br>C. Oxaloacetic acid (OAA)<br>D. Acetyl co-enzyme A</div><div class="column-right">P. 6-carbon compound<br>Q. 2-carbon compound<br>R. 4-carbon compound<br>S. 5-carbon compound<br>T. 3-carbon compound</div></div>','[{"text": "$\\\\mathrm{A}-\\\\mathrm{R}, \\\\mathrm{B}-\\\\mathrm{S}, \\\\mathrm{C}-\\\\mathrm{T}, \\\\mathrm{D}-\\\\mathrm{P}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{A}-\\\\mathrm{Q}, \\\\mathrm{B}-\\\\mathrm{R}, \\\\mathrm{C}-\\\\mathrm{S}, \\\\mathrm{D}-\\\\mathrm{T}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{A}-\\\\mathrm{T}, \\\\mathrm{B}-\\\\mathrm{S}, \\\\mathrm{C}-\\\\mathrm{R},  \\\\mathrm{D}-\\\\mathrm{Q}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{A}-\\\\mathrm{T}, \\\\mathrm{B}-\\\\mathrm{P}, \\\\mathrm{C}-\\\\mathrm{Q}, \\\\mathrm{D}-\\\\mathrm{R}$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'• Phosphoenol pyruvate (PEP) is a 3-carbon compound.<br>• Ribulose biphosphate (RuBP) is a 5-carbon compound.<br>•Oxaloacetic acid (OAA) is a 4-carbon compound.<br>Acetyl co-enzyme A is a 2-carbon compound.',NULL,'Medium','Match the following',2014,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49101,'biology',80,477,963,'Greenhouse crops such as tomatoes and bell pepper produce higher yields. This is due to:','[{"text": "$\\\\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ is a limiting factor to photosynthesis", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Tomatoes and bell pepper are not $\\\\mathrm{C}_{3}$ plants", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ enriched atmosphere leads to higher yields", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Due to diffused light in green house", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Greenhouse crops such as tomatoes and bell pepper are $\\mathrm{C}_{3}$ plants. They respond to higher $\\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ concentration by showing increased rates of photosynthesis leading to higher productivity.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2015,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49102,'biology',80,474,957,'A plant is provided with ideal conditions for photosynthesis and supplied with isotope ${ }^{14} \\mathrm{CO}_{2}$. When the products of the process are analysed carefully, what would be the nature of products?','[{"text": "Both glucose and oxygen are labelled.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Only oxygen is labelled but glucose is normal.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both glucose and oxygen are normal.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Only glucose is labelled, and oxygen is normal.", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Glucose will be labelled as the carbon that forms it will be derived from the carbon dioxide supplied which is the supplied isotope. Oxygen will not be labelled and is normal as the supplied oxygen is normal as well.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2016,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49103,'biology',80,474,958,'The outcome of Calvin cycle includes:','[{"text": "6, 18 ATP, 12 NADHP", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "One glucose, 18 ATP, 12 NADHP", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "6,18 ADP, 12 NADP", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "One glucose, 18 ADP, 12 NADP", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Calvin cycle is the sequence of photosynthetic biosynthetic phase in which ribulose-5-phosphate is carboxylated, converted to three-carbon carbohydrate precursors and regenerated. It is also called the reductive pentose phosphate cycle. The reaction of Calvin cycle may be summed up as:<br>$6 \\mathrm{CO}_{2}+18 \\mathrm{ATP}+12 \\mathrm{NADPH}+12 \\mathrm{H}^{+}$<br>$\\xrightarrow[\\text { dark resolution }]{\\text { Enzymes of }} 6 \\mathrm{RuBP}+\\mathrm{C}_{6} \\mathrm{H}_{12} \\mathrm{O}_{6}$<br>$+18 \\mathrm{ADP}+12 \\mathrm{Pi}+12 \\mathrm{NADP}+6 \\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2017,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49104,'biology',80,475,959,'In the maize plant, $\\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ fixation occurs in both mesophyll and bundle sheath cells. The enzymes involved in these cells for the process respectively are:','[{"text": "RuBisCo and PEP kinase", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "PEP kinase and Pepsin", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "RuBisCO and PEP carboxylase", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "PEP carboxylase and RuBisCO", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'In the leaf of $\\mathrm{C}_{4}$ plant, two distinct photosynthetic tissues are present - bundle sheath and mesophyll cells, it is called Kranz anatomy. The carbon dioxide that enters the mesophyll cells is accepted by a 3-carbon molecule called phosphoenol pyruvate (PEP). The enzyme phosphoenol pyruvate carboxylase (PEPcase) catalyses the carboxylation of PEP to 4-carbon acid oxaloacetic acid. Oxaloacetate is then converted to a similar molecule, malate, that can be transported in to the bundle-sheath cells. Inside the bundle sheath, malate breaks down, releasing a molecule of $\\mathrm{CO}_{2}$. The $\\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ is then fixed by rubisco and made into sugars via the Calvin cycle.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2018,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49105,'biology',80,473,956,'During chemiosmotic synthesis of ATP in photosynthesis:','[{"text": "The protons accumulate in the intermembrane space of chloroplast.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "The proton gradient is not required.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "The protons accumulate in the intermembrane space of mitochondrion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "The protons accumulate within the lumen of the thylakoids.", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'During photosynthesis in plants, ATP is synthesized by ATP synthase using a proton gradient created in the thylakoid lumen through the thylakoid membrane and into the chloroplast stroma.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2019,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49106,'biology',80,474,958,'The enzyme that is not found in $\\mathrm{C}_{3}$ plants is','[{"text": "ATP synthase", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "RUBP carboxylase", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "NADP reductase", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "PEP carboxylase", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'PEP carboxylase is not found in $\\mathrm{C}_{3}$ plants.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2021,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49107,'biology',80,471,950,'Chloroplast movement is influenced by','[{"text": "Light exposure", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Dark condition", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Atmospheric condition", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Number of mesophyll cells.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Chloroplast movement is influenced by light exposure. Light-induced changes in the cellular distribution or orientation of chloroplasts have been observed in nearly all green plants including algae, mosses, ferns, and angiosperms. Under low light conditions, chloroplasts accumulate along the cell walls that are perpendicular to the incident light. Under high light conditions, they accumulate along the walls that are parallel to the incident light. These are the regions of plant leaf cells where internal fluence rates of light are the highest and lowest, respectively and it is believed likely that the light- induced chloroplast movements serve an adaptive function.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49108,'biology',80,470,949,'Which is wrongly matched<div class="matching-question"></div>','[{"text": "Jan Ingenhousz - Showed that sunlight is essential for photosynthesis", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Joseph Priestley - Showed that plants release $\\\\mathrm{O}_{2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Engelmann - Green substance is located within special bodies in plant", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Sachs - Plants produce glucose/starch Site of Photosynthesis and pigments of photosynthesis", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'It was not until about 1854 that Julius Von Sachs provided evidence for the production of glucose when plants grow.In 1881, the German plant physiologist T.W. Engelmann using a prism, split light into its spectral components and then illuminated a green alga, Cladophora placed in a suspension of aerobic bacteria.',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49109,'biology',80,472,951,'The pigment molecules responsible for photosynthesis are located in the','[{"text": "Cytoplasm of the cell.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Matrix of the mitochondria.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Thylakoid membrane of the chloroplast.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "All of the above", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Pigments are chemical compounds which reflect only certain wavelengths of visible light. More important than their reflection of light is the ability of pigments to absorb certain wavelengths. Because they interact with light to absorb only certain wavelengths, pigments are useful to plants and other autotrophs organisms which make their own food using photosynthesis. Photosynthetic pigments are located in the membranes of the thylakoid membranes in specific areas called quantasomes. The quantasomes are mainly present in grana lamellae.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49110,'biology',80,471,950,'Which part of the plant do not perform photosynthesis?','[{"text": "Cactus stem", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Guard cell of stomata", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Mesophyll cells of leaf", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Leaf epidermis", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Photosynthesis occurs particularly in specialized cell called mesophyll cells. These cells contain chloroplast, which is the actual sites for photosynthesis. The cactus stem and guard cell of stomata contain chloroplasts, so they can manufacture food by photosynthesis. The epidermal cells do not contain chloroplasts, so they do not perform photosynthesis.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49111,'biology',80,474,958,'In Calvin cycle, RuBisCO incorporates $\\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ into ribulose, 1, 5-bisphosphate which rapidly splits into','[{"text": "2 molecules of 3-PGA", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "2 molecules of 2 -PGA", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "3 molecules of 3 -PGA", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "3 molecules of 2-PGA", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'In Calvin cycle, carboxylation is the most crucial step where $\\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ is utilised for the carboxylation of RuBP. In this case $\\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ reacts with ribulose 1,5 biphosphate to yield two molecules of 3-phosphoglycerate, a reaction catalyzed by the chloroplast enzyme ribulose biphosphate carboxylase-oxygenase, referred to as RuBisCO.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49112,'biology',80,473,956,'ADP is phosphorylated and NADP is reduced, this happens during.','[{"text": "Dark phase of photosynthesis", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Light phase of photosynthesis", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Photorespiration", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Calvin cycle", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'ADP is phosphorylated and NADP is reduced occurs during light phase of the photosynthesis. The \'light-dependent reactions\' is the process by which plants capture and store energy from sunlight. In this process, light energy is converted into chemical energy, in the form of the energy-carrying molecules ATP and NADPH.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49113,'biology',80,473,955,'In a crop field a weedicide is used to remove weeds in order to increase the yield. But the effect of this weedicide is that, it blocks electron transport from photosystem II to photosystem I. This will result in','[{"text": "Enhancement of dark reaction.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Failure of ATP synthesis.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Lack of reduction of NADP + .", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both (2) and (3).", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'During the electron transport from photosystem II to photosystem I, ATP is synthesized and NADP $_{\\mathrm{z}}$ is reduced to NADPH. So, the blockage of electron transport will stop ATP synthesis and the reduction of $\\mathrm{NADP}^{+}$to NADPH.',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49114,'biology',80,474,958,'Consider the following statements with respect to photosynthesis and identify the correct statements.<br>I. The first carbon dioxide acceptor in $\\mathrm{C}_{4}$ cycle is PGA.<br>II. In $\\mathrm{C}_{3}$ plants, the first stable product of photosynthesis during dark reaction is RuBP.<br>III. Cyclic photophosphorylation results in the formation of ATP.<br>IV. Oxygen which is liberated during photosynthesis comes from water.','[{"text": "I and II", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "I and III", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "III and IV", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "II and III", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'In $\\mathrm{C}_{3}$ plants the primary $\\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ acceptor is $\\mathrm{uBP}$ and the initial stable product is PGA. While in $\\mathrm{C}_{4}$ plants the primary $\\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ acceptor is PEP and the first stable product is OAA.',NULL,'Hard','MCQ type-2',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49115,'biology',80,473,955,'In non-cyclic reactions of photosynthesis, electrons from chlorophyll molecules in photosystem-I are used in the formation of NADPH. What is the source of such electrons?','[{"text": "Light", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "NADPH", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Photosystem-I", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Photosystem-II, which is related to splitting of water molecule", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'In non-cyclic reaction of photosynthesis, electrons from chlorophyll molecules in photosystem I are used in the formation of NADPH. The source of those electrons is photosystem II which splits water molecule.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49116,'biology',80,474,958,'To make 100 molecules of glucose, how many molecules of ATP & NADPH are required?','[{"text": "1800 and 1200 respectively.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "1200 and 1800 respectively.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "1800 and 600 respectively.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "200 and 600 respectively.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'18 ATP and 12 NADPH molecules will be required to make one molecule of glucose through the Calvin Pathways. Therefore to make 100 molecules of glucose 1800 ATP and 1200 NADPH molecules are required.',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49117,'biology',80,475,959,'Conversion of Phosphoenol pyruvic acid to pyruvic acid and ADP to ATP are examples of','[{"text": "Photophosphorylation.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Oxidative phosphorylation.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Photoelectric phosphorylation.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Substrate level phosphorylation.", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'During the conversion of phophoenol pyruvate (PEP) to pyruvic acid, the phosphate group from PEP is transferred to ADP. Therefore it is substrate level phosphorylation.',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49118,'biology',80,475,959,'Fixation of $\\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ molecule through Hatch and Slack pathway requires an enzyme called ____','[{"text": "PEPcase", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "RuBisCO", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "RuBP carboxylase", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Catalase", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'In $C_{4}$ plants, a 4 - $C$ compound oxaloacetic acid (OAA) is the first stable product, and phosphoenol pyruvate (PEP) is the $\\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ acceptor. This reaction is catalyzed by the enzyme PEP carboxylase or PEP case in mesophyll cells of the leaf.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49119,'biology',80,475,959,'In $\\mathrm{C}_{4}$ plants, the process by which $\\mathrm{C}_{4}$ acid is converted into $\\mathrm{C}_{3}$ acid in the bundle sheath cell is known as','[{"text": "Carboxylation", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Regeneration", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Reduction", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Decarboxylation", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'In $\\mathrm{C}_{4}$ plants, the process by which $\\mathrm{C}_{4}$ acids are converted into $\\mathrm{C} 3$ acids in the bundle sheath cell is known as decarboxylation. Shortly after the discovery of $\\mathrm{C}_{4}$ cycle the crassulacean acid metabolism (CAM) pathway was dissected and elucidated as a $\\mathrm{C}_{4}$ variant. This pathway is found in desert succulents and epiphytes. In the typical $\\mathrm{C}_{4}$ cycle, the fixation reaction occurred in a mesophyll cell and the decarboxylation reaction occurred in a bundle sheath cell, Decarboxylation is a chemical reaction that removes a carboxyl group and releases carbon dioxide $\\left(\\mathrm{CO}_{2}\\right)$. Enzymes that catalyze decarboxylation are called decarboxylases.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49120,'biology',80,475,959,'Match the column-I with column II and choose the correct answer using the codes given below.<br><div class="matching-question"><div class="column-left">A. Emerson effect.<br>B. Hill reaction<br>C. Calvin cycle<br>D. Hatch & Slack cycle<br></div><div class="column-right">P. $\\mathrm{C}_{4}$ cycle<br>Q. Photolysis<br>R. $\\mathrm{C}_{3}$ cycle.<br>S. Photosystem I & II</div></div>','[{"text": "${\\\\rm{A  -  P; B  -  Q; C  -  R; D  -  S}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{A  - P ; B  -  R; C  -  S; D  -  P}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{A  -  R; B  -  S; C  -  P; D  -  Q}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{A  -  S; B  -  Q; C  -  R; D  -  P}}$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'$\\mathrm{A}$ : Emerson effect is the increase in the rate of photosynthesis after chloroplasts are exposed to light of wavelength $670 \\mathrm{~nm}$ (far red light) and $700 \\mathrm{~nm}$ (red light). It involves both photosystem I and II.B : Hills reaction (or photolysis of water) is the light- dependent transfer of electrons by chloroplasts in photosynthesis that results in the cleavage of water molecules and liberation of oxygen. Hill reaction was proposed by Robert Hill.<br>$\\mathrm{C}$ : Calvin cycle (also called $\\mathrm{C}_{3}$ pathway) is the set of chemical reactions that take place in stroma of the chloroplasts during photosynthesis. The cycle is light-independent because it takes place after the energy has been captured from sunlight.<br>D : Hatch & Slack cycle (also known as $\\mathrm{C}_{4}$ pathway) is a metabolic cycle involved in the non-light- requiring phase of photosynthesis in certain plants having specific metabolic and anatomical modifications in their mesophyll and bundle sheath cells which facilitate the temporary fixation of carbon dioxide $\\left(\\mathrm{CO}_{2}\\right)$ into four-carbon organic acid. These acids are next broken down to three-carbon organic acids along with $\\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ in bundle sheath cells, where this unbound $\\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ is then fixed into carbohydrates in a normal Calvin cycle pathway.',NULL,'Hard','Match the following',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49121,'biology',80,472,951,'Chlorophyll is suited for the capture of light energy because','[{"text": "Certain wavelengths of light raise it to an excited state.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "In its excited state chlorophyll gives off electrons.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Chlorophyll\'s structure allows it to attach to thylakoid membranes.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "All of the above.", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'The "tails" of chlorophyll molecules are associated with the thylakoid membranes of the chloroplasts. When excited by light, the chlorophyll moves into an excited state and passes electrons to acceptor molecules. This begins to set up the proton gradient across the membrane that will drive ATP synthesis.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49122,'biology',80,477,961,'According to Blackman\'s law of limiting factor, at any given time, photosynthesis can be limited by','[{"text": "Light only", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ concentration only", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both light and $\\\\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ concentration", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Either by light or by $\\\\mathrm{CO}_{2}$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'According to Blackmans law of limiting factor, at any given time photosynthesis can be limited either by light or $\\mathrm{CO}_{2}$. Blackman proposed the law of limiting factors in 1905 to determine the rate of photosynthesis. According to this law, when a process depends on a number of factors, its rate is limited by the pace of the slowest factor.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49123,'biology',80,470,949,'Match the scientists given in column-1 with their work, given in column-II & select the correct answer using the codes given below.<br><div class="matching-question"><div class="column-left">A. Priestley<br>B. Jan Ingenhousz<br>C. Sachs<br>D. Engelmann<br>E. Niel</div><div class="column-right">P. Determined the action spectrum of chlorophyll<br>Q. Providence that in green parts of plant glucose is made & stored as starch<br>R. Plants purify air only in the presence of light<br>S. Demonstrated that photosynthesis is essentially a light dependent reaction essential role of air in the growth of plants<br>T. Revealed the essential role of air in the growth of plants</div></div>','[{"text": "${\\\\rm{A  -  T, B  -  R, C  -  Q, D  -  P, E  -  S}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{A  -  Q, B  -  R, C  -  P, D  -  S, E  -  T}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{A  - T, B  -  R, C  -  P, D  -  S, E  -  Q}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{A  -  R, B  -  T, C  -  S, D  -  P, E  -  Q}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'<br>$\\text { A - V, B - III, C - II, D - I, E - IV }$',NULL,'Hard','Match the following',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49124,'biology',80,470,949,'A student sets up an experiment on photosynthesis as follows:<br>He takes soda water in a glass tumbler and add chlorophyll extracts into the contents and keeps the tumbler exposed to sunlight hoping that he has provided necessary ingredients for photosynthesis to proceed (viz., $\\mathrm{CO}_{2}, \\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{O}$, chlorophyll and light). What do you think what will happen after, say, a few hours of exposure of light?','[{"text": "Photosynthesis will take place and glucose will be produced.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Photosynthesis will take place and starch will be produced which will turn the mixture turbid.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Photosynthesis will not take place because $\\\\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ dissolved in soda water escapes into the atmosphere.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Photosynthesis will not take place because intact chloroplasts are needed for the process.", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'According to the experiment performed by the student, photosynthesis will not take place because intact chloroplast is needed for the process of photosynthesis. Photosynthesis is the process by which green plants create energy from sunlight. It occurs at the cellular level in the leaves of plants and is the way in which they produce oxygen and carbohydrates. The oxygen is released into the atmosphere, and the carbohydrates, simple sugars, are used by the plant for growth. In order to carry out photosynthesis, green plants need several ingredients. Chlorophyll, the pigment in plants that makes them green, is essential to the photosynthetic process. This chemical is produced naturally by all green plants and its role in photosynthesis is to absorb light.',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49125,'biology',80,471,950,'Which one of the following correctly identifies $\\mathrm{X}$ and $\\mathrm{Y}$ and their functions in the given figure of chloroplast? &emsp;&emsp;&emsp; $\\begin{array}{l} \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{\\rm{X}}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{\\rm{Y}}\\\\ {\\rm{Structure}}\\,{\\rm{Function}}\\,{\\rm{Structure}}\\,{\\rm{Function}}\\, \\end{array}$','[{"text": "$\\\\begin{array}{*{20}{c}}\\n{\\\\begin{array}{*{20}{l}}\\n{{\\\\rm{Grana  - }}}\\n\\\\end{array}\\\\,\\\\,}&\\\\begin{array}{l}\\n\\\\begin{array}{*{20}{l}}\\n{{\\\\rm{Photolysis}}\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\rm{ Stroma}}\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,}\\n\\\\end{array}\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\begin{array}{*{20}{l}}\\n{{\\\\rm{of water}}}\\n\\\\end{array}\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\n\\\\end{array}&\\\\begin{array}{l}\\n\\\\begin{array}{*{20}{l}}\\n{{\\\\rm{C}}{{\\\\rm{O}}_{\\\\rm{2}}}}\\n\\\\end{array}\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\begin{array}{*{20}{l}}\\n{{\\\\rm{fixation}}}\\n\\\\end{array}\\n\\\\end{array}\\n\\\\end{array}$<br><br>", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\begin{array}{*{20}{c}}\\n{\\\\begin{array}{*{20}{l}}\\n{\\\\begin{array}{*{20}{l}}\\n{{\\\\rm{Grana  - }}}\\n\\\\end{array}}\\n\\\\end{array}}&{\\\\begin{array}{*{20}{l}}\\n{\\\\begin{array}{*{20}{c}}\\n{\\\\begin{array}{*{20}{l}}\\n{\\\\begin{array}{*{20}{l}}\\n{{\\\\rm{C}}{{\\\\rm{O}}_{\\\\rm{2}}}}\\n\\\\end{array}}\\n\\\\end{array}}\\\\\\\\\\n{\\\\begin{array}{*{20}{l}}\\n{\\\\,\\\\begin{array}{*{20}{l}}\\n{{\\\\rm{fixation}}}\\n\\\\end{array}}\\n\\\\end{array}}\\n\\\\end{array}\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,}\\n\\\\end{array}}&{\\\\begin{array}{*{20}{l}}\\n{\\\\begin{array}{*{20}{l}}\\n{{\\\\rm{Stroma}}\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,}\\n\\\\end{array}}\\n\\\\end{array}}\\n\\\\end{array}\\\\begin{array}{*{20}{l}}\\n\\\\begin{array}{l}\\n{\\\\rm{Photolysis}}\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\begin{array}{*{20}{l}}\\n{{\\\\rm{of water}}}\\n\\\\end{array}\\n\\\\end{array}\\n\\\\end{array}$<br><br>", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\begin{array}{*{20}{c}}\\n{\\\\begin{array}{*{20}{l}}\\n{\\\\begin{array}{*{20}{l}}\\n{{\\\\rm{Stroma - }}}\\n\\\\end{array}}\\n\\\\end{array}}&{\\\\begin{array}{*{20}{l}}\\n{\\\\begin{array}{*{20}{c}}\\n{\\\\begin{array}{*{20}{l}}\\n{\\\\begin{array}{*{20}{l}}\\n{{\\\\rm{Photolysis}}}\\n\\\\end{array}}\\n\\\\end{array}}\\\\\\\\\\n{\\\\begin{array}{*{20}{l}}\\n{\\\\,\\\\begin{array}{*{20}{l}}\\n{{\\\\rm{of water}}}\\n\\\\end{array}}\\n\\\\end{array}}\\n\\\\end{array}\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,}\\n\\\\end{array}}&{\\\\begin{array}{*{20}{l}}\\n{\\\\begin{array}{*{20}{l}}\\n{{\\\\rm{Grana}}\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,}\\n\\\\end{array}}\\n\\\\end{array}}\\n\\\\end{array}\\\\begin{array}{*{20}{l}}\\n\\\\begin{array}{l}\\n{\\\\rm{C}}{{\\\\rm{O}}_{\\\\rm{2}}}\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\begin{array}{*{20}{l}}\\n{{\\\\rm{fixation}}}\\n\\\\end{array}\\n\\\\end{array}\\n\\\\end{array}$<br><br>", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\begin{array}{*{20}{c}}\\n{\\\\begin{array}{*{20}{l}}\\n{\\\\begin{array}{*{20}{l}}\\n{{\\\\rm{Stroma  - }}}\\n\\\\end{array}}\\n\\\\end{array}}&{\\\\begin{array}{*{20}{l}}\\n{\\\\begin{array}{*{20}{c}}\\n{\\\\begin{array}{*{20}{l}}\\n{\\\\begin{array}{*{20}{l}}\\n{{\\\\rm{C}}{{\\\\rm{O}}_{\\\\rm{2}}}}\\n\\\\end{array}}\\n\\\\end{array}}\\\\\\\\\\n{\\\\begin{array}{*{20}{l}}\\n{\\\\,\\\\begin{array}{*{20}{l}}\\n{{\\\\rm{fixation}}}\\n\\\\end{array}}\\n\\\\end{array}}\\n\\\\end{array}\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,}\\n\\\\end{array}}&{\\\\begin{array}{*{20}{l}}\\n{\\\\begin{array}{*{20}{l}}\\n{{\\\\rm{Lamella}}\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,}\\n\\\\end{array}}\\n\\\\end{array}}\\n\\\\end{array}\\\\begin{array}{*{20}{l}}\\n\\\\begin{array}{l}\\n{\\\\rm{Photolysis}}\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\begin{array}{*{20}{l}}\\n{{\\\\rm{of water}}}\\n\\\\end{array}\\n\\\\end{array}\\n\\\\end{array}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'In the given figure of chloroplast $X$ and $Y$ are respectively grana and stroma. Grana are a membranous structure within a chloroplast made up of stacks of thylakoids. Stroma is the matrix of the chloroplast which is a thick fluid in between grana where various enzymes, molecules and ions are found, and where the dark reaction (or carbohydrate formation reactions) of photosynthesis occurs. Grana and stroma are involved in photolysis of water and $\\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ fixation respectively.',NULL,'Hard','MCQ type-3',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','biology/80_49125_question_1.png'),
(49126,'biology',80,469,948,'You are asked to examine a cell using a powerful light microscope. The image you see has a clearly defined nucleus and mitochondria. It also has a large central vacuole and chloroplasts. From what group of organisms did this cell most likely come?','[{"text": "Bacteria", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Protists", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Fungi", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Plants", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'We can eliminate bacteria because they lack organelles completely. Of the organelles listed the only unique one is the chloroplast, which is found exclusively in plants.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49127,'biology',80,469,948,'Photosynthesis is the transformation of','[{"text": "Light energy to chemical energy.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Chemical energy to light energy.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Light energy to kinetic energy.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Solar energy to potential energy.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Green plants capture solar energy (light) from sun and convert it into chemical energy (food) with the help of $\\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ taken from atmosphere, water from soil and releases oxygen.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49128,'biology',80,472,951,'Which of the following statement is incorrect regarding pigments?','[{"text": "Pigments are substances that have an ability to absorb light, at specific wavelengths.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Chlorophyll b is the chief pigment associated with photosynthesis.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Leaf pigments can be separated by chromatography.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Accessory pigments protect chlorophyll a from photo-oxidation.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Chlorophyll $a$ is the chief pigment associated with photosynthesis.',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49129,'biology',80,473,956,'Assume the thylakoid membrane within a chloroplast is punctured so that there is no separation between lumen & stroma of thylakoid. Which of these processes would be most affected?','[{"text": "The splitting of water", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "The synthesis of ATP", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Reduction of NADP ${ }^{+}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "The flow of electrons from PS - II to PS - I", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'According to chemiosmotic hypothesis (Mitchell 1961) the ATP is synthesized on ATPase complexes located on the nonappressed portions of thylakoid membranes particularly towards margins. Therefore, if the thylakoid membrane within a chloroplast is punctured so that there is no separation between lumen and stroma of thylakoid, synthesis of ATP will be affected the most.',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49130,'biology',80,472,951,'Accessory pigments','[{"text": "Play no role in photosynthesis.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Release e- and get oxidized.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Transfer of e- to NADP.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Allow plants to harvest visible light of wider range wavelengths.", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Accessory pigments are light-absorbing compounds that traps light energy and channels it to chlorophyll a which initiates the reactions of photosynthesis. Accessory pigment includes the carotenoids, phycobiliproteins, and chlorophylls b, c, and d. Indeed, they not on enable a wider range of wavelength of incoming light to be utilized for photosynthesis but also protect chlorophyll a from the photooxidation.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49131,'biology',80,469,948,'Which of the following does not participate in the process of photosynthesis?','[{"text": "Red algae", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Green algae", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Brown algae", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Slime moulds", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'The algae have the chlorophyll pigment and make their food by photosynthetic process in which oxygen is released as a byproduct.<br>Although all algae essentially contain chlorophyll a pigment but, in some forms, the green colour of chlorophyll pigment is masked by other pigments. For example, in phaeophycean (brown algae), a brown pigment - fucoxanthin and in Rhodophyceae (red algae) a red pigment-r-phycoerythrin masks the green colour of the chlorophyll.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49132,'biology',80,474,958,'The total requirement of ATP & NADPH for each molecule of $\\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ fixed & reduced in photosynthesis in the Calvin cycle is','[{"text": "2 ATP & 2 NADPH", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "2 ATP & 3 NADPH", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "3 ATP & 2 NADPH", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "4 ATP & 3 NADPH", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'For every $\\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ molecule entering the Calvin cycle, 3 molecules of ATP and 2 molecules of NADPH are required.',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49133,'biology',80,473,955,'Which one of the following event occurs both during cyclic and non-cyclic modes of photophosphorylation?','[{"text": "Involvement of both PS - I and PS - II.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Formation of ATP.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Release of $\\\\mathrm{O}_{2}$.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Formation of NADPH.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Formation of ATP occurs during cyclic and non - cyclic photophosphorylation. The net product of cyclic photophosphorylation is ATP, NADPH and $\\mathrm{O}_{2}$ and in non - cyclic photophosphorylation it is ATP only.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49134,'biology',80,473,955,'Cooperation of the two photosystems of the chloroplast is required for','[{"text": "ATP synthesis.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Reduction of NADP ${ }^{+}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Enhancement of dark reaction.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Generation of proton motive force", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'The cooperation of two photosystems is required for the upward electron transport from water to $\\mathrm{NADP}^{+}$of the two photosystems. Photosystem I is more directly associated with the reduction of $\\mathrm{NADP}^{+}$, whereas the photosystem II is more closely associated with the extraction of electrons from water.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49135,'biology',80,474,958,'Which of the following pair is mismatched?<div class="matching-question"></div>','[{"text": "Photosystem I - Uses the $\\\\mathrm{P}_{700}$ molecule in its photocenter.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Antenna complex - Contains hundreds of pigment molecule.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "PGA-3-carbon compound.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Dark reaction - Takes place in the grana of the chloroplast.", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Dark reaction is a thermochemical reaction. It takes place in the stroma of the chloroplast. Dark reaction, also called $\\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ fixation or carbon assimilation, occurs even in the presence of light but it doesn\'t require light. Dark reaction consists of 3 phases carboxylation, reduction and regeneration of $\\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ acceptor.',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49136,'biology',80,474,958,'Which of following ratio is correct for the production of one molecule of glucose through 6 rounds of Calvin cycle?<br>&emsp;$\\begin{array}{*{20}{c}}\n{{\\rm{C}}{{\\rm{O}}_{\\rm{2}}}}&{{\\rm{ATP}}}&{{\\rm{NADPH}}}\n\\end{array}$','[{"text": "$\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\begin{array}{*{20}{c}}\\n1&{\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,2}&{\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,2}\\n\\\\end{array}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\begin{array}{*{20}{c}}\\n{\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,6}&{\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,18}&{\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,12}\\n\\\\end{array}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\begin{array}{*{20}{c}}\\n{\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,6}&{\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,12}&{\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,18}\\n\\\\end{array}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\begin{array}{*{20}{c}}\\n{\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,5}&{\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,6}&{\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,9}\\n\\\\end{array}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'$6 \\mathrm{CO}_{2}, 18 \\mathrm{ATP}$ and $12 \\mathrm{NADPH} 2$ are required for the production of one molecule of glucose, 18 ADP and 12 NADP through 6 rounds of Calvin cycle.',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49137,'biology',80,474,958,'How many molecules of ${\\rm{RuBP}}\\,{\\rm{\\& }}\\,{\\rm{C}}{{\\rm{O}}_{\\rm{2}}}$ respectively are required for production of 6 molecules of 3-PGA?','[{"text": "3 and 2", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "2 and 3", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "3 and 3", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "3 and 1", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'One molecule of RuBP and one molecule of $\\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ are required to produce two molecules of 3-PGA. Therefore, for the production of 6 molecules of 3- PGA, 3 molecules of RuBP and 3 molecules of $\\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ are required.',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49138,'biology',80,475,959,'By looking at which internal structure of a plant can you tell whether a plant is $\\mathrm{C}_{3}$ or $\\mathrm{C}_{4}$ ?','[{"text": "Kranz anatomy", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Distribution of mesophyll cells", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Bundle sheath cells", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "All the above", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'$\\mathrm{C}_{4}$ plants possess Kranz type of leaf anatomy. The mesophyll is undifferentiated and its cells occur in concentric layers around vascular bundles. $\\mathrm{C}_{4}$ plants possess dimorphic chloroplasts. While in $\\mathrm{C}_{3}$ plants leaf anatomy is not of Kranz type. Only one type of chloroplasts are found.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49139,'biology',80,475,959,'$\\mathrm{C}_{4}$ plants are more expensive in terms of ATP required for $\\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ fixation, the additional number of ATP required by $\\mathrm{C}_{4}$ plants in comparison to $\\mathrm{C}_{3}$ plants is','[{"text": "$6 \\\\mathrm{ATP}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$12 \\\\mathrm{ATP}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$18 \\\\mathrm{ATP}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$24 \\\\mathrm{ATP}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'A total of 30 ATP are used, so the difference is 12 ATP.',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49140,'biology',80,474,958,'Chloroplasts are disrupted and the stroma separated from the lamellae. The isolated stroma will fix $\\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ if it is supplied with','[{"text": "$\\\\mathrm{O}_{2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "RuBisCO", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Light", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "ATP + NADPH", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Chloroplast is disrupted and the stroma separated from the lamella, the isolated stroma will fix $\\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ if it is supplied with ATP+NADPH.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49141,'biology',80,473,954,'Breakdown of water during the photosynthesis molecule leads to release of','[{"text": "Electron and proton only", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Electron and oxygen only", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Proton and oxygen only", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Electron, proton and oxygen", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Breakdown of water during the photosynthesis molecules leads to release of electron, proton and oxygen.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49142,'biology',80,477,964,'During monsoon, the rice crop of eastern states of India shows lesser yield due to limiting factor of','[{"text": "$\\\\mathrm{CO}_{2}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Light", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Temperature", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Water", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'According to law of limiting factors given by Blackman, when a process depends upon a number of factors which are separate, the rate of the process is limited by the pace of the slowest factor. Rate of yield is dependent on light as photosynthesis is dependent on light. During monsoon, the light is dim and so this reduces rate of photosynthesis and hence yield.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49143,'biology',80,474,958,'To form six molecule of glyceraldehyde 3 phosphate in Calvin cycle','[{"text": "$9 \\\\mathrm{ATP}$ and $6 \\\\mathrm{NADPH}$ are required.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$6 \\\\mathrm{ATP}$ and $6 \\\\mathrm{NADPH}$ are required.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$3 \\\\mathrm{ATP}$ and $3 \\\\mathrm{NADPH}$ are required.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$9 \\\\mathrm{ATP}$ and $36 \\\\mathrm{NADPH}$ are required.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Glyceraldehyde 3-phosphate is a three carbon molecule.<br>For a three carbon molecule to be synthesised, three turns of the calvin cycle are required, with the entry of a molecule of carbon dioxide at each turn. Each turn of the calvin cycle consumes three ATP and two NADPH molecules. Therefore 9 ATP and $6 \\mathrm{NADPH}$ molecules are required for three turns of calvin cycle that will result in the formation of glyceraldehyde 3-phosphate.',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49144,'biology',80,471,950,'The diagram below represents an experiment with isolated chloroplasts. The chloroplasts were first made acidic by soaking them in a solution at $\\mathrm{pH} 4$. After the thylakoid space reached $\\mathrm{pH} 4$, the chloroplasts were transferred to a basic solution at $\\mathrm{pH} 8$. The chloroplasts are then placed in the dark. Which of these compounds would you expect to be produced?','[{"text": "ATP", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "NAD", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "3 PGA", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{C}_{6} \\\\mathrm{H}_{12} \\\\mathrm{O}_{6}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'ATP synthase works via a concentration gradient; high $(\\mathrm{H}+$ )in the lumen, and low $(\\mathrm{H}+)$ in the stroma. When the chloroplast is bathed in $\\mathrm{pH} 4$, the entire thing will have a higher $[\\mathrm{H}+]$; once it is put in the basic solution, you\'ll still have the high $[\\mathrm{H}+]$ in the lumen but no longer in the stroma. This is exactly what normally occurs via the light reactions (cytochrome b6f pumps protons into the lumen when light activates the photosystems). So if you have a proton gradient, you don\'t need light to drive ATP synthesis.',NULL,'Hard','MCQ type-3',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','biology/80_49144_question_1.png'),
(49145,'biology',80,475,959,'The given figure shows the diagrammatic representation of the Hatch & Slack pathway few labelling are marked as P, Q and R.<br>Which of the following option shows the correct labeling of $\\mathrm{P}, \\mathrm{Q}$, and $\\mathrm{R}$ &emsp; &emsp; $\\begin{array}{*{20}{c}} {{\\rm{P}}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,}&{{\\rm{Q}}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,}&{\\rm{R}} \\end{array}$<br>','[{"text": "$\\\\begin{array}{*{20}{c}}\\n{{{\\\\rm{C}}_{\\\\rm{3}}}\\\\,{\\\\rm{acid}}\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,}&{{\\\\rm{Reduction}}\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,}&{{{\\\\rm{C}}_{\\\\rm{4}}}\\\\,{\\\\rm{acid}}}\\n\\\\end{array}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\begin{array}{*{20}{c}}\\n{{\\\\rm{Fixation}}\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,}&{{{\\\\rm{C}}_{\\\\rm{3}}}\\\\,{\\\\rm{acid}}\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,}&{{\\\\rm{Regeneration}}}\\n\\\\end{array}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\begin{array}{*{20}{c}}\\n{{{\\\\rm{C}}_{\\\\rm{4}}}\\\\,{\\\\rm{acid}}\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,}&{{\\\\rm{Decarboxylation}}}&{{{\\\\rm{C}}_{\\\\rm{3}}}\\\\,{\\\\rm{acid}}}\\n\\\\end{array}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\begin{array}{*{20}{c}}\\n\\\\begin{array}{l}\\n{\\\\rm{Carboxy - }}\\\\,\\\\,\\\\\\\\\\n{\\\\rm{lation}}\\\\,\\\\,\\n\\\\end{array}&{{{\\\\rm{C}}_{\\\\rm{3}}}\\\\,{\\\\rm{acid}}\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,}&{{\\\\rm{Reduction}}}\\n\\\\end{array}$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'In the given diagrammatic representation of Hatch and Slack pathway (also known as $\\mathrm{C}_{4}$ pathway), the steps marked as $\\mathrm{P}, \\mathrm{Q}$ and $\\mathrm{R}$ are respectively $\\mathrm{C}_{4}$ acids, decarboxylation and $\\mathrm{C}_{3}$ acids. It is a metabolic cycle involved in the non-light-requiring phase of photosynthesis in certain plants having specific metabolic and anatomical modifications in their mesophyll and bundle sheath cells which facilitate the temporary fixation of carbon dioxide $\\left(\\mathrm{CO}_{2}\\right)$ into four-carbon organic acid. These acids are then broken down to three-carbon organic acids along with $\\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ in the bundle sheath cells, where this freed $\\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ is then fixed into carbohydrates in a normal Calvin cycle pathway.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ type-3',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','biology/80_49145_question_1.png'),
(49146,'biology',80,474,958,'The given diagram represents the Calvin cycle. At which stage (indicated by P, Q, R and S) is ${\\rm{C}}{{\\rm{O}}_2}$ incorporated?','[{"text": "$\\\\mathrm{P}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{Q}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{R}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{S}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'In the given diagram of Calvin cycle, $\\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ is incorporated at stage $\\mathrm{P}$.',NULL,'Hard','MCQ type-3',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','biology/80_49146_question_1.png'),
(49147,'biology',80,476,960,'<div class="matching-question"><div class="column-left">$\\mathrm{C}_{4}$ photosynthetic pathway is more efficient than the $\\mathrm{C}_{3}$ pathway.<br></div><div class="column-right">Photorespiration is suppressed in $\\mathrm{C}_{4}$ plants.</div></div>','[{"text": "Both statements are true and explaining each other.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both statements are true but not explaining each other.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Only statement A is correct but statement B is not explaining the statement A .", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both statements are wrong.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'$\\mathrm{C}_{4}$ pathway/Hatch and Slack pathway ensures the Calvin cycle to be operated only in bundle sheath cell. It is an adaptation to photorespiratory loss. Therefore, $\\mathrm{C}_{4}$ plants are adapted to photorespiratory loss.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ type-2',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49148,'biology',80,472,951,'Match the pigments given in Column I(Pigments) with their colour in chromatogram given in Column II(Colour in chromatogram).<br>Choose the correct option from the following :<div class="matching-question"><div class="column-left">A. Chlorophyll ‘b’<br>B. Carotenoids<br>C. Chlorophyll ‘a’<br>D. Xanthophylls</div><div class="column-right">P. Yellow orange<br>Q. Orange red<br>R. Yellow<br>S. Blue green<br>T. Yellow green</div></div>','[{"text": "${\\\\rm{A - S,B - T,C - R,D - Q}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{A - P,B - Q,C - R,D - T}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{A - T,B - P,C - S,D - R}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{A - T,B - P,C - R,D - S}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'A chromatographic separation of leaf pigments shows that the colour we see in leaves is not due to a single pigment but due to four pigments. Chlorophyll-a (bright or blue green in the chromatogram), chlorophyll-b (yellow green), Xanthophylls (yellow) and carotenoids (yellow to yellow orange).<br>A - T, B - P, C - S, D - R is the correct option.',NULL,'Easy','Match the following',2024,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49149,'biology',80,474,958,'Which of the following are required for the dark reaction of photosynthesis?<br>A. Light<br>B. Chlorophyll<br>C. $\\mathrm{CO}_{2}$<br>D. ATP<br>E. NADPH<br>Choose the correct answer from the options given below:','[{"text": "A, B and C only", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "B, C and D only", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "C, D and E only", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "D and E only", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'For dark reaction of photosynthesis, $\\mathrm{CO}_{2}$, ATP, NADPH are requird.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2024,'NEET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49150,'biology',80,474,958,'How many molecules of ATP and NADPH are required for every molecule of $\\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ fixed in the Calvin cycle?','[{"text": "2 molecules of ATP and 3 molecules of NADPH", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "2 molecules of ATP and 2 molecules of NADPH", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "3 molecules of ATP and 3 molecules of NADPH", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "3 molecules of ATP and 2 molecules of NADPH", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'For fixation of 1 molecule of $\\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ in Calvin cycle 3 ATP molecules and 2 NADPH molecules are required.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2024,'NEET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49151,'biology',80,476,960,'Given below are two statements:<br>Read the Statement - A and Statement - B carefully to mark the correct options given below:<div class="matching-question"><div class="column-left">In $\\mathrm{C}_{3}$ plants, some $\\mathrm{O}_{2}$ binds to RuBisCO, hence $\\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ fixation is decreased.</div><div class="column-right">In $\\mathrm{C}_{4}$ plants, mesophyll cells show very little photorespiration while bundle sheath cells do not show photorespiration.</div></div>','[{"text": "Both Statement A and Statement B are\\ntrue", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Statement A and Statement B are\\nfalse", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Statement A is true but Statement B is\\nfalse", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Statement A is false but Statement B is true", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'In $\\mathrm{C}_{3}$ plant, some oxygen bind to RuBisCO , and hence $\\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ fixation is decreased.<br>Statement B is incorrect, photorespiration does not occur in $\\mathrm{C}_{4}$ plants as they lack RuBisCO in mesophyll. Hence statement A is the only correct option.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ type-2',2024,'NEET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49152,'biology',87,1128,2700,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Cardiac output is the volume of blood pumped by left or right ventricle in one minute.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> It is calculated by multiplying the heart rate by the stroke volume.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are incorrect", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Cardiac output describes the volume of blood being pumped by the heart, mainly by the left or right ventricle, per unit time. Cardiac output (CO) is the product of the heart rate (HR), which is the numberof heart beats per minute, and the stroke volume (SV), which is the volume of blood pumped from the ventricle per beat; thus, $$\\mathrm{CO}=\\mathrm{HR} \\times \\mathrm{SV}$$. Thus, both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49153,'biology',87,1128,2700,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Dupp is produced during ventricular diastole.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> It is given out during closure of pulmonary and aortic valves.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are incorrect", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'The first sound (lubb) is produced by the contraction of the ventricles and the closure of the Atrio-Ventricular systole. The second sound (dupp) is produced by the closure of the aortic and pulmonary valves during relaxation or, diastole. Thus, both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49154,'biology',87,1126,2698,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> In amphibians, the heart is two chambered.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> The deoxygenated and oxygenated blood is returned to the heart through the sinus venosus.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are incorrect", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Amphibians have a three-chambered heart. The three-chambered heart consists of two atria and one ventricle. Both atria empty into the single ventricle, mixing the oxygenrich blood returning from the lungs with the oxygen-depleted blood from the body tissues. Thus, both Assertion and Reason are false.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49155,'biology',87,1124,2696,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Blood platelets play a very important role in coagulation of blood.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> When blood is shed, the platelets disintegrate and liberate thromboplastin which activates prothrombin into thrombin.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are incorrect", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'When there is bleeding, platelets disintegrate and liberate thromboplastin and 5-hydroxytryptamine. Thromboplastin activates prothrombin to form thrombin whereas 5-hydroxytryptamine has a vasoconstrictor effect and helps in hemostasis. When blood is shed, it loses its fluidity in few minutes and sets into semisolid jelly; this process is called coagulation. Thus, both assertion and reason are true and reason is the correct explanation of assertion.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49156,'biology',87,1129,2701,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> ECG is a record of electrical activity of the heart that shows certain waves called PQRS and T waves.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> It gives important information concerning the spread of excitation to the different parts of the heart and helps in diagnosis of heart disorders.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are incorrect", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Electrocardiogram (ECG) is the process of recording the electrical activity of the heart over a period of time using electrodes placed on the skin. These electrodes detect the tiny electrical changes on the skin that arise from the heart muscle\'s electrophysiological pattern of depolarising and repolarising during each heartbeat. It is a very commonly performed cardiology test to rule out any heart disorder. Thus, both assertion and reason are true and the reason is the correct explanation of the assertion.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49182,'biology',87,1123,2695,'A certain road accident patient with unknown blood group needs immediate blood transfusion. His one doctor friend at once offers his blood. What was the blood group of the donor?','[{"text": "Blood group B", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Blood group AB", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Blood group $\\\\mathrm{O}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Blood group A", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Blood group \' $\\mathrm{O}$ \' individuals do not have either \' $A$ \' or \' $B$ \' antigens on the surface of their $\\mathrm{RBCs}$ hence, they are considered as universal donors.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2012,'AIPMT','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49157,'biology',87,1123,2695,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> In the first ten days of a newborn a large number of red blood corpuscles are destroyed.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> This is one of the causes of jaundice in newborns.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are incorrect", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Jaundice occurs when there is a build-up of a naturally occurring substance in the blood called bilirubin. It is produced by the normal breakdown of red blood cells. This is the most common cause of newborn jaundice and occurs in more than $$50 \\%$$ of babies. Because the baby has an immature liver, bilirubin is processed slower. The jaundice first appears at 2 to 3 days of age. It usually disappears by 1 to 2 weeks of age, and the levels of bilirubin are harmless. Thus, both Assertion and Reason are true and Reason is the correct explanation of Assertion.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49158,'biology',87,1129,2701,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> The record of electric potential that is initiated in the cardiac muscle is known as electrocardiograph.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> This is valuable for diagnosis of arrhythmia and also for preliminary studies of the functional abnormalities of heart.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are incorrect", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'An electrical impulse that is initiated in the cardiac muscle is recorded as electrocardiogram and the device used to record is called electrocardiograph. Thus, both assertion and Reason are true & Reason is correct explanation of Assertion.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49159,'biology',87,1126,2698,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Open circulatory system is found in most arthropods.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Arthropods contain haemolymph which directly bathes internal tissues and organs.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are incorrect", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Hemolymph, or haemolymph, is a fluid, analogous to the blood in vertebrates, which circulates in the interior of the arthropod body remaining in direct contact with the animal\'s tissues. It is composed of a fluid plasma in which hemolymph cells called hemocytes are suspended. In addition to hemocytes, the plasma also contains many chemicals. It is the major tissue type of the open circulatory system characteristic of arthropods (e.g. arachnids, crustaceans and insects).',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49160,'biology',87,1130,2702,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Systemic blood circulation occurs in human beings<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> The purpose of this circulation is to carry oxygen and nutrients to the body tissues and to remove $$\\mathrm{CO}_{2}$$ and other wastes from the tissues','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are incorrect", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Systemic blood circulation occurs in human beings. The purpose of this circulation is to carry oxygen and nutrients to the body tissues and to remove $$\\mathrm{CO}_{2}$$ and other wastes from the tissues',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49161,'biology',87,1127,2699,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Left ventricle of heart has thickest wall than that of right ventricle.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Right ventricle need to pump blood to nearby lungs only','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are incorrect", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Systemic blood circulation occurs in human beings. The purpose of this circulation is to carry oxygen and nutrients to the body tissues and to remove $$\\mathrm{CO}_{2}$$ and other wastes from the tissues',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49162,'biology',87,1129,2701,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> ECG is an important method for checking the health or health related problems of the heart.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Function of valves is checked by ECG','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are incorrect", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Electrocardiogram (ECG or EKG) is done to assess the heart rate and rhythm. This test can often detect heart disease, heart attack, an enlarged heart, or abnormal heart rhythms that may cause heart failure. ECG is important method for checking the health or health related problems of the heart, function of valves is checked by ECG.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49163,'biology',87,1132,2704,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Atherosclerosis is a disease characterized by the thickening of arterial walls.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Deposition of cholesterol and triglycerides in the arterial walls cause atherosclerosis.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are incorrect", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Atherosclerosis is thickening of blood vessels due to deposition of fat including cholesterol. Frequently causes myocardial ischaemia leading to myocardial infarction.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49164,'biology',87,1130,2702,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> A portal system is a complex of veins which begins in capillaries and ends in capillaries before the blood which courses through it is returned to the heart<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> All vertebrates have a hepatic portal system.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are incorrect", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'A vein carrying deoxygenated blood from a system of capillaries divides into a second capillary system in the tissues before the blood finally returns to the heart. This vein is known as the portal vein. Thus, a portal vein together with the network of capillaries forms the portal system. The portal venous system is responsible for directing blood from parts of the gastrointestinal tract to the liver. A hepatic portal system is found in all vertebrates. Thus, both Assertion and Reason are true but Reason is not the correct explanation of Assertion',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49165,'biology',87,1127,2699,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> SA node is contraction centre or pacemaker.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Nodal fibres of SA node are embedded in the wall of the right atrium.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are incorrect", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'The nodal fibres of SA node are completely embedded in the wall of the right atrium. On the other hand the muscle fibres of SA node possess the highest rhythmicity among all cardiac muscle fibres and can initiate excitatory waves at the highest rate. The SA node determines the rate of heartbeat and is thus called the pacemaker of heart. It controls the rhythm of the heartbeat in a normal range. Thus, both assertion and reason are true but the reason is not the correct explanation of assertion.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49166,'biology',87,1127,2699,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Human heart is myogenic, as it generates its own electrochemical impulse.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> It is not under any neural or endocrine control.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are incorrect", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Human heart is myogenic, as it generates its own electrochemical impulse. The heart is composed of cardiac muscle, an involuntary muscle tissue that is found only within this organ.The cardiac muscle is self-exciting, meaning it has its own conduction system. Thus, the assertion is true and the reason is correct explanation of Assertion.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49167,'biology',87,1130,2702,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Pulmonary circulation includes flow of oxygenated blood from right ventricles to the lungs.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> The main purpose of this circulation is purification of blood.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are incorrect", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Both Assertion and Reason are incorrect, Deoxygenated blood flows out of right ventricles and the purpose of this circulation is oxygenation&nbsp;',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49168,'biology',87,1130,2702,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Arteries carry blood from heart to various body organs.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Veins carry blood from various body organs to heart.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are incorrect", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'The arteries are the blood vessels that deliver oxygen-rich blood from the heart to the tissues of the body. Each artery is a muscular tube lined by smooth tissue and has three layers: Veins are blood vessels that carry blood toward the heart. Most veins carry deoxygenated blood from the tissues back to the heart; exceptions are the pulmonary and umbilical veins, both of which carry oxygenated blood to the heart. In contrast to veins, arteries carry blood away from the heart.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49169,'biology',87,1128,2700,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> First heart sound is \'dubb\' while second heart sound is \'lubb\'.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> \'Lubb\' is due to closing of auriculo ventricular valves while \'dupp\' is due to closing of semilunar valves.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are incorrect", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Lubb is the first heart sound which is dull, loud or low pitched of long duration and it produced due to closure of atrio-ventricular valves. Dubb is the second heart sound which is sharp, high pitched of shorter duration and is produced due to closure of semilunar valves at the base of arteries.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49170,'biology',87,1132,2704,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Arteriosclerosis is known as hardening of arteries.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> The arterial walls become thick and inelastic due to deposition of cholesterol and calcium salts.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are incorrect", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Arteriosclerosis is known as hardening of arteries and the arterial walls become thick and inelastic due to deposition of cholesterol and calcium salts.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49171,'biology',87,1127,2699,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> SAN initiates the heart rate<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Sympathetic nerve fibres increase the cardiac activity.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are incorrect", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'SA node (sinoatrial node) is known as the heart\'s natural pacemaker The impulse starts in a small bundle of specialized cells located in the right atrium, called the SA node. The electrical activity spreads through the walls of the atria and causes them to contract. This forces blood, into the ventricles. The SA node sets the rate and rhythm of your heartbeat. Normal heart rhythm is often called normal sinus rhythm because the SA (sinus) node fires regularly. The sympathetic nervous system releases norepinephrine (NE) while the parasympathetic nervous system releases acetylcholine (ACh). Sympathetic stimulation increases heart rate and myocardial contractility.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49172,'biology',87,1127,2699,'In humans, blood passes from the post caval to the diastolic right atrium of heart due to','[{"text": "Stimulation of sino auricular node", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Pressure difference between the post caval and atrium", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Pushing open of the venous valves", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Suction pull", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'The deoxygenated blood passes from the inferior vena cava/post caval to the right atrium by pushing the venous valve(Thebesian valve)&nbsp;',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2008,'AIPMT','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49173,'biology',87,1122,2693,'Globulins contained in human blood plasma are primarily involved in','[{"text": "Osmotic balance of body fluids", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Oxygen transport in the blood", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Clotting of blood", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Defence mechanisms of body", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Globulins primarily are involved in defense mechanisms of the body.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2009,'AIPMT','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49174,'biology',87,1129,2701,'In a standard ECG which one of the following alphabets is the correct representation of the respective activity of the human heart?','[{"text": "S-start of systole", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "T-end of diastole", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "P-depolarisation of atria", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "R-repolarisation of ventricles", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'The P-wave represents the electrical excitation (or depolarisation) of the atria, which leads to the contraction of both the atria. The QRS complex represents the depolarisation of the ventricles, which initiates the ventricular contraction. The contraction starts shortly after $$\\mathrm{Q}$$ and marks the beginning of the systole.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2009,'AIPMT','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49175,'biology',87,1130,2702,'Fastest distribution of some injectable material/ medicine and with no risk of any kind can be achieved by injecting it into the','[{"text": "Muscles", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Arteries", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Veins", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Lymph vessels", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Intravenous injection is given for rapid distribution of drugs/substances.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2010,'AIPMT','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49176,'biology',87,1127,2699,'If due to some injury the chordae tendinae of the tricuspid valve of the human heart is partially nonfunctional what will be the immediate effect?','[{"text": "The pacemaker will stop working", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "The blood will tend to flow back into the left atrium", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "The flow of blood into the aorta will be slowed down", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "The flow of blood into the pulmonary artery will be reduced", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Percentage of Haemoglobin in Human Body: Men: 13.5 to $$16.5 \\mathrm{~g} / \\mathrm{dl}$$, Women: 12.1 to $$15.1 \\mathrm{~g} / \\mathrm{dl}$$ and Children: 11 to $$16 \\mathrm{~g} / \\mathrm{dl}$$.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2010,'AIPMT','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49177,'biology',87,1127,2699,'\'Bundle of His\' is a part of which one of the following organs in humans?','[{"text": "Brain", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Heart", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Kidney", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Pancreas", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'The bundle of His is an important part of the electrical conduction system of the heart. It transmits impulses from the atrioventricular node located at the ventricles of the heart.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2011,'AIPMT','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49178,'biology',87,1130,2702,'Arteries are best defined as the vessels which','[{"text": "Supply oxygenated blood to the different organs", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Carry blood away from the heart to different organs", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Break up into capillaries which reunite to form a vein", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Carry blood from one visceral organ to another visceral organ", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Arteries are smooth and elastic muscle tubes that carry oxygenated blood away from the heart to other parts or organs of the body.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2011,'AIPMT','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49179,'biology',87,1129,2701,'Given below is the ECG of a normal human. Which one of its components is correctly interpreted below?','[{"text": "Beginning of the systole", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "End of systole", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Contraction of both the atria", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Initiation of the ventricular contraction", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'The QRS complex represents the depolarisation of the ventricles, which initiates the ventricular contraction',NULL,'Medium','MCQ type-3',2011,'AIPMT','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','biology/87_49179_question_1.png'),
(49180,'biology',87,1122,2693,'Which one of the following plasma proteins is involved in the coagulation of blood?','[{"text": "Albumin", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Serum amylase", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Globulin", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Fibrinogen", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Fibrinogen, the protein present in the blood plasma is converted into the insoluble protein fibrin during the clotting process.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2011,'AIPMT','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49181,'biology',87,1132,2704,'Which one of the following statements is correct regarding blood pressure?','[{"text": "$$130 / 90 \\\\mathrm{~mm} \\\\mathrm{Hg}$$ is considered high and requires treatment.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$100 / 55 \\\\mathrm{~mm} \\\\mathrm{Hg}$$ is considered an ideal blood pressure.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$105 / 55 \\\\mathrm{~mm} \\\\mathrm{Hg}$$ makes one very active.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$$190 / 110 \\\\mathrm{~mm} \\\\mathrm{Hg}$$ may harm vital organs like brain and kidney.", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'A blood pressure above $$150 / 100$$ is considered hypertensive and if it ranges far above like 190/110 then it may possibly harm vital organs of the body.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2011,'AIPMT','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49183,'biology',87,1122,2694,'Which one of the following human organs is often called as the "graveyard" of RBCs?','[{"text": "Gall bladder", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Kidney", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Spleen", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Liver", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'The spleen plays important roles in regard to red blood cells and the immune system. Old and damaged RBCs are destroyed in the spleen. Hence, it is known as the graveyard of RBCs.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2012,'AIPMT','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49184,'biology',87,1127,2699,'The given figure shows schematic plan of blood circulation in humans with label A to D. Identify the label and give its functions?','[{"text": "C-Vena Cava- takes blood from body parts to right auricle, $$\\\\mathrm{pCO}_{2}=45 \\\\mathrm{~mm} \\\\mathrm{Hg}$$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "D-Dorsal aorta - takes blood from heart to body parts, $$\\\\mathrm{PO}_{2}=95 \\\\mathrm{~mm} \\\\mathrm{Hg}$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "A- Pulmonary vein - takes impure blood from body parts, $$\\\\mathrm{PO}_{2}=60 \\\\mathrm{~mm} \\\\mathrm{Hg}$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "B- Pulmonary artery- takes blood from heart to lungs, $$\\\\mathrm{PO}_{2}=90 \\\\mathrm{~mm} \\\\mathrm{Hg}$$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Vena Cava takes blood from the body parts to right auricle. Partial pressure of $$\\mathrm{CO}_{2}$$ is ${\\rm{45mmHg}}\\,{\\rm{Hg}}$ under which $$\\mathrm{CO}_{2}$$ is taken from tissues.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ type-3',2013,'AIPMT','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','biology/87_49184_question_1.png'),
(49185,'biology',87,1129,2701,'The diagram given here is the standard ECG of a normal person. The P-wave represents the','[{"text": "Beginning of the systole", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "End of systole", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Contraction of both the atria", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Initiation of the ventricular contraction", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'$$\\mathrm{P}$$ wave in an ECG shows contraction of both the atria.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ type-3',2013,'AIPMT','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','biology/87_49185_question_1.png'),
(49186,'biology',87,1123,2695,'Person with blood group $$A B$$ is considered as universal recipient because he has','[{"text": "Both A and B antigens on RBC but no antibodies in the plasma", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both A and B antibodies in the plasma", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "No antigen on RBC and no antibody in the plasma", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both A and B antigens in the plasma but no antibodies", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Blood Group \'AB\' contains RBCs that have both antigens \' A \' and \' B \' and thus it does not have reactive antibodies in its plasma to these antigens.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2014,'AIPMT','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49187,'biology',87,1131,2703,'How do parasympathetic neural signals affect the working of the heart?','[{"text": "Reduce both heart rate and cardiac output", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Heart rate is increased without affecting the cardiac output", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both heart rate and cardiac output increase", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Heart rate decreases but cardiac output increases", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'If the parasympathetic nervous system is stimulated it will increase the resting potential and decrease the rate of diastolic depolarisation. Thus, these circumstances will slow down the heart rate and the cardiac output also.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2014,'AIPMT','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49188,'biology',87,1125,2697,'Which one of the following is correct?','[{"text": "Lymph $$=$$ Plasma $$+\\\\mathrm{RBC}+\\\\mathrm{WBC}$$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Blood $$=$$ Plasma + RBC + WBC + Platelets", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Plasma $$=$$ Blood - Lymphocytes", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Serum $$=$$ Blood + Fibrinogen", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Blood contains Plasma, WBCs, RBCs and Platelets. The plasma is the straw-coloured fluid and forms about $$60 \\%$$ of blood. The white blood cells (WBCs), platelets, and the red blood cells (RBCs) form about $$40 \\%$$ of the blood.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2015,'AIPMT','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49189,'biology',87,1126,2698,'Which one of the following animals has two separate circulatory pathways?','[{"text": "Whale", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Shark", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Frog", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Lizard", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Whale is a mammal and has a 4 chambered heart. It has double circulatory pathways viz., systemic and pulmonary circulation. Shark is a fish and has a single circuit for blood flow and a two-chambered heart. Frog an amphibian and lizard a reptile both have a three chambered heart but double circulatory system, still there is mixing of blood due to a single ventricle.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2015,'AIPMT','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49190,'biology',87,1122,2694,'Erythropoiesis in adults occurs in','[{"text": "Spleen", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Red bone marrow", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Kidney", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Liver", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'The short life span of RBCs makes it important for the process of erythropoiesis which means formation of red blood cells. All blood cells are formed in the bone marrow. Bone marrow is soft, highly cellular tissue that fills the internal cavities of bones.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2015,'AIPMT','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49191,'biology',87,1128,2700,'Blood pressure in the mammalian aorta is maximum during','[{"text": "Systole of the left ventricle", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Diastole of the right atrium", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Systole of the left atrium", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Diastole of the right ventricle", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'The role of the left ventricle is to pump newly oxygenated blood to the body and other organs excluding the lungs. Hence, blood pressure in aorta will be maximum when left ventricle pumps the stroke volume into its lumen during its systole.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2015,'AIPMT','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49192,'biology',87,1128,2700,'Doctors use stethoscope to hear the sounds produced during each cardiac cycle. The second sound is heard when','[{"text": "AV node receives signal from SA node", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "AV valves open", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Ventricular walls vibrate due to gushing in of blood from atria", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Semilunar valves close down after the blood flows into vessels from ventricles", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'By using a stethoscope, two heart sounds "lubbdupp" can be heard during each cardiac cycle. These sounds are due to the closing of the heart valves. The first heart sound is generated when atrioventricular valves close at start of ventricular systole. The second heart sound occurs when semilunar valves close at the end of ventricular systole.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2015,'AIPMT','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49193,'biology',87,1127,2699,'Blood pressure in pulmonary artery is','[{"text": "Same as that in the aorta", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "More than that in the carotid", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "More than in the pulmonary vein", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Less than in the venae cavae", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'The blood pressure in different blood vessels in the body is as follows:<br>Artery $$>$$ arteriole $$>$$ capillary $$>$$ venule $$>$$ vein (venae cavae). Since, the pressure is higher in artery than in veins hence, the pressure in pulmonary artery will be more than in pulmonary vein.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2016,'NEET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49195,'biology',87,1125,2697,'Serum differs from blood in','[{"text": "Lacking clotting factors", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Lacking antibodies", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Lacking globulins", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Lacking albumins", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Serum is part of the blood that is left over after the red blood cells, the white blood cells, the blood platelets, and clotting factors have been removed from the blood. The blood serum contains all the proteins that are not used in blood clotting, the electrolytes, antibodies, antigens, hormones, and substances such as drugs and microorganisms.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2016,'NEET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49196,'biology',87,1122,2694,'Adult human RBCs are enucleated. Which of the following statement(s) is/are most appropriate explanation for this feature?<br>(i) They do not need to reproduce.<br>(ii) They are somatic cells.<br>(iii) They do not metabolise.<br>(iv) All their internal space is available for oxygen transport.','[{"text": "Only (i)", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "(ii) and (iii)", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "(i), (iii) and (iv)", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Only (iv)", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Red blood corpuscles of adult mammals are nucleated, biconcave in shape. The structure of RBC is advantageous in gas exchange because it has more surface area than sphere',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2017,'NEET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49197,'biology',87,1122,2693,'Match the items given in Column I with those in Column II and select the correct option given below :<div class="matching-question"><div class="column-left">A. Fibrinogen<br>B. Globulin<br>C. Albumin</div><div class="column-right">P. Osmotic balance<br>Q. Blood clotting<br>R. Defence mechanism</div></div>','[{"text": "A - P, B - R, C - Q", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "A - P, B - Q, C - R", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "A - R, B - Q, C - P", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "A - Q, B - R, C - P", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Fibrinogen, Globulin and Albumin are proteins.Fibrinogen is essential for formation of blood clot.Globulin forms part of antibiotics that are involved in defense mechanism of body. Albumin is plasma protein that maintains osmotic balance.',NULL,'Medium','Match the following',2018,'NEET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49198,'biology',87,1127,2699,'Match the items given in Column I with those in Column II and select the correct option given below :<div class="matching-question"><div class="column-left">A. Tricuspid valve<br>B. Bicuspid valve<br>C. Semilunar valve</div><div class="column-right">P. Between left atrium and left ventricle<br>Q. Between right ventricle and pulmonary artery<br>R. Between right atrium and right ventricle</div></div>','[{"text": "A - P, B - Q, C - R", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "A - P, B - R, C - Q", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "A - R, B - P, C - Q", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "A - Q, B - P, C - R", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Tricuspid valves are Atrioventricular valve present between right atrium and right ventricle. Bicuspid valves are Atrioventricular valve present between left atrium and left ventricle. Semilunar valves are present at the openings of aortic and pulmonary aorta.',NULL,'Medium','Match the following',2018,'NEET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49199,'biology',87,1129,2701,'Match the Column-I with Column-II<br>Select the correct option.<div class="matching-question"><div class="column-left">A. P-wave<br>B. QRS complex<br>C. T-wave<br>D. Reduction in the size of T-wave</div><div class="column-right">P. Depolarisation of ventricles<br>Q.  Repolarisation of ventricles<br>R.  Coronary ischemia<br>S.  Depolarisation of atria<br>T.  Repolarisation of atria</div></div>','[{"text": "A - S, B - P, C - Q, D -R", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "A - S, B - P, C - Q, D -T", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "A - Q, B - P, C - T, D -R", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "A - Q, B - R, C - T, D -S", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'In ECG P-wave represents depolarisation of atria. QRS complex represents depolarisation of ventricles. T-wave represents repolarisation of ventricle i.e. return from excited to normal state. Reduction in the size of T-wave i.e. if the T-wave represents insufficient supply of oxygen i.e. coronary ischaemia.',NULL,'Medium','Match the following',2019,'NEET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49200,'biology',87,1129,2701,'The QRS complex in a standard ECG represents:','[{"text": "Depolarisation of auricles", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Depolarisation of ventricles", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Repolarisation of ventricles", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Repolarisation of auricles", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'The QRS complex represents the depolarisation of the ventricles, which initiates the ventricular contraction. The contraction starts shortly after Q and marks the beginning of the systole.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2020,'NEET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49201,'biology',87,1122,2694,'Match the following columns and select the correct option.<br><div class="matching-question"><div class="column-left">A. Eosinophils<br>B. Basophils<br>C. Neutrophils<br>D. Lymphocytes</div><div class="column-right">P. Immune response<br>Q. Phagocytosis<br>R. Release histaminase, destructive enzymes<br>S. Release granules containing histamine</div></div>','[{"text": "A - S, B - P, C - Q, D -R", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "A - P, B - Q, C - S, D -R", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "A - Q, B - P, C - R, D -S", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "A - R, B - S, C - Q, D -P", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Eosinophils - Release histaminase, destructive enzymes<br>Basophils - Release granules containing histamine<br>Neutrophils - Phagocytosis<br>Lymphocytes - Immune response.',NULL,'Medium','Match the following',2020,'NEET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49202,'biology',87,1124,2696,'Which enzyme is responsible for the conversion of inactive fibrinogens to fibrins?','[{"text": "Thrombin", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Renin", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Epinephrine", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Thrombokinase", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Thrombin is a naturally occurring enzyme that converts fibrinogen into fibrin, which is an integral step in clot formation',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2021,'NEET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49203,'biology',87,1123,2695,'Persons with \' $$A B$$ \' blood group are called as "Universal recipients". This is due to:','[{"text": "Absence of antigens A and B on the surface of RBCs", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Absence of antigens A and B in plasma", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Presence of antibodies, anti-A and anti-B, on RBCs", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Absence of antibodies, anti-A and anti-B in plasma", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'A person with blood group $$A B$$ is sometimes called a universal recipient because of lack of antibodies and presence of both $$\\mathrm{A}$$ and $$\\mathrm{B}$$ antigens. People with type $$\\mathrm{AB}+$$ blood are universal recipients because they have no antibodies to A, B or Rh in their blood and can receive red blood cells from a donor of any blood type.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2021,'NEET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49248,'biology',87,1129,2701,'In a standard ECG, which one of the following alphabets is the correct representation of the respective activity ofthe human heart?','[{"text": "P-Depolarization of the atria", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "R-Repolarization of ventricles", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "S-Start of systole", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "T-End of diastole", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'The P-wave indicates atrial depolarization.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49204,'biology',87,1128,2700,'Which one of the following statements is correct?','[{"text": "The tricuspid and the biscuspid valves open due to the pressure exerted by the simultaneous contraction of the atria", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Blood moves freely from atrium to the ventricle during joint diastole.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Increased ventricular pressure causes closing of the semilunar valves.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "The atrio-ventricular node (AVN) generates an action potential to stimulate atrial contraction", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'During joint diastole stage AV valves open and passive filling of ventricle occurs. The tricuspid and the bicuspid valves open due to the fall in the ventricular pressure during the joint diastole itself (not during atrial systole). Increased ventricular pressure causes closing of the atrioventricular (bicuspid and tricuspid) valves. Atrial contraction is stimulated by the action potentials generated by the SA node.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2022,'NEET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49205,'biology',87,1124,2696,'Given below are two statements<br><div class="matching-question"><div class="column-left">The coagulum is formed of network of threads called thrombins.<br></div><div class="column-right">Spleen is the graveyard of erythrocytes.<br><br>In the light of the above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:</div></div>','[{"text": "Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Statement I and Statement II are correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Coagulum is formed of network of threads called fibrins (not thrombins). Spleen is the graveyard of erythrocytes.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ type-2',2022,'NEET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49206,'biology',87,1128,2700,'Considering the stroke volume of an adult healthy human being is 70 mL, identify the cardiac output in one hour from the following:','[{"text": "30.24 Lit/hour", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "302.4 Lit/hour", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "50.40 Lit/hour", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "504.0 Lit/hour", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Cardiac out put = stroke volume × no. of heart beats / min\n<br>Stroke volume = 70 ml\n<br>No. of beats per minute 72\n<br>No. of beats per one hour 4,320\n<br>Stoke volume × No. of beats <br>= 70×4320\n<br>= 302,4 lit/hour',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2023,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49207,'biology',87,1127,2699,'Identify the option showing the correct labelling for p, q, r and s with reference to conducting system of the human heart.','[{"text": "p – AVN, q – SAN, r – Interventricular\\nseptum, s – Bundle of His", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "p – Bundle of His, q – SAN,\\nr – Interventricular septum, s – AVN", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "p – Interventricular septum, q – AVN,\\nr – Bundle of His, s – SAN", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "p – SAN, q – AVN, r – Bundle of His,\\ns – Interventricular septum", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'p- Sino atrial node (SAN)\n<br>q - Atrio – Ventricular node (AVN)\n<br>r - Bundle of His\n<br>s- Interventricular septum',NULL,'Medium','MCQ type-3',2023,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','biology/87_49207_question_1.png'),
(49208,'biology',87,1129,2701,'Match Column I with Column II.<br>Choose the correct answer from the options given below :<div class="matching-question"><div class="column-left">A. P-wave<br>B. Q-wave<br>C. QRS complex<br>D. T-wave</div><div class="column-right">P. Beginning of systole<br>Q. Repolarisation of ventricles<br>R. Depolarisation of atria<br>S. Depolarisation of ventricles</div></div>','[{"text": "A-R, B-P, C-S, D-Q", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "A-S, B-R, C-Q, D-P", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "A-Q, B-S, C-P, D-R", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "A-P, B-Q, C-R, D-S", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'In a standard ECG, P-wave represents the electrical excitation (or depolarisation) of the atria which leads to the contraction of both the atria.<br>• QRS complex represents the depolarisation of ventricles which initiates the ventricular contraction.<br>• T-wave represents the return of the ventricles from excited to normal state.',NULL,'Medium','Match the following',2023,'NEET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49209,'biology',87,1122,2694,'Which of the following statements are correct?<br>I. Basophils are most abundant cells of the total WBCs<br>II. Basophils secrete histamine, serotonin and heparin<br>III. Basophils are involved in inflammatory response<br>IV. Basophils have kidney shaped nucleus<br>V. Basophils are agranulocytes<br>Choose the correct answer from the options given below:','[{"text": "IV and V only", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "III and V only", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "II and III only", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "I and II only", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Basophils secrete histamine, serotonin, heparin etc. and are involved in inflammatory response.<br>Basophils are granulocytes.<br>Basophils are least (0.5–1%) abundant of all WBCs.<br>Monocytes have a kidney-shaped nucleus',NULL,'Medium','MCQ type-2',2023,'NEET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49210,'biology',87,1124,2696,'Which of the following prevents the conversion of prothrombin to thrombin in an undamaged blood vessel?','[{"text": "Calcium ions", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Thromboplastin", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Fibrinogen", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Heparin", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Heparin is produced by mast cells and basophils, it combines with antithrombin and increases its effectiveness in blocking thrombin.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2009,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49211,'biology',87,1123,2695,'As per the guidelines of the Indian Red Cross society, which of the following persons is recommended for blood donation?','[{"text": "People not in good health, under the influence ofalcohol or drugs.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Ladies during menstruation, pregnancy and breast feeding.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Healthy women but unwed and below the age of 35 .", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Persons who are immunized with live vaccines.", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'As per guidlines of Indian Red Cross Society, persons who are immunized with live vaccines are recommended for blood donation.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2011,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49212,'biology',87,1128,2700,'The volume of blood that enters into the aorta with each ventricular systole is called:','[{"text": "Cardiac cycle", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Stroke volume", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Cardiac output", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Vital capacity", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Stroke volume is the volume of blood ejected from the left ventricle (or the right ventricle) into the aorta (or pulmonary trunk) each minute.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2012,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49235,'biology',87,1128,2700,'Blood pressure in the mammalian aorta is maximum during','[{"text": "Systole of the left ventricle", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Diastole of the right atrium", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Systole of the left atrium", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Diastole of the right ventricle", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Systole of left ventricle has maximum pressure because it has to pump the blood to aorta, which distributes blood all parts of body with much pressure.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49213,'biology',87,1127,2699,'The frequency of heart beat in our body is maintained by:','[{"text": "AV Node", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "SA Node", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Node of Ranvier", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Chordae tendinac", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Cardiac excitation normally begins in the SAN. SA node cells do not have a stable resting potential. Rather, they repeatedly depolarize to threshold spontaneously. The spontaneous depolarization is a pacemaker potential. When the pacemaker potential reaches threshold, it triggers an action potential. Each action potential from the SAN propagates throughout both atria via gap junetions in the intercalated discs of atrial muscle fibres.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2012,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49214,'biology',87,1122,2694,'Study the diagram given below and identify the cells labelled as A, B, C and D, and choose the correct option.','[{"text": "A - Eosinophil, B - Erythrocyte, C - Neutrophil and D - Basophil", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "A-Eosinophil, B- Lymphocyte, C-Neutrophil and<br>D - Monocyte.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "A - Erythrocyte, B - Basophil, C - Neutrophil and<br>D - Lymphocyte", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "A - Eosinophil, B - Monocyte, C - Neutrophil and<br>D - Lymphocyte", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'• \'A\' represents eosinophils. They have bilobed nucleus connected by either a thin or thick strand of nuclear material.<br>•\' B \' represents monocytes. The nucleus of a monocyte is usually kidney-shaped or horseshoe-shaped.<br>• \'C\' represents neutrophils. They have two to five lobed nuclei connected by thin strands of nuclear material.<br>•\' D \' represents lymphocytes. The nucleus of a lymphocyte stains dark and is round or slightly indented.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ type-3',2012,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','biology/87_49214_question_1.png'),
(49215,'biology',87,1129,2701,'In the following diagrammatic representation of a standard ECG the \' T \' represents.','[{"text": "Depolarisation of artia", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Depolarisation of ventricles", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Repolarisation of artia", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Repolarisation of ventricles", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'$\\mathrm{T}$ - wave is third wave is a dome - shaped upward deflection and it indicates ventricular repolarisation. It occurs just as the ventricles are starting to relax. The systole ends at the end of $\\mathrm{T}$ - wave.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ type-3',2017,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','biology/87_49215_question_1.png'),
(49216,'biology',87,1122,2694,'The blood cell that secretes histamine, serotonin and heparin is<br>','[{"text": "Neutrophil", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "T - lymphocyte", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Killer cell", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Basophil", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Basophils liberate heparin, histamine, and serotonin in allergic reactions that intensify overall inflammatory response',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2017,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49217,'biology',87,1123,2695,'Select the correct Rh-blood groups of the parents, whose child is affected with erythroblastosis fetalis.','[{"text": "Both father and mother are $\\\\mathrm{Rh}+\\\\mathrm{ve}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Mother is $\\\\mathrm{Rh}+\\\\mathrm{ve}$ and father is $\\\\mathrm{Rh}$-ve", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both father and mother are $\\\\mathrm{Rh}$-ve", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Father is $\\\\mathrm{Rh}+\\\\mathrm{ve}$ and mother is $\\\\mathrm{Rh}-\\\\mathrm{ve}$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'If the father is $\\mathrm{Rh}+\\mathrm{ve}$ and mother is $\\mathrm{Rh}$ - ve, the probability of child being $\\mathrm{Rh}+\\mathrm{ve}$ is more. If a woman is $\\mathrm{Rh}-\\mathrm{ve}$ and the foetus is $\\mathrm{Rh}+\\mathrm{ve}$, it can lead to $\\mathrm{Rh}$ incompatibility and potential complications in the pregnancy and thus erythroblastosis fetalis.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2018,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49218,'biology',87,1132,2704,'Choose the correct statement from the following','[{"text": "Erythroblastosis foetalis may result when foetus in $\\\\mathrm{Rh}$ negative and mother is $\\\\mathrm{Rh}$ positive.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Histamine, serotonin and heparin are secreted by basophils", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Atherosclerosis is often referred as anginapectoris", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Person with blood group $\\\\mathrm{AB}$ can donate blood to person with blood group", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'• Basophils secrete inflammatory mediators, such as histamine and serotonin and prevent blood clotting by separating heparin<br>• Erythroblastosis foetalis may result when foetus in Rh positive and mother is Rh negative.<br>• Atherosclerosis is often referred as arteriosclerosis.<br>• Person with blood group $\\mathrm{AB}$ can donate blood to person with blood group $\\mathrm{AB}$ only, but can receive blood from person with blood group $\\mathrm{A}$, $\\mathrm{B}, \\mathrm{AB}$ or $\\mathrm{O}$.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2019,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49219,'biology',87,1122,2694,'Match the following columns and select the Correct option.<div class="matching-question"><div class="column-left">A. Eosinophils<br>B. Basophils<br>C. Neutrophils<br>D. Lymphocytes</div><div class="column-right">P. Immune response<br>Q. Phagocytosis<br>R. Release\nhistaminase,\ndestructive\nenzymes<br>S.Release granules\ncontaining\nhistamine</div></div>','[{"text": "A - S, B - P, C - Q, D - R", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "A - P, B - Q, C - S, D - R", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "A - Q, B - P, C - R, D - S", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "A - R, B - S, C - Q, D - P", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'$\\mathrm{A}-\\mathrm{R}, \\mathrm{B}-\\mathrm{S}, \\mathrm{C}-\\mathrm{Q}, \\mathrm{D}-\\mathrm{P}$',NULL,'Medium','Match the following',2020,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49220,'biology',87,1128,2700,'Calculate the cardiac output of an Individual having 70 heart beats/ $\\mathrm{min}$ with a stroke volume of $55 \\mathrm{ml}$','[{"text": "$3750 \\\\mathrm{ml}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$125 \\\\mathrm{ml}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$3850 \\\\mathrm{ml}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "None", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Cardiac output $=$ Stroke volume heart beat $55 \\times 70=3850 \\mathrm{~ml}$.)',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2021,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49221,'biology',87,1129,2701,'In a standard ECG one of the following functions of its components is not correctly interpreted','[{"text": "$\\\\mathrm{P}$ is the contraction of only left atria", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "QRS complex represents ventricular contraction", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{T}$ is the end of systole", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{P}$ is the contraction of both atria", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'$\\mathrm{P}$ wave is the contraction of both the atria.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2021,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49222,'biology',87,1128,2700,'Cardiac output is/are<br>(i) Product of heart rate and stroke volume.<br>(ii) Product of auricular and ventricular volume.<br>(iii) Volume of blood pumped by each ventricle in one minute<br>Which of the above statements is true?','[{"text": "Only (i)", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Only (ii)", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both (i) and (ii)", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both (i)and (iii)", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Cardiac output is the volume of blood pumped out by each ventricle per minute, i.e. stroke volume $\\mathrm{x}$ heart rate.',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49223,'biology',87,1128,2700,'Which one of the following will be the cardiac output (in litres per minute) of a heart that has a stroke volume of 0.07 litres and is beating at a rate of 90 per minute?','[{"text": "$63.30$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$63.00$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$00.63$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$06.30$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Cardiac output is the amount of blood flowing from the heart (i.e from the left ventricle into the aorta) over a given period of time. It can be calculated by stroke volume (the volume of blood pumped by heart/ heart beat) and heart rate (ventricular systole/min).<br>Cardiac output $=$<br>Stroke volume $\\times$ Heart rate<br>Stroke volume -0.07 litres<br>Heart rate -90 / minute<br>According to the formula: $0.07 \\times 90=6.3$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49224,'biology',87,1127,2699,'Select the correct option regarding the state of heart valves during ventricular systole.<br>$\\begin{array}{l}\n{\\bf{Semilunar}}\\\\\n\\,{\\bf{valves}}\\,\n\\end{array}$ &emsp;  $\\begin{array}{l}\n{\\bf{Bicuspid}}\\,{\\bf{and}}\\\\\n{\\bf{tricuspid}}\\,{\\bf{valves}}\n\\end{array}$<br>','[{"text": "Open&emsp;  &emsp;  &emsp;  Close", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Close&emsp;  &emsp;  &emsp; Open", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Open&emsp;  &emsp;  &emsp;   Open", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Closed&emsp;&emsp;&emsp;Closed", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'During ventricular systole, the bicuspid and tricuspid valves close and the semilunar valves open.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49225,'biology',87,1131,2703,'The cardiovascular centre which regulates the heart rate and stroke volume is located in the','[{"text": "Cerebellum", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Hypothalamus", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Pons", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Medulla oblongata", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'The cardiovascular centre which regulates the heart rate and stroke volume is located in the medulla oblongata',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49226,'biology',87,1129,2701,'${\\rm{P}}$\' wave of ECG occurs just before the','[{"text": "onset of ventricular contraction.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "end of atrial contraction.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "beginning of atrial contraction.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "none of the above.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'The P-wave indicates atrial depolarization, which causes atrial systole. During this wave, the impulse of contraction is generated by the SA node.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49227,'biology',87,1130,2702,'During systemic circulation, blood leaves the ___ (i)___ and ___ (ii)___.','[{"text": "(i)- left ventricle, (ii)- goes directly to the aorta", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "(i)- right ventricle, (ii)- goes directly to the aorta", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "(i)- right ventricle, (ii)- moves to the lungs.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "(i)- left ventricle, (ii)- moves to the lungs", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'During systemic circulation, blood leaves the left ventricle and goes directly to the aorta while during pulmonary circulation, blood leaves the right ventricle and moves to the lungs.',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49228,'biology',87,1130,2702,'Which circulation provides nutrients and oxygen only to cardiac muscle tissue?','[{"text": "Pulmonary circulation", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Systemic circulation", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Lymphatic circulation", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Coronary circulation", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Coronary circulation is a system of coronary vessels that circulate blood to and from the cardiac musculature.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49229,'biology',87,1132,2704,'Which one of the following statements is correct regarding blood pressure?','[{"text": "$80 / 120 \\\\mathrm{mmHg}$ is normal blood pressure", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$130 / 90 \\\\mathrm{mmHg}$ is considered high and requires treatment.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$120 / 80 \\\\mathrm{mmHg}$ is considered an ideal blood pressure.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$105 / 50 \\\\mathrm{mmHg}$ makes one very active. thickened.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Blood pressure is the measure of the force of blood pushing against blood vessel walls. The heart pumps blood into the arteries which carry the blood through out the body. It is usually expressed in termsof the systolic (maximum) pressure over diastolic (minimum) pressure and is measured in millimeters of mercury ( $\\mathrm{mm} \\mathrm{Hg}$ ).',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49230,'biology',87,1132,2704,'The state of heart when it is not pumping blood effectively enough to meet the needs of the body is called.','[{"text": "angina", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "cardiac arrest", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "heart attack", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "heart failure", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Heart failure occurs when the heart is unable to pump sufficiently to maintain blood flow to meet the body\'s needs. Common causes of heart failure include coronary artery disease including a previous myocardial infarction (heart attack), high bloodpressure, atrial fibrillation, valvular heart disease, and cardiomyopathy.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49231,'biology',87,1123,2695,'What is correct for blood group \' $\\mathrm{O}$ \'?','[{"text": "No antigens but both anti - A and anti-B antibodies are present", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "\'A\' antigen and anti - B antibody", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both antigens and antibodies are absent", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "A\' and \' B \' antigens and anti-A and 15 anti-B antibodies", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Blood group \' O \' is known as the universal donor since, it does not contain antigens.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49232,'biology',87,1122,2694,'Which one is not a granulocyte?','[{"text": "Neutrophil", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Lymphocyte", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Eosinophil", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Basophil", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Lymphocyte is not a part of Granulocytes.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49233,'biology',87,1132,2704,'Which of the following factor (s) increase blood pressure?<br>(i) Increase of cardiac output<br>(ii) Constriction of blood vessel<br>(iii) Activation of parasympathetic nerve<br>(iv) Increase of blood volume','[{"text": "i,ii,iv are correct", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "ii, iii, iv are correct", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "i, iii, iv are correct", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "All are correct", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Increase in Cardiac output, Constriction of blood vessels.<br>Increase of blood volume increases the blood pressure.<br>Activation of parasympathetic neurons decreases the rate of hear beat speed of conduction of action potential and there by cardiac output hence blood pressure decreases.',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49234,'biology',87,1128,2700,'Cardiac cycle in man takes about','[{"text": "0.5 seconds", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "1.0 second", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "1.2 seconds", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "0.8 seconds", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'A cardiac cycle (atrial systole + ventricular systole + diastole) is completed in 0.8 seconds.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49236,'biology',87,1129,2701,'Identify \'Lub\' sound in the following diagram showing the relationship between ECG and heart sounds.','[{"text": "A", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "B", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "C", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "D", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'\'Lub\' sound is produced due to the closure of atrioventricular valves (bicuspid and tricuspid valves) at the beginning of ventricular systole. The QRS complex represents ventricular depolarization. Ventricular systole begins shortly after Q wave. So, B represents \'lub\' sound',NULL,'Hard','MCQ type-3',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','biology/87_49236_question_1.png'),
(49237,'biology',87,1129,2701,'Given below is the ECG of a normal human. Which one of its components is correctly interpreted?','[{"text": "Peak P and Peak R together - Systolic and diastolic blood pressures", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Peak P - Initiation of left atrial contraction only", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Complex QRS - One complete pulse", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Peak T - Initiation of total cardiac contraction", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'ECG (Electrocardiogram) is a graphic sketching of the variations in electrical potential caused by the excitation of the heart muscle and detected at the body surface. The normal ECG is a scalar representation that shows deflections resulting from cardiac activity. It comprises the $\\mathrm{P}$ wave, $\\mathrm{QRS}$ complex, $\\mathrm{T}$ wave and $\\mathrm{U}$ wave.',NULL,'Hard','MCQ type-3',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','biology/87_49237_question_1.png'),
(49238,'biology',87,1128,2700,'What is the stroke volume of a person whose heart rate is 60 beats per minute and cardiac output is 5.4 litres per minute?','[{"text": "$100 \\\\mathrm{~mL}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$75 \\\\mathrm{~mL}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$90 \\\\mathrm{~mL}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$60 \\\\mathrm{~mL}$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Cardiac output $=$ Stroke volume $\\times$ heart rate<br>$5400=$ Stroke volume $\\times 60$<br>Stroke volume $=5400 / 60=90$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49239,'biology',87,1128,2700,'Which of the following set of events can occur simultaneously?','[{"text": "Atrial depolarization, ventricular depolarization, atrial systole.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Ventricular depolarization, atrial systole, ventricular diastole.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Atrial depolarization, ventricular repolarization, atrial diastole.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Atrial depolarizaion, ventricular diastole, atrial systole.", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'A uricular depolarization, ventricular diastole and auricular systole are the events that occur in cardiac cycle simultaneously.',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49240,'biology',87,1129,2701,'Mark the incorrect statement regarding normal ECG.','[{"text": "Patient is connected to 3 electrical leads (one to each wrist and to the left ankle).", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{T}}$ \' wave represents atrial repolarization.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Q marks the beginning of ventricular depolarization.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{R}$ represents ventricular depolarization.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'T \' wave represents ventricular repolarization.',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49241,'biology',87,1130,2702,'Which structures are directly involved in the<br>"systemic circulation"?','[{"text": "Superior vena cava, right ventricle and left ventricle.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Right ventricle, pulmonary arteries and left atrium.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Left ventricle, aorta and inferior vena cava", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Right atrium, pulmonary trunk and left ventricle.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Systemic circulation involves circulation of blood between heart and various body parts except lungs. The oxygenated blood from the left ventricle of the heart is passed through aorta, arteries, arterioles and capillaries and reaches the tissues.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49242,'biology',87,1132,2704,'In normal human blood pressure, what does the<br>" 120 " represent?','[{"text": "The maximum pressure that can be sustained by the arteries before bursting.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "The lowest pressure in the arteries that will keep someone from fainting when they stand up.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "The highest ideal arterial pressure for someone at rest.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "The pressure in the veins.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'The systolic pressure, the highest pressure in the artery during each heartbeat of a healthy adult is about $120 \\mathrm{~mm}$ of $\\mathrm{Hg}$.',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49243,'biology',87,1132,2704,'In arteriosclerosis','[{"text": "arterial walls become very thin and weak so that the blood oozes out of the walls.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "sex linked heredity is involved.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "blood fats to coagulates", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "arterial walls become in elastic and thickened.", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Arteriosclerosis is the thickening, hardening and loss of elasticity of the walls of arteries.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49244,'biology',87,1132,2704,'Hardening of the arteries due to deposition of cholesterol is called','[{"text": "Thrombosis", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Atherosclerosis", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Rhinitis", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Angina", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Hardening of arteries due to deposition of cholesterol is called atherosclerosis.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49245,'biology',87,1132,2704,'Select the correct match<div class="matching-question"></div>','[{"text": "Heart attack - Sudden stoppage of heartbeat", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Angina pectoris - Acute chest pain due to decreased blood flow to the heart muscle", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Heart failure - Heart muscle damage due to inadequate blood supply", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Cardiac arrest - Inability of the heart to pump adequate blood", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Sudden stoppage of heartbeat-Cardiac arrest<br>Acute chest pain due to decreased blood flow to the heart muscle - Angina pectoris<br>Heart muscle damage due to inadequate blood supply - Heart attack<br>Inability of the heart to pump adequate blood -Heart failure',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49246,'biology',87,1122,2693,'Primary function of globulins in plasma is','[{"text": "Help in Osmotic balance", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Form blood cells", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "In defense mechanisms", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "In clotting", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Primary function of globulins in plasma in defense mechanisms.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49247,'biology',87,1124,2696,'Coagulum is formed mainly by','[{"text": "Threads of fibrin", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Thread of thrombin", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Threads of RBC", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Threads of fibrinogen", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Coagulum formed mainly by threads of fibrin',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49249,'biology',87,1127,2699,'A labeled red blood corpuscle is released into the arterial circulation in the leg. It is recaptured 30 seconds later in the left lung. What is the minimum number of chambers of the heart it must have passed through','[{"text": "0", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "1", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "2", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "3", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Minimum number of chambers of the heart it must have passed through is 2 . They are Right atrium and Right ventricle.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49250,'biology',87,1127,2699,'Which of the following parts of heart possess semilunar valves?<br>(i) Aorta<br>(ii) Vena cava<br>(iii) Pulmonary artery<br>(iv) Pulmonary vein','[{"text": "(i) and (iii)", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "(i), (ii) and (iii)", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "(i) only", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "(ii) and (iii)", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Semilunar valves are pocket-like structures attached at the point at which the pulmonary artery and the aorta leave the ventricles. Aorta and pulmonary artery possess semilunar valve. The pulmonary valve guards the orifice between the right ventricle and the pulmonary artery. The aortic valve protects the orifice between the left ventricle and the aorta.',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49251,'biology',87,1129,2701,'The given figure represents diagrammatic presentation of an ECG. Each peak in the ECG is identified with a letter from $\\mathrm{P}$ to $\\mathrm{T}$ that corresponds to a specific electrical activity of the heart.','[{"text": "QRS - Depolarization of the ventricles", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "R wave - Marks the end of the systole.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "P-Electrical excitation (or depolarization) of the atria", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "T wave - Return of the ventricles from excited to normal state (repolarization).", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'T wave marks the end of the ventricular systole.',NULL,'Hard','MCQ type-3',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','biology/87_49251_question_1.png'),
(49252,'biology',87,1122,2694,'Select the correct combination of a type of leucocyte, its percentage (of WBC), and its function.','[{"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Neutrophils are the most abundant (60-65 per cent) cells of the total WBCs. They are phagocytic. Their nucleus is 2 to 5 lobed.',NULL,'Hard','MCQ type-3',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49253,'biology',87,1128,2700,'The typical \'lub-dub\' sounds heard during heartbeat are produced due to','[{"text": "Closure of semilunar valves", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Closure of bicuspid and tricuspid valves", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Closure of bicuspid and tricuspid valves followed by semilunar valves", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Opening of bicuspid and tricuspid valves followed by semilunar valves<br>", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'During each cardiac cycle, two prominent sounds are produced which can be easily heard through a stethoscope. The first heart sound (lub) is associated with the closure of the tricuspid and bicuspid valves whereas the second heart sound (dub) is associated with the closure of the semilunar valves.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2024,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49254,'biology',87,1127,2699,'Following are the stages of pathway for conduction of an action potential through the heart<br>$\\begin{array}{*{20}{c}}\n{{\\rm{A}}{\\rm{.}}\\,{\\rm{AV}}\\,{\\rm{bundle}}}&{{\\rm{B}}{\\rm{.}}\\,{\\rm{Purkinje}}\\,{\\rm{fibres}}\\,\\,\\,\\,}\\\\\n{{\\rm{C}}{\\rm{.}}\\,{\\rm{AV}}\\,{\\rm{node}}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,}&{\\,{\\rm{D}}{\\rm{.}}\\,{\\rm{Bundle}}\\,{\\rm{branches}}}\\\\\n{{\\rm{E}}{\\rm{.}}\\,{\\rm{SA}}\\,{\\rm{node}}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,}&{}\n\\end{array}$<br>Choose the correct sequence of pathway from the options given below','[{"text": "E-C-A-D-B", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "A-E-C-B-D", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "B-D-E-C-A", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "E-A-D-B-C", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'The correct pathway of conduction of action potential is $\\mathrm{SA} \\rightarrow \\mathrm{AV}$ node $\\rightarrow \\mathrm{AV}$ bundle $\\rightarrow$ Bundle branches $\\rightarrow$ Purkinje fibres',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2024,'NEET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49255,'biology',87,1129,2701,'Match Column I with Column II :<br>Choose the correct answer from the options given below :<div class="matching-question"><div class="column-left">A. P wave<br>B. QRS complex<br>C. T wave<br>D. T - P gap</div><div class="column-right">P. Heart muscles are electrically silent<br>Q. Depolarisation of ventricles<br>R. Depolarisation of atria<br>S. Repolarisation of ventricles</div></div>','[{"text": "${\\\\rm{A  -  P, B  -  R, C  -  S, D  -  Q}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{A  -  R, B  -  Q, C  -  S, D  -  P}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{A  -  Q, B  -  R, C  -  P, D  -  S}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{A  -  S, B  -  Q, C  -  P, D  -  R}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'${\\rm{A}}\\,{\\rm{ - }}\\,{\\rm{R,}}\\,{\\rm{B}}\\,{\\rm{ - }}\\,{\\rm{Q,}}\\,{\\rm{C}}\\,{\\rm{ - }}\\,{\\rm{S,}}\\,{\\rm{D}}\\,{\\rm{ - }}\\,{\\rm{P}}$ is the correct option.<br>A - P wave - R. Depolarisation of atria.<br>B - QRS complex - Q. Depolarisation of ventricles.<br>C - T wave - S. Repolarisation of ventricles.<br>D - T-P gap - P. Heart muscles are electrically silent.',NULL,'Easy','Match the following',2024,'NEET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49256,'biology',97,1192,2853,'The French philosopher, mathematician and biologist of seventeenth century is','[{"text": "Rene Descartes", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Stanley Cohen", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Herbert Boyer", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Smith", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Rene Descartes, the French philosopher, mathematician and biologist of seventeenth century. Above line are present in Introduction to unit IX.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49257,'biology',97,1192,2853,'By 1969, ____ performed studies on a couple of restriction enzymes of the <i>E. coli</i> bacterium with especially useful properties.','[{"text": "Rene Descartes", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Stanley Cohen", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Herbert Boyer", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Smith", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'By 1969, Herbert Boyer performed studies on a couple of restriction enzymes of the E. coli bacterium with especially useful properties.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49258,'biology',97,1192,2853,'Name the process that employs controlled use of biological agents, such as live organisms or enzymes from organisms to produce products and processes useful to humans is called as','[{"text": "Bioinformatics", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Molecular biology", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Biotechnology", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Genetic technology", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'The techniques of using live organisms or enzymes from organisms to produce products and processes useful to humans are called biotechnology.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49259,'biology',97,1192,2853,'The techniques/processes that are included under biotechnology are','[{"text": "In vitro fertilisation", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Correcting a defective gene", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Developing DNA vaccine", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "All of these", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'In vitro fertilisation, synthesis of recombinant gene, correcting a defective gene and developing a DNA vaccine are all parts of biotechnology.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49261,'biology',97,1192,2853,'The definition of biotechnology given by EFB encompasses','[{"text": "Traditional view", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Modern molecular biotechnology", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Hybridiation", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both (1) and (2)", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'The European Federation of Biotechnology (EFB) has given a definition of biotechnology that encompasses both traditional views and modern molecular biotechnology. The definition given by EFB is as follows:<br>\'The integration of natural science and organisms, cells, parts thereof, and molecular analogues for products and services\'.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL);
INSERT INTO `qb_questions` (`id`, `subject_id`, `chapter_id`, `topic_id`, `subtopic_id`, `question_html`, `options`, `correct_index`, `explanation`, `explanation_image_url`, `difficulty`, `qtype`, `year`, `tag`, `created_at`, `question_image_url`) VALUES
(49262,'biology',97,1192,2853,'The integration of natural science and organisms, cells, parts thereof, and molecular analogues for products and services\'. This definition is given by','[{"text": "EFB", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Stanley Cohen", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Herbert Boyer", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "All the above", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'\'The integration of natural science and organisms, cells, parts thereof, and molecular analogues for products and services\'. The definition is given by EFB.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49263,'biology',97,1192,2853,'Which one of the following techniques made it possible to genetically engineer living organisms?','[{"text": "Recombinant DNA techniques", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "X-ray difdfraction", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Hybridization", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Heavier isotope labeling", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Recombinant DNA molecules are DNA molecules formed by laboratory methods of genetic recombination to bring together genetic material from multiple sources, creating sequences that would not otherwise be found in biological organisms. It is done by the process of recombinant DNA techniques.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49264,'biology',97,1192,2853,'The core techniques that gave birth to modern biotechnology are<br>I. Genetic engineering<br>II. Bioprocess engineering<br>III. HGP<br>IV. Bioinformatics<br>Choose the correct option','[{"text": "I and II", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "I and III", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "II and IV", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "II and III", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'The two core technologies that enabled birth of modern biotechnology are genetic engineering, which is the process of manipulation of genes, to introduce into the host organisms. Bioprocess engineering, i.e. processes that help the growth of the desired prokaryotic or eukaryotic cell in large quantities in a sterile medium for the manufacture and multiplication of biotechnological products.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49265,'biology',97,1192,2853,'Genetic engineering techniques include','[{"text": "Changing genotype only", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Changing phenotype only", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both 1 and 2", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "None of these", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Genetic engineering includes techniques which alter the chemistry of genetic material (DNA and RNA) to introduce these into host organisms and thus change the phenotype of the host organism.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49266,'biology',97,1192,2853,'Match the columns I and II.<div class="matching-question"><div class="column-left">A. Genetic engineering<br>B. Bioprocess engineering<br>C. Sexual reproduction</div><div class="column-right">P. Permits variation<br>Q. Alter the chemistry of genetic material<br>R. Enabling growth of only the desired microbe</div></div>','[{"text": "$\\\\mathrm{A}-\\\\mathrm{R}, \\\\mathrm{B}-\\\\mathrm{P}, \\\\mathrm{C}-\\\\mathrm{Q}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{A}-\\\\mathrm{Q}, \\\\mathrm{B}-\\\\mathrm{R}, \\\\mathrm{C}-\\\\mathrm{P}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{A}-\\\\mathrm{P}, \\\\mathrm{B}-\\\\mathrm{R}, \\\\mathrm{C}-\\\\mathrm{Q}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$A-Q, B-P, C-R$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Genetic engineering-Alter the chemistry of genetic material<br>Bioprocess engineering-Enabling growth of only the desired microbe<br>Sexual reproduction-Permits variation',NULL,'Easy','Match the following',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49267,'biology',97,1192,2853,'Genetic engineering is possible because','[{"text": "DNA can be visualised by electron microscope", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "DNA ligase can cut DNA at specific sites", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Restriction endonucleases can be purified from bacteria can be used in vitro", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "We can cut DNA at all sites by endonucleases", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'The discovery of Restriction endonucleases made the way for all possibilities of Biotechnology & Genetic engineering.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49268,'biology',97,1192,2853,'The specific sequence of DNA that initiate replication of alien DNA for cloning of DNA is called as','[{"text": "Promoter", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Origin of replication", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Origin of combination", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Initiator", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'In a chromosome there is a specific DNA sequence called the origin of replication, which is responsible for initiating replication. Therefore, initiate replication of alien DNA for cloning of DNA which has a specific sequence known as \'origin of replication\'.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49269,'biology',97,1192,2853,'Plasmid can be characterised by the following','[{"text": "Autonomously replicating", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Extra-chromosomal DNA", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Circular ds DNA", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "All of them", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Plasmid are self replicating, extra chromosomal ,circular Double stranded DNA.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49270,'biology',97,1192,2853,'Plasmid\'s replication is','[{"text": "Depending on the genomic DNA", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Autonomous", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Needs an initiator", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Does not replicate", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Plasmids are extra chromosomal double stranded circular self replicating DNA present in bacterial cell.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49271,'biology',97,1192,2853,'Match the columns I and II.<div class="matching-question"><div class="column-left">A. Recombinant DNA<br>B. Origin of replication<br>C. Plasmid</div><div class="column-right">P. Use of gene cloning and gene transfer<br>Q. Circular DNA<br>R. Initiating replication</div></div>','[{"text": "A – R , B – P , C – Q", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "A – Q , B – R , C – P", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "A – P , B – R , C – Q", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "A – Q , B – P , C – R", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Recombinant DNA- use of gene cloning and gene transfer Origin of replicationinitiating replication Plasmid-Circular DNA.',NULL,'Easy','Match the following',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49336,'biology',97,1194,2856,'If we are able to link an alien piece of DNA with bacteriophage or plasmid DNA, we can multiply its numbers ____ of the plasmid or bacteriophage.','[{"text": "Number can never be assessed", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "More than the copy number", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "1 less to the copy number", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Equal to the copy number", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Equal to the copy number',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49272,'biology',97,1192,2853,'The construction of the first recombinant DNA was done by using the native plasmid of','[{"text": "<i>E. coli</i>", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "<i>Salmonella typhimurium</i>", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "<i>Agrobacterium tumefaciens</i>", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Retrovirus", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'S. Cohen and H. Boyer constructed the first recombinant DNA using antibiotic resistance genes present on the plasmid of drug resistance strains of E. coli. This gene was linked to the native plasmid of  <i>Salmonella typhimurium</i>.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49273,'biology',97,1192,2853,'The first recombinant DNA was constructed by ____ in the year ____.','[{"text": "Stanley Cohen and Herbert Boyer 1972", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Stanley Cohen and Herbert Boyer 1963", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Stanley Cohen and Herbert Boyer 1968", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both (1) and (2) are correct", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Stanely Cohen and Herbert Boyer in the year 1972 generated the first recombinant DNA molecule by lining a gene encoding antibiotic resistance with a native plasmid of <i>Salmonella typhimurium</i>.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49274,'biology',97,1192,2853,'The linking of antibiotic resistance gene with the plasmid vector became possible with','[{"text": "DNA polymerase", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Restriction endonuclease", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "DNA ligase", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "RNA ligase", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'The linking of the antibiotic resistance genes with the plasmid vector became possible with DNA ligase. It is the enzyme that joins two complementary nucleotides.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49275,'biology',97,1192,2853,'When recombinant DNA is transferred into____ A____, a bacterium closely related to ____ B ____, it could replicate using the new host\'s DNA polymerase enzyme and make multiple copies.','[{"text": "A - <i>Salmonella</i>, B - <i>Salmonella</i>", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "A - <i>Escherichia coli</i>, B - <i>E.coli</i>", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "A - <i>Salmonella</i>, B - <i>E.coli</i>", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "A - <i>Escherichia coli</i>, B - <i>Salmonella</i>", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'A - Escherichia coli, B - Salmonella',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49276,'biology',97,1192,2853,'The different basic steps of genetically modifying an organism are given below randomly<br>I. Identification of DNA with desirable genes<br>II. Maintenance of introduced DNA in the host and transfer of the DNA to its progeny.<br>III. Introduction of identified DNA into the host<br>Which of the following represents the correct sequence of steps?','[{"text": "I, II, III", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "I, III and II", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "III, II and I", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "None of them", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'The correct sequence of step of genetic engineering are as follows<br>The first step involves the identification of DNA with desirable genes.<br>These genes are introduced into the host and are maintained in the host and transfer of this DNA to the host progeny takes place.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49277,'biology',97,1193,2854,'The DNA of interest obtained from the donor for recombinant DNA technology is called as','[{"text": "Foreign DNA", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Alien DNA", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Gene of interest", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "All of them", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'The DNA of interest obtained from the donor for recombinant DNA technology is called as Foreign DNA (foreign DNA and gene of interest.)',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49278,'biology',97,1193,2854,'The key tools required for the recombinant DNA technology are<br>$\\begin{array}{*{20}{c}}\n{{\\rm{I}}{\\rm{.}}\\,{\\rm{Enzymes}}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,}&{{\\rm{II}}{\\rm{.}}\\,{\\rm{Vector}}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,}\\\\\n{\\begin{array}{*{20}{l}}\n{{\\rm{III}}{\\rm{.}}\\,{\\rm{Host}}\\,{\\rm{organism}}}\n\\end{array}}&{\\begin{array}{*{20}{l}}\n{{\\rm{IV}}{\\rm{.}}\\,{\\rm{Donor}}\\,{\\rm{DNA}}}\n\\end{array}}\n\\end{array}$<br>Select the correct option.','[{"text": "I, II and III", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "I, III and IV", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "I, II, and IV", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "I, II, III and IV", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Key tools for recombinant DNA technology: Restriction endonuclease enzyme and DNA ligases for recombinant DNA production. Vectors that help in carrying and integrating the desired gene. Host organism is that one in which recombinant DNA is introduced. Polymerase enzymes.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49279,'biology',97,1193,2854,'Select the correct answer<br>I. In the year 1968, the two enzymes responsible for restricting the growth of bacteriophage in <i>Escherichia coli</i> were isolated.<br>II. One of these added ethyl groups to DNA, while the other cut DNA.','[{"text": "Only I is correct", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Only II is correct", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both are correct", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "None are correct", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'In the year 1963, the two enzymes responsible for restricting the growth of bacteriophage in Escherichia coli were isolated. One of these added methyl groups to DNA, while the other cut DNA.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49280,'biology',97,1193,2854,'The enzymes responsible for restricting the growth of bacteriophage in \'<em>Escherichia coli</em>\' is the one that','[{"text": "Cuts DNA in a particular fashion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Adds methyl group to protein.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both (1) and (2)", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Adds formyl group to DNA.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'The enzymes responsible for restricting the growth of bacteriophage in \'Escherichia coli\' cut DNA in a particular fashion.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49281,'biology',97,1193,2854,'The first restriction endonuclease type II,____ was isolated from ____ bacterium.','[{"text": "Eco RI <i>Escherichia RY13</i>", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Eco RII <i>Escherichia coli</i>", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Hind II <i>Haemophilus influenzae</i>", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Bam HI <i>Bacillus amyloliquefaciens</i>", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'The first restriction endonuclease type II, is Hind II was isolated from <i>Haemophilus influenza</i> bacterium.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49282,'biology',97,1193,2854,'The restriction enzyme isolated and characterised five years later to 1963 , responsible for the cleavage of following sequence consisting of six base pairs is<br>5\'-GTCGAC 3\'<br>3\'- CAGCTG 5\'','[{"text": "Pst I", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Bam HI", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Hind II", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Eco RI", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'The restriction enzyme responsible for the cleavage of the given sequence is Hind II.<br>$\\begin{gathered}\\downarrow \\\\5^{\\prime}-G T C \\quad G A C-3^{\\prime} \\\\3^{\\prime}-C A G \\quad C T G-5^{\\prime} \\\\\\uparrow\\end{gathered}$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49285,'biology',97,1193,2854,'In restriction endonuclease called EcoRI, what does \' $R$ \' part stands for?','[{"text": "Name of the strain", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Name of the genus", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Name of the species", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Name of the class", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'EcoRI is a restriction endonuclease enzyme where \'Eco\' stands for the genus and species name of the organism it is isolated from i.e Escherichia coli. The R represents the particular strain, in this case RY13. The last part of its name, the \'I\', represents the order of discovery.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49286,'biology',97,1193,2854,'The roman number following the name of restriction enzyme indicate','[{"text": "Number assigned based on its function", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Number of enzyme", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Order of enzyme", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Order in which enzyme is isolated from strain of bacteria", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'The Roman number following the name of restriction enzyme indicate the order in which enzyme is isolated from strain of bacteria.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49287,'biology',97,1193,2854,'Match the columns I and II.<div class="matching-question"><div class="column-left">A. Stanley Cohen and Herbert Boyer<br>B. Two enzymes responsible for restricting the growth of bacteriophage<br>C. Hind II<br>D. EcoRI</div><div class="column-right">P. 1968<br>Q. RY 13<br>R. 1963<br>S. 1972</div></div>','[{"text": "$\\\\mathrm{A}-\\\\mathrm{R}, \\\\mathrm{B}-\\\\mathrm{P}, \\\\mathrm{C}-\\\\mathrm{Q}, \\\\mathrm{D}-\\\\mathrm{S}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{A}-\\\\mathrm{S}, \\\\mathrm{B}-\\\\mathrm{R}, \\\\mathrm{C}-\\\\mathrm{P}, \\\\mathrm{D}-\\\\mathrm{Q}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{A}-\\\\mathrm{P}, \\\\mathrm{B}-\\\\mathrm{R}, \\\\mathrm{C}-\\\\mathrm{Q}, \\\\mathrm{D}-\\\\mathrm{S}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{A}-\\\\mathrm{Q}, \\\\mathrm{B}-\\\\mathrm{P}, \\\\mathrm{C}-\\\\mathrm{R}, \\\\mathrm{D}-\\\\mathrm{S}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Stanley Cohen and Herbert Boyer-1972. Two enzymes responsible for restricting the growth of bactbacteriophage-1963 Hind II -1968 EcoRI-RY-13.',NULL,'Easy','Match the following',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49288,'biology',97,1193,2854,'Restriction endonuclease is also known as','[{"text": "Molecular sewing machine", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Molecular scissors", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "DNA repairer", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Molecular glue", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Restriction endonuclease is also called as molecular scissors as they cleave DNA at or near specific recognition sites.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49289,'biology',97,1193,2854,'Restriction enzyme belongs to which class of enzymes?','[{"text": "Ligases", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Hydrolases", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Nucleases", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Proteases", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Restriction enzymes belong to a larger class of enzymes called nucleases, which are of two kinds, i.e., exonucleases and endonucleases.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49290,'biology',97,1193,2854,'Select the correct answer<br>i. Exonucleases remove nucleotides from the ends of the DNA<br>ii. Endonucleases make cuts at specific positions within the DNA.','[{"text": "Only i is correct", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Only ii is correct", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both are correct", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "None are correct", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'i. Exonucleases remove nucleotides from the ends of the DNA.<br>ii. Endonucleases make cuts at specific positions within the DNA.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49291,'biology',97,1193,2854,'In a genetic engineering experiment, restriction enzymes can be used for','[{"text": "Prokaryotic DNA only", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Viral DNA only", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Any DNA fragment", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Eukaryotic DNA only", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Restriction enzymes, also called restriction endonucleases, bind to DNA and cleave the double strand, forming smaller pieces of DNA. There are three types of restriction enzymes; Type I restriction enzymes recognize a DNA sequence and cut the strand randomly more than one thousand base pairs away from the site. Type II restriction enzymes, the most useful for molecular biology laboratories, recognize and cut the DNA strand predictably at a specific sequence which is usually less than ten base pairs long. Type III restriction enzymes are similar to Type I, but these cut the DNA about thirty base pairs from the recognition sequence. So the correct option is \'any DNA fragment\'.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49292,'biology',97,1193,2854,'Restriction endonuclease enzymes are used to cut ____','[{"text": "Single-stranded RNA at specific sites", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Double-stranded DNA at specific sites", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Single-stranded DNA at any random sites", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Double-stranded DNA at any random sites", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Restriction endonucleases enzymes cut double-stranded DNA molecules at specific sites called recognition sites which have specific base sequences.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49293,'biology',97,1193,2854,'Restriction endonuclease binds to DNA and cuts two strands of double helix at specific points in their','[{"text": "Glycoside bonds", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Hydrogen bond", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Sugar-phosphate backbone", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "N-glycosidic bonds", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Restriction endonucleases recognise their specific sequence and bind to the DNA and cut each of the two strands of the double helix at specific points in their sugar-phosphate backbone.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49294,'biology',97,1193,2854,'Restriction endonucleases are enzymes which','[{"text": "Breaks the hydrogen bonds at any position within the DNA molecule", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Recognise a specific nucleotide sequence bind to it and then cleaves both the strands of DNA", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Stops replication of DNA", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Remove nucleotides from the ends of the DNA molecule", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Restriction endonucleases recognise a specific DNA base sequence (recognition sequence, recognition site), bind to the DNA and then cleave both the strands of the DNA at those specific points.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49295,'biology',97,1193,2854,'The restriction endonuclease recognises a specific ____in the DNA.','[{"text": "Replication nucleotide sequence", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Palindromic nucleotide sequence", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "ORI", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "None of the above", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Each restriction endonuclease recognises a specific palindromic nucleotide sequence in the DNA.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49296,'biology',97,1193,2854,'The specific sequence recognized by "molecular scissors" is called','[{"text": "Isobar", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Isomer", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Palindrome", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Misnomer", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'The specific sequence recognized by "molecular scissors" is called Palindrome.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49297,'biology',97,1193,2854,'Palindrome sequences','[{"text": "Read same on two strands when orientation of reading is different", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Read different on two strands when orientation of reading is same", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Read same on two strands when orientation of reading is same", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Read opposite on two strands when orientation of reading is same", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'The palindrome in DNA is a sequence of base pairs that reads same on the two strands when orientation of reading is kept the same.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49298,'biology',97,1193,2854,'Restriction enzyme cuts the DNA strand a little away from the centre of palindrome site between','[{"text": "Same two bases on same strand", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Opposite bases on same strand", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Same two bases on opposite strand", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Opposite bases on opposite strand", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Restriction enzymes cut DNA strands a little away from the centre of the palindrome site between the same two bases on the opposite strands.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49299,'biology',97,1193,2854,'The foreign DNA and the vector are cut with the','[{"text": "Two different enzymes", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Same restriction enzymes", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both 1 and 2", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "It could be same or different", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Same restriction enzymes are used to cut both the foreign DNA and the vector, so that the resultant DNA fragments have the complementary kind of sticky ends.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49300,'biology',97,1193,2854,'The sticky ends of a fragmented DNA molecule are made up of','[{"text": "Unpaired bases", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Paired bases", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Methyl group", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Endonuclease", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Sticky ends of DNA are also called as staggerred end, it is also called as cohesive end. Because they have unpaired bases, it can hybridize on any other complementary DNA bases.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49301,'biology',97,1193,2854,'The linking of foreign gene with the plasmid DNA is possible with the enzyme _____ and is able to form ____ bonds.','[{"text": "Exonuclease, phospho-diester", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "DNA ligase, N-glycosidic", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "DNA ligase, phospho-diester", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "DNA Polymerase, phospho-diester", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'DNA ligase is a type of enzyme that joins DNA strands together by formation of Phosphodiester bond.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49302,'biology',97,1193,2854,'Select the correct answer<br>I. Restriction enzymes cut the strand of DNA a little away from the end of the palindrome sites, but between the same two bases on the opposite strands.<br>II. The overhanging stretches are called sticky ends on each strand','[{"text": "Only I is correct", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Only II is correct", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both are correct", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "None are correct", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Restriction enzymes cut the strand of DNA a little away from the centre of the palindrome sites, but between the same two bases on the opposite strands.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49303,'biology',97,1193,2854,'Which of the following restriction endonuclease is obtained from <em>E.coli</em>?','[{"text": "ECOrI", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "EcoRI", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "ECORi", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "ECORI", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'EcoRI is a restriction endonuclease isolated from the species <em>E.coli</em>.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49304,'biology',97,1193,2854,'Specific DNA sequence GAATTC is cut by which of the following restriction endonuclease','[{"text": "Hind II", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Hind III", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "<i>Eco</i> RII", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "<i>Eco</i> RI", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'EcoRI is the restriction enzyme, derived from Escherichia coli, strain R and is the first to be isolated. EcoRI recognizes and cuts at the specific restriction site 5\'-GAATTC-3\' and produces sticky ends. This is the common enzyme that is used as a tool for the restriction of the bacterial plasmid and desired genes in molecular biology techniques.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49305,'biology',97,1193,2854,'The specific DNA sequence where EcoRI cuts is','[{"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'EcoRI recognizes the sequence GAATTC, and cuts both DNA strands between the $\\mathrm{G}$ and the A nucleotides. EcoRI recognition site -<br>5\' GAATTC 3\'<br>3\' CTTAAG 5\'',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49306,'biology',97,1193,2854,'The restriction endonuclease enzyme binds to the DNA & cut :-','[{"text": "Any one strand of the double helix", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Each of the two strands at specific points in their base - sugar bonds.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Each of the two strands at specific points in their base - phosphate bonds.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Each of the two strands at specific points in their sugar - phosphate back bones", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Restriction enzymes are enzymes that cleave DNA. By recognizing a specific sequence, restriction enzymes cut DNA molecules at a precise location. Each restriction endonuclease examines the length of a DNA sequence. It will connect to the DNA and cut each of the two strands of the double helix at precise spots in its sugar-phosphate backbone after it has found its specific recognition sequence.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49307,'biology',97,1193,2854,'Which of the following option is true regarding Eco RI enzyme?<br>(i) Restriction endonuclease enzyme<br>(ii) Isolated from Escherichia coli RY13<br>(iii) Cuts at specific position within the DNA<br>(iv) GAATTC is its specific palindromic site<br>(v) Obtained from a eukaryotic cell','[{"text": "i, ii", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "i, ii, iii", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "i, ii, iii, iv", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "i, ii, iii, iv, v", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'It is obtained from a prokaryotic cell',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49308,'biology',97,1193,2854,'Number of $\\mathrm{H}$-bonds in the recognition site of EcoRI is','[{"text": "6", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "4", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "14", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "12", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Number of H-bonds in the recognition site of EcoRI is 14. <img src=\'/img/data/biology/97_49308_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' /> Total H-bonds is 14 .',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
