(49434,'biology',97,1197,2859,'Which of the following statement is false about cloning vector.','[{"text": "\'Ori\' is a sequence responsible for controlling the copy number of the linked DNA", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Selectable marker selectively permitting the growth of the transformants", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "In order to link the DNA, the vector needs to have single recognition site for the commonly used restriction enzymes", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Alien DNA is amplified by PCR", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Statement 4 remarks about PCR and not cloning vector.',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49435,'biology',97,1198,2860,'Place the different steps of recombinant DNA technology in its correct order.<br>I. Isolation of a desired DNA fragment<br>II. Amplification of the gene of interest<br>III. Extraction of the desired gene product<br>IV. Ligation of the DNA fragmentation into a vector<br>V. Insertion of recombinant DNA into the host<br>Correct order is','[{"text": "I, II, III, IV and V", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "I, II, IV, V and III", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "II, IV, V, III and I", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "I, IV, V, III and II", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'I, II, IV, V and III',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49436,'biology',97,1193,2855,'Which among the given statements are correct<br>I. In elution the separated bands of DNA are cut out from agarose gel and extracted from the gel piece.<br>II. Pure DNA fragments cannot be seen in visible light and without staining<br>III. We can observe orange bands of DNA in a ethidium bromide stained gel exposed to UV light.<br>IV. Pure DNA fragments can be observed in visible light.','[{"text": "I and II", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "I and III", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "I, II and III", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "I, III and IV", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Separated DNA fragments can be visualized only after staining the DNA with Ethidium bromide and exposure to UV light as pure DNA fragments cannot be seen in visible light and without staining.',NULL,'Hard','MCQ type-2',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49437,'biology',97,1194,2856,'Choose the incorrect statement.','[{"text": "pBR322 is a lab constructed plasmid", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "If a foreign DNA ligates at the Bam HI site of tetracycline resistance gene in the vector $\\\\mathrm{pBR} 322$, the recombinant plasmid loses the tetracycline resistance due to insertion of foreign DNA", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Copy number refers to the number of copies of plasmid present in a cell", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Copy number of plasmid varies from 501000 per cell", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Copy number of plasmid varies from 50-1000 per cell is incorrect statement.',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49438,'biology',97,1195,2857,'Which of the statements given is incorrect?','[{"text": "In microinjection method, foreign DNA is directly injected into the nucleus of animal cell", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "<em>Agrobacterium tumefaciens</em> is a natural genetic engineer", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Electroporation is the formation of temporary pores in the plasma membrane of host cell by using lysozyme or calcium chloride", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Restriction enzymes belong to a class of enzymes called nucleases.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Electroporation is the formation of temporary pores in the plasma membrane of host cell by using lysozyme or calcium chloride, 2 is incorrect statement.',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49439,'biology',97,1194,2856,'Which statement is incorrect?<br>I. Ethidium bromide can not be used for staining DNA<br>II. Bacteriophage is not used as vector<br>III. Pure DNA fragments in agarose gel can be seen by naked eyes in UV light & without staining','[{"text": "II only", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "I, II only", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "I, II, III", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "III only", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Incorrect statement - I, II, III',NULL,'Hard','MCQ type-2',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49440,'biology',97,1195,2857,'Read the following statements carefully and select the correct option :-<br>I. Transformation is a procedure through which a piece of DNA is introduced in a host bacterium<br>II. In biolistic method of gene transfer, microparticles made up of gold or tungsten are coated with foreign DNA<br>III. Micro-injection method for injecting recombinant DNA is used for animal cell. <br>IV. Primers are chemically synthesized oligonucleotides that are complementary to the regions of DNA in PCR<br>How many of the above statements are correct?','[{"text": "Four", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Three", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Two", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "One", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'All the statements are correct.',NULL,'Hard','MCQ type-2',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49441,'biology',97,1195,2857,'Consider the following statements<br>I. Source of the restriction enzyme Hind III is <em>Helicobacter influenzae</em><br>II. DNA is a hydrophilic molecule<br>III. Separated DNA fragments are visualized after staining the DNA with ethanol and using UV light<br>IV. Presence of more than one same recognition sites within vector will generate several fragments of vector, which will complicate gene cloning Which of the above statement are correct?','[{"text": "I, II &amp; III", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "II, III &amp; IV", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "II &amp; IV", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "I, II, &amp; IV", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Correct statement are II &amp; IV',NULL,'Hard','MCQ type-2',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49442,'biology',97,1192,2853,'What is true about the first case of constructing a recombinant DNA-<br>I. It was performed in 1970<br>II. It was performed by Stanley Boyer and Herbert Cohen<br>III. It was a transfer of an antibiotic resistant gene<br>IV. Gene was transferred from <em>Salmonella typhimurium</em> into <em>E.coli</em><br>V. gene could not replicate successfully in the target bacterium','[{"text": "I and V", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "I, II and III", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "III and IV", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "III, IV and V", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'True about the first case of constructing a recombinant DNA-<br>iii. It was a transfer of an antibiotic resistant gene<br>iv. Gene was transferred from <em>Salmonella typhimurium</em> into <em>E.coli</em>.',NULL,'Hard','MCQ type-2',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49443,'biology',97,1197,2859,'Read the following statements regarding DNA polymerase used in PCR :-<br>I. It is isolated from archaebacteria<br>II. It remains active at high temperature<br>III. It is used to ligate introduced DNA in recipient cells.<br>IV. It serves as a selectable marker<br>Find the statements which are correct ?','[{"text": "I, II", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "I, II, III", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "All are correct", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "All are wrong", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'DNA Polymerase used in PCR is Taq DNA Polymerase. It is extracted from thermophilic bacterium <i>Thermus aquaticus</i>. Optimum Temperature for its functioning is $72^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C}$. Taq DNA Polymerase will synthesize DNA between the primers in the process of PCR.',NULL,'Hard','MCQ type-2',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49444,'biology',97,1195,2857,'Eukaryotic genes may not function properly when cloned into bacteria because of','[{"text": "Destruction by native endonucleases.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Inability to excise introns.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Failure of promoter to be recognized by bacterial RNA polymerase.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "All of the above.", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Eukaryotic genes may not function properly when cloned into bacteria because of<br>(1) Destruction by native endonucleases.<br>(2) Inability to excise introns.<br>(3) Failure of promoter to be recognized by bacterial RNA polymerase.',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49445,'biology',97,1193,2854,'Which of the following statement is incorrect?','[{"text": "Each restriction endonuclease recognises a specific palindromic nucleotide sequence", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Specific base sequence is known as recognition sequence", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Restriction enzymes are made up of nucleotides", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Restriction enzymes belong to enzymes called nucleases", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Restriction enzymes are made up of nucleotides is incorrect statement.',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49446,'biology',97,1194,2856,'Normally, the gene encoding resistance to antibiotics are considered useful selectable marker for <em>E. coli</em>','[{"text": "Because the normal <em>E.coli</em> cells do not carry resistance against any of these antibiotics", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "As it makes <em>E.coli</em> identifiable because of insertional inactivation", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Because it makes <em>E.coli </em>identification through insertional activation", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both (1) and (2)", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Normally, the gene encoding resistance to antibiotics are considered useful selectable marker for <em>E. coli</em><br>(1) Because the normal <em>E.coli</em> cells do not carry resistance against any of these antibiotics<br>(2) As it makes <em>E.coli</em> identifiable because of insertional inactivation.',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49447,'biology',97,1195,2857,'Select the wrong statement.','[{"text": "DNA is a negatively charged molecule", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "The presence of chromogenic substrate gives blue colour colonies, if the plasmid in the bacteria does not have an insert.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Since DNA is a hydrophilic molecule it cannot pass through cell membranes.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "In microinjection, cells are bombarded with high velocity microparticles of gold or tungsten coated with DNA.", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'In microinjection, cells are bombarded with high velocity microparticles of gold or tungsten coated with DNA is wrong statement.',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49448,'biology',97,1199,2861,'Which statement is correct?<br>I. A large scale production of desired biotechnological product is done by using bioreactors<br>II. Downstream processing is one of the steps of R-DNA technology.<br>III. Bioreactors are vessels of large volumes in which raw materials are biologically converted into specific products.<br>IV. The presence of chromogenic substrate gives blue colour colonies, if the plasmid in the bacteria does not have an insert.','[{"text": "I and II", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "I, III and IV", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "I and III", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "None of these", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'I, III and IV are correct statement.',NULL,'Hard','MCQ type-2',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49449,'biology',97,1194,2856,'If we ligate a foreign DNA at the BamHI site in pBR322, the recombinant plasmid will :-','[{"text": "Show ampicillin resistance only.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Show tetracycline resistance.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Grow well on tetracycline containing medium.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Not grow on ampicillin containing medium", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Plasmid is an extra chromosomal circular, self replicating, double stranded DNA, found generally in bacteria. Presence of plasmid gives certain traits to the host, one such trait is resistance to certain toxins and antibiotics. pBR322 is the first artificial cloning vector. It has 2 antibiotic resistance genes, Tetracycline (tet $\\mathrm{R}$ ) and ampicillin (ampR). When BamHI is used, DNA insert or ligated DNA will be placed within tet $\\mathrm{R}$ gene making it non functional. Hence when foreign DNA is ligated BamHI in pBR322, recombinant plasmid will show resistance to ampicillin only.',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49450,'biology',97,1195,2857,'Consider the following statements and select the correct option.','[{"text": "DNA being a hydrophilic molecule cannot pass through cell membranes", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "The T-DNA causes tumours", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Tumour formation is induced by $\\\\mathrm{Ti}$ plasmid", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "All of the above", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Correct statements are-<br>(1) DNA being a hydrophilic molecule cannot pass through cell membranes<br>(2) The T-DNA causes tumours<br>(3) Tumour formation is induced by Ti plasmid',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49451,'biology',97,1193,2855,'Consider the given statements w.r.t. gel electrophoresis.<br>I. DNA can be separated by forcing them to move towards anode under an electric field.<br>II. DNA fragments separate according to their size through sieving effect provided by agarose gel.<br>III. The used matrix in this technique is agarose which is an artificial polymer.<br>Which of the above statements is/ are correct?','[{"text": "II only", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "I and II", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "I, II, and III", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "II and III", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'w.r.t. gel electrophoresis.correct statements are-I. DNA can be separated by forcing them to move towards anode under an electric field.<br>II. DNA fragments separate according to their size through sieving effect provided by agarose gel.',NULL,'Hard','MCQ type-2',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49452,'biology',97,1194,2856,'What happens, when DNA fragments is inserted into plasmid (pBR-322) using enzyme Pvu-I or Pst-I ?<br>i. Inactivation of ampR gene<br>ii. Inactivation of tetR gene<br>iii. Unable to grow in the presence of ampicillin<br>iv. Unable to grow in the presence of tetracycline<br>v. Unable to replicate<br>Mark the correct choice :-','[{"text": "ii and iv are correct", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "i and iii are correct", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "i, iii and $v$ are correct", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "ii, iv and v are correct", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'i and iii are correct',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49453,'biology',97,1197,2859,'In PCR, short primers are added to singlestranded DNA molecules in a test tube and the appropriateenzymes are included to makeacopy of the DNA. Which of the following primers is needed for copying the single-stranded DNA sequence 5\' TACGGTAGGTC3\'?','[{"text": "5\'GACCT", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "5\'ATGCC", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "5\'GGCAT", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "5\'TACGG", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'5\'GACCT',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49454,'biology',97,1193,2854,'Which of the following statement is not true.','[{"text": "Hind - II always cut DNA molecules at a particular point by recognizing a specific sequence of 4 base pairs.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Besides Hind - II, today we know more than 900 restriction, enzymes.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "The name Eco RI comes from <em>Escherichia coli</em> -13 .", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Type - II restriction endonuclease is most useful in genetic engineering", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Hind II is the first identified restriction endonuclease Hind II will cut DNA molecule at particular point by recognizing specific sequence of six base pairs.',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49455,'biology',97,1193,2855,'A mixture containing DNA fragments $A, B, C$, and $\\mathrm{D}$ with molecular weights, $\\mathrm{A}+\\mathrm{B}=\\mathrm{C}, \\mathrm{A}>\\mathrm{B}$ and $\\mathrm{D}>\\mathrm{C}$, was subjected to agarose gel electrophoresis. The position of these fragments from anode to cathode sides of gel should be:','[{"text": "A, B, C, D", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "B, A, C, D", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "B, A, D, C", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "C, D, A, B", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Shorter lengths of DNA move faster than longer lengths so move further in the time the current is run. So, since D has the heaviest weight (implying it has the longest strand), it will tend to remain far from the anode. B being a lightest fragment moves farthest from the cathode. Hence The positions of these fragments from cathode to anode sides of the gel would be D, C, A, B.',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49456,'biology',97,1196,2858,'Read the statements<br>I. When cut by the same restriction enzyme, the resultant DNA fragments have the same kind of sticky ends and these can be join together using DNA ligases.<br>II. The bacterial/plant, animal cell is broken with RNA, proteins, polysaccharides and lipids','[{"text": "I is true, but II is false", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "I is false, but II is true", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "I and II are true", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "I and II are false", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'I and II are true.',NULL,'Hard','MCQ type-2',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49457,'biology',97,1198,2860,'Choose correct statement','[{"text": "If a protein encoding gene is expressed in a heterologous host it is called<br>Recombinant protein", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Ligation of alien DNA is carried out at a restriction site present in Ori", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "In pBR322 one antibiotic resistance gene helps in selecting the transformants, whereas the other helps in cloning", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Rop codes for proteins involved in ligation of foreign DNA", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Correct statement is If a protein encoding gene is expressed in a heterologous host it is called Recombinant protein.',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49458,'biology',97,1199,2861,'Read the following four statements (I-IV) and select the incorrect ones.<br>I. Downstream processing is one of the steps of rDNA technology.<br>II. Disarmed pathogen vectors are also used in transfer of rDNA into the host.<br>III. In genetic engineering, DNA from different sources are cut with the same restriction enzymes so that both DNA fragments have same kind of sticky ends<br>IV. Restriction enzymes are used in isolation of DNA from other macromolecules.<br>Which of the two statements have mistakes?','[{"text": "II and III", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "IV only", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "I and III", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "I and II", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Restriction enzymes are used to break DNA molecule.',NULL,'Hard','MCQ type-2',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49459,'biology',97,1196,2858,'Which of the following statement is incorrect?','[{"text": "Nucleic acid is fragmented by nucleases", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Construction of recombinant DNA involves cleaving DNA segments with endonuclease and rejoining with ligase.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Genetic engineering is making artificial limbs and diagnostic instruments", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Ti plasmid transforms cells of plants", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Genetic engineering is making artificial limbs and diagnostic instruments is incorrect statement.',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49460,'biology',97,1195,2857,'Read the statements about biolistic technique<br>I. This method is also known as gene gun method.<br>II. In this method, cells are bombarded with high velocity microparticles of gold or tungsten coated with DNA in plants.<br>III. In this method, cells are bombarded with high velocity microparticles of DNA with gold or tungsten coated in plants.<br>Which of the statements given above are correct?','[{"text": "I and II", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "I and III", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "II and III", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "I, II and III", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'About biolistic technique correct statements are<br>I. This method is also known as gene gun method.<br>II. In this method, cells are bombarded with high velocity microparticles of gold or tungsten coated with DNA in plants.',NULL,'Hard','MCQ type-2',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49461,'biology',97,1196,2858,'Which statement is correct?<br>I. Genes encoding resistance to antibiotics like ampicillin, chloramphenicol, tetracycline or kanamycin, are useful selectable markers for <em>E.coli.</em><br>II. Selectable markers helps to select the host cells which contain the vector and eliminate the non-transformants.<br>III. Large amount of recombinant protein can be produced by gene cloning.<br>IV. In the process of recombinant DNA technology, after several treatment the purified DNA is precipitated by adding chilled acetone.','[{"text": "I, II and III", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Only IV", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "II, III and IV", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "All of these", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Correct statements are<br>I. Genes encoding resistance to antibiotics like ampicillin, chloramphenicol, tetracycline or kanamycin, are useful selectable markers for <em>E.coli</em>.<br>II. Selectable markers helps to select the host cells which contain the vector and eliminate the non-transformants.<br>III. Large amount of recombinant protein can be produced by gene cloning.',NULL,'Hard','MCQ type-2',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49462,'biology',97,1193,2854,'Which of the following enzymes catalyse the removal of nucleotides from the ends of DNA?','[{"text": "Endonuclease", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Exonuclease", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "DNA ligase", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Hind - II", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Exonucleases remove nucleotides from the ends of DNA',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,'NCERT Exemplar','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL);
INSERT INTO `qb_questions` (`id`, `subject_id`, `chapter_id`, `topic_id`, `subtopic_id`, `question_html`, `options`, `correct_index`, `explanation`, `explanation_image_url`, `difficulty`, `qtype`, `year`, `tag`, `created_at`, `question_image_url`) VALUES
(49463,'biology',97,1193,2855,'Which of the given statements is correct in the context of visualizing DNA molecules separated by agarose gel electrophoresis?','[{"text": "DNA can be seen in visible light", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "DNA can be seen without staining in visible light", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Ethidium bromide stained DNA can be seen in visible light", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Ethidium bromide stained DNA can be seen under exposure to UV light", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'To visualize DNA, it must be stained with Ethidium bromide. In the presence of ultraviolet light, Ethidium bromide exhibits fluorescence.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,'NCERT Exemplar','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49465,'biology',97,1194,2856,'The most important feature in a plasmid to serve as a vector in gene cloning experiment is:','[{"text": "Origin of replication (ori)", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Presence of a selectable marker", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Presence of sites for restriction endonuclease", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Its size", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'In a plasmid DNA, Origin of replication is a sequence from where replication starts.<br>Any piece of DNA can be made to replicate, when linked to this sequence. Hence to get many copies of target DNA, it should be cloned in a vector at ori site.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,'NCERT Exemplar','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49466,'biology',97,1197,2859,'Which of the following contributed in popularising the PCR (polymerase chain reactions) technique?','[{"text": "Easy availability of DNA template", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Availability of synthetic primers", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Availability of cheapdeoxyribonucleotides", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Availability of \'Thermostable\' DNA polymerase", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Thermostable DNA polymerase is isolated from a bacterium <i>Thermus aquaticus</i> popularises PCR, because this enzyme remains active during high temperature.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,'NCERT Exemplar','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49467,'biology',97,1195,2857,'Significance of \'heat shock\' method in bacterial transformation is to facilitate:','[{"text": "Binding of DNA to the cell wall", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Uptake of DNA through membrane transport proteins", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Uptake of DNA through transient pores in the bacterial cell wall", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Expression of antibiotic resistance gene", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Heat shock transformation alters membrane fluidity creating pores, in the plasma membrane of bacteria and allows for plasmid DNA to enter the bacterial cell',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,'NCERT Exemplar','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49468,'biology',97,1193,2854,'Which of the following bacteria is not a source of restriction endonuclease?','[{"text": "<i>Haemophilus influenzae</i>", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "<i>Escherichia coli</i>", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "<i>Entamoeba coli</i>", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "<i>Bacillus amyloliquefaciens</i>", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Entamoeba <i>coli</i> is a protozoan and not a bacterium, hence it is not a source of restriction endonuclease.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,'NCERT Exemplar','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49469,'biology',97,1195,2857,'A bacterial cell was transformed with a recombinant DNA molecule that was generated using a human gene. However, the transformed cells did not produce the desired protein. Reasons could be:','[{"text": "Human gene may have intron which bacteria cannot process", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Amino acid codons for humans and bacteria are different", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Human protein is formed but degraded by bacteria", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "All of the above", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Any nucleotide sequence in a gene that is removed by RNA splicing during maturation of final RNA product is called intron. Intron can be different in different organisms.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,'NCERT Exemplar','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49470,'biology',97,1192,2853,'Rising of dough is due to:','[{"text": "Multiplication of yeast", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Production of $\\\\mathrm{CO}_{2}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Emulsification", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Hydrolysis of wheat flour starch into sugars.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Rising of dough is due to the production of $\\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ and this leads to create bubbles in the dough.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,'NCERT Exemplar','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49471,'biology',97,1194,2856,'The transfer of genetic material from one bacterium to another through the mediation of a viral vector is termed as:','[{"text": "Transduction", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Conjugation", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Transformation", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Translation", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Transduction is the process of transfer of genetic material from one bacterium to another through the mediation of viral vector.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,'NCERT Exemplar','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49472,'biology',97,1193,2854,'\'Restriction\' in Restriction enzyme refers to:','[{"text": "Cleaving of phosphodiester bond in DNA by the enzyme", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Cutting of DNA at specific position only", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Prevention of the multiplication of bacteriophage by the host bacteria", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "All of the above", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'"Restriction" stands for restricting the multiplication of bacteriophage by the host bacteria.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,'NCERT Exemplar','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49473,'biology',97,1193,2855,'In agarose gel electrophoresis, DNA molecules are separated on the basis of their:','[{"text": "Charge only", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Size only", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Charge to size ratio", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "All of the above", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'The differential mobility of DNA depends upon charge & size of DNA.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,'NCERT Exemplar','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49474,'biology',97,1196,2858,'While isolating DNA from bacteria, which of the following enzymes is not required?','[{"text": "Lysozyme", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Ribonuclease", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Deoxyribonuclease", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Protease", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Deoxyribonuclease is not required because this enzyme catalyses the hydrolytic cleavage of phosphodiester linkages in the DNA backbone, thus it degrades DNA.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,'NCERT Exemplar','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49475,'biology',97,1194,2856,'An antibiotic resistance gene in a vector usually helps in the selection of:','[{"text": "Competent bacterial cells", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Transformed bacterial cells", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Recombinant bacterial cells", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "None of the above", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'As a result only bacteria containing a plasmid with antibiotic resistance will grow in the presence of antibiotics. This gene helps as a selection marker.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,'NCERT Exemplar','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49476,'biology',97,1193,2854,'The role of DNA ligase in the construction of a recombinant DNA molecule is:','[{"text": "Formation of phosphodiester bond between two DNA fragments", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Formation of hydrogen bonds between sticky ends of DNA fragments", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Ligation of all purine and pyrimidine bases", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "None of the above", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'DNA ligase facilitates the formation of phosphodiester bond between two sticky ends of DNA fragments.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,'NCERT Exemplar','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49477,'biology',97,1197,2859,'Which of the following steps are catalysed by Taq DNA polymerase in a PCR reaction?','[{"text": "Denaturation of template DNA", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Annealing of primers to template DNA", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Extension of primer end on the template DNA", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "All of the above", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Extension of primer end on the template DNA',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,'NCERT Exemplar','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49478,'biology',97,1198,2860,'Which of the following should be chosen for best yield if one were to produce a recombinant protein in large amounts?','[{"text": "Laboratory flask of largest capacity", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "A stirred-tank bioreactor without inlets and outlets", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "A continuous culture system", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Any of the above", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Continuous culture system is needed to produce large quantity of recombinant proteins.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,'NCERT Exemplar','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49479,'biology',97,1193,2854,'Which of the following statements does not hold true for restriction enzyme?','[{"text": "It recognises a palindromic nucleotide sequence", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "It is an endonuclease", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "It is isolated from viruses", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "It can produce the same kind of sticky ends in different DNA molecules", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Restriction enzymes are not isolated from viruses.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,'NCERT Exemplar','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49480,'biology',97,1193,2854,'<div class="matching-question"><div class="column-left">Restriction endonucleases are also called as "molecular scissors"<br></div><div class="column-right">When fragments generated by restriction endonuclease are mixed they join together due to sticky ends by addition of DNA ligase</div></div>','[{"text": "Statement A is correct but Statement B is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Statement A is incorrect but Statement B is correct.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both statements are correct.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Statements are incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Statement-A : Restriction endonucleases are also called as "molecular scissors"<br>Statement-B : When fragments generated by Restriction endonuclease are mixed they join together due to sticky ends by addition of DNA ligase .Both are correct statement.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ type-2',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49481,'biology',97,1194,2856,'<div class="matching-question"><div class="column-left"><i>Agrobacterium tumefaciens</i> is popular in genetic engineering because this bacterium is associated with roots of cereal and pulse crops.<br></div><div class="column-right">Retroviruses in animals including humans are able to change normal cells into cancerous cells</div></div>','[{"text": "Statement A is correct but Statement B is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Statement A is incorrect but Statement B is correct.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both statements are correct.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Statements are incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Agrobacterium tumefaciens is a rod-shaped gram-negative soil bacterium. This is used extensively in genetic engineering because of its ability to transfer DNA between itself and the plants. A.tumefaciens naturally infect the wound sites in dicotyledons and cause crown gall tumours. These tumours are incited by the conjugative transfer of a DNA segment (T-DNA) from the bacterial tumor-inducing (Ti) plasmid.<br>Statement B is correct.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ type-2',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49482,'biology',97,1194,2856,'<div class="matching-question"><div class="column-left">pB322 is a plasmid vector.<br></div><div class="column-right">A plasmid is an extrachromosomal genetic elements of DNA that is capable of replicating independently of the host chromosome.</div></div>','[{"text": "Statement A is correct but Statement B is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Statement A is incorrect but Statement B is correct.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both statements are correct.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Statements are incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Plasmid is extrachromosomal genetic material in bacteria, which is found in cytoplasm and has autonomous replicating property. It can be integrated into bacterial chromosome and can replicate with that. It can also be used for transfer of genetic material from one cell to other.<br>So, the both statements are correct.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ type-2',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49483,'biology',97,1193,2855,'<div class="matching-question"><div class="column-left">Ethidium bromide (EtBr) under UV radiation gives bright red coloured bands.<br></div><div class="column-right">The cutting of DNA by restriction endonuclease resultsinthe fragmentsofDNA.</div></div>','[{"text": "Statement A is correct but Statement B is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Statement A is incorrect but Statement B is correct.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both statements are correct.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Statements are incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'To visualise DNA in the agarose gel, a positively charged dye ethidium bromide $(\\mathrm{EtBr})$ is added in the agarose gel or electrophoresis buffer.<br>This dye intercalates itself into the DNA backbone and gives orange fluorescence upon exposure to the UV light.<br>Thus, DNA is visualised by exposure to UV light as orange coloured bands on the agarose gel.<br>Statement B is correct.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ type-2',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49484,'biology',97,1194,2856,'<div class="matching-question"><div class="column-left">Recognition site should be preferably single and responsive to commonly used restriction enzyme.<br></div><div class="column-right">In pBR322 alien DNA is ligated generally in the area of <i>Bam</i>-HI site of ampicillin resistance gene.</div></div>','[{"text": "Statement A is correct but Statement B is incorrect.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Statement A is incorrect but Statement B is correct.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both statements are correct.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Statements are incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Recognition sites should be preferably single for given restriction enzymes so that the commonly used restriction enzyme can cut at a specific region only.<br>In pBR322, generally, the alien DNA of the gene of interest is introduced into the BamHI site.<br>Since it is in the region of the tetracycline gene, ampicillin is used as the only selectable marker.<br>Statement A is correct.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ type-2',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49485,'biology',97,1193,2855,'<div class="matching-question"><div class="column-left">Restriction enzymes recognize palindromic sequence.<br></div><div class="column-right">DNA is negatively charged, so it moves towards anode under electric field</div></div>','[{"text": "Statement A is correct but Statement B is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Statement A is incorrect but Statement B is correct.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both statements are correct.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Statements are incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'The palindrome in DNA is a sequence of base pairs that reads same on the two strands when orientation of reading is kept same. Restriction enzymes cut the strand of DNA a little away from the centre of the palindrome sites, but between the same two bases on opposite strands.<br>Both statements are correct.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ type-2',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49590,'biology',97,1194,2856,'An antibiotic resistance gene in a vector usually helps in the selection of<br>','[{"text": "Non-competent cells", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Competent cells", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Non-recombinant cells", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Transformed cells", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'An antibiotic resistance gene in a vector usually helps in the selection of transformed cell.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2022,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49486,'biology',97,1198,2860,'<div class="matching-question"><div class="column-left">Purified DNA ultimately precipitates out after the addition of chilled ethanol which can be seen as collection of fine threads in the suspension<br></div><div class="column-right">A bioreactor has cultures large volume of living cells</div></div>','[{"text": "Statement A is correct but Statement B is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Statement A is incorrect but Statement B is correct.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both statements are correct.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Statements are incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'At the last step of DNA extraction, purified DNA precipitates out after the addition of chilled ethanol. This DNA appears as collection of fine threads in the suspension which can be spool over the glass rod, this method is called as DNA spooling.<br>A bioreactor has cultures large volume of living cells. So,both statements are correct.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ type-2',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49487,'biology',97,1194,2856,'<div class="matching-question"><div class="column-left">In recombinant DNA technology, human genes are often transferred into bacteria or yeast.<br></div><div class="column-right">Both bacteria and yeast multiply very fast to form huge population which express the required gene.</div></div>','[{"text": "Statement A is correct but Statement B is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Statement A is incorrect but Statement B is correct.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both statements are correct.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Statements are incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Human genes such as insulin gene can be transferred into bacteria and yeast through recombinant DNA technology. These organisms multiply rapidly to form the huge population that expresses the transferred gene because of yeast and E.coli produces a large number of progenies in the short period of time.<br>Both statements are correct.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ type-2',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49488,'biology',97,1193,2854,'<div class="matching-question"><div class="column-left">All endonucleases cut RNA at specific sites<br></div><div class="column-right">Endonucleases are found in viruses</div></div>','[{"text": "Statement A is correct but Statement B is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Statement A is incorrect but Statement B is correct.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both statements are correct.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Statements are incorrect.", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'A palindromic sequence in DNA is one in which the 5\' to 3\' base pair sequence is identical on both strands. Restriction enzymes recognize and cut within specific palindromic sequences, known as restriction sites, in the DNA and produce DNA strands with the sticky ends. It cuts both the strands of DNA at the palindrome site. This is usually a 4- or 6 base pair sequence.<br>Both statements are incorrect.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ type-2',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49489,'biology',97,1193,2854,'<div class="matching-question"><div class="column-left">Restriction enzymes Hind II is obtained from <i>Haemophilus</i><br></div><div class="column-right">The first restriction endonuclease-Hind II</div></div>','[{"text": "Statement A is correct but Statement B is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Statement A is incorrect but Statement B is correct.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both statements are correct.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Statements are incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Hind II was the first discovered restriction endonuclease enzyme. It has been isolated from Haemophilus influenzae. It cuts DNA molecules at a particular point by recognising a specific sequence of six base pairs. This specific sequence is known as the recognition sequence for Hind II.<br>Both statements are correct.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ type-2',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49490,'biology',97,1194,2856,'<div class="matching-question"><div class="column-left"><i>Agrobacterium tumefaciens</i> is a pathogen of several monocot plants.<br></div><div class="column-right">Retroviruses in plants have ability to transform normal cells into cancerous cell.</div></div>','[{"text": "Statement A is correct but Statement B is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Statement A is incorrect but Statement B is correct.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both statements are correct.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Statements are incorrect.", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Statement-A : <i>Agrobacterium tumefaciens</i> is a pathogen of several dicot plants.<br>Statement-B : Retroviruses in animals have ability to transform normal cells into cancerous cell.<br>Both are incorrect.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ type-2',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49491,'biology',97,1194,2856,'<div class="matching-question"><div class="column-left"><i>E.coli</i> having pBR322 with DNA insert at BamHI site cannot grow in medium containing tetracycline.<br></div><div class="column-right">Rop codes for the proteins involved in the replication of the plasmid.</div></div>','[{"text": "Statement A is correct but Statement B is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Statement A is incorrect but Statement B is correct.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both statements are correct.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Statements are incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'pBR322 has recognition sites for several commonly used restriction enzymes.<br>Recognition site for BamHI is present in tetr region i.e., region responsible for tetracycline resistance. When an insert is added at the BamHI recognition site the gene for tetracycline resistance becomes non-functional and the recombinant bacteria with plasmid pBR322 that has DNA insert at BamHI lose tetracycline resistance.<br>Both statements are correct.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ type-2',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49492,'biology',97,1195,2857,'<div class="matching-question"><div class="column-left">DNA is a hydrophilic molecule<br></div><div class="column-right">It cannot pass through cell membranes.</div></div>','[{"text": "Statement A is correct but Statement B is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Statement A is incorrect but Statement B is correct.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both statements are correct.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Statements are incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'DNA is a hydrophilic molecule, which is negatively charged. Cell membrane or plasma membrane is hydrophobic. It mainly comprises lipids which do not allow hydrophilic molecules such as DNA to cross the cell membrane.<br>Both statements are correct.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ type-2',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49493,'biology',97,1197,2859,'<div class="matching-question"><div class="column-left">PCR technique is helpful in detecting bacterial and viral diseases even when symptoms of disease are not yet visible<br></div><div class="column-right">PCR is helpful in the identification of the coronavirus.</div></div>','[{"text": "Statement A is correct but Statement B is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Statement A is incorrect but Statement B is correct.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both statements are correct.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Statements are incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'The technique of polymerase chain reaction (PCR) has been applied to detect HIV in suspected AIDS patients and to detect mutations in genes in suspected cancer patients.<br>Both statements are correct.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ type-2',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49494,'biology',97,1192,2853,'<div class="matching-question"><div class="column-left">Biotechnology deals with techniques that use living organism to produce products useful for humans.<br></div><div class="column-right">Construction of recombinant DNA involves cleaving DNA segments with endonuclease and rejoining with ligase.</div></div>','[{"text": "Statement A is correct but Statement B is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Statement A is incorrect but Statement B is correct.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both statements are correct.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Statements are incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'The technique of using living organisms or enzymes from organisms to produce products and processes useful to humans are called biotechnology.<br>Recombinant DNA is made by combining DNA from two or more sources. DNA fragments are cut out of a specific position in the chromosome using restriction enzymes (also called restriction endonuclease) and then inserted into other chromosomes or DNA molecules using enzyme ligase.<br>Both statements are correct.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ type-2',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49495,'biology',97,1194,2856,'<div class="matching-question"><div class="column-left">Maintenance of sterile environment is essential for manufacture of biotechnological products.</div><div class="column-right"><em>Agrobacterium tumefaciens</em> delivers a piece of DNA known as \'A-DNA\' which transforms normal plant cells into tumour cells and directs these tumour cells to produce chemicals against pathogens</div></div>','[{"text": "Statement A is correct but Statement B is incorrect.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Statement A is incorrect but Statement B is correct.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both statements are correct.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Statements are incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'<em> Agrobacterium tumefaciens</em> is a pathogen of several dicot plants. It is used for the gene transfer in higher organisms. It is able to deliver a piece of DNA known as \'T-DNA\' to transform normal plant cells into a tumor and direct these tumor cells to produce the chemicals required by the pathogen.<br>In order to produce desired biotechnology products such as antibiotics, vaccines, enzymes, etc, we should provide a sterile environment in the lab.<br>A sterile working place is the inevitable part of biotechnology labs. A large number of microflora(bacteria, fungi) are present in the air.<br>So, only statement A is correct.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ type-2',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49496,'biology',97,1194,2856,'<div class="matching-question"><div class="column-left">Origin of replication is an essential part of a vector and is responsible for initiating replication<br></div><div class="column-right">The vector requires a selectable marker, which helps in identifying and eliminating nontransformants and selectively permitting the growth of the transformants.</div></div>','[{"text": "Statement A is correct but Statement B is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Statement A is incorrect but Statement B is correct.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both statements are correct.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Statements are incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Origin of replication is a sequence from where replication starts and any piece of foreign DNA is linked to this sequence. The replication occurs inside the host cells. This new sequence is also responsible for controlling copy number of linked DNA. Therefore, if any person wants to produce many copies of the target DNA he/ she should clone in a vector whose origin gives support to high copy number.<br>Selectable markers are essential to identify and eliminate non-transformants (no recombinant DNA), and selectively permitting the growth of the transformants (host cells bearing recombinant DNA).<br>So, both statements are correct.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ type-2',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49497,'biology',97,1194,2856,'<div class="matching-question"><div class="column-left">Coronavirus are also now used as cloning vectors in animal because of their ability to transform normal cells into cancerous cell<br></div><div class="column-right">Ligase acts by forming phosphodiester bonds.</div></div>','[{"text": "Statement A is correct but Statement B is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Statement A is incorrect but Statement B is correct.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both statements are correct.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Statements are incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'The mechanism of DNA ligase is to form two covalent phosphodiester bonds between 3\' hydroxyl ends of one nucleotide ("acceptor"), with the 5\' phosphate end of another ("donor"). Two ATP molecules are consumed for each phosphodiester bond formed.<br>Only statement B is correct.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ type-2',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49498,'biology',97,1193,2855,'<div class="matching-question"><div class="column-left">In gel electrophoresis, DNA fragments are separated based on their size .</div><div class="column-right">The first recombinant DNA was constructed by using a piece of DNA from plasmid carrying antibiotic resistance gene in the bacterium<em> Salmonella typhimurium</em> and linked it to the plasmid of <em>E.coil</em>.</div></div>','[{"text": "Statement A is correct but Statement B is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Statement A is incorrect but Statement B is correct.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both statements are correct.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Statements are incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Gel electrophoresis is a technique used to separate components of a mixture on the basis of their size. The mixture could be a DNA, an RNA or even a mixture of proteins. A gel matrix of suitable material (e.g., agarose gel) is spread upon the electrophoresis plate. The mixture is loaded onto a grove in the gel and an electrical field is applied to it. The particles of the mixture move away from the start towards the oppositely charged end based on their molecule size and charge. The particles with a smaller size have been reported to move faster and farther away from the start.<br>The first recombinant DNA was constructed by Stanley Cohen and Herbert Boyer in 1972 . They cut the piece of DNA from a plasmid carrying antibiotic resistance gene in the bacterium <em>Salmonella typhimurium</em> and linked it to the plasmid of <em>Escherichia coli.</em><br>Both statements are correct.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ type-2',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49499,'biology',97,1195,2857,'<div class="matching-question"><div class="column-left">In chemical mediated gene transfer method, certain chemicals such as $\\mathrm{Ca}$ phosphate help foreign DNA to enter the host cell<br></div><div class="column-right">Microinjection method is used inplant cells.</div></div>','[{"text": "Statement A is correct but Statement B is incorrect.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Statement A is incorrect but Statement B is correct.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both statements are correct.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Statements are incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Micro-injection is a method used to Inject a recombinant DNA into the nucleus of an animal cell. This is the only way to introduce alien DNA into host cells.<br>$\\mathrm{B}$ is incorrect statement.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ type-2',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49500,'biology',97,1194,2856,'<div class="matching-question"><div class="column-left">The tumour inducing plasmid (Ti plasmid) of <i>Agrobacterium tumefaciens</i> acts as a cloning vector in recombinant DNA technology<br></div><div class="column-right">EcoRI cuts the DNA between bases $\\mathrm{G}$ and $\\mathrm{A}$</div></div>','[{"text": "Statement A is correct but Statement B is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Statement A is incorrect but Statement B is correct.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both statements are correct.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Statements are incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'The tumour inducing Ti plasmid of Agrobacterium tumefaciens has been modified into cloning vector which is not pathogenic to the plants, however, it is still able to use its mechanisms to deliver genes of our interest into various plants.<br>EcoRI recognizes the sequence GAATTC, and cuts both DNA strands between the $G$ and the A nucleotides Both statements are correct.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ type-2',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49501,'biology',97,1196,2858,'<div class="matching-question"><div class="column-left">Use of chitinase enzyme is necessary for isolation of DNA from fungal cells.<br></div><div class="column-right">Fungal cell wall is made up of cellulose and chitinase is able to digest it.</div></div>','[{"text": "Statement A is correct but Statement B is incorrect.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Statement A is incorrect but Statement B is correct.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both statements are correct.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Statements are incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'These enzymes degrade cell wall, plasma membrane degrading enzymes like lipase, etc. are also needed. Since yeast is a fungus and fungal cell with is made of chitin (fungal cellulose), isolation of DNA necessarily requires use of enzyme chitinase.<br>Statement A is correct.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ type-2',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49502,'biology',97,1192,2853,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> All genetically modified organisms are rich in chimeric DNA (r-DNA)<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Chimeric DNA constitutes genes of two different organisms.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are incorrect", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Chimeric DNA means a DNA molecule obtained from different organisms.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49503,'biology',97,1193,2855,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Ethidium bromide (EtBr) under UV radiation gives bright orange coloured bands.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> The separated DNA fragments can be seen after staining the DNA with compound EtBr.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are incorrect", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Agarose gel electrophoresis is used to segregate DNA fragments according to the mass and size. Ethidium bromide is the fluorescent stain used in this technique. Ethidium bromide, when exposed to ultraviolet light, produces a fluorescent effect. Hence the DNA tagged by it can be traced quickly on the transparent gel.<br>The separated DNA fragments can be visualised only after staining the DNA with a compound known as ethidium bromide followed by exposure to UV radiation as we cannot see pure DNA fragments in the visible light and without staining.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49504,'biology',97,1193,2854,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Restriction endonucleases are also called \'molecular scissors\'.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> When fragments generated by restriction endonucleases are mixed, they join together due to their sticky ends.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are incorrect", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Restriction enzymes are also called "molecular scissors" as they cleave DNA at or near specific recognition sequences known as restriction sites. These enzymes make one incision on each of the two strands of DNA and are also called restriction endonucleases.<br>Restriction endonuclease are molecular scissors which cut a DNA molecule within certain specific site called restriction site. Common restriction endonucleases are EcoRI, Barn II, Hind III etc. When the DNA fragments are mixed they joint to each other due to free 5 phosphate group and $3 \\mathrm{OH}$ group.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49505,'biology',97,1195,2857,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> In recombinant DNA technology, human genes are always transferred into bacteria (Prokaryotes) or yeast (eukaryotes)<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Both bacteria and yeast multiply very fast to form huge populations which express the desired gene','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are incorrect", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'In recombinant DNA technology, a specific DNA molecule is inserted into a vector DNA molecule that can carry foreign DNA, and the resulting recombinant DNA is introduced into a host organism (often the common bacterium Escherichia coli or the yeast Saccharomyces cerevisiae). Large numbers of genetically identical host organisms which carrying the same specific foreign DNA molecule can be produced as they multiply rapidly and allow the DNA or its protein product to be produced in large quantities. Most often bacteria are used as the host organism, because of their fast growth and the ready availability of techniques for manipulating and growing bacteria in small- and large-scale cultures',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49506,'biology',97,1194,2856,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> <i>Agrobacterium tumefaciens </i> is the causative agent of crown gall disease of dicots.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> <i>Agrobacterium tumefaciens</i> causes infection by entering plant through wounds and injuries.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are incorrect", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Ti plasmid integrates segment of its DNA into chromosomal DNA of its host plant cell.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49507,'biology',97,1193,2854,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Restriction enzymes of different organisms that recognize identical sequences are called isoschizomers.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> They are present only in eukaryotes.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are incorrect", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Restriction enzymes are found mainly in prokaryotes like bacteria and archea.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49508,'biology',97,1193,2854,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> All endonucleases cut DNA at specific sites<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Endonucleases are found in viruses','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are incorrect", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Endonuclease is a type of restriction enzymes which cut DNA at specific sites and Not all endonuclease cut DNA at specific sites. They are not found in virus but they are found in bacteria.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49509,'biology',97,1194,2856,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> <i>Agrobacterium tumefaciens</i> is a pathogen of several monocot plants.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Retroviruses in plants have ability to transform normal cells into cancerous cell.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are incorrect", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Agrobacterium tumefaciens is a pathogen of several dicot plants. It is used for the gene transfer in higher organisms. It is able to deliver a piece of DNA known as \'T-DNA\' to transform normal plant cells into a tumor and direct these tumor cells to produce the chemicals required by the pathogen.<br>Retroviruses in animals have the ability to transform normal cells into cancerous cells. In microinjection, cells are bombarded with high velocity microparticles of gold or tungsten coated with DNA\'.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49510,'biology',97,1194,2856,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> <i>E.coli</i> having pBR322 with DNA insert at BamHI site cannot grow in medium containing tetracycline.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Recognition site for Bam HI is present in tete region of pBR322.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are incorrect", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'pBR322 has recognition sites for several commonly used restriction enzymes.<br>Recognition site for BamHI is present in tetr region i.e., region responsible for tetracycline resistance. When an insert is added at the BamHI recognition site the gene for tetracycline resistance becomes non-functional and the recombinant bacteria with plasmid pBR322 that has DNA insert at BamHI lose tetracycline resistance.<br>So, the correct answer is \'Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation for Assertion\'.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49511,'biology',97,1193,2854,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Restriction enzymes Hind II and Hpa are produced from two different genera of bacteria.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Hind II is produced from <i>Haemophilus</i> while Hpa is produced from <i>Haematococcus</i>.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are incorrect", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'In the nomenclature of restriction enzymes, the first letter of the name of the genus in which given enzyme is discovered is written first in the capital. It is followed by the first two letters of species name of the organism and these three letters are generally written in italics. Hin and Hpa, both are produced from a single genus Haemophilus but from two different species i.e., H. influenzae and H. parainfluenza respectively.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49512,'biology',97,1197,2859,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> PCR technique is helpful in detecting bacterial and viral diseases even when symptoms of disease are not yet visible<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Very low concentrations of bacteria and viruses in human body can be detected by amplification of the nucleic acids using PCR technique.','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are incorrect", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'The technique of polymerase chain reaction (PCR) has been applied to detect HIV in suspected AIDS patients and to detect mutations in genes in suspected cancer patients.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49513,'biology',97,1192,2853,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> The construction of the first recombinant DNA emerged from the possibility of linking a gene encoding antibiotic resistance with a native plasmid of <em>Salmonella typhimurium</em>.<br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Plasmid is an autonomously replicating circular extrachromosomal DNA so they are used as vectors','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are incorrect", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Both are correct and the reason is the correct explanation of the assertion.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49514,'biology',97,1195,2857,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> Bacteriophages because of their high number per cell, have very high copy numbers of their genome within the bacterial cells.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Therefore they are used as vectors','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are incorrect", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Both are correct and the reason is the correct explanation of the assertion.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49515,'biology',97,1193,2855,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> The matrix used in gel electrophoresis should have controllable pore size<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> Agarose concentration can be changed to change pore sizes','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are incorrect", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Agarose is a polysaccharide obtained from red algae. Agarose dissolves in hot water and when it is cooled, it forms gel. Pore size depends upon agarose concentration. In general, a $1 \\%(\\mathrm{w} / \\mathrm{v})$ gel will have a pore size of $150 \\mathrm{~nm}$. While a $0.16 \\%$ gel has pore size of $500 \\mathrm{~nm}$',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49516,'biology',97,1193,2855,'<strong>Assertion:</strong> In gel electrophoresis, DNA fragments are separated.<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> DNA is negatively charged, so it moves towards anode under electric field','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are incorrect", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'DNA fragments can be isolated with the help of gel electrophoresis, where DNA moves towards the anode (+vely charged).',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49517,'biology',97,1193,2854,'Which one of the following palindromic base sequences in DNA can be easily cut at about the middle by some particular restriction enzyme?<br>','[{"text": "5\'- CGTTCG - 3\' 3\' - ATCGTA - 5\'", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "5\' - GAATTC - 3\' 3\' - CTTAAG - 5\'", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "5\' - CACGTA - 3\' 3\' - CTCAGT - 5\'", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "5\'- GATATG - 3\' 3\' - GTACTA - 5\'", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'A restriction enzyme (or restriction endonuclease) is an enzyme that cuts DNA at or near specific recognition nucleotide sequences known as restriction sites. Restriction enzymes recognize a specific sequence of nucleotides and produce a double-stranded cut in the DNA. Many of them are palindromic, meaning the base sequence reads the same backwards and forwards. Recognition sequences in DNA differ for each restriction enzyme, producing differences in the length, sequence and strand orientation (5\' end or the 3\' end) of a stickyend of an enzyme restriction. If we cut the DNA with EcoR1, the DNA would be cut right in the middle. All the pieces would be the same size, which would be $15 \\mathrm{~kb}$ long. Hence 5\' GAATTC 3\'; 3\' CTTAAG 5\' palindrome sequence can be easily cut at about the middle by EcoR1 enzyme.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2010,'AIPMT','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49518,'biology',97,1193,2854,'There is a restriction endonuclease called Eco RI. What does \' $c o$ \' part in it stand for?','[{"text": "Colon", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "<i>Coli</i>", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Coelom", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Coenzyme<br>", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'The convention of naming restriction enzymes is the first letter comes from the genus, second two letters come from the species of prokaryotic cell from which it is isolated.<br>Ex: Eco RI, R is derived from name of the strain. Roman numbers following the names indicate the order in which enzymes were isolated from the strain of bacteria.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2011,'AIPMT','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49519,'biology',97,1193,2854,'Given below is a sample of portion of DNA strand giving the base sequence on the opposite strands. What is so special shown in it?<br>5\' - GAATTC - 3\' 3\' - CTTAAG - 5\'','[{"text": "Palindromic sequence of base pairs", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Start codon at the 5\' end", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Deletion mutation", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Replication completed", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Palindromic sequence are sequence of base pairs that reads same on the two strands when orientation of reading is kept the same.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2011,'AIPMT','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49520,'biology',97,1195,2857,'Biolistics (gene gun) is suitable for<br>','[{"text": "DNA fingerprinting", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Disarming pathogen vectors", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Constructing recombinant DNA by joining with vectors", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Transformation of plant cells", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Biolistics (gene gun) is suitable for transformation of plant cells.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2012,'AIPMT','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49521,'biology',97,1194,2856,'Figure below is the diagrammatic representation of the <em>E.coli</em> vector pBR 322. Which one of the given options correctly identifies its certain component?','[{"text": "Ori-original restriction enzyme", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Rop- reduced osmotic pressure", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$A m p^{R}$, tet ${ }^{R}$ antibiotic resistance genes", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Hind III, EcoRI- selectable markers", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'In pBR 322, ori represents site of origin of replication, rop represents proteins which participate in replication of plasmid, Hind III, EcoRI are recognition sites of restriction endonucleases while ampR, tet R represents antibiotic resistance genes.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ type-3',2012,'AIPMT','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','biology/97_49521_question_1.png'),
(49522,'biology',97,1194,2856,'In genetic engineering, the antibiotics are used','[{"text": "To keep cultures free of infection", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "To select healthy vectors", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "As sequences from where replication starts", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "As selectable markers", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Antibiotics are used as selectable markers in genetic engineering to indicate the success of transformation',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2012,'AIPMT','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49523,'biology',97,1197,2859,'Which one is a true statement regarding DNA polymerase used in PCR?','[{"text": "It serves as a selectable marker", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "It is isolated from a virus", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "It remains active at high temperature", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "It is used to ligate introduced DNA in recipient cell", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'DNA Polymerase has thermostable property. Hence they are active at high temperature.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2012,'AIPMT','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49524,'biology',97,1197,2859,'The figure below shows three steps $(\\mathrm{A}, \\mathrm{B}, \\mathrm{C})$ of polymerase chain reaction (PCR). Select the option giving correct identification together with what it represents?<br>','[{"text": "A-Denaturation at a temperature of about $50^{\\\\circ} \\\\mathrm{C}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "B-Denaturation at a temperature of about $98^{\\\\circ} \\\\mathrm{C}$ separating the two DNA strands", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "A-Annealing with two sets of primers", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "C- Extension in the presence of heat stable DNA polymerase", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'C-Extension in the presence of heat stable DNA polymerase.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ type-3',2012,'AIPMT','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','biology/97_49524_question_1.png'),
(49525,'biology',97,1196,2858,'Which of the following is not correctly matched for the organism and its cell wall degrading enzyme?<div class="matching-question"></div>','[{"text": "Algae-Methylase", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Bacteria-Lysozyme", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Plant cells- Cellulase", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Fungi - Chitinase<br>", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Methylase is a type of transferase enzyme that transfers a methyl group from a donor to an acceptor. Cell wall of algae are made up of cellulose, pectin and mucilage. These substances cannot be degraded by methylase.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2013,'AIPMT','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49526,'biology',97,1193,2854,'DNA fragments generated by the restriction endonucleases in a chemical reaction can be separated by','[{"text": "Polymerase chain reaction", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Centrifugation", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Restriction mapping", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Electrophoresis<br>", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Electrophoresis is a technique used to separate macromolecules based on their size, this technique will apply a negative charge so that proteins move towards positive charge.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2013,'AIPMT','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49527,'biology',97,1194,2856,'Colonies of recombinant bacteria appear white in contrast to blue colonies of non recombinant bacteria because','[{"text": "Insertional inactivation of alpha galactosidase in nonrecombinant bacteria.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Non recombinant bacteria containing beta galactosidase", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Insertional inactivation of alpha galactosidase in recombinant bacteria.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Inactivation of glycosidase enzyme in recombinant bacteria.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'In a recombined plasmid, there is insertional inactivation, antibiotic resistance gene gets inactivated due to the insertion of a foreign gene(DNA) and helps in the selection of the recombinants. If the plasmid has an insert it will produce coloured colonies, when the plasmid does not have an insert it will produce colourless colonies',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2013,'AIPMT','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49528,'biology',97,1194,2856,'Which vector can clone only a small fragment of DNA?','[{"text": "Cosmid", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Plasmid", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Bacterial artificial chromosome", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Yeast artificial chromosome<br><br>", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Plasmids are small extra nuclear circular DNAs which carry extrachromosomal genes in bacteria and some fungi. They replicate independently.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2014,'AIPMT','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49529,'biology',97,1193,2854,'Which of the following restriction enzyme produces blunt end?','[{"text": "<i>Xho</i> I", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "<i>Eco</i> RV", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "<i>Sal</i> I", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "<i>Hind</i> III<br>", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'<i>Eco</i> RV is a type II restriction endonuclease isolated from certain strains of <i>E.coli</i>. It creates blunt ends and it recognizes palindromic sequence.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2016,'NEET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49530,'biology',97,1193,2854,'A foreign DNA and plasmid cut by the same restriction endonuclease can be joined to form a recombinant plasmid using','[{"text": "Ligase", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Polymerase III", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "<i>Taq</i> polymerase", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "<i>Eco</i> RI", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'DNA ligase is a specific type of enzyme that facilitates the joining of DNA strands together by catalyzing formation of Phosphodiester bond.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2016,'NEET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49531,'biology',97,1193,2854,'Which one of the following is a restriction endonuclease?<br>','[{"text": "DNase I", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Protease", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "RNase", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "<i>Hind</i> II", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'<i>Hind</i> II is the first restriction endonuclease.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2016,'NEET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49532,'biology',97,1194,2856,'Which of the following is not the feature of plasmid?','[{"text": "Transferable", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Circular structure", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Independent replication", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Single stranded<br><br>", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Plasmids are extra chromosomal, self replicating usually circular, double stranded DNA molecules that serve as vectors which carry foreign DNA segment and replicate inside the host cell.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2016,'NEET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49533,'biology',97,1199,2861,'Which of the following is not a component of downstream processing?','[{"text": "Separation", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Preservation", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Purification", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Expression<br><br>", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Downstream processing includes separation and purification process. The product obtained is subjected to quality control, testing and kept in suitable preservatives.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2016,'NEET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49534,'biology',97,1198,2860,'Stirred tank bioreactor have been designed for','[{"text": "Addition of preservatives to the product", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Purification of the product", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Availability of oxygen throughout the process", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Ensuring anaerobic conditions in the cultural vessel", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Stirred tank bioreactor have been designed for availability of oxygen throughout the process .',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2016,'NEET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49535,'biology',97,1197,2859,'<i>Taq</i> Polymerase enzyme is obtained from','[{"text": "<i>Bacillus subtilis</i>", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "<i>Thiobacillus ferrooxidans</i>", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "<i>Thermus aquaticus</i>", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "<i>Pseudomonas putida</i>", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Taq Polymerase enzyme is obtained from the bacterium <i>Thermus aquaticus</i>.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2016,'NEET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49536,'biology',97,1194,2856,'A gene whose expression helps to identify transformed cell is known as :','[{"text": "Vector", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Plasmid", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Structural gene", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Selectable marker", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Selectable markers are helpful in identifying and eliminating nontransformantsand selectively permitting the growth of the transformants in recombinant DNA technology.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2017,'NEET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49537,'biology',97,1193,2855,'DNA fragments are','[{"text": "Negatively charged", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Neutral", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Either positively or negatively charged depending on their size", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Positively charged", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'DNA fragments are negatively charged, therefore, when an electric current is applied to the gel in Agarose gel electrophoresis, DNA will migrate towards the positively charged electrode',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2017,'NEET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49538,'biology',97,1193,2855,'The DNA fragments separated on an agarose gel can be visualised after staining with :<br>','[{"text": "Acetocarmine", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Aniline blue", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Ethidium bromide", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Bromophenol blue", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'On Agarose gel electrophoresis, DNA fragments can be separated and later can be visualised after staining with a compound known Ethidium bromide',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2017,'NEET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49539,'biology',97,1193,2855,'What is the criterion of DNA fragments movement on agarose gel during gel electrophoresis?','[{"text": "The smaller the fragment size, the farther it moves", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Positively charged fragments move to farther end", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Negatively charged fragments do not move", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "The larger the fragments size, the farther it moves", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'During gel electrophoresis, DNA fragments separate according to their size through sieving effect provided by agarose gel.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2017,'NEET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49540,'biology',97,1199,2861,'The process of separation and purification of expressed protein before marketing is called :','[{"text": "Downstream processing", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Bioprocessing", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Postproduction processing", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Upstream processing<br><br>", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'The process of separation and purification of proteins are called Downstream processing. This is done after the completion of biosynthetic stage. Here the product has to be subjected through a series of processes before it is ready for marketing as a finished product',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2017,'NEET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49541,'biology',97,1193,2854,'Following statements describe the characteristics of the enzyme Restriction Endonuclease. Identify the incorrect statement.','[{"text": "The enzyme cuts DNA molecule at identified position within the DNA.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "The enzyme binds DNA at specific sites and cuts only one of the two strands.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "The enzyme cuts the sugar-phosphate backbone at specific sites on each strand.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "The enzyme recognizes a specific palindromic nucleotide sequence in the DNA.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Restriction enzymes cut DNA molecules at a particular point by recognising a specific sequence. Each restriction endonuclease functions by inspecting the length of a DNA sequence. Once it finds its specific recognition sequence, it will bind to the DNA and cut each of the two strands of the double helix at specific points in their sugarphosphate backbone.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2019,'NEET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49542,'biology',97,1193,2855,'In gel electrophoresis, separated DNA fragments can be visualized with the help of:<br>','[{"text": "Ethidium bromide in UV radiation", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Acetocarmine in UV radiation", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Ethidium bromide in infrared radiation", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Acetocarmine in bright blue light", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Ethidium bromide in UV radiation',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2020,'NEET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49543,'biology',97,1194,2856,'The sequence that controls the copy number of the linked DNA in the vector, is termed:<br>','[{"text": "Ori site", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Palindromic sequence", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Recognition site", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Selectable marker", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Ori site',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2020,'NEET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49544,'biology',97,1193,2854,'The specific palindromic sequence which is recognized by EcoRI is:','[{"text": "5\'-GGAACC-3\', 3\'-CCTTGG-5\'", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "5\'-CTTAAG-3\', 3\'-GAATTC-5\'", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "5\'-GGATCC-3\', 3-CCTAGG-5\'", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "5\'-GAATTC-3\', 3\'-CTTAAG-5\'", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'5\'-GAATTC-3\', 3\'-CTTAAG-5\'',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2020,'NEET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49545,'biology',97,1197,2859,'Match the organism with its use in biotechnology<br>Select the correct option from the following:<div class="matching-question"><div class="column-left">A. Bacillus thuringiensis<BR>B. Thermus aquaticus<BR>C. Agrobacterium tumefaciens<BR>D. Salmonella typhimurium</div><div class="column-right">P. Cloning vector<BR>Q. Construction of first DNA molecules<BR>R. DNA polymerase<BR>S. Cry proteins</div></div>','[{"text": "${\\\\rm{A - S,}}\\\\,{\\\\rm{B - R,}}\\\\,{\\\\rm{C - P,}}\\\\,{\\\\rm{D - Q}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{A - R,}}\\\\,{\\\\rm{B - Q,}}\\\\,{\\\\rm{C - S,}}\\\\,{\\\\rm{D - P}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{A - R,}}\\\\,{\\\\rm{B - S,}}\\\\,{\\\\rm{C - P,}}\\\\,{\\\\rm{D - Q}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{A - Q,}}\\\\,{\\\\rm{B - S,}}\\\\,{\\\\rm{C - R,}}\\\\,{\\\\rm{D - P}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'${\\rm{A - S,}}\\,{\\rm{B - R,}}\\,{\\rm{C - P,}}\\,{\\rm{D - Q}}$',NULL,'Medium','Match the following',2020,'NEET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49546,'biology',97,1193,2854,'Choose the correct pair from the following:<br>','[{"text": "Polymerases-Break the DNA into fragments", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Nucleases-Separate the two strands of DNA", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Exonucleases-Make cuts at specific positions within DNA", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Ligases- Join the two DNA molecules", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Ligases-Join the two DNA molecules',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2020,'NEET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49547,'biology',97,1193,2854,'Identify the wrong statement with regard to Restriction Enzymes.','[{"text": "They cut the strand of DNA at palindromic sites.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "They are useful in genetic engineering.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Sticky ends can be joined by using DNA ligases.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Each restriction enzyme functions by inspection of the DNA sequence.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Sticky ends can be joined by using DNA ligases. The following statement is with regards to ligase and not endonuclease.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2020,'NEET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49548,'biology',97,1197,2859,'Which of the following is not an application of PCR (Polymerase Chain Reaction)?<br>','[{"text": "Molecular diagnosis", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Gene amplification", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Purification of isolated protein", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Detection of gene mutation", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Through PCR, molecular diagnosis of diseases, gene amplificatin and identification of mutated genes can be done.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2021,'NEET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49549,'biology',97,1196,2858,'During the purification process for recombinant DNA technology, addition of chilled ethanol precipitates out:','[{"text": "RNA", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "DNA", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Histones", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Polysaccharides<br>", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'The initial role of the ethanol and monovalent cations is to remove the solvation shell surrounding the DNA and permitting the precipitation of the DNA in pellet form. The ethanol also serves to promote the aggregation of the DNA.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2021,'NEET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49550,'biology',97,1194,2856,'Plasmid pBR322 has PstI restriction enzyme site within gene $a m p^{R}$ that confers ampicillin resistance. If thes enzyme is used for inserting a gene for $\\beta$-galactoside production and the recombinant plasmid is inserted in an E.coli strain','[{"text": "It will not be able to confer ampicillin resistance to the host cells.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "The transformed cells will have the ability to resist ampicillin as well as produce $\\\\beta$-galactoside", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "It will lead to lysis of host cell.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "It will be able to produce a novel protein with dual ability.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'due to insertion of foreign DNA it will not be able to confer the ampicillin resistance in to the host.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2021,'NEET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49551,'biology',97,1197,2859,'During the process of gene amplification using PCR, if very high temperature is not maintained in the beginning, then which of the following steps of PCR will be affected first?','[{"text": "Annealing", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Extension", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Denaturation", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Ligation<br><br>", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'The initial denaturation step is commonly performed at $94-98^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C}$ for $1-3$ minutes.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2021,'NEET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49552,'biology',97,1193,2854,'A specific recognition sequence identified by endonucleases to make cuts at specific positions within the DNA is:','[{"text": "Degenerate primer sequence", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Okazaki sequences", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Palidromic Nucleotide sequences", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Poly (A) tail sequences", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Endonuclease will recognize the palindromic sequence for cutting',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2021,'NEET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49553,'biology',97,1197,2859,'Which of the following is a correct sequence of steps in a PCR (Polymerase Chain Reaction)?<br>','[{"text": "Denaturation, Annealing, Extension", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Denaturation, Extension, Annealing", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Extension, Denaturation, Annealing", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Annealing, Denaturation, Extension", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'There are three main stages in PCR they are:<br>a.Denaturing - when the double-stranded template DNA is heated to separate it into two single strands.<br>b.Annealing - when the temperature is lowered to enable the DNA primers to attach to the template DNA.<br>c.Extending - when the temperature is raised and the new strand of DNA is made by the Taq polymerase enzyme.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2021,'NEET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49554,'biology',97,1193,2855,'DNA strands on a gel stained with ethidium bromide when viewed under UV radiation, appear as:<br>','[{"text": "Yellow bands", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Bright orange bands", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Dark red bands", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Bright blue bands", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Ethidium Bromide is a dark red, crystalline, non-volatile, odorless powder that is moderately soluble in water. This material fluoresces a red-orange color under ultraviolet (UV) light and with increased fluorescence when bound to double-stranded DNA.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2021,'NEET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49555,'biology',97,1197,2859,'Given below are two statements  one is labelled as  Assertion and the other is : labelled as Reason.<br><br><strong>Assertion:</strong> Polymerase chain reaction is used in DNA amplification<br><br><br><strong>Reason:</strong> The ampicillin resistant gene is used as a selectable marker to check transformation<br><br>In the light of the above statements, choose the correct answer from the options given below :<br>','[{"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct and Reason is the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are correct but Reason is not the correct explanation of the Assertion.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Assertion is correct but Reason is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Assertion and Reason are incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Polymerase chain reaction is used in DNA amplification. The ampicillin resistant gene is used as a selectable marker to check transformation. Here both assertion and reason is correct but the reason is not correct explanation of the assertion.',NULL,'Medium','Assertion and Reason',2022,'NEET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49556,'biology',97,1193,2855,'Which one of the following statement is not true regarding gel electrophoresis technique?','[{"text": "The separated DNA fragments are stained by using ethidium bromide.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "The presence of chromogenic substrate gives blue coloured DNA bands on the gel.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Bright orange coloured bands of DNA can be observed in the gel when exposed to UV light .", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "The process of extraction of separated DNA strands from gel is called elution.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'The presence of chromogenic substrate gives white coloured DNA bands on the gel.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2022,'NEET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49557,'biology',97,1193,2854,'In the following palindromic base sequences of DNA, which one can be cut easily by particular restriction enzyme?','[{"text": "5\' G A A T T C 3\'; 3\' C T T A A G 5\'", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "5\' C T CA G T 3\'; 3\' G A G T CA 5\'", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "5\' G TA T T C 3\'; 3\' C A TAA G 5\'", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "5\' G A T A C T 3\'; 3\' C T A T G A 5\'", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Palindromic base sequence of DNA which can be cut easily by restriction enzyme is $5^{\\prime}$ GAATTC3\'; 3\'CTTAAG5\'',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2022,'NEET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49558,'biology',97,1194,2856,'Which of the following is not a desirable feature of a cloning vector?<br>','[{"text": "Presence of a marker gene", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Presence of single restriction enzyme site", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Presence of two or more recognition sites", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Presence of origin of replication", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Presence of more than one recognition sites within the vector will generate several fragments, which will complicate the gene cloning.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2022,'NEET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49591,'biology',97,1193,2854,'What does the sample of given base sequence represent?<br>5\' - GAATTC - 3\'<br>5\' - CTTAAG- 5\'','[{"text": "Completion of replication", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Initiator codon at 5\' end", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Palindromic sequence", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Deletion mutation", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'It is representation of palindromiv sequence fro the restriction endonuclease EcoR-I',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2022,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49559,'biology',97,1193,2854,'Given below are two statements :<div class="matching-question"><div class="column-left">Restriction endonucleases recognise specific sequence to cut DNA known as palindromic nucleotide sequence.</div><div class="column-right">Restriction endonucleases cut the DNA strand a little away from the centre of the palindromic site.<br><br>In the light of the above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below :</div></div>','[{"text": "Both Statement A and Statement B are incorrect", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Statement A is correct but Statement B is incorrect", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Statement A is incorrect but Statement B is correct", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Statement A and Statement B are correct", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Each restriction endonuclease recognises a specific palindromic nucleotide sequences in the DNA. Restriction enzymes cut the strand of DNA a little away from the centre of the palindrome sites, but between the same two bases on the opposite strands.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ type-2',2022,'NEET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49560,'biology',97,1197,2859,'Select the correct statement from the following','[{"text": "There are no risk factors associated with rDNA technology", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "The first step in PCR is heating which is used to separate both the strands of gene of interest", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "DNA from one organism will not blend to DNA from other organism", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Genetic engineering works only on animals and not yet successfully used on plants", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Statement (2) - is correct<br>The first step in PCR is heating which is used to separate both the strands of gene of interest is called degeneration.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2023,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49561,'biology',97,1197,2859,'PCR is used for','[{"text": "DNA ligation", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "DNA digestion", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "DNA amplification", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "DNA isolation", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'PCR is used for DNA amplification (Increase in number of copies)',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2023,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49562,'biology',97,1195,2857,'Which of these is NOT a method to make host cells ‘competent’ to take up DNA?','[{"text": "Elution", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Biolistics", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Use of disarmed pathogen vectors", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Micro-injection", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Except Elution, remaining three methods to make host cells competent to take up DNA',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2023,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49563,'biology',97,1196,2858,'From the following tools/ techniques of genetic engineering, identify those which are required for cloning a bacterial gene in animal cells and choose the correct options:<br>$\\begin{array}{l}\n{\\rm{I}}{\\rm{.Endonuclease}}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{\\rm{II}}{\\rm{.Ligase}}\\\\\n\\begin{array}{*{20}{l}}\n{{\\rm{III}}{\\rm{.A}}{\\rm{.tumefaciens}}}&{{\\rm{IV}}{\\rm{.Microinjection}}}\n\\end{array}\\\\\n\\begin{array}{*{20}{l}}\n{{\\rm{V}}{\\rm{.Genegun}}}&{\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{\\rm{VI}}{\\rm{.Lysoszyme}}}\n\\end{array}\\\\\n\\begin{array}{*{20}{l}}\n{{\\rm{VII}}{\\rm{.Cellulase}}}&{\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{\\rm{VIII}}{\\rm{.Electrophoresis}}}\n\\end{array}\n\\end{array}$','[{"text": "I, II, IV, VI, VIII", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "I, III, IV, V, VII", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "II, III, IV, VI, VII, VIII", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "II, III, V, VII, VIII", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Following tools /techniques of genetic\nengineering are required for cloning a bacterial\ngene in animal cells<br>1) Endonuclease<br> 2) Ligase\n<br>3) Microinjection<br>4) Lysozyme<br>5) Electrophoresis',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2023,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49564,'biology',97,1195,2857,'In gene gun method used to introduce alien\nDNA into host cells, microparticles of ________ metal are used','[{"text": "Copper", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Zinc", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Tungsten or gold", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Silver", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'In gene gun method, microparticles of tungsten or gold are used. Gold or tungsten are inert in nature so they do not alter the chemical composition of cells.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2023,'NEET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49565,'biology',97,1193,2855,'Upon exposure to UV radiation, DNA stained with ethidium bromide will show','[{"text": "Bright red colour", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Bright blue colour", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Bright yellow colour", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Bright orange colour", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'In recombinant DNA technology the separated DNA fragments can be visualised only after staining the DNA with a substance known as ethidium bromide followed by exposure to U.V. radiation. DNA is visualised as bright orange coloured bands in ethidium bromide stained gel exposed to U.V. light.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2023,'NEET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49566,'biology',97,1196,2858,'During the purification process for recombinant\nDNA technology, addition of chilled ethanol precipitates out','[{"text": "RNA", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "DNA", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Histones", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Polysaccharides", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'During isolation of the genetic material, purified DNA ultimately precipitates out after the addition of chilled ethanol.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2023,'NEET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49567,'biology',97,1197,2859,'Main steps in the formation of Recombinant DNA are given below. Arrange these steps in a correct sequence.<br>A. Insertion of recombinant DNA into the host cell<br>B. Cutting of DNA at specific location by restriction enzyme<br>C. Isolation of desired DNA fragment<br>D. Amplification of gene of interest using PCR<br>Choose the correct answer from the options given below','[{"text": "B, C, D, A", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "C, A, B, D", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "C, B, D, A", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "B, D, A, C", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Recombinant DNA technology involves several steps in specific sequence such as isolation of DNA, fragmentation of DNA by restriction endonucleases, isolation of desired DNA fragment, ligation of the DNA fragment into a vector, transferring the recombinant DNA into the host, culturing the host cells in a medium at large scale and extraction of the desired product.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2023,'NEET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49568,'biology',97,1194,2856,'Which of the following is not a cloning vector?','[{"text": "BAC", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "YAC", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "pBR322", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Probe", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'A single stranded DNA or RNA tagged with a radioactive molecule is called a probe and it helps in the detection of mutated gene. Option (2), (3) and (1) are not correct because YAC, BAC, pBR322 are vectors.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2023,'NEET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49592,'biology',97,1194,2856,'Genetic material in retrovirus is','[{"text": "DNA", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "RNA", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both DNA and RNA", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Proteins", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Retrovirus contains genetic material RNA rather than DNA.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49569,'biology',97,1193,2854,'The enzymes which are absolutely necessary for recombinant DNA technology are:','[{"text": "Restriction endonucleases and topoisomerases", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Endonucleases and polymerases", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Restriction endonucleases and ligases", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Peptidases and ligases", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Recombinant DNA is the method of joining two or more DNA molecules to create a hybrid. The technology is made possible by two types of enzymes, restriction endonucleases and ligase. A restriction endonuclease recognizes a specific sequence of DNA and cuts within, or close to, that sequence DNA fragments generated by digestion with a restriction endonuclease can be joined together again by the enzyme ligase.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2012,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49570,'biology',97,1193,2854,'EcoRI cleaves the DNA strands to produce:<br>','[{"text": "Blunt ends", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Sticky ends", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Satellite ends", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Ori replication end", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'EcoRI make cut in the two DNA strands at the different position that are staggered by two or four nucleotides leading to formation of short single-stranded overhangs at each end, thus called sticky or cohesive end. They are called so as the base pairing between them can stick the DNA molecule back together again.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2013,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49571,'biology',97,1194,2856,'Identify the desirable characteristics for a plasmid used in rDNA technology from the following.<br>(A) Ability to multiply and express outside the host in a bio-reactor<br>(B) A highly active promoter<br>(C) A site at which replication can be initiated<br>(D) One or more identifiable marker gens<br>(E) One or more unique restriction sites','[{"text": "A, C and E only", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "B, C and E only", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "A, C, D and E only", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "B, C, D and E only", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'The desirable characteristics in plasmids to function as effective cloning vector are:<br>They should have unique cleavage sties for several restriction endonucleases which could be used as cloning sites.<br>The replication origin of the plasmid should be compatible with the host for which it is designed.<br>Presence of marker genes in the plasmid (such as antibiotic resistant) so that cells that are transferred with the plasmid can be easily selected.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2014,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49572,'biology',97,1193,2854,'Identify the DNA segment which is not a palindromic sequence.<br>','[{"text": "5\'GAATTC3\'<br>3\'CTTAAG5\'", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "5\'GGATCC3\'<br>3\'GGTACC5\'", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "5\'CCCGGG3\'<br>3\' GGGCCC5\'", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "5\'GCGGCCGC3\'<br>3\'CGCCGGCG5\'", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Palindromes are groups of letters forming words that read the same in each direction. i.e., $5^{\\prime}$ to $3^{\\prime}$ on both strands or $3^{\\prime}$ to $5^{\\prime}$ on both strands. Hence, (3) is the correct answer, as rest of the options are reading same from both the sides.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2014,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49573,'biology',97,1193,2854,'EcoRI is :','[{"text": "A restriction enzyme", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "A plasmid", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Used to join two DNA fragments", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "The abbreviation for bacterium Escherichia coli", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'EcoRI is a restriction endonuclease enzyme isolated from species E. coliRY13. It recognises the sequence .',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2014,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49574,'biology',97,1194,2856,'The function of a selectable marker is:<br>','[{"text": "Identify ori site", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "To destroy recognition sites", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Eliminating transformants and permitting non-transformants", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Elimination of non-transformants and permitting transformants", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'A selectable marker is a gene introduced into a cell, especially a bacterium or to cells in culture, that confers a trait suitable for artificial selection. Under the selective conditions, only cells that contain plasmids with the appropriate selectable marker can survive. Commonly, genes that confer resistance to various antibiotics are used as selective markers in cloning vectors.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2015,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49575,'biology',97,1193,2855,'With respect to DNA fragmentation:<div class="matching-question"><div class="column-left">Gel electrophoresis and elution are two important processes.<br></div><div class="column-right">After staining with ethidium bromide it has to be exposed to U.V. light.</div></div>','[{"text": "Both Statement A and Statement B are correct.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Only Statement A is correct and Statement B is not correct.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Only Statement A is correct.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Only Statement B is correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'The isolated DNA fragments can be separated by gel electrophoresis which separates DNA fragments on the basis of charge and molecular weight. The separated DNA fragments can be visualised only after staining the DNA with ethidium bromide followed by exposure to UV radiation. DNA fragments cannot be seen in visible light without staining.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ type-2',2015,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49576,'biology',97,1193,2855,'Which one of the following statements is wrong with respect to separation of DNA fragments on gel electrophoresis?','[{"text": "The DNA fragments move towards anode under electric field though the matrix", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "The commonly used matrix is agarose gel.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "The DNA fragments resolve according to their size.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "The smaller DNA fragments remains close to the cathode.", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Gel electrophoresis is a technique that uses differences in electrical charges to separate the molecules in a mixture. Since DNA molecules have negative charges, they migrate towards the positive electrode (anode) when placed in an electric field. This rate of migration depends on size. The smaller the DNA molecule, faster is the migration. So, the DNA band that is the farthest from the well where it was loaded, is the smallest in size.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2016,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49577,'biology',97,1194,2856,'Which one of the following statements is not correct about a plasmid?<br>','[{"text": "It is a circular DNA.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "It has antibiotic resistant gene.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "It has the ability of autonomous replication.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Its DNA is an long as chromosomal DNA.", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'A plasmid is a small, circular, double stranded DNA molecule that is separate from the main chromosome. It is found in bacteria.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2016,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49624,'biology',97,1194,2856,'The "Ti plasmid" of <i>Agrobacterium tumefaciens</i> stands for','[{"text": "Tumour inhibiting plasmid", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Tumor independent plasmid", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Tumor inducing plasmid", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Temperature independent plasmid", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Ti plasmid of <i>Agrobacterium tumefaciens</i> is tumor inducing plasmid, containing T-DNA which causes tumor in several dicot plants.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2024,'NEET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49578,'biology',97,1193,2855,'Elution means:<br>','[{"text": "Separation of DNA fragments on agarose gel", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Cutting and extraction of DNA bands from the agarose gel", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Making the DNA bands visible under UV radiation", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Isolating alien DNA from the choice organism", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'After the DNA fragments have separated, the bands are cut and extracted, and the process is called elution (unbinding). The purified fragments are then joined with cloning vectors to produce recombinant DNA molecules using the enzyme ligase.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2016,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49579,'biology',97,1193,2854,'Restriction endonucleases are isolated from some bacteria. Their role in bacteria is:<br>','[{"text": "Defense against virus", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Synthesis of proteins", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Act as genetic material", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Help in reproduction", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Restriction enzyme, also called restriction endonuclease, a protein produced by bacteria that cleaves DNA at specific sites along the molecule. In the bacterial cell, restriction enzymes cleave foreign DNA, thus eliminating infecting organisms.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2017,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49580,'biology',97,1193,2854,'From which bacterium the REN-Sal-I is isolated?','[{"text": "<i>Escherichia coli</i>", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "<i>Streptococcus aureus</i>", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "<i>Haemophilus influenzae</i>", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "<i>Streptomyces albus</i>", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'The restriction enzymes are isolated from the bacterial sources and are named according to the name of the bacterial species. The restriction endonuclease Sal I is obtained from Streptomyces albus.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2017,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49581,'biology',97,1198,2860,'A transformed bacterium with human gene, fails to produce desired protein. The reason could be:<br>','[{"text": "Human gene may have intron which bacteria cannot process.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Amino acid codons for human and bacteria differ.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Human protein is formed but degraded by bacteria.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "The bacterial promotor gene cannot induce transcription of human gene.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'The rDNA having human gene was successfully inserted into the bacteria, bu the transformed cells failed to produce the desired protein, it is because of the inability to process the heterogenous RNA.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2017,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49582,'biology',97,1193,2854,'Identify the palindromic sequence in the following base sequences.<br>','[{"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Palindromic nucleotide sequence in the DNA molecule is the sequence of base pairs that read same when orientation of reading is same.<br>$\\begin{gathered}5^{`}-\\mathrm{GGATCC}-3^{\\prime} \\\\|||| \\mid \\\\3^{`}-\\mathrm{CCTAGG}-5^{\\prime}\\end{gathered}$<br>This is a palindromic sequence.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2018,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49583,'biology',97,1195,2857,'The technique of bombarding plant cells with high velocity microparticles of gold or tungsten, coated with DNA, is :','[{"text": "Microinjection", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Biolistic method", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Heat shock method", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "By disarmed pathogen vector", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'The two physical methods for introducing DNA into the cells include use of microinjection or a gene gun (biolistics). In the former, a very fine pipette is used to inject DNA molecules directly into nucleus of the animal cells to be transformed and in the latter; bombardment of cells is done with high velocity microprojectiles that are usually particles of gold or tungsten that have been coated with DNA.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2018,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49584,'biology',97,1193,2854,'Choose the bacterium which is not a source of REN.<br>','[{"text": "<i>Haemophilus influenzae</i>", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "<i>Escherichia coli</i>", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "<i>Agrobacterium tumefaciens</i>", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "<i>Bacillus amyloliquefaciens</i>", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'No restriction endonuclease is extracted from Agrobacterium tumefaciens. Rest three contain REN, Haemophilus influenzae has HindIII, Escherichia coli has EcoRI and Bacillus amyloliquefaciens has BamHI.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2018,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49585,'biology',97,1194,2856,'In cloning vectors, antibiotic resistant genes are helpful for;<br>','[{"text": "Transfer of foreign gene to the host", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Selection of recombinants", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Making the host cells competent", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Cleaving of vector by REN", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Antibiotic resistance gene present in the vector is mainly used for the selection of transformed bacterial cell. This transformed bacterial cell contain a recombinant vector (vector ligated with gene of interest) with antibiotic resistance gene (tet gene) so that it can resist from the antibiotic (tetracycline) containing media. The non - transformed cell failed to survive in the antibiotic containing media during transformation.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2019,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49586,'biology',97,1197,2859,'Suppose DNA samples collected for DNA fingerprinting analysis are less than the required quantity. Which of the following techniques is helpful to make the samples sufficient for above analysis?','[{"text": "Electrophoresis", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Chromatography", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "PCR", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "DNA probing", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Polymerase chain reaction amplifies the DNA, so if DNA is present in small quantity , it can ne increased by this technique',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2020,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49587,'biology',97,1195,2857,'Biolistics method is suitable for gene transfer into<br>','[{"text": "Viruses", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Animal cells", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Bacteria", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Plant cells", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'High-velocity microprojectiles are also used to deliver DNA into the plant cells. After being accelerated, small tungsten or gold particles (microprojectiles) coated with DNA pierce cell walls and membranes and enter intact plant cells without killing them. The disarmed pathogen vectors then infect the cell and the recombinant DNA molecules are transferred. This technique is termed as biolistics or gene gun',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2020,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49588,'biology',97,1193,2855,'Identify the labels $\\mathrm{M}$ and $\\mathrm{N}$ in the following Agarose gel electrophoresis representation<br>','[{"text": "M - Digested DNA bands, N - Undigested DNA bands", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "M - Hybridized DNA bands, $\\\\mathrm{N}$ - Unhybridised DNA bands.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "M-Largest DNA bands, N - Smallest DNA bands.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "M - Smallest DNA bands, N - Largest DNA bands.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'M- represents largest DNA particles, N represents smaller DNA.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ type-3',2020,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','biology/97_49588_question_1.png'),
(49593,'biology',97,1194,2856,'What happens, when DNA fragments is inserted into plasmid (pBR-322) using enzyme Pvu-I or Pst-I ?<br>i. Inactivation of ampR gene<br>ii. Inactivation of tetR gene<br>iii. Unable to grow in the presence of ampicillin<br>iv. Unable to grow in the presence of tetracycline<br>v. Unable to replicate<br>Mark the correct choice :-','[{"text": "ii and iv are correct", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "i and iii are correct", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "i, iii and $v$ are correct", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "ii, iv and $v$ are correct", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'i and iii are correct',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49594,'biology',97,1193,2854,'Restriction endonucleases are','[{"text": "Present in mammalian cells for degradation of DNA when a cell dies.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Used in genetic engineering for ligating two molecules", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Synthesized by bacteria as a part of their defense mechanism", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Used in invitro DNA synthesis", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Restriction endonucleases are molecular scissors which are used to cleave the DNA segments in Recombinant DNA technology. They are used in Invitro DNA Synthesis. They are synthesized as a part of their defense.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49595,'biology',97,1197,2859,'Which is true about biotechnology techniques?','[{"text": "PCR is used to cut DNA molecules", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "A DNA probe consists of radioactive single strand of DNA", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Restriction enzymes were first discovered in Bacteriophages", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Pvu I is a name for a DNA probe", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'The correct statement is - A DNA probe consists of radioactive single strand of DNA.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49596,'biology',97,1193,2854,'Match the following<div class="matching-question"><div class="column-left">A. <i>Thermus aquaticus</i><br>B. pBR322<br>C. Ligase<br>D. <i>Hind II</i></div><div class="column-right">P. Plasmid<br>Q. DNA Polymerase<br>R. Restriction endonuclease<br>S. Molecular glue</div></div>','[{"text": "${\\\\text{A - Q, B - P, C - S, D - R}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${\\\\text{A - P, B - S, C - R, D - Q}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\text{A - R, B - Q, C - S, D - P}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\text{A - Q, B - S, C - P, D - R}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'${\\text{A - Q, B - P, C - S, D - R}}$',NULL,'Easy','Match the following',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49597,'biology',97,1197,2859,'In PCR, short primers are added to singlestranded DNA molecules in a test tube and the appropriate enzymes are included to make a copy of the DNA. Which of the following primers is needed for copying the single-stranded DNA sequence 5\' TACGGTAGGTC3\'?','[{"text": "5\'GACCT", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "5\'ATGCC", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "5\'GGCAT", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "5\'TACGG", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'5\'GACCTACCGTA3\'',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49598,'biology',97,1194,2856,'Insertional inactivation is related to','[{"text": "Gene gun", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Microinjection", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Selection of recombinants", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Gel electrophoresis", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Insertional inactivation is related to selection of recombinants',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49599,'biology',97,1193,2855,'Which of the following statement is correct in the context of observing DNA fragments separated by agarose gel electrophoresis?','[{"text": "Ethidium bromide stained DNA can be seen under exposure to UV light", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "DNA can be seen in visible light", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "DNA can be seen without staining in visible light", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Ethidium bromide stained DNA can be seen in visible light", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'The separated DNA fragments can be visualised only after staining the DNA with a compound known as ethidium bromide followed by exposure to UV radiation (you cannot see pure DNA fragments in the visible light and without staining). You can see bright orange coloured bands of DNA in a ethidium bromide stained gel exposed to UV light',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49600,'biology',97,1193,2855,'i. In elution the separated bands of DNA are cut out from agarose gel and extracted from the gel piece.<br>ii. <i>E. coli</i> cloning vector $\\mathrm{pBR} 322$ shows several restriction enzymes, Ori, antibiotic resistance genes and Rop.<br>iii. The down stream processing and quality control testing vary from products to product.<br>iv. Competent bacterial cell cannot take up the plasmid.','[{"text": "All are incorrect", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "All are correct", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Only iv is correct", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Except iv all are correct", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Except iv all are correct',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49601,'biology',97,1194,2856,'Identify the enzyme (1) and (2) in the given diagram representing recombinant DNA technology.','[{"text": "Enzyme 1- <i>Taq</i> Polymerase, Enzyme 2Restriction endonuclease", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Enzyme 1-Restriction endonuclease, Enzyme 2- Ligases", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Enzyme 1- Ligases, Enzyme 2Restriction endonucleases", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Enzyme 1- Restriction endonuclease, Enzyme 2- <i>Taq</i> Polymerase.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Enzyme 1-Restriction endonuclease, Enzyme 2- Ligases',NULL,'Easy','MCQ type-3',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','biology/97_49601_question_1.png'),
(49602,'biology',97,1192,2853,'Plasmids are suitable vectors for genetic cloning as','[{"text": "They are indispensable", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "They are self replicating units", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "They are essential for bacterial reproduction.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "None of the above", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Plasmids are suitable vectors for genetic cloning because they are self replicating units',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49603,'biology',97,1194,2856,'Due to ampicillin resistance gene, one is able to select a transformed cell in the presence of ampicillin. The ampicillin resistance gene in this case is called','[{"text": "Selectable marker", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Recombinant gene", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Recognition site", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Origin of replication", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Due to ampicillin resistance gene, one is able to select a transformed cell in the presence of ampicillin. The ampicillin resistance gene in this case is called selectable marker',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49604,'biology',97,1193,2854,'Which is incorrect?','[{"text": "EcoRI cuts the DNA between bases G and $\\\\mathrm{A}$.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Each restriction endonuclease recognizes a specific palindromic nucleotide sequences in DNA.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "When cut by same restriction enzyme, the resultant DNA fragments do not have the same kind of \'sticky-ends\'.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Making multiple identical copies of any template DNA is called cloning.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'When same restriction enzyme is used resultant DNA fragments will have same kind sticky ends since they identify the same sequence.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49605,'biology',97,1193,2855,'Which statement is incorrect?<br>I. Genetic engineering is also called recombinant DNA technology<br>II. Bacteriophage is not used as vector<br>III. MALAYALAM is a palindrome<br>IV. Ethidium bromide can not be used for staining DNA','[{"text": "II, IV", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "I, II", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "I, II, III, IV", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "III, IV", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'II, IV<br>II. Bacteriophage is used as vector.<br>IV. Ethidium bromide is used for staining DNA',NULL,'Easy','MCQ type-2',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49606,'biology',97,1193,2854,'The key tools required for recombinant DNA technology are<br>i. Restriction endonucleases<br>ii. Polymerase enzyme<br>iii. Ligase<br>iv. Vectors<br>v. Host organisms','[{"text": "i, ii & iii", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "i, iii, iv & v", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "i, ii, iii & iv", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "i, ii, iii, iv & v", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'In Recombinant DNA Technology, the tools required are Restriction endonuclease, ligase, host organism, Polymerase enzyme and a vector.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49607,'biology',97,1194,2856,'The first restriction endonuclease type II, P was isolated from Q bacterium.<br>$\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{\\text{P}}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{\\text{Q}}$','[{"text": "$Eco\\\\,{\\\\text{RI }}\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,Escherichia\\\\,{\\\\text{RY13}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$Eco\\\\,{\\\\text{RIII  }}\\\\,\\\\,Escherichia\\\\,coli$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$Hind\\\\,{\\\\text{II}}\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,{\\\\text{ }}Haemophilus\\\\,{\\\\text{influenzae}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$Bam\\\\,{\\\\text{HI }}\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,Bacillus\\\\,amyloliquefaciens$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'The first restriction endonuclease type II, is Hind II was isolated from <i>Haemophilus influenza bacterium</i>.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49608,'biology',97,1194,2856,'Identify the false statement','[{"text": "Transformation is a procedure through which apiece of DNA is introduced in a host bacterium", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Fragments of the DNA can be separated by gel electrophoresis", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Endonuclease remove nucleotides from ends of the DNA.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Restriction endonuclease recognizes a specific sequence in the DNA called palindromic nucleotide sequence.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Endonuclease remove nucleotides from specific nucleotides of the DNA.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49609,'biology',97,1194,2856,'Choose correct statement','[{"text": "Presence of more than one recognition site for one enzyme within vector generate several fragments, which will complicate gene cloning", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Ligation of alien DNA is carried out at a restriction site present in Ori", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "In pBR322 one antibiotic resistance gene helps in selecting the transformants, whereas the other helps in cloning", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Rop codes for proteins involved in ligation of foreign DNA", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Presence of more than one recognition site for one enzyme within vector generate several fragments, which will complicate gene cloning is correct.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49610,'biology',97,1193,2854,'Match the following<div class="matching-question"><div class="column-left">A. Endonuclease<br>B. Plasmid<br>C. DNA ligase<br>D. Exonuclease</div><div class="column-right">P. Joins two ends of\nDNA<br>Q. Make cut at\nspecific positions<br>R. Remove\nnucleotides from\nends of DNA<br>S Autonomously\nreplication self\nreplicating\ncircular extra\nchromosomal\nDNA</div></div>','[{"text": "${\\\\text{A - Q, B - S, C - P, D - R}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${\\\\text{A - P, B - R, C - S, D - Q}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\text{A - Q, B - P, C - R, D - S}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\text{A - S, B - Q, C - P, D - R}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'${\\text{A - Q, B - S, C - P, D - R}}$',NULL,'Medium','Match the following',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49611,'biology',97,1193,2855,'Consider the following statements<br>I. Pure DNA fragments in agarose gel can be seen by naked eyes in UV light & without staining<br>II. DNA is a hydrophilic molecule<br>III. Separated DNA fragments are visualized after staining the DNA with ethanol and using UV light<br>IV. Presence of more than one same recognition sites within vector will generate several fragments of vector, which will complicate gene cloning<br>Which of the following statement are correct?','[{"text": "I, II & III", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "II, III & IV", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "II & IV", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "I, II, & IV", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Ethedium bromide need to vizuvalate the DNA IN AGA in agalosegell',NULL,'Medium','MCQ type-2',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49612,'biology',97,1194,2856,'Following are the steps involved in recombinant DNA technology, arrange them in the correct order<br>i. Extraction of desired gene product<br>ii. amplification of gene of interest<br>iii. isolation of desired DNA fragment<br>iv. ligation of DNA fragment into the vector<br>v. insertion of rDNA into the host.','[{"text": "i, ii, iii, iv, v", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "ii, iv, v, iii, i", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "iii, ii, iv, v, i", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "i, iv, v, iii, ii", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'iii, ii, iv, v, i',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49613,'biology',97,1198,2860,'Bioreactors are<br>i. Transgenic animals<br>ii.Fermentation factories','[{"text": "i only", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "ii only", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both (i) & (ii)", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "None of these", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Bioreactors are fermentation factories.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49614,'biology',97,1199,2861,'Read the following four statements (I-IV) and select the incorrect ones.<br>I. Downstream processing is one of the steps of rDNA technology.<br>II. Disarmed pathogen vectors are also used in transfer of rDNA into the host.<br>III. The first transgenic buffalo, Rosie produced milk which was human alpha-lactalbumin enriched.<br>IV. Restriction enzymes are used in isolation of DNA from other macromolecules.<br>Which of the two statements have mistakes?','[{"text": "II and III", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "III and IV", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "I and III", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "I and II", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'First transgenic cow was named Rosie . Restriction enzymes are used to break DNA molecule.',NULL,'Hard','MCQ type-2',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49615,'biology',97,1197,2859,'Palaeontologists unearthed a human skull during excavation. A small fragment of the scalp tissue was still attached to it. Only little DNA could be extracted from it. If the genes of the ancient man need to be analysed, the best way of getting sufficient amount of DNA from this extract is:<br>','[{"text": "Subjecting the DNA to polymerase chain reaction", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Subjecting the DNA to gel electrophoresis", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Treating the DNA with restriction endonucleases", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Hybridising the DNA with a DNA probe", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'The polymerase chain reaction (PCR) is a technique in which a short region of a DNA molecule is copied many times in vitro in presence of DNA polymerase enzyme. It is used in research work carried out to study evolution.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2009,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49616,'biology',97,1198,2860,'Some of the steps involved in the production of Humulin are given below. Choose the correct sequence.<br><br>(A) Synthesis of gene (DNA) for human insulin artificially.<br>(B) Culturing recombinant <i>E. coli</i> in bioreactors.<br>(C) Purification of humulin<br>(D) Insertion of human insulin gene into plasmid.<br>(E) Introduction of recombinant plasmid into <i>E. coli</i>.<br>(F) Extraction of recombinant gene product from <i>E. coli</i>.','[{"text": "A, C, E, F, B, D", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "B, A, D, C, E, F", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "C, E, B, A, F, D", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "A, D, E, B, F, C", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'The steps in the production of genetically engineered insulin are listed as follows:* Step 1 : An insulin-producing gene is isolated from human insulin extracted from pancreas cells.* Step 2 : A plasmid DNA is extracted from E. coli and converted in to a plasmid vector by digesting it with restriction enzyme.* Step 3 : The human insulin-producing gene is then inserted into plasmid vector forming recombinant DNA.* Step 4 : Later on, this recombinant DNA is introduced into the bacterium, now called as recombinant bacterium.* Step 5 : This recombinant bacterium multiplies in a fermentation tank and produces a large number of copies of human insulin.* Step 6: Finally, insulin is extracted, purified and packed, which is now ready to be injected into diabetic patients.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2010,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49617,'biology',97,1194,2856,'Read the two statements A and B and identify the correct choice from those given<br><div class="matching-question"><div class="column-left"><i>Agrobacterium tumefaciens</i> is the causative agent of crown gall disease of dicots.<br></div><div class="column-right"><i>Agrobacterium tumefaciens</i> cause infection by entering the plant through wounds and injuries.</div></div>','[{"text": "Statement A is correct and B is wrong.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Statement B is correct, and A is wrong.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both statements A and B are correct.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both statements A and B are wrong.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'<i>Agrobacterium tumefaciens</i> is a soil phytopathogen that naturally infects plant wound sites and causes crown gall disease via delivery of transferred (T) -DNA from bacterial cells into host plant cells. The neoplastic tumour-like cell growth is induced by expression of the oncogenes residing in the transferred-DNA (T-DNA) transported from these bacteria into the plant nucleus.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ type-2',2012,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49618,'biology',97,1197,2859,'DNA polymerase of <i>Thermus aquaticus</i> is','[{"text": "Thermolabile", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Thermophobic", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Exonuclease", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Thermostable", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Taq polymerase (DNA polymerase) is used in PCR technique to amplify the desired DNA.The enzyme is called thermostable DNA polymerase because it can catalyse the reaction even at high temperatures (More than $90^{\\circ} \\mathrm{C}$ ).',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2024,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49619,'biology',97,1194,2856,'If a recombinant DNA bearing gene for resistance to Ampicillin is transferred into <i>E.coli</i> cells, host cells become transformed into Ampicillin resistant cells. What happens when these <i>E.coli</i> are grown on medium containing Ampicillin?','[{"text": "Non-transformants will grow and transformants will die", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Non-transformants will die and transformant will grow", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both non-transformants and transformant will die", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both non-transformant and transformant will grow", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'The ampicillin gene in this case is called a selectable marker, and it can be used to select a transformed cell from non transformed cell in the presence of ampicillin.<br>Non-transformant bacterial cells (host organism) will die because the recombinant DNA containing amplicillin resistant gene has not entered into it.<br>Transformants will grow because it has ampicillin resistant gene.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2024,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49620,'biology',97,1193,2854,'Which of these enzymes is required to cleave a plasmid?','[{"text": "Ligase", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Endonuclease", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Exonuclease", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Polymerase", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Restriction enzymes belong to a larger class of enzymes called nucleases. These are of two kinds: exonucleases and endonucleases. Exonucleases remove nucleotides from the ends of the DNA whereas, endonucleases make cuts at specific positions within the DNA. Hence, they are used to cut foreign DNA (desired DNA) and plasmid DNA at specific positions in r-DNA technology.<br>',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2024,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49621,'biology',97,1193,2854,'<i>Hind II</i> always cuts DNA molecules at a particular point called recognition sequence and it consists of ____','[{"text": "8 bp", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "6 bp", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "4 bp", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "10 bp", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'The first restriction endonuclease - <i>Hind II</i>, always cut DNA molecules at a particular point by recognising sequence of six base pairs.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2024,'NEET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49622,'biology',97,1198,2860,'What is the fate of a piece of DNA carrying only gene of interest which is transferred into an alien organism?<br>A. The piece of DNA would be able to multiply itself independently in the progeny cells of the organism.<br>B. It may get integrated into the genome of the recipient.<br>C. It may multiply and be inherited along with the host DNA.<br>D. The alien piece of DNA is not an integral part of chromosome.<br>E. It shows ability to replicate.<br>Choose the correct answer from the options given below:','[{"text": "A and B only", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "D and E only", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "B and C only", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "A and E only", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'The fate of a piece of DNA carrying only gene of interest which is transferred into an alien organism are:<br>(B) It may get integrated into the genome of the recipient<br>(C) It may multiply and be inherited along with the host DNA<br>$\\rightarrow$ This piece of DNA would not be able to multiply itself in the progeny cells of the organism but when gets integrated into the genome of the recipient, it may multiply and be inherited along with the host DNA.',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',2024,'NEET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49623,'biology',97,1199,2861,'Which of the following statements is incorrect?','[{"text": "A bio-reactor provides optimal growth conditions for achieving the desired product", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Most commonly used bio-reactors are of stirring type", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Bio-reactors are used to produce small scale bacterial cultures", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Bio-reactors have an agitator system, an oxygen delivery system and foam control system", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'The statement (3) is incorrect because bioreactors are used for processing of large volumes ( 100 - 1000 litres) of culture.Small volume cultures cannot yield appreciable quantities of products. To produce in large quantities the development of bioreactors is required',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2024,'NEET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49625,'biology',97,1194,2856,'The following diagram showing restriction sites in <i>E. coli</i> cloning vector pBR322. Find the role of \' X \' and \' Y \' genes :','[{"text": "The gene \' X \' is responsible for resistance to antibiotics and \' Y \' for protein involved in the replication of Plasmid.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "The gene \' X \' is responsible for controlling the copy number of the linked DNA and \' Y \' for protein involved in the replication of Plasmid.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "The gene \' X \' is for protein involved in replication of Plasmid and \'Y\' for resistance to antibiotics.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Gene \' X \' is responsible for recognitions sites and \' Y \' is responsible for antibiotic resistance.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'X \' in the given diagram is ori while \' Y \' is rop.<br>\' X \' which is ori is responsible for controlling the copy number of the linked DNA and \' Y \' which is rop codes for protein involved in the replication of plasmid.',NULL,'Hard','MCQ type-3',2024,'NEET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','biology/97_49625_question_1.png'),
(49626,'biology',94,789,1698,'Degeneration of a genetic code is attributed to the :','[{"text": "Entire codon", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "First member of codon", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Second member of codon", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Third member of codon", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Degeneration of a genetic code is attributed to the third member of codon',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49627,'biology',94,789,1698,'The code is nearly universal: for example, from bacteria to human UUU would code for','[{"text": "Methionine", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Serine", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Alanine", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Phenylalanine", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'UUU codes for phenylalanine.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49628,'biology',94,789,1698,'One of the following is true with respect to AUG:','[{"text": "It codes for methionine", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "It is also an initiation codon", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "It codes for methionine in both prokaryotes and eukaryotes", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "All of the above", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'AUG is the most common start codon. It codes always for methionine in eukaryotes and a modified Met (fMet) in prokaryotes.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49629,'biology',94,789,1698,'AUG codes for','[{"text": "Methionine", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Valine", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Histidine", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Phenylalanine", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'AUG is the most common start codon. The start codon is the first codon of a messenger RNA (mRNA) transcript translated by a ribosome. It codes always for methionine in eukaryotes and a modified Met (fMet) in prokaryotes.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49630,'biology',94,789,1698,'The salient features of genetic code are as follows:<br>i. The codon is triplet. 61 codons code for amino acids and 3 codons do not code for any amino acids, hence they functionas stop codons.<br>ii. One codon codes for only one amino acid, hence, it is ambiguous and specific.<br>iii. Some amino acids are coded by more than one codon, hence the code is generate.<br>iv. The codon is read in mRNA in a contiguous fashion. There are no punctuations.<br>v. AUG has dual functions. It codes for Methionine (met), and it also act as initiator codon.<br>Select the incorrect options','[{"text": "i & iii are incorrect", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "i, ii & iii are incorrect", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "i, ii ,iii & iv are incorrect", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "ii & iii are incorrect", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Some amino acids are coded by more than one codon, hence the code is degenerate, One codon codes for only one amino acid, hence, it is non ambiguous and specific.',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49631,'biology',94,789,1698,'UUU, UUC code for which amino acids ?','[{"text": "Alanine, phenylalanine respectively", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Phenylalanine, alanine respectively", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Phenylalanine, methioninerespectively", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Phenylalanine, phenylalanine respectively", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'UUU, UUC code for Phenylalanine, phenylalanine respectively',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49632,'biology',94,789,1698,'In protein synthesis, a polypeptide of five amino acids is synthesized. Which one of the following can be the correct polypeptide ?','[{"text": "Glycine - valine - methionine - histidine lysine", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Lysine - methionine - valine - glycine histidine", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Valine - methionine - glycine - histidinelysine", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Methionine - lysine - glycine - valine histidine", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Polypeptide chain starts with methionine.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49633,'biology',94,789,1698,'UAA, UAG, and UGA are known as non-sense codons because they','[{"text": "Do not code for any amino acid", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Code for amino acids, which are not essential amino acids", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Code for the initiator amino acid (methionine), which is later removed from the polypeptide chain", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Are never present on mRNA", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Non-sense codon is a codon for which no normal tRNA molecule occurs. Termination of translation is caused due to the presence of nonsense codon. There are three nonsense codons viz., UAA, UAG, and UGA. They do not code for an amino acid and thus signal the end of protein synthesis.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49634,'biology',94,789,1698,'Match the columns I and II.<div class="matching-question"><div class="column-left">A. Unambigous<br>B. Degenerate<br>C. Dual functions<br>D. Universal</div><div class="column-right">P. Some amino acids are coded by more than one codon<br>Q. AUG<br>R. One codon codes for only one amino acid<br>S. From bacteria to human UUU would code for phenylalanine</div></div>','[{"text": "$\\\\mathrm{A}-\\\\mathrm{R}, \\\\mathrm{B}-\\\\mathrm{P}, \\\\mathrm{C}-\\\\mathrm{Q}, \\\\mathrm{D}-\\\\mathrm{S}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{A}-\\\\mathrm{Q}, \\\\mathrm{B}-\\\\mathrm{R}, \\\\mathrm{C}-\\\\mathrm{P}, \\\\mathrm{D}-\\\\mathrm{S}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{A}-\\\\mathrm{P}, \\\\mathrm{B}-\\\\mathrm{R}, \\\\mathrm{C}-\\\\mathrm{Q}, \\\\mathrm{D}-\\\\mathrm{S}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{A}-\\\\mathrm{Q}, \\\\mathrm{B}-\\\\mathrm{P}, \\\\mathrm{C}-\\\\mathrm{R},  \\\\mathrm{D}-\\\\mathrm{S}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'$\\mathrm{A}-\\mathrm{R}, \\mathrm{B}-\\mathrm{P}, \\mathrm{C}-\\mathrm{Q}, \\mathrm{D}-\\mathrm{S}$',NULL,'Easy','Match the following',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49635,'biology',94,789,1698,'There are 64 types of codons in genetic code dictionary because','[{"text": "Genetic code is triplet", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "There are 64 types of tRNAs found in cell", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "There are 44 meaningless and 20 codons for amino acids", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "There are 64 amino acids for coding", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'The genetic code is composed of 64 triplets of nucleotides and these triplets are called codons. A codon is made from three of the four possible ribonucleotides, there are $4^{3}$ or 64 combinations, which leads to 64 different codons.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49636,'biology',94,789,1698,'What is not true for genetic code?','[{"text": "It is nearly universal", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "It is degenerate", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "A codon in mRNA is read in a non- contiguous fashion", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "It is unambiguous", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Genetic code is a complete set of relationship between codons and amino acids (or stop signals). Genetic code is the nucleotide base sequence on DNA that is interpreted into a sequence of amino acids of the proteins to be synthesized. The codon in mRNAis read in contiguous form and it does not pause after the triplets. This means that there are no punctuations in between i.e., without any full stops, commas etc to synthesize a functional protein means each codon is immediately adjacent to the next, without any spacer nucleotides in between.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49637,'biology',94,789,1698,'In a codon, wobbling is generally restricted to','[{"text": "Third $\\\\mathrm{N}$ base", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Second N base", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "First $\\\\mathrm{N}$ base", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Any base", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Because of the degeneracy of the genetic code, the third base is little biased for the amino acid than other two bases. This third position in the codon is referred to as the wobble position.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49638,'biology',94,789,1698,'UUU would code for Phenylalanine (phe). Some exceptions to this rule have been found in','[{"text": "Chloroplast codons, and in some protozoans.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Mitochondrial codons, and in some plants", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Mitochondrial codons, and in some monera", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Mitochondrial codons, and in some protozoans.", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Mitochondrial codons, and in some protozoans.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49639,'biology',94,789,1698,'Match the columns I and II.<div class="matching-question"><div class="column-left">A. AUG<br>B. UUU<br>C. UUC<br>D. UAG</div><div class="column-right">P. Phenylalanine<br>Q. Methionine<br>R. Phenylalanine<br>S. Non-sense codon</div></div>','[{"text": "A – R , B – P , C – Q, D – S", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "A – Q , B – R , C – S, D –P", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "A – P , B – R , C – Q, D – S", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "A – Q , B – P , C – R , D – S", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'$\\mathrm{A}-\\mathrm{Q}, \\mathrm{B}-\\mathrm{P}, \\mathrm{C}-\\mathrm{R}, \\mathrm{D}-\\mathrm{S}$',NULL,'Easy','Match the following',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49640,'biology',94,789,1698,'Initiating codon of prokaryotes is','[{"text": "GUG", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "AUG", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "UGA", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "UAA", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'The codon AUG specifies the first amino acid, methionine, in protein synthesis also called as initiator. Inititing codon or start codon is the triplet of nucleotides, commonly adenine-uracil-guanine (AUG) or, in sometimes, guanine-uracil-guanine(GUG), that in eukaryotes and archea code for methionine and in bacteria code for formylmethionine, the first amino acids in all protein sequences.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49641,'biology',94,789,1698,'Which one of the following is the starter codon?','[{"text": "UGA", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "UAA", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "AUG", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "UAG", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'The most common start codon is AUG and is also called chain initiation codon. Start codon is the first codon of a messenger RNA translated by a ribosome',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49642,'biology',94,789,1698,'One codon codes for only one amino acid, hence the code is','[{"text": "Ambiguous and specific", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Unambiguous and specific", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Ambiguous and non-specific", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Unambiguous and non-specific", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'One codon codes for only one amino acid, hence the code is unambiguous and specific.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49643,'biology',94,789,1698,'The non-sense codon are','[{"text": "UAA", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "UAG", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "UGA", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "All of these", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'UAA, UAG and UGA are stop codons.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49644,'biology',94,789,1698,'Find out the incorrect match.<div class="matching-question"></div>','[{"text": "UUC- Phenylalanine", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "UAA- Non- Sense codon", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "GUG- Valine", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "AUG- Tryptophan", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'AUG codes for methionine.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49645,'biology',94,790,1699,'Mutations are responsible for','[{"text": "Variation in organisms", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Increasing the population rate", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Maintaining the genetic continuity", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Change in the environment", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Mutation is sudden change in chemical structure of gene or altering position of chromosomes. They occur in somatic cells or gametes genetic mutation. Hence they lead to variation in organisms.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49646,'biology',94,790,1699,'Mutations which alter nucleotide sequence within a gene are','[{"text": "Frame shift mutations", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Base pair substitutions", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both (1) and (2)", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "None of these", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Mutations which alter nucleotide sequence within a gene are Frame shift mutations and base pair substitutions',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49647,'biology',94,790,1699,'Which of the following function of DNA is necessary for the purpose of evolution?','[{"text": "Replication", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Transmission of heredity", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Mutation", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Genetic expression", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Mutation of DNA is necessary for the purpose of evolution, which causes variations.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49648,'biology',94,790,1699,'Sickle cell anaemia is a classic example of:','[{"text": "Frame shift mutation", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Point mutation", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Deletion mutation", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "sentence mutation", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Sickle cell anaemia is a classic eg. of point mutation.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49649,'biology',94,790,1699,'In sickle cell anaemia, which amino acid modification occurs in the $\\beta$-chain of amino acid?','[{"text": "Glutamate $\\\\rightarrow$ Valine", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Tyrosine $\\\\rightarrow$ Valine", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Tyrosine $\\\\rightarrow$ Glutamate", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Valine $\\\\rightarrow$ Glutamate", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Glutamic acid at the 6th position is changed to valine in beta chain in $\\mathrm{Hb}$ in sickle cell anaemia.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49650,'biology',94,790,1699,'One of the following which causes mutation is','[{"text": "Crossing over", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "X-rays", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Cosmic rays", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Colchicine", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Mutation can be artificially induced with the helps of mutagens which are classified into 2 groups. Physical and Chemical Mutagens. Physical mutagens are mainly radiations like X-rays, gamma rays. X-rays are produced in a X-ray machine when energy charged particles like cathode rays ( electrons) impinge on a suitable target like tungsten.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49651,'biology',94,791,1700,'The presence of an adapter molecule was postulated by','[{"text": "Griffith", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "James Watson", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Franklin", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Francis Crick", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'The presence of an adapter molecule was postulated by Francis Crick.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49652,'biology',94,791,1700,'Select the incorrect options<br>i. tRNA has an anticodon loop that has bases complementary to the code.<br>ii. tRNA has an amino acid acceptor end to which it binds to aminoacids.<br>iii. Watson postulated the presence of an adapter molecule that would on one hand read the code and on other hand would bind to specific amino acids.<br>iv. For termination, there is another specific tRNA that is referred to as initiator tRNA.','[{"text": "i & iv are incorrect", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "i, ii & iii are incorrect", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "i,ii, iii & iv are incorrect", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "iii & iv are incorrect", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'The presence of an adapter molecule was postulated by Francis Crick. For initiation, there is another specific tRNA that is referred to as initiator tRNA',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49653,'biology',94,791,1700,'There are no tRNAs for','[{"text": "Start codon", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Anticodon", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Initiator codon", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Stop codons", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'There are no tRNAs for stop codons.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49654,'biology',94,791,1700,'In actual structure, the tRNA is a compact molecule which looks like_____.','[{"text": "Inverted J", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Inverted T", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{L}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Inverted L", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'In actual structure, the tRNA is a compact molecule which looks like inverted L.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49655,'biology',94,791,1700,'The structure of_____ resembles Clover leaf?','[{"text": "m-RNA", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "DNA", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "r-RNA", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "t-RNA", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'The secondary structure of tRNA looks like a clover-leaf.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49656,'biology',94,791,1700,'In the following diagram the direction of anticodon is?','[{"text": "t-RNA -3\'-3\'", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "t-RNA-3\'-5\'", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "t-RNA-5\'-3\'", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "t-RNA-5\'-5\'", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'t-RNA-3\'-5\'Fig 6.12 NCERT',NULL,'Easy','MCQ type-3',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','biology/94_49656_question_1.png'),
(49657,'biology',94,791,1700,'The sequence of nitrogen bases (triplet) on tRNA is','[{"text": "Terminating codon", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Degenerate codon", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Anticodon", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Initiating codon", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'The three nitrogen base sequence (triplet) present on a tRNA strand are known as the anticodon. The triplet includes the four types of nitrogen bases found in RNA viz., cytosine, guanine, adenine and uracil.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49658,'biology',94,791,1700,'Before the genetic code was postulated, the tRNA was called','[{"text": "sRNA (sedimented RNA)", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "mRNA (messenger RNA)", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "sRNA (soluble RNA)", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "sRNA (sensitive RNA)", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'The tRNA, then called sRNA (soluble RNA), was known before the genetic code was postulated.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
