(49682,'biology',94,792,1701,'With respect to ribosome what does A & P represent.<br>','[{"text": "A - Peptidyl site $\\\\quad$ $\\\\quad$ $\\\\quad$P - t-RNA binding", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "A - tRNA-binding site $\\\\quad$ P - peptidyl site", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "A - m - RNA site &emsp; &emsp; &emsp; P - peptidyle site", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "A - t-RNA binding site $\\\\quad$ P - ATP site", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'<img src=\'/img/data/biology/94_49682_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' /> A- t RNA binding site, P- peptidyl site',NULL,'Hard','MCQ type-3',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','biology/94_49682_question_1.png'),
(49683,'biology',94,792,1701,'UTRs present on mRNA refer to','[{"text": "Unrelated regions at both 5\' end and 3\' end", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Untranslated regions at both 5\' end and 3\' end", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Untranslated regions at 5\' end", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Untranslated regions at 3\' end", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'The UTRs are present at both 5 \'-end (before start codon) and at 3\'-end (after stop codon). They are required for efficient translation process.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49684,'biology',94,792,1701,'What is incorrect for UTR?','[{"text": "Present in between the translational unit in mRNA", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "The UTRs are present at both 5\'-end (before start codon) and at 3\'-end (after stop codon)", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Required for efficient translation process", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Not identified by any tRNA", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'The UTRs are present at both 5\'-end (before start codon) and at 3\'-end (after stop codon). They are required for efficient translation process.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49685,'biology',94,792,1701,'Find out which statement with respect to translation is correct<br>I. The UTRs are present at both 5\'-end (before start codon) and at 3\'-end (after stop codon). They are required for efficient transcription process.<br>II. For initiation, the ribosome binds to the mRNA at the start codon (AUG) that is recognised only by the initiator tRNA.<br>III. The ribosome moves from codon to codon along the mRNA.<br>IV. At the end of transcription, a release factor binds to the stop codon, terminating translation','[{"text": "II & III are correct", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "I, II & III are correct", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "I, II ,III & IV are correct", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "I & IV are correct", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'The UTRs are present at both 5\'-end (before start codon) and at 3\'-end (after stop codon). They are required for efficient translation process. At the end of translation, a release factor binds to the stop codon, terminating translation and releasing the complete polypeptide from the ribosome.',NULL,'Hard','MCQ type-2',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49686,'biology',94,793,1702,'In eukaryotes, the regulation could be exerted at<br>I. Transcriptional level<br>II. Processing level<br>III. Transport of mRNA (from nucleus to cytoplasm).<br>IV. Translational level<br>Which is the correct','[{"text": "I and II", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "I and III", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "II and IV", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "I,II,III, IV", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'In eukaryotes, the regulation could be exerted at<BR>(I) transcriptional level (formation of primary transcript),<BR>(II) processing level (regulation of splicing),<BR>(III) transport of mRNA from nucleus to the cytoplasm,<BR>(IV) translational level.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49687,'biology',94,793,1702,'In prokaryotes, the predominant site for control of gene expression, is the control of','[{"text": "Processing", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Translation", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Rate of transcription initiation", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Exit of mRNA from nucleus", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'In prokaryotes, control of the rate of transcriptional initiation is the predominant site for control of gene expression.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49688,'biology',94,793,1702,'Select the correct option<br>A. Gene expression results in the formation of a polypeptide<br>B. Beta galactosidase causes hydrolysis of a tri -saccharide, lactose into galactose and glucose<br>C. The development and differentiation of embryo into adult organisms are also a result of the coordinated regulation of expression of several sets of genes','[{"text": "A & B are correct", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "A & C are correct", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Only A is correct", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Only C is correct", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Beta-galactosidase ${\\rm{(\\beta  - gal)}}$, which is primarily responsible for the hydrolysis of the disaccharide, lactose into its monomeric units, galactose and glucose.',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49689,'biology',94,793,1702,'Select the appropriate option with respect to gene regulation<br>I. The activity of RNA polymerase in transcription at a given promoter is in turn regulated by interaction with accessory proteins<br>II. The regulatory proteins in an operon can act both positively (repressors) and negatively (activators)<br>III. The operator region is adjacent to the promoter elements in most operons<br>IV. Lac operator is present only in the lac operon and it interacts specifically with lac repressor only','[{"text": "Only III is wrong", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Only II is wrong", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Only I is wrong", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "All are correct", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'The regulatory proteins can act both positively (activators) and negatively (repressors).',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
