(49853,'biology',94,782,1687,'The fact that a purine base always pairs through hydrogen bonds with a pyrimidine base in the DNA double helix leads to:','[{"text": "The antiparallel nature", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "The semiconservative nature", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Uniform width throughout DNA", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Uniform length in all DNA", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Uniform width throughout the DNA.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,'NCERT Exemplar','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49854,'biology',94,782,1687,'Both deoxyribose and ribose belong to a class of sugars called:','[{"text": "Trioses", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Hexoses", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Pentoses", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Polysaccharides", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Ribose $\\left(\\mathrm{C}_{5} \\mathrm{H}_{10} \\mathrm{O}_{5}\\right)$ is the pentose sugar in RNA, while deoxyribose $\\left(\\mathrm{C}_{5} \\mathrm{H}_{10} \\mathrm{O}_{4}\\right)$ is the pentose sugar in DNA.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,'NCERT Exemplar','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49855,'biology',94,788,1695,'The promoter site and the terminator site for transcription are located at:','[{"text": "3\' (downstream) end and 5\' (upstream) end, respectively of the transcription unit", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "5\' (upstream) end and 3\' (downstream) end, respectively of the transcription unit", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "The 5\' (upstream) end", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "The 3\' (downstream) end", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'5\' (upstream) end and 3\' (downstream) end, respectively of the transcription unit w.r.to coding strand.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,'NCERT Exemplar','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49856,'biology',94,783,1688,'The net electric charge on DNA and histones is:','[{"text": "Both positive", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both negative", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Negative and positive, respectively", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Zero", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Zero',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,'NCERT Exemplar','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49857,'biology',94,789,1698,'Which of the following is true with respect to AUG?','[{"text": "It codes for methionine only", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "It is an initiation codon", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "It codes for methionine in both prokaryotes and eukaryotes", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "All of the above", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'All are correct.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,'NCERT Exemplar','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49858,'biology',94,788,1697,'With regard to mature mRNA in eukaryotes:','[{"text": "Exons and introns do not appear in the mature RNA", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Exons appear but introns do not appear in the mature RNA", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Introns appear but exons do not appear in the mature RNA", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both exons and introns appear in the mature RNA", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Exons appear (coding sequence) but introns (Non-coding sequence) do not appear in the mature RNA',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,'NCERT Exemplar','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49859,'biology',94,782,1687,'Who amongest the following scientists had no contribution in the development of the double helix model for the structure of DNA?','[{"text": "Rosalind Franklin", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Maurice Wilkins", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Erwin Chargaff", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Meselson and Stahl", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Messelson and sathl experimentally proved that DNA replication is semi conservative',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,'NCERT Exemplar','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49860,'biology',94,794,1703,'The human chromosome with the highest and least number of genes in them are respectively:','[{"text": "Chromosome 21 and Y", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Chromosome 1 and X", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Chromosome 1 and Y", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Chromosome X and Y", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Chromosome 1 has 2968 genes, while chromosome $\\mathrm{Y}$ has 231 genes.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,'NCERT Exemplar','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49861,'biology',94,787,1694,'Discontinuous synthesis of DNA occurs in one strand, because:','[{"text": "DNA molecule being synthesised is very long", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "DNA dependent DNA polymerase catalyses polymerisation only in one direction $\\\\left(5^{\\\\prime} \\\\rightarrow 3^{\\\\prime}\\\\right)$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "It is a more efficient process", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "DNA ligase joins the short stretches of DNA", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'DNA dependent DNA polymerase catalyses polymerisation only in one direction $\\left(5^{\\prime} \\rightarrow 3^{\\prime}\\right)$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,'NCERT Exemplar','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49862,'biology',94,782,1687,'DNA is a polymer of nucleotides which are linked to each other by $3\' \\to 5\'$ phosphodiester bond. To prevent polymerisation of nucleotides, which of the following modifications would you choose?','[{"text": "Replace purine with pyrimidines", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Remove/Replace 3\' OH group in deoxy ribose", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Remove/Replace 2\' OH group with some other group in deoxy ribose", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both \'b\' and \'c\'", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Remove/Replace 3\' OH group in deoxy ribose',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,'NCERT Exemplar','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49863,'biology',94,793,1702,'Control of gene expression in prokaryotes take place at the level of:','[{"text": "DNA-replication", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Transcription", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Translation", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "None of the above", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Transcription is the first step of gene expression. In this process, a particular segment of DNA is copied into mRNA. Thus, it controls gene expression.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,'NCERT Exemplar','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49865,'biology',94,793,1702,'Which of the following statements is correct about the role of regulatory proteins in transcription in prokaryotes?','[{"text": "They only increase expression", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "They only decrease expression", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "They interact with RNA polymerase but do not affect the expression", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "They can act both as activators and as repressors", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Regulatory proteins affect the ability of RNA to recognize initiation sites. Regulatory proteins have both negative (repressor) and positive (activator) role.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,'NCERT Exemplar','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49866,'biology',94,782,1687,'While analysing the DNA of an organism a total number of 5386 nucleotides were found out of which the proportion of different bases were: Adenine $=29 \\%$, Guanine $=17 \\%$, Cytosine $=32 \\%$, Thymine $=17 \\%$. Considering the Chargaff\'s rule it can be concluded that:','[{"text": "It is a double stranded circular DNA", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "It is single stranded DNA", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "It is a double stranded linear DNA", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "No conclusion can be drawn", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Chargaff\'s rules states that DNA from any cell of all organisms should have a 1:1 ratio (base Pair Rule) of pyrimidine and purine bases. This means that the amount of guanine is equal to cytosine and the amount of adenine is equal to thymine. This pattern is found in both strands of the DNA. In this case, percentage of adenine is not equal to that of thymine and same holds true for cytosine and guanine. Hence, it is a single stranded DNA.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,'NCERT Exemplar','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49867,'biology',94,786,1691,'Which of the following are the functions of RNA.','[{"text": "It is a carrier of genetic information from DNA to ribosomes synthesising polypeptides.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "It carries amino acids to ribosomes.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "It is a constituent component of ribosomes.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "All of the above.", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'All the statements are correct.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,'NCERT Exemplar','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49868,'biology',94,787,1693,'If Meselson and Stahl\'s experiment is continued for four generations in bacteria, the ratio of $\\mathrm{N}^{15} / \\mathrm{N}^{15}: \\mathrm{N}^{15} / \\mathrm{N}^{14}: \\mathrm{N}^{14} / \\mathrm{N}^{14}$ containing DNA in the fourth generation would be:','[{"text": "$1: 1: 0$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1: 4: 0$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$0: 1: 3$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$0: 1: 7$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'The ratio of  $^{{\\rm{15}}}{\\rm{N}}{{\\rm{/}}^{{\\rm{15}}}}{\\rm{N}}$ remains zero in subsequent generation. The ratio of $^{{\\rm{15}}}{\\rm{N}}{{\\rm{/}}^{{\\rm{14}}}}{\\rm{N}}$ remains constant (remains one) and that of $^{{\\rm{14}}}{\\rm{N}}{{\\rm{/}}^{{\\rm{14}}}}{\\rm{N}}$ increases.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,'NCERT Exemplar','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49869,'biology',94,782,1687,'In some viruses, DNA is synthesised by using RNA as template. Such a DNA is called:','[{"text": "A-DNA", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "B-DNA", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "cDNA", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "rDNA", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Complementary DNA (cDNA) is double -stranded DNA synthesized from a messenger RNA (mRNA) template in a reaction catalysed by the enzyme reverse transcriptase.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,'NCERT Exemplar','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49870,'biology',94,788,1695,'The RNA polymerase holoenzyme transcribes:','[{"text": "The promoter, structural gene and the terminator region", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "The promoter and the terminator region", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "The structural gene and the terminator region", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "The structural gene only.", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Structural gene is only transcribed.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,'NCERT Exemplar','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL);
INSERT INTO `qb_questions` (`id`, `subject_id`, `chapter_id`, `topic_id`, `subtopic_id`, `question_html`, `options`, `correct_index`, `explanation`, `explanation_image_url`, `difficulty`, `qtype`, `year`, `tag`, `created_at`, `question_image_url`) VALUES
(49871,'biology',94,788,1695,'If the sequence of nitrogen bases of the coding strand of DNA in a transcription unit is:<br>5\' - A T G A A T G - 3\', the sequence of bases in its RNA transcript would be;','[{"text": "5\' - A U G AA U G - 3\'", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "5\' - UAC U UAC - 3\'", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "5\' - C A U U C A U - 3\'", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "5\' - G U AA G U A - 3\'", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Uracil in place of thymine',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,'NCERT Exemplar','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49872,'biology',94,791,1700,'The amino acid attaches to the tRNA at its:','[{"text": "5\' - end", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "3\' - end", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Anti codon site", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "DHU loop", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'The amino acid attaches to the tRNA at its: 3\' - end',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,'NCERT Exemplar','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49873,'biology',94,791,1700,'If the base sequence of a codon in mRNA is 5\'AUG-3\', the sequence of tRNA pairing with it must be:','[{"text": "5\' - UAC - 3\'", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "5\' - CAU - 3\'", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "5\' - AUG - 3\'", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "5\' - GUA - 3\'", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'5\' - CAU - 3\'',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,'NCERT Exemplar','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49874,'biology',94,793,1702,'In <i>E.coli</i>, the lac operon gets switched on when:','[{"text": "Lactose is present and it binds to the repressor", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Repressor binds to operator", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "RNA polymerase binds to the operator", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Lactose is present and it binds to RNA polymerase", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'In <i>E.coli</i>, the lac operon gets switched on when: Lactose is present and it binds to the repressor',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,'NCERT Exemplar','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49875,'biology',94,792,1701,'To initiate translation, the mRNA first binds to:','[{"text": "The smaller ribosomal sub-unit", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "The larger ribosomal sub-unit", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "The whole ribosome", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "No such specificity exists.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'To initiate translation, the mRNA first binds to: the smaller ribosomal sub-unit',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,'NCERT Exemplar','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49876,'biology',94,788,1697,'<div class="matching-question"><div class="column-left">hnRNA is larger than mRNA.<br></div><div class="column-right">hnRNA is made up of exons and introns.</div></div>','[{"text": "Statement A is correct but Statement B is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Statement A is incorrect but Statement B is correct.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both statements are correct.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Statements are incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Premature mRNA consists of the bulk of hnRNA and includes nuclear RNA transcripts which do not end up as cytoplasmic mRNA. The hnRNA contains both the introns and exons, but, the mRNA contians only the exons.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ type-2',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49877,'biology',94,789,1698,'<div class="matching-question"><div class="column-left">The genetic code normally represents DNA language.<br></div><div class="column-right">It was George Gamow, a physicist, suggested that in order to code for all the 20 amino acids, the code should be made up of three nucleotides.</div></div>','[{"text": "Statement A is correct but Statement B is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Statement A is incorrect but Statement B is correct.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both statements are correct.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Statements are incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Both are correct',NULL,'Medium','MCQ type-2',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49878,'biology',94,782,1687,'<div class="matching-question"><div class="column-left">The nitrogen bases of the two chains of DNA are held together by hydrogen bonds.<br></div><div class="column-right">Both chains of DNA are antiparallel.</div></div>','[{"text": "Statement A is correct but Statement B is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Statement A is incorrect but Statement B is correct.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both statements are correct.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Statements are incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'The two nitrogen bases of DNA are purines and pyrimidines.DNA double helix is held together by hydrogen bonds occurring between the two strands and involve a base from one strand with a base from the second in complementary pairing. In other words, the nitrogen bases form complementary strands with purine of one and pyrimidine of the other held together by $\\mathrm{H}$ - bonds. The two biopolymers strands are antiparallel, when they run parallel to each other.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ type-2',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49879,'biology',94,789,1698,'<div class="matching-question"><div class="column-left">Non-sense codons are responsible for terminating peptide chain.<br></div><div class="column-right">Non-sense codons are not recognized by tRNA.</div></div>','[{"text": "Statement A is correct but Statement B is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Statement A is incorrect but Statement B is correct.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both statements are correct.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Statements are incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'The polypeptide chains are terminated because of the three non-sense codons UAA, UAG, UGA. And during the process of translation, these triplets (nonsense codons) are not recognized by tRNA.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ type-2',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49880,'biology',94,792,1701,'<div class="matching-question"><div class="column-left">mRNA attaches to ribosome through its 3\'end.<br></div><div class="column-right">The mRNA has polyadenylate residue at the cap.</div></div>','[{"text": "Statement A is correct but Statement B is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Statement A is incorrect but Statement B is correct.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both statements are correct.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Statements are incorrect.", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Both are incorrect.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ type-2',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49881,'biology',94,789,1698,'<div class="matching-question"><div class="column-left">Amber codon is a termination codon.</div><div class="column-right">Opal is a start codon.</div></div>','[{"text": "Statement A is correct but Statement B is incorrect.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Statement A is incorrect but Statement B is correct.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both statements are correct.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Statements are incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'In a typical genetic code, UAA (ochre), UAG (amber) and UGA (opal) do not specify any amino acid so they are called termination codons or stop codons.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ type-2',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49882,'biology',94,789,1698,'<div class="matching-question"><div class="column-left">One codon codes for only one amino acid, hence, it is unambiguous and specific.<br></div><div class="column-right">The Genetic code is nearly universal.</div></div>','[{"text": "Statement A is correct but Statement B is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Statement A is incorrect but Statement B is correct.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both statements are correct.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Statements are incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Both are correct',NULL,'Medium','MCQ type-2',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49883,'biology',94,782,1687,'<div class="matching-question"><div class="column-left">Adenine cannot pair with cytosine.<br></div><div class="column-right">Because there would be two hydrogen atoms in one of the bonding positions and none at the other.</div></div>','[{"text": "Statement A is correct but Statement B is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Statement A is incorrect but Statement B is correct.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both statements are correct.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Statements are incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Adenine (purine)cannot pair with cytosine (pyrimidine) because purines do not pair with purines and pyrimidines do not pair with pyrimidines. And pyrimidines are much farther from each other and they need to come closer enough for the hydrogen bonds to be formed between them.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ type-2',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49884,'biology',94,782,1687,'<div class="matching-question"><div class="column-left">According to chargaff, the percentage of adenine is equal to the percentage of Thymine in DNA.<br></div><div class="column-right">According to chargaff, the percentage of adenine is equal to the percentage of cytosine in DNA.</div></div>','[{"text": "Statement A is correct but Statement B is incorrect.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Statement A is incorrect but Statement B is correct.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both statements are correct.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Statements are incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'According to chargaff, the percentage of adenine is equal to the percentage of Thymine in DNA.As adenine pairs with thymine.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ type-2',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49885,'biology',94,793,1702,'<div class="matching-question"><div class="column-left">The operon is a unit of gene expression.<br></div><div class="column-right">Regulation of lac operon by repressor is referred to as negative regulation.</div></div>','[{"text": "Statement A is correct but Statement B is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Statement A is incorrect but Statement B is correct.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both statements are correct.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Statements are incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Both are correct',NULL,'Medium','MCQ type-2',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49894,'biology',94,794,1703,'<div class="matching-question"><div class="column-left">The functions are unknown for over 60 per cent of the discovered genes.<br></div><div class="column-right">The human genome contains 3164.7 million nucleotide bases.</div></div>','[{"text": "Statement A is correct but Statement B is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Statement A is incorrect but Statement B is correct.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both statements are correct.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Statements are incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'The functions are unknown for over 50 per cent of the discovered genes.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ type-2',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49886,'biology',94,788,1697,'<div class="matching-question"><div class="column-left">In eukaryotes Replication and Transcription occur in the nucleus, but translation occurs in the cytoplasm.<br></div><div class="column-right">mRNA is transferred from the nucleus into the cytoplasm where ribosomes and amino acids are available for protein synthesis.</div></div>','[{"text": "Statement A is correct but Statement B is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Statement A is incorrect but Statement B is correct.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both statements are correct.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Statements are incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Replication and Transcription both takes place in the nucleus of eukaryotic cells, as they are in need of DNA and many other enzymes. But translation (i.e., synthesis of protein from RNA) occurs in the cytoplasm, here ribosome amino acids along with mRNA and other proteins also participate.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ type-2',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49887,'biology',94,791,1700,'<div class="matching-question"><div class="column-left">The tRNA molecules possess anticodons.<br></div><div class="column-right">In actual structure, the tRNA is a compact molecule which looks like inverted L.</div></div>','[{"text": "Statement A is correct but Statement B is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Statement A is incorrect but Statement B is correct.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both statements are correct.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Statements are incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Every tRNA has an anticodon that can form base pair with a codon. Specific anticodons have advanced bases which can pair with more than one codon, specifying the same amino acid. In actual structure, the tRNA is a compact molecule which looks like inverted L.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ type-2',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49888,'biology',94,792,1701,'<div class="matching-question"><div class="column-left">Proteins are polymers of amino acids.<br></div><div class="column-right">Nucleic acids are polymers of nucleotides.</div></div>','[{"text": "Statement A is correct but Statement B is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Statement A is incorrect but Statement B is correct.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both statements are correct.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Statements are incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Proteins are polymers of amino acids that are joined together by peptide bonds. Whereas, nucleic acids are polymers of individual nucleotide monomers. Between the phosphate and sugar segments of each nucleotide, phosphodiester linkages formed.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ type-2',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49889,'biology',94,792,1701,'<div class="matching-question"><div class="column-left">A single-strand of mRNA is capable of forming different polypeptide chains in prokaryotes.<br></div><div class="column-right">Termination codons occur in polypeptide.</div></div>','[{"text": "Statement A is correct but Statement B is incorrect.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Statement A is incorrect but Statement B is correct.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both statements are correct.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Statements are incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Termination codon occurs in m RNA',NULL,'Medium','MCQ type-2',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49890,'biology',94,792,1701,'<div class="matching-question"><div class="column-left">DNA code is copied in the synthesis of mRNA</div><div class="column-right">tRNA containing codon moves out of nucleus and after attaching on ribosomes form the template.</div></div>','[{"text": "Statement A is correct but Statement B is incorrect.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Statement A is incorrect but Statement B is correct.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both statements are correct.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Statements are incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'In the transcription process, the DNA of a gene works as a template for complementary base-pairing, and an enzyme RNA polymerase II catalyzes the pre-mRNA molecule formation. This leads to the synthesis of mature mRNA. The mRNA formed is a single-stranded copy of the gene, which is to be be translated into a protein molecule in the next step.tRNAs are present in all cells. The adaptor role of the tRNAs includes two separate regions of the molecule. By the anticodon loop the mRNA template is then recognized, that is located at the other end of the folded tRNA, which binds to the appropriate codon by complementary base pairing.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ type-2',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49891,'biology',94,789,1698,'<div class="matching-question"><div class="column-left">Genetic code is nonoverlapping.<br></div><div class="column-right">No single base take part in the formation of more than one codon.</div></div>','[{"text": "Statement A is correct but Statement B is incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Statement A is incorrect but Statement B is correct.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both statements are correct.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both Statements are incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Both are correct',NULL,'Medium','MCQ type-2',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49892,'biology',94,783,1688,'<div class="matching-question"><div class="column-left">Histones are basic proteins of major importance in packaging of eukaryotic DNA, DNA and histones comprise chromatin forming the bulk of eukaryotic chromosome.<br></div><div class="column-right">Histones are five major types $\\mathrm{H}_{1}, \\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{~A}, \\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{~B}, \\mathrm{H}_{3 \\mathrm{~A}}$ and $\\mathrm{H}_{4 \\mathrm{~A}}$.</div></div>','[{"text": "Statement A is correct but Statement B is incorrect.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Statement A is incorrect but Statement B is correct.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both statements are correct.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Statements are incorrect.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'In eukaryotes, nucleosomes are basic unit of DNA packaging, consists of DNA segment wrapped around eight histone protein cores in a sequence. DNA forms a nucleosome along with histone octamer which consists a big portion of chromatin. Generally, Histones are five major types $\\mathrm{H}_{1}, \\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{~A}, \\mathrm{H}_{2} \\mathrm{~B}, \\mathrm{H}_{3}$ and $\\mathrm{H}_{4}$.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ type-2',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
