(49992,'biology',94,782,1687,'Complete the flow chart on central dogman.<br>','[{"text": "$\\\\begin{array}{l}\\n\\\\begin{array}{*{20}{c}}\\n{{\\\\rm{(a)  -  Replication;}}\\\\,}&{\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,}&{{\\\\rm{(b)  -  Transcription;}}}\\n\\\\end{array}\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\begin{array}{*{20}{c}}\\n{{\\\\rm{(c)  -  Transduction;}}}&{\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,}&{{\\\\rm{(d)  -  Protein}}\\\\,\\\\,}\\n\\\\end{array}\\n\\\\end{array}$<br><br>", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\begin{array}{l}\\n\\\\begin{array}{*{20}{c}}\\n{{\\\\rm{(a)  -  Translation;}}\\\\,\\\\,}&{\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,}&{{\\\\rm{(b)  -  Replication;}}}\\n\\\\end{array}\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\begin{array}{*{20}{c}}\\n{{\\\\rm{(c)  -  Transcription;}}}&{\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,}&{{\\\\rm{(d)  -  Transduction}}}\\n\\\\end{array}\\n\\\\end{array}$<br><br>", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\begin{array}{l}\\n\\\\begin{array}{*{20}{c}}\\n{{\\\\rm{(a)  -  Replication;}}\\\\,\\\\,}&{\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,}&{{\\\\rm{(b)  -  Transcription;}}}\\n\\\\end{array}\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\begin{array}{*{20}{c}}\\n{{\\\\rm{(c)  -  Translation;}}\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,}&{\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,}&{{\\\\rm{(d)  -  Protein}}\\\\,\\\\,}\\n\\\\end{array}\\n\\\\end{array}$<br><br>", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\begin{array}{l}\\n\\\\begin{array}{*{20}{c}}\\n{{\\\\rm{(a)  -  Transduction;}}}&{\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,}&{{\\\\rm{(b)  -  Translation;}}}\\n\\\\end{array}\\\\\\\\\\n\\\\begin{array}{*{20}{c}}\\n{{\\\\rm{(c)  -  Replication;}}\\\\,\\\\,}&{\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,\\\\,}&{{\\\\rm{(d)  -  Protein;}}}\\n\\\\end{array}\\n\\\\end{array}$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Central Dogma consists of Replication, Transcription, and Translation which are the threemain processes used by all cells to maintain their genetic information and to convert the genetic information encoded in DNA into gene products, which are either RNAs or proteins, depending on the gene.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ type-3',2021,'NEET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','biology/94_49992_question_1.png'),
(49993,'biology',94,788,1697,'Identify the correct statement.','[{"text": "In capping, methyl guanosine triphosphate is added to the 3 \' end of hnRNA.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "RNA polymerase binds with Rho factor to terminate the process of transcription in bacteria.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "The coding strand in a transcription unit is copies to an mRNA.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Split gene arrangement is characteristic of prokaryotes.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'The rho factor binds to RNA polymerase and terminates transcription',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2021,'NEET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49994,'biology',94,782,1687,'If Adenine makes $30 \\%$ of the DNA molecule, what will be the percentage of Thymine, Guanine and Cytosine in it?','[{"text": "$\\\\mathrm{T}: 20 ; \\\\mathrm{G}: 30 ; \\\\mathrm{C}: 20$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{T}: 20 ; \\\\mathrm{G}: 20 ; \\\\mathrm{C}: 30$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{T}: 30 ; \\\\mathrm{G}: 20 ; \\\\mathrm{C}: 20$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{T}: 20 ; \\\\mathrm{G}: 25 ; \\\\mathrm{C}: 25$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'According to Chargaff\'s rule, adenine always pair with thymine with double hydrogen bond and Guanine always pair with cytosine with triple hydrogen bond. So if Adenine is $30 \\%$, then Thymine also will be $30 \\%$. Remaining $40 \\%$ of DNA is equally shared by Guanine and cytosine which makes $20 \\%$ each.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2021,'NEET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49995,'biology',94,792,1701,'Which of the following RNAs is not required for the synthesis of protein?','[{"text": "mRNA", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "tRNA", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "rRNA", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "siRNA<br>", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'siRNAs are highly specific and usually synthesized to reduce the translation of specific messenger RNAs (mRNAs).',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2021,'NEET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49996,'biology',94,788,1697,'Which is the "Only enzyme" that has "Capability" to catalyse Initiation, Elongation and Termination in the process of transcription in prokaryotes?<br>','[{"text": "DNA dependent DNA polymerase", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "DNA dependent RNA polymerase", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "DNA Ligase", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "DNase", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'The transcription of the genetic information of the DNA-base-sequences into RNAstructure is performed by the DNA-dependent RNApolymerase',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2021,'NEET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49997,'biology',94,783,1688,'Which one of the following statements about Histones is wrong?<br>','[{"text": "Histones are organized to form a unit of 8 molecules.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "The $\\\\mathrm{pH}$ of histones is slightly acidic", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Histones are rich in amino acids - Lysine and Arginine.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Histones carry positive charge in the side chain.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'histones are basic',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2021,'NEET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49998,'biology',94,789,1698,'<div class="matching-question"><div class="column-left">The codon \'AUG\' codes for methionine and phenylalanine.<br></div><div class="column-right">\'AAA\' and \'AAG\' both codons code for the amino acid lysine.<br><br>In the light of the above statements, choose the correct answer from the options given below.<br></div></div>','[{"text": "Both Statement I and Statement II are true", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Statement I and Statement II are false", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Statement I is correct but Statement II is false", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is true", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'AUG codes for methionine whereas AAA & AAG will code for lysine',NULL,'Medium','MCQ type-2',2021,'NEET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(49999,'biology',94,795,1704,'DNA fingerprinting involves identifying differences in some specific regions in DNA sequence, called as:<br>','[{"text": "Satellite DNA", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Repetitive DNA", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Single nucleotides", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Polymorphic DNA", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'DNA fingerprinting involves with identifying differences in some specific regions in DNA sequences called as repetitive DNA because in these sequences a small stretch of DNA is repeated many times.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2021,'NEET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(50000,'biology',94,792,1701,'The process of translation of mRNA to proteins begins as soon as:<br>','[{"text": "The larger subunit of ribosome encounters mRNA", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both the subunits join together to bind with mRNA", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "The tRNA is activated and the larger subunit of ribosome encounters mRNA", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "The small subunit of ribosome encounters mRNA", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'The process of translation of mRNA to protein synthesis begins as soon as the smaller sub-unit of ribosome encounters mRNA.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2022,'NEET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(50001,'biology',94,783,1688,'Read the following statements and choose the set of correct statements :<br>(a) Euchromatin is loosely packed chromatin<br>(b) Heterochromatin is transcriptionally active<br>(c) Histone octomer is wrapped by negatively charged DNA in nucleosome<br>(d) Histones are rich in lysine and arginine<br>(e) A typical nucleosome contains 400 bp of DNA helix<br>Choose the correct answer from the options given below:','[{"text": "(a),(c),(d) Only", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "(b), (e) Only", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "(a),(c),(e) only", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "(b), (d), (e) Only", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Heterochromatin is transcriptionally inactive. A typical nucleosome contains 200 of DNA helix.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2022,'NEET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(50002,'biology',94,795,1704,'DNA polymorphism forms the basis of :<br>','[{"text": "DNA finger printing", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both genetic mapping and DNA finger printing", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Translation", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Genetic mapping", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'DNA polymorphism forms the basis of genetic mapping and DNA finger printing.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2022,'NEET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(50003,'biology',94,794,1703,'If a geneticist uses the blind approach for sequencing the whole genome of an organism, followed by assignment of function to different segments, the methodology adopted by him is called as :<br>','[{"text": "Gene mapping", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Expressed sequence tags", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Bioinformatics", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Sequence annotation", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'In the human genome project, the blind approach of simply sequencing the whole set of genome that contained all the coding and non-coding sequence, and later assigning different regions in the sequence with functions is referred to as sequence annotation',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2022,'NEET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(50004,'biology',94,787,1693,'Ten <i>E.coli</i> cells with ${ }^{15} \\mathrm{~N}$-dsDNA are incubated in medium containing ${ }^{14} \\mathrm{~N}$ nucleotide. After 60 minutes, how many <i>E.coli</i> cells will have DNA totally free from ${ }^{15} \\mathrm{~N}$ ?','[{"text": "40 cells", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "60 cells", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "80 cells", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "20 cells<br>", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Initially we have $^{{\\rm{15}}}{{\\rm{N}}^{{\\rm{15}}}}{\\rm{N}}$. After 1 st generation in medium containing $\\mathrm{N}^{14}$ nucleotide we will get, ${{\\rm{2}}^{{\\rm{15}}}}{{\\rm{N}}^{{\\rm{14}}}}{\\rm{N}}$ DNA After 2nd generation in medium containing $\\mathrm{N}^{14}$ nucleotide we will get, $2{ }^{15} \\mathrm{~N}^{14} \\mathrm{~N}$ DNA and $4{ }^{14} \\mathrm{~N}^{14} \\mathrm{~N}$ DNA.After 3rd generation in medium containing $\\mathrm{N}^{14}$ nucleotide we will get, ${{\\rm{2}}^{{\\rm{15}}}}{{\\rm{N}}^{{\\rm{14}}}}{\\rm{N}}$ DNA and ${\\rm{6}}{{\\rm{ }}^{{\\rm{14}}}}{{\\rm{N}}^{{\\rm{14}}}}{\\rm{N}}$ DNA.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2022,'NEET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(50005,'biology',94,793,1702,'In an E.coli strain i gene gets mutated and its product can not bind the inducer molecule. If growth medium is provided with lactose, what will be the outcome?<br>','[{"text": "z, y, a genes will be transcribed", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "z, y a genes will not be translated", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "RNA polymerase will bind the promoter region", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Only z gene will get transcribed", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'In an <i>E.coli</i> strain I gene gets mutated and its product cannot bind the inducer molecule. If growth medium is provided with lactose then z, y, a genes will not be translated.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2022,'NEET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(50006,'biology',94,782,1687,'If the length of a DNA molecule is 1.1 metres, what will be the approximate number of base pairs?<br>','[{"text": "${\\\\rm{6}}{\\\\rm{.6 \\\\times 1}}{{\\\\rm{0}}^{\\\\rm{9}}}\\\\,{\\\\rm{bp}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{3}}{\\\\rm{.3 \\\\times 1}}{{\\\\rm{0}}^{\\\\rm{6}}}\\\\,{\\\\rm{bp}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{6}}{\\\\rm{.6 \\\\times 1}}{{\\\\rm{0}}^{\\\\rm{6}}}\\\\,{\\\\rm{bp}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{3}}{\\\\rm{.3 \\\\times 1}}{{\\\\rm{0}}^{\\\\rm{9}}}\\\\,{\\\\rm{bp}}$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'If the length of DNA molecule is 1.1 metres, then the approximate number of base pairs would be ${\\rm{3}}{\\rm{.3 \\times 1}}{{\\rm{0}}^{\\rm{9}}}\\,{\\rm{bp}}$.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2022,'NEET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(50007,'biology',94,788,1695,'During transcription the DNA strand with 3’ →\n5’ polarity of the structural gene always acts as\na template because','[{"text": "Enzyme DNA dependent RNA polymerase\\nalways catalyse the polymerisation in\\n3’ → 5’ direction", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Enzyme DNA dependent RNA polymerase\\nalways catalyse polymerisation in both\\nthe directions", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Nucleotides of DNA strand with 5’ → 3’\\nare transferred to mRNA", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Enzyme DNA dependent RNA polymerase\\nalways catalyse the polymerisation in\\n5’ → 3’ direction", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'During transcription the DNA strand with\n3’ → 5’ polarity of the structural gene always\nacts as a template because Enzyme DNA\ndependent RNA polymerase always catalyse\nthe polymerization in 5‘→ 3’ direction',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2023,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(50008,'biology',94,784,1689,'Unequivocal proof that DNA is the genetic material was first proposed by','[{"text": "Frederick Griffith", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Alfred Hershey and Martha Chase", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Avery, MacLeod and McCarthy", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Wilkins and Franklin", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'The unequivocal proof that DNA is the genetic material came from the experiment of Alfred Hershey and Martha Chase. They used bactriophage and E.coli.<br>The transformation experiments by using Pneumococcus was conducted by Frederick Griffith.<br>Avery, Macleoid and McCarty gave the biochemical characterisation of Transforming Principle. Wilkins and Franklin produced X-ray diffraction data of DNA.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2023,'NEET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(50009,'biology',94,788,1697,'What is the role of RNA polymerase III in the process of transcription in Eukaryotes?','[{"text": "Transcription of rRNAs (28S, 18S and 5.8S)", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Transcription of tRNA, 5S rRNA and snRNA", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Transcription of precursor of mRNA", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Transcription of only snRNAs", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'In eukaryotes there are three major types of RNA polymerases.<br>RNA polymerase I transcribes : 5.8S, 18S, 28S rRNAs<br>RNA polymerase II transcribes : hnRNAs (precursor of mRNA)<br>RNA polymerase III transcribes : tRNAs, ScRNA, 5S rRNA and snRNA',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2023,'NEET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(50010,'biology',94,794,1703,'Expressed Sequence Tags (ESTs) refers to','[{"text": "All genes that are expressed as RNA.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "All genes that are expressed as proteins.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "All genes whether expressed or\\nunexpressed.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Certain important expressed genes", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'All the genes that are expressed as RNA\nare referred to as Expressed Sequence Tags\n(ESTs).',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2023,'NEET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(50011,'biology',94,792,1701,'How many different proteins does the ribosome consist of?','[{"text": "80", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "60", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "40", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "20", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'The ribosome consists of structural RNAs\nand about 80 different proteins.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2023,'NEET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(50012,'biology',94,793,1702,'Match Column- I with Column- II<br>Choose the correct answer from the options given below:<div class="matching-question"><div class="column-left">A. Gene ‘a’<br>B. Gene ‘y’<br>C. Gene ‘i’<br>D. Gene ‘z’</div><div class="column-right">P. β-galactosidase<br>Q. Transacetylase<br>R. Permease<br>S. Repressor protein</div></div>','[{"text": "A-Q, B-P, C-S, D-R", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "A-Q, B-R, C-S, D-P", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "A-R, B-S, C-P, D-Q", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "A-R, B-P, C-S, D-Q", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'In a <i>lac</i> operon,\nGene a codes for enzyme transacetylase.\nGene y codes for enzyme permease.\n<br>Gene i codes for repressor protein.\n<br>Gene z codes for enzyme β-galactosidase.',NULL,'Medium','Match the following',2023,'NEET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(50013,'biology',94,783,1688,'Given below are two statements:<div class="matching-question"><div class="column-left">In prokaryotes, the positively charged DNA is held with some negatively charged proteins in a region called nucleoid.</div><div class="column-right">In eukaryotes, the negatively charged DNA is wrapped around the positively charged histone octamer to form nucleosome.<br>In the light of the above statements, choose the correct answer from the options given below:</div></div>','[{"text": "Both Statement A and Statement B are\\ntrue", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both Statement A and Statement B are\\nfalse", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Statement A is correct but Statement B is\\nfalse.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Statement A is incorrect but Statement B\\nis true.", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'In prokaryotes, the negatively charged\nDNA is held with some positively charged\nproteins in a region termed as nucleoid.<br>\nIn eukaryotes, the negatively charged DNA is\nwrapped around the positively charged histone\noctamer to form a structure called nucleosome.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ type-2',2023,'NEET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(50014,'biology',94,788,1695,'Which one of the following is the sequence on corresponding coding strand, if the sequence on mRNA formed is as follows<br>5’AUCGAUCGAUCGAUCGAUCGAUCG AUCG 3’?','[{"text": "5’UAGCUAGCUAGCUAGCUAGC\\nUAGCUAGC 3’", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "3’UAGCUAGCUAGCUAGCUAGC\\nUAGCUAGC 5’", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "5’ATCGATCGATCGATCGATCG\\nATCGATCG 3’", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "3’ATCGATCGATCGATCGATCG\\nATCGATCG 5’", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'The sequence of coding strand is same as\nRNA except thymine at the place of uracil.\n<br>Template strand → 3’-TAGCTAGCTAGCTA\nGCTAGCTAGCTAGC-5’<br>\nCoding strand → 5’-ATCGATCGATCG\nATCGATCGATCGATCG-3’\n <br>↓Transcription\n<br>mRNA → 5’\nAUCGAUCGAUCGAUCGAUCGAUCG\nAUCG 3’',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2023,'NEET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(50015,'biology',94,781,1686,'The sequence of events mentioned below are symbolised by alphabets. Choose the correct answer where the alphabets are matched with the processes.<br>\\[({\\text{RNA}}\\xrightarrow{{\\text{A}}}{\\text{DNA}}\\xrightarrow{{\\text{B}}}{\\text{DNA}}\\xrightarrow{{\\text{C}}})<br>{\\text{mRNA }}(\\xrightarrow{{\\text{D}}}){\\text{Polypeptide}}\\]<div class="matching-question"></div>','[{"text": "A - Replication, B - Transcription, C - Translation, D - Transduction", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "A - Reverse transcription, B - Translation, C - transcription, D - Replication", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "A - Replication, B - Transformation, C - Transcription, D - Translation", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "A - Reverse transcription,<br>B - Replication,<br>C - Transcription, D - Translation.", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'• Reverse transcription is the synthesis of DNA from an RNA template by reverse transcriptase.<br>• The replication process relies on the fact that each strand of DNA can serve as a template for duplication by DNA polymerase.<br>• Transcription involves copying a gene\'s DNA sequence to make an RNA molecule by RNA polymerase<br>• Translation is the process by which a protein is synthesized from the information contained in a molecule of messenger RNA (mRNA).',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2008,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(50016,'biology',94,793,1702,'According to the lac operon concept, which functional unit of the bacterial gene material is responsible for suppressing the activity of the operator gene in the absence of lactose?','[{"text": "Promoter gene", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Repressor protein", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Regulator gene", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Structural gene", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'In the absence of lactose, the regulator i gene codes for active repressor protein and produces it all the time. The repressor protein binds to the lac operator. This binding prevents RNA polymerase from catalyzing the transcription of the three structural genes. The enzymes for using lactose are not produced.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2008,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(50017,'biology',94,787,1694,'When DNA replication starts:','[{"text": "The hydrogen bonds between the nucleotides of two strands break", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "The phosphodiester bonds between the adjacent nucleotides break", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "The bonds between the nitrogen base and deoxyribose sugar break", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "The leading strand produces Okazaki fragments", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Two strands of the double helix are held together by hydrogen bonds between the bases that are weak and readily broken. DNA is replicated by uncoiling of the helix, strand separation by breaking of the hydrogen bonds between the complementary strands, and synthesis of two new strands by complementary base pairing.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2009,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(50018,'biology',94,788,1695,'Compare the Statements A and B.<div class="matching-question"><div class="column-left">A monocistronic mRNA can produce several types of polypeptide chains.<br></div><div class="column-right">The terminator codon is present on the mRNA.<br>Select the correct description.</div></div>','[{"text": "Statement A is correct, and Statement B is wrong", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Statement A is wrong and Statement B is correct.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both the statements are correct.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both the statements are wrong.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Eukaryotic mRNA is called monocistronic, i.e., formed from one gene. They contain only one initiation site AUG, that presented one mRNA-one polypeptide chain hypothesis. The terminator codon is present on mRNA molecule, which stops the formation of a polypeptide chain at that point. The three termination codons are UGA, UAA and UAG.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ type-2',2009,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(50019,'biology',94,795,1704,'Blood stains are found at the site of a murder. If DNA profiling technique is to be used for identifying the criminal, which of the following is ideal for use?','[{"text": "Erythrocytes", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Leukocytes", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Platelets", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Serum", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'From the given options, leukocytes are to be used for identifying the criminal because they are nucleated, whereas erythrocytes are enucleated.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2009,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(50020,'biology',94,794,1703,'The main aim of the human genome project is:','[{"text": "To identify and sequence all the genes present in human DNA", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "To introduce new genes into humans", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "To remove disease causing genes from human DNA", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "To develop better techniques for comparing two different human DNA samples", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'The initial goals of the Human Genome Project were:<br>• To map all the human genes and identify 20000-25000 genes in human DNA.<br>• To determine the nucleotide sequences of 3 billion base pairs.<br>• To construct a detailed physical map of the entire human genome.<br>• To store all information in databases.<br>• To improve data analysis tools.- to transfer the related technologies to relevant industries.<br>• To analyse and address ethical, legal and social issues arising from this new genetic knowledge and develop policy options for the public to consider.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2010,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(50021,'biology',94,788,1697,'Compare the statements I and II.<br>Statement I : RNA produced during transcription in eukaryotic cells cannot be straight away used in photosynthesis.<br>Statement II : RNA splicing phenomena helps in the removal of exons.<br>Choose the correct description.','[{"text": "Both the statements I and II are correct.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both the statements I and II are wrong.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Statement I is wrong and Statement II is correct.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Statement I is correct and Statement II is wrong.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Transcription is the formation of RNA from a DNA template. The RNA may be rRNA or tRNA which are used directly or mRNA which is translated into protein. The primary transcript from a typical eukaryotic gene contains introns as well as exons. Introns are removed from this primary transcript or pre mRNA by a process, called RNA splicing.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2010,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(50022,'biology',94,795,1704,'In genetic fingerprinting, the \'probe\' refers to:','[{"text": "A radioactively labelled single stranded RNA molecule", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "A radioactively labelled single stranded DNA molecule", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "A radioactively labelled double stranded DNA molecule", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "A radioactively labelled double stranded RNA molecule", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'DNA fingerprints are specific banding patterns of genomic DNA produced when it is cleaved with a specific restriction enzyme and hybridised to appropriate DNA probes which are small segments of radioactive DNA that help in locating a specific gene in a DNA sequence.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2010,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(50023,'biology',94,788,1696,'Which one of the following is correct?','[{"text": "Introns are present in mRNA and exons are present in tRNA.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Codons are present in mRNA and anticodons in tRNA.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Every intron is a set of three terminator codons.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Exons are present in eukaryotes while introns are present in prokaryotes.", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'The mRNA encodes a protein as a series of contiguous codons, each of which is recognized by a particular tRNA. One end of the tRNA matches the genetic code in a three nucleotide sequence called the anticodon.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2011,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(50024,'biology',94,789,1698,'In genetic code, 61 codons code for 20 different types of amino acids. This is called:','[{"text": "Colinearity", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Commaless", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Degeneracy", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Non-ambiguity", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Degeneracy of codons is the redundancy of the genetic code, exhibited as the multiplicity of three-base pair codon combinations that specify an amino acid.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2011,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(50025,'biology',94,786,1691,'The term, genetic RNA refers to:','[{"text": "Genetic material of RNA viruses", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "The RNA that carries genetic message", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "The RNA that helps gene regulation in lac-operon", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "The RNA present in mitochondria", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Genetic RNAs are those that carry genetic information of the organism. It is found in viruses generally.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2011,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(50026,'biology',94,795,1704,'The term, \'southern blotting\' refers to:','[{"text": "Transfer of DNA fragments from in vitro cellulose membrane to electrophoresis gel", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Attachment of probes to DNA fragments", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Transfer of DNA fragments from electrophoresis gel to nitrocellulose sheet", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Comparison of DNA fragments from two sources", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'A Southern blot is a method used in molecular biology for detection of a specific DNA sequence in DNA samples. Southern blotting combines transfer of electrophoresis separated DNA fragments to a filter nitrocellulose membrane and subsequent fragment detection by probe hybridization.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2011,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(50027,'biology',94,789,1698,'Identify the sense codon from the following.','[{"text": "UGA", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "AUG", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "UAG", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "UAA", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'UAG, UAA and UGA are stop codons (or non-sense codons) as they do not specify amino acids . AUG has dual role to play. It serves both as an initiator codon as well as codon for methionine and thus is a sense codon.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2012,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(50028,'biology',94,793,1702,'The Lac Operon is turned on when allolactose molecules bind to:','[{"text": "Promoter site", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Operator site", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "mRNA", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Repressor protein", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'The regulator i gene codes for active repressor protein but lactose or allolactose binds to the repressor protein and inactivates it thereby preventing it from binding to the lac operator. RNA polymerase can now access the promoter and transcribe the structural genes.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2012,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(50029,'biology',94,788,1697,'The most unstable RNA is:','[{"text": "Messenger RNA", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Soluble RNA", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Ribosomal RNA", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Heterogeneous nuclear RNA", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'hn RNA is the primary transcript or the precursor of mRNA. It is short-lived and undergoes processing for forming functional mRNA.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2013,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(50030,'biology',94,788,1696,'The portion of an Eukaryotic gene which is transcribed but not translated is:','[{"text": "Exon", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Intron", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Cistron", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Codon", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'In most eukaryotic genes, coding regions (exons) are interrupted by noncoding regions (introns). During transcription, the entire gene is copied into a pre-mRNA, which includes exons and introns. During the process of RNA splicing, introns are removed, and exons joined to form a contiguous coding sequence and are translated.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2013,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(50031,'biology',94,788,1695,'Read the Statements A and B. Choose the right one.<br><div class="matching-question"><div class="column-left">Synthesis of mRNA takes place in $5^{\\prime} \\rightarrow 3^{\\prime}$ direction.<br></div><div class="column-right">Reading of mRNA is always in $3^{\\prime} \\rightarrow 5^{\\prime}$ direction.</div></div>','[{"text": "Both the statements are wrong.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Statement A is wrong, Statement B is correct.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Statement B is wrong, Statement A is correct.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both the statements A and B are correct.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'The antisense strand of DNA which runs from 3\'-5\' direction act as a template for RNA synthesis by RNA polymerase. So, the RNA is synthesised in 5\'-3\' direction. During translation, the ribosomes attach to the $5^{\\prime}$ end of the m-RNA and transcribe till the $3^{\\prime}$ end. So, the reading of mRNA takes place in $5^{\\prime}-3^{\\prime}$ direction..',NULL,'Medium','MCQ type-2',2013,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(50032,'biology',94,787,1694,'The sequence of nitrogenous bases in one strand of DNA are 3\'TACGCGACG5\'.. The complementary DNA strand should have:','[{"text": "5\' AUG CGCTGC 3\'", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "3\' ATG CGC TGC 5\'", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$5^{\\\\prime}$ UACGCG ACG $3 \'$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$5^{\\\\prime}$ ATGCGCTGC3\'", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'The complementary DNA strand should have $5^{\\prime}$ ATGCGCTGC 3\'. It is because adenine pairs with thymine and cytosine pairs with guanine.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2013,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(50033,'biology',94,784,1689,'The result of the following reaction/experiment carried out by Avery et al., on <i>Streptococcus pneumoniae</i> has proved conclusively that DNA is the genetic material:','[{"text": "Live \'R\' strain + DNA from \' S \' strain + DNAase", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Heat killed \'R\' strain + DNA from \' S \' strain+ DNAase", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Live \' \'R\' \' strain +DNA from \' S \' strain + RNAase", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Live \' \'R\' \' strain + Denatured DNA of \' S \' strain + protease", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'The group of mice injected with a mixture of live $\\mathrm{R}$ strain and heat-killed $\\mathrm{S}$ strain of pneumococci, neither of which is lethal by itself. Some of the mice which received the mixture also died of pneumonia. The live $\\mathrm{S}$ strain as well as $\\mathrm{R}$ strain bacteria were recovered from the dead mice. It was found that only DNA was responsible for transformation because when DNA was treated with DNase (which degraded DNA), transformation was not possible.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2014,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(50034,'biology',94,793,1702,'Which of the following events would occur in \'Lac-operon\' of <i>E. coli</i> when the growth medium has high concentration of lactose?','[{"text": "The structural genes fail to produce polycistronic mRNA.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "The repressor protein binds to RNA polymerase and prevents translation.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "The repressor protein attaches to the promoter sequence and derepresses the operator.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "The inducer molecule binds to repressor protein and RNA polymerase binds to promoter sequence.", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'The regulator i gene codes for active repressor protein but lactose or allolactose binds to the repressor protein and - vates it thereby preventing it from binding to the lac operator. RNA polymerase can now access the promoter and transcribe the structureal genes. Enzymes needed for using lactose are made.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2014,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(50035,'biology',94,788,1697,'Read the statements and choose the correct option.<br><div class="matching-question"><div class="column-left">The primary transcript produced in eukaryotes is translated without undergoing any modification or processing.<br></div><div class="column-right">The hnRNA in humans has exons and introns.</div></div>','[{"text": "Both the statements I and II are correct", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both the statements I and II are wrong.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Statement II is correct and statement I is wrong.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Statement I is correct and statement II is wrong.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'In eukaryotes, the genes contain noncoding sequences (introns) intervening between coding sequences (exons). These genes are hence called split genes. Eukaryotic hnRNA transcripts can therefore undergo extensive post-transcriptional processing while still in the nucleus. These introns are removed from the RNA transcripts by a mechanism called RNA splicing during RNA processing prior to their transport to the cytoplasm for translation.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ type-2',2014,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(50044,'biology',94,789,1698,'The codons UUU and UUC codes for phenylalanine only. This feature of genetic code is called:','[{"text": "Comma- less", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Non-overlapping", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Degenerate", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Non-ambiguous", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'The genetic code is degenerate. Except the two amino acids methionine and tryptophan, all others are specified by more than one codon. Two codons UUU and UUC specify phenylalanine. Six different codons specify the three amino acids leucine, serine and arginine. Three codons specify isoleucine.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2016,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(50036,'biology',94,795,1704,'Some of the steps of DNA fingerprinting are given below. Identify the correct sequence from the options given.<br>A. Electrophoresis of DNA fragments<br>B. Hybridisation with DNA probe<br>C. Digestion of DNA by RENs<br>D. Autoradiography<br>E. Blotting of DNA fragments to nitrocellulose membrane','[{"text": "$\\\\mathrm{C}-\\\\mathrm{A}-\\\\mathrm{E}-\\\\mathrm{B}-\\\\mathrm{D}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{A}-\\\\mathrm{C}-\\\\mathrm{E}-\\\\mathrm{D}-\\\\mathrm{B}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{C}-\\\\mathrm{A}-\\\\mathrm{B}-\\\\mathrm{E}-\\\\mathrm{D}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\mathrm{A}-\\\\mathrm{E}-\\\\mathrm{C}-\\\\mathrm{B}-\\\\mathrm{D}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'The various steps in DNA fingerprinting are:<br>• DNA isolated from samples is digested with restriction endonucleases.<br>• The DNA molecules are separated by gel electrophoe<br>• They are then transferred onto nitrocellulose or nylon membranes for immobilization, a process called Southern blot.<br>• A radioactively labeled VNTR DNA probe containing the sequence of interest is then hybridized with the immobilized DNA on the membrane.<br>• Hybridization takes place with only those DNA molecules that contain a nucleotide sequence complementary to the sequence of the probe. Non-hybridized probe is then washed off the membrane.<br>• The washed membrane is exposed to X-ray film to detect the presence of the radioactivity. Hence, it can be detected by autoradiography.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2014,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(50037,'biology',94,789,1698,'The codon AUG has dual function. It is an initiation codon and also codes for:','[{"text": "Formaldehyde", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Methionine", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Phenylalanine", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Serine", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'The codon AUG has dual functions. It codes for methionine(met), and also acts as initiation codon.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2015,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(50038,'biology',94,795,1704,'Match the following.<div class="matching-question"><div class="column-left">A. VNTR<br>B. Introns and exons<br>C. Dystrophin<br>D. Satellite DNA</div><div class="column-right">P. Largest gene<br>Q. DNA\nfingerprinting<br>R. Bulk DNA<br>S. Splicing</div></div>','[{"text": "${\\\\text{A - Q; B - S;C - P;D - R}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${\\\\text{A - S; B - P;C - Q;D - R}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\text{A - R;B - S;C - P;D - Q}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\text{A - Q;B - P;C - S;D - R}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'A-Q, B-S , C-P, D-R<br>${\\text{(A)}}\\,{\\text{VNTR}}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{\\text{(Q)}}\\,{\\text{DNA}}\\,{\\text{fingerprinting}}$<br>$\\begin{array}{*{20}{l}}\n  {{\\text{(B)}}\\,{\\text{Introns}}\\,{\\text{and}}\\,{\\text{Exons }}}&{{\\text{ }}\\,{\\text{(S)}}\\,{\\text{Splicing }}} \n\\end{array}$<br>$\\begin{array}{*{20}{l}}\n  {{\\text{(C)}}\\,{\\text{Dystrophin }}}&{\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{\\text{(P)}}\\,{\\text{Largest}}\\,{\\text{gene}}\\,\\,\\,}&{}&{}&{{\\text{  }}} \n\\end{array}$<br>${\\text{(D) Satellite}}\\,{\\text{DNA}}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{\\text{(R) Bulk}}\\,{\\text{DNA}}$',NULL,'Medium','Match the following',2015,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(50039,'biology',94,788,1697,'RNA polymerase I transcribes eukaryotic ribosome which does not consist of:','[{"text": "$28 \\\\mathrm{~S}$ rRNA", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$5 \\\\mathrm{~S}$ rRNA", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$5.8 \\\\mathrm{~S}$ rRNA", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$18 \\\\mathrm{~S}$ rRNA", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'In eukaryotes the RNA polymerase transcribes rRNAs which consist of 28S, $18 \\mathrm{~S}$ and 5.8S.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2015,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(50040,'biology',94,795,1704,'If an inheritable mutation is observed in a population at high frequency, it is referred to as:','[{"text": "DNA polymorphism", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Expressed sequence tag", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Sequence annotation", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Linkage", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'If an inheritable mutation is observed in a population at high frequency, it is referred to as DNA polymorphism. The probability of such variation to be observed in non-coding DNA sequence would be higher as mutations in these sequences may not have any immediate effect in an individual reproductive ability. These mutations keep on accumulating generation after generation, and form one of the basis of variability/polymorphism. There is a variety of different types of polymorphisms ranging from single nucleotide change to very large scale changes. For evolution and speciation, such polymorphisms play very important role.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2015,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(50041,'biology',94,789,1698,'In 125 amino acid sequence if the $25^{\\text {th }}$ codon for amino acid is mutated to UAA, then:','[{"text": "A polypeptide of 124 amino acids is formed", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "A polypeptide of 25 amino acids is formed", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "A polypeptide of 24 amino acids is formed", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "No polypepetides are formed", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'UAA is one of the three stop codons which do not code for any amino acid. If the codon for $25^{\\text {th }}$ amino acid is mutated to UAA then the polypeptide chain will be terminated at that point because tRNA cannot bring next amino acid as UAA is termination codon. So, in this case a polypeptide chain of 24 amino acids will be formed.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2015,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(50042,'biology',94,792,1701,'Find the sequence of binding of the following aminoacyl tRNA complexes during translation to mRNA transcribed by a DNA segment having the base sequences . Choose the answer showing the correct order of alphabets.','[{"text": "A, B, D, C", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "B, A, D, C", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "C, D, B, A", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "D, C, A, B", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'The given DNA sequence is:<br>DNA sequence 3\'TAC ATG GGT CCG5\'<br>TRanscribed mRNA<br>5\' AUG UAC CCA GGC3\'<br>On translation with $\\frac{{{\\text{UAC}}}}{{\\text{B}}}\\frac{{{\\text{AUG}}}}{{\\text{A}}}\\frac{{{\\text{GGU}}}}{{\\text{D}}}\\frac{{{\\text{CCG}}}}{{\\text{C}}}$<br>Peptide chain formed',NULL,'Medium','MCQ type-3',2016,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','biology/94_50042_question_1.png'),
(50043,'biology',94,787,1693,'<i>E. coli</i> bacteria grew in ${ }^{15} \\mathrm{NH}_{4} \\mathrm{Cl}$ medium for several generations are allowed to grow in ${ }^{14} \\mathrm{NH}_{4} \\mathrm{Cl}$ medium. After 2 generations, the bacteria are isolated from the medium and DNA of bacteria centrifuged in $\\mathrm{CsCl}$. The result of the density gradient of DNA is:','[{"text": "Only hybrid DNA", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both hybrid and heavy DNA", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both heavy and light DNA", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both hybrid and light DNA", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'• After one generation (after $20 \\mathrm{~min}$ in which<br>E. coli divides), all DNA molecules would be ${ }^{14} \\mathrm{~N}-{ }^{14}$ Nhybrids. Their density would be halfway between that expected for totally heavy and totally light DNA.<br>• After the second generation (40 min), the newly synthesized strands would continue to contain only light isotopes as they are now growing on medium. Two types of bands would appear in the gradients-those containing hybrids and those containing only .',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2016,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(50045,'biology',94,794,1703,'Read the following statements carefully and choose the correct statements.<br>I. In a transcription unit, the promoter located at the $5^{\\prime}$ end of coding strand.<br>II. The single strand DNA having the $5^{\\prime} \\rightarrow 3^{\\prime}$ polarity is the template strand.<br>III. RNA polymerase binds to the operator during transcription.<br>IV. Single base DNA differences occur in humans are called Single Nucleotide Polymorphism (SNPs).','[{"text": "Statements I and II", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Statements II and III", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Statements I and III", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Statements II and IV", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Promoter is the site on the DNA to which an RNA polymerase molecule binds prior to initiating transcription. It is located upstream of structural gene towards its end (with reference to the coding strand). Template strand has polarity. It serves as a template for RNA synthesis.<br>About 1.4 million locations in the genome show single base DNA differences. This is known as single nucleotide polymorphisms (SNPs, pronounced as snips).',NULL,'Medium','MCQ type-2',2016,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(50046,'biology',94,794,1703,'The average length of hnRNA in humans is:','[{"text": "3000 bases", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "2.4 million bases", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "1500 bases", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "500 bases", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'The average length of hnRNA in humans is about 3000 bases. From this heterogenous nuclear RNA exons are spliced later and after going through post-transcriptional modifications synthesize the mature mRNA that measures about 1800-2000 bases.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2017,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(50047,'biology',94,787,1693,'If <i>E. coli</i> is allowed to grow for 40 minutes in a medium containing ${ }^{15} \\mathrm{~N}$, then the number of${ }^{14} \\mathrm{~N}^{14} \\mathrm{~N}$ containing DNA would be:','[{"text": "zero", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "20", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "10", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "2", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'If <i>E. Coli</i> is allowed to grow for 40 minutes in a medium containing $\\mathrm{N}^{15}$, then the number of $\\mathrm{N}^{14} / \\mathrm{N}^{14}$ containing DNA would be zero as medium contains $\\mathrm{N}^{15}$ and not $\\mathrm{N}^{14}$.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2017,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(50048,'biology',94,787,1694,'Polymerisation of DNA nucleotides during the synthesis of lagging strand occurs in:','[{"text": "3\' $->5$ \' direction", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "5 \' $->$ \' direction", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Any direction", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Promotor to terminator direction", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'DNA synthesis takes place only in $5^{\\prime} \\rightarrow 3^{\\prime}$ direction in both leading and lagging strand. Lagging strand (template having $5^{\\prime} \\rightarrow 3^{\\prime}$ polarity) that grows away from replication fork is synthesized discontinuously because initiation of each fragment must wait for the parental strands to separate and expose additional template.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2017,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(50049,'biology',94,793,1702,'In lac-operon concept of gene expression, allolactose acts as:','[{"text": "Repressor", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Inducer", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Co-repressor", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Co-enzyme", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'The lac operon is an example of an inducible operon which induces transcription of structural genes in the presence of lactose or allolactose and hence it is called an inducer. In other words, the presence of substrate induces enzyme synthesis.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2017,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(50050,'biology',94,789,1698,'Find the nucleotide sequence of the mRNA which codes for the sequence of amino acids.<br>\'Met - Leu - Val - Arg - Ala\' and choose the correct option from below.','[{"text": "AUG - GAU - GAA - UAU - UGU", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "AUG - GAU - GAA - CGU - GCC", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "AUG - CUA - GUG - UAU - UGU", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "AUG - CUA - GUG - CGU - GCC", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'The codons for the given amino acids are:<br>• Met : AUG<br>• Leu: UUA, UUG, CUU, CUC, CUA, CUG<br>• Val: GUU, GUC, GUA, GUG<br>• Arg: CGU, CGC, CGA, CGG, AGA, AGG<br>• Ala: GCU, GCC, GCA, GCG<br>Thus, of the given options nucleotide sequence for Val-Arg-Ala would be<br>AUG - CUA - GUG - CGU - GCC.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2017,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(50051,'biology',94,788,1695,'In the given transcription unit, identify the regions I and II respectively.','[{"text": "Promoter and Terminator", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Rho factor and Sigma factor", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Terminator and Promoter", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Operator and Inhibitor", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Promoter is the site on the DNA to which an RNA polymerase molecule binds prior to initiating transcription. The promoter also contains the information that determines which of the two DNA strands will be transcribed, that is, it defines template and coding strands are defined. Promoter is located upstream of structural gene towards its end (with reference to the coding strand). Terminator is hence located downstream of the coding strand towards it -end (with reference to coding strand). <img src=\'/img/data/biology/94_50051_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />',NULL,'Medium','MCQ type-3',2018,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','biology/94_50051_question_1.png'),
(50052,'biology',94,792,1701,'Which of the following sequences of mRNA are required for translation process but are not translated?','[{"text": "Stop codons", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Anticodons", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Sense codons", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "UTR", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'The sequence of RNA which has a start codon on side and the stop codon on the other and codes for polypeptide is called the translational unit of the mRNA. Some sequences are not translated in the mRNA. They are present before the start codon at the end and after the stop codon at the -end. They are known as untranslated regions (UTRs). However, they aid efficient translation.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2018,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(50053,'biology',94,795,1704,'In which of the following steps in DNA fingerprinting technique are labelled VNTR probes used?','[{"text": "During isolation of DNA", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "During digestion of DNA by REN", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "During electrophoresis", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "During hybridisation", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'The human genome contains a number of short DNA sequences that are present as tandem repeats of varied lengths at several chromosomal locations. These Variable Number of Tandem Repeats (VNTR) are important components of DNA fingerprints. When DNA is cut with restriction endonucleases, a pattern of bands is produced. It can be visualized by Southern blot hybridization using radio labelled VNTR sequences as probes. This pattern is known as DNA fingerprints.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2018,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(50054,'biology',94,792,1701,'Match the processes of Column I with the enzyme involved in Column II and select the correct options among the following<br>Select the code for the correct answer from the options given below<div class="matching-question"><div class="column-left">A. DNA\nreplication<br>B. Translation<br>C. Transcription<br>D. Reverse\ntranscription</div><div class="column-right">P. RNA\npolymerase<br>Q. DNA\npolymerase<br>R. Reverse\ntranscriptase<br>S. Aminoacyl\nsynthetase</div></div>','[{"text": "${\\\\text{A  -  Q; B  -  S; C  -  R; D  -  P}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\text{A  -  Q; B  -  S; C  -  P; D  -  R}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${\\\\text{A  -  Q; B  -  R; C  -  S; D  -  P}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\text{A  -  Q; B  -  S; C  -  R; D  -  P}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'• The major enzyme responsible for DNA replication is DNA polymerase III. DNA replication is continuous in one strand and discontinuous in another because DNA polymerase III can polymerize nucleotides only in $5^{\\prime} \\rightarrow 3^{\\prime}$ direction on $3^{\\prime} \\rightarrow 5^{\\prime}$ strand as it adds them at the end.<br>• An aminoacyl-tRNA synthetase, also called tRNA-ligase, is an enzyme that attaches the appropriate amino acid onto its tRNA. Aminoacyl tRNA therefore plays an important role in RNA translation, the expression of genes to create proteins..<br>• RNA polymerase is the main transcription enzyme. Transcription begins when RNA polymerase binds to a promoter sequence near the beginning of a gene. RNA polymerase uses one of the DNA strands (the template strand) as a template to make a new, complementary RNA molecule..<br>• A reverse transcriptase (RT) is an enzyme used to generate complementary DNA (cDNA) from an RNA template, a process termed reverse transcription.',NULL,'Medium','Match the following',2018,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(50055,'biology',94,789,1698,'The codon on mRNA are:<br>CAU-CCU-AAA-CUG.<br>Identify the correct sequence of amino acids.<div class="matching-question"></div>','[{"text": "His-Pro-Lys-Leu", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Pro-His-Lys-Leu", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "His-Pro-Leu-Lys", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Pro-Leu-Lys-His", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'CAU codes for histidine (His), CCU codes for proline (Pro), AAA stands for lysine (Lys) and CUG stands for leucine (Leu). Thus, the correct amino acid sequence will be His Pro - Lys - Leu.',NULL,'Medium','Match the following',2018,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(50056,'biology',94,789,1698,'Which of the following amino acids is coded by single codon?','[{"text": "Valine", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Phenylalanine", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Tyrosine", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Tryptophan", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Tryptophan is coded only by UGG. Valine is encoded by all codons starting with GU (GUU, GUC, GUA, and GUG). Phenylalanine is encoded by the codons UUU and UUC. Tyrosine is encoded by the codons UAC and UAU.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2019,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(50057,'biology',94,795,1704,'In a crime investigation, the investigating officer collects different biological samples from the crime spot for DNA fingerprinting analysis. Which of the following samples is not helpful in this analysis?','[{"text": "Skin Shreds", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Erythrocytes", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Semen sample", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Hair follicle", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'DNA fingerprint is the same for every cell, tissue and organ of a person.<br>Therefore,saliva, hair follicle, skin, bone, seminal fluid, blood, and even hair root tissue is generally used for forensic investigations. DNA fingerprinting can be used to ascertain the identity. Since polymorphism is inherited, it is also used for testing paternity.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2019,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(50058,'biology',94,789,1698,'A mature mRNA consists of 900 bases without any stop codon in between. Calculate the number of amino acids coded by this mRNA during translation.','[{"text": "900", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "299", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "300", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "450", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'3 bases contain 1 codon<br>900 bases contain 300 codons<br>The number of amino acids coded by this mRNA during translation of 300 codes is 300.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2019,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(50059,'biology',94,795,1704,'Which of the following nitrogen bases is found only in DNA?','[{"text": "Cytosine", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Adenine", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Thymine", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Guanine", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'The nitrogenous bases are adenine (A), thymine (T), cytosine (C) and guanine (G) in deoxyribonucleic acid (DNA) . In ribonucleic acid (RNA), uracil (U) is present instead of thymine.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2019,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(50060,'biology',94,792,1701,'Which of the following types of RNA carries amino acids towards ribosome during translation?','[{"text": "rRNA", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "tRNA", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "dsRNA", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "mRNA", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'t-RNA carries specific aminoacid to the ribosomes during translation.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2020,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(50061,'biology',94,788,1697,'In eukaryotes, the entire base sequence of a gene do not appear in mature RNA because','[{"text": "Transcription in eukaryotes consumes more energy", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Coding sequences are removed during processing", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Introns are removed during processing", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Some gene sequences are removed by exonuclease", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'In eukaryotes, the entire base sequence of a gene do not appear in mature RNA because introns are removed during processing',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2020,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(50062,'biology',94,783,1688,'The length of DNA helix in a typical<br>nucleosome is','[{"text": "$200 \\\\mathrm{bp}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$1000 \\\\mathrm{bp}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$3.2 \\\\times 10^{6} \\\\mathrm{bp}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$6.6 \\\\times 10^{9} \\\\mathrm{bp}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'The length of DNA helix in a typical nucleosome is $200 \\mathrm{bp}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2020,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(50063,'biology',94,788,1695,'For the given sequence of DNA, identify the complementary sequence of bases on its mRNA from the options given below<br>DNA 3\' - ATGCATGCATGC - 5\'','[{"text": "5\' - UACGUACGUACG - 3\'", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "5\' - TACGTACGTACG - 3\'", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "3\' - UACGUACGUACG - 5\'", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "5\' - GCATGCATGCAT - 3\'", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'DNA 3\' - ATGCATGCATGC - 5\'<br>m-RNA 5\' - UACGUACGUACG - 3\'',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2020,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(50064,'biology',94,793,1702,'When <i>Escherichia coli</i> cells are cultured in a medium where Lactose is absent, the \' $i$ \' gene of Lac Operon continues to produce repressor mRNA, because it is','[{"text": "A non-coding gene", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "An operator gene", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "A constitutive gene", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "A structural gene", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'When Escherichia coli cells are cultured in a medium where Lactose is absent, the \' $\\mathrm{i}$ \' gene of Lac Operon continues to produce repressor mRNA, because it is a constitutive gene',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2020,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(50065,'biology',94,793,1702,'Match the scientists of Column I with their contributions in Column II<br><div class="matching-question"><div class="column-left">A. Griffith<br>B. Jacob & Monad<br>C. Meselson & Stahl<br>D. Hershey and Chase</div><div class="column-right">P. Lac operon<br>Q. DNA is the genetic material<br>R. Transforming principle<br>S. DNA replicates semi- conservativelys.<br></div></div>','[{"text": "${\\\\text{A  -  P; B  -  Q; C  -  R; D  -  S}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\text{A  -  P; B  -  S; C  -  Q; D  -  R}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\text{A  -  R; B  -  P; C  -  S; D  -  Q}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${\\\\text{A  -  R; B  -  Q; C  -  P; D  -  S}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'i. Griffith - r. Transforming principle<br>ii. Jacob & Monad p. Lac operon<br>iii. Meselson & Stahl s. DNA replicates semiconservatively<br>iv. Hershey and Chase -q. DNA is the genetic material<br>i - r ii - p iii - s iv - q',NULL,'Medium','Match the following',2021,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(50066,'biology',94,791,1700,'In which region of the t-RNA molecule is the amino-acid binding site located?','[{"text": "5\' end", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Anticodon loop", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "3\' end", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "None of the above", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'$3^{\\prime}$ end region of the t-RNA molecule is the amino-acid binding site.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2021,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(50067,'biology',94,788,1695,'If the sequence of nucleotides in a template stand of DNA is 3\'-ATGCTTCCGAAT-5\'. Write the sequence in the corresponding region of the transcribed m-RNA.','[{"text": "5\' -TAC GAA GGC CTT- 3\'", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "5\' - UAC GAA GGC UUA-3\'", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "3\'- UAC GAA GGC UUA-5\'", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "3\'- TAC GAA GGC CCA-5\'", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'If the sequence of nucleotides in a template stand of DNA is $3^{\\prime}-$ ATGCTTCCGAAT-5\'. The corresponding region of the transcribed $\\mathrm{m}-\\mathrm{RNA}$ will be $5^{\\prime}-$ UAC GAA GGC UUA- 3\'.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2021,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(50068,'biology',94,789,1698,'The number of codons effective in coding twenty amino acids :','[{"text": "64", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "61", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "20", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "32", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'61 codons are required to code for 20\namino acids of a poly peptide chain',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2022,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(50069,'biology',94,795,1704,'Which aspect form the basis of DNA fingerprinting ?','[{"text": "The satellite DNA showing high\\ndegree of repetition in DNA\\nsegments", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "The ratio of purines and pyrimidine\\npresent in DNA", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "The amount of DNA found in samples of\\nblood, saliva and skin.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "The Sequence of DNA present in the\\nridges and grooves of finger-prints.", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'DNA finger printing is based on DNA\npolymorphism. It is exhibited by satellite\nregions of chromosomes as high degree\nrepetition.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2022,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL);
INSERT INTO `qb_questions` (`id`, `subject_id`, `chapter_id`, `topic_id`, `subtopic_id`, `question_html`, `options`, `correct_index`, `explanation`, `explanation_image_url`, `difficulty`, `qtype`, `year`, `tag`, `created_at`, `question_image_url`) VALUES
(50070,'biology',94,783,1688,'The given diagram represents :','[{"text": "Nucleosome", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Chromosome", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Mesosome", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Ribosome", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'The diagram represents a nucleosome.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ type-3',2022,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','biology/94_50070_question_1.png'),
(50071,'biology',94,788,1696,'If the sequence of bases in DNA is ATTCGATG, then the sequence of bases in its transcript will be','[{"text": "UAAGCUAC", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "CAUCGAAU", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "GUAGCUUA", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "AUUCGAUG", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Transcription is the initial step of gene expression where a particular segment of DNA is copied into RNA (i.e., mRNA) by the enzyme RNA polymerase. Uracil is pyrimidine base in RNA, rather than thymine, thus uracil will pair with adenine. All other bases would remain same, therefore the primary transcript will be :<br>ATTCGATG $\\longrightarrow$ UAAGCUAC',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(50072,'biology',94,788,1697,'Which is correct?','[{"text": "Introns are present in mRNA and exons in tRNA", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Codons are present in mRNA and anticodons in tRNA", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Every intron is a set of three terminator codons", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Exons are present in eukaryotes and introns in prokaryotes", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'<br>• Both introns and exons are present in mRNA and tRNA does not possess exons.<br>• An intron is any nucleotide sequence within a gene that is removed by RNA splicing during maturation of the final RNA product.<br>• Prokaryotes can\'t have introns, because they have transcription coupled to translation.<br>• They don\'t have time/space for that, since intron splicing will stop the coupling.<br>• Eukaryotes evolved the nucleus, where splicing can be done.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(50073,'biology',94,788,1697,'Select the wrong statement','[{"text": "Sigma subunit of RNA polymerase recognizes the promoter", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Monocistronic mRNA is produced in Eukaryotes", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "In prokaryotes the primary mRNA transcript undergoes splicing", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Transcription occurs in cytoplasm in prokaryotes cytoplasm", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Prokaryotes genome doesn\'t have Introns hence the splicing mechanism doesn\'t takes place in it.',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(50074,'biology',94,789,1698,'Which one of the following pair is correctly matched<div class="matching-question"></div>','[{"text": "UUA - valine", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "AAA - lysine", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "AUG - cysteine", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "CCC - alanine", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'The correct pairing of the codon with the corresponding amino acid is as follows:<br>(2) AAA - lysine<br>So, the answer is (2) AAA - lysine.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(50075,'biology',94,790,1699,'Which of the of the following statements about mutations is false?','[{"text": "Mutations are the source of new alleles for genes.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Mutations are random events and can happen in any cell at any time.", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Organisms are able to create mutations to meet their specific needs.", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Most mutations random tend to be harmful or have no effect on organisms.", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'The false statement about mutations is: (3) Organisms are able to create mutations to meet their specific needs.<br>• Mutations are typically random events that can occur in any cell at any time.<br>•Organisms do not intentionally create mutations to meet specific needs. Mutations are changes in the DNA sequence that can result from various factors, including errors during DNA replication, exposure to mutagens, or other natural processes.<br>• While mutations can sometimes lead to the creation of new alleles for genes, most mutations tend to be either harmful or have no significant effect on organisms, and they are not purposefully generated by organisms to meet specific needs.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(50076,'biology',94,783,1688,'In the given figure choose the correct labeling.','[{"text": "A-Core of histone molecule, B-DNA, C-H1 histone, D- Octamer of histones", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "A-Octamer of histones, B- Linker DNA, $\\\\mathrm{C}$-Nucleosome unit, $\\\\mathrm{D}-\\\\mathrm{H} 1$ histone", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "A-Octamer of histones, B-H1 histone, C-Nucleosome unit, D-Core DNA", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "A-Nucleosome unit, B-Linker DNA, C-H1 histone, D-Core DNA", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'A-Core of histone molecule, B-DNA, $\\mathrm{C}-\\mathrm{H}_{1}$ histone, $\\mathrm{D}$ - Octamer of histones',NULL,'Easy','MCQ type-3',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','biology/94_50076_question_1.png'),
(50077,'biology',94,783,1688,'Read (i) to (v) and find the correct option<br>i. Nitrogen base is linked to pentose sugar through $\\mathrm{N}$-glycosidic linkage<br>ii. Phosphate group is linked to $\\mathrm{OH}$ of a nucleoside through phosphoester linkage<br>iii. Two nucleosides are linked through $\\mathrm{N}$-glycosidic linkage<br>iv. Negatively charged DNA is wrapped around positively charged histone octamer to form nucleosome<br>v. Chromatin that is more densely packed and stains dark is called euchromatin','[{"text": "i alone is wrong", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "iv alone is wrong", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "iii and v are wrong", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "i, ii, iii wrong", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'iii and v are wrong.<br>• i  is correct as nitrogen bases are indeed linked to the pentose sugar through $\\mathrm{N}$-glycosidic linkage.<br>• ii is correct as the phosphate group is linked to the $\\mathrm{OH}$ of a nucleoside through phosphoester linkage.<br>• iii is incorrect because nucleosides are not linked through $\\mathrm{N}$-glycosidic linkage; instead, they are linked by phosphodiester bonds to form the sugar-phosphate backbone of DNA or RNA.<br>• iv is correct as DNA is wrapped around a positively charged histone octamer to form a nucleosome.<br>• v is incorrect because euchromatin is less densely packed and stains lightly, while heterochromatin stains dark.',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(50078,'biology',94,782,1687,'Total amount $\\mathrm{A}$ and $\\mathrm{T}$ in DNA is $45 \\%$ Amount of guanine will be ..','[{"text": "$22.5 \\\\%$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$27.5 \\\\%$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$35 \\\\%$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$55 \\\\%$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'$\\mathrm{A}+\\mathrm{T}+\\mathrm{G}+\\mathrm{C}=100 \\%$<br>Since $\\mathrm{A}+\\mathrm{T}=45 \\%$<br>$45 \\%+\\mathrm{G}+\\mathrm{C}=100 \\%$<br>$\\mathrm{G}+\\mathrm{C}=100 \\%-45 \\%$<br>$\\mathrm{G}+\\mathrm{C}=55 \\%$<br>$\\mathrm{G} / \\mathrm{C}=55 \\backslash 2$<br>$\\mathrm{G}=27.5 \\%$<br>hence ans is $27.5 \\%$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(50079,'biology',94,782,1687,'In DNA percentage of thymine is 20 , then what is the percentage of guanine?','[{"text": "20", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "40", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "30", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "60", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'If thymine $=20 \\%$, as thymine will always pair with adenine i.e, adenine is also $20 \\%$. Then cytosine and guanine accounts $60 \\%$, as guanine will always pair with cytosine. So, guanine $=30 \\%$, cytosine $=30 \\%$, thymine $=20 \\%$ and adenine $=20 \\%$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(50080,'biology',94,782,1687,'Which of the following statements is correct?<br>I. The biochemical nature of genetic material was not defined from the experiments conducted by Griffith.<br>II. Working on transformation Avery et al concluded DNA is genetic material but not all biologists were convinced.<br>III. RNA is the genetic material in TMV, QB bacteriophage etc.<br>IV. DNA is the predominant genetic material while RNA performs dynamic functions of messenger and adapter.<br>V. Viruses having DNA genome and having slow mutate and evolve faster.','[{"text": "iii and iv", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "All except v", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "i and ii", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "All", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'All the statements are correct except V as viruses with RNA as genetic material mutate faster.',NULL,'Hard','MCQ type-2',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(50081,'biology',94,792,1701,'DNA segment, 3\' TAC ATG GCT CCG 5\', transcribes one mRNA. Four tRNAs with anticodons are required for translation. What is the order of<br>tRNAs ____<br>i. AUG<br>ii. UAC<br>iii. CCG and<br>iv. GCU','[{"text": "i, ii, iv, iii", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "ii, i, iv, iii", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "i, ii, iii, iv", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "ii, i, iii, iv", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'DNA segment, 3\' TACATGGCTCCG 5\', transcribes one mRNA. The resulting mRNA would be complementary to the template DNA strand. The tRNA molecules that come in, would have anticodons complementary to the mRNA sequence. Therefore, the tRNA should have nucleotides that are similar to the template DNA sequence. However, Uracil would replace Thymine in tRNA. The order of tRNAs is hence, UAC, AUG, GCU and CCG.',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(50082,'biology',94,788,1696,'In transcription unit the terminator is located towards','[{"text": "3-end of coding strand", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "5-end of coding strand", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "3-end of template strand", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "5-end of template strand", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'The correct option is (3) 3-end of template strand. In a transcription unit, the terminator sequence is located at the $3^{\\prime}$ end of the template strand. This terminator sequence signals the end of transcription and is recognized by RNA polymerase, leading to the release of the newly synthesized mRNA transcript.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(50083,'biology',94,788,1697,'Structural genes in Eukaryotes possess coding and non-coding sequences called as 1 and 2 respectively','[{"text": "1- exons, 2- neutrons", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "1- exons, 2- Introns", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "1- Introns, 2- Exons", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "1-Enhancer, 2- Silencer", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'In Eukaryotes, coding sequence are expressed as exons. Exons are interrupted by Introns which are also called non coding sequence. These does not appear in mature or processed RNA.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(50084,'biology',94,789,1698,'Which out of the following statement is incorrect?','[{"text": "Genetic code is ambiguous", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Genetic code is degenerate", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Genetic code is universal", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Genetic code is non-overlapping", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'The incorrect statement is (1) Genetic code is ambiguous.<br>1) codes for a specific amino acid or a signal for the initiation or termination of protein synthesis.<br>2) The genetic code is degenerate, meaning that multiple codons can code for the same amino acid. This is due to the redundancy in the code.<br>3) The genetic code is universal, as the same codons typically code for the same amino acids in nearly all organisms.<br>4) The genetic code is non-overlapping, meaning that each nucleotide is part of only one codon and is not shared between adjacent codons.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(50085,'biology',94,789,1698,'Codon is read','[{"text": "On the tRNA", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "in a contiguous fashion", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "As an initiator and terminator", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "As nonsense codons", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Triplet codons are read in a contiguous fashion during translation. Each triplet codon in mRNA corresponds to a specific amino acid or serves as a start or stop signal for protein synthesis. This process ensures that the correct amino acids are added in the proper sequence to form a functional protein. Therefore, the correct answer is (2) "in a contiguous fashion."',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(50086,'biology',94,791,1700,'Anticodon site is located on','[{"text": "tRNA", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "mRNA", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "rRNA", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "DNA", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'The anticodon site is located on transfer RNA (tRNA). The anticodon is a sequence of three nucleotides on the tRNA molecule that is complementary to a specific codon on the mRNA during translation. It ensures the proper pairing of tRNA with the mRNA codon and the incorporation of the correct amino acid into the growing polypeptide chain. So, the correct answer is (1) "tRNA."',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(50087,'biology',94,782,1687,'Study the given portion of double stranded polynucleotide chain carefully. Identify A, B, $\\mathrm{C}$ and D.','[{"text": "A-phosphodiester bond, B-purine, C-phosphate, D-pentose sugar", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "A-hydrogen bonds, B-pyrimidine, C-pentose sugar, D-phosphate", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "A-phosphodiester bond, B-pyrimidine, C-phosphate, D-pentose sugar", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "A-hydrogen bonds, B-purine, C-pentose sugar, D-phosphate", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'A-hydrogen bonds, B-purine, C-pentose sugar, D-phosphate',NULL,'Easy','MCQ type-3',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','biology/94_50087_question_1.png'),
(50088,'biology',94,787,1694,'This Diagram indicates','[{"text": "DNA replication", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Reverse transcription", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Transcription", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Translation", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'The process of transcription is shown in the diagram.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ type-3',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508','biology/94_50088_question_1.png'),
(50089,'biology',94,784,1689,'Following representations $\\mathrm{P}, \\mathrm{Q}$ and R denote few steps of Griffith Experiment. Identify the correct one(s)<br>P. R strain $\\rightarrow$ Inject into mice $\\rightarrow$ Mice $\\rightarrow$ die<br>Q. S strain (Heat killed) $\\rightarrow$ Injected into mice $\\rightarrow$ Mice die<br>R. R strain $\\rightarrow$ Inject into mice $\\rightarrow$ Mice live','[{"text": "P only", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "R only", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "P and R", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Q and R", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'In Griffith experiment, S-strain bacteria have mucous (polysaccharide) coat and they are pathogenic and cause pneumonia. R - strain do not having any mucous coat and are non-pathogenic and they do not cause pneumonia. when R - strain bacteria injected into the mice, the mice will survive.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2024,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(50090,'biology',94,791,1700,'In tRNA, the region that binds with mRNA is','[{"text": "Anticodon loop of tRNA", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Amino acid acceptor end tRNA", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Amino acyl synthetase loop of tRNA", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Ribosomal binding loop of tRNA", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'In tRNA, the region that binds with mRNA is anticodon loop of tRNA. Because, the anticodon loop of tRNA has nitrogen bases which are complementary to the triplet codons present on the mRNA molecule.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2024,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(50091,'biology',94,792,1701,'The mRNA has Untranslated Regions (UTRs)','[{"text": "At 3\'-end beyond Terminator codon", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "At 5\'-end before AUG", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "At both $3^{\\\\prime}$-end and $5^{\\\\prime}$ - end beyond terminator codon and before AUG respectively", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "AUG and Terminator codon flanks the UTR<br>", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'An mRNA has some additional sequences that are not translated and are referred as untranslated regions (UTR). The UTRS are present at both $5^{\\prime}$-end (before start codon) and $3^{\\prime}$ end (after stop codon). They are required for efficient translation process.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',2024,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(50092,'biology',94,788,1695,'In structural gene, the template DNA strand has nucleotide sequences 3\'-ATGCATGCATGCATGC- 5.<br>Find the correct and complimentary nucleotide sequence on coding strand.','[{"text": "5\'- ATGCATGCATGCATGC - 3\'", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$3^{\\\\prime}$ - GCATGCATGCATGCAT-5\'", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "5\'- TACGTACGTACGTACG-3\'", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$3^{\\\\prime}$ - TACGTACGTACGTACG-5\'", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'In transcription unit, structural gene is responsible for RNA formation. There is a complementarity between template strand and coding strand. Because both are segment of DNA only, (i,e. Adenine pairs with Thymine and Cytosine pairs with Guanine respectively). Therefore this is the correct complimentary sequence<br>\'5\'-TACGTACGTACGTACG-3\' on the coding strand.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2024,'KCET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(50093,'biology',94,788,1695,'A transcription unit in DNA is defined primarily by the three regions in DNA and these are with respect to upstream and down stream end;','[{"text": "Repressor, Operator gene, Structural gene", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Structural gene, Transposons, Operator gene", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Inducer, Repressor, Structural gene", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Promotor, Structural gene, Terminator", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'A transcription unit of DNA is defined primarily by the three regions in the DNA:<br>(i) A promoter<br>(ii) The structural gene<br>(iii) A terminator<br>The promoter is said to be located towards $5^{\\prime}$-end (upstream) of the structural gene (the reference is made with respect to the polarity of coding strand)<br>The terminator is located towards 3 \'-end (downstream) of the coding strand.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2024,'NEET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(50094,'biology',94,793,1702,'The lactose present in the growth medium of bacteria is transported to the cell by the action of','[{"text": "Beta-galactosidase", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Acetylase", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Permease", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Polymerase", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'The $\\gamma$ gene lac operon codes for permease enzyme, which increase the permeability of cell to $\\beta$-galactosides.<br>So, the lactose present in the growth medium of bacteria is transported into the cell by the action of permease.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2024,'NEET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(50117,'biology',93,272,530,'Choose the incorrect match.<div class="matching-question"></div>','[{"text": "Genotype - Physical appearance of an organism", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Phenotype - Physical appearance of an organism", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Homozygous - Identical alleles of character", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Heterozygous - Genes of an allelic pair are not same", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Genotype is the genetic constitution of an organism.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(50095,'biology',94,793,1702,'Match Column I with Column II<br>Choose the correct answer from the options\ngiven below:<div class="matching-question"><div class="column-left">A. Frederick Griffith<br>B. Francois Jacob & Jacque Monod<br>C. Har Gobind Khorana<br>D. Meselson & Stahl<br></div><div class="column-right">P. Genetic code<br>Q. Semi-conservative\nmode of DNA\nreplicatione<br>R. Transformation<br>S. Lac operon</div></div>','[{"text": "${\\\\text{A - R,}}\\\\,{\\\\text{B - Q,}}\\\\,{\\\\text{C - P,}}\\\\,{\\\\text{D - S}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\text{A - R,}}\\\\,{\\\\text{B - S,}}\\\\,{\\\\text{C - P,}}\\\\,{\\\\text{D - Q}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "${\\\\text{A - Q,}}\\\\,{\\\\text{B - R,}}\\\\,{\\\\text{C - S,}}\\\\,{\\\\text{D - P}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\text{A - S,}}\\\\,{\\\\text{B - P,}}\\\\,{\\\\text{C - Q,}}\\\\,{\\\\text{D - R}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'${\\rm{A}}\\,{\\rm{ - }}\\,{\\rm{R,}}\\,{\\rm{B}}\\,{\\rm{ - }}\\,{\\rm{S,}}\\,{\\rm{C}}\\,{\\rm{ - }}\\,{\\rm{P,}}\\,{\\rm{D}}\\,{\\rm{ - }}\\,{\\rm{Q}}$ is the correct option Frederick Griffith series of experiment witness miraculous transformation in the bacteria.<br>The elucidation of $L a c$ operon was a result of a close association between geneticist, Francois Jacob and a biochemist, Jacques Monod.<br>Meselson and Stahl gave semi-conservative mode of DNA replication.<br>Har Gobind Khorana developed chemical method to define combination of bases in genetic code.',NULL,'Hard','Match the following',2024,'NEET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(50096,'biology',94,787,1694,'Which of the following statement is correct regarding the process of replication in <i>E.coli</i>?','[{"text": "The DNA dependent DNA polymerase catalyses polymerization in one direction that is $3^{\\\\prime} \\\\rightarrow 5^{\\\\prime}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "The DNA dependent RNA polymerase catalyses polymerization in one direction, that is $5^{\\\\prime} \\\\rightarrow 3^{\\\\prime}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "The DNA dependent DNA polymerase catalyses polymerization in $5^{\\\\prime} \\\\rightarrow 3^{\\\\prime}$ as well as $3^{\\\\prime} \\\\rightarrow 5^{\\\\prime}$ direction", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "The DNA dependent DNA polymerase catalyses polymerization in $5^{\\\\prime} \\\\rightarrow 3^{\\\\prime}$ direction", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'In Prokaryotes, like <i>E.coli</i> during replication, the DNA dependent DNA polymerase catalyse polymerization only in one direction, that is $5^{\\prime} \\rightarrow 3^{\\prime}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',2024,'NEET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(50097,'biology',94,788,1695,'Which one is the correct product of DNA dependent RNA polymerase to the given template?<br>3\'TACATGGCAAATATCCATTCA5\'','[{"text": "5\'AUGUACCGUUUAUAGGUAAGU3\'", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "5\'AUGUAAAGUUUAUAGGUAAGU3\'", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "5\'AUGUACCGUUUAUAGGGAAGU3\'", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "5\'ATGTACCGTTTATAGGTAAGT3\'", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Template DNA is :<br>3\'TACATGGCAAATATCCATTCA5\'<br>5\'AUGUACCGUUUAUAGGUAAGU3\' mRNA',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',2024,'NEET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(50098,'biology',94,788,1697,'Match Column I with Column II:<br>Choose the correct answer from the options given below :<div class="matching-question"><div class="column-left">A. RNA polymerase III<br>B. Termination of\ntranscription<br>C. Splicing of\nExons<br>D. TATA box</div><div class="column-right">P. snRNPs<br>Q. Promotor<br>R. Rho factor<br>S. SnRNAs, tRNA</div></div>','[{"text": "${\\\\text{A - Q,}}\\\\,{\\\\text{B - S,}}\\\\,{\\\\text{C - P,}}\\\\,{\\\\text{D - R}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\text{A - R,}}\\\\,{\\\\text{B - Q,}}\\\\,{\\\\text{C - S,}}\\\\,{\\\\text{D - P}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\text{A - R,}}\\\\,{\\\\text{B - S,}}\\\\,{\\\\text{C - P,}}\\\\,{\\\\text{D - Q}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\text{A - S,}}\\\\,{\\\\text{B - R,}}\\\\,{\\\\text{C - P,}}\\\\,{\\\\text{D - Q}}$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'${\\rm{A}}\\,{\\rm{ - }}\\,{\\rm{S,}}\\,{\\rm{B}}\\,{\\rm{ - }}\\,{\\rm{R,}}\\,{\\rm{C}}\\,{\\rm{ - }}\\,{\\rm{P,}}\\,{\\rm{D}}\\,{\\rm{ - }}\\,{\\rm{Q}}$ is teh correct<br>• In eukaryotes, RNA polymerase III codes for snRNAs, tRNA and 5 s rRNA.<br>• Splicing of exons is performed by snRNPs.<br>• TATA box is present in promoter region of transcription unit.<br>• Rho factor is responsible for termination of transcription.',NULL,'Hard','Match the following',2024,'NEET','2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(50099,'biology',93,272,530,'During his experiments, Mendel used the term _____for what is called today as Genes.','[{"text": "Factor", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Traits", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Characters", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Alleles", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Mendel proposed that something was being stably passed down, unchanged, from parent to offspring through the gametes, over successive generations. Mendel called these things as ‘factors’. Now we call them as genes.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(50100,'biology',93,272,530,'Smallest structure having the power of replicating itself is','[{"text": "Ribosome", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Gene", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Chloroplast", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Mitochondria", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'A unit of heredity that is transferred from a parent to offspring and is termed as a gene.Being a part of DNA it can replicate',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(50101,'biology',93,272,530,'Genes which codes for a pair of contrasting characters are','[{"text": "Character", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Trait", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Alleles", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Factor", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Genes which code for a pair of contrasting traits are known as alleles, i.e., they are slightly different forms of the same gene.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(50102,'biology',93,272,530,'An allele is the','[{"text": "Total number of genes for a trait", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Total number of genes on chromosome", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Alternative form of a character", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Alternative form of a gene", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Genes which code for a pair of contrasting traits are known as alleles, i.e., they are slightly different forms of the same gene.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(50103,'biology',93,272,530,'It is said that Mendel proposed that the factor controlling any character is discrete and independent. This proposition was based on the','[{"text": "Observations that the offspring of a cross made between plants having two contrasting characters shows only one character without any blending", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Results of  ${F_3}$ generation of a cross", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Cross pollination of parental generations", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Self pollination ${F_1}$ offspring", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'It is said that Mendel proposed that the factor controlling any character is discrete and independent. This proposition was based on the Observations that the offspring of a cross made between plants having two contrasting characters shows only one character without any blending.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(50118,'biology',93,272,530,'The phenotypic ratio of a monohybrid cross in ${F_2}$-generation is','[{"text": "$3:1$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$1{\\\\rm{ }}:{\\\\rm{ }}2{\\\\rm{ }}:{\\\\rm{ }}1$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1{\\\\rm{ }}:{\\\\rm{ }}3$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$9{\\\\rm{ }}:{\\\\rm{ }}3{\\\\rm{ }}:{\\\\rm{ }}3{\\\\rm{ }}:{\\\\rm{ }}1$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'The phenotypic ratio of a monohybrid cross in ${F_2}$-generation is 3:1 ratio<br>A phenotypic ratio of 3/4th tall : (1/4TT + 1/2 Tt) and 1/4th tt, genotypic ratio of 1:2:1.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(50104,'biology',93,272,530,'Which of the statements are correct based on mendel’s experiments of inheritance of one gene<br>i. He proposed that something was being stably passed down, unchanged, from parent to offspring through the gametes<br>ii. He called these things as ‘genes’.<br>iii. Genes are the units of inheritance.<br> iv. Genes which code for a pair of contrasting traits are known as alleles.','[{"text": "i is correct", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "i, ii are correct only", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "i, iii and iv are correct only", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "All are correct", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Mendel called these things as ‘factors’. Now we call them as genes.',NULL,'Hard','MCQ type-2',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(50105,'biology',93,272,530,'An allele is dominant if it is expressed in','[{"text": "Second generation only", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both homozygous and heterozygous states", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Homozygous combination only", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Heterozygous combination only", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Being dominant, the allele will be expressed in the homozygous and heterozygous condition.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(50106,'biology',93,272,530,'The allele which does not express in the ${F_1}$ generation in presence of another allele is','[{"text": "Dominant", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Co-dominant", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Incompletely dominant", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Recessive", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Only dominant characters are expressed by dominant genes, in ${F_1}$ generation, while recessive genes and their expressions are suppressed.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(50107,'biology',93,272,530,'Capitalisation of the character appeared in the ${F_1}$ generation is done to indicate its ______ character.','[{"text": "Recessive", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Half dominant", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Dominant", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Half recessive", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Capitalisation of the character appeared in the ${F_1}$ generation is done to indicate its Dominant character.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(50108,'biology',93,272,530,'A red-flowered pea plant was crossed with white-flowered plant. In ${F_1}$ generation all plants were red. It confirms that white colour is :','[{"text": "Dominant character", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Recessive character", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Non-genetic expression", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "None of the above", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'After crossing red-flowered pea plant with white-flowered plant, all plants were found to be red in ${F_1}$ generation. That infers that white colour is recessive character which remained hidden.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(50109,'biology',93,272,530,'In a cross between axial and terminal flowered pea plants, ${F_2}$ progeny show','[{"text": "Axial flowers", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Terminal flowers", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both (1) and (2)", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "None of these", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'In a cross between axial and terminal flowered pea plants, ${F_2}$ progeny shows both the types of flowers, in ${F_2}$ generation, both the dominant and recessive characters are expressed.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(50110,'biology',93,272,530,'Which of the following is considered as a recessive character of Pea plant?','[{"text": "Green pod", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Round seed", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Axial flower", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Wrinkled seed", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Mendel studied seven different pair of contrasting characters, on the basis of studies on garden pea (Pisum sativum). Dominant and recessive characters are as follows:<br>Character &emsp; &emsp;  &emsp; &emsp;   Dominant &emsp;  &emsp;  Recessive<br>The length of stem &emsp;  &emsp; Tall  &emsp;   &emsp;  &emsp; Dwarf<br>The position of flower  &emsp;  Axial   &emsp;  &emsp;  &emsp;Terminal<br>The colour of the pod   &emsp; Green   &emsp;  &emsp;  &emsp;  Yellow<br>The shape of the pod   &emsp;  Inflated   &emsp;  &emsp;  Constricted<br>The shape of the seed   &emsp;  Round   &emsp; &emsp; Wrinkled<br>The colour of the seed &emsp; Coloured &emsp;  White coat<br>The colour of the <br>cotyledon  &emsp; &emsp; &emsp;  &emsp; &emsp; Yellow  &emsp;  &emsp; &emsp;  Green',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(50111,'biology',93,272,530,'Which of the following statements are false?<br>I. A recessive allele does not express in the presence of a dominant allele.<br>II. A dominant allele do not shows its effects when paired with a recessive allele.','[{"text": "I", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "II", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Both", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "None", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Statement II is wrong.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(50112,'biology',93,272,530,'The external appearance of an individual for any trait is called as','[{"text": "Morphology", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Phenotype", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Karyotype", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Physique", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'The external appearance of an individual for any trait is called as Phenotype',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(50113,'biology',93,272,530,'The physical appearance and genetic constitution of a character is called ______ and ________ respectively.','[{"text": "Genotype, Phenotype", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Phenotype only", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Phenotype, Genotype", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Genotype only", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Heterozygous organisms are not pure and is called hybrid. Heterozygotes does not breed true on self -fertilization. e.g. Tt.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(50114,'biology',93,272,530,'Organisms phenotypically similar but genotypically different are said to be','[{"text": "Monozygous", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Multizygous", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Heterozygous", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Homozygous", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'Heterozygous organisms are phenotypically similar to homozygous dominant but genetically dissimilar.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(50115,'biology',93,272,530,'An organism having a pair of dissimilar alleles is','[{"text": "Homozygous recessive", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Heterozygous", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Monozygous", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Hemizygous", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Alleles can be similar as in the case of homozygotes TT and tt or can be dissimilar as in the case of the heterozygote Tt.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(50116,'biology',93,272,530,'Select the most appropriate option<br>A. It is convenient (and logical) to use the capital and lower case of an alphabetical symbol to remember this concept of dominance and recessiveness<br>B. Alleles can be similar as in the case of homozygotes Tt and tt or can be dissimilar as in the case of the heterozygote TT.','[{"text": "A is correct", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "B is correct", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both are correct", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Both are incorrect", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Statement B can be corrected as - Alleles can be similar as in the case of homozygotes TT and tt or can be dissimilar as in the case of the heterozygote Tt.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(50121,'biology',93,272,530,'A cross is made between the two organisms both of which have a genetic make up of Bb for a particular trait, and two offsprings are produced. The first offspring exhibit the dominant trait. What is the probability that the second offsprings will exhibit the recessive trait?','[{"text": "100", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1{\\\\rm{/}}4$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Zero", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "${\\\\rm{3/}}4$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'The $1/4{\\rm{ }}:{\\rm{ }}1/2{\\rm{ }}:{\\rm{ }}1/4$ ratio of $BB:{\\rm{ }}Bb:{\\rm{ }}bb$ will be seen.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(50122,'biology',93,272,530,'A pea plant that is heterzygous for the flower gene makes gametes. What is the probability that one of its gametes contains the recessive white allele for flower color?','[{"text": "$50\\\\% $", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$25\\\\% $", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$0\\\\% $", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$75\\\\% $", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'50 % of the gametes will have dominant allele and 50 % will have recessive allele.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(50123,'biology',93,272,530,'When homozygous dominant parent is crossed with heterozygous parent, the percentage of offsprings with different phenotype than either parent is','[{"text": "75", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "25", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "50", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "0", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'When a homozygous dominant parent (i.e., TT) is crossed with heterozygous parent (i.e., Tt), the offspring produced is of the same phenotype as that of parents, that means 0% of offsprings of recessive character will be produced.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(50124,'biology',93,272,530,'If a cross is made between AA and aa, the nature of ${F_1}$ progeny will be','[{"text": "Genotypically Aa, phenotypically a", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Genotypically aa, phenotypically A", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Genotypically Aa, phenotypically A", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Genotypically AA, phenotypically a", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'${F_1}$ will phenotypically A -dominant but genotypically Aa - heterozygous',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(50125,'biology',93,272,530,'If T (tallness), Y (yellow colour) are dominant, when a plant heterozygous for both traits is selfed, then the ratio of pure homozygous dwarf and green would be','[{"text": "$1/16$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$3/16$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1/3$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1/4$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'When a plant heterozygous for tallness and yellow colour are selfed, they will produce ${F_2}$ generation with 16 different genotypes. Out of all, there will only be one homozygous recessive genotype. Therefore, the ratio of pure homozygous dwarf and green is 1 : 16.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(50126,'biology',93,272,530,'In genetics, the use of checkerboard was done by','[{"text": "Darwin", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Correns", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Punnet", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "Mendel", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'The use of checkerboard was done by British geneticist, R.C. Punnet, in 1927.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(50127,'biology',93,272,530,'What features can be understood from Punnett square?','[{"text": "Formation of the zygotes", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Production of gametes by the parents", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "Production of gametes by the ${F_1}$ and ${F_2}$ plants", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "All of them", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'It is a graphical representation to calculate the probability of all possible genotypes of offspring in a genetic cross. The possible gametes are written on two sides, usually the top row and left columns. All possible combinations are represented in boxes below in the squares, which generates a square output form.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
