(50986,'chemistry',44,397,792,'How many electrons in a fully filled f-subshell have ${{\\rm{m}}_l}{\\rm{ = 0}}$ ?','[{"text": "4", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "2", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "6", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "1", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'In an orbital having ${{\\text{m}}_l}{\\text{ = 0}}$, two electrons having opposite spins can be accommodated.',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(50988,'chemistry',44,400,795,'When 4 f subshell is completely filled with electrons, the next electron will enter into a subshell for which, $(n + l)$ value is equal to','[{"text": "5", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "7", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "9", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "3", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'The next electron will enter into 5 d subshell whose $\\left( {{\\text{n }} + {\\text{ }}l} \\right)$ value is equal to 7 .',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(50991,'chemistry',44,397,792,'An atom has 2 electrons in K shell, 8 electrons in ${\\mathrm{L}}$ shell and 6 electrons in ${\\mathrm{M}}$ shell. The number of electrons having $l = 0$ is','[{"text": "6", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "4", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "2", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "8", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'The electronic configuration of the atom is: ${\\text{1}}{{\\text{s}}^{\\text{2}}}\\,{\\text{2}}{{\\text{s}}^{\\text{2}}}\\,{\\text{2}}{{\\text{p}}^{\\text{6}}}\\,{\\text{3}}{{\\text{s}}^{\\text{2}}}\\,{\\text{3}}{{\\text{p}}^{\\text{4}}}$.<br>For s orbital, $l = 0$. Therefore, number of electrons having $l = 0$ is 6 .',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(50994,'chemistry',44,393,784,'The ground state energy of hydrogen atom is -13.6 eV . The energy of first excited state of ${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{Li}^{2+}}}$ ion in eV is','[{"text": "$-$3.4 eV", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$-$13.6 eV", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$-$25 eV", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$-$30.60  eV", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'${\\mathrm{\\mathrm{En}=-13.6 \\times \\dfrac{Z^{2}}{\\mathrm{n}^{2}} \\mathrm{eV}}}$<br>$ =  - 13.6 \\times \\frac{{{3^2}}}{{{{(2)}^2}}}{\\rm{eV}}$<br>$ = \\frac{{ - 13.6 \\times 9}}{4}{\\text{eV}} =  - 30.60\\,{\\text{eV}}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(50997,'chemistry',44,401,798,'The maximum number of electrons in an atom with the following quantum numbers is <br>${\\rm{n = 4}},l{\\rm{ = 3}},\\,{{\\rm{m}}_l}{\\rm{ = }} - {\\rm{1}},\\,{{\\rm{m}}_{\\rm{s}}}{\\rm{ = }}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\rm{ + 1/2}}$','[{"text": "1", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "2", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "4", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "3", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'According to the Pauli\'s exclusion principle, in an atom, no two electrons can have the same four electronic quantum numbers.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(50999,'chemistry',44,397,792,'How many electrons could be held in the second shell of an atom if the spin quantum number ${\\mathrm{\\left(\\mathrm{m}_{\\mathrm{s}}\\right)}}$ could have three values instead of just two?','[{"text": "6", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "4", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "12", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "16", "isCorrect": false}]',2,'If spin quantum number (${{\\rm{m}}_s}$) had three values instead of just two, each orbital would hold three electrons. There are total four orbitals (one 2 s orbital and three 2 p orbitals) in second shell. Hence, the number of electrons in second shell would be 12 .',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(51002,'chemistry',44,392,780,'For hydrogen atom, the shortest wavelength of Lyman series is ${\\lambda _L}$ $ \\mathop {\\rm{A}}^{\\circ}  $ while the shortest wavelength of Paschen series is ${\\lambda _P}$  $ \\mathop {\\rm{A}}^{\\circ}  $. The value of ${\\mathrm{\\dfrac{\\lambda_{\\mathrm{P}}}{\\lambda_{\\mathrm{L}}}}}$ is','[{"text": "9", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$\\\\frac{1}{9}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$\\\\infty $", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "1", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'For shortest wavelength, ${\\mathrm{n_{2}=\\infty}}$<br>${\\mathrm{\\therefore}}$ For Lyman series, ${\\mathrm{\\dfrac{1}{\\lambda_{\\mathrm{L}}}=\\mathrm{R}_{\\mathrm{H}}\\left[\\dfrac{1}{1^{2}}-\\dfrac{1}{\\infty^{2}}\\right]=\\mathrm{R}_{\\mathrm{H}}[1]}}$<br>For Paschen series, ${\\mathrm{\\dfrac{1}{\\lambda_{\\mathrm{p}}}=\\mathrm{R}_{\\mathrm{H}}\\left[\\dfrac{1}{(3)^{2}}-\\dfrac{1}{\\infty^{2}}\\right]}}$<br>${\\mathrm{=\\mathrm{R}_{\\mathrm{H}}\\left[\\dfrac{1}{9}\\right]}}$<br>$\\therefore \\quad {\\lambda _{\\text{L}}}{\\text{ = }}\\frac{{\\text{1}}}{{{{\\text{R}}_{\\text{H}}}[{\\text{1}}]}}{\\text{ = }}\\frac{{\\text{1}}}{{{{\\text{R}}_H}}}$ and ${\\lambda _{\\text{p}}}{\\text{ = }}\\frac{{\\text{1}}}{{{{\\text{R}}_H}\\left[ {\\frac{{\\text{1}}}{{\\text{9}}}} \\right]}}{\\text{ = }}\\frac{{\\text{9}}}{{{{\\text{R}}_H}}}$<br>$\\therefore \\frac{{{\\lambda _{\\text{p}}}}}{{{\\lambda _\\ell }}}{\\text{ = }}\\frac{{{\\text{9/}}{{\\text{R}}_H}}}{{{\\text{1/}}{{\\text{R}}_H}}}{\\text{ = 9}}$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(51005,'chemistry',44,394,787,'A particle \' ${\\mathrm{A}}$ \' with mass \' ${\\mathrm{m}}$ \' has de Broglie wavelength of ${\\lambda _{\\rm{A}}}$ $ \\mathop {\\rm{A}}^{\\circ}  $. Another particle \' B \' with mass \' 4 m \' has de Broglie wavelength of ${\\lambda _{\\rm{B}}}$ $ \\mathop {\\rm{A}}^{\\circ}  $. The value of ${\\mathrm{\\lambda_{\\mathrm{A}}}}$ is ____ times that of ${\\mathrm{\\lambda_{\\mathrm{B}}}}$ if the kinetic energy of both the particles are equal.','[{"text": "4", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "2", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "6", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "8", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'${\\mathrm{\\lambda=\\dfrac{\\mathrm{h}}{\\sqrt{2 \\times \\mathrm{m} \\times \\text { K.E. }}}}}$<br>${\\mathrm{\\lambda_{A}=\\dfrac{h}{\\sqrt{2 \\times \\mathrm{m} \\times \\text { K.E. }}} ; \\lambda_{B}=\\dfrac{h}{\\sqrt{2 \\times 4 \\mathrm{~m} \\times \\text { K.E. }}}}}$<br>$\\frac{{{\\lambda _{\\text{A}}}}}{{{\\lambda _{\\text{B}}}}} = \\frac{{\\frac{{\\text{h}}}{{\\sqrt {2 \\times {\\text{m}} \\times {\\text{K}}{\\text{.E}}.} }}}}{{\\frac{{\\text{h}}}{{\\sqrt {2 \\times 4{\\text{m}} \\times {\\text{K}}{\\text{.E}}.} }}}} = \\sqrt 4  = 2$<br>$\\therefore {\\lambda _{\\rm{A}}}{\\rm{ = 2}} \\times {\\lambda _{\\rm{B}}}$<br> $\\therefore $ The value of ${\\mathrm{\\lambda_{\\mathrm{A}}}}$ is 2 times that of ${\\mathrm{\\lambda_{\\mathrm{B}}}}$.',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(51008,'chemistry',44,389,769,'The density of fluorine nucleus in ${\\rm{g}}\\,\\,{\\rm{c}}{{\\rm{m}}^{ - 3}}$ if its radius is $5 \\times 10^{-13}$ is ____. $({\\rm{Mass}}\\,\\,{\\rm{of }}\\,{\\rm{F}} = 19\\,\\,{\\rm{amu}}).$ [Assuming the shape of the nucleus is spherical]','[{"text": "$6 \\\\times {10^{ - 12}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$6 \\\\times {10^{10}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$6 \\\\times {10^{11}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$6 \\\\times {10^{12}}$", "isCorrect": true}]',3,'Mass of the nucleus of F atom$ = 19 \\times 1.66 \\times {10^{ - 24}}\\,{\\rm{g}}$<br>$(\\,1\\,\\,{\\rm{amu}} = 1.660 \\times {10^{24}}\\,{\\rm{g}}\\,)$<br>Volume of the nucleus of F atom $ = \\frac{4}{3}{\\rm{\\pi }}{{\\rm{r}}^3}$<br>$ = \\frac{4}{3} \\times 3.14{\\left( {5 \\times {{10}^{ - 13}}} \\right)^3}$<br>$ = 525 \\times {10^{ - 39}}\\,{\\rm{c}}{{\\rm{m}}^{\\rm{3}}}$<br>Density of the nucleus of F atom $ = \\frac{{{\\rm{Mass}}}}{{{\\rm{Volume}}}}$<br>$ = \\frac{{19 \\times 1.66 \\times {{10}^{ - 24}}\\,{\\rm{g}}}}{{525 \\times {{10}^{ - 39}}\\,{\\rm{c}}{{\\rm{m}}^{\\rm{3}}}}}$<br>$ = 6.0 \\times {10^{13}}\\,{\\rm{g}}\\,{\\rm{c}}{{\\rm{m}}^{ - 3}}$<br>$ = 6 \\times {10^{12}}$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(51011,'chemistry',44,393,784,'The energy of first stationary state $({\\rm{n}} = 1)$ of ${\\rm{L}}{{\\rm{i}}^{2 + }}$ if ionization energy of ${\\rm{H}}{{\\rm{e}}^{\\rm{ + }}}$ is $19.6 \\times {10^{ - 18}}{\\rm{J}}$ ${\\rm{ato}}{{\\rm{m}}^{ - 1}}$ is','[{"text": "$ - 4.41 \\\\times {10^{ - 17}}{\\\\text{J}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$ - 8 \\\\times {10^{ - 17}}{\\\\text{J}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$ - {10^{ - 17}}{\\\\text{J}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$ - {10^{ - 19}}{\\\\text{J}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'${\\frac{{{{\\rm{I}}_{{\\rm{H}}{{\\rm{e}}^{\\rm{ + }}}}}}}{{{{\\rm{I}}_{{\\rm{L}}{{\\rm{i}}^{{\\rm{2 + }}}}}}}} = \\frac{{{\\rm{Z}}_{\\rm{1}}^{\\rm{2}}}}{{{\\rm{Z}}_2^2}}}$<br>${\\frac{{19.6 \\times {{10}^{ - 18}}}}{{{{\\rm{I}}_{{\\rm{L}}{{\\rm{i}}^{{\\rm{2 + }}}}}}}} = \\frac{4}{9}}$<br>${{\\rm{I}}_{{\\rm{L}}{{\\rm{i}}^{{\\rm{2 + }}}}}} = \\frac{9}{4} \\times 19.6 \\times {10^{ - 18}}$<br>$ = 44.1 \\times {10^{ - 18}}$<br>${ = 4.41 \\times {{10}^{ - 17}}\\,{\\rm{J}}{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\rm{ato}}{{\\rm{m}}^{ - 1}}}$<br>${\\rm{E}}_{{\\rm{Li}}}^{2 + } =  - 4.41 \\times {10^{ - 17}}\\,{\\rm{J}}\\,\\,{\\rm{ato}}{{\\rm{m}}^{ - 1}}$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(51014,'chemistry',44,394,787,'The accelerating potential (in volt) that must be imparted to a proton beam to give it an effective wavelength of $0.005 \\mathrm{~nm}$ is','[{"text": "43", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "32.85", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "50.85", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "12.75", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'For proton,<br>$\\mathrm{u}=\\dfrac{\\mathrm{h}}{\\mathrm{m} \\lambda}$;<br>$\\therefore $ mass of proton $ = 1.67 \\times {10^{ - 27}}\\,{\\text{kg}}$<br>${\\rm{u}} = \\frac{{6.625 \\times {{10}^{ - 34}}}}{{1.67 \\times {{10}^{ - 27}} \\times 0.005 \\times {{10}^{ - 9}}}}$<br>$ = 7.94 \\times {10^4}\\,{\\rm{metre}}\\,{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\rm{se}}{{\\rm{c}}^{ - 1}}$<br>Now accelerating potential is $\\mathrm{V}$, then velocity ( $\\mathrm{u}$) acquired by the charge particle having charge $\\mathrm{Q}$ and mass $\\mathrm{m}$.<br>$\\because \\quad \\mathrm{Q} . \\mathrm{V}=\\dfrac{1}{2} \\mathrm{mu}^{2}$<br>${\\rm{u}} = \\sqrt {\\left( {\\frac{{2{\\rm{QV}}}}{{\\rm{m}}}} \\right)}  = \\sqrt {\\left( {\\frac{{2 \\times 1.602 \\times {{10}^{ - 9}} \\times {\\rm{V}}}}{{1.67 \\times {{10}^{ - 27}}}}} \\right)} $<br>or  $7.94 \\times {10^4} = \\sqrt {\\left( {\\frac{{2 \\times 1.602 \\times {{10}^{ - 19}} \\times {\\rm{V}}}}{{1.67 \\times {{10}^{ - 27}}}}} \\right)} $<br>${\\rm{V}} = 32.85{\\rm{ volt}}$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(51017,'chemistry',44,392,780,'If the lowest energy X-rays have $\\lambda=4.0 \\times 10^{-8} \\mathrm{~m}$, at what z (minimum) would a transition from the second energy level to the first result in the emission of X-ray(Nearest integer)','[{"text": "3", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "2", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "4", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "5", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'<br>$\\lambda  = 4 \\times {10^{ - 8}}\\,{\\rm{m}} = 400\\mathop {\\rm{A}}\\limits^{\\rm{^\\circ }} $<br>energy of photon $=\\dfrac{12375}{\\lambda} \\mathrm{eV}$<br>So; $\\frac{{12375}}{{400}} = 13.6{{\\rm{Z}}^2}\\left( {1 - \\frac{1}{4}} \\right)$<br>$ \\Rightarrow \\,\\,\\,{\\mkern 1mu} {\\kern 1pt} {{\\rm{Z}}^{\\rm{2}}} = 3.04$<br>$ \\Rightarrow {\\rm{Z}} = 1.74 \\sim 2\\,({\\rm{Helium}})$',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(51024,'chemistry',44,393,782,'Which state of the triply ionized Beryllium $({\\rm{B}}{{\\rm{e}}^{3 + }})$ has the same orbit radius as that of the ground state of hydrogen atom?','[{"text": "1", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "2", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "3", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "4", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'Radius of ground state of hydrogen atom $ = 0.529\\mathop {\\rm{A}}\\limits^{\\rm{^\\circ }} $<br>$0.529=0.529 \\times \\dfrac{2}{Z}$ (given)<br>$0.529 = 0.529 \\times \\frac{{{{\\rm{n}}^{\\rm{2}}}}}{4}$  $[{\\text{For}}\\,\\,{\\text{B}}{{\\text{e}}^{3 + }},{\\mkern 1mu} {\\mkern 1mu} {\\text{Z}} = 4]$<br>$\\therefore \\,\\,\\,{\\mkern 1mu} {\\kern 1pt} {\\rm{n}} = 2$',NULL,'Hard','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(51027,'chemistry',44,397,792,'The maximum number of electrons that can be accommodated with $n=4$ is ____. $(n=$ principal quantum number)','[{"text": "36", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "45", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "14", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "50", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'Magic numbers are 2, 8, 8, 18 and 32 with  ${\\rm{n}} = 1,2,3,4,5$ and 6 respectively.<br><img src=\'/img/data/chemistry/44_51027_explanation_1.png\' alt=\'Question Image\' style=\'max-width:100%;\' />',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(51030,'chemistry',44,393,784,'The energy of hydrogen atom in its ground state is $-$13.6 eV . The energy of the level corresponding to the quantum number ${\\mathrm{n=5}}$ is','[{"text": "$-$0.54 eV", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$-$2.72 eV", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$-$1.4 Ev", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$-$2 eV", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'<br>${{\\rm{E}}_{\\rm{n}}} = \\frac{{{\\rm{E}}\\,{\\rm{for}}\\,{\\rm{H \\times }}{{\\rm{Z}}^{\\rm{2}}}}}{{{{\\rm{n}}^{\\rm{2}}}}} = \\frac{{ - 13.6 \\times {1^2}}}{{25}}$<br>$ =  - 0.54\\,{\\rm{eV}}$',NULL,'Easy','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(51033,'chemistry',44,388,762,'In an oil drop experiment, the following charges (in arbitrary units) were found on a series of oil droplets<br>${\\mathrm{2.30 \\times 10^{-15}, 6.90 \\times 10^{-15}, 1.38 \\times 10^{-14}}}$<br>${\\mathrm{5.75 \\times 10^{-15}, 3.45 \\times 10^{-15}, 1.96 \\times 10^{-14}}}$<br>The magnitude of charge on the electron (in the same unit) is','[{"text": "$2.15 \\\\times {10^{ - 15}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$1.15 \\\\times {10^{ - 15}}$", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "$4.45 \\\\times {10^{ - 15}}$", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "$6 \\\\times {10^{ - 15}}$", "isCorrect": false}]',1,'The magnitude of the charge should be smallest and other charges should be integral multiple of that smallest charge. So, in this problem, smallest charge is ${\\mathrm{2.30 \\times 10^{-15}}}$, but other charges are not integral multiple of this charge.<br>So, smallest charge is ${\\mathrm{1.15 \\times 10^{-15}}}$ because other charges are integral multiple of this charge.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
(51036,'chemistry',44,397,792,'The orbital angular momentum quantum number of the state ${\\mathrm{S_{2}}}$ is','[{"text": "1", "isCorrect": true}, {"text": "2", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "0", "isCorrect": false}, {"text": "3", "isCorrect": false}]',0,'${\\mathrm{S_{2}}}$, i.e., ${\\mathrm{3 p}}$<br>for ${\\mathrm{p}}$ orbital $l = 1$.',NULL,'Medium','MCQ',NULL,NULL,'2026-07-25 18:34:34.081508',NULL),
